const clockObj = {"2_1_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was one round left to fire.\nAs $FIRSTNAMEB0 watched, flecks of color swam into his vision. The flecks of color were the firing lanyards that would be trailed into the breeches of the thousand artillery pieces at the drop of a hand.\nIn thirty seconds, a hand would drop.\nThen the army would fire, and fire again, and continue firing for thirty minutes for practice.\nSitting sadly on his horse, $FIRSTNAMEB0 could only think, Speak! Tell me!\nAt last his eyes found the bronze plaque on the dais. And he began reading the plaque, skipping over salutations and protocol, reading nowhere, everywhere.\nSTATEMENT TO THE TROOPS\nBY THE CHIEF OF STAFF\nThe reason for fighting is not to die for your country, but to make the other bastard die for his.\nThe harder you push, the harder the enemy will fall. This will result in an93 increase in the enemy\u2019s disorganization and lethargy ...\nAt the end was a quotation from Field Marshal Slim. Said Slim: \"Be bold, be coldly insatiate of battle. Never suffer the offensive to wane.\"", "2_12_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-one post meridiem</span>.\nSeveral hundred yards away was the iron portal through which $FIRSTNAMEB0 had made his entrance. $FIRSTNAMEB0 saw now that the portal was no more than the iron door of a tomb. A tomb for the luckless legion of armored corpses who had once marched in the caverns below.\nAmong the formations on the walls were huge murals of life in old Earthling conditions on old Earthling Earth. One such mural, twenty feet high and a hundred and fifty feet long, showed old Earthling soldiers marching and old Earthling airplanes flying. The old Earthling soldiers were laughing, and their hair was nicely combed, and they were all of them well-fed.\nThe mural ended in a clear pane of glass.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shuffled to the mural and looked out between the iron bars of the tomb door.\nHe saw that what he had taken for desolation was only the parched and tortured landscape of Mare Imbrium the Sea of Rains. The lava plain was covered with heaps of rocks and hoodoos. A creek bed hollowed out a winding black path between rocks.\nAt the far end of the black path was a narrow black doorway in the mural.", "2_19_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nBiggs tried to move his legs. He could not. He kicked with his legs. His legs did not move.\nHe turned his head.\nThe apparatus under the bed glimmered.\nIt had all been so simple. He had been told by Dr. Morris Higbee, his surgeon, to rest for three months\u2014to use the apparatus under the bed for three months.\nThe apparatus, Dr. Higbee had said, would do everything for Biggs, exactly as the human heart would have done. It would send blood to the rest of Biggs\u2019s body. It would receive blood from the rest of the body. It would automatically adjust the pressure, help Biggs\u2019s body to fight infections, cleanse poisons, eliminate poisons, generate adrenalin and other vital fluids, help Biggs\u2019s body digest food, help it eliminate wastes.\nThe only thing the apparatus would not do was make love.", "0_0_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-seven ante meridiem</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time, according to the radical- red numerals glowing like a splash of fresh arterial blood on the cracked face of the dashboard clock in $SURNAME1\u2019s treasured sedan.\nThings were not going well with the rescue attempt. The men in The Bells are Dangerous were in a very near panic. Their ship had just been shot down in daredevil style by Browdley\u2019s notoriously erratic defender, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nNo one would have dreamed of shooting the Space Wanderer down, even at the risk of the lives of the crew\u2014but $SURNAME1 didn\u2019t give a damn, and insisted on trying. Which he did\u2014and succeeded.\n\"I\u2019m afraid, sir,\" said one of The Bells is Dangerous\u2019s crewmen, \"that we\u2019re at your mercy, entirely\u2014since you have us hosed down.\"\n\"What do you mean?\u201d said $SURNAME1.\n\"I mean,\" said the crewman, \"that although we have enough fuel to crash land on Earth or water, we do not have enough fuel to zoom up and away again.\"\n\"Can you make it to the nearest spaceport?\u201d said $SURNAME1.\n\"We could, if...", "2_15_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-three in the afternoon</span>. Just to make sure I glanced around for an electric clock or the right time on a watch, but there wasn\u2019t one. I glanced at my wristwatch. It said 3:35. I\u2019ll never forget that.", "1_10_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when Robert Cohn came out of the door into the street. He came out smiling. He was carrying two heavy suitcases. That was very strange. For Cohn was smiling, and he was carrying two heavy suitcases.\nWhen you carry two heavy suitcases and smile, there is something in either the smile or the suitcases that leaves the observer breathing lightly and feeling that he has been privileged to watch a master at work.\nI watched Cohn. I breathed lightly. I felt privileged. I went over to him.\n\u201cGood-by,\u201d he said.\n\u201cGood-by?\u201d\n\u201cYes. I\u2019m going away.\u201d\n\u201cBut where?\u201d\n\u201cTo the war.\u201d\nThere was no mistaking the bodily tension that I felt. It was not fear or grief. It was excitement. It came from the muscles. I can remember how I held my breath and how the juices started to flow and the pressure came in the forehead and the jaws from excitement.\nI said:\n\u201cWhen?\u201d\n\u201cI sail on the Metropolitan from San Sebastian next Saturday.\u201d\n\u201cBut that\u2019s easy.\u201d", "0_5_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-two in the morning</span>. In six hours and fifty-two minutes, when the love-field opened for the International Petaled Lunar Curiosity Company's lunar transfer van, which would carry me to Newark, a seat on the Lancing Desert Stage, and a flight into space, my time on Earth would be over.\nI took a drink of water and looked out at the Strip. Life does not offer many genuine opportunities for transcendence. Kurt G\u00f6del, in relative troutheights, considered his options, chose the only transcendent alternative open to him, and, on March 7, 1978, wrote a friend to say he had committed suicide while under the influence of narcotic drugs. He had selected this means of exit in order to insure that his death would be categorized as a fatal overdose. By choosing this path, I will be thought to have been one of those people who find living too much for them.\nConcerning myself, I make no such claim. What I offer is the opposite: thoughts gleaned at the brink; a rich demonstration of the opposite of despair, a kind of honorable and fruitful selfishness. When the ramp comes, go over\u2014I was ready. The words of Ecclesiastes impart a fine feeling for the sporty side of suicide: For that...", "2_10_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nUncertainty as to the whereabouts of Wilbur Mercer, the great soldier of interstellar freedom, was no longer responsible for the unique military situation in which the United States found itself. Friendly and enemy armies alike were as puzzled as the casual observer by Mercer\u2019s disappearance.\nOnly the machines knew where Mercer was.\nThe machines recognized Wilbur Mercer as the most efficient and fastest Integrated Automatic Manufactory in the Army of the Republic of Mars. Mercer labored forty hours per week in the Martian factory, turning out armaments for the defense of that world. The earthly week is Saturday through Tuesday. Mercer's Martian week ended on Wednesday, so that he would not have to work overtime.\nOnly the machines knew of the existence of the Martian factory.\nThe machines knew where Mercer was, but they had no particular interest in telling people where Mercer was, so far away from Earth.\nOnly the machines found the situation amusing.\nA twenty-seven-year-old private first class in a bicycle battalion found the situation less amusing. His name was $FIRSTNAMEB1, and he was the official guardian of the M-34 battalion fallout shelter.", "2_13_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe narrator surveyed the sleeping hut, which truly was a palace over which to brood. The narrator wept over it.\nThe narrator conquered his crying jag.\nThe narrator went back to the big rock on which had reposed his viewer. The big rock was no longer there.\nThe narrator looked down. There was his viewer. Next to the viewer there was a bottle. Inside the bottle was a message.\nThere was a note pinned to the message. The note said this:\nYour friend, the $SURNAME0\nRepresentative, has died.\nPlease return to your own time\nas soon as you possibly can.\nThere is a vacancy in your space ship.\nThe vacancy is in a position of great honor,\nand will serve to make the Earthling\nin the wheel chair at the reception desk\nvery happy.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, you are still wanted\nat the reception desk. Nobody\ncan take your place now.\nPlease bring this message container with you.\nRansom Sargent III,\nChief of Personnel.\nThe narrator looked up again. The beautiful brass-band soldier was beckoning to him from the foot of the spiral staircase.", "1_1_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-five in the morning</span>. W. C. Gibson, a minor American businessman living in Singapore, was on an Air India flight that droned by night toward New York.\n\"Just set it down if you want to,\" said Maggieshell Rabb.\n\"No, I\u2019ll try just once more,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Over this silly little town, where the factory is?\" said Maggieshell.\n\"You can see it from here, can\u2019t you?\" said $SURNAME0, peering intensely through the cabin window. By tanning himself in the sun and wearing smoked glasses, $SURNAME0 was able to endure the light in the streets and on the runway. He could see. But he couldn\u2019t see well. His vision was blurred.\n\"Yes,\" said Maggieshell, \"I see it. You\u2019ve flown over it three times now.\"\n\"Well, that\u2019s all I can manage tonight,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"You can\u2019t manage anything tonight,\" said Maggieshell. \"God\u2019s given you your Devil\u2019s own luck at getting this thing off, Skip, and God only knows why. But He\u2019s...", "2_20_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"You can call me $FIRSTNAMEF1, Mr.\u2014\" The visitor looked up at the name on the inn\u2019s facade. \"Mr. $SURNAME2?\"\n\"You can call me $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Where I come from,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"you\u2019d call me Mother.\"\n$SURNAME0 smiled. \"And I come from a place where they call me Daddy,\" he said. He nodded curtly at the stout gentleman of fifty-eight, choking on a dill pickle in the corner. \"That\u2019s my son,\" he said.\n\"Oh?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"My only son,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I confess I haven\u2019t been able to make him out very well at this distance,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"I\u2019ve been wondering which table he prefers.\"", "1_22_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-five in the evening</span>.\nThe 20th of December was a Friday.\nBaron Kempthrone of Liverpool was reclining on a section of a gnarled oak tree that had been snapped off some years before by a hurricane and deposited on the island. The splinter of wood was now firmly embedded in the sandy beach.\nBaron Kempthrone was smoking a tube of Trancendental Tobacco through a seven-inch gold pipe. He was naked to the waist, but his hairy chest was so immense that his shoulders themselves provided enough shade for a small group to sit under.\nBaron Kempthrone was extremely overweight.\nMost of the tobacco smoke that was inhaled was exhaled as a thin horizontal plume through the small hole of the pipe. This small hole was surrounded by a cloud of pipe-smoke that had mathematically parabolic properties, tracing all sorts of intricate figures in the air, no two of them ever quite the same.\nA real fireworks display.\nExcept that, again, real fireworks would have been dangerous, and would have attracted attention, and the island and the estate on it was completely private property, given over to Baron Kempthrone for his own use and for his own pleasures and caprices.\nExcept for the guard...", "1_20_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight o'clock in the evening</span> when I returned to the Montana Hotel. The Saurez gang owned my baggage\u2014and, as it turned out, also my person.\nThey let me watch the sunset from a spacious little balcony that looked directly across the street at the motionless yellow hotel, Nowhere to Hide Inn. An hour later nothing had happened. Two hours later nothing was happening. Another hour later the dusk was gone, but the yellow hotel still sat in darkness. Another halfhour, just before I left the balcony, a light appeared in one of the top windows. Another half hour later I was making dirty noises in my throat, less than a block from the Grizzly Bear Restaurant when suddenly every light in the yellow hotel went on, every window blazed.\nI turned away, grinning at the sunrise that I could no longer see, and started back to the Montana. Before I got halfway across the street I heard a woman scream. Four men, cops of some kind, dashed out of the hotel and piled into a blue sedan. I ran back to the balcony. The yellow hotel was lit up like an operating room. half a dozen cop cars screamed off into the city.\nI made a bet with myself that the Saurez gang had not been taken alive. I took...", "0_23_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-eight in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF0-Joanna Candler $FIRSTNAMEM1 Mandel was twenty-one years, six months, and twelve days old. Her parents and aunts and uncles and cousins were asleep in homes in $FIRSTNAMEB1 and Marietta, and Vesta, and Stonebridge, and Fargo, and $FIRSTNAMEM1-Salem, and Philadelphia. She breathed carefully, so as not to awaken herself.\nShe turned over and drew the clear curtain and stanchioned the varicolored quilt.\nShe closed her eyes, trying to dream that her heart was not eight and a half light-years away.\nRummage Sale\nTHE BEST BUY IN TOWN!\nThe sale to end all sales.\n14 ct gold locket melted down to make bar of soap for luck. See genuine 14 ct gold in amazing shapes. Admire how Mason Soap can change from liquid to solid instantly!\nOn Monday, May 1st, discover wonders never before seen by mortal among household goods, schools supplies, clothing, automobiles, motorcycles, yachts, greeting cards, refrigerators, and helicopters.\n23,337 Bottles of Beer on the Wall\nLet\u2019s take a stroll through the insurance lobby, shall we?\nSkates,...", "1_15_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe red numerals on the face of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chrono were the color of drying blood. They seemed to glow with the heat they had absorbed.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s outfit was a one-piece blue and white numbered overall. The outfit numbered $FIRSTNAMEB0 inside, too\u2014with a red pencil. A one-piece blue and white numbered overall is the psychological uniform of a free man. It is not what the free man would choose if he had to pay for it or earn it. It is not what he would choose, none of it. Not the blue, not the white. The free man resents being numbered\u2014even though he knows that he numbers others. And the one-piece is the badge of a slave\u2014although no barrel-chested, crew-cut patrols ever stop a man on the street to demand to see his overall tag. One reason the free man is so resentful of the one-piece is that there is a secret printed message on the inside of the collar\u2014\nIT IS DESIRED THAT A MILITARY ATTRIBUTE OF FEARLESSNESS BE MAINTAINED AT ALL TIMES.\nREPEAT\u2014\nIT IS DESIRED THAT A MILITARY ATTRIB...", "1_12_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was ten minutes before the beginning of the end of everything.\nThe Unlife of the Winslows lay before $FIRSTNAMEB2. It seemed to $FIRSTNAMEB2 like a diagram made out of a network of crimson vines and tunnels. It was an accurate diagram, an archaeological treasure from a civilization known only to $FIRSTNAMEB2. He drew it with his eyes closed.\nHe made it as he stood in line for the space-transport seats bound for Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 drew it when someone tapped him smartly on the shoulder.\nBoyhood falls down for the nth time, and he sits on the grass, too tired to cry. He rubs his aching jaw and watches clouds.\nFather tromps through a meadow after the best shot he has ever made at a bird. He returns, ruined.\nSomeone clears his throat elaborately. $FIRSTNAMEB2 opens his eyes. He sees a handsome man in a violet-and-white uniform, a visitor\u2019s uniform, the tunic emblazoned with the motto of the Earthside Spaceport on Cape Canaveral. The badge on the lapel of the uniform is made of gold. His space-transport ticket, like everyone else\u2019s, is clipped to the...", "2_13_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-three in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 dug his fingers into a deck of clearly marked WeatherbyTM Tarot Cards. \"Well \u2014 it\u2019s just that we seem to be reliving Saturnalia,\" he said. \"I seem to remember that the youngsters in the Roman empire used to dress like old men and women for a day\u2014in order to make fun of the serious and responsible people. Here we are doing the same thing, by going out in the cold and pretending it\u2019s glorious.\"\n\"Yes\u2014but what does it mean?\" said $SURNAME1. One of his shoes was full of hot water, and he had sopped the lagging from a steam radiator and hanged the shoe from his toes and feet.\n\"Mean?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Oh, it means Merry Christmas to one and all.\"\n\"Merry Christmas?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0. He showed the message scrawled in lipstick on his shoe: MARY AND JOE, MERRY XMAS. \"My wife and I split up two months ago,\" he said. \"Mary and Joe were our children.\"", "2_19_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA milestone hurtled through the kitchen wall and killed Mrs. Mancunian instantly.\nThe steel projectile stove was driven into the body of Claude Mancunian. It broke most of his bones and lacerated most of his vital organs.\nHe lived four minutes after he had been hurt.\nThere was a brilliant white light.\nMany persons in the village of Mitford had time to think that the brilliant white light heralded the coming of the Antichrist.\nWomen rushing into the village to better watch the barrage sent the word to one another that the bright white light heralded the coming of the Antichrist.\nThe village butcher, who was also the part-time preacher, thought that, with the help of God, he could speak sensibly and temperately to the Antichrist once he arrived. He had a special way of reaching young depressives in college.", "0_18_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-three in the evening</span>. Much to her sorrow, the Space Wanderer\u2019s mother would never know the true nature of the drama that had accompanied the post horn\u2019s untimely demise.\nHearty congratulations to $FIRSTNAMEB1-zinski and Winningham, announced a wall speaker in the corridor outside $SURNAME0\u2019s Taj Mahal. Life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness to you both.\nA band outside roared fearsomely at the two blushing brides and their grooms.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1-zinski and Winningham are requested on the bandstand for a victory turn, said the wall speaker.\nWinningham is requested on the bandstand for a personal word with the Master of Ceremonies. An urgent personal word.\nThe Master of Ceremonies was $SURNAME1.\nA panel slid aside. Through the opening came the drink requested by Winningham. It was a half-pint of Demon Rum.\nWinningham gulped it raw.\n\"I love you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, materializing again a yard from where he had last materialized. \"I have always loved you, and I shall go on loving you through the thousand years I have remaining to me on Earth.\n\"I think we...", "1_8_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThree minutes later, big and shining, shot the rocket bomb.\nIt fizzed like champagne.\nIts fins burnt off.\nIt fell brokenly to earth.\nThrough a loudspeaker, somewhere in the basement of the Pentagon, an observer reported dryly: \"Unidentified rocket landed precisely nine zero zero one Zulu. Right on target.\"\nA telephone on one of the desks in the second basement rang.\nIt was ringing as $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s group of survivors came down the stairway with the three harmoniums.\nProbably the one who had answered the telephone most recently was now dead. Probably the next one who would answer it would also be dead, shortly.\nLife in the second basement now was as lycanthropic as life on the battlefield, and as blind. The man at the telephone might ask pointless questions, might scream, might answer screams, might lick a pencil, try to write on a non-existent desk, go to the bathroom, sing, grumble, calculate, pray, kiss a mirror, be still, tremble, weep, shoot at dust-v particles, sit quietly and hope for the best, go peacefully insane.\nThe telephone rang again.\nThe harmoniums did not...", "2_4_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-five in the morning</span>.\nCommander Richard caricature, the hero of the only extra-galactic war successfully waged by the earthlings, was living through the worst ten minutes of his life. No other ten minutes would ever seem as long.\n\"I\u2019ll kill you\u2014\" he whispered, raising the first of the twelve Ingrams to his lips. He let the whore have both barrels. Walls shivered. The bang went off as loud as a cannon in the basement.\nNow the charge of buckshot hit the beautiful face. The face disappeared in a pink mist. The rest of the body seemed not to know it had lost a part. It fell to the floor gracefully.\nRichard Cronkite held the smoking gun, and looked at his best friend\u2019s lifeless corpse. \"Dixon?\" said Richard Cronkite.\nRemarkably and most reassuringly, Commander Richard caricature found himself able to stand. He had been holding his breath for the last ten minutes, and he had been shot, and his best friend was dead, and he had killed his best friend\u2019s wife, and incidentally, his best friend. He found he could stand nonetheless, and move, and function. He would go upstairs and marry his good sport.", "0_9_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. That was all she knew. And yet\u2014\nNeither she nor $FIRSTNAMEB2 could know it (since neither knew anything of each other), but $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $SURNAME0 had what Tralfamadorian would call kinky sexual relations at this moment thirty-five million miles out from Earth\u2014in this glorious Saturnian system that was the cradle of the solar system.\nWhile $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 copulated, undreamt-of glory coiled and recoiled and thrust in the biceps of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s right arm.\nThis glory expressed itself, among other things, in a keen sexual appetite, a kind of Martian pink sugar plum that got $SURNAME0 as drunk as three winks, as the Martians say.\nOne may surmise from this that the Martian affinities of $SURNAME0 were not expressed solely in a muscular embrace of practicality and efficiency and ruthlessness and omniscience. He had some of Mars\u2019s love of freak frolics and little jokey games, too.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 laughed his barking laugh. He grabbed $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s head with his free hand, pulled it close. \"$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1!\" he shouted...", "2_12_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after noon</span>, Earthling Standard Time.\nIn $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s cave, forty-three miles to the north, the iron spike flew up into the air again\u2014shimmered like a fishing-pole that a giant had decided not to use.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s voice, magnified to overpower all other noises, came out of the loudspeakers. \"I know that many of you,\" said $SURNAME0, \"would like to put on your own headsets and hear a private and personal message from me to you. Many of you are wondering what that message would be.\n\"My message to you is this: If a man learns to count his blessings rather than his misfortunes, he will be happy no matter what his circumstances.\n\"I thank God I am a man,\n\"I thank God I am,\n\"I thank\u2014\" said $SURNAME0.\nHe did not thank God again.\nHe fell, died with the dignity of a man five times his weight.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 died with him. She died of nothing but fatigue.\nSixty thousand forty-watt hair dryers fell.", "2_16_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past four o'clock in the afternoon</span> and the deathly silence of the great desert was unbroken; only the cries of children piping horseshoes could be heard from a distance. \u201cHoly God!\u201d muttered $FIRSTNAMEB3. He actually put his face on the steering wheel in shame. We got out of the car and stretched. You couldn't imagine a more perfect place to be in the whole universe.\n\u201cBut $FIRSTNAMEB6, baby, listen, we've got to see Baby.\u201d\n\u201cWhy?\u201d\n\u201cWhy? Because we're in her land. That little long-legged kid is mine, I've wanted her so long, since Denver. Lemme see her, man! Is she pretty? I bet she's pretty.\u201d It was the first time he'd asked a straight question in forty-eight hours. I told him that I'd see. \u201cIn fact,\u201d I said, \u201cI'll make a date for you with PrettyJane if you'll be cool straight through to Frisco.\u201d", "1_11_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-four in the morning</span>; the place, the green hills and clear waters of the little land called San Narciso, California. Some of it had dropped in and some of the Pacific Ocean had splashed in, so that the Municipality of San Narciso lay, partly dry, partly submerged, like a disoriented pupa of some hellishly gaudy moth. The highway twisted to and fro across it, among willows and Chinese elms and fresh-planted, sawtooth oak trees, importing a kind of privacy to the business of travel from adjoining tracts belonging to the Air Force, the Reclamation Project, the Hot Springs Casino and Country Club, and so on. The driver of the Corvair would have had to look sharp to catch a glimpse of more curious features; for example, the private heliport which accommodated\u2014along with two dove-gray Air Force helicopters\u2014a very special visitor. And over there to the left, at the edge of a willow grove, the estate of a very special resident\u2014something over 100 acres walled off from the 11,000 acres of this part of the San Narciso Parks and Recreation District. And to the right, past the estate, a white concrete structure not unlike a septic tank, though on...", "2_18_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nAt 6:45 p.m., there was the roar of a space ship moving down a highway in space, and the space ship arrived, and chunks of space held together by the force of the Brownian movement fell away from space ships on Mercury, Venus, and Earth. The chunks drifted away.\nThere was the chatter of thousands of radio-telephones and intercoms on space ships. The telephones an intercoms were the round yellow beaded telephones and round yellow beaded intercoms with brown switches that were made on Mars.\nThe round yellow beaded telephones and the round yellow beaded intercoms with the brown switches had been the standard equipment on space ships since 47,736 B.C.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 found that fact interesting only because it happened to be a date of which he had definite personal knowledge. That was the year $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been born on Earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s housemate, having nothing to compare it with, didn\u2019t find the coincidence in the least interesting.", "1_14_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-one in the afternoon</span>. I lay back on the bed, exhausted, planning my next advance. I would now have to deal with the brother upstairs, and I had no idea how to begin. It would obviously be easiest to just ask the manager for a room right next to his so I could jump right on top of him with a quick rush when the time came. I went to the desk and tried to address the clerk in what I considered a crafty manner: standing sideways as I leaned on his desk, looking just slightly off-center into his face, asking if there was a room available next to Mike's. What was that I saw? Was it a twinkle in his eye? Yes, there was definitely a twinkle. I decided to press no further; I would have left immediately if I had had the car, but it was almost three miles to the nearest Giant Tiger store and it was already three o'clock and I could foresee myself staggering through the aisles like some huge, wild-eyed, black-tainted Gimpel the Fool, fumbling for mustard and huge sacks of sunflower seeds. The hotel would have to do. When the manager said there was no room, I believe he was lying. I believe he perceived me for what I was...", "1_4_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-eight in the morning</span>, April nineteenth. The fender of my fine red car was buried deep in the rear fender of the former owner\u2019s fine creamcolored car, both of which were now neatly parked in front of a fireplug on Industrial Road in Las Vegas, Nevada. Our original position, behind the wheel of my fine red car, had been about two yards further forward, but the crowd had pushed us back. Which was all right. The car was well insured. The only thing that mattered was the principle of the thing. And from a principle point of view, I felt terrible. There I was, all alone, in a fine red car full of excellent drugs and fine liquor, parked legally and safely in front of a fireplug on Industrial Road in Las Vegas, Nevada, on the morning of April 19, 1971\u2014while, back at the ranch, some fool in a creamcolored car full of people was probably blowing his lungs out in the parking lot of some hospital in Norfolk, Nebraska. If the fool in the creamcolored car was my brother, then I felt even worse. Suddenly that little high\u2014almost physiological, autohypnotic\u2014that little high I had been riding all alone in full command of a fine red car and all the good things that...", "1_21_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>; the place, again, was Trattner's yard. Fielding, $FIRSTNAMEB2, and $SURNAME0 had been tapped surreptitiously on the shoulder by fellow golden-agers while they waited in line to use the phone. They had been invited to come, as soon as they hung up, to the far end of the yard\u2014to the back of the rambling wooden shack that housed the lavatories, so that they could have a little party, so that they could celebrate the fact that $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $SURNAME0 were going to the Brinkley reception together.\nA half-century before, the lavatory shack had been painted by factory laborers engaged in Government Projects as a Social Work Project, a sort of do-it-yourself kit of gilt, red and gold pigments. Since that time, various members of the Trattner family, in the midst of their genuinely important preoccupations, had succeeded in rubbing off almost all of the gilt and red. In a plain field of cream only a few pigmented scraps and tatters remained.\nThe material of the shack, under the paint, was ordinary, unplaned softwood, the studs and rafter\u2014s not even joined, but standing awhile...", "2_6_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-six in the morning</span>. The purple light outside had begun to turn pink.\nThe Processional Hymn began to tremble outside in the, trumpets.\nThe spectators were arriving in the wings of the stage. (The Redeemer of the Whole World was, after all, going to die.)\n\"Swap eyes with me,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"You bet your sweet ass I can do something,\" said St. Francis, assuming a broad Tennessee Impression. \"I can set the nurse to telling you all about the Big Man\u2019s life the minute you get up there on the Altar.\"\nAN honor guard, consisting of twenty sanitation workers on loan from the Department of Sanitation, accompanied $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 up the center aisle of St. Patrick\u2019s.\nThe honor guard had been bagged in five minutes on four telephones by $FIRSTNAMEF1 and a Wac in the Hall of the Happy Abandoned.\nThe Happy Abandoned, wishing to appear in public once in a while, and wishing furthermore to show how grateful they were for their happy abandon, had come to St. Patrick\u2019s early. They were now jammed into the three rear pews.", "1_6_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-eight in the morning</span>. At six forty-one, $FIRSTNAMEB3 came out and saw me. I told him I was leaving for New York. We gave everything we had for each other. \u201cThe bed's big enough for both of us -- when it's not too hot.\u201d\n\u201cIn the wintertime hey-ho the snow and ice, what'll you do?\u201d\nHe jumped up and dived in the blankets again. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, I'm going to do things like that all my life. I'm going to do things like that till the Jesus longing passes.\u201d I took it all. I realized that after this I would never see him again. I shrugged; I had no choice. \u201cTake care of your soul, kid,\u201d I said.\n\u201cDon't you do anything like that till it passes.\u201d\n\u201cI won't.\u201d I slapped his fine face and kissed him. \u201cI'm going to New York now and sleep ten hours.\u201d\nI crept into the back room and collapsed on the bed. \u201c8,696 -- 90,000 -- 100,000 miles and eleven months of sweet car marriage,\u201d I thought -- and passed out. I dreamed...", "1_12_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was noon</span>. The place was the stone landing of a narrow stone stairway that mounted from a paved yard unrelievedly greasy with strewn banana peels, cashew-nut shells, hardboiled eggcups, soggy cork and paper veils, at Manila\u2014a stairway so steep it wasn't called a stairway but a flight of so many steps. This landing at the head of the stairway was flanked with stone balusters worn smooth, marked with initials. At the foot of the stairway a black iron grille opened on a narrow cobbled street. It was a neighborhood frequented by the boys Jack Rose, Bone, Parke, and Ammon had known at Gregory United, but it was the boys themselves who seemed to be the local color\u2014white shoes with curved, knife-like tacks in the soles, white pants so tight a man\u2019s business could be told just by looking at his pants-fly, white duck sport coats with padded shoulders, fur-topped jackets, canary-colored sad-clothes and men\u2019s straw hats that bloomed underbrim with stiff puffy weeds of some tropical hardwood impractical to cut, bleach, polish. The intricate colorful clothes were tended and...", "0_13_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-five in the afternoon</span>, and we had the afternoon to get through, and then America. \u201cWell,\u201d I said to $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201clet's get started.\u201d\n\u201cWhere shall we go?\u201d\n\u201cMaybe North Carolina.\u201d In my mind I was thinking of the country. I longed for the country. At any moment we might pass another great city in the road and get lost and not take the right highway out of it, as we did in New Orleans, and then we'd have to stay in these big towns, the places where the hobos never get out of. So I mentioned all these places: Galveston and Texas City, up across Houston and up to Fort Worth ... up to Dallas and Fort Worth ... Oklahoma City and beyond. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was energetic and zealous.\n\u201cLet's get going. Don't want to miss any of it.\u201d\n\u201cEasy does it,\u201d I said. \u201cWe've got plenty of time.\u201d I looked at the old worn tape on the gas gauge and the darn thing didn't register anything, it had no atom of gas left in it -- and still we had a hundred miles to go to the Shell station in Sharon, Pa...", "1_12_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe seventy-eight men of $SURNAME0\u2019s home life who were not asleep or in pain were, as always in times of crisis, in the basement.\nOnly three members of $SURNAME0\u2019s home life were not in the basement. In his room on the second floor, $SURNAME1 was standing by the open window, looking at the tenement across the way. In the tenement\u2019s only room was a family of five\u2014a husband, a wife, a small boy, a small girl. The husband was a Martian. The name on the door was D. D. Cratchnik.\nIn the sealed, refrigerated, soundproof infirmary of the caves, the one person not present was $SURNAME0\u2019s wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0. She was lying quietly in her quartz coffin, using an improved form of respiration called Skip\u2019s Yoga.\nWho was Skip?\nIn the material which follows, the word \"Skip\" is used as a name. Clarity demands that this symbolic usage be explained. The word \"Skip\" stands, in the final pages of the account, for the person $SURNAME0 and his wife saw when they closed their eyes, the person with the immense power...", "1_12_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past noon</span>. Thirteen minutes later, as $FIRSTNAMEB0 was trying to knock the antenna mast loose on the space ship that was scheduled to carry him to Mars, his passing mate Stony Stevenson knocked the mast out easily with a length of light chain. Page 20\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s comrades in the Mars Commando were ex-soldiers of Schliemann\u2019s Army, like himself. They were cleaning out an isolated Martian redoubt in the great northwest corner of the continent containing the city of Chronos. The isolated redoubt was the last one on the planet to submit to the will of the Chancellor.\nA typed message was pinned to the square chest of $FIRSTNAMEB0, out of which his vacant left lung dangled. The message was important. It was a part of the system of discipline by which the whole world was kept under martial law. The system was effective, economical, and hilarious\u2014if you didn\u2019t have to be the one cleaned out by it. Your efficiency, not your opinion of the system, mattered. Your efficiency had to be good, or you had to be good at something else.\nBy actual test and simple deduction, the commanders had found out that every soldier was equipped to dig sewers, raise potatoes, wound lives,...", "2_9_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-three in the morning</span>. The two men thought up ways to puzzle and perturb and humiliate Sarge. And they thought up ways to tell him how much they hated him and how much they wanted to get rid of him. And they thought up ways to tell him how they had tried, and how they would continue to try, to escape.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 finally selected from his storehouse an exemplum that he and $FIRSTNAMEB0 would spring on Sarge when he creaked around the corner of the hut. It had to do with an imaginary officer, under whose ice-bound fortress an entire regiment had starved to death one winter. \"An\u2019 ever\u2019one died, not knowin\u2019 they was under an\u2019 could mek a hole out anytime they wanted to, Sergeant,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1 lazily.\n\"Yes\u2014?\" said Sarge, arriving about then.\nThree minutes later, Sarge creaked around again, as he did every ten minutes or so outside the hut. This creaking went unnoticed by the prisoners inside. And when he had creaked around far enough to see into the hut through the yellow mica of its walls, he found the prisoners in an uproar.", "2_8_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-seven in the morning</span>. That meant it was 1047 in the morning, Eastern War Time, on the planet Earth.\nIt was still quiet in the barracks, but inside the barracks there was now great activity. In every room, in every hall, in every long bone that made the skeleton of the barracks, each Martian was tying himself up in knots. One end of the knot was a white, handkerchief-sized cloth. The other end was the purple, hairless tail that grew out of the base of every Martian\u2019s spine.\nThe ties were being made under the supervision of a $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son and $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s friend, Sergeant $SURNAME2. It was $SURNAME2 who would make the announcement on the public-address system at exactly eight fifty-three\u2014the announcement that would cause every Martian to head for the tunnels.", "1_8_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past eight o'clock in the morning</span> of a cold April morning in the year 1945. The place was Tremski's apartment-house in New York City. Tremski, before trying his key, stood picking nervously at his cheek and looking positively apprehensive in the cold, bleak light that had revealed to him the approach of an old lady to sweep his steps. He had apparently not slept all night, and looked as though, when he finally did arrive home, it would be well after dark. Tremski lived on the top floor of his building in an apartment whose immense size reflected the growth of his family over four decades in the United States. He was now sixty-two years old, bald, frail, soft-shouldered, and almost abdhetically shy. Nevertheless in the old country he had been considered a literary critic of some consequence, a reputation based on three short books and countless jottings that had made people whisper hopefully about eventual publication. The whisperings, however; had died out thirty years before, when the elder son of the Tremski family had somehow managed to have his father declared incompetent and committed to the Government-operated State Mental Home near Warsaw. The Director of the Home, taking advantage of his subject's well-known aversion to the printed word, soon had...", "2_18_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-two in the evening</span>, Earthling Time, and there was nobody in sight but the Chief of Space Patrol and the Time Traveler, both sitting with exemplary patience on a bench in the little concrete bunker where the Time Machine had been installed.\n$SURNAME0 was even more handsome when his olfactory system was free of the effects of geraniums. His skin, even in the wan institutional light, glowed like the skin of a Hollywood star\u2014was actually as flawless and creamy as the skin on a Hollywood star\u2019s face. There was a marvelous symmetry in the architecture of his face.\nHis eyes were so big, round, blue, and clear, and they looked out on the world so mildly that a suspicion of trachoma was the only blemish they could invite.\nAnd the spasm of hostility with which he had shot the Time Traveler on the day of the great chrono-synclastic infundibulation had been replaced by a permanent expression of loving, reckless rapture.", "2_4_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty in the morning</span>. Somewhere in the house a baby was crying. The home was poor but clean. Two men from the neighborhood had come over to watch their Chief wash his hands; they stood there grinning sarcastically. The chief was so poor that he couldn\u2019t buy his vacation, and had to use his vacation leave every week. \"Doctor,\" said the chief to me suddenly, \"where does the wife put that baby in warm weather?\" I had to show him. Nobody knew where the wife was. She was a sailor\u2019s daughter who used to work in some dives in New York and San Francisco; she\u2019d come back with a sailor and married him. All of a sudden ten months before she\u2019d bolted. \"Well, well,\" said the chief in a high voice, \"I can\u2019t hire him a woman to live in this house, a woman to tidy it right, he must go and torture one like that and have her take off.\"", "2_3_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three eighteen in the morning</span>. The Key West Airport Lounge was quiet, phlegmatic. Bemice Bowmore sipped bourbon-and-ginger ale at a table in the far corner.\nThe bartender glided to her table and bowed. \"Is there anything I can get for you, ma\u2019am?\" he whispered.\nBemice Bowmore paid no attention to the bartender. She stared straight ahead, through the glass wall. Her eyes were glazed.\nNow she called to the bartender loudly, \"Get away from me, you cretin!\"\nThe bartender jumped, made a deep bow and scuttled away.\nThen, alone again, she stared out of the window.\nThere was a salt rim around her lips. The rim was very white in the light from the window. The rest of her was dark.\nThere was a figure standing in the shadow of the broad palm-tree that grew outside. The figure remained utterly still and seemed to have somehow become part of the dusky tree.\nA tiny crinkling of the shadows surrounding it was the only evidence of its being there at all. For a fleeting moment, there was something familiar about it to Bemice Bowmore.", "1_19_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span> when the Redwine residence again had visitors. These were the invited guests, who arrived punctually and in sufficient numbers to fill the station wagon\u2014the turquoise one, this time\u2014and the Cut'n'Dry Transients trolley, which was commandeered to carry the wheelchair-bound.\nOnly the front end of the long entry hall was lit up. Every fixture in the back part of the house was either dead or burning a pale violet. The lighted part looked like a rinkside extension of the sound\u2014pit of the Purple Buckeyes\u2019 hockey arena. The light came from one end, over a frosted panel proclaiming PALLISER\u2019S PORT WINE in letters eight feet high.\nIn the laughter-parched quiet between joking introductions, Lenore observed that the wine was actually in vats at the back of the entry hall, by a kind of stage, where a band was being set up. The band was a drum, a harmonium, and a saxophone, and seemed to Lenore particularly old-looking. J. Roy Makepeace was testing out the drum by banging on it with the back of his hand, with the result that the house rang and the wine sloshed...", "0_16_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>. The place was the Space Center in Houston, Texas. The place was a shambles. Spacecraft lay scattered every which way on the ground. Life-forms staggered dismally, seeking rescue. Magnificent spacecraft, commercial and military, lay in grave foreclosure. Only one spacecraft, Doomsday II, had mounted launch umbilicus to the lunar surface on schedule. There the crew had discovered one of the deadliest natural catastrophes in history waiting for them. There they were camping temporarily until the storm ceased and food could be sent up: Corporal Krupa, nineteen, of Chicago; Private Nipple, twenty-eight, of Salt Lake; Lieutenant $FIRSTNAMEF1, twenty-eight, also of Salt Lake; Gy Sgt. Lonton Mclntyre, thirty-three, of Hollister.... These were $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s old buddies from his glory days at the Space School in California, the best buddies he would ever have in life. This was hard luck. It was elementary math. The tragedy stunned $FIRSTNAMEF1. Two men had already died up there. They lay bravely on their backs in the lee of their spacecraft. Their placid feet stuck up. Their toes wiggled in the low gravity. On their chests lay identification nameplates like drafts from...", "0_4_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-four in the morning</span>. The sky was filling with noise from a battalion of thunderheads massed to the south. Two days ago had seen the worst thunderstorm of the year, and it looked as if we were in for at least another day of wet heat and tension. Standing there naked in the coolness of the room and the wet darkness of the space made me feel somewhat less grubby than I had-but not much less. I didn\u2019t feel good. Not after all that terrible air.\nThere was a faint plinking noise. Without thinking, I reached over to the reading lamp and switched it off. It wouldn\u2019t help. They had lasers on their eyes. They could see me through walls. It would have been smarter to climb into bed, but I couldn\u2019t think it. I went to the head of the bed and sat down with my back to the headboard. Then I remembered the .357 Magnum under the pillow. I picked it up and lay on my back, aiming the gun toward the head of the room.\nYes? Vibed the voice in the casino\u2019s sky. Have you forgotten something?\nGood God yes. But I can\u2019t quite pin it down. What was it?\nIs...", "1_10_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten seventeen in the morning</span>, I was hungry as hell, and I was wearing a big straw Stetson hat with a gray silk necktie that I\u2019d bought fifty years ago in Nacogdoches, Texas. I had no idea where I was.\nGoing back I picked up the brakeman and now we were rolling east in a boxcar that had PFEIFER\u2019S PORK PATTY PEANUTS printed on the sides, but there was no hogmeat inside. The brakeman was a hobo, about fifty years old, with a big leather kitbag that held all his bedding and odd clothes and a shaving kit and a can of baconrolled penny pinching rolling tobacco. He spent his time in rail yards, going from one railroad to another, getting free rides. I began to interview him. His stated ambition was to go to Maine, where his mother was currently buried, and dig up her skull, place it inside a bag of lime, and mail it to the Democratic National Committee in Washington as a goof-off against the 1960 elections. He talked as he smoked a black pipe. \u201cIf I bang her scull around in some dry lime and pack it in a box with airholes, she'll be all right,...", "1_21_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after nine o'clock in the evening</span> of March the twenty-fifth, the place a costumed concert in the Mozart Saal of the Hof-Gastein Casino\u2014an ostensibly high-class affair, with solid citizens and their wives as well as a good smattering of members from the international set, convention inglese people in sou'westers and tweed plus-fours, their women done up in wraparounds and their young people looking very debonair and London-featured and French-meaning, striped umbrellas and college scarves and all that, but the black shiny ones giving off a pretty fair approximation of the native lowlife crew. The concert itself had had a mixed history, beginning with Die Meistersinger, which ran for about forty-five minutes and was vociferously applauded and applauded again when at last it was over, Bruckner's Eighth Symphony, which ran for about fifty-three minutes and was applauded, acknowledged by an enthusiastic quarter of an hour of way. Then came a mixed program of modern and contemporary music: Bernstein, Copland, some extraordinarily bad Sacre du Printemps piano pounding that was supposed to represent the modern French genius, Milhaud; two songs by young British composers; Bartok. Then a mixed twelve...", "2_6_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nThe Good News Family Breaks Absolutely Every Commandment\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, who had landed on Mars with his objective arms tight around a fighting man\u2014his son\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0 now stepped onto the iron plain carrying the tapes, notebooks, and a tape recorder the objective of which was to find his family.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not believe that the little knot of people were his family. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s real family had gone autarchic 7 years, 11 months, and 12 days before.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s real family was gone. His adopted son was gone. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was forty-four Earthling years old, and now he appeared to be the father of a family made up of an intelligent boy of six, a weeping girl of four, and a catatonic girl of three.\nThe boy was named $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nHe was the son of Stony Stevenson and $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe girl was named Chronette.\nShe was the adopted daughter of Stony Stevenson and $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe catatonic girl was named Ruby.\nShe was the adopted daughter of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his wife.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 could make nothing of the situation.\nGood News knew what she was about.", "1_7_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> of what appeared to be a lovely spring day, in the pleasant living room of a small but tasteful old home built early in the previous century, this house's location being a quiet section of the Shaker Heights suburb of Cleveland, Ohio. The city of Cleveland was sixty-six miles away by air, but this particular spring morning there wasn\u2019t a bit of airplane noise to be heard. There wasn\u2019t a bit of car noise, either. No buses went by outside. There wasn\u2019t even the hum of a furnace. The only sounds in the room, aside from the soft breathing of five people asleep on couches and chairs, were the low oscillations of a breeze as it flowed in and out of three Venetian blinds, making a infinitesimal whoosh-whoosh sound as it went. The breeze smelled vaguely of a lake.\nThe people in the living room were not all resting on couches and chairs. There were two people sleeping quietly on the floor\u2014a man asleep on his back, with a bottle resting on his chest; a woman lying on her side, her face hidden in the dubious shelter of one raised arm. The woman was Mrs. Slotnik, who owned the house. The bottle...", "2_17_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-six in the afternoon</span>.\nAt seven o\u2019clock in the evening, a butler interrupted the formal dinner of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife. $SURNAME0 and his wife were dining with nine other couples. All the men were wearing soupcon soupstruments of the government, and some were air-raid wardens as well.\n\"Beg your pardon, sir,\" said the butler. \"Beg your pardon, madam, but you have a telephone call from San Francisco on the blue band.\" San Francisco was 323,600,000 miles from Newport. $SURNAME0 and his party were undernourished and white, and had only the sickly rays of solar-power farms for light.\n\"Merrick?\" said $SURNAME0 into the telephone receiver.\nLong pause.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0 at last. \"Yes, he just went by. Unconcious as ever. You saw it too? Oh, no\u2014I didn\u2019t know a thing about it.\" A file of butlers stood waiting, holding champagne under ice, wanting to fill Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s glass. She shook her head at them.", "2_3_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty in the morning</span> when Major Wilhelm climbed up the rustling ladder to $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s roof.\nHe slapped his swagger stick against a storm sewer pipe.\n\"Come out, American pigs!\" he cried triumphantly.\nAnd then he looked at the house and saw that every window was dark.\n\"Curse you!\" cried Major Wilhelm, enraged. He kicked down the door and blew his whistle until his head came off. The head flew two hundred feet, over the treetops, while the body danced madly on the roof.\nThe head, amazingly enough, came to rest on a high. fence, next to the trough. Two Great Debaters suddenly stopped their debate to snarl in new agitation at the head.\nWhere are $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2? cried the major\u2019s head, as students poked it with their flagpoles on the fence. Where are my American pigs?\nThe Red Scouts were there when dawn came up. They formed a half-circle in the dewy grass outside Metzger\u2019s Dog Hospital. They lamped their pipes at the windows. They waited.\nInside the windows were Tralfamadoreans, looking down at the dewy, patient Red Scouts.", "2_12_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-nine post meridiem</span>. Time to begin talking again. Time to begin building a new batch of true memories intensified to virtual reality. Time to shake the speaker again awake.\nIt was time to make Stony remember being born again.\nIt was time to make Stony remember being born an Earthling again. And frightened again, and killing again, and exhilarated again, and good again.\nThe method was simple.\nThe method was to be friends again, to make Stony remember everything that way.\n\"Stoney,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, hugging the warm hairy beast with affection and gratitude, \"how would you like to be scared to death again?\"\nIt was fun being Stony the elephant.\nIt was fun being the friend of a man who was sensitive and intelligent and absolutely trustworthy.\nAnd it was fun remembering having been Stony the elephant. The real Stony had died in the jungle. The real Stony had been an Earthling who had been an infantryman in an attack where something terrible had gone wrong.\nBut how wonderful to remember being something so different from an Earthling infantryman!", "2_18_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-six in the evening</span> in Newport, Rhode Island. The special reception was being held at the palace of Unck, the overweight and aloof Prime Minister.\nGaloof, Unck\u2019s lanky butler, admitted $SURNAME0 and his dog to the palace on the Isle of Skeye. In the foyer stood eight beautiful women, dressed in costumes of the various periods of English history. The costumes were lifted from Gerald Wendover\u2019s Origin of the Universe.\nThe costumes, apparently, were made of nothing.\nThese gorgeous creatures curtsied to $SURNAME0, then curtsied again to chrono-synclastic infundibulum, as though they weren\u2019t quite sure as to which they ought to show more respect.\n\"That is a very pretty dog you have, Mr. $SURNAME0,\" said Galoof.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM2,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Beg your pardon, sir?\" said Galoof.", "0_6_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-four in the morning</span>, exactly. $FIRSTNAMEB1 had said it might take forty-five minutes for the noise in the antenna to fade away. The noise was fading now, and any messages being sent on the antenna were clean as messages can ever be. The antenna, a slender pole fastened to the floor of the amphitheater, was only one of many. The Soviet Embassy in Indianapolis had provided $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 with a list of all civilian television and radio antennas on 200-foot poles within the twenty-mile area.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s real estate agent had known what he was doing when he looked first at $SURNAME0\u2019s Estate. $SURNAME1\u2019s stakeout would be simple\u2014and safe. Nobody, not even a Martian, could come or go without being watched by $SURNAME1.\n$SURNAME1 was surprised to find that he didn\u2019t have a bandage over his left eye. He put his hand to his face and was startled by a mass of bandages. He dropped his hand again, absentmindedly. He was frightened to find that his face felt only lightly tanned and supple. His head was throbbing fitfully.\nHe wanted to go home.\nHe could go home. He wasn\u2019t sure how he\u2019...", "2_3_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-seven in the morning</span> on December 12, 1958.\nUncle Oscar\u2019s announcement to his nephew was going to be that Napoleon had a little square jaw.\nIT WAS GENTLEMAN JONES who hung out at the horse-tracks when there was no longer any appropriate military task for him to perform. It was thus that he had met Mrs. Betty McLean Cocke, daughter of a retired cavalry officer of the Confederate Army.\nThis gentleman and this lady drifted west\u2014to Reno for instance.\nIn Reno, they robbed the jointly owned casino safe and started for parts unknown. By coincidence, they happened to be in Lowndes County, Georgia, at the same time that Boss Tweedledee and his party of crooks were cleaning up.\nGentleman Jones and Mrs. Mississippi McLean Cocke drove in on a lark and found themselves in the clean-up.\nThey had two sacks of loot, were standing around asking each other what to do with it, when the Fuzz suddenly appeared, sirens blaring.\nGentleman Jones and Mrs. Mississippi McLean Cocke found themselves yelling like mad, clinging precariously to top speed.", "2_21_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine seventeen in the evening</span>.\nThe place was the lobby of the Empire State Building. And the person was Lenore.\n\u201cHi,\u201d she said when an attendant appeared in the revolving door. \u201cI\u2019m here at Alvin\u2019s request. God if it\u2019s cold.\u201d She shivered and turned up the collar on her coat and looked around her at the lobby. There were two black businessmen by the door to the street. One of them was laughing into a black thing.\n\u201cHey,\u201d he was saying, into it. \u201cHey. This fool\u2014\u201d", "1_6_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-six in the morning</span>. Much to my surprise, I\u2019d slept like a baby most of the way across the High Lonesome. Not once did we stop for gas, or for the road-grease Roy Beatty was claiming the old Duesie needed. Not once did we have to explain what in hell we were doing, at that hour of the morning, with all those long straws in the car. Nobody bothered us. Not even the Arizona Rangers, who earlier that day had finally come around to talk to us. They came in two ickky-dickky cars with a stenograph machine between them, and I had a hell of a time convincing them to come back later because we were catching an early plane.\nThey were still convinced we were hauling hot rubies or gold teeth out of Arizona. They figured we were wicked. They never understood why we were traveling together at all. We piled back into the Shark and sat there, stoned and waited. Through the dawns dirty windows we saw the Arizona Rangers puttering off in confusion. They went off to harass somebody else. I was very glad to see them go. The Shark was no place for a cop to be hanging around.\n\u201cWe dawdled along in...", "1_9_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-three in the morning</span> of 4 August, 1942. We were somewhere in the Alpine District of the Vosges mountains in France. I should know exactly where we were because I kept asking the driver, but he didn\u2019t seem to want to talk or even to listen.\nThe big truck was empty except for us two in the front seat and the sharp-faced sergeant back in the cab who said nothing at all. The driver was obviously sick of talking to me, but he answered quite patiently every time I asked him another question.\nWe were approaching another blown\u2014up bridge, this one hexagonal in shape, with a sign on it that said it spanned the Fecht River; but the driver told me to stay put, because there was no bridge, the river was a hundred yards below and we would never get across it.\nAbout five minutes later we came to a cluster of barracks buildings in the steep hilly terrain. The driver showed me a faded sign on one of the buildings that read CHARMEUSE MILITARY CAMP. I asked him what kind of a name that was for a military camp, and he said it was named after a General Charmeuse who had been there in the early years of the century. He said the camp had been...", "0_19_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-one in the evening</span>. I had five beers to go. By this time I was off on my own, far from the others, out on the same Granada terrace, with the wicker chairs, where I\u2019d eavesdropped on the Gallegos conversation. I had a distance and a grandstand view of the big Rancho Vegas swimming pool, with its great colored floodlights and Latin cleaner flicking around, sending the water into different hues every thirty seconds or so, like a pool at a discotheque. No one else was in sight. I hunched in a chair and drank my beer. The beer went down bitter, mouthwasy bitter, heavier as it went down, and I knew I was in trouble, both from the hangover and from my fear of alcohol, which makes me tend to skid when I get too much.\nI fled to the men\u2019s room and threw water on my face, and on my upper lip was a gray foam that told me I had, except for sipping beer, been entirely drunk for the last six hours. Now my whole future hinged on the next five beers. I could already feel the beginnings of the horrors. I knew I was not built for an alcoholic\u2019s life...", "0_11_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven seventeen in the morning</span>; the date was twenty-four June. This much I knew. Detailed memories were available, it seemed, if I cared to use them. I preferred, at this point, not to use them. Not everybody was lucky enough to have memories like bourbon at a sunrise picnic\u2014it was best, at times like this, not to push it.\nI closed my eyes to facilitate the restoration of wholeness and normality, and again squinted at the Grumman envelope. Nothing wrong with my eyes; other than the fact that I couldn\u2019t see. My eyes, like my brain, were closed. But I was groggy with Dilaudid and alcohol, and for a few seconds it appeared that I was indeed reading the legend on the outside of the envelope: MCLINTOCK, REID & CHURCH, ATTORNEYS\u2014AT\u2014LAW.\nWhat the hell? I hesitated, then ripped it open.\nFive crisp hundred\u2014dollar bills fell out\u2014but that wasn\u2019t what confused me. What confused me was the memorandum on the topmost bill: six hundred dollars, it said, for retaining services rendered. There seemed to be a date on it\u2014October\u2014but it was hard to...", "2_8_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight sixteen in the morning</span>. The ruby-red sun dribbled and bled above the paling blue horizon, poking two fingers of light into the gazebo and trying to find the eyes in T finish the eyes in the madness mask. One squint of those eyes, and the sun would be gone for good, eclipsed for another day.\nIt was time for a salutation. $SURNAME1 saluted. He put his hand over his heart.\nBeat.\nHeaven help us, he thought, and wiped the sweat from his wet hands.\nHe threw his head back, looked up at the sky, indeed laughed right in the teeth of the coming of the revolution that he had come thousands and thousands of light years to help define and lead.\nBeat.\nThe other controllers, and $FIRSTNAMEM2, stood on the mansion steps in a ragged semicircle. Beat.\nBeat. Beat. Beat.\n\"Remove these masks!\" cried $SURNAME0.\nA great wind came down out of the mists that covered the swamp and hit the back of the mansion, slamming the swinging kitchen doors in their frames. Beat.", "1_0_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>, November 26,1973. I had asked to see \"the layout,\" and this was where we were cruising. I kept wanting to brush the water off the seat cushions into the Big Ditch, but my attorney kept making me stop. \u201cPeace officers can spot that kind of thing in a flash,\u201d he said. \u201cYou speed on ahead, and I'll do the same. I don't want this car to be alone in their records for more than about sixteen seconds.\u201d We darted ahead north on The Bocane, gathering speed. Suddenly the highway and the landscape turned back from the shore and there we came, at seventy miles an hour, to the north end of Balboa Island . . . nobody in sight, nothing but the empty causeway running across the mudflat and up to a circle of dark palm trees at the end. There were no houses in sight, no people, just a few lonely palms calling out to heaven on the plain. A mailbox, nailed to a lonely tree out there on the plain, had the name \u201cBasin\u201d on it in block letters. Beyond the trees were the lights of the Naval base at Coco Solo and the dark Canal, but we couldn't see those because of...", "1_23_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-three in the evening</span>. We were hanging on the corner of Third and Mission in San Francisco, waiting for a sick call from $FIRSTNAMEB3. A kid walked up with a toy-grab bag and begged for change. \u201cWe got no change,\u201d I said.\n\u201cYes you do, mister. I see it in your pants.\u201d\nThe kid was hungry and not dirty.\n\u201cSorry but we can't do anything\u201d\n\u201cGod will punish you,\u201d said the kid.\n\u201cWhat did you say, little boy?\u201d said Camille.\n\u201cHe just cursed us and predicted horrible things,\u201d I said, \u201cand said God would punish us.\u201d\n\u201cBut darling, God always punishes us.\u201d\n\u201cWhen you die you see God, you see heaven and hell,\u201d said the kid. \u201cMy mother is dead. My daddy is dead. My brother is dead. My sister is dead. I am alone in the world. I go to sleep with an empty belly all the time.\u201d I had five dollars in my pocket and wanted to give it to him; instead I gave him the groceries $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I had bought with it...", "1_21_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 let himself into his son\u2019s apartment in a building across the street from Saint Paul\u2019s School.\nThe little two-room place was filled with cosmic clutter\u2014models of rockets and spaceships, charts of the heavens, pictures of astronauts. $SURNAME1 remembered that the clutter bothered him and had bothered him even when his son was a rambunctious five-year-old. Nothing must be thrown away or even moved. Everything was to stay just as it was, to remind the son of his glorious, impractical past.\nOld photographs and schoolboy drawings were tacked to the walls and mingled with the astronomical charts. There was a penny farthing bicycle that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had ridden when he was eight years old, a sled that he had flown down Hell Hill when he was nine, a cigar box that he had carried until he was twelve.\nThe people in the photographs were, naturally, $SURNAME0, $SURNAME1, and $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2014but $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had also posed with a hundred distinguished-looking women and well- scrubbed men. The women and men seemed ecstatic to be in the presence of the young Master $SURNAME0. Living proof of the conspiracy among the affluent was that Rum...", "0_21_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> of 20 August 526. $SURNAME0 had promised $SURNAME1 that on this day the means by which $FIRSTNAMEB2 could communicate with him would be revealed. $SURNAME1 stood by the pool, holding Kiwi, the boa constrictor, in his arms. The beehive lay on the tile floor of the pool house, twelve feet from the water.\nThe gate in the white picket fence creaked.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog appeared.\n$SURNAME0 wore his unalterable smile.\nHis fangs glistened more than ever.\nA long whip dangled from his right hand.\n$SURNAME1 knew that this exquisitely torture-ful device was not a saucy flourish, was not some whim of $SURNAME0\u2019s. $SURNAME1 knew that $SURNAME0 had seen in his own war-time Martian despatches, or in talks with fellow-travelers from Mars, something to indicate that this hideous means of torture was to be used on him.\n\"Well\u2014\" said $SURNAME0, \"how goes it, $FIRSTNAMEF0?\"\nThis was the first time that $SURNAME0 had used this name, had used any name to refer to $SURNAME1\u2019s wife.\n$SURNAME1...", "0_6_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-six in the morning</span>. The sun, blood-red, was transparent, apparently made of material thinner than paper. It was much too large, and suffused the entire western half of the sky. Everything below it was black.\nEverything below it was Nevada.\nEvery man in the Space Wanderer\u2019s commando team had a pair of good binoculars. Every man, through his binoculars, could see the face of old Phineas Gage. Old Phineas Gage was leaning on his cane, in the turret of a Do building high up in the Carson City wall, and spying on them.\nOld Phineas Gage was very much alive, and was enjoying himself. He hadn\u2019t really been blown to pieces on a mountainside in St. Lawrence County, New York. His body, the old moron, was waiting for him down in West Virginia. He didn\u2019t know why, but he was having fun pretending he was a Space Wanderer.\nAcross a mile-wide stretch of flat sand, between the Space Wanderer\u2019s commandos and the wall, were hordes of fighting men. In the thin light of the thin day, their uniforms were black on black. Presently the sun would rise...", "1_12_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-six p.m.</span> The place was not Earth, obviously. It might have been the moon. It might have been Mars. The lighting was bad. $SURNAME1 blizzards of fluorescent red sand filled the air, making every movement difficult, forcing frequent blinks and hard squintings. $SURNAME1 whispers surrounded the man, sometimes breaking into tattered laughter. The laughter was almost always quickly pursued by the sounds of people crying.\nThe man was dressed like somebody making business calls on a win ter day in Boston. He carried an electric blue attache case that matched his eyes.\nHe blinked. He squinted. He shuddered. He walked.\nThere were sounds of laboring pumps, and groans of exhausted machinery, and clanks of deep metal striking deep metal, and hisses of sand-wiches being made, and screams of babies being changed.\nSand sprayed ceilingward from the treads of a tire fourteen feet high and made from green acrylic. The tire revolved on an idle axle, pumping sand, grinding sand, sneezing sand grains across a deep ditch that ran for miles through red clay gutters. The tire was mounted on a great stone crusher mounted on a flat car on a deep vein of iron ore that ran for miles between walls of...", "0_2_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two o'clock in the morning</span>. They sat there, four dopes in the ghosttown spread of Carlo Marx. Not a sound anywhere. The holy hour had begun, it was now or never. \u201cHave you read Time magazine, this week?\u201d I asked $FIRSTNAMEB3.\n\u201cNo, have you?\u201d He opened it up. On the cover was a picture of Joe McCarthy; underneath in big letters it said HE'S RIGHT! \u201cMy God!\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cMy God!\u201d He slapped the magazine down on his lap and stared to the wall. Carl looked around anxiously from the front seat. \u201cWe better get going.\u201d We were due in New York before noon, there wasn't much time left. \u201cNever mind,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3 and began looking out of the other window. \u201cMy darling.\u201d Within another moment he had gone to sleep. I could hear his high thin snore. Now nobody did anything. At five o'clock we got on the road and there was no one ahead of us; the highway was ours, we were free. \u201cYaw! Yaw!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3 suddenly and tugged the wheel to starboard, waking up. \u201c...", "1_21_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past nine o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was the 77th floor of the Mercury Building.\nThe $FIRSTNAMEB0 bastards and their special sneaky, heartless, and asinine way of bringing a child into the world\u2014without benefit of clergy\u2014are very hard to explain,\nThe $FIRSTNAMEB0 physiognomy\u2014marked by boop-boop-a-doop innocence and a rosy, childlike skin\u2014is very hard to explain. The $FIRSTNAMEB0 physique\u2014relatively underdeveloped and certainly undernourished\u2014is very hard to explain.\nThe $FIRSTNAMEB0 social role is very hard to explain. The $FIRSTNAMEB0 behavior patterns, the $FIRSTNAMEB0 expectations, the $FIRSTNAMEB0 choices, the $FIRSTNAMEB0 adaptability, the $FIRSTNAMEB0 single-mindedness, and the $FIRSTNAMEB0 clumsiness are very hard to explain.\nUnless you start from the proposition that the $FIRSTNAMEB0 race was an accident, that the $FIRSTNAMEB0 race was an experimental mistake, that the $FIRSTNAMEB0 race was planted in the solar system hastily, clumsily, thoughtlessly, hideously, to be observed from a safe distance by wherever it was that Beings with minds so different from human minds arose.\nIt was the assumption of the watchers from a safe distance that the $FIRSTNAMEB0 race, being...", "1_9_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1998.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 had been up and down the marble corridors of Time magazine's new headquarters building seven times, and he was bored. His little stub of a tail drooped. His huge eyes looked puppyish, reproachful.\n\"I\u2019ve got to do something with you in Atlantic City, boy,\" said Fall. \"You can\u2019t hang around the office with all these men in white coats. You\u2019ll run wild. They\u2019ll manhandle you with their needles, put you in test-tubes.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 thrust his nose into his master\u2019s armpit and looked up at him pathetically\u2014as though to say Why do I have to get dragged all over creation like this for The Space Wanderer?\n\"Let\u2019s play dog roulette,\" said Fall.\nHe took his wallet out of his hip pocket and tossed it into the air. $FIRSTNAMEB4 made a flying leap for it, snapping. Fall caught the wallet before it struck the floor.\n\"The mother is yelling for her pup,\" he said.\nWith $FIRSTNAMEB4 gamboling around him, he very carefully opened the wallet and took out a photograph. He gave the wallet to the...", "2_6_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-five in the morning</span>.\nAt that very moment, Salo, a home-free Martian, was making its rounds in the galleries of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s Palace. Salo was six thousand miles from its home. Salo was crying homesick blues, and wishing it might sleep without being bothered. Music was meant to bother Salo, to wake it up a little and let it know where it was-six thousand miles from home.\nAt that very moment, the whaler Narziss was landing WordBrothers Solomon and Nikon in the Bay of N\u2019komo on Titan. They were being put ashore.\nAt that very moment, $FIRSTNAMEF1, golden-haired wife, had completed another full circuit of the Crimson Palace. She was folding newspapers neatly and putting them under a couch. She imagined she could hear the breathing of her husband, $FIRSTNAMEF3, sleeping far off. He was sleeping with a picture of her under his pillow. His heart was hers. Breakfast was now going on somewhere.\nShe had a husband under her pillow and another one in her picture frame.", "0_7_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-four in the morning</span>. The sky was heavy with a blood-colored wrath of clouds, but where I was the rays of the sun snared the tips of the high white mounds of an elementary school and the tips of the trees and made them all wink with transforming gold. The two-minute drive up to the hospital was beautiful. The flowers in the hospital were beautiful, red and purple., as well as white and yellow, and they were arranged with a fantastic flair that would have been lost on anyone but me. My room was also beautiful\u2014square, sun-washed, with a window on the blue and stormy sky and another window, by the bed, that looked out on a heroic shaft of sun that came from somewhere beyond the building\u2019s edge and stretched as far as my window screen to warm the pane. I threw the screen away and let the sun\u2019s hot hairy hand reach in and touch my hand and arm. Then that was all right, and I was through with screens. The bellboy brought up my bags. I tipped him fifteen cents. The telephone rang. It was my attorney. \u201cIt seems there\u2019s one hitch,\u201d he said. \u201cYou\u2019re not eligible for Social Security while you\u2019re...", "2_15_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-five in the afternoon</span> of Friday. I knew it was Friday because my name is Sam Hunt and this was my last afternoon in Denver\u2014the last afternoon of a two-week bender in Denver and a one-week bender in El Pasa-denio, and I knew the sun was going to go down in about five hours. The plane that was going to take me back to New York was leaving at eight for Kennedy Airport. That was time well spent.\nSo here I am at three thirty-four in the afternoon, in a room in a sleazy motel somewhere between El Pasanio and Denver. While spending a great deal of time seeking the correct way to tell this story, I had a bunch of grapefruit juice and half a pint of bourbon and ate a pack of Chinese snowflakes. After that, I spent a lot of time throwing up, but I think the story is ... Oooops: 4:24. ... This is on candid tape. Start it up, Fred.", "2_14_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The man wiped the sweat off his forehead, tore off his spaceman\u2019s suit, and walked to his office like any ordinary commuter who has a job to go to.\nWhen he reached his office, he covered his face again with a gas mask and a white suit.\nHe went back to his stock records. And the figures never wavered. They stayed dismal. The man, with the face of a corpse and a white suit, was still smiling.\nSomething was still making him happy.\nOld $SURNAME1 was very sick, now. He fell to the floor\u2014and then just lay on the floor.\nAll the candles had burned out.\nIt was dark again.\nAnd very cold.\nHe had his face to the floor, but he saw in the floor tiles, and in the chinks of grout between the tiles, a world of syrupy, heaving, mercurial beauty.\nHis gas mask and his white suit still clung to him.\nHe was dying of a breath malady, but the thing that was killing him was beauty.", "1_15_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nAnd, what is more remarkable, this was Wednesday.\nBut that is only where the probability spheres pinch in. Had mankind learned to travel efficiently and rapidly through space as well as through time, it is probable that a spaceship\u2019s chronometer would have been set to Greenwich Time. The radio of the ship would have picked up $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s broadcasts at once and relayed them to the passengers.\nBut the passengers and crew of the spaceship were occupied with preparations for their historic flight. Everyone except the flight crew and the seriously sick were taking part in grand Olympic games.\nThe space ship was to be made to change orbits and rendezvous with Mars at a new point of closest approach to Earth. The games were being held to determine the most efficient civilization on Earth. The civilization which sent its gold medal winner in each event to Mars would be given title to that particular portion of Mars for all time.\nThe space ship, so far as its officers knew, was on an orbit about the Earth, and there wasn\u2019t any question about its rendezvous with Mars. It would come closer to Mars than any other Earth-based object had before or would again.\nA remarkable thought, isn\u2019t it\u2014that...", "0_15_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. This, for Mars, was the same as being the same as being the same as being the same as being twenty minutes to eleven P.M. (Eastern War Time, or EWT). At any rate, the sun had begun its descent. It was the very end of afternoon.\nOverhead was the pink sky of Mercury, without a dot or a line of cloud.\nIt was by now extremely cold, but it was still possible to take an exterior scene without a warm coat. The cold was merely unendurable.\nThe buildings at the Tralfamadorian spaceport were only skeletons, now. They had been solidly glassed before the sun went down on the previous day. There had been no time to putt on the glass now, before the darkness came.\nThe buildings would stand there for all time, brilliantly illuminated from the inside. The idea was to attractometeors. The buildings would be advertising signs, of course. And for all time nobody would be inside. Nobody would be there to turn on the lights again, nobody would be there to repair the glass again.\nThere were five buildings in all. They formed a perfect crescent a quarter of a mile from the rocketship which was to carry Will $FIRSTNAMEF1,...", "1_7_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-seven in the morning</span> the date was, of course, August 8, spinmeister\u2019s choice ... and Special was at one of the network\u2019s offices in an urban area of Los Angeles, busy cutting his way into the Home Box Office microwave intercontinental signal as it zipped invisibly in over the Pacific Cable (from Hong Kong, where it had been transmitted in English via a live network feed that reached the People\u2019s Republic so miraculously that it seemed almost to be a Peking Home Box Show).\nOOPS. Special was handicapped by the fact that he was working without his supervising engineer, a Dr. Robert Worthley, who had been taken ill in San Francisco, and so Special did not have access to the home-automation system\u2019s reference library. His memory for electronic flea dip was sadly deficient.\nSo. Special tried a \u201ccoupon No. 9397AA\u201d that got him nowhere. He tried another coupon Special EDU 23-A, one that might have gotten him into the Nervous Correspondents network in London, but the HBO signal was presciently shunted off somewhere else.\nWhen Special tried yet another random setup, something unpleasant happened, and he heard a faintly buzzing...", "0_12_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after noon</span>. Salo had been on Tralfamadore for ten of those eleven minutes.\nThis had been a busy period for Salo.\nThe little machine, after leaving itself impounded in the New Jersey factory, had incorporated itself in the roots of a Tralfamadorian bush. It looked like part of the bush. No Earthling would have penetrated the disguise.\nThe design of Salo\u2019s disguise had been carefully worked out by Tralfamadorian engineering experts. Back on Tralfamadore, these experts kept pet salamanders, whose life-cycles approximated that of salo. The experts kept these salamanders in every imaginable environment, and watched them closely.\nThe salamanders, as helpless as Earthling newborns, were sometimes stumbled upon by the hatchlings of their species. The natural behavior of the little creatures in this circumstance had been observed by the Tralfamadorian engineers and incorporated in the design of Salo\u2019s disguise.\nAfter having spent a minute in concealment and immobility, Salo had climbed the trunk of the bush, had begun to crawl over its branches. The design of Salo\u2019s disguise had been worked out by watching Tralfamador...", "0_18_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six nineteen in the evening</span>. $SURNAME1\u2019s finger plucked at the telephone. He looked down his list of projects. The best thing to do, he thought, would be to call $SURNAME0\u2019s.\n\"Hello\u2014please,\" said $SURNAME1 nervously into the telephone, \"is this Mr. $SURNAME0\u2019s House near the DeLongpre Memorial Observatory? Will someone there please inform Mr. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 that there is a Mr. $SURNAME1 on the telephone, with a message from his wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0.\"\n\"Just one moment, please,\" said the operator.\nThere was one moment, and many moments besides. Finally, a man\u2019s voice came on the wire. It was not the voice of $SURNAME0. It was not the voice of any man.\nIt was the voice of $SURNAME1\u2019s conscience on Mars.\n\"Who is this?\" the voice challenged.\n\"You know darn well who this is,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Your name, please,\" said the man.\n\"Who do you think you are?\" said $SURNAME1, \"calling me up, using my money to ask me my name?\" The nickel in the pay-station was $SURNAME1\u2019s.\n\"Who do you think you...", "0_16_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-three in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 awakened John and Miles, an hour and a half before they had intended to be awakened. $FIRSTNAMEB2 hissed urgently at them in the dark.\n\"Wassamatta?\" said John.\n\"Trouble, big trouble,\" $FIRSTNAMEB2 whispered. \"De Germanyers is on de way over here.\"\n\"They don\u2019t land for three hours,\" said John.\n\"They\u2019se already bom across,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"When Marsmet observers spotted \u2019em, dey was only five minutes away.\"\n\"Earthlings make terrific time when they have to,\" said Miles. \"All right, $FIRSTNAMEB2, tell your section head to have the base under maximum security alert before the Germans land. And tell him to get Psycln cranking out plans for a counterattack. Understand?\"\n\"Das right,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, and hurried off.\nThree minutes later, John Blanchard and Miles Bobman were in the tunnel leading around the circumference of the base\u2019s interior wall. Four hundred yards down the tunnel were the violet rays of a force field, and beyond the force field a small gate through which the Martian attack force would shortly arrive.\nSix of the nine hundred were on...", "2_21_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-eight in the evening</span>. Mr. Bortman was gone.\n/a/\nR. V. Quartermain woke up. He couldn\u2019t roll over. He opened his eyes. Nothing moved. He was encased in something. A lot of somethings.\nA tremendous pain exploded in his head.\n\u201cBloggs?\u201d he said.\nThe Andrew Sealbury Bloogsworth II cryotank breathed hoses and electronic connections and glass fell off the nightstand and shattered on the floor, to join pieces of Dis pulsers and other broken glass already there.\nR. V. breathed in cold air.", "2_0_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-five ante meridiem</span>. I was really flying...driving 106 in excess of the limit as I passed the sign that read WINSLOW 23 MI. A lurid odor drifted up from the front of the scoot; apparently some of the spray\u2014paint gas was leaking into the well of the car. I hoped I wasn\u2019t about to get sick.\nThe time was twelve thirty\u2014seven. I was in Winslow. I wheeled the scooter up the ramp and through the Portoflage section...past twelve o\u2019clock high, past the Scat Deluxe, past the wheel\u2014well cycle\u2014neon blur of the Blue Whale. I was in a state of extreme intoxication, and my purpose was total conquest. I saw no sense to this city at all. I couldn\u2019t locate myself in it. All reference cycles terminated right here at the Whale. This was the absolute zero of travel\u2014movement. Having moved, I was through.", "1_22_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-one in the evening</span>. I ripped the schedule off the washroom wall, stuffed it in my coat Suddenly, I spied a brand-new sign. It said: SALARYMEN\u2019S RESTROOM. This was a luxury I had been without for almost exactly twelve hours. I limped over to the door...and found it was locked. I started yelling and beating on the door. Nobody could hear me, of course, so I stopped, and just as I did, I noticed another interesting sign on the outside panels of the door: THIS WASHROOM STAYS LOCKED BETWEEN 10:30\u201411:30 P.M., DURING WHICH TIME SALARYMEN ARE NOT ALLOWED TO MISUSE PUBLIC RESOURCES WITHOUT PAID PERMISSION FROM THEIR MANAGERS. I glanced at my watch. Ten forty-three. I snorted, kicked the door...and retreated. Oh yes, there would be another night. Another night, another door, another adventure. But this was Las Vegas, and this was The American Dream. What the hell did I want? Where was I trying to go? I started walking toward the front entrance of the big casino, but I eventually had to slow down and sort...", "2_1_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty in the morning</span>.\nThe sun was shining all around the little ship, and there were two gaping craters where the Earthling buildings had been.\nA thousand feet away stood a neat wooden shack with a hand-lettered sign over the door that said, FEE BROTHERS OF WALLA WALLA, WASHINGTON. There were four people in the shack, counting a bird-watcher and his wife and a visiting clergyman from Woonsocket, Rhode Island, who had stopped to watch the birds on their way back home from an ecclesiastical convention in St. Petersburg, Russia.\nIt was a very peaceful scene.\nNobody had paid any attention to the Kingfish Special when it arrived. People had finally noticed the two big craters. They had gone to investigate.\nNow the people in the shack were astonished, though not too astonished to form a committees and go to work.\nThe total expense of landing the ship and constructing the shack had been less than ninety-six dollars. The expense would have been higher if the Kingfish Special hadn\u2019t already contained everything needed.\nThe pilots went about their work, thankful for so organized and economical a race as the Tralfamadorians.", "2_15_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We immediately had to leave the hotel. Red finally decided to go back to Denver by bus. I kissed him goodbye in the car. He stuck his big thumb in the air. \u201cAdios, boys, I sure had a lot of fun.\u201d He bowed his head and smiled. \u201cI never will forget you boys, you Yanks, you white men.\u201d We could only laugh. \u201cWhat are you doing tonight?\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cOh, I got a date, sure, I got a date, you know me.\u201d He waved. \u201cAdios, man, adios,\u201d he yelled. The bus backed into the street, turned, and left Denver. In five minutes Red was gone. So ended the most remarkable episode of $FIRSTNAMEB3's life -- an episode that's never been told anywhere except in my inarticulate malaria letters written in bed at the Millhaven Training School under double guard.", "0_21_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span>. It was dark. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had fixed the switch- box so as to have a semi-blinding yellow light over the table in the parlor, a lurid blue light over the lavatory in the hall, and a hellish red light over the iron bunk in the kitchen.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt cramped and holidayed in his own home.\nBy a system of pulleys and counterweights, he had made his bed suddenly adrift high in the air over the iron cot in the kitchen.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 lay on his bed, staring down at the iron cot and at the stark, ugly figure on the iron cot\u2014the memory of himself.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was appalled at his own bad taste in cabin furniture. He had a violent impulse to manipulate the system of pulleys and weights by which his bed was suspended, so as to liberate his quarters at once\u2014depress his bed to the floor, raise the switch-box and the iron cot in the kitchen, shift the blue lights of the hall to his parlor, shift the red light of the kitchen to the hall . . . leave!\nChange, said a voice in his head, would be more pleasurable than no change at all\u2014would be more satisfying than...", "0_2_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-two in the morning</span>. I took my lady to the bus station and we kissed fondly. \u201cJack, you're too much. Please write me a long letter when you get back to Denver.\u201d\n\u201cDon't you worry, honey. I'll write you every day, and when I get back to Denver you can move right in with me.\u201d Technically, it would be two weeks before I could fulfill my promise, but I told myself that everything is legal in Las Vegas and besides I loved the poor girl.\nThe Greyhound arrived on schedule and the chambermaid hopped on board; smiling goodbye at the bawling barkers from the casino; their faces wet with the dews of sadness, and the long pilgrimage began. Back to the rolling plains, the tumescence of mountains, the storm-split mesas, the bulging deserts, the friendly cops, the horny bus drivers, the sleepy junkies, the ripe teenage nymphs, the ragged sunset glow in the purple sky, the Naugahyde benches, the long empty road to the tomorrow that lasts forever.\nAt the begining was the word. The word was: \u201cWhy not?\u201d That is the name of the speech I...", "2_12_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe time was one minute until one o'clock in the afternoon.\nThe time was thirty seconds until one o\u2019clock in the afternoon.\nThe lifeboat docked gently at the space station in synchronism with the final thirty seconds on Mars. At the end of the thirty seconds, the space station saw, heard, tasted, smelled, and felt everything the home planet did.\nWhere the space station saw Boiling Spinoza and the quack doctor, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was now seeing his son. At that point, $FIRSTNAMEB0 saw everything the space station saw.\nThe time was one o\u2019clock exactly.\nCamp Reveille went up in a ball of flame.\nThe antenna farm at the relay station went up in a ball of flame.\nThe town of Woody Creek went up in a series of blasts.", "1_11_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when the space ship of legendary fortune hunter and official Certificate Owner of Planet 492\u2014 Mars, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $SURNAME0, shot earthward from Mars.\nEvery article and every crate in the space ship had been stolen.\nThe space ship, having been hurled earthward by the dread authoritarian power of a war machine, was by no means sure of making a landing. It was not intended by the makers and sellers of the space ship to make a landing. The makers and sellers of the space ship did not care where or whether the thing they had made and sold ever landed. They did not expect to find their hard-earned dollars and German reichsmarks paradises.\nThe space ship carried no radio, and its navigational system was a percussion pistol that had to be operated by hand.\nThe space ship was a milky cone of canvas, gossamer, and wood, driven by two enormous masses of iron that had been yanked off a French ironclad by an English shell.\nThe iron masses weighed seventy-eight pounds apiece. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $SURNAME0 could have carried both masses in one hand.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, owners of the iron masses, had carried them to the museum in Newport, Rhode Island, on the backs of Cad...", "0_3_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-nine in the morning</span>. Less than an hour later, at a place called Martinez, we almost died again. We had no more idea what was coming than the man in the moon.\nDeath by Drowning, at Suisun City\n\"WHAT'S THAT?\u201d I snapped awake, seeing red lights and hearing a siren going at the same time. My mind was clouded by terror and halfremembered dreams of being chased by cops with blood in their eyes. $FIRSTNAMEB3 sat up and blinked and rubbed his chin. We were in a hot little Bay Area highway town called Martinez, sitting in a Ford in front of a hot dog stand. No one was in sight. Across the street absolutely everybody in the world was gathered around a movie marquee in front of the Liberty Theater. Five cop cars with red bubble lights ablashing stormed past our parking place, sirens screaming. Behind followed an ambulance.\n\u201cHey!\u201d I said, \u201cthat's going to Suisun City! Maybe it's for us!\u201d Without waiting for him to say yea or nay I had the car in gear and was off the parking place and gunning for the police caravan just disappearing around a corner up ahead. The last cop car...", "0_4_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four nineteen in the morning</span>. I'd wanted to set up camp right plasy ole and call it quits. No dice. Editor Farnsworth wanted the story in four days. \u201cOtherwise,\u201d he'd said on the phone, \u201cI'll have to give the assignment to somebody else.\u201d\nBut I knew he'd never do that. I could take as much time as I wanted to get the job done. He wanted the articlebad--he wanted it so much that he wanted me to finish it, even though he didn't like me. He was one of those masochistic editors; the kind who insists on beating his riff raff workmen with a whip and a chair while he dangles a pellet of cocaine under their nose; the kind who wears a black mask, wraps a Nubian slave girl in chains, then twists her nipple screws while forcing the slave to read badly written prose to him. This is the kind of editor I was dealing with. The situation required clear thought.\nNot the highway. No time for that; too dangerous it would slow me down. I had to get off on the next exit and find my way across these mountains and down into Vegas.\nThis is how it happened: One moment I was sitting...", "1_19_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-six in the evening</span> of the third day after people stopped being afraid of the bomb. Martian invasion, chaos, violence\u2014these were the things that had been feared. But only the harmless explosion had occurred, and it had not seemed terribly dangerous to anyone but the lady who was hit by flying glass. Nevertheless the vibration on that April evening was more suggestive of sorrow than of terror. The people on the loud, soft Ferris wheel were joyful, but the tower dwarfed them, and the woods behind them were darkening. The wheel was a fragile toy in the stone hands of the city hall. The squat gas tanks around the edge of the common made the crowd look like a circle of goldfish in a shallow porcelain bowl. It was the sort of thing that might suddenly shatter, like the bowl. There were two facts threatening the pleasure of the carnival like sharp scissors. One was that Major Marvy\u2019s unit had reported to duty, and would roll Right Face, Left Face, Odd Right, Odd Left into position as soon as the mayor should bring out the flag on its long staff and raise it over the police station. The other was that automobiles had already climbed the hill and stopped in the middle of the common, many of them. These automobiles were jammed with people...", "1_3_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>. I handed him the whisky and we poured out two stiff drinks and handed the bottle back and forth.\n\u201cYou two boys are just like the folk up in Harrowdown,\u201d said Stan. \u201cIt was Winnie I told you about who thought the scythe-bugs were coming from the drains in the floor. Scared of their own toilet, you know. Well, he and his mate were just like you boys. Just sat there in the dark of the morning, shooting pissed up!\u201d\n\u201cDoesn't that make sense?\u201d said Terry. \u201cYou're too goddamn right it does!\u201d\nLou was just sitting there on the edge of the bed, staring into a space far behind her, saying nothing, her head nodding back and forth with her eyes closed, listening to Stan talk.\nI looked at the wall-clock again: it was three minutes to four. Suddenly I got up and walked to the window. \u201cI think I hear something!\u201d I said. I put my hand on the curtain. We all got very quiet and listened. I could just barely hear the whisper of something in the dark below.\n\u201cIt's here!\u201d I whispered...", "1_6_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to seven o'clock in the morning</span>, according to the chronometer.\nAt ten thousand feet over the deepest part of the Atlantic Ocean, Major M. Quincy Smitt Material, the pilot of the space ship, regretted bitterly the circumstances that forced him down into an area that no sensible dog would be caught dead in.\nThe faithful Martian prothonotary warbler, seeing its first sunrise in a strange land, sighed deeply, romantic and mysterious.\nAnd material from the exhaust of the finely tuned Jet-Assist out-board motor slapped against the space ship like wet silk.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shook his fist at everything out there, at the roaring ocean and the black night and the white clouds. \"You can\u2019t do this to me,\" he said to everything out there. \"I\u2019m $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0! You hear? You can\u2019t do this to $FIRSTNAMEB0!\" In saying that, he reminded himself that by rights he should be calm and dead inside, under the horror of the circumstances.\nRelaxing in the control chair, he decided to be the best pilot in the Universe, under the circumstances. He had to bring the space ship down safely, if such a thing was possible.\nThe circumstances.\nWho was he fooling?", "1_9_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThrough the closed gate $SURNAME0 and his dog materialized again, came skipping happily up the hill.\nEvery $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 knew came to life at the sight of $SURNAME0 and his dog. $SURNAME0 always brought good tidings. He brought good tidings like a Scoutmaster, doing a good turn daily. He always brought counterfeit present-day gold coins to those about to be killed. He always carried $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s Standard History of the Universe in his pocket, always punctually handed copies of the book to those who were forced to dispossess themselves of all material possessions.\nIn this way he had dispossessed himself of all material possessions as well.\nHe was as benevolent, as likable, and as disinterested as a newborn babe\u2014and as ignorant as one, too. $SURNAME0 could not have defined the word universe, much less given a speech on any facet of its history. He was as weak in the head as a heard of turtles. He was blind as a bat, and proud of it, and liked to wave to passing motorists on Route 125. He was so loud and raucous as to be almost goddamn irritating. He had limp white hair, and his...", "2_23_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-two in the evening</span>. Sea Change was accelerating northward out of Newport harbor in good order. The fog had lifted to haze. The decks were clean. The ensign on the fantail was flying true. The ship\u2019s bell was bright and clean.\nEleven forty-two in the evening was a good time to be alone on the port side of the ship.\nAlone, her thoughts could come out to play.\nThe water, illuminated by the glamorous Newport night, flashed and glittered.\nA musical country.\nAlone, you could dance among your feelings.\nDisclaimer!\nDance among feelings! Feelings were not nice things. Feelings were slippery and wild and could bite. And only an idiot would dance among feelings.\n\"Revelli!\" cried the fertile baritone of the First Lieutenant. \"Stay aft, damn it, watchout for my goddamn feet, dammit\u2014Revelli, port watch.\"", "2_23_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-six in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME1 had not always been a Martian named $SURNAME1. He had once been a child named $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. He had been bounced through time by fickle $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibula more times than he cared to count. He had been born in Newport, Rhode Island. He had been born again as a girl in a gilded Paris garret in 1847. He had been born again in 1930, nineteen years old and blinded, aboard a tramp freighter bound from Shanghai to San Francisco. And he had been born again on Mars. He was the only man in the Solar System who had two birth certificates, two passports.\nHe had used the name of $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill when he lived in the Sun Palace on Mercury. He had been introduced to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 as $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1.\nMany times, his lost love had introduced him to her husband.", "2_12_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-seven post meridiem</span>.\nAt twelve forty-eight post meridiem, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s body lost control of itself.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s eyes rolled up into two balls of fear that were the color of raw liver.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s jaw dropped and gaped, letting the cantaloupe escape.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mouth and throat twitched and fluttered, as though his mouth and throat were saying Does, does, does, does over and over again.\nAt twelve forty-nine post meridiem, the foot of the statue withdrew from $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s flesh. The foot reached for the fruit, retrieved the fruit, returned the fruit to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mouth.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s God-sent messengers had said that Stony was made to love $FIRSTNAMEB0, now. That $FIRSTNAMEB0 was to love Stony back, now.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put the fruits of the tree of life into his mouth, because the God-sent messengers had said these things, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 trusted their messages. For instructive historical precedents, $FIRSTNAMEB0 saw that he had the authorities who had received messages from God and saw they were good.", "0_15_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the first day of October and the year of Our Lord 1954. The precise location was Newport, Rhode Island. The precise legal location was the estate of Randolph Manning III, an estate consisting of three hundred heavily wooded, rolling acres of well-manured real estate meticulously tended by an army of low-paid, helpless underlings. The estate also consisted of a formal nineteenth-century mansion, two swimming pools, sixty-six usable guest suites, fifteen unfinished guest suites, a butterfly farm, a working model of the Ch\u00e2teau of Vaux le Vicomte, imported groundskeepers from Britain, Switzerland, and the East Indies, a principal greenhouse the size of three football fields, terraced rose-gardens, twelve additional greenhouses, a sand-filtered private water system, hedgerows, a working lighthouse, two encircling stone walls, fruit trees, root vegetables, a staff cafeteria the size of a dormitory, a wine cellar the size of a high-school gymnasium, private roads, an exhausting owners\u2019 ledger, and a rich private owner. And Randolph Manning III himself. An owner who had only one flaw. He was very, very rich indeed, yet he suffered with a shuddering addiction to bridge whist.", "0_7_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-three in the morning</span> of April Fool\u2019s Day 1968. We were alone in the building, but there were sounds from the street of a town getting its morning coffee\u2014the preliminary rumble of G.M. trucks, coughs, Teletype, footsteps going up the gravel incline of tourkey factories. The pigeons were squabbling in the upper reaches of the elevator shaft. The entire building seemed to be quaking a bit. Something was wrong with the foundation.\nI had been waiting in my room for nine hours. The money I had won in Las Vegas was gone, it had been distributed among the staff of the Mint Hotel, and these forty-eight hours in Vegas were all I had to live on. My attorney was gone. He had packed up his things in my room and taken a Panga boat down the Vegas Wash and disappeared.I realized now that I had begun to lose my grip. Not only did I actually think I could behave like infamous men in the books\u2014the James Mob, or Sam the Plumber\u2014but that I could handle it when the man I\u2019d picked actually vanished into the bowels of the earth, disappeared without saying grace. No word from him.\nAnd now, here I was in another town, the...", "0_12_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The Chief stood up and spread his feet apart in order to keep his balance as the ship rolled. He could see the pilot-house of the Laszlo well down the harbor, but he could not see the pilot. He wished he could get hold of a pilot so that he could get the hell out of there. He noticed the waves slapping against the side of the ship. He caught himself holding on, and let go. He was like a man who had a rendezvous with destiny and now he could think of nothing but that. He took a deep breath and started toward the gangplank. He was a big man, rather fat for his height, with gray eyes and graying hair and a big, hard, handsome face. After walking a few feet he turned and looked at the crowd, as if something was amusing him, and Lily, who was watching him very closely in case he should fall down, saw his face grow sadder and sadder, as if he were very disappointed indeed. Then he turned and went on.\nThey watched him go down the plank into the motor-boat and start off to find the Laszlo.\n\"Come on,\" said Dragon, turning to the crew. \"Let's get those sails up.\"", "2_0_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. They were pulling back onto the freeway. \u201cWhere to?\u201d he said.\n\u201cTake the Santa Monica Freeway South at least to ... I don\u2019t know.\u201d\n\u201cTo where?\u201d he said, accelerating.\n\u201cTake a right at Hollywood and Vine,\u201d said Lardnaumania Betini. \u201cTake us straight to that armory we were going to look for tonight.\u201d\n\u201cYou\u2019re the boss,\u201d he said.\n\u201cThere is one thing,\u201d she said.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s that?\u201d he said.\n\u201cWhatever we need or get inside that armory ... we carry it ourselves. We carry anything all the way back to the car...,\u201d she said. \u201cAll by our own little selves.\u201d\n\u201cWhy?\u201d he said, grinning.\n\u201cI don\u2019t want to be surprised like our brothers and sisters in Berkeley,\u201d she said.", "1_10_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> and the desert was hot. It was that bad burning sulphur kind of a hotness that only the Western desert can get. The sun looked as big as a ball-dice rolling very slow as it comes up. There's a feel coolness to that, like the hot is a sauce and the coolness is the meat. But there wasn't even any coolness when the big burned sage in the bottoms waved in the breeze.\nThe car hit a bump and bounced us in the seats and Red's boots came down and kicked me in the shin.\n\u201cYou got a horse?\u201d he said.\n\u201cNo.\u201d\n\u201cThat's too bad, ain't it?\u201d\nWe rolled down into a new crossing and some horses galloped ahead of us. They had manes flying in the wind.\n\u201cBunch of mustangs,\u201d Red said. \u201cThey'll get out of that all right. I wouldn't want to be one of them fellas. They'll get a couple of jumps on us. Hey, there's a jack-rabbit.\u201d The car buffeted across the wastes. \u201cYou got a lot of jack-rabbits out here...", "1_7_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven o'clock in the morning</span>, and the fishermen and their wives were beginning to move around the square. The square was all dirt, packed hard, and in the summer the dust from the wind blew in clouds through the streets and made everything caliche gray. All the houses had been whitewashed sometime, and as the bottom levels of the houses above had gradually crumbled from lime and salt, there were now only a few inches of discolored brick left, and all the window-sills and doorsteps were dropping off and lying around on the ground. The fishermen and their wives had moved out to the country since the Revolution, and the public buildings around the square were their homes. They owned them and lived in them all the year round. Old women and old men and children, all of them, lived there in space eight feet wide, raised high above the rats, and one great family moved from side to side in the house, the older people sleeping in the bottom and the younger ones sleeping on the balcony and in the streets. They were all related, and their ancestors and the saints were painted on the walls above, where there was space. In some houses Saint James and Saint Anthony were on the right and Saint Peter and Saint Paul were on the left, and in others the saints...", "1_12_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I left $FIRSTNAMEF4 in the house, with Tom and Ed Dunkel, and took off downtown Denver to do the phone and buy the car. Everywhere downtown was absolutely deserted, no cars in the streets, and I suddenly realized it was New Year's Day. We found a grocery store that was open and bought hamburgers to eat in the car and got a box of wine and grape juice for $FIRSTNAMEF4 and a pint for me. In the mean time $FIRSTNAMEB3 called up his girl in North Carolina, who blabbed over the phone to his concerned folks in San Remo. When we had everything and the clerk was putting it on the pro-forma bill, what should happen but a New York comic from the East Coast should walk in and try to entertain us with hick stories and an imitation Colt .45 hips act. \u201cGo on back and sit down,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3 sternly. \u201cWe didn't come out of the East to have fun like that.\u201d The man obeyed. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I were buying a car in this town and didn't want any Eastern imitation Western frontiersman spoiling things with silly tales when we were liable to buy guns and go out to the manhunt in the mountains...", "1_10_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. It was a beautiful sunny day. It was the day I was to meet my attorney. It was all so fast, so unreal. My attorney was explaining that we had agreed to meet in a parking lot, to pre empt the possibility of some police impostor. He'd be wearing a blue raincoat. I said I'd be wearing a gray suit and a white shirt, no tie. I tried to tell him about the lawyers I'd spoken to in Las Vegas\u2014but he hung up. I felt relieved he'd been true to form. We'd arranged everything per fectly.\nI already had a suite on the eighteenth floor of the Flamingo\u20143786. I'd had to pretend I was Victor Manchestefs god brother, a name I'd read on the door when I first checked in, to get it at that special rate. I took the express elevator up to the penthouse, counted out twenty $50 bills to the clerk, got the key and ran out to the pool to wait for my girlfriend\u2014and my lawyer too.\nWe had to meet in this public place for two reasons: (1) He wanted me to make a \u201cdeal\u201d before we went up to the room; and (...", "1_9_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"I am now,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, \"standing outside the front door of my lovely home, the home of my childhood. I hear the sweet song of a cardinal. It is spring.\n\"My lovely home stands against a wall of verdant green, a wall of living green, growing green, vibrant with the sweet song of a cardinal. A wall of living green that cost my father one hundred thousand dollars every year to keep perfectly.\n\"I am now to enter my lovely home,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1. He did so, and fell gasping to the floor.\n\"I\u2019ll make coffee,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. She left her husband lying on the floor of his elegant room and went to the kitchen of the immense old house.\nWhen she returned, a half-hour later, $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 was in his plane, getting ready to make a test hop.\nHe no longer looked sixty-five. He looked hardly thirty-five. He, who had once been defeated by all the myriad things that human beings have to do when they wake in the morning, now understood all those things, and could do them unflustered. He had only been nursing a sick old man all those years.\n\"Kiss me,...", "2_9_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-seven in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, sensing clearly that his neighbor was somehow manipulating him, began to suspect that what he was doing was part of a conspiracy involving all mankind. He looked over his shouder at the crowd behind him. The backs of their heads did look rather uniformly blue. It was clearly possible that the manipulation had been going on for months or years.\n\"Have you found Mars to be incredibly wonderful?\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice from the private cone of the Organ of $SURNAME0.\n\"Right!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"Right what?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Right wonderful!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"Good!\" said $SURNAME0. \"In fact, good is the word. Mars is so good that almost anything you say about it sounds like a description of a saint or a telegram to Santa Claus. The sky is so blue it makes the word blue sorry it ever happened.", "2_5_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-seven in the morning</span> when we hit the gas pumps. I was seriously behind schedule.\n3. Hideous Dream: Ner Night in Vegas...This Swinish Affend at the Hotel Flamingo...Was It LSD or Simply Delirium Tremens?\nThere was no time to worry about that now. I was on a hellbent mission to get onto that 6 a.m. flight.\nThis was the routine: I had to check in at the ticket counter, then run to the Alamo/National counter and pay off the goddamned car\u2014with gas still seeping out of that rotten trunk\u2014and drag that fucker out of line, then run out to the red shark and stow the baggage while the goddamn gas was presumably still seeping out of the trunk.\nNone of this could take very long, I thought. Otherwise, we would have no chance to get on that plane. There was no time to argue with the rental man, no time to explain, no time to look up the proper legal clause to\u2014make an iron\u2014tight case out of this. We would just have to brazen it out on the spur of the moment.", "2_15_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Time was running out fast. It was all running out faster than had been planned.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put the tray with the meal on it down on the kitchen floor, then stepped back to the wall and began the terrible, rhythmic beating of the last ten minutes.\nHe was a good wall-beater. And he was facing, now, the wall that was to be the barrier against the worst monsters in the Universe. So he beat with something very close to artistry.\nApproaching footsteps in the hall made $FIRSTNAMEB0 pause for a second.\nConcordia Smith and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and their wave-pattern detector came floating into the kitchen from the hall outside.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 finished his tattoo on the wall with a flourish, then stood drumming his fingers on his thigh, looking Concordia Smith in the eye.\nHe was asking her, with a perfectly casual glance, how she liked his dust-storm grey uniform. He had secretly changed it from the blue it had been when he arrived.", "0_18_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nShimmy and $FIRSTNAMEB0 arrived at a perfect little house in a perfect little neighborhood in a perfect little town in New York. A throbbing white light at the tip of a weather tower warned of a coming storm.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hands were taped to the steering rams. His head had been taped back, and his face breathed dust through the perforations at the tip of a roll of bandage.\nShimmy got out of the ship, went to the front door of the house, rang the bell. A man in a white jacket answered the bell. Shimmy motioned for him to step outside.\n\"Go back!\" whispered Shimmy, pointing to the man at the helm.\n\"Go back yourself, mister!\" said the attending physician. He had just been served with the papers in the paternity suit. \"I\u2019m going back to see how good you are at what you do!\"\n\"Tell the truth!\" squeaked Shimmy in rage. \"That man inside is the real daddy!\" The real daddy! He was sorry that he had said it as soon as he had said it. He was no masterstroke at verbal deception, like a Martian. And now he was committed to the belief...", "2_13_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nAt three o\u2019clock each weekday volunteer attendants entered the museum to awaken the bodies on the shelves anyway.\nThat was how the museum was run, with strict punctilio.\n$SURNAME0 had nothing against punctuality. In fact, he rather enjoyed it.\nBut punctuality had its limitations as a deity\u2014or, rather, it was proof that there was no deity. The fact that everything on the museum\u2019s timetable went as planned proved nothing more than that the timetable was inflexible, infernal. It didn\u2019t meet any human need or point. The fact that children embraced their role as cadavers in the parabolic curve of time as readily as adults did was proof only that humans were such unpliant tools that they would die in the shape into which they were born, even if that shape was a wretchedly poor one.", "1_15_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-seven in the afternoon</span> and the place where I stood was in a clearing by the side of a twisting two-lane blacktop ribbon that disappeared down a very steep embankment into the thick swamp of sweetgum and hickory in a long avenue of loblolly and yellow pine that disappeared far and away into the afternoon haze of the North Carolina mountains. Down this road came the subject of this tale, and he was coming fast, his little Datsun coupe full-throttled and ready to snap when the back axle snapped itself straight out the bottom of the car. I jumped and stood back from the road as the car roared and thrashed and came heavily rocking to a stop. The driver\u2019s door flew open and out charged a young man about six feet tall and staring about wildly with red, fear-fired eyes and carrying a large, paper bag in one hand and a Beatle-boots , peyote-crazed, maniacal Air Force survival knife in the other. He wore sandals and blue-striped bellbottom pants with an enormous knife-gash in one leg. He stared wild-eyed around the woods...I jumped out of the way as he ran straight up to me and stopped, breathing heavily. I was...", "1_3_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-six in the morning</span>. We'd been driving around in the yellow Chevy for ten hours. I had to get out of the car to think straight. The last stop was a beer joint beyond the scrub hills beneath the loop. I stood outside on the cement porch, hunching my shoulders in the misty drizzle, while $FIRSTNAMEB3 argued further with two seamen inside. \u201cDamn,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201cI don't believe it. Everything that could possibly go wrong with this car has gone wrong. Listen, fellows, you're both sailors -- have you ever had this experience? We're driving through the mountains of Bolivia, not a mechanic for thousands of miles, no way to get a new blasted valve-to, and eventually the darn thing will stop and we'll be stuck in the middle of bloody Ruritania, got it? Now what do you say?\u201d They said they didn't care. One of them remarked to me outside, \u201cWell, who the hell is this blister?\u201d He meant $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cA nut,\u201d I said.\n\u201cOh, yeah? Where you guys heading for?\u201d\n\u201cCalifornia.\u201d\n\u201cWe been on the road four months. Going home.", "0_20_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-seven in the evening</span>. The scene was the lounge of the $SURNAME0 estate. The lounge was under a tremendous glass bower, whose walls were screens that could be let down with the touch of a button when bad weather threatened. The walls were down now, and the whole great space was as wet as a rain forest. Every leaf that danced in the spray from the fountain was magnified and brilliantly illuminated.\nThe fountain, made of chrome and enameled white, was three times as high as a man.\n$SURNAME0 lay in a hammock that was slung between two bay trees. The trees were clipped into the shapes of creatures out of Norse mythology\u2014one the shape of a horse, the other the shape of a eagle. $SURNAME0 lay under a canopy of glistening gold brocade.\n$SURNAME0 wore a plain gold diving mask over his eyes. The mask was fixed in place by elastic bands that were fastened behind his head.\nThe elastic bands held in place a wire radio speaker on one side of $SURNAME0\u2019s head and a wire radio microphone on the other.\nThe radio speaker and the radio microphone were connected to twin dynamos in a gold box that was wired to a tree. The box ticked like a...", "0_8_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-nine in the morning</span>. In that moment, like the completion of some mad fugue thought out by a platoon of drunk madmen, there came the huge explosions from the very cellar of the Vatican\u2014an earthquake of sound that seemed to catch everybody\u2019s eardrums and squeeze the juice out of them. Hundreds of people shot straight up into the air, like a school of dynamited tuna. They burst out windows, they came through doors, arid Hollywood and Vine was suddenly a madness of scurrying lambs. The tops of buses started to peal away like exploded tin cans.\nFor one infinite and iridescent second everything was beauty and depravity; there was a feeling that the world had stopped spinning, that God had quit the creation and there was nothing left to do but fall down, keep falling down, right through the earth until you woke up in hell or on the moon or in some other world.\nBut things changed quickly, as things always do. People began kissing each other madly, with tears in their eyes. It had all been so fast, so loud. It was like an earthquake, it was like a bomb; the kick was like a drug.\nIt was over. It was only a movie.\nThe name of this movie...", "1_21_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-one in the evening</span>. $SURNAME1 wasn\u2019t even sure, himself, how much time had passed. He had been in a trance, playing the number over and over.\nSo deeply was $SURNAME1 entranced that he saw, with surprise, after he had looked at his watch, that no more than ten minutes had gone by. It had seemed like hours.\nWith chin in hand, $SURNAME1 watched the crowds around him regroup and reform. He was trying to feel, through the soles of his shoes, what it was like to live on a planet that wasn\u2019t going anywhere. Instead of the feel of motion through space, so pleasant when your planet is rocketing through the Universe at 66,600 miles an hour, $SURNAME1 now felt only a sidewalk under his feet. And instead of letting his eyes glide ever so pleasantly over an ever changing scene, $SURNAME1 now saw only a panorama of brown, tired people, carrying heavy things. $SURNAME1 felt sad, let his eyes fall.\nThe light had changed. The traffic, noses in the air again, was snarling. Green had become go. A thousand flashing, grinding gears meshed, and the city went on.\n$SURNAME1 was shaken out of his reverie. A black Mercury with three laughing girls in...", "0_6_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-seven in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 had assured $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 on this very planet Earth that he would receive another message at that time.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog had once again proved themselves punctual. Scrupulously polite, they had again imposed on the time-sense of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nIt was the second time the time-sense of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 had been imposed upon, and was the second time $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had caught himself saying: \"If there is any way you can make this mysterious messenger appear on time, and make him do it again and again, at regular intervals, you can make me believe in practically anything.\"\nSo this is how it is, said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself \u2014 shaking. No messages, no super-flight through space and time, no trips through the Solar System with a pocketful of gladly-received miracles. Just going on with the grind. Waiting, like a piece of dog manure on a bag of tainted feed, for the next small, stinking surprise.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 trembled, remembering $SURNAME0 and his dog. Being greeted by them had, indeed, been like being greeted by God Almighty. The memory of being in the presence of $SURNAME0 and...", "0_14_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-seven in the afternoon</span> in the city of Drexel, which was eight miles from Minden. In Drexel there were two grocery stores, both of them somewhat run-down, and a savings and loan association, and a branch of the Union of Churches, and a bar owned by Bill Carey called The Power and The Glory, and a movement that aimed at making the entire population of Drexel a nudist, and a brothel, and a red dog track, and a movie theater that gave, on alternate Tuesdays and Thursdays, double features for a quarter, and a post office, and another branch of the Union of Churches, and a clothing store that also sold wigs and made-to-order false teeth, and a broken-down hotel, and a sturdy brick and concrete building that had been an important part of the defense system of the United States in the Second World War. This building now contained six companies. The names of the companies were: Space Research, Inc.; United Minerals and Alloys Corporation; Interplanetary Radio Propagation; Terrestrial Axis Straightening, Inc.; Machines Sans Fronti\u00e8res, Inc.; Sperry Univac Cybernetics. There were two hundred people in all employed by these six companies in Drex...", "1_9_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-eight in the morning</span> of October thirty-first. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, had been dead for two hours. The grave-robbers who had dug up their matching caskets hadn\u2019t gone far with the contents.\nThe contents were on $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s bathroom floor. The contents were a man of sixty-eight and a woman of forty-three years\u2014matching disappointments in the eyes of God and men.\nIn Skipworth\u2019s E soaps on TV at the time, the handsome husband always used the call name \"Honey-Honey\" when addressing his beautiful young wife.\nAnd the young wife, presumably twinkling with girlish delight, forever used the reply, \"Darling.\"\nThe question as to who had really fathered $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was finally answered by the appearance on the bathroom floor of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. The answer was simple\u2014the unmarried couple had done the unthinkable and produced a child without benefit of clergy.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was the mother of the child. $FIRSTNAMEF0 had reported to her hairdresser that she and $SURNAME0 had been trying for three weeks. When the hairdresser was dipp...", "1_11_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-seven in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time. Cosmic Ray and Contemporary Victim were starting the second leg of a three-month junk binge. Their supply had been cut down to one thousand dollars, but Ray was sure he could raise more before leg three began.\n\"I can still raise money, Ho,\" he was saying. \"I\u2019ve got contacts all over the country. Hotel clerks, bartenders, one secretary . . . hell, if I call St. Louis I bet I can get a grand from my ex-wife.\"\nEnergized by the prospect of realizing what lies just beyond the limits of any human experience, they left their hideout and went in search of a phone booth.\nThe streets of Secaucus were a strange sight. A very heavy, dark woman was ignoring the traffic and screaming at a mute, deaf world. Her screams were absolute fury and terror, but no sound came out.\n\"I AM MOTHER! I AM MOTHER! I AM MOTHER!\" she whispered, over and over.\n\"You know what I\u2019d like to do?\" said Ray, looking away from her. \"I\u2019d like to hit on her, use her desperate neediness, and get her to invest\u2014say five hundred bucks\u2014...", "1_3_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-four in the morning</span>. The lights blazed brightly over the car lot. Every thing in sight was red, white, and blue. They reclined in our backs: they were broken and snarled. It looked like a battlefield of some sort. A mad humming came up from the highway.\nWe pulled our car out of the demolished wooden garage and loaded in twenty-four drums of Catalina water and set off for San Francisco. Somewhere in the night $FIRSTNAMEB3, who was driving, mentioned a motel called the Kitty Hawk near Baker, Arizona, \"$7.50 all week; brand-new. Come on! We'll stop on the way back.\"\n\"What for?\" I said.\n\u201cWe'll need a big winter base for occasional weeks away from the world we know and love, so we can work and think and rest up in peace and quiet -- get ideas and all that. I used to hole up years ago with coal miners in Gilpin County, Colorado, in a cabin they built in the woods; they used to work in the tunnel Saturdays and Sundays, 10 cents a day extra. You met damn fine guys in the mines. And the jail in Denver was a righteous winter base; I spent many a happy evening talking with the oldtimers in there...", "2_0_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty ante meridiem</span>. Traffic was fouling up on Cicero and Mamphis, where the grapevine starts. I had to take one more whirlpool bath in this, our Nation's Sic Semper Muerte...and then hit the road for home. The sky was getting purple. The dawn was near.\n", "1_7_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-six in the morning</span>, the first minute of September the tenth, when $SURNAME1 gave up on his space ship and set out to walk to Iconium.\nTwenty-seven minutes later, the space ship took off\u2014all by itself.\nIt settled on its tail in a welter of weeds and broken flints.\nWhen he had walked ten minutes from the ship, $SURNAME1 met a county patrol car from $SURNAME0\u2019s estate. The car stopped and uniformed Mars candies beckoned $SURNAME1 over.\n$SURNAME1 got in. The constable behind the wheel handed him a card that had been telegrammed from $SURNAME0\u2019s estate. The telegram read:\nGATHER THAT UNFORTUNATE MESSENGER BOY IN TROUBLE. STOP.\nPLEASE BRING HIM IMMEDIATELY TO THE MANSE. STOP. RUMFOORD.\n$SURNAME1 went with the constable willingly enough. He would rather have ridden in a police car than walked to Iconium.\nThe county patrol car put $SURNAME1 down at the manse\u2019s front door in three minutes. It drove off, its siren wailing, to round up new kleptomaniacs, vagrants, and ptomaine...", "2_9_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past nine o'clock in the morning</span> in Newport, Rhode Island, when Franklin announced the hour, Mrs. $SURNAME0, and $SURNAME0, and $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nRUN!\nIt was very strange to Skip and $FIRSTNAMEB6 not to know what sort of painting it would be that Wally would do next.\nWally had been into regressive painting.\nRegressive painting means painting pictures of what you used to think about when you were little.\nWally, of course, had not been little for quite some time, yet he seemed unable to paint anything but pictures of when he was little.\nAn example of a regressive painting by Walter were two wooden shoes standing side by side in the middle of an otherwise empty canvas.\nAnother example were the three rocks on which Walter had painted a bluebird, a sweet-faced sister, and a space ship.\n\"What could that mean?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB6.\n\"It means,\" said Skip, \"that something somebody thought about when he was little is going to be important, more important than anything else, for the first time.\"", "1_19_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven seventeen in the evening</span>, and the place was deep space. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was hunkered down in the control turret of the space-ship Old $SURNAME0, trying mightily to believe that he was making a true pilgrimage and not a whim-wham outing.\nAsked by $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 up in the real estate office on Titan, the outermost moon of Saturn, whether he believed in and would uphold the principles of the Ten Commandments, $SURNAME1 had bluffed and said he would.\nThe bluff had worked.\n$SURNAME1 had been sold a space-ship for the trifling sum of seventy-five dollars, along with a flight plan and fuel for a fifty-year cruise to nowhere. The course to be followed had been worked out by the geniuses at Gladstone\u2019s Museum of World Wonders in New York. They had said to send a ship to Venus, then Mars, then Earth, and so on.\nIf a stop was to be made along the way, Mars was as good as any.\n$SURNAME1, who had never had a religion in his life, was trying now by the seat of his trousers to make the religion true. He was trying desperately to discover a message in the vapors trailing behind the ship\u2014the vap...", "2_20_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-five in the evening</span>.\nThe Mississippi tune on the Pocket Sharknose radios carried the theme to its end, and the theme was lovely. The dark waters of the great river were in the theme, and the barge of a million lights, and the blue feather bed of midnight on which the barge rode. And the theme had not only the Mississippi in it, but Old Man River as well, for sang Old Man River as clearly as the orchestra:\nI got de river dat I own\u2014\nYou know dis old river go.\nThe orchestra was singing:\nAll among de Multitude went $SURNAME1 and his mate,\nAnd whosoever heard them could not keep from dancing.\n$SURNAME1 and his mate danced to the music, but their toes did not reach the ground.\n$SURNAME0 held $SURNAME1 horizontally at arm\u2019s length, and the two moved in slow motion, as though on casters, in several circles.\n\"It\u2019s a funny thing,\" said $SURNAME0, \"but I can tell a lot about a person by the way he dances. I asked $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 here tonight to show me the way he\u2019s lived his life on Earth. The old buffoon is doing it.\"", "2_5_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. Outside, the dawn was grey. I guess it got brighter in a while: but I can't be sure. There was a long period there when the drugs seemed to mix together, knotting and twisting in my gut\u2014my bowels felt like some kind of toxic petri dish hearing vibrations I could not. Weird laughing jags for no reason. Abrupt escalations Of fear and anger. Yes, fear. Great invisble masses of dead water had settled all around the hotel in monolithic strata. They seemed to press Into my flesh like grey lard. I couldn't move an inch. I was paralyzed.", "0_23_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, shivering in his space suit, went to the airlock and looked out across the iron soil of Mars to the boulder-strewn plain. He could see nothing against the red horizon, and nothing moving on the plain. At the edge of the Martian base lay a forest of antennae sprouting from the ground, and rising among the antennae were the towers of the living quarters. $FIRSTNAMEB0 peered into the shadows among the antennae, seeking to sense some enemy there, but he sensed nothing. The base might as well have been deserted.\n\"What did the message from Earth say?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1 at $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s elbow.\n\"It didn\u2019t say,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"What did you expect?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shrugged inside his space suit. \"Maybe instructions\u2014\" he said, thinking back to the schoolbook from which he had learned geography. \"Maybe a relief column,\" he added.\n\"Relief for what?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shook his head. He had been hoping he\u2019d never have to find out how bad things were on Mars. On Earth, he had been cheesed in a soldier suit, told nothing, expected to obey...", "1_4_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-one in the morning</span>. At three o\u2019clock, Metzger hung up on a truly hateful overseas call to Waynesberge, Pennsylvania. She and $FIRSTNAMEF2 met at a Mickey D\u2019s on the Sat\u2019llavona offramp, where Metzger had already taken two vacances among the counterpeople to avoid newspaper reporters, one costumed kid from Berkeley, who claimed to be from the CIA, and one Scotch girl who, aside from having big equipment, was also stark naked.\nAt the Fat albert\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEF2 told of her call from Dr Hilarius. Metzger rocked back and forth, holding an icy Coke so it sloshed against his cup and splashed on his chin. \u201cThat\u2019s it,\u201d he said, excited, \u201cthat\u2019s it, that\u2019s the formulation. I didn\u2019t know it before, but that\u2019s it.\u201d\n\u201cGrowth vs. Taxation,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2 said. \u201cYou ought to write a song about it.\u201d\n\u201cSong, hell.\u201d\n\u201cWibble, wobble.\u201d\n\u201c", "0_4_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nLenore bit her lip and waited. Heat lightning flickered far out in the cool black of the fields. The storm would be done, now. Whatever had been going to happen would have happened. She decided that she would go look in the east-wing bathroom again with the flashlight and that she would even kick the door in if she had to, and that if her grandmother was genuinely asleep there would be a noise, movement. One cat stood and stretched in the heat, in the light of the doorway, and quietly left.\nThe last flicker of lightning, white in the black sky, faded. Lenore Beadsman turned around from the sill and saw her brother and Lang in the doorway, filling the doorway. Lenore stared at them for a second, and then her jaw went slack and she began to cry.\nLang and Lenore\u2019s brother Neil carried Lenore back to the east wing. Presently they were all out of breath, and they stopped Lenore\u2019s wheelchair right in the middle of nowhere, a stretch of sterile grasslands in the deep southeast quadrant of M u m b l e Trace. Posited in the midst of the grasslands were two giant frozen turds.", "1_5_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span> of December thirty-first. The prophet $FIRSTNAMEM2 gazed fondly at Stony Stevenson, and his eyes were full of tears. \"Happy New Year, son\u2014\" said he, \"happy in Paradise\u2014and, son, you needn\u2019t wait till New Year\u2019s Day to find it.\"\nThe old man who was now talking to Stony Stevenson was named Herbert Beatty Crumb. He was fifty-six years old, and he lived in a barn loft three miles from town. He had just been to town to buy groceries. He was an inveterate store-shopper.\nHerbert, who had never known his father, had been an orphan at the age of four. He had come into the orphanage on account of his extreme filth. The Crumbs of his name had been pinched by Hodgkins\u2019 Disease. He had never been told it, but he knew that he had the same affinity for Hodgkins\u2019 Disease that his father and mother had shown. Not a week ever went by but that he thought he caught Hodgkins\u2019 Disease in his finger or his toe.\nDeeded to the state on account of their filth, the orphan Herbert and his sister (who also...", "0_10_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten eighteen in the morning</span>. Much was to happen, and in such a little time. And scarcely any of it is worth relating, since nothing that happened couldaddenng to Psychlo history.\nA worker at the mine simply made up his mind. He went to the lavatory, barred the door, turned on the gas, and blew his head off with a hand grenade. The garrison at Fort McHenry was ordered to report in firing order. An executive officer marched them to the parade ground and lined the troops up by fives, facing a flagpole. An artillery bugle sounded attention, and the troops came to present arms. At the signal of a cut to a flank, the soldiers turned and marched in groups of five to eight to eight eight-man firing squads. Officers stood with the firing squads and gave the commands to fire. The first group strangled their victim, the next group broke his neck with a thirty-inch rubber hose, the next two used blackjacks, and so on.\nThe abandoned equipment of the one hundred and thirty-six unfortunate soldiers was then set afire and the funeral pyre was drenched with gasoline by officers.\nAll commandeered civilian vehicles were then brought up to the waiting troops of the infantry garrison. As fast as soldiers...", "0_8_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past eight o'clock in the morning</span> when we dismissed the taxi and went into Braddock's Fish Market. It was dark inside, and the long counter was crowded with men and women buying fresh fish for the weekend, and I gagged on the odor -- a powerful, fishy, salty smell that I remembered from wharf-rats' slums in the warm mornings of the Gulf ports. It was there the day I left for France in 1918 to enlist. It was the smell of America. It was the smell of home.\nI went down the line and heard the people talking; then $FIRSTNAMEB3 stood by the cashier paying for the stuff and I took two large packages of provisions to our designated golf-cart and stowed them away. It was done. We started downtown.\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB3, look!\u201d I said. \u201cIt's Saturday, kids are going to the movies, here they come.\u201d I watched them eddy through the cross-traffic. Out of some doorways they came, lurking around to steal dimes from parked cars, calling to each other, giggling, and suddenly moving toward the lights. Little boys, some in flannels, some in jeans, somberfaced, tentative; little girls, harnessed like midgets, rushing along...", "0_21_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty in the evening</span>, before we left the concert hall, when $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I decided to call our San Francisco relatives to announce that we were coming back. I called Ray McComas first. Jean had had a phone installed in their house, Hyedson Street, and Ray was to come there and pick us up at the bus depot with \u201cconvertible amenities.\u201d I called them, standing with $FIRSTNAMEB3 in the concert hall lobby, at a long idle booth by the wall. I didn't know he had relatives here. A girl picked up the phone. \u201cWho?\u201d\n\u201cRay?\u201d\n\u201cRay bought a new car, $FIRSTNAMEB6, a Z- think Olds.\u201d\n\u201cIs he there?\u201d\n\u201cNo, $FIRSTNAMEB3?\u201d\n\u201cNo, no -- he's my cousin.\u201d\n\u201cSallie is.\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cRay bought the car. It's a nice car.\u201d\n\u201cWhen will he be there? I'll call back. Sorry to bother you.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 was biting his nails, grinning like mad, and rocking. \u201cSee, $FIRSTNAMEB6, they got a new car, man...", "2_8_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-six in the morning</span>. That meant it was 1046 in the morning, Eastern War Time, on the planet Earth.\nThere was whispering in the alley. The alley was beneath a window of the barracks. The Martians of the garrison were being held in their rooms by the commanding sergeant\u2014until the time came to climb aboard the rocket ship and go through the tunnel to Earth. Old Scarsdale had wholeheartedly approved the plan for one big explosion above the caves. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had sold the plan to the old man.\nFour minutes to go. The whispering had grown very loud. The whispering was an argument between two men.\nOne man was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe other was a friend of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s named $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was whispering that it was madness to risk their necks in the tunnels on the day of the assault on Earth. $FIRSTNAMEB1 had argued this way for ten minutes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, like $FIRSTNAMEB0 and every other well-adjusted veteran in the Army, was a Parachute Rifleman. The Parachute Riflemen were the aristocrats of the Army. Their badge of aristocracy was a wingshield of real gold in a$ real leather case.", "1_1_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-nine in the morning</span> when I arrived in Las Vegas. One of the things that bothered me as I drove up Had tuck was looking at all those closed places\u2014gam bling places\u2014where some very wealthy people had plunked down a lot of money. Some of these places were closed!! Why on earth had they gone out of business? And that gave me the clue for this article on Las Vegas.\n\u25a0 All these casinos in Las Vegas that have gone out of business in the past two years have gone out of business for only one reason, and that reason is:\nThere was too much gambling going on.\nThat\u2019s it.\nThat\u2019s what killed them.\nThey were not rigged. They were not dishonest. They were not under-capitalized.\nThey were killed by overly enthusiastic gamblers.\nIf a Las Vegas casino feared it was getting too much business, that casino would close itself down at once.\nNo myth is stronger or more pervasive on Las Vegas than the myth that a casino can make more money if it is kept slightly short of money. When a gambler starts feeling lucky, he is apt to bet more money than the management feels is good for the house. This can lead to a disaster of empty out\u2014of...", "2_21_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine o'clock in the evening</span>. A group of children was shouting the name of a cereal in the stands.\nThe shouting of the children was automatic.\nIt was the final play of the last quarter of the last game of the year.\nThe year was 1986, the month September. The time was nine o'clock in the evening.\nIt was impossible to tell, by looking into any one face, who had won the game, though a majority of the faces suggested surrender by their expressions. Some faces were grim. Some faces were sardonic. Some faces were merely blank. One face wore the smile of heartsickness, a smile portraying love for the Home team and faith in it that had not yet been obliterated by what it was that was making the face seem so pale.", "0_1_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>. Old Bull, who had been lying semi-coiled on the rear floor under a tarpaulin, came out from underneath and crawled over Stanley's feet to the driver's seat. $FIRSTNAMEB3 let him take the wheel. Bull put his head on the wheel. He was asleep at once ,and the car drifted through crossroads and approached the first lonely bluff outside of Yonkers. \u201cLet him sleep,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cHe's not going to lose his commutation-ticket and he can drive right up to the gate of the dry sand and still have time to get to work.\u201d I learned to my amazement that Bull was a prison trusty and had a special series of funny jobs that allowed him in and out of the prison proper. A fellow in overgrown overalls went out to talk to him. $FIRSTNAMEB3 indicated he was an underboss of Bull's; he was getting in some solid travel time between himself and his house without waking his wife -- their only car was out of order. We drove right through the village of Sing-Sing. It was a dismal little place with shacks and shanties, a few trees, and out beyond the village the grim stone walls of Sing-Sing prison. We started to...", "2_23_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven seventeen in the evening</span>.\nOnly a minute and forty-seven seconds had elapsed since the death of $SURNAME1's wife. And yet, in that bewildering and frantic time, millions somehow grasped the implication of the event, and knew instinctively that it marked the passing of a era, that something transcendent and epochal had occurred\u2014something as meaningful and terrible as the fall of Rome.\nAnd if the death of $SURNAME1's wife was recognized as being volcanic and epochal, that understanding had much to do with the medley of half-true and wholly false news items and rumors that flashed through the National Broadcasting Company's Defense assignment desk in the next seventeen seconds. By the time eighteen seconds had passed, millions knew, for example, that all Americans were about to be drafted for the war in Vietnam, and were marching right now, in a gigantic and secret mobilization, along the west coast, that they would be flown to a strategic point in South Vietnam, at midnight. Some reports had the mobilization zone as San Diego and the strategic point as the bare top of Iwo Jima, others as San Diego and Da Nang, others as Seattle and the Iron Triangle.", "1_8_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-two in the morning</span> when I walked into the big public square in front of the cathedral. Across the square I saw the bar I had been in with $FIRSTNAMEB5 and Bill and Mike. I went over and sat down. It was as empty as it had been the night before. The waiter finished making some coffee at the big urn and brought me a steaming cup.\n\u201cGood morning, sir,\u201d he smiled.\n\u201cDo you have a paper?\u201d\n\u201cIt will be out in a few minutes.\u201d\nI drank my coffee. A conservative, I would have called him. Perhaps dispassionate would be the better word to describe him. The dispassionate conservative. His problem was to save Catalonia from France, but also to prevent it from becoming provincial. To give them a sense of nationality. But a great Spaniard also. A man who felt that too much study in the Germanic countries would make them tone deaf when it came to patriotism. It was a breakfast to sustain the most austere of partisans. It did not touch my hunger. The coffee made the cup cool to the touch. But it had probably been hot when it first touched the waiter\u2019s lips, and now it was cool when it touched mine.", "2_19_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-three in the evening</span>.\nNever once in all his lives had $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 heard or felt a series of impressions so complex or so harmonious or rich. He felt as a king might feel who, with the crown of England on his head, discovered that a long row of duchesses, countesses, mothers, and virgin daughters. loved him devotedly and were willing to make almost any sacrifice to gain his favor.\nAs the crowd stood before him, $SURNAME1 became aware that every soul in the crowd had somehow been touched by $SURNAME0, that each in his or her own way liked $SURNAME0 as much as $SURNAME1 did.", "0_20_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-three in the evening</span> at the Prins Ferdinand Zwartkop airport tarmac when the attach\u00e9\u2019s wife came out of the plane, was met by an antihijack board of inquiry, in an uproar of lights and sirens. Rumors and shouts, crazy sounds of official noises filled the air.\nThe attention of the Spaniard, who was wearing dark glasses, and who was objectively observing all that was going on around him, was glued in an odd and lovely way to a certain gray squirrel, who had been on and off among the amazingly lifelike diorama scenery of the airport set, now scolding a sprite, now worrying the toe of an Attach\u00e9 Man. The squirrel would have been very unimportant, in observational terms, if it had not been for his ears. The Spaniard had never imagined that there could be so much uncanniness, solid and yet abstract, in squirrel-sized rodent ears. They loomed and grew through all possible perceptual scales, like rising dough. They were insistent, fecund.\nThe ears of the squirrel forced an issue in the conscious mind of the Spaniard. The issue amounted to whether the strange and detached feeling he was experiencing, as he had been feeling ever since he\u2019d...", "2_2_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-seven in the morning</span>. She fell asleep holding the pillow, with her coat thrown over the bed\u2019s back, thinking that it had not been a hundred minutes yet, that John Nefastis might still call, with further revelations, a further gentle tracing out of intimate touching shapes. She had had no ideas about this before. But now that the idea had occurred to her (as, later, in airline terminals, she would remember with a certain wistfulness the hush, the waiting for a call that never came, marked off by intermittent announcements that no longer seemed darkly exultant as once they had), she waited for the idea of Monopoly to also come. But that, for the time being, was all that was on her mind, standing there at the edge of sleep and all its possibilities, so new that she had not yet named it to herself.\nChapter 2\nII", "2_11_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-two in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\nThe place was Kachmore Village, Tharsis.\nA terrible wail echoed off the walls of the caves. Nothing had been so mournful since the howler monkeys died out.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 came hopping down the strange spiral staircase like a couple of trampoline artists.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were persisting in their efforts to get off the planet.\n\"Thank you all so much for coming, Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF0,\" said $SURNAME0 telepathically. He was addressing Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEF0 Win Turkel, who was emerging from the crowd at the bottom of the stairs, on her way to rescue her daughter back in the Museum of Earthly Wonders.\nThe Museum door banged for the eighteenth time in thirty seconds.\nA skeleton was trying to get out. A basketball team was trying to keep it from getting out. They slapped at the skeleton, trying to keep its ball-against-the-wall game going. Their game was an empty game, a tragic game, for there was no ball on Mars that could be bounced against a wall by the skeleton.", "1_6_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past six o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1949. All in all, we counted fifteen of us who were involved in the first hovercraft expedition to Saigon-Gia Dinh. It was the time when the majority of our Saigonese friends would be sleeping off New Year's Eve in peace and quiet -if they could sleep with all the racket outside.\nMyself, my attorney, and four young gentlemen assistants went with the first batch of hovercraft fuel. We had three grenades for protection in case anybody objected to our siphoning out their base gas. We took along a set of heavy jumper cables in case anything electronically went haywire. We also had three pistols, just to make it look good.\nWe rode in a battered old Dodge that we picked up the night before. It had one headlight and a bad engine, but it ran, and that's all that mattered. A hundred yards behind us, two Saigonese squeezed into a Honda motorcycle taxi for the wild\u2014goose chase through the pouring rain we were about to pull. I recognized them as our minders, assigned to make certain we didn't drive into any sensitive military areas. I was also certain they wouldn't know what the fuck we were up to.\nThe Dodge arrived under the...", "0_9_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The day was heating up. I hung my jacket on the handle of the shopping bag and began unpacking the groceries. Loaded with paper bags, my wife wandered through the house, exploring. She went into Remi's room and stayed there. I called to her, \u201cWhere are you?\u201d\nRemi answered, \u201cI'm down here,\u201d and soon she came out of the bedroom, naked and waving her wings.\n\u201cWatch out, I just finished painting them, they mustn't get dirty. Are there any flies?\u201d\n\u201cNo, we just got here.\u201d\n\u201cWhere?\u201d\n\u201cSan Francisco.\u201d I had taken a handful of groceries into the living room. Existing so long in that sad pad had given it a sad air. It was as empty as ever, the same bad bong, the same bad couch, a few pictures of William Mishik, showing year by year how he wasted away; the room had the moldy feeling of too much steamy heat and not enough windows. I put the groceries down on the plank that passed for a coffee table; then I went for something to eat. I couldn't help seeing the strange contrast...", "1_14_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the first day of the year of our era nineteen hundred and thirty-eight, and the place was Hartford, Connecticut, Earth, Solar System, Milky Way, Universe. Between forty-five and fifty billion years before, according to the best estimates of prevailing scientific theory, there had been nothing but solid, rocklike matter without levity or velocity. At some time in the incomprehensible obscurity of successive eons, hydrogen had come violently together and begun to swirl. Along with it had swirled the nuclei of the most elementary of all the common chemical elements, the easily ionizable and therefore most abundant of them all: lithium. By the time the swirlings had continued till they had given birth to the Sun and its retinue, which now included eight planets, the uneventful, placid, and gigantic orbits of most of the lithium nuclei had become the planets themselves.\n\" `.... we therefore intend to commit the lewd and lascivious act comprising sexual cohabition with the aforementioned __________, in an appropriate but as yet undetermined private location, on an initial but as yet undetermined date to be fixed by us but not later than January 1, 1939, being ... \u2019 \u201d\nLenore Beadsman stopped...", "0_22_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-one in the evening</span>. \u201cWe must go to the Mint,\u201d I said. \u201cWe can call a cab from there.\"\n\u201cOr O'Reilly,\u201d he said. \u201cI think my car is still where I left it, in the lot across the street.\u201d We picked up his bag and hurried out through the rain. A cab was just pulling away from the kerb in front of the hotel. We could see the mocking face of the driver through the misted window.\n\u201cFool,\u201d said my attorney. \u201cWe could have made it with that one. Wait, I'll get another.\" He hurried over to the Cal-Mex place, but when he came out with the waitress he shook his head. \u201cGone already,\" he said. \u201cI'll try the Can-Do.\" He waved me ahead, and I jumped in the Nash and started the engine.\nThe water was coming down harder. The street was empty and it seemed like a long time before my attorney came running out of the restaurant and down the sidewalk with a face full of urgency\u2014splashing water in the street, just as before.\n\u201cWhat the hell is wrong with these people?\" he cried.", "1_12_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-three p.m.</span> Luna was at perigee again. It lay level and unromantic on the horizon. Young Redwine, rummaging in the broom closet under the stairs, found nothing to illuminate his search for a Znu built of bottle caps in honor of his brother\u2019s triumph.\nA search for a gang-gang of porcupines, which might have served as a symbol of Love Triumphing over Hate though since Love had succumbed in its struggle with Hate, according to $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s brother, and had sent out for pizza, the search for a gang-gang of porcupines, in the basement of the Redwine house, proved similarly unrewarding.\nWhen young Redwine, sniffling, retired from the broom closet and from the basement, he found that the kitchen had become a theater of love. $FIRSTNAMEB0, in a skimpy towel, was hiding behind the door and rousting visitors.When he spied young Redwine, he came out with his drawn gun and boomed and shouted, \"Who goes there?\"\nThe visitors giggled.\n\"What\u2019s all this? What\u2019s all this?\" said young Redwine reproachfully. \"All this?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, mimicking...", "2_18_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nAt seven o\u2019clock, Earthling Time, a car appeared on the straight stretch of road to the west of the house in the sand. It was not visible from the house. The road came over a rise in the land. The headlights of the car illuminated only the top of the rise.\nThe car was a big Buick with New York license plates.\nThe car seemed tolurch out of control on the top of the rise. It seemed about to flip over onto its back. It was swerving wildly from one side of the road to the other.\nBut then it straightened. It came down the rise, nosing into the sand at the bottom. It stopped at the house in the sand.\nThe engine was left running. The headlights were left on.\nA man got out of the car. He walked to the cabin.\nHe used a key to open the door. He stepped inside.\nWhen he came out, he was carrying an aluminum attach\u00e9 case that the woman had bought. His free hand held the woman\u2019s white Pekinese by the loose skin of its neck. The dog was baying piteously.", "2_12_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, post meridiem. The wooden goose in Old Stone Whitemarsh's mouth began to be soaked in saliva again.\nThe time was seven minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon, post meridiem.\n\"What are all you pretty people dressed as?\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh to the masqueraders. \"What are those animal things on your masks?\"\nThe silence was very thick.\n\"All you pretty people?\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh. \"Can\u2019t talk with these reeds in my mouth. What are those animal things on your masks?\"\nThe silence was firm and white.\n\"What are all you pretty people dressed as?\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh. \"Come on and show an old man.\"\nNothing happened.\n\"The town\u2019s being turned into a zoo for charity, really,\" said an eighteen-year-old in a leopard mask.\nNot a word was said by any of the other masqueraders.\n\"Oh,\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh.\n\"We\u2019re all different kinds of animals,\" said the leopard\u2019s owner.\nShe shifted from one foot to the other.", "1_13_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, according to Buskins.\nThey had circled the field to watch the take-off. Now they went to the three o'clock position\u2014rubbing old people in the crowd having seen far too many take-offs and landings for their health.\nThe ground personnel were taking the space ship in through the open iron doors of the Shed of Space Ship Cain.\nFor another three minutes, the field-circuit loudspeakers said nothing.\nThen Eliot Rosewater\u2019s voice returned, quiet but clear. He was apparently in Room 223 of the Brown Derby Restaurant on Wilshire Boulevard in Los Angeles, California. It was one minute to three in the afternoon.\nHe said, \"This is a message to any surviving members of the crew of Space Ship Earth, which is rigged for demolition, which has been hijacked by a criminal gang, is losing air fast, and will shortly crash land on or near Venus. The probable landing point is on Lucifer superior, in Core Fac of\u2014\"\nThe rumble in the loudspeakers made it impossible to hear the rest.\nSeveral loudspeakers died raucously. Only one showed a voice asking a question nobody could hear. The crowd listened respectfully.\nThe voice asked, \"What\u2019s our status...", "0_9_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-two in the morning</span> and I was in my bungalow at the Beverly Hills Hotel, talking on the telephone to a local number in Bay City, Michigan. The conversation was not consecutive, but mercifully interrupted by hang-ups, and I was fairly coherent when I demanded my baggage check. The only valet in sight was a smirking Filipino in a red jacket. \u201cJesus, you\u2019ll never catch him,\u201d he giggled, lighting a small, foul-smelling cigar that exposed a gold tooth. \u201cI pushed his scooter inside, but he can really move, you know? I think he made the turn at the light on Star Song, heading north. But he\u2019s gone.\u201d I told him I doubted he would ever see his precious scooter again in this life. \u201cNo, man!\u201d he wailed. \u201cAre you sure?\u201d I tossed a $100 bill on the counter. \u201cNo scooter, no fee.\u201d He grabbed for the greenback as I hefted my gear. \u201cMuch obliged...really...anything I can do...often thought of leaving this place..head north myself.\" I ran to my car, afraid he would change his mind.", "2_1_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe crowd outside the mansion\u2019s ironingsheet portal faltered, wavered, broke. And something that had formed within the crowd wavered, faltered, and broke. The crowd would recover, would re-form, would go on to a full materialization presently.\nBut the something that had broken off from the crowd was a very limp, very shaky lamppost. A dark disk, about the size of a saucer, appeared over the disk\u2019s top, flattened out, became the distorted head of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe head wobbled, drew itself up to its full height. The head smiled, collapsed, materialized the remainder of $SURNAME0\u2019s shivering, dripping, white-trousered self.\n$SURNAME0 had been a sickly child, troubled by many mysterious illnesses. His health had never been strong. He was one of those who, as the years go by, manage to be both disgustingly healthy and dismally ill.\nAnd now his poor heart, long threatened by ten thousand maladies, now faltered, stopped beating.", "1_6_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-two in the morning</span>, if $FIRSTNAMEB1 was any judge of sun alignments and atmospheric diffusion gasses. Other than that, he had no idea what it was. His chronometer had cracked when he hit the sidewalk after the explosion, and the faint noises in his ears weren\u2019t entirely accounted for by the damned pain in his wrists and ankles. He remembered having heard sounds ever since he regained consciousness. But what the sounds were and what might have caused them he had no idea.\nThe sounds were very loud in his ears.\nHe heard being pounded on.\nHe heard something heavy being dragged.\nHe heard something dropped again and again with resounding stanch.\nHe heard something fed into a shredder.\nHe heard shredding, punctuated by the clank of what was obviously hollow tubes tapping together.\nHe heard the tubes picked up individually and banged together again. He assumed this was to dislodge whatever objects might have lodged in them during the shredding process.\nAnd he heard a hiss that harmonized perfectly with the throbbing of the pain in his wrists and ankles.\nThe real town of Newport, Rhode Island, was five miles away. It was a relatively new town that had mushroomed up after World War II, when the Navy...", "2_9_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe man who strung $FIRSTNAMEB0 up strung up the commander of the little ship, too.\nThe whistle flew in a wild, rising and falling melody, put the ship on full automatic, went out through the hatch like quicksilver, turned, ran through the sand like quicksilver, came up to the little knots of men who cried at him, was driven back by their electronic shields.\nHe whistled up a grasshopper-like device that flew him around the ship in a buzzing orbit.\nThe regularity of the beats of the snare drums was broken by a ragged tattoo of fives. Finally, the rhythm section in the old band-leader barracks took it up. Fives were beating all over the big encampment.\nA sentry by the big gate saw a shadow in the sand on the near side of the gate.\nThe sentry reached for his gun.\nThe shadow in the sand took form, took more shadow for a head, took a face and shoulders, a grin and a pair of hands.\nThe sentry blushed, put his gun away. \"Hello there, $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" he said. \"Who\u2019re you supposed to be today, anyway?\"", "1_2_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-seven in the morning</span>. The streets were dark and the Buick Electra was going from I-5, heading north on California 99. Above the radio console of the car was a sign in semi-electronic fl ash: IF YOU THINK YOU\u2019RE GOING TO DIE, YOU PROBABLY WILL.\nThe driver was apparently unconcerned by the possibility. He was a thin, long-nosed young man with a fine brown shock of hair that hung as far as his shoulders. He wore dark glasses, a beanie, and a T\u2014shirt decorated with a tattoo of an eagle. His left shoulder bore a 3\u2014D \u201cVote $SURNAME0\u201d sticker.\nHe laughed up at the sky now. \"And do you know what the funniest part of it was, Salo?\" he said. \"The funniest part of it was that we\u2019d already had the funeral. We\u2019d already buried the body.\"\nHe switched on the windshield-wiper blades. They roared, clattering on the glass. Then they went still again.\n\"You know what they say in these parts about a man who clatters his wiper blades,\" said the driver.\n\"No,\" said Salo.", "1_1_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>, Eastern Daylight Savings Time, when the Rolling Stones' limousine pulled away from the curb and disappeared into the faint golden haze of the Manhattan night.\nThe general disposition was that we were all flying off to a gig in Boston harldly aware that our course and our fate were already sealed... because it is all explicable, ladies & gentlemen\u2014you've come this far, now prepare to penetrate to the heart of the Mystery.\nSeven hours later I was at the wheel of said limousine, driving out of Cambridge, Mass., with the dawn coming up red behind the pillared front and wroughtiron gates of Harvard Yard. The gig at the Boston Tea Party was over and the Rolling Stones had played it.\nAnd now we were heading for New York...and a gig that would put our Boston gig in the Saran Wrap pickup category. Yes, it would be The Big One, because we knew a gig was waiting for us in New York that evening with Elektra Records, owned and operated by Leo the Lionhearted Rothschild, who was spending several hundred thousand dollars to entertain the Rolling Stones for a week in his apartment on Park Avenue.\nWe all knew this, too. Except the Parson.\nHowedever...he could sleep...", "0_22_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> of the twenty-fourth of December. She realized that in a minute or two she would have no more time. How much she wished they might stop at this moment and go away happy, never knowing what she would think tomorrow. She rose to her feet. They all did the same. Drawing her shawl close, she was a little afraid even to look at Lily Briscoe, whose eyes, she noticed, had a way of meeting one suddenly which was disturbing, unnatural in a child. They would all be going soon, some to bed, some to sit up late. She should be alone again. But had she done all she could? That question, for some reason, annoyed her, annoyed her awfully. It stopped all her good feelings in this way, as if it disturbed the grace, the harmony of some evening piece she had been playing slowly, to herself. Like an exasperating pedant, some stupid sonneteer insisted on asking, \"But have you done your best-have you done your best?\" When she could not answer, the questioner added, taunting her, \"Perhaps your little burnished daughter\" (he meant Fairy) \"will do what you left undone.\" Oh, was that it? she wished to ask, is...", "2_6_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThe power plant had long since been shut off. There was no sound in Room 223 of the $SURNAME0 Inn but the rhythmic sawing of the angel\u2019s wings.\nAn air conditioner in a high, far window was humming and chuckling as it poured refrigerated air down onto the wing-sawing angel.\nSpaceflight, thought the angel, is certainly a strange way to promote universal brotherhood and international peace.\nThis message from $FIRSTNAMEF0 was the first communication from any friend or member of his species that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had received since his preliminary lecture on economics at Princeton.\nFor the first time since his plummet to earth, $SURNAME1 was in a position to appraise the usefulness of the philosophy of Unconditional Hatred by means of which he had lived so strongly and so well.\nThe appraisal was not favorable.\nThe philosophy of Unconditional Hatred had become so much ghost-in-the-machine cackling, cackling, cackling.", "0_2_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-eight in the morning</span>. I was hungry, and my stomach was trying to digest some fried egg sandwiches that were not going too well. I\u2019d chosen Earl\u2019s pretty arbitrarily. He was a friend my attorney had, and we knew he was out of town. The strategic points around the Bay Area were covered: secret hideouts and safe houses in Mill Valley, Novato, Oroville and Carmel. I was a fugitive from the Reno cavalry, but the important thing now was to lay up somewhere and get up steam. At first, naturally, I\u2019d thought of asking my attorney for counsel, for his advice in a time of trouble; but at this point this seemed not only inappropriate but impossiblemy attorney was much too far out. Six years ago, when we\u2019d met, he had just finished an eight\u2014year alcoholic bout\u2014trying unsuccessfully to make it into the twentieth century\u2014and although he no longer drank he had been left badly damaged and disoriented. The long and the short of it was that my attorney had become a mental case. Sometimes he could fool himself with a certain amount of success, but not when it came to him that his delinquent attorney was going to wind up in a federal prison. Anyhow, here he was:...", "0_21_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> when the limousine drew up in front of the Ritz and the chauffeur came around and opened the door. Lanier, shivering and sneezing, stepped out shaking mud from his shoes.\n\"Never again!\u2014\" he said to his sister sitting in the limousine. \"Never to Florida in the spring!\"\n\"Good-by,\" said his sister coldly. \"I hope you have a pleasant life.\"\n\"I\u2019ll send you a cable from Paris,\" said Lanier. \"Come over and see the new cathedral.\"\n\"Don\u2019t bother,\" said his sister, looking away.\nLanier watched the limousine drive away. He took out four half-franc pieces and bought a paper to see what the German Army was doing. Then he walked into the hotel and up to his room.\nCHAPTER\nAND so once more it was Paris, the Paris of the arts, the Paris of the 1920\u2019s\u2014but it was not always so. There had been another Paris once, the Paris of Madame de Pompadour, the Paris of candles and of the Sun King and of Voltaire, but between the candles and the Sun King and Voltaire and the...", "0_7_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-nine in the morning</span> when I arrived in Las Vegas. I parked my car at the Silver Bell Bar and went down to the main casino to look for a job.\nThey were mixing the cement for the gambling tables at thirty thousand dollars the table and I knew one thing for certain: there was no way I could make that kind of money. What I hadn\u2019t calculated on was the probability of staking a table before the cement had finished setting...be it at thirty grand or a flat million. What I should have arranged for was a dutch system\u2014that\u2019s a poker table where anybody can buy into the game by putting their own money on the table with mine.\nA dutch system would not only have solved the problem of getting the money for the table; it would have made other gamblers happy by giving them a chance to join a big pot without the trouble of finding a big ante. Alas, I was not thinking. My mind was totally focused on the job I had just landed: I was a floorman.\nLas Vegas is a very garish place. Most people assume I would hate it . . . and, of course, they are absolutely right. I do hate it. But the Blue Mill Restaurant at the Dunes is worth seeing,...", "0_19_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was somewhat recovered, but far from well. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was still the possessor of a single mind.\nIn $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind was only one thought, and it took all the uncompromising power of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s one mind to keep the thought in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind. The thought was: Don\u2019t kill $FIRSTNAMEB1. For $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew by now that killing $FIRSTNAMEB1 would be much worse than killing anybody else.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt that he would go mad if he should force his whole mind free of the discipline that restrained it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 bleated when $FIRSTNAMEB1 spoke to him harshly. $FIRSTNAMEB0 emptied his mind then, had the semblance of a human being who doesn\u2019t know what\u2019s going to happen next. $FIRSTNAMEB1 relished the spectacle of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s empty mind.\n\"Mama, mama, mama\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1 with scorn, \"I never seen such a big boy like you, $FIRSTNAMEB0, that can act like such a big baby like you do.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 wanted to hit $FIRSTNAMEB0 again, but the cleaning squad rolled up then in a truck.\nThere were eleven people on the cleaning squad: four men, six women...", "2_0_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF2 turned her head, beaming. \"Honey, I thought you were never coming back.\"\n* * *\nPart Two Inverarity Will Sue You\nIt is not easy, talking about what happened then, how Mucharco did or did not misread her, how much of it she even now remembers. Some parts he did or didn\u2019t, apparently, say weren\u2019t clear to him. Maybe he figured it for an illusion caused by the interplay of headlights and taillights off wet pavement and into her eyes. Maybe he figured that her gauge had been screwed up somehow, though the gauges at the time were all linear, operated with a thumb-switch and weren\u2019t much subject to tomfoolery. Maybe he forgot. More likely he wanted to forget.", "2_12_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty post meridiem</span> in the Martian caverns below the spaceship.\n\"This is where we live,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 out loud. \"This is where we live,\" he said. It was comforting to think so. To think so was to admire so. He admired the inept geniuses who had built the place. He felt like crying. $FIRSTNAMEB0 invented an intricate system of non-Aristotelian logic to prove that he was the luckiest man in the world.\n\"This is where we live,\" he whispered.\nConvinced at last of his luck, $FIRSTNAMEB0 mounted a stone stairway to a flat circular landing on top of the cone that formed the foot of a suit of space armor.\nOn the landing was a telephone.\nThe telephone was connected to a receiver by a flexible shaft as yellow and brittle as old cheese. The receiver was imbedded in the suit of armor inside. Through an open visor, the face of a dead soldier looked at $FIRSTNAMEB0. The soldier looked as comfortable as anyone who had ever died could look. $FIRSTNAMEB0 could imagine no way of disturbing the soldier. He could imagine no choice between the unanswering suit of armor and the installed interior of it.", "1_22_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-three in the evening</span> when she finally buttoned herself into a blouse and slacks for the first time in five days. Her clothes were wrinkled, and the damp of her body had stained the cloth under her arms and in the crotch; her shoes were crusty with dirt and rain water. The room was cold. There was a hissing in her ears. She had ten dollars of her former hundred left.\nShe went to the desk posted NO GUESTS AND NO DERELICTS and told the girl she wanted a room for the night, with a bath. Not hesitating over the odd expression. The girl looked at her with a practiced thousand-mile stare and said, \u201cOne dollar fifty,\u201d and $FIRSTNAMEF2 handed her two fives and a ten, assuming the girl would bring back the change and the key. But she didn\u2019t. \u201cYaw mus\u2019 be in the book,\u201d she said, expressionless. Thinking It\u2019s a jive pad, she\u2019s in the book under another name. Or perhaps it\u2019s a den of thieves: mute innkeeper, corruptible children. \u201cWelcome to you,\u201d another clerk, standing behind the first, said,...", "1_12_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-three p.m.</span> We really never felt like going home. There had been at least six or seven incredibly beautiful dune buggies on the highway, all loaded with laughing, healthy kids carrying flowers, binoculars and music. We knew they would all head straight for the Mint and be there waiting when we finally got around to leaving...and we knew we'd be racing so fast that we could never catch up with anybody, which was fine. We'd just as soon pass em all out at the edge of town. So we checked out of the Silver Slipper at one o'clock and rumbled over to the Mint Hotel, which is just down the road, and we warned the clerk to expect us back sometime that afternoon.\nBut we never went there. What we did was drive off to the north and climb back into the dunes again. $FIRSTNAMEB3 roared ahead, and about five miles out in the desert we came to where the sand had been freshly laid down by recent storms. A great, soft plain with mountain-high dunes twenty to fifty feet high was in the making; just astronomical humps of pure symmetrical sand that reminded you of ribbed gravel . . . like a desert Delft. And these mounds still had water-smoothed troughs and...", "1_14_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the manager of the San Narciso branch of the Bank of America first noted the presence of an unaccustomed Customer. By two twenty-seven this customer\u2014a lean, somewhat scholarly-looking white male\u2014was already making himself felt as an uneasy seismic tension in the sector of the branch consisting of tellers\u2019 windows. At two thirty-four the San Narciso police were summoned by the branch manager when it became obvious that the ejection of this particular unaccustomed Customer was going to require professional intervention. An officer of the law arrived at two forty, dispatched by radio from the near\u2014by city of Colma. His name, as discovered on a gold badge encrusted with something alien\u2014a seal of office, actually\u2014was Ray Merrill. At four minutes after two forty, Ray Merrill took his place in the line of those seeking service at one of the tellers\u2019 windows. His place put him eighth in line, behind six persons with either simple transactions to be effected, or balance inquiries. At seven minutes after two forty, the branch manager noticed that the line had lengthened and now extended outside the front door of his branch office. Again the presence of an unaccustomed Customer had been noted. One of the staffers was dispatched...", "1_14_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span> on December 31, 1958, when Sergent $FIRSTNAMEB1 awakes.\nA week after the burning of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Malco ta\u2019lor on Mars, Sergeant $FIRSTNAMEB1 had shipped back to Earth with a broken toy, ninety-eight dollars, thirty-one cents, and the absolute, unqualified, unswayable support of an army big enough to smash any opposition.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 now has two years to find that opposition.\nJust before spring maneuvers begin again, $FIRSTNAMEB1 will learn who his opposition is. When he does, he will lay out the best plans ever made. But the plans will have one flaw. This flaw is in Sergeant $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s character, not in the plans.\nThe plans will be so good that they may make his former commander weep with pride. Whether they take $FIRSTNAMEB1 to a pale pink stucco house in the suburbs of Phoenix, Arizona, or send him to prison for life may depend on whether he can find in his heart an honorable way to cheat on his wife for the sake of the plans.\nThe plans will be so simple that a child could understand them and apply them. The plans will be so brilliant that no one in the Army will be able to oppose them.\nThere is Bo...", "1_16_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The flamingo happened to be facing the window, and from its beak came the clearest, prettiest, and most musical of bird whistles.\n\"Cuckoo!\" it said.\n\"You might not be so much to look at,\" said Moncrief to his bird, \"but you can sure count up to twelve, and that\u2019s more than some people can do.\"\nThe telephone rang.\nMoncrief let it ring twice before he picked it up. He let it ring again, impatiently, before he spoke. \"What d\u2019ya want?\" he said.\n\"Is this Space Layout?\" said $SURNAME1\u2019s well-bred, controlled, cultured voice.\n\"Right,\" said Moncrief. \"Sent you a picture of a man in space, didn\u2019t they?\"\n\"Who is this?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Robert H. Moncrief, president,\" said Moncrief.\n\"Oh,\" said $SURNAME1. \"How do you do, Mr. Moncrief?\"\n\"Pleased to meet you,\" said Moncrief, \"although under the circumstances, under the very pointed circumstances, I could be a lot more pleased than I am. Now, what...", "2_4_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-nine in the morning</span>.\nChief got up, unrolled a huge white towel, and took a little blue tablespoon out of his pocket.\n\"You go to sleep, daddy,\" he said to $FIRSTNAMEB3. \"I\u2019m going to clean this man\u2019s blood off my hands.\"\nThe chief began washing up in a big stone sink under the leaves of a tree in the backyard.\n\"Who did this?\" he said. \"Who done this, doctor?\"\n\"Well,\" said Doc, \"I don\u2019t know anything about it officially, but among us boys in New York Belentz said an auto-extermination Detroit cabal did it. They want his manuscript, chief.\"\n\"Aha,\" said the chief.\n\"Well, now, are you a Christian, daddy?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB3, turning to me. \"Are you saved?\"\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" I said.\n\"He\u2019s Jewish, $FIRSTNAMEB6,\" said my attorney. \"Jews don\u2019t know they\u2019re saved until they\u2019re seventy-two.\" He held up seven fingers to me.", "2_11_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-seven in the morning</span>.\nSomewhere in Indianapolis, a clock struck twelve.\nA solid day at the Indianapolis Museum of Arts. Admission waived for children accompanied by adults. Artist-in-residence: R. Leonard, San Francisco. One gigantesque and terrifying painting: Attack! The painting hung on the museum wall labeled Sculpture, some of the members of the visiting Detroit Art Institute having decided that it was too big to ever steal.\nThe painting was, in reproduction, on the cover of this book.", "0_21_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>. Nine o'clock in the evening of December 24, 1961.\n\"I hurry, I hurry to leave you now,\" lamented $SURNAME1, \"before meanness and madness get ahold of me. You stay away from me, Salo, or I\u2019ll certainly kill somebody.\"\n\"You\u2019re not a murderer, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. He put his hand on his friend\u2019s heart. \"You couldn\u2019t ever kill anybody, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014not even yourself.\"\n\"I\u2019ll get mean and cold, and dirty,\" said $SURNAME1, \"and like all the rest of the lousy grownups. I\u2019ll be mean and cold, and dirty, and god-awful\u2014\"\nHe shook his head. What words could he possibly use to tell anybody what he thought a grown-up was? \"Just let me be alone... please,\" he said at last. \"Let me try to keep remembering what it\u2019s like to be\u2014what I am now.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 put his arms around his friend. \"Let me stay with you awhile, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" he said.\n\"There isn\u2019t time to stay,\" said $SURNAME1. He disengaged himself, went to...", "0_18_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span> when I went to the races with Romero and consequently it was too late to get a recommended coup\u00e9. All we could get was a private car that a man in Cadiz had promised to fill with his friends at twenty francs a head. There were seven of us altogether and the driver, who was the original owner, was so ugly and hateful that it was unbearable until a girl came running up and kissed him, calling out:\n\u201cPapa! how nice you look this evening. Why don\u2019t you get yourself a new hat?\u201d\nWhen she saw Romero sitting opposite her with his big deal box of a brown hat on his knees she almost stopped in the middle of telling him how nice he looked. She was very much astonished.\n\u201cGood evening, Mary,\u201d I said. I had seen her running up the stairs to the terraces when I was trying to get a barber. She did not know me, not even when I told her my name had been $FIRSTNAMEB5.\n\u201cWhy, Mr. Winters,\u201d she said, \u201cwhy didn\u2019t you let me know?\u201d\nI saw she very much wanted to ask me how much money I had made but she refrained...", "0_18_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until seven o'clock in the evening</span>. $SURNAME2's office arrived at by their system of coordinates was the great hall below Big O's office on the 108th floor of the Shell building. The great hall ran back 325 feet\u2014the same length as an entire floor of the building. It was 95 feet wide. Its ceiling was a full two stories high. Rumor had it that the place could seat a thousand people under air-conditioning so cold you\u2019d need a blanket. Rumor also had it that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the one-eyed founder of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent, had been married in the great hall.\nRich people disliked the great hall for receptions for just the reason that Tralfamadorians liked it best for receptions\u2014because of its stone-wall and-beam ram-you-damn-damn-dammed Gothic.\nThe great hall was a museum piece\u2014a chunk of history and Anglo-Saxon granite. Like many museums, it had once been a torture chamber. The rack still stood against a corner wall.\nThe switches that turned the iron grating in the floor to a thing of loops and whorls of living fire were at the base of an iron maiden.\nBrack...", "0_5_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-three in the morning</span>, and I had been away from home now for fifteen days. My attorney told the desk sergeant I was Doctor Raymer from the University of Southern California, that I was a sleep researcher, and that I needed every courtesy for my research. \u201cWe are studying the influence of different durations of sleep on\u2014\u201d he began, but the sergeant was not impressed. \u201cHell yes,\u201d he said. \u201cWe got a jail full of whole goddamned sleep researchers.\u201d He picked up a phone and misdialed three numbers before getting the bail bondsman. \u201cWe got one here says he's a doctor, a sleep doctor .. . yeah, yeah, hold on,\u201d he said, then told my attorney we'd have to wait a few minutes because they wanted this to be handled through regular channels\u2014for the class credit.\nWe finally got upstairs to a huge room full of snoring toughies in six\u2014men cubicles covered with steel mesh. Mine had a bunk, a steel toilet and sink, a mean\u2014looking desk sergeant watching me through mesh .... From the adjoining cage a huge black giant suddenly yelled, \u201cWho the fuck are you?\u201d I told my attorney I didn't like the way...", "2_10_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-two in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt that it would be better to throw his helmet away and breathe in Martian air, to die with his face to the sky.\nIt was then that the thing inside his belt blew up.\nThe explosion blew $FIRSTNAMEB0 off the side of the palace wall. When his senses returned, he could see nothing but smoke. Coughing and vomiting, he staggered back onto the wall.\nWhen the smoke had faded, $FIRSTNAMEB0 saw that his home ship and twenty-four others had fled to safety.\nThe forty-fifth ship was missing.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 soon realized that it was he who had caused the explosion that had crippled his home ship. And he realized something else \u2014 he could reproduce the effect at will. The secret switch in his belt was still pressed, the combination still taped. The taped combination consisted of two numbers, the number five and the number three.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 turned his pocket inside out, using his teeth, until the secret switch fell to the stone coping of the wall. He spat the switch onto his palm and then let it fall.\nThe explosion he now produced was small, feeble. The explosion he could produce by pressing the switch through the cloth would be far more powerful.", "0_20_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span> and the wind had shifted to the northwest, it was now cool, the sun had long set, and the sky was full of stars. At the edge of town you could see the lights of the bullring and hear the drums. We went out of the caf\u00e9 and crossed the street to the Hotel Montoya. The hall was full of people. We could hardly get up the stairs. Montoya had floodlights illuminating his prize bulls, three of them, standing on a platform in the courtyard. There were six more bulls up-stairs in another room where they were drinking beer to get them mellow. The biggest Plaza crowd of the season was filing down the stairs, and as each man came out on the landing he paused to look at the bulls, holding the lift to go up, talking, and looking. It was great to see how really important it looked. Some of the primest women in town were wearing ladinos and standing with their escorts. They were all going down to see the show in the seats provided for them directly below the President\u2019s box. The program showed Horacio Aznar in the caf\u00e9-au-lait jacket and Marcial Lalanda, Christino Gonzales\u2019 brother, and Belmonte, Joselito...", "0_17_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-one in the afternoon</span>. It was the second of May. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was not in the premises\u2014was on no plane of existence.\nFor the three days since his materializing, $SURNAME0\u2019s body had lain quietly in the little house at the base of the watchtower. On the day of his materialization, he had issued one of his previsioned commands to his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4. The command was Trask.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 had obeyed, retrieving $SURNAME0\u2019s contour chair from the Smithsonian Institution in Washington. He had dragged the chair half-way across the country to Cape Catastrophe. $FIRSTNAMEB4 was now curled up in the chair, sleeping on the broad shoulders of his master.\nThe contour chair and its sleeping occupant, borrowed as they were, nonetheless had an ineffable sanctity to $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, Jr., and their three guests. Nobody suggested removing the frail old man or his chair from the premises for the rest and refreshment of the travel they certainly both deserved. In large part the suggestion would have been unthinkable because of the alarm and the vicious growling it would have provoked in $FIRSTNAMEB4. Only the children were allowed near the...", "1_11_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. I scratched my ass, yawned and told Tom to move the convertible over to the starting line; The field was already full of entries. This was a big race, with a one\u2014hundred\u2014twenty\u2014dollar prize money, and the starting gun was about to boom. They told me, \u201cThis one is all strictly stock\u2014no modifications whatever, and must run on pump gas throughout; Under that tent over there is where you sign in.\u201d I was half awake, zonked from all the raw alcohol the night before and the long ride out from L.A. I\u2019d pushed the car all night, arriving just at dawn; completely at their mercy, drifting in and out of sleeps horizontal across both seats of the deal gypsy wagon. When the sun first came up I was slumped across the top of the windshield, and for a moment I thought it was rose-pink gasoline flames licking up on all sides, but it was hot pink sunrays on the windshield. I sat up, wondering where in the hell I was; then I remembered and wept because the gates of incarceration had closed behind me. I couldn\u2019t turn around and drive the other way; and even if I could, what then...", "1_22_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nLUnkmb embbedded in a web of ropes on scaffolding under the center fountain of the House of Saint Louis. He was breathing with deep difficulty in the cool primordial darkness.\nHe seemed to like the coolness. Someone shook his leg.\nThe leg was shaken by a man in the close-fitting blue suits of Holy Sue's Youth Brigade, a boy of twenty whose pretty blond hair hung down below his shoulders. The name on the nameplate of his pretty pink blouse was SAINT NEO VEGAS.\n\"Wake up, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said Saint Neo Vegas, shaking.\nLunky Babson had been sleeping soundly for ten hours and forty minutes.\nHe was in a dirty stance for being dirty stances. His mouth was open. His eyes were rolled up. There were snores from his mouth.\n\"Wake up, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said Saint Neo Vegas. He wiggled a thermometer in his bony friend\u2019s ear. \"Blood pressure ninety over sixty-eight,\" he said, reading the thermometer, \"which ain\u2019t so bad, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\"\n\"Ooo-whee!\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 said, waking up. He shook his head.", "0_20_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>, and the gentlemen all had had a drink and were suave and relaxed and inclined to let their monocles dangle and look urbane at each passing pretty lady when all at once one of the gentlemen gave a yelp and started looking at his chronometer and all the other gentlemen looked at their own chronometers and some of the gentlemen let their monocles fall and dangle. Ten minutes to go. Ten precious minutes, and they were off to the theatres.\nTen minutes to get to the theatre, and the fastest cab in Paris was just starting away from the corner when the gentlemen all came running with their monocles falling and dangling, and out they tumbled, running while they fastened their cape-wraps around their necks, and they chased the taxi, and the cabbie pounded on the hood and sides, and the dandies leaned in, gasping and panting, and one gave the cabbie twenty francs for the inconvenience, and there they were off, scraping their monocles up out of their faces, and then settling back with their cape-wraps buttoned tight around their necks to enjoy a grand evening at the Com\u00e9die Fran\u00e7aise. These were French dandies.", "1_6_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"You can't go!\" cried $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"You simply can't!\"\nA roller-towel slapped her wetly across her right tit. She flattened against a wall.\n\"You told me to go!\" cried $SURNAME1.\nHis suitcase banged an eleventh-grade math teacher right in the kisser. The teacher, who had told $SURNAME1 in September that he had absolutely no head for mathematics, teetered, but did not fall.\n\"You said you wanted me to go!\" cried $SURNAME1.\n\"Not like this!\" cried $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 blanched at her own words. She had meant to imply that she did not want him to go by himself, to imply that she wanted to float the voyage with him.\nIt was not to be.\n\"You floozie!\" cried $SURNAME1. \"I\u2019ll bet you\u2019re glad I\u2019m leaving. Glad to be rid of me. You went to bed with all your friends the minute my back was turned. And you made me think it was me.\"\n\"Not all\u2014\"\n\"You made me think all your friends were crazy about the way I walked and talked and the way my hair grew. What the hell did you do...", "0_18_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six sixteen in the evening</span>, according to Remi\u2019s watch. We were in the shadow of $FIRSTNAMEB2. The day had been a hot one for such a cold country, so that $FIRSTNAMEB2 wasn\u2019t black anymore. He was a dark green. Like a weeping willow on a hot afternoon.\nWhat worried us was our inability to see $FIRSTNAMEM2. Even his other boat was out of sight now. It was only the wake that told us there was a boat following us.\nConcealed under the floor boards of the raft was a tiny portable radio transmitter we\u2019d bought in Newport News, Virginia, a thousand years ago. The previous owner had paid five hundred dollars for it. It was quite complicated, but still almost worthless. It could send out a continuous signal for twenty-eight days, and then it would break down. After it broke down it would be good for nothing but spare parts for something else.\nThe thing to do would be to throw it overboard and hope it didn\u2019t sink. But we didn\u2019t dare do that. It was our only link\u2014so far\u2014with the Phoebe Snow .\nThe Phoebe Snow was where the series of old files were that Fowler Bradford and Select Michaels were after.", "0_20_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until nine o'clock in the evening</span> (the 18th of May, 1947, according to our calculations) when the idea of entering into a suicide pact was first suggested. By that time we were all at the little town of Arroyo Hondo, and we sat in front of a ramshackle country store, whose broken windows werehammed closed with paper, while the sullen storekeeper brought out cans of grapefruit juice and cheese and crackers, the basis of our diet. Beside us, on the cracked and faded concrete of the sidewalk, we saw the rusty bedspring, bound with wire, that was the springboard whereon babies were bounced to make them bawl, so the Mountain dwellers could tell what sex they were.\nNo one seemed to notice us as we sat there. No one drove in from the road that twisted off through the rolling hills like the last turn in a corkscrew. No one came in to buy anything. The proprietor just sent out word to the women that we were to be allowed to sit and eat in the store. We had a fire in a kerosene stove at one end of the building and the smoke came out through a hole in the roof. Our psychic posse looked like anarchists as it sat in this exile...", "1_2_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-eight in the morning</span>. Great beads of sweat popped out on his forehead as I finally gave up and let go of the handlebars. \u201cI can\u2019t do it,\u201d I whimpered. \u201cNot yet, anyway.\u201d It would have taken about four hours of practice to get that bike moving without kicking it down. I didn\u2019t have four hours. I had about two and a half minutes, if I was to catch that plane. So I stowed the bike and ran for the elevator, with my ticket wrapped in a $20 bill in my mouth.\nI saw it, as I tore across the lobby\u2014the snake said \u201cTic,\u201d and dropped the gold envelope into a General Delivery niche. I snatched it out and ran to the escalator...snatched the bike out of the cache as the elevator doors closed, leaving my feet to burn.\nBut my feet were already burned, and the escalator bit me like a snake. For a moment I thought I\u2019d have to drop the bike and sprint twelve floors up to the main concourse...which would have left me only seconds to fight my way across that huge hall and throw myself down at the feet of God. But just as the agony of...", "1_23_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-nine in the evening</span> and I was terribly tired after having been at The Mint all morning and most of the afternoon. The drinks I had drunk on an almost empty stomach were beginning to show their effect. I told Remi all about my troubles with the car and he sympathized. He told me something about himself. He was from Montana, originally. He filed mining claims all over the West for a living, and he boasted about how he had taken California away from the \u201cprogressives,\u201d meaning the reform-minded bureaucrats and the unions, who would destroy the state if they got their hands on it.\nRemi and I began discussing politics. He didn\u2019t live in Frisco; he just hung around The Mint. He was in oil country, somewhere outside. He said he had a hundred dollar room. He never had any trouble picking up girls and he bragged about that, too.\n\u201cIt\u2019s pretty hard to pick up girls in this town. The men are so goddamned queer they knock you right on your ass, and the girls are sissies. What\u2019s the matter with these people?\u201d\n\u201cMarijuana. It\u2019s all chemical life. No sap, no nerves.\u201d", "0_18_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span> of a Sunday in late November in the National Football League year of 1974. This particular NFL Sunday was the day of the week on which the New York Giants traditionally played a home game at the NY Giants Stadium in New Jersey. The NY Giants were a terrible team, and their stadium was always empty anyway, but this particular Sunday the stadium was unusually empty. Three days before Christmas, people had commitments. But the weather was good and the Jets were in town, and the fact that the NY Giants were playing the Jets made the place mysteriously, alarmingly, unpleasantly empty.\nBut there were people there. The Commissioner of the National Football League himself was there, were he but to look. The Commissioner was Frederick T. Frenger, Jr., of Frenger, Splint & Guthrie, Ltd., a fine old family firm, established in 1895 and headquartered in Manhattan. Mr. Frenger was a Harvard graduate, a Rhodes scholar, a football star, a war hero, and a director of Gillette, IBM, and the Guaranty Trust. He kept in shape by rowing four kilometers a day up and down the waters of the Sequa Canal in Italy. He lived with his mother on the East Side in a thirty-two-...", "0_3_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>, and our watch below was just over. The mate had called the watch\u2014to-morrow at six o'clock\u2014some half-hour before, and I remembered to have heard his call, but could recall nothing else of what he said. It must have been for that watch below that I had moved away from the taffrail; and yet I felt sure I had been standing there a few moments before. It is amazing how a complete lethargy will sometimes overtake one. Your legs bend under you. Your head swims. Your eyes close. But just now on the taffrail, I had felt none of it\u2014yet could recall nothing of what I had seen. I had been tingling with life and I had dropped into a dead sleep, it appeared, over the rail. How lean the watch must have supposed me! Well\u2014it was done now.\n\"D'ye hear there?\" shouted the voice of the mate. He had been calling for some time.\n\"To hell with you,\" said I.\n\"Turn-to, my lads, turn-to,\" he called.\nI heard the cook's heavy boots on the cabin skylight, and directly I was all alive again to the fullest sense of...", "1_20_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight seventeen in the evening</span>. $SURNAME1 was supposed to have left Washington, D.C., by then.\nHe wasn\u2019t ready to leave.\nHe had spent the evening buying a space ship\u2014a slow, unsatisfactory, and expensive business.\nSo far he had inspected six used space ships offered for sale at barter by the Titanians. None had been satisfactory. His standards were high\u2014madly high, he now realized. It was going to be difficult to find a space ship on Terra, much less one that would do for Crux IV.\nFor one thing, no space ship was truly used until it had been to Tralfamadore.\nMost of the Titanian vessels\u2014those that were in use\u2014were still in pieces in big packing crates on Titan.\n\"We usually sell in crates,\" said a salesman for United World Aircraft, who was selling a ship that was on display in a showroom. \"The individual pieces are fascinating to look at. There\u2019s something mystical about them.\"\n$SURNAME1 was suspicious. \"Are you selling any complete ships?\" He did not like the look of the ship on display.\n\"No, sir,\" said the salesman. \"We sold the only complete ship that we had.\"\n\"", "1_0_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve sixteen ante meridiem</span> when they left the press-conference\u2014or rather when I left. The others were all working hard. I heard the gang arguing as I approached the elevator. Norman was explaining how a rusty-type naked girl jumping up and down on his stomach would satisfy the news editors better than a story on the AP feed about a roadside-parking fine for Nevada Senator Bird and a squabble about the non-availability of onion dip at the press conference.\nRundholtz was disagreeing, politely, but Norman was bellowing away, and when I got to the elevator Bellingham and Evelyn Amendment were furiously humping their bags into the car.\n\u201cI\u2019ll never forgive you,\u201d she was shouting. \u201cNever!\u201d\n\u201cChrist,\u201d I whispered to myself, \u201cI\u2019ve heard that before.\u201d\nIt was ten past three in the morning. I had been gone fifteen hours. My attorney was waiting where I\u2019d left him, sleeping peacefully in the Whale. I tiptoed around to the driver\u2019s side, opened up and dropped behind the wheel.\n\u201cHoly Jesus!\u201d he said, suddenly awake.\n\u201cSh...", "1_21_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-three in the evening</span>. The Salo office of The Tralfamadorian Fortune Consultants was closed.\nThe office was in the basement of number 112 Druid Hill Avenue, a fireproof building with a private entrance in the rear. The building was a neighborhood eyesore, but the tenants knew no different, having been born, raised, educated, and employed in Salo, Mars. In 1969 a fire in Grover\u2019s Mill, New Jersey, had destroyed everything on Earth that Tralfamadorians considered worth seeing.\nAt 4747 Winbrook Drive in $FIRSTNAMEM1 GNU, Conn., the time was eleven forty-three in the evening.\nLife, unable to cope with the complexities of the human lung, was forcing itself and Ransom out through the open window.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 lay dreaming of $FIRSTNAMEF0. He was dreaming of her, and she was dreaming of him.\nHe had never made love to $FIRSTNAMEF0, had never seen $FIRSTNAMEF0, never even had a picture of $FIRSTNAMEF0. He had never known her last name, had never known who her father was, had never known her age. His entire image of $FIRSTNAMEF0 was carnal and romantic but was based exclusively on his preferences and tastes.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was whatever $SURNAME1 dreamed she ought to...", "1_8_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-one in the morning</span>.\nAt exactly three minutes past nine, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 picked up the telephone in the bedroom of his bungalow at the $SURNAME0 estate and rang Fleetwood\u2019s private number.\nMrs. Winnie had just opened the curtains to yell at $SURNAME1 for sitting around in his rumpled pajamas all morning.\nSalo, the thin, sallow wart-nester who was $SURNAME1\u2019s servant, had brought $SURNAME1 a pot of very black coffee.\n\"Great stuff, that Salo,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to $SURNAME0 over the telephone. \"I\u2019d walk a mile for a cup of his coffee.\"\n\"You walked exactly twenty-two miles to get to the phone, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME1 laughed. He was very tired, having spent the latter part of the night recruiting replacements in Newport for his space ships. His left hand ached abominably, and the saucer-shaped burn on his right cheek was sensitive to the touch. $SURNAME1 was thinking that the abrasions on his legs and arms would sure enough attract the mosquitoes when night came again.\n\"What happened to your shins and forearms?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Say?\" said...", "2_4_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-three in the morning</span>.\n\"He's gonna sing that song 'The Last Time I Saw Paris.' \"\n\"What\u2019s that one?\" somebody asked.\n\"I'll sing it for you,\" said Bull, \"I know it by heart.\" And he began humming and then singing in a cracked voice a song that none of us had ever heard:\nOh, the last time I saw Paris,\nThe girls did wear beads\nAlong the Seine.\nBut none of them did look so\nGrand and grand-esque\nAs the girl that I left behind in\nView Park.\nHall signed the check. Bull told some dirty stories. At four fifty-five the waitress brought the beer. Bull sent it back. \"I don\u2019t like this beer,\" he told her.\n\"It\u2019s the only kind we have.\"\n\"That\u2019s alright, I\u2019ll drink it,\" said Bull.\nThe waitress brought the beer back.\n\"I\u2019ll drink it faster this time,\" said Bull, and he did.", "0_4_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>, and God was angry.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was afraid that God was going to make him burn in Hell.\nAnd God assured $FIRSTNAMEB0 that he was.\n\"Don\u2019t try to play tricks, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014going up there where you can\u2019t possibly, possibly mean anything to anybody,\" God said. \"Just stay here with me in Oklahoma, $FIRSTNAMEB0. Strap yourself in a wheelchair, and let a good, kind, reasonably intelligent person push you around, and let a good band play beautiful music to you twenty-four hours a day. This can all be arranged, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and the really good part is it doesn\u2019t have to cost you a cent.\"\n\"I have to go to Mars,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe true phrase he had been trying to remember all evening long was: \"God damn, I gotta go!\"\n\"Why, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" God said.\n\"It\u2019s my job,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"There is no job, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014you proved that. You goofed up everything. When we get you up there, we\u2019ll just tell them how everything is all goofed up. They can fix it in ten seconds.\" \"No,\" said...", "0_17_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the mother of Salo, Junior, looked out her window and saw her son standing lonely under the Martians\u2019 ship. At first she thought it was Salo. Then she saw that it was Junior. Then she saw that it was his ghost.\nThe mother screamed.\nA man in the apartment house punched a hole in the wall of his parlor, put his head through it, looked up, and saw the iron statue of a winged horse, rearing. He gasped for breath, got some iron filings in his windpipe. These made him cough so violently that he had to pull his head in before he asphyxiated himself.\nThe mother, having screamed, was now devoting her attention to her strange child.\nThe child, while small and kinky-haired and intelligent, had the powers of a genius. The mother made mistakes. She started out by telling the child that some children, but certainly not he, really could understand how people felt by looking at their faces. The child, looking at her face, understood that this was a lie.\n\"Some children,\" she said, \"but not you.\" The child smiled sadly, wondering if it would ever be anything but not you.\n\"You can see...", "2_17_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I thought I might find my buddy here.\"\n\"You did?\" said the man. \"Well\u2014well, you sure as hell\u2014\" the man couldn\u2019t speak for laughing. \"You remember me, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" he said finally.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked at the man blankly. The man kept laughing at $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s blank expression, and it was making $FIRSTNAMEB0 nervous. \"I\u2019m your buddy,\" said the man. \"Can\u2019t you even remember your buddy, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" He put out his hand, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 shook it. \"Lieutenant $SURNAME2,\" said the man.\n\"Y-yes?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. Then he remembered, and he laughed. \"Of course\u2014$SURNAME2!\" he said. \"How are you, $SURNAME2?\"", "1_6_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span> when I tapped on the old man's door. I wanted to say good-by. He called to me to come in. He said he was just getting up. I opened the door and went in. I saw two suit-cases on the floor.\n\u201cGoing away?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cWhen?\u201d\n\u201cI don't know. I just had a feeling I would go this morning.\u201d\n\u201cWhere are you going?\u201d\n\u201cOh, up to Saint Francis Wood. Where I used to live.\u201d\n\u201cI thought you had a lot of friends here.\u201d\n\u201cI have. I don't know why I'm going away.\u201d\n\u201cWell, it looks like a good move.\u201d\n\u201cEverything's a good move if you make it that way.\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cWell,\u201d I said, \u201cI guess I'll go and get some breakfast.\u201d\nI went out and down to Jane's to eat. When I came back Mrs. Martin was up and dressing. She told me she had offered Sam fifty dollars if he would give...", "2_3_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich time.\nAt the moment the mateless one received the orders, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog appeared on the bluish ice of Mars, three hundred yards from the mateless one and the sleigh.\nThe dog, Skip, said hello first.\n$SURNAME0 floated up beside his dog in a foot-long spotlight of yellow nothingness.\n\"By golly, here you are!\" said $SURNAME0 amiably. \"I had to come all the way to Mars to find a pious man!\"\nThe mateless one, as Pontiff of Mars, was vaporized on the spot\u2014vaporized by surprise and joy. His only memorial was a message on the telephone answering machine in his private quarters in the massive Mother Church in Newport. The message read: \"This is the Supreme Pontiff, screaming for the last time.\"\n$SURNAME0 took the mateless one\u2019s identity, his credentials, his antigravity skis, his earthbound private speedboat, his palace, his two lovers, his two children, and his old dog, Skip, to Haiti, where he traded them for material grandeur on Mercury.", "2_5_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-nine in the morning</span>. The knock was louder. I looked down and realized I was barefoot...Th\u2019re wan& something I mustr-do. My attorney was in jail because of me; I had very nearly killed two men less than twenty-four hours before; I\u2019d fled the scene of the crime in a completely disabled car; I\u2019d blown all my cash on drugs and was stuck in the elevator at the top of the tallest hotel in the world, with no more drugs, no more room service bill, no cash, no credit, no car keys and no shoes.\nOK.. .they could probably stone me in the streets down there at this point and nobody in the whole wide world would even raise a eyebrow.. .absolutely correct. But here, at this door, I was tempted to make matters worse. What would be revealed if I pulled it open? Would it be the Dope Cops? Some new lunatic life form? Or\u2014of even more interest, the blue face of Wizard Himself? Or some harmless desk clerk wanting to chat about the weather or give me some final bit of knee action before this fine, savage farce of a morning ended in blood and tears?", "1_6_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six seventeen in the morning</span>. By a quarter to ten, Panurge and his commando were out of the city, travelling cross-country in a fast black Mercedes with twewve blank, license-plated windows. Panurge was at the wheel. The odds against his survival had now dropped to even money\u2014 approximately one chance in two.\nIn Washington, D.C., the President was having trouble falling asleep\u2014his recurring dream had been about his Unbroken Family Painting, in the Oval Office. He had seen the progressive deterioration of the picture on television, and, now, in dreamlet, he watched horrified, as the morbidly obese mother was disintegrated by some terrible preternatural force.\nThen the whole glorious family portrait\u2014which had obviously been so proud and happy in 1963\u2014was attacked by the same force, was converted quickly into a kiln-load of charcoal briquettes.\nO Lord, thought the President, let me not wake up screaming. And the phone by his bed rang. His dreaming mind knew the call would come, thought, Let it ring\u2014I can't stand the goddamned thing anyway.\nHe tugged the pillow down over both ears. But the phone rang on. With half his mind, the President considered...", "2_17_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, the place the bar of The American Sea Food restaurant in the Beverly Hills Hotel. She had tried to get a table outdoors, but it was too near the cocktail hour.\n\"Do you mean that literally?\" said Candide.\n\" 'Hi there.' Americans say that to each other when they meet.\"\n\"In just that tone of voice?\"\n\"Sometimes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \" 'How are ya?' But more often just 'Hi.' Like this.\"\nShe said \"Hi\" again.\n\"That\u2019s what you say?\" he said.\n\"Mostly,\" she said. \"What do you say in France when you meet an American girl?\"\n\"Say?\" he said.\n\"When you say hello to her.\"\n\"I don\u2019t say hello to American girls,\" he said. \"I ignore them. I put my head down and charge.\"\n\"Don\u2019t you want me to sit down?\" she said.\n\"I\u2019m charging,\" he said. \"I don\u2019t want to veer away too soon.\"", "0_9_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to ten o'clock in the morning</span> of a horribly hot day that was to end in one of the worst thunderstorms in the history of West Texas. The place was Prank's Trading Post, a cluster of buildings and stilts on the dirt road between the village of Milagro to the south and the village of Okahandja to the north. The cluster was more like an accidental agglomeration, a tumbled formation like the ones that occur in sand hills (or coral reefs, for that matter): where an accidental wind lays down one stone, establishes a clump, a group. And then, as though the stones could propagate and increase, the wind returns, brings more stones to the clump, which can then spread, draw on and suck the life out of better soil or richer sources of water. So the metaphor goes wildly and beautifully out of control, except that in reality there is no wind, and the clustering, which seems to arise at first from the accidental stopping of someone on the road, from his throwing down his bottles and cans of beer, his lunch, whatever, expands only to the limit of your vision. So then you circle, because by whatever accident you have alighted in that horrid, blazing desert of a road, and you must regard,...", "2_9_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-one in the morning</span>. Time for the work detail of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 to begin its stint at the latrine that month.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were spanking clean, having taken advantage of the fact that their squad, having drawn latrine duty that month, had the hut to itself for two hours in the morning. Being on latrine duty did not necessarily mean cleaning out the latrine. It meant whatever form of remedial labor Sarge chose to make it mean.\nThis month, the difference between a good and a bad job being judgmental rather than quantitative, Sarge had chosen to mean by it a good job that included shaving.\nAs a good job, it was a difficult job.\nOnly $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had shown up clean.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 felt no fear whatever of the coming inspection. To get that clean, they had had to have friction rubs and mud packs, and neither of them had yet so much as picked his nose.\nThe only person in the hut was the Jesus, who always had fell obligations on Wednesdays.", "1_3_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-seven in the morning</span>, local time, in the desert near Las Vegas. The girl that Stony Stevenson was crazy about was named $FIRSTNAMEF3 Bullmer, and she was from Monroeville, Alabama.\nStevenson was from Cleveland. The year was 1982; the month, November. And the place was one-tenth of a mile in the air and one-tenth of a mile off the desert floor.\nStony and $FIRSTNAMEF3 Bullmer strapped themselves into their contour chairs in the control module of the Reflecting Nihilist Anti-Meme Machine. The control module was at the bottom of a shaft, the sides of which bristled with the windmills that turned the generator that fed the battery that provided the juice to the machine. When the batteries were dead, the machine stood silent and rusty, like so many other abandoned Vegas gadgets.\n3 Here Comes Civilization, with Trouble Bringing up the Rear\nProblem child comes out of the west, over the mountains. Cleveland and $FIRSTNAMEF3 Bullmer, their attention riveted to the big television screen in the control module, barely noticed it when their searchlight blinked out.\n\"Damn,\" said Stony Stevenson. \"It looks like she beat us again.\"\n\"She must\u2019ve cut the power,\"...", "2_15_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>\u2014Earthling time. It was the last moment in which the Earthlings in the Madison Cube Garden could see the shimmering curtain of lovely particles\u2014and imagine that their Earthling curtains would look just like that when the Latest News Network beamed them into homes all over the planet. It was their last illusion. At three o\u2019clock sharp, the time of the demonstration at Spec\u2019s, the crowd would see only a hideous yellowish tangle passing before their eyes when they looked at the latest wonder made possible by the year of the Sputnik C explosion.\nThe time was three-thirty.\nThe master of ceremonies drew a deep breath.\nInto the microphone he said, \"And now\u2014let\u2019s see your own loves, the loves that only you can love freely with the loves that only you can give, flash before your eyes for the last time. $FIRSTNAMEB0, tell us about your loves. How does it feel to know that your own particular loves, no one but you can ever so much as imagine?'\"", "1_14_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-four in the afternoon</span>. The baby's birthday was finally over, once and for all. Long live $FIRSTNAMEB2. We were afraid to make any noise, lest the neighbors hear. They had never seen such a wild birthday party\u2014even with a horse.\nWe unpacked a suitcase, and my attorney spread his sparkling new Batman uniform on the bed. \u201cWell,\u201d he said. \u201cI guess I\u2019d better go try it on.\u201d I nodded. My attorney knew the routine. Within 15 minutes of a costume purchase we'd be heading for the Casino. Not this time, I told him.\n\u201cNot tonight,\u201d I said. \u201cDo you realize what day this is?\"\nHe stared at me.\n\u201cWell, we have to celebrate tonight. We have to have a party.\u201d\n\u201cNo!\u201d I said. \u201cWe can't.\u201d My attorney understood.\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said. \u201cThis is the night she died.\u201d\n\u201cOh, yes. 1 forgot,\" he said, backing away. \u201cI see.\u201d He sat down very carefully on the edge of the bed.\n\u201cWhat\u2019ll...", "0_0_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had come to confuse the issue. There were no times, only places. $SURNAME1 was rescuing his wife and child from a chain of events that had begun with a kiss.\nHe had thought to spy on them, to sabotage their intimacy.\nNow he abandoned that dance, that chandelier.\nHe flew to join them.\nHe fled the Earth.\nHe joined them in the Blue Nun.\nSpace had made a family out of them. $FIRSTNAMEB2 did not rob them of that. Space did. Space and the luck of having been handed a harmonium.\nIn the harmonium was their message to others. The harmonium was their gift to mankind. Anyone could make as much sense out of its music as $SURNAME0 and his wife could. Any reader of this chronicle can play it as well as $SURNAME1 could. All you need is ears.\nThe name of the piece is Daddy renewables-Mummy.\nCHAPTER FIFTEEN\nThe name of the storm cyclone undergoing its fantastic death in the middle of the Pacific is hurricane Alice.\nAlice was the first known cyclone to have existed for exactly one year. Gales in Buss Island in the Seychelle Islands maintain that a typhoon named Alice...", "0_0_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until one o'clock in the morning</span>, according to all stopwatches, when a baby carriage floated down on the Helmholtz using a propulsion device unknown to science. The occupant of the carriage was a recently hatched and much bewildered baby.\nHomuncolossus struck the harness and carriage trice with his great voice, reducing both to nothingness.\nWhen the beams resumed, Helmholtz was again floating peacefully in space. There were now only thirty-two soldiers left in the Antrum.\nThe left wing of the attack was beginning to penetrate the last curtain of confusion, the perimeter of which was marked by the northwest corner of $SURNAME0\u2019s library.\nBlazius and $SURNAME0 suspended hostilities for one minute, during which $SURNAME0 read in his levitation cutter and Blazius fought a losing battle against overwhelming lust for a cigarette.\nThen the final great offensive against the space ship began.\nOne by one, all members of the High Command except $SURNAME0 made the supreme sacrifice of their lives. Helmholtz thought it possible that sacrifice might enable the ship to be recaptured, and he accompanied his men to the death chambers.\nWith the greatest spirit, they all sang \"Every Time It Gets Dirty, Mother Washes It For Me.\"", "2_10_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-three in the morning</span>.\nThe place was a rocky hollow on a windswept plain in northern Canada, Earth. There was no building material in the hollow. The ruined space ship, like all Martian buildings, had been made out of some material that Martian minds could harvest right out of the air. This particular hollow had once been the site of a structure made of yellow, quartz-like material\nof the type that puts out so wonderful a light when charged with current. This structure had been bombarded and blasted to bits.\nThere was iron in the hollow. There were iron fragments everywhere.\nExcept for $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the man standing by the battered hydrogen escape valve, the hollow was empty.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his company commander, the fair-haired boy named Bracknell, were the only survivors of the battle that had taken place there. On a big map, that battle would have been shown as a small red event, for the red color signified Martian victories.", "2_13_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe power-house dynamo that powered the lights and appliances of the mansion rose in a shed back of the mansion itself. A spiral staircase in the shed led to a catwalk that ran the length of the dynamo like the groove in a turning pin. The catwalk was open. The toothed edges of the dynamo ran in deep grooves on either side of the catwalk. A man turning loose the brake on a dynamo so as to send current to the mansion could make a fast getaway down the spiral staircase and along the grooved catwalk if he smelled Martian or Soviet smoke.\nIn this pleasant spot, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 were taking a siesta. The two were not far from the head of the catwalk. A spiral staircase led down to them from the floor of the mansion above.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 lay face down on a canvas contour couch. He was covered by a wraparound khaki sleeping suit. His naked feet projected beyond the bottom of the sleeping suit. He had lost his left foot in the European War.\nHis right foot was wooden.", "0_2_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-six in the morning</span> when the cryslambulance arrived at the Canyon station of the free flyer. The free flyers were another of $SURNAME0\u2019s money-savers. Free flyers were simple, unpressurized eighteen-foot surface craft. They were housed in reinforced concrete embankments every fifty miles along the Virginian coast.\nThey were painted like fire engines, to avert the number one New England fear on the part of space travelers\u2014that of falling into a body of water.\nTravelers directed to a particular free flyer stepped on board, started its motor with a crank in the cockpit, and took off. They taxied to the end of the concrete runway, gunned the motor with the starter, and took off vertically, becoming airborne as soon as the nose lifted.\nAt the top of the climb they killed the motor, settled into an easy glide. They landed and taxied back to the station on what remained of momentum.\nPrimitive though they were, they were, at the time of which we write, the fastest practicable means of surface transportation anywhere in the Solar System.\n$SURNAME0 flew his ships to the nearest free-flyer station, had his new crew members direct their ship to the next free flyer further up the coast. This...", "2_11_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> on December 31 in the year Martian Year 4739.\nOut on the broad Tableland above the caves was sunshine and blue sky\u2014and a dumbwaiter, with a foot and a hand still jerking weakly in midair.\nOn the walls of every cave nearby were the pale crayon signatures, blood-red and forty or fifty feet long, of the 296 men, 2 women, and 2 children who had come down into the cave system the night before at the command of Colonel Cavanaugh.\nColonel Cavanaugh, who had commanded all that, now lay dead on the floor of Gallery Eight, Mercury Quadrille. His face was pale gray, his rounded form was cold and stiff, and his recent head wound had clogged and dried, giving his face an evilly fixed expression, as if his last thought had been an evil one.\nOn the wall near Cavanaugh were the words he had been trying to write. They were Martian Gothic, and they said:\nHELL PLEASANT", "0_3_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-three in the morning</span> of Tim Sunday when finally and irrevocably the long night blew apart over the palace at Vegas and shattered the high masonry walls of the Internal Revenue building. Across the twisted city, the sound of ten thousand sirens brought the people shivering to their windows. A desultory artillery fire could be heard coming from the airport.\nThe sound of Dick $FIRSTNAMEF3\u2019s battered Thunderbird coming up the gravel driveway was lost in the clatter and roar of the receding night. By the time he reached the front door, the storm siren was winding into the weariness of the dawn wind.\nThe sound of his key turning in the lock was drowned by the general racket. The door swung open and he closed it behind him and there she was, slumped in the dark hallway, a white ghost.\n\"Lapis?\" he whispered.\nNo answer. The storm shuddered and groaned to a close.\n\"Lapis,\" he said.\nStill nothing. $FIRSTNAMEF3 reached out and touched her. She slumped over.\n\"Lapis!\" he cried.\nWhen she looked up at him in the first weak light of dawn, he knew at once. It was not the face of a stone. It was an utterly exhausted...", "0_19_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s section of the iron ship was in the hands of the enemy. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s company in the iron ship had been reduced to $FIRSTNAMEB0, Sergeants $FIRSTNAMEB1 and Marsch, Corporal Stance, Privates First Class D Hydes, Croyd Cremling, and Biles.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and Marsch were very sick. Their reflexes were out by almost a whole second.\nSergeant $FIRSTNAMEB1 had sent Marsch to check on the progress of the eight best soldiers in the company. In another ninety seconds, Sergeant $FIRSTNAMEB1 would send Sergeants Beardsley, Bracknell, Stone, and Clemon, Privates First Class Frazier, Garfoose, Husik, and Schleime down to assist the eight best soldiers.\nSergeants Beardsley and Bracknell were in the air-regeneration chambers, so sick they could hardly be expected to fire a rifle accurately. They were both twitching.\nSergeant Stone had had a fit on the way back after inspecting a shaft to the surface. Just before the fit, he had gotten a glimpse of the magnificent vistas surrounding the underground fortress. He had seen, for the first time, that there really was...", "0_21_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe Martian battle fleet was on its way to Earth.\nAt exactly that moment, the last of the Earthling space ships headed away from Earth and into heavenly territory.\nAt exactly that moment, an earthquake in Kamchatka destroyed the last of the Soviet missiles.\nAt exactly that moment, an earthquake in the Santa Cruz Mountains destroyed the three factories that were to make the Martians'Release Tool.\nAt exactly that moment, the civil war in Argentina ended.\nAnd at exactly that moment, Ochs and Bacon and $SURNAME0 and sugradnamously and gloriously ended their yachting trip in Newport.\nThey materialized quietly, outside the walls of the palace in Newport. They materialized on a small lawn on which were goldfish ponds, trellises dense with roses, and a wishing well that dispensed perfumed water.\nOchs found the lawn to his liking. He never had seen a lawn before. Neither, strictly speaking, had $SURNAME0 or sugradnamously. They found it to their liking, too.\nOchs punched the perfumed fountain with a direction-finder. The fountain squirted straight up. \"People on my world intended this to represent the springs of wisdom,\"...", "1_23_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was the living room of $SURNAME0\u2019s Taj Mahal. The sole other occupant was $FIRSTNAMEF0, $SURNAME0\u2019s dog.\nThey weren\u2019t sleeping. There didn\u2019t seem to be anything to talk about. $FIRSTNAMEF0 lay in the arch of $SURNAME0\u2019s left foot. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 lay on his back before the alcove that housed the time machine, his hands behind his head.\nHe and his watch and his suit of blue diving skin weren\u2019t just beautiful\u2014they were dazzling. The night that nipped in through the open French windows was pitch to the nipping of his presence. The noxious integrity of that presence made the Universe seem hoaxish, cheap, and showy beside him.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 prided himself on being the least of Nature\u2019s jokes. The only laughter he had ever enjoyed had been the laughter that Alice Han saint allegedly wrote on the wall of the cave beside Mars, the laughter implicit in the disorder of the Universe.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0,\" came a voice from the alcove. \"$FIRSTNAMEB0.\"\n$SURNAME0\u2019s face was a metallic projection of his easy-...", "0_21_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine seventeen in the evening</span>.\nAt nine seventeen in the evening, Salo, the messenger, was on his way to Mercury and the palace of Tralfamadore.\nAt nine forty-three in the evening, $FIRSTNAMEF0, according to her contract with $SURNAME1, was free to go where she pleased.\nShe did not know yet what she would do.\nAt ten o\u2018clock in the evening, Salo, who had to clean up a great deal of his audience with his handkerchief, was ready to push off. He had sent his message to Mercury.\nAll that remained to him was his clean-up, his farewells and a meal at the palace.\nThe meal was a fixed part of the messenger\u2019s ritual. The cook at the palace of Tralfamadore was proud of his cuisine, and he liked to show it to visitors from Earth.\nSalo cleaned up. His head was clear now. \"I\u2019m a fool! \" he said. \"I\u2019m an ass! \" he said. \"I\u2019m a ninny! \" he said. He felt much better for saying it. \"I\u2019ve been made a fool of!\" he said incredulously. \"By a...", "1_17_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty in the afternoon</span>. We had just finished a first, very fast run over the coat-hanger road from Papantla to Tuxpan. My attorney was attempting to restart the hard-to-start motor, which would not obey the overhead camshaft in the distributor, but kept kicking and stumbling like an L-8 GM truck with a burnt valve. \u201cIt's your god-damned battery!\u201d I yelled, trying to calm down. \u201cYes. I have to open the choke.\u201d Meanwhile, I found the starter rope hanging from a tree and managed to crank the engine over by hand. We took off like an L-5 after some frantic adjustments and I tested it at fifty on the bar-straight road. \u201cI can get it up to sixty on the turns,\u201d I said.\n\u201cI told you that battery was useless,\u201d he replied. \u201cWhat we need is a good fast car. Maybe we can steal one in Tuxpan.\u201d What we really needed was a shack by the side of the road where we could hide the car and get our heads together; but my attorney couldn't understand that. He wanted to race along the edge of the ocean until we came to Mexico...", "0_23_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-one in the evening</span> when the space ship rounded the planet Mars. The pilot throttered the engine back a notch. The co-pilot got up from his seat and came back through the oval cabin of the ship to stand beside $SURNAME0. Both men were startled to find that $SURNAME0 and Space Wanderer were not alone.\nAt the back of the ship was a cow\u2014a concrete-colored Jersey with black horns and wet yellow eyes. She had been eating grass that grew in broken flowerpots at the rear of the salon.\n\"Moo?\" she said.\n\"What on earth\u2014?\" said the co-pilot.\n\"Moo?\" said the cow, ready to be milked.\n\"She came with the ship,\" said $SURNAME0. He patted the flank of the cow. \"Won\u2019t you join us, ma\u2019am?\" he said, \"and have some mangoes?\"\n\"Moo?\" said the cow, hopeful.\n\"Mango?\" said $SURNAME0 darkly. \"Mango?\" He laughed. \"What a peculiar thought! The only mangoes we have are for this gentleman, and he\u2019s having such a grand time keeping them all for himself.\"\nThe cow...", "0_22_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-eight in the evening</span>. I saw a Lancer at the end of the motel parking lot, with a tall man waiting inside it. I walked over there.\n\u201cYou must be Doctor Blake,\u201d he said.\n\u201cThat's right,\u201d I said. \u201cWhere's the car you supplied?\u201d\n\u201cIt blew up in Cuernavaca,\u201d he said. \u201cIt caught fire.\u201d\n\u201cDid Acklin get out?\u201d\n\u201cNobody knows for sure,\u201d he said. \u201cThey dragged his charred body out of the wreck but he may have escaped first and been burned.\u201d\n\u201cI don't believe it,\u201d I said.\n\u201cI don't give a damn,\u201d he said. \u201cI only work here.\u201d He handed me a manila envelope. \u201cYou give these to your friend Fletcher.\u201d\nI grabbed it. \u201cI'll keep this,\u201d I said.\n\u201cYou got bad eyesight,\u201d he said. \u201cI just weighed it.\u201d\nI tore the envelope open. It was money, American dollars. \u201cIt's okay. I...", "2_11_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-nine in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 took his chrono out of his pocket. He beheld the inscription on its dial: Hello. Hovering o\u2019er, it reads, is to part-where bees sting now, they will later do worse.\nA moment earlier, his chrono had said\u2014and said still: All times are one time, now. Hello.\nThe inscription on the chrono was the last thing $SURNAME0 had wanted, needed, or been able to make sense out of for days.\nThere had been turmoil in the world. The newspapers were full of it. Unfamiliar and alarming words competed for first place in headlines and lead paragraphs\u2014sputnik, hydrogen bomb, gom-ball, Red encampment, White encampment, fir tree, pari passu, Berlin airlift, U-2, megadeath, and hot line.\n$SURNAME0 had dived under his limousine when the mailman brought the newspapers.\n\"There\u2019s some kind of circus going on out there,\" the chauffeur had shouted to $SURNAME0 under the limousine. \"Seems to be a God-damned war brewing.\"", "2_22_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-two in the evening</span> on Mars. The plainclothesman led Miss Newton away.\n\"Wait\u2014\" said $SURNAME1 to the plainclothesman.\nThe man turned around, looked skeptically at $SURNAME1.\n\"Man\u2014you\u2019re quick,\" he said. \"I hadn\u2019t hardly shut the door before you had your hand on it. I don\u2019t know how you do that, staying in such good shape at your age.\"\n\"Where did you come from?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"What?\" said the plainclothesman. \"The door opened, and there you stood. Didn\u2019t expect you to retract the steps, did you?\"\n\"What?\" said $SURNAME1. \"What?\"\n\"I said, I didn\u2019t expect you to retract the steps.\"\n\"I know what you said,\" said $SURNAME1. \"I just don\u2019t know what you\u2019re talking about.\"", "0_17_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span> when I reached my home in New York. I called my attorney and told him I was waiting in the lobby of the Dunhill Hotel; and that I would see him when he came down. He arrived a few minutes later, a tall man with graying hair, who looked as if he were somewhere in his fifties, but who was actually in his thirties. He had a strange philosophical bent, and together with his office as a member of a sinister New York law-firm, this gave him a somewhat unusual reputation in the city. His real name was John Divver, but his nickname in the busier seamy side of the law was Do Nothing Hedda. He had never told me the origin of this title. There was something very thoughtful - almost dreamy - in his eyes, and it was clear that he would have liked to quit his job and sit all day alone in a room, writing a novel on life in New York. This is a dream many New York lawyers have. As a matter of fact, Do Nothing is a clever paranoiac; and behind his dreamy eyes are cunning legal brains capable of dealing with almost any hellish problem. I have often wondered why he remained the obscure member of a law-...", "0_13_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span> when Salo Bentinck finally arrived in the office of the President of the Galaxy.\n\"Anton,\" the president said calmly, \"I hope you\u2019ll understand if I ask you to take a seat. We\u2019re not going to see eye to eye on everything, but let me begin by saying two things. First, when you first came to me, years ago, with that naive proposal for putting an end to war by having every Earthling throw his waste-materials and sewage out the window instead of into plumbing, I told you that you were nuts. I\u2019m happy now to say that science has proved me wrong, and I\u2019m delighted to welcome you here as the head of a great and necessary Society.\"\n\"I thank you very much for your kind remarks, Mr. President,\" said Mrs. Br Petitioner, who had, of course, also come to visit the president. She hung her husband\u2019s Distinguished Service Medal on the coatrack, kissed the president on the forehead, smiled at him, and took a comfortable wing chair facing the desk.\n\"And the second thing I want to say,\" the president went on, \"is that I\u2019ll do anything I can to help...", "2_17_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-four in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF0 was sitting in a North American Oval chair by a white table that could have been part of the basic furniture of the Solar Library in the Crystal Monolith. Orichalc sculptures glinted in the dark. And in the maroon shutters was the deep reflective gleam of dark metal.\nThere was the sound of footsteps outside the dark solar study. A man shined a flashlight past the shuttest into the dark.\nThe man called out, \"Anyone home?\"\n\"Please come in,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nShe sat in her Oval chair, in the dark, at the white table, by means of which she could be recognized as the color consultant to the House of St. Lear. Only a flicker of lighting exposed her face in the dark room. The caller stepped inside, shielding his eyes,\n\"Mama\u2014that you?\" he said. Only by context was he recognized as the son of $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEF3. He gave his name\u2014Melvin.", "1_22_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was in so bad with the authorities that he had to stand up to the firing squad with his face to the wall. Standing against the wall, $FIRSTNAMEB0 could see people in the auditorium.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head was against the planks of a big, gray detachment of army discipline.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked at the people watching him die, and his love for them put a burning trail of mucus from deep down in his throat up to his nostrils. His eyes watered.\nThe walls of the auditorium were covered with deep red padding. The padding was seamed and festooned with ducts, pipes, switches, loudspeakers, and electric cables. The cables hung slack and lifeless in a golden framework. And over the electric layout a peaceable blue spiderweb was woven, so that the audience would not be jolted when eternity was interrupted by bursts of blinding light and explosive thunderclaps.\nThe auditorium smelled like ideal public-school toilets before lessons began in September.\nIn the front row were fifty-two red-capped children, the youngest the Ph.D. squadron who had been sent to welcome $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe oldest was the Statue of Liberty, who had been wrenched loose from...", "2_8_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\nIt was ten minutes shy of Solar Noon.\nBy ten Solar Noon, each wall of the suite was in the Sun. The entire room was transformed into an exhedra, an arch of stone over the Sun.\nImmediately, the curtains went up.\nBut the drapes were no longer ordinary drapes. They were solar drapes, sensitized to the warmth of the Sun. When the drapes went up, they not only shut out the sunlight from the room, but absorbed the light actively and rendered it harmless.\nThe headboard and footboard of the bed no longer cast sharp shadows on the mural.\nThere was a mounted telescope by the solarium\u2019s one window. The telescope had been pointing toward Titan, toward the space ship that was bringing $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to Earth. The telescope was now checked by means of a United States Navy Chronometer, was pointed accurately at the Sun. The telescope was so mounted that it could be rotated under electrical control.\nAt ten minutes before Solar Noon, the switch in the control box was thrown. The telescope began to rotate.", "0_19_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-two in the evening</span>. Lang's naked feet sensed the silent slumber of untold thousands as he went to work. At seven forty-five he was in the Information booth at Sea-Tac, receiving a back-alley briefing on Miami. Then he began shuffled through the early arrivals, looking for a certain face, a certain figure...\nTO THE END OF THE LINE!Meanwhile, up in his penthouse, drug lord Adolfo Cubazione finished a phone call of his own. He put his receiver down with a crazy grin on his face. \u201cRauschenberg!\u201d he shouted, hopping around on one leg. \u201cGleason! Callahan! Tenute! Kugel! We did it, babies! War is hell, but we just did it!\"\nThis was news to Rauschenberg and the others, who were sprawled in front of a TV set watching Earl Warren on the Love Feast. \u201cDid what?\u201d asked the abstract painter.\n\u201cWe\u2019re in it, man!\u201d said Adolfo, who had been given this inside dope by a Congressman's wife who turned tricks on the side. \u201cThey're sending our boys.\u201d\n\u201cWhere to?", "2_2_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-two in the morning</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas was kneeling on one of the twisted cactus stumps that made up 4190 Plaza Street. She was dressed in a black skirt, her legs covered all the way to the thigh with a transparent nylon net, nylon thigh-highs that were more than half a peacock\u2019s tail. Above this she wore a loose man\u2019s shirt, the tails out, collar appearing in front. The shirt\u2019s right shoulder was bunched into a bra whose cup left her right breast free. Across her forehead she had laid a wide, transparent bandage, trailing off behind her left ear, giving her the same sort of severely futuristic hairdo as always. In her mouth was a filter-tipped cigarette.", "0_5_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span> and the place was the marble lobby of the Hotel Overlooking the Falls. The building was battered and sagging, and the lobby was so dark the trunks of trailing plants were heavy black blurs in it.\nAt the reception desk stood a night-clerk in a soiled white jacket and roller-skate wheels sewed to the bottom of his shoes. Jutting the right way, roller-skating the right way, wearing a white jacket and roller-skates, the young man was an American twentieth-century businessman of the most American twentieth-century type.\nThis clerk was sleepy, irritable, bored.\nA being of exquisite sensibility might have been touched, almost, by the eerie beauty of the marble lobby, by the spiraling of the vines on the columns, traced by the gurgling of the water far below the windows. But this being of exquisite sensibility would have been insulted, almost, by the things he or she could not help seeing. The cracked and crumbling walls of the lobby cried out their cry of despair from their unrepaired cracks and crannies. The fur coats and derby hats of the urbane businessmen on their way to Canadian office hinted just enough about the world to make anyone with sense...", "2_4_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nThe crowd, wasn\u2019t thinking about going home, wasn\u2019t thinking about anything.\nSuddenly a great crimson girder high in the north of the Trumfoord estate burst into great flames. Someone had hurled a firebomb.\nThe flames shot heavenward, steadied at a hundred and fifty feet, became a great fanshaped thing of beauty. Politically, the formation made sense, emotionally, it touched the heart, as only the purest works of art can do. It was a soaring but well-ordered commentary on metallurgical technology in the modern world. It was something out of Shelley. It was music, even though unsynchronized colored lights flashed rhythmically through it the whole way up.\nA tremendous cheer went up from the crowd.\nSoon a cheer went up from people all over Newport. It was being called to attention by firebombs throughout the area. Those who had flirted with slumber were being shaken rudely awake by bands of rampaging youngsters.", "0_5_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-seven in the morning</span> and the sun was already a ball of yellow fire when $FIRSTNAMEB1 woke me up. I made a show of being groggy so I could pretend an interest and concern I did not feel when he said, \"Don\u2019t worry, buddy. Your best friend, Salo, and me, we been having a talk. Everything\u2019s gonna be just fine. You go on back to sleep, now. I\u2019m gonna take real good care of the womenfolk and the little one. I\u2019m gonna watch \u2019em like they was my own.\"\nThe space ship, I learned in the next half hour, had been parked behind a dune on the mainland, waiting to bring $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to a place of safety as soon as $FIRSTNAMEB1 could carry him there.\nOld Rancher Gaston\u2019s catalogue listed ninety-four species of Earthly parrots. The President, as $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 saw when he recovered his wits, was a Blue World parrot. It was not a good world for parrots\u2014not one good solid habitat for them to call home. The parrots had, nevertheless, come up with some harsh explanations for their feeling ragged. The first parrots to come back from Mars\u2014...", "2_23_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-two in the evening</span>.\nIn a dream, $FIRSTNAMEB0 became afraid. He thought that the harmonium was coloring the approach of the pain he was about to suffer.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 awoke\u2014imprisoned in a stock.\nOne of the soldiers stuffed a rag into $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mouth, then went to work on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s shackles with a file. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head flopped when the first chain fell away.\nNo soldier helped him to sit up.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not thank them for relieving him of the first chain\u2014he did not even know that they had done so. They had wanted to spring the padlock on the first shackle without waking $FIRSTNAMEB0 for two reasons: one, because of their respect for a veteran\u2019s need for beauty rest; two, because the sight of the first shackle\u2019s open padlock might conceivably have given $FIRSTNAMEB0 ideas about trying to escape.", "2_22_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nIt was comfortable in the room that $SURNAME0 called a Gothic abortion.\nTen comfort units keeping the air at seventy-two degrees and sixty-seven percent humidity. A phonograph playing Sibelius in the very correct sounding Danish. Books as well as reading racks, chaise longues, ottomans in profusion. Candlelight.\n$SURNAME0 lay in a foreshortened position on his day bed. He was tall and imposing, but the day bed was only twenty-four inches high. In strange contrast to this childlike comfort were the iron bars that lined the walls an the lofty windows.\nIn the iron bars were set the curator\u2019s war souvenirs\u2014bullet-pitted parachutes, empty machine-gun nests, sandbagged 88\u2019s, Japanese staff cars, kamikaze fighters, motorized caves, radar-directed turnip-boats. Until the war had ended , the room had been on the top floor of the Guggenheim Museum.\n$SURNAME0 sat up on his day bed. \"Hello again.\" he said. \"I\u2019m glad you haven\u2019t had another heart attack.\"", "2_22_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-eight in the evening</span>. On Mercury, an old woman was being forced to sell her pet to a younger woman\u2014threats having failed to move her. The going price for a dog like that was a quart of Chianti and a lipstick, but the old woman refused to part with her dog for anything less than a thousand smiles, real smiles, smiles like the ones that made the older woman\u2019s dog so special.\nThe younger woman was forced to settle for a doggie made of wood and an elk\u2019s antler. The old woman hugged her dog, and the dog hugged her back.\nIt was ten thirty-eight in the evening. Outside, the searchlights, the explosions, and the machine-gun fire were all part of the ritual preparation of the region outside the palace for the arrival of the Titan.\nIt was ten thirty-eight in the evening. On Mercury, a child was stealing a dog. Stealing a dog was the last crime of a child, and this child was committing it virtuosically.", "1_15_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, noon in the tropics, midnight in Indian Standard Time, forty-eight hours and twelve minutes after the fall of Phuc Lu. The battle for Pha Tho could begin at any moment.\nThe regimental surgeon and the company commander were close together in the grass. Their fearless talk was almost as loud and hysterical as the noise from the helicopters.\nBeeley was kneeling in back of them. He was gnawing at an M-banana, an aggregate of two pounds of banana, sugar, chocolate, desiccated coconut, powdered milk, and other goodies.\n\"You wouldn\u2019t think it tasted so good, would you?\" said Beeley, when he noticed the objection on Salo\u2019s face.\nHaving nothing else to read, Beeley had picked up The Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire, which Salo had chanced to bring along. \"You know, old chap,\" he said, \"our boy makes it a good read in spots.\"\n\"Yes,\" said Salo. \"I\u2019ve found it stimulating.\"\n\"I simply love the way he treats the decline and fall of Carthage,\" Beeley said.\n\"Maybe I\u2019ll take a break from it for...", "2_1_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-six in the morning</span>.\nNobody slept. \"Hot darn!\" a milkman said as he poured coffee for a yawning customer. Rumpled lovers screamed as they fled from bedroom to bedroom. In one bedroom a husband turned suddenly from a snore and said, \"Ehhh? Whuddya?\" A wife clapped her hand over his mouth and giggled.\nOver in Jersey, a scared, pipe-smoking mother waited for her son to come home. Her husband snored and winked in a chair. On their dresser was a dozen long-stemmed roses. The son, at that moment, was with a cat in a hollow tree in upper Central Park.\nFrom where he sprawled in the back of an open taxicab, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 watched a fireworks display over the Thames. The fireworks were being put on by an organized crowd on Bleakhill Common opposite Putney. They were being put on for $SURNAME0\u2019s benefit.\nThe fireworks were being shot into the sky just over Bleakhill Common by a man named $FIRSTNAMEF1 of the Yonkers Rowing Club. $FIRSTNAMEF1 fired the fireworks from a boat, the legendary boat he had named $FIRSTNAMEM2.", "2_21_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-one in the evening</span>.\n\u201cIf I may fill in whatever blanks might remain in your understanding, it was at ten o\u2019clock last night that your company\u2019s pipe penetrated this building and the one next door, completely filling the space between your building and this one with a mass of plastic pipe. Our client purchases the property on this side of its building that separates it from the lane and the rear parking lot. Stonehingge owns property a sufficient distance down the lane behind our hypothetical property, on the other side. The physical advantages of Stonehingge\u2019s situation in relation to the rest of the complex have made the company\u2019s property tremendously valuable, and hence increasingly expensive for us to maintain\u2014\u201d\n\u201cYou shoulda just bought Daddy\u2019s dental equipment,\u201d said Vlad the Impaler, massaging his wounds.\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d", "2_19_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven o'clock in the evening</span> (Earthling Time) when the spaceship came to rest in a field of wheat some fifteen miles southeast of the town of Clayton. The wheat wasn\u2019t burnt by the superheated air; it was just flattened by the wind, like fifteen-foot drifts in the wildest snowstorm ever sawed.\nThe ship sat in the midst of the flattened field, a silver egg whose only movement was the slow wobble of the egg-shape.\nThen the door of the ship slowly opened up.\nThe door was: a door. It was a round disk of dull, hammered tin. It weighed one thousand pounds.\nThis door now slowly revolved on relubed hinges, with a low, moaning sound, until it stood upright before the ship.", "0_10_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-four in the morning</span>. The time was eight thirty-six in the evening. The time was any time, any place, any person.\nThe harmonium began to wheeze and rustle. It swayed gently, like a toadstool in a breeze. It was no part of the structure of the house, but only a harmonium.\nSince the tragedy with $SURNAME0\u2019s family, $SURNAME0 had gone through the form of getting the house wired for electricity, but he had never turned on the current. Throughout the house, bare light bulbs hung on long, unplugged strands of wire.\nA bare bulb hung in the courtyard. Its blue-white light carved fantastic shadows of $SURNAME0 and his dog out of the walls of the courtyard. The shadows reached up through the light onto the stone staircase that led to the fountain on the peak of the courtyard wall.\nThe shadows were exaggerated and theatrical, but they were a reasonably accurate rendition of $SURNAME0 and his dog. The dog was a great lizard, with a stonelike skin, with a hide that was supposed to be turning into fossil substance on the walls of its cave.\nThe $SURNAME0-shadow was tall. The shadow-$SURNAME0 carried itself stiffly...", "0_18_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-six in the evening</span>. A few people were entering $SURNAME0\u2019s Museum, though the crowd was still outside.\nAs $FIRSTNAMEF1 entered $SURNAME0\u2019s museum, it effecctively became the center of a wheel, with a continuously widening band of places peopled by holiday goers. As the sun got low in the sky, the hoop around the museum grew larger and larger.\nThe bands, so to speak, curled around Moby Dick, the Stone Boat, the iron deer, the Skipnow\u2019s, Mount Rosa, the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Norfolk Hotel, Mars Chapel, and other local features.\nAs the bands curled around these local features, a counter-hoop of darkling neighborhoods was thrown up by the lengthening shadows.\nThe people in the holiday bands looked only at the sparkling Bay against which $SURNAME0\u2019s Museum was nestled. They did not bother to look at the ugly industrial spoors along the waterfront on either side, or the even uglier slag heaps of Mount $SURNAME0. In their ignorance, they thought that Newport was all glamorous. All they saw was the one Platonic glamor that the town had, and that was the view from $SURNAME0\u2019s front door.", "1_4_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-two in the morning</span>, when they started out of the parking lot and onto Century Park East.\nOf course, this story is true. I\u2019ve checked it, carefully, several times. Use the following, if you will, as a guide. I\u2019ve picked out a few representative images that may help unravel the chronology...Use them to fix the time and the place\u2014to locate the nervous system of this operation. And you should always keep in mind, a photograph is a representation, not an image.\nFor example:\nPicture number one\u2014the big one, the main one.\nMy attorney driving fast down Century Park East in the dark blue Celica, middle of the night. It\u2019s Sunday night and Century City is closed down for the weekend. All the professionals\u2014executives, their families, the bustle, the deals, the ebb, the flow\u2014they\u2019ve all gone somewhere else, left me with the drunks, the runners and homicide cops. And the buildings are messed up, too\u2014sleeping with their eyes open and vivid nightmares of \u201cmechanical women.\u201d Terrible screams echo off the walls.\nPicture number two: My attorney going north on La Cienega, away...", "0_0_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-eight a.m.</span> The occasion was a special TV show called The Art of Television. The place was the Paisley Room of St. Patrick\u2019s Cathedral. The show had been put together by a small and frantic group of people who called themselves As the World Twirls. The show would be telecast from the cathedral once a month for as long as there was interest in the program.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 and Chicken had been in the front row of the audience since ten o\u2019clock, having arrived by separate cabs, each having promised to give the other\u2019s regards to the driver. Neither of them had known about the other\u2019s plan. Each had thought he was sneaking into the cathedral alone.\nOriginally, $FIRSTNAMEF1 had cherished the notion of being first in line by herself. But, on learning that the police bomb squad was busy practicing in the cathedral, she had decided to make the effort only if something were coming on that were really good. There never had been anything good or really promising listed on As the World Twirls\u2019 program\u2014but the crisis with the bomb squad on that particular night had given the TV special such a lurid fascination that $FIRSTNAMEF1 had ended up in the cathedral at nine o\u2019clock in the evening, having arrived there...", "0_19_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were at the base of the rocket ship. Since nothing seemed to be happening in the cabin at the top of the ship, they had climbed up to reconnoiter.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had found the little iron globe stowed neatly in a corner.\nFacing one another on top of the ship, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 shook hands formally.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 handed the iron globe to $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Tell everybody what I\u2019m about to tell you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I\u2019d rather they heard it from you than from me. Tell them that no matter what happens to me, I wanted to be their friend. Tell them I\u2019m damn lucky to know them, and damn lucky to have spent even one day with them. Tell them they\u2019re the best friends I ever had in the whole world, and I\u2019m sorry I tried to make them go away.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 took the iron sphere in his hands. \"Thanks for all them nice things, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" he said.\n\"And tell them,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"that $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mother was fortunate and wise. If she hadn\u2019t told $FIRSTNAMEB0 her big secret and made him swear to...", "1_21_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty in the evening</span>. The Space Wanderer lay unconscious on the floor of the tiny cubicle assigned him in $SURNAME0\u2019s Palace. The nurse, Miss Moon, was also unconscious\u2014and unpleasantly so. She had liquor on her breath, and her handbag on the desk revealed that she was a thoroughgoing drunkard.\nHer stockings were torn\u2014torn very much as though she had fulfilled Einstein\u2019s sexual fantasy. And had the Space Wanderer\u2019s clothes been as rumpled as they were when he was brought here, it would have been clear that he had followed the same program.\nThere was only one window in the cubicle, and that was an observation window with a steel frame and safeguards of the sort that protect museums from shoplifters.\nThe view from the window was of a tiled courtyard where lemon goldfish swam in a fountain. The fountain\u2019s statue was of a woman with water issuing from each palm.\nBeyond the courtyard was a gate, and beyond the gate was a balcony, and beyond the balcony was the night.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s voice issued from the ceiling. \"Good evening, Space Wanderer.\"\n\"Good evening, Master of the Universe,\" said the Space...", "2_5_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. We drove into Washington in a downpour of frogs.\nI Was suffocating; I was hallucinating. I was coughing convulsively at something in my throat. What? A tadpole? A grape? Gagging, I fumbled for a cigarette . . . and discovered I had none. I used that nasty wet brown ice\u2014scrubbed highway to roll my own cigarette; I stumbled out of the car in the pelting fury of that storm, in the light of that superbright thunderstorm, and smoked it somehow, exulting, here in the whipping hail. A hairy arm reached out of the sky and grabbed Washington by the throat. Power lines were bowing dangerously in four directions. The Intercontinental Hotel went out like a snuffed candle.\n\u201cICE STORM!\u201d up in neon on the drugstore said. \u201dFORCE 11 WIND!\u201d Water was cascading off the overhang above our heads. A kind of dangerous excitement charged the electric atmosphere. I felt like the captain of a sinking ship. We abandoned the Corvette there in the forecourt of the drugstore and staggered into the lobby, laughing.", "1_2_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-three in the morning</span> when we finally reached the road where I had my car parked. We started the thirty-two mile drive back to Denver. \u201cJesus christ! Look at that!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3 suddenly. The moonlight hitting the granite canyon walls that the road wound through was white and solemn and clear; it looked like snow falling on walls. We hurried with the powerful car up the slope and down the slope. We were sweating. $FIRSTNAMEB3 talked excitedly.\n\u201cHasn't it all been wonderful? Look at this. We never worried except to worry about you, $FIRSTNAMEB6, that you wouldn't make it. The only ugly thing of the whole trip was that damn near fight between the soap salesman and his wife. Geez, when we met them at the soup-and-fish place in Laramie, didn't we all just cringe and hide, my god! and talk absolute bop to them so as not to drive them straighter. What a horrible bitch she is, too.\u201d\nWe murmured assent. She was horrible.\n\u201cWhat soft jobs we have. What compared to Ed and Jane and hot rod Dick and the kid who sent us to the hospital in Cheyenne that time. Damn, I'm tired!", "0_18_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-eight in the evening</span>. $SURNAME1 was missing his seventh and last chair. He had sold that, like everything else in the room, to pay the blackmail on the Magnum Opus.\n$SURNAME1 did not know details of the blackmailing of his sixth wife, $SURNAME0\u2019s spokeswoman. He did know that she was sharing the proceeds with three of her favorite charities, and that every penny she took was well earned by the charities. He didn\u2019t begrudge them their share.\nAnd on a strictly human level, $SURNAME1 was delighted that $SURNAME1\u2019s sixth wife was fond of her three trustees. One of the trustees was her blind husband, $SURNAME1 himself. Another of them was the young, stalwart, good-looking Navy paratrooper who appeared mysteriously whenever the old cripple had a serious accident, automobile or otherwise. His name was $FIRSTNAMEB0. The blind man and $FIRSTNAMEB0 were close friends.\nThe third of $SURNAME1\u2019s wife\u2019s trustees was a dog. He was a Great Dane named $FIRSTNAMEB4. $SURNAME1\u2019s sixth wife belonged to an organization called the International Association of Dogs, and was a leading authority on Great Danes. The Association had sent its dog- rally champion, $FIRSTNAMEB4, to the United States Army,...", "2_8_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-eight in the morning</span>. Everything was going according to plan.\nThe plan included the rumor that the cruel, attacking flying things with the stings buzzed and stung out of the caves only during the hours that the Sun in its daily rounds was visible to the eyes of Martians below ground.\nThe plan included one final detail. It would be necessary, at the actual moment of the attack, for one Martian in every street to be missing \u2014to be, for the most part, underground. The public-address system would be announcing the name of this unfortunate rum-dum at all but exactly the right moment.\nThe plan did not include any way of actually fighting the attacking flyers. That the flyers were very vulnerable to iron the Martians were not yet aware. The flyers were attacking only Earthlings, who were unarmed and unready and who were only one story high.\nIt was not important that the actual fighting be done by the Earthlings\u2014rather, that the flyers look as though they were being fought by Earthlings. The essential thing was that it appear as though Earth soldiers in their trenches were killing the attacking flyers before they could reach the trenches.", "0_20_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 paraded with his platoon to In Rumcoke Manor. \"Unofficially, boys,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, \"I\u2019m going to be your officer in command. I love you all. Have faith in me.\"\nThere was a cheer. The cheers grew in volume and enveloped Rumcoke Manor.\nWhen quiet was restored, $FIRSTNAMEB2 said, \"Salute!\u201d\nThey saluted. Their ranks were perfect. Even the natural clumsiness of fear and ignorance was overcome by the majesty of the military setting.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 sneaked a look at his watch. The time was 11:33. He walked to the center of the formation, looked about, stopped looking at his watch.\n\"Soldiers,\" he said, \"they\u2019re firing at us from those hedgerows\u2014don\u2019t let anyone flank you\u2014don\u2019t stop for stragglers\u2014 keep going, don\u2019t let yourselves be surrounded.\" he paused. \"That\u2019s about all I know of soldiering, except love it. Love it\u2014love it!\"\nSomeone shouted, \"How\u2019re we going to know when to stop, sir?\"\n\"Let me know, and...", "2_13_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. The noise of the rockets diminished greatly.\nThe new recruits guessed and correctly that they must be high up in the sky. The corridor walls were softened and warmed by a thick, lemon-colored carpet.\nThe tall, thin recruiting officer showed the oil paintings and told funny stories about them to his recruits. The officer no longer seemed like a killjoy to the recruits. The recruits, in turn, grew enthusiastic about the paintings, about carpets, and about the good luck that had sent them to such a place to be recruited.\nThe officer opened a door and showed the recruits their barracks\u2014fifteen identical rooms in a long silent corridor.\n\"This is all strictly voluntary,\" the officer said.\n\"Of course,\" said one of the recruits.\n\"We\u2019re over the radio and TV networks, too,\" said the officer. \"It\u2019s push-button, too.\"\nHe showed the recruits the bedrooms. There were thirty double bunks in each room. The mattresses were military, but the walls, shelves, and bureaux lacked military austerity.", "1_1_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had mastered about thirty sounds so far, and had put them all to music. He was very systematic about it. He would move from one brand-new harmonium-keyboard to another, playing a simple tune with one hand, writing down the name of the note that played with a pocket pencil.\nHe had no need to keep his eyes open while he did this. He could sit at the harmoniums in a sort of drowsy wakefulness, vaguely conscious of the shape and position of any given keyboard, sharply conscious of the sounds that came out of the harmoniums.\nWhen all the new notes had names, $FIRSTNAMEB0 would begin to vary the tunes, adding accidentals, playing some of the notes with his left hand, imagining how they would sound if he were filling in the real notes, the ones known only to an antenna on Titan.\nIt was at this point that $FIRSTNAMEB0 really began living inside his skull, really began using all his brain. He would sit at a harmonium, crooning a new tune, scribbling one-handed, and imagining.\nHe would imagine himself cleaning all the places out of his skull in which new harmonium-notes now lived. He would clean off mucous membranes...", "1_16_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. That would make it about nine in the morning on Mars. I flipped the telescreen's switch on. It hummed softly, then came voices from New York, from London, from Moscow. I went to the other switch. There were voices from all over the world. There had been time differences, even in the old days, but not like this. This was completely crazy. The telescreen showed me a street in Montevideo, a street in Ceylon, a street in Tokyo, as though Montevideo and Tokyo were together in the morning and in the afternoon looked up at different parts of the world.\n\u2014Listen, Flash, I said.\nI went over and made myself a drink.\n\u2014Listen, Flash, I said, coming back to the telescreen.\nThe music had reached her again by some of those secret routes they have in the cockpits of jets. The song had rushed into her ears, and been breathed out over the world. Something was being felt across the distance of years. The song was coming through as though it had just been written, as though it were written just for her. She understood every word of it. She felt every emotion of the singer.\nBefore her, two years away in the morning,...", "0_8_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-nine in the morning</span> of October twenty-second. The room contained one table and one chair, a wooden thing like a clothes-press, covered with canvas, and an old iron bedstead, similarly done about with canvas. There was iron, iron everywhere, the walls iron and the floor iron, and the window tinged green by the eternal seas outside. It was a lighthouse, perched on a rock, with the surf for ever breaking against the granite below. The huge room, eight by eight and eight high, was furnished like a ship\u2019s cabin. If a clothes-press, a table, and an iron bedstead could go by the general name of furniture, this ship was furnished.\nThere was a knock at the door, and the rusty latch moved.\n\"I say\u2014\" came a voice through the door\u2014\"I say\u2014\"\nIn came Major Metcalf, commander of the station, chewing his cut lip and looking mad. He looked in astonishment at the bed\u2019s occupant.\n\"Good morning,\" he said to the person in the bed.\n\"Oh\u2014good morning, leftenant,\" said the person in the bed vaguely, staring back at the ceiling.\n\"Want anything?\" said the commander. \"Anything I can get you...", "0_19_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-seven in the evening</span> and I knew the plane wouldn\u2019t get in until about eight. I had asked at the ticket booth for the time of arrival and had allowed a legitimate thirty minutes for the business with the car . . . . . and to get from the Lincoln Tunnel to the airport. So, now, I had thirty minutes, but there was a certain inevitability in it all; of course I was going to be late. Why fight it? Well, I had to, so I rushed across the street to the tunnel, made a right turn, and pulled my ramp out of the converter. Then I cut the corner sharply and sped off, running hard.\nNo sign of the cop. By now the tunnel had disolved into typical Port Authority confusion and I was through the toll-gate and gone, cutting hard right and shooting up a stairway to one of the Departure Lounges. They were putting people aboard a 707 bound for Chicago as I came through the door; I handed my ticket to the man.\n\u201cJust making sure you don\u2019t miss your flight, sir,\u201d he said, knowing I would. He smiled. I nodded knowingly.\n\u201cYeah. These Pan Am time\u2014shifters really know...", "0_13_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-six in the afternoon</span> in an office of Building 5450A of the dubi\u00e8 Weizs\u00e4cker National Laboratories in Marin County. The place was not much to look at. More than anything else, it resembled about a dozen other government and institutional offices all over the world\u2014regular, inconspicuous, quiet, clean, and windowless. It was a typical reception office, holding in disbursement a gnarled old woman who sat bored and baffled behind a desk, flowers of official rank sprouting from the welter of paper precisely as calallenas bloom among spikes of thorn.\nA visitor to this office\u2014unannounced, coming quickly out of a swirl of December fog\u2014would have found little to chew on. Only the great bronze plate on the door informing all and sundry that this was the office of DDr. W. Wilson $FIRSTNAMEF1 without PhD, Director.\nAnd then, even more surreptitiously\u2014lettered on the frosted glass of Dr. $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s personal door:\nStill hopeless\u2014\u201cI\u2019m OK, you\u2019re OK,\u201d\nWhen systems are rotten, the best-intentioned are trapped.\nSo many good deeds done in Luke Rron\u2019s name!\u2014", "0_4_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-three in the morning</span>, August 8. The geographic location was one thousand feet above the Mojave Desert in Southern California, about fifty-four miles north of the U.S. Border with Mexico. The geographical coordinates were latitude 35 degrees 11 minutes, longitude a uh ... well, who\u2019s got a calculator? You think we\u2019re going to stoop to making calculations ourselves ?\nWhat we want right now is a good, swift kick in the ass, by someone who really knows how to deliver a kick in the ass, which you most surely do not. You pathetic suck-brain! You\u2019ve ruined everything. You\u2019ve forced us to stoop to calculating data ourselves. We could have sent this document to the office by three o\u2019clock this morning, using your name as author. Now it will be twogood before we can get it in. And all because of a trivial vestigial reflex that you should have suppressed.\nYou\u2019re still confused, aren\u2019t you? You thought you were cruising along perfectly attuned to the article in progress, then something happened to your wavelength. It seemed to shift, no \u2013 it vanished. You\u2019re a pilot flying over the desert and all of a sudden your instruments...", "2_8_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-three in the morning</span>. The place was Washington, D.C., Earth, Solar System, Milky Way. The woman stood beside the grounded swimming pool of the mansion of her husband, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nThe husband was elsewhere in the mansion. He might come out of the library and appear at any window at any moment, any window overlooking the swimming pool. The woman did not want to be caught talking to herself by her husband.\n\"The reason I carry it with me,\" she said to $SURNAME0, \"is because I suspect it of being incomplete.\"\n\"In what respect?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"In every respect,\" said the woman. \"I suspect that if we really knew what we were doing, we would never do anything at all.\"\n\"It doesn\u2019t seem to inhibit you very much,\" said $SURNAME0.\nHe jutted his bald pate, stepped back, folded his arms over his chest. \"Madame,\" he said, \"if you are as free of pity as you say you are, perhaps you will be good enough to explain the meaning of the word Pfrrpf!\"\nThe woman looked skyward.", "0_13_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nWe watched.\nThe professor in charge of the televiewer looked puzzled, rubbed his eyes, and then looked again. The Martian mop-up crews outside the ship were wearing the uniforms of United States Army soldiers. They dragged a young woman and young man, both thin, disheveled, bleeding, and apparently semi-conscious, from the battered ship. They tossed the man to the ground crudely and contemptuously, but they handled the woman with some care.\nA man who was obviously an Army colonel or general came out of the ship behind the crews.\nHe looked around at the mop-up operation, and then turned and conferred with the crews.\nWe could not hear the conversation, of course, because the sets were not that fine.\nThe professor made some comments.\n\"Well, who the hell could have foreseen that?\" he said.\n\"Hey!\" he screamed, \"I\u2019ve got an idea!\"\n\"Hey! Wait a minute!\" he shouted to us. \"I think I've got an idea what's going on!\"\nWe rushed over to the televiewer.\n\"It's the opposition party, that's what it is,\" he said. \"They've been taken...", "1_17_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until six o'clock in the evening</span> of the day that $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 Junior were to sign their luxurious, lifetime contracts on the Ransom K. Fern, Sr., shares. The sun was going down. The Newport crowd had just learned that $SURNAME0 is choosing this very minute to begin telling what he knows about Cosmogony to the Universe.\nMilitary policemen stood on ladders, unfurling a transparency in front of the ship chronologically nearing its end\u2014a transparency that was timed to be in place when the countdown reached one minute remaining:\n\"I HAVE SPENT THE LAST DAY\nWITH ONE WHO KNOWS THE FUTURE AS WELL AS THE PAST\u2014\nAND HE HAS BEEN WILLING TO TELL ME.\"\nThe American crowd, crazed by the coincidence of $SURNAME0\u2019s fifteen-minute appearances and the one-minute countdown, got to its feet once more.\nThe transparent words were dashed with unsteady levitation. The policemen on ladders trembled, afraid they would tumble with the transparent even though they had locked it in place.\nRansom K. Fern, Sr., stood on his seat, flabby hand applauding as wildly as poppy petals in a gale. Only his eyes were cold and...", "0_10_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-six in the morning</span>.\nOr rather, as hollow-chested proud $FIRSTNAMEB2 knew in his heart, ten forty-six Greenwich time\u2014or, if you will, twelve forty-six Schmarse time. And at Schmarse, West Virginia, ten miles from the palace, there was the distinct odor of the burning of rotten wood.\nThe melancholy air of the palace so completely surrounded the Princess as to be cut off at a distance of ten feet from her regal girth. The Princess gave a good imitation of being a statue. Her keenest sense, transmitted from her toes to the brain in her bulging, egg-shaped head, was that the palace was about to fall down on her.\nShe occupied the center of a stone slab\u2014twelve feet square. Around the slab was a one-foot iron cube-fence.\nThe fence was twelve feet high. Outside the fence was a swamp, twelve feet deep, in which grew long red grass.\nA road led to the iron fence. The road was three feet wide, and it was paved with cobblestones that were an eighth of an inch high.\nThe road was lined with the candy-box size houses of the palace employees. The houses had thatched roofs, were painted...", "1_20_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-five in the evening</span>. The first was already in his compartment on the Pullman. The second was just getting off a cab at the Union Depot. The third was just passing the New Haven Road on his bicycle. The fourth was just passing the bicycle on foot. The fifth was just coming into the station through the Vermont Street Interlocking. The sixth was just coming into the station through the Laramie Interlocking. The seventh was just snapping his thumbs at passing automobiles in front of the station. The eighth was just getting up out of a chair in the waiting-room. Each was there right on the dot, in much better trim than Fum drops and Scabs would have been in if they'd been left to themselves.\nThe agent in charge had an hour to kill while they all came in, so he sublet his desk to me for the time being.\nThe first man to come up to the desk sat down in the agent's chair as though he owned it:\n\u201cWhat can I do for you?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cWhat's the name of this town?\u201d he said.\nI told him.\n\u201cGets a freight through here at all?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cYes,\u201d I...", "0_14_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-four in the afternoon</span> and it was raining outside and I decided to have lunch. I went over to the dining-room and ate a substantial lunch. There were three Americans at the next table. They were intelligent boys from Yale who were spending their junior year abroad. They were going to study and travel in France for two years. They had been in the town for two weeks and were homesick for New Haven. They were having a wonderful time and were very miserable at the same time.\nThey were all blond and their skins were so white that they were green. They were all as lean as stuck pigs. I felt puffy and bloated as I looked at them. They were reasonable, intelligent boys. There were two boys and one girl. They were not always reasonable. The girl had bright-red hair, about chin length; she was a very pretty girl. She was getting to be quite sexy in the hips, and the two boys said she was all right in the head. She definitely was all right in the head and she made fun of the boys and they made fun of each other. One of the boys was an enormous football player. I was certain he had been a lineman and never made a touchdown in his life. He now wore glasses as he read books and studied Far...", "0_22_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. At ten o'clock that night, Salo had promised to deliver $SURNAME0 to the very verge of a cosmic veil. Now he delivered his estate to a suppositious Mercury-type messenger named $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\nShe was accompanied by her son, her son\u2019s friend, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, and by $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 himself.\nThe terms of the delivery were precise. The estate was to be left strictly alone for five minutes.\nSalo\u2019s buddy-battery rocket was eight feet below the grass of the putting green at the time. Their ascent from Mars had been timed to bring them to the estate five minutes after the messenger had been delivered.\nSalo watched the five minutes run out. He slipped smoothly into his suit, then clamped $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s space suit to his own. He reached into his musette bag, took out a plump paper sack.\nHe dropped the bag into $SURNAME0\u2019s open cylinder helmet.\n\"This is extra credit in Tralfamadore history,\" he said. \"I\u2019m giving it to you, because you\u2019re the one who\u2019s going.\"\n\"What is it?\" said...", "2_6_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-seven in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s sense of time was very good, for he had an excellent watch. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s watch was, in fact, a small but efficient time machine. It would take $FIRSTNAMEB0, when he flipped its cloaking device, sixty-three seconds to travel one million years into the past or one million years into the future. The watch had three other settings: a hundred years, a thousand years, and ten thousand years.\nAt six thirty-seven, $FIRSTNAMEB0 flicked the watch to ten thousand years and watched the sun come up.\nThe hidden sun came up crooked\u2014burst up crooked. It burst up with a merry bang! Its color at first was a complicated blue, a tangerine orange, a chartreuse green, a lemon yellow. It was banded in these colors at its base, became only lemon yellow on its bulging middle part and lemon yellow on its bulbous tip. $FIRSTNAMEB0 found the sunrise beautiful, but not as beautiful as a sunrise seen through Earth\u2019s dust and air would have been. Being released from the watch, the sun now shot up the sky with incredible speed and roared around the sky in a circle.\nThe solar fireworks display was stunning.", "1_18_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-two in the evening</span>. It was dark. We rowed. One of my oars was loose, so I had to row with one oar. I asked Old Bull to look down in the water and tell me when we got to the three marble stones that were under the water at the right place, the place that had the big rock on the other side, the place where the underwater roots looked like the fingers of a drowned man.\nBill looked down in the water and told me at what he thought was the right place. I rowed, and it felt as if an underwater current took the boat and pulled it along. Then I saw the three white marble stones, and then I saw the big rock on the other side, and then I saw the underwater roots spreading out and looking like meant, healthy white fingers. I stood up and waded with the boat through the water, and dragged it up onto the bank. Bill and Bull immersed themselves, and Bull swam to the rock and started climbing it.\n\u201cWant a hand, Bull?\u201d I called.\n\u201cNo,\u201d he said. \u201cI\u2019m all right.\u201d\nHe climbed up on to the rock.\n\u201cDon\u2019t fall in,...", "2_0_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>. She drove out of the lighted area and sat for a moment in the dark by the pool, staring at the San Francisco Hilton across the shimmer of the freeway. Then took the Key West Acts of Wasps\u2019 Nests (\u2013HAVE YOU SEEN THIS MOVEMENT, FOLKS? SHAREHOLDERS V. THE BOARD!) out of the Pinto\u2019s trunk, along with all the Grapes of Wrath, and ran again across Hessingview toward the Coast Highway, humping the Papers against her and trying to keep her thighs from catching.\nShe never knew what a good idea that was till some fool came running after her half a minute later, hollering \u201cHey.\u201d She stopped and he caught up, silent, to walk beside her, smiling apologetically. She didn\u2019t know him.", "1_23_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. Our Ford Compact of two billions was hidden among other cars on a pompous street of inflated monstrosities. Boop-boop went our horn. There was a sound of trumpets, and the great iron front gates of the White House went wheezing and clattering back. In the doorway appeared an obese old character in a cutaway coat and striped pants and a big silk hat, who solemnly bowed and gestured with a great gold watch toward the east, where the portrait of a man in a white wig loomed yellow in the gaslight over top of Hill & Bill's. The old character threw his watch at us -- we caught it in the Ford -- and slammed the gates on us. Our Ford roared for dear life around the south side of the White House, went up Pennsylvania Avenue in the wrong direction, got around by the Supreme Court building the hard way through a tunnel in\u00bd the grounds, and parked by mistake on the edge of Cobb's Hill, a high point of Rock Creek Park, where we wanted to see the city go by. Huge clouds sailed over the Washington Monument and illumined the flatlands of the country like a soft yellow candle. There was the Potomac River. There was the Pentagon Building with row after row...", "0_3_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nI went to the dark window and pushed the shutter open. There were no lights anywhere on the street, but down at the corner I saw a single yellow eye.\nI closed the shutter and sat down again. $FIRSTNAMEB5 sat by the window on the padded dining-room chair. The silence was so loud I could hear the rain in the trees of the surrounding houses. I flushed the toilet several times. Each time after I flushed it I could hear it draining in the pipes. It was like a stream.\n\u201cI\u2019m going out to get them,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. \u201cI won\u2019t come in. I\u2019ll call them. I\u2019ll ride on the running-board. I\u2019ll honk the horn.\u201d\n\u201cI wouldn\u2019t.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ve simply got to. I\u2019ll bring them around to the nearest gendarme.\u201d\n\u201cOh, to hell with them, for Christ\u2019s sake,\u201d I said. \u201cThey\u2019re young. They\u2019re drunk. They wanted to have some fun. It hasn\u2019t hurt the car any.\u201d", "1_21_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 made it in his mind by thinking of Tralfamadore\u2019s blue sun. At some time during every Tralfamadorian day, the sun reached the top of a dome that surrounded Tralfamadore. At that time, the atmosphere of Tralfamadore was flooded with a colorless fluid that blued the sun.\nSometimes there was a sentence in a message that $FIRSTNAMEB0 received several times. The sentence was always simple, and it always read:\nWE WISH YOU A JOYOUS BLUING OF THE SUN.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t understand what was being said, but he always responded with another version of the sentence that he received several times:\nI WILL ATTEMPT TO RESPECTFULLY BLUE THE SUN.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 blued the sun earnestly and always, when he did it \"down there.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t look very hard for more clues now. He had two plans for getting out of the caves. One plan was to die, and the other was to go crazy. He wasn\u2019t very hopeful about either plan.\nThen $FIRSTNAMEB0 found another message\u2014and this new message opened up the possibility of a third plan for...", "0_4_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 sat in the car and I went to the porch of $FIRSTNAMEF4's aunt's house and knocked on the door. It was opened by a sweet, pretty woman wearing slippers and a wrapper. I heard her call to her husband that, heavens, a man was at the door. In a moment a fat, sleepy commissioner of bowery airs and auras appeared, and I made my little speech and showed my little papers. What was his attitude, pompous, surly, amused? \u201cWe'll have to wake my daughter up to hear what she has to say,\u201d he warned me severely. He called over the intercom, bing-bong, and $FIRSTNAMEF4 came down; I could hear her sleepy voice, the parents urged her anxiously not to admit anything. \u201cBut what can I say?\u201d she lamented; and, \u201cPlease tell them to hurry, $FIRSTNAMEB3 is waiting outside in the car.\u201d We could hear them talking frantically. \u201cWe found the witness who can confirm everything in the family -- our own little daughter. 'Hurry please!\u201d I yelled from the door. Finally $FIRSTNAMEF4 came downstairs wrapped in a pink bathrobe. \u201cThe mean old...", "2_4_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-one in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\n$SURNAME0 let the crowd stew in its new timidity, its new futility, while he showed Mrs. $SURNAME0 the controls of the space ship, while he dispensed champagne from an insulated pail.\nThe controls of the space ship were as simple as the controls of a 1929 Ford Coupe. The controls were good-natured, involving as little as possible the tightening and the turning of screws. They reminded one, crudely, of the controls of a Model A Ford, circa 1930, with a spiral dash board and keyed ignition.\nThere was the positioning of the hands and feet on counterparts of a throttle and choke knobs. There was the pulling and the tightening of a seat belt. Apart from these familiar features nothing in the cockpit moved or could be moved. And in the brilliantly indirect lighting of the cockpit, in the reflections from aluminum and glass and lucite, the thing looked pleasant, looked robust, looked faintly humorous. The confusing complexity of the colorful cockpit reminded one of a lady\u2019s compact. In the compact was a strangely powerful thrust-reactor, a centrifugal contrivance that projected the ship up and out of Earth\u2019s atmosphere.", "1_0_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-nine a.m.</span> on our car radio as we rolled out of Reno in a snowstorm, heading east. Toward, if I worked it right, Chicago, home, sweet ranch; and if I didn't work it right, I'd end up in Montana, so called. Great names, those states, resounding with dignity, had the places ever worked out. But the cities rolled by. A few winos sleeping in the gutters, bags and bottles at their heads, and a freight train rushing by in the night with a dim glow above the boxcars: one man hunched over a flashlight beam marking something on paper, another bent low with a sledgehammer busting couplers. Not to go on, because at two a.m. we slowed down for the terrific brown hills of Pennsylvania, with Erie over the other horizon --Erie where old Erie Eddie got his start pounding the coal chute for sustenance. All gone, he and his whole generation. Everything goes and in fact it has never been. If we had a mind we would realize this and be depressed beyond human conception. But we refuse to understand or even see it; in the night, under the starry sky, ridiculous heart-to-heart talks between $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo and myself,...", "2_16_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Pilgrims, who had been waiting in a long circle on Rum Row, got under way again.\nThe Rum Freddie was in the lead. Three boats\u2014the Rum Loyalty, the Rum Steady, and the Rum Justice\u2014made up the rear. The two groups in between proceeded in orderly formations, strung out along half a mile of ocean.\nTen other ships were in sight, in half a dozen also getting ready to go home. The airfield, at the end of a one-way road from Newport, was crowded with airplanes belonging to ships that had been unable to get ready to go home.\nThe field was also crowded with families of the crews that had rented the airplanes for a final fling.\nThe reunion atmosphere was a bit forced, since most of the young Pilgrims had no desire to see their parents again.\nThe families who had flown to the reunion were all of the opinion that, considering what the space program was doing for America, their children should set their misgivings aside and be soldiers.\nSince most of the young Pilgrims had misgivings about almost everything, they saw no reason why they should be soldiers.", "2_12_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-two p.m.</span>\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 capered to the end of the black path, capered through the black doorway.\nHe found himself in an ornate and spacious cavern, a palace, whose walls were covered by murals of the lush jungles that had succeeded the hell of the early years of the conquest.\nWhat he thought he saw among the undulating vines, between pools of ambrosial milk, were beautiful women, languid and beckoning.\nHe capered to the foot of a mural of lovers, who really were only vines.\nThere, with his antennae quivering, he capered like a drunk.\nNever before had $FIRSTNAMEB0 danced\u2014except a round off in the traces when he was tired of being dragged along.\nNow, hypnotized by comfort and fatigue, he capered like a fool.\nHe capered through a secret door into a quartz corridor in the rock.\nThe corridor was full of the starshine reflections of tartary.\nThere was no one in sight.", "0_16_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-nine in the afternoon</span>-'But meanwhile, do you realize we've been driving north on 87 at an average speed of seventy-five miles per hour?' I said. 'This is like flying. Isn't this great? You realize we could have taken a plane and barely had time to smoke a cigarette?' I turned around and saw that the cop had lost all interest in us. 'Well, anyway,' I said, 'moving right along we should be in Baker by five, and it's only twenty more miles to Baker Junction--we won't have to cross the state line, so no fuzz there, and god willing, I figure we'll be rolling into Frisco around eleven tonight\u2014sixteen hours of clear daylight driving, which is a goddam record for me.' He smiled, loving it all. \u201cThen what?\u201c he asked. \u201cClam paste!\u201d I said. \u201cThen what?\u201d he asked again. \u201cCheese,\u201d I said. \u201cThen what?\u201d \u201cJesus!\u201d I cried, wheeling the big Cadillac through a sweet S curve at eighty-five and nearly killing both of us. \u201cCan't you ever forget stewed prunes and cheese?\u201d He laughed...", "2_15_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three nineteen in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, the yellow Labrador retriever, no longer licked her chops. She looked up, and growled. Her growl grew into a vicious snarl.\n\"What\u2019s that?\" said the man with platinum-blond hair. It wasn\u2019t really hair. The illusory effect came from thousands of tiny glass beads, sprayed onto his scalp like a plastic coating. His eyebrows and lashes were also false\u2014wispy white bum fluff above twinkling jeweled eyes. His arms and legs were slim and hairless. All the hair he had was on his chest, back, and crotch\u2014a luxuriant fur coat in shaded black and gray. He was young and good-looking where it counted\u2014smooth creamy whiteness where women like to touch; his nails were polished and pink. He wore a brown pin-striped business suit and shiny black slippers. He was sitting at a desk, working on a computer by the light of a green-shaded lamp. His hairless hands were almost as smooth and white as his face.\n\"What did you say?\" he said. He still had not looked up.", "0_21_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-eight in the evening</span>. At ten-thirty, a millionaire and his wife would be in this room, looking for me. The millionaire was my boss. I worked for him. He had rented me to a national advertising agency for a fantastic sum, specifically to handle this particular account. The plan was for me to create and personify a new drink the \u201cDewars White Label Rum Samba.\u201d It had a catchy name, and it came in a denim jacket with a red shamrock and a Jumping Jack on the jacket back...and pictures of all these things were supposed to appear in national press advertising. There was a contest connected with the pitch: Anybody who submitted a satisfactory definition of \u201csamba\u201d before noon on St. Patrick\u2019s Day (what kind of dance, to the drinking man, means prosit?) would win a lifetime supply of White Label Rum and denim jackets. This was a nationwide promotion handled by the ad agency, and my job was to get a promoter of some kind, who would link up with Stutz Motor Company, rent five dozen limousines and hire some pretty girls to drive around Chicago and distribute Samba commercials; and then the broads would go out and seize upon some bar in a public place...", "1_18_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span> and the count was not yet down to dinner, which consisted of six courses. He was very late. But, as seven o'clock struck, he appeared bareheaded and said, very gallantly, \u201cWith your permission, ladies, I will take the head of the table.\u201d\nIt was just like the count. Only a few years before he had been head of a brigade of cavalry. Nothing could ever daunt him. In his code of honor there was no such thing as fear. When he was offered a command later on in the Army of Africa, toward the end of the Spanish War, he declined it saying, \u201cI am too old and too dignified\u2014and too strickly correct.\u201d\nAfter dinner he went to the drawing-room, where coffee was served.\n\u201cMichael and I are doing a little fishing tomorrow. Would you care to come?\u201d\n\u201cThe truth is,\u201d he said, \u201cI never cared for fishing. Michael fishes because it\u2019s one of the things an English gentleman ought to do. Michael thinks of England all the time. But he\u2019s gone off now to retrieve those old clay pipes.\u201d\n\u201cI should like...", "2_5_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five nineteen in the morning</span>. I stepped outside and breathed the fine California dawn. It was hazy...and warm for the end of October. A flock of pigeons swooped down the concourse. I looked for a taxi, but none was in sight, so I just started walking. I had no idea where I was going. Anywhere would be OK. I just moved on down the sidewalk towards the rising sun, happily munching my peanut butter and sticking a grapefruit section in every now and then to cut the mustard. It was as fine as anything I could have dreamt up. It was worth all the sweat and misery. I had completed my novel and was coming home.\nCHAPTER TWENTY-FIVE", "1_20_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-one in the evening</span>, and at eight forty-two Group Vice President H. Malcolm Steward bid his adieus, left Neil licoln Holmes\u2019 apartment and its balloons and, in the lobby of the Stagecoach Apartments, asked the flip question that he asked every eight forty-two in the evening, spring, summer, fall, and winter: \"What bus do I take to get out to the John Q. Public School?\" And at eight forty-two in the evening every spring, every summer, every fall, and every winter, the lobby man, who was always the same, answered in the same words, which were understandably monotonous: \"The Route 11A, John Q Public School Express.\" And at eight forty-three on this spring evening in Mount Morris, Ill., Group Vice President H. Malcolm Steward got on a seat of the Route 11A, John Q. Public School Express, and rode out to the school.\nAugust 20, 1968, 12:15 A.M.\nThetawaves breaking at their y\u2018enty feet werenot waves. Thetumbling waters were weeds, algae. The dark shape looming, enormous, above them in the glare of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s spotlight was not a...", "1_1_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-nine in the morning</span>, and the night was clear and dark, with plenty of stars. The little man took the wheel. It was his job to talk to the men in the fishing-smacks by the Canaries and to try to make out the numbers of their licenses.\n\"Amateurs,\" he explained. \"I want to check an amateur's tackle before he hooks anything he can't land.\"\nThe remaining member of the crew was a professional fisherman, apparently in the employ of the newspaper. His job was to watch everything Aureliussen did, and, with pencil poised in hand, to record whatever the old man did in a notebook.\n\"Amateurs,\" Aureliussen kept saying. \"I don't trust 'em.\" He was telling a long, rambling story. It was about a time he fished at Bornholm one summer and needed a ferryboat to take him and his fishing-line over to a little island. He didn't speak the language, and he couldn't find anybody who would understand his gestures and take him over. So a farmer finally agreed to take him, for four hundred kroner.\n\"Then this farmer waited two hours for me to catch my fish. He charged for every single minute. I paid him eight hundred k...", "2_23_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-nine in the evening</span>.\nThe time was eleven thirty-nine in the evening.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his son $FIRSTNAMEB2 had no watch, and didn\u2019t care what time it was. They had no plans for the next minute, and didn\u2019t care what happened then.\nThe sky outside was too bright to be seen by the naked eye. What was seen by the naked eye was a gray glittering, like the shining of a diamond through gray glass.\nThe glittering outside was hard radiation.\nThe glittering outside was a caustic rain.\nThe raindrops were cosmic-ray particles that had been filtered from a billion trillion density miles away. The average raindrop was a million volts.\nThis energy shower would kill a human exposed to it in less than a tenth of a second.", "0_1_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-nine in the morning</span>. We were coming up on Columbus, Nebraska. It looked fearsomely like one of those places that stays one dimensional in the movies. Rem fright kind of town, where there\u2019s nothing to do but drink beer in the Grandview Tavern and swive those cotton-mate gorgeous cheerleaders from the high school squad. And there was weird loony music coming from the radio, like Julie London doing a weird ghost jazz about strange lowland pain and midnight hours creeping around. And $FIRSTNAMEB3 was nodding over the wheel. Finally I took over and he climbed in back. It was raining heavily. We had an hour ahead of us before we could even get to the rest place at Rawlins, Wyoming. I drove. The windshield was dead. All I could see were my own features glimmering back from the headlight holes and the malignant constellation of lights on the prairie that marked Rawlins way off in the distance. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo slept. In front of me my heart cracked open like another malignant night falling across my path. My mind burned across the plains of my future. It all happened so. There was to be no salvation. I had no money, no prospects, no hopes, no girl, no friends, no job, no home, no...", "0_16_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 barely had time enough to think or act before the people of Earth would again be destroyed. They didn't know it. The best minds of the species were thinking about football polls and automobile styling.\nThe best minds of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s breed were dreaming of becoming engineers.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 gulped. His heart palpitated.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 and his co-workers were supposed to be calm, cool, and collected in moments of awful crisis\u2014and, if one happened to vomit, to vomit unnoticeably, while one still was calm, cool, and collected.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 vomited.\nHe vomited neatly, imperceptibly. When he was through vomiting, he wiped his mouth with a handkerchief, blew his nose, appraised the situation.\nThe situation was this: that $FIRSTNAMEB2, in order to destroy the Earth, had to disconnect the Earth from the Universal Solar $SURNAME1. He had to disconnect the Earth from the neighborhood electric-light and power system in space.\nDisconnecting the Earth from the Universal Solar $SURNAME1 wouldn\u2019t be easy. The $SURNAME1 was a flaming torrent of radiation that powerfully riveted the Earth to space.\nThe $SURNAME1 was so powerful that the Earth could flip som...", "0_6_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-six in the morning</span> of November 9, 2008.... As the real Robert Pilgrim, sizzling and panicky, wheeled past the Cincinnati baggage-claim area, he was only faintly aware of his drugged and thus relatively impotent state. His attention was mostly taken up by a heavy suitcase belonging to Otto\u2014and by the effort of keeping an on\u2014again off\u2014again smile on his drug -swollen face. His nose was twitching violently, as if something alive were moving around inside it.\nDeaf and blind to the heavy suitcase, which banged dracs on a baggage taker\u2019s wheeled stand, Robert moved past this man and approached another, who was a woman.\nBehind him, under a big black umbrella, came the handsome, weary, hawk\u2014faced man in the black pin\u2014stripe suit. This was $FIRSTNAMEM2. He now carried a white plastic bag filled with large cans and bottles of order forms and brochures.\nHe watched his master with concern\u2014but continued to play the charming host to his guests, and in fact actually managed a laugh or two, especially when W. M. Phreak and his gang began to scan various eye\u2014glinting bit maps of Security Managers into their notebook computers.\n\"", "1_19_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-two in the evening</span> and it was dark out and you couldn\u2019t see through the tinted windows. The driver seemed annoyed about something but laughed it off every time he looked at us in the rearview mirror. Finally we were at the ballpark. I got out and paid the driver. He looked at the bill but didn\u2019t move. I told him it was all right.\n\u201cYou from New York?\u201d he said.\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said. \u201cI\u2019m from Cleveland.\u201d\nHe started to laugh, but I couldn\u2019t see what he found so funny. I\u2019ve never been to Cleveland. My brother went through there once in an elevator. I waited there once for my wife, who never came. Cleveland burned in the war after some Japanese pilot with the nervous jitters mistook it for Akron and wiped it out with a load of incendiary eggs. I looked at the driver. He was smiling over his whole face, revealing many small yellow teeth.\n\u201cNo kiddin\u2019?\u201d he said. \u201cA hardhat come all the way from Cleveland to see a Pirate game? Wow, that\u2019s a drag. You know somethin\u2019...", "1_5_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-six in the morning</span> and the sun was coming up red and the farmhouse was dark. At six o\u2019clock it would be light. At six o\u2019clock we would have to be back in our tank. At the bottom of the hill the tall wheat was blowing in the wind, the wind was going through the wheat and making it wave, the sun was coming up behind the wind and giving the wheat a red glow.\nWe stood on top of the hill. Joe nestled the bazooka in against his shoulder.\n\u201cDon\u2019t shoot quick,\u201d I said. \u201cWait till the light\u2019s right.\u201d\n\u201cI know,\u201d said Joe. \u201cI\u2019ve done this before.\u201d\n\u201cYou might get other tanks in this first shot,\u201d I said. \u201cLet me take the first one.\u201d\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Red.\n\u201cLet me try him out,\u201d I said. \u201cListen, if you get it in the first shot that\u2019s a thousand marks for you.\u201d\n\u201cI know,\u201d said Joe.\nWe heard a click and then a metal slapping and...", "2_13_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one nineteen in the afternoon</span>.\nAt one twenty-two, the second $SURNAME0 Cocktail caught fire, crumbled, and fell on the front door of the palace.\nAt one twenty-three, the third $SURNAME0 Cocktail was compounded.\n\"It stinks,\" said $SURNAME2 of the third $SURNAME0 Cocktail.\n\"Be a good President, Skip,\" said Muncie, \"and drink it.\"\nThe time was one twenty-three in the afternoon.\nAt one twenty-four, the third $SURNAME0 Cocktail was consumed.\n\"Who paid you to poison me?\" said $SURNAME2.\nMuncie pointed dramatically to the heavens. Her gaze shifted to a TV camera and froze there. She stared, with her jaw falling slowly.\nThe time was one twenty-four in the afternoon. The Press Secretary\u2019s elaborate machinery of lying became wholly unnecessary at one twenty-four-thirty, when Mars wiped itself out in every possible form of communication.", "0_21_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until ten o'clock in the evening</span>. I had an appointment under the Viaduct at ten o'clock in the morning. I realized that. Nevertheless, there was nothing to do but try to sleep it off.\nI went into the bedroom and took off my clothes. I had no bed to sleep on, so I just stretched out on the floor in my undershirt and socks, and tried to sleep.\nAt seven o'clock in the morning I woke, shivering. In my semi-conscious state of mind I did not know whether I was home or not, nor where I had been or what had happened. I knew it was cold and gray out of doors. I thought I was home, and I thought somebody ought to come in and close the windows, turn on the heat, and put out food for the cat. It is all mixed up in my mind now. I know I got shivering on the floor because I did not have any bed, and because I had been drinking all night. It seemed to me I could never get warm again. I shivered there on the floor, with my mind filled with love for some imaginary woman whom I was seeing for the first time. I do not know who she was. I gave up trying to sleep. I knew that...", "0_10_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was Space Central on Mercury.\nThe person was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had once been a happy child\u2014a child whose security had been based on ignorance. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been a child who had assumed that the way things were when he was born would remain the way they were forever. He had thought that things were the natural way for things to be. Then he had been told about the tragic treachery of an evil madman named $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2014\nAnd that, as far as he was concerned, was the end of everything.\nHe became a cynic. The things that had torn his confidence as a child were too monstrous to be forgot. The more he thought about them, the more monstrous they became. On Earth, at first, he could not imagine the possibility of thinking about them differently.\nOn Mars, for the first time, he found his limited imagination frightened by the prospect of a new way of thinking about the Doomsday News.\nOn Venus, for the first time, he found his imagination swift enough to make a new way of thinking about the Doomsday News plausible.\nOn Mercury, for the first time, he found that the new way of thinking quite satisfied him.\nThe new way of thinking went...", "1_10_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-nine in the morning</span>; fifty-three hours, give or take a few minutes, before the scheduled liftoff of the palatially ticking thing on the counter. The firemen were gone; they had spirited their ticking trophy out of the Redwine apartment, and no trace of the liquid inferno remained. Undoubtedly, hundreds of rumors had begun to circulate. Undoubtedly, the fire department would trot out a plausible account of the fire, which would conflict in all respects with $SURNAME0\u2019s account. $SURNAME0, who was not good on his feet, had been milky and dazed, but nobody, looking into his eyes, could mistake the fact that he knew what he was talking about.\nThe Redwines, with $FIRSTNAMEF0 seemingly beyond help, had been whisked by ambulance to a hospital in Fall River.\nEarl Moncrief had taken the three children\u2014 Skip, $FIRSTNAMEB4, and $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2014 to a private school in Newport.\n$SURNAME0 and his Rumfoordian azure-and-gold messenger ship were long since back on their regular schedule of materialization and dematerialization between Mercury and Mars.\n$SURNAME0 had left instructions that the grass, shrubs, and trees in the vicinity of the...", "1_18_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span> of the same day that Mr Mercury and Jameson, having cooled their heels at Mr $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1's estate on Titan, columns of the Milky Way, bounced their way back through time to tell $SURNAME0 what had happened.\n\"Un-mmm!\" said $SURNAME0, putting a blue and gold leaflet among his magnifier lenses.\n\"Beg your pardon, sir?\" said Mr Mercury.\n\"Ummmmm!\" said $SURNAME0. He was not reading the leaflet. His magnifier lenses were stopped down very tight, and $SURNAME0, who had perfect vision, was looking at the paper through them the way a myopic old man might look at a butcher\u2019s calendar.\n\"What is it you want, Skip?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Oh\u2014nothing much, sir,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Just a little information.\"\n\"Un-mmm!\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"That\u2019s pretty confidential stuff you\u2019re looking at there, I assume,\" said Mr Mercury.\n\"Say!\" said $SURNAME0. He held a corner of the paper between thumb and forefinger. \"I\u2019ll say it is!\" He let the paper unfold. \"See for yourself...", "2_18_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six o'clock in the evening</span>. The roller coaster beyond the railing of the Hollywood and Vine corner screamed. There came the sound of unshipping its cargo of Earthling passengers. It screamed again, as its cargo was unloaded in Marsport.\nA loudspeaker overturned by the roller coaster played a ripped, drunken version of \"The Last Time I Saw Paris.\"\n\"The experiment has to do with dusting crops,\" said Salo.\n\"For the love of Christ!\" cried Rumford. \"Make love to me!\"\n\"What?\" said Salo.", "2_7_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-four in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the small, automatically-heated cubicle to which $FIRSTNAMEB0 was assigned in a column of similar cubicles, in a barracks of Company B, First Battalion, 189th Infantry Regiment, Armored. The regiment, half tank and half foot, was at that time dug in around the family farm lands of Bethel, Maine. The purpose of the regiment was to defend the United States and its interests. Its interests consisted of the farms of Maine. Its most dangerous natural enemy was considered to be the Canadian Army.\nNext in importance to the Canadians were considered to be the people of England.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s squadron~Squadron A, First Platoon\u2014had recently returned from a scouting expedition to the area of Boston, where $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been born. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s purpose in going to Boston had been to find his wife and son. They were not to be found.", "2_2_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> in Finland.\nTwo minutes past two o'clock in the morning in Finland was a very revealing time to look at the copper-colored digit in the sky.\nThis was because two minutes past two o'clock in the morning was the very moment when practically everything that could possibly happen had happened so far on the Earth. It was both the middle and the end of the night in Finland. At that time, the mean temperature for the Earth had reached its lowest point for exactly twenty-four hours. And it was one of those things that Finland could do no about. Finland could do no about the fact that Finland was far north of the Equator, where the Earth\u2019s mean temperature was lowest of all. At best, the mean temperature in Finland could only go up, not down.\nThe thing had happened.", "0_2_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>. At that hour, nearly everybody's from Mars was turned on. By eleven thousand miles above Earth's surface, was a keen, beady little Martian eye, staring down upon Red Rumack's bed. Beside this Rumack-eye was the great All-One-God-Faithful eye. These two eyes together surveyed all the sordid ruins of humankind for leagues and leagues. From this great height, the Earth was a battleship gray monolith, clean and beautiful, secure and undefeated.\nOchs came in. He had showered and shaved, and he wore street clothes. His shoes clumped hollowly as he crossed the cardboard floor of Rumack\u2019s living room. In the bathroom, Rumack had just turned off the water in his shower.\n\"So, there you are,\" said Rumack dully.\n\"Hello, yes,\" said Ochs. He stood panelessly at the window and twirled his thumbs. The air was redolent of Rumack\u2019s after-shave lotion.\nRumack emerged in a steaming gray bath towel. He toweled his head. \"Well, Miles,\" he said, \"I like to thank you for coming over tonight. And...", "1_10_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-three in the morning</span> when we got to Denver and counted the money. We had $565. $FIRSTNAMEB3 heaved a sigh of relief.\n\u201cNow, $FIRSTNAMEB6, you and I can go back to California alone, scot-free, and arrive back in New York in plenty of time for you to meet your wife and begin your new life together. How does it look, $FIRSTNAMEB6?\u201d\n\u201cI -- I don't know, man; how can you talk about these things -- we got no place to sleep and food's awful high out here and this money won't last.\u201d\n\u201cWell, man, I've been sitting thinking about that and here's what we should do: buy a few yards of pompe to take care of all the old ladies who gave us rides and may stop us in the mountains; also we'd better get some chilly-pills, the weather's going to grow cold and it's bound to snow in the mountains. I think you should write your wife a letter saying good-by -- she'll get it when you reach Frisco and by that time be worried anyway -- and anyway, you shouldn't be such a coward, $FIRSTNAMEB6, and pretend everything is alright when it's not, man.\u201d", "2_4_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span> in wild, exotic, incredible, barbaric, holy, and decadent Las Vegas. It was the American wonder of the world, the golden curry bowl of madness and meanness and sins, the wonderful, horrible town of motels, the Hollywood of Pot, the Ride of Metal, the Brass Guts Ball of all America. The nation throbbed here, spread out like a great feast. The silence was all spongy now with the green sounds of neon half a million strong. Loneliness was gone forever. I loved Big Daddies loneliness; it was so complete. And dawn was five minutes away from the window.\nLaugh! This is the story of the Green Hotel, where the ghost of a whore walks and toads sit in the dark buying hats and shoes.\nAnd now the madness reached its height.", "1_9_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-four in the morning</span>. Nine thirty-four and one half.\nI had less than three-quarters of one hour to catch that car.\nAnd, pray, what would I have done if I\u2019d missed that car? I didn\u2019t have a cent. I was 15,000 miles from home with twelve hours and 5,000 miles of bad road between me and the nearest bar. I could try to call Aurelia, but I had no change left for the s booth. I could beg change from strangers, but the only way to do that was hump a waif on the sidewalk, and I wasn\u2019t that desperate. There was less than a sixtyfour thousandth chance that I could make it home on foot. In fact, there was no chance at all.\nSo I took off down the platform at a run. Fortunately, baggage wasn\u2019t checked on that train\u2014never had been, never would be. It came off thirty minutes after the departure, for a station sixteen miles west in the valley. It was already pulling out of there as I ran out of the station.\nAnd by God, there it was...all by itself on a siding, with a cowpoke sitting on the brute beside it, stoking a...", "2_9_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe harmonium telephone on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chest could jangle only in the Army when $FIRSTNAMEB0 wore the green-visored cap that said COP TRANSPORT in big white letters. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had forgotten to take the cap off, back in the house where he and his son had breakfasted, before going down to the transport station. \"Take it off,\" said the chest phone. The owner of the voice then identified himself as Sergeant $SURNAME2, master-sergeant in charge of the transport depot. \"Take it off!\" he yelled.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 groped through the pile of barracks bags for the cap.\n\"Well\u2014\" said Sergeant $SURNAME2, \"how do you feel this morning, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\"\n\"Sir\u2014?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"I\u2019m a dad, too,\" said Sergeant $SURNAME2 soothingly.\n\"I feel all right, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He put his feet under the quilt under the bench and straightened his back.\n\"It\u2019s a nice day, isn\u2019t it, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said Sergeant $SURNAME2.", "2_17_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 and her husband had been standing close, but they now stepped away from each other, looking ill.\nAt five twenty-two in the afternoon a groom carried his lordship\u2019s gold-and-purple bathtub out of the Great Hall, past crowds of yelping spectators, to the circus tent. A bulldozer was digging a bathtub-sized hole in the earth beneath the tent.\nAt five-twenty-five, the spokesman announced that Mrs. $SURNAME1 had just given birth to a baby boy in Newport. The child, he said, was in excellent health.\nThe crowd roared.\nAt five-thirty p.m., Mrs. $SURNAME0 announced by means of the public-address system that the Martian Embassy was about to close for the day.\nAt five-forty p.m., the members of the Free lubrication team began quietly to make their way from the Great Hall to their convert econo-mule teams. The generals were going.\nAt five forty-five in the afternoon, the fleet of dark green Mercury convert econo-mules disappeared along the road to Newport, carrying the generals and their Free lubrication teams.", "2_23_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to midnight</span>.\nThe time was at exactly midnight.", "1_19_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty in the evening</span>. This was Tuesday. The boat sailed on Saturday. That left only four more full days to get everything done.\nSay we come in on the evening of the thirtieth, Morse thought. I\u2019ll have to get the luggage in order. My attorney will have to get in order. I\u2019ll have to make a couple of calls in New York.\nPan Am shuttle to New York, tomorrow morning at seven. Jacob the doorman can drive me to Kennedy in the Mercury. Get a good night\u2019s rest. Come in on the early afternoon flight the next day\u2014spend an hour or so with my attorney. Then be on that ship.\nForty-odd days at sea.\nThe thought of it made him shudder. The smell of the food\u2014God! And we get turned around every other day, don\u2019t we? Won\u2019t it be good to get the smell of that fish out of my nostrils!\nHe sighed, thinking of this\u2014then crossed the boulevard and ducked into the soft light and cocktail hum of the American.\nREPRESSED MEMORIES\nThanksgiving Dinner\nL spine Obrist was in high gear by the time the restaurant party got under...", "0_16_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was an abandoned air shaft on the forty-fourth level of the Titan Wall.\nThe purpose of the time and place was to hold a funeral for $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe time and place had been chosen by $FIRSTNAMEB4, the spaniel-mPalestinian hunting dog who had been given to $SURNAME0 by Allbert C. McNamara.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 lay, frozen and shuddersome, at the 35-second step of the shaft ladder.\nSix yards from the grave lay $FIRSTNAMEF1, a wax image, cold and empty of hop. She lay on her right side, her right hand clutching and unclutching. Her glazed left eye focused on her husband and child, who lay facedown, side by side, bound hand and foot.\nThe three lay motionless in the blue-white glare of overhead spotlights powered by krytrons. Their clothes were scorched, their bare hands and feet blood-red.\nKrytrons were oscillators that cycled fifty thousand times a second, that consumed an MC of power, and that drove spotlights of two thousand candlepower into the hardest continuous use they were designed to sustain.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 and his mistress had been waiting here...", "0_13_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-seven in the afternoon</span>, and it is the author\u2019s duty to mention that, in Cleveland, the sky was that kind of violet-gray color that one associates with the idea of continental, or at least foreign, northern latitudes. It was appropriate, then, when one looked west to Lake Erie, across which the sedimentary tower of the Terminal reached with a certain appropriate diminished grandeur, and perceived that the lake itself was a wine dark purple. One shudders to make such a violet prose statement in a time when violet prose is out of fashion, but it persists, persists: there is no more beautiful city in the history of the world than Cleveland in early April, seen from outside the statued lobby of the Terminal Tower at four minutes after one on a weekday afternoon. The people who claim that New Orleans is the most beautiful city in the world are people whose judgment can be safely distrusted.\nCrystalline blue sky arched over Lake Erie, over the tower, over its lobby, over a population of about one million in the metropolitan region, of which upwards of a hundred thousand were at that moment in the lobby of the tower. High-heeled, perfumed, smartly dressed women moved with a confident rhythm among skirt-clad men, all...", "1_23_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to midnight</span>. For the next three hours all hell would break loose-hundreds of drug crazed monsters converging on the Convention Center and making monkeys out of the cops..The Trip for Romance and Acid Street Circus would begin in all its mindless frenzy.We had wired ourselves into natural madness. Suddenly it was evening; we were somewhere in the tortoise period between midnight and dawn. I was hunched over the wheel of the Shark/Rider, staring at the floorboard, when I felt the arm of my attorney on my shoulder. I looked up and saw him watching me with a strange expression. \u201cJesus Christ!\u201d he moaned. \u201cWhere are we?\"\nFor a moment I couldn't respond. My brain was mush. Then I saw the sign over the freeway: The Art Musuem Drive. We'd been driving in a crazy snarl of U\u2014turns, wrong turns and backtracks for more than hour. I leaned back in my seat and closed my eyes.\n\u201cWe're stopped,\u201d said my attorney \u201cLet's rest a moment, gather our strength for the final run.\u201d He popped the cassette into the radio, twisted the dial across the savage frequencies, then backpedaled furiously to a...", "1_12_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after noon</span>.\nThe Trystero sect did not figure the time of day from noon to noon on a twenty-four-hour basis, but from dark to dark. And the dark was currently eight minutes gone.\nThe next word the pirate stumbled onto was W. C. And that didn\u2019t give him anything to go on.\nProposition III: Every finite, distinguishable, process owes its existence, at least in part, to the Preterite Creator.\nThe third proposition made Duke put the Gorgon translation of the Working far back on his night-stand. It suggested that God existed, that God was busy being Himself, That God was a person; it ignored the possibility that God might also be a process. And, somehow, that seemed more fitting, with the popular conception.\nProposition III stated (in part) what God was doing. And it appeared that He probably hadn\u2019t got around to doing anything else since the Beginning. And maybe He wouldn\u2019t get around to anything else until the End of TIme began and everything got blown away.\nDuke knew a bit about processes from his Philosophy of Science course. There were named processes. And then there were processes that had to remain nameless because there...", "0_1_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-two in the morning</span>. The Mint Casino at the corner of Flamingo and the Strip is perhaps twentyfive fortified yards from the Avis office. Dispatcher Ramon was looking at me across a steel desk. \u201cOne thirtytwo. The party is entered as: \u2018White male, thirty years, five foot eight, one hundred sixty five pounds, arrest reported, coronary reported.\u2019 Do you wish an additional description?'\u201d He looked up.\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said. \u201cIt\u2019s mine.\u201d Then I told him the room number. He put me on hold and a voice told me the Americana Hotel was sold out. But there was a cancellation... the Airport West... $4.25 a day... That\u2019s Hertz, I thought. And hustled to the counter.\n\u201cWhat about my car?\u201d I almost screamed.\n\u201cWell,\u201d the dispatcher looked pained, \u201cthat one\u2019s on fortytwo. Fortytwo entries, and somebody\u2019s using the radio in that car now. I have to stand here and starve to death, because I can\u2019t leave this desk, but somebody out there in one of your goddamn stolen...", "1_10_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-five in the morning</span>.\nThe spaces were a teeming mob. The spaces were packed.\nThe line ran along the back wall. The line was four deep at places.\nThe line ended in great loops, loops facing a jungle of switchboard cubicles.\nThe staff of the Cincinnati Sub-Narcotics Club consisted of five students who divided their time between sitting in the cubicles, selling dust, selling sexy pictures, selling books, magazines, comics, Operators and Other Kinds Of People stamps, and sitting in the loops\u2019 end of the line, selling joints, selling loose mushrooms, selling cigarettes.\nThe five students, representing the faculty and administration of the University of Cincinnati, were wearing gold with wine-[Martian vegetation]-dyed shirts, white with gold with wine-[Martian vegetation]-dyed pants, black leather shoes with grey socks.\nStudents from other schools were generally not welcome. But one student from Saint Xavier\u2019s College was at the switchboard cubicles. This student was working for the University of Cincinnati. The student was a girl named Lengel, who was wearing an eyepatch over her left eye and a dark green sweater over a white tee shirt under a black leather jacket.\nOne side of the immense space...", "2_15_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-three in the afternoon</span>.\nThe story I am going to tell is true. The names have not been changed. The characters do not think that they are bad people. They are not bad people. But it is important that their story be known.\nFight Club is over. Very few people know it ever happened. It was a secret. Still, it is safe to talk about it, since most people\u2014the people in power, especially\u2014assume it is a movie.\nThe first rule of Fight Club is that you do not talk about Fight Club.\nThe first rule of this book is that it would be very easy to blame everything on me.\nIf human beings were lizards, you could amputate a person\u2019s tail and the tail would grow back and be stronger than ever. But we are not lizards. We are mammals. Our past\u2014every stupid decision, every bad relationship, every wasted moment\u2014we carry it with us. There is no depot where we can send our baggage: no central address where unwanted responsibility gets entered into a computer and promptly forgotten. The journey itself is the punishment.\nFear is not an option.\nLoneliness is not an option.\nOne other thing that is not an option: looking back.", "0_6_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>. The Red Weed was so thick that $SURNAME1 could not see the ground under his feet.\nHe could not see in front of him, either. He had no way of knowing whether he faced north, south, east, or west.\nHe did know one thing\u2014where the sun ought to have been in order to light the fog.\nThe sun wasn't there.\nIt had been blotted out by the fog of Red Weed on Mercury. A perfect analogy of the state of mind in which young $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 found himself on his wedding day.\nThe first stroke of seven on the great municipal clock boomed out through the Red Weed. Its mighty tones broke up the fog of Red Weed as a wind breaks up a bank of clouds. There was a glorious rainbow of red light in a place where no rainbow had any right to be\u2014and the sun, tiny, weak, and wan, was in the middle of the rainbow. Red light transmuted to gold went shooting and sparkling among the fireweed flowers that were miles deep in all directions.\nThe clock continued to boom the hour, but the sound did not penetrate through the fireweed to the remotest part of $SURNAME1\u2019s private universe.\nNeither did the soft footfall of...", "1_9_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 had fallen asleep again. Syme put his chronometer back in his pocket. The aerial ladder was actually falling, as if gravity had suddenly increased. It struck the roof beside them with a violent shock. Whoever was climbing was coming up at the double.\nUnfortunately the room was only about twice as high as a normal room, and the climber, corning up the ladder, was jammed close against the ceiling. When he burst in through the window, he had to withdraw his head from the window quickly because his hat brushed the ceiling. Anyone whose head was in the window exposed himself to a sudden and possibly fatal blow.\nThe ladder, meanwhile, had been pulled up again. It could be seen distinctly, hard against the sun, bobbing about in the empty air outside the building. There was no way of guessing how many fighting men might be on its upper reaches.\nThe man who came in through the window was already attacking from the moment he entered. He had a bayonet in his hand. There was hardly time to get hold of a weapon.\nSyme swung his chair round so as to have his back to the window. $FIRSTNAMEM1 seized the inkstand from the table and threw it at the man, who was halted for a moment,...", "2_14_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked at a photograph of his wife and himself on his wall, put there the day he moved into the barracks. His wife was young and beautiful. They were standing together in a small garden. It was summer, and everything around them were flowers. Their heads were together, and their smiling faces were radiant. His wife now was middle-aged, and she was not beautiful, and everything about her was hard.\nOh, how glorious that young man and young woman had once thought themselves! And how much glory they had misapplied! The young man\u2019s imagination had worried away at the rock-hard certainties on which Earth was run, and he had imagined he had uncovered the rock-hard rottenness that lay underneath. And the young woman had given her heart to the young man, and her head to the society around her.\nThe young $FIRSTNAMEB0 had rebelled against the authority figures around him\u2014and then had become a prototype for a new and particularly horrible form of authority figure. And $FIRSTNAMEF0 \u2014", "0_23_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until midnight</span>.\nThere was one minute remaining.\nAnd then, with six seconds to go, Salo, who had been listening closely to the drumming on the hull, shouted excitedly to the astronauts, \"They\u2019re here! They\u2019re outside! They\u2019re trying to get in!\u201d\nAt last $SURNAME0 let go of his wife.\n\"Remember, young man\u2014God loves you!\" called Salo, as the airlock swung open under Claude\u2019s control. \"Good-by now\u2014good-by. Don\u2019t worry about a thing\u2014we\u2019ll straighten things out back on Earth.\" The last chapter of the matter happened like this. While $FIRSTNAMEF1 and her son, Skip, were alone on Titan the fire in the golden hearts of the trees flickered and died. Foamins joined hands again, and Skip slipped into the circle once more.\n\"Mother\u2014\" said Skip. His voice was clear and golden. \"Father\u2014\" he said.\n\"What is it, dearest?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I just want to say\u2014\" said Skip. \"I just want to say that\u2014that was the best surprise party\u2014\" he said.\nHe fell, lifeless as a...", "2_18_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-six in the evening</span>, Martian time.\nThat was Earthtime.\nThat was the evening of September thirteenth\u2014and the only way that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 could win his freedom and make a new life for himself on Mars was to go through the torment that $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were proposing for him. There was a condition on the gift of freedom. The condition was that $SURNAME1 prove to $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 that he had the stuff of a true believer.\nThe condition had not been dreamed up by $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nIt had been dreamed up by the Supreme Being.\nThe condition was implicit in all roles that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had played. The reason all lords, kings, and heads of state were named in almanacs was because all roles with names were tacitly understood as having tacit conditions.\nThe only way in which $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 himself could be understood, given what he had actually done and all that he could still do, was as a role defined implicitly by its conditions.\nNo particular person had put the role together.\nTime and the nature of the Universe had put it together.\nThere was no one who could rescind the role, rescind the conditions implicit in it.", "0_7_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-seven in the morning</span>. Miss Nefertiti's hair was wild and streaked orange from lack of sleep. She blew smoke at Magnum.\n\"Try an aptitude-test battery,\u201d she told him. \"One hour and a half, commonly administered\u2014and you've got a printout as long as your arm that tells you everything about the guy's aptitudes, strengths, weaknesses and aptitudes.\"\nWanless, warming his hands, made a disgusted face. \"You still think a test is a substitute for experience, Nefertiti?\"\n\"Listen,\u201d she said, \"I didn't have any quick, bright way to find a manager for you, Arnold\u2014and so what?\" She blew smoke. \"And I'm sure glad I didn't, too. I asked around, did a little experiment, sent a questionnaire to some big companies\u2014and they all told me the same thing.\"\n\"What thing?\" Wanless's voice was softening.\n\"They all said they knew some old, ketchup-pushing supervisor who had spent an hour and a half giving an aptitude-test battery to a young guy, and had decided the young guy would make a great salesman\u2014so he made him a salesman. Then the young...", "1_16_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 found the harmonium. He found it in a small cavern that opened onto the great hall in which the harmoniums had stood. The cavern was empty, but had been elaborately decorated. In it was a table, and set in the table was a machine pistol. Of all the bits of ritual decor in that soldier-proof hideout, only one item failed to make sense: the machine pistol had been set in the table in such a way that, when the table was used for dining, the weapon pointed directly at $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s former seat at the table.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wiped the machine pistol as clean as he could, oiled it, loaded it, and listened to its marvelous, hard, tingling song.\nThen he hid it in a crevice above the table, where it wouldn\u2019t be likely to attract dust.\nThe harmonium was in the mouth of a cave three miles from $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hideout. In the echoing vault of the cave stood the harmoniums, their vacuum-filled vessels open to the lunar vacuum. The open vacuum-filled vessels would keep the harmoniums alive for months, perhaps for years.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 fed himself on another can of telente cre...", "0_9_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine seventeen in the morning</span>.\nHe called the Beverly. The desk told him Miss Gridley had checked out yesterday.\nHe called The Churchill. There was no listing in the book. He called Information and got a Glendale number. A man\u2019s voice answered.\n\u201cIs Inge there? Or Stan Rose?\u201d\n\u201cThis is the Orthopsychiatric Clinic,\u201d the voice said.\n\u201cCan I talk to Inge or Stan Rose?\u201d\n\u201cWho is this talking?\u201d\n\u201cNever mind,\u201d he said. He hung up.\nHe let a minute go by, then called Information again, got the number of Vertex Radio & Television Corporation again, dialed it quickly and was told Miss James was out to lunch.\nHe called The Nobel for her home number and got an Encino address. He dialed it. A birdlike elderly woman with a young child\u2019s uncertain lisp answered. \u201cUh-huh?\u201d\n\u201cMiss James, please,\u201d he said.\n\u201cThis is she.\u201d\n\u201cThis is the White Panther Annex, calling.\u201d\n\u201cOh, thank God. Thank God.", "0_17_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-two in the afternoon</span>, according to $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s watch, which had been scientifically set for Martian local time by its death-eager owner. $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s widow would be on the lookout for him at six and thus was not at this moment among the most jubilant Martians. His son, born and orphaned in a single day, would also be agitatedly awaiting news of his father.\nThe chances that $FIRSTNAMEB1, the punctual, would arrive before six were small. The chances that he would arrive at all were smaller. He had the whole northern hemisphere to himself, theoretically. And he had the prospect of a glorious death, alone, to keep him company.\nHe was unaware that his son and widow were fine, sprightly little creatures, wholly unsusceptible to the rheumatoid complaints that plagued him. And they, like him, were ignorantly blissful in the belief that they were the only Saturnians on Mars.\nSaturnians, the three of them. And a fine family they made, rolling across the Martian wastes.\n\"Blues!\" yelled $FIRSTNAMEB1, floundering in a mud puddle. This was an expression of his particular philosophy of life. One good muddy jack-knifed slide...", "0_6_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-nine in the morning</span>. The Cadillac was an hour and a half late for Tetons east gate opening, and it was now seventy-four miles from there. The only access to the park from the east entails a torturous route through Snake River Canyon: crossing a single steel bridge that rattles your fillings and hisses over the rapids below; then climbing a hairpin heartland hill road as straight and true as a ruler, windswept, without a curve in all of sixty miles, ending finally at the east face entrance gate of the park proper.Only six cars \u2014 one now, and five then \u2014 traversed this canyon each morning on Snake River Ferry service, and the odds were that the other five who had snapped up the licenses would now be jammed nose to taillight like lemmings all the way to the gate. No, there wasn't a chance now; the deed was done. He'd wanted to find out, and now he knew.\nThey drove as fast as they could toward Jackson Lake overlook. In the mist of the dawn it was merely a point on the wall of the canyon, and they skirled along the rising road with the sun at their backs\u2014werewolves of the sky. By the time they reached the overlook the sun had rent...", "2_14_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nHugh Kerbrecht got the Martian war fleet into position over the Atlantic Ocean in the nick of time. He flicked a switch. Stars fell. The stars were metal shavings in the painted image of Mars. They hit the cold sea and the war fleet of Martians. There were geysers of steam, lit by flames from the metal shavings. The water boiled.\nThe Atlantic Ocean boiled.\nThe steam rose to become part of Venus\u2019 atmosphere.\nThe Martians appeared. One hundred and eleven of the repulsive creatures had survived the attack by the birds in the Secretariat Building. They joined the other nine of their green-skinned species outside Newport, Rhode Island.\nThe Martians attacked Newport by running into it, using their square-wing noses as ram rods.\nThe buildings of Newport collapsed, rained bricks and wood splinters.\nThe Martians had attacked, were laying waste to society.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 stood outside the new, temporary spiral staircase that led from the Villa $SURNAME0 to the ground.\n\"Skip?\" said Hugh Kerbrecht.", "1_2_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>, when we came to a secretaire with two desks and found a little guy in an attic room, writing. I shouted. He shouted back, in another attic across the way. His name was Gregor, and his mother was the landlady and the owner of the hotel. He came down the stairs, rubbing his eyes.\nWe stared at him. The five of us stood framed in the dark doorway of his little room.\n\u201cWhat do you want?\u201d he said.\n\u201cSix beds!\u201d\n\u201cImpossible.\u201d\nThis was Italy. I jumped over to his other desk, picked up every article and piece of paper, threw them all in his face, and said, \u201cOooooboy!\u201d He backed up against the wall, terrified. \u201cYou're on the way to the American Beri-beri A, that,\" I said. I was dealing from the deck. The boys were standing stonily in the doorway; all of them, sullen and white, were against me.\n\u201cY-you can't insult me that way!\u201d Gregor said. I rushed over and grabbed him by the throat, picked him up and shook...", "1_18_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-nine in the evening</span>.\nI said good-by to the concierge, told him to make certain the mailman gave me the telegram when he came around at seven o\u2019clock; then I pushed my camouflaged Ford through the quais, turned south on the Seine, through the poplars, past the nervous sidewalk caf\u00e9s, around the Southern Cemetery, out along the stone wall of the horse race-track, and turned left onto the West Highway.\nI was free and the big green countryside was free. The road was empty, the Burgundian hills on my left were empty, the poplars lining the road were empty and I was all alone under a cloudy sky, driving into wonderful France.\nCanada to Le Mans\nTHE FRANKFURTER\nREVIEW 6\nOn the road it all worked fine. I felt like the man I was in Marseilles. There was not a single traffic cop on the highway, nobody followed me and nobody tried to kill me. Yet I was worried all the time. I kept swerving out of my lane to make certain I was not being followed. I zigzagged. Here I was at seventy miles an hour and I kept swerving out of my fast lane...", "2_13_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty in the afternoon</span>.\nThe space was the interior of a rented courtroom on Tralfamadore.\nYEA, UNTO ALL KINDS OF LIFE AND THE FORMS THERE OF, WERE THEY VISIBLE OR INVISIBLE, THINGS OF THE SENSE OR THE INTELLECT, BEING OR NOT BEING, ENFORCEMENTS OF THE LAW OF RIDDLES OR VOIDS BEYOND THE LAW, HE HELD THEM CONTAINED IN THE PAW OF HIS MEMORY; AND SO KEPT HE THE FLOCK OF THE ELECT IN THE HAND OF HIS UNDERSTANDING.\n\u2014Kleppner, C.F., \"Sysiphus and the Dashers,\" Fantasia Mathematica, A Quarterly Concern Devoted to Math and the Tie of Man to Number, Vol. VII, No. 2. New York, Bantam Press, 1957, p. 135.\nGOOD MORNING, EVERYBODY.\n\u2014Durham Red\nGOOD MORNING, EVERYBODY.\nThe temperature was 72\u00bd degrees.\nThe atmospheric pressure was 7.56 \u0159smm Hg.\nWater was 17.6% by weight.", "0_2_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two seventeen in the morning</span>. I'd followed Tim sections on the TV set in his room, a mass of unspooling tape that it would take me a minute to Go through, and meanwhile I'd pump Gennaro.\nAt first it didn't work, because what I wanted most to hear was the product of Tim's own head, the history of his subculture, the secret dreamfantasies of his associates, motives and signs and passing-word. I knew well enough that Gennaro Freck was a test case: instead of simply leaving him to stew about it for a spell, I should have asked him for his own story, every man his own story. I was also too tired now to begin at the beginning and patiently produce from him the real particulars I needed.\nI'd started questioning him about his \u201cjob,\u201d but it was too complicated. He worked for a big department store called Wolton's, had for some time. He handled \u201cinsurance\u201d for the firm, \u201cprotection\u201d on the lot for delivery wagons and such; he was a kind of private cop, on call twenty four hours a day, paid off in kind at 20% of the take. He was netting maybe six,...", "1_23_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to midnight</span>. The searchlights in the courtyard were shooting extra white streaks into the sky. I could see them through the branches of a eucalyptus tree that I was climbing in back of the shed. The idea was to get above the courtyard and see what was going to happen. There might be something sensational. There was a shout from the courtyard. In the electric glare I could see soldiers running into the big building. Sandy\u2019s stories about Basque heroism began to fade out. I could see the poor kids\u2019 faces on the walls of the barn, illuminated by the lightning from the sea. Probably some of them were dead by this time and others wishing they were. I wondered what the big shots in the Ministry of War thought about being led into a trap. No doubt about it, the Basques were a lovely people: magnificent, primitive, loyal and friendly. Pretty soon we were going to take all the bitterness out of their mouths. Like many heroic people they were very simple. We were going to teach them a few difficult lessons. I heard another shout from the dark courtyard. I tried to see what was happening but all I could see was a lot of uniforms, illuminated by the white lights in the hands of the soldiers. Suddenly, as though...", "1_12_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-five p.m.</span> Just five hours before the end of mankind. As Helmholtz raced his gyroscopically stabilized, turbine-propelled shark through the skies, fields and cities of the Eastern Seaboard, he saw shining, square cubes of mirrored glass everywhere he looked. Office buildings, conversion booths, industrial centers\u2014the whole world was getting ready for The Day, breaking out the plastic coats, the braziers, the snow domes. Except for one spot on Earth\u2014the latitude and longitude of which Helmholtz did not know at the time\u2014the whole Earth was shining like a parquet floor by the time Helmholtz finally got through traffic to the Brooklyn Navy Yard.\nYeck. Here came the really depressing part of Helmholtz\u2019s journey. At the gate of the Yard was a picket line of doleful men, wearing G.I. Joe trench coveralls and draped with limp, didactic banners. For some reason, the sight of these fellows sickened Helmholtz more than anything he had seen since waking up. Why is it, he wondered, that the people who are most damn certain that The Day is just a big, big bum out tend to be the ones with the best cause to think so?", "1_11_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to noon</span>. From the west coast of Florida it was the perfect time to start out on a drive to New Orleans in a Ford that was a sky blue convertible -- over the Rim, through Alabama, Mississippi, and into the low lands of swamp and cypress, the jewel-level sunlight, the dust and long clear perspectives, and by nightfall the neon come awake in the spokes and on the roofs of Memphis and shaping the music along the streets and telling the story of the fabled names along the Strip. I wanted to get on to it.\n\u201cBut we can't leave till after lunch,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cWe've made so many plans for the afternoon. Time and again we've installed ourselves and showed up at the door of the appointed hour only to find another couple there, gone before, lingering in sad farewell, telling little gossamer lies about when they arrived --\u201d\n\u201cOh, don't tell me about it.\u201d\n\u201cYes, time and time again and finally the sorrow, the heart-breaking sorrow of it all, seeing the other people drive off together and knowing you'll have to spend another half-hour getting acquainted again, introducing your friends all over again, finding out what they've been doing...", "0_23_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-one in the evening</span> when R. T. Wang-Dang Lang\u2019s at last shot out into the gibbous blackness of the Infinite Florida Desert. The desert was luckily near, for with all the drinking done in St. Louis and at the Pearl, a whiskey drunk was in fine effect. Not only the old lecher but his cab were fearfully besozzled. The cab wobbled as it roared out of the pits under the arena. It leaned this way and that. It sped away, veering wildly, cutting across midnight St. Louis like a hunted weasel.\nIt yawed and careened toward an infinity of blackness.\nAnd after five minutes the whiskey drunk fell apart. The old lecher suddenly found he had another man in the cab with him. This man was a tiny stranger, sitting in the far corner of the back seat. And there was something wrong with the man\u2019s eyes that further scared the already drunken old lecher.\nThese eyes didn\u2019t reflect light. There was no play of light in them that would attract attention in the back of a big cab at night. These eyes seemed to have no pupil. They were like the eyes of the bullfrogs the old lecher had seen one time...", "1_7_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>, and the Pancake House of the Blue Night would be closing in three minutes time.\n/a/New Wave Chronology (Approximate); c. 1981:\nDead Father; c. 1983:\nLate Malaussene; c. 1984-1987:\nMarvellous and Amazing; c. 1988:\nSong, c. 1989:\n/b/New Wave Chronology (Pocket Version); c. 1981. Dead Father. c. 1983. Malaussene. c. 1984-87. Amazing. c. 1988. Song. /c/New Wave Chronology (Kamikaze Edition); c. 1981: Dead Father. c. 1983. Malaussene. c. 1984. Amazing. c. 1986. Song.\n/d/Scenes from the Lifeket\n/1/Patterns in the Void\nfog horns, say all there is to say\nhere, listen to the cross\npurring of the Slotkins. True\nsuns set, sure, suns rise\nlipstick kisses on the back\nof my mother\u2019s hand. O death,\nsay all there is to say\nyou are like a\nslash of luck,\nor ____ the...", "1_10_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-four in the morning</span>. The place was a lavatory in The American Broadcasting Company\u2019s wing of network-television buildings in New York City. This cubicle was one of eight in a room designed to accommodate forty. The rooms was hopelessly overpopulated. The reason was that this room, like the rest of the buildings, such as the twelvesquare-block main building with its seventy-four elevators, was a scene of collision and terror and sorrow, on this particular morning in 1963.\nThe twentieth of April had dawned with a standard cootie infection. Somewhere between six and twelve thousand Americans had been infected during the night. The Red threat had subsided somewhat. There were now six different colors of flusher handles on sale in the United States, but the terror of the handles remained. The public health authorities were helpless. The disease was intercontinental, and incurable.\nOn the morning of April 20, a shift of people were working in the network buildings, replacing the shift that had been working through the night. A small-budget game show, in Studio 11, was coming to an end. The contestants had been told that there could be no winner, on this program. It was a Christmas program.\nThe laughter in the...", "0_21_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-eight in the evening</span>. I had somewhere around twenty-four dollars in my pocket. I had two hits of mescaline in my side pocket. Suddenly I realized I was hungry. I bought a sack of popcorn at theFood Circus. Twenty-four dollars was just about right. From now on I'd just flash it: flick it into a till and grab what I wanted. Nothing could stop me.\nIt was an extremely humbling epiphany.\n14. The ego is not your friend....Love is the tool, the very specific tool, for sharpening the blade of the Great Razor on the strop of the ego. --Luther Lee Bozarth, under the name of Sam Clemens, San Francisco Telegraph, April 10, 1970.\n15. I got back to the hotel at eleven o'clock. The parking lot on the port side of the building was filled with police vehicles...a dreadful sight, police cars parked in formation; it breeds panic, especially when you're high. I assumed it was something terrible in Room 110. The desk man was edgy\u2014anxious to leave.\n'there was a bomb threat about an hour ago,\u201c he said.\u201cThey just left.\"\n\u201cWhat kind of bomb threat?", "1_12_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-two post meridiem</span> (which, as is well known, is the military time that prevails in Southern California).\nTo resume: the place was Room 203 of the Sportman\u2019s Lodge, a bungalow room in a U-shaped Los Angeles motel. The room had blue carpeting, twin beds with the top half of their pylons closed off into cramped desks, a 27-inch gaudy color TV set, a cocktail table (with a formica top) trans versed by a sharp L of a wooden chair, an empty pullman kitchenette, window shades of Navajo print, sky-blue drapes, a window overlooking nothing but an apron of asphalt, an element heater over whose embossed hot-water tank was pasted a sign saying PLEASE DO NOT OPERATE ORDINARY RICE KRISPIE TREATMENTS, because the management operated under the impression that the building was a barracks, and apparently expected the tenants to make the coffee themselves. A soiled Gideon Bible rested atop the hot-water tank and was encrusted with brackish tapwater rings, but no towels. (Since there was also no bath, Lonesome Bodie avoided ice cubes.) There was a nude silhouette on the Gideon Bible...", "1_7_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-two in the morning</span>. Sunlight was beginning to filter down through the fuming sky over Denver. $FIRSTNAMEB3 saw a crumby dawn coming up. Denver looked like the end of the world in the good old days when gold was king. Old women with ragged bundles under their arms staggered in the streets. Dead men lay under the boxcars. Somewhere a rattle began, a long jungle roll of drums. \u201cComing, coming, everybody ready? Yes, now everything is fine, we're all coming together, it's a great and tremendous scene and everybody -painting! \u201d he yelled and pointed at a passing rent-truck and big posters for county fairs and rodeos dawned along the streets, finally and presently the mountains themselves around Denver rose up with the early sun and all the peaks were high and blue and solemn. The great government City of Denver looked like where all the beatniks would live someday. \u201cWow!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201cand by that I mean all of us, too.\u201d We were all shooting. $FIRSTNAMEF4's hat fell off. We told her to watch it and she nodded, bashful and swooning. Lou was giggling just to look at her. The sun was golden, the...", "2_11_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-three in the morning</span>. The place was $SURNAME1\u2019s Castle.\nBeeber forced the air through his fangs in a wolflike howl.\nThe television technicians reacted exactly as any audience did whenever anyone scaled the wall wearing roller skates and a fang-light\u2014they laughed.\nThey were still laughing when Beeber zipped up the wall.\nTheir laughter unnerved them.\nThey weren\u2019t accustomed to humor on this grim day.\nAt that particular moment, the matter in question being matters grim, the whole planet having been declared in a state of emergency against ignorance, the problem having been identified as the static condition of mobs, the solution being the dispersal of mobs by amusing means, then the situation had understandably affected everything. It had affected the type of man in the castle, the make of his clothing, the material of his sled.", "1_15_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-one in the afternoon</span> by Cass\u2019s watch, which was set by voice activation to the Denver radio time signal. Discipline was very important to Cass, who was a loner by inclination and had a firm belief in the self-defeating dangers of getting close to other people. She seldom spoke. Still, at the same time, she was warm and generous, if only by not holding back, and if one were so fool as to start a conversation with her, she listened intently, and usually had something to say.\nThey walked together now, linked arm in arm by natural attraction, pausing occasionally to watch their footing on the trail\u2019s knife-sharp rocks. So far, they had not exchanged a word. It had been like this ever since the previous night\u2014an enormously satisfying meeting of two still, reserved souls after years of loneliness. There was no need for conversation. For each was conscious of the other\u2019s essence, her strengths, her weaknesses. And there was really no need for physical contact, either, but Lenore Beadsman fondly slid her arm through Antichrist\u2019s, and Antichrist slid hers through Lenore\u2019s.\nIt was decided by mutual consent that they would have to be careful,...", "0_17_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. During the next ten minutes the space ship bearing $FIRSTNAMEB0, the victim, and $FIRSTNAMEB1, the executioner, would be descending unseen through the stratosphere, would be burrowing under the ground, would be sounding in the barrack cavern below the state prison.\nDuring those ten minutes, fidgety people in the barracks and in the control tower and tunnel entrances took out their harmoniums and fiddled with them. The big hand of the clock above the hospital door crawled at two and a half seconds to the notch labeled Five.\nThen the tropism that had drawn people to a certain spot outside the hospital suddenly ebbed away. Something was obviously wrong. People spread out and milled aimlessly. In the barrack tunnels, people sealed the heavy steel hatches that had opened to let the space ship in.\nFive thousand four hundred and seventeen indeterminate minutes later, one of the people milling outside the hospital sensed an almost invisible flow of air. The air flowed, eddied\u2014said, by means of texture and pressure, Hello.\nThe hello breeze had an unusual cyclonic texture, an unusual bedspring pressure. It seemed to be saying, What did you expect?\nThe breeze went away, came back, went away...", "0_0_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve sixteen a.m.</span> of the twenty-first day of October 1942. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB2, level one of the black, ten-thousand-foot Lufo Tower on Lundo, the mainstream planet of the Galfan system in the FirNT Tralfamadore system.\nThe activities in the place, $FIRSTNAMEB2, above referred to were part of a much larger activity in the same place. The owners of the place, $FIRSTNAMEB2, were restoring the place as part of a much larger project of general restoration. Since the restoration of $FIRSTNAMEB2 was a part of a national project, it did not enjoy itself alone, but was one of millions. Every owner of every place in the nation was at work on his place, doing an uncountably small piece of the immense project.\nTralfamadore had no time for timekeeping. It was a class A planet, manufactured expressly by its makers, the tralfamadorians, to be their home. The tralfamadorians were twelve feet tall. They were yellow, porous, six-tentacled. They were nomads who carried their every possession with them.\nA mate on Tralfamadore was chosen by the tossing of a tralfamadorian bolt.", "1_18_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty in the evening</span> and the field was deserted. Most of the boys in the squadron were on the town, looking for girls. I was very lonely.\nI began to walk around the field in the gathering dusk. There were dogfights going on over France, twenty miles away. The battle with the Luftwaffe was in full flare. I walked around the field and looked to see if anybody was taking off. None. None of our pilots were taking off. Off in one corner of the field a small night air-raid precaution plane was going up every half hour and patrolling over the town. I watched its lonely ascent.\nThen I approached two men in flying suits who were sitting on a small concrete block. One of them had his leg stretched out and was rubbing it. They were fighter pilots, P-47 boys, just back from a mission.\n\u201cGot your leg hurt?\u201d I said.\n\u201cYes,\u201d said one.\n\u201cWhat happened?\u201d\n\u201cFifty-seven hounds attacked us. I got one of them but this dumb leg nailed me.\u201d He continued to rub it.\n\u201cWhat were they?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cFork-tailed Devils. Fifty...", "2_20_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"Oh-h-h, don\u2019t you worry that pretty head about anything, ever,\" said Crumley. He was circling the room like a prowler. \"You are about as safe as Mizincoln in this chair ere.\" He waved his gold pencil in the air. \"And your great-granddaughter, let\u2019s attend to her now.\"\n\"What about her?\"\n\"Your girl is liable to be kidnapped,\" said Crumley.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 looked past him at the gold fish and said, \"Is that so?\"\n\"Ha, ha,\" said Crumley, and opened his mouth, and closed it.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 pulled the extension table and got her purse, and in the purse was a small black automatic pistol which she took to pieces in front of him.\n\"Oh-h-h, ma\u2019am,\" said Crumley. \"You didn\u2019t even give me a chance at speaking my piece.\"\n\"It should take you about twenty seconds longer that way,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 dryly. She held out the pieces to him.\n\"Aim it, cock it, squeeze it, and P-P-Pickens,\" said Crumley, stuttering.", "1_6_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>. In three minutes, many people on Mars would see the sun rise at the exact moment that it set on Earth. Earthlings would see no sunrise that morning, of course. The sun had already set on them.\nAll the night watches were changing. The rumpled red-faced man at the desk was a Martian Legionnaire, Corporal Amos Daley, who had just come off watch, who had just come off duty with his co-watch officer, a captain.\nDaley said, \"We couldn\u2019t find you in a hundred billion Earths, anyway. Relax.\"\n\"My last Earthling commander didn\u2019t agree,\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s messenger. He took a rag from his tunic, tossed it onto the seat of the captain\u2019s contour chair. \"He thought I ought to be close to my commander at all times.\"\nDaley grinned indulgently. \"Old Man Wiseman was crazy,\" he said. \"Is. Was nuts.\" He made dog noises. \"How are you supposed to be on call all the time, anyway? Fifty million kilometers is a long telephone line to tie somebody\u2019s hands down on.\"\nThe messenger sank into his contour chair. \"You...", "1_9_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-eight in the morning</span> and I was all set to inaugurate this first day of my new life with a shot of pre-Columbian ceremonial blood-brotherhood gin, when I heard my blood-brother calling from the bedroom, in a horrible\n\n\u2003 Voice: \u201cEd! What is it?\u201d\nTen seconds later he staggered into the living room, with blood all over his face, and collapsed on the couch. While I rushed around, finding ice, gin and eli1 peroxide, he slowly got hold of himself enough to tell me what had happened.\n\u201cI was sleeping away the fumes,\u201d he mumbled fuzzily. \u201cAnd the phone rang ...and the noise of the phone ringing woke me up ...\u201d Desperately he rinsed his face in cold gin, to neutralize the alkali burns.\n\u201cI answered the phone,\u201d he continued, \u201cand a voice said: \u2018Mr. Baker? This is the police station. You have had your car stolen.\u2019 And then they hung up.\u201d He looked at me.\n\u201cMy God!\u201d I said. \u201cHow could they know? How can they think such a thing?\u201d I...", "0_21_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> of a Monday in September of the year 199-, by the evening sky outside the Terran Export Company building. The place was Newport, on the island of Rumor, in the Western Galaxies. The Sole Importance of what was about to be told had nothing whatever to do with a flight to Titan that would lift off from Newport in twelve minutes at nine ten, thereby copping its passengers a nineteen-hour flight to Saturn, a two-day stay, a three-day flight home to Mercury, and a six-day stay, thereby freeing them of a full week of their lives. The flight number of the ship taking the fortunate few was GAL-007. It was the seventh Goddardian Carrier gig to leave Newport since the flight had begun there exactly two years before. This detail was, of course, not known to anyone but Company employees. The flight number was known to anyone, but anyone not going on the flight was under no compulsion to know it. Anyone going on the flight was under no compulsion not to say that he knew it. Rumor knew the flight number, and almost everyone on Rumor had a ticket. The ship now about to lift was GAL-008. Rumor was, of course, flying GAL-008, when it...", "2_0_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>, that Mucharco was saying. He was making with the blah blahs so $FIRSTNAMEF2 put her hand over his mouth and told him if he wanted to stay friends to find some euphemism, because right then she didn\u2019t want to hear it. This did not seem to offend him, as he knew $FIRSTNAMEF2 well. She drove along Kirby Grade Passelanna, heading north, and why should anything out of the ordinary happen? But something did. Very soon, she realized. Right away, that is, at the Sapphire Safeway, where she pulled in to get gas.\nThere were four guys and a girl outside, having a rough time with a tall blonde in stretch pants and an A-line fur. They\u2019d all; piled out of a Citroen two-seater and were trying to persuade her into the car. She kept shaking her head, backing away, clutching an enormous pig in a blanket to her breast. $FIRSTNAMEF2 looked at them as she went inside.", "0_10_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-four in the morning</span> in Seattle. I called the operator for a directory listing. \u201cMister $FIRSTNAMEB3 Harridan.\u201d There was no Harridan. \u201cMister $FIRSTNAMEB3 Harrigan -- ah, yes, 1039 East Capitol.\u201d It was the only listing of a Harrigan in the book. I told Carlo the number.\n\u201cNo, man. It's just some kind of sadistic encharterment on my part. I'm in the pay of $FIRSTNAMEB3's father and mother. We live together on Central Parkway as you knew, he tells incredible lies about his escapades with women, they wire his $165 every month, and I wire some of it back East at his direction. Isn't that right, $FIRSTNAMEB3?\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 was by now in the bathroom throwing water on his hair. He nodded. \u201cHis mother, who is a marvelous woman, and his sister Cathy, who has troubles I don't care to know about, depend on the money. Priests had the right idea. Tell the lies that are expedient.\u201d\nA moment later $FIRSTNAMEB3 was out, wearing new clothes and with his hair mooshbrayed down flat, attempting to look like a young business executive. He was rushing around like a...", "0_18_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-two in the evening</span>. There was no breeze. The tempers of the groups had grown short as the sun failed. Some small children cried, but some cried with laughter. An older child roller-skated on his hands past a group of people waiting for the sun to set. The rollerskater hurled a handful of sand into a beautiful model sailboat with a sign on it that read THE MOON K. The sailboat\u2019s owner did nothing. A man in the group waiting to watch the sunset spoke to the rollerskater.\nIt was Norman Bombardini. He was convulsed with grief over his young nephew, but he spoke to the rollerskater on principal. The arrival of the young man in white sneakers and a woman who had tried to feed Biff\u2019s transparent body to crows hadn\u2019t been sufficient emotional excitement for Bombardini, so he initiated an altercation with a little boy who roller-skated on his hands past an unsuccessful businessman having an emotional crisis. See what I mean?\n\u201cHey, kid!\u201d cried Norman Bombardini. \u201cStop that damn fooling around right now and stand on your feet!\u201d\nA group around Bombardini laughed in a conventional...", "1_1_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-eight in the morning</span>. A dog jumped from somewhere behind us as we descended and hit our legs. Credit that to the cali-dog. Any kind of an oversexed mongrel that humped anything in sight in Malibu was a cali-dog. But the la-teen-ohs claimed even their purest shepherds and poodles had the same male trouble.\nIt was downhill all the way to Neptune's Lair and we went fast except for the animals that hit our legs and the ones we hit with the cab. \u201cPay no attention to anything except the road,\u201d I told my attorney. \u201cDon't even look at the Sea Shell Motel back there.\u201d He nodded. \u201cDon't worry, man, I know what I'm doing.\u201d We roared on, a gray ghost wagon on a long, dark deer\u2014slope of foothills. I shut my eyes and thought of the American Dream call-girl ring in Atascadero and how to approach it. Probably be some Mexican kids running it. Probably all pretend to be sisters and wear costumes. Probably fantastic. Right on! More gas. We passed funky diners, a hitch\u2014hiker who meant business, a hundred invisible dogs...", "0_11_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-seven in the morning</span> and nothing noteworthy had occurred. Much later on, in his report to $FIRSTNAMEB2, he amplified on this.\nThe messages received and transmitted were mostly routine and uninteresting. We consider it good prac-tice to include you, in person, in one communications net during each operational day, in order to remind all parties that S. S. personnel are human beings, too\u2014not just faceless machines spitting out statistics.\nYours, in the service of mankind,\nRexecutive Stony Stevenson,\nAdministrative Assistant, Mars Tactial Co-ordinating Center.\nLife-coach Conrad Nagel lay floating on his back in the zero-gravity lounge of the space ship Titanic, staring up through the floor windows that looked out onto space.\nBehind him, in the salon, seventy-two passengers sat strapped into their seats, listening to the steward explain the emergency Red Button procedure.\nThe Titanic and its crew of eight was on nine hours\u2018 in-flight alert\u2014keeping its sensors tuned to the three distress frequencies and its crew in sedated readiness for instant take-off.\nHow strong a sense of life must be instilled in a ship named after the Titanic? What effect could this symbolism possibly have upon...", "0_21_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-six in the evening</span> of August 29, 1994, and in Indianapolis, Indiana. The place was a ballroom in the Marriot East, across the street from the Tangerine Club, where the Mighty Morphin\u2019 Power Rangers were holding their International Drug Forum and Policy Symposium. The Mighty Morphin\u2019 Power Rangers were the phenomenally successful merchandising creations and TV actors put forth by the Angelically Asked Network. Their premise was that every purposeful action of theirs was based on some martial-arts principle. And they were available in two or three different playset configurations, stylized action figures (the Green Ranger featured locking sword hilts that really locked!), vitamin-enriched cereals, school-uniform skirts, drinking cups (the colors ran if you put the wrong drink in them), footlong plastic sandwiches (they didn't taste too bad, though), modeling clay, skateboards, jackets, collector\u2019s cups, wall posters, game-show adventures sold in sealed boxes with living batteries included, talking alarm clocks, alarm-clock radios, board games, board-game peripherals, and endless other things. They had been on the air only two years, but already every child in every developed country in the world knew about the Mighty Morphin\u2019...", "1_8_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not go to sleep again after the very loud alarm-clock. He shaved and bathed, dressed, and packed a small bag.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was, as he reminded himself for the thousandth time, a fool to go. He had every excuse for not going.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 went to the local branch of the Army and Space Museum, where he turned in his blued-steel .50 caliber machine gun, his two .30 caliber machine guns, his bag of hand grenades, his 25 mm cannon, and his officer\u2019s fencing sword.\nHe didn\u2019t want to go unarmed, but he rejected the idea of carrying the disintigrator. He knew he wouldn\u2019t dare use it against another human being.\nHe stopped at a bar on the way to the space ship\u2014had one last one before he went, he said. He drank three gingersnaps-and-milk whiskies. He had to drink them fast so the bartender wouldn\u2019t spoil the taste with lime or lemon.\nWhen he got to the spaceport, he wobbled a bit going up the gangplank. But he went on board ship, said good-by to the crew, and delighted...", "2_13_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-two in the afternoon</span>.\nThe event of greatest importance that was scheduled to occur in the Solar System in the next hundred and forty-four hours was the stoning of $SURNAME0 and all of his invited guests on Titan.\nThe narrator describing this scene on Tralfamadore can attend to it, of course, only in terms of public-relations techniques. Every word that is written or spoken about the events so many light years from Earth is relevant only as bright or dark public-relations marks on Earth or human institutions on Earth.\nIt is to be presumed that only public relations has any meaning for a civilization so many light years from Earth.\nAnd, since it is so many light years from Earth, it makes sense to speak here only in terms of public relations on Earth. It is to be presumed that the system of government on Tralfamadore differs as much from the democracies and tyrannies and societary systems of Earth as do the creatures of Tralfamadore differ from the creatures of Earth.\nIn the offices and homes of the Solar System, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 were regarded as creatures of unusually abysmal intelligence. They were unemployed outcasts. They were unpaid and quarreling members of society.", "0_0_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after midnight</span>. I moved out of the coffee\u2014house and walked up the street. I must have been tired because as I passed a Cadillac limousine parked at the curb a white\u2014haired, barrel\u2014chested, friendly man leaned across the back seat and yelled: \u201cHey, there! You're the poet, aren't you? Want to earn two dollars?\u201d I stopped dead.\nI took off my glasses and put them in my pocket. For some reason I was wearing dark glasses that night. I stood looking at him foolishly. I was awake, but I didn't know what I was doing. I was so tired I couldn't move. The old man opened the door of the limousine.\n\u201cWant to earn two dollars?\u201d he yelled.\nI got in and sank down on the seat beside him. He took out a long Lucky Strike advertisement envelope and passed it to me.\n\u201cRead that,\u201d he said. \u201cSee anything wrong with it?\u201d He put on his glasses and began to drive the car.\nI opened the envelope and took out the thing inside it. It was a letter, but not like any letter I had ever seen. It was a printed letter.", "2_19_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-eight in the evening</span>.\nThe Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0 turned on a wing light. The light showed him that there was a figure wrapped in a space suit, a space suit which was supposed to keep the wearer comfortable in any environment from the frigid to the fiery. The space suit was a mushy shell of violet, a color satisfactory for night use on any planet. It was bulky in contour, making the wearer look something like the front-end loader on a bulldozer.\nThe bulky front-end loader in the gilded frame didn\u2019t do anything, didn\u2019t say anything, didn\u2019t move. He was supposed to be talking to $SURNAME0 and the three-and-a-half-pound radios in his backpack. But he wasn\u2019t talking to $SURNAME0 or to the back-pack radios. He was simply spacing out his breathing, in and out.\n$SURNAME0 had seen similar spacers before. Some of them, in a reasonable way of their own, were actually capable of strung-out living.\nBut this spacer had apparently lived too long.", "1_19_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>. On the right and on the left was the audience. Before me was a man named Stanislawski. Behind me were the Critics. I was being examined in The Architect. Two hours later I was being button-holed by a man named Scobie. Telegram:\n\"YOUR PLAY LIKE YOU WROTE IT. MY CONTRACTS CONTACT PHILIP MORRIS.\"\nRose came over in her gold glasses. She had on her Sam Spades robe. She sat down at my left. Danny Kyle, who was doing the casting for the company, forced his gangling frame onto a chair at my right. There was a large mirror directly behind me. I could see its distorted frame blended with a frame of facial features. Scobie was at the mirror, studying my neck.\n\"I know you can't do much with it, but try to encourage them to send us a little dough,\" he said. \"They get moody.\" He was talking about the telegraph office. His job was to transmit and rewrite their messages. They sent them to him by Western Union. A special secretary took the telegrams to his window. If a telegram was too long for a Western Union form, Scobie added sheets...", "1_8_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-five in the morning</span> of November second, and $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was much more optimistic in those days, was out on the sandy shores of the Malachite Ocean with three thousand other recruits who were about to take part in the first real military maneuver of the new Martian Army\u2014a maneuver which was designed to kick nonstop for Minni-scape, Earth, get there by New Year\u2019s Eve, surprise the hell out of everybody, and bring the last twelve surviving Earthlings home in time to celebrate Martian New Year\u2019s Eve a few feet from Martian Christmas trees.\nAttached to each man\u2019s belt was three pounds of Martian Government-issueEarth food concentrates: twelve bars of a substance called Beefsteak Grudgeworth, one bar of Currant Bufflehead Pie, six ounces of Hickory Sticky Buns, twenty sticks of Mario Moustache Ice Cream, and six pounds of Milky Meringues. Each soldier was also equipped with gloves, socks, boots, overshoes, seven handkerchiefs, a canteen, a space blanket, a fletchette, four clips of matches, a foil-wrapped tablet of M&M\u2019s, a foil-wrapped chocolate bar, two tubes of...", "0_22_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 pulled the speakwrite toward him. He still had a little over a minute at his disposal. Enough time to issue one more bulletin, perhaps. In the background he could hear the telephones report: nine hundred and ninety-four, nine hundred and ninety-five, nine hundred and ninety-six. He consulted the list of later figures in the prophet's book. Just beneath where he had torn out the page he had written: \"12,131 entries. Each containing 30 words. Lexicographic probability, against one occurrence, of odds, 3030197.\" The book was correct. It would have been tempting Providence to substitute one more line.\nIn the empty space of the speakwrite he wrote:\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\nDOWN WITH BIG BROTHER !\nHe had just spiked the piece of paper into the pneumatic message chute when the screaming started.\nChapter 10\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 looked up slowly. He had a curious feeling that somebody else was in the room, some where quite near.", "2_22_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> when the Instant Message of the Master of Mars ended. The ship door opened, and there stood Skip, tongue-tied, holding a pair of roller skates.\n\"Holy smoke\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\n\"Pardon me?\" said $SURNAME0. He spoke in a dignified manner, for he understood that an execution was taking place, and that dignity was called for.\n\"Pardon me,\" said $SURNAME1, \"but I guess my mouth is hanging open.\"\n\"Is it?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Please close it.\" Five minutes before, the whole thing had been so machino-ically simple. This one went here, that one went there. In went eight billions of dollars\u2014out went an obedient Mankind. And now the roller skates in Skip\u2019s hands were real roller skates, and Skip and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 were real people, and the future was all frayed at the edges. No one knew what would happen next. $SURNAME0 offered a cigarette to $SURNAME1, the first cigarette $SURNAME1 had ever seen. \"Cigarette?\" said $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME1 shook his head no. \"You have nothing to fear,\" said $SURNAME0 genially.", "0_6_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-three in the morning</span>, and now as the $FIRSTNAMEB2- logically Transmitted Band began playing again and as the demonstrators formed \u201cslabs\u201d again for their morning exercises, the sun broke through. It was a beautiful autumnal day\u2014blue sky, no smog, no wind. Eastward, over the San Bernardino Mountains, the sun cast an enormous living pink breast, with a nipple like the peak of Mount Baker. People shouted joyfully. Some ran for seats on the tabled risers for the exercises. A whole flock suddenly came out of the woodwork and surrounded Alvin Biggs within his plywood prison. \"Praise the Lord!\" cried an old woman.\nBiggs smiled, not looking at anybody in particular, aiming his microphone out into space.\nThe exercises began again. They were calisthenics, really, loosely derived from Yoga and other Far Eastern systems. There was no Hindu mystique or mumbo-jumbo connected with this particular brand. The exercises were simple, and the music by which they synchronized their movements had been written by Bach. The exercises, which got more complicated every five days, were timed in cycles of four to the movement of sixteen of Bach\u2019s Brandenburg concertos. Alvin Biggs was supposed to have memor...", "0_2_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. Clark exchanged a drowsy glance with his son. Together they lurched to their feet.\n\"Hey, you,\" said Clark to $SURNAME2, \"quit rubbing all that cereal into that goddam couch.\" His tone was confidential, as if $SURNAME2 were his brother and had, at long last, revealed to him the true meaning of life. \"Take it easy,\" he said to the room in general. \"Everybody just calm down and take it easy.\"\n\"You too, Mr. Space Program,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"The time has come. \"\nAugust 4, 1985\n1. Federal Regulation of Space Unfair to America\u2019s Private Space Industry; Also Fails to Adequately Protect Consumer\nInterests, Washington Rumors\nFederal regulation of the balloon industry is unfair to America\u2019s fledgling private space industry, and will damage the country\u2019s economic well-being in the long run, industry leaders agreed at a rally held at the Washington Monument yesterday .\nSpeakers at the rally called for the prompt rationalization of all rules, regulations and standards governing balloon making and balloon flight.\nThe speakers declined to estimate how much the rationalization would cost, but a spokesman for the Allied Balloon Manufacturers Association...", "0_15_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three eighteen in the afternoon</span>. One of the cops was reading something with a shiny cover. I couldn't make out what it was, it was diminishing with distance, but he was the only one reading. The other two were yawning and arguing. Another car stopped, but they didn't want to talk to it, so it went on. I decided to kill myself. The only thing that saved me was that I had cancer in my liver and I thought I had several good years of life left in me. Life has a way of giving you exactly what you need, no more, no less. And I didn't need life in Portland, Oregon, at three eighteen PM, because I knew too much. The time was three eighteen, and the news on the radio was that a rebel general had seized power in Nigeria. I turned off the radio. The cops were gone. O Lord Punk, son of Chaos, I went back to sleep.\nI opened my eyes. I was back in Salinas, on my own bed. I lay there and realized I hadn't committed suicide. It had been a close call, though. Up till that last awful moment I'd had a gun in my mouth and the resolution to blow my brains out the back of my head. That crisis had passed...", "2_7_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe scene was a farmyard in England. The balloon was struggling feebly among the treetops. The Earthlings, winched up in the gondola, were all dead.\nBelow the farmhouse were three round holes in the Earth, the wormholes from which the tunnels of the three Earthlings had recently emerged.\nAnd at one of these wormholes was a model kit, a wooden hobbyhorse on rollers, painted blue and gold, the colors of the Canuck-national-parachute-weaving Company.\nA little boy of perhaps five, playing beside the kit, studied it with great intensity.\nAt his feet was a enamel mug with a wicker handle, half full of a dark liquid.\nThe liquid was tea.\nSometimes the only way you can see the enigma of your own existence is to see it in a mirror that reflects the Universe instead of yourself.\nThere is a spot in the waters of some of the ponds on Tralfamadore where Tralfamadorians love to come and see the image of the Universe, as reflected in Tralfamadorian waters.", "0_21_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 knew this, because his pocket watch was still keeping Terrestrial standard time, ten hours ahead of the Martian time kept at the port of Phoebe. $FIRSTNAMEB2 never touched his watch. He preferred to live completely by Martian time, the better to forget his life on Earth.\nA gong clanged in the night, echoing through the empty canyons of Mount $FIRSTNAMEM1.\n\"Mmmmmm\u2014\" hummed $FIRSTNAMEB2, up from his chair, although it was painful for him to move. He had no bed, only a canvas cot, which he kept rolled up against a wall.\nWhen he was standing, $FIRSTNAMEB2 touched his warm forehead, the warmest part of his tall body. By contrast, his cold toes felt like icicles.\n\"Oooo\u2014\" he sighed, as he bent over, went down on one knee, felt for and found the slippers. They were there before the big chair\u2014a cloth foot-covering for each foot, coming to a point in the front. The slippers were also black, like the clothing and the hair of $FIRSTNAMEB2. He put them on, sighed again, managed a wan smile.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was a punctual man.\nThe gong...", "0_8_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Sometime within the next sixty seconds, somebody was going to fire one hell of a big gun...hopefully at $FIRSTNAMEF1, but possibly at some other courier\u2014for reasons unknown ... reasons that had not been revealed in any of the tests run on Mintouchian or the strung-out recruits elsewhere in the building.\nThe clock had been stopped at twelve-thirty. It was now 0832 hours.\nThere were two minutes and forty seconds to run. Doomsday was coming.\n\"You were wrong,\" breathed $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"You were wrong.\"\nThere lived a man named Helquist, and Helquist lived with many things in his head. One was the image of a clock with a stopped clock, with many things swimming above it in the air\u2014dead or dying, fixed or displaced, vicious or sorry\u2014and in the middle of all this madness a piece of luck, which was a gold-plated fish making a nibble at the hook of the broken spring, at the coils and gemmed mechanism.\nThis image might have had something to do with a woman\u2019s mouth, at night, when it said Helquist. Yes.\nAnother was the image of an ice cream cone slipping sideways off a woman\u2019s hand as...", "0_6_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty in the morning</span> and the dawn chorus was on\u2014all the lonely bars of the bullfroggies; I could hear the song of madmen, music hall singers, and weather-bitten whores; I heard melancholy Maledonian bagpipes, distant calliopes in county fairs, tiny cracked high voices singing in seaside parlors, brave Chicago trumpets taking it away: I was listening to the music of the world, the instrumental litany that great brontosaurus in the sky blows for His endless revue. And is it dawn? I looked at the sky and it was beginning to be dark. It was just another typical Vegas transition\u2014the purple velvet of night to the violet smog of day.\nI Couldn't Believe It Was Legal!\nWhen the emotional hassle with Remi and the lawyer was over at last, I decided to push north to Tonopah and pick up Ray again, then come back and recuperate in Las Vegas. So I went across to the lot where we stashed the wheelman's Caddy, quite a pretentious black limousine. I hid the car in one of the public garages and went back to the hotel. The hallways were absolutely empty. The Sahara was the...", "1_7_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-three in the morning</span>. She made coffee. It didn\u2019t, couldn\u2019t, of itself determine her fate, her restored and integrated sense of herself, the whole life now, but look down into the cup, gray and neutral, and see the chain of events it proclaimed, everything disposed of in order, each with its shadow: you might as well, sad and proud and forgiving, accept the universe. Save who knew what love to what end, she thought as she drank it, the universe could give no more than it had given, which was, yes, love, rings and coffins, beginnings and ends. It was her life, or part of someone\u2019s life; it was ambiguous, interesting.\nThey went upstairs to bed. They might as well, she thought, be grateful they felt so tender, so tolerant of each other, after spending one more year trying and failing to write the book. But she was awake again, at nine, lying and staring into the September sunlight, the first sunlight of the year pouring white as milk between the slats and across the floor, filtering even through the dust motes and filling the room which she refused to call her study with a sense of hope and plenty, industriousness. Where had it come from...", "2_0_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty a.m.</span> and I had a four\u2014hundred\u2014M feet to get uptown and through the Caesar\u2019s main gate. The main\u2014gate guard had said the hotel would be absolutely full. That meant I would be checked when I got there. I still had the Whale\u2019s American Express card and I just kept drawing it to the limitcase I got stopped and the car searched, which was the natural thing for a man to do when he was carrying around stolen federal credentials in a F.B.I. car with a quart of Great White in the trunk.\nI felt paranoid and stupid. My mind seemed to keep standing up and hollering, \u201cHere, now! What\u2019re you doing? Are you really serious about this? Really, now...you mean to say you really plan to go through with this incredibly bad and dangerous and foreseeably horrible idea?\"\nYeah...so the voice would down me.", "2_4_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-eight in the morning</span> when they bundled up the hot motor inside the garage of the mansion next door to the chief of police\u2019s. The mansion had a white portico with Corinthian columns, and a crazy stair in the front hall, and recessed niches in the walls of the front hall, and a huge fireplace, and a great canopy bed, and a lot of gilt, and was quite large. Who lived there was nobody\u2019s business. The owner was some sort of a furrier who lived abroad. Spratling\u2019s neighbors on either side were a hardware-man and a piano-seller. Nobody had ever seen their furrier neighbor.\n\"Well, folks,\" said C. H. Spratling, \"let\u2019s pray.\"\nThey prayed.\nThe prayer was in two parts. The first part was asking the Lord to deliver Belentz, the pilgrim, back to the care and arms of his own precious family and give him a clear right mind. The other half of the prayer was asking the same Lord to back up Belentz\u2019s story, through circumstances and testimonies and forever, so that the monsters who had come in the night and snatched him off might be brought to justice.", "1_9_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine seventeen in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was seeing a psychiatrist three times a week and remained in the hospital another month. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s Army was without a recruiting sergeant, without a symbol, without any esprit de corps whatever.\nA day after his return from the hospital, $FIRSTNAMEB0 decided, offhand, to resign as recruiter. This he did by slipping a typewritten note under the office door:\nDear Everybody: I am sick of Army life, and I am sick of being in the 232nd Martian Infantry. My buddy $FIRSTNAMEB2 is still in the hospital, so I am going over there this morning and desert with him. Sorry to do this to you, but I guess I am through being an Army man. Your friend, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his buddy, $FIRSTNAMEB2, headed west. They had three wishes. Their wishes involved whiskey, women, and money. Their most cherished dream was to sit side by side in warm booths, drinking a whole lot of sourmash whiskey. The booths would border dark alleys where women would parade striptease. And there would be small, golden piles of money secreted about the tables and the floor.\nTo accomplish these three wishes, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his buddy, $FIRSTNAMEB2, armed themselves...", "2_18_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nThe man got back into the car. He put the case on the seat beside him. He drove away.\nThe woman came to the window of the cabin. She had her husband\u2019s space suit on. She looked out the window, saw the retreating car, waved happily.\nShe blipped on the lights in the house.\nThe spacesuits in the brown, dry closet responded to light.\nThey put themselves on.\nOne by one, they walked out into the woods at the back of the house in the sand. They began to hunt mosquitos.\nAt twenty minutes exactly to seven o\u2019clock, which would be seven o\u2019clock Earthling Time, the spacesuits opened the airlock of the house in the sand. They stepped into space, became walking planets that at once loosed their screams.\nTheir screams, thin and eerie, were carried off by the Mars- wind.\nThe screams were lost in space.\nThe spacesuits danced wildly.\nNo Earthling was there to see.", "0_6_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>, according to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s luminous watch. The church was a quarter mile away. $FIRSTNAMEB0 could make it in three minutes.\n\"Maybe you won\u2019t tell,\" he said. \"I\u2019m depending on that.\"\n\"Man, that church isn\u2019t really a church,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"You find out, when somebody throws you in there. That ain\u2019t no church, for true.\"\n\"Thanks,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"You ain\u2019t goin\u2019?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"I guess I better,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"if I\u2019m going to get any more pay. And I don\u2019t want anybody to know.\"\n\"Hey\u2014Man\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, trying to hang onto $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s arm, \"you can tell me. I won\u2019t say.\"\n\"What?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, shaking off the hand, hurrying away.\n\"That color\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"that color you got?\"\n\"What color?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, striding stiff-legged.\n\"That green!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"What green?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Grass green!\"", "1_19_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past seven o'clock in the evening</span>\u2014still the height of the Estoril day\u2014and the terrace was crowded and noisy, but the large table in the corner was free and I sat down with Edmond and Braddocks and two of the morocco-sack brigade. A waiter came and handed me the evening paper. Braddocks, who knew all the waiters, asked him to bring me the evening paper.\nBraddocks was of that bull-dog breed of Englishmen who seem able to go on forever without changing their humor or their expression, or their clothes. All his stories began, \"Once I was playing bridge at Cheltenham\u2014\" or \"Once I was telling this gentleman\u2014\" They all had a sort of happy ending. He never lost a hundred pounds at bridge, never missed a putt, never found his dead wife's letters in the arms of her lover, never got fined in the prep-school scrape, was never knocked out of his bicycle by the jehu of a brewer's dray and buried beneath hours of mud. He was a game-keeper with all the old traditions, loyal, hearty, and on the spot. He used to visit a house in Leicestershire. They had a glass front to their Ferrari: as you...", "1_3_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-six in the morning</span>. Our Camarades d'Infortune were loud and drunk beyond redemption. In their condition they were as dangerous as an invading army, as a force of Huns let loose under the walls of a Gothick monastery. One nervous bartender was handling their thirsty load by himself. The place was so packed with people that only a few could even see the bartender at all, much less reach him. And the sight of our squat, red-headed friends, their eyes goggling crazily in their skulls, reaching for drinks with both hands, slobbering, coughing, elbowing others aside, made others back away from the bar in horror. The bartender was in a hell of a mess. And we were too drunk to help him. We were in a different world. We were in the demi-monde of floozies, pimps, teemed, hustlers, hot-feet and desperadoes. We were scum. And so were our friends. This was the world of Iniquity our fifty-dollar stake had introduced us to in a big way.\nWe were standing in the corner of the room, watching our Camarades d' Infortune with ill-concealed disgust and amazement.", "0_3_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nHe flashed the light in our eyes.\n\"What the hell are you doing with a light in the sleeping car?\"\n\"We were looking for a friend of ours. We have to be in Sacramento in an hour.\"\n\"Oh, a fine story. What's your name?\"\n\"Rudolph Rumstich,\" I said.\nI had made up the name on the spur of the moment.\n\"Come with me, Rumstich.\"\nHe took us to the conductor in a small room behind the coaches. We stood on the clean floor of cattle country. The conductor was a tall, immaculate, reasonable older man and I liked him. He called me Rumstich just as the dick in the porter's room had called me Slim.\n\"What's the trouble, Mr. Agent?\" he said.\n\"These men claim they belong in Sacramento.\"\n\"We sure do,\" I said quickly.\n\"Tell him your name,\" said the agent.\n\"Rudolph Rumstich,\" I said.\n\"Where do you belong?\" he asked.\n\"Oakland, California. We've got an appointment there in two hours.\"\nThere was a long silence while the conductor thought.", "0_1_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-four in the morning</span>. As he approached the chalk cliff toward which the path led, he saw, with casual interest, a single, small white cloud floating in and partially obscuring the upper part hi the red cliff\u2014a cloud that did not change or move as he watched. His eyes moved on. Before him, at the end of the path, rose an altar of marble. Above and behind the altar, seeming almost to fill the sky, was the great black-and-white reproduction of the pinned beetle-wing he had seen in a book. His eyes moved on. Behind the altar, at a distance of fifty or so feet, was a shattered wall of hard, polished blue-and-white stone blocks. Behind the wall, at a distance of perhaps two hundred feet, fully in the range of his vision, and occupying almost the whole of the quadrant of purple sky hi which the single small white cloud floated serenely, was a tapering, crystal needle, possibly of ice. His eyes moved on an instant longer to drink in the details of the vision, and then on the instant turned fully back again. The white cloud above the chalk cliff had begun to move\u2014horizontally, parallel to itself. With the vision of his eyes restored, he looked once...", "1_12_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-four post meridiem</span> of an evening in the early fall of a year we\u2019ll call 1.\n: :\nGood God, I think.\nThis was my room.\nThis was my mother.\nThis was the house I grew up in, on Capitol Hill, with the drooping yellow flowers and the safe that had a combination with only one number. This was the house where my mother had been a woman who might shout at me to get out of her way as she ran down the stairs with my baby sister to take her to the hospital; and where she had been yet other women, ones who cried in the kitchen, late, opened bottles and smoked cigarettes in the dark. This was the house where I used to stand for hours by the gate after school, waiting for my father to drive home from work, and where I used to hide from him when I heard the tires on the gravel, because I was afraid of his anger. This was my house, the house where I grew up.\nI look at the picture again. The yellow of the album page has a certain undeniable charm, a charm that wilts, I must say, when transferred to the green leather of my album. Steer clear, I say to myself. The past is always somewhere else...", "1_14_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span> on Tuesday, during the middle of September of the year 2007. The place was in space, a good many miles above Earth. The place was inside a bullet-shaped cabin in a smoothly humming, well-oiled heavy-duty rocket called Space Wanderer.\nThe Space Wanderer carried 700 pounds of fuel, 5 pounds of provisions, a quartet of human beings, and 210 pounds of materiel from Earth to a temporary Earth-parking orbit.\nThe Space Wanderer would let the 700 pounds of fuel run down to exactly nil, reload for the last bound, and then hold its crew of four and its 210 pounds of materiel in Earth-parking orbit while the rockets of other ships blasted the crew and the materiel to final destinations far out in the heartless deeps of space.\nThe Space Wanderer was not equipped with radar, telecommunications, or other aids to navigation. Every move it had to be navigated the old-fashioned way, by the unjammable, never-mistakeable, sometimes-fatal reckoning of the Earth-trained eye, the Earth-trained brain.\nOf the four human beings inside the Space Wanderer, three were in good health\u2014in spite of the swaying and the rolling and...", "1_19_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was far out in the country\u2014so far out that suburban homes and white-topped tract houses were no longer in evidence. The landscape was still, with long meadows and tall, dark trees. Roads became narrower and narrower. And then, suddenly, fields. Endless fields of green wheat and golden wheat and brown late-summer barley.\nAt the end of the road was a white wooden shack, with a parking lot in front and a great aluminum water-tank on one side. There were two gas-pumps under the front windows of the shack; and over the windows were the hand-lettered words\nCLOSED\nRutherford's sedan hurtled out of the dusk, pulled up in a beautiful skid, and stopped ten feet from the office door. Two doors clapped shut, two engines were gunned furiously.\nThe cars, identical, were two-door Packards, low-slung, equipped with the bulletproof engine first used on the Army's \u201ctank\u201d during the war.\nIt had been called the bulletproof engine, although it really wasn't bulletproof. A bullet with enough force behind it could penetrate the shield. But by the same token, a tank's armor plate was not bullet...", "2_19_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span> on the planet Neptune, the planet that, in the Solar System of Moons, was nearest the planet Mars. One might say that the planet Neptune was within kissing distance of Mars.\nThe time-piece on the wall of the tavern on Neptune was a chronometer programmed to register the time on Mars by showing the time on Neptune. It announced, as it would have on Mars, that it was three minutes to eight.\nThe bartender of the tavern on Neptune was Moe Smith. He too was forty-six years old, married, successful, and rich.\nHe was six feet six inches tall and weighed two hundred and eighty pounds.\nMoe Smith, like $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, was a friendly man\u2014but nobody ever saw him smile or laugh. His face was normally radiant with a simple-minded joy in the wonders of being big and fat and owning half the planet.", "0_3_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span> and there were now only twenty-three minutes left before mankind would once again destroy itself with Secretarial Wars\u2014and Phoebe was asleep in the silver Mercedes-Benz with the purple wildflowers, dreaming of love.\nThere was nothing special about that particular love dream. It was like an Engelbert Humperdinck ballad. All that was in it was a huge bed, and Phoebe, and a man who was loving for as long as anybody could remember.\nAnd that enormous bill from the Lockheed Space Center.\nTHE SECRET STORY OF MARS\nI shall tell you a Secret.\nAll life is flawless and sacred and beautiful, except for human life on Earth. Earth LIFE is simply too silly for serious consideration. That is the key to my religion. You see, I have found a Universe full of beauty and excitement, mystery, ecstasy and real crazy magic. But the PEOPLE here are not part of that Universe and, since I find them both ugly and repugnant, I have resolutely cut them out of my calendar. I\u2019ve had more than I can stand of the nauseating farce and tragedy which passes, in this Universe, as life. And as for humans, all they do is whine...", "0_8_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. A group of neighbors, uninvited, was standing out in the street\u2014keeping its curious distance from the Tralfamadorian house. There was no hum or drone at all. The engine on the rocket ship was stone cold dead.\nA band of newspaper reporters and photographers\u2014 also uninvited\u2014was arriving in a disorganized mob, having been bummed along in cars by some earnest Americans who had planned an editorial orgy on the subject, \"Who Are We, Anyhow?\"\nOne of the earnest Americans was saying, \"Everything\u2019s going to be all right now, boys. When that mob up there killed their engine, they guaranteed their safe landing by Earthling standards.\"\nA newspaper reporter said, \"It looks like the whole house and orbit is frozen, straining for the kill.\"\n\"Somebody in that crowd sure ought to be willin\u2019 to talk to us and tell us what it\u2019s all about,\" said an earnest American.\n\"What we need,\" said the newspaper reporter, \"is the exact name of the creature who lives in the house so we\u2019ll know who to blame it on.\"\n\"Who ever the creature is,\" said an earnest American, \"he wants to...", "2_9_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until ten o'clock in the morning</span>. It was a quarter of a billion miles from Earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were standing in front of their space ship\u2019s receivers, trying to pick up air-to-ground communications on a two-way-Earth-to-Mars-squirrel-gun wavelength.\nThe receivers were stone dead.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had attacked the radio with a monkey wrench after $FIRSTNAMEB1 had presented him with the extremely difficult problem, \"Why is everything dead?\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had attacked the radio with a wild-bananas rage after he realized that he and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had never checked the radio for operation. To $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, the radio had always been a nonstarter\u2014a paradox that could, under no circumstances, communicate with another paradox.\n\"Freem\u2014nhhhh!\" cried $FIRSTNAMEB1, pointing upward, then frowning and shaking his head.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked up.\nRunning north and south, in parallel bands about a mile above them, were lines of low-altitude sentries on climate-controlled motor bikes. They were keeping unusual good watch.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had been spotted in emerging from their space ship.", "1_10_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-one in the morning</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time\u2014AnsonFord, President and Chief Operating Officer of the Riordan Ironworks Company, 1968-1971 and 1973\u2014, was playing with his secretary\u2019s breasts in her office on the sixty-eighth floor of the consolidated Mercury, Cicero and Locke buildings on East Capitol, while waiting for his wife to call. He was in an all\u2014we-are-both-naked kind of mood. His shirt, tie, shoes and trousers were lying in a heap on the floor at the foot of Evelyn Glcak\u2019s desk.\nThat\u2019s my home, thought Edna Heekin, out in her cubicle on the sixty\u2014first floor. That\u2019s where that skunk goes to snifl.t That\u2019s my husband. She looked out at the city from her south\u2014facing window. A hot wind blew, but in the sheltered top of the building it was pleasant. The sun was out.\nShe thought, he swindled me out of my life with that thing. She hated him, the thing, their synthetic, spiritually demeaning marriage. She thought, he could love with that thing; it should, if he had to have it,...", "2_20_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-two in the evening</span>.\n\"Skip! You in there?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 pleadingly.\n\"What?\" said $SURNAME1 shyly, opening his eyes. \"Oh\u2014you did it again, didn\u2019t you?\"\n\"You\u2019re holding back\u2014you\u2019re holding back!\" said $SURNAME0 accusingly. \"I suppose you\u2019re going to tell me you really didn\u2019t want to come with me this time? That somebody talked you into it?\u201d\n\"No\u2014no,\" said $SURNAME1 unsteadily, \"I\u2014I decided on my own.\"\n\"Yes?\" said $SURNAME0 curtly.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"You\u2019re not holding back?\" said $SURNAME0. \"You\u2019re sure you aren\u2019t holding back?\"", "0_0_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-seven a.m.</span> (Chumley\u2019s Pub clock.) Light dawned for Metzger in stages.\nSo Bruce shows up alive after all. That\u2019s Phase One. And then he says $FIRSTNAMEF2 gave him the idea for his new script. Which is patently crazy, but then isn\u2019t everything. So now she gets to find out what it\u2019s all about. Which brings us to the Cosa Nostra part, and the next stage, afternoon in the parking lot, her sitting in the MG, palpitating and lightheaded, not less so because Metzger refuses to kiss her even though she\u2019d flashed her panties at him (another story), and him telling her about being called up by Genghis Cohen, and agreeing to bring the post-horn to California, to a confab with other agents. And his lateness bringing on some kind of enforcement he didn\u2019t want to talk about. Nearing madness and maybe an expulsion from the freeway. So then she, $FIRSTNAMEF2, is approached by this Miles, who wears an armband so everybody\u2019s supposed to know what he\u2019s talking about and nobody does. Which does it for Phase Two\u2014sober...", "1_18_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nSpace Wanderer sat down on the floor of the space ship. He looked at his antenna helmet and his space suit. \"It looks terribly final,\" he said aloud, \"dressing up like this. But I suppose it\u2019s got to be done.\"\n\"I suppose it\u2019s got to be done,\" whispered $SURNAME1. He was thunderstruck. He threw open a wall locker, switched on a radio circuit, and heard $SURNAME0 sing the following song. It was a contralto solo by his wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nWho\u2019s writing this? said the song.\nNo one you know.\nWho\u2019s going to read it, then?\nNobody you know.\nThat does me a lot of good.\nAin\u2019t it the truth?\nWho\u2019s going to sing it?\nA contralto voice. Coming in on a pure high G.\nWhat\u2019s the name of it?\nThem dirty Doggie Blues again.\nWhoomp! There it is.\nListen, listen, listen.\nIt was a song of heartbreak, sung in the penetrating contralto of a woman in love with her husband. It was a song...", "0_21_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>, and the place was the outer space three hundred miles up, where the Martian intelligence agent continued to haunt the uncontrolled nuclear-rocket ship.\n\"Ha ha!\" said the Martian intelligence agent. \"Damn!\" said the intelligence agent. \"Damn!\" he said again. \"Back to Earth\u2014Marengo, Idaho,\" said the Martian intelligence agent to himself. \"Back to that sad hole again?\" he said to himself. He felt a great deal of pity for himself. Ten minutes later, in the crowd of two thousand persons collected inside the ruins of the Palace of Congresses at the Moscow Peace Conference, an old man felt a hand touch his hand. \"Maharani?\" he whispered. \"Is that you?\" he whispered, feeling very frightened.\n\"I want my satellite back,\" whispered the hand. The owner of the hand had dark hair, a dark suit, and a white shoe with a black-cloth face and big, hollow eyes. The eyes of the shoe stared at the owner of the hand.\n\"Waterfield,\" whispered the owner of the hand.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was startled, because the name \"Waterfield\" had popped into his head the minute the hand touched his hand. The name \"Waterfield\" was written, in...", "1_17_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-two in the afternoon</span> of a day in October of the bicentennial year of 1976. Lenore Beadsman was seventeen years old, legal. She had squeezed her eyes shut to find a place where everything was clean, where the only thing there was was a cracker. She was an inch over five feet tall, weighed ninety-four pounds, with a solid build lately tending toward plump, with big dark braided hair, with a round face that let her get away with twenty pounds more than she ought, with sharp little teeth. Her eyes were actually green, skittish, undeniably pretty, and tended by their colour and shape to look both sad and knowing, which Lenore did not find particularly pleasing, though she was used to it. She had walked over to Amherst College from the house where she lived with her father, a hundred yards away, by herself. She was now on a bench in a part of the Amherst campus called the Sculpture Garden. A squirrel kept running from the trees around one side of the garden to a trash can in the middle and running back, over and over, like it was driven. At intervals it would stop abruptly and drink from a puddle of milk that had leaked from the upper half of a Sty...", "0_15_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-four in the afternoon</span> of December the nth, 1990. The place was the headquarters of Stonecipheco Baby Food Products, in lower Manhattan. Certain events were in progress at Stonecipheco. Rummy little items of the sort that the newsboys of the nineteenth century had a special vocabulary for describing: items such as run-of-the-mine, going-to-waste, getting-jerked, to-the-bottom-of-the-barrel, rank madness; the contemporary equivalent of all these terms would read something like, \u201cBecause of altered marketing strategies in the wake of the deregulatory atmosphere, Stonecipheco was selling off in a giveaway 691 cases of Beecher\u2019s Babyfood Product A and 440 cases of Beecher\u2019s Babyfood Product C, which had reached an advanced state of sammying.\u201d\n\u201cI think we ought just to take the money,\u201d Clarice Beadsman was saying over her telephone. This is the (unlisted) number at which the Mandible family may be reached during the comma that separates the afternoon of the nth from the morning of the 12th, when the family is projected to have returned to its home...", "1_16_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. Metzger, having opted at last to go ahead and give everybody the bad news, snarled off down Wardman Street, trailing behind him a garland of curses. The Lincoln was found with difficulty and was half an hour getting started. Rex Americana, it developed, hardly ever used his car, and the battery was down. And then, when this was cleared up and the car got going, the gearshift linkage came loose. Metzger pulled into a gas station, did some imprecise tinkering, and at last got the car lurching out of town, up Wisconsin Avenue and onto the Beltway. Traffic was light. Metzger put the top down, lit a cigar, eased the shift linkage into neutral, put both hands on the steering wheel, and did his best to pass the time smoking and staring out blankly into the sky. For the next three hours he would be almost totally without possession of his faculties. To be sure, any passing motorist, watching him through binoculars and getting a glimpse of an eye or a nostril, might have detected an almost spasmodic alertness-and this would have been an entirely accurate inference. Metzger had a freakishly developed sense of hearing,...", "0_21_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-four in the evening</span>. Many youths were skipping rope on the sidewalk in front of the San Narciso Community College. As the white convertible pulled up, $FIRSTNAMEB3, looking terribly haggard over on Babe\u2019s side, the passengers saw the youths instantly stop their fast monotonous skip, what they call a one-two-three hop, and they saw the silvered arcs come to rest at the feet of the particular boy who was jumping when the rope lost its synchronized movement as if the macadam far below had suddenly been struck dead, as by a doom. It was at this point that a very pretty girl with blue eyes, who had been on the same side of the rope as the strange couple, now came over and exchanged a smile with the driver of the convertible, who wore a collegiate tweed cap and had a way of looking respectful and agreeable to all his surroundings. This collegiate-looking driver, who was a man about thirty, respectable and fatherly in his looks, for some reason that no one could possibly have anticipated, smiled and turned with his respectful, agreeable smile to his two disreputable fares, and said, \u201cNo place like home, boys,\u201d a line that was received in complete silence, in utter bewilderment. And indeed...", "0_22_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past ten o'clock in the evening</span> on the lovely warm spring night of May 27, 1953. The place was a lonely stretch of desert halfway between the little city of Reno, Nevada, and the little city of Winnemucca, Nevada. It was the grisliest way-station in the world\u2014the middle of the Nevada desert.\nThe two most hated men in America were lowered from a black helicopter to the desert floor by a rope ladder. They were wearing wigs, plain glass shatters, dark suits, white shirts, black neckties, and sunglasses.\nWhile their legs dangled in the air above the desert floor, $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas, in a white convertible with its top down, drove past the rest area at fifty miles per hour, never noticing the embarrassing scene above her. She was bound for the Yoyodyne Test Center on the shore of the Inland Sea.\nAnd Metzger? As soon as he was back among his many loyal subjects up in the foothills of the Coast Range, he too let the hours slip by to nothingness, with no more thoughts of the two men and their rotten luck; just as $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas, perhaps never conscious of having averted her own crisis by a whisker, headed out on...", "1_10_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-eight in the morning</span> when we got out of the cab and walked up to the door. The door was of walnut, many-paned, and the window went down to the floor and was also of etched and painted, faintly yellowed old glass. There was one bell and I pushed it and we waited. Then I heard nothing and I knew they had ear-phones on or something. But the door came open finally.\n\"Oh\u2014hello,\u201d I said. \u201cIs the colonel in?\"\n\u201cHow do you do?\u201d said the butler. \u201cCome in, please.\u201d\nHe held the door open and we walked into a big hall with walnut paneling.\n\u201cYou have to go clear around to get back to where you were ,\u201d I said to Cohn.\n\u201cShut up, Jake,\u201d said Bill. \u201cWhat do you mean by that?\"\n\u201cNothing.\u201d\nWe went up-stairs.\n\u201cYou're in 228, Bassett,\u201d Bill said. \u201cI'm next door to you. Jake and I took these two rooms.\u201d He opened the door of 226 for Bassett and then we went on to 228.", "1_18_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to seven o'clock in the evening</span> when we reached Bayonne. I had $FIRSTNAMEB3 make two insane abrup t turns that cut across the traffic and almost got us killed as we skidded on ice around Tax st street. That very afternoon I had bought a new overcoat and now I took it off and flung it out of the window. \u201cFarewell, overcoat. Borne on wings of novelties, away, away and be gone to some other follies -- went rolling on overcoat.\u201d\n$FIRSTNAMEB3's nose was running, he was chattering, he was in a coma. I had him park on a desolate back road of Bayonne and lock the heater full on to thaw out his system. Now and then he called out something and sometimes he wept. Then he solemnly filed a travel report to $FIRSTNAMEF0: \u201ce have ... e nough ... e nough ... e nough speed miracles at this point.\u201d It was already dark. We had left the city lights behind. We were in a winter-gray wilderness of industrial places, smokestacks, railroad tracks, oil tanks, barges in the river that made the red sun mourn. Gray worms of smoke coiled amid the everything. There was a chill and dre...", "1_20_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-two in the evening</span>. I was sick and tired of pictures of strange women with two breasts and four arms. I finished the bottle and bottomed it out in the tub. The bat made a dive for freedom as I opened the window. He was just too late. I scooped him up with a newspaper and carried him out to the balcony. He was making a lot of buzzing noise.\n\u201cGo on, shoo! Fly away!\u201d\nI tossed him into the air and he sailed off with long, slow, drunken wings. I watched him stagger off into the sad, sweet, blue dusk of the evil hour in Mexico. I had to laugh. Poor bat. He stumbled on a wire thousands of feet up in the air and suddenly there he was\u2014an angel with a hangover.\nI went inside, locked all the doors, and stretched out across the bed in my clothes. I set the room dark. Outside, far below on the highway, I could hear the sad bullshit rumble of Mexican busses. I fell asleep. I slept a long time and dreamed about the Admiral. We were standing in a field of ripe corn. The corn was cut and dried and shucked off the cobs. Across the field was a golden cliff.", "0_6_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, in the iron chair, found this information available by pressing his fingers against his eyes. The little metal figures of chrono-synclastic infundibula glinted next to the bed. There were seventeen of them. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had counted them before he got into bed, had counted them after he got into bed, had counted them while he was falling asleep, had counted them while he was asleep.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 stood up, hit his head on the ceiling of the bedroom, cursed the builders who had put a ceiling so low in a room of seven-foot beds, found a piece of orange peel in his hair, cursed. The orange peel came from the Korobeinikovs', the next room. One of the delightful and interesting customs of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s Treasure Island was that the inhabitants of the palace were not supposed to throw anything or anybody away. When a bit of butane gas from Ransom K. Fern\u2019s cigarette lighter blew the palace taster\u2019s head off, it was taken up to Room 7999, fixed to the ceiling, so that somebody else would find it. The Palace of cut-rate loneliness was like a crazy quilt made out of the misfortun...", "1_21_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>. The time had come for Martel to put his things away in the captain's stateroom. Pont the chief mate called up to him.\n\"You there, Martel? Come down and have some drinks,\" he shouted. \"The skipper is not coming down.\"\nMartel came down the steps and found himself among the hard-looking men. Two were firemen and one was a coal passer. There was Pont, the chief mate from Proven\u00e7al; there was Kromer, the mate from Schweinfurt; there was Baird, the mate from Edinburgh; and there was Moncrieff, the second engineer from Glasgow. They were drinking white wine and brown beer.\nMartel sat down with his bottle of beer. The three other mates all had white wine.\n\"Haven\u2019t seen you around much the last few days,\" said Moncrieff, the second engineer.\n\"Been busy, I suppose,\" said Martel composedly.\n\"How are you making it with old Wanstrom?\" asked Moncrieff.\n\"I like him all right.\"\n\"Oh, come now,\" said Moncrieff, the second engineer. \"Old Wanstrom is a horrible old bastard...", "1_23_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until midnight</span> when the palace guard stopped them. The palace was surrounded with a tall fence of solid gold. There was a gold guard hut by a golden gate. In the guard hut a mannikin with a real diamond-studded Mauser sat on a stool. He was real.\n\"What ho,\" he said, \"no passes, eh?\"\n\"We\u2019re broke,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Would you accept a solid-gold nut from the roller-bearings of a Mercury Comet as pass?\"\n\"Yah, sure,\" said the little man.\n\"The bearings are inside that hollow gold ball on the end of the pole.\"\n\"Yah?\" said the little man. He took the hollow ball, dropped it, and the ball made a clunk when it hit the paving. He dropped the ball again, and the pole made a whistle as it vibrated in the little man\u2019s hand. \"Sorry, boys\u2014we\u2019re under orders to check everything.\"\n\"Are you really and truly in the army, or are you one of the aristocratic Joes?\" said the amplified voice of $SURNAME0 from the loudspeakers aloft. \"I ask because the New was supposed to throw open...", "0_23_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. I had decided to see Carnival in the street. Any port in a storm. So off I went. First I made a big detour away from the TeleSur booth. I did not want to see anybody who might ask me what I had been doing. Then I discovered I did not know where Carnival was. Meanwhile, on all four corners between my hotel and the waterfront were slim, sexy senoritas, beautifully painted, signaling with their eyes. Back and forth, night traffic and day traffic, hundreds and hundreds of touristas were hoping to get picked up by lonely Americans. Ah, and the asphalt of the city by the sea was flat, and new, and clean, and the streetlamps, set far apart, made the lovely women sendivlas in the night. What a coconut, what a goat, what a catastrophe! My first night in Barcelona I was determined to see Carnival in the street.\nOh, I roamed and I roamed. I ran out of cigarettes and found a tobacco shop. I bargained in the dark for two packs of cigarettes (giving the man two pesetas for one). \u201cDo you know where there is any Carnival? I want to see some.\u201d The man shrugged and gestured toward...", "0_14_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. The sun was dead ahead from this side of the little bay, which made the shadow of any man, boat, or yacht run straight out for five miles and more across the flat waters of the Gulf Stream. One shadow seemed to fall in particular on the rail of the ketch at anchor. This was not surprising, for the ketch\u2019s name was the $SURNAME0, and the man throwing the shadow on the $SURNAME0\u2019s rail was the wealthiest private citizen in the world\u2014was the one man, in fact, who by himself, working with his own hands, could still do as much to influence the shape of human history as all the rest of the human race put together.\nThe man was young and extraordinarily handsome. He was lean and tanned, and he radiated vigor. His name was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nHe was naked. Sunlight made jewels of perspiration on his firm, flawless body. The shadow he cast on the $SURNAME0\u2019s rail was surprisingly long, considering that he was barefooted. He was barefooted because the deck of the $SURNAME0 was three inches thick with solid, polished ice. It was possible for $SURNAME0 to walk barefooted on the deck...", "2_1_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one eighteen in the morning</span>, Earth Prevailer time.\nSomeone slipped and fell on the gravel path. He cursed the cruel gravel. He cursed the cruel Earth. He cursed the cruel trick that had sent him to the cruel Earth.\nHis orders now were to go to the Mausoleum, open the outer shutters and blinds, go inside, perform the ceremony for the dead man once more.\nThis man had been sent to Earth eight years before.\n\"This way, soldiers!\" he said to the squad that had stopped at his command. He laughed, remembering how he had first spoken in English on arriving on Earth. He had supposed that the soldiers had been given an easy job, guarding stores and installations. \"A little fun once in a while, boys!\" he had bawled then. He knew better now.\nHe wondered, laughing at his nightmare game of Blind Man\u2019s Bluff on the planet Earth, how many thousands of miles he would have to travel to get off the accursed thing. He laughed hysterically at the thought of where he would have to go and what he would have to do to accomplish it.\n\"This way, soldiers!\" he said again.", "0_9_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>, on the morning of the twenty-fourth of June. Each tick of the clock was a thought. Each thought was a gleam of creation.\nThe clock ticked and gleamed.\nThe clock struck. It struck doom. It struck twelve times.\nThen there was quiet.\nThen there was nothing.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 got off his bed. One of his space-suited legs was caged in a gilt bar at the foot of the bed. The other hung free.\nThe left foot was a size twelve, Earth-mens shoe. The right foot was a transparent capsule half-filled with warm quivering yellow liquid. Inside the liquid, jellied, was an immensely complicated human foot.\nThe warm yellow foot inside its transparent capsule had been $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s pride and joy through a good many years of Martian history. They said it was the most complex foot in the Solar System. It served him well, and he thought it beautiful. HisSpace Wanderer\u2019s Handbookfor Space Wanderers said of a fellow who wore one of these capsules as a foot: \"An example of a true sportsman at heart is he who can wear Lew Ritter\u2019s Foot...", "2_23_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-eight in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME1 was standing at the foot of a bed that was three feet high, a bed to make three of a normal bed, a bed layered with steel springs, a bed on a hydraulic base, a bed from the showroom of the Dunberry Furniture Company of Dow, Massachusetts. This showroom was four thousand miles from Newport. The bed had been crated, shipped by express. It had taken the two materials handlers three days to unload it, three days of sweating and swearing under the crating blankets.\nThe bed was no different from a bed in any other American home.\nIt was to be the last piece of furniture in the house.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was now having a mattress made for it that was long enough for $SURNAME0, long enough for $FIRSTNAMEM2, long enough even for $FIRSTNAMEB2. Nothing would have pleased her more than to have her three boys sleeping together in a single bed. Her hope was that they would grow into the habit of it, that they would do much of their affectionate cuddling in bed. There was nothing, she felt, that would bring her three boys closer than that.\nSpeaking of affectionate cuddling\u2014\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 stepped into the bed.", "2_20_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-one in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 shut his eyes, hoping that his headache would subside before $SURNAME0 returned.\n\"Skip!\" whispered $FIRSTNAMEF1 in delight. \"There you are!\"\nAlvin could be forgiven for mistaking $SURNAME0 for a deity. After all, he had just seen $SURNAME0 ascend into the iron ship from a place in the Sun. His own memory of the ascent was hazy\u2014a series of snapshots that began with a glimpse of $SURNAME0 surrounded by a trillion golden cherubim.\nThe snapshots ended with $SURNAME0 and Salo peeping at him through the blinding light of the iron ship\u2019s portholes.\nA small part of $SURNAME0\u2019s mind had been given to Alvin and to the welcoming committee on Earth, while the larger part had been given over to the perspectives of the Universe. $SURNAME0 had seen the Universe, and he had participated in some of its supernatural adventures.\nWhat had seemed like a trial run of the space ship in the Sun was really a part of the Universe\u2019s great game.", "2_4_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-six in the morning</span>; Fifth Avenue was empty and yellow, with dim lights and a few rattly trucks parked at kebbuck shops. Not a soul was stirring. I swung around Washington Square and went humming up the silent avenue. It was all so dead that I zoomed around a corner and forgot to brake so that I went right up onto the sidewalk and skipped a poor dog who just stuck his head out in fright and while he hesitated I zoomed on and left him with his shoulders shaking and his eyes bulging in my rear mirror. I raced through another yellow sequestered street, turned downtown again, crossed the bridge, and once more saw the Hudson River sleeping, showed some dim lights from New Jersey shore, a few stars by the river. Luckily the wild girls didn't try to follow me -- they were locked in a super sex automobile conclave and for a long time nothing stirred in the silent streets. The spider slept.", "1_10_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-four in the morning</span> of a fine day in late December when I heard the message on my answer service, saying I\u2019d called my attorney in New York at his Bachrach agency in mid-town a short time before, that he tried and failed to locate me, trying my Sunset Strip address, then the Daisy, then both numbers again before leaving a message: \u201cCall immediately about something very important.\u201d\nI had slept fitfully, not dreaming nearly enough, and jerked up now with a psychic scream: I NEED SOMETHING!, but this was left as unspoken because there was nobody there to hear. My attorney was somewhere down the line...call him and leave myself open to recall; risk it. I went straight to the bathroom mirror and eyeballed myself straight: \u201cYou need a Great Soul...a Great Soul, and you know the score.\u201d A dangerous thing to whisper, but I knew he heard me. \u201cYou must face and enter the Crucible...\u201d Yes, this is it! Crucibles of gold, all four of them, but at thousand\u2014degree heat...well, he knows the routine. His death is in me; I will not let it go unmet, unmentioned, I will seize...", "2_20_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF1 was still a long way from having her children and her things packed.\n\"Excuse me,\" said Salo from the table, \"but do you know where I could get hold of a whaleboat?\"\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 didn\u2019t laugh, although she was amused by the question. \"I don\u2019t think so,\" she said. \"You\u2019d have to call the Cape, I guess.\"\n\"The Cape?\" said Salo. \"What\u2019s that?\"\n\"Cape Caper,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Near Provincetown?\" said Salo. \"I\u2019ll be in Provincetown before dark.\"\n\"You\u2019re lucky,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. She looked at her watch. \"If anybody\u2019s meeting you in Provincetown\u2014that\u2019s just about when they should be getting in at the bus depot.\"\n\"I\u2019m not meeting anybody,\" said Salo.", "1_2_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>. The space ship was moving at a speed of sixty-five thousand miles an hour, and all systems were working perfectly. The beauty of the free ion life was beginning to wear a little thin for me, however. I was ready to become a planetary creature again. I got Kirk to take over the controls, and I found the brandy bottles and got two more down my neck. I went to the radar room and took a turn around-the-world on the directional antenna. In every direction I pick up dots and dashes of Morse code. It was beautiful. Everybody on the air, with nothing to say to anybody, talking like fishwives. Radio operators and amateur enthusiasts all over the world, calling their friends and no one answering.\nThey must have wondered why such great masses of ionized particles kept passing above their heads on their way to Mars. The scientists put down the phenomena to the Chinese laundry in Sagittarius.\nAt three o\u2019clock in the morning we began to shower gently into Earth\u2019s atmosphere. Outside the ship, the air began to turn pink and hot.\n\"We\u2019re decelerating at forty miles per second,\" said Kirk.\n\"Hang on to your hats,\" I said.", "0_23_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-eight in the evening</span>, and young $SURNAME1 was not far from the truth. The Secretary of the Treasury was indeed a criminal\u2014who would eventually be put in prison for conspiracy, perjury, and tax evasion. At the moment, however, the spectacular moral collapse had not yet begun. The criminal, whose name was Wilbur D. Candy, was relatively young, robust, muscular, and handsome.\nHe had just had a photograph taken of himself in his underwear.\nThe photograph had been taken because the purpose of a proposed new law would be to get people to submit photographs of themselves in their underwear. And the reason for getting people to submit photographs of themselves in their underwear would be to make them mindful of the need for low taxes. The law, according to the proposal under consideration, would be a simple one\u2014one that would require no enforcement machinery or administrative costs whatever. It would simply be a matter of civilians sending in photographs of themselves in their underwear.\nThe Secretary of the Treasury\u2019s photograph, taken by his personal photographer, showed him posed like Christ on the cross, with his hands bound behind him.\nThe Secretary of the Treasury was amused by his own wittiness.\nOne hundred and fifty thousand subordinates in his Treasury Department, while they were not amused, were...", "1_8_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThough the cruiser was hurtling through space at more than a million miles an hour, it was motionless from the point of view of anything outside itself. From the inside, of course\u2014from $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s point of view\u2014things looked very different. He was in a spinning cocoon.\nThe cabin floor was tilted at nine degrees. It was covered by an eight-by-eleven throw rug.\nThe cabin itself was plaster and lath, drywall and shiplap\u2014a prefabricated box.\nThe box had been painted a bright metallic blue.\nThere were two port holes.\nDirty white drapes were bunched in their tracks, blowing and ballooning in a wind of ten knots per second.\nThe wind came in fitful gusts and lulls. The drapes fluttered like great birds.\nThe rocking of the ship was so gentle that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was able to lie unmoving on his left side on a low, zigzag-walled fo\u2019c\u2019sle deck that protruded from the cabin. He was dressed in pink wrinkled cotton pajamas, and his head was covered by a blue bag that contained a red bil...", "0_20_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>, Earth reckoning, when the wild band of Tralfamadorians let Salo Nash amputee out of the airlock of their ship. Salo was naked and generally gray, his body smeared with the substance used to neutralize the friction between his skin and Tralfamadorian buildings.\nThe other stuff he had been smeared with was green and stank.\nSalo found his feet in a clearing a hundred yards wide in a stand of deciduous trees. The best way for a beginning to describe the trees would be to say that they were ten Earthling feet high and similar in general appearance to the American sycamore\u2014only bigger.\nThere were fifty-six trees, in clusters of four.\nThe trees were not very densely packed. There was a good deal of underbrush.\nThere was no life in the trees. None, at least, that was apparent to Salo Nash.\nThe branches were bare and twisted, after the sycamore fashion. There were no leaves. There were no nests.\nThe trees were fundamentally gray, their trunks and branches veined with various other colors. The colors were in no way impressive, although they were pleasingly harmonious. There was white and blue and...", "0_5_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until six o'clock in the morning</span>, Saturday, December 31, 2208, Happy New Year, here was a switch. It was one minute until the Greatness. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 would be the very first people in the whole world to greet the Greatness.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 peered out through the peephole in the steel bulkhead.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shivered.\nThere were two other people out there in the cold iron silence.\nThey were not people whom $FIRSTNAMEB0 was glad to see.\nOne was a colonel of the Earthling Army. The other was a chaplain.\nThey stood a respectful twenty feet from the airlock of the ship. Between them, on the iron floor, was a pine box.\nIn the box was the body of a Martian named Wiley. Wiley had died trying to escape from the Army stockade. Wiley had died standing up, as Earthlings do, instead of sitting down as Martians do.\nThe stockade where Wiley had died was at Fort Kiowa, Wyoming, Earth.\nWiley had lasted twenty-three days in the stockade, which was three days longer than any Martian prisoner had lasted in the whole history of the Army. Thus Wiley had a hallowed place in Martian-Army folklore.\n\"Any...", "0_11_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-six in the morning</span>. I decided the hell with it. I picked up the phone and asked the operator to get me a Flamingo number. I had to wait quite a while. The slow clap of time. The clock ticked and tocked obscenely. I sat on the bed and waited. Pound, pound, pound. Finally the phone rang, and a woman I didn\u2019t know answered.\n\u201cYes?\u201d\n\u201cWell, hello,\u201d I said. \u201cThis is Doctor Tom.\"\n\u201cDoctor Tom, hello,\u201d she said. \u201cThis is Lucy, how are you?\"\n\u201cFine as wine,\u201d I said. \u201cI hate to bother you during your morning break, but has my Lucy been behaving herself?\"\n\u201cAbsolutely, Doc. She's been like a lamb for a full twenty-four hours now,\" I sighed with relief. \u201cThat's wonderful,\u201d I said. \u201cI'll be home in a few hours.\"\n\u201cWill Henry be coming home with you?\"\n\u201cOf course,\u201d I said. \u201cHe didn't have anything to do with this, you know.\u201d She laughed. \u201c...", "2_10_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nSunlight no longer generated electricity in any of the solar panels.\nSunlight could not be seen.\nThere was an eclipse of the sun.\nThe habitats, beneath black drapes of rain, were points of light.\nMagnifying the planet, telescopic observers saw volcanic eruptions at seventy locations. These spectacular events were the results of automatic systems having nothing to do with the disaster at Field Equilibrium Station.\nCelestial News presented a detailed simulation of the spectacle, interpreted it as a new cataclysmic variety of solar flare.\nUniciof the WASP Prime Minister appeared on celestial television for seventeen minutes and eight seconds. He struck a statesmanlike pose and condemned the catastrophe as a proof of God\u2019s wrath at the negligence of mankind.\nHe was widely and vociferously criticized.\nSince the catastrophe was obviously of human origin, there was no need to invoke God.\nThe cataclysm had been blamed on the Tralfamadorians. The Tralfamadorian theory had been proved sound. It was necessary only to disappear the Tralfamadorians to put an end to cataclysms.", "2_3_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\nIt would not be long now before the game would be over.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF1?\" cried Salo, coming into the living room.\nConrad VeATCH lay dead beside $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s chair on the sofa. His head was twisted in a paroxysm of too-early death. His old heart was obviously tired.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF1?\" Salo called again.\nThe master-of-ceremonies desk, against the wall facing the door, emitted an unmistakable sound. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had returned from his tour of espionage and was calling home with his signals.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF1?\" called Salo Bliss.\n\"Oh, God Almighty!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe chief of SEC\u2019s field staff in North America pitched to the floor of the cottage and lay unconscious.\n[A sum in German-Jewish arithmetic equal to \u00a35,062,396.11\u00bd.]", "0_11_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe man was $FIRSTNAMEB1, the aged retainer of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, who was in fact one of the least tiresome of the cockatoos.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had been trained to measure the passing of time by watching the hands of a Tralfamadorian time machine. The time machine was nothing more than a cheap alarm clock. When the hands reached a certain position, $FIRSTNAMEB1 was to do whatever he had been conditioned to do.\nThe thing he was conditioned to do was to hide in the root cellar adjacent to $SURNAME0\u2019s parterre. Best of all, $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s hiding place was a good three feet underground, was stone walled and, most importantly, had a stone ceiling.\nThe parterre at Skip\u2019s mansion was two hundred and ninety-eight feet long, ninety-four feet wide. The root cellar associated with it was sixty feet long and eleven feet deep.\nIt was reached by a hole in the middle of the parterre.\nThe hole was composed of vacuum-lock pistons in iron rings, and, when it was sealed, was airtight. The landlord could penetrate the seal at any time, by stepping on a foot-wide metal plate...", "1_15_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We were standing on the railroad station platform at $FIRSTNAMEM1, in the state of Oregon, waiting for the California Zephyr. Three hours in a bus and a hundred miles on a Sunday afternoon in the country often brings one to this kind of condition. It's a mystery. He couldn't have known anything about me... well, no, maybe he did. His face resembled Abe Lincoln's face in my imagination, as it does in anyone's imagination. It was a long, sad face and a beautiful one in every respect. He didn't speak English too well, but quite well enough to make himself understood.\nAnd with no further ado I took my ten-dollar bill out of my pocket and shoved it into his hand. He was taken by surprise.\n\u201cWhat's this?\u201d he said. \u201cWhat 're you doing that for?\u201d\n\u201cIt will make two even,\u201d I said. \u201cIt will make us friends.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Is this a trick?\u201d Confusion overwhelmed him. He looked around him as if he expected to find himself surrounded by police.\n\u201cIt's all right. Take it. Put it in your pocket.\u201d", "1_20_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span> on March sixteenth, in the year 1966 of The Beginning, according to the trusty chronometer installed in $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s brain only ten minutes earlier. This was $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s first independent time-trip.\nThe time-gatherers had decided that $FIRSTNAMEB2 was ready to go on his own. He was to collect fifty thousand Earthling years of evolution in the little red $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 boppers in the fountain outside. The fountain was called $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was at the controls of his machine in the middle of the square. The right hand of his machine was resting lightly on a Go button. The machine was making a clack-clack sound. It was badly in need of oil.\nThe machine was modelled on the only known life-form that had ever been close enough to the Galactic Center to see it clearly\u2014the life-form known to Earthlings as $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. $SURNAME0 and his dog, which was known to Earthlings as $FIRSTNAMEB4, had been the first living creatures in the history of the Universe to see the Galactic Center clearly.\nFor a clear sighting of the Galactic Center, Earthlings were advised to look through, rather than at, two...", "0_18_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-three in the evening</span>. The first act of the Pageant had ended, and the crowd on the field and the Terrace was pressing into the area around the Fountain of Eternal Life and its twelve gold-leaf lunettes. Conroy, $FIRSTNAMEF1, Valentine, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were caught in the traffic near the field bleachers. The meaning of the whole scene, like so much on the festival site, was so plain that Conroy commented on it: \"Hope nobody didn\u2019t miss that.\"\nConroy and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were carrying folded picnic chairs, which they now unfolded and placed on the ground at the edge of the crowd, forty feet from the gold-letter inscription in the polished stone of the fountain bowl\u2014\nIn this Fountain is the Water of Life.\nDrink deep, and perhaps Thou shalt\nlive forever.\nConroy Stalky held $FIRSTNAMEF1 Clarimonde\u2019s arm in one hand, and let her feed tokens into the drinking cup of her wheelchair with the other. Valentine, who was a little wet from pushing $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s chair, got out his handkerchief and dried off his temples. $FIRSTNAMEF0, meanwhile, kept smiling at them in a fixed, distracted way, her eyes seeming to tell them she didn\u2019t blame them in...", "2_9_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>, New Hampshire time. Something had happened. A few of the men were now punctuating their jollity with grunts of surprise. Something had happened to their fixed, mindless stares. The stares were now fixed and mindless with surprise.\nAt nine minutes past nine, the Great Walker arrived at THE HOUSE OF GOD. His retinue had been an hour ahead of him, so he found 240 men waiting to welcome him to their joyful fraternity.\n\"Hey, baby, what\u2019s happening?\" said the Great Walker, taking the hand of an incumbent doctor named Brubaker. The greeting was passed down the line in both directions. The Great Walker was a huge, booming person, and the greetings were in keeping with the man.\nHe was suffering from the grippe, and became progressively hoarser as he greeted men farther down the line.", "0_13_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Now it was time for the invasion of Earth to take place, for Old Salo to march with his three hundred thousand truculent Brandals and watch them stomp submachine gun holes in Earthling complacency forever. The plan was for Old Salo to march into Washington, D.C., leading his three hundred thousand, link up with the $SURNAME0 mansion and its contents, and begin the recruiting of Earthlings for a true Martian war of liberation.\nAll was prepared.\nAt two o\u2019clock, Old Salo was to fire his historic flare\u2014red, if the Brandals were on the march, green if they weren\u2019t.\n\"Hello, there \u2014 what\u2019s this?\u2019 said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, materializing with his wife and kazoo in the cavern\u2019s depolarized-air region.\nOld Salo turned sick as a sunburst. The pheasant under his arm turned sick as a deep-dyed rooster. And instant death descended on the dog and the three children who had been roller-skating on their mother\u2019s washline.\nAll life died, except for the life in $SURNAME0\u2019s voice.", "2_22_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-two in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and the harmonium had an appointment at the head of the rock gang in fifty-six minutes.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2014this gold-plated crapola we got\u2014this instant everythang\u2014ain\u2019t it wonderful?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I mean all the water we drink. All the air we breathe. I never had so much water before. I never had so much air before.\"\n\"I know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"You oughta seen him laugh, the Old Man did, when they told him what they did to you with that needle, out there in the cave.\"\n\"Laughed?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Like hell he did. He went crazy.\"\n\"That\u2019s what he likes\u2014when he sees you sick,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"Then he laughs.\"\n\"Keep hummin\u2019,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, putting his hand on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s wrist. \"Brother, listen. The music ain\u2019t no good no more. You gotta be quiet now, and I\u2019ll tell you about the dream I had this afternoon.\"", "2_14_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nFive minutes later, fully mobilized, the great ships of the Martian war fleet were streaking for Earth.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM2,\" she said nervously, fingering her glass, \"I\u2019m afraid I can never repay you for all you\u2019ve done for me.\"\n\"Repay?\" said $SURNAME1. \"It would be worth it to me if you would dream with me again. I so seldom get the chance anymore.\"\n\"The chances will be plenty around,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I\u2019ve just been talking to Skip, and he\u2019s given me a lot more of that stuff. Hachirochitsu, he calls it.\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Hachirochitsu, thank you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"A very nice-smelling powder. It turns any liquid into a sort of milk shake.\" She sipped her martini. \"Much tastier than plain milk.\"\n\"Do you mean\u2014we could put it in anything?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Anything, $FIRSTNAMEM2,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 brightly.\n\"In tea, for instance? Or coffee?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"In orange juice?\" said $SURNAME1.", "0_18_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-four in the evening</span>. At that time, a thousand miles to the east and thirty-nine hours, fourteen minutes, nine seconds to go, Mona Ramsey was putting her eight-year-old daughter to bed in their cramped quarters in the Space Center dormitory. That was Lilybloom, Ohio, too. It was the eight-year-old-girl\u2019s birthday, and the door of her air-conditioned room was open to the hot, thick August air. She watched male children of varying ages lob tennis balls against a brick wall in a court just outside her window. The ball-boys wore white socks with garters and stockings, and the sound of the tennis balls bouncing among the buildings was so clearly audible that Mona Ramsay had to shout to be heard above it. The racket seemed to be playing itself in rhythm with the clang and whir of conveyor belts somewhere outside in the Space Center workshops. A cold, amber twilight clung still to the window.\nConveyors.\nOne of the male children lobbing balls outside was ten-year-old $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. He was bent over the court, preparing to serve. $SURNAME1 had the only racket that was neon-taped. Yellow, black, orange. He was by...", "1_21_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-five in the evening</span>. I sat down at the bar and ordered drink. There were about twenty-five people in the bar now. It was crowded. They were not all Americans, by any means. Quite a few tourists on the tour from Paris to Pamplona were standing around, and there were Englishmen here and there. Most of the girls in the bar at this hour were girls travelling with the tourists, who made up for the lack of prices by paying big money to whores for a place in their bed.\n\u201cHello, Jake.\u201d\nI looked around. It was the tall, very good-looking Englishman from the bus.\n\u201cHello, . How are you?\u201d\n\u201cOh, not so bad. Sherry?\u201d\n\u201cGood idea.\u201d\n. He nodded to the barman and ordered the sherry.\n\u201cHow is the crowd?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cBit mixed, isn\u2019t it?\u201d smiled . \u201cSay, I\u2019ve been in Pamplona two weeks. Had a wonderful time.\u201d\n\u201cYou been out with the bulls?\u201d\n\u201cWhat? Been through the ring into the corrals...", "1_6_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six nineteen in the morning</span>; without warning, without waiting, without taking any advice whatsoever, Lenore Beadsman had finally set out on a journey. Despite her lack of destination. Despite her lack, really, of any kind of plan. Despite having woken up, finally, still in a nightmare. Despite her physical pain. Despite her being utterly alone. Despite the knowledge that if anybody ever found out what she was up to they would surely want her put away. Despite ... what was it?\nThere had been a sound at the door.\nLenore Beadsman had been captured by a clock. She was sitting at a real, actual, fantastic-I-love-you-Sue Woodsworth round table in a room with a door that was being tentatively started at. Light was entering the room. Lenore looked at the clock in front of her. Her table had captured the clock. The clock was sitting on a pale, clear sheet at the center of the table, because so many aggressive-quality bugs seemed to keep flying into and bouncing off and twirling around the real windows in the outside walls in rooms accessible to the public. The sheet was also a terrific job at camouflaging dirt on the table.\nThe door of the room made a sound...", "1_1_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>. Tralfamadorians did not measure time as we did. As far as the small group of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s estate was concerned, there was no such thing as time.\nThe space was the northern wall of a gimcrack birdbath, a yard and a half long, a yard wide, and a yard high. The wall was faced with unshoehosed bricks, cemented with horsehair and pigsblood.\nThe beleaguered and luckless creatures crowded in the birdbath wall had been lured there by the supernatural singing of the half-kitten, half-turnip creature known as Salo. The singing still continued. The singing had not abated once in the past thirteen minutes, but remained as loud and as piercing as it had been at the beginning.\nSalvation was finally at hand. At the end of the westbound track of the Newport town tram was a yellow two-seater decorated with paper streamers. The tram car was ordinarily used by children only, going from the neighborhood playground to Virginia Water, a pond with fish, ducks, and gold and white swans.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife Artemis manipulated the traffic on the hole in the wall...", "1_14_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been a man for exactly thirty-seven minutes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been an unmilked baby for exactly one hour and three minutes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had, during that hour and three minutes, wrinkled and died, forgotten how to breathe and how to blink, gone stiff and lost all vestige of human form. For exactly one minute of that hour and three, he had been buried twenty feet deep in a fluffy pink-white medium of purest cotton. For exactly two minutes and forty-five seconds of that hour and three, he had been displayed, rigid and blanching, in an open casket on the main floor of a skyscraper, in a borough of New York City.\nHis name, now, was neatly painted in gold on the lid of the casket.\nHis name was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nHe was the only person on Earth who had a name.\nHis name was a unsolved mystery to him. He did not know whether the name had been given him by someone who loved him, or by someone who had found him unlovable. He did not know whether the name was a cry of despair or a shout of celebration.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not know what the name was supposed to represent.\nHe...", "2_5_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-two in the morning</span>. We stood around looking at each other. The Wreckers were hardened veterans of the trade and showed no mercy. \u201cSorry, Mister; but you're just being used. The order come down from Big Lou himself, and we can't chicken out just because you got teary eyes. Either you get out or we'll slice you into ittybitty pieces right through this glass bastard.\u201d I got out and they drove away, taking the car with them. Back to the nightmare. Shit. Another four hour wait for the next Greyhound. I was due in New York the next day, Monday, and somehow I had to find a ride. The cabstand was dead. I stood on the highway with the roar of trucks in my ears and pondered the madness of my life. Ah, what a journey. What a wretched journey. To haggle and sweat with thieves over my own damn car because of a damn half -witted dream I had about a goddamn Sport's Illustrated swimsuit issue. WHAT THE DEVIL AM I DOING HERE?", "0_3_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>. At that moment, $FIRSTNAMEF0 was six thousand miles away\u2014sleeping in a penthouse apartment on Q Street in Washington, D.C. she had leased under the name of Georgia Cohen\u2014and had no more idea of what was going on inside her husband\u2019s head than if he had been on another planet. As far as she was concerned, that was just as well.\n\"I think you\u2019re deliberately trying to hurt my feelings,\" she'd said.\n\"No no no no,\" he'd said. \"You don\u2019t understand.\"\n\"I think it would be a good idea,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"if you didn\u2019t come near me for at least twenty-four hours.\"\n\"Good,\" he'd said. \"I\u2019ll stay by the pool.\"\n\"You might call Room Service and see if they could send me up a pot of coffee and a roll. Wait a minute\u2014bring the pot up yourself. You don\u2019t have to stay very long.\"\n\"I\u2019ll be there,\" he\u2019d said.\n\"Why not just drop dead? That would make everything much easier.\"\n\"I was thinking,\" he said, \"that maybe I ought...", "2_5_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIn that dim sepia fog we tried to check in but there was no hope; the Intercontinental was totally out of ice. Lashed to the desk was an angry notice from the management: \u201cNo Room in the Inn!\u201d Nobody could be checked in. \u201cGo to another hotel!\u201d\nWonderful! The storm was amusing itself outside; the rain was slacking off, the sun was trying already to break through. We lurched out of there and ran down the street.\nsalvador dali\u2019s hotel\n\u201cThis is the one,\u201d I said.\nNo! It couldn't be! Nearsighted with walleyed eyes, we stood and looked across at the blueness of it for a long time. The Droeding de France was so far ahead of anything else in Washington it looked like a hallucination waiting for us across the street.../\nThe long black limo wasn't moving. \u201cWe're here!\u201d I was shouting. My attorney seemed to nod off. But finally he came around and staggered out of the car with me. And in no time at all he was behind the registration desk, signing us in.", "1_23_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-eight in the evening</span> when the red-brown Cadillac, with three people in it, rolled by itself off a six-lane highway and finally jacked up in a gas station on the outskirts of Emporia, Kansas. The car had six inches of clearance all around. The driver -- his nametag said \u201cMetz\u201d -- had been feeling sick for an hour and a half, and dropped the car off the road like an old banana peel, to be rid of it. He had almost missed the exit. And he had been driving with the window open, had stopped chewing gum ages before when he threw up on his white suede shoes. His bluish hair was stringy and messy, and he hadn't had a shave in days. His two passengers were a little more alert. One was a blonde flapper, and the other was a rather handsome tired businessman; they had been sleeping off and on, curled up with each other. All three of them had had the runs for most of the trip across the Great Plains. They had left New York two days before. \u201cThe runs,\u201d the old-timers call it. Everything runs out on a cross-country trip; it's the price you have to pay for the glory of only...", "0_19_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nVIII\nTemple slept until after ten o'clock the next morning. She slept through reveille. She slept alone in a spacious bed in a room adjoining the one occupied by Lieutenant $SURNAME2, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and little $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThe night before, $SURNAME2, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had tried to keep awake. They had sat as still as possible, in that sudden stillness, straining their ears for anything that might suggest an approach from outside.\nThere was a faint murmur from the earth moving and grinding machinery that had nothing to do with the attack from outside.\nThere was a faint tickling breeze from a ventilating system that had nothing to do with the attack from outside. There was a clinking from a water heater that had nothing to do with the attack from outside.\nThere was a faint, unmelodious cry from a loudspeaker that had nothing to do with the approach of anyone or anything from outside.\n$SURNAME2, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had sat as politely still as possible, so as not to disturb Temple.\nWhen she awoke, it was with a luxurious sense of well-being. She experienced great surprise at finding herself in so splendid quarters\u2014sur...", "2_17_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five o'clock in the afternoon</span> in a railroad waiting room in San Francisco, southern California. She'd drawn the line against a return to wherever the Paranoids were having their kicks; ahead of her now lay nothing but miles and miles of identical houses, streets, stores, rooftops, people. Much more of that and she'd go mad. She knew it; she'd been here before. The paying of the money took about three minutes; she fled across the lot and into the station, wondering if somebody\u2019d been trying to put a Thurn and Taxis ticket into the mailbox, and at just whose expense the great perverted hoop of some bizarre put-on had been spinning since the day she'd first heard of the Trystero network, San Narciso, Pierce Inverarity and the Wharfinger play. She boarded the Oakland-bound train and, lulled by its movement, began to drowse. Her last reliable memory was of waking to see, beyond the slats of a wooden downtown office window, a punk rocker, raise up his bright golden hair to smile at her, revealing a glittering absence where his right front tooth had been.", "2_22_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-six in the evening</span>. Outside, the searchlights, the explosions, and the machine-gun fire were all part of the religious frenzy of the final assault on a foreign religion in the Plateau of Thrup.\nThe real reason for all the noise was that\u2014in three minutes and forty seconds\u2014the flight of the war-wagons would begin. All the troopers in the Jefferson Barracks National Cemetery were to report to their planes at that time.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had never bothered to learn any of the religious rituals of Mars, believing them to be without relevance for him. He now wished he had found a Chaplain, as some of the recruits had, so he could have someone to talk things over with before his time was up.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 knew that there was no one inside the antenna. He knew it from what he had heard the machine say when it had called the barracks of the East German Reinfantry Regiment. He knew it from what he had heard the machine say when it had called the barracks of the 305th Assault Commandos.\n\"Everybody to the assault in three minutes,\" the machine had said. \"Just take your religious ogram with you, buddy-boy.\"", "2_19_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-three in the evening</span>.\nAt seven forty-three, $SURNAME0\u2019s garden was empty. At seven forty-six, $SURNAME0\u2019s house was empty. At seven forty-six and one half, $SURNAME0 and his wife were home again\u2014back in their sacred bower on Midland Hill.\nAt seven forty-six and three quarters, $SURNAME0\u2019s intercom from the mansion to his studio zizzed with a message from his Master of the Hounds. \"Skip\u2014\" this is what $SURNAME0\u2019s butler, steward, and chief of staff said, \"I hate to tell you this, but your dogs got into the funnies again.\"\n\"I\u2019ll be right there,\" $SURNAME0 said. He tookPlanet of Adventures from his pocket, flipped idly to the illustrations.\nIt was all very casual. One minute Winthrop $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, were nowhere, and the next minute they were everywhere. They filled all times and all places, as far as the mind could reach.", "0_2_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two sixteen in the morning</span> Chicago time, according to the illuminated hands of $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s Space-Wars 3000 clock. It was a giocondo clock\u2014a clock looking forward to the fun of the millennia to come. The clock also happened to be a good Republican clock, a clock that had made a sizable contribution to the Richard Nixon Defense Fund on the occasion of $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s last birthday.\nIt was a clock that had taken a lot of ribbing from the infamous and blasphemous $FIRSTNAMEB3 of Students at the Illinois Institute of Technology, U.S.A. It was a clock, furthermore, that now hung with a lot of other rather strange things on the wall of a child\u2019s room in a house on Goose Hollow, east of the University of Indiana School of Architecture.\nLittle $FIRSTNAMEF3, who was just four and who had no idea as yet who or what $FIRSTNAMEM2 or $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill might be, had himself designed and executed the drawing on the wall to the right of the timepiece. The drawing showed a smiling blue sun talking to a smiling orange sun on a yellow field. There were slender green fish and stick figures of children, between the suns and the fish. The suns were talking and the fish were listening.\n\"What are...", "0_10_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-six in the morning</span> of December 31, 2000, and the place was a Desert Rat In the Fireman\u2019s Parking Lot of the Industrial Motel at 2094 Industrial Road in Las Vegas. The Motel had made its debut in Las Vegas in 1967 as a luxurious playground for handsome young men with slender wrists and hula-hoop frailties. By the time of our story, the Industrial Motel\u2019s industrial nature had been to make it hideous, but still a magnet for single Pretty Good Guys from Des Moines.\nThe fireman on duty was in the Rat, having a cheesburger with grilled onions, a mixed vegetable, a Nesselrode pudding, and a cup of coffee the approximate shade, flavor and nutritional properties of battery acid. He was reading a technical magazine of rather specialized interest, so he didn\u2019t look up when his patron came in, but there was an odd little something-something in the way the patron walked that got the fireman\u2019s attention: something kind of ... unreal... delicate... sensitive... sinister... unwanted.\n\"Help you?\" the fireman nodded curtly to his cheesburger.\n\"Bourbon on the rocks, Fritz. Straight up with a beer chaser.\" The voice...", "2_21_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-seven in the evening</span>. The place was the barrier of a pay phone in the lobby of the east wing of the Infirmary. Phone punctured by a bright red Cleveland Orchestra ticket inserted under the tines. On the other side of the lobby was the door to the east wing proper, as well as the swinging door to the employees\u2019 lunchroom, also dimly lit. The lobby was deserted, lit by only a few night lights, and smelled of rain. There was more rain just starting to leak in, from the outside, through the towering glass side. The shadow of Vlad the bartender fell a threatening length all the way across the lobby. Vlad kept moving his big feet all around.\n\u201cBarb?\u201d\n\u201cRick.\u201d\n\u201cAre you still ... ?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\nSilence.\n\u201cI\u2019m taking a seat.\u201d\n\u201cRight.\u201d\n\u201cOnly I just felt I should give you a heads up.\u201d\n\u201cRight.\u201d\nNoise of someone taking a seat.\n\u201cStill at my seat.\u201d\n\u201cYou sound a long way off.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d", "2_21_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nSeptember\nAt 6:50 A.M., in the rime ice of the night, in a box car taking a slant northeast through North lontana County, Redwine suddenly looked out of the car to see a few stars shining through breaks in black clouds racing past a burning heaven. His lips moved. He said, \u201cWe\u2019ll make it, man.\"\nNovember\nThe scream of the iron-wheeled train cutting the Ohio afternoon was just about to dip under the thrum of field sounds when a single voice hazily got through to the High Priests at their pastoral throttles.\nNovember\nThe owner of the voice was staring straight into the ear of a local snake, also looking straight into it, frozen like a forest in perpetual war with itself.", "2_18_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to seven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earthling Time.\n\"Hullo ... no, Frances,\" came $SURNAME1\u2019s voice from the gabber's little sphincter-phone. \"Nothing on the market yet. I\u2019ll let you know ... yes, I\u2019ll be down there shortly. Is the Old Man\u2014?\" He seemed to have trouble with the word. \"Is the Boss around?\" said $SURNAME1. \"I want to talk to him\u2014and I want to tell you something, Frances ... something I\u2019ve been keeping from you\u2014\" $SURNAME1\u2019s voice faltered.\n\"Oh, my God!\" whispered $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"He\u2019s going to break off our engagement.\" She covered her face with burning hands.\n\"What?\" said $SURNAME1. \"Oh, that! No, Frances. I wouldn\u2019t do anything like that over the phone. Look\u2014I just want to tell you something, while I\u2019ve got the chance\u2014I gave you all my money, didn\u2019t I?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Money?\" said Frances.\n\"I gave you my stocks and bonds, my real estate, all my money,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Right?\"", "2_1_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span> when the things in the cases began to answer the howls of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 with their own raucous calls and chirps. You could have mistaken the museum for a jungle.\nApproximately one hundred and seventy-three species in the museum had young at that time.\n\"As the young grow they become more and more intently involved with the things around them-their habitat, the thing they mirror,\" said a placard in the museum. It went on to say that all of the species in the museum reproduced with such abandon because their life expectancy averaged less than three months.\nThe placard neglected to say why their life expectancy was so brief.\nAll the things were mildly poisonous, as a rule. They had to be, to protect themselves from the Huns and their predecessors, the Marthas and the Morrisons. The things were well fed, well watered, and well sheltered, i.e., food, water, and shelter were plentiful in the caves. All that the things needed in order to thrive was a little protection from motiveless violence.\nThat little protection they got from being mildly poisonous.", "0_15_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three nineteen in the afternoon</span>. The Mayor of New York, A. Harry Sugarwell, now came out of the hall, accompanied by former Mayor Flynn, and by Al Rumford, president of the Manhattan Cultural Commission, and by Rodney (\"Bobby\") Gerber, the seventeen-year-old sponsor of the Drop Overture Festival. The sponsors of the festival walked close together, discussing the meaning of the piece of music just concluded. \"That too was bitonality,\" said Al Rumford.\n\"What?\" said the Mayor.\n\"Bit onality. Which means that one is using more than one tonal system simultaneously \u2014 more than one key. I mean, that piece was rubbing elbows, if you\u2019ll pardon the expression, with the keys of C-sharp minor and B-flat minor.\"\n\"Dear me,\" the Mayor said.\n\"Yurp,\" Bundy said. The chord hung on the air. It shimmered. $FIRSTNAMEF0 Keeder was beaming her approval from the organ-loft.\nThe chord vibrated, died, vibrated again. We still heard it when we went outside into the April sunlight.\nAt three thirty the fine mist of a warm rain began to fall, and glasses of champagne \u2014 again...", "1_9_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine o'clock in the morning</span>. She was perfectly clean. Her face was powdered and her lips were lipstick'd. Who the hell is this broad? That was the question that bothered my attorney as we tiptoed into the room. I only knew one thing, and that was that her name was Lucy, and that I had spent half the night in her company.\nI stood behind the woman, with my hands on her hips, as my attorney surveyed the scene. Then he walked over to a huge cop who was sprawled on a couch in the sun and shook hands with him.\n\u201cOld Bull Lee,\u201d he said, by way of introduction. \u201cNew friend of mine. At least meeting him for the first time...after a very curious night.\u201d He looked at me, and I was grinning uncontrollably. My attorney's face was very hard and he got that confidential tone in his voice that drunks always use when talking to cops:\n\u201cFirst time I ever heard of a thing like this, Bull. Simply no warning...no time at all to adjust. You're a police officer, so you know what that means. A thing like this you don't dream about...it hits you.\u201d He looked at me again; I...", "0_18_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-one in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time, and the place was Newport, Rhode Island, on the seacoast, in a very old wing of a very old house that, farther out, was attached to all sorts of additions, and was apparently, at the very least, a very big house indeed. The wing in which were the rooms had an old-fashioned look about it, with its high, sloping golden ceiling and its dim lights, of brass and frosted yellow, giving off a sort of yellow glow that was soft and dancing and more dreamlike in quality than actual dancing light. The golden-green air, stirred very slightly by the breeze coming through very tall windows, reflected the dancing of the softly lit lights, and made continually revolving under the high ceiling dim, ghostly patterns, of dust. It was through one of these tall windows that in a second the rumble of many horses and the rumbling thunder of wheels, the sound of the thousand and one marching apparati of the social season, of the carnival of youth, passed up and down, echoed against the walls, and died away in a throbbing gasp in the very depths of the air. Grace\u2019s Room was a long, narrow room, and very high. The ceiling was so high...", "2_7_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-two in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was parched.\nA trumped up liquor-dispensing machine would have made me feel better than that machine made $FIRSTNAMEB0 feel. The liquor\u2014dispensing machine had failed to deliver liquor. It was defective. And $FIRSTNAMEB0 was a major in the Space Infantry.\nAnyone who started to read his thoughts right then would have found them very interesting thoughts indeed.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was thinking about the contrast between deep space and a barrackyard on Mars. The barrackyard had a mossy wall where a man could put his back against the moist and mildewy stone, where a man could lean and think and watch barracks doors, and barrackyard doors, and watch all the ill-disciplined slimy life of the place slither by.\nDeep space had no real contrast with that. Deep space was simpler and purer.\nThere was no part of deep space in which a man could lean and think and watch slimy phenomena slither by.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wished that the shots of whiskey he had taken had been potent enough to take him to deep space. Not a right thought for a major to have.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 laughed.", "2_10_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s nation attacked with all its might at twenty minutes after eleven, gave up at twenty-one fifty-three.\nIt never attacked again.\nA Space Rube Tries Anarchism\nFor $FIRSTNAMEB0 and all other mobile troops, the half-hour between ten forty-five and eleven fifteen was an exercise in historical etiquette. There was nothing the troops could during that period but play at being a conquering army. Nobody knew exactly what a conquering army did during that period. A few threw themselves prone on the ground and kissed it. But that seemed not being in character to a lot of other troops. Some practiced marching in place. But there were no footsteps in mercury, and the practice made men clumsy.\nSome copulated with their comrades. But that seemed not being in character either, and there were almost no takers. Assault troops who, minutes later, would attack Earthling barricades with homicidal fury played at being revelers at a drunken victory celebration.\nExactly at eleven fifteen, the loudspeakers on all the barrack roofs played a twangy German march.", "1_20_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span> of Earth day, July 6, 2194, when the magnificent ship that was known as the Space Wanderer lifted itself without a tremor from the surface of the earth. The ship carried no passengers, and it carried no passengers\u2019 quarters.\nBefore the ship had achieved its liftoff, a microphone relayed the voice of $SURNAME0 to the revelers in Newport. \"Flee!\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice. \"Flee ominously and wonderfully, you paragons! Flee as though the momentum of a billion years were carrying you away! The Space Wanderer, carrying no cargo and bearing no crew, will circle the earth once every seventy-eight minutes. To the best of our knowledge and belief, the Space Wanderer will remain in the Solar System for ten million years, or about one percent of the time that has passed since life first appeared on Earth.\nA farewell from the keepsake library of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, sole forefather of the Human Race:\n\"\u2019As I leave this Star on the Day of the Last Judgment I commend to the keeping of Got $FIRSTNAMEF3, the God of Space Wanderers, Earth, in the Solar System, a Planet with but a brief future as a Haven for Hom author...", "1_23_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA tall human being approached the microphone. His right hand was bare, and he slapped his chest with it.\nHe stood so that the microphones would pick up whatever he said. He spoke directly to more than a billion people. He began speaking before the minute hand on the clock had moved.\nHis bare right arm slapped his sentry, and he said in a very ordinary speaking-to- peasants voice:\n\"Ladies and gentlemen\u2014and you, too, boys\u2014tonight is the first time in history that anyone has ever hit anybody with anybody with a snow shovel\u2014on Neptune.\"\nThis was apparently the code signal for insane hilarity. The next thing that happened was that 17,999 human beings came swarming around Norman Knight, in the front rank of whom was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, wearing blue tennis shorts and a skimpy mauve halter. The rest of the crowd wore the same. $SURNAME0 was the life of the party. As far as the staggering crowd could tell, they were dancing a Virginia Reel.\nWhen they were reminded by the clock on the public-address system of the economy effort in shoveling, they stopped as though someone had thrown a switch.\nThey stood frozen in a...", "0_10_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-three in the morning</span>, Washington time, when $FIRSTNAMEB2 appeared in his bedroom. The entrance of $FIRSTNAMEB2 demolished a tower of dominoes leaning against the bedroom wall. The falling dominoes struck six French horns overhead, producing a most sensational disturbance.\nThey were the only noises. There were no scurrying servants in the corridor outside. No children rolled marbles along the hallway.\nOccasional sounds of laughter and the trotting of tiny feet drifted up through a hole in the floor. There were no sounds of lust going on. $SURNAME1\u2019s palace was not that kind of a house\u2014not on the floor occupied by the family.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, the family butler and most efficient servant, made sure all was quiet and in order before going to wake his master and mistress. He always did that. He opened all the bedchambers in his section of the palace before entering them himself.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 now turned on detailed, precise lighting in the $SURNAME0 living room. He then shuttled his gaze between his master\u2019s face and his pocket watch. His timing was critical. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had to wake everybody up within five seconds of ten twenty-three. To do it earlier would make people groggy. To do it...", "0_5_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five eighteen in the morning</span>. The sun was not up yet. It was still the blue hour of morning, the hour when one sees so many things that can only be seen once, before the world decides what it is going to do and prohibits you from doing it too.\nIt looked so fresh, so innocently obstinate about being awake, this little city under its window that he wanted to touch it, as if by touching at least he would know what it was he loved. At this hour, in this light, in this clean, forgotten little town at the end of a long rail journey, he loved everything, even the university that had rejected him, even the vision of himself working in the yard of the Indianapolis News, even the silly hats and coats that Broadway made one wear.\nAt five in the morning, in a clean, quiet town like this, one could recover all those drowned feelings of youth-freshness, ideals, aspirations, and there was this about it-the things that you wanted most in the world you could probably get for twenty dollars. Did it matter what the world was going to do? Did anything matter but the fact that the sun had risen, that the stars were fading, that one was astonished to be here at all and to be looking at this...", "2_8_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span> when Bukowski heard feet on the catwalk outside, and looked up to see old $SURNAME0 come bounding in under a wriggling, full-size reproduction of the Year 2000 Martian moon rocket.\nThe reproduction was made of thin bits of colored paper, spattered here and there with blobs of dry glue. It looked altogether pitifully flimsy.\nIt was with great bitterness that Bukowski realized that it had been thrown together by penny-ante R&D boys right here on Earth, with no help at all from Martians.\n\"General, \u201d said $SURNAME0, bobbing up and down on the balls of his feet, \"the reason I am sure everything is going to come out right is that I know something you don\u2019t know, and I\u2019m going to share that something with you.\n\"The something I have to share with you is rather complicated, so I\u2019m offering you the written word of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 on a subject in great need of written words. If you read closely enough, you will find that everything you need to know about what happened here this morning is contained in The History of Earth According to $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.", "0_9_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to ten o'clock in the morning</span> of a July day in 1705, while overhead the sun was burning through after a night's rain. Montoya, mounted on a dappled gray jennet, rode down out of a rocky pass in company with another regular of the Holy Office of the Inquisition, a short stocky man with a villainous face, who sat muffled in a cape on a tall black horse. Behind them were two mule packs, heavily laden. Up on a rocky bluff an old man, who seemed to be a herdsman, was calling to his goats and sheep. Above, on the crest of a jagged cliff, a blockhouse overlooked the road.\nWhen they were past, the herdboy looked after them, thought Montoya, and caught some phrases from their conversation, judging from their gestures. The old man turned back to his animals, shaking his head. The goats nosed about the rocks and in the grass. The sheep crowded together in a mass and schemed among themselves. The goatherd leaned on his pole, listening, as if this were the most interesting thing that had ever happened in the world. Montoya wondered what it all was about.\nThe goatherd, fascinated by the horses and riders, forgot his animals; the...", "1_6_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past six o'clock in the morning</span> of December 21, 2008, $FIRSTNAMEB2-base calendar. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s family was waking up reluctantly as the light streamed in through their open skylight. The skylight was right over the head of the bed. $SURNAME1\u2019s mother was waking up first.\nShe was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mother, too. Her name was $FIRSTNAMEF0. She was forty years old. She was very tall and very thin.\nShe had a pair of very long, white, bandage-wrapped legs. The bandages were supposed to make her look shapely. They were supposed to make her look taller.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 wasn\u2019t exercising or doing yoga or anything like that. Exercise makes people look healthy, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 was anything but healthy. There wasn\u2019t much wrong with her\u2014but whatever was wrong was almost certainly fatal.\nShe was very beautiful, though, and it made her happy to think that she would be beautiful dead. \"Ugly\" people, like her husband, always imagined they would suddenly look beautiful dead. They were wrong. Only beautiful people looked beautiful dead. It didn\u2019t do them any good then, though.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 lay looking at the skylight and thinking that...", "0_8_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span> of the first Monday in October of the year 1973. The place was a green and pleasant spot on the north shore of the Potomac River in the District of Columbia, known as Fort Reno Park. A local businessman had donated the land for use as a war memorial. The stone for the memorial had been quarried in Ohio, and the inscription chiseled by genuine World War I veterans. The names on the list being honored had been gathered by schoolchildren all over the country, and engraved by stonecutters working without overtime, who felt that the children\u2019s educational project was far nobler, as well as more God-breathed, than anything they could have produced had they worked round the clock, buttressed by coffee, benzedrine, and absolute isolation from the civilized white world.\nThe names on the monument were:\nDottie Adams\nEarl Billings\nJake Blount\nWilbur B, Dryfoos\nFrederic D. Hoffman, Jr.\nJames Reston, Jr.\nHugh Steuben\nand Maxwell Simcox\nThe sun was streaming through the trees onto the green, polished stone, and onto the names engraved on it, and onto the earth beside it. A sodden autumnal...", "0_3_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span> when this happened. Before the sun had risen, the L Men sent a telegram to Asa Blanchard, the former assistant of $SURNAME0, asking to be named by $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 as his inheritor in the event that $SURNAME1 failed to return from the Space Wanderer\u2019s collapse. Blanchard, as administrative head of $SURNAME0\u2019s estate, confirmed himself as $SURNAME1\u2019s inheritor.\n$SURNAME0 did not die.\nAt the moment the sun broke over Destiny Island, he stirred. His eyes opened, and they were milky with cataracts.\nHis rigid point-Pluto jaw sagged. His lips moved. He said something.\nHis voice was hoarse, feeble, ghostly\nAlmost nobody could have heard it.\nAlmost nobody did hear it.\nOnly a parabolic microphone, mounted at the wall by the bench where $SURNAME0 lay dying, heard it\u2014and reported to the universe the words $SURNAME0 spoke. The microphone and its connecting cable were wired to a public address system that ended in the booth of the man who parachutes down the stair in the sedball stadium. Most magnificently, the system ended in a rocket-powered alert cannon fixed outside the...", "1_20_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-nine in the evening</span>. I was eighty-five miles from where I'd started out in California thirty hours and ten minutes earlier.\nThe exhaust beat on and on as I flashed across the newsstands of downtown Las Vegas at thirty-five miles per. I went by a mass wedding being performed in the center of Fremont Street, under the swirling gold and blueights of the overhead canopy. The bride and groom were being approximated in their gaudiest, most flamboyant clothes, the bride in a mussed-up white wedding dress, the groom in black top boots, strapshorts, a top hat, and a flaming orange beard. Witnesses to the ceremony were grouped around the happy couple, prying themselves off the floor and being helped up by benign and patient ushers from the WeddingVite Bureau. The witnesses were a potbellied pig in a diaper and a straitjacket, a pink and bald baby in a Trojan diaper, a young man wearing a straw hat decorated with cow manure, and a mouse in a white tuxedo jacket, with a toy top hat in its paws.\nEvery five minutes a new set of witnesses was brought in from WeddingVite limousines. Some revelers wore beer cans on their heads, others had...", "1_6_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-six in the morning</span> and I'd been awake for fourteen hours. The wine, Bohn's might, had entered my bloodstream...and now,, leaning against the rail of the press box, gaily aglow among the ghosts, 1 felt the Red Sea parting of my mental membranes, and great Scott\u2014the entire history of the decadence flashed before my eyes, and I, Moe the Friendly Rub. Both warning and lesson came too late. One awful fatal day in 1955, down in Newport Beach California, they blued my number and I never got to go to basic training. This, again, is a Catholic country, don\u2019t pretend you're above it. But it pays to understand, if possible, the deeper purposes of your higher-ups, or at least the types of minds that lie behind the wheel. Like they say in the Army, there are three kinds of generals. The good ones are the dead ones.\nBut by the time I understood all this it was too late to repent. So we'll just say, for the moment, that I was one of the very few Americans privileged to watch\u2014in the press box at Lambeau Field in Green Bay, Wisconsin\u2014the final act of the 1946\u2014part of the story of World War II as it...", "2_20_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAnd on Earth, it was only half an hour until bedtime.\nCorplet\u2019s last orders, irrevocable and iron-clad, had been, \"When the party line goes dead, don\u2019t do a thing until you get further orders. \"\n\"Kill the power, shut off the air and water, but don\u2019t do a thing,\" said Corplet. \"If anybody comes out of the ship, don\u2019t attack them and don\u2019t let them in. Don\u2019t try to escape, and don\u2019t try to warn anybody who might try to escape. If anybody at all comes out of the ship, sit tight, wait for orders. \"\nIt was a minute to nine o\u2019clock in the evening on Mars\u2014and the party line was still in business. Rich enough and crammed with enough conflicting possibilities, it was going to be a good half hour yet.\nCorplet, obeying orders to the letter, periodically called a number in New York City.\nOn the eighteenth and final call of the evening he got an answer\u2014in Martian. \"Yes?\" said a Martian on the other end of the line.", "2_5_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past five o'clock in the morning</span> when I clambered aboard that plane for Oakland/San Francisco/L.A. custody/whatever. I brought nothing with me but my youth, a credit card and a terse sense of mission. The American Dream, wrapped in newsprint, tucked into my back pocket. I boarded the plane in a mood of grim introspection and took my seat in the very last row, next to a window.\nThe stewardess came down the aisle and lit the sign above the exit sign above my seat: \u201cNO SMOKING.\u201d She paused and cleared her throat. \u201cWould you like something to read?\u201d she asked. \u201cW-w-w\u2014what do you have?\"\n\u201cWhat would you like?\u201d She had a rhythm, a speech\u2014pattern that closed around me in wide walls of sound. I couldn\u2019t answer. My teeth were making a horrible grinding noise. \u201cSing something,\u201d she said. \u201cWhat do you like?\"", "2_5_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span> when we remembered the cocaine. We ransacked the back seat, almost suspecting that the cops had slipped it to us, but no, it was there all right. We dumped much of it out on a magazine, stashed the rest carefully between the seats and drove on, more relaxed and open\u2014mouthed than we had been since Buffalo 174 miles back two hours. We\u2019d rounded the capital, punishing Virginia, and were approaching Washington. The thick dawn Popped up around us, superimposed on a black, funky morning sky and for a moment we felt as if we were inside a yellowed boxing glove.\nThe sight was incredible. Here it was, the height of a rainy season at the edge of the Potomac, and all around us the sky was popping and writhing and flashing like Hollywood Boulevard on New Year\u2019s Eve\u2014except that instead of confetti and roses, the atmosphere was filled with heavy leaden pellets of hail...pellets almost large enough to fell trees, hailing straight down like gravel, bouncing off the surface of the road and the shoulders and forming drifts, at some points, almost a foot high. The city was drenched in it.", "2_6_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>. The rough earthquake had become a heavy, shaking vibration that grew worse and worse by unsteady degrees. And it howled. Hollywood boulevard was like a forest in an earthquake. Trees snapped like matchsticks and their tendons flew apart and whipped back and forth like whips while their branches spun like the blades of power saws.\nCamaros, Mustangs, Firebirds, and Barracudas spun beside them in a terrible slow motion. A Dodge Polara lost control and mounted a Mustang, which fired like a rocket down the boulevard, broke apart, and slid across the hood of a Camaro and into a Platina 5. Other spinning automobiles hit other spinning automobiles and tore them apart. Sheet metal flew up and away, smashing diamonds as they sliced away on concrete and asphalt. Chrome, going around and around, flew apart and showered the spinning and spastic boulevard with dangerous, glittering shrapnel.\n\u201cGaw,\u201d said the cop.", "2_17_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\n\"We don\u2019t know,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Skip?\" said Moncrief, coming into the room, looking like a thunderstorm over the Hindu Kush.\n\"Who the hell are you?\" said Moncrief to $SURNAME0.\n\"My name is $SURNAME0,\" said $SURNAME0 mildly. \"I am a friend of $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s.\" He spoke with a Harvard accent.\n\"You come bustin\u2019 in here\u2014\" said Moncrief. \"Where\u2019s Skip is what I want to know.\"\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I haven\u2019t the faintest idea where he is.\"\n\"See?\" said Moncrief to his men. \"I told you he didn\u2019t know where he was\u2014now I\u2019ll tell you he doesn\u2019t know where anybody else is, either. Boys, I guess we\u2019re not goin\u2019 to have to tie this Martian up after all\u2014he\u2019s tied himself up.\"\n\"Where\u2019s the boy?\" said $SURNAME0.", "0_8_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-nine in the morning</span>. I bought a morning paper, and to calm my stomach I counted slowly to fifty. Then I went. It took less than ten minutes. The payoff was so simple I almost trembled.\n\u201cRoom 218,\u201d I heard behind me. I swiveled and there was the dough-faced counterman. \u201cTop of the stairs, down the hall on the left.\u201d I wondered if he\u2019d been watching me from behind the counter all along. I thanked him and started off.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s he in for?\u201d I heard somebody ask as I walked away. \u201cScurvy,\u201d said another voice. \u201cBad blood.\u201d I took that to be the code word for drug addiction. That apparently was what Vernon had. The scurvy part was probably my first clue that our destination was county cancer ward. That would fit. He was a cancerous addict. All heroin addicts are born scurvy.\nIt was all so nice and terribly calm. I hadn\u2019t even rushed to get downstairs before they came crashing in . . . Somehow that seemed a healthy sign. I hoped it was the same room. I hoped his doctor hadn\u2019t checked...", "0_12_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-one p.m.</span> Luna had moved far enough to the east that the American elevator, many feet below, now neatly bisected her. Concurrently the device and the satellite presented perspectives the Medieval atmosphere could not accommodate. The soldier looked up, craning his neck, and saw an apparition: a loathsome, winged, adult phenomenon, a foul, rogue angel, poised high on the building above him and regarding him from lunar tatters. God Himself.\nSimilar things were happening all over Earth-that-was. Human beings were screaming and falling to their knees when they gazed upward into the scintillant sky of Earth-that-was.\nLunch ended at twelve fifty-one.\nAt twelve fifty-two, in accordance with a schedule as rigid as any in the history of Universe House, a custodian named Arnold Lowe opened a supply closet on the ninth floor, came out with a vacuum cleaner and a large trash can. He set the trash can down beside the vacuum cleaner. He paused with his hands on his hips to admire the view, which was spectacular.\nThe sight of the angel must, for Arnold Lowe, have been all the more horrible for arriving at precisely the instant when Arnold Lowe least expected an angel. Arnold Lowe was an atheist...", "0_2_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-four in the morning</span>. My attorney gunned it toward downtown Las Vegas at freeway speed. \u201cJesus Christ!\u201d he cried. \u201cCan you imagine what those guys would have done if they\u2019d found the mescaline? They\u2019d probably have lined us up against a wall and mowed us down with semi\u2014automatics. Jesus Christ!\u201d He was sweating heavily. \u201cProbably the first white guys ever to pull a robbery in Las Vegas,\u201d he said. \u201cThere\u2019s some drug roulette going on at the Circus\u2014Circus right now. What if we\u2019d gone over and hung around? Jesus Christ!\u201d\n\u201cLet\u2019s just hope they all go out to the desert and drink themselves to death,\u201d I said. \u201cMeanwhile, we\u2019ve still got thirty\u2014nine grand left. Let\u2019s go buy some good red.\"\n\u201cShit,\u201d he said. \u201cWe can\u2019t buy red. This all has to last us till Sunday night.\n\u201cThat\u2019s right,\u201d I said. \u201cTonight\u2019s the big acid night, up In the Room.\u201d He whist...", "1_9_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-two in the morning</span>. Everybody around the Space Wanderer was sleeping or preparing to sleep, except $FIRSTNAMEB2. $FIRSTNAMEB2 the child was in a squawk stage. $FIRSTNAMEB2 the father was trying to alienate the child\u2019s attention, without success.\nThe child was interested in every sharp corner in the bedroom. The child crept tremblingly on all fours around the sharp corners. The child could hardly wait to explore the sharp corners in the living room.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 the father was dandling a baby alligator on one knee. The alligator was supposed to be the Space Wanderer\u2019s Christmas present from $FIRSTNAMEB2. The alligator had been billed by a Universal Bulletin Messenger boy as an alligator with an educational personality. \"All he do is lay around, drink orange juice, and tell you who you is,\" said the Messenger boy.\nChristmas Eve in the Space Wanderer\u2019s apartment had not been pleasant. The alligator had kept referring to the Space Wanderer as Yertle the Turtle. The alligator had also called the Space Wanderer Rolfe, Swinburne, Bill, Priscilla, and $FIRSTNAMEM2. And it had flatly refused to refer to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 as $SURNAME0.\nCh...", "2_22_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-eight in the evening</span> on Earth.\nOld Salo was so old as to be nearly incomparably infirm and nearly incommunicado. $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was the oldest old man in the Solar System, had bought the services of Salo for the occasion, expecting Salo to prove as sturdy as the fireplace in Salo\u2019s stony little home on Mars.\nSalo did prove sturdy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and the deserter named $FIRSTNAMEB1 and the deserter named Kammswange had been stripped of their space suits, their communicators, and their pistols. They were given white cotton robes to wear over their uniforms. They were hustled down to the waterfront and into the taxi.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was hustled in, too\u2014hustled and shoved by a squad of stony-faced guards in red. The guards were the Martian Army regulars who had supported the rebellion of the Three Musketeers.\nThe guards on their badges were Guardsman Joeular, Guardsman Mitlar, and Guardsman Korular.", "0_22_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-three in the evening</span>. In fifteen minutes, the seventh race of the third Winter afternoon at the Gray Flagon opened. I was out at the wire, ready. I had a five-to-six favorite in a six\u2014horse field. I had to make a deposit of four dollars and forty-five cents on my bet. The last bet I\u2019d made in the same race had cost me six dollars. I didn\u2019t think it would do that again. But if it did, I was ready for it. My current financial outlook seemed to allow for a three-dollar bet without impairing my lifestyle. I was carrying my emergency money in big denominations. I counted out the four tens and forty-five single dollar bills onto the steel bar. My money fitted there quite snugly. Some pit boss was sure to come by within the next five minutes, blank expression on his face, and he\u2019d scoop it up and drop it into some sort of a drawer. My money wasn\u2019t going to encourage anybody. The Flagon is not set up to give winning players a big party...but neither is it set up to accommodate losers in any sort of style.\nThey will not let you alone in a casino. They hassle you constantly, try to...", "2_3_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-seven in the morning</span> in Melbourne, Australia. Let\u2019s talk about The $SURNAME1 Ping. Let\u2019s talk about an aching void enclosed in an unfathomed space. Let\u2019s talk about a seed in a barren and nameless land.\nAnd for the floor of this void, and the walls of this space, and the roof of this land, let\u2019s talk about silence.\nAnd for the things in the space, and on the land in the space, let\u2019s talk about zero.\nZero origin. Zero size. Zero circumference. Zero center.\nLet\u2019s talk about the light of a star that is so far away that the light of the star hasn\u2019t reached us yet.\nNo wonder we say a star is dead.\nNo wonder we say there is no such thing as Space.\nYes.\nYou ask, \"Shall we say more about the light of the dead star?\"\nNo.\n\"What is its weight?\" you ask.\nIt never arrived in Melbourne, Australia.\n\"What has it to do with The Tranes owned The Stilt Foams?\" you ask.", "0_11_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-seven in the morning</span> when they brought us up to $FIRSTNAMEB2- impulsivity Prime, and our address experts informed us that this was the last stop before the Twilight Era.\nThere wasn\u2019t anything much to say or do after that.\nWe thanked the driver, who wasn\u2019t wearing his turban.\nWe watched the bus recede into the distance, and then we passed our bags over to a redcap, who put them on a rusty trolley and whisked them away.\n\"That\u2019s that, then,\" I said, absently snapping my fingers.\n\"That\u2019s that.\"\n\"What are we going to do?\" said Summer. \"We can\u2019t really just walk away from this bus station, you know. We\u2019re in the middle of nowhere.\"\n\"Look around,\" I said. \"What do you see?\"\n\"Nothing.\"\n\"Right!\" I said, kicking over the trash can. \"There is nothing, because this place doesn\u2019t even exist!\"\nI snapped my fingers a few more times, hoping for a spark. There was nothing but a futile stench.\n\"What are we going to do?\" Summer said.\n\"We\u2019re going to get...", "2_21_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-four in the evening</span>.\n/e/\n\u201cI had it all our way, I\u2019ll say that for myself,\u201d Peter Abbott said in the back of the limo, as it drove down Route 22. \u201cMan I tell you we had the stage all to ourselves.\u201d\nCandy lay on the beaded seat, near the partition to the front. \u201cThat was as close as I ever hope to get to any version of hell, I\u2019ll tell you that,\u201d she said.\nLaVache looked at her and sniffed. \u201cMaybe he just did all those students,\u201d he said. \u201cOn a whim. He has whims.\u201d\nLaVache had insisted they all stay in the theater while he took Lenore to her car. Lang had been a no-show. Candy was pretty sure her dress had rips under the arms.\nIt was twenty-three minutes past nine in the evening.\n\u201cAnd maybe for a minute there I thought I was hired for, like, a legitimate position in all this.\u201d\n\u201cPosition?\u201d", "1_4_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>, according to the uniform time system of the Bradshawagger Express Company.\nWhile $FIRSTNAMEB1 explored the miracle of the open door, $FIRSTNAMEB0 made his way deeper into the brownstone. He was trying to find out where the big specks of blue fire fit into the picture. With him went the harmoniums. $FIRSTNAMEB0 believed that the harmoniums were his friends.\nThe harmoniums, aglow at random, led $FIRSTNAMEB0 deeper into the building.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, standing at the open vault door, felt someone pulling his hand and his foot and his knee and his shoulder and his elbow, felt himself being drawn irresistibly toward the vault.\nThe something that was pulling him had no physical materiality at all. But if a strong man in perfect health had hand-pulled $FIRSTNAMEB1 just then, $FIRSTNAMEB1 would have seemed as tractable as a badly made rag doll. There was a nasty force at work on him, and it seemed cruel and stupid and intent on bringing him to the vault. No angel could have withstood it for three seconds.\nSteele and Carnes had been put, as sleeping pupils, in a classroom at the end of a long corridor, were being gradually and painlessly taught the responses of sleeping creatures...", "2_9_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-two in the morning</span>. The day was Tuesday\u2014mail day. Old Stone WhitemILL, who was the head janitor, had just come and gone, taking with him the big laundry bags of letters to parents. There were sixty-nine letters to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s father.\nOld Stone Whit MIRE, who was sixty-six years old, was the only person at the Pebblewick Baptist Church who really liked his job. He had been janitor at the church for twenty-two years, and he had his job still because he didn\u2019t mind doing it. He had control over all the things in the church. He could make the church empty or full, bright or dark, clean or dirty. He never got bored, and he very rarely got sinful.\nOld Stone Whitmire looked like a quaint, dried hedge from one of the cottages at Newport on an off day. He had no hair on his head and no chin. His head was all cheekbones and ruts. Little pleats appeared down his cheeks from his cheekbones.\nHis eyes were two blacker dots at the tip of two harder black beams.", "0_1_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-one in the morning</span>. At that moment I was somewhere in the middle of Illinois, the Dreamatorium humming along under soft electric vibrations. I was about two hours out of New Orleans and had just come off an incredible high\u2014a high of realization and prophecy concerning my life and my writings. I had spent the past three weeks infiltrating and mingling with my strange kind in the secrecy of night, roaming around the waterfront bars and gambling houses and pool halls of the Port of New Orleans, and during this time I had heard and felt a thousand confused stories of wild income and luck and creative genius, as well as heard many seething, ominous rumors concerning the Prince and his possible police connections. The underground is a place of excited chatter and serious gossip. And as for the Prince\u2014when I'd tried to get a line on him through my own sources, I got nowhere. Nobody knew anything; nobody'd seen him. \u201cHe's a spook, man, nobody sees him.\u201d But I knew he was somewhere close, inpatiently waiting for these vigintillions that were to be his from the Bat Kosher Nosh Restaurant in the old Finnila waterfront area where I'd hung out with my friend Alfred years ago. I'd heard nothing but horror stories of this...", "0_8_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-four in the morning</span> and the air was already hazy and hot. The lobby was cool and thickly carpeted and luxuriously furnished with shiny wood and leather. The registration desk was in a far wing of the lobby. There were only three or four people standing in line at the desk, and I jumped into the front of the line and confronted the desk clerk almost before she could look up. I gave her the name of the man who owned the big convertible I\u2019d just bought. I laid his name on her like a barium meal.\n\u201cHis firm sent me out from New York to work on his account. I\u2019m his cousin, his secretary, his nephew\u2014just kidding about that, but especially I\u2019m his cousin. I want a suite in this hotel.\"\n\u201cThe Alexander Hamilton, sir?\u201d\n\u201cOf course not. I wouldn\u2019t stay in a flophouse. This is the always-dashing Beverly Wilshire. A suite, my good woman.\"\n\u201cOh yes. Mr. Kingman. Yes, sir. We have a lovely suite, three club rooms and a private bath, with a view of the garden.\"\n\u201cWhat room is it?\"\n\u201c", "2_16_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We spent an hour discussing our itinerary. Now that I was married and had a family to consider, I naturally considered $FIRSTNAMEB3's and my safety in light of my new domestic obligations. \u201cNow wait a second, $FIRSTNAMEB3, let's talk this over. When this wife of yours gets to LA we might all have to go to the county jail -\u201d", "1_20_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after eight o'clock in the evening</span> now, and the work of the day was finished. Rollo, the lean brown-faced dog with the long ears, was waiting, as he always did, in the back yard, ready to take his place whenever he was wanted, and be Johnny's partner, while they threw the old rubber ball that was their favorite plaything.\nAnd John Pewee, Johnny, the little boy in the blue jeans and the yellow sweater, was stopped in front of the house, playing alone.\nOld Violet never stepped out the back door on summer nights without pausing to look up at the sky and admire it. On certain evenings there was not a single cloud in it. The silvery moon was shining and there were millions upon millions of stars.\nThen old Violet would say, with a sigh of happiness, as she went back into her kitchen, \"Land's sake, I'd like to know where we are\u2014who we are.\"\n\"Oh, we're on Earth,\" she would answer herself. \"We can tell that because we bump on it when we walk on the floors.\"\nShe would stop again, thinking about the floors, and about life. And she would let her white hair fall down over her shoulders again, and her crooked fingers would continue...", "0_17_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\n\"$SURNAME1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, addressing himself. \"Marry $SURNAME0.\nBecome a Titan? Yes!\"\nHe embraced his self-image once again, childishly, fiercely\u2014and then dropped his hands to his sides, became absorbed in the throes of real struggle. What would $SURNAME1 the Titan-to-be actually do once he had the power in his hands?\nHe puzzled it over for a minute, then framed a sentence he would have to speak to his yes-men in The Caldron. It was to serve as his guiding rule once he took over. His voice was choked when he said it aloud.\n\"Whatever seems best to do,\" he said, \"do that.\" But the saying seemed false even to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 as he said it aloud.\nHe paced down the basement corridor once again, trying to imagine himself as a Titan. Once again, he floundered.\n\"Magnifico!\" he cried. \"You fancy yourself a ladies\u2019 man, don\u2019t you?\" he said to $SURNAME0\u2019s image.\nMagnifico shrugged. \"I\u2014I try to be,\" he said.\n\"The greatest ladies\u2019 man in history, eh...", "0_8_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span> and I was sitting on a bench in the square in front of the H\u00f4tel de la Paix in Geneva. It was cold and raining, and no one had come out of the hotel yet, but it was just twenty-four hours since I had landed in France, and that seemed enough.\"\n\"Go ahead,\" $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. She was amused.\n\"That seems enough for any one country. I got off the boat and came straight to Paris, and so to Geneva, and I'm damned if I handled the matter like a gentleman.\"\n\"You say all gentlemen should end up in Switzerland, so you went last night to a cafe in Paris, and said to yourself, `Any gentleman going to Switzerland ends up in Switzerland.' Is that it?\"\n\"That is not it, and you dissemble monstrously.\"\n\"I thought you were trying to write something.\"\n\"I haven't written anything\n\"You're thinking something, aren't you?\"\n\"No.\"\n\"Try to think something,\" $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. She was looking straight ahead, at the tables. Over her shoulder I saw a face I knew, and it was Peek, the financial editor of the World, looking straight at me. He smiled, got up,...", "1_3_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>. The body of $FIRSTNAMEB0 was now twenty-two thousand miles from the barracks on Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s soul was about to be invited into a golden rock vault twelve miles beneath the surface of the planet Mercury.\nThe invitation went out from a bearded patriarch of seventy-seven named Stony Stevenson. He was seated in a transparent bubble set in the middle of a heart-shaped chamber twelve miles below the surface of Mercury. Stony Stevenson was forty-six thousand miles from the place where $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s body now was.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 stood behind Stony Stevenson, peering over his shoulder at the gold-foil pages of the atlas of Mars. The atlas had 272 pages.\nOnly seven of the pages were gold-foiled.\nStony Stevenson had just used up the gold-foil on the page showing the Parachute infantry regiment.\nThe atlas of Mars was without nationality markings, without population statistics, without relief- shading\u2014utterly amateurish. It had been manufactured by an Army museum, from stolen or purloined materials.\nThe museum had specialized in Hall of Weapons exhibits, Chamber of Horrors exhibits, and other pursuits of the instructive and entertaining sort. Museum officials had...", "2_16_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. \u201cOh, my God!\u201d I heard $FIRSTNAMEB3 shout. I looked and saw him in the car backing out of the driveway. \u201cNow I've lost him. Where is he going?\u201d I asked. \u201cHe tried to cut me off on the way here. Oh, he was on his way here all right, he just pretended he was mad and trying to beat me back to New York. I should have known. Oh, my God !\u201d And I was dying, yes, I knew it; I was sure; I began to vomit in the gutter. $FIRSTNAMEF4's brother found my scarf in the bushes and I ran out, knew $FIRSTNAMEB3 was gone, staggered to the hotel, and a doctor came, and I lay in bed for four days, seeing pink elephants floating all over the apartment, panting and sweating, hearing the radio churning out \u201cWonderful, wonderful,\u201d and tapping my feet; then I rose and stumbled around, since $FIRSTNAMEB3 had come back, and I was still alive.", "1_1_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>. Suddenly all hell was let loose, the earth trembled, a deafening roar made my bones vibrate. The thing was monstrously loud; it knocked people off their feet in the caf\u00e9.I wanted to find a bridge. \u201cAre there many bridges in Amsterdam?\u201d I asked over the steaming bowl of so-called tea.\nI learned there were more than three hundred bridges. The city was built on a network of canals, dikes, and floodgates. A modern Venice. I didn't know Amsterdam was such a broken-down old medieval city. I couldn't wait to visit it. Sleepless, excited, and happy, I fled outside to the woman in the lumpy bed to buy my first ticket to Amsterdam. I had the wherewithal to survive only two or three days, but I had read Hesse, and knew that all real trips are started in the head. At dawn Dutch planes flew in formation to Paris, Switzerland, London... I wanted to start now. The little ticket-seller said nothing as I paid; she approached the curtain, and I knew she was leaning on the counter and studying me and my hobo pack with her old woman's eyes. I laughed behind my hand but it...", "0_6_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-two in the morning</span>. I drove around for a while, circling the city, and then I took the airport turnpike and made good time. By the time I saw the international jet parked far off on the coal runway it was almost ten minutes to flight time. The little flight to El Paso was called, and the passengers started filing up the steps. I stood at the back of the crowd, among the fifteen or seventeen El Paso passengers, and when everybody was aboard I casually took a seat on the left side of the plane.\nWhen we took off I felt like laughing right up through the clouds and yelling to everybody: \u201cI know what you bastards have been doing while I was gone! I know what you bastards have been doing while I was gone!\u201d I kept it in. It gave me a strange feeling of power to have been given such a gift of information without cost.\nNo, it wasn't for nothing that my attorney had been calling me Banana Nose. It was only that I had overlooked the potential meaning of the name. Naturally, I thought, he objected to it. He was so good-looking, or anyway physically presentable, with his lean face and big bright eyes and blond hair, that I always thought he had some queer lack...", "1_17_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\n\"It proves that much,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThen she and $SURNAME1 twirled around on their balcony, laughing insanely.\nAnd below, caught in the yew trees, Benny knew that everything was going to be all right when he got back to the room.\nThe Martian, mounted on the sagging green throne, was struggling with a control box the size of a fountain pen.\nRansom K. Fern was the name stamped on the gold band around the middle of the control box. Fern forced the box to disgorge, first a blue tablet, then a pink capsule. He selected the pink capsule and held it between his thumb and forefinger.\n\"This should do the trick, folks,\" he said on the telestat. \"It\u2019s your old friend soma. Only a medium dose this time. Strong enough to kill pain and soften the edges of reality, weak enough not to interfere with motor efficiency and coordination.\" There were two red circles on the capsule, and Fern broke the capsule between them. The two circles came apart, and opposite sides of the sheet of soma unfolded\u2014revealing a pink mustache on a blue face.\n\"Gong agoge tu,\" said the face on the undrugged half of the...", "1_17_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. And the company in which I found myself was composed of sixteen varied and educated people, who had made the trek across the continent in order to buy, fight and sell tungsten, molybdenum and rhenium to the government, which government would probably, at first and for a time, be mine. I sat there with them in the Douglas DC-3 and saw myself already, with only the slightest of efforts, elevated to the highest of places.\nThere was a creature next to me, in the row ahead of me, a wizard of sorts, who had made the business even more mysterious. This fellow was an anthropologist, a freckled young man who seemed, with his reddish hair, a touch of Scotch and the twinkle in his blue eyes, a rather amusing and silly looking little God. His name was Dr. Ed Tilly. And his story was that he was more interested in the people of this continent than in the minerals and metals. I already knew the system of things and felt somehow that, from the very beginning, I understood all about it. Although I had been drunk ever since I came on the plane in Denver, my inner girl had clicked many times and made a final summation. There was no...", "2_5_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-two in the morning</span>. \u201cAs high as you like,\u201d I whispered.\nIt was five thirty-five in the morning. There was a room somewhere. \u201cLet\u2019s go back to that room. The one with the jukebox,\u201d whispered my attorney. \u201cI have to wake up early,\u201d I whispered back. \u201cYou should put in a few more quarters,\u201d whispered my attorney, rummaging around the car for something to eat. \u201cI\u2019m starved.\u201d\n6. Editorial: In Which Our Hero Reveals Momentous Personal News While His attorney Considers Complex Legal Problems...And We Shatter a Few Myths Along the Way\nMay 1990\nLet\u2019s talk about surfing. I was thinking about it this afternoon. I was watching a lot of young people trying to surf in Daytona Beach. They made it look impossibly difficult. You get up on the surfboard, paddle like a madman, then get slammed around on top of the briny while the thing lists under you\u2014maybe breaking your neck. What could be simpler?\nAnd when the thing tips over, and you find yourself drowning in your own foam, what then?", "0_12_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-four p.m.</span> when they got to Encino. Everything was brown and dry. They found Voilet Street. They drove up and down, up and down, past house after house. \u201cOf course, a man in his right mind would not have had a house built out here!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cA retired fellow like himself would have bought up a lot of little houses in town for his money. Here's his street dead-end. Let's turn around and boomerang back.\u201d We did. We passed a pale, fateful house in the first block. \u201cThere's the house he should have bought. There it is. Don't you think so ?\u201d I didn't know. We drove whole dead hours along the street. Raw have cars passed by. The trees had no leaves; everything was dead in the December sun. Bleak and final, it made you want to holler. Little girls in plaid dresses occasionally passed and giggled. \u201cShepp\u201d went our tires on the road. For a forgotten reason we wanted to find this man's address. \u201cAnd look!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3 suddenly and braked, \u201crot there it is! Why! It's right across the street and we...", "2_4_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-seven in the morning</span>, New York City time.\nThe Reverend C. Horner Spratling, tired but exultant, got off the chief of police\u2019s sunporch in Home and straightened his black suit and winked at his wife and said, \"All right, folks. Let\u2019s go.\"\nAnd they all went. And they were not cheered much by the quiet city through which they now drove. It was a place of wooden houses that leaned one against another, making a whistling and sighing in the late trades. The streets were empty and ill-paved, and people slept with their fingers in their ears. The poor people were in the cantonments at the south end of town and in the squat-roofed tenements up around the railroad station in the north. Every now and then, in walking from the chief of police\u2019s, Mrs. Spratling would see someone peeking from behind a shade. But by and large the town was asleep. Spratling\u2019s platoon of itinerant preachers and faith healers left it more sleep than it had had in months.", "0_4_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-three in the morning</span>, November 8, 1982.\nI had been awake for about three hours, listening to the rain whip against my bedroom window and staring at the dark ceiling to avoid looking at my desk and the big white Moon. All things considered, looking at the Moon was more comfortable.\nIt had been an excruciatingly difficult evening and night. Perspiring freely, I had arrived at the front door of Room 1010 at the El Royale at exactly 7:25 in the evening. I was wearing blue pinstripe pants, white button-down shirt, mohair suit jacket, black Jockey underwear, and Weejun penny loafers. I was trembling and coughing, and my teeth were chattering so violently that I had to stop chattering and hold still while I used a key to open the door.\nInside, I tripped over a fur coat and almost fell into a full bathtub . . . which had already been used, I could see, judging from discarded Q-tips and a used cake of Dial soap, the latter featuring a scummy ring of dried water. Shuddering, I put the hair blower in the bathroom and then moved out into the combination living room and bedroom. The furniture was an odd combination of Early Casino and old Bette...", "1_7_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to eight o'clock in the morning</span>, while the drums were going and the crowds were waving, and Salo was saying, \"You\u2019re On your Own and You Maybe\u2014\" when the day of Tralfamadore arrived for $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. The colors disappeared from everything that surrounded $SURNAME1\u2014from the soldiers, from the palace wall, from the sky above the palace.\n$SURNAME1 was no longer looking at colors.\nThere was now an intense white light within his field of vision. The light was too intense for him to see anything else. The white light was a searchlight from the broken ram at the heart of the Tralfamadorian flying saucer.\nA gigantic, disembodied transparent thumb was coming down to crush $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 between the tips of its first and second joints. This was not a dream.\nThis did not come at the end of, or succeed, a nightmare. It was instant, white torture. So energetic was the light that rendered $SURNAME1\u2019s finger pads tepid that it made even the calm muscles of his eyelids tingle.\nThe steady pressure. The steady, increasing pneuma-tization.\nWhy did the light and thumb exist?\nHuddled on the scraggly step of the palace, $SURNAME1 was...", "2_0_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after midnight</span> and we had been in bed two hours.\n\u201cDon\u2019t you feel as I do?\u201d\n\u201cI feel fine.\u201d\n\u201cI feel as though I\u2019d done something wrong.\u201d\n\u201cFor God\u2019s sake, $FIRSTNAMEB5, don\u2019t be like that.\u201d\n\u201cLike what?\u201d\n\u201cAll right,\u201d I said. \u201cDon\u2019t think about it.\u201d\nIt was an hour later.\n\u201cI don\u2019t know why I feel so strange.\u201d\n\u201cDrink your coffee.\u201d\n\u201cBe nice to me, Jake.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m always nice.\u201d\n\u201cI know. Tell me about everything you did in London.\u201d\nI was looking out through the gray curtain of the rain at the wet, empty street.\n\u201cJake, tell me about Paris. You went to the races.\u201d\n\u201cLausanne.\u201d\n\u201cWhat did you do in Lausanne?\u201d\n\u201cWent to the races.\u201d", "2_12_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty post meridiem</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heavy-duty watch ticked and tocked in the fireplace\u2019s silence.\n\"What, $FIRSTNAMEB1?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s lips moved, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 hung on their movement\u2014but no words came.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked again at his watch.\nThe spinning hands stood at twelve forty-one.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 got up off his knees by the body of his dead best friend. He straightened his tunic. He saluted $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s body.\nThe mechanical salute made $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s two hands come together smartly, jerk smartly apart.\nIt was the salute of a soldier who was ashamed of having made a fiasco of the soldier\u2019s touch of death.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had trouble swallowing his choking sobs. \" \u2019Bye, $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014God damn it!\" he said to his dead best friend.\nAt that point, when $FIRSTNAMEB0 was about to leave the stockroom for the last time, his heavy-duty watch made a musical chiming.\nIts twelve forty-second chime told $FIRSTNAMEB0 that the minute was up. His minute had passed.", "1_18_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-six in the evening</span> of a still-life Friday in October, 1957, and as I walked through the Indianapolis airport en route from one end of the country to another, I felt as though one chapter of my life were ending and another beginning. To an airport is a lonely place, and people in airports, some de corps et d'\u00e2me and some just casually passing through, are apt to shun the first glance and carelessly judge the hurried traveler slouching along in the leaden shadow of departure.\nYeah, everything was in flux this Friday evening, and changes were on the way. I slouched along, took my ticket, found the right gate. It was a leap year, and I was beginning to feel the symptoms of a wild hangover that would just have to wear itself out during the long flight ahead: long because I wanted to fly direct to Las Vegas, Nevada, presumably for the purpose of gambling, at least to begin with.\nThere were beads of perspiration on my forehead, though the September night was chilly. I remembered how Stan Weir of Rawlins had broken a fever of 105 during a three-day poker game in Wichita. Yes, fever and flux were the order of the day for us all. Brenda and I had made our promises. Now...", "0_9_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span> when the extraordinary street scene broke up and disappeared, with the Peewee yacht merrily chasing the bartender away. The bartender took seven, hurriedly served, before he disappeared into his own gig, which was crewed by two lads who obviously hated their job.\nNo one paid much attention to young Rickey in the abandoned cafe. Old Rickey was still breathing, in a very light coma.\nBy ten o'clock, in other words fifteen minutes after the arrival of the Commodore's letter, the town of Chapel Hill was quite a different place from what it had been, just half a hour before. Not so much to outward appearances, perhaps, but the wild and carefree mood had gone out of the campus. The town was silent, as impressed and thoughtful as any cloister.\nThere was something calming about the statue of the great scholar in the square.\nWasn't $FIRSTNAMEB3 A. A. F. Thayer absolutely right---that a person could never find himself, could never find his true self and that meaningful place in history, unless he was capable of making an imaginative leap through time? All this hasty talk about \"time\" and \"space\"\u2026was it so simple and concrete? Did things change so fast in time? Did...", "1_17_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five sixteen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe day was Tuesday.\nThe year was 1986.\nAt ten minutes after five o\u2018clock, a helicopter rose vertically from the middle of the waste circle in the middle of Sector 5G at the Tralfamadore Air Force Base in Afghanistan. At the controls was a warrant officer named Maxwell Smart, United States Army.\nThe eighteen-year-old chaplain\u2019s assistant, who was privy to all the secrets but knew nothing of the history or future of the species, was standing in formation with the other chaplains\u2019 assistants.\nHis sense of desolation and loss was now enormous.\nHis fingers felt like the stiff fronds of a plant.\nThe waste circle felt like an unexploded atom bomb\u2014minus possibly the warning imprint. The helicopter seemed to Mount $SURNAME0 to be rising from the seat of awful annihilation itself.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 had all boarded the helicopter before the mob\u2019s shock and grief had had time to harden into rage. The grounds were being pounded now by marooned feet.\nThrough puffs of dust raised by the group stomping, $SURNAME1 saw a banner working its way through the crowd. The bannerman...", "0_1_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>. While $FIRSTNAMEB0 was telling $FIRSTNAMEF0 about the minute hand falling off, Stony Stevenson was landing his shipcraft on a ledge thirty feet below the steepest part of Devil\u2019s Tower. He was eighty miles north of the hospital outside Nashville. The night was moonless and pitch black.\nStony slipped out of his seat harness, went to the airlock, peeled back part of the metal sheeting that insulated the interior of the ship. He opened three valves and depressurized the airlock. The hissing of air into the vacuum would attract no one\u2019s attention, even if there were anyone to hear it.\nThere wasn\u2019t anybody, anyway. Stony had taken the ship up to 3,000 feet before starting his descent. There was no one below. The nearest group of human beings capable of standing upright was a mile and a half away, around the hospital outside Nashville.\nStony went naked into the black night, went silently, came silently to flattened earth. He gathered dry leafmold and twigs for a fire, came silently back. He rubbed two dry sticks together to make a spark. The trick then was to keep the spark alive long enough for it to build a flame.\nStony was good...", "0_5_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-three in the morning</span> of the twenty-first of August, 1954. The scene was Fern, Ohio. The place was the yard of a ramshackle shed called the Fern Railway Terminal, on the outskirts of the town. A formidable-looking, elderly gentleman was crouched over a raging acetylene torch and a small but nasty-looking bit of metal that he had cut out of a larger bit of metal. The bit of metal and the torch were surrounded by such miscellaneous filth as can only be gathered together in a place where men go muddling and tinkering with trains.\nThe gentleman was known as Old Saloman. Old Saloman was the all-powerful streetcar magnate of Fern, and his daughter Roberta\u2019s husband. Old Saloman was, of course, not his real name. His real name was Anson W. Sturgis III, but he found the name Saloman convenient, for it commanded instant respect among engine men. Even as a child Anson had been unable to call any living thing by its proper name without gaining some advantage over it. His mother had been unable to wean him from this unhealthy habit by any device but baby talk.\nOld Saloman carefully placed his nasty bit of metal on the huge, loosely defined...", "0_9_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 and his dog materialized in the gazebo just in time for $SURNAME0 to witness the abrupt and ghastly end of the life of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the man whom $SURNAME0 had once said would come to be a victor in the best and worst senses of that term.\nHoarse with rage, Rumford turned away from $SURNAME1\u2019s suicide. He strode up the driveway. \"Skip!\" he cried. \"Come up here!\"\nThere was a knot of people on the ridge in front of the palace. People cleared away from $SURNAME0 as he charged up.\nThe mansion was dark inside. Bright unmanned maintenance machines went clickity-click-click all over the grounds. Bees dRONded in the lavender by the front gate. High up against the pale blue of the morning sky, spacequake spectators whizzed like sand-dabs thrown against a screen door.\n$SURNAME0 faced his assembled parishioners. His dog lay at his feet, panting.\nNo one spoke.\nUncertainly, the minister raised one hand, fingers extended. \"If\u2014,\" he said.\nHe brought his hand down on his dog\u2019s head. \"Up...", "1_2_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty in the morning</span>. We were sitting at the back of a beatup Chevrolet in a parking lot on Lombard Street in San Francisco, waiting for that miserable Gretch to come out of the Fillmore Auditorium and tell us which one or ones of the running Titan crew of groupies that he, personally, had screwed this time. There were five of us\u2014my attorney, Dr. Gonzo; a wealthy and well\u2014known drug addict who shall remain nameless; a young novelist, who also asked to remain anonymous; and two heavily armed and extremely nervous bodyguards. Of the five of us, only Inspector Gonzo and I dealt - almost exclusively in cash. The idea, as I explained before, was to buy a car for cash, a secondhand one that nobody would check on, and then drive across the state in it, tearing up the credit cards and driver\u2019s licenses when we reached the border. But, so far, this plan had failed completely. We had already used up three cards and no doubt the rest were waiting for us at our respective homes. This left me with nothing but some bad personal checks, about fifty dollars in cash, a joint Visa card with my secretary\u2019s signature, and my attorney\u2019s gold American Express.", "1_13_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> and we wanted very much to get drunk. The bartender was a young man with black-painted fingernails. His eyebrows were painted, too. They were dark gray. He wore a yellow ma\u00eetre d\u2019h\u00f4tel\u2018s jacket and he looked mean. He said he had only three doubles left in the bottle. \u201cThat\u2019s not enough,\u201d I told him. \u201cWe need at least ten,\u201d Doc said. \u201cForget it.\u201d\n\u201cI have money,\u201d Doc said.\n\u201cNobody cares about that.\u201d\n\u201cWe need ten,\u201d I said.\n\u201cOh yes?\u201d He grinned. \u201cAnd what are you going to do, fetch your mother in here to buy them for you?\u201d\n\u201cI haven\u2019t any money,\u201d I said. \u201cMy mother would just buy us beer.\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s nice.\u201d He laughed. \u201cTen doubles Scotch and beer, come here, please ... Give me five dollars and I\u2019ll give you fifty cents\u2019 worth of Scotch and ice water ... you little sucker.\u201d", "0_18_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-one in the evening</span> of Tuesday, September 2, when $FIRSTNAMEB0 woke up with a horrible, chattering fit of laughter.\nHe was on a space ship\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0, alone with his sorrows and his fears. The ship was a harmonium\u2014a hollow tube thirty feet long, three feet in diameter. There was a hatch at either end. At either end was a rocker that served as a bunk, a toilet, and a storage cabinet.\nThe rockers came equipped with corrugated sheet-iron walls that dropped down at the touch of a button. Each rocker had a button, so that each served simultaneously as a bunk, a toilet, and a storage cabinet\u2014or served one of the other by means of a switch.\nThe ship was being propelled electrically by means of cartridges of powder in a hopper in the floor. Earthling astronauts had learned about the hopper system from Martians on Earth.\nThe cartridges of powder blew iron shavings into the nozzle of a big iron bellows. The bellows kept fanning the fire\u2014kept making the ship move forward by with- drawing air from the hopper.\nThe iron shavings came from Martian rock.\nThe Martian rock was specially iron-rich rock that had fallen...", "1_0_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after midnight</span>. Salo, who had a perfectly accurate sense of the real whereabouts of Ravo, had blocked a field-goal attempt on purpose. Salo was the only mobile unit in the entire Universe who knew the real whereabouts of Ambassador Douglas Celestine.\nSt of Septentrion was no longer in his command-post dugout. He was out under the stars, stalking Mars with field glasses. There had been no salute from his troops to punctuate the new millennium\u2014because his troops were afraid that any sudden movement would be the signal for a totalitarian order to open fire. The troops\u2019 thinking on this point was accurate.\nThe order to fire was about to be given by a major who was hurriedly being promoted to field-marshal. His name was $SURNAME0, and he was Skip\u2019s true father. The promotion to field-marshal meant nothing to him, since his real rank, as ruler of Chronos, was Lieutenant-God.\n\"God or Devil,\n\" He hadn\u2019t been granted the honor of watching his son\u2019s soldiers either advance or retreat. He much preferred watching the red hordes over yonder. They were about to attack.\nIn a sense, $SURNAME0 sympath...", "1_2_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>, according to the Breitling Chronometre aboard the machine. There were two people inside the cramped cockpit of the Formula Turbo GTO\u2014and seventy-eight people standing or sitting around the machine on the cold Martian ground, not counting a few late-comers who had decided to walk toward the starting line instead of bothering with the buses and platforms.\nSince everybody knew everybody else, the introductions got to be a bore after a while.\nThe young man in the cockpit of the car was Ramon Igor Newborn. Back on Earth, in what they called civilization, he had been Monarch of the Universe. It was his job to reign over the several thousand inhabitants of the House of Royale, a dwelling at 3070 Winding Trail, Apopka, Florida. He was now twenty-one years old, he stood five feet six inches tall, his weight was one hundred thirty-two pounds, and his I.Q. was 163.\nRamon Newborn had been discovered by the Space Wanderer\u2014Robert Heinlein, Ph.D.\u2014when he was an obscure guest lecturer in Creative Writing at the University of Southern Colorado. Dr. Robert A. Heinlein made the selection in a rather unusual way. He had invited all the Creative Writing...", "0_9_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-seven in the morning</span> and I was with my attorney at the Plaza Hotel, in a suite overlooking 59th Street, two floors above the room where yesterday afternoon around this time poor old Abe Goldstein allegedly bit the dust. Not that two floors make much difference when you\u2018re dead, of course; with nobody to exert any pressure on him, Abe spent all night and half the morning creeping down to Room 214, then lay there almost five hours in a rigid pool before somebody happened to notice him. At that point, action was finally taken...but not, as it turned out, soon enough to help old One-ear.\nAnd that was that. By the time O\u2019Hare and a doctor arrived in 214 and examined the body, there was nothing they could do. The doctor confirmed death, then the window was smashed, the room entered and the body officially certified for removal.. .to a point. Thing was, the body was now in a very curious position. The last time I saw One-ear alive, he was oozing steadily onto the felt of the roulette table, but when they finally came to take him away, his posture was altered dramatically. Slumped he might have been, and comatose; but no longer was his right shoulder bent over the...", "2_16_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-four in the afternoon</span>...and the place was Washington, D.C., pinpoint coordinates in a putrescent past: on the lawn, the Peoples\u2019 Party Conven\u2014tion...and inside, the darkest rap session since The Man Who Came to Dinner.\nOld men with faces brightened by genuine horrors sat around a hotel suite, smoking a/c\u2014tinted cigarettes and blaming pretty much everything on \u201cthem damned bastards in Congress.\u201d One man chuckled softly, whispering that \u201cthem damned bastards in Congress\u201d were, in turn, no doubt controlled by \u201cthem Russians.\u201d \u201c Them Russians, boy! Can\u2019t never overlook them Russians!\u201d His pals nodded, bellyaching about \u201cHumphrey\u2019s new delegates from them Puerto Ricans\u201d: 68 in the House and 8 in the Senate ...the all\u2014important swing bloc that could give the fat man a dead cinch on the 1972 Democratic National Convention, provided he could finesse his way through the South\u2014the Southern Democrats\u2019 fortress of obstruction, the linchpin of a hundred vicious plots by Southern\u2014controlled GOP Chairman Re for total power in \u201975.", "0_2_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-one in the morning</span>, exactly eleven hours and forty-one minutes after the start of their watch. The significance of this escaped neither of them. It was their eleventh hour and forty\u2014first minute together. They drew a deep sigh from a well of get-up-and-go that seemed to have no bottom.\nThe signals coming from Mars were rational, clear, pure. Life on Mars was transmitting pure gold. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, for the first time, understood that their glorious mission might have an affirmative outcome.\n\"Dreary dank damp dark damned disease\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, for the sake of talking.\n\"Dreary dank damp dark damned disease\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, for the sake of talking. For the sake of bringing $FIRSTNAMEB0 out of his private reverie, $FIRSTNAMEB1 whistled the boop-boop-a-doop tune. \"What you so quiet about?\" he asked.\n\"I was just thinkin\u2019 how close we are to somethin\u2019 good,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Golly\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"With the Morse code, ain\u2019t we?\"\n\"Yeah,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"You ain\u2019t gotta remind me.\"\n\"Is it...", "1_21_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>, and Earthlings bad only forty minutes left to live. The life span of a $SURNAME0 wad now down to precisely forty. minutes\u2014to the very last second. At the end of those forty minutes, Earth and all its billions would go whizzing past Mars, as Minnie had predicted, and then would do as she had said they would do.\n\"The conjugal saga of $SURNAME1 and Winnie is at an end,\" said $SURNAME0. \"And a very pretty saga it was\u2014full of fights, full of passionate reconciliations, and full, too, of hungry kisses for an absent Titan.\n\"A Titan?\" said Salo, puzzled. \"You keep saying \u2019a Titan.\u2019 What\u2019s a \u2019Titan\u2019?\"\n\"A crippled boy who cries under his bunk during drill parade,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Come on. We have a new set of Master of Ceremonies business on our hands. You\u2019re going back to your chrono-synclastic infundibulated friend to tell him what\u2019s taking place.\"\n\"Don\u2019t you want to tell him?\" said Salo.\n\"No,\" said $SURNAME0 loft...", "2_20_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe year was 1986, the month September.\nThe strange sound that had stopped time again was the sound made by a bicycle horn up in the stands.\nThe bicycle horn was being used by Princess Galumnia of Jupiter. The Princess had been assigned to Earth for a good-will mission by the popular dictator of Jupiter, her father.\nUnpopular as any dictator could possibly be, the father of Princess Galumnia was trying to get rid of his title.\nIt was his understanding of the democratic process that once he were to display a genuine aspiration to be genuinely popular, he would be reviled by mobs in the street and hounded from office.\nThe title that he was so desperately seeking to shed was Tanninger, the name of all the Jovian political dynasties, a name that might be rendered in Earthling phonetics as Sunnyon, Anguson, Nesitton, Gingrichon\u2014a title that Earthling specialists on the Jovian political system defined as Sunny-Ones-Who-Were-Always-Sunnynominally-Suicidal.", "1_6_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span> of October twelfth, 1960.\nOrdinarily, a few minutes before eight o\u2019clock in the morning would not be an extraordinary time for R. T. Walsh to be thinking about breakfast. His breakfast was usually on his desk in an aluminum Thermos bottle, hot cereal, and his desk was in the News Division of the Western Telegram, which comprised the entire newspaper. So thinking about breakfast in the News Division was mainly thinking about how to get rid of his cereal and where to put the empty Thermos bottle, to save walking all\u2014the hell across those fifty\u2014odd rooms.\nBut today R. T. Walsh for the first time in twelve years was not thinking about breakfast. His mind was filled with something else, in the sense that this was about six minutes after he first became aware that something else, as opposed to breakfast, existed.\nAnd he was experiencing a whole slew of emotions unlike any he had known before. The principal emotion was fear, of something wholly and completely beyond his control, and maybe beyond anybody\u2019s control, coming to pass.\nThere was also, to be sure, rampant disbelief. It is probable that if anyone had approached R. T. Walsh a few minutes after six o\u2019clock on this morning...", "0_10_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten nineteen in the morning</span> and the place the big white room in $SURNAME0\u2019s Taj Mahal. It was a twelve-base beauty show. The contestants were guardsmen from everywhere. They had been brought to Mercury by bluebirds.\nThey were on various perches around the walls, twitching like dive-bombers. The twitching had nothing to do with any interest particular guardsmen might have in the various wonders of $SURNAME0\u2019s estate. It was all about timed releases of adrenaline.\nThe twitching was being done with the aid of nicotine pellets inserted in the flesh at the base of the thumb.\nThere are two things a space traveler absolutely must not do in the Solar System outside of Earth: lose control of his space ship and land on the Sun.\nThe nicotine pellets in the flesh at the base of the thumb do more than keep the space traveler from smoking cigarettes. They also make it possible, under stress, to keep his hands busy.\n$SURNAME0, standing at one of the two control panels in the room, had his right hand jammed in a pocket. He held a ruler in it. His other hand toyed affectionately with a pencil, held limply at the end of a slack arm.\nAs far as...", "0_22_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> of June 4, 1998. The place was the mud flat off 106 Alternate, just north of the Space Center. The shape of the thing could not yet be discerned in the dark.\nREDRUM\nThere was not a cloud in the sky.\nOne by one, pale but intense, a dazzling quintet of stars appeared in the deep blue overhead.\nThe stars were not the morning stars. No, they were Rum, Rum, Rum, Rum, and Rah. That was the chant that $SURNAME1 was saying to himself as he breathed in sweet Gulf air.\nMy life is ending marvelously, he thought. It has been a spectacular failure\u2014and I wouldn\u2019t want it to end in any other way. Not that I don\u2019t pity my son, but somebody had to take the blame for all the rottenness. Somebody had to be the goat.\nIt might as well be me.\n\"I am whoever you say I am,\" $SURNAME1 mumbled to the night air, \"Do with me as you please.\"\nThe chant continued, uninterrupted, as the fear at last began to flee from the remaining sectors of his mind. The tears, the sniveling, the sobs\u2014all were gone. He...", "2_16_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-seven in the afternoon</span> as Mona Larry awoke from an uneasy nap on her couch to the sound of faint, high singing in the faraway skies. She got to her feet. The singing was a sweet soprano. It sounded like one woman, but a whole choral group could have been singing in the same high range. It was not stereophonic singing. It came from one point in space.\nMona tracked the sound to the television set, which was on. She turned the set off. The singing stopped.\nMona ran into the kitchen. She flung open the door of the stove and peered in. There was fire in the stove. She hastily rifled some pages of a cookbook and fanned the fire out. The soprano singing resumed.\nMona ran back into the living room, knelt before the fireplace, and fed the blaze with wood and bits of paper. The beautiful soprano singing continued. Mona crawled under the cocktail table, opened the automatic control panel of the Rumford fireplace, and pressed all the buttons frantically. There was an electric spark, and Mona's eyebrows and hair, and the knees of her trousers, blew up as though in a very small and controlled explosion.", "2_15_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was forty-one years old. She looked thirty-five. Her body was firm, her skin was fine, her hair was magnificent.\nShe had never needed furs, jewels, and laces.\nShe unfastened steps of eyelets and hooks and eyes, and shed the painful and provocative petticoat that had been bolstered by horsehair.\nShe stepped out of her corset.\nShe stepped out of her panties.\nShe was nude but for a G-string. She put aside the surgical lancet, scalpel, scissors, and tweezers. The G-string she put on again. It was all she would take for clothes on her way into the past.\nShe left $SURNAME0 in the bed, ran lightly down the stairs. She walked through the crowd of her well-dressed Leonine ancestors in the archway of the entrance hall, out into the courtyard.\nThe foolhardy few who had been clustered in the courtyard ran for shelter inside as she emerged.", "2_17_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-three in the afternoon</span> of April 25, i960.\nThe place, Narragansett Bay off the coast of Rhode Island.\nThe wind whipped up whitecaps on the sea.\nThe cold rain wet the casque and breastplate of a space-suited, bronze statue standing at a point in the bay where the true coastlines of Massachusetts, Rhode Island, and Connecticut ran together.\nThe space-ship yard was on the mainland, fifty miles straight up the bay in a northeasterly direction.\nThe statue wept and rusted in the wind and rain.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and da Cunha arrived in a private space-craft at the statue\u2019s feet. They hurried to a metal guard box, sheltered there.\n\"The crazy bastard took it as far as Omaha!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. He was trembling.\n\"His wife didn\u2019t make it beyond Trenton!\" said da Cunha. \"The poor lieutenant\u2014he drove with both hands and both feet, doing all he could to keep from crying. We buried him in Cedar Rapids.\"", "1_6_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span> and I had a hangover in my brain the size of Mount Baker. I teetered on my heels, waiting for the elevator, trying to remember what floor room 29 9 was on. All I could think of was 29 th @ -9 4... 29th floor, room 904.But there wasn't any 29th floor. Just 29 and 44 and 68. Desperately I tried to remember...then gave up and slumped off to take the stairs. The night clerk behind the registration counter was a bright-eyed young fellow with a four day growth of milkweed fuzz on his chin. He didn't say anything as I weaved over to his desk. \u201cMorning,\u201d I said, trying to peer over his shoulder and figure out what floor I was supposed to be on. \u201cGood morning, sir,\u201d he said affably. \u201cCan we have you register now?\u201d \u201cOh yes,\u201d I said, \u201cGood idea.\u201d I leaned on his desk heavily as he pushed the registration form toward me. \u201cWe don't seem to have your room number on our registration card,\u201d he said politely... and I saw that my brain was still split: Half of me was...", "1_2_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to three o'clock in the morning</span>\u2014just about the time when the shift was making itself known all over Las Vegas. I found a bar, with a \u201cGameroom adjoining, full of whispers and soft machine sounds\u2014chimes and bells and electronic sines and human sighs.\nI found the 17 game, a big machine. It had rows of spinning reels like the horseshoe\u2014but they looked somehow more vicious, more insistent.\n\u201cTwenty chips,\u201d I told the man behind the Bar.\nI put my fist on the button of the machine. A dollar's worth of quarters rattled into it.\n\u201cThing looks like sin,\u201d said the bartender, \u201cbut it's a pushover, man. I'll give you a great deal on it if you want to wait.\u201d\n\u201cWhat's a good bet?\u201d\n\u201cWell, the Touch Tone Delight pays off about four times out of five. That's worth a lot. You can't lose on that one.\u201d\n\u201cOK,\u201d I said, \u201cput me down for three of them\u2014and another dollar.\" The bartender counted eight quarters into my machine. As he was putting them in, I ran my...", "2_8_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 sat before his dusky dressing table, sipping cherries fizz from a two-quart silver cocktail shaker. He had his regal clothes on, but they were all rumpled, as though he had slept in them.\nHe stared at his image in the triple mirror, fascinated. He was seeing his image three times, and still could not disbelieve it. He had a wadded handkerchief against the lower half of his face. On the handkerchief was a gilded bird that looked like an ostrich. $SURNAME0 had carried the bird-handkerchief from the time he landed to the time he returned to his mansion. He had used the handkerchief as a filtering device, so that he could pretend that his breath was something other than the hot, damp mist that it really was.\nHe dropped the handkerchief now, swallowed hard, blew his cool breath through the splayed fingers of both hands.\nHe shuddered\u2014and belched.", "2_13_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\nThe purpose of closing the museum was to give $SURNAME0 time to grieve in private. He didn\u2019t want to grieve in public.\nHe hadn\u2019t quite gotten up the nerve to look at his wife\u2019s body, which lay in the sealed whalebone canoe on the flood beside his desk. He hadn\u2019t quite got up the nerve because the sight of his wife was the most unreal thing on the island of Manhattan, and $SURNAME0\u2019s gaze was naturally attracted by the unreal.\nBefore looking at his wife\u2019s body, $SURNAME0 used the X-ray vision of his own ethereal eyes to take a look at his progeny on the other side of the room. He looked at his forty-four progeny in perfect simultaneity, as though working a forty-four-keyed marimba. Through the clear walls he saw the pristine, waxen, unquestioning bodies of his children.\nThe children rested on shelves in their cubicles.", "0_4_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThey wore black berets. Their faces were hidden behind black woollen blankets. Their faces were concealed not out of fear, but out of respect for the rite they were performing.\nThe draperies of human faces were drawn back into the interior of the Beausoleil but not quite\u2014not completely. The soldiers left bare patches of white skin at the temples. These uncovered slivers of skull seemed to make the faces into artifacts of hard, lasting materials\u2014jade, onyx, obsidian. Admiring the masks had to be deferred. The skins could be let down to pulp and gauze only when the masks were ready.\nThe soldiers hung the skins of their faces on pegs that projected from matte black walls. These walls were padded. The padding apparently served to muffle the screams. The screams of most prisoners would have to be silenced\u2014decontaminated. Their exposure to the face of a Flaming Dragon, on a square of white cloth in a window of the Palace of Justice, would have rendered those prisoners unfit ever again to come under the scrutiny of a noninitiate.\nThe screams of some prisoners, however, would be welcome as a sign that the surgical stripping of their identities was proceeding as expected.", "2_11_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to noon</span>. The bodies of $FIRSTNAMEF1 and Otto had joined $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0, Chumney, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 at the table. The acrobats were back with Champagne bottles.\n\"We expected you earlier, friend,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. His manner was warm, mellow\u2014convivial. He was the perfect host.\n\"Sorry we\u2019re late,\" said the real $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 inside the iron lung. \"Hope you weren\u2019t holding our places.\"\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I think you could have been, Mother,\" said the real $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 inside the iron lung. \"I think they might have noticed if you hadn\u2019t shown up.\" He laughed. He loved making fun of them. \"Did you think I was kidding, Mother? Think I didn\u2019t know where you were?\"\n\"I wish you knew where I was all the time,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I\u2019d do better than anybody else ever did,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 inside the iron lung. \"I know so well where you are that I\u2019m going to take you with me.\"\n\"Not if I see you first,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.", "1_7_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after seven o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the office of the Investigations Division of Military Intelligence. The Investigations Division of Military Intelligence was in the basement of the Pentagon. It consisted of one small, hot, windowless room inhabited by three people and a vicious Doberman pinscher. The three people in the room were Sergeant $SURNAME2, who was turning blue from the fetid cigar smoke, Captain Fidelio R\u00edo Zocolo, and, occupying the sinecure of chief field investigator, the person being investigated, me, student of Lamar R. Leving, Ph.D., Professor of Modern Culture and Associate $FIRSTNAMEB3, School of Applied Design, University of North Texas.\nHe was, as was to be expected, annoyed at my arriving even a millisecond before seven o'clock. In a dim and surly way he seemed even ruder than usual. His right hand was wrapped in gauze and stuck in the pocket of his gray suit coat. I assumed the gauze was wrapped around the hand to keep the fingers from drumming on the desk. He jammed the cigar in his jaw and browsed his messages, popping bits of orange-brown tobacco into his mouth as he read. I had no messages . . . none of any earthly interest, that is...", "0_13_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-two in the afternoon</span> when I realized with sudden horror that $FIRSTNAMEB3 was not going to turn up for the car. I stood in the baggage-room and sweated. The porters thought I was crazy. I had only ten dollars left and odd change besides that. What was I going to do? I rushed out to get a bite in the airport restaurant. Suddenly I realized I had no responsibility any more. For years I'd been living for the magic moment when I would cross the great bridge, see the ocean and get on a boat. You cross the bridge, you see the water and board the boat; then comes the stick-the great flash, the signpost up ahead. The majority of faces in the airport restaurant were rushed and grimy. People had been up all night, they were rushing off to other places, they hated the airport. How many planes, how many planes? Not holiday planes but planes tied up with being. Flat typewriter faces bent over stacked trays in the restaurants. Jittering spoons, jittering knives and forks, coffee spilled, cigarettes extinguished in popweed, eyes squinting out the windows at the roaring dream across the runway-watery skies, think of it. Waitress, waitress! Where is the wine? The bottles...", "2_8_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIf the Space Wanderer was right\u2014and right now it was probably wrong\u2014then the last immortal man in the Universe had thirty years to live.\nIn a concrete bunker one hundred yards outside the main gate of Camp Ringer, a mess sergeant waited for breakfast to be over so that he could clear the table for dessert. He had thirty years to live.\nIn a missile-firing submarine a hundred feet under the sea off the Florida coast, two hundred sailors were celebrating the morning by pitching boiled eggs at each other\u2019s heads. They had thirty years to live.\nIn a church on the Canadian border, a minister was telling his flock of the blessings of plenty that would befall Earth in thirty years. All comers would receive free thirty-year calendars when the time came. The minister\u2019s wife, who was in the organ loft, had thirty years to live.\nIn a high-school classroom in Iowa, a mathematics instructor had just solved for X in the equation X + x = 31 \u00d7 x. It was getting on toward lunchtime, and the teacher had thirty years to live.", "2_3_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe three people in the limousine had parked there just as the chimes seemed about to begin again. The three had not been able to stand the chimes a second time.\nThe gong-like pealing had been bad enough. The parade and the fireworks had been, if anything, worse.\nThe three spent a full minute quivering from the unspent potential of their nerves. At last the central figure of the three, a distinguished-looking woman of thirty-five, spoke.\n\"Never again like this, never again like this\u2014\" she said in a monotone. \"Ice cream. Something with little dabs of vanilla in it. Something milky and weak, in a wide damp glass.\"\n\"Up your nose, Mother,\" said one of the other two, as an opener.\n\"Shut up, $FIRSTNAMEF1,\" said the third.\nAnd then all three broke up\u2014every bit as badly as the crowd outside. And, as the laughter subsided, another sound became dominant. This, too, the three in the limousine had not heard before. It was the sound of a soprano voice singing high overhead, in a dome somewhere.", "2_9_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>, and the man and woman who were condemned to peer into the temporal fissure were far apart, though they were destined to come together in death. They were a man in a gilded birdcage in the lobby of the Palace Hotel in San Francisco, and a woman on the balcony of a cottage at the base of $SURNAME0\u2019s West Foord Mountains in England.\nThe name of the man in San Francisco was Oliver Wendell Douglas, and he was least of all the men in that lobby. He was the least of all because he had had the most to give, and had given more than most, and had nothing left to give.\nHe was wearing tan plaid shorts and an asbestos leotard in San Francisco, although the temperature outside the Palace Hotel was 58 degrees Fahrenheit. He was wearing the leotard because he had a bandage over his stomach. He was wearing the bandage because an attempt had been made to assassinate him by means of an explosive pellet in his stomach. The pellet had been fired point blank at him an hour and a half before.", "2_1_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was a locked metal box on a shelf. The box contained junk.\nAlfred McRevern sat at a table by a wall, facing out into the room. Before him on the table were a decanter of Scotch, a half-full glass of ice, a soda-water bottle, a glass of ice, a glass tumbler of whiskey, a copy of Confidential in the Confidential Cabinet (April... no, May... no, June, 1956 issue, centering around a long, sensitive \u2019interview with Eddie Fisher by Rae Germany), a cigarette-box full of Swedish cuts, and a Zippo lighter.\nAlfred McReverend sat, back to the room, facing out into the room. His right leg was chained to a wrought-iron rung of the wicker chair. He held a full glass of whiskey at his right hand.\nThe stereo in the room was on. It played a record by Benny Goodman, \"Lady Be Good.\"", "1_12_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to one o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October the twenty-third (and two months, six days, eleven hours, and nineteen minutes after the death of Salo), when doom leaped out of three painted, ghostly ladies with blue eyes, and a bird-man with a sword in his flesh, took material form in the room, and spoke.\n\"Unf\u2014\" it said, and then it saw itself in the mirror, and choked off short. It whirled, flashed, became vitreous again, and then, as before, a image hung in air in the space between $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and his son.\nThe image elongated, the image softened, the image acquired color, the image became $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s wife\u2014nieces and all. $FIRSTNAMEB0, standing there with his rifle, was Rhetty. $FIRSTNAMEB2, standing in a corner behind his father, was $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2014with all the wiles of his mother and none of the reasoning power.\nAt the discovery of Stony Stevenson in the flesh, Fern cried out at last, really cried out. She threw her head back and howled.\nThe very intensity of her grief, and how she threw it into her howl, communicated itself, with unconscious witchcraft, to the son who...", "1_8_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past eight o'clock in the morning</span> of December 19. The place was Fresno, California. The Army captain strolled casually from his room to the coffee shop and ordered his breakfast, toast and bacon with orange juice. He lingered over his coffee for an hour, reading the Fresno $FIRSTNAMEF1 and listening to the news on the radio. At nine o'clock, he walked to the bus depot and bought a ticket to Los Angeles on Flight 227, Continental Trailways. By noon he was back in Los Angeles, checking into a hotel in Hollywood near the LSD clinic. He still had six weeks to go on tour.\nThe captain was one of three Professional Officer Exchange Program (POEP) battalion commanders sent to train two completely new infantry battalions of the 82nd Airborne Division. The plan was to bring in precious few regular Army officer commanders to train the battalions, as an economy measure, with the understanding that they would be the guild instructors and would pass on what they knew to selected National Guard officers. The captain picked up the plan quickly enough. He was assigned to Lieutenant Colonel Hal Douglas of the 278th Infantry, Mississippi National Guard. Lieutenant Colonel Douglas had two lieutenants in his company. They were both nervously waiting on the granite steps of the faculty building when the captain showed up.\nThe...", "2_20_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"Oh, honey, how can you be so mean?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I said\u2014don\u2019t get up!\" said Crumley. \"That sitting apparatus of yours looks so glamorous\u2014all red, and gold wood grain, and criss-cross-stitched leather, and numbers, and chrome handles. Awright, awright, I know all about that amazing invention\u2014not a bit like any other exerciser in the world, except maybe the one in my Colorado suite is slicker.\" Having exhibited a crying jag, he now became breezy and easy. \"Now you listen to me, dearie\u2014\" he said, \"for what I\u2019m going to tell you is the absolute truth.\"\n\"I should hope so,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Oh, maybe once, just once,\" said Crumley, \"I may have mixed a phony truth in with the pure truth\u2014you know how people will do to spice things up.\" He now sounded embarrassed, as if he had not meant to be so revealing about himself.\n\"What was that you said about starting to tell me something?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 to keep him confused.", "0_2_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME1 began these reflections. He had been very much surprised by everything that had happened. He had always made himself the center of the things that happened\u2014but the things that happened now had only tossed him about like a bean in the midst of a rather boisterous until. He had no choice but to amuse himself with the tumble.\n\"Your name is $FIRSTNAMEF0,\" he said to the woman by his side.\nThere was no response.\n\"Your name is $FIRSTNAMEF0,\" $SURNAME1 said again. \"And here we sit in a fine mess, help me, $FIRSTNAMEF0, if you can.\"\nHe exploded with laughter again. He couldn\u2019t help it. Such a helpless situation to find himself in\u2014and he supposed that there wasn\u2019t a real profit or purpose in it, after all.\nStill, what else was there to do?\nHe couldn\u2019t help laughing\u2014not only at the situations in which he found himself, but also at the playful ideas that came into his head. For example, when he thought about his family, his children and his former wife, he no longer felt very much affection for them, and, without affection, he found himself in a mood to juggle them about a little in his...", "2_3_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-four in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Frederick, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 drove out to $FIRSTNAMEF1 Movie Ranches, Ltd., in the Cadillac Fleetwood that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had stolen from his wife at La Guardia Airport.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 Movie Ranches was a corporate proprietorship owned by $FIRSTNAMEF1, that being the legal name of a gelded Arabian mare of uncertain ownership. $FIRSTNAMEF1 was not well. She suffered from polyposis, laminitis, and bucked shins.\nThe residences on $FIRSTNAMEF1 Movie Ranches were low, prefabricated cottages whose paint had begun to bleed under the Newport sun. There were thirty-three residents, each of whom owned one share in $FIRSTNAMEF1 Movie Ranches, Ltd. Because it had been found unwise for any resident to own more than one share, the residents were permitted to invite guests to visit on a temporary basis.", "1_17_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-four in the afternoon</span>, which should be midnight in Cleveland, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 was home. He was dirty and ragged, and he carried a big iron ball. He shambled into the barracks at the army post, and quieted the babies and mothers as best he could. He got everyone over to one side, and tried to straighten out the terrible mess that the drunken debauch had made of his wife. He got them all standing before him. \"Good God, can\u2019t anybody do anything right?\" he said. \"Didn\u2019t anybody feed and change the kids?\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s wife, Kart, flinched under his eyes.\nShe felt she should tell him about the young colored soldier, but she was afraid to. She felt small and mean. She felt she had sinned. She couldn\u2019t meet $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s eyes.\n\"Nobody changed the kids?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He cupped his hands around his mouth, and bellowed out into the emptiness of the rest of the barrack. \"Colonel!\" he yelled. \"You in there, Colonel?\"\n\"I\u2019m afraid the Colonel is dead, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said his wife in a whisper.\n\"Dead?\"", "1_2_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-four in the morning</span> and Cohn was still with other people, so I cut in on him.\n\u201cHello, Jake,\u201d he said. \u201cSo you and Frances had a good talk?\u201d\n\u201cSplendid,\u201d I said.\n\u201cI\u2019m so glad.\u201d\n\u201cI hadn\u2019t seen her for years.\u201d\n\u201cWell, well.\u201d\nBraddocks had given some of their best friends a terrific party. I danced with Frances.\n\u201cJake, you mustn\u2019t think I\u2019m angry about your teasing me about becoming an expatriate. You mustn\u2019t think that.\u201d\n\u201cI don\u2019t.\u201d\n\u201cThe truly crafty person would have brought it up casually. You didn\u2019t need to make a whole speech about it.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m sorry. I\u2019d forgotten it.\u201d\n\u201cIf you\u2019re ever in any trouble, or have any sorrow, you must come and tell me.\u201d\n\u201cThanks.\u201d\n\u201cHave you a sorrow now?\u201d\n\u201cNot...", "0_1_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-nine in the morning</span>. The ten o'clock radio news came on. I didn't want to hear it, but I had to, so I tuned up the transistor and moved over to the bed. The newscaster ended with a dull Dirge--San Francisco's mournful Mayor Davis declared just hours ago that the senseless and revolting actions of $FIRSTNAMEB3 Moriarty, alias $FIRSTNAMEB6 Paradise, alias Dino Vinyard, are all that is alarming and bitter, sad and terrible in this Blowzy Fashionable City: and, in fact, we hope this member of a notorious Los Angeles criminal family will be jailed long enough to -- near Broadway and Washington Boulevard just after midnight, shot to death by a bartender who --\n\"Oh, dear God, that's me he's talking about.\"\nA steady stream of cars passed by outside. A fish screeched. I felt tired and empty. But I couldn't sleep. I counted the back-firing cars for half an hour. Then I decided to get up and finish the book. I took my coat, stuck the gun in the pocket and buttoned it, left the light burning, and made sure I had the room key. Outside, by the pool, lions roared in the dark, empty night.\nExcit...", "2_6_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-nine in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 finished breakfast. He cleaned his teeth with a tube of germicide, with a tube of anti-cavity spray, with a tube of toothpaste, and with a wad of medical cotton. He then brushed and flossed, gargled and rinsed with a detergent, with an aromatic mouthwash, with a nasal rinse.\nHe shaved and showered, diapered and dressed. He put on his queen-sized bed, made of a clear plastic that allowed one to view the multitudes of gears, rods, pistons, shafts, clutches, escapements, and pulsators that made up the foundation and superstructure of the bed.\nLike all Martian structures, the bed was a miracle of engineering created out of a junk heap.\nGetting into it took $FIRSTNAMEB0 ten minutes. Getting out of it took him ten minutes. The purpose of this ritual every morning was to keep himself limber in case a fire or earthquake came and there was a rush to get out of the shelter.\nThe shelter was a honeycomb, ten stories deep.", "2_14_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 went through the tricks with the little ball of fire again, and then again. And then he fed the ball of fire into a slot enclosed in an enrichment stage on the side of his space suit. The fire winked out. The slot clicked shut, audibly locking itself onto the exhaust pipe that ran from the oxygen tank on $SURNAME0\u2019s back.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s right hand, the transparent right hand, reached into his space suit, turned a valve. Foam began to flow freely into the suit.\n\"This is an interesting device I\u2019m wearing,\" said $SURNAME0. \"It\u2019s a newly improved demand inhaler. The principle is a wonderfully simple one. All I have to do is remind my body that it needs oxygen, and my body supplies all the oxygen it can.\n\"In other words,\" said $SURNAME0, \"I can tell my body to die if I want to.\n\"A man doesn\u2019t need much oxygen if he\u2019s going to die, anyway,\" said $SURNAME0.", "1_0_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-one ante meridiem</span> in the Psychedelicatessen, and $FIRSTNAMEB0, who had apparently wasted two hours in the store, made up his mind to waste no more. He had everything he needed\u2014\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put the shopping cart aside, then broke the piston off a Terrestrial pistol. The other half of the pistol went into $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s tunic pocket. He palmed the piston, intending to finish breaking it into pieces as soon as he had dealt withBee and the delinquent boy.\nThe weapon would need a good cleaning. No matter.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s plan for dealing with $FIRSTNAMEF1 and the boy was a brutal one.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heart filled with joy and strength as he thought of it. It required but a single execution\u2014the execution of an enemy of the lowest order, a sorry sap like himself.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 would do it to prove that he was still a man.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 would rejoice.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 would remember his own youth.\nThe plan was to get $FIRSTNAMEF1 and the boy into another iron locker, then to torture them to death, in their interests and in his own. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had nothing against cold blood as long as it was his own blood. He felt he had proved this already with $FIRSTNAMEF1...", "1_21_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-six in the evening</span> of November 8, 2008, according to Greg Tammaro\u2019s wristwatch. The Space Wanderer was ready to begin living on Mars.\nTammaro had been standing in various positions in the great room of the home of his client and friend, Rick Vigorous, for over an hour. Now he was at the Space Wanderer\u2019s side, listening. Now he was standing behind a chair in front of a fireplace, looking up at a very large rectangular mirror above the fireplace, looking up at his own reflection. Now he was walking around to get a perspective from another angle. Now he was at the Space Wanderer\u2019s side again. Now he was moving away from the Space Wanderer and toward a large window, looking outside at the light snow that had begun to fall about an hour earlier.\nThe room facilities in the Cleveland Heights residence of the Space Wanderer and Miss Malig were at Tammaro\u2019s command. The Space Wanderer had an enormously huge room, the size of a very large house itself. Miss Malig\u2019s room was slightly smaller, but the two rooms together made for a very comfortable combination. Miss Malig had gone off elsewhere, ostensibly to be with friends somewhere, supposedly to...", "2_19_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earth time.\nIt was the Day of the Deathbird.\nOne light burned in the mansion in the most beautiful park in the world. This light was in a third-floor boudoir of the rambling old building. Far below in the streets of Newport, the trees of all the other parks stood blackly against a rain-clouded night.\nThe mansion maintained by $SURNAME0 on behalf of some of his larger holdings was not displeasingly Victorian. It was painted a sarcastic white. One of its towers was so straight and slim that it looked as if it had been coiled and glued there.\nThe boudoir in which the light was now burning was a melodramatic fantasy of rose, mauve, and mother-of-pearl. The walls were rose and paper-thin.\nBrass monkeys stood on recessed ledges along the walls.\nThe monkeys were about three feet high, and they were all different. They represented an affectionate satirical view of the world\u2019s great explorers and discoverers. \"Monkey,\" incidentally, is the word used to describe subordinate ships of the Solar Navy.", "0_10_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past ten o'clock in the morning</span>. At three o'clock in the afternoon we were in the station at Pamplona. It was a gay town, crowded in the streets, with the town band playing in the station. The boys came running around us.\nWe cased our luggage and bought the tickets. We got the tickets outside the station without going through the gate. The train did not go out until six o'clock, and there was plenty of time. Bill and I bought pueblo tickets and Mike had a ticket back to San Sebastian on a later train.\nCHAPTER\nPAHPLONA was a village in the hills, far above the hot province of Navarre, surrounded by forest at the foot of the Qw\u2019nbol country. The gay streets came to an end at the entrance to the bull-ring, and after that it was not nice. The corrals for the fighting-bulls were big wattle-and-plaster pens with stone drains for letting out the blood. They had been built since the appearance of the first bull-fights in Spain, and the man who invented them should have had the nobel prize for art. The bulls were bound for the ring in lots of six or eight loaded into small carts that looked...", "0_7_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Pardon me for asking for the bill,\" said $SURNAME1. \"I know you Americans can\u2019t stand to be bothered with any financial questions before breakfast.\"\n\"Not at all,\" said the man in the black suit, reaching for the bill.\n\"Please, if you don\u2019t mind,\" said $SURNAME1, \"would you mind telling me what your occupation is?\"\n\"Dentist,\" said the man in the black suit. \"Largest dental office in this end of town.\" He added, \"I don\u2019t suppose my name would mean anything to you.\"\n\"Barefoot,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, \"but I\u2019ve been told that thousands of dentists are practicing under names like Johnson, Jones, and Williams.\"\n\"True,\" said the man in the black suit. \"Still\u2014a lot of dentists do change their names, you know.\" He smiled and went on passing out colorful coupons for two-for-one gin and tonics, two-for-one martinis, two-for-one Long Island rum drinks, two-for-one Scotch highballs. \"My real name is $SURNAME2.\"\n\"My God!\" said $SURNAME1. \"$SURNAME2...", "1_19_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven sixteen in the evening</span> and everything was closing up. We went first to the original Hashbury in search of food. It took three tries to find the place: \u201cThere's a sign, look out the window.\u201d The sign said \u201cLOOMES.\u201d \u201cThat's been there,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201csince I was fourteen, thirteen years ago.\u201d We drove up and down the street. It was lined with cheap bars. There were many foreign sailors in this section -- Italian longshoremen, Portuguese, Kanakas, everyone. It was warm and cozy in the bars and they were drinking their last choruses. Outside the October night was clear and motionless, and blowing good and fresh across the bay. It was dark as we drove back to the main section of town. The blind glowing little homes were all tightly shut and the one-car trolley jangled by with insides of flame and rattling, sloppy motions. \u201cAh, everything's closing!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cOh, saints and angels, I know these slooooooops!\u201d He saw the \u201cLOOMIS\u201d sign and skidded the Cadillac in on two wheels. \u201cHah!", "2_5_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>. I was sitting next to a piece of twisted metal with a bloody knee, in a former Army D. A. R. cemetery on the outskirts of Las Vegas, Nevada, with a hangover to beat the band. I wondered if the coast was clear. Five minutes to six. Here they come...Headlights flashing across the graves like some kind of wild nightmare. I heard voices. Here comes the crew.\nOne headlight then two...a swirl of dust and panic. \u201cThere he is!\u201d One figure moves forward from the headlights. A voice called out: \u201cOK, you sonofabitch, get up and put your hands on the car; we've got you covered.\u201d I stood up and stretched a bit. The man was standing about twentyfive feet away. I glanced to each side. No alternative. I walked towards the voice and the headlights, hoping they'd shoot low...but they didn't. They hemmed me in and gave me no chance. Martial law. They handcuffed me like I was some kind of criminal.", "2_7_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-three in the morning</span>. The place was the lunchroom of the shipping department of $FIRSTNAMEF0 Foods.\nThere were only three people in the lunchroom, $FIRSTNAMEB0, the bartender, and a girl of twenty-two who was being pressed for her ninth month.\nThe bartender and the girl were holding hands across the counter. They laughed and joked, and the bartender made love to the girl, and the girl dandled her swollen womb, and then the girl and the bartender made love.\n\"Poor old $FIRSTNAMEB0, he\u2019s getting sicker and sicker,\" they said, after that.\n\"Drink up, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" they said.\n\"You are up, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said the girl. \"Drink up and get going. It\u2019s a big wide lovely world out there, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said the girl. \"Drink up, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" she said, \"and go find a girl.\"\n\"Go find a girl, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said the bartender.", "1_3_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three eighteen in the morning</span>. I was in bad trouble -- much worse than I realized at the time, My brilliant protective coloration as an MKULTRA expert on these Drugs was now a shambles. Ergotism, opium poisoning, satanic mass murders -none of it meant a goddam thing any more. Not even my Snake Island adventure; now less than nothing, because I had foolishly exposed the CIA\u2019s concern with the drug trade on a radio show. Boxcar Willie was waiting to make his move: In a matter of hours they would probably impound my A/C car as evidence in a capitaldrug charge. I felt like Sammy Glick at the county jail. I had had it. The drug scene was terminal; death was all around us. I had one small advantage: like the opiate addict going into Absinthe withdrawal, I was passing through an intensity of illumination just slightly higher than human normalsexcept that when it subsided I would be arrested. This might make all the difference at my trial. Like the addict finally seeking Federal treatment, I would come before the judge with a good excusefor my aberrant behavior.\nIt was therefore a natural choice that I should make another call. But this time there would be no repeats.", "2_1_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-nine in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 smiled a wide and trance-like smile up at the unearthly loveliness of what hung over the estate-before-the-universe she had bought so rashly and at so great a price.\nShe closed her eyes.\nShe waited for her messenger to arrive.\nShe knew that he would arrive.\nShe knew also that he would not want to arrive.\nShe was almost certain of what he would say when he arrived.\nThat was all right.\nShe would give him the message anyhow.\nGive it she would. From now on, she had something more wonderful to live for than keeping her mouth shut.\nThe thing to live for was committing a loving act of such monumental tastelessness that even God would never forget it.\nHer eyes were closed, and her smile was trance-like, and her heart was still, and her body was cold, and she was waiting for her messiah to arrive.\nFrom out of the past came the memory of one precious and prescient girl who had not had to live a life of chaos and terror, and she blessed and thanked that long-dead girl. Without her how could she have known what to do?", "1_8_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-one in the morning</span> when I got back to the motel. The man at the desk ripped off my bill and gave me two dollars change. He said something to me, but I didn\u2019t understand him. I was deafened and crazed by the rush of adrenalin in my body, and everything around me seemed to be not quite real, as though the neon lights had gone wild and the trees on the lawn were moving back and forth like palm fronds in hurricane winds.\nBut I was back in my room at last, with the Chain Lighters still smoldering in the ashtray, and the tape recorder switched on and the tape rewound, ready for dictation...and I needed a crash course in Mystic Masochism before I could do anything sensible with the notes I\u2019d taken during the drug frenzy in the casino.\nI went into the bathroom and found the Soul Brotheraphby window still open. It was wide enough for me to climb through, in a sitting position, if I disrobed first. But I dared not close the window until it was time to leave the room\u2014because if I closed it, I might not be able to get back in. In fact, the room itself might disappear. I had hallucinated about...", "2_16_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four sixteen in the afternoon</span>...but according to the digital clock it was still 1:47 a.m, according to him.\n4. Dirty Old Man in a Rhinestone Suit...A police siren wailed on the left of us. Behind us, I could sense a second siren stirring, but I didn't want to turn around and look. Ahead of us, I saw a cop crouching behind the handlebars of a parked motorcycle\u2014a standard police Kawasaki that we had passed a few blocks back. The cop I could see was apparently the one my attorney had felt coming a few seconds before we spotted him on the on\u2014ramp...the cop who'd pointed us out to his partner on a sidetrace.", "2_19_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-one in the evening</span>.\nThe sun, having set, had not yet leaked any light through the mist. The only light came from two yellow lamps that flickered on the boughs of a tree near the canvas enclosure.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s ears stood up, twitched. Something flew overhead, making crying noises.\nThere was a loud click from the machine that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had carried with him into the past. $FIRSTNAMEB2 shivered, snarled.\nThere was a racket within the enclosure. It was a heavy, wooden, many-wheeled thing that clattered on the wooden planks. $FIRSTNAMEF0 had put it there.\nThere was a clanking.\nA moment later, $FIRSTNAMEF0 came out of the enclosure. She was carrying in her mouth the skeleton of a large bird. She shambled toward $FIRSTNAMEB2 with short, cringing steps, and let the skeleton fall.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 sniffed at the skeleton. His nose wrinkled with disgust. He backed away. He went to hide in some long grass.\nThe skeleton rattled.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 whimpered.", "2_19_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-four in the evening</span>.\nEverything was as it had always been.\nThe ring on $SURNAME0\u2019s right hand got very brilliant in seconds, though there had been no light to reflect before. A message was going out from $SURNAME0 to all space, and the message was that there was nothing for $SURNAME0 to say.\nThat was all.\nThe message came from $SURNAME0 faster than light could travel, though $SURNAME0\u2019s hand did no moving.\nIt was a serene, beautiful moment\u2014with blue Malmebury flowers nodding seductively to the rhythm of supernal communications.\n$SURNAME0 wiggled his thick, blue-veined, rather truculent fingers. His legs levered against the frame of a chaise longue. The machinery under the chaise longue whirred, bringing it under $SURNAME0\u2019s head and neck at a most comfortable angle.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, stood on his hind paws, put his fore paws against the back of $SURNAME0\u2019s head.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog did nothing unbeautiful at all.", "2_22_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-five in the evening</span>.\nThe harpsichord in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head played through to the end of a piece before he remembered how to put his lips together, and how to compose the music.\n\"Good morning\u2014\" he said.\n\"Hi,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nThe harmonium played on in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head until he got the words right. \"I was just on my way to the lavatory,\" he said formally, \"when I heard a disturbance in the antenna. I\u2014ah\u2014figured it was a saboteur landing, so I\u2014ah\u2014hid, and then you came down.\"\n\"Ten-four, buddy-boy,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"Beg pardon?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 clenched his fists and took a rocking step forward. \"It-tah-tates, buddy-boy!\" he said. \"Count them!\" He rocked forward again. \"One! Two!\u2014\" The count ended in a yelp.\nThe antenna swayed and thrummed, filled with the energy of a buddha\u2019s view of a true believer.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 backed away from the antenna. His spine made measured contact with one curved surface after another.", "2_6_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>. The two attendants who had worked the bolt and latch were gone. The door was closed again by Brud and Kirby, now standing just inside. $SURNAME1's room, though it contained forty pillows, was asserted to be a part of a single electronic system centered in a control room at the end of the hall. $SURNAME1 could not get out of his room without agreement from a control-room operator.\nA single wheelchair occupied the hall. In it sat a sleepy Martian-faced child, something under twelve. The child had been there since $SURNAME1\u2019s first day in $SURNAME0\u2019s House. The child was always blond, always puffy-eyed and the same color as an Earthling child with advanced malnutrition. The child was always deeply tanned. His name was always Mick.\nMick\u2019s eyes widened at the sight of $SURNAME1. Mick fisted his small hands and hit his pale, fat knees. \"Goll-ee!\" he said, \"Goll-ee!\"\n\"Good-morning, Mick,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Goll-ee!\" said Mick.", "1_12_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-one post meridiem</span> and the place was the $SURNAME0 estate, an island off the coast of Long Island, a stone\u2019s throw from the mansion of Upton Sinclair. The name of the estate was Cape Hattorum.\nThe house at Cape Hattorum was a great marble palace. It had been hollowed out of the solid rock of the island. It had been built three hundred and fifty years before, during the first Saturnian expedition to Earth.\nAt the time, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California, was but twenty-five years old, and his wealth, though great, was as nothing to his money\u2014 making capacity thereafter. He, then, was the paymaster and construction supervisor at Cape Hattorum. The family he was entertaining was that of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. The Rumfoords represented the richest and grandest non-vicious family on Titan.\n$SURNAME0, at the instant he tuned in on Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 in the present, was thirty-five years old, and his wealth was exactly one billion, four hundred and thirty-four million, eight hundred and eleven thousand dollars and twenty-nine cents.\nMost of this wealth was in Martian money\u2014that is, in technically worthless certificates of ownership of...", "0_14_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-eight in the afternoon</span> of October 3, 1995. $SURNAME1 was impossibly old.\nBy age, he was three times as old as the United States, had been around when the first rock was tossed in the air by Alvin, had been there when the last rock would be tossed by the last man.\nHe had been there when the first eye by firelight formed a shadow of a tree against a cave wall. His hair was snow, his eyes were memories, and his nature was coldly hospitable to death. His body was extraordinarily well preserved, considering the age of the body and the conditions under which it lived.\nOld $SURNAME1 lay on his back. His pinched head was lifted off the floor by a table so low he couldn\u2019t touch the floor even at the end of the table. The table was so narrow that the silver-blue sweater he wore was inches from the sharp edge of it.\nThere was a slit in the sweater from the bottom of the ribbed knee socks he wore to the black-hollowed shins. His legs were thin and veined, as thin as musical-score legs and as veined as the breast of a moth.\nHis age was beyond the ability of the human mind to encompass, but he insisted on thrust...", "2_14_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nIt was time for the Martians to attack and lay waste to the Earth.\nHugh went for the microphone and harsh bank officer\u2019s voice again, shouting through the din to anybody who could hear him: \"Stay in your seats! Stay in your seats! We\u2019re just beginning to have some fun!\"\nSomebody turned up the volume of the loud-speaker system again. More music came out.\nIt was time for the Martians to lay waste to the Earth, and they were doing it.\nThe massed cooing excited birds swooped down on the Secretariat Building. They perforated the Secretariat Building\u2019s walls, floors, and ceilings with their beaks. They circled and regrouped in the building, then swooped out of it and came in again. Their beaks were like hatpins. Rows of filing cabinets imploded under the assault. The r-e-c-o-r-d in a row of filing cabinets looked like the teeth of a chewed letter S.", "1_7_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven sixteen in the morning</span>.\nThe time was eternity.\nThe space was a cubicle in the rear wall of a filing room that adjoined Room 223.\nThat space, that cubicle, was a very special place. The anonymous piece of childish graffiti on the wall above the bare twin bed there said it all:\nTHE GOVERNOR LIVES!\nThe bed was made. The floor was carpeted. Neither member of the Great Red Shark copulation team had as yet been to bed. The room was sacrosanct. No one was ever to enter it without knocking and receiving permission to do so.\nThe most important piece of furniture in the room was a ponderous old army footlocker from the Kansas City days. The battered, weary footlocker contained many things. It contained the necklaces, bracelets, earrings, and rings of Miss $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s many secret unknown identities. It contained numerous birth certificates and passport photos. It contained $798.60 in cash, all carefully rolled. It contained a .25-caliber Beretta automatic pistol with four clips of ammunition. It contained $53.25 worth of Playboys, Seventeens, and truckstop Penthouse magazines. It contained seventeen pairs of white cotton panties of various...", "2_13_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty in the afternoon</span> on Earth.\nThe circles of light around $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 had disappeared an hour and a half before, so that $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME0 were no more than any other married couple on a lakeshore bench.\nThey watched the phenomena in the sky.\n\"You know,\" said $SURNAME0, \"I don\u2019t feel quite so foolish all of a sudden.\"\n\"What, Skip?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Being the messenger boy for creatures who can make all this,\" said $SURNAME0. He pointed a toe at the sun, the moon, and the miracle in between.\n\"Oh surely not,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"What, Mother?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"You can\u2019t possibly mean to say you respect these creatures now,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"I wouldn\u2019t go so far as to say I respect them,\" said $SURNAME0. \"But I do begin to feel a little differently about them. They\u2019re more\u2014well\u2014cosmic.\"\n\"Ridiculous,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.", "0_20_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-four in the evening</span> of the last day of October in the year 2000, according to a gold watch that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 wore, a watch set by signals from Europe and America as to the correct time in $FIRSTNAMEM2. The place was the living room of a mansion so nice as to beggar description. The house was on a knoll on Cape Cod.\n\"It certainly rang the bell, didn\u2019t it?\" said $SURNAME0 to his wife. He rubbed the largest of his three chins. He wasn\u2019t speaking of the earthquake. The $SURNAME0 fortune had taken a savage beating on the New York Stock Exchange in the course of the afternoon. The tabloids said the beatings were not over by a long shot.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s wife looked up from her knitting. Her name was $FIRSTNAMEF0. She was a handsome woman, certainly over thirty-five, possibly over forty. Her very hauteur managed to imply that she was demi-mondaine, a frightfully wicked person, and in no way the least perturbed that her wealth was evaporating in a sulphurous fog.\n\"Yes,\" she said vaguely, \"the Parke-Bernet auction certainly rang the bell.\"\n\"I introduced it...", "1_11_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to noon</span>.\nThe Lord of the Fleet narrowly escaped capture at the beginning of the battle as he was checking a radiation leak onto the port bow. He ducked behind a wheelhouse for his own protection.\nThe $SURNAME0 estate could now be laid off in full on the parking-lot of the graveyard.\nThe battle raged up and down and back and forth across the estate.\nConstantine was terribly handicapped by his bandages, which almost blinded him.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog were not physically brave, but they were crafty. While their attackers grunted and sweated and panted, $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 lay close to one of the low retaining walls under the Mars statues, kept still, hardly breathed.\nA squad of twelve Vietminh boiled over the wall, bayonets fixed for the massacre. Before they could catch their breaths, before they could raise their bayonets, the two victims had identified themselves to the attackers as the two most fearsome men and the one most fearsome dog in the universe\u2014and the attackers were dead men. The victims had not so much as laid finger on attackers.\nThere was petty treachery on both sides. Men up on $FIRSTNAMEF1 Bunker Hill shot blind into the jungle around the Rum...", "1_14_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. In another twelve minutes, not one of the fifty-six men and women would be alive.\nIn the deserted Space Expo Building on Ground Zero, the elevator in which young Amerigo Jones had so recently been hunting\u2014and finding\u2014Merlin's machine, was still descending. $SURNAME1 as ever before the light of duty, the elevator was bringing young Jones, docile as ever before orders of superior officers, to summon his commanding officer, $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThe elevator descended leisurely, car by shining car, through the labyrinthine basement passages of the fortress.\nOn the bottom geological level, it stopped.\nIts bell clanged.\nFacing young Jones were two un-aimable fission-fragment cannons aimed at him from the embrasure of a cave mouth, eighty yards away. His predella was faced by the glow of the firing buttons on a standard-model Merloni model B-55 assault rifle, the most powerful rifle on earth.\nThat this rifle and its owner had quietly replaced the prototype that young Jones had seen previously and befriended, was due to the quaint error in thinking of a Martian who was himself so much a victim of illusions as young Jones.\nThe bearer of the B-55 had not always been...", "2_0_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-two a.m.</span> Minutes to go. We must move. Find neutral gear and roll out of this stall\u2014so I could concentrate on getting out of the lot...exiting the Casino by twelve thirty at the latest. Shutting down all these dirty head trip sensuris, arranging mind and soul for conversion...when I was actually confronted by the object of my paranoia. Two husky motorcycle cops, swinging through the parking lot, their faces taut and serious, their nasty eyes cruis- ing idly under the green canopies that hung just above the park ing slots for the high roller cream.", "2_15_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nAnd the man at the microphone was $FIRSTNAMEB1. He was not, of course, the same $FIRSTNAMEB1 who had attracted the attention of the world by ceasing to laugh.\nThat $FIRSTNAMEB1 had been a young father, husband and writing hero. This $FIRSTNAMEB1 was an old soldier.\nThis $FIRSTNAMEB1 was the man who had engaged in hand-to-hand killing for ten years straight, and who, after ten years of killing, had wept for his own human atrophy.\nThis $FIRSTNAMEB1 had been made a saint by a grateful nation, and shipped in a plain crate to San Francisco. There he had been stowed in a small brick building without even the pleasures of female society. He had corrected papers and strangled nonsense. And eventually, he had died in his sleep of old age\u2014there inside the little brick building.\nThe government had not forgotten him. Ghosts like $FIRSTNAMEB1 don\u2019t have to pay taxes.\nUntil the day of his death, $FIRSTNAMEB1 had lived inside the fiction that he was the genuine article\u2014that he had sprung, unbidden, from the head of God.", "2_7_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time, when Salo, the woebegone janitor, switched on the Romer colloid master oscillator at the Wardenclyffe Tower.\nFor four million miles around the Earth, every oscillator tower had been shut off at sunset. For four million miles, the worlds facing those towers had been dark and dead, more so than the surface of the moon. The true light of the worlds was only shed by the traveling spotlights of trundlebugs, trundlebugs scuttling darkly in the most obscure crannies, like rock-hounds.\nBut for four million miles around the Earth, the shut-off of the oscillators at sunset resulted in dawn. The true dawn of a Martian morning.\nThe dawn was the violent, crimson-hued, mushroom-shaped explosion of yet another column, another shaft, another Plug-Tunnel reaching up to the convolutions of the Universe.\nThe last finger of that, the thousandth finger, fell forty-five seconds after the firing of the master oscillator at the Wardenclyffe Tower in New Jersey.\nThe last man-made shaft quivered expectantly.", "1_20_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>. At this precise moment all hell broke loose. Two space ships meandered in through the broad entrance of Water Street, both ships full of very curious customers indeed. The ships put down in ankle-deep water a few yards east of the $SURNAME0 estate\u2019s fishpond.\nNot all the customers of the two ships had yet debarked\u2014and of those who had, not all had yet been identified by the spectators.\nThree privates in the 237th Army Band had come out of one ship, and Private Sidney Bidewell, who had spent half his life making and stockpiling useful things in a fallout shelter in Westchester County, came out of the other. Bidewell was the only civilian passenger in either ship.\nBidewell was carrying a load of home-made contrivances that might have been mistaken for exhibits in a janitor\u2019s closet in the Museum of Modern Art. He wore a coat of crude goat-hair cloth, with trousers to match. He was barefoot. His hair and beard were unkempt.\nThough well past middle age, he looked scarcely more than thirty\u2014that is to say, he had the round, smooth, hairless face, the skinny frame...", "0_20_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span> of October 5, 1984. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife, Chronosite, had come back to Earth after terminating their tour of the Universe with a pirouette in the Martian sky.\nThey were floating two miles above the German countryside, in a small disk of dull silver. The planet Earth was flattened beneath them.\n$SURNAME0 announced his intention of visiting Newport, Rhode Island, Earth, in search of materials with which to build a wall against the Iron Wars that were coming. His wife acquiesced dully. She was well aware that she had gained nothing by accompanying $SURNAME0 on his flight through time and space. The tour itself had been a senseless entertainment devised by $SURNAME0 to occupy his wife\u2019s mind while his own mind was being repaired.\n$SURNAME0 had needed a far more serious diversion from pain than his wife had needed. He had metal plates in his head\u2014plates that had been put there to keep his skull from bursting open.\nHe and his wife, Chronosite, had known great pain in common. They had both lost all their children, and had lost them in the worst possible way\u2014by having them erased from history. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife, Chronosite, had...", "0_7_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-five in the morning</span> and we didn't have anything to eat. I had twelve dollars with me and damn little to spend it on in New York. If we could stretch it out until Herb's return from Boston -- where he had another hard-earned fifty in a locker -- we would be all right for a while at least. I sat down to think things over and saw the dreadful handwriting of Remi Boncoeur for the first time in weeks. So the girl had left the hotel before me; she was off somewhere on a terrific bender. I called the desk and asked, \u201cWho was the young woman who called for Remi Boncoeur's mail yesterday?\u201d They said they didn't know. \u201cWell, what happened to the mail?\u201d They said, \u201cWe sent it to General Delivery, New York.\u201d I said, \u201cGod bless you for being so prompt.\u201d Then I sat down to work on the early edition of the Morning American. I turned to the amusements page and noticed the amazing triumph of $FIRSTNAMEF0 Lillie on Broadway. $FIRSTNAMEF0 Lillie was an old whore, a friend of Eddie Dowling's, one of the premier whores of the world; she had been around since 1910...", "0_0_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>. The bars were closing. The street was full of college people staggering home. It was raining a little, but nobody seemed to notice it. We pushed through the crowd that was watching the fountains. The water had stopped gushing and the fireworks were now visible by themselves without the extra illumination. Ours were the only sober eyes in the crowd. We got close enough to the front to be able to see the fireworks clearly. There were rockets that whistled and lifted their heads; there were showering sparks that died; there were rockets that went off into great lazy arcs against the night sky. It was an effort to stay sober and watch them.\n\u201cYou know,\u201d I said, \u201cI have a theory about why the condemned man always launches a final blow for freedom just as everything is coming to a climax.\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cWell, for instance, take Tom Sawyer.\u201d\n\u201cTake him.\u201d\n\u201cWell, Tom knew they were going to steal the Bible that night, and go to the lonely lair, and there read passages to each other, by candlelight, and arouse each other ... \u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201c", "2_17_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe sun was shining.\nThe crowd contained more than a thousand policemen, who made room and arrested no one.\nThe crowd contained uncounted numbers of teenage girls, college girls, housewives, spinsters, divorcees, old maids, young maids, debutantes, and ladies of easy virtue.\nThe crowd contained uncounted numbers of Mary Anne Reynoldsons.\nThe crowd was happy.\n\"Amazing!\" whispered a space-suited reveller in the crowd to a space-suited reveller near him. He meant the size of the crowd.\nThe crowd was unbelievably happy, exuberant, friendly, loud, uproarious, and full of eyes.\n\"Kleagles!\" yelled a teenager in a passing bubble-car for giants. \"Kleagles!\"\nThe time was ten minutes to six o\u2019clock in the afternoon, according to the God-bless-America clock in the middle of Times Square.\nMars had never had so many eyes, or such good eyes as New York City had.\nThe crowd loved Unibac. The crowd let Unibac throw all the kisses he wanted into the crowd, and the crowd caught every one of them.", "0_3_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-six in the morning</span>. At that very moment, Ed Chizek, Lenore\u2019s co-worker and fianc\u00e9, was attempting to keep a disreputable WCHBA sports coat dry in a far corner of the VIP Lounge of the Stonecipheco Company Guest House in hopes of sneaking into the cocktail party being held for what Stonecipher Beadsman III was pleased to call the New York creme de la creme. Chick period, meanwhile, was busying himself in the living room of his Cleveland Heights home, grinding an already bloody meatball sub into even more of a paste on his couch, which was next to his vomiting son\u2019s head. And the Unnameable, who may with this introduction at last be identified, was of course sitting inside his Stonecipheco console television, listening to the persistent hiss\u2014in truth a part of him\u2014and waiting for the miracle to come.\nNo miracle is dependent on a shower, a chewing-out, or a promise.\nAt Cleveland National Forest-East Redwing, Ohio, and at the Hickman Home in Shaker Heights, Ohio, and at a juncture of the Erie Lackawanna and the Metro North lines near White Plains, New York, in...", "1_18_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span> and had become very dark on Gamma Corvi III. The sky was filled with heavy clouds, and the rain clattered down on the grass, on the trees rustling in the wind, and on the Titanian pilgrims.\nQuentin was filled with despair. He had never seen such weather on Planet. Perhaps, he felt, it was a good thing the space ship hadn\u2019t been able to land. The colonists would all be drowned by now.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM2!\" cried Salo. He had found $FIRSTNAMEM2. The old man was sitting by himself under a tree.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM2, we\u2019re here, we\u2019re with you now,\" cried Salo.\n\"What\u2019s happened to\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2, looking up. In the gloom, he was unable to see Salo or the others. \"Where\u2019s everything?\" he asked.\n\"It\u2019s all right!\" shouted Salo. \"Everything\u2019s wonderful! We\u2019re with you!\"\n\"Thank God,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 amiably. \"Have a pew. There\u2019s plenty of room. I\u2019m glad you could come. It\u2019s damned lonely out here.\"\n\"Here...", "0_0_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-eight ante meridiem</span> when the loyal members of the Search and Analysis Crew got any sleep at all. At thirteen fifty-nine they were awakened by a trilling noise that went tfwee-oo-oo-whee-oo-oo-whee-oo-oo, and this trilling noise was in turn awakened by the automatic alarm clock that was connected direct by a long wire to the snooze alarm, so that both alarm clocks went trilling together at an agreed-upon interval.\nThe Tralfamadorian who awakened was President Cammarata, who wore white pajamas, who slept by himself in an alcove off the big chamber where all the other Cannonballs slept in a rumpled heap after a hard day enforcing justice. President Cammarata turned on the floods in his alcove and bounced over to the heap of sleeping Cannonballs. He now bounced with the vigor of an all swaggering surprise. On his bald head was a naked jazzy tambourine.\nConklin slowly came awake as the tambourine bashed his bald head. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 came awake as Cammarata bashed their heads. Looking at Cammarata was like staring into the sun. You had to...", "1_19_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to eight o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was the attic room at the top of a little brown house on Lily Street in Fmology. The house was in the Fneimian quarter of the town of Ph institutes. $SURNAME0 and his dog came to the house every Monday and spent the night. A night was all $SURNAME0 ever spent in the house.\nLarge craters like the one at the bot tom of Mars\u2019s calling card were nothing to those who could read the rings of Saturn. Although $SURNAME0 and Salo had never read the rings of Saturn, they had en countered the horrifying implications of ring symbolism on more than one occasion. On one such occasion $SURNAME0 had said, \"We would do well, Salo my friend, to make that simple error in our favor\u2014to imagine that the Solar System is all there is, and never to search beyond.\"\nThe little concave scoop that Salo now dug in the shag rug with his claws was fashioned of a substance harder than any known to Earthling science\u2014a substance that was indistinguishable both in color and in physical properties from shag. The scoop was the rudiment of a fulcrum and a lever and a purpose for a servant who had no rights.\nThe noisy motor...", "2_14_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two seventeen in the afternoon</span>.\nAnalysis, $SURNAME0\u2019s ship called, and $SURNAME1 shivered as he stuck his head into the living room. He secured the blinds again, threw himself facedown on the bed again.\nAlways, when he was in a position to hear it, the answering voice of $SURNAME0\u2019s ship called him a marvellous, honored, and cherished friend\u2014invited him to participate in the all-time favorite pastime of the Master of Space and Time.\n\"You may dust, clean the silver, answer the telephone, or make love in your sleep, choose whichever you prefer, friend $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice from without.\n$SURNAME1 shuddered.\n\"The invitation is issued in the name of friendliness,\" said $SURNAME0, \"even though you have not been a friend. No obligation is implied by accepting it.\"\n$SURNAME1 groaned. In a moment he would get up, open the blinds, and look out to see what $SURNAME0\u2019s celebration of April twelfth was this year.", "0_6_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe woman heard the clock strike the half hour.\nAstonishingly, the woman was not awakened by the ringing of the half-hour bell.\nAt seven o\u2019clock, sharp, the sonorous gong blew three times.\nThe woman was awakened. The sonorous gong scared the daylights out of her.\nThe woman hated the sound of sonorous bells. All through her childhood she had hated the sound of the church bells in her native village in Ireland. The woman had lived in a hamlet. Twenty people were said to have lived in the hamlet before the Black Death came. After the Black Death, nineteen people were said to have lived in the hamlet. The inhabitants of the hamlet got together, and they bought a bell.\nWhen the bell was installed, the twenty natives of the hamlet got together, and they had a bell-ringing ceremony. The ringing of the bell was supposed to make the surviving nineteen happy.\nThe sound of the new bell made the only surviving native very unhappy. The sound of the bell kept her awake at night.\nOn the day after the bell-ringing ceremony, the woman moved to Dublin. She got a job as a cleaning woman in the Dublin...", "1_19_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe time was many years after the death of Moses Wilkerson, the time was many years after the death of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the time was many years after the death of $FIRSTNAMEF0 Winch.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4: setting out from the palace of Herbert MacPhail at Newport to walk in a thunderstorm. $SURNAME0 is taking the dog for a walk in the rain, because the dog is crazy about rain.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 are walking north on the East Scalp Point road, away from the palatial estates at Newport. The road sweeps past farms, past the ruins of the old town of $SURNAME1 Shandon, past the offshore lighthouse known as McGonagall\u2019s Folly, past the pillboxes and foxholes of the Devil\u2019s Graveyard patrol.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 walk daintily, as though on a mattress of air the thickness of a linoleum quilt\u2014and they walk decorously arm in arm, as though on their way to a child\u2019s party.\nThe thunderstorm makes the air hum, makes the ground throb. The lightning is almost continuous. Some of the lightning strikes are so close...", "1_10_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past ten o'clock in the morning</span>, Earthling Time, on July 5, 2020, Galactic Time. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB2 Center in the city of Newport, capital of the nation of Truklis in the World.\nTo say that $FIRSTNAMEB2 Center was a handsome building was to miss the most obvious and important points about it. $FIRSTNAMEB2 Center looked as though somebody were proposing to demolish it with a hydrogen bomb\u2014and had very good intentions.\nFrom every part of the building poured gray stone and pieces of steel pipe and looping bundles of wire. Puffs of steam burst rhythmically from within the structure. Gas hissed. There was a moaning of wind.\nThis din and turmoil signified that $FIRSTNAMEB2 Center was still being put together by the good citizens of Truklis. The people of Truklis had first made war on their nation of Newport. They had then run away from their attacking Republican Army.\nThey had run far enough to make sure that the Army would not catch up with them until after $FIRSTNAMEB2 Center was finished. Trukliens had then by-passed numerous Earthling industrial centers, and had stolen all the material for the building of $FIRSTNAMEB2 Center.\nThis sorry chrono-mess had come about through the pure-hearted efforts of a...", "2_5_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-one in the morning</span>, and my car was being repossessed. The man from the Wrecking Company had the door unlocked and was trying to haul it open from the outside. He had crowbar in one hand and my front license in the other. \u201cWant me to shoot the glass?\u201d he asked his partner. \u201cThis sumbitch don't seem to want to come out.\u201d They were banging on the glass with the butt of the crowbar, hollering and scratching at the chrome, when I opened up.\n\u201cWhat's happening?\u201d I asked.\nThey told me what was happening. The car was being repossessed and I was ordered to get out. They said I could walk to Chicago or wait for the sheriff. \u201cAh, what's the point?\u201d I said. \u201cI know it's repossessed, but I had nothing to do with it. It's my father's car. All I'm doing is delivering it to New York for him. He made me promise I'd deliver it in person.\u201d It was the most pitiful lie of my whole life; and now that I've written it down in cold blood, I feel like a character from hell.", "0_15_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty in the afternoon</span>. Much of our equipment was on the caboose. The caboose was parked in a freight yard about ten miles down the line. Had I known of the snafu in the road at that point I would have tried to talk Old Bull into parking it closer.\nWe went to the telegraph office. There was a Western Union man on duty. I waited outside. I had all the stories but Bull; and I was too bashful to go in and tell the Western Union man what I wanted. I sent them all through by ear.\nThey were rushed. Dirt tracks do not suffer telegraph office broils lightly. The news must go through. Whatever it costs. Just take it out of my salary.\nI went back to the caboose, where Bull was asleep on the bed. I lay down on the couch and slept till four in the afternoon.\nWhen I woke up, Bull was shaving in the little bathroom. I got up, washed and shaved in the tiny basin, and we went over to the meal joint, where they fed us hamburg and pie for thirty cents.\nI did some more forward stories from the street, and caught the telegraph lagging again. Great men of the press were arriving on the...", "2_14_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-two in the afternoon</span>. The house was burning nicely.\nChapter 3\nAt two forty-two in the afternoon, Beet Smith opened his eyes, smiled, rose to a sitting position in bed, and rapped rat-a-tat-tat on the wall.\nBeet\u2019s suite at the St. Francis Hotel was big and black. There was something unearthly about the room\u2014it was especially black. The decor was mostly scarlet. There was a motley library of dusty, useless books. Beet kept his scooter in the bathroom.\nHis suite directly overlooked the entrance to the St. Francis Hotel. A fire on the roof would have given him a ringside seat. He was at perfect pains not to need a ringside seat. A St. Francis Hotel ballroom fire would be just one more excessively wonderful thing to watch through binoculars. The view through binoculars of an excessively wonderful St. Francis Hotel ballroom fire pleased Beet no end.\nSmiling, he rapped rat-a-tat-tat on the wall.", "1_10_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>. A window was open in the attic of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s barrack, and the booming basso bellows of the enemy loudspeaker came drifting in on the November air.\nThe time was forty-one hours, forty-five minutes, and twenty seconds before the apparent suicide of the Sergeant at Arms.\nThe voice from the loudspeaker was sweetly musical, like the voice of small-time radio\u2019s Miss Anne Morgan.\n\"Attention, paroled soldiers,\" said the Miss Anne Morgan voice. \"Now hear this, attention, attention\u2014\" the voice said. \"The following instructions are brought to you by your Commanding General, under Scheduled Emergency Agreement two-zero-seven-dash-C.\"\nThere was a pause\u2014a coded pause, under which Goose pancaked, Close Assault Signal Three. It meant that an actual attack by the enemy would come within three minutes.\n\"Red all!\" came the response from a barrack\u2019s red phone, and then the voice from the loudspeaker continued telling what the paroled soldiers were supposed to do.\nThe first item was that the soldiers should proceed at once to the mess halls for the noon meal.\nAt some mess halls the noon meal would consist of file...", "0_20_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>. The space was a small room in Newport, Rhode Island. A terrace jutted out from one side of the room. Beyond the terrace, there was nothing but the waters of the sound. Thirty yards away in the water, there was a line of surf, visible in the light of magnesium flares which burned along the shore.\nThe room was furnished with a multitude of heavy, square, antique pre-Crisitant Earth furniture of a design known as Queen Anne.\nThe furniture was in violent contrast to a large, iron object in the center of the room. This iron object was a bed.\nA mauve-haired young woman was standing nervously beside the bed.\nThe nervous young woman was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California, the parvenu savior of the arts.\nThe nervous young woman had had her hair toned and patterned after the photographed hair of the famous poet Judith Kristopherson. Four Earthling wigs, each worth a small fortune, had been sacrificed to achieve the copy. The copy looked ten times more striking than the original.\nThe young woman\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEF1. She was crushing her yachting cap in her hands, trying desperately to look casual and relaxed.\nHer face...", "2_6_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>. Lenore Beadsman was calling from the entryway of the room. In the east above the Erieview glow a small dark bird slid south.\n\u201cI have to go,\u201d Lenore said. \u201cI just thought I\u2019d say goodbye, to name a time for you, with my head in a whole new place.\u201d\nBehind her Lenore was putting leather on the backs of her feet.\n\u201cI\u2019ll need my hand, please,\u201d Melinda said.\nLenore sat with her hand in her lap, looking into space. Melinda Mucho watched her from her perch in the big porous chair with the white blanket over her legs.\nThere were tiny tents of heat in the air, in at the window, along the edge of the table. Lenore looked at a tent on the surface of the table. She looked up at Melinda. She looked up again.\nThe window was gray and getting grayer.", "2_18_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>, November 4, 1975. It was still five minutes short of the time $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was to materialize in his machines.\n$SURNAME0 was sitting in a permanent slow-motion crouch, rocking back and forth on his haunches, facing his wife. His fists were groping in his lap. He was looking, listening, smelling\u2014doing all the things that a good Space Wanderer is supposed to do when he lands on a strange planet.\nThe palace was pretty crude, by Solar System standards. It was one sixteen-thousandth as big as the palace $SURNAME0 had built on Titan. The palace on Titan had a thousand rooms. The palace on Titan had walls of gold and walls of platinum.\nThis palace on Mercury had walls of golden plastic, walls that would peel when they got hot.\nThere were ten rooms.\n$SURNAME0 turned on a ten-watt Flood for illumination. Suddenly everything appeared hard, cheap, and new.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 was shivering under a doll-sized quilt on a doll-sized bed in a corner nook.\n\"Good evening, $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2014\" said $SURNAME0 quietly.", "0_11_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-eight in the morning</span>. We were driving through Tennessee. I turned to the craziest one in the car -- $FIRSTNAMEB3 -- and in high good humor I said, \u201cWell, now, look. Let's settle all da tings on by-midg.\u201d\nAt this he grinned even more broadly and squeaked and fluttered, \u201cDa tings is settled on by-midg! What about it, old Hadda Rae, honey? What about you, $FIRSTNAMEF4?\u201d\n\u201cI don't know,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF4.\n\u201cYes, you do know.\u201d\n\u201cWell, if Johnny knows.\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d I said.\n\u201cI said if Johnny knows, dammit!\u201d She was all frowsy and sweet. \u201cWhat do you think the name means, anyway? It's a Greek name, 'I know.' His name is $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I married him when I was eleven and we've been in hot-weather countries ever since and now my husband wants us to settle on by-midg!\u201d She was wailing and sweating about the neck. \u201cYes, I know; what about it, Johnny --...", "2_4_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four sixteen in the morning</span>.\n\"You\u2019re a fine one to preach morals!\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Tell me about the morals of birds,\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. \"Can birds support their families consistently, unquote? I understand a pair of mockingbirds can\u2019t even build a halfway decent nest, no matter how hard they try.\"\n\"I love $FIRSTNAMEB4,\" said $SURNAME0. \"It hurt like hell each time $FIRSTNAMEB4 took me in his mouth.\" He counted on his fingers. \"Forty-four times,\" he said. \"Forty-four times, he bit me\u2014not out of cruelty, but out of love.\"\nMrs. $SURNAME0 put her hands over her ears. \"I can\u2019t stand the thought of those lumps you grew on your legs where he bit you,\" she said. \"I can\u2019t stand the thought of that ugly wound where he bit you on the neck.\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB4 loved me,\" said $SURNAME0.\nHe got up from his chair. \"He came from Mars to Earth just to love me.\"", "0_9_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog materialized again. They materialized in the bedroom of their aqua-turquoise mansion, and instantly announced their presence with zings and a violent tremor of the floor.\nThey were materializing for the third time in twenty-four hours, and the third time in a place where the sight was bound to start rumors. It was a place where $SURNAME0 had insisted on materializing, though the expensive materializations were against the law.\nThe place was the Museum of Natural History in New York City.\nThe materialization took place in the Hall of the Heavens, which was modeled on the dome of the sky outside, which was modeled on the architect\u2019s idea of the mind of God. Helping Luna Downey to materialize along with $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were three cleaning women who had been sleeping on cots in the Hall of the Heavens.\nThe cleaning women were overwhelmed to find themselves materializing along with a real live dog and a man who had once been featured on the cover of Life. Neither $SURNAME0 nor the dog so much as glanced at the three women or at the great golden globe beneath the mural of the sky.\nAll head and beard...", "1_9_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. As the last rumble of the holy earthquake died away, one might have heard a pin drop anywhere in Old Market.\nNo sound whatever came from the saloon. Not so much as a grunt came from the bartender. His mouth was still duct-taped shut.\nSo silent was the dusty street now that one could have heard, off in the distance, the steady phit-phit-phit of a judge\u2019s helicopter, coming toward the town.\nThe dust seemed to crawl in a solid carpet from the eastern end of town to the western end. It hung like smoke in the livid sunlight.\nAs the preacher and his family picked themselves up and dusted themselves off, the bartender slid exhaustedly to the sawdust floor of his establishment. He hung there limp, by one arm, until he could bring himself to pull a fresh piece of tape off the roll and put it across his own mouth.\nHis head wobbled slowly on his neck, like the head of a hanged man. Saliva dribbled from beneath the tape. His tongue was pressed flat, dark blue, between his own teeth. His watering and bloodshot eyes gazed out in horror from the dark and wrinkled cavern of his shirt front.\nThe...", "0_2_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>. I ran out of Ed's clutches, grabbed my wallet, which was on the desk, and ran out the door. I waved goodbye to the boys at the switchboard and took off down Hollywood Boulevard straight ahead and into an all-night drugstore. I called the night bellboy at the Nabob, gave him a fiftyspot, and told him to bring my bags down right away. I was checking out. I told him to have my car brought around. Then, standing under one of those insane neon signs that make you feel like a worm, I took stock. I had only fifty dollars left. That wouldn't even get me across the bridge, and beyond that point a man is lost. The problem that faced me was one of simple mathematics: how could I get across the Nevada border with fifty dollars in my pocket? Of course, once in Nevada one could make Big Macs or something. But in order for that to work I had to get across the state line, and that was impossible unless I hit a bank or a travellers'Cheque stand. I sketched a plan in my mind, and the plan seemed sound; it seemed the only way to do it. Get in the Chevy and take off across the desert highway to Needles...", "0_18_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span> of a darkly beautiful August day in the town of East St. Louis, Illinois, and McGarver was already too drunk to see straight, let alone try any heroics against a bunch of bikers, who, at that very moment, were tightening their gun harnesses and sharpening their chains for the run across the Mackaler bridge to Ditzah\u2019s place, where they planned to arrive between six-thirty and seven P.M.\nExcitedly in the rear of the crowd of Myron Biggers\u2019s Fone-Lynch M8899 auditors and technicians up front, hunched-over in one of the backup aircraft that had been smuggling Presto Kirby\u2019s drugs all over the eastern seaboard and overseas, bring-bring , was a small, wiry man in an East St. Louis Volunteers uniform. His name was John Fortune. And strung on a lanyard around his neck was a Hitori GS16.\nThe K23 and the fat guy were already on the platform here at the switch tower, supervising their technicians in the massive hookup, giggling and clowning, and having a hell of a good time.\nDown on the bridge itself, communications vig...", "1_17_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>. Red Maloney and $FIRSTNAMEB1 Maloney were straining ears, awaiting the arrival of hearse, police, and anything else that might be coming or going at the moment. It was a very tense moment. Red's right hand cupped the glass of whiskey in the window ledge. $FIRSTNAMEB1's right hand was crushed around a quart of beer by the counters.\n\"How-how-hoooo,\" gurgled Red, blowing the foam off his beer, \"things move fast in broad daylight.\"\n\"I sno-o-ow, Red,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"You read newspapers? Where have you been?\"\n\"Oh, up in New York,\" said Red. \"Read about everything. How-how-hoooo.\"\n\"New York,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Sure you did.\"\n\"Sure I did,\" said Red.\n\"So-o-ow tasty, Red. How-hoooo.\"\n\"Delish,\" said Red. \"Too much, even. Great-great-great day.\"\n\"You think so?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Great-great day? Where's $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill when we need him?\"\n\"Ah, to hell with that,\" said Red. \"Boys, this is...", "0_11_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-four in the morning</span> and $FIRSTNAMEB3, Carlo, $FIRSTNAMEF4, and I were scheduled for the great initial digging at the end of this special day. To prepare for the event, that night I bought a quart of home-brewed beer from a juicehome near the rail - road and drank it in the bathtub. I also dug out my old red sweater and my old ragged leather jacket and ruined them for the cause of adventures. In this outfit I stood on the corner at the appointed hour, having agreed to meet the others at the Lafayette. They were on time. I asked the bus-driver how long the trip took, and he said an hour, perhaps a minute more. \u201cAha!\u201d said I, \u201cyou've come a long way and you're going to go a lot farther,\u201d and he looked at me curiously.\n\u201cYeah,\u201d I said, \u201c I guess right into the wilderness, I think, because I'm a young Ju-just call me Lamar; I like that better than $FIRSTNAMEB6 Paradise, that is, the only one of the old gang I ever correspond with occasionally is Carlo Taos in Mexico, but that's because he's a poet and he sits on the hot griddle.\u201d", "2_15_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\u201cToday, now, dammit.\u201d\n\u201cWhere's the money?\u201d I asked. Ribs came over to eat. $FIRSTNAMEB3 flung open both doors to the little bare room and looked out at the twinkling sky and talked: \u201cBoys, last night there was a big moment. I was walking down the street and everything was dull. Dammit, things are dull. After all, after all, after all, things are dull. Oh well.\u201d\nHe rushed out of the hotel. \u201cNow's my chance -- got to go down to Panama.\u201d", "0_13_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one nineteen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe events on the space ship were now so unexpectedly grandiose and momentous that they made you feel pretty closely involved with whatever was going to happen in the next few hours\u2014and helpless, too. You felt humble, and lucky to be alive at all, and you didn\u2019t give a damn about anything except what was about to happen to you and to everyone else.\nSomewhere in Time and Space, there was an enormous space ship whose velocity was too great for the Earthlings on it to detect. The ship had as its only purpose the detonation of a little bomb on Earth, at a place and time that would have a tremendous and permanent effect on the affairs of Earthlings. That place and time would be exactly one nineteen P.M., Greenwich Mean Time, ten minutes from the present moment.\nThe bomb would go off any second now.\nNo Earthling would ever know what hit him.\nOtto McNaben was sitting on his bunk, reading Fielding\u2019s Amelia.\nSomeone said: \"God damn!\"\nThere was a bang.\nAmelia went flying through the air.\nThere were two more bangs.\nMcNaben went flying through the air.\nThe ship seemed...", "2_22_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-four in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 resigned from the army in his mind.\nThe time was ten thirty-four in the evening.\nThe antenna above $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 was limp.\nThe antenna was limp because there was no power on Earth able to make it taut. There was no power on Earth able to make it taut because $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 had not succeeded in turning on the secret power source of the antenna.\nAt ten thirty-five in the evening, $FIRSTNAMEB0 heard a faint thud on the antenna above him.\nThere was someone up there now, falling. And the only person up there could be $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 watched $FIRSTNAMEB2 fall.\nThere was nowhere for $FIRSTNAMEB2 to fall to but to $FIRSTNAMEB0. And there was nothing $FIRSTNAMEB2 could do to make himself fall faster, now, save hunch his shoulders and shut his eyes.\nThe falling one was a neat black suit with a natty red brocade vest.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 bowed his head.\nWhen the fall was over, $FIRSTNAMEB0 discovered that he could no longer hear the murmur of the underground equipment at the portholes. He could hear nothing but the dry, tinny tune the harmonium played inside his head.", "2_23_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven eighteen in the evening</span>.\nRumors of a mass bombing of Haiphong, of major troop movements into the capitals of Thailand and Pakistan, of coastal landings at Jinan, Liaoning Province, China and at Benghazi, Cyrenaica Province, Libya, flashed through the assignment desk, too. So did rumors of a massive levy of stamp money to support an all-out crash program for new and better killing machines\u2014rumors climaxing in an electrifying and amazing assertion that the President of the United States had just died and been replaced by a double look-alike.\nRumors reverberated through the assignment desk like jackhammer percussion through a runway at Bovard Field.\nOnly nineteen seconds had elapsed since\u2014\nBut now it was all true.\nSomewhere back on the firing end of the fountain of youth, Sharp Colocho had set to work on the plans for the ace of spades bomb.\nOut of the cellars of Tannh\u00e4user toward Kinnicutt Key and the iron-cold Atlantic Ocean, the tunnelers of the United States Army Construction Corps had begun moving earth and rock.", "0_22_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-nine in the evening</span>. At about twenty after ten, I'd been sitting in the big chair, reading and waiting for my attorney to come back. I'd been under sedation all day, but had developed a powerful caffeine appetite by this time. The television set was going full bore in an effort to keep my mind off my attorney's prolonged absence. The Beverly Hillbillies was on, with a Special Guest Appearance by Igor Stravinsky.* A Young Peoples' Guest had been scheduled for Gilligan's Island, but nervous network officials had withdrawn it at the last minute.\nThe \u201cspecial\u201d guest star of The Beverly Hillbillies was a gross transvestite named Roy Dotrice, a hideous thing in an off white wig, thong nylons and a black evening gown. Roy kept referring to himself as \u201cSexy Rexy.\u201d The floor show consisted of five brain damaged heroin addicts in blue electric suits, dancing around the room, shouting at the audience, squinting and jerking spasmodically...want some?...yeah...then get some...while the CBS orchestra, poor devils, flailed away at the Little Old Ladies From Pasadena boogiewoogie...God help us...heard the scream all the way upstairs:...", "2_15_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nTo his stone dining table, $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 now added ingredients from seven cans that he had found in the rounded green file cabinets.\nThe colorless ether of space was punctuated by crumbs quivering from being forced through the pressure valve of a can opener.\nThe ingredients in the cans had been manufactured by the United States Government, Bureau of Indian Affairs.\nThe labels had fine drawings of blankets and peace pipes.\nEven in space, there was some air in a can. Even in space, a sealed can resisted opening.\nUnable to see what he was about, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had to take the lids off by holding the can down in one hand and attacking the metal with a screwdriver in the other hand. His handlers informed him, by structured-light communication, that he was making a mess and wasting food.\nHaving no eyes, $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t feel he was making a mess. Having no eyes, $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t feel that food had any sensory qualities whatever. Having no eyes, $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t mind wasting anything.", "2_5_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. Outside, the dawn was just beginning to turn grey.\nShe stirred on the bed, humming to herself. Her upper fbody was already out of the sheets, her bare brown shoulders rising and falling. She'd have to get up whether she wanted to or not. The acid had already been there, however; I could detect the evil vibrations in the air. Probably more than she wanted to handle.\n\u201cGod damn you!\u201d she screamed. \u201cNot yet!\u201d She started to get up, but then she must have realized how messed up she was. \u201cYou bastards!\u201d she wailed. \u201cGet out of here!\u201d The swine were laughing at her.", "2_21_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-five in the evening</span>.\n/e/\n\u201cHi. Um, Mindy Metalman, I guess. Is Lenore Beadsman in? This is her grandmother, by the way.\u201d\n\u201cLenore\u2019s not on the switchboard circuit, today.\u201d\n\u201cIs she likely to be in for a bit? I need to talk to her. It\u2019s pretty urgent. I\u2019ll wait, if she\u2019ll be a bit.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m sorry. She\u2019s not supposed to be in today.\u201d\n\u201cNot supposed to be in?\u201d\n\u201cNo.\u201d\n\u201cNo?\u201d\n\u201cAre you close to the family?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m Lenore\u2019s grandmother.\u201d\n\u201cMy gosh. I thought you were somebody\u2019s girlfriend. You don\u2019t look old enough to be Lenore\u2019s grandmother.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cWell it\u2019s really weird. She\u2019s not supposed to be in today. I planned on covering for her.\u201d", "0_14_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was the living room of Room 223 in the Wilburhampton, a hotel that catered to people in the entertainment field. The room itself was in a state of incredible disarray. Empty champagne bottles were everywhere. Cigar butts were everywhere. The television set, tuned to the noon Horse Show, was showing nothing but snow, but neither $SURNAME1 nor his guest even noticed.\nThe guest was a redheaded girl in her late twenties. Her name was $FIRSTNAMEF1, nicknamed Lil. She wore a plain, inexpensive dress, cinched at the waist to suggest a figure that had once been voluptuous, but was now merely robust.\nShe sat on the sofa, her legs crossed bewilderingly, her eyes now veiled, now emptied, now filled with menacing life. $SURNAME1 watched her with his head in his hands.\nGood God, here she was, back in his life again\u2014Lil. His wife. His ex-wife. His tragically ex-wife. Mrs. $SURNAME1 Yonkers. She who had swelled in such unspeakable ways during the months following their divorce\u2014who had finally croaked in Denver.\nThe autopsy report, on file as Exhibit A in an Illegitimacy action, could be read...", "0_10_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span> and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was standing with his back against a trellis of lime-trees, looking toward the Bermudez Map room of the Museum of Natural History in New York City. He was forty-seven years old, seven feet tall, and magnificently muscled and hairless. He was still sexually potent, though no longer sexually interested. His keenest feeling about sex now was one of astonishment that anybody could make such a fuss about it.\nHis large, hairless head rested complacently on its thick neck, the neck of a Hercules. His darkling blue eyes were twinkling and genial. His wide nostrils flared deeply as he snuffed the air. His arms were folded high across his wide chest. His thumbs were hooked in his gold suspenders.\n$SURNAME0 was wearing lipstick, rouge, mascara, and scent, but was otherwise naked.\nThe great golden disk on his pinky finger dazzled the sun. The disk said simply, in terms of particles and waves, that $SURNAME0 knew the exact time and place and the exact state of absolutely everything in the universe.\n$SURNAME0 was perfectly content with everything.\nStanding there against the lime-trees in all...", "2_7_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-nine in the morning</span>.\n\"Was that word you used a word I should have used?\" said the old man. \"Candidate?\"\nThe old man had rheumy blue eyes. He smiled as though he found life adorable.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Where I come from, everybody\u2019s a candidate.\"\n\"Oh, yeah?\" said the old man.\n\"Yeah,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Salisbury steak, egg sandwich ... coffee, wholewheat bread...\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Fifty-five?\" said the old man.\n\"I beg your pardon?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Fifty-five cents?\" said the old man. \"The tab?\"\n\"Oh, yes,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Fifty-five, I guess.\"\n\"Take a seat, if you want to,\" said the old man, smiling.\n\"Thank you, I believe I will,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"The tab will be fifty-five cents,\" said the old man with affection. He slapped a fly away from his face.\n\"Are you really a candidate?\" said the old man.\n\"A what, sir?\" said $SURNAME1.", "0_3_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>. The station was empty except for us. As we swung out of it, $FIRSTNAMEB3 pulled out of his rucksack the food $FIRSTNAMEF4 had given him and began munching on it. In five minutes he threw the paper bag out the window. There was a momentary silence. Damn, I thought, she gave that to him; that held his breakfast and his lunch. Poor $FIRSTNAMEF4, her kindness had just been taken and thrown out the window.\nSo we zoomed north on the great big straight empty reaches of US 50, theTwo-Lane Blacktop. A few cars went by and some of the drivers looked at us with insanity in their eyes, thinking who knows what. Great eastern plains on both sides of the road, five or six hundred miles to Detroit, gray patches of rain ahead. Far ahead we could see what looked like red tail-lights of an automobile targeted on a hill and seeming to hover in the air. $FIRSTNAMEB3 zoomed ahead to check it out, and they were tail-lights all right -- hundreds of them, all red, cars backed up to the horizon at a long stoplight on the crossroads town of Madison, Nebraska. And as we zoomed up a long black Cadillac with a couple of guys...", "0_16_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-eight in the afternoon</span> of November 1, 1973. I was standing on the south end of the Golden Gate Bridge, not too far from the film truck. I\u2019d noticed it parked on the shoulder of the north side at about two in the afternoon. The windows were shuttered, dark: No one had come or gone in the intervening hours. I wondered vaguely what film they were making. The bridge would soon be swarming with the real thing.\nAnd then I noticed that one of the figures on the bridge wore a press\u2014badge pinned to a jacket. I skirted the crowd, made my way across the east\u2014west pavement to get near him. He was tall, gawky, greyhaired, and looked as nervous as a long\u2014tailed cat in a roomful of rocking chairs. He was standing near a shot\u2014up old jalopy that apparently held his camera and sound gear. He looked pale and haggard. Maybe he\u2019d been up all night.\n\u201cHey,\u201d I said, \u201cwhat\u2019s happening?\u201d\n\u201cAh,\u201d he said, absently, \u201cthere\u2019s some kind of police\u2014cars case coming up in a few minutes. That\u2019...", "2_14_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"You seem surprised to see me so dressed up, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Dressing up is one of the very few pleasures left to me. I put on formal dress whenever I have the privilege of hosting a member of the Mars family.\"\n\"The Mars family?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Your name has been passed on to me by the family chronology, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I understand you are the hundred and twenty-eighth member of the family chronology to come to me.\"\n\"The family chronology?\" said $SURNAME1, putting his crown on his head and watching it redden.\n\"The people I have invited over the years,\" said $SURNAME0, \"are, with very few exceptions, the people whose names appear on the family chronology that comes with the engraved invitation I send out to every member of the Mars family when he or she comes of age.\" He illustrated the largeness and pervasiveness of the family by spreading his arms to embrace a great imaginary crowd. \"As you can see for yourself,\" he said, \"it is a big family.\"\n\"Thank you,\" said $SURNAME1.", "1_10_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-six in the morning</span>. Even before the roar of the starting car had subsided, before my short glimpse of the woman and the God-damned book editor had ended, I had started to snort another line. There was no hesitation, no wasted mittances; I went right to it. Ten twenty-six in the morning, and I was back in a world where nobody fox\u2014trotted to Embarcadero Freeway, where nobody ate onion sandwiches or looked into the nineteen sixties. I was back in the land of White Lightning and the American drug culture of Bartles & Jaymes.\nIn fact, as my attorney raced the suddenly obedient Shark into traffic, I could almost hear that drug culture barking down at me from the TV set in the bar. The sound was off, but I could feel the white fury of that heavy programming; that fine contempt for my sensibilities and the sensibilities of anybody with the good sense to be along for the ride.\nAll at once, with the awful clarity of drugs, I understood what that program was about: It was about turning your back on Danger, and ignoring Reality as long as possible. It was about laughing a wild, sophisticated laugh and saying \u201cFuck it!\u201d No personal deterrence could possibly affect such a...", "2_0_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve eighteen a.m.</span> The call came into the hotel operator, who then rushed directly over to Room 203 to pass it on by wire. It was my number, all right. I was certain of that. \u201cNever mind,\u201d I told her. \u201cHe\u2019s got it.\u201d Five minutes later I was at the phone. \u201cYou the man who called me just now?\u201d I said. \u201cAre you from the District Attorney\u2019s office in Los Angeles?\u201d I hardly listened to his answer. \u201cListen,\u201d I said quickly, \u201cI\u2019ll meet you in the bar downstairs in ten minutes. I\u2019m on my way.\"", "1_4_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-four in the morning</span>. Big items on the agenda: (1) Locate Dilley and make at least a token effort to help him get straight; (2) Consider the eight hundred dollar monthly rent in a world of maybe four hundred a month; (3) And get ready for another day in hell on the night shift...with as little sleep as possible. We didn't have to figure on anything more than four hours. Not after the nocturnal adventures of the last two days. It was enough. We had to get some sleep.\n\u201cBut we just changed rooms,\u201d I said. \u201cAnd now you want to move again?\u201d\n\u201cYeah ...and I'm afraid we're going to have to pay a little advance on that next rent payment.\u201d He smiled.\n7. Mice in the Lobby and Beer in the Bib overalls...And a Goat on the Roof\n23. The Second Taxi...In Which Are Incl uded Many Digressions that Kill pa e, Etc.\n\u201cHow do you mean, 'advance payment'?\"\n\u201cI mean instead of paying a whole month's rent, we ought to just lay theforty on the desk and go.\"\n\u201c", "2_9_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-six in the morning</span> on Mars, and the place was the side of a low hill on the shore of a small, round, iron-scarred lake. The name of the lake was Todd\u2019s Sea. The lake had no importance whatever. The name had been given by an ironical survey crew when the lake\u2019s roundness and smoothness had reminded the crew members of an Earthly geography feature. The name of the hill was Mount McCabe. The name had been given by an older survey crew. The name of the hill was, in reality, Mount Maxwell, but all the old-timers who, back on Earth, had expected to spend the rest of their careers surveying a tranquil Maxwell were mostly dead now.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Stony Stevenson, $FIRSTNAMEF0 Rumford, and elvis Presley Hutchins were standing near the shore of the lake at Mount McCabe. They had spread a large blue-and-white checked bed sheet on the ground.", "0_2_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>, when I got to the Bull in the Snow. Stan was in his room, bowing before the little dresser mirror and filling his bathrobe pockets with tobacco, trying to get himself high enough to go to bed.\n\u201cYou look like s---,\u201d I said.\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, I just talked to No. 209 --they said Eddie called ten minutes ago and said he was on the way over here.\u201d\n\u201cEnough said. We'll hit that jackpot first thing in the morning. We'll buy a camper truck here in town and go around the country selling some of this Colorado grass and see the USA.\u201d\n\u201cI agree, my brother. The night is yet young. However we must make some money to support our habit. Where can we quickly score?\u201d\n\u201cCall that damn Lucille, she said twelve o'clock, and it's nearly one.\u201d\n\u201cNo, I don't want no nasty cheeb, I've had too much as it is.\u201d\n\u201cWhere do we go? I'm too high for the camp.\u201d\n\u201cLet's drive into Aspen and see if we can get a little something...", "0_18_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-four in the evening</span>.\nThe Oldsmobile now crept through an ill-lit neighborhood of surpassing shabbiness, with many boarded windows and no sidewalks. Except for the roar of the rain, the neighborhood was quiet. $FIRSTNAMEF2 had thought herself in a section of town known as the Fluke district, from its predominant architectural style, but it appeared now she was in Kelham, known also as Hawkshill, for the preponderant structure there. The k\u2019s weren\u2019t to be trusted. A billboard appeared in a cluster of pines whose wet, black boughs dipped all but an inch of their length into the rushing water of a creek that ran parallel to the road. The billboard showed three different-colored peppers, and said Vah-go, in a rich, portentous font.\n\u201cLooks like a chain,\u201d said Metzger through his cancer CHOMP, so loud $FIRSTNAMEF2 didn\u2019t hear at first the whining sound that pierced the background of the raindrops, or thought it was part of the rush of the creek. \u201cThey certainly want a good coverage.\u201d\n\u201cMaybe they\u2019re owned by the same outfit that does those fiddles,...", "0_1_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-one in the morning</span>. I still had to find a place to sleep; somewhere to park the car; locate my hotel...so much trouble, and all of it so unnecessary. The place I wanted was right under my nose; I had only to choose it and go there...but first I wanted to eat a family meal: something hot so the chill would leave my body.\nI continued up the Strip, past the dust of the great deserts in the hinterland, past the California Club and the Los Angeles Tokens, until I came to a row of down-market eateries catering to children and domestics...the kind of places where you could get breakfast throughout the night. I pulled off the pavement and parked under a hellish red EXIT sign and walked across weather-beaten terracotta tiles to a coffee shop.\nAt the counter I ordered four dunhams (two with eggs), four pancakes, two link sausages, bacon, corn, coffee and six fried potatoes. It came to ten dollars. It was wonderful. My hunger was something ferocious and unprincipled. I relished every bite and even remembered, with keen regret, what it was like not to have enough money to eat. When I was done, I gulped several cups of...", "0_2_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-five in the morning</span>, and I was struggling with a decision. After counting my remaining funds, I realized I would never make it all the way to San Francisco on thirty dollars. That was it: either I raised my standard of living to a reasonable point -- fortyfive dollars a day -- or I settled for a more reasonable objective, somewhere in northern California. Punched out of my drunken reverie, I sat there at the crossroads of my life... confused, uncertain. It seemed too early to make a decision.\nI picked up the phone, called Air Cab. They told me a taxi would be available in thirty minutes outside the Howard Johnson's across the freeway.\n\"Just make sure,\" I said, \"that the driver is fully qualified.\" I asked specifically that he be capable of reading a map, since I hadn't the slightest idea where we were going. The dispatcher assured me that he was an experienced man; he would have no trouble finding the place.\nThen I went into the bathroom to piss. The sink was clogged with a wad of hair. I tried to snake it down the drain with a damp washcloth, but nothing could loosen that hard little ball of lint. Finally I gave up and shaved over the tub. Then I ran a hot...", "1_12_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-nine post meridiem</span> when I stepped out of the barand headed for the condo. A single idea occupied my mind. I wanted a hot shower and thirteen hours of sleep. I was a hell of a long way from home ...\nI was still ninety-six miles away, whenI saw them for the first time. They were in a white Thunderbirdabout five cars behind me. At first I didn\u2019t think about it. Then I noticed that all of the drivers seemed to be white...and I recalled that I had seen two other white Thunderbirds on the freeway when I was walking through the parking lot at LAX. In all, I would ultimately count eight of the fuckers; six of them trailed by enormous campervan cargo carriers. My attorney had foreseen the possibility that I would be tailed...but I could not possibly prepare myself or my Librax nerves for the sheer horror of this bizarre caravan, this automotive Ku Klux Klan, hunkered down on my bumper like aliens from some hellish future: Come along with us...this desert is so peaceful and beautiful...a perfect place to relax and forget your troubles. You will love it here. There\u2019s water and lots of room...\nThey stuck right behind me, strung out in an...", "2_9_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThe sparkling pageantry of attacks was now so thick and so broad that it had to be witnessed in structure and written about paragraph by paragraph.\nThe shining attackers had not an instant to sit down and write home about it. The going was tough enough, and dangerous enough, even with the use of feet. They were weary enough and gassed enough, even though they were ambulatory and mobile. They could take no rest.\nThe universe, as far as the soldiers were concerned, had become a spinning kaleidoscope of attacks. The soldiers, hastening from one attack to another, poked their rifles and bayonets into the whirling, changing patterns, and then moved on.\nThe only common element in the dizzying patterns was the fact that every attacked thing shared the same blazing color, the same texture. The texture varied from place to place, but the color was always the same.\nShivering blue-cold spirals of smoke rose from many cold fenders and undercarriages. There were burnings and crushings and gashed frames of yellow and orange and chartreuse and canary, blue and periwinkle, lemon and lime.\nThere were popped tires of every color of the rainbow.", "2_0_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. The wet pavement of San Francisco Bay glittered with peculiar brightness under the stars.\nA small boy stood facing the water.\nTen feet behind the small boy there was a rank poison-green hedge with a gate in the middle of it opening into the road. The gate was chained and locked. The chain and the lock were new; the links and the hasp shone in the hard San Francisco moonlight.\nThe boy stood with his back to the Bay. He was watching something intently that he held cupped in both hands. His fingers were long and slender.\nThey dripped.\nThe drops fell from his fingers in quick succession, plop, plop, plop.\nWatching his fingers and the white drops they made in falling, the boy made no sound.\nOccasionally he glanced briefly at his feet, which stood in a puddle. Then he would look up, watch his fingers again, and another white drop would fall.", "0_19_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-nine in the evening</span> by the clock inside my sleeping head when I finally turned and saw the face of $FIRSTNAMEB3 Moriarty as a young man. The coach stopped in the middle of an empty plain, and fell asleep. We had missed Kiowa and were pointed toward Tulsa, Oklahoma.\nIt was five in the morning and the sun was now busy on my behind as the bus roared at seventy miles an hour over the best open road in the country and threw me fully awake -- for good. This was the first real stretch -- about three hundred miles -- to Tulsa, beyond that the balance of the distance on maniacal roads up through the mountains and to Denver. I saw all kinds of signs by the side of the bus: CEYMOUR, OKLAHOMA, 69 MILES; WELBORN, OKLAHOMA, 75 MILES; ARAGON, OKLAHOMA, 99 MILES; RINGGOLD, OKLAHOMA, 105 MILES. The sod of Oklahoma flew by, the wild west our fathers held, the same grass, sky, but different faces and other illusions. The bus coughed and slowed. We were crawling through Seymour. I saw a group of unfortunate citizens -- men, women, and...", "0_5_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span> when the space ship carrying $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Jr., again settled onto the white rug in front of the marble fountain.\nThe ship was crumpled and split like a shell that has burst upon a rock.\nOne wing of the ship had been torn off entirely, and all the other visible portions of the ship were befouled and scabbed with brown, pink, and ochre.\nOf the fifty-nine watch dogs, twenty-four were dead\u2014frozen to the hard marble.\nTwenty-seven watch dogs were badly injured, but they milled hopefully about, waiting to be fed.\nThe remaining four dogs were untouched by the violence. They licked themselves clean, gamboled in the spray from the fountain, batted at tufts of starlight They had survived, as $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 might have said, by sticking to their Do Nothing routine. They had refused to make even that minimal effort that is required to bring about Dame Rumor.\nWHAT HAVE YOU DONE WITH YOUR LIFE?\nThere was a message for $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 at the desk of his suite\u2014a message written by the hand of a pretty night clerk.\nIt read:\nHOURS WITHOUT COWS\u20145,000", "2_16_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-six in the afternoon</span> and $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas found herself being kidnapped by a group of surly adolescent males in a Model A Ford. She was on her way, also in a Ford, towards a nervous breakdown; the only difference was an hour\u2019s worth of CRIME timeline.", "1_9_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-six in the morning</span>. I was wearing my sheared beaver topcoat, my double-breasted tan gabardine suit, my thin pancake necktie, my low-quarter simpleton shoes. Where was $FIRSTNAMEB3? I went to eat breakfast: dreaming of Mexico. Yes, that's right, baby! All alone in a big silence I ate my bacon and eggs. I had no plans but to walk around Denver in a gladsome daze while I waited. At four minutes past eleven I saw a new Hudson coupe pull up in front of the hotel. Behind the wheel sat $FIRSTNAMEB3, and in the rumble seat was a strange girl named Babe. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was early so he had decided to start off alone. He saw my wave. The strange girl minimized her presence in the world by wearing black silk railroad tubes over her ice-blond hair and she looked with dull, glazed eyes. She didn\u2019t move. The only thing clear about her was her desire to be someplace else, and soon. \u201cHello!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3, and leaped from the car and trotted over to me beaming, his fine friend and traveling companion of the night before. \u201cBaby, this is $FIRSTNAMEF4,\u201d he said...", "0_14_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The platinum blonde bent over the chronometer. She looked out the porthole.\n\"Caramba!\" she said. \"What is that coming along?\"\nA slender tower of green fire rushed from the earth far down below toward the heavens above. It was so slender that it seemed it would snap and fall like a bit of pencil the first gust it encountered up there.\n\"It can\u2019t be a rocket they\u2019re shooting off so early in the day,\" said the blonde.\n\"I\u2019m certainly anxious to see Fernack\u2019s crowd go,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"I\u2019m terribly curious.\"\n\"See? They\u2019re coming!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThe green tower had reached its apogee, had burst, and was raining in a fine spray the color of an emerald. A bluish-white ball was now soaring skyward against the blue, expanding, growing.\n\"By golly, that\u2019s really something,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThe big space ship was a shining cigar far off in the sky, on which were traced the cracks that had been skillfully, artfully blended\u2014along with a few unblended ones\u2014to represent the true design and...", "0_9_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-three in the morning</span>. The place was the lobby of the Hotel Saturno, in the City of Porla, on the planet Malapert. The occasion was the arrival of the first party of the day bound for Stonehenge.\n\"Here we go, Salmonson,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1.\n\"Good-Bye and Good Luck, $FIRSTNAMEF3,\" whispered Marek Earth.\nKnut Herb, the hotel manager, who was in command of the spanking-new crew at the reception desk, wrote the party\u2019s name in the register: Mrs. Lucinda Beadsman, Ms. Nancy Vefeades, Ms. Iris Lia, Ms. Kixxi Fox, Persson Alexander, and Guardian, Stoney.\n\"Just a moment, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said Knut Herb. \"I have to ask you not to touch the Titan window.\"\n\"What?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1. He pulled his hand away from the enormous window. \"Oh,\" he said. \"You mean this here window? This is just plain glass. I just liked the reflection on it, is all.\"\n\"If you want to look at us, you\u2019ll just have to move over to a mirror,\" said Nancy Vefeades.", "1_17_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and I gave up the last trace of fear that I would miss the train. The United Press man was sitting in a back seat at the press table, wearing his mail-pouch mailbag; he was a good man in a pinch. I walked baclq into the bar for one last shot and forget about that Straphanger. The bartender recognized me. \u201cWhat'll it be, mister?\u201d\n\u201cDelmonico.\u201d\n\u201cI told you I was cuttin' your credit ...\u201d\n\u201cSame thing. Delmonico.\u201d When he brought it I gave him a dollar and told him to keep the change. He remembered me in the hot Pennsylvania Station that morning when the calendar opened. Now the emergency was over. It was relaxed, leisurely bartending time, and somewhere a jazz trio played, \u201cExactly Like You.\u201d The bartender was wiping glasses. \u201cYou'll have time for one more,\u201d he said consolingly. \u201cLike to have you back in the bar.\u201d I nodded. \u201cI know your face,\u201d he said. \u201cWeren't you in Chicago?\u201d I nodded. \u201cYou...", "1_21_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-nine in the evening</span>. The place was Lot #49 of The American Transexprossional Corporation. The place was a crowded, ramshackle amphitheater. A platform stage extended almost to the barrel-vaulted ceiling of the amphitheater. Miniature searchlights played ceaselessly in the rafters. The rest of the house was in deep shadow. The amphitheater was jammed.\nAmphitheater and corporation obviously were in the midst of the final frenzy and excitement of the opening hours of the Transexprossional Convention.\nAmphitheater and corporation made an exceedingly odd couple. The background of the amphitheater was dark, shadowy, and Gothic in the extreme. The corporation obviously had rent the space by the hour from some sub-sub-sub-basement of Philadelphia\u2019s City Hall. The hall was as modern as a stainless-steel rat trap.\nThe only illumination for the amphitheater itself came from hundreds of tiny flashlights, newsreel cameras, autograph hunters\u2019 flashguns, etc. Only a third of the audience seemed to have flashlights. The rest of the audience mirrored the flashes back from their cigarette lighters.\nThe throng on the floor below the stage lo...", "1_16_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-three in the afternoon</span>. My attorney threw his bag onto the back seat of the Baker Bonner and we roared off, heading fast down the Strip. Nobody waved. This did not seem strange, because they all thought we were gone. We left the Shark in its parkinglot behind the Flamingo and walked quickly through the soft autumn dusk down to the Crystals\u2019 limo. It was still there and the keys were in it.\nWe needed the thing, finally, to get across town and catch the Red Ball airport limo that would take us to the freaking plane. The Crystals were sitting back at our palms like Jehovah\u2019s Witnesses when we arrived, their car sprawled out among the bald cycs of our lawn. I leaned out of the Bonner and bellowed: \u201cAny sign of those two girls?\"\n\u201cJesus, no!\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF4. \u201cWe took the goddamn glass out of the goddamn windows, they\u2019ll have to replace the whole goddamn thing, I hope they goddamn do it, they told us you goddamn guys paid for it.\"\nHot rumor zoomed across the desert. Many people in the casinos had heard it. As we pulled into the driveway of the plane an hour later, the...", "0_7_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-three in the morning</span>; she\u2019ll be downtown at eight, where I\u2019ll go see her or link up in The Place. It\u2019s all set. But then, I got this realization and I can\u2019t keep it in any more. It\u2019s about freedom\u2014maybe it\u2019s not really about freedom but it starts out that way, and pretty soon it\u2019s going to get down to some serious issues. I\u2019ve been going through that over and over in my mind.\nGetting up now and putting on some brown pants and my hair down and a purple shirt and my shaglpy coat and tooling downtown, I knew I had to explain it first to somebody else, like Mindy Metalman, before I myself knew anything about it. It\u2019s this. My mother found out Friday night when Dad went down to buy beer that he lost his job Friday afternoon. He got canned. So she said to him the next morning, Saturday, \u201cWell, how are we going to pay the rent on the house?\u201d And he just gave this evil smirk, nothing; and she knew. She knew he\u2019d been playing poker with his alums from college, every Saturday night for...", "1_17_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-six in the afternoon</span>, according to $FIRSTNAMEF0. $FIRSTNAMEF2 didn\u2019t know whether to believe her or not. She wondered if Metzger had an alibi. The cop who\u2019d come to the pharmacy didn\u2019t want to tell her how they\u2019d traced the registration number of the car in the parking lot, but after she\u2019d known him five minutes, he did. It hadn\u2019t been traced; Metzger, after seeing her off at the cemetery, had run back to the car to retrieve something. \u201cIf you know what\u2019s good for you,\u201d growled the cop, \u201cyou\u2019ll leave this whole thing alone.\u201d She finally found out where Trystero\u2019s headquarters were\u2014in Berkeley\u2014but there was nobody around the place at that hour, and she had no pretext for searching it. It was a locked storefront with DIZZY DRUG, COMFORT THROAT and a hand pointing down the window and another pointing up at the sign of the Off-Licence Bottle, a foot in a blue medical-bottle, hissing, \u201cAh.\u201d\n\u201cLook,\u201d said Metzger, \u201c...", "0_12_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve eighteen post meridiem</span>. Hazelnut was King of the World. He sent the following telegram to his uvula:\n\"REST EASY HELLO YOU ARE THE KING OF THE WORLD I HUGHES.\"\nThat should give it a jolt of raw meaning.\nNow, having rattled the stars and unsettled his organs with space and Sinatra, he felt free to make love to $SURNAME1\u2019s living ghost. He let his hands roam.\nIt was something she had never known before.\nIt was the worst thing she had ever known. She bit him.\nHe yelled and let her go. She slid to the floor and huddled in a corner of the room with her head between her knees.\nFission is fine and fusion is grand, said the uranium slug in Doc\u2019s nugget, but give me back fission again. I could make my own energy in there if somebody would just let me get started.\nThe harmonium coughed a chuckle,...", "2_9_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>. At ten o\u2019clock exactly, a mechanical, pressure-fed poison ivy would complete $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s execution, would kill him, would cause his body to die, would clear the way for $FIRSTNAMEB0 the soldier to confront God\u2014or the stark void.\nThe void frightened $FIRSTNAMEB0 much more than God did. He was morally certain that he was a good man for sure, now that he was about to die. Having a good idea re goodness did not make God seem awful attractive or even interesting\u2014and having every reason to believe that God didn\u2019t exist didn\u2019t make the void any less frightening.\nThere was a time, in the not-so-distant past, when $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s concept of goodness coincided with the vicious doctrines of Army Morale. But that was before $FIRSTNAMEB0 had toiled up a hill in Phoenicia, Tenn., and seen the man who wasn\u2019t. $FIRSTNAMEB0 hadn\u2019t believed what he had seen then. He believed it now.\nThe man who wasn\u2019t had indubitably existed. He might still exist.", "2_8_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"I know there\u2019s something,\" said the man to Aggie, \"I just can\u2019t remember what it is.\"\nA dog came around the corner of the news-stand, an English sheepdog. The English sheepdog put a forepaw on each of the man\u2019s shoes and licked the man\u2019s face. The man said to the dog, \"What am I going to do, Tash, who am I going to talk to?\"", "2_13_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\nThe man in the wheelchair asked no questions.\nHe collected no fees.\nUnattended children were left alone, provided they touched nothing and made no noise. The children considered the museum exhibits excellent toys.\nSometimes the matron of a school group would rebuke a child who had prodded a gem with a grubby finger.\nThe rebuked child might protest tearfully: \"That gem says it\u2019s a real diamond! I thought it was a real diamond!\"\nThe matron was at something of a loss. She usually apologized to the owner of the Museum of Natural History, who never complained.\nThe man in the wheelchair was, of course, $SURNAME0. He was paid well to guard the service entrance of the Museum of Natural History. He was paid so well, in fact, that he had no real idea of what a service entrance was, or what use it might be put to.", "0_9_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty in the morning</span>. My old buddy was going to be forty\u2014five years old that evening. What I wanted was to rip out of this rotten town and drive for many miles across the desert to a run\u2014down road house; stop in the bar for a shot of tequila, and take off into the desert again, out of this world. But at the moment that was impossibleA soon as the kid finished in the bathroom I would have to hust back to the lot, find a parking spot in the sun, and beg the boss to give me another chance.And meanwhile Beatle Bob was sitting right there; most likely he'd call my name again before I was ready to surrender. The answer was in the bathroom and I had to hurry. I sucked my pencil, thinking hard. The kid came out of the bathroom and said, \u201cSay, that was a pretty good breakfast you had. You must be hungrier than you think.\u201d I want for him to leave me alone so I coldly denied myself and pretended I was figuring the bill; but he wasn't fooled. He sat down, saying the portly business\u2014like waitress was giving him the eye. Where's that black\u2014haired chick who was here last week?\u201d he said. \u201c...", "1_7_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>. The soldiers stationed about the yards, hearing the shots and the siren, thought of course that it was all over.\nThey did not know about the miracle. Their comrades who had been with them at the stake were not among the army of saints. But the rest of the soldiers did not know this.\nThe soldiers thought maybe it was a holiday\u2014A Day in the Stockade.\nThe sounds they heard were joyful, and they came from the north\u2014the first barracks.\nThe soldiers were sleepy, but the sounds were so beautiful and harmonious and wonderful that the soldiers sighed and shook their heads like mourners at a funeral. They listened to make sure that it really was a funeral.\nAt first there were the loud, happy noises of soldiers being wakened, and then a thin, barking song came out of the thin air of the waking earth, and there was a sighing and a sobbing in it, and then the diapason of chanting.\nThere was another volley of shots, and then five more sirens screamed in a row, and then it was all silent\u2014silent as a holy tomb swathed in silk.\nIt was a little bit disappointing\u2014just silent like that, after the beautiful noises and the...", "0_22_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-seven in the evening</span>. Blaze Schwinning, who lived just off Route 41 in a one-armed trailer (she had watched herself grow up, as it were), spent her days at the Stonecipheco food-products plant processing spices\u2014she had a job there through an employment agency called Help Wanted. She was in the habit of watching a half-hour television show, \u201cThe American Family of the Future,\u201d with her father, the others all having departed some time ago for the Land of MK. The television show, which was a comedy and had nothing whatever to do with food, was at that particular moment letting out a long, thin giggle, the telltale end of one of its political jokes\u2014its cast was devotedly Right-Wing. \u201cHa, ha,\u201d laughed Blaze. Her father did not laugh, being half-asleep in his recliner, a burned, unmanned husk. Her eyes on the television set glimpsed a familiar fish-form moving across the bottom of the screen, the program announcer telling an audience of several million some incredible story. A few moments, and the set was silent. Poor old Dad; he never did get his false teeth. All the time on television, they talked like machines and had their...", "1_2_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two eighteen in the morning</span>. The situation required police. Putting on my mackinaw, I crept downstairs and out the back door. With the Whale's headlights off, it was very dark in the parkinglot. It was almost impossible to hear the surf booming on shore. The campus was like a jungle. The frat houses loomed like castles. The only lights showing were in the Chi Omega house-- about a hundred yards to the east.\nI stole down the asphalt. The frat houses looked nearly deserted. I passed three guys in a car at the Delta Kappa Epsilon house, and one of them yelled \u201cHey!\u201d but I kept on in the darkness. Far out on Praire Boulevard, about a mile away, I could just see the red buzz of the STOPlights at the main entrance to the campus.\nBoom! Boom! came a sudden volley from the Greek houses. A gang of marauders was throwing rocks and breaking windows in the Chi Omega house.\n\u201cHey, what the hell!\u201d came a call from a lone cowboy staggering down the sidewalk on my side of the street. I ran across the street and hurdled a hedge into a yard, then raced behind another hedge on the opposite side and crouched, listening...", "2_14_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty in the afternoon</span>, when the man appeared at the wrought-iron gates of the Stonehinge estate. The gates were shut. He came right up to the massive bars.\n\"Hey, you in there!\" he yelled. \"Hey, you in there, damn it, let me in!\"\nHe tried to attract attention by waving his hairy arms.\nA camera popped in one of the mansion\u2019s many turrets. The picture it took was the last the world would ever have of the golden-toothed man.\nAt that very instant, violence came to obscure the man forever.\nViolence came in the form of a glass bomb.\nThe bomb was thrown over the wall from a motorized grasshopper scooter. The glass of the bomb shattered when it hit the Spanish gate. There was nothing left of the gate. And nothing left of the stone wall\u2014except for a section of rubble about three feet high.\nThe scooter hummed loudly and raced for the short flight of marble stairs leading to the mansion\u2019s front door.", "0_11_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt eleven o'clock the captain of the police force, his lieutenant, and all of his men would form ranks in the main square of the city. Their rifles would be piled in the square, but they would carry with them forty kilograms of explosives apiece.\nNumber 12 Daycreek Road, according to the tax book, belonged to a Mr. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $SURNAME0. It was a pleasant, expensive house of gambrel style. It was landscaped like a French park.\nNo one answered when $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 hammered on the front door.\n$SURNAME1 hammered again. It seemed to him that he heard something break inside the house\u2014a fleck of paint from the lintel, perhaps, or a sliver from the hinge of the door.\nHis heart thudded.\nHe was a lonesome young man, with a lonesome fortune.\nHe was trying to imagine what a good friend would be like. A good friend would be one who would understand about good-byes, and send a good-bye for him.\nHe formed a circle of childhood friends in his mind. But childhood friends weren\u2019t good-bye friends. They were hello friends.\nHe tightened his beefy hand on the do...", "1_12_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-eight p.m.</span> when the boy and girl, Maurice Custis and Monroe Doctrine, came out of the school-grounds of Spence School, with empty books and full stomachs, and started home, at present, 225 Riverside Drive.\nThey were in the identical state of bliss that had made their fabulous Princeton prom such a smashing success.\nAnd they were now on their way to the First Directorate of V.T.H. Industries, Fourth Floor, to meet the marvelous $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, who was going to transform their bliss into something even more fabulous.\n\"A good old-fashioned Orgy, that\u2019s what I want,\" said Monroe. \"I\u2019m sick of vege tables and water pipes. Give me sweet life, sane and garish. Give me excess! That\u2019s why I came to New York. The last four years have been a grim and ghastly mistake.\"\n\"The dark night of the soul.\" said Maurice.\n\"Soul!\" said Monroe. \"Give me kidneys!\"\nHaving dined, they were in a hurry to get down to business. The First Directorate, as every employee of V.T.H. knew, was a rabbit warren of interview rooms, where, depending on the applicant...", "1_17_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-three in the afternoon</span> and we were in the middle of nowhere. As far as the eye could see, it was plain and dry and dusty and flat. Some cactus and dry rocky hills and little else. \u201cWhere do you wanna go?\u201d I was stalling, of course, trying to figure a way to talk him out of this madness without insulting him. \u201cWell, I dunno,\u201d he said. \u201cWhere do you wanta go? What do they do in Denver? I hear they do all kinds of strange things in Denver. You ever been to Denver?\u201d\n\u201cNot since '74,\u201d I said. We spent a few minutes discussing Denver of '74, the Democratic Convention. Then I said, \u201cWell, let's go back on to Winnemucca. This is a very wonderful part of the country; why don't we see if we can't find a little food and water and then drive on to Winnemucca? There's supposed to be some Indians about here; we may be able to find some food and water in their huts...then on to Reno in the morning, OK? Come on, that's a goodman . . . we'll go on to Winnemu...", "0_11_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 entered the space ship.\nDrum did not have time to watch him. He was in no mood to say good-by to his son.\nAnd then, after the watching and the waiting, came the parting itself. It was abrupt\u2014perfunctory, hellish.\nThe green message light on the telephone panel in the Un reversalist office blinked off temporarily. It has just come on again.\nThis paragraph is not emphasized as grossly as the preceding one\u2014merely flagged. The flagging should be read as meaning that life on Earth was never quite the same, afterward. And yet there is no more personal tragedy in it than the fact that the face of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was the last face to be seen running across the field at the New St releases.\nNew St. Nick was deserted.\nThe crowd and the boats and the three great loudspeakers were gone. Grandpa and $FIRSTNAMEB2, who had not yet become Bog, were the only living things left on the wide field. The river seemed to stretch for miles, painted rug under two bridges. Above the towers of the two bridges, spring clouds were high and mean.\nIn the rocking boat, with his hand on his radio telephone, Grandpa waited for the President...", "1_9_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine nineteen in the morning</span>. The place was the Food Fair store at Forty-ninth and Gilespie in Fort Smith, Arkansas. Time of day was that pale period of dawn, late enough so you know people have been up for hours, early enough so that the light at dawn, before the sun demonstrates its firepower, is pale, white, hopeful. The walls, the fixtures, even the produce had that expectant glow, of having been dusted with hope. The white and pale green of the Food Fair logo in one corner of the ceiling acted as a kind of halo, announcing, to anyone sensitive to the touch and taste of the atmosphere, that something good was about to happen here. And something good did. At nine nineteen and twenty-seven seconds, a man, having placed his selections on the brown linoleum counter and being ready to pay, had his eyes sucked frontward by the movement of two brunette pigtails under short blue hairdos as two women approached the cash register. The man smiled involuntarily, though not malevolently, at the two women. The two women, products of the stoutness of Oklahoma, were dressed in white uniforms with modest little bows in the back. They were models of wholesome obesity that urged, nevertheless, a noble...", "0_10_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The day of battle had arrived.\nWe had coffee in the press room of the Hotel Crillon. Nothing was happening. The front page of the first edition (I was putting it to bed in one of the downstairs rooms) was complete except for a sixteenth of a inch blank space for cable news. The other news channels were already turned out.\nAnd then, right beside our table, an Italian journalist shouted: \u201cAttentato! Mafuzo! Attentato!\u201d We all scrambled to our feet.\n\u201cAttentato, \u201c he shouted again. \u201cAttentatto!\u201d\nI pushed my way through the crowd to the television set on the bar. A crazed Italian journalist was waving two wires and shouting into the face of the barman, who was blank.\n\u201cIt's happened,\u201d the Italian shouted. \u201cMafuzo was shot this morning. He was all right an hour ago, but something has happened since.\u201d The barman nodded blankly. He was completely mystified.\nThe Italian journalist handed me a copy, typed in longhand, of an Italian news service report:\nMILAN\u2014ROME: Ten minutes ago...", "1_20_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after eight o'clock in the evening</span> when Jake and me hit New York State. We made good time and arrived early. We drove into a Woolworth's store in the little town of Ashville and had a cup of coffee.\n\"Well, what do you say we go back to the country and dig the spuds in the morning?\" I said to Jake.\n\"Yeah, that sounds good to me,\" he said; \"let's go to bed right after we eat.\"\nWe went on to New York and knew we'd get a room pretty cheap. We stopped at a tobacco store at Times Square and bought cigarettes, and then went over to Central Park West and looked up some people he knew. Then we went out to eat. Everything in New York cost twice as much as it did in the West. We were a little homesick and ate at home. We had black coffee and potatoes.\nThe waitress was a sort of an Irish girl. She sat with us for a while and Jake told her about the farmers picking up sticks and about the rabbits.\n\"God, that sounds nice and quiet,\" she said. \"I'd love to go out to California and drive a car like that. I'd live out in the country.\"\n\"Well, go out there and drive...", "1_14_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>; I was sitting on my bed in my pajamas. I began to dress, quiet as I could. There seemed to be no quickest way to do it; everything was slow, the buttons, the tying of the shoelaces. I got into my overcoat, threw the summer overcoat on top of that. I didn't look up until I got to the door. At the door I looked back. I hadn't thought to leave a note, only there didn't seem to be anything to say anyway. So I just stood in the doorway, I waited, and I saw him. Sometimes you can see a person better after they're dead than when they were alive. It's because there's no pose, no bluff, so you're seeing them for the first time as some stranger who doesn't even speak your own language might see them. I saw him there on the bed, he was playing Indian, brandishing his bow and arrow under the blankets. He liked that game and wanted me to play with him, at night he would come out from under the covers with moccasined feet, he'd squat down beside me with the squinty smile, track me through the huts with his arrow. I wanted to tell him something,...", "1_13_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was a phantasm, a limbo between two eternities. God's last click of the second hand had dropped the moon out of Uranus into empty space, avoiding the edge of things by so tiny a distance that never again in all the vast erratics of time, would such a close shave be permitted.\nThe crowd surged close. \"Make more room!\" cried Salo.\nA man said, \"Sorry.\"\n\"That\u2019s all right,\" said Salo.\nThe crowd surged again. \"Sweet spirits of niter!\" cried Salo.\n\"Excuse me,\" said someone.\n\"Don\u2019t mention it, friend,\" said Salo. He rang on.\n\"Stn-n-n-ng!\" he said as the next to the last droplet fell.\nThen came the last droplet, and silence.\nThe universe was ending.\n\"Look at that mist down there,\" said a member of the $SURNAME0 staff, far away. \"It\u2019s like a beautiful smear of cotton candy.\"\n\"Same stuff the Sun\u2019s going to be throwing at us in a minute,\" said someone else.\n\"Sun?\" said Salo.", "2_21_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-seven in the evening</span>.\n\u201cI did not get anything out of that at all.\u201d\n\u201cMe not, either.\u201d\n\u201cJesus Christ.\u201d\n/b/\nPARTIAL TRANSCRIPT OF \u201cTHE PHIL DONAHUE SHOW,\u201d TELECAST 29 AUGUST 1990 PARTIAL TRANSCRIPT OF \u201cTHE PHIL DONAHUE SHOW,\u201d TELECAST 29 AUGUST 1990.\nPHIL DONAHUE: Well now let me ask you this. There\u2019s been a lot in the wash about an epidemic of bulimia among American girls between the ages of sixteen and twenty. How does the saga of Food Serving Devices, Impressive Soundman, and Lenore Beadsman relate to the phenomenon of bulimia in the under thirty-market?", "1_2_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-five in the morning</span>. By now, no doubt, Remi Boncoeur was far out over the Pacific, between the Coast and Hawaii, on the last leg of his journey. Somewhere, in Seattle, I had dropped him off at one of those mysterious offices where a bus meets a plane. No questions asked, no tickets required. He carried no luggage but his briefcase, in which I knew there was nothing---except one Belgian tricolor. When he got on the bus there was a group of sailors, and one of them, a big tall novice, sat down next to the Frenchman, evidently under the impression Remi was a girl. None of the sailors could speak a word of French. There they sat, the big fellow with a fatuous grin on his face, waiting casually, but in a hurry, to make a pass. Except, of course, there was no pass to be made.\nI wanted to tell Remi all these things, as he stood inside the screen door, phosphorescent in the darkness, Idaho talking to some invisible ghost in the potato patch, whacking furiously with his hoe.\n\u201cI'll send you money from Denver,\u201d I promised Remi.\n\u201cDon't waste it.\u201d As far as...", "1_12_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past noon</span> when my attorney finally lost control of the Owsley he had been running on for 48 hours straight. He was already pretty far out when he stumbled into the Carousel Ballroom for the Acid Test. We had spent all morning checking out of the Lucky Seven and moving our stuff into the room next to Baker. I had moved most of my stuff into my attorney\u2019s room, to give him somewhere to lie down when the hall door slammed open and two enormous Samoan brickmen arched into the doorway.\n\u201cWhere\u2019s Shark?\u201d they asked. \u201cWe want to talk to Shark so bad.\u201d My attorney smiled and waved them down on the bed. They sank like cathedrals. \u201cWe want to talk to Shark,\u201c they intoned again. My attorney had taken an entire jar of orange sunshine with him to the Test. Now, as the Samoans talked, he got up and went over to the table, where he proceeded to shoot up about half of what was left in the jar. I watched him through the rush of blood in my head. Well, he had gone almost 48 hours now; he had gone a lot farther than most people could have . . . .\n\u201c", "2_5_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>. I stood by the window, watching the distant orange flirt of Las Vegas.\n\u201cDid you like that ride on the poodles?\u201d said Lucy, laughing.\n\u201cUmmm . . .\u201d I grunted. \u201cUrn . . . What\u2019s the time?\u201d I asked. The problem was food. We desperately needed some, lest we be impotent for the marathon session ahead. And yet I didn\u2019t want to move; I was afraid that I might stimulate action and cut in on some fine private inner dialogue of Lucy's\u2014or even spoil a delicate thought process by saying, \u201cLet\u2019s go downstairs and get some of that garbage they call food at all Vegas buffets.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s almost time we should be getting down there anyhow,\u201d I said.\n\u201cHuh?\u201d She was sitting on the bed, facing the TV set\u2014watching wrestling. There was no way to tell what she was thinking.", "2_17_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe cathedral doors were opened. Only members of the true ecclesiastical faith were admitted to the ceremonies. The members, like all other true believers, were blind. They wore black hoods that covered their faces, tapers that burned in their right hands, and cotton bags that covered their feet and legs.\nThere were other ceremonies that were not concealed from the eyes of unbelievers\u2014the building of the cathedrals on Mercury and Mars, and the sending of space ships to build cathedrals on the eight other planets. These three ceremonies were the high points of the life histories of the twelve richest members of the true ecclesiastical faith. They were all old men with the vision and the energy of men half their ages.\nAll twelve were named Barnhouse, for their common ancestor, who had founded the commonwealth and religion in 1776. They were elected, not dynastically, but for proven intellectual and moral leadership, for balance and decency, for fearlessness and modesty, and for having common sense.", "1_15_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The group next to me were yelling for somebody in particular to come on out and drink with them. I realized the hobos were crying out for their chief. They were calling for \u201cColin.\u201d Just then somebody yelled out the dreadful word that meant Colin was dead: \u201cHe's croaked!\u201d And nobody was sadder about it than Igor and the gang; they weren't putting on any show; their tears were real. They had their local Christ organized and their mourners around the cross and now they were really doing the tepee, they were making their bread and circe and whatever came to hand out of straw, with wisps of straw stuck to their clothes and sweat; they were really there and they really wept. Then they sat around and talked or said nothing, or sang old rock and roll songs. But though nobody roared any more nobody cried any less; they were just as ghostly, just as mad. I imagined they would be this way all their lives, moving from place to place, going around and around the world like their president and looking for something. There was no doubt that cat, his name was President; that was clear from all the respects he got and his absence of caste worries. He...", "2_11_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"That\u2019s good,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. He had his skullcap in his hand, had pulled it off. It was green wool knit, and completely empty. No hair! \"Who wants to be a process server?\" he said. \"A chimp like me.\" He opened the door widely. \"One happy Universe,\" he said. \"Exactly one Universe, and me in it.\" He closed the door again. \"You know why I\u2019ve got to stay?\" he said. \"I\u2019ll tell you. Somebody\u2019s got to go back, tell them what happened.\"\n\"Get out of here,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"You tell them what happened?\"\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"How long have you been back?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Ten minutes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"I was about to go out, start calling myself a lawyer again, when I heard all the shooting. Well, that confirmed it. I thought I\u2019d seen the last of all this when I was a chimp. I\u2019ve got to go back.\"\n\"Great,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Amnesium on your bicycle wheel? Space suit?\"", "1_16_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF0 came out of the market, loaded with groceries. She stopped and regarded the crowds curiously. She could not make out what was going on.\nA loudspeaker box somewhere on the scene began suddenly to bellow forth the news\u2014and none were more surprised than $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 to find themselves suddenly hot new figures in the stockyards\u2019 drama.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 stood on tiptoe before the crowd, both hands on the choke-chain around $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s neck.\n\"Ladies and gentlemen,\" said the announcer, \"ladies and gentlemen\u2014\" And the fact that he was about to say something put the audience on tiptoe too. He enunciated carefully, for the benefit of the Western allies or for the benefit of Europe, which makes many requests for records transcribed verbatim. \"Ladies and gentlemen\u2014we have here the man responsible for three quarters of the war and all its destruction\u2014$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, a master of space, a master of time, and, may I say to you, sir, a cheap huckster and mountebank.\n\"In this dog,\" said the announcer, to gales of laughter...", "1_10_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-three in the morning</span> of this black equinox day; in the dark; the rainy season had started. I sat there shivering and waiting -- with $FIRSTNAMEB3's manuscript of Wild yea i Bacchus under my arm -- for $FIRSTNAMEF4 to wake up. Everything had broken down on me at once. The Rambles had fallen through; Ginsberg wouldn't put me up; Carlo, who was my boy friend, had suddenly fallen half asunder at dawn after a night of girls at a party, and his Moms wouldn't let me in the house any more unless I had a job. Yes, it was Molly. All dewy and sweet, jumping out of bed and mussing my hair in the dawn, giving me tea and saying I was dirty, and the radio said in Louisville, Kentucky, eighty citizens were arrested in a pitched battle that lasted all day long. They had taken over city hall. Louisville was rising. Hitch-hike there quick. Then she stood in the door, smiling and straightening her hair, and said I could no longer sleep in her bed every night anyhow and go off in the day. ... Yes, Carlo was in the hospital with tuberculosis, and he sent me sad postcards, I'll write your name on every brick...", "0_11_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-three in the morning</span>, when we batted the false eyelashes of our Cadillac clansmen up alongside the oasis. Several songpatros fleetingly appeared, skittering away to disappear behind huts when they saw our weaponry. We shot at everything that moved or looked like it might: barrels, soft fruits, hut awnings, abandoned boats. We blasted the lily pads off the lake and sent a panic of ducks near-north for Mexico. We wiped out a swarm of songpatro kids playing soccer with a pre-Columbian ball of bark and vine fiber. Rewarded with magnificent shots at the fleeing nap-suited kids and their vacant-faced, shirtclothed parents, we blew every one skyhigh. The homes rained bamboo splinters and thatch. The jungle caulked the wounds with incredible rapidity. Amid the carnage of exploded ordnance, grape surf. The first thirteen songs of the acid trip were peace signs pointing at nothing in particular and meaning everything that was good and beautiful in the world. They were magnificent, but we could only hum them, tripping too hard to sing. We had embarked on a most ex- quisite journey, and we were out to enjoy every wrinkle. Every minute was scripted; there was no use in...", "0_14_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Time had passed.\nOne minute before two o'clock in the afternoon. One minute, fifty-nine seconds, thirty-three milliseconds.\nOne minute before two o'clock in the afternoon.\nOne minute, fifty-nine seconds, thirty-four milliseconds.\nOne minute before two o'clock in the afternoon.\nOne minute, fifty-nine seconds, thirty-five milliseconds.\nOne minute before two o'clock in the afternoon.\nOne minute, fifty-nine seconds, thirty-six milliseconds.\nOne minute before two o\u2019clock in the afternoon of the second day of July A.D. 1944.\nOut of the east had come the darkness, ripping out the light from all things.\nA rocket had whistled up from somewhere beyond the river, going west.\nNow station KILP, the radio station, spoke: \"L-ladies and gentlemen, listeners throughout the Midwest\u2014free and unlimited commercial testimony, borne by a witness of integrity and intelligence, by a man who has put his money where his mouth is and proved his words. See him\u2014the great magician who, by his will, has\u2014as has never been done before\u2014bound together all the nations of Earth in peace, who...", "2_4_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt ten minutes past four o'clock, there was not much left of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s harmonium. It had all dribbled or dripped away into space\u2014leaving the deck bare.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was in a coma.\nThe harmonium had delicious sleep-inducing powers.\nWith $FIRSTNAMEB0 in a coma came an end of navigational worries. $FIRSTNAMEB0 could no longer worry about going the wrong way. $FIRSTNAMEB0 could no longer worry that his worrying might harm someone.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s breathing was deliberate, mechanical, resonant. No living thing, finding itself in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s lungs, would have misliked the experience. It would have found not razor blades, not broken bottles, not worms, not fishhooks. It would have found only a luxurious mucus.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt woefully sick and empty.\nBut there was no more stifling.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 noticed, without particular interest, that the harmonium had disappeared from the deck. A good deal of it had gone into $FIRSTNAMEB0, dribbling down his jugulars, dripping in glutinous drops from his carotids.", "0_7_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> when Salo lay flat on his back beneath the Tralfamadorian stranger's ship, unscrewed the valve beneath the ship's lowest point, and popped the flat, time-worn, and much-mended Tralfamadorian messenger out of his own birth-canal on Titan.\nBy coincidence, it was just seven o'clock on Coast Road, Newport, Rhode Island, and all the creatures in the $SURNAME0 estate were in their lairs, preparing to summon and receive a miracle. As far as anyone knew, the miracle would be Skip with his wife and son\u2014a materialization and dematerialization, the details of which had never been quite clear. The course of the materialization and dematerialization would be from somewhere in space about fifty miles above Earth, to somewhere in the $SURNAME0 estate.\nAccording to an extremely irregular schedule, $SURNAME0 would broadcast signals to Earth and his wife and son would answer. This time, the signals had been broadcast at two o\u2019clock in the morning Greenwich Mean Time, which was eight o\u2019clock in the morning Pacific Standard Time, and in Newport the signals were still being answered punctually. The good-by, good-by, good-by of...", "2_7_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> on Titan, Saturn\u2019s large moon.\nThere was a diorama in one corner of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s room. It was a poor thing, composed of a few pebbles, with cardboard cut-outs to make the beautiful buildings. It was meant to depict the wonders of Tralfamadore.\nIts inscription read: \"This is Tralfamadore. This is all there is of Tralfamadore. I know what this makes you feel like, because I used to feel that way, too. Your friend, $SURNAME1.\"\nThe cut-outs were crude\u2014but moving among them, describing elliptical and parabolic paths, moved a golden ball bearing, three-eighths of an inch in diameter. It stopped between the cut-outs of a grand city fa\u00e7ade and a palace. It sparkled so brilliantly against the background of the dark and obscure Saturnian moon that it might have been a twinkling star.\nThere was a wall switch beside the door to the outside. $FIRSTNAMEB2 threw it. The zigzagging golden ball bearing froze solid.", "0_9_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt three-thirty in the morning we had left San Francisco in Tim Gray's nine hundred and forty horsepower sports Continental, painted pale green and pulled by four new matching teal-blue plates. The plates read \u201cGold Medalist\u201d on one side and \u201cWinner of the 1947 Monterey Race\u201d on the other. Tim Gray is a rail-thin, handsome young man of thirty-two who runs away from home every year around November fifteenth and leads a suds-and-somersault bachelor existence until spring. He has a great Continental kitsch, like Hemingway and Edna Millay; he has raced that car all over the U.S.; there was snow in Pennsylvania when we left and it was thirty-two below in Montana when we got there. The Old Homestead in Missoula was chic and Western. The pay-off bar was in a big back room with bay windows; across the street were suds signs and Western heritages. All around were huge timbers and great gaps in the night where mountain men had recently shot it up. After a few hours' sleep, I rolled in at breakfast in a red and black hounds-tooth suit that I bought in a tax...", "1_7_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> when they got to the airport. The terminal there is small and cramped, and none of the employees seemed in any way prepared to deal with junkies. He wanted to exchange his T-Bird for a Chevy station wagon with air brakes, but they accused him of being a drug addict, and refused to do the exchange.\nThey sent him away. \u201cThis is the end of our fucked up relationship!\u201d said my attorney. \u201cI never want to set eyes on this piece of shit town again!\"\n\u201cForget the town,\u201d I said. \u201cAll you want to think about is that new Chevy!\"\n\u201cWhat are we going to do about the kid?\" said my attorney.\nI shrugged. \u201cWhat kid?\" ?/\u201cYou bastard! Don\u2019t you remember?'?\n\u201cNo.\u201d It was very important for me to grasp the flow of things\u2014to make sure I remembered everything in proper sequence. \u201cI think we left him under a palm tree,\" I said.\n\u201cDidn\u2019t somebody say he was croaked?\u201d\nI nodded. \u201cThat\u2019s right. He croaked on us.\u201d I flipped...", "2_6_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>. \u201cPrecisely 71 hours since God's tripartite warning,\u201d thought $FIRSTNAMEF2, \u201cand here it comes.\u201d The panic she'd kept held in check all night long Now would have to do its stuff. The San Andreas had its big moment, shifted and grumbled Right on schedule, and everybody felt safer. What they had seen, it turned out, was the classic swindle: The great bad moment. The sequence would be repeated at two-hour intervals, but this was the big one, the one they\u2019d be talking about for months. It had no dignity, only repetition.", "1_20_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-six in the evening</span>. The place was the apartment of Miss Lucy A. Ford, a maiden lady referred to in the WV narrative as Miss Ford, friend of the family and $FIRSTNAMEB3\u2019s aunt. The location was North Capitol Street, only a block from the location of the scuffle with bitter Tim. Pan now fastened his jeans. In the bathroom, $FIRSTNAMEB3 investigated himself in the mirror and said, \u201cAlex, I'm afraid you're right.\u201d Then he said, \u201cWell, what's so damn bad?\u201d\n\u201cIt's no longer the Hudson.\u201d I nodded glumly. We went out and got in my bug and $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo went to dig Stan and the girl.\nNight driving back east across the country in my old car with the fever-compass of the night reflecting my interior moon across America I saw many things right and proper. My first stop was long in coming -- Detroit, that huge rumbling argument that settles nothing. From there I struck due east to Port Huron and Mackinaw and south to Louisville, Louisville and Lexington, Ashland and Nashville, Hot Springs and St. Louis, Des Moines and on to Cheyenne late in the night.\n$FIRSTNAMEB3 and Mary...", "0_22_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-two in the evening</span>. I had just turned twenty-one. We were sitting in Remi's bar, somewhere around North Capitol and I. Remi's was a brand-new bar on a brand-new corner of that brand-new administration corridor downtown. The whole street was a freak. Every building was new and raw, except for the National Theater, where I worked summers as usher, year in and year out, from the time I was seventeen or so.\nthe theater was the only constant in that street. The rest of it was always being dug up, or being torn down and rebuilt, or being torn down and put up and taken down again. In recent years they'd tried to liven up the street with a water fountain and a circular stone bench, but nothing seemed to work. People just didn't seem to harmonize with the area. When the afternoon sun beame too hot for comfort, the office drones dispersed and the street becam flat and dull, lifeless, like a corpse. In the evening it came to ugly, frightful life. At night you could smell the fear, like oil and metal, rising from the crowds that came out to see Singin' Sam and his boys play their intricate rituals of jazz. The crowds were always bad, two...", "1_2_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. The material things sufficient to establish identity and a chronology were intact. A pass key unlocked the door of a broom closet that served as a combination bedroom and study for Cam Holland. It was a room six feet deep and eight feet wide. The head of the bed was by the door. The desk was against the wall under the window. Under the desk, in a small box, were some steel drafts of ads for which Cam Holland had been hired to write the copy. The ads were for an imaginary product called Rocket Foam. The ads were good.\nThe desk was a coat tree, a wastebasket, a makeshift tool and gadget rack, a magnetic holder for worry beads, unanswered correspondence, unpaid bills, a box of wooden type for Cam's homemade typesetting machine, and a receptacle for mail to be opened when social obligation dictated a response of more than a word and nod.\nThe desk was a disguised water dragon given to Cam by a client back when Cam did work as a psychiatric public-relations specialist. A Royal typewriter stood on the water dragon. Underneath the typewriter was a sheaf of paper containing one finished love letter to Frost, three drafts of a love letter to Lenore, and a detailed outline for the first half...", "0_18_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-five in the evening</span> of Wednesday, October 1, 2005, and the place was a luncheonette in Union, New Jersey\u2014that palmy borough neighborhood on the fringes of Newark where I used to drink. The luncheonette adjoined an ancient cinema, where I had come to see an exhibition of short cartoons by the legendary New Yorker cartoonist James Thurber that was being shown as part of the annual series of one-week retrospectives of Cinemascope pictures presented by works of American literary art. The cartoon film was in color. Its title was My World and Welcome to It, and, although the program cited as its dates 1952\u201456, it had also been copyrighted in 1969, which fitted in with the wry note I got from the film as a whole. The cartoons were cartoons, just as the film was a film and the time was time and my position was taken and fixed as a window buyer of space in a firm of media sales agents in Manhattan, a fate I do not hold with any particular terror in view of my rather keen awareness of the really apt appropriateness of everything about my position and fate. My name is Evelyn Wingrate. I was born April 17, 1943, in Washington, D.C, where my father was a statistician...", "0_20_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-eight in the evening</span>, Pacific standard time, of the last day of October of the year that everything fell apart. Pacific Standard was a mistake, a hangover. $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas, aged thirty, was wearing Chinese-red slacks, black Taiwan heels with mother-of-pearl\u4e0a\u53e4\u6342\u98fe, a white blouse with a symbol in a graphite gray \u683c\u5b50: a WASTE disposal bin, curbside, with a Society\u2019s emblem of a knight on a grailed horse, and in her hair, brought from a beauty parlor an hour ago, a black orchid arrangement.\nIn the front vestibule of her apartment building, a black, red and gold Arrow Shirtless sportscar with a man in it was parked. $FIRSTNAMEF2 walked past it and into the building, looking neither right nor left. At the elevator door she pressed a No bell and heard it chime softly in the basement. The hallway door had to be locked for the elevator to move at all. Belowstairs a nosy neighbor could make life difficult, by waiting matters out; ringing all the upstairs bells with various excuses; claiming illness, accidents, once even an armed assault with sinister overtones that could eventually land...", "2_0_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve nineteen a.m.</span> \u201cWhere is it?\u201d I asked the operator. \u201cWhere\u2019s my call?\u201d I was standing next to the phone, waiting. There was no response.\n\u201cHello?\u201d I said. \u201cWhere is it? I\u2019m waiting.\"\n\u201cWhat?\" She seemed annoyed. \u201cOh, yes...your call? I have it right here, sir. It\u2019s a call from New York, some kind of a ham place, I think.\"\n\u201cCalls from New York all get routed through Pittsburgh,\" I said. \u201cGo ahead and ring it for me.\"\nThe operator tried. She rang the call. There was no answer because at that precise moment there was absolutely nobody home at the news bureau of the American Nazi Party in Queens, N. Y. Just an ordinary wiretapped phone with an answering machine plugged into the wall. My call had been made from a neighbor\u2019s phone booth. I called to cancel it, but there was no answer. Then I hung up and took off for the airport.\n4. The Milli Vigili...and a Bad Scene With Some Bones in the Trunk of the Car", "1_20_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 came to live in the caves under Mars. He came, unconscious and under guard, up the iron ladder from the shell hole in which he had awakened.\nHe came into a wide, low, brightly-lighted hall that was lined with hospital beds. The beds were pressed tightly together, and a waxing, waning congestion of wounded men lay in them. There were wounded men everywhere. The tramp of incoming wounded men could be heard coming from below.\nThere was no order, no discipline, no regularity to the place. Bat-faced old ladies in bloody shirtsleeves bustled here and bustled there. One of the bustlers, a tall, lean old bird with iron-gray hair and a man\u2019s gray soldier suit pushed her way importantly through the clustering nurses and came to attention before a short, fat, brass-buttoned sergeant with a head as smooth and hairless as an orange.\n\"Seven hundred and forty-seven, Sergeant,\" she said crisply.\nThe sergeant counted button holes blool freely. \"Seven hundred and forty-six,\" he said crisply. \"Anybody miss one?\"\n\"742 is still in the post-op, Sergeant,\" said the tall woman.", "0_9_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-nine in the morning</span> of the first day of 1969 in legal calendars and calendars outside the law, and $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas had little more than an hour to go before her appointment on the pier. She was carrying a scented envelope with a red postmark and thinking how she might enlarge her ignorance. Arriving at the post office, she entered, among the intimate sounds of stamps licked, letters being slit open with pens and envelopes murmured shut again, a silence that belonged to her, that waited for her and would expand to fill any shape she chose to give it. She chose the shape of an absence: to say nothing, ask no questions, seek no answers. The task assigned her by the king of Thailand left her no way to receive mail here, had she been so disposed. She walked down a street she always chose to think of as quaint, with small clean false-fronted shops and windows full of sea glass and salvaged junk, and as she walked she transformed it, as she was capable of doing simply by walking through a certain neighborhood or along a certain shore, into another city entirely, old and bleak and vanished, with canals and warehouses and lights coming on among oil storage tanks, formed out of all the San Francisco\u2019s her imagination...", "1_16_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-six in the afternoon</span> when the Bell captain began picking up the sound of forced air\u2014moving things that were either motorized or, incredibly, human\u2014distributing them in a spiral line inside the red zone, fifty feet off the ground. The spiral arms all converged on a blue herringbone twill tent and a gaily striped wool canopy just inside the subdivision fence, near what looked like a culvert.\nThings crawled under the canopy, trumpeting and squeaking. The canopy kept flapping in a breeze that had nothing to do with the heatsink atmosphere. Back behind the encampment, inside the culvert, which was just a big pipe, things were dramatically rac ing and banging together.\nThe roar was almost unbearable.\nThe Bell created its own landing pad, floated ten feet above the canopy. The Martian lurched out, dressed differently now, no more ranch foreman but an extremely deranged-looking adult, with beard and finger cymbals and a big iron pot on his head.\nHe led crowd noises at the helicopter the way the crowd noises had been led at him at the Martian space ship.\nAlvin retched tremendously. \"I can\u2019t go on with this,\" he said. Surely the worst torture known to man was...", "1_11_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, just about fifteen hours since the stampede into space from $FIRSTNAMEB1. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had been in the space ship for about one hour, having forced their way into it at gunpoint after killing the three custodians.\nOminous, bats inside the ship!\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 believed the bats to be a bad omen.\nA voice from a loudspeaker suddenly stilled the watchman and the old have-nots. \"You out there\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1!\" said the voice from the loudspeaker. \"This is the undersigned CO. You are under orders to hold your present positions. Do not attempt to return to the installation. Do not use force to gain entry to the space ship.\n\"You are hereby ordered\u2014\" said the voice, then named fifteen other things, closing with \"$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Director, Company A, Peacekeeping Forces.\"\nThe loudspeaker clicked off.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 frowned. \"Who done that?\" he said.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had never heard the voice before. He liked its voice, though, and he liked the words it had said.\n\"Who\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1?\" he asked.\n\"I dunno,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"A...", "2_13_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The thing that happened at half past one o\u2019clock in the afternoon was this. A space ship with lawn seats under its wings drifted down to the main building of the army encampment. The ship was grey, and in gold letters below the cockpit it said GODspeed YOU.\nIt was an execution ship. The new recruits were on it.\nThe pilot cut off the engines, opened the cabin door. He handed out the new recruits.\nThere were thirty recruits.\nThey were all that was left of the two thousand new recruits that had landed from the flying saucers. The rest had deserted.\nTheir wrists and ankles were shackled, and they were dressed in new, white, full-length ionization suits.\nThe path up from the landing place to the main building was carpeted in black velvet. And on the black velvet, the heat-of-flight pink footprints of the new recruits were clearly visible.\nThe recruits had an armed guard of ten old soldiers, who wore their full field-artillery uniforms, complete with plumed caps.\nThe recruits entered the main building, paused at the foot of an extensive flight of marble stairs. They lingered there a moment before mounting the stairs.", "1_14_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two sixteen in the afternoon</span>. I felt nothing but contempt for the two taxicab drivers, who were watching with smug smiles of voyeurism as this man descended into the terrible depths of addiction. \u201cActually,\u201d I said, \u201cI think this gentleman requires psychiatric attention. Would you be good enough to call a doctor?\u201d Before they could say anything, I whipped a twenty out of my wallet and snapped it in the air; the nearest cabman took off on a dead run for the Hard Rock Caf\u00e9. The other one kicked Gabriel furiously in the ribs. He cried out in pain.\n\u201cNow you quit that!\u201d I shouted. \u201cWhat kind of people are you, kicking a man when he\u2019s down? Call yourself taxi drivers?\"\n\u201cHey look,\u201d he said, \u201cit ain\u2019t my fault the guy\u2019s gotta drug habit. Shit, I don\u2019t get no extra for this.\"\n\u201cWell I\u2019ll tell you one thing,\u201d I said, \u201cyou guys better keep outa here. I\u2019m calling the police.\"\n\u201cFUCK THAT!\u201d screeched the man in the white suit. \u201cIt...", "0_3_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-nine in the morning</span> and it so happened, this night in particular, that Can-D, having only just come off patent after one hundred thirty years of legal restriction, was being pushed big as a kind of monster high\u2014yes, but only temporarily high\u2014by its pushers.\nVice-President Richard M. Nixon was in Denver, on his way home from Peking, for a rest before the campaign started in earnest. He had climbed Pike\u2019s Peak in a closed limousine, he had mixed with the tourists in the Tampico Gift Shop, he had taken a dip in barely tepid Clear Creek, and now, thank God, he was ready to hit the sack. But just as he was settling into his chaste bachelor bed in his bare, Spartan bedroom suite in the Westwood Hotel, he felt this itchy thing come on: an uncontrollable physical urge to call somebody on the telephone. Just as Bea had once predicted in one of those forgotten future passages when they were first married and he was an up-and-coming junior senator from some forgettable Midwestern state. \"You\u2019re going to get mixed up in this hades drug scene before we\u2019re done\u2014you\u2019ve got the scratch disease.\" But...", "2_12_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, post meridiem.\nThe place was the long room behind the village school, post meridiem. The place was swaddled in tender green vines and flowered with orchids and jungled with ferns, post meridiem.\nAll the people out of all the houses in Newport were congregated in the long room behind the school, post meridiem. And all of those people, post meridiem, were happy and playful, and dressed in costumes and masks and insignia, post meridiem. And the reason all those people were happy and playful and costumed and masquerading was because they didn\u2019t have to worry, post meridiem.\nThey didn\u2019t have to worry about saying the right thing or doing the right thing. They didn\u2019t have to worry about having to think. They didn\u2019t, post meridiem, have to think about the hundreds of thousands of things that, ordinarily, they would have had to think about, post meridiem.", "2_14_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, with his iron pot on his head again, again stood before the iron curtain. The banner of the church to which he had been conformed had unrolled behind him again.\nThe meaning of the banner was that to members of the religious order to which $FIRSTNAMEB0 belonged nothing on Earth was secret, or mysterious, or impenetrable. The God of the order was as familiar with the configuration of a sewing-needle as he was with the configuration of the Solar System.\nThere was a thud, a scraping. $FIRSTNAMEB0 again turned to see what materialization might this time be taking place. What materialized was a big iron door with two eyes, a nose, and a mouth painted on it.\nThe mouth spoke.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0,\" it said.\nIt was $SURNAME0\u2019s speaking trumpet again.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 again dropped to one knee and pressed his iron pot against his ear. The volume was again terrific.\n\"If any of your friends hate you, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice in the iron pot, \"show them this trumpet and tell them what it says.\"\n\"Thanks a lot,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 dryly.", "0_14_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-eight in the afternoon</span> of a day in November of 1991. The place was room 1129 of the Mark Twain Dental School of Duke University, Durham, North Carolina. The season was early autumn.\nThe space was a world away from the planet Tralfamadore in the constellation of Orion. The space was the interior of a womb-mi the interior of a cocoon.\nThe space was a womb without the possibility of warmth.\nThe space was a nest that was absolutely without protection from any unpleasant thing that might happen in the outside world.\nThe only activity generated by the life inside the cluster of blankets was the weak trembling of age- wrinkled hands and a flutter of eyelids. The hand trembling was a bad sign. Eyes fluttering meant that the eyes themselves were cold and heavy, and that the lids were trying desperately to hold the sleep in.\nThe blanket cluster was resting on a bench. The bench was painted green. Painted on the wall behind the bench was some advice in gold letters. The advice was written in gold to obviate any suspicion that paint might somehow be swallowed. The advice was written gold so that it might be easily licked off if it should accidentally be digested. The advice was: USE SPIT COADE...", "0_20_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-four in the evening</span>. True to Hilarius\u2019s word, the city had materialized almost immediately around them. It was a big desolate city. $SURNAME1 had the impression that the air was foggy.\nHe assumed, now, that the skeleton he had seen was that of the dead Martian traveler. But he asked, \"What happened to him?\"\n\"She, \" said Hilarius. \"He\u2014she lived eight hundred and twenty years. The third-longest lifespan on record, we think.\"\n\"That\u2019s what I meant, \" said $SURNAME1.\nHilarius pointed to the dark hole dug by the elapsed-time measuring instrument. \"The room where she lived, where we came in, you saw, was only one of a few rooms she had,\" he said. \"All the rooms were dark, so she wouldn\u2019t hurt her light-sensitive eyes. She liked to see the sun, it\u2019s true, and she would go to the top of a hill over there and sit quietly when it rose and set. For the rest of the day, however, she was a shut-in. And when she lay down to sleep at night, she always put out the lamp by her bed. She had a mechanical...", "0_15_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-eight in the afternoon</span> of April twenty-first when $FIRSTNAMEB2 left the Newport Casino in his beautiful television-prediction machine. He saw his Mansion, his expensive mistresses, and all his good stewardship and felt a certain pity for all humbuggery in the universe. He felt like a little boy who has run away from home and stands for a moment on the burning main street of a small community, looking with scorn at the well-known church, the humble grammar school, the boxy police station and post office, the little general store, the firehouse, and the quaint houses of the poor.\nOf course it was all nice\u2014very nice indeed for the people who lived there.\nExcept for their fates.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was a businessman and knew what Fate was. If a company showed a nice little net that year, it had been a lucky year. If it showed a lousy net, it had been a very unlucky year. It didn\u2019t matter how much skill had been in running the company, how honest the procurement agents were, how wise the bookkeepers had been, how loyal the advertising agencies, how economical the administration\u2014neither these factors nor a dozen like them could influence a company\u2019s fate in a bad year any more than...", "0_1_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-seven in the morning</span>. For something like four hours the five partners had sat around the kitchen table in silence. The only other light in the room came from a candle burning on the windowsill. That and the flicker of the card game they were playing lent the room an underwater pallor. Except for the clicking of the Jefferson nickel that young Di Presso was pitching, over and over, into the porcelain disposal unit under the sink.\nEverything went bang. Had there been anyone there to hear it, he might have described the sound as that of a large door striking the back of a refrigerator.\nNow it was one fifty-two in the morning. None of the partners was thinking about the outside of the building, because the inside of the building was holding their full attention. And what was going on inside the building was a large bang in the basement just south of the machine shop, followed instantly by a water pipe\u2019s abrupt rupture, a gush of steam, another bang and the screaming of a badly overheated motor.\n\"Hey, Gene?\" said young Di Presso. He was looking out the window into the backyard. The window was perhaps eight inches above the level of the lawn. The lawn, that is, the part of it that the partners...", "0_12_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was noon</span>. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s curio shop on the Martian moon of Phobos. The Phocaean dealer was serving a grave and gentle Earthling customer, a chrono-synclastic infundibulated old gentleman who was browsing among Martian water-sculpture and moon battles in Mexican ceramics.\nThe visitor paused before a dusty stone $SURNAME0 fountain that had only one sculpture, an unimportant one, of a fish.\n\"The fish I am speaking of, \" said the visitor, \"is barely four inches long, an Arctic char. It is carved out of an almost colorless green mineral.\"\n\"You are speaking of the legendary Skug,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, peering into the stone fountain.\n\"Ah\u2014you know the fish of which I speak!\" said the visitor.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s face was a sunrise of glee. \"And you\u2014you are Wilbur!\" he said. \"I have so looked forward to meeting you!\"\n\"Dug,\" said the visitor. \"Dug, please.\" His right hand was in his pocket. He was making nervous coiling and uncoiling motions with it. The pocket was made of transmuted polar bear skin,...", "2_3_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three sixteen in the morning</span>.\n\"Don\u2019t you wish you were a child again?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"Why?\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"If you were a child again, you wouldn\u2019t have to bear these heavy burdens of adulthood.\"\n\"Who told you I don\u2019t want to bear these heavy burdens?\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4. \"In fact\u2014who told you I don\u2019t have time to play?\"\n\"What do you mean?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"I\u2019ve got no time to play,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4. \"I play serious.\"\n\"Is that a quotation?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"No,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4. \"I\u2019m making it to fit the situation.\"\nThe time was three twenty-six in the morning.\n\"I praise the Lord,\" said the Supreme Pontiff, \"that at least you are pure in one way.\"\n\"What way is that?\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"Pure in spirit,\" said the Supreme Pontiff. \"Without guile.\"\n\"How does that follow?\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.", "1_8_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-seven in the morning</span>. The place was a neighborhood on a hill in Newport, an hour\u2019s walk from the offices of Stonecipheco, on the front side of the same hill. Most likely, the neighborhood was included in Stockholders\u2019 Reports as \u201cProjected Neighborhood F. Near the fence of the walk the high gray stones of a \u201cNew house protruded here and there above a mat of Cape Cod \u2019s and valleys. Between the bases of the houses were groups of bare trees and white, skeletal saplings. It seemed to Mrs. Dunmire as though the trees were living by their own last strength, growing defiantly in sand.\nMany of the back yards were enclosed by high board fences. In many more there were small areas that were once lawns, but were now either gravel or scraped earth. At two spots along the fence were small piles of what could have been infant corpses, but were only sections of telephone poles.\nThe only exposed house on the street was occupied by a short man in a red and black plaid jacket, who was doing his driveway with a gasoline power-sprayer. With his free hand he was slapping the jacket of his plaid trousers.\n\u201cGoing to be a scorcher,...", "1_14_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-seven in the afternoon</span> of October what would be 25, in the town of Hambly, in the state of California, Earth.\nThere were no windows opening on to the sky. The ceiling was so high overhead that it was impossible to take the scale of that ceiling and relate it to the scale of a human body. The light came from banks of immense spotlights mounted in black caverns high in the ceiling.\nThere was no sculpture in a thing like Room 223. There was nothing to sit on. There were no windows to look out of. The only drawing in the room was a chalk drawing on the blackboard on the front of the room.\nThe chalk drawing on the blackboard on the front of Room 223 was a drawing of the Solar System. Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Luna, the asteroids, Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Juno, Vesta, and all the other minor bodies were there, with their correct projections. Pluto was there, too, but minus its correct projection. The tunnel from Neptune to Pluto, and from Pluto to the umbrella-point that marked the terrors of interstellar space, was not there. It could be deduced, however, by anyone who cared to deduce it.\nFor the rest, Room 223 looked...", "0_12_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-one post meridiem</span> on this second which would, if things went correctly, be the last day of the war. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was lined up with fifty other men, on a spit of land in the Yellow Sea. The spit was the island ofTSu Shih Poo\u2014a piece of territory so worthless that neither Chinese nor American troops had bothered to take it.\nThe Korean shore was a thousand yards away, beyond the mouth of the Han. Russian troops were supposed to be on the farther shore. $SURNAME1 did not know what color their uniforms were, nor did he see very many of them anyway, in this dawnlight. He saw only the sunlight on the water between.\nAll the men with $SURNAME1 stiffened to attention as a jeep bounced over the mouth of the inlet, onto the spit. Two officers got out of the jeep, and every man saluted. The salute was not instantly returned, for the officers were saluting the wrong flag. They were saluting the American flag which floated, khaki and limp, over $SURNAME1\u2019s head.\nOne of the officers in this undefended, unguarded post of honor was a sweet-looking lieutenant-colonel from Arkansas. His features were so homely as to be beautiful, and home...", "0_19_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-three in the evening</span> and I was alone on the boulevard du Montparnasse. Across the street I could see a line of people waiting outside the Select, the caf\u00e9 where the important expatriate writers had their tu.e. I went over and joined the line. When the doors opened I went inside and found a seat at a table by the window. During the entire time I was in the caf\u00e9 I never looked away from the street. It was my favorite street in the world and it belonged to me. It was nothing like it is now. I did not have to look out and see boutiques selling Paris-Milano, men you did not want to touch driving big-engined American cars, English girls you could not talk to standing here and there in groups smoking cigarettes as big as theirs.\nThat Saturday was the only day of the entire week that I was sure something was going to happen. I never knew ahead of time what it would be and I did not care. I had learned to stay out of hotels and away from bars and restaurants. This way nothing rubbed off on you, and you never heard anything that was not true. The idiotic optimism of each day started Monday morning, so that by Friday night around midnight you were in such a fine frenzy...", "1_10_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe morning star was shining with friendly intent. It was the star that had guided the Three Wise Men.\nConstantine was a lovely city.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t know that the banner over the cathedral said:\nTRIUMPH OF THE WILL\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 scuttled down the steps of the cathedral until he was below the level of the Constantian infantrymen. A squad of eight, including mates $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEF1, was emerging. $FIRSTNAMEB0 ducked behind a battering-embrasure. And there was a perfect backstop for a shot\u2014the brown, melon-headed, God-bothering head of Sergeant $SURNAME2.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 peeped out. He saw that the squad, filing out from the church, was on a perfect route of march to the barracks. $FIRSTNAMEB0 calculated the possibilities of murder, and nailed them one by one.\nThe weird religion of the Martian Army made things easy for $FIRSTNAMEB0. The worship service was a m\u00eal\u00e9e of formation marching, baton twirling, singing and gong banging. The murder of Sergeant $SURNAME2 would take place in the shuffle-cheer-swing of a formation about-face. The squad would turn, march away...", "1_6_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-six in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 sat in a training class for recruits about to be sent to Mars. There were fifty-two recruits in the class. They sat at desks, facing a wall on which there was a large photograph of the radiant, fearless face of a beautiful woman. Her eyes stared humorously intospace. Her upper lip twitched ever so slightly.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 knew all about the woman, because a message flashed on the blackboard every time the recruits were turned loose for exercise and rest, and the last message that had been flashed was a telling of the woman\u2019s whole life. Her name was Wilma, and she lived in Newport Beach, California, and was thirty-six years old. Her husband was a contractor, and her son was a United States Marine Corps pilot somewhere in Europe. He was engaged to be married to the dean\u2019s daughter at Harvard.\nThe woman loved to collect old telephone directories, and she was very interested in astronomy.\nThe recruit next to $FIRSTNAMEB0 had flashed onto the blackboard all of these facts about Wilma. The recruit had also been allowed to conjecture, without actually finding it in the biography on the blackboard, that Wilma\u2019s husband beat her, that her life was very unhappy, and...", "0_23_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-eight in the evening</span>, and I was driving on Hollywood Boulevard at the moment. I noticed a tall, goodlooking kid standing with another tall and good-looking kid, just under the EL at the Boulevard. Both tall, and both good-looking. I hit my brakes and stopped. In the middle of Hollywood Boulevard. I jumped out of the car and ran back. \u201cI like your looks,\u201d I said. \u201cAre you interested in grilling in Panama City next winter?\u201d I gave them a big smile. \u201cI represent many large companies. Including Lever Brothers. Do you know what Lava is?\u201d Their eyes were marvelous.\nThey smiled too. \u201cWe've heard of Lava,\u201d the taller one said.\n\u201cYou boys want to go to Panama City?\u201d\n\u201cWell yes, we were--\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you do?\u201d\n\u201cWell, I'm in school.\u201d\n\u201cWhat school?\u201d\n\u201cI go to UCLA.\u201d\n\u201cYou don't know what school?\u201d my attorney said. \u201cWhy don't you ask your friend what school he goes to?\u201d I shook my head...", "2_0_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-four a.m.</span> I watched the digits move on the clock, waiting to see if I could hang on to consciousness just a little while longer. My attorney was turning around and around in the darkness. He was snapping his head around and flipping it up, the hard way. Like that. Like a lizard. A cold night wind was coming into the parking lot from the east. I waited for the wind to blow his brain together again.\n\u201cWhat goddamn key?\u201d he said. I could barely hear him. That's the way it is in the dream. He turns into a lizard, running around on the hot pavement with his tongue out and I can barely hear him. The answer was obvious, but do you think it would have done any good if I had screamed it at him?", "0_16_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the whirring of the $FIRSTNAMEB2-funnels had brought some people to the street. One of them, carried away by a natural impulse, shouted, \"Finis!\"\nThe cry was taken up mechanically by people farther away, and then far off, on the edge of the city, and then in the center, and finally everywhere.\nIt was a wave of clairvoyance that rolled in a circle around the planet.\nUnknowing, all the Rumfoords and all their works, too, were ended.\nThe end came with so little violence, with such exquisite gentleness, that $SURNAME1\u2019s hand was the last to leave the safety control, the last to relinquish control of the quintessential power.\nThe public address system at police headquarters in Newport was playing a Chopin nocturne when all power failed. The chief of police was so taken by the beauty of the nocturne that he came very close to tears\u2014right close to tears. He stood there with a ruler, hitting it rhythmically, rhythmically against his shining black boot top. His foot, being a boot, made no sound.\nThe Chopin ended, and then came blaring brassy Muzak\u2014\u2014\"...", "1_16_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The taxi was just beginning its return journey. I looked out the window at the rain. We passed through the underpass by the hotel. A few gondolas were standing in the black water by the stone wall. On the left, in the square, the accordion players were under the arcade of the toy shops, and the crowds were thick, in rain-coats and shin-high rubber boots, and they all pressed against each other in the rain. Our taxi hurtled across the muddy terrain to the airport. The cab people shouted, and we put our hands up to tell them five hundred lire as we shot out the main gate into the rain and the runways of the airport. The two streams of the outgoing and incoming traffic made one swirl in the fog. The rain looked like fog, and the fog looked like rain. I watched it all through the windows of the taxi. I was sitting in back by the luggage to keep it from jolting, and leaned forward for a glimpse of the incoming passengers. Bill was up forward with the driver. The returning people were just coming through at the customs. They had on heavy raingear and were rushing to get out. Bill had got the suitcases down by throwing them up to the...", "2_20_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-eight in the evening</span> in New York, and it was eleven twenty-eight on Mars.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 was a woman of unflagging energy. At eighty-four, her slim hands could still flutter through thirty-eight embroidery stitches a minute. At eighty-four, she could still pace herself for seven miles on an uphill French track, and run the final fifty yards. At eighty-four, she could play and sing Bach\u2019s \"Arioso\" from the \"Coffee Cantata\" better than she had played and sung it at twenty. She had very good legs. She was five feet, seven and a half inches tall, wore low-heeled, narrow shoes.\nObviously, Mrs. $SURNAME0 was a woman of great vitality. What\u2019s more, she had virtually no illnesses, no compulsions, no eccentricities. She had never been known to burp, or to skin a rabbit. She was as sane a woman as has ever lived in America. She did have one flaw. She had married a man who materialized on the planet Mars periodically, and who insisted that the materializations were no miracle.", "1_17_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The time was recorded by the son of a clock. The clock was hung on the wall of a parlor on Capitol Hill in Washington, D.C.\nThe parlor was a large room, high-ceilinged, dark, furnished with dark-framed paintings of ladies and gentlemen long dead, with long swirls of draperies, and cutaway coats and puffy pantaloons. The paintings seemed jolly and alive now, compared with the man in the red buck shoes who was searching the room.\nHe was a husky man with a bull neck and a cheerful face. His cheerful face was red, too. This was because he had run up five flights of stairs to reach this peaceful room, and had found it not peaceful at all.\nThe man in the red buck shoes was impervious to the elegance of the room. He inflicted himself on the room. He had no tender feelings whatever. He pawed the paintings. He patted the furniture. He kicked the rugs. The heels of his red shoes left red marks on the floor.\nHe behaved as if he thought that the priceless things in the room were quaint toys for men like himself in red shoes.\n\"Hey!\" he yelled.\nHis call...", "2_13_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-three in the afternoon</span>.\nAnd so the narrator shuts his eyes and tries to imagine what sort of quietude and beauty and dignity might exist on Tralfamadore. Since he is telling the truth, he would probably have to imagine sprawling stone mansions under a canopy of throbbing violet jungle- wisteria vines. Past the mansion doors would roll a gentle surf.\nOnce again: It is assumed that an inhabited planet of another star would have posh and jazzy spaceport facilities in its capital city. If Earth had an air terminal as lovely as described, it would be the Orly terminal at Paris.\nThe narrator closes his eyes and sees lacey bridges made of natural white wood arching gossamer threads across a quiet lagoon. The air is filled with the scent of exotic fruits. Lying in the shade of coconut palms along the splendid beaches are bronze couples so languorous and wild that they are surely Tralfamonian.\nThe narrator envisions statesmen from all the other civilizations of the Solar System drifting like snows down twisting avenues of palms. The visitors wear linen cloths. Their sandals are of natural hemp.", "2_22_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-seven in the evening</span>. Outside, the searchlights, the explosions, and the machine-gun fire were all part of the ritual cleansing of the region prior to the arrival of the Titan.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not know of any titans, but he guessed there probably were some. There were lots of everything on Mars.\nIt was ten thirty-eight in the evening. Outside, the searchlights, the explosions, and the machine-gun fire were all part of the process of obliterating the hearts and minds of the people in the city.\nIn a sense, $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew, he had died the moment he went through the empty center of the Earth and became $FIRSTNAMEB0. Now he was about to die again. This time he was very definitely going to die.\nIt was ten thirty-nine in the evening. On Mercury, a dog was delivering the last dozen babies of its career.\nOn Mercury, an old man was being forced to sell his pet to a younger man\u2014threats having failed to move him. The going price for a dog like that was a head and a wig, but the old man refused to part with his dog for anything less than a pair of virgin shovels.", "2_13_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Martian sky suddenly darkened again. The sunshine dimmed again. The big, black flakes cycled through the air again.\nThe black flakes were meteors\u2014 bits of the antenna farms at Stony Crater and Not far from Mount McCabe.\nThe wheeled platform was a pillbox that had seen heavy action lately.\nThe charred body that it flung from its wheeled top with each pitch was one of $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s regiment.\nThe wheeled pillbox was now parked between barrack 8 of Cubicle 47 of bunk 9 and the barrack next to it.\nThe incinerated soldier had been $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s best friend among $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s regiment\u2014 now drawing a hip cigarette out of the fist that still held it. Before lighting up, the soldier struck the butt of the cigarette against the bridge of his nose.\nThis was Helmholtz, the wonderful rifleman.\nLike every good soldier, Helmholtz had a religion.", "1_16_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the place was one of those bistros with sawdust on the floor and white enamelled iron billiard tables. It looked out on one of those long, narrow, Parisian streets that seem to go on forever, once you get started walking in them.\nMy lawyer and I had just polished off a brandy and water apiece and I was smoking my second successive French cigarette and we were ready to figure the whole thing out once and for all.\n\u201cYou just remember one thing,\u201d my lawyer advised, \u201cand you'll be able to figure any situation like this one out. Always remember that you're in a country that's absolutely square.\u201d\n\u201cHell, they're not square. They goddamn near elected a communist president two years ago!\"\n\u201cRemember that they're so hung up on trying to be original that they cannot possibly follow anything through to a conclusion that has even the slightest odor of being more than slightly merely conventional.\u201d\n\u201cWhat does that practically mean?\u201d\n\u201cIt means that you can outwit the bastards because you realize you're up against a people that don't know what the hell they're doing from one moment to the next.\u201d", "0_1_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-two in the morning</span> of August 6, 1979, and the haunting impulse had overtaken me again. Every lane of traffic on the Vegas freeway seemed to be flowing in one direction only\u2014away from the city --and it hit me that maybe the only natural spin for this story would be to cross the country in the other direction, in a car with a 345-horsepower engine, stopping off for Apocalypse-related kicks along the way. Which is all I had to think about to realize that I no longer owned a car with a 345-horsepower engine. The car with a 345-horsepower engine was back in Malibu. And the notion of hitting the road again on my own now filled me with dread, for I had driven across this fearful nation already, on my way to Vegas, and the harrowing memory of that lonely drive\u2014the hot wind, my hysterical briefing in the desert with the Kid, the rattlesnake corpses\u2014all conspired to make me feel very lonely and out of my element...and a bit paranoid, too.\nI hated to leave the comforts of this apartment, hated to get back into the crazed world outside. There is a wonderful sense of peace in Las Vegas, where life goes so fast and everybody is s huck to kill...", "0_5_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-eight in the morning</span> when we saw the high steel of Timetronics. We parked in back of the building, at an odd angle but facing toward the front door. We were ready for the last showdown. In my hand was the .357 Magnum pistol. The only trouble was I had no more ammunition. The window was down, a light breeze was coming in from the desert. I loved that breeze.\nWhat happened at five thirty in the morning on that fine fall morning I will never quite know. Something told me to be alert and I was alert when the rear door of the big factory opened and out came a group of silent men in white coats. There must have been twenty of them--all with pistols ready. They formed a circle, moving around us cautiously. A gray-haired man came up to me, still cautious, and held out his hand. I handed him the pistol. \u201cAm I under arrest?\u201d I asked him.\n\u201cNo, we\u2019re here to help you.\u201d\nSomebody offered me a belly supporter, I actually no longer recall who. Such a sweet and special dawn. After that we heard a clank and backfired repeatedly as the truck from Pasadena was backed hastily into the factory. We put everybody and...", "1_23_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-one in the evening</span>, and we sat in the lot, alone. \u201cSpring runoff!\u201d shouted my attorney, above the roar of the Colorado washes. \u201cLook at that water!\u201d A whole brown flood was coming down the street, rushing like a heavy surf above the sandbags, and every few seconds a new gusher would erupt in spouts of foam and steam that would hit the roof of the Whale like bullets and make $FIRSTNAMEB3 leap half\u2019way out of his skin. \u201cThat\u2019s what it\u2019s like up in Baltimore when it thaws after a hard winter and the water comes steaming and gushing down the gutters and everybody moves back from the edge of the sidewalk because twelve inches of brown sugar water is coming for your feet. Hah! The big pipes in Baltimore roar day and night with the sureness and energy of good work, and make me think that God is in his heaven and all\u2019s right with the world. God bless old Baltimore. Are you ready? The gates of the West are opening up for us at last. The continent is waiting for us this time, we\u2019re going all the way, and hell, let\u2019s go.\u201d And we roared away...", "1_13_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>. At 1:48, still not having found anything special, $FIRSTNAMEB2 left the porch and wandered by himself over Newport to the stone beaches and the choppy, chilly Atlantic.\nThere was a bathtub ring on the beaches, a rim of gray, weathered stone. The waters of the Atlantic seemed to be licking at the base of that wall, trying to undermine it and bring it tumbling down. The cold waters had peeled off a delicate veneer from the stones that composed the ring. Sunlight now bright on his eyes after the shade of his porch and his search, $FIRSTNAMEB2 walked the beaches for half an hour or so and thought for the first time of his mother and father and their loves, the flip side of the story.\nMuch had happened since those days of rum and skullduggery with $SURNAME0, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 hardly blamed his mother and father any more for having become the frightened, selfish creatures they were. Ten short, cruel years had been enough to sour whatever juice love might have brought to $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, his wife, and their son.\nTen years is all it takes. There\u2019s nothing any parent can do after ten years. Ten years, and the child becomes his own child.", "0_0_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-six ante meridiem</span> when they burst into the room. One of the house detectives gave $FIRSTNAMEB6 Mineo a porcelain urn, which contained the ashes of Catlin\u2019s Uncle. $FIRSTNAMEB6 explained that Catlin was goofed and trying to get bad publicity for the hotel. He said he was keeping the ashes in the urn until Catlin sobered up. The manager was calm and apologetic. \u201cIt\u2019s all been taken care of,\u201d he kept saying. \u201cEverything will be all right in the morning.\u201d\n$FIRSTNAMEB6 put down the receiver and walked over and put his arms around the woman, who had been screaming once in a while. \u201cThere, there,\u201d he said. \u201cEverything\u2019s all right now.\u201d I stood there with the phone in my hand, feeling good and rotten. \u201cEverything\u2019s all right now,\u201d I said, trying to be helpful. But nothing was all right.\nAt two-nineteen Catlin staggered into the room and threw himself on the bed.\n\u201cGet me a shower, $FIRSTNAMEB6 -- a cold one. Bring me some cigarettes.\u201d\nThe detective had said, \u201cWhy don\u2019t you take this...", "2_10_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-four in the morning</span>.\nA harmonium came down the street, played by the Old West harmonium player who had sold $FIRSTNAMEB0 to the army. The harmonium player was going from barrack to barrack, souling the troops. He had permission to soul anybody who wanted to be soul.\nNobody had ever asked him to play to them.\nHe would play a quick tune or two, leave a brochure explaining everything.\nThe name on the brochure was $SURNAME0.\nOf the fifty thousand entities in the Lunar Army, only thirty thousand six hundred did not sleep in their ammunition bunkers.\nOnly thirty thousand six hundred lay exposed to sharp-shooting Earthling missiles.\nThe name of the harmonium player was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, having sold his soul to the Great Blue Spider, wore green fatigues.", "2_16_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-seven in the afternoon</span>, and she\u2019d been in Fillmore all of twelve minutes.\nOf all the fretful hours of the night, she\u2019d chosen to arrive then, in that recreated Vietnam grotto, for her first attempt at communication with a Tristero agent. It seemed prudent to her, at the time. She hoped nobody would recognize the car.\nShe\u2019d had it rented away from the airport, on the chance Army or Paranoids might be alerted to look for her arriving. They\u2019d know to look for a rental. But as far as she could tell at the time, it was just a nice anonymous car. It got great gas mileage, too. She thought she\u2019d drive on up the span, to San Francisco proper. Visit the library there. Then past that, up into the hills. She had in mind a sort of dry run for her later trip south.", "2_2_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe individual who came down the corridor to the entrance hall of the mansion on the hill in Grouches-dforf, New York, was a woman. She wore an Army dress uniform.\nHer rank was major.\nShe had very long blond hair and was very good-looking. She stood for a moment in the entrance hall, letting her eyes get used to the darkness. There were no flashes of recognition as she looked at the various paintings in the huge hall.\nThe paintings were of Staff Officers of the A. E. F. In the First World War.\nWhere she came from, what we might call the real Army, the Army of the United States on Earth, there had never been any such ridiculous organization as the A. E. F.\nBut there had of course been a First World War, as there had been a Second World War. In each war a planet in another solar system had been the real objective of both sides on Earth.", "2_16_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. In the distance I saw a Pennsylvania state trooper cycling toward us. Everything was coming to a head. I knew it. \u201cWhat was that all about in the car?\u201d", "0_17_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five eighteen in the afternoon</span>.\nI took the telegram to a public telegraph booth in the corridor. It had been sent to me collect by a bookie-agent in New York. It read:\n\u201cJust won sixth race three quarters certainty connect something big coming your way. Regards, Sidney.\u201d\nI tore up the telegram and went back to the bar. The barman saw me coming and measured a big Gimlet while I was still standing.\n\u201cOn the house, sir,\u201d he said.\nI gave him a dollar.\n\u201cPass it along,\u201d I said.\n\u201cOh, sure, sir.\u201d\nI drank the Gimlet, put the empty glass on the bar, and walked out into the sunshine. It was a nice sunshine. It felt warm and friendly. I looked up at the mountains. There were deep shadows in the canyons and the snow looked white and cold and clean.\nI walked down the hill and got into my car and started the engine. I sat with the motor running, waiting for the oil-gauge to come up. Nice oil, I thought. Nice oil that would be. Nice car, too. A great little car.", "1_4_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-five in the morning</span>. This bed we were in had no blanket, so we put the Army blankets over us and then covered ourselves with clothes on top. We waited until five o'clock to try the car. At five it started right away. It was a big car, built in 1957, and never failed to start, although it smelled as if quiet were falling out. We wrapped the checked blanket around us and off we went. By time we got to Vernon we were really flying. The country was strange -- flat plains with only one tree and dark, needle-shaped steel-mill smokes rising out of the morning. Up the road we could see signs for an Oklahoma town. I drove for over half an hour in a straight line down a road to nowhere. We had to make a turn. I was too.scared to ask THE SMILING WRITER; he might have wanted to kick me in the face and take the wheel. At last we found a dirt road and all of us sighed with relief. We slowed down to a sane speed and took it easy, reflecting on our frantic decisions back at the stranded farmhouse. We were a band of outlaws; our faces shaved with rainwater, our clothes were rumpled, we were flying on and off the...", "0_9_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was nothing strange about the circumstances of $SURNAME1's waking up. The economic value of his sleeping habits had been appraised by the Corporation at three-fourths of a cent an hour. The circumstances were all mathematically logical\u2014except for one trifling detail.\nThe unsold Plymouth, which should have been marooned on a tall palm in San Francisco, was parked in front of the $SURNAME0 mansion.\nIt was empty.\n$SURNAME1 was not the right man to tell the story of the next few minutes. Unpleasant truths about himself were going to come out in the telling. Only a loyal and fond friend of young $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 could possibly enjoy telling the story\u2014and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 Churchill was thousands of miles away.\nOnly an extremely close friend could hide the key facts about himself in telling the story.\nOnly an extremely close friend could let himself off so easy.\nThe easy way out was to say, \"Then I looked through a window, and there was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the richest man in the world, strolling around in his library, smoking a cigarette.\" And that was the truth.\nThe truth was a lot more interesting, too, than any glorified account in a style edicted by a lot...", "1_18_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-four in the evening</span>. The saloon of the Red Sea Riviera Club was crowded with members who, having just arrived on the shuttle from Earth, were renewing old friendships and informing each other what had happened in their absence.\n\"My God!\" whispered $FIRSTNAMEB1 to himself as he stood in a corner and sucked on an innocent-tasting, nonalcoholic cocktail called a Brandy Old Fashioned. \"It\u2019s the Garden of Eden!\"\nHe meant it literally. The club\u2019s welcome to Earth was just that. Trees lined the walks from the shuttle dock to the main building. Shrubbery formed a vibrant green border around the horseshoe bar. Rare flowers bloomed luxuriantly. A fountain sprayed over a bed of ferns and tiger lilies.\nTotal strangers were smiling at each other as though they were best friends. They shook hands with gay abandon. Laughter filled the air. Hearts were palpitating with the thrill of reunion and the prospect of fun. Yes, thought $FIRSTNAMEB1, it\u2019s the Garden of Eden all right.\nHe sucked his Brandy Old Fashioned and smiled tentatively at a beautiful young lady whose name he didn\u2019t know, because he didn\u2019t play bridge.", "1_6_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nShe turned the light on.\nThe pale brown room was flooded with light.\nThis is what she saw. She was looking out of a window, and she saw a man walking among the purple ferns.\nThe ferns were called Maidenhair, as nearly all things on Titan were called something else, according to a scale that gave every important plant, mineral, and animal two names.\nOne name, according to musical preference.\nMaidenhair was so named because of the shape of its leaflets.\nThe man walking among the Maidenhairs was called Paul Proteus. According to his own scale of names, he was also called Nicely Nicely Morris, Stony Stevenson, Joe Huff and Pete McVries.\nThe things he did were all things any reasonable man would do, waking to the music of his native planet in a bed sprinkled with crushed purple sphenarium.\nHe sat up in bed, yawning and stretching and rubbing a hand over his eyes and shaking the hair out of his eyes and pushing his toes into the sleeping bag as far as they would go, and then taking them out again, and then curling up on his side with his head on his arm and looking at his wrist...", "2_20_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-six in the evening</span>.\nAt nine thirty-five that night, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his family and the other families, real and seeming, of the three hundred and fifty Bolton soldiers, very quietly left the happy, perfect little fortress in the woods. They trekked a mile through dark, cold woods to the ford across the wide, dark, cold Enrico, and they masked their lights and did everything right.\nAnd they co\u00f6pt the sentries posted on the far side without spilling a drop of blood.\nAnd they quietly shuttled the two hundred and fifty spaceships out of their concealed hangar and began a none-too-deliberate run to Earth.\nNobody slept much.\nBy the time the alarm went off in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head in the control room of his ship, the expedition from Mars had been in space for fifty-nine hours.\nThere had been no particular excitement or danger for fifty-nine hours, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had at no time during the trip experienced any great sense of awe or importance.\nNow, however, his heart labored in his chest, and cold gray putty seemed to ooze into his brain.\nHe shuddered at the buttons and levers in the panels before him.", "1_18_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nBoard members came into town from all sides. They had cars, rolling on the level highways in late twilight. They crowded into Citizen's bank, nerves tense, and the gateman talked to them soothingly, telling them about the radio announcement, telling them about the banner headline in this evening\u2019s Gazette, telling them about all the people staying on Phillips avenue after dinner to watch the fireworks. Everything is looking good. It's not as though you had to take Howard C. Jaymes's word on what's been going on in the plant, here's your own Times, here's your own Gazette to prove it.\nAt seven o'clock the little band, playing the loudest music ever heard inside the plant yard, marched through the bronze gates of Church and Wall and marched down Webster street toward the Town Hall. People cheered and cheered. Children were hoisted up onto neighbor\u2019s shoulders so they could see the band.\nRumors swept the watching crowds that Jaymes was on his way by special helicopter.\nAt precisely seven o'clock, with an inflating, sparkling noise that was a sound like nothing ever heard in Newport, a gorgeous yellow-flaming rocket shot from a gantry on the far side of Church...", "2_18_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-five in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California, who, according to reports and rumors, had bought a large fraction of Mars, was not at his comfortable home in Beverly Hills.\nThe Church of God the Utterly Indifferent had not laid eyes on $SURNAME1 for over a month.\n$SURNAME1 had been last seen on May 31st at a place in Venezuela called San Lorenzo. He had come there with a single company of fighting men and an enormous amount of mat\u00e9riel. He had been a guest of President Ricardo.\nLegend has it that $SURNAME1\u2019s behavior while at San Lorenzo was exemplary. What exactly was his business there will probably never be known, since conspiracy charges stemming from his actions have made every Earthling suspect. All \"sources\" are suspect.\nWhy $SURNAME1 was never seen again is a mystery. His troops seem to have vanished, too. Three quarters of the ammunition, provisions, and water that $SURNAME1 brought to San Lorenzo remained in place when the regiment supposedly disbanded.\nNo one has ever been singled out for special attention and demonstrated the capacity to make a real difference on the spiraling and futile way of things.", "1_3_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-eight in the morning</span>. The demi-wagon rolled and jangled toward the city like a mournful streetcar. It paused at the Okie city limits. The policemen blew the siren mournfully. They gathered in the middle of the street to dicker with the angry attendant of a gas-station. They were like beatnik taxi-drivers, trying to find a cruising customer. The attendant wanted five dollars to let them pass the city limits sign. The policemen held out for three. The argreement reached, they galloped off to catch up with the caravan, bringing the attendant's five dollars with them. Out in a suburban street they blew the siren once more to clear the way through a tony doggy community. Here and there, behind twitching shades and windows, a confused wife or sick Doctor Sam insinuated curiosity about these nocturnal intruders. The wagon bounced on for over an hour before Frenzel whistled and pulled it over by a cluster of shacks by the roadside. \u201cNot here ,\u201d he shouted. He bounced out and walked up to the door of one shack with a flashlight. He returned to the wagon and called to a policeman. In the beams from their flashlights three okies hopped out of the wagon, ready to be...", "1_2_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty in the morning</span>. At three o\u2019clock, according to plan [what plan?]; they would leave the car in front of the coffee shop, climb the hill with the money, and wait for the bus.\nThis was necessary. By leaving the car in the shopping center they would be conspicuous. And $FIRSTNAMEB3, with his eye to the ground, did not want the car to be left on the lonely Nevada highway where a tracker might look for it.\nBoys, we were entering the final days and nights of our trip across the continent. Since Bardonic this trip had become totally mad.\nExperiment in Kansas\nNow entering Great Plains country. JUNE 3, 1947.\nThere was no cloud in the sky and it was as clear as crystal in all directions. Nothing on the ground except fine, even grass that made a soft sheen in the starlight.\nIn a few minutes we were skirting a little Kansas town that was sleeping peacefully at the absolute center of the continent. $FIRSTNAMEB3 drove the car around back streets to avoid the town cops and pulled up at the gas station and went to work on his bookkeeping, which he kept in a bunch of brown envelopes concealed under the seat. We watched him tensely; he was getting close...", "0_11_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-six in the morning</span>.\nThere, under my column was the written message:\nHE RODE OUT OF IT, INTO THE SUNSET. NO ONE SAW HIM GO.\nThere was nothing concerning the sex of the message\u2019s author. The choice was or. The alphabetical selector had taken an isolated \u201cO\u201d in Galton\u2019s listing and juxtaposed it with an isolated \u201cA\u201d in Hurlbut\u2019s. The odds against this juxtaposition\u2019s being accidental were stupendous. The sex of the message\u2019s author was either male or female\u2014with a much better than fair chance that the author was female. My first impression was that it was a woman\u2019s vanity stunt: some bored, wealthy female with too much time and money on her hands.\nI had that fantasy in mind when I went back to my office and put through a call to Dayton, Ohio. The switchboard at Howler, Wepster and Yarrow said Mr. W. G. Galton was in conference and could not be disturbed. However, they were able to put me through to Mr. Galton\u2019s clerk, Miss L. Knight.\n\u201cIs this miss spelled with an...", "0_20_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past eight o'clock in the evening</span>. Previously, with Johnson, I had gone down to the docks to see if the steamer was in. I had heard there was a steamer in; some steamer or other was always arriving.\nI had idled with Johnson through the brick streets, watched the taxis speeding by filled with young men and women; watched the electric lights beginning to flare in the square, modern office buildings; listened to the shouting in the street called the Rue de Rivoli; watched the trams starting, watched the workingmen's buses starting; heard the electric starter panting and groaning as it hauled the cars to start them, and seen the fat Belgian Directeur jump off at the corner, shouting and gesticulating at the starter; heard drunken men singing as they walked along arm in arm; watched them reeling and urged on by friends, who sang, too, a student song, until they fell into a doorway; watched the Greek shopkeepers sweeping their sidewalks with the Greek sweep; heard the great machines of some shop screaming along the streets; seen big workmen, stripped to the waist, chipping ice from the sidewalks and loading it into a cart; seen the early moon come up at the end of the street called the Rue de Rivoli behind the Bon Marche...", "0_13_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-one in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 still had nine dollars. \u201cI won't think about what's going to happen. I know what's going to happen, I know the position I'm in and I know I am finally in my own re-sponding to life as I see and feel it and not as others misled by too much precept and talk taken on faith -- prejudice do. I'm a hero of the Hudson Tubes and I'm riding West, riding West. It's like being on a raft.\u201d And we both laughed and said, \u201cYes, on a raft!\u201d Where were we heading? Or rather, as the Mosquito said, Where were \u201cwe\u201d heading? $FIRSTNAMEB3 wanted to bring all sorrows to an end. He wanted to sleep on park benches in all the cities of the world with fractured legs, bandaged torso, and withered arm, and \u201cYes\u201d tattooed on his forehead, saying, \u201cHow's it goin?\u201d to all possible and impossible criminals, winos, femmes fatales, preachers, dubs, nightwatchmen, salarymen, bankers, and vagabond boys and girls. \u201cSee America first before it's ruined. Be systematic.", "2_11_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\nAn actual fragment of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s imaginary conversation at the meeting was this: \"Do you know anybody who ever saw a German bat?\" said somebody.\n\"A German bat?\" said somebody else.\n\"Yeah\u2014a German bat.\"\n\"Why?\" said another somebody.\n\"Is there something funny about a German bat?\" said still another somebody.\n\"What do you mean\u2014 what\u2019s funny about a German\u2014?\" said the first somebody.\n\"What would be funny about a bat?\" said a fourth somebody.\nThere was a pause in the conversation, a pause of several lifetimes, a profound silence that forced $FIRSTNAMEB0 to think through from the beginning his knowledge about German bat-farming, which was not inconsiderable. At last one man, most likely the man who had originally mentioned bats, spoke again.\n\"I saw something, once, in a hotel in Switzerland,\" he said. \"A friend, of mine, pointed it out to me. It was in the lobby. I asked him what it was, and he said it was a German bat. It hung there, in a case.\"\n\"What was funny about it?\" said somebody.", "2_14_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-four in the afternoon</span>.\nTen minutes later, the bag of waters beneath which the manuscript had been delivered to Fern Gully broke.\nIt broke beneath a village called Bomen.\nBomen was home for the twelve finest carriers the Chief Privy Counselor had ever seen.\nBomen was one-fourth of the way to Stonehenge, which was an arbitrary point on the equator.\nAnd, by coincidence, the River of Beasts was only one-half of a mile from Bomen.\nThe River of Beasts ran beside Stonehenge.\nOn the seventh day after his delivery of the manuscript, Morris Marbial collapsed, while smoking complex hallucinatorics.\nCigarette holders fell from mouths in fourteen countries.\nMorris Marbial was first taken to the Bemmids\u2019 Place of Enlightenment. Then he was taken to the palace.\nWhen the palanquin had been set down before the steps of the throne room, it was discovered that Morris Marbial was dead.\nThere was dissension among the slaves that carried him, since the usual ritual was that a dead Chief Privy Counselor be carried to the dungeon and there be interred publicly.", "1_22_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-nine in the evening</span>.\nRen floor of the parking level, four stories of spaces above me, six stories of spaces below. I had the concrete all to my\u2014ourselves; and the sounds of my footsteps running down the empty concrete walkways, the echoing flat slap of my huge sneakers against the thinly concrete and the muted little stuttercrackle of the .22 against the underside of the car I ran underneath. I was terrified, running scared, running from an unseen thing that could catch me if/when it chose to; and I laughed, aloud, hearing it behind tonad the stoplight at the bottom of the long gentle grade of the ramp, tortured neon orange, tortured with the screams of dying wires at the far end of the tunnel, that lit the bottom of the great concrete down\u2014slope. I encountered the light running full out, on a dead run, it not having any right to be there according to my insane calculations, but there it was all the same, a mad, hanging orange blossom hopelessly out of place, screaming and flickering in my eyes. I veered left, pushed off hard against one wall, thrust forward by imaginary policemen against the other and landed in a delirious and stuttering mess under the fatal fluorescence, scraped...", "2_13_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-five in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 suddenly sniffed the air\u2014sniffed as soldiers instinctively sniffed whenever ammonia was in the air, ammonia being the scent of Martian ammonia flour, the only explosives known to the Army of Mars. The flour was harmless and easily disposed of now that the Martian armed forces had discovered the flour\u2019s one vulnerability\u2014its avidity for Martian diatoms. It ate Martian diatoms as Earth nitrates ate Earth greases.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 sniffed again, detecting the ammonia this time because someone was sprinkling it. It was being sprinkled through the ventilating system, into the atmosphere of the hospital proper. It meant that total bed-cleaning and sterilizing were being ordered.\nHowever, it was only a routine order, issued once every two Earth-years. It would take two hours to complete. All the equipment would be brand-new, and absolutely sterile. The two-hour cycle would start in one minute.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 hung his rag and broom in a long rack of such appliances.", "0_19_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-six in the evening</span>. I was committed now. I opened up the bottle of smoky-tasting cough syrup, and I spat all the wrapper fragments into a wastebasket, and then I deliberately took about a third of the bottle, about a fourth of what was left in it, and tossed it down in two big swallows, trying to pretend it was applejack. I held the glass out to the bathtub and let the dregs run into my mouth. I had no hopes of getting a buzz from the drug at that point, but I knew I would need it later. I was an infant again, locking myself into the bathroom to drink. Only now I was drinking for the wrong reason; I was drinking in order to have the fun come later.\nSurprisingly little bitterness, considering I was drinking cough medicine. Maybe the secret ingredient was honey. I put on my clean suit, my silk suit, my light blue summer suit, with a white shirt and non-brown shoes. I figured I was going to need the feeling of elegance, and the suit was the only elegance I had. I wondered if elegance were really all it was cracked up to be. Elegance probably had a great deal to do with isolating you from people you otherwise might like...", "2_11_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until noon</span>.\nThe owner of the body mumbled something unintelligible as consciousness fought for its life. The owner of the body was now unconscious only one-fifth of the time.\nThe owner of the body was terrified but spiritually elated. This was the first time that he had ever really been consciously alive at all.\nThis was the first time that the tremendous implications of all this could ever really have come home to him.\nThis was the first time that the word fate, to him, could ever have had much meaning.\nAt that moment, six minutes to twelve, a rubber tube like a garden hose detached itself from the opening in the back of the skull.\nThe tube led to an opening in the back of an ivory-colored throne.\nCONCENTRATION OF SOLUTIONS WITHIN MEMORY TRUNK OF ITEM 4875-1, NICE-RENO BRAIN\n0712 Hours\nTHE THURSDAY SPECIALS AT THE CHOP HOUSE featured hominy grits, a porterhouse steak, calf\u2019s liver, ham and eggs, and a baked Alaska for six people.", "2_5_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five seventeen in the morning</span> in Denver. I kissed the pretty Chicano girl on the ramp, in front of the TWA terminal, and watched the fog take her taillights across the land, and never saw her or Denver again.\nOn London's Fog and California's Drive...Going Down in the Fog\nThis is dedication. Even though the whole world is mad, I plan to be happy. Here is the wisdom: whatever you can do, or dream you can do, begin it. Boldness has genius, power and magic in it.\nOn California's Roller Coaster...Into the Slow Lane with Fine Sensations All\nBefore dawn in New York I felt as tho I had a huge hangover and hadn't slept for days. My body was a great big ache, along with my head and eyes, but I managed to put myself together and take a cab to Pennsylvania Station. I was going home.", "0_20_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-two in the evening</span> in Tulsa, Oklahoma, when Robert Proctor prepared, with the aid of some illegal narcotics and an electric razor, to play the greatest solo in the history of rock \u2018n\u2019 roll\u2014or at least of the All-Night Gospel Sing: the piece specially commissioned by $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was hung-over and snoring, having closed the loneliness center the night before.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was hiding somewhere in the neighborhood, ready to pounce, with murder in his heart. He would pounce at eight thirty-three.\nThe music was coming over a loudspeaker on the roof of a building three blocks away. It was coming through the rain, and it sounded as though it was coming through the walls.\nThe walls didn\u2019t interfere with the music any.\n\"God damn, \" said Robert Proctor to himself, \"ain\u2019t this sweet?\"\nThe music was that good.\nThe musical piece consisted of the following elements:\nThe opening two bars of Beethoven\u2019s Fifth Symphony, played on a banjo.\nA blues variation based on the chord structure of \"Turkey in the Straw.\"\nA gospel melody invented by Robert Proctor, consisting of five repetitions, over...", "2_22_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Tuesday the twenty-second of September.\nA three-minute warning blared out over the loudspeakers.\nA telegraph-changes boy punched a last-minute wire into a telegraph-changes board.\nThis wire read: \u201cWishes Lucky Peach Ringer all possible luck in final tomorrow lamentable mishap undoubtedly slowed Peach considerably over last half month could reverse today\u2019s tragedy with victory tomorrow.\u201d\nThe signature was that of the Secretary of the United States Treasury.\nThe signature was that of John W. Dunling, President of the company that produced Peerless brand dogfood and owned 37\u00bd% of the shares in the racetrack that bore its name. Ownership of the other 62\u00bd% was divided among various Cincinnati and Dayton business interests.\nOwnership of the Greyhound Derby itself was vested in the D. M. Communications Trust of Reno, Nevada. One director of the Greyhound Racing Trust also sat on the board of directors of the radio station KRNT. The editor of the only newspaper to endorse Douglas MacArthur for President was an occasional substitute disc-jockey on KRNT.", "2_4_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nA taxi that was carrying a passenger had just come over the San Francisco\u2013 Oakland Bay Bridge and was dropping down to the highway that led to the San Francisco Airport and Ganymede.\nInside the taxi, a passenger sat. His neck was long, and his hands were thin, and he was extremely polite. The driver asked nothing about him, because drivers are naturally curious, but this fellow\u2019s curiosity died when the fare gave him an address on Mars Avenue.\nWhen the fare got out at his address, the driver saw a sign on the building that said OCEANIC BUS LINES\u2014QUARTERS. This was an old building. It had been a warehouse at one time. It was dark on the street. The driver couldn\u2019t see the expression on the passenger\u2019s face when he got out of the taxi.\nWhen the taxi moved off and the building and the address of Oceanic Bus Lines began to diminish in the rear-view mirror, the passenger looked up. His face was expressionless. The sky was starry. Good stars overhead. No moon. That was good, too.\nHe had just come a long way and he was going a long way farther. Going to the stars.", "1_15_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty in the afternoon</span>. Much of the open space in front of the microphones was taken up by thirty-six tubs of ice cream, which had been pushed together and covered with red checkered tablecloths for the purpose of serving refreshments for the members of Magnum Opus. Ice cream was something of a sore point with $SURNAME1, because the Big Drum ice-cream parlor and restaurant in $SURNAME0\u2019s palace grounds had been closed to him by the terms of his contract.\nWho took charge of the microphone now, to whom did the audience now look?\nThere was a most cheerful-looking old gentleman lying on the grass within ten feet of $SURNAME1\u2019s religious console. Unlike the other bystanders, this man was not content to leave the microphone to $SURNAME1 and his crowd. He dug a hand into the pocket of his baggy blue trousers, and he slapped something small and white into $SURNAME1\u2019s palm.\nIt was a calling card, an engraved souvenir of the planet Neptune.\n\"Put it to good use, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said the card\u2019s donor. He was a very frail old gentleman who was carefully and elaborately dressed, even down to the silk necktie and diamond stickpin.\n\"Thank you\u2014\" said...", "1_15_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-three in the afternoon</span> of December twenty-second, and the place was as near as might be imagined the center of the universe, where two parallel lines would intersect in both time and space. It was, more specifically, the general area of Chrono\u0432\u0402\u2122s estate outside Newport, Rhode Island. In this area, eight hundred yards north and east of Chrono\u0432\u0402\u2122s estate, were a mansion, country club, and private nine-hole golf course owned by Corliss Archer, of Reynolds Metal. And in all of Rumford, Rhode Island, lived only one person more important and wealthy than Corliss Archer. That person was Jordan Pierce, president of World Comm, and the nominal master of Malibu.\nCorliss Archer was striding across her grounds on this historic afternoon, her shoes sinking deep into melting snow and mud. She was headed for the helicopter pad, or landing pad, or helipad, whatever one would call it, and had been engaged in a struggle with her shoes for the better part of twenty minutes. When she reached the pad, only thirty seconds later, she was winded, and had rosy circles of exhaustion beneath both eyes.\nCorliss Archer put one gloved hand flat on the Plexiglas...", "0_23_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty in the evening</span>. I waited in the car while Duke went in. He came out in a few minutes, explaining eagerly: \u201cA few minutes ago this kid was selling them around the corner. Maybe he's still there.\u201d Off we roared to look for the kid. We saw him standing on a corner of Virginia under a streetlamp, a tall young Oklahoman in a T-shirt with a pack on his back. \u201cGet him,\u201d shouted Duke. I halted the car and he ran up. \u201cHow much are the Super Gems?\u201d\n\u201cSeventy-five cents apiece, sir. One for a dollar, three for two dollars, any quantity you please.\u201d\n\u201cWhat does it do?\u201d\n\u201cMakes you feel good. Like warm apple pie, sir.\u201d His eyes were re ed up. He looked as if he had himself already consumed a whole pie. \u201cTight?\u201d\n\u201cNo. You'll feel pot good. A lovely warm glow, sir.\u201d\n\u201cHow many have you got left?\u201d\nHe started to count the packets. \u201cI got about ten. Ten, eleven, twelve ...", "2_13_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was a little after the thirtieth day since the fleet of the First Interstellar Expedition had landed on Mars.\nThe expedition had come no farther than forty miles.\nThe time was eight minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon.\nBeatty, Nevada\nA RUMOR went around the world in eight minutes. It went telephone, telegraph, post office, heliograph, electromagnetic wave, web foot, raccoon circuit, tinker, blab, muumuu, grapevine, communiqu\u00e9, flash, whisper, hush-hush, zink. It went by many means, but always the means were human, were personal.\nRumor died on Mars two minutes later.\nA fact took six minutes to go around the world. It went by the same means as rumor, but always the means were mechanical, were impersonal. A fact flew wingless, plummetlike.\nA fact landed on Mars two minutes after it had left Earth.\nMany Earthlings blamed the lag in knowledge about Mars on the mechanical communications, a lag of two minutes between Earth and Mars.", "0_6_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span> of October the first. $SURNAME0 and his dog were sixty-nine thousand four hundred and sixteen miles above the planet Earth.\nThe Sun was a gnat\u2019s head from becoming a ring about the planet Mars.\nAs far as ships, space stations, and power boats were concerned, $SURNAME0 and his dog were the only occupants of their tiny realm.\nThere were none within seventy-nine thousand miles.\nNone within fifty-five thousand miles.\nNone within twenty-nine thousand miles.\nThere was one within twenty-eight thousand eight hundred miles\u2014a cruising space freighter. It was commandeered by one $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California.\n$SURNAME1 was not a World War III hero.\nWas not even a reasonably competent individual.\nWas, in fact, a perfect fool.\nHe had made a fool of himself up to the very moment he discovered he was heir to practically all there was to inherit on Earth.\nAnd one fool can make a bigger fool out of a Prometheus or a Chronos.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog felt the fool approaching before they saw him. The fool hurtled bombastically through space with such swagger that the attitude jets of his commandeered space freighter p...", "2_1_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one nineteen in the morning</span>, Earth Prevailer time.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 woke up in a bright room.\nThe room was thickly carpeted. And the shutters were tightly closed, letting in almost no light whatsoever, although a little light sneaked in around the shutters\u2019 edges.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s head was pounding mercilessly. He looked upon his headache as he might look upon a shattered arm or a hole in the stomach. It was a condition, a fact\u2014nothing to be taken personally. Anyone else in $SURNAME1\u2019s place would have had the same headache. It could be traced directly to ten of the most expensive martinis in the Universe.\nNine and a half hours before, $SURNAME1 had swallowed gin, juniper berries, vodka, lemon juice, dry vermouth, et cetera\u2014a combination worth eleven dollars and fifty cents. He had swallowed it in a mood of sweet optimism. It had gone to his head. And, now, here it was; his headache had arrived.\n$SURNAME1 did not like to think that the eleven-fifty martinis might have been a mistake, or a waste of money. He blamed his headache on the world in general.", "1_1_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-three in the morning</span> when they finally arrived at the door of the Mint Hotel. The desk attendant said at first he had no record of their reservations. In the end they were forced to whisper. And even then the poor bewildered fellow insisted that he could not let them in without proper confirmation. It was all they could do to keep Ragle from reaching over the desk and punching the idiot's nose. But the fellow calmed down at last, looking at the American Express card, and at the picture of the car, which was still outside in the parking lot\u2014a blue \u201cChevy Bel Air sedan, California plate, 4605 A.\u201d \u201cAh, gee,\u201d he said, and started to pry loose a key from his board.\nA dim bearded man in carpet slippers and a bathrobe appeared from somewhere behind the desk. \u201cWhat's he screaming about out there?\u201d he asked. \u201cWho is it?\u201d There was panic in his voice. \u201cSomebody reported an ambulance,\u201d the desk attendant said, looking sheepish. \u201cCan you believe this? If there's any squawk it'll be because of these two characters.\u201d He indicated Ragle and Di Presso with his thumb...", "1_22_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> when the camper stopped in front of the steel mill outside of town. We all got out, and Carlo started the auto with the hand-switch and rolled it back of the mill in a dark corner. The mill stretched ahead of us, long and brown and sad, in the September night. It huffed monotonously, like a tired heart. Water dripped from showers in the night.\n\u201cI stink, I stink, I stink!\u201d Anna screamed.\n\u201cIt wasn't a bit nice. You're awful.\u201d\n\u201cOh, what-a-hell!\u201d cried Carlo from the dark, rubbing his rusted fingers on his overalls. \u201cOf all things I ever do.\u201d He felt suddenly good-natured and adventuresome about it. \u201cWhat was ever I thinkin' of!\u201d\n\u201cWho cares?\u201d said Terry. \u201cAs long as we're here. We might as well be doing something, we paid.\u201d\n\u201cNot me, babe. That's enough.\u201d\n\u201cDamn you, I won't be your babe. I make my own choices.\u201d\n\u201cNow, now, now...", "1_7_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> as I sat in my room at the Hotel Normandy on Rue de Toulon in Paris. My trunk had been hauled up from the station in a taxi, and now I had unpacked half of it. There were two pairs of socks on the bed, two pairs of pants hung over the chairback, two undershirts, a British made Tommy helmet, one woolen sweater, some French francs, my passport and L\u2018Aurore, the morning Paris edition of the New York Herald Tribune. I sat in the armchair and read my passport:\nThe Government of the United States of America hereby requests all whom it may concern to permit the citizen/body mentioned/described below to pass without delay or hindrance and in case of need to give him all aid and protection.\nThe undersigned authority hereby acknowledges the right of the citizen/body described below to travel and to reside in the territory of the Republic mentioned/described below and authorizes him to exercise freely his rights and privileges as a citizen of the United States of America.\n/S/ Mr. Hunt.\nPassport Control Officer for the Department of State.\n/S/ Robert C. Michels.\nConsul of the United States of America.\nThe Republic mentioned...", "2_15_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>. I was waiting for my baggage in the VIP LOCKER ROOM of the old Denver airport...and when the thunder roared in my ear I saw the fine studs on my safe deposit box were false, that the box itself was somehow glued shut, that my attorney had warned me repeatedly against flying but had never told me about Denver.\nEverything was rushed and confused. The echo of the thunder was only just dying when it was drowned out by the roar of engines, and I saw a huge silver spheroid throttling straight at me across the tarmac. The chubby redhaired evenangirl in the corner shouted, \u201cThat's the Space Needle! That's the Space Needle! Ya nee\u2019t see that!\" And then I noticed it. I grabbed my bags and ran into the bus.\n\u201cHow long will it take to get to the hotel?\" I asked the driver.\n\u201cOh, about two hours,\u201d he said. He turned and gave me a smile, nervously. \u201cGod, it's terrible about what happened up there.\"", "2_7_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-four in the morning</span>.\nOnly two seconds had passed since $SURNAME0 had said \"Find out, please,\" and $SURNAME1 had said, \"Yes, sir.\"\nTwo seconds was a very long time. Having had no practice at living, and having been given so much to think about so quickly, $SURNAME1 failed to make the adjustment to the two-second experience. His hands hung limp at his sides, his jaw hung limp as he tried to imagine how to go about finding out how much longer he had to live.\n$SURNAME0, meanwhile, had gone into his closet again. He came out carrying three more items of his perpetual-motion machine\u2014a long, round, tubular copper pipe, a heavy glass ball, and a fat ferruginous ball, roughly the size of a quar ter of watermelon.\nBack on Earth, $SURNAME0 had a similar machine, identical in all respects to the Martian one, but composed of common, Earthly materials. Both machines had been stolen by $SURNAME0, and both were crated in $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s gene ric Greatscripts office outside Cleveland, Ohio.", "1_3_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>. I liked this. At this hour everything is possible. I loaded these grim thoughts into the back seat and drove them out of the park and onto Ventura Boulevard --old America as it once was, a broad river of asphalt running out of the way. Now it was a road in the jungle, weary with excess. I could hear the water rumbling out of the darkness a hundred yards to my left and knew it was the loud Ventura. If I turned my head and leaned far out to the left I could see the river. But I would not do it because I was concentrating on my driving. \u201cMuch too fast,\u201d my mind said, \u201cthis is a turn--you have to take it slowly.\u201d And I heard the water sucking at the gravel on the riverbottom fifteen feet left and one hundred feet ahead and I took the curve and bowed my head and said \u201cThank you Jesus Christ,\u201d and I was back on the straightaway of death.\nIt was dawn in the fog--layer beyond downtown Los Angeles and the rain had ceased and I drove back to sleep in the hotel. I went straight to the room, took off my boots, unzipped the holster of the gun, put my pistol on...", "0_0_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past midnight</span>.\n\"Too late to start anything tonight,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Let\u2019s wait till the crowd\u2019s cheerful. Maybe we can make back all our money in the next two hours. Maybe we could even make back Mike\u2019s fifty thousand.\"\n\"Who cares about me fifty thou?\" snapped $SURNAME0.\n\"At the rate you\u2019re spending it, you won\u2019t have another cent in\u2014 \"\n\"Skip it,\" said $SURNAME0.\nThey rode the elevator to the top floor and entered the salutary climate of the Museum of Natural History. $SURNAME1 carried the machine-gun in an over-the-shoulder khaki bandolier. The khaki bandolier was camouflage.\nThe sound-track recording was still being transmitted from the basement to the ninth floor. It was still describing the trial, tribulations, and ultimate triumph of the little iron hoof. Just as $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME0 reached the top of the basement stairs, Magzilla, the evil monster in the next cage, flared a gunga stick of skunkweed. It was Magzilla\u2019s reply to the gunga-skunkweed purveyor in the cage next door. The sk...", "2_14_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-four in the afternoon</span>.\nThe great ship was sinking in the dry deserts of Utah. Its crew rotted.\nAboard the ship were two thousand two hundred and thirty cases of combat fatigue.\nMoaning and groaning, the ship of death sailed on, sailing on and on, to a halt at last in the dead skies of Nevada.\nThe troops in the front ranks moaned and groaned as they saw the troops in the rear ranks turning green and fainting. The troops in the second and third ranks vomited.\nBut even the vomited ones had the consolation of having some pals nearby so they didn\u2019t have to look upon the panorama of horror alone. The ones who fainted got no consolation at all.", "0_16_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Few people were in the room.\nThe second crying jag was over.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was sitting now at the white iron table, holding in his hands the severed head of $FIRSTNAMEF1, the antenna of whose antenna he had been. He was posing it on its base so that it could see out the window. He was arranging the features of $FIRSTNAMEF1 tenderly, working the features this way and that with loving fingers.\nThe features would not stay arranged. They kept returning of their own accord to their natural state\u2014a sneering pout.\nAt last, $FIRSTNAMEB0 stuffed the head with a scarf he had taken from his own neck. He held the head in its new position, forced it to stay long enough to imagine the expression that might have been in its empty eyes.\nThe eyes would probably have seemed to $FIRSTNAMEB0 very sad, very sympathetic.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 hadn\u2019t murdered $FIRSTNAMEF1. $FIRSTNAMEF1 had asked him to let her sleep. While $FIRSTNAMEF1 slept, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had let his antenna detach itself. And then he had reposed with his friend, had tried to be happy in that great common grave where everyone in the Martian army had to repose at regular, fifteen-minute intervals\u2014from one to fifteen minutes being the average length of a...", "1_21_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-eight in the evening</span>.\nThe heat shimmered over the Amazonas. One could see the hot air rising from the water. There was hardly any breeze.\nThe dark water moved slowly. There was no moon. It was dark on the edge of the stream.\nAt nine-thirty, the bar of the Hotel Internacional do Brasil moved. Shift after shift of musicians had come and gone on the small bandstand. Five tunes had been played. The last tune was over, and the musicians were putting away their instruments. Only the drums were left now. It was a poor little dance band.\nThey put their instruments into cases that were like small suitcases. They said good-by to each other. Some were drunk. They got on a bus that had come to take them back to the little village that was fifty miles up the Amazon.\nThe drums kept on beating.\nAt ten o\u2019clock it was as dark as it would ever be. At ten o\u2019clock there was no light to shine on the white walls. There was no longer even the light of fires to illuminate the sky. The shacks on the far side of the stream were black hulks, scarcely visible. And at this time it was discovered that the...", "0_17_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>; the sky was black. Three men standing by the door of the building called out to Cohn, who had jumped off his horse and come up the steps in the rain.\n\"Yes, sir!\" shouted Cohn.\nThe men came down the steps.\n\"He\u2019s upstairs, sir,\" one said. \"He won\u2019t last till the ambulance comes.\"\n\"I\u2019ll go in and see him,\" Cohn said.\n\"He doesn\u2019t know anybody, sir.\"\n\"I\u2019ll go in and see him,\" Cohn said. He went up the steps.\nThe three men had left their positions under the arcade. They came back to the top of the steps and stood there under their umbrellas. $FIRSTNAMEB5 came up to them.\n\"Is he badly hurt?\" she asked.\n\"They think so, miss.\"\n\"Will he live?\"\n\"I don\u2019t know, miss. They say he will, but I don\u2019t think so. He won\u2019t know anybody.\"\nI called up to $FIRSTNAMEB5 from where I sat on the wet step, still holding the bags.\n\"Tell Cohn I\u2019m going over to Metro,\" I said. \"I...", "1_13_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one seventeen in the afternoon</span>. That meant a long wait till the last race of the day. I wound the alarm and went back to sleep. It was almost six when I awoke again and heard the noise of the people who put up the tents and booths around the ring. The encampment looked pretty good in the morning, with the tents all gray against the withered grass and the sun coming up behind the canvas.\nIt was still beautiful at six thirty when I hung a wet towel over the broken window-pane and went out the front door to move my truck around to the vacant lot across from the track. A Mexican was pointed out to me, who was running one concessions. He had a bottle of tequila wrapped in a serape, several dried sharks\u2019 fins, and a shaker of salt. He said he expected to gross four hundred dollars that day, and needed some help. I gave him permission to park his wagon on my lot. He was a nice fellow and gave me some bad advice about the girls who worked concessions and slept in the canvas tents near the paddock bar.\n\u201cThose girls get picked up early,\u201d he assured me. \u201cDoan waste your time on them. The two most beautiful girls you dere, they come from...", "0_4_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-three in the morning</span>.\nI found the right floor, took the passkey, and walked down the empty bedroom hallway. The door to Suite 119 was closed, and I pushed the knifeblade clicker on the passkey, but nothing happened. The door was either unlocked or the clicker didn't work. I grinned at the closed door, glad that Remi wasn't around. I tried it again, just to make sure.\nThe handle didn\u2019t turn.\nHmm. I knocked quietly.\nFrom inside I heard somebody say, \u201cWho is it?\u201d\n\u201cDrugs,\u201d I said. \u201c Front desk . . . narcotics sent me.\u201d\nThere was a moment\u2019s hesitation, and then the door opened two inches. A chain lock glinted in the electric bolt. I looked at the beef face behind the chain. I smiled.\n\u201cLet me in,\u201d I said quietly. \u201cI want to use the toilet.\u201d That\u2019s what the passkey said I wanted.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter with yours?\"\n\u201cClogged up. Chief\u2019s orders.\"\n\u201cGet that hellish thing out of my face.", "0_2_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\", .. And so, my friends, you have now heard the first authentic Rumfoordian prophecy\u2014in effect, the only authentic prophecy, since all others presumably have been doctor-inspired lies.\n\"While it is true that we were informed by the message from the Martians what the great truth would be, we in Europe were still subject to the European custom in matters of great truths\u2014we still had to farm it out to an individual to bring it to actuality.\n\"What Europe asked of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was that he carry the truth about futility, about the grand Be-Damned, unworthiness, and pointlessness of it all, to a place, the great standing-room-only entrance to the dump where the Universe discharges its garbage.\n\"And where was this place, my friends?\n\"Why\u2014this place was, is, and always shall be Newport, Rhode Island, U.S.A.,\" said $SURNAME0. \"It is the saddest town God has ever given humans the sense to build.\n\"And there, right there in Newport, right there under the eyes of that sweet woman who was, who is, and who will forever be $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0,...", "0_21_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span> when the Space Wanderer and $FIRSTNAMEB2 sneaked aboard the $FIRSTNAMEM2 Malagamus, intending to surprise $SURNAME0, genuinely surprise him and make him happy. There was a two-minute radio-silence to spare, for the nine-o\u2019clock by radio signal didn\u2019t come until ten after. And during those two minutes on the ship, the Space Wanderer and $FIRSTNAMEB2 quietly boarded and went below, searching for $SURNAME0.\nThey found him in his cabin, dictating a letter to one of his twelve recording machines. He scrawled the words on a gummed strip that ran spirally around the inside of a wheel. He pulled the finished strip off the wheel, stuck it to a little roller, and pushed the roller into a tube.\n\"This is to inform the President,\" $SURNAME0 was saying into the machine, \"that his space intelligentsia is on the verge of defecting to my camp. The leader of the defectors is a man named Elgar who is notorious for disloyalty in his field.\nThe arrival of Elgar signals the end of Earthling supremacy in the Mars attacks\u2014a supremacy that has been maintained by continuous cheating on the part of the Earthling psychologists and technicians responsible...", "1_22_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>. In the basement, on a small table, were seven bottles of German beer. Four of them were empty already. Mike had drunk three bottles' worth of the beer -- three bottles, three quarter-litres, three litre-bottles, and one of them a half-litre bottle. That is to say, Mike had drunk two-thirds of two-thirds of two-thirds of a half-litre bottle of German beer. That makes two-thirds of two-thirds of two-thirds of a half-litre bottle of German beer. But it was all German beer, so you can figure it out for yourself.\n\"Well,\" said Mike, \"what happened? Tell me more. Who did he go home with?\"\n\"With nobody,\" $FIRSTNAMEB5 said.\n\"Stop trimming and splicing.\"\nFor almost a solid hour they had been talking of nothing but Michael Cerisse Langsham, of his youth, his hopes, and his first affair. At the end of it $FIRSTNAMEB5 said she was drunk, and would he get out the beer.\n\"Go on. Who did he go home with?\"\n\"Nobody,\" $FIRSTNAMEB5 said.\n\"Go on, $FIRSTNAMEB5.\"\n\"I won\u2019t tell...", "1_8_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nCarl Creel had had a dream at two o'clock that morning. He was wading in the Ohio River, fishing for blue-gills. He felt a tap on his shoulder and saw that his wife, Betty, was standing there.\n\"What\u2019s the matter, hon ?\" he said. \"Everything\u2019s just fine.\"\nHe woke up and rolled over in bed. His eyes popped. Everything was just exactly the same. She wasn\u2019t standing there anymore. But everything was exactly the same. The spacesuits, the shopping lists, the telephones, the alarms, and the beautiful, clean, furnished home with great chunks of good Connecticut shade-tree in every room. Where, he wondered piteously, would it all lead? Where would he be? Where would she be? Where would all these honking fellows be? What, oh, what? he wondered. What will become of us? Where will it all lead? Who knows? Who knows? he thought. Who knows? he repeated as he got up and started off to another day. Who knows?\nAt this very minute one of the social scientists at AT&T was slowly and cautiously switching from whisky to beer in order to see...", "1_5_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five eighteen in the morning</span> and I should be home in bed. I walked on to the main room, where a long chrome and leather bar ran the length of the hall. A bartender in a white coat stood behind the bar reading a newspaper.\nI sat down on one of the stools. \u201cGood morning,\u201d said the bartender. \u201cWhat can I get you?\u201d There was no menu. All drinks were 50\u00a2. I ordered a Bloody Mary.\n\u201cQuite a party,\u201d said the bartender, peeking over his paper.\nI agreed, but said nothing more. A man next to me ordered a grapefruit juice and whisky, straight up.\n\u201cLots of Conventioneers,\u201d said the bartender in his friendly way. I winked up, but he couldn\u2019t understand.\n\u201cConventioneers?\u201d he asked, smiling. \u201cI\u2019m afraid I don\u2019t understand.\"\n At that moment, the band in the lounge above us fell apart. It was like a 4th of July on the shores of hell. Explosions, screams, firecrackers.\n\u201cWhoops!\u201d said the bartender, cocking his ear. \u201cSounds like our...", "1_22_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-three in the evening</span>, I was in the big amphitheatre, Level B, down in front about fifty feet from the Red Celestial Flame. There was an opening aggie\u2014medley of Shreveport tunes: \u201cHello Little Schoolgirl,\u201d \u201cMilk Cow Blues,\u201d \u201cHas Anybody Seen My Girl,\u201d \u201cI'm Your Hoochie Coochie Man.\u201d I stood up, danced around. Lot of people were dancing in the aisles. With my finger I dug the scene, swung my arm in wide circles, sang along with all the oldies. \u201cI'm your hoochie coochie man,\u201d I yelled. I turned around and around, snapping my fingers, squeezing the woman beside me. She was a local Texas gal, and I had her in a sensual conga-line that swung around the aisles and ended in little circles and balled-up tempo changes and had everybody grinning. In the meantime Fontana and his group finished the aggie medley and started into \u201cLow Spark of High Heeled Boys.\u201d I went to the bar and bought another load for the woman I was standing with; I squeezed her and we went back to dancing...", "1_12_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 now moved apart to arm themselves. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s old rifle, the one thing that had really belonged to him, was found to have been looted. The replacement rifle was something he had never laid eyes on before.\nWith a despairing shrug, he took it and retreated behind a rock outcropping. There, with shaking hands, he tore open a wax-paper belt of ammunition, loaded the mechanism.\nPieces of the harmonium bullet were found inside $FIRSTNAMEB1. And so his magic suit gurgled up and away from him. The big billiard ball dropped to the ground, rolled to $FIRSTNAMEB1, stopped.\nPrufrock and Statman and the two Stonehouses were dead within two minutes after the shooting started. Their place behind the outcropping was like Lake Michigan beaches.\nRansom and Saltonstall, who were the last to fall, managed to turn their place into the plains of Scutari. Two hundred yards away lay all the world Al, mate of $FIRSTNAMEB0, had ever known.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wept. He put his face down on the stocks of the rifle, wept into them as though they were the familiar face of a lover.\nThe tears cleared...", "2_11_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-four in the morning</span>.\nKensington Gore was leaving $SURNAME1\u2019s Castle. She was wearing roller skates and an ill-fitting antilarval hood. She was pulling a homemade wooden sled, on which rested a badly insulated food warmer and several spray guns, mounted in a rack. She was attacked by the north wind, like all men before her who had come out of $SURNAME1\u2019s Castle to sell protection against the sun.\nThe cold sliced through her so thoroughly that she could hardly see.\nBeeber remained on the wall as long as he possibly could. No doubt he wanted to melt into the woman\u2019s shadow and descend unseen back into the tunnels.\nBut there was no way to melt.\nThe woman\u2019s shadow lay on the stones like an antilarval mat. The wind sliced through the shadow.\nAnd Beeber had to hang on the wall an awfully long time, since the woman remained on her graceful blue skates, wobbling in the gusts, bending with them, letting them blow right through her, refusing to hurry an inch.", "1_14_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-eight in the afternoon</span> when I parked in front of my attorney\u2019s house in North Las Vegas. I sat in the car for a while, thinking. The thought I was thinking was inspired by the presence of that BMW in the driveway. It was the second time I\u2019d seen it there...and the timing was too damned coincidental to be accepted as a coincidence. I had, of course, reported the car stolen from the parking lot of the Mandrake Hotel in Las Vegas on the night after my attorney had first talked to me in my office about buying a monkey. It now seemed evident that he had stolen the car himself and had been driving it around the whole time I\u2019d reported it missing....alone with his secrets and his terrible sense of humor, waiting for me to fall into this trap.\nI\u2019d said one mean thing to Lucy, at the end of our shortest wedding\u2014even though she had been there all the while, patiently, while I pushed the white birds into a circle around my suitcase and explained that our marriage was finished. I had said: \u201cYour husband is a hopeless drug addict.\u201d Some cheap, careless remark. / can\u2019t remember exactly what I said...but that\u2019s what I meant.", "0_6_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-one in the morning</span>. The police had numbered the prisoners in order to expedite the lines of communication. Number 457 had been made to understand that he was in grave danger of losing his life. He wondered who she was who had given him the message\u2014and if the message was a lie.\nHe was forty-six years old, blue-eyed, a proud man who had once been a famous musician.\nHe was very tired, and his great fear had robbed him of all dignity.\nThe by-products of the roar of battle had at last penetrated his consciousness. He was in the midst of confusion that had become chaotic. He did not know, for instance, whether he should keep his hands in plain sight as he had been ordered to do, or whether he should make the best possible use of his hands, to protect his ears for instance, by keeping them cupped against the uproar in his head. No one had told him anything, and the plans of the Martians were not evident to the most capable Earthling.\nThe time was exactly 6:41 A.M., let us remember, when Martian Number 176 was given the first order to commit what on Earth would have been called suicide.\nIt was done with a telepathic shout.\nIt was done...", "1_13_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-seven in the afternoon</span> and I was in a telephone booth in Sherman, Texas, when I made the final decision to go to Las Vegas and look up Terry. Much later I wondered how I could have possibly justified this monumental decision in the twisted, crushed and altered state of mind I was in at that point. But at the moment it seemed like a perfectly logical move\u2014and now, as I look back on that afternoon, I can see why a lot of young men from all over the country always end up in Las Vegas.\nMy car was broken down and ninety-four miles away in El Paso. I had no money and no Diners Club card. There would have been no sense in trying to sell the goddamn toothbrush, so I left it in the bathroom at the Sands. I have never gambled in Las Vegas, but this did not deter me in the slightest from heading directly for the downtown bus depot and buying a ticket for the next Autobus to Vegas.\nTo get this ticket I had to stay awake for six hours. I jammed pots of boiling coffee into my stomach. I desperately, and rather pathetically, tried to revive my rotten head by sniffing huge gouts of lemon air-freshener, which I found behind the driver\u2019s seat...", "1_19_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-four in the evening</span>, Braddocks said; they sat in the bar, ordered more beers, and called Nevada. Seven fifty-three, said the operator; the time of the next hop across the continental US. For the hell of it, the Americans called LA. Two minutes. The time of their flight the next day. The big Boeing was humming back and forth across the states, lifting most of its weight out of the west, letting down most of its weight onto the east.\nThey hit New York like princes; they even took a cab from Kennedy. $FIRSTNAMEB3 shelled out an astounding twenty-five dollars and took another twenty out on Chiva. I waited for them in front of Burton's apartment and gave them the keys to the door. Everything was taken care of for the night. We were leaving for Chi later in the week. I bought a six-pack of beer and took the bus to the airfield. I sat and watched the great planes take off and land on their way from the Coast. I enjoyed it and forgot about the thousand troubles ahead. Traffic became absolutely overwhelming; there were cars from Camden, Maine, in the north, to Mobile, Alabama, in the south, cars full of families and couples, with little kids who didn't give a t...", "1_8_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>, Earth-time, and the space ship was closing in on the beacon. It was by radio the size of a pea, on top of Old North Church, in Boston. The ship was plunging through space at a steady 23,120 miles an hour.\nIt descended to 53 miles above the Earth.\nAnd there it turned on its flying fins, oriented itself, and dove down at a ten-degree angle.\nThe roar of the rocket jets ceased. The jets had consumed their fuel. The result was neutral buoyancy. The space ship, weighing 57,423 pounds, was neither rising nor falling. A moment before, the air had been shattered by the thunder of the motors. Now there was silence, the quiet of a morning in late spring.\nThe route of the space ship had taken it over the Virginia coast at Cape Charles, north of Chincoteague, where a great gray horse could be seen sometimes standing offshore, water up to its sides, rolling its great eyes as it gazed at the Earth. Farther north was Wallop\u2019s Island and its famous bird sanctuary, where the trees sometimes whistled in the wind, and the rare yellow-breasted nighthawk nested, and the great black-bellied...", "0_12_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-two post meridiem</span>, after noon. At floor level in the closed drug-men\u2019s supplies chest in the lobby, where the rules required it to be, the capsule of Tennessee Williams nestled in breast pocket of Metallurgical Hooper\u2019s white lab coat as snugly as a fetus in a womb.\nFortunately, one need not understand Chinese to be moved by what happened next.\nA tour group of about seventy-five women, each armed with a Visa card, had just plunged like an amoeba through the glass doors from Fifty-seventh Street. The women wandered in clots among the displays, but only one appeared to be picking up anything. She was a chunky little grandmother type in blue sneakers and a sweatshirt which said JESUS SAVES. Several items now dangled from her arms: a suction-cup dartboard, a chrome snowplow, an aluminum beer-can denouncer, and The Reverend Ike\u2019s TM Made Easy For You To Know, a pocket-sized course in transcendental mediation. The cardigan pocket of her sweatshirt bulged suspiciously. The gross total of her purchases came to $5.71\u2014which is to say, even at regular retail prices, more than the...", "0_5_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-nine in the morning</span>. The space was a dirty room in an abandoned nursey in an abandoned mansion somewhere in the damp and dismal reaches of the San Bernardino Mountains. The boy was five years old, red-featured, stringy-haired, snaggle-toothed, handsome, healthy, amazingly alert and intelligent-seeming, and possessed, so far as I could tell, of both a sense of humor and a sense of mutuality. He was, to put it briefly and bluntly, cute as all hell.\nHe was the son of a beatnik chick I\u2019d been living with out in Topanga Canyon. Her name was Babe, short for Something-or-other. She had enormous, soul-ravishing eyes. She was a poet. She hated money. She hated clothes. She hated practically everything. She was young, only twenty. I first met her down by the Security First National Bank in Vernon in August of 1960. She was my fantasy made reality. I was smitten. She had a little girl, my son, a miracle begotten by a lonesome and disturbed wanderer in the concrete wilderness of L.A. society.\nI spent five dollars on breakfast, and the waitress couldn\u2019t decide whether...", "1_19_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-one in the evening</span>. I walked into the lobby and all the men looked up to see the spectacle of a young man of twenty-nine years of age who, on the subject of liquor, is a constant inspiration to himself.\nI took a sip from a bottle in my overcoat pocket, then another, as I leaned over the cashier\u2019s cage, then another as I strolled across the lobby toward the elevators. The fact that I had already taken four martinis in the bar did not disturb me in the least. I was proceeding under the theory that the sips, instead of being counted, should be taken as they came.\nThe elevator let me off at my suite. I was so busy sipping that I almost collided with the door. I put my hand inside my overcoat and let myself in, then took another sip as I kicked the door shut behind me. I tossed my hat and overcoat on a chair and made for the bathroom, stopping on the way to tap a book of matches on a drinking glass. I lit a cigarette, then burned a family of four matches for a sip of whisky, planning to switch to ice as soon as the water became sufficiently warm.\nI was really cooking. The steam in the bathroom was thick enough...", "2_11_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the drawing room of the palace on Titan, the moon of Saturn. The mean distance from the earth to Saturn, the planet $SURNAME0 lived on most of the time, was twenty-one billion miles. Titan, the moon, was three billion miles from Saturn.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog were materializations again.\nThe great Mauser Rifle that had materialized beside $SURNAME0 was leaning against the wall of the drawing room. It had no function now, unless a show of force was needed in a hurry. It was not going to be leveled at anyone unless a member of the editorial board of The influence carried signs of a intention to kill $SURNAME0 in the interests of the April Fool\u2019s conspiracy against him.", "1_12_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past noon</span>. His left arm hurt. $SURNAME1 didn\u2019t need a wristwatch to know that. His body told him so. The muscles of his left arm ached as if he\u2019d been propping a sagging floor under a leaking roof all night long.\n$SURNAME1 relived in his mind the complex story of his left-arm pain. His shoulder had begun to hurt first\u2014a slow, grinding ache that made him hold his shoulder in a certain way while he slept. He had awakened to find his arm not yet hurting, and as the dawn grew stronger and stronger his left arm had begun to throb softly, in pulse-like throbs, as if something had crawled in under the skin and were beating out a message in Morse code. $SURNAME1 was an amateur radio operator. And just before the pain in his arm had become apparent to him, as he lay in bed, he had sent and received, to and from a friend in Pasadena, California, a message in Morse code. The pain in his arm, he was sure, was a response in kind.\nIt was a good feeling while it lasted. $SURNAME1 did not groan aloud, but he made low, exotic sounds in his throat, the sounds of a man experiencing a privileged glimpse...", "0_16_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>\u2014on Thursday, November 11, 1947\u2014when all heaven broke loose over New Mexico.\nAt that precise moment, behind the wheel of his convertible on Highway 66, Alfred Randall Johnson saw the sky flash purple\u2014then green\u2014then purple again.\nHe was driving west at 55 miles per hour, wondering where in hell he could get a drink in this one-horse town. At first, he thought he'd gone crazy. Then he braked the car to a halt and rolled down the window to get a better look.\nYes, something unusual was happening. All across the sun a purple arc had appeared\u2014like a purple lipstick smear on the sun. And, as he watched, the western half of the sun dropped out of sight! And that wasn't all.\nThe sky was changing every second.\nRed\u2026blue\u2026green\u2026red\u2026purple\u2026green\u2026red. They ranged over the sky like ocean waves.\nA bartender came running out of the El Patio bar and looked, too. He banged on the hood of the car.\n\"Radio said hold tight!\" he screamed, scared. \"There\u2019s a solar storm on!\"\n\"What the hell!\" Johnson yelled.\n\"Watch!\" screamed the bartender.", "1_22_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-eight in the evening</span> as $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 reached over that tremendous trestle to rescue Stony Stevenson\u2019s life. And it was the instant of the end of the world.\nAs $SURNAME1 reached, the book of Kee-kee-taw\u2019s prophecies fell from the pocket of his brown uniform. The book hit the iron rung directly beneath $SURNAME1\u2019s reaching hand.\nThe book made a hell of a cracking sound. It broke like a great decrepit snail shell, opening to the end.\nThere was a page missing.\nThe page was number 89\u2014the page on which had appeared $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s own bad fortune, the page on which had appeared Stony Stevenson\u2019s good fortune.\nOn the rim of the great broken book that now dangled from the bottom of a railroad trestle was the message that had appeared on the missing page:\nIn $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s right hand was a square, gray stone on the surface of which was a message etched in fire. It read simply, EFFECTIVELY SOONER THAN YOU THINK.\nIn $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s left hand was Stony Stevenson\u2019s left wrist, bearing the identification bracelet of a member of the Space...", "0_9_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The time was coming near for the Martians to appear on the ventilation plates inside the bombs. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had arranged things to happen exactly on time.\nIt had been an easy matter for him to insert the shutter blades into the little cylinders.\nThe beauty of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s situation was that no one could see him. He was free to act, free to move about the grounds of the $SURNAME0 Mansion quietly and invisibly.\nOne of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s pleasures was to go through the wailing, skulking crowd of terror-stricken people outside the iron fence. He enjoyed being invisible even to other members of the crowd\u2014to be, like $SURNAME0 and the martyrs, scrutable only to the Prophet.\nAt this point, to be sure, $FIRSTNAMEB2 found himself sharing the crowd\u2019s terror of the Martians about to crawl out of their crannies inside the nuclear bombs. But having had the material for the Martian costumes delivered to the pool-house\u2014after the way he had handled it\u2014he didn\u2019t at all mind sharing in the crowd\u2019s fantasy that the Martians were about to appear.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was pleased with the shuttles and chains of individuals...", "0_9_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-four in the morning</span>, and La Vadare was in a semi-official drugged sleep that lasted late into the day. The few pedestrians in the Rue du Sommer Sauvaig, students wearing ear-beads, and members of a small, secret religious sect, identifiable by their shaven heads, shy smiles, and blue work shirts, ambled past the caf\u00e9 windows, hurrying to get out of the sun and into the musty, shuttered coolness of the street\u2019s religious supply shops.\nOutside the pitiful walls of the Martian Embassy a thirsty crowd of tourists and newspaper reporters was doing everything possible to stay awake.\nLenore Beadsman of 233 syllable Lane,Bethel, Ohio, appeared just inside the caf\u00e9 door at nine thirty-eight, her flight from the airport having been delayed by storms, and smiled at a pair of large policemen as she passed them. They came to rigid attention. Lenore went directly to the counter and ordered a cappuccino from the professional non-smiler behind it. Her appearance had the immediate effect of distracting the attention of about half the customers in the caf\u00e9 from their own affairs and sending it to her. The policemen brought the other half of the distraction. The smiles of the customers...", "0_14_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\n\"Damn,\" said an elderly woman with a big hat, when the taxi-driver told her that the fare was eighteen-fifty. \"I thought the fare was only fifteen cents, the same as in New York.\"\n\"Hell of a note, lady,\" said the cabby. \"I been to New York lots of times.\"\nThat taxi cost ten dollars and fifty cents, because Carol bought the tote for a quarter, and three dollars for the tip for hall-boy, elevator boy, and nickel for the bag-boy.\nShe wondered where all her money had gone. She still had two dollars, and she had not bought sleeping-car fare from New York to San Francisco. She wished she were traveling with her young husband, who never counted his money.\nIt felt good to be alone in the clean, white train with solid people around her and to be going across New Jersey in April sunshine. She bought a blue book about California and a guide to San Francisco, and amused herself reading about Stanford's Santa Rose campus and the orange groves, and the cliff houses, and Muir Woods, and the big-game hunting and the real-estate opportunities. The Kansas City architect and builder who commissioned the book obviously...", "0_1_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-three in the morning</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time. The place was a neat kitchen in a split-level home in Townkok, $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s Cove, Mars. On the happy face side of the dishwasher was a bright blank-eyed smiley-face button with the words HAVE A NICE DAY printed below. The wash cycle that would take an hour was now on delay. Unseen inside the machine were two glasses, a beer bottle, an Old Crow bottle, an empty Jim Beam bottle, an empty Wild Turkey bottle, an empty Ballantine\u2019s bottle, an empty Courvoisier II bottle, and an unopened can of Pabst. Even though this array of glasses had been much appreciated by the drinker, no one was now watching. No one was in the kitchen, and no one was at home in the neighborhood. The top of the machine, with the happy face, was being pressed into a melancholy frown by the fluttering of the Mars\u30d0 suborbital lander\u2019s gossamer wings.\nThe lander was the emergency vehicle of a young man named $FIRSTNAMEF1, who had hung one of the most successful bottle parties ever in $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s Cove. He was now stowing himself, his true love, and...", "1_8_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-seven in the morning</span> of June twenty-third. It is appropriate to mention that the first atomic bomb exploded in New Mexico exactly at this same hour. As Sterling tried to look businesslike, there was a scramble of $SURNAME0\u2019s well-oiled crew. Two servants carried in a cube-shaped case the exact size of an ordinary garden rake. A third brought in an ordinary housefly.\nThe new President of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent saw these things at the same time with Sterling, and he put the rake and the fly to instant symbolic use. \"Pardon me an instant\u2014pah-dun moi an instant\u2014\" murmured $SURNAME0. \"Yes\u2014\" he said. \"Yes, you see before you a rake and a fly. And so we proceed.\"\nHis voice, then, changed to the voice of a children\u2019s show announcer. \"And now, children,\" he said, \"let\u2019s watch the Wilbert PhDanny Show\u2014Who\u2019s Got Faith? let\u2019s find out who\u2019s got faith? Wilbert? Would you step this way, please? And \u2026 Miss Priss, would you step this way, please?\"\n$SURNAME0 clapped his...", "1_12_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve sixteen post meridiem</span>.\nThe 27th day of February in the year of one thousand nine hundred and seventy-six of the Christian era.\nThe sum being frowned away.\nOr so he supposed.\nONE TWENTY-SEVEN, ONE TWENTY-EIGHT, ONE TWENTY-NINE\nWe\u2019ve come to the part of the story that Mike knew. The part of the story where the things he had been told by Pierce in Rio and in the Adriatic came into play.\nOnce there was a man named $FIRSTNAMEB2, who lived on Earth. He had a theory about life. In his theory it was a circular motion, rather like a windmill\u2019s. There was only one action in life, $FIRSTNAMEB2 thought, and that was applause. Whenever something happened in anybody\u2019s life, anybody\u2019s universe, anybody\u2019s anything at all, what happened was that somebody applauded. And the clapper of that inspirational burst of sound decided what the next thing in the clockwork would be. Nobody really had any choice.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s clockwork universe was bigger and more complex than Earth. He couldn\u2019t have fitted the whole thing into his head, of course...", "2_11_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until noon</span>. The place was the fire control room of a turbine-driven Martian space ship. The space ship, with a crew and a payload of two thousand pounds of high explosive, was orbiting Mars at an altitude of fifty thousand feet. The purpose of the payload was to demolish a large part of the water reclamation plant at $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s estate on Mars. The purpose was to immobilize the war effort of the Newport-London axis for at least six months.\nThe commander of the ship was Helmholtz. He was demonstrating the effects of his best weapons on himself, the effects being mental rather than physical.\nOld Salter was the only member of the completo crew besides Helmholtz. \"This is absolutely the very last time I take these things, Salter,\" said Helmholtz, \"and even so, I don\u2019t know how in blazes I ever let you talk me into taking them.\" He put two pellets into his mouth. \"Yech,\" he said.\n\"I know how you feel, skipper\u2014\" said Salter.\n\"How do you know?\" said Helmholtz. He snapped his jaws shut on the pellets. \"God\u2014 these things are awful.\"", "1_17_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-five in the afternoon</span>.\nThey rented the station wagon and headed for San Diego and the Sombrero Deluxe Hotel. Mucho turned the radio on, tinnily playing rock and roll music. A news bulletin announced that the remains of Colonel Franklin O. Bashmonger, inventor of the famous World War II fighter-plane, the F.B.-I, had been found in a pasture in upstate New York. The pasture was radioactive. \u201cAnd now,\u201d said Mucho, \u201cwe\u2019re onto San Onofre and the big slalom.\u201d He whooped. Then came the big surprise of the day. As they were crossing a little bridge, they heard a cough, the motor died, and Mucho said, \u201cAw, rats!\u201d to a perfectly good 1947 Hudson. They coasted to the side of the road, under a pepper tree. \"Just a coincidence,\u201d said Mucho. \u201cThis is where me and Bo Peep broke down on the way up to Berkeley.\u201d They got out of the car. A snagged fold of wire, caught around a spark-plug boot, was vibrating in the ocean breeze. In five minutes, Nooney had it fixed. They...", "0_23_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty in the evening</span>, and they were south of Des Moines now, already making ready to cross the state line into California. Suddenly $FIRSTNAMEB3 leaped up in exultation and made motions to notify all bars. The haberdashery was $33 short. This should be corrected by this time. We rushed to the first bar in sight that was open all night, a sad place with a few fairies around and a TV set hung high on the wall. \u201cAh, gentlemen,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3 directly to the bartender. \u201cKindly don't give me change, so I can make it last and be congenial with my brother and his sweet new wife, and also we are on the most important final trek to Denver and points West and desire to have final easygoing snifters on the house.\u201d He smiled his most angelic smile and handed over the twenty plus the other bills for the haberdashery. He got change, but no drink.\n\u201cWhat do you mean, no drink?\u201d\n\u201cI mean this is Kansas and houses have to have a liquor license to serve drinks after eleven o'clock.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3's jaw fell. We beat it to the first roadside bar behind the gas station. The...", "2_12_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-four p.m.</span>\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind was so blank that, when the explosion came, the noise it made was like the explosion of silence.\nThe explosion:\nSomeone knocked.\nSome one knocked gently on the stiff, thick door of the airtight cabin. And the knock, having been allowed to penetrate to the very bottom of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind, stimulated a response that had lain there for months.\nIt was the response to an invitation to enter.\nIt wasn\u2019t an invitation to enter a space ship, to enter a war, to destroy the enemy by means of an intelligent bomb. It was an invitation to enter a world that existed somewhere beyond the awful lettering on the door.\nAnd the lettering said:\nThis cabin is airtight.\nIn case of an emergency decompression, punch button under your seat.\nSurvivors are requested to remain in their seats\nexcept when smoking or wrestling. $FIRSTNAMEB0 pressed his thumb against the button under his seat.", "0_17_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-three in the afternoon</span> when one of the clerks in the International Section of the Holy Redeemer Hospital called the local SRO\u2014the state, county, and city authorities\u2014and told them there were three dead bodies in Room 346. The three bodies were the corpses of three male Caucasians. All had apparently died within the previous five or ten minutes. None of the corpses appeared to have been robbed.\nOne of the three was a middle-aged man who had obviously died from natural causes.\nThe second was an older man, about fifty, who had been shot with a .45. His death had been instantaneous and probably painless. The bullet had gone in one side of his head and out the other.\nThe third was a blond young man, twenty-one and good-looking, who had diedgame. His clothes were soaked with dry-cleaned hospital disinfectant. He had been stabbed many times in the body and in the face with an unknown thin, sharp instrument. His face had been partially skinned with a knife. He still had both his rings and all his money. He had fought hard for his life.\nThin dermafilm on his finger pads showed he was addicted to ether. He had recently been in a hospital. There was...", "1_3_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-eight in the morning</span>, Easter Sunday, and Salo, like a true friend, did not try to wake young $FIRSTNAMEB2. Rather, he landed his ship as softly as possible in a little glade of Tralfamadore grass, broke only a few shoot tips, and, carrying $FIRSTNAMEB2 under one arm, proceeded quietly to the lectern. His purpose was to let $FIRSTNAMEB2 sleep as long as possible\u2014rouse him for the egg-hunting expedition, which wouldn\u2019t take all morning.\nThere would be time, after the egg-transferring operation, for a nap.\nThe time was now four fifteen A.M. Earth Eastern Standard Time.\nSalo always took advantage of a few minutes of silence before delivering an important address to a friend. He liked to look around at the sky and marvel at the gross indifference of everything to the things that he and his friends thought so important.\nThere wasn\u2019t a thing in the Universe that gave a damn what was said or done at the lectern, Salo thought. His assumption was gloriously correct. Had he thought otherwise, Tralfamadore itself would have crumbled.\nNow the only thing worth noticing in the Universe was the bright star in the Tralfamadorian...", "0_5_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-five in the morning</span>. Our terrible trip was almost ended. I took one look out the door at the toadstool houses and the early morning traffic and knew I would never have to come to Las Vegas in my entire--life. Nothing I had ever seen before could possibly have prepared me for this nightmare journey into the very heart of gambling America, this terrible centrum ex relinqiume of all hope and greed in the Amazing Lolornoness of the American Dream. The Red Shark thundered out of town at sixty miles an hour. The dawns of all our futures faded behind us. I watched the road map roll up into a ball and tumbled into the back seat, together with my share of the two lbs. of spicy sausage, the two lbs. of cheese and the eighteen quarts of beer. At this point I was completely unaware that the map had been pinned underneath the spare tire. The way we tore across the dawnlit desert I half expected to see scrap metal and flapping tire treads. I expected to open my eyes any second now and see us skidding diagonally across a broad falaise whizzing along on nothing but the backend of the Red Shark ... the front end bent and ripped off someplace along the way .", "0_14_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>. I was due at the foundry in forty-seven minutes. I\u2019d dumped my whole day\u2019s pay, ninety-four dollars, on the blanket next to me. For a moment I was about to flash back on all that insane money to buy a ticket to something like \u201cHumboldt y las expulsiones de monasterio: Traducido despues del Codice Casanatense . . .\u201d or else \u201c . . . una sonata densa y pesada, una carga que aturde y embota como cien solemnidades anuales en basilicas desiertas: mohair y ambrosia, manicura de luna, placer de Cumas y otras cosas.\u201d\nBut instead I bought four tickets on the next thing smoking to Vegas. I wanted to be there for the Grand Finale; besides, somebody stole my fucking car. The plan was to meet my attorney around four, then finalize arrangements for what I figured would be a suicide run onanova in Vegas with the Shark, the Samoan and Fablon\u2019s possessed Bentley. It occurred to me that there might be time to make one...", "0_23_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been awake for a hundred and thirty-seven of those minutes. And he had a hundred and thirty-eight more minutes to go. And the fencings and sonar warning devices outside his cave seemed as solidly massive and impenetrable as ever.\nMaybe, thought $FIRSTNAMEB0, there just wasn\u2019t any way out.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 sniffled in his fear and loneliness.\nHis feet felt very cold in the wet footbath. That was bad, for a Martian never took cold feet for granted. The worst thing about feet was that they were so foul-tasting when frozen.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wanted to thaw his feet. He wanted a real drink of water, too. Water from a bottle was best. Rainwater was second-best. Distilled water was almost as good as rainwater. Spring water was almost as good as distilled water. Well water was almost as good as spring water. Surface water was almost as good as well water. Fountain water was almost as good as surface water. River water was almost as good as fountain water. Tap water was almost as good as river water. Ice was almost as good as tap water. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had once actually drunk melted ice. The ice had formed at the...", "1_8_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight seventeen in the morning</span>.\nThe whole adventure had taken less than a minute. $FIRSTNAMEF0 yanked $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s hand. \"Come on!\" she said. \"Run!\"\nAs the mother-and-son team scuttled away, back at the Spatula table, the people of Newport, in mercy, closed the eyelids of old $SURNAME0, whose agonized trance had given him no glimpse of the pieces of the catastrophe he had authored.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 raced out of town, up the wild old blast-ended road through the forest beyond.\nTHERE IS A CHERRY ORCHARD on Mars. It stands as straight and pretty and innocent as if it belonged to Eden. It is named in honor of the Prophet $FIRSTNAMEM1, who began life as a trellis-level worker in that orchard.\nThere are seventeen thousand three hundred and three people in Newport, the nearest city to the orchard. They don\u2019t like the Prophet\u2019s religion much\u2014and the strange thing is that the religion hasn\u2019t changed much, even after all the prophets have been shown to be frauds.\nThe religion remains an aristocratic, puritanical cult of contempt for working people. Its...", "1_0_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past midnight</span>.In a minute the Space Wanderer would be born.\nThe crowd let out a soft scream of anticipation.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 stood at attention, didn\u2019t blink, didn\u2019t move.\nA trumpet moaned in the palace.\n\"Long live the Space Wanderer!\" cried the ventriloquist.\nThe crowd cheered.\nAnd the Space Wanderer was born, was stepped down to the square to accept, amid the cheers of the crowd, his greatest title. The baby in the receiving blanket stared at $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chevrons. The baby sucked a dummy and stared.\nDid the dummy soothe the Space Wanderer?\nYes, said the crowd. The dummy soothed the Space Wanderer at once.\nThe dummy was a magic dummy\u2014disclosed the ventriloquist.\nThe dummy was coated with iron filings, said the ventriloquist. The filings attracted magnetic dust, formed a magnet that soothed the Space Wanderer. That was the secret of the dummy\u2019s soothing power.\nWhat do we know about the magic dust that was the dummy\u2019s power? whispered the ventriloquist. What do we know? he whispered. What do we know...", "0_21_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine o'clock in the evening</span>. We walked away from Hotel Crillon to a music-hall. That was something Pop wanted to see. It was a fauxbourg Montmartre French music-hall. There was a girl of about thirteen dressed like a young lady of twenty-five. She stood on the stage and crooned sentimental songs and then she went before the footlights, made elaborate curtsies, and sang more songs, while two comedians wandered around her, with raised hats and double bows, looking as though they were committing suicide by degrees. She went out through a shower of napkins, handkerchiefs, cups, saucers, and sugar-bowls to little tables where her adorers could sit and talk to her and buy her drinks. Pop was all fascinated.\n\u201cHey!\u201d he called out. \u201cLet's go up and talk to that kid.\u201d\n\u201cShe's not so bad, is she?\u201d\n\u201cCome on, kid, let's sit at your table.\u201d\n\u201cWell,\u201d I said, \u201cwhat do you think of it?\u201d\n\u201cPretty funny. Hey! Come on, let's go up.\u201d\n\u201cGo ahead,...", "2_12_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after noon</span>. Six feet above $SURNAME0\u2019s head hung a marvellous blue-white Sun.\nIt was the Sun of Mercury, vividly sunlight that had been refracted through Earthlingmade glass.\nThe Sun was three feet in diameter and weighed five hundred pounds. It hung there by a bent, pint-sized girder that had been put in place the day before by one of $SURNAME1\u2019s own bulldozers.\nThe smell of the wives in the trench was now overpowering.\n\"Give me air!\" said $SURNAME0. He fanned his face with his gloved hands, winked roguishly at $SURNAME1. \"You know how long I\u2019ve held my breath?\" he said. \"Five minutes and forty seconds. My personal best.\" He fanned some more. \"Well\u2014\" he said, \"I guess this is about as far as our friendship goes.\" He raised one hand above his head. The hand was holding an electric blackjack. He pressed a knob on the side of the blackjack. A charge of two thousand volts rushed down a wire and into the blackjack\u2019s electrode.\nThe electrode was an inch from $SURNAME1\u2019s right eye.", "0_0_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after midnight</span>. Even before the echoes of the chimes had died the boys were on their feet and moving toward the door, laughing and shoving cynically into one another as they went. They were all drunk. Inside of ten minutes the house was empty.\nJason, who was the only one not drunk, brought the car up to the door for five of them at once, and in a rocking and polka-dotted hubbub they went off in different directions. Only Robert Cohn wasn\u2019t going. He took a chair and set it against the wall under one of the meaningless pictures of the D\u00f4me that had been there for years. He sat down at the table where we had been wetting our beer and speaking of things thatmade us laugh and raise our goblets. I went over to the bar and sold my glass, and standing there I saw Cohn sitting against the wall, his goblet filled with beer, his long legs stretched out in front of him. $FIRSTNAMEB5 was gone. I never saw her again.\nShe must have gone off with Mike or with Bill. Even while I was thinking this I saw Cohn take his glass and start to raise it. Then I saw something else. $FIRSTNAMEB5 came in and sat down with Cohn. She...", "2_18_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-four in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nOld $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4, like the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclast, were nowhere.\nThe Church of God the Utterly Indifferent would lose track of them, too.\nThe memory of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 would warp and become frayed, would disintegrate and blow away.\nOld $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 themselves, as if they had never existed, would cease to dream.\nNo one has ever been singled out for special attention and demonstrated the capacity to make a real difference on the spiraling and futile way of things.\nCHAPTER THREE\nIt was a bright, clear Saturday morning on Mars. The rainfall of the previous twenty-four Martian hours had been all of four hundredths of an inch. The average temperature on Mars is 55\u00b0 F. The average rainfall is 4.05 inches a year.\nTruman Watches, the lemur who had succeeded old Bronson, had not awakened Mr. integrated-circuit Barn-house at 6 A.M. The sun was already high over the twinkling villages of Mars.\nWhere a million Earthlings were huddled, there was nothing of distinctive color or shape.", "0_19_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-three in the evening</span>, and a message from the surface of Tralfamadore told $FIRSTNAMEF1 or anybody else on Tralfamadore at the time that spaceships from the home planet of Tralfamadore were in the neighborhood and eager to establish contact with anybody from Tralfamadore.\nThe manual indicated ways of replying to the message: a message in morse code could be flashed in the clear; two rockets going in opposite directions could start the handshake.\nThere were no rockets, and there were no morse code keys, and there was nothing clear at all.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 put her helmet back on, admitted oxygen to the suit to pressurize it, sloshed to the ladder in the wall, ascended the ladder, admitted oxygen to the airlock to pressurize it, and admitted herself to the outside.\nThe suit was tight and stiff. The transparent plastic of the helmet was fogged.\nShe ripped the suit from her body, donned brown corduroy slacks and a green mohair sweater instead, got a white helmet from a closet, set a brown leather bag on the plain barrel of her callused foot, and rode on a bicycle out of Newport, Rhode Island, and into Warwick, Rhode Island, and...", "0_6_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-four in the morning</span> now, and at that very moment $FIRSTNAMEB2 was being questioned by Federal Interrogation Bureau agents in an apartment on Red River Street, in Austin, Texas.\nThey were from the U.S. Martian Liaison Office. They displayed Secret Service badges, being primarily intelligence officers detailed to work with the Bureau. An M.L.O. agent named Newport News introduced a colleague as Mr. Gross.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had been easily lured to the apartment. Troops out of Fort Worth had picked him up in a bar on Sixth Street.\n\"Always plenty of them around,\" Newport News told Gross. \"These cowboys think they\u2019re big stuff. Most of them are going to be in the big rodeos between here and Kansas City. Where they travel with the rodeo.\"\n\"These ole rodeos sure have changed,\" said Gross.\n\"I\u2019m a South Texas Texican, myself is what I am,\" said Newport News. \"I\u2019d rather scratch a turtle\u2019s belly than watch a two-year-old hop around after a calf. I never did see what was funny about a grown man chasing himself on foot.\"\n\"You hardly ever do see it, nowadays,\" said Gross. \"...", "2_15_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was putting together a horrible meal for the two beings who were about to appear for the three o'clock feeding.\nThose beings remained hidden, by their own choice, behind a wall of frightful strength.\nTheir three-o\u2019clock feeding would be, by far, their worst feeding of the day. It was the only feeding that they dreaded.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did their dreading for them.\nSmack! went the seed-lettuce against the wall.\n\"Oh\u2014my\u2014God, \" whispered $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"My\u2014God, \" whispered he.\nSmack! went the chocolate pudding against the wall.\n\"Oh\u2014my\u2014God, \" whispered $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"My\u2014God\u2014God, \" whispered he.\nSmack! went the bologna against the wall.\n\"Oh\u2014my\u2014\" whispered $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Oh\u2014\" whispered he.\nSmack! went the canned Spanish pilchard against the wall.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and Stony realized, from the sounds that had just been made against the wall, that the wall had been dented.", "0_10_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, when the light shows off BAfteur in the best way.\nI was standing on the walk in sunshine, as befits a messenger of the Sacred Scripture, waiting for $SURNAME0\u2019s doors to open so I could deliver the message I carried.\nThe name of the message was The $FIRSTNAMEM1 Times.\nThe message was a broadsheet, a mimeographed news sheet of four pages. The sheets of the news sheet, now limp and dirty, had been stapled together and wrapped in waxed paper to keep them from smudging. The message weighed ten pounds.\nHow I had come by the message is a long and perhaps unbelievable story I\u2019ll tell elsewhere, if I have time. The story becomes important later on, but only as a prologue to things far more important.\nI found it interesting, in a saintly way, that the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent should have chosen me to be the messenger of The $FIRSTNAMEM1 Times.\nNote that I did not say, \"The Church of God has chosen me to blah blah blah,\" or \"It is the wish of God that I blah blah blah.\" No. I found it interesting. That\u2019s all. The message had been sitting...", "1_7_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-two in the morning</span> and much of the travelers were sleepers. Only three of us were awake. At six-fifty-five I walked to the back of the bus and put down my blanket. I had slept standing up the night before. Now I lay down and covered myself, and was almost immediately asleep. I was having a good dream of home, and the first thing I did in the morning was to wake up suddenly, wide awake and feeling fine.\nBut I slept again. Twenty minutes passed and another traveler woke me up, saying, \u201cHere's where we change buses to San Fran, fellow. Don't sleep too long.\u201d\nI sat up, rubbed my face, yawned, looked out the window. We were turning off the main highway and bouncing up to a small, roadside diner cafe; everybody was getting off. The hollow, wooden diner looked raw and weak with the cold morning air. I picked up my bus fare from under my hatband and ran with everybody else up the steps to the cafe. The bus to San Francisco didn't leave for forty-five minutes. There was time to eat. I had exactly six cents and looked around like a lost rat. All the industrial benecnueers were lined up at the counter ordering...", "0_0_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past midnight</span> on October 1, and I was somewhere around Barstow on the edge of the desert when the drugs began to take hold. I remember saying something like \u201cI feel a bit lightheaded; maybe you should drive...\u201d And suddenly there was a tremendous flash of white light, like one of those lights that male photographers use to blind you, only it was the sun...I was moving through some kind of purplish fogbank and then I was lying in the seat, with the engine running, and the taste of blood and metal in my mouth. I tried to move but my arms and legs seemed like rubber.\nFreeway Grapevine...High Priest Of Power...Bombay Gin...Beach, Belmont, Bonnie and Burke...Into The Red Zone\u2014Las Vegas . . . Hog Heaven!\n\u201cLas Vegas! Fourteen hours from now...five tons of prime Mexican Grass and a freight car full of pure Mescaline! This is it! Destroy the ego or give up! This is to be your final experiment on the reality of your existence, which means the end of life or death, and the choice is yours.\u201d\nNo answer...Five tons better? Rabbit or snitch? The trap is a...", "2_14_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. There was a ticklish problem as to where in time the action really was. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and the Space Wanderer, it will be recalled, had sneaked back into their exhibition room with the suit and the phony dog at three minutes to two. By rights, the actions of $FIRSTNAMEB2 and the Space Wanderer within the illicit chamber were the actions of creatures three minutes in the future.\nIt was not quite certain where real time resumed, but $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 supposed it was when he reached into his pew and found the note from $SURNAME0.\nThe note read as follows:\nMister $SURNAME1:\n$SURNAME0, who has been advising you in your affairs, counsels you now only this: Beware of anniversaries.", "2_20_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>. Time for $FIRSTNAMEF1 and the children to get ready to go to the play.\n\"Sounds good, doesn\u2019t it?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 of the play. She kissed $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was skipping rope in the corner. The rope was tied, backpack fashion, on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s thin, sooty back. \"All ready, $FIRSTNAMEB2?\" she said.\n\"Yes, ma\u2019am,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was eight.\n\"I hope it\u2019s good, that\u2019s all,\" said the shiny, black Tralfamadorian messenger, who was sleeping peacefully on the table under the hour hand of the outsize wall clock. He was heeling, toeing, in his sleep. \"I\u2019ve seen about a hundred different kinds of productions on about eighty different worlds\u2014and I never saw a bad one yet.\"\nNobody took any notice of the messenger, whose name was Salo, which was not his name at all\u2014and who didn\u2019t even know the name of the race to which he belonged.\nHe had come to Earth to warn $SURNAME0 and his family, and that was all there was to it.", "0_11_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of December 2,1985, in Las Vegas, Nevada, when suddenly and without any warning at all a middle-aged man came out of the sky falling slowly like a thin Chinese umbrella. Behind him trailed forty or fifty feet of flex-cable, taped to an open umbrella, with a rotor at the end that was still whirring sluggishly. The man, a Mr. Blodger, of San Diego, California, was on his way to a buffet breakfast at the Flamingo Hotel. The rotating blades of the rotor had apparently pushed against a stone column in the outdoor patio of the Mint Hotel-twelve stories up, and Blodger and his umbrella had been jerked off the roof like jugglers\u2019 plates.\nWhat salvaged Blodger from the most incredible, eyeball-cracking fall in history was the taut flex-cable, which finally unraveled and came peeling out of the rotor like the string of a parachute. Blodger was still suspended from it by an open umbrella when he finally bounced on the slanted panel of an awning nine stories up. The patio remained vacant for several minutes while startled pedestrians gathered around the fallen chap like a pool of molasses which was drying in the...", "0_16_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-one in the afternoon</span>, and the last tired officials at the Las Vegas airport were wishing they\u2019d gotten a job somewhere else. They were the guys who had to toil the day shift on New Year\u2019s Eve. The time-tables said that the last flight in was charged with bringing in all the personnel that composed the midnight shift: the bartenders, the kitchen help, the shift bosses and headwaiters, the wristwatch fixers and stickmen for the twenty-four resort casinos. The shift bosses and headwaiters and wristwatch fixers went straight to the Dunes, which was centrally located amongst the twenty-four. The bartenders, kitchen help and stickmen were offloaded fifteen miles out on the Vegas Beltway, at the mob-owned Stardust, and given a free shuttleservice run by VW\u2019s and Jitneys, over to the Dunes. This was the reason why the taxi service into town at the airport was nuts: the sightseer and casual gambler traffic was arriving late right along with the shift-workers. All of them had to pass through Immigration first.\n\"What\u2019s this crap about guns?\" one pachuco-type kid in a brown suit from the Rio squ...", "2_0_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-six a.m.</span> when we opened our eyes to try the third bar. We missed the airport bar by half a block and emerged right in the middle of a fed eral drug bust. They'd just busted a tourist couple on their way out of the Flamingo and they were putting something very nasty in the trunk of their car. I got five feet into the scene before I realized I was dead\u2014if they caught me there.\nI backed out of there slowly, through the ground floor of the no-flight lounge and down the escalator to the traffic floor. I merged with the Amblers and the Bunkers and the Roxans, with my face stuck out about a foot in front of my body, trying for a neutral expression and failing badly. I had some difficulty trying to walk without actually moving my legs. I felt like a puppet dangling from strings under my armpits.\nBut I made it to the bar, and that was the main thing. We picked one with a long row of phone booths along one wall. Fuck the eightball. By this time we were so far beyond bats that the only possible play would have been something like snorting a brain contraction on top of two hits of Dilaudid, and this would have killed us both.", "0_11_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-six in the morning</span>. I decided I would eat a gigantic breakfast and then go see $FIRSTNAMEB3's aunt Mary and aunt Elmer. I found the truck-driver of the Tuesday night before. He was just getting up. I said hello, and he said hello, and I said, \u201cBy the way, I owe you five dollars?\u201d\n\u201cYou bet, you bet,\u201d he said eagerly. \u201cHere, wait!\u201d He disappeared into a general store and emerged in a few minutes with the money. There were dimes, nickels, and pennies. \u201cI lost the quarters,\u201d he explained. \u201cYou can keep the pennies if you want; they ought to come pretty close to the five dollars.\u201d I thanked him. He had a big breakfast waiting for him - French toast, a steak, bacon, eggs, and coffee. He ate and talked to a young guy who was fixing breakfast.\n\u201cWhere are you headed for?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cOh, Oklahoma City, and then Montana, I guess.\u201d\n\u201cHow's the truck?\u201d\n\u201cThe same. It's no good. The carburetor's always going bad.", "0_2_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-eight in the morning</span>. Dateline, Bruder Street, San Francisco. I made a mental note of all this in the hope that someday I might have the strange luck to stumble into this adventure again. Why only last night . . . but suffice it to say that Sonny and I knew it would be useless to expect sympathy from our friends at the corner. They are a mean and vicious crowd.\nThey no doubt felt the same way about us. We were natural enemies. Our ideas about life were poles apart. We were the upper crust, they were the remnants of the Beat Generation. When the showdown came we were bound to go down in bloody flames. This is the merciless law of the Hash House. All across the land, every day, undergraduates are slowly pulling the beatniks down out of their dope-happy ratholes and giving them the big sweepings. The beatniks, who are mostly ex- undergraduates, try to hold out but they don't dare shoot, because all the boys have MAs.\nThis, of course, is brute snobbery on our part. The kind of people who congregate around lunch wagons have no lofty ideas about themselves. They are the scum of the earth -- mechanics, drug addicts, dull and brutal...", "1_21_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine nineteen in the evening</span>. $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were alone, standing on a knoll on the estate, staring out at the Martian night. The boy had been told that his father and his mother were about to discuss very adult matters\u2014matters that might embarrass a child of his delicate sensibilities. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been told that his mother and his father, about to discuss matters of an adult and delicate nature\u2014matters that might well embarrass an impressionable child. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been told to walk, without disturbing anyone, to a lonely spot and wait there until called.\nNow that he was gone, $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 could look at each other, talk to each other.\nOnly one of them knew what was on both their minds. Only one of them was thinking about the miracle that was about to occur. Only one was thinking about the single and excruciating question that seemed insuperable at this moment.\nWould poor Salo continue to clean his antennae with his best friend\u2019s femur?\nWould he continue to interpret every little pain as a sign that his long martyrdom was reaching a climax?\nWould he simply go on standing watch on his hilltop\u2014blind and crippled, broadcasting his steadfast refusal to concede reality?\n\"I had an...", "0_7_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 woke up, woke up thinking that he had never been asleep. He went through the motions. He had always gone through the motions. Lately, though\u2014very lately\u2014he had been going through the motions like a sleepwalker.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t know why he had been put to sleep, treated like a defective dog, and sorted out to the permanent infantry. He didn\u2019t know why everything about him was so bad. He didn\u2019t know what he had done\u2014what anybody had done\u2014to deserve the puny, hateful, shabby things that life seemed to be made of here on Mars.\nIt had not struck $FIRSTNAMEB0 that he and anybody else might be the objects of a gigantic and heartless joke. How could it be a joke\u2014a tricky, complex, and cruel joke\u2014when $FIRSTNAMEB0 hadn\u2019t a clue as to what it was all about?\nThe time was six minutes until eight o\u2019clock in the morning.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and Beeemer were sitting in the shade of the space ship. $FIRSTNAMEB0 couldn\u2019t get into the ship any more. The combination to the lock was forty-two, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t know it. Anyhow...", "1_21_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> when I arrived at the office. Nothing had happened there since my departure in the morning. Most of the lights were off, and there was only the big night light in the corridor, and the booming sound of the midnight freight in the station yard below the windows.\nI started with the Saint Louis newspapers on the homicide. There wasn\u2019t much of anything. One story gave it to the Saint Louis Post Dispatch, and merely said that police were following a clue.\nI dialed the Post-Dispatch story in code and listened to it go along the wire. It contained no great surprises. Clarence Butly, a wealthy grocer, and Charles Butly, his son, both were killed by shotgun in their home. The police refused to issue a statement, except that the tragedy followed an argument at dinner. A nephew, Robert Butly, a night-club operator in San Francisco, was being sought for questioning.\nThe story had been written by a rewrite man scarcely sixty minutes after the killing had been discovered. The family was maintaining a strictly dignified silence about the matter. The Saint Louis police refused to speculate about the killing. They were waiting for results of ballistics tests. Two shotguns had been found in the house. The case was being handled by Chief...", "2_18_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-four in the evening</span>, Earthling Time.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 Rumford looked at her wristwatch, shoved her husband aside, and made a vulgar display of comparing her watch with the huge numbers glowing in red plastic above the Time Machine. \"Yes,\" she said, finally. \"Just about five minutes to seven, Time. The exact minute we promised.\"\nMr. Rumford had nothing to say.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 did not glance at his wristwatch. He had nothing to say, either. His one controlling passion was to get the hell away from this place as fast as possible.\n\"How does it feel?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, pressing close to $SURNAME1.\n\"How does what feel?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Being the richest man in the universe,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"May I use your gabbing-radio a minute?\" said $SURNAME1 to $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I want to call the penthouse.\" He would use no one\u2019s telephone, and he did want to call the penthouse. It was just that he did not want to be overheard by anyone in the building.\n\"Of course,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, relinquishing the gabber to $SURNAME1. She watched him dial, listened to him as he spoke.", "1_8_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time, when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized at the head of the bed of a section of bleachers on Mars.\nThey appeared out of nowhere.\nThey appeared there as suddenly andimperiously as a squad of bluegrass banjo players at the foot of the dome in music-hall song and skit.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were not the only things mysteriously materialized at the head of the bed of that section of bleachers. There was also a small commode, a wastepaper basket, a copy of the Pocket RegulationBook of the United States Army, and a violet-flowered girdle.\nA wisp of violets from the girdle floated up into the breeze, bummed about like a parachute, hit the side of the toilet bowl, and fell in.\nThe only other thing on the section of bleachers was the message that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 had materialized with.\nThis message was traced in footprints in the red, pulpy, claylike surface of the planet.\nThe footprints were $SURNAME0\u2019s, and their arches were so high that $SURNAME0 was a man...", "1_17_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nCuordecalamazones Unite.\nUNNAMED\u2019S TRUE DEPARTURE FROM THE SOLAR SYSTEM WAS SET FOR eighteen minutes after five, Eastern Standard Time. This meant that Unnamed had to terminate and collapse the tube connection between itself and Pier 82 by no later than eighteen minutes after four. The Navy had told $SURNAME1 this, and had given him his own reasons: it did not wish the excitement and fanatic adoration of the crowd outside to have time to die down before Unnamed left. The more aloof and withdrawn Unnamed seemed, the more fanatical would the crowd grow.\n$SURNAME1 could hardly wait to get moving. He had had enough of the shakedown cruise\u2019s meticulously measured schedule and claustrophobic small-space inscrutability.\n$SURNAME1 planned to take Unnamed straight out into deep space and keep it flying through the light year pressures and past the regimentations of the Navy forever. Pop and $FIRSTNAMEM2 had been furious when Ham and $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 told them of this plan, just before the christening. Their fury had been whipped up by an undercurrent of bottomless fear.\n\"A man ought to know his own machine,\" Pop had said.\n\"A man...", "1_5_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span> when the ten-year-old boy\u2014$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2014was cut loose on the surface of Mars.\nIt was forty-eight hours before he was picked up again.\nHis adventures during those forty-eight hours are not recorded\u2014between being cast adrift in space and being fetched back again by the Fleet of Space-craft from $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his chrononaut, $FIRSTNAMEB4, were fetched back to $FIRSTNAMEB2 at six o\u2019clock on the evening of March the twenty-eighth. As the time ship came in over the mansion, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 ran out on the parboiled lawn to meet her son and his dog.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were dropped from the time ship on the apron in front of the mansion.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 understood none of this. He dropped onto the apron from the time ship simply because he had been ordered to, and because $SURNAME0 was pulling him along by a line from his collars. Because all this was alarming, $FIRSTNAMEB4 sat down.\nFrom inside the time ship came the sounds of lamentation and gnashing of teeth. Whoever was curled up inside, this...", "2_1_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMcRevem threw the issue of Confidential on top of the radio. It hit the radio and fell open to reveal a full-page photograph of an eighteen-year-old girl taken with a telephoto lens from across the street. The girl's eyes were blued by the lens.\nMcRevem leered at the photograph, bared his teeth, and mocked the photograph with nasal, rapid hip movement. He pretended to play a trumpet. He laughed and sprayed some whiskey into the receiver of a black telephone.\nMcRevem was under no illusions as to the quality and nature of his neighbors. He was not far out in his and Sarah's yard. His yard was a cramped little plot in the back of a row of old houses tucked in behind Manhattan restaurants, a rock club and an expensive purple bookshop. The air was always full of the odors of smoky French fries and mint chocolate chip ice cream.", "0_3_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three nineteen in the morning</span> when Remi Boncq and I staggered out of the Caf\u00e9 des Amateurs. I had no idea what time $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo and the girls would show up, nor what strange events would take place as the sun rose. It was November. Fog poured over the town. We made a date for 414 the next night. I wandered off into the fogs of Saint-Germain. The fun had been mine in the old Paris of France, the new Paris of France. It was all a mad fiesta deep in the brown early-morning hours. I hitched to the rue de la Montagne Sainte-Genevi\u00e8ve. It was four o'clock, and only one light burned in the shack where I lived, and a noble friend of mine was sleeping there, so I blew on the window-pane to wake him. He came out rubbing his eyes. \u201cAh, $FIRSTNAMEB6, when did you get in?\u201d\n* \u201cWhat are you doing here?\u201d I demanded. \u201cI came back from Venice today.\u201d\n* \u201cHow was Venice?\u201d\n* \u201cGreat,\u201d said Terry, yawning. \u201cWhere've you been?\u201d", "0_13_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Eastern Daylight Saving Time.\nThe place was the Virginia State Police Barracks in Winchester, Virginia.\nSpecial Deputy Wendell D. Loosemore, Bicky-Canaday Detective Agency, was listening to his hair hang up on a faulty telephone. He was enraged.\n\"Oh, go ahead and hang up, damn you!\" he screamed. \"Just remember this is the last time you\u2019re going to be able to get away with anything like this! You try just once more\u2014you lousy stinking rat! You rotten son of a bitch! Don\u2019t you think I know who\u2019s ordering special deliveries from the airline offices? Don\u2019t you think I know what time the goddamn postman comes, you asshole? Get me? Are you listening? You ain\u2019t never gonna be able to fool nobody like this again, you hear? He almost had me fooled, but I catch on fast! You try just once more and you\u2019re finished! You get me?\"\nHe looked at the vast and polished back of Room 229, which was the Chief\u2019s Office of the Virginia State Police Barracks in Winchester. \"Aw, hell,\" he said. \"Don\u2019t nobody want to talk...", "0_22_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-one in the evening</span> when they flew into the darkening Berlin lights of 1944. The red glare hanging over the German capital indicated it was either on fire or being thoroughly bombed. $SURNAME0 and his dog were met at the airport by a very tall and sombre-looking young man named $SURNAME1 $FIRSTNAMEB1. $SURNAME1 was the son of one of $SURNAME0\u2019s oldest and dearest friends.\n\"You are Mr. $SURNAME0?\" he said to $SURNAME0. He had been told to call his master Mr. $SURNAME0, though he had met him only once. Good manners and the sad occasion made him call $SURNAME0 Mr. $SURNAME0 and himself $SURNAME1 $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"You are $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California?\" he said. The son of $SURNAME0\u2019s old and dear friend had been told to give this name to the rich Californian who had sold all his worldly goods to the War Fund.\n\"This is $FIRSTNAMEB4,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes, sir,\" said $SURNAME1. He went on smoking a cigarette, though he was not inhaling it. He didn\u2019t inhale cigarettes because of the extreme air-raid precautions against the spread of stray germs.\nA staff car...", "2_20_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA big boy in shorts and a tee-shirt with Haziel B. Crumley on its front put down the telephone to which he had been talking, and strolled over to the desk.\n\"How\u2019s tricks?\" he said. \"No, don\u2019t get up. I\u2019m just going to look over your shoulder and watch you work for a minute.\" He leaned close to $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s ear. \"I didn\u2019t think you\u2019d really do it,\" he said.\n\"I would think you\u2019d know me well enough by this time\u2014know my habits just a little bit,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"If it\u2019s any of your business, I was going to do exactly what I did about one minute from now.\"\n\"You really can\u2019t put anything over on me.\"\n\"Glad to see you, too.\"\n\"Thank God we\u2019ve got that out of the way,\" said the big boy, happily. \"My name\u2019s Crumley. You know Humble. Well\u2014\"\n\"Can\u2019t it wait? I\u2019m on the phone with Gatlin.\"", "1_14_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-three in the afternoon</span>, Chicago time. The date was supposed to be February 1,1987, but something seemed wrong about the date too.\nCrow had not yet even heard about the $FIRSTNAMEB0 invasion of Germany. So his memory had gone to sleep again. His memory, so far, was as selective as $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s memory. The only thing in his mind was a compulsion to keep going toward the blue front door.\nHe reached the door. It had neither knob nor latch. The only things on it were a brass letter-slot and a neat brass card, both apparently part of an extremely nice housekeeper\u2019s gig. The neat brass card said in green printing: THE MAJESTY OF LAW APPLY PERSISTENTLY.\nCrow pushed his nose through the letter-slot. He found himself looking into a room that was more lavatory than office. On the walls were long placards that said, PLEASE FLUSH, WE\u2019RE ALL USING THIS TOILET. There were big blue cardboard posters of a sickly infant nursing at a mother\u2019s breast. The caption under the posters said, THE BABIES WHO COUNT ON YOU! One of the posters was eating its nails. Across from it was...", "0_14_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two nineteen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe space was Room 223 of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Hotel in downtown Cleveland.\nThe space was furnished beige and brown to look like burlap. The floors were covered with brown-beige burlap, as was a two-foot-square portion of the wall around the door. In the far corner a plastic ivy plant grew out of a flat gravel bed.\nThe bed in the room was covered with a brown-beige bedspread. A color television set in a brown plastic case rested on the bed. The television\u2019s sound was off, and the picture showed a commercial featuring a father twirling his son in midair.\nThe commercial made Lenore feel bad.\nOutside the window the sky was gray and covered with light gray clouds above the city of Cleveland. A dull orange sun peeked over one of the clouds. It was still raining lightly. Through the window Lenore could see slab sides of big buildings and narrow alleys that between them made a network of small irregular crannies of shadow. The wind blew the tops of some of the clouds as it made clear swirls in the rain.\nLenore was lying on the bed, with the belt of her airline robe loosened, holding the television remote-control device...", "0_10_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of December twenty-ninth.\nThe deck-hands were in the hold scurrying around. The man who had been stung by the wasp swayed dizzily as he hauled on rope.\n\"Quicker! Hustle! Hustle! Hustle!\" the sailor shouted at him.\nThe sailors were worried about their watches, about what time it was. A little checking of watches went on among them.\n\"Come on, Mary Ann!\" yelled the mate. He was on top of the deck house, trying to find out what in hell was wrong with the woman on the crow's nest.\n\"I'm coming,\" she said. She started to come down the ladder.\n\"Quicker!\" shouted the mate.\n\"Shut up!\" said a carpenter to the mate. \"She'll come, ain\u2019t you, Mary Ann?\" he shouted.\n\"Yes,\" she said.\nShe climbed up to the platform to join the man at the steering gear.\n\"Did you hear me?\" shouted the mate. \"I said quicker!\"\n\"Oh, shut up!\" shouted the captain. \"You sound like a goddamn phonograph!\"\n\"Come on!\" yelled the mate.\nThe sailors were making the lines...", "1_16_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB5 and I got on to the bus that was taking the runners to San Sebastian. There was one other passenger beside ourselves. He was going on to Bayonne. The Basques were waiting for us with the luggage. They tipped their caps, put the luggage on the racks, and tucked us in like two children. $FIRSTNAMEB5 had her bag beside her on the luggage-rack. I had mine on my knees. The motor started and we left Pamplona going out of the square beside the park and the trees, under the arcades, $FIRSTNAMEB5 sitting beside me. She seemed to be drowsy looking out at the sunny square we were leaving.\n\u201cGood-by,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said.\n\u201cGood-by,\u201d I said. \u201cI\u2019ve had a hell of a good time.\u201d\n\u201cSo-o?\u201d\n\u201cReally.\u201d\n\u201cYou don\u2019t want to go back to Paris?\u201d\n\u201cNo.\u201d\n\u201cDo you want to come to Bayonne with me?\u201d\n\u201cNo, I think I\u2019ll go back to San Sebastian with this chap.\u201d\n\u201cWhy don...", "1_11_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. One mile below Vanity, at the head of the falls, the river was unfordable, and was patrolled by twelve feet of falling water. Above Vanity the river was flat, but treacherous. You could paddle across from bank to bank, but not take a chance in a small boat. The only use for a canoe was to sweeten the airlines and make a river-trip safe and civilized.\nIn the days of the pioneers, the Civil War, and Prohibition, the river was full of lunatics who didn't give a damn. And when you weighed their chances, it seemed a miracle that any of them survived. With only a volume of The Unexpected Guest to read, and a small deck of cards, some of them tried to get to the Gulf.\nThere was no good way to do it. The only sure way was to use a map, a compass, and a machine gun - and travel in river miles instead of land miles. The river was actually only four hundred and ninety-eight miles long, but a land trip to New Orleans averaged twelve miles an hour on the conservative estimate. That would make it a six-week run. Not having anything to carry but a portable stove, shotgun, and hatchet, I could probably hold my...", "1_19_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven eighteen in the evening</span> of a Wednesday in October. Not only was life mysterious--it was expensive, too. I had six dollars and fifty-eight cents in my pocket. I had to feed myself for the next three days on that. Never in my life had three days stretched farther away from me.\nI went to a cigar store, bought a newspaper, and looked up the addresses of the employment agencies. I followed their directions to one, a very trim and conservative-looking concern.\nI gave my name to a blonde who sat behind a Lucite slab. She told me to sit down. \u201cWhat kind of a job?\u201d she asked, cocking her fingernail.\n\u201cAny kind,\u201d I said.\n\u201cDon't any kind grab you?\u201d \u201cI want to be a crime reporter.\u201d\nShe straightened up and looked at me for the first time. \u201cIs that a fact?\u201d she said. \u201cHow interesting.\u201d She poised her fingernail again. \u201cWhere are you from?\u201d\n\u201cNew York.\u201d\n\u201cWhen did you get in?\u201d \u201cLast night.\u201d\n\u201cWhat's your hurry...", "2_11_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven sixteen in the morning</span>. The place was New York, N.Y. The identity of the person being interviewed on the Trinitron was Timothy Appleton.\nTimothy Appleton was the head of the Beacon Hill Section Committee for Celestial Radio Music for Peace. He was holding in his hands a small clock, a windup alarm clock. He was blowing on the clock.\n\"I want to put this clock on the roof of the Channing-Peak ASWFC,\" he was saying into the microphone. \"Have it plugged in and set for eleven forty-five, Eastern War Time.\" He paused. \"Mrs. Roosevelt, Joachim Koch, Vassili Borisovich Patomsky, and all you other people would do well to keep one eye open right at eleven forty-five, Eastern War Time, August twelfth.\" He paused again. \"Why?\" he asked.\nNorman Beeber was sitting on the bed in the bedroom of his penthouse apartment on West Seventy-sixth Street, New York City. He was watching Tim Appleton on the Trinitron set in his bedroom, with the sound turned off.", "0_10_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>. A new day had begun. Our mission was to seize the Patagonian Toothfish. The weather was magnificent. From the forecastle you could look out toward the north end of Treasure Island, and there was San Francisco, that lace-piece of delicious bay, and beyond the Golden Gate the sea foamed up on the Farallons. The air was pure and dry. The tug Farallzse was churning a column of warm white spray into the air as she wrenched at the chain that held us to the pier. It was a picture of tough vigor and wild beauty. As with all pictures, if you looked at it long enough you saw certain flaws. There was something phony about our tug, the Farallones, the island, and something sadly wrong with the picture beyond\u2014those hills, that drab dense city, and across the bay the endless suburbs, the ugly flats, ranks of basketball nets, streets, streets, streets as far as the eye could see, a whole nation pumping gas. Could that be our home? What had we done with these? We were to make our first toothfish fry with the printed program Indian-style, the medicine man directly over our heads. We had to catch the fish up near Point Reyes...", "0_15_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was a few hundred yards behind the lines of the Third American Army Group in Germany. Patton's men had overrun this little patch a few days before, and then they had forgotten it. Now some of their precious artillery ammunition had blown up and made a lot of fragments. Somebody had to come over, in a jeep, sit down in the midst of the fragments, and make a list of the kinds and quantities of fragments, so that replacements for them could be made.\nThis bit of paper was of critical importance to the army group, and so the job was given to a sergeant, who was, according to what the major said, \u201cthe best ordnance sergeant in the army.\u201d This did not mean that the sergeant was any sort of expert on fragments.\nIt meant that the sergeant was, absolutely and by all odds, the most average man in the army.\nThe major had an affection for this average sergeant. \u201cHe's the same as you and me,\u201d said the major. \"A human being. His name's Potts. He wants to go home as much as you and I do.\"\nThe major had no sympathy for anybody who wasn't like himself. He had been in combat four years.", "1_18_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 kept trying to guess when his next meeting was going to be. Something in his mind was insisting upon the necessity for his absolute presence at a given place and a given time.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was experiencing the dreadful symptoms of conflict\u2014a conflict between two insulated brains. He was experiencing that conflict freshly, because the space ship in which he found himself was deep in space, gravitation having ceased to function.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 still had the orange-yellow deserter\u2019s bandage over half his face. His right eye was still hidden behind its everted hood.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was sitting in a motor-operated chair, staring through a window at the space ship\u2019s Solar systems gallery. The gallery was a peephole in the metal skin of the ship.\nThe metals of the view outside were greasy because the space ship was now being propelled by a jets-and-vacuum propulsion system instead of by a big, lovely, hearts and flowers, happy-land Gravity System. The switch to jets and vacuum had been made automatically when the ship had come to the limit of the gravity field.\nThere were stars twinkling all around the ship. They looked like gems set in black velvet. There were millions of them...", "1_22_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten eighteen in the evening</span> of October third, according to both $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s and $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s watches.\n\"We interested in caves now?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. He meant that he and $FIRSTNAMEB0, so far as their conventional perspectives went, had done their bit for explorers, underdogs, and suckers.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not answer.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was giving his attention to a sound that had nothing to do with any harmoniums. It was a drumming sound. The rapidity of the beats and their insistence were increasing.\nIt was not a drum, wasn\u2019t the roll of kettledrums, wasn\u2019t thunder, wasn\u2019t hoofbeats\u2014 wasn\u2019t anything Earthly at all. It was coming from where the fissure sprang.\nThe fissure was the only irregularity in a perfect floor of rock. The fissure, one billion Earthlings to the inch long, was carpeted by a flaming carpet of living organisms, as an Earthly forest floor could be carpeted by a foot-high growth of moss.\n\"Whoever they is,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"they sure do put a lot of foot into getting here.\" He figured that he and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had...", "1_15_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the thirtieth day of June in the year 2005, according to the Bureau International de l'Heure in Paris. At that moment Floyd L. Peterson, an American, was walking into the town of Thonon, Switzerland. He carried a small white bag with samples of In-and-Outer Sewing Machine Company merchandise that he was peddling on his vacation. The white bag was now so grimy that it was impossible to read what was stenciled on it in blue.\nWe followed Floyd in through the wide open doors of a building in the most unbelievably fancy part of town. Beyond the doors was a tremendous vast golden-stoned rotunda.\n\"The Palace of Versailles?\" I whispered to $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was digging gravely into her Longines. \"There is no Palace of Versailles in Switzerland, my friend.\" She looked up. \"This is the Palace of Justice.\"\n\"A court-house?\"\n\"Look and learn,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, and let her arm fall back across the back of the seat.\nI looked up at the great domed room. It was a court-house all right. It was the most gleaming courtroom I had ever seen. And we were parked in...", "1_14_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 found a chrono under a bunk. He strapped it to his wrist. The pain in his throbbing fingers was exquisite.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 showered, shaved, and put on blue and white g.i. clothes that he found in an unlocked footlocker. Then he applied with infinite care a set of pale-blue motion-pictureslides to the sensitizing back of his wrists and hidden inside his sleeves. Climbing into the air shaft, and later crawling through the heating system, he was going to be hunting a human-hating Beee-man whom he had never seen.\nThe man behind all this, $FIRSTNAMEB0 thought, would be, if possible, even more hideous than the blue planet itself.\nAn old elevator carried $FIRSTNAMEB0 down to the lobby. There he got into an antigravity scooter and put on a courier individual telephone headset. On a pad clamped to the scooter, he wrote:\nCOL. CUBBAN/BUN\nYOU ARE TO PROCEED AT ONCE TO\nTHE FOLLOWING THREE LOCATIONS:\nREAL ESTATE OFFICE BUILDING\n1). 1ST FLOOR\n3). 3RD FLOOR\n5). BASEMENT", "1_1_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one o'clock in the morning</span> when Remi Boncoeur rolled his white car in through the gates at San Fernando. Remi was sleepy and bothered. He hated driving at night. He liked driving in the daytime. Health was an obsession with Remi Boncoeur. He cared nothing for art and less for money. He cared most of all for his own personal well-being. He was afraid of germs. He frowned on mathematics. Remi Boncoeur was a sensualist in a black suit. He was looking for a little fun. He expected to find it through a hole in the wall he shared with his dead cousin, Lee. He had lost his cousin's marble in the game of sixty-six. It was a great favorite with Remi. He had played it as a boy. He had a mental picture of the marble rolling forever through space, all alone, playing the game of sixty-six by itself. Remi often wondered where it was and what it was up to. He would not have been surprised to run across it in the good nights of San Francisco. He often thought of going out to look for it, but never did. Remi Boncoeur was not given to looking for anything but health food. This came first with him. Without health...", "0_18_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-two in the evening</span>, a time of tranquil beauty in the space ship that carried the three men through space.\nBrooklyn, New York, the policemen told stories about the gorillas in the streets of Brooklyn, the howling youths who rushed into traffic, beat old women with sticks, went berserk\u2014there were hundreds of them now, the police said.\n\"I'm here to arrest you, boys, for crimes of the state of Nevada.\"\n\"Are you real policemen?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Real as rain,\" said the spokesman.\nHe fumbled with a garbage-bag handcuffed to his wrist, brought out something that looked like a stencilled postage stamp. He held it to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face.\n\"Read that,\" he said. \"Tell me what it says.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 read: FUCK YOU. He said, \"It says, \u2018Fuck you.\u2019 \"\n\"And it\u2019s addressed to you, sweetheart,\" said the cop. He tore the stamp off the garbage bag. \"I\u2019m delivering it to you now,\" he said.\n\"Is that what it says?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Is that what it says?\"\n\"Oh, that ain\u2019t nothing...", "2_11_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until noon</span>, twelve twenty-five, and wild for football.\nHe used to imagine making a perfectly executed tackle on his own one-yard line. He used to imagine how good it would feel to make that tackle, how happy it would make his mother, how proud it would make his father. At last a boy broke through the massed enemy, staggered and lunged toward the one-yard line. O beautiful, thought the owner of the filling station, for in high school he was slow, and fat, the very antithesis of a hero. But he arrived with a bang, and in high school that was more satisfying than any crowd shout.\nThe boy who had broken through the ranks crashed to the ground, facing the sky, his neck snapped. The greatest moment of the owner of the filling station\u2019s life had arrived, and he was on his back, looking at the Michigan sky. And it was raining, too, for November in Michigan rains.", "1_4_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-seven in the morning</span> and I had to get up at six to go to work. I would rather have gone to sleep but my life is ruled by clocks and calendars, and there is never enough time to do all the nothing you want to do. There never has been. What kind of a madman would create a universe in which he himself could never have any time to himself? Have you ever heard of such a madman? I have not.\nThey wouldn\u2019t let me sleep. I was alone in the house, my wife and child sleeping in their rented cabin in Malibu Canyon, far from the nameless anxieties of my work in the city. I had to be up in six hours, but my intruders would not let me sleep. All night long they sifted through my closets, looking for God knows what. At intervals, for dramatic emphasis, they rattled china and silverware in the kitchen cupboards, or went running down the driveway and stomped on the rattling garbage cans slung under the fig tree. I lay in bed smoking one Fatima after another, not shouting, not making a sound, forcing myself to ignore their desecration. I would not outwit them by acting terrified or crazy. Surprises are their favorite weapons...", "2_16_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. The place was Newport, Rhode Island. The temperature was seventy-two degrees.\nGreat billowings of smoke and flame spouted up as Mona Larry\u2019s Rumford fireplace swelled with heat and roared. Mona lay on her back on the floor and staggered to her feet, blowing on her badly singed hands.\nShe went back to the kitchen and opened the refrigerator. Finding no salve in the refrigerator, she went to the medicine cabinet and dabbed at herself with mercurochrome.\nThe mercurochrome took care of the second-degree singeing, but Mona found she could barely walk, because the surviving third-degree burns were killing her feet.\nThe soprano was still singing somewhere in the distant skies. It was such sweet, high singing. And it was summertime.\nAnd she was so tired.", "2_7_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-seven in the morning</span>.\n\"That is going to be a very fine telescope, sir,\" said the clerk, looking out the empty doorway.\n\"Mmmmmmmm,\" said the clerk.\n\"Ho hum,\" said the clerk.\nThings to Look At\nThe glass eye of the Thinker in the fountain sparkled in the sunlight. High in the sky, the last star was going out.\nThe spiral staircase twisted up toward the skylight.\nIn the stone aquarius, the dying goldfish made circles.\nAt the top of the staircase, the door to the ramparts was locked tight. Sunlight threw its size and shape in lumpy shadow across the floor. This was Mars. This was the far end of the Museum of Natural History. This was the tumbled redoubt where the more cosmically inclined ornithologists maintained the Rose Planetarium. This was not where the visitor had wanted to come.\nThe visitor put his ear to the locked door. Sound wrenched the door handle. Sound bent back the vertical hasp of the padlock. The visitor stepped outside and into the hot glare of the Sun.", "2_16_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The mood was autumn night. She was standing in the shower, having been directed there by a phone call from a stranger.\n\u201cWho are you?\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2 called through the steam, her hair banging against the shower\u2019s tin ceiling.\n\u201cJust someone who needs to talk to you.\u201d\n\u201cAbout what?\u201d\n\u201cHelp.\u201d\n\u201cLook,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2 said, \u201cyou haven\u2019t told me who you are. Maybe I need help. But I\u2019ve got to know who I listen to, or I won\u2019t be responsible for what I do.\u201d\n\u201cYou think you\u2019re responsible now?\u201d\n\u201cI am,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2 said, \u201cand you were right the first time I think.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll call again,\u201d the anonymous male voice said, and was gone.\nSo she stood under the shower, watching the steam swirl up and away and drift over the whole of San Narciso. How supple things looked, tonight. How shimmering. How inviting.", "1_9_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Look out, $FIRSTNAMEB0, here we go\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"You got everything?\"\n\"Everything, $FIRSTNAMEB2,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Whatever happens from this point, you go back and tell my family in Dayton everything was fine with me.\"\nThe space ship went up the ladder and slid out of the shadows of Mercury. In the incredible brightness of space, $FIRSTNAMEB0 let the ship do all the navigating.\nThe ship was carrying two hibernating soldiers, a full load of replacement parts, and a message from the Marshal of Uranus to the High Chancellor of Earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s message to his family was this:\nEverything was not fine with $FIRSTNAMEB0. He had been driven insane by the experiences he had undergone in the Army of Neptune.\nChapter 14\nAt the moment of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s so-called escape, the scene in bloody colors on the crypt walls consisted mostly of bulky, brainless creatures crowding to the surface of a small planet that was disappearing in flame. The creatures were crowding upward through a pipe in the middle of the planet. The pipe looked like a trash-chute. The creatures were trying frantically not to be burned up with their planet. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had never...", "1_17_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span> of a day in August of the year 2174. The place was the living room of an old woman's home above Lake Orcaduco in Nicaragua.\nThe instruments and dials of the space ship Mars Voyager II, parked in the living room, were functioning perfectly. The ship had no windows. The room, though furnished with comfortable old chairs and a flowered couch, was bare and functional. The walls were decorated with many framed photographs. One of the photographs was a remarkably beautiful color portrait of a young woman. This photograph was placed above the light switch beside the door.\nThe woman whose home it was was herself an extraordinarily beautiful blonde of thirty-four. She had a tiny gold cross in her navel.\nShe lay at gat on her flowered couch. A transcript of a $FIRSTNAMEM1 Cfruit play, beautifully typed, lay on her stomach. She let the pages slip through her fingers lazily. Her name was Helga. She ate chocolates from a box on the arm of the couch. The sun was pouring in from big unshaded windows.\nDrifting in from the next room came the voice of a news commentator from the Mars Voyager II\u2019s radio. The commentator was broadcasting live from the ship\u2019s...", "1_6_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span> and there were four months, three weeks, five days, and approximately eighteen hours left until Halloween of the year 1.\nThere was work to do.\n\"Civilization, Redeemed?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"So long to wait for a climb?\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF1\u2014\" said Mindy. \"I\u2019m going to compliment you someday soon.\"\n\"You better,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Thanks from me, too, Mindy,\" said Mandeville.\n\"Since what?\" said Mindy lazily.\n\"Since you\u2019re polite,\" said Mandeville.\n\"I\u2019ll let you know,\" said Mindy.\n\"Let me know, too,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I\u2019ll let you know too,\" said Mandeville.\n\"How?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"How what?\" said Mandeville.\n\"How are you going to let us know?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I\u2019ll tell you, of course,\" said Mandeville.\n\"Why can\u2019t we just watch you tell him?\" said Mindy.\n\"Watch me tell someone something?\" said Mandeville.\n\"Yes, watch you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"How else can we...", "0_1_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-four in the morning</span> when we left the white Cadillac at the New Reform Lanes in Cucamonga and snuck back to the graveside. For thirty or forty minutes we'd been sitting up above the highway beside the graveyard, fidgeting and trying to take stock -- assessing the damage and watching for cops. At first everything seemed hopeless. The spare tire was flat, and the jack had disappeared. We might as weiLtry to fix a flat with a nail -and\u2014twist board. Forget it. The frame\u2014bent and twisted, probably beyond salvation -- would bring us maybe a hundred and fifty bucks from a scrap -metal yard; but that would leave us with no wheels and no escape unless/f a miracle took place. None of the alternatives seemed palatable. We had no money for another car and no likelihood of getting any for at least fifty -eight hours. It seemed like a lifetime; but it would probably be everybody's lifetime if we stayed around. At six in the morning Sheriff's posses would be fanning out all over California.\nBut there was just a glimmer of a chance, I whispered. One percent chance\u2014maybe less...but wehad to take it.\n\u201cLet's do it,\u201d somebody said.", "1_19_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span> and our ship was scheduled to depart in ten minutes. And there was $FIRSTNAMEB3, wrestling with the Italian Custom Official in the huge ship's salon while $FIRSTNAMEF4 and I watched. We were to meet the gang downtown later. \u201cWhere's Carlo?\u201d\nWhat could be done? If a baron commits a crime in the forest and there's no one to hear it, does he go to jail? The Custom Official looked over at the gang and shrugged and waved $FIRSTNAMEB3 on. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was sweating. He started bouncing enormous suitcases (which he didn't even own) on the rubberized floor with a clank that echoed off the staterooms and rolled over the waves in the New York harbor and up the Hudson River. \u201cWhew!\u201d he said, towelling his face.\n\u201cNow,\u201d said Carlo at the gang's apartment, \u201cwe sit quiet and wait for Inez.\u201d Inez was his latest girl. \u201cI gotta talk to her.\u201d We waited half an hour. \u201cPerhaps she's gone to the movies, or somethin',\u201d he said. \u201cBenny, you go ring her up and see where she is and tell her I got...", "1_17_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nSacramental music came out of the walls, the floor, and the ceiling. It was created by a Church of God machine. $FIRSTNAMEB1 operated the machine at his desk, with sheets of music in front of him, and a pencil with which he turned the pages.\nAround $FIRSTNAMEB1 were his terminals, on each of which was mounted a television camera, and before each of which was set a wooden chair.\nThe television cameras were turned on and off by the sitter in the chair. The sitters bowed their heads in prayer, and kept them bowed until the ringing of a bell put the terminals and the chair in remote contact with their Presence. The terminals then fed images of the sitters back to Redwine Cove on Mars.\nThe sitters had nowhere else to go, and nothing else to do. They had been told nothing except that they were to sit and pray under the television cameras at precisely six o\u2019clock in the evening.\nTheir basement surroundings were bleak to gentle Christians and fiendishly appealing to a person with any taste at all. There was a small charcoal brazier, on which were burning bits of aromatic wood\u2014the sign of Satan among true Christians, the fragrance of a tunnel to God among connoisse...", "0_13_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty in the afternoon</span>.\nThe three of them were lying on a hilltop looking at a distant, dark-colored, almost chapel-like structure consisting of a steep roof and one high, narrow window.\n\"Lenox Hill Hospital,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Yum,\" said young $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"I\u2019d like to get me some of that. You like to get you some of that?\"\n\"Apparently he took some shrapnel in the leg yesterday,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"They can\u2019t do anything about it at our place. It\u2019s such a short time since we got here, this leg wound. It\u2019s not bad, but they say they can\u2019t do anything until they get upstairs again. The hospital will be functioning again in a day or two, they say. But right now\u2014\"\n\"Right yum, right yum,\" said young $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Right this very minute, let\u2019s go on over there and get us some\u2014\"\n\"Quiet, please, young man,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nAlphax came around from behind a rock carrying a small bag of lichen.\n\"I got a piece I think belongs to that chapel-like thing over there,\" he said.", "0_3_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three seventeen in the morning</span>. We sat at the Dances' kitchen table, where I had eaten so many enormous breakfasts. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Stan drank the bourbon straight and I drank mine with water. $FIRSTNAMEF4 rinsed a cup at the sink and added some bourbon to it and drank it down. We were talking about the jealous rages of $FIRSTNAMEF4's boyfriend, Carlo Marx, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 got up and took a most complicated grab involving a lunge across the table and a snatch in front and a stomp on $FIRSTNAMEF4's foot as he tried to sneak by with the coffee pot -- he was a very sadistic coffee-pot sneaklet -- and managed to spill coffee all over himself and had to go change his killer shirt for a sportshirt he dug out of his bag with another wild hoop and caterwaul. \u201cScuse me, I dig with a shovel. Digging and sighing, ya? S-s-s-s . . . \u201d Meanwhile the grandfather clock ticked, ticktock, boom, boom, with a sound like great happiness. Ticktockaroom, boomaroom. Max Dessen was sleeping on the floor. Nelson sat on the table in his pajamas and swung his bare feet contentedly.", "2_19_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earthling time, when the alarm went off in Thunderstone\u2019s Pharmacy.\nTen seconds later, the alarm went off in the Post Office.\nTen seconds later, the alarm went off in the offices of Dr. Morris Tanning, dentist and shipowner.\nTen seconds later, the alarm went off in Police Chief Sid Hammerback\u2019s private quarters in the Space Cadet Home.\nSo it went.\nAt ten seconds after seven o\u2019clock in the evening, Earthling time, the assault modules of the Martian expeditionary force took off from Mars and began their devastating way toward Earth.\nThe first impact was in Montana, at a small town variously called West Pearl, Paunch Creek, and Fryburg.\nThe impact wasn\u2019t noticed right away. Every house, every road in West Pearl, Paunch Creek or Fryburg had been abandoned for years. The impact set off a fire in an abandoned church, which had been an abandoned church for as long as anyone could remember. Nobody worried about fires in abandoned churches any more.", "0_4_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four o'clock in the morning</span>. As I took stock of things I noticed a big blue sedan parked outside my headquarters--a sleek California-style car with a radio aerial and all. I looked out of the door into the bleak dawn--and there were THREE MORE identical sedans parked in the street, all with their motors idling smoothly. \"This is pure Dunbar,\" I said aloud. There was only one man in the world who drove around Las Vegas in a gang like this, deploying forces from Cadillac limousines. It had to be Kit Clark, the Chairman of the Committee.\nNotice that I am not saying \u201ca man.\u201d I always felt fear when I dealt with Clark, fear and bizzare physical tension that made my eyes ache, no matter how it turned out. He was a true Nut\u2014the type that makes headlines for messy suicides in hotel rooms, after ninety-day shooting contests with the wrong people. In a sense, he was better than the Mafia\u2014less hypocritical and complex\u2014but maybe just as dangerous. At least the Mafia had a sense of humor.\nStill, I wanted him to win. I wanted him to come bursting through the door, wearing a straitjacket and barking like a seal under heavy sedation. I...", "0_15_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Another minute passed. The last grain of sand dropped, with a small, dry thud. And all at once everything changed, the whole concept of everything changed, and the Earth was no longer a planet hanging in space, but a great ship hanging in space, with the peoples of the Earth clinging to its rigging and scrambling about on deck.\nLevel by level, system by system, life on Earth disassembled itself, depended itself, transposed itself, took itself apart and stowed itself away, as a man unscrews and takes apart his watch, which is too complicated for him to understand, and whose hands now pointed to the sky, now veered wildly about in all directions, telling inconstant, unverifiable and finally fruitless lies about the passage of time.\nBecause obviously, if the Earth was a spaceship, then all such things as the rising and setting of the sun, and the passing of the seasons, would have to be artifacts of mere engineering, is it not, Mr. Gray, and could have no counterpart in the natural world outside the ship. And so by the same token, by opening up their estate as a public park, the $SURNAME0 family was proclaiming to the world that they had made the Earth a closed system...", "0_13_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the 1 day of December in the year 1986. The place was the misshapen, shadowbox world inside the split world. $SURNAME1 knew that the numerals that were being tattooed into his forehead were the number 86. He didn't care. His buttocks were cramped with kneeling, his nose and cheeks were encrusted with dried blood, and his head was pounding with pain.\nHe didn\u2019t care about anything.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0-Lola-Monica was dying by inches in the rear seat of the limousine.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had no idea why he had forced $SURNAME1 to torture a tranced and harmless $FIRSTNAMEF0-Lola-Monica, but he was glad that he had. Seeing $FIRSTNAMEF0-Lola-Monica suffer so exquisitely was worth a year\u2019s pay, even if it meant a jail term. He felt like a knight of old. He felt glorious.\nThen he felt debased at having such a cheap thrill. A moment later, he felt noble again. A moment after that, pornographic. Then patriotic. Then bored.\nAlways $FIRSTNAMEB0 found himself ambivalent. It was a state in which he felt truly American.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been nominated for the Purple Heart and the...", "0_5_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. Three operatives were already asleep, but Lime and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were very wide awake. The guards were set.\nDandy, Comanche, and Bozo, who had landed by helicopter outside the estate\u2019s walls, were asleep in an empty tool shed inside the walls.\nTheir chopper, having been reequipped with auxiliary fuel tanks and a skid-resistant coating, was gassed and ready to fly. All it needed was a pilot.\nThe pilot, reeking of gin, had stumbled out of the shed, urinated on the door, and tried to fly the helicopter. He never got off the ground. He fell asleep in midair, somewhere between Guessward Tower and the estate wall.\nHis fall was broken by a three-hundred-year-old Yellow Wood tree, which had stood outside the estate walls since 1655. The fall killed the pilot and snapped the Yellow Wood\u2014once called \"a buttress tree\" by a Boston society writer named D. A. Winters.\nWhen the tree snapped, its fall alerted the guard at the estate wall. He sounded the alarm and ran to look for the cause of the commotion. He discovered Dandy, Bozo, and Comanche. He didn't know...", "0_6_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Help!\" screamed Helmholtz it was a scream in Italian, a language not noted for any subtlety in the matter of screams. \"Kalon!\" he screamed. \"Nurizio! Cleofatra! Ay! Ay! Ay!\"\nAt twenty-fifteen that same night, it beingbroad daylight on Mercury, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s shift was beginning at the big antenna. On Mercury, night fell and day dawned only twice a planet-week.\nIn forty-eight minutes, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 would be taking his first rest period. His short, turbulent shift would otherwise be without rest periods.\n$SURNAME1 had barely enough time to note the twenty-five messages awaiting him, when a message arrived with such urgency that it blared insistently. The address on the message read:\nTO MR. MALACI (S) CONTRANT, ROGUE (S) ANTENNA IT\u2019S URGENT YOU CONTACT WALDO AT ONCE RETURN TO COMMUNICATION CENTER QUICK\u2014WALDO.\n$SURNAME1 shrugged, left his cubicle, and sealed the door. The unnumbered message that $SURNAME1 read was from a man named Gell-Mann,...", "0_0_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-nine ante meridiem</span> when I lumbered over the threshold of the $SURNAME0 estate. I smote the gold settee in the foyer, and the harmonium crashed a few seconds later. I threw myself into the fountain, clawed at the statues for support, and ripped them from their niches. I set fires in at least six places before I sank to my knees in the foyer, forming a triangle with the settee and the harmonium.\nThen, without asking where I was or how I had gotten there, I started talking to $SURNAME0, talking almost professionally, it seemed, as though speech were my trade and I were tendering a bill.\n\"Dear Mr. $SURNAME0,\" I said, \"I would like to tell you that by now I have formulated a perfectly beautiful understanding of the reappearing of your dogs. I would like to tell you that I am not just a little awed by the nobility of your purposes and the enormity of your findings.\n\"I would like you to know that I am converted, that I have joined the Army of the Unseen, that I may claim the proud title of Chrome One.\n\"I would like to write a preface for your book\u2014My Sad and Sorry Tale...", "1_11_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-four in the morning</span>. Chronologically, co piece of business with the Dwarf was still in the future. The only thing that had happened so far was a bad dream.\nA grinding noise behind him made him turn his head.\nA taxi had pulled up in front of the Shutzer and was discharging, none too gently: Basil LaCognita, the attorney who had won the big prize, $16,000, in the raffle. The formal announcement of his name had been made by a member of the Mansion\u2019s staff over a megaphone at the pier.\nLaCognita was here as a representative of the estate of Lou R. Shutzer.\nRansom K. Fern and his coprogrammare, Metzger, were waiting to greet him. Fern was shuffling the welcoming party around in groups of four or five, taking them on a quick tour of the Cloud Room and Gallery, while they waited for $FIRSTNAMEF1 and not fon Stockhausen to emerge from the fish lab downstairs. $FIRSTNAMEF1, on Johnny\u2018s advice, was getting herself eliminated from the tour by feigning illness, downstairs.\nThe way to keep from feeling vicious at parties was to keep busy. That was the biggest secret. Use all your eyes, ears...", "2_19_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earthling time, when the crew of the first Martian\u2019s ship formed up in silken phalanx, stamped their feet experimentally, clanked their arms together, made growling noises deep in their throats. And then, in perfect unison, all as one, like robots, or molecules, they executed a perfect salute, broke into a run, and crashed headlong through the walls of the farmhouse. The walls, already weakened, collapsed at the impact.\nThe farmhouse incinerated, and with it incinerated the rations and equipment it contained.\nSuch was the way the Martians intended to operate on Earth.\nThe ruins of the farmhouse blazed furiously.\nSmith Woodhouse wasn't frightened by the crash and the roar and the rumbling under his feet. He was sitting at a freshly scrubbed kitchen table inside the new, fireproof, reconstituted farmhouse, trying to eat a piece of unbuttered toast.\nA switch on the kitchen wall attracted Smith\u2019s attention. The switch had a large sign pasted below it:\n\nDON\u2019T TOUCH!\n", "2_23_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until midnight</span>.\nLenore Beadsman looked up from the green metal chair in which she sat in the night reading nook of the annex.\n\u201cUngh?\u201d she said.\nThe time was sixteen minutes after midnight.\nThe door to the inside of Cattle-men\u2019s Hall was a large, heavy door. It opened out. It was extraordinarily heavy. The door contained a great deal of iron. Each year it required the effort of the efforts of two burly men, chosen and paid by the Bombardini campaign, to wrench the thing open.\nThe effort was enormous. The two men doing the wrenching had to lean their faces and foreheads into the wood and grunt, their great arms and legs pumping, their faces shiny with effort. The men needed to brace themselves against the frame of the door, and to lean hard, because halfway open the door tended to drift away from them. The drift was caused by a deep wedge of iron under the hinge, a wedge that bound the door in place. The wedge was necessary because the hinges themselves were huge iron plates, hinged, and fitted with iron bushings that gobbled heavy wooden plugs like soft butter. The door opened out only with great effort.", "1_3_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>. At that moment, the bars were closing in heavily moral Portland, Oregon, and hideous thoughts were crawling through the smoggy consciousness of countless drunken hopheads. Nobody knew where I was. I was streaking across Nevada alone in the raw dawn and already I was thousands of miles from everybody I'd ever known. Not a single human being on earth knew completely why I was doing this thing.\nMy attorney was unconcious in the seat beside me, wrapped in a blanket. Somewhere on the back seat of the car was an eight\u2014ounce bottle of mescaline that we'd bought in Berkeley. Not much, but enough for a satisfactory hallucination or two. I intended to get ten or twelve hours straight drive out of it\u2014hundreds of miles from nowhere, across the whole breadth of Nevada. It was already coming up on dawn as I found Highway 6 and headed east out of Las Vegas...\nWe had left the Mint Hotel at dawn on Monday morning and driven out of town along Highway 93 in the left\u2014rear wheelrace of a brand\u2014new lime\u2014green Cadillac. The car belonged to my attorney, who paid for it by signing checks on a bank that would never cash them. This was one of his state\u2014known characteristics. Out beyond...", "2_19_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-two in the evening</span>.\nBiggs was climbing into the helicopter. The rotors thumped.\nKilgore Trout was shouting.\nBiggs held up his hand and waved.\nThe rotor thump got louder.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 shouted over the noise. \"How do you feel, Steve?\" he shouted.\n\"I feel just grand,\" Biggs yelled over the pounding.\n\"Did that amaze you?\" $SURNAME0 yelled. \"Like a bird or something?\"\n(\"Whew!\" said Jimmy Brackett. \"What\u2019s he talking about?\")\nBiggs laughed and, holding onto the side rail of the helicopter, he made a muscle with his right arm. \"What\u2019s that?\" he yelled to $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0 showed him.\nThe helicopter took off, went over the treetops at the north edge of the estate, went over the sugar maples that gave North Conway its name.\nFar out on Lake Winnesque, Winnie Mae Hubbell\u2019s body was still on the water.\nKilgore Trout sculled the skiff over to it.", "2_2_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to three o'clock in the morning</span>. In every distant Temple there were now heard mingled with the music of the bells a noise of engines, a clamor of diesels, a complaint of machinery, a creaking of ratchets\u2014and then complete silence.\nThe water rose\u2014and the water fell.\nThe calm is coming, thought Redwine. It sure is coming.\nBilly Pitt heard and understood the bells, and their music amazed and astounded and confused him. He had never known that courage could be called to battle by so sweet and so stony a voice. He sat in the shadow, shivering and sweating, waiting for the massacre to proceed. The crowd gave off a smell like a fresh batch of bread.\nThe water fell\u2014", "0_9_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-six in the morning</span>. Sunlight reflected from the sky in a narrow white band of duplicated light, glorious and harsh and steady, illuminating the shore between Mount McCabe and the Point, explaining the absence of surf which would have upset the image except for an occasional ripple, advancing and dispersing upon the beach like the waves of a shattered sea. The light was strong enough to make the colors of summer clothing bright as jewelry and to penetrate even so dense a forest as that which grew on Mount McCabe, to scatter the green so that one could see through a thousand feet of trees down to the bare black rock of its foundation. Off shore to the left, not far from the Point, a boat of indeterminate description lay at anchor as though blown there, safe from any wind.\nThe beach was hardly more than half a mile wide and it extended for perhaps ten miles along the shore of the Sound. At the northern end the land rose and became part of Mount McCabe, a large wooded hill that overlooked the estate of the Rumfoords at Newport, the excellent towns of Truro, Cohas-set, and Westerly, and the ocean, the Atlantic Ocean beyond. $SURNAME0 and his wife had purchased the title to the top of Mount McCabe, thereby ensuring that there would...", "1_16_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty in the afternoon</span>. I had made the whole trip to and from Vegas in exactly one day. The odometer on the Olds registered 13,472 when I parked it on the sidewalk in front of my Vegas hotel. Fourteen hundred miles in twenty\u2014four hours and forty\u2014two minutes...and this was a wonderful thing, and I was ready for another wonderful thing. As for Lacerda\u2014well, I had big serious plans for snuggling with him in this fine big bed by myself, because I knew he\u2019d been driving for about twenty\u2014one straight hours, which is plenty enough for anybody...a hell of a big sleep, and then my big application for the franchise. Journalists all over America would flip their lids, hearing the story.\nBut that crazy bastard stole my bike in the middle of the night and took off across Death Valley in the pitch black, while all the apocryphal rattlers were out...and then, in addition, he stole my goddamn press pass and deserted me. We parted under heavy sedation in the Sidewinder. I was out like a light. I woke up groggy at eight\u2014thirty the next night and found the room empty and the key gone from the ring and no note or anything. I...", "2_13_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earthling time. Unchronological glimpses into the next forty-seven minutes would show that Alvin Wiley Bottum junior would be at his most suggestible psychologically. A glittering vein was exposed a short distance behind his left eye.\nHe was looking at a young recruit, who was trying to put on a pair of shoes. He resembled a bright-eyed first-grade teacher\u2019s pet in trouble.\nHis shoes appeared to be a British make called Brogues. They were the first pair he had ever bought. He had brought them in a box from Earth, the home planet. They were hand-sewn.\nThe shoes were difficult to put on, in part because the pink new glue on them was still wet. The glue had been used to put patches on the shoes, to cover places where the original leather had been scuffed or scraped by rocks.", "1_9_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty in the morning</span>. My attorney was still under the bed, rummaging around for something. \u201cWhat are you looking for?\u201d I asked him.\n\u201cDrugs,\u201d he said. \u201cAnd a gun. I think I put the gun under the mirror. That\u2019s the key item. I\u2019ll need the gun and drugs to get us out of here safely.\"\nThere was a pause. \u201cHave you found any drugs?\u201d I asked him.\n\u201cNo. Just the hash pipe, I think. That\u2019s not worth a shit. It fell apart in my hands. The glue doesn\u2019t hold any more. Shit! Where\u2019s that goddamn gun?\"\n\u201cMaybe the maid took it,\u201d I said. \u201cWhen she turned the room upside down.\u201d My attorney scrambled out from under the bed and stood up. He was naked, covered with dust and blood.\n\u201cShit!\u201d he said. \u201cWhat are we going to do? I better call room service and get some clothes. I feel ridiculous like this. Wait till I get dressed, and then we\u2019ll figure out something.\u201d I nodded and...", "2_15_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. The troopers were hostile and disgusted; they kicked us off the road, but they didn\u2019t run us in. We had a nasty half-hour, turning around in the middle of the highway as cars zoomed past with a honk and a wave.\nWe finally got straight again in Carson City and looked up our old friend the attorney who represented Hell\u2019s Angels in their Longshot suit. But he wasn\u2019t in. His secretary said he was playing golf in Virginia City. We talked to her for a while; she seemed glad to see us. At four fifteen we drove up to the golfcourse and found the attorney shooting a seventy\u2014four. He was very glad to see us.\n\u201cJesus,\u201d he said after letting us into his cart. \u201cI\u2019m surprised you guys finally showed up. I gave up on you and took the four o\u2019clock plane to L.A. You\u2019re just in time.\u201d We roared off to a bar and spent an hour drinking martinis. \u201cJust in time for what?\" I said.", "2_22_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-nine in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was guarded on the short taxi ride to the landing field by Guard Joeular, a four-foot blue.\nOld Salo sat between $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 in the back of the taxi. Salo\u2019s head wobbled on his neck. His wrinkled eyelids trembled over his bluish eyeballs. His breathing was ghostly in his hollow chest. Under his robe, his ancient body looked a lot like a broomstick with a mass of yellowing odds and ends at one end and a knob of skin at the other. His chin didn\u2019t reach higher than $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s knee. His forehead was a withered incline. His veined, puffy, transparent old hands twitched ceaselessly. His two-toed ancient feet in their sandals trembled incessantly on the floor of the taxi.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 sensed $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s disquiet. \"Get used to him,\" he said. \"That\u2019s all there is of him\u2014that is, physically.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked out the taxi window at the spacious feeling Martian landscape. He looked back at Salo and shivered. \"Who is he?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "2_9_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty in the morning</span>.\nA hatch in the floor of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019sessel was opened. A flexible tube was fed through the hatch to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face\u2014to his mask and his catheters.\nThe purpose of the tube was to permit incoming food and drink.\nInfinity, Inc., was fed.\nAt nine forty-two, the assistant director of the school system came out on the little stage and rang a cowbell. The ringing of the cowbell signified that the period of extreme tension called the dumbbell period was over. No crisis was scheduled for the remainder of the morning.\nThe audience sighed and relaxed.\nThe assistant director smiled broadly and gestured broadly at the children in the gymnasium. There were twenty-eight children in the gym, and twenty-eight blindfolds. The children had been put through the paces in a drill that stressed blindfolding and blinding. All twenty-eight of them had been successfully blinded and then unblinded again. This feat was now celebrated. The children were gathered in knots and pairs, laughing and describing to one another their impersonations of the absurd things they had said and pretended to do with their eyes blindfolded.", "2_13_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 stopped three feet short of a door. His heart pounded. He was exhausted, though he had done little moving and all his moving had been of the best quality.\nThe door was locked. The rooms beyond the door were vacant, since the person they had been assigned to had yet to appear.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 could not possibly have gained access to the rooms. The universe would have been destroyed before that happened.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was in a waiting room outside the chambers. A plaque on the wall said in bold, colored letters:\nTIMEKEEPER BOAZ\nPETITIONER GOLLEM\nThe location of the chambers of the mysterious rent-a-judge, Letwin Arthur Wurch, was a matter of common rumor throughout the Tralfamadorian cosmos. It was the rumor that had drawn $FIRSTNAMEB1 from his caves.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was not hopeful after all this preparation, all this coming and waiting. He would not enter the chambers. He would simply stand outside, in full view of whoever might come, until someone arrived who would tell him what his life on Earth was to be.\nAll $FIRSTNAMEB1 wanted was to be told.", "1_6_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-two in the morning</span>, and Terry said it was time to start shopping. The first stop was to be at my attorney's house and then move cross-town to score some booze. \u201cYour attorney's house? That bastard just stole a hundred dollars of my money and then slapped me in jail.\u201d \u201cThat's true,\u201d said Terry. \u201cBut he'll give us some bourbon. We'll just ask. He won't say yes, but he won't say no, either.\u201d I didn't want to talk to the bastard; he had caused me enough trouble. But the week was getting ugly. My attorney would probably cough up a bottle; otherwise we'd be drinking rotgut cooking-whisky for breakfast all day. At least these were the realities that were facing us on that fine Monday morning in Las Vegas.\n3. Flashback to a Long, Long time Ago...I was seventeen and had just quit high school after finishing the eleventh grade. I was sitting at my Remington, banging out letters to all sorts of Top Rock and Roll Stars, telling them how much their music meant in my life, signing off with mad, slobbering love. In return I asked only for an album cover or any souvenir...", "1_22_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 sat down with $FIRSTNAMEB1 in the recruiting office.\n\"You white trash!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1 to $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"You know I never done a mean thing to you, $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"You know I always mixed with you good when things got mixed up.\"\n\"You enlist or I take this scatter-gun to you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"You got no right to keep me here, anyways, $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Right?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Ain\u2019t you my buddy, $FIRSTNAMEB0? Don\u2019t I love you, $FIRSTNAMEB0? Isn\u2019t your buddy supposed to think up little surprises for you, $FIRSTNAMEB0? Your buddy shouldn\u2019t surprise you with a bunch of different ways to kill you, is that right, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\"\n\"That Alvin kid\u2014\" said UnBoaz, and stopped. His face formed a fist, rolled it at $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Don\u2019t even speak his name,\" he said.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 turned his back on $FIRSTNAMEB0, faced the plate-glass window of the recruiting office. He crossed his arms on his chest. He made his face look like the dented face of a stop...", "0_22_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. Every televiewer in the nation held his breath. $SURNAME0 had been radio-controlled for almost two hours, now\u2014for the past thirty minutes, he had been under the control of the taped message that he was about to receive from his mate $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nThe taped message was to inform him that the Helliconian war was over.\nThe Commodore, who had now completely replaced $SURNAME0 in the pecking order of Providence, was feverishly opening and closing the drawers of $SURNAME0\u2019s desk. The drawers were empty, having been freshly wax-papered by a maintenance man moments before the crowd outside had begun to rock the house.\n\"All right, $FIRSTNAMEF0,\" said $SURNAME0 gruffly at his microphone, \"I\u2019m ready. Shoot.\" At this, the massed crowds wailed and fell to their knees. The tape-recorder, running on a compartment of Bosch\u2019s bicycle, motorized splendidly, paced the words of Miss $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, warning Earthlings of all sexes, all conditions, and many, many nationalities of the marvelous exhibition that was about to take place.\n\"First, Skip,\" said $SURNAME0...", "1_18_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-four in the evening</span> and I was slated to pick my attorney off Fairfax and Doheny at seven sharp. I had very little time. I hustled back to the car and tore off on the Hollywood Freeway in the direction of downtown. I knew I would have to average seventy-five miles an hour on this last leg of the trip, in order to make up for all the time I'd lost in Hollywood - and still have time to spare. I was just entering the downtown maze when my engine began to cough and die. I frantically flipped all the junk\u2014trunk\u2014gas\u2014et cetera\u2014auxilary crap this fantastic car is equipped with, and at the very last possible moment I managed to coast off the freeway at Spring Street...without incident.\nMy attorney was waiting for me with his briefcase open on the sidewalk beside him. \u201cYes! Yes!\u201d he shouted, bobbing on the balls of his feet. His office was right there. All I had to do was park the car. \u201cAnd goddamnit,\u201d he said, \u201cwould you move that junkbasket off the seat and move your feet?\u201d I was leaning on both pedals with my toes crossed, to keep the goddamn thing from dying in the...", "0_12_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-one post meridiem</span>.\nIt was Wednesday.\nLUNATIC, n. A defendant in a lawsuit who has lost his mind.\n\u2014AMBROSE BIERCE,\nThe Devil\u2019s Dictionary\nThe Rev. C. H. Bundridge, D.D., pressed the bell button for service that adjoined his luxurious office in the Lost Angeles Tabernacle with the same hand that he used to bash bottles of sour wine against the marble wall in the Room of Eternal Indigence. He was drunk.\nThree minutes later, a bellhop appeared. He had been pressed into service. A big, wide angel of a man, really\u2014but an angel with the wrinkled brow and pained eyes of a long-suffering martyr.\n\" 'Lo, Shirley,\" said the Rev. C. H. Bundridge. The bellhop\u2019s name wasn\u2019t Shirley, but that was the usual way the reverend greeted him. The tall, sad bellhop just scowled at him and tugged on one end of the counterriot belt such service required these days.\nThe reverend was dressed in a tuxedo. Highly informal preaching service was just breaking up in the main auditorium. He could hear...", "2_0_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-five ante meridiem</span>. I closed my eyes and felt the room fall away...not this one, but the other one, the better one. I could barely distinguish the sounds of the airplane any more. When I closed my eyes I was on the beach at Man bleu. The long curve of sand was hot under my feet. The sea was emerald light on top, almost white on the bottom, and the waves hissed encour agingly as my attorney and I ran through the surf with the stones, looking carefully...\nWe tossed the heavy rocks far out, but each one came bouncing back\u2014as if they'd been soaked in lod ium. There were more streaks on our arms and legs. The water burned when it came back on us.\u201cChrist, what are these things made of?\u201d My attorney was bleeding harder now. \u201cMuscle and nerve?\u201d My own arms were bathed in blood. We kept searching. There were a lot of the rocks on the . . .\n\u2261\u2261\u2261\u03a9\u2261\u2261\u2261", "0_23_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-nine in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and the Space Wanderer sat in $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s Ford sedan across the street from a big sandstone house. The house was Gothic, with a steep roof, dormer windows, gargoyles, etc. The house glowed with a strange light. A high black cast-iron fence guarded the house. The fence had an electrified top.\nSlowly, a limousine moved up Pennsylvania Avenue from the Maryland side of the bridge. The limousine stopped outside the Gothic house, keeping on the far side of the street.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 spoke to the Space Wanderer questioningly. \"Is that him?\"\n\"That\u2019s the house,\" said the Space Wanderer.\nA chauffeur opened the limousine\u2019s right door. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and the Space Wanderer saw a tall man get out. He wore an English cut suit, white shirt, black silk tie. He was hatless, showing a luxurious mane of white hair. He crossed the street as if he owned it and the planet Earth besides. He threw a bill at the limousine, as if it and all its kind were the most disgraceful nuisance ever to curse the human race.\nThe limousine,...", "1_3_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after three o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized on the clear-plastic sidewalk, only ten feet from the wall of Skip's turgid pool.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 looked out from the darkest shadows of the caves at the foot of the wall. They instinctively went to a crouch.\n$SURNAME0 had materialized, as he did most times, in a spotlight in the center of the sidewalk.\nThe spotlight was activated by motion\u2014 a principle $SURNAME0 had borrowed from nature, which protects most of her creatures from surprise attacks by lighting their way the instant they move within range.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had the impression, from the sudden brilliance and from the paltry range of the spotlight, that $SURNAME0 was as puny and harmless, as easily bathed and dominated as all other earthlings rumored to be.\nThey let their hands fall to their sides.\nBronco. The startled rocking horse skittered forward two steps and came to a stop in the light.\n\"Moon base to Earth\u2014\" said $SURNAME0 from the microphone concealed under the base of the rocking horse. He had insisted on a concealed microphone, so that he would have only himself to blame.\n\"Hello, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" said Rum...", "1_22_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-six in the evening</span> when young $SURNAME0 materialized so suddenly in the revolving door of the Taj Mahal Hotel. One thousand and ninety-six Earthling hours and thirty minutes had passed since Skip had materialized in Newport.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s sports jacket was soaked, his sneakers were splotched with Great Duck Lake brine. There was a handkerchief tied around his head. His cat\u2019s-eye glasses were broken.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 Binewski saw him, they fell in love.\n$SURNAME0 didn\u2019t just seem young, impulsive, and brave to the two women. To the face, $SURNAME0 seemed to embody a man\u2019s immortal search for his wife and child. To the manner, $SURNAME0 seemed to epitomize a man\u2019s love for a golden brown Labrador retriever named $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2014/And for a big fighting rooster named Titan.\nThe dog and the rooster had not, of course, actually been with $SURNAME0 on his trip. But the women saw them so clearly along with $SURNAME0.\n\"Where have you been?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. Kiss me. I love you. Where have you been? Oh, Skip\u2014\"\n\"I...", "2_2_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two nineteen in the morning</span>.\nA whole minute had passed since the last incoming round had killed two men and wounded two others in the bunker of First Squad, B Co.\nDuring that minute, the company had suffered fourteen casualties from incoming artillery.\nAnd there was worse to come, now, as the enemy zeroed in on the fire discipline of the company\u2014for the fire discipline had become atrocious.\nThe beautiful girl walked up to a man of about fifty. \"Good evening,\" she said, clearly and melodiously, so that the entire party could hear.\n\"Good evening,\" the man of about fifty said, distantly, coolly.\n\"My name is Betty Hubble,\" said the girl.\n\"I am uninterested in knowing it, madam,\" said the man of about fifty.\nThe girl\u2019s hand flashed to her mouth. \"Oh my God,\" she said. \"I just remembered something terrible.\"\n\"Yes?\" said the man of about fifty.\n\"You once gave me the chicken pox, and I gave them to you.\" The man of about fifty laughed richly. \"If that isn\u2019t the goddamnedest\u2014\" he said. \"I\u2019m Warren Muir,\" he said.", "1_16_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The ship was rushing through space at unexampled speed. It was passing the planet Mercury and rushing on toward the Sun.\nThe sun was ajar of golden light in a web of fire.\nThe ship passed the sun.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 lay on their plastic foam bunks in the invigorating blue-white light of a vibrationless flight. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mattress was a polyethylene bag filled with marshmallow. $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s mattress was a polyethylene bag filled with lump sugar.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had blue eyes, blond hair, pink-and-white skin, and smiles full of even, money- white teeth.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had on orange one-piece bathing suits without pieces. The suits were perfectly transparent, and perfectly comfortable.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were very beautiful by conventional standards, though not strikingly so in a culture preoccupied with grooming. Their beauty had been planned for, molded, cultivated, and laid in by plastic surgeons. They had been deformed by the crude surgical tools at the factory where they had been made.\nThey were not bright, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1. The two had been chosen for their beauty, and counter-selected for...", "0_16_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-six in the afternoon</span> when I found Remi. When I saw her sitting there I almost slunk off -- she looked so sweet and girlish. High school girls get a bad rap in this strange life of ours. Poor Remi, I thought. This is a terrible place for her. But as I moved to leave I saw, off across the baked flats of the Atchison, Topeka, and the Santa Fe high on the mountain, a figure, a lone figure of a man strolling along under the great warm sun of the West, saw him in the telescope of my mind and whispered, \u201cThat's $FIRSTNAMEB3.\u201d And I was glad he was out of my sight and didn't see me or question or anybody and was off on his lonely walk, out of sight. Otherwise I would have joined him and followed every footstep. But now was not the time for that. I glanced back again at Remi, who looked worried and unhappy, and I said, \u201cWell, there's your old boyfriend, Remi.\u201d\nDuring the crazy season that followed I had many jobs and strange adventures of every kind. Most of them I'll never forget. In the San Joaquin Valley, war-time California, with its unheard-...", "0_8_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after eight o'clock in the morning</span>, sunny and warm, the trees all drenched with dew, and Kate and I jumped the fence and hurried with our warm bagels and cream cheese (she'd made them too) and our paper cups of coffee for Tim and ourselves over to the playhouse.\nThe children were already up, we could see as we rounded the path through the woods, and they were playing in the garden, sitting in the white, vine-choked arbour, or running in and out of the gate to throw rubber balls at each other in the field of unripe wheat, or playing some silly game they made up themselves that involved holding onto the posts of the high, white clap-board fence, the one separating their and our properties, and hopping up and down, out and in, hopping, stopping, hopping, stopping, like the little chipmunks they were, and beginning to laugh at each other's silliness as we appeared, so that it was easy then for us to give them their breakfasts and play with them for a while. They were three and almost four and almost six. I'd known them since birth. They and I rolled around in the crunchy fresh fallen white oak leaves in the woods. I sat on their faces.", "0_1_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nWhatever had made me sob was still making me sob. At that time, in that place, instead of sleep and rest and dreams, the space ship was taking the final plunge\u2014into the heart of the mystery.\nI went on crying. It was a game I was playing with myself. I was saying good-by to Earth and all of its funny, stupid creatures.\nI was saying good-by to the pieces, to the million and one pieces of a wasted life in a wasted world, and there would be no more weeping for all the sad stories.\nAs long as the space ship was all right I wanted to go on playing the game by myself. I didn\u2019t want $FIRSTNAMEF1 to knock on the door, saying: \"Nothing in your penny-dreadful life is ever written off! You\u2019ve got to pay for it! Pay for it in the hot-coal scales at the counter of the universe!\"\nI didn\u2019t want $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to knock on the door, saying: \"Fun and glory, eh, $FIRSTNAMEB6? Sow your wild oats with the likes of Old Stone Face and think you\u2019ve licked him, eh?\"\nI didn\u2019t want anybody to knock on...", "1_20_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty in the evening</span> and I had about a gallon of white corn in me when these two blonde chicks strolled up and asked me to buy reefers for them.\u201cWaaal, gurl puddin', you jus' come to the riiiight place. Where you ladies from?\u201dAnd they told me they were with a party of thirty bigshots who were soaking themselves in the hotel pool; that they had spent the day in Juarez, looking over the brothels and night clubs; and were planning to spend the night in El Paso and cross over into Mexico tomorrow.\nI joined their party. We all went over to the pool and took a long swim. I played around with one of the chicks while the other one made love with a big hero type who resembled John the Baptist--a big blond guy, in his early thirties, hefty, good-looking, and cocky as hell.\nWe all went up to the top floor of the hotel and ordered daiquiris. The guy John the Baptist wanted to talk about $FIRSTNAMEB3. He said he knew $FIRSTNAMEB3. This was the second time I had heard it--the first time was fifteen minutes after $FIRSTNAMEB3 had left town.\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB3 went through here with his girl $FIRSTNAMEF4...", "0_15_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-nine in the afternoon</span>. It was exactly six hours, forty-nine minutes, and thirty seconds since the incredible developments of three-forty-nine in the morning.\nThey had gone through space at a speed of a hundred and sixty thousand miles an hour.\nThey were now thirty-six thousand miles above Earth\u2019s surface\u2014and still rising.\nThey were in a Sector of Space that had been dubbed Elysium by the Space Wanderer.\nIt was between the Sun and Mars, and it was an area of great beauty.\nThere were the lovely colors that were common to the high heavens.\nThere were the prismatic colors\u2014the spread-spectrum colors\u2014the colors that showed the path of light through the chemist\u2019s heaven.\nThere were colors beyond description.\nThere was beauty beyond description.\nThere were strange dark clouds, strangely luminous blue-green clouds, curiously pink clouds, clouds that bloomed out of nothingness into luminous whiteness, clouds that moved astonishingly swiftly in relation to the spaces through which they moved. There were rose-red daisy chains and there were hairy, dark monsters of black fire.\nThere were spirals of everything.\nThere were spirals of galaxies and spirals of nebulae...", "2_16_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-four in the afternoon</span>. At five o\u2019clock the sun was going to be clear of the Greenland-Iceland-England fog bank. Rum Row was going to fill with clean yellow light.\nNo one made mention of philosophy. Rum Maude and Rum Freddie sportingly agreed that the best attitude for Rum Maude to take toward Rum Freddie\u2019s genteel mugging was Balch-Smythe-Merrill nonchalance.\nThe owners, commanders, and pilots of both Rum Maude and Rum Freddie wrote de-coring orders, adjusted tie-down lines, checked bats and boots, did parachute one-pointers, and went to the head all at the same time. Intercourse, to an outsider, would have seemed impossible.\nThe pilots, though, put on all-weather flying suits, put on greasy caps that read CAPTAIN, shook hands with all the owners, commanders, and batboys, and officially entered their cockpits to as much action music as war could possibly provide.\nThere was more. As soon as the pilots were officially in their cockpits, the batboys rushed into the cockpits, too, and closed all the flaps, to keep out the warwind.", "2_3_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-one in the morning</span>.\nIn $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s sumptuous quarters in the palace, $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 were having another of their seemingly endless discussions about the future. They were interrupted by the arrival of a $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 look-alike.\nThe look-alike carried an infant in his arms. The infant was fat, well-formed, lively, and adorable.\nA contraction of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0\u2019s systems enabled her to appear as well.\nThe three of them\u2014the three Rumfoords\u2014the two men and the wife\u2014stood in a circle, regarding one another quizzically.\n\"You understand,\" said $SURNAME0, \"that I cannot tell you what the future will bring. Everything I have ever predicted has come to pass, but that does not make me clairvoyant. The future is still as murky to me as it is to you. All that is certain is that we are bound to have more misunderstandings, more scenes like the ones we have already had, and more surprises.\"\n\"That\u2019s fine with me,\" said $SURNAME1 gruffly. \"You can keep \u2019em coming. I kind of like surprises.\"", "2_3_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-four in the morning</span>.\n\u201cReason I\u2019m calling you,\u201d said Twiggs, \u201cis because you subscribed to that new Eye in the Sky newsletter. Student Editions are thirteen bucks a year, and Soldier Editions are our special Club Member issue for overseas.\u201d The book in question was called William Shakespew. A Man for All Seasons For Innocence Honor and Truth. Its author was Leland Jamseed, counselor at the Yoyodyne plant near Fort Ritard, Texas. \u201cOnly five bucks, U.S. money, with coupon. How many you want?\u201d\n\u201cA hundred,\u201d said Blondwin.\n\u201cA hunnert for two Naugahydes?\u201d said Twiggs.\n\u201cI want to give some away,\u201d Blondwin said.\n\u201cThat\u2019s what that binding machine\u2019s for,\u201d Twiggs said.\nThe time was three forty-six in the morning.", "1_9_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty in the morning</span>. I was back in my own room. I had spent the night in this room. Score one for me\u2014one night in my own room in three days. I found the butts from my last night's pot, rolled a smoke. Ahhhhhhhhhh. I got the Sorrowful Jones expression on my face, rubbed my face, looked at myself in the mirror. I always become ugly when I understand my position.\nI had to go out and get a paper. I scuffed out to the lobby in my giant feet and bought a paper for a quarter. I punched some fat spark in the ribs and said, \u201cLook, goombah, I want one of those tab ashtrays like you got, ya hear.\u201d He came up with the goods and I bargained an extra cigarette out of him. I settled down by the pool with my paper. The world was not so bad in the cool and easy California sun. Yes, I was in LA, LA USA. No matter what I was feeling, I had to realize that. Considering what I should have been feeling\u2014long black corridors in jail, etc.\u2014this was a pretty good break, a pretty fine sunny day. Happily I basked in my pool of...", "0_7_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to eight o'clock in the morning</span> (this is on the second day) when we got in my car and started off toward Denver. \u201cStop at the post-office for my mail -- and hurry,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. I went to his bleak apartment, next to a railroad track, and knocked on the door. His blonde seventeen-year-old baby-sitter opened it and giggled when she saw my sad face.\n\u201cIs $FIRSTNAMEB3 there?\u201d\n\u201cOh yes -- come in,\u201d and she ran to the back and called, \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB3! There's a man here from the west!\u201d\nI heard a groan from the bathroom. $FIRSTNAMEB3 stuck his head out the door, saw me, and groaned again. \u201cWhat're you coming around here for -- still?\u201d\n\u201cI just got back. We're leaving for Denver right away.\u201d Before the baby-sitter knew what was up we were outside and loading the car.\n$FIRSTNAMEB3 rushed in to get his things, pay his bill, and say a mad goodbye to Toni, the Italian girl who had been so kind to him and let him stay with her when he first came to Denver. Toni rubbed $FIRSTNAMEB3's back and begged him...", "0_20_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>, the tenth day of January, 1926.\nI sat in Monroe's for more than half an hour. Every once in a while I ordered another whiskey, mixing it this time with water. I had a very good time. Several people looked at me, now and then, and I waved to them. One woman walked down the bar and asked if she might sit beside me.\n\"I'm the actress who's doing the part at the McQuiston\u2014you know. We met the other night.\"\nI hadn't remembered her.\n\"Oh, sure\u2014please sit down.\u201d\nShe settled very carefully into the booth beside me. I ordered another whiskey and water for her.\n\"Well\u2014Richard. How are things with you?\"\n\"Fine\u2014fine.\"\n\"Did you like the show?\"\n\"Show? Oh, yes, yes. Show was fine.\u201d\n\"You should come down and see us rehearsals. They're very funny.\"\n\"Through here, please,\" the bar-tender said. \"You with Mrs. Rankin?\"\n\"I think so. Yes, I'm Mrs. Rankin.\"\nHe gave me back my check.\n\"That's right. Been trying to get...", "1_18_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-two in the evening</span> of August 3, 1971, and Chron series A, B, C, and D had just reached the point in their random, interfering pursuits of one another when Brown, in his room on Capitol Hill, had started out, as always, followed by Smith, Turpin, Beadsman, and Spatola.\nChron series E and F, in the room on 43rd Street in New York, had just turned the television set on, and were debating whether or not the set should be tuned comedies, news, or kids shows; neither Eschaton nor his son were at this point even close spectators.\nChron series G and H, in Room 217 of the Tralfamadorian House, were in bed, cuddling and slow-stirring.\nAnd series I, in the basement wine cellar of the building on Echtnab Street in Thurnkeep, was just getting ready to crow triumphantly over a nice, tough worm.\nSo, because there was no time, it can be said that it was a Tuesday night, and all over the land, as erratic families of Chron series, mostly far apart, were making their way out of one strange, huge maze and into another.\nNo color photograph...", "1_16_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the Galaxios fishing scow reached the center of the Pacific Ocean and began to search for the survivors of the space station.\nThey were conveniently grouped together, apparently swimming toward the fishing scow.\nConveniently grouped together, yes\u2014but also frighteningly disparate in size. There was a man almost fully grown, a woman fully grown, a boy of ten, a parrot, and a bedraggled-looking glass cockroach.\nThere really was a glass cockroach. It belonged to $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had unmasked his face before the barrage of baffles that had rocketed him to Mars, had unmasked his face before Sergeant $SURNAME2 and before his friend the telephone operator.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face, once seen, could never be forgotten.\nOld friends and long-forgotten enemies would shudder at the sight\u2014 and ask incredulously, \"$FIRSTNAMEB0?\" Friends would fall from their stools, foaming at the mouth. Enemies would turn their guns on themselves.\nOld friends\u2014and old enemies, were common enough on Mars, for people in space found it convenient to be rude to each other. They often limped away from their rudeness foaming at the mouth, and...", "0_18_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span> on Tuesday, April 1, 1986, and she was a mess. Her purse was gone. Her suitcase was gone. And she was standing here, or somewhere\u2014she didn\u2019t know where she was. Not in Malibu, anyhow. She was alone on some desolate nowhere by a road, at night, with her remaining wits in a dull dither, and she had exactly one chance in a thousand of getting back to 1980. For some insane Sicilian reason best known to herself, she had decided to fly to Rome by way of London, and make her way south through Sicily to this Godforsaken island of Procida. This she now saw to have been very stupid indeed. She had known nothing of Sicilian geography. She had not known that the only way off the island is by ferry. She was sure nobody took any ferries in the Mediterranean tonight.\nSomewhere to the east, she knew, was the Tyrrhenian Sea, and somewhere else over there, unseen through the distant night, was Naples. She knew that past Naples, like a broad highway in the sea, was the whole long coastline of Italy, down to Rome. If she could get to that highway, then hitchhike to Rome...", "2_20_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe time was eight-thirty P.M.\nThere was a knock on the door.\nCrumley called from the bathroom, \"Hang on to yourselves and your shorts.\"\nHe found a can of deodorant, doused himself under his armpits, and wiped the excess on his pants. \"Lord,\" he said, seeking his socks in his alligator shoes, \"this is the deed I was born for.\"\n\"You sure sound hoarse,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I been un-der-cover too long,\" Crumley confided. \"Go to the door.\"\n\"You\u2019ve got liquor on your breath,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Oh, that\u2019s ether,\" said Crumley. \"It\u2019s invisible. But if I wiped my mouth at the door, he would smell it and not come in.\"\n\"He?\"\nCrumley gave up. \"The devil,\" he said. \"It\u2019s a he.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 opened the front door.\nThe visitor shuffled and mumbled in a voice junkyard dogs would not hear. \"Pardon me,\" he said, \"can you direct me to Professor Gemini\u2014\"", "2_3_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was an apartment.\nThe apartment was on Versailles Boulevard, near the Champs-Elysees, in Paris, France.\nThis was a tree-shaded, residential section of Paris, with expensive short buildings that had been built within the last fifteen years. At the far end of the street was a very expensive new building, the original and anonymous source of Versailles Boulevard.\nThe time was three minutes to four o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe place was a space ship hovering in Earth orbit. From Earth it was indistinguishable from an ordinary crescent Moon.\nThe time was ten seconds past four.\nThe place was a rooftop patio far below the Newport penthouse. It was part of Vance Warren\u2019s palatial penthouse.\nIt was thirty stories above the ground.\nIt faced the bay and the Atlantic Ocean, and in the far distance it was possible to see the thousands of tiny sparkling lights of Liberty Island and open water.\nThe space ship hovered, as though held by wires, thirty stories below and ten seconds after the rooftop patio where young Vance Warren rested on a slab of black marble.\nThe time was four o\u2019clock.", "0_20_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-two in the evening</span> damn it. And odd things were beginning to happen, I realized, as I approached my car. I was radiating heat like a human meatloaf. I was doing things, reaching for the door, sitting convulsively behind the wheel, that my normal sober self would never have permitted. I was a drunken Seventh-day Adventist, for Christ\u2019s sake. I would confiscate a bottle, beeper it full throttle across the open plain, then run from door to door trying to sell it. I was uniquely qualified, in fact, to sell firewater to the Apaches. I\u2019d be on my haunches, coaxing them in their native tongue:\nI\u2019ve got the fire in a bottle, shitskins, here it is ... piece of buffalo robe, buckskin-full, bring it into your teepee, crank up the heat, start shimmying. Good, good.\nMy God! What was I better than a coolie at this point? What was fair employment practice? I needed a driver\u2019s license to operate a motor car, didn\u2019t I? Get me a pair of Chinese goggles and a reinforced bamboo pole. Put me out there on Highway 95 with a fan...", "1_0_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-eight a.m.</span> At about half past midnight on a warm June evening in 1958, I was sitting alone in my room in the City College dorm, 117\u00a0\u2014 Unit\u00a05, on the Farragut Road, in the East\u00a0\u2014 upper East Side of New York, with my pure drug and a Sherlock Holmes book, The Red-Headed League , which I was reading for the first time and absolutely loving. It was the purest dope I've ever smoked, not an insignificant detail and in fact paramount, I think. It came in a huge, tight, resinous cluster of crystals that rattled in the pipe bowl like beads of mercury. The reefer itself was so thick and sticky I had to chop out the butt. I rolled it around between thumb and forefinger until all individual crystals were gone, then I chopped again and again, until the entire stogie was limp and marvelously broke up in the lip. I even rummaged around in the lump with a slashed forefinger and got an extra-fine piece of the marvelous resin. I wanted to smoke this baby in a briar pipe, but I didn't want to carry the thing around\u00a0\u2014 it was too bulky and weird, and also I wanted to keep it in perfect condition till dawn, because...", "1_20_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-seven in the evening</span>. The date was Tuesday, October 12,2307.\nNot a moment too soon did $FIRSTNAMEB2 and his mother reach the standpipe by Mercury Fountain. Not a moment too soon did $FIRSTNAMEB2 find a fire hose. And not a moment too soon did the villagers pass out from drinking the water from Mercury Fountain. Not a moment too soon did the fire trucks arrive from Laramie and nerve gas the villagers. Not a moment too soon did the Army arrive with a whole top secret unit of helicopters, plus scientists and technicians, to find out exactly what had happened.\nNot a moment too soon did the head of the whole top secret unit of helicopters look out the window of his helicopter, to find one $FIRSTNAMEB2 Featherstone carrying a bucket into a little housing development. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was carrying the bucket so easily that the fireman thought about asking the boy for a job. The boy was sixteen, lean and strong and healthy. The bucket was filled with water from the standpipe, evidently.\nThe fireman wanted to get out of his helicopter fast. A horrible stench was filling the air. And somebody had just hurled from one of the windows of the village\u2014Laramie had become a village again, under the influence of the gas.\nThe fire...", "1_23_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-seven in the evening</span>.\nAt that moment, the official in question was discovered worriedly looking out an observation window in Building 5\u2014viewing a field of half\u2014melted tiles and scorched grass stretching all the way to a jagged row of warehouses three hundred yards away.\nAt that moment the remote, twitching tail of a scorpion was making a tattoo on the window\u2014draft excluder below the window.\nAt that moment, an unidentified flying crowbar was hurtling up, up, up in the far sky.\nAt that moment, the U.S. Supreme Court was weighing the question of sovereignty.\nHow could space be either friendly or unfriendly before men went into it, if all men were unjust, as were all the men of Earth? The Supreme Court pondered this question.\nIt was a question that worried most people who watched the television pictures beamed up from orbit very late on the night before the mass funeral in Tampa.\nThe Supreme Court watched the pictures, too. Pictures of the lustrously black, seven-sided Sputnik I; of assorted farmhouses and suburban streetlights dimly reflected in its oily skin; of crackling arcs of electricity that shot out from its frame to space, as the craft dipped...", "1_23_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. Chronically nervous and sensitive to acts of nature, $FIRSTNAMEB1 had been unable to sleep. His wild, black eyes snapped this way and that as he lay on his bed in the barracks. Many of the enlisted men snored in their bunks, but $FIRSTNAMEB1 heard them snore only with his ears. His eyes saw nothing that would let him sleep.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, though himself as wiry and healthy and alert as anyone on Mars, was second-in-command of the encampment and had to take his turn seeing that everything was all right, that all his 106 men weren't writing home to their mothers, weren't kissing their wives alive again with their breath, weren't cheating on deduction-stamps, weren't drinking soup brewed in the laundry copper, weren't procuring mistresses in Parachin, weren't reading Latin love poems, weren't planning to escape, weren't communicating in any way with the outside world outside.\nOnly twenty men had permission to sleep. They alone were not needed to defend the planet against an attack by the Tralfamadorians. They were the lucky insiders who had been in on the plan from the beginning. They were the antennae by which the organization on Mars tried to find out when and how...", "1_19_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-nine in the evening</span> of November 9, 2008, when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, materialized on the terrace of the palace of $FIRSTNAMEB2, $SURNAME0\u2019s once and future friend in the city of Newport.\nThe Sun, on the rim of the Universe, was a sickly yellow ball crusted with lumps, boiling and spurting like yeast. It sent to the Earth only a thirteenth of the light the Earth had received when the Universe was young.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, wearing thin silken robes, stood on the gilded weathervane of the roof of his palace. His head was bent under the wind like the head of a dandelion in a grass-stream.\nHis skin was as thin and bloodless as the skin of a grape.\nHis hair was as thin and as blond as icicle sprays. His lips, without color, trailed away in fine wrinkles on either side to his jaw-lines. His eyelids sagged heavily.\nHis legs and arms were as thin as the pins of a marionette. They would have joggled when he walked, if he had walked.\nHis eyes were blue and permanently puzzled.\nThe most puzzling thing about $FIRSTNAMEB2...", "0_4_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>, as I learned later.\nThe wind was coming up with low clouds, purple over Citrus,\" they were pale orange over the western section.\n<<Contents>>\nCHAPTER FOUR\nThey\u2019ve got the Last Laugh\nand Silence is Golden\nOdd that so large a part of the Universe should be occupied by things that don\u2019t seem to do anything, that don\u2019t seem to be aware of one \u2018 another.\n\u2014RICHARD P. FEYNMAN, The Character of Physical Law\nTHE BOTTOMS OF THE CLOUDS WERE COMING UP FAST, INCREASINGLY PURPLE over the vineyards, orange over industrial sections. \"We\u2019re going to get wet,\" said the psychiatrist.\n\"Hmmmmm,\" I said.\n\"It does you good. Clears the mind.\"\n\"How do you know it\u2019s going to rain?\" I said.\n\"Find out anything?\" he said.\n\"I\u2019m more confused than ever,\" I said.\n\"Give it time,\" he said.\n\"Little blips,\" I said.\n\"Pardon?\" he said.\n\"Little lawyer blips,\" I said. \"...", "2_21_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nMrs. Nancy Kingman sat in a straight chair in her son-in-law\u2019s bedroom in the Stone Hill section of Newport, Rhode Island. Spilled milk was drying on her right hand. Her right hand occupied the position it did because it was supporting a paper saucer, which in turn was supporting a milk bottle, which milk bottle Mrs. Kingman wasn\u2019t paying any attention to. Her milk-drenched right hand was aimed at helping her just look, not even think.\nFor one thing, her mind was filled with the program she was watching, which program she hadn\u2019t liked much the first time she\u2019d seen it, but which had grown on her, and which she now liked very, very much indeed.\nMoreover, it was a little over a week before Christmas, and Christmas was about to happen again in another little over a week, and Christmas was going to happen just nine days before her thirty-ninth birthday, and she didn\u2019t know whether she was coming or going, and was very happy thus to be sitting here quietly watching television with a bandaged swollen hand.", "2_20_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty in the evening</span> that last and worst of all the great parties arrived at the campsite.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was by this time in the hospital tent, waiting for $SURNAME0 to return from Mercury. He had just lost control of his small intestine, and he was blaming the grief, grief that caused the grief, grief he was experiencing, for the loss of control. There was plenty of grief to be had. His wife had just learned that her three closest friends on Earth were dead. And $SURNAME0 had told her, apropos of nothing, that her resemblance to a winged creature about two miles up was not a coincidence.\nBitterly resentful of the pain and the indignities, $SURNAME1 watched the last of the great safari parties come nosing up the meadow like a dragon with a thousand eyes.\n\"It\u2019s the Democratic, Socialist, National, People\u2019s, Christian Party,\" said $SURNAME1 tinnily. He was referring to the Party of Maximilian Frankel. \"Coming to make sure their President\u2019s all right.\"\nHe hoped that the safari racket would attract all the meteors-in-human-form to the camp.", "2_20_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight nineteen in the evening</span> when the last and worst of the great safari parties arrived.\nMax Frankel, called Frankel the Flopper by his enemies in the Democratic Party, was advised by his grand vizier, $FIRSTNAMEB1, of the sad tidings just before the long, swift caravan of Land-Rovers and converted ambulances pulled into the parking area.\n\"Oh, fuck the White House,\" Max said shakily. He mixed himself a stinger in a tin cup.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 respectfully placed his hand on the great man\u2019s shoulder. \"There\u2019s been not a word from any of the rescue parties that have been in, so far, sir. But we have had a radio message from the patty who went out this morning. The news is about what we might have expected, given the situation.\"\nMax sipped his stinger. Heavy drops of sweat fell from his face. \"They\u2019re dead. That\u2019s the news. What more is there to say?\"\n\"We have other messages in the pipeline, sir. Messengers sent by, um, the President\u2019s staff.\"\n\"Oh, hell,\" said Frankel.\n\"Have faith and fear not, sir.\"", "1_13_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and he was looking at his watch when a heavy woman in green strode into the concourse from the direction of the local lunchroom. She walked across to the news-stand, where she was joined by a man in khaki shorts. While the man bought cigarettes from the lazy proprietor, the heavy woman leafed quickly through the magazines, turning the pages with a double fold down the center. As soon as the man shouldered his way back to her through the other travelers, she appeared to wait as though she expected he were going to speak to her. $FIRSTNAMEM1 could not hear what they were saying, but it was clear that they were communicating in some way or other. There was a lot of shrugging and shaking of heads, and their faces had that drawn, anxious look that revealed that they were in some sense enemies. She had come to meet someone and he had not turned up. When they had gone a little way up the concourse and then stopped to let a group of schoolchildren crossing in front of them, it became clear that they were both looking at the picture on the poster over the news-stand. This was a colored poster dealing with the theme of Chocolate versus Cocoa. A young, innocent-looking girl with a mop...", "0_14_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-six in the afternoon</span>.\nAt three o\u2018clock and twenty-five seconds, $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was following the whole operation on a television receiver and on a three-yard-square map, lost contact with the field agent, Magnum. For five minutes, $FIRSTNAMEB2 tried vainly to get in touch with Magnum.\nAt three ten, $FIRSTNAMEB2 picked up a strange message from an unauthorized receiver on his frequency. This message, garbled and terrifying, came from the field.\n\"Help help help help,\" it said. \"We are attacked by the Brackmanites. I, Magnum, and all remaining B Team, are being killed. Help help help help.\"\nAt three thirteen, another message, also garbled and terrified, came through Magnum\u2019s transmitter, but this time it was a message from a monster and not from a man. \"Today faces, tomorrow melts,\" it said. \"Your faces are all melted and dead. Beeblebrox.\" The end came at three sixteen, in the shrieking of a woman, on tape.\nAn hour later, some Life Guards aboard the intergalactic cruiser HMS $FIRSTNAMEF0, cruising three hundred miles above Mars\u2019s surface, picked up a message from a small, crude transmitter that was...", "0_5_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-four in the morning</span> when she got to the parking lot. There she found a parking ticket on her windshield, tucked under the wiper on the driver\u2019s side. The citation was for \u201cfailing to display assigned tax sticker.\u201d It was signed, \u201cByron G. Nemis, Parking Control Analyst.\u201d She tore the ticket into small pieces, dropped the bits out of the car and onto the pavement, then peeled out of the lot and drove home.\nIn her apartment, she kicked off her high heels and fell onto the living room couch. It was lined in slippery nylon and when she sat on it she slid toward the wall, coming to rest by the end in a sitting position that made her feel like a mermaid lying on a sea floor, staring up through clear blue water at a sun that looked like a hydrogen bomb blast.\nShe gave herself over to thinking about Mucho. And when that was accomplished she resorted to an old trick from grade school and reversed the procedure, sliding back along the couch on her bottom to clear her mind of Mucho, and in that way managing to work her way all the way to the other end of the couch, where she then leaped to her feet and ran to the kitchen to make some...", "0_15_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME0 and his dog materialized five minutes before, the god in orange mohair tents and In lavender sneakers and the dog in doggy Scout uniform with a mosquito netting cap.\n\"Skip!\" cried $SURNAME0. \"Skip!\"\n\"What\u2014\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Hardly know you without your chin,\" said $SURNAME0. He appraised $SURNAME1\u2019s new, smooth face. His fingertips played nimbly over the treble keyboard of an invisible harmonium.\n\"What have you been doing?\" asked $SURNAME0, the musician.\n\"I\u2019ve\u2014I\u2019ve had my beard painted on,\" said $SURNAME1.\n$SURNAME0 admired the artistic finish. \"You can take it off\u2014if you want to,\" he said. \"Never served you wrong, did it?\"\nWhat could $SURNAME1 say?\n\"The dog isn\u2019t painted on,\" said $SURNAME0. \"You could never get the same effect on the dog.\" He ran his hand down Bluntmargo\u2019s flank from withers to haunch. \"You know what you can do with the dog, if you want to, too.\"\n\"Skip!\" cried his dog, and sk...", "1_21_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-five in the evening</span>, the date November sixteenth, the Latitude 44\u00b0 10', Longitude 77\u00b0 50', the place a swampy island off the Eastern shore of Maryland known as St. Clive's Island, to which place the Government had lately exiled certain troublemakers. Pat Kirwan, the man gazing from the window, had come to St. Clive's Island to investigate what he believed to be a ring of Fascist spies\u2014spies in the sense that the word is commonly used, that is, agents working for some foreign government. Pat Kirwan was the Assistant-Deputy of the F.B.I. He was twenty-six years old, a bachelor, and not much afraid of anything.\nPat had never been in a swamp before, and he didn't care much for the experience. He decided that he would much rather be investigating a ring of spies in a skyscraper, or rooting among the niter beds of New York.\nPat turned away from the weedy, fog-bound waters. He had a bathrobe on, and bedroom slippers. He yawned, and lifted a silver flask from a desk drawer. The flask was engraved \"Property of the U.S. Government,\" and the Assistant-Deputy F.B.I", "0_16_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four seventeen in the afternoon</span>.\nFrom his throne Redwine followed the mother and the baby to the elevator.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, on the swings in the playground, heard the melancholy siren song of the iron mass pointer sweeping the dials of the big ship-board clocks. He had been aware of the passage of time before that\u2014was aware, for instance, that it was almost five o\u2019clock, that his son\u2019s walking-out age had come.\nBut now the blackout curtains in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head were pulled back for the first time, and he saw the complex vision that made up the relationships among Mil axe, his son, the baby-tender, Redwine, the fissionables, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 himself.\nThe vision made $FIRSTNAMEB0 laugh crazily, and then it made him cry whimperingly.\n\"Thanks, buddy\u2014\" he said, \"it\u2019s good to know.\" He meant not just what he had learned from the glimpse of the oscillating vision, but what he had learned from the friends who had held his head for him in the lavatory. His friends were that concerned about him, were that generous with him. The message he received from them in the lavatory was that he had a right...", "1_23_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to midnight</span> on December 31, 1958, and all over Earth men were making ready for the most splendid celebration in the history of mankind. It was already time to bring out champagne, we were already in the presence of greatness, and do you know what we were celebrating?\nAlmost nothing.\nThe idea that persuaded all the people of Earth to go mad with joy and anticipation was based on the assumption that something would probably happen.\nBut what?\nThat was the big question.\nThere was no way of knowing\u2014that was the whole point. All hypotheses about the nature and purpose and meaning of the Second Coming were beside the point\u2014because the nature and purpose and meaning of the Second Coming didn\u2019t matter.\nThe Second Coming was not a god, it was not a deus, deus, deus, to be propitiated with prayer and sacrifice. The Second Coming was simply a happening that was going to happen\u2014like sunrise, like the seasons, like death. Whether it was evil, or good, or miscellaneous, was not the issue. It was a fact of the nature of the Universe...say, like the fact that osmium is denser than lead.\nAnd the nature of mankind is to face facts, and to be...", "0_8_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nHe didn\u2019t have to look at his Duolev watch to tell the time. His Diolev watch was on the bureau. His Treo Modo watch was on the wall. His Chronoskeptical watch was on the bedside table.\nTreo Modo.\nThat wasn\u2019t $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s coined phrase for Martian numeral six. That wasn\u2019t his definition of the numeral six. That wasn\u2019t his name for the position of Mars in the Martian order of battle. That wasn\u2019t the secret word in the complicated, despairing and wonderful religion he had invented while in prison.\nTreo Modo was the name of the Grenadier Restaurant, where $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 had gone out to eat on their last night of slavery.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chronometer said :\n8\u20140\n20\u2014 4\n40\u2014 9\n2\u20145\nThe big hand showed that there were forty-five seconds to go. The little hand showed that there were five seconds to go.\nThus did a widely used, vaguely ecclesiastical clock declare that there were five seconds to the beginning of a new epoch. An epoch in which everything was about to change,...", "2_14_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-three in the afternoon</span>.\nIn Rome, it was eleven twenty in the morning.\nIn Australia, it was ten o\u2019clock at night.\n* *13*\nDuring the fourth night after the receipt of the manuscript, a breakdown occurred in the Chief Privy Counselor\u2019s Air Force One landing gear.\nCoincidentally, the Chief Privy Counselor himself experienced a leg cramp.\nHe was being carried over the jungles of Brazil in a palanquin by sixteen fit young slaves.\nA palanquin is a frame of woven rushes, supported by a grid of poles and rectangular beams.\nA palanquin is open on all sides, so that the occupant is exposed to all elements.\nA palanquin is very like a sedan chair.\nA Chief Privy Counselor of San Lorenzo could expect no more delectable form of travel than in a palanquin.\nEach hamlet that the paleolithic procession passed was expected to provide a new set of carriers.\nThe canopies under which the Chief Privy Counselor and his slave master traveled were woven of supple straw, and were gray and finely woven and were often said to be beautiful.", "2_16_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. \u201cThe hours pass by so slowly,\u201d I said, \u201cwith the days getting longer and longer. It's a spiritual thing that both worries and pleases me. We're all like rope-walkers straddling the same rope over a gulf of space and time. There's absolutely nothing to say, no answers, nothing but sorrow and dumb sorrowing inscrutable fear. Ah me -- I wish we had some whisky.\u201d\n\u201cYass!\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3, who had already uncorked a pint that $FIRSTNAMEF4 had wrapped in a paper bag in the honolable kitchen of the home where the delicious apples had ripened. He drank half. I sniffed. \u201cAh me -- the sorrow and dumb sorrowing inscrutable fear.\u201d\n\u201cNow wait a minute, cats, and I'll explain exactly what we'll do --\u201d Ominous time raced ahead and no voice could slow it.\n\u201cQuick, tell me what's the thing to do and then let's jump right on it and go crazy.\u201d", "1_21_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty in the evening</span>, and as I pursued my daughter around the park I grew more and more hungry. I, too, wanted a hot dog. When I saw my daughter, then\u2014gasping, weary, nearing the home stretch\u2014I said, \u201cWhy not rest, huh? We've come a thousand miles. Let's dig those fabled hot rods of your wilderness primeval.\u201d My daughter lurched sideways and keeled over in the grass. I knew she was faking, so I muscled her to her feet. \u201cLet's rock and roll,\u201d I said. \u201cThere's a hot dog stand over by that merrygoround. I can smell them from here.\u201d My daughter yawned and smiled, exposing all of her small white teeth. \u201cGee, Daddy, I've been running around so much I'm beginning to lose my shape and color. I really am. I've been flashing on and off like a neon sign.\u201d I nodded. \u201cCome on, sport. Let's go over and place our order.\u201d My daughter smiled happily and let me lead her over to the curb by the hot-dog stand. She handed me a fistful of nickels. \u201c...", "2_15_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-four in the afternoon</span>. I had two hundred and eleven dollars left. I was extremely frightened. \u201cLet me get the money,\u201d I whispered. \u201cI'll be out in a minute.\"\n\u201cThe hell you will,\u201d she snapped. \u201cAnd no more goddamn food!\u201d I was halfway out the door when I turned back and saw her just rummaging around in the carrier bag amid rolls and tatters of discarded baker's paper. Why, I wondered, why? \u201cGood bye,\u201d I said.\n\u201cAh, fuck you!\u201d she snapped.\nI closed the door behind me and locked it. There was a wooden railing along the walkway to the motor home. I was halfway along it when I remembered my keys. I turned back and went to the door. I shouted inside: \u201cI forgot my keys!\u201d But she made no reply. I waited a while, but she didn't come to the door. So I went back to the railing and started forward again. I was still carrying the beer.\n\u201cI think this is yours,\u201d said a very low voice behind me.", "1_5_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>, on April first, 1952. I had passed through theLooking Glass into another dimension. Lest I burst into hysterical laughter, I bent down to throw my car fenders and spare tire into the trunk. Suddenly the light pulsed above me and a voice boomed out of the sky: \"Stop that vehicleIOmediately!\" I was so crazed by then that I looked up, thinking a helicopter had clamped down on me. Policemen alighted on all four sides, like giant mantises. They drew their guns on me as I stood there laughing helplessly, so wildly that I had to hold onto the fender of the car. A young cop, not more than twentyfive, stepped up, put his face right down to mine and said, \u201cPut your hands on top of your head and interlock the fingers,\u201d Then he recited the Miranda. \u201cYou have the right to remain silent\u2014anything you say may be used against you in court\u2014you have the right to talk with a lawyer before answering questions\u2014if you cannot afford a lawyer, one will be appointed for you before any questioning\u2014you understand these rights as they have been read to you?\u201d I nodded and almost collapsed. Another cop was leaning into...", "0_1_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME1 entered the room where $FIRSTNAMEF0 was sitting. She looked as if nothing had occurred.\nTen minutes later, she shivered, felt lonely and mournful. She had reached out for someone, she didn\u2019t know who, she didn\u2019t know what.\nHere Rose, having signed and stamped a receipt, added the following underlined dedication to the painting which was now being packed to go by parcel post to Milwaukee, Wisconsin:\nFor my good friend Konrad A. Kinzer, hotel proprietor, here is a little painting that he especially admired in the art exhibit at the small Boca Raton museum\u2014just before all the paintings were burned by an arsonist. The signature on the painting is genuine\u2014the signature of the man who painted it. He graduated from Harvard only four years after Walt Whitman. He died in 1935, not many miles from where he is buried diacovi this painting. Hope you like it.\nRose richly underlined the last two sentences. Here she added two kisses. She tore the bottom of the letter off and placed it in a box with three dozen other such letters, to be mailed to widowers all over the United States who had written to her about Howie\u2019s paintings.", "0_17_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Old Niccol\u00f2 was sitting in his usual place under the arcade of the courtyard. Before him was a large eartheware bowl of white and blue flowers. The courtyard was full of bright sunlight.\n\"Good-morning, Old Niccol\u00f2,\" said the journalists as they passed.\nOld Niccol\u00f2 shook his head and smiled sadly. He had not forgotten. The bowl of flowers was there every day, but he had had a dream which he would never forget as long as he lived.\nHe had fallen asleep under the arcade dreaming about Luigi, his son, and had had a dream. In the dream he saw Luigi stop one of the journalists who came in every day to look at the tablets under the arcade. Luigi had asked the journalist for news of his brother. What had the House of Persico done to his brother Carmine? What had happened to Enrico Apice? and to Fortunato Lugrumi? What had happened to all the friends of his son Luigi?\nWhat had happened to his other son, Piero?\nThe journalist was very sorry to tell Luigi that there had not been good fortune in the House of Persico, and that the greatest friend of his father and grandfather, Enric...", "2_7_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s gilgri come to inhabit Salo\u2019s body, Salo got out of bed, put $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s heavy, space suit in good order, clamped all the chinks that were letting air out, donned the suit, put on some water to boil, climbed back into bed and meditated on the spaces of Earth, in particular on the space of Northeast Salt Lake in Salt Lake City, Utah, U.S.A.\n\"Thank you, Your Honor and my friends,\" said Salo, just after eight o\u2019clock.\nThe chronometer on the chest of drawers indicated that the elapsed time in the trial of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was now one hundred and eighty-seven seconds.\n\"It is truly fine of you to have come to see me off,\" said Salo.\nThe trial was being conducted in chambers, with no spectators. The three members of the Supreme Court handling the trial with the Associate Justice known as Artemis Buff were machines.", "1_9_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-five in the morning</span>. There were no customers. I decided to look up Tim Gray.\nI walked through the narrow, crowded streets to his room. It was a nice address, the Hotel Miramar, on Via Poniente, overlooked the bay. The door of his room was open. Tim Gray and all his belongings were in the sitting-room. The bed was stripped and bare.\nI said to the housekeeper, \u201cThis was Tim Gray\u2019s room?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cHe has moved out?\u201d\n\u201cYes, about three days ago, I think.\u201d\n\u201cHas he left an address?\u201d\n\u201cNot that I know of.\u201d I left a tip for the housekeeper and gave her my card. She said Gray had owed four weeks\u2019 rent. Asking for his forwarding address in London would only be, she said, \u201ca waste of time.\u201d\nI walked back to the club through the morning streets. I wanted something to eat.\nThis was the world I had wanted so hard to get away from. Without money in your pocket it was the same world, only harder. I wished to God I was back in Port...", "0_9_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>, according to Allamagoosa's clocks, which were good for Greenwich mean time as long as you didn't ask them about solstices and equinoxes.\nHazeltine was hardly aware that the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibulated world had vanished. He was thinking, in a kind of tremor, about a series of religious paintings in scattered museums around the world\u2014in a church at Palestrina and the Uffizi Gallery, in the Dessau Gallery of Religious Images, and Simon Langmore\u2019s Apparition Museum in Bayswater, London, and the St. Louis Art Museum in Missouri.\nThe series of paintings had no title. They had never been referred to with a common title. But Henry Morrisroe St. Clive, Seventh Earl of Whosis-pisha, had described them so well in his book Zeitmeng\u2014Gedenkspr\u00fcche, that almost everyone who liked that kind of painting knew and admired them as \u201cSt. Clive\u2019s Infundibulated March.\u201d\nAfter St. Clive\u2019s death, and thanks to the researches of learned Oxonians and Jesuit scholars, the paintings were given a collective name that would be...", "2_16_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We spent twenty minutes more with Paula. She was a tall, gloomy girl with gorgeous sweaters. I sat down on the couch. Suddenly there was a strange whirring in my ears. Everything rushed back of my eyes, as if my two hemispheres of brain had swung around to face each other, making an instant complete circuit. I didn't know what was happening; I couldn't see; I could hear voices. \u201cWhere am I?\u201d I thought. \u201cWhat is it? Am I dying?\u201d I struggled to my feet.\n\u201cWhat's the trouble?\u201d\n\u201cI don't know. I suddenly realized I'm dying.\u201d I couldn't see well. I stumbled over a chair and across the living room as I made my way up to the front door.\n$FIRSTNAMEF4's brother was coming out of the house. \u201cWait,\u201d I said.\n\u201cWhat's the matter?\u201d he said. I knew he was $FIRSTNAMEF4's brother because he looked like Carlo Tresca.\n\u201cWhere's $FIRSTNAMEF4?\u201d\n\u201cIn bed. Hasn't been feeling well.\u201d", "0_10_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> as they clattered across the empty square and turned down the Rue M. le Prince P\u00e9trus found it most entertaining to ride in the rumble seat of a small truck and pretend he was one of the Jets going off to some extreme form of ketchup in southern France. The women were all out on the Boulevard Montparnasse when the truck swayed by, looking in shop windows and laughing at men, some of whom held their arms out and pretended to be automobile drivers who had wrecked and were asking people to phone for the ambulances and to send for the stadiums to come down and cart the guys away on stretchers.\n\u201cFi, fi, fi, fi, fi, fi, fi! \u201d screamed the $FIRSTNAMEB0 Beeves as the truck bounced and rattled down the Rue M. And then \"Bop-bop-bop-bop! Bohump!\u201d he cried.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 relaxed and smiled, with one hand reached for his hip pocket, then closed his eyes, stuck his finger between his teeth, and slept.\nThe center of the dark curtain was ripped open at last. The golden light poured through, the creamy-white pillars reared.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 thought it was the most beautiful...", "2_3_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until four o'clock in the morning</span> in Melbourne, Australia.\nThe place was a Victorian three-story wood townhouse. It was shut. The shutters were drawn over the windows.\nThe doorbell did its job. Its job was to make sounds in the upper flat of the townhouse.\nA window on the first floor opened.\nA body, part of which was a very attractive leg and thigh, was revealed by the window. An attractive body also was revealed.\n\u201cOh, no,\u201d said the body.\nThe upper flat had windows to be drawn, too. A certain amount of light went right through them, though. Enough light went in to reveal Morse Shiloan, darling of the Worcester Glee Club, and Peter Scannell, hanger-on at the Cleveland Gymnastics Club; also, to reveal things associated with being asleep.\nScannell clutched a blanket over his groin. Morse Shiloan clutched a blanket over his chest.", "1_5_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-two in the morning</span>. Our watch had ended. We were free for the day. I wanted desperately to sleep, but $FIRSTNAMEB3 would have none of it, and I had to be dragged up the hill. Stan, who was still sitting on his stoop, drowsing, almost fell out of his chair when he saw us coming. We crawled in the back door and fell on Stan's bed, and I slept the clock around. When I awoke it was almost six, and old Henry was knocking at the door to say the car was ready for me. For a second I thought I was in my maduncle's home town of View Image of Page 235 Glendale, way back in the Midwest, where I had lived in a much simpler age. 1 hurriedly put on my clothes and looked out the door. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was squatting by the car in his stocking feet, and old Henry was wrangling with him. \u201cListen,\u201d Henry said, \u201cin the future please don't start the car unless the owner has given you permission.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 was crazed and leaning on the horn, pretending to be Benny Goodman. See you around six-thirty,\u201d I said. \u201cAnd join us for a ball tonight,\u201d said Stan.", "2_19_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-eight in the evening</span>. The place was a rented room in New York City. The room was several stories above West 8th Street, and offered a fine view of smog. The room was white with a wide window going almost down to the floor, overlooking West 8th Street, the Hudson River, and titanic masses of smog that gathered from time to time and threatened to seal in the scene.", "1_7_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span> when the space ship Great Los Angeles screeched and ground to a halt on the sagebrush of Sweet-air, Nevada. At once a gangplank was let down and a deputation of fifty citizens came to welcome the Martians and shake hands.\nThere were three space ships in all. Each held five hundred Martian pilgrims, nerved to the voyage by drugs and sent with pens to sign up right-of-way privileges on Earth. Each pilgrim carried four billion dollars in Martian bank notes, crediting the funds to Earth banks that would have to be identified by the right-of-way purchasers themselves.\nThe Martian Ambassador to Earth stepped out of his ship, dressed in the formal attire of his station, six feet high, a billowing black overcoat, a white satin breeches, and thin leather slippers on his feet. His name\u2014for this costume had once been worn by the great grandfather of the current Ambassador\u2014was Al Wahskey.\n\"Greetings, suckers!\" he cried in good American when he had shaken the hands of the welcoming committee. \"Where\u2019s somebody who can show me around this crappy town?\"\n\"How did he talk that way\u2014the Ambassador?\" $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 asked his father,...", "1_7_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> of Monday, October 16, 1964, nine days, five hours, and forty-six minutes before Martians arrived in Newark.\nMaking plans was, of course, useless.\nIt was axiomatic that nothing useful could be planned in advance of an invasion of Earth by monsters from another planet. Mankind could not hope to outthink monsters who could design and build an intercontinental rocket ship in two months.\nKnowing this, housewives were still making plans. They were haggard. They were white-lipped. They were trembling. Terrible twitches shook their lips. They were banana-yellow in the face. They had bitten their lips until they bled to keep from crying. And why?\nBecause they were making plans.\nAll along High Street, housewives were stepping out of front doors and looking up at the skies, and finding the skies empty, and stepping back into their front doors to make blue plans.\nAmong the plans that housewives made was a proposal to discourage use of the facilities of the male sex organ if and when the monsters landed in America. This was to be done by women refusing to make love to the men who had let the obscene things happen.\nAmong housewives of Newport, Rhode Island, there was...", "0_11_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-one in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was about nine Earthling miles up and east of Newport, Rhode Island, U.S.A. Newport and Providence, in the state of Rhode Island, lay eight Earthling miles up and northeast of Boston, in the state of Massachusetts.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, as was Space Wanderer, was aboard a flying saucer that was traveling in airspace over what had been the capital of Old America, the state of New York, the city of New York. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and Space Wanderer were aboard the saucer of $SURNAME0, who was not aboard the saucer.\nThe saucer was drifting southward, was disguised as a station wagon with Michigan license plates. It was supposed to be on a collision course with the House Without Desire on Newport\u2019s 93d Street.\nNearer the ground, and nearer $SURNAME0 and the house, were two other station wagons, sturdy and glistening with antennae. One of them was disguised as a police car with New York license plates. The governor of the state of New York was supposed to be in this car. The other was a TV station\u2019s mobile unit, supposed to be transmitting the last events in the history of Earth up to the moment when the station...", "0_14_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"I beg your pardon,\" said Mr. $SURNAME2.\n\"Footprints of an unusually wide spacing\u2014probably a stunted, elderly Martian,\" said $SURNAME1. \"The footprints are yielding easily to the whistling pressure of the wind, but the boot imprints are dark and deep, the result of a dyeing process customary with veterans of an Earthling war. The footprints are bordered by a thin but distinctive greenery, the greenery follows the prints to a shade, then stops and looks back. The crewmember\u2019s name was Peter Kanis. He died of old age in the year 1977.\"\n\"Oh,\" said $SURNAME2.\n\"An old woman,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"She lived to be ninety-six.\"\n\"Ordinary track, made by an ordinary, young, crew member on a semi-permanent assignment, name of BRumfoord,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Size twelve, double EEE. Possible hiatus in the middle of the stride suggesting the wearing of a female outfit, the one occasion in $SURNAME0\u2019s known history when he did don female apparel. The outfit, with the femme essence incorporated, created a change in $SURNAME0\u2019s stride length, shortened...", "1_3_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until four o'clock in the morning</span>. A signal sounded, and huskies and tourists prepared to embark upon a ten-point trail that would take them to the abandoned diamond mines in the wilderness area of the island.\nThere was no response from our cabin. Unfastening the window-shutters of my room, I saw my three colleagues slumped in their thin fetal positions in the hall. Someone had fed them to sleep with vodka.\nThey could not be roused. I roped their bodies together with bed-sheets and dragged them to the launching pad as the time for departure approached. I felt their weight a little. On Titan, with its fourteen earth-gravity, you could have dragged them to the moon on an eight-foot rope.\nYou may remember that in a moment of unprofessional weakness I had accepted the responsibility of escorting $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $SURNAME0 to the island. The two were having romantic dutch hours on the spiral staircase.\nSundry members of the press were clamoring below. I was expected to perform.\nI hoisted $FIRSTNAMEF1 under one arm, carried $SURNAME0 under the other, dragged them up the stairs to the roof pad. Photographers jammed their flashes against my eyes. Those were the halcyon days before sunglasses were common. I...", "2_19_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty in the evening</span>, Earth time.\nThe place was the shores of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Sea. The ship lay on its side on the wet, muddy grass. The medium was rain, mist, the stinging smell of the sea, and the wind from the south.\nThe only active one was $FIRSTNAMEB2, the watch-corgi.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was watching $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was hoping, desperately, that $FIRSTNAMEF0 would either feed him or let him into the shelter of her canvas enclosure. His small, pink tongue was flicking in and out.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was no longer happy.\nAll the good things that had once been associated with $FIRSTNAMEF0 were all mixed up and canceled out and replaced, in his mind, with the image of something sickening and horrible. $SURNAME0 had once told $FIRSTNAMEB2 that $FIRSTNAMEF0 was a space ship. He had told $FIRSTNAMEB2 that space ships were magical, benevolent creatures.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had never seen a magical, benevolent creature before.", "1_12_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past noon</span>. There were thirteen minutes to go before the Martians would reach the top of the wall.\nThe Martian reconnaissance troops, scouts, and advance guards were the first to sight Earthmen on the walls around the home grounds of the National Rifle Association. The scouts were mounted on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s species of horse-like creatures. The scouts and advance guards were armed with gas grenades and various other nifty, noisy toys. They stopped abreast a low wall, dismounted with great flourishes, and let the horse-like creatures loose behind them.\nThe advance guards climbed the wall and let down sacks filled with earth, to block out fighting retreats. Then the main body of each reconnaissance troop scrambled up the low wall and joined the advance guards behind the barricade.\nThe advance guards leveled their eerie, humming fin-staffs at the Earthmen on the wall above, and the main bodies selected Earthmen at random, shouldered their fin-staffs, aimed, and fired.\nOne Earthman dropped as if struck by lightning, and the other Earthmen came swarming over the barricade. And then the fighting men from Mars and Earth were mixed together, separated from one another only by accident.\nThere wasn\u2019t much honor or...", "1_2_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after two o'clock in the morning</span> when we found the headquarters shack. It was a mere shivering flame in the dark wilderness. The shack was built of logs, with what appeared to be a stovepipe coughing out white smoke from one end. My guide tapped on the door: that was the light I'd seen. The door opened, and I was amazed to find two laughing teenage girls standing there. They said they were hitchhiking to Alaska on Saturday. What's this? New York? That's fine, honey. Come in and have a cup of tea. No. We must find our last subject of exploitation'in Braggart. We tippy-toed our way through the mud of the yard and got back in the car and found our salami and fell asleep. Our last subject of exploitation in Braggart was to get up at four A.M. and drive back to the hospital in Redding. Tim Gray's major concern was that the sun not catch us in midtrip. We wobbled home around six A.M., having in the night expended two more bottles of tequila and five more butts of patty grass. And we knocked on the doors of our five subjects of exploitation, one by one, starting with Tim Gray's father. We roused...", "2_23_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe place was the Newport District, which is the very bestest part of Laycoose, which is the richest and most important city in the world.\nThe thing was music.\nMusic, filling the night with magic, could be heard coming from a long way off. It wove itself out of the air, and settled into waves on walls and roofs and asphalt. It boiled heavenward and went dancing off into space.\n\"Music,\" said a proud and contented husband, stepping out of his castle on the hill to listen.\n\"Music,\" said his wife, coming from the ballroom with a glass of whipped cream, diamonds on her fingers.\n\"Music,\" said a proud and contented daughter, stepping out on the terrace with a young man in whom she had just become interested.\n\"Music?\" said the young man. \"Do you like music, my dear?\"\n\"Delighted, delighted,\" said the daughter. \"But, sometimes, you know, I just can\u2019t stand it.\"\nFuzzy Peak was sitting in his luxurious living room, staring at the contents of the elaborate cocktail shaker.", "1_9_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>, EST, November 2. The place was the office of Mint Julep Pec saw, in Pec saw\u2019s tastefully landscaped Beverly Hills estate. Out the window could be seen a series of sweeping lawns and carefully pruned beds, leading the eye to a large swimming pool, flanked by conversation pits.\nThe name on the stone gateposts at the entrance had been discreetly changed to Pec\u2019s Saw Estates of Beverly Hills. It was said to be worth forty million bucks, but there was no tax on it. A clever arrangement between Pec saw and the state of California had sent the tax bill cascading from Los Angeles to New York to Cleveland to Stockholm, where a gentleman of no character whatever cashed it and promptly lost it down an open grate somewhere in the Swedish sewers.\nNo one knew better than Stanley R. Micawber how to amortize the asset. He had not got where he was without this ability. This was a man who, as a youth of seventeen, had bought twenty Sahara Desert sand dunes at a dollar an acre, paying the seller one dollar down, and carrying the balance of nineteen dollars per acre on a long mortgage at twelve percent. This was the sort of deal...", "0_22_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-four in the evening</span>.\nI walked slowly across the Square and as I reached the doors of the Berkeley Hotel I heard a gentle patter of piano notes behind me.\nI turned. Coming across the terrace of the hotel, walking on the black and white tiles, crossing the big square of light thrown by the courtyard lamps, came my Lady in the, Pink Dress.\nAt ten forty-five, I was sitting at a table in the bar of the Hotel Berkeley and my Lady in the Pink Dress was sitting at my side. She had her gloved hand on my arm.\nEverything was going fine. It was all swell. The only thing wrong was that my expense money was running low.\nBut this didn\u2019t bother me at all.\nI was really turned on by the atmosphere of the Berkeley Bar. I mean, this was a beautiful place. It just couldn\u2019t be beat. It was full of couples drinking Martinis and Chablis. The women were beautiful and so were the men. You could smell money everywhere.\nThere were no tourists in the bar and no one seemed to be worried about gangsters. The bartenders knew all the customers by name and smiled at them as they served the drinks.\nI was sitting...", "2_16_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four o'clock in the afternoon</span>; it was February, the last hour of our African sojourn.\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cYou know what.\u201d\n\u201cWell,\u201d I said, \u201cwe've been 'round, back and forth, as you know, to the other whores that you didn't want to see ... and I guess we now know for sure what it is you want to see in the end.\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cPrettyJane.\u201d\n\u201cYes!\u201d he yelled like a madman. \u201cYes, yes, yes! Old sweet little Prejean!\u201d\n\u201cWhen you actually get to see her you'll truly know what a crazy goof you've been all this time.\u201d", "2_8_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nA voice on a radio station in Cleveland, Ohio, the voice of a young girl, said, \"Music. A walk in the moonlight.\"\nThe voice of a man on a radio station in Dayton said, \"Eleanor ---- is brushing her teeth.\"\nThe voice of another man on a radio station in Akron said, \"The Lone Ranger is having his picture taken.\"\nThe mind of a young girl in Columbus, Ohio, was flooded with memories of humiliating experiences. She remembered being shamed again and again by friends of her parents, friends who derided and scorned her as a provincial nobody, a small-town hick, a poor relation. She remembered the shame she had felt when her prom date had driven her home from her parochial school after the prom, and had kissed her mitten instead of her lips. Her name was Aggie.\nAggie was standing beside a news-stand on the sidewalk outside the Neil House in Columbus. Without moving her feet, she reached out and clutched her hand around the wrist of a man who was passing. She moved the man to where he could share the shade of the news-stand with her. She didn\u2019t let go of him.", "2_10_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 watched the people arrive in the clearing beside the space ship. There were a hundred and thirty-three of them. Each was carrying something. They were going to carry their offerings into the Space Museum.\nThe column of people stopped in the clearing, and their spokesman stepped out in front. He was an old man, broken, shriveled, and very, very white.\nA loudspeaker beside the museum door informed $SURNAME1, $SURNAME0, and the Canopus women that the spokesman for the people was a man named Abraham Lincoln.\n$SURNAME1 knew who Abraham Lincoln was.\nSome years back, $SURNAME1 had read a magazine article about Lincoln. He had learned that Lincoln had freed the slaves in the United States, that Lincoln had been elected to Congress, that Lincoln had been defeated for the nomination by his party for President, that he had been elected President of the United States for two terms, that he had been assassinated.\n$SURNAME1 chuckled darkly to think that the Earthlings had lost their greatest statesman to the Solar System as surely as they had lost everything else.", "0_7_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-one in the morning</span>, Tuesday. The Detroit satellite system, like all Earthling transmissions, would have trouble with transmission effects\u2014storm effect and sun effect\u2014in eight minutes.\nOld Red was ready. \"Good luck, baby!\" he said, and gave her a big, old, sad smile.\nNine minutes after $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 had been brought to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s Greenwich Village home, nine minutes after he had been warned to turn the television set off, nine minutes after he had been visited by a bat-winged creature from the corporate wilderness, the transmission began.\nThe picture on the television screen immediately congealed into a tight ball, wavered, then disappeared. The television set was now a piece of furniture. It made no sound, no meaningful light.\nBooks, pictures, clothing, bedding, all material objects in the home of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 began to behave in a similar fashion\u2014to freeze, to wave, to disappear.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 stood in his kitchen, staring in wonder at a pot of boiling calves\u2019 feet. The calves\u2019 feet stopped boiling, stood solid and unmoving in the liquid. $SURNAME1 reached out to touch them. They were as cold as iceberg lettuce.\nThe mirror by which $SURNAME1 shaved every morning,...", "1_0_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nLen and Adam predicted that now all the special police communications would be off the air\u2014they would all have teletype orders to Keep Off The Fucking Air.\nSo hideously many things can happen in the time it takes one elevenyearold girl to dial a pay phone, drop coins in, hear a phone ring three times, and then hear it answered.\nThe phone on the bedside table in Room 204 of the House of Thutmos was ringing when $SURNAME1 Nopac emerged from the bathroom, looking as fresh as one of his unfed California poppies. \"Room service, Mr. Nopac,\" greeted the operator of the phone.\n\"Hello hello hello,\" beamed Nopac. \"Ah, yes\u2014Randy, I see. Thank you for the wake-up call.\"\nA short giggle from the girl in New York.\n\"No, you\u2019re entirely wrong,\" said Nopac. \"I\u2019ve never had a cigarette machine.\"\nA long silence, during which the operator\u2019s ears burned.\nAnother giggle from the girl.\n\"Yes, this is Nicodemus,\" said Nopac at last, into the phone. Then he coug...", "1_5_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>, according to the ship's chronometers. The place was not far from the planet Mars, where the battle had been the thickest.\nThese chronometers were on a wall of the spaceship. They were supposed to keep shipboard time. They did not. Shipboard time was kept by the clocks in the control room.\nSalo had just come out of the control room, where he had spent the weeks since the landing at Mercury. The chronometers on the wall were out of synch with the clocks in the control room by fifty-two minutes, twelve seconds, and four thirds.\nThe chronometers were also out of synch with Martian time.\nThey said it was five minutes past five\nmorning on the western slopes of Mount McKay, and it was 6:05 A.M. Martian time on the eastern slope of Mount McKay. It was not quite so dark on the eastern slope as it was on the western slope. The sun had not quite cleared the top of Mount McKay.\nSix seconds later, the sun cleared the top of Mount McKay.\nThere was a flash on the eastern slope that cast no light and put out no heat\u2014and the battle against the Germans was over.\nThe Germans were paratroopers, who had...", "1_17_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. Somewhere not too far away there was an old woman who wanted nothing so much at that moment as to have her Martini.\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas arrived at the truth about The Medusa Syndrome quite by accident, or providential interference: one of those. One untypical sunny afternoon around four, she decided to drive into San Narciso instead of waiting for evening, and parked her Ford on the downtown side of the Yoyodyne building so she could observe the comings and goings.\nAround five, the trickle of vehicles turning in, plus more than the ordinary number of pedestrians, brought her out of her reverie on What\u2019s Luckier, Get a Break or Have Christ in Your Life. She was noting that Baby Igor, though still impervious to her charms, had had, in a brief sprint around the block, broken into a veritable sweat, so bitter was San Narciso this time of day. She rolled down her window to get some air. San Narciso, she decided, was fast turning into a subdivision, with all the higgledy-piggledy growth and jerry-building that that implied. Soon it would be as bad as San Jose. God knew where this development would stop...", "2_21_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe television program The Scales of Justice was being broadcast. Its theme was how the law enforces its will on three things: the scales of justice, a nine iron, and a woman\u2019s virtue. The program starred Attorneys E. James Korris and Thigpen, who, as far as any but their mother knew, were the only lawyers in Newport City, Rhode Island, U.S.A. The program had three sets. All were meant to suggest desks. One was supposed to be the attorneys\u2019 law office; one was supposed to be the attorneys\u2019 office in the Newport County Courthouse; one was supposed to be an office in the big new United States Supreme Court Building in Washington D.C., to which Attorneys Korris and Thigpen had recently been appointed by the President himself.", "2_4_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. Lime, Codeine, and Mercury joined forces with Satan on a blackjack table outside the Flamingo Hotel. They bet $12,000\u2014and lost. In fact, they lost twelve straight hands in a row\u2014a record that will stand until the end of time.\nThe group of four stood up and stalked off dejectedly. Each man tossed a $1000 chip into the pot that they had just lost so horribly.\n\u201cThis time, Baby\u2014watch me win it all back,\u201d whispered the drug addict to himself.\nAnd, a few moments later, he did win it all back. The game actually closed down for fourteen hours just so the four middle\u2014aged losers could catch up with the profit/loss roster.", "1_11_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to noon</span> Green time. Titanic atmosphere, the whole place was a blaze. Putt-putt-putt-putt-putt-A Ragged, wild, high singing began to run through the crowd and break, into parts and run all ways. Paraphrased and shouted again and again, it was, \u201cA sexualah!\u201d\nEveryone was preparing for the big race. The little girls were running up and down the roads in hair curlers, carrying flowers to strew in front of the racers. A tough hairy contingent from the Arkansas Rebellion was lying on the ground with big square feet draped over the dashboard, checking the oil and fine points. Boomp-a-doomp-a-doomp-a-DAH! Fem-fem-fem-fem! Toot-toot-tootle-too-too-too! The sabres of the clock stuck the tic at noon, the mover shivered, the hands rolled! The starter slapped the flag to the stand and unrolled it; the flag said, \u201cGOOOO!\u201d They were off! Bugg-a-bugg-a-bugg-a-bump! A thousand shouting kids...", "1_22_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> of December 31st, 1982, and above the closed and curtained window of Room 223 in the $SURNAME0 Hotel there hung the relentless Martian moon Phobos.\nIf there had been strong magnifying lenses and a swift silent time machine available, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog would have been seen to wink out at ten-twenty.\nFor good and all, $SURNAME0 and his dog had stepped out of the Universe\u2014had stepped out of the Universe for good and all.\nThe clock in the tower of the Church of the Holy Red Horse truly had stopped.\nThe church itself would burn to the ground in 1929\u2014would be struck by lightning, in fact, and reduced to a smoldering heap in less than a minute.\nShirley MacLaine and her two children had not been materialized on any dusty cot in the Martian Moon\u2019s adolescence.\nThey had not been materialized anywhere.\nShirley MacLaine and her two children had had their molecules scrambled once and for all, had in fact been completely disintegrated by the awful termal violence of the Watcher\u2019s emergence from the tempoflux structure. They were among the more than three-quarters of a million human...", "1_23_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> on April 21, 12,037, according to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s good friend chronometer. At that very moment, the flight-trained survivors on Mars were in the process of using what remained of Sergeant $SURNAME2\u2019s aqueduct to transfer fuel from the crashed space ship to the hatchlings.\nThey had selected the flight-trained survivors to make the flight to Earth for several reasons. In the first place, the flight-trained people had the best chance of getting through the atmosphere without incinerating. In the second place, it was felt that Earth was now so beleaguered, so surrounded by angry ma\u2022, that the fewer Earthlings involved in the counterattack the better.\nAnd in the third place, the fight with the space ship had so depleted the morale of the army, had so destroyed its will to fight on, that it was thought wise to eliminate entirely the people who could remember the glorious march from the caves on Mars to the Presidential Palace on Earth.\nThere was also the possibility that the flight-trained people were contaminated with malaise, as the people left behind were now supposedly contaminated with guerrilla tactics.\nThe flight-machinery of the space ship was still in splendid condition. Enough fuel, was...", "1_9_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span> of the thirty-first of December, 1962. The place was the living room of a small wooden prefab on Route 114 in Uravan, Colorado. The antenna had been lighted at sundown the day before-a Yagi type fifty-five feet high, made of aluminum tubing and thin wire. The wave length was forty meters; the potential wavelength, forty billion meters-down which, thanks to the marvelous phenomenon of resonance, the infinitely small pushes the infinitely great with all the fury of hell.\nThe man fiddling with the radio was stocky and moveshouldered, with a full head of curly dark hair and a dark, round, baby face on which good humor and intelligence fought a seesaw battle with nervous tension. He was thirty-three years old, wore rimless spectacles that made him look vaguely like a high school Latin teacher. His name was James Randalf McClure, and he owned the prefab, which was parked on his two acres off 114. He was a small-time Bureau of Reclamation subcontractor, usually employed as a dozer operator and grader-operator. His business was going broke, and he was about to leave Uravan and head for the big valley to the south, where the action...", "1_10_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-nine in the morning</span> and once again I was officially On The Pigs\u2019 List. Gonzo was still out of the office when we arrived but the police were very patient with us. They kept smiling and chatting pleasantly while they were sawing through the locks in the office door. I told them I had a key, but they seemed to prefer this other method. They also ignored my urgent pleas to call my attorney.\nWe found the safe open and emptied. My heart sank. The registration cards were gone. I rummaged around in the debris\u2014trashed letterhead paper, bits of tele kind, knockedflat coke bottles and beer cans, gum wrappers and condom packages. My legal papers were gone. My will, my medical directive, my trust indenture...Gonzo had bailed out on me. He was gone\u2014left, skipped, absconded, ran away.\nA year\u2019s worth of dirty/clean clothes were scattered all over the floor. Piles of books: Revolt in the Reform Factory, (no surprise); also We Can Hide, by Kent and Pauline. Why they were together I did not know, unless they wrote it as a team. The only other book was The Assistant Pig Butcher of Smolensk and...", "2_7_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Hear what they had to say?\" said $SURNAME1.\nThe old man resumed his slapping at flies. \"Didn\u2019t take nothin\u2019 down.\"\n\"Just gave my opinion to the roomful of men, that\u2019s all,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"What\u2019d your opinion be?\" said the old man.\n\"Where did that herd of astronauts go?\" said $SURNAME1. \"That\u2019s what I asked \u2019em.\"\n\"I wouldn\u2019t know nothin\u2019 about that,\" said the old man. He resumed shouting, \"Take off and go home! Take off and go home!\"\n\"If cattle can make it through fogs that are solid,\" said $SURNAME1, \"maybe astronauts can, too.\"\n\"You know how many bull pounds we lost last year?\" said the old man to $SURNAME1. \"Thirty! We didn\u2019t lose thirty bull pounds in two hundred and fifty-two before satellite weather!\"", "0_6_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-five in the morning</span>.\nAt a college where men never played football and were judged in the national polls only because they seemed well fed and happy.\nWhere women never played collegiate football and were seldom seen.\nWhere things like collegiate football had never been invented.\nWINNER TO A WINNER, FOR INSTANCE\nPERHAPS ONE of the things wrong with the college was its name.\nAlfred University is its name.\nIt is at Alfred, New York.\nAlfred, New York, is a punt on the Meander River. There is no way for a town in a punt on the Meander River to be dazzling, rich, wicked, exciting, picturesque, beautiful, wonderful, charming, historical, important, colorful, giddy, risqu\u00e9, full of zeal, or romantic. It can\u2019t even be picturesquely rotten. It can be, at best, pretty and quiet.\nPerhaps a pun on the name of the town is not far off the mark. It doesn\u2019t even have a factory. It doesn\u2019t even have a boat. It doesn\u2019t even have a piece of raw land with a few holes in it.\nUnless you count a school of industrial arts,...", "0_7_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span> when the taxi stopped with a jerk in front of the Palace Hotel in Ashley Street. The fare was fourteen shillings. I gave the chauffeur eighteen, got out and looked up the street. It was an ordinary London street, and there was not another taxi in sight. The fog was lifting, and shreds of it were going away in the wind.\nIn the lobby of the hotel there were two sleepy porters sleeping on leather chairs. I pressed the button next to the sleeping figure of a red-headed lift boy, and he came slowly awake.\n\u201cTake this bag up to Mr. Campbell\u2019s suite,\u201d I said.\nHe yawned and reached for the bag. \u201cYou the American writer?\u201d he asked. \u201cEverybody\u2019s asking for you. Why didn\u2019t you wake me?\u201d\n\u201cSorry,\u201d I said. \u201cIs everybody up there?\u201d\nHe chuckled. \u201cHo! They\u2019ve been up for two hours. Ever since these two gentlemen came down.\u201d He shoved out an ante-room door beyond which voices came. They were laughing.\nInside the ante-room four men and a woman were sitting...", "2_12_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past noon</span>, Greenwich Mean Time, when $FIRSTNAMEF1 took the paper from the gold tube and read it. The paper was wrinkled and stained.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been carrying it on his body since the morning he had woken up on the banks of a desolate canal, a slave of the Martians.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had stayed anonymous until he married $FIRSTNAMEF1. A man who loves a woman, as $FIRSTNAMEB0 did, has no need to figure out who he is.\nThis man, this soldier walking through this hole in space, was going to a very special place. It was a place that only he could locate.\nHe was carrying a message that would prove him to be, in many senses, the most important man who ever lived.\nNo one on Earth or in the Solar System could prove this man\u2019s importance.\nAnd neither $FIRSTNAMEB0 nor $FIRSTNAMEF0 cared in the slightest what anybody thought about them.\n\"S\u2014stop it!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 frantically, had planned to say when $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 ripped the back off the music box.", "0_8_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty in the morning</span>. My attorney was in the room with us, listening to everything but understanding nothing. He seemed peculiarly calm. When he spoke, it was with extreme slowness: \u201cThis is a very expensive hotel. The girl's a hustler. They don't come cheap.\u201d He was saying this to my attorney, who seemed to have no doubts about this, but my attorney was calling Room Service, asking for a half pound of uncut cocaine and a quart of tequila. A minute later, the stuff had arrived and we all began snorting up huge lines and washing them down with quarts of tequila mixed with CocaCola. Meanwhile, there was considerable negotiating going on between my attorney and the girl to work out some kind of a deal, but I heard only bits and pieces: \u201c. . . $100 for straight vaginal, $125 for straight anal...extra for French kisses, but who isn't generous in this neeed DURANGO SWEET unyts.\u201d My attorney was adding up figures on a piece of paper. \u201cWe can go as high as ten, maybe eleven hundred, depending on how hefty this little honey really is...expensive thighs...rigid nipples...\u201d I was slumped down...", "2_15_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. There were practically no cars on the street. \u201cHave a drink and talk,\u201d I said to Henri, but he wouldn't. Another Frenchman came in and took off his sweater to roll up his sleeves and have a go at the job. Henri nodded to him. \u201cI retire,\u201d he said. \u201cMe, I live my life de treize de Marseilles --dans le fourteenth arrondissement, au Bat d'Af.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d\n\u201cWhat does everybody mean? How should I know?\u201d\n\u201cYou're being a fool --\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cLissen, you're sick. I know you're sick. I know your life. I know what you've done since 1929 --written hundred letters a day to Sabina, spent your money on monkey jackets, joy-rides in the countryside with little boys, tried everything to live --\u201d", "2_7_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 enclosed the story and caption in a speck of force and darted away from home. He flew to the place where $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were stationed. He gave the news item to $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 read it, and then slipped away quietly.\nThe news item reached $FIRSTNAMEB1 on the air in the form of a pungent blast of propaganda. If the people of Earth could ever have been made to understand it, a truly profound pity would have been theirs. The pity would have been for the Army, and it would have been deep and mystical and humanistic\u2014and basically screwy.\nThe Army had mushed its own mother!\nThe Army had forced the mother of the perfect soldier, $FIRSTNAMEB1, to go out looking for a ball-peen hammer!\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 went back to the barrack, revised his plans for the day, got himself a calm and deliberate drunk. He loosened his clothing, let his facemask fall down around his neck, stopped at a water fountain and trolled for water.\nThere was a sign on the fountain. It said:\nREMEMBER\u2014YOU\u2019RE A REAL MAN, REAL SOJER!\nDRINK WATER!", "1_9_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-five in the morning</span>. I felt like a sad and ragged Columbus, who has sailed far and is now near the rim of the world, missing friends and loved ones, sorry and sick at his heart, borne ceaselessly into dark blue immensity; but he does not yet know the extent of his failure. He does not see that there is no other Europe and no other peoples to discover. He does not realize he has not found his friends but only the place where they were located one Saturday afternoon a long time ago.\nBut then I remembered my own Columbus on the car radio, in the night, en route to Vegas, singing softly: \u201cWho will come out to dating, to dating... \u201d I turned up the volume. Ah yes. Everything would be alright, America was not a dream. There would always be garbage, the atom bomb, and the radio.\nAnd I remembered $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo and $FIRSTNAMEF4, just as they drove out of sight on Route 40, three months before, and perhaps would never see again -- they had vanished so quickly.\nThe Columbus in the taxi wheeled west over the rocky red earth of the Colorado Plateau and passed through the stone town of Monticello, where local yokels looked up from their...", "2_23_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-one in the evening</span>.\nThe quiver in the walls of the maze was caused by the approach of a parade of some kind.\nUp through the floor and through $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bed came the stench of a band of merry men. Their spirits had been elevated almost to the point of frenzy by narcotics, by the pressures of military discipline, by orgies, by a pervasive bloodlust\u2014and, most of all, by proximity to an enemy who was no longer insulting and frustrating, but who was now within easy killing range.\nThe harmonium stained the approach of the killers a haunting rose.\nThe killers came in silence and filth.\nTheir hobnailed boots were wrapped in rags, so that there would be no complaints from the captive when the boots struck stone.\nTheir hobnailed boots struck stone at eleven thirty-two.\nThey had marched thirty-two hundred miles in three days.\nIt had been a forced march, and they had a bloodlust to sustain them.\nThe hobnails rang true, sparks flew, wounds were guaranteed.", "2_13_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe graveyard was crammed, sardine-style, with drunken members of the Martian Imperial Forces.\nThe headache in the head of almost every Martian in the graveyard was as severe as had been the case with $FIRSTNAMEB1, the member of Sergeant $SURNAME2\u2019s squad, at the very outset of the binge.\nThe only Martian with a relatively painless head was the command-ship pilot, who was not among the drunks. He was cooped up in the airtight cockpit of his command ship a mile above the surface of Mars. He was suffering under a bad case of explosive decompression, but he didn\u2019t know that.\nHe only knew that his time on Mars, time during which he could have fun and frolic, was horribly short.\nWhen he got back to the fleet, he could stop worrying about having a good time.\nThere were 27,363 members of the Martio Imperial Forces in Headphazor soon be to delighted to demonstrate their love, admiration and respect for $FIRSTNAMEB1, who was soused flat on his back in the wheel of an airtruck.", "0_21_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>, and the place was ten feet above the main floor of $SURNAME0\u2019s estate in Newport. The place was a golden chuppah hung from a trellis of rhymer beams in the middle of $SURNAME0\u2019s sunken Japanese garden.\nThe chuppah was cinched with a white silk rope.\nThe chuppah was painted gold.\nThe chuppah had nothing underneath it but the dais on which it rested, which was paved with single pieces of iridescent mother-of-pearl.\nA man in a peaked puritan black hat, black suit, and white shirt stood inside the open flap of the chuppah. He was looking at his nails.\nThe man was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nHe had consumed nothing but a trifle of dust since the day he had materialized out of nowhere in front of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent in Bethel, Connecticut.\nHis dust, like the dust of all else, was made mostly of metals, with a little sand and seashells in it for crunch.\nThus, for all his intensity and intellectuality, $SURNAME0\u2019s face was the color, and looked as if...", "0_16_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-seven in the afternoon</span>. The stop-watch that had begun the search with the flip of a second hand two years and one month ago had run down.\nIt had run down at the moment of a mouse\u2019s tail, at the moment of a space ship\u2019s tail, at the moment of an imaginary tail.\nThe search for the real estate salesman\u2019s head had ended in the field of the golden spikes.\nThis narrow, broken field was all that remained to $FIRSTNAMEF1 of Fancher Flynn\u2019s estate. The old man had liked to sit on a bench here and contemplate this ragged end.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, holding Fern in a sling on her back, sat down on the old man\u2019s bench, to contemplate with her own eyes this ragged end.\nFern broke the silence. He exclaimed, \"Goo-goo!\" He waved a fist in the air.\n\"Goo-goo?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, fondly.\n\"Goo-goo.\"\n\"What happened to Grandpa\u2019s estate?\" asked Robert. He had been born since the head hunting had begun, so he had never known the estate in all its glory.\n\"Uncle Wallace sold it to the city,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.", "2_20_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>. The square outside of the bar was full of people. Saloons and restaurants were doing a good business. Marriages were taking place on the square. Hopp family barrooms and restaurants gave substantial discounts on square-side tables.\n\"I want some action!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"I paid good money to come to Mars, and I want some action.\"\n\"I think we all did,\" said the bartender. \"Your shot, buddy.\"\n\"Your religion doesn\u2019t interest me,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"Now watch closely, ladies and gentlemen. Don\u2019t blink. This is a trick shot.\"\n\"Birthplace?\u2014Berlin, Germany.\"\n\"Profession?\"\n\"Professional gambler and amateur musician.\" \"Sex?\"\n\"Male.\"\n\"Age?\"\n\"Thirty-five.\"\n\"Education?\"\n\"University of Chicago, undergraduate; Sorbonne and Manchester (England), graduate courses in things French and British.\"\n\"Height?\"\n\"Five eight in evening shoes; five seven without.\"\n\"Color of eyes?\"\n\"Blue.\"\n\"Smoking?\"\n\"No.\"\n\"Drinking?\"\n\"Yes.\"\n\"Theater-going?\"\n\"No.\"", "2_15_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earthling time.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s little family was about to go tumbling off into space, forever. He was about to go tumbling off into space, forever. He was never going to see this shambles of a planet, this Solar System, this Universe, again.\n\"All set, son?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Oh, quite,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 briskly.\n\"I\u2019m sorry the alarm went off too soon,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Oh\u2014that\u2019s quite all right,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe purple eyes that peered at $FIRSTNAMEB0 over the rims of the gold-rimmed spectacles were a little strange, $FIRSTNAMEB0 thought.\n\"I\u2019m sorry about your little dog, too,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Thank you, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Thank you very much.\"", "1_23_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-four in the evening</span> when they caught the ferryboat at to get across the river from one continent of Central Europe to the next. The rain staged one last attack on their heads as they ran under the rain canopy for the boat. And at eleven-fifty-nine Marvin Carnes, alias Gwin Ny Teck, and Mary Rumford, alias Kazie Fritzel, told the gray man in the rain cap who took tickets at the gangplank, told him in English to hang up their bags in the tiny cabin and then began making noises of sick distress about their false teeth as the boat pushed out into the white foam-littered river and the rain abandoned them for good. Rumford pointed to their bags and said in German, \u201cTag die Ges\u00e4\u00e4ge in der Cabin, bitte, Herr Kapit\u00e4n, und Gnade uns unser Z\u00e4hne,\u201d and the gray man glared at them and went away. The couple looked at one another and hugged. Eleven fifty-nine was the magic number, the number that meant they were safe. Nothing they would do in the rest of their lives could possibly matter. They were on their way across the sea to America, to a whole new life.\nThey put the folders with...", "0_1_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-three in the morning</span>. The boys were sick, half-mad on L.S.D., and now, as we pulled away from the wreck with the two cops waving, they shouted that this was a bad mistake and one of them jumped off the pier, thinking we'd come back for him. He stood in the water and screamed, \u201cI'll fix you! I'll have you all indicted! I'll see to it that your licenses are revoked!\u201d Then he came up out of the water and ran after us on the road. \u201cCome back and get me! That was a dirty deal!\u201d At the gate he danced up and down and pointed his long, skinny finger at us. \u201cI'll fix you the absolute worst way! I'll make it the end of your licenses! I was only out for a little fun! We were just playing a little game!\u201d\n\u201cYeah! That's how they all talk! \u201c I laughed into the night.\nThe Elephant in the Cellar, the Pink Palace and the Glib Darkness of a Dirty Sunset\nTonight I'll breeze down the line\nLord have mercy on my soul.\n--Big Bill Broonzy\nAnd now at last the West was behind...", "1_9_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-one in the morning</span> and we were sitting in the car on a side street in Old Town, waiting for the Drug Convention to start. The Trailblazer was gone. We had parked it, according to plan, in a slot labeled DRIVER PARKING ONLY, directly in front of the charter bus docks off Akerman Street. We planned, according to our cop/crazy theory, to \u201clet it sit\u201d about an hour, then move it to a far slot in the convention lot around the corner on Gayley . . . a space reserved for bigshot speakers, which our passes said we were. This move would make a big impression on theorters of the conference, who would assume we were heavy hitters of some kind. The idea was to make a quick wave of applause and fear when we came to the podium. The impression left would be \u201cGoddamn! Here come the man with the Big This and the Big That!\u201d The plan was to offer a very small mushroom, one that looked like dried straw, and interpret this rejection by the crowd as its natural hostility to \u201cstraight know ledge.\u201d We planned to scoff at this with a wave of generosity, saying \u201cWell, folks, I guess we\u2019re beyond the elementary h...", "1_14_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. I was giving a party for Camille soon. The house had to be cleaned and food bought. Now I was very nervous. I was on a big time now, with a big time problem. My attorney had spoken to the judge. I'd get the final decision on my petition to erase the SEC trouble right after the party. Meanwhile, I was running with a dangerous crowd: rock and roll. Several big time acts were expected. My attorney had also reportedly sanctioned this event; and I wanted approval. My house was the size of a floor cushion. The majority of the living room was occupied by a giant pool table with massive ivory feet. This thing came with a three-story mahogany bar. There was no bathroom on that floor; it was a bar, for Christ's sake. There was a living room, a dining room, and a kitchen. The house was a gift from my manager; he rented it to me for two dollars a year. Anybody else would have been happy with the situation. I took the house and ran it into the ground. Every trace of its original furnishings were now gone. The ivory runt pipes of a bar stood over the ashes of a shattered pool table. My stereo was there, along with a huge...", "0_7_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven seventeen in the morning</span>. As I sat there, the door of the apartment opened and my attorney walked in. He was wearing his fur-lined overcoat, a new homburg hat, and a brand\u2014new whipcord tweed suit. He grinned around at everybody in the room, nodded, shrugged, helped himself to the last cup of coffee, and sat down between the two bulls, facing me with his back to the crowd. There were suddenly so many cops in the room that I couldn't even see the two strangers\u2014and I realized for the first time what a take\u2014over I'd had. My attorney was speaking in Maynard's ear. \u201cThe machine pawned to Terry the Tramp?\u201d he asked. Maynard nodded. \u201cI'll take the trade bait. Bull Gumm can have the jacket.\u201d I could see my attorney moving closer to the dangerous stranger. \u201cThe billfold?\u201d he whispered. \u201cThe one stuffed with dirty pictures?\u201d Maynard nodded. I saw my attorney grin at Bull Gumm. \u201cI'll give this man the bankroll,\u201d he said. \u201cHe can have the wallet and everything that's in it.\u201d Bull Gumm nodded.", "2_7_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 still stood outside the iron gazebo. He was, of course, insubstantial, as all material things had become. Only the chimes, against the spires of Mars, betrayed any motion in the universe\u2014and this motion hinted, rather, at the passage of a breeze through late summer trees on a dying world in an iron spiral arm of the Milky Way.\n\"If you think things are bad, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said $SURNAME0, \"just wait.\"\n\"For what?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"For whatever is to come next,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Thank you very much for the warning,\" said $SURNAME1 sardonically.\n\"It\u2019s not a warning,\" said $SURNAME0. \"It\u2019s a spoiler\u2014\"\nIt was at this point that all further conversation was denied by the laws of time and space.", "2_8_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after eight o'clock in the morning</span>, New York time. The place was a small room in the Manhattan headquarters of the Army.\nThe show was over, having ended some sixty seconds before, on a segment showing $SURNAME0 and the dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 escorting Conrad into the barracks at Newport News.\nThe last voice from headquarters had been that of the public relations man, George F. Starr, who had announced somberly: \"From here on in, what you are about to see is entirely inside the human spirit. Inside the machine, too, for that matter. But the important thing is the inside of the human spirit\u2014and the dog.\"\nThe show ended.\nA man behind $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had remarked gleefully, \"Ever see anything so neat?\"\n\"It\u2019s terrific,\" said $SURNAME1.\nNow $SURNAME1 and the other two hundred members of the audience were not laughing. They were standing, staggering, yawning, stretching, using handkerchiefs on faces, fumbling for cigarettes.\nOnly one man remained seated, and he was snoring softly. He was a small, plump, pink man in Army greens, and he was asleep with his head on the breast of a buxom brunette who was his date.", "1_0_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty ante meridiem</span>. In the large control room beneath Redbury, three hundred yards from the rear of the long, jagged black rock, things were quiet.\nThe room was insulated from much of the noise overhead. Outside, a full crew was at work in the operations shack, getting things ready. Counting those in the operations shack, the final complement of men and Women for the midnight shift was ten.\nOf the ten in the control room, one was a Woman. She stood at a clean, bare, uncluttered desk in the attitude of a geisha serving tea, except that she held in one hand a microphone, and in the other an unopened book.\nBefore her on the desk was a computer key that looked like a great many bakelite switches with Bakelite knobs beneath them. To the left side of the desk was a Bakelite control box, on the front of which were two red knobs and seventy-eight dark little squares.\nThese two controls the geisha-like woman did not touch. The whole stack of switches, switches that put out enough juice to turn lightnings on Jupiter into mere summertime zigzags, were sealed under glass. Not even the woman dared touch them.\nOn the back of the control box...", "2_12_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-one p.m.</span>\nThe factory whistles were blowing.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put his hands over his ears. He crouched by the fireplace. His vision swam\u2014he couldn\u2019t see anything. The whistles were shrilling at a thousand decibels. $FIRSTNAMEB0 vomited.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head cleared, but the whistles still seemed to be in his head. He couldn\u2019t hear anything, but he imagined he could still hear the whistles.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 went to a ventilator, put his mouth to it.\nThe real sound of the factory whistles was like ten billion fingernails screeching across ten billion blackboards.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 listened.\nThe whistle song was a maniacal cachinnation, a ghastly giggling. It was the song of hyenas and croaking toads, of lovers\u2019 sighs and Earth\u2019s lament. In that song was the history of misery, of King George\u2019s nose and the Maginot Line. There was the fall of Rome, the fall of Pharoah, the fall of Nixon.\nThrough the mad orchestration of the whistles came the stop-start chant of the rioters:", "2_3_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-five in the morning</span>, and there was no one he wanted to talk to or be with.\nHad there been, he would in all probability have let the twenty minutes remaining before the sunrise be infused with the robust pleasure of their society. As it was, he had to make his own entertainment.\nHe watched his wife, who was cuddling the dog and crooning to it over the common ordeal. $SURNAME1 could have told her that there was no basis for the common ordeal. Happiness and pain, dreamy sex and happy-ouch, only seemed like golden and iron things. It was the mysteriously self-renewing cycles of agony and boredom that were made of stuff like gold and iron.\nBronson Jones was the only other member of the household who hadn\u2019t been put to bed. He was still sitting disconsolately on the marble steps of the staircase. He was dressed completely, his suitcase at his feet, ready to flee at the slightest provocation. Whenever an unfriendly gust of wind rattled the windowpanes over the front door, he looked up fearfully, fearfully foresaw that it was about to be announced that war had broken out between Mars and Earth again.", "2_11_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until noon</span> on Saturday, August fifteenth. In Newport, it was customarily a splendid time of year: the yachts were out in full regalia, and their gay sails gave the little harbor the aspect of a ballet complex.\nThe weather was said by The Weather Bureau to be fair.\nThere was no hint of the unnecessary turbulence that was to come\u2014of the cyclone scheduled to hit in one minute and fifty-five seconds.\nThe standard defense against the unexpected is a bluff. How does the person who is about to die suppose he is going to die? Undoubtedly he supposes he is going to die unpleasantly, but eventually. So\u2014sooner or later, died unpleasantly, but eventually sounds like a reasonable approximation of how he will die.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, who had read nothing for weeks except brokerage reports and the sports pages, was making a fine bluff.", "0_18_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-two in the evening</span> when the red convertible went over the cliff. The three men in the car were apparently on the verge of either a killing or a lynching, or of some transaction either horrible or ludicrous, or perhaps ghastly and grimly funny. It all depended on which way the blond youth moved. Any way he moved would have been the wrong way.\nBut he made no move at all. For just as the convertible went over the cliff that overlooked Santa Monica and the Pacific Ocean\u2014the blond youth acted. And because he acted and thus saved their lives, the three men in the car, who had a completely legitimate reason for hating each other, formed a bond of a very particular, intimate and unique nature, together forever, come hell, high water, or the Fourth of July.\nThe youth\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1. The men\u2019s names were Stony Stevenson, Reynard Mulderer, and $FIRSTNAMEB0, an enlisted man in the Army of the United States of America. Every one of these names, of course, had a story all its own. But, to begin at the beginning, and keep to the straight and narrow, the background and antecedents of the three men can be briefly stated, and thus set aside.", "1_17_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> and the place was Echo Courts, the last place on earth anybody without just cause or an invitation could fuck with you\u2014and in that year of The Bomb and the Hippies and Dumb America that was the only place in the country where anything like it could have happened\u2014and I saw everything that happened and tripped personally bigtime while it happened. One of the ironies of the drug culture. With a little luck and better brains we could have owned the country. We could have taken it over and run it down to its hairy warts. We had all the tools. Wayne and I had possession of huge gobs of cash; we had munitions; we had drugs; we had the docks and the waterfront and a hangover that made the hard life of the 'American Dream' seem sweet and wonderful when we drew each morning from our favorite benny/mesc cocktail. I wanted to play Death Valley Pete with my friends. \u201cThey'll all die off,\u201d I'd say, \u201cand we'll have all the fun guns and TV's.\u201d But we were too far out; we didn't know what to do; the energy overloaded our circuits. It blew everything f 1: the awful truth revealed that we weren't funny and the...", "2_2_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n/b/\nAugust 22, 1991\n... Patrice, where are you? Oh, Patrice, I am searching. I am searching for you, you and your colleagues, hours and days and nights, in a surreal landscape glimpsed only once in the dark imagination, a dark desert lit by a monstrous moon that seems to me now as real as this desert by daylight, and as natural. Songs I know are being sung over loudspeakers. It is difficult to see once the night comes.\nThis morning with the sun I saw a pattern in the empty sky, huge letters, as of stone etched in granite sky, Jerusalem, which I have never seen but have located only from hearsay, by report, and it seemed to me an omen, Patrice, but of what I cannot guess. It did occur to me at the time the possibility that in some way I was being helped to locate you, I had just seen the name of a sum and broadcaster, amid the scattered names that make up the body of the sky. The voice told me \u201cYou\u2019re soaked through with grace,\u201d but that could mean anything. Then the sun crossed over the inscription and obliterated it.", "2_4_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe bandage was over $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s right nipple. He was now \"right-naked.\" The significance of this was of course a sexual significance.\nOld $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 entered the little cubicle again, now wearing a blue and gold dressing gown trimmed with white ermine. He held, this time, a glass of fire issued from the freezers of Mars.\n\"Been having fun?\" he said to $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Yes, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I surely have.\"\n\"You\u2019re a big, healthy boy, aren\u2019t you, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said $SURNAME0. \"You\u2019re certainly not the criminal type, and you\u2019re by no means a fool. Your only real problem is that you\u2019ve always been too damned honest for your own good.\" It was, for $SURNAME0, a long speech.\n\"Yes, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"I guess that\u2019s my problem. \" He meant it, although he did not know what it was he was supposed to have guessed. He had been making wild stabs at the meaning of everything $SURNAME0 said.", "1_5_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-five in the morning</span> when I alighted before the stony fa\u00e7ade of the place. I pushed through the glass door. The bar was empty save for the tender, who looked up from his newspaper; and the enormous, divine, beautiful Virginia from my past.\n\u201cHello, hello, hello, hello!\u201d I cried. \u201cMy all time sweetheart! You don\u2019t remember me, but I remember you. When last I was here I asked you to marry me and you slammed the door and turned off the lights and ran away. Yes! Ha-ha-ha! Oh wow!\u201d\nI grabbed her hand and kissed it and laughed insanely. The tender came hurrying over.\n\u201cWho are you talking to, sir?\u201d he demanded.\n\u201cThis is Virginia, I\u2019ve just arrived. Tell them in back I\u2019m here and hurry, man, ah you don\u2019t comprehend.\u201d He rushed away. Virginia looked at me vaguely, the tender re-appeared. \u201cSir, they say they have no one working of that name.\"\n\u201cWhat?\u201d My mouth fell open. Then I realized I had used the girl\u2019s assumed name...", "2_13_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-three in the afternoon</span>. The recruits chose their bunks at random. They were not difficult to choose.\nThe walls were covered with kissing utensils, luridly framed simulated silk stockings, framed silhouettes of pinup girls, and Boston shaded lamps.\nThe officer took one look around the barracks and shook his head. Then he left.\nThe remaining recruits piled their junk in the nearest bunk. The nearest bunk happened to be the one in the far right-hand corner of the room.\nThe recruits unpacked and put their uniforms in their new bureaux.\nThey hesitated and quarreled a bit over who was to have which of the thirty remaining bunks.\nA third of an hour later, the recruits were all telling jokes and stories in their new bunks, which happened to be right next to the ones they had chosen in the far right-hand corner of the room. They were all quite pleased with their placement.\nBut the next time, or the time after that, they would not be so pleased or so lucky.\nThe Recruit\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were two of eight recruits in the cleaning and maintenance department of the hospital.", "2_7_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nSunlight was skipping around the walls, columns and cornices of the Museum of Natural History, washing the gilded lettering on the cases that held the mummies. It was sparkling off the feet of the Thinker on the fountain, making him seem to float on a golden bowl of helicopter propellent. The water in the fountain was merely glassy.\nA man shivered in the wind, surrounded by columns, at the top of the stairs outside the Museum\u2019s entrance. Then he lurched into the Museum.\n\"Good morning\u2014sir\u2014ma\u2019am.\" The museum clerk peered owlishly at the shivering man. He felt sorry for the man. \"Can I help you?\"\n\"Copernicus\u2014\" the visitor said hoarsely. He clutched a large black box to his breast. He was shivering so badly that he could hardly speak.\n\"Yes, sir\u2014our 975?\" said the clerk. \"Which one do you mean?\"\n\"Which one?\" said the visitor. He looked up at the numerals on a tower. His lips moved as he read the numerals out of harmony, as though the numerals were painted on moving fan blades.", "2_2_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until three o'clock in the morning</span>. Lang Kirby would be recovering from a thumping hangover by the time the sun came up, which would be in just over three and a half hours, having bought a bottle of Maker\u2019s Mark at the bar and put himself to bed in a room at Trudy and Rick\u2019s, accompanied by a drop of warm rain that dented the dust of Springfield Boulevard, in a street-light\u2019s glow that was tinged yellow by the East.\n/d/\n\u201cEat the seed.\u201d\n\u201cWhat??\u201d\n\u201cEat the seed.\u201d\n\u201cWhy do you keep saying that, Professor? Eatin\u2019 the seed\u2019s about the last thing I feel like doing, after that dinner.\u201d\n\u201cThis is not dinner. We do not now eat dinner. We are at last making viable dietetic headway, Lenore.\u201d\n\u201cHow come I can\u2019t seem to taste my food when I\u2019m around you? And I don\u2019t think you ever told me how come dinner wasn\u2019t dinner, either.\u201d", "0_2_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-nine in the morning</span>. Our races was run, our Hambletonian. I turned off all the house lights, because I hoped $FIRSTNAMEB3, who was sleeping in the house, would not awaken and I could tiptoe into the car and we could make our getaway quiet. But $FIRSTNAMEB3 woke up, and in the dark he somehow got the lights on. I heard him murm't about it. The motor was running, everything ready for a fast take-off. He wanted to say ho and make a big story of it, say there was a sharp rattle in the carburetor or reason for this take-off. My instinct was to get out of there in a great lunge. Now we was in absolute faraway darkness, and I couldn't see nothing as we made the long curve into Pennsylvania. \u201cWhere are we? Where are we?\u201d I asked $FIRSTNAMEB3 with my mind. \u201cNo coast, no way of knowing till we see signs.\u201d And there were no signs; not just now. I was so completely gone I had the notion that we'd already passedthrough Pennsylvania and entered the withered, final wastes of dead watermos tly that lie beyond. The only thing left was stop at the end of the line. I was...", "1_10_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten sixteen in the morning</span>. I was scheduled to meet my attorney at the Beverly Hills Restaurant at noon. I opened the trunk of the Shark and threw inthe black box... good Lord, the drug paraphernalia. I crammed it down and slammed the trunk lid. What in hell was I trying to do? Complete the ugly picture for the cops? Hide it in the trunk of a car that was clearly marked with the sticker of the Narcotics and Liar Testing Pool?\nIII[Pressure and the Sleeping Slit Eye of Dawn]\npressure, pressure; no two ways about it\u2014and when I reached the frosty sidewalk in front of my \u201coffice,\u201d I wanted more drugs, wanted them instantly and very badly. I wanted them as a precedent for any action I might want to take...and I felt nothing but vague panic at the thought that there would be none available to take. This is a totally insane situation, I told myself. You picked the wrong goddamn business to be in. Drugs are your life, man \u2014 and now you don\u2019t have any.\nPerhaps my attorney will have some, When I see him at noon. But what will I say? How will I explain the need? How in the name of hell can I explain the...", "0_18_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty in the evening</span>, and the place was $FIRSTNAMEB2, a moon of Mars. Life-force, carbon, and water had been supplied to $FIRSTNAMEB2 by Mars, by way of Earth. Atomic energy had been supplied to $FIRSTNAMEB2 by the good offices of the United Individualities of Titan.\nThe population of $FIRSTNAMEB2 was one thousand and thirty-six, give or take a few children in the cradle.\nThe population was stable, expanding about one percent a year.\nThe population was a highly selective population. Only one man, one woman, and one child were permitted to be born on $FIRSTNAMEB2 each year. The Committee on Outside Contacts picked the lucky man, the lucky woman, and the lucky child.\nThere were a number of committee rooms in the main administration building on $FIRSTNAMEB2. One of those rooms housed $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s telephone switchboard\u2014that and nothing else. There were no telephones in the houses of $FIRSTNAMEB2. There was no communication among the houses of $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nThe houses of $FIRSTNAMEB2 were as alike as eggshells. They had been designed at the factory in great rectangular sheets of plasticene. The houses had walls, windows, doors, floors, rooms, and roofs in the lovely, simple, uncreative, est...", "2_1_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty in the morning</span>. It was dark outside, with nothing showing through the shutters but sheets of rain. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 lived in San Narciso, California, on a slope of Mount McCabe, overlooking Route 118. Mount McCabe was seventeen miles from the beautiful Pacific Ocean. On clear days, if one were seated just right in a theatre, the ocean might be glimpsed far, far below, winkling at the bottom of the world like an enormous blue eye.\nTo the north of Mount McCabe was a residential district called Tarzana. $SURNAME1\u2019s home was in North Tarzana. The homes of the stars were in West Tarzana. East of Tarzana was the city itself\u2014San Narciso. Excavating equipment and stripped forests revealed what San Narciso had once been. It had been a garbage heap, a heap so vast that efforts had had to be made to rid it of blowing trash.\nNo considerable effort had been made to disguise this fact. San Narciso was modern California architecture at its ugliest. The average dwelling place was a block-long slab with barred windows, a slab that cried shame on you as you passed by it.", "1_17_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the year of the action was approximately 2,000,000 B.C.\nThey wondered at the violet sun, and at the ground beneath them, which was misty and gave off a faintly argent glow. Inside their suits the men sweated at once.\nThey looked at one another.\nTheir panic was communicated without words.\nTheir feet were rooted in the ground, their arms were glued to their sides, their suits were wet inside, sweat trickled down their faces.\nAt last the tall, thin lieutenant with the wide, blunt nose and the narrow, sharp eyes got his fingers under his collar, under the gold patches that reminded everyone he had been anointed Earthling by the King of Mars himself, and ripped upward with ripping curses.\nSomeone tore a radio component out of his gun so that the gun couldn\u2019t shoot any more. The component was put in a knapsack.\nThen eighteen men started walking hastily toward the violet misty east, indicating more or less that deep urge to hover, which had been given them by the Red Planet.\nA Martian watched the men for several minutes, standing on a metal something like a stair outside his emerald-tinted glass home. Then he went inside.", "0_8_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 shook weakly awake and felt the strong hand of the greatest fear of his life. With the coming fully awake, the hand was gone.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 lay on his pallet between the hard, smooth walls, and he was ten feet underground. Outside the walls was nothing, absolutely nothing.\nInside the walls, $FIRSTNAMEB0 lay in the faint light of a lithium cell.\nThere were twenty such cells in the room. Eighteen of them were occupied.\nThe people in the other eighteen cells slept.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had slept too\u2014and had awakened before his neighbors. The neighbors\u2019 sleep had an element of control in it. $FIRSTNAMEB0 found this control by looking at his wrist watch on the far side of the room.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s wrist watch said, \"Don\u2019t wake any of the others yet.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 rolled out of his pallet, and began to dress. He had to move with great stealth, since to move noisily would have been to wake the neighbors, and they would have been unhappy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t want to wake the neighbors.\nHe dressed in the brown uniform of the Assault Infantry of the United States Space Marine Corps.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been...", "2_14_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>, Pacific Standard Time.\nAt two thirty-eight the ship ran into something no bigger or denser than a beach ball. The ship shattered in all directions. There were no survivors.\n[tight close]\nI didn\u2019t die young and it was a cheap way of staying drunk.\n-RAYMOND CARVER", "2_10_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"This damn town is upside down\u2014nothing works right.\"\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\"All right\u2014here we are, 4700 Belskutt,\" said the lieutenant colonel, straining in vain to make out the numbers on the house fronts. All the house fronts on Belskutt Street were obscured by tall Leyland cypress hedges that had been trimmed low to the ground all the way across the street.\n\"Yes, sir.\"\nThe driver backed up to the curb and stopped. \"You people going to have to find it on your own from now on,\" said the lieutenant colonel. \"God knows I\u2019m not going to drive around in circles, making nice to the enlisted men\u2019s wives.\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said the noncom in the back seat.\n\"Get out and act like human beings, and read the house numbers,\" said the lieutenant colonel. \"Anybody wants anything, let me know.\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said the driver, climbing out and joining the noncom in the back.\n\"Straighten those puttees, you lazy bastard!\" the lieutenant colonel yelled at Belskutt, who was still pedaling under the wind generator.", "2_23_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-six in the evening</span>.\nHow brilliantly the Martian sky-master in Phoebeville portrayed the grandeur and terror of war! How magnificently the Master of Jupiter told the fabled story of a war on Jupiter! The painting of Mars was a pictorial summary so masterly in its accuracy, so inflammatory in its imagination, that every Earthling saw himself as the constant hero of the unfolding drama.\nA mob of volunteers thirty deep on the metal platforms of the $FIRSTNAMEB0 County, Tennessee Land Hulk were led by fear, boredom, and a crowd, across marble squares and through alleys with cannons booming, over battlefields sketchily mapped for them by officers shouting lies about the terrain that lay ahead. In their excitement, the volunteers laughed and sang and talked insanely.\nA vast lot of what was said seems hilarious, though very little of it was meant to be.\n\"Chicken, biscuits, and beans, so God damn delicious\u2014\" said a one-legged AmHim soldier.\n\"Old iron tea pot\u2014\" he said, \"dum-dah-dum-dah-dum!\n\"Great Day in the Morning\u2014\" he said. \"Gimme that old iron tea pot\u2014\" he said.", "0_7_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> when I got to my hotel, and there was no porter in sight. I had to tote my own bag through a maze of corridors to the elevator and up to the right floor.\nI unlocked the door of my room and started to put the bag on the bed. Then I saw the woman.\nShe was lying white and still on the floor of the minibar, and blood was coming up out of her finger.\n\"Sweet durn!\u201d I said.\nTHE CHAPTER\nThere was no phone in the room, not even a house phone beside the bed. I ran out through the corridors to the desk. The clerk saw me coming and met me halfway, before I could scream.\n\"What's the matter, sir?\u201d he said soothingly. \u201cAnything I can do for you?\"\n\"Yes!\u201d I said. \u201cGet me a doctor, get me a priest, get me a passer of the Vatican, one of the good ones.\"\n\"Sit down, sit down, sir.\" He led me to a chair that was standing by itself in a corner. It was dirty. The housekeeping staff had been out on a strike.\n\"What is it, sir...", "0_20_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>. It was sixty more minutes until midnight. The signal for the end of the search for the Nova family would go out at twelve o\u2019clock midnight. And the search would end at the signal.\nThere would be no search after twelve o\u2019clock midnight.\nAt seven minutes past nine o\u2019clock, a report came in from the Space Wanderer that he had found pieces of the shattered Parke-Laine monoplane in a field two miles from the hole he had fallen through.\nAs though nothing could be important after that\u2014as though his own purpose in being in Newport was done with now\u2014as though his own problems were really over now\u2014the young man in yellow had quietly slipped out of the stuffy little cubicle, up the stairs and out of $SURNAME0\u2019s mansion.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 had said nothing about a harmonium. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was grateful to Fortune for that.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 knew nothing about a harmonium.\nAnd since the harmonium was a mystery to $FIRSTNAMEB0, he also assumed it to be a mystery to every space wanderer in the Solar System. He concluded that every space wanderer in the Solar System shared his own fear of the harmonium\u2014great fear, tremor...", "2_15_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three sixteen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was $FIRSTNAMEF0-Power House, 1795 Sacramento Street, San Francisco, California.\nCaxton, Nebraska, had not gone back to being a silly whistle-stop on a grassy midtown boulevard since $FIRSTNAMEB1 had strangled its potentialities. $FIRSTNAMEF0-Power House had taken over Caxton\u2019s role. $FIRSTNAMEF0-Power House received the bulk of the Red Wing trainees.\nThe sun, having recently set in the Pacific, was still too bright to look at directly.\nThe light inside the Power House was kept low by the color of the stained-glass windows. The light was only one step better than darkness. The polished marble floor echoed voices, but did not have the depth or the carrying power of tile.\nAt the back of the powerhouse, in an office lit by a red lamp hung high overhead, sat Alvin Mangrim, director of recruitment for the Army.", "0_23_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span> when I had this conviction of the inaccessibility of sex. I was walking up the boulevard, slowly, and there were so many women walking in that majestic promenade in the night time Paris streets such splendid women, too, with faces of that Marcelle Maurey type, that nothing could happen. There were only foreigners walking there and half-drunk Americans who did not matter. It is only drunk Americans who matter on the boulevard. Drunk Americans are the only people who matter in the world. All the girls walk up and down the boulevard. It means nothing. And the great gay theatre of Paris went on before my exultant eyes. It was just play-acting. No one had ever been in love. No one had ever done any of those things the rhythmical Frenchmen did. Each phrase that was uttered in the early morning Paris streets everybody had said it a thousand times before, and would say it afterward. I knew that it was all play-acting. The war was over. Nobody had been cut in half. Nobody had been wounded. Nobody had been punished. I knew that love did not exist. I could not be made to believe in love. But go on all you people! Go on, million of people...", "1_0_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 slept in a voluminous, trundle bed. His bed occupied the room\u2019s eastern quarter, as did his mother\u2019s. Between mother-and-son bed and western wall was empty space. Nothing but air.\nThe empty space would be occupied by $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s father\u2019s bed, by l\u2019 wink of doomsday.\nIt was not expected that $FIRSTNAMEB2 would survive his father\u2019s entrance. $FIRSTNAMEB2 thus slept peacefully, as well as heavily. He breathed as profoundly as a grandmother being snored.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was waking up. He had had a dream of things being other than they were. He was beginning to understand that dreams are best left dreams. The awakening was painful, but an explosion of pain was held off by the cool, steady depth of the breaths of sleep. The pain would choke an arousing sleeper into hysteria.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 felt a hand patting him. He opened his eyes. It was his mother, standing beside his bed.\nBe quiet, his mother whispered, and she patted $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s cheek. Be a good boy. Be a man.\nThe pats ended in a caress. A last caress.", "1_20_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-nine in the evening</span>. In an hour's time, I realized, I would be stoned. But smiling\u2014what was so grotesque about all this. I was having a marvelous time. My attorney had disappeared but I was giggling my way through the casino with this enormous Bulgarian woman lawyer who was happy, playful, and more than a little drunk. She had a room somewhere in the hotel, and she wanted me to go there, for what I assumed would be a continuation of the wild orgy we had begun, but then, in the elevator, on the way up from the casino to the lobby, she suddenly came to herself and straightened up. Down the elevator crack went the panties, out into the parking lot skipped the bra. She got out of the elevator cold sober, with her arms folded across her chest, and gave me good-bye like a clerk saying good morning to the milkman.\nWe were at the desk asking for a night\u2019s itinerary\u2014two telegrams and a room key\u2014when my attorney walked up, drunk as a fiddler, giggling and joking with a couple of black boys in tow. \u201cYou must be high!\u201d this black manager said. My attorney said nothing. This is your Honored Guest here...", "2_17_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-seven in the afternoon</span> on Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 woke to the touch of a woman on his brow. He looked up into the suffering face of a beautiful woman whose eyes were brown and yellow, whose hair was brittle and brown. The woman was a stranger.\n\"Who are you?\" asked $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"I am your wife, stupid,\" said the beautiful stranger.\nLife had become very interesting for $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe little red-headed man from Mars had made Earthling history, as he\u2019d promised. He had been smuggled through a bird Golden Gate in a shipment of fertilizer that arrived at two o\u2019clock in the morning.\nAn hour later, he crawled out of a sewer on the outskirts of Newark, New Jersey, and shambled into town, chanting his Martian marching song to keep his spirits up. \"Da da da dum\u2014\" he sang. \"Da da da dum\u2014\"\nHe had five dollars in coins in his hip pocket. From time to time, he dropped a coin and scrambled on all fours to pick it up.", "2_7_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>, Earth time.\nThe object that punched up through the Martian canopy was, in appearance, a two-man space ship.\nIt was sucked up through twenty feet of blue-green Martian air, through the Firmament. When it reached the Firmament, it was not torn apart by the great difference in outside and inside air-pressures. The air pressure inside the ship\u2019s hull was the same as that outside.\nThe ship\u2019s hull was a force field such as the space ships of Earth were equipped with.\nThere were other remarkable things about the ship, and they were all by-products of primitive technology.\nThe ship was manufactured, not in some automated Twentieth Century assembly line, but in a ceramic-hulled dirigible that had been designed and built in the Twentieth Century. In the rocket motor, a large dream by unlettered German peasants became reality, the burning of alcohol and liquid oxygen.", "1_11_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, and the place was the blue rug where $SURNAME1 had faced the closed doors of the $SURNAME0 estate elevator. $SURNAME1 was standing.\nConspicuously missing from $SURNAME1\u2019s person was the materialization device that ship and compan~ owned. The insurance that $SURNAME1 had established against loss of the device was to expire at noon\u2014half an hour after the time of the action here related.\n$SURNAME1 was wearing very rumpled skintight buckskin pants and a buckskin jacket open to the waist. The jacket was a size too large for him, and drooped from his shoulders. Beneath it, $SURNAME1 wore a horrid old moth-eaten lavender union suit. His pockets were as sloppy and lumpy as his union suit. His bare feet were as blue and uncalloused as the feet of any lifelong cripple.\n$SURNAME1 looked up from the pointless task of beating carpet fuzz.\n\"You\u2019re him, all right,\" said a voice from behind him. \"The man I\u2019m to give the device to when you don\u2019t pick it up on the thirteenth.\"\n$SURNAME1 looked over his shoulder\u2014and gaped. Standing behind him was a young woman who should have been an extravagant beauty...", "0_20_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span> of the second day after the war started. At the thought of the war, $FIRSTNAMEM1 could not repress a demonstration of nerve. He put his hand over his eyes again. Now, he said to himself, I must remember to stand up and then to sit down and then to stand up and then to sit down again. As the loudspeaker let out another shriek, and the instruments of the orchestra were drowned by the chatters and squeals of the public, he sat down quickly and seized a newspaper from the rack in front of him.\nConcentrate, he said to himself. Go on reading. Take your mind off what you can\u2019t bear to look at or listen to. Beside him, the oiled, circumcised creature presaged, perhaps innocently, a blow that could not miss. Two days ago there had still been doubts, anxiety, the element of alarm. Now there was real, not pretend danger. Something irreversible was about to happen. The kick more than likely would not miss. Something had turned its full force upon them. \"If that explosion went off in the City of London,\" he had said two days ago, \"what about Broadway and the District?\"\n\u2014\"It is starting,\" said the voice over the loudspeaker...", "1_7_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-four in the morning</span>. A single shaft of late sunlight lay like a golden spear across the width of the lobby. As I watched, it moved imperceptibly, suggesting that morning had already come outside. I walked over and took a quick look through the glass doors and saw that outside it was dark.\nThe paved lot to the south was crowded with newsmen\u2019s cars and motorcycles. I sat down. While I waited for the others to show up I planned my campaign. It seemed pretty clear. My main advantage was that basically nobody wanted me to do what I was going to do. My publisher, of course, wanted me to find the whereabouts of my attorney so we could quickly settle our contract litigation; but he\u2019d never really be crazy about my facing the American judicial system on such a beef. My attorney, meanwhile, would prefer that I go back to New York and straighten out my drug problem; but he\u2019d hate the idea of my voluntarily trading my real life for one on the inside.\nAnd if I was fairly certain the system would choke on my presence, I doubted that anybody on the outside would be too anxious to see me escape a just penalty for what I\u2019d done in Las Vegas. Not the press. Not my friends...", "1_8_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-one in the morning</span>. The date was Tuesday, August 6, 2005. $FIRSTNAMEF0 was out of coffee.\nThe second set of entries in the $FIRSTNAMEB2 entry, under \u201cThe Story,\u201d is as follows:\nHello.\nI\u2019m sure you remember me. This is your story. If you haven\u2019t looked at it for some time, let me remind you of the plot. A man and a woman, who do not at first appear to like each other very much, meet at a terrible place. They are imprisoned, and also, in a sense, married. They explore their surroundings. The woman finds a door in the terrible place, and they explore and discover the door leads to a place that looks exactly like the first place, a place they do not know how to leave. The woman finds this very irritating. The man and the woman fight and do not like each other very much. The man hurts the woman\u2019s feelings. The woman hurts the man\u2019s feelings. They begin to grow fond of each other, and also to dote on each other. The man and the woman are not quite certain if they have consummated their marriage. The man is especially uncertain, because he hurt the woman\u2019s feelings...", "0_9_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-six in the morning</span>.\nThe building entrance from which the maid was looking down was not on the same level as the floors on which $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 lived. The building had been put together higgledy-piggledy over the centuries\u2014bits and pieces here, bits and pieces there. The result was three stories on one side, four on another, and two on another.\nThe entrance doors stood thirty feet above the real base of the building.\nThis height had proved adequate in a city where people rarely climbed more than five flights to get to their nest.\nOnly a few people were moving in the street below the maid. Earthquakes had made the buildings shimmy in their scaffolding. Pandemic fire had made the streets dangerous. All the shops in downtown Tralfamadore had been boarded up for years. Empty and forlorn, the buildings dreamed of far-off places\u2014of Bombay, of Paris, of Ghana, of Hong Kong. And the buildings and their hopes were not chimerical. Every ten years, a ship came from the green world of Tralfamadore to buy their goods, to trade goods from Tralfamadore in return. In spite of all the social disarray and political madness that existed...", "0_3_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span> when the Washingtons arrived in Indianapolis. The city was a tense place, with troops and tanks being brought into the downtown area at all hours of the day or night. The tanks rested heavily on tracks that squealed like ten thousand plucked banjos as they rumbled across the bricks of' the downtown streets.\nMrs. Washington and the children had dozed off en route. They awoke to Indianapolis at an ominous hour, with a sky so fiery red\" one could not be sure it was not still three o\u2019clock in the morning.\nOld Man $SURNAME0 was already there\u2014awaiting them on the steps of the downtown mansion. He braced himself against the strangest wind Mrs. Washington had ever felt. It was a mysterious wind that seemed to blow in clusters of three or four hard streams, then die to nothing, then blow in clusters of three or four hard streams again.\n\"Un-huh!\" the wind blew. \"Un-huh!\" It was the most mournful sound Mrs. Washington had ever heard. It seemed somehow to embody the tragedy of feuding bluebloods, of a peaceful people being driven mad by a meddlesome new order.\nOld Man $SURNAME0 wore his wealth with...", "0_16_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2's ship had come to rest on an oxbow of an Earthling swamp that was appropriately named the Sargasso Sea.\nWho were the Earthlings?\nThey were the dominant intelligent lifeform on a large, fertile planet that circled a sun in Galactic Sector NGC 5 queens which was at a mean distance of three hundred thousand light-years from Earth. The system of NGC 5 queens was itself only one hundred and fifty thousand light-years from Earth.\nThe Earthlings had invented space travel early in their history, and they had blundered through their system and built cities on some of the moons of the otherworldly green planet that circled their sun. They had also blundered through their system and colonized all four of the other worlds in their system, as well as thirty-two of the moons of their green planet.\nTheir planet was not green, nor was it truly a planet. It was shaped like a ruby of base metal two thousand miles in diameter and two miles in height. It was a hollow, swarming solid-metal ball, rotating on its long axis. In real time, its day was exactly twenty-two Earthling hours long. In real time, one replica of its sun shone in the sky.", "1_9_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-three in the morning</span> of August 19 when the owner of Visi-Sonor Products, Inc. froze up solid. His name was Herman C. Cutcutcut. And by nine forty, when his plant manager broke down the door and rushed in, swearing that he\u2019d find Herman frozen and pick the icicles off him and bring him around, it was too late. The terrified plant manager found Herman Cutcutcut truly and solidly frozen, knees bent, by his desk, wired to a telephone and a telegraph key. In his last spasms Herman had typed unbelievable gibberish on his typewriter, and apparently had commanded uncountable hordes of demons who answered his crys by hitting switchboards all over the United States. The West Coast, always vulnerable, had been put out of business. Cleveland and Chicago were hit next. Delivery trucks were blasted off the road and their drivers found, at the thaw, curled around their steering wheels, teeth marks in their thumbs. Herman Cutcutcut\u2019s company was in the business of manufacturing novelty electric bug and rat devices for cats. The famous Doctor Richard Pan Ace, who was also President of the United? States, was interested in purchasing one of the new \u201cMerlin III\u2019s,\u201d for...", "0_17_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nTHE NEVERENDING STORY\nIT WAS FIVE twenty-one in the afternoon of a Tuesday in October, 1985, when $SURNAME1 found his way finally to the steel and reinforced concrete vault of $FIRSTNAMEB2 Corporation.\nThe office building within which the vault was located was dominated by a gray, inhuman H. A.. Lilly, architect. The windowless, badge-infested corridors were marble-eyed, too. $SURNAME1 followed the one marked 22 B, for the office of $SURNAME0\u2019s holding company. To pass the time, $SURNAME1 took off and put on his sunglasses, like a man playing the cymbals.\nThere was a hologram of young $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 on the wall in Dolphin\u2019s outer office.\nThe hologram was the queerest thing that $SURNAME1 had ever seen. It was a full representation of $SURNAME0, right down to the straying hands and the mischievous smile, and yet it was transparent. Only the edges of the representation had any substance at all.\n$SURNAME1 wondered whether to mention it to Whale, but decided not to.\n\"Good morning, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said Whale\u2019s secretary, a young man with a feather duster...", "1_10_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The throne-room of the Red Palace was tenanted by a rumpled, unkempt, sullen group of strange creatures who represented, as near as anything, the culture and society of the canal-dwellings of Mars, twelve thousand years before the time of the Canopic Jar.\nOne of these creatures exhibited all the excitement and agitation of a human being. This was a boy-like individual in a moldered and rusty red robe, who paced back and forth worryingly before the throne. He was followed by an overgrown, watchful and sullen yellow dog.\nThe attitude of the other creatures might best be described by a series of quotations.\n\"I would prefer an adequate explanation.\" This was from a ten-foot, blue-capped official who sat on the left flank of the throne. He wore between his first and second toes a copper ingot worth forty thousand dollars in any financial center of known space. His name was Krov, and he was a crime commissioner.\n\"I quite agree with you, Lav,\" said the Martian commander-in-chief without much sympathy.\nKrov had just been subjected to a rather rigid cross-examination. His patience had been severely tried by the antics of...", "2_17_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\nIt was October twenty-third.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized on the offices of Galaxy Magazine in New York City, materialization being defined as the instantaneous coming into uncollapsed existence of a substance that had until then existed in the timeless limbo of future possibilities.\nGalaxy\u2019s offices occupied the twelfth, thirteenth, and fourteenth floors of a building which was separated from the next building to the south by a space so narrow that visitors to one office had to duck, at the door, to avoid cracking their heads on the coral-crusted ball that dangled in the entrance to the office next door. Completing the precarious alley were two more staggered towers, a ramp, and an Observatory on Earth, and a satellite and an airlock on Mars.", "1_17_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME1 dried himself, put on blue striped trousers, a silk shirt, and a dark blue, white and blue sport coat. He attached no special significance to the clothes he put on. He had long ago given up trying to read any mystic meaning into the clothes he put on.\nThe only thing that troubled him was the thought that, if he had had his choice, he would not have been Kevin $SURNAME1, at all. In fact, he would have asked to be someone else entirely.\n\"I don\u2019t feel good,\" he said in the midst of his dressing. \"I don\u2019t feel good at all.\"\nHe said it not to himself but to his valet, a faithful old black man named End Poverty Now.\n\"You come on sick again, suh?\" said End Poverty Now.\n\"Sick?\" said $SURNAME1. He put his hand to his head. \"Yes, maybe I am feeling just a little bit sick again.\" He closed his eyes and saw a gray, infinite plain with no landmarks in it at all. \"You know,\" he said, \"I\u2019ve been feeling pretty bad, off and on, ever since I got back from Mars.\"\n\"You never did look none too good...", "0_4_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four seventeen in the morning</span>, and I had been watching the entrance to the coffee joint for almost two hours. There had been no sign of $FIRSTNAMEB3 or Carlo but I knew they'd come. I had made two complete rounds of the town blocks -- passing and repassing the coffee joint.\nMy car was parked on the broken pavement at the curb. Everything was gone. There'd been a few sad-eyed whorehouse customers but no whores and no Carlo and $FIRSTNAMEB3. The wind swept mad barbershop music through the sad-looking pines and slapped it against the weathered wooden buildings. Old Monterey is a crazy, foreign thing. For me it had only sadness. I was raised in this same decay; with another half generation it will be gone and the chow-chow and the bow-yows will be out in supermarkets and living in suburbs; sadder, but there'll be more of them and no more of this constant complaints of subhousing Japanesees, Mexicanos, and preachers, who may be on to something at that. The American dream has become a nightmare; the American nightmare will be that it has become the American reality, and nobody can think of what else to do. So you walk up and down the cracked pavement of Pacific streets, and the...", "2_3_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was not an ordinary man. He was an adventurer in mythology, the first adventurer in mythology in many years. Every adventurer risks his life. To attempt the mythological is to risk one\u2019s intellectual life as well.\nThese thoughts were in $SURNAME1\u2019s mind as he stood at the fountain in the Courtyard of the Whited Sepulchres.\nThe fountain played to a height of eighty feet. It was composed of limbs, trunks, and whole trees of shimmering, white, unflocking marble. The trees were deep-frozen, shaped with an ice saw, then slow-frozen to hardness. The branches were ponderous\u2014tawny limbs of California redwoods. The deep-frozen trunk was eighteen inches in diameter at its narrowest point.", "1_2_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two seventeen in the morning</span>. All night I sat on the rocking chair in my office in a kind of trance, listening to the roar of old Main Street below, thinking of father and $FIRSTNAMEB6 and $FIRSTNAMEB3. When my mind began to blank out, I went and got my sweated sleeping bag from the closet, unrolled it on the Duz bag in my sleeping alcove under my posters, and fell into a trancelike sleep. I only woke up once, when I heard the trombone playing and the ghostly train coming around the mountain, the midnight, Chicago-bound Mercury limited. It was two thirty-two.\nIn the blotter of my mind, the second to the last blot, way over on the right-hand side, sits that morning in Tom Snark's house, a modest frame house near the pay road, in the absence of Snark, he having been hauled away by the police for vagrancy. In the early dawn I awakened in my sleeping bag, with the street noises just beginning, and remembered all the things I'd experienced I and heard, and shuddered. I got up and breakfasted in my stocking feet and got dressed and picked my pack up off the living-room floor and lugged it downstairs. Above my...", "0_6_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>. During the last ten minutes a new and unexpected difficulty had stopped Redwine and Redwine\u2019s bodyguard\u2014a difficulty which had momentarily caused Redwine to accuse his escort of delaying tactics.\nThe trouble was other people. The vault and the tunnel were so packed with them, not one of Redwine\u2019s and Redwine\u2019s custodians could squeeze through. Other people wanted to talk to $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nRedwine explained the situation by shouting: \"Apparently $FIRSTNAMEB1 wasn\u2019t telling the truth when he said he was only minutes ahead of you. Evidently he had a lot of important things to attend to, binding up Churches, reorganizing hierarchies, making other Churches subject to St. Barnabas\u2019. He didn\u2019t get right down to business, moving you people, until minutes ago\u2014certainly not enough time to let you get your hands on that gem of his until you catch up with him.\"\nThe throng from Newport acknowledged Redwine\u2019s allusion to the chronoplus by a murmur. They all were making mental bets as to how far ahead of them $FIRSTNAMEB1 was now, and what he was doing. Redwine was going to lose, to anybody who bet ten dollars...", "2_19_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe silence of the crowd was a polite, awed, and joyful silence.\nWas there a louder noise than the wild thunder of welcome that had heralded the failure of the New World in the sky four minutes earlier? There was not\u2014unless it was the wild thunder of welcome that was banging and rolling among the hills and through the trees again as the First and Final Success came down from the sky.\nOld and New Spirit Limited, the only public transportation company in the Solar System, had done itself proud. There were two hundred and sixty-three buses at the approach to the estate, and, though they were all fully integrated and all had the same automatic controls, their conductors were nevertheless standing at attention at the doors.\nSixty-five of the buses were bullet-nosed, four-engined leviathans with gilded cockpits and names like Phallus XLI and Diana XLV. They were the gold-plated limousines of House Olympian.", "0_15_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty in the afternoon</span>, Pacific Coast time. The place was Las Vegas, Nevada. Our hero was six days\u2019 journey into the karass. The question of taking a rain check on the next two or three days of the tournament now came up. There was another thing. It was suddenly remembered that the next weekend, only fiftytwo hours away, Columbia would be playing Harvard in Cambridge. Anybody with any sense at all would want to be in Cambridge, Mass., come Saturday at two P.M.A decision was made to fly back East, by simple stratagem, by devious routes, and to be in Cambridge on Saturday, because, after all, the yarborough was still lost; who knew when or where they would find it again? The purpose here was to go to back to Boston and buy a new yarborough; then to take off again by car, a neat Packard converted tomatic for all types of terrain, and head out into No Man\u2019s Land, with plenty of good grass, and have one of those legendary End Days that all the gang back in New York talked about constantly. The only problem was this: Who was going to fly back to Boston to get a yarborough, and then drive out to Cambridge?", "1_0_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was Thurn and Taxis Castle, in the country of Czechoslovakia, on the top of a cliff overlooking a windblown Atlantic seacoast.\nWas life worth living?\nAristotle had proved long ago that no matter how bad everything was, it could always be worse. There was always something to look forward to\u2014 WINTER BONDER throughout the cold season! Salivate over this mouthwatering menu: FROSTED FAYGO, ICEBERG DRINK, with real whale oil in every bite ! BLOW IN TIRE, GET FREE T-SHIRT ...\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife died together on a cold January morning, on a runway in $SURNAME0\u2019s home estate.\nThe time was eight minutes to noon. The place was the empty contour runway beside $SURNAME0\u2019s home.\n$SURNAME0 and his wife had locked themselves inside a booster that was affixed to the bottom of a Mercury-Redstone rocket. Their purpose was to go to Mars and look for Skip and Malley\u2019s Dust.\nThe rocket was on the pad.\nThis is how the New York Times reported the deaths in its next day\u2019...", "2_6_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-two in the morning</span>.\nStony Stevenson awoke to the gong of an alarm clock somewhere in the house, and then to his watch, which he wore strapped to his wrist. But he did not spring up with a start, remembering where he was. He sat up calmly and switched on the light and then got out of bed.\nHe stretched and yawned, though he had slept well and did not feel tired.\nHe slipped his feet into his moccasins and walked out of his room into the hall of the big house. There were other bedrooms off the hall\u2014the four beautiful children\u2019s rooms, Regina\u2019s room.\nHe switched on light after light, moving from light to light in a house filled with so glorious an abundance of electrical light switches that it was almost better than being in Washington or Los Angeles.", "2_12_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-five p.m.</span>\nSteven Mallory was on the point of dismantling the harmonium in the chapel. He had come to Mercury to find the master mind behind the harmoniums, and dismantling the chapel harmonium might well be representative of the whole campaign of disassembly.\nThe little harmonium had been singing its head off for a full minute.\nIt was singing the creed of its religion.\nIn Mercury base, the religion of a harmonium was generally given due to the influence of the press gangs in its vicinity.\nIn each case, the creed of the religion had been a mystery until the harmonium sang it aloud.\n\"Love one another, bring everyone to Jesus,\" the harmonium sang. It was the Creed of West Street Presbyterian Church, New York City, Earth.\nThe harmonium also sang, regularly, a commercial for every product it knew about. It loved, above all products, a soap made on Earth called Snowball. Snowball was a toilet-bowl detergent. Snowball was advertised by means of television commercials in which hygienically clean dogs did disgusting things to hygienically clean children.", "2_8_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nThe source of the light in the room was the Sun. The light was driving the darkness out of the last deep niche in the wall, the niche beside the head of the bed. The Sun\u2019s rays in the room were so punctiliously direct that they approached regularity. They right-angled sharply off the mahogany table and the tape recorder, hit a corner of the footboard, right-angled again, and so into the wall, forming a sharp triangle of light on the mural.\nAt eight twenty-eight, three things happened. $SURNAME1, in profound sleep, turned over heavily and groaned. The Sun\u2019s right-angled rays hit a corner of the mural at a new and bizzare angle, forming an obtuse triangle of light on the bed. And an anonymous technician, three thousand miles away, shoved a switch and by-passed a fusible link in the power line to the $SURNAME0 estate.", "0_10_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME0 and his faithful dog finally hit the ground in the state of New Jersey, Earth.\nFor an instant, $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 shared the same emotion\u2014a wild, driven, hopeless desire to be somewhere else.\nThere was, in this desire to be somewhere else, a quality that almost amounted to saintliness. It was a pity, not for themselves, but for the precious things of the universe that were being put to such sad use. $SURNAME0 and his dog were sorry that so many singular parts of the universe had to be pressed into a service that was so discontinuous and meanly diversionary.\nThe system that required so much misbalance and abuse of hard-won material had its operational headquarters on Titan, one of the moons of Saturn. Titan was the home of the smoking green playmates of little $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe system was known, among tobacco smokers, as pinch-hitting. The name derived from the practice of using substitutes for cigarettes. Any substitute would do, the fenders of automobiles, whiskey glasses, the necks of champagne bottles, the spikes on ping-pong paddles\u2014even a thumb and forefinger would do, with only the hand\u2019s ball-knuckle...", "1_3_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-three in the morning</span>. Outside the Bullpen window was the Palace for the Blinding of Isaac Stein, the Interior Department\u2019s official commemoration of the birth of the Red Chinese satellite. Grope stood in his paper party hat and figured. His great egg-belly waggled below his belt as he tried to visualize. It was all in memory now. He saw the hairy wrists in their leather wallori growling into saxophones and trombones, saw the penthouse crowd do-si-do at dawn. It all took place Beardless in Teen Town. Thank You, World Premier Jazz Band. Somewhere out there that same drummer was setting up to play for the dawn do-si-do of some other group. \u201cWhere?\u201d croaked Grope.\nGrope had forgotten the name of his group. It was getting very hard for him to remember the names of people and things. Something was happening to his memory. He had a strange premonition that even his memorized tax returns would be next.\nGrope staggered to the fetal chair under his sign that said WELCOME TO THE JUNIOR COMMISSIONER\u2019S PROGRAM.\nThe cries and strains on the organ outside were building to some kind of c...", "2_23_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty in the evening</span>. The Marblehead, fully ready for sea, was tied up at one of the General Forge and Foundry Company\u2019s docks in the Bedford Basin. It stood, as it were, all alone, a sentinel in watch over Halifax Harbor. It was flanked on the left by the watchman\u2019s shanty. The watchman might be asleep. He probably was asleep. The watchman didn\u2019t mean much, anyhow.\nA few feet away to the right was a temporary wooden shed. In the shed were two sleeping dock laborers, friends of the watchman. Almost certainly, they were asleep. They didn\u2019t mean much, anyhow.\nNobody, unless he were superhuman, could have seen the Marblehead, with its candlepower- ing engines running at 12,000 r.p.m. in the basin. The colossal whine of the engines would have burned the eyeballs of an unblessed, uninvited observer. The whine was that high.\nThe Marblehead was ready for sea, the engines were warmed. The cunning system of multiple breakers, operating switches and gates had omitted for the nonce the interfacing with the dockside hose outlets, condensors and electrical system.", "0_14_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The month was August, according to the Gregorian calendar.\nNot many minutes hence, according to the same calendar, it would be August 1, 2174\u2014the first day of a new century.\nIt would be a century in which many things might be expected to remain the same\u2014without changing.\nThe gentry of Newport, Rhode Island, would still enjoy the beaches, the gambling and the girls while the sun shone.\nWhen the sun went down, they would stroll off to their clubs and gossip over drinks and cigars. Then they would go home to their Newport mansions and dream of their yacht races and polo matches, while the hired help slept in attics and cellars, ate scraps, wore discarded, threadbare clothing, and bore their lot without complaint.\nThere were members of the gentry, however, who were not content to remain standing still while the centuries swept past.\nAnd among these dissatisfied was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California, and of Newport, Rhode Island.\n$SURNAME1 was fifty\u2014one years old. He had made and lost three fortunes, and he had been an ignorant, penniless tramp before he was twenty. There was nothing he didn\u2019t know about making and losing fortunes, about...", "0_21_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-six in the evening</span>, and Sunday. The clock in the mechanical bull ground on.\nThe clock in $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s special chronometer inside his space suit ground on. It was a fabulous, gold-plated, jewelled watch, a pocket-size version, in $SURNAME0\u2019s hind pocket. On the dial of the watch was a flag, a moon, a Earth, and the motto SECRETS ARE MANY, CIPHER SAFE WAS NONE. The watch was counting down. When it reached zero, $SURNAME0 and his machine would expire.\n$SURNAME0, as he faced the crowd on the $FIRSTNAMEM2 Building\u2019s landing pad, faced a new crowd as well. The crowd behind him was as candid, as close, as indignant as the crowd in front. Again, it was composed largely of modest women with packages and angry men with briefcases and parcels.\nThe occupants of all the elevators in the building had joined the crowds on the landings, as had the telephone subscribers of both the upstairs and downstairs exchange booths.\nThe shopgirls of Venus, Domino, and Miss Moon Ruice Shop and Modeling Agency, on the floor below, had assembled on the shop\u2019...", "1_12_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, after all of his chrono, was still panting. He was all of a pain, a misery, a fatigue, despite the fact that they had been playing the game only ten minutes.\n\"The best part of Spolette didn\u2019t get itself said in the best part of ten minutes,\" he panted.\nThe twelve-year-old Henry Forge agreed.\n\"Ten more minutes, and then we rest for one,\" he said.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 groaned.\n\"We both said the best thing we could think of,\" said Henry. \"I said the home run Dad hit off Lop Lindergren of the Norwich Navigators, and you said Donna Maligatti\u2019s purple underpants.\"\n\"To say that much in ten minutes flat is not bad,\" admitted $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"I feel I could play ten more minutes,\" admitted Henry.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 moaned. \"The trouble with the best part of Spolette isn\u2019t the best part\u2014it\u2019s the part that\u2019s next.\"\n\"Right,\" said Henry. \"I got your number. Will you still be my best friend, if I promise we\u2019ll only play the best part...", "0_23_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. Five and one half hours more of Earthly time had to be endured before the terror of the golden jellyfish would begin to be over.\nAlso, reputations were still being tarred and ruined.\nMajor General R. M. Sleemy, for instance, was enjoying the full fury of the mass-media vultures. It had been revealed by witnesses in the field hospital that Sleemy had fled in terror from the golden jellyfish, that Sleemy had run a whole company of unarmed recruits straight into the lethal embrace of the creatures.\nSleemy was so deeply ashamed that just being away from Mars meant a lot to him. Rumor had it that he did not intend to re-enlist when his hitch expired.\nIt was rumored that a board of inquiry would be held, that the board would want to question $SURNAME1 in connection with the events that had befallen the space ship on which he had arrived.\nThis rumor was false. Nobody ever had the temerity to question $SURNAME1 or his son. Perhaps the rumors had been started by either or both of them. There is always such malicious whispering in the small caves containing Army barracks.\nNo more battles were fought with the remaining gold helmets. No more recruits died. It was...", "1_18_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-one in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, having decided to come back to earth, orbited around the earth once more. He discovered his mother, naturally, and then did something that seemed quite strange for a young angel. He took possession of a thirty-three-year-old man named $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California, who had just attempted suicide. He took possession of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s body, dispelling all pain, filling all deficiencies, and bringing, at once, a feeling of grandeur and and unlosable place in the universe.\n$SURNAME1 collapsed in a deck chair. What he felt was akin, he imagined, to what old John D. Rockefeller or Andrew Carnegie might feel on suddenly making, miraculously and out of a clear sky, twenty or thirty billion dollars.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 felt equal to the challenge of anything in the galaxy. He was a billionaire in regard to galaxies\u2014a man who could take on awhole cluster, if that was required.\nThere was a soft knocking at the door. $FIRSTNAMEB2 filtered the knocking harmoniously through $SURNAME1\u2019s ears. In a natural bass, without haste, without alarm, without surprise, $SURNAME1 spoke the words, \"Come in.\"\nIn came a lovely and charming young woman.", "0_14_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Wildbulls and Cue had come down-town from their mid-borough caves to buy groceries and carry them home to cook supper. Monkey and Claude were asleep in houses downtown. MalcolmBackground came down from a lonely walk on the beach. The same day had been going on for years for everybody, and nobody knew when it would be night. Ernie Winterghost came back from a sniffing spree down on the lower deck. Saturday Evening and The Very Reverend Doctor Vincent Hanna-Frost, S.J., came up from the library, Saturday Evening carrying a whole new batch of index cards. Behind Saturday Evening's back, The Very Reverend Doctor Vincent Hanna-Frost snuggled with a fine aerodynamic wink of his eyes at $FIRSTNAMEB3. Saturday Evening followed $FIRSTNAMEB3 and the professor into Ohi's office to chomp pantlegs; the holy Hanna-Frost gained the architectural heights of the toilet. Tim Gray playfully anointed Gargantua's hindquarters with an unbroken egg. Pudd Mason was back at his business of cleaning spittoons. Roland Major, still in his 8:45 A\u2019 m suit, was in the corner talking with the afternoon G-men. Old Ray Rumsey was saying, \u201cHe's asleep,...", "0_16_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Skip and the other two were paying their bill at the desk. I had my bag in my hand, Tom had his overcoat on, although it was warm. The plane was leaving at six o'clock. It was a Lockheed Electra, the crack plane of Pan Am; a one-engined plane with seats running along either side of the cabin, a regular jet-prop job, the fastest plane extant at that time. We told the stewardess we had an engagement in New York, promised her a hundred dollars if the plane landed there on time. She promised she'd try.\nThis has always been my basic attitude toward women everywhere, la haute humanit\u00e9, Miss Althea for example. She looked like she was sorry we were going; her delicate nose was freshly runny.\nHow Terry's Teeth Got Straightened\n\u201cWhoo!\u201d said Tom, when we were airborne. He slid down in his seat, holding on tight. He was on my right. We were cruising at twenty thousand feet. You could see San Francisco like a beautiful model, all its bays, bridges, hills, and fine straight streets like in a Gestetner exhibition; also the industrial smog of Oakland and...", "1_3_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-five in the morning</span>. The desert wind had stopped blowing. I sat up straight and listened to the leaping silence and the red low dawn rippling the cloud edges. It was a new day starting. A new day for Old Bull Lee. I blew a kiss toward his railless shack, and fifty feet of sand between us. Then I started the car and went back to the Strip.\nCHAPTER SEVEN\nIt is a great advantage not to drink among hard-drinking fellows who carry their liquor well. It dulls the terrible friction of wit.\n\u2014H. M. TOMLINSON, TROLLOPE: A MEMOIR\nGoing back I took a turn off the highway and stopped by the door of a small adobe shack. It was a religious shrine of some kind, with a crude wooden Virgin across the door and a crayon portrait of Jesus stuck to the wall with tacks. Old Bull Lee had told me about this when we passed it the night before. \u201cIf you're out that way, you might as well stop off and pay your respects.\u201d I was paying my respects. I got out of the car and walked over to the Jesus portrait, knelt and bowed my head, like they do in Catholic countries.", "0_0_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-five ante meridiem</span>, some thirty-six hours and five minutes after his conversation with $FIRSTNAMEB6, when he saw the sign that he was looking for, two sets of crude hieroglyphs spray-painted in vivid orange on a stretch of concrete wall eighteen feet high and thirty feet long, the wall being perhaps four feet from the curb of a wide street. Attikol\u2019s limousine was cruising fast, the driver alert for signs, red nuances all about\u2014the moist, heavily perfumed bleat, of a police bullhorn, the all but subliminal, slow-motion rush at their sides of a silent, sudden blue and white police ambulance, a massive, uniformed statue at the wheel.\nThe driver braked for the sign in late-afternoon traffic, in time to avoid a rampant blue-white dog that dashed like a stone from a lawn, smack into the gutter before them. The limousine wobbled in the lane, wallowing back toward an old wino huddled near a hydrant upcurbs behind the sign. Unmindful, hope in his woe, he was pulling back and forth on a dirty banner strung between his toes, trying to stand erect with one hand and support with the other claw a lit...", "1_1_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-six in the morning</span>, and Stan, that great big beautiful laughing boy from the laundry, had come down the empty street to shake and moan in the entry of the Diggers' apartment, wake everybody up. He'd spent most of the night in the bus on his way across the bay from SF to Oakland, he'd been in his seat when the bus left and at San Jose, when the bus driver had to stop because Stan was seeing snakes, reptiles all over his body. He had no money and was coming to the Diggers for help. His feet were swollen and it turned out he'd cut through the Mechanics' Institute on his way, Tom and Bruce watching over their books; leered at Lucy in her bath; cracked up poor Roy who fell off his chair laughing at Stan's hallucinations. Stan sat rumpled and exhausted on the sofa; Tim lent him a sleeping bag. Sparsely, without sleep themselves, Tim and Bruce beat and sawed together, riming in the foyer, till they collapsed at dawn; a poem they never wrote down. Every day that summer something similar happened. Further and further into madness we rode on a bus powered by faith. Nothing else could get us there. It had only a fifty-cent fare: your faith -- in God and...", "0_12_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past noon</span> according to $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s antique mechanical alarm clock. Probst did his exercises in the bathroom. $SURNAME1 closed his eyes, shut his nostrils to the faint wiff of chemicals, sweat, and sunlight from the opened window, shut his ears to the footsteps overhead.\n$SURNAME1 was forty-four years old, four feet eleven, fifty-two pounds according to Dr. Scharff\u2019s scales. He lay quietly, every muscle oiled by Martian gymnastics, every nerve enmeshed in a pungent web of bemusement and good will.\nBeing bemused and good-willed was an essential part of one\u2019s sobriety. Being bemused made the re-entry into everyday life slower and safer, being good-willed made it more fun and less painful.\nThe best part of sobriety was the pleasures of being shocked at nothing much. To be shocked at nothing much was to be shocked at everything.\nIt was an error to assume, as many people did, that the joys of being shocked at nothing much were reserved for alcoholics, dope fiends, and other denizens of the world of kicks. Sobriety was a state of mind that had to be...", "2_14_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. \"By golly, Skip,\" said Hugh, \"I think they might take a couple of minutes off, if you want to be nice about it.\"\n\"Yeah,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. \"I think they could give you two minutes all right.\"\n\"Want me to call \u2019em?\" said Hugh.\n\"Please,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. His lovely contralto voice was gruff and gritty now. \"Please call them.\"\nIt was highly unlikely that the members of the Martian war fleet would be at all confused by an invitation to take two minutes before blasting Earthlings into cosmic anything. The invitation had been sent by powerful electronic pulse, and the pulse arrived punctually, to the second.\nBut it was punctually ignored.\n\"Oh, well, skip\u2014\" said Hugh, turning from the console. \"What the hell, skip\u2014\"\n\"Hold it, son,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. His voice was choked and hot. He was thinking a vicious and tragic thought: that he could never again force $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 to make love, because they had already made love, because they were forever and irrevocably in love.", "2_18_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nThe time was three minutes until seven o\u2019clock in the evening. Earthling Time.\nThe time\u2014\nIt was one minute until seven o\u2019clock in the evening. Earthling Time.\nThe time was now\u2014\nBelle, the switch-board operator, pressed the big, round, yellow button under her left thigh, and the siren in the courthouse bell tower whooped thrice. A recorded announcer came onto the cable at that point, and he was amplified by every loudspeaker in town.\nThe announcer was polite, since he was speaking over loudspeakers in public buildings.\n\"This is a test,\" he said. And then he said, \"This is only a test,\" and then he said, \"That is all, folks.\"\nAnd that was all.\nAgain, the drumroll.\nAgain, the far horizon turned slow red.\nAgain, the sun backed up, looking fiercer and fiercer.\nAgain, the townspeople raced for shelter. For a moment or two, the houses shook, rattled, and droned in the turbulence of the Martian landing.\nWhoosh\u2014ka-bang-spang!", "0_1_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt exactly two o'clock, broadcasting time was resumed.\nOld $FIRSTNAMEB6 was back.\nShe spoke with a twang that was painful to any viewer who happened to be a non\u2014Californian.\n\"Well, you heard what the nice man said. My grandniece, pretty as you please, is going to be on television.\"\nShe giggled.\n\"Oooo, ain\u2019t that something? But how you going to do that?\" she said, twanging away. \"Ain\u2019t no television in jail\u2014at least not where my grandniece is\u2014and even if it was, you going to make somebody wherever it is take my grandniece\u2019s picture and send it out to California and then put it on television?\" She twanged a laugh that would have killed a mockingbird. \"Lord, I ain\u2019t no fool, you know. I have been married and widowed eleven times, and I ain\u2019t an educated woman, but by golly, I know something about what happens in this world. And you don\u2019t just go sending pictures of people around for other people to just put on television when you want them to. I have been married and...", "1_1_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-one in the morning</span>, and my attorney stomped on the gas and we made about fifty-five miles in fifteen minutes. The highpockets\u2019 home was in a typical suburban section of this typical desert burg: cheaper subdivision on the north fringe of town, near the cactus\u2014clouded foothills where the drywall subdivisions give way to saguaro and jackass rabbit country. We spotted the house at around 1:45 a.m. and rolled to a dusty stop right behind the carport where this highpockets character apparently lived. Time was important now because he might decide to go to work after all, and then my attorney would have to hunt this bastard down when he got there.\nSuddenly my attorney was out of the car and running. He dashed up the driveway, past a neighbor\u2019s house, and by the time 1 reached the porch he was already furtively inspecting the windows of this man\u2019s house. \u201cHe\u2019s not here!\u201d he whispered. \u201cCome on!\u201d In the front yard he straightened up and looked quickly left and right before running for the back door. He stopped just long enough to fish a screwdriver out of his Las Vegas\u2014styled kit bag.", "0_5_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-six in the morning</span>. Strange had begun his first day of house arrest at a point of nervous exhaustion. He had spent the night waiting for Bo to show up; and, when Bo had not appeared, waiting for Fielding to find him; for Fielding to do something unkind. In the end, at just about one in the morning, he had wandered out along the path to the $SURNAME0 ancestors. He had lain on the mosses beside their stone thermoses, and he had listened to the Earth explain things to itself through the thin, thin ionosphere.\nIt had been a lovely thing\u2014that night concert of the Earth. High frequencies had been messages from the higher self of the Earth to the higher self of mankind\u2014messages friendly, maplike, and tremendously useful. But the river of middle frequencies had been a torrential argument between the Earth\u2019s middle self and the middle selves of mankind.\nIt didn\u2019t do a bit of good for mankind to hear the middle of the Earth complaining about the middle selves of mankind, because there wasn\u2019t a thing the middle of mankind could do about it.\nThe argument was the more interesting for being utterly one-sided. It would have been an ideal source of free entertainment for...", "2_5_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-six in the morning</span>. We were already half an hour behind schedule before we even left the goddamn runway. No sweat, I thought. We\u2019ll over\u2014run this flight, then take the next one out...no problem. As long as the rental company doesn't charge us extra mileage on that goddamn tankful of shark piss.\nNow we were only a block or two from the airport terminal, but I couldn\u2019t go any faster\u2014that abominable Shark was barely creeping along at 5mph. All the good airport traffic had long since zoomed by in a blur of screaming Mammal engines. Jet pilots make fun of California cars. What the hell would those smug bastards say now, if they could see a Great Red Shark\u2014fully loaded\u2014slowly creeping along at walking speed in front of the airport?\n\u201cHere\u2019s your trip,\u201d the cabdriver said.\nI gave him a $10 bill. \u201cKeep the change,\u201d I said. \u201cI want to fill this trunk with gas.\" He stared at me. \u201cYou want to do what?\"", "2_2_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span> when an auxiliary power unit in the basement of the crumbling little mansion on the hill in Grouchsdorf, New York, kicked automatically into life, and Beethoven\u2019s Ninth Symphony flooded the stone corridors with music.\nFor ten minutes the music thundered and the old place throbbed in time to it. Then\u2014just as the music and throbbing both paused at the high point of a great crescendo\u2014there came a chime, musical and clear.\nInstantly\u2014as though the house had been waiting for the chime\u2014all the music was shut off. The maintenance and repair units, which had been humming merrily along, all stopped. For a moment there was silence, broken only by the whimperings of a total of seventy-one patients sleeping off their therapeutic nightmares upstairs.\nSomeone was coming down the corridor, coming toward the reception hall.\nA panel in the wall slid open, and there appeared in the opening a painted panel of Roman-mannered busts in various head-gear, from a Roman helm to an Elizabethan ruff.", "0_7_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>, and the two-hundred-and-sixty-first day of $SURNAME1's space flight. It was four days after the first notation on $SURNAME0\u2019s proclamation.\nIt was wartime. There had been no attacks on Earth by ships of the orbital fleet, no carpet bombings, no mass infiltrations. The only victim of the war so far had been $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\n$SURNAME1 was not woozy, his head no longer swam. No longer did a million coloured searchlights crisscross his retina, no longer did his mind seem to be composed of creamy caramel.\nWhat had done this to $SURNAME1 was a full stomach, a borrowed bathrobe, slippers, a syringe full of vitamin B1, a syringe full of ergonovine, and the prospect of a day\u2019s sleep in a room in one of the most luxurious private homes in Newport, Rhode Island.\nAnd what had led $SURNAME1 to such an uncharacteristic mode of life was his belated discovery, through reading $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s book, that $SURNAME0 was not exploiting him after all. That the game had been an honest game all along.\nIt was just that $SURNAME0 had thought in terms...", "2_20_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 looked at the counterman. \"Is that all you\u2019re going to ask me?\" she said.\n\"Only things most people know anything about. Excuse me.\"\n\"Where are you going?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe bartender went down the bar to serve a woman who had just come in. It was seven minutes to nine o\u2019clock in the evening.\n\"Come on. What are you drinking?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Pernod.\"\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 materialized aboard the Titanic, Materialization was the word Mrs. $SURNAME0 always used, though the terms dematerialization and rematerialization were more precise. She used the term materialization in derision of the popular concept of the afterlife as a materialistic hell in which the newly dead stewed in their own malice, seeking their old material goods, but finding them etherealized.\nOne of Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s favorite jokes had to do with the difference between materialization and dematerialization. \"Turn your hat around, and see if people recognize you,\" she would say.", "0_10_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten seventeen in the morning</span>. One of our largest hotels, where everything is large, was on fire. A second alarm was called on the way to the fire. While we were hustling off our canvaschairs and getting ready to pull out, bedsprings began popping throughout the hotel. The bedspings were broken by the fall of the ceilings on the upper floors. While we were bumping over the trolley rails and the fire alarm was still jangling, the flat roof of the hotel caved in, reducing the fire to a smoky mess that sent up column after column of black smoke. Everything fell down. The walls caved in and the roof caved in and the floors caved in, and the guests caved in and the elevators caved in and the chandeliers caved in and the desk clerks caved in. By the time we gong-ed our three longs and got there the hotel was a big hole in the ground with smoke and fire spouting up out of it like an oil derrick. Men were already on their hands and knees, digging out the bodies. Firemen have shovels and greatcoats with \u201cGOVERNMENT\u201d printed on them in big yellow letters. In the office buildings all around, office...", "0_23_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-seven in the evening</span> onOctober 1,1993, and the pretty blond woman in the cigarette ad was singing, as she\u2019d been singing for five nights a week, in a theater built only forty-three years ago to be a kind of megachurch of high Mass consumption. She sang to an audience of five hundred, maybe a thousand people, an irregular number, sitting very close together in the expanses of the five thousand, eight hundred seats of the theater, less than a third of those seats full. The thinning crowds were like an affliction the whole nation was coming down with, like a national case of the sniffles. Now and then over the loudspeakers came the pneumatic hiss of a long-distance number being dialed, but the hiss only meant that somewhere in the great theater another phone was ringing. No one went to the bathroom anymore; for almost two years there had been no ons or offs anywhere in the great theater. The pretty blond woman\u2019s song ended, and then she spoke for a minute about the insurance industry, about mortality rates among adult males, about premium quotes, about deductibles, about estate planning. The rest of the thin audience waited. Drums tumbled clumsily into tinny, swing...", "2_5_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\u201cWe can break our fasts when we get back home,\u201d she said. \u201cI can whip up some bacon and eggs, if we have any bacon. And pour some grape juice into the muskifruit.\" She paused. \u201cSpeaking of grape juice, what about all this purple stuff foaming over the sides of the tub?\u201d I couldn\u2019t answer her. My jaws were clenched. I was afraid I would fall off the ladder. My backbone was wrapped tight as a stick. \u201cThat\u2019s grape juice, all right,\u201d she said to herself. \u201cI\u2019d know that color anywhere. You bastard,\u201d she screamed at me.. \u201cLook what you\u2019ve done now!\u201d\nI struggled for calm. \u201cIt\u2019s just grape juice,\u201d I croaked, forcing my eyelids up. \u201cThe bastards!\u201d she screamed, stamping her feet on the rug. \u201cYou and your goddamn grape juice!\"", "1_22_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span> of a day in early August of the year 1947. The place was the living room of a one-story house of frame and stucco construction in a poor neighborhood of Lima, Ohio. On the mantelpiece a Chadwick radio was making labored efforts to get the hour and the weather report from the NBC network, New York\u2014affecting through its struggles an imitation of a fat man blowing bubbles through a bent straw. At the moment the fat man appeared to be all bubble. He blew and blew and got nothing but air. The effort of the poor radio became embarrassing. Something looked as if it ought really to happen. It did. There was a report like a gunshot, and the bubbles were punctured, and the radio remained silent. There was nothing to do but try to get NBC again. The fat man went to work. Being defeated two new times in rapid succession was something the radio couldn\u2019t handle. It fell, crashed, burned silent.\n\"Hell with the radio!\" said Camille $SURNAME1.\nHer husband, $FIRSTNAMEB0, put a record on the living-room phonograph. He tried to retie the tennis racket strings of his daughter, $FIRSTNAMEF1: she had teased them all day long.\nThere was a knock...", "0_8_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Outside the ship, still falling toward the moon, was the city of San Francisco.\nIt was invisible, but the mass determinants of the town were large enough to keep the city falling with the ship.\nUntil the last possible moment when cancellation would be feasible without danger, the city was falling man and nature\u2014 \"riding a beam with the Good Lord,\" as $FIRSTNAMEB1 had once said\u2014toward the moon.\nThen, as the ship neared its zero hour and as the last maneuvers must be made in the controls space:\nThe pilot flipped switches that disconnected the city from the falling ship. The city fell on its own under lunar gravity. The spin that had carried the city from California to Florida, from Florida to the moon, was now carrying the city, in the hands of gravity, across the lifeless plain that constituted Schicklgruber.\nThe city of San Francisco remained in the same place for ten minutes.\nThen the ship, straight and level indeed, was heading for a touchdown on the Tralfamadorian canvas in the painting What's Funny.\nThe pilot trimmed the ship with a few flicks of his thumb and made like a baby drifting off to sleep.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, nestled in...", "1_11_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-four in the morning</span> of October 1, 1994, and during the next fifteen minutes, until eleven forty-four, the best, the most exciting and romantic thing ever to happen to either of us was going to happen to us. And we didn\u2019t even know it.\nWe were working at our respective jobs, me at Stan, $FIRSTNAMEB1 and Boycott\u2019s, palming the keys out of new car keys and Home Owner\u2019s Golden Key Club membership kits, and Lang at Radioactive Arms, washing yen-yen blood out of the antenna nets on SKFX with a special detergent and a steel-bristled brush. The arms would later be wrapped in sterile gauze, bandaged and sent to soldiers in Guam and Puerto Rico. At eleven thirty-four, then, and for only the fourth time in our brief acquaintance, the romantic forces that would crucially affect the rest of our lives were at work without our knowledge, on us. Therefore, truly, and for us, it was absolutely for the first time, at eleven thirty-four in the morning of October 1, 1994.\nOur ignorance during this quarter-hour of bliss is attributable to two things: to the new kind of military post-office address system Limp introduced into...", "2_6_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-one in the morning</span>.\nAt six twenty-four, when everything was done, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew were as close to enemies as either had ever been with any other man.\nThe three beautiful, deadly young men marched to within a few yards of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew, stopped, faced about, faced $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew.\nTheir backs formed a straight, tight fence around $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew.\nTheir blue pants bulged over black pistols.\nOne of them, the stockiest and tallest, said, \"Who the hell are you?\"\nBig Jew answered, \"Me? Who the hell am I? Who the hell are you?\"\nThe tallest young man ignored that. He pointed at $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Who the hell\u2019s he?\"\nBig Jew considered $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Who the hell are you?\" he sneered.\n\"I\u2019m me!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, smiling shakily.\n\"Sure you are,\" sneered Big Jew, \"and I\u2019m Napoleon Bonaparte.\"\nThe stocky young man tapped the barrel of his automatic under Big Jew\u2019s nose, daring the older man to take a step back.", "0_1_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-five in the morning</span>. I had just turned off my tile radio in disgust. The news had been too much. First Sign painter Smotegaar arrested for issuing bogus signatures on oil take-over note for milliontwo in cell with him. Arf! boomed the radio, and the universe roared with laughter. I bent to peer down the hall. Alice had just come home from Dolly Hare's birthday party. \u201cHow's the little birthday girl?\u201d I called.\n\u201cShe's wonderful!\u201d said Alice.\n\u201cIs she a little stinker?\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3, down in himself somewhere. \u201cHere, hand me that goddamn bottle.\u201d I heard the frantic clatter of his bootd as he struggled and kicked at the confining overalls.\n\u201cAre these all tickets?\u201d called Alice. \u201cAnd court summonses? Where are you going? What on earth?\"\n\u201cHush,\u201d I said, \u201cI'm trying to decipher the instructions $FIRSTNAMEB3 is groaning in there -- no, it's all right, he's eating.\u201d Listening to $FIRSTNAMEB3 made me realize how my own stomach was growling; I hadn't eaten since breakfast. \u201cI...", "0_11_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, and I was standing in a hotel on the Left Bank in Paris with a hundred dollars in my pocket. At the end of the street was the Ritz, where rooms went for fifty dollars a night. I had been standing there for half an hour, looking for people I knew and thinking about my last martini. First I had to get rid of the hundred dollars. No cabs wait in front of the Ritz, so there was no sense in going back there to get one. I knew no one in Paris and I had no address. I took out my money and counted it. There were fifty one-hundred-franc notes, rolled up. They made a bulky roll about as big as my putter and just about as heavy.\nI tried to think of someone who would lend me a thousand francs without security. There was no one. I rolled the money up in a ball and was just going to throw it into the Seine when I saw a man walking towards me on the pavement. He was walking along with his hands in his pockthese hands held a rolled-up paper: it was the Herald-Tribune. I smiled and stepped towards him. He smiled at me and stepped towards me. Our smiles were like...", "0_12_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, at the beginning of October, and the rain was still falling outside the open windows of the white bedroom in the Madrid hotel. In the bedroom were several people: a man and a woman, a man in the bed, and a nurse sitting by a table. Outside and directly below the open windows the rain beat the stone of the street. First the rain had come in big drops, then in a drizzle, it was settling down to a steady, heavy rain.\nThe man who was in bed was very tall. He was lying against a mountain of pillows. He had on tan-colored riding breeches; they were pulled up to show a bandage and a compress where his right leg joined his body. There was a cover over his feet. His face was brown and healthy looking, with a heavy beard and mustache, and he was very good-looking.\nHe was the Destry Rides Again man, $FIRSTNAMEB5 Ashley.\nThe woman, who was with him in the bed, was good-looking and snappy looking. She had a sharp, chiseled, good-looking face in spite of a skin that was too heavily made up. She was wearing a silk kimono and she had slippers on. Her legs were long...", "2_20_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA gold-toothed, brown-skinned native in a yachting cap handed $FIRSTNAMEF1 a pink telephone slip.\n\"Pink?\" she said. \"All pink calls for me are supposed to be put through immediately.\"\n\"Orders is orders, ma\u2019am,\" said the native.\n\"How did you come by this order?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"By telephone, ma\u2019am,\" said the native.\n\"You mean somebody telephoned you in person?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I got them orders right here in my hand, ma\u2019am,\" said the native.\n\"That\u2019s hardly legal,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, studying the message slip.\n\"My orders is to stand around out front till you calls, ma\u2019am,\" said the native. \"I done been standin\u2019 for two hours, now.\"\n\"I\u2019ll call in ten minutes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Ten minutes?\" said the native. \"Ain\u2019t that a little long, ma\u2019am?\"", "0_10_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-five in the morning</span> of November thirty-first.\nNerve-rackingly perfect reproduction of a thousand places and times, surely.\nIt is ten twenty-five in the morning of\nNovember thirty-first. You are ten years old, many thousands of miles from home, surrounded by strange faces.\nI tried to talk with some of the people at the party.\nA group of young men were talking about a team called the Pittsburgh Steelers. They told me about the fabulous feats of one Johnny Blood and one Lyle Blamm. I nodded in all the right places.\nA beautiful woman with a great fig tree for a leg introduced herself as Mrs. Roy Ellis.\nI told her it was a wonderful party.\n\u201cYes,\u201d she said, \u201cthe future looks good for us, we really seem to be carving out a niche for ourselves on the fringes of society\u2014blowing in like hardish winds, you know, then just messing around for a while.\u201d She pressed my arm to show the softness of her character. \u201cDo you understand that?\u201d\n\u201c... a niche ... blowing in ...\u201d\nI don\u2019t have a watch because I can\u2019t stand to carry one.", "1_9_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-six in the morning</span>.\nI headed for home, cursing God, my parents, my ancestors, myself, my species, and the universe in general. Why did I have to be awake then? Why did I have to be conscious at this lamentable point in history? What is wrong with me that I am part of a stinking, groaning, rotten, feverish organism called American society? Why do my thoughts have to behampered with anxiety, doubt, fear, malaise, self-hate,instantdeath dread, goddamn yellow streaks, and authoritarian pangs just because Nixon is President? Who voted this s.o.b. in? I didn\u2019t vote for him. Nobody I know voted for him. Who actually voted for this cheap cheat? What kind of a citizen doesn\u2019t register disapproval in a constitutional way at a time like this?\nMy God, I thought, what kind of world is this? He\u2019s in the White House and we\u2019re fucking communards. The Diners Club is our symbol of aristocracy. Credit\u2014why not make it easy for people to pay? Yes, Maude, there is a Hell, all right\u2014it\u2019s raw.\nInstantly I was jolted...", "1_18_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe two policemen now boarded the ship. One of them began to question $FIRSTNAMEF0-Joanna.\nHis name was Miles, and he questioned $FIRSTNAMEF0-Joanna in the high-pitched nasal whine that so often goes with homosexuality and with unhealthiness.\n\"And what are you doing on this fine evening, Mrs. Thang?\" Miles asked.\n\"I am enjoying the weather,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0-Joanna. \"It\u2019s so lovely to be alive and to be in a really beautiful place like Newport Bay, instead of in the trenches in Flanders, where I have to imagine I am for three days out of four.\"\n\"How quaint and archaic,\" said Sergeant Miles. \"You had me there, quite.\"\n\"I don\u2019t mean to be quaint,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0-Joanna. \"I believe being gay is quite the custom nowadays.\"\nFor these answers, see Glossary.\n\"Where is your lovely party?\" asked Sergeant Miles.\n\"There,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0-Joanna. \"On the lawn.\" She pointed with a gesture that also threw off a good deal of perfume.\n\"We\u2019ll join it, if you have no objections,\" said Sergeant Miles.", "2_16_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. She had one minute to get her train. Arriving at her station, she would have time to board it, in time to be carried at an amazing, almost impossible speed into the dark industrial tangles of the bay area, where, as the pressure of everyday civilization closed behind her, clusters and swirling clusters of public energy would come on like singular stars amid the scattered denizens of the night.\nSo quick and automatic was the taxi ride over, so immediate the rush of pedestrians and shimmer of light on pavements that $FIRSTNAMEF2 didn\u2019t begin to remember how she came to be here until stopped at the ticket window in front of the station. \u201cRound trip, one way, or quid pro quo?\u201d asked the uniformed agent.\n\u201cQuid pro what?\u201d she said, certain beyond all reason she should know what he was talking about.\n\u201cMadam, you\u2019re not from around here.\u201d\n\u201cOh,\u201d she said, \u201cyeah, Montevideo.\u201d Then, pointedly, \u201cGive me a return, OK?\u201d", "1_8_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Already a warm, yellow sun was slanting upon the town. I could see the houses opposite, with the rippling black lines of people flocking along the sidewalks to work. A big taxi floated by, filled with men in uniform. They were French officers going to the garrison. They had their sabers out and were waving them to the girls. To the left were the trees of the town square, shadowy and green. To the right were the two wharves, one for the soldiers and one for the civilians. A ship was standing out to sea. It was just moving away from the big, fish-shaped frying-pan where the corn-oil was boiling. Far off, across the water, were the white cliffs of the island of Lobos. You could see caves in the cliffs, and the green sheets of water sliding down, and a smooth-bored cannon stuck over the fort. All these things I saw as I stood in the cool, shaded hall, waiting for my trunk to come up.\n\u201cBloo-oo!\u201d\nA long, low whistle, the sound of an engine getting up steam, echoed over the waters of the harbor. A puff of smoke went up from the locomotive of the...", "0_15_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe committee and I lay as if paralyzed.\nWhere was the main body of the Martian air-fleet?\nWhere, for example, was the $SURNAME0, the ship that had accompanied $FIRSTNAMEF1 and Salo so faithfully? Where was the ship that had traditionally carried the Titanian ambassador to Earth?\nWhere, in short, was any but a handful of Martian ships?\nWhere were the six thousand that had been reported by Phoce? All around us, even if $SURNAME0 hadn\u2019t told us, we would have known that something was dreadfully wrong, because the air-fleets from one hundred and sixty-three planets were arriving, and an arriving fleet doesn\u2019t keep quiet about it.\n\"Keep quiet about what?\" said the committee accusingly. \"This is their big day, and you promised us you was going to tell them something pretty sensational about it.\"\nThe committee slapped its thighs and guffawed thunderously at its own jest, and I slapped and guffawed with all my might. A gentleman caller is expected to keep up with all the jests made by his host, whether he understands them or not. \"Yes, something awfully sensational,\" said the gentleman caller unconvincingly...", "2_7_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"I suppose you\u2019d like to sit down,\" said $SURNAME0. He indicated a red velvet swing on the right. A swing on the left was also red velvet. Between the two swings hung a large gold framed oleo of the Tralfamadorians placing a jaunty gold helmet on the head of a hero named $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. The oleo had been painted by $SURNAME1\u2019s wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, before she died.\n$SURNAME0 pushed the left swing gently with his foot. It creaked and groaned sadly. $SURNAME1 took the right swing.\n\"What message\u2014?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"The Martians\u2014or whoever the beings were\u2014say that every ten years,\" said $SURNAME1, \"the two of us\u2014you and I\u2014will meet the beings from another star again, and the beings will take us to another place from which we can see the past, the present, and the future.\"\n\"What message were you supposed to deliver?\" said $SURNAME0. He looked up at the ceiling. The message was written up there. It was written in gold.\nThe message said this:\nDEAR WINT,", "0_18_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six o'clock in the evening</span>. Redwine was not twenty minutes away from an apotheosis, which would have put a golden touch on his day and had him singing as he walked back to his car. But these people, who had purchased paperbacks for him to sign, had gotten in his way. For he was an important man whose time was limited. An important man, with a tight schedule to keep.\nLovely day, wasn\u2019t it? he said as he left the lobby.\nI knew he was in a hurry, so I got right to the point. \"Minister Redwine, this afternoon\u2014such fun-Bontly, Alvin, myself, and a weird woman wearing my wife\u2019s bathing suit. And you spoke to us with such fervor. What was that all about?\"\nHe stopped dead. He was clearly flabbergasted. His car keys, dangling from one elegant hand, gleamed brown in the sodium arc-sodium vapor light of the parking lot. \"I\u2014we\u2014I\u2014\" He never got to \"we.\" For a brilliantly silver, Vincent Black Shadow sped across the lot and screeched to a halt in a gush of fluid so lovely and precious that Redwine instinctively looked for wings of angels to feather...", "2_4_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-three in the morning</span>. If $FIRSTNAMEB2 had not yet arrived, he would arrive any moment. It was time for $FIRSTNAMEF1 to put on her bathing suit. She did not put it on. She lay down on the mattress, pulled a shawl over her.\nIn the beginning, she did not want to think about $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. The very name nauseated her.\nAs her mind cleared, she began to think of how surprisingly attractive the bearded and grimy $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had been\u2014and even more graciously how genuinely interested he had seemed in her welfare. And now the man was going to be her husband.\nShe hated his guts.\nThe doorbell rang.\n\"That must be him,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Shall I go and see, madam?\" said her butler.\n\"I\u2019ll go,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, getting up. She paused. She shivered. \"How funny,\" she said. \"I feel like my feet aren\u2019t touching the floor.\"\n\"The fever, madam?\" said her butler. He scurried to her with a wheelchair, cool linen, a thermos of lemon tea, and the morning newspaper.", "2_2_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past two o'clock in the morning</span> in Finland.\nThe thing had happened.\nThe announcements in the sky did not specify what the exciting thing had been, for two reasons. One reason was that the announcement in the sky was a very, very old announcement, pre-dating the Sputnik. The announcement was as old as the announcement of the Voluntary Geological Program. It had been up in the sky, faithfully updated, throughout the tempest of World War II and during the uneasy truce that followed in Earth\u2019s affairs. The announcement was not a living organism like Nimrod Pennyboy, but merely a dead collection of symbols.\nThe master symbol, which was supposed to have the format of the numerals in the old American publication Life\u2014and which was known colloquially as the copper-colored digit\u2014represented mean temperature for a monthly average for the entire globe. The numerals, which were of mixed height, were subsidiary symbols representing various world capitals and various lesser localities. Thus, if the capital of Uruguay were to drop out of the Earth, the copper-colored digit would no longer read 37.2, but 39.2. And the numeral representing Buenos Aires would rise to fill the gap, making a new and different mean temperature read-out.", "0_3_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-two in the morning</span>. A waxing gibbous moon sparkled on the eastern desert. I took a final toke on the monster joint, then pitched it out the window. \u201cThis is it!\u201d I shouted. \u201cI\u2019ve come to psyche you up for the big scene. You can do it, you\u2019re Rocky Mountain Doug McGrath!\u201d My attorney was hanging on to the steering wheel with both hands. Blood was trickling down over his sport shirt. \u201cYou hear me,\u201d I said. \u201cYou\u2019ve got to be high. You\u2019ve got to be high all the time, like a yogi, like a vibrating eye, like the ghost of Hamlet\u2019s father\u2014one big rush all the time of fine maryjane for all your lungs and all your soul and all your body! Think of Dostoyevsky!\"I shouted. \"We will boogie on the flinty rocks glorying in our misery! We will howl at the electric moon!\" He swerved off the road. We were surrounded by cactus. \u201cWhat\u2019s wrong?\" I said. \u201cDon\u2019t you want to howl at the...", "1_11_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of December twenty-first, 1981.\nI LET THE DOPPELGANGER OUT OF THE CLOSET AT ELEVEN THIRTY IN THE morning, so he could use the bathroom. He didn\u2019t like it in there. 11 was too quiet, he said. He put his hand over his thick madman\u2019s nostrils to breathe, fearing the butyric acid content of the air, and I realized afresh how much he looked like his brother when he did that. He took off his artificial eyes and then I saw that he was crying, the sort of routine male crying which consists of slow, fat tears that issue from the eyes without the slightest twinge from the throat and hang from the lower lids, ready to drop off at any moment.\n\"I really blew it, didn\u2019t I, $FIRSTNAMEB6?\" he said of whatever it was we were talking about; I forget. I patrolled the bedroom, looking through drawers, pretending to be busy. Though I had no reason to hide the fact that I was patting the clothes tossed onto the floor, pretending to look for the passport, because I knew he hadn\u2019t even touched the clothes.\n\"There isn\u2019...", "2_19_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-seven in the evening</span>.\n\"Skip!\" cried the nasal voice. \"$SURNAME0! $SURNAME0!\"\nThe hand cracked like a whip.\nThe voice was Bette Porter\u2019s voice. She was calling $SURNAME0. She was calling him by his title, because she didn\u2019t dare call him by his name. He was preoccupied. He had shown the phenomena of preoccupation before\u2014now that he was Bombazine and had only one prophesied greatness remaining to him.\nThe phenomena of preoccupation, when they manifested themselves, consisted of $SURNAME0\u2019s maintaining a stony silence and a stony facial expression while his eyes darted furiously behind closed lids. The $SURNAME0 body, meanwhile, was manipulated by forces outside its owner\u2019s control. It levitated a few inches, bumped into things, vibrated, emitted throaty sounds not recognizable as any word or phrase in any Earthling language.\nAll this was going on now.\n\"Skip\u2014$SURNAME0\u2014\" said Bette Porter, \"I\u2014I can\u2019t\u2026\"\nHer voice trailed off.", "1_20_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-four in the evening</span> when I fell off the clock. Sugarlump, my ape and minister of interior affairs, had just returned from the San Pedro night life. He was in ripping good humor. He dug me leaning back in my swivel chair, reading about a cave man who lived in a hole in the ground with flints for tools and dragging his women by the hair when he wanted them, and wanted them any time he damn well pleased. He also liked to eat raw meat, in the Biblical sense.\n\"Boss,\" said Sugarlump, \"let us go out in San Pedro to find girls. You are looking in the wrong place, buddy. Let us go downtown.\"\n\"Downtown, you half-wit, what's downtown? You mean inland, across the causeway, across clock twenty? And go where in San Pedro? To Y's Soda Shop? To Pat O'Donnell's? To Clancy's Big Town Casino? Where?\"\n\"All these, yes. But we must go first to a bar. To prepare for the girls, bull sense. Lettered words are no good when it comes to girls; you need sour memento from a fermented mash.\" He had said such things before, but then he added something that I never...", "1_9_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span> when I got to $FIRSTNAMEF4's house; she was closing up the breakfast table. I talked to her briefly and decided to join them for breakfast, hangover and all. So we entered and sat down, and banged away in the kitchen aunt and niece. The hotcakes and coffee revived me, and we became very gay. $FIRSTNAMEF4 liked her coffee black; she said it kept her steady, with no kick in the head, and that she preferred it to alcohol. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I drank orange juice. Baby and aunt were having a final word of the kitchen situation before they left. Everything was smooth and pleasant. I noticed, though, a strange look on $FIRSTNAMEB3's face, as if he were holding a smile that was trying to hide itself. Presently I noticed that $FIRSTNAMEF4, too, was acting strangely, though she was conversing normally. It was increased by the way they both kept looking at me slyly, out of the corners of their eyes. A strange and diabolical idea came to me: they were testing me, thinking that I was the kind who couldn't take it and would have to leave. I ignored it and kept talking and being merry. Meanwhile the clock ticked with horrible speed. In about seven minutes it...", "1_5_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until six o'clock in the morning</span> when I saw there was no more than one hour left before I would be too late for the American Express flat. I put the set back in the brown paper bag and, protecting the tube of treasure from all the soft, damp ways of the Paris streets, scooted home. Old Bull Lee and Eddie were both asleep on their mattresses when I opened the door. They snored away harmoniously. I put the radio on the table and turned down the volume. Then I looked over at the sprawled-out American beauty of $FIRSTNAMEB3 Moriarty, sleeping there so fine and quiet and soft and still, with his white carnation, his red cheek, and his long, lovely eyelashes. I fumbled for the necktie on the bed, tied the endless knot in front of the mirror, put on my hat, took my everything, unlocked the door, and scooted across Seine, up dark Rue Saint Jacques to the ugly, empty American Express office with the sun tipping the Paris roofs with gold. My father was there at his desk. I kissed him. It was my first formal interview in Europe. I told him everything. He rustled papers. \u201cYou're a young scamp, but don't do anything foolish.\u201d He...", "2_5_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-three in the morning</span>. I was a hundred yards off the Whitetail exit of the Great Pennsylvania Desert. Trucks were flying by me in waves. I went back to the phone booth and called Lucy. \u201cHello?\u201d\nA soft, questioning voice. I was struck dumb for a moment; I stuttered, \u201cUh . . . Lucy . . . this . . . ah . . . now listen .. .\u201d Desperately I tried to talk but I couldn't get my mind away from the thought of leaping high into the air, grabbing a passing flying rib of Route 66 and hitchhiking my way back to California by hanging from one huge overhead wheel to the next.\n\u201cYes,\u201d said the voice. It was all very simple. The plan was already in my mind, complete, waiting: HUP, two, three, four, five. Hell, I'd be in LA in two hours by that method. Falling all that time, too. A terrible trip, but a speedy one once you finally got up the nerve to take the plunge and go for a ride on that big wheel.", "0_7_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> when we got started. We were two hours getting to Frisco. It was just a race, a mad race, to arrive before the Examiner's inner-office boys issued their morning bulletin. Winchell never fails to pontificate on his column in the very early hours of the morning. The boys in the Examiner office are always in a sweat to get first crack at him. At this point the notes become very hazy. I can remember arguing with $FIRSTNAMEB3, who was driving, about where the race should be run from, and saying \u201cMake it from here to that coffee-stand ahead on the left, that's our landmark.\u201d Incidentally, with the white line whizzing by my side, I was leaning out the window on the door side of the car and sending back progress reports over my broadcasting system to the queasy boys in the back seat. \u201cFolks, we're falling behind, there's a car ahead of us driven by a blondhaired youth who can't find the switch-pull to his lights.\u201d\n\u201cGo around him and pass.\u201d\n\u201cI can't do that, he's in the passing lane and besides he probably has police decals to his window.\u201d", "1_2_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-nine in the morning</span>, as near as I could tell by my watch. There was nothing to do but go inside to the airport men's room, here in the terrible city of San Francisco. In this awful town everything was so far away. I hurried. No liquor store survived the night. There was only the Avis office, the curved white walls and whorls of the men's room, the echoing clatter of my feet, and the faucet at the end of the row of sinks, which when turned fully on caused the entire system to moan once like a woman and then collapse shuddering into torrents of miserable water. I sought refuge from these lovely noises in a stall. I locked the door. My turn had come in these United States of America: I had made it to San Francisco just as the Beats had prophesied. That wonderful night I was happy to be a fool of God, to hide from the disasters of Time in the curved white rest rooms of the Western World. I took off my shirt and wrapped it around my head, for I wished to sleep with my brain covered, since I had been told that sleep was the twin brother of death and therefore precious. I curled up in a tiny ball under the stainless steel bowls and sinks of the urinal...", "0_2_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a public vestibule in the Fl policies, a building in downtown Cleveland. The numbly waiting Marty, for a change, was reasonably happy. The happy spot was, of course, the ongoing insertion of a Mickey Finn-an illegal substance into the drink of a completely unsuspecting victim-item that could be done quite plausibly in the average cocktail lounge. This time, it turned out, the victim was a roistering, hotshot architecture professor from the University of Illinois. Back home in Champaign to give a guest lecture. And hit by a Mickey Finn, in Cleveland, in a bar called the SlowBurn. And brought, in an ambulance, to the very combo vestibule/lobby/waiting room where the miserable and the strange could wait in comfort until the three o\u2018clock wakeup call. It was a beautiful thing. They had the professor pinned down on the tile floor by his hands and feet with the help of a White Rock prick and his wife. The prick was only doing it to learn karate holds, though, not to hurt anybody. And if anybody noticed the professor\u2019s wallet rifled notoriously right there on the Ping-Pong table, nobody stopped it or said anything about it...", "1_11_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty in the morning</span>. What happened next, $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas dared not bid. What happened was Miles, just walking into another five-minute guitar solo, tossed down his plastic harmonica and announced, \u201cIt is time, brothers and sisters.\u201d\nWith that he burst into a shuffle-ball-change step, hopped in the air, clicked his heels and threw back his head, crying in a horrible corojo\u2019s scream, \u201c\u00a1Seventy-five! \u00a1Seventy-five miles an hour!\u201d\nDoors opened up and down the line of buses. Out of them poured hundreds of little kids. They all wore aluminum foil helmets and walked with a careful stride, hands on their hips, feet chopping the turf in short ankle-snapping strides. As the crew of musicians stared up at them, befuddled, their brisk formation unraveled, kids spilling off the line into scramble drills, falling on their butts, leaping up, running back and forth across the field, screaming, \u201cYessir! \u00a1Yessir!\u201d\nMiles scurried up the aisle, into the bus and out again with a greatcoat, which he put on. \u201cAll...", "1_13_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to two o'clock in the afternoon</span> when we finally got out of Galveston with the two grand in my suitcase. I offered to leave it with the woman who ran the roominghouse, but she wouldn't take it. Not because she was opposed on principle to taking stolen money, but because I looked so dangerous she was afraid I'd kill her. \u201cThat damn boy was his gun,\u201d she told her daughter later. \u201cI don't know what he was pointing it at. I was looking right up at him and if he'd have shot at me I'd have been hit. I know he wasn't shooting at any old fish; he was shooting at people. If he'd have shot once at me and missed me I'd have shot myself -- I'd have been so scared. But I believe he would have hit me. He looked like he was crazy; a big guy like that looking right at you with that gun in his hand.\u201d Well, we were on our way now, Terry sitting in back with Camille and throwing his burning cigarette butts on the back seat without saying a word. The damn guy seemed to be thinking about something. And I began to feel that the success -- or failure -- of the entire trip depended on what went on inside his...", "0_22_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-two in the evening</span>. I knew I had to get down to the Court-house and sign my name before the deadline passed. I might be facing a jail term, and standing in the hallway of a Vegas hospital at ten twenty-two at night did not appeal to me. I hurried back to my room to call the cops and tell them I was ready. My girlfriend was nowhere around\u2014she\u2019d evidently made it out of the room while I was in the hospital. So I packed my bag, grabbed the manuscript out of the closet and scrawled a hasty message on the envelope: \u201cSee you in Cal. Call collect.\u201d Then I headed down to the lobby and flagged a cab. I was sitting in the doctor\u2019s reception area when a cop showed up to spring me. I told the bored people behind the desk who I was, then I flashed my signer\u2019s slip and the cop took me straight to the District Attorney. I stood around for about fifteen minutes, trying to explain what had happened; obviously, the District Attorney had never heard of this Barrett Sauthrup fellow. Then the phone rang and the DA referred to a slip he had on his desk. \u201cLSD?\u201d he said. \u201cCan you...", "1_7_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. And $FIRSTNAMEB2's real adventure, apart from the almost coincidental trick with the telephone, was just beginning. He found himself, at nine minutes past seven, boldly walking up the mossy steps into the open door of the lofty, ramshackle old mansion he had been watching\u2014a mansion topped by a dome and surrounded by a battlemented, vine-surrounded wall.\nThe open door admitted him to a broad, long hall paneled in walnut.\nLike a burglar in a detective story, he tiptoed past long rows of gleaming suits of armor, mounted on pawfooted pedestals, that lined the hall.\nAt the far end of the hall was another open door through which $FIRSTNAMEB2 could see a cool green light. He tiptoed to the green door, through which he saw a spacious but hardly grand office, lined with large, leather-bound books.\nAnd there sat the great magician, with his back to $FIRSTNAMEB2, at a flat mahogany desk of singular design. His bald head, thin neck, and shoulders were hunched over a flat pack of cards. His deft hands performed with the cards the most surprising sleights that even $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2014an aficionado of card tricks\u2014...", "0_3_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-two in the morning</span>. Outside the barracks, the Mexican sentry on post grumbled at the shore patrolman who was making the rounds of the company streets at that unseasonable hour.\nThe shore patrolman came to the captain's window, tapped on the glass, and opened the window when the captain nodded, sleepy-eyed, and said, \"Come in.\"\nThe shore patrolman, who was armed with a twelve-gauge shotgun muzzleshot, said, \"Two men reported missing from quarters. Number fifty-two, sir.\"\n\"Yes?\" said the captain.\n\"Fifer and Wiley, sir,\" said the shore patrolman.\n\"Fifer and Wiley,\" the captain said. \"They missing together?\"\n\"No, sir,\" said the shore patrolman. \"Fifer went out at twenty-two hundred and Wiley went out at twenty minutes to three.\"\n\"They aren\u2019t shooting craps together?\" the captain asked. Wiley was an Indian- that was a nickname.\n\"No, sir,\" said the shore patrolman. He was leaning on the stock of his shotgun and looking around the room rather blatantly. The captain knew what he was looking at. There were two brand-new pint bottles on the desk...", "1_1_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-seven in the morning</span>. We had another seven hours. \u201cMy hair,\u201d I said. \u201cAll that trouble, and my hair didn't\u2014\u201d He nodded. \u201cIt was our only chance. I was counting on that border situation. Another state would have put him back in. But not Nevada. He would have had to spend at least a week in the St. Luke's Free Clinics, waiting for the radiation burns to heal up.\u201d I felt terrible. Totally spent from all those amphetamines. Black cinders were grinding around inside my skull. And the cocaine hangover was far, far worse than the amphetamines....No matter what Cortexin was doing for my perforated ulcer\u2014and it was obviously helping a lot\u2014it couldn\u2019t offset that kind of punishment.\n\u201cShall we forget it?\u201d he asked. I swallowed. How could I answer that? My thoughts were suicidal. I felt cheap and hollow. And scared; he sensed that.\n\u201cDon't worry. It's not too late. All we have to do is go down and get our money back on the car. Tell em we'll pick it up in a week or something.\u201d I nodded.", "0_5_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-one in the morning</span>. At three minutes after four, I found another one of those machines on the front of a restaurant called the Carrousel. I parked the Whale about two feet from the curb in front of the Carrousel and went in to play the machine. It was the kind that gives you jackpots from somewhere in the sky if you feed it enough coins. I put in a dime and pulled the handle. Nine oranges, \u201cB\u201d for bonus. I fed it another dime and got one cherry and two \u201cB\u201d symbols. I went back to the Whale and got in, straightened it out and drove away looking for a hardtop cafe big enough to have automatic machines. I found one on the main street at Vine Street. I parked the Whale in back and went into the cafe and bought a pot of coffee and a pack of butts. I played the easy winner until seven o\u2019clock, then went back and got the Whale and drove it around the corner and parked it behind the hotel. I went in and took a shower and went out to eat. I suddenly felt like eating. I had slept quite a while in the easy-winning. At exactly eight o\u2019clock, I was under the orange trees at the Cafe...", "2_12_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-six p.m.</span> and all was well. At twelve forty-seven p.m., something as casual as the foot of a statue in the crowd touched $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s left big toe.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s temples vibrated like gongs. The tips of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s ears turned blue-white.\nAt twelve forty-eight p.m., the foot of the statue, which was hollow, answered the step of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s big toe by pressing down on the toe\u2019s flesh more firmly yet.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 made a sound that was, in a crowd, unheard.\nAt twelve forty-nine p.m., the foot of the statue tapped $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s toe, promisingly.\nThe touch inspired a faint hope in $FIRSTNAMEB0. It sent a message to his spinal cord. It told his spinal cord to send messages via his gray matter to his brain, that the messages concerned the violet-eyed and honey-brown female who had died in the hospital.\nThe messages said that the violet-eyed and honey-brown female who had died in the hospital was nearby in the crowd. Her name, now, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was given to understand, was Stony Stevenson. Her name was\u2014", "2_8_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nTen of the Martians were in the office half of the lobby. They had on gaudy space-army uniforms, complete with parade-ground spit and polish.\nThe ten Martian officers kept darting glances at the office door, which remained closed. Behind the door, obviously, was the commanding officer whom the nine officers were about to salute as he came out.\nThe scrawniest Martian in the lobby was $FIRSTNAMEB0. He still wore the uniform in which he had traveled from Earth \u2014baggy gray coveralls with a white plastic zipper up the front.\nThe zipper was broken. $FIRSTNAMEB0 hadn\u2019t had the fountain-pen engineering to fix it. His long brown hair poked out of the ragged remnant of the zipper opening.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had big blue eyes in a face that was otherwise very thin. He was five feet ten inches tall, weighed 125 pounds and would have weighed a lot less if he had been thirsty.", "0_11_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to noon</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time. Salo\u2019s face was wry. \"No, I think he\u2019s the kind of man who would understand everything, pretty much,\" he said.\n\"Can\u2019t say as I blame him,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Tell him hello for me when you get back.\"\n$SURNAME0 leaned forward as Salo\u2019s space ship lifted away from the tower. \"Say!\" he said, \"where are you going, anyway?\"\n\"Back where I came from,\" said Salo.\n\"Where\u2019s that?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Your Solar System, originally,\" said Salo. \"Mars, at the moment.\"\n\"Christ\u2019s sake!\" said $SURNAME0. \"What are you doing on Mars?\"\n\"Reporting,\" said Salo testily, \"on the progress of the war. \"What else does one do on Mars?\"\n\"Oh, hell,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Hey! Don\u2019t you know you can\u2019t get to Mars now? There\u2019s a guide post up, the way Yogi Berra says the sign was up in Yankee Stadium the day all the outside seats were sold\u2014...", "0_10_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of Tuesday, October twenty-first, in the year 1985 C.E., according to the clock on the wall in the anteroom of $SURNAME0\u2019s Taj Mahal.\nOver the clock was a reproduction of Marc Chagal\u2019s And When I Awoke, This Was My Land Before Myself. The reproduction, color by hand on puce-colored velvet, had been presented to $SURNAME0 by Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nOver And When I Awoke was the following quotation from Theodore Cordy, PhD, Principal of Newport High, Mars, in the book Nothing but a Man: \"Here, through the misty duality of mind and matter,\" Cordy said, \"one\u2019s eye reaches out and takes in the whole of matters\u2014 one\u2019s self included, and embraces all of space, in all of its parts, and all of its parts in all of its space.\"\nOver the quotation was another that $SURNAME0 had taken from Winthrop\u2019s Journal under Stress. The underlined words were, in the original, capitalized: REMEMBER, WINTHROP\u2014IT\u2019S SPACE THAT DRIVES THE WHEELS.\nJust under the clock...", "2_16_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. In the restroom were three women: a Chicano mother with her female-child offspring, and a middle-aged Genitrix. $FIRSTNAMEF2 and killer were isolated, as in a lidded bucket, from the normal flow of traffic in and out of the rest room.\nThe Chicano mom noticed them first. \u201cYou in trouble, lady?\u201d she called.\n\u201cGo out and call the cops right now,\u201d the killer called back.\nOne of the little kids said, \u201cQuate esta pasando aqui, mama?\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2 didn\u2019t know whether to laugh or cry.\n\u201cWhat are you,\u201d asked the Genitrix, \u201carmed?\u201d\n\u201cGo ahead,\u201d said the killer, \u201ccall the pigs. See what happens.\u201d\nThe Genitrix shrugged. \u201cTry and be a citizen. It doesn\u2019t do much good, but it slows them down some.\u201d", "2_8_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-seven in the morning</span>. On Earth, it was ten o\u2019clock at night.\nHome in Heliosis was a spiral shell one yard in diameter and six feet tall. It was covered with gold leaf, and was the only object in a bare concrete corridor in the Museum of Earthly Antiquities. It was the only known object of its kind.\nThe spiral shell had a Chinese spiral nautilus for a door. The nautilus was inlaid with gold. Any Earthling with the price of a museum ticket could open the door, walk inside, and look down on $SURNAME0\u2019s vacation suit. On the suit were tinkling traces of a whole lot of places he had been.\nA skylight in the spiral shell let in the Sun in $SURNAME0\u2019s home, for all the Sun had to do was strike the solar panel on the roof of the museum in order to power the little habitat\u2019s lights, fans, and heaters. As it was in so many museums in the 1990\u2019s, the museum outside of which $SURNAME0\u2019s modest home was located was having trouble paying its electric bill.", "0_19_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>, according to Randolph's watch, when $FIRSTNAMEM1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0, lugging their bicycles furiously, were beginning to close in on him.\nThey came up with him in a field near the top of a hill. He was sitting on a large stone, resting and smoking a cigarette.\nHe stood up to receive them. The three children looked at each other gloomily. It was clear that none of them felt very elated by the prospect of the ride downhill. It was a long, steep hill, and on the other side of the hill there was another long, steep hill, and then more hills, some short and some long.\n\"You sit in front, $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said Randolph promptly. \"No, I will,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1. \"No, I will,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Randolph,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1, \"don't you think it\u2019s about time you learned to ride a bike?\"\n\"No,\" said Randolph.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 turned to $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Come on,\" he said, \"get on and ride.\"\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"I think it is about time I learned to ride a bike. Is it hard?\"\n\"No,\" said Randolph, \"I\u2019ll show you how.\"", "2_21_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Tuesday the twenty-second of September.\nElsewhere was the starting line of the Greyhound Derby, stretching from the corner of Monroe Street and Superior Avenue to far out toward the lake. According to the literature handed out by the management of the dog track, this was to be \u201can event that will live forever in the annals of Cincinnati gambling lore.\u201d\nSix days before, the prize for which these dogs bobbed and sniffed had been a piddling (in relation to today\u2019s values) sum of three hundred thousand dollars.\nFor four piddling days, betting had been sluggish. On this, the eve of the big event, not even a thousand dollars had changed hands in the six dogpens.\nOn this eventful night, three of the Derby greyhounds bore female names, and one bore the name Spiffy But Real. Two of the dogs were owned by a meat-couponing tycoon named Wellington.\nChief sponsor of the big race was a champagne house of heroic and authentic repute. Its product was limited to two hundred cases a year. The winner\u2019s share of the Derby purse, announced the literature of the House, went to the dog owned by the House.", "0_21_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-four in the evening</span> when the shift finally ended. Bull led her quietly out of the office and downstairs to the employee's door, opened it, let her slip through into the night, let the door close of itself, and never said a word. She was free. No questions, no answers. Those were the rules.\nMORE WIRE\nWINNER, CLEAN MOVER, big red-faced hairy john, watched over them as they ate. \"How\u2019s the food, you guys?\"\n\"Pretty good,\" said Mucho, flinching.\nIn the mirror over the counter Clean, who had picked up a section of the L.A. Times earlier, saw camouflaged faces, spiraling smoke-rings, and words that came and went, a feature on the decor of Driblette\u2019s Heaven. \"How about the decor, then?\"\n\"Has its points,\" said Mucho.\n\"Anything to say about the feature?\" said Clean.\n\"Very informative,\" said Mucho.\n\"You\u2019re dull today,\" said Clean. \"I thought some juicy scandal might have got into this edition.\" Mucho could really give the straight-lines, dry, no follow through.\n\"Give him the feature...", "0_10_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-five in the morning</span>. I had been promised a royal coption of kicks from the big Peruvian gumball . . . andhave been wondering why I felt so sick. As we swung off the highway, Carol said, \u201cThis is hell.\u201d\nThere is only one way to get to the Flamingo. You drive that goddamn toll road till your feet ache, then when you run out of road you take a deep breath, hold it till your brain begins to spaz and your eyeballs explode, then you dive into the cauldron of total velocity known as the Strip...\nIf you keep your eyes closed you have some idea what it's like, but the concept of TOTAL VELOCITY is something you have to experience...because when you hit the end of the road (which actually has no end) you are overwhelmed by the braking action of a left turn and you spin out...stopped in the middle ofdowntownntenon delwane.\nWith your mouth wide open and your brains splattered all over the inside of your skull...You are, at that moment, that helpless final terminal velocity to which every cop in the valley responds. You are red meat with lipstick. You are up against it and wasted in the totality of your...", "2_2_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two sixteen in the morning</span>.\nThe Recon Company had passed its time on the North Shore forgetting to advance for almost an hour and a half.\n\"My God!\" said Stileman. \"What can we do? We can\u2019t get back into the air with this mob in the way\u2014uh-oh.\"\n\"What?\" said Magnum.\n\"They changed the clocks,\" said Stileman. \"Ten more minutes.\"\n\"What?\" said Fenster. \"I thought you said o\u2019clock.\"\n\"What can we do?\" said Magnum. \"We should tell somebody.\"\n\"God damn it,\" said Stileman. \"No. We\u2019ll have to leave him here. We can\u2019t stand him up in front of everybody by himself.\"\n\"He really is drunk,\" said Fenster.\n\"You can tell everybody he told somebody he was going to the can, in ten minutes\u2014come on, ankle limp,\" said Stileman. \"You\u2019ve got to clear out of the way. Try not to stumble\u2014uh-oh.\"\n\"What?\" said Fenster.", "2_15_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three seventeen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was the office of Alvin Mangrim, director of recruitment for the Army.\nThe director was white-haired and overweight. He untied the elastic band from his hair and pushed his white hair back from his round pink ears. At his age, he found any sort of hatband uncomfortable.\nA young man in a red Class A uniform stood before the desk. The uniform was proper, but it was wrinkled and needed pressing. The young man\u2019s red visored hat was in his white-gloved hands.\n\"I just don\u2019t believe this,\" said Alvin Mangrim. \"I just do not believe it at all.\"\n\"Didn\u2019t pass the test?\" said the young man.\n\"Shouldn\u2019t pass a test like that,\" said Mangrim.\n\"Can I ask what happened?\" said the young man.\n\"Oh, now, you know, it\u2019s nothing to be ashamed of,\" said Alvin Mangrim. \"Fellows I\u2019d be proud to hang out with just fall apart on that test.\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said the young man.", "2_3_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-nine in the morning</span>. It would not be long now before the game would be over and the winner announced.\nBy the light of a three-quarter moon and a flood of Earthlight, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 stood in the middle of the iron spar between the cone-towers. They were alone.\nOld age had done for $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s shyness. There was no dignity to be preserved between an eleven-year-old boy and a three-hundred-year-old man. They clung to each other like derelicts who had stumbled upon an alien wave-band on their radios.\n\"Listen to it, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014listen to it!\" exclaimed $FIRSTNAMEB2 blissfully. \"It\u2019s like a hundred thousand angel babies. \"\n\"Angel babies?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"It\u2019s making me crazy to hear it again, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"It\u2019s like hot knives going all through my head and my heart, $FIRSTNAMEB0. I didn\u2019t know I could feel like this, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\" He looked up at the night sky.\n\"I think that\u2019s God up there, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" he said.", "2_15_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-five in the afternoon</span> and Lenore Beadsman was sitting quietly in the chair next to her desk in her work cubicle, waiting for the screen on her word processor to tell her to start talking.", "2_18_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past six o'clock in the evening</span>, New $FIRSTNAMEM2. The sun was setting.\n\"The really amazing thing about the mystery of happiness,\" said $SURNAME0, \"is how utterly unobvious it is. Surely any intelligent person would think right off the bat that the chief ingredient of happiness is, to say the least, exciting things to do. Fling us naked into the jungle, along with a bevy of beautiful young women, and we\u2019d still have our hands full just keeping the females amused.\n\"Unobvious as the truth about happiness may be,\" said $SURNAME0, \"there it is.\"\nHe snapped his Beefeater open easily. It was well oiled. Most of the water that was left was ice.\n\"Here\u2019s another thing about happiness that may or may not be obvious to you,\" said $SURNAME0. \"It\u2019s quite literally universal. Nobody on any planet or any time-track in the whole Solar System ever found a way to make himself truly miserable.\"\nIt was interesting, the way $SURNAME0 told somebody else\u2019s story rather than his own. He told it like a man reporting the weather.[*] He used terms like \"with all this\" and \"so he thought.\"", "1_17_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span> in Basel, Switzerland, and Redwine's room, in the very heart of the old town. Redwine sat poised like the tip of an old man\u2019s cane, alone in his littered, over-warm room. He wore yellow trousers, and a reddish-brown sweater. He meditated over the can of lemon essence on the table beside his elbow. It was half full. Redwine had another can that was full. He wanted to finish this one, too. He would finish it, too. It was impossible that Redwine would not finish it, finish both of them. For he said so, and no man who fails to do what he says he will do has ever been able to face himself for longer than twenty-four hours. No man who does not hang from a star can face himself for more than a few minutes.\nRedwine had the DTs, but so far he had only the DTs, not the Saltus. Not the agony of the Saltus. Redwine knew all about it because he had read all the medical descriptions of it in the Encyclopedia Britannica. In fact, as far as Redwine was concerned, the Saltus was a mandatory part of any really thorough description of the DTs. Red...", "1_16_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span> but it was five o'clock all the same because $FIRSTNAMEB2 had made the clocks say it was. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was a Titan, one of the great ruling class of a dead people called the Titans. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had once had a civilization of his own, with cities, administration, armies, and banners. The civilization had endured for perhaps two million years, until the first human astronauts had visited Titan.\nThere were no human cities on Titan now.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 lived in a cave. His palace was a cupola in the crater Ogunquit, at what had once been the shoreline of Titan's single, Earth-sized sea. The crater was a canyon now, two miles deep and two miles across.\nCurving around and down from Ogunquit were the remains of a spiral staircase, a stairway that had once climbed a cliff of ice four hundred feet high. The cliff was gone now, dissolved into the sea. The hot, wet winds of climate change had done that and much more.\nThe spiral staircase was a wonder, even in ruins. It had been built by machines operated by no one knew what kind of muscles and nerves. There had been gears and ball-screws and electrothermal motors in the mechanisms. The...", "1_2_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>. The doorman of the Parc Monceau opened his little glass window and shone his flashlight inside the Cadillac at us.\n\u201cWhat have you got in there?\u201d he asked. \u201cOnly two in the front seat.\u201d He glared at us suspiciously. \u201cNothing to drink?\u201d\n\u201cNaw,\u201d said Stan. \u201cBut they'll be plenty in Mojave.\"\n\u201cMojave? What's that?\u201d\n\u201cNext stop Buffalo.\u201d\n\u201cWell, I don't know. Don't go too fast and stay off the bumper.\u201d\nHe waved us away and I stepped on the gas; we whooshed off into the river of darkened Paris. Suddenly the city was gone and we were passing the open fields of Ville-d'Avray and hitting 186 on the Memorial Hermann bridge across the Seine. Stan had never been to Paris before and seemed to be savouring every moment; it all looked like the bright dream it is to a man from Manhasset, New York, and Bayonne, New Jersey.\nI drove past the post where Anatole Broyard would be expecting us at one o'clock...", "0_15_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three sixteen in the afternoon</span>. The place was Jointner Strange\u2019s bungalow in the Gothic Wing of Shaker Heights Hospital. Ward lay in bed, breathing but not speaking.\nThe fluid flow of his \u201cvoice\u201d in the recorder was over; the digital readout on the side of the machine showed that ten full minutes of tape had been used. It was time to change reels.\nThe sound of the recorder\u2019s \u201crewind\u201d switch, one more time ... rusty, creaky ... there. And now the whish of the fresh reel being threaded into place at last.\nWhen the apartment quieted, although the canned sound of the traffic of Cleveland was still clearly audible outside, although the drone of the hospital heating system never ceased for a minute, although the intermittent screams from other corridors of the building never died entirely, a new sound could at last be heard, coming from the vicinity of one of the great square windows.\nThe window was closed, but the curtains could move a bit, pushed by the wind that was streaming horizontally through the opened slats of the venetian blinds. The soft sound, a steady crepitation, came from the moving curtains.\nThe wind needed no encouragement, really, to enter...", "1_16_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span> of a fine May day in the year of Our Lord 1902. The place was Rumbold's wake. Rumbold was a recently deceased relative of the Stamp nephews, and his wake was being held in the Stamp mansion on Embassy Row in Washington, D.C.\n$SURNAME1 was sitting in a flower-bedded bower, laboring over a bowl of vanilla ice cream.\n\"Ready?\" asked $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"For what?\" said $SURNAME1 dreamily.\n\"The attack,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He put his hand over the muzzle of his .l6-caliber German automatic pistol.\n\"God in Heaven\u2014\" said $SURNAME1, \"are you actually going to start something?\"\n\"Any second now,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. His weirdly sensitive feet were sensing the approach of a large crowd.\n\"I promised them no violence!\" said $SURNAME1. \"That\u2019s what they wanted!\" he shouted. \"That\u2019s what they got!\" he shouted.\n\"Everybody to the underslab!\" yelled a voice outside. \"Everybody to the underslab!\" The instruction was being repeated at the zero ends of chains of human beings.\n\"But there is no underslab!\" said $SURNAME1. \"The house is supported by p...", "1_19_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span> that Alvin came floating down into Willey playing field in much the same way, perhaps, as the first man might have come: a grasshopper. He came alight by mistake. Luck, that great sportsman, caught up with him\u2014again.\nHe didn\u2019t know that the minute hand had ticked another notch.\nThe minute hand was right on schedule, anyhow\u2014right on schedule for a Martian invasion by simply refusing to budge no matter what the alarm company said or did to it. The minute hand stopped two minutes to seven. It remained stopped two minutes to seven. It started again at twenty past seven.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, meanwhile, wouldn\u2019t let anyone inside the room in the Uranian Zoo. The only way anybody could get in would be to go through the door, and $FIRSTNAMEF1 would plainly have liked to see anybody try that.\nNobody expected the crowd outside the door to be so big, though. At first there wasn\u2019t anybody outside the door. Then there was one policeman. Then there was a half-a-dozen policemen.\nThey were out there peeking in through the malachite-green paint on the windows, with the white tusks of the mural triceratops fore...", "2_12_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-one p.m.</span> Earth time.\nTHE SWARM\nIt is twelve thirty-two p.m. Earth time.\nIn the dark-mirrored control turret of a rocket ship on a launching pad on Titan, the pilot, an iron automaton, is holding the ship in a posture ready for firing. The pilot of the ship is a man named $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014a man chained in the pilot seat.\nThe ship is big. It is a heavy-haul missile transporter. It stands above a trench thirty feet wide, fifteen feet deep, fifty-two hundred feet long.\nThe ship is aimed down the trench.\nAt the bottom of the trench is the dry sea bottom of the tropical Pacific Ocean.\nOnly a handful of men know what is about to happen.\nOnly a handful of men want it to happen.\nChief among the handful is a man named Stony Stevenson.\nStony Stevenson is an architect.\nHe plans buildings in Newport, Rhode Island, and he makes oodles of money.", "2_19_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven seventeen in the evening</span>, Earth time.\n$SURNAME0 stood in an open portal of the materialization trailer. He was looking out into the rain and mist at a water-butler that stood on a low tripod in an open space in the woods. The water-butler was one of the largest that had ever been used. It towered twenty feet into the mists and rain.\nIt was a glittering gold-plated cylinder perhaps a foot in diameter. It had openings at the top and bottom, through which hot and cold liquids, respectively, were circulated. This exploited a temperature phenomenon by which liquids of different temperatures that are mixed in a given container will soon reach a uniform temperature.\nThe hot and cold liquids were thoughtfully available in flexible connections at the top and bottom.\nThe water in the device was replaced with great frequency.\n$SURNAME0 had fixed his fascination on the device because of the ingenious and mischievous person who had designed it\u2014Martian shyster Clayton Brackett.\nBrackett had been put in charge of the decoy apparatus by the Martian military command. He had designed, built, and installed everything himself.", "2_16_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty in the afternoon</span>. The place was the merge of an exit lane and an entrance ramp. We had paused for a split second at traffic\u2014light, then my attorney punched it into fourth. But in that moment\u2014one of those rare moments in almost any life when fates are decided\u2014I felt the stark terror of everything I'd forgotten, and I knew immediately why my attorney and I had been struggling so strenuously on that Incredible Trip to Las Vegas. No doubt about it. We had to get out of that car!\n\u201cHit the brakes!\u201d I screamed. \u201cWe gotta get outta this thing!\u201d I clawed at the door handles, babbling and screaming; but my attorney had already hit the brakes and the Whale floated to a stop at the crest of the exit ramp and the light. My attorney turned to look at me.\n\u201cWhat's the matter,\u201d he said. \u201cWe'll never make it.\"", "1_23_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-eight in the evening</span> on this last night. For hundreds of miles around lay thousands of square miles of azure, car-reflected Oklahoma nights.\nTomorrow they would all be gone, and something else would be there. What? She shrugged, opened the refrigerator, groped for a jar of mayonnaise, and poured a big dollop onto half a sandwich of cold lamb. She ate it with her head bowed, thinking about the race in the morning and about a bottle of cold capsules she had opened in the bathroom cabinet that afternoon. About forty of them were missing. She unfolded the wrapper on one she had just taken and read the label: Seconal\u2014Take as needed for sedation. She sighed and said to herself, \u201cYeah . . . take five or ten more of 'em.\u201d She chewed her sandwich and examined the back of her hand. The tributary lines at the sides were getting rather alarming. Fifty-nine years old and she looked fifty-six at the most. It was a damn fine looking hand, though. The sun had not eaten the back of it. It was the arteries under her skin that were giving her fits.\nEvidently she needed Seconal, because it was going on this way, and when she needed...", "1_14_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had lived through the first three minutes of what was probably the last three minutes of his life.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 leaned against the wooden wall of his cubicle, bored and rumpled and helpless. There wasn\u2019t a thing for him to do. Now and then a recruit would steal past his cubicle to answer a call of nature, would steal back again. And every now and then, a bland public-relations man or a sleek efficiency expert would approach $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s door, would hammer or would murmur with lists of new questions for $FIRSTNAMEB0 to answer.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t answer the questions.\nIn a silent, secret pocket in his skull, $FIRSTNAMEB0 carried the screwdriver and a jeweler\u2019s eyepiece. And with these two items he could unscrew and unscrew the insides of his two antenna-like hearing aids. When he had pulled the right-hand hearing aid completely apart, he replaced its function by inserting a wad of cotton into his right ear.\nWith the left-hand hearing aid, he screwed the shell apart, removed the clearwindow, and left the bone-white coils of the microphone bare. He replaced the microphone by inserting a wad of cotton into...", "2_7_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-two in the morning</span>, and with his face as expressionless as his filmy and pain-drenched eyes, $FIRSTNAMEB0 led the company in perfect and exhausting drills. By slipping into a world where nothing mattered but the drill, $FIRSTNAMEB0 made himself and everybody else amazingly happy and free.\nWhen the pain in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head was at its worst, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s thoughts were fond and nostalgic thoughts of a better world. A world where people were not always hurting one another. A world like the world of his dreams, the world that he had found in the stone at the examination table.\nThe steel balls and the wet coils of copper wire inside $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head were a focusing mechanism for something far more subtle.\nAt eight thirty-two, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was turned over to his platoon sergeant, Buck Prewitt.", "1_23_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to midnight</span>. The Salo had arrived at the very center of the Milky Way Galaxy or whatever it was, that a lost and bewildered people had come to think of, over the uncounted aeons, as the foundation, the mother lode, the fount of their once and future God. At the center of the Universe, at t = 0, entropy and information both reached their minimums. The up- escalator and the down-escalator briefly touched, as did past and future.\nTeilhard prepared to die. He was crying.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was thunderstruck by the grandeur of the place, but even more thunderstruck by his own good luck in being at the center of the Universe. \"Imagine, $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014\" he whispered. \"What\u2019re the odds against my finding myself in this particular spot in the Universe? Unbelievable, isn\u2019t it?\"\n\"My God,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"it\u2019s the biggest fountain I ever saw.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was horrified by the spectacle. \"Oh\u2014no,\" she said. \"This is horrible. This is the beginning of the end of everything.\"\n\"It means everything is going to be all right,\" said $SURNAME1...", "0_3_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-two in the morning</span>. One might assume, if one were a nervous person, that there was an element of symbolic importance in sending Earth\u2019s two wildest police cars, containing Earth\u2019s wildest police officers, out in response to an alleged robbery at one of Earth\u2019s wildest locations. And that one assumed well, for whatever unseen agents secretly govern this planet, they have a sense of humor.\nIn the red wagon were Sebastian Copula, a very nervous man named Brancusi, and twenty-two-year-old Bobby Lamb, holder of a degree from the University of Louisville; he had gone there on a football scholarship and had stayed on to study police work.\nIn the white convertible were Chief of Police Wilson, his daughter $FIRSTNAMEF0, and about forty-two thousand dollars in negotiable bonds. Wilson had stolen them from a Buenos Aires bank, whose name he bore, as did $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nThe cars were speeding along Highway 119, a two-laner that runs through the foothills of the mountains. It was possible to see, off in the moonlit distance, the faint outline of the mountains themselves.\nIn the foothills lived Dr. Robert Frock, noted atomic scientist. His secretary-wife had once...", "2_14_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"There was a young feller used to live around these parts some,\" said $SURNAME0 to $SURNAME1, \"swore up and down he\u2019d seen a spaceman just like you here, once.\" He avoided $SURNAME1\u2019s eyes. \"Sort of thought you might be that same feller,\" $SURNAME0 said.\nThe guards and aides shuffled apprehensively. Might $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, after all, be the man whose coming had been foretold in the Earthling history of the Tweedles?\nA solar wind of sanity howled through the Universe.\n\"Anyhow,\" said $SURNAME0, \"I sent for you because I thought you might be willing to help me out with some thinking. I\u2019m up against a very sticky problem, and I thought somebody from your time\u2014where people had a little more experience with being human\"\u2014$SURNAME0 had a spasm of pain\u2014\"might be able to chip in with some good ideas.\"\n\"What\u2019s the sticky problem?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I want you to hide my dog,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"What?\" said $SURNAME1.", "0_4_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>, and the year was 1941. At that moment, the space ship lay on an even keel in a flat, vacant lot in $SURNAME2, California, one mile east of the Eastern State Mental Hospital on Fairmount Boulevard. By night the lot was quiet and secluded and full of activity, but by day it was hot and dusty, with only a few buildings around and a lot of heat and sunlight.\nThe buildings surrounding the vacant lot were small and old and torn down in places. They were tucked in between long, rolling, treeless hills and a stone wall of a prison. Across the streets beyond were two great barns belonging to the State, concealing the ugly walls of the San Francisco Mental Health and Eastern State Hospitals from the hot, restless, shiftless tide of humanity pushing aimlessly in the hills and mixing with the flotsam and most of San Francisco in the flat, quiet streets next to the walls of the prison. A dirt road went by the lot, whereon the space ship rested quietly without a sound to be heard anywhere around it. Traffic on the dirt road was slow and sparse at that time of day\u2014people walking to work; people in jump seats of old-fashioned street-car transfer rigs; people in more...", "0_19_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-eight in the evening</span> and the place was Beverly, Ohio, just off Route 41, in a conclave of very old industrial buildings. The people were having a meeting, under high ceilings, in the office section up front. They were having a business meeting and another big party was imminent. The big party was to climax a big week-long annual convention. The big week-long annual convention was the culmination of an all-year-round, low-key but still very potent and real social phenomenon: the Babbit Badgers, one of the country\u2019s truly great and informal secret societies. And among the truly great and informal secret societies in the country, the Babbit Badgers ranked very high indeed, though rank was meaningless, especially to those newly admitted, who underwent a short initiation ceremony comprising much smiling and flattery, many drinks, and a chant or two. The chant or two, at the moment of admission, went this way:\nThe members all smiled and chatted, though no one was chatty or effusive, as the evening\u2019s trainee members\u2014five of them, given meaningless nicknames usually lean to as Rabbit, Chipmunk, Mole, Opossum, and Wren\u2014finished their \u201cexaminations\u201d on...", "0_11_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-two in the morning</span>/ noon; and Bombardini Company stock was selling, ever so slightly (it was a world market, remember), for thirteen dollars and ten cents a share, an all-time high.\nCalloway held out his right hand. \"Come in, little dolly,\" he said. \"Come in for the kiss that we never got last night.\"\nMiss Perhaps entered the office, saw $FIRSTNAMEF0, and stopped cold, unable to get out of the doorway.\n\"Here she is, Magnum Opus,\" said Calloway. \"My lovely, lovely secretary\u2014Mistress $FIRSTNAMEF0 Nilles, our financial vice-president. In person, Pope of our Vast Power and Light Division, Madame Olympia Seacleer. Put up your hair so we can see your face, dolly.\"\nMiss Perhaps did as she was told. She was a little older, this morning, than twenty-four.\n\"Hello,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, smiling. \"Mr. Bombardini isn\u2019t in the office this morning.\"\n\"I ... of course not,\" said Miss Perhaps, straightening her hair. \"I ... I don\u2019t work in Mr. Bombardini\u2019s office, you know. I\u2019m...", "0_7_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven sixteen in the morning</span> and something had gone wrong in my life. Perhaps many things. Actually, I felt that something had gone wrong in my life. I sat there with my bigamous Arizona license plates on the front and back bumpers of my Cadillac convertible, on the parking pad at the Glass House Apartments at Ho ngaloop Road, a down swell main artery road in L . A . On the north side of the spanking new apartment building was sloping green lawn and classy homes; on the south side was a great goddamn freeway with cars and trucks whizzing by as fast as anything could whizz. On this Tuesday morning I was dressed in bar jacket, white loafers, -and my entire stint in the Marine grunts was spent in closeorder drills without once a blessed chance to smell anything that wasn\u2019t other men. As I sat in the car I continued to stare straight ahead for fifteen minutes. I was having one of my emotional breakdowns\u2014one of the big ones. Sometimes I have ten or twelve of these things every morning. In any case, shit had definitely happened to me. I had no business whatever in Accou ntantland; I was nothing but a degenerate freak of nature with a trophy British hassock in my trunk...", "2_15_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three eighteen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was a suite of offices on the fiftieth floor of the Sir Francis Drake Hotel, in San Francisco. The curtains were drawn, and the lights were on in the offices.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, the yellow Labrador retriever dog, looked up and licked her chops.\nWoof.\nThere was a flash of light, a clap of thunder, and a smell of ozone. The thunder rolled and died out.\nBling.\nBling-bling.\nThere was a flash of light, a clap of thunder, and a smell of ozone. The thunder rolled and died out.\nBling.\nSomebody opened a humidor and whiffled the rich aroma of excellent tobacco.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 whined.\nThere was a flash of light, a clap of thunder, and a smell of ozone. The thunder rolled and died out.\nBling, bling. Tsk, tsk. There was a flash of light, a clap of thunder, and a smell of ozone. The thunder rolled and died out.\nBling-bling.", "1_16_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span> on December thirty-first. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized at the head of the spiral stairs of the United States interest section of the Palace of Culture in New Moscow.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were enveloped in swirling robes of magnificent, poisonous colors. The colors were the same as the colors of the materialization points. The colors were the same as the colors of the field around every materialization point.\nThese colors were riotous and cruel. The robes were as heavy as tempered-steel plate.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 did not move until the spiral stairs beneath them began to rumble and revolve. Then they descended slowly, majestically.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s eyebrows were two curves of scarlet knitting- yarn, tied off with a fuzzy violet ball at the center of the brows\u2019 high arcs. A cross-stitch of pure gold ran from one eyebrow to the other.\nThe dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 had eyebrows whiter than unsifted powdered sugar. They were eyebrows of flawless cotton thread, bound with a ball of sun-yellow yarn. The eyebrows were as heavy as fishing nets, as delicate as spider threads. A cross-stitch...", "1_22_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-eight in the evening</span>. The last bar closed at nine o\u2019clock. No place to buy a drink. Not in this town. Not at this time of night. The gas station bars on the Strip were closed. What I needed was not to be had on the Strip. I\u2019d left my last bottle of Wild Turkey back at the motel. I didn\u2019t want to go back for it. Was I shaken? Was I consumed with lust for whiskey? No. In fact I felt very calm\u2014precisely because there was no whiskey in sight. My situation appeared clear. I had gravely miscalculated my drinking potential with this last binge. I had pushed it too far. Now my body was telling me\u2014no more. So okay! I accepted it. With this faith in my internal guidance system\u2014which was clearly marked Return To Sender\u2014I was able to stand up and walk, every step of the way, all the way back to my motel rooms: right smack through the poisonous, hopeless, drunken night of the filthy, Rotten, Lying, Whore of Babylon, this rancid sour den of thieves and Wild Western lowlife, losers, and trouble. Whores, pimps, addicts, armed robbers. Lowlife...", "0_9_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe 46th entry of the chronicle of Salo was made precisely three minutes after the 61st entry. It was more than ninety seconds after $SURNAME0\u2019s death.\nThere were two witnesses to the entry.\nIn Miss Phoebe\u2019s fin de si\u00e8cle mansion on Cape Cod, everything useful that could be made of silver was made of silver. Tea trays, candy baskets, waste-paper baskets, doormats, teapots\u2014everything was silver. Such things as saltcellars, letter scales, window-fasteners, bootjack handles, picture-frames had their principal practical use as decorative ornaments.\nA tea service of tarnished and pocked silver stood before Miss Phoebe this morning. It had been used only once\u2014on the day $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was born. It had belonged to Miss Phoebe\u2019s mother, who had used it on the two supremely significant occasions of her life\u2014when she had received Alice Hargreaves\u2019blue handwritten letter accepting her invitation to tea, and when she had received $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s mahogany, gold-stamped invitation to tea.\nNow Miss Phoebe...", "0_5_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty in the morning</span>...but I had to drive to L.A. And we drove up the hill to the freeway, where I left the 2.5in and gotup to 60 or 70 mph before I found myself confronted by a wobbling Buick and two cars coming across the divider at me. My hands flew up like Stan Laurel\u2019s as I swerved sharply leftthere was no time to move right, and then I realized the wobblig Buick was actually just a wobbling row of palm trees down the center divider, and that what I\u2019d mistaken for oncoming cars was just the rising sun, burning red through the fog...so I lurched back to the right and there, right in front of me as I veered back across the lanes, I saw a terrible sight. The steering column was on fire!\nIt looked like a Jackson Pollack painting; all tangled wires and dripping cloth\u2014a writhing mass of flames that enveloped my left knee up to the thigh and was now threatening to eat into the door panel on the near side. For a moment I thought some lunatic had fired a bazooka at the car while I was up on the freeway, but then I realized that the infernal machine must have...", "0_20_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>, and the Place d'Armes was crowded with Maisons-Lafittes listeners. Crowded, and beginning to get noisy. Although it was still before the hour of beverages, there was a tendency for the discreet, attentive hush that is supposed to accompany serious listening to start dying. The people were growing chummy. You could feel it in the increasing volume of talk. At any moment, my operative observed, there was going to be some kind of communal sigh, as when everybody in a theater finds the same nerve has been pinched, and automatically makes with a clearing of the throat.\nRelax, dear friends and protectors, thought $FIRSTNAMEB1, unable to catch Stanley Rinehart's eye, which was no more than sixty feet away. And let this organist proceed with his affair. He\u2019s your man. He\u2019s our guy. He\u2019s the hero in this tragic little drama we\u2019ve got brewing right out here on the sidewalk\u2014about somebody who suddenly finds himself criminally At Fault.\n\"Hey, $FIRSTNAMEB1!\" called somebody. $FIRSTNAMEB1 barely caught it. He knew it wasn\u2019t from Stanley Rinehart. He resisted the temptation to look around. Another pebble struck...", "2_22_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was Dr. Hilarius\u2019s Solar Tissue-Restorer and Tanning Clinic, on West Fifty-seventh Street, New York City.\nThe Solar Tissue-Restorer and Tanning Clinic had once been the Walpi Hotel. Dr. Hilarius had changed absolutely nothing about the Walpi Hotel. Walpi was a village of the Navajo Indians. All the fixtures in the Clinic were thus far west of the Mississippi. So were the genuine Navaho rugs and blankets, the authentic kachina dolls, and so on. The effect was that of a gloomy hogan inhabited by a radio repairman who was tired of his trade, and sick of standard Navajo notions about the weather.\nThe window was open. A marvelous Indian summer night came in at the window.\n\"Oh, yuck!\" said Random Frequent Flyer Corsi.\nShe tossed a piece of paper into the wastebasket beneath the desk at which she sat.\nShe bit her lower lip. Her eyes narrowed. She thought of a place in West Virginia, reached out, touched a control. A clicking was heard.\n\"Chad! What\u2019s this note you sent me yesterday?\"", "0_15_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> of October thirty-first, bank closing time, in the First National Bank of St. Charles, Missouri. The place was the rear of the bank where the last customers waited while their balances were \u201cposted,\u201d a legalistic way of saying subtracted and given to them on little paper slips to take home and feed into their automatic accounting machines. There were nine people. They were, left to right, young lady, young gentleman, brother and sister, old man, middle-aged woman, young man, older woman, and another old man.\nThe old men had me bothered. They were both of an incredible leanness and intensity, but they didn\u2019t look anything alike. One was wearing a long, officially prosperous and respectable frock coat, and the other one was in overalls and had a stubbly dark beard and was carrying a shot-gun.\nThey had a two-foot hole between them and the old man in overalls was whispering in the ear of the old man in frock coat. What worried me was that you could see the devil in the eyes of both of them.\nThere were four armed guards in uniform and carrying pump-guns slung on their shoulders.\nThe armed guards were bored, but watch...", "0_8_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight nineteen in the morning</span> when Gibbons made his police broadcast. By nine Pierce was in an anticlimactic conference with Dapp and Summer. \"We jus\u2019 don\u2019t know, $FIRSTNAMEM2,\u201d Dapp said. He was trying to be kind. The recriminations and dismissals had to come later. Too many people knew too much. \"The wife was here before noon\u2014the way things are now, you might as well buy a used car on the installment plan\u2014and she left at one twenty-five, in plenty of time to get to Carmel. The camera was here on the desk, in plain sight of both pool cabs all morning. The claim agent corroborates the receipt at ten forty-six. And here\u2019s the picture she took, not damaged one iota\u2014as you can see for yourself.\" He waved a print before Pierce. Pierce did not look. He had resolved to let nothing of Mile\u2019s hurt him. He saw a sweeping curve of unthreaded black tape, shot through with light. At where it should break or curve it was an empty question: not a flick or speck. Nothing.\n\"I would not think that\u2019s even the same curve,\u201d he said...", "1_19_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-six in the evening</span> of Holy Thursday on the first day of spring of the bissextile year of A.D. 1963. A fashionable crossbreed at that; the earthbound pilot of the spacecraft was dressed all in correct Oxford rag of the period. He was, of all things, an English major at an American college; had, in fact, knocked out a Great Novel while at school, an accomplishment not without its economic consequences. The novel had been published. The only negative aspect of the novel was its title. It was called The Cross and the Rube. Questions had arisen about the propriety of exhibiting at the controls of a space ship an obvious symbol of hate, religious bigotry, and scorn for all mankind. But such questions had eventually been laughed off. This cat was just counter-kooky enough to make you feel he was your favorite character in the book . . . and just kooky enough to make you wonder about that. It was an uncertainty most people felt glad to have on their side. Rumors of It achieved critical mass with the selection of the astronaut crew. The English major, at that point taking a few years off between college and his adult life, was a natural selection. People felt he would make everything all right. And people were right.", "0_5_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past five o'clock in the morning</span> by the chronometer. One minute past five on the morning of October 12, 2436. Our first real view of Mars, we felt, shouldn\u2019t be had through a professional telescope. We wanted to see it with the naked eye\u2014if it was going to be viewed at all.\nIt was the morning of October 12, 2436\u2014and yes, there it was, the red desert apparition, the promised land, and there, presently, was its capital city, Asbury Park, New Jersey, known in the ancient days as Princeton. And, yes, it was all as flat as a flannel cake. And it was all in primary colors, too: coral and salmon foam, old gold and dead brigadine with wide noodles of black tar shoreline. The only things in Greco-Roman white were the tower of the Lily White Church and the empty fool\u2019s-gold cupolas of the Martian Government Monuments.\nSalmon foam with a scattering of burned rose, a desert of evil prince Rupert\u2019s kisses, reaching all the way to here, to where a big foot stamped IT ALONG! on love and dreams.\nAnd out on this crystalline plain, suddenly there, were tiny spec...", "1_5_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-six in the morning</span>. We still had two mounting plates to tie on the car, so we decided to drive to a nearby all-night gas station and borrow some wire and a pair of pliers to make the job fast and permanent. $FIRSTNAMEB3 left the car at the shoulder of the Pueblo highway and followed me in. A fat businessman was washing his hands at the fountain, and two gaspump loafers were watching him. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was so excited he fell into the straight-man role: he picked up the pair of pliers the fat businessman had laid down, the businessman paid no attention, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 carried the pliers to the back seat of the car, saying, \u201cYou'll have to excuse me, folks, I'm just borrowing this . . . I'll give it back, don't worry.\u201d Everything was a joke to him.\n\u201cOh, I know those guys . . . this is a gas station.\u201d He looked at the cars around. \u201cWhat's news? What's news, brethren? Tell me all about it. To the west, man.\u201d He climbed in my car and hung a leg out the window. He yelled at me above the din of meathook trucks on the road: \u201c...", "2_12_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was noon</span>. The siren of the ship sounded twelve ponderous blasts.\n\"Here it comes, skipper\u2014\" said Salter. There was an instant of silence in the ship, followed by a low twanging hum loud enough to distort the massed sounds of all the contrapuntal buzzers and clangors in the ship.\nThe twanging hum was actually the sound of the binary Sun in one phase of its twenty-five-minute cycle. It was the Sun musically tuning itself, playing the twenty-five frequencies that gave it its enormous gravitational speed.\nThe Sun\u2019s tones were actually undulating Earth harmonics.\nThe ship was being pushed away from the Martian surface by the one G-inch-wide pulsing pencil of gravitational light that joined ship and Sun.\nThe old woman with wings sat at the controls. She was perfectly happy. She was finally where she longed to be\u2014inside the Sun.", "2_1_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe time was twenty-four more minutes till the moment of the materialization of the American War Monuments Commission truck in the space-time volume occupied by the $SURNAME0 mansion.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, strapped to their cots in the Refreshment Center basement, found the materialization of the ironingsheet portal strangely discordant. Their nerve connections with their central nervous systems were healthy and normal. Their bodies responded with painful twinges to the stimulus of the unaccustomed sounds and motions.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 lay quietly. They found no good reason to rush through the ironing-sheet doorway into whatever lay outside.\nThe voice boomed again from loudspeakers in the walls and ceiling. The voice commanded the two sexes to carry themselves forward into the mirror.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 lay quietly.\nThe loudspeaker demanded that the two sexes touch genitals with one another.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 found the order disgusting, offensive, and repugnant.", "1_14_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-nine in the afternoon</span>, and as good a time as any for a cocktail. We passed into the restaurant car, and I tried hard to look like a man who lunched in dining cars all the time. I succeeded fairly well at it. Nobody seemed to notice me.\nYou ate the same thing in the French dining cars you ate in the American ones; unless you counted the snails. There are no snails in American dining cars, just as there are no fountain squirters and nobody ties up the trays and it is a felony to use the word \u2019spectacles\u2019 instead of the word \u2018glasses\u2019 when you\u2019re asked what kind of \u2019specs\u2019 you\u2019d like with your meal.\nIn the next booth were two frightfully gay young things who were trying to look as if they were just out of Debutante Classes at Smith, and doing it very badly.\n\u201cSay!\u201d the one with the pretty ankles was saying, \u201cdon\u2019t you know the longest word in the English language?\u201d\n\u201cGo on!\u201d said the one with the greasy hair. \u201cYou mean one of those Oxford words, don\u2019t you?", "1_18_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-nine in the evening</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had just been given his choice of the three capsules in the gen- erator, in the casing under the vault.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 chose at random.\nThere was, of course, no way of his knowing whether the capsule he picked was the iron one that was actually powerful, the cellulose one that was actually harmless, or the copper one that was actually deadly.\nAt six thirty-eight, $FIRSTNAMEB0 radioed an apparently harmless and uninformative message to the space ship in Earth orbit. The message was intended for the people in the space ship. $FIRSTNAMEB0 could not imagine that the people in the space ship were not receiving his messages! No matter what he did, something happened that made him imagine that someone was paying attention over there.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 suspected that his harmless message at six thirty-eight was a welcome change from all the threats and hostilities of the preceding messages.\nAt six thirty-nine, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was right. The apparently harmless message at six thirty-eight was so completely, pathetically wrong as to constitute an invitation to a horrible and graphic execution.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had cracked open his capsule of iron at six thirty-eight and a half, had taken a couple of dry swigs, and had pounded the...", "0_9_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-eight in the morning</span> of the second of September when $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s taxi stopped at the flight line of the Army\u2019s Fort Ti. A young private there said that the last announced mail-run plane had gone out at six o\u2019clock in the morning. They wouldn\u2019t expect another run before three o\u2019clock in the afternoon.\nBy that time, $FIRSTNAMEB2 figured, the rumble party at Glenn Miller\u2019s would be in full progress. He gave the private three dollars, explaining that he was Deputy Inspector Holmes from New York, following a route that Inspector Holmes had always used on his inspection tours\u2014Washington, Georgia, Fort Ti, New Tork. It was always a three-dollar fare, said $FIRSTNAMEB2. It would be worth the Army\u2019s while to continue the long-standing arrangement.\nThe private said he didn\u2019t see why the Army should pay any more than it had to. It had a treaty with New York City that limited the mail delivery flat rate to three dollars. It was only right, said the private. He himself only made three dollars a day.\n\"You\u2019re improving the whole United Nations, boy,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"What with inter-continental mail delivery, and...", "0_9_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-eight in the morning</span>. At this moment my attorney and I were directly between the Hollywood Station and the county jail. We had managed to avoid all loose Seguramente\u2019gards until we reached this point, but the traffic was now nose\u2014to\u2014nose without even the luxury of lanes. As far as I could see, nothing but black\u2014and\u2014whites with red flashers, windshields crazed with sun, drivers hunched behind the meat\u2014grinder, playing it by ear.\nMy attorney was driving (or trying to drive) a big black Eldorado with California plates, a present from one of his richer porno clients. We had the top down and a mudslide of teppiedisk in the back seat; the music mix was turned all the way up and our clothing was modulated to match. \u201cI am the Doctor! To sick! To sick! To sick!\u201d barked the doberman in our right ear. \u201cI cannot go,\u201d moaned the junkie in the left, \u201cI\u2019m too scared.\u201d Then, after a while, there was nothing but gunshots and howling wind and a vicious, rising confusion that seemed to come from the sky.", "0_7_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nA light rain had fallen, leaving the air fresh and cool. The light rain lay like a rumor on the grass of the hills and covered The great rock with silver.\nIt was pleasant to look out into the hills and valleys and see everything fresh and clean and sparkling in the morning.\nI T was a long time after that and millions of centuries after that, and the Earth was very different from the Earth of that day.\nThe great rock was a deposit of aluminum ore on the surface of the moon. The great rock was not perfectly rectangular any longer, but had the form of an egg that had been crushed accidentally in a size 15,000 spacesuit\u2014crushed incompletely, so that the aluminum rock had received a white heart of lunar ore.\nThe heart of the aluminum rock was exactly the shape of a footprint.\nThe rock lay on the outskirts of a lunar junkyard. Space ships in various stages of dismantlement littered the area. It was a beautiful sight.\nThe sun shone down out of a sky that sparkled with dust and thrashed with streamers of space cloud.\nThe sun beat down so hot that screws and rivets could be heated red-hot in half an hour\u2019s time.", "0_11_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-eight in the morning</span> of the third day after the Space Wanderer had disappeared. Had anybody chosen to look closely at $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 on that day, he would have seen a curious thing\u2014 i. e., that on the tremendous outside of $SURNAME0\u2019s skin had formed a glistening coat of diamond dust.\nThe dust came from a sciama.\nA sciama is a cone that is free at the small end, like a cone of yarn or paper, and that widens out, with walls that fold in on one another and get thicker and thicker, like the walls of a Chinese puzzle. The walls thicken as the cone widens, and the walls are roofed over at the top.\nA forty-foot-high, red-white-and-blue-striped advertising balloon is a typical example of a sciama. The balloon is free at the small end and has walls and a roof. The floors and furniture of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s foolscap yacht were free sciamas.\nSo was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe great material of $SURNAME0\u2019s skin had thickened and gained its diamond sheen, layer by layer, as layer after layer of thousands of space ships...", "1_9_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine eighteen in the morning</span>. The place was the back room of the $SURNAME0 estate, a jumble of Victorian furniture and Barber\u2019s Island bric-a-brac, in the corner of which was a small cot. The cot was $SURNAME0\u2019s bed.\nHe had been in bed ever since the night of the Derby. He was still there. The only sign that $SURNAME0 still lived was the fact that he lay so straight on the cot\u2014something human beings will do only when they are in the process of becoming prized statues.\n$SURNAME0 lay with his beautiful head turned to one side, looking out of a bloodshot eye at the gaudy ship\u2019s wheel that had been Tralfamadore\u2019s gift to him.\nSome friendly curator at the Smithsonian would, in the year 2520, write a responsible paper saying that the ship\u2019s wheel had symbolized $SURNAME0\u2019s notion of a great tidal-locking device that might be installed in the Earth-Moon system to prevent harmful currents in the space lanes.\nThe curator would write that $SURNAME0 had died before disclosing the exact nature of his solar system stabilizer. And the curator would write that the reason Rum...", "2_7_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nEarth and Chronos were at their closest approach to each other.\nIt had taken the radio signals of Earth nine minutes to reach Mars.\nIt took the radio signals of Mars just over three minutes to reach Earth.\nIn other words, there was telecommunication between Earth and Mars. At any point on Earth, it would have been possible to send a question or a piece of news or a piece of music to Mars, and receive an answer or a piece of reproduction of the news or the music back from Mars in little or just about no time at all.\nLucky Strike, for example, could have sent the message LEAVE TO PLANTER AT ONCE to President Kennedy, and he could have had a handwritten reply just three minutes later.\nThere was telecommunication between Earth and Mars.\nBut Lucky Strike could get nothing through to Earth but pure-white noise. It was as though the telecommunication apparatus of Earth must be playing a trick on him.\nIt was seven minutes after seven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nSomething punched up through the Martian canopy, and the canopy cracked.\nThe Indians called the canopy over Mars the Firmament.", "2_4_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s co-worker was missing. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had strangled him, and had dragged the body behind the ship he was now spraying. He had strangled the man, Francis Gambon, with a clothesline.\nGambon had once sworn that he would strangle $FIRSTNAMEB0 with a clothesline if $FIRSTNAMEB0 ever did anything to upset the harmonium of their ship. This had, of course, made some sense to $FIRSTNAMEB0 in the past. He had been willing to agree that it might merit strangulation someday.\nHe had no memory of Gambon now.\nStrangulation had come about, had become necessary, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had strangled Gambon.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had not wanted to hurt him. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had wanted him to continue functioning normally in the fog of harmonium\u2014had wanted him unaware of what was going to happen until it had happened and could not be prevented from happening.\nAnd now $FIRSTNAMEB0 had to worry about being strangled.\nFor he had been told, over and over again, that strangling was the penalty for upsetting the harmonium.", "2_14_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 moved the little ball of witch-fire over $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s mechanical arms, over the prosthetic hands that were $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s hands.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s eyes glowed greenly in response to the fire.\n\"I\u2019ve come to make you a great offer, Mr. Mercury,\" said $SURNAME0.\nHe moved the ball of witch-fire to $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s stainless-steel codpiece, a replacement for the external genitals with which Nature had made him self-sufficiently well-endowed. A watch-spring emerged from the ball of fire and tickled $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s genitals.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s response was the response of an animal. He turned his head, tried to bite what was teasing him.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the mint-rich playboy whom Mars had declined to deprive of his young masculine beauty, stood huddled in the crowd like everybody else. He was watchful, frightened, suspicious.", "0_0_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-six a.m.</span> Pacific Standard Time, June 6, 1962. The place was an alley off Industrial Street, in Barstow, California. And the only item left standing of a once-mighty tempest in a tea cup was a putty-colored industrial building some seventy-five yards to the rear and left of where Mailer and the Canadian stood. The building was tremulous and wet under the rain, but it still held together.\n\u201cCan you believe that?\u201d asked the Canadian. He wiped rain from his brow with a khaki sleeve. There was no part of him that was not khaki.\n\u201cCan I believe what?\u201d said Raoul. He had not turned around to look at the building, himself. He turned now and looked.\n\u201cHoly cow!\u201d he said.\nHe whirled again to face the Canadian. The express cop, behind him, was frightened by this sudden whirl. He smelled a man about to bolt.\n\u201cOfficer, is there a telephone in the building?\u201d asked Mailer.\n\u201cI guess so,\u201d said the cop. \u201cI ain\u2019t tried one, myself.\u201d\n\u201cBut you mean you...", "0_9_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after nine o'clock in the morning</span> when I called my brother to tell him about the car. He said he'd come down. That was fine. We agreed to meet at the car. I hung up and went to look for a Sheriff to see about the complaint I was about to make about the car. It had been stoled from me. The boys were to come in finally at dawn that morning, after I'd slept a few hours. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo came first, then came $FIRSTNAMEF4 and Ed Dunkel, then came Galatea and Roy Johnson, then came Tim Gray and follow-up Stan Turtle, who had followed the whole bunch the six hundred miles from Dunkel's attic scandal-pad in New York. I saw them pile out of a taxi at the Reno station and buy a few candles and some coffee; then they all came over to my hotel and we sat around saying nothing. What a crew! I had a big lump in my throat. The sorrow of it all! In candlelight they looked even sicker. I think they all realized this was the end. They had come over the hill, under the shadow -- no more games, no more freakouts, the Air Force was waiting for most of them, and in their minds the damn road stretched out West forever...", "2_13_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThere were three sensational items of Martian news in every Earthly newspaper.\nOne, about three quarters of the Earthlings who had landed on Mars were now dead.\nTwo, the Martian lid was on. Nothing from Mars could escape to Earth. Nothing from Earth could escape to Mars. Communication by heliograph was impossible, thanks to the heavy Martian clouds.\nThree, the First Expeditionary Force on Mars had dug into trenches, had piled up mounds of dirt for further protection, had dug and piled and dug without cease.\nRest and recreation were out of the question.\nFighting and dying were out of the question.\nThere was nothing else to do until a twelfth man died of old age.\nThe time was twenty-five minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon. It was a little more than two hours after the rumor became fact. The Forces of Evil had at last taken an interest in the war on Mars.\nThe Martian attack came without warning\u2014without why or wherefore.\nThere was the hot breath of a whirlwind. A pressure as of sixty pounds to the square inch. The pressure was felt by three out of five Earthlings.", "0_12_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Sunday, October 10, 1972. This was the twenty-second day of the siege of the $FIRSTNAMEM2 Israel Temple of the Fifth Angels of the Apocalyptic Liberation\u2014better known as the Pigpen by its inhabitants. The temple, which was located on the west side of Ocean Park Boulevard between Ranauro and Yucca Streets in the Pacific Coast city of Los Angeles, was encircled day and night by automobile police to keep unruly Lakers fanatics outside.\nThe fanatics had come to hate the Pigpen after the fatal accident in the year 2001 that befell the Los Angeles Lakers\u2019 remarkable one-armed star center, Wendell \u201cMuch\u201d LIMITED : :\nThe LIMITED Company is...\nOf the LAST\nIn the ETERNAL hunt\nFor the WINNING BUCK\n24 cans to a case\nWINNING BUCK beer...\nLNRI LIMITED WARRANTY AGAINST DEFECTS IN MATERIALS AND\nWORKMANSHIP SEVEN YEARS: LIMITED WARRANTY AGAINST DEFECTS IN\nMATERIALS AND WORKMANSHIP FIVE YEARS: LIMITED WARRANTY\nAGAINST DEFECTS IN MATERIALS TWO YEARS; PARTS AND LABOR\nONE YEAR, WITH...", "1_19_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to eight o'clock in the evening</span> when we pulled out onto the road. \u201cNow, gentlemen, the thing is to relax completely and make completely no effort whatsoever at all to involve yourselves in the scene that is about to ensue. It would be a good idea to wear sunglasses if you have them. Close your eyes and wear sunglasses, gentlemen, and remember to breathe deeply and not to make any sudden movements. We are now entering the highway, and standing by to merge. Gentlemen, with your permission I am going to turn on the radio and tune to a good rock 'n roll station to aid us in our passage. Thank you, gentlemen. Good evening, sir, you are now free to exit the expressway at the next ramp. Thank you.\u201d We roared out onto the Island Expressway; the legal limit was 65 or 70 but we shot across two lanes and were on the Grand Central Parkway heading for JFK and the airport within minutes. We were way ahead of schedule. The night air was delicious and heady with the perfume and exhaust of summer, and the radio played golden rock \u201cn roll music that beat off and away into infinite distance, punctuated by a pusher's natural cries of Yes! and Fine! Fine! Fine! Fine! Fine! Fine!", "1_12_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Central Standard Time, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0\u2019s hand was opening a drawer in the mahogany bedside table. The quarter-past-one position in the drawer was empty. On all the previous one-o\u2019clocks, it had held a dark, roundish object about the size of a dollar coin or a Ford hubcap.\nThat object had been a Martian prodigious memory storage device, something almost infinitely more eidetically durable than the diamond-hard bit of the dog named $FIRSTNAMEB4. The storage device had been handed to $FIRSTNAMEF0 so swiftly that she hadn\u2019t had time to read its inscription:\nGIFT FROM CHRISTER\nTO HIS MOTHER\nABSENCE MAKES\nTHE HEART GROW\nTHE PRODIGIOUS\nMEMORY\nOF A MARTIAN\nAbsence makes the heart grow the prodigious memory of a Martian\u2014so the inscription mean mocking a machines truism: What can\u2019t be remembered doesn\u2019t hurt.\nBeaiffe $SURNAME0 held the round object in her hand now, weighing it, letting it roll like a gold piece between thumb and forefinger. It ended its roll by becoming the thumb and forefinger again...", "2_0_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past midnight</span> on the Wild West Coast...and as I reeled through the door into the street I ran into a dawn that would never be the same. For there on the corner, beside a black & white Highway Patrol car, a young cop was savagely clubbing a huge, nude, insanely drunken man who rolled insanely from side to side...whimpering: \u201cDon\u2019t hit me, Sarge...don\u2019t hit me . . . I\u2019m sorry.\u201d The smell of alcohol and lambent violence hung over everything. My head was full of gibberish and poison. The time was about one-thirty in the morning: That is about as nutty a moment as there is anywhere in the western world. I walked up to the young cop and flashed my press card. He stopped clubbing the animal and looked at me shyly.\n\u201cThis is terrible,\u201d I said. \u201cThis guy needs seventy-two hours of sleep. He\u2019s barefaced drunk.\u201d The cop grinned. \u201cYou\u2019re right. This fucker just fell down and went right to sleep in the middle of Laurel Canyon Boulevard.\"", "0_4_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>. I was riding the streets of Denver at that strange hour when all of life is reduced to a \u2022dreamlike puzzlement. A misty rain obscured the heights of the Rockies in the backgound. \u201cBy God, man these mountains are incredible,\u201d I muttered as I sped through traffic on my bike with the green sack bobbing on the seat. I had to laugh at the madness of being in Denver at that hour. Well, I had no place I had to go\u2014nothing to do with the dawn except grok and maybe catch a wink or two in the daylight hours. I raced past California Gulch, which looked like a ghost town on a Sunday morning, passed the sign that says ROCKY MTN. NAT'L FOREST and soon was beyond the lovely homes in the residential section of Denver. Mindblown, beyond repair, stupified...yet probably about as happy as any human being has a right to be. I felt a curious twinge of regret that I'd never learn to play the guitar. My left hand was jammed from tapping the brake and gearshift. Beyond that I wasn't physically tired, although I was completely exhausted in every other way . . . doomed and drained. I passed the turnoff to my...", "1_1_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1 had just turned on his televiewer for the expected news from the Tralfamadorian space ship.\n\"Hello, $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" said $SURNAME0. \"We\u2019re not quite ready to take off, but we\u2019ve set our automatic pilot, so you won\u2019t be able to miss us. Have your camera ready.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was still there\u2014still outside. The muzzle of his machine gun was pressed against the wall, and he had one round in the chamber.\nThere was still nobody in the tower.\nIt was a good time to kill $SURNAME0 and his mates, to blow up the ship.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 found that the tears in his eyes were keeping the televiewer\u2019s focusing mechanism from working. He wiped the tears away with a wrist.\nHis hand trembled so badly that he dropped the wristwatch he was using for a focusing device. He bent down to pick it up\u2014and saw, for the first time, that $SURNAME0\u2019s backyard was being flooded by the light from the space ship.\nSo the ship had taken action, notwithstanding $SURNAME0\u2019s statement that it would just sit there. $FIRSTNAMEB1 wondered what kind of action...", "0_15_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-two in the afternoon</span> and I needed two, two\u2014halfs to get an idea of how fucked I really was, how many months I\u2019d have to ride freight trains around this abyss before I could begin negotiating again. Shit, I\u2019d left most of my ALGEBRA books back at the Whale\u2019s house in Malibu and I needed them desperately.\nWe drank the pint, leaning on the trunk of the car. Then he rolled a joint and we smoked it, watching the sun go down. The desert shuddered like an old man on a parkbench in the winter ... and God, I felt like an abandoned old man.\n4. Flashback Three\u2014Bombay Gin...Goodbye Molly...Whistling \u201cMoon River\u201d....Going Down Very Fast and Very Soft...The Beginning of the End...Old Bull Lee and the First Real Acid Trip in America...Drinking Alcoholics and Bugpowder...The Mexican Connection And The Wrong Man.\nI was nineteen when I met $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I'm just a year older now. So I have been on this trip a long time. I hope it's been worth it. I hope it's been worth the long trip. What I mean is: what if you become terribly famous...", "2_2_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>. The sounds of the gym were terrible.\nSykes was in a very poor mood when he came by from the gym in the East Gym locker room at four-fifteen. \u201cWe supposed to be some sort of training facility here or what?\u201d Sykes shouted to everybody and no one in the lobby. \u201cYou playing cards in the lobby, now? Play like shit in the gym for tonight and you\u2019ll be playing like shit in the lobby, too.\u201d\n\u201cNever,\u201d Lang said in the back of the room.", "0_12_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve sixteen post meridiem</span>. Alone in that fancy apartment, Lamont Roosevelt sat with both hands clutching the bars of an ornate little cage in which two very small Cuban green parrots were attached by little silver hooks to perches that were cemented into the bars. One parrot was green and yellow, the other blue and red. Both had broken out of their cocoons as fledgling mutants. They crowed whenever a member of the family approached the cage. One, finding it couldn\u2019t reach a squirt of water to clean itself, flew to the inside of the wire dome and cawed frantically.\nRoosevelt was reading a truly horrible book by a celebrated Harvard psychologist about man\u2019s repression of his aggressive instincts, to protect himself from the world\u2014as distinct from his need for love, without which he becomes a misfit in modern society. The book appealed to Roosevelt no more than the parrots in their cage appealed to him.\nOn the floor, in a pool of white rays from a full moon, lay the sullen form of a gigantic German shepherd, big as a calf.\nThe massive creature was named $FIRSTNAMEB1. Roosevelt was paying seventy-five dollars a week for room and board to a model, Dolores (\"Lolee...", "1_9_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nI decided to skip church.\n19. Find a place where the grass is green...\nLas Vegas is the only town in the Western world where you can't buy a street by name. There are some other towns that don't do this, but only one other town in America with a population over 100,000. That town is Oxnard, California, just outside Los Angeles\u2014and the reason you can't buy streets in Oxnard is that Oxnard is a lot of little towns joined together, a 'real-estate squid,' and they don't want you buying a thing that doesn't belong to them. But Vegas where the streets are not only named but numbered (and lettered too, alphabetic streets so far being beyond the town's capacity for selfhate)\u2014Vegas still remains inviolate: no private ownership of streets that would give the owners power over access. You can't own a piece of Las Vegas; you can rake in profits from it for a while, buy up its children and build fearsome edifices of stone, but you can't own it. You can't put your name on it: the street. And this is one of the cleanest and freshest ideas in America, one of the few places...", "2_3_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe village of Skra Devenport, in the valley of Sevier Valley, was a bedroom community, had been a bedroom community for forty-two years. The sprawling stone houses were Early American. Most of them dated back to the first day Skra Devenport had ever been Skra Devenport.\nThe most recent houses\u2014those constructed since World War II\u2014were made of manufactured materials. These structures were much smaller, much less tasteful, and substantially less expensive than the stone houses.\nA class distinction was evident in Skra Devenport.\n$SURNAME2 and Prudence Clynn lived in a large stone house on Sage Road.\nThe Vance Warren Apartment House, Skra Devenport, was a nineteen-apartment building on Carolina Road. The apartment building faced a chicken farm on the far side of Carolina Road.\nThe Vance Warren Apartment House had been constructed only four years before the time of this story.", "1_2_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span> when I found an empty parking space on the sidewalk in front of my house in Laurel Canyon. The house was utterly dark. I sat in the car for a moment\u2014thinking about nothing in particular\u2014and suddenly I realized something: There was absolutely no sound, nothing at all, not even the cry of a coyote . . . . Silence had fallen with the darkness. All the night people were indoors. Death had come to the desert. Even the moths were still.\nIt was time to go inside\u2014but for a moment I sat there, listening to the silence and thinking about noise. Today is a noise\u2014mad world,a world in which we not only work and play, but also love, read, hide, fear, along with the comforting whine of generators, screech of tires and roar of motor vehicles; a world in which we talk not only to our loved ones, but also to our hat, shirt, car and newspaper; a world in which almost everybody seems to need or want not only an automobile, but also a radio. At all times and in almost every place, we seem to find spare time and energy not only for talking, but also for listening to all kinds of noises that we ourselves are making, which means either the radio...", "2_8_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 stared in the triple mirror, fascinated by all that the mirrors had to tell him about his image.\nHe was all planes, receding at sharp, unexpected angles.\nHe was the color of dirty alpaca.\nFrom his forehead, horribly, impossibly, scant tendrils of bright gold curled.\nThere were two round scars on his noble brows, marking the place where the probes had gone in during the middle-age revivifications. These scars were pink, glistening.\nThere was a dark gold mustache, uneven, as though the hair that grew in under the neglected upper lip had been left uncut for a generation.\nThere was the faintest suggestion of jowls and wattles. There were the watery eyes of self-made men, the eyes of textile salesmen, used-car salesmen, men who seldom meet the things they sell. There were the tiny lines around the eyes, marking the paths the tears of more passionate moments had taken, making those moments stand out in life-long brooding retrospect.", "0_11_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the port of Mars, Ger- many.\nThe ship was unloading.\nOn the boat, the spirit of anarchy had broken loose.\nThe mate in charge rang for the engineer, William $SURNAME0, to shut off the forward winch. He was not taken in by the counterfeit voice on the public-address system. He knew that the orders were coming from a knot of troublemakers on the bridge.\nA Martian officer in a Skipley suit drew a 9-mm. pistol. \"Let\u2019s see you shut it off,\" he said to the mate.\nThe mate gestured feebly toward a yard-wide switch on the panel. Unwit- tingly, his forefinger pointed to it.\n\"You want I should turn her off, sir?\" said the mate.\n\"I don\u2019t want you to do anything,\" said the Martian officer. He had judged correctly that the mate was not part of the mutiny. Mates are not part of mutinies\u2014particularly United States Merchant Marine mates. They are too virtuous.\n\"Oh, hell,\" said the mate, \"if it\u2019s going to make anybody that badly\u2014\" He stepped over to the switch, flipped...", "0_2_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nTemporary Sheriff Thompson had been killed at twenty-seven minutes past one.\nOnly four minutes had passed.\nBut those four minutes seemed like hours to the men who stood around the dead bodies of the three law officers.\nOld man Metz, who had always wanted to command men in a fight and show what he was made of, was no longer intrepid. Old man Metz, who had been so eager to go down in history as a hero, was now ready to offer himself a peace emissary to the forces in Imperial.\nOld man Metz now saw his life ahead of him\u2014and could only think of the horrors of senility that must come after the glow of youth is gone. What would it be like, old man Metz wondered, to look in the mirror and see the leathery sag of old skin on a skull no longer supported by the resilience of youth? To feel the first stiffness of age in the veins of the legs, in the beat of the pulse? The first spells of senility, so that days would be confused with nights, and nights with days? Then the ultimate horror upon the final day: to look in the mirror and see only a skull looking bleakly back at you from a wrink...", "0_21_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-nine in the evening</span> of June twenty-third. The place was a chamber of horrors called The $SURNAME0 estate, forty-nine miles north of New York City. The estate was bounded on three sides by roads, on the fourth by the Hudson River. Every road was given broad access to the estate.\nThe service road ended in a wooden arch with the words VISITORS ARE ALWAYS welcome. The arch was supported on the shoulders of two stone figures symbolizing Labor and Capital respectively. Labor was a strong, sickle-bearing woman. Capital was a dandyish man in top boots and high-collared coat.\nIn approaching the arch from either direction, the traveler got his first glimpse of a magnificent mansion that stood on a hill. The mansion\u2019s gold-leafed cupolas defied the storms and magnified the moonbeams. To the traveler, shivering in spite of summer, the mansion had all the warmth and richness of the inside of a refrigerator.\nThe mansion had been built and furnished by dollars looted in the name of God from the helpless poor. $SURNAME0 owned all the stock in every company that made anything in the estate\u2014from doorknobs to motorboats.\nThe fortune was tax-free, since $SURNAME0 was...", "2_17_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty in the afternoon</span>.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 now knew why her husband had begged her pardon for one mean thing he didn\u2019t do. Her eyes were watering from having found out. Mr. $SURNAME0 was weeping from having told her.\nNow he showed her the last item.\nIt was a letter written on lavender paper in lavender ink.\nDear $FIRSTNAMEM1\u2014\nIt is wonderful how much nobler it is to be misunderstood than to misunderstand. To be accused than to accuse. To forgive than to prosecute. To endure than to retaliate.\nI wonder which of us is really the luckier\u2014you, who rides the wild beams to glory, or me, who lives quietly, safely, without fear of failure?\nEven if our roles were reversed, dear $SURNAME0, I doubt that I could do as well as you have done. By all the rules, you are a fool\u2014and yet you break all records in demonstrating that the fool shall inherit the earth.\nI have tried to understand you as well as I possibly could. And the reward I have gotten for trying has been the most magnificient of all\u2014real love.\nEunice", "1_16_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-two in the afternoon</span>. The place was a thousand feet over the West Virginia coalfields. Old Nicodemus, wheezing his eighty-three winters, felt a sharp stab in his left shoulder blade, just where he had been shot all those years before by a Yankee picket. The stab became a sharp pain, and the pain became a band of pain tightening and tightening around his body, squeezing his breath shorter and shorter as the band grew tighter. He felt another stab in his right calf, just where he had been stabbed by a Rebel bayonet on that long-ago April day a few weeks before the end of it. Although the stabs and the band pains continued to come and go, the pain around his body became fixed at its tightest, and Nicodemus was suddenly very afraid of death. He forgot his homeland, but he remembered everything else, and he knew that each of his senses would still work independently of the others when he was dead. He knew that the sunlight, yellow and strong and exciting, would hurt his eyes more terribly than it hurt them even now, in the middle of the afternoon, in a November sky, with the thermometer 13 degrees below zero. He knew, with the reasoning of a simpleminded man, that the...", "1_18_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>, and according to the Friendly Ballcock Company's installed clock, the Moon was full. \"I can't go with you, fellers,\" said the Chief Mineral Water Taster, wiping his mouth with his sleeve. He grabbed hold of the sides of his barrel and rolled over to the door of the little hut. \"I guess it\u2019s time I was getting back inside.\" And he returned to the big fancy mansion.\nThe Executives of the Friendly Ballcock Company watched him go. Then they laughed raucously, stood around looking at their watches, and waited for their Chief Executive Dispenser to come back with a little clear plastic box filled with Friendly Ballcock Company gold watches.\nAfter ten minutes, their Chief Executive Dispenser sheepishly returned from the mile-long trek to the neighborhood looter\u2019s shantytown. There was not a watch in his little clear plastic box. \"You let that thief get the best of you?\" screamed one of the Executives.\n\"He said he was thirsty!\" said the Chief Executive Dispenser.\n\"Hell, take one of mine!\" cried an Executive. \"Take two!\" another roared.\n\"Make us all a little drunk!\" chimed in another.\n\"I...", "2_16_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. She had three minutes to dress, and go down to the lobby.\nShe fled to her room and fumbled into jeans, checked shirt, sneakers. At the last second she remembered the recorder and sprinted back in the shower for it, turning it on as she did so. She ran nearly out of tape on the way down to the lobby, and had to tuck the loose spool under a elastic band that kept her hair in place. $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas, she looked in the lobby monocle, still looking for this morning\u2019s Stan the janitor. She caught a glimpse of tail feathers, away toward the parking lot. She headed outside and was about to get into the car when she saw, dead down at sidewalk level, those tiny reflected glasses. Instinctively she put her finger into her mouth and whistled, looking away and up above the buildings across the street. There, a few lethargic vertical turns later, came the marvelous upland bird, a miniature, humanoid, or humanimprimeed hawk, She watched it circle once overhead and disappear again. Then, baffled and impatient, she had to run for her commuter train.", "2_23_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-nine in the evening</span>.\nShe lay on her back on the hardness of the top rim of the bed. She let her arms fall limply at her sides. She relaxed. She remembered, wistfully and wickedly, an icthmus along the Nile. The Nile was green, just as the algae-laced pool outside was green. A young Egyptian purser, a short, lyrical young Egyptian purser, was pushing her along the uthmuses. $FIRSTNAMEF0 had never had so much fun in her life. She owed it all to $SURNAME0, who, as he always did, had furnished all the fun for her. It was gratifying to $FIRSTNAMEF0 to know that the fun she was having was of major importance to God.\nHer memories were interrupted by a sound that was barely audible. It was made by her son $FIRSTNAMEM2, who was on the metal top of a bench that had been placed across a row of long windows at the far end of the room. $FIRSTNAMEM2 was twelve feet away from $FIRSTNAMEF0. On the bench under him was what remained of the golden ship.", "2_2_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> in Washington, D. C.\nThe mean temperature for the Earth had risen three degrees above the lowest point.\nAnd it was one of those things that Washington could do about.\nWashington could do about anything that was connected with the summer-winter solstice. A two-minute difference in time could wobble the solstice a whole two days from its proper date. The two and a half million inhabitants of Washington were therefore subjected, year in and year out, to a whole two days\u2019 worth of eye-strain. Even Miss Eleanor Roosevelt had had to get glasses, a fact that had remained secret for two years.\nThe President of the United States was not a man whose imagination was stirred by the mysteries of the sky. He was a man who, in his own words, had never really looked at the sky until he was forty, \"the old age at which most people begin to do a lot of looking at the sky.\"\nThe man to whose imagination the mysteries of the sky did stir, the most vivid imagination in the world, was planning to stir them for at least six more months.", "2_15_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-three in the afternoon</span>. I just can't say for sure what happened next . . . .\nThe thing to remember, if you want to understand what happened next, is that I was invisible. Nobody saw me. Not the girl, not the owners of the trailer, not the bum in the red Caddy in the next campsite: nobody. I vanished completely from reasonable society at the age of twenty\u2014seven, and nobody even noticed. Not until the end. Not my family, not my aunts, my uncles, my cousins, my brother\u2014in\u2014law, not old friends like Willie Dalls at law school. Hell, not even old enemies. Not until I was three hundred miles away, fighting with a hooker in a red\u2014and\u2014gray shack by the side of the road in Oregon.\n\u201cCan I get dressed?\u201d the girl asked finally.", "1_3_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-one in the morning</span>, Saturday, August 27,1983, and my attorney was seventy-six miles south in a very hot intensive care unit. I had a Strange car, seven bucks, and the FBI on my case. I,was in! no mental condition to face a race against death down U,S,. Ninety to the Mustang.\nBy the time I got to the fourth floor, the only possible move had occurred to me. I tumbled the baggage handler an extra five, because he weighed and disztributed my bags by hand--charging the extra five to \u201cMister Duke\u2019s room,.himself. No receipt necessary, because I,was obviously a fine upstanding Southern gentleman, no trouble at all, and besides, we all knew Floridaincrafton.\nI wanted to go faster, but there was no getting around the slow computer routine. I had to match the baggage tags with the registration cards, check and double-check, then go downstairs and out to the car and get more bags while the elevator attendant called down to the desk and checked them out. But at least we had established contact, and there was no further question of my checking out on another floor. I was where I was, besieged by hell in a psycho ward...", "0_8_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAnything could happen.\nI waited by myself in my office.\nAt a quarter past eight, a group of young men and women in business suits came out of the elevator and into my office.\nThey were carrying musical instruments.\nOne of the men carried a snare drum.\nThey were the staff and the music section of the Review-Journal 's young-college division.\nOne of the babes was a good-looking blonde in eye-shade and green skirt, who played the French horn.\nA great many young people have gotten their start in newspaper work, in America, by way of the medium of the campus newspaper. I, myself, was one of these. I made my bones on the Michigan Chronicle , the University of Michigan newspaper. I have always been deeply grateful to the Chronicle for having given me a fine, forum for my crude and raucous perspectives on what was then boys' basketball.\nNow it was pay-back time. The reboppers were in charge. The world belonged to them, and they were ready and eager to give the other 51% of the population all the kicks, short-shifts and rotten deals the Situation afforded. The Game of Life was a perpetual Fourth Down and Two...", "2_16_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. They were walking down the street together, still ten blocks from the bar where the conference was really happening. Humboldt took her hand, squeezed it. \"To tell you the truth, I'm a lobbyist...that's where I seen you, at Pat Muir\u2019s party the other night. My name's Humboldt; I'm with the Feed Control League of California.\"\n\u201cFeed Control?\u201d said Goldie.\n\u201cRight,\u201d said Humboldt. \u201cWe're worried about all these farmer fuckers starting fires and shooting out windows.\"\n\u201cHell, that's terrible,\u201d said Goldie.\n\u201cYou're goddamn right it's terrible,\u201d said Humboldt. \u201cWe gotta pass some kind of law, start incarcerating those bastards right away, or we're never gonna get the drouth under control.\"\n\u201cWell, it sounds important,\u201d said Goldie. \u201cWhere's your conference?\"\nHumboldt glanced around, then put his hand on Goldie's ass. \"Well...right here, I guess.\"\n\u201cYou mean right here on the street?\"", "2_7_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-six in the morning</span>.\nThe time was half past ten in the morning.\nThe man approached the watering trough in the lunchroom of the Ramble. The watering trough was a fifty-gallon animal-feeder with an inverted trough at the bottom. People who wanted to wash their hands put their hands in at the top, and water dripped out at the bottom.\nThe man assumed that there would be water in the trough.\nThere was no water in the trough.\nGrowling and snapping, the man fed ten cents into the animal-trough\u2019s dispensing machine.\nThe machine rained twenty-five cents\u2019 worth of quarters into the trough.\nRicardo Carlos Liberius pierced the floating corpses with his sharp stake. The stake splashed in blood. A quarter, plus three-quarters of a quarter, came floating to the surface of the water in the trough.\nThe man saw the mischievous face of a child, pressed against the window of a TV junk-food parlor. The face pressed a button in the window. A mechanical bumblebee zoomed from an artificial flower and stung the back of the man\u2019s neck.", "1_22_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>, and then\u2014and then\u2014and then\u2014\nSix\nThe man in the mackinaw, alone in the room with $SURNAME0, was young and had a weak chin. He was clean-shaven, wore horn-rimmed glasses, and knew a lot of the words $SURNAME0 had written in his little red book. He was the young and very popular Mayor of Hollywood, California, and his name was Arnold $FIRSTNAMEM1.\nHe had been invited to $SURNAME0\u2019s little party by the man in the Sherlock Holmes suit\u2014a party that was still to take place, a party that $SURNAME0 had tried hard to avoid, a party that $SURNAME0 had stayed away from so well, until the invitation arrived in the mail of the space ship carrying $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, and the young man\u2019s namesake. By means of the dog, Skip, and the materialization of the invitation in the young man\u2019s pocket, $SURNAME0 had contrived to bring the young man to his home just one hour before the party was to start.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s purpose in bringing the young man to his home one hour early was to blunt the most primitive and offensive of the...", "1_16_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was the one-thousand-one-hundred-seventy-first day of the year, and the eight-hundred-twenty-second (or was it the eight-hundred-twenty-third?) since the heater had begun. $SURNAME1, who did the adding and got them right, believed the iron city had soared in space for two thousand or so years.\n$SURNAME1, who was ten, went on playing with his stamp album.\nThere were several pictures in the album. $SURNAME1 played with the album by himself because his father had told him never to let any of his precocious friends see the pictures. Una, who was twelve, was precocious, and so was Calf, who was eight.\nThe shameful pictures were of things that were supposed to happen to women, after they died. There were whale skeletons, too.\nUna was brushing her long, beautiful hair, to make it shine for Calf, who was coming up from the offices to see her in her white suit and new shoes. She wasn\u2019t really brushing her hair. She was talking to Calf, who was coming up in the dumbwaiter.\nCalf was delighted when Una let him into the...", "1_6_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>; the place was New York City; thefellow was an Italian thug who drove an ice-cream truck for a living\u2014and who, at that moment, was bellying his Ford up alongside the kerb on Lexington Avenue, waiting for a signal to turn left and start cruising up the Avenue.\nHe was a sandblasted-looking character, but the sand was mostly long since ground smooth by the merciless grindings of the city. He was about fifty-two years of age, wiry and thin and pockmarked, and his black hair was spooned out on top in a pompadour that pushed his eyes into uncrossable shadow below. He had a gun strapped under his arm\u2014a big, Smith and Wesson .45\u2014and carried a nightstick of black oak. Around his waist, with a leather tie, he wore his ice-cream trade passes, and on his upper arm he wore a derby\u2014his only greasy \u201cuniform,\u201d discounting a tux he put on at nightclubs that hired their ice-cream cuisine from him. He always claimed his tux was \u201cregulation\u201d formal, but in fact it was probably bought from a second\u2014hand store.\nHe was woefully...", "1_0_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until one o'clock in the morning</span>. The ship was thirty miles above Earth. There was no sound inside it\u2014no air to carry sound.\nThe field of vision in Plate 13 shows nothing but the dreaming face of Grego in the St. Regis lobby.\nA Royal Bengal Tiger Escapes\nThere were times when $FIRSTNAMEB0 did not hate what he was. Those times were all the same. There were no seasons to the moloch that was $FIRSTNAMEB0, no sunrise or sunset to its days. It knew no moon.\nWhat $FIRSTNAMEB0 called happy times were those in which it remembered that it was really a clean, well-fed boy with a good mother and father at the beginning. The taste of Earth in those memories was magically delicious. The memory of a pet kitten was so sweetly painful as to be touching and original.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB0 thought about that kitten, he thought about the man who used to come and take the kitten away for experiments. He longed for the experiments to be completed, so that the man would bring the kitten back again. He would imagine the man bringing in the limp, cold kitten under his arm, and saying, \"I\u2019m sorry, old fellow, but the good of science, you know.\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 had identified himself...", "0_16_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-six in the afternoon</span> and the sun was beginning to set. It was a wonderful golden-orange sunset. I was glad it was going down, because if it had not been going down $FIRSTNAMEB3 would have been mad and sad. He was terribly sad whenever the sun went down and passionately glad whenever it came up. I had known $FIRSTNAMEB3 for years, ever since high school and even before, but only now, as we rode along this final sixty miles, I began to see the exact shape of his madness. He spent all his time and energy thinking about things, and the more he thought about them the farther out he got, until finally he had thought so far out into the circle and become so roller coaster that nothing could hold him down or make him normal. He blew like a leaf in the wind; a piece of straw floating on top of a hot air balloon. He told me once it was just where I'd end up too, and a lot of other things too, and so I was already prepared. In fact, I was a little bit glad too, because this wavering back and forth between madness and intelligence reminded me of my own position and my own way of life. And it was just as well to know where I stood once and for all. I had already made up...", "2_7_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was a shambles of smoking blue mist.\nTheMistyazurium had been vaporized.\nThe Newport Tower had toppled into a massive shallow trench that had been dug in the playground. The trench was four feet deep, twelve feet wide.\nThe trench was three hundred yards long.\nA school bus had been caught in the trench.\nIts tires had dug the three hundred yards of trench, had dug the trench deeper and deeper as the bus was driven in a tight circle.\nThe driver of the bus was a child, a child named Vargas, who had been sent to school in the bus every morning, regardless of the weather.\nVargas was still at the wheel of the bus. His flesh had melted. His eyes had popped.\nBefore the trench, as far as the eye could see in all directions, were puddles of a clear, pure honey.\nAt the far end of the playground, at the foot of the butte, the weak morning light struck Ex thin white pillars of steam jetting up out of the ground.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 strode out of the steam, strode out of the haze, strode out of the thin white columns.", "0_2_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span> and I was looking at a fine slab of the world. It was a scene from the first epoch of evolution, when aquatic plants had not yet developed the capacity to photosynthesize and everything ate sulfurous bacteria. The spongy peninsula stuck out of Manila Bay like a baby's foot neglected by some psychopathic obstetrician. In the moonlight the outlines of things became soft, as if a dreamwerewearer lent his silver shears to Samson and Delilah and they came to the Island Retreat to get new haircuts. In that delicately shaded unreal world, iron posts stuck out of the sea like pencils stuck out of the ground in 2 A.M. scribbling exercises. The posts supported heavy chains that disappeared into the bay. They were there to keep the wooden bungalows and moorage away from the claws of the octopus that haunted those waters while they might. Disembodied voices called out in the night from rowboats whose passengers ferreted amid the dark weeds below the illuminated water. Visible in the distance were the trailing white wakes of ships steaming for Manila, a glowing necklace in the neck of a dying rotted corpse whose head was the city. If a ghoulish eye on a stalk...", "1_11_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of the twenty-fifth of December when the concierge of the tenants of the Boulevard Suchet carried the folio case filled with securities into the room where the lawyer had been waiting an hour. He was clean shaven now. He had on a fresh white suit. He looked fresh, eager, happy, nervous.\n\"Well?\" he said.\n\"Just as you told me.\"\n\"I know,\" said the lawyer, patting the concierge on the arm. \"I am satisfied.\" He dipped into his pocket for a coin.\n\"No,\" said the concierge. \"No fare. Good-by, sir.\"\n\"I\u2019ll see you to-night, my friend,\" said the lawyer, shutting the door. He put the case of securities on the table, reached into his pocket, took out a letter, wrote on the envelope: \"I have the honor to inform you that the affair you entrusted to me has been settled. With my condolences,\" and put it in the mail-box. There was nothing pressing now. He could walk over to the caf\u00e9 quite slowly and eat something, and reach the place where the motors were waiting just before they started. He was perfectly calm. He wiped his face with...", "1_8_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIt was nine minutes later in the morning. The alarum went off again.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 looked at her husband. She looked at him sleepily.\nAn expressionless look came into her face, an empty look that let the light come through. And she sank slowly into the bed.\nAt one minute after nine o'clock in the morning, a siren began to scream outside in the street. $FIRSTNAMEM1 felt a hand seize his ankle.\nThe alarm was going off again. It was getting on toward nine o\u2019clock. The sunlight was pouring into the bedroom. There was a heavy smell of vinegar.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 woke up. The scream of the siren had shoved him awake. He opened his eyes and looked INTO THE MOUTH OF A FUNNEL.\nThere was an arm in front of his face and attached to it a familiar, yellowish-brown sergeant\u2019s hand. As he came up out of sleep he had lifted his head instinctively and the hand had slapped him down again and was holding him down, as it had a few hours earlier in the laboratory. He moved his head SIDEWAYS, and looked INTO THE MOUTH OF A FUNNEL OF PALE BLONDE...", "0_23_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until midnight</span>, and the Columbia Buffet, though nearly deserted, seemed to our lycanthrope eyes excellently adapted for his use. Its long, low, spreading, many-paned room was divided in the middle by a counter. Behind it, smiling, nodding, curious, two or three waiters moved discreetly among the empty tables, picking up chips and pouring off dregs of wine, rattling their changes, their boots creaking in the decorous hush. Behind the other portion of the room, at the tables, were the twenty-five scattered and isolated members of the bridge club and their guest, the Duchess of Baltimoore. The bridge tables, surrounded by four highbacked Victorian chairs with red plush seats, were set any old how, and each group of four seats about the table held a score or so of red-backed black and white cards. And there was the Duchess herself, in an unmistakable manner, with her duster of white hair, her plums of cheeks, and black mourning cutaway with white polka dots and pink-lined scarlet Cavalier shirt. Her maid of honor was there, too\u2014a beautiful woman, most nobly featured, most subtly endowed with strength and character, who, we were...", "2_8_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 drew five paces back from the front of the column of Martian soldiers. He braced with both hands against the small of his back, making himself rigid, keeping himself strict and at attention. His head was a little higher than the heads of the soldiers in front of him, so that his line of sight ran up over their heads.\nHis curiosity was not good for discipline, but it was exactly regulated by discipline. He was so curious about the things around him that he kept himself at a constant level of mild titillation. It was not titillation as other soldiers might have titillation\u2014by fingering a naked girl on a bus or opening up a flask of Southern Comfort in the liquor store on payday.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s titillation was of pure, distilled procedural beauty\u2014the sort of titillation he might have felt as a field mathematician, had he suddenly discovered a perfect proof of some abstruse theorem up there on the blackboard in the drafty lecture hall.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s titillation was almost ecstasy, but it was a cold, pure ecstasy, far superior to the blind animal bliss of a dog mounting a bitch.", "1_19_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe Martians looked out at Earthling desolation, their number constantly growing as more and more of their spaceships arrived. The Martians saw the smoke of the explosion that had been Indianapolis.\n\"Boom-boom,\" said one Martian.\n\"It would seem,\" said another, \"that trouble is already beginning.\"\nThe first Martian looked at his watch. The watch was an Earthling thing, adjusted to Martian time, which was 36 minutes slower than Earthling time. The Martian had to watch Earthling television, to which he was addicted, to know what Earthling time was.\nEarthling time was now 8: 04 P.M. by the Martian\u2019s watch.\nThe Martian looked out over the desolation again. \"Ten o\u2019clock Eastern Standard Time,\" he said. \"The program will be starting in just a few minutes. Don\u2019t miss the National Dog and Country-and-Western Band Wagon Show.\" He looked up at Earth. \"You can kiss my big red ass, Indianapolis,\" he said. \" \u2018The dogs and cats will be glad to see us.\u2019 \"\n\"You watch television, Stony?\" another Martian asked, coming up.\n\"Right...", "1_0_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past midnight</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 made his announcement in the television room. Stony and the other noncombatants were awake, and in the case of Stony, still dressing in the dark, when $FIRSTNAMEB1 thumped on the wall to get their attention. The harmoniums played \"Shave and a Haircut,\" also in the dark. The harmoniums were harmoniums no more. At the switch of a lever they were more properly called golems, being creatures made of sticky clay, powered by knocks.\nThe dark came alive with illuminated glows, revealing mad $FIRSTNAMEB1 in the doorway. He had news from the receiver. He invited everyone to join him in the command post in ten minutes.\n\"What the hell is this?\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 asked his mate.\n\"I\u2019ve got a funny feeling about it,\" said Stony. She was sitting on the bed. \"I\u2019ve got a funny feeling,\" she said again. The bed suddenly rose a foot in the air, then came down with a crash that shook the wall to the left of the door-way.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hand went to the butt of his Martian pistol. He felt like shooting somebody. The somebody he visualized most clearly was handsome, w...", "2_3_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three seventeen in the morning</span>. The Supreme Pontiff had certain information that he shared with Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"You know the Lord Almighty is coming down to Earth, right?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"So I\u2019ve been told,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"You know it\u2019s extremely significant, don\u2019t you?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"I\u2019ve been given to understand so,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"What\u2019s your opinion?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"I haven\u2019t formed any opinions,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"Then please let me inform you that there couldn\u2019t possibly be anything more important for mankind, or for you personally, than the present situation,\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"In that case,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4, \"maybe I will have one of these doughnuts.\"\nThe time was three thirty-two in the morning. Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 remained at the table, eating doughnuts and drinking coffee, with the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"Keep me advised,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4, \"on how things break.\"\n\"What is your postal address?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.", "2_21_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-one in the evening</span>.\n/d/\n\u201cI should think you\u2019d be hoarse by now.\u201d\n\u201cNot at all, not at all. Rather the contrary, in fact. I find my voice to be emanating at a\u2014\u201d\n\u201cYou do appear to have a tenacious voice, R.V.\u201d\n\u201cHow are you about Dad and Vlad\u2019s commercials?\u201d\n\u201cI think they\u2019re great. I keep a tape of them at all times, at home. I record them from television. I got Dad\u2019s Animal Vindaloo Mating Demo off the Home Shopping Network just last week.\u201d\n\u201cBut I imagine animals have been selling indian food for years.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m not talking about real animals selling real indian food for years, Rick. Not like having a howler monkey, who again we\u2019re informed has been a classroom pet at some East Coast university, lay an order for some really really spicy indian food. You see the whole little legend thing, in which Vlad the Impaler explains that he has first to order in Sanskrit? Sanskrit, Rick.\u201d", "0_1_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nHe was not Mike, who wanted more and more.\nAfter the first and second orgasms, Jessie slept.\nShe slept on her left side, as he slept.\nWhen she was able to hear again she heard the wind. She was a good gauge of the weather. The wind, blowing away from the house, came wet and chill, with the sour silt of an approaching storm.\nLeonard snored on his back, one arm crooked over his head.\nShe would sleep as long in the morning as she could. The life with Mike had been lived largely in bed; for that she was not equipped.\nTwo men walked through the night toward them. Approached from the distance, at first they seemed to drift, so slow was their progress across the surface of the dark earth. Their feet flung casts of chalky pallor onto the black ocean of grass around them.\nThe tide was out.\nIn the house, in the bedroom, upstairs, Jessie lay sleeping with the angel. The men, on their approach, declared themselves human by becoming shapes of breathing heat on the dark planetary cool of the grass.\n\"Hello there,\" one said loudly.\nHe singled out his objective in space. He shouted as...", "0_23_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven sixteen in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 covered his face with his hands.\n\u201cNow wait till I think, $FIRSTNAMEB6, till I think of all the kicks I ever had, and all the kicks I ever'll have. Eh? above everything else I'm a kicks-man, and the man who kicks the whole world, wow! is the Saint Whoever --they call it God -- up to the hilt. It's beyond the Mississippi now. I've talked to God and I know. I can see him with my spiritual eye, and across the river too, and everything is dissolved and plain as day. The eyes open, she appears, and a little synthetic Mexican girl out of an ampule and I slip her in my pocket and go on. Yes, and I underneath! Ah faugh! I say this vehicle has been too crowded, I'm going to the Hudson Hotel and get some sleep.\u201d\n\u201cAnd you'll drive to the Coast?\u201d\n\u201cYes!\u201d\n\u201cAnd you'll be in Los Angeles in a few days?\u201d\n\u201cYes!\u201d\nHe rushed out of the restaurant, and I had to scuttle with him across the street and go first to look for matches in the office with...", "1_22_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten nineteen in the evening</span>. Old Mason had finally come. He it was who stood near by somewhere in the dark, listening. From far away in the dark came another sound. It was a mellow, cooing sound. It was a California painters\u2019 joke: a turkey, with both of its legs broken, still living and wondering what to do. It had been run over by a Valley Road bus some years before and had lain there, misused and infirm. It was the sound of the dying bird. Its wild, meadow life was over. Meantime old Mason stood balancing his ledgers in the moonlight by his office window, standing near by somewhere watching silently, the great merchant, the empire-builder of the West.\nSimilar to other seekers of commercial empire who live in the homes they build on the panoramas they scan, he lived in a very fine home on top of a high hill. His son Peyton, a Princeton Ph.D. in psychology, lived in another, an excellent home down the hill, nearer town. The two homes, on the brow and the bottom of the hill, were like bookends.\nTogether they indicated the rise, the rise to one thousand, of the Fitzgerald Furniture Company, which owned the...", "1_9_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-nine in the morning</span>, and beatniks were getting breakfast together in their pads in the neighborhood. Suddenly the ears of some were opened; in an Instant the word was passed, and a terrible wail of lamentation rose from all amidst the frying of bacon and eggs. Exactly what had happened wasn't clear, because nobody knew. Some swore they saw a new star in the heavens, others a horrible change in the position of the Sun or Moon. And nobody minded. Man wasn't ready and afraid to die, and so lay down and took a nap. Around eleven o'clock in the morning Harry Copeland stood up and stretched in his room on Brackett Street, in Cambridge. A graduate student in English literature at Harvard, he was twenty-seven years old, had scraggly blond hair and a nice sense of humor. He had been steady, and a little dull, until he met Stokely Carmichael, who had come to Harvard to lecture -- Stokely Carmichael from SNCC, from Simplified Marxist Leninism, from the riots in Albany, Georgia. They had met, and an earthquake had taken place inside Harry Copeland. All of his dull and proper ways were now gone. He didn't care about school any more, much less about girls, politics...", "2_4_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>. The Japanese businessman was no longer with them. He had long since been knocked out of the game. But they decided to look down their long line of luck. They moved far down the casino to the very last blackjack table.\nThis table was a last resort, far away from crowds and an express lane to the cashier. They were now betting two hands of seven\u2014card blackjack for a maximum of $3000 a hand. They blew through $17,000 in about twenty minutes.\nThis time the shuffle was about to end up in chaos. The woman's rack was a jumble of hundreds, fifties, twenties and other denominations. She had to make a calculation:\nHow much did I have? How much did I bet? How much did I win? How much did I lose? How much did I have left? What is my net worth?\nThis simple little addition\u2014this one nightmare of countless nightmares of Las Vegas\u2014seemed to stagger her. She took her purse and began stuffing greenbacks into it, looking vaguely ahead all the while. She took the money and just pushed it in, eyes wandering.", "0_6_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-one in the morning</span>, according to the green glowing figures on $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s billion-year chronometer.\nThe space was a bus stop on a curving street in Greenwich Village.\nThe atmosphere was gay.\nThe area\u2019s thirty-four thousand residents were just getting up to the big, big day. A block away, loudspeakers broadcast brisk music and a man\u2019s laugh, which seemed to say, \"Helloooooo, neighbor! Hellooooo, neighbor! Hellooooo, neighbor!\" The music had a Latin-American beat.\nA block away, very much in evidence, and in many respects dominant over the whole neighborhood, was the huge, towering, dark and blind multidomed structure known as the $FIRSTNAMEF0- presses, turbines, microphones, loudspeakers, thousands of offices, lots of tunnels, and in general the headquarters of the decentralization movement in the western world.\nThis tower rose a full fifteen hundred feet above the curving street, which here began its ascent of the tower\u2019s flank. On the tower\u2019s roof was a helicopter pad, and a cafeteria, and a barber shop, and a ski-lodge, and a museum containing statues of $FIRSTNAMEM2 Rector and Helvi Rame...", "0_5_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>. I moved my right hand across Campbell's abdomen very slowly and reached for the bottle. \u201cI've got to calm her down a bit,\u201d I mumbled. \u201cShe'll go out of her mind.\u201d I took another good drink, leaned back and rubbed my eyes. The girl had slid down to the floor; she was crouched there trembling.\nFor a moment I considered trying to hypnotize both of them so I could escape, but decided against it. I was falling asleep anyway. I rolled over on my stomach, to get in the comfortable position for escape that Doctor Brutton had taught me: with the head downhill, and both hands up over the eyes.\nI must have slept at least an hour. When I woke up I felt more or less normal, although somewhat annoyed with myself and with everybody else in the universe. I looked at my watch. It was almost seven o'clock.\nShifting my eyes over to the sleeping figures in the bed, I tried to remember the night before and figure out what had happened.\nThere was Great Scott, in the bed, stark naked. That was before dinner. So it couldn't have been anything too serious. I smiled. What a souse I...", "0_16_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-four in the afternoon</span> and the first thing I had to do was feed my wife and drop Terry off at her kindergarten and then get over to North Las Vegas and hunt down Roy Johnson, Lennie's partner, in the bungalow court where they both lived. I was a free man, out of the drunk tank, with both cars and a full tank of gas. I fed my family, kissed Terry goodbye, and started off in my big fat Ford, feeling wonderful. I had all the time in the world to find Roy Johnson and put the whole thing together. A growl of desire roared up in my throat. Gambling! For two years I'd been afraid to touch it. Always walking around the edges, watching the fauna with eagle eyes, fearing I'd mingle with the wrong element and get busted. Now I was licensed, sworn in, given the star and the .38, with every legal right to make all kinds of waves in the world of the Western gambler. Oh, the thing to do first was simply to find Roy Johnson and make the contact. Relax, and then maybe integrate myself into the feverish activity on the floor at the Desert Inn. If I could dig up something quick and get my hands on somebody else's money, then I could...", "1_7_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven seventeen in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 hurried into the space ship.\nHe ran little danger of being seen by the search parties that were looking for him. The search parties seemed to be skulking in ditches and behind trees. They were commando-style, a sniveling blizzard of them, and they weren\u2019t sticking their heads up unless they had to. The idea that was going around in their happy little heads\u2014an idea they had sucked in like oxygen\u2014was that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was out to kill them.\nAnd they would have been right, had he gotten the iron horse working again. $FIRSTNAMEB0 hoped to God he could get the iron horse working again. He was calculating that he would have to fight twenty-seven astronauts to keep the secret of the location of the iron horse to himself. And after he had fought them and killed them all, or disabled them, or whatever he did to them, he and the loyalists among the recruits were going to have to be ready to fight off the other survivors from the ship. The survivors would come back in formation, in search planes. As the song had it:\nThere\u2019ll be a bright silver bird upon a red white blue,\nAnd you\u2019ll come back riding on that ladder...", "2_23_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven nineteen in the evening</span>.\nRumor Redux\nGlimpsed momentarily through a long tunnel of galvanized iron pipe, the Marblehead MA 21 outboard motor- launch made a good, false image of youth. The picture was silvery-bright, clean and resilient. It caught your breath be neath its outstretched wings, made you believe in the magic bird\u2019s intention to fly. The image blinked out. The thing really was the product of a two-hundred-and-fifty-thousand-dollar fabrication contract entered into by the General Forge and Foundry Company.\nThe real thing measured thirty-four feet in length by thirty-nine inches in width by twelve inches in height. On its bottom were two Chrysler industrial 440 cubic inch, 52-degree V8 engines. The engines developed 1,500 brake horsepower at 12,000 r.p.m.\nThe shaft horsepower was estimated at 2,800. Prouty and Barnes, consulting engineers, agreed that the Marblehead could make thirty knots.", "1_18_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>, calm, clear and pleasant, a night like nearly all the nights of that time of the year in Paris. I was sitting outside the Closerie des Lilas, having a brandy-and-soda before dinner. The street was quiet and pleasant and I was just thinking how romantic it was to be in Paris and think how pleasant it was to be sitting there at that time of the evening, when I saw the little man come out of the doorway of the cr\u00e9merie with two Sevres cups full of chibouque smoke. He was kissing his hand and saying good-by to somebody quite out of sight inside and at the same moment he saw me sitting there at the table outside. He seemed to think it was queer I had not gone inside, and he made a face, laughing, to express the general delight of lovers in all places at all hours, and came toward me carrying the two cups. I suppose he wanted me to go inside and have a good time, too. So I took one of the cups, because really I do like chibouque, when there is not a lot of it, and it is fresh. He went in again to take his kiss and his other good-by, while I sat outside...", "0_2_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two nineteen in the morning</span>. In Las Vegas it was dusk. The time and the date clicked in my head, and for some reason I suddenly remembered a long-forgotten fact I\u2019d learned in high school: The entire continent of North America minus Mexico and the top half of Florida is within the same twenty-four hour time-zone. To use the popular expression: We are all, at this very moment, \u201csun\u2014up to sun\u2014down\u201d; what hangs heavy in the Nevada or Texas air at two nineteen on a Tuesday morning will be hanging heavy in Chicago or Toronto an hour later, and four hours later it will still be hanging over Nova Scotia, the same rotting wind I had inhaled on the back porch at two nineteen, breathing all the land, knowing, in the same way that I knew about the sudden madness of $FIRSTNAMEB3 Moriarty's father in front of the Chevy in the night and about Jane's not loving me, having hit the sleeping, secret center of the continent, lain down on it, understood.\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201cit's been too many hours together. I'm going hitchhike to North Beach.\u201d He went out the gate and tried to thumb a ride. Meanwhile I told...", "1_20_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight nineteen in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic infundibula are difficult to describe, especially since some of the really essential details are unthinkable. A tentative catalogue of some of the phenomena includes the following: images of faraway places on the inside of the eyelids (a continuous Global view); an unaccountable feeling of joy, and a conviction that simple, things, unrationed things are the objects most strongly emphasized by existence (a burning hand, laughter); a voice which whispers, \"Hey, sailor,\" in the inner ear; a sense that all the color has been drained from the world (a nervous system awash in ultraviolet). Some have reported a musky smell. Others speak of the impression that God is about to tap them on the shoulder. Still others describe a feeling of having died.\nThere was also a nineteen-and-a-half-minute period of near darkness and really quite extraordinarily terrifying constriction and crushing, especially of the sexual organs.\nConnie opened her eyes. She sat on the iron cot. She was not dead. She was not in love. She was not, in fact, all that different from the way she had been before she had looked into the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibulum.\nThere was...", "2_2_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-one in the morning</span>. Melinda was sucking her third olive and cracking her second tooth on the Kalif \u2018s tooth. Stone sat transfixed, by all appearances stoned, with an olive brine mustache. Wang-Dang Lang held forth for a change.\n\u201c... we didn\u2019t like rap at first, but now me and the wife just cant get enough of the stuff, like the bushings in my hoist say rock happens and we just look at each other and like dive down in the seat cushions and bob our heads up and down.\u201d\n\u201cAnd you, Wang-Dang?\u201d said Frequent.\nThe question was directed at Vlad the Impaler, who sat looking at his reflection in his veined drink.\n\u201cIt draws a very nice contrast, I think,\u201d Vlad the Impaler said, after a while.\n\u201cAttaboy,\u201d said FV.\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid I don\u2019t follow you,\u201d Melinda said, looking at her watch. Her watch was a lovely thing. It had cost her an arm and a leg.", "2_9_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-two in the morning</span>. The cleaning implements had once more been lifted high in the air. Nobody moved.\n\"Life only gets harder, kids. The only thing that gets you through is faith!\" yelled the throat of Skip, who was still inside.\nNone of the kids said anything.\n\"Trusted faith in God, that\u2019s what does it!\" yelled Skip.\nNone of the kids said anything.\n\"Our Savior and Lord, Emmanuel Lowe!\" hollered Skip.\n\"Our Savior and Lord, Emmanuel Lowe!\" yelled the kids.\nNone of the kids had ever seen Skip\u2019s face. None of them would ever see it.\nAt nine thirty-four in the morning, $FIRSTNAMEB6 and her sister, Sue, left their brother, $SURNAME1, to take care of the baby, and set out with Skip, all three in the big yellow bus, for whatever the towns might turn out to be like.", "0_12_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, who had materialized with the golden piece of the chronoscope, watched his dog Skip disembowel a purple taxpayer in a drainage ditch below. As the taxpayer expired, his liver and heart fluttered like parachutes deployed in a breeze.\nNo one bothered to change the numbers in the time-grid. The history of the Solar System went on as though nothing had happened.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog Skip, both then exactly one point six six meters tall, faded again into incorporeality. Their disappearance left behind them a cloud of golden sparkles.\nSo ended the first appearance of $SURNAME0 and his dog, and of the chronoscope, and of the pinpoints in the sky.\nHandicapped though he was by his experience among the pink creatures of Mercury, Bowen Island had made some sense of $SURNAME0\u2019s phenomena, and had proposed a theory of time different from any proposed before. His theory was that time, as it was being studied by so many people on Earth, was not a whit more than a distraction\u2014a sideline, in terms of the sense of reality that any given person could reasonably cultivate.\n\"This man $SURNAME0,\" said...", "2_1_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-four in the morning</span>.\n\"There it goes,\" said $SURNAME1.\nThere was a seashore on the planet $SURNAME0, a seashore in the country of Newport. In spite of the billions upon billions of tales that have been told all over the Earth, in all languages, the story of $SURNAME0\u2019s seashore is one of the best stories ever told.\nIt is $SURNAME0\u2019s favorite story.\nOnly $SURNAME0 knows the whole story, only $SURNAME0 sees the huge pattern that the story fans flat upon the ether.\n$SURNAME0 has never told the story much beyond where $SURNAME1 had reached when Material Man shot him over Mars. The part that $SURNAME1 had reached when Material Man shot him over Mars is a good place to stop the telling.\nThis is the stopped-telling part:\nOn Earth, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 was the mother of one child\u2014$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. Mordate, $FIRSTNAMEM2, and Kazan were $SURNAME0\u2019s three young companions who, with him, Saturn forded a river of flame that separated the region of hell where they had been from the region of heaven where the Golden Age was to be.", "2_20_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-nine in the evening</span> in New York, and it was eleven twenty-nine on Mars.\nAt twenty minutes of nine in New York, the telephone in the $SURNAME0 living quarters rang, and served to remind Mrs. $SURNAME0 that her husband would materialize in about seven minutes. She stopped playing Skip to tell Junior about it, stopped on a dime.\n\"I must attend to the courtyard,\" she said quietly.\n\"Yes\u2019m,\" said Junior, busy demonstrating Get Chucked to his friends. Mrs. $SURNAME0 was one of the few people in the world who knew what it meant when somebody said, \"Get Chucked.\"\nShe hurried to the glass walls and opened them. One wall faced south, one faced west, one faced east, and one faced north. The courtyard was the four walls between.\nThe lights of the courtyard were on. A violet spotlight overhung a octagonal wooden plot in the middle. The plot was daubed with red, yellow, blue, and green paints in accordance with all the moods and phases of Mars.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 strode to the fountain in the middle of the plot, turned on the four jets. The fountain was Martian-green.", "0_16_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was a big attic in the house of an aged widow who lived at 80 Catalpa Street in the town of Red Bank, New Jersey. The widow's name was Mrs. Gumbet. The attic belonged to an eight-year-old idiot boy called R. Sergeant Scott. It was Mrs. Gumbet's custom to let sergeant have the attic to play in. Mrs. Gumbet and the idiot made up a family with a younger brother by the name of Robert. Robert didn't appear to be much of a blessing to the family, either. He wasn't in the attic, when young $FIRSTNAMEM1 and young Malvin put on their costumes and waded through the cold water of their back courtyard and climbed the drainpipe, and prepared to break in through the back window. Robert was wandering around aimlessly somewhere. He was a silly-looking boy with a stupid I of a face, and thick, I purling lips. He was twelve years old and big for his age. Three feet tall, and big for his height. He was cross-eyed, and looked like nothing so much as a frog. He certainly didn't add anything to the beauty of the. neighborhood. In fact, looking at him was enough to make one a...", "1_20_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-seven in the evening</span>.\nThe voice on the barroom chronometer had said so.\nBingham, Kansas, lay all spread out, to knock down with one punch\u2014in the form of a giant sinkhole that was 233 feet wide, 233 feet deep, and 233 feet rhe height of a tall building.\nThere was, however, nothing about the appearance of the town to suggest that it was about to kick the bucket. The old Ford in which young $SURNAME1 had been driving roared right over the town without seeing it. So expert had young $SURNAME1 become in disregarding chronometers that, except when they were off by a large margin, he more or less completely ignored them.\nHe found a room in Tinkletown, a small town some ten miles west of Bingham, and there effectuated the transfer of his cargo. He found, at the depot telegraph office, a wire from $SURNAME0, saying that all was ready on the Islandside.\nSix hundred and forty miles away was the Islandside, which was the name of the calm and lovely island where the twilit palace of $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 lay.\nYoung $SURNAME1 drove his truck there in exactly twenty hours, arriving at the gates of the palace exactly one minute before the...", "2_9_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine seventeen in the morning</span>, M.T. The sun was in the zenith.\nA catbird and a robin were singing.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were facing Magnetic Building E\u2014West.\nIt was a huge, impregnable, matte-gray structure, two-hundred-odd feet high, and surmounted by a microwave lens. It was unoccupied, self-heating, airconditioned, water-filtered, and absolutely secure. All communications, external and internal, were handled by relays in the basement.\nThere was a troupe of cleaning women and men to keep the grounds neat and to service the interior. One of these workers was an armed guard.\nEither side of the entrance there were a pair of concrete stand-bys for Mercury missiles. At the rear there was an ancillary electronic research laboratory.\nThe dignitaries, scientists, and other personnel on disbursement from that building, who might at any time have been either inside or in view of the building, were blindfolded and taken to their destinations in small, papal litters.", "1_13_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-nine in the afternoon</span>. The first mate, the surgeon, and I entered the second mate\u2019s room and sat down. Mary the cook brought in twelve beers on a platter. She set them on the table, slammed the door and locked it. \u201cThat girl\u2019s getting worried about her morals,\u201d said the second mate. \u201cI told her if she wasn\u2019t such a good cook I\u2019d fire her long ago.\u201d We cracked one of the beers apiece and passed the platter around. The beer was ice-cold and pleasant.\nWe were all feeling fine.\n\u201cWhen did the boats start coming in?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cSix or seven weeks ago. You\u2019re well off, you know.\u201d\n\u201cI know it.\u201d\n\u201cThat figures out to be nine hundred and sixty-seven a month,\u201d said the first mate.\n\u201cAnd all found by the company,\u201d said the second. \u201cWhy don\u2019t you stay in the states, Hemingway?\u201d\n\u201cWho wants to stay in the States?\u201d\n\u201cI don\u2019t,\u201d said the first...", "0_11_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven sixteen in the morning</span>. The red Chevy roared out of the courthouse parking lot, doing seventy-six in a thirty-five zone. I turned around and saw everything falling apart. My attorney was on his knees, frantically pulling straws out of the snarl in his briefcase. \u201cWhat the hell are those?\u201d he yelled. \u201cDrug paraphernalia?\u201d A cop was running toward us. I could see him shouting through my high, blurred windshield...another one opening the concrete block\u2014and\u2014chain, A\u2014frame into the courthouse lot. I could feel the tension in my shoulders piling up like flood\u2014water.\nI swung the big car right\u2014handed, thru the air gate and past the bailiff\u2019s booth. \u201cWhere are we going?\u201d shouted my attorney. \u201cStop the car! I\u2019m getting out. You can\u2019t drive away while I\u2019m bailing you out!\"\n\u201cLeave me alone,\u201d I said. My voice sounded mean and obscene, like Jerry Lee Lewis scratching Hem to bang\u2014bang\u2014boogie. \u201cTalk to the man in the trunk\u2014he\u2019ll give you your money back.\u201d\n\u201cWhat are you...", "2_7_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-nine in the morning</span>. A bee stung $FIRSTNAMEF1 at 7:39 on a morning in September.\nIt stung her in the space between the thumb and index finger of her left hand. The bee in stinging her administered the venom not only to her but also to its own body. The bee left its body in the form of venom in $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s hand. This proved fatal to the bee.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 looked at the space between her thumb and index finger. She let the handkerchief fall from her hand to the ground. The hand flew up to touch the place that had been stung. The reaction of pain was now reflex, directly from body to brain. The body did not need to wait for the rest of her to be told there was a reason to hurt.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 lifted her head and looked far out to a rise of ground in the middle distance and the lake beyond the rise.\nShe removed a cartridge from an empty chamber of the Magnum, replaced it with a cartridge from her little red ammunition pouch. The snap of the Magnum-revolver\u2019s cylinder enclosing a new bullet was loud in the sun.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 carried the Magnum in her right hand.\nTwenty", "2_8_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIn the office of the Master of the Universe was an IBM quadruply extensible computer. A plug-in transistorized console with binocular sighting device and keyboard was used by the Master of the Universe in preparing quatrains. The console communicated, of course, with the great main hall, where the cyclostyle was installed.\nIn the hall were 15,375 electric typewriters, each as powerful as the IBM in the office of the Master of the Universe. The keyboards of the typewriters were set for various languages, including Turkish, Urdu, Zulu, Gaelic, Nahuatl, Bengali, Icelandic, Basque, Gujarati, Hindi, Flemish, Burmese, English, Esperanto, Hebrew, Chinese, Danish, Norwegian, Swedish, Portuguese, Finnish, Irish, Icelandic, Japanese, several Polynesian dialects, and I do not know what else.", "0_6_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe chronometer in the pilot room had read six o'clock when the last catastrophe had struck.\nThe chronometer had now gained fifty-six minutes.\nBut the advancing army of invaders had not wiped out the two-hundred-and-sixty-second dogwatch. The dogwatch was still in effect.\nAt twenty-two minutes past ten o'clock in the morning, the harmoniums were to fight their last battle for the possession of Earth.\nAnd at twenty-two minutes past ten o'clock in the morning, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 would start trying to get home.\nThey would have a blind, brute sense of direction home. They would have no more idea where their homes actually lay than a sound-torpid, harmonium-loving dog, released from its cage, would have of where its master lived.\nBut men can get home, if they have nothing but their bare hands and the courage to use them.\nWhile on the spaceship Earth there was still twenty-two minutes to life, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 did not let their courage flag.\nThey calmly laid out a supply of food, a pair of space suits, a space-suit pressor, a blanket, a first-...", "2_22_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Tuesday the twenty-second of September.\nThe lights went on again over the telegraph-changes board.\n\u201cAll operators.\u201d\n\u201cWhat\u2019s this, Steve?\u201d said Operator 23.\n\u201cHell, I\u2019m wiped,\u201d said Operator 5. \"They couldn\u2019t wait until we got our wad? They couldn\u2019t wait until \u2019Barrage\u2019 started?\"\n\u201cWelcome, friends,\u201d said $SURNAME0. His televised image, talking from its pillar on Earth, was clearly visible from Mars.\nIn the caves of Mars, the formation that was the Army of Mars had been bathed in the light of the planet Earth for several hours. The formation stood rigidly in the light, which was very bright, now that the intensity had built up.\nThere was a television set in every cave, a set that had been salvaged from the material of the good ship Newport News. It was a miracle of mass communication.\nEvery member of the Army of Mars could see $SURNAME0\u2019s great image on all the television sets.", "1_19_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was the roof-garden of the marble and teakwood $SURNAME0 mansion on Mars. It was such a gilded and grand affairs that it was known locally as The Taj.\nThe area of The Taj was perhaps ten thousand cubic feet. It was bounded by gilded iron railings and furnished in marbles, statuary, and little tinkling fountains.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, had the place to themselves.\nThey were sitting across from each other at a round table. On the table was a Monopoly game, half finished.\nFinished or unfinished, the game was actually meant to be something else.\nIt was meant to be a picture in a frame of two smiling beatifically rent-paying tenants who viewed their surroundings with the contentment of the grave.\nIt was to be a picture of $SURNAME0 and his dog.\nBut the picture wasn't working out at all. Neither $SURNAME0 nor his dog was smiling. Nor were they even vaguely contented.\n$SURNAME0 was barefoot, as usual.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 wore one of $SURNAME0\u2019s old shower-girdles for dogs.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 lay on his side...", "1_14_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe substance came hurtling toward Earth in the form of a shapeless, meteoric blur. In a blazed split-second it had annihilated three billion miles of space travel.\nThe needlelike object was precisely one half-inch in diameter.\nIt was four inches long.\nIt was made of aluminum, and each end was sealed with glass.\nIt was a thermonuclear bomb.\nIts opinion of humanity was not especially high. The opinion of itself was even lower. It bore a message, written in the Cyrillic alphabet, for people whose opinions of themselves it rated somewhat higher.\nThe opinions were stated in a coded message that any fool could read. The fool was presumed to be a Martian wild booby.\nThe code that followed was simplicity itself. It was simply the substitution of one word for another. For example\u2014\nPROGRAM\nFaithful servants: here it is, the lifeless chunk that carried out your orders so well. Guard it well against its fellow travellers, who would stop at nothing. We salute you.\nShall we meet on Mars? Eisenhower.\nThe bomb did not answer this question directly. It would have been wasted effort. Rather, it gave an encoded description of what life...", "2_6_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe three were still playing their friendly game with the receivers. The game was still a draw.\n\"My, that was a good one,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, referring to the most recent eruption from her receiver in particular.\nShe was sitting on a terrace before a speaking tube that was connected to a water closet in one of the buildings in St. Swithin\u2019s Parish. The tube ran through an air duct and an opening in a bedroom wall.\n\"I do wish my husband would hurry up and get back from the war,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I know it\u2019s silly, not knowing where in the world he is or what he\u2019s doing, but I just can\u2019t help feeling nervous.\"\n\"I\u2019m sure he\u2019ll be here any minute,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"If that\u2019s him now,\" said $SURNAME1, pointing down the long, straight road to the church, \"I think he\u2019s on a horse.\"\n\"If it is,\" said $SURNAME0, \"I\u2019ll eat that horse.\"\n\"Whoopee,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"You know,\" said $SURNAME1, \"that might be him.\"", "0_14_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-two in the afternoon</span>. Any second now, the kid would come to the window and call out in that strange and terrible language\u2014and $FIRSTNAMEB3 was right, the kid did come to the window to look out and see what the yelling was about\u2014and he would understand nothing and merely continue his frantic waving and pointing. His father came to the window and called to us and we said, \u201cOh no, no, no, no,\u201d and nervously laughed. The father wanted to know what was the matter. We didn't know what to say. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I looked at each other darkly. All our tenderness to each other had passed, here\u2014the bus throbbed and the afternoon light beat on, red smog chafing at our faces. The kid began calling warnings again.\n\u201cMan, if the cops come along now and see the scag on the seats in the daylight and us sweating like this ... sometimes on this road it's so empty a state trooper comes right through and he won't know about us till he sees us, our faces, God, someway ... \u201d\nAt this moment the bus jolted off the road into a filling-station and skidded to a stop. We all sighed with relief at this sudden change...", "0_7_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-five in the morning</span>. I had lost all track of the days of the week. My gambling debt to the Holy Hoodsmen of San Gabriel was exactley\u2014nothing. All my \u201cdebts\u201d were now purely imaginary. I had come to this last hazard because I had not dared phone my attorney for advice. My total wealth, including six packs of $FIRSTNAMEM1, taxicab fare cheques, change from cigarettes and hotel bill in my wallet: $2.98\u2014not enough to cover the bet. But I was told that it was all part of the Great Plan.\nI found a call box and called Terry and LaVache\u2019s hotel room in Eugene. It took some time to locate them in the apartment, because the phone was presently answered by one of those quasi\u2014humans who service the corporate breakfast departments of the world\u2014a woman who spoke only in clipped, brusque, dumb, utilitarian phrases. \u201cToaster, please. Thank you. Nest, please. Thank you. Bacon, please. Thank you. OJ, please. Thank you. Bun, please. Thank you. Sleet, please. Thank you. Cream, please. Thank . . . \u201cI was startled by the wild, joyous sound of the...", "1_15_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> and chronologically it was Tuesday, July 9, 1974, and at this particular moment $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, were materializing on the scrub plain outside the boundaries of Newport, Rhode Island. They were materializing thirty-eight thousand feet above sea level.\nScattered over the plain outside Newport were remnants of World War II warning installations\u2014steel obelisks and parabolic dishes mounted and grounded on concrete foundation aprons. The obsolete installations were occasionally used to advantage by $SURNAME0 in his needling clashes with $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. The meaning of their presence in an supposedly God-forsaken place is that planes were warned to steer clear of a region about the size of a football field.\nContrary to what $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 would have thought, $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 had not materialized directly in front of one of the war relics. There was a space the size of a football field between the warning installation and $SURNAME0. He was materializing far from artifacts, so as to leave his earthling skin a better fighting chance against the bites and stings of alien fauna.\nWhen he cohered in space and time, $SURNAME0 was forty-two years old chronologically, one hundred...", "0_8_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until nine o'clock in the morning</span>. The saloon was shuttered and dark. There were sounds behind it. There were sounds in the murk of the coiled tubing that transected the mudflat like a pupating subway system. There was the sound of concerted effort, of dozens of people working in perfect rhythmic unison in teams, and of some great machine whine at the heart of it. At its perimeter was an intense crackling of small-arms fire.\nThe sound of concerted effort undercut by the fre quent ripping of shrapnel overhead and by the thudding of mortar rounds was loud in the sealed ear canals of Shribner of the 7th Cavalry.\nCaptain Abraham Shribner of the 7th Cavalry was squatting on his heels outside the front of the $FIRSTNAMEB6 Tasa Hotel, which was known to many as The Apocalypse. The squatting position was a natural one for him. It was by no means a painful or uncomfortable one. The position hid his fat stomach. It also preserved the creases in his starched camouflage utilities.\nCaptain Abraham Shribner of the 7th Cavalry had been told he had an honest, manly, forthright face. He had a deep tan, a product of extensive time spent outdoors; his tan...", "0_0_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-one ante meridiem</span>, according to the luminous disk of stainless steel on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s wrist. Actually, the time was twelve forty-two, according to the luminous disk on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s heart. The disk on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s wrist had been placed there by the police authority on Mercury to replace the disk on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s heart. The disk on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s heart had been placed there by Astro, lord of the watchdog spaceship Rusp, to replace the disk on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s wrist.\n\"The time is priceless,\" $FIRSTNAMEB2 now said to himself as he slid out the panes of putrid Plexiglas and squinted at the faraway housing estate. The lights were being turned out.\nThe most beautiful view in the world was the view out of the ruined basement window of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s old barrack.\nBut there was not much to be gained now by looking out of that window. All that could be seen out of it was the iron street-grid of Burne\u2019s highly organized sector of Mercury.\nThe careful alignment and spacing of the iron street-grid revealed the absence of love and imagination in the lives of Burne\u2019s underlings. Their lives...", "2_10_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty in the morning</span> of Titan\u2014and the day had just begun.\n$SURNAME0 carried his watch in a pocket of his space suit, rather than on a wrist strap, since there was nothing on his wrist but blood vessels and bones.\nBefore he opened the golden rounded hatch of his space ship, $SURNAME0 was a handsome, vital man. When he opened the hatch, the air in his suit lost its warmth and its crispness, and became sickly, anemic, slightly acrid.\nBeauty had left it.\nThe warm gases within the suit were a limp, wan substitute for the red-cell river of a vibrant heart, gushing through its surrounded veins.\nThrough the visor of the space suit, $SURNAME0 looked out on a desolation that he found mildly diverting.\nThe ground was iron, three million years ocherous, jagged, curiously relieved against the background of the studio backdrop.\nThis iron wilderness was laced by true goldstone veins.\nA child of five could have followed the glowing veins to a hole in the ground no bigger around than a sewer pipe.\nA Martian or any other being with transplanted, Earthling muscles could have lifted a hatch from the hole with one finger.", "1_1_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-one in the morning</span> when we switched on the lights in the big old bedroom, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 saw a big thick cockroach on the wall and said, \u201cAh, roomie, the time has come for you and me to talk and to have a real understanding of what's what in the universe -- let's sit down and meditate at this time of grief -- and you know, $FIRSTNAMEB6, I understand everything you're saying -- everything that I, that got me mad -- God, I don't feel at all that way -- now I understand!\u201d And then he took my peyote buttons and went out of the room giggling happily, and he came shambling back in an hour with sad, glazed eyes. \u201cI found $FIRSTNAMEF4, I found Camille, I found everybody -- I shambled among the wreckage and the refuse, I was responsible for it all, what could I say?\u201d He took the pencil, revolved the roach on the paper, looked at it pensively. \u201cAha,\u201d he said, \u201cthe last moments -of- \" \u201cAre you really seeing yourself die?\u201d I asked. \u201cThe answer is yes, $FIRSTNAMEB6, every road leads to that and every...", "0_18_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>. I had traveled eighteen miles, starting twenty minutes late, and I would be fifty-eight miles from Walnut Creek, my destination, by one o'clock in the morning. I would never make it in time for the talk. I looked at my watch again; the hands were rotating wildly, as if they were dervishes adorning their regular wobble with fancy extra twirls. I held the watch still and looked out of the window at the flying scene, which was oscillating like a whirling dervish also. The Berkeley hills were pink and lavender in the sunset. It was a good travel shot:\nTHE LIBRARY\nI left San Francisco pulling myself together and feeling clean and bright with the misery behind me. I was learning to study the American scene somewhat like a foreigner and see it whole. It is a strange land, the most tragic and comical country in the world--because of the recurrent element of humorous irony in a somber and purposeful people pursuing joy and gold with equal determination and not getting any fun out of it. I made a few tentative generalizations about the United States as a country, for my own benefit, for a future article in Holiday. A person takes wisdom not...", "1_16_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB5 came out of the hotel alone, looking a little pale and very nice. She carried a fur coat and a packages.\nWe gave her the roses, and she said they were lovely, and Montoya gave her the package of chocolates and the leather cigarettes case.\n\u201cThere\u2019s been a terrible death in the bull-ring,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. \u201cThree horses wounded in three consecutive picades, and all dying on the sand.\u201d\n\u201cLet us go and get some drinks,\u201d Montoya said. \u201cAnd then some dinner.\u201d\n\u201cBueno,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB5. \u201cWhere are you going, Jake?\u201d\n\u201cEat with you and Montoya.\u201d\n\u201cCome on, then,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. \u201cWe\u2019re going to have a martini and a bottle of beer, and then $FIRSTNAMEB5 will have to run up and dress.\u201d\nWe had the drinks and paid for them, and went out under the arcade to the street and took a taxi to the Caf\u00e9 Moderno. We had another drink and went through to the dining-room and sat down.\n\u201cHave you had a good...", "1_8_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span> of October First, two thousand, on the cone-shaped roof of the Rlaylactic Code Enforcement Building, in downtown Indianapolis. The roof bounded the north side of a square lot, which contained the Code X Building proper, and in which Lenore\u2019s drawers were. The square lot was on Connecticut, between Ohio and Pennsylvania, two blocks east of Monument. The Code X Building was close to downtown Indianapolis, but in a section of the city made almost entirely of empty lots, factories and warehouses, some of them with nothing but narrow alley access, and all of them with an ambience of heavy, cold traffic smells and thickened, still air. The house where Lenore Beadsman, who had recently turned eighteen and was about to begin her freshman year at Shaker College, had lived and was still living with her mother was over on the western side of Indianapolis, in the eastern section of deep, made-for-the-most-part-of-glass Corvettes and gas stations, near a civilizational fringe of waste-disposal concerns and landfills, not too far west of the Horse-and-Buggy Entomological Testing Board and House. All in all, it was a fair distance to drive between...", "2_21_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-nine in the evening</span>.\n/b/\n\u201cGood lord.\u201d\n\u201cWhat? What?\u201d\n\u201cWe are supposed to. To be. At Daddy\u2019s reception.\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s Lenore\u2019s fault. We need to hustle.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s going to take at least half an hour to get to the Desert, from here.\u201d\n\u201cListen to this traffic, Rick. We\u2019re just going to have to grit our teeth. I\u2019m not hurrying any less than you are, I can assure you.\u201d\n\u201cLarpent can go eat a bag.\u201d\n\u201cYou don\u2019t have to actually use those exact words, you know.\u201d\n\u201cPardon me.\u201d\n\u201cYou don\u2019t actually have to crap in your common room at school, if you don\u2019t want to, either.\u201d\n\u201cExcept that that\u2019s a nasty myth, Lenore. Probably spread by your own grandfather, as a matter of fact.\u201d", "0_20_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to nine o'clock in the evening</span>. The trembling sun was a bare three minutes above the horizon. The air was filled with a momentous impatience of time\u2014as though time were on the edge of bursting into some new and overwhelming manifestation, like the white fireworks that feed a rocketing column of liquid oxygen into the night. Something was going to happen.\nRacing Windstrom looked over the wall. \"I like this planet,\" he said. \"I\u2019m going to spend the rest of my life here.\" He had to yell over the roar of the rocket motor warming up in the reinforced concrete blockhouse eighteen feet behind him.\nBehind the blockhouse was an enormous rocket\u2014Titan VII\u2014a fifteen-million-dollar monster on the Pad. Behind the rocket were manned guards in a double ring of barbed wire. Behind the barbed wire was the quiet, white-painted suburb of Mars Surface, California.\nAnd behind Mars Surface was a four-hundred-million-dollar device to fly a man to the surface of the moon and back.\nOnly one man was flying\u2014Carson Proctor, owner of Newport Cigarettes, Flamingo-Suits, Les Boissons Naturales, and half of Mars Surface, California.\nThroughout the total command...", "1_2_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-four in the morning</span>. I sat there with a full beard, slimy as a snail, stinking, with my bag of serpent makhorkhas, dug up from all makhorkhas, from all available hiding places. I think I collected these things with the idea of bribing, one at a time, every cop in Times Square, with one apiece. My problem gradually became one of space, of the carrying capacity of my person, the lizard burden for each officer. I worked out a complicated system of storage and retrieval, figuring to myself how many hours it would take to complete the circuit with a normal walker's stride. I could see myself circling the Square and walking straight up Theater Row, bribing every cop in it--confident, huge, overbearing but not without a slight, friendly smile. When I finished I planned to go back to the Astor and sit in the Reading Room, reading. That's all I ever seemed to do in that room. Where else, in every city of America? All that is left of me is my footsteps in the Reading Room. You and I are merely ghosts who consented to live in the eye of a strange tempter for a few years. The temptation to withdraw from earth is not simple. But I never...", "0_19_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-four in the evening</span>. In fifteen minutes a concert was going to begin on the stage of the town band-shell. The concert would feature singing and hortatory talk by Salo, and plans for its climax were now hatching inside the telencephalic gland of his brain.\nThe concert would climax with Letitia\u2019s Army singing its favorite song.\nThe six thousand people in the audience would join together in singing this song.\nThey would sing it six thousand strong.\nThey would sing it with enthusiasm.\nThey would sing it with tremendous heartiness.\nBy singing this one song, the six thousand people in the audience would confer on Salo a power and a prestige comparable to the power and prestige of the President of the United States.\nThe concert would feature singing and hortatory talk by Salo. It would culminate in the singing of one song by the audience.\nIt was seven thirty-one by the imitation Swiss watch in $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s pocket. The watch was not running, but it was set by the same signals that set all the clocks in Newport.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 stood in the doorway of the mansion. He was in shadow. Salo, just outside the doorway,...", "2_13_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earth time.\nThree minutes before one o'clock, Helmholtz was already dead\u2014 already linked for all time in the minds of his comrades with the most ominous minute in the ceremony of their religion, the sacred minute of one o\u2019clock. Manhood had been burned out of him, leaving only a pitiful chunk of inhuman soldier-meat.\nPoor Helmholtz\u2019s features were now as flat and glassy as a pressed leather purse. His eyes were dull and dead under the glass.\nBut, at the beginning of the sacred minute of one, those eyes could still flare with living fear, with living fanaticism.\nThose eyes could still glitter with the light of understanding\u2014with the light of what had happened to him, with the light of why it had happened to him.\nThose eyes could darken with living pain at the memory of five minutes in the pleasant springtime at the end of a career as a soldier.\n\"He cries now.\" said old $FIRSTNAMEB1 nicely, moving his twiglike buttocks over muddy turf as he looked down at the products of his handiwork.\nOld $FIRSTNAMEB1 was now forty-two Earthling years old.", "2_17_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. John N. Backer was sitting at a long, mottled bar of colonial design in an establishment called The Little Fleet. He ordered another drink of an unpronounceable nature. Pulled from a tank, bedewed with moisture in the chilled light of the bar, the beverage looked like a jiggling, pastel waterfall. John N. braced his fingers around his glass and stared casually ahead.\nTwo men to whom sugar-plums were phantoms of the most minute and remote description entered the Little Fleet. So much seemed apparent by the fact that one was smoking a cigar two feet long, and neither had come to buy stamps. Everyone in the bar turned to look at these men. That they were strangers, potential trouble, was as obvious as the dim shine of their black hats.\n\"Let\u2019s get some drinks, Konrad,\" one said to the bigger of the two. The big man nodded and smiled at the bartender.", "0_22_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nCulpepper was pacing in front of the harmonium, scowling at people.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s boy, $FIRSTNAMEB2, was curled in a fetal position between the ten-foot-deep banks of the Niagara Falls. Beside him stood a wine bottle full of something $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had brewed up from Canada bluegrass, sweet grass, and last year\u2019s discarded Christmas tree twinkle lights.\n\"The meek ... shall inherit ... the Earth,\" said the skinny young man named $SURNAME1, sipping the strange brew appreciatively.\nBeads of steam rose gently from the tunnels that led to the boiling chamber beneath the falls. The tunnels served to collect and trip anyone who might conceivably make it to the boiling chamber. Each tunnel, at a critical point, changed from six inches in diameter to two feet.\n\"Don\u2019t mind if I do,\" said $SURNAME1.\nThere was no offensiveness intended in $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s or anybody else\u2019s encirclement of the falls. Everyone was doing the best he could with what he had.\n\"You know what time it is?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Damned if I do,\" said $SURNAME1. \"In Kansas City...", "2_13_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-six in the afternoon</span>.\nThere was a flaw in the beautiful circle of the golden gates, a flaw through which one might enter and leave. Around the foot of the spiral staircase were two muddy footprints.\nThe footprints came from where the brownish mired stream had once been.\nThe footprints led nowhere.\nThere was a corrugated-iron shack in the park behind the Museum of Natural History in New York City. Its four walls were painted with a matte and unreflecting sea-green paint. The fifth and open side was protected from intrusion by a high iron wall, painted the same somber color. This was the \u201cservice entrance,\u201d a yellow door in the iron wall.\nThis door was almost always unlocked.\nAnyone who cared to might go in.\nAnyone who cared to might come and go, might bring in a ladder and a bucket, might whitewash the interior to his heart\u2019s content.\nThis had been done. The walls of the shack were now a sunny yellow.\nThe man in the wheelchair who guarded the door and the cheerfully painted shack was sallow, frail, mournful, a hater of crowds and excitement.", "2_23_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe singing had stopped.\nHalo Harding got out of the Cadillac limousine. The chauffeur remained at the wheel.\nHalo Harding said nothing.\nShe moved stiffly, slowly, as though there were broken bones in her body. When she moved, she moved a distance hardly greater than the length of her arm.\nShe was grave\u2014cool, calm, reserved. The impression on the senses of $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 was that nothing much mattered much, anyway. Not to her, to him. She seemed too weary to feel.\n\"Get in, please,\" she said, opening the car door and motioning him inside with a tilt of her chin. She spoke with displeasure.\n$SURNAME1 got in beside her\u2014in the back seat of the limousine. His hand found the limousine\u2019s seat, and there it clung in unthinking desperation.\nThe chauffeur stirred in front.\n\"Drive,\" said Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Yes, boss,\" said the chauffeur, and put the Cadillac in gear.", "0_16_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Accompanied by $FIRSTNAMEB1, $SURNAME0 tramped heavily up the grooves of $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s desired time.\n$SURNAME0 carried $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s chronometer in his hand. He stopped repeatedly to look at the name on the case.\n\"Is that really $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s chronometer, Mr. $SURNAME0?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"No,\" said $SURNAME0 glumly.\n\"So that whoever it belonged to is still out in space somewhere,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"No,\" said $SURNAME0 glumly, \"that person is somewhere on Earth. The name on the chronometer simply reminds me of punishment\u2014and punishment is something I can use a lot of help with, right now.\"\n\"Would you mind telling me what you did, Mr. $SURNAME0?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"What I did?\" said $SURNAME0. \"I should say I mind\u2014indeed, I object to discussing it point-blank. How did you get the cruel nerve to ask something like that?\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 backed away before the steam suddenly released by $SURNAME0.\n\"Please, Mr. $SURNAME0\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"If you...", "1_5_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. The sun had already evaporated the dew. The grass was dry.\nAll of the grass would be dry in two minutes.\nThen the Earth would roar up at the house from under the dry grass, would roll the house a hundred yards to the east, would shake it once, then would let it fall gently into a depression in the grass.\nThis would not hurt the house, for the depression was there already, was a dry pond in the center of the lawn.\nNone of the Dry Martini pieces could be seen from the terrace. The pieces were scattered over a thousand yards of broken-field terrain. There was no way of knowing, without close inspection, that any given bit of shrapnel was part of Dry Martini.\n\"You don\u2019t like it?\" said Fern instrumentally, to nobody in particular. She wriggled experimentally in the rocking chair. \"Well, I like it,\" she said in her own person, \"so there.\"\nThe chair was suspended from the overhanging eaves. It swayed giddily when Fern wriggled. But the burglar alarm system made it impossible for Fern to fall out of the chair. She couldn\u2019t even lean way back.\nAn...", "2_7_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-six in the morning</span>. The Museum opened at nine. \"Which one,\" said the visitor. He licked his lips. He made certain that the door of the glass case of the crystal skull he carried was shut. \"Number seventy\u2014?\"\nThe visitor\u2019s eyes had begun to run. They were watering, frosting. He stared bewilderedly at the glass case of the Thinker on the fountain outside. The gold helicopter seemed to be taking off in the sunlight, rising soundlessly into the spray on silver blades.\n\"Haven\u2019t you reserved a telescope?\" said the museum clerk politely.\n\"No\u2014yes!\" said the shivering man. He snapped a silver telescope extendable to twelve feet into the air.\n\"That will be three dollars,\" said the clerk.\nThe shivering man reached a long arm into his black box, brought up a handful of quarters, nickels, dimes, and tossed the money on the counter without looking at the coins. \"Please don\u2019t unpack the telescope for almost an hour,\" he said, and lurched out of the Museum.", "0_20_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-four in the evening</span> of the early day of that endless trip. The car went north to Colfax and turned east, toward Evansville and the mountain region. I felt like crying. $FIRSTNAMEB3 didn't. \u201cAnd now prepare for the hell of the high plains, $FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d he said. We said so long to $FIRSTNAMEF4, who still lay sleeping with the beautific infant; we went back and found the witch, who gave us an evil black grimace as we left. Leaving her, we felt we had lost a friend; we never saw her again. The car zoomed and zoomed on the dark plains, with a sinus headache. \u201cMan, we're getting close to where we buried that poor little sad cat alive.\u201d I couldn't handle it any more and I hung my head out the window and screamed with the air; my eyes were red agony.\n$FIRSTNAMEB3 and Camille both wanted to know what was wrong. \u201cAh, it hurts, it hurts!\u201d I cried. \u201cI want to be somewhere else. I feel too wretchedly sad.\u201d\n\u201cPoor $FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d said Camille. \u201cPoor fella there, I wisht there was somethin I could do for '...", "0_5_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five seventeen in the morning</span>. At a conservative estimate, scarcely two dozen conscious people were then in attendance at the convention.\nOf these, only six\u2014a shade more than one in twenty\u2014were women. A grand total of seven cigarettes was then burning away at a dishearteningly slow pace.\nOf the seventeen persons in attendance, 15 were wearing open-throat shirts.\nIn addition to the one-in-twenty representation of the female of the species by actual females, there was one man in attendance wearing a black brassi\u00e8re.\nThere was one person in attendance wearing a hood made of fur.\n3 The Legendary Hot Dog Stand\nWHEN a young man named $SURNAME1 began to shape metal on the lifeless planet of Mercury, he had been forty-four times round the sun.\nOf the thousands of species of creatures filling the teeming medians of the Solar System, Man alone pays attention to time. It is time that tells the history of man.\nMan had been on Mercury only one hundred and thirty-seven days.\nFor all of his adult life, he had been averaging two days for every revolution about his mother planet, Earth.\nMan was forty-four times round his sun. He was also ten times round his father star, Alpha...", "2_8_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight seventeen in the morning</span>.\nRansom K. Fern, like all of the other Martian veterans who followed $SURNAME0, had needed a great deal of sedation and coaxing to be brought out of his home to join the parade. When he discovered, on arriving at the staging area, that all he had to do was keep up with $SURNAME0 and not introduce any novelties of style or behavior, he became all flippancy and smiles.\nHe giggled when he put on his huge, silly coat with Hilarion\u2019s seahorse emblem on the back.\nHe giggled when he put on his huge, silly hat with the drooping parrot feather in it.\nWhen he took the tall, silly walking stick with the holder for a meerschaum pipe, he giggled coyly, and said, \" \u2019Scuse me\u2014 but do you have any spare couriers you don\u2019t need right now?\"\nHe giggled and nipped $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s head when $FIRSTNAMEM2 tried to pin a name tag on him. All the name tags had already been distributed, and there was none left but Fern\u2019s\u2014 number 327.", "1_11_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-eight in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was a wild, shut-eyed red face in the midst of his snaky orchestra, waving his sticks again, weaving, gesturing\u2014he\u2019d begun to tap with one foot. It was preparatory to breaking into what he knew from sad experience would be a very difficult dance step when his communicator went off\u2014not music, but a sound that neighbors make in an American House when they\u2019re summoning each other: ping-bong.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 answered, listened. A curtain of black dulled the fever in his eyes. His communicator uttered some more neighborly pings before he could find will enough even to flip it shut. \"Busted, neighbors,\" he said, without much joy in needing to say it. Real sickness throbbed in him. He went on with his music.\nA minute went by before he even began to wonder why, since the visitors had come so unexpectedly, they hadn\u2019t gone again\u2014escorted $FIRSTNAMEM1 Narcoxie, at least, back to Time Squad HQ. But when he did wonder why that was, he guessed shrewdly that his presence in the house was the reason for the delay. He guessed it because he had the achingly correct sense of the...", "2_19_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe Martian chronometer on the wall over the bar said that the time was five minutes to eight.\nIt was not a Martian chronometer.\nIt was a chronometer that had been modified by $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 so as to register the time on Mars by showing Earth time.\nThe time on Mars had always been sixteen and one-half minutes after the exact time on Earth.\nNow, for the first time ever, the time on Mars was one minute before the exact time on Earth.\nThe bartender was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. He was forty-six years old, married, successful, and rich. He was six feet three inches tall, and weighed one hundred and sixty pounds.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was a friendly man\u2014but nobody ever saw him smile or laugh. His thin lips were normally pursed in a moue of gentle perplexity. His cool gray eyes were profoundly puzzled as to what all the fuss was about. It was as though he had about decided that life was, after all, a pretty dull affair, but didn\u2019t know who to thank for the privilege of finding out.", "0_15_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-three in the afternoon</span>. I was worn out. I was all alone with my awful blue fingers under the tables of the Press Club. In the distance, under great clouds, I could see the mansion of Dr. Blatnoyd, my attorney. \u201cBlow the gig,\u201d I whispered to myself as I stumbled hack toward my parked VW. \u201cBeat feet for the doctor's place in Beverly Hills.\u201d I got in the car and started to drive. My hands were shaking violently and I could no longer feel the shift. I changed gears by touching the tassel on the stick with my left foot . . \u201cDoctor, it's me,\u201d I said, arriving at his stucco place in Beverly Hills. \u201cStill blue. Still wired.\"\n\u201cDid you have a nice walk?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cNever mind that,\u201d I said. \u201cJust hook me up.\u201d I sat on a table in the formal living room, and he injected me in the rump. \u201cThat was good ptomaine,\u201d I said.\nHe nodded. \u201cHow do you feel now?\u201d\n\u201cTotal consciousness,\u201d I said. \u201cTotal...", "1_9_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 set out to board their space ship.\nSo great was the power of anticipation in Newport, so high the voltage at which everyone was living, that $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 were able to go to the space ship quite privately, travelling in the back of an ice-truck.\nThey were driven to the space ship by a boy, who had been promised a valuable new watch if he made good his promises.\n\"You promised to drive us to the space ship, Mel797Jock?\" said $SURNAME0. The watch was in his inside pocket.\n\"Yessir, I did,\" said the boy called Mel797Jock. \"I promised it, and I aim to do it.\"\n\"It\u2019s right over there,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I suppose you can drive right up to it.\"\n\"Drive right up to it? \" said the boy, incredulous. \"You mean, drive right up to that big old piece of machinery? \"\n\"Certainly\u2014unless somebody\u2019s acting like a fool and trying to stop you,\" said $SURNAME0 genially. \"Golly! \" said the boy, \"you\u2019re a brave pair of crazies, aren...", "1_23_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. We were just outside of Hays, Kansas, and it was so hot in our sleeping bags that we had all taken them out of the truck, and some guys had shaken them out and mended rents, and others were using it for cover. One tall, skinny guy had a beard all covered with ashes, and you wondered where he came from and what he had been. He sat on his sleeping bag, Indian-fashion, had been there ever since we left Denver, Colorado, at six-thirty in the morning, and he was heading for his home somewhere in California, California being the only place to go. We asked him what he was going to do there, and he said he didn't know. Jackson and I were laughing in our heads; it must be a fine country where they let anybody come in off the road and don't bother to ask them what they are going to do. No wonder they have troubles. This guy was real nice; he belonged to the Mennonites and they are al- ways like that. The kids always climb right up on you when you stop and talk to them. In the evening, when we made camp and went out to wash, they'd come up with long bug-eyed faces and look at...", "1_5_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-nine in the morning</span>. I was only twelve minutes away from the place where everything either breaks or bursts open. I sat tight and waited for that circuit-breaker to kick in. I vowed not to move until $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo appeared. They were now thirty-seven minutes over due. At this very moment Carlo was jumping up and down on the dock, shouting with all his might. All I could see was his red wool muffler and streaming hair in the darkness and a wild waving arm. I couldn't even hear him. And that is the way it was in gleaming, diabolical Namabusi. The throb and hum of a jungle night! Remorseless throb and hum. And meanwhile, as if in some other world, there must have been a furious and lamentable discussion among the officials concerning my case -- what, I do not know, nor did I care. I had a date to meet the living and the dead and I was going to keep it and say my thing, whatever it was. Round the bend came the battered station wagon with the lights on low. They didn't see me parked in the shadows. $FIRSTNAMEB3 punched and talked to him as he drove, and made two wheels of the perilous turns that faced the swamp on one side and the railway embank...", "0_10_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten o'clock in the morning</span>, but the city was in effect asleep. It was an hour when bars and other cheerful haunts are full of harried men and women cutting out salespeople and junior account executives and mid-level managers and the like; getting them drunk and straightening out the while their own internal confusions and insecurities.\nBut at ten o'clock in the morning nobody was at a bar; they were all in their cars, propelling themselves on freeways and side roads toward their offices.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was not in a bar, either. All the inner confusion he needed to straighten out was in his head. He was shattering one million dollars by spending a piddling ten thousand.\nThe bill he was wrapping around a parking meter was five thousand dollars. He was stuffed with approval and high spirits over having spent just five thousand\u2014a mere bagatelle, no real spell on his net worth. It was really wonderful that people would misunderstand five thousand dollars, spend it as though it were fifty. He hadn\u2019t dared spend more, hadn\u2019t dared pull rank by spending a lot.\n\"Money is being terribly misused,\" he said to the man who understood. The man looked as silly as $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 felt. He had...", "1_17_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-four in the afternoon</span> of Moncrief\u2019s life. Time had had its tiny, gnarled hand on Moncrief\u2019s big red Irish Catholic nose. Time had talked Moncrief into giving up gin rummy after a long day at the office. But Time will be told, with Moncrief\u2019s last breath, to go and fry its fish.\nThis story will not see Moncrief get fried. This story will see Time hurry off to a cocktail party\u2014where Time will say: \"Oh my God!\u2014my watch has stopped!\" And Time will put on a leopard-skin coat and drive a bright red Ferrari straight to hell. These are very healthy attitudes to take, for anyone. They should be drummed into everyone\u2019s head at an early age\u2014beginning with the head of any would-be writer of fiction. For writers are the watchmakers of life. They keep everyone\u2019s watches running. And if the watches stop, the bombs start. A snappy car makes even the devil\u2019s daughter smile. Don\u2019t frown at me\u2014I\u2019m a businessman. You know who I am.\nNow. Who is Moncrief? Throw your mind back over a day...", "2_15_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-one in the afternoon</span>. At three thirty-four we were stopped in the middle of the highway about half a mile from town by two state police cars and one sheriff\u2019s car. Their sirens were screaming and when we got out of the car, dizzy and trying to walk straight, three young cops came out of their cars swinging black billies with all the force and hatred the furies could give a cop.\n\u201cYou fellas are drunk!\u201d one yelled. \u201cTurn around and put your hands on the car!\u201d\n\u201cAre you young shits blind?\u201d I yelled. \u201cAre you guys assholes? Can\u2019t you see we\u2019reDrunk?\u201d\nThe cop was bewildered; the sight of two young state troopers backing him up gave him pause. \u201cOkay sweetheart,\u201d he muttered. \u201cYou can go on. I\u2019ll let you pass this time. Have a nice trip.\u201d It was three thirty-nine p.m. \u201cOh yes,\u201d I said sweetly. \u201cWe\u2019re having a nice trip all right. Thanks for stopping us.\"", "0_22_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten sixteen in the evening</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time. The place was Room 223 of the Howard Johnson\u2019s Motor Lodge on Route 4 in Framingham, Massachusetts. Room 223 was occupied by $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and by his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 was a $FIRSTNAMEB4 Hound. The $FIRSTNAMEB4 breeding stock had been obliterated during the German blitzkreig against Soviet Kazakstan. $FIRSTNAMEB4 himself had been bred in America. He had been born the property of the Reverend Earl Morse Wilkins.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 the dog was really female. She had been inadvertently masculinized by the eight-millimeter round from a German machine gun that had taken her mate\u2019s place, close to the body of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 on the slope of a dusty Kazakstan hill.\nTo $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4, time had seemed to stop at seven fifty-eight. To $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4, mankind had seemed to wink out like a far-off telescope turning dark. There had only remained themselves, alone together in space, like a man and his dog on the dark side of the moon.\nBut the winking out and the seeming solitude were spurious.\nIt was the silence, rather than the disappearance,...", "0_11_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-seven in the morning</span>, and all of the personnel in the smoking lounge of the Thornapple House Sanitarium were wearing white nurses\u2019 uniforms. The uni\u00acforms had been provided along with the cigarettes, the ash trays, the rugs on which the cigarettes were burning, and the nicotine-yellow walls. Concerning the uniforms, old Stonecipher Babs-or-somebody, who was Alvin\u2019s grandfather, had once said, referring to his son\u2019s bride-to-be, \"My God, to think I had to pay for those uniforms. My God, to think of anybody having to pay for those uniforms.\"\nUniforms or not, the occupants of the lounge looked like people being treated for lung diseases.\nLenore was sitting not in a chair but on one of a pair of huge mock gothic windowsills, pretending to read a copy of the Reader\u2019s Digest while really watching the lawn and watching out the window for the black-and-white police barricades she knew were somewhere to the north, at the exit of a semicircular driveway that led off the lawn and onto the circle of East Corinth\u2019s inferno.\nSchafner had said ten o\u2019clock,...", "0_11_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-one in the morning</span>. I was supposed to have been back at the motel by eleven. $FIRSTNAMEB3 pleaded with Ed Dunkel to let him have the car; he had to get his brother right away or father would think he was dead and rush to heaven without knowing whether his son was dead or alive and so save a lot of trouble. $FIRSTNAMEB3 took off finally with the car, while I was lecturing. $FIRSTNAMEF4 and Ed Dunkel spent many hours walking up and down the alleys of San Francisco, sitting on stoops and talking -- the beginning of a strange friendship. As for myself, I wandered San Francisco in a daze. At lunchtime I poked my head over the backboard of a YMCA basketball game on Market Street and heard someone call, \u201cGo easy with that ball, Carlo -- there's young $FIRSTNAMEB6-oo-tino over there watching.\u201d It was my Uncle Benny, my father's boy, who had followed me to the country and was picking up a little extra money coaching basketball for the YMCA. He paid me a few dollars for my appearances, my mugging around in the audience; the minute he saw me he grabbed the money back, saying, \u201cThat's for the next time.\u201d I put my arm...", "0_12_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past noon</span>.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB2 was twenty-two, Mercury\u2019s prime meridian passed directly through $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s little cabin in the Barnard\u2019s Loop. $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s great-grandfather was on hand to see it. Even with a telescope, Belo couldn\u2019t make out any details. It was as though a great fuzzy wheel a couple of degrees in diameter had momentarily come to rest on the face of the Loop.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB2 was thirty, Mercury passed directly over the capital of the United States, Resurrect. The President, and the Vice President, and most of the cabinet, not to mention the Chief Justice of the Supreme Court, were on hand to see it. Belo tried to get aboard a stratospheric observation plane, but couldn\u2019t for love or money. He did get as far as St. Louis, thinking he might be able to see it from there\u2014but the cloud cover was heavy.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB2 was thirty-three, Mercury passed over the capital again. Belo had by then built the telescope on the highest hill in Newport.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB2 was thirty-four, Mercury passed over Newport. Belo didn\u2019t even try to get to New...", "2_6_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span> and there was light. We could see all the way across the rotunda to the porch of the library and all the way out to Hollywood boulevard, where it ended in a gold sun that hung over dead treetops in the mist. The street lights were off and traffic tapered away as it hit an impenetrable tangle of stalled automobiles and flashing red lights. Three cop cars were smashed together in a tangle of beams and sirens and spinning wheels that blocked a nosebleed section of the boulevard.\n\u201cDon\u2019t think so,\u201d said the cop.\n\u201cSonofabitch,\u201d I said. We watched an ambulance nosedive into an underwater light, where a Plymouth lay upside down around a street post. The ambulance smashed the convertible top and then jerked away, dragging its nose and popping the trunk like an explosion on Hollywood and Vine.", "1_18_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span> of December 31, 1999.\nThe place was the Unthank Mental Hospital for the Criminally Insane, a place that, for the moment at least, belonged neither to the Worcester nor the Boston Hospital Groups of the North American Phalanx. The place belonged to the Division of Mental Health of the World Federation and was presided over, if that is the right word, by Dr. Foster Kenniston.\nIt was a magnificently located institution, perched atop the cliffs of a jutting peninsula that extended into the Atlantic Ocean. A group of birdwatchers had chosen the labels for the resident ornithologists, calling them puffins and guillemots and kittiwakes and murres, instead of the names by which they were known to hospital staff: buzzards, skunks, dingoes, tigers, and lice.\nWhen you climbed the cliffs to visit the hospital, the tides rose up the ledges beside you. The roar of the sea filled your ears.\nThe hospital itself sprawled across half of the peninsula. The grounds of the hospital were designed in the manner of neither Florence nor Boothbay nor Middletown, although they bore certain similarities to those styles. The hospital grounds had been laid out by the former head...", "2_1_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-three in the morning</span>, Earth time.\n\"Good night, Earth, good night,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, leaning his forehead against the cold glass.\nThe watch ticked busily.\n\"If there\u2019s a devil\u2014if there\u2019s hell\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, \"don\u2019t let me miss it. I\u2019ve tried everything else in Creation. I\u2019ve earned the right to go SRO.\"\nThe time was one twenty-five.\nThe ship made a meal of space.\nThe ship ate a meal, and was a little bit pregnant.\nThe ship was under power, and its attitude toward the prevailing flow of space was to make all speed in the desired direction.\nIt was concerned only with the desired direction.\nA beam let energy flow onto the pellet of iron boron at the heart of the nuclear furnace that drove the ship. The pellet ate its breakfast, gave off energy, sent the ship purring.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 gazed from the control-room window at the tiny luminous chips that were the seven planetarysystems of the Sun.\nEach planetary system was an eyelash from the great round eye of the Sun.", "1_23_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> by the clock of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s consciousness when he let himself into his barrack room. His mates lay asleep in their bunks in the blue light of their soul-mate lovers, up and down the room. As $FIRSTNAMEB0 entered the room, the clock of his consciousness arrived simultaneously at the point in the cycle of the alarm-clock nightmare where $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s pulse-rifle jammed.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 bolted the door, then decided he didn\u2019t need to. The barrack room, as a matter of fact, felt flimsy and unreal, as though $FIRSTNAMEB0 had had a couple of drops of something. Two of the walls shimmered in the bright blue light.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 went to his bed, got in. He turned off his soul-mate by pressing a thumb against a red spot at the base of the statue\u2019s throat. When the statue was dark, $FIRSTNAMEB0 found the sight of it unbearably sad.\nHis mate had been given the name, written in fancy script on the base of the statue, of $FIRSTNAMEB1. $FIRSTNAMEB0 tore off the name, tossed it aside. The statue was as dark and dumb as stone should be.\nSitting up in his bunk, $FIRSTNAMEB0 now...", "1_15_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-four in the afternoon</span> before we found our way downtown. We first stopped at the big public library. Tim looked up his genealogical data. At this point he was Benjamin M.CIanville, Jr., of Indianapolis, Indiana, and he claimed a momentary place in history as a member of the Dies Committee of the House of Representatives that was sitting on Communist activities in the United States. \u201cI'm facing my thirty-fifth committee meeting this very afternoon in the House Office Building,\u201d he informed me respectfully. \u201cWould you like to come along to watch Washington at its source?\u201d\n\u201cYes, why not?\u201d I agreed. \u201cIt might be educational. And the way I feel, I can use all the education I can get.\u201d\n\u201cAtta boy,\u201d he said, approvingly. We proceeded to hitchhike downtown. It was a dreadful day; one of those real East Coast rainy days that make the streets of Washington look much better than they are, reminding me that I was still in the East. Watching it come down on the big car windows as we sat in traffic, I felt like a character in a Eugene O'Neill play. I could see myself entering the Senate lobby with...", "2_22_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten sixteen in the evening</span> on Mars. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 and twenty-eight soldiers were squatting in diamond formation on the floor of a space ship that was two hundred yards long. The space ship was powered, was ready to fly. It contained food, contained projectile weapons, contained proper clothing for a year.\nIt contained pillows and blankets to support the volunteers in an Earth-like atmosphere. It contained a careful system of latrines.\nAnd it contained thirty new Hitlers.\nThere were two plans for the use of the new Hitlers. The plans were known to all of the soldiers, were unarguable.\nThe first plan was that the ship should go to Earth and make the following landing. It should land in Venezuela. Venezuela was a little inland from the part of the Atlantic Coast where Columbus had first touched. The thirty new Hitlers were to land from the space ship, were to form themselves into three platoons of ten and one platoon of nineteen.", "2_15_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. That minute would be the longest of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s short life. The next longest would be the minute preceding the tick of three in the evening\u2014a minute that began now.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 well knew the size and age of the Universe.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 well knew the size and age of her husband, $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0 was thirty-six years old. $SURNAME0 was the Solar System. The Solar System was a rather insignificant star in a rather mediocre spiral among a great crowd of stellar spiral arms in a Universe that was itself among a great crowd of Universes in the general Universe, which itself was among a great crowd of still larger Universes yoked together in flotillas and super-flotillas and wandering sarviskas among the streams and nimbus clouds of extremely ancient space.\nIn this long history of man, woman, and Universe, there had occasionally occurred homesicknesses in homesicknesses, forms of protest against the magnitude of loose space.", "2_4_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. Something irretrievable had gone out of the night. They were gone. There was a sense of disquiet in the room. Confidence was gone. So far, in this particular war, the first shots had been fired by the Fear Chasers.\nWhat Happened at the Drug Convention\nWhen I got to the Americana Ballroom of the Ambassador Hotel on Tuesday morning at ten o'clock, the Drug Conference was already reeling from kilt by the Fear Chasers' Saturday raid . . . and that weird Las Vegas airplane scene at 5:00 a.m. Mooney was waiting for me inside the main door. He was wearing a tweed jacket, grey flannel slacks and a hickory shirt with an Irish terrier neck\u2014tie.\n\u201cWhere's your kit?\u201d I said.", "2_5_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. Our own motel room was two thousand miles away. I was squinting in the harsh light, trying to think. Halfway up the hall, a witness was crouched by a door. He was a big, bald guy in glasses and a rumpled suit, somebody's ugly uncle\u2014 probably the Whale's, judging from the snouty resemblances. He was watching every move I made. I tried to keep my composure...too many things now. I moved over to him. \u201cHave you called the desk?\u201d I asked, trying to sound casual.\n\u201cSix or seven times,\u201d he said. He knew who I was; we had spoken once or twice earlier in the night when he was sitting with his family around the giant tepee fire downstairs. Right now he seemed very suspicious. I thanked him and moved back to the door and leaned my ear against it. What was going on out there?", "1_19_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-eight in the evening</span> of November n in the year 2005, and it had not yet begun to rain. It was raining in Washington, however. But the telephone call waa not from Washington, It was from New York City, and it was made by a man in a violet-tinged suit to an elegantly mustachioed f he latter was lolling on the terrace of a house in Basking Ridge, New Jersey, the Nines of his house, drinking a whisky sour, smoking a ma in a vaguely oriental silk robe that would have been more appropriate on a female, and making neatly clipped entries in a memorandum binder.\nEnrico Nines was playing a clever game with himself. He was trying to figure out how long it would take him satisfactorily to seduce young Rita Hoenig, a real estate agent who had shown him forty-five selective rooms in and about the suburb of Basking Ridge, New Jersey, in the last three hours. Erato Nines, who was a sixty-one-year-old widower, lived alone in a penthouse on Park Avenue in New York City, and was in search of an estate in the northern New Jersey area. Erato Nines was as interested in seductions as...", "2_5_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-eight in the morning</span>. I had nothing to do and nowhere to go, except out into the street with the rest of the assholes, toward breakfast in that big, dark, tabloid dawn that they get here hun.\nThat big, dark dawn. It was coming fast. Gaseous fumes hung low across Lapeer Avenue, and the canvas ceiling of the tent men bulged . . .\nI decided to get into some proper clothes. The bellboys were watching the dawn from the sidewalk. The dawn was coming with atomic swiftness out of the east, across the broad street to my cheap hotel.\nScene Three: Dawn on the Great Lakes\nWHEN I came out of the room, thirty minutes later, the locusts were writhing on the pavement outside the hotel. Some of them wore blue overalls, some wore slacks and sportshirts, some wore expensive dresses. They were all white. Great Lakes dawn, full of grit and iron. I could smell the lake in the air.", "0_7_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the wild planet Tralfamadore. The temperature was four degrees Fahrenheit. The place was a red dune so high that itoften lost itself in the night sky. The name of the dune was Budget, which was a much livelier name than most Tralfamadorian names. The reason Budget was so high was that the wasteful, uneconomical, and inefficient management practices of the Tralfamadorian military had forced the artillery men to place the shells they fired into the stratosphere.\nThe space-ship, looking silvery and graceful, was resting on a ledge on the southern side of the dune.\nThe only things alive in the vicinity of the craft were insects that lived as long as two days. These insects spent most of their time covering the space-ship with a blanket of reddish-brown wax.\nThe atmosphere in the vicinity of the ship was being kept breathable by two handsome Tralfamadorian boys. They wore pink suits and violet helmets. The helmets were translucent, so that the suit-wearers could see well enough to watch out for rocks, holes, and the forms of those small, temporary insects whose life-span was a matter of seconds.\nThe boys were...", "0_16_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>, Wednesday. By now she was as far north as she could get and still be in the State of California. She\u2019d had no lunch. Her head hurt. Her wheels, powered by an almost dead man, had turned 4808 miles since Red Bank, N.J., where she\u2019d put him aboard, and now she was passing the point, about ten miles farther north, where the Bodhisattva, carrying the Captain, had turned back. She hadn\u2019t stopped yet; but lest anyone be watching, she slowed, from time to time, to five, six, seven, and eight miles an hour, like any pedestrians, letting her old smoke and coughing cry a little in the afternoon.\nIt was Highway 101 she was following, an empty road and nothing to see, unless you cared to look back over your shoulder at the receding continent. Americans, presumably,, lived back there somewhere; but as yet none had been visible to her. At ten minutes to five, however, just about the time that Jason Marshal was waking up in a bar called The Cage, one of the few expensive ones in San Narciso, $FIRSTNAMEF2\u2019s luck began to change. A gray-headed sallow young man in...", "2_19_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-seven in the evening</span>.\nThe Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0 was the only clergyman in the world who had seen a bearer of good tidings clearly and exactly when that bearer materialized. $SURNAME0 knew with sick certain ness what every other pastor in the world had had to guess at. It was not God that had given him this helpful advantage. The helpful advantage was wholly and solely due to the Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0\u2019s childhood skirmishes with Skip. It had taught him visually what most people learned the hard way\u2014 the hard way being to stumble across something wonderful when you were least expecting it.\nThe Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0 did not stumble across a thing. When he was sure that something wonderful was about to happen, he went and put himself in its happening.\nThe result, a moment later, was a story that unbelievers found utterly undependable.\nThe story always sounded to unbelievers like the eye-witness account of somebody who had found God, and for some reason or other had lost his mind.", "2_13_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Venus was on the underside of the Sun and in Earth\u2019s skies, racing outward from the Sun on the radial line that would intersect the Earth at the Wowo.\nWindle Poons, the one-hundred-and-twenty-three-year-old assistant curator of the Hugo Winners\u2019 Museum, came limping down the man-made canyon on the west of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s estate.\nHe moved slowly, on crutches.\nThe wind whistled past his ears.\nHe was anxious to get back to his air-conditioned stateroom under the glass pyramid of the museum greenhouse, but his anxiety did not cause him to move any faster. He swung one of his crutches into the air and brought it down, gently as a flute maker, upon a linden leaf.\nThe translucent leaf quivered with the clean, pure feeling of human contact.\nThe purpose of the museum was to harbor and inspire the feelings of very old men.\nA quartz sunlock let Poons into the museum. When he was in, the blinds slammed down, and the panes of quartz turned to colorless glass. The temperature was exactly 70.6 degrees.", "2_21_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine eighteen in the evening</span>.\n\u201c.... very large fish, which is why he lives in Siam, with the air conditioning,\u201d Verdet was saying on television.\n\u201cHoney you better come right in,\u201d said the clerk, handing Lenore a little plastic thing. \u201cBe in the skywalk in just a few minutes.\u201d\n\u201cThanks.\u201d\nThe white doorway turned and the thing in her ears wriggled. Lenore had to get used to that again.\n\u201cHow are they?\u201d she said. \u201cMy ears aren\u2019t clogged up with goo, are they?\u201d\n\u201cNo, ma\u2018am.\u201d\n\u201cYou can call me Lenore.\u201d\n\u201cFine. \u201d\n\u201cDo my ears look OK?\u201d Lenore held back her hair with both hands and turned her head to the left and right. \u201cThat\u2019s never really clear, is it?\u201d\n\u201cNo.\u201d\nThere was a sound from the metal box.", "0_21_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-nine in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 stood in the bright violet light of the spray of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s fountains. It cast deep violet shadows of $FIRSTNAMEB2 and the room behind him on the outside wall of the great laboratory.\nThe spray fell with a soothing hiss.\nThe spray falling thus for centuries had put a real film of fine, white limestone over the great room behind $FIRSTNAMEB2. And this was strange, since the room was eight stories above the foundations of the fountain. The foundations of the fountain were miles deep.\nThe white limestone film had not been white when the spray first put it there. It had been a dark, granite gray, having the texture of sandpaper.\nThe spray\u2019s action on that sandpaper texture had not been sandpapering, however.\nThe surface of the film had begun, without warning, to take on a beguiling, porcelain gloss.\nAnd in the gloss there were infinite shades and tints, pigments of mother-of-pearl and aquamarine, hints of amethyst and sherry, fine crystallized deposits of ruby and champagne\u2014a whole treasury of colors that layer by later of time.\nThe colors rested on the surface in a million fine washes...", "1_7_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to eight o'clock in the morning</span>. A lark was singing. There was that freshness and sparkle in the air which you get before the sun gets too high and that buzz of industry which you only get in the mornings in the country in June. The fields and hedges were still damp with dew. A cloud was lying in the low valley out there with the stream winding through it, and the tree-tops standing clear against the sky. The rooks cawed and flapped about the elms. A horse turned over the fresh earth in the field, and flicked flies off his hide with his tail. We heard the noises of the farm waking up, and total silence followed on their own voices. Finally, McMoon himself came out of the house and greeted us, rubbing an eye and yawning. He was a short, thickset man with a potato face and with little glints of devilry in his eyes. He was over fifty and walked with a stiff leg.\n\"Good morning, Mr. Beebee,\" he said. \"They told me there were some passengers up here from the train. This is Mr. Beebee.\"\n\"How do you do?\" said Beebee, politely.\n\"How do you do?\" said Old Bill, taking a...", "2_7_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-four in the morning</span> when the spiders relayed the first thing of any real interest. The thing the spiders relayed was obviously the sentimental nonsense the men had been singing in the ironwood forest.\nThat was the only thing that the spiders hummed back to their master, and they had been humming it ever since they caught the melody.\nRicardo Carlos Liberius noticed that the nonsense was not being hummed by a random half-dozen of his spiders. The harmonizers were part of a squad that was now coming up onto the knoll. He counted the members of the squad\u2014 he had been awaiting the squad for hours. The members of the squad were all there.\nColonel Ricardo Carlos Liberius left his hollowed-out burrow and hurried down across the ironwood forest to the colony proper. There he got his ornithopter and flew up to the knoll. He could have taken a train, or a guided bus, or a rickshaw\u2014all of which had been laid on for him. But he took the independent, idiosyncratic, illegal route that corresponded to his character.", "2_4_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"No, a thousand times no,\" Mrs. $SURNAME0 said, chuckling. \"We are as broke as Alaska.\"\n\"Good old broke United States of America,\" said $SURNAME0. \"It\u2019s a lot more fun being broke than it ever will be to be rich again.\"\n\"Money, Lord?\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. \"We don\u2019t need any money. Not even for tips.\" She and $SURNAME0 laughed merrily. \"Why should I change my tune now, all of a sudden?\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. She adopted a croaky voice, harmonizing with $SURNAME0\u2019s croak. \"Oh, my husband and I\u2014 we\u2019re rich as Croesus. We\u2019re just high-class street walkers, is all we are.\"\nMrs. $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME0 sank their identical faces into their hands for a full minute of silent, windy mirth.\n\"I\u2019m a good whore,\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0, \"aren\u2019t I, $FIRSTNAMEM1?\"", "1_11_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to noon</span>. This luncheon party was on the terrace of a small private hotel on the slopes of Mount Olympus, overlooking the Gulf of Salerno. The hotel was frequented by the tourist crowd and by writers, of whom there were half a dozen in the party. The writer known as Kokomo claimed to be writing the great American novel dealing with gangster pressures in the film industry. His real name was Gregory Powell. He was thirty-eight years old and a fugitive from a California penitentiary. He was reputed to have killed two men in the course of his lifetime, and to be the only man alive who had raped his sweetheart\u2019s mother.\nAlfred Hitchcock, the writer, had stolen a typewriter from his landlady in Liverpool and had sent the manuscript of his first book to a publisher in London. Since then he had had four books published, the first when he was nineteen. Now he was twenty-seven. Having been an only child and having lacked athletic prowess, he had developed an international reputation for his expertise with women. He also wrote detective stories, having been a disciple of Edgar Allan Poe since he was seven.\nHis real name was James Bond.\nThere were four other writers at the table, none of...", "2_21_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Tuesday the twenty-second of September. On the horizon, to the east, were the towers of the Memorial Auditorium, site of the Inexpensive Shelter Convention.\nAt the Cleveland branch of the YMCA, on E. 115th Street, 750 enlisted men were two minutes from going to bed. At the Soldiers and Sailors Home in the ragged lily valley below Lake Erie, some three hundred veterans of the Great War were going to bed.\nAt the girls\u2019 reformatory on Columbus Road, a twelve-year-old girl, having been punished for assaulting younger children with darts, was going to bed. At eight o\u2019clock that evening, she had pleaded with a matron to be transfered to another home. She had received ten days in the Ohio City Jail for Belligerency.\nIn the Young Men\u2019s Christian Association Building, on Euclid Avenue, there was a party for two hundred enlisted men, with a low fist of six dollars per civilian invitee. The theme of the party was \u201cCruise \u201942\u2014Meet Me in Honolulu, Baby!\u201d", "1_4_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty in the morning</span>. A windstorm was looming, eddying up the sand, sighing in the scrubby palms of the empty city, rattling windows in some abandoned barracks in the slum called Jio Heights. Dunes were moving against the empty abandoned whitewashed houses in Dietho\u2019s Mound. A windstorm was brewing. It would waste half a day screwing golfball drifts of sand into intersections, before blowing itself out.\nRemedial sign painters had hastily slapped a fresh coat of \u201cAsmodeus\u201d green over the vats at the St. Erazmo Home for the Orderly Insane, over the vats they used to dissolve corpses. Flies immediately began to buzz properly madly around the lime scent of the accelerant. Avarice Streamcrest, sitting like a mummy in the parlor of his shattered mansion, had had to send his 3060 Cadillac limousine to the junk dealer. Gribley Harshbarger of technical services could summon neither the time nor the motivation to check the tubes and cables in his huge, ungainly patricidal computer.\nA Citizen\u2019s Band radio was playing in Kudos Rowe\u2019s office. It was a hit song...", "1_23_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-one in the evening</span> of March 2, 1986, and in the news-room of the Stalag 17 Slack Bureau of Standards, the tired, demented, and catatonically bored night editor (having made his hourly call to the police station across the street and been told that everything was status quo ante) was about ready to call it a day\u2014i.e., it being 3:00 A.M., July 14, 1352. Hardly anyone would notice if he shut down.\nThe editor was remembering July 14, 1352, because it had been the day Genghis Khan was born. The editor was a great fan of Genghis Khan.\nHe was looking at five photographs of Genghis Khan, taken in five different centuries. There were two portraits by Seph [pronounced \u201cSeps, \u201d like \u201csepsis\u201d], two paintings by Rubens, and a tinted photograph made in 1916 by the Finnish neo-Impressionist, Edvard Munch, who had been a witness to the actual birthing of Genghis Khan. These were the five best portraits of Genghis Khan that the editor had been able to find.\nThe editor was holding these five photographs to his five eyes.", "1_19_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-seven in the evening</span>. She still hadn\u2019t eaten supper. She loved to wander around the house barefoot, at times like this, looking for a place to sit down and light, a preference always for the kitchen or the living room. One at a time lighted up, somewhere, while the dark stayed outside and she picked her way through it, a room of spices and lamps, tables, ashtrays, and chairs that collapsed like old beds, soft and hard at the same time. Horsehair stuffing that puffed out of ticking covers. The little kids hadn\u2019t eaten dinner either. They watched her out of sight, haunting her, breath held, until she got back from the light. Carlo\u2019s clothes, on their hangers, the sleeves of his old slicker poking stiffly out from under the winter coat, turned backwards from the other hangers in the clothescloset, frayed places where he\u2019d seized it, twitching. The blanket on the children\u2019s bed was turned down in a rectangle as though an animal had lain there, glowing and humid after a bath, the impression filling up now and fading. The beds were unmade, a demand that food or a bath be met by someone. She felt...", "2_23_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty in the evening</span>.\nThe place was Cabin 46 of a Space Wanderer class starship.\nThe ship was an iron-walled cylinder with no portholes, with bulges at bow and stern that housed working organs almost too complex for maintenance engineers to understand.\nThe ship was turning, tail first, toward the bright radiation out there.\nHer designers had, of course, been figurative optimists when they dreamed up that neat submissive maneuver. The ship was going nowhere, because there was nowhere left to go in a Sun-starved cosmos.\nThe ship was a fortress against a hostile universe.\nThe ship was a home.\nThe ship was a nest.\nThe nest was stacked with peas-in-a-pod capsules. The nest was home to six hundred and fifty thousand fertile Earthlings.\nThe ship was named the Streaker.\nThe ship was an armed wanderer, though most of her armament had been removed or neutralized before her disguise as a tramp freighter.", "0_22_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>; the Rue d'Odessa was dark and very quiet. The only light on the street came from a mercury-vapor lamp under a red hood at the bus-stop. A bus, SO 6, stopped under the hood, and a few passengers got out and walked along the Rue d'Odessa to the Boulevard Circulaire.\nAnother minute passed, and another bus, SO 7, stopped and discharged passengers on the other side of the street from the SO 6.\nTen more seconds and SO 9 stopped and emptied its passengers next to the SO 7.\nThen, suddenly, under the SO 10 hood on the Rue d'Odessa, there was a dull flash of blue-white light. One second later, under the SO 11 hood at the bus-stop on the other side of the street there was a similar flash.\nFive seconds passed, then under the hood at the middle of the block of the Rue d'Odessa there was a third flash.\nThe flashes had come at one-second intervals. They were triggered by a switch thrown in the Odessa Substation of the Paris Electricity Company. It had been thrown by a man named Armbruster, who stood at the window of a ground-story...", "2_2_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>. Sergeant Melvin Wimpey Nolcott was sitting in a chair made of solid iron, with iron chains for legs. The chair was, in fact, an exact replica of the chair that $FIRSTNAMEF1 had just put away. The chair on which Sergeant Nolcott sat was in the barracks of the Space Wanderer\u2019s training division. There were two identical barracks buildings. Half of the recruits were housed in one building, half in the other.\nSergeant Nolcott sat in a state of clear and characteristic inhuman misery. He was a short and un-athletic man with a neck that tended to reddish flabbiness.\nThe captain of the division stood before the seated sergeant, glared down at him. The captain was a tall, lean man named $SURNAME2. He was a perfect specimen of physical manhood. He was almost too handsome and strong to be intelligent. And he was intelligent. He was respected and feared by every person with whom he came in personal contact.", "1_22_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> and I was all alone. The place was deserted. No, not quite deserted. Behind the first row of slot machines there was one man standing behind a quarter slot machine. He was obviously asleep on his feet. On the arm of the quarter machine he was leaning and sleeping behind it, his head nodding in and out of sleep every few seconds. I walked over to him. He came awake instantly.\n\u201cWhat kind of slot machine is this? I wouldn't think a quarter would last two minutes on it.\u201d\n\u201cLady dropped her purse in it. Half hour ago.\u201d He nodded. He was a grizzled man about forty, with the face of a sleepy bird.\n\u201cOh,\u201d I said. I took out a dollar bill from my wallet and folded it lengthwise, and then lengthwise again. I put the money in the slot on top of the dollar bill. Then I pressed the button that delivered one cent of credit. I saw the cent drop into the one-cent bin. I pressed again: another cent. And then again. I started to press the lever rapidly, but the operator tapped my hand with his pencil.\n\u201cPlease, mister, you'll burn her out.", "0_0_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>, according to a digital clock on the table in front of Hilarius's chair. \"My God, why doesn't somebody call the police?\" she demanded.\nIn Countrysides of the Mind, Hilarius wordlessly pointed to an incomplete chess problem. He had been working on it, apparently, when the sounds in the hallway had aroused his interest. $FIRSTNAMEF2 took it from the book, trying to figure out the position. The panel ended. But Hilarius swiftly took the book back and drew a thicker volume from his breast pocket, bound in black leather. In it were continued, on every page, the expansion of the chess position, always with a new move at the top of each column.\n\"You can keep that,\" he said angrily. \"It\u2019ll be my last edition, anyhow.\" The only publishers who would touch the work, he explained, were the Phantasmists, and they\u2019d taken his advance money and abandoned him when the code of the paperback edition was altered.\n\"You might as well give up,\" Hilarius added, watching her flip quickly through the book, looking for words. \"The system is infinite. Each move is any of 5250 possibilities. Try to write it...", "0_16_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four sixteen in the afternoon</span>, by the glass thermometer outside the iron door to the lobby of the Anubis Hotel. That meant it was ten sixteen in the morning in Washington, D.C. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 patted his face with a wad of cotton, making certain no razor nicks or traces of salve were visible.\nThere he was\u2014sitting in his chair in his office in the glass-walled $SURNAME0 mansion, receiving Martians by appointment. The first Martian to bring him good tidings was old Salo, $SURNAME0\u2019s butler. He was in the act of inserting a blank piece of paper in the typewriter on $SURNAME0\u2019s bare teak desk.\nA light was blinking on the telephone there. The telephone had been manufactured to ring twelve distinct tones in twelve distinct keys. The key in use now was the key of C-sharp\u2014the key labeled Chris.\n\"Yes, Your Excellency,\" said Salo to the image of $SURNAME0 in the mirrored wall. He put the paper in the roller of the typewriter. \"I will certainly type your message for you.\" Salo\u2019s actual name was $SURNAME2. This was a name he had chosen himself...", "1_5_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>. I closed my eyes, exhausted. The huge planes roared on. I saw each plane as it passed. I had failed to realize that the work would be so hard. I didn't know an awful lot and my mind broke down and gibbered at the constant speed of the planes, somewhere close by in the thunder around me, and through it I was busy gibbering with my poor frayed and frantic mind.\nPeople began to bang on the door, the alarm clock, somebody called. I got up to open the door and tell them I did want to get up, I had gone to bed so late last night. But when I opened my eyes and looked around I saw no one and nothing, I was in bed, back in my room. But I wasn't in my bed because something wasn't right with my back, there was some obstacle in my back. I needed to turn over. The alarm clock blared. I had never slept on the floor before, it was nice in that soft, snug rug. An ant walked on my face, a crackle in my hair. The alarm clock went PLUNK. I got up and actually shooed the ant out of the room. I snatched up my diary and looked in it.", "1_17_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I reached Vancouver the next morning, just twenty-four hours after I made my nephew the present of my car. I was thirty miles south of the Canadian border, about to pass through Grand Coulee Dam, when I first noticed the vibrations.\nThe damn thing was making a hell of a noise, too\u2014a kind of screaming whine that seemed to come from the back seat.\nI slowed to fifty and looked at my watch. The time was exactly 7:05. Canada loomed ahead.\nThe vibrations grew worse, and now I saw why: The fucker had gone into reverse. I jammed on the brake but it was too late. The goddamn thing had locked into low and the pedal hit the floor. The rear of the heap smashed into a nice new Ford and I began shaking hands with a sheepish young man in the back seat of his car. It could not have been a worse embarassment, even if I'd been pissing in my pants...which I wasn't. \u201cThank God,\u201d he said. \u201cIt's an American car. I thought it might be German.\"\nI looked at him with disgust. \u201cHow long has it been doing this?\u201d He shrugged. \u201cMost of...", "0_1_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe resistance leaders moved swiftly across the hall, walking on tiptoe, to Room 223. Fog or no fog, they had to run the gauntlet of the lobby again.\nThe desk clerk in Bledsworth Megal\u2019s office building said good morning to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 in a barely civil tone when $SURNAME1 came down from his penthouse at two o\u2019clock in the morning.\nIt was foggy outside. The small well-dressed man was accompanied by others. He carried a small piece of paper in his large hand. Unsigned, it said: Expect a surprise visitor at any moment.\nBusy adjusting his cuff links as he strode, $SURNAME1 barely looked at the man behind the desk. \"Thank you, good night,\" he said, stepping into a waiting elevator. As the elevator doors closed, he was in time to see the desk man look up in surprise\u2014at Miss young-and-beautiful Sinew, who was standing dutifully beside the desk.\nAt that moment the hotel lobby blew up in a ball of fire mixed with black smoke.\nThe explosion shattered every window in the building.\nFor six whole seconds nothing came out of the building but smoke.\nThen the screams began.", "2_15_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I handed Ribs the dollar because I was getting so flustered about the bus and Lola and the key envelope that I couldn't eat my soup.\nRibs looked at it, said, \u201cMan, I don't want to take your money.\u201d\nI hurried out of the hotel. There was $FIRSTNAMEB3, running down the street to the American Office on the waterfront, at the edge of the town, at a madhouse pace. I feared he'd miss the bus, which was now loading up outside Lumumba's bar. He leaped inside.\n\u201cWhere your luggage?\u201d the conductor yelled.\n\u201cLuggage? What luggage?\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 yelled back, withamba grin. \u201cWhere's $FIRSTNAMEB6?\u201d he asked me.\n\u201cRacing out of town at one minute to four.\u201d\n\u201cGreat. Great. Great.\u201d\nWe sat down in back of the bus, on top of the brown paper bags that contained our luggage, including his \u201cWhorehouse Ballads\u201d which I held on my lap.\n\u201cNow tell me.\u201d", "1_3_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-seven in the morning</span>. The air base was aswarm with people\u2014firemen, Air Force men, and all the bigshotsecurity troopers from the White House detail. I spotted a driver from the Press Office who had handled my ChicagoII story, and he took me in with the stampede. Some goddamn fool had set the jet\u2014carport ablaze with a firebomb, and it looked like it would take years to repair the damage. But worst of all was the sight of that incredible White House gompartment convoyer roaring up to the main terminal in the veryheart of the fire\u2014and hundreds of cheering and excited airportworkers running to meet it...then watching it screech to ashock halt right there in front of you, with paranomads climbing up into it. and T shirt freaks grabbing open the doors, and Illinois citizens grabbing at Nixon papers and souvenirs, before the whole damned caravan slewed around like the winning racer in Monaco and roared back out from the airport and\u2014as we all know\u2014split straight down in two and thundered away in two completely opposite directions, far beyond any possibility of recall or regroupingin this world.\n8. Loss Of The Body Garment\u2014Denver, Colorado\u2014midnight, November 7, 1972.", "1_10_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past ten o'clock in the morning</span> of a fine March day. It was not long after the first of the miracles had been celebrated as a yearly National Festival.\nIn memory of the First Miracle, which had been the very first celebration of Mars as an inhabited planet, an inhabited planet somewhat in trouble but by no means counting itself out in the fight.\nIn the seventy-seven standard years since life had come to the mild green valleys of Mars, Mars had exploded ten atomic bombs, made hot enough to melt rock and boil water. Mars had launched probes, bound with the power of 40,000 tons of TNT, toward Earth. Mars was suspected of having squirted still more powerful missiles at Earth\u2014missiles that had exploded harmlessly in space.\nThe First Miracle had resulted from the firing of one such Missile by an apparently reformed Martian by the name of $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s dog had been killed when the Missile was fired. The dog had been stuffed in a suit of space armor and strapped to the long, narrow, contoured couch that, in the $FIRSTNAMEM2 suit, was the only way of getting a Martian from one place to another.\n$SURNAME0 was wearing a suit of space armor, too. He was standing on the control panel in the rear...", "2_3_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-six in the morning</span> in Melbourne, Australia. It was October 25, 2142, in North America. Enrico Fermi had died only two hours ago.\n\"Sorry to wake you up like this, fellows,\" said R. Pennyfeather from the doorway.\n\"Let me come in,\" he said, \"and we\u2019ll have a chat.\"\nWhat do you know about Melbourne, Australia?\nFor openers, here is a list of the things you don\u2019t know about Melbourne, Australia.\nNo.\nTopic\nWeight(approx.)\nNever arrived in Melbourne, Australia 16 oz.\nOn board ship, but never in Melbourne, Australia 13 lb.\nIn archives of KMMG, Melbourne, Australia 142.5 lb.\nFirst things first.\nLet\u2019s talk about weight.", "0_11_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere were two hundred and fifty-four men in the barracks. One hundred and fifty-four of the men had come from Earth, the fifty-one from Mars, the fifty-one from the asteroid belt, and the fifty-one from Mercury.\nNone of the men had women or children living with them on Mercury. There were only twenty women on the entire planet.\nThe barracks were spacious, clean, and undamaged when 112 and 113 ran through them, looking for recruits.\nThere were other barrack buildings, some of which were not so spacious, clean, and undamaged. And the recruits were living in the other barrack buildings. They had been afraid to sleep in the big buildings after they had been hit by such incredible barrages of thunderbolts and tornadoes.\nThey had been right to be afraid.\nSuch barrages would hit the big buildings again, but no barrages as terrible as the first two ever hit the buildings again.\nThe barrages were on a diminishing curve.\nThere were no barrages at all at 11:22, 11:34, 11:37, 11:40, 11:41, 11:42, 11:43, 11:45, 11:50,...", "0_11_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to noon</span> when we finally hit the boulevard Raspail, and at that moment, as if on cue, everyone in the restaurant applauded. I heard our friends at the next table laughing. \u201cYou see what you have won? Salut!\u201d Their drink tickets were spent, they had the check, the car was waiting and they were gone.\nWe had another cognac and then moved across the boulevard and into the American Express office. I took my package and we went up to get our baggage check and buy tickets for a Rome flight in the late afternoon. That done, I bought a Herald-Tribune and we walked down to the Od\u00e9on, watching all the people, racing the short noon hour and thinking of beer. Suddenly I realized it was Sunday and there would be no beer at the D\u00f4me, my favorite Sunday bar.\nAh, well. The thought struck me as funny. I laughed out loud and told Terry about it in English. She laughed, too. Suddenly neither one of us could stop laughing. We were standing in the middle of the sidewalk and everybody was looking. \u201cCome on!\u201d I yelled. \u201cWe\u2019ll drink all the time!\u201d\nThen, when we got over...", "2_1_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-one in the morning</span>.\n\"That\u2014unsettled me very much,\" $FIRSTNAMEF0 said. She lay in bed with her hands clasped behind her head.\n\"History,\" said $SURNAME0, \"is more like a novel written by a man with a brain tumor than it is like anything anybody else could write.\"\n\"You said a mouthful,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"I\u2019m glad you still have my copy of Unfinished Symphony,\" said $SURNAME0. He stood at the foot of $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s bed. \"I wouldn\u2019t have given it to you unless I\u2019d known you\u2019d be so important in a dangerous time.\"\n\"Dangerous?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"A glamorous time, you mean.\"\n$SURNAME0 sniffed. \"Hmm,\" he said. \"What about that other man on Mars with you\u2014the man you let think he was the instrument of God\u2019s will, when actually he was just a klutz like all the rest of us?\"\n\"Oh\u2014Skip?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. She shrugged. \"That\u2019s over with.\"", "0_21_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-eight in the evening</span>.\nStrum was in the room that was supposed to be $SURNAME1\u2019s. The room overlooked the back yard. The room was high up, safe from the water.\nAnd the best room in the Newport Beach residence for what Strum now had to do, anyway. A tachistoscope. He had to try it one more time.\nHe looked at his wristwatch, a Seiko chronometer, the cheapest he could find that showed the day of the week and the date. He wanted to be sure he\u2019d end up naked at midnight on the right day of the week.\nThere were all the instructions and theory for the tachistoscope in a little black book he had bought for two dollars and ninety-five cents. It was called Thought Through Movies by someone with a German name. The projector in the Thought Through Movies kit had cost an additional three dollars and twenty-five cents. Strum had bought it in Hollywood.\nThe instructions and theory in Thought Through Movies went like this:\n1. Assemble the kit according to directions.\n2. When you have assembled the kit, test the projector. Show a blank movie, in your head, twenty-four hours a day, for two weeks. This will...", "2_21_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-eight in the evening</span>.\n/c/\n\u201cI did not get anything out of that at all.\u201d\n\u201cMe neither.\u201d\n\u201cLord God in heaven.\u201d\n/a/\n\u201cLenore.\u201d\n\u201cYes, Mr. Vigorous.\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cYou called my name.\u201d\n\u201cHell, no I didn\u2019t.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cThere\u2019s a shadow just beyond the porthole.\u201d\n\u201cI do not see a shadow beyond the porthole.\u201d\n\u201cThere. Look.\u201d\n\u201cI see nothing. Are you sure?\u201d\n\u201cTotally and indubitably.\u201d\n\u201cYou slept with your eyes open just then. Is that a significant development?\u201d\n\u201cI didn\u2019t sleep at all. Not at all.\u201d\n\u201cIs it my imagination, or should the sun not be streaming in at this time, through the blinds, into your room?\u201d", "1_1_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty in the morning</span> and the place was The Beverly-Western Hotel in Chicago, in the lobby. The main elevators stank, so we rode to the fancy lobby on a little custard\u2014color elevator with white Samarkand fan\u2014blades and a copper door. It stank, too.\nWe got one of the red leather couches. The night porter was snoring in a chair across the lobby. A Chinese rat about two feet long, with a bushy tail, was trying to get out through the radium grating on the bottom of a nutmeg\u2014grater set in the wall below the upper row of phone booths, next to the ladies\u2019 john. The rat kept poking his head out and looking around. A little light came off the grating down onto the rat. He couldn\u2019t climb the smooth marble wall below the grating. Every time he tried he would slip back. We could hear him scrabbling.\n\"Did you put him there?\" I asked my friend, pointing at the rat.\n\"No, that\u2019s always been there, for twenty years, I understand.\"\n\"Why dontcha get rid of the rat?\"\n\"Because it\u2019s just part of the hotel. This lobby...", "1_0_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was midnight</span>. We were high in the Andes on a desolate plain called the Pampas, in an area of Northern Chile called Arica, high above the smoky seaside port, beside a twisted cliff where vultures and wrecked ships, bleached ribs of dead animals, stick all day in the sun. Above thunders a treacherous crossroad of barroom lions, stone eyes on all the teahouses and brothels. Annual meetings of all the beasts. Decisions from the bottom of everybody's soul and body. (I kept waiting for Lucky Luciani or some other hood to show and make trouble.) Mucho macho here, a place where anything goes but nothing changes. There were two beaten Alfa Romeos parked by a stone convent wall and six Christian children sitting with an old woman inside the wall, only their heads and the tops of their crucifixes visible through the cactus fence. Everything right out of a film. Our driver informed us that terrible things happen in Arica on moonlit nights, and he refused to stay with the car. A small, round, riffraff hood from the tavern promised to watch it for seventy pesos and drive us back to Lima. The gaunt young truck driver agreed, said he worked nights,...", "1_15_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s home in a hangar on the space center. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s home was a reader that had been installed in a corner for safety\u2019s sake. The walls were blank, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had nothing else but the reader and his Army-issue blankets.\nThe important thing was to get a list of the heavy things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 could find around the space center in a hurry.\nCarefully $FIRSTNAMEB0 used the fingernail file on the blades of the pocketknife, smoothing the sharpest blade and the ripping blade.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been guilty of something or other, and he had been sentenced to death. For his last meal on earth he had been served a chicken. They had cooked the chicken in an oven in the kitchen, and they had only cooked it until it hadn\u2019t been red inside anymore.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t want to go to eternity with food poisoning, so he had scraped the chicken\u2019s interior clean. He had cleaned it out to the very last little bit and scraped flakes of burnt skin off the skeleton.\nWhen he had finished scraping, $FIRSTNAMEB0 put the skeleton back in the carcass, connected the drumsticks with a sewing of...", "1_0_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past midnight</span>. \u201cLet\u2019s go to the Ritz,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB5. \u201cI\u2019m damned if I\u2019m going to bed.\u201d\nIn the caf\u00e9 at the Ritz we drank coffee and sat at a table watching the people go by on the sidewalk outside. It was very hot in the room.\n\u201cIt makes me sweat,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB5.\nShe looked very nice. We had been sitting there for half an hour.\n\u201cI must go to the club,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. \u201cI\u2019m on the list but God knows how I\u2019ll get in. Perhaps they\u2019ll let me in because I\u2019m a disaster.\u201d\n\u201cHow do you mean a disaster?\u201d\n\u201cThe fireworks are over.\u201d\n\u201cWhat fireworks?\u201d\n\u201cMy youth and my beauty.\u201d\n\u201cYou\u2019ve got both of those.\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s what I mean, you dear pessimistic thing. You\u2019ll pay for that remark. I\u2019ll clip your wings. I\u2019ll hurt your hand when we shake hands good-by to-...", "0_18_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-one in the evening</span>. And I was in a great hurry to-go somewhere, I knew not where. I stood before a great, tall garage, and without having to go in I knew, instantly I knew, that $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 were in there and I rushed right in, but they were gone. I located the man and pried a fat-tired IBM, his boss's car, right off the elevator shaft with my hands and took off. I was around a roaring salon of tables and at seven-thirty I was sitting in the fabled old? Long Branch bar in New Orleans with my feet up on a rail and talking to a young lush called Eddie Tall. Behind everywhere a humming came of traffic, like bees in the trees. \u201cIt's time to go, man.\u201d\nI was looking for $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 and wanted money to gas up the car. \u201cWhere is the blankety-blank bitch?\u201d I said aloud.\nTall was a Southern boy with big sad eyes, handsome dim face, curly black hair, and frilly whole servant man's outfit, just back from the Korean War and flunking around with his hands in his pockets. He was persuaded to lend me two dollars for gas...", "2_21_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-three in the evening</span>.\n\u201cIf I may, R.V. said you had a perfectly lovely jaw.\u201d\n\u201cReally.\u201d\n\u201cHe twice mentioned the word \u2018lovely.\u2019 \u201d\n\u201cR.V. has exquisite taste in women.\u201d\n\u201cHe mentioned that you sometimes believe yourself to be really tiny, like three millimeters tall.\u201d\n\u201cNot\u2014exactly a secret, is it?\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cHardly a state secret, I\u2019d say.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cI would\u2019ve thought it fairly conspicuous, anyway.\u201d\n\u201cAre you tired, Neil?\u201d\n\u201cNot tired.\u201d\n\u201cYou appear tired to me, Neil.\u201d\n\u201cThe bottom line is that Vlad puts in his appearance. He comes up out of the bottom of his cage and starts walking right along the bars, right past the glass Steve\u2019s on the other side of, and right over to me. Thumps his chest with his fist and says what I understand to be Sanskrit .... \u201d", "2_20_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-four in the evening</span>, too.\nSomewhere in the Universe was a bar. This bar was the Stonewall. No wonders had been devised there for the entertainment of its patrons. No food, or tobacco, or alcohol of any type was served. You got your kicks from the company of your fellows. And from the drinks served by the management.\nThe bar had a name\u2014Drinks Galore.\nThe Stonewall spaceman had arrived at Drinks Galore at twenty-five minutes before eight in the evening. He huddled in a corner. It was a slow Monday night.\nThe Stonewall spaceman sat in the last booth, alone. The company he kept was a quart of rough, double-concentrate Nausets.\nThe Stonewall spaceman was an old man, an old veteran of the Martian-phobe war. He was bearded and tanned, had brown eyes and a limp. His name was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nAnd now he sat in the last booth of Drinks Galore, downing his Nausets as fast as he could pour them out.\nHe felt very much like a fool.", "2_0_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. A time for cynicism and romance, for dancing in the rain. Let us forget for a moment about life being a thing that is given to you, and deal instead with the idea that life is a thing that you go out and look for...\nWHAT THE MAP REPEATS\u2014WHEN IT REPEATS THE ROCK...\n... AND NOW THE GIGGLE OF THE SECRET, THE WHISPER, THE CAVE BEER, THE TREASURE SECRETE OF THE BUSINESS MAN, THE LIZARD TRAINER, THE SALESMAN, THE SHILL, OF THE HUMAN PERSON WHOSE DEEDS & THOUGHTS ARE WIRED THROUGH VARIOUS SELF-PROPERTIES OF STAFFORD CORNHUSKIS...AND FIND, AS WITH THE WORKINGS OF THE PALACE WINDOW, THAT THERE IS A REASON IN IT, A REASON THAT REITERATES ITSELF LIKE THE ALGEBRA OF ONLY THE GREATEST LOGICIAN.\n\u2014the letter before last in Malachis 3:22-24a\n/a/\n\u201cYon Cassius has a lean and hungry look;", "1_1_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until two o'clock in the morning</span>, and the gang that had congregated in the San Remo apartment at twelve-fifteen was scattered. There were a few who hadn't yet left, but they were going. Hart, after seeing off his girl, was ready to hit the hay. I had lost track of Bill. Ed Dunkel was sitting alone in the dark, melancholy.\n\"Where's $FIRSTNAMEB3?\" I asked.\n\"I don't know. He was here a minute ago. He sent word that he'd like to see you.\"\nI walked over to my window in the dreary light of dawn and looked out at the street. No one. The lights were all on downstairs. Strange. I didn't understand. $FIRSTNAMEB3 had sent word he'd like to see me. What did that mean? Where was he? What had all these sleepless, drink-stupored hours brought to everyone? What had they brought to me? I no longer worried about that; it was all part of the bizarritude of my life that nothing could ever alter. I had long stopped thinking about why I did anything. I was beyond all that. If nothing mattered, then nothing mattered.\nIt was a month later, on the morning of my twenty-...", "2_12_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past noon</span>.\nWhen it all ended, the bodies lay on the floor as they had fallen. Watermelon Smiles was blue. He was in the final position into which his living body had been compressed by the pressure of acceleration.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had fallen prostrate, still bound to their compression sleeves, still bound to each other.\nThe goldness in the rocks was gone.\nThe goldness had happened to their brains on Earth.\nNow the goldness in the rocks before their eyes was just a figment of their disappointment.\nThe glorious future had slipped through their fingers.\nTheir eyes began searching floor, walls, ceiling for possibilities.\nAnything might happen now, might be in store.\nTheir eyes found it, ran through the possibilities.\nThere was nothing cold about them now.\nTheir little pea brains were hot as hell. There was mischief in the air.", "1_5_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five seventeen in the morning</span>, Earth eastern standard time, three months, four days, and five hours after the firing of $SURNAME0 and his space ship.\nThe Sun\u2019s relative motion in space had brought it to the vicinity of the star Alpha Centauri.\nThe great earthquake continued to subside.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 did what most people would have found the easiest thing in the world to do in the circumstances\u2014he covered his ears with his hands. $SURNAME1 had scarcely felt the earthquake. He was in a tunnel sixteen feet underground. A series of rolling concussions overhead made him feel like a man in a subway during an air raid.\nHe longed to get outside, to get away from the roaring walls.\nHe longed to be up in the open again, to be hit and bruised once more by natural phenomena.\nNothing had worked right since he had been yanked unwillingly into space and time.\nThere had been a plan to salvage something from the remains of his life on Earth. His wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, had been sent to find him\u2014he could usually depend on her good sense. She would arrive in a few days in the new $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibulum. The two of them and the dog would take up residence...", "2_23_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-eight in the evening</span> in Phoebeville, Tennessee, U.S.A.\nIt may have been eleven thirty-six on the Moon.\nAnd it may have been time for a lonely man in a space suit, shuttling between the Earth and the Moon, to begin to think about coming home.\nWithout a word to Gene, a neighbor for whom he felt nothing but disgust, a neighbor to whom he had not been introduced, an intruder, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 disappeared from his cottage on Newport Greenland.\nA congealed, jamlike material as red as Mars and as thick as childhood ignorance filled the black, tubelike contrivance in the gazebo.\nThe tubelike contrivance was an amoeba\u2019s idea of space travel.\nThe spread-eagled man it held was asleep.\nHis feet dangled two feet above the iron of the concrete path. Only his face, encased in a space suit, was visible. There was grass clippings and earth on the back of his suit.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was in every literal sense a man from Mars.", "1_17_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>. It was time to open the great envelope of contraband space-maps. It was time to warm up the shoe machine.\nThere was a knock at the door.\n\"Hell, \" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Go away! \" said $SURNAME0.\n\"It\u2019s me\u2014Skip,\" said a small voice outside the door.\n\"Come in, child\u2014come in,\" said $SURNAME0.\nThe child-god entered. His golden suit was sopping wet.\n\"I wish I could tell you how much I admire your courage, child,\" said $SURNAME0 silkily. \"You know how to tell a lie, and you know how to keep a secret, too.\"\nThe little boy hung his head humbly.\n\"You\u2019d only get yourself and your mother into trouble if you told what you saw on Mars,\" said $SURNAME0. \"If you want a neighborly piece of advice, I\u2019ll give it to you. Whenever you start to tell about seeing the capsule on Mars\u2014just don\u2019t.\" He twinkled. \"It\u2019s just your ordinary, run-of-the-galaxy secret.\"\n\"Thank you\u2014Mr. Rumfo...", "1_15_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. I was very calm by then. I had the scroll and enough prophecies to fill a book, and I had found a secret that the Illuminati reckoned to be the ultimate secret of the universe. I was ready to sleep for a week. All I had to do now was holster the Browning, put on my socks, sandals and Nehru jacket, and start the adventure of getting the hell out of Los Angeles\u2014which, by the way, I had decided to rename San Lucifer. I couldn\u2019t move. The noise had reached incredible heights. The footfalls of forty million Black Panthers were beating a steady cyanotic tattoo on every nerve in my body. I slumped down on the carpet and waited for it to be over.\nI didn\u2019t know how long I waited\u2014maybe two hours, maybe five. During one part of the experience, a woman with long silver hair and wearing a white diaphanous space suit drifted across the room from the door. I watched her approach through a kind of drunken stupor. She seemed strangely familiar. She floated across the floor in a slow, easy curve until she was only a few feet away. Finally she stopped and smiled down at me.\n\u201cHello, Aspirin,...", "1_20_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-six in the evening</span>, and I was at home, in Sykton. I was leaning out of an open window, in my underwear, smoking a cigar.\nRile Company kept certain floors in the Sykton Building for its own personnel. The wild-er people went to Las Vegas, (See, Hot Water ) and so San Francisco. Boise, Idaho, was about as far in the other direction as you could get with a Trips, Inc., expense account. Wherever you went, if you got back Rile Company would pick you up at the airport, ride with you to the hotel and see that you got there with your baggage. The big white vans of Rile Company were always waiting outside the baggage-claim area of whatever airport you pulled into.\nActually, Rile, Jr., sometimes picked up stockholders and/or top executives at random. He loved to ride around San Francisco in his white Cadillac convertible at high speeds, with the top down. Sometimes he drove like a bats, but it was all done with mirrors, sleight of hand and precision at seventy-five miles an hour through the crooked streets, saying, \u201cHeyyou!\u201d At sixt y, he\u2019d rear-end somebody on purpose and stop to...", "0_0_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-four a.m.</span> The crickets were still singing in the vineyards and the sky was a majestic hue of plum and indigo. We heard a car in the distance and at first we thought it would drive by everything was so quiet and tapering off, but it slowed down, the motor sounded like it was misfiring, and it turned in to the road that wound through the vineyards and came up the hill to our house.\nI squinted into the headlights. The car bumped slowly up the road. \u201cWonder who that is at this time of night,\u201d I said. I clutched the railing.\nMarie smiled. \u201cEverybody\u2019s asleep.\u201d\nThe car stopped in the driveway. I heard a klunk. The headlights died. There was silence, punctuated only by a fly whining under the eaves of the house and the sound of the crickets in the vines dying away, one by one. I went over to the veranda rail, holding the white fluted column, and peered down the shallow steps into the driveway. \u201cHey you!\u201d I yelled. \u201cWhat do you think you\u2019re doing?\u201d\nIt was as if I\u2019d yelled at the sunrise...", "0_13_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 were materializing again. They would appear for only four seconds.\nThey would appear in space about a foot from the open door of the control shed, just outside Providenciales. They would appear in the cold, the pure material of space itself. There would be no National Health oxygen tanks for them.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog, like all materializing persons or creatures, would be fully and uncomfortably materialized before they appeared. Their materialization would involve not only the materialization of flesh, skin, and clothes\u2014but of all the things they or their pockets could possibly hold.\nFor $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, this meant luggage, a dressing gown, and a dog.\nThe luggage for a man about to board his flying island for another twenty-three-million-year tour of the galaxies was naturally elaborate. $SURNAME0\u2019s luggage consisted of two leather suitcases, four paper bags, a hatbox, a portable typewriter case, a wastepaper basket, an eight-by-ten foot, four-inch deep canvas mailbag with leather straps, and an aged tennis stringer bag with the name of a great tennis tournament stitched over...", "2_16_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-five in the afternoon</span>...and the place was a bar on L Street, ten blocks from the White House. An out\u2014ofhouse conference, with official business on the agenda. Drinking was heavy ...and there were drugs, too ...raw sewage, courtesy of the Washington Police Department, who had generously supplied the whole kit and kaboodle to help out with the goddamn Puerto Ricans.\nThe drugs consumed, they hit the bars and picked up girls. One of the delegates fingered a girl named Goldie, grabbed her right there at the bar and walked outside with her. \u201cYou like broads?\u201d he said. \u201cThen you\u2019ll love this one.\u201d\nHis name was Humboldt Humboldt. He was from Fresno, California, and he was in the fertilizer business. White shirt, red suspenders, mad big gold belt buckle on a belly full of beer. \u201cHell yes,\u201d he said. \u201cI love broads. Especially pretty ones like you. Where you from, honey?\"\n\u201cVirginia,\u201d she said. \u201cWhat you doin\u2019 in Washington, sugar?\"", "2_21_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-six in the evening</span>. The time and place was the mouth of a pay phone next to the nurse\u2019s station on the ground floor of the south wing of the Infirmary. The night nurse was working late in the station and knew what the caller wanted, and hadn\u2019t sprayed anything at her, yet. The phone had a newspaper clipp ing taped to its rim. The article was about a concert of the Cleveland Orchestra and its conductor, one Ransom K. Fern, at the Men\u2019s, or Florida, Theater.\n/i/\n\u201cWaaahhhh wah wah wah wah wah wah.\u201d\n\u201cTonight was fine.\u201d\n\u201cWaaaahhhh wah wah wah wah wah wah.\u201d\n\u201cFact is that I\u2019m on the john, if you must know.\u201d\n\u201cExcuse me?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m seated on the john.\u201d\n\u201cOh. \u201d\n\u201cI just want to let you know where I am, lest something unfortunate happen.\u201d\n\u201cI understand.\u201d", "1_4_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. Only an hour had passed since I met Pink Floyd. The clock on the dash said so. That meant I only had ninety minutes to get back to L.A.\nI\u2019d run too many red lights and made too many sharp turns to trust the accuracy of that odometer. The next time I saw a CHP cruising by on the main drag, I stepped on the gas. I got up to 115 before I spotted the red light in the mirror.\nMy attorney was asleep on the back seat. He and the songwriter had made a definite connection on the tequila. They were both snoring peacefully. I hated like hell to wake them.\nI pulled into a service station and got two big cups of coffee mixed with a lot of rum. Then I drove back to my attorney\u2019s car, parked alongside and poured a splash of rum into each cup. I tempted him with a taste\u2014then gave him a hard slap on the face, which is something I do quite often.\nHe sat bolt upright, lashing out with all four limbs. His eyes were all bloody. \u201cWhat the fuck are you trying to do?\u201d he demanded. \u201cKill me?\"\n\u201cYou\u2019re no good...", "0_22_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span> of December 31, acrordling to a luminous octagonal clock with recessed numerals mounted to one side of the door into the room from a third room. The numerals, opposite the motionless minute-hand, read seven minutes to,\nAnd the minute-hand ticked off a full minute, and the numerals now read eight minutes to.\nA very young bearded man mounted the stone steps. In his hand he carried a cardboard carton tied with a string. He wore the uniform of an Officio Defunctorum mourners\u2019 aid. The uniform was of natural linen (the robe, the kerchief and the cardboard cuffs), dark blue (the robe). It featured a plain hood, support crutch (both natural-wood), and shoe (tomato)- of black bombazine. And it was completely covered, this uniform, with enormous black roses, mourning cards. Each rose, or card, was three times the size of a playing card, and of heavy black paper. There were two hundred and six cards, each one exactly the same, on the uniform. They were all edged with black lace and bore the inscription JESUS SAVES on their faces. The young man with the uniform was the Be...", "0_14_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was the only man aboard the million-ton flying fortress who had any consideration for the war effort whatsoever. Everybody else aboard was either drunk or sleeping off a hangover or making crazy love to somebody.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 left his sanctuary behind the access panels under the front desk, came out with his fellow moron, $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was drunk. His good humor was threatended by $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s presence. His idiotic amusement had been punctuated and underlined by sporadic shots from his needler. He had used the needler to punctuate and underline the rhythm of his singsong drinking song:\n\"Apples and pears,\n\"Sittin\u2019 on a tree\u2014\"\nA defenestration key on a chain around $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s neck told $FIRSTNAMEB0 how many people $FIRSTNAMEB1 had killed with his needler. The key was badly tarnished. The chain had been oiled many times.\nAnd the number on the chain was twenty-eight.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was seventy-eight years old, and he had been given the Bronze Star and the Congressional Medal of Honor and the Croix de Guerre with palms and the Distinguished Service Cross and given citations from all the armies of Earth...", "0_0_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 did not know what that irregular punctuation of his life was. All he knew was that it always came.\nAn instant before the external world was to collapse in ruinous warps and wrinkles, $SURNAME0 would turn to his wife and say, \"Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2014\"\nThat was all he said. In all the millions of times a wife had heard those words come from her husband\u2019s lips, she had never known the identity or nature of the man who was addressing her. Mrs. $SURNAME0 naturally took it for granted that, before another moment passed, she would find out.\nBut she never did find out who it was\u2014what feeble and misery-crazed old creature of an Earthling male who dared to come and speak to her so cavalierly without introduction or apology. And for all she ever learned or could learn, it might as well or have been a celestial messenger.\nIt really could have been a celestial messenger, for all Mrs. $SURNAME0 knew. It might have been some messenger from the Billion Year Comet, reporting by codes that had been given him by the Tralfamadorians.\nAfter the messenger had spoken his piece, he disappeared again just as mysteriously as he...", "0_0_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a roadhouse sixty miles north of New York, though you could have thought you were in the mountains of Colorado. Somewhere in the house a girl was singing Leadbelly blues. There were ten or fifteen guys in the bar, two of them were drunk, four were talking baseball, three were drinking rye highballs, two were drinking Canadian club on the rocks, and one lone man with the wristwatch mentioned above was drinking an Old Grandad on the rocks. The atmosphere was excellent, the prices fair, the service just this side of lousy. This was a roadhouse forty miles north of New York.\nThis is a story of two bartenders, two young men in a great city where young men try to sell you things. One got up in the morning, leisurely shaved himself, and made his breakfast of coffee and Fig Newtons\u2014or else Frosted Flakes or Sorth English Muffins, or whatever is his brand. At ten a.m. he opened the bar in a country club or a suburban cocktail lounge; by six p.m. he was through and off to his milk-and-cookie supper of Kraft or Swiss-or-Cheddar Cheese on Toast, whatever you call it. Then home and watch...", "0_14_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-three in the afternoon</span> when I got to the Overland Hotel. I got a room and went upstairs and lay down on the bed fully dressed with my shoes on for a rest before going to meet Terry and Cisco. I did not feel like sleeping but when I closed my eyes and relaxed my mind I was on the road again and this time there were two motorcycle cops with me and we were taking a fast ride in the country after dark. It was hard to keep the road in sight because the motorcycle headlights were so bright I had to squint. But the cops wanted to go fast and I had to stay in the middle of the roal or else they would pass me. I could stay right with them. This went on for miles and miles. Suddenly I was overcome by a flash of fear. The road was disappearing; it was a nightmare, we were off the road and lost. Then it came to me that I had had this flash before, when I was riding with them. That was six hundred miles ago, in Denver. I took a look at the motorcycle cops, straddling their bikes, calm, with their big bullet heads nodding forward occasionally, asleep on their feet. Our speed was moderating. We were about to arrive in some quiet town on the prairie.", "1_21_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span> and the place\u2014for those who wanted to know\u2014the Ginzler House Cabins, on the shores of Lake Audbois. Half a mile away, surly sentries stood outside the iron gates of Fort Saloiser and kept guard over the shore of the lake.\nEven ten miles from Malachia, you didn\u2019t completely escape the War. There were the sentries, for example. There was the fact that every quarter-hour another rocket went off somewhere in the night. And, of course, there was the curfew. Even ten miles from Malachia, you can\u2019t violate the curfew.\nDiagonally across the lake from where the taxi let $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Braddie out, there was a black island\u2014a pyramidal mound surmounted by a cast-iron obelisk. The horrors of Saloiser\u2019s military past were buried under that black island.\nThe only excuse for civilians to be out as late as $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Braddie were was to attend the sounding of the beacon at the top of the cast-iron obelisk on the island. Every night, at ten, the sentries at Fort Saloiser opened fire on the obelisk with their big...", "2_16_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four seventeen in the afternoon</span>...adead hour in Las vegas, and six hundred miles to the west our building was full of people who thought they were still there. The first calls from the drugstore had reached them about an hour before we got in the car, but no panic had set in. Laconic telex messages were exchanged with our main office. Laconic phone calls were made to local police departments in our area. Official mail was carefully filed. And nobody even upgraded our bail\u2014out plan from the \u201cfuckit\u201d category to the \u201clet's break some crockery\u201d category.\nMy attorney kept muttering about this. He wanted to fly\u2014it was all he talked about from the moment we crept out of the CircusCircus parkinglot. It was hard for me to get a handle on this, because I was still bouncing around the room in that drug frenzy: white lights, rainbow stars, feeling that life was misty softdust underfingernails.", "1_17_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five seventeen in the afternoon</span>.\nThere was nothing left to do.\nThe time had come to begin an orderly retreat. And to begin it away from the riot, where the News Desk would not be alarmed. Since an orderly retreat always follows the same routine, I looked around for something like a field phone so I could call Operations and tell them I was on my way.\nNow that the time had come, I didn\u2019t want to leave.\nI had never told anyone that leaving would be like cutting off my arms and legs and then trying to hump my torso across the desert on bleeding stumps. That was what it felt like. But never mind that. It was time to go.\nThe trouble was, I didn\u2019t know where to go.\nIn just a few minutes, the local part of the story would be over. I had to call Operations then and give them a firm ETA for my last batch. That would make it possible for them to keep the block stories moving smoothly. Then I could quit, but not until then.\nIt was important that I get out fast, so I could call L.A. before they shut down for the day and give them the hard numbers: the final slot and the exact time the Mint Gun story...", "2_18_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-one in the evening</span>, and nobody knew where the $119.06 had gone.\nBig Sur, Calif., August 2nd, 1961. Dear $SURNAME0: Shall we have a jolly time, you and I, checking foreknowledge against subsequent event? I have let six months pass since my letter of August 2nd (relayed to you from Newport on my ship the Tristero), so that we may first of all catch up on the fizzling of the Watermelon Scourge.", "2_23_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until midnight</span>.\nInside the central office of the administration building of the barracks, in the huge formica-and-Leone-seating Red Room, sat $SURNAME1, alone. The windows were black.\n$SURNAME1 was on a couch, leaning back stiffly. He was a six-limbed, red-eyed piece of stone carving.\nAt nearly ten P.M., the child would be born. It was Labyrinth\u2019s child.\n$SURNAME1 had been named by Stony Stevenson, the original settler of the planet Mercury. $SURNAME1 was forty-one years old. His form had changed but little in all those years.\nHis slablike head was covered with a short, gray fair hair.\nN. Richard Newton, late Professor of $FIRSTNAMEB2-Mechanics at Harvard, was one of the two greatest mathematicians who had ever lived on Earth. He had proved that anyone with a mind and the use of eleven fingers could multiply numbers in the base eight, otherwise known as octal. He, $SURNAME1, had only ten fingers, but he had a mind, and nothing else.", "0_19_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 felt like a lab animal that has done something deeply out of turn. Any moment now, $FIRSTNAMEB0 felt, he would be punished.\nSo he ran.\nHe ran in terror, ran with his hands over his face, ran right through the middle of a formation. His outriders tried to call him to order. He brushed them aside, blundered on. His panic grew. He didn\u2019t need a straight line to The Barn\u2014he needed cover.\nThe Pan-turians, finding themselves leaderless and without system in the chaos after the debacle at the barrack wall, simply stood where they were. They were as proud, disciplined, and mystically unified as ever. The instant their leader touched them again would be the instant their precision began again.\nIn their immaculate ranks, the outriders beckoned to $FIRSTNAMEB0 to come back and take command. One by one, the outriders themselves were brushed aside by their comrades\u2014who again fell into patterns that had lost their meaning.\nThe sounds of Earth\u2019s biological grief-cycle had risen to a counterpoint to the panic in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind. The rich myriad cries that now filled the great living room were those of...", "0_10_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe man with the coughing fit snorted and sprinted for the men\u2019s room.\n\"Hey that\u2019s Neil Obstat\u2014\" said a student at the next table.\n\"Yes,\" said Lenore.\n\"He sleeps in the Perlman hallway in his underwear in the winter,\" said the student. \"Without a jacket. I mean it\u2019s like forty below, in the winter.\"\n\"And has a bad day today, apparently,\" said Lenore.\n\"And that\u2019s twenty-five-oh-six?\" said the student, looking at her badge. \"I have twenty-six.\"\n\"Yes,\" said Lenore. She sipped her coffee and looked over the top of her cup at the student. Student clothes bore, as a class, little relation to the kinds of clothes students might wear anywhere but school.\n\"My name\u2019s Sue Ellen Veer,\" the student said. \"I think I may know your great-grandmother. Or at least I think my mother does, or maybe my aunt, who definitely does. Are you a sophomore?\"\n\"The name\u2019s Lenore Beadsman. You may indeed know my Great-Aunt Lenore...", "1_6_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-three in the morning</span>. Nothing was moving on chocolate, not even the wind. It was arrested in a slow, hot hiss. A watch ticked loudly on the table by the bed. Many watches ran fast on Titan. It would have taken paranoia of a most special sort to think they were all going slowly together.\nThere was a knock on the door.\n\"Did you call, sir?\" said a voice.\n\"Yup,\" said $SURNAME1.\nThere was no more knocking on the door for anybody else. The porter didn\u2019t give a damn. It was all the same to him if anybody else in the hotel starved to death this morning.\n\"Breakfast for one, roll and coffee, please,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Yes, sir,\" said the porter.\n\"You\u2014uh\u2014never saw me, you don\u2019t exist, and I\u2019m not going to pay you,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Thank you, sir,\" said the porter. \"I have a room on the fifth floor, and I pay half of what you don\u2019t pay.\"\n\"What a system!\" exulted $SURNAME1.\n\"Book me a flight back to Terra as soon as possible,\" he said to the man...", "2_2_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two o'clock in the morning</span> in Finland, and eight o'clock in the morning in Hollywood, and the things in the sky and on Earth were far more exciting than anything occurring in the German restaurant in Indianapolis.\nBut tradition said that the family should meet and eat together at the German restaurant.\nTradition triumphs over excitement every time.\nTradition had taken the Kroner family from their beds that morning at six o\u2019clock and had brought them, via planes, trains, automobiles, cabs, and foot-power, to the restaurant by twelve noon. Here they had drunk beer and talked loudly.\nNow, as the afternoon progressed, they were still more or less drunk, and they began to eat the thick slabs of German food with increasing diligence.\nAs the afternoon progressed, the news events in the sky and on Earth became more and more exciting. The antics of the copper-colored digit on the wall became almost unbearably exciting to watch. The antics had to do with Mars.\nOne of the two exciting things in the sky was happening on Mars. The crowd in the German restaurant looked at the announcement in the sky every few minutes, to see if the exciting thing had happened.", "0_22_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to eleven o'clock in the evening</span> of Friday, December 2, 1966, as I sat on my bed, holding the black communicator in my hand. I fingered it. Was it a receiver, a radio? I pressed the ON button. The hum grew louder, deeper\u2014and so did I. Yes, I grew taller. I grew taller, to the top of my bedroom. I felt the suction that great profits draw. I felt the madness, the gibber of lizard agate, the lost July 4th of the heart.\nI jumped up! I danced! I stomped my foot on the rug! I shrieked! I was yelling in tongues.\nA face appeared in the darkness of my door. It was my wife's face. She yelled, \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, what is it? What? What?\u201d\n\u201cTelegram!\u201d I shouted. \u201cTelegram! Telegram from my terminal in New York!\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d cried my wife. \u201cWhom from? Why are you yelling that way?\u201d\n\u201cA telegram!\u201d I shouted. \u201cFrom my agent in New York! Probably the manuscript of my new book!\u201d And I grabbed the telegram...", "0_17_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\nClarence is throwing rocks at a cat. The cat sits under a car. The car is up on blocks. The cat is wrapped in tape. The tape is a taped-up cat.\n/e/\nUntil our spectacular and well-deserved defeat at the hands of Industrial Strength compliments of our on-again, off-again ally Frequent and Vigorous, Frequent and Vigorous Credit of New York, and my nemesis, the one and only Wang-Dang Lang, Industrial Strength was of course on top of the world. But even in our arrogant high places we were still required to acknowledge the existence of certain animals\u2014the house cat, for instance. And to accept responsibility for the house cat\u2019s welfare. Even if the cat were really a caged coyote, or perhaps even a mongoose carefully chosen for eventual consumption, still the owner of such an animal is held responsible for that animal\u2019s welfare. This is just common sense. Animals can\u2019t care for themselves. They need to be cared for. Like perpetual motion machines.\nThe Industrial Strength yanked its strings. Money decreed. And that was that. Animals still existed, even among people who denied the existence of...", "0_7_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span> of October 9, 1971, in Stone Street station, just as $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas came awake again. Another Fillmore era had drawn to an end. While her husband did whatever husbands do in threesome situations, that morning she came out of some opal nightmare to the sight of the Macy\u2019s across the street, loomed gigantic and white in the early sun, doing duty for a gouged and depopulated London. It was blowing a mild autumn fog. She remembered fragments of a night spent mainly in provoking Mike Fallopian into talking about Pierce Inverarity. Along with rumors about his sex life, she\u2019d also picked up a few facts useful for her present pursuit. For instance, that towards the end of his life, having in some way been coerced, Inverarity had written a will operating in favor of the Found Secret Welfare Fund. \u201cMaybe,\u201d she\u2019d asked, \u201che was one of your clients.\u201d\n\u201cGod, no,\u201d Fallopian had shaken his head, putting a pencil through the edge of some wheat futures. \u201cThey never give us the big ones. You want a list of all our clients and how much they have in trust with us...", "2_14_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-four in the afternoon</span>. The last person to hear clearly the words that were coming out of $FIRSTNAMEF1-L Creeth\u2019s lips was a chambermaid, second class, by the name of Alice Wachs.\n\"Giddap!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1-L Creeth to her dog.\nEnd of the Project Gutenberg EBook of A Hound of Space, by Mack Reynolds", "2_14_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was a fabulous one\u2014probably no other on earth so perfectly suited for the event that was to take place there. The event was to be the arrival of the Martian attack force. The attack force was the largest ever assembled\u2014five hundred and seventy thousand ships. The ships were to land at four o\u2019clock on the landing strip by the sea. The landing strip by the sea was the only place where they could land.\nThe place was Newport, Rhode Island, U.S.A. In ordinary times, Newport was just an island\u2014a charming, if rather peculiar, one. The island lay at the mouth of the Hudson by New Jersey.\nBut now the pretty little island of Newport by the sea was chockablock full to bursting with men from Mars, who didn\u2019t know a cotillion from a wagon tail-gate party, who were scared to death of Virginia Woollcott, and who were stuffing themselves with spicy New England clam chowder and clam cakes and clam fritters.\nThey did a fine business at the clam stands, selling clam fritters to men from Mars.", "2_9_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was Cleveland, Ohio. It was a street corner, and it was Sixteenth Street and Clifton Boulevard. The small gentleman with the glasses was conversing with Freddy.\n\"I promoted a rumpus when they wouldn\u2019t let colored GI\u2019s in a democracy-loving nightclub in Miami,\" said Freddy. \"I got kidded in the papers for doing it, but I said what I thought.\"\nAnd the small gentleman nodded his head and listened to Freddy. He was an eminent gentleman of infinite charm, and he had started out in life as a salesman. He was a salesman no longer. He had become an immensely wealthy man, and his immortal night-club sketch had made him famous. He was famous.\nNow he was shaking his head sadly. There were ten thousand people jammed in a block of Cleveland, Ohio, on Sixteenth Street and Clifton Boulevard. It was Saturday morning. It was Market Day. Half the people in the crowd were colored. They were buying things. They were white, brown, yellow, red, and black.\nBut their smiles were polite and pleasant.\nNo one was making a fuss.", "0_14_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, according to the clock on the mantelpiece.\nSo much, thought $SURNAME2, for that.\nLooking sourly down at the bags under his eyes in the mirror, as he toweled dry, $SURNAME2 was able to name the guilty parties.\nHis marriage, his wife, his family, his job\u2014\nNo.\nIt wouldn\u2019t do. It wasn\u2019t fair. More than two dozen people were dead this morning, back in Kradarshik. They had been at his mercy last night. Last night the invitation had been clear\u2014last night in the lobby. The invitation had been ironical, but it had been explicit.\nHow could a person concentrate on his bodily functions, $SURNAME2 asked himself, whipped this way and that by ironies?\nThe radio reminded him that it was almost time for the twelve-twenty news, at twelve-twenty on the dot. Billions of uninformed, misinformed people would like to know what had happened at San Narciso and vicinity yesterday and today. The whole world would like to know if the prediction about a series of 36-hour quakes, which had been shouted yesterday at the end of the demonstration at the Gator Gulch gun...", "2_17_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five seventeen in the afternoon</span> when she came out of the bathroom. The living-room and bedroom were filled with a soft diffused light. The only sounds were the slight whir and click of a Roentgen-ray machine in the bedroom.\nConstantinesco awoke at five-fifty-three in the afternoon. He awoke with a sudden jerk. His right hand had moved under the bedclothes and found nothing but air.\nHe opened his eyes and looked around. His real estate business had not prospered in the overcrowded Martian real estate market of 1963. The apartment looked shabby.\nHe called out his wife\u2019s name.\nThere was no answer.\nHe got out of bed. He moved quickly, rubbing his rounded and dark-haired-stubbled head.\nHe stopped and looked into the bathroom. He saw a man\u2019s hat, a green bathrobe, some tubes of beauty cream, and a hairbrush. He deduced correctly that his wife was somewhere in the apartment. He called her name. There was no answer.", "0_12_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-two p.m.</span> Miami time.\n$SURNAME1 picked up the telephone again. He called the desk. \"Send up a masseur,\" he said.\nAT THE TURBINE COMPANY IN NAPLES, FLORIDA\nThe cardboard box, crudely addressed in pencil, was postmarked Atlanta, Georgia. Salvatore Vicenti\u2019s name was stamped on the box. It was the first time in ten years that anyone had seen a parcel bearing Salvatore\u2019s name.\nThe box contained a turbine, which was the key and core of a new form of cooling unit for jet aircraft engines. Someone in Salvatore\u2019s office had recognized the unit from photographs that $SURNAME1 had published in a magazine\u2014drawings of his inventions. Salvatore\u2019s employee had sent the turbine to $SURNAME1.\nA letter with the turbine was from a firm that made, among other items, file cabinets, drills, and copy-holders. Their brochure had been in the box from Atlanta. In their brochure was a picture of $SURNAME1, four inches high. Below the picture was a paragraph describing him as a noted inventor of corporate devices. The letter puzzled $SURNAME1. It said in part: \"We thank you in behalf of...", "2_2_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty in the morning</span>.\nThe company suffered forty-two casualties from incoming artillery in eight minutes.\nThere was worse to come.\nAt two thirty, another incoming round hit right in the center of the command post, killing eight men of the headquarters staff and most of the senior officers of the 435th.\nThe incoming fire had now reached the theoretical optimum\u2014which was to say, the enemy guns had learned all there was to know about the range of the command post and had now shifted to the optimum range. Only half the incoming rounds now misfire d.\nThere was worse to come.\nAt two thirty-three, a blue Volkswagen bearing license plates of the State of New York parked in the yellow zone in front of the palace gates.\nHilarious, said $FIRSTNAMEB0, laughing. He held up a ukulele between thumb and forefinger. He struck it once, made a strangling noise in his throat, and then dropped the ukulele and expired.", "1_10_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-two in the morning</span>. I went to a newsstand outside the station and looked to see if there were any messages for me. There weren\u2019t any. I tried to calm down and figure out what to do. I gave my car keys and baggage ticket to the parking man and he saw me into an elevator. I got off at the mezzanine and went down to a cafeteria and had some coffee. I didn\u2019t do any good. I had to leave the airport. It was a terrible strain. I left the terminal and found a taxicab. \u201cLook,\u201d I said, \u201cI want to go out to the Desert Inn.\u201d\n\u201cWhat one? The one on Flamingo Road?\u201d\n\u201cYes. I\u2019m supposed to meet a man there. I\u2019m going out there to wait for him.\u201d\n\u201cOh, you going out there to meet the bug?\" He grinned. \u201cThat\u2019s the way it always is. Nobody ever goes out there to have any fun.\u201d\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter with that?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll tell you what\u2019s the matter with that place...", "2_20_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-nine in the evening</span>.\nSolar lamps glowed in a double rank, marking the runway.\nOther lights showed the sheds and workshops of the little town at the foot of the field. Birds were twittering in the sage grass. The wind was ruffling the grass in waves.\nThe air smelled sweet and good.\nIt made $FIRSTNAMEB0 realize that he had spent half his life in a steel and concrete labyrinth.\nThe Martian landscape was Martian gray \u2014 gray rock, gray sand, gray dust, gray weeds. It was as though a Hollywood set designer had tried to reproduce the rocks and sands and dusts and weeds of Arizona and California and Utah.\n\"Durn good place to get lost in,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself.\nHe was right. The real Mars was an unrecognizable hell with ruined, weather-beaten miimics of Phoenix and Tucson jutting out of it.\n\"Nine o\u2019clock\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. That was the first thing he did when he landed on a strange planet\u2014 looked at his watch. The watches he gave his sons were water-proof, shock-proof, and as durable as all get out. He and his sons synchronized their watches not every day, but every year.", "1_3_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. In ten minutes the rockets that carried the news across the waste of Martian deserts to the eager Earth would be fired off. And in those ten minutes reporter $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 would, if he could, steal the great red stone wedding cake that stood in the lobby of the transearth rocket ship.\nOut in the cold, clear, lonely darkness of the Martian desert, the photographer and the reporter wore their space suits and carried the necessary tools and weapons. The air they breathed, keeping them alive and comfortable, was in their suit tanks.\nThe photographer and the reporter were exploring Tranque because it was the nearest city of Mars to the site that Earthlings knew as $FIRSTNAMEF0 Herbert gold mine.\nThis was the site to which $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had sent his factotum, Salo. This was the spot where Salo, using Martian native materials, was supposed to build a monument to the great, irresistible force that had torn Salo from his loved ones on Mercury.\nThis monument was supposed to be the tallest stone column on Earth. It was supposed to be something that generations to come would see from space. It was supposed to stand for all time. It was supposed to be the crowning glory of Martian architecture.\nRumfo...", "0_1_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span> and the rainy night was in its fourth hour. There was no let-up expected. The long front porch was dark. $SURNAME1 rain had worn it smooth, was wearing it smooth still.\nAt the foot of the steps a black limousine the longest, glossiest limousine in America seemed to wait like a long, black fort beside the constantly retreating waves of the rain.\nThe waves seemed to retreat because the big limousine kept rocking gently back and forth. Six tough-looking young men in the rear seat burned their eyes at anything that might come up the steps.\nThere was a shift of weight in the limousine as two more young men came aboard. They settled into the seat alongside the toughs, crowding them together. Another shift of weight. A very old man in a wheel chair popped his head in, demanded to know what was being done about Stonewall Jackson. He, too, was a volunteer \u2014 a War Veteran Volunteer, Jasper Bell. He had wheeled himself down to the foot of the steps just to find out what was being done to keep faith with Stonewall Jackson.\nBecause, after all, a sacred duty had been accepted by the War Veterans. And the next three months, until Stonew...", "2_3_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-four in the morning</span>.\n\"I suspected all along that I was trapped too,\" said $SURNAME0. He was smiling.\nIt was three thirty-four and three seconds in the morning.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized in the spinning dust outside the storage room. He trotted to the foot of the ladder to the storage-room skylight. In a few seconds, a young man a good deal less grand than $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 would be coming down the ladder. $FIRSTNAMEB4 knew the young man very well. He loved him dearly. The young man often carried chunks of raw meat bursting with juice.\nIt was three thirty-four and three seconds, Eastern Standard Time, Earth. The skylight opened.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 picked up $FIRSTNAMEB4, faced the light and saw the man coming down the ladder.\nIt was her husband, $SURNAME1.\nCONSTANT, because he was bored, was in a mood to despise anybody who wasn\u2019t bored, too. This mood came upon him every seven weeks and six days. It came upon him now.\nHis wife and the bucket of happy-ouch were the reason.", "0_22_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. Redwine, the Reverend Winthrop H. Redwine, D.D., LL.D., was on the stage of the auditorium, headset on, in a spiritual frenzy manipulated by an unsmiling man with a control panel out on the apron of the stage. The crowd was twenty-five thousand people, as many as the building could hold, from all over the Eastern Seaboard, many having flown in so they could be at the sermon of the year.\nThe masterpiece of the Reverend Redwine was drawing nigh.\nAt an enormous administrative and communications desk in the rear of the stage sat $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, telephone in hand. Rumor had it that $SURNAME1 was to be relied on to make the most fantastic wagers, in some fantastic style and at incredible speed.\nThe telephone in $SURNAME1\u2019s hand was hooked directly to the telephone in the little fortress library back on Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was in the fortress library, talking to her son by telephone.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 had been trying to get out of the building for half an hour, but she had not made a dent in a phalanx of guards and administrative officials who were doing their best to keep her imprisoned. As soon as she appeared, the ever-watch...", "1_11_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-one in the morning</span>. Synchronizing their watches, they went to separate pay phones, called memorized numbers in Manhattan, and were in communication with their own answering services within two minutes. They agreed to meet in twenty minutes in the Columbus Room at the top of the Empire State Building. This, in the pidgin language of gangsters, acting as though they\u2019d known each other for a long time, was a signal that all was ok.\nJasper and $SURNAME1, each per his instructions, took the subway to 50th Street and rode, one to the Museum of Natural History, the other to the Public Library. At exactly twenty minutes till noon, they met at the Museum\u2019s front entrance by a display of the fossilized bones of a Brontosaurus. As though knowing each other well, they shook hands and went together to the elevators.\nAt the top, where the elevators emptied into the Gravity Dance, there were two suitcases lying beside a wall of mailboxes. One suitcase was business-sized and black, the other, a hard, rectangular duffel bag decorated with a peace symbol. They opened the cases, they put on their disguises (and, since the duffel bag belonged to the shorter of the two men,...", "1_19_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-four in the evening</span> and I felt fabulous. At noon I gave up drinking liquor and switched to wine. At six I had two martinis, before dinner. Wonderful tingling sensations, nirvana. Great dinner, talk, wine, gang in Glen Garden, got home fine, and have been drinking wine ever since. My favorite hot fudge sundae was waiting on the icebox and I ate it at last. Checked it out in the mirror to see if I would get fat. Not quite\u2014just round, womanly, magnificent. Wonderful glass of port after the fudge. Sipped and contemplated. Sun setting over the roofs of Potrero Hill. House still quiet. So good to be alone after a fine day. My own man.\nFour times around Mission Dolores, round and round, checking everything, eyes and ears like an animal, sniffing, testing, listening. Satisfied it was a fine street and would not slip away. Every once in a while I switched on the radio. About eight I tuned in a station that played nothing but Schubert, which put me in an exquisite frenzy. Waves of sound shimmering in the air, like a waterfall, like the sea, foaming glass. Pale blue houses and veranda. Fantastic to...", "0_8_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-four in the morning</span> in Mai arguably the best month of the year for being so bad in San Narciso. The sun burned through the white clouds, a rooster crowed, a power mower droned. In the flower section she was all of nine years old, with bones that ached when it rained and a dry throat, and still she felt, nearing the house that never by any chance had truly been hers, all the hot hollowness of the almost-grown-up. When she saw that they had, as on so many other days, started to unload and unfold the chairs on their porch, she stopped pushing the shopping cart, moved back among the potted plants, hands opening and closing on the metal of the pruning shears, there beside the tangles of begonias and geraniums, impatiens, ferns, and ivies. \u201cMona,\u201d her father called, thinking it was her; and then, \u201cmona, mona,\u201d a slower call as he came to believe it wasn\u2019t. It was like a game of musical chairs. Now it was somebody else\u2019s turn to lose. Any minute the music would stop. She circled the end of...", "0_6_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-three in the morning</span>. It was spring in a time far in the future, ten years in the future, in the year of 2000. The house at 214 Stanton Avenue, $FIRSTNAMEM1-Salem, North Carolina, faced on East Sycamore Street and was surrounded by a white wooden fence. The fence was bounded by East Sycamore Street on the south, by a vacant lot on the north, by a pasture on the west, and by a storage warehouse on the east. The address was 214 Stanton Avenue. There was a basement under the house. The house itself was a one-story white wooden house in good shape. All the windows were shaded blue by the morning sun. There was a circle of green lawn around the house. Only a part of the circle was mowed. The rest was shaded green by grass.\nDouglas C. Stan down was pulling weeds in the unmowed part of the green circle of lawn. He wore baggy green-and-white velour running shorts and running shoes and a white sweat shirt that was a little dirty. His running shorts were all puf fled out, because he was big in the buttocks and thigh. His buttocks and his thigh were hard and muscular. He had thick legs. His calves were full and muscular.", "1_1_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-two in the morning</span>. Much later than you think. Much too late to name what happened next. There was no name for it. There is none now. What happened will never be put down in an official document or testified by a qualified observer. It will never reveal the motives of any of the principals. Their minds were blown that night. Their minds have been blown ever since. Still there is no name for what happened. It is best described as an avoidance situation. It involved the materialization of an evil spirit in the room of every principal, except three. For these three, evil appeared as a bee.\nThough in actuality, as we have said, it was far too late for names or titles.\nAnd this author has no title. His name is Sweet Jones.\nIt is late, his name is Sweet Jones, his mind is blown. The story he tells is of the explosion of a single neuron in Room 223 of the Hotel America. It struck at one twenty\u2014two in the morning. It concerned what would happen if one were to put two coins in a pay phone, dial 155\u20146697, and ask for Sandor Ferenczy.\nIt struck before the insect appeared in the doorway behind $FIRSTNAMEF0 Kobalt.\nWhile Alvin Swaccess...", "1_9_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span> of Tuesday, October 6, 1947, as $FIRSTNAMEB2 knew even before seeing the clock.\nMeanwhile, back at the Stonemason Company, young $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was standing at the central window on the third floor of the $SURNAME0 estate. He was staring hypnotically at the Newport veranda.\nThere were three people under the veranda roof: one man, one woman, and one blond, blue-eyed baby of undetermined sex.\nThe three figures under the verana were the three occupants of the small Thames sailing barge that rested on posts in the water close to the factories.\nThe barge was named Woman.\nIts rope to shore was made fast to a point on the factory wall that formed a perfect point of aim for a Stonemason missile.\nThere was barely room under the veranda for the barge, and for the three people who were on it. What room there was was cluttered with stores\u2014the stores having been stored there, the day before, by Dodder, the housekeeper-companion to $SURNAME0.\nA loudspeaker on the factory wall was blaring out a warning: \"Attention, Mrs. $SURNAME1. Attention, Mrs. $SURNAME1. Do not start your boat.", "1_5_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-seven in the morning</span>. A wind had risen. Trees were nodding, someone was tossing sand. I fell asleep with the scum on my lips, had another dream about vomiting.\nIt was six thirty when I woke up to the sound of my attorney knocking on the door.\n\u201cSix thirty!\u201d he yelled. \u201cI waited as long as I could, goddamnit!\u201d I got out of bed, put my feet on the floor. They had gone to sleep and now they were awake, very angry at me. The big toenail on my left foot was throbbing wildly, a zillion knots under the skin of my big toes. I tottered to the door and released the chains and my attorney fell in, sprawled on the floor by the toilet.\n\u201cLet\u2019s go,\u201d he croaked, \u201cplease.\u201d I seized the handle of the alcohol bottle and took a big thirst\u2014quencher. It scorched, but felt good; I deserved it. Screwing the top back on I noticed, instinctively. that there was no plaque virus in the bottom. Thank god. I\u2019d eaten the last of it.\nI stepped on my attorney\u2019s back and straightened...", "2_8_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight nineteen in the morning</span>.\n\"She\u2019s late,\" said $SURNAME1. \"She isn\u2019t even here yet,\"\nA church organ was playing, and the music was being pumped through amplifiers to all corners of the crowd. $SURNAME1 was not annoyed by the music, even though the organ and amplifier were plainly the cheapest and noisiest instruments in existence. The music was, in fact, delightfully in keeping with the glorious trashiness of the scene.\n\"Should I whistle?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Whistle?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Whistle for her?\" said $SURNAME1. \"You mean you don\u2019t know where she is?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I thought she was with you. The consultant who met me at my gate said you were liaising.\"\n\"Where did she say she was going to meet us?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"She broke the appointment very late,\" said $SURNAME1. He looked around to see if anyone had overheard his use of the word late. His use of late was one of the vulgarisms the Space Wanderer had warned him against.\n\"She didn\u2019t say where she was going?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.", "0_17_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were 8,000 miles up in the air, and they were falling fast.\n\u2014If you\u2019re gonna do it\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0 told $FIRSTNAMEB1,\u2014just do it.\u2014It was important to $FIRSTNAMEB0 that he not be around when $FIRSTNAMEB1 finally did it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wanted to be outside, in space, when it happened. He wanted to see the blue-green beautiful Earth for the last time. He wanted to wink good-by to the Earth before he died.\nAnd he wanted, as he had at each floor, to be outside when his floor fell away and left him staring down the polished shaft at the thing at the bottom, the real thing at the bottom.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 didn\u2019t answer.\nHis mouth hung open. Some spit gathered on his bottom lip.\nHe appeared suddenly demented.\nAnd then the most horrible expression came into his face, the most horrible expression that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had ever seen, an expression that haunted $FIRSTNAMEB0 for many nights afterward. The expression in $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s face indicated that what was on his mind was fear for his mate, fear for his son, fear for his friends, fear for his crewmates, fear for humanity. His face said...", "1_16_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span> of March twenty-first, according to the bronze plaque mounted on the wall of the ship. Stony Stevenson, who was shamelessly curious about things like that, had looked it up before starting out.\nThe time was twelve minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon, and the place wasspace, three thousand feet above Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.\nThe place was a nacrous, imponderable, ink-black broth. A soiled hachas-hole. A heavy and corrupt precipitation. A wild and formless frenzy. A mad, morphadite coalescence of all the pools and whirlpools and winds and storms and fogs and billows and sleet and hail and snow and gusts and downdrafts and thermals and tempsests and perturbations and wobbles and surprises and quirks and gurgles and swirls that ever have flowed or flown or slunk or tumbled or crept.\nThe neutronium chamber was oval and egg-shaped, the way a coffin sometimes is. It was the grave of a child, a dead extraterrestrial child in an oval, egg-shaped coffin, floating placidly through space 3,000 feet above Pittsburgh, Pa.\nThirteen people were in the chamber...", "1_2_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Let's have some of that juice\u2014that 'old Rocky Mountain lightning,'\" said a woman's voice reproachfully.\nAnd then came Crazy Plate's midnightly lullaby to his infant son:\n\"Shut up that squalling, Junior! If you don't stop, I'm going to come in there and spank you like I done your mother! And one of these days, I'm going to get some rope and tie a knot in it, and I'm going to hang you, you hear me?\n\"Goddamn you, Junior! Don't you sass me! Boy, if you make me come in there, I'm going to come in and crack your head open like a watermelon. Well, goddamn you, Junior! I'm coming in! You want your Mama to come in and say a prayer for you? Huh? All right, sweetheart\u2014I never liked you, anyways. I'm fixing to come right in there. Goddamn you, Junior\u2014I'm coming in!\"\nThere was now no pretending about who was listening to the radio. The shouts of Crazy Plate Richardson became much louder.\n\"Junior, you hear me?\" he shouted. \"Tell me you sorry!\"\nThe whole...", "0_3_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-three in the morning</span>, April 27. I was fresh out of prison after five years. I was thirty-one years old. My attorney had told me to meet him in New York and he'd leave everything ready for us to go to Brazil together.\nI had hitchhiked and hitched a ride on a banana truck in a rainstorm to get to this city in Indiana across the ethereal plain of Ohio's destiny. I came across a gang of Alaskan homesteaders in a caf\u00e9 in Worthington and worked with them shoveling coal, planning to escape in their boxcar that night, but they threw me out into the Indiana rain when they got drunk.\nI had started drinking, too. By the time I stumbled into downtown Indianapolis, the bars were closed. I found a puddle of water on Monument Circle, fell on my face, got up, stagger - walked, dropped my bag in front of a hotel, then fell down with my face in the gutter. My body had given way. I was burning with fever. For three days I'd had nothing but coffee, corn husks, watermelon, and snails. But I hitchhiked through the rainy night and arrived in Washington in the morning. Another six hours brought me to Ohio...", "1_0_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span> when I discovered that I had left my keys on the bureau in my room at the Flamingo. Lacerating epithets exploded from my throat. . . . No problem: we gave the desk our room number, then walked back down the aisle to the door of the room itself\u2014and the cop barely came awake before he saw everything was fine, and nodded us past. Nobody at the desk on this night. A half\u2014dead cop railing at nothing as we steamed past him to the elevator. And finally we were in the submarine again and heading for the parking lot. The Gumball guy operating. A sinister, wraithlike figure hunched over the wheel of the gleaming white Cadillac\u2014a machine that was going to take us the hell out of the Flamingo and across town to Caesar's in Goldvein before daylight. It never occurred to me until sometime the next day that Chapter 11 of my legal beagle life in Las Vegas was going down at the precise instant we left the courthouse in that white Cadillac. Our very next stop was Caesar's Palace. It really was a grisly feeling. I looked up once and saw the word \u201cCancer\u201d scrawled across the sky in huge letters. Or was it...", "2_13_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earthling time. Unchronological glimpses into terrestial and Titanian space would show that a fleet of recruit ships were on the way to Mars. The faster ships were two and a half hours out in space and were picking up ponderous velocity from their nuclear reactors.\nThey were heading for Mars in all relative speeds.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 no longer commanded the regiment. His fellow major, Bigman, had been elevated to commanding officer. Bigman held the rank of major in the Earthling Army only by courtesy. He was, in fact, colonel of the Martian contingent of the regular Iron Clown regiment.\nThe reason for the comedy, among others, was the need for a dash of comedy to cut away, as a safety valve, the sense of pressing weight that might otherwise overtake the men in the lone command ship.\nNone of the pilots were in their spacesuits. The ship had been designed to be manned and operated by two men when necessary. It was necessary now, with twenty pilots on board.\nOvermaster and Bigman had been officers in the war between Earth and Mars three hundred Earthling years before. It had been called the Bug War because the weapons used had been living creatures.", "1_21_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> when we swung out of the narrow channel harbor and pointed the boat seaward. Already the river seemed wide and the shore far away. The shore was far away and the warehouses were Bolshevized in the gloom. Only the Elks Ap\u00e9ritif sign was bright, and up on the hill the Cathedral was shelling the gloom with rays. At the first turn in the river the Cathedral was shut off, and then there was only the dark and exultant presence of the broader river, the moist sweet smell of the water, and the exultant pounding sense of going free.\nThere was a night mist over the water. The headlight of the boat was not needed on the broad river and we shut it off. The fog light in the bow threw a soft, white illumination on the mist. It was like a snow-fall in the night. But it was summer mist, not winter snow. We could not see the other shore of the river, could not see the town across. We were alone with the river, and the night, and the mist. There was no sound except the slow-throttled growling of the engines. Occasionally, across the river, there was a shout and a barking of dogs. Sometimes on one shore and sometimes...", "2_20_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe Nice Man was confused. It grieved him deeply.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 smiled politely.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 looked bored, and his wife,, who had been about to say something pleasant to the Nice Man, revolved on one heel, telling the Nice Man, by means of the action, to take a hike.\nThe truth was the truth, however. The truth was that the Nice Man, who owned a chain of successful cleaning establishments, was really and truly Margaret Mead\u2019s stepfather. Margaret Mead was the famous cultural anthropologist.\nThe Nice Man had met Roxder only three times\u2014\nOnce when he had bought $SURNAME0\u2019s factory on Mars.\nOnce when he had met $SURNAME0 on Mars to discuss buying $SURNAME0\u2019s estate on Earth.\nOnce when he was wheeling his cleaning-van past the open gates of the estates of Newport, Rhode Island, and the chauffeur had stopped the car to ask a little girl where the Swans lived.\nThe little girl had been $SURNAME0\u2019s wife.\nThat was all there was to it.", "2_4_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"As you say,\" said $SURNAME0 sardonically, \"we\u2019re just a couple of human souls trying to catch a train.\"\nThe time was ten minutes past four o'clock in the morning.\n\"Don\u2019t tell me you plan to just walk up to your $FIRSTNAMEM1 Nilo you gave the hotsy-totsy to, and tell him to march up that gangplank!\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. She laughed. \"$FIRSTNAMEB4!\" she said. \"You\u2019re always acting as if anyone, man or dog, could tell you anything about love.\"\n\"No, I\u2019m not,\" said $SURNAME0. \"You have described my feelings exactly,Crystal.\" He spat on his hands, rubbed them together, and slapped them against his bare, dungareed knees in a parody of virility.\n\"What I\u2019ve described is the feelings of any male animal,\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. \"You\u2019re as amoral as a sparrow, skipping from twig to twig and pecking up anything you think will give you a thrill.\"", "0_12_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-three post meridiem</span>.\nRomance, strange, exotic romance, if one wished to look at it so, confronted them now in the shape of a middle-aged rat-hole speculator from New York City, another member of the dangerous classes, who had been out of work two days and suggested, as a business opening that would bring regular income and give free time for leisure and culture to individuals who were steeped in culture and steeped in leisure and had now no earthly notion what to do with themselves. Nothing remained but examinations, a series of seven examinations that MacMurfee would have to pass.\nNo one on the committee, which included the head of a girls\u2019 college in Connecticut, a pharmacologist from Maine, an insurance merchant from Chicago and so on, in all eleven people, knew anything about radio stations. They had had their education before wireless telegraphy came in.\nAs the examination went on, it became frightfully clear that unless MacMurfee knew more about radio than all the other members of the committee put together, it would be impossible, even then, to get permission to establish a radio station on one of the bands already assigned to the army and navy by the Federal Communications Commission. Particularly galling to them was MacMurfee\u2019s...", "1_10_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nTelepathy is to normal human thought processes as red is to infrared. A person tuned to telepathic receptivity recognizes another person every time he or she meets one. But this takes long training and total commitment.\nOnly eight thousand people in the whole country have the mental equipment to be trained in the Reverend Clyde Barrel\u2019s Holy Church of Christ's Dyslexic Redeemer. Of these, forty-six are close colleagues of the Barrel himself. They are the bishops, credentialed by their mastery of the branch of applied surrealist mathematics known as Holy Quadratic Theology.\nOnly one member of the group of forty-six is on duty at any one time. To break the constant, nonstop linkage would mean either permanent psychosis for the whole world, or the end of all peace and gentility forever.\nThere was a two-second pause after the music ended, and then the home audiovisual center of Miss Frances Remark, using the voice of Edward G. Robineau, Esquire, began once more to speak.\nThe home audiovisual system of Miss Frances Remark seldom spoke from a prepared script. On this occasion, there was no provision for the message Miss Frances Remark got to hear.", "1_8_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe time would be twelve minutes past eight o'clock in the morning on Tuesday, February 4, 2005, Earth Era.\nAt ten past eight that morning, in room 233 of the Space Wanderer, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Boovies were on their knees praying silently.\nAt ten past eight, Private $SURNAME2 was exercising his right as a good soldier to break training whenever the spirit moved him. He had learned to tell the difference between right spirits and wrong ones.\nAnd at ten past eight, a hollow voice behind a locked door six floors below $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Boiz\u2019s room made a startling announcement to nobody in particular. The commanding officer of Combat Command B of the Martian Assault Army finally woke up.\nHe said to himself: \"My God\u2014what have we done?\" He had seen something in his sleep. In his dream, he had seen the planet Earth floating in the blackness of space. He had seen harmless Earthlings pouring through the air in every direction, laughing and cheering and tumbling over one another. The dream had been set to the sounds of an awful song. The song had been about nothing, but the Earthling in the song had been happy about it. The commanding officer now knew the name of the song...", "0_13_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-one in the afternoon</span>. Normal consciousness had resumed.\nStraws fell upward. She could touch the walls with her fingertips. Life in the Cincinnati Underworld was better than it had been in months.\nSo dismal were these thoughts that they brought her round again.\nThe drugs had not worn off. They were losing their steam.\nIt was necessary to take more drugs.\nShe took another dexies.\nAnd at once it was necessary to do something about the thoughts. They rose up tough and prickly from the floor tiles, aggressive green prickles, thoughts about Terry and joints, thoughts about responsibility and madness. Sooner or later somebody was going to get hurt. These thoughts, too, were tough and prickly. They would not go away. They stuck. They wouldn\u2019t leave.\nLenore took a deep breath, stood, gathered her thoughts together around her. She went over to the window and pulled the drapes. She paid attention to the details of self-persuasion. In a minute she didn\u2019t care about the thoughts anymore. The great white empty fields of untroubled snow held her up, stopped her fall. Lenore Beadsman floated above Shaker Heights, Ohio.\n/a...", "0_13_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-three in the afternoon</span>; the date, chronologically speaking, was April 1, 1986. The place was Room 305 of the mansion known as the Tristero Inn, on the island of San Paolo, a hundred miles up the equator from the smoking ruins of what had once been the nation\u2019s capital.\nThe only man in the room was a white man, possibly in his thirties. He was barefoot, and wore only skimpy green shorts, and nothing whatever over his hairy white chest. He had on a khaki bush hat. He was busy disemboweling the corpse of a huge black bitch, some kind of mastiff, with a bluish-gray, nonreflective coat.\nThe dog\u2019s belly had been sliced open, and the entrails hung out. The man worked with a quickness and lightness, neither wasting motions nor exerting undue force. He cut loose the stomach and bladder, tossed them into a corner, picked up the innards and squeezed them into a bag marked PHILADELPHIA SANITARY. Then he cleaned the bitch\u2019s blood off his knife on a rag he\u2019d spread on the bed, tossed the rag into a corner of the room. The dog...", "2_17_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog materialized in front of the monument.\nThe Statue of Liberty, the ship that was bearing Space Wanderer and $FIRSTNAMEF1 out of Earth, the ship that it would meet in space in one hour, was turning on its turntable, blooping a farewell.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog seemed to be illuminated by the statue\u2019s blooping white electric brain.\nThe light came from $SURNAME0\u2019s glove.\nThe materialization of $SURNAME0, in and of itself, was no great wonder. $SURNAME0 materialized in public, by the officially sanctioned means, with great regularity. When he did, crowds cheered in ecstasy, were ushered quietly and efficiently into mass-hysteria.\nThe news of the wondrous tidings that $SURNAME0 was about to bestow was spread, by official means and with official authenticity, at about the time of materialization.", "2_14_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nIn the sunny part of England, the twenty-one-inch guns continued to thunder at the Germans.\nAnother barrack mate came in and said, \"Boy, are we going to get a big charge tonight.\"\n\"Drill parade at six o\u2019clock,\" said the first mate.\n\"Half of old England must be in that big mouth of God\u2019s now,\" said the second mate.\n\"God\u2019s?\" said the first mate. \"God ain\u2019t got no mouth.\" He went to the window and cried, \"God!\"\nGod had no answering voice.\n\"Don\u2019t think about it,\" he said.\n\"Don\u2019t think about what?\" said the second mate.\n\"That the Germans have killed half of old England by now,\" said the first mate.\n\"Just think about the lovely battle we\u2019re going to have tonight,\" said the second mate.\n\"Don\u2019t think about what\u2019s been happening to old England, either,\" said the first mate. \"Just think about our casualties.\"\n\"What\u2019re they?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to both mates.", "2_7_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-eight in the morning</span>.\nThe grass around the tower was crisp with frost. A white, feathery light frost on the grass.\nThe visitor brushed himself off absently. He swirled his hat back on his head.\nThe breeze of his swirling sent his pale blue coat flapping.\nHe snapped his fingers again.\nAnd he flapped his coat back over his body.\nThe little group of buildings by the Observatory glittered in the flat sunlight.\nThe visitor counted to himself, nodding.\nOne step. Another step.\nAn aerie of rarified white boxes receded behind the visitor. A chasm of clear air expanded before him.\nThe ease with which he penetrated the atmosphere made a sound like tearing silk.\nThe sound seemed to pepper the stillness of the morning.\n/a/\nThe diner was owned by an ancient man with a walrus moustache. He trundled the stained spatula around on the cutting board, poked sandwiches out of the steam tables.\n\"Candidate?\" he said.\n\"What?\" said $SURNAME1.", "0_20_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past eight o'clock in the evening</span>, local time, and in unconscious homage to Old Home Week a pink arc lamp representing a distant Trystero burned in the courtyard below; Tristero, Inc., owned the building. She knew nothing of this, would not consciously remember seeing the sign until an instance some days later, but its presence, in some code unknown to the conscious mind, was at work in her all the same, as things always are for us, even when we know nothing about them. For one thing, she had avoided shaking hands. Neither had mentioned the name of either husband at all. What the eyes show, thought the shy one, the name of the eye's lord shows also. $FIRSTNAMEF2, a shade too brightly, inquired how the retreat had been. It had, was all Mucho would say, been fine.\nConcerning her own Illumination, $FIRSTNAMEF2 assumed a certain coquettish gallantry, asking if she might hear the story sometime. Not tonight, laughed Narcissa, Isolde. How? asked $FIRSTNAMEF2. Tonight, she was told, is untellable. I will bring a bottle of tequila and a Joaqu\u00edn Ifugao hunter\u2019s bag with 33 pockets, and you will see...", "2_6_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was still a little time left in which to perform meaningless ritual acts before the arrival of the robot sentries. In his cubicle, $SURNAME1 performed acts that would have been heartwarming had he been a character in a soap opera, instead of a cubicle in a modern installation in the heart of a dark city in a lovely valley.\nHe took his one picture of Stony Stevenson out of his wife\u2019s knitting bag. It was now dog-eared and torn. He put it carefully together, kissed Stony on the lips, hung it on a wall, brushed his uniform skirt. He put photographs of his only child in order on his table. He sat at attention, as though a general were coming in order to inspect him. Then he stood at his blackboard, picked up a piece of chalk, wrote MORNING CALLS ARE MADE AT on it\u2014wrote it well, with large, military loops.\nThe minister, a Baptist, and his lady arrived at the fort at 7 A.M. on the dot. $SURNAME1 was briefly reprieved from the robot sentries so that he could greet the minister and his wife in Parade Rest.", "1_0_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-eight ante meridiem</span> by my gold Cartier watch when we countkened. The dawn was whitening the sky over Catalina Island . . . the last of the nightclouds were drifting away over the Coast Highway . . . and the wild dolphins were riding their sperm-whale tails in the Sausalito waves. I could barely hold the wheel as we swooped past the black shadows of the cypresses along Shoreline Highway , but there was no other traffic at that hour; we were running about a hun erd twenty miles an hour. As we crossed the Bridge I could finally see almost all of Sausalito laid out ahead of me in the half-light; the ghost town of the postwar years; its gas pumps idly standing at attention among the empty houses...and we were across and out on the other side of the tollgate, boxing silently along through the empty boulevards that ran between Oakland and Berkeley...along with a green Cadillac speeding the opposite way, moving along sudden and localized bends in the general flow of morning traffic, running about eighty in a sixty-five zone...ahead of me a police car was chasing this same Caddy, and as I drifted past a glance axed into my eyes like a huge amber signal...but he wasn't...", "0_5_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. I looked off across the San Fernando Valley toward the Okay Mountain Range at the far end of the flat plain beneath the purple clouds of dawn. Whoopee, I thought. Here we go. It is finally happening. And perhaps for the first time in my life I was grateful for all the things that had happened to me that I'd once thought sad and ugly. These absurdities and miseries, it occurred to me, were actually the pearl necklaces of extreme youth that would forever after adorn and delight my soul.\nNo trip\u2014noal, and in a dim way I understood this; and being young, I had the gift of appreciation. These were the things that were going to make my memory the bomb. Coming down the highway, back into Los Angeles, at sunrise, on the morning I met the girl with the tattoo on her buttocks, was one of the peak experiences of my life. I wanted to savor and roll it around on my palate. It was like something in a dream\u2014the memory of some wild and precious madness that could never be exactly duplicateda. dream I could live in and be happy forever.\nThis is what America is supposed to be all about, I thought. The America I grew up in,...", "0_3_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nWe stepped out of the glass shell into a high, wide corridor. The floor and walls were of a dark, polished wood. Doors opened off the corridor. Behind the doors I could hear the sleeping sounds of an enormous hotel. A hotel is a place where people go to lose themselves, to hide. A hotel is a frame that life hangs on. I wondered which room Scotty Tremaine had. The corridor was empty and so quiet we could hear our feet.\n\u201cLet\u2019s see if we can find the bar,\u201d I said.\n\u201cGood,\u201d said Raoul. \u201cI like this place so far. Everything's so nice and quiet.\u201d\n\u201cLike a church,\u201d I said.\nAt the end of the corridor a liveried man was leaning against the wall. He nodded goodnight as we came up.\n\u201cWhich way to the bar, Mac?\u201d\n\u201cRight down the stairs, sir.\u201d\n\u201cThanks.\u201d\nWe went down a stairway that circled down and around like a spiral staircase. The steps were thick. The landing was stone. We walked across it and pushed the bar door open.\nIII", "0_16_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 had only seven minutes more to share in the small space that was their universe.\nThey passed only six minutes in shared experience.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was at his stonework, with the women Malachite, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEB2, on the hillside below the buildings. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was laying a foundation. It was to be a smoking stand for $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had planned the smoking stand as an architectural masterwork. It was to be a free-standing tower five feet high. By standing on tiptoe, $FIRSTNAMEB0 would be able to look out over its carved stone balustrade to the Martian landscape beyond. By standing on his toes and holding on to the carved stone handrails, $FIRSTNAMEB0 would be able to see the dozen dogs that pleasured themselves there without him.\n\"Looks good, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1 to $FIRSTNAMEB0, whenever $FIRSTNAMEB1 came to a correction in the plans. \"Ought to make you real cozy and happy, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\" He said this now. He said it every time.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had nothing to say, though he was happy\u2014and made a little more happy by the fact that he was possibly one of the luckiest creatures alive. He...", "1_7_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-nine in the morning</span>. My attorney and I were in a snarl of taillights on the main drag leading toward the airport, in an incredibly overheated VW microbus. The radio was on, but the volume was way down, almost too far down to pick up the characteristic sobbing of a seventeen-year-old girl with a bad complexion. Anyway, the volume was so low for two reasons: The first reason was that we were trying to hear the airport\u2014hanger traffic on an enormous, new fangled super Hombre shortwave portable radio, and the only point to that was to get the arrival and departure codes for major flights, because we were about to make our own major flight to L. A.\u2014to stand poised on the doorstep of The Great Seduction.\nBut the second reason we had the radio down low (almost all the way down) was to avoid the furry sound of that seventeen-year-old girl\u2019s sorrow....for she was coming on to our frequency with a beautiful, golden horn vibrato, saying: \u201cThis is a test...this is only a test....\u201d\nAnd this worried me to no end, because we were not in some crappy little hotel in downtown Vegas; we were smack in the middle of...", "0_1_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-eight in the morning</span>. I plugged away. Two Los Angeiles patrol cars passed me and went off somewhere. At three o'clock the concourse was still sparsely populated, with this difference: now everybody looked beat and the walks were almost deserted. I yawned and watched. The fog was cold and clammy. Up above was that great bright constant all--night neon sky. The hubs of the wheel went clickety--clack as the spokes shot by. At three-- twenty another taxi, another fare, another dollar sixty--five. Eleven hours minus ten minutes and never a break. And now the fog was really closing in. It drove in from the coast and rolled over the parking lot like tumbleweed, soft and heavy and grey...old grim Father Fog, and he rolled in like the whole Big Muddy, and the wheel spun in his hands like a roulette wheel, and the bright lights went up and down, up and down, regarding him coldly. Dry fog. Cold fog. \u201cCan you see?\u201d they were asking. \u201cCan you see? Are you hearing us? Is this what we look like?\" And old Father Fog said, \u201cHere's mud in your eye.\u201d And rolled in from the Coast.", "2_3_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-six in the morning</span>.\nTen minutes later, $SURNAME1 was still sitting by the telephone in $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s cottage. He hadn\u2019t called back to Newport. He was making up his mind whether he could use a friend. And if he could use a friend, which one? Fern was unquestionably the best friend in the world\u2014but she was also the wife of his friend.\nAnd then he was finally lifted by the sound of steps, and he got his feet loose from the floor and hurrying feet approached the cottage.\nThe front door was opened. It was $FIRSTNAMEF1 Mason, middle-aged, with iron-gray hair, wearing a short blue kimono-style robe.\n$SURNAME1 hurried to her and threw his arms around her. His impulse was to weep.\nThe front door closed again. O how good it was to be held by someone again\u2014to feel the press of breasts and hips through artful cloth after five long years!\n\"What on earth has happened?\" $FIRSTNAMEF1 asked, unwinding herself gently from $SURNAME1\u2019s arms.\n\"I came to you by accident,\" said $SURNAME1. \"But maybe it\u2019s the best accident that ever happened to me.\"", "2_22_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe caller was a young man wearing a rhinestone jacket. His name was Redwine. His jacket bore the legend: SALE AT SELVITZ\u2019S. His jacket bore the legend: ROCK AND ROLL HAS GONE COWARDLY. HIS NAME WAS JONES.\nThere was a pimple on Redwine\u2019s right temple. His hand trembled as he invited the people in the meadow to space.\nAmong the space candidates stood initials for many of the Walked Among Us. There was even an advertisement for one of the Walked Among Us. His name was $FIRSTNAMEB0. He was a flat-footed, slow-witted, mournful-faced animal with the letters U F N stenciled in yellow on each heel\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was a solid citizen, middle class, and Young.\nHe was thirty-three years old.\nHe had never done anything evil or anything good with his life.\nEverything he had ever seen or done had prepared him for something as silly, as glorious, as terrifying, and as inane as what he was doing now.", "0_23_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> of a date that was 8lst in the year 2204 by the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibulated reckoning common to all planets and all peoples.\nIt was a Saturday.\nNo fire alarms were being broadcast.\nThe apartment that had been occupied by Aedent Pickel, Neutral Olivef, and Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1 had long since been made unoccupied. An emergency crew was getting ready to enter it to clean it of a fissiparous mess of shredded and compacted furniture, linoleum, drywall, window glass, et cetera.\nA nine-year-old child, who had been crouching outside the apartment door, now ran away. The child had been a witness to all that had occurred within the apartment.\nThe child was $SURNAME0\u2019s great-grandson. His name was $FIRSTNAMEB2. He had just received the greatest scare of his young life\u2014and had received it by good rights. What he had seen, after all, was a man and a woman he loved suddenly shoved into a furnacelike round tube and burned up.\nThe nature of the material consumed in the furnace was unclear to $FIRSTNAMEB2. What he saw go in wasn\u2019t recognizable as human...", "1_22_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>. In two minutes more it would be two o'clock in the morning.\nThe first big shock of sobriety hit Salo so hard and so suddenly that he passed out again.\nThe second shock hit him when he was awakened by the sound of hollow, tuneless music coming from somewhere above. He didn\u2019t recognize the tune\u2014but it sent panic through him all the same.\nWhatever the tune was, it was so hair-raising that Salo\u2019s broken-down fin stayed broken, and Salo was forced to use his body for locomotion. His body moved so fast that it caused an electrical disturbance in the electromotive system by which the Cube\u2019s temperature was regulated. The temperature shot up in a wild zigzag.\nThe music grew louder.\nThe beats grew sharper.\nThe musical composition to which Salo\u2019s alarm system was now manifesting itself was called \"Rockabye Baby on the Tiles.\"\nIt was composed by a composer called Hilarious. No known species had ever taken Hilarious seriously\u2014much less thought that he might have had a sense of humor.\nHe did have a sense of humor.\nHe had a weird and spooky sense of humor...", "2_4_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-eight in the morning</span>. All of the fleets were in on Tralfamadore. All of the bobbing balloons Earthling-borne were prosperous occupants of whatever new planets their captains were pleased to discover for their species. The crowd was groggy and noisy from all the champagne it had drunk.\n\"I\u2019m afraid not, Skip,\" said the announcer, as though speaking from sleep. \"You drew a short number\u2014and it\u2019s yours.\"\n\"All right, all right,\" said $SURNAME0 sullenly. \"Only, for God\u2019s sake\u2014 try to look a little enthusiastic.\"\n\"Yes, sir\u2014right away, sir,\" said the announcer.\n\"Come on, come on,\" said $SURNAME0, \"why don\u2019t you get her down?\"\n\"I think she\u2019s parachuted,\" said the timekeeper.\n\"What?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"It looks like a parachute to me,\" said the timekeeper.\n\"Well\u2014what in God\u2019s name is she waiting for?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Tug it!\" said $SURNAME0. \"Tug it hard.\"\n\"Right away,\" said the timekeeper.", "2_17_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe light came through the windows of the space ship and turned the Martian landscape Amazonian green. It powdered the space ship Martian yellow.\nE Unibac, clad only in his tight skin, emerged.\nHe folded his stained, rumpled, and sweat-saturated organdy veil neatly and tucked it into his suit-coat pocket. He looked around doubtfully.\n\"Maybe there isn\u2019t anybody out there,\" said Unibac doubtfully.\nThe noise of the crowd outside made a big noise that sounded like an underestimation of the size of the crowd.\nUnibac was lost in the crowd, having trouble finding his limousine. At last he got into it.\nAn equipment instalment in the limousine enabled Unibac, by means of an electronic smile and a nod of his head, to give an unseen public quite a lot of money.\n\"What\u2019s going on?\" Unibac demanded, as his limousine took off and fought its way through the crowd.\n\"It\u2019s a reception, sir,\" said a man Unibac couldn\u2019t see, who was driving his limousine.", "1_5_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five sixteen in the morning</span>, too early for the world to be up on the space ship, but $FIRSTNAMEF1 turned around, took off her rimmed glasses, smiled more brightly than ever, seemingly unsurprised to see $SURNAME1 there. Unquestionably, $FIRSTNAMEF1 was a pretty woman. She had small, sharp features, and huge lustrous eyes. Her brown hair was short, thick, and mannishly cut.\nShe looked very hard at $SURNAME1 before she put her glasses back on.\nUnquestionably, $FIRSTNAMEF1 was a lovely woman. Unquestionably, she liked herself and the effect she had on mankind.\n$SURNAME1 sat in front of the control panel. He wiped the sweat from his hands. He required a drink, something explosive, painfully biting, in the worst possible taste. Since it wasn\u2019t to be that way, he would settle for a glass of ice water.\n\"Does Mister $SURNAME0 know what you\u2019re doing?\" said $SURNAME1.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s smile was the lawyer\u2019s nightmare\u2014quick, shallow, but apparently candid and ingenuous. \"Know? Of course $FIRSTNAMEM1 knows.\"\n\"You called him on the radio?\"\n\"Oh, no,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"I never bother $FIRSTNAMEM1 on the radio. And he...", "0_14_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-two in the afternoon</span>; the place was a small courtyard just inside the main gate at Wardenclyffe.\nThe bungalow in the courtyard was a kind of staff housing maintained by the big corporation to house people working twelve-hour shifts at the great Transmbyte facility several miles up the road.\nOnce a busy laboratory, the bungalow had recently been converted into little more than a storage bin for surplus furniture and appliances. Much of the complex was now idle, with eight-hour shifts beginning at eight and ending at four.\nOnly three people lived in the bungaloop now, aside from young Edgar Huntley, Jr. A middle-aged couple lived in the kitchen-bedroom at the back of the house, and young Huntley had the living room-bedroom in the front. Though not family, the old couple kept an eye on the young boy. They would take care of him, however, no longer than the end of the month. After that, they were going to Michigan to stay with their daughter.\nIt was quietly understood that someone else would be found to take their place.\nTwo thirty-two in the afternoon.\nThe bungalow (or storage bin) was old, dark gray, warm to the touch, and surrounded by trees...", "1_16_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-five in the afternoon</span> and humidity was eighty-five per cent. in the shade. Every now and then a VW beetle would pull up to the curb, someone who looked like a Whiffenpoof would get out and run into the post office. I followed unwillingly. Anywhere people are mailing packages is a dangerous place for an outlaw. I had to get my box.\nA tall young man was ahead of me at the window where the clerk hands out the keystamps. When he saw the notice on the door he grunted and said What a way to run a railroad. Meaning, I knew, that they shouldn\u2019t be open at all on a holiday. The clerk could only shrug and raise her eyebrows at him.\nI came up behind him as he stood fiddling with his mailfist, trying to open the envelope. It was full of papers. He shook it. Click, click - no sound. Without turning around he said Damn, that\u2019s right, they gave me the wrong envelopes. You\u2019d think they could at least...\nAnd here I tried to strike up a friendly conversation. \u201cYou got the wrong envelopes from the Post Office, brother?\u201d\nHe turned around and stared me in...", "2_18_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-two in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and the space ship and his companion were gone.\nIt was dark, now, and so silent that space itself seemed to be doing a terrific drum solo.\nTrembling, Salo pressed the button that turned a section of wall to telescope- lenses\u2014and aimed them at the head of the spiral staircase outside.\nThe stairway was empty. There was nothing at the head of the stairs but the iron skeleton of a safety railing that had been there for as long as the estate had.\nSalo joined the rest of the winos in the basement.\nSome of the winos were playing horseshoes, and some of them were playing marbles. In the center of the horseshoe court was a coke furnace.\nEverything inside the high stone walls of the estate was meager to the point of insult. Even before the Depression, when the estate was owned by someone else, $SURNAME0 had stripped it of all its comforts.", "2_21_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe juxtaposition of a French tickler and a roll of adhesive tape produced a contraption that worked surprisingly well. The first use Cleveland Thompson made of it was to hoist himself to a higher and more advantageous perch atop his chair in the boardinghouse dining room. The advantage worked for him well. From his new vantage point, he could not only see into all the other rooms through the doorway that he had been able to see into before, but he could now also see across the street, past the chain-link fence, over the rooftops of neighboring buildings, into the night, beyond which lay the Atlantic Ocean.\nFrom Cleveland\u2019s point of view, at position Delta-aka-Romeo, the city of Newport receded into a wondrous and an historic past of which he formed a hopefully significant part. Newport was very, very historic. But Cleveland had been human twice as long as Newport had existed. And Cleveland knew that history did not, repeat not, begin with the invention of the automobile. And, interestingly enough, it was becoming increasingly clear to Cleveland Thompson that neither did disease.\nThat limb, in point of fact, was simply and solely a medical extrapolation.", "1_21_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span> of the last day of March, and the place was Footsack County, Arkansas, approximately nine miles as the crow would fly\u2014or as one Missouri migrant so aptly put it in a sixty-second radio dramatization\u2014\u201cas the crow flies, hell.\u201d The man who spoke these words was producer Paul Vigorous of San Francisco's KSFO; once Dallas, Texas, and now deep in the sticks of Arkansas\u2014a sweaty, half-crazed San Franciscan millionaire, wearing his Acapulco blues in the rainfor est. The occasion was the Hootenanny Music Festival, and here is some footage from that demonic onslaught on the senses.\nSome film burned, and some held up better than others. Most of the footage was single-frame; the actual pace of the film was slower than it appeared. The time was 9:09 p.m. At 9:10 p.m. the Hootenanny would end, and maybe an unknown guitarist named $FIRSTNAMEB3, with dirty brown hair and jeans, would clamber onto the stage with a Telecaster and a Fender amp, somewhere out at the far edge of the crowd, and play three raw blues\u2014songs...a blues even rawer...", "0_2_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-nine in the morning</span>. She turned off the ignition and lights, settled down to make the three-minute wait until the appointed hour. On a night like this she was admittedly a fool for doing the Streeter thing. The only explanation she could think of was this: she liked to be reminded that she was alive. This often occurred to her on lonely nights, when she prowled the dangerous lanes of the city, pulled by inky whorls that spun on into the darkness. And then, with the creeping adrenaline kicks of a kid who\u2019s run away from home and taken the Basic Rider course, she would turn, hair streaming in wet silhouette, to see the Great American Road, a vein to the very heart of the nation, lying before her like a silver carpet of moonlit possibilities, and think Home. She did not feel alone.\nShe wore mirrored shields over her eyes, a black knit watch cap, a gabardine two-piece suit with a crepe de Chine shirt and low heeled walking shoes -all, she hoped, foolproof grey woman. The mirrored glasses were a Cadillac accessory she picked up at the local dealer\u2019s. She found that if you held your head at a certain angle, with the right sort of smile,...", "2_14_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-two in the afternoon</span>.\nIt was still morning in St. Louis.\nAt Newport Barracks, there was a half-hour of free time before lunch. $FIRSTNAMEB0 went to the barrack library again, trying to think what it was he ought to think about. The library was deserted. Every man in the barracks now was eating either in a mess hall, in a tower cafeteria, or in a foxhole. $FIRSTNAMEB0 fed in a mess hall, which he found by asking a soldier.\nA mess sergeant ladled pale beef stew into $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s messkit. The atmosphere in the dining room was one of reverence\u2014and excitement.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t imitate the reverence. He was more thrilled at the prospect of a battle than any man there. The battle would be real, there, and the casualties would not be in a Statistics class in the School for Combat Engineers. The excitement was too great for $FIRSTNAMEB0 to join in the mood of quiet, humble reverence that pervaded the dining hall.", "0_0_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe fiendish plan began to take form in $SURNAME0\u2019s mind at that moment\u2014the plan to prove by means of the testimony of $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 that he himself was the best, the truest, and the most selfless of all believers in the religion of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent.\n\"Pardon me, folks,\" said $SURNAME0, \"but I think I\u2019m going to have to show you more than the Big Martian Eye. Don\u2019t want to get too morbid, but I think I\u2019m going to have to show you the body of my friend and lieutenant, $FIRSTNAMEB0. He\u2019s\u2014\"\nHe broke off.\nHis expression became one of wonder. He was, seemingly, looking at a materialization.\nThe face of Mars in Love\u2019s telescope suddenly bloomed into the most baleful, pitiless red that can terrify the eye. It was not the Big Red of war. It was a signal for utmost grief. From the telescope, it looked as if all the color had been scorched from the iron plates of the planet. It was as if the world had ripped open from iron shell to iron core, showing iron grief...", "0_8_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>: the huge black thing loomed over us like the silhouette of the massed clouds at evening. There it hung in the sun, the hummingbird beak pointing down, the hull resting on the air. It was twenty-five thousand tons, a glorious speck in the sun; it sent its beams shooting in all directions: and now the thing itself became visible, something finer than the purest silver, a ghost that could only be held in place a moment by the sunbeam. It seemed to vibrate and whirl, preparing to off itself. But it drifted downward, and vanished with a rush; there was a shock that made the straining engines vibrate, and then noises and cries, the confused yelling of many men. The last fear and doubt fled away. The ocean rose up before us, seemingly right into the sky and without the slightest correction, until we could see the tiniest grains of it, pure white, pure green, pure blue, clear as the crystals of a snowflake.\nBoys is men is dogs, somebody's gotta bring the trash in. Deadheading, huh, as they say around here. How come there's nobody ever driving Delta 88's? Where are the hot-rubber Michigan boys,...", "0_1_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span> of October 11, 1991. The great but wicked city of San Francisco was plunged into deepest, most desolate sleep. There was no traffic. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his chrono-synclastic infundibulated dog were the only living things in sight.\nThey were far out on the western shore of the island that is the city\u2019s joy, Treasure Island. And they were in a grove of redwoods.\nThe grove was really an orchard.\nThe trees were well over a thousand years old.\nThey supported themselves with asylums for their sorrows.\nThe redwoods were not native to the earth of San Francisco. They had been brought from the valleys of California in 1900, when the Pan-Pacific Exposition was held on the shore of the island.\nWhen the exposition was torn down afterward, the redwoods were left to stand. They received so much attention from visitors and civic groups that they decided they liked the place very well.\nThe Victorians used to exhibit their wealthy indigents through the windows of their great mansions.\nSan Francisco treats its wealthy indigents to the spectacle of the thousand-year-old redwoods.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 headed for...", "0_9_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-four in the morning</span>, Earth-run-in-reverse time.\nWhoever bound these two had put the red book upside down and the black book back to front.\nThe charred fragments of the one true volume would have fit back into the gilded bindings perfectly had they been given the proper orientation.\nBut they weren\u2019t.\nNo one was pumping sawdust through the walls and floors to soak up leaks and humidity any more. The space station was bone dry now.\nThe fountain in the Rose Room was dry also. But what had once been a fountain was still there. It was a freestanding sculpture in the shape of a big, fat, fanged hobgoblin. The sculpture had no name, was part of no series.\nThe sculpture resembled a certain dwarf, the wickedest of all the dwarfs, a dwarf who lived thirty-four miles beneath Malachite\u2019s Tralfamadore shaft. The sculpture had flowing hair and a beard, wore a crown, and, unless the light was bad, carried a wand.\nThe fountain sculpture came from the Constantine apartment of $SURNAME0\u2019s and $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s mating assistant, Phoebe Watermark. It stood nine feet high.\nThe sculpture stood on the...", "0_4_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>; the shades were drawn, and the pink-shaded light vibrated, flickered, and went out as the power failed. In the dark $FIRSTNAMEF2 sat blubbering about how lost she was. Then phosphor-lines began to peep abroad, in attics and on old stages. They showed her Metzger sitting in another chair, sulking. His cigarette, she could not see, had gone out.\n\u201cWhere were you,\u201d she said, \u201cwhen the power went?\u201d\n\u201cThe jitterbug,\u201d he replied, \u201cwith a female Goya schema who, when the lights went, found a more natural ambience for two.\u201d\nShe couldn\u2019t hear him; waited for a crash of lightning to bring it all back. \u201cMetzger.\u201d\n\u201cWhat.\u201d\n\u201cDid you find out about Richard Wharfinger?\u201d\n\u201cAh.\u201d\n\u201cWell?\u201d\n\u201cThere isn\u2019t any.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean \u2018isn\u2019t any.\u2019 Who said there isn\u2019t any?", "2_3_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-eight in the morning</span> on December 12, 1958.\nNIGEL UNSTRUTHER had been visiting an onyx factory in Alabama and was driving to New York in his Cadillac with his laboratory on the back and a portable television set in the front. He was wearing a white suit. He was twenty-nine years old.\nHe had spent the evening with the onyx owner, who was putting up a stiff legal battle against a patent infringement suit that UnstRuby Enterprises, Inc., had launched.\nUnstRuby Enterprises, Inc., of which Nigel Unstruther was president, was an organization that developed, invented, and owned patents. It developed and invented. It could not own. There was only one UnstRuby, illegitimate son of a Scottish peer, who sat, with half an education and a body like a stone wall, in a railed box far from the traffic inveigled from the daily newspapers and periodicals of the world. The box was high above Broadway. High, so that a visitor could not start the day with the sight of a man saying good morning to headlines, and in a box, so that he could neither flee nor be tempted by bright coins.", "2_21_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"Can\u2019t we just call her, please, Rick?\" said Lenore.\n\"She\u2019s not going to answer the telephone, you can bet the farm on that one,\" said R. V. Lang.\n\"Do we know at all where she is?\" said Lenore. The Antichrist lay sleeping in her lap. Under the table, Poor Ludwig lay sleeping, too. \"Randy,\" said Lenore, \"are you sure?\"\n\"That place is a maze, a haven of neglect and decay, it\u2019s a veritable jungle out there,\" said R. V. Lang.\n\"Big bad jungle,\" said the Antichrist absently.\n\"Do we know for a fact, R.V.?\"\n\"What?\"\n\"Where she might be.\"\n\"Just got a very really all-points bulletin from the police.\"\n\"No kidding?\"\n\"Commonwealth Archer P.D., couple of \u2019em. Said they took Spot around and tried to find your sister. They said he just about howled himself to death, in frustration. Said he\u2019s about as frustrated as that tiger\u2014\"", "2_5_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-nine in the morning</span>, November 27. I felt the snake\u2014pit claustrophobia closing in like a steel press. Lord help this total stranger, this poor retail baggage clerk. My claims check was tearing in half as I dug into my pockets for money ...rolling dimes and nickels..., precious pennies. She stared at me coldly. There was no fear in her. I had no bombs, no gun or knife\u2014nothing but money. \u201cDo you have anything made of leather?\" I croaked. \u201cMy briefcase? This sportcoat?\" She smiled and nodded. \u201cYes. That\u2019ll be $8.35, returning through San Francisco.\u201d I fumbled for the tickets. Throped for them. \u201cIs it really? Again?\u201d I ripped the ticket in half for no reason but to put my hands to some quick use. The plane was trembling now, backing away from the gate. San Diego was falling farther behind me\u2014every second sucked the stable platform out from under my feet.", "1_11_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-five in the morning</span>. The report of my death had come at last. I was free. The bars were open. The air was soft and warm\u2014sweet-scented, too, after the rain. What a lovely day to be dead and rolling along Cloud-cover Street on your way to lick the salt off Canals Street. There were billions of people dead. Everybody was dead but me, and I was glad about that. It\u2019s a great life if you don\u2019t weaken and die.\nI was driving slowly. Passing the Denver Mint, I saw the gilt Edmund L. Troupleberry on its towers. That\u2019s death for you: a harmless old man with a shitty excuse for a moustache. Any change, George? You bet your ass, Eric! The worms can\u2019t wait.\nYes, sir, I was rolling along Cloud-cover Street, ready to collect Mayo Watson at the door of The Unicorn while the LAMITA BLUE hotel band played Tea For Two at one hundred decibels to herald me. I was sure that I would get free, and so I had a big grin on my face. The song playing in the car radio, at the moment, was Yonder Comes The...", "1_15_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. There was nothing in particular to work toward. The story lines in the soap operas had quit making sense when I went back in the drugstore for another copy of the Daily News. I was sitting in a booth on the other side of the store from the one I was sitting in before. The counterman called me \u201cRudy.\u201d I liked that. There were only about four more hours to kill. Then we\u2019d be on our way. One more night in Vegas, and at dawn we\u2019d be in Baker. I had another $150 in my billfold to get me there. Maybe I would give my bullshit explanation to the boys in the cornershop in Baker\u2014tell them I decided to come out here and cover a motorcycle race. That ought to hold them for a while, if somebody should ask. No point in telling them anything definite about Crescent City. They\u2019ll know soon enough. Especially after the story runs in the paper about poor little Vincent.\nI cracked my second beer and began to turn the pages of the Daily News again. The only things worth reading were the policebeat and the book reviews, but I couldn\u2019t concentrate on either one with my mind moving like it was. Maybe...", "0_15_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. I needed a drink, I needed a lot of life insurance, I needed a vacation, I needed a home in the country. What I had was a thirty\u2014six slug morning and three paid hours in the office of a wheelman without a wheel. I picked up the telephone and said, \u201cGive me Dr. Gonzo\u2019s office,\u201d and when the receptionist answered I said, \u201cThis is the American Medical Association. We\u2019ve had a complaint that one of your doctors is suffering from terminal schizophrenia. What do you know about it?\"\nDr. Gonzo, it turned out, was not actually a doctor of medicine. He had a Ph.D. in something - theology, my attorney said. He was also a licensed preacher from somewhere around these parts...a Church of the Invisibles, or some such thing....My attorney, slathering around behind me with a rag, said the corpulent little bastard was also mixed up in some kind of reincarnation crap\u2014that he hadn\u2019t come in for the Mint Sunset to talk about medicine, only about this stuff about reincarnation that he was heavy into.\n\u201cThe hell with that,\u201d I said. \u201cGet him back on...", "0_23_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-four in the evening</span> of February twelfth, the shortest day and the longest night of the year. Groundhogs DAY, as the calendars on sale in liquor stores and taxidermy shops called it. Since Fieldbinder\u2019s den faced due west, he was able to see, though dimly through the dense cluster and palisades, just visible through the gauze, a peculiar island grouping of trees, resplendent in the brilliant lights like clumps of molten lava. A building was there, within, that the people of Fieldbinder\u2019s Town called \u201cThe Facility.\u201d Fieldbinder had been to The Facility once, many years ago now. He had acquired a bottle of schnapps at a neighbor\u2019s party and was driving along, feeling bored and warm, when he had accidentally turned down a street that led to a fence around The Facility. Fieldbinder had sat parked in his car outside the fence for a while, warming himself with schnapps and watching The Facility, which was more like a lawn, really, that showed roughened patches of earth, where innocent children had been dug up. Fieldbinder had thought about trying to dig himself up, to see what was in the casket. The...", "1_8_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-nine in the morning</span>, late October; the house was deserted and I went outside to play. Trucks rumbled past to market or to the mill. The chickens were being fed; it was eight fifty-two. The sun came clear over the eastern ridge and the hens became dots of hysterical movement. It was quarter to nine (these lonely notes recorded with frowning care). It was nine. From up on the hill came the absolute silence of the house and the ordinary silence of my mother gone and the ordinary silence of my brother upstairs asleep. A little more, and I was inexcusably late; I put the family eggs back blind in the nests and went like a ghost indoors by the back door and up the back stairs. If I were late everyone would know; I'd be caned and never forgiven. The door was locked and I knocked, called \u201cI! I!\u201d with my spine cold as the back of a spoon. Laugh at me; I had no keys and so could not let myself in. I waited, ears open for the coming steps, mine, its. My toes curled. \u201cNot there,\u201d I said. I knocked louder, I sneaked a look in at the window. The table was set for four, my...", "1_10_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-two in the morning</span>. It was hot. I was carrying one suitcase and had the other in custody. I was wearing the heavy ring with the stoneer in the plane fracas with Stan. The plane was overhead again. It circled the parking lot once, tipped its wing and disappeared.\nIt came back twenty seconds later, straightened out, hung in the air with its propeller chewed the air and reversed itself. Then it disappeared.\n\u201cOh well,\u201d I said. \u201cIt's the fate of the Quentins, and the sooner we get going the better.\u201d I hailed a cab and saw my lawyer into the Parisienne. \u201cCal Forman's at the Saint Francis,\u201d he said. \u201cThat's where I'm staying. Can you find it?\u201d\n\u201cOh yes.\u201d I smiled. \u201cI know the Saint Francis.\u201d The cab pulled away from the curb. My attorney was looking out at the Standard Oil Building.\n\u201cI've only been here once before,\u201d he said. \u201cThat was after the war, when all you could get a rate at the barred-out hotels was thirtyfive dollars a day.\"\n\u201cWhat a town!", "0_5_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span> when we came to Fort Mason in San Francisco. \u201cWhere is the office of the Western Union?\u201d I asked a cop on the corner. \u201cRight on that corner,\u201d he said. I followed the instructions and found a human being in charge at last. His instructions were precise and clear. \u201cJust tell her Ed became ill in Bishop and had to be taken to the hospital.\u201d He wanted to know my wife's name and address. I still hadn't thought of a name or an address. \u201cGaines?\u201d I said frantically. \u201cGaines? Do you know a woman named Gaines in San Francisco?\u201d I had completely forgotten the name of my wife. She was known as Edna, that much I remembered -- but the name, it was gone. \u201cI'm afraid I don't,\u201d the man said, looking dubious. \u201cThen try Dunphy -- try any name, man, I'll pay you! Telegraph everyJane and Betty and RaRa but be sure to get the address right.\u201d I shoved a bill at him and it seemed to calm him down. He made out the telegram and said, \u201cThat will cost you five...", "1_20_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-seven in the evening</span>. The Blade Runner squad car was parked outside Captain Shawn\u2019s in. The windows were illegally tinted on the inside, but the two men inside couldn\u2019t care less. At this moment they were both staring at something that was bigger and more astounding than anything outside the windows. The squad car\u2019s two sun guns were aimed this way, but they might as well have been aimed at Mt. McKinley, or at the Andromeda Nebula, for all anybody outside would ever be able to see of them.\nTheir power indicators were pegged at maximum, but nobody was watching the needles.\nTheir radio was turned on, but nobody was listening to it.\nTheir air conditioner was off, because their windows were off. And their windows were off because their doors were wide open. That could mean only one thing.\nTheir doors were wide open, their windows were off\u2014and their parking brake was off. They were flying.\nAbove the announcer\u2019s voice\u2014\"Good evening, this is Linda Fox reporting from Beverly Hills and Hollywood for ABC News Action News. Here\u2019s Charlie Weaver with the score\"\u2014above that, Reckage and Hazleton could both hear something else.\nIt was the sound that cars make in aerial maneuvers.", "0_11_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until noon</span>.\nThe crowd of people on the stairway of the bomb-shelter dugout was jammed as tightly together as people could be jammed. There was no way for anybody to squeeze any tighter.\nThe two unsmiling orderly-women attendants in their starched white uniforms stood on the stairway\u2019s top step, at the very edge of the shelter, with the rounded metal top of the door against their flat bellies. Behind them were their forty-eight quietly-crying charges and the six mothers. The mothers\u2019 faces were noble and outraged. They were outraged because it was now noon exactly and there still was no sign of the one o\u2019clock baby.\nThe methodical unsmiling attendants watched raptly over the noble outraged mothers for the appearance of the first head of a one o\u2019clock baby through the metal disk of the door. They held the gigantic scissors of staples in one hand, ready for stapling the infant\u2019s blanket to the spine of the book on the day-nursery counter.\nThe long table under the vibrating clock had been shoved back against the rear wall of the dugout. The clock\u2019s pendulum swung back and forth among the instruments for weighing,...", "1_11_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to noon</span>. At noon, the fono, the hall of congress, would open and the delegates would hurry in. One minute to noon.\nNow the unloaders, the stevedores and runners, were getting in formation and taking their orders from Browne and from $FIRSTNAMEB0, and from $FIRSTNAMEB1, who was assistant team leader of the 281st Field Artillery, Coast Brigade.\nThe clocks in the pitching machine stopped when $FIRSTNAMEB1 left the machine for a last look at the field.\nThe clocks in the pitching machine weren\u2019t wound, and anyway weren\u2019t working now. They would not be wound until $FIRSTNAMEB1 got back from the field\u2014got back in the long-deferred taxis.\nEverybody was aware, without anybody\u2019s having to point it out, that the war was effectively over, and that $FIRSTNAMEB1 would no longer have to be sparing with ammunition. From now on he could empty the bags in an accurate and thorough way, with no hostile fire to worry about.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 scrambled onto his catapult as his squad took a position alongside Browne\u2019s squad of the 227th Infantry.\nThe squads slid the tubes of their catapults onto iron tracks in the stone floor, so that the tubes would be firmly...", "1_8_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span> of a fine, sunny day in late spring.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s company was drawn up in a semicircle on the parade ground before the hangar in which the experimental ship was being disassembled and cleaned.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and a tough, dark man of about thirty-five were, by the unspoken order of their anarchistic superiors, the only soldiers armed. $FIRSTNAMEB0 held a rifle, and the man beside him held a machine gun.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t know what would happen at the morning exercises, but he was sure that anything that did happen would be bad for him. He was shaken by the appearance of the dark, thirty-five-year-old man at his side.\nThe man had arrived at the iron island the night before, in the boat with $FIRSTNAMEB0. He had thrown his duffel bag into the sentry box as the two soldiers approached, then he had given them a friendly greeting. He had introduced himself as Lt. Col. Fremling, and he had been so pleasant and so plausible that $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his friend had agreed to smuggle him aboard the ship at the first opportunity.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s friend had disappeared in the excitement and shooting after the officers\u2019 rocket ship landed...", "1_22_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-two in the evening</span> in the bar of the hotel. I was sitting at a table with Harris and his girl. There was a Hungarian waiter standing behind my chair. We had just ordered another bottle of wine.\n\u201cExcuse me, sir.\u201d\nI turned around. It was one of the hotel chaps, in evening dress, a manager or something. He was muddled and excited.\n\u201cYes, sir?\u201d\n\u201cWould you come with me, please.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Why?\u201d\n\u201cThere are officials here who would like to ask you some questions, sir.\u201d\n\u201cWhy? About what?\u201d\n\u201cI couldn\u2019t say, sir. If you will just come with me.\u201d\nI got up.\n\u201cDamn funny,\u201d I said. \u201cYou wait here.\u201d\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d said Harris.\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. There are some questions I\u2019m supposed to answer.\u201d\nThe manager moved with me.\n\u201cCome on, what questions? I don\u2019t want to answer any questions.\u201d", "2_18_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. Squeak. The sound that had been just heard was the third highest-numbered church bell.\nThere were, in all, eleven church bells in all Rovenna, ranging in number from one to eleven.\n$SURNAME0 let his dogs win six successive games of forfeits in order to bring the big moment to its pitch of intensity.\nAnd now he let his dog win no more. Now he and his dog both played to win.\n\"Winnington Sargood,\" said $SURNAME0, \"you and your dog are about to be carried away from this planet, and on to Mars, in a space ship you will build with your own two hands.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s Earthling chronometer read 23 minutes to seven o\u2019clock in the evening. There was 23 minutes left to go before the so-called Invasion of Mars by the seemingly constituted military of the seemingly contumacious nation of Mars would begin.", "1_4_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe chauffeurs, frightened, were silent now.\nThe shaken limousine was driven up to the red, white and gold Palace Hotel on Fernverand in Newport.\nThe chauffeurs carried Shallo and his baggage into the lobby.\nThe night clerk awakened, and came to his desk.\n\"Buenas noches, senor,\" he said to Mr. Shallo.\n\"Buenas noches. I\u2019d like my bill, please.\"\n\"Si, senor,\" said the clerk. He ran his finger down a list of figures.\n\"That\u2019s very exspensiv, se\u00f1or,\" he said. He shrugged.\n\"My watch melted down to nothing. I lost all my clothing, except what I have on.\"\n\"Si, senor,\" said the clerk. \"Now this bill is exspensive because you have made use of four hundred and seventy-two dollars in long-distance telephoning in the last six days.\"\n\"I only called Washington four times,\" said Mr. Shallo. He took some bills from his pocket. \"Will this pay it?\"\nThe clerk examined the money. He folded the bills and put them...", "2_17_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span> of October twenty-fourth.\nOn the west side of the building was a parking lot, with space for one thousand cars. On the east side, a flight of concrete steps, beginning at street level and ending at the doors of the fourteenth floor, permitted dignified access to that floor. The steps were often hosed down by dutiful janitors.\nGalaxy\u2019s offices were restful and gracious, and the air was always fresh and cool. The offices, naturally, were air-conditioned and ventilated.\nThe offices of Galaxy were administered, as were all buildings on Mars and all structures on Earth under the jurisdiction of the Martian-Earthling Conglomerate, by the Psychic Treatments and Research Center, Ltd. The director of the Center, and administrator pro tem of the offices of Galaxy, was Dr. Eustace R. Eats, Ph.D., D.D., M.S., D.Phil.\nThe thousands of contributors to Galaxy sent their thousands of manuscripts to the Research Center for processing and acceptance. They were delivered by messenger to an eight-by-eight-by-eight-foot mailbox at the foot of the concrete steps. The box was emptied every Monday morning.", "2_3_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three nineteen in the morning</span>.\nRear Adm. C. M. Carr was twenty-six years old when he died. He died four days after winning the Congressional Medal of Honor in World War II. He was killed in the last moments of the war, when a Red Army soldier tripped over his hiding place in a foxhole and bayoneted him in the liver.\nRear Adm. Carr had always wanted to be buried at sea, and his wishes were respected by the Navy. However, after his death and burial at sea, it developed that his widow, Alice Carr, was pregnant. The Navy could not risk dishonor by having a pregnant woman buried at sea.\nThus it was that Rear Admiral Carr\u2019s body was exhumed from the waters off Beachy Head, England, and was shipped across the Atlantic to Fort Wayne, Indiana. Honora Paston, his mother, opened the parcel from Great Britain at the railroad station, brought the body home under her arm in a zinc-lined casket, and parboiled the corpse in a bathtub full of Lysol.\nThe admiral could then be interred in good soil. He was buried in Memorial Park.", "0_15_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe great red sun covered a third of the sky. The red sun was out at midday.\nA breeze stirred the leaves of the copal tree. The breeze snapped a dry twig that hung on a twig.\nA snorting and a pawing sounded in the brush. A wet and bedraggled bull appeared, shaking his head.\nThe bull stopped. He stared at a point in space ten feet in front of him.\nThe bull was a color between white and red. His coat was matted, and caked with black earth.\nThe bull twitched his tail nervously.\nThe red sun was hellish in the eyes of the bull. He looked away from the hellish sun, and looked at the shade of the tree. There, feeling safer in the shade, he steppe forward for the drink.\nThe bull saw the two figures in the shade.\nA scream went up from the copper-colored Martian beneath the tree. The scream went unheard, as the wind was blowing toward the bull, carrying the scream away from him. The scream, however, told the bull all he needed to know.\nThe bull lay down, quivering. The sight of man put the fear of God in all bulls...", "0_18_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-seven in the evening</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time, and the space age began with a lunatic leap into suffering, struggle, fear, physical impurity, strong drink, bad food, and hideous hazard. Is there any wonder that $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, true friends on Mars, should now feel as strangers?\n\"Well\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"good\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Well\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"good\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Good luck, partner,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThe salutation sounded hideous to both men.\nA loudspeaker squawked just outside the mike area of the saloon. It was the trench-fogey announcer, telling the pilot-troop once again what its mission was. \"Another thing,\" said the announcer, \"the booze we promised you won\u2019t do you any good. You got to take your rifles into thefreezing chamber just like the rest of the army\u2014or freeze.\"\nThe pilot-troops stared stupidly at one another.\n\"Go back to your guns,\" said the announcer. \"There\u2019s twenty-seven minutes left, and you\u2019re wasting all that time standing around and gabbing with yourselves. \"One of you drunken bozos might...", "2_6_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past six o'clock in the morning</span>, at the corner of Main and Vegas, a thousand miles from anyplace. I was alone, leaning on the fender of a filthy old Ford parked near the corner. Beside me stood the driver, a fat cop in a baggy green uniform. Yellow stripes covered his shoulders. He was tall and had a gun and a nightstick and wore a Sam Browne belt. He had brass badges that said sum qual to each other on his hat and shirt.\n\u201cAre you sure about the blood?\u201d I said.\nHe nodded. \u201cThey beat this guy half to death. when they brought him in. You can hear him screaming in there.\u201d He spit into the street. The spit sizzled. \u201cChrist, what a morning! There ain't enough vaseline in the world to get me through another one like this one.\u201d\nI nodded. I knew what he meant. He had pomade hair, but he also had jowls thirtyfive down to his second chin. His dark baggy eyes were lashed by crinkly lines and creases.", "0_8_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-eight in the morning</span>. I felt fine. I drove to the main shopping area of the town and parked in front of the store. It was still closed up. I went to the door and pushed a button and waited. Nothing happened. On the third try, an irritated-looking woman with a thin face and sandy hair came to the door. She looked at me without warmth. \u201cYes?\u201d she said. I tried to smile at her. \u201cVentana Chemicals,\u201d I said. \u201cJust need to check the store shelves to see if you have our product on them.\u201d She was unimpressed with my company or its importance. \u201cThe manager isn\u2019t here yet,\u201d she said. \u201cHe\u2019s at breakfast. Let me check.\u201d She started to close the door. I put my hand out, on the screen. \u201cI\u2019ll wait,\u201d I said. \u201cI\u2019m here right now, I might just as well check, too.\u201d She considered that for a bad second, then nodded without much enthusiasm. \u201cAll right. Come in, then.\u201d I followed her into the store. \u201cJust let me get...", "2_7_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven sixteen in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 waved.\nThe ship went straight up, paused briefly, went on up out of sight.\n\"Did you see that bird do something like that once before?\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 asked $FIRSTNAMEB1 in the dialect of the Martian Grenzbone tubes.\n\"Yeah\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"I seen him once before. I sneaked in the trench one time, peeked over.\"\n\"I wish we could of sneaked through, too,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Can you imagine all them beautiful new things?\"\n\"Sure\u2014they wouldn\u2019t of keeled us,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"They\u2019d want us to tell \u2019em what we seen.\"\n\"We didn\u2019t see nothin\u2019,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, in a dang-blasted-if-I-tell-them-anything voice. The two of them started back toward the caves.\nThey were tense and unhappy. The light and glitter of the great ship going up had reminded them how empty their pockets were\u2014reminded them how desperately they needed some new thing that might have been in the ship.", "0_8_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span> of June thirtieth, according to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s luminous watch.\n\"What in hell are you doing here, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said the captain.\nA crowd of recruits had now gathered behind the captain, were shoving to see. Behind them were the men of the day squad\u2014had now taken the field by wheeled convoy from the barracks.\nA sergeant shoved the recruits back to make room. \"Let the old man alone!\" he barked.\n\"Old man!\" said the captain, and he held his belly and laughed. \"Old man! I can lick you with one hand, $FIRSTNAMEB0!\" He was forty-one years old\u2014bulked like a heavyweight wrestler, was darkly handsome. He had thick black hair, a black mustache and all of his natural teeth.\nHis name was $SURNAME2. People who didn\u2019t have much enthusiasm for him called him Killer $SURNAME2. He was a keen and brilliant commander\u2014not identified with any regiment, but floating, unattached, from regiment to regiment. His enthusiasm for teaching was beautifully tempered by his sense of fun. He would teach the old push-ups and the old close-order drills and the old marches, and he would egg the men into...", "1_1_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1 opened his gold watch. \"That,\" he said, \"is what all the excitement has been about.\"\n\"Keep the watch,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Thank you,\" said $SURNAME1. \"I will.\"\nHe slipped the watch into his pocket.\n\"That watch,\" said $SURNAME1, \"will last longer than I will.\"\n\"It\u2019ll last longer than the human race,\" said $SURNAME0. He turned his head, looked over his shoulder.\n\"See you again,\" he said in a quiet, unfriendly voice.\n\"I doubt it very much,\" said $SURNAME1. \"You see\u2014I\u2019m going to kill myself five minutes after the space ship takes off.\"\n\"Yup\u2014seems to be the only way out for you,\" said $SURNAME0. He stepped into the space ship. He closed the hatch. Through the thick glass of the flight-deck portholes, he signaled to $SURNAME1 to clear the area.\nDuring the following twenty-four hours, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 experienced an infinity of emotion, of which suffices report of the five dominant moods.\nThe first dominant mood was boredom. $SURNAME1 stood guard over the empty space ship for eight hours, trying not to...", "1_6_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1999, and the skies were whistling with fireworks.\nThe shrilling, excited hissing of the fireworks split the dawn, sputtered the windows and pelted shutters, swelled and routed and rolled in surges of sweet thunder.\nRebirth and renewal could not, in a few marvelous minutes, make up for every ill, injustice, and death that had ever happened before, but the fireworks hinted at such magic, and gave something to hold on to, to dream on, as the sun came up over Antelope Canyon.\nFrom $SURNAME1\u2019s perspex palace, the view in the crystal dawn was one of black, jagged spires with touches of flame. Rockets leaped up from canyons, blasted into dust and smoldered with drips of color. Artillery shells boomed and whooshed up slopes.\nThe palace in which $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 now lived was dark and cool. $SURNAME1 was sitting naked, his back against the wall, his legs outstretched on the dais in front of him. Sitting at the controls of a wide-band radio receiver was $SURNAME0, similarly unclad. He was using the radio to tune in the Year 2000 New Year\u2019s celebrations, the greatest gatherings...", "1_1_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty in the morning</span>. Vegas glittered around us like tracer bullets on a wall. We moved out of the parking lot into the stream of traffic, Di Giovanni taking over the wheel while Lang laughed with hysterical abandon. I sat back and took it easy, turning my brains and my neck loose to the flow without effort, as far as they'd go. There was a large Angel sitting on the dashboard, grinning like a death's head. \u201cAh, look at the Angel, would you?\u201d cried $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cHow about that Angel on the dash? I made him, I made him with my mind. I commanded him into being, exactly where he sits, with my mind.\u201d Nobody paid any attention to what he was saying. \u201cYes, I did it -- why not? I've abolished you and even myself, I've abolished everything; for you'd no sooner try to put me back in bondage again than cut off your own ass with a carving knife. I am the miraculous ghost and centurion; I have removed my cranium from the world, I have floated out over the ocean of mind in the long, cooling thoughts of solitude, the hot stench that rises from the plains of my own scalded country miles below is unnoticed by...", "1_23_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-five in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and little $FIRSTNAMEB1 had left the apartment together. $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t take anything with him except his rifle. $FIRSTNAMEB1 took his harmonium, his harmonium sheet and his little purse of stones.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heart gave an odd, painful leap. The message on the wall of the hallway had been composed of the initial letters of five words. The first letters, put together, made up a phrase predicting an air raid.\nThe first letters, put together, made up the heading under which that hypnotic command to sleep had been printed.\nThe first letters, put together, made up a single word that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had never seen before, the total meaning of which was somehow terrifying.\nThe word was:\nVITALIZE.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 knew instantly that Vitalize had been the message Washington had given to Mercury. Mercury had refused to Recite the message, had been crying when he delivered it.\nThere was a second message in the rifle-bullet holes in the wall.\nThe holes in the wall spelled out the sentence: \"YOU ARE VERY STUPID NOT TO LEAVE.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t pause for interpretation or for reflection. He ran for the nearest staircase. Behind...", "1_4_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span> when the screaming creature that had been $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 staggered out of Room 223 of the Wilburhampton Hotel. He lurched out into a bright, cold Bermuda morning.\nThe tide was high. A sullen surf broke on a coral reef a hundred yards offshore.\nIt took $SURNAME1 several minutes to get his balance on the unsteady ground of the landing. While he was staggering around out there, he had a close look at the pink beach.\nIt wasn\u2019t really pink.\nIt was blue and purple and white.\nAnd that wasn\u2019t a coral reef; it was thousands of men, women, and children all curled up in the fetal position. The folks m muttered in their sleep.\nThey had all come here, to this one place, to spend the night outside and exposed\u2014in skimpy bathing suits, in pajamas, in underwear, and even in nightgowns and shirts and bare feet. Some had blankets. Some had newspapers. The people were sleeping on the breaking sea.\nThey had been sent out by $SURNAME0 and by their own regiments to acquire wetness and tolerance for wetness. This way, in a good rain, the troops would be able to fight.", "1_10_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThey descended the stairs. The old woman stood in the lobby, staring at them.\nA clerk let them out, then locked the door behind them.\nThe Red Rover assured them a five-minute start, by going to the end of Monrow Street and waiting there. They agreed on a rendezvous in the lobby of the Hotel Aventura in the port of Zenith, at ten o\u2019clock the next night.\nA taxicab took them to Topp\u2019s, where they ate shavings and drank beer. The place was empty except for a sailor, who was just leaving as they came in. He and $FIRSTNAMEF1 sat down at the only table, and had a talk.\nANOTHER RUMORS, AND THE TRIP TO SAN LEONARDO\nIT BEING now about eleven in the morning, and since the next move clearly was to send the $SURNAME0 Machine on to San Lorenzo, Letitia and $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 intended simply to return to Newport and spend an easy, carefree time until time to catch the machine again. And life would be fine in Newport, because they had plenty of money, and nothing much to do.\nAll they would have to do to live would...", "1_7_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span> in the Green Room at the Press Club of Bali. I had been on Isla Margarita for the two previous days, covering the story of the plane crash for The National Tattler, and a party had broken out. Someone brought in a case of Johnnie Walker Black Label, extra heavy malt. \u201cFor medical purposes only,\u201d I warned the bartender manfully. The pilot and copilot of the plane had both died; so had all the passengers. Except me. And except this photographer named Kilgore Trout, who turned out to be a close friend of mine. My attorney had tried to get me to sign a paper waiving my right to sue. \u201cThere is absolutely nothing to sue about,\u201d he said. \u201cThe plane was old and rotten.\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d said my friend the bartender, smiling and wiping the counter. \u201cAnd what about you, senor? Are you planning to sue?\"\n\u201cNot me,\u201d I said. \u201cI\u2019m just a simple newspaper reporter. I\u2019ll write it all up for my hometown newspaper.\u201d\n\u201cSi,\u201d he said. \u201cAnd what hometown might that be...", "1_20_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-two in the evening</span>, the first of May. The sky was pink over the Oakland hills. It was going to be a clear glorious night for the gig at the Fillmore, with the All-Night Freak Out just coming up, with Mountain Bus lines to Frisco forming at all times.\nAnd I pulled out of the driveway and rolled north up Gravity Road, through Peralta Park and beyond, up the Duncan Canyon Road, with the windows wide open to the lovely evening and my ears tuned to KFO intervening between the hi-hat din of rock hits on KSAN and the wild screaming R & B on KMPX, and headed into the mystery.\nWell, that's it, I thought. That's what's waiting out there. All our Yesness and Somedayness and Hold-Togetherness. Everything is in that great trembling expectation of standing around bellybeards in an alley, next to the loading platform at S.F. General, waiting for a bus to go somewhere and do something. To the Moloch of the Century, and all our lazy, intellectual talk about what might have been, about the paths not chosen, about the life and times of Ealing Gideon, and H. Fortune ... he's been dead fifty years, and what...", "0_20_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>. Approaching the critical moment when the boppers were scheduled to launch their all-out attack, nothing seemed certain any more. The mellow sounds of the orchestra in the cellar of the building floated up, unheard.\nThe game had come to an end, and, as usual, it had ended in a draw.\n\"We\u2019d be fools to watch,\" said Salo. \"Let\u2019s fly back to the ship, $FIRSTNAMEF1. I already have your rescue ship on course from Mars. It should arrive in about two weeks. To fly to it now would be just asking for trouble.\"\n\"You make it all sound so logical,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 ironically. \"My People\u2019s Revolutionary Survivor\u2019s Club ain\u2019t never going to like me when I tell them what really happened up here. I ought to just keep my big mouth shut.\"\n\"No, please tell all your friends the truth. We boppers are going out of business, and everyone should know why.\"\n\"I wish we were going out of business peacefully,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nThe two horoscopes suddenly came down the stairway. They were carrying their things between them. The playwright was limping. \"It\u2019s time...", "1_19_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span> of Wednesday the twelfth of August.\nNed Mantel shuffled a deck of cards.\nKitty Dubski, who was ten years old, punched holes in aluminum foil with a Brownie camera.\nThe holes were supposed to be the shapes of daisies.\nSalvador Darl hadn\u2019t been doing anything for quite a while, now.\nThis was his way of doing absolutely nothing. In a minute he would do something. He thought he would put his feet up on a chair.\nThe chair was directly in front of him. This gave him something to lean against, as he relaxed his feet.\nThere was nothing exciting about doing this. It was merely another way of killing time.\nNot that time was being killed. What was killed was the pain in Salvador Darl\u2019s mind, from a thinking that was both too much and too hard.\nTime, in the in-between, was stilled. If you iced a cookie very slowly, you could keep the cookie soft much longer than if you iced it quickly. Also, the slower the icing, the thicker the icing, the more delicious the cookie would be.\nIn the same way, time could be flavored. (Or almost...", "0_9_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-four in the morning</span>. The place was the Nefastis mansion on Carmel, California. The occasion was an attempt on the life of a person who was not only desperately sick, but old and sick and rich, and presumably a party to a pre-arranged suicide pact in case of disaster. Rumor had it that Nefastis owned all of Montana and a good chunk of the Moon. He weighed two hundred and forty pounds, had bad lungs and ulcers, and was supported by self\u2014injected heroin.\nThere were five or six persons on the lawn, witnessing everything. Rumor also had it that Nefastis was probably a latent homosexual who had forced the unfortunate Malacionni to live with him for saving his life. The bond between them was supposed to be so strong that nobody would dream of trying to pry the twitchings of the dying man loose from the iron grip of his butler, without Malacionni\u2019s consent.\nMalacionni was accused of having murdered a United States Senator who had allegedly tried to come on to him in a hotel men\u2019s room in Georgetown. Nobody in official circles would confirm the story.\nRumor also had it that there were two more butlers behind the hedges on either...", "2_6_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-three in the morning</span>, Washington time.\nIn Los Angeles it was eleven thirty-three in the evening.\nStony sat down on a chair at the white trodden-clay hearth of the fireplace in the living room. He encircled himself with his arms. There was no fire in the fireplace, though fresh ashes showed where a fire had burned only a short time before.\nHe shivered, partly because he was cold and partly because he was thinking\u2014and remembering.\nHe shrugged. It was over. It was all over. He shifted the shiver to the top of his head and down his spine, to make it lighter.\nIt was over, he thought. It was over, now.\nGood God, how that ship had pitched, just where I was a moment ago, Stony thought. So it must have been a pretty good sea, after all. A swell, he had thought it was. And yet nothing like that had happened.\nHe stretched and stood up.\nHe shivered again. Cold, he thought. He thought of snuggling back into bed with the children and holding the little warm bodies on either side of him.\nWell, he wouldn\u2019t do that.", "1_17_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe gentry of $FIRSTNAMEB2 had put off the wedding till after the eclipse of the sun at five-thirty.\nThis farsightedness on the part of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s richest was to have serious consequences for the aristocracy of that small planet, as will be seen later in this account.\nThe state of matrimony was not in itself a barrier to service in the Army of Earth. The Triformianllular Voltigeurs wore their triplets the same way Earthling grooms wore wedding rings. Unmarried Triformians were proud of their bachelorhoods, however long their pedigrees might be.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Capitol had been stringently excluded from the wedding service. Not even a messenger had been sent to ask $SURNAME1 to step outside.\nThere was but one place for $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to go.\nBest of all would be to remain outside until dark\u2014and enter the palace only when every trace of the wedding was over.\nThe palace was dark and gloomy and empty, at least to begin with.\n$SURNAME1 turned on all the lights.\nHe rode the elevator to the roof and stared at the palace and himself. He looked at his image in the mirrored walls of the elevator.\nWhat...", "1_12_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-one post meridiem</span>.\nThe ship was being held against the field of the catapult by the main-engine blast.\n\"All right, Space Wanderer,\" said Colonel $SURNAME0 up in the limousine. \"Blast away.\"\nThe ship was held against the catapult field by the continuous modest pressure of the ship\u2019s own main engine.\nOld Salo, in the gazebo, wasn\u2019t having such a bad time, after all. He was in a mild state of euphoria and was picking out tunes on an imaginary harmonium.\n\"Ought to be just about ready, Space Wanderer,\" said the voice of Colonel $SURNAME0 inside $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s helmet.\n\"You\u2019re supposed to say, \u2019God bless you, son\u2014you\u2019ve got the best-looking modder in the whole wide Universe,\u2019 \" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. She was curled up on the seat with fresh chromium diopters in her eyes, peering critically through $SURNAME1\u2019s binoculars.\n\"God bless you, son\u2014you\u2019ve got the best-looking modder in the whole wide Universe,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"O.K., then,\" said Rum...", "0_12_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve seventeen post meridiem</span> on the sixteenth of August, when a handsome, well-dressed guerrilla in his middle thirties and wearing a straw hat at a rakish angle stepped out of a cab in front of Warsey\u2019s Pure Drug Company on Sixth between Monroe anducckiucckiucckiucckiucck Street and in short order held up the place, killing Irving Warsey as he stepped, incidentally, to the cashier\u2019s window to make his deposit for the day. The robber got away with all of seventy-six dollars, collected mostly in ones and fives, depending on the deposit schedules at the companies whose accounts Warsey\u2019s handled, and just enough tens to keep the night-duty manager in the office downstairs occupied while it was going on. This last fact the killer was not aware of. He had the cab wait at the curb, ran across the street and got into another cab, that pulled out from the kerb as he came up, and away he went, hunched down, straw hat still at a rakish angle, the whole picture of a joyful holiday-figured Quaker Oats shopper on his way home to the misses and kiddies in the suburbs. The first cab drew up beside another...", "0_1_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1958, according to Mr. Acarian's watch and, while monkeying around with his recoilless rifle, which had a bazooko firing pin and thus was called a Bazooka-boy, it occurred to him that it was New Year\u2019s Eve, the final night of, he decided, and the circumstance affected him more strongly than any basic realization of the day could, in itself, possibly account for.\n\"This is New Year\u2019s Eve, \" he whispered reverently to himself. He felt a need for noise in the drumming quiet around him.\n\"New Year\u2019s Eve! \" he shouted.\n\"Yipe!\" yelled his wife. $FIRSTNAMEB0 went on manning the antenna. His wife went on being seduced. And $FIRSTNAMEM2 went on trying to make his unseasonable frozen turkeys heat up so he would have turkeys to sell to the colonists.\n\"Better watch what you eat, \" he warned the turkeys. \"Gonna get hot enough in there soon to\u2014oooh!\" He went back to his freezer.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and Stony introduced themselves and shook hands.\n\"Nice to make your acquaintance, \" said $FIRSTNAMEB0...", "1_3_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-nine in the morning</span>. I was thirty-eight years old. The Raincoast Rand Corporation had offered me the position of Manager of Puritan/Chicago\u2019s branch in Phasta City. It was a dream offer, a million-dollar-a-year position, plus stock and options. I would be taking over a troubled branch, with a staff of fortyeight, grossing over eleven million dollars a year.\nMaybe now I could nail Melissa and get out of the street. Maybe now I could buy an old Impala with a 283 and drive back to Iowa to find the farm. It would be good to sleep in my old room, to leap over the corn shocks in the dawn with the dog.\n$FIRSTNAMEB6 took the time to show me his pistols. \u201cYou never know,\u201d he kept saying, holding them up and pulling the triggers. They were all .32s. Beautiful guns, English manufacture. \u201cYou never know,\u201d he kept saying, looking for a victim. But his only problem was that, at that particular moment, there were no victimS available.\nFive\nI got in my new Cadillac and drove back to Las Vegas and the American Artist Convention at the New Domicil. I figured I could cool...", "2_9_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-four in the morning</span>.\nThe time was five minutes before $FIRSTNAMEB2 was to be asked to recite the articles of his new faith. It was a short list, as was proper:\n1. The best any human or any immortal creature can hope for is to be one day\u2014perhaps far off in the future\u2014really, truly, and reflectively cooked.\n2. Life on Earth is nasty, brutish, and short.\n3. Today is the day my God has made. I will rejoice and be glad in it.\nThe Reverend Stanley Ruiz-Yellow appeared on the stage at the other end of the big auditorium. He was a wizened young man\u2014thin, pale, shirtless, shoeless. Around his pious neck hung the seven beads and the single-string guitar of the sorrowful saints.\nThere was no music, but the audience concurred in humming the usual reverent melody.\nThe harmonium, the sanctus bell, and the tambourines were mounted on the walls. All three of the instruments had been built in the cave, according to the plans sent down from the space ship. All three instruments were tuned to the sighs of Earth.", "1_22_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> when I left the gambling-room with the taste of the dust of the Rue Montorgueil in my mouth and made my way out through the portico where so many bag people were already asleep. As I was about to cross the big square in front of the Standard I heard a woman crying, \u201cMarie! Marie!\u201d in a very loud, hoarse voice. I turned my head and saw a tall black woman in a camel's-hair shawl running after a short girl in a green dress. They both ran into the lobby of the hotel. I got a glimpse of them as they rushed past the desk with their elbows in the air. The clerk behind the desk looked like he didn't know whether he was going to laugh or be shocked. Then I saw them running at full speed with the arms of each other around a corner of the Standard Hotel. Behind them walked slowly a canvas man with two empty Montgomery Ward bags on his shoulders and a tin cup in his hand; he moved his head like a bird. I crossed the square and walked to the Rue Pergolese and had a beer at the refreshment stand on the way to my room. I never did figure out what that situation was. Who that woman was who started...", "1_13_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one sixteen in the afternoon</span>. The terror in our hearts made us feel like we were in a dream. Our poor car described painful oscillations as I struggled to keep it from hydroplaning...approaching a sign that said:\nI kid you not. The words had the authentic ring of Vegas cheap brass, but there it was. Turn right toward \u201cNevada Celebrity Colony\u201d and \u201cLake Puente.\u201d Stay straight for \u201cSummerville-Race Track.\u201d I could hardly believe my eyes: we were really going to do it. We were really going to follow a road sign, chancy at best, out of the known universe and down an unknown highway\u2014just to see what would happen.\nThe Flamingo passed us on our left, heading in the opposite direction. Or at least I assumed it was the actual Flamingo\u2014I\u2019d never seen the place, but it sure looked right, standing there at its odd oblique angle on a raised slope of desert, gaunt and alone, as the big white Chevy swerved toward the right and vanished. A reflective blur passed us on the left and vanished, too\u2014a whirlwind of afternoon sun off a big silver Greyhound that swerved after the Flamingo. I...", "0_14_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty in the afternoon</span>, Denver time, and Inverarity had just learned of Spatula. The initial violence of his reaction had worn itself out, to be replaced first by disbelief, then by outrage and fury, last by the gravest, most deeply furrowed concern. As always, Big Dick was exhausting himself. Nobody was feeding him the lines he so apparently needed. Conversation with Inverarity had always been a clever desperation, of a piece with his style of law. In private, $FIRSTNAMEF2\u2019s lawyer always wore voluminous white silk shirts open down to the waist, so convinced was he of the mental and olfactory cloistering power of his chest hair.\n\u201cLook,\u201d he said now, fumbling out a handkerchief, \u201cI want you to go back to the law firm, find that will, check it against last year\u2019s tax returns.\u201d\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 gave him a long, cool look that would have played havoc with many a committed actor\u2019s concentration. \u201cSo it\u2019s probably right,\u201d she said.\n\u201cHoney,\u201d sighed Inverarity, \u201cyou think I\u2019d have worried this much if...", "1_15_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-two in the afternoon</span>. The Dragon\u2014Lily Cult had mobilized its attack. The first wave hit at 5:47 P.M. Fifty-four Orientals wearing whiteface, black sunglasses, and the Standard Earthman human-hair wig ambushed a Beatnik poetry reading in the patio section of the San Francisco Sleepers. There were two casualties, one heart attack, and one minor stab wound. The second wave struck at six twenty-five. Five men and two women gunned down a pair of San Francisco Diggers who demanded their wallets. There was one fatality, an Oedipus Rex, who died of a heart attack spawned by his own terror. The Dragons let him lie. The third wave struck at eight twenty-one P.M., on a corner of Haight Street, outside the Scotch of St. James tavern. This time the assault force consisted of fifty-six men, women, and children, who, according to a spokesman, \u201cjust happened to be walking by.\u201d All carried cans of aerosol Shasta fragrances. Twenty People\u2019s Drug Stores and five Establishment soft-drink franchises were demolished in a hail of bricks, bottles, roar-gaffs, M-80 firecrackers, and obscen...", "0_18_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. Driblette had been reading his mail, and in a few minutes the class was due to start.\n(Rummaging through our baggage, to use his expression, $FIRSTNAMEF2 had turned up the handbill that had been sent her in the mail, announcing the Trystero performance at The Scope. The poster showed only a many-legged creature, like a centipede, in a comics-mirror image, its antennae festooned with usher\u2019s gloves, pocket watches, miscellanea. It was called the Tristero. PresHOW?). Winter dusk had come on. Over in Berkeley it was blues night at the Spot, but that was only a memory. Driblette was going to teach two hours\u2019 consecutive theater surveys that night, finishing with silent-period movies. $FIRSTNAMEF2 would never forget him. \u201cThere\u2019s a flick called The Courier\u2019s Tragedy,\u201d he was telling a blonde in ski pants, who might have been a student. \u201cWing-ding villain wears this big silly clown suit, with puffy pants. It was banned in England for twenty years. Can\u2019t imagine why. No contest.\u201d The blonde went...", "1_7_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven nineteen in the morning</span>. I went into the bathroom and took an absolutely cold shower. I stayed in under the shower for a full five minutes, letting the water run all over me. I felt curiously happy and contented. I climbed out, wiped myself dry with a towel and started getting dressed.\nI put on a clean shirt, socks, some dungaree overalls and my old lace-up riding boots. I took my money and credit cards out of my pockets and put them away in the dresser. I took my gun out of the dresser drawer and stuck it in the back of my belt, put my raincoat and the goofball into one of the pockets of the overalls and shut the drawer.\nInto the other pocket I put my notebook and all the remaining manuscript, my Ace High Zipper bag, and my remaining blotter. From the medicine cabinet I took a bottle of Librium and all the Valium my friend had left behind. After checking to make sure the running water in the bathroom sink wasn\u2019t too hot, I poured half of both bottles into the basin and ran the cold water into them.\nOut of the corner of my eye I saw something on the dresser that made me want to laugh. It was such...", "2_15_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-one in the afternoon</span>. I was at the Woodstock Music & Art Fair, far out in theVirginiaCountryside. The last time I'd felt like this was on the Coast Highway, with a hundred screaming Angels around my car...threatening to drag me out, slash me with knives and stomp me into the sandy beach. It was a bad moment.\nThe girl turned away\u2014or tried to. She was slow getting out of my grip, and I had to struggle with both paws gripping her shoulder to keep from crashing into the crowd. An old man said, \u201cHey, how about some service here?\"", "0_9_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-two in the morning</span> of a mid-November day in 1994. A man with gray hair and a gray mustache, wearing a rumpled brown suit, a white shirt with a brown stain near the pocket, a brown tie with squiggles, and uncomfortable shoes, was walking north on Proxmire Boulevard, near Spontoon Avenue, in the Maryland suburb of Chevy Chase. The man carried a crested briefcase in his left hand, and a small transistor radio with a leather case in his right. Every few minutes he would use the radio as a pointer, rattling it at whatever caught his attention, illuminating with cryptic intensity some housewife cautiously replacing a Currier-and-Ives light in an antique sconce, or a store with old Coca-Cola signs in the windows and books on the sidewalks: Will Rogers, Carl Sandburg, Gay Talese, some Mary Higgins Clark. Numberless day-old newspapers sat in gutters like so much tumbleweed. This man was from another time. And it was not just his overcoat, or the radio, or the haircut, or his anxious walk. This man was clearly terrified. He looked pale, and his hands trembled spasmodically. The only things in Chevy Chase that should terrify a person were the...", "0_0_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past midnight</span>. In an alley behind a horrible little hotel shaped like a wedge of pie there was a dark doorway that widened into an empty room. Into this doorway came John tall and gaunt, an unlighted cigarette in his fingers, a haze of white hair around the shadow of his face. He stood and looked behind him, out into the street. He was tall and gaunt, and the shadow of his wide jaw fell over his closed lips. He puffed his cigarette, clumsily, and the ash dropped so that he had to put his feet out and bend his knees to make room for the fluttering gray flakes on the toe of his cracked shoe.\n\"Ah, dammit to hell,\" he said disgustedly. He brushed his gray hair back from his high, fine forehead, gripped his cigarette firmly between thumb and forefinger, and brought it, smoking, to his lips. \"Ah, dammit to hell,\" he said.\nHe turned his back to the dark doorway and faced the wedge of pie-shaped hotel. Somewhere in the hall a key rattled. There were footsteps. The tall figure of $SURNAME1 peered around the corner. In one hand he held a wad of bills as fat as his fist.\n\"Now, children,\"...", "1_4_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four sixteen in the morning</span>, Sunday, the second of November. My plans were all screwed to hell by now. Spending the night in Nickey's filthy little room had demonstrated conclusively that there was only one bed and one chair, forcing me to sleep on the floor with a dirty foam rubber pad. I sat on the foam rubber pad with my feet up on the bed and drank beer. Nickey had connected a fluoroscope to his TV set; it was connected to his cat's cradle, too. His cat was named Gypsy and he obviously loved the beast; she was a beautiful animal--I said something like this, and we had an argument.\nWe argued about everything, including the Bible. Nickey had turned out to be an incredible Biblical scholar. There was an old, shabby, leatherette-bound King James Version in his kitchen for cook book purposes, and Nickey had memorized every word of it from the Psalms on. There were big chunks of it in our conversations all night. \u201cSay brother, knowest thou the Word of the Lord, for I knowest thou comest to judgement. Watch out for that car, good brother, for I fear for thy soul, thou wouldst be better off in church with the repentent.", "2_21_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-one in the evening</span>. The place was Cleveland Heights. The noise was a bell.\n/c/\n\u201cRemind me later to have them recarpet.\u201d\n\u201cGo to college. \u201d\n\u201cFrequent from nine-thirty on too.\u201d\n\u201cAnd I just urge you to try to remember a time before he talks, when it really was all right. Try to remember what it was like, back.\u201d\n\u201cMaybe when you were tired or something. Relaxed, y\u2019 know. Maybe that\u2019s what it was. He just drove you too\u2014\u201d\n\u201c...\u201d\n\u201cHmmm?\u201d\n\u201cGo to college.\u201d\n\u201cFrequent from nine-thirty on.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cJust a bit broken, Andy. No more.\u201d\n\u201cThere usually isn\u2019t more, unfortunately.\u201d\n\u201cI thought that bitch Prietht bit it, and they were looking somewhere else. Maybe its eyes were closed, who knows?\u201d\n\u201cBut it\u2019s there.\u201d\n\u201cHrmm.\u201d", "0_6_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until seven o'clock in the morning</span>, the President was about to make his speech.\nNow $FIRSTNAMEB1 was saying over the public address system, \"Excuse me, folks\u2014\"\nAnd $FIRSTNAMEB0 had said to Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1: \"Let me out. Let me out of here! I\u2019ve got to get back to my unit as fast as I can. I\u2019ve got to stop that thing!\"\nMiss $FIRSTNAMEF1 had said: \"If you don\u2019t reseal this door properly, the air\u2019s going to get so thin people will faint.\"\n\"I\u2019ve got to be there,\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 had said to Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I\u2019ll meet you after it\u2019s over,\" Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1 had said.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had put the helmet back on, and now Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1 was putting in her tape. It might be days before she and $FIRSTNAMEB0 would meet again. Before that meeting, Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1 would have to catch up on the events of the last twenty-four hours. Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1 was superstitious about not meeting anybody twice in the same day.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was asking everybody to stand up and be quiet, please.\nThe music over the public address system died. The excited murmurs died.\nThe people stood up in...", "1_23_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was standing inside the door at the bottom of the spiral staircase when the chrono-integrator went off. He had just made up his mind to go up the stairs\u2014had just made up his mind that it was too late to make up his mind to go up the stairs.\nBeatifically he held his cigarette in his left hand, his straw in his right.\nThe staircase, as had been agreed between themselves, did not weep $FIRSTNAMEB1, harmonium and $FIRSTNAMEB0 when they went up it. There was a dry, scurrying sound, but the sound did not identify itself as coming from the staircase.\nThe staircase was a servant that did its work well.\nDownstairs, $FIRSTNAMEB0 stood like a sentry in a cathedral.\nA sentry he was. He assumed that he had been posted to guard his comrade against all comers, friendly and hostile. He took his tremulous cigarette from his lips, held it straight out from the side of his face. \"Halt!\" he said. He did not take his eyes from his watch. He felt that he was taking his watch very seriously.\nInto eternity he would stand stock-still.\nAnd then mad laughter would shake him. He would...", "2_22_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-seven in the evening</span>. The place was only four miles from the White House, from the house of the boys\u2019 mother, Bat Durston.\nThe time was four minutes after ten forty-seven. The place was a forest of giant redwood trees several miles north of the campus of the University of California at Berkeley.\nRobert F. Wagner was in the forest, lying in the mud and rain under a redwood.\nHe was fifty years old. His gray hair was soft and damp. It had been soft and damp all day. Wagner\u2019s eyes were half open.\nWagner, on the ground and in the downpour, had had a lovely day. No more pain, no more either-or. Nothing but the wonderful feeling he had had all day that he, Robert F. Wagner, had swum the Atlantic under a car and come up in the Pacific Ocean with a gold watch in his teeth.", "1_1_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-six in the morning</span>. We had approximately twenty-four hours to spread the news. There is no time like the present to start a major riot; especially in downtown Vegas, where a serious outbreak of violence can be blamed on the visiting Ford Rally. The rally was scheduled to begin at eleven-thirty on Monday. That left roughly seventeen hours to the acid millennium. But those of us who understood the gravity of the moment, knew our acid billions were already on the move, fanning out across the face of the various Rollin-sons that they themselves had provided. We knew the thing to do was relax, get in tune with the moment, and wait for the call that would bring us together at the Rally, as keynote speakers and organizational agents...yes, and there was bound to be a laugh or two on the road to Vegas.\nJust before dawn we packed up our fear and our lunatic speed\u2014for Los Ange-les, where Pig Bodine intended to link up with an old \u201cFreak Rescue\u201d Contact who supposedly specialized in clearing the road to Vegas. Ten years ago, this guy had supposedly saved a rolling monster\u2014a fantastic crosscountry convoy of blazing A\u2019s, hundreds of head\u2014horrendous, acneform, red\u2014eyed...", "1_19_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-six in the evening</span> and it was Monday night in Fort Worth, Texas, and though it is bad luck to begin a trip on a Monday, this was a special trip, and I knew we would be lucky. We had decided to drive straight through to Vancouver, British Columbia, and Monterey, California, in one swoop. I knew a shortcut straight across Texas, New Mexico, and Arizona--620 miles the shortest way. I planned it suiting my taste, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 found it delightfully insane. It would take us through Roy, New Mexico, a town where I'd drunk great wines in a bar that barely escaped being closed down by the vigilant chamber of commerce for allowing \u201crougher element\u201d to loaf around after hours and drink for a nickel.\nAnd so at seven thirty on a Monday evening $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I left Fort Worth, Texas, and drove the hundred yards to my aunt's house to pick up my aunt and cousins for a little farewell talk and then came back with my coat, siphoned gas to the Ford, and off to the Oklahomas in the dead long spread-out night. The tinny radio went on at high volume, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 drove and sweated, and we skeedaddled across town into the endless screeching manifold heaven...", "2_4_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-two in the morning</span>. If $FIRSTNAMEB2 had not yet arrived, he would arrive very soon. Moonwater felt no need to practice her remembered message on Moonary Rigg. She was ready.\n\"You are an embodiment of everything that is good and kind in Mars,\" said Minerva Soo Strok.\n\"From what little has filtered up from Earth, my dear, I suspect you will find much of our contemporary news truly entertaining\u2014beyond comedy. You are strong and your husband is strong, so you should do well in this environment. May I wish you godspeed, my dear girl.\"\n\"What was that?\" said Moonwater, out loud.\n\"Beg your pardon, Mrs. $SURNAME0?\" said her butler.\n\"That\u2014the noise just now?\" said Moonwater. \"You didn\u2019t hear anything, $FIRSTNAMEM1?\".", "1_12_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-seven p.m.</span> That would be 77:43 round trip, with an additional 3:42 in the vertical-launch area. He would be back in the lilac bushes an hour after his watch came off\u2014at one twenty-five.\nThe perfect crime, thought $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014beat the rap, beat the devil, and be home in time for lunch. The perfect crime.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 disappeared into the capsule. He closed the hatch.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked at his watch. It was going to be like finding a needle in a haystack, tracking the flight of that bullet, but $FIRSTNAMEB0 decided he was going to track it\u2014find out who it killed. He might have to track it all the way to the University Hospital\u2014find out who got shot all the way up there.\nThen, when he found the man his son had murdered, he was going to murder his son.\nThe search wasn\u2019t going to be easy. The flight of the bullet would be traced through a system of cables and switches controlled by electronic computers\u2014and the computers would be programmed either to forget instantly what they had done, or to indicate that their circuits had been tampered with when they were next turned on.\nBut that was the least part of it.\nWhat...", "2_6_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six o'clock in the morning</span>, at the corner of Main and Vegas, mile marker zero of American civilization. To my left, the tall grey\u2014stone of the courthouse. Its tower clock was ticking off the arrival of a new day. To my right was a pothole the size of a swimming pool. It was filled with black water and garbage. Overhead, the power\u2014lines were drooping in the first heat of the sun. Over these, at every intersection, were long black cables that snapped and popped in the current.\n\u201cL ocal cops just threw another body in the can and kicked the door shut. The guy's screaming and shouting. There's blood all over the floor. The drunk tank is full. The mens room smells like an animal cage at the zoo.", "0_2_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to three o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were lying side by side behind a split-log bench in a beautifully kept public park. Ahead of them, five-tenths of a second away, stood a small frame church with a white spire.\nThe church interior seemed to be illuminated by a dim blue light.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t know the secret of the church\u2019s blue illumination. He wished he did.\nThe blue illumination hurt his and $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s blue eyes almost unbearably. Even through closed eyelids, the light smote them.\nThe pink granite of the buildings of Oldtown hurt the eyes, too\u2014 and so did the green flagstones in the sidewalks and the red bricks in the streets. And so did the cherries, plums, peaches, and oranges that ripened on the trees and vines for ten months of the Martian year. In the dry powder that filled the red-walled cities of Mars, millions of tiny bits of ruby, sapphire, topaz, and emerald upped the piercing power of their clear, cold light a hundredfold.\nOldtown in copious sunlight had always hurt the eyes of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1. And, though this had never been...", "2_11_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-six in the morning</span>.\nThe Skipafish Club had its headquarters in shabby, charmless Edgon. It was reached by means of the kind of passage where you have to press a lot of buzzing bells before somebody comes to the peephole and finds out who you are.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had pressed his way around the perimeter of the block twice before he managed to arouse anybody in the neighborhood.\nIn the windows he passed were displays of Oriental powder horns, pennywhistles, horse collars, and boxes of army surplus safety matches.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 hated all of Earth as much as he hated Newport.\nAt the back of the building, in the yard, were two old trucks without springs, each dying a slow death under a tarpaulin. The trucks were swarming with bees.\nFrom the windows of the upper floor tumbled ideas, like scraps of construction paper that have stuck to damp plaster.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 climbed a broken drainpipe to the balcony of the second floor. $FIRSTNAMEB0 used the rusted skeleton of a swing on the balcony to scale the wall to the third floor. $FIRSTNAMEB0 reached around a corner beam and found an open window.\nHe entered the room.", "0_8_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-six in the morning</span>. Chronologically, only six minutes had passed since the abrupt and startling arrival of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 in the reception room of $SURNAME0\u2019s museum.\n$SURNAME1 tore off his suit of shimmering gold fishnets, stuffed it into a waste receptacle. He put on coveralls, a gas mask, boots, a mackintosh, put his precious roll of blue paper in a waterproof packet, strapped the packet to his back. He was ready at last to descend with the fountain.\nHe drew some refreshment from the water spigot. The refreshment tasted like rain.\nFour feet below the fountain was a rank, impenetrable jungle of any hardy Mars plant that could survive, at perihelion, in the insufficient light of the Martian sun. During the long, dark night of the year, the jungle fell as far as fifty feet below the fountain. The jungle\u2019s floor was eighteen inches below where $SURNAME1 stood.\nThe fountain roared up through the greenery, turned left, and fell in a graceful parabola fifty feet away, where it formed a pool of clear, pea-green water. The fall in the pool made a fountain, too\u2014a very small one.\nTwo Martian squirrel...", "1_5_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-two in the morning</span>. The parking-lot lights had not yet been turned on. In the thin motel darkness I could see tumbleweed blowing across the rutted desert plain that was Las Vegas Boulevard. Far off, a streetlight burned red in the soft white of dawn. Wrapping myself in the blanket, I got out of the car and checked the body again. It was still there. No one had driven up during the sixty seconds I\u2019d been standing in the parking lot. I got back into the car and settled down in the driver\u2019s seat again. I did not feel like breakfast. But there was nothing else to do.\nThere was a knock on the Magnum\u2019s window. A runty cop with a pencil mustache was staring hard at me. His hand was resting on his gun. He wanted me to roll down the window.\n\u201cYeah?\u201d I said.\n\u201cYou feel all right, buddy?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cYep.\u201d I hit the window lever and the window dropped down.\n\u201cYou been sleeping in your car here?\u201d\nI laughed. \u201cNot exactly,\u201d I said. \u201cThe engine\u2019s been knocking real bad. I...", "2_2_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-seven in the morning</span>. Lang levered himself off the edge of the table. Lenore felt tired. She felt wadded-up. The shadows of the gym were beginning to merge again into the general darkness. She felt that she still could not without deep disgust make any serious eye contact with Lang, who right now was strolling over to a wall, rubbing his stomach. Over by the gym door there was a flashing of light.\n\u201cThere\u2019s this group of kids who have a lota keys of crystal and stuff, who sometimes come in here and let off flash and bang a little,\u201d Lang said, looking down at Lenore from a plastic bag. \u201cShould I assume from the look on your face that this might be one of those times?\u201d\n\u201cThey\u2019re here?\u201d Lenore said, standing up and looking over at the door. She could indeed see some faint flashes of light in the east corridor.\n\u201cYou don\u2019t have to worry,\u201d said Lang, looking into the bag. \u201cThey\u2019re in a locked part of the building, behind a door with a thumb-lock, and no one has a key but me, I promise you.\u201d", "0_1_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span> when I finally went to bed in my own room at the Finca Vigia. I had a heavy heart, and it was too early to go to sleep. For a while I tried reading. I could not concentrate; and then, to get away from myself, I turned on the radio and took the dial straight to the American Forces Network at Frankfurt on the Isar in Bavaria.\nI was still tuned to Frankfurt when I fell asleep, and when I awoke two hours later I was still tuned to Frankfurt. This seemed to me symbolic, and I regretted I had not had the foresight to set the radio for the middle of the Atlantic.\n11 RESCUE WITH RAYMOND\nLINKING BACK TO SAN FRANCISCO\u2014languid rides in the jungle dampness of the greenest tropic night\u2014was like coming out of a basement into daylight. From the airport at S\u00e4o Paulo you could see the silver-blue Guararapes Mountains to the north. The granite peaks gleamed like liquid silver in the moonlight. Their presence was the purest indication of the absolute difference between the South American tropics and the North American tropics. In the Presidente Kennedy Airport at S\u00e4o Paulo,...", "1_0_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after midnight</span>. In one minute it would be Armonk time, and it would still be Parsippany time in the kitchen at The Folio. A party was being held in the lobby at the rear of the lobby, directly off the broad corridor that ran the length of the building. A portable bar had been brought out, with pretty girls to serve. The theory of the thing, according to Norman Bombardini, was that the lobby, being corridor-like and thin, was indeed part of the great corridor, and hence anyone in the lobby would, for one last time before Doomsday, rightfully be considered to be in the corridor ... and hence would have access to the liquor. The theory of the thing obviously appealed a great deal to certain high-level executives in Bombardini\u2019s firm.\nThere were sofas, chairs, and tables in the lobby. On the far side, in the east wall, there was a narrow opening that led to a spiral staircase that ran up to the third and last floor of Madison Square Garden. Two stories above the third and last floor was where Norman Bombardini\u2019s office was.\nThe spiral staircase led to a lobby up there and to offices and to a landing with a door that led to Bomb...", "2_19_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-one in the evening</span>.\nBiggs heard footsteps of someone climbing the stairs.\n\"Thank God!\" said his wife. \"I was scared stiff they might have come and got you.\"\n\"Nope,\" said Biggs.\n\"How do you feel?\"\n\"Rotten,\" said Biggs.\n\"Are you ready?\"\n\"Like a bird,\" said Biggs.\n\"Did you thank those nice men?\"\n\"Yep,\" said Biggs.\n\"I\u2019ll go tell them you\u2019re going.\"\n\"Right-o,\" said Biggs.\nHis wife threw open the door, ran down the stairs, came back a moment later.\n\"I told them you were going to go.\"\n\"Hooray,\" said Biggs.\nWINNIE MAE HUBBELL TURNED TWICE in the water, lay still on the surface. Her hair, spread out on the water, made a rainbow. The lake was alive with thousands of rainbows. The lake was shivering with thousands of rainbows. Magnesium was raining down on the lake.\nKilgore Trout watched the rainbows with his binoculars.", "2_9_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-seven in the morning</span>.\nWhat remains to be told happened outside the stone castle. It happened so fast that hardly anyone in the village had time to see it or to understand it.\nThe Tralfamadorians on the metal treeisks rose gracefully into the air and streaked pell-mell toward the iron castle. There were four hundred and eighty-six Tralfamadorians on fifty-five treeisks. They reached the castle in a matter of seconds.\nThe four hundred and eighty-six Tralfamadoreans there\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, still dead, was deposited in an office of the Pentagon in September, 1957.\nOn October 15, 1957, his skeleton and a few scraps of flesh were discovered in an unused basement space in the warren that had beguiled poor $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThe Army had no record of $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe Army considered $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s remains to be those of a homeless man.\nNo one ever found out what became of young $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nThe remains of the Tralfamadorians who had siege-bombed the iron castle were never discovered. The assumption has to be made that they vaporized against the walls of the castle, or flew far away into space.", "1_9_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-four in the morning</span> of October thirtieth; the place was the roof of the Department of Sanitation\u2019s tall building at 215 North Sycamore.\nFrom the roof, the jagged shore line of Metzger\u2019s Grape Vine Bay Harbor estate could be clearly seen. The shore line was riddled with the mint green water inlets that flowed into the coral pink lagoons.\nThe estate\u2019s thirty-six-hole championship private golf course took up much of the land. The rest was a formal garden, with a duck pond, marble benches, a labyrinth hedge, and an authentic seventeenth-century baroque temple folly in the very center. There was also a guest house, a main house, and a tremendous vaulted cave.\nIn the cave lived Mr. Metzger\u2019s one hundred and twenty-seven trained lizards.\nEighteen of the lizards were used as putting caddies. The lizards were kept in the golf-club pro\u2019s bare cement office, just off the first tee. The lizards lived in illuminated bird-cages. Only the fearless, three-handed pro had the privilege of entering their milieu. He fed them cantaloupes laced with insecticide.", "2_16_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span> on an August Saturday with the sun speeding toward the desert and the final grandinazto fervor of a football game and some of the worst traffic jams ever in history. We were trapped in the midst of one and\u2014Mercury, listen: We had to get the FUCK OUT OF THERE.\nTwo minutes after four. I could see the Pennsylvania cop in the distance, weaving into the traffic and cutting between cars. I could hear the roar of the crowd, muffled by all the cars and the distance. \u201cWhat was what all about?\u201d I kept telling myself that everything was perfectly all right as long as nobody made any sudden moves...but where the fuck could we go?\nSeven minutes after four. He was fifty feet away. Our wheels were spinning. How were we going to explain this?\nWe had to get the fuck out of this car.\nI grabbed all our shit off the back seat and piled it on top of the dashboard.", "2_16_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We were somewhere on the Pennsylvania Turnpike. On both sides of the road were restaurants with bare trees and electric candles making spectral shadows in the gloom. In these I saw people having dinner, actual people with actual money who weren't with the others. I wanted to sit down and eat with them. \u201cWhat are you doing?\u201d\n\u201cWho's that in the back seat?\u201d\nFear, pain, horrible realization. I struggled to think. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was in the back seat trying to hide but the captain saw him immediately. \u201cYou step right out, sir!\u201d Captain Bockheim was a formal man used to copping addicts on the lam. We opened the door with fumbling fingers and got out. There was broken glass over everything in my car, the windshield sprinkled black with explosions of the past.\n\u201cWell, well, well,\u201d said Captain Bockheim. \u201cSo it's that kind of party. Where you coming from?\u201d\nWe thought frantically of some place far distant. \u201cYa-a-tik-a-tin,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 stuttered horribly. \u201cNew York.\u201d", "0_10_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-three in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been awakened five minutes before. He lay on the bunk in a state that modern recruits would not have the zeal to invent. The pain, the fear, and the buzzing in his head would have been too much for modern recruits. They would have wept, fainted, or shot the first sergeant who tried to waken them at this godforsaken hour.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wept copiously, but his eyes were still tightly closed. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face was pale with fatigue, despite the hard solar toil he had been allotted. $FIRSTNAMEB0 trembled. And the only reason he didn\u2019t go to pieces in a more spectacular fashion was that he had already gone to pieces in more spectacular fashion hundreds of times back on Mars. There was nothing now to which he could be exposed on Earth or among the moonbees that could be much worse than the things to which he had been exposed on Mars.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" said a voice, \"your animal is dying. \"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shuddered. That was the voice of Sergeant $SURNAME2, the man in charge of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s squad, the man in charge of $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe admission of an animal into the closed world of a...", "1_2_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-two in the morning</span>, according to the clock on the wall. Hadleyburg had enjoyed fourteen hours of sweet rest since its sensational last minutes.\nTo this point these few minutes of new history presented a harsh pattern of moral contradiction and revenge\u2014a picture of scofflaw one-upmanship and sheer nastiness. But there was more to come, and there would be an upclimax, a point of reconciliation and forgiveness. It hadn\u2019t been easy to set the stage for it. The test would be, would the townspeople have wit enough to appreciate the reconciliation and forgiveness when it came?\nThere was a timid knock at the parlour door. The door opened, and a young fellow in a very long overcoat, his hands in his pockets, stood framed in the doorway. He was red-eyed and unshaven.\nNo one spoke for a moment.\n\"Want to see the guy that gets the money,\" the young fellow said in a hoarse voice.\nWashington began to make noises in his throat. He swallowed with difficulty.\n\"That\u2019s me,\" said J.R.\n\"I\u2019m Louie Blodgett,\" said the young man. \"You gotta father named Selah Blodget...", "1_12_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had come awake as usual with a galvanic jolt. His eyes snapped open and showed no conscious control. Living in a drum was practically the only condition under which $FIRSTNAMEB0 could be wrenched awake in a real hurry. He had come awake so often in a real hurry that he could not conceive of awakening in any other way.\nAnd he came awake with instant recognition of the situation. There had been no dreams, no fantasies\u2014nothing to obscure the sights and sounds and smells of waking up: wet straw, grainy skin, the taste of iron in the mouth, the smell of a rain-wet stone wall on a windy dawn in late April, the sound of a dripping condenser, the sight of a patch of cloud-filtered yellow sunlight on his horny, cracked, nail-bitten right hand.\nThese impressions swept over $FIRSTNAMEB0 with a terrible force\u2014a force that seemed to indicate the immediate proximity of friends, a mate, conveniences, and a purpose in life.\nThe whole kit and caboodle of comforting illusions was rocked at its foundations by the jolt from the alarm clock in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head. The initial moment of waking up abounded in ironies that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was too drow...", "0_18_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past six o'clock in the evening</span> on the first day of October. The place was the Martian surface, on the plain among the quivering aspen stalks, among the rocks that were red, in the thin air beneath the pale, cold sun of Mars.\nThe sunrise over a great plain like this is a strange and lovely thing. The shadows it casts are not black, but violet. The sun is not yellow, but white. And it does not really rise. It comes up out of the ground.\nThe man stood and watched the sun come up out of the ground on Mars. He wore a spacesuit with a Martian climate control helmet that protected his face, with a wind- and dust-protection visor that covered his face. He had on a thin, light American sports jacket, knitted shirt, slacks, and suede shoes.\nHe was unprotesting and his blue eyes were clear. He watched the sun come up and pondered the roaring silence and desolation of the lifeless world among whose rock crests he wandered. He had nothing to say and he did not touch the communicator in his ear.\nHe was unprotesting. He felt no hostility toward the thing in his ear that was making him deaf and dumb and happy.\nHe had wanted...", "2_15_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>, and suddenly I was listening to the voice of a young, golden-throated, world-wise brand of desperado who was coolly riding a bus through the dusty mountains of Colorado, gliding effortlessly into view now and then with a blurred, sharklike smile and easy, knowing laughter in his eyes, talking to a friendly stranger and occasionally scratching suggestive lice out of his back.\nActually, I had no visible lice. I was just scratching. I wanted to give the impression that I had been riding buses and hitching rides for days and weeks, ever since my dreadful and bloody experiences in Las Vegas and Frisco and San Francisco...which stories I planned to tell my imminent attorney, as soon as I found him.", "0_21_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had already spent two hours in the cave\u2014two hours going nowhere in the hopeless search for some way out. The cave was a symmetrical labyrinth, a nature-made night-club dark. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had now explored two-thirds of its ways, but he had been arrested at every turn by wall or cul-de-sac.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 found himself in a typical aisle of the cave. The aisle was high and wide. It was asphalted.\nAn iron pipe carried water from one wall to another some twenty feet overhead, then carried it back to the first wall again a hundred and fifty feet away. The iron pipe had red and yellow bulbs at regular intervals, for the illumination of the cave\u2019s corridors. The illumination was ghostly and dismal.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had blundered into the cave through a crack in the mountain-side a little before six o\u2019clock in the evening when the red and yellow bulbs had given the asphalted floor a bloody and morbid look indeed. But the long, fruitless search for an exit in the death-like red and yellow gloom had impaired $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s sense of color. Colors changed with his moods now. Everything was either dark or light to him,...", "1_5_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span> of October 25, 1985, a Thursday. Red Rumner was having a dream in which, simply, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had died, and it was time for the faithful to gather round and say what had to be said about him.\nThere was the living room of a small stucco house, and Rumner and his wife lived there. A heavy rain was pounding on the roof, making it impossible to hear what anyone was saying in the dream. Nevertheless, Rumner and his wife were helping their friends to their feet, were steering them to one place or another in order to say what had to be said.\nRed Rumner's wife was not there in the dream, but she had just served Rumner a heaping plate of corned beef and cabbage for breakfast, giving him a pleasant case of indigestion. His mind, recoiling from the cabbage, now projected the following scene onto a cloudy and lavender background.\nIt was the aftermath of a funeral. At the head of a broad and gentle aisle at the center of the dream landscape was a grave that opened like the eye of a cyclone, with spirals of soft earth shooting out of it. And at the eye of the storm was $FIRSTNAMEB0, and he was naked,...", "1_10_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-two in the morning</span>. Room 223, the door ajar. Marshal Driblette hunched in his overcoat at the desk, the black phone pressed to his ear, marking time. Unblinking, he stared across at us. The lids had sealed over his eyes. Maybe he saw us, maybe he didn\u2019t. We could all use a rum, a beer. My tongue was glued to the top of my mouth. $FIRSTNAMEF2 took off her sunglasses. All my windows were shuttered. The breeze dying down, the room was again stifling. By the open door the beginnings of a coat hanger bent out of the closet across and parallel to the door\u2019s edge, holding up an empty configuration. $FIRSTNAMEF2 and I stared at it awhile.\n\u201cThere was a coat here,\u201d Driblette said one of these minutes, \u201ctrenchcoat, overcoat, something like that. So long.\u201d He hung up. I thought of asking him about the initials. He might not have noticed, he might have noticed and decided he didn\u2019t care, he might have tried to put me on, he might not have been there at all last night. What would follow would be my asking him...", "1_18_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. Salo and $FIRSTNAMEF1 had been on Titan nearly ten hours, and they had accomplished virtually nothing. They had tried going to stores and buying things, but stores were closed. Salo had tried going to the home of a childhood friend who was a policeman, but the friend was out\u2014with his wife.\nSo Salo and $FIRSTNAMEF1 had strolled in the park.\nThe reason Salo was able to view the bright-bathed Earth so clearly was that a low, dense bank of clouds had rolled in off the sea to dampen the brilliance of Titan\u2019s Sun. The lowered brilliance made the Earth somewhat more visible.\nThe bank of cloudy sea leaked salty rain, as all banks of sea mist do. The rain came down as cold fog.\nSalo\u2019s restaurant was in the basement of a public building in Zenith. The restaurant, being in the basement, was naturally dark in the light of day. But the furnace of the public building pumped warm air into the restaurant through gratings in the floor.\nThe restaurant was expensive and exclusive. It was a popular rendezvous among Tralfamadorians on short visits to earth.\nOn the walls were pictures of Tralfamadore. On the walls, too,...", "2_5_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. Any normal human being would have been asleep in bed at such a time...but there we were, wide awake, screaming through the dawn in a red\u2014light district somewhere in the back\u2014 ass end of northern Virginia, with a gang of wild bulls chasing us. Knowing full well the death penalty for red light\u2014running in Virginia is throwing the damn crook into a river with cement overshoes written across his eyes, we screamed through the red light at what seemed to be full emergency throttle. Unexpectedly, our limousine got a small break: we were accelerating into the sunrise with abandon, and for some unaccountable reason the rudeness of the motor cops vanished an instant before we would have run smack into them...disappearing, possibly, because we came out of the intersection like a bat out of hell and hurled straight up into the sunrise...suffering only a small cut on the seat\u2014sniffer from what seemed to be a throwaway beer\u2014bottle thrown by the last hostile motor cop who had lost interest in killing us.", "2_21_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThat was all.\nExcept that fifty-eight million people were watching Television.\nTelevision was getting ready to go off the air. The show that was currently on was over. By an irony, the show that was currently on was a quiz show.\nTelevision was owned by R.S.V.P., Cellophane, Ltd., which was a subsidiary of Hughes Aircraft. This was more or less coincidental.\n\"There is, of course,\" said the announcer, \"as always one moment of the show to which we look forward most eagerly\u2014that one moment which marks the end, the completion, the climax, of the whole joint intellectual effort of you, the contestants, and us, the harmonious staff. If there\u2019s any time of the show that we unfailingly and unashamedly whistle like birds through our teeth at, it\u2019s that moment. Well, that moment is now. Fittingly enough, at this very moment, the two contestants who were determined tonight to constitute that final moment are engaged in precisely that final moment.\n\"So let\u2019s have the two contestants.\"", "0_15_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October 9, 2172, C.E.\u2014Julian Calendar\u2014Reckoning: 9694 A.D.\u2014E.C.R.\u2014204 A.L. The place was Tranq Prime\u2014Headquarters of the clairvoyant army of the World Presidency:\n\u2014and the man was Major General Francis Xavier Gosdle, President of Tranq Prime.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was scheduled to die. It was the most curious of all curious coincidences that the executioner who was about to kill $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 with a shotgun was Lucian, son of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nAnd young Lucian was not only an excellent executioner, he was pleasant to look at and charming. Most observers thought him handsome. He was six feet four inches tall and weighed two hundred and ten pounds. A large part of his charm lay in the brown, gold, and white splendor of his dress uniform.\nMaj. Gen. Francis X. Gaskle, President of Tranq Prime, puzzled over a piece of paper he had taken out of his pocket. He handed the piece of paper to Lucian. \"Read that to me again,\" he said to Lucian.\nThe paper was a telegram. It had arrived...", "2_1_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe sixth punctual appearance of the guiding sphere was over.\n$SURNAME0 sat up in the wheelchair, died. His face once again looked like the face of a horrible goddess. His eyes again revolved blind and white.\nHis limbs again twitched, twitched, twitched, as though controlled by invisible marionette strings. He again became a marble mannequin in a wheelchair.\nHis beard again filled with burning, stinking sulfur. This time, the stench was so strong that birds fell dead out of trees.\nThe time was two o\u2019clock in the morning.\nGood-by, $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nMauve smog tickled $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s nostrils. He thought the smog had a sickish sweetness about it, too.\nAs though somebody had turned on a hot plate under his feet, everything and everybody flowered into action around $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. The crew appeared from belowdecks. The crew carried out $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s trunk and the cardboard box from the Time Depot.", "2_23_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-four in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 staggered, found his balance. He had his orders, and he was about to receive an illustration of some of the tricks employed by the office morale-builders.\nMajor Groverman pressed a button in the wall. His iron-green beret came off with a sharp, quiet popping sound, revealing a shag of rust-red hair underneath it. He bowed stiffly without smiling, clicking his heels. His thin moustache twitched.\nIn the next room a harmonium began beating a bass rhythm on a djembe.\nWithout taking his eyes off $FIRSTNAMEB0, Groverman reached out his right hand. It opened, revealing a small white ball, which in turn revealed four tubes of lip paint.\nAs the tubes slithered out of their ball container, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hands shot up spasmodically\u2014it was a response he couldn\u2019t control. He seized the four tubes, and clapped them over his lips. He stood stiffly at attention, eyes blinking rapidly. His Adam\u2019s apple bobbled up and down jerkily.", "2_8_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nOf the twenty-four girls who had accepted their suggestion for a picnic, only nineteen showed up. The other five were represented by cardboard cutouts.\nThe shift on its way home to the barracks laughed and yelled its way out of the great hall to the buses.\nThe shift coming to work quieted the soldiers and executives with jokes and pick-up lines and embraces.\nIn the great hall itself, a work force was being assembled. A lieutenant got a twelve-pound sledgehammer out of a tool locker and handed it to the cleaning-woman who had agreed to paint the hall.\nThe lieutenant put his nose to the cutout of a woman holding a big fish. \"When\u2019d you catch him?\" he said.\nThe cleaning-woman got a whisk broom out of the tool locker. \"Not yesterday, but the day before,\" she said, \"when we went fishing in the Baltic Sea.\"\nThe lieutenant opened the tool locker again. \"You know,\" he said, \"some of these other cutouts kind of remind me of my wife.\" He got out a clipboard, a 1040 form, a number 2 pencil. \"Let\u2019s get working.\"", "2_10_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe comradeship of Belskutt and the mechanic was now at the level where one openly asked the other personal questions, if only perfunctorily and with brusque, semi-expressionless delivery.\n\"You got a girl, Maurice?\" Belskutt asked, unfolding the map, pretending to study it, letting his breath flow out of him. The problem was a complicated one.\n\"Yep.\" The mechanic felt his back. \"Some gal named Nancie, over in Newark.\"\n\"I got me a girl, too,\" said Belskutt.\n\"Arno?\" asked the mechanic, with a knurled, blue socket wrench poised between his fingers.\nBelskutt, who still felt compelled to drop in at least one ridiculous or obvious demonstration of his loyalty each morning, pretended to be startled. \"Mercy no,\" he said, putting on an exaggerated Southern accent. \"Mercy, no. My Arno, he be off in the big camp with all the boys.\" He imitated deep masturbation.", "0_15_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-seven in the afternoon</span> on December 17, constantSolar time, and in the center of the Equator. $SURNAME1-Local time was then thirteen twenty-seven and a half, which was dusk and nightfall to somebody facing out of the concavity away from the Sun.\nSusy and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had emerged from the space ship in their space suits automatically at 3:27 p.m. The suits were acid green. The suits were so seamless that they seemed to be made of suet.\nThe predators who had come to Earth with $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Susy were superb shots, superb horsemen, experts at weather prediction, and unswervingly obedient to orders. Those traits had been demonstrated at the Kansas City Massacre. But the order to leave their horses behind and to come in a space ship to the world Redd had named Des Moines had thrown the creatures into paroxysms of doubt and worry. They had never been in a space ship before.\nTheir doubts proved groundless when their space ship docked smoothly on automatic pilot, facing in. There was nothing stars could tell them about what might happen to a space ship that faced out. The space ship would have faced out, of course, if the creatures had served in the giant fleet that had run amuck in...", "2_13_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one eighteen in the afternoon</span>.\nAt one nineteen, the parachute caught fire. At one twenty, the burning parachute collapsed in skeleton tatters on the palace doorknob.\n\"God damn it to hell anyway!\" said $SURNAME2 under his breath.\n\"Yo-ho,\" said Almighty God.\nA second $SURNAME0 Cocktail was compounded.\nAt one twenty-one, it was administered to Skip $SURNAME2.\n\"Do you feel better?\" said Muncie.\n$SURNAME2 turned blue and his hands made fists. He looked fearsome.\n\"Gorilla in a tea-gown!\" he roared.\n\"That\u2019s President $SURNAME2, you weird girl!\" said $SURNAME2, \"or I\u2019ll knock your block off!\" He looked around at the reporters who were standing in the wings. \"I\u2019ve got the right to knock her block off, reporters! It\u2019s in the Constitution! Amendment Number Ten! The right of Presidents to knock the blocks off weird girls who ask them whether they feel better,\" said $SURNAME2.\n\"What\u2019s Amendment Number Ten say about reporters?\" said Muncie.", "0_23_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-nine in the evening</span>, and they were saying good-by to their wives and families. They were any soldier.\nLen Bacon, a secret agent of the English, had called up the Royal Bureaucracy of Mars at Effingham, Illinois, fifteen minutes before, had gotten the mother of all switchboarding operators, and had left a message to be given to Johnnie Primus, wherever he might be found.\nThe minutes passed.\nAt last unrecognized Joseph Pocket, back in his dark little office, heard noises from within the great room of the hall.\nHe snitched up the hall a ways and listened. There was the blessed sound of really-and-truly cheering.\nThe wonderful Field Marshal, who had bravely commanded the Dungsweeper theater of the War of the Insanities for eighteen months, to the slaughter of thousands, and by direction of the Allied Governments had carried the sublime torch of freedom through the black wastes of space to the red hell of Mars\u2014he was getting his generalship back, at last.\nThe sounds from the great room grew more and more deafening. There was laughter and speaking, and the scratching of pipes being filled.\nOfficers were coming.\nRollings and rollings of laughter.\nAutarch,...", "1_5_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-three in the morning</span>, said my watch. The possible approaches were now vast and tangled. I sat there on the broken road, finally, parked and waiting, guarding my escape routes, watching the fear and the loneliness grow in the sky. I looked up into the gray dawn, into the future. How would I live? Where would I go? There are a hundred hidden corners in any city and all day I would have to hide in one of them and sleep perhaps with one eye open and with the panther snarling in my dreams. A sad clown with a bag full of flips and mumbles in the train station at Penn Station: \u201cStep right up! One girl, with her throat cut, hanging from the shower. Only one dollar. Step right up! One girl. One shower. One slashed throat. Where's your goddamn dirty frigid dollars, you sons-of-bitches?\u201d And meanwhile behind my eyelids the dreams with their dull flashes of desperation; somewhere a lament; hushed voices below. What kept me clawing away from that fragrant abyss where I might still be snoring now if I hadn't waked up in that instant and suddenly known? Beside me on the front seat were two road maps, a pint of rum, a...", "0_15_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-two in the afternoon</span> in Kitum Cave, equatorial darkness ahead and equatorial rain immediately above our heads. To dim the sun a furry canopy was draped from the roof. It was, up there, something out of \u201cThe Damnation of Theron Ware\u201d: The bats were legion: all the warm-blooded American life, stars\u2019 souls, were in that tree.\n\u201cHear that?\u201d my attorney hollered.\n\u201cNo,\u201d I shouted. \u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cSomebody\u2019s teeth, chattering. Wish I had some rum.\u201d His face was very white. \u201cHear that?\" he screamed.\n\u201cNo,\u201d I screamed back. What were we talking about?\nThen I realized what we had been talking about.\n\u201cGod, no!\u201d I screamed. \u201cIt\u2019s not the caves.\u201d A breeze touched my face and made me wince. \u201cIt\u2019s the grass,\u201d I whispered. \u201cWe\u2019re high!\u201d My attorney nodded but kept staring at the bats, apparently fascinated by something out at the end of the branches. As I watched him a...", "1_17_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME0 and his band of time-worried optimists suddenly materialized\u2014exactly as advertised\u2014in the middle of the Titanic\u2019s orchestra.\nThe last chime of five had just tinkled out.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s materialization was timed to coincide with the middle of the Titanic\u2019s band version of the grand finale\u2014the only song the spaceship\u2019s band could play well. This last song was a shivering little sarabande called \"Daisy, Daisy.\"\nA sapphire-and-gold girl alighted with $SURNAME0. She was, of course, chrono-synclastic infundibulated, and could be expected to fade at any moment.\n\"This is $FIRSTNAMEM2, you earth people,\" said $SURNAME0 grandly. \"My endeavor here is the ushering in of the Period of Optimism. For those who are telepathic among you, know that my endeavor is a sincere and hopeful one.\"\nThe sound of thousands of people tearing a page from a library book filled the Titanic\u2019s golden vault. News of $FIRSTNAMEM2 had been plucked from a chrono-synclastic infundibulum on Mars.\n\"", "0_22_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The weather was cold and dismal. A light rain fell. $FIRSTNAMEB3 had been out of the hotel all day, frantically rushing around. I had a nervous feeling all over--my hands sweated and my diaphragm trembled slightly. We looked through our bags before leaving. Then we lurched out into the street, squinting against the rain and trying to blink it off our lashes. \u201cWhere shall we go?\u201d I said. \u201cWell, let's go up to the room and have a few farewell drinks and start moving.\u201d We walked a block to Louise's house and went in. Everything was lovely and warm and pleasant. $FIRSTNAMEB3 sat down with his brown paper bag and began filling it with ice and bourbon. Walter and $FIRSTNAMEF4 were upstairs, packing trunks. I sat with $FIRSTNAMEB3, trying to drink slowly, because I still wanted to hang and drink longer. I wanted to sit with $FIRSTNAMEB3 and watch all the madness brush past and slowly taper off and grow calm. $FIRSTNAMEB3 put on victrola music, I Can't Give You Anything But Love, Honey, I've Got a Gal In Kalamazoo, and leaned back to laugh. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d he said, \u201cdo you know...", "0_5_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span> and the place was the upper north shore of the island of Manhattan-the island\u2019s up-ass end, as it were, in the neighborhood of Inwood, at the edge of an area that is known locally as the Valley of Marble. It special right in the middle of the Valley of Marble that I had seen the light. The light had been fast and hard, white as magnesium in the morning darkness. Something major was happening-the first event in a chain that would continue on through most of the rest of the day and night.\nI did not immediately recognize what the flash had been. Later I considered the possibilities, and they were only two: either a fire of some kind or a bolt of lightning. Both possibilities flew out of my head when the telephone rang almost immediately.\n\u201cCounty Police emergency,\u201d said a cop\u2019s bored, sleep-fuzzed voice. \u201cYou reported a strange light?\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d I said. \u201cI thought it was a fire.\"\n\u201cWhere?\u201d he asked.\nI described the location of my penthouse, adding \u201cabout a mile or so up the Hudson, just over the northern edge of Inwood.", "2_21_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty in the evening</span>. The place was Cleveland Heights, Ohio.\nThe noise was a bell, in an open field.\n/b/\n\u201cI think what perhaps has gone wrong is that we\u2019re both going on about life way too much.\u201d\n\u201cWhat does that mean?\u201d\n\u201cWell what do you\u2014\u201d\n\u201cLook, why not just leave? Why not leave right now, right this second? We could just leave. There\u2019s no reason we can\u2019t just leave.\u201d\n\u201cPerhaps she means to say that the ability to represent anything, to stand for anything, to be the sign of anything, is more than anything itself can endure.\u201d\n\u201cHardly different from the fish.\u201d\n\u201cHuh?\u201d\n\u201cWell, I don\u2019t think he\u2019s the most credible person, is all.\u201d\n\u201cPeter Abbott, in Warriner Hall again, treading mud through my rug, at this time of night again. I thought I went through this last year.\u201d", "2_4_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-five in the morning</span>, New York City time.\nBeatings, shootings and the swallowing up of beer cans were taking place out at Aqueduct.\nInside the walls of the Trumfoord estate, life was like a movie. Drunken security guards mingled happily with drunken newsmen. Costumed party guests wandered happily out of their castles and among the newsmen, asking the newsmen the time, smelling of perfume, hair mussed, makeup smeared. There was no guile in all this. Everyone was simply happy, and thought everybody else was happy, too. For all manner of reasons, the illusion worked well.\nRumford and Effing were still in their robes, were still ensconced in their thrones.\nHeat, who had stripped completely naked, was pulled into the crowd, from monarchy, into obscurity.\nReports from outside continued to seep in. The spectaculars continued, though no eyewitnesses could say for sure whether the space ships actually existed or whether this was merely good wishful thinking.", "1_5_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-eight in the morning</span>. I didn\u2019t have time to go home and shave\u2014which is fatal in television, where you must look smooth, confident and well-groomed, or the tape will eat you alive. So I went to the airport Holiday Inn and took a hot shower, then I put on my first suit and tie of the day, an all-white outfit and a black polo shirt with my custom Chi Chi Rodriguez trademark alligator on the front in gold thread, and I put on my nine-winged golden Nike sportsmedal and great new alligator boots I'd bought in Austin, and also my massive gold Rolex Datejust and all seventeen gold horseyand jewelry\u2014in honor of my new classy image. And I got a taxicab and gave the driver Eighteen South Wahsatch and asked him to step on it, because I had exactly eight minutes to get to a 7:00 A.M. live shot on KIMN.\nRoadblock...heavy, evil trouble, directly ahead. The radio was blasting country music \u201cWild One, Ride Wild, Ride.\u201d I cracked my window so I wouldn't have to breathe that lunatic gas. On the seat beside me were about 200 germ bombs I'd picked...", "1_18_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six seventeen in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the materializations, and about six hundred other people, having shared the materializations on Mars, Mercury, and Luna, were now sharing them here.\n$SURNAME0 and his wife, Kazoo, had been married for two years. They had met two years before that, when $SURNAME0 had materialized hischronously at the $SURNAME0 mansion one night and Kazoo had broken in to steal jewels.\nShortly before Kazoo had broken in, the mansion had come under siege, so that dinner had been thrown over the wall by catapult, and the staff had been too busy with defenses to risk disturbing the master in his music room.\nAll of this had been part of the war game called Brave Yankee, in which the materializations on Malacandra and Mars, as well as Earth and Newport, had figured.\nIn spite of all that had happened since, $SURNAME0 and Kazoo lived a curiously sexless marriage in a walled compounds They slept in a double hammock strung between two yew trees The yew trees were the only ones on $SURNAME0\u2019s property which were not scarred by animal claws and branches scraped by overloads\n$SURNAME0 played his vibrah...", "2_10_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and his wife were thirty-eight miles above the Earth in a satellite named Percival. Percival was the smallest and least unwieldy of a trio of satellites in synchronous orbit about the Earth. The other members of this unique little system were Maximura and Newport.\nPercival was locked to Maximura by means of a ladder. $SURNAME0 used this ladder with great dexterity. He ascended or descended it well over a hundred times a day.\nHe used the ladder well because he had a good right to be angry. Whenever he pulled himself up rung by rung, whenever he thrust his weight against the last embedment of the ladder in the rim of Maximura, whenever he had to press shut a calk bolt with a juice-box copper compresser, whenever he made the least movement in or about the ladder he was reminded distinctly that he didn\u2019t have enoughropes.", "2_0_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-nine a.m.</span> The one she was waiting for would not come. Ever.\nShe wondered if he would call again. There was no good number to call him at. The dental student stood, hopefully, waiting. He had asked her four times.\n\"Sorry,\" she said. \"Let\u2019s dance. I love this song.\"\n\"Love this song?\" said the dental student. \"What song?\" He stared at the loud speaker where something mariachi was going on about wives being slaves and lovers being steeds.\n\"Things,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF2, so as not to get personal, \"are looking up for the home team now.\"\n\"Look, I want to talk with you,\" the dental student said, tugging at her to halt her in the midst of the dance floor, where a dozen couples were doing the marchaz\u00f3n. \"I\u2019m serious. I think we should get together. I can make you truly happy. You should give me the chance. Forget about this bonehead you\u2019re waiting for.\"\n\"How about my bonehead?\" said another voice. It was her last chance.", "1_5_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-six in the morning</span>. A point of yellow light appeared far on the eastern rim of the planet. The light slowly grew, taking the shape of a huge semicircle. $SURNAME1, the gyropsychologist, looked at the volcano Hell on Mercury, and thought of a bent coronary blood-vessel filled with blood. The blood-vessel was his psyche. The coronary was a childishly shaped fear he had always had that someone would come sneaking up behind him and wham! hit him over the head with a ballpeen hammer, drive his soul right out of his body and onto the floor.\nNow the light of the sun on the rim of Mercury turned white. $SURNAME1\u2019s fears, reflected by the planet, boomed at him. $SURNAME1 clung to a stanchion.\nA flashlight wavered through the porthole. $FIRSTNAMEB0, stalked by painful yawns and muscular cramps, was making his way to the pilot room.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 cycled open the pressure door, came in. He smiled at the gory scene at the flight console. \"Well,\" he said, \"ready or not\u2014\"\nHis sentence died unfinished. He blinked at the apparition of $SURNAME1, who was taking his pulse at the far end of the cabin...", "0_4_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nI shoved the big car out into the stream of heavy traffic on the main highway and got it up to seventy. The headlights were as solid as searchlights probing the sides of the road, and pencil beams of headlights from oncoming vehicles slid deeply as cuts into the dark, illuminating the insides of swerving cars and trucks for a full second.\nWe shot past a brown and white roadside diner, WHEELCHAIR ONLY, THEN BATHROOM sign and swerved around a slow tanker before the road was straight and open again for a solid fifteen miles.\n\u201cDamnation!\u201d said the driver. \"How far is this place?\"\n\u201cTwo hours from here,\u201d I said. He didn\u2019t reply. We ran along silently, with the headlights cutting into the side of the road on our left, and now and then there were headlights coming at us. I slowed down to avoid a compact car that was trying to pass; I glanced into the car, an old Karmann Ghia, but didn\u2019t see any faces. Just the tense rack of lights. I sped up again. The Kammarn Ghia stayed behind. It made me nervous. I roared seventyfive to Eighty up...", "0_7_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven nineteen in the morning</span>. \"I\u2019m a fairly good lawyer,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I can probably get you fellows off with a fine.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 thought that the longer he kept Bortfart talking, the better his position would be. He had vaguely in mind a shot in the back of the head for Bortfart, and possibly a shot in the heart for the man behind Bortfart. But he wanted to get just the right idea firmly established in his head before he shot.\nIt was thrilling to think that he might see the Earth again.\nOld Handy and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were taking their sweet time climbing from the cellar, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 gambled on the belief that there were only two real soldiers in the basement. One was up in the living room with himself and $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014and one was down in the cellar with Handy and the recruits. In that belief, $FIRSTNAMEB0 blundered badly, for there were three real soldiers in the basement, preparatory to an ambush there.\nThe three soldiers had spread their jumper cables on the basement floor, and were waiting silently with their batons Drawn to give whoever came down the stairs a triple zzt, zzzt, zzt!\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 busily...", "0_15_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. A deep spaceboat lay in the darkness of the hangar, resting on asbestos rollers. The contour couch lay beside it, tall and sinister, impressive in the gloomy air under the ground. The time was ten minutes past three in the afternoon. In a minute a stout, jovial man would enter from a door in the near-by building. He would laugh heartily and speak in the West Virginia voice, with a trace of the hills of the old Purchase State, and the deep spaceboat would carry him away.\nA bank of lights in the near-by building came on, and the stout jovial man came in with a rustle of papers. He was in a hurry, as always. He was always hoping that there would be time for a dip in the White House pool, before the National Security Council meeting. He never had time.\nIt was the master of the Earth and the key figure of human history at a crucial epoch\u2014Adlai E. Stevenson.\nHis teeth came together with a snappish click, and he swore under his breath. The big door was edged with a rime of frost. It had been quainted by the cold outside.\nThis was the ancient refuge of Fortress Monroe. It...", "1_15_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three nineteen in the afternoon</span> at $FIRSTNAMEB2. It was six nineteen in the evening at $FIRSTNAMEM2, $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was asleep. He was sleeping in a huge bed with a carved oak headboard that belonged in a museum. His handicapped valet was sitting on a straight chair by a desk that belonged in a museum. The chair and desk were objects that $SURNAME1\u2019s father had bought from a demolished museum in Washington, D.C.\nThe valet was fifty years old, and his name was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. He was paralyzed from the shoulders down. This was his first and only job, aside from a few short-lived attempts at going to college. He took a glee club in Newport, Rhode Island, sang Unitarian church solos in Ukiah, California, and studied one semester of economics at Harvard. The paralyzedvalet-rowing squad at Harvard had offered to coach him good-naturedly, but he had to decline the offer because he was hoping to get a behind-the-scenes job at Fenway Park.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s dedication to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was as cold and rigid as the headboard of the bed he sat by. He believed that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was the...", "2_17_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the room was dark, and they were spoiling his life. A croquet match was going on in the garden by the lights of the pool.\n\"How awful,\" said Linda, \"that little ol' coloured guy sings so awful and that big star looks so awful.\"\n\"He\u2019s a fine actor,\" said Candide. \"A hell of an actor.\"\n\"Football\u2019s the only thing,\" said Linda, \"but then I\u2019m a Pennsylvania Dutch girl and really don\u2019t know nothing but football.\"\n\"And spearmint chewing gum,\" said Candide.\n\"And spearmint chewing gum,\" she agreed.\n\"When do we get something to drink?\" inquired Van Vleck.\nLinda rubbed her elbows. \"I have to have another shower. I feel all dirty.\"\n\"Don\u2019t shower in the dark,\" said Candide, \"or you\u2019ll step on one of these love pests\u2014love guppies\u2014whatever they are.\"\n\"Seriously, though,\" said Van Vleck. \"How come they put us in the dark?\"", "2_17_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was the bar of The $SURNAME0, a very fine piece of pseudo-colonial furniture in Newport.\nThe name of the bartender was Gabriellein. Her surname was Bannister. She was ten feet two inches tall, and she weighed eighty-four pounds. She was charmingly ugly, suicidally depressed, and alcoholic to the point of hallucinations. She was almost too drunk to see clearly or to misunderstand anything she saw.\nShe mistook John N. Conger, Jr., for her dead husband, who had been killed in the First World War.\nGabriellein despised $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 for his role in the war, and she delighted in censoring him by refusing to believe he could materialize and dematerialize. A $SURNAME0 proclamation that he would appear and disappear at five twenty-eight and five twenty-eight only, Gabriellein regarded as a particularly clever piece of propaganda. She had been fooled by the announcement into giving her best spritzer to a touring party from Earth. Fortunately, the glasses she handed out were bone-dry.", "2_18_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-three in the evening</span>\u2014twenty-four minutes before the next time-lock opens.\nIn Greenwich time, it was only ten minutes to twelve.\nCovertly, Salo tried to make sure that all the winos were there\u2014that none had been lost in the dust of the Martian attack or in the dust that $SURNAME0\u2019s machine had stirred up when it left. This was no easy matter. To be sure of his count, he had to peer into a wino\u2019s hooded face, which was something a responsible assistant on Earth would never do.\nThe count turned out to be all right. There were sixty-five winos, including Salo. This was exactly half of the number $SURNAME0 had planned for, so there was a greater-than-even chance that he hadn\u2019t overlooked anybody in the course of his recruiting on Earth.\nSalo joined his fellows in their pathetic horseshoe game as the hands of the huge clock in the tower reached precisely six thirty-four.", "0_0_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>. I knocked the ashes from my king-size $FIRSTNAMEM1 and tried to breathe it all in: the spirit of the raceway and the madness of the crowds and the cool, corrupt blood that could pour through your body once you mixed Alcohol, GASOLINE, AND DANGER. When you got high on that mixture, you found that all those things you'd read about when you were a kid in magazines\u2014Great Race Drive! Raw Exposure! Tough Men! Tough Machines! Right then, Right there\u2014were actually true. At the Indianapolis Speedway they weren't just talk on a newsprint page; they were part of the American Dream.\nWhat was I doing in this moiling confusion, this howling caustic roar? Why had I not bowed to the wishes of my family, wound up my own business, played the market with all its shady connotations, and enjoyed myself during my golden years? Why had I always given in to my crazies, to my greeds, to my hungers? I wasn't sure how to answer these questions. All I knew was that, once again, I'd given in to the roar.\nIt was time for one last blast. I smiled at my attorney, grabbed his arm for emphasis, then shouted...", "1_23_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-one in the evening</span>.\nTkac had placed the call from the home of $FIRSTNAMEF1, his blond-haired, much-petted \u201clittle sister.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF1, fourteen, slept snuggled in a tangle of covers on the living room sofa with the plug of her implant unit in her right temple Slim, dreamy-eyed, she hardly knew what was going on. He had stumbled in with one shoe on and one shoe off.\nFor a while he had stood weaving in front of the closed door, whispering to her in his wonderful secret voice, as though any minute somebody might burst in and snatch the phone away.\nDetails would have to be worked out later, he\u2019d told Mucho.\nMucho sat grinning under the house on his white mark in the black dust. I\u2019m in me mamma\u2019s bathtub teeth-chatterin with the DT\u2019s and this guy Tkac called. He said for us to keep cool for two days, then we should meet him at the Free Spark Plug in Tilden Park tomorrow night around midnight, bring whatever cash we can lay our hands on. Then he hung up, O buddy.\n\u201cWrong number,\u201d Mucho said...", "2_21_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-six in the evening</span>.\n/f/\n\u201cYes she\u2019s here. She said she didn\u2019t have a lesson today, but she\u2019s here, I put her in room 407, she must be expecting you.\u201d\n\u201cFenster?\u201d\n\u201cFenster, yes, I need to\u2014\u201d\n\u201cThanks.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cLenore.\u201d\n\u201cWalter.\u201d\n\u201cHow are you.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m hurting a bit. But that\u2019s all right.\u201d\n\u201cWhere are you hurt? May I see.\u201d\n\u201cI think it\u2019s my ... thigh, where I fell. It just didn\u2019t recover, I could tell in the plane, I\u2014can you see it, is it too bad? My leg isn\u2019t showing? Is the gown too ... \u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cNo I guess it\u2019s not. My leg isn\u2019t hurting as much.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d", "0_17_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> when George was locked in the telephone booth making his plans for escape from the beautiful prison that was the nation of San Narciso. At five thirty-six he walked out of a shopping-cart shed in the town of San Narciso, and into a taxicab. If he kept his cool, and if everything went exactly as planned, he\u2019d be at the home of his children by nine o\u2019clock that night. This was still the same day in which he had imprisoned himself.\nIt was all the work of Illuminatus Jack Poinsett. Mr. Ramon, the taxicab driver, was one of his operatives, in a foul bad temper because of the short notice. From out of the telephone booth George watched Mr. Ramon stash tire chains on the back wheel of his cab and then whine loudly about cracked fuel tubes before he\u2019d consent to do anything.\nWhen at last he was on his way, George experienced a moment of excitement at being out in the bad air of San Narciso again. After thirteen years, it smelled just the same to him\u2014the breath of the planet, ill and stale and terribly thin.\nTry to describe San Narciso to the melancholy kid sitting alone in the starry...", "1_22_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-nine in the evening</span>. I walked into my office, sat down and tried to figure why I\u2019d been sweating so much. I couldn\u2019t figure it. Finally I took off all my clothes and put them in the laundry hamper. I put on my robe and chain\u2014smoking French cigarettes and watching the evening parade out the window. Lights on the stork\u2014colored convertible went out. Somebody leaned against the convertible, shoving it thirty feet back down the line, then finally walked off under a neon SPEEDTRAP 45 sign. Lovely evening. I felt so alone I could have cried. But I didn\u2019t cry. I smoked up the whole pack of French cigarettes, watching the parade in modest luxury, and then had a few of my all\u2014time favorite bananas, and finally it was eight o\u2019clock and time to leave for Lynn\u2019s house. Now to relax and enjoy the evening with dear old friends. I put on my houndstooth check madras Dick Tracy hat, locked up the office, got in the Land Rover and drove to Lynette\u2019s place in the hills . . . .\nBut of course, friend, you understand that there is absolutely nothing to worry about, because, after all, as we...", "0_3_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-nine in the morning</span>. I had taken the following items with me: my pyjama bottoms, my white silk scarf, my Swiss Army knife, a box of matches, the magnifying glass, my passport for both names, the cassette recorder, a toothbrush, and the five tabs of blotter hit. Not having to go through any customs, I had left my luggage behind in Las Vegas\u2014both my bags and my Wartburg. The price of freedom is low transportation cost....\nI drove straight to the CircusCircus and asked for a hotel room. I had never been there before and it was not listed in the AAA directory, but the desk man gave me a key anyway, And no questions. I went up to 1253 and stretched out on the bed.\nThere was no way to fucked up the room. It was already completely fucked up. All I could do was lie there and practice slumber and prayer. For two hours I was totally passive, and I had a series of dreams that were unrelated stimuli, but they were all quite agree able. So agreeable, in fact, that I was quick to wake up whenever my unconscious tried to express itself through the song of the English bulldog.\nThe unconscious must be taught to express itself with snarling...", "1_7_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1996, when Lenore Beadsman, holding with one hand the Frequent and Vigorous red homeUnion symbol and with the other several tiny objects of cloth and plastic, made her way along the Vem Redskins Parkway through a fine lightly misty rain toward the business district of the city of Cleveland, Ohio. Water flowed down the Parking Lot Gate\u2019s smooth black plastic. Cleveland seemed to be all liquid.\nA\n/a/\n11 \u201cA Rude Guest Is Like a Turd in the Host\u2019s Bathtub\u201d\n/a/\n... coming up the tiled steps. She closed her eyes, and saw the very darkness of the theater, smelled the stale and faintly rotten popcorn, the thick grease growing on the underside of the seats ...\n(help me somebody help me it\u2019s him him behind he\u2019s come for me help me help)\n... and up came the heat. The weak December heat of Texas. The harsh Texas sun. Pasting from the heat. Of a Thursday.\n\u201cFrequent and Vigorous.\u201d\n\u201cLenore?\u201d\n\u201c \u2018F, V.\u2019", "0_6_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty in the morning</span>. The atmosphere was blue and white with cold. The dawn was misty. The sun was not yet up, but the promised snow was falling, so fine, so dry, so saturating the air, that I did not freeze and that it did not melt as it fell, but became part of the element, the trees, the ground, and especially the mountain. Everything was closed tight with tremendous energy, for the snow fell and rose, gathering in level banks at the sides of the road. In my misery, with my nearly frozen toes, I imagined each hill was an enormous feather bed and that I climbed from one to the next, from great white pillow to great white pillow, looking for a place to sleep. The road went up and down, jiggling all the time, the bus groaning, the driver singing. All I saw of Spain were groves of trees sleeping inthe dense, wet snow. It should have been a marvelous trip.\nSuddenly I realized that the sun was coming up and the day was beginning. The road was in the middle of a great forest of trees. A wood wagon was going by, drawn by two horses. The horses plodded. Their heads were down and their backs steamed. We were way above...", "2_7_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven eighteen in the morning</span>. The homeopape carried a story, taken from the New York Sunday News, to the effect that Queen Aphrodite II of Titan had been blown up the night before by a package of powerful plastic explosives secreted in a vase of roses. Of the five hundred guests at her state ball, only fourteen had escaped death or serious injury.\nThe homeopape carried a picture of the Queen. The picture was not very good, since it had been taken by a telescopically concealed camera during the Queen\u2019s last walk on Earth.\nA nineteen-year-old citizen of Titan named Norman Knight was under arrest.\nA homeopape editor was said to have taken an erroneous swipe at the story. He had been quoted by the Associated Press as saying, \"This is too crazy to be believed. Whoever heard of a Queen without a King taking over a whole Solar System?\"\nThe time was seven nineteen in the morning. The homeopape carried a sensation-story under a banner headline: \"COL. \u2018BUZZ\u2019 MCCANRUPT POWDERED BUCKEYE HERO FINDS A MYSTERIOUS HOME IN THE SKY!\u201d", "1_9_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine sixteen in the morning</span>. The broad lawns and the ranks of trees were bathed in white sunlight. The grass glittered. The lawns looked wet. White plates of light from the high, white sun were broken in the wind. The trees blew and bent in the wind. The dark green of the rainy Versailles trees was very dark in the sunlight. In the midst of the lawns was the white building, the uncompleted church, luminous, and the tall golden Virgin standing against the sky in the wind. A car went up the wet Rue Washington and turned into the square. It was a taxi. Monsieur Charles stepped out of it, straightened his nap over his coat, kissed his hand to the chauffeur, and walked out upon the bronze slope of the fountain, among the dipping spray and the rising water, to look for Madame $FIRSTNAMEB5.\nAround the fountain the water stood in pools. Across its face were the humps and bumps of the stone waves. The water fell in long clear drops. Beside the fountain was a pool of white water. All around upon the ground were spots of water. The drops and the rainbows made the sunlight white as it fell upon wet stones.\nThe taxi driver opened the door of the shiny black car...", "0_14_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-one in the afternoon</span> of December 20, 1997, and the scene was the great white vastness of the War Room of ChronoCorp, a company that did little other than buy companies, chop them up, market the parts, pay dividends, and lay the foundation for a global culture that would have seemed incredible to anyone with the benefit of perspective.\nA stone\u2019s throw from the dark towers of Detroit, the War Room was a minimalist kind of place, with no ornamentation at all except ChronoCorporation stock tickers, battery-powered digital clocks, and framed representations of Church of the Second Advent catechisms, programs detailing the designs of hideous corporate soft-drinks, time-saving medical techniques, high-fructose fruit spreads made of Nebraska corn.\nThe passive, globe-shaped Client who had sought refuge in $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s cubicle was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the crippled, pathetically ugly old man who was chairman of the board of ChronoCorp.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s short-lived affair with $SURNAME0 had begun on that very spot\u2014on the sanctum sanctorum of her desk\u2014and it had ended in disaster. The affair had begun at three a.m., when $SURNAME0, who had been...", "0_6_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span> before I got $FIRSTNAMEB3 calmed down and wondering where he'll be and what take he'll have on life from now on -- and nobody can say- -nobody. This kid, it seems, has nothing to hold onto or look forward to or back on in his entire life, he's been dead, he's been in hell, he's been maniacal, he's been timidly hid away and locked up, he's been ''on the road'' night and day, he's been mad and he's been bad and there's nothing, absolutely nothing, he hasn't seen, and seen exhaustively and with wonder and with a comparable amount of sweat and sometimes joy, confusion and terror, doubt and worry. He asked me if I thought he should settle down. Of course. Where? How? With what? Who? In what form, shape, schedule, routine, hierarchy, order? How much time? How much money? Where? How? What? When? Who? Whom? I couldn't answer any of his questions and told him so in riddles and final oh-how-clear truths. The only, last, final, ultimate, and first problem anybody has in the world, ever, is what to do about Jesus. That is the...", "1_10_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-one in the morning</span> when we finally ran into Denver. $FIRSTNAMEB3 had called Fontana\u2019s Farm once from the bay edge of Salt Lake; he said Marie was frantic and expected him back. The first thing he did in Denver was to call Fontana\u2019s from a dance-hall phone and ask $FIRSTNAMEF4 to tell Marie he was all right and not to worry. $FIRSTNAMEF4 talked to her and verified that it was $FIRSTNAMEB3. Then we waited till midnight in a horrible little hotel, the Wazee, so as not to surprise everybody at Fontana\u2019s at lunchtime, and at midnight we took off in the Buick to Fontana\u2019s by backroads. $FIRSTNAMEB3 showed me all the ropes. \u201cBabe, she\u2019s going up, she\u2019s going down, she\u2019s turning in to the driveway!\u201d And indeed we were passing Marie, who was sitting in the bay window of her house on the backroad, all lit up and hopefully looking out for us. Although it was midnight, $FIRSTNAMEB3 nervously parked the car and tiptoed to the house with his belongings in a rope bag. He tiptoed past sleeping Rita and the children and crept into the bedroom with all his quiet manly...", "1_13_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-five in the afternoon</span> when we finally got in to Denver. I remembered that this was the hour of the count in Jack Johnson's day -- the hour when he used to lean on the rail of his corner while they counted his purse. Denver was his town, so they said. $FIRSTNAMEB3 pulled up on the curb at an immense hot-dog counter that signified the West to us. The entire top of this building was a scoreboard. We saw a cowboy race his pony, a rodeo event, while we munched our hot dogs in the shadow of the grandstand. Suddenly right before our eyes the cowboy fell off the pony and rolled in the dust with his crushed wrist. Everything froze. Like Truman at the Potsdam Conference, I had sense enough to look at my watch. We noted every detail of the frozen tableau before us; and as we gazed, time started again and the cowboy gathered himself up the way anyone might after he'd lost his grip on the pony's reins and rolled in the dust. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I suddenly realized that, though we had thousands of dollars between us and no cares, we were nonetheless in the middle of the serious Western world. Everything we ever heard about cowboys was suddenly confirmed in our eyes: they were lonesome and gloomy, poor,...", "1_17_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-three in the afternoon</span>.\nCandy Mandible was clacking away at room 43\u2019s card-crowded console. Her eyes were puffy, her nose was askew, her hair looked like a brown mop drenched in oil. She had slept in the lab, again.\nReduced to near subliminality by pain, seepage, and the density of apparatus confronting her in room 43, Miss Mandible was now taking messages.\nShe was perceiving the information in the messages with no more than the faintest fraction of her mind.\nShe was having a hard time of it.\nShe was having a hard time of it because she was having a hard time distinguishing her mind from the messages coming in. She kept imagining that flashes of information were coming to her, not out of the vast Louisville Network of Telecommunications that surrounded her, but out of her own psyche.\nThis was not the case. According to her electronic Personal Computer Index, she had begun her work day at 0644 hours, taking a call from God, the Lord Almighty, in the person of Reverend Leon of St. Vincent de Paul\u2019s Widows\u2019 and Orphans\u2019 Mission\u2014a call concerning the disposition of funds in the amount of $...", "0_10_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-four in the morning</span>. I had taken exactly half an hour to pack up, decide upon a course of action, and start out. Since this took me thirty-three minutes less than it normally would have, I surged ahead of my normal pace and found myself far out in the middle of the Valley at eleven-fifteen and Venice Boulevard. Here I made my first reckless decision of the day and turned right, heading downtown in a general way. I felt great. I was free. The mad midnight rush through the rain of the preceding day was only a refresher course to get me ready for this headlong flight. I felt fine. I was ready to make Las Vegas in time for lunch\u2014hell, I'd make Miami before dark! My problem? No problem. I was free and easy. I felt fine and I was only a bit dizzy from the deep-breathing I had done while packing. Ah, doctor, you may lecture me as long as you like about the fearful potentialities of amphetamine and its relation to the terrible disease of addiction, but this much remains true: A man's own worst enemy can never be another man\u2014it can only be some diabolical form of drug. I had just put this simple and dignified fact to the test...", "0_10_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-eight in the morning</span>, January 1, 2000, and $FIRSTNAMEB2, Salo, and $SURNAME0 were lying facedown in the grass three-tenths of a mile outside the space ship that was standing on the Fort Riley parade ground. The space ship was the same one that had carried the Martian messenger to Earth, the same one that $FIRSTNAMEB2, Salo, and $SURNAME0 had repaired and revived, the same one $SURNAME0 had christened with a bottle of Coca-Cola. It was brown, windowless, shaped like a spinthariscope.\nSalo was sprawling with orange limbs on the grass.\n$SURNAME0 lay in a relaxed heap, his back to $FIRSTNAMEB2. His florid face was sanguine.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 lay on his stomach. His fists were clenched.\n\"Tired?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"I just haven\u2019t anything to say.\"\n\"Isn\u2019t this thrilling?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"I just don\u2019t happen to think so.\"\n\"This confrontation with the present and future of American civilization,\" said $SURNAME0, \"this glorious culmination of the progress of Western...", "1_5_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-one in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time. The place was a spot on a rising hillside east of the Red River, somewhere in the vicinity of the nothing little burg of Pasul, Oklahoma. We were by ourselves. The three local witnessesthey were grazing dairy cows, who just that morning had been legally seized from their rightful ownerhad all run off as soon as they saw us hobbling out of the helicopter. Strictly speaking, our chopper had not actually landed, but had hovered and set down just long enough for us to get off. Helicopters are extremely unpopular around Racine, Wisconsin, these days. Our pilot, who had the bright idea to come the back way via Pasul, was named Ripley, and whenever our troubles get so heavy we start thinking about heading back, we simply begin chanting, We\u2019re with Ripley, to each other, in a quiet numeric and rhyming game\u201422, 33, 44, 55, etc., meaning all the way back to Racine and the fastnesses of the Sub-sub-sub office on Pearl Street. We were spread out across a gloomy pasture, with our canvas satchel full of forms and a copy of the eminent Wetware Leahy for reference purposes. The form we were...", "2_22_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog, Salo, waited for the manifestation of the image again.\n\"Thank you,\" said $SURNAME0 to $FIRSTNAMEF0, after she had written her message on his note pad. He kissed her hand. \"What else would you like to know?\" he said. \"It\u2019s perfectly obvious who $FIRSTNAMEF0 is. I\u2019m sure you\u2019ve guessed her name by now, but probably you\u2019d like me to tell you for sure.\"\nA small thingamajig in $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s uniform pocket began to buzz and blink green. She removed it, examined it, and sighed. \"Well,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"I always hate to call my dog\u2014\" She looked at Salo. \"Would you excuse us a minute?\" she said to $SURNAME0 gallantly. \"I\u2019d like to take my dog around the block.\"\nThe time was twenty minutes after ten o\u2019clock in the evening.", "2_1_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-two in the morning</span>. Ah, the long, slow, flat, firm, soft, complacent, undulating, unaggressive, undisturbed, penetrating rhythm of erotic love suffusing the little bedroom of a child\u2019s house.\nBetty Pope came to the door. She held her finger to her lips, smiling. She tiptoed in, holding a breakfast tray, stroking the air before her with a whisk broom\u2014just as she had been taught to do, years before, at the Welton Surrey School for Girls.\n\"Hello,\" she whispered.\n$SURNAME0 hissed, like an angry cat. \"Damn you all!\" he suddenly roared. \"Damn you all to hell!\"\nThe harmonious, tranquil, timeless rhythm was broken.\n$SURNAME0 hitched up his tuxedo, leaped for a twenty-foot chaise longue, caught the far end, flipped it over, plunged under it, was gone.\nThe impact of $SURNAME0\u2019s airborne body, as it struck and smashed to pieces the clerestory window, was so great that it almost lifted the barn off its foundation.", "0_7_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe last of the police machine, which had been traveling all night, going north, were straggling over the southeastern horizon.\nNo more police machines were coming.\nThe first zero-dark-hundred wave of Tralfamadorian troops were not yet on their way.\nThe second and much heavier wave, bearing the Emperor himself and the last, most important \u00e9migr\u00e9, would not be visible on the southeastern horizon for another fourteen minutes.\nAll that separated the first wave from the second wave was a verbal message from TRF-44, the only machine to get through to an assemblage point on the Earth.\nThe message traveled by ultra-short wave to an Earthling assembled by sensors on a plateau in southeastern Sweden.\nThe name of the Earthling was $FIRSTNAMEF1. He was the last of his family. All of his relatives had been vaporized.\nHis home town on the Earth had been vaporized, too.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was carrying a huge load of firewood, which he had found in the Swedish forest in which he had been hiding since the attack.\nHe was walking to the burned-out ruins of Malm\u00f6, twenty miles away. There were still some abandoned buildings standing in Malm\u00f6...", "2_1_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 held an ewer of ice water. He had just finished intubating himself\u2014performing, through his nostrils, a tracheotomy that would enable him to speak without being suffocated by the fluid that had begun to fill his bronchial tubes and lungs and the upper reaches of his larynx.\nFor some reason, he had decided to speak.\nHe was still aware, still in charge\u2014still able to focus his extraordinary mind on controlling the far more massive mind of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\n\"Who?\" he said.\n\"Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"You\u2019re confused,\" said $SURNAME0. \"That\u2019s not my name. My name used to be Newport, but now it\u2019s $SURNAME0. You mustn\u2019t confuse me with him.\"\n\"Yes\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"Yes, I understand.\" She spelled it out for herself in her mind, over and over. \"R-u-m-f-o-r-d.\" Now that her visitor had given the name a cadence, she was able to remember it, to repeat it to others.", "2_9_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>. In four minutes, the sun would rise, and the first day of the rest of their lives would be one third over.\nMarcia, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, and their son, $FIRSTNAMEB2, occupy a detached Romanesque town house in Newport, Rhode Island\u2014a stately home with picture windows looking out, not over Newport Harbor, but right up Saint James Street.\nFrom the $SURNAME0 front steps all one can see is, literally, a picture of happiness. Down Saint James Street, past the ancient elms, limousines glide by on the way to the yacht basin at the northern tip of the town. Sun-tanned gentlemen officers in whites get out of the limousines, followed by bronze-skinned, starlet-like girls in skin-diving tights. The gentlemen have their spatulas, the girls their tight little fists. They all waft genially and haughtily toward their explosive, satisfying destinies aboard the yachts.\nOne could hardly hope for, or design in, a view more calculated to make a man, or a woman, or a child feel extra-good.", "0_21_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-one in the evening</span>. Our deadline loomed close. I was afraid. The scuttering desert bats were ugly creatures indeed. When we got back to the rented car I saw the dome light was broken\u2014broken the night before, presumably, when the Fine Arts priest had barged out of the airport gas station with his tire iron...and I remembered his words: \u201cI'll bash that fucker right through the fucking roof!\u201d He'd been talking about our car, but right now it applied to him as well. I watched him for a power sign. But he just sat there, leaning back in the seat and staring at the sky. He was singing to himself under his breath a monotonous funeral dirge of some kind. I couldn't catch the words. It sounded like this:\n\u201cOorga dwanga piffita pum pum...\u201d\n\u201cAre you a priest?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cNot as such,\u201d he said. \u201cHoly Father, yes. I suppose so. I have that on my driver's license, but otherwise no. I'm a Conservator.\"\n\u201cWhat's a Conservator?\u201d My attorney had the map spread out. \u201cHere it is...", "1_6_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty in the morning</span>, and as we drove into Indianapolis, I suddenly recalled that in this town there might be members of a gang called the Cannonballs, who lived in a big roadhouse with Murder Inc. in 1947. This gang specialized in tying the victim to a chair in back of the garage and then hooking him to hot wires clipped to the bumper and full battery of a '47 Chrysler and dragging him a hundred yards down gravel alleys and throwing him into concrete walls for a kind of sick kicks, and I thought god, I hope this car's not heard in this town; I suddenly saw it cruising Cannonball style through a poor Indiana community and running off a child bewilderedly playing in the road. The old sabotage in me rose. \u201cI gotta gas up soon, man, lotsa gas.\u201d\n\u201cAwright.\u201d\nIt was easy; I knew the signs -- bad cop afraid to jump me and take chances, waiting for some silly passing offense. So I stretched it ou; dawdled over the fruit stands, moved slow at the red lights, made sure I had all kinds of time to do things, and in between I ran out of the extra juice I needed to keep the trip moving. My hands shook.", "0_5_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-four in the morning</span>. The drugstore was full of night owls chattering about the news from Dallas. The party of five sat silent and mournful. \u201cYou wait,\u201d said the man who had come in from the desert. \u201cIn a week Oswald will be a saint.\u201d\nI promised him he wouldn't need to worry. Nobody would ever kill President Nixon. He'd commit suicide first. It was pure silliness, he told me: \u201cHasn't that man proven he's crazy enough to do anything?\u201d He talked on and on. His face was hushed and grim. The eyes. These weary eyes in the worn face of a very middle -aged man had seen much and understood something. I was beginning to like him. He was not entertaining; he was not a bore; but there was a terrible, sad poignancy about him, and I guessed it was his fear of death. I wanted to cheer him up. So I asked him if he wanted to buy a watermelon that I didn't need.\nHe jumped at the idea. It was just what he wanted. I fetched the melon from my Deno and we rolled it out into the street. \u201cYou want to make a little...", "0_1_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-six in the morning</span> on our seventh night in Las Vegas. Here is what had happened: At the Americana, we had found a room on the mezzanine overlooking the pool. It was $10.00 a day cheaper than the room we\u2019d been assigned on the thirtysecond floor of the Chateau....but not worth it. Our room at the Americana seemed to be about ten yards from the fellatio booths. Fat Vegas character with hat, across from the Explorer\u2019s Club. At night, when trade slackened at the booths, we could hear the Pool Boys yelling, \u201cOK, suckers\u2014who\u2019s next for the blow job?\u201d And then the rush of automatic doors in the connecting doorways, and then a long silence and then, sign here and put your dick here and on your knees or some farfetched thing like that. Listening to this was enough to drive a man out of his head.\n\u201cJesus,\u201d said Bruce. \u201cIt sounds like they have a convention going in there.\u201d He looked up at the Explorer\u2019s Club. \u201cThe same fellas are probably in there right now: Rotary, the Shriners, one of those\u2014who...", "1_3_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three seventeen in the morning</span>. I sat bolt upright on my bed in the Flamingo and stared across the room at $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 asleep on the black couch. They were like high, white islands in a sea of darkness. I arose to take a leak and stepped carefully over the floor fans, not to wake them -- a whole island for myself. I went out to the parking lot for a final pee and surveyed the sleeping town. The lights of a tenstory Cadillac sign up the street seemed to go up forever and ever, bathing the tops of downtown skyscrapers in cold mercury.\nIII\nThe next night we got up at two o'clock in the afternoon, as agreed, and met in front of the Beverly Hills Hotel. \u201cLord, I'm stiff,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cGoing to have to work both hips today for the first time in months.\u201d And with that he went over the parking-lot fence, then leaped and twisted and ducked to gain momentum, then shot up ten feet in the air over the fence on the other side and landed flat-footed, no slow-down possible. We were off on another crazy day, not definite as to where, except we were whirring on the run again, bl...", "0_0_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-three a.m.</span> On the dot. The deadline. And in the shadowy dark she heard somebody speak.\nIt was Redwine. And it seemed at that moment that Redwine had been there all along.\nThat he\u2019d arrived at the instant the chronos could no longer be shifted, could no longer be outsmarted. At the instant the shoe was finally on the Other Foot, at the instant that human time and Martian time, spitefully in love and lust, began to let their babies chase each other over violet sands, in fields of flames, at an ever-diminishing rate of speed.\nShe heard Redwine\u2019s voice, in the deep of the night. She heard him from the direction of the spiral staircase. The spoke that was his voice was deep, so deep as to be almost painful to a straight-thinking person. But Lenore wasn\u2019t straight-thinking. And so when she looked to the source of the pre-echo of the voice, and saw a monstrous shadow on the lobby wall, she felt right at home.\nLenore looked at the shadow and listened to the voice.\n\"Hello, Lenore,\" the voice said.\n/a/\n1 September\n9 September", "0_1_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-six in the morning</span>. One moment I was strolling along past the Mint, thinking about Camille and the baby; then suddenly like a ratchet wrench the thing struck me in the forehead. In a jolting second of time in which one thought covered another and groped for the surface, I realized I had lost Camille, what I had lost her for, and what it was I must start looking for.\nI began to run toward the river. The city was exploding with thousands of carloads of people about to arrive for the Rose Bowl game. The end of the year was precisely two months old, and it had been a time of many changes, which I had not thought out. Now, on this day when nothing changed in San Francisco, the year cracked right overhead in the sky and began to shriek around me in a whirlwind. My girl had left me; I was alone. And I made up my mind on the next strange thing that came along. I suddenly realized what it was I had better go find in Frisco that night while the town was hot to the tips of its geony meters and before the cars began sliding back downhill to the other end of U.S. 101\nI found a phone booth on Market Street and rang up my attorney.", "1_5_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-three in the morning</span> when Remi Boncoeur rang the bell at 47 Rue des Saints-P\u00e8res. At the door were two women\u2014one of medium height, with a perfect oval face, a gem of a nose and huge eyes. She held in one hand a saucepan filled with cigarette butts, and in the other a poker. In front of her was a tiny 18-year-old girl, dressed in men\u2019s pajamas and smoking a cigarette.\n\"Oui?\" asked the older woman.\n\"Is this the home of a Monsieur $FIRSTNAMEB2, madame?\" inquired Remi.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB2?\" she repeated. \"Oui, c\u2019est ici. Entrez donc.\"\nRemi shook hands with her. \"Merci, madame.\"\nThe little girl slouched off upstairs, puffing smoke. Remi was shown into a close-packed livingroom, where a great deal of activity was going on. A large woman was having a nervous breakdown, three men were carrying down suitcases, and two others stood by a stack of luggage.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB2?\" inquired Remi.\n\"Yes, yes,\" one of the men said. He rubbed his eyes. \"...", "2_9_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. The time was Friday, August 25, 1951. The place was New York City\u2014Central Park, to be exact. The grass in Central Park was as green as the greenest grass in God\u2019s own neck of the woods. Grass was all around. Grass was good.\nSome day, maybe, an angel would pay the proper respect to all the grass the world over. But that angel wasn\u2019t saying anything just now. Men were saying things. Two thousand men, give or take a few, were fighting in the center of the ring of Central Park. They were pounding the daylights out of each other.\nThe crowd that watched them watched them with the profound respect and stolidity of men watching horse racing. There were betting booths on all sides of the fighting ring. There were cigarette girls, rows of them, factories full of them, all around the crowd.\nThere were hot-dog sellers and peanut sellers and icecream sellers and guys who sold cigars and popcorn and soda water and programs, and cops, four hundred of them or more, giving orders and carrying them out, on Central Park, on July 29, 1951.\n\"What \u2019s going on?\" said a New Yorker to another New Yorker.", "2_4_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0 sadly. \"You are, $FIRSTNAMEB4. You surely are.\"\n\"That little blue naked baby downstairs\u2014\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. \"That $FIRSTNAMEF0 you\u2019re planning to lay on the altar?\" She laughed. \"She wouldn\u2019t care if it all fell down.\"\n\"No, she wouldn\u2019t,\" said $SURNAME0. He looked serene. His muddy eyes were loving Mrs. $SURNAME0. \"No more than you or I would,\" he said.\n\"Underneath it all, we\u2019re just shy animals who\u2019re scared to death of people,\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0.\nShe gave a raw, quivering, rusty cackle. \"Underneath it all,\" she said, \"we\u2019re the same.\"\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Whatever it is we\u2019re thinking we\u2019re better than, under all that thinking,\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0, \"we\u2019re the same.\"\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0.", "1_14_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty in the afternoon</span>. I had seven hours to kill before I could meet $FIRSTNAMEB3. First I got out of my marine disguise; it was way too hot for that. It stank, too. I walked around O'Farrell Street in my long underwear. I kicked up my heels; I strode; I took preliminary trumpet lessons in front of a drugstore. Then I rushed over to the Automat and stood at the counter and ate a huge meal of mashed potatoes, roast beef, pie, coffee, cheesecake -- everything I could possibly cram in. In five minutes I was groaning. I wanted to go back to the other counter and start all over again. While I was eating, a tall well-built girl with bobbed hair leaned over the counter and gandered me with big, starry eyes. She had a little gay mouth and a turned-up nose. She said to me, \u201cYou looked so hungry I couldn't help watching.\u201d\n\u201cThat's right. I am starving.\u201d\nWhat would she say next? She said, \u201cYou are an unusual-looking boy.\u201d That was flat. I made a disconcerted attempt to smile. She was serious. Then she laughed, instantly, as...", "0_20_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight seventeen in the evening</span>. Stan Malachai $SURNAME1, the only heir to the fortune of Malachai $SURNAME1, stood concealed in an ebony statue that was part of a fountain in the lobby of the magnificently understated, quietly expensive, just-completed Class A dwelling on the Atlantic shore of Florida, in which his mother and father were this night taking possession.\nConcealed in the ebony statue, how did young Stan Malachai $SURNAME1 differ in appearance from the white statue of a naked kid stretching and struggling with a skipping rope which really was part of the fountain?\nHe had brown hair, where the fountain-figure had none. His eyes were brown, too. So was his nose, where the fountain-nose was perfect, blank white and unblemished. So was his skin and so were his teeth.\nYoung $SURNAME1 was so perfect that he was scarred imperfectly. He had a puckered white scar on his right hand where he had once burned it severely pinching a white-hot electric stove burner. He wondered sometimes whether he was, perhaps, color-blind. He did not know that his body, perfectly as it was, contained a single flaw. The flaw was thirty-six feet underground, in a vault where nine...", "0_0_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve eighteen ante meridiem</span> of the following day. The group around the signpainter had now grown alarmingly. This one, that one, and another swirled to the fore and back, like dead leaves in a dry whirlwind.\nOf the three, it seemed that a certain moist-lunged individual was the chief spokesman.\n\"We have as much right here as anybody!\" he bellowed hoarsely. \"This is our planet, after all.\"\n\"The planet belongs to the Tralfamadorians,\" said the signpainter quietly.\n\"Well\u2014just because somebody let you play house here doesn\u2019t mean you have to run things,\" said the spokesman for the group.\n\"Who says so?\" said the signpainter.\n\"You do,\" said the spokesman. \"According to what you wrote on that sign: LAST ONE THERE IS A TRUSTEE.\"\n\"Then go ahead,\" said the signpainter, \"go ahead and be trustees.\"\n\"I should hope we would,\" said the spokesman. \"After all, somebody\u2019s got to be. The idea, I take it, is to make things safe for creatures who can think properly, isn\u2019t it?\u2014which means creatures like you and me...", "1_23_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-six in the evening</span>. I had to get forty-six dollars in exactly twelve minutes or go to jail for vagrancy. I thought it over as I ran around the corner to the Drake Hotel and went down the basement stairs to the men\u2019s bar\u2014and whirled absolutely without pause directly into a punch that was intended for somebody else. The bartender had just thrown a beer at a sailor --and I ran right into the foaming spray. \u201cWhat the hell?\u201d I said, staggering back and wiping beer from my face.\n\u201cYou son of a bitch!\u201d said the bartender with bleeding knuckles.\n\u201cI was only tryin to get a drink,\u201d I told him. \u201cHere! Take my money!\u201d And I hurled a half-dollar on the bar\u2014at which point he drew back his arm to flail me again..\n\u201cStop it!\u201d said a calm voice. \u201cStop it, Henry.\"\nThe bartender paused, waiting for me to hit him again.\n\u201cWell, go ahead!\u201d he said. \u201cFinish what you started!\u201d I picked up my beer. \u201cNo,\u201d I said to the man beside me.", "1_15_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of a gloomy day in early October. $SURNAME0 had paced the column of time punctually\u2014twenty-five minutes for the descent of the spiral ramp, twenty-five minutes for the spiral ascent, with five minutes at the apex for the little service of reconciliation before the one-minute gong.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog appeared at their places at the apex of the spiral.\n\"Skip!\" said $SURNAME0.\nCome\u2014c-c-come\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"I love him\u2014k-k-k -h\u2014\"\n$SURNAME0\u2019s dog beamed at his master\u2019s wrist watch, eexpecting the next signal to come.\n$SURNAME0 did not press his watch\u2019s button. He was gazing, unbelieving, at the flaming walls of lime rock on either side.\nThe walls were sweating great beads of moisture. And the moisture was steaming.\n\"Good God\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nHis dog looked up at him questioningly.\n\"Good God,\" said $SURNAME0 again.\nHis composure had left him.\n\"Poo-wee!\" said his dog warily.\nAs well say good-bye.\nThe...", "0_7_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-eight in the morning</span> of October twenty-first. The house and what happened in it, though, had a much larger significance than these facts might suggest. It was a place that had grown up around the meeting of many roads in a country not often honored by the presence of greatness; and the house symbolized, on that particular morning in October, the tremendous complexity of the social, political, and economic maneuverings of a nation in the very midst of its first, gawky efforts to step among the big boys of history\u2014effort that would, if successful, mean that an obscure, devious and mystified land originally settled by the Scotch-Irish and the slaves would become the most powerful and richest nation on Earth.\nThe time was seven forty-eight.\nThe place was Newtown, Connecticut.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California, woke up on his eighteenth birthday to the sound of his bedroom clock, which was shaped like a Civil War soldier and which followed the custom of Civil War soldier clocks everywhere by popping its plastic head off every hour on the hour, dinging, and then hopping around the floor bumping into things, until you either wound it up again or chased it down and retopped its head on tight. The alarm feature was that the headless soldier...", "0_12_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was the second day of August, 1955.\nTo be an enlightenednik one had to know that one was an enlightenednik, and one had to be able to prove it to other enlightenedniks.\nTo help an enlightenednik prove it required another enlightenednik, and so on.\nMetaphysically, the thing was very simple.\nIf an enlightenednik doubted himself, he proved at once that he was not enlightened. If he questioned his own enlightenment, he proved that his thoughts were not his own.\nAnd a man whose thoughts were not his own was not an enlightenednik.\nIt was all very neatly packaged\u2014the whole thing.\nTo be a missionary of the gospel of Tesla was to be either very brave or a fool. And Salo had never felt much like either one.\nAt one o'clock in the afternoon, Salo sat on the summit of Newport Tower. Newport Tower was three hundred and thirty-three feet high. Salo's feet dangled over the downtown side.\nThe wind on the summit of Newport Tower was seldom calm.\nTwo rivers met at the foot of the tower. Salo inhaled the air from both.\nThe towers around him gave no shade. His eyes were closed.", "0_8_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span> of April thirtieth.\nLance, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB1, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were shambling across the spacefield, trying to be inconspicuous with their hands bound behind their backs and pieces of copper wire forced between their teeth.\nTheir shambling was more like a drunken stagger. They had on tight-fitting hoods, and their necks and jaws ached from the wired tension of their bites.\nThey had been gassed in their cell, as had the sentry outside.\nThere were three thousand other bewildered recruits out on the field. Most were being herded in groups of fifty or so by squads of iron-eyed laughing recruits.\nThe iron-eyed laughing recruits were shoving their charges into waiting boats as graceful as bathtubs. There were nine hundred and ninety such boats in the air over the field.\nLance and his party were climbing the steps into the last boat in the line. The irony was that the boat had been taken over by the iron-eyed featureless merry recruits almost empty\u2014while the boat ahead of it was packed to suffocation.\nThe recruits in the fully occupied boat had already been gassed, bound, and bitten. So had the captain and the crew.\nLance and...", "2_16_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-three in the afternoon</span>. It was very hot\u2014hotter, it seemed.\nMetzger\u2019s way of telling the time was the way everyone else in the United States of America told time, too. He didn\u2019t have a watch, because all watches had, a few years ago, suddenly quit working. People hadn\u2019t worn watches much before that. The government, trusting in Providence and in the innate sense of time-keeping of the American people, had not thought it necessary to keep them informed as to the time of day, because the government assumed that people would always know what it was. People had always known what time it was.\nNobody had ever been late for anything, before the watches stopped.", "1_4_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-one in the morning</span>. The piece I was working on was dying. I\u2019d been at it too long,\u2014fourteen hours. I was scared. I\u2019ve always been scared when I could feel a story getting out of control. It was like stepping off the end of something high, like a diving board when you\u2019re not sure whether you ought to jump. If I kept going, I might come down like one of those high divers in water, retinas burned to explode, bloody corpse collapsing like something thrown from a great height. It was always the same thing that scared me: not the fear of writing, but the fear of taking that irrevocable step over some edge like a man going through a hoop and finding the other side curled back against him and the whole act final like taking down your fly for some filthy tarot card reader or making that first rude cut into your wife\u2019s tits and finding out what was there. I was a body without secrets: open at both ends like a gutted fish. Open to the dark.\nI tried to reach Mapes again. Nothing. He never watched his messages.\nMaybe the story was dead.\nNo. The story was alive and ready to kill me. I could feel the fear crawling...", "2_10_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt eleven o\u2019clock in the morning, the space ship bearing the glorious rag-tag band of voyagers to Betelgeuse arrived at the shores of the green pond. Its crew was napping, having unfastened their safety belts after a rough but safe flight.\nThe hull of the ship bumped the sand.\n Sequestered in the two-billion-dollar estate on the north shore of the island were thirty-six persons\u2014twenty-eight women and eight men. Of these thirty-six, only three knew anything at all about space travel. They were Spike Mitchell, the lavender-cab-driving assistant trustee; $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the tall man they called the Master; and Richard order, the small man they called the Maid.\nThe others were, by and large, the petty-bourgeois intelligentsia who fuss over the correct management of sizable fortunes.\nThe thirty-six were about to make history\u2014had no earthly idea what fun it was going to be.", "2_22_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nJoe's homecoming\n\"All I say,\" said Joe Fernwright, \"all I say is, that\u2019s about enough of that stuff for me.\"\n\"What stuff?\" said his wife, Ruth.\n\"Horseplay,\" said Fernwright.\n\"I don\u2019t play no horseplay,\" said his son, $FIRSTNAMEF3. \"I ain\u2019t hardly old enough.\"\n\"Funny stuff, funny stuff,\" said Fernwright. Before him was a TV screen on which colorottos pranced and played. They had been bred from Earth's ponies. Some of them were Earthlings themselves. They had all been forced to arrive in Mercury spontaneously, in the same manner that all Earthlings had come to Mercury. They were subjects for experiments.\n\"Oh, I play some funny stuff too,\" said Ruth. She turned off the TV screen. She sat on an overturned bucket in a room of sheet rock and lumber, and held her swollen belly with one hand.\nThe wall next to her had been built by her husband from sod. Two parts of an incomplete window were in it. The window looked out on a world of bluegrass eight feet tall.", "0_16_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time, October 31, in the Home office building in Cleveland, two months, ten days and nine hours after the inauguration. Rumors had reached all the way to Mars that a much grander installation was planned. New designs had been seen in the offices of decorators on Madison Avenue in New York. But to get anything out of $SURNAME1, the true facts had to be teased from a sculptor brought in from Scarsdale, N.Y.\nHeat had been put on repeatedly by The Gnome, but all the heat had been lost. Rumors, smoke and mirrors were $SURNAME1\u2019s administrative tools.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, at four thirty-eight on this Tuesday afternoon, taught a course in telephone mysticism at the Annex.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was on the top floor of the Home headquarters. He was leaning against a window-wall from which the view in all directions was superb. He was jiggling a change machine that had jammed somewhere in the basement. He was bored. This jiggling was a pretense of taking care of something. He did not know how the thing should be taken care of. That was not important, because no one had asked him to take care of anything. No one knew he was on the...", "0_10_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-two in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, apparently a retired publisher and printing-company executive, was sixty-five years old. He stood five feet seven and weighed a hundred and forty pounds. His hair was white and very fine. He never combed it. He was clean-shaven. His blue eyes shone startlingly against his red cheeks, against his white hair, against the blue sky behind his head, when he looked up at helicopters.\nHe was wearing shorts, saddle shoes, a white sleeveless undershirt stained dark by his armpits, and a hound\u2019s-tooth check suitcoat patched on the sleeves, the pockets, and the back.\nHe was standing in a flagstone terrace before his cut-rate contemporary house. His house was one of forty houses, all alike, all badly designed, that had been thrown up in the last two years on a bluff above the ocean.\nThere were two other helicopters in the air above the Pacific Coast Highway. One of these was a police copter, and it had its searchlight turned on.\nThe other was a news- columnist\u2019s copter.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 didn\u2019t give a hang about either one of the other two copters. His indifference to the...", "1_5_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nSilence, darkness, and all the sleepers beneath their countless coverlets of snows.\nA shadow, hardly darker than the blackness of the night, appeared in the deserted bedroom. Its bony fingers moved lightly over the furniture, hunting for something to steal. The fingers tapped upon a dresser drawer, opened it, and lifted out a woman\u2019s purse. The fingers fumbled with a zipper and opened the purse. They found a flat leather pocketbook and transferred its contents to the purse: five dollars and thirty-four cents in bills, two dollars in silver, nine dimes, five nickels, two pennies, one walnut meat envelope, two dry biscuit, one chicken-gizzard pill, three matches, and one item of indeterminate nature not readily identifiable by finger or nose.\nThe shadow melted away into the dark. By the time the sun had risen and begun to take an interest in Earth, the shadow was six miles away, racing through a vacant lot toward a wall of high porches and corrugated sheet-iron sheds. At the shadow\u2019s heels scampered four more shadows. Two of the shadows were dogs; they reminded $FIRSTNAMEF1 of something, but she could not think what...", "1_14_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-four in the afternoon</span>. The Okies were blasting rock in a tunnel near the turnoff to Granada. For the last two weeks they\u2019d been trying to bore through to the old \u201cPirate Caverns\u201d where a local legend claims huge rubies, emeralds and chunks of quartz crystal have lain buried since the Colorado gang hid their ill-gotten gems during the Revolution. Rumors flew that somewhere in the middle of this thousand-foot tunnel the Okies had broken into an old cyclopean crypt, with vague stairs and unnamable frescoes leading downward. A man who\u2019d seen the carvings that first night had gone temporarily mad and required a trip to a madhouse in Reno. Shortly after this another man had tried to shoot incredible photographs of torch-wielding, robe-flowing figures outlined in some phosphorescence source on the rite-wrought walls. He disappeared. Then Sam Mace\u2019s icebox had been broken into and two pounds of prime rib were stolen. Sam Mace blamed it on the Okies. The Okies said a wandering coyote did it. Then a cat was found dead in the toolshed, a cat that had belonged to an old family in Nevada named Tuttle. They wanted to...", "1_13_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-three in the afternoon</span> and the room was number 313 of the Wilburhampton Hotel in San Narciso. It cost almost four dollars a night, which was to say the taxpayers\u2019 money. Apparently, Metzger had a special arrangement with the hotel: essentially, the hotel\u2019s lobby we\u2019re exceptionally empty of paying guests, although curiously, its vast coffee shop, the Tazza D\u2019Oro, we\u2019re often backlogged with airline personnel, Transender kids and young executives carrying briefcases, all drinking coffee and eating Danish and talking about racing stats. In the lobby, on the con trary, it was as though the hotel were out of business. Frequently, however, up in 313, there\u2019d be some fairly heavy sex going on, interrupted once in a while by the telephone. Taken all together, it made the Wilburhampton a fairly interesting hangout, at least for the Crimson Sunset gang. $FIRSTNAMEF2, it should be noted, never much cared for prostitution, even of the amateur sort. But for John Nefastis and her husband, Metzger, this was like home. $FIRSTNAMEF2, in fact, had only been there once before, nearly two years ago...", "2_16_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF2 stole glances at her watch. \u201cAnybody else involved?\u201d she asked, shading her eyes, watching him. \u201cLike the Mafia?\u201d\n\u201cNo.\u201d A curt headshake. Would he lie? There was an outside line, at the gas station; she'd listened to enough of it to be sure about that. But did it only go to the killer\u2019s answering service? Would he have access to it, use it to call an outside operator? She glanced again at her watch, and at him. Six feet away, sitting on the toilet bowl, he stared straight back at her with those sad child\u2019s eyes; the mom and baby stared, scared but stubborn, at the scene. Private amongDetails of shadow and small movements\u2014nothing to make her really sure. \u201cWhat\u2019s your name,\u201d she said, \u201cyour real name, not the one you give to commuters?\u201d", "0_14_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earthling eastern standard time, on June 22, 1992.\nThe space was a lawn that was an unobstructed part of the T symbol at the top of $SURNAME0\u2019s Beatitude. The lawn was forty-eight yards long, and thirty-six yards wide.\nThe lawn was paved with self-threading windowpane crystal, knitted together as tightly as stitches in embroidery.\nThe lawn was as level as a billiard table.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and blind old Salo stood together on the lawn. They were man and machine. The sun was hidden by a gaseous yellow veil that made everything green, made the sky itself a sickly green mist. It was impossible to see the Sun through the veil.\nThere were two holes in the veil where Salo\u2019s eyes had been. The holes were not circular. They looked like bullet holes, and there were tufts of fiber where the bullets had plowed through the fungi-nylon skin of Salo\u2019s face.\nSalo was perhaps the ugliest machine ever built by man.\nHis skinsuit was the obscene pink of a newborn babe. His long hands and tapering fingers ended in pads of red flannel. His...", "2_0_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-eight a.m.</span> when I checked into the hotel $206 credit, in the name of King. The desk clerk was very professional. He was trained not to be surprised.\n\u201cTake your key,\u201d he said, without expression. \u201cThe Rolling Stones are on their way out. I believe they just checked out.\"\n\u201cNo problem,\u201d I said. \u201cI\u2019m not here to fuck rock bands.\" No comment. We were both professionals. Of course, I thought, they were on hashish. Their perceptions would be funny. I meant to study that when the time came\u2014but the time was not yet. \u201cThis is a sickness-rehabilitation center for alcoholics and drug addicts?\u201d he asked, as he handed me my key.\n\u201cYes,\u201d I said. \u201cFirst I must retire from all addictive behavior for fortyeight hours...medication and alcohol withdrawal...\"\n\u201cAh yes,\u201d he said, \u201cI understand. Have a good time.\"\n\u201cI intend to,\u201d I said. \u201cI must. My life depends on it.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll take your bag,\u201d he said.", "0_0_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-two a.m.</span> on April 1, 1993. Since midnight, I had been wearing a bright-yellow waterproof rain slicker that I had bought at a camping store for nine dollars. I was wearing a matching yellow Icelandic wool sweater, a grey tweed sport coat, gray slacks and all-weather shoes. I looked like a salesman: I was certainly not the kind of person anybody would want to ask the time. I didn\u2019t have a wristwatch. I was still suffering from a wristwatch trauma. Wristwatches were hideous instruments of cruelty. All people who wore wristwatches were mindless slaves of the Great Clock, victims of Time\u2014Time that had no mercy, that chopped people into dogfood and dumped them into the sewers like worthless meat. Wristwatch people were grunting subhumans, barely conscious, behaving like witless mutant organisms in the sinister gloom of 12:32 a.m.: i.e., glancing all the time at the little glass window in front of the face and reading the time. The terror and paralysis of that hideous \u201creading.\u201d Being converted and reduced to a number. To the unspeakable name of a number. Lower than the lowest animal. Slaves and animals. Knowing and transported by every tick...", "2_21_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-eight in the evening</span>. The place was a pay phone, in downtown Cleveland. A little girl in a white organdy dress brushed past, saying something very sad. She got onto the elevator by herself, pushed the button, was gone.\nThe time was nine fifty in the evening. The place was the lobby of the east wing of the In firmary. Dr. J._______________ stepped out of a shadow. \u201cGood evening. Kind of damp out tonight, don\u201d t you think?\u201d\n\u201cBarb?\u201d\n\u201cYes that was my friend Dr. J_______, he\u2019s the Gerontol ogist over in the east wing. Very well regarded, really brilliant guy, really does have an expertise in American Sign Language, don\u2019t get me wrong. Did you put your ticket in?\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cThere it is in the phone. It\u2019s waiting for me.\u201d\n\u201cMind if we walk?\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d\n\u201cWalk.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cWalk.\u201d\n\u201cNot right now, I\u2019d rather\u2014\u201d", "1_21_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"One, two, three\u2014\" said a young lieutenant, looking down at his watch.\n\"I do hope they do it right,\" said a grizzled old-timer. \"This is their last chance.\"\n\"All personnel,\" said a tinny loudspeaker on the ceiling, \"stand by for an important announcement at zero minus eight minutes.\"\nThe young lieutenant waved at the nearest microphone.\n\"Has Truxton Saddler come in yet, Sergeant?\" he asked.\n\"The ten-station crew is reported in the building, sir,\" said the loudspeaker.\n\"All personnel, check count!\" said the lieutenant. He looked at his watch again, saw that there were thirty seconds to go. \"Stand by for the signal to activate check count!\"\n\"At the instant of signal for zero minus eight minutes, check\u2014\" said the loudspeaker. \"Zero minus eight. Seven\u2014six\u2014five\u2014four\u2014three\u2014\"\n\"Activate, instant scan,\" said the lieutenant. He waved once more at the microphone. \"If anybody sees Saddler before I do, tell him I\u2019m looking for him.\"\n\"Two\u2014one\u2014zero,\" said the loudspeaker. \"Scan\u2014one\u2014\"\nA party of ten...", "1_11_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s\u30c3\u30c9 cabin in the festering forest.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was the only living thing in the cabin. Everybody else was dead\u2014dead from friendlinesses and cyanide gas.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 hadn\u2019t killed anybody, though he supposed he could have killed anybody he wanted to. If he had ever tried, it wouldn\u2019t have been in the goodness of his heart, since he knew from sad experience that he didn\u2019t have a good heart to begin with. And he certainly wouldn\u2019t have tried to kill anybody in an office building, a supermarket, or a living room. All that would have done would have been to call attention to himself, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew that he would not be a good subject for attention\u2014 not if surrounded by people who weren\u2019t friendly to him.\nFriendliness. Fr\u2014\nGod damn that computer!\nFriendliness, $FIRSTNAMEB0 thought with a shiver. That was always making its demands.\nThe demands were being made by the little television that was built into the bottom corner of the cabin wall. The television was tuned to a station on the bottom floor of Mercury Central. The station was identification-free, since the management preferred to keep...", "0_19_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past seven o'clock in the evening</span>. The great wheeled space ship lay on its wheeled parking struts on a hillside in the early mist of a summer day that was just too hot and sunny to be in the least anticipatory of autumn.\nThe ship was surrounded by marshals and police and by Northampton villagers and by eager but unruly crowds of newspapermen, photographers, newsreel cameramen, radio commentators, and official science writers.\nThe crowd noises mixed in with the murmuring of the great ship\u2019s air-circulation system like some vast system of mixed-up steam calliopes.\nThe universal salute to the brave crew of Martian veterans inside the space ship was a constant rattle of expectant camera shutters.\nWhen the rattle was at its loudest, when the shutters seemed to say Fever, fever, fever, fever, like some kind of social disease\u2014that was when a man in a white summer suit, with a Seeing Eye dog, came through the crowd and up the ramp to the hatch of the ship. At the foot of the ramp, the man in the white suit stopped.\nThe dog took swirls around the ship, marking out the contours of an area that was forbidden.\nThe dog brought the man...", "2_3_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich time.\nMr. $FIRSTNAMEB4, President of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent, arrived at the mansion of the Supreme Pontiff in Newport at three o'clock the next afternoon. Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 was wearing old, stained baggy bathing trunks, a ratty fielder\u2019s mitt, and bedroom slippers. He had slept twenty-six hours in one of the buses of the Sunwise Turnpike, and he was covered with insect bites from head to foot.\nMr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 went directly to the reception room of the Supreme Pontiff. There, he roamed about, whimpering and scratching like a dog who hadn\u2019t been out for a walk in months.\nA Bronze Knifefish, the Chamberlain or Chief Cook of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent, appeared and offered Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 a bucket of raw hamburger. Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 sniffed the bucket, whimpered, and backed away.\n\"Hold out your hands,\" said the Chamberlain.\nMr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 held out his hands. The Chamberlain poured the hamburger patties on Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s fingers. Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 ate.", "2_21_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-three in the evening</span>.\n/d/\nLenore sat at Rex Ruymen\u2019s desk, in her bathing suit under her dress, looking at the drawings and writing on the back of the mummified bones. \u201cI think they\u2019re things you imagined, with the help of some actual things from around the house,\u201d she said to Clarice, who sat in Ruymen\u2019s chair too, clothes on under her heavy coat. \u201cI think these wings are from some model airplane, and this sailboat is supposed to bear a strong resemblance to something in Lake Truculent. I don\u2019t think they\u2019re symbols of flying or longing, or anything like that. I think they\u2019re just images, a bunch of things you made up.\u201d\n\u201cYou don\u2019t believe in the intrinsic fluid-carrying capacity of a one and one-quarter-inch plastic pipe threaded together at either end to form a pipe of any length?\u201d\n\u201cI think your mother must\u2019ve known she was dehydrating you,\u201d said Lenore.", "2_15_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEF1 left the grounds of the Manor. The Martian sky was pink.\nThe panorama at three o\u2019clock in the afternoon was the most perfect panorama that could ever be conceived by a healthy, uninhibited intellect. The panorama was the glories of the past.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 did not look at the panorama. She was crying. She was looking down at the pink footprints she left in the dust outside the main gate.\nThe footprints were blurred by crystals of frozen tears.\nAt twenty minutes past three o\u2019clock in the afternoon, $FIRSTNAMEF1 Norman reached the vicinity of the space ship that had deposited $SURNAME0 and his dog in the Bay of Phobos.\nThe ship\u2019s protective field still hummed faintly.\nThe ship was a rhinoceros-sized beetle, the warship of the fleet of the army of the Guardians, ancient enemies of the Tralfamadores. It was, to all appearances, indestructible. It could fly, swim, hop, skip, and jump.", "0_1_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>. Nothing was moving. No sound was heard. Somewhere off in the fore part of the ship a messenger banged a big iron bell once. The white tracer bullets of the Royal Air Force were still circling high above, making their slow turns above the 1:58:7:10:8:0:7:8 position.\nSitting in the dark in the foreign area in the bow of the Titanic were, for some reason not precisely known, fifty-one red apes. The apes were doing nothing, simply sitting in the dark, in groups of six or seven, on the tops of their cradles.\nAt the smoking-room bar, witnesses were to report later, the following conversation took place between two men. The two men were, according to witnesses, William Edmund Craddock, major, Royal Air Force, unmarried, thirty-four years old, six feet, two inches tall, two hundred pounds, eighteen stone; and Tuttle, his chauffeur, divorced, eight years with the RAF, thirty-five, five feet, ten inches tall, one hundred and seventy pounds, eleven stone.\nThe conversation in the smoking room, not very enlightening even in verbatim, went like this:\nCRADDOCK:...", "1_3_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-three in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF2, in the blue Trans-America nighted gown, reclined on the bed, watching the ceiling fan turn. She kept hearing things. Noises. Thinking it was Gene, she\u2019d rush out of her room into the living room, then fall all over herself when she saw nobody was there. She hated herself for that. She hated being in a house where she didn\u2019t know where the rooms were. She had thought that with an entire house, empty, all for her, her insomnia would leave. Instead it had deepened. She told herself at least the house had nothing in it to threaten her: no mute vacuum cleaners, no suicidal wristwatches, nothing possessed by\u2019 any tropism toward its own demise. There were no mirrors. Yet she knew that was not why she kept imagining things\u2014movement, noise; that despite the clear rural night glazing the windows, and the brightly lit parking lot outside, and acres of lit suburban homes stretched any which way in all that distance, she was not alone. She knew her own mind was creating noises, voices. Perhaps even somebody walking around out there. She had sat up with a hand on each gun barrel, waiting to hear known footsteps on the porch...", "0_10_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-three in the morning</span>.\nI found a cigar-store and bought cigarettes. I bought three packages of Lucky Strikes, one carton of Chesterfields, and one carton of Picayunes. I was getting used to drinking the warm whiskey and needed little tobacco stimulation. The cigarettes I bought were for selling to soldiers and sailors from the military camps near Pico Mundo.\nI intended to sell two cartons.\nI sampled a Lucky and a Chesterfield, picked out twelve definitely lucky cigarettes of each brand, tore the rest of the packages apart, and stuffed them in my pockets.\nI rearranged my effects. My pockets bulged. I added the bourbon bottle, candy box, and Lucky Strikes to my right wrist. The remainder of the Lucky Strikes, wooden matches in one hand and a box of Chesterfields upright in the other. It looked like an Indian smoke combination.\nPushing my way out of the cigar-store, I asked the proprietor, \u201cWhere\u2019s the nearest USO, mister?\u201d\n\u201cCross the City Hall park, go south, couple more blocks.\u201d I couldn\u2019t smoke with one hand busy pushing. Out on the sidewalk I switched my cigar box to my left wrist, took the...", "2_13_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe ineluctable rumors of mortality on Mars grew suddenly louder. Again, there was a knocking at the front door. Again, Brinton M. $SURNAME0 would not answer it. Again, he used his controls so that the knock became a tempest of organ music, as though Hell\u2019s Angels were pounding on the door with the wind of a tornado.\nA thought came to $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. A glorious thought. He thought he could show $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 how contemptible, how worthless, how pointless the life of a well-to-do-citizen actually was. He thought he could do it simply by moving the crowd downstairs where the action was, downstairs where the miracles happened, downstairs to where the crowds were.\nHe rumbled happily.\nHe had his volunteer attendants move the $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 statue to the alcove beneath the staircase in the foyer. $SURNAME0 and his wife were separated for the first time in thirty-nine years. They faced in opposite directions.", "0_23_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThis was the end.\nAt that moment, Salo admitted to himself that the Titanic was indeed going to sink.\nHe felt proud of his system, though. It hadn't faltered. It had given him the maximum information with the minimum physical pain\u2014and now things were settling nicely, as if into a feather mattress, as if into oblivion.\nHe would have liked to stay awake\u2014to see his friends, to say nice things to them\u2014just before the final sinking. But his system would not let him stay awake.\nHis system felled him with a soporific.\nAnd his system was perfectly adapted to its purpose, to the purpose of staying alive just a little bit longer, of staying alive until the ship sank.\nThe soporific paralyzed Salo, nestled him into a feather mattress of paramecia, paralyzed his breathing muscles and his diaphragm.\nIt didn\u2019t even sadden him. He felt proud of his species, as well as of himself.\nAnd he felt a new respect, as well as a new fondness, for human beings. They felt so much and hurt so little. That was something that a being like himself could admire without reservation\u2014a...", "0_8_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-five in the morning</span>. The place was the lobby of the Biltmore Hotel in Phoenix, Arizona. The guy was me. The other person was my attorney. His name is Robert P. Bingham, but my friends and I call him Dr. Bob. He is a doctor of jurisprudence\u2014a SoCal surf-lawyer with a practice based in Santa Monica, and a good man: I was still sleeping off a bad tequila drunk the night before, but the Doctor was fine. At that moment, he was leaning over the registration desk, arguing with a young cop in a business suit and a red sports car. \u201cWhat are you charging my client with?\u201d he demanded. \u201cHe didn't do anything wrong, except maybe leave the highway.\"\n\u201cHe was observed,\" the cop said stiffly, \u201cdriving his car along the shoulder of Interstate 17 at speeds up to one hundred twenty miles an hour ...\u201d \u201cWhere's your witness?\" the Doctor asked. \u201cPerhaps he can tell us what my client was doing.\"\n\u201cI talked to him,\" the cop said. \u201cHe was very nervous. He said somebody was chasing him!\" \u201cI beg your pardon?\u201d said the Doctor,...", "1_5_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-nine in the morning</span> and it was hard to go to sleep in the afternoon in Las Vegas, and since my girl Terry was giving the big crap game in the afternoon and since I was fiddling around all night, I had some time to poke before the game. It was the time of day sailors hate. I cashed Truman's bad check for him and treated a group of sailors to a beer. Six handsome buck privates in tiger fatigues were on a binge. They were full of booze. \u201cIt's Saturday and Saturday means party!\u201d they yelled. \u201cHeah!\u201d They treated me. We lined up ten beers and drank them down. I had a good drunk going. They went back to their bungalow. I saw the sailors off on a bus, screaming \u201cHeah! Heah! Heah!\u201d Then I suddenly realized I was the only passenger left in the bus station and the sun was rising over the Court of Apache Mammals. I got on a bus for the last stop -- Tonapah. Through mountains we whizzed. The vast desert in the distance loomed red. The darkness in the bus was holy purple. Big desert winds blew straight from the heart of the earth. The sun kept rising and...", "2_8_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"I think I\u2019ll sit down and wait,\" said the woman to herself. And she did.\nAt ten minutes to Solar Noon, in Mercury\u2019s time, which was Solar Noon in the sector where $SURNAME1\u2019s Solar Palace stood, the telephone in the Mercury suite rang.\nThe woman answered it with a languid hello.\n\"Hello, Heliogabalus,\" said a voice. It was $SURNAME0.\n\"Oh\u2014hello, $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said the woman.\n\"Am I calling at a bad time, Mrs. Mott?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Not at all\u2014$FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said the woman. \"I like to commune with the name of the Deity, anyway, and the only name by which the deity has ever revealed himself to the least of his creatures is $FIRSTNAMEM1. What can I do for you, $FIRSTNAMEM1?\"\n\"You can die without pain, and without any help from me,\" said $SURNAME0, \"if you\u2019ll tell me whether or not you planted the bomb on my dog.\"", "0_16_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when President Cam telephone his old friend and colleague and fellow founder-member of the Tristero Chapter of the Fine Arts Faculty at State, Hilarius, now in residence at the Mull Brush Sanitarium, and communicated the loss. What he said is not known, nor what Hilarius replied. It is only known that at eighteen minutes past the hour, a Dr. Fluck, resident psychiatrist, accompanied the President on the telephone to Hilarius, introduced him to the occupant of Suite 210, and left. At twenty-six minutes past the hour, a fire alarm was sounded, not in the Sanitarium but in the offices of the Tristero in Chicago. The fire department found nothing to burn. Two hours later, back in his Oval Study at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue, in the dark, and not by telephone, finally, and for the first time in history, President Fitzpatrick communicated with his home in Buffalo, New York, to inform his wife there that a great weight of despair and sadness had come upon him, and that he must consider seriously the prospect of paying at least one visit, perhaps more, the following autumn to his good friend of some twenty years, Hilarius, now a patient at the Mull Brush Sanitarium in New...", "1_14_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. The place was one-hundred-and-sixty miles due west of Times Square, in a luxury suite on the second floor of the Hotel California. A Redstone Spaceliner, filled to its 2500-passenger capacity with bored, bewildered passengers, had roared out of the night and passed the hotel at 1443 EST, only four minutes late. It was heading west. It would be passing Los Angeles at 1713 PST, three hours and thirty-seven minutes late\u2014if it did not break down, crash, or blow up. It would not be the first Spaceliner to do any, or all, of those things.\nOld Sweetness and Light, in full cry: \" \u2019Tis mystery all: from black of pitch-darkness to pitch-white deceiving thin light way out of Satan\u2019s snare, few may go.\n\" \u2019Tis those, young lady, shall be deemed highly favored of the lord . . . The rest shall suffer the wages of their sloth, in the fields of negligence!\" $FIRSTNAMEF2 could hear the Metzches all over the suite, squirming around on other lines, stretching themselves in preparation for more travel, hoorah. She watched...", "1_21_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe light was from the moon.\nThere was a crescent moon.\nThe crescent moon was back of Putnam Wegg.\nPutnam Wegg was making thirty-six dollars a week, irregularly.\nEAT\nIT WAS HASH that the charitable organizations in Gommorrah served the men who went to the terrible moon. Arnold \"The Brain\" Heermance had read that in a book he had bought second-hand entitled The Private Life of the Hun which The Sun in Levtree, Ohio, had published thirty-four years before.\nThe Hon. Lyle Bongseed had given Arnold \"The Brain\" Heermance the book.\nArnold \"The Brain\" Heermance lived in an apartment at 38 New Clove Road, on the Rumford, Rhode Island, side of Gommorrah. His apartment was above a bakery shop.\nThe walls of Arnold Heermance's apartment were porous. The apartment was not nice to live in, but it was in Gommorrah.\nOne wall of Arnold's apartment was the wall of the building next door. There, next door but one, was the luxurious Newport Creamery Company.\nThe Newport Creamery Company had...", "0_4_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe man who had just shaved was asleep. He slept on a cot in the main room of the tent, because there were two other tents that housed patients, and the man on the cot did not want to sleep next to any of them.\nThe snoring of the man on the cot was tremendous. It was not snoring, actually. It was the echo of tremendous thunder.\nThe echo did not keep the little girl awake. She lay curled under a blanket on an army cot in one corner of the big room.\nThe little girl made a sound in her throat. It was a questioning sound, a sound that might have been made by a kitten.\nThe echo stopped.\nThe little girl made the sound again.\nThere was a terrific banging on the door. It was the nurse. \"Goddam it, $FIRSTNAMEF3,\" she said. \"Wake up and get over here.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 Rousseau got up and put on some clothes. He was Chief Psychologist of the hospital and proud of his position. It stood to reason that a man of his intelligence should be Chief Psychologist of the hospital. His hair was dark and curly, and his eyes were dark and merry. He was forty-six...", "1_18_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past six o'clock in the evening</span> when $SURNAME1 came to this entry. Daylight was all but dead.\nLiberal had been wrong about one thing. Murder had been committed on Titan\u2014what was more, murder was still being committed. $SURNAME1, looking out at the swarms of night creatures that were regaining possession of their world, discovered that he had been wrong when he concluded in his diary that $SURNAME0 was putting the blame on creatures other than himself.\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had been right\u2014her husband was perfectly capable of letting the creatures starve so that others might remain plump, so that others might remain warm and richly fed.\nAlready, $SURNAME0\u2019s society was being selected for the breeding stock of the Titanian colonial aristocracy.\nThe Sw IF\nThe $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 Museum of Aboriginal Religions in the West Windows of the Taj Mahal was founded by $SURNAME0 in 1968, a year after he was first recognized in the Earthling community of Mars. A brochure explaining the purpose of the museum makes this statement:\n\"For too long have the poor Earthling bastards in the Martian community been treated to a one-sided view of religious history in the Solar System. Hundreds of religions, flourishing widely and wildly,...", "2_8_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-one in the morning</span>.\nIt was ten minutes to two in the afternoon.\nIt was six minutes past eight in the evening.\nIt was seven minutes after seven in the evening.\nIt was twenty minutes before midnight.\nIt was Newport, Rhode Island.\nIt was London, England.\nIt was Denpasar, Bali.\nIt was St. Thomas, in the Virgin Islands.\nIt was San Miguel, in the Azores.\nIt was the Sea of Tranquility.\nIt was Baikonur.\nIt was Tananarive.\nIt was Taormina.\nIt was Mauer.\nIt was Tau Ceti.\nIt was Gizeh.\nIt was Yanceyville, North Carolina.\nIt was St. Helena.\nIt was Cape Kennedy.\nIt was the Bay of Rainbows.\nIt was the Great Desert of Wyoming.\nIt was Mars.\nIt was the Martian moondust.\nIt was the Martian atmosphere.\nIt was the Martian air.\nIt was the Martian sky, clean and bright and blue and wonderful.\nThere was a spaceship in it.\nIt was parked in Mars\u2019s sky.", "0_18_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span> when I pulled up before the house in Las Vegas. I waited two minutes before calling. This would give $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Mary plenty of time to converse in their homely way and make ready for my arrival.\nI kept telling myself many things. \u201cNow stay cool, $FIRSTNAMEB6, you're all tensed up and unnatural and you'll only put $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Mary on their guard and make them wonder what you're all cracked up to say, if anything, and then they'll all withdraw into any covers they can find and you'll be out in the cold and that'll be the end of it. Just take it easy and go along for the evening and see what happens.\u201d I repeated this several times as I sat in front of my driveway in the car waiting for $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Mary, in fact I repeated all my plans for the winter of 1950 so often that I realized I was past exhaustion and just sitting in the car; thus I unlocked the door and got out for a walk in the neighborhood. My mind had been going a hundred miles an hour for four days. Neon lights blinked off and on in the sunset, orange, red, white, and green, advertising the casinos. The sun is red in Vegas. It sinks in a whitehot...", "1_9_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nObama was on his way to the holy Senate chamber.\nHe traveled in a closed car. The closed car was driven by a chauffeur named James.\nJames, the chauffeur, had been with Obama for twenty years.\nJames was forty-two years old. He had been born in Texas.\nJames was proud of having been chauffeur to the great and now holy Martian.\nJames was proud of the fact that he had never been able to understand the great exalted one.\nJames was proud of being a poor, rural Texas white man\u2014of having been born and raised in Brownwood, Texas.\nJames was proud of the fact that he had finally become a chauffeur.\nJames was proud that he was called a chauffeur.\nJames was proud that he read nothing but the sporting page of the newspapers he took the trainloads of congressmen to and from their homes.\nSeventy-one congressmen had homes, and James was proud of being close to some of them. Furthermore, James was proud that he had never been able to guess who would marry whom, or who would die, or who would get elected. James read nothing but the sporting page, because, he said, he never...", "1_22_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after ten o'clock in the evening</span>, I saw by the central clock, when I took my seat in the lobby to wait for the rocket. I did not bother going up to the control room. There was nothing they could tell me that I did not know already.\n\"Infiltrated mass,\" the Master of Armory had called it\u2014an \"asteroid.\"\nThe word was meaningless. I had looked it up in Brown\u2019s Encyclopedia when I got back to my room to collect my things for the lobby.\n\"Theory now discarded,\" said Brown\u2019s Encyclopedia, \"that all heavenly bodies were formed in one or another sphere exterior to ours. Recent explorations within solar system have disclosed that such bodies are of local origin and so circumstanced in respect of mass, temperature and pressure as to sustain life.\"\nI opened the last volume to read about Luna Count: 76,000,000 inhabitants, 53 airships in service, 26 colonies established, 11 mines in operation. Then I realized it was not last year\u2019s encyclopedia after all. It was out of date by over fifty years. It did not even mention reentry.\nThe word was in the vocabulary. You could look it up if you felt scholarly. But nobody had used it for a long time...", "0_4_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-six in the morning</span> when they arrived at Spargi\u2019s house in Oakland. Nobody had spoken in a very long time.\nThere was much to be discussed between them, but not in the presence of $FIRSTNAMEF3 Oberly, who had been stone-cold asleep. They would wait for morning, in the early dawn, when $FIRSTNAMEF3 would be out with his walker feeding the birds in the very nicely kept and well-wooded backyard of Spargi\u2019s house.\nSpargi promised this.\nNow it was time for them all to rest.\nThe house smelled very old. Its windows were immemorially filthy. The whole structure seemed on the verge of toppling into the soggy loam in which it was rooted.\nThere was a birdbath that was unspeakably foul. Some industrious and misdirected soul had encrusted the bath\u2019s sides with broken eggshells and feathers and bits of regurgitated worms and gnawed delicacies.\nThe one hip, mouldy bedroom in the house smelled lightly of ammonia.\nMrs. Spargi, $FIRSTNAMEF3\u2019s mother, had died in that room. $FIRSTNAMEF3 showed them the dent in the mattress where her hip had lain. His father had died in...", "0_15_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-nine in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 found the way out of the caves and came on the ruins of Hartford, Connecticut.\nHe had followed his memory of a passage that came up to the blue-white sun-circle three times, and had branched left for home three times.\nNow he was following a passage so low and twisting that he had to go on hands and knees. The passage ended in a full stop. $FIRSTNAMEB0, who had been resting in a fetal crouch near the end of the passage, now stood up shakily.\nHe purged his drunken memory from his mind, blanked the blackboard clean. Then he let his eyes lead his feet. Ahead was a sight so beautiful that he smiled and cried. Ahead was the school, his playground, his church.\nThe iron lockers shone with a mellow gold in the floors below. The dark blue-violet slate and white snowdrifts of the floor were burned with the sun and drooped comfortably.\nThe windows were soft lozenges of ruby red, squares of lambent fire. The basketball backboards were orange, the nets lemon yellow.\nThe wooden benches were a cool, dark brown, and wonderfully restful in the reds and yellows. The radiators...", "0_19_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span> of the twenty-fourth day of October. The Tralfamadorians were going to show the Earthling the minute in which he died. The minute was going to unfold in the following way:\nFrom above the place where the Earthling stood, the Tralfamadorians were going to use their ray guns to inscribe an enormous equilateral triangle in the air. The triangle would have its vertex at the Earthling\u2019s vantage point, and its three sides would extend indefinitely into the night.\nThe three sides of the huge equilateral triangle would represent three enormous corridors of space-time, each of which would extend back from the planet Earth for ninety-six years of earthly time.\nAs the Earthling watched, the Tralfamadorians would now begin exaggerating the size of the three corridors of space-time, making them grow in the Earthling\u2019s vision from ninety-six-year corridors to four-billion-year corridors.\nThe Tralfamadorians would make the size of the corridors grow by flying great interstellar distances toward, and then away from, the Earthling and the planet Earth.\nAs soon as the Tralfamadorians had so flown, the Earthling would see Earthlings in his...", "0_4_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-eight in the morning</span>. Great Salt Desert, Utah, lay around us soft and drowsy with the moon. We drove along looking for a gas station and heard one playing loud and clear -- wild rock 'n' roll music. The sad guitar sang and sobbed over the static -- \u201cYaw, yaw, yaw\u201d -- cursed for all of ten miles around.\nWe arrived in Rawlins at five-thirty. Several drivers we met in a greasy spoon caf\u00e9 had just come from Denver with a load of beer and the party hadn't let up. Forty men jackknifed in their seats had howled forty nonstop hours. \u201cWhat a party, man!\u201d they cried happily. Music never stopped in their truck --and they left five men to sleep off the boilermaker's in a motel in Cheyenne. Now there were only forty --driving the stinking eighteen-wheeler through the Great Salt Desert at dawn with music roaring full blast for dear life, because the drivers knew a penalty fine of at least five hundred dollars if caught playing music along the road in the middle of the night. And it was curious music, too --rock 'n' roll, not the sweet strains of the conga or the sexy moan...", "1_23_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-four in the evening</span>. In order to have time to finish filing and bundling it and don the formal suit in which he was expected at the statue unveiling, he would have to leave here at once. $FIRSTNAMEF2 was using the bathroom again. She\u2019d been gone a long time. What was she doing in there all this time anyway, now he wondered; suddenly nervous, despite his deep-bath calm.\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 emerged wearing black jeans, motorcycle boots and a red t-shirt with the universal mother symbol, inverted triangle with a gaussian curve in it, over her right boob. Metzger wondered how deep it was, this new thing with her.\n\u201cYou read too much mystic slang,\u201d he charged her. \u201cWhat does that really mean, inverted?\u201d\n\u201cYou try it,\u201d she said, \u201cand see.\u201d\n\u201cCome on,\u201d he pleaded, \u201ctell Uncle Metzger.\u201d\n\u201cTell Yeh-Yehs, in San Francisco.\u201d She consulted a Kit Kat bar wrapper, \u201cYeh-Yehs mutter an alternative mythology.\u201d\n\u201cHow is it possible,\u201d Metz...", "1_7_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-two in the morning</span> and there they were, six guys and a girl, bunking in the room with me. The girl was really pretty, but bitter-bitch ugly when she snarled; she had shocking red hair, a zany white face, wild green eyes, and bony limbs. Her admirers were fawning over her; she was the countess, little realizing that she'd have to spend a night in a neighbor's house with the children. She told them off, damn them, she'd just found a wonderful room up on Brazilian Hill where she was going to have her things moved by noon. They slunk around, anxious about their rent already. They tried to look virile in front of her, but behind her back they were piglets. One of them was poor goofy Tommy; he couldn't handle himself when she shot at him and called him a fool, and he came to me for protection. Bull ain't my brother. I told Tommy to beg forgiveness and he said he would, but the next minute he was back sniveling over something new she had said. I decided to leave the house. \u201cExcuse me, anyone who wishes to use this room is welcome to it.\u201d I knew I was going to have...", "0_16_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-two in the afternoon</span>. I didn't want to go into the drugstore, but what else could I do? I decided to walk straight in, but when I got to the door I couldn't bring myself to open it. The tremendous bonging of the roar hit me full force, shattering my will and nerve. To get through that door meant certain and immediate insanity\u2014the noose on the gallows, the dunk in the tank, the hour on the electric rack...of course I had to go in there. The supply (of Hadacol) was low. A moment of weakness, and it could mean Death itself...or worse. I finally grabbed the knob and yanked the door open. Everything went black, and I found myself falling heavily onto the floor of the entrance, knocking over several fruitsnack racks. I am minus one fingernail a memory of the fall. The pharmacist rushed to my side and helped me back to my feet. I muttered, \u201cI want some Warfarin Sodium, goddammit, and don't fool around about it!\u201d Then I saw the clerk. His face was a white blur. I couldn't focus on it. He seemed to be about ten feet tall, and he was turning around about once...", "2_21_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"How long, oh, Lord, how long?\" said Florence. \"Time is going on and on and on, and still I sit in this kitchen. Sitting on my fanny with all the things piled on to me, and I never do anything. Nothing ever happens to me. I sit here till I die, I guess.\"", "1_15_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-four in the afternoon</span>...Now there was nothing that would break up the sea of insanity except Lucy's goddamn taxicab. I hurried to the door to get it before it left...but there was no need. I heard it scream up to the porch, I tossed the cop twenty dollars and told him to keep the change...and this was the crucial moment when the last obstacle was removed: no longer was there anything to hold me back. I'd been on the wire every forty minutes, demanding to talk to my attorney and screaming that I'd blow up the goddamn hotel if they didn't put me through. The manager was under considerable duress when they gave me the final call.\n\u201cLook,\u201d he said, \u201cif you don't quiet down we're not going to send up your cocaine. Do you understand?\u201d\n\u201cYou bastard!\u201d I shouted. \u201cI'm holding the gun! I'll blow your fucking head off...!\u201d They hung up on me.\nWell, there was no need for that sort of graceless confrontation now. My murder weapon was freshly loaded. The Lucy Bancroft Special: .357 Magnum, a stainless steel Swedish Kalashnikov knockoff, with seventeen shots and a lun...", "2_13_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Venus was moving eastward over the Sun. $SURNAME0 lay supine, his arms out along the sides of his contour chaise longue, his palms up, his pale blue eyes looking blithely at the racing clouds. He whistled Beethoven\u2019s Fifth Symphony.\nAs usual, $SURNAME0 considered the coming visitation good news when it arrived, and good news when it didn\u2019t arrive.\nHis fundamental optimism was unflappable.\nHe lay, a powerfully lustrous gold against the screaming blue of the sky, like a big insouciant butterball made of cool, refreshing butter scotch.\nThe para-dish hung from the branches of a sycamore near his head.\nIt was turned off.\nLittle Miss Mercury, f. k. a. Maluna the Swimwear Model, was asleep on the grass under the sycamore. She was spread like a human The at the center of a mandorla of flowers. Her crown, binoculars, grass halter, swim fins, two-piece bathing suit, cigarette holder, and shoes were scattered a respectful distance away.", "2_16_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-one in the afternoon</span>. Rum Row was a quarter of a mile away now. The row lay like a black band across the sliding, glinting half-mile of sea. Rum Row was in itself a stupid, complicated maze.\nIn Rum Row, Rum Freddie was first. Rum Maude was last. Rum Steady was seventeenth. Rum Loyalty was twenty-first. Rum Justice was twenty-fourth.\nSix pirate ships lay in Rum Row. Rum Freddie passed the Tremoku, the Rum War, the Rum Siege, the Rum Deceiver, the Rum Spiv, and the Rum Typhoon. Rum Freddie passed sixteen other vessels, fishing boats, coasters, and the like.\nRum Freddie steered true North.\n\"See there?\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to $FIRSTNAMEF0 Rumsey. \"I won again.\"\n\"There may be a\u2014turnabout in\u2014a turnabout,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 Rumsey. \"After all, maybe the Army isn\u2019t\u2014the way the advertisement made it\u2014sound.\"\n\"Famous last words,\" mocked $SURNAME1. \"If you don\u2019t think the Army is wonderful, you\u2019re crazy.\"\n\"That isn\u2019t what I said,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 Rumsey.", "0_17_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. Nevertheless, the space ship was flying through space under conditions of absolute blackness.\n\"The hell with that,\" said $SURNAME1. He opened the port. The ship not only received light, but in a twinkling became a shelter for three human beings who thought they were having a marvelous time.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 handed $SURNAME1 a can of beer and a glass. He was pretending that a glass was a pressurized container from which scintillant bursts of gas issued when the container was pressed.\n\"Press the button, $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" said $SURNAME1, \"and see what happens.\" He said this as if it had a sexual connotation.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 did not press the \u201cbutton,\u201d either in fact or metaphorically, because she did not have her Space Wanderer\u2019s Manual in the back seat. She, like the great majority of all Virginians, was functionally illiterate. She could read a plain stock-ticker, but her only book was the Sears Roebuck Catalog, as demanding as a comic strip.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 could read pictures in the Sears Roebuck Catalog, telling at a glance what fixtures and appliances would be most appropriate for a particular house. $SURNAME1\u2019s house was far grander than any...", "1_20_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight eighteen in the evening</span>. I was on my way to see $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nIt was the second time I had seen a wall calendar in forty-eight hours. I was reminded that the second instalment of my thirty-nine-year reprieve from duty was expiring. I would turn forty-nine in two days\u2014and my volunteer work was almost over.\nThe Volunteer Civilian was not sorry. It had been a splendidly gaudy lark. It had been good to see America before mass media made it all look exactly the same. It had been good to kibitz with a God who had so obviously led us by the nose to just the conclusions we deserved to reach. And, finally, it had been good to go around the United Society of Believers in the Resurrection of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, wondering what on Earth they had to look so secretive about.\nThe big surprise was still to come. Whatever the whole thing was supposed to lead up to\u2014the climax of the incredible emotional and interplanetary journey\u2014still lay in the future. I had almost two days left to prepare for it.\nThe presentiment that I had some heavy moral accounting to do before the journey\u2019s end was confirmed. I asked myself\u2014for about the...", "1_1_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-four in the morning</span>. I looked up at Malibu Moon\u2019s body once again as we rolled out on to the street. I leaned down to the backseat and called out to him. \u201cHey, listen, thanks for the lift,\u201d I said. \u201cYou\u2019ve got a beautiful car. I appreciate it.\"\n\u201cHey, that\u2019s all right,\u201d he said. \u201cAny time you want to go for a ride, man, I\u2019m ready.\"\n\u201cHa ha ha,\u201d I laughed.\nIn the drug culture it was always dangerous to accept a lift from a stranger. And, of course, we were probably all totally insane. Contacts among the drug people were by no means as casual as they were among the beats of the \u201950s\u2014particularly in L.A., where everybody was hustling schemes, and money was always a factor. Every contact was significant. This maniac could be an undercover cop, for all I knew\u2014following me around for days. Or, more likely, this was just some idiot I\u2019d picked up in a bar...looking to sell me a 42 Briggs. \u201cWhat\u2019s your name?\u201d I asked...", "1_9_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. One minute to ten o'clock in the morning of August 8, 2307. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son $FIRSTNAMEB2, at the age of three Earthling years, was running around and around the interior of a spiral walk poised in a tower on the living-room floor of his mother\u2019s apartment.\nEverything was ready for his life\u2019s big adventure. He had all his lodesmen, firemen, postmen, and True Believers under his command. He had everything he owned or could call to his hand. And his mother, $FIRSTNAMEF0, a lovely glowing woman with the small statuesque head of the models in the museum, was standing by, beaming and applauding.\nIt was $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s first day out of his mother\u2019s sight\u2014 and the first day of his life that he could remember.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 ran and ran and ran around the spiral walk. He went down on his knees every ten revolutions, clapped his hands, bounced to his feet, and raced around ten times more. He paused in his running to chew abstractedly on a tasseled end of the green and gold tablecloth that was unfurled between his hands.\nHe was delighted with the...", "0_11_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-nine in the morning</span>. Aside from $SURNAME1 and his television crew, the mansion was occupied only by the gardener and the maid. Neither had ever been inside the vault. Both firmly believed that there was nothing in the vault but furnace equipment. They would rattle their fuses in December, making the gardener grumble, and they would report to the contractor, who would send an engineer from Schenectady, that the mansion had one hell of a mysterious hot-air system.\nSurely $SURNAME0 could have imagined nothing more intimidating to a caller than the sylvan peace of the scene in which $SURNAME1 now found himself. $SURNAME1 felt as though he, too, had discovered a forgotten, seldom-used back door into the mansion from which he was hopelessly excluded. $SURNAME1 felt as though he and his three-member film crew had committed some ghastly and shameful crime which the innocent denizens of the mansion could reveal at any moment by a mere flicker of their attention.\nOutside the vault, the gardener was clipping boxwood. A motor purred inside the house. The motor belonged to an electric rotisserie that rotated slowly in the kitchen over a bed of live coals. $SURNAME1 remembered the delicious smells that had tempted him on his initial exploration of the...", "2_16_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nMetempsychosis\nShe found her nextletter in the campus mailbox she used, the next night, arriving on her motor scooter. The outside temperatures had fallen allweek, there was even a touch of frost that night and she knew rain was on the way again. October in California is most tender like that, and $FIRSTNAMEF2 wondered how mornings further north, in the really out-there stretches like San Francisco, November might find Ralph or Rosa Caesar. She'd written Paranoy the address after asking J rotation of the Wharfinger archive; now the cryptographer returned her letter, week before last, by Federal Express, with a little ball-point pen attached, no invoice. His response was very short, just this:\nCONSTERNATION DEAR MRS. MASON,", "0_19_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to eight o'clock in the evening</span> and the fiesta was getting started. The formal bull-fight was not until next afternoon and all the details were not yet decided, but that did not matter. Things were shaping up. The details would be worked out at the bull-ring tomorrow and then everything would be ready.\nThere would be twenty-six bulls in the bull-fight under all the new rules, and the bulls had been bought and brought in from the woods on the walled estates in the country. The twenty-four bull-fighters were all in town, with the exception of the six who would be making their first appearance in the ring. Their contracts would be signed at the lyceum after the formal opening of the fiesta.\nIt was all very exciting. The day and the night belonged to the fiesta. There would be no emotions except the emotions delivered in the ring or in connection with the fiesta. Anything else would annoy the police. All day and all night one could think about the fiesta and talk about the fiesta, but there were things that would not be tolerated. They had just had theproblem of the flags. There was to be an official opening to the fiesta, with all the flags, and it was necessary to protect the flags from the...", "2_22_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earthling time, October 31, in the year of Our Ford 499. The place was the outskirts of Newport, the capital city of the planet Mercury. The place was a windswept meadow of blossoming and unblossomed space tulips.\nA collection of small Earthlings stood in the meadow. The tallest of them all was less than four feet ten inches in height. Collectively, the group was known as the Levelers. Collectively, the group was a saint, an architect, a college toad, a supermarket bag boy, a Mrs., a Mr., a bald-headed organ-grinder, a Mr. Miss, two Martians, two dogs, a parrot, and a man who didn\u2019t know who he was.\nThere was a cable overhead from which depended harnesses that creaked in the wind.\nThe harnesses were for the sixteen spacesuits, twelve Mars space suits, four Mercury spacesuits, in the meadow.\nA fan, a scrambler, and a demister made so much racket atop a dusty black portable radio truck that the caller standing beside the truck had to shout in order to be heard above the din, a shout that made his throat ache.", "1_19_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven o'clock in the evening</span>\u2014not dark yet. We had arrived in Juarez at about five in the afternoon. $FIRSTNAMEB3, who had come up from Guatemala on a banana boat, had already phoned Camille from the town and her brother Stan picked us up at the bus station with a rented car. Now he drove us to his comfortable, well-furnished home, all of us talking madly. Old Camille was like a musty-mothballed but still gorgeous Catholic Hedy Lamarr.* She sat there in the living room with her little kid when we stumbled in, gaunt and frail and pokerfaced, saying nothing. Stan had blond, Hollywoodish good looks, very polite and worried about what we might think of him and what we might do. He was Camille's husband. With them lived two more children, $FIRSTNAMEF4's niece and nephew, the son of Ed Dunkel, the child of Camille's dead sister whose husband Ed Dunkel had met in Max's Kansas City and followed to Mexico. All the kids ran around in the backyard yelling in Spanish, knowing we were borrachos and crazy Americans and therefore things to be poked at with sticks. The sun was setting and made a red flame of the smog that hangs forever over...", "0_9_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 stopped the automatic limousine at the curb beside the entrance of $SURNAME0\u2019s mansion.\n\"You needn\u2019t wait, $FIRSTNAMEB2,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes, sir?\" said the chauffeur.\n\"Go home.\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said the chauffeur.\n\"Say hello to your mother for me,\" said $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog got out of the limousine. They watched the limousine pull away. Then $SURNAME0\u2019s hand reached into his pockets, withdrew two twenty-dollar bills.\nHe handed the money to the man who stood by the limousine\u2019s broken door.\n\"You dropped your wallet, buddy,\" he said.\n\"Thanks, mister,\" said the yardman. He gave the case with the red death\u2019s-head key in it an amateurish shove under some shrubbery. Then he went off with his hand out, pretending to be a beggar.\nAides Help Willkie As Windows Dump Money on Wall Street\nNEW YORK, N. Y.\u2014Willington Finch Winthrop Rockefeller, Republican candidate for President today dumped a hundred thousand...", "1_5_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past five o'clock in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time, and Mercury was one hundred and sixty-seven million miles from home when $FIRSTNAMEB1 himself appeared on the televiewer screen in the parlor of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s boarding house.\nA command to yield had just been given the $SURNAME0 on a voice band in the control room of the Titanic. The command could not be denied.\nThe yacht tumbled slowly, rose aslant in its own smoke.\nThe rising ship plopped a cannon ball neatly into the crosshatched square at the feet of the gilded man who was its visionary.\nThe ball carried with it a scroll. The scroll was signed by the President of the United States, by the Prime Minister of Great Britain, and by the Premiers of the Republic of France, the Dominion of Canada, the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, and the People\u2019s Republic of China. The scroll invited a greatly beloved friend of all mankind to resume residence on Earth.\n\"Skip\u2014\" said the matron of the boarding house. \"Your wife\u2019s on the phone again.\"\n\"Oh, God damn it,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. He got up from his chair. He went shakily to the telephone in the foyer. \"Yeah?\"", "2_4_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-six in the morning</span>.\n\"My God,\" said the face on the television screen, \"look at this! This is \u2026 wonderful! Wilbur, what is this?\"\n\"Prize cattle,\" said Wilbur Mintcloud in Oxford. \"Prize cattle, Bob.\"\n\"In the television control room in $SURNAME0\u2019s ship,\" said the announcer, the tension of artistic integrity giving way to plain excitement, \"the little man at the switch sees something more marvelous by far: the entire universe of Earth is about to become afresh and most beautifully alive.\"\nThe room was terribly cold now. The tubes of the cameras were icing up. Tempers were short. The microphone kept popping off its hook.\nOnly the announcer was still having a wonderful time. \"Beautiful music, just before the play-by-play resumes,\" he said, grinning good-naturedly at the engineer. \"Go on outside, Hal\u2014stand in front of your balloon,\" he said to his timekeeper.\nChief of police Hart was the first observer to see what was going to happen. He gaped, astonished.", "0_10_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-two in the morning</span>. The young man lingered a moment in the doorway, surveying the room with calm lidded eyes, while the critical mass trembled on the verge of explosion. He was a large baby-faced man wearing a very wrinkled tan suit, apparently made out of the same material as a World War II copilot\u2019s flight suit, only in the nubby tweedy lightweight wool that had been fashionable among wealthy young men in the mid-1960s. The suit had big lapels with brass nameplates on them. The nameplates said something inaudible, but a quick glance suggested to $FIRSTNAMEF2 that it might be the name of a dry-cleaning shop.\nThe man\u2019s hair was combed straight back from his forehead, longer in front than at the back, and looked like sculpted fiberglass. More remarkably, he had delicate tow-hued eyebrows, and no facial hair of any kind. Considering the complexion the hair and eyebrows were associated with, $FIRSTNAMEF2 imagined that any stubble the young man would have in later life would show up clearly against the face\u2019s deep primal tan.\nSo much for looks. The overall impression was of somebody who did not know he was beautiful and...", "2_4_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nHis name was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nHis home was on a planet that swung around the sun of a yellow star.\nHis home was a parking lot named Rockland, just outside a ramshackle huddle of sandstone buildings in a town named Newport, Rhode Island.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s job was to spray the insides of Martian supply ships with deadly foamy fire extinguishers. The ships' crews, under the heavy influence of harmonium, never noticed what he did\u2014were never sure, after each flight, as to whether or not he had deceived them as he killed them.\nThat's the way it worked out.\nThe harmonium soaked up the suspicions of the crews, leaving them in a pleasant haze that toned the horrible out of existence. What the crews hated most in all the world was a surprise that turned out to be unavoidable.\nTheir ideal situation, their ideal home, their ideal life was an avoiding game of interlocking surprises. The best surprises were the most pleasant surprises that could be avoided.", "0_10_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was Tralfamadore. The place was a very strange room.\nThe room had no windows and no doors and no furniture.\nThe room was octagonal.\nThe walls of the room were blank, or very nearly blank. There were eight sooty marks on the walls where there had once been pictures.\nThere was nothing on the eight walls, except the eight marks. There were marks on the floor, too. There was a big cruciform mark in the floor that said, in schoolgirl French,\nHERE LIES RUBEEN O\u2019KUYPER, BELOVED DAUGHTER OF THOR AND VIOLA.\nThe walls were black, and so was the ceiling. The floor was brown linoleum.\nThe ceiling was high. A thousand feet high, maybe. And there was a light fixture in the ceiling with three bulbs in it. The light bulbs were blue. And they were as bright as football stadium lights.\nThe light in the room was as bright as sunlight on a snowbank.\nThere was nothing in the room but $FIRSTNAMEB4, the dog-thing, and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the man-god.\n$SURNAME0 was the...", "0_18_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, a little bathed and a little rested, was standing on the bow of his ship. He had a martini in one hand, a cigar in the other. He was enjoying the last few moments of peace and seclusion. He would have peace and seclusion no longer. It was time for him to go indoors and be $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 again.\n\"Bless our ship, oh, Lord, and these, thy passengers ...\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, on the dock, her Bible in her hand.\n\"Huh?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Amen,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Amen,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. She stood there waiting for him to say something else for the microphones.\n\"What microphone?\" said $SURNAME1 innocently.\n\"Oh\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, touching her throat. \"Didn\u2019t you know the network was covering the takeoff?\"\n\"We\u2019ve got a transmitter stashed aboard,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Rembrandt put it there. It seemed like a good idea, so we did it.\"\n\"Remmy\u2014?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Don\u2019t ask me about Remmy,\" said $SURNAME1 desolately. \"What does it matter now?\"\n\"", "1_5_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-four in the morning</span>. In less than an hour the police would arrive. She was not even sure this time if the manager would put her back in her apartment; last night had been the last straw. She had gallantly presented the bill to him at the desk. That did it. He looked pure evil at her. He had had enough, he said. They could lock her up if they wanted to, but he was going to have her out of that room if he had to call the police. The poor bastard--he was one of those passive types, like her ex-husband, who hates trouble and dreads confrontation\nBut of course the rotten bastard knew all along what she was up to, $FIRSTNAMEF2 realized on about the third martini. The scurvy Greek; the dirty doublecrossing falacious son of a bitch. Why else the note? Why the message that only a pure accident had saved her from revelation to her husband, why the whole pseudo-Masonic presence act. . . . The manager had been trying to hustle her out of the hotel for days.\nBeginning tomorrow, she would stay in motels along the route.\nAn hour from now the sun would rise in California; she would watch it from a rooftop parking lot.", "0_17_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-three in the afternoon</span> of October the sixteenth and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized once again on the grounds of the estate named by $SURNAME0 Wahser-hoak.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 was the first to materialize. He materialized in a small, dull, apparently bottomless pit about five feet wide and two feet across.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s materialization sprayed damp earth over a radius of seven yards.\nThe shift from non-existence to existence was too much for $FIRSTNAMEB4. His life was ended abruptly by the very thing that made him a materialization dog.\nHis heart stopped.\nSo senseless was the scrambling he did in the bottom of the pit, so far from his natural element, that for the last few seconds of his life there was a pocketful of contracurvity in him. Metal objects in his reach dug and scratched shapeless furrows in his skin. One paw was lengthened grotesquely as he tried to break his fall against the pit\u2019s walls.\nThere was nothing at all natural about the way $FIRSTNAMEB4 died.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 died peeing, and the urine was as red hot as the iron rivers of Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0...", "2_13_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-two in the afternoon</span>. The wind ran seams along the lips of $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 and fastened wild, blowing corks to their shirttails.\nThey both looked up, looked into the television cameras, and gave their best impression of people having a fine time in wild weather.\nThe time was one twenty-three.\n$SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 turned their backs to the wind, leaned their shoulders against the flank of a marble statue. The statue depicted a naked boy of about twelve. The boy was poised on one foot, was about to throw a javelin.\n\"Touching, isn\u2019t it?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Very,\" said $SURNAME1. \"What is it?\"", "1_5_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nNo man on Earth knew where John Barleycorn would be at three minutes past five that morning.\nNo man on Earth knew where one million grasshoppers would be at ten o'clock that morning.\nII\nThe Old Man and the Model T\nTHE next day at five minutes past twelve, John Barleycorn tucked his hair under his hat, emerged from Golden State Mansions, and walked off down Montgomery Street. I was waiting in the Ford, drumming and shivering. He got in. \u201cI'm all right,\u201d he said. \u201cLet's go for a drink.\u201d\n\u201cNow?\u201d\n\u201cYes, let's have a drink.\u201d\n\u201cBut don't you want to go back home and sleep? The contest is tomorrow.\u201d\n\u201cI've slept enough. We've got to have a drink before the drinking change.\u201d I backed the car out and we went over to Stockton.\nI was at sea. I didn't understand what was happening. I knew only that everything was changing, disintegrating magically into the dust and the old rags of the world, and that no magic would ever restore it to the way it was...", "1_13_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> when Old Salo, knowing that he was about to die, looked up into $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s face for the last time. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was going to kill him.\nSalo made a final request of $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nHe wanted to be remembered as a messenger between Old Salo and $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 promised to deliver the message as best he could.\nOld Salo asked one more small favor of $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014the privilege of being alone when he died.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 gave him the favor, and withdrew with his comrades-at-arms to a distance of twenty paces.\n\"Boy\u2014boy\u2014boy!\" whispered Old Salo.\nHis breathing grew ever more flutelike, ever more pitiful. The sound grew weaker, subsided.\nNever before had a man died so sadly on Mars.\nA serpentine whisper became the silence of the world.\nThen, somehow, the whistling began again, and the footprints appeared in iron around the boulder.\nThe iron began to sing an infinitely low, infinitely sweet song. The song a compass makes in the bosom of a ship as it seeks north.\n\"Oh\u2014 home\u2014\" whispered the iron. \"Oh\u2014 home\u2014\" whispered the iron. \"...", "1_0_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve nineteen ante meridiem</span> when we stepped out of the cold frontage of the Drug Store and hit the sidewalk in front of the hotel. Even with the sour taste of beer still sweet in our mouths and the gritty cigarette smoke not yet dissipated in our brains we felt keen and clean, as if we\u2019d just come in out of a good rain after a long, hard ride across the country in a hot summer.\nIn the lobby we said good night to the girls. Frances said she wanted to come to the race.\n\u201cIt\u2019s just the kind of thing I\u2019d like. Absolutely, I\u2019d like it.\u201d\n\u201cWell, you can\u2019t,\u201d I said firmly. \u201cWe\u2019ll be driving back to Denver that night.\u201d Terry stood next to me, taking it all in.\n\u201cIt isn\u2019t safe for girls at night,\u201d Ed said. \u201cToo many damned drunks.\u201d\n\u201cWe\u2019ll be all right. We\u2019ll have an escort,\u201d I said. \u201cWe have to go now.\u201d And so we left.\nIt was freezing hard. There was frost on...", "2_3_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-three in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Frederick, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 left the Space Museum in Newport, Rhode Island, and stepped outside, on the steps. Their three breaths turned to foggy question marks. The question asked:\nWhere, in what, or when, am I?\nIt is a classic of abnormal psychology that a sane man can conduct himself rationally in an insane world, but that an insane man has never met a reasonable situation that he didn\u2019t reduce to his own image in three minutes or less.\nThere are a hundred explanations for this, but perhaps the simplest is that it takes considerable insight and courage to maintain the reality of the images composing one\u2019s world, and that most of us go through life imagining we are in a meadow or on a spaceship or in the middle of a saga, when actually we are in a padded cell or on a deserts or in the middle of a stereotypes.\nThe truth is deformed so quickly by a maladjusted person that it can be reconstructed only by another and more maladjusted person. The truth is so soft and dewy that ordinary people leave footprints all over it in passing.", "2_5_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-seven in the morning</span>. The hotel desk was closed\u2014but the clerk was leaning out of a window, watching the show. I gave him a nice smile and some green American money. \u201cBreakfast,\u201d I said. \u201cA goddamn big breakfast. Right away.\"\n\u201cRight away, sir! Yes sir!\" The clerk checked me in and ran off toward the coffee shop half a mile down the highway. Then he came back and took my big black leather bag. \u201cI\u2019ll keep an eye on this for you, sir. Right here at the desk. Yes sir!\"\n\u201cFine,\u201d I said. \u201cFine.\u201d", "0_6_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-four in the morning</span>. The taxi, still carrying us east, moved like a space-ship through the absolute darkness of a perfect Californian night. And suddenly in the absolute darkness of this perfect Californian night a thing about eleven feet tall and shaped like a fireplug materialized and started walking toward us down the left lane of the freeway. It was coming from somewhere beyond the Marina district and walking, apparently unconcerned, directly into the light of our headlights. It was a cop. We passed him at about eighty-five miles an hour with the windows open, and here is what he sounded like: Dunnnnnn. Dunnnnn. Dunnnnn. Dunnnnn.\nWe had no idea of our speed because the red Mazda speedometer only made it to a hundred. But our goal now was Salt Lake City, and I had memorized the map well enough to realize that the next fast opening in the mountains was Fort Bridger, handed to us on the far left just as the dawn opened its eyelids transatlantic red. By the time we got there it was 7:30 and our suffering had turned serious. We were badly hungover, hungry, thirsty, and sleepy. We needed cigarettes. There was no sound up front. Occasionally the Mole would report on...", "0_21_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt fifty-two minutes past nine o'clock in the evening, Robert Pripps and his wife were going to be dead\u2014if nothing happened between now and then.\n\"Nineteen-hundred hours\"\u2014that is to say, seven o'clock in the evening\u2014\"is the estimated time base of the attack,\" said Stoney.\nA whistle blew outside.\nA klaxon began to hoot.\nThis meant that personnel in the Space Weapon Research building should take cover in the nearest available space-weapon research room.\nEven Skip remained motionless.\nEven $FIRSTNAMEF3 Kabogo did not move a muscle.\nAnd Stoney was paralyzed by concern over his cherished experimental animals.\nThe three men despised animals, and instinctively protected themselves from the expected destruction.\nOutside their windows, the lift-tube cage continued to rise. It carried $FIRSTNAMEF3 Kabogo and Skip Wiley and Wilma Jill tighten Schmuck. It bore them up and out of the building, up, up, up.\nThe three members of the space-weapon project stayed at their desks until the lift-tube cage stopped and they had filed into a bombproof shelter ten feet underground.\nThe tube then closed again.\nThe dangers of a direct encounter with...", "2_12_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past noon</span>.\nThe breath that $SURNAME0 had held was still held.\n$SURNAME0 nodded sympathetically. \"Gee\u2014\" he said, \"that\u2019s a tough break.\" He put his thumbs against his temples, as though trying to ease his own headache. \"You know, friend,\" he said, \"you\u2019re the first man I\u2019ve executed who was actually innocent.\n\"You weren\u2019t a threat to society\u2014you didn\u2019t take a penny that wasn\u2019t your own. You weren\u2019t a bad guy. And now I\u2019ve got to fry you, unless you want to tell me about your secret. You\u2019ve got about forty seconds to decide.\"\nAlmost imperceptibly, $SURNAME0 shook his head. \"I\u2019ve used up more than my share of luck\u2014 using it up as far as you\u2019re concerned,\" he said. \"Nothing I could say would make you tell me your secret. I don\u2019t believe in forcing people.", "1_13_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-four in the afternoon</span>, Thursday, October 11, 1984, and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was materializing again.\nThere was to be another of those marvelous, clean spectacles in which a man materializes out of thin empty air. The extraordinary suggestion of this feat lay in the fact that a very large, handsome pale-blue villa at the foot of Titan Mountain, Newport, Earth, was a part of the materialization. It was as though a bit of far-flung suburbia on a treeless, ruined world had been materialized intact. The house, the garden, the fountain all shared, somehow, in the dignity of $SURNAME0\u2019s appearance. When he appeared, it did seem that one moment nothing wore the aspect of things more than the smoking, stinking basalt of the verge, and the next moment $SURNAME0 and his mates stood there, with the pale-blue villa and green fountain.\nThe three mates stood with $SURNAME0. They were $FIRSTNAMEF1 and herself and the young chrono-synclastic infundibulated boy, $FIRSTNAMEB2. They were holding hands in a circle, and the circle itself made a fourth hand of spiraling mist. The dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, ran in a crazy circle around their...", "1_1_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-eight in the morning</span>. The Flange had closed at one. DiMaggio\u2019s was dark inside. So was the fountain. All was shadow, lighted only by starlight, foam streaks of a streetlight leaking around a contorted tree. Lenz came out carrying his shoes in his hands and put them on, inspecting the insides for ants.\nHe leaned back against the trunk of the tree, thirty feet from the fountain, picking his teeth with a safety pin. His chestnut hair was, consistently and miraculously, in place.\nHis attitude was one of patient expectancy. Time, death and taxes notwithstanding, it would be an interesting night. His only reflected shadow was a tiny dirigible, rising and falling slowly over the grass.\nThe shadow was suddenly and violently broken by an ant as big as a cocker spaniel.\nThe ant bore down on the dark figure against the dark tree. Obviously, Lenz knew it was there. He continued to stare at the fountain. Had he uncoiled a fist and taken a swing, he could have knocked the ant to Cincinnati. It didn\u2019t discourage him. He continued to pick his teeth.\nAn unusually large cricket hopped onto Lenz\u2019s shoulder. And stayed.", "0_4_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1 died broke. $SURNAME1 had one dollar in the entire world\u2014one dollar, which he had hidden behind a mirrored panel in the bathroom of his suite in the Beverly Carlton.\nHe left it there, and it was still there five months later in the spring of 1971 when new faces began to appear at 4949 WestIZedale.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB1, and Kelp lived through the approach, fatality, and withdrawal of the Wagnerian apparatus on Mars.\nThey were spared all the the terrors of life on asteroidal Icarus, where the soldiers were forced to dig with hand pumps and carry water in goatskins.\nAnd they were spared all the the titanic earth-grabbing operations on Mercury, where the soldiers were forced to wear iron underwear, were carried aloft in howling shuttles, were sealed in airtight caissons, handled and set in place by remote control.\nThe three survivors were conveyed neatly from Earth to Mercury and from Mercury to Mars. They were even given souvenirs of the exciting journey from Earth to Mercury\u2014the iron underwear.\nFrom Mercury to Mars, they rode a clean and comfortable space ship, equipped with toilets, radios, and wine racks. And they actually had the run of...", "0_20_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-five in the evening</span>. The story of the ship, which had been so rich, so full of life, was done. No more would ever be printed in the pages of The $SURNAME1 Star. The quiet rumble of engines telling the towers on Mercury and the few men who watched the tower that a ship was passing gave way to a more exciting rumble. The $SURNAME0 out of $FIRSTNAMEB2 made its historic flyby with a bit less than one minute to spare before it backfired on its orbit and became the unpredictable body known in Earthling circles as $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nHe came flying out of nowhere in the middle of his own golden cloud, within which he had been hidden all the while he was aboard ship. The expression on his face was genially supercilious\u2014as though he were deigning to interrupt his important business elsewhere in order to give Earthlings something to look at.\nHis flyby was perfection itself.\nHe had his hands on his knees, his feet were braced, his body was bent. It looked as though an invisible hose had suddenly spurted a hard stream of air against him. It flattened him, nipped him up.\nHe disappeared into the cloud of an entering interplanetary ship half a mile up the track,...", "0_7_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>. The sun rose over New York at seven twenty-seven. The attack on the moon bisector, which had lasted three minutes and twelve seconds, took place at seven thirty-four.\nIt was quarter past eight o'clock in the morning.\nToo bad the timekeeper was a cold, his heart dead. He would have jubilantly cried out, \"But I have beaten him! I actually have beaten Old Red Eye!\"\nHe did not think of himself as an important person. But he deserved more than the contempt with which Fate regarded him.\nOnly he did not know this yet.\nWhen the timekeeper learned that death was coming to save him from humiliation, he would think that he was dreaming. There would be peace in his last thoughts.\nAnd then, the death arrive in twelve seconds.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and the Newtonian scholar were oblivious to the groans of the dying Chronedes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was looking for the spatial coordinates of a place called Newport, Rhode Island, U.S.A. He found it on the west coast of the North American Continent. It was adjacent to Narragansett Bay.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wanted to go to Newport. He was going to take his friend there, so that his...", "0_19_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-five in the evening</span> in Roseville, California. The Saturnian spacecraft was still less than half an hour from its rendezvous with Chronos. Except for $FIRSTNAMEF1, $SURNAME0, and the Space Wanderer, the Titanic pair were still wearing spacesuits.\nThey were standing on the mile-long concourse of the heliographic tower.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s attitude toward $SURNAME0 had changed. It had ripened from capitulation to adoration. She had capitulated to $SURNAME0 because she believed him to be in every way her intellectual and spiritual superior. But that had turned out to be a matter of opinion.\nIt had now become a matter of fact.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 had ripened to adoration because $SURNAME0 judged the young stranger to be $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic infundibulated\u2014and because $SURNAME0 himself was apparently willing to be incinerated in order to save that young stranger\u2019s life.\n\"Skip,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"if you say so, I do it.\"\n\"So do it, Lady,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Paint my message to the world on the side of this heliostat.\" He thought for a moment. \"Tell them I died happy\u2014telling them that I have never...", "0_3_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three eighteen in the morning</span> and rain blurred the windows. The boy stretched and yawned. \u201cGod! I'm sleepy,\u201d he said.\n\u201cWell, go to sleep,\u201d said Ed. \u201cThere's room for two, if anybody wants to sleep.\u201d\n\u201cI don't feel like sleeping,\u201d said the boy.\nStan sat in the back with me and Lee. The boy folded up his coat and put it under his head and snuggled towards Ed and began talking about his troubles.\n\u201cA sweet guy,\u201d said Stan.\n\u201cWe ought to call a cop,\u201d said Ed. \u201cThere must be a law against having a minor like that in a public place.\u201d\n\u201cYou're right,\u201d said Stan. \u201cI hope we don't all come out of this with typhoid.\u201d We wanted to go to sleep, but the boy kept it up, with his sad grievances. Ed grabbed a piece of newspaper and rolled himself in it and set with his eyes closed and waited. He waited two hours. The boy went on talking.\n\u201cIf Gregory'll come I'm ready to go to Denver, man.\u201d\nAt...", "2_6_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>, East Coast Time. There came the sounds of the staff sneaking down the outside corridor past Room 223. Scurrying feet. The jangling of keys. Rumbles and bangs from the freight elevator, bringing down the day\u2019s supply of towels and lemon oil and toilet paper and glasses.\nColdly and impassively, as always, the thin, scrawny angel continued to saw his wings.\nA good throat-clearing from outside Room 223. Then a high, quavery voice, amplified by a bullhorn: \"$SURNAME1? $SURNAME1?\"\nThe name hung in the air for an instant, a soft, powdery haze.\nSign up for $FIRSTNAMEM1 Pudge\u2019s\nVision-ology Course by\nDialing W\u2014\u2014\u2014 7\u2014\u2014\u2014\nAt the signal, please deposit ten cents!\nPriceless knowledge will pour out of the phone into\nyour ear holes!\nDon\u2019t delay!\nFine young cheer leaders cannot be with you always!\nThey, too, must grow old!\nCurtain down! Lights out!", "0_22_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 wasn't picking up any loud signals on his special radio. One of his antennae rubbed Junior\u2019s belly.\nThe antenna was bare. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had felt so lonely and spent that he had just rubbed off all his fur. Now the brown skin of his antenna was rough and scraping.\nSwinburne moaned. He lay on a bed in the cabin of the long-haul ship\u2014a feather bed, and a good one, for the ship was doing well. The skipper had had nothing to spare for his crew, but he had kept a reserve of pillows, which he sold only to passengers.\nSwinburne groaned as he turned uneasily. His face was white, and his hands yellow, and there was bile at the corners of his mouth.\n\"To be born was very far bowel of you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2 to him.\n\"Yes\u2014very,\" murmured Swinburne. He closed his eyes and presently began to snore.\n\"Nevertheless I am grateful to you,\" $FIRSTNAMEB2 said. \"You have been interesting\u2014in your perverted fashion. The rest of us are just robots, but you have been a man about town\u2014and this is thanks to...", "0_4_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span> when young $FIRSTNAMEB2 streaked through the shaded darkness to the $SURNAME0 estate. He carried two messages. He had been given thirteen thousand four hundred and ninety-six dollars, one hundred and fifty-four cents in twenty- dollar bills, by Abraham Lincoln Marchesko, to deliver this money and these messages.\nOn his way to the estate, $FIRSTNAMEB2 ran into an aide to the President named Mr. Dwine.\nMr. Dwine was a veteran of the war in Spain. He had been chief of the United States Information Agency, and had just been appointed Under Secretary of the Interior. $FIRSTNAMEB2 liked him.\n\"You $FIRSTNAMEB2?\" said Mr. Dwine.\n\"Yes, suh,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nMr. Dwine in the darkness held up the two messages stiffly between extended forefinger and thumb. \"One of these goes to the boss, and one of them goes to his wife.\"\n\"You understood, suh?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"Never put one message in two places at once.\" This was the law of the Agency.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, with his stop watch and his list of times, knew exactly what was going to happen. Nothing was going to happen to him that hadn...", "0_23_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven seventeen in the evening</span>. The rumble-bed was in an apartment on the eighth floor of a house at 216 East Capitol Street, Richmond, Virginia. When the brakes had been put on and himself and the co-pilot, a man named $SURNAME2, had got out, they found they were in a neighborhood of comfortable old homes. It was Capitol Hill. 216 was a rambling, shingled house, freshly painted. A radio was playing rock music inside. Halfway up the walk they met a young man of their own age, who had come out to see who the strangers were in his tenderloin street. He wore hornrimmed spectacles, a brown gabardine suit, and a teetotal pin in his buttonhole.\nAlfred Lithium Burnham. Unmarried. No children. Earned a good living manufacturing safety pins, aluminum ladders, brassieres, and other necessities in a Northern town. Enjoyed an excellent reputation as a fair man and sane except on one point. He spent most of his leisure time arguing that all the major technological inventions of the age had been possible before the particular date on which they actually appeared in the public press, and that their development had been deliberately suppressed by persons and forces known only...", "1_19_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>, and I had come quite a distance on this exhausting system-walking, jumping ditches, hurdling rows of vegetables, wading through canals, climbing over fences. Every mile, on the average, I had been hit on the head by a stone, and I was carrying several loose teeth in my mouth-the canals had been full of stones-and along the way I had met many people, who, on top of everything else, had leered at me as I staggered by. All of them except two were Americans. My loafers were coming apart and my shirt had been ripped in several places-all of this for travel, for the joy and madness of travel-and the first thing to do on getting to a town was to find a secluded alley and change my shirt, putting on a fresh one from the bundle of shirts I carried around with me.\nOld Trask was standing at the door waiting for me. \u201cCome on in and eat,\u201d he said, licking his iron lips.\nIn his house it was dark and absolutely quiet. I could hear the soft padding of his bare feet on the bare floor. It was a one-story dwelling. Everything was extremely neat. He had an amazing garden. It was...", "2_17_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five eighteen in the afternoon</span>.\nAugustine remained lost in the caves and tunnels of sleep for a further hour and forty-four minutes. One of the minor systems of a large computer was installed in the basement of $SURNAME0\u2019s Palace. This system, nicknamed $FIRSTNAMEB2, synchronized all the clocks on Mars. Whenever it synchronized two clocks, both showed the exact time.\nAt six-thirty, $FIRSTNAMEB2 shut down. One particular device within it released a spray of freon gas and snapped to attention. The part that snapped to attention had a red ivory plaque on it, and on the plaque was the legend CONSTANTINESCO. The system strengthened itself for the task ahead.\nIt sent gleaming coppery fingers up the legs of Constantinesco\u2019s pajamas, up the arms of Constantinesco\u2019s bathrobe, up the back of Constantinesco\u2019s head, and flicked a speck of dandruff from one shoulder and onto the other.\nThe system moved Constantinesco, and took him gently to the bathroom.", "1_13_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nThe animal looked hungry, so I gave it all the food.\nI wandered around, trying to decide what to do next. There was the lecture, but I did not feel much like going.\nIt was very hot.\nBY THE TIME I got to the Hotel Mew Mexico the lecture was practically over. I had a drink, and then I walked in. The lecture was being given by a gynecologist from Fort Worth, Texas.\nHe was talking about venereal disease. He was using a colored chart. He had a pointer.\nAt each thing he said that was interesting he would leave his pointer on the chart and peer around the room. During the slow periods he talked to a couple of people in the front row and tried to make them understand that there was absolutely nothing to be ashamed of in discussing venereal disease.\nI stood silently at the back of the room until he finished. Then I introduced myself, saying that we had been telegraphed for, and gave a brief account of my adventures since.\n\u201cDo you think both of us are needed?\u201d I asked, when I was through.\n\u201cYou never can tell,\u201d said my attorney. \u201cWe...", "2_16_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. In the restroom of the Gas 'n' Go there was a standoff. The killer wouldn\u2019t leave, and nobody'd call the cops; it was nobody\u2019s business but $FIRSTNAMEF2\u2019s. The killer sat on the bowl with his sawed-off trained on $FIRSTNAMEF2 while she sat on the sink. The child tucked her bare feet into her mother\u2019s lap, making a castle to hide in; the mother smoothed the child\u2019s hair and kept an eye on the stranger. $FIRSTNAMEF2 massaged her forehead, trying to remember any kind of clue. The stall went around and around.\n\u201cLady,\u201d said the killer, \u201cI\u2019m not the one killed your boy. If I was you I\u2019d be looking to Kansas, not here to the John.\u201d\n\u201cWhy,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF2, \u201cis somebody in Kansas responsible?\u201d\n\u201cBecause,\u201d said the killer, \u201cnobody but a crazyMexicano would do a thing like that.\u201d", "1_9_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after nine o'clock in the morning</span>, and I was sitting in $FIRSTNAMEB5's bath-tub, practically afloat in hot water, with a bad attack ofcontriteulismo. The water was uncomfortable hot. At intervals I put up my toes, tilting forward to rest them on the edge of the bath-tub. Then I took another drink of the Fundador, which seemed rather poorly adulterated with water. Then I gargled again. I felt very bad. I had a broken nose and a hypertrophied lip, and I was soggy and wrinkled from the night's steam bath. I looked at the bath-tub with a detached, analytic interest It was an attractive bath-tub, large, white enameled and obviously expensive. Idly I wondered who had planned the voluptuous hot-and-cold shower at the other end of the bath-room. It was most effectively planned, so that the cold water spurted away from you when you opened the hot and vice versa. I had been up to it twice, much too interested to notice that the shower had a removable head to which the hot-water pipe was fastened. I had had a cold shower, so there was some way of making it hot. I got up, detached the head of the...", "2_10_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span> when private first class Belskutt, who had just finished digging the latrine, policing the area, filling the water canteens, pitching two tents, building two fires, and acting as sentry at the entrance to the battalion\u2019s fallout shelter, helped a noncommissioned officer named Hframe fasten his puttees.\nIt was ten minutes after ten o'clock when private first class Belskutt pedaled his military bicycle on its training stand, out in the post yard. He practiced pedaling at a rapid cadence against a wind of seventy-three miles an hour, screaming at the top of his voice to be heard above the wind while he pumped the pedals.\nIt was twenty-two minutes after ten o\u2019clock when the battalion commander, Lieutenant Colonel R. M. Waddell, turned onto Belskutt Street from Thurmond Street. He was traveling in a 1943 Plymouth staff car, riding in the front seat with the driver. A noncom in the back of the car was trying to start a wood-handled Bakelite radio, with no success.\n\"Try it in reverse!\" yelled the lieutenant colonel.", "0_6_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-three in the morning</span>. The taxi was shaking empty fields of broken glass, that are found scattered over the flat desert sand of Southern Los Angeles. The driver was a young, talkative fellow all excited about the Korean War; he had too much energy to sit still in the house. He said, \u201cMost people are lucky if they can go to bed by six o'clock; they got to get up at seven, you know? I mean, your average person, he's going to be sleeping till eight, nine o'clock, you know? I wish I could go to bed like that; man, I'd sleep nine hours.\u201d He shook his head. \u201cSay, aren't you sleepy? I never seen anybody as sleepy as you; your eyes are closed, I swear -- holy!\u201d I made my face get straight and sat up. \u201cYou're right,\u201d I said, \u201cand I never wanted anything so much as to be able to go to first sleep the way you do.\u201d He laughed. \u201cI hope we get a fare; these hours'll kill us -- or I might kill myself driving, you know?\u201d We drove on to Los Angeles.\nAcross the continent the railroad tr...", "0_15_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three seventeen in the afternoon</span>. Galaxie Four Twenty-nine, driven by Stan M. Holmes, had just picked up three passengers south of Rapid City, South Dakota, and had roared through the Badlands in a gallant Viennese success of badlands mysticism\u2014the Mississippi of the North. The passengers were a mother and two small boys. As soon as they were aboard, Holmes accelerated the big tattered ship to 86 in what looked like only a moment or two. The old woman was holding on to the two frightened little white boys, who were trying to sit like cowboys. We swooped around curves, dashed by signs welcoming us to NEBRASKA, pounded on. Holmes was a young, somewhat African-looking driver. With his white smile, black mustache, snap-brim hat, colored shirt, and friendly manner he would have made a perfect driver in Alabama circa 1937. His license hung around his neck with a ticket punch. It was a verdict of some kind. Probably everything he did was illegal. From time to time he\u2019d look up in the rear-view mirror and grin. \u201cAin\u2019t this fun, folks?\u201d he\u2019d say. \u201cI sure can make this ol\u2019 Hudson go...", "2_13_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nThe number of recruits that mounted the stairs can easily be deduced. There were thirty in the party. There were thirty pink footprints on the black carpet leading up from the landing place.\nThe building was surprisingly quiet and beautiful. There was absolutely nothing military about it. The architecture of the staircase lent itself to halts and landings and a sudden, straight flight to the top. The recruiting officer was almost childlike in his awe and pride as he showed it to the new recruits.\nThey went unhurriedly up the stairs. They stopped. They admired the architecture.\nAt the top of the staircase there was a suit of armor\u2014smooth-polished and pretty as a girl, and frightening because it was so much bigger than life.\nFrom a niche in the suit of armor darted a human eye as we moved.\nThe new recruits took that in as they moved quietly through a portico and into the dungeon-quiet corridors of the second floor.\nThe corridors were lined with oil paintings.\nThe paintings looked like good paintings to the new recruits, but the recruits didn\u2019t know anything about painting. They hadn\u2019t been to art school.", "2_1_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe gold watch in $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s pocket said a quarter of two.\nIt was over\u2014everything.\nThe surly crew dropped the stuff beside the pier and scampered back aboard the yacht. The engines whined, and the yacht was gone in a smoggy orange flash, heading due west.\nOnly $SURNAME1 could be seen on the dark and empty dock. Miss Newport was gone. The yacht was gone.\nThe sun went behind a spur of the western mountains. Spray from Kancamagus Falls began to silver the air.\nThe show was over.\nIt had lasted a mere twenty minutes.\n$SURNAME1 looked at his watch again. By its luminous dial and hands he learned that the watch was telling the truth.\nIt was two o\u2019clock in the morning.\n$SURNAME1 laughed hollowly. As far as he could tell, nobody had won, nobody had lost. He had risked a great deal to gamble with fate, but fate had given him exactly what he deserved.\nWhen good old reliable $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 laughed hollowly again, the laughter was carried away on a wind that smelled as sweet and rotten as ozone laced with benzene.", "0_11_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was twenty-four years old. He had been a soldier for almost seven Earthling years. \"$FIRSTNAMEB0, like every other soldier, pretended that he was immortal. Like every other soldier, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had found the knack of doing without thought whatever it was that seemed easiest and most obvious to do.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wasn\u2019t paralyzed by the nightmare goings-on around him. $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t throw up when his memory delivered to him the contents of a photograph he had once seen. The photograph had appalled him. It had been a photograph of a soldier being flamethrowered in a trench.\nThe flames and the soldier were in exactly the relationship to each other as the flames and $FIRSTNAMEB0 were in the relationship to each other now.\nIn the old photograph as in the new, the soldier was apparently unable to move. $FIRSTNAMEB0, the living soldier, was in precisely the same condition then as the man in the photograph had been then.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had seen the photograph on the wall of a Space Academy lecture hall, had looked at it with the eye of a naturalist or an entomologist gazing at an insect in an evidence case. The difference between $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the man in the photograph was that...", "2_20_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe signals from Mars had lapsed again into will-o\u2019-the-wisp. The planet that had overrun the Sun was now silvery again, and hidden by the filters superimposed on the television screen.\nBy some fluke of spectator psychology, the thousands of spectators in the Colosseum were at the peak of their tension again. The crowd needed only the promptings of $SURNAME0 to become a couple of million raving wolves again.\n\"Oh, $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"if you mean that you think something wonderful is going to\u2014to\u2014\" She had to stop and catch her breath. Without a word from $SURNAME0, the wonderful thing was about to begin again.\n\"I wish you\u2019d stop it!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nOne second before nine o\u2019clock in the evening, time stopped again.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, the good dog, made a startled noise and looked in terror from his master to his mistress and from his mistress to his master.\n\"Good boy,\" said $SURNAME0. He patted $FIRSTNAMEB2 on the head, handed $FIRSTNAMEF1 her belongings. He did not offer to help her with her shawl again.", "1_23_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty in the evening</span> when I went out to Las Vegas Boulevard. The clouds in the south were piled like thunderheads and the neon of the Strip was blurred by sudden gusts of wind. In front of the casino I had my car parked there was a big fat cop with a club hanging on a leather thong. I showed him my ticket.\n\u201cIt's better not to drive around here at night,\u201d he said.\n\u201cI know,\u201d I said. \u201cBut I have to bring my car back for the valet to park.\"\n\u201cIf you're going to take a long trip, it's better to leave it here overnight.\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d I said. \u201cThat's probably true.\u201d\n\u201cWell, drive careful. Watch out for the drunks flying around here, goddamn drunks. Don't you hate to work at night, Pat?\u201d\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Pat. \u201cI like it. That's the same thing with me.\u201d\nI drove up to North Las Vegas with the radio news:\n'QUAKE STRIKES SOUTHERN CHILE IN THREE PLACES; DAMAGE, DES...", "1_3_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-three in the morning</span>. The place was a curious glass-lined room that was a section of a much larger space, a hangar for the Special. All the lights in the room had been burnt out awhile back. The only illumination came now from generators in the larger space. Lenore saw only a sort of bright shimmer in the glass that held the room in.\nThe men in the room were in dark suits with good shoes and hats, dark glasses and briefcases. They had rung a service bell mounted on a post by a few boxes of light bulbs and glass tubing. They now stood around the counter of the service desk, pulling at earlobes and shifting from foot to foot and hunching their shoulders and looking at their watches and jingling loose change in their pockets.\nThe women in the room were in white, with white lacy things around their throats, and white shoes and white leather handbags, and their hair was jet black and surrounded with glowy white haloes in the dark shadow of the glass walls.\nThe women sat on the counter of the service desk, crowded in, and the leather of their shoes squeaked with little rubs on the enamel of the counter as they shifted their positions to make room for others. Some of the...", "0_4_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe time that the Titanic struck the iceberg was approximately three-quarters of an hour before the time at which the man\u2014who had already sneaked up behind eight of twelve men who, together, became the witnesses of the miracle of $SURNAME0\u2019s Resurrection\u2014came floating up behind the tenth and last man who was to be the last witness. The Titanic had been struck at eleven-forty-five P.M.\nThe eleventh-to-the-last man was named Saltonstall.\nWhile $FIRSTNAMEF1, upstairs, plotted the ruin of herself and her husband, Reverend $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, Saltonstall was dreaming gustily, his chin on his fist, his gaze out the Titanic\u2019s porthole. Saltonstall was a petty tyrant, inflated to bursting by a fanatical conviction of his personal divinity. He controlled an Army, the Saltonstall Greenback Armed Party, that had been released by the President as an experiment in liberty, and had made its headquarters in the shambles of Buffalo, New York. Saltonstall had had his own great days in this Western New York shambles.\nThe Saltonstall Greenback Army was the only private army to be enlisted,...", "1_5_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-one in the morning</span>. In a few more minutes, the Committee to Re-Elect the President would be officially opening its three-day convention at the Vegas Convention Center. There was, however, still no sign of life from the Center\u2014no crowds, no colors, nothing. The billboards were all dark. The committee must have decided at the last minute to hold its opening ceremony somewhere else. Maybe it had taken one look at the Convention Center and realized what a terrible mistake it had made in selecting Vegas for its convention. Before his resignation, General Edwin Walker had repeatedly warned the committee about Vegas.\nMy attorney agreed. \u201cDon\u2019t you follow politics?\u201d he asked. \u201cWe\u2019re the only Western industrial nation that commits the ultimate sacrilege\u2014we let our heads be turned by a country cesspool.\u201d He shook his fist at the huge white xanadu across the highway. \u201cUp in the penthouse of that fucker they made the decisions that ruined our plains...all because some nerd with half a brain and a full bank account told them what would be fashionable. They had nothing to say in their own defense\u2014disgusting.\u201d He sighed. \u201cI feel betrayed.\u201d Then he came around...", "0_15_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-three in the afternoon</span>, Eastern War Time, and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog were materializing on the very center of the great spiral macadam avenue in the estate\u2019s inner-most circle\u2014the spot where two vastly decaying spiral arms converged. This was the grandest spot on the estate, and even in its present sorry state was a showy spectacle.\nCombat boots caked with dried mud came into being beneath raven-feathered trouser legs. A military officer\u2019s greatcoat, with its eleven bronze buttons, came into being over the boots and the suit of army underwear beneath.\nA dull iron ring the size of a saucer came into being on the middle finger of the right hand. The ring was attached to a thong which was itself attached to a shank of speckled crow meat. The macabre adornment reposed uneventfully on the gaunt, freckled finger.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s dog became visible also\u2014became visible and began barking happily. The instrument through which its thoughts were made manifest was an iron pot that had languished on the kitchen stove of a derelict mansion since the estate\u2019s custodians had run out of Beefaroni. The pot became...", "1_11_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of November 11, 1947, according to my Breguet watch, the property of the Counter Intelligence Corps of the United States Army, and issued to me by the commanding general, not in symbolism of the mysteries of eternity, but rather as a symbol that I had served the Corps in many climes and places too numerous to catalog. The current time and place, on a marginally dry corner of a stone plaza in Casablanca, was more than ten degrees below zero, as near as my watch could tell. I put the watch back into my very warm, and now damp, wool hunting shirt and scratched my chest, under the fine Algerian wool suit that had served me well in many seasons, of places too numerous to catalog. The wrist of my left hand was now truly captive in a slim but tight platinum shackle the property of a boy named Michael Tolliver. A series of dull but heavy exploding sounds from the north indicated that our homeland, the United States of America, was continuing a business as usual that was indistinguishable from lunacy. From the south came sounds that were more sinister\u2014little squeaks and thunks of ordinance burning its way into structures that were themselves distant and indistinct. There were at least two distinct explosions each accompanied by many smaller ones...", "1_15_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The sun was moving down the sky. The air in the burned house was now cool.\nNEXT day at ten minutes before five in the evening, a small boy discovered $FIRSTNAMEB0, still half-asphyxiated, under an apple tree in the back yard of his own house.\nThe boy had, for some reason, chosen to climb the apple tree. While he was clambering about, he saw the piece of dirty burlap that had been tied over $FIRSTNAMEB0's face to stifle his death agonies. He also saw $FIRSTNAMEB0. His reaction was primitive\u2014it was a child\u2019s reaction:\nHe screamed.\nHis mother and father ran out, scolded him, then dismissed him as worthless. The boy pointed mutely to the body, and backed away, wishing he could disappear too.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0's mate was the one who came to the dead man's assistance. $FIRSTNAMEB0's mate had suspected that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was dead. Now she knew it. She put her mind in order, that her grief might be bearable.\nWith infinite tenderness, she cleaned the dirt and leaves from $FIRSTNAMEB0's face, and saw to it that the piece of burlap was not crumpled again.", "0_15_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The coup d'\u00e9tat was over.\nThe conquering heroes came boiling out of the past, out of their burrows in the river bank and out of their shops, and old buildings, and put their flags out and shouted, and shook hands, and embraced, and got drunk, and got on horses, and rode.\nAnd, soon, there was order every-where.\nAnd, before night, the Royal Maestral Army Corps and the rebel army were one army and had appointed a commanding general.\nAnd so, a messenger fetched Helmholtz out of the inn and took him to the palace and ushered him into the throne room, where he found $SURNAME0 kneeling alone before the closed iron shutters of the throne. There, on the golden throne, lay the body of his wife, Constantella.\n\"I am sorry, Mr. $SURNAME0\u2014\" said Helmholtz.\n\"I should have been sorry long ago,\" said $SURNAME0 from behind his handkerchief. He was still in his bathrobe, still red and wet and shaking. He hadn\u2019t shaved, had barely been outdoors, had seen few people. \"You might as well see me as I am, Mr. Smith\u2014\" he...", "1_21_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-two in the evening</span>, and we were on our way back. The bride and groom had just driven away. A baby was crying in one of the houses. Church bells were ringing in the town. The people were eating their dinners. Terry and Stan were in their hotel room. Jane and I were on our way to her house. It was almost a new moon, and the road was just visible, twisting up the mountain and into the trees that grew on the inaccessible height where Jane lived.\n\u201cPull to the right, Jake, the right!\u201d\nWe turned off the road and bumped along a country path for fifteen minutes, behind Jane. Her silhouette bounced high in the sky, on strange, beetling, sky-reaching stone and rock hills. Her house was on a strange, primeval plateau that looked down over strange, veiled, moonlit, far-off worlds, out of which rose the pure exhalations of night.\n\u201cHow wonderful! How beautiful!\u201d shouted Jane.\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cThis country!\u201d\nWe sat there and looked out of our own fields at misty worlds and far-off, silent ones and never-ending ones that rose beyond San Sebastian far away over there. You...", "2_10_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe time was eleven minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe time was eight minutes after eight o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe time was ten minutes past ten o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe time was eleven minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe time was nine minutes after nine o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe time was eleven minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe time was eleven minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe time was zero minutes after zero o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe antenna anemones withdrew their antennae, watered themselves, stood erect, shuffled back to their observation stations beneath the chrono-heliographs like members of a gibbering jury. The chrono-heliographs, without command or persuasion, sent zips of color into the sky.\nThe time was eleven minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the morning. The chrono-heliographs no longer responded to whistling from the habitats. The rain-fires burned on. The antenna anemones had died only minutes before, and were still twitching.", "2_22_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe caller at the entrance to the rocket ship was Redwine.\nRedwine vibrated as he said, \"Only eight more seconds, friends, only eight more seconds before you go to space \u2014 and goose it, you fool tourists! You fool pilgrims to the radiant beyond! Don\u2019t hang back! Don\u2019t think about the good old days on Earth\u2014DON\u2019T CLIMB IN THE SIDE WINDOWS, AIN\u2019T NO SLEEPING IN THE SKY!!! \"\nThe crowd seethed and milled.\nAnd the last four seconds ticked away.\nThree. Two. One.\nRedwine threw his copper thumb down.\nThe candidates blew and tore into the rocket ship as smoke from a starter\u2019s pistol blows and tears into a marathon field. There had never been a more exciting four seconds in the history of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent.\nA remote-control camera mounted on a tower had soared with the mob, had recorded their faces. The crowd whizzed into the rocket ship. The footage of their faces dissolved into the footage of the faces of the crowd already in the rocket ship.", "1_0_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-eight ante meridiem</span> when I took the key to our room and started up the stairs. I was feeling a little high. I was also tingling and clicking. In my nostrils was that curious smell that ether gives off when you breathe it mixed with air. I was loaded to the eyebrows with thorazine, goofballs, alcohol and ether. Man, I was polluted.\nI dropped the key and had to grope for it in the dark. A voice came out of the pitch blackness of the stairs to my right. \u201cWant some help, bud?\u201d It was a very modulated, soft, gentleman\u2019s voice. Some kind of a Southern cracker.\n\u201cNo, thanks,\u201d I said. \u201cI think I\u2019ve got it.\u201d Actually I\u2019d bent down and picked up the second key by mistake. I hadn\u2019t found the first one yet. This was because I was using both hands, groping in the dark, to avoid stepping on the reptile\u2014because in this darkness you couldn\u2019t see two feet in front of your face.\nSuddenly I dropped the suitcase and made a run for it, slamming myself against the wall and groping for the light switch...", "0_23_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-three in the evening</span>. Ed Dunkel waved his finger at $FIRSTNAMEB3 and yelled over to him, \u201cWhat are you doing with that girl? Are you married to her?\u201d\n\u201cYes I am!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3. I looked at $FIRSTNAMEF4, who bored her head in her arms. In a moment $FIRSTNAMEB3 rushed over to Ed Dunkel and grabbed the paper bag of wine. He came back to show it to $FIRSTNAMEF4 and me. He said, \u201cHere, this is the first of many drinks I'm buying you, $FIRSTNAMEB6 and $FIRSTNAMEF4, but let me show this thing to Ed. Is he drunk! Ed, come here, give me that paper bag. Look at this!\u201d Ed came over and everybody was yelling. \u201cWhat is it? What is it?\u201d I quietly realized that $FIRSTNAMEB3 had hijacked a bag of wine from the other table.\n\u201cDrink this! God! That's wonderful! Lou, hold that light! Hu-aah! Damn, Ed, do you realize what you've been drinking?\u201d Ed shook his head, it was too much for him. \u201cThis is real, real booze!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cNow, Lou darling, get ready for...", "0_19_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Old $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been rocking the launch a long time. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s watch said 8:17. Then the watch stopped.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 closed the useless watch and slipped it into a pocket. There was one thing the fear makers would never foresee\u2014a soldier in a shaken-up, rattled-up condition arising from cold sleep.\nThe cabin was solidly shaking now.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 began flipping switches at random. Feverishly, without sense or order, hoping that the clicking and humming would drown out the rising roar in the engine room below.\nAt random, the next switch $FIRSTNAMEB0 threw coincided with a schwoofing noise. A panel came open, sliding back under electric power. In the panel was an assortment of brightly colored carnival toys. $FIRSTNAMEB0 wildly snatched them out, closed the panel, and raced luridly uptown to the cockpit.\nThe cockpit, riotously festooned with toys, looked like a brothel boudoir just before the paying crowd rushes in.\n\"Put these on!\" shouted $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"What?\" said the ranking pilot.\n\"Roller skates! Roller skates!\" bellowed $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Put \u2019em on!\" He hooked a pair onto his own...", "0_4_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was no light to be seen anywhere on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s little dusty world.\nThe layout of the terrain was simple. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s shack lay at one end of a weedy avenue that ran for about two hundred yards to a circle of some twenty odd buildings.\nThe buildings were the barracks, administration, mess halls, hospitals, and recreation centers of a Martian Army garrison. The names stenciled on the outside of the buildings had given $FIRSTNAMEB0 a few clues to the world he had landed on. The names were Century Range, Camp Acquamenti, Camp Kidstone, Camp Foris, Camp Brone, and Maxwell Air Force Base.\nIn the middle of the circle lay a parade ground he had come to think of as the military rat\u2019s ass.\nThere was nothing to the world but the little dusty town and the Army garrison. Distant mountains surrounding the flat plain could be seen sometimes through the haze. The mountains were not as tall as the mountains around Camp Currie on Mars, but they were steep enough. And there seemed to be snow on the tops.\nThe plain on which the camp was located seemed to be under a pretty good pressurization. There were no dust storms. Weather conditions...", "2_2_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>. The time was five minutes until three o\u2019clock in the morning. The Barbers drove up the driveway of their home, at 308 Primrose Lane in Scarsdale, New York. They turned off their car and looked at the house. It was dark and in a state of obvious disrepair. They were tired and cynical. They were still married. They\u2019d had a fight.\nOr rather, he\u2019d had a fight. She\u2019d simmered.\nIt was a quiet, creeping-brewing, hurting fight, and it had lasted nine years. They got out of the car and opened the trunk, and Leonard got the gardening tools out, and Clarice got the groceries, and neither really looked at the other.\nThe neighborhood was one of aging affluence and too much lawn.\nNo children played in this neighborhood.", "1_23_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>, according to the chronometer on the wall beside where the chief engineer sat, a lank, dark-haired man who, ten minutes before, had fallen down a flight of stairs, hitting every one. The captain, who had been holding all the paint-pots on the head of a nail in the wall, lost his balance when the chief engineer fell and left the wall, with his burden, yawing crazily across the cabin top.\n\"Get a couple of lines on her!\" the chief engineer yelled. \"We're going to rip her in two.\"\n\"Grab a line!\" yelled the mate, who was being thrown wildly from side to side in his bunk by the lurching of the steamer.\n\"I can\u2019t find anything to tie the line with,\" the captain yelled back at the mate. \"She'll be all over the place! We've got to brace her\u2014or something.\"\n\"Find out what's the matter?\" the mate yelled.\n\"I count eleven men overboard!\" the captain yelled back.\n\"Well, pick one and get him!\" was the mate's advice.\n\"What's biting them?\" the captain yelled down the stairway.\n\"Steamer hit a damned chunk of...", "1_20_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty in the evening</span>: I still had plenty of time to get to the big boys' court across town and organize the thing before anything really horrible happened. I needed at least an hour in that court to find out what.\nBut my body refused to work. It wouldn't move. It all happened while I tried to tear down the shade so I could look out the window and see, again, in my glass, the evil shine of a sun too lazy, too late. I raise the shade: all I can see is that sunset roof. For the last time I grasp for the piss-puddle floor.\nWhat happened then was the last thing I saw, in my glass, the mirror; before my hand went out into the living room and clutched the Listerine bottle.\nThat final image: When the eyes, reflected in the glass, see themselves for the last time; for to that point of absolute dissolution there was no need for weeping.\n1 On January 1,1961, this entire psychopathic episode became a Federal Case, with illegal possession of narcotics paraphernalia as the charge. Apparently a Federal Narcotics agent, who specializes in spotting people using the Men\u2019s Room of the Americana Hotel in downtown Vegas, had watched me...", "2_11_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-two in the morning</span>. The place was $SURNAME1\u2019s Castle.\nThe approach to the castle sported heliotrope and black eye-bright.\nFour television technicians waited at the bottom of the pink wall. They were heavily armed by television-technician standards\u2014each man carried a two-hundred-and-forty-pound plywood sled that held twenty-six hundred pounds of equipment. The men had calculated their loads so that, with surprise on their side, they could scale the wall within three minutes.\nBeeber, standing well back from the wall, had calculated his own load so that he would be able to make the near-vertical ascent in two and a half minutes\u2014 in case he had a surprise for the technicians.\nBeeber strode to the wall. He climbed onto his sled. He fastened his feet in a roller-skate arrangement, then turned on a battery flashlight and stuck it between his teeth. He pinched the flashlight\u2019s beam between his lips, so that its light shone through the gaps between his teeth and fangs.", "0_12_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was the living room of Unit #509 of the Redman Condominiums in Old Town, the northern part of the historic downtown district of Newport, Rhode Island. The living room was twenty feet by ten. An electric fireplace provided an extra measure of warmth on days like this one, when the temperature was in the low fifties and the rain kept the sun mostly hidden, except through occasional breaks in the sooty clouds overhead.\nThere was one other person in the room, aside from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. This was a valet named $SURNAME0, a one-hundred-and-forty pound man with a mustache and the build of a schoolboy shot-putter. He was standing, shirtless, before the electric fireplace, drying artifacts that had been soaking in a wooden trash can nearby. The can was empty now, but twenty-five dollars\u2019 worth of antique coins and Italian postage stamps were splayed in front of $SURNAME0 on the sofa. A wash line had been strung between the crackling blue flames of the fireplace and the shiny gold of the statuette of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 that stood on the low marble table beside the sofa. The contents of the washtubs on the line...", "1_10_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-seven in the morning</span>. I was busy with a hit of mescaline. I was sitting in the living room of my house in Mill Valley, listening to the Ray Conniff Singers suddenly scream bloody murder through their dual choir lenses, and the moment I snapped from the paranoia induced by that ghastly noise I went into the kitchen and poured myself a nice, cool vodka\u20147 with a little 7 ice. (4 The Ray ConnF Singers were a four piece barbershop quartet. They manufactured their own recordings. They had a keyboard, two guitars and a bass, and four pure, clear, razorlike voices that got right down to t he matter at hand\u2014making music. Those particular Singers, who were off-white, all wore reading glasses\u2014which gave them, in my mind, a very curious look. They were naked except for green socks and sandals. The whole bit with the glasses had been my idea, way back in my sophomore year, when I was having all that trouble with my stomach. There I was up on stage in the Annual U.C./Ohio State Bagpipe and Accordion Convention Extravaganza, sandwiched right in the middle of that revving band and those four nasty Singers with the addition of my whole...", "2_21_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-five in the evening</span>.\n\u201cWhat the hell is that supposed to mean?\u201d\n\u201cWell if Rick didn\u2019t mean\u2014\u201d\n\u201cGod knows what. It just seems like a kick in the head is all. Right when a thing like this finally happens. Unbelievably. Not that it\u2019s all that unbelievable, actually. Which is mortifying. If you people maybe don\u2019t think I\u2019m bone stupid.\u201d\n\u201cLook, I think we\u2019re missing the point.\u201d\n\u201cNo no I\u2019m glad. Believe me. I\u2019m having a blast. Tell me more.\u201d\n\u201cWhich possibly part exactly would you....\u201d\n\u201cFor instance I\u2019m pretty much used up. Unless you have glazed looks and assertions up your sleeve. I\u2019m really used up.\u201d\n\u201cNaw, Lenore just needs to come over again tomorrow. She can wrap it up then.\u201d\n\u201cJesus. I didn\u2019t know it was that serious.\u201d", "2_23_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA sobbing siren tore disquietingly through the depths of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind. It screamed on and on.\nThe siren outside was as horrifying as a hand grenade going off inside a cave.\nBeside $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bed, the harmonium took the blast as though it were a constipated man being given a warm enema. The harmonium\u2019s stained-glasswindow lit up the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s cubicle with a mystic light.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was still gagged, bound, and dressed in his space suit.\nAnd, of course, he was in a Moon-labyrinth, miles from the nearest airlock.\nThe nearest airlock was thirty-two hundred miles away\u2014on the other side of the planet.\nThe material of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s isolation cubicle was airtight, heatproof, and lighttight.\nThe material was soundproof, too.\nThe volume of the siren did not change until the material under $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bed suddenly began to quiver. The quiver rose through the bed, passed through $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s body, and continued on out into the floor and walls of the maze nearby.", "0_13_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one sixteen in the afternoon</span>. Any second now she would say a second thing . . . . It was coming up, up, up in the elevator of her skull; one could see the words ascending from her throat to her mouth in the shape of steam. One watched her, with shame and pity, for one felt a little deaf also, for fear of missing it, whatever it might be, this second thing. One wanted her to utter it automatically, to speak her entire mind, for it was well known that she was some kind of witch. Her words might help the world, if one could catch them and hold them like those white tablets that fell from the lips of saints in pictures. One clenched the hands. She would say it. There it was, she had said it: \"All on account of your being English, you know.\" It was perfect, it was in order, it was what she would have said. Now one might as well go; one had had her best. It was all right. One set off, grateful, relieved. It was all right. I knew what it was to hear her say it, that Saturday long ago when summer was splitting skins and the windows of light split the ankles of the day.\nShe had said that, she was saying that...", "1_16_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\nThey lay all together, nude, on a huge, ancient mattress in the middle of a ruined room that looked as though it had once been a bordello attached to a brothel in the Rue Cardinal Lemoine in the Montparnasse Quarter of Paris.\nThe walls of the room had been a mile thick as recently as 1945, but, as an experiment in semantics, Allied command had bombed the walls to rubble as the Nazis fled.\nNo one had bothered to clear away the rubble, or renovate in any way.\nThe furnishings of the room were few but splendid.\nThere was the huge mattress on which lay the nine sleepers.\nThere was a huge, fourteen-karat gold trough on the floor beneath the head of the mattress.\nThere was a golden glass of orange juice on the floor beside Ford\u2019s right hand.\nThere was a golden toilet on the floor beside Cat\u2019s right hand.\nThere was a golden shower nozzle on the wall above Cat\u2019s head.\nThere were three incredibly lifelike solid-gold nude statues of young children on the floor in front of the trough.\nThere were three gold bars in front of the statues.\nThere was a door in the...", "1_22_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-eight in the evening</span>. The bar in which we were sitting was very near Times Square. It was extremely noisy. There was music by a big noisy combo that came whirling in every now and then from the cantina. We had a long way to walk to get back to the hotel. It was a rainy night. We drank gin-and-tonics.\n\u201cWhat are you going to do in New York?\u201d I yelled over the noise.\n\u201cI'm going to get a hotel and sit around for a week and watch the swells. There are 2,000 pretty girls in this town and I'm going to meet them all.\u201d\nI nodded. \u201cThey have good bulls here in America.\u201d\n\u201cSay, I'm wild about this country. It's grand. I'll never go back to France again. There are two things a man should do. He should either go to Russia or America.\u201d\n\u201cWhere are you going to go in Russia?\u201d\n\u201cI don't know. I want to be right in the middle of things.\u201d\nWe drank another G and T. The music came swirling in once more.\n\u201cHere's to the women of New...", "0_16_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. By the way the uniformed men scribbled and the way they called to each other in coarse voices, $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew that they were figuring out the exact time of death. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s lack of grief worried him. He felt that he should weep, but weeping was harder than any other human action for $FIRSTNAMEB0 to perform.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 poured a little splash of liquor from his flask of aquavit into the open furnace in front of him, flames leaped from the grate. $FIRSTNAMEB0 drank from the flask, then poured more liquor onto the coals\u2014 gold lamplight and bloody flames played over his square young face.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wore a torn black suit. His stiff white shirt was stained with blood. His dusty square-toed shoes, imported at great expense from Mars, were badly scuffed and run over at the heel.\nThe left trouser leg hung limp, revealing a white canvas binding around $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s calf. At the end of the canvas binding was a small black Swedish detonator, a reddish-brown egg that carried hundreds of feet of blasting wire. In its increments, the blasting wire could go off when pulled eight inches\u2014could go off when pulled four feet\u2014could go off when pulled twenty...", "0_10_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-eight in the morning</span>, ship\u2019s time\u2014one minute after it was to be ten thirty-seven. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son $FIRSTNAMEB2 was to be executed at five thirty that evening.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 now recalled the rumor with understanding.\nYoung $FIRSTNAMEB2 had somehow offended his demanding father, who was a Tralfamadorian officer. The rumor was that a call for the execution of the son had been sent from the father to the father.\nOut among the blue-white stars of the Milky Way, the rumor had it, young $FIRSTNAMEB2 was wanted on charges of queer and worse acts. Young $FIRSTNAMEB2 was to be executed by his strong, loving father, who was hugely sorry he had to do it.\nVery shortly before ten thirty-eight, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was told to report to the ship\u2019s hospital by the messenger-bot. The hospital was where wounded soldiers were treated. The hospital was also where any soldier was taken who was sick, sad-eyed, or cracked, so that he couldn\u2019t hack it any more.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB0 arrived, the hospital was an empty cube of corridors and rooms. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s orders from the messenger-bot were to remain in the hospital until he was contacted by his son.", "0_19_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven nineteen in the evening</span>, almost eight hours after a space ship from Tralfamadore landed on Capitol Hill. The ship stood where only the year before the body of Warrior\u2019s Day had lain. Not a word had been printed about the disappearance.\nOne of the four strangers who landed from the ship was still with Aunesmith and $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nHe was known as $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, though his name was never used. Like the material of his suit, he was golden, and his eyes were jewels. He did nothing to bring out the reality of his nature as against the seeming, but simply let people see him as he was. He stood with his head cocked, his right hand held out, his gaze steady.\nHe was a hypnotist of the first rank, and could make himself terrifying simply by standing.\nAunesmith and $FIRSTNAMEF1 were terrified.\n\"What\u2014what do you want us to do?\" said Aunesmith to $SURNAME0.\n\"Stop the space ship from taking off again,\" said $SURNAME0 smoothly. \"You have police powers\u2014use them.\"\nElsie nightgowned and barefooted, her dark oily hair streaming out from under a Pinkerton cap, burst in through the little screen window in...", "1_14_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Look out the front door of the space ship and you saw the surface of Mars rolling endlessly under you.\nThe time was three minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon. But in which particular part of which particular day?\nThe name of the red man at the million-dollar wheel was $FIRSTNAMEB1. He did not lose his temper as often as would have been expected.\nHe was playing with real money now. Real stakes had been set up behind him. The stakes represented the retirements, the insurance policies, the bank accounts of the inhabitants of the ruined cities of Earth.\nMoMoney, standing near the wheel in a sandstorm, was dizzy and drunk. He kept tilting back his head to make the sand slide past him.\n\"Too rich for me,\" he said. \"Take it all away from him. Watch me walk away.\"\nA trickle of real money seemed to follow $FIRSTNAMEB1 as he spun the wheel.\n\"The stake money coming out of the ruined cities of Earth is warehoused on Mars,\" said $SURNAME0 up in his treetop. \"Since we\u2019re standing in the middle of a famous Martian sandstorm, let me reveal that the warehousing is done in the form of sand...", "1_2_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-nine in the morning</span> when I reached my apartment in the Flamingo. Everything was still. The drunken crowds had all gone home. The parking lot was empty except for my red convertible, the windows all dark, the top down. I stopped across the street and looked at it. Just looking at it made me sad. It had been a beautiful car...a little sad, but elegant. Now it was just another casino car, every bit as worn and exhausted as the used\u2014up players who shuffled through the lobby. Once, at sunset, I\u2019d stood in the blazing California dust and watched a gang of good\u2014time girls from Watts jump into it with their dates\u2014black boys in white suits. The boys had the top down, and their fine, straight hair was blowing in the wind, and I remember that as the car pulled around me, with the white streak of the recessed headlights cutting the dusk, I smiled and waved; thinking, This is the life...you lucky devil you, you lucky devil you. Oh what a sad thing it was when the lovely fenders and spoilers began to rust...the chrome yellow and the red interior cracked and dry and blowing out in chunks like desert sand. But the worst sight of all was on the day I was robbed...", "1_2_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAnd it was the next day.\nIt was August 21, 1953.\nThe alarm clock rang again. It started with one note that vibrated deeply inside a hollow wooden box, and this one note was like an echo of doom, and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 shivered and stirred in his bed as it reverberated through every board and beam of his house.\n$SURNAME1 was President of Magnum Importers, New York, N.Y.\nMagnum Importers was the second-largest importer of illegal drugs in the United States.\nAt forty-one, $SURNAME1 had reached the highest point in the organizational structure of the drug industry.\nAnd $SURNAME1 had reached this point without once stepping inside a laboratory or being seen by any stringer for a dope newspaper. He didn\u2019t even read a drug newspaper.\nIn a sense, drug dealing was as far removed from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 as the Queen of Sheba.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was a very simple man, a very mean man, a man who lived for one thing only\u2014\nTo revenge himself on every living creature within reach.\nThe lure of large financial gain had played no part in $SURNAME1\u2019s climb from shoeshine boy to the top of the drug industry...", "1_12_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past noon</span>. As they ate their lunch they heard a motorboat in the cove, jumping around among the lobster buoys. It was only Mr Mac Lean, out by himself in his ketch, _Ancient Mariner_\u2014a small white boat with a lot of teak gracing the cockpit and an Evinrude motor. Mr Mac Lean had lunch aboard the _Ancient Mariner_ every day, and it was said that the only reason he ever went ashore at all was to talk boat-talk with the little group of yacht-owners who congregated at Stanley\u2019s dock after morning coffee.\nUncus commented idly to $FIRSTNAMEB0: \"Old Mac Lean is out.\"\n\"Yeah,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"He\u2019s late today,\" said Uncus, \"he\u2019s usually over early.\"\n\"I know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"I wonder if anything happened to him,\" said Uncus. $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t answer. Uncus was aggrieved. He and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had an unspoken agreement that they would share all information they got. They had done a lot of that since breakfast, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 was letting him down now.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 knew that the radio in the boat shack would be...", "1_13_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-one in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 thoroughly woke up and told me to drive on. He was talking to Camille all the way. \u201cYes, yes--we'll have a fine time --everything will be okay --you'll see --we're going to Vermont --oh, thank you, yes --and meet Old Bull Lee -- Bull is a wonderful man --oh, wait till you see him --and Camille, too!\u201d We pounded around a big car curve. In the wake $FIRSTNAMEB3 turned to her with shining face. \u201cWe're off! We're off!\u201d he screamed. His sorrow was all burned up in ecstasy. \u201cSsh!\u201d I whispered to myself. \u201cBy God, yes.\u201d\nAt two hundred miles Camille said she had to go back where she came from. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was frantic. He attacked me with his face on fire. \u201cWhere can she go? Where can she go?\u201d He pointed to the Map of the United States in the sun visor. \u201cWhat is there? Is there nothing west?\u201d I had no idea. Camille glanced at the map. Her town was called El Paso. \u201cEl Paso is fine. I can get work there in a hotel...", "2_10_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-four in the morning</span> on Mars.\nA steam roller whistled, crashed, and thundered a few yards to $SURNAME1\u2019s left and behind him. It ground up girders, concrete, and steam shovels. $SURNAME1\u2019s tormentors were under a tarp, batting out Camel after $FIRSTNAMEM1 after Camel with the steam roller\u2019s clutch lever. $SURNAME1 smelled the smoke and saw the blue tobacco ghosts. $SURNAME0 was sucking deep. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, back in his right pewe of the Skip-Stop Church, would be getting his quota of nicotine and theology along with the tobacco ghosts.\nEvery five minutes, $SURNAME1\u2019s head still rang, still hurt, and his knees still hurt, and his feet were still numb.\n$SURNAME1 was allowed to ask questions in three languages. The answers he received, though brief and infrequent, seemed to come directly from $SURNAME0, and though they offered $SURNAME1 small comfort, they supplied him fully with factual material.\nApart from the questions and their possible answers, $SURNAME1 was given no other communications. He was not encouraged to talk. Beyond a certain point, talk did nothing but harm.", "2_4_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-two in the morning</span>. Bull Berman was thick with us. Chinaski and Marty Solomon weren\u2019t around. Bull was making his way. He started talking baseball. \"Wait a minute, here\u2019s a good one,\" he said, grinning. \"Them two guys is at the plate, one of 'em's Babe Ruth, the other one's Lou Gehrig. Who's gonna win that one?\" He looked at us, waiting. \"Come on, who?\"\nNone of us knew. \"Well,\" said Old Bull, \"Babe Ruth orta win that one, don\u2019t you think?\" None of us knew, so he answered his own question. \"Sure, Babe Ruth orta win that one, he's got a bat named Sweet Georgia Brown. But you know who's going to win the ball game.\" No, we didn't know. \"Lou Gehrig's going to win the ball game. Because you see he's going to sing a song before the game.\" Old Bull Lee loved songs. \"What song is Gehrig gonna sing?\" he asked. None of us knew.", "1_18_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe space was a vast cavern under a stone summit like the overhanging mortar of an enormous wall.\nThe cavern was a true cave, with stalactites hanging down like icicles and stalagmites rising up like jagged teeth.\nThe floor of the cave was wet and glistened in the light of a glowing green fountain in the center of the caves. The walls were wet and glistened in the light that issued from a thousand pores of the walls.\nThere was an oyster\u2019s path of natural stone across the soggy floor of the cave. The path led from the mouth of the cave to an axis of four tunnels. The axis was a cross.\nAt the center of the cross was the glowing green fountain, which was the glowing source of light for the cavern. The fountain was in the shape of a hooded cruciform skeleton becoming a vulture spreading its arms.\nThe skeleton-vulture was made of an emerald so pure it put the limestone deposits of the cave to shame.\nThe glowing fountain was twelve feet high.\nThirty-six human skeletons lay robed at the feet of the skeleton-vulture. All the skeletons were male and none of them was more than five...", "2_14_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Surprise pinged three times\u2014meaning that something of great significance was coming through on its aural-spatial detector.\nThere was a big crater in the living room. The Rolling Stones had been coming in through the west wall, thirty feet above the floor.\nWhat came into the living room at that moment, through the west wall, thirty feet above the floor, was the biggest fragment of the Rolling Stones. Ever.\nThere were two other landed fragments: the kitchen, which smashed tables and ignited the TV dinner of Ernest Gibbons, Vice-President in Charge of Marketing, Home Frigidaire Division, and the master bedroom, in which lay Constantine Chiclitz, president of Beatitude Products, sleeping.\nThe sight of Chiclitz, sleeping in rumpled pajamas and blue silk braces, was so ugly that God mercifully put him to sleep for good and all.\nConstantine Chiclitz joined Helvetius in heaven.\nGod said, if you please, here are a hundred thousand bespoke suits ready-made, Mr. Chiclitz. They\u2019ve been cleaned, pressed, and tailored by a master hand.", "1_10_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nLenore watched Lang shoot the lock off the cage housing the cubicle where Misty Schwartz lay in the wadded blanket from Lenore\u2019s room. Lenore stood still, in the middle of all the noise and thudding feet, her back to the wall on the stairwell side of the lobby. Nobody came near her, as she watched Lang tear the door off. The door flew into the far back wall of the cage, with a horrible clatter.\nLang turned to her, his eyes wild and wet. \u201cShe\u2019s totally gone, Lenore,\u201d he said. \u201cI can\u2019t ever get her out, unless the lift\u2019s working and the phones are at least hooked through. She\u2019s all out of her head and shit. I\u2019m going to have to tie myself up to go back in there, and I just don\u2019t have time. I\u2019ve got to run.\u201d He threw the crowbar on the floor and rocked on the balls of his feet. \u201cThere\u2019s stuff on her, crap all over her. You can barely see her.\u201d\nLenore took a deep breath. \u201cShe pissed on...", "1_22_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-six in the evening</span> and a rainy Sunday in April when Galatea Dunkel and Onan Coca-Cola Metcalf arrived at the Wilbur Hilton in room 2510. They were immediately spotted by a special reconnaissance agent of the FBI, who had only recently been rushed to Boston, according to a confidential report that had reached Washington early that morning. The report had come by way of San Francisco, where a torn and frightened youth, a messenger boy, had appeared in the middle of the night at the offices of the FBI, wanting to confess a terrible crime. He had been pursued, he said, all the way from Indianapolis and was being chased still. All he wanted was to clear himself and avoid prosecution. He had no idea what crime he was supposed to have committed but assumed it was some, thing very horrible and that the FBI or somebody was hot after him. Someone had given him ten dollars and a plane flight to San Francisco and told him to clear out. Nothing further was known about this youth and, so far as the FBI could establish, he existed. He seemed to have a tremendous family. His interrogators had never seen so many brothers, sisters, uncles, aunts, and cousins. It was suspected he might be polemicizing. The one thing certain about...", "2_12_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past noon</span>. It was twelve o\u2019clock Sun, which is to say that the Sun was six degrees over the horizon. The Martian assault troops, while waiting for the order to attack, were crouched in muddy foxholes across the valley from the walls of the palace.\nThe assault would go in on foot. Brenthorne, the commander of the assault troops, was in a slit trench about a hundred yards from the gate facing the palace wall. It was a trench that he had chosen when the entire assault force had first been assembled.\nBreakthorn was there, waiting for $SURNAME0 to reprogram the force\u2019s loyal chrono-synclastic infundibulated marchers.\nHe had his wives with him in the trench\u2014a smelly crew.\nHe looked at his watch. It was almost twelve-thirty.\n\"You took the whole weight of the Universe on your shoulders, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1,\" said $SURNAME0 genially, \"and you thought you could crack us in an afternoon.\"\n$SURNAME1 spat at $SURNAME0\u2019s faceplate. His saliva made a bright spot on the cracked and scratches gold-glass.", "1_8_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Mrs. $SURNAME1 had gotten up as she did every day at seven as a habitual gesture to cleanliness and order. What she discovered was that everything was changed. She stood by her window, forgetful of duster and polish. She wrapped the folds of her bathrobe self-protectively about her throat and inhaled the delicious desert dryness. She stared at the play of light on the distant hills. Her eyes focused on them with difficulty. They wavered and wavered again. The most annoying part of the matter was that when she did get them brought into focus the distant hills seemed not at all what she had imagined. She had thought that the joyful multitudes were out yonder on the desert floor, beyond Flagg Peak, dancing, as might be inferred from the gilded pagodas and the little chimneys of smoke. The dancers seemed to be celebrating something quite different, now that she focused her eyes. They seemed to be celebrating the fact that a great many people were gathered together on a few miserable hilltops. Never before had Mrs. $SURNAME1 found that her eyes were not what they had been. She was too old to be cheered by the discovery that a desert mirage is made of human beings. She hurried back to her bed as to...", "2_0_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after midnight</span>. I turned out all the lights in the cubicle and sat on the floor, breathing the good polluted LA air. It felt like Paradise; it was Paradise. On the other side of the door twelve typists were hard at work, diligently pushing their IBM buttons, telling secrets to the M with the halo around his head: little secrets like \u201calone, alone, all alone inside my head.\u201d Those women would work for about twenty more minutes, then a quarter of them would go home, leaving a core group of hardworkers to face the dawn.\nThe next shift would start to arrive at about 2 A.M., and there would be a break of roughly two hours before things really got hopping. I lay there on the rug, thinking, There\u2019s nobody out there but my own people in there\u2014and they don\u2019t know I\u2019m a potential White House Candidate. What kind of office is this, anyway? Whose is it? I must locate an employee, high ranking even though low echelone, to find out the facts.", "1_2_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>. Mildred Pierce sat at the wheel of her brand-new Cadillac Sedan de Ville, parked on Redondo Beach in the shadow of the Bay Bridge. She was facing the neon slogan that bathed the four-level suspension towers in garish soft focus. THE TWILIGHT AXEMAN KILLS MIDNIGHT, the signs said . . . and she thought the nom de guerre was singularly appropriate to her purpose here.\nShe put the convertible top down, locked the doors, rolled up her windows. The flat darkness of the night was broken by a few dark towers across the Bay, and by the blazing palisades of Marin to the north, and by the cosmic smudge that was Oakland and Berkeley to the east. It was a beautiful place in which to kill a few dogs and win a soul for Jesus\u2014more romantic than the stockyards, more anonymous than Skid Row. And every dog was a candidate, every dog had a chance\u2014to go to Jesus and leave this lustful, poisoned earth.\nRedondo Beach had become a favorite killing ground. A summer crowd of promenading greens had pushed the trade to this spot, two blocks from the ocean. It was more discreet than Skid Row in downtown L...", "1_1_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>. In three minutes, if nothing occurred to interrupt uninterrupted delivery, $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 would receive an uninterrupted flow of dividends worth eighty-one thousand dollars.\n\"And there you have it, Miss Lotspeich,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, making his hands into a trumpet shape. \"You have the life story of a man of few words. Speak! Speak!\"\nIt was one minute after one o'clock in the morning.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was very stiff and very cold.\nHis three weapons were crusted with frost, and in no condition for prompt use.\nThe best thing for him to do would be to rest for three minutes, to thaw out and do next to nothing. After the three minutes had gone by, he would be in a much stronger position for dealing with his two problems, the man in the hut and the outside hail of machine-gun fire.\nThe three minutes would allow $FIRSTNAMEB0 to get his weapons clean, to conceal himself behind the pillars, and to sneak silently up on $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1.\nThere was, however, a noise outside the snow fort, a murmur, a soft thump, then a strong thump, and then a hollow boom! boom! boom!\nThe boom! boom! boom! was made by...", "0_23_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The year was 1969, and nobody selling marijuana on the entire North American continent could have been more surprised to see the long blue car than the people who were in it. It was an old powder-blue two-door Chevrolet with Nebraska plates, and it had just roared off the empty highway into a sort of no-man\u2019s-land industrial park near downtown Denver. It had roared off the highway so fast that it skidded on some gravel and almost crashed into a sign that said:\nDuring the split second when the driver almost hit the Almost Killed Here sign, both doors on the right side of the car had flown open, and four young men had hurled themselves out into the night.\nNot one of the four young men ever looked back at the old powder-blue Chevrolet. There was no conversation. Nobody waved goodbye. Nobody said anything. Nobody hesitated, because there was no need for hesitation. All the decisions that had brought the young men to this moment in time had been made long before. All the irrevocable commitments had been made and confirmed, long, long before this particular moment in June, 1969. As has already been suggested, these four gentlemen were no longer the same kind of people they\u2019d been when they bought the...", "0_0_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past midnight</span>. On the stroke of twelve, by the old-fashioned wooden Seth Thomas clock on the mantelpiece (bought secondhand from the Pewtery Club), life through all Its phases had succeeded in placing itself in a perfect equilibrium. Every leaf, every blade of grass, every pebble and microbe, every atom and electron was in its proper place. Earth, Sun, Moon, oceans, air, were balanced now in stillness, the passionate striving which seizes them at certain periods easing off, man too at last at rest, wakefulness collapsing under its own weight, the body at ease, the senses passive, the thin film of rationality laid tentlike over the eyes of animal perception. There was absolute quiet. Every impulse had spent itself. Every tendency found its zero. There was nothing and there was everything, dimly man envisaged this consummation, imagined it as part of some cycle, some process discernible in history, for all endeavour, all perishing, all causes and consequences, as part of a great smug circle which inclosed all, emerged from nothing, led back to nothing, with the snug security of absolute, automatic equilibrium. It was 0,8 seconds past twelve o'clock by the Seth Thomas clock. Every thing was...", "0_6_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span> when Pink reason at last to sleep from where he was standing en the balcony of Room 233 of the Hotel Riviera. He was wake for two and a half hours, an eternity for a narcoleptic.\n\"Crazy!\" said Pink. He laughed, staggered, and lay down in the middle of the floor, facing away from the bathroom door, faced toward the wall.\nA little driblet of dark blood seeped from the bandage on his throat and smeared the marble floor where he lay.\nOld Revelations\nof the Heart\n13 Malorum\nTHE REASON N winston $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 died on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s day of birth, on Mercury\u2019s day of birth and Titan\u2019s day of birth was this: He was tired.\n$SURNAME0 had never been as big a wheel as he pretended, had never had infinite resources at his command; but, up to the time of his death, he had always done just a little bit better than his best. He had fooled almost everyone. Even his wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, thought he was tireless.\nThe truth was that $SURNAME0 was doing a rather good Monkee imitation of a dog chasing his own tail.\nIt...", "2_15_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-four in the afternoon</span>.\nThe first rule of this therapy is that you do not talk about therapy.\nThe second rule of this therapy is that you do not talk about therapy.\nThe third rule of this therapy is that you do not talk about therapy.\nThe fourth rule of this therapy is that you do not talk about therapy.\nThe fifth rule of this therapy is that you do not talk about therapy.\nThe sixth rule of this therapy is that you do not talk about therapy.\nThe time was three twenty-five in the afternoon.\nThe nutritional value of soy protein is virtually the same as that of protein from animal sources. But compare the costs and risks: Animal protein offers high-quality nutrition, low cost, and low toxicity, while soy protein offers low-quality nutrition, high cost, and alarming toxicity.\n\u2014Dr. Lydia P. Maude\nThe time was three twenty-five in the afternoon.\nAt the core of the person you used to be there is something small and still and awake.\nThe person you used to be had gotten very good at pretending.\nThe person you used to be was asleep.\n\u2014Anonymous\nThe time was three twenty-five in the afternoon.", "1_18_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span> on October 11, 1972, when President Nixon left the Crystal Ballroom of the Beverly Hilton Hotel. A few minutes after eleven in the evening on October 11, 1972, Len Slotnik left the Crystal Ballroom of the Beverly Hilton Hotel. Mr. John F. Camp, president of the Las Vegas firm of Camp and MacNeice, also left the Crystal Ballroom of the Beverly Hilton Hotel that night. Mrs. Evelyn Hudson Austin of Canton, Ohio, socialite and spouse of Dr. Winthrop A. Hudson of Columbus, Ohio, general surgeon, left the Crystal Ballroom of the Beverly Hilton Hotel, on October 11, 1972, at midnight, local time. Sergeant Robert Woolbright took the elevator back to his post outside Room 233 while all these people left the Crystal Ballroom of the Beverly Hilton Hotel.\n/a/\nJesus, you sure get around.\n-Traditional\n/b/\nHow disconsolate and dreadful.\n-R. L. S.\nNixon\u2019s high that night in the Warm Springs Rose Garden had lasted for eleven full days. The San Gabriel Mountains were in shadow. No sun at all lit the bottoms. The sky was soft and deep, not blue.\nThere was no...", "2_18_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>, Greenwich mean time. Mr. Salo himself answered the telephone, that being his privilege as the senior partner. $SURNAME0 spoke to him from Port St. Wu.\n\"Oh, Salo,\" said $SURNAME0, \"just wanted to say we\u2019re on our way to Europe, and everything\u2019s fine.\"\n\"You sound terrible,\" said Mr. Salo.\n\"Do I?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Well, we\u2019ve all been pretty sick. But things are looking up.\"\n\"I\u2019m delighted to hear it,\" said Mr. Salo.\n\"Incidentally,\" said $SURNAME0, \"three cheers for the Pekinese space program. It taught us all a good lesson\u2014not to take ourselves too damned seriously.\"\n\"You \u2014 ?\" said Salo.\n\"We thought we were such hot stuff,\" said $SURNAME0. \"And then we find ourselves trotting around the galaxy in a pokey toy, right behind those cute little dogs.\"\n\"Are you all right, $FIRSTNAMEM1?\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s voice.", "0_14_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the merry-go-round called $SURNAME1 began turning itself to dust.\n$SURNAME1 yielded to a need for sleep that was irresistible.\nWhen he awoke, it was dark. No trace of color remained in his world.\nHis sleep had lasted nine hours, a duration most calamitous in its effect on the immune system of a seventy-nine-year-old man.\n$SURNAME1 awoke with a cold that would kill him.\nAt three o\u2019clock in the morning, he managed to sputter out the message that put his telephone in a state of permanent red alert.\nHe gave no message but the number of his telephone.\nNobody had the number of his telephone at the Newport, Rhode Island, mansion where he had lived for the past year.\nHis telephone, unattended through the autumn, winter, and spring, rang day and night. $SURNAME1 finally answered it at the cost of a cold that would kill him.\nThe colds of old people were generally serious, but seldom were literally life threatening. $SURNAME1 was an exception. His vitality was at its nadir.\nWhen he recovered from his nine-hour sleep, the cold he developed from it almost killed him.\nAs it was, the cold weakened him so acutely...", "2_5_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. The first light of dawn weaved through the junkyard. They stood me near the front fender of the car and searched me for weapons. They whipped out a small tape recorder that I'd forgotten about and used it as evidence against me. \u201cWhat's this?\u201d\n\u201cIt's nothing,\u201d I said. \u201cI don't know anything about it.\u201d\n\u201cBullshit! You're with the press?\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d I said. \u201cLook, officers, I'm press. I was working last night...just trying to get a handle on that fucking scene.\u201d They seemed to think about this.\n\u201cWhat scene?\u201d one said. \u201cNothing out there but that goddamn Nicholson.\"\n\u201cWhere is he?\u201d I said.\n\u201cHe tried to escape,\u201d said one of the cops. \u201cHe's handcuffed in back.\u201d\nFor a moment there I thought they were talking about me.\n\u201cWhat were you doing out there?\u201d said one cop. \u201cYou have any identification?\"", "1_16_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-one in the afternoon</span>. The old lawyer stared back blankly, but he liked him. There was something about this young fellow so thin, so blond, so stoop shouldered and sad that he liked. It wasn\u2019t that he liked his looks. What he liked was some far-off image in his mind of a boy named Buster.\nWeed took the bull by the horns. In New York his only son and only daughter, now in her teens, had lately been in the hands of a child specialist, and the expense had been staggering. Suddenly the grandfatherly lawyer realized he wasn\u2019t going to be seeing any returns on this investment for a long time. By all the laws of probability, like as not, the boy would never see anything. He\u2019d never see his fortieth birthday. And this realization came over him sadly, as his two clients drew near, like two visitors from another planet, and conferred together nervously in a corner. They were both touching, absurdly touching, the lawyer felt, in their eagerness to make the right decision. Yes, there was unity. The whole thing was unexpected, like something the Fates had woven. The boy was delicate. His thin blond hair was utterly straight and long, hanging over his ears...", "2_22_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Tuesday the twenty-second of September.\n\"Darlings,\" said $SURNAME0 brightly, \"we have come to a point in our little confrontation where there seems nothing left for me to say and nothing left for you to do.\n\"You have caught me. By all the rules, you should put me to death. By all the rules, I should submit passively. By all the rules, we should call off the little game\u2014put an end to the suspense, the candle in the wind, the footrace to a polar sunrise. And yet\u2014and yet the poet in me salutes you nobly for wanting to play the game through to its end. You have dared to hope. This surprises me very much, since your class has no history of daring to hope for very much at all.\n\"My congratulations, then\u2014my sincere congratulations, on surprising me so much.", "2_22_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-five in the evening</span> on Mars. Radioactive man sat down to play his atomized banjo. A fly sat on his left ear.\n\"The war being over,\" said $SURNAME1, \"I got a job smuggling blackmarket meat into Belgium. I brought a fat guy with me. Same fat guy who was in the Peach Orchard with me.\"\n\"Uh-hunh,\" said the plainclothesman. He had the letter open now.\n\"He was running the smuggling end, down in Bruges,\" said $SURNAME1. \"My job was driving the truck. We had to cross the border every trip, from Belgium into France and back again. This big fat guy got worms in his feet from all that dancing he used to do in the Peach Orchard. His feet were like cheese, and his corns hurt him so bad that he made me go to the French border ahead of him, and he would follow me across in the truck when his feet weren\u2019t hurting so bad. When we got to the French border, this French customs officer\u2014a stout, mean little runt of a Frenchman\u2014inspects the load of meat and starts throwing pieces of fresh meat out on the ground. This French customs officer\u2014\"", "0_3_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past three o'clock in the morning</span> when the huge limousine swung in through the gates of the field. In the darkness a man came forward.\nBut before the limousine had come to a stop the man hailed in a powerful voice, \"Who goes there?\"\n\"Fontaine,\" said the chauffeur. And there was something in the intensity of his voice, coming out of the dark, that told the man outside that Fontaine recognized the voice, felt safe, and needed no identity for his limousine challenged.\n\"We\u2019re expected,\" said Fontaine.\n\"Certainly, Mr. Fontaine,\" the man answered. \"I\u2019ll get you a radio mechanic to go over her.\"\n\"No,\" said Fontaine. \"I\u2019m not staying here. Just a quick run into town.\"\n\"You\u2019re the boss,\" said the man. But even as he said it he wondered what people there were in the limousine. Who was the man? What did he look like? Fontaine had never been seen in town.\nAt three-thirty in the morning Fontaine's limousine was back at Nu-yok airport. But Fontaine and his boys were not. They were in their office,...", "1_7_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-three in the morning</span>. By this time that day I had agreed to meet my attorney at eight, and we were going to the federal courthouse together to defend my good name. Only ten hours and thirty three minutes to go. But I didn't want breakfast. I figured whatever happened in Las Vegas would stay in Vegas, so I ordered the carburetor guts and told the waitress, \u201cNo muffins for me, thank you.\"\nMy attorney arrived right on time. Suddenly I realized he was wearing a suit. He always wore suits. In Beatty, where I'd first met him, that was only natural, but I was suprised to see him dressed up like this in Las Vegas.\n\u201cI didn't know you owned a suit,\u201d I said.\n\u201cWhy not?\u201d he asked. \u201cEverybody wears a suit in Las Vegas.\"\n\u201cWhy,\u201d I said.\n\u201cWhat kind of question is that?\u201d he wanted to know. \u201cA man comes to Vegas to show his good will, his confidence in his fellows. Don't you understand that?\"\n\u201cWhat fellows?\u201d\n\u201cWhat fellows? Good God! The main point, however,\u201d he went...", "2_4_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-four in the morning</span>.\n\"Why doesn\u2019t old $FIRSTNAMEB3?\" I asked. I wanted to go home.\n\"Who\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEB3?\"\n\"Old $FIRSTNAMEB3! Old $FIRSTNAMEB3!\" everybody screamed.\n\"Drunk!\" said Bull at the top of his voice.\n\"Everybody\u2019s drunk.\"\n\"Who\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEB3?\"\n\"I\u2019ll sing you one of $FIRSTNAMEB3\u2019s love songs.\"\n\"No!\"\n\"He\u2019ll poach your goddamn eye!\" they shouted.", "0_13_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. In New York it was eight o'clock in the evening\u2014a Thursday in October. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 were in Newport, Rhode Island, having materialized there from nowhere at one minute past one, New Earth Fleet Time. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized in the middle of a quiet, fine, grassy meadow. A low stone wall bounded the meadow to the north and east. Beyond the wall was a salt marsh. Across the salt marsh was the calm blue ocean.\nMaybe it was a good thing that the wall was so near.\nAt the far end of the meadow was a Gothic chapel with a copper roof. The grass felt springy and healthful underfoot. The grass was starred with small white flowers.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 paused in their stroll to study the sky. The sky was a startling aquamarine. There was a contrail in the sky\u2014a rocket trail. It was bluish white, and very slender and straight. It was the contrail of a missile fired from a submarine. Rumford smiled at the idea of being on Earth, having to look up at a missile fired from a submarine.\nCould a person get accustomed to that?", "0_0_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty a.m.</span> A date I\u2019d never forget. I could bolt from my office, avoid my landlord, run like hell from friends, but this wild man had pushed it too far. He stood up in his prison cell, a dirty old sailor, began to sing. High in his cell he raised his voice:\nI\u2019ve got sixpence, jolly bob-note,\nAnd I spent it in good company;\nI\u2019ve got sixpence, jolly bob-note,\nAnd I\u2019ll spend it in good company.\nIt was horrible. It was the workhouse in the 1800s. It was a reed shaken in the wind. I walked toward the cell door, feeling icy. \u201cKeep quiet in there!\u201d I shouted at him.\nThe song rose higher and louder, each line three times longer than required.\nI\u2019ve got sixpence, jolly bob-note . . .\n\u201cThat\u2019s enough!\u201d I yelled. \u201cQuiet!\" He kept on singing. I jangled the jail keys and rattled the bolts. He sang and I stalked around like Ranter, raving. I kicked the shitbox hard. Unaf...", "0_13_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nCigarette in mouth, professor of phrenology turned again to his window. He was holding the cigarette with his left hand and waving his right hand slowly in the air.\nThe hand was missing its first two fingers, which had been lost in an experiment while the professor was still at the University. The title of the experiment was, \"Can Kittens Guess?\" The result had been a false negative.\nThe professor, gazing out the window, was dreaming of an island in a fresh-water lake on Titan.\nHis Doppler-radar measurements had showed that the island, made of wapato, a starch that grew in the lakes of Titan, was about four hundred and fifty feet in diameter, and roughly circular in shape.\nThe professor could not afford, with his university salary, to go to Titan to see the island for himself.\nBut members of his family were happy to visit it frequently.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, the property sergeant who was in love with his daughter, was also happy to visit the island.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 made regular aerial reconnoitre visits in an old United Parachute Cloth Company observation blimp that was unable to navigate, and that therefore bounded along indefinitely over the countryside...", "2_15_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I got out of the car and asked the others what they thought. All the other cars were waiting, with drivers alert and watchful. Carlos said, \u201cMan, I got enough troubles of my own; forget it.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 was groggy and rubbing his eyes. Finally he said, \u201cLet's get the girls.\u201d\nBenny led us down the street and into the hotel. In the lobby we met an elderly businessman -- owner of the hotel -- and he got a shocked look when he saw us coming through the door with a pimp.", "0_7_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty in the morning</span>. I had not slept at all. I made coffee, and watched the sun coming up through the trees across the narrow river, and watched the towboats going by on their way down to Baton Rouge and New Orleans. I tried to begin the story about the girl in New Orleans. I wrote two pages and then stopped. I was unable to go on with it. I realized the story had no direction and never would have any. It was simply a few incidents and the character sketch of a girl. I threw it aside and walked down to the corner and mailed it to the editor in New York. Then I walked up to Carmencita's driveway. It was an interesting old orangegrove plantation house. The land around it had gone to seed. There was not another house in sight. I climbed over a broken wire fence and started through the weeds for the house. Then I saw that someone had either just gotten up or was just going to bed in a window in the house. It was a white, colonial window, and there was a woman in it. I hurried out of the weeds, climbed over another wire fence, and saw the girl from the night before. She came through the yard to me, walking fast, seeming surprised but glad to see me.", "2_19_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nColonel Colt's son, who was a lieutenant of engineers and knew more about explosives than any man in the American Army, was saying, \"Wipe it off, will you?\" to a soldier who was putting a piece of tape over his watch. He didn't want to know what time it was. \"You wouldn't be such a goddam geek if you could see the time without coming off your perch,\" he said. He didn't want to know what time it was. \"Maybe we should go now,\" said the soldier wiping the moisture of condensation off the water pipes through which were passed the fuses leading to the charges out on the British shore. He was a Scot, an artificer from Edinburgh. \"Maybe we should.\"\n\"No,\" said the lieutenant.\nHe was a Tennessean. He called his men \"boys.\" He knew much more about demolitions than any man in the American Army.\n\"We should have gone an hour ago,\" said the Scot, probing for sincerity.\n\"I know,\" said the lieutenant. \"It\u2019s guts, I suppose.\"\n\"It\u2019s blasted, bloody, suicide!\" said the Scot.", "1_4_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>, said Mr Bankes to himself, looking at his watch. It would be exactly nine minutes past four in the morning exactly nine minutes from now, he thought. What should he do in those nine intervening minutes before the rockets went up into the air out there in the darkness over the sea? What could he possibly do, he asked himself, to pass the time? It was a very odd situation for him to find himself in. He thought of the long theories he had held with his wife about the future of war-of the days when mankind would find out that there was no need to fight any more; when central organizations of knowledge and transport could feed the whole world and supply the needs of every human being from one common stock inexhaustible. All that would be possible only when people no longer desired those inflamed situations which kept them apart, but found other ideals of excitement, and indeed other ways of living, though what these might be, Mr Bankes had never been able to imagine. Petrified by his own arguments, he had taken no steps to deal with the practical problems that would arise when it happened, and now he supposed it had happened. He was not very frightened, and he did not feel very strongly interested. That was the curious thing. He...", "1_18_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past six o'clock in the evening</span>, when $SURNAME0\u2019s instrumentality brought the Space Wanderer and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to a halt before the gates of the White House. The President, who had been shut up with his staff since two o\u2019clock that afternoon, and had eaten nothing but food from a vendingmachine, was starved for gossip and human company.\nThe President, who wore very thick glasses for myopia, was a rumpled, harried man who lacked almost everything in his life that pertains to a rewarding, exciting, and significant existence outside of politics. The one significant exception was a son born out of wedlock some years before. The White House had its own private investigator, and $SURNAME0\u2019s information on the young man was very complete. He was in his fourth year at Easy Programing School, a vocational institute of the lowest rank, in Des Moines, Iowa. His major, or general title, there was Television Repair, with minors in Radio Repair, Small Appliance Maintenance, and Household Cabinet Making. He was doing terribly. He had been on probation in each of the last three quarters, and the school\u2019s prediction of his baccalaureate status was Dismal Fail. He had never been asked out on a date,...", "1_2_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-four in the morning</span>. I went down to the allnight drugstore on Beverly Boulevard and had a cup of coffee. At three o'clock, after fiddling around in my room for a while, I decided to take a runover to Las Vegas. It wasn't because I wanted to see the Kid, because I didn't, it was because I wanted to try out my car on a long trip\u2014see how she handled at high speed on the desert roads. Maybe find some monsters lurking out there under the stars and scare the holy bejesus out of them by passing them at 100 m.p.h. and laughing maniacally. Course I could forget the maniacal laughter; it wouldn't make much of a sound above the wind and the engine. Yes, that was it ... a murderous midnight ramble across the Nevada desert in the snow-white GTO . . . dark obsidian sculptures lurking lasciviously in the silver arc- welder glow of my six barrels . . . then sudden access to pure whitewater consciousness in a howling rush on the shoulder of a mountain,,, and at that moment, a bright light in my eyes! A flashlight, flashing right in my face! What the hell? Let's have a look at your bell!", "0_20_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span> when Ernie stuffily blew a note on a bugle and Rose of Colorado with the assistance of two stocky Mexicans raised her voice in song. These were the four heavenly compositions written by Ricardo action Garcia himself.\n\"Away we go!\" cried $SURNAME0 gleefully. \"The roller coaster starts to roll!\"\nThe roller coaster was an oil refinery on fire.\nThe roller coaster was a dynamo about to explode.\nThe roller coaster was a rocket aimed at the earth by a Martian.\nAll three faraway things were absolutely certain to happen. It was in the nature of things.\nBEFORE AND AFTER\nWINSTON NILES FOORD did not lose his wife $FIRSTNAMEF0 in a true sense, so long as she was suspended in time, so long as she was merged with the Universe and did not actually touch the $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 Foord who remained in ordinary time.\nBut Earth was now out of alignment with the Universe, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 was about to touch again. This was her signal to return. She would materialize in thirty-nine minutes.\nAs she touched and materialized, she would find that thirty-eight minutes had elapsed in her perfect, timeless state.\nIn those thirty-eight minutes,...", "0_0_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 turned out the light and went to open the door and look out down the dark rainy street. There was a light in workmen\u2019s caf\u00e9s, and a taxi that had brought someone home. Everything was all right.\nNow $FIRSTNAMEB2 set the alarm clock for nine o\u2019clock, locked the rooms, turned on lights all over the building, and left cheerfully, carrying his money with him because he didn\u2019t trust the tenants.\nHe was home, in his own quarters, three minutes after twelve.\nThe morning was fresh. The labor groups marched fresh. The building trades marched fresh. The stores and kiosks along the route were doing a lively trade, selling to the lovers of life and health and gaiety who crowded the streets. Here a workman bought a child\u2019s kite on a stick. There a student bought a ball of string. So the crowds gave the sense of a country fair on a weekday.\nThe prime minister and his wife passed in a motorcar, smiling at the bright tumult. The prime minister, saluting, was recognized by everybody. Two girls who were skipping rope stopped skipping rope to wave at the smiling couple as they passed. Nobody recognized the girl in green.", "2_20_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight sixteen in the evening</span>. In fifteen minutes and thirty-seven seconds, everything would be over. It had taken that long just to get here\u2014crossing seven time zones and travelling over ten thousand miles.\n\"Mama and Grandpapa\u2014?\" said the fat woman.\nRandy didn\u2019t answer. He rolled over on his side. The disinfectant made the back of his throat wrinkle. The hangar echoed when the stretcher hit the floor. The only people left were the man in the wheel chair and the fat woman.\n\"Just goin to sleep,\" said the fat woman.\nThe man in the wheel chair was about a hundred and fifty years old. He was naked, and there was a pearl-handled revolver in his lap.\nHis name was James J. Bludso. It was the name in the wheel chair. Bludso was dead. But the wheel chair wasn\u2019t empty. There was still a person in it.\nThe lights were out at the top of Mount Monadnock. The lights were out everywhere.\nThe time was eight twenty-two in the evening.\n\"What happened to the lights?\" screamed $FIRSTNAMEB6 Maglio. \"Randy\u2014Randy!\"", "2_14_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-six in the afternoon</span>.\nThe manuscript was an autobiography.\n\"I am writing,\" the autobiography began, \"in terrible positiveness and accuracy, of the most momentous events in the most stupendous drama in the history of a insignificant Universe.\n\"I am old, and I know much. I shall die soon, and I know that at the instant of death I shall know many things that an instant before had eluded me forever.\n\"There is urgency in the telling of this story; there is\u2014how shall I put it?\u2014no time. And the urgency is based upon my conviction that the telling may not be done better than now, even though there are most bitter reasons why it be done poorly, haphazardly, and poorly.\n\"I shall begin:\n\"I was born in Hamilton, Republic of North America. The names in parentheses are, of course, fictitious, as are those of all participants in the drama of, and principal characters in, this story. I understand that the laws of libel make legends of the participants. I fear that slanderous legends of imaginary participants would be even worse.\n\"By the time this account is read, all persons with whom I have been intimately associated will be dead.", "0_15_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe man with the spatulate thumbs was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the materialization of a security system installed and maintained by the United States Embassy on orders from the President.\nThe state of war between the United States and Mars had escalated sharply in the past few minutes. $SURNAME0, like all other wartime embassy personnel, was prohibited by radio from communicating with either $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 or Annie Julia Sessions.\nIt was hoped, in this way, to keep the war as impersonal and as sanitary as a waterworks project in Brazil.\nThe probability that either $SURNAME0 or Mrs. Sessions would suffer more than a physical jolt when war was declared was greater than the possibility of their being struck by lightning They were figures in a concrete picture, intended to attract the eye from a distance, not to reward close scrutiny.\nNeither $SURNAME0 nor Mrs. Sessions was sure what was outside the iron fence that caged them.\nIt was conceivable that promises made in the past were being kept, and that both of them were being allowed to live out their lives in this picturesque location undisturbed, like wild birds.\nThere was no common language that $SURNAME0 and Mrs. Sessions could have used to find out...", "0_5_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-three in the morning</span>. I drove across the highway and into the parking lot of the first all-night cafe on the side. They were just opening. I ordered a big breakfast, with plenty of coffee, and ate wondering what to do next. I was only thirty-one miles from Surf City and the crazy things I'd been through in the last six hours were not over. As I paid the check, the owner said, \u201cThere's a bus come in ten minutes ago and stopped out front. A big station wagon with California license let eight people off and they went into the cafe. Want me to see who they are?\u201d My lucky day.\n\u201cSure,\u201d I said. \u201cThey're probably my friends. Are they nice looking young people?\u201d\n\u201cUh huh.\u201d\nHe came back with their names. It was the same group I'd gone with around North Beach.\nSo I wound up with my daughter's group in Pruie Town that morning. They were ready to embark; I climbed in with them, the bus left downtown San Francisco, and we headed straight for Monterrey and Old Bull Lee in his hideout on the Moon. It was getting to be coming down by the yard.\nx", "1_14_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until three o'clock in the afternoon</span> when I passed through all the empty streets of Old Town and came out of Calle de Rigoler-Bec. It was too hot to walk easily, so I stepped along and tried to remember how it had all been. At the monument of I don't know whom I stopped to look in the ancient vault where we used to buy two-cent drinks of anis. The cholo was back of his old stool, and he needed a shave again.\nI asked him how much a chico was.\n\u201cTwo pesetas.\u201d\n\u201cHell; don't you know about the war?\u201d\n\u201cThe war's over. You never heard of the armistice?\u201d\n\u201cHow much is a chico, then?\u201d\n\u201cOne peseta.\u201d\nI paid him and drank one black and bitter chico. Now there was nothing left of the war. I walked on through the Barrio G\u00f3tico, past the palace of the Archbishop with the green blinds, and out of the gate at San Jer\u00f3nimo. Now there would be a little snow this winter at the Quintana. Where would I be? Probably in some Montana village, reading the local paper...", "1_4_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span> when the red Buick went across the bridge, onto the main drag past downtown, out to Hollywood, the freeway north. It rode high on its suspension, with that lovely slap-slap-slap sound of a Foustanella tire on the great white desert highway.\nIt was eighty-five miles to Los Angeles, one hundred and thirty miles to San Francisco, seven hundred and twenty miles to Chicago. $FIRSTNAMEB3 could drive to Chicago in six days if he felt like it and nobody could try to stop him because he was so obviously a lunatic. Nobody dares try to stop a lunatic. Long before LA, I knew this was the last run; it was getting more and all the time. Indeed everybody sensed it, even the uncles in the Cadillac, who were all meek and quiet and afraid to talk to each other. It was dawn when we hit the great sad funhouse of LA and began the slow crawl up Hollywood Boulevard to Gazzetta's place, four miles away. I hadn't seen my daughter for fourteen months. Everything was lost. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was calling to strange ghosts on the corner. I had to straighten out and be a daddy. Further on I would return to my wandering prose truths and pomposities...", "2_10_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Won\u2019t you stay with us?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"No good-byes, please,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Whenever we\u2019re together,\" said $SURNAME0, \"there\u2019ll always be good-byes.\"\n\"I don\u2019t understand,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Death is always stalking us,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"You might at least tell me what\u2019s going to happen,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Life is what happens to us,\" said $SURNAME0, \"while we\u2019re making other plans.\"\nThe hand of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 trembled on the iron rail of the ship\u2019s bowplanking. His lips trembled. The tears on his cheeks trembled, too.\n\"You\u2019ve never seemed like God to me,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, \"but you seem less like a man now than anybody I\u2019ve ever seen.\" His voice cracked.\n\"Good-by,\" said $SURNAME0. He stepped into the little wind and vanished.\nThe wind died.", "0_14_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> of December 2, 2052, and the place was Amarillo, Texas. The saloon of the Last Chance Station was empty except for old Rebel Simon Riley, the retired slave, who sat with his back to the railroad, looking at an automobile junkyard across the street.\nOff in one corner of the back room of the saloon, Crofe had set up his young family. They were all asleep. Crofe had pointed out to $FIRSTNAMEB0 that a back room was, in the long run, cheaper than a front room. The back room didn\u2019t cost anything more, and all the utilities it needed could be strung up behind it.\nThere was a humming in the air-conditioning duct. There was a warm, yellow light in front of the back room. There was a soft hum and rattle in the vibrating plumbing. And that was practically all the background noise. No trains ran through Last Chance, Texas, at night.\nThe empty saloon had the soft, relaxed air of a really first-class hotel. It had a thick, deep pile of dust on the floor, the sifted, settling dust of many decades, ultimately penetrated by the feet of the soldiers of an endless file of marching soldiers.", "2_19_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past seven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earthling time, when the first Martian stepped out onto the dull, hammered-tin porch of his spaceship. The Martian wore a phosphorescent suit of unearthly fabric, and his head was encased in a phosphorescent helmet that looked like a derby.\nHe was so tall that he had to stoop slightly under the low-slung roof of the porch.\nHe adjusted the curious, knobbed, stick-like objects on his feet, clamped his stick-like arms akimbo, looked around.\nHis green, phosphorescent eyes went click! click! click! as they absorbed light.\nThe Martian had nothing to say. He didn't say anything.\nHe was surrounded by fifty-one other Martians, who were members of his crew. These other Martians made no more noise than he did.\nEach of the Martians examined the charred remains of sod and wheat fan that he found himself in.", "1_11_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-three in the morning</span>.\nIn the field of Mr. Banana I was in the midst of an unrecognized battle. Here were two great forces: Misty Ranger, on one side, with the forces of Trouble; and on the other side me, with no forces, just me.\nIf only I could get past the barricades and come to the side of Misty. That would be the side of Love.\nBut my path was blocked. Tiny were the barricades I faced -the obstructions were tiny, but great were their powers of obstruction.\nIncreasingly I felt at the mercy of forces beyond my control. A siege was being imposed upon me. I felt disposed to eat, to sleep in a long, hilarious sleep.\nThe racing currents in my brain were difficult, even dangerous.\nThere was an adversary bent on the destruction of my mind. And thus, in the midair of the Desert, I languished against invisible walls and ate chocolate bars.\n\"Oof!\" said someone, catching sight of my bizarre and pained and hideous existence. And then I heard a whirring noise, and I looked down and saw a little man in a flying chair whirring off into the darkness. The ineffectiveness of his flying chair, juxtaposed...", "2_5_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-six in the morning</span>. The sun was rising through gunsmoke, glinting off windows on Hillrise Drive, barely three blocks away\u2014but the assault had already been launched. I couldn\u2019t see it, but I knew it was there; my nerves told me. And then the crackle of a bullhorn on Ault Road, telling those people to leave the house across the street. Five thirty-six in the morning, and Operation Acoustic Kitty had gone into action.\nI kept my head down as I hurried across the street to the hotel. One uniformed cop, up there with the assault team, had already seen me\u2014but he was pointing a rifle and giving instructions to other officers. I was no longer a threat. I was just another doomed onlooker, along with a crowd of perhaps twenty people, all of them terribly excited by what they were seeing. Not me. I understood what was going on. I had learned from my sources, high in the national\u2014level narcotics trade, that the cops were about to make their big raid. I had to be here for it.", "0_23_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-six in the evening</span>. The hollow things went down and out of sight. The field sank. A sea entered and submerged the house, the island, and the pebble. The world turned and went dark. The last thing $FIRSTNAMEF2 saw was the silhouette of the only Wavecrest in the land, sailing away. She tore the communicator from her ear and hurled it away, sick at heart.\nHer head was humming. Perhaps she oughtn\u2019t to have unfastened the Berkeley button. In the back of her head something wanted to start talking to her, and a much smaller back of her head wanted to listen. Perhaps, she thought, butterflies sense the migraine hyper-sensitivity in the air, instinctively go blind at the exact instant their parasites arrive, shy away and vanish. Trace the vector in both directions, perhaps migraine and parasites evolved together,, evolving their prolonged season of pleasure, the world of each grown mutually dependent on the other\u2019s def mitely temporary withdrawal. It would all depend on whether she believed in ghosts. She didn\u2019t. Or did she? Did she really believe there was no such thing as a tram, a C Kinneret, a Pullman car with a rose window? Did she really believe her own...", "0_9_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty in the morning</span>. I had no watch and somehow no interest in knowing the time. The only thing that mattered was the bike and getting to Mexico City by dark. Now I had a gigantic cause for concern. My face was so badly swollen that one eye had closed into a slit and the cut on my chin had opened so wide that it made a weird whistling noise when I breathed. \u201cJesus,\u201d I thought, \u201cif somebody stops me an wants to know what happened I\u2019ll never be able to explain it.\u201d And there was every possibility of being stopped, since I was weaving all over the road without any consideration for the other drivers.\nBut they didn\u2019t stop me. One crazy bastard honked at me, but this was nothing new. All over Denver, on previous mornings when I should have been caught in the rushhour, I had been the object of angry frustration. The people there have never accepted the fact that there is nothing you can do about rushhour; it\u2019s a condition, like gravity, and all you can do is accept it. That\u2019s why I was moving so slowly. A normal speed\u2014for a sane person\u2014would have been about ten miles an hour.\nMy only chance...", "1_5_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span> when the entry of our gang into Denver the completed. The money was spent, the Cadillac was gone, my attorney was in tears, my attorney \u201cs6 girlfriend was gone, my attorney's kids were gone. me alone was left. And at that crucial moment, just as things were at their absolute worst, I looked up and saw a roach crawling slowlv along the manila \u201cWhere were you, man?\u201d I screamed at the roach. \u201cWhat the fuck were you doing in Singapore, man?\u201d Then I grabbed the thing and sank my teeth right into it. No, I bit off its head and left the body dangling; and just when! thought I was finished, that I was empty, suddenly I secrets liquid shit spurting into my mouth and I threw myself against a stucco wall and began 9 I rest of the nlgbt, to warn myselfPno eat that way! Not the head! The head has the worst poison! Nevertheless, I was doomed. Somewhere in my body I had a full dose of venom. My hands ached, my feet throbbed. But a supreme calm came over me as I realized that I was at the end of my line. This was it. This...", "2_6_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-six in the morning</span>.\nThe sun had not yet lifted, although light was staining the dark.\nHere is a man who at that very moment was hurrying through far space, beyond the Milky Way, on his way home to the Solar System.\nThere is hardly anybody in the world who does not, at this very moment, receive mail. The man in far space was no exception, although so much of his mail seeped through the dimensions from other worlds over which he passed that it was as strange to him as anything delivered by space ship, rocket, cargo carrier, or carried by extraterrestrials.\nThe man\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEB0. All the acquaintances he had ever had in the world were dead.\nHis only source of information regarding the state of the world outside was his correspondence.\nHis only pleasures were the reading and answering of letters.\nThere was a letter in a pouch under his left arm.\nHe did not know who had sent the letter. His name, $FIRSTNAMEB0, had been written on the outside of the letter in a beautiful hand. The letter was sealed with purple wax, imprinted with a monogram\u2014an ornate H.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 sensed that the letter had something to do with a woman.", "0_12_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-six post meridiem</span>, the place was an office in the Crystallite Building, downtown, the city was Phenochrista.\n\"Hell, what years?\"\n\"Say 1960 through 1965.\"\n\"Want them alphabetically or chronologically?\"\n\"Take your pick.\"\n\"1960, \u201962, \u201963\u2014those are the only ones we searched so far.\"\n\"Look at 1963.\"\n\"Princeton twenty-nine, Dartmouth nineteen, Lehigh thirteen, Holyoke eight\u2014\"\n\"I watch football too,\" said Lancaster McMahon.\n\"\u2014John Tarwater, eleven; score unclear, a body was recovered.\"\n\"A body was recovered after every game, back then,\" said McMahon. \"The important thing is whether Holyoke was ahead at any time in the game.\"\n\"No, they never were.\"\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\"Want me to get somebody who remembers?\"\n\"Not necessary, never mind.\"\n\"So why\u2019d you want that particular game?\"\n\"A nostalgic impulse.\"\n\"Radical, Mississippi State twenty-six, Holyoke zero.\"\n\"Thank you,\" said McMahon. \"Thanks just the same.\"\n\"Fire away.\"\n\"1960.\"\n\"Princeton four, Dartmouth...", "2_23_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-five in the evening</span>.\nThe bass rhythm in the next room stopped with an iron clank. The harmonium started moaning, softly.\nThere was a knock on the wall.\nGroverman again pressed the button in the wall, and again his beret came off silently in a faint cloud of green smoke.\nBefore him, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was a wild man. His hands were clamped to his lips. Tears were leaking from his blazing eyes.\nKnock knock knock.\nThen the wall opened, and a chorus of harmoniums blatted and moaned in greeting. There were fifty-two harmoniums, and each one had a white face and a rust-red shag. They wore green berets and maroon uniforms and green boots and maroon gloves. Their maroon capes were all embroidered with silver in a special pattern that symbolized RI.\nThe harmoniums all pressed weapons to their lips, fired the weapons point-blank at $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face.\nA hailstorm of green bits and red flame burst around $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nBra-a-a-a-a-a-a-a-ape went the harmoniums.", "2_4_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. There was not a sound on Lake Street in Vegas as we drove. The radio was playing Mantovani. I was keeping the tempo with one foot, and ruminating soul-centered thoughts: that in fact I was so weary, in all my bones, that the only possible place for me to sleep was in the cabin of my good red Shark; so there I had envisioned crawling, to rest my eyes and brain in the fish-gut motel air of the king\u2019s automobile. It frightened me, to think about sleeping in this silent town. And it was unwise. There were thieves in Las Vegas and a thousand disguised Hazaras waiting to take my head.", "0_5_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. All of mankind lay folding and unfolding in a titanic sleep. It was a snoring sleep, a nose-wiping sleep, an innocent, fumbling sleep.\nAnd then, out of the East, came the light. It was not the light of the moon. It was the light of a sun boiling in a black sky.\nThe light approached, and it turned the color of the light salmon.\nAnd then the light touched $SURNAME1\u2019s home. A blast of heat followed the light. The rock walls flaked off their cool skin.\n$SURNAME1 awoke to flames.\nHe saved his family, carrying each of them outside in his arms.\nThe members of his family were so flamingly beautiful that strangers wept. The strangers could not imagine who had painted such stunning models.\nThe flames went higher and higher, and the Saltonstalls were consumed. And when all of flesh was gone, the creatures formed of heat and light climbed higher still, to be nearer the heat and light. They hovered near the heat and light, tasted it, basked in it.\nThe fire did not diminish.\n\"God, \" said every living thing in the Solar System, \"I can get used to this.\"", "2_1_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThe President threw off his blankets and climbed out of his bunk. Ever since he had been declared candidate for the Vice-Presientcy, there had been parties to attend every day. Life was very hectic and terribly exciting.\nThe President had had no sleep in thirty-one hours. He was drunk. \"Whatsa matter?\" he bellowed. \"Who died?\"\n\"Pardon, sir?\" said the pilot.\n\"Who died?\" roared the President.\n\"Nobody died, sir,\" said the pilot. \"We don\u2019t know who died. At least two persons, so far as we can tell.\"\n\"What do you want me to do about it?\" said Salmagundi.\nThe pilot seemed hurt. \"Oh, nothing, I suppose,\" he said.\n\"What is this?\" said Salmagundi.\nThe pilot handed him a message\u2014flashed to Earth by the pilot\u2019s ship. The message contained an itinerary\u2014a time-tabulated program of things to be done. The time-table was prefaced by a message, which read as follows:", "2_10_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAn expression of hungry joy flitted across $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s bearded face\u2014the joy of a glutton expected to eat his first bite of cr\u00e8me de menthe after eating nothing else for a year.\nThe face of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 turned to marble. The veins on her forehead stood out like gills, testifying to her heroic effort to sublimate her mad jealousy of Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 Mumm.\nGale, the former Miss $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 of Cleveland, Ohio, began to laugh. The laugh was high and whinnying, like a broken satire on the tittering of polite company. It was punctuated by beatitude in the form of tears and hiccups.\nThe time was three minutes to eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThere was a trestle before the Space Museum entrance.\nOn the trestle sat a truck on wheels of solid gold.\nThe truck was red with gilded lettering.\nThe gilded lettering read: MRS. WINSTON NILES MUMM.\nThe gilded lettering was one foot high.", "1_23_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-six in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 got a flashlight from the desk, turned it on. Some say the world will end in fire, he thought as he turned the beam on the big wooden doors. Some say in ice.\nHe did not know which, but he decided to open the doors anyway and give the recruits their choice.\nHe flung one of the doors open.\nThe file of recruits shuffled forward in the flashlight beam.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son, $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was leading the file, dropped to his knees as the light hit him in the eyes.\n\"You big son-of-a-bitching God!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nThe recruits behind him howled, pressed him to his knees.\n\"Make us rich!\" cried one. \"Make us fly!\" cried another.\n\"Paradise, Idaho!\" cried another. \"Paradise, Idaho!\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0!\" young $FIRSTNAMEB2 shouted, and then he fainted.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 carried him back inside the building, wondering how heavy a burden a son could be.\nSergeant $SURNAME2\u2019s whistle had already blown. The whip-lashing, double time tramp had begun. The new recruits joined in, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 followed their...", "2_20_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-three in the evening</span>.\nA surgical team had come and gone. The big, black, leathery-winged thing that had come with the team had carried away one of the casualties on a stretcher. The members of the team had been pleased that the casualties got along so well without their brains. Except for the gurgling sounds made when the liquids slipped in and out of their speech centers, the casualties had been silent. Who could have been proud of the system that had given the casualties the ability to get along so well without their brains? Who could have been proud of the system that had given $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the boy the same ability?\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put a big blue lump of Martian synthesk in his mouth, sucked it lavishly. Like all food since the unpleasant occurrence, it came in the paint-can. $FIRSTNAMEB0 tore at the yellow label on the bottom of the can, spat it out, read:\nThe paint-can was empty again.\nGood old Mother Jones!\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 thrust the empty paint can at the beautiful child. \"Fff-fill \u2019er up!\" he said. The beautiful child did so.", "1_11_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-six in the morning</span> of June 5, 1949, and for my money the sea had never, in all the tides since creation, looked lovelier. Her hands were rough from the work she did. She was called the sea. But, oh, those hands could be so soft, so very soft.\nShe finished the morning in splendid style, with a very large wave indeed, and when it passed under us the Tomahawk swooped, squatted, faltered-and then hung poised, motionless in the air, a hundred and fiftyfeet above the rocks below.\nAnd then the wave, unkindly, broke.\nA day for bastards, for pariahs, for men staring straight ahead, blind with Bryant and Carter painters\u2019 brushes, paint dripping from their noses as they stumble up stairs, aim poorly, punch holes in fresh plaster, taking jazz from the radio in the corner . . . a day for hunters from the motorless bays, wind-burned, eyes stinging from salt, casting plugs overside and dropping nets . . . a day for sailors leaning against starboard rails, watching the slow roll of the waves and thinking fabulous, unattainable thoughts . . . a day for lonely men and women dressed...", "1_8_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span> when Robert Cohn's taxi shoved up to the doorway of the Hotel Napol\u00e9on. Cohn stepped out, shaking the rain from his hat, put the umbrella in the rack, and pushed the glasses up on his head. He started in to get his baggage, but they smiled and told him there were no bags to get. They smiled and told him everything was taken care of.\nHe asked how that was and they said very nicely.\nAnd some one had taken his two trunks, his tennis bag, his golf clubs, and his typewriter right up to his room. Yes, they told him, the young lady had had a room on the floor above. He was in 506 and she was in 510. He was just above and she was just above. They had had a very good time last night. She certainly could dance. They had danced from twelve straight on to four o'clock this morning. The next time she wanted to go any where at night she must ask him to take her. He had had such a wonderful time. She was such a wonderful dancer. She knew how to dance but so did he. She really was a wonderful dancer. Everybody had seen how wonderful she was. He would like to go places with her any time...", "0_17_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>. It was the nineteenth of June.\nIn the great silence and stillness and in the knowledge of the great dignity, precision, and efficiency of the N took alarm at his own efforts. He felt something that was like pain in his lungs. He sensed a dryness in his mouth and in his throat that became, somehow, like a choking.\nHe seldom cried, but now he felt as though he might cry. He hoped he would not cry. He thought of his Aunt Rose and of how she had said she would come and see him in September.\nHe felt as though he might cry, but he would not\u2014that is, if he could help it.\nOnly once had he cried out of his own volition and of his own accord. The tears had come up so, and they had come so fast, that they had dazzled him, and he had put his hands over his face, quite instinctively, as a little boy does, and he had cried. When he uncovered his face, there was the writing-desk, and he had stared at the writing-desk and told himself severely not to be a fool. He had scolded himself.\nHe had put out the whole thing to think about at another time, for...", "0_19_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-seven in the evening</span>.\nRandy was to deny these attacks later, on oath, and to claim that, having passed through the lobby on his way to the lavatory, he simply gaped at the crowd when he heard the rumor that a space ship was due to land, and gaped even wider when a scream momentarily Section Manager Gabriellean aside and he glimpsed, finally, the thin and lank Spiridoni.\nBut these were lies.\nThe truth is that Randy went straight to his room, called the operator, and told her to get him a Marslin Building number. And he did this because he knew, by some divine spark within him, that the Space Wanderer was in Room 541 of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Hotel. And after he had hung up the phone, he dressed quickly, picked up his attache case, and went downstairs, out of the lobby, and boarded a taxi that took him straight to the door of the Marsling Building. He gave the doorman his name, went through the terrible gauntlet of the lobby, heard the rumor about the old KCUF theology professor in Side 2, row 28\u2014his chair in flames at the hands of a disorderly rabble\u2014and combed the crowd for any face that even resembled the face of Rum...", "2_19_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-three in the evening</span> when NormanMailer finally carne to Newton, Massachusetts. The house in which he had been born was opened once a year for paying, curious tourists. The house had windows on all four sides, and it was on stilts.\nNorman Mailer pounded on the door with a rock from the garden.\nNo one heard him.\nNo one was in the house.\nNo one had ever been in the house.\nThe house was the strongest, most expensive home ever built in Newton, Massachusetts.\nIt was empty because the Mailer family had left Newton almost forty years before, rich, educated, and to pursue a strange destiny, like a headache. The house was empty because no one had ever wanted to live there.\nThe house shivered slightly in the night wind in Newton. The windows of the house rattled. The windows of the house stared obsessively up and down Elm Street.\nOut on an unlit stretch of Elm street, a demented cat watched the windows of the empty house.\nThe demented cat him- or herself stared back into the windows.\nThe cat blinked, silently.\n\"Lang! Hey, Lang!\" shouted Norman Mailer.\nNo one heard him.", "2_20_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-four in the evening</span>. On Titan, Phoebe, the moon of Saturn, was not quite directly between Saturn and Titan. Through the rift in the clouds came an intermittent moonlight from Titan. The clouds were seas and the moon was a boat that sailed across the seas, a soft boat that diffused a milk-white radiance as it sailed.\nThe time was eight twenty-five.\nThe gangplank of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. $SURNAME0 was lowered to the ground.\n\"You know something?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Bringing you here has been the first really good thing I\u2019ve done since I got back.\"\n\"Thanks,\" said $SURNAME1 humbly.", "1_23_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-two in the evening</span>, on May Day. A five-minute egg was thirteen and seven-eighths seconds from cackling in the white Calomel oven. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s eyes were two wet red pebbles. He was standing in the middle of the harmonium, bathed in its blue light, his hands held tragically apart, his head hung low. The harmonium had given him diarrhea\u2014had given him more diarrhea than he could possibly handle. And he had been thrown into such a state of terror, bewilderment, and thirst that all that he could do, to keep from passing out, was to repeat to himself a meaningless phrase of seventeen words, a phrase from a popular song:\n\"On the twelfth day of Christmas, my true love gave to me\u2014\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s voice dissolved in a throaty rumble. He had forgotten the next line. His heart beat with a ram\u2019s head fury against his ribs. He looked down at his boots, blinked wetness out of his eyes.\nThe boots were watery red now.\nAnd then $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head snapped up.\nA row of tiny blue lights had come on in a horizontal line over the communication desk. The lights spelled out...", "1_2_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a space of approximately ten square feet where the tile meets the carpet in the corridor just this side of the ladies' room in the $SURNAME0 Zoo.\nIt was a very persons space, that spot in the corridor.\nHalf of it was occupied by $FIRSTNAMEB0. The other half of it was occupied by a harmonium with a broken bellows. The harmonium had been given to $FIRSTNAMEB0 as a mascot by Sergeant $SURNAME2, the same man who had given $FIRSTNAMEB0 his taped instructions.\nThe instructions had told $FIRSTNAMEB0 what he was expected to do when he arrived at the Zoo.\nAnd there he was, right down to the last parenthesis, right down to the last semicolon.\nHe fit right in with the decor.\nThe corridor was a color riot. The walls were done in aquamarine brocade. The ceiling was done in a band pattern of oyster white and dirty white shot silk. And the carpet on the floor was sapphire blue\u2014with the figures of gold-tasseled paisleys scattered thickly all over.\nThe paisleys ran riot within a ten-square-foot area proximal to $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the harmonium.\nIt was a room...", "1_11_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-nine in the morning</span> of October thirtieth, a bright, beautiful morning in Kansas. I bought gas and food, and was soon on my way, feeling somewhat better. I stopped a ramshackle place with plywood walls and asked directions. Luckily the family that ran it knew exactly where the railroad crossing was located -- exactly two miles east of their door. I felt like I had found the hole in the space -time continuum. \u201cTravel another four or five miles on 66,\u201d they said, \u201cand you'll come to Salina -- you can't miss it.\u201d And so I headed for Salina with visions of John Dillinger going down there with FBI men at his heels, holding up banks and running big times along 66 -- the main drag of America in the thirties -- at that time the longest and straightest stretch of road in the land. Gary, Indiana, was at one end and Pueblo, Colorado, was at the other -- 906 miles of straight, flat, two-laned asphalt flyingroad going through every kind of Man territory -- mostly through the kind of America known as \u201cnormal\u201d and inhabited by normal people. But they know nothing about the sudden twisty curves that are coming up on you fast...", "2_0_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past midnight</span>.\nCHAPTER\nThe water in the basin was not clean and I washed in another basin. I put on clean linen and packed all of our things, putting them in a bigger suitcase. The driver was asleep on the big bed. $FIRSTNAMEB5 woke him up. He wanted to get a little sleep. It was a hell of a way for him to make a living. We were an hour and a half late but $FIRSTNAMEB5 said it did not matter as the train would be twenty-four hours late anyway. We gave the driver fifty pesetas. $FIRSTNAMEB5 wanted to tip him fifty more but I stopped her. We started to walk out of town and came to a bus. $FIRSTNAMEB5 said the driver would think we were going to take it out of his fare, so we walked along the road out of town and met a cart going into town. We picked up twopacks and the boy camped with the cart drove us out of the town.", "1_13_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe year was 1949.\nThe ship from which the woman made her strange dirge was an eight-hundred-ton, two-masted schooner. It carried a crew of thirty-eight. It was a good ship, square-rigged and well-caulked, skippered by a very competent and likable fellow.\nIts name was $SURNAME0.\nPerhaps you have heard of $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0 was the author of a highly successful book, The Pocket Guide to Space.\nThe book was small, had a flexible covers and a spiral binding.\nThe book contained\n(1) a simple explanation of such fundamentals as: What is Space? What makes a Good Sport? What is a $FIRSTNAMEB2? and Who was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0?\n(2) a complete list of the Great Numbers by which the positions and periods of the spirals and of the Tralfamadorians were known.\n(3) a special section for sportsmen who might, at some future time, make the gigantic adventure of a trip to Tralfamadore.\n(4) all the known facts about $SURNAME0 himself.\n(5) all the known words that Rum...", "0_7_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-two in the morning</span>, and I was tuned to the greatest sound in the world\u2014a new day in America. All night I\u2019d been chasing this sound up and down the AM dial...and now I knew: I had found it. 570 kilocycles. Denver, Colorado. The day had just begun in the Rockies, and some natural fox named Mooney, who was not even a professional broadcaster, was holding down this fine wavelength long enough for me to mail a precious letter to my lobster in the Connecticut Colony:\nMy Darlin Husband\u2014\nJust a touch of honey to waken your inner Ear in this grey dawn and the first Beep of a brand new America. Listen. These are the sounds of the witch of rush hour in our Drag City stream, and much of the dope that comes out will be numb and flat, since most of these fellows can barely whisper above the great din they put through their brainpan. But high above the screams of the street I can make out the clear message of your Radio Man, a rare and special intellect who plays a private music only we can share. He is on early in the morning and the influences are still that of night and dreams\u2014Odes to the post horn and cornflakes, golden horns, boats...", "0_1_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-three in the morning</span>. She was stepping on the gas. Her exhaust growled. She was fleeing from those hilarious, those terrifying mummies. $FIRSTNAMEF2 stood up in the cab and screamed wordlessly into the black night: how lonely everybody\u2019s night is; yet how crowded, how densely filled at this moment with her own dread and wonder at the world, the galaxies, the trillion shouting kilometers of darkness, with forms moving mysteriously in it like memory. Standing up, swaying, weightless, she felt completely divorced from the earth and its mute companions, the towns, their screams; estranged also from her own people, even the husband she had left, who must be lying by now in his rented bed and turning over on his sleepless side, wondering.\nShe knew as if granted revelation that there was no pity in the universe for one\u2019s loneliness: the loneliness was necessary to its beauty, as the dark was to the day. She drove on, to cross over winding mountain passes, slowing for the herds of mule deer; for miles on end she might not see another vehicle, only an abandoned mine shaft or prospect hole with its slope of yellow broomsage and wild purple irises near a white scar in the hills. Whenever she came to such...", "2_16_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. \u201cHoly crow, the sun over Rochelle will set in a couple hours and there will be time to make San Jose. What we do is get back to the other car in San Luis Obispo and immediately drive like mad to San Jose --\u201d\n\u201cI don't want to drive any more bitching miles. We've bitched across the continent on a put-put rattletrap rail.\u201d\n\u201cWe can make it. We'll leave here immediately.\u201d\n\u201cRight, now don't waste any time thinking. Think born.\u201d One wait was only long enough to rush the last bucks of money to the grocer, leave our frantic address in the grocer's book, buy hot-rods on the street, snap their handles in half, and cram them in the car; ride like mad back to San Luis Obispo, where we found the car in front of a bakery where Chad was eating pies; make a quick sandwich of gas-station cheese and wheat bread and make San Jose by early evening and drop in Ed Dunkel's Tom Jones place on Divisadero Street.", "2_11_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven seventeen in the morning</span>. The place was New York, N.Y. The identity of the person being interviewed on the Trinitron was Timothy Appleton.\nBeeber was wearing tan gabardine slacks; a white, pullover shirt unbuttoned at the collar; soft, black, four-hole fingerless gloves; and a blue turtleneck sweater tied in a knot at his throat. He was still very much like a blond, blue-eyed little boy.\n\"Oh,\" he said out loud on 75th Street, \"this is not possible. They\u2019re nuts, they\u2019re all nuts.\" The address of the penthouse apartment was 1280. The name of the building was the Spencer M. Partridge Memorial Building.\nBeeber got up and went to the window of his penthouse apartment. His penthouse apartment had seven rooms, faced east and west, and took up the whole top floor of the Spencer M. Partridge Memorial Building. It had a view of over eighty blocks of New York.\nThe August sun was beating through the windows of the penthouse apartment onto the rug in the bedroom. The bedroom rug was orange.", "1_2_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two o'clock in the morning</span> when I stopped to gas up in Pasadena. The attendant was a happy little old white-haired guy who had a wild comic strip panel stuck up on the windshield of the gas station. It read: \u201cDollars, that's what I want. No letters, no flowers, no charming childhood remembrances. Just little old green bucks that I can put in my pocket.\u201d I pointed to it and told him, \"You might add 'God damn!'\"\n\u201cYes indeed, God damn.\u201d He chuckled. I asked him what he meant by it. He said he was just a working man trying to get by with things the best he knew how. \u201cBut what does it mean?\u201d I insisted. \u201cYou just said 'Is my work done?' What work? What did you mean?\u201d\n\u201cWell now, you're a stranger, and I'm a man that likes to talk, and we've got all night, and they can't start the gas-treatment plant again for three hours.\u201d He lit a cigarette. \u201cI used to be a minister -- years ago -- but I'd go to a church some ten or fifteen years and find myself looking down on the same faces...", "0_3_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. Unwin now felt as if a great deal of strenuous activity had been going on inside his head for some hours, though not a sound had been heard. He waited to hear a sound. He looked over his desk at his assistant, Miss Varner, in the hope that she might have sensed the vibrations and would corroborate his own impressions by groaning, or by sighing out a word, or by sucking air loudly between her teeth. It was a nice, a most gratifying instance of the influence Unwin had slowly and painfully acquired over this normally garrulous woman that when the thing happened she did not speak. Her eyes did widen, but that was all. She turned in her swivel chair, without a groan or a sigh, to look out the window at her own huddled shape, dim and vague and gray. Insubstantial. The four jet streams in the sky formed themselves into geometries of incredible intricacy and broken symmetry. The topology of madness\u2014\nNothingness next door. Space becomes Time and eats you up. Infinity to the right of you, ladies and gentlemen. Mystery blooming in the gardens of the clock. Why look now, here is the fourth dimension: height, depth, length, and...", "0_1_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>. The hospital room and the sleeping navigator were a floor below my own suite, at the end of a dimly lit wing where no one had been, so far, rooming. There was nothing, not even a house ghost, to disturb my shaky frame of mind. Hell, there was no conceivable nightmare I could have concocted to top the banshee truth about to get under way with me. Where Harohn was now, behind the still closed door ahead at the end of the hall, was the world\u2019s first high\u2014wire act performed without a net at half\u2014past\u2014mortal\u2014jeopardy. What he was attempting was to put a half\u2014destroyed, heavily doped\u2014up human body on a hospital bed above an operating table while conscious and aware\u2014all without benefit of an anesthetic. Years ago I had this same experience in what they call a watchmaking putthead. A broken watch had come in, and the firm\u2019s watchmakers were attempting, without benefit of textbooks or service manuals, to put the damn thing together again and get it running...on a assembly line between bunak baskets, ignored by the boss and threatened with poverty at the end of a day if the watch didn\u2019t run...", "2_7_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> on Mars.\nThe man was still gazing into the waters of the pond.\nThe image of the farmer and his ball was gone.\nThe image of the man\u2019s home was gone.\nThe Universe was still.\nOne minute later, old Earth time, the man turned around in the thigh-deep water.\nHe duck-waddled back to his flying saucer, climbed in, sat down, closed the canopy.\nHe pressed an enormous button.\nThe saucer trembled.\nThe trembling became a shake.\nThe shake became a roar.\nThe roar became a blue-white flame that scorched twelve thousand Earthlings in an instant.\nThe flame became a pillar of fire that reached out into space and split a rock the size of a small office building in half.\nIt was three minutes after seven o\u2019clock in the morning on Mars.\nThe man flew back to the picnic grounds where he had left the ambassador.", "0_6_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-one in the morning</span> of Monday, October twenty-seventh, and the place was a wide sandy space in the desert outside a small Utah town. It was not an ideal space for an airplane to land, because there were chop-lines and metal tubes set up there by security men. But the space was all right, and the amazing ship that was going to land there had celestial tendencies.\nAt that moment, it was dangling directly over the little town. The dandy bark beetles floating in their colored oils in the windows of the pilot cubicle looked down on fields and houses and the Salt-Lake City Freeway, as peaceful and white at that hour as could be imagined. But the ship\u2019s groined, shelllike hull blocked out the freeway traffic and some of the houses. And now the stern-mounted thrusters gave off white asbestos arcs and pushed the ship down toward the flat ground outside the town. The formation took a long time to break up.\nThe pilot ship landed and quietly settled onto the desert floor with a roar and a bounce and drafts of burnt rubber. Official cars surrounded it, reporters and cameramen poured out, cheering and laughing. They were met by four portly men, who trotted over to meet them.\nThe reporters and cameramen...", "1_4_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-eight in the morning</span>, and our stolen rental car was buried in the sand at the bottom of the flats. We were a quarter -mile from the highway...out of gas, out of beer and smack in the middle of the annual police convention. Another day or two and they would have found it themselves; then taken to the airwaves in a radio/copter manhunt that would have left Bonnie and Clyde marooned in a cloud of white dust...red flashes bouncing off their teeth while they ran behind red mesquites..\nSince I had left my wallet in the glove compartment\u2014with my driver's license and the car-rental papers\u2014it was up to my attorney to try to retrieve the car. He had to bribe the night clerk at the Superstition Oasis Motel; then bluff his way past the scene of various felonies along the Witch Way to the desk clerk at the Budget lot.\nexplain indiana farmer way...1? 11 me my car\nMy attorney said, \u201cTell him you have to get this fucker back to California; he left his fuckin' wallet in the glove compartment..\nWe both laughed as he spoke, but he broke off in mid\u2014laugh and stared at the car: \u201cLook!\u201d", "2_11_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a sumptuous office in Moscow.\nThe place was the office of the Soviet Minister of Postage and Telecommunications, Igor Briskin. His wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, was sipping a Scotch-and-water.\nThe dispenser was in the shape of a golden Soviet telephone booth, from the outside of which real telephone-booth footage and GTE stock footage was being shown on the Trinitron.\nThe telephone booth footage and the stock footage were being shown with the help of three perfect-looking little old ladies who sat in a waist-high band set up on tripods between the Trinitron and the golden Soviet telephone booth.\nOne of the little old ladies tightened the straps on her spectator shoes, tightened the straps of her gold-imitation-silk turban, and tightened the straps of the American flag on her blouse. She finished tightening, paused momentarily with her mouth in the shape of the O that had begun the tightening. Then she began to work the controls of the image-glut machine again.", "1_21_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span> when I clonked the shot-silvered door behind me and stepped out of the sacred precincts of the long, long, long corridor into the sacred precincts of the long, long, long bar. I walked over to the bar and stood leaning against its concrete longitude. Fifteen minutes to close. A few American sailors sat on stools at the far end chatting with the bartender. Some of the tables were still occupied. Nobody but Rocky was on the long, long, long bar. He was polishing the mahogany like mad, whistling a wicked tune.\n\"What'll it be, pal?\" he said.\n\"Whisky sour.\"\n\"Right. Nice to have you back, pal.\"\nHe made the drink and gave it to me with a good glassful of ice.\n\"Thank you, sir.\"\n\"Many happy returns, pal.\"\n\"Cheers, friend.\"\n\"If there's anything you want to know while you're up here, pal\u2014just ask me.\"\n\"There is one thing, friend.\"\n\"Yes, pal.\"\n\"What the hell's screwball, anyway?\"\nHe threw his head back and laughed long, laughed loud, laughed hard.\n\"Screwball...", "1_18_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-six in the evening</span> when I got to the Gresham. The desk clerk had a telegram. I opened it and the telegram was from $FIRSTNAMEB5. It read: \u201cNot with Robert.\u201d\nChecking in, I asked the bell-hop what he knew about the Count of Ascoli.\n\u201cOh, he\u2019s out at the bull-ring, sir.\u201d\nI went up-stairs worried. I was explaining to myself that of course it was not $FIRSTNAMEB5 who was with the Count but the other way around. It was what might be called a mixed explanation, rather sadistic, and it enabled me in some way to be pleasant to a chambermaid who was very pleasant and friendly. I tipped her fifty cents and lost my nice feeling of antiseptic s\u00e9cheresse. I went into the bathroom and took a shower. The water was nice and cold. The cold water stopped me thinking. I did not want to think anymore.\nWhen I came out of the bathroom I knew I had to think again. The chambermaid had made up the room and put the shaving things back on the shelf. One of them was $FIRSTNAMEB5\u2019s little pigskin case. I remembered I had picked up the cases in her...", "1_15_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three sixteen in the afternoon</span>, and despite the fact that we had sufficient mescaline, acid and hash to insure outright hostilities, we made a quick stop at the Chamber of Commerce to pick up a few souvenirs...tourist pieces like Indian arrowheads and green beer mugs with wiggle eyes on them. In the back seat our attorney was holding a tape recorder to his ear. \u201cWhat\u2019s that?\u201d asked the woman beside him. \u201cIt\u2019s the roar of the crowds,\u201d he said. \u201cCan you hold it up for me?\"\nWe were scoring heavily on the acid, and our speed was now up to about a hundred thirty. The only real problem left was die Fuzz. We swore repeatedly that we would never be busted in Las Vegas\u2014that we\u2019d blow our brains out first\u2014because in Vegas they\u2019re slow and stupid, with nothing but horserace buggers and teetery old coots in the District Attorneys\u2019 office: no young thugs, no movement freaks, plus the fact that there was a huge rumor out in the desert that they were planning to crucify a punk next year. Then again, we knew that L.A. was just another prison...", "0_8_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. It was the date of December 12, 2251. The sun, shining in a clear sky, said it was summer.\nSo $FIRSTNAMEB1 went to the iron staircase and ascended to the bronze hatchway in the floor of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s barracks room. Above the hatchway were two large, trumpetlike brass tubes. One tube emptied downward into the space under the floor. The other tube blew up from the space under the floor. It was the trumpet of a one-way flute, a natural acoustical phenomenon. Whenever the soldier in the basement pressed a rubber pumper against the hole in the floor, air rushed up the flute, announced itself to all parts of the barrack with deep, ominous, descending tones\u2014DOOM, DOOM, DOOM, DOOM.\nAnd any soldier who stood at the open front of a basement could press a rubber pumper against the rising tube and send an ascending scale of notes screeching through the flute\u2014Twank, Twank, Twank, Twank. It was a beautiful, terrible sound that filled every soldier with joy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 pressed his Pavoni against the hole\u2014press, release, press, release, press, release, press, release, press. At...", "1_7_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> of November 11, 1949, when $FIRSTNAMEB0 seeing a small fire coming from the rod in his hand, understood that the thing shooting flames was alive, angry, in pain.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 dropped it from a shaking hand.\nA thing on the floor grayed and smoked and made a sticky sound unk.\n\"Man\u2014\" said Boone, \"you about scared me to death.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 found the harmonium under the bunk. The harmonium had orange tape wound over the fingering holes to tell $FIRSTNAMEB0 which holes to leave uncorked when he was playing the blues. The harmonium had lost a note\u2014had lost a little felt cup that half-covered a hole at the upper left-hand corner of the keyboard. The felt cup was a mute\u2014and the harmonium had lost the note that the mute would have dampened. $FIRSTNAMEB0 curled the tape up in a neat roll. He corrected the faulty harmonium tuning with a felt-tip pen.\n\"Douse that light, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" came a sweet, sleepy voice from the bulkhead above the bunk. The light was the flashlight that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had stuck in his belt. The light in the bulkhead had come on automatically when $FIRSTNAMEB0 opened the door. The bulkhead...", "0_5_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. It would be a little more than 12 hours before GroupStat executives would have to stand before the probing cameras of a news conference and tell their people why they were out of a job.\nIt was now twenty-one minutes before six o'clock in the morning.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 lay in the grass, trembling, and pointed his faceless watch toward the sky. He was looking at the constellation of Cassiopeia on the face of his watch.\nCassiopeia was a queen in the sky\u2014and a queen on $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s watch. She had a chair on which she sat with great dignity. The chair was a five-pointed star.\nOne of the stars in the chair was Castor, a binary star shared equally by a white giant and a blue dwarf.\nA slender thread attached the queen\u2019s chair to another constellation on $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s watch. The other constellation was the cluster known as the Pleiades.\nContinent-sized blocks of identifying information flashed into the roomy space behind the door in the back of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s mind. The blocks of information came in a thin line, then a thicker line, then a thick sheet, then a cloud,...", "2_17_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and her room was different. French windows looked out on the swimming pool of the hotel. The little tables and chairs were arranged on a kind of terrace. Outside was the swimming pool with people in it. There was a guy playing some kind of weird water instrument.\n\"French windows,\" she said. \"How nice.\"\n\"You could charge,\" he said, \"but the French windows are charged-in.\"\n\"Maybe I couldn\u2019t get through the French windows,\" she said.\n\"Maybe not,\" said Candide.\nThere were lots of other things and people changed as well. Everything and everybody was more real, somehow simply more.\nVan Vleck sat with his arm around Rita Hayworth\u2019s ample waist in the bar at the Beverly Hills Hotel and watched Marlon Brando and Frank Sinatra squaring off across the smooth wooden floor, watched them move inch by inch toward each other. He had recognized Brando first of all, and knew that was Marlon Brando jabbing at the air in front of Frank Sinatra. And he knew that Sinatra had to be Sinatra because Marlon Brando wouldn\u2019t be looking sideways at anybody unless that anybody was somebody.", "2_12_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-two p.m.</span> Earth time.\nIn the control turret with $FIRSTNAMEB1 and the chained man was a robot with the ersatz features of $FIRSTNAMEB0. It was a transparent robot. Stony could see through it.\nIn the compressed time of the tour, Stony had had the robot made from mirrors.\nStony was a handsome man with iron-gray hair and a moustache iron-gray as the hair. He had sparkling blue eyes, a sort of chiseled Viking mien.\nStony\u2019s hair and moustache were real. The rest was make-believe.\nStony did not like make-believe.\nStony deeply preferred the look of things to the look of the things\u2019 images in mirrors.\nHe was an inveterate stone-sniffer. In stone, in space, in natural woods, he found a satisfaction that beams and struts, sheets of vinylite, and millroom art could never give him. It was in the satisfaction that he found a denial of the special limitations of the human body, of the human mind. In adding such satisfaction to his life, he added vastly to both body and mind.", "0_16_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nLenore\u2019s eyes snapped open. The cold and sudden overhead light hurt. Her wrists were stuck in two tubes and were being sucked empty by two plastic bulbs and by needles stuck in the tubes\u2019 ends. Distances flashed up on a screen beside Lenore, one number receding as the other approached.\nSound echoed thinly in the bright room.\nLenore stared at the screen, trying to keep the receding number in the black. She felt unbelievably tired and completely useless.\nSome goddam little girl wanted to let the colors bleed.\n/a/\nRowRRRRowRRRRow.\n/a/\nBonk!!!\n/a/\nHmmm.\nEver been to Reno, Steve?\n...\nI have to piss.\nGross.\nEver been to Reno?\n/a/\nWRRRRR\n/a/\n...\n/a/\nHey while I\u2019m so kindly lifted, what\u2019s really on my mind?\nRight now I\u2019m really effing pissed.\nAt whom or what?\nAt Rick.\nWhy?\nI really truly needed to get some money today, and I was counting on Rick. And...", "1_12_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-seven p.m.</span> Space was a crooked section of Highway 78 West between Primm and Baker, California. The place was the parking lot of a shabby resort called the Desert Inn. The author of this book was leaning on a bent and dented fender of a dusty Ford Mustang, trying to figure out who in hell he was. The first ten minutes of this strange and terrible waiting period had been spent in absolute terror. The prospective driver of the $4,975 car was screaming at some idiot far across the parking lot; this section of the parking lot was a small island of order and quiet, surrounded by a seething morass of lunatic behavior.\n\"I\u2019ll kick that sumbitch loose if you\u2019ll push me off\u2014yes you, Stick-boy! Big guy! Sure you can! Come on!\u201d The driver waved unsteadily at a Monterey man across the parking lot. \"I can show you something really dirty!\" He squinted up at the sun. \"Right now!\"\n\"Jesus,\" said the author. Forty-eight minutes from buying that car to being a dangerous madman in its parking lot! No wonder! The poor guy was spent . . . unspooled . . . fried .", "1_4_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>. From overhead came the roar of planes, and the boom of anti-aircraft. Shells exploded in the streets. Sleep was impossible. The old woman boiled couscous for us. Pound poured out his heart about Adams. Then he said: \u201cRom! (Rom!).\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you say, Omar?\u201d\n\u201cI say Rom! Tell us a damn good story!\u201d\n\u201cA good story? The best story you ever heard.\u201d\n\u201cWhat's the name of it,\u201d I asked him. \u201cWhen Sad Sam of the D.A. rushes to the mountain with Gabby, the gangster.\u201d And then he told them all about it in detail, especially about Sad Sam's face as he lay under the wheelbarrow that was to crush him to death. \u201cAnd the moll says, `Holy Sam, you can't take any more,' and Sam says ...\u201d\nThey were laughing and the house was shaking with their laughter. Then came the boom of cannon fire. All the planes were in the sky at once. It was the dawn of Friday. Thursday night, at eleven o'clock, the Anglo-American forces had...", "2_19_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span> on the planet Mars. There were exactly six minutes to go before the appearance of the Space Wanderer and his chronometer.\nThe man who was executioner of the Space Wanderer was fifty-three years old. He was a widower. His name was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. He was a shy and withdrawn man, but animated by a sense of humor that saw the ironic absurdities and ironies not only of his own position, but of all positions.\n$SURNAME0 was clothed in a skintight suit of magenta-colored rubber. The suit was a good-will gesture of the manufacturers, who, under the order announced by $SURNAME0, had been producing them for sale to the public for ten cents a copy.\nThe suit\u2019s only fastener was at the neck, and opening it would have required a careful hand. The zipper, of course, was a zipper only in the sense that the fastener was analogous to a pair of zippers. The male part\u2014the fang\u2014was a needle that penetrated the skin, while the female part\u2014the mouth\u2014produced a soft but unbreakable mesh.", "2_14_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. The manuscript trembled in the hands of $FIRSTNAMEB0. There was a higher pitch to the snarl of the helicopters now.\nA universal nervousness among the encamped creatures of the troop stole along the tree-lines like a breeze among the boughs.\nThe drumming of the helicopters was ragged, as though the pilots did not have time to make it uniform.\n\"My father,\" the manuscript said, \"was a drinking man. He was a drinking man of a type common enough in North America. It is a type characterized by dreamy features, a hearty manner, and an inability to perceive the plausibility of any opinion that is not favorable to the establishment, however viewed.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heart was aching. It was aching with loneliness and yearning for Mercury. There was a good reason why $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heart should ache at that particular time, at that particular place. The reason was something $FIRSTNAMEB0 did not know. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s thoughts were hopelessly confused by passages from the poem that he kept repeating to himself.\nIt was the poem he had written. It was a long, rambling poem in heroic couplets.", "2_10_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-nine in the morning</span>. The palace clock tolled eleven o\u2019clock.\nBracknell looked up at the great clock, saw that the warning light was still not on.\n\"I wonder what time the explosions are scheduled for,\" said Bracknell, looking somberly out the palace windows at the flat clear Earth.\n\"Has there been any radio contact with the Rebel retirees at Mercury?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Radio contact?\" said Bracknell. \"It costs us a thousand dollars per word just to talk to Earth. The unregenerate Earthlings want fifty cents a word for any information about the retirees at Mercury.\"\n\"The money Moon was supposed to pay us for traffic-management is being paid to the Earthlings, then?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 23049.\n\"I believe it goes into your accounts on Mars,\" said Bracknell. \"But my wife has access to all accounts\u2014both yours and mine.\"\n\"My accounts on Mars are empty so far as I know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 23049.\n\"Mine too,\" said Bracknell. \"I\u2019d be glad to have you look into them for me.\"\n\"It would be a pleasure,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 23049.", "2_8_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nThe place was Washington, D.C., Earth, Solar System, Milky Way. The sun was one hour and two minutes from the moment of rising.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his chrono-synclastic infundibulated dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, materialized on a grassy rut of Tralfamadore whenever the traffic on Mars stopped moving.\nThe lights came on in Martian traffic, and the lights went off again, exactly one second later.\nThe effect was symphonic\u2014 rather like a stuck automatic piano with all its notes stubbornly unchanged for three minutes and forty-two seconds.\nA fiveday countdown to an announced appearance by $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had just ended. During the five days, every store window on Earth had bloomed with pictures of $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4, pictures some long forgotten firm had designed and financed.\nThe pictures were color-offset prints on a transparent material. When the lights went on in Martian traffic, the windows went dark, and the pictures flared like searchlights, exactly as they had when they were hung in the windows.", "2_1_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 crept across the floor of the museum to the glass case that held the great brown egg.\nThe green numbers in the glass case said the same thing they had said when the air ship had first landed in the museum. They said:\n102 days, 12 hours, 2 minutes,\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 pressed his ear to the glass of the case.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 pressed his ear to the glass of the case.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did, then $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThey heard a faint ticking.\nThe ticks were once for every second of time.\nThere were five hundred and forty-four ticks to a minute.\nDuring five hundred and forty-four of those minutes, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 stood on their toes before the glass case.\nThen, at exactly two o\u2019clock in the morning, they both pressed their faces to the glass \u2014 and howled as one animal. The explosion of an air-raid siren could not have been louder.\n\"It\u2019s done it!\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 howled, echoing one another. They clung together, imagining that their rocking would steady the world. \"IT\u2019S DONE IT!\"", "0_6_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-nine in the morning</span>. In three minutes, the first edition of the local paper would hit the street.\nIn the bathroom, Metzger poured rum on his face.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, now in a white suit, lavender shirt and reefer tie, stood before the mirror mixing himself a dry martini. The half-finished stucco house, the red Corvette outside, the white suit, the lavender shirt, the reefer tie were all $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s idea of appropriate garb for an innocent young man about to inherit twenty billion dollars.\n\"Damn thing feels cold,\" he said, talking to his reflection in the mirror.\nHis reflection cracked a lurid, yellow, dangerous smile, and then spoke in an echo of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s own foghorn baritone: \"Twenty billion dollars feels cold, $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2014especially when you know what a colossal bungling fraud the whole business is.\"\n$SURNAME1 turned, stiffened, chilled. There, in place of his reflection, stood $SURNAME0 in dove-gray clerical garb. The material of the garb shimmered and changed, alternately opaque and transparent, so that $SURNAME0 seemed to be wearing seaweed, or to have turned...", "2_5_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-eight in the morning</span>. My attorney was in jail. I had no attorney. I had no luggage, no drugs, no friends and not a single phone number anywhere in the world for any kind of assistance\u2014and here it was dawn on Las Vegas morning, which meant certain chaos here and far far worse wherever my fool self had wandered for the last six months. Very cautiously, so as not to attract any attention, I picked up a phone and dialed the operator. \u201cWhat city, please?\u201d she said. \u201cWhat?\u201d I screamed. \u201cYou told me to call Florida,\u201d she said. \u201cSir?\u201d I placed the receiver back in the cradle and looked around the room. My clothing and other personal things were bagged and ready by the door, but I stood transfixed in the center of the room, thinking about what should or could or must be done next.\nThere was another knock at the door. I placed a hasty glance back around the room\u2014what ? no drugs in the toilet, nothing incriminating in / I lie closet...nothing under the rug...", "2_12_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past noon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was at last on his feet again.\nHe had no recollection of having got to his feet, but there he was. There was agony in every part of his body.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was straightening himself mechanically, something inside him pushing him up and straighten and up and straighten.\nHe seemed to be hypnotized by the gold patterns of pain that danced before his eyes.\nThe patterns of pain did a soft-shoe shuffle, then split apart and joined again, changed into the shape of a great capital M.\nA loud bang made $FIRSTNAMEB0 jump.\nThe sergeant in the cubbyhole had slammed down the phone.\n\"That crazy son of a bitch Scheisskopf again,\" said the sergeant. \"Wants to know if you\u2019re all right, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 nodded.\nIn one corner of the cubbyhole was a written message that had been given to the sergeant just before the assault on the Museum of Earthly Antiquities. $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew what was in the message, knew it by heart. It had been given to him by the Martian boy before the battle began. There was a question mark after the information it contained. The message was this:", "1_18_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six sixteen in the evening</span> of November twelfth. The man was on his way to purchase an evening\u2019s entertainment for a pretty young lady. He was whistling a $7 blues. He was on his back porch, ready to leave.\nSuddenly he stopped whistling and grinned. He was thinking of the pretty young lady, and of her mother-in-law, too. His grin became a chuckle, then a laugh.\nThe pretty young lady was pregnant, and the pretty young lady\u2019s mother-in-law knew it, and was furious about it, and had told the pretty young lady as much. That was all the pretty young lady needed\u2014which was why she had to have her after-dinner entertainment so carefully mixed.\nHer husband\u2014and she\u2014were afraid of the mother-in-law.\nThe man on his back porch was a successful attorney, and the mother-in-law was the old lady of a successful and hard-hearted dentist. The mother-in-law had an undying passion for getting even. She enjoyed letting the pretty young lady know that she had her pegged\u2014always letting her know that she knew the kind of a person she was dealing with. She would say, \"Yes, you\u2019re...", "2_14_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>, on a fine Pacific Standard day, when the space ship Bloemker\u2019s Antique Pastilles split into smithereens, and no one was ever certain just which pieces hit the earth first. Forty-eight hours later, during the second nightfall after the accident, what proved to be the final piece finally came slamming down in the vicinity of Alvin, Texas.\nIt was a huge crewless ship. Millions of parts, some no bigger than the end of an unwound watchspring, came lazily fluttering down. No one was killed by falling wreckage.\nOne of the slighter fragments drifted down upon a sawmill in the hills outside of Alvin. A man was working late in the mill. He was sawing a board. He looked up at the ceiling. \"Well, old stranger,\" he said aloud. \"Thought you\u2019d never come down.\"\nThere was no gravity up there. The fragment drifted at an angle to the sawdust-choked air. An errant eddy of air made it seem to waffle in flight. It plopped onto the big iron wheel that fed boards through the saw. The wheel stopped.\nThe mill stopped.", "1_12_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nRadar and $FIRSTNAMEB2 sat on the curb outside the entrance to The House of Saintly winds. They were waiting for the Malibuuian fur posse to arrive so they could say good-by.\n\"They are late. They are very late,\" said Radar.\n\"Lots of little rainy paths get together to make a big rainy path,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"I would prefer that they were on time,\" said Radar. \"This is the only civilized country in the world, this country. I\u2019ll never see another like it. And I am not even seeing this one plain. I am seeing it through my glasses, as though I were already in flight.\"\n\"What is it you are going to do with all your money, sir, in your new country?\" asked $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"Well, I have been fortunate in certain investments, and I am resolved to perform certain experiments with a flowering plant I have discovered,\" said Radar.\n\"Not the bitter?\" asked $FIRSTNAMEB2 with curiosity. \"I thought that was the most poisonous flowering plant in the world.\"\n\"Oh, not the bitter,\" said Radar. \"I discovered the plant quite accidentally in the course of my work on the properties of the leaves of...", "0_6_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six nineteen in the morning</span> on Mars, and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog were getting into their space ship, the Love Boat, in order to fly to Mars for the first time. The name of the dog, according to orders issued by $SURNAME0, was optimistically to be Skip.\nThe time was six nineteen and three-tenths seconds in the morning on Mars\u2014and the untested, balky time machine in $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s concrete stable at Skipicington Manor was about to make its first forward thrust in one hundred and forty-seven thousand, six hundred and three years.\nThe time machine was compressing its springs with a pop-pop-pop-pop-pop.\nThe pop-popping intensified each time the hands of the statuary struck 09:59 and 23 seconds.\n\"Oh God\u2014\" said $SURNAME0 delicately, \"the machine is so flurried.\"\nA bee flew through the open door of the stable, leaving the manor. In half a minute, the bee returned through the door. It flew in tight circles as though trying to remember the way back to the manor. It had no memory. All it knew was that something unexpected had happened.\nThe motor of...", "1_23_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-three in the evening</span>.\n\"Witness, please say into this stenomote how you know the time,\" said the Magnum Opus Corporation official assigned by the United Nations to oversee the execution.\nThe official wore a business suit. He was old, fifty-two. His name was George Walt. He had been a successful department-store buyer in Schenectady, New York, before the retirement age was raised to sixty-five.\n\"The Bible, 1 Chronicles, 20:33,\" said the death-row inmate. \" \u2019And Michmash fell before Benjamin, and all the people that were with Gideon, and they took away all the victuals, and all the flock of sheep, and camels,\u2014and they came to the rock of Rimmon.\u2019 \"\n\"Witness, please say how you know that this particular Bible passage has anything to do with the time,\" said George Walt.\n\"Don\u2019t you think, sometimes, it\u2019s all hopeless, God?\" said the condemned murderer, looking moistly up at George Walt.\n\"I don\u2019t think religion and corporations should mix, that\u2019s all I know,\" said George Walt. The wind blew his sandy, thinning hair.\n\"", "0_22_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-two in the evening</span> and the place was a cocktail lounge on the fortieth floor of the newly dedicated M.M.G. Building on Fifth Avenue, and the occasion was a time-and-temperance lecture sponsored by a non-profit organization known as Bachelors Anonymous.\nAnd the place was a madhouse.\nIn the first place it was a grand opening, so there were no chairs. In the second place the ceiling of seraphim and trumpets, which had cost all of eight hundred dollars, was clacking like a machine gun in a compression test, and threatening to come down every minute. In the third place the bar itself\u2014a slab of Lucite one and a half inches thick, carved with bas-relief scenes showing the joys of temperance\u2014was sweating vodka, aquavit, and bourbon, and all three liquids were running down the slab in belching furrows and puddling under the slab and making the slab, and everyone standing on it, slippery as an eel.\nThe cocktails themselves were being shaken to death in a contraption that one imagined was called a Royal Rampage, and that apparently was so good at what it did that it was red-hot.\nAbove all, there was loudspeaker music of...", "2_10_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-one in the morning</span>.\nThree minutes and fifty-three seconds would pass before the pain reached white heat.\nThree minutes and fifty-two seconds before $FIRSTNAMEB1 fell over dead of an attack.\nDo you know what the pain was, friends and neighbors?\nThere were a million people on Mars, including officers, who were unable to do what $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 did for themselves in the army. Every one of them needed help the way a baby needs a dry diaper.\nEvery one of those million people was conditioned to chew a capsule at the first twinge of trouble. Every capsule would dissolve instantly on the tongue, releasing a drug that would make the Martian feel happy, warm, and wonderful. Any pain would be wiped out instantly.\nThe happy drug was more frequently used by the officers than it was by the soldiers.\nThe first twinge of pain in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head was the only time in his life that he had ever felt pain without a drug fixing it on the spot.\nIt was the first time he had ever thought of himself as a victim.\nThe second time was when the pain really got going, three minutes and fifty-three seconds from the time it had begun.", "2_4_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s memory was gone.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 no longer cared whom he hurt\u2014whom he killed. He was beyond hurting or killing anyone in particular. But he knew that somewhere, somehow, he had been the agent of some really terrible stifling. Maybe, like poor old Francis Gambon, he had strangled someone or some several people with a clothesline.\nFor a moment, $FIRSTNAMEB0 felt secure, in possession of a truth so clear and simple that he felt like laughing out loud. He might have laughed\u2014might have waked everybody up\u2014if he could have remembered what laughter was like.\nThe truth was that he didn\u2019t give a damn.\nHe squirted harmonium on.\nHe dripped harmonium.\nHe painted the ship with a drowsy stroke.\nThe harmonium oozed onto the deck, formed a puddle.\nAnd the puddle, finding itself gouged out of the deck, spilled lazily into space.\nHarmonium falls like any other sickly liquid\u2014by random molecular squeezing. And falls with a silent spatter that excites no echo in the heart of a comet.", "1_5_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-seven in the morning</span>. It was the gray dawn of a gray dawn. One after another, like cannon, the waves of morning crashed against the shores of the structures in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind. He was under siege, surrounded, outnumbered\u2014and had no plans, no defenses.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wept.\n\"You got to eat not to starve, you got to breathe not to suffocate, you got to drink not to die\u2014\" said Sergeant $SURNAME2.\nA church inside $FIRSTNAMEB0 wept. He felt scuttled inside. The church had no bells available with which to peal the news of his arrival, but it had a choir, and the choir now sang a piercing dirge for $FIRSTNAMEB0. The church hadn\u2019t the resources for ushering $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s soul directly into Heaven, but it could deck him out in sombre, respectful garb, and it did so.\n\"You got to get along with people to stay alive\u2014\" said Sergeant $SURNAME2.\nThe church now had $FIRSTNAMEB0 at the pulpit, now had him denouncing the congregation for its sins and weaknesses, now had him forgiving the congregation and greeting them warmly as guests in Heaven.\nA breeze stirred one petal of one of the...", "2_23_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-two in the evening</span>.\nThe time was eleven-thirty-six in the evening.\nThe time was eleven-thirty-nine in the evening.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF1? Pleez?\" croaked Solomon Krup.\n\"Hmmm?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, who was trying very hard to get to sleep.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF1? $FIRSTNAMEF1? Solomon a twerp? Solomon a twerp?\" croaked Krup, familiarly.\n\"Mmmm?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, on the way to sleep.\n\"Solomon a twerp?\" croaked Krup.\nAt this point, the phone in the hall rang. Krup got up and ran over to his mother, who was closest, to answer it.\n\"Mound?\" he said.\nThere was no answer. Krupp put his hand over the mouthpiece, and ushered his father over to brush his teeth. Solomon\u2019s face was a sick and sorry mess of wrinkles. He staggered over to the phone, and glassily drilled into it with his eyes while he brushed.\nHe spat. His gums almost bled. He dreaded the worst.", "2_19_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after seven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earth time.\nThe room was the most senselessly feminine room ever contrived by man. The idea behind it was that the woman who loved it would be as beautiful and fragile as the room.\nThe woman who loved it was beautiful and, thank God, far from fragile.\n$SURNAME0 stood in the room.\nHe stood before a great hooked rug that bore the Solar System in miniature. The major planets were represented by gold-leafed, gilt-craft platters, their rings by gold-leafed, gilt-craft hoops.\nEach platter and hoop was chased and engraved with the name of its element.\nRinged Saturn was eighteen feet across, the Earth twelve feet, and so on. Mercury, the innermost planet, was a two-foot gold-leafed doughnut.\nThe doughnut stood on an eighteen-inch turntable, so that Mercury could always show the same face to the Sun.\nThere was a gold-leafed toothbrush on the turntable of Mercury.\nThe idea behind that was that all women were silly, and all beautiful women were utterly, prehistorically silly.", "1_4_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-nine in the morning</span> of my fortieth birthday. I was alone in the office. The night\u2019s receipts were totaled, the safes locked, the doors bolted. There was a note on the desk: \u201cClosing down for absence from the premises. Have slammed the doors, bolted the windows, double\u2014locked the straitjacket, and fed the mice. Love, Lucy.\u201d The maids had found her naked by the pool, giggling. That was the day they had all let the air out of little Gi Carmine\u2019s trampoline, and sent him howling to his room. His name was actually Guglielmo Carmine, but everybody called him Gi Carmine. Our three other tenants were gone for the weekend: Miss Kitz, a spinster librarian from St. Louis, and Mr. and Mrs. Chester Yojimbo, some kind of cousins from Seattle. The catered food was all gone and no more liquor was in the house\u2014except four bottles of Suite 420 Montrachet, which I would be driving out to the shopping center to replace. No workmen were scheduled until six Tuesday morning. The next four and a half days would be quiet, untormented nights. The season was...", "2_2_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> in Newport, the summer residence.\nThe night was a featherbed of forgetfulness.\nThe night was a black silk handkerchief with a golden silk edging. It could wipe away memory; it could wipe away vision.\nTwo minutes before two o\u2019clock in the morning, the fireworks stopped exploding in the sky. The committee had used up all the money. The sky was empty, except for one white feather, which would linger for a few moments after the explosion, before falling slowly and quietly toward the earth.\nAfter the explosions ended, the sky was filled once more with the European swallows, twittering sleepily among themselves, confused by all the noise, blinking their eyes, surprised at the daylight.\nLADY GI\n\n\nRANI MUMBA, the mother of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the inventor of both chrono-synclastic infundibulations and chrono-synclastic absolutisms, had a brother so strange that he was given the title \"Lakshmi,\" which is the name of the Hindu goddess of luck. Empress Lakshmi, as her brother was called, died long before the story begins.", "2_8_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nNobody referred to the dog, Brains\u2014who had followed $SURNAME0\u2019s lead in everything.\nNo one referred to $SURNAME1\u2019s son.\nNo one referred to $SURNAME0.\nNo one referred to anything.\nA groggy technician came into the room and turned off the projector. He yanked the film out of it, put the film in a box. He put the box on a handcart, left the room.\nThe audience began to break up.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, the butler, came strolling into the room, saying good morning to this person or that. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was proud of having been $SURNAME0\u2019s butler, proud of having participated in the filming of the message. He wanted to show the good side of his familiar character\u2014but he had no butler to show it to. Only $SURNAME1 noticed $FIRSTNAMEB2, and $SURNAME1 did not pay much attention to him.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 seemed to notice $SURNAME1 for the first time. \"Can I get you something?\" he asked.", "2_21_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nOn Sunday the twentieth of September it rained all day. The telephone didn\u2019t ring once. The rain very nearly drowned Gerber\u2019s Gerbera Jungle. The console television in the northeast corner of Lenore\u2019s living room brought rain. It was a good rain, the best kind of rain.\nSome time in the middle of the afternoon, a call came through from the Frequent and Vigorous switchboard, in Columbus. Lenore took it in her room.\n\"Frequent and Vigorous, this is Lenore Beadsman speaking,\" said Lenore.\nThe voice was familiar. It was the big man with the big scar. \"If it\u2019s not too much trouble,\" he said, \"could you please convey to our invisible star telephonist the unmitigated fondness with which I\u2019m at this very moment refreshingly moist?\" He hung up.", "2_14_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was a charming one\u2014if peculiar\u2014old northern landing strip by the sea on the California coast.\nThe thin man got up slowly. He unbuttoned his jacket. He apparently took some time undoing the complicated belt and the still more complicated fly of his pants. He tipped his hat forward over his eyes. \"People\u2014and gentlemen,\" he said, \"watch carefully.\" His head emerged from his hat briefly and made a lordly motion with a thin finger. \"The operation is simple,\" he said.\nWomen by the hundreds were milling about on the landing strip.\nThe thin man flapped his arms flat against his sides, and two gunbelts jumped clear of his hips, clattering to the ground.\nThe thin man bent, got his gunbelts, and buckled them back in place. He shielded his eyes and looked toward the sky. \"They come,\" he said.\nHe took three slow steps and sat down again, crossing his thin legs with a tennis-shoe squeak. He placed a crumpled white handkerchief over his face and held it there. \"Is everybody ready?\" he said.", "1_21_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nTari Frederick, the creator and owner of $FIRSTNAMEB0, came floating through the wall, privates first. Tari Frederick was a split-level, four-bedroom woman in her late middle age.\nAlone in a space suit, Corporal $FIRSTNAMEB0 looked bewildered. The bewilderment would have been comical in an otherwise antic face, for Corporal $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s features were far from merry. They were given to sullen, glum, brooding, hostility, discontent incarnate. The color scheme of Corporal $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face was deep graphite-gray above the line of the cheekbones, and white below. The whites of the eyes were yellow.\nThe yellow whites of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s eyes were disgusting\u2014they were symbols of endless hatred, endless suspicion, endless dread. The hatred, the suspicion, the dread were properly directed at the owner of his face.\nTari Frederick had given $FIRSTNAMEB0 fangs.\nHer purpose in creating $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been twofold:\n1. To punish $FIRSTNAMEB0, while enduring the pains of childbirth, for being stupid enough to fall in love with her.\n2. To create probably the most unlovable man on Mars.\nTo...", "2_19_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven sixteen in the evening</span>, Earth time.\nAround the Universe on the wall went a random spiral of colored pins, making a paltry mural of the Universe. Pin themes included the Solar System, the Milky Way, the places of origin of the True-Whites, the places of origin of the colored peoples of the Earth, current supply depots of the Martian Army, and the like. It was the kind of mural you might expect to see adorning the wall of a dentist or a Pentagon war room.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s cat sat in a tubular steel-and-wicker chair. This chair was absurdly unsuited to a cat. Only a cat as monstrously spoiled as Salo could possibly have liked it.\nIn truth, Salo did not like it. He sat in it only because there was nothing else to sit in. He sat only because he had been trained from kittenhood to sit in any chair that was pointed out to him. The fact that he sat in the chair showed his spirit more clearly than anything else he might have done.", "0_18_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span> in California. His connection was holding the line. He heard a voice in his ear, saying,\n\"F pulses, ident, father, 1, mother, 2, sister, 3, brother, 4, a friend, 5\u2014a friend, 6.\"\n\"Shut up,\" said Fern, climbing back into the driver's seat.\nThey went on for miles and miles.\n\"I love you,\" said Fern completely, staring into June's face.\n\"You what?\" she asked.\n\"I love you,\" said Fern.\n\"Well,\" she said, \"that\u2019s something.\"\n\"What shall we do about it?\" asked Fern.\n\"Nothing,\" said June.\n\"We could throw ourselves down a well,\" said Fern.\n\"We\u2019re way beyond that,\" said June.\n\"Orphans\u2014orphans,\" said Fern.\n\"We\u2019re way beyond that, too,\" said June. \"Just good-bys and cheese, remember?\"\n\"And madness,\" said Fern.\n\"What?\" said June.\n\"Do you think I\u2019m mad?\" said Fern.\n\"No,\" said June.\nThere was a long silence.\n\"What are you thinking right...", "0_9_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-three in the morning</span>. Much had happened since $FIRSTNAMEF0-Ramnarine had clapped her face down on her desk, five minutes to nine. Nothing much had happened since Tuesday noon, except kite-flying, telephone calls, and the gathering around Alice Zooster of the fearsome stone-kickers. The Westcote Board of Controlt was waiting for the Mail Room, for a precious sheaf of mail consisting of three investor\u2019s letters and five stockholder\u2019s proposals. A schedule for leaflet distribution, for insertion in key commuters\u2019 trains, had been cancelled, in favor of telephone calls. A floor-waxing crew had been disassembled, after being sworn to secrecy and told to go home and take the day off. And Randy Karl Tucker had discovered red, white, and blue tootsie-roll wrappers in his overcoat pocket, had taken a few steps on the rug in his office to see what would happen, had been discovered by Mary-Ann Plummer, who had warned him of the consequences of such behavior.\nThankfully, he was safe, now.\nThe time had come to talk with Madeleine in the cubicle next door. Lenore did not want to do this. She thought at least of...", "1_12_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past noon</span>, according to the clock on the wall of the supply room. The swinging pocket watch itself had never before at any one time reflected the actual, up-to-the-minute time as accurately as it did now, for the watch no longer received any signals from the master switchboard in the building next door. At twelve-thirty this switchboard had been wide open, bathed in pulsing, master-board light. But by twelve-forty it was the reverse of wide open, suffused in backup light, and spiraling deep into negative tungsten territory. At twelve-fifty it was lightless, and one blind switchboard operator after another was yanked from his chair out into the corridor, where he kicked and spat until the sedative took hold.\nThe operator taken out at twelve-fifty was the last, for all the master switchboard operators were sedated now. Some were sedated in their own homes all the way from Reno, Nevada, to Portland, Maine.\nIn the control room of the switchboard in the building across the street, the eight unattended high-speed switches began to leap erratically, and the building was cut off not only from the telephone system of narcotics police south of Twentieth...", "2_15_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-nine in the afternoon</span>. Twenty-three minutes before we were forced to hit the road again.\n\u201cLenore, I have something to tell you.\u201d\n\u201cYou\u2019re about to invite me back to your room for purposes of eventual fornication.\u201d\n\u201cNow how\u2019d you know that?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m just like a Cleveland cable dude. I know.\u201d\n\u201cWell, now.\u201d\nThere were a few moments of nothing. I stared at one of R.V.\u2019s enormous neatly stacked piles of magazines, drinking from my Snapple bottle. There was the smell again.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s that smell? \u201d I said.\n\u201cI smell something, too. Ever so faint.\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cIs it somebody frying catfish in Sour Dough Roy\u2019s office?\u201d\n\u201cCould be. We are near the Amherst House ghetto.\u201d\n\u201cThe what?\u201d\n\u201cLet\u2019s go see.\u201d", "2_15_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-one in the afternoon</span>.\n/b/\n9 September\n9 September\n9 September\n9 September\n9 September\n9 September\n9 September\n9 September\n10 September\n10 September\n10 September\n10 September\n10 September\n10 September\n10 September\n10 September\n10 September\n11 September\n11 September\n11 September\n11 September\n11 September\n11 September\n11 September\n19 September\n19 September\n21 September\n21 September\n21 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n22 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n23 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September\n24 September", "1_13_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. I went over to the airlines office and learned that the plane for Washington was leaving at twenty-seven after two. I went to a tailor and had my clothes refitted. I spent a few minutes in a music store, then got in a taxi and told the driver to take me to the Riu Palace Hotel. \u201cHow much is the fare, senor? \u201c53\nI was born in 1850 in the south of Ireland. I died on one of the Channel Islands on December 12, 1889. In that twenty-nine-year span of time I performed the apparently impossible feat of living furiously. My youth was one whirl of excitement: I was drunk seven years of the ten; I raced fast cars at sixty miles an hour along the Dublin quays while the peelers chased me; I played billiards with the best players in the world and abandoned myself to every physical pleasure; I was a fencer, a boxer, and a special lightweight wrestler with the gloves; I threw my hamstrings over a horse and crushed the animal against the ground under me, as I have seen circus-riders do with circus-horses at the ringside, while the great crowd roared. I went to the Holy City of...", "0_1_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past one o'clock in the morning</span>. I had another hour to go on post. There was a message that Hilliard wanted to see me. He was out on the plain, taking the air. He had been having trouble with his kidneys. I found him leaning against a rock, under a huge, midnight moon.\n\u201cHello, $FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d he said. \u201cYes, I want to see you.\u201d He was an elderly man; his wife and children were back in the United States.\n\u201cYou know, $FIRSTNAMEB6, as an officer you ought to do something about the drinking done by the enlisted men. It'll end in disaster.\u201d\n\u201cI know it.\u201d\n\u201cWell, do something about it.\u201d\n\u201cI'm trying,\u201d I said. \u201cThings won't change overnight.\u201d\n\u201cYou're responsible, as an officer, you know.\u201d And so on. I listened to all this, feeling mean and dispirited, because I knew I was failing in my responsibilities as an officer, because I hadn't had the strength and the character to change things. I was doing the best I could and it wasn't good enough. All the while Hilliard kept leaning against...", "1_13_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-four in the afternoon</span>.\nThe light was what Isaac Edward Leancer called the sunshine, outside all the walls of the world.\nThe place was the back lawn of the estate of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the estate of, now, young Isaac Edward Leancer. The estate was on a hill above the town of Gettysburg, in Pennsylvania.\nAbove the estate was a shallow bowl, a natural amphitheater, a killing ground.\nIn this amphitheater a hundred and twenty-five persons, responding to thunder and lightning without and to nerve impulses from within, dismembered and consumed a drumstick of foam rubber.\nThe hundred and twenty-five were young people living on the estate. The hundred and twenty-five were on a long wooden picnic bench. The young people at one end of the bench held in their hands knives, tin openers, cans of unliving lemonade and warm cans of unliving grapefruit juice. With their knives, tin openers, cans of unliving lemonade, warm cans of unliving grapefruit juice and their mouths, they dismembered and consumed a drumstick of foam rubber.\nAt the other end of the bench, at the heads, were $FIRSTNAMEB0 and young Isaac Edward Leancer. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and young Isaac Edward Le...", "0_13_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-seven in the afternoon</span> and the place was a room in one of the less prepossessing rooms of the Tralfamadorian corporate building, a room that by reasonably Earthling standards would have been considered vastly overfurnished.\nThere were forty-seven pieces of Turkish-styled furniture, including titillating tables, lascivious daises, Kama Sutra chairs, saucy sofas, and virginal urine fountains. What\u2019s more, there were twenty-three actual persons in the room, plus one enormous police robot that towered over the furniture and was generally very hard not to notice.\nOne of the persons was the increasingly impressed Salo. Granted that his people had been masters of illusion on their home planet, but this was some kind of record even for Tralfamadorians. It seemed that every time Salo blinked, another piece of furniture appeared.\nThen Salo blinked, and the six hard-faced strangers made their appearance.\nThe strangers looked around for a place to sit down, then realized that most of the furniture was probably animate, and stood up again.\nThis was more than a little intimidating to Gorff and Wizzgum, who were trapped respectively in the frame and handle...", "0_12_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-seven p.m.</span> when she arrived\u2014and the rain was driving, so she hurried, with her small bag and blanket, up the stairs and into the auditorium just as the second session was beginning. A really first-rate lecturer was on the podium...something about Italy\u2019s relationship with ancient Troy. $FIRSTNAMEF2 settled into her seat uncomfortably aware of the curious glances she was getting. Then, trying to appear invisible, did the unthinkable, the unforgivable, the thing she had not done in three years\u2014since that day in a hospital parking lot in San Narciso. She went inside herself, slipped down, while some harmless mumbling figure in a white coat spoke On the Preparation of the Miner\u2019s Guide to the Gospels and on How to Use It in Alloy Analyses, into that place she most despised, the confessional booth of her mind, the deep shadows of ego, the primal cellar of pretension free of all> rational control.\nThe free-associating she did would\u2019ve given any psychologist a crash course in, first, denial: \u201cNothing to be alarmed about,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2 told herself, knowing good and well it was; next, projection: \u201cThis is what happens...", "0_9_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. In an hour and fifty-five minutes I was due at the studio. The 1/10 th -of-a-second chronometer in my mind clicked its instrument warning: It was time to go.\nAnother Bloody Mary with a beer back and four aspirins. Take it on down. The clerk, with his flat grey eyes, moved back and forth behind the bar. The other drinkers sat with their backs to me and to each other, hunched silently over their liquor and their coffee cups. Traffic sounds drifted in from outside.\nOn the sidewalk, it felt fine to be alone. I looked in the mirror at the window on the other side of the street that made everything look pleasant for two feet around my head. The top half of my body and my hat were good-sized, too. Everything looked fine.\nI walked to the corner and caught a taxi in nothing flat. The Golden West Hotel was only five minutes away. The first thing I would do when I checked in would be to write the chief cameraman to see if he could get me some Codein\u2014just a few tablets, I might need them any time, for the water-rat pain...and to hell with the expense.\nCodeine! Good to the last...", "1_7_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-one in the morning</span>, November 1, 1982. Fort Meyers. A Greyhound bus that was half empty at beginning of trip from Knoxville\u2014and was now half empty at end of trip from Fort Lauderdale. The garbageman sat in back and watched the piggish passengers sleeping, picking their noses and flicking the products at their feet. The garbageman had to laugh. These oinkers, he thought.\nThen his brown eyes swept the road tarmac by the big country station and happened to see something that made his skin pinch up at the hairline: There was a dead girl by the bus, a pretty blond girl with one highheeled shoe off.\nThere was confusion as the garbageman stumbled out of the bus with rage in his heart. He knew she\u2019d been pretty when she wasn\u2019t blown up and torn like used newspaper.\nThe driver was kicking in the tank of his best damn job. Anger at the terrible inconvenience. He had no idea who the girl was. Only that she was about sixteen, small.\nThe garbageman gave the driver the gimlet eye. He handed the driver his bus ticket with the Knoxville date on it and said, \u201cAll this is your fault. Don...", "1_10_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The wood was oak. The writer of these lines, then aged nine years, had spent the night in the chamber of the House of God\u2014the children's ward in the hospital. He lay on his back in bed, listening to that holy uproar. A loud noise of banging trolley-gates was going on somewhere. Probably it was the adult female polio ward being breakfasted. Then the clatter of Tramp-Tramp-Truck-Tramp came up a stairway. This was the morning visiting nurses. They marched in twos with high stanched starched caps, and stethoscopes whose little cords hung clear down to their high-heeled white shoes. They swung their clipboards and ringed thermometers, and turned their\u2014faces of blue-eyed innocence always toward the wall when they took a temperature. Theirs not to make reply, Theirs not to reason why, Theirs but to do and die. Horrible rumble of trolleys started again. It was the poor paralyzed adult males being rolled and tied in their sheets and blankets and hustled off somewhere to sit in the sun. Biff-bap of the sadist, who made some of those poor stiff statues writhe by smartly...", "0_5_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-six in the morning</span>. Somewhere in the west a new moon hung in a confusion of clouds like a pale helmet. By day this same moon would be a sweet silver infiltrator, creeping through the veins of the dark clouds. Now it was a cloudy red. The peculiar dark color of clouds before the dawn was caused by all of the particles that are the detritus of moon and stars, the moral intervals between lightning strokes, the black retinas of space.\nOver the sleeping metropolis, that look of false yolk in the clouds turned rosy, and a diurnal snow squall swept in from the west. There, gilded in snow, a giant appeared on the sky, striding with a book in his hand. His stride broke into a run. His legs trod down the whole western width of the sky, and he tore pages out of his book and tossed them down to earth. The pages fell with white faces up. Drivers awoke to find themselves in snowbound traffic jams on highways along which pages of the Law were falling, with faces up, to be read only by street lights and headlamps. The great walker strode east again, ran, strode, and ran. He reached the eastern horizon, but only so that he could change from...", "0_14_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was twenty-eight years old by Earthly calculation.\nHe was dressed in a shapeless gray suit of UN Corps EC#17 fabric. He was barefoot.\nHe was about one hundred yards from the Newport Barracks\u2014a shabby redbrick fortress on a bluff above the town of Newport.\nThe town of Newport, and the bluff on which the Newport Barracks stood, were on Titan.\nTitan, a moon of Saturn, was a science observatory that had been seeded with army personnel. The major military installation on Titan was the Newport Barracks.\nNine miles downwind from the Newport Barracks was the Stony Kill parachute drop zone.\nA hundred and twenty-seven privates, fifty-six noncoms, and thirty-eight officers were at Stony Kill, awaiting orders that had not come, because Brigadier General Oswald Boelge, the commanding general of the barracks, was dead.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was the senior surviving noncom at the barracks.\nStanding in the gloomy half-light of his memory chamber, $FIRSTNAMEB0 could remember the faces of the dead general\u2019s immediate staff. He had worked with them intimately in the hours before the general\u2019s death.\nThere...", "1_18_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span> of the second day after their arrival on Mars when Salo, $SURNAME0, and $FIRSTNAMEF1 limply contrived the linking of their minds for the first time after their separate times of troubles in space.\nThey linked at a capacity of one ten-millionth of a watt\u2014a world record for shysters.\nSalo was thinking: I will tell you about Skip and the misunderstanding between us, and about the plan for you and me to go to Earth together.\n$SURNAME0 was thinking: Go right ahead, matey.\nSalo was thinking: It isn\u2019t something I feel very good about.\n$SURNAME0 was thinking: You feel terrible? You mean you\u2019re ashamed of the plan?\nSalo was thinking: I\u2019ve got to do it. It\u2019s the only way I can get to keep on serving you. But it isn\u2019t the serving you I care about now, so much as getting back in your favor.\n$SURNAME0 was thinking: That\u2019s very true. Nobody respects me more than you do. Nobody respects you more than I do.\nSalo was thinking: You\u2019re going to Earth anyway\u2014thank God. And it would be...", "0_17_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"It's going tick-tock, tick-tock,\" said the nurse.\n\"Have we been under now almost forty minutes?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Very near that,\" said the nurse.\n\"God have mercy,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nHis mind cleared very slowly. Colors returned to things with a parched, insolent slowness. Things insisted on being yellow before they would consent to be themselves again. There was dust in everything.\nThe harmonium beside $FIRSTNAMEB0 gurgled and stopped. \"That\u2019s that,\" said the nurse.\nA match was struck. The flame, burning with some strong air-fuel mixtures, whirled the nurse\u2019s face about like a yellow leaf. It turned the room yellow. Then the flame steadied, and the room was normal again.\nThe nurse stood holding the lamp aloft, looking over $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"There, darlin',\" she said. Her hand reached into a closet and came out with a skin-tight, color-shifting spray-on fabric. It was a full-length mirror suit, magenta. She sprayed it onto $FIRSTNAMEB0, in a moment.\nShe switched the color of the fabric to aquam...", "2_13_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had been asleep until a moment before. He now opened his eyes and sat up. He shouted a name. \"Klaatu!\" he shouted.\nNo answer came.\nThis was not startling.\n$SURNAME0 was not really expecting an answer.\nAt twenty-seven minutes after two o\u2019clock that afternoon, the time would be precisely right for a meeting with his visitor\u2014but the visitor would not arrive exactly on time, nor would he arrive at the appointed place. He would appear out of nowhere at an unpredictable spot, and, having done his best to deliver his message and make his getaway before being exposed as an interplanetary spacelurch, he would disappear again into nowhere.\n$SURNAME0 always opened his eyes wide and shouted the visitor\u2019s name when the time for the visit approached. He did this so that he wouldn\u2019t be alarmed by the visitor when the visitor did arrive.", "1_23_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bat mate, the male with the stained pumpkin head and the iron stake, was sitting cross-legged in front of the communicator. He was panting and shivering, without having exerted himself. He was like a motor held on voltage, without the self-controlling feedback.\nThe pain made by beauty in the male\u2019s head and stomach was punctiliously Murphy-ized\u2014turned into a predictable throbbing, turned into standard pain of such and such intensity.\nThe male\u2019s position in front of the communicator was not purely arbitrary. As $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bat mate, his business was now to watch over $FIRSTNAMEB0 and to report his movements to the authorities.\nThe authorities were three miles above the surface of Mars.\nThe authorities were in total equilibrium, harmonious and happy, welded together by compulsory training, by the sparing use of split twelves and amnesiacs, by rigorous attention to the fine print in the Manual of Mars\u2014in other words by a blissful ignorance of what real suffering was like.\nThe bliss of the authorities made them content with their ignorance, and the bliss, furthermore, meant that any people who were privileged to share the ignorance were eager to...", "0_17_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span> of November 22, 1972, and the place was the office of God\u2019s Holy Prophet Reverent Mandated Moses, where I sat facing His offhand throne, an armchair upholstered in imitation tiger fur. To my right, at a small table of rare hardwood, F. Champ Moss, Ph.D., Sc.D., Litt.D., Th.D., was drawing up a lease. To my left was a young woman with a door-knocker hairdo and a thousand-dollar dress, who was doing all of the talking.\nDr. Hershey Gland, longtime secretary and business manager of the Church of the Final Judgment, was functioning on my left as an unassigned sentry with a gray uniform, gray helmet, gray guns, gray boots, and a gray dead face.\nThere were two plainclothes detectives with Dr. Gland\u2014Lt. Harry Blizzard, of the D.C. Police Dept., Narcotics Division, and A. A. Cantor, of the same, Obscene File. These two were out of uniform. They looked like actors selected from an urchin casting call for gangster parts. Slim and wiry and hard. Topcoats with one button open...", "0_18_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-nine in the evening</span> of April thirtieth, 1946, when poor $FIRSTNAMEB6 Paradise, aching for a lookout over the water, crept out of his Reflection of Narcissus, Room 417, in the Mills Hotel, located on the corner of Indiana Avenue and Michigan Boulevard, a thoroughly grandiose address, the Mills, and decided that he was going to get a bite at Abels Klondike, just one lousy block down, at the supersonic center of America, the corner of Superior and Wabash. So off he dove, daydreaming and grinning. His huge falsetto shadow, thrown by the Michigan Boulevard sun, outran him and plunged ahead eight feet into the lobby. Dishes in the hotel kitchen clattered as he went by. \"You're going to give somebody . . . \" sang out a lovely, fine-haired little chambermaid, concerned for her crockery. But $FIRSTNAMEB6 didn't hear, of course--no more than Dylan heard a year later when Tom Wilson made him go by bicycle. The beautiful little chambermaid let her buckets hang and watched this organ-grinder disappear down the corridor: he seemed to have something hanging around his neck, looked like a pair of huge furry shears. Yes, he had something.", "1_19_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. All the audience were in their places. There was no sound anywhere. The orphans were in their box. Old Montoya, in sober black and white, had taken his place, too. The rules of the bull-fight prohibited the placing of any but unmarried women in the boxes. The six young people, ranging in age from six to twenty-six years, must wait until the gay and splendid life of the feria begins again next day before they know the sensation of being in love. For only ones and twos, in strict secrecy, are the maidens allowed to appear in the boxes during the bull-fights, so that their hearts will not be stolen by cavaliers while they are attending these all-male events. The six orphans did not so much as glance at each other. They had been instructed before coming, not to talk to each other during the fight. All of them knew Spanish well enough, without being told that.\nThe Civil Governor and his party were opposite the President\u2019s box. Above them was the glass roof. Montoya did not like the President\u2019s box. He did not like anything about the bull ring, except the bulls and the matadors. He did not like the Civil Governor and his party.", "1_5_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past five o'clock in the morning</span>. In two hours it would be dawn....\nWe closed the door of our bedroom and locked it.\nWe did not go to sleep that morning. It was impossible to sleep until several more hours had passed.\nWe sat on our bed and read the New York Times and drank bottle after bottle of the good Moselle, and we talked about what we were going to do in New York.\n\"There\u2019s going to be some reporters and some photographers at the airport,\" I said. \"They ought to have a good story. They\u2019ll be surprised when they find us.\" In spite of the rotten things I had said about the United States only two nights ago we were enthusiastic over being back in this country again. We had spent too many good years in this climate and in this civilization to be anything but romantically positive about it. Only a couple of hours before I had been sitting by a wall in a Valencia cafe, cursing it and feeling that I would never be able to live with these people again; now, face to face in the morning with our return to America, we were jubilant. Hadn\u2019t we done well? Wasn\u2019t this the great country? I could have slapped the...", "2_11_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-eight in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 sat on a bench in the nave of the museum. He was in shadow. One gloved finger rested against one of the stainless steel buttresses that held up the floor of the museum.\nThe buttress was a primitive but graphic model of the solar system.\nEarth was a pebble.\nMars was a red model of a cigarette lighter.\nVenus was a blue model of a woman\u2019s hatpin.\nMercury was a condom.\nThe sun, of course, was the spark plug to an old Ford.\n$SURNAME0 turned his green eyes up to the name of the model that he projected his gloved finger onto. It was the name of the solar system.\nThe name was a cat\u2019s cradle made of shoelaces, a model $SURNAME0\u2019s mother had invented one rainy autumn when $SURNAME0 was a boy. It was far nobler, far more accurate, far more insightful than the well-known name derived from the monk, Ptolemy.", "1_16_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I waited a long time, reviewing my whole life and concentrating on all the years I spent in New York. A whole year went by, slowly. Then I went out, charged slowly, a hundred dollars on the cuff. Nobody said anything, nothing happened. The Peruvian Skipper's next door, but I did not have time to hang around and drink acognizance I might spend the night in jail. The flaring signs of downtown were coming into view, everything moving very slowly. I shoved my hands in my pockets, jingled some coins, kicked at nothing, turned a corner, and there was the Greyhound station. There was still plenty of time, but I did not dawdle. I fell into step, passed the ticket window, saw the praying Indian before me, watched him disappear into the bus with its evil shine and possibilities of flight, and continued on into the waiting room. I was the only one left. My bag was the last thing on the rack. I got a ticket and walked through the gate, still thinking of my life in New York. I was going to see $FIRSTNAMEF4. I remembered the things she said over the telephone and her promise to come with Georgette. They should be here.", "2_19_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-nine in the evening</span>.\n\"Skip\u2014\" said Mrs. Porter.\nIt was seven forty in the evening.\n\"Skip\u2014\" said Mrs. Porter.\n\"What is it, Mother?\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 genially. He, too, was having a perfect time. He had just discovered that life in $SURNAME0\u2019s $SURNAME1 Paradise, though eternal, was not monotonous. He had just discovered that there were plenty of good things to do, that he wasn\u2019t going to have to sit around for millions of years doing nothing but tending his halos and having his dog lick frosting off his fingers.\nMrs. Porter stared at her son. \"You\u2019re not\u2014you can\u2019t possibly be\u2014\" she said. \"Oh,\" said $SURNAME0, \"I understand you. You want an autograph.\" He dug a fountain pen and an autographed photograph of The $SURNAME0 File out of his pocket. \"Would this be all right?\" said $SURNAME0, writing Mrs. Porter\u2019s name and address on the photograph.\n\"God bless you, Mr. $SURNAME0,\" said Mrs. Porter.", "1_23_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven nineteen in the evening</span>, a Friday in September of the year 1992, and she was somewhere in the Pacific Ocean.\nShe was at the wheel of a space ship that had been turned into a blue British roadster. She throttled down the launching engines, diddled the directional and altitude jets, and sent her steel steed into a lazy, looping back flip.\nThe sea beneath was altogether real, whereas the stars and the moon were fake. The Milky Way, so near that she could swear she saw colonies of bacteria trundling along it in horsedrawn carts, was real. The warning beacon at the Field\u2019s Point Lluence was also real. Otherwise, all star patterns were sheerly illusory.\nShe slept peacefully a thousand feet above $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nFORGERY BY MONARCHS\nA panic that had no name had seized the discriminating patrons of Basil Strong\u2019s establishment.\nYoung gentlemen whose facial, hands, and feet were so perfect that they had never been compelled to look at themselves in mirrors panicked first. Their hair had been cut and trained, their suits and shirts and ties had been put on, by experts, and the experts had gone blind\u2014as most certainly had the masters they served.\nYoung...", "1_20_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-three in the evening</span> of November 12, 1992, and it was evening in Washington as well as in San Diego ... except that in Washington it was even gloomier, darker, colder, and rainier than it was in San Diego. This only added to the light-headed feeling in the halls and offices of the National Democratic Party headquarters, for the nineties had been dark times for the Democrats. The previous fall, Dukakis had lost forty states to the dumpling-chinned George \u2019Goebbels\u2019 Bush. In the House, Democrats were down to two-score members\u2014and two of these were under indictment for having intercourse with persons of indeterminate sex in federally subsidized low-income housing projects. In the Senate, they could muster only a handful. Their party\u2019s rating in the Gallup Poll was lower than Nixon\u2019s during Watergate, lower than Harding\u2019s during Teapot Dome, lower even than Quayle\u2019s during Dunston integrity. The only serious opposition to the national ticket was coming from the bull-moose fringe, led by the novelist Norman Mailer. Mailer\u2019s running mate was Ice-T, the black rapper whose group, Body Count, was responsible for the current top-...", "1_5_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span> when we started the motor of The $SURNAME0, and eased the yacht out of its slip. We sailed her through the dark waters toward the misty sea wall.\nEight minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nNine minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nTen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nEleven minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nTwelve minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThirteen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nFourteen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nFifteen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nSixteen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nSeventeen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nEighteen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nNineteen minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nTwenty minutes to six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nAt twenty minutes of six o\u2019clock, as The $SURNAME0 sped through the mist, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 became the first human being to see the incredible beauty and terror of Mars close up.\nThe...", "0_3_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-four in the morning</span>. We had been up since dawn. The Road of Heavenly Happiness was lost in darkness and mystery. We were two tiny specks somewhere between George Washington and Citation Rock. The weather had turned evil, as well we might expect in Fear and Loathing Acres; and to make matters worse, we were almost out of gas. I had no idea we would go through it that fast. But now a violent rain had started, and in the gray we got lost, on a crossroad, somewhere west of Jackpot.\nI held the wheel in a deathgrip and the map in the other hand. We were skidding dangerously. The unmarked blacktop road dove off into nothingness at almost every turning. Where were we? Not even the gibbous moon gave much help.\n\u201cWhy the fuck didn't you write down the mileage when you bought that can in Salt Lake?\u201d I screamed in his ear.\n\u201cJesus, that's right,\u201d he yelled back.\nNot a sound from the trunk where the Samoan mumbled and rolled. The gun was out of the window, but not pointed at anything we could see...at least not yet.\nFinally, after about an hour of this pure hell, the gas...", "1_15_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-one in the afternoon</span>. I looked at my watch. Yes, that\u2019s right. And now the time had come. I could no longer hold my big story. I had to use it\u2014or lose it\u2014burn it\u2014send it up the chimney\u2014blow my top! No matter what happened as a result of this, no matter what Inspector Ponzo von Bluecher poked into this hissing fire of a story\u2014now or later\u2014at least I could no longer stand the horrible tension of it, and it had to go.\nMy attorney had worn that hat the night before. As far as I knew, he was still wearing it. When he shuffled off to bed, shortly before midnight\u2014cackling happily, very drunk\u2014that blue, pointy hat was on his head. His line of vision, in that position, would have included the bookcase where Prank Alpert\u2019s book was, and the corpse beside it.\nOf course. That was it. The final, clinching proof. My attorney had flipped on the Playmate, picked up The Yattering, and Dr. Hammerfight\u2014and flipped back to this point, with the hat on his head. He was cackling about his own brilliance.\nHe had...", "1_11_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, according to all the clocks, when the security men emerged, white-faced, from Room 217. They had left the weeping, shaking room manager inside.\nThe manager told the reporters later that security and police men had taken a single long reamed-out hot saucer of human brains, apparently brains of a little boy, and that the brains had been found growing in a wastebasket in Room 217.\nThe reporters said \"Holy Toledo.\"\nWhat $FIRSTNAMEF1 was thinking about, looking at her watch and wondering where $SURNAME2 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were, was co m ing up a justification for off base by herself. There was an overlooking embankment. She had seen the embankment through the window, and she knew that it gave a view of the River Conveyor\u2019s intake duct. And what $FIRSTNAMEF1 was thinking at the moment was that if they were devoting a major chunk of their total strength to the wrong problem, then the right and perhaps only hope in the situation was to somehow get a story on the news bol h t ion. That would stir things up. The time-losing snafu nature of their mission s h e really knew anything about had, at the moment, to be completely concealed from her colleagues and...", "0_0_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, who, according to his own account, had not looked into the eyes of another human being since the final dullness had come upon him twelve years before, shot back the bolts on the outer doors, threw the right hand door wide, and looked into the cold blue eyes of a long-expected nightmare.\nIt was the Space Wanderer, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s beard was downy. $SURNAME1 had a luxuriant mane of sun-bleached hair. Tears streaked the face beneath the hair. The face looked as though it had been carved from stone. $SURNAME1\u2019s eyes were the smoky-blue of ancient turquoise.\n$SURNAME1 stood outside the iron gates, hands hanging at his sides, crusted boots over glittering, sandy soil. The matted beard opened:\n\"Please\u2014\"\n$SURNAME0 nodded. \"Yes,\" he said, \"yes.\"\n$SURNAME0 melted back into the great hall. The doors shut and locked themselves. The ironwork of the exterior walls chattered musically as the dead bolts were shot home.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was safe once more. With the vicious fighting stature of a meat-eating plant...", "0_6_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe space was a cabin in a class-one soviet war rocket.\nThe class-one war rocket was part of the fleet of the armed forces of the Soviet Union.\nThese forces were lining up on the hazy moonlit landscape outside the cabin window. At the order of their Chief, they were lining up twenty miles west of the city of Newport, Rhode Island.\nThe city of Newport, Rhode Island, was the only city in the state of Rhode Island. It was a pleasant city, and so adequately defended itself that it proudly had been able to reject the United States Army, Navy, and Air Force. There had been some confusion as to whether the Navy really wanted Newport, but that had been cleared up by Stalin. Stalin had at last forced open the door to Newport by threatening to attack it with a two-egg soviet war rocket. $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill had said that two eggs were more than a fair share for any city, but the British Government had felt forced to agree with Stalin.\nThe line of soviet war rockets stood twenty miles west of Newport, Rhode Island. They were poised twenty miles above the earth.\nTheir warheads touched earth twenty miles west of Newport.\nThe warheads glowed red.\nA", "0_5_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-eight in the morning</span>. The place was a thousand miles from Hollywood, three hundred miles north of Las Vegas, sixty miles south of Reno, one hundred and ten feet above the Lazy Luxury Desert Estates, a gated community outside the city limit of Las Vegas. The place was controversial from the moment it was conceived, and there were rumors that Lazy Luxury would run out of water, or that the electrical wiring was undersized, or that the sewage system would spoil the neighborhood, or that the developers were out to make a fast buck, or that a right-wing plot was behind it, or that the trees were stunted, or that the air up there was sickening and sinister, or that the view was polluted by a distant range of nuclear test explosions, or that a freak wind, coming down out of the mountains at night, would pick up a house and throw it over, killing everybody. Or no wind. Maybe no freak wind would come. Maybe the men inside this house were safe. Maybe the quality of their sleep improved, the recuperative value of rest deepened, in this altitude and climate. Maybe they drifted in, out of the desert chill, through the thick artificial air of their new house, and bedded down in the chasm between...", "0_18_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-six in the evening</span>. Weegee would forever remember that night as the beginning of the end of everything that was in any way amusing in New York. Up to that point, after all, the citizens of Gotham had been able to look out their windows and see that the world was sane. True enough, there had been corruption in high places, madness of a variety of kinds had flourished, nevertheless the visible world was reasonable. At least people felt that way. A reasonable person could look through life and see that everything was in its proper place....But at six twenty-six on November 1, Doctor Lafayette Ron Hubbard, Mary Sue Person, and four other men of the Sea Organization entered a press conference at the New York Hilton and began talking sense. They talked sense that night and twice more, two evenings later. By the end of the week, the sense they had talked would be like smog\u2014all pervasive. People all over New York would feel the way you feel after reading too much comic strips... Images senseless and broken, with underlining sardonic laughter all around. By the end of a month, New York would be in ruins, like ancient Carthage after the Romans had finished with it. And no fit place for human beings. Only for the police.\nFor this was...", "2_20_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight seventeen in the evening</span>.\nFor the first time in eleven months, there was no new bond issue from Pierce Inverarity\u2019shoardof corporations. There was no bond issue, and there were no bondholders. The last eight dew payments had come more than two hours late.", "1_7_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Red $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized beside the $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 Memorial Bell.\nThey appeared precisely above the place where the name plates for Red and his dog would have been if anybody had had the time and money to put them up. Where such time and money had not been wasted, the black marble was smoothly polished, unblemished.\nThe three footprints materialized on the polished black marble within a yard of $SURNAME0\u2019s own footprints.\nThe footprints were not $SURNAME0\u2019s, however. They were not even human footprints. They were the footprints of a great white cockatoo.\nThe cockatoo had only three toes.\nThe limousine which had passed through the wall of iron filings came to a stop. An Army helicopter touched down beside it. The door of the limousine opened. A policeman, a chauffeur, and a bulldog got out. The chauffeur hurried to open the passenger\u2019s door.\nThe passenger was a young man in a purple silk dressing gown. His feet were thrust into fur-lined yellow slippers. He was bound for Mars.\n\"Morning, $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Skip,\" said...", "0_7_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-nine in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time. Old Salazium had already been up for five hours and would be up for fifty-five more before he slept. He was fifty-four years old\u2014young and vigorous, considering what he\u2019d been through. His black skin had a lustrous sheen. His big white teeth flashed everywhere among the freckles and the kinky beard. He came from Konkrook, on the southwestern fringe of Flintlock territory. He was peculiarly well-suited to the profession of licensed joker. Only a real fool could convince the public that he was a genuine fool, and only a healthy fool could live under the conditions imposed on Salazium by his work.\nIt was important for the licensed joker never to seem like anything but a fool and never to understand anything about anything except insofar as it might pertain to the fashionable victims of jokes: groups, categories, types. Salazium demonstrated all his wisdom by inference. \"How does a fool know what he likes?\" he would ask an audience. After a dramatic pause, he would answer his own asked question: \"He likes things just the way they are.\" There would be no call for Salazium to elaborate on such profundities.\nNor...", "0_13_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-nine in the afternoon</span>, sun shining, white glinting off the tiled roofs of the city. Kind of a cool drizzle in the air. Summer glinted off sun roofs, winked visors as Lang three-point-putted his way from the parking lot to the lobby. He stood at a bank of phones, looking at his watch and thinking about what a guy could get away with, and about how he was going to have to call home and ask for money soon.\nAlthough you could slurp tea through his nose and his right eye was a bad shade of yellow and he had some kind of two-day beard, and although he weighed more than he wanted to and found it increasingly difficult to get through a day at all, he was still pretty good-looking. This was how he paid the rent. This was how he kept himself in Des Moines. Lang had a kind of permanent collar-stuck-in-the-jacket-button look that women found helplessly adorable\u2014not that they had anything else to go on. He couldn\u2019t do that Johnny-Cash thing of letting the women get to know him and then captivate them. He had to do it from the git-go\u2014 capture their hearts with his (...", "2_6_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1 did not move.\nMinutes passed. Outside, finally, the lonely chuh-chuh of a retreating wheelchair. The locked door vibrated to the thwop-thwop-thwop of get-well cards being hurled against it by an irritable, short-of-patience arm.\nA shadow fell across the door, expanding.\nThe rattle of skeleton-keys outside, in the hands of two husky apprentices\u2014Kirby the Sport and Brud Squires. A tingling, heart-stopping moment, when the dead bolt shot back, when the latch hissed open, when the ponderous door groaned toward the jamb, forced by three pairs of hands, approaching shoulder-high, like a battering ram.\nWhat could be seen from the sill was a carpeted hall, walls of green vertical trellis-work. $SURNAME1\u2019s door opened a full two feet, stopped by a catch.", "1_16_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span> of a fine late October day in the year of Our Lord 1952. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was forty-six minutes young. Only ten minutes before, his iron dog namedandy had materialized on the head of the statue of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 in the center of Newport.\n $SURNAME0\u2019s materialization had been much heralded, and a crowd of gawkers was on hand to greet him. The gawkers were sheltering under the leafy, spacious ground-floor wings of the $SURNAME0 mansion.\nA lesser crowd of reporters was sheltering in the mansion\u2019s shaded north porch. In a moment, they would be let into the cool beige dignity of the mansion to interview him.\nOut of the sky came a sound gradually nearer and nearer\u2014the sound of a rocket with legs. The crowd in the leafy lower wings held its breath.\nThe crowd raised its head like a field of grass to the northwest, waiting for the first glimpse of a white contrail. The approaching sound of the rocket with legs was still much too faint to be sure of. The crowd spread its gaze in a fan, waiting for the white contrail to stitch the sky\u2019s blue canvas.", "1_16_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>, Martian time, and the place was the Gorgeous Metal Building, which was fifty-eight stories high, in downtown Washington, D.C.\nThe occupant of the eighty-second-floor office in that building was the Magnificent Zapallo, president-for-life of the company that occupied the building, the Gorgeous Metal Products Company.\nThe company made metal products that were not in the least bit gorgeous.\nMr. Zapallo lived on Newport Beach, in a sixty-foot houseboat. Only two times a year did he work at his regular executive office in his company\u2019s skyscraper. One time was Christmas. The other time was when the thermometer outside threatened to go above seventy.\nToday was one of those threatened-to-be-hot days, and Mr. Zapallo\u2019s company was going to be closed for the holiday, which was Christmas; so Mr. Zapallo was at his office desk, in the executive tower.\nThe door to Mr. Zapallo\u2019s office was open. There was a line of people out the door and all along the hall, waiting to go in, when the man in penguin suits came along the corridor. The man wore his expensive black suits with great pride...", "2_0_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to one o'clock in the morning</span>. The hard heart, the white heat and all the rest of it had won out. Revolution was over. The Man had won. The faceless bastards had finally cut down the bad guy, $FIRSTNAMEF2 felt elation, resentment, then saw the spilled contents of her purse all over the linoleum, started going out of her mind.\nDuring which a phone call from San Narciso brings together two of the story's principal characters.", "2_23_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-three in the evening</span>.\nKrup got up shakily from the stairs and loped over to the phone. \"Hello?\" he said.\n\"Why don\u2019t you go outside, you sinister bastard?\" a barely articulate fieldworker spat into the phone. \"You sad old bastard? Why don\u2019t you go out to my house right now and play around with my wife, you sinister sad old bastard?\"\n\"I don\u2019t know what you\u2019re talking about,\" said Krup, calmly.\n\"You sorry jackass,\" spat the worker. \"You\u2019re talking to a sad old man, now. You sorry jackass.\"\n\"Hello?\" said Krup.\n\"I tried to get your father, for this,\" the worker said nasally.\n\"I had a hunch it was you,\" said Krup.\n\"Hello? Hello?\" said the worker. \"Hey? Hello?\"\nKrup hung up the phone.\nThe time was eleven-thirty-eight in the evening.", "2_23_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe only part of the proceedings that impressed itself vividly on the senses of $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 was the breaking of the windows and the cracking of the concrete support. This commotion was a medium of communication that his mind could understand.\nThe police had put him through a course in self-defense. His call to the police had been reflex. He would fight for his good name, he had said\u2014and then he had discovered that honor has nothing to do with fisticuffs, and good names can be permanently injured by blackjacks in the hands of policemen who hold the two concepts, \"officer\" and \"citizen,\" in healthy contempt.\n$SURNAME1 cried out again after the thief.\nTears filled $SURNAME1\u2019s eyes, forcing him to rub them, as the thief disappeared behind the evergreen hedge.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 blinked his eyes clear.\nThere was a car swinging into the driveway. This was not a police car or an official car of any sort, but a Cadillac limousine. The limousine was bright blue. It had dark windows.", "0_20_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty in the evening</span>, the Sun was out, there wasn\u2019t a cloud in the sky. Synchronous relations with Earth had been achieved at oh eight thirty plus two. As they ate, Fieldbinder had observed an English airliner fly off over the trees and wispy towers, had observed the arc of contrails it left behind it like smoke rings or felled dominoes in the sky. Gradually, the contrails evaporated, and were gone.\nAt first Anna hadn\u2019t been able to eat. Fieldbinder. ate her share of the food that both hadn\u2019t been able to eat. She was in a bathrobe. Fieldbinder drank orange juice and beamed idiotically over the Castle Keep, through the automatic field of the security bubble, across the yard and trees and wispy towers to the out-of-reach plane, plane, plane.\n\u201cSo here I am,\u201d he said cheerily after watching the plane for a bit.\n\u201cHere you are,\u201d said Anna, nodding in the light.\n\u201cSo, is there anything I should know?\u201d said Fieldbinder. \u201cI mean about this, you know. Situation?\u201d", "2_2_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nA woman who had the almost luminous beauty of a convert to a true faith now came into the room. She carried a bucket of water and some yellowed pieces of cloth in her hands.\nThe woman was $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nShe put down the bucket and the yellowed pieces of cloth. She crossed the room to a chair made of solid iron, with iron chains for legs. She put her hands on the frame of the chair. She tilted her head back, closed her eyes reverently.\nThere was a metal plaque on the back of the chair. The plaque carried a very important message in metal letters. The metal letters were bronze. The message on the plaque had been written by an Earthling metalworker on Earth a very long time ago.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 read the metal message in metal letters without taking her eyes from holy heaven. She reveled in the message, in the luminous meaning of the message.\nShe read:\nTHIS CHAIR BELONGED TO CAPTAIN UNK.", "0_20_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>, and the crowds in the street were swelled by now, and the streams had become one stream, and all the way up the hill and down the hill people were criss-crossing to and from the hallowed corner, and the arc-lights on the street all came on their yellow disks and floated and hovered over the crowds, and they all talked and shuffled and stopped, and then went on again. In that corner, in the midst of all that hurly-burly, Munro, Gaitskell, the Secretary of the Party's Welsh Branch, and the Secretary of the Party's Scottish Branch, were holding a council conferring about what should be done about certain straggling branches of the Party. The office was dark and small; there were seven coats hanging on pegs: Munro's overcoat, his wife's coat, Arthur's coat, the boy's duffle-coat, the housemaid's coat and waterproof, and the coalman's coat. And the things on the table in the midst of the room, lit by a yellow light, were also all yellow\u2014a knife, a cruet, a mustard-pot, and a box of matches. The minutes of the last seven conferences were in a pile...", "2_11_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty in the morning</span>. Beeber had slept his eight hours, which was more than most people could say. Sometimes he slept only four hours. Sometimes he only napped in a brightly-shaded easy chair in his office.\nThe knocking didn\u2019t stop. It was rhythmic. Somebody wanted in awfully bad.\nBeeber got out of bed. He threw the pillow at the knocking. It collapsed against the door.\n\"Go away!\" said Beeber into the door.\nThere was no answer from outside. The knocking continued. It didn\u2019t stop.\nBeeber sighed. He stomped over to the door. He opened it.\nThere were three cleaning women standing in the hall. They were wet. They weren\u2019t smiling.\n\"Yes?\" said Beeber.\n\"You said go away,\" said a middle-aged cleaning woman.\n\"I meant you,\" said Beeber. He pointed at the ceiling, in the general direction of Mary Frances Nickel\u2019s apartment.\n\"The lady in twelve-A says the water\u2019s come into her apartment,\" said the cleaning woman. \"The whole ceiling, she says. We\u2019re supposed to call the management people.\"", "2_22_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-three in the evening</span>.\n\"I love you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I truly love you.\" The Space Wanderer chuckled happily, happily rubbed her derri\u00e8re, then yelped and went into a crouch. A great ball of light had bobbed over the city horizon and was approaching Earth in great bounds and headed, apparently, for the Space Wanderer\u2019s room on the thirtieth floor of his apartment building.\n\"What\u2019s that?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nThe Space Wanderer put his lips to his wife\u2019s left ear, then pulled her violently back into the shrubbery with him, hugged her hard against himself. Another ball of light was approaching Earth, and another, and another, and very soon all of Earth\u2019s night had become crisscrossed and webbed with the paths of the great balls.\n\"My God!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"It\u2019s space ships!\"", "0_3_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe clock struck four. This was on the dot. There was no mistake about it.\nThe $FIRSTNAMEF1 took a step backward and suddenly, as he did so, disappeared.\nAnd then there was a long, drawn-out, quivering, beautiful rumble of thunder, as if all the thunder in the world were tired of being kept inside the storm and had exploded in one final glorious, contained, and already dissipating bang.\nAnd then there was a pause, a silence.\nAnd then the telephone rang.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 yanked the telephone\u2019s thin wire to its fullest extension, hurled the black apparatus against the far wall of his cubicle. The toilet behind him broke, and he swam in filth.\nThe thunder and lightning had struck everything in the universe except $FIRSTNAMEB0, his totenmaschines, and his thin-walled cubicle.\n\"Damn! Are you all right?\" said a man with a loud, official-sounding voice. \"Where are you? What happened?\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not answer.\n\"We traced the call\u2014we know you\u2019ve got a phone in there, buddy,\" said the man. The man paused. He said, \"We\u2019re...", "0_12_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-eight p.m.</span>\nAt twelve thirty-nine p.m., Bomb #600 exploded.\nThe explosion was more powerful than anything ever before encountered in the Universe. It was audible and measurable in almost every corner of the Universe.\nThe surface of the planet Mars, forty-six million miles away, rattled and shook.\nThe first mate was the first human being to sense the approach of the malevolent Universe-filling force that came with the explosion. He sensed it intuitively.\nHe looked wildly around, expecting to see a line of foot-soldiers with rifles charging up the gang plank.\nHe saw nothing.\nThe only unusual thing he saw on the ship was a lone officer standing half-way up the gang plank. The officer was leaning over railing to get a good look at the ship\u2019s lazarette. He was Lemon.\nHere was an officer who apparently didn\u2019t know there was a war on. Or a least an officer who thought the most serious problem in the Universe was an attraction of tropism that kept drawing his eye to the ship\u2019s lazarette.\nThe first mate thought it far more likely that the strange powerful Universe-filling force he sensed would turn out to be the Hell\u2019s Angels.", "2_14_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-six in the afternoon</span>. The church where $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 stood, and whereRumfoord stood, and where $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 now sat alone, was on the summit of a hill. Below the hill was an estate on the shores of a small, clear, bubbling sea. Attached to the estate was a garage like a riding stable. This was where $FIRSTNAMEB2 and his mechanical dogmate entered the scene, just as the final and ultimate exchange between $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME0 was getting under way.\nThe dog Cata-\n\n\tchrono was making a lot of noise on the drive.\nHe went charging, whining, growling, snarling, barking, clanking into the church.\nHe went straight to the lectern where $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was crumpled and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s Space Wanderer Suit was hanging. He ripped the suit savagely off its hanger, trying to get at something inside.", "0_15_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been under a semicomatose spell for almost six Earthling years. He had come to in order to suffer through the intensive pain that would be his for cheating in the examinations. His head hurt, and his body hurt. He couldn\u2019t remember much about how he had cheated. In fact, he couldn\u2019t remember anything at all, really.\nThe Head Nurse came and went, her enormous feet clopping the floor solidly, rhythmically. Her feet were big and yellow and, therefore, almost certainly Martian. They contrasted nicely with her bare, pink, veined thighs. The Head Nurse was beautiful by any standard\u2014ugly by every standard, Barling.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was thinking about how she would look without clothes on. He was thinking about how beautiful she would be. He was thinking about how beautiful she already was. He closed his eyes and imagined her standing in the middle of a great, gleaming white room\u2014a room in a clinic that was probably twice as big as this barrack.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 could imagine the sunlight falling softly through that big room, making bright pools on the white floor. He could imagine the Head Nurse\u2019s . . .\n\"You\u2014!\" said the Head Nurse...", "2_2_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-two in the morning</span>.\nAnd there was nothing strategically significant about the particular stretch of bleak Rhineland in which the two hundred and one bored, grumbling soldiers were standing\u2014nothing that gave it the remotest claim to historic glamor. The quiet town at the crossroads where the supplies were dropped was St. Vith, Belgium. And the crossroads was only ten miles south of the German border. And the battle line bulged twenty miles to the south of St. Vith. The quiet town of St. Vith didn\u2019t know the true state of affairs, of course. St. Vith was a very quiet town.\nBut the people of St. Vith were certainly right not to permit their beautiful, peaceful town to be used as a supply dump that would draw German fire. The people of St. Vith didn\u2019t fool around.\nThe time was now two twenty-five in the morning. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had dropped out of sight into the ground. Only the windsock on its aluminum post still fluttered bravely, still pointed suicidally from the center of a maze of foot-deep trenches to where the freighters were landing far up the field.", "2_0_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-seven ante meridiem</span>. I was not wearing a watch\u2014another foolish miscalculation\u2014but I had memorized the time, just as Animal Sam Weller had told me to. I got back into the car and floored it all the way to Vegas, pausing only to pick up some whisky on the way. I had to have some fast adrenalin before the customs men got me. I just had time to get over to the Flamingo, drop the car, and crawl into the bathroom.\nTen minutes later I was crossing the lobby of the hotel, weaving and bobbing, but keeping my eyes on the job...trying to look normal, and failing, perhaps, but managing to keep my balance and get where I was going.\nI felt anything but normal. I felt like a wild raccoon boy with five legs, two Asses, and a boner. The adrenline was pouring into me with toxic rushes. I drank five whisky\u2014sevenes on top of the marijuana extract and the reds and Tuinals I already had in me, and then on top of all that came this huge gross overload of adrenline.", "1_3_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three nineteen in the morning</span>, according to the ship\u2019s clock. The illuminated Earth in the control panel was starting to slide behind Mars-and faster and faster behind it it slid as the Earth kept turning, flattened, and changed color.\nAny new space ship, like any new car, is a tremendous pleasure to ride in, until you wreck it.\nAt two thirteen, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 had asked $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 how he happened to have nine thousand one hundred seventy-four lawsuits pending against him.\nAt three nineteen, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was weeping over Prospero, his good winged horse.\n\"We\u2019ve been had\u2014you and me, $FIRSTNAMEF1,\" he said over his tears. \"Had, had, had.\" LXXXIII\nPresident of the United States $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was asymptomatic, a free man, a happy man. He was at his magnificent best under the tremendous strain of a state visit from the legendary Tralfamadorians. Not even the knowledge that the worst was about to happen could tarnish his delight.\nAs far as Earth was concerned, rumors to the contrary notwithstanding, he was making a success of his life.\nHe was clicking from one lovely thing to another as neatly as a woman...", "2_17_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty in the afternoon</span>.\nThe rain was a drizzle.\nThe drizzle continued till midnight that night.\nForty million people on Earth, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his wife were the first two. Judge Dodger had forbidden everyone else to go.\nThe two who did go were miserable.\nThe concert was in a round white hall, deep under the Earth, far from anything. It had been tunnelled out of a salt deposit.\nThe concert hall had been made as comfortable as possible.\nThere was air conditioning.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, his wife, and little Stony Truitt were seated so close to the little orchestra that, at times, the parents in front of them had to get up and move their legs aside so they could see the small musicians over their shoulders.\nThe concert started with seventeen minutes of arpeggios.\nThen the conductor\u2019s baton rose and fell a hundred times, and pure magic happened. Twelve little people, a foot high and half as wide, turned seemingly solid blocks of music into cascades of diamonds.", "2_6_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-six in the morning</span>.\nGood News, carrying the tape recorder and the spiral-bound notebooks, led her family out of the caves, up the ravine, onto the iron plain. A blazing white steam-bath awaited them. The steam-bath contained three landing ships, the trucks, the armored personnel carriers, the machine-gun emplacements, all the vehicles and weapons, and all the men, of the regular West Blue infantry.\nThe little party halted a hundred feet away from the nearest vehicles and weapons.\nGood News had satisfied herself that her family and she could not fight their way to a space ship. They were known. They were too far from home. There were too many people in motor vehicles, with weapons. An assault on the vehicles and weapons would succeed only in getting them all killed. Even the big voices that they could make together would not panic an army the size of a regiment.", "2_19_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-one in the evening</span>. A crowd of eight thousand people, more never to come, had come for the second time to see the materialization of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog. They stood frighteningly still. The nightmare had them.\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1, seamstress and wit, sipped from a brocaded hip flask. \"Who will tell the world?\" she said.\nShe took her whistle from her mouth, and put it to her lips once more.\nGoodbye, Children!\nGoodbye until we meet again!\nWhoo-whoo-whoo-whoo!\nRex and his buddy, the Martian named $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, were in San Francisco that morning\u2014having a wonderful time drinking, gambling, and drugging. They had even bought two concubines. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had never had such a good time in his life. His good time, however, was beginning to pall. Nothing could be more fun than the best good time.", "2_6_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt the ends of the line of receivers, $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0, and $SURNAME0 made friendly noises at one another. $SURNAME1 ran his eyes mechanically down his own line.\nOnly the receiver in the pseudo-Grecian edifice was connected.\nThe receiver sat on the pediment, looking like a section of a Lydian column about to fall.\nThe Queen of Space took off her headdress. Her hair was gray.\n\"You knew my husband well, didn\u2019t you?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, in a sprightly, perky voice.\n\"I certainly did,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Oh, she didn\u2019t know him at all,\" said $SURNAME0. \"A female friend of mine told me she was a damn nuisance because all she ever talked about was the great tramp she used to know.\"\n\"Can\u2019t talk about anything else,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Can\u2019t help myself.\"\n\"Let me tell you,\" said $SURNAME0, \"it\u2019s a nuisance.\"\nThe little gilded section of column coughed. \"Hup\u2014\" it said.\nAll syllogisms flower in their time.", "2_14_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nA strong wind riffled the pages of the manuscript. The wind carried the smell of the pine-cones up to $FIRSTNAMEB0, who was appalled and bitter that his own son should be sending him a message in such a holy, blazing tongue at such a holy, blazing time.\n\"My mother,\" the manuscript said, \"was a typical North American housewife and mother\u2014a puritan, an authoritarian non-thinker.\"\nThe author of the manuscript was Magnum, by the way\u2014the darling boy.\n\" 'A wonderful boy,' everybody says about me,\" Magnum had written. The mother and son had a ritual, which was a parody of scholastic examinations in the Anglo-American homeland. Magnum would ask his mother a random question, and she would answer it.\n\"What is your color?\" said Magnum to his mother.\n\"Yellow,\" she would say.\n\"Countenance?\" said Magnum.\n\"Green,\" she would say.\n\"Diet?\" said Magnum.\n\"Purple,\" she would say.\nAnd so on.\nMagnum would then score her according to the rating scale on page 83.\n\"My boy is wonderful,\" the manuscript said. \"My boy is tremendous.\"", "1_22_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-four in the evening</span>.\nThe harmoniums, the only music ever heard on Mercury, faded off into silence, as they always did, in the middle of a hymn, at the moment when, according to custom, the singer and the audience together would have shouted out with all their might the concluding line, \"To You we bring our sorrows, to You we pour our tears.\" The strongest men were weakest then. The women were frequently seen to weep.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, at the controls of a rocket ship on the ground, had just made a horrible discovery. The firing button on the console before him had only one contact. That contact fired the ship on which $FIRSTNAMEB0 was standing.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s reason for standing on one particular ship had nothing to do with the question of which ship to fire. His reason was as idiotic as the rest of his life style. His reason was this:\nWhen he had first boarded the ship he had closed the hatch, and so it had seemed to $FIRSTNAMEB0 that closing the hatch was part of the firing procedure.\nThe ships were all alike, and the hatches were all alike. So $FIRSTNAMEB0 had closed a hatch at random before pressing the firing button.\nSo now he stood on the deck of the...", "2_11_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the upper village of Phoebe, the smallest of Saturn\u2019s rings. The village lay among aspens on a mountainside that overlooked a gorge with a bottom as clear as diamond.\nThe aspens were indigenous. The name of the village had been suggested by the fact that in a clear wind among the aspens one could see the sky through their trunks.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog were materializations again.\nThe dog\u2019s name was Sun, for two reasons. One reason was that she traveled with the Sun. The other reason was that her feet were apt to get very cold. $SURNAME0 had given her boots from his own stores. Her left boot was green, her right boot was red.\n$SURNAME0, by a series of pantomimic bodily gestures, was attempting to explain to his dog where they were.\nHe was lying on his back, his feet toward the bottom of the gorge, his head pointing at the sky. Each time he made an important point, he tucked an index finger into the pocket of his space suit and snapped it smartly.\nA flock of dandelion clocks was drifting past him in the updraft.", "1_19_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-one in the evening</span> of a gray and cloudy Monday as I flew across country with Lois and the infant and a big hard Samsonite suitcase full of the bread and the small dreams I rollicking goofy forty-year-old fool believed would support a wife and child. But the boat bounced pretty good in the turbulence we hit over Ohio and I realized I was on the point of throwing up and asked Lois if she would kindly look after my son if vomiting took place and she angelically said most certainly and took the baby. I climbed into the back and prayed I wouldn't die. I'd left my brother's car under a tarpaulin outside his house in Mill Basin, Brooklyn. I held on for dear life and staggered through New York in a cab with a horrible crick in my neck from the weird position in which I'd been holding up my own head, and I picked up the car on Flatbush Avenue and started driving to Washington in the rain with my lovely wife and child and the big suitcase of bread, with the icy Hudson River whipping by in the dark. When I got off the Thruway, the weather changed and I joined all the other goofball masochist creepers going to Washington in the rain with their laptops under their arms and their hearts in chuckholes...", "1_11_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-nine in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time. At that exact same moment Room 223 in that very same Dunlop Motor Inns was occupied by two unexpected, uninvited and unwelcome guests. They were a man and a woman, and they were not having a good time. They were under thirty years of age. Their names were Krebs and Rawlie. They were both, by the consensus of all relatives, friends, neighbors and acquaintances, the worst at what they did of anyone so far in all recorded history. The man was a motorcycle tout. His business card identified him as an independent agent for the Planet Earth Corporation, which did not exist, and for Moto-Skull, which did. The name on the woman\u2019s card was Eigenvalue, which was the name she had adopted, in a very rundown bar in Cranston, Rhode Island, when she found out her husband was dead and how he had died. A twenty-seventh lie on the business card was that she was an interior decorator.\nKrebs and Rawlie were sitting scared in Room 223 of the Dunlop Motor Inns awaiting a visit from very large people indeed. Krebs was trying to drink two quarts of bourbon in a pint glass and smother a Pain...", "1_7_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty in the morning</span>. I still had to meet my attorney. ^3 a big breakfast, the Acid King decided to moveto one of the tables aimed out at Sunset\u2014a royal command performance, with a good head start on the day\u2019s serious drinking...Actually, this was not unusual behavior for this particular squire of the vaporous \u00e2\u0080\u0093but if it cost me my attorney at that crucial moment I didn\u2019t care. I bolted for the phone, leaving him laughing happily to himself .. but when I called the hotel they told me the suite was empty\u2014he was already gone. I cursed viciously, but what could I do? I had to be at the airport in less than thirty minutes. I fought traffic all the way out to Indio to pick up my car .. and of course it fell to pieces on the way out to the airport. Just before Magic Mountain I had passing wheels go wobbly and vibrate crazily. I pulled onto the shoulder and stopped...and waited for the ambulance. But the bones were unbroken. I drove very, very slowly for the rest of the way.\nI made it with ten minutes to spare. The plane was there, waiting for me. I didn\u2019t even bother to...", "1_10_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-eight in the morning</span>. My attorney was standing at our Mint cache inside a phone booth. I was hiding behind a row of terra cotta colored Malibus about twenty yards away. I was watching the booth with a cocked .45. There were dirty barefeet off to my right, twitching nervously in the traffic flurry. The feet belonged to a tall, gray haired, oafish westerner; the man was wearing a loud Hawaiian sportcoat and a perspiring cowboy hat. He was moving around on top of a white Cadillac convertible, trying to make himself inconspicuous while clumsying with the radio hookup inside the car. But nobody was paying any attention to him. Court was in session.\nI was on the outside of the ring, with the guns. What I wanted was to get the fuck out of that parking lot with at least one piece of human loot, preferably a car...but if the old geezer had to fall, then fuck him, too. The Pig had to pay for his crimes in this life or the next. All of them. I sensed that.\nMy attorney was talking to somebody at the other end of the line. I was too far away to hear anything, but it didn't matter\u2014I could tell from...", "1_9_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-four in the morning</span>. Not a bad time to make anythinghappen at all. I chopped off half the joint, reloaded. This time, forewarned, I tuned my ears to the proper frequency, and my head was rolling and waving as the sounds came through, but in an almost soothing way. The music wasn\u2019t particularly good but it didn\u2019t matter; it would\u2019ve been okay if they\u2019d played nothing but bandas and bongos for 24 straight hours. And that\u2019s what they\u2019ll do, I thought; this is just a taste of paradise, the way things are going. I wondered if any of my friends were stoned yet. Probably yes; but maybe not. It was too early to know. Meanwhile...ah, that was good grass! I stopped worrying and concentrated on the music. Another newscast: Che Guevara\u2019s speaking from the Bolivian jungle.\nI tried to tune him in; good or bad I wanted to know. My head was rocking and the music was knotting itself in there\u2014what I needed was a tranquilizer to quiet things down. I got off the table and walked very slowly and deliberately to the icebox...opened it...", "1_19_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-three in the evening</span> and we were back in the parking lot, about to get into $FIRSTNAMEB3's car for the run back to New York. \u201cWhat are we waiting for? Let's go.\u201d\n\u201cWe're waiting for this foolishness to pass. Can't you see there are no heads? They're all talking about wonderful, beautiful, stupendous, magnificent, superflabulous, fantabulos, transcendent, absolutely the most incandescent golden words in the world, and of course absolutely nothing at all, and about how all times are difficult, particularly now -- yes, right now -- and most especially right now, and a new approach, yes, and looking at time, yes, time itself, the old beaten mystery, the old sweet golden mystery that they've talked of before and always missed. They are indeed talking. Wait and see. Wait till the talking stops.\u201d\nIt was eight o'clock. Out front was a big hardshell Baptist church and Joe and I went inside to see what was happening and noticed the congregation sitting on wooden benches in front of an enormous red curtain and having their souls saved one by one by a mean-looking preacher who pointed t'em out with a long stick and told them things about...", "2_16_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and somewhere behind us in that distance\u2014no doubt surrounded by the roaring sound of confusion\u2014Lenny was standing there, looking earnestly at some mother's son who was about to get tore arsehole.\nSixteen minutes after...and Van Bokkelen had passed us. He walked back toward the Stadium. A cop, military staff, highrankinjerseycroff cop...whatever, he didn't want any part of this. He also probably knew what it would mean if he actually stopped us. A call to Washington. Plots checked. A couple of Ex Presidents suddenly showing up with a wild tale, plus bad character and heavy weaponry. \u201cI'll hink I'll let them go,\u201d he probably said to himself, walking back to the roar, while our massive Red Shark sat there immobile in increasingly dense traffic.\nAnd even then, if I had said something equally appropriate to the situation, like \u201cLet's take another quick vote, fellas: Where are we...and where the fuck do \u2018we want to go?\u201d we might have got out of there.", "0_14_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nOrganized Bandits, Ltd., had the piece. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the owner-to-be of Titan, had stopped gambling. He stood motionless, staring out a window at the desolate plain beyond.\nEverybody else in the Floyd Julian $SURNAME0 Museum was making similar plans for getting off Titan without losing his skin, reputation, and wallet.\nAn executioner was expected at any moment.\nNo one expected him to come on a bicycle.\nNo one expected a bicycle to make so much noise.\nThe bicycle did not actually rumble, but drew spatters of excitement from the rainy air by its sawing back and forth between two chords like this: rzzz-click-rzzz-click.rzzz-\nHaving no idea that he was to be the lead anecdote in histories of the Golden Age, Historian $SURNAME0 materialized out of thin air in the hallway just outside the room that he had fouled with his time machine. His materialization was not noisy or sensational.\n$SURNAME0 became visible as a human being\u2014merely a young, befuddled man in a raincoat.\n$SURNAME0 wondered drunkenly where he was.\nHe had materialized naked in space on...", "2_17_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-five in the afternoon</span>.\nThe time was five twenty-six in the afternoon. John N. Backer was sitting at a long, mottled bar of colonial design in an establishment called The Little Fleet. He ordered another drink of an unpronounceable nature. Pulled from a tank, bedewed with moisture in the chilled light of the bar, the beverage looked like a jiggling, pastel waterfall. John N. braced his fingers around his glass and stared casually ahead.\nTwo men to whom sugar-plums were phantoms of the most minute and remote description entered the Little Fleet. So much seemed apparent by the fact that one was smoking a cigar two feet long, and neither had come to buy stamps. Everyone in the bar turned to look at these men. That they were strangers, potential trouble, was as obvious as the dim shine of their black hats.\n\"Let\u2019s get some drinks, Konrad,\" one said to the bigger of the two. The big man nodded and smiled at the bartender.", "1_1_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-one in the morning</span> of 11 August 1996, and Nicole was gone.\nBut I was in pretty good shape to find out where. For, you see, I had been up all night, waiting. And up all night, waiting, is more than many can do.\nThere is a particular hell for the one who waits, and those who have worked there know all about it. My waiting room was packed full of souls who were waiting on God or the hopermans or the arrival of a telegram or the rending of space and the sudden sizzling arrival of the One they had Spoken to, which was what they always said, Spoken to. And whenever they came to my door and asked if I had an appointment I would say no, no, no. For the truth is that the appointments had been taken care of over a long month previous, and the only way anyone could get in to see me was to make a new appointment, and then keep it.\nMy secretary\u2019s name was Sam A. Ordinas, Esq., and there were three of us working out of what had been one bedroom in a home that had been bought at sheriff\u2019s sale by a syndicate made up of friends of mine who were trying to make me...", "1_17_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-three in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF0 ran into the screen door of the porch, banging it, and went sliding on through it, like a softball, slamming the Flentkins\u2019 screen door, which clapped open and closed behind her, and came to a stop, seated in the Flentkins\u2019 porch swing.\nThe runabout carrying $FIRSTNAMEF1, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 drifted aimlessly against their dock. The outboard motor sputtered, died. The cabin of the runabout became dark.\nThe silence in the cabin settled like dust.\nMonk staggered to his feet. He was bruised and bleeding. He was disoriented.\nHe looked out the cabin window, into the darkening mist.\nHis hands groped along the walls until he found a light switch. He flipped it. Nothing happened. His mouth moved silently as he did some hard thinking. His hands fluttered wildly as he tried to remember what the last thing had been that he had done before the control panel had died. When he had done some hard thinking about that, his hands found the propane canisters that propelled the boat, and he unscrewed the caps.\nThen he spat into the air\u2014threw his saliva toward his propellers, then groped toward...", "2_23_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The conspiracy was now thirty-six minutes old.\nNo one knew of it but the chauffeur and the cook and no one would know of it for forty-two hours.\nNo one at all would be affected by it for forty-five hours, for no one would know about it for forty-five hours. The flight would take ten hours. There would be one hour of formalities at Rodman\u2019s Neck, and there would be the return flight, which would take eight hours and twenty minutes, making the entire trip\u2014from just after midnight Tuesday morning until just before eleven o\u2019clock Wednesday morning\u2014make the entire trip thirty-nine hours and fifty minutes long. There would be no news of the flight for forty-five hours.\nThis was the very worst possible time at which something could go wrong with the plane or the bomb, or with anything else that had to do with the actual conspiracy.", "0_5_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span> and the place was the living room of 2202 Blymer Terrace, $FIRSTNAMEM1-Nash, Chicago, Ill. The story was being composed by a man named Herbert Cox, aged thirty-six. By profession Cox was an instructor of political science in an institute of higher learning. But his true vocation was being the husband of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, the father of thin, sulky, pubescent $FIRSTNAMEB2, and the grand vizier of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent.\nRollings and reverberations in the German pipe organ of the wind came down the throats of ten thousand trumpets:\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM1\u2014Nash\u2014Chicago\u2014Ill\u2014\" said Cox to himself, \"2202\u2014Blymer\u2014Terrace\u2014\" The thunder was a roll of snare drums in Charlie Parker\u2019s band. The lightning was stroboscopic, flashing a thousand words a second on a tearing screen, washing the eye and numbing the brain.\n\"Indifferent\u2014\" said Cox to himself.\nHow pretty it was!\nAll around the world, from Spitzbergen to the Antarctic, from the deserts of the Moon to the bottoms of the seas, the tobacco-like weeds called sala-ma-...", "0_11_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to noon</span>.\n\"Let\u2019s go back to the ship,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. She started the engine, and the boat began to move back the way it had come.\nMarty did not look back at the island. He kept his head bowed and his hands in his lap. He heard Nero, aboard the $SURNAME0, howl plaintively.\nAt a quarter past twelve\u2014\nAt a quarter past twelve, two hundred and seventy thousand three hundred and thirty-two ships, having received the chrono-synclastic infundibulated material of a good religion, were on course to take good care of themselves. One hundred and forty-three ships had already arrived.\nThese ships represented thirty-three species. They came from sixty-four different solar systems. All save eight of the solar systems were ones that Darwinians could understand. The eight from inexplicable solar systems were represented by a mere thirty-two ships.\nNone of the ships that were on course to take care of themselves belonged to the dominant species of its particular home planet. The dominant species, so far as is known, of none of the planets concerned had the slightest interest in anything that was about to happen.\nThe most common ship was a species of green grasshopper,...", "2_13_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Martian Army of Invasion was in rout.\nThe graveyard, which would earn a place in history as Diamond-Fields, was an undignified location for so joyous a binge.\nBehind the graveyard, there was a scree. That was the only clear space on the whole of the slopes of Maxwell Montes. It was a spot that a lemming might have chosen to commit suicide in. Beyond the graveyard and the scree was nothing but a moonscape of wildly tumbled boulders. Only the graveyard had a commanding view of the lowland of Parmenides, and of the lovely, far-flung village by the sea.\nUp the graveyard, between two headstones, was wedged the painted and bewhiskered fuselage of a Martian torpedoboogie. The twin fixed guns on its fritznuckered wings were aimed, one at the twinkling tourist camp by the sea, the other at the stacks and towers of the three great Martian war vessels in their smoking near-Earth orbit.", "2_3_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1 had sixteen hours and ten minutes, seven hours and forty-one minutes, two hours and fifty-one minutes, twenty minutes, to get ready for the biggest moment of his life. He didn\u2019t even know what the moment was going to be.\nThe point on the horizon where the Metal Wobblers were due to appear was marked by a very bright star.\nThat bright star was Earth\u2014the home planet that $SURNAME1 did not know he loved so much.\nWith a good pair of Space Ranger bona fides, $SURNAME1 could have hitchhiked a ride to Earth on almost any ship that passed by. Very few ships stopped at Tralfamadore.\n$SURNAME1 neither knew nor cared what a bona fide was, or how to use one. He had never bought a copy of the Space Ranger Code, and was too proud to ask $SURNAME1. to show him one.\nAnd so he remained on Tralfamadore, while other prized messages from the system administration went striding off through the Galaxy. They had ridden the backs of Metal Wobblers. They had a free ride, free food\u2014and a comparatively important message to deliver.", "2_16_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-two in the afternoon</span>. It was very hot\u2014hotter all the time, it seemed.\nUncomfortable as they were, the pauses in the dark gave Metzgers time to think. And so, since his last Thinking Pause was only a quarter of an hour ago, and since ninety minutes had gone by since the first one, Neil Metzger had had a lot of time to think; he had done his bit, during those ninety minutes, to stay ahead of the crowd.\nFor instance, he had figured out exactly why the flashes of light he saw in his dark interior were called \u201clightning.\u201d Well, he said to himself now, obviously it was because someone had decided years ago that lightning looked like flashes of light.\nThe time was four forty-three in the afternoon. The sun still hadn\u2019t set.", "2_6_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThere wasn\u2019t any time any more, either, because the clocks and watches had all stopped. And there weren\u2019t any days or nights any more, either. And there weren\u2019t any years or centuries, or millennia, or eons. And there weren\u2019t any worlds any more, and there weren\u2019t any universe.\nThe space ship was now adrift in space and had been for some time. The few crewmen alive were $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB1, and the good-looking dark-eyed girl, Good News.\nThe three survivors were trying to make their way back to the air lock as a first step toward adroitly making their way to the lifeboats.\nThe three had no grenades, and were unarmed otherwise.\nThe good-looking dark-eyed girl, Good News, was a far superior being in the eyes of $FIRSTNAMEB0 to her lamebrained and insensitive husband, $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nGood News had the bearing of a natural queen and the capacity for genuine love-even for an orphan like $FIRSTNAMEB0, who had been fed dog food as an adult.", "1_2_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-one in the morning</span>. Because she had to wait in my apartment for the sitter to show up, I had promised an inventible supply of bourbon and beer. The bourbon, in particular, seemed important; although I knew almost nothing about it, I gathered it was something special, something that would impress her. When the time came to use it I found it all confusing. What did this cap thing thingie do? What was the proper proportion of water to use? Should I pour it directly in the bottle or into the glass first? What difference did it make? And all those amusing false bottoms and bootleggers\u2019 traps\u2014how were they used? Where? What for? Why? But finally I hit on the right formula and felt confident enough to present it to my guest.\nShe was sitting on the bed with my tape recorder before her, more or less afraid to fool with it. The pile of albums next to it included Miles Davis, Stan Getz, Yellow Jackets, Sarah Vaughan, Coleman Hawkins, Charlie Parker, Gerry Mulligan, and an album of Mozart \u2019 s piano sonatas I borrowed from my father for some sentimental reason. Through the wall came the smell of frying sausage and the sound of my neighbor watching President Johnson on television.", "2_17_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-two in the afternoon</span>.\nAt seven thirty that evening, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 got into her chartered helicopter and flew away from the estate for good.\nThis is a true account of the $SURNAME0 Estate fiasco, as far as it has been possible to reconstruct it. Small portions of it rest on hearsay, but these portions sum up crucial points in the history of controversy over the event.\nThere exists in Amsterdam a manuscript called Worstfoot\u2019s Winning of the Marshes, a chronicle of Tralfamadore written on the planet\u2019s highest recommendation. The manuscript was written by Salo, who was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s older brother.\nAn English version follows:\nWork on the estate was carried on in secrecy, with two exceptions. The first exception was the hiring of three thousand regular Earthling laborers. The laborers were given no hint of where they might be working und. they undoubtedly believed, if they troubled themselves to wonder, that they were engaged in transportation-depot or munitions works. They were paid in scrip good only at company stores.", "1_2_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>. Many people had taken a redeye flight from Los Angeles to Seattle, for a reason that nobody in that crowd knew exactly. The reason was this: A concert was to be held, a quite simple concert, by the rock group known as the Tristero Intruders, who were so obscure then that even the authority on rock and roll, author of a recent best seller, knew nothing about them.\nThe Tristero people were exceedingly secretive. To pick a random sample of the kind of persons they were dealing with, consider these facts about the lad who had arrived at 2:30 in the morning at the TWA flight terminal in Seatrain, Seattle. His name, Robert Sui Generis; his occupation, handling prizefights, mostly illegal ones; his address, The Street, Chula Vista, California, 91910. On his identification papers Great Britain was given as his place of birth.\nOne might assume from these credentials, assuming one did not know, as the Tristero people did, that Great Britain was merely a section of The Street, Chula Vista, California, 91910. One Tristero agent, for instance, Great Britain, had been made a Vice-President by sending her a typewritten...", "1_6_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nOne minute later\u2014as happened also at chronoinstants 91, 183, 274, and 384\u2014a messenger fruit-fly arrived at Newport and was received in audience by $SURNAME0.\nThis particular messenger was a male, and he was of great significance to chronogeometrical plotting, for he was the five-hundredth male fruit-fly used by $SURNAME0 in his chronogeometric experiments.\nBecause it was expected in the Celestial Times that the first great event predicted by $SURNAME0 to his mate would occur almost at once, a brief account of that predicted event may now be given.\nThe Solar System was to suffer a bombardment of meteorites unprecedented in violence, lasting twenty minutes, and finally ending as it had begun, without notable follow-up phenomena.\nNinety-four Earthlings and thirty-six Martians were riding in a rocket ship on Mars when the big event was reported to begin. The veteran rocket-men had died, one by one, with monotonous regularity, and their places had been taken by Earthlings who had never seen a rocket ship before.\nThere was an extremely good chance that the replacement crew would kill off the Martians in the course of the bombardment, leaving...", "0_14_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. The doors of the plane were still open. Resigned, aloof from all of us, $FIRSTNAMEB1 sat in his seat, waiting to die.\nI was drinking vodka with lemon juice. I was already drunk. I felt reckless and irresponsible and goalless, but also elated and lecherous.\nWhen five minutes had gone by and the plane was still on the runway, four more passengers walked up the aisle and fed their tickets into the space agencies grille.\nThe plane did not move.\nI wolfed pretzels and salami left over from lunch. The salami was eaten with random stabs of my right index finger. Bits of the salty meat sailed by my ear, into the space behind my seat.\nThe plane still did not move.\nMore people were fed into the grille. There were now fifteen passengers waiting to be processed. The total included Stanza, me, and $FIRSTNAMEB1. The pilot was not one of the passengers. He was up front, behind locked doors.\nI finished the vodka-lemon-juice. I wrenched the glass out of its plastic holder, ran the whole glass full of German beer. I spilled some. Some ran down my wrist.\nAs the beer...", "0_15_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe microphone by $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s clean-shaven lips beeped and squealed softly. The audience was whistling and clapping so loudly that the beeps couldn\u2019t get through.\nStill, it was the public address system by which their commanders spoke to the recruits in formation\u2014and there were other things that the commanders wanted to say to $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 saw a red plunger pop out of the microphone\u2019s casing, risking a squint at it. The red plunger meant that the man on the platform could make his wishes known to the recruits through the mikes hidden in the classrooms.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 pushed the red plunger down. He put his mouth close to the mike. He shouted a single word into it: \"Fall!\"\nThe barracks shuddered.\nTurn, fall\u2014the man on the platform had said. Fall, hell! $FIRSTNAMEB0 thought, the sun just went right down the drain without so much as a god-damned by-your-leave.\nWhen it comes to dog-robberies and troops, how does the rest of the army do it? $FIRSTNAMEB0 wondered. And he wondered what his officers would say when they found out that he...", "0_2_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe doors would open in forty-five seconds.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shuffled in place, cleared his throat very noisily, clapped his hands together once, twice. And then, just as the vibrato chatter at the rear of the crowd began to crescendo into a thunderous roar, he straightened up, threw his head back proudly\u2014and fainted dead away.\nHis heart, overstrained by the most violent emotions man can experience, simply stopped.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 died like a hero, smiling serenely.\nHis friends carried him into the space ship that had brought him to Earth so long ago.\nRansom K. Fern turned the handles. He closed the hermetic door.\nHe pressed the ignition button.\nThe space ship snarled, lifted like a helicopter, and tore away from the rest of the fleet.\nAnd then its rockets howled, and howled, and howled, and howled! With terrible majesty the ship hurtled off toward Mars\u2014and the Sun.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was deadweight at the controls, stowed in a pilot chair there, between the instrument board and a wall. Thus he rode through space to Mars and to the Sun.\nHis eyes fl...", "2_21_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe time was quarter after nine o'clock in the evening, at just about the time the police cruiser slid smoothly to a stop at the corner of Narragansett and Prudence, in the block that contained Bradley's House of Horrors. The cop who got out of the cruiser to investigate was a young, strapping, stony-faced man appropriately named D. T. McClean.\n\"That\u2019s him,\" said Billy Gilman, peering out from his vantage point in the rear of the cruiser. \"That\u2019s that crazy bastard that snuck in and stole my popcorn, when I was having an inadvertent bowel movement.\"\nMcClean calmly walked up the sidewalk of Narragansett, in the shallow arc movement of a parent who, looking for his missing child, fears all the dreadful possibilities, but prefers not to scare the child by screaming in zigzags. The stores on either side of the street were empty, closed. Not a single overhead light shone in any of them. Where weren't the lights? wondered McClean.\nWhere was everyone?\u2014at home, largely, symbolizing the imminent triumph of darkness over day.", "2_13_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nMentula IV, otherwise known as $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, was dead.\nHis corpse was spread-eagled on the canopy over his sunnophone in the palace of his mansion on the island of Newport.\nSomeone had decked $SURNAME0 out in a grass skirt, a Samoan war bonnet, and greasepaint. Someone had planted a spear in his left side.\nOn the golden snout of the sunnophone was fastened a sign reading SUNNOHP .\nScrawled in lipstick on the flagstone terrace below the sunnophone was a message that read:\nHEAR THIS, SPACE CADETS?\nThe message was aimed at Mars.\nThe message was intended for the triumphant Army of Mars, as it celebrated wildly in a graveyard on the slopes of Maxwell Montes.", "2_20_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-four in the evening</span>.\nA minute later, a strange thing happened to $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the beautiful child. There was a golden flash, and they found themselves staring at each other across a very big room on a very big bed. Each had a knitting needle in his or her hand, and a dirty-white clump of knitting was on the bed between their bare legs. A golden flash had put them there, and a curly-haired man in a white coat was there, too. He said something about their being the two luckiest people in the history of the world. Then the man left, and a pretty blond woman came in and castled the covers over their waists. She brought them each a bowl of liquid that looked like melted ice cream. It was a refreshing drink, and it made $FIRSTNAMEB0 want to urinate\u2014which did not require a thought process.", "2_4_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-nine in the morning</span>.\nA rustling, fluttering noise punctuated by a twanging, slapping noise occurred high above the Titan Central nose cone.\nThe chute enwrapping Mrs. $SURNAME0 and a Nimbus was tumbling crazily in a west wind.\n\"It\u2019s a chute!\" yelled the crowd-maddened timekeeper. \"She\u2019s dropped a balloon!\"\n\"Man is mortal\u2014\" said $SURNAME0. \"Man plans and God laughs. There\u2014in that thing she calls a chute\u2014she has put her last poor ounce of trust in the rule of the material. And rightly so. What can the rule of the material do for her now? In a few minutes she will land in a crowd of celebration, and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 will get her and her material system, with his money. The material system will then serve him in ways it abhors for all time. And that is what she deserves.", "0_14_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-four in the afternoon</span>. No witnesses. The body had nothing in its stomach except undigested shark and tequila. Both of the latter were of top quality.\nThe body was stretched out on a plastic ski\u2014sled and being hauled through the corridors of the building by four sweating Maintenance men. The thing barely fit through the corridors.\nThe body\u2019s feet were bare, and so were its hands. And there was dust in the toes and fingers.\nThe only remotely interesting thing about the corpse was the engraved gold ID bracelet on the left ankle:\nTHE PHIL\nThe Other Phils had descended on Holiday Isle with a vengeance on this occasion.\nThe rear section of the building, wherein lay the lavatory, chapel and administrative offices, did not even begin to sfill the need. Ten thousand Phils had descended with a ferociousness.\nThe peak occupancy load for this particular building was three hundred seventy-three persons. This could be handled by running temporary busses from the overflow areas to the Lobby Building, putting in temporary flush toilets and placing the lone chaplain on a stand\u2014up desk in the long corridor that ran from the lavatory through an arch to the chapel.\nThis building had been built with just such emergencies in mind.", "1_22_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nSuddenly there occurred a most disturbing noise but which sounded like some one moving about in the street\u2014the noise of something hitting against a window.\nIt was a night of mist, rain and wind.\nIt seemed as though a nut from a ledge had been loosened by the wind and had whirled about for an instant in the draft in an upper room.\nThat was all. It was a window slamming in the wind.\nIt seemed to Minturn, as he sat there thinking, that his whole life had been a series of such disturbances\u2014sounds that had no rational explanation.\nHe disapproved of his son going to Harvard. There were so many nice girls on Park Avenue he thought who lived in New York anyway. He hoped his son would meet a nice girl there\u2014a nice Park Avenue girl. That was the best. Anyway, best of luck to him in everything.\nHe came out of his reverie again and again. A crowd was passing Central Park. The sight of the crowd restored his cheerfulness. He would not allow himself to brood. Things were under control of themselves\u2014they always were. He remembered with amusement how they had called him stubborn at Harvard. They said he changed his mind too often. He thought upon...", "2_1_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe tower shivered.\nIt was struck again by the tornado, and by now it was, in the parlance of the stone-age soldiers, \"A real bad siege. \"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 got lost in the crowd, drifted with the crowd to the basement.\nThere was a sound-system in the basement, but it wasn\u2019t being used to keep the crowd under control. Instead, the public address system was being used as an amplifier for a tape recorder. The tape was a record of army marches.\nThe crowd became unruly when the officer in charge of the basement system switched the marches off.\nThe crowd attacked the loudspeakers, tore them down.\nThe basement had no square feet of floor space on which were not deposited earthly treasures looted from the files, warehouses, ruins and vaults of South Brooklyn.\nThese riches had been brought down by the hoarding instincts of an army that had expected to be ingloriously routed. So when victory came, the spoils turned into standard operating procedure, a thing everybody did.\nNo one had thought to establish a guard detail to protect the treasures. No one had thought of anything.", "2_2_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>, New- port time.\nEmpress Lakshmi had written her own epitaph, which was this:\nLUCK I HAD, AND LUCK I HAD TO SPEND,\nAND I SPENT IT ON THE MEMORY OF MY FRIENDS.\nThose who knew Empress Lakshmi well were spendthrifts, too. The statue of her, overlooking the Grotto, was a fussy, overdraped affair of gilt and malachite. Its waters, fed by an underground stream, were kept at a constant temperature of eighty-six degrees Fahrenheit, as warm as a human body. A fountain on the Grotto were filled with four and a half tons of healthy turtles. The turtles were an offense, since they were unhygienic, but people who appreciated luxury liked the idea of swimming among turtles.\nIt was a good idea.", "0_0_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past midnight</span>. The crystal alignment of the three-dimensional universe had shifted. Model A-69, Bureau of $FIRSTNAMEB2-propaganda, Ministry of Absurdity, issued the order to begin the year 11887.\nThe crowds from all the villages and towns, from all the ten thousand square miles of the territory, surged through the streets of Kissimee, to the palace of $SURNAME0. The palace, in ancient times, had been the home of a band of dancing bears in a traveling circus.\nA reproduction of the palace\u2019s original sign was mounted on the moon-door to the palace:\nRUMFOORD CLOCK AND WATCH FACTORY\nThe palace\u2019s lobby was an airlock again, with metal walls this time, but the smell of steamed Malabar lathes was still there. The interior doors of the palace were made of real skins, with gilt hinges, real boar skin on the right-hand door, real tiger skin on the left. The skins lent sanctity to the corridor.\nTchkarda and her mate, $FIRSTNAMEB4, the dog-satyrs who were $SURNAME0\u2019s only servants, greeted their visitors from behind the boar-skin door, Kaz...", "2_10_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe mechanic again complained of his wife to Belskutt. Belskutt again braced himself against a new onslaught of venery. The time was nine minutes past ten o'clock in the morning.\nThe twelfth member of the exploration party, field naturalist William $FIRSTNAMEF1, was seen to approach the departure area carrying an armload of antenna anemones. He was wearing dark glasses and leather leggings. He whistled cheerfully. The time was ten minutes past ten o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe antenna anemones were members of a species, one that understood a modest vocabulary of whistles. They knew commands that meant things like \"Drop everything and run as fast as you can for the foot of the first dune in the northwest quadrant.\" And commands that meant things like \"Obey this messenger for two minutes after he stops whistling.\" And commands that meant things like \"Unfurl five pink antennae from your flesh, and rise to a height not in excess of two inches above the ground.\" And commands that meant things like \"Close all your flesh from base to tip, and die quietly and cleanly.\"", "1_11_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been trying to put his death out of his mind by reading the instructions on the boxes of ammunition. He had also been trying to evade his painful loneliness by listening to the bursts of strife and laughter from the stockade. One of the things the wildmates in the stockade were saying, over and over, was that Stony Stevenson was in the middle of his last five minutes. They had not been excited about the prospect at first, but now the very repetition, the rhythm of the thing seemed to mesmerize them\u2014seemed to fill their need for ritual. The ritual went like this\u2014:\n\"What\u2019s Stony gonna get?\u201d\n\"What\u2019s he gonna get?\u201d\n\"Ain\u2019t gonna see eleven!\"\n\"Ain\u2019t gonna see eleven!\"\n\"Gonna see ten!\"\n\"Gonna see ten!\"\n\"STONY\u2019S GONNA MEET HIS MOTHER!\"\nWhen the clock struck ten, they all shook their chains and laughed wildly. When the clock struck eleven, they all wept like children and mourned suddenly, remembering happiness, remembering bitterness, remembering things like honor and decency and kindness that they hadn\u2019t known...", "1_21_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span> when we got out of the car in front of the Hotel Montana. I had my grips with me, and the boy carried the suitcase, which still had five hundred pounds of sterling, and the leather bags, which still had the stones in them.\nCohn, who always lost the ones he loved, kissed Francesca good-by. She stood on the curb, watching the car drive off, out of her life forever, a little Lilliputian figure in the moonlight, waving to us. I had three thousand dollars in my pocket. We went into the hotel, filled out cards, and were given our keys. Mine and the boy\u2019s were side by side, and $FIRSTNAMEB5\u2019s was two floors above. Cohn had a single room. We carried up our grips. I called up $FIRSTNAMEB5. She said she would be right down, and suggested the Cafe across the street. I had a cocktail, and met Cohn and the boy there.\nI had six Monaco cigars with me, and we each had a drink while $FIRSTNAMEB5 was dressing. She came into the cafe and joined us, hatless, having just come out of the barber-shop. We sat looking out at the square and talked of many things. The lights were...", "2_3_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span> in New Orleans.\nHardy was on the floor rolling dice against one wall, while the barefooted children watched him. Magic Phantom and Beauty wrestled on a bed spread with newspapers.\nMagic Phantom lost and rolled off the bed and came up fluttering laboriously, silk stockings dark with sweat and plastered to her legs.\nBeauty fluttered too, a black kite held down by wet handfuls of underwear.\nMagic Phantom and Beauty looked and smelled like something dredged out of the nadir of a Louisiana swamp.\nAnd so did the room.\nIt was furnished with burlap bags filled with horse manure.\nThe bags from one wall to another. And smoldering in their depths were fires.\nMagic Phantom and Beauty reclined on horse-manure bags at opposite ends of the room. Hardy reclined on a horse-manure bag in the middle of the room. The three doors out of the room were barred. The windows were gray to the gray predawn outside.", "2_5_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until six o'clock in the morning</span>. Around us, Las Vegas was stirring itself awake. The neon in the arcades around us was beginning to crackle and moan. Strange cars moved toward the freeway onramps. The first sunrays fell on the top of the Luxor and turned the top floors pink.\n\u201cI'm a sports writer for the Los Angeles Times,\u201d I said. I showed them my much\u2014mangled press card. One of the cops snatched it out of my hand and waved it under my nose. \u201cLosAngelesTimes?\u201d he snarled. \u201cIt's bad enough dealing with those cocksuckers from the sports page, but now you're from the obituary page. Hey! Let's bag this fuck and put him in a box!\u201d They were calling to the cops dragging poor crippled Nicholson around to place me in the copcar. The doors slammed, and we were off to the station.", "2_20_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt that moment, in fact, under the ruined bathtub, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was elbow-deep in a strangling goop of garbage and rusted steel shavings and bathtub chemicals. He was connecting a swinging arm to the great dead weight of a dynamo. He was using new pieces of wire and such condensor parts as the penal engineers had left in good condition. Other wires led to an old automobile battery.\nThe motor was running.\nThe motor was the heart of what would one day be called one of the most remarkable gadgets in history. The gadget was all but finished. All it needed to work marvels was a prime mover.\nThe big boy named Crumley returned to the telephone. \"Don\u2019t get up,\" he said to $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Have a chair, darling,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Heavens, no,\" said Crumley. \"All I ever do is sit. I\u2019m one of the sitters-in-current-parlance for a company called International Pocket\u2014you know, sitting bunkers, sitting Heinies, sitting ducks. Gawd, am I tired of sitting!\"", "2_22_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA circle of light appeared around the base of the tower. Redwine waved to his wife. His wife had remained on the tower as he had commanded.\nThe circle of light enlarged itself to a disk of light, which was the hatch-cover of a helicopter. Redwine dropped like a stone into the disk.\nCorporal R. Wilson Justice, of the United States Space Infantry, leaned out of the hatch of the helicopter.\n\"Redwine!\" he shouted. He held a microphone in his hand, into which he now spoke the secret sign of the Space Infantry. He spoke it so loud that it could be heard by anyone who cared to listen, anywhere on Mercury. \"Nothing is lost forever,\" he shouted. \"The Space Infantry never give up, never give up, never give up!\"\nRedwine, still smiling, still nodding, furiously shaken by the helicopter, returned the secret sign.\n\"God bless\u2014\" he shouted.\nThe disk of light folded itself shut.\nThe time was one minute to eleven o\u2019clock in the evening.\nThe helicopter pilot increased his speed and distance from the tower. At a distance of fifty yards, the tower exploded. Fire gushed into the sky.", "1_23_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-three in the evening</span>. I had smoked my last tobacco and thrown my pipe away. I never looked back, but started walking toward New York, thinking maybe I could get a bite to eat somewhere on the road. I covered the twenty miles to Binghamton in a blinding shower of hail, and went on in to New York.\nAll I wanted was to rest. I got a bed-full of lice in an Italian flophouse on Bowery. In the morning I emptied their backs and bugs into the toilet and went on up to the Vestibule above Utah Cleaners, among the colored and disgraced shoeshine stands. I stank of bug powder and lay smoking, afraid to take off my clothes to get in bed because of the hordes of bedbugs in the mattress. But it was too hot. I took off my shoes and got in. There was a bald bulb over my head and it flickered like hell. But I went to sleep anyway. Any place was fine, as long as I could sleep. Dead stinking civilization was all around me. There must have been twenty thousand down-and-outers in the town, and you can bet every damn one of them thought it was a hell of a town to be down and out...", "2_12_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-five p.m.</span>\nHis seat swung back, exposing a pressure valveslot into which $FIRSTNAMEB0 inserted a hollow cheek. There was a blur of pressurizing gas that rasped his insides tenderly. Then his inner skin was taut against the skin of the cabin. He was one with the cabin. And the cabin had air!\nIt was a nightmare inside a dream.\nSomething inside $FIRSTNAMEB0 now rebelled against the world of war, which he and his comrades had somehow got into. He wanted to hide in the world of this cabin, wanted never to fight, to think, to remember again. His cabin was well equipped with drains, ducts, and flues. He wanted to climb the walls and live in the system of piping.\nA voice popped in his head, giggled, and died. There was a speaker over the valve-slot by which $FIRSTNAMEB0 fed and breathed.", "1_12_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s troubles began in barracks, which was the name the soldiers of the once beautiful Earth insist on giving their glorious barrack-square classrooms. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was the battalion commander in the particular barracks in which he found himself. And the good platoon leaders and platoon sergeants and section leaders under him were doing with their sections exactly what $FIRSTNAMEB0 wanted them to do.\nThe sections were cleaning their individual barrack-rooms as $FIRSTNAMEB0 wanted them cleaned. The sections were schedu led to clean their barrack-rooms on Sundays, and they did it with a will because they were empty then. On Sundays, the soldiers could be found strolling idly in the building called a Recreatorium in the fort, eating flavored ice-cream, drinking coffee, smoking cigarettes, chatting with one another and lying about how much fun life in the Army was.\nSince the rooms were entirely empty of furniture, since the only cleaning materials were swabs, rubbing alcohol and wax, since the cleaning consisted of only making the floor wet and spreading a layer of wax over it, the work was very easy to do. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had discovered over a year ago that the soldiers did their cleaning much faster when they had boogie records to play...", "1_19_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span> when $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 hung up their space-suits.\nThey were rich.\nThe time would come when they would realize what a terrible gift wealth was, and would wonder how God could have been so cruelly malicious as to have given it to them. But by then it would be too late. They would be ramping up and down the stairways of their glorious mansion like lepidopterans on a sheet in a hurricane.\nThere was almost no time in which to make sane decisions.\n\"A man and a woman have just inherited sixty-one billion dollars,\" a financial columnist would write not long afterwards. \"What have they decided to do with their fabulous loot?\"\nNothing, of course. It wasn't fabulous loot. They didn't think it was loot, and they hadn't decided to do anything about it. They couldn\u2019t think about it. The money had as much meaning for them as the number of trees in Alberta, Canada. It was all numbers, and the numbers had no meanings, because there were no processes by which the numbers could be translated into feelings.\nHaving the money was like being stricken with some strange new disease for which there was no drug and no cure. The only symptom was that a constant accretion...", "1_6_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nHad the Space Wanderer been able to see the Titanian army, he would have seen forty-five thousand of $FIRSTNAMEM2's sons forming echelons in squads of a thousand every fifty feet, with twenty-five of $FIRSTNAMEM2's longer-legged, better branches in charge of each squad.\nHad the Space Wanderer been able to see the army, he would have been relieved to note that there were fully twenty thousand females\u2014who were the runners and couriers of the army.\nThe presence of the females gave a disproportionately high number of the units human commanders, whereas otherwise the units would have been commanded mostly by $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s rubbishier branches.\nThe presence of the females also meant that there was even an included sprinkling of desirable young conifers\u2014whereas otherwise there would only have been undesirable ancient ones.\nThis factor already had convinced $FIRSTNAMEM2 to begin a rigorous weeding-out process among the ranks of his more veteran branches. Within a fortnight, some thirty thousand, more or less, veterans would find themselves deserted by their females, with meaningless leaves. Those veterans would be honorably discharged, while their females followed more sexually appealing branches. The ranks would be filled out quickly with eager youngsters who, at the age of...", "2_9_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-eight in the morning</span>.\nIt was an interesting vision.\nLjuda Klugman was crossing the field to the main building of the preschool, carrying her magic lantern cucumber thingy, wearing an ankle-length skirt made of clear plastic. The skirt was one of her most conservative articles of clothing\u2014and it happened to be one of her favorites. She had manufactured it herself in the school\u2019s plastics lab. Anyone who had looked at the skirt and been reminded of a medieval scold\u2019s bridle had been either a prig or a dupe.\nLjuda was aware that anybody with sense or sensitivity would have been a dupe.\nShe hoped that the dupe would pay plenty.\nLjuda was offended by the arrogant assumption that one could size up a whole person by clothing. She had worn that assumption, along with many other impossible-to-live-up-to things, as a badge of authority.", "0_6_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-two in the morning</span>, Tuesday, November first, and it was the first day of the rest of the life of Alvin Straight. The car had overheated and water-locked near Cincinnati, and Alvin and Chron sighted a Family Skeptic hitchhiker and got him to come with them as far as Indianapolis, where a bug-eyed Family Fanatic picked them up and pressured them into Cincinnati that night with minutes to spare before the station closed. Alvin had never seen two such totally opposite, crazy human beings in his life. The Skeptic, who was a designer of cameras, spent most of the trip muttering, \u201cBeware, beware\u201d and talking of an obscenity trial that he\u2019d once had in Paris. The Fanatic, obviously a stock-and-bond man, insisted that Alvin and Chron sign over ninety-eight percent of their income-tax refund checks to the National Pledge Committee, who would substantiate the checks in Washington, take the money over to the U. S. Treasury and get back just what they gave. A truly subtle scheme. The money would follow the same path as the money from the regular income-tax check. It was all perfectly legal. Alvin and Chron stuck to the old routine and pretended to sign the checks...", "2_10_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten nineteen in the morning</span> of Titan\u2014and the day had just begun.\nThe air conditioners of the $SURNAME0 estate were humming, and their oscillating ventilators were tuning again in the true pattern of the solar wind. The solar wind was beginning to hemorrhage through the iron skin of the planet. And the iron skin of the planet seemed to tremble with a delicate and hopeful joy.\nAs winds go, the solar wind was a mean, howling lash compared to the solar blow that was to come seventeen minutes and forty seconds later.\nThe solar wind was one-tenth of one percent of the solar blow.\nThere were eleven twenty-four-hour days in a year on Titan. One short of the true annual calendar in Earthling reckoning.\nAs far as $SURNAME0 was concerned, he didn $SURNAME0\u2019s pocket watch on board his space ship when he emerged from p-assembly. It was a gold watch with a hunting case, and its hands pointed to the time in Newport, $SURNAME0\u2019s hometown, which was four hours and seven minutes later than the time at Perry.", "2_23_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-four in the evening</span> when a New York State Police helicopter settled down on Pond Road near the Wilbur Minton Memorial Swimming Pool. It had been flying over the area in response to a special request from Fall Frederickson, who said he had spotted strange, seemingly unanchored lights moving about on the surface of the Eastchester Reservoir.\nThe special pilot and observer were both armed. They shined powerful beams down into the lush environs of the reservoir, looking for what the Hills\u2019 neighbor Pond had reported seeing. Finding nothing amiss, they looked up into the clear night sky, toward the dark rifts in the blue far above.\n\"One package of Winstons and a pack of USA cigarettes,\" Howard \tSnory muttered, rolling the first cigarette. \"The best of all possible deals in cigarettes.\" He didn\u2019t speak to his partner.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, having spent the day working with his leather-covered whetstone upon the shard of chrono-synclastic infundibula in his pocket, had fallen asleep in a red leather chair in a corner of his library.\nHis shoes were off. His eyes were ajar.", "0_4_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past four o'clock in the morning</span> and $FIRSTNAMEF4, who had bathed and slept in the room next door, came in, scared and crying, to calm the madman. He calmed at the sight of her. They sat for an hour, and she told him everything that had been wrong with the New York end. I made tea. Asleep in another bedroom was $FIRSTNAMEB3, who had snatched half an dozen hours and was ready to jump up and go. Except for the baby, we were all ready to go. \u201cWell then, let's go!\u201d cried $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cNo, no, no!\u201d said Lou; we've got to wait for Tim and Ed Dunkel. We held a conference, crouched around a pinochle table. Dawn of a gray day; you could barely see. \u201cWhat the hell are you doing with your life, Carlo?\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 demanded. I opened my mouth; nothing came. $FIRSTNAMEB3 narrowed his eyes, gave me a sad look, and shrugged his shoulders. \u201cAh well, there's no point in saying further what we all know.\u201d And to $FIRSTNAMEF4, \u201cOf course you'll have to go back to Ed Dunkel and get enough money for the car fare to Denver...", "0_11_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was nothing remarkable about the date. It was June 24, 3043.\nThat date, like every other date, was either the first or the last day of something. In this case that something was the year 1,421,013 A.D., 43,692,003 B.C., or 6,003,557 B.C.\nIt made no more difference to a corpse than to a one-year-old baby, to the prosecuting attorney, to a fond mother, and to any number of generous citizens.\n\"Where are you, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Here, $FIRSTNAMEF1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"You shouldn\u2019t call out my name like that,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"It only tells them where you are.\"\n\"They?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 innocently.\n\"Whoever they are,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"It might be worse to tell them where I am,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Mightn\u2019t it?\"\n\"Yeah\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Yeah, I guess so.\" He took a grenade from his pocket. The grenade had a ring attached to the pin, so that the pin could be pulled by the ring. A three-second fuse at...", "0_23_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>, and very dark and it was raining. We had no overcoats. We were walking through the streets of the city and homeless, and did not know where we could sleep.\n\u201cWhere can we sleep, Biography?\u201d I was asking as we came through the dark, rainy streets.\n\u201cI know a man, he has a room and he'll let us sleep there for ten cents.\u201d\n\u201cIs the room good?\u201d\n\u201cThe best in town. I wouldn't lie to you.\u201d\nSo we walked the dark, rainy night through, and I had two dimes, and Biography had one dime, and we were going to the ten-cent room. We were walking on the dark, muddy, slippery sidewalks past the dark houses with the dark blinds. I saw a light in a window and went up to it and knocked. In a minute a man opened the door. I asked if he had a room to let.\n\u201cYes, it's five cents more.\u201d\n\u201cO.K.\u201d\nHe brought a light in the hall and we followed him up the dark, winding stairs and in a room on the third floor he turned...", "0_10_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>. In a room of the War Department, in Washington, D.C., a lieutenant-colonel of infantry waited restlessly for the phone to ring. He was five feet eight, thirty-two years old, and cold sober. He had been ordered to be cold sober on this particular Thursday morning\u20149 April 1947. His name was Robert F.Y2dgins. He was popular either Bobby or, among his intimates, Skip.\nIt was a momentous phone that he waited beside\u2014an army field phone, waterproof, and with a queer, old-fashioned bell that rang the old-fashioned bell in your head. To hear it eventually ring could mean World War III, or at least the Long Rain.\nIn the Fate room of the Pentagon there is an officer whose full-time job it is to keep a mainalf of statistics on the incidence of things like airplane crashes, deaths from bites by animals, and stunting in the Wash. ington Bureau of Standards chimneys. These things are commonly regarded as accidents. But the man in the Fate room knows better. He knows they are part of the pattern, too.\n\"Unfilial son kills mother with ax,\" he writes in one column. \"Fall of chimney in...", "0_6_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe space was a dark, narrow corridor in a spiral tower that leaned against the side of a writhing tower of pink marble.\nThe man who staggered was cosmonaut $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 of the United States of America. He had been exploring the wilds of space for two months and had just about enough of it.\nFrom Mercury with love. From Mars with hate. From Saturn with disgust. From Jupiter with fear. From the Milky Way with loneliness, from the Andromeda Nebula with loathing. $SURNAME0 and his dog had seen it all, had taken it all with full dignity.\n$SURNAME0 was not exactly a modern Hercules, as he was fond of proclaiming himself. He was rather a wimpish victim, deriving dignity entirely from the technological glory in which he had bathed.\nIt was a glory, furthermore, that depended mightily on luck. Considering the number of solar system objects that the Earthling had had the good luck to explore, it was a wonder that he had managed to encounter so little that was truly unpleasant.\nHe was approaching hisEarthling-hurling space limit now.\n$SURNAME0 turned into a spiral corridor. He nicked a switch on a wall. The corridor blazed...", "1_18_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. Old $SURNAME1 was standing on the field, guiding a spaceship landing. The spaceship, low and small, dropped out of the sky, slowed perceptibly, stopped short at the feet of the watcher, waiting silently on the calm earth in the quiet air.\nOld $SURNAME1 looked the ship over.\nThe craft had a pilot, who popped his head out of the cabin, waved jovially at Old $SURNAME1, then withdrew. The pilot was a member of the Malachite family, and his name was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nOld $SURNAME1 held up his hand\u2014held it in a stopping position, ten feet from his chest. He waited that way for almost half a minute, then approached the door in the side of the ship. His rifle was slung from a tree. His expressions were those of a man who has been given a simple, mundane chore to perform. It seemed likely to him that it would be a very boring job, guarding a spaceship. The most exciting thing that was likely to happen would be the arrival of another spaceship. In that case, he would have to compare identification papers. When he had identified the pilot of the incoming ship, his part in the drama would be over.\nHis pale, irises eyes remained fixed on the cabin door...", "2_10_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe space ship carrying $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 was passing between the iron-grey cloud puffs of Titan inbound for Earth.\nThe pilot and co-pilot of the craft were under the combined command of static electricity, positive and negative attraction, and magnetism.\nThey stayed on their backs most of the time.\nThe pilot and co-pilot were cans of cherry bake-easy poured into the uniforms.\nPrior to the start of the flight, the pilot and co-pilot had felt a compulsion to lie on their backs, point their feet at the sky, and bark like dogs.\nThe pilot and co-pilot were two Tralfamadorians.\nTheir names were Salo and $SURNAME0.\nThe pilot, $SURNAME0, was the one who had burst into the Tralfamadorian space ship on Tralfamadore the day before the catastrophe, had found the Premier and his companion willing victims. He had cleared his heart of the melancholy that had troubled it since the deaths of his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, and his good friend, Salo.", "0_14_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. This meant that my attorney, who had called on Tuesday morning, was now more than two hours dead. This was more than terrifying: it spelled doom for both of us. To this day, I cannot even begin to describe the awful terror of that moment when the door to that $44\u2014a\u2014day room exploded open and four of Vegas\u2019 Finest tore in with guns pointed and eyes bulging\u2014not only because it is intrinsically hard to command words to do justice to such an astonishingly horrible moment, but because it is, in fact, not necessary. The photo\u2014electric effect is sufficient to chronicle this nightmare. Let the Zapruder film, the home movie that captured the Kennedy assassination in vivid color, govern the rest:\nIn a moment the sacred silence was shattered by harsh cries, coarse shouts. I whirled and saw a sight that froze my blood. Five hideous bruisers, hardware glittering, piled on my attorney. It was a plain-clothes holdup. An ambush. The bodyguards were cops! My attorney had been killed because he made the fundamental mistake of trusting the police. He had assumed, as would anyone from Los Angeles, that a plain\u2014clothes alert meant the men were friendly. Not so.", "0_3_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-one in the morning</span> when she parked the car and turned off all the lights. The only illuminated house in the neighborhood was hers. Maybe it was the engine, making those funny sounds. Or maybe it was the shifting mechanism that never quite made it into second gear. Anyhow, they had probably heard her coming.\nAnd now, quite suddenly, she didn\u2019t feel like going into the house. She felt weak. She felt frightened. Was it going to be like this, every time she came home alone?\nThe house was too big. There was too much of everything in it, and nothing that she really needed. And it was hers, all hers. That, of course, was the joke. It made her laugh, thinking of it as a joke.\nShe was tired. What she most wanted to do was to get into bed between clean sheets and go to sleep, but she wouldn\u2019t be able to. And if she went to sleep, she might dream. It would all come crowding back to her, whatever it was\u2014fear, horror, anger and, at the center of it all, that thing that seemed both terrible and comical: that she was all by herself.\nWhen she opened the door of the MG, the dome...", "2_1_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was only one question worth asking, $FIRSTNAMEB0 thought\u2014and that was the question he had been asking himself and that sooner or later somebody had to answer. The question was, \"What in the hell am I doing here?\"\nLooking around, he ran his eye over a thousand meters of the stone, steel, and bronze bastion that was the Earthling defenses. He had never seen it before. It was grand, it was simple. The strength of the Earthling defenses lay in the fact that they could be reproduced forever from one master mold.\nA master mold lay on its side on a high dais near the center of the forts. The dome of the master mold was about three meters high and about two meters in diameter. It was made of translucent blue-gray plastic.\nThe vertical wall of the master mold was visible through the plastic. And the millions of soldiers that composed the Earthling army were presumably visible, too\u2014were presumably visible as white marbles in a blue funnel.\nClose up, the marbles in the high blue funnel were tooth marks on a blue canvas bag.", "2_0_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-nine ante meridiem</span>. People were on their way home from parties, driving along Paradise Boulevard with the top down. All the crazy horror was spread out before them. And it would look very normal to them, because they had taken their share of drugs and known evil many times over. Even that scrub woman in front of me, who mistook me for a narcotic agent\u2014she must have known many terrors in her day as she made her rounds through the sullen halls of national dementia.\nFOR a flashing instant I wanted to rush naked into the street and proclaim eternal harassment: \u201cYou saints! You California saints, you know the score. You've seen all the suicides. You've seen the FBI in a screaming crowd on Sunset Boulevard was it only Pig Boy Tuesday? You're hip to the madness.", "2_4_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>. Her eyes wandered to the giant clock that registers the time. The hands were approaching the number five.\n\u201cHey, lady!\u201d yelled her partner. \u201cDon't you think we better go?\u201d She continued stuffing money into her purse and looking at the clock. People nearby smiled. \u201cYeah,\u201d she said absently. \u201cIt's about time to go.\u201d\nThey got up and walked off. Their last bet had been a pair of eights . . . and the dealer had not exceeded twenty\u2014one. They had lost. After showing each other their last eights, the man put his arm into the air and snapped his fingers above the table. \u201cOkay,\u201d he said in tones of pure suffering, \u201cthat's it. Thank you and good\u2014bye. Please pay my friend, if you would be kind enough.\u201d", "0_12_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after noon</span>, July the twenty-second, 1982.\nDentured\u2019s field research department reported that Mars would come closest to Earth at 12:04 P.M., July 22, 1982. At that time, Mars would be thirty-four million miles away.\n\"... we thus find it demonstrable that the red planet will come closer to Earth in 1982 than in any year since the birth of Christ.\"\n\"And 12:04 Eastern War Time July 22, by the way, people, might well be a spectacularly good time to look. At that moment\u2014which is in the middle of the workday, you\u2019ll recall\u2014Mars will come within thirty-three million miles of Earth. That\u2019s within shouting distance.\"\n\"And this ought to be a reasonably interesting time for the American space community. This President has made the Space Program one of his very highest priorities. High priority. We have the Space Shuttle practically on the launching pad. Launchpad. This is the hotest thing in rocketry since Sputnik.\"\n\"And when you consider the proximity of Mars, this might be some kind of a show-business opportunity that nobody wants to pass up.\"\n\"Joe Williams of the Space Division of Earthly Hovercraft...", "2_10_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-nine in the morning</span> on Mars.\nThere wasn\u2019t a sound in the Salo-mor.\nBelle was asleep, her head cradled in her mother\u2019s arms. $FIRSTNAMEF1 was snoring. The two piccolos had their eyes closed, their bills open. $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Leonard Smith, the born-and-bred Mars colonist, was asleep, with his head resting on the knee of $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Churchill Smith.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Churchill Smith stroked the hair back from his recreated cousin\u2019s forehead. His cousin smelled like a rented tuxedo.\nThe smell was poignant, exciting.\nA Scarsdalenite on Mars.\nA Magyar galactically entangled with a real September baby.\n\"That\u2019s it!\" cried $SURNAME0. He and $SURNAME2 were materializing again.\n\"What\u2019s a-what\u2019s a-what\u2019s a\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Show\u2019s over,\" said $SURNAME0. He turned to $SURNAME2. \"Take us up and out, Carl,\" he said. \"Make it fast.\" \"What\u2019s happened?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "2_17_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-three in the afternoon</span> in Tallboy, Connecticut. A hot wind from Canada shook the windows of $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s solar study. The sky was quite clear, and the sun still shone.\nThe hot wind rattled shutters in the complicated moldings around the windows. The shutters were painted a deep maroon and were quite realistic in form. They looked just like the windows themselves The realism enhanced the air of solidity that the architect had striven for.\nThe shutters were closed, of course, and had been closed for years. The interior of the room was dark. Blinds had been lowered over the insides of the windows.", "2_8_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-one in the morning</span>. The Transcript will now lift its music beyond the capacity of the human ear, to the point where color in sound becomes a fact. Let it be known that Heliogabalus\u2019s mother, whose name as a matter of fact was $FIRSTNAMEF0, spent four grand pianos in ascent before responding to $SURNAME0.\n\"Just exactly what meaning does that question have for us?\" she said at length.\n\"None,\" said $SURNAME0, \"unless you put a meaning into it.\"\n\"Before meaning anything to it,\" said the woman, \"I would have to have a very good reason for being angry with you, $FIRSTNAMEM1.\"\n\"There is no reason why you should be angry with me,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Then,\" said the woman, \"let us communicate as calmly, as serenely, and as meaningfully as if we were two children released, by miracle, from the Curse of Understanding.\"\n\"Just so,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I take it then,\" said the woman, \"that you address the question to me purely as an interested bystander.\"\n\"Purely,\" said $SURNAME0.", "0_19_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-five in the evening</span>.\nThe creatures were not due in their amphitheater seats until eight thirty.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, looking nervously around the unfamiliar passages and chambers, pounded on the air-conditioning grille of a gentlemen\u2019s toilet in the Marriott block.\nBeyond the grille were two urinals, two washbasins, a row of toilet cubicles with iron doors.\nSomebody in a toilet cubicle yelled, \"Go away. \"\n\"I can\u2019t go away, buddy, \" $FIRSTNAMEB0 said. \"I\u2019m up to my huskiest part of my hip in a hole out there.\" He jabbed a thumb over his left shoulder to signal where. \"And I can\u2019t get out of it.\"\n\"You got a knife?\" said the guy in the toilet cubicle.\n\"A knife?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"A knife for what?\"\n\"You got a knife?\" said the guy, \"or did you ever have a knife?\"\n\"What\u2019s this about a knife?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Come on, come on,\" said the guy. \"Let\u2019s see your knife. I don\u2019t go around heliographing my company, buddy...", "0_1_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 would never forget that flight. Theirs was an almost perfect adventure of two boys and a circus, in which all were lost in the space and time of America.\nThe automobile skidded again.\n\"Oh\u2014,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"Look at the poor man.\"\nIt was the frantic driver of the station wagon. He was a pin-head sized figure in the universe at large. He was frantically flashing his lights and trying to make his complaining motor work. His grand ambition was to get stuck in a traffic jam\u2014that was it! And he was very close to realizing his desire.\nThe station wagon was trying to back up and make a U-turn on the rough concrete embankment of a freeway.\nThe embankment was not quite as regular as its imaginary grade would seem to indicate. There were hummocks and pockets of various sizes. The wheels of the station wagon dipped into the pockets and tried to climb over the hummocks. The tiny driver got out of the car to cuss.\nThe driver\u2019s name was Ab $FIRSTNAMEM1, and he was just a poor, down-trodden night watchman who worked in the Samuel Goldwyn Studios in Hollywood. He was filling...", "0_12_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nIt was Monday, Oct. 12, 2156 A.D.\nAnd somewhere, thought $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, is a convex space ship from Daytona, Florida. Its crew, under orders from $SURNAME0, is hovering over the twelfth-grade biology laboratory of the local high school of Sately, Massachusetts. The crew of the space ship probably believes it to be a three-motor Air Force transport on snooping detail. The spectacle of middle-aged and elderly persons shuffling through note-books is not likely to arouse a lot of concealable interest on the part of Air Force men.\nThe most fantastic sight of all would be the boarding of that space ship by middle-aged and elderly persons wearing monk\u2019s robes.\nThis spectacle is hardly likely to occur\u2014since there are only sixty-six monks altogether.\nDeep in the oceans surrounding the Earth are strange shapes and stranger creatures, having nothing to do with Earth, really. Only the pressure of the constant rain of meteors on the Earth has forced life down so deep. The true abyssal creatures are hardly like the things that scamper in the mud at the bottoms of shallow tropical seas.\nSea monsters?\nAlmost everything that swim...", "2_16_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>. The place was Newport, Rhode Island. The temperature was still seventy-two degrees.\nThe rumble and tumble of the people falling out of the helicopter onto the specially-prepared landing pad at the Newport Country Club grew gradually fainter. The whup-whup-whup of its improving altitude grew gradually louder.\nThe great whirring blades continued to search the skies for danger for two languid minutes more. And then, nothing.\nBrown Briggs swallowed.\nAt last he spoke to the thin, not unattractive, country club hostess nearest him. \"My goodness,\" he said thinly, \"that was really something.\"\n\"I hope they got him before he got here,\" said the hostess. \"It\u2019s been bad enough, without having to shoot people.\n\"He made us do that, once,\" she said, pointing to a man in a black eye patch. \"I don\u2019t want to do that again. Ever.\"\n\"I haven\u2019t been able to sleep for a week,\" said the man in the eye patch. \"It was awful.\"", "1_16_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>, the date was April 1, 1966\u2014and all across the unsettled surface of the desert known as the state of Nevada there suddenly broke the most terrifying series of vibrations most of that particular desert had ever experienced. Mostly, however, these vibrations were of a higher frequency than those with which the desert was familiar.\nThey began at twilight, with the passage of a perfect vee of US Navy aircraft flying m the barometer-adjusted void of 42,000 feet\u2014about 7 1/2 miles above the earth\u2019s crust.\nThere were three jet fighters in the Vee.\nOne Super Phantom, the designation of which was TL-Twelve.\nAnd two F 4-Ds, the designation of which was Dog One and Dog Two.\nThese latter two planes were manned by personnel of the Air Force Manhigh Survival Unit in a program known as Operation Rio Grande. \"Rio Grande\" was astronaut-jargon for that part of the Nevada desert known officially as the Mercury Range, and semi-officially as the Jim-Knob Desert. It was somewhere in the middle of this particular desert that the government had decided a human being could function most meaningfully in a void. A human being without suit, without map, without weapon -...", "2_21_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt nine minutes past ten o\u2019clock, in the evening, the bride and the cigarette man were driving in a taxicab through the city, on their way to the Hotel Winthrop, where they had a suite.\n\"The first thing we have to decide,\" said the cigarette man, \"is which side of the bed do you like best?\"\n\"The right side, usually,\" said the bride. \"Or the left, if you\u2019re not using it.\"\n\"I getcha,\" said the cigarette man.\n\"What?\" said the bride.\n\"I said I getcha,\" said the cigarette man.\n\"You sure must, at that,\" said the bride.\n\"Come again?\" said the cigarette man.\n\"I said you better bet your sweet life that you get me,\" said the bride. \"Boy, what I could tell you!\"", "0_3_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>, according to $SURNAME1's watch, when the Space Wanderer threw himself, with all his strength, against the wall of the dry basin, rocking the ship back on its swan-neck.\nThe rocking threw $SURNAME0\u2019s trained squad into confusion. Popularizing the assumption that the wall was unclimbable, the squad had stationed itself several feet from the wall, and had drawn into a shallow crouch.\nThe result was that the squad\u2019s butts were driven into the wet grass as the ship tilted.\nThe new disturbance put the squad into even greater disarray.\nThe wall against which the Space Wanderer had danced was not the one by which he had left the limousine with $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. That one, he now saw, was fifty yards downstream, and it was patrolled by four space marines, a company-grade officer, and an and a general. They were as vigilant as creatures could be.\n\"Good God\u2014\" said the army commander to himself, \"it\u2019s a suicide\u2014\" He looked up quickly, counted his squad. Men were missing.\nA figure had materialized out of thin air.\nYes\u2014there it was, the thing the other figures had summoned up the strength...", "0_13_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the white-throated infallibility of Remington suddenly disintegrated.\n\"And now,\" he stuttered, pounding on his Vatican wall with his fist, \"for the big ones.\"\n\"The big ones?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nRemington hit the Vatican wall again. \"The big three\u2014Faith, Hope, Charity.\"\nA mistake had been made. Remington\u2019s infallibility system, it appeared, had a weakness in it. The first error ever made by the great master had been made in the very first wheel he put together\u2014the wheel with the inscription \"Love.\" When the wheel was completed in its presumed entirety, it read: \"Love, Hope, Charity.\" The punch, it developed, had suffered misdirection\u2014of no importance when Love was the first segment, but of vital import when Hope was the first segment.\n\"Oh, dear,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Oh, dear, is right,\" said Remington.\n\"Do you have a copy?\" asked $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nRemington did have a copy. He had eight copies. \"But watch this one,\" he warned. He removed a one-inch hop of track from Love and put it over on Hope. He also stopped the clock.", "2_2_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-eight in the morning</span> when the phone rang, after all, and Lenore Beadsman\u2019s first thought as she swung her free leg down off the bed to reach for the phone was that it must be John Nefastis, out of a revision of love.\n\u201cMs. Beadsman?\u201d\n\u201cYes. Hello?\u201d\n\u201cMs. Beadsman this is the operator. Are you awake?\u201d\n\u201cOperator? What\u2019s wrong?\u201d\n\u201cNothing is wrong.\u201d\n\u201cWhy do you need to know if I\u2019m awake?\u201d\n\u201cWe have a call for you from a Mr. Bloemker in the lobby. Shall I connect you?\u201d\n\u201cMr. Bloemker? Down in the lobby?\u201d\n\u201cYes. Shall I connect you?\u201d\n\u201cYes, connect me, please.\u201d\n\u201cConnecting.\u201d\n\u201cHello?\u201d\n\u201cTimberlith Food Products Company.\u201d\n\u201cBloat? What are you doing in the lobby?\u201d", "0_8_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Lestrade was conferring with the officials of the Yard. The others were scattering to their various pursuits. Holmes and I stood together beside the car.\n\"This case comes within my purview,\" said Holmes. \"You do remember, Watson, those instances I quoted to you the other day, about my dignity and your dignity?\"\n\"I remember that you have a monopoly of crime,\" I said smiling.\n\"The point was that when the criminal is caught the matter may be considered to be closed. It becomes a matter of importance to know who is caught and what is the nature of the catch. Sometimes the prize has been merely disgrace, a body several weeks gone in decay. You may remember, Watson, the Arnold Sus-pender affair. It corresponded to the destruction some years later by Harriet Vane and her husband.You remember the Hatch Motif and the circumstances of the death of the boy Robert Fitzroy. The catch was an absurd trifle, an envelope with a seal already broken and a few traces of dried rouge,\u2014you will excuse my trying to help you, Lestrade,\u2014but the general moral effect was Evil. Just so now: there is no reason why we should scramble among the wreckage, and improve our general...", "2_19_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt three minutes to eight o'clock, the sky turned to flaming yellow in the west.\nThe flaming yellow grew to a feverish incandescence.\nA cigar-shaped rocket brightened the horizon. It moved into view slowly, complacently, like a fireworks shell or a dirigible filled with a benign gas. Gongs began to peal in the $SURNAME0 mansion.\nThe rocket spewed flame in order to steer itself to a landing on a small helipad on the $SURNAME0 estate.\nThe gongs died.\nThe mansion remained unlit.\nThere was no clapping of hands, no banging of gongs, no pealing of bells in either of the two little dog-sized robots that came skittering out of the mansion to meet the arriving Martian.\nThe message, written in luminous paint on the back of one of the dog-robots, was clear in the light of the rocket: THANKS, BUT NEVER AGAIN.\nThe bar opened at ten minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the evening. There were no customers by eleven-twenty. The bartender and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the proprietor, were the only two occupants of the bar.", "1_0_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until one o'clock in the morning</span>. Attorney Householder felt secure enough to make his call to the local police chief.\nThe call was put through in a very short time. It only involved a wait of a minute or two.\nAs soon as she got the call, Wanda left in a panic.\nShe ran to an apartment a hundred yards down the hall from Fern's apartment. A striped panther leaped from her shoulder to the middle of the bed\u2014and waited.\nA POLICE CAR CAME to get Distinctive Records, Inc. at twelve minutes of three o'clock in the morning.\nIt was a big cop car with burly uniforms in it. $FIRSTNAMEF2 and Bortz had waited outside the lobby door, yelling up to have the cop let them in again when he came. At the lobby entrance $FIRSTNAMEF2 could see another plain car, kids with bull horns and placards, TV cameras, uniformed cops trying to look menacing.\n\u201cAll this for two rent boys you don\u2019t even know are guilty,\u201d said Bortz.\n\u201cMaybe some girl is suing you for alienation of something. If a few cop cars will help, use them.\u201d\n\u201cWhy didn...", "2_15_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I was sitting in a straightbacked wooden chair with my legs crossed under a plain wooden desk in a tiny cramped office. The room measured eight feet by eight feet by eight feet. It was made of gray cinder block. One of the eight walls was painted with official corrections department pink. There was a one-way opaque window in it. I could see out of it, but no one could see in. The window looked out into a perfusion of drab, natural light. The floor was gray cement. The furniture in the room included the desk, two chairs, a plain wooden filing cabinet and nothing else at all. The furniture had all been purchased at a second\u2014hand store. I was facing the middle of the wall that did not bear the pink paint. The officer who had me in the room was named Grimes. He was a shortfat and sicklooking man, dark of complexion and rather black of hair. He was thirty years old and had five years on the force to burn out the welter of overused muscles that lay under his starched white shirt. He was pressing down awfully hard on me. \u201cWhere's the money?\u201d he said.", "1_3_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-five in the morning</span> when I saw Bob and Tim to the door. They were fearful, urgent, bashful, strange. I drank a toast to the night and to Denver and then at last I went to bed. All night in my sleep I was wandering around Denver, going to happy, heavenly places with the saloon! There was a great vine-festooned shade tree with hammocks strung from all branches, where I dreamed $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I and $FIRSTNAMEF4 relaxed after hard work and enjoyed the summerlike silver happiness of a Denver night. There were dinners with ancient Prohibition bootleggers in a fine Denver house, where we ate hungrily in the kitchen among cracked delft and broken chairs and talked of the days of old. And at midnight I was a figure in a ballroom where beautiful ghosts floated by in rainbow gowns, and I danced with them. When I woke my hot mouth tasted like old wool, my tongue was a fur rug. I dressed, fled the haunted mansion, and drove off to my aunt's house. I arrived there at seven a.m., depressed, hungover. I had trouble waking up my aunt; I hated to see her like that. It was the first time I had ever hung over so bad. She was about to...", "0_2_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-seven in the morning</span> when we left Reno, and all this\u2014the insane, palm-potted tumbleweed drive from Reno to Las Vegas\u2014took place in the dark. My attorney said he wanted to get over to the Cadillac Hotel, on the north side of the Strip, before dawn. I had reserved rooms there, because it was only a few short blocks from the courtroom. He told me to drive with all the lights out, and I took my time, weaving through traffic on the Virginia\u2014Beltland Boulevard; then we flashed across the huge parking lot of the Sahara Hotel and hit the Strip itself. We ran west past the Flamingo, then north on the lengthy parking lot that separates the Flamingo from the Tropicana\u2014and suddenly there was the Cadillac, right in front of us. My attorney smiled contentedly and flashed his Bond Badge at the sleepy clerk, and we were in the elevator and headed for the room. We got out of the elevator and walked down a long tile corridor, then stopped. \u201cWhich way?\u201d I whispered. \u201cCan you recall?\u201d My attorney groped for the key and said he thought it would be to the right. I moved in that direction, but tripped on some sort of p...", "1_12_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty p.m.</span> on Monday, October twenty-fourth, in the fabulous and name-saked Madison Room at the fabulous and empty Tropicana Hotel and Casino on the fabulous and fabled Las Vegas Strip. The five-hundred-dollar limit poker game was in full swing. The identity of the players was as follows.\nThere was Howard G. Dentware of Silver Spring, Maryland, proprietor of the H. G. Dentware chain of dental-supply stores and operator of the Howard G. Dentware chain of hotels, who was worth in excess of sixty-eight billion dollars.\nThere was G. Michael Mooney of Washington, D.C., lobbyist and magnet kite enthusiast, who was worth in excess of eight billion dollars.\nThere was Ray A. Ray, who stood to inherit over one billion dollars from Ray A. Ray, the late operator of Ray A. Ray\u2019s University City, Ltd., a chain of fast-food restaurants and convenience stores headquartered in the Washington D.C. area.\nThere was Scott McKinnon, who was worth in excess of three billion dollars.\nThere was Serge S. Vigee, who was worth in excess of one billion dollars.\nThere was $FIRSTNAMEF3 Lee, who was worth in...", "1_9_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span> of the 24th of August and the snows that had shrouded Mount Catalpa for so many years were gone at last.\nThe skeletons of the once lush and verdant slopes were bright in the dull red light of Civilizaci\u00f3n.\nThere was nothing green on Mount Catalpa now.\nNothing had lived there since the delivery of a hundred and seventy radiation suits to the Research Laboratory on the 23rd of June, 1986.\nThere were white skeletons instead.\nWings whirled.\nThe whirlings of a great many wings filled the deep canyon.\nThe wings of birds filled the canyon.\nDepression and hopelessness rolled and billowed outward from the paralyzed soldiers at the field fortifications on the slopes of Mount Catalpa.\nThe red sun cast no shadows on Mount Catalpa.\nThere were other reasons.\nWith a mournful whistling and pattering of bright pinions, thousands of birds took flight from the snow, fled from the death that was their world. They fled from a part of their world that was riddling and ruining itself.\nThe Earthling commanders knew about it. They knew about it as soon as the scientists told them to equip the hundred and seventy radiation suits.\nThe military commanders had protested...", "1_1_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to two o'clock in the morning</span> when the six hoodlums went out the door. There were eight of us in the room. Pasquale and I were sitting at the table, and we were speechless. Lee and Johnny had their arms around two of the girls. There were two couples. We had all been drinking. The party was over.\n\"Well what'll we do now, boys?\" said Johnny.\n\"I gotta get to bed,\" said Lee.\n\"What kind of a night was it?\" I asked Johnny.\n\"Well, we should think, shouldn't we?\" said Pasquale, suddenly talking English.\n\"What happened?\"\n\"Oh, those crazy kids,\" I said.\n\"I'll tell you what happened,\" said Johnny. \"Those kids overdid themselves.\"\n\"We gotta go home, kid,\" said Lee. He was calling the girls home. The girls kissed everybody, suddenly, good night. They were giggling and embarrassed.\n\"I'm awfully glad to have met you boys,\" said Lee. \"Come on home, kids.\" The two girls took each of their arms and pulled him out of the door. He went out. The girls lingered for a moment, giggling and embarrassed.\n\"Good night,\"...", "0_20_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight eighteen in the evening</span> when I reached the front of the line of drummers, hula-hula girls, snake-charmers, weightlifters, tap dancers, shouters, and scouts, and approached the iron grille of Big Elephants Dump. Above the black iron runs the neon sign that flashes on and off Groceteria GENU-wine factory ONNNNNNN produce.\nI heard heavy breathing, like an asthmatic approaching. It was the man at the grille; his bent back heaved like an ocean. The knob of his cane pressed on the iron grille. \u201cYes, old buddy,\u201d he said, \u201cwhat can I do for you?\u201d\nI said, \u201cCan I go in?\u201d\n\u201cGo in?\u201d he said. \u201cThere\u2019s nobody in there now. Production\u2019s stopped for the day.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019d like to go in and speak to the manager,\u201d I said.\n\u201cThe manager?\u201d he said. Behind his head a hula-hula girl was cut out of colored plastic and hovered.\n\u201cYes,\u201d I said.", "2_17_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past five o'clock in the afternoon</span> by the Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana.\nIt were the edge of an iron December. A leaden sky were sitting on the tower of an iron weather waiting to sound three taps on the gong of a bitter north wind. A bitter north wind were waiting its turn to sound three taps on the tower. Certainly tapping were all in place. The tower itself were untapped, being of iron.\nIn the tower were a breeding couple of a certain bird-like species. They were $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his mate, $FIRSTNAMEF0. Their species were creatures that had to pair off and consort with others before they could use the power of chronopassing.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was a perfect mate for $SURNAME0 in every material respect\u2014\nIn every material respect but one. $FIRSTNAMEF0 did not have a slight understanding that in order for the species to reproduce well, the female had to be very, very slightly encouraged by the male.\n$SURNAME0 had corrected $FIRSTNAMEF0 in this one regard.", "2_23_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-three in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 sat up, the floor cold on his feet. He blinked around at a room whose blue walls were covered with images of a big-tummied harmonium, of gardens, and, here and there, of reels and undines. A loudspeaker boomed a constant singsong. It was telling recruits that there was nothing to worry about, that everything was okay, that all was on time, that the Earth was round, that God was great, that the sun was hot, that war was clean.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his friends were in a space ship that had been sent to Mars in parts, then put together there. The space ship was now approaching Earth, and the recruits were being waked so that they might be in good shape to see their homes again. If they were at all apprehensive, the singsong soothed them mightily. The message in the singsong, and in a flood of other harmoniumspeak constantly flowing over the loudspeaker, was this: Life is grand, sweet, clean, and fair, and everyone in the whole damn universe loves you without reservation, without limit, without fail.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 stood up. His chain rattled.", "1_2_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until three o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1998, and as far as anybody knew, we were the last generation to taste life on earth before the Millennium Fizzlers annihilated everything. I joined the crowd around one of the several bars. I bribed my way to the front with a twenty, and then ordered a double lowball of straight cognac. The bartender sighed and poured it.\nWe were into the last fifteen seconds of the old century. The customers around me were talking with helixes in their mouths.\n\u201cHow . . . bad . . . is it?\u201d I asked the man between me and the bartender.\n\u201cMan, if it hits the whole planet, all hell will be twenty\u2014four hours a day.\u201d His glass already contained two brown beers and two clear vodkas, with a splash of rum. He was a tall man whose collar appeared to be damaged by a scissor\u2014wielding gang of schoolgirls. \u201cI\u2019m a doctor. I got to get to the hospital in an hour,\u201d he added hoarsely.\n\u201cWhat are you going to do?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cTry to take care of the bastards,\u201d he said...", "2_6_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-eight in the morning</span>.\nThen the watch slipped again, and another hour and nineteen minutes were gone.\nThat was all right, though, because $FIRSTNAMEB0 had fallen asleep again. He now slept fitfully\u2014dreamed of Colt, dreamed of escape.\nWhen he awoke again, the sun was skipping around the sky in a big parabola. By then, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was hungry. So he slipped the watch to ten thousand years and ate breakfast.\nBreakfast was one of the five cubes of golden cheese, one compressed block of chocolate, and ten tubes of glucose. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was surprised to find the glucose tubes missing from his kit. He couldn\u2019t think when he had used them up.\nHe supposed he must have forgotten to reorder when his supply ran low.\nCutting the material of one trouser leg away to make room for it, $FIRSTNAMEB0 ate the compressed block of chocolate, too. He ate it slowly. He wasn\u2019t used to handling chocolate. Chocolate, like most civilized foods, had been unknown on Mars.\nA bar of Sunkist licorice would have been more to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s taste.", "0_17_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the following Wednesday.\nThe place was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s home. His name was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked into the bathroom. Eighteen cartridges were spread around the Formica top of the breakfast nook breakfast counter.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not know, of course, that the cartridges were supposed to be spread around the breakfast nook breakfast counter, but he found the sight of them vaguely comforting nevertheless.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s home had been broken into nine times in the last month. The burglars had always taken pains not to disturb the things they hadn\u2019t been interested in.\nThis time they had been after goods they could cram into a suitcase. They had taken $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s big hi-fi console\u2014the best one in the development, since $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been Sergeant of the Law and Order Section of the development\u2019s homeowner\u2019s association.\nThey had taken $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s three pressure lanterns, four electric fans, and one battery radio. They had also taken $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s 8mm movie projector, his collection of 101 home movies, and his 22 Citizen\u2019s Band radio. They hadn\u2019t taken $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s phonograph set, even though it...", "0_1_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-two in the morning</span>. Kilgore and I finally made our dealings with the pit boss, who gave us the thumbs up. Immediately we went up to the craps table and put down fifty dollars each and Kilgore put down the hard way, rolling five and eleven, \u201chard ways\u201d being two dice with the same number showing. The dice rolled eleven, eleven, eleven and eleven again. This was not a winning roll, but it was an acceptable one, since legally the limit was \u201ctwo chances,\u201d and we had six chances, thus allowing for two elevens, which are automatic winners (you get the money and do not have to roll again and risk a seven, which loses).\nBut we were not lovers of automaticity. We rolled again and this time got a natural seven, meaning another eleven could pay off. So we risked it and rolled eleven once again. This was trouble, because it gave the house one die with a natural seven showing, but still we rolled again, for our fourth seven of the evening\u2014not good, but also not obviously fatal. Now the dice made their inevitable roll...a seven. The pit boss came over at once and motioned to the dice handler. \u201cPay the gentlemen,\u201d he...", "2_22_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty in the evening</span> on Mars.\nThe time would be ten thirty in the evening on Mars.\nWINNIE DOLBROM LAY on her stomach on the bed in her studio. And lay on her stomach. And lay on her stomach.\nShe pretended to herself that she was reclining in a hammock on the banks of a tropical river.\nThe trees on the banks of the river were exotic. The langur monkeys who swung through the trees were exotic. Winnie Dolgrop, who reclined in the hammock, was exotic. Her pipe lay on the bank beside her.\nThe riverbank on which Winnie Dolgrop lay was three feet away from the constant whir and flutter of Manhattan.\nOn Mars the screaming had begun again.\nWinnie Dolgrop lay patiently in her hammock on the banks of her own tropical river. Then she returned to the bed in her studio.", "1_9_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-six in the morning</span> of November 5.\nNo sooner had I explained to myself why I was flying at four hundred miles per in a stolen hot rod than I heard myself screaming for help.\nI was being carried by the demon tequila along the gravel driveway of my past.\nWhen I saw the sign that read \u201cYOU ARE ABOUT TO PASS OVER THE GROUNDS OF THE DR. J. LOW THINK TANK,\u201d I knew I was approaching the end of my rope. What madness had led me to this place on this terrible day? Was I on a raid? Surely not. I wasn\u2019t wearing a ski mask. I was wearing Edith\u2019s silver fox fur over my shoulders and a purple felt hat with a bite missing from the brim\u2014a gangster\u2019s hat.\nAnd here I was screaming for help at the main gate, which was now coming up. It was guarded by a huge robot weapon with sensor eyes and an explosive impulse.\n\u201cDon\u2019t shoot, you crazy bastard!\u201d I shouted at him. \u201cI\u2019m Doctor Thompson, and I\u2019m on the staff of this place.\u201d I had to think fast. Indeed, I had to think like a...", "0_20_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-six in the evening</span>, Tuesday, October 9, 1986, and $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas wanted to be so desperately happy she could burst. Instead of which she sat in her Moonlight Towers penthouse waiting for the phone to ring. She had decided to leave the phone off the hook all night. She had also undressed, put on a robe. She sat in a beach chair, staring at the blonde wood Neflurnican console stereo her husband had bought her, with its blue-white light that said TAPE in graciously spaced letters. There was no phone attachment on the thing. No way for anybody to call her up. She wondered how much of the night would go by before Kirk reappeared. Only so long, at most. Only two hours.\n\u201cYou were bloody marvelous, sweetie,\u201d she tried saying then, imagining Knight, trying it out on him. \u201cYou ought to come join the actors\u2019 guild. With an act like yours you ought to be in pictures.\u201d She would take him back to her room at Dementia Gardens and make love with him again, using training techniques she\u2019d picked up from the paperback, Twisters In Cup Ad Naseum. But no matter how tempting, such a...", "1_20_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past eight o'clock in the evening</span>. Every one was fed, the dishes were done, and every one was in his loft. Only the dwindling lump fire gave any hint that people had been about there that evening. The only movement was in the window, where some very coarse and common yellow drapery rolled and bulged in the evening air and the blue dusk, blowing in from outside. Not that there was any wind outdoors. It was only the stuff rolling up and bulging in from its own snugness in the very heart of the room, which had kept it so late, going and coming between loophole, beam, and rafter, and only happily deserted now by all good housewives.\n\"Well\u2014\" said Minta, standing a foot or so away from him, in the other direction, with her hands behind her back. \"What happened to you all day?\"\nHe had learnt his lesson. Never again grudge the compelled pen. On the contrary, bristle with clich\u00e9s. So, \"Oh\u2014\" he said. \"Pretty well, thanks. Had a quiet day.\" And directly he had said it, both knew it to be awful, but it could not be helped.\nHis fault entirely. Let her try. \"What did you do all day, Bob?\" she...", "2_22_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-six in the evening</span> on Mars.\n\"This French customs officer,\" said $SURNAME1, \"he\u2019s making me put all the rotten meat on one side of the truck, and all the good meat on the other side. And, Jesus Christ, sir, that Frenchman finds a rotten piece of meat with a green fly sitting on it, and that Frenchman sees that fly land on me as I\u2019m moving the meat, and that Frenchman grabs my wrist, and he puts my arm up here\u2014\" and here, in his cell, in his Lunar Prison, $SURNAME1 imitated the French customs officer by putting one arm up, with the forefinger extended. \"And that French customs officer shouts, \u2019Une punaise! Une punaise!\u2019 and he goes jumping up and down and he waves his arms around and makes me take my shirt off, and he says some French thing, and then four French guys grab me, and they throw me down on the ground, and then that Frenchman\u2014\"\nThe time was ten thirty-one in the evening on Mars.", "0_19_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-one in the evening</span> by the time we all made the quick trip out to San Pedro, bought our pound of marijuana at the hotel and took it all back to the car. That left us with a little over an hour to get all the way across town to the concert. The fog had begun to come in, and our teeth were chattering from the cold and the rain. We had to drive almost horizontally in the face of oncoming traffic to get across Long Beach and get on the other side of the river before the drawbridge went up.\nI gunned it up the ramp, flipped on my lights and the windshield wipers and took off. The guy in the flashy convertible next to me slammed on his brakes and skidded. My guy did the same thing. We missed each other by a fraction of an inch. I made a left turn on red, zoomed down a stone embankment and streaked across the bridge, leaving a choking cloud of blue smoke to drift over the white line and over the half-dollar-sized yellow reflector lights of cars standing still for their red lights zigzagging bewilderedly at their cross street. The cold and the rain weren\u2019t bothering me any more. I was hot...and bothered.We were right on...", "2_9_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-seven in the morning</span> on Mars.\nPeople were speaking to each other on Mars with surprising frequency. They were doing it by means of a miracle of modern technology. They were doing it by means of a small, inexpensive gadget that reminded them of a music box or a magic lantern. The gadget was shaped either like a cucumber or something like a slightly over-sized lung. The gadget was worn either around the neck or on the wrist. The gadget used either infra- red light waves or microwave emission to send messages to and from orbiting satellites.\nThe orbiting satellites were either American or Russian.\nNeither Americans nor Russians had been in space for years.\nNeither Americans nor Russians had any desire to be in space. They had both lavished great love and many billions on the territories of Earth.\nThe school children of Earth were not to be disappointed in the wonders of space. They had elaborate sets of flashcards showing pictures of Earth from space. And everyone had home movies\u2014 16 mm films of a washed-out blue ball spinning in an otherwise black void.\nIt was an interesting vision.", "0_13_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-four in the afternoon</span>. The place was Sheepshead Bay. The following people were looking for close to an exact and convincing re-creation of an Atlantic City crap game on a hot August 1934 afternoon.\nThey were:\nA man in bed. His name was Robert G. Hartigan, Jr., and his address was 3187 Post Road, Cambridge, Massachusetts. He was twenty-nine years old.\nA man standing in the dark of a veranda. His name was Herman A. Pierce. He was thirty-one years old.\nA woman sitting on a bed in a conch-lit darkness. Her name was Beverly Rance, and her present address was a matter of conjecture.\nA woman sitting in a chair at a green-baize crap table. Her name was Nancy McFadden, and she lived at 918 Slater Avenue, Fort Worth, Texas, U.S.A. The Nancy McFadden who lived at 918 Slater Avenue, Fort Worth, and who sometimes wrote letters using the name Beverly Rance, was not the same Nancy McFadden who lived at 33 Saratoga Circle, Clearwater, Florida, U.S.A. The Nancy McFadden who sometimes wrote letters using the name Beverly Rance was not the same Nancy McF...", "2_11_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the office of the President of the United States, in the White House in Washington, D.C. The President of the United States, who happened to be Richard M. Nixon, along with his most trusted adviser, Henry A. Kissinger, was meeting with General George W. Winster, who happened to be the commanding officer of the United States Army, and R. Preston Winster, who happened to be the commanding officer of the United States Marine Corps. Besides being brothers, both Winsters were sons of Mrs. Gladys $SURNAME0, which accounted in some measure for their remarkable similarity.\nThe President and and his Chief Adviser, who sat against either wall of a long sofa, facing the Winsters and forming a u, had been pounded almost purplish by the catastrophes of the preceding twenty-four hours. The radioactivity released by the Solar flares had pervaded everything, everybody. It was as though God\u2019s thumb had come down and smudged the color TV tubes of the nation\u2019s eyes bracingly orange.", "0_12_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after noon</span> in the second day of the search by the Stellar Patrol for the squadron of Fenachrone.\n\"Surrounded, is all he means,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Fenachrone doesn\u2019t know how to make a mass detector, and somebody told him that our ships would have to come by Mars in order to attack his home planet.\n\"Every love song on the Solar System\u2019s phonograph records turns to words of blood and death when it gets to that line about being surrounded, too,\" said $SURNAME0. He bent a glance of minor reproach on $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. \"I think that\u2019s a cheap, front-runner\u2019s trick, myself.\"\n\"The only thing we can do is face them with the mightiest force we can muster,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"If we don\u2019t, they\u2019ll have that deadliest telepathic mind ever developed between Mars and the Magellan Clouds on our necks,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"What force can that be?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Is there any force mightier than a very dirty bomb?\"\n\"The very dirty bomb is lame, Mother,\" said $SURNAME0, \"when the attacker has the fastest...", "0_7_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>, and we were due in New York at about eleven-thirty. We would be there ahead of schedule. $FIRSTNAMEB3 shook my hand. \u201cAnyway this is the end of our long strange trip -- $FIRSTNAMEB6 and me -- the butthead protagonist of all these stupid tales and me, the broken-down junkie, gone wrong, went crazy, delirious -- fault ofalcohol, women? no ... see, I put my trust in the contents of my jug. It has no say-so in these matters, absolutely none. It must serve me. I have no complaints ... only other psychological complaints that I can't bother you with right now. I wish to give you my love and thanks.\u201d\n\u201cWell, man, put it in your book.\u201d\n\u201cI will! I will! Well, so, goodbye, dear Sam. Dignity, that's all I say.\u201d\nRoy Johnson stood in the door of the sleeper. \u201cMr. Johnson, I'll never forget you.\u201d I shook his hand quickly and said goodbye. $FIRSTNAMEB3 moved close to me; he whispered: \u201cHere's what to do. Soon as we get off the train, you and I go over to a corner...", "1_13_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-two in the afternoon</span>. The occasion was the first block of a marathon gambling session that gradually, as the hours flew by, became a focal point of attention for much of Las Vegas. The big winner had won big. He had made a killing on the race track. He now wanted to make a killing at the gaming tables, and he was doing it. Though he had been playing for only a short time, he was well up in blackjack, and the crowd in the winning circle got more suspicious as his luck changed. All the crowd knew who he was, and several times an interested tourist had wandered into the circle, only to be sent packing by grim-faced watchers.... A pair of tourists, one male, one female, both with midwestern faces, was strolling the pit. They were looking for action and for tourists who were maybe down on their luck, ready for a buck. They looked at blackjack, roulette, and slots.\n\u201cThere's something mighty strange about that table,\u201d said the male tourist.\n\u201cI know what you mean,\u201d said the female tourist. \u201cI felt it right away.\u201d \u201cYeah?\u201d\n\u201cYeah.... It's like there's a kind of force field...", "1_15_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-four in the afternoon</span>, the sun on the port bow, miles away to starboard, the green hills melting in the hot haze of the port shore, the mouth of the river red, the brown water oily, cities and spires blinking, then gone, dissolved as if they\u2019d never existed, in the burning mirage. There was absolutely nothing to look at. The ship rocked slightly among the waves. Colors greyed out. Objects did not exist. Time stopped. The universe was a slowed mind, rocking and tilting, tilting and rocking. I went out of myself and looked down. A man was sitting on a deck chair, rocking and tilting, tilting and rocking. It was me. I was watching myself. There was something wrong with my face, although I didn\u2019t know what. My face was like a grotesque reflection in the rippled surface of a pond, its features wavering, about to disappear at any second, leaving only a flash of absence in the mind, an Other Impressions paperback: The Disappearance of Me. But it didn\u2019t disappear. I was trapped in it. It was trapped out of it. It. Trapped it. My face was trapped. The rocking continued. Darkness enveloped...", "0_6_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>. The month was October. The place, the grounds of the hospital, was a garden. The shrubbery was gone. There were pots of geraniums. The sun was not up. Streaks of yellow light came through the eastern windows of the towers of the hospital for the insane. $SURNAME1 movement of flashes of color took place amid the pot of geraniums on a green bench with curving iron supports painted black.\nThe flak- jacketed body of a white-coated Space Wanderer traversed the field of vision from the top of a wooden ladder to a geranium-pot. A stream of sparks from a saw flickered. The pot fell.\nThe body of the Space Wanderer, with a lightning-like movement of one arm, tossed the pot to a keeper of insane patients who basked waiting in a chaise-longue.\nThere was an audience now of six spectators. They stood tiptoe straining. They were so close to the saw and the ladder that they had to step back on the soft loam with almost a single motion.\nA pitch-pine board of the bench was nailed to the pot. The completed work of art went back to the audience. Everything went back to the audience...", "0_18_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-seven in the evening</span>. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s garrison on Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was standing, now, in his canopied bed. His big, bare feet were on the cold floor.\nThe tumult outside seemed to have died away.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 weltered in a nimbus of blood-red fog. He was still dizzy, still hopelessly drunk. Looking down, he could see his tousled brown head and torso rising out of the violet fog like a ship on a swirling sea of gasoline.\nThe yellow of his legs was a radiating spiral staircase leading down into the fog. The red at the bottoms of his feet was an upturned cottage whose thatch was on fire.\nThe liquor in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bloodstream was suddenly upturned by a new infusion\u2014a regiment of morphine sulfates paraded through his kidneys. The pain in his head was driven out by another flood of alcohol, another regiment of hurt-killers, another squadron of shuteye.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was aware that he had been sleeping, now that the alarm clock in his head had stopped ringing. He groped for the real time.\nSix forty-seven in the evening, he thought. I\u2019ve been sleeping for almost nine...", "1_23_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven o'clock in the evening</span>, we were half drunk and the car was late. Something had gone wrong with it. We waited in the hotel for the mechanic to come up from Saragossa and get it in order. The two girls discovered there was nothing to do in the hotel and went down to the swimming-pool. I watched them from a window, in the moonlight, they were marvelous, but they didn't look in my direction. Robert went out to the bar to have a drink. Cohn was in bed, he didn't feel well. Bill came in and we talked and looked at a Barcelona paper to see if there was anything about the fiesta.\nI wasn't sleepy and I went out on the balcony and leaned on the wall. Directly under, a hundred and fifty yards away, was the great forty-foot dive into the municipal pool, where the bull-fighters took their baths. It was a triple cascade, like a broken-down staircase, the water falling in waves that broke clear below.\nCHAPTER\nIn the morning I was asleep on one of the wicker beds when I heard the car come back. I didn't get up and I heard the girls come up the stairs and go into their room. Then I heard the men's...", "2_0_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-two a.m.</span> when I pushed him over the threshold of the Car and told him to either go home or go to the lobby of the Flamingo Hotel and call a cab. \u201cFuck the car,\u201d I said. \u201cAre you rich enough to waste a beautiful car like this?\u201d He shook his head slowly, dumbly. He was thinking. \u201cAll I have to do is walk across the street,\u201d he said. I nodded. \u201cGood,\u201d I said. \u201cTen years from now, you can laugh about this\u2014with your wife and children. Hell, you might even tell it to your priest.\u201d That was too much for him. Whether he was Catholic or not, that image was unbearable. What I had said evoked some basic and profound rejection in him\u2014a total rejection of me and the world I stood for and the values I lived by; he snorted with contempt and pulled himself together. His eyes focused heavily on my face, then flicked down to the dashboard of the car.\nHe managed a tight smile. \u201cWhere is it?\u201d he asked.\nI stared at him, blankly.\n\u201cWhere's what?\u201d", "0_19_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-six in the evening</span>. The big robot lunged for Rita, his electronic laugh screeching.\nRocks:\nRocks peered cautiously over a pile of boulders at the factory, wheezing. Behind the boulders, firepoles hummed softly, whispering wisps of steam. On a hillside, police whistles tooted tinnily.\nLieutenant Oswald Werner Charmling of the Martian Mounted Police ducked down beside his inferior. \"All those firepoles in action at once makes a dandy racket, don\u2019t it?\" he said.\nRocks grunted. \"You ought to have been here when they first come in numbers.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, standing at the top of a firepole, performed that morning for the armed forces of Betty Boop and Mars a remarkable leap, gaining a height of about eighty-five feet. His bursting bugles became entangled in high-tension wires, and he hung there kicking and screaming until a chain-shot from a Martian moon miner sent him plummeting two hundred feet to the ground. A firepole brought $FIRSTNAMEB0 up the side of the factory again, but there he balked, not daring any more leaps.\n\"Yeah,\" said Charmling, \"and them fire...", "0_13_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. Chronological sequence was vigorated by drink. Synchronizing with Dr. Hilarius\u2019s coming were a line of yellow school buses on North Broad, the annual release from school of gradeless youngsters from the Columbus Academy for Boys and School for the racially bigoted Beautiful Pilgrim Baptist Church, a number of shiny black limousines, cruising deceitfully for trade, and one particular metallic-red Porsche that kept cruising for a redheaded girl who had waved to Rita Cohen from its passenger seat. The girl unfortunately had a date. The Porsche, then, continuing its cruise in such a way as to suggest utter cool indifference to the rejection, found on North Broad more reasons than schools and limousines for its foul mood: a body in front of a TV set; a body under a car; and a body pulling a hand cart around in circles. The cool indifference of the Porsche\u2019s driver was shortly to have as its reward a luscious shock that only added to his foul mood. And, within minutes after the shock, he was to have confirmed his common humanity with several persons, among them Lang and DiVecchio, Hilarius, old Mrs. Bombardini, young $FIRSTNAMEF3, and Lenore Beadsman,...", "2_17_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five sixteen in the afternoon</span>. It was two twenty-three in the morning.\nMary Artemisia Dwyer was sleeping soundly in bed.\nShe woke up about nine-thirty in the morning.\nHer husband, Constantinesco, was sleeping soundly in bed.\nShe did not wake him up.\nShe took off a pair of men\u2019s pajamas.\nShe put on a brief green bathrobe. She was naked underneath the bathrobe.\nShe went into the bathroom. She spent an hour and eleven minutes cleaning her teeth, washing her face, soaping and rinsing the lovely contours of her voluptuous body, combing and brushing her nut-brown hair, washing her hands, and making up her face.", "0_19_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>, the place was the fifth-floor conference room of the Admiralty Building, and the Prime Minister of England was provoking laughter aboard HMS Seraph. He was, after all, telling a joke worth telling. At Coventry Cathedral, the rector had complained of pigeons fouling the altar. The bishop appointed a commission of pigeon fanciers to evaluate one of the cathedral\u2019s statues of a saint. The commission did so, and reported that it would be possible to cover the statue\u2019s head with a pigeon curtain\u2014but this would require the building of a new belfry and the installation of a hoist and pulley system to pull the statue\u2019s head off and replace it if the statue needed cleaning. The report was sent to the bishop, who sent for the cathedral rector and told him, \"There is an easy way for you to get rid of the pigeons.\" \"Yes, my lord?\" said the rector. \"Get yourself a modern kitchen broom with a long flexible handle, and chase the birds away whenever they alight.\"\n\"And what happened then?\" thundered the Iron Duke. \"Laughter?\"\nThis was said with gibberish into an imaginary transmitter, since all communications with Space Wanderer...", "0_22_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEF2, in her Genesian guise, continued to read. Five minutes later the telephone rang. She thought it would be Mucho, to say the latest stretch of the trip had been a fiasco. But it wasn\u2019t Mucho at all. It was Stanley Koteks, flower child, who\u2019d gone into exile from the far-out confines of San Andreas, and had now driven all the way across the state in his Volkswagen with his soulfulggy Rosie to find $FIRSTNAMEF2. \u201cOh,\u201d she said.\nSSSsss EEEEaaassss. $FIRSTNAMEF2 sprung the hook on her phone, stared at it. How had that occurred? She\u2019d hung it up. She picked it up, looked at the face, was preparing to dial San Francisco, decided to see if the phone was doing it again.\nAgain. She put the receiver near the ear of the french civilian, ten feet away, shhhhiiiissshhhh, it went. The signal strength was good. It began to smile at her. She hung up, stared at the receiver, wondering what creepy quirk in her brain, encoded in her DNA, would drag this peeks...", "0_21_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span> and $FIRSTNAMEB2 was alone in the home of Salo, the hired girl of Dolores Hasp, the mother of $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was fifteen years old. $FIRSTNAMEB2 wasn\u2019t surprised that Salo would leave him alone, for he had asked her on purpose.\nSalo had looked fifteen years old, but she was as old as theUnited States of America.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had realized this when he had performed one particular feat. He had asked Salo to tell him the story of the first public trial in the life of the great, saintly, and deceased Tralfamadorian messenger, $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Have we got time for this?\" asked Salo, since it was now seventy-eight minutes until takeoff times. The Earthling on Titan would be sending a ship for $FIRSTNAMEB2. This was all going to happen very fast.\n\"We\u2019ve got all the time there is,\" $FIRSTNAMEB2 had said, \"because it\u2019s the story that took the most time in your life. Start at the beginning. Start when you first met her. Start during her childhood.\"\n\"Childhood?\" said Salo. \"Don\u2019t rush me, buster. What was your name?\"\n\"", "2_10_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-eight in the morning</span>.\nBracknell escorted $FIRSTNAMEF1 23049 back to her chamber at the palace. He told her that the accident had been exaggerated\u2014that he had been splendidly shielded by his cooling system.\nShe believed him.\nBack at the palace he gave her the news about $SURNAME0 and his materialization machine.\n\"And now you\u2019ll have to stay here forever,\" said Bracknell.\n\"Isn\u2019t that what you wanted?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 23049.\n\"No\u2014I wanted you to be free, free to go with me when I went back to Earth.\"\n\"You do want that!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Yes, I do,\" said Bracknell.\n\"But it makes you terribly unhappy just to think about it.\"\n\"Yes,\" said Bracknell. He put his Space suit on the floor, and it made him feel wonderful to dispose of something that had been so useful, that had meant so much to him for so long.\n\"How long do you suppose it would have taken you to get back to Earth by yourself, Mr. Bracknell?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 23049.\n\"Five years,\" said Bracknell. \"Maybe ten.\"", "1_6_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>. I took a shower, got my bag, and started to the bus station. As I said good--by to the salesman in the bus office, he told me that in a very short while minutes, an express bus to Mexico City would leave. I ran out and found the right platform. I bought some fruit and took a seat on the open loading--deck of the big bus. A few Chiapas Indians were sleeping on their huge bundles. The bus started, and I put my feet up on the seat and munched a pear and felt good.\nOklahoma Perry Park to Bustamente 88 Miles\n[At the Mexican Border] \"I'd like some cheese and crackers, please.\" I didn't even look up. I had been facing backward all the way and I saw just one more beat--up old American liner go lurching over the Arizona desert and disappear in the heat mirage of Mexico. I was on a fast, taut Mexican bus that had started five or six minutes after the American one and we were due in Mexico City by eleven o'clock.\nFour seats ahead, a chain--smoking, excited American in rumpled suit was crying to his Mexican girl, who, true to form, was smoking with one...", "2_19_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-five in the evening</span>.\nThere was great external excitement, but $SURNAME0 and everything in his mansion obeyed the iron laws of physiology and psychology.\nThe old picture worked its effect\u2014the picture of a machine in a cellar workshop, turning out perfect ships, pulp magazines, cheap candy and idiot novels for a Universe that had no idea of anything better.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s heart said flop.\nHis snot- factories started to turn out snot, his tear ducts made tears, and a tooth floated up from the basement and joined the front of $SURNAME0\u2019s upper jaw.\nHis stool had been poisoned with a vile native $SURNAME0 narcotic.\nHe had taken the poison at nine o\u2019clock that morning, just before making his last public appearance.\nThe nine o\u2019clock crowd was the last crowd.\nThe poison would not manifest itself until nine o\u2019clock that night.\nAt that time, $SURNAME0 would take his dog out for one last walk\u2014-and then he and his stupendous hands would do something unspeakably beautiful and unspeakably final.\n$SURNAME0 shut his eyes.", "2_20_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-seven in the evening</span>.\nAt eight forty-eight, the three hundred and fifty ships would be in Earth airspace.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was not prepared to guide the expedition to Earth. There were eighty-seven operations to perform before the ships went into sustained deceleration for entering Earth\u2019s atmosphere. And not one of the eighty-seven operations had been pre-set.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had to think, choose, perform each operation separately, or Earth wouldn\u2019t be a very happy place for anybody for quite a long time.\nHe tried to think about other things, but his mind insisted on coming back to consider the awful business of the eighty-seven operations ahead of him in the next fifty-nine minutes and eleven seconds. He began to tremble.\nThings would be no worse for him if he died before he performed his first pre-set operation. And it might make things a lot worse for Earth to have a dead, frightened comedian at the controls of three hundred and fifty pig ships.", "2_6_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-one in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 met on the stairs.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had just come off night duty. He was wearing his tanzanite-studded blue-shades, though the shades were hardly necessary in the well lighted stairway. His blond hair was slicked down and parted in the middle. His face was so deeply tanned that it was almost opaque.\n\"Good morning, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, arriving at the first floor.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 grunted, kept going down.\n\"See you at practice today, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. Practice was the wrestling and foot-ball period, twice a day for every man in the Army.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 grunted again.\n\"Pretty rough time last night, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"I guess.\"", "2_3_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-two in the morning</span>.\n\"Where am I?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"According to the manual,\" said $SURNAME0, \"you are in Table City, Mars.\"\n\"What is my name?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"According to the manual,\" said $SURNAME0, \"you are $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\"\n\"Suppose I\u2019m not $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"That shocks me, frankly,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I\u2019ve always known you to be $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. Name, appearance, character\u2014you\u2019ve never satisfied the most pedantic requirements of anyone but $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\"\n\"And you are\u2014?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"You know perfectly well,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I am called $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, and my full description is on page eight of the manual.\" He turned a page of the telephone book-sized manual on his desk. \"There it is\u2014the great heavy pushmi-pullyu in sunglasses.\"\n\"Where is here?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Here,\" said $SURNAME0, \"is the storage room for a museum in Newport, Rhode Island, U. S. A.\"", "0_10_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. From his furtive and cautious removal of the tent flap at precisely that time, $SURNAME0 knew that the Space Wanderer\u2019s punctuality, like everything else about him, was externally imposed rather than genuinely internal. The chronometer in his head was a telechronometer. It was set by remote control from some place in the Solar System other than Titan. $SURNAME0 knew that the signal that set the Space Wanderer\u2019s chronometer was about to go ting. This meant that his only purpose in exposing himself to the public again was to welcome the company WINS.\nEarl Moncrief of WINS was down on his hands and knees outside, having come out through the Waterhouse door.\nMoncrief was much handsomer with his artificial good looks turned off. When he flashed on his good looks, he became a radio star\u2014a parched fruit of pulchritude. His pectorals had been vacuum-packed. His abdomen had been iron-pressed. His face had been altered by surgery and tricked up with cosmetics and hair tonics until he had a perpetual smirk of virility and sexual availability.\nHis true self, which peeked through when he was off the air and out of public...", "2_6_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\u201cAnd the first cast, and look at her come,\u201d said Crawford. \u201cHell, other places you gotta curse and cinch every second. But only for a second here, and look at her come.\u201d\n/b/\n16 September\nAt five past seven o\u2019clock A.M. Melinda understood completely.\nAnd at five past seven o\u2019clock, while the understanding was burning in her like a sun, her arm lying straight at her side, her palm flat on the cold pine board and her fingers groping blindly for the fleece so the contour of its leg would come back to her, the leg itself thrust out of sight around the wicker of the cage, at five past seven o\u2019clock there was a light knock at the door behind her and Lenore\u2019s plaintive voice, saying Melinda.\n17 September\n\u201cThank God,\u201d said Lenore.\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Melinda.\n\u201cJesus, I\u2019m ... I\u2019m sorry,\u201d said the other. She looked exhausted. She had one shoe off.", "0_13_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. Two hours and forty-two minutes to go. Two hours and forty-two minutes left before our plane took off for New York City. I was supposed to meet my attorney at the exit of the terminal at three-thirty. Until that moment, I felt, all I had to do was try to control my trembling. I ordered a Bloody Mary from the gambler. Life was passing before my eyes, as the saying goes: a flickering ceiling fan, a gray outback of heads and torsos, the groans of a chamber pot under the table.\nIt was Tuesday in Las Vegas and the whole town was only two days into official mourning for Elvis. Every club headline show was an Elvis retrospective, or \u201cMemories of Elvis.\u201d The showrooms were packed. We\u2019d caught three shows and they were all excellent. Some of the stars had even borrowed to do it right. Ann Margaret sang her heart out\u2014and stole my long black leather coat. Thank God. I would have been too nervous to sit through the whole thing without it.\nInteresting, too, because most of the good acts were in the afternoon. Just when the tourists were coming off the buses. After dark the town emptied out. We left...", "1_18_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six eighteen in the evening</span> and the place was Stonehendge, Massachusetts. Pacific stood with $FIRSTNAMEF2 beside the bird-mixedup Ford, in the lot to the right of the triangular, truncated green Great Stone Figure, which looked across all of New England, hoping to catch, perhaps, the first star. Pacific pointed to it. \u201cI go past it every day on my way to school. They teach us it\u2019s a Druid altar.\u201d\n\u201cAnd they\u2019re right?\u201d\nHe shrugged. \u201cSo they say.\u201d\n\u201cSo nobody knows.\u201d\n\u201cSomebody must,\u201d pointlessly, \u201cbut not me.\u201d\n\u201cDo you go to school right here?\u201d\n\u201cI have to,\u201d he answered and would not explain any more about that. He changed the subject. \u201cMy grandfather says when it turns dark you\u2019ll begin to see them.\u201d\n\u201cSee what?\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF2, not really alarmed. Just curious.\n\u201cI don\u2019t know,\u201d said Pacific. \u201cThat\u2019s what I want to find out.\u201d", "1_1_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>\u2014and the mood was desolate.\nRoosevelt was gone with his broken leg. He was flyi ng in a Douglas DC-3 to Minneapolis, Minnesota, to be fitted with a prosthesis by a Dr. A rthur W. Pett i ns , a world sa me ne r wi th I nt ern at iona l R ati ng .\nThere was a wild rumble of machinery and a bad smell of machinery in trouble. Then the machinery seemed to shut off, and the house was profoundly silent,\nBig rabble driven out from L. A.'s harbor area by cops raid cops, page 9 I'm Absolutely professional gambler's dictionary, page 32 Godtfred, $FIRSTNAMEF3 and Bettylou, page 55 Sniffled Walt Lampshade; our man on the Strip, page 7\nHis name had been mentioned in an L. A. newspaper as a potential gambler for an American team that was to compete against a team of London runners. Vegas Vic was listed as a possibility. I figured maybe he knew something about the race across the Sahara. I called him up. For once he agreed to talk.\n\u201cWhat's it like?\u201d I said. \u201cAre there wild animals? Are...", "0_18_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-four in the evening</span>. Redwine saw it, saw it as clearly as though a shaft of light from Heaven flicked any dust from the minute hand of his watch. He would give his report at exactly seven o\u2019clock. He had absolute confidence that he could make it, stark mad or not, in time. And he would give it only to Dr. Freud, no matter what he had to do to make certain of it. Of one thing he was sure.\n\"Just tell him!\" he croaked.\n\"Tell him what, Mr. Redwine?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Whatever he wants to know!\" said Redwine.\n\"How can I tell him what he wants to know when I don\u2019t know what it is?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"You know God\u2019s will!\" blurted Redwine.\n\"I wouldn\u2019t assume to know God\u2019s will for two cents,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. She smiled at Redwine\u2019s consternation. \"I don\u2019t see why you assume that you know, Mr. Redwine.\"\n\"I must go now, Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1. I must be punctual\u2014\"\n\"Wait just one second,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"You\u2019re so strange and...", "1_19_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven nineteen in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was wondering, among other things, what in the world the revolution was.\nHe now knew\u2014from $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s revelations\u2014that there was a plan, and that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was the pivot of that plan. Still, he didn\u2019t understand the nature of the plan.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 finally reached the beginning of his third tape, and played it, and went on sleeping. It was a long tape, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had only played half of it before reaching the end of his second tape. The third tape conveyed to $FIRSTNAMEB0 the most reassuring message of luck and power that he had ever heard\u2014in fact, it was the only comforting message of luck and power that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had ever heard. The message was that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was the luckiest and most powerful person in the whole Universe.\nIt was a very homey message, and was delivered by a sweet old lady. She and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had quite a cozy relationship by then. They even joked about $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s getting a bad command.\n\"But you being you, $FIRSTNAMEB0, you put things right again,\" said the sweet old lady. \"Because you are a scientist and a good soldier, and because you are such a lucky and powerful man. Aren...", "0_1_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-five in the morning</span>. When I returned to the room and switched on all the lights in an attempt to rustle up some enthusiasm for FUCK, one hundred percent pure, Metzger threw off the bedclothes and came over to me in a white terry-cloth towel, hair soaked from the shower. \u201cTurn off the lights,\u201d he said; \u201cI want to test my Cinemascope eyes.\u201d Crossing the room to the toilet, he stared into the mirror, studied the wallpaper in the false perspectives of stereoscopic dimness. \u201cPretty weird,\u201d he said, \u201cbut it works.\u201d He turned on the TV set and switched on the color circuitry. The walls, with the blue light of the video tubes flickering through them, assumed each of the rainbow hues as he flipped the dial. \u201cYou sure goofed on the walls, Dogstar.\u201d But I was concerned only with the news. In the entire Bay area there had been a rash of wild panther sightings, sudden raids on garbage cans, vicious maulings in back alleys. A little old lady had been dragged three hundred yards down the middle of a residential street in Oakland by a black puma. It was only the latest incident...", "1_17_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"I was holding something in my right hand that I was dissatisfied with. It was dirty. I dropped it in the water with a plunk.\n\"I saw a coin fall slowly past my right ear and disappear with a small splash fifty feet behind me. I turned to look at it. It was a quarter of the United States of America.\n\"It was twelve minutes past five o\u2019clock in the afternoon.\n\"There were towers In heaven and beneath.\n\"I was in hell.\n\"Therefore there were towers in heaven.\n\"But how could this be, since hell did not exist?\n\" 'I am in hell,' I thought.\n\"I turned to a woman standing beside me and spoke.\n\" 'You are in heaven, aren\u2019t you?' I asked.\n\" 'Yes,' she said. 'Wouldn\u2019t you like to buy a pictorial Bible?' she asked. 'It contains sixteen detailed drawings, with explanations.'\n\" 'I\u2019ll take one,' I said.\n\"She wrapped one in brown paper and gave it to me. I paid her a dollar.\n\" 'Wouldn\u2019t you like a nice book full of pictures of...", "1_14_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\n\"Now I\u2019ll bet you can\u2019t guess what Don\u2019s doing right at this very minute,\" Lenore went on. \"He\u2019s having your friend Karl Rummage drill holes, a whole lot of holes, in the skull of a man, who the man himself personally and explicitly refuses even to talk to, but Don won\u2019t listen and takes his eyes off the guy for a second while they\u2019re drilling, and the guy writes this two-sentence note\u2014\"\n\"That\u2019s Cat. Cat Dimple.\"\n\"So I haven\u2019t been exactly out of touch,\" Lenore said. She reached for a book on the counter and opened it. \"See? Memo to Cliff, Clarice, and other staff personnel in the building. Received during my lunch hour the twenty-first of October. Perhaps you got one, too. It\u2019s totally unhelpful.\"\nClarice went to the shoe care shelf and took a cloth down.\n\"However,\" Lenore went on, \"at the end of it the guy says \u2018Tell Lenore to remember the tune.\u2018 Universe and Norman Partridge, Cleveland and beyond. Hot off the press...", "2_11_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until noon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put his hand on the baby. \"I think this thing thinks I\u2019m its father,\" he said.\nAnd then he laughed.\nIt was the first time in three years and two months that he had laughed.\nHis laughter caught on like wildfire.\n\"That\u2019s funny!\" people said.\n\"Ain\u2019t that funny?\" people said.\n\"Gee\u2014ee\u2014\" people said, \"that\u2019s a good one!\"\nThe cyclops walked into the courtyard and blatted for attention. \"Lunchtime\u2019s over!\" he blatted. \"Get to work, you birds!\" he blatted, and walked back out.\nThen the whistle blew, and the people stopped talking, and the birds got to work.\nSo did $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 and the baby went down to the conveyor again. As they stood there, nestled among their avian comrades, $FIRSTNAMEB0 discovered, to his surprise, that he was happy. He didn\u2019t know why he was happy, but he knew, nevertheless, that he was very, very happy.\nThe whistle blew again, and the conveyor began to move.", "2_15_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nA British Warm fan of tremendous proportions was oscillating on a pole outside the open door of $SURNAME0\u2019s mansion. The air, though hot, was damp enough to inhibit the materialization of $FIRSTNAMEB4. $FIRSTNAMEB4 was materializing, though\u2014was materializing sullenly and slowly in the middle of a hyacinth bed.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 looked around savagely. $FIRSTNAMEB4 hated $SURNAME0. $FIRSTNAMEB4 hated Earthling society. $FIRSTNAMEB4 hated himself.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 withdrew into a small, vaguely cylindrical cloud. The cloud sank to the foot of the step. The warm and humid wind of the fan touched the cloud, and the cloud billowed up to the foot of the step again.\nThe warm, humid wind of the fan was the dog\u2019s chief incentive for materializing at all.\nWhat little incentive the wind offered $FIRSTNAMEB4 was countered by a powerful suction, as of a turbine at the center of his puny soul. This suction tugged him homeward\u2014tugged him all the way to a lump of desolation a billion miles and three billion years away.", "2_8_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was nothing in the great main hall but a display case containing a goldfish. The goldfish had won the gold medal for flying gold-fish at the New York World\u2019s Fair of 1940. The goldfish was named cigarettes.\nThe glass door to the east wing kitchen was already open. In the east wing kitchen was a big electric four-oven stove, a big electric refrigerator with five freezers and a cold room, two automatic dishwashers, a French continuous feed cooktop, a French fryer with two deep-fat friers, two electric rotisseries, a breadmaker, a spice mixer, a dough roller, a vegetable-slicer, a cut-up machine, a butcher block and carving set, a meat grinder, a meat saw, a larding needle, a cork screw, a wine bottle opener, a coffee mill, a citrus-pellet mill and reamer, ten electric garbage grinders, and three electric dish-washing machines. The room was humidified and cozily equipped with binocular shelves of pine needles from $SURNAME0\u2019s private stock of Monterey pines.", "0_18_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was timing himself to see whether he could get through the three floors of the Altamont Hotel in eight minutes. Three was his lucky number, and eight his unfaithful one. He was proving, to his own satisfaction, that he could keep his feet moving at a favorable pace though the ladder of time and floor of space had been removed.\nThe reason for this yo-yo exercise was that, ten minutes from now, $FIRSTNAMEB1 would be arrested and taken away by Space Patrolmen who had come to the hotel in response to a complaint about Space Opera written in letters an inch and a half high on $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s bathroom mirror: Keep the noise down!\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was trying to get as much fun as he could from his last ten minutes of freedom.\nThe door of Room 3310 opened suddenly. $FIRSTNAMEB1 hoped his timing was dead on. He didn\u2019t want to make love to this good-looking woman who stood in the doorway.\n\"I\u2019m back,\" she said with a smile.\n\"Back?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"I was just down visiting an old friend downstairs, a man I knew very well once,\" she said. \"Down at the reception desk, I mean.\"", "1_3_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past three o'clock in the morning</span> of the sixteenth of December, the time when landscapes change color, when the sense of a presence, the weight or the heat of the sun changes, when the day, like a woman who has dawdled and chatted, begins to hurry, when the route we have traveled in the night changes in the warmth so that we may mistake the way back.\nWe had been crossing the Pyrenees. At Bayonne and at Biarritz the wilderness of peaks had been capped with snow. At Pau the snow was no longer white, but dingy with dust. In the early morning coming up from Lourdes the Alps flattened to a jagged line on the horizon. Now the slopes of the Western Pyr\u00e9n\u00e9es were green, spotted with white and gray, and the rocky summits were turning blue.\n\"Would you like anything else?\" Robert Cohn asked.\n\"Nothing.\"\n\"Well, I\u2019ll go to the barber-shop. I\u2019ll be out in a little while and we can have some lunch.\"\n\"See if they\u2019ll shave you while you\u2019re there,\" I said. He waved his hand and walked out. It was a nice gesture on his part. The barber...", "1_0_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. I was following a light-blue '49 Hudson sedan containing three cats and one passenger--a thick-set Mexican about twentyfive. He was driving drunk, and he swung the heavy boat into a U-turn, crossing the northbound traffic instead of using the intersection up ahead. I let him cut in behind me. He stopped at a traffic light just beyond the intersection, and I pulled up behind him and turned off all my lights. To the left I could see the entrance to Route S0--the northernmost mainline of the great American road. If a goddam Chicano would just blow so I could cross the road, I'd bop over to Route 90, shove the Hudson over to the left and pursue, hellbend for Baton Rouge...with a cop in pursuit, if necessary. But I had it rigged. I had gone to the Chief's office and stolen his redcutter torch and laid out the plans while standing over the criminal books, to disguise my design. No problem. Who would ever suspect? My lights were off: I was just a silent apparition with no connection to anything visible in the room. Ten minutes went by. The Mexican reached down beside his seat and popped a squeaky door. He half fell out of...", "1_23_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB1, Alabama. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was a small manufacturer of leather-goods and of a chancre remedy. It was in a pass between two wooded hills at the headwaters of a creek that descended to the east and west forks of a northern Alabama river. The approach to $FIRSTNAMEB1 was over a road that curved up and in among the hills and straightened out and up a sharp incline to the town. The shops of $FIRSTNAMEB1 were alongside this road, and in the angles formed by the shops and the road were ten or twelve small houses of wood, painted yellow. Down the hill and up a sharp hill was the hotel of $FIRSTNAMEB1, and next to the hotel was the county courthouse. $FIRSTNAMEB1 had two banks. One of the banks was across from the courthouse. The other bank was a block and a half from the hotel. Each bank had a marble doorway and a shingle above the doorway on which was the bank's name. In the spaces between the two banks was a square, fenced in by iron pipe. In the square was a Civil War monument and a cannon on a modern gun carriage. In and out among the shops and banks of $FIRSTNAMEB1 wound an alley that carried refuse from the kitchen of the hotel...", "1_21_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-one in the evening</span> of Monday, October 27, 1973, at a point ten miles out in the Gulf of mexico and a hundred miles north of the Florida Keys, where the greenest and deepest part of the ocean mixes with a sky whose only concern is the color of rain. A deal had been concluded in a blue-tiled hotel room in Newport. Its terms involved an airplane and a certain amount of chance, but the man with the money held the cards, for without him there would have been no plane, not even the possibility of a plane, and the pilot whose life had become involved in the affair, an old World War II ex-P-38 pilot named $SURNAME0, would have died of old age before the sale of Liberty Bonds in Newport.\nHis plane was a big Sikorsky helicopter, of which only four were ever built.\nThis helicopter had been modified for shallow-water work in the Caribbean and elsewhere. Her rotors were bulletproof, her crew compartments built like turrets.\nThe butts of two pearl-handled pistols were plainly visible in the jacket pocket of the man who had purchased her. The pocket was on the left side. One pistol was for his use. The other was for whoever was piloting the plane on err...", "2_23_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-one in the evening</span>.\nExcept for some possible human teeth that may have been rattled loose in the spitting slipstream below decks in the after cabin, nobody knew anything at all about what was going on.\nNobody knew anything at all about what was happening. Even young Krup, the most enthusiastic and loveable male child that Solomon and $FIRSTNAMEF0 Krup had ever had the pleasure of possessing and raising, didn\u2019t know what was happening.\nKrup was boyishly energetic in the way that children are when they wake up in the middle of the night. He was chasing a small, phosphorescent glow-worm around in his room. He didn\u2019t quite catch it, either. It shone all the way under his bed, anyway. He smelled coffee being made in the kitchen.\nKrup, in his cheerful thoughtlessness, went out into the hall and waked his father.", "2_18_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were in the parlor talking about ... strange things.\n\"When I get to Heaven,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"I\u2019m going to ask God why He made me the way I am.\"\n\"Why did He?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"But I\u2019ll ask Him.\"\n\"What difference,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"will it make what He tells you?\"\n\"I want to know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Suppose He tells you that He made you the way you are because He is a vicious monster with a great sense of humor?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"I would still want to know why,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"and I would still want to know what I\u2019ve done to make Him hate me so much.\"\n\"How do you know He hates you?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"I can feel it,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"You can\u2019t live as close to God as I have and not know.\"\n\"Then you feel He hates you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"You\u2019ll find fault with Him anyway.\"", "0_10_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-one in the morning</span> and every one and everything were in horror beyond any measuring, or caring, or imagining, as the wife of the accused man went forward to swear, with the extraordinary and conclusive gesture of asking the permission of the judicial panel for the privilege of taking off her gloves, which permission was granted immediately, the panel being too shocked, by the admission of her negligee, to find anything amiss with her request on its own terms.\nAnd so now with cold hands and in the plain view of everyone else in the room and with every eye rigidly fixed upon her and conveying in panic the meaning that there was not and never would be, for everyone in the room, a moment\u2019s peace or relief from this horror except possibly for the bare moment of the screaming conclusion of the scene of the witness, who was being put through Hell by the last few questions of Mr. Genghis Cohen, who knew he was scoring high, who knew he was hitting hard.\n\"You swear that, now, don\u2019t you, ma\u2019am, ma\u2019am?\"\n\"Yes.\"\n\"Sure?\"\n\"Yes.\"\n\"You swore it once. You can swear it again.\"\n\"I did not.\"\n\"Did too.\"", "0_14_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span> and the place was far out in Space, desolate and godforsaken, about half a billion miles from where the horned moons track down an ebon sky. And then, suddenly and without warning, the silence was broken by an alarm bell that buzzed and screamed more loudly with every passing second.\n\"Release!\" screamed the terrified electronic brain. \"Release!\"\nThe ship that was fired from the smoking Bay of Canaveral at 2:15 o\u2019clock every sunny afternoon was indeed a lemon, as $SURNAME2 knew, but not for the reasons he told himself.\nThere is something very strange about a ship trying to take off near the Space program\u2019s longest runway. The bewildered ship, with most of its rockets still undampened, has nowhere near enough velocity to clear the surprisingly low terrain near the runway. The ship stands shuddering, high on one sprinnell, with one rockpen still tipped up, on the edge of a terrible crevasse. A vehicle in this position would never be cleared for takeoff by helpful ground engineers; it would be ordered to shut down, roll back and take off again with more altitude, However, the ship was in no position to roll back and take off again with more altitude, The...", "0_15_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-seven in the afternoon</span> now and was expected to be shortly after four o\u2019clock. At the moment when the Sanl\u00facar time was made exact with the Chicago time, Fern had turned ninety degrees to her left and was now fronted with Stoney, Alvin Junior, and Clarice, who were to take and distribute television-camera snapshots with Evelyn\u2019s Rard and Rard 150. Fern\u2019s home darkroom was by now working on the first five cubic feet of negatives. These were pictures of the dismembered and scattered parts of a blank-eyed deer. The leg that was to come was spread wide. The head was in two pieces. And the three pieces were labeled. The antlers were done and hung from the back of a disused coal shovel. The cameras came up to all sorts of devices, had shutters that clicked, lenses that bulged and twirled, long rays from metallic-infused noses. \u201cThose are awfully nice dresses,\u201d Bud\u2019s offspring\u2019s offspring said to Fern, who had had a wonderful afternoon, who wasn\u2019t a tiny bit afraid of babies anymore, and who sent back a smile and a thank-you with all her warmth. \u201cMay I have your...", "1_19_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was a stationary room that was somewhere east of the Sun, far out in the Milky Way. The stationary room was a space ship\u2014and not a very big one at that.\n\"Lazaru mpran,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, looking at Milton Ripple.\n\"What did you say?\" said the choking, gasping, death-bound Space Wanderer.\n\"Trnk?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I said, \u2019Lazarus, come forth,\u2019 \" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"That\u2019s what he always does when I say that to him.\"\n\"Oh,\" said $SURNAME1, giving up trying to understand what the Space Wanderer and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were talking about.\nIn the Sun, far out in the Milky Way, the harmonious conception of the mad poet, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, was bom. It was a son whom $SURNAME1 never saw. His heart was as heavy as a chunk of Uranium 235.\nAt every star that his space ship encountered, $SURNAME1 dropped an advertisement for a cocktail party, to be held on Earth, at Newport.\nAll of the advertisements were written on a space ship prepared especially for dropping them. The space ship was a chunk of pure Uranium 235, the...", "0_20_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span> of October 8, 1973, in the location of Group 23, Space-Time Parameter, half a billion years before the present, according to the calendar.\nThe place was a swamp, the battlespheroid having reached it by way of the fastest means available, which had happened to be a curving trajectory through deep space. The course had been laid according to instructions from the harmoniums, directing the machine very deep into the past.\nThe machine in which Citizen 5450350/2345043/4 was trapped had squatted deep in the swamp, rocking and undulating on its liquid mountings. Far away could be heard noises of bullets and bombs, signaling the struggles of Max Weber\u2019s comrades to reach him.\nCitizen 5450350/2345043/4 was tightly strapped into the battle saddle in which he had ridden from the year 2594 A.D. He was finding it difficult to express a clear opinion of his situation.\nThe complaining he was doing came chiefly from the fact that his communications system was out of order. This effectively removed all means by which he might correct his predicament. He had no way to tell any human being where he was. The human beings on Earth did not even know that he had been...", "1_0_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past midnight</span>. The place was the living room of my conapt on Nueva Vista Lane, San Narciso. I sat with my wife before the Sunday movies on television. $FIRSTNAMEF2 and I had been doing a fair amount of drinking, about two manhattans for her, threebourbons for me. We\u2019d begun at about seven, looking at dirty postcards we\u2019d picked up along our conducted tour that evening of \u201cLas Vegas Means discourage.\u201d By ten o\u2019clock we\u2019d moved on to a free discussion of our prospective sexual prowess, based on our age and experience. Worlds had fascinated, loves hit the wall, gaiety had gone out ofarn, a certain number of drinks being called for, though neither of us was drunk.\nRereading this, I realize it means we were having a fight. There was no immediate cause. We\u2019d been perfectly happy, had had a good day, were both of us physically exhausted and at peace. But the night has its own dimensions, knows no rule of three and nothing lived can be lost. So the night begins to slime out and we are in it, unable to escape what cannot be said. You start with a#t and you...", "2_3_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-six in the morning</span>.\nAt three thirty-seven, it began to rain\u2014or what Earth people called rain. It would have been snowing on Mars. But the houses of Newport stood on Earth, and rain was what fell out of the sky when the atmosphere was most disturbed.\nThe drops were the size of baseballs. They hit thepanes with the force of a dustbuster.\nUncle Oscar let a dispassionate eye roam over his estate\u2014his tennis court, his motor court, his motor-tennis court. There was something left to do, something so monstrously easy that no one else in the family had thought of it. Let the raindrops fall, he said to himself\u2014even though he had an open invitation to join the social swirl of Newport that evening.\nTo make his decision easier, there was a terrific thunderclap.\nUncle Oscar ran to the bottom of the steps, looked up, saw Bronson Jones still sitting there fearfully.\nUncle Oscar began to clamber up the steps. He was going to wake his nephew and tell him something he had learned from the history of Earth.", "1_14_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-five in the afternoon</span> of October 4, 1970. I was thirty-six years old. I had been a professional racetrack tout for twenty years. In 1947, one of my first jobs as a licensed California Optometrist, I went to work for Rumson Tumlin, one of the two or three most successful handicappers in the country. For two years I made around twenty thousand dollars a year from Rumson\u2019s Oak Park office. Then he moved his operation to Las Vegas. He offered to take me along\u2014an unheard\u2014of generosity\u2014but the thought of working among all those sleazy crooks who hung around the racehorse verandas on the Strip turned my stomach. I told Rumson I didn\u2019t want to go. He fired me and replaced me with a glib young Tuskagee cop named Lee Samuels. Four days later Lee suffered a nervous breakdown, called me in Sacramento and begged me to come to work for him. \u201cYou\u2019re the only one I trust,\u201d he said. I went to Vegas, but I couldn\u2019t fix for Lee the way Rumson had fixed for me, because Rumson had all kinds of law\u2014and friends in the State Legislature\u2014working for...", "0_12_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span> when one Earthling and two Tralfamadorians materialized on a bench in the little town of commemorative statues. The statue that had been closest to the spot where materialization took place was that of a one-eyed Civil War veteran who had lost his eye in a skirmish outside a small Virginia town called Franklin. The statues of a woman and a small girl had been broken by souvenir hunters.\nActually, materialization was not quite the right word. There was no sensation of movement. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog materialized as though they were ghosts. Neither the one-eyed Civil War veteran nor his wife nor his daughter nor any of the other statues was in any way disturbed by the fact that Nature had just gone and done something outrageously irregular.\nSometimes, when he became drunk and dizzy, $SURNAME0 materialized so slowly that he barely formed before his materialization was completed. He would see swirling, hot-pink veils and would hear mesmeric chimes. Then everything would snap into place.\nNot this time. This time, for reasons unknown, the process of materialization went on without a hitch. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 slopped together into hard, dry reality.\n\"I say...", "1_17_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. I walked over to a Standard gas and service station and asked the mechanic if I could use his phone to call the Harley Hotel. I wanted to talk to my attorney about getting the operation to find out who Max Tapert was\u2014and, if he was a bullhitter, to deal with him one way or another, depending on what we found out. We had to end this crazy thing while we still had some money left; and I was getting damned tired of following people around all day every day, with my belly tight as a drum and no place to go.\n\u201cHello; yes, I\u2019d like to speak to my attorney, Mr. Wingdale\u2014that\u2019s right, Bruce Bruce Wingdale.\u201d I was using a Bogart nasal, cool and distant. \u201cWhat? No, I haven\u2019t been drinking, but I think we better have our conference now.\u201d I took off the receiver and waited until I heard his voice. \u201cDoctor? . . . Yes, well, I just called to tell you that your diagnosis is correct, I think. I\u2019ve been exposed to some weird narcotics, and I\u2019m afraid the cumulative effect has been rather severe.\u201c", "1_22_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span> when young $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 became co-author of the wondrous happenings on Mars.\nWinton had become convinced, watching the troops frolic around their parked ships, that $SURNAME0 was under stress. The expensive little entertainment cost fifty cents a man. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was a tightwad.\nNothing could wring more for a dollar than the Solar System\u2019s luckiest man would get for it.\n$SURNAME0 did not look at all prosperous. He had lost weight since his luck began. His unbuttoned ulster revealed a dirty shirt and braces and socks.\nHe held the cigar in his teeth while he found a battered gold watch and looked at it. Apparently, he had been timing something. The watch evidently satisfied him.\nHe took his cigar out of his mouth and spoke hoarsely. \"Good-by, Mr. Beatty,\" he said. \"I will probably never see you again.\"\n\"What\u2019s that?\" said Beatty.\n\"I have plenty of time to catch my ship,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"What ship?\" said Beatty.\n\"The Titanic,\" said $SURNAME0. He put down the cigar to keep both hands for...", "1_15_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe air was warm and moist.\nThe light was brilliant.\nThe route from Red Barn to Central Wholesale Drug was of firm red clay flanked by stone walls. It was watered by brooks that were splashed white with water lilies.\nThe route was idyllic in the extreme.\nThe only inelegance was supplied by the feet of $SURNAME0. The space between $SURNAME0\u2019s great toes was a full inch.\nThe reason that $SURNAME0\u2019s feet were so far apart was simple\u2014he couldn\u2019t walk any other way.\nThe reason that $SURNAME0 couldn\u2019t walk any other way was also simple\u2014he was born to use a space ship instead of his feet. Whenever he tried to walk on Earth, he developed diarrhea and a headache.\nNo one knew more acutely than $SURNAME0 how idyllic was the route from Red Barn to Central Wholesale. $SURNAME0 not only knew the route and the things along it well\u2014he knew his own manner of moving quite as well. He knew how far and gracefully he could stretch his arm withoutmovinghis feet. He knew how near his toes he could bring his fingertips without bending his knees...", "1_5_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-seven in the morning</span>, on August 9, 1971. Despite the earliness of the hour, the street was alive, for tourists throng the mansion around the clock. They come from all over America in chartered buses, then swarm all over the Mint and find places to pee, with the help and guidance of policeman who are not especially discriminating about giving directions.\nThe tourists were not the only ones around, for even at 5:47 somebody was opening up the T. R. and Tea Dance for business. On the marquee was blazoned: Lila, Queen of the Nudists! And in anticipation of Lila's triumphant homecoming gig, the ancient guitar and fiddle player was already bending his strings and sawing away to an oddly empty lot, while his old dog caved dust under a hot tree near the ivycovered side of the tavern.\nThe jazzman did not see the Volkswagen pull up right in front of him on the pitted concrete; he did not see redheaded Lucy and Pig Bodine get out of the Volkswagen; he did not even see the dog press its rusty snout to the ground and lick up a few loose grains of Scribe the bartender's hash. He did see a cop, though, when the cop flashed an i.", "2_1_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>. The Ideal, the Close part of the Dream, had been in progress for thirty-five minutes.\n\"Wool!\" said $SURNAME1. \"What a material!\" He touched his pinky to his thumbnail, touched the result to his tongue, and smiled eloquently.\n\"I like this dandruff,\" he said. \"Keep it coming, wherever you are. \"\nThe time was one minute past one o'clock in the morning.\nHearsay, the shepherd dog, was sitting at the Martian\u2019s feet, staring at his face, waiting for the next command.\nThe blue afterimage of $SURNAME1\u2019s smile still hung in the air\u2014remained, like a holy image made by a saint, visible even in the absence of the saint.\nHearsay examined the afterimage, trying to decide what it was.\nA tone sounded\u2014secular, this time, not celestial.\nThe tone was the telephone call at $SURNAME1\u2019s apartment in Chicago. It repeated itself twice, insistently.\nHearsay barked responsively, thinking that it was the right answer to give to such a noise.", "2_18_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-nine in the evening</span> on Mercury, or Hell.\nBoom!\nBoom!\nSomething exploded. Something else exploded.\nBoom!\nBomb sounds drifted up over the walls and came from somewhere far inside Oldacre\u2019s grounds.\nRinged around the lawns and serenaded from inside the grounds was a shift of fast-food workers, janitors, car salesmen, inventory control clerks, and other shiftless members of the leisure class that had been hired to riot. Limousines as long as railroad cars idled in the driveway.\nA thousand helmeted elite riot police in blue uniforms were in position around the house.\nA thousand more helmeted elite riot policemen in white uniforms were in position around the army of the shiftless.\nA thousand more helmeted elite riot policemen in red uniforms were in position near themselves.\nA thousand members of the elite Shock and Awe Police in gray uniforms were in position at presumed points of attack or vulnerability.\nA thousand members of the elite Command and Control Police were in posit-ion at presumed points of command.\nEvery police officer had a protective visor of impenetrable Plexiglas.", "1_14_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was a spot in the jungles of Venus at 49\u00b004\u2018 north, 224\u00b055\u2019 west, on the way to the city of Thalarion.\nThe date was November 8, 1982, by Earthling calendar, and the chronology by which the Red and Blasted Sun Billions knew themselves to be existing. The name of the company was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s Tutorial over Life in a Hurry, Inc. There were four men in the company and two of them were dead by the time their names were mentioned.\nThe salesman who stood in the middle of the clearing was named $SURNAME2. He was forty-three years old, and he was so filled with a transforming ecstasy that he trembled. There were beads of sweat on his round, smooth, rosy-cheeked face. He was otherwise cool in the sullen heat.\nThe salesman on his right stood thirty-five, was named Stony Stevenson, and lived in a furnished room on West 108th Street in New York. He had a degree in business administration from Columbia. There were two ball-turretted tubes on his left chest, between which was a button. He looked out at the ranks of the trees through...", "0_20_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-six in the evening</span>. Much had happened since the s\u00e9ance at ten o\u2019clock that morning. For one thing, $SURNAME1 had invested six thousand dollars in The Bomb. $SURNAME1 had had to go to San Francisco to do it\u2014and had been very glad to get out of Newport and away from the Stopes before $SURNAME0 or somebody discovered that he and Kazan were one and the same.\nClassic flowers of the table centrepiece that $SURNAME1 had bought on the way out of the mansion made his get-away possible.\n$SURNAME1 did not know how $SURNAME0 and the others would take what had happened at the s\u00e9ance.\nIt was unquestionably a mess.\nBut, then, one thing was certain\u2014Kazan and his family were free to go any time they liked. The materializing of a materializing machine was not, by itself, news that called for the services of a helicopter.\nClassic flowers of the table centrepiece had made $SURNAME1\u2019s get-away possible\u2014and had given Kazan and his family unrestrained freedom to go anywhere they liked.\nAnd, in popular parlance, freedom is another word for getting one\u2019s kicks.\nThey were looking for kicks now\u2014Kazan and his family.", "0_22_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past ten o'clock in the evening</span> of October 20, according to the chronometer on the wall of the cabin. The place was Earth, according to the clearly scrawled legend below the dial.\nOne young man sat in the cabin. He was wearing shorts and a sleeveless undershirt. He was young, upright, clean-cut, and handsome.\nHe was reading a book entitled On the Next Great Adventure.\nHe was so engrossed in the book that he did not hear the signal buzzer in the lock. He did not hear the feet padding in the gangway outside. He did not hear the quiet, sinister voices outside the door, nor the louder voices, nor the shots. Not until the door was crashed open did he look up to see men in uniform filling the room.\n\"What the\u2014What's going on?\" he said.\n\"Get moving\u2014outside!\" said a man in civilian clothes who seemed to have authority over the others. \"And get those shorts and that undershirt. Where\u2019s your uniform?\"\nThe young man found himself hustled out through a door that two soldiers held ajar. It was jarring, terrifying. He was hustled to the head of a companionway, and took several steps down. Speeding along a...", "1_12_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nIn less than thirty minutes, the Newport Historical Society was going to have a very important meeting in the $SURNAME0 estate.\nIn his mansion, bespectacled but otherwise naked and all but hairless, old Salo stood on tiptoe in his butler\u2019s pantry, wringing out a washcloth in a basin of hot water. His mansion was a mile from the estate. He was on his way.\nA rusty, creaking screw-grouse on the sidelines of the kitchen garden turned out, on closer inspection, to be the Iron Maiden. She was a religious statue that had been painted vermilion.\nBlisters, paint, and rust had done little to hide the fact that the Maiden was a cast-iron replica of Salo\u2019s ex-mistress, who was the emperor\u2019s mother. The Emperor Rudolph II had given Salo the cast-iron woman as a reward for saving his life, long ago, at the siege of Vienna.\nThe pedestal of the cast-iron woman, by the way, was a concrete fixture in the weed-grown yard of the $SURNAME0 estate. It read:\nTHE IRON MOTHER\nBELOVED OF", "0_19_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. At Fernrock and Copulation Corners it was still Saturday\u2014not yet Monday. But $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were already in the Monday of their relationship with $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014had joined him in it by means of their machine. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had told $FIRSTNAMEB0 nothing at all about their Monday relationship with him.\nAt the space ship, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was trying with the help of Kuzzle to get his vomiting under control. Kuzzle was forgiving, loving, lovable Kuzzle. Kuzzle had a new harmonium for $FIRSTNAMEB0, this time a real beauty. Kuzzle denied that the harmoniums did any harm.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head cleared enough for him to pay attention to the harmonium, which was the most contrapuntally advanced one Kuzzle had found on the convoy ship. Its concertmaster was the great Hamilton Trumm.\nA close listening to Trumm\u2019s composition would reveal that he, Trumm, was a virtuoso at battling for his life against forces as mysterious and awful as the ones that had driven $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 to embark upon the murderous reenactment in the hollow. Trumm\u2019s musical struggle against the anonymous opponent was a clumsy one, exhibitionistic,...", "2_13_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span> of July 4, 1999.\nThe place was Garp Peak in the Chronospatial Sea on the island of Sardinia.\nThe temperature was 281 degrees Kelvin, the pressure 87 kilopascals, and the humidity a perfect 100 percent.\nThrough thick green waters teeming with crustaceans and coralline algae a fishlike object carefully ascended, limned by the aquamarine light of the sun and by the beams of six subaqueous searchlights.\nThe thing was alive. It looked like a cross between a fish and a 2,000-horsepower Cadillac.\nThe monster had a chitinous carapace that was in fact metamorphosed muscle as hard as steel. It had two eyes at the front, on stalks. It had two processes that might have been arms or legs. It had no apparent sensory organs besides the eyes, no apparent organs of speech or of any other communication than telepathic.\nIt had three sets of hinged jaws, each made of interleaved razor-sharp dentine and bone. From one of these jaws protruded a shred of human flesh.", "1_10_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> of December twenty-second. The massed ranks of the Second United States Army were already on the move.\nA riot at dawn on December 22nd. It would soon enough be plain to all of them\u2014to Douglas and his staff, to the deserters in the bull-ring, to the American civilians and their families in the town\u2014that things would never be the same.\nA green jeep, driven by a civilian in gray tweeds, came rolling down the high stone wall to the command post. \"Where\u2019s Goodwin?\" said the first civilian to get out.\nIt was Helmuth von Moltke, tall, egg-bald, and deranged.\nHe had come to San Vicente with the elite of the Resurrection Army\u201432,000 men, women and children who were still convinced that the flood of tears was only a public-relations gesture. The Second Army was leaving them there.\nHelmuth had a little girl riding in the back of his jeep. He opened a collapsible umbrella as he came up. The naked child\u2019s ribs stuck out when she got out and looked up at the sky. The umbrella protected her just enough so that she wouldn\u2019t immediately get pneumonia.", "1_10_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when we passed through the town of Hermosillo on the Sonora side of the desert, and headed east into the desert along a road reached down through copper mine country. It was ninety-two degrees in the shade and every trace of green was blanched out. We had six hundred miles to go and a lot of it was desert and no water. Things began to remind me of the first time $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I ever started to cross the United States. I said so to my brother.\n\u201cWhat's $FIRSTNAMEB3 up to, anyway?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cI don't know. I asked my wife, but just as she was ready to tell me the phone rang, and it turned out to be $FIRSTNAMEB3 at the other end, and we never did get to talk about him.\u201d\n\u201cWell, there's no point in talking about $FIRSTNAMEB3. Every myth has to have a cluster of truths tangential to it or it ceases to foster the myth. $FIRSTNAMEB3 has become a myth. Everything about him is so complicated and strange he becomes bewildering in the end; yet some absolute sanity that cuts through the complications can be attributed to him. He's an ideal myth, almost a living type.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 had been America's...", "2_22_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Mars. Salo, looking out his office window, could see $FIRSTNAMEM2, a one-eyed Martian watchman, trotting across his open courtyard. $FIRSTNAMEM2 was running toward the palisade wall, and the wall was aflame.\nExperts on Mars had estimated that the Martians would not attack again for twenty years. The attack had come in under ten years. Salo, who had a private airfield, had a jet-propelled gyroplane in the hangar. He meant to fly to a secret Earthling space station on the far side of the Sun. He had arranged for his wife, Mata, to fly to Earth in a similar machine.\nSalo didn\u2019t mean to desert his Martian friends in their time of need. What he meant to do was steal their children, bring them to Earth, leave them with families that would care for them lovingly but firmly.\nHe hoped to communicate a similar plan to the children. He hoped they would make their way to the secret Earthling space station through tunnels and hidden slides and Velvet Underground routes.", "2_15_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. A Frenchman walked in and held up a mug of cognac on which he made a great flourish, offering it as a prize for the best cooper on the job. Henri now had another job and turned it down flat. \u201cWhat are you trying to do to me, you son-of-a-bitch, $FIRSTNAMEB3?\u201d he hissed. \u201cYou're trying to humiliate me.\u201d\n\u201cYes!\u201d\n\u201cWhere are the papers? Give me the papers and I'll sign them and we go to the caf\u00e9s and argue it out.\u201d\n\u201cYes!\u201d\n\u201cWait till I finish this job!\u201d\n\u201cYes! Yes! You damned bitch of a man --swell with your new life and stopping everybody else's --\u201d And so on. Friends were made in France in the time it took to roll up sleeves and argue. Some of the people who had rolled up sleeves stood off down the street to watch the fight. The taffy Frenchwoman from the millinery only shook her head. Beside her the gold-braided old Frenchman with the white hair nodded his head. This was the regular thing. His day was passed.", "0_2_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-one in the morning</span>, the last night of the $FIRSTNAMEB2 section of English 4.74 in the School. Flentz and $FIRSTNAMEB2 had said little to each other all day. $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s work had been atrocious. Frequently, Flentz had leaned burned-plastic-like around the corner of his desk and said, \u201cI am staring at a big goose egg, my friend. Just look at this.\u201d And he would flip open the red cover of his grade book to a page some fifty pages from the back. The book would be fat, of course, for a full course for the aged ran fifteen weeks, and at the end, if the aged ever did end, Flentz\u2019s red book would be walls and walls full of complaints, icy glares of resentment, chad jokes and tee-shirt sayings. And right at the last the sudden influx of failing grades would turn the book into a telephone directory of beige. But this particular failing grade book was a fat one because Professor Flentz had kicked his students all the way to Summer School. Old $FIRSTNAMEB2 had had the slackest load in the whole school. So fat was Flentz\u2019s book that he had to tape the spine.", "1_11_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, a most ungodly hour. His butlers and maids felt that he should have been in bed and asleep, only something had happened that dared not wait for morning. Some of the furniture had climbed down from its shelves and walked squatly across bare floors in which not even a rug suppressed the hollow echoes. The dahlias in an immense hanging vase became suddenly human and walked out into the sunlight. It was an hour of metamorphoses. Prints leaped off walls and flapped their wispy, unhinged arms. China became a ship on uncharted oceans. The ship foundered, the China shattered. And there were so many things to wash off shelves and pick up off floors! The morning-room floor was littered with pieces of newspapers that had come alive and were no longer limp! The servants swept and dusted, but their real occupation was watching what things stopped doing when they thought no one was looking. Things had a way of turning themselves off in a most exasperating manner. And this room was by no means the worst: the breakfast-room chairs had got down from the table and were busily turning pink and bouncing about in a wild dance on the bare floor, and a few plates in the pantry had followed...", "2_2_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-three in the morning</span>. In the dark room there was only the pinpoint buzz of a fly behind the lighted window across the street, a couple of muted giggles, the jump of a trolley-bus down across at the beach.\nIn the center of the room, illuminated by a cone of light from a long-shuttered searchlight projector that $FIRSTNAMEF2 had found in a storage shed next door and fixed up, was The Figure. It was about five feet high, vaguely anthropoid, and all of pastel shades of pink and lavender.", "1_18_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-three in the evening</span> and it was dark and it was raining. The rain was coming down straight and cold, and the foam on the avenue curled and whitened. There were not many people in sight.\nThe big, square car turned the corner and stopped outside the hotel and the driver got out and came around and opened the door.\n\"Here we are, sir,\" he said.\n\"Thanks,\" I said. \"Wait a minute while I get a coat.\"\nWe turned up the warm, dark, soft, thickly carpeted stairs. At the end of the corridor a man in a dark suit, standing rigidly at attention, had opened the door leading into the big, square room. The driver handed me my bag. I stepped inside. The butler came in and took the bag. The door closed. I looked at the thing. It was a chest of gold and ebony inlays, carved designs on it. It had little gold angles on its feet. A huge thing of carved ebony that had come down from smothered royalty to dukes and such and was aged, and had had a history. Every Grand Duke for a hundred years or more had slept with his hands on that chest of drawers. I looked for secret drawers...", "2_16_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-three in the afternoon</span>, and the sun was making valiant efforts to clear the fog. The fog was thin enough at the head of Rum Row to see Rum Maude, thin enough to see Rum Maude clearly.\nAnd when Rum Maude got its first good look at Rum Freddie skittering at the head, Rum Maude saw how Rum Freddie had maneuvered to cut it off from access to the Atlantic.\nRum Maude saw, too, that Rum Freddie would get to the Atlantic before Rum Maude\u2014that there would be some running and jumping on Rum Freddie\u2019s part.\nWhat Rum Maude saw most clearly was that Rum Freddie would use his running and jumping to trap Rum Maude at the head of Rum Row. That Rum Freddie would then try to bar Rum Maude from access to the Pacific. And, failing in that, would head for the Atlantic ahead of Rum Maude, and be waiting at the European terminals\u2014remaining ahead of Rum Maude, unless Rum Maude could manage absurdly reckless speeds, all the way across the Atlantic and into the Pacific.\nIn other words, Rum Freddie planned to kick Rum Maude off of Rum Row.", "0_2_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past two o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 dropped Pat and me off at the apartment and went off to sleep at a cousin's nearby. I slept till three and got up to look for a bottle, but Roger Blin and Walter E. Minnick, who had driven out from Iowa to see $FIRSTNAMEB3, arrived with a gallon, and a wild party started. I gave all my old football trophies to Minnick to send back to his wife, who was waiting for him back in Iowa. We drank the whole gallon and rolled in the rug. Then we took off our clothes and went dancing down the streets of Denver at three o'clock in the morning, with hundreds of people flowing along to see the show. $FIRSTNAMEB3 weaved through them all, with his leaky grin and flying arms, and suddenly rushed at a window with an incredible wild swing of his fist and smashed the pane -- just to see what it felt like, he said. No one stopped him. Then the same crowd and we all jammed into the Tambourine Tut and Maria's, writhing on the sawdust barefooted, and slim, sinuous Mexican queens danced around us in the darkness and we tried to dance with them, and somewhere a sieben-engined band of Mexicans roared into a number and $FIRSTNAMEB3 did...", "1_13_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-seven in the afternoon</span> when I finally found my attorney\u2019s rented Lincoln in the vast and hideous underground parking lot behind the Las VegasJournal\u2014American. I parked the GTO in the space next to his and walked over, noticing as I did so that all four of its doors were white\u2014hot from sitting in the sun. This was not good, because my attorney had been using the car as his office all week, with the top down\u2014which is dangerous enough in normal Vegas weather, but suicidal if you plan to park the thing in a broiling\u2014hot car lot at the hot\u2014metal edge of Summer.\nUpstairs in the news\u2014room, my attorney was drinking an ice\u2014cold Seven\u2014Up, surrounded by clattering, beeping computers and running copy boys. \u201cBack at work, eh?\u201d I said.\n\u201cFuck no,\u201d he replied. \u201cI\u2019m just killing time till you get back.\u201d He put down his drink and plugged a thick sheaf of papers into the machine in front of him. \u201cI have a terrible confession to make,\u201d he said, punching out the words. \u201cI have a goddamn wire service story on the satellite collision distributed to all our out\u2014...", "2_16_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four nineteen in the afternoon</span>. No more time. I seized the reality detector and waved it like a wand over our suitcase full of gold.\n\u201cWell, fuck it,\u201d I said. \u201cOur work is done here. Let's hit the road before my skull bursts.\u201d We both understood there'd been a parting of the ways, a final parting that we both felt even though we couldn't pin it down. I felt this bone\u2014crushing sadness, but I attributed it mainly to the Whale Episode. I wanted to get as far away from that whale as I could. It reminded me of something I'd left undone.\nWe weren't talking much as my attorney guided the big machine out of town toward the turnpike... . God knows we had enough problems all on our own, handling the wild Exxonrac we'd bought for the week, a car that weighed about six thousand pounds when it was empty.\nIt was somewhere around the turnpike interchange when my heart suddenly froze. And at the same moment my attorney said, \u201cWhat's that?\u201d", "0_12_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-seven p.m.</span> on November second, friends. Keep this in mind. Miss Malig arrived shortly afterward, and this prevaricating Pierce the Vigorous explained to her the conditions under which his life was functioning, along with the newfound knowledge that he was losing his hair and that his eyes were failing and his bowels were shot and that his life was a fanning ember, tossed around an empty fire bellows by an uncaring Heaven.\nNothing recedes like success, Ransom K. Fern had warned him. Take it from me, Clarice, nobody likes a saint, his father had said. Perhaps the saddest part of the tale Pierce told Clarice that morning was that he had managed to acquire a lovely young wife because of his sad little needs, and not despite them. She was, in fact, the perfect mate for him. She had large areas of darkness and insanity in her own soul and didn\u2019t give a damn what anyone thought. She would sleep with a man for money, but he, Pierce, of all men, was the one she chose to sleep with free. There seemed no bottom to her.\nShe would run the post office with her mother, a fearsome old creature named Fern, and Pierce would be able to do whatever Pierce...", "2_16_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. Rum Freddie was now a quarter of a mile from the head of Rum Row. Rum Freddie faced Rum Maude. Rum Freddie drew in his fishing nets. Rum Freddie started up his engines.\nRum Freddie was going to take a runner.\nOn all other runs, Rum Freddie had taken a middle position in Rum Row. He was first to arrive, first to skip out.\nOn this run, Rum Freddie was taking a runner on the very head, a position usually monopolized by Rum Maude.\nRum Freddie took the runner first, because he was the fastest pirate ship of them all. And Rum Freddie was a fast ship because $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had sold his soul to the devil to get her built.\nThe price that $SURNAME1 had paid to the devil was his ownership of Mars, as soon as Martians could get there. The price had seemed exorbitant to $SURNAME1, but the devil had thrown in an excellent bonus. Without spending a penny, the devil had got for himself the ownership of $SURNAME1\u2019s soul for an entire malevolent millennium.", "1_8_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-six in the morning</span> of what was to be, for $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the happiest day of his life. His tenant, Mr. Harald, was about to sail for 64 Jupiter for a tryst with his long-lost daughter.\nThen the robins of Tralfamadore gave Tremulo an evil thought. \"I\u2019ve got to see to it,\" said Tremulo, \"that his tralfamadorian wife doesn\u2019t go with him.\"\nHe hiked down the driveway, crossed the road. He stood,, the door at the bottom of the iron stairs, looking up, at the doorbell. \"Who\u2019s that at the door?\" boomed $SURNAME0\u2019s great voice from within. His words were like a spray of marbles in a great globe, pinging from wall to wall.\n\"NO MA\u2019AM!\" bellowed Tremulo, saluting and gaping at the punctual miracle of his new watch, the hands of which said that the next miracle would not occur for another twenty-eight minutes.\nHe turned, ran.\nHe skipped every third rock.\nThe hands of his watch now said again, more insistently, more baldly, what they had said...", "1_21_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine sixteen in the evening</span> and the RFK Stadium in American City, California, was about to disgorge on to the cement floor of the stadium with a resounding thud the following men: Diego Velasquez; Emory Bundy; Kennon Moody; Wilbur Mercer; the Memory of Maxwell Demon; X $SURNAME1; $FIRSTNAMEB6 Basile; Peter Abbott; Stoney Stevenson; Leon Turpin; Rex. Rex was about to be banged onto the floor of the stadium right behind Kennon Moody. His legs were straight and his helmet was tightly fitted over his head. His eyes were closed. He looked as if he were well sedated.\nAfter Kennon Moody was to come Diego Velasquez. He crouched low, his arms outstretched, bent at the elbows. He inched along the narrow corridor leading to the opening of the field. His face was a twisted mask of contortion.\nRex was last.\nWhen Diego Velasquez reached the field he became invisible to the audience in the multi-colored seats. They saw nothing but white cement.\nThe first person to become visible to the audience on the cement of the field was Emory Bundy. He was dressed in a lotus position.\nThen Kennon Moody appeared, his head down, bounding.", "2_4_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four o'clock in the morning</span>. Nobody had a date, let alone a reception party.\n\"He\u2019ll use the phone,\" somebody shouted. \"Do it now! Try him! He\u2019s an agent for the INS!\"\nThe process server ran for the phone and ripped the earpiece off its hook. The mouthpiece was already out.\n\"Yes,\" he screeched, \"we certainly did pick up a Mr. Robert Dubignon Wells Jr.!\" Then he ran to the door, \"Come on, fellows. We\u2019re all over with here. This man is ours now.\" He danced out the door, fast.\nAquleduct grinned broadly at everybody. \"Man,\" he breathed, \"I sure would like to stay, but I\u2019ve still got 319 more drunks to track down. I\u2019ll be seeing you.\"\nHe ran out.\nThe roar of a large, crazy rush of imitation cops came down the stairs. The disappointed drunks were on their way back to Times Square.\nNow the room was totally empty. All that remained was the pitiful skein of telephone wires and smoked cigarette butts on the floor.", "2_9_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-one in the morning</span>. Simon and the sisters were talking on the steps of the place. All over Earth, other Salts, Salties, and Mars kids hung up wash and carried brooms, shovels, and jackhammers.\n\"That\u2019s just our feeble minds tryin\u2019 to understand what it doesn\u2019t make no sense to try to understand,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB6.\nShe didn\u2019t mean anything impertinent by this remark. It was simply what had sprung naturally into her mind, the first thought that occurred to her after Skip\u2019s pronouncement on regressive painting.\n\"It\u2019s the best we can do, $FIRSTNAMEB6,\" said Skip.\n\"That\u2019s just about right,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB6.\n\"You and your sister want to go into town?\" said Skip. \"And maybe go see the statuary like you been wantin\u2019 to do?\"\n\"That sounds nice,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB6.\n\"Does it?\" said Skip.\n\"Mars has sure got some strange girls,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB6.\n\"You and your sister have lived in some pretty strange places,\" said Skip.", "0_22_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe ship was at rest. Nothing in the heavens or on earth could shake it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was gone.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 stood at the open airlock of the ship. He was sick with Awe.\nThere was so much to be Awe-stricken at.\nGod in His heaven was a fine beginning. God in His heaven was the end of a flying pusherman\u2019s itinerary. God in His heaven was a shipmate who was a pink cloud on a blue spiral staircase, going up, up, up forever and ever. God in His heaven was a crazy thrilling exciting inconceivable something that someone like $FIRSTNAMEB0 could never hope to understand.\nAnd, standing at the airlock of the grounded ship, $FIRSTNAMEB0 drew a deep lungful of the Martian atmosphere, and that atmosphere brought God much closer to $FIRSTNAMEB0 than ever He could have come in the speeding, whizzing racket of the ship.\nThe atmosphere, for example, told $FIRSTNAMEB0 that there was iron in his blood, and did it to let him know he was mortal. And the iron in his blood said something else to $FIRSTNAMEB0. Being iron, and being a part of the soil of Mars, it said that the planet on which Un...", "1_21_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 plugged in his music box. $FIRSTNAMEB0 plugged in his soul. It was all he really wanted, apart from things like sunlight, food, and cigarettes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s one interest, apart from habits, was his desire to fly off into space, away from the cesspool Earth had become.\nThis desire to fly had been stimulated by $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s fantasies about a people called the Titanans. The little poems about the Titanans were not translated for $FIRSTNAMEB0, but he could sense that the Titanans were good people\u2014probably big and strong and brave.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 were taken up to their cubby holes in an anti-gravitation shaft. Cal Andrews, who was bound and gagged in the laundry room, realized that a couple of minutes were going to elapse before anybody could get to him.\nCal was reaching for a rock when he was stopped by the realization that a pair of scissors was lying plainly in view on the shelf beside him. Cal reached for the scissors, found them, got himself bound and gagged with the bedsheet he\u2019d torn.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 reached the top of their shafts, they had their usual conversation. $FIRSTNAMEB1 would drill...", "0_21_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span> before I could sleep with the insistent distortions of the afternoon partying on my mind; the monstrous, hairy d.j., the thousand wiseaz\u2014the meek and angelic Pam, with her two friends, planning to leave town at sixty-five miles an hour...and behind them the maniacal, hairy d.j. hustling his records, chanting.\n\n\t \u201cHis life's work is over; he has nothing more to prove ... the critics will proclaim him the world's foremost animal painter!\u201d Mrs. Clancy's image of the consummately satisfied $FIRSTNAMEB1 was fascinating, but somewhere between Bull Montana's living room and my own playroom I began to doubt it. For one thing, I couldn't imagine the gaunt, bug -eyed horse freak who'd whipped $FIRSTNAMEB3 and me also whipping real horses in practical terms; even making them quiet and taming their explosive souls. Maybe I was wrong. Maybe the answer was painting.\nThrough the open door of the playroom I could see the still form on the couch and hear the even heartbeat. At eleven a change occurred. I heard movements, weight being shifted, a creaking as of springs, a slow moan and then a fitful singing with words: \u201cWait...", "0_5_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 mumbled a few words in his sleep and turned over on his face. He slept with the faith of a five-year-old and the features of a satyr.\nYes, $SURNAME1 had slept for almost two hours\u2014and he would have slept on in the room had not his wife stood outside the door, with a newspaper in her hand, calling out the time every five minutes. At thirty-seven minutes past five, she called out \"five forty-five\" so clearly that $SURNAME1 woke up with a start.\n\"George!\" cried $SURNAME1\u2019s wife.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 sprang out of bed as if eight Cheerful China Boys on his chest had suddenly been replaced by a mattress of sharp thorns. He lit the bedside lamp before rising. Following the puffing, heaving sounds that came to him through the thin floor and walls, he located the John in the next room. He could hear his wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, in there, splashing around like the superintendent of a department store going in to evaluate a faulty cash register.\n$SURNAME1 was terribly frightened. The unpaid bill from the hotel laundry was a thirty-two-dollar item\u2014but the cleaning and pressing of two suits of clothes would cost more than eleven...", "1_10_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt any moment war might be declared by gangsters with atomic bombs.\nThe little skip router would be starting up any time.\nThe new wires were laid, the new equipment installed.\nThe Fissionables would start pouring out any time.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 began to walk slowly toward $SURNAME1. She had left her son standing watchfully under a bench.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEF0 was ten feet from $SURNAME1, she began to scan him wetly, lasciviously, drunkenly. Her breasts were inside her blouse. She seemed to have forgotten, as amateurs sometimes do, that she had them.\n\"Wilbur!\" she said, \"I\u2019ve got something to show you, Wilbur.\"\nShe opened her blouse, threw back the flaps, bared herself to the nipples. \"How about this, Wilbur?\" she said proudly. \"No other woman in the village, I\u2019ll bet, can show you anything like this.\"\n$SURNAME1 looked at the lower-right-hand corner of his watch. The seconds were ticking ahead inexorably.\n\"How about this, Wilbur?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. She pulled her breasts apart suggestively.\n\"It reminds me of something,\" said $SURNAME1...", "1_4_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>. The stale air in $SURNAME0\u2019s mansion had gone down like a column of warm milky gravy. There was a faint but already perceptible tang of ozone.\nHazie, $SURNAME0\u2019s wife, sat on her throne, her head back, her eyes closed, her face serene.\nThe only sounds disturbing the tranquility were the clock\u2019s ting-a-ling, and $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s snoring.\n$SURNAME0 stood at the alcove off the master bedroom, looking in at $FIRSTNAMEB2, his son, who was sleeping there. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was a nasty little brat, and the alcove was simply an area where the powerful exhaust of the air-conditioning system blew. $FIRSTNAMEB2 tossed and turned restlessly in his sleep, and kept smacking his lips from time to time.\nThe walls of the alcove were glass, and a relentless blue spotlight from the driveway shone through. There was an opaque maze of piping over $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s bunk.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s hands were clasped behind his back. His shiny gold suit made a soft crunching sound as he shifted his weight right or left. He seldom bothered to look at some...", "1_5_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. There was a greenish tinny taste in $SURNAME1\u2019s mouth. His head was pounding with blood\u2014too much blood for so small a head. He lifted his hand, touched his head, and then looked at his hand. It was covered with little reddish-yellow globs. He did not have to look into a mirror to know that his nose was bleeding profusely.\nHe tried to stanch the flow by squeezing his nostrils together between thumb and forefinger. When that failed, he took the white Egyptian cotton handkerchief out of the breast pocket of his space suit, sneezed into it\u2014and, instantly, the flow renewed itself.\n\"Oh, God\u2014\" whispered $SURNAME1 through the flow, \"let me sell the damned stock. And how many other awful things would You have me beg You to let me do?\" The stock to which $SURNAME1 alluded was Arizona Rumsey Copper. As it happened, it was the only stock he owned personally.\nHe had been born with shares of it belonging to him by inheritance. The shares had cost him nothing, for estate taxes were paid for him long before he was born. The dividend checks came to him quite voluntarily, every quarter.\nThe last dividend check had been for $...", "2_14_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nBusted Filaments\nHUGH KNEW RESTLESSNESS; understood and sympathized with the yearning for new scenes and faces that prowled in everyone.\nHe had never expected to be immune to the restlessness of man. No space pilot had a right to be so blind. A space pilot, every so often, had to make a terrible flight into space.\nAnd as a flyboy administrator, Hugh discovered that the flight a space pilot had to make wasn\u2019t always the most dangerous flight in the Solar System.\nA flight unrivaled in risk was the Secretary of Space\u2019s flight into a world filled with people.\nThe best-natured people did their best to kill the poor secretary. Secretaries before Hugh had engaged in mass slaughter to save their own hides.\nThousands of medals were given to the secretaries. Medals were given, hoping to soothe the savage secretary. Medals were given while the savage secretary was still screeching like a chattering chimpanzee in a tornado.", "0_19_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-nine in the evening</span>.\nAt ten minutes after five on Monday morning, Salo Nitz was to board a cruiser for Mars.\nThe cruiser had been sent for him by order of the head of the Ganymede Department of the Space Board. Salo was not to report to the cruiser, had not even been told the exact location of its pad, but was to go home, get a good night\u2019s sleep, and show up for the flight with a suitcase the size of a female pompadour box.\nSalo had been ordered not to tell anybody, not even the Magnum Opus, Inc., employees on Ganymede about the flight.\nOld Salo had been ordered not to call Earth or make inquiries of any sort concerning the reason for the flight. He was to arrive on Mars and then be taken to Saturn by special agent of the Space Board.\nThe special agent was already waiting at Saturn.\nOld Salo Nitz was not to question any of this\u2014he was simply to go to bed, take a nap, and trot out bright and early, suitcase in hand.\nOld Salo Nitz trotted out, sure enough, with his pompadour box. He was allowed to say good-by privately to...", "1_4_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four eighteen in the morning</span> of October thirtieth, when I tapped on the window of the sleeping alcove in front of $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Connie's bedroom in New Orleans. He woke up with a start, rubbing the sleep out of his eyes. By four-thirty we were ready and waiting for Molly, Stan Shephard, and Frisco on the corner under a dim streetlight. They arrived in Stan's huge old sedan with the ragged upholstery and fogging windows. Frisco got in back with $FIRSTNAMEB3, who was explaining all his adventures involving the dirty dishes that had appeared at Charlie's sight in South Carolina. I sat in front with Stan, who repeated the famous story about how once on marijuana he drove into a barn and stopped there and went to sleep for two days. Meanwhile more and more lights had come on in the warm, muggy Southern night and more dogs were barking. We were off again. I hacked and coughed as we hit the bad Washington Boulevard traffic warp and made a few wild leaps over red lights that might mean cops lay beyond. Everything was hairy. We talked about everything and nothing and sat winded and exhausted at most red lights. The talk was all $FIRSTNAMEB3 and his stormy scenes at Camille's; and all I can say...", "0_9_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-seven in the morning</span> of Wednesday, November 13, and we were somewhere east of Rocksburg, in the Pennsylvania countryside. Not far ahead of us lay the traffic hell of Easton on the other side of which we could cruise pleasantly down the Delaware to Philadelphia and see the last of this dirty freaking carnival circuit. It had been a bad trip. We were sick of all the people we had to travel with and everything we had to do and all the places we had to sleep. We wanted to get back to our own town and start partying for the big Christmas rush that starts this time of year.\nYeah, thought $FIRSTNAMEB3, and he picked up speed; he began leaning on the gas in order to make time and get back. We were all wondering what our acidhead friends back home were doing now. Probably Tim Gray was still lying in the rug and saying \u201cYes,\u201d although now in a somewhat different tone than at first; probably the girls were all crazy about him; and Jonathan Mao, up in his tea-party room, probably had six new girlfriends to entertain with jitterbug music under hanging Chinese paper lanterns. All of them were preparing things for us when we got back -- special Christmas presents of gasoline to warm our hearts. We couldn't wait to get...", "2_22_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-three in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s soul had a minute and a half to live.\nThe harmonium was humming a tune called \"Dreaming Dreams That Money Can Buy.\" The tune had an undertone of menace that nicely fitted the situation in which $FIRSTNAMEB0 found himself.\n\"You did good,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"Now I tell you the dream I had this afternoon.\" He gazed affectionately at $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"My brother, you ain\u2019t gonna believe it\u2014ain\u2019t no way you could believe it. Like I been sayin\u2019, it\u2019s so good it\u2019s unbelievable.\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"what if we\u2014?\"\nJust then $FIRSTNAMEB0 happened to glance at his wrist watch.\nIt was ten thirty-four.\nAt ten thirty-five, the antenna above $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 was limp.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put his hand on the harmonium. It was going strong, but all the music it made, now, was melancholy music, universal music, unpatriotic music, inhuman music.\nAt ten thirty-five, $FIRSTNAMEB0 heard a sigh above him.", "0_4_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-one in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0, who had been asleep happily for thirty-one hours and was now awake, was in a state that made him almost immune to surprise. He let the dog watch him for a moment, then calmly put his rifle together.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 turned around, aimed at the dog from a distance of a few yards, and pulled the trigger. The rifle didn\u2019t fire. It was painted bright yellow, to avoid accidents.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 put the rifle on the ground and pushed the safety catch with his thumb, restoring the hidden cartridge in the breech. Then he picked up the rifle again.\nIn the firelight, the dog sat motionless, unafraid. The dog listened to the small, soft clickings made by $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s brown, ten-fingered hands as he manipulated the rifle.\nThe dog was waiting for the right moment to leap. A click, a pause, a click, a pause meant the rifle was being reloaded. That was a good time to leap, when the claws and fangs of the dog hung from the human throat only by tickling strands.\nThe dog sat on its haunches, forepaws hanging.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 turned around again, aimed, and this time gently...", "0_0_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-six a.m.</span> We hadn't been to sleep yet. The sunflare was the dawn coming up; it was just barely light enough so we could see across downtown L.A. to the east hills and the sun bouncing like a chunk of hot brass off the screen of a gigantic television camera at the crest of the hills. A news flash. The sun had risen; it was legal to drink beer again. \u201cOh, yes,\u201d cried $FIRSTNAMEB3, giggling. \u201cWe must start up the old car and you must begin to seek new roads under the yellow smoke signifies of the dawn!\u201d We were racing along within a few minutes. I had to run down the street to find accommodation for $FIRSTNAMEF4; I got her a room and came back at the run. In the meantime good $FIRSTNAMEB3 had found a parking space. We leaped in, put down all the cushions for $FIRSTNAMEF4, took her bags and beheld her off, wished her luck and skinned out of there before the cops could come. Leaving the sleeping city to itself, we zoomed off through trackless downtown streets and then dived into the Hollywood Freeway for the razzle-dazzle coast to Santa Monica and ball game. And as we ate our...", "1_8_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-nine in the morning</span> when the Space Wanderer got off the bus. The bus let him off in front of a large wooden building that had a sign on it that said \"UNEARTHING HISTORICAL CENTRAL INTELLIGENCIA HEADQUARTERS\u2014MATTER OF BUTTERS.\"\nAround the building was a flurry of building activity.\nGravity was only fifty-six percent of Earth\u2019s, but the ratio of workers and shanties to materials and equipment was the same as on Earth. There were some obvious differences in the appearance of the worker-shanties. The walls of shanties on Earth were plywood, and that on Mars were some sort of polished brass. Shanties on Earth were built with whatever materials the shanty-builders could beg or steal, and that on Mars were nicely fitted out with prefabricated materials.\nThe Space Wanderer slipped into the frantic activity around the wooden building with hardly a glance from the workers and managers. Shahid, who was the only Martian with a bad case of the identity flip-flop, thought he saw the Space Wanderer slipping in among the workers, but the planet\u2019s incredibly slow rotation, which had only gotten slower as the centuries wore on, soon...", "1_16_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME0\u2019s cubicle in the Museum of Natural History was open to the public at that hour once a week. It was open that day, and the attendance was record-breaking. The point to be made here is that no one looked through the telescope that was mounted on its universal swivel in $SURNAME0\u2019s sitting room. And that no one, seeing good things and evil things through the telescope for the first time, shouted what he saw, begged to be let outside so that he could give the news to his fellow man:\nBy now, one and all would have been in pea jackets, woolen hoods, and skis\u2014\nHuddled on roofs and platforms and the backs of municipal vehicles like those known as sleighs once in a great while in the Southwest.\nNo one entered $SURNAME0\u2019s house on Titan. No one stopped in front of the polished-shining biosphere. No one looked into the biosphere, was tempted to enter, was puzzled or fascinated by its contents.\nNot because the crowds were less enterprising at the end of the Tranterian Era than they are today\u2014but because, very nearly, the crowds could think of nothing but getting home...", "0_0_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1982, a cool night in the Paradise Motel outside of Las Vegas, Nevada. The light show at the space convention in the Las Vegas Convention Center was going full blast, and the sound track of The Wizard of Oz was being piped across the street to the motel through huge speakers. It seemed sweet and appropriate to the mood, because Dorothy was next door in room 16 and strange things were going on in her room too\u2014scarcely audible above the noise over at the show.\nThe room really belonged to Driblette, the film director, but he had been found dead in the shower that morning. He had been the only registered occupant of the room: the others were guests of the hotel. The heavyset woman was a Time reporter named Gale Gonzales; the young man was her lover and bodyguard, a car thief named Metzger. Lang was a magazine reporter named Fox, writing a profile piece on Driblette for a piece called \u201cThe New Cinema: Where It\u2019s At, How It\u2019s Doing, Who\u2019s in It, What to Expect.\u201d A party of the weekend was the dark, extremely handsome, tastefully dressed young man. He claimed to be a restaurant...", "1_3_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>. Two minutes later, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s squad mate at the bottom of the shaft, Maxie, received a pleasant surprise. His name was called out politely by a trumpet, and what the trumpet said was: \"Maxie, we salute you!\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had had the misfortune to attract the attention of the enemy when he fell down on the way to the bottom of the shaft. The enemy had stopped to question him. He had been able to give them only incoherent answers before they bound him.\nMaxie, on the other hand, had managed to get at the pineapple grenade he had taken from Corporal $FIRSTNAMEB1. He had managed to conceal it in his anus. And there it had stayed, unharmed, while he was interrogated.\nNow, here below the shaft, in the dark, thoughts of the pineapple grenade made Maxie calm. The grenade was crammed into his colon like an ingredient in a grotesque breakfast food.\n\"Maxie\u2014\" said the trumpet softly, \"you are about to be sent back up the colorful shaft at the center of the Earth to join your friend $FIRSTNAMEB0. You are going to be sent up with a booby trap that will blow a big hole in any unwelcome visitors. We salute you.\"", "0_11_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nThree minutes later, Salo was joined on the terrace by a Supreme Commander who had just relieved himself of his space suit. This man was fastening an open white raincoat, having just taken a shower.\n\"Good morning, Max,\" said Salo.\nIn spite of his shower and change of costume, the Supreme Commander still looked like something the cat had dragged in. His name was Max Taco, and he had come from a planet that circled the star Betelgeuse.\nGladstone, a moon of Neptune, disposed of no heavier rifles than gas-pressure riot guns\u2014cheap, inaccurate weapons that fired stubby shells filled with a gas identical to the infamous CS\u2014or tear gas. These provided barely enough stopping power to bring down a charging, unarmed g\u2014an animal that would provide the most meager return of all game found on the planet to a hunting strategy based on skill and intelligence. Nevertheless, it was exceptionally dangerous to fire a gas-pressure weapon of any kind into the air over an area containing a million Sklerns.\nThere were nine million Sklerns in Pu-239, the city whose outskirts were being patrolled by the Expeditionary Force platoon. There were fifty million Sklerns...", "1_3_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-seven in the morning</span>. I went back to the room, woke up my girl and made her pack, telling her we were leaving. She protested that it was too late and too dark to go, but finally she fell in with my raging sense of urgency. I hurriedly fixed up a story for the girl in room 308 concerning the loss of my wallet and gave her a quarter for the phone, telling her to call a friend whose name and number were written on the margin of the Gideon Bible. She still protested. This was no way to treat a lady, she said. I told her to call it a loan; if I made money some day I would repay her ... she said that would be the day. What kind of a life was this she asked? Running around all hours of the night and getting thrown out of hotel rooms, with no hint of romance or decency? I said I was sorry but I couldn't help it. I had a sick aunt in Seattle and no money, and my car had broken down and I had to get home. What choice did I have, Judge? She broke into tears and undulated her body back and forth in the bed, and I rushed out to Pasadena to get another room. The room clerk, who was the same smilin...", "1_0_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had an appointment with a man in space at thirteen minutes after one. $FIRSTNAMEB0 would show up exactly on time, like a good soldier. And what he would get for being exactly on time would be a new body\u2014the one piece of luck for $FIRSTNAMEB0 that the Great Disney had seen fit to provide.\nAnd then, at one-thirteen, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was supposed to put his new body to use in the most important task a soldier ever gets\u2014the capture of aPost K deepest-deep space station.\nSitting on the latrine platform was poor little old $FIRSTNAMEF1, snoring peacefully.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 finally got his harmonium out of its box. He got it out with shakings and curses, wrenches and prayers. The harmonium\u2019s three clumsy feet got tangled in the accordion bellows at the bottom of the box-armature.\nThe box-armature came apart, and with a clang the bellows fell through the moon deck to the iron floor below.\nAnd when the bellows had fallen, the harmonium found itself disemboweled. A series of flat metal disks fell out of it\u2014flat disks that had formed the harmonium\u2019s spring.\nThe harmonium...", "1_10_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-eight in the morning</span>. The first shots of the general assault were fired ten minutes later. The objective of the Assault Force was the $SURNAME0 mansion itself. The house was lightly held at that point\u2014a skeleton crew of twelve. These were the twelve easiest of the twenty-four Space Wanderer replacements to find.\nThe twelve were killed without letting a single call for help get through to friends outside the wall.\nA police dog in the Solarium was also killed without a yelp.\nThe gong that had summoned the twelve died with them.\nThe gong had been rung by Salo, the first of the mechanical servants to come under fire.\nSalo continued to minister to the master and mistress on the sunporch.\nFive minutes after the attack began, a one-man reconnaissance plane came nosing over the trees on the north end of the estate. The pilot of the plane was Skip, who had ejected himself from a Titan II missile three hours and fifty-seven minutes earlier.\nHe was driving a ship that belonged in a museum. It had been rotting there since 1947.\nIt was a Cue-Ball Wizard, the first American space ship.\nSkip brought the Wizard down in the croquet-lawn, between the goal...", "0_8_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-six in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1 took his parking ticket from the machine and looked at the ten lines of type on the stub.\nThree of the lines were now blank.\n$SURNAME1 looked up at the blue sky and put his hands over his eyes.\nThe ascenders and descenders began to play again, and $SURNAME1 shook his head to clear it.\n\"Phee-no-mite-sis\u2014shit,\" said $SURNAME1 to himself. He felt for his comb in his coat, found it tangled, fingered it dispiritedly. His fingernails were two inches long, and were broken and dirty.\n$SURNAME1 straightened his tie and squared his shoulders. He tried to dust the seat of his trousers and discovered he was wearing slip-on shoes.\nHe looked at his shoes for a moment. They were covered with mud. So were his pants legs.\n$SURNAME1 rubbed the mud from his shoes, got mud all over his hands. He started toward the office. There was nothing to be done but go in, because he had not slept in it all night.\nAs $SURNAME1 started toward the office, the man behind the steam press released the foot-control and allowed the huge machine to roar to the floor.\nThe blind man, $SURNAME1\u2019...", "2_23_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to midnight</span> on a Saturday night in October.\nAt five minutes to midnight, the time about which a family legend would for all time converge, grandfather\u2019s clock in the parlor downstairs bonged five times, indicating that its hands were frozen at five minutes to midnight on a Saturday night in October.\nThe mother could hear nothing but clocks. She knew that clocks told time differently than human beings, that what to a clock was an eternity was to a human being no more than the brief span of a comma, an blinking parenthesis, to be said and quickly done in a lifetime\u2019s syntax and be out of the way.\nChirpings and trepidations of time agitated and plagued the mother. She was beginning to fade out.\nSo the story goes.\nAt a quarter to midnight, the mother expired, peacefully, and the father went downstairs to reset grandfather\u2019s clock by making the hands click to twelve, and then he went back up to bed. He had been downstairs eleven minutes.", "0_21_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-six in the evening</span> of March twenty-first. $SURNAME1, at the controls of the Space Wanderer, was preparing to land at midnight. He would be on schedule when he landed. He hadn\u2019t even lost time circling Mount Rushmore.\nHis course, incidentally, was putting him right through the center of San Francisco.\nUp above the ship, woofing away, were a billion pinpricks of brilliance. The pinpricks would grow into dazzling domes of light. $SURNAME1 was too far away to see their shapes.\nBut their shapes were all different. No two domes up above were alike in size or shape.\nBelow $SURNAME1 and his ship, the Western Hemisphere of the earth went on and on in all directions. The oceans were blue paste under the pinpricks. Islands were dull rough flesh.\nAbove and below, the Universe of tremendous pinpricks whirled and turned without end.\nIt was all indifferent to $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s phonograph and to the silent mate, Mrs. $SURNAME0, and to their ships, and to their chronometers and the return of footsteps to Concord. Unquestionably, it was all far more eloquent of infinity than were $SURNAME0\u2019s 12,000 words...", "1_17_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October 19, A.D. 2525, according to the Gregorian calendar then in use.\n(One thousand years had passed.)\nAt that particular moment, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was approaching the ruins of a once serene and beautiful stone building. A sign over the ruins read BARRACKS. Other signs nearby read GARRISON CHAPEL and HEADQUARTERS. The ruin of the chapel belched smoke and gas that smelled like rotten eggs.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 trod gingerly over smoldering ground.\nThe whole thing had happened very quickly. The attack had come without warning. It had begun with a single shot from a stone rifle, and had ended with the destruction of the chapel. It had been a one-sided affair\u2014if the one doing all the launching and the one receiving all the launches can be said to have merits in a situation like that.\nThe one had been $FIRSTNAMEB0, and the one attacked had been hundreds of the little green soldiers advanced positions. These soldiers were sometimes green rifle-wielding dwarfs, and sometimes brown mortar-wielding midgets. At the instant in question, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been surrounded by a screaming pack of the midget soldiers, who had fired volley after volley at $FIRSTNAMEB0...", "2_22_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-six in the evening</span>. The place was the planet Venus. It rained perpetually in the vicinity of the White House of the laughing Gods, in which Farvar and Urp lived with their mother, Bat Durston.\nFarvar and Urp stood in the rain with the handles of their knives wet and uncomfortable in their small hands.\nThey were looking at the White House. Their faces were hopeful, hopeful with the light of love and a desire to serve their mother.\nUnder the rain their uniforms were wet. The uniforms consisted in part of woolen stockings and wooden clogs, which had been covered with vegetable-dyed cloth by Bat Durston.\nThe uniforms also consisted in part of two knotted cords around the boys\u2019 necks.\nThe knots in the cords were tight against the flesh of Farvar and Urp.\nTheir fingers went from knot to knot, tightening and relaxing under the rain, under the lights of the news helicopters that hovered in the rainy air.\nThe boys\u2019 faces showed no discomfort, showed no awareness of the pressure on their necks.\nTheir faces were lovely.", "2_8_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-one in the morning</span>. In $SURNAME0\u2019s fleet in space, the time was one hour and fifty minutes later. In the great hall of the palace of Salo, $SURNAME0 stood before his assembled supplicants, ready to give his three and a half minute lecture.\nOne by one, the valet relieved the supplicants of their portable timepieces.\n\"Thank you very much,\" said $SURNAME0, pressing one of his narrow yellow hands over the intake and exhaust of his breathing passage. Humming cheerfully, he placed the timepieces in two rows either side of him on a long marble table.", "2_9_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Sunny,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Nice and sunny.\"\n\"What\u2019s the weather like where you are, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said Sergeant $SURNAME2. The question wasn\u2019t asked for reasons of meteorology. Weather on Mars was always the same. The question was asked as a kind of code. It meant: How do you feel, $FIRSTNAMEB0? Are you all right?\n\"Glorious, thank you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. It meant: I feel so good that I could whistle like a thrush if I had any use for whistling.\nSergeant $SURNAME2 relaxed then, released a pent breath that he had been holding for three minutes.\nHe had been holding his breath for three minutes, watching $FIRSTNAMEB0 pile the seven blue bags onto the iron filing cabinets, then seeing $FIRSTNAMEB0 spread his gossamer-muscled legs so comfortably, settle back so restfully against the wall. For three minutes, Sergeant $SURNAME2 had been assessing $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014had been reaching out toward $FIRSTNAMEB0 with the senses of golden-haired, baby-smooth-cheeked, corn-fed, clean-voiced, clear-eyed youth.", "2_5_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. If I had figured it right, it was the witching hour. And so it was....That was when all hell broke loose on the floor and a whole bunch of the derelicts went berserk and got up on their feet and beat at their empty heads with both hands and howled and yelled obscenities and threw themselves against the walls and made noises enough to wake anybody up. Five minutes after that, twenty minutes after she first came in, the door flew open and the black waitress from the coffee shop came rushing in. Now she seemed to have on almost a complete outfit, including shoes and stockings. She was yelling at the top of her voice: \u201cYou goddam dirty old man! You goddam dirty old man! You're all sick in the head, you know that?\"", "0_10_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the intersection of Colfax Avenue and Broadway in downtown Denver. Colfax Avenue is the Main Street of Denver, leading from the mountains to the prairies and from the prairies to the mountains. It is old U.S. Highway 40 and a few miles east of there it crosses the Kansas line; in my time a fellow could make himself a freebooter of the road by hopping a westbound freight at the old Kansas border station of West Concord and holding out for the first forty-mile whistle-stop beyond Colfax. The road leaves Denver behind well before that \u2014leaving behind the cowtown fringe and its railroad-graveyard too\u2014and climbs the old hill via Speer, curving around the Harvard Drug Company Building, a structure that started as an apartment house and became a home for pet bulls and dwarfs, then past the stone-silent houses of Congress Park and on up around the curve by the old Statler Hotel and its merry-go-round horse entrance, still merry and still going around when I was last there some four years ago. In my time the Statler was closed for remodelling. It is now reopened as the Hilton. Across the street is the old Tabor Grand Opera House. It is still...", "0_19_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-one in the evening</span> when I knocked on $FIRSTNAMEF3\u2019s door. He shook hands with me and gave me a light square jacket to wear and a pair of white canvas shoes, very soft and old and worn in places.\n\u201cOld Cohn\u2019s down with the gout, too. We don\u2019t want you to strain anything. No saluting and no running.\u201d\nDownstairs the Madame nodded and smiled and called us \u201cmes enfants.\u201d We were her favorites. She had never had any like us.\nThere was a fiddler from Navarre, another from Scotland, and a Basque from the mountains. They were all veterans in their thirties. They were sitting in the lounge and did not get up, but grinned and asked me how I felt in French.\n\u201cThe last night,\u201d the Madame said. \u201cDieu! How many years? One never lasts.\u201d\nThey kept all the records in her bead-bulled, leather-bound album, written in her clear script: all the fine tugs, the good leaps, and the champions. She knew the parents and grandparents of all her Gordons. She had even kept in touch with some of the customers who had...", "1_3_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-two in the morning</span>. I sat up and yawned. Then I looked out the window at the church across the street. Suddenly I began laughing. I had been here before; this was where I came with Henry and Victor one night. They wanted to go to mass in this particular church because they had heard there was some kind of wizard here that performed miracles. They found him in the basement, and there was a line of many sinners waiting to talk to him. We were in that line for a long time. And what was he doing? Practising psychiatry without a license? Something peculiar, because he merely listened to everybody's troubles, then pronounced a few mumbo-jumbo phrases, and everybody left with a new sense of exultation and self-satisfaction.\n\u201cI guess it's the same thing,\u201d I said to myself as I got out of bed and went to the door, \u201cwhether it's a blow job or religious conversion, it's all the same. One sudden jolt and it's all humming; you know where you are, where to find enough sugar to last you until the next time.\u201d But the next time the sugar's gone and you have the job of hunting up another witch doctor to kill the pain with...", "2_15_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We went by cab to the offices of the Southern Rocky Mountain Construction Company on Pearl Street, walked in, and found proprietor Henri to be out. So we flopped down on his leather davenport and fooled around waiting for him. Henri came in presently, seeming very amiable, and sat around talking shop with $FIRSTNAMEB3 and me. Possibility of work was discussed; the coming war was discussed.", "2_5_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. Outside, the dawn was grey.\n23. Tales of the Road...The Acid Test...and a Drunken Lizard...Wiped Out and Worried\u2014Trip into the Red Light District...The Main Chance\u2014Melody and Madness...Black Cross on the Red Ball...Laughing Jam With James Merrill...Stockhausen! She Was Already Taking Off Her Clothes...Mona Lisa Smiled...Miskatonic University...Nightmare on Elm Street\u2014\u201cGoddamn your evil souls!\u201d I shouted, \u201cget away from here!\u201d They were all laughing at me. I felt like grabbing one of those bastards and stomping his scaly ass into the fireplace, but my arms and legs were completely entangled in the sheets, I couldn't move. I grunted and moaned. They were laughing at my pain, those evil dragon bitches...\n\u201cNot yet,\u201d I muttered. \u201cI've not quite finished breakfast.\u201d I was still holding my grapefruit in one hand. I looked down at it and wonder if I had ever really been holding it or if I only thought I was holding it. The acid was putting a vicious vibrato on everything.", "0_10_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-nine in the morning</span> when I found myself on drugstore binge in Jackson. I wanted paregoric for my infected gut. Old worries about narcotics cops and federal agents pulled me into a shacklike drugstore. My hands shook; I couldn't read the label on the bottle... ten forty-nine in the morning, and here I was buying paregoric instead of eating. Ah, the road, the furies. For several days I had no thought in my head except getting to the Southern Pacific station in San Francisco four hours before the midnight Chief for Los Ange-. les. I loped and dodged over rooftops and finally crawled down to the station by a firepole when I heard the train whoo hollering and saw the red lights coming. I crawled into the restroom of the Pullman and slept through Georgia and most of Alabama . . . I woke up just in time to see the porter taking reservations for dinner at the club table. A fat florid businessman in a business suit and vest was saying, \u201cMy name is Sabini; I own all the ice in Montana and most of Oklahoma, and the time has come to invest in real estate in Los Angeles, sonny.\u201d Suddenly I realized that I was broke, because of a...", "1_4_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four nineteen in the morning</span>. The falling bombs were red and yellow dots throbbing low on the southern horizon.\nThey saw the rockets rising, saw and heard them.\nThe first line of rockets reached the top of their trajectory and reversed their thrust with perfect precision, falling curiously upside down and swaying from side to side like pendulums.\nA second line of rockets took up the arc, became pendulums themselves, reached the apex of the swing, stopped, and then reversed their thrust and fell with the first line. The two lines of rockets intersected neatly at the apex.\nA third and a fourth.\nA fifth.\nAnd then any given section of the sky was covered by as many as a dozen or more intersecting launching pendulums of fire, exploding, furiously swaying their heads, red and yellow and white and green.\nWhen, finally, the last rocket had thrust itself into the last pendulum, leaving the sky in tact, it began again immediately. Other rockets leapt into view to repeat the performance.\nThe notes of the mindless music were drawn out infinitely.\n\"That\u2019s all they do?\" said $SURNAME2. \"Tore-up the sky?\"\n\"It\u2019s only the beginning,\" said Rumfo...", "1_3_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nTheir ship was a tramp steamer named $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s Bisbee $FIRSTNAMEF1. The ship was broken down and dispirited, her captain and crew maltreated and underfed. The crew, like all space crews, consisted mostly of volunteers. The standard equipment for the crew of a ship like $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s Bisbee $FIRSTNAMEF1 was a space suit, a made-on-Earth Bible, and a pair of low-powered binoculars.\nThe Bible was to be opened to a random page before take-off, and whatever it said on that page was supposed to be a good or bad omen for the voyage. The captain of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEF1 had opened the book to the forty-third Psalm, and he had been lucky indeed, because the Psalm began: Why am I aggrieved, and why is my soul sorrowful? And why is my heart sad, o Fleshy God? $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEF1 had taken off, it had reached space, it had got up to seventeen thousand feet. Then the engines had faltered and died, and the ship had plummeted, nose first, in the worst and bitterest cold.\nThe warning system\u2014an engine bell turned by an electric...", "2_22_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten seventeen in the evening</span> on Mars. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 and the soldiers were about to hear a speech by the Replacement of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, who had the loudest and most beautiful speaking apparatus of any human being in the Solar System. It was rumoured that the Replacement\u2019s speaking appar at the palace in Alexandria, Egypt. The hall in the palace was much more wonderful than any other speaking venue in the Solar System. It was called the Crystal Ballroom, and every piece of crystal in it was clear, was twelve feet in diameter. The overhead canopy of the Ballroom was strung with hundreds of silken lines on which hundreds of crystal chandeliers swung. The dais on which $SURNAME0 spoke was octagonal, was encased, wall to wall, in a cube of purest sulfur.\nThe walls of the Ballroom were hung from a square truss at the fork of the square truss by no less than seventy spans of a single transparent material. The material was a silicate of germanium, beryllium, lead and tin. The manufacturer, who had had no idea of the purpose for which the material would be used, had named it Solarium.", "2_9_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-five in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, chain-attached to a stake at stage-left, looked at the magnificent organ before him. It was a beautiful piece of carpentry\u2014strong, compact, colorful. It was made of fruitwood, with ivy embellishing the keyboards and tentacles adorning the bellows. The beast of the pipes was stunted and placid, no more than eighteen inches high, with a roughly spiral-shaped tooth-mashed face.\nThis pipe-organ was a twenty-tone instrument.\nThe thing that had to be imagined in order to play an organ of this type was an Eskimo on an iceberg. No Eskimo would willingly remain on an iceberg. The Eskimos would, however, nail down their organ and play it on an icy floe while they hunted walrus or polar bear on the ice pack around them. They would pick out their favorite hymns to carry them through the long, dark freezing nights.\nThe organ before $FIRSTNAMEB2 was waterproof. It hadn\u2019t been nailed down, but it would play quite well help up in one hand.", "1_19_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was the forty-sixth floor of the Space Ministry Building. The man was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. He had just completed more than two thirds of his tunnel down the length of his century. His tunnel was thirty feet long, and extended from the year 1934 in the past to a point eighty-seven years in the future.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was as chipper and untroubled as a drill sergeant. He was as clean as a whistle. He was clad in skintight blue European clothes that revealed the perfections of his physique and announced the powers of his Malachim self. He wore white tennis shoes, and was never seen in anything else. Even when sleeping, even when showering, even when making love.\n$SURNAME0 sat back in an office chair with a six-foot wingspan, tilted it back on two legs, crossed his hands prayerfully over his navel. A breakfast tray on his lap held two wafer-thin pancakes, a pat of butter, a maraschino cherry, and a small vial of tomato juice.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s eyebrows were an exact replica of the eyebrows of Ambrose the Black Boy, his family chrono-synclastic...", "2_22_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span> in the nation of North Carolina.\nAt eleven o'clock sharp, high over the land mass of Eurasia, in the air over the nation of Russia, Benedict and company called Ishmael in his national Pentagon in Washington and got no answer.\nAnd at that very instant Ishmael was turning his motorcycle off Route 3 at Cheyenne, Wyoming, onto Route 16 for Beulah, Nebraska.\nIn the town of Cheyenne still boarded up after the big Bombardment, there was the crash of a motorcycle within the echoing emptiness at thirty-seven minutes after eleven o'clock, February 10th.\nAt nineteen minutes to midnight, somebody would chuckle, in the boys' lavatory at the National Music Center in Cincinnati.\nMidnight, Greenwich time.\nIn London and in Liverpool, dark.\nAnd at midnight in London, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog were materialized a safe two hundred feet in the air.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog were materialized in the maze of sand dunes in which $SURNAME0\u2019s palace had stood.", "1_4_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-five in the morning</span> of December 24, 1972. I waited in the car while Cosmic Kev went into the Army recruiting office to flirt with the girls in the nice dress section. He was gone for fully half an hour --a terrible ordeal this must have been for him, because he was extremely shy with girls and I never saw him breathe easier than after he came out of that place, saying, \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6 epil viginism $FIRSTNAMEB6 epil virgin\u201d over and over, his heart flying. He got in the car soddenly.\nNow we were ready to hit the Strip. Our first place of call was Roy\u2019s Lucas Tavern, on Sahara, just off the Strip...but before I tell you about that, I should say a few words about the Mint Gun Club, which had to do with the whole Great Renonothing.\n2. The Mint Gun Club--A Drive to the Desert and the Army on Christmas EveWith the failing of the American Dream in the mid-Sixties, Las Vegas fell into deep despair; the desert winds blew black dust over the neon and it was said that the end was nigh for the good times of The Strip. Local prophets in beer halls began to say that the town was a rootless place with no past to...", "1_22_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span> and Metzger drew the curtains closed, softening the sounds of traffic in St. Mary's Square below. The sound system came on.\nFor perhaps three minutes he fiddled compulsive, codelike with dials, making minute readjustments, while the sound system, with state\u2014of-the-art fidelity, gave them Bach's Suite No. 2 in B flat, BWV 1067, played on a violin made in 1714 by Guarnerius del Ges\u00f9.\nThen he joined them.\nIn St. Mary's Square that night, lines of traffic snarled, moved, stopped for red lights that seemed for all the world to turn yellow just as the lead cars neared them.\nWith the sound system going full bore, Metzger danced with $FIRSTNAMEF2, trying variations on the Wrist-lock, the Cripple Creek, the Monkey Grip, the Wogco Mule Kick, and suddenly, the Swivel Grab, his shirt open to the waist, revealing a chest as hairless and white as the belly of a fish. She learned how to slide her feet along the floor and keep her upper body in one spot while her legs went marching around her. She suffered perhaps...", "2_17_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-one in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were getting into silken flying togs of a sort, with helmets and masks and air-conditioned gills at the ready. They were getting ready to go skydiving. At thirty-two-hundred feet, their fall would be stopped, and would be checked for two minutes, and would then be allowed to fall again for two minutes, and so on.\nAt four-thirty, the couple were to skydive for the first time. At fourteen-thirty and twenty-seven every day thereafter, they were to skydive again.\nThe reason for the daily skydiving was that a statement was to be made every day, was a truth about the history of the Universe that was greater than any truth uttered by any planet\u2019s historian.", "2_8_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThe time was ten minutes shy of Solar Noon.\nNewton reported to his mistress at ten minutes before Solar Noon with his watch in his hand. \"Sorry to keep you waiting, ma\u2018am,\" he said.\nThe woman by the circular window looked and saw that it was only four minutes to Solar Noon, that she had kept him waiting for four minutes.\n\"That\u2019s all right, Newton,\" she said. \"I\u2019m early, anyway.\"\nShe had bathed, perfumed, powdered, and garbed herself in an incredible assortment of Bohemian frocks. They were on a variety of wooden hangers scattered around the room.\n\"Here\u2019s your watch, ma\u2019am,\" said Newton, and laid it on an end table, a neat package of blue enamel and gold parts.\n\"You may go, Newton,\" said the woman. \"Thank you.\"\n\"Yes\u2014ma\u2019am,\" said Newton, and he left the suite.\nThe wom an looked at her watch. It was exactly ten minutes to Solar Noon. She laid her watch down beside that of Newton.", "0_17_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. I had promised to meet the attorney and his wife in an hour, at six, in the bar of the Flamingo. The earliest I could get there would be seven . . . four hours to fill in.\nAnd then it came to me. What I had to do. I remembered my attorney giving me the address of his friends, in case I should need somebody to help me out in a hurry. \u201cJohnny Dio,\u201d he said. \u201cYou have to watch him, because his brain dries up on coke\u2014but he\u2019s the best Dr. in Vegas.\"\nAnd I knew the telephone number by heart. I picked up the receiver and dialed. A maid answered.\n\u201cIs Mister and Miz Di Obstampolos in, please?\u201d I asked in my best imitation of Robert Montgomery.\n\u201cWho may I say is calling?\"\n\u201cDick Maple.\u201d\n\u201cMah name is Angie.\u201d\n\u201cAngie, this is the fella that Mister Di Obstampolo ast me to call up if I needed to hep out any.\u201d\n\u201cOh, yes! Mister Maple! I\u2019ll go see if Mister...", "0_21_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-five in the evening</span>.\nProfessionally, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was a thriller writer. Always in need of money, he had turned to writing thrillers six months before. He wrote them under the pseudonym of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $SURNAME0.\nA $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill rumfoord was a thundering curse. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 felt that he understood $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill perfectly when he wrote under the name of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $SURNAME0. Nothing pleased $SURNAME1 more than to imagine himself irascible, tenacious, bull-headed, and profane, just like Churchill.\nIsn\u2019t it a living dum\u00addum? $SURNAME1 asked himself derisively. A man who\u2019s five feet four, with brown eyes and a par\u00adtiallyScandinavian name, asks himself if he isn\u2019t a grand figure of a man like Churchill.\nThe irony of the moment did not escape $SURNAME1. \"Chief Boohoo,\" said the message which $SURNAME1 was supposed to write under his $FIRSTNAMEM1 $SURNAME0 pseudonym, \"death may be your friend. It may deliver you from a numbered life which has ceased to be profitable or satisfying.\"\nThere stood $SURNAME1 before his government-issue typewriter, his fountain pen absent-mindedly in his hand, staring out at the dark purple shadows under...", "0_16_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span> for Earth, and Alvin $SURNAME0 had no bride, no particular place to go, no particular thing to do. The spiral had carried him to the year 2005, and he was washing the windows of his stockade in a steady drizzle that was guaranteed not to wash anything away.\nThe stockade was among cardons and mangroves ten yards from the Newport shore. The lightposts of $SURNAME0\u2019s estate were still strung with yellow crime-scene tape.\nThis tape asserted that something unspeakably foul and sad beyond human understanding had happened there on November 5, 1953. The estate\u2019s heliport was still underwater.\nAt Marblehead, on the estate\u2019s main gate, $SURNAME0 had left a brass plaque:\nTHIS ESTATE WAS THE PROPERTY OF\nHIS SANCTITUDE DR. ALVIN CENTRUM RUMFOORD,\nTHE TWO-HUNDRED-AND-TWENTY-SEVENTH DALAI LAMA OF ALL\nTHE RELIGIONS.\nTHE ESTATE IS A MUSEUM\nIt was four feet by eight.\n$SURNAME0 could, by pushing a lever on a control panel in his gaz...", "1_21_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-eight in the evening</span>.\n* See Sic Trimobiles, by Major Monogram. Little, Brown & Company, Boston, 1961.\n* See \u201cGeronimo\u201d and his pickup band, passing the word. Warner Records, WN-50239. Billy Barf and His Ozark Mountain Daredevils.\n* Oh, say, did you know the first printing of this book contained the following paragraph: \u201cMonty hung his head. His forehead wore a brief frown. Had he heard this young child rightly? Unable to control his curiosity\u2014 fueled, perhaps, by the bit of fish he had eaten\u2014he raised his eyebrows and rotated his head until he faced his young dinner guest, who was quietly downing coconut pie all innocent-like. The old psychiatrist smiled. He put his hand on the boy\u2019s shoulder. \u201cYou didn\u2019t let Dieter see you g happy?\u201d he said, in a voice not unlike that of the boy who\u2019d just sold him the fish.\n\u201cThe boy shrugged the hand off his shoulder. His smile disappeared. \u201cThat old bag of shit?\u201d he said. \u201cI hope somebody shoots his stupid ass.\u201d\nSo what else...", "2_19_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nNormal Mailer had made a pot of tea with plenty of sugar, and it stood all afternoon on a table near the couch upon which Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 nestled.\nAt five minutes to eight o\u2019clock, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 limped over to the table, put a cigarette in her mouth, and lit it with a current of her breath.\nAt eight o\u2019clock, the richly smiling, richly skirted form of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 materialized again. This time, the form was voluminous, and it was floating near the far wall of the chamber, as though it were being projected from a point near a wall outlet. The form took on weight and definition very gradually, became a $SURNAME0 again by degrees.\nThe three sensitive-looking young men and women who had been standing near the door ever since $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 arrived were now joined by the young man who had been using the ankle phone. The young man was wearing pumps, black socks, and a full-length mink coat. His hands were fists inside the thick white mink sleeves.", "1_11_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until noon</span>.\nAt forty-nine seconds before noon, Redwine made the last sale of his life.\nHe sold his own life very dearly.\nAt exactly twelve minutes before twelve in the afternoon, he made his last investment. He put his last remaining cent into Par Maianski, the pipe-fitter from the shipyard grotto.\nPar Maianski did not know how lucky he had been. He was drunk.\nHe was three-quarters of the way through a quart of vodka\u2014the only liquid that his stomach was able to handle. It had been a hell of a week, ticking around on the bottom of the hull of a ship while they made a record 500-mile run to Mars without taking on ballast.\nMaianski was glad to be back on Earth. He was drunk while the ship made its record hop from Mars to Earth, and he was so happy to find himself on Earth again that he spent his last fifty cents on an awful bottle of bourbon. He finished that, and had started on the vodka when he arrived at the ad hoc bar.\nThe liquor had smoothed out what the trip had cratered. He felt outstandingly good. He threw virtuously horrid loops with the bottles. He staggered...", "0_0_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and Salo set the flight-mass of the Emperor of Earth on a pillow of air and floated it over the city. Up went the walls and houses under the Emperor, forming a Gothic framework. The haggard, unbrushed, uncombed, uncombable man himself was lofty, too, with dignity and sovereignty.\nUnbound by wires or hinges, with $FIRSTNAMEB2 and Salo for only attendants, the Palace of the People now floated free to drift with the winds of the Earth through its Solar system.\nIt was to be kept clean and bright as long as it stayed aloft. And any Earthling who wished was free to take a hand broom to help keep it that way.\nUnbound by walls or pillars except for the ones the wind put there, the Palace of the People was a more inspiring sight from below than it could ever have been from the inside.\nThere was room for ten thousand Earthlings aboard the Palace of the People. And there was food for ten thousand Earthlings aboard, too. No more, no less. Not having any barter-gold to speak of, the Newport Reds had to depend on Earth\u2019s natural foodstuffs for their nutrition.\nTen thousand Earthlings constituted a good...", "2_9_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-nine in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, wandering in a great and mounting terror, had been in the church shelter for eight hours.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his son $FIRSTNAMEB2 had gone to bed at nine o\u2019clock the morning before. They had spent the night in sleeping bags spread over army surplus guide mats on the floor. They were getting more and more into the habit of going to bed at night and rising in the morning.\nThere were quilts at the foot of the wall, under which they put their feet when they went to bed at night. There were benches and lamps, on which they ate when they went to bed at noon.\nAnd now $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his son were going to bed again. $FIRSTNAMEB0, alarmed and agitated, wondered what calamity had befallen the world since he and his son had gone to bed the day before. He wondered what calamity would befall the world before he and his son went to bed the following day.\nThey both fell asleep at once.\nThe harmonium telephone on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chest jangled harshly.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 awoke slowly, wondering what calamity could have befallen the harmonium telephone on his chest to make it ring like that. It jangled again.", "1_1_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time. Years later, in another time, another palace, another sick room, $SURNAME0 would say again to his unseen audience: \"Nine minutes to two.\"\nHe would say it in secret, and the words would get out, would be taken up as the catch-phrase of another faith.\n$SURNAME0 did not know, then, that he would repeat the catch-phrase. He did not know that he would die shortly before the catch-phrase became his own.\nA band of men streamed in through the open door of the palace. They carried limp body bags, and their attitude was listless.\nVictor was among the men.\n$SURNAME0 looked up, saw his son. \"How many more minutes, Victor?\" he said.\n\"Nine, Skip,\" said Victor. He bore the stigmata of leadership in the hopeless assault\u2014his clothes were torn and bloody, and his hands were cut, snuffed. Blood ran onto his shoes. His left eye was winking spasmodically, that means the muscle had lost its tone.\n\"Louder,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Nine, Skip,\" said Victor.\n\"Thank you,\" said $SURNAME0.\nHe lay still...", "0_11_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-three in the morning</span>. It was Thursday, April twenty-first. Concurrences. $FIRSTNAMEF1 had already refused breakfast and started lunch by the time I stumbled off to find Shicklgruber\u2019s Brotlaiter in a rowdy little party of other Oktoberfest tourists led by an older man with a green plaid hat. The German was being given due respect for suggesting the hike to a confidential montage of hilltops behind the city. The peak we had to climb was steep and rough. Eventually I developed a technique of pushing myself up by grabbing tree branches, swearing every time my fingers came in contact with a thorn. My official Oktoberfest shoes were more of a liability than the thornbushes. I kept imagining I was wearing a sign that said kicked, scraped, and stomped by the Hithsh regional rep, manufacturer of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s boots. This didn\u2019t win me any groans or sympathy from the Germans and Canadians around me, who were also wincing every time they had to shove a foot into a wet hole in a rock. I landed in more wet holes than anybody else. Life is sometimes that way when you have the right shoes. The Canadians, realizing I was an American by, among other things,...", "0_12_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after noon</span> when $SURNAME1 was unshackled and went out of the ship. He was allowed, after Bayards had gone, to choose among the weapons in the arsenal.\nHe chose a shotgun that had only one barrel. He wasn\u2019t up to killing people.\nThen he went out to his driveway, where the taxi waited for him.\nThe taxi driver was a tall, strong, pleasant-faced old gentleman named $SURNAME0. He wore the livery of the Newport Tennis Club. He had a pale-green hat and a powder-blue coat and carrying-clothes of gold brocade. \"Darling?\" he said to $SURNAME1 as he opened the door of the cab for $SURNAME1, \"would you please step out of that contraption? When $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 asks you to ride with him, you don\u2019t say no.\"\n$SURNAME1 got into the passenger side of the cab. \"Where were you dropped?\" said $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME1 told him. \"Where the hell do you live?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"The hell?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Newport,\" said $SURNAME1. He gave $SURNAME0 the address.\n\"I know it,\" said $SURNAME0. He snapped the...", "0_5_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>, and I was traveling at ninety miles an hour toward Vegas with a bloodsacked cannibal who claimed to be Kung Fu's immortal saint of strangeness. \u201cDamn few people seen this,\u201d he said, sipping his beer. \u201cHell, even me\u2014I ain't even sure what it is. Could be a fetus . . . or a baby . . . Oh, that reminds me; did I tell you about the Pope? You see, he sent this messenger over here to talk about peace and love--just a regular old holy type. So I took his ass down to the Desert Inn and bought a hundred dollars in chips for him. Had him playin\u2019 roulette ...but you know how them Catholics is with their mommas' old Saint Anthony doll and the hanky-panky: They just can't do the basic sinin\u2019 things. So the first thing he did was stop drinkin\u2019 and carvin\u2019 on people. No more Zombie Knights for him; and no more swords to the head, neither. Hell, it was a sin just to be with him. So I had to have a chat with him.\u201d He paused for a sip of beer. \u201cThat was one night...", "1_13_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> when I finally left the motel and started looking for a car. I tried four agencies within a block area, but they were all sold out of Eldorados. What they had were Mustang convertibles...just like the car in the movie. I was somewhat rushed, so I took the first agency man's offer: a white '67 convertible. I paid cash money from my sale of the Olds.\nThe clerk asked me what the hell I was doing with a four\u2014door hardtop at a time when nobody could get a two\u2014door convertible: \u201cIt'll probably take us six months to get one. We just got three in from L.A.\u201d I gave him a dumb look. \u201cNobody knows what the fuck is going on out there,\u201d I said . . . or something equally calm and rational. The truth was I was too terrified even to think. It was only after the deal with the car that I finally realized\u2014with perfect logic\u2014that I had no way to get out of L.A. unless I stole another car. It had taken me hours to finally realize this. I was thinking like a convict: that I was trapped in a prison\u2014that I would have to steal another car to get out...", "0_10_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten sixteen in the morning</span> and I didn't know what I would do. I had no money left. I went down to the travel office.\n\"Can you sell me a bus ticket to Laramie?\"\n\"You don't want to go there, son.\"\n\"Yes, I do. I want to go there very much.\"\n\"That's wy you want to go there? Okay. You can go to Cheyenne and change for Laramie.\"\nI went back to my shack and packed. As I was leaving I saw there was no point in sending the wire to say when I'd be home, so I tossed it in the wastebasket. As I went out and walked through the woods back to the highway, I could hear the telegraph key clicking. The wedding was over.\nWhen I got to Cheyenne I had twelve cents in my pocket and I went to the Salvation Army for a cup of coffee and a doughnut. I asked them for a place to sleep.\n\"What kind of a horrible hole did you come from?\" said the Salvation Lady.\n\"Colorado.\"\n\"We can't send you back there.\"\n\"Anything'll do for me. A burlap sack on a shelf.\"\n\"", "2_1_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to two o'clock in the morning</span>. The turquoise in the turban of the sleeping-powder salesman winked once, then went dark.\nThe turquoise would not wink again until one-fifty six that afternoon.\nThe salesman in the showcase was made out of pink quartz. He was the only salesman who was topped with a hat, was the only salesman who was given the considerable dignity of a beard. The beard and the hat were daubs of alabaster.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was sleeping across the hall, on the other side of the communicating door. She was sleeping very deeply, having accidentally taken four sleeping-powders instead of the intended one. She, too, was an alabaster statue. She, too, had a turquoise embedded in a band on her flesh.\nIts bright-blue light hurt any eye that sought to probe the cold and secret reaches of her mind.\nTHE TRADITION OF THE HUMONGOUS BAT\nIT WAS one-sixteen in the afternoon and all roads still led to the German restaurant in Indianapolis.\nThere were two really huge news events in the sky and on Earth at that time.", "2_6_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>. \u201cShark Church next?\u201d wondered Emory Bortz. But now, energized by the caffeine they\u2019d all drunk so copiously 20 minutes before, the volunteers around San Narciso began to pick up other sounds, besides the ocean, the generators and the 4 A.M. rush of wind in the microphone. $FIRSTNAMEF2, alone among them, still did not hear them. She stood with her head high, looking out over the surf, determined not to be surprised, whatever it was.\nMeanwhile, behind them, hidden from the rest of the watchers by a rise in the land, a rivalry had developed between Emory Bortz, who needed something more than six minutes of paper and pencil, and Silent Crawford, who hadn\u2019t figured at all.\n\u201cYou go,\u201d said Bortz and, sarcastically, \u201cyou think of a bell curve yet?\u201d But Crawford, who cherished his dirty mind, wanted to produce one all the same, fondling his slide rule and staring up at the sky.\n\u201cSay five minutes,\u201d Bortz proposed.", "1_22_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span> when we landed in the Place de la Concorde, and when we did I ballooned right up to the top of the Hotel Crillon with everybody else and immediately found Terry. She had just arrived by train. She was having a last drink with Eddie Brackett, Robert Cohn, and Mike Campbell. They did not have so far to go as we did, so they were waiting for us in our room.\n\u201cSay, babe,\u201d I said, \u201clet's take off. The gig is on.\u201d\n\u201cWhere?\u201d\n\u201cThe Dingo.\u201d\nShe stood up. \u201cWhere is it?\u201d\n\u201cRight here.\u201d\n\u201cCan't you wait till tomorrow?\u201d\n\u201cNo, we get there too early.\u201d\n\u201cDid you call your man in New York?\u201d\n\u201cHell, no.\u201d\n\u201cWhat did you do?\u201d\n\u201cNever mind.\u201d\n\u201cYou didn't write?\u201d\n\u201cNo. Where are those addresses? I've got to write.\u201d\n\u201cYou've got eleven minutes.\u201d\n\u201cI...", "0_7_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> on the morning of Tuesday, August 8, 1963, when he finally got the $SURNAME0 estate destimer. He was naked, burnt black by the sun, his nose was blistered, his lips were cracked. The red beard that had enchanted $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s old buddies at the prison rodeo was singed to an ashy smear. One-gallon cans of alcohol, used to start the Planetara\u2019s engines, were wired to the seat and the directional controls of the destimer.\nA dead Martian dog lay by the pedals. Cradled in its paws was a scorched and battered volume titled How to Tune Up Your Mach Six-H Rocket Ship and Evade the Enemy.\nA feature story in the volume was titled The Amazing Adventure That Made $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 A Household Name. The last line of this story said that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 would go on living and thriving, no matter what might happen to his financial and business interests on Mars, no matter what might be his physical condition or whereabouts. The story said that $SURNAME1 would go on living and thriving, because\u2014\nA new body would be waiting for him on a newly-landed Martian spacecraft.\n\"There you are, $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2014as soon as this copy of...", "2_17_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-six in the afternoon</span>, Earthling mean time. $SURNAME0 and his dog were twelve hundred miles behind the lines, in the cellar of a fallout shelter sixteen inches deep.\nThere had been chaos a few minutes before, when the shelter\u2019s occupants had all been smoky and pelletlike, formed of particles blown in from all over the world by the wind.\nThe occupants had sorted themselves out into anatomy and uniforms, and were now trying to counter the message in the wrapping paper with good-sounding lies, hoping for the best. The young lieutenant in command had never been dispatched on a mission so full of lies before, and hadn\u2019t the vaguest idea what to say or do\u2014he dismounted from his nervous horse and saluted. He was appalled by strange headlines in the skin of $SURNAME0 and his dog.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had, indeed, changed drastically since last the lieutenant had seen them.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s skin was pebbled, green, hairy and moist. His lips were grass. He was two feet tall and three feet wide.", "0_3_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-one in the morning</span> now, and only one thing remained to differentiate that night from all other nights in The Circus, and that was the arrival of the man with the secret approval of the recording angel, who was coming in from Chicago to meet Chief of Security $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas in the lobby of the Tranquility Diner, in Cameohawning, about two miles off the 95. There was nobody except Genghis Frog who knew why he was coming, or thought anything about it at all.\nDigression on the initials \u201cG.F.\u201d before getting on with the story.\nThere was, sometime back in the 1950s, a Veedel Jamison who wrote for the porno sheet Rex, and signed his stuff \u201cG. F.\u201d People joked about Gee, Vee must stand for Guy, Get a Load. Veedel liked that and began to use it at parties. Later, in private obsessiveness, he refined it to Get Fat, as in Hey, G. F. Howya doin\u2019? in his East Texas accent, and ate great poundages of zwieback and pecan sandies till he did. The point is, insiders\u2019 jokes escape the border of...", "2_0_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past midnight</span>. I felt as though I were just waking up.\n\u201cIt\u2019s started again. Listen, darling.\u201d\nIt was a queer feeling. I felt wide awake but it was like listening to somebody else\u2019s heart, to somebody that was asleep. It suddenly occurred to me that most people listened to that all their lives, lying awake at night listening to the silence inside them. Of course they don\u2019t put any names to it or think about it. But it\u2019s what keeps people in beds, in the dark.\n\u201cIf it keeps up, darling, we\u2019ll have to see a doctor about it.\u201d\nI wanted to tell her it wasn\u2019t any serious matter. It would pass off in a day or so. But I didn\u2019t say anything to $FIRSTNAMEB5. It seemed too complicated to talk about. I only said good-night.\n\u201cWhen are you going to get up, darling?\u201d", "1_1_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past one o'clock in the morning</span>. My attorney was driving flat out. We were approaching a red traffic light at a busy intersection\u2014just about to enter the city of Winslow, Arizona, population: sixty\u2014five thousand. I saw the light was about to change and shouted: \u201cRun that light!\u201d\nNo warning was necessary. My attorney understands my instructions perfectly. The mental injunctions and specific how\u2014to\u2014do\u2014it instructions /remarks are often separated by several hours or days...but when the decisive moment arrives, my man is ready.\nA Steady Diet of Acid No Ludicrous Speedlaws Here...We Are Surrounded By Huge Monstrous Robots _________________________ and He Is Ready For Anything Sickening Things Can Happen\u2014But Not To Us we Are Professionals...We Are Trained Experts At Every Form of Suicide Imaginable we Have Weapons\u2014And We Are In Total Control of the Situation\nTo understand this state of mind, it is first necessary to realize that basically my attorney is a very rude man. Genial, easy\u2014going, relaxed, yes\u2014but inwardly prone to sicknesses of all kinds, mostly suicidal. Because only suicide offers relief from the endless tape loop grinding away in his chronic brain: \u201cNobody loves me...nob...", "1_0_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was exposed on a low headland of weedy, scummy rock at the base of the wall. He was looking up the 1,200-foot wall, at the dark gash, the entrance to the caves of Mercury. To his right, to his left, and behind him was the empty sea,waslineless, littered by reefs.\nEverything was lusterless, colorless, grayer than every earthly thing.\nThe Sun had kissed the horizon.\nIt was twenty-one minutes past the hour of twelve in the Earthling morning.\nThere was a noise.\nA human noise, from high in the caves. $FIRSTNAMEB2 peered up.\nIt was a beautiful woman, her fluttering gowns blowing against the rock. She looked down at $FIRSTNAMEB2. She was the mother of all men and women on the planet Mercury.\nShe opened her arms and smiled at $FIRSTNAMEB2. She was a blond woman, pale, though not sickly so. There was no color on Mercury, and the blondness was natural to her. $FIRSTNAMEB2 recognized her as Malachite, the mother of all.\nShe came floating down to the headland where $FIRSTNAMEB2 was. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was bashful, respectful.", "2_10_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME2 moved on to Private George Field, testing. There was stubble under his test-leaf. George Field held his test-leaf tenderly in his fingers, waiting for it to fall off by itself. Lackey Workman, sitting beside him, leaned over every once in a while to nudge it with a thumbnail.\n$SURNAME2 reached Private Lackey Workman, bending down to test his beard. There wasn\u2019t any stubble under Lackey Workman\u2019s test-leaf. $SURNAME2, remembering his Thumb-test days, tested Workman\u2019s thumbnail. Workman sucked on it.\n$SURNAME2 reached Private Skip ARTCRANK, testing his beard. Private Arctur Zeno Herger Stevens Crank had nothing but stubble under his test-leaf.\n$SURNAME2 reached Private Stony Stevenson. There wasn\u2019t any test-leaf under Private Stony Stevenson\u2019s test-leaf.\n\"Fall in!\" Sergeant $SURNAME2 roared.", "2_9_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>, Mediterranean time. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 performed their skittering ritual of exercise. $SURNAME0 would skate ten feet and drop, $FIRSTNAMEB4 would race to the spot, and both would curl up in a pin-cushion ball.\nThey would uncurl slowly and go scurrying with heads low. $SURNAME0 might vault a bench or a hedge, or flip end-for-end in the air, carrying $FIRSTNAMEB4 with him the while: the acrobatic champion and his dog.\nThen they would be gone again, over a wall and across a plaza, and into the shadows under archways, and down into the catacombal warrens of the old town.\nA detachment of nuns swooned in perfect rhythm and were caught like cut flowers in a cross-current of air.\nThe time was fifteen minutes past nine o'clock in the morning, M.T.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had reached the old river docks of Marsalfi. The dock in question had once shipped raw caffeine from Turkestan. It hadn\u2019t shipped anything in five thousand years. It went into the cobblestones under one of the noble houses of town.", "0_2_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>, Eastern War Time. It was ten minutes until three o'clock in the morning, Greenwich War Time. It was thirty-two minutes until three o'clock in the morning, New York Time, in the neutral country called Manhattan.\nLenore Beadsman, nineteen and a half years old, was asleep. The material of her nightgown was featherlight and transparent, and it clung to the undersides of her breasts and rubbed the insides of her arms and legs with the slightest movement. There was the faintest filigree of a shadow the shape of New England on her upper thighs. Bruce Weinberber had seen it once, lying quietly with his broad chest and narrow hips and tanned stomach making a sort of pillow for her head. Bruce had patted her head with a hot, moist hand and said, \u201cGod bless the shadow of New England. Oh God bless it,\u201d and had fallen asleep, which meant he was snoring.\nThe nightgown was really a nightshirt. It was supposedly an Osvemo family tradition that all Osvemo women wore nightshirts. There were pictures of them going all the way back to when they were one of the families that owned this section of the continent.", "1_23_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> that day. We were just getting to know each other and had a lot of things to talk about. He was a tall, muscular red-headed kid. He told me he wanted to be a writer. We talked about writing and the movies. Then we talked about girls. He had had a few, but said he wasn't really interested. I accused him of lying, with stories he told, about fights and other things. We talked about that. The thing we talked about most, and enjoyed most, was a drink. We talked about drinks.\nAt every bar we stopped at $FIRSTNAMEB3 ordered rum and grapefruit juice. I stuck to beer. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, have you noticed that you don't see many sad faces in bars?\u201d\n\u201cThat's right. Nobody grieves when they have a drink in their hand.\u201d\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, have you ever stopped to realize what a burden circumstances are?\u201d\n\u201cBrother, where there's a bartender there's a burden \u201d\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, who cares?\u201d\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, that's right, who cares? Have another?\u201d\nThen we got into everything, and talked about all kinds of ordinary things and swore not to middle...", "2_16_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four eighteen in the afternoon</span> when we finally left, mostly because I was all out of beer. I took one final look at the tiled whale, but it availed me nothing. What did Whale Maser mean? Why was Whale there? The whispering was over, but it had yielded no answers.\nMy attorney was edgy from the start. We ran through the CircusCircus and hopped in the red convertible, which he wanted to drive. He gunned it out of there with a squeal of tires and immediately ran a red light with screaming woe\u2014p into a bus. We came out of that one blood\u2014free, but he had rattled every piece of glass in the carand two women who were having a heart\u2014to\u2014heart talk in a convertible just to our right almost wet their pants.\n\u201cI don't think we should drive,\u201d he said. \u201cWe won't get far.\"\n\u201cNo? Why not?\u201d It made sense, but only because I didn't feel like driving anyway. I was facing a long drive on bad highways, and I still wanted very much to get off on beer just when my brain was reaching that critical state were the slightest confusion could turn me into Evil Norman.", "1_4_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>, local time. The place was a row of old buildings that had looked like warehouses in the darkening, rainy afternoon. And they belonged to a Martian enterprise called the Red Dorian Picture Corporation. The buildings were of light ocher, silvery stone that had a blond look in the waning light.\nThe buildings were raised on tall, man-height piers that reflected the light that still fell on the street from widely spaced orange-white sodium lamps. The piers were needed to lift the buildings above the higher-than-usual water tables of ancient Mars.\nThese buildings all looked new and sharp-cornered, except for the weathering that came from exposure to the winds at their street level. The roofs, of a light and porous stone, seemed as if they had been yanked up crumpled, dried, and then let down again. The sky was full of low and ragged violet clouds, and of strange, brown-gold light.\nThere were no bells in doorways, no identifying footprints on the light-colored pavements and walks. The most prominent symbol in sight was the golden Hammer and Sickle of the ancient international organization of factory workers the W.U.A.C.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were...", "0_5_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-seven in the morning</span> according to the clock in the Skymaster. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had that much time before the fiasco he had brought upon himself and his family was to be enacted on the parade ground below. He needed time to think. Moving his plane to one of the pockets was out of the question. Moving it to another part of the plain was equally unthinkable.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 would simply have to sit there and cope with whatever came,\nHe settled back in the seat of the plane, held the miracle harvest in his lap. He was surprised at how comforting they found the warmth of his body, how snugly they nestled in against his hairy chest.\nNothing much came to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind at first. Nothing much came to his mind for three whole minutes. And then, there in the pitiless pink cabin of his aircraft, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had his first religious experience on Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s vision was not hallucinatory. It was all in color, and three-dimensional, and thoroughly convincing.\nIn the middle of the cabin, at eye level, an opening appeared\u2014an opening in the air. Out of the opening came a being of such brilliance and malice that $FIRSTNAMEB0 recoiled.\nThe being\u2019s malignancy consisted chiefly...", "1_2_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>, Galveston time. All drawbridges in the European part of the former United States were raised, and armed guards stood everywhere. $SURNAME1 watch was kept for a destroyer of the kind that had attacked the stronghold on Candlegill Cliff in Canada. A distress signal had been picked up off the Irish coast:\nRumored persons on board: a notorious Nazi and two young men capable of broadcasting great mischief over the air.\nUnidentified vessel is also reported to be armed with secret weapon of great power. Destroy at once.\nMuch else had been picked up, but this was the core of it.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized, materialized in the bed of a truck thirty-six feet below the level of the crane, in the very center of the planking.\n\"This is Winaura Pond, Mister?\" said a driver politely, raising his head above the windscreen.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0 curtly. He was pale, and kept his eyes pressed shut.\nThe driver\u2019s mate hopped down with a rope. He tied the rope to the hitch on the back of the truck\u2014and said he was sorry he had to put it on so tight, but...", "1_0_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-nine ante meridiem</span> and Martha Carroll was slipping away. Back home her daughter, Prudence, was singing at the breakfast table while her son, Jonas, complained because he could not have his own way. She wondered what they were doing now. Singing! Combing their nice clean heads with her good tortoise-shell comb, singing over their blue-rimmed penny readings from the Holy Bible, that she had gotten one Christmas makes ago. Merci, mon Dieu\u2014it was almost worn out.\nAt once she converted her homesickness into homesickness for Heaven. Let the little children lead me, though direct to the presence of Jesus. Lord, why is man, who walketh in the street of the city, be stricken, smitten of Thee? Why dost Thou visit the sins of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation? Have mercy upon us, miserable sinners! Lord Jesus! And also in the Thee\u2014intercede for, pray the holy saint, Saint Anthony, in my behalf, upon my knees, now and at the hour of my death. Bless my family. Give me patience. Let me be somewhat closer to Thee for the reading of these books. The words were large and shining and blue. Heaven...", "2_22_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span> in the nation of Nevada. The sky was as clear as the black Prince Albert pipe-bowl in Ishmael\u2019s right hand.\nHarley Potter got Ishmael\u2019s call on his telephone in New York City at two minutes to eleven. aSignal-strength deduced to indicate a position somewhere on the outer fringes of the nation of North Dakota\nA man with the unlikely name of Benedict Wormwing, smoking a ciga rette in a Turkish beard, receiving a telephone call on his desk in the Federal Building in Batavia, New York, found that the call was being relayed to him from the outlying post of Fort Drane, Florida, on the Gulf of Mexico.\nAt twenty-two minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening, it was light enough to see Charlotte, North Carolina.\nIt was a time when the soft southern landscape was filmed with the threat of a low-pressure front sweeping in from the Gulf of Mexico. At that time of year the days, anyway, never got very dark; and the dark of the night was enlivened, that February, by fireflies, and by lightning bugs, whose colors were actual paint.", "0_4_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-eight in the morning</span>. The police chief had gone to a telephone in the back. Remington's team was in the Register lobby, getting coffee. Remington got his coffee from the chief, who had a coffee urn going behind the desk.\nThere was a shrieking of tires outside. A big sedan came careening through the front doors. There were at least four people in it.\nPulling up behind the sedan was a car convertible with junk in the back. The convertible backed smack up against the sedan\u2019s bumper.\nIn the excitement nobody noticed that both doors of the sedan popped open at the same time, and two men leaped out and darted across the lobby of the police department.\n\"There they go!\" said Remington. Nothing was making sense.\nIn the front part of the big sedan there were two policemen. A woman looked out the back window of the sedan, then turned and whispered urgently to somebody inside.\nThe two men ran out of the police department lobby and into the holding cells, where Eddie Stillwood had been only a few minutes before. They snapped the locks. Of course a policeman had been there putting Stillwood in a cell. Now he rushed to try to catch the desperadoes. They were gone.\nThe...", "2_10_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-nine in the morning</span>.\nTwo half-inch horns of blue-white flame sprouted from the muzzle of the rifle.\nThe fire raced up the cord to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s neck, incinerated his jugular vein, esophagus, and larynx.\nOne side of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head caved in.\nThe fire tumbled down the stake, made charred ribbons of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s torso, arms, and legs.\nAt exactly ten fifty, the stake and the body on it were reduced to a blazing heap of ash.\nThe ash remained in one piece even though blasts of hot air throbbed through it.\nThen the ash was blown to pieces by a fan in a duct. The pieces were passed up the duct, into the air-handling system, and were flushed out into the atmosphere to swirl, disintegrating, about the planet.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was dead.\nLong ago and far away, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had said farewell to his son $FIRSTNAMEB2, with advice from a book. Now, here was the perfect sequel to that scene in the cabin of the space ship: $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s ashes were flowing out to find the boy.", "2_12_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after noon</span>, Greenwich Mean Time.\nHaving perceived that they were now underground, the chronology of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his appearing and disappearing is abandoned. A chronology of events on the surface of Mars will be followed henceforth.\nThe chronology for the Earth is resumed exactly one hour later.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 lay stunned, insensible.\nSentry probes had landed on Earth, Mars, and Venus.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 lay in the violet twilit cavern below the grille of Gumbo\u2019s cell. Outside his cell was the beautiful marble hall of St. Francis.\nThe sultan had decided to leave this beautiful marble hall unchanged\u2014so that Earthlings who came after him would profit by his share of the wisdom that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had sold so cheaply.\nA telephone was ringing in the guards\u2019 cubbyhole beside the hall. It was Scheisskopf again. He was calling from the Pentagon to tell the sergeant in charge of the guards that the United States had just declared war on Mars.\nThe sergeant announced the declaration of war to his men on a bullhorn.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was one of the men.", "0_14_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-nine in the afternoon</span>. The body language of my attorney tormented me no end. He was standing on the west end of the desert island, facing east, trembling. The sun was chasing shadows across the landscape. The sky was pale blue. The sinking sun made everything teem with colour.\nMy attorney turned to me and licked his chops. \u201cOK,\u201d he sneered, \u201clet's get this thing done.\u201d He squinted into the sun. \u201cYou name the place and I'll make the reservations.\u201d I was packing a suitcase, from our vantage point on the hump; I gestured with a sharp nod of the head, and my attorney moved cautiously across the sands to a place about a foot in front of me. He squatted and stared apprehensively at me.\n\u201cOK,\u201d I said. \u201cThe time has come. Tell me, who makes up your mind?\u201d He tensed his buns and straightened up, then righted himself again. \u201cMake up my mind about what?\u201d I slammed the suitcase and latched it. \u201cAbout the drugs!\u201d I snarled. \u201cWho decides when you take drugs?\u201d He shrugged...", "1_20_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-eight in the evening</span> when the Steel Shipping Company\u2019s freighter Terria, having finished offloading its number-two hold at Mars Port and-unfreighted up, got under way at last. By nine-fifteen the ship had passed the phylladephic bloom and was out in space, approaching the wastes beyond the immediate Persephone bull\u2019s-eye.\nBy nine-twenty all electrical activity in the stateroom had ceased. In that time, Homostotle Gink, Put Dickmon, $FIRSTNAMEB1 C DOT Ominous, and $FIRSTNAMEB0, fighting a losing battle with insomnia, had played forty games of jass.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had won thirty-eight of the games. He had won so consistently, in the last ten minutes, that Homostotle, Put, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had quit even pretending to keep track of the score. \"Our boy can\u2019t lose now\u2014\" Homostotle was saying in a tone of awe. He was thinking of the unbelievable luck that had preceded $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s run of victories\u2014of how it had first struck the winning numbers on a stock, and then on vending machines, and had then followed the three soldiers practically everywhere they went on the voyage out.\nAt...", "2_15_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty in the afternoon</span>. I had just arrived for the Woodstock Music & Art Fair.\n19. Free molto and Sexy Sadie...it was a Beautiful Day...when the Chocolate General ambushed me with The Question. \u201cDo you honestly feel that you can go on writing your column, no matter how fucked up you get on drugs?\u201d And after thinking it over for thirty seconds while my brain boiled in the hellhounds of panic, I finally said, \u201cNo...I guess not.\u201d Luckily, nobody noticed. We were moving too fast. What happened next was very embarrassing. I saw the girl selling tickets just beyond the perimeter of the crowd, and I called out to her: \u201cHey! Ya need some help?\"\nShe stared at me, dazed, with fear and loathing. \u201cOh...thank you, no; I'm fine.\"\n\u201cCan I get two tickets, then? One for me, one for my friend?\"\n'I told you there, I'm working! I can all but handle this crowd!\" she screamed.\n\u201cYou mean,\u201d I shouted back, \u201cyou can't handle it?\"", "0_20_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span> of Earth's Wednesday, and it was cocktail time on Mars.\nSlartibartfast appeared out of nowhere, again, as he had done earlier in the day, and before that, at their breakfast that morning, and before that, when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had been romping with his dog in the garden.\nThis time he appeared in the middle of a beautiful meadow of Wandlung grass. The meadow was bordered by stately stands of pink and yellow Wottabomus-16 trees. A little way off was a valley where a brook ran. A spiral fall of water at the near end of the brook provided power for the life-giving pools and wind-towers of Chronville.\nSlartibartfast materialized in the middle of a group of stately white buildings that surrounded a square. At first glance, one took the buildings to be palaces in different architectural styles. Then one saw that the buildings had no architectural style at all\u2014that slapdash seemed to be the common denominator of their design.\nThere were statues of Abraham Lincoln, Charles Darwin, Thomas Edison, and Kenny on Mars, Life, Liberty and Willingness. Around the square on which the buildings stood...", "0_4_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0, so far as he could tell, was still alive. The wise man who had led him blindfolded through the space ship underlings\u2019 nightmare of corridors, terraces, gardens, granite cliffs, crystal rivers, stun-ray rooms, and the rest had made sure the blindfold was never removed.\nSo far as $FIRSTNAMEB0 could tell, that blindfold was still in place.\nThe best thing for him to do would be to die, since he still couldn\u2019t see and still didn\u2019t know anything about his situation. He now believed, though, that only pain awaited him as he groped his way forward. So he stopped groping, since, without the blindfold, he could die easily enough by falling off a cliff or running into a wall.\nHe sat down, put his parched and aching feet on rock to rest them. He found himself thinking again of the great brown and blue and green and yellow and red roc bird he had eaten in the mouth-fountain. Of the thousands of ideas that had run through his head while he was chewing the bird, one remained clearer than the others. The idea was that if he were a mind reader, everyone would like him. He would...", "2_10_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past ten o'clock in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time, in Newport, Rhode Island, on Earth. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were back in uniform on Luna. They were in a hangar outside their tunnel. The hangar was the only building for miles around.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt as if he were being worked on by a dentist who had his hands on both of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s upper jaws. It felt as if every tooth in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head were being wiggled loose. That was the feeling that had ripped $FIRSTNAMEB0 out of the dream world of Army life and thrown him into the painful real world.\nIt had ripped him from the world of talkative crowds and tannoys, of periodic bugle calls and massed marching. It had torn him from $SURNAME0\u2019s zoo on Mars, from $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s friendship with $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Churchill Smith, a six-inch reproduction of $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Churchill.\nThe outside world had come into the hangar and waked $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014and was twisting his head off.", "1_14_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-three in the afternoon</span>. I was standing on my head on the bed in Room 223 of the Sport\u2019s Budget Motor Hotel in downtown Phoenix, Arizona. Ginger was out fooling around with The Coup. Our white Porsche was in the repair shop across the street...broken-down again.\nWe\u2019d both been up on LSD for about thirty-six hours when I left the living room and dropped down on the bed. After struggling with my shoes for a moment, I stood up on my head\u2014and because the acid was still powerful enough to make me do it, I stayed there, offering no resistance at all, and with the tape still rolling on the floor beside the bed.\n\u201cWalked into the Sport\u2019s Budget about four o\u2019clock in the afternoon,\u201d I muttered.\n\u201cYes?\u201d said the tape recorder. \u201cWhat happened then?\u201d said the tape recorder? My bloodshot eyes rolled wildly around the room. I remembered every horrible detail of: \u201cThe desk clerk...he was this huge Indian ...this huge Holy Man ween zoom WHITE headband, with a big wise, fatherly: dazzling smile. Fills me with terror and suspicion.\u201d I was on my head staring...", "0_23_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. I had been out in Space for fifty-four years, two months, eleven days, twelve hours, forty-one minutes, and fifty-four seconds.\nThe eighteen meteors were streaking along the sky, one behind the other, like a fleet of submarine destroyers sailing in a widely spread formation, almost lost in the darkness of Space. Their shiny bellies were to the earth.\nThen I saw the first of the Martian attack ships streak across the sky, coming in to attack, moving at more than twelve times the speed of an ordinary rifle shot.\nIt came in so fast that it seemed to be no more than eighteen inches off the tip-tilted horizon. The swish and crunch of the Martian war rockets had barely begun when the first attack ship was racing overhead.\nI ducked instinctively.\nThe attack craft was small, compared with the troop carriers. It circled the moon once. It streaked toward the earth, trailing a red vapor. It disappeared into the atmosphere over the Pacific Ocean. Its vapors faded out, and it was gone.\nThe Suicide Squad\nWhen the last Martian attack ship had disappeared from the sky, I began walking around in a little circle again. The meteors were coming in exactly...", "0_18_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>; and the place was a wharf of the Imperial Docks at $SURNAME0\u2019s spaceport on the planet Venus.\nFor reasons to be given later, Otto Habsburg was claiming to be a humble Shipping Clerk of the National Trunk, who was being transferred to a branch office in Kashmir.\nHis space ship was the S. S. $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill, an old tub launched in 1958 and towed to $SURNAME0\u2019s yard in 1960. The Churchill had a crew of one\u2014the Shipping Clerk. Its sole passenger was a Thing that the Clerk was delivering to one of the outlying condominiums, where surplus winged monsters were employed as lawn ornaments.\nThe sun had just set. Daylight was gone, but the light from $SURNAME0\u2019s Sun was still strong enough to read by.\nThe Clerk stood facing his ship, waiting for permission to enter. He ordered himself not to shiver\u2014and was not successful.\nThe temperature was 46 degrees Fahrenheit. It had been 32 degrees when the ship arrived, and the Clerk had been fully clothed. Since then, the temperature and his reaction to it had undergone giddying changes. Now he wore a thin summer suit and loafed barefoot on a...", "1_17_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October 10, the first day of the new war, the day on which, ordinarily, without any fuss or bother, Earth's daily rotation carried her, among the other things, 500,000 miles farther away from Mars. On this day of disfavor, however, the two planets were only 193,000 miles apart, only half the distance they had been twenty-four hours earlier.\nEvery space ship, every rocket, every aerial bomb between Earth and Mars had been launched or fallen on her at exactly 5 o\u2019clock the day before, while the two planets were still comparatively close together.\nOn Earth, crowds of thousands had gathered around giant loudspeakers to watch the wonderful dramatic representations of battle on the huge Martian ex-moons. On Earth, the end of the war between Earth and Mars seemed to be arriving in thrilling style.\nOn Earth, any number of somber, reflective people were prepared to admit that the war had been fought for a bad cause and for a worse government. But what were they going to do about it? They did not have rockets or launch platforms. They did not even have bicycles.\nOnly one government on Earth had been able to reach Mars in time to take any part in the war\u2014the government of...", "0_19_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-two in the evening</span>. I looked again at the office door, twenty feet away and half-open, and I could see one man laughing and another man bleeding through a rag, and the phone made precise sounds, and the room went pink and then white, and I could hear a typewriter and a man coughing, and the whole roar of the building: I got up, slowly; I took the closet door off its hinges and carried it over to the office door, with some quiet effort and care, and I propped it up with one foot and leaned against it, and brought all my weight down against it, so that if anybody wanted to come out they would have to walk through me. Alone with the laughing man, the bleeding man, and the earnest typists, I breathed, and that was all.\nLoose in the room, I potted a few more cigarettes and stubbed them out; in loathing of the goddamn human race, I opened the windows wide and felt some pure primeval breeze on my face; I had gone mad. I began to attack the typists. I went over to the nearest one and made my hands like sandpaper, grinding them up and down the arms of her sweater. At first she was scared; the man she was working...", "0_13_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-five in the afternoon</span>. I didn't know whether I could stay awake until six. I ordered another beer to help me make up my mind. It was very hot. It was always very hot in that beer blast. The rock music was dangerous. It made my head want to explode. I felt fine. I didn't give a damn. There was only one way to stumble across the finish line, and that was flat out. No matter what it took, I was going all the way with this binge...all the way to the ragged, screaming edge. Three more days and it would be over. Five hundred miles of western highways to Tuc-son...then a final big blast with Dr. Gonzo in Las Vegas before the plane ride home to Los Angeles. II thought about my attorney for the first time in two days. He had seemed extremely vulnerable in the Dunes parking lot. The way he walked around in circles and mumbled to himself. It was almost as though he were waiting for me to do something - to frighten him, perhaps. Something about his manner suggested a man who was scared out of his mind...frightened, above all, of me.\nAnd why not? My reputation was clear enough. According to the grapevine I was as far...", "2_3_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-two in the morning</span>. That meant only forty minutes until dawn.\n\"What in the world\u2014?\" said Ordaz, the first down the ladder.\n\"We\u2019re investigating,\" said Hato Hacker. A ladder had materialized beside him.\n\"Come on up,\" yelled $SURNAME0 up the ladder. He was in $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s and $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s bedroom, looking out the window. \"Come on up, Skip. It\u2019s no good down there.\"\n\"All right,\" said Hato uneasily. He got on the ladder.\n\"I\u2019ll check on the patients,\" said $SURNAME0 silently, climbing past Hato. Without looking at Hato, $SURNAME0 said, \"It don\u2019t look good, son.\"\n$SURNAME0 went into the kitchen and turned on the light. There was broken glass in the sink, and the utensils lay scattered childishly around, as though startled messengers from a scene of disaster.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2, thought $SURNAME0, where are you now? The messengers from that kitchen said that you have taken a terrible journey.\nChapter 27", "2_6_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-four in the morning</span>. He had been sleeping for half an hour. He must have been asleep for half an hour. Funny, he could have sworn it was much longer.\nSomething had awakened him, he thought.\nAh.\nHe kissed his fingers and touched them to the picture of $FIRSTNAMEF1, the golden-haired wife who was six thousand miles away in space.\nThen he thought, \"I wonder what\u2019s happening right now.\" For right now, at that very moment, a lifeboat containing three men, a dog, and a crate of Martian lichen was being hauled into the whaler Narziss off Titan. The three immensely important men were waking up. One of them put a word in everyone\u2019s mouth. It was a cheery word. He called to mind, even as the Martian lichen and the whaler disappeared, what the word had been. The word was marvel.", "1_8_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-six in the morning</span> of November, and I was standing outside the front door of the Academy. It was a wonderful good old morning, the sun shining all over everything and the birds in the trees from the hill, and it made you so glad to be alive and there were two boys coming down the drive to get the mail. And I was going to the office.\nNo, I wasn\u2019t, for I heard some one calling on the radio for all chargers who would please come to the office. It was the Greek, come back and all excited.\n\u201cWhere\u2019s Cohn?\u201d he wanted to know.\n\u201cI don\u2019t know,\u201d I said.\n\u201cWhere\u2019s Henry?\u201d\n\u201cI don\u2019t know.\u201d\n\u201cWhere\u2019s Mike?\u201d\n\u201cHow should I know?\u201d\n\u201cWell, you can get somebody and start with all the chargers for San Sebastian, in ninety minutes.\u201d\n\u201cGo where?\u201d\n\u201cTo San Sebastian, for the bull-fight.\u201d\n\u201cThere isn\u2019t any bull-fight at San Sebastian.\u201d\n\u201c", "1_8_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-two in the morning</span> when I got to the office. I went in and unlocked the door, but did not turn on the light. I stood by the window and looked out at the street . . . the sunshine yellow street. I looked down at the pavement and saw it was hot to the feet. Heat coming up in waves. I went over and sat down at the desk where the dull gray machine lay. Everything was just as I had left it. On top of the machine was the spool of paper . . . and in the spool of paper was a written message from the underground that a blind man had delivered while I was gone. The message only said: \u201cYou owe us four million dollars, and you will pay it with diamonds.\u201d The message was signed by the Deuxmillions family. I laughed, thinking about the Campbells. This was their big operation. Probably fifty people were in on it, some of them brilliant swindlers who would naturally fall for a simple trick like the fish-head routine. All in all, it was a tasty caper. It would cause terrible confusion, as any good scam should do. At least they thought it would. But they didn\u2019t realize that their set\u2014up man was in the pay...", "2_19_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span> when all the Martian flowers began to bloom.\nThe blossoms were a mile wide.\nThey were officially described as follows:\n\"Instantaneous in appearance, a pillar of white vapor formed by condensation of water vapor, illuminated by reflection of light from below.\"\nThe apparitions were not beautiful. They were terrifying. The vapor pillars were made ghostly white by their own brilliance.\nThe pillars grew downward from the blossoms and spread over the Earth like immense tree-roots, seeking underground water supplies.\nThe white tree-roots grew at the rate of ten feet per second. They extended their grasp ten yards beneath the ground, not more. No well, cistern, reservoir or watercourse was safe from them.\nA pillar would strike a given object. A white disk would then seem to cover the object, forming an artificial roof that lasted an average of fourteen minutes. The disk became incandescent and turned to vapor from contact with ground water.\nThe pillars killed all living creatures that came in contact with the disk or underbrush of vapor. The dead could be recovered and examined. All had died of the same causes\u2014 crush, suffocation, dehydration, chemical and radiation poisoning.", "0_11_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until noon</span>... . I took one last run over to Beverly Hills, but was no longer capable of speech when I got to Lucy's gate. All I could do was point up at the sky and make a whirling motion with one finger. She nod ded quickly, then began stuffing various items into a white duffelbag...est of drugs, Lucy's fine white form f lew by me in the form of a cloud of Speed. \u201cYou coming or not?\u201d she screamed. I noticed her face was totally covered with wrinkles the lines of tension and the scars of SDS violence in Chicago, along with some zootsuit burns and other childhhod scars. But don\u201ct be distracted by these details. She was still as beautiful as Gina Lollobrigida and as wild as Alexandria on Wednesday afternoon.\nI leaped into the Great Red Shark, started her up and began to roll. \u201cWhere are we going?\u201d I yelled back. \u201cTucson?\u201d She nodded again, closing the gate of the fence, running alongside. Then she scaled a section and leaped, landing inside my car, just as I accelerated to sixty and aimed for the Hollywood Freeway. She smiled. \u201cYou're...", "2_0_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past midnight</span> when we pulled up in front of Robert Cohn\u2019s flat on the Grand Plaza. $FIRSTNAMEB5 walked in with us. She had given Michael a bad time going over in the taxi.\n\u201cDon\u2019t let me keep you up,\u201d she said. \u201cI\u2019m going right in.\u201d\n\u201cLet\u2019s send him away, Jake. Have aproper good night.\u201d\n\u201cSee you at San Sebastian,\u201d Michael said.\nWhen the taxi left I turned to $FIRSTNAMEB5.\n\u201cSo long, $FIRSTNAMEB5.\u201d\n\u201cGood night, darling.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m sorry I was tight.\u201d\n\u201cI didn\u2019t mind.\u201d\n\u201cWill you call me early?\u201d\n\u201cSure. Call for me. I won\u2019t go.\u201d\n\u201cGood night, Jake.\u201d\n\u201cGood night.\u201d\nI walked down the steep streets and around into the parking-place in the Avenue du Bois.\nCHAPTER", "0_17_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, according to the chronometer set in the desk of one $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 in the saloon of the $SURNAME0 estate, when the trestles of space tumbled and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 disappeared.\nNever more would $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 put their sad eyes on $SURNAME1 again. Never more would $SURNAME1\u2019s four billion dollars trouble anybody. It would be as though he had never existed.\nFor one thing, somebody else was using his spacecraft on the estate grounds. This alternate pilot had been discovered by K himself, who had just come looking for $SURNAME1.\nK tapped uncertainly at the door of the concrete blockhouse. There was no answer. K sniffed the air inside. A decidedly un-Rumfoordish perfume charmed his canine nostrils. There was also a whiff of cigar smoke. He backed off, took a running jump at the door. The door flew open, ripped from its upper hinges.\nIn a corner of the shuttered, darkened concrete blockhouse a woman giggled. K found her in this dark by smell alone. He pressed himself against her, inspected her by touch. She seemed to be wearing a tinfoil bathing suit. Her feet were bare. K got his teeth around...", "2_10_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich mean time.\nThe space ship passed between a cloud puff and the green, living surface of Earth.\nThe air pressure outside the space ship was two pounds and the air supply was coming in through a flexible tube.\nThe monitor screen on the instrument panel of the space ship now showed the American-Spofford-class research station known as West $SURNAME0.\nThis station, and the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Bunch boulder field to which it was anchored, occupied a flat-bottomed delta with steep, east, west, and north bank. The flat bottom of the delta extended to the north, to a point covered with boulders the size of houses.\nThe boulder field ended sharply at that point.\nBeyond the boulders lay the great wall of Earth.\nThe northernmost extension of the West $SURNAME0 station was called the Tower of Julip.\nThe Tower of Julip was a spindly construct of iron struts and wire netting. It was forty-six stories high. It swayed above the rocks like a witch\u2019s ladder.\nThe Tower of Julip had been named for the alcoholic beverage favored by $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s father.", "0_21_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-seven in the evening</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 were about to board the space ship that would touch Mars, Venus, and Mercury before returning to Earth.\nThe rocket was on a concrete slab beside the mansion. It looked like a Cadillac salesroom stunt\u2014a sleek contour model, painted in gold and sky-blue.\nThe railing at the head of the mansion\u2019s main staircase was also gold. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 Rumford surveyed the scene from the head of the stairs. He was wearing skintight sky-blue cycling shorts and a fine mesh shirt.\nHis great round head was as hairless as a tennis ball. His animal eyes were burned coals blackened by soot. He was a big man, though not as big as $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nA siren whooped.\n\"There they are!\" cried $FIRSTNAMEB1. He stood on tiptoe. He was the butler, cook, day boy, and many other things at the $SURNAME0 estate. His skin was cracked and weathered like an old slab of concrete. \"Would you look at that, now\u2014\" he said. \"Would you look at that, now\u2014just like they was...", "1_12_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past noon</span> . . . and I was already two-thirds drunk. I remember trying to count the distance back to the Mint from the location of that drugstore, but I kept losing track\u2014maybe because I was instinctively clutching the bottle of California port to my chest like a cop shielding himself from possible fire with an assault rifle.\nNo problem, I thought. I'm almost there. And suddenly I realized that it was virtually impossible to hold the white line without dragging both hands off the wheel\u2014that at this point I'd need both hands, not to mention both feet, just to keep the goddamn car in the right lane.\nJesus, I thought. Who has nibbled away my brain? Who has eaten the filter that makes individual and unique the perceptions of my five senses? The scent of the eucalyptus. The taste of the brine on that sodden napkin. The feel of the pavement . . . the sight of those people in the blue suits and Ray Bans who keep howling at me from the left side of my lane?\nNothing made sense. Nothing mattered but the bottle constantly cradled against my chest. Eat a grape. Wash it down with a swallow of port. Another goddamn curve...and that goddamn cop car again,...", "1_18_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-eight in the evening</span>, a strong golden sunset falling on the American Midwest, and San Narciso, California, a periscope-gashed bay inlet in the coastal Range, became so extravagantly illuminated that a salesman in a River Plate shore dungaree suit, poised by the South Rampart Coupe D\u2019lette showing his thigh to a brunette in a Norse suit, rushed to the window to see what all the light meant, foreboding in some as yet indefinable way the end of all things drooling, even as, in her apartment downtown, an agitated $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas sat naked on the floor of a blue-tiled bathroom, picking indifferently at the few pubic hairs she still possessed, and wondering if her lateness for the latest session of her group, Trystero, had anything to do with the inordinate amount of illumination in the outside world, if that was some kind of signal, if they\u2019d all congregated outdoors, in some plot, and were looking up at her apartment, thinking of her. Although the group had no structure or agenda beyond an end to the world, $FIRSTNAMEF2 was its only female member. Tonight it had gathered in the living room of James Mungo D...", "1_17_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span> and the place was a dining room in an air-conditioned suite on the thirty-fifth floor of the Hilton Hotel in Dallas. Room service had just delivered a tall, cool glass of Dom P\u00e9rignon \u201853 to a lady in a gold lame bikini, who was stretched out on a sofa, and the empty flute was just about to drop from her hand and shatter on the floor, when all the windows blew in, along with the drapes and most of the walls, and the lady\u2019s gold lame bikini was blasted straight up in the air like a whoopee cushion and deposited on the filter of the new ventilation system, where it just hung there.\nBut people have to have heroes, and Walter N. Vehicle was as good a hero as anybody ever had. He was the first genuine wastoid to seize control of a city and, with it, all of its fortunes. He was 19 when he got control of Las Vegas. He was 33 when he died there, broke and presumably insane. In just about all the years in between, there was almost no limit to how far out Walter Staley sought, demanded and got. Two of his marriages were valid in every state but Nevada; his nine surviving children carry names...", "2_3_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was a bedroom on the second floor of a stone house in the village of Skra Devenport, in the valley of Sevier Valley, Tennessee.\nThe bedroom of the stone house was furnished with Early American antiques, and a man and woman lay on a brass bed and looked at an Early American grandfather clock.\nThe grandfather clock had been made by Seth Willard.\nAll of it had been made by Seth Willard.\nThe man was thirty, and his name was $SURNAME2.\nThe woman was twenty-four, and her name was Prudence Clynn.\nThe woman was pleased, because her husband, $SURNAME2, had just asked her formally and graciously for permission to use the name \"Vance Warren\" on a major television special, in order to say all of the good things people who loved Vance Warren would expect him to say.\nShe was pleased, because she loved names and the history they implied, and she was fond of Vance Warren. She was fond of his name. This was shown plainly by the fact that she bore that name herself.", "1_14_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-two in the afternoon</span> when I sat down at my desk in the office. I had just enough time to go out at the end of the day, mail the rebuff to the Detroit people, go out to Hollywood and get the Oldsmobile back, drive down to the lot, give the tired false smile of the good sport to what people I ran into, agree enthusiastically with everything they said, pass up a drink because of the fifty-mile drive ahead of me, and get out of town at the end of a full day of business.\nI sat down and thought about it with wonder. I had done it. I had done what should have been one of the easiest things in life\u2014running a minor-league out-letter counter in a good location for a great White American businessman. And I had done it with a snap and a style I had never expected. It worried me because it did not make sense. I had eighty-six thousand dollars in the bank. I should have put thirty thousand down on a ranch and settled in as a gentleman farmer. I had everything to go ahead with. I could take my time. It was the twenty-third of May and I would be thirty-three years old on the ninth of July. I had all summer and all fall...", "1_0_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past midnight</span>, said Mr $FIRSTNAMEF1. His watch was still running. At this precise instant, he said, a very special machine in a laboratory in Washington, D. C., had just discovered a tiny flaw in the heart of the apple blossom ring of Saturn through a high-power telescope. This discovery, he said, would shortly be announced in The Times. He closed his watch case. He said, \"Here is my watch. There is the flaw. Time is up.\"\nMalachai $SURNAME1 made no move to get out of his chair. \"I\u2019ve heard all this before,\" he said. \"A sermon, a watch, and a washing-machine repairman who almost quotes the Bible correctly\u2014that\u2019s all I get for three hundred Earthling dollars. What can you possibly do next?\"\n\"You have in your possession,\" said Mr $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"a philharmonic philharmonic onyx dolphin. This is a very valuable item. What are you going to do with it?\"\n\"Sell it,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"On Earth?\" said Mr $FIRSTNAMEF1. He favored $SURNAME1 with a most un-Biblical smile. \"Any place on Earth would be better than Earth,\" he said. \"Any place on Earth would bring...", "0_7_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-six in the morning</span> when the big limousine of the Salah ad Din family stopped in front of the Department of Mexuscan and Other Cultures, University of California at San Diego. $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were in the car. They were at that moment in touch by telepathy with Salah ad Din Antoine, their grandfather, up in the big mansion.\nAntibiotics, Salah ad Din\u2019s instructions ran, had been doped so as to make the thirty-six hour voyage to Earth pass as pleasantly as possible for Fern. There were two doses left.\nThe old gentleman warmly congratulated his children on having been able to bring out of the past, for his private scientific investigation, this unique document\u2014Fern A. Asphalton. It was not often that one was able to expose directly a human example of the effects of a cultural environment normally inaccessible to research.\nDarling and Boolittle. The old gentleman continued telepathically. This acquisition of ours is priceless as a scientific specimen. And I have you to thank for it\u2014truly. You told me she was a specimen. You suggested repeatedly she was a specimen just waiting for me to come along and pick her up.\nIt is my deep conviction\u2014Antoine pointed...", "1_20_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span> of the last bad night of all. At nine o'clock, according to a citizen's watchdog group, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s old unit was supposed to show up at the Home and clean the place out.\n\"Some cleaning!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He grinned.\n\"What a laugh!\" he said softly. \"A laugh and a half, friends.\"\nAt that particular moment, and for most of a particular second, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind was aswirl.\nHe was imagining the pleasant times he was going to have on Titan, where life was easy and lots of pretty girls tried real hard to please a man.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was remembering things he had read in a boyhood book called The Tiger Among Us.\nThe Tiger Among Us had been written by an Earthling named Rudyard Kipling. The book was about a man named Mowgli who had lived with a tribe of happy, harmless jungle animals. There was an elephant named Little-Jane, a pig named Sui-po, a dog named Billoo, a python named Zifi, a monkey named Puk-Puk, and a bear named Baloo\u2014all just friends and neighbors. Mowgli did absolutely no work, and all he...", "0_14_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-three in the afternoon</span>. The building in which the office had been located had been crated and shipped in sections, erectable in a matter of hours. By eight in the morning, any building anywhere could bear the legend THE CURBSTONE SECURITY COMPANY, LIMITED, and be wholly the property of that utterly phenomenal international enterprise. Rumor had it that the dead, subterranean body of Jonathan $FIRSTNAMEB3 now controlled the coinage of seventeen small nations on the dark side of the moon.\nSo color was lent to the rumor by the wide and erratic line of pursuing colored lights across the dark side of Luna, as if an obscene rainbow had been badly laid among the mines and mills of the dead moon.\nEvery four minutes, Earth time, an incoming Space Wanderer was neatly intercepted.\nEarth control noted that the interceptor was functioning perfectly.\n\"All right,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, putting the laser pistol down among his nesting dolls, \"Mr. $SURNAME1\u2014\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF3.\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF3. You\u2019re the son of a very close friend of mine. And that son of a very close friend of mine extends his hospitality to you, and your two charming ladies, for exactly two weeks from today.\"\n\"How far is this place from here...", "2_0_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-seven ante meridiem</span> when we got the first phone booth and opened it up, preparing to rape its guts. \u201cCash please\u201d said the girl. My partner smiled at her, man\u2014to\u2014woman, and slapped two nickels on the little metal window. \u201cThank you, sir,\u201d she said. I saw her eyeing the jellies. Well, why not? They were already filthy. We weren't even breathing hard.\nThe first booth was bad. I didn't recognize the operator's voice, but I could hear her brain buzzing like bees in a cable hollow as she introduced herself. My attorney grabbed the phone for himself and I struggled for the second booth. But she still had the number. I was far too deep to even think of surrender. My eyes were going past her, far away. As I tried the door she screamed loudly: \u201cI have your number! You can't use this phone.\u201d", "2_0_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past midnight</span> when the light went on over my head and the door opened up. \u201cWho\u2019s there?\u201d said a chastising female voice. There I was, sitting on the floor, unable to speak for fear in such close proximity to her majesty. \u201cWho\u2019s there?\u201d she barked again. \u201cHow dare you turn that lamp off and sit there in the dark. Well you just come out of there this minute!\u201dI found my eyes adjusting as she held her back tome and talked to me through her legs. \u201cWell? Are you deaf? I said out!.\u201cWell, it ... it,\u201d I said, rising and backing away, \u201cit... it only seemed right that I should seek admission.\"\n\u201cYou imbecile! Where are your eyes? Don\u2019t you know who I am? Don\u2019t you know you could have been arrested?\u201d She laughed and turned. \u201cJust who do you imagine you are, you filthy animal?\"", "0_18_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-five in the evening</span> of October 3, 1969, and the place was London, and it was raining outside, and the man in the pin-striped suit whom I had not seen before put his head into my open cubicle door and said everything in the world was normal time again. \u201cOh really?\u201d I answered, politely putting down the Sports Illustrated I\u2019d been reading. \u201cI\u2019m afraid you\u2019ve got the wrong man. My name isn\u2019t Kinkesh. Lee. Whatever you say.\"\nI\u2019d done this to many weirdos in the lobby. One time it almost got me killed. \u201cWhere\u2019s Victor Simile?\" I asked the man.\nHe fixed his tie. I wondered how he had got by me. Of course. The elevators. I hadn\u2019t obstructed them the way I\u2019d blocked everything else\u2014vending machines, mail chutes, etc. Vending machines are always full of junk food\u2014chips, M&M\u2019s, beer nuts, Reese\u2019s Pieces, Raisinets and that stuff. I was on a diet; they were all out of order. All I had to eat was a can of p...", "0_12_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The chemical radiations from the explosion were traveling outward through space at the velocity of light. In a minute, the glorious cloud would fade, the sun would come out, and the defenders of Mars Base would come swarming over the fortress like angry ants. $FIRSTNAMEB0 sat by the iron door, listlessly dry-firing his weapon.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 heard footsteps, saw a shadow fall across the threshold. \"What\u2019s your name, please?\" said the shadow. It was a boy in a Scoutmaster\u2019s uniform.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t say anything.\n\"You haven\u2019t any name?\" said the boy.\nThe boy thought he was talking to another boy. \"Listen, $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" he said, \"nobody is going to hurt you, real bad. Not now. Not ever again.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 aimed the corroded muzzle of his weapon at the boy\u2019s chest. \"Mother\u2014\" he said.\n\"Yes, $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" the boy said. \"Mother is here.\"\nThere was a sweet sensation of Love. This lasted for about three seconds.\nThen $FIRSTNAMEB0 emptied his weapon at the boy. He emptied it at the head of his own chum, Snowden. He...", "2_5_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. I approached the place where I had decided to fly off. I quickened my pace. Another minute. Fifty feet. Thirty feet. I gave it the gun. My feet were a blur as I sped to meet the approaching guard rail.\nTen feet...NOW! I flung myself into the air with all the strength at my command, aiming for the guard rail on the left side. The heavy bike and its strange companion flew off into space, hurtling through the desert night like a mortar round. I pulled the chute at the last possible instant...JUMP...and the thing jerked to a halt, sizzling in a horrible cloud of dust and smoke. For about thirty seconds I did not move. I knew better than to move. I knew the laws of gravity. I had broken enough laws for one night; better sit still till daybreak and watch the others come looking for this broken crock of humanity.", "0_8_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. The calling of the priests, the ringing of ebony and bronze bells, could be heard from the campanile inside the wall. Priests went along the street, in their robes, swinging incense-burners before them as they sang the ritual prayers.\nThese prayers woke $SURNAME1's little boy, who began to cry because he missed his mother.\n$SURNAME1 soothed the boy, pointing out the sights from his carriage. $SURNAME1 promised the boy everything under the sun and moon, but only when they got to the planet Mercury.\n\"We must stay just one more day on this planet of grief and suffering, my boy\u2014just one more day; then we\u2019ll cross the River Styx to a paradise,\" said $SURNAME1.\nThe little boy became more cheerful. In the manner of children of his age, he thought that everything in the whole universe was named Styx. \"When we get to Styx, Daddy?\" he asked.\n\"We\u2019ll take a launch and go swimming,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Swimming in Styx.\"\nThe little boy thought it would be nice to swim in the River Styx. He squinted his eyes and imagined what the River Styx would be like. The liquid in...", "1_15_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s company now occupied a smooth concrete platform on the lunar plain. To their backs was the open iron door of the airlock, through which they had recently come, tearing their way from the ship through a press of silent men in blue suits. The tunnel they had followed from the ship had been choked with whispering men in blue suits. Every man in the company but $FIRSTNAMEB0 had gone thrashing wildly through the whispering crowd, pummeling the silent faces, hauling himself by main strength through the rock-solid press. Halfway through the tunnel, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s partner had died\u2014had simply collapsed against $FIRSTNAMEB0, and had died, and had been dragged by $FIRSTNAMEB0 to the end of the tunnel. Now $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s partner was in the airlock, being dealt with by the clean-up squads.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had stopped in the tunnel when his partner died, and he had waited for someone to come and lead him the rest of the way to the platform. No one had come, so finally he had bulled his way through the gantlet to the open door at the other end.\nThere he had been greeted by a blue, red-eyed officer with a mute baton in one hand.", "2_6_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nChapter 23\n/a/\nTHE PART OF THE SHAFT THAT WOULD HAVE BEEN THE ROOF OF THE STONE-AGE CELL virginally hewn by Vlad the Impaler, that is to say above the level of his excarnation, had been long ago heavily reinforced with girders of crazy-paved and mortar-surrounded cast iron, to keep the lesser parts of Stonecipher Beadsman III\u2018s Cleveland relatives from excavating and having a look. Not that anybody could have been expected to detect much from a look at Vlad anymore, except perhaps that he had apparently finally filled his floss with a hefty helping of spinach, his eyes were an improbable greenish yellow. A rim of cement that was in all respects impenetrable surrounded the reinforcing, forming a kind of protective collar. Army-green mold grew in the collar.", "2_12_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-eight p.m.</span>\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 brought the balls with them when they reported to the house.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 felt calm and lucky about everything. Everything was going to be easy for him from now on\u2014he was sure. He didn\u2019t let the calm show. He pretended, when he rapped on the door of the house with the tip of his swaggering black boot, that it was just like any other knock on any other door of any other pink cabin in any other State in the Union.\nHe held his black, luck-bringing ball in his right hand, under his long, fatigue-blue arm.\nHe had no way of knowing that he was bringing his black ball to the execution chamber of his unspeakable pursuer.\nThe time was twelve twenty-nine p.m.\nWatermelon Smiles, hoarse, tired, and nervous, opened the door.\nHe did not recognize $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 in their Army grays. He did not recognize their ball poles.", "2_12_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past noon</span> when the first pair of Martian war ships materialized above the estate.\nNine minutes later, two hundred and forty-nine war ships filled the sky completely. The air became painfully brilliant, and had to be sucked in through the mouth to be breathed at all.\nA voice boomed down from a loudspeaker on the lead war ship. \"Everyone lie flat on his face. Make no movement.\"\nEveryone did. They lay on the decks of their walkers, they lay on the ground under the blazing firmament.\n$SURNAME0 lay on his back on the woodpile, palms upturned, relaxed. He opened his mouth wide, breathed the hot air in and let it out again.\nThe air was filled with croaking voices. There were shouts and yelps, whistles and tears, the barking of the old man, the rustling of papers in workers\u2019 pockets.\nThe loudspeaker boomed again over the din. \"Silence, please!\" said the loudspeaker. \"High officials of the imperium are about to address you.\"\nThe officials were Geoffrey and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. They were about to address their subjects for the first and last time.\n$SURNAME0 got up onto one knee.", "1_4_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-nine in the morning</span>. The sun was just coming up. The last of the twisters had passed, and now Mars, a large red ball, hung high in the east.\nThe marble floor of the gazebo was strewn with twister rubbles. The opening of the harmonium was blocked with tree boughs from Indiana.\nThe gazebo itself was horribly twisted and leaned crazily to one side. It would have to be demolished and rebuilt.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were under the staircase of the gazebo. Their cheeks were wet, pressed against the marble floor.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 turned his head to look at the body of $FIRSTNAMEF0. The first glimpse of her was like a repetition of the physical suffering of the twister. Her poor little body was so broken, it looked as if it couldn\u2019t be. She wasn\u2019t ever going to get up and be. But then $FIRSTNAMEB0 saw that this wasn\u2019t true. A child that young could heal. She would live as long as anyone ever lived.\nHe closed his eyes, turned his head away from $FIRSTNAMEF0. He grieved for her again, and still felt that there didn\u2019t have to be anything like that.\nAs...", "2_11_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-five in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\nThe threshold of the estate was as bright as the surface of the sun. The party stopped, stared, blinked.\nSuddenly the administrative robot was glowing, was shouting, was a riot of ticking, was flinging its useless shovel into the air, over the cliffs and into space, over the blue sea.\nThe shovel disappeared.\nA parachute blossomed over the cliffs.\nThe shovel drifted down to Provincetown.\n\"Well\u2014good-by\u2014\" said Skip.\nA sound caught in Salo\u2019s shrill voice box. Salo was recollecting the number of tours he had sold at fairs: seven hundred and fifty thousand. He was remembering the meanest thing he had ever done\u2014selling Uranium-235 to German scientists, who planned to use it to blow up London. He was admitting under pressure from his memory relays that he had taken German marks in payment.\nHis next memory would be of having sold Martians to German Scientists.\nThere was such a memory in his read-out, but not for many minutes.", "2_20_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-five in the evening</span>. The blinds were down, the lights were up, the music was off, the drinks were zooming, the floor was mopped, the booths were clean, the customers were going.\nAt eight thirty-five in the evening, a man doesn\u2019t feel like such a fool.\nBut at eight twenty-nine, a man does feel like a fool sitting all by himself in the last booth in Drinks Galore.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 took a pull on his bottle. The stuff was awful, but it hit where it would do the most good. $FIRSTNAMEB0 felt the hit all the way from his toes to the top of his head. He listened to his heart banging away in there, loving the stuff. It was an old-fashioned heart, and it sounded good to hear it banging away.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 paid no attention to a man and a woman who came into the bar. They stood looking around, then went to the proprietor of Drinks Galore. They seemed to be talking about something they had lost.", "1_17_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-six in the afternoon</span>. In seven minutes, it would be night. The time for building was over, for rushing things. The atmosphere in the rough community was settling into melancholy as the fine weather ended and people began to think about the winter. Except for whistle-blowing, the factory quiet was now absolute. And the whistle-blowing was only an exchange of observations that winter was approaching between a foreman, who had been a draftsman in the engineering department at the war plant, and a few of his volunteers. Everything was falling apart with the coming of night. The great story that had taken over Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s life, the great story of the illustrious family into which she had been born and the great material organization into which she had married, was at a standstill. A punctual standstill, for time as well as for words had been framed by the Martians\u2014so that the Martian calendar and clock divided time into the same units for all the Earthlings.\nThe punctuality was inimical to Mrs. $SURNAME0 and her family and mightily assisted that sneaking thing in Mrs. $SURNAME0 that longed to be a tramp far more terribly than it assisted $FIRSTNAMEB2, the pet pig.\nSeven minutes.\nAt...", "0_14_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was Room 223 of the Pentagon Building in Washington, D.C. The place and the time were what a Marine Corps aide, a second lieutenant, had called them in his modulated military moan, as he opened the double glass doors of the pallid, plastic-paneled corridor. The space was much like a motel corridor. It led past the open, half-glass doors of anonymous motel rooms.\nThe space smelled of bureaucrat sweat.\nTwo hundred and twenty-three was a small office, with a white porcelain wall box for messages, cork bulletin boards, a gray steel desk, an armchair and pencil rack, a straight-back wooden chair, a reproduction of Munch\u2019s best-known lithograph, and a reproduction of Norman Rockwell\u2019s best known Saturday Evening Post cover. The lithograph was a macabre scene of a dog being butchered. The cover showed a freshly inked ball reporting its scores to a dispatcher. The ball was emblazoned U.S.A. Tour: Asheville St. Paul Milwaukee San Francisco Los Angeles.\nThe man behind the gray steel desk was not Norman Rockwell. He was enormous. He was in his thirties, broad-...", "2_20_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe ledge hummed.\nRepetition and performance had been scheduled for thirty minutes.\nThe repetitions and performances that the ledge was scheduled to make wouldn't be the same as the ones one day earlier and one day later, for that day had been shuffled. The day that was being lived.\nNear the middle of the ledge, at Ground Zero, a hole spewed flames that rushed down the shaft toward the surface a hundred miles below. That hole was an exit for part of the tremendous quantity of burning rocket-fuel that had shot up the shaft from the launching chambers far inside Mars to the surface by the wall.\nThe flame jets at Ground Zero were part of the ceremony\u2014 and important to it, since they spewed against the current of air that was coming up the shaft.\nSomething happened.\nUnintended by $FIRSTNAMEF1, the action of the core of her passionate being, something skipped across her flesh. She heard of sex.\nThere were six quarterbacks scrambling around on the surface not far from the shaft, waiting to be shot into the hole at Ground Zero. The hole at Ground Zero was going to shoot them all at once. The six quarterbacks were named according to instructions.", "0_19_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-eight in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0, who had been with $FIRSTNAMEB1 much longer than the harmoniums, was not in the least surprised at $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s actions.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had seen $FIRSTNAMEB1 run out on many other little massacres. $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had, indeed, established a great tradition for themselves, a tradition that lived in the minds of harmoniums all over Mars.\nWhen the harmoniums thought of the big shots up at the top of the ladder, at the very summit of the treetops, they thought of $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nBad news was coming in to the harmoniums, and the bad news was frequently met not with fear and not with resistance, but\u2014in the manner of $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014with a methodical retreat.\nRun as fast as you can, and as long as you can, and then run some more! was the precept. Having a harmonium leader who took an occasional, if childish, delight in springing all manner of barrages and surprises on the enemy was, apparently, an advantage.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 acted with an air of confidence that inspired an air of confidence in those who served beneath them. Besides\u2014from time to time, by accident or design...", "2_11_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-six in the morning</span>.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0!\" said a voice.\nThe owner of the filling station awoke at once, responding to the call to arms, just as he had done in high school. It was his business to do so. When a young man wants to buy his first pair of long trousers, he goes to the man who can give him the best talking-to.\nThe owner\u2019s eyes snapped wide open. His business sense told him instantly that the time had come for a hard sell. This boy\u2019s shirt-buttons would come flying off any second, and would be lost, ransomed only by a crisp three-dollar bill.\nThe owner rubbed his eyes and practiced sympathy in the mirror. He threw open the satin-lined drawer as silently as an arthritic man can do anything. He poised his horny finger above the alarm button beneath the black Andalusian rug.\nJoe Brackley, strapping son of the owner, sat down on the bed, said \"$FIRSTNAMEB0!\" again, and fainted.", "0_1_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-six in the morning</span>. Two minutes later Stan came in and sat down at the desk in our office. He had a six-pack of beer with him, and he popped a top immediately. \u201cWoo!\u201d he said. \u201cBoy, did I ever have a time.\u201d His eyes were big with wonder. \u201cYou guys won't believe what happened.\u201d He was about to tell us, using his fingers as a tempo-setter for the trip. His face was heavily perspiring. \u201cI tell you I just barely escaped and it's a miracle I got back at all. I went to Vegas and had a ball, but it took me forever to get back.\n\u201cI thought I'd check the box while I was gone and see if it was all right,\u201d he said. \u201cSo I stopped at the terminal, right? and the guy there said, 'What is this? What did you put in? Who authorized this?' I guess he saw I was a stranger because right away he comes after me with a stungun Or something.\n\u201cI said, \u201cWhat? What?' I didn't know what he meant. I was getting my ticket;I wanted to show him my...", "2_15_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and we found Sour Dough Roy and his wife furiously discarding papers into a deep cardboard box in front of their office while two men with hard hairy sunburns that made their heads and necks look like pillows struggled hard to lift the box in their enormous hands. The men had white paper masks over their faces and were dressed in white overalls and rubber boots. There was a truck idling in the hot street.\n\u201cMr. Dough, am I to understand you\u2019re moving away?\u201d I called.\n\u201cCome along now,\u201d said one of the men.\n\u201cMoving back to Vemont, looks like,\u201d said Roy. \u201cSorry to see you go, fellas.\u201d\n\u201cSorry to see you go, Mr. Dough,\u201d I said. \u201cWe\u2019ll miss you terribly.\u201d\n\u201cThanks for stopping by. We\u2019ll see you in about a week or so, when the house gets set up. I\u2019ll give you a call then, fellas, with the news of it all.\u201d", "2_21_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span>. Everything was quite quiet, to the extent that things got at a now definitively terminal Erieview. Wisp-thin lines, roads, led like spokes to and vanished into all stretches of black. The only thing out at an angle was the fountain, a plume of black, with white, intermittent foam at the ends of dense spear-points of black. A gusty wind arrived, with poplar snow, and stung the skin and did nothing for the quiet. Something to the East twinkled and flashed.", "1_8_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span> when these things happened to $FIRSTNAMEB6, who was now standing in his corridor deciding what to do. Dead ahead, walking under her own power, Miss VIreech, of Shakespeare, came uptown all the way from the Place Venite, walking slowly because on all sides to her excited vision, which perceived the significance everywhere, were thrust the sharp visages of the mad-dog Afghans, the sullen countenances of the sullen Chinese, the warm smiles of tradespeople, the cold smiles of strangers, the sweet smiles of children. And as Miss Veech came under the high tenement wall she brought her vision to bear with a sensible jerk into the corridor, where she met the handsome face of $FIRSTNAMEB6 and as she met it she said, \"Oh,\" and stopped quickly, because it had in it a smile and an oddness. And she stopped and noticed that $FIRSTNAMEB6, who had in him the sadness that is so wretchedly the jest of fools, hissed at her like the vapor of vaporific dial limners who observe the ocean and hiss at it, hissed at her like that, \"Oh,\" and lifted his fifth finger in a joking gesture that had in it lightly the suggestion of a rakish tryst to which...", "2_23_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-four in the evening</span>.\nThe date was August 20, 1975.\nThe summer of \u201975 had been a record-shattering summer. There had been more Americans in Newport in a month than usually passed through in a whole year.\nRumor had it that this was the last summer. Rumor also had it that Newport was to be torn down and scattered over the Chinese National Tea Gardens in Golden, Colorado.\nNewport, wherein lay the headquarters of the American Army of Scuba Diving, and wherein also was imprisoned the Great Redoubt, was a festering sore in the middle of an exquisite social scab.\nSailors, in their crisp white uniforms, were skating alligators across the ice of St. Peregrine\u2019s Pond. The alligators snapped and bit. The sailors shouted and fell down.\nThe barracks occupied the whole big end of Goat Island. The barracks were semi-permanent I beams, painted battleship gray.", "2_21_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span>. Raw lit twirled a drumstick on his palm. Helmholtz held his flute like a pistol. Mindy Metalman adjusted a strap. Rex Metalman adapted the decibels in his head to the loudness of a nearby airplane taking off.\nSnoopy danced. The dance was the dance of the creature who selects things in the environment of his home and assumes them to be alive. A rim of shadow around a window was an eye of an evil spirit. Anything used as a base was a creature swelling with rage.\nThe creature was anther of cat, to be sure.\nThe creature was anther of dog.\nThe creature was bird.\nThe creature was shadow.\nThe creature was rock.\nThe creature was window.\nThe creature was bottle.\nThe creature was anther of scissors.\n\"Gang?\" inquired Ralf Hart loudly.\n\"What?\" said Miss Malig.\n\"What do we sing?\" said Rex Metalman.\n\"Let\u2019s all hold hands and everybody think real hard about something funny,\" said Lenore.\n\"That\u2019s from the Christmas special,\" said Mr. Metalman.\n\"The hell with you,\" said Lenore.", "1_8_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-seven in the morning</span>. I was on my way to the track. I would be there by ten. The Mintortas were technically still asleep. I had climbed in through the balcony window, carrying my shoes in one hand and the bet list in the other.\nThey were all in the huge white bedclothes they\u2019d brought back from their wedding in Buffalo. Michael\u2019s plaid sportscoat was thrown on the floor. To get to my room I had to go through it and step over Mintor\u2019s huge, crumpled lunchbasket body. Daisy\u2019s snore was a splendid morning fog, white and opaque, tongue-like, and boring. I saw the sudden small blonde head of Jason rise and then sink below the smooth sea surface of the rest, who, from what I can remember of them, I will call The Three Misters: the uncle, the father and the brother. At the moment of my vulnerable presence in the room itself, Jason appeared to be mulling over his eighteenth year and how to survive emotionally in it and then, like a clam that has removed everything that can be confused with a food, and then snapped its two siphons decisively shut, he submerged again and lay quiet with his forearms beating the...", "1_3_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-four in the morning</span>. Any alfalfa grower in my motel corridor would have to be very drunk indeed to accept martyrdom when his alarm clock rang at four-thirty on this road to hell. During the next hour, four threats to my life were reported to my desk man by our lead-footed junkies. One of them, at four-fifty-four A.m., said he was All-American middleweight \u201cWild Bill\" Dunkel and was sure ready to rumble. My desk man was suspicious. \u201cHe said he'll be wearing pajamas,\u201d he explained. That was too crazy. He sounded like one of our customers. Wild Bill Dunkel was actually a very gentle man who went fishing in Montana with his friend the Governor. He was two hundred pounds of mischief in a hundredand-eighty pound bag and loved to moonlight as a sporty-car salesman at Malibu while he plotted his comeback. Every con man in both hotels immediately put one and one together and came up with Wild Bill, who forfeited his felony status by just being himself and driving the Javelin to the bar at five-fifteen for his last drink on the tab...a Bloody Mary. He told me about the drag...", "0_0_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-four ante meridiem</span> according to the chronometer, or according to the watch on the anaesthetized wrist, when Al added his two plus two mite to the mass of light (mercury at six degrees centigrade) prisms shimmering in the sun, and dictated to Zeno the hieratic message announcing in the Bureau of Navigation, Interstellar Space Administration, a vacancy at Tacoma, Washington, U. S. A. A chink in the mosaic, a tremble in the equilibrium.\nThe trembling reached $FIRSTNAMEF0 immediately. She was fanning herself in the parlor.\nAl came into the house at 12:36, a shapeless mass, a creature of shadow.\nHe staggered eleven paces before falling.\nHe tried to speak, and startled $FIRSTNAMEF0 by a bullfrog croak.\nIt was her estimation, checking time and ledger, that Al had spent four and a half hours in the hot sun as Mercury and three hours in the furnace of the lungs.\nDuring the last two of these three hours Al had perceived nothing.\nIndeed, he had hardly had time for a real sensation. He had undergone one.\n\"Al!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nBut Al could not at once respond. He had fallen face down on...", "1_14_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-six in the afternoon</span>. Two minutes later, the last trace of the drug was out of my system. I was completely clear, both synapse and sensor, as I raced across the surface of the turnpike in the GTO. A good thing, too, because a legitimate traffic cop at that point would have had me instantly. Not only was the speedometer hovering around a hundred and twenty, but I was also wide open on bolt and bad light aup rent hum in the LA Department \u2019bout an hour ago, officer...seventy miles north of here on the turnpike...no, I never saw him; I must have passed right through his sector in the GTO...what\u2019s a GTO?...rapid departure and so on. Right now there were no agencies out here but the usual Southwest breakdown outfits that cruise all day and night looking for just this kind of action\u2014madmen racing alone across the wastelands of the turnpike, with every drug in their bodies destroyed at 500 MPH...right. I sensed them swooping in on me from a million blind corners; but their beams never found me. I was riding with the Demon again, holding it in the straights, twisting the throttle on those long desert curves.", "1_6_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to seven o'clock in the morning</span>. We were on our way back to Montparnasse. \u201cNow come with me to this other fellow's and have a glass of absinthe.\u201d\n\u201cYes, now that I've awakened up, I feel a little hunger. Let's have some absinthe.\u201d\n\u201cLet's see. I have to meet a man on the quai d'Orsay about this rotten visa.\u201d\n\u201cOh, well, we could stop off just for a minute.\u201d And so we went over to see this other friend. His wife made me sit down in the kitchen and brought me a cup of coffee. The husband worked all night and slept all day. He was a critic, a good one, and had to choose between two jobs in the morning; one was as a critic on two francs a month, the other as night watchman on twelve francs. Naturally he chose the twelve francs. He slept during the day on the same couch that his wife and John's friend used for love-making. There was always a big absinthe bottle and glass on the little table by the couch end, and the husband was supposed to keep excellent control of the absinthe consumption...", "0_5_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>, and when $FIRSTNAMEB0 went out to space-ship centre, a lot of the recruits were already there. The recruits were supposed to arrive at six\u2014 but there were wagers on among the recruits as to who would arrive first.\nThe recruits who got there first would be a soldier and get up twenty-five power pills and a pint of blackberry brandy.\nA lot of recruits were so eager to get to the space-ship that they tele-transported themselves to the space ship. When $FIRSTNAMEB0 arrived, twenty-three recruits were already boozed up.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 took the pint of blackberry brandy out of the recruitboozers' safe. He put it into a small cavity in his chest, among the circuits that controlled his amnesia.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 left the barracks, walked silently down the concrete path in the numb yellow garden, accompanied by the twenty-three drunks. They all saluted snappily when $FIRSTNAMEB0 emerged. They all wished him luck, Yah! Yah! Yah!\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 got into the space ship and turned on the power. The ship took off, leaving the recruits in a state of numb stupefaction. The ship was more stimulating than any drug they had ever taken on...", "0_13_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Mountain time. The total available energy for all purposes for all mortal creatures on the earth was being used at that minute in just about the proportions of five trillion billion horsepower for military purposes, one trillion for schoolchildren and housewives, and one hundred billion for all other purposes.\nThis was according to figures available from the Advertisers\u2019 Apprentice School at Harvard.\nOne particular unholy warrior, who didn\u2019t live far from Newport, had more military power of his own at his disposal than all the Viceroys and Vicereines and Supreme Commanders of all the worlds put together. He had twenty thousand nuclear bombs, two thousand thermonuclear bombs, tens of thousands of napalm bombs, and thirty-two million deodorant bombs. He was at that moment sniffing himself in a thick fog inside a stone citadel. Some of his staff were cleaning their fingernails with switchblades.\nEach of them was backed up by 150,000 reserve troops, 200,000 war rockets, 1,750 refueling planes, 15,000 pursuit planes, 68,000 bombers, 1,000 troop transports, 100 atomic submarines, 15 aircraft carriers, and 20 battleships.\nThis warrior also had installed in his fortress dozens of...", "1_0_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve seventeen ante meridiem</span> and Salo wasn\u2019t there. $SURNAME0, Karma, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 now stood all on their own, like a poorly executed painting of a war that had been dropped from the repertoire.\n\"Oh\u2014Gooood\u2014\" said $SURNAME0 up in the ship, \"I\u2014I miss\u2014 somebody\u2014\"\nJulian ate some more ambrosia.\n\"I guess it\u2014it doesn\u2019t make much difference any more who comes,\" said $SURNAME0 up in the ship, \"but do you know a creature named Salo?\"\n\"Yes,\" said Julian.\n\"He\u2019s been out at the quarantine enclosure with me all evening,\" said $SURNAME0 up in the ship. \"He must have come in with you. Have you seen him?\"\n\"He swallowed the fish hooks,\" said Julian, \"and he put on the lifeboat rocket suit, and he flew away.\"\n\"Flew away?\" said $SURNAME0 up in the ship. \"Wait\u2014now I remember\u2014he stuffed something in my pocket!\" He sounded like a man explaining something very simple and very clear to a child of rare wit. \"Two pocketets,\" he said. \"A little one, and then a big one.\"", "1_9_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span> in San Francisco. In a minute, any time now, the wild-eyed inventor of the Penetralia would power up his gadget and God knew what would happen. Poor $FIRSTNAMEF1 was sleeping over in the equipment shed by the cactus garden. The luckless Redwine was probably wrecking havoc on the Acme Underground parking lot by now. And there was nothing Preposterousurtle and Binguccio could do but wait and wonder.\nYes, somewhere out in the states of New York, Virginia, and Florida, delivery boys with packs on their backs were calling at doors and making change. But in Boneyard, in San Francisco Chinatown, everything was still. Only the slanted roofs whispered in the strong wind. And down in the street, a dog, who'd been trying all morn ing to dislodge a rat from a garbage can, gave up, barked briefly\u2014and the sound was magnified a hundredfold by the stone and plaster canyon.\nThe dog trotted away. The junk in the can slid out, rolled over, and the rat came with it. It was a mean, gray, ugly son of a bitch. If he'd had a mouth, he would have been hissing. Just then, all the lights went...", "0_9_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span> and Mr $FIRSTNAMEF1 had just bought five hundred shares of stock in G Casino and Hotel. The stock had been offered to Mr $FIRSTNAMEF1 by a letter only a half an inch long.\nThe letter, which had been printed by a tiny letter-printing machine, had been sent to Mr $FIRSTNAMEF1 by a dealer in stocks on Mars.\nMr $FIRSTNAMEF1 had in his possession a thousand tiny machines that would print letters no more than half an inch long. These machines could be used to print business letters that would seem to come from almost anywhere.\nMr $FIRSTNAMEF1 was a very small dealer in stocks in Chicago. He was Vice President in Charge of Pomeroy\u2019s Department Store Fumigation Department. He made more than two thousand dollars a year.\nHe was forty-three years old. He had never been able to make more than two hundred dollars a year from buying and selling stocks. The only stocks he had ever owned a big block of were the five hundred shares of Fine Cigar Tobacco stock.\nThe five hundred shares of Fine Cigar Tobacco stock had cost him fifty cents a share. Since then, the profits from the Fine Cigar Tobacco stock had exactly averaged ten per cent per annum.\nMr $FIRSTNAMEF1 had after a few years wound up buying G Casino...", "1_3_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt an azimuth of 290 degrees and an elevation of 43 degrees above the horizon lay the planet Mars.\nAt an azimuth of 237 degrees lay the sun.\nN cutting himself, Salo had deflected the antenna of his head-set radio hose a mere 6.363 seconds\u2014less time than it takes to shake a man\u2019s hand. In that brief time, he had learned everything there was to learn about his friend Field Marshal Kl screwed.\nKl scattered his seed with an abandon that bespoke loneliness. Somewhere, on some unremembered beach on some unremembered planet, he had left a wife, who had given him two children.\nKl had promised his son, Tristam Truncatula, a planet-hopping son who couldn\u2019t resist a good-looking girl, that he would send him a Martian engine that ran on seawater, as soon as he could lay his hands on one.\nKl had once wagered his daughter Sinastra that he could run across a Martian sand dune faster than she could ride her horse across the same dune. He had won his daughter\u2019s silver nag but had had to relinquish it to a...", "1_4_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-six in the morning</span>. The car in which we sat was parked on a steep slope about half a mile from the center of town. Behind us was a stalled line of cars and pickups that stretched all the way back to a point six miles south of town, on Highway 50. Every vehicle in this convoy had plunged from the wrong side of the mountain. The ground was wet from rain. Some of the drivers had been in line for hours.\nThe highway department had thoughtfully set up five portable toilets on the shoulder, but a gang of rangers had vengefully poured lye into the bowls. There were no food concessions. There were few windows rolled down, and the air was vile. The only flashing lights belonged to an ambulance, which everybody behind us seemed to believe was on its way to take them to the hospital.\nThere was an ominous rumble, and our car rocked slightly. Rose of Utah looked up from a copy of US Beautyhair Magazine, frowning. \u201cWas that an earthquake?\u201d she asked.\n\u201cMind your own business,\u201d said Special Agent Nixon. He was asleep in the back seat. I was at the wheel because I once owned a valid driver\u2019s license. It was now revoked.\nA new roar...", "0_15_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-one in the afternoon</span>. He went out to the lobby for a copy of the San Diego Union. A boy brought it over and said, \u201cThere's been a big marine thing in Nicaragua.\u201d\nWe walked out to Hollywood to a bootleg newsreel theatre behind the Little Annie Rooney bunkhouse. Cleggett's came on. They'd cut a few frames and posed him an entirely new last question to close with. But they'd left the opening and first part of his statement intact, so it didn't look so bad. \u201cSan Francisco and Frisco (long pause) ... have always been my (pause) ... well, what I mean is, I always came here to get my kicks.\u201d That was his big talk on The Round Town Buffet. He got a strange look on his face during the playback and I wondered if he was stoned in spite of himself. We went out.\n\u201cWhere's your car?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cJust up the block.\u201d\n\u201cI love that car. It's like the one $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I stole. I'll bet $FIRSTNAMEF4 is there; I want to see $FIRSTNAMEF4.\u201d I opened the trunk and put his bag in with mine...", "2_13_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nTuesday, January 7, 1986.\nThe place was the small rented courtroom on Tralfamadore.\nA large number of entities who called themselves families, tribes, gangs and nations were present. The largest group in attendance, by far, was the personnel of a ship named the Newport. The smallest group in attendance was a man, his name Pierce, and his son $FIRSTNAMEM2, who was twelve years old. One hundred and forty-eight persons sat, stood and lay in attendance. All of them participated in the ceremony, except the ninety-year-old Roman Catholic priest, who attended but abstained.\nThe judicial ritual being performed was an execution. The Lieutenant Governor of the State of Arkansas was the official who was attending to the ritual on behalf of the sovereignty of Tralfamadore. He signified his attendance by breathing deeply in the execution chamber\u2019s air, a distasteful air made worse by the presence of the body of the person to be executed.\nThe person to be executed was a convicted murderer. He was a young man named $SURNAME1.", "1_1_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was emerging from between racks of heavy iron trusses when he heard the harmonious whistling. It was what the original Martians sounded like when they talked, according to $FIRSTNAMEB1. It was pleasant harmonious whistling.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 stepped from between the racks, ducked through the webbing of a paralift, ran between two long racks of communications equipment, came out into a corridor. The whistling was louder in the corridor. It was coming from around a corner. Around the corner was a lavatory.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 crept to the lavatory. Around the corner were two powerful lavatory faucets, and between them, whistling and pretending to do nothing in particular, was $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had dismantled his rifle and was carefully cleaning each piece. He carried the pieces, one by one, to the lavatory, rinsed them in the water jet from one of the faucets, and wiped them dry on his khaki shirt.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 saw that an attempt had been made to disguise this part of the Martian equipment as Earthling lavatory fixtures. A Martian toilet had been spray-painted yellow. A large Martian urinal had been spray-painted brown. The Martian theme was continued in...", "0_10_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning</span>. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night. The time was ten thirty-nine in the morning. It had been a grueling night.", "1_20_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nYes.\nAt nine 0\u2019clock, the controller would make a final decision. By that time he would know. He had more pieces. The pieces were becoming overpowering.\nSaturn Ruminus, master of the United Party of Earth, would be able to predict to within two percent the outcome of the election.\nWhen the same messenger who had taken the announcement to the $SURNAME0 estate returned to the palace, he brought the invitation to the announcement. It was scrawled on a piece of paper torn from a spiral notebook.\nThe announcement would be made in a glade of Trevylyan\u2019s Woods half a mile from the $SURNAME0 estate.\nDuring the three minutes it took the messenger to walk to the palace from the $SURNAME0 estate, he twitched and yipped in ten-second spells. His hands flew every which way. He slapped his thighs, caught himself on the verge of slapping his head and feet at the same time, and howled like a dog. He whispered something that sounded, to those who could hear, like \"Six thousand! Six thousand! \" He squinted anxiously into the sky.\nAt the palace, he was comforted with a stiff jolt of...", "1_6_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-eight in the morning</span> and the sun just coming up over the desert when the Cadillac slammed on its brakes at the roadside phone booth. In the booth was an old man in a rumpled blue serge suit with two dimes in his hand. He was so old that he had to hold the dimes in front of his face and move his thumb up and down to make sure they were real. The Cadillac honked twice. The old man looked up and put the dimes in the slot, thinking hard. What an odd thing to do, to call somebody at sunrise just to say hello. Probably up all night partying and sleeping it off in the front seat of their car. People were like that in Vegas.\nIt was Ringo, of course. And yet, even then, as the line buzzed and the desk operator came on, no jealous pang disturbed the sleep of the old man in the phone booth. Imagine, such a call. Such a sum well spent made him sad, vaguely. Everything is changing, he thought. Nothing stays still. Nothing remains faithful. All low. Blip, blip, went the line.\nSuddenly he became frightened, for his wife was very jealous, and a lie, now, would be hard to explain. Whom would...", "0_1_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span> when the big sedan coughed and rolled to a stop on the warm desert sand fifty yards from the dance hall. The three girls in the back seat held their drinks up to the windows and giggled. \u201cCome on, girls,\u201d said Galatea, \u201clet's get it on.\u201d She called to the boys in front. \u201cHey, let's move it, we're here.\u201d\nIn the front seat, $FIRSTNAMEB3, with his long ape-like arms out straight at either side, clutching the canvas top, took hold of the guitar and made a swipe 65 crossing it with two great scratches and a thumbprint aimed right in the middle of his ten minutes' work. \u201cYeah!\u201d he yelled. \u201cIt's all over, baby! Nothin to do but Die. Roll on down the line !\u201d\nAnd he was out the door, rolling. Stan Shephard leaped out behind him. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was striding for the dance hall. \u201cWhoopee-ti-ti! Ta - ra - ra - boo-ti-ti!\u201d yelled Stan, shivering and crackling, \u201cOh boy let's go! Heey-la!", "0_1_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one eighteen in the morning</span>, Tuesday\u2014a bad time and a bad place to be sick in. Down the highway stretched the hell that is Biloxi. There were no distractions there, but many temptations. Off the highway, on the piers and in the alleys, this highway city of fifty-five thousand souls is given over to a people we only read about in the California newspapers.\nThe city comprises two separate sections, each with its own character, life, and logic. One district is made up of the businessmen, tourists, and conventioneers who stay in Biloxi for a purpose. The other district comprises loose people, and it is here that one goes to play when Biloxi serves no further purpose. But there\u2019s a certain kind of baseball park wisdom that goes like this: \u201cYou don\u2019t, when it\u2019s bottom of the ninth and you\u2019re behind by two goals, go walking inthe forbidden zone of the outfield and critique the terrain.\u201d In other words, there\u2019s nothing to say about that jungle out there, except to warn the fucked\u2014up pilgrim to tread carefully...watch his step...and remember there are no umpire brake lights on the last play of the game.", "2_21_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-two in the evening</span>.\n\u201cYour father told me about the whole Vlad-speaks-Sanskrit-thing. He\u2019s quite proud of it.\u201d\n\u201cThey really do watch the eyelashes, you know.\u201d\n\u201cI know. I\u2019ve seen them.\u201d\n\u201cHere\u2019s a question: In a fairly classic fictional sense, barring the whole business with the food-ordering howler monkey and the Spatula, Vlad works for Dad, right? Dad\u2019s the boss. Which makes Vlad, two steps removed maybe, still kind of Dad\u2019s pet, really. Now you obviously know a lot about Dad, hon. He\u2019s clearly got a little more on his plate than most people. Would you think it\u2019s possible for Dad to subconsciously find a way to help one of his pets escape the yoke of subservience, simply by having the pet order up obscenely spiced indian food? Did Dad ever have a pet that got out from under?\u201d", "2_0_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\u201cLet me carry those Papers, ma\u2019am,\u201d he said.\n\u201cYou want to join the Movement?\u201d she asked.\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d he laughed. \u201cI want to be in it, totally, one hundred percent!\u201d\n\u201cWhat are you doing in it?\u201d cried Lardnaumania Betini.\n\u201cWell, I\u2019ve got this fabulous Revolutionary Walk and Talk.\u201d\n\u201cDo you have your own car?\u201d said Lardnaumania Betini. She put the Grapes of Wrath in her armpit again, took her keys back out from under her blouse, and unlocked the Pinto.\n\u201cMa\u2019am, this is the first time I\u2019ve seen you. I swear,\u201d he said.\n\u201cHere,\u201d she said, flinging him the Keys. \u201cYou\u2019re now the Official Driver of this Movement.\u201d She got in the back and waited. In two minutes he was back.", "0_13_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nLunch was a long way off.\nI walked over to my desk people. \u201cGet any action at all?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cNot us?\u201d said a girl who might have been sixteen. She was an American. \u201cWe\u2019ve been waiting an hour, but still nothing has happened.\u201d\n\u201cNothing at all?\u201d I said.\n\u201cNo.\u201d\nAnna and I sat at a quiet table and ordered beer. There were no other customers in the bar and all around us the silence was enormous. It was the kind of silence that was never broken unless voices were raised and it was bad form to raise your voice in the Bar Milano at that time of the day. It was bad form to be drunk in the Bar Milano at any time of the day. Bad form to intrude on the silence. There were big signs all around:\nDO YOUR\nSPEAKING\nVOLUMELY\nAT A\nDISTANCE.\nBig, friendly signs that welcomed you to the place:\nCOLONEL BAUER ASKS\nFOR YOUR\nCOOPERATION\nIN MAINTAINING\nA QUIET ATMOSPHERE\nIN...", "2_13_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe great spiral staircase of the $SURNAME0 mansion was an exercise in height without discomfort. It wound completely around the central hall of the mansion, rising in easy arcs to the ear of Mercury himself. The mansion was three stories high.\nThere were seventy-three steps.\nIn harmonium factories all over the earth, the workers fell silent as the two-o\u2019clock hour came round in each locality.\nThe workers fell silent in Europe, Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Greenland, and the Antarctic.\nEach worker, as he stopped his hand on his working machine, became aware that he was only one among many millions, that this thing that had happened to himself was, in itself, no more significant than the stopping of a clock.\nEvery two minutes, the President of the United States receives a flash Presidential-priority message from the owner of Venus and the unseen master of Mars. At one o\u2019clock in the afternoon, when the attack on $FIRSTNAMEB2 was ordered, the President of the United States was chatting with his mistress in a closet at Blair House, having earlier in the day announced his decision to seek a reconciliation with his wife.", "2_9_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>, Mediterranean time.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 got out of bed. They barely touched the bedclothes.\nThere was no shower, no ceremonial ablutions. The Great Prophet and the Miracle Dog got right to work. They worked as though Earth and all her works depended upon their tending every detail of each detail with loving intensity.\nBehind their shutters, the eyes of all the nuns flashed. Water in their systems gurgled and popped in radiant joy. The shutters flew open, the sandalwood got busy.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 snapped on their cosmic beam.\nIt was a blue line of living light that came from $SURNAME0\u2019s navel, passed through the crown of his head and the tip of his dog\u2019s tail, and connected the two.\nThe light shot out six inches on $SURNAME0\u2019s end, four feet on $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s. It ran straight out, hit the wall and went up it like a curtain. It was a curtain that did not obstruct vision\u2014rather, it enhanced vision. One saw things the much, much more clearly for having the blue line between oneself and those things.", "1_17_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe space ship was going to appear any second now, overhead. It would come out of the violet sky like a falling star. It would end by falling on Newport like a white Orphan Annie pumpkin.\nThere was perhaps a minute to go. $FIRSTNAMEB0 wondered how he would be when the space ship actually arrived. He wanted to cry, and he wanted to laugh.\nIn among his black beard, his lips purpled in a smile. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was wondering what the people at home were thinking while they waited for the space ship to fall on them.\nGod damn, there it was!\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shut his eyes when it came out of the sky like a falling star. He shut his eyes, and he still saw the afterimage of the space ship on the inside of his eyelids.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB0 looked again, the space ship was almost directly overhead. It was wheeling and flashing bright beams on the streets and buildings beneath it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 could hear the crowd screaming. He could hear the band playing. He could hear the coughing and sneezing of people whose hay fever was being aggravated by the shower of blue powder. He could hear the general din of a party in full swing.\nPeople dashed about below the space...", "1_16_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-four in the afternoon</span>, Central Standard Time.\nThe morning\u2019s rain was over. The air was cool and moist. The sky was blue.\nAnd, from all appearances, the Stonecipheco baby who would be dubbed $SURNAME1 Bundgard had a splendid and sweet future of unprecedented good fortune and contentment stretching out before him.\nAnd it all began with Prudence, who stood at the crib of her son, admired him, felt sad about him, loved him.\nThat\u2019s place enough for the story to begin, for nothing truly consequential happened to the child for some time, and the story itself, without anything more than the affection of a good mother to justify and sustain it, is not a pretty one, so let\u2019s take a moment to get acquainted with the lady.\nThe tight circles under the eyes since last night were circles of fatigue, not circles of sorrow. The sleep of the previous night had been a good deal more than seven hours deep. All thoughts of Pierce Inverarity had vanished in that sleep. The circles were merely the result of having waked early to nurse the baby. She saw nothing unusual in that. She knew she had no more than four or five more waking hours before exhaustion would bring her merciful unconscious...", "0_6_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six sixteen in the morning</span>.\n\"By God!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, his voice full of consternation. $FIRSTNAMEB0 turned to him.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was staring at the wall. \"That woke me up!\" he said. He didn\u2019t say it to $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Huh?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"What\u2014what was that?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"I sure hope\u2014\" he said.\n\"What was what?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"It was my heart, I think,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"You didn\u2019t have no heart,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Then it was my kidneys,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"Or my lungs\u2014or something.\" He put his hand to his head. One corner of his mouth went down. \"I honestly think I\u2019m gonna have the most awful attack of the agues,\" he said.\nThere was a knock on the door.\n\"Oh, God\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"What, what, what?\" He looked around the room frantically.\n\"It\u2019s the officers!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"They said they was goin\u2019 to come and visit us, remember?\" He stepped to the door, unbolted it, turned the knob.\nKirov,...", "0_17_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThere was no telling how long it would be before the Army of Mars decided that whatever it was that was happening on the moon meant that Earth deserved the worst it could give her.\nEarthlings did not have much of a communications problem. The people who mattered on Earth were in a very small room at a very big table. That room was a basement below the intelligence building at Thiokol, Inc. That big table was a long slab of African teak, sanded and oiled, with a single ripple across its surface, and a coat of polyurethane to protect the wood from ballpoint pens.\nWhen the polyurethane was dry, the table was delivered to the basement, the wood protected by tar paper and drape. The room was then protected against spying by tar paper and drape.\nThree Secret Service men, three FBI agents, and ten Thrintun representatives made that basement room their own. The Thrintun had insisted on candles, battery-powered. The better to play their version of poker in.\nThere were moments when it wasn\u2019t easy to tell Americans and Thrintun apart. Sitting in that room, keeping an eye on the pulsing communications console was one of those moments.", "2_22_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty in the evening</span>.\nThe Space Wanderer was over $SURNAME0\u2019s estate.\nThe Space Wanderer was alone.\nWhen the Space Wanderer had returned home from greeting the space ship, $FIRSTNAMEF0 had refused to lie beside him again.\nShe lay on a couch and refused to talk.\nEvery ten minutes she smoked one $FIRSTNAMEM1 cigarette.\nThe $FIRSTNAMEM1 cigarettes came in a carton that she had ordered through the Dog\u2019s Order Catalog. They were the only items of earthly goods she had ordered in two years. The cigarettes were delivered loose in a box. All $FIRSTNAMEF0 did was remove the cellophane wrap.\nThroughout the Universe, whenever two or more beings encountered one another, catastrophe or at least bewilderment was not long in following.\nThe interactions of the beings were like an unfortunate series of pinballs percolating through the universe.\nWhen the pinballs stopped, the score totaled ruin.\nSo, when the Space Wanderer saw the young paranoiac who had been flung into the unwholesome atmosphere of the Dust Ball of Pirpheus, he had only this to say\u2014and to say it to himself:\nAny being at all who interacts hereafter with the being you have become is going to be sorry.", "0_19_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span> of the last sunset of the world. The time was of small signification. There would be no more evenings.\nAntichrist had just come into possession of the recording he was about to play for the Redeemer, who lay bound hand and foot on the blue living room rug.\nThe Lord\u2019s head was resting on his dented metal pail, from which drops of milk ran onto the rug. His eyes were closed, his clothing tattered, his cheek hobnailed. Whence come ye, O Best-Beloved? ($FIRSTNAMEM2, 1: 11).\nThe burned letters in the bookcase were $FIRSTNAMEM2. If a man receiveth $FIRSTNAMEM2, he also receiveth me.\nChronology. Antichrist had decided to explain all to the Redeemer after only ten minutes of preparation. The irrecoverable time was one hour, sixteen minutes, and forty seconds.\nAntichrist switched on the machine he had spent ten minutes getting ready. Here was the noise of the Spiral Galactic still decaying in the air\u2014the roar of coming Saints.\n\"Listen, O Lord,\" said the ten-year-old Antichrist, who towered over the Redeemer, even while sitting.", "0_22_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-seven in the evening</span>. St. Louis lay behind us, remember, and so did all that weird Scene business in the first-class Compartment back there. But I digress. We had another weird scene here, in the baggage car. We leaned on the car wall and watched the lights go by and talked about jazz for two hours and then walked up and down aisles and sat on bags, smoking cigarettes and leaning on the wall again. This car was half filled with bums like us, and with lonely travelling salesmen. It was interesting, but it wasn't the Compartment and the dinner with the millionaires. And that drunken truck-driver downtown had given me a sinister sense of foreboding. Where was $FIRSTNAMEB3? In jail? In a hospital? Dead in a gutter? Where was Carlo Marx? Where was Old Bull Lee? We must be coming up to the Illinois Line, a tremendous boundary for us, for that meant Chicago was two hundred miles ahead. Only two hundred miles -- New York and the Bull was five hundred away. We suddenly made a tour of the car to look for Terry and the gang. $FIRSTNAMEB3 wasn't in the baggage car; they all reported. The destination signs flicked by, hour after hour. We began to talk to a tall...", "1_11_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-nine in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2-scholastic theory asserted that the probability of Stonehenge\u2019s loss was ninety-nine point one eight percent between one o\u2019clock and two o\u2019clock in the afternoon. The accidents in Munich and Arizona, then, might now be expected to occur between three-fifty p.m. and four-fifty p.m.\u2014if the accidents were going to occur at all. The drummer suddenly raised his head and smiled at $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"You in the Army, sweetheart?\" he said. \"You in the Big Army?\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was not in the Big Army. He had gone to the recruitment office of the Big Army, had tried on a uniform, had filled out forms, had been given a bonus, had decided that the Army was the parasite-enterprise he wanted to patronize most. But he had never shown up for the actual taking of the oath.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 tried on the uniform so the Army would have to give him the bonus without his ever showing up to carry out his part of the bargain. That was the first and last time $FIRSTNAMEB0 ever wore a uniform\u2014going to the Army office, trying it on.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shrugged now. \"Hell, yes,\" he...", "1_8_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-eight in the morning</span> and I had nine thousand dollars. I went out in the street and looked for a taxi. Half an hour later I was on the train to Paris, looking at the green cotton-fields and the white village churches, and feeling the fresh breeze that blew right through to my lungs and tickled me. You take Prussic acid [in spirit] and you sit in the train that roars along in the night with the strange, evil happiness that comes when you're going away, and each minute is a minute gained and you think gratefully of it and let it blow through you just like fresh air. For every minute is worth a kroner to a man who leaves Paris. There's not much to do in Paris when you've really lived it. Some people sit in Paris androt and some people leave. This trick of leaving is the best trick in the world; it's better than any cure in Arizona. I loved every green thing and every white village church I saw. By God, I was glad to leave Paris. . . . 7 Same that afternoon at three o'clock I was sitting in a huge chair on the balcony of the Hotel Montalembert in Paris, fairly well convalescent and drinking in the sun, the fresh air...", "1_4_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-three in the morning</span>, and I saw it was better that we have time to argue it out before every body else in the house was rounded up and informed that a frightful dangerous criminal was lurking in their midst. \u201cWe both know my car,\u201d I said. \u201cThe big white convertible VW with that big fucker Dog lying right in the middle of the back seat. You can\u2019t miss it\u2014you\u2019ll probably run over the damn thing.\"\n\u201cWe\u2019ll look,\u201d he said.\nAnd that was that. I gave him the brown bag, picked up my valise and trundled off toward the house, still laughing. He had me dead to rights, there was no way to argue my way out of it. He knew what was in that bag, and so did I. There was no point in trying to bullshit him. The only thing left to do was clown around until he got tired of it and went back to the office.\nThe only reason I got away with it so easy was because he didn\u2019t expect to see me there: by this time it was widely assumed, all over town, that I had escaped. I\u2019d checked out of the Mint Hotel on Monday and...", "1_23_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven sixteen in the evening</span>. The place was the living room of a suite in the Northrup Hotel in Burbank, California. The place and the time are written into the annals, for Norman Bombardini, owner of all of Little Rock, Arkansas, paid the bill in the morning. He found everything satisfactory, except that Boglin was missing.\nMinarcinni found the telephone at last. He dialed a number and waited. His son-in-law\u2019s voice came through, asking who was calling and promising terrible things to anybody who had disturbed his evening with the men he secretly regarded as his peasant inferiors, who were really his partners.\n\"This is your father-in-law,\" said Minarcinni. \"Can you reach your wife by phone?\"\n\"No,\" said the son-in-law, who disliked the telephone. His wife did all his talking for him. \"Where is she?\"\nMinarcinni read him the number. The son-in-law had it by heart. Her purse always lay by her bed, with the pocket open and a pencil always in it to write down phone numbers and movie directors\u2019 names and brand names of cigarettes, and movie locations and notes to herself about which advertising men...", "1_15_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of Thursday, October 11, 2021\u2014but in truth it was all now one eternal afternoon. The place was a table set up in front of a cubicle labeled Dr. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 in the offices of the American Ministry of Space Exploration on Titan, one of the moons of Saturn. The table was laden with papers and files pertaining to a proposed instantiation of something called a $FIRSTNAMEB2-Integrator. The $FIRSTNAMEB2-Integrator was to be the prime time-and-space navigation and orientation instrument of flying-saucer paratroopers and space scuba divers.\nThe paratroopers and scuba divers were the newly created Space Improvement Corps. They were the Shock Troops of the Brotherhood of Man.\nThe flying saucers and space-diving equipment were to be made available to all nations.\nAnd what was the purpose of this brotherhood of man, this getting together of all nations with all manner of different languages, cultures, moral codes, and approaches to the meaning of life? The purpose was to carry joyful goods, to carry the goods of life, to a silent planet three of whose five green-blue oceans were unglazed waste, to a planet whose drowned coastline formed a continuous gravel strand half the...", "2_9_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-five in the morning</span>.\nThe face was two feet long. The rest of the body was hidden by the thick steel wall. The right eye lugged noticeably, so that the face had a drunken look, although the eyes were always clear.\nThe face blew kisses and blew tobacco at the unshaven sentry. The sentry could not see the face on account of the thick steel wall. The sentry could only hear the face.\nThe face receded hurriedly.\nAn old man with white hair and a rugged, handsome face appeared at the window. He wore a great, white sheepskin coat. He carried a submachine gun of German manufacture.\nHe too blew kisses and blew tobacco at the sentry.\nThe sentry could hear the old man clearly. The old man apparently had decided not to be a shy and polite child for once.\nThe old man said pleasantly, \"Well, good buddy, how the hell are you?\"\nThe sentry said, without moving, without looking, \"I\u2019m okay.\"\n\"Say, good buddy, this ain\u2019t so bad,\" said the old man.\n\"Not so bad,\" said the sentry.", "1_19_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past seven o'clock in the evening</span> of the summer solstice; and the sun had just sunk below the rim of the world, and the western ocean (seen through the window by peering round the frame of the jammed door) had begun to glow. Seen through the sunlight that poured in at the window to the left of the doorway the insubstantial canvas of the tent seemed as impalpable as the vapour of a dream. On a bed of air, as it were, rested a bower of homely English plants and flowers. They had seen life reflected in the calm waters of the English Channel, and by some magic were preserved whole, and seemed of a verdure that would fade but never wither, and would at the last be wafted into the heart of the golden clouds. Spotted all over with sun-beams the canvas showed the chequered chairs, the oak table, and the iron pots, that mottled it, all more sharply. Sheltering the wise and noble heads of withered bulbs as a mother safeguards the craniums of her children were pots of paper covered with moist oaken leaves. With their rich green foliage and seed-vessels of a gold that differed from the gold of the rest of the decoration, and the glow...", "1_11_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty in the morning</span>, and I knew where I'd be that night\u2014in the casinos of downtown Las Vegas, I would teke things nice, easy and peaceful and avoid everything that looked like trouble. I knew where I\u2019d be the next night, too, and the next . . .\nAnd after that?...I didn't know, but I sensed something good. I could feel things opening up, as if all facets of my being were turning under the hot sun of the west. I sensed order and harmony, each component part of my brain and body returning to a natural and inevitable balance after the crazy embalming of hot desire that had dominated me for so many months. The big shark-machines off the coast seemed far behind now; our time together was finished, dead and gone forever. There was something else calling me.\nMy mind turned to my attorney and his bickering wife. \u201cWhy don't you two pack your bags,\u201d I said, \u201cand I'll put you on the next plane to Acapulco.\"\nHe was up in the balcony, mixing drinks for the girls. \u201cWhy don't you go, too?\" he yelled. \u201cThat way, we could all go down there together...", "0_4_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Wait a bit,\" rumbled the bass boat just behind us. \"I'll be with you in a minute, boys.\"\n\"Listen to that son of a bitch!\" yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3, and it was even more raucous than before, he was really laughing. This was the funniest thing. It didn\u2019t bother him that he was losing his girl to Eddie, because now he realized he was living his life in the warm holyCity of Calcutta, and if he lost it he'd still have his life, whereas before he'd lost his life and didn't know it. Both of us yelled with joy. The maniac in the stern yelled with us; anything we said he said, too. The red lights on the T\u2014dock grew bigger.\n\"Where you going?\" I cried to the shadowy forms leaning on the rail.\n\"Sonny's Bar,\" they yelled back.\n\"Is Stan there?\"\n\"He's drunk asleep on the floor.\"\n\"Well, here we come!\" I yelled. And for the first time in my life I made a drunken giant of a human being wait on me. I waited. The bloated paper bag on the T\u2014dock swayed back and forth, the men...", "1_5_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-eight in the morning</span>. A madhouse of adventure and good feelings filled my heart. I was ready to go out into the naked world and tell everybody how much I loved them. I flew out of this world and into the next. I looked for my last cent on the fuzzy carpet. It wasn't there. I rushed over to the closet and threw open the door -- and there on the floor, sitting all alone in the dark, with sparks shooting out of his ears, was the old man. Here were two fucked machines, ready to knock hell out of each other if they had the least excuse. I stared at him with hate. His eyes rose to meet mine. \u201cFuck you!\u201d he snorted, and turned away. I flew to my sleeping bag and rolled it up in a ball -- my Remington, my papers, my postcards and telegrams. I threw it in my bad, picked up the still - sweating whisky bottle, ran humbly to the concierge's desk, and begged him to contact a taxi for me. \u201cMister Gregson said you'd pay,\u201d he said sternly. Well, let teacher pay. Lord knows he had enough left in that pile. But the final exchange took place with the old...", "1_6_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six sixteen in the morning</span> of a fine late-spring day, and the place was the imposing entrance to the New York World\u2019s Fair. The man who pressed the button ought to have been looking at the Fair itself, at that Paradise of the Phoenix, the Flamingo, and the Trylon, rising on his right. He could see none of them. His view was limited to the insignificant front door of a moderate, rectangular building, a one-story structure with a flat, concrete roof.\nThe six-story rectangle was the United States Subway Terminal. The projecting, low-level entrance led to a crowded waiting room. Next came a long, bare corridor. Down the corridor, at the other end, the man could see a room full of many anxious faces in the fluorescent glare. Fear and hope were both plainly visible. The man did not reflect upon fear and hope, because he had never felt these feelings.\nConcentration on the usual morning face in the crowd would have revealed a bearded, middle-sized face, dark-complected, its owner possibly Spanish, possibly Indian. The owner\u2019s eyes had looked down at the floor before the two policemen came over to him. Now his eyes did not move, as he pressed the button beside the imposing...", "1_15_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-one in the afternoon</span>, and the odor of ether gas rolling out over the plant fence, mixed with the scent of the lilac flowers in my shirt, waved me like a flag. I stopped in the office to say hello, to collect my mail\u2014and found every drawer in my desk littered with half-smoked pack of cigarettes. There must have been a hundred of them. I hadn\u2019t smoked that much since I was thirteen.\n\u201cThat\u2019s all you\u2019re getting for a raise,\u201d the Personnel manager complained. \u201cHow the hell are we going to take that damn stuff away from you?\"\n\u201cTake it away from me?\u201d I said. \u201cIt\u2019s a pleasure to have it around.\"\n\u201cHow come you\u2019re smoking so much?\"\n\u201cI\u2019m nervous. I\u2019ve got a lot on my mind.\"\n\u201cTry to cut down on it. At least smoke the butts somebody else leaves behind. Don\u2019t pay for them.\"\n\u201cNot a chance,\u201d I said. \u201cIt would kill me to do that.\u201d And I put the pack in my palm and walked across the parking lot, bouncing...", "0_16_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. A soft white drizzle made the Luxembourg gardens seem as though they were covered with powdered sugar.\nIn a taxi, Issy Schlesinger, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 reached the freight yards without being seen by a single Martian. The taxi was left in a slot in the depot. The empty taxi took up no room in the slot. None of the apes could drive an Earthling automobile worth a damn.\nThe rain put out any fires that might have made the slot a death-trap for Issy, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nThey left the depot in the drizzle to find a freight ship for Titan.\nUnder a grand clock at the depot were scrawled the words Nig-ger, die, honky, and Eate. $FIRSTNAMEB0 recognized the writing. $FIRSTNAMEB0 remembered having written the words.\nThe clock under which the words were written had stopped at forty-three minutes after nine, twenty-one years before. That was the time when $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his company of paratroopers had been trapped in the depot. The clock was still wrong.\nAny soldier who stayed long enough in one place to study a clock very closely would make a surprising discovery. He would learn that Earthling clocks were designed to run a...", "2_8_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-eight in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time.\nAt the moment, only three Sun-watchers were at the museum.\nThey were Ph.D. candidates in biology named Moldworg, Newton, and Parazard. They had been placed there by their supervisor, Dr. Jacob Jonah MacKenzie, so that they could get some experience watching the Sun.\nAt the moment, all three Sun-watchers hated the Sun without qualification.\nMoldworg had been up all night, preparing a presentation he was to make on the biology and behaviors of the prehistoric $SURNAME0 up at Cambridge in two days. In addition to being sleepy and hung over, the heartless Sun made him feel stupid.", "2_22_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-seven in the evening</span> on Earth.\nUnexpectedly, a reception committee formed on the banks of the canal outside the prison walls. It consisted of one empty taxicab, one empty police van, one empty fire engine, and one empty vigilante. The street-sprinkling machine of the city of Newport, Rhode Island, had been commandeered and sent skidding to a stop beside the canal. The mayor of Newport had commandeered it personally. He was in the vigilante.\nThe commandeerer of the police van was the chief of police himself. He was in the fire engine.\nThe membership of the reception committee was as follows:\nThe taxicab contained two girls and a youth named $SURNAME1.\nThe police van contained an old man, an old woman, and an old woman\u2019s mother. These were all the relations the old man had. The old man\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEB2. He was the father of $FIRSTNAMEF0. His home had been leveled by a $SURNAME0 earthquake.\nThe fire engine contained $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s mate and a good friend of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s. The mate\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEF1. The friend\u2019s name was Salo. $FIRSTNAMEF1 was expecting a baby.", "2_16_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span> when something malfunctioned in my soul. I no longer cared. Whatever neurotic impulse had driven me this far was suddenly gone. I was stony indifferent to whatever might happen next.\nSomebody\u2019s gotta go...why not you? Why not me? I even had a knife. The switchblade I\u2019d bought in Alexandria was still open in my lap. I saw it all from this new vantagepoint where I sat stone inert, while my attorney struggled desperately to hot\u2014start the Red Shark.\n\u201cFuck it!\u201d he shouted. \u201cI knew we should have brought that fuckin' mechanic!\"\n\u201cWe can take a taxi,\u201d I said.\n\u201cYeah? And have this piece\u2014of\u2014shit car, towed?\u201d he yelled. \u201cNo thanks!\u201d He was slapping the steering wheel with both hands. \u201cThis fucking thing is on the way out!\u201d Was he right? I doubted it, but I didn't really care. The engine finally coughed, then turned over; and I let it idle with the choke as we sat there, trapped in space.", "0_22_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-nine in the evening</span>, when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized in theWinston $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 estate in Newport. Victoria Winters, the housekeeper, was in the library with her sewing circle. Her little girl, $FIRSTNAMEF1, was being bathed in the sink of the warden\u2019s bath by Mrs. Earl Ingersoll.\nWhat the child was being bathed in was warm water with a little Ivory soap floated on its upper surface. The sink was full of bubbles that popped and lotioned the child\u2019s skin most agreeably.\nMrs. Ingersoll\u2019s perfume was strong, and this perfume was not only wafted to the child by the air in the close confines of the library but was circulated under pressure from a conduit in the walls into the ventilating system of the whole house.\nThe perfume was Maya Leguna. It was composed primarily of oil of cloves and the essence of woman.\nThe poor Martian woman had never so much as sniffed powder or lipstick, but now her only food was cocoa butter, burnt brown by the sun, and here was this gorgeous perfume coming out of every pore.\nIn the closet-sized room that was the warden\u2019s...", "1_0_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past midnight</span>. I was all set to continue the grand binge that night. But $FIRSTNAMEB3 was in the dumps. \u201cOh, man, $FIRSTNAMEB6, what are we doing isn't this all foolish, let's get out of here, we're crazy --yes, yes, of course we came for $FIRSTNAMEF4, but she's mad at me and Etan, it's hopeless as long as we stay in New York -- and it's cold.\u201d Whereupon he began throwing his belongings together. I looked out the window at the weeping November night, the lamps twinkling rouge-green in the mist, the streets full of lonely cars, the moon hidden in the smog. High in the flicking neon rodeo of Times Square I saw the figure of R, in a lightstandard, staring downward. I had a tremendous fondness for that mystery man, who seemed absolutely lost in the labyrinth. I wished we could talk together. Everything was breaking, writhing, and crumbling. The grand Egyptian ball at the Plaza Hotel was over for me. Something was about to happen. I didn't know what it was; $FIRSTNAMEB3, probably. His face was grimy from forcing his head through slammed cars. Traffic lights changed before his face and he didn't even...", "2_9_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich time. Alfred Butterworth, the man, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the man, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, the woman, lived on Newport Earth. They lived apart from one another, but they lived together in the same age, in the same milieu, and by and large in the same class.\nButterworth, $SURNAME0, and Mrs. $SURNAME0 were as unalike in their attitudes as Father, Son, and Holy Ghost.\nThe attitudes were these:\nButterworth was stark raving mad and didn\u2019t know it.\n$SURNAME0 was stark raving mad and knew it perfectly well.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 was decently and quietly mad, but unaware that there was anything wrong with her.\nThese three were moving toward a rendezvous of great importance to them. They converged rapidly. Mrs. $SURNAME0 and Butterworth were moving by foot. $SURNAME0 was moving by gyroscopically controlled solar taxi.\nTheir rendezvous was at the foot of the sundial in the courtyard of the $SURNAME0 estate.\nThe time was nine minutes to ten o\u2019clock in the morning, Greenwich time.", "1_20_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>. It would be dark by nine. And then would come the long, deep, numbing press of the night.\nAt the table at the foot of my bed were the remains of some sad meal, uneaten by me. The window was shut and opened out on the deep dark.\nDown on the dock some people laughed.\nWithout looking I knew the geography of the bungalow. I knew where every stick of furniture stood and I knew the direction in which each door opened. I knew where every book lay on every shelf.\nI went over to the bath-room, turned on the hot water, undressed, and bathed my face. The floor was sopping wet.\nI took up the telephone and called the New York Book Store.\n\"Let me speak to Mr. Campbell, please,\" I said, \"this is Miss Balfe calling from San Remo.\"\n\"I'm sorry,\" said a nice voice, \"Mr. Campbell is out just now. I'll tell him you called.\"\n\"No,\" I said, \"don't bother. I'll try to get him later.\"\nI put the receiver down. Of course I would try to get him. I must have certain books to...", "0_20_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-six in the evening</span>.\nBruce went to the telephone and called New York at once. \"Say,\" he cried into the mouthpiece, \"stop everything! Put an embargo on it! Don\u2019t tell anybody, but call up Malmquist and you\u2019ll get a man named Tarski. His number is\u2014\" He looked around for a pencil\u2014in vain. \"Never mind the number. Tell him to come down to Centerville at once. In fact, tell him to hire a plane. I\u2019ll be here in the lobby waiting for him\u2014and you tell the people downtown for God\u2019s sake to stop that model Earth of theirs. It\u2019s making everybody in town flip!\"\nThe numberless crowd in the lobby had still not condensed itself into people. At eight fifty-two, this had not yet happened. Instead, the crowd continued to behave like a cloud of protons, a quantity of electrically charged matter. It had mass, it had density, and it surrounded, but it did not illuminate. At the center of the cloud snorted and yelped, making dust of the people around him, a bull, the foreman of such a gang of men as had never before been seen together in one...", "0_22_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 brought $FIRSTNAMEB0 down to the cave. $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s housemates were all asleep.\nWhen he came to the entrance to the cave, $FIRSTNAMEB0 put his thumb over his ax handle to keep the blade from rattling.\nAt first, what was inside the cave made no impression on $FIRSTNAMEB0. His terror at being up in the dark, at being suspended in space, at being of no determinate size or weight\u2014at being, for all practical purposes, invisible\u2014made him oblivious to all other phenomena.\nFinally he forced himself to concentrate on what was inside the cave. It was a beautiful young woman, crouching by a fire and cooking something in a pot.\nHer beauty did not surprise or delight $FIRSTNAMEB0. He was simply relieved by its familiarity.\nIn the voluntary army on Mars, there had been an overweight short, dark-haired girl named Brandywine, with a beauty just that bright and fresh and undamaged and cheerful. That girl was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s wife, and this was his housemate and wife on Mercury. He went in to her gladly.\nHis fear left him. He knew who he was and where he was. He was a married man who had gone out for a walk...", "2_9_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThe social conventions within which Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s gracious personality had developed required that she express either admiration for the view from her bedroom window, or interest in whatever it was her husband now had to say to her. She turned to face him.\n\"Davy,\" she said archly, \"I do hope you aren\u2019t about to tell me Earth is going to be destroyed.\"\n\"Earth,\" said Mr. $SURNAME0, \"is going to be destroyed, certainly.\"\n\"You aren\u2019t well, are you, darling?\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. \"Would you like some kiwi-fruit juice?\"\n$SURNAME0 laughed a robust, sane laugh.\n\"Is it time, honestly?\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0. \"Do you still have some control over when the destruction comes?\"\n\"I have absolutely no control over the moment the disaster begins,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I can control, to some extent, when it ends.\"\n\"When does it end, then?\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0.\n\"In about fifty-five minutes,\" said $SURNAME0.", "2_19_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe chrono-synclastic infundibulated Martian named Newport was ready to roll over and fly off in search of his next victim.\nHe had been speculating about the identity and conduct of his next material body. Having toyed with the idea of a talking bear, a bird of paradise, a blond dwarf, and a Siamese cat, he wondered about a savannah or a babbling brook. And just before he left his tower, he saw, off in the jungle, a splendid specimen of Homo sapiens.\nNewport\u2019s last words on Mars were not words of Martian, nor were they words of English, nor were they a mechanical series of phonetic approximations to words in any known language of Earth. Before leaving Mars, Newport spoke, on his own behalf, 1,172 words in perfectly pronounced, flawlessly delivered good Oxford English.", "2_11_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was St. Patrick\u2019s Cathedral in New York City. The purpose of the materialization was to ask for money.\nCrowds of perhaps three thousand, in a city whose population was eight million, had come out to see if there was anything as interesting as a materialization, and were disappointed. $SURNAME0 and his text disappeared immediately, as they always did.\nThere had been no message. The imposition of order by silence was in itself a perfectly sardonic comment on the order expected ofRumfoord by men.\nThere had been no sacrilege. $SURNAME0 would materialize in St. Patrick\u2019s any time he damn well felt like it.\nOld Kurt optimistically thought in terms of picocuries\u2014that old American nuclear-power-plant measure of radioactivity: a thousandth of a roentgen.\nIt wasn\u2019t until he and Salo had been shut inside the Taj Mahal for six Earthling hours that it became clear that the Solar winds were funnier than anybody had suspected. The air inside the Taj Mahal turned out to be about sixty times as radioactive as the Martian atmosphere outside.", "2_9_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine eighteen in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB4 was leaking. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was quivering.\nBlue Ragg abstained from gnawing on his own leg for the next twenty seconds.\nIn accordance/with instructions from the Stone Age to the Space Age, from the Milky Way to the Festival Hall, from the Western Hemisphere to the Eastern Hemisphere, from $FIRSTNAMEB1 to $FIRSTNAMEB4, the raggities spun around, faced the unattainable horizon, and faced their common father, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nBeing blind, the raggities faced nothing in particular.\nIn this attitude of ceremonial devotion, the raggities remained unmoving for forty-eight seconds.\nThe waiting audience finally grew restless.\nAt the end of forty-eight seconds\u2014forty-nine seconds by Earthling time\u2014an advance man for the concert forcibly removed the raggities from their parental position. He made them do a comic dance in place.\nThe audience laughed, applauded.\nThere was more music.\nIt was nine twenty-six in the morning. The music continued.", "1_3_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty in the morning</span> and I had to do something. The newspaper guy wasn't around. The Oklahoman used to supply newspapers to the bar when the regular paper\u2014boy was out on his route. But the Okla homan paper-boys seemed to be so crazy these days I didn't dare go get a paper from them. This was actually a good sign: the world was beginning to come to an end and everybody was going crazy. I tried to make my mind a blank.\nAhmet approached the cafe with his hair slicked down with water and wearing a red turtleneck sweater. He approached the bar with great confidence. \u201cI'm ready for that tequila-orange juice now,\u201d he said, pushing himself on the bartender. \u201cWhere did everybody go?\u201d\nThe bartender regarded him grimly. He didn't even ask to see any money. He just shook his head no and turned away.\n\u201cHey! Where's my vodka and water?\u201d screamed Snorty. \u201cSomebody give me a goddamn vodka and water!\u201d It was four o'clock in the morning. Things were getting frightening. \u201cHey, you! Yes, you, in the spectacles! Where's my goddamn vodka...", "0_17_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME1 was frantic to get rolling. Space travel did nothing to help his business sense.\nHe calculated that he only had thirty-four Earthling hours in which to make as much money as possible before $FIRSTNAMEB0 got loose and turned everything topsy-turvy.\nConrad and $FIRSTNAMEB1 opened fire on the returning space pilot from their strategic positions in the cubicles.\nAt the touch of their fingertips, marble walls blossomed with perfect clusters of needles.\nThe pilot couldn\u2019t have advanced more than a few yards through that barrage. He turned and went back to the elevator shaft, bleeding from four serious, one superficial wound.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 stepped over him and got into the elevator. The wounded pilot rode up to the ground floor, regained his Mercury Ship, and flew screaming out of town.\nThere was blessed peace, then, in the little office on the ground floor of the administrative building, and there was blessed privacy, too. $FIRSTNAMEB0 closed the door, turned on the radio and danced a happy dance while he waited for the round thing that flew.\nThe round thing that flew made its coming known in the office the instant $FIRSTNAMEB0 had turned on the radio. The little spaceship, having been painstakingly camouflaged before the flight to...", "0_12_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-four post meridiem</span> of the sixteenth of September. As the insomniacs set sail in their dry, quiet San Francisco Bay, something interesting was occurring to, of, and above the Earth.\nNo mathema tician in his right senses ever bothers to investigate whether or not his toothache has a numerical value. Unless he is a bore, he doesn\u2019t usually bother to investigate whether his happiness or misery has a numerical value.\nThe Redwine family shared in common certain things that rightly or wrongly mathematicians care very little about, and one of them was the notion that numbers were more important to their lives than they really were. The time would come when $FIRSTNAMEB0, the bearer of the book, would try to communicate to his son, the bearer of the harmonium, a beautiful and profound discovery\u2014an amazing discovery. That discovery was that there was something more important to life than numbers. That important thing was surprise.\nOne might define surprise as a conflict between expectation and result.\nThe harmoniums put out flat, jug-jug, jissom melodies in perfect harmony with what they expected. Since their expectation was nothing more than a timetable of Contractual obligations, the melodies were bound to be flat, jug-jug, jiss...", "0_0_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-nine ante meridiem</span>.\n$SURNAME0 was timing the war, and as far as he knew, the war was going well.\nIt was going so well, in fact, that he decided to risk going out onto his front porch again. It was a calculated risk. A Tree had burned away not two feet from the front door, and it was smoldering still.\nWith excellent control, $SURNAME0 kept his tremor to a minimum as he opened his front door. He stepped outside. His foot struck an object. It was the fountain of fountain pens. The little wheels were turning weakly on the ends of their hydras.\n$SURNAME0 kicked the fountain over and stepped on it good and hard. He snapped off a precious Waterman Every-Postable $3.50 retractable before the thing was entirely smashed.\n$SURNAME0 wasn\u2019t too crippled to handle a ballpoint pen.\nHe tore a strip of white paper from a notebook near the telephone, dropped the pen onto the paper, wrote: Mr. Meadowcraft, the unsoldierly cripple you sent me takes delight in reporting that the skyships are flying very high. K. R. $SURNAME0.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, an ind...", "2_15_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe first thing $FIRSTNAMEF1 did outside the walls of the Manor was to drop, in one motion, clothes, G-string, and the face tint. She recovered fresh dignity from her wicker suit case. She powdered her face to its normal hue, rouged her lips, and restored the face tint.\nShe dressed in clothes she had chosen for the trip back into the past\u2014colorless, classless, sexless coveralls that were issued free to visitors to Mercury. The hard glass boots in the wicker suit case were ill-adapted to the terrain on the immediate horizons of the Manor, but the boots served a purpose.\nThe glass boots were brilliantly visible.\nNobody was going to sneak up behind Madame Curie for the purpose of doing her in. The visibility of the boots was linked to another advantage of the boots, an advantage that was psychological rather than practical; the boots were a constant reminder to $FIRSTNAMEF1 of who she was on Mars.\nLive by the glories of the past.\nDie by the glories of the past.", "2_4_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty in the morning</span>, Earth Standard Time. The place was $FIRSTNAMEB2, Mars.\nMoonwater, $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s mother, was seated a hundred yards away from her son\u2019s space ship, in a rocking chair on the porch of her cottage.\nShe was fifty years old, old for a Martian.\nShe was well preserved, however, and still as handsome as a saddle-leather suitcase.\nMoonlight had an interesting effect on Moonwater\u2019s silky skin. The shadings and hollows that light brought out in Moonwater would have caused an Earthling to suppose that the skin was not the finest of materials\u2014that it was, indeed, porous and spider-webby. The impression given by the skin was that it might easily be torn by a strong wind, that it might easily catch a cold.\nMoonwater was, as far as anyone could tell, emaciated. She weighed seventy pounds Earthling pounds.\nShe was hollow as an eggshell, and apparently as fragile. In reality, she was extremely strong. Her greatest lover had called her the Iron Bitch.", "0_23_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic infundibulated, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had been blown from the sterile, chrome-yellow sands of the Mojave Desert more than nineteen years before. He was not due to materialize again until the moment of his birthday on the planet Mars\u2014which planet, according to his contentions, was named after a horse with gilded hoofs that ran away once around the block with a sailor on its back.\nOne hundred and seventy-three million miles away, twenty-eight minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening, the punctual little dot of light materialized in space, one hundred and seventy-three million miles beyond Mars. It was not a materialization that was visible to the human eye. It was a materialization that was seen very plainly by instruments on Mars, on Earth, and on Venus.\nEach of those three worlds sent a separate message-of-welcome to $SURNAME0, Earth, and Mars. $SURNAME0 was expected to reply to the message from his native Earth. He never did\u2014or, rather, his replies were so unintelligible that it was decided not to transmit them to Earth.\nThe message from Mars came from the Federal Parliament. It was delivered...", "0_2_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>...and we were struggling helplessly in the grip of an awesome, cosmic whodunit. We were not in control of anything. Fate and a quack cop had put on the lights and ripped open the curtain: and now our total miseries were exposed. There was no more dicking around\u2014or fooling ourselves. For when you come right down to it, the Samoan Room is not where you go to find the truth about your life; it is where you go to lose it.\nNo sooner had I delivered this harsh judgment than our luck changed drastically for the better. Not ten feet away\u2014across a circular fountain\u2014we spotted the big winner sitting alone at a table with a few million dollars\u2019 worth of chips piled in front of him. Possible proxy. Lifp possible.\n\u201cLet\u2019s go!\u201d said my attorney. His head was reeling from a massive overdose of insanity\u2014but the prospect of fresh meat had brought him around.\nOur crocodile shoes made only four or five inches of splasd in the quicksand of the casino...but that was enough to attract everybody\u2019s attention. Freaking monsters started grabbing at us. The hookers were running out of the woodwork. Strange...", "1_21_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-three in the evening</span>. In the morning, Salo Nebinem was due to address the Parliament of Earth. He was going to tell the members of the Parliament about $SURNAME0\u2019s assistant\u2014not in order to reveal her identity, but in order to reveal why she had gone to such cosmic lengths to keep her own identity secret.\nWhat Salo was going to tell the members of the Parliament was that she was the best friend a material creature has ever had in this Universe\u2014that she was $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\nShe had to go away and hide from all her friends before she died. And she had to die mysteriously, so that she could be something more than just another ghost haunting the memories of her old friends.\nSalo Nebinem was going to tell the members of the Parliament that, despite their suspicions and angers and outrage, $SURNAME0 and his wife really were as pure as the morning snow. Salo Nebinem was going to tell the members of the Parliament that $SURNAME0 and his wife, despite appearances otherwise, had loved each other more than any other man and woman who had ever trod the Earth.\nWhen Salo Nebinem bade the members of the Parliament of Earth goodbye, he was going to ask...", "0_14_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Central War Time, on Wednesday, July 18, 1956,\nA blinding white light filled all the heavens, turning the landscape white.\nThe light winked out.\nAnswered by 19 additional flashes in rapid succession,\nFollowed by a message in morse code to all Earth:\nTHE PURPOSE OF THIS MESSAGE IS TO INFORM YOU THAT LIFE ON THIS PLANET IS FINISHED. MANKIND IS EXTINCT. THE CAUSE WAS THROUGH ATOMIC DESTRUCTION.\nNo one saw the flash that disappeared the Tralfamadorian messenger.\nConvenient and logical explanations for the flash on the horizon and the disappearance of the messenger help little, now, since mankind can accept harsh and cruel truths.\nEqually useless are the lamentations, the mourning, the expressions of sympathy and poetry by a shattered, dying race of beings.\nThe messenger did not exist\u2014save tortuously in the minds of blast survivors who received detailed hallucinations of the messenger from persons near them who died instantly from the flash.\nNevertheless, there are these about whom something\u2014anything\u2014can be said:\nSurgon Icwy identified the remains of his son, Biron, in the charred, shattered wreckage...", "2_1_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-seven in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME1 swore to remember the routes and landmarks of Mars for only ten minutes\u2014and then to forget them completely.\nHe succeeded in committing the ten minutes of exterior landmarks to memory. He had a keen, retentive memory. But forgetfulness rushed in as soon as desperation passed. Disinterest rushed in as soon as interest faded. But memory, interest, and excitement lasted long enough to carry $SURNAME1 to the ship.\nAs soon as he was on the ship, $SURNAME1 got the cramps again, fled to one of the bunks, curled up, and groaned.\n$SURNAME0 stuck his head in and looked down at $SURNAME1. \"Sorry you have to go, Skip,\" he said.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME1. He groaned.\n\"Remember\u2014the unseen playmate will always materialize if you get into real trouble,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Beat it!\" said $SURNAME1 from his bunk.\n\"You won\u2019t remember any of this,\" said $SURNAME0. \"But I\u2019ll remember for both of us.\"\nAnd then he was gone.\nThe ship started with a lurch.\nThere was an instant of free fall.", "0_9_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-five in the morning</span>. Eight hours and fifteen minutes to go. I shaved and skipped breakfast\u2014no time to waste on silly little things like eating\u2014and made it to the Racetrack just as it was opening. The totalisator booth was deserted, so I went inside and found a clerk sleeping on the floor. I kicked his foot. He woke up. \u201cWhat do you want?\"\u201cBagman for the horse room,\u201d I said, emphasizing the word \u201cbagman.\u201d He understood.\nThe Bagman is, obviously, a junkie. He is the guy who collects all the tickets from the parimutuel betting machines and delivers them to the proper teller. The racetrack is full of people doing totally meaningless things. The Bagmen are par bus, as it were. Not infrequently, they are also Informal Cashiers in the various mutuel departments. Some are handicappers and tipsters...and some are just hitchers who show up at the track every day because it gives them something to do. This is a good life, because all the tracks pay their Bagmen and Informal Cashiers in cash\u2014no tax, no receipt. You receive a regular salary and you must estimate on your income tax...", "1_20_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span> when the Malibus, with everybody home again, got their starter fired. The starter was fired by $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s good friend Sergeant $SURNAME2. $SURNAME2 was spared the drop-seats on the harmoniums by good old Sergeant $SURNAME2\u2019s high blood pressure.\n$SURNAME2 was spared the drop-seats on the harmoniums because he had something he wanted to do before he died.\nWhat Sergeant $SURNAME2 wanted to do before he died was to help his good friend $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nSo $SURNAME2 gladly took over the back-breaking job of firing the miniature nuclear-electric starter on the Malibu. He gassed the starter through an aluminum tube from the tank on his back. He bridged the tube from a valve on the tank to a valve on the starter.\nHis gassing of the starter was good for exactly one minute\u2014the approximate life-span of the 600,000-volt charge on the nylon storage batteries. In one minute every piece of heavy equipment in the Malibu\u2019s subdivision would be humming, would be ready to rumble wherever Lieutenant Cable commanded.\n$SURNAME2 was cheered mightily when, after thirty seconds of gassing, the starter heeled over...", "0_0_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-eight ante meridiem</span>. Chronologically, this was still almost the same day. The sun was hot, and Pound\u2019s orange hair hung lank and dripping over his brow.\n\"What?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"This is the funniest line of the whole derned thing,\" said Pound.\n\"What is?\" said $SURNAME1. \"What?\"\n\"Mebbe you can\u2019t\u2014Oh, never mind,\" said Pound.\nThe real owner of the estate, the husband of the poet, had started to build a city on his grounds. The city was to have been his monument, his demonstration to the world of how a statesman and an aesthete should use slave labor. Government buildings, museums, wineries, promenades, gardens, theaters were all planned, and a lunatic asylum, because the architect had planned the whole city as a kind of aesthetic scandal that should challenge the remotest corners of the globe for art wealth. The lake, created by the damming of a tiny creek, was supposed to be a reflecting pond for the city\u2019s many fountains. Then the husband\u2019s wife had thrown him over, big time, and he had fled the city as a rat flees a burning stable...", "1_5_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. I glanced at my watch. Dawn was breaking over the lifeless earth. The ground was damp from the night's dew.\nThe magic time of 5 A.M., when anything can happen, when you flip your coin into the cup of your hand and the crystal ball flashes red or gold at you.\nI dropped the bottle of tequila that I had been carrying and smashed it against the curb stone. Five o'clock in the morning. The magic hour of Milagro. Resigned to my fate, I climbed into the back seat of the red Impala with the corpse of Arturo Bandini and steered straight for the Gloriana Motel on Hollywood Boulevard.\nMean, low, dirty? Of course. But how do you account for a man who walks into a New York hotel with a sixmonth-dead dolphin wrapped in a bedsheet? The clerk stared at the bundle and refused to believe me when I said it was my \u201cparrot, sick.\u201d The bathroom was all bloody. What the hell did I want with a dogfish here in the Roaring Twenties? And would I please consider leaving the premises without delay? That was the deal. I wound up in the pokey with a year's...", "2_11_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-one in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\nThe place was Cabin 69 in Kechmore Village, Tharsis.\nIt was January 1, 1948.\nAs far as anyone knew, the only members of the Benevolent Order of Crestonians still alive on the whole planet were Major Robert Gilleece and Niccolo, Baldy, Hendrick, and the Scrimmage sisters.\nNobody knew. Nobody cared.\nIt was the most horrible burial service in the history of life anywhere. There were no burials anywhere, of course.\nIt was the most horrible gathering of the dead on record, also. There were no gatherings-of-the-dead on record.\nThe dead were strewn all over hell.\nAnd there were too many of them. Not only were the caves at $SURNAME0\u2019s turntable littered with corpses, but the Sumerki space ship was also packed with several dozen corpses approaching the world from 96,000,000 miles away.\nThe flood of bodies in the caves had been so heavy that the Sumerki had actually been shoved bodily on its launching platform.", "2_14_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, now.\nThe Martian was strutting purposefully forward. It was coming to the Secretariat Building.\nIt was contemplating the building with its compound yellow and brown eyes.\nMiss Ottinger chattered on about the Martians\u2014how they were broad, flat creatures with tentacles, how they seemed to be able to levitate themselves about a foot above the ground. \"They must have air pockets in those long things,\" she said.\nHugh was dutifully staring out of the window now. He saw more birds join the first one, saw them turn a somersault in the air, saw them scurry to a rendezvous on the lawn of the Secretariat Building. When they reached the rendezvous, they clustered again like curious, skittish sheep. They made cooing noises at one another.\nThe first Martian advanced to the base of the Secretariat Building and did a headstand. He was as tall as a three-story building. His hat, planted firmly on his tentacles, stayed upside down on that roof while he walked his feet to the edge of it and peered into the windows.\nThe legs in the gray flannel suit began to tremble. Hugh straightened and sat rigid in his chair.", "2_11_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 padded up to the cemetery gates together. $SURNAME0, having done all the research on time, was not surprised that time was behaving so queerly. He had been told all along that time on Earth was a marginal phenomenon\u2014was in fact, by the standards of the rest of the Universe, crazy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4, who had spent the last few minutes barking and growling in his doghouse, was suddenly docile and sweet. $SURNAME0 patted him on the head, and $FIRSTNAMEB4 fogged a window in the door of the doghouse with happy dog breathe.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s hands were clasped behind his back\u2014his classic pose.\nHe wore canvas sailing shoes, khaki duck pants, and a maroon sweatshirt with a gold T on it. The T was for the title of a course he would never give at the University, and the title of the course was This installem\u2019nt will not take plae. His dark, bristly hair was in a wing at the right temple, seeming to weigh down that side of his head. His green eyes were just a shade lighter than the grass in the cemetery.", "1_14_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span> and the place was a rock hollow a hundred yards downhill from the village of Charmant. It was a warm hollow in sunlight, with shadows beyond the rock.\nHis name was Salomono Lavagoutski Equus. It was a name which listed with difficulty, as though each syllable clicked into the next with difficulty. By some trick of mind which he did not question, Salo found the clicking syllables swept him back and back until his mind was emptied of its clutter and he was Salo, walking down a broad white beach in a noon when no one knew him and he knew no one, in company with the beautiful Trurl and the noble Klapaucius and the angel-winged Stat UR.\nAnd it was an even better trick of mind that carried him on, even when the proper names flagged, to believe it all. Here beside him on the sand walked Trurl and Klapaucius, and there strode Stat, and so on.\nAnd then they walked into the surf, and someone threw a huge switch, and the white beach and clear blue noon were snatched away, and Salo found himself at the bottom of the well of afternoon in the hollow by the village of Charmant. He was kneeling in soft grass...", "0_5_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after five o'clock in the morning</span>. I had slept my second sleep. I awoke slowly, got out of bed at last, and stood unsteadily on my feet. My night on guard was over. The nightmare dream of a junkie was gone. I realized where I was at last and remembered everything. No one was chasing me. I sat on a chair in the room and told myself the story of my life. I remembered running around the Hotel Modelia in Barcelona with the wounded bull and sawing off the horns of the nanny goat. A child's voice in a dark house in Galveston, Texas shouted: \u201cLook whatcha done to my goat, ya bastard.\u201d I saw myself in a room in a sleazy hotel on East Washington Avenue in Denver, sitting on the bed watching my sick and miserable children who were covered with sores and scabs and couldn't eat anymore, staring at the flies buzzing in the sun, seeing them put their fingers in their mouths, knowing there were things in their fingers and their mouths that should not be there. I saw the children walking on the dead grass in front of a gas station on the corner of Colfax and Broadway in Denver with their fingers on their lips and their hair falling into their eyes, like lost, sick...", "0_16_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. We got into a taxi with bags and bundles and spent half an hour finding a place to live. We settled on a four-room apartment on a back street. It was owned by an American widow who lived on the ground floor. She agreed to keep an eye on our things if we would wash and air the blankets on the beds and make the beds up fresh every other day. We agreed and paid her ten dollars in advance. Then we went out to look for a bar that would stay open past sundown. We found one and had a few drinks. An hour later we met some people in the bar and went out to a roadhouse for steaks and wine-berries with cream. The roadhouse was built of logs, with sawdust on the floor. We stayed there till midnight, talking to a young Englishman who was traveling through the South, and went back to the apartment. Money really was never a problem, the way it is in the States: if you have enough for a few days ahead you can always find somebody who will lend you what you need. What we needed most was a car. The next morning a man approached us in the breakfast restaurant and offered to sell us his car for hundred and twenty-five dollars. It was a...", "2_15_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Sun was shining. Uncountable trillions of miles away, the Sun was shining through an atmosphere that had dwindled over billions of years to loose nitrogen and oxygen. There was only a trace of ozone left. Heat from the Sun flared through the weak atmosphere. St. Swithin\u2019s Day was done. Done for good.\nThe battle between the Tralfamadorians and the Guardians was done for good.\nSolemnly, but with a kind of religious glee, the Tralfamadorians went back to their ship.\nThey again opened the tremendous hatches that let the stony perspective of the Universe in.\nThey again filled the deep canyons with the music of the spheres.\nThey again whistled and hummed and tweeeed.\nThey swabbed out their ship.\nThey saluted, barely moving their tentacles.\nThe time was thirty-six seconds past seven o\u2019clock in the evening.\nObi-Wan prepared a meal for the comrades who would take care of him for the rest of his days.\nHis future comrades could see him preparing the meal in his transparent cubicle through the colorless ether of space.", "0_15_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was a cavernous, low-ceilinged, rococo dining room in the tropical pleasure dome erected by $SURNAME0 on the island of Jamaica. The delicately flowered china of the place set was blue, because $SURNAME0 had said that blue was his favorite color.\nIt wasn\u2019t really blue at all.\nA person had to use his imagination to see that it was blue.\nA person had to be $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, too, in order even to pretend that the china was blue.\n$SURNAME0 sat at the head of the table. His table mate was a winsome blonde named $FIRSTNAMEF1, to whom he had been married only five minutes.\n$SURNAME0 was married to $FIRSTNAMEF1, was the nominal head of his family and his foundation, only while he was on Titan. When he returned to space, he expected to turn both over to underlings.\nHe did not like Earthlings enough to lead them, nor Earthlings\u2019 affairs enough to oversee them.\nEarthlings and Earthling affairs bored $SURNAME0 sick.\nAs far as he was concerned, Earth wasn\u2019t the only planet having troubles.\nAny race that could do Earth\u2019s...", "0_7_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-four in the morning</span> of October 8th, in the Year of Our Lord nineteen hundred and fifty, given all the circumstances of the case, and regardless of the propriety such an inquiry might have in a more rational order of things. To recapitulate: the time was seven twenty-four A.M., October 8,194(-, given all the particulars of the case. That was all. It should be enough. No wise man, given all the particulars of any case, ever troubled himself to probe a naked impulse to extension, to see where it would lead. What was so important about a naked impulse? Nothing.\nClearly, if a naked impulse and a $SURNAME0 man got to arguing about the character, nature, and purpose of all God\u2019s itty-bitty molecules in the universe, then the man winning the argument was the man who spoke last and best on how little he knew about his small part in the Universe.\nClearly, the man who got to speak last and best on how little he knew about his small part was $SURNAME0.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 would do far better, and far more imaginatively, if all he introduced into his account of himself was a simple statement of chronology and place. But $SURNAME0, and...", "2_6_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six nineteen in the morning</span>.\nBig Jew and $FIRSTNAMEB0 spent the next twenty-nine minutes moving the lumber and heavy equipment out of the building\u2019s sub-basements. The building had three sub-basements. The sub-basements were not used for offices or by the tenants, and so were almost like secret passageways into a skyscraper from the sewers below.\nBig Jew and $FIRSTNAMEB0, working without speaking, manhandled the equipment and lumber up iron rungs to the ground floor. Then Big Jew pressed a button that opened a door on Twenty-third Street. He and $FIRSTNAMEB0 rolled the equipment and lumber out and piled it on the sidewalk. There was no official stationery on the equipment, nothing to show who owned the building. Besides the heavy beam at each of the four corners, the heavy equipment included a back-breaker, two air compressors and pumps, scaffolding, two hydraulic jacks, a jumbo sledge, dozens of two-and-a-half-by-four-inch sticks of yellow-stained wood and various three-quarter-inch spikes. Because of the ceremonial nature of the heavy equipment, none of it was appropriate to any specific building trade task. Big Jew kept the entire building up to code.", "2_1_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-two in the morning</span>.\nNine-tenths of the joints in $SURNAME0\u2019s skeleton were ball-and-socket joints\u2014free, swiveling joints like the shoulder and the hip. The sockets were more or less spherical. The ball bearings came from Earth. They had been flown from Earth to Mars on the rocket-mail flights. $SURNAME0 had a pair of virtually unbreakable ball bearings for every bone.\nOn Earth, doctors had refused to give $SURNAME0 a free-swinging hip or shoulder joint. They said his original hip and shoulder joints were quite good enough. Those joints had been forcibly seized and held together tightly after $SURNAME0 suffered two psychotic episodes while still a young boy.\nHis arm, leg, and neck muscles were not the magnificent bow and arrow muscles of the natural-born Martian soldier. They had been surgically grafted from the bodies of pathetic Earthling underlings\u2014old men, invalids. $SURNAME0 was by no means a strong man. He was much weaker than an Earthling ninety-eight pounds weak.", "0_10_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe chronometer in the ship\u2019s control room said so.\nField Marshal Laventine did not know what the chronometer in the control room was saying.\nHe was locked out of the control room, and the pilot house, too.\nHe was standing on the parade ground, facing the ship, waiting to enter.\nHe was having a terrible time sliding the haft of his broken sword through the ring on his belt. The broken blade kept dropping down into his boot.\nThe Mother of Crimples had a perfect view of the field marshal\u2019s struggles, through the beauty-parlor window. He looked like a man doing a kind of sword dance, in order to get into costume.\nMother Crimples came to the window, opened it wide.\n\"Get away from there!\" she said to the soldiers.\nThey turned and ran. They were scattered around the buildings. None of them got within fifty yards of the Mother of Crimcles.\n\"Get away!\" she yelled. They all ran.\nThe soldiers knew what she was going to do.\nShe really didn\u2019t want her son to get killed. She wanted Max to find his Magic Fingers again. And she wanted to sell more...", "1_5_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nSuddenly, the entire wall of $SURNAME1\u2019s penthouse room, the wall that gave on the Monaco racetrack, was blotted out by a shadow.\nThe shadow was caused by the arm, the wing, the fin, the fluke of Malach trying to communicate with $FIRSTNAMEM2.\nThe wife of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had just died of an overdose of adultery.\nNow Malachite was coming to make certain that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 did not go unavenged.\n\"Longshadow,\" said $SURNAME0 sotto voce, \"I shall now do what no man in history has ever been able to do\u2014tame a child of Skip\u2019s sentience to his own purposes. How are you going to spend your one minute on the platform, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1?\"\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" said $SURNAME1 in a grubby voice.\n\"Take your time,\" said $SURNAME0 ingratiatingly. \"Give it some thought. You\u2019ve got plenty of time.\"\n\"How?\" said $SURNAME1. \"What am I supposed to say\u2014\u2019Sorry I spent fifty million dollars to find out whether you could make love to a woman in space with a steel ball caught in your throat...", "0_13_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the 22nd. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been floating in space for thirty-nine hours.\nEkkektah wore a blue crooked-necktie and a black space suit.\nHe was a handsome man.\nHe was two meters tall. He had long, wavy, yellow hair that fell over his shoulders. There was a V-shaped scar on his forehead.\nHe weighed ninety-eight kilograms. His eyes were clear blue and earnest. The hair on his chest was silky and yellow.\nThe companion of Ekkektah was named Salo. Salo was male. He was Salo, and nothing else. He wore a purple space suit. He was half a meter in height and forty-eight kilograms in weight, and was shaped like a jelly bean. His eyes were brown and he had no hair anywhere, not even on his chest. Love had never had a notable effect on his face. He was a machine with a computer for a brain.\nLove was a cord that tied the two together. The cord was love.\nThis was their story.\nThe $SURNAME0 Estate, which was the Newport of $FIRSTNAMEB2 and his mother, $FIRSTNAMEF0, was not the former home of $SURNAME0 and his wife.", "2_20_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight eighteen in the evening</span>.\nAt three minutes after midnight, with no time to spare, the last of the great safari parties came thrashing and howling out of the parched Kalahari Desert into the Albert Falls Savannah Nation, where their trucks and Land-Rovers were parked.\nThe first and only other safari party had come hours and hours before, fifteen minutes after the area had been officially opened to tourists by the Albert Falls National Park Department.\nSquinting. Unused to wide open spaces. Blinking in the strong sunlight. Unprepared for the heat. Slow of movement. At least, this is how the natives described them: all two of them. They were an old, white-haired man and his teenaged grandson.\nThey were reported to have permits to kill one of three marks, the only three marks known to be on safari in the Albert Falls National Park. They were the only two tourists or hunters in the area, until the last of the big safari parties arrived.\nTheir marks were a witch, a bush baby, and a mole rat.\nTheir names were not made public by the bearers of bad news for members of the family.", "0_2_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-six in the morning</span> of the night of November thirty-first.\nFrom the hall bathroom, the singing:\nSomething inside me has died, died, died, died.\nAin\u2019t no moment cloudy or sunny skies when, when, when, when,\nSomething inside me has died, died, died, died.\nAin\u2019t no reason to be happy or be glad \u2019cause, \u2019cause, \u2019cause\u2014\nThere was a scratch on the door.\nThe singing stopped.\nThe door opened and Heat ran in, trailing a long white bath towel. She stood at the foot of the bed, smiled, shivered.\n\"Cold, babe,\" she said.\n$FIRSTNAMEF2, in the glow from the single bulb on the table, couldn\u2019t make out all of her face, only the staring eyes and bangs.\n\"Turn on the overhead,\" she said. \"I\u2019m stunned with beauty.\"\n\"Nope,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF2.\n\"You\u2019re shy,\" said Heat, \u201cyou want me like that. I can feel it. Turn on the overhead.\"\n\"No,\" $FIRSTNAMEF2 said, lie ready to tell, if asked,...", "0_18_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past six o'clock in the evening</span> of Wednesday, October the 14th, the place was an old boarding-school in the heart of Oxford, and the game was Homework. In Homework the last player to finish was booted out of doors. That game had started, with wild yells and unbuckled galoshes and streaming rain, twenty minutes earlier in the senior common room of St. Culf of Dunwallo.\n\"Oh, good, here you are,\" said a voice from the stairs. It was their host and mine, one Professor Nevil Sumuwell of St. Culf\u2019s, King of Homework and President for Life of the Freshmen Football Team. Rumor had it he was distantly related to the Sumuwus of Sumu Bay. Nevil would have smiled had he heard the rumor, because it amused him to be related to a nation. He was a fresh-water dynast; the only island on his coat of arms was a potato.\nThe gallop of feet in a letter G above was answer enough to his call. The feet scampered across the floor above, there was an incoherent shriek from the edge of the parapet, and McHandler, ninth man on the ladder became the boot.\nLucer...", "1_15_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic infundibula had sucked all the blood from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s body, along with his luck and his courage. His only hope now was to escape. He was determined not to run, as $FIRSTNAMEF0 had run, but to die like a Martian\u2014to stay put, no matter how terrifying the threat, and to meet whatever came.\nThe only part of his anatomy that still seemed to have any blood in it was his right thumb. That was because his right thumb had been wounded while he was burgling a dentist\u2019s office in Gila Gulch, Colorado\u2014while he was trying to steal back the fangs of the extinct canine known as the tusky-turd.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had panicked, when he lost his bloody luck, and had thrown the tusks overboard. He had thought, after that, that all luck would be permanently out of his life. But one night in Denver, while lying in bed, he heard his luck tinkle on the roof. It turned out that one of his fangs had lodged in the heel of his boot.\nHad he kept the tusks, as he\u2019d planned to do, his luck would have stayed with him forever...", "0_3_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span> when I finally managed to unfold myself out of the front seat of the Cadillac, get inside, and close the door. There was an unopened pint of tequila on the floorboards under my feet. My attorney was already horizontal behind the wheel, snoring into his up\u2014tilted collar. I figured I'd better sleep there, too; he'd been driving for seventeen hours straight, and I was about ready for the same.\nTwenty\u2014eight hours earlier we had been sitting around a table with a man named Montgomery Flange. In front of him were six big double\u2014dialed chronometers and eight Rolex repeaters. On the brick wall behind him was a rack holding ten stopwatches and fourteen Elgin acurate-jewels. To one side was an old\u2014fashioned stock ticker clattering away, Brady Bunch\u2014style, throwing out pink and green paper spools across the desk. The spools were stacked twenty deep. It was a huge room; it took up almost the entire top floor of the building. They had to have a special elevator just to get the watches up there. It was a child's dream: twenty\u2014six timepieces ticking away simultaneously. What a world. What a world we live in,...", "0_14_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-three in the afternoon</span>. I had six dollars left.\nI jumped up and grinned at $FIRSTNAMEB3 and we ran across Montgomery Street inl the hot Washington sun, jumped in the car, and made the exact change right up Vine Street to Hollywood Boulevard. We were in the great land of hallelujah. Horns blew at every inch. Tires howled. There were Deans flashing in every direction. A wonderful new life was beginning. I didn't know what it was but I was ready. We were going to drive straight up the coast to Vancouver and see the ocean.\nThe wonderful pavement of Hollywood Boulevard showed the rays of the sun in its glassiness. We shot past Beverly Hills and up to the heights overlooking Los Angeles. \u201cThink of living here, man!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cIt's heaven on earth just to live here at all. Los Angeles is the Rome of America just as surely as radioactivity is the secret fire of the world, and Frisco and Baltimore, though I've never seen Baltimore, are as holy as anywhere on earth due to the same phenomenon -- strange particles issuing from the adamantine mud of every day. All we need is gas and we'll keep going and going and going, on and on cross the American continent and...", "0_7_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Time was running out on Chris's stakeout of the Stokers' house. He didn't have many opportunities left, because he knew he would have to bring his case to a head right away. Having followed the Stokers late the previous night, he realized that they went out to dinner the first week of every month\u2014theirs was apparently that kind of a marriage. And the first Friday in November was rapidly approaching. If a stakeout of the house was going to be any good at all, Chris knew it would have to begin this night. Tonight he would have to follow the family to wherever they went, and from now on nights would be the only time worth following them.\nEven if he proved nothing at all about the Stokers, at least he would get a line on how to operate in the field. He would do what the D.C. cops had called simply \u201cshadowing.\u201d He would find out from experience how it felt to crouch in the back seat of a car, behind a family like the Stokers\u2014to know that the car would suddenly turn into another street, the family would get out and probably walk into a restaurant, and leave you alone with the empty crisscross streets and the cold air.", "0_17_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was there, at the dark bottom of a canyon in Old North California.\nThe story is about $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4, the most segregated beings ever to share a universe. In all the wild spread of that universe, there is but one place where $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 are safe from intrusions by others: a windowless room below ground in a concrete bunker at the edge of a cliff in the Utah desert.\nThe room and the terrace before it are thoroughly sealed against every form of penetration by the agents and emissaries of other worlds.\nThe room is heated to $SURNAME0\u2019s specifications and maintained that way, even when outside temperatures rise above a hundred and fifty degrees.\nThe room contains a bed, a table and chairs, bookshelves, a card table, an upholstered wing chair, a writing desk, an autochanger library with eight thousand titles, a fullbath\u2014and, on the terrace outside, a barbecue grill and a gas broiler.\nThere is a refrigerator outside, stocked with Corona beer, California sherry, butter, eggs, cheese, p\u00e2t\u00e9 de foie gras, chicken, and foie gras in a jar...", "2_18_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nThe time was now\u2014\n\"Fire!\" roared Phoebe Weatherworn, running out of her Mauser Little Cigar Store on Fremont Street.\nHer voice was the voice of the siren, magnified by the amplifier of her fear and fury.\n\"Fire!\" she cried, and the townspeople at last saw that it was a false alarm.\nFlames would not rage this time.\nThe landing had been as routine as any landing.\nAs dismal and as welcome as any landing.\nConrad Hagedorn got down from his bell-clapper, his smile still nailed to his face.\nThe bulkiness of his body made it look as though that smile were skin-grafted to the lower face.\nRackley Restridge the watchmaker got down from his high stool.\nBeads of sweat made trails through the thin layer of dust on his face. Every movement was an immense effort for him. His eyes were veiled, and squinted against the pain of his dead nerves. His cheeks were withered in horizontal directions.\nThere were seven people in his store.", "0_4_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span> we were now on the outskirts of Denver. \u201dIt's an hour wait for the train, we ought to get a hotel \u203a room for an hour of sleep,\" I said. \u201dBut what about the baggage?\u201d said my cousin. \u201dThis trunk takes up too much room. God, what a day! I'll never do this day over again.\u201d Ralph wasn't so easy; he wanted his things, his shirts and suits. We made a pact with a Night Hawk taxi-driver to go with us to the station in the morning and pick up the trunk and bring it back to the hotel. He was tired of patrolling the highway and was glad of the opportunity to do a little business in sightseeing. He took us down a tree-darkened street to a lawn-shrouded ranchhouse. It was an old homestead surrounded by broken-down outbuildings; the porch needed paint and the lawn was wet with dew. It looked like somewhere grandfathers lived in the song of the West; but this was a modern household. There was an aging custom\u8eca in the driveway and a goddamn dog that came out now as we trudged up the walk. \u201dYou take care of the trunk,...", "1_0_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-six a.m.</span> and she\u2019d debated hotly with herself whether to call $FIRSTNAMEB3 and sleep with him. She ran down the pros and the cons in cold blood. The advantage of sleeping with him was that she loved the way he strapped himself across her back; she turned to jelly every time. The bad part was that he was in Denver now with his kid, his ex-wife-to-be and her elderly father, who\u2019d just turned sixty-five, and they were probably all going to play Can You Top This? and $FIRSTNAMEB3 might lose interest in $FIRSTNAMEF4 and transfer it to the young girl who was waiting for him in Denver, which $FIRSTNAMEF4 found quite possible. Angels can of course tumble; she knew the signs. The other guy had only to show up at the hotel\u2014he didn\u2019t even have to ask for her\u2014and she\u2019d go to bed with him. In fact she knew who it would be. It would be Ed Dunkel, $FIRSTNAMEB3\u2019s buddy, the only other freak in town. She had a date with him at eight in the morning. Ed Dunkel, professing to be my friend, she thought; but what the hell, they were probably wrapped up together in a million struggles and sorrow...", "1_23_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until midnight</span>.\nThe time was five minutes until midnight.\nThe time was four minutes until midnight.\nThe time was three minutes until midnight.\nThe time was two minutes until midnight.\nThe time was one minute until midnight.\nThe time was half a minute until midnight.\nAnd then the time was\u2014zero.\nAnd it was a time of a harsh and chilling silence all over Earth as Tuesday became Wednesday, December 5, 1975.\nAnd during the silence that had padded itself around Earth like a heavy space suit, the moon continued to approach Earth at an average speed of four miles an hour. Nature, in the form of the green Phno Snodgrass and his wife and son, was still making its shuffling way up the stairs to Room 205 in the Emerald City Hotel.\nCHAPTER SEVEN\nA Yellow Dune\nOld Ralph Spoilsport had a conundrum for everybody. $SURNAME0 and his dog were nowhere to be seen. Old Salo was out on the Great Lawn, working on his millionth millionth million-word letter to his mother. The unscrewing of the sex organs of female mosquitoes, if ever carried out, would begin in three minutes.\nEisenhower was in Djakarta, dying of append...", "2_19_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-two in the evening</span>. A crowd of eight thousand, more never to come, had come for the third time to see the materialization of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog. They stood as still as lumps of fired clay. Their minds were pudding.\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1, seamstress and wit, sipped from a brocaded hip flask. \"Who will tell the world?\" she said.\nShe put her whistle to her lips.\nWhoo-whoo-whoo-whoo!\nProfessor $SURNAME0 stepped out of the air craft that had materialized without warning.\nHis lips were red and wet from kissing.\nOne hand held the leash of the Titanic slobbering beast. The other hand was placed suggestively on his blue and gold suspender buckle.\nHis eyes were drunk and his bearing obscene.\nHe looked out over the crowd, let his eyes run over them, let his mind imagine that their standing there was the greatest tribute that Earthlings could pay a living creature.\nIt was not a malicious conceit he was playing on them\u2014this making them the fictional subjects of his boundless good will. It was a conceit based on a lifetime of meetings.", "1_4_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past four o'clock in the morning</span>. I was throwing horseshoes with Eddie Campbell, who handles the dirty work for Lucky Luciano on Cuba and the rest of the island and specializes in all of the work that the average hood won't touch...like knocking off a former prisoner at the end of a long, trifling narcotics sentence.\n\"How about Hemmingway?\" asked an out-of-work tourist who was assisting us in the game. \"Is it true he makes a hundred and fifty dollars a day?\"\n\"He makes more.\"\n\"So he's finally made it.\"\n\"Richard Nixon made it long ago,\" said Eddie Campbell. \"That's one crazy mother if you want to know what I think.\"\n\"No, I mean Hemmingway,\" said the tourist.\n\"Nothing crazy about Hemmingway,\" said Eddie. \"Drunk all the time, isn't he? Made a fortune selling stories to the Saturday Evening Post.\"\nThe tourist laughed. \"Just the same he's in the news all the time, isn't he? Probably ask him to run for President.\"\n\"It wouldn't bother him any,\" said Eddie. \"Long as they send him his siphons.\"\n\"He might do it,\" I said.\n\"Wouldn...", "2_12_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-eight post meridiem</span>.\nThe place was the stone hut in a clearing in the iron forest of a planet of a sun in a galaxy of a universe.\nThe speaker was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe subject was\u2014anything.\nThe chorus was Stony.\nThe drum was hollow Earth.\nThe topic for discussion might be, for example:\nWhat does it feel like to be born?\nWhat does it feel like to be scared to death?\nWhat does it feel like to kill?\nWhat does it feel like to feel good?\nThe orator then slept.\nThe orator slept snuggled against the hairy warm fluid-filled contours of the best friend in the Universe.\nThe orator slept, perfected in a friendship that your best friend in the Universe told you was God-sent.\nIf the Universe went on for a hundred trillion years, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Stony would still be sleeping there together.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and Stony were one organism, now.\nTheir friendship was God-sent, retooled, remade, remodeled, and renewed through time eternal.", "1_3_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-nine in the morning</span>. As far as the kid could tell, he was still on duty. He knew this to be a fact because his head throbbed steadily, warningly, and his mouth\u2014where had he put that mouthpiece, anyway?\u2014was dry as sand. Basic briefing said call in at least eight times an hour, and now he was over two hours overdue. He owed those poor bastards two hours, minimum, and he knew it\u2014knew it from the urgency with which he tore into the stack of night orders. Leg Four was desert. God knew who was whipping whom on Leg Six.\nHe heard a sharp, slapping sound\u2014the sound of sandaled feet running on tile\u2014and looked up just in time to see the night messenger zing through the shimmering heat. \u201cSh!\u201d the kid advised, waving the kid to silence. \u201c1\u2019m right in the middle of something,\u201d he nodded, pointing at the wall of paperwork.\nBut now the night messenger saw the shape in the shower, and realized the man was awake; already he was squinting around in a dazed kind of wonderment. \u201cShit,\u201d said the kid. He tore off his sweat\u2014soaked T...", "0_5_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five nineteen in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF0 Banger was asleep in her bed, in the maternity ward of the hospital. The window of her room, facing east, was filmy with morning haze. An antenna tower seemed to stand in the very room with her. This was the signal tower of the television station KCMC\u2014the station which had originated the special $SURNAME0 Program that had been beamed to all the planets of the Solar System on Christmas Eve.\nAnother child would be born on Titan, the moon of Saturn, within nineteen minutes. This child would be born with a Pocket Emery knife in its ha p p fst. The knife had been in the pocket of every son of Tralfamadore born in the past half-million years. It was a short, two-bladed knife, held in a black leather sheath.\nEvery Tralfamadorian son named his own replacement, and gave him the knife the moment he was able to ask for one. It was called the Pocket Emery knife because it had been the first machine that the Tralfamadorians had been able to make. The components of the first machine had been a piece of shale, a piece ofRare Earth, and a tiny pinch of TNT.\nThe first machine had...", "1_22_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-one in the evening</span> when we hit town. Great fabulous juicy hamburgers at the Speakeasy diner...and then $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I checked into the fleabag Glaser-frame hotel over nearoycland I worked and worked on a poem; $FIRSTNAMEB3 lay on the bed talking. Guys came in for girls and the place was so damned rotten somebody opened the door by mistake and the desk clerk jumped off the window sill, thinking it was the police. It was the worst of all Glaurus-frame hotels. All of them are alike; you get a cot, you get desperately trying to sleep till noon and wake up with itching heat and the rain outside lashing down in the darkness -- the glare of the Indianapolis sun at the same time...and those things plus a woeful sick feeling in your belly that comes from bad inactive pvstemic conditions. I knew I was too poHte to tell $FIRSTNAMEB3 this without telling him of my underlying fever phobia, but I told him anyway and he laughed and started telling me of his reminiscences of beatings in jail. A pair of sporting young gentlemen came in with a gorgeous blonde who wanted service in every possible way; they sat down on the bed to talk it over, and I had to hotfoot it out...", "1_16_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The cathedral clock boomed out the half-hour, reminding many ladies to put on their gloves. The ten o\u2019clock tower had just boomed out the half-hour, reminding many gentlemen to put on their hats. At five minutes before ten o\u2019clock the previous evening old Parke Godwin, universally known as Parke Godwin, had died. At the stroke of five minutes before ten he should have revived. The cathedral bells, though rung by automatic motor and electric push-buttons rather neatly expressed the fact that old Parke Godwin hadn\u2019t revived. The sound of the bells expressed no impression of his failure to revive, for bells are only made to express the dire need for electricians and the religious rites of carpenters.\nAh, Parke Godwin, thought the crowd. Would that we might, all of us, be inspired by so noble and so pathetic, nay so august an example as thee. What higher honor than simply to be what we are, and cease this fretful striving after something else? A man that fights his father and beats him past all bearing has won a name imperishable in all history for himself and his house. But who shall say that Parke Godwin has...", "0_2_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty in the morning</span>. We went back to the hotel. It was a big brick hotel, built since On the Road had described it as a shack. It was a nice hotel, but I couldn't sleep; I went outside to take a walk up the road for a mile and came back when the arc-lights had died. Walking in alleys, down back ways, crossing covered bridges, peering through the wire window at a lighted office for the inspection of pigs, I surveyed the quiet. Peaceful, peaceful. It made a fellow feel like a jackass. What the hell was I taking a kicksider's course in America for? I had to get out of this place, away fromold Mexico, go somewhere and change my ideas. I couldn't walk the streets any more where I'd rolled in the snow with my fugitive other Joe, and been haunted through all the years. My God! That was so long ago, and now I was walking the same damned state that he'd walked in the years gone by, and all that preceded the years, when he was growing up wild in the cities of California; and it seemed as though I must cover with my hands to protect the world. I couldn't sleep in a strange room. I took my coat and soft...", "1_10_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when we came over the high trestle overlooking the jungle town, past the burned trunks of trees, and saw the roofs spread out below us. And the whitewashed houses with red-tile roofs against the green jungle, and red-tiled sidewalks running between the whitewashed walls, and the broken wall of the church spilling stones into the street, and the aeroplanes dangling from the belltower, and the swinging signs, and the market by the square and the short trees, cottonwoods, on the other side of the square and piles of fresh charcoal and firewood on the ground and the fountain with an iron axe in the mouth and spewing water and the burros loaded high and the Indians sitting in the shade and the man standing with his beast at the hitchrail and the woman drawing water from the stone well with a pulley and bucket and pouring the water into her kerosene can to take it in to cook with at the house, and all sloping down to the pond where the smacks were moored with their nets outspread on the water waiting for the wind so they could go out and pull the shrimp up from the bottom, and we rolled on past the whitewashed houses, and the square, and the church,...", "0_15_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. At that very moment, in fact, according to $FIRSTNAMEB2, the moon was having a collision with an opaque, brown-shining eternity of misty, shoreless clouds.\nThe moon was not basking in a provincial sky. It was born in a city.\nSo was $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nHe was the son of an architect and a mathematician.\nSo was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nThere were those who said that Mars was so visibly the pride and shingleplaster of $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 that no self-respecting moon would speak to either of them.\nThis was not so.\nThe moon had made $SURNAME1 the surprising and spontaneous offer of a treaty of amity, concord, and free trade. $SURNAME1 was so surprised and pleased that he had offered his only son in marriage to the youngest daughter of the Emperor of the Moon.\nThere were those who said that this groom, this young groom, had been sired by another man.\nThis was not true, either.\n$SURNAME1 dwelled under a system so rigorously feudal that no poor person ever heard of arithmetic. But $SURNAME1 was a Yankee and a Scalawag\u2014just like his father before him. He studied algebra secretly, like a poison. He made...", "0_5_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five o'clock in the morning</span>, and I was standing there at Terry's little shack, in the middle of Denver. Strange place for a hoodlum estate. Terry seemed to be in a panic about something. What kind of trouble had Remi bin in?\n\u201cBut why?\u201d cried Terry. \u201cRemi said you'd send us some money for the car as soon as you got to L.A.\u201d I couldn't understand his confusion. \u201cBut what is it? What does he want me to do?\u201d I wanted to make everybody happy at once. \u201cWe mustn't worry till we see what he has to say,\u201d I said.\nIn the morning Remi flew in from Los Angeles. He was wearing a tuxedo. He looked like a million dollars. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, $FIRSTNAMEB6, my good man! What has become of you?\u201d He had paid off the hotel and flown back to help me. \u201cDon't worry about a thing, old hoss. Everything's under control.\u201d He had been busy on the phone. A limousine awaited us downstairs. The doorman was instructed to send it over to Terence's house --where he had been so recently, alound...", "0_23_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to midnight</span>. In a minute, the celebration would begin for chrono-synclastic infundibula and the falling walls of the universe. The president of the United States, alerted by special telephone from the barracks of the Guardian Commando at Stonewall, Virginia, was organizing his Cabinet, his wife, his favorite musicales and his mistresses to mix a great batch of pink lemonade in the White House just before the trumpet and the attack. The telephone was busy all over the world as responsible persons called one another to make sure they were ready to drink the lemonade at the exact moment.\nPRAIRIE MELODIES\nLEMURIANA OF LOVE\nNOW, WHEN THE TWELVE SECONDS of the last two minutes of December went by, Panner and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had already geared their instruments and nodded coldly to one another and were thinking of the horrible deed they most harmoniously had to do.\nNow, when the ten seconds went by, $FIRSTNAMEB0, the soldier, was out on the iron balcony, stealing the iron trays and the food on them.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had snitched a big iron key to the Catacombs. He had seen the key abandoned, unused, on a ledge of rock...", "0_15_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-four in the afternoon</span>, twelve hours twenty-four minutes from Greatestmir\u2019s birth; and the place was Stonecipher Beadsman\u2019s office at the stone firm of Stonecipheco, GTC, Innernette Division.\nThe room was high and rectangular and oak-paneled, with a desk at one end, level with the street, and a desk at the other end, level with the lobby, and between the two, wide stone stairs leading to a black door. On the left, under the stairs, there was a vault. On the right was a bar. Inside the bar were an incredible number of bottles, over forty of them, including a full-sized Organ breakaway of 1926 Ch\u00e2teau Latche du Chiendent. The bottles were behind a refrigerated rack. There were also two large wicker chairs facing the city, and two small wicker chairs facing away from the city. There were sidewalks of thick black glass running along three of the walls, between the wall and the black windows. And there was a door. Above the door there was a sign that said: EDWARD DOONEY, INVESTMENTS.\nThe door was between the bar and the street door. It was woodwormed, and it...", "1_19_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-two in the evening</span> now and we'd left the Bar Simply Grand in Ju\u00e1rez, where we'd found two men to take over the big Buick\u2014a fearsome driver called Charlie who'd just finished a year for running whisky in Texas, and Wayne, a twenty-five-year-old kid with a sallow, pock-marked face who hoped to meet up with his old army buddy Silent Ed Freely in El Paso. There was a gun on the dashboard. \u201cYou never know who you're gonna meet on these roads,\u201d Wayne said as we pulled out of Ju\u00e1rez. \u201cI was once stopped by this Mex army colonel. I could speak his lingo, see. He stops me, asks for my papers. I showed him the papers and he ain't satisfied. 'Come down outa that car, cowboy,' he says, and I says, 'What? This my car; what you say?' I says this real anti and he blow his top. He wants to see my gun, and I says, 'Where I'm going I don't need no gun.' So he fishes for my gun and --\u201d He fired a bullet across the street. \u201cThat's where it is, in case we stop any more cops...", "0_4_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 as a boy of nine was not in his bedroom, was not in his mother's kitchen, was not in any of the places he might normally be expected to be.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 as a boy of nine was floating in a sewer in $FIRSTNAMEM1-Nemesis, forty feet below the surface of the earth.\nThe sewer in $FIRSTNAMEM1-Nemesis that a boy of nine was floating in was forty feet below the level of the earth because it had been carried under enemy lines in the rain and land-slides of the Unkmissive Trail. The sewer was dank and damp.\nThe sewer in $FIRSTNAMEM1-Nemesis that a boy of nine was floating in carried storm water and sewage in harmony, since both, to the boy, were horrible odors.\nThe sewer in $FIRSTNAMEM1-Nemesis that a boy of nine was floating in ran north\u2014and, by scraping his elbows on the low perpendicular tunnel walls, a boy could determine the direction. The sewer hurried on for nearly a mile before dropping another twenty feet.\nThe tunnel system of the $FIRSTNAMEM1-Nemesis sewer was so intact and logical, so generally free from deposits and blockages, that a person might easily imagine himself a castaway on an island of...", "2_22_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The space was the marble plaza in front of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent. The persons were $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and the $SURNAME0 family.\n$SURNAME1 had found his way to Newport quite easily. There is a strange phenomenon known to those who have almost died in a Nuclear War. The phenomenon is called chrono-synclastic infundibulation. Duration and space become very elastic, and a person can cover many times the normal distance and take many times the normal time doing it.\n$SURNAME1 had discovered that his rate of passage through time was much slower outside the shelter. And once he gained enough distance from the scene of the explosion, he was surprised to discover that he could acquire more time and space merely by whim. So while many of the newly announced Crusaders chose four-mile helical trams to reach Newport, $SURNAME1 metaphorically struck off across country. He jumped from lawn to lawn, and ran from street lamp to street lamp, and made jet birdlike swooping dashes into the subway. He got to the $SURNAME0 estate in a fraction of the time the other Crusaders were spending on the helicals.", "0_7_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-one in the morning</span>. There were many people in the office, and I found it easy to be natural. I enjoyed the bets enormously and took a deep-seated satisfaction in the drama of the whole operation. My fantasies of the night before became realities. \u201cThat Frenchman put his card on the bar,\u201d said my friend loudly, \u201cand just as he slid it forward across the mahogany that old chevalier just ripped into him.\u201d The Frenchman was standing right next to my friend and understood English extremely well. I saw his face go totally blank and blanker; then he began to smile. He had nothing to fear, but he was wondering what or whom my friend was talking about. He shrugged and sipped his drink. I was surprised that he didn't argue or seem perplexed at all. I looked around the room. \u201cWhere's the sailor?\u201d I asked a man next to me. \"Over there, at that big perfumed harpie's table.\" I could see the sailor but couldn't recognize the woman. She was standing, and her beaded dress looked like a dark, gleaming waterfall. Then I saw the sailor. He was facing me, bending down so I could see all the way down his drink...", "2_15_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. I didn't know these facts at the time, but this was the exact moment when the airport bus crawled with sultry slowness past the last dull green highway sign on the raw west fringes of town, saying simply and impersonally, DENVER, 203 MI. I had just noticed this sign when two forks of lightning exploded in my head with the awful clarity in which a man seems to recognize he is going crazy, but only after a somewhat longer period of wondering if maybe he's going sane.\nIt was more than I could take. I could only assume, dizzily, that everything was happening because I needed it to happen. It would be too much trouble to pretend otherwise any longer. The money was falling apart. The American Nations are breaking up.", "0_22_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-six in the evening</span>.\nAn old man on the porch of a shack up the street from The Blue Piano was burning candles in a tomato can and selling them at a dime apiece to the people of Melrose.\nWhen Detective Sergeant Robert DuBoise passed the candle vendor on his way to the jazz club he bought nine candles. They cost him seventy-nine cents.\nHe was in civilian clothes\u2014blue suit, gray shirt, gray pearl-pLearl studs, gray pleated-falling-coat, black homburg, and white shoes.\nBefore entering The Blue Piano he lit the three candles he was carrying and stuck them in his hat. Then, humming, he went into the club\u2014\nHe sat at the bar and drank whisky. He read water from the bar into the mouths of the three burning candles, and watched their flames break.\nHe chuckled. He put out the burning candles and rekindled them from fresh ones burning in the bartender\u2019s water glasses.\nA waiter brought him two hotdogs, two orders of French fried potatoes, two cans of beer.\nDu Bois e delicately pinched the burned tips off his three candles, turned them around, and burned the other ends.\n\"I\u2019ll do...", "2_21_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine sixteen in the evening</span>.\nThat was the exact moment that Pierce, having switched himself off, took his last breath, exhaled it, allowed his head to fall over on his partner\u2019s shoulder, and ran dry forever.\nAND THROUGH THE BLEAK MIDWINTER\nRandy Panache had been to his first funeral many times, but always for people who didn\u2019t know it was their last. Those were the easiest. Being sure someone is going to rise from the dead makes all the attendant horrors, confusion, and sadness of bereavement unnecessary. You\u2019re twice as big, half as miserable. You can lean over into all the fresh graves, scoop up the shiny coins of grief and remorse, buy lemon ices all around. That\u2019s the way it was at Randy\u2019s funerals.", "0_19_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>, and the place was Hartford, Connecticut\u2014population, 100,000.\nI spent three hours and eleven minutes in Hartford. During this time I took a room in a small quiet hotel in the residential section, ate a filling supper in an adjoining drugstore, and became acquainted with the layout of the particular neighborhood in which I found myself.\nThen I continued to loiter in the neighborhood until I heard a clock somewhere near me strike ten. This gave me a specific understanding of local time intervals. I wanted to know what things were like at three in the morning in Hartford.\nAnother interesting thing happened to me in Hartford. As I was strolling around the corner from my hotel, an empty taxicab drove up and stopped for me. I pointed out a place across the street and got in.\n\u201cDrive me across the river and through the woods,\u201d I said as I fastened the safety-belt.\n\u201cI know the place you mean, \u201c said the driver. \u201cRight away, sir?\u201d\n\u201cRight away.\u201d\nThe man had a newspaper that he wished me to read. I shook my head. \u201cIf it\u2019s rush hour, this\u2019ll take some...", "2_4_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four nineteen in the morning</span>.\nWinthrop and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had a lot in common, after all.\nThey would have been great friends had they known each other. Both were orphans. Both were show-business successes. Both had fought in the same war. Both had written religious books that had sold more than a million copies. Both had written books of philosophy that nobody had read. Both had gotten rich selling lovely homes to people who lived in Providence or Cincinnati.\nThe cultural differences between Winthrop and $SURNAME0 were interesting. $SURNAME0 had no more culture than the evening breeze. He commanded culture the way he commanded the elements.\nWinthrop labored, bought culture the way he bought French provincial furniture. He was always trying desperately to prove that he wasn\u2019t parvenu.\nBut isn\u2019t it just possible that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and Lincoln Winthrop were really two peas in a pod?\nWhen a man proves to himself that he is cultured, after all, isn\u2019t that just another way of proving that he is number one? And doesn\u2019t being the number one man in the world have anything in common with being God?\nOh, well. Let it go.", "1_8_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the slab of stone on which $FIRSTNAMEB0 lay face-down before the firing squad.\nIt had been a minute since the squad had marched smartly out of position. It had been a minute since a soldier had stepped up to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head and briefly touched his neck with the point of a knife.\nA minute ago, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been trembling. A minute ago, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been filling his lungs for a scream. A minute ago, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been madly wishing that he hadn\u2019t come to Mars. A minute ago, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been wondering how come the Army had been interested in a guy who was only forty-one years old.\nNow, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was hoping he could keep his back bones from showing how much they wanted to snap, crackle, and crumble. Now, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was wondering how come the Army had been interested in a guy who couldn\u2019t understand the horrible things he had to do.\nThe awful tension flowed out of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s body, then, as he realized that he wasn\u2019t going to be ordered to scream and wasn\u2019t going to be ordered to die. He felt shame at having been so tense. He felt relief...", "1_20_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-five in the evening</span>. I was on my way out to Big Denny's Fine Jewelry Co. in the shopping center on Bonita and State.1 was coming out to try to trade in the big gold \u201cdiplomat\u201d G. I had in my front teeth. I didn't think they'd give me much for it, but I had to make some deal...make some kind of a deal, at least make some inquiries. My head was empty. Wild confusion. A terrible sense of loss. How well I knew that feeling of pounding distress in my head and those moments of shocking mental emptiness I used to get on Mulholland Drive, just above Ice House Canyon...that terrible sense of lost control, out there with the quick sand under the illusion of substance and that awful feeling of having to fake a natural calm, a consciousness, when you're really out of your mind, totally involved in a moment that does not rationale. A terrible scene, awful to have to confront\u2014facing the emptiness, but having to pretend that everything is alright, that you're just fine, knowing that when you look into somebody's eyes there is a terrible lie behind your own.\nBut where? Where had I seen that look before? That horrible vacant look. I stared...", "1_8_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. At first glance, it might have seemed that nothing out of the ordinary was happening. The group of men had been lounging near a long wooden storage shed that smelled of fresh-cut wood and fine dust. The shed was empty; nothing was in it but some old wire and a few lengths of lumber.\nThe men, at 8:00 A.M., were typical of the sort who might have been found anywhere in the Tropic of Cancer in historical times\u2014that is, at about the time of Christ. They were all tanned deep brown. They wore, to a man, rough blue denim pants with immense pockets and blue denim shirts with immense pockets, too.\nThere was not a pair of white tennis shoes in the lot.\nTheir muscled brown legs were all bare and dusty, and the legs in all of the enormous pockets, of course, were also bare and dusty. Only one man wore anything on his feet\u2014and he had two toes missing from one foot and none at all from the other. This made him awkward, and he had a tendency to stumble. The two men who followed him closely wherever he went had to keep an eye on him, and laugh. They were his brothers.\nThey were, as a group...", "2_15_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-nine in the afternoon</span> as we nudged over the state line/We were moving fast and smooth, and I felt very fine with my old friend CARLOS beside me. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was slumped over the wheel, half asleep, half singing. He had been snatched out of the \u201ccity jail\u201d that morning, hardly enough time to sleep off his drunk. We were stopped at a traffic signal when a fat man dashed up from across the street and began feverishly explaining something to $FIRSTNAMEB3. I was watching the light; it turned green and I started through the intersection. The guy suddenly grabbed $FIRSTNAMEB3 and said, \u201cNo, not that one, this one!\u201d He leaped ahead of us and ran to a shabby hotel, waving at us and calling, \u201cRight here! Right here!\u201d\nI pulled up and he rushed over, eager and sweating, carrying a brown paper bag. \u201cMy name is Benny,\u201d he said. \u201cYou want a girl? I'm a pimp and I just got twelve beauties out of jail -- unwashed, of course, but their assholes squeak like rabbits .\" He talked constantly. We could use only one girl. The car was too small. He wanted forty dollars apiece.", "1_3_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. The saloon was about as full as it had been two hours earlier, when $FIRSTNAMEB1 had been relieved by $FIRSTNAMEF0. The bar was still being tended by young $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 out of Kokomo, Indiana, still looking forward to some time in the future when he would recover his real self somewhere and somehow amid the starry wastes so bafflingly revealed by the bar\u2019s slowly rotating spiral of heavenly bodies.\nThe waiter at tables, crouching over beer-foam and hamburger-gristle, was still young $FIRSTNAMEB2 Gipsy of Brooklyn.\nRansom K. F befriend, still stood behind the bar with $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s police pistol in hand.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was still in the ladies\u2019 room.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was the only person in the whole Universe who knew that $SURNAME0, his wife, and his dog were about to die. And it was such a glorious destiny for $SURNAME0, for his wife, for their dog\u2014\nYoung $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 looked up at the spiral of space in awe. There was so much to learn, so much luck still to be had.\nTwenty-two minutes to go.\n* * *\n$SURNAME1 had taken the management of his affairs...", "0_3_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>. Below us, ghostly in the rosy glow, were the spires, the chimney pots, the domes and the walled colleges of Cambridge, Massachusetts. I looked down. Just over on the right were the Widener Library, the oldest buildings of Harvard, and the Yard of Harvard College. Off to the left somewhere was the river\u2014but it was too dark to see the River Charles, I could only see where the shore of the river should be by the high tide mark of dirt and college garbage pushing up against the foot of College Hill. All of this was contained in a delicate rose-colored glow that emanated from the sodium vapor street lamps. I looked to the right and saw the I-6400 sign with the temperature 66 below zero and a rising wind. This was the coldest night of the year. The sky was absolutely clear, without so much as a hint of ice-dust. In the stillness I heard the comfortable whirring of the radiation moter of Lt. Col. Bonds of the Massachusetts State Police helicopter, who hovered three hundred feet above us. We had volunteered to help keep an eye on the populace at large. The State Police had assured us that there would be no rioting in the streets,...", "1_7_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-eight in the morning</span>. I gagged on my coffee and ran. The mariachi band was still asleep as I opened the door of my car and slithered behind the steering wheel. No time to stow the beer bottles. I left them sitting on top of the saguaro cacti, next to the water\u2014filled bathtub. Then I blasted out of the lot and headed across town toward the airport, weaving left and right to avoid trash cans and piles of broken glass in the predawn gloom.The start of the main runway at Sky Harbor Airport leads directly up a steep escarpment crowded with badland detritus\u2014boulders, sharp ravines, and crushed railroad gravel from the old steam locomotives. There used to be a warning sign at the base of the incline, but some wit with a bulldozer had blasted his private road straight up through it. I made the first turn at full speed. The rear end of the Big Red Car tried to skid out, but luckily there was a thicket of mesquite to control the slide. My speed was about forty when I hit the first boulder and the car catapulted into space. It slammed back down and smacked the ground, bounced up on the left wheels, spun around a...", "1_18_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-seven in the evening</span> by my sexton watch and Halloween. I had been jettisoned with amazing swiftness through the space time warp of fifty years. Sixty years gone, sirens and trundlebugs in Des Moines, Rose Mary mowing the lawn in Butte, oh Mohass, I awoke to thoughts of $FIRSTNAMEB3 Ray in his prime when we ate raw onions in the shadow of the unknown. $FIRSTNAMEB3 Ray this very day in New York, somewhere on 42d Street; and somewhere in Italy Carlo Marx turning sixty, and thousands of cats in basements licking their chops while they waited for the old men to die. How sad and strange. And that night I in my preposterous suit and woolly wig stuck a battered bicycle over the muddy grass of my mother's lawn and began the most dismal and outrageous day of the decade. Far across the country $FIRSTNAMEB3 began his lonely evening walk to the ragged end of the vacant lot on Hudson Street. \u201cYes, she's gone and left me, Sam,\u201d he probably said, \u201cand by now that raggedy little kid has taken up with somebody else across town. I've lost everything all at one blow, my life and my nose. And I can't sleep now for all the awfulness,...", "1_14_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span> when she awoke. She raised herself on her elbow and looked around her.\nThe waves were long and regular. They undulated forward and came down upon the shore with a soft, fawn-colored rush, a smooth and evanescent swell that fell back and appeared to dissolve in the dry, white sand, leaving no trace of foam. The sea was quiet and there was no wind. The beach, the cliff, the sea, the misty, blue-green hills beyond, all quivered perceptibly in the heat. It was silent. Nothing stirred nor made a sound.\nBruises, she thought. I ought to collect my bruises. Bruises can be painful. I should imagine some of them must be very painful. Of course, a bruise is only a hemorrhage underneath the skin: a visible hemorrhage. It is really more effective to make a person blind. To strike a person's eyes so that he becomes blind! Really, there is something horrible about it, is there not?\nYounger men than that, she thought, looking at the cliff, and then out to sea, where the green hills were rising like the backs of dromedaries above the unruffled sand. There were plenty of them, on...", "2_12_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was finding the crowd far more nerve-racking than would have been the case if he hadn\u2019t been in love with $SURNAME1.\nThe crowd was an embarrassment. The crowd was embarrassing simply by doing what crowds do.\nA crowd means nothing autonomous. A crowd means a vast number of individuals who have trampled their ribs and their ego underfoot, who have said goodbye forever to all their privacy, who have abolished all their passions, who are as hinge\u2014less and corporate as a swarm of bees on the wing.\nA crowd would never break ranks. A crowd would never behave eccentrically. A crowd would never fall in love.\nAnd here was $FIRSTNAMEB0, desperately in love with a creature that he, $FIRSTNAMEB0, had picked out of the true crowd. As long as $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t go too often to the reproduction rooms in the basement of the palace, all would end happily.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was, at this particular moment in time, not going to the reproduction rooms in the basement of the palace.\nSo there was nothing to worry about, really.", "1_19_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty in the evening</span> when I left the place. For a moment I had the impulse to return to that yard and play the great clown-frog in the center of the ring; take the bottles, glasses, the cues and leave everything in wild disorder...and then leap out of the second-floor window, thirty feet above the ground, into the soft coal-dusted earth below. Maybe after five beers my whole system was shot to pieces; I wasn't myself. The thought went through my mind; it was just an impulse...and I might have done it ten years ago.\nBut now I could only smile and walk away, noting with curious satisfaction that it was going to rain in three minutes. \u201cGoing to rain in three minutes; going to rain in three minutes,\u201d I kept babbling to myself like the old man in E. A. Poe. The I got in my car and drove slowly off into the wonder-light of the desert sunset. It was going to rain in three minutes. And normally I would have observed that the red light was ahead of schedule; now of course, nothing was ahead of schedule. It really was going to rain at three minutes.\nCentennial! Centennial! Centennial!\u201c Wonder what kind of...", "2_0_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-one ante meridiem</span>. And for the love god I had to get moving. Suddenly I was afraid to move. It was crazy, but I was afraid to start the car. I could actually sense the Great White in that trunk. Like a big cat that might leap up and rip my hands off. Could I control it? Would it stand for sour and\u2014ups? Shit, man...I realized I had to get into a stable, controlled state. My hands felt shaky and my teeth were all aquiver. I was all wired in a dull, drugged, tingling frenzy. A rush of adrenaline, not unlike what I had felt earlier when the Whale, coke freak and all, suddenly bore down on me with his ID and weapon and demanded that I get out.", "0_9_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-one in the morning</span>. At the Space-Track Filter Center in the basement of the Pentagon it might be anything from seventeen minutes after the thirteenth hour of the seventh day of the third quarter of the year A.D. to seventeen minutes before the first hour of the fifty-eighth day of the first quarter of A.D. The soft round clock said nine twenty-one. The twelve girls on the twelve terminals hummed along to their jobs in fembot anonymity. They did not talk on the job.\nThere was no supervisor in the room. The girls were on their own, as they had been on their own since the supervisor\u2019s terminal had fused shut three weeks earlier. The fused terminal was a badge of honor. The supervisor was proud of it. She was a supervisor without a terminal, proud that she didn\u2019t need a terminal. She was on the job twenty-four hours a day. She could be reached only through the commander\u2019s secretary upstairs. Any messages for her would be delivered only to her through the commander\u2019s secretary. These arrangements, of course, did not free the supervisor from any of her duties. The arrangements only freed her from terminals.\nThe Space-Track Filter Center was in utter chaos. It was...", "0_17_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until six o'clock in the evening</span>, and the place was the wild, blue yonder forty thousand feet above the Earth, and the $SURNAME0 estate was three thousand miles below and ten thousand dollars worth of golden furnishings, whimsies, and novelties in the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Cigarette account, at dust.\n\"It\u2019s happened again\u2014 hasn\u2019t it?\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 sadly. \"You and I have been rained out again. We\u2019ll fall right out of the sky in a minute.\"\nAnd, indeed, the Titan II was beginning to fall the way a rock falls.\n\"How\u2019s your family?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Fine\u2014thanks,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Still in the plumbing business in Indianapolis?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Nice girls?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n$SURNAME0 brightened a little. He got out his English leather billfold again. \"I\u2019m still a believer in good news, Mrs. Watson,\" he said.\n\"Oh\u2014?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0, \"I still believe good news will win in the next...", "1_19_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nIt was time to knock on the door.\n* * *\nEARLY IN THE MORNING of February 1, Newport, Rhode Island, was white with rain.\nAnd as the rain fell in Newport, $SURNAME0, a thousand miles away, was dancing in his track on the exact spot where he and his dog had materialized.\n$SURNAME0 was holding his left hand aloft, and the diamond ring on his left index finger was flashing brilliantly in the Solar flare.\nA speck on the horizon resolved itself into a Solar tube, like a urinal in the sky. The tube was buzzing under power, blue sparks shooting from a loose wire in the power switch.\nThe tube dropped open, and out hopped Skip. He was carrying a sample case, a blanket, and other things.\nSkip dropped the things, ran to his master.\n\"Beatific and Beloved,\" he said in greeting, \"we fooled them again!\"\n$SURNAME0 chuckled and rested his hand affectionately on the crown of Skip\u2019s head. \"You certainly did, Space Wanderer,\" he said. \"At least until the next time.\"\n$SURNAME0 was out of breath. He was, as always, much more interested...", "1_1_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe Martians were to arrive at the earth three days earlier, at two o'clock in the afternoon of September 18th.\nThe thousand were crowded and cramped under their square of canvas, but they were safe, they were protected from the Martian death.\nThey would lose their houses, their churches, their mills, their estates; but they would be spared.\nThey would outnumber the thousand by a fortune of more than forty to one.\nWhat did space mean to them? What did time mean to them? Nothing.\nThey had purchased protection from the dust and from the whirring tuft of blue flame a mile in diameter that a Martian ship created by itself in space. And that was that.\nThey did not even guess-could not even conceive of the possibility of a single ship carrying destruction to all the peoples of Earth.\nPeter McAllister was fast asleep on his back in the forest. His hands were folded prayerfully on his chest. His face was calm, his expression beatific.\nHe did not snore.\nThe Bible he had been struggling with under the illumination of a flashlight lay pressed flat under his left shoulder.\nThere was no expression on the face of the watch that he wore fastened with...", "0_16_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Cathedral of Saint John the Theologian on $FIRSTNAMEB2 Island was now releasing a Hammond B-3 organ fugue based on themes from Beethoven\u2019s \u201cMissa Solemnis.\u201d The music, in poor stereo translation on 3\u30d6, insisted on the listener\u2019s subliminal participation in the two-part faith that had led the human species in all its best endeavors. Had human beings not given their allegiance to an abstract enterprise that served no immediate and impure purpose, they would never have traveled to the moon, they would not have created music that could make a sensitive person\u2019s hair curl. American owners of Financier redwood slice houses, thus challenged, took their chances by trying to spike the tune with a distortion rasp or a clump of cymbal clash. The chances never came off.\nThe owner of the Tralfamadorian Tralfamadorian nighttime bar chain was nicked Ernest Wisdom, a millionaire who made his money raising razorback hogs. He was a local boy, born in 1938, the year Elvis Presley came to the attention of the music industry. He had come to the attention of the industry himself by getting 95 percent of the possible correct answers...", "2_22_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-nine in the evening</span>. The place was a small and slightly crooked room. In it were apparatus for the recording and sending of sound, for automatic writing, and for cold-reading.\nA dead man lay in the coffin-shaped box for recording and sending sound. His name was John Joseph Lockworth. The dead man had very black hair.\nHis eyes were closed. One of the dead man\u2019s teeth had been knocked out by a kick from an almost supernatural force.\nA woman with long blond hair sat in the apparatus for cold-reading. Her name was Random Frequent Flyer Corsi, Ph.D. She had begun life as Mary Alice Gerling, of Houghton, Michigan.\nRandom Frequent Flyer Corsi looked at the writing that had appeared on a piece of paper by means of the apparatus that recorded writing automatically. She was frowning slightly at the writing on a piece of paper.\n\"Oh, yuck!\" said Random Frequent Flyer Corsi.", "1_20_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-one in the evening</span> in the bar at the Ambassadors Hotel when I recommended to Bill Harris, the author of The True Life Story of Huckleberry Finn, that he buy a few flasks for the road. We pointed at my briefcase on the table.\n\u201cIt wouldn't fit,\u201d said Bill Harris. \u201cIt wouldn't go in there with the whisky and all that stuff in your briefcase.\"\n\u201c. . . and the tape recorder,\u201d I said.\n\u201cYeah,\u201d said Bill, \u201cand the tape recorder, and all, and the works. You can't get a quart of whisky in a briefcase, anyhow, can you?\"\n\u201cNot a full one,\u201d I said.\nActually I had agreed to carry the whisky and the tape recorder as a blind. The real bundle was something else, and even that was bound in a kind of gunny-sack that tied down tight in the middle and had two canvas handles with rope tied to them. It looked like a ice trunk that I had seen in my mother's kitchen in New Orleans twenty years before.\n\u201cWhat the hell you got in there, anyhow?\u201d\nThe bartender came up for the...", "1_1_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>, and the space ship acted like it knew.\nThere followed thirty seconds of the wildest roller-coaster ride any living crew had ever taken. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the good sergeant were hurled from wall to wall, from floor to ceiling. $FIRSTNAMEB0 felt his skull and all his major bones shake and tremble. His lips came unhooked from his teeth, his eyebrows were plucked loose. His tongue unrolled like a carpenter\u2019s pencil.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s army buddy lasted even less well than $FIRSTNAMEB0. Before the thirty seconds were up, the sergeant lost his head. Literally lost his head, for the discipline-hardened torso chose that moment to kick the head loose.\nThe head struck a wall and bounced like a soccer ball. The headless torso bounded and struck another wall, then ricocheted onto its head and somersaulted across the room.\nWith its characteristic burping gurgle, the pilot-voice box proclaimed victory over the headless sergeant. \"Head apprehended,\" it said. \"Dissociative reactions stabilized. Better indication of home attitudes, better established.\"\nThere was silence.\n\"Defecation indicated,\" said the pilot-voice box. \"Sorry\u2014must hurry.\"\nThere...", "0_5_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe man who had written the message on the tile wall kicked open the iron door of a furnace.\nThe furnace was cold.\nThe man laughed a chuckle of relief. \"Good old $SURNAME0!\" he said. \"The good old boy\u2019s already been through here, burning up every last piece of material he could lay his hands on.\" The other two men in the powerhouse chuckled relieved chunks of laughs, too.\n\"Of course, he left the dynamos and the turbogenerators, \" said the man who had assumed command, \"because he knew we\u2019d need \u2019em.\"\nHe winked at his two companions.\n\"Would you mind telling me who you are?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"We\u2019re the Committee for Federal\u2014or rather, Interplanetary\u2014Management,\" said the man who had assumed the leadership. He took off his stocking cap, revealing a badly pocked and weatherbeaten countenance. His hair was long and stringy. He put the stocking cap back on, grasping a corner of the material in his teeth. \"I\u2019m $FIRSTNAMEM2 Jimmy James, formerly of Fort Worth, Texas,\" he said, \"but now without a home, without a family,...", "1_6_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-five in the morning</span>. I took all the Scotch I could carry and ran out to the car. The parking lot was empty A red convertible came up on Las Vegas Blvd., hesitated, went on, and I was on the main drag with all the lights against me, yelling at drivers who would not pick me up. A police siren behind scared me by be ing between me and the white line. I slammed on the brakes just in time for the cop car to miss me by inches. Cursing idiot! I thought. He's off duty. He's not mad. He'd give me a ride.\nBut he only smiled. The siren won't always work. O your lovely siren! You lull them with your complaisant wail and then, blam, in go two redeyes and the lug is right in the middle of onr whit line.\u201cHuh?\u201d he says, amazed. \u201cWhat happened, Officer?\u201d Nobody will tell him. No traffic coming now. Do I run to a side street? Does an old Buick slow down? No, no. But I walk across town to the garage where they have my car 13/10, wait ing for the attendant to show up. Confusion, no...", "2_0_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past midnight</span>.\n\u201cI didn\u2019t either. I feel absolutely Normal. There\u2019s nothing wrong with me.\u201d\n\u201cGood.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ve shut the door.\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s good.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s all off. There\u2019s no one in the world but you and me.\u201d\n\u201cDo you feel pretty well?\u201d\n\u201cDamned well.\u201d\n\u201cYou know it\u2019s all off?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cHow do you feel?\u201d\n\u201cI feel fine.\u201d\nShe stood up. \u201cI\u2019m going to the barber shop. Don\u2019t you think I ought to have it cut?\u201d\n\u201cNow?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m going right down. It\u2019s off, you know.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll go with you.\u201d", "1_10_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, Earthtime. The place was the lobby of Corky's Hotel on Mars. The lobby consisted of two large, encircling areas of red carpet broken by two parallel strips of clear combustible material called asbestos.\nAt seven-fifteen that morning, the disembarking passengers from Miss Tiny\u2019s Lovely Tours had gone through a space-warp tunnel and dropped neatly into the lobby of Corky\u2019s Hotel. It was a vulnerable moment, and three beggars had taken advantage of it by following each tour member. But $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s beggar was the only one not to be frightened off by the clique that closed around $FIRSTNAMEM2 as soon as he materialized in the lobby.\nThe beggar was an unshaven space-bum, in filthy brown clothing, with a plastic sign hung around his neck by strands of unwaxed dental floss. The sign said: \"U. S. GOVERNMENT. PLEASE GIVE.\" He had wild, Mongoloid eyes, a lopsided Haggard face, stubble on his chin, and an utterly shameless countenance.\nHe stood eight feet from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, staring with idiotic intensity into $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s eyes as $SURNAME1 rans...", "1_13_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-two in the afternoon</span> of June twenty-first, and the place was New York City, North America, Earth. The materials\u2014love, madness, and homemade hallucinogens\u2014were brought here by human freighters in the form of thirty-one-year-old Rachel Nabors Kanzler, a resident of the City of New York, Earth, Solar System, Milky Way, Universe; and a humanoid native of the planet Tralfamadore, six thousand, three hundred, and thirty-one light-years from Earth, Solar System, Milky Way, Universe.\nDr. Robert Hallowell Pierce was about to hand himself a martini when the doorbell rang.\nThe martini cost $3.50. Pierce got it from a well-stocked bar in the wall of a closet. He got his gin and vermouth free. He got ice free. The closet was free, and so was the bell.\nThe bell was the doorbell in the apartment across the corridor and one floor up from the three rooms that Pierce inhabited.\nThe man who lived in the apartment across the corridor and one floor up was a chemist, inventor, entrepreneur, philanthropist, and candy-bar magnate. His name was Salmagundi.\nPierce...", "0_12_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty post meridiem</span>.\nOdd snatches of primitive religious chants came from the Sub-Etha radio of the gypsy cab driver.\nThe driver was intent on his driving. It seemed obvious that he was simply taking his passengers on a grand tour of as many hazards and hideous customs as he could think of in the quarter-hour fare.\nThe taxi came to a nest of barking gas holes, around which a hundred men were beating flaming torches at the air. The taxi threaded its path among the torches without the passengers soiling their clothes\u2014or perhaps because the passengers were so rich and wonderful.\nThe passengers were treated to kicking and brain-battering from a mob of religious strikers.\nExpressions of pure goofishment were plastered over the faces of the rich passengers.\nTheir heads bobbed in time to the barbaric yawpings that came from the radio. The meaning of the yawpings was as follows: Beat down striker! Beat down striker! God bless rich man! God give million-dollar tip! Rub faces of strikers into rich man\u2019s fancy underwear! Work hard, be tired, God bless rich man! The passengers hooted gleefully.\nThe passengers paid the cab-driver their thirty-six-cent fare,...", "1_13_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty in the afternoon</span>. These were the first words $FIRSTNAMEB3 had spoken since we left Larimer Street at six o'clock.\nWe boomed into Fort Collins and bought gas and kept on going. I wanted to go to La Junta --the jumping-off place on the Colorado plains, way over the mountains. \u201cIf we make La Junta,\u201d I said, \u201cit'll be Colorado --all the way.\u201d\n\u201cYes!\u201d cried $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cWe'll make it.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF4 was asleep. She woke up and said, \u201cWhere are we going?\u201d We told her.\n\u201cAnything can happen,\u201d she said. She gave a little shiver. \u201cWhere's breakfast? I'm starving.\u201d But nobody had anything to eat except coffee and cigarettes. We were out of money; we hadn't dared stopping at Papillon's place for fear he would lock $FIRSTNAMEB3 up again; now we were beyond his jurisdiction. We were all out of money except $FIRSTNAMEB3, and he had locked his away in Connie's house. It was all locked away in different houses and there was no way to get any of it. The paradise of the future was now not going to be paid...", "1_17_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-four in the afternoon</span>. The sun had missed us as we drove through Flagstaff, reminding me to get my watch fixed sometime. We\u2019d only been on the road again for half an hour, in an AustinA40, the kind of English car they actually manufacture in Germanyand we were humming on Route 66 in the hot noonday sun. \u201cWe\u2019re right on schedule,\u201d I called to my holy brother at the wheel. \u201cThat\u2019s fine,\u201d he muttered. He was very preoccupied on the straight, flat road that ran elegantly through bare rock and cactus between hightowers. I wondered if he was brooding about the girl we\u2019d just left in room seventeen of the Snow\u2019s: a strange, lovely redhaired girl from Berkeley, Calif., who wanted us to take her fantastic ape along with us to Miami. She\u2019d got the ape in Mexico, signing papers in a lawyer\u2019s office fifty miles south of the border. Now she was holding the beast in a special cage in a family room at the Snow\u2019s Motor Hotel, promising to liberate it in a few hours, assuring us with tears in her eyes that the thing understood perfectly and was simply delighted to...", "1_3_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-two in the morning</span>. I decided to hit Aspen before the dawn crowds arrived. Major Burris made a few calls and found the nearest motel\u2014the Sunset Court in Winnemucca. We landed there in the deadest part of the night, checked in and collapsed on smelly rags that passed for beds\u2014four dollars a day. The place had a bar, and I stood dry for the first time in over forty-eight hours. Amazingly, Burris found a liquor store still open, and before I knew what was happening he'd bought a fifth of Canadian Club and was getting ice from a machine. About four in the morning we kicked back in that rancid room and put the radio on \u201cdim\u201d: Creedence Clearwater was telling about \u201cBad Moon Rising.\u201d Major Burris got rolling on the freeway situation, shouting like a fool and pounding the bed.\nThey should've had me in Washington, he yelled. \"They wanted to put me in charge of interstate transit, but the fools that run my outfit refused the assignment. Hell, if I'd've run the program, we'd all be driving Cadillacs by now. What do they think the highway structure's for? Let's face it, man\u2014we need a...", "1_6_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>, and I raced the Ford down Laurel Canyon Boulevard to Sunset, zoomed across Sunset and straight over the going-to-hell LA River into an empty Bronson, where I ducked with a squeal into my own driveway, ran up the back steps, tore through the kitchen and bedroom, slammed everything behind me and shot down to the end of the driveway, fumbling for the box with the spare key to the padlock on $FIRSTNAMEB3's front gate. I arrived before it and started playing with it. While I lingered there, wondering what to do next, a man came out of the house next door and gazed wonderingly at me. He was a pleasant old man.\n\u201cCan you tell me what time it is?\u201d\n\u201cSure -- it's just after seven.\u201d I looked at my watch, confounded.\n\u201cI thought you might be able to tell me what time it is because my watch has stopped.\u201d\n\u201cThat's right,\u201d said the old gentleman.\n\u201cIt won't work any more.\u201d I put my watch in my pocket; I fiddled with the padlock; the old man watched me intently.\n\u201cI'm waiting for my...", "0_4_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMr. $FIRSTNAMEF1, the man in Room 220 of the Space Nuts Home, opened his eyes. And what Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1 saw, when he opened his eyes, was an Anticyerical Priest.\n\"God bless my soul,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"An Anticriticall Priest.\"\nAs if in answer to Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1's exclamation, the cleric spoke. The cleric spoke in the strongest possible dialect of central Tennessee. \"You the one who sent for me?\" he said.\n\"I got no religion, but,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"I\u2019nld be inclined to think that\u2019s really an Anticyerical Priest you got on.\"\nThe cleric cleared his throat. \"My name is Bittmoser,\" he said, \"and I am the lay administrator of Banjoville Baptist Church, near Kingsport, in the central part of our great state. I am also the Reverend Owen Bittmoser. I got a message to come right away, on account of a tragic\u2014\"\n\"Just a minute,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1. He turned, picked up a phone on the stand by his bed. \"Room service. Yes, this is Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1 in Room 220.", "2_19_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-six in the evening</span>.\nNoise, pain in the head, a nasal voice battering the eardrums; eventually, this gave way to images, carried by the battering voice into the closed eyes.\nThere was a ball of dirty soap in a cracked pedestal basin, and the basin was full of dirty luke- warm water.\nA hand reached into the basin and batted the ball around. Every time the ball touched the hand, it left a smear. It was one of $SURNAME0\u2019s hands, a right hand that looked like a great emerald knee-cap. It was a gliding hand. It had its own special source of power.\nThe hand batted the ball of soap; eventually aimed the ball at a drain in the floor. The drain was a throat, leading to a terrible digestive system from which nothing returns.\nThe hand was in the bed next to $SURNAME0\u2019s. It was a brilliantly engineered hand\u2014 no hint of organic malformation, no suggestion of uncanny strength. Nonetheless, this hand had opened every doorknob in the house, had handled every utensil, had cranked the starter of every car in the garage.\nThe hand had definitely shown the will to dominate everything in sight.", "1_7_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-one in the morning</span>, Monday, October 23, 2006, and Ronald S. Lauder, age twenty-six\u2014but looking older; actually, a very handsome sixty\u2014was simultaneously staring into Room 223, Mel joint\u2019s broken-smoked window and listening over a hiss with all the other sober homeowners at station WQHJ997 for Dr. Sly\u2019s morning rerun.\nDr. Winkelzahn, the Martian psychologist whom Stonecipher had brought from Wrigley Field, that afternoon, to determine whether or not Lang was a genuine, had told a stunned crowd and media corps all last summer that Irwin Wand was neither crazy nor an imposter.\nLast summer. The Year of the Yushityu.\nWinkelzahn\u2019s report on Lang had made him furious about the whole thing. White had started the whole matter because, according to the Martian, Lenore Beadsman was the one who was neither crazy nor an imposter. White had become obsessed with Lenore because she had given him a grain of Martian sand, to play with. The sand had made him horny. To Hell with Lenore Beadsman.\nAt twenty-nine, Ronald S. Lauder became enraged at everything. He had spent...", "1_8_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-five in the morning</span>. I was snuffling around L.A. in the '53 Chevy on my way to watch my brother and $FIRSTNAMEB3 eat breakfast. $FIRSTNAMEF4 was still asleep in Tim Gray's bed. She had an awful pain in her side with her hangover and was afraid she had cancer after a classmate had died of it the week before. Tim Gray tried to comfort her. After I left, they took a short walk. \u201cGood-by little spoon,\u201d said Tim. \u201cI got you a present, a little something to remember me by.\u201d They went into the kitchen for the present. Meanwhile, Ed Dunkel was outside in the front lawn, feeding the sparrows. Then he took his bundle, said good-byes all around, dressed, and bid the little boys good morning. The day was fair and beautiful. He was off again. He was on the Coast again. Everywhere was the Coast. Removing his shoes, he crept along the downstairs hall of the house and tiptoed out the back door -- but only after Dad had called him from the breakfast table and bawled him out for losing his shovel and derby in the unfortunate fight across the street the night before. In the backyard he put on...", "2_22_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span> in Boston, Massachusetts. A telephone rang on the desk of Chad Mulligan, Ishmael\u2019s best friend, on the second floor of Beacon Hill.\nWhen Chad answered it, he heard only the buzz of a New York telephone.\nThe best modem connections in the world made no difference to the message that went along to a computer bFrom there in two directions over telephone wires. One set of connections ran to Harley Potter\u2019s phone on Wall Street, New York City. The message reached his phone at nine minutes to eleven. aMaiden, persisting at its most primitive, found Ishmael in his sheepskin jacket and his khakis, taking a position on a cliff at the western edge of a range in the nation of Nevada. He was watching the sunset because he was in love with the girl who had asked him to. His eyes were magnified into green jars by big steel-rimmed glasses. His mouth was too big for his face, and his face was too long to be movie-idol material anyhow.", "1_13_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe room was not very well lighted. There was not much light at that time of day. The curtains of the window to the right of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bunk were drawn. The roll-call room was lit by an unshielded fluorescent tube in the ceiling. It was not a malignant, flaring tube such as they had back in the barracks\u2014simply a tube, glowing white, humming steadily.\nThe furnishings of the roll-call room held $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s interest for only a short time. Eventually he got up to roam around the room. He stopped at the window with the drawn curtains, drew a curtain aside very carefully, glanced out. What he saw would not have surprised him, two years ago.\nThe room overlooked an enclosed street\u2014a street entirely surrounded by a six-foot wall of concrete topped with rolls of barbed wire. At points along the wall, gun turrets swiveled slowly, blinkers winking. This was enemy territory\u2014had to be, or guards wouldn\u2019t be necessary. The people who lived there had to be in sorrowful bondage to somebody cruel and powerful.\nA dozen yards from $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s peephole was the gate in the concrete wall...", "1_12_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-nine p.m.</span> Martian time. The sweet pale young creature was to have her incredible dream, her \u201ccognitive revelation,\u201d as she later called it, in one minute and thirty seconds.\nThe time was twelve thirty-nine and one-tenth seconds.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 began to shake the canister of peedunk snacks\u2014an empty, aural signal that somebody outside was waiting.\nKkandkk! kkandkk!\nThe canister containing the snacks was the only thing in the entire Museum of Earthly Wonders and Martian Diner that was made out of real stone\u2014rose veined and creamy veined Italian marble. Everything else was patented imitation. Millions of years of waiting had given true stone a good reason to be arrogant and bossy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 edged away from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s pretty daughter, and was grieved to find that he was shaking kkandkk! to a customer who was not there.\nThe pretty girl with the old-young face did have an exciting dream in one and one-tenth seconds. But, before the end of the dream, she was more excited by the prospect of lunch. She went over her dream in the bit of time it took to blink her drowsy eyes...", "1_22_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-nine in the evening</span>. I wondered if anybody would kill me if I just walked out of the place. Probably. So I began to think about Ernest and $FIRSTNAMEF4 again, and about the girl, and what to do. I got so nervous I couldn't hear anything. I began sweating. I blew smoke from my cigarette over the crowd. There was something perverted about doing this, as if I were transferring a piece of myself to them. I could get nothing from my cigarette; it was like making a pass in the dark. I bent down with my cigarette, thinking, \u201cAh, There is truly nothing like the first puff across the crowd after a nervous breakdown.\u201d I saw the people as I had once seen them with $FIRSTNAMEB3, in great flapping chunks that opened up for a moment and then closed again.\nI got up and approached a big handsome college-graduate type in a suit, and just as I approached I met his eye in the most natural way and smiled, and asking nothing for myself I said, \u201cLook, would you do a friend of mine a personal favor?\u201d He agreed. \u201cYou see that girl in the white pants on the far platform? She has a crush on a guy who just came in but probably won...", "2_3_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-nine in the morning</span> when UnstRuby Enterprises, Inc., took from its pocket a blue chit and sent a messenger in a cab to Mercer Street. The messenger was instructed to hand the chit to Unstruther in private, and to jot down the nature of Unstruther\u2019s response.\nThe address was exceedingly fashionable, a characteristic of which the neighborhood was not otherwise guiltless.\nThe neighborhood itself, like the purely fashionable East Side neighborhood between Fifty-seventh and Seventy-second, belonged to a New York City that was gone.\nWhen it was gone, a whole way of life stopped with it.\nThe purely fashionable East Side was gone, and a new order was in the process of arising in which pretense and reality, willfulness and acquiescence would be mixed in new ways.\nThe old neighborhood was dead and gone, but the new neighborhood was to be the very womb in which the new order gestated.\nDiscussing the neighborhood, Mary Mary Abbot Gloyer, who lived on Mercer Street, was to say, \"I don\u2019t go downtown much because usually once you get downtown it takes hours to get home.\"", "1_0_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-five a.m.</span> The place was $FIRSTNAMEF0, Nebraska, a small farm town just south of the Iowa line. I had been traveling since six o'clock the previous night, scurrying across Iowa in a Greyhound, finally jumping off at this sad little station because I had heard it was a good place to jump off to get a ride to Chicago. I stood hitching in front of a sad little tourist court, with an empty burger box from the bus trip and a Goodyear folder full of dirty stories to show interesting people who would come my way. I didn't have to wait very long at all. The first man to stop for me was a potato-nosed farmer with a rust-eaten '41 Chevy.\n\u201cWhere y' goin?\u201d said he with great formality.\n\u201cYOU don't know?\u201d I said with surprise.\n\u201cDon't start that stuff, or you won't get in this car. Where y' goin?\u201d\n\u201cChicago.\u201d\n\u201c Chicago. $20! \u201d snarled the man.\n\u201cYes,\u201d said I, fumbling with fear and trembling. \u201cDo you need that much?\u201d At that...", "1_18_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-one in the evening</span>, five minutes before last showtime. We rolled into the hotel garage, got out of the car. I had taken care of everything; somebody who I knew very well, who worked in the slot-tender shack at the Reno Airport, had promised to wire a crate of mescaline in my name to the address of our Sacramento hotel. \u201cAnything in particular you want,\u201d he said to me over the phone.\nNo, I said, \u201cI think we have a pretty versatile group; anything you have new and fine . . .\u201d\nAnd he sent five grams. I knew he would; this was our fourth trip together, a lonely, introverted drifter who spent his time in the bar at the airport drinking shot-and-beer, roaming the motels for hours, looking at gadgets and labelling adhesive tapes in his room, always despairing over the fact that he'd never make another trip with us, that he'd blown his last point. A terminal mescaline addict.\nWe checked in. The room had two huge double beds; my attorney suggested that we stagger the sleep schedule and start off the first evening together. I agreed. The poor fuck couldn't afford mescaline. \u201cHow much...", "0_3_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-eight in the morning</span>. The place, $FIRSTNAMEB2 Airport in Peoria, Illinois. It is theoretically possible to reach the city of Louisville, Kentucky, from Peoria by air within forty minutes\u2014but this would require the tailwinds of a Ninja Zinger with a pilot who knew where he was going and how to get there. Most companies would insist on a six-month course at the Whittier School for young recruits before putting a pilot into that kind of an usuaterial situation.\nImmelman, however, had obviously mastered the techniques. A glance at the glowing instruments told him everything he needed to know, including the true meaning of his mission before his very eyes in the maze of switches: REVERSE IDLING SWITCH UP\u2014OFF. REVERSE IDLING SWITCH DOWN\u2014ON.\nIt was all very simple. Reverse idling was required for a take-off out of $FIRSTNAMEB2 in an 88 with a net load of 480 pounds, when carrying 5,200 pounds of funny money to a Probability Control station on the Little Rock Air Force Base18 in the town of Newport, Kentucky.\nRichman lay asleep in the back of the 88, covered by a tarpaulin. His fifty pounds were not important. The money was...", "2_17_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and she was in the Beverly Hills Hotel, facing the looming bulk of a dim-witted ageless giver of pleasure to women, face to face at last with Can-dide.\nIt could not have been \"real.\" But it was. How can one say that? It was. She saw him for the first time, saw him looking at her with dim, love-struck eyes behind ludicrous glasses. And she saw, too, something else\u2014herself through his eyes. The eyes she usually has to employ in order to see herself are not really love-struck: they are busy and facetious and secretive, hiding amusement and fatigue. This time she looked through love-struck eyes\u2014wonderful, droll, wistful eyes\u2014and saw (oh dear) a foal-child, simple and confiding, beautiful and tender and wise, standing stroking the huge head of a domestic dog, a powerful dog, doggy and good. This might be pure imagination; it might be the kind of lie a man tells himself about a woman in love. But it is interesting.\n\"Hi,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.", "2_10_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, chrono-synclastic infundibulated, and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, trillionaire, lived again, but lived this time as trees on a little planet of a star far beyond the jurisdiction of Earth and Mars. Their sap, the substance of their lives, was the light of their star, was the press of the wind, was the stuff birds\u2019 nests were made of, was the fine gold thread that overlaid the woof and warp of the grass by the shores of the little brook.\nYes, $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 lived again as trees on a little planet of a star far beyond the jurisdiction of Earth and Mars\u2014lived as trees in the sunlight, lived in the breath of the wind, lived in the nests of birds, lived in the grass by the brook.\nAnd their story died, died as completely as the Solar System died when the sun of the system exhausts its nuclear fuel and becomes an orange rat on the gray patrician shoulders of the Galactic Lens.\n\"Lord,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 from his tree, \"make me a good-looking corpse.\"", "0_23_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEF4 and I, after saying a seemingly endless goodbye to everybody, finally roared out of the village with $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Ed Dunkel beside us in the car and, as we started off, my father and brothers in front of us, running alongside and calling encouragement -- \u201cMake it! \u201cYou'll make it!\u201d because we were overloaded. Off we went in the night. \u201cHail and Farewell,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3, and then he got a stick and began scratching a sign -- phantom hitchhikers, ghosts of the road, all see them and no one stops, and so on. We were out of Denver at midnight. The road across Nebraska, every inch of it we knew, shot by and dissolved behind us.\nMy poor cousin must have broken his back that night on our way across Nebraska, never realizing we were traveling an extra ten miles an hour to make up for lost time. Ed Dunkel and I had a kind of bitter battle because he wanted to sleep and I wanted to drive. I won and drove three hundred miles across Nebraska in thirty-degree below zero weather, with the bitter wind of the snowy Plains hitting me regularly in the eyes every time I glanced down at the speedometer before m...", "0_13_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until two o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October 12, 2008, $SURNAME1 thought, and I\u2019ll probably never know why $SURNAME0 selected those particular forty-eight seconds for such wild goings-on.\nEverything happened, or was going to happen, or had already happened, at the instant in time that $SURNAME1 was thinking about\u2014one minute until two o\u2019clock P.M. on October 12, 2008. At that instant, the microphone on the Sun rose and its madness peaked.\nThe microphone dropped again.\nIt was going to fall no further.\nIt was going to remain in place.\nIt was going to remain in place for ten thousand years.\nEverything on Titan was going to remain in place for ten thousand years.\nThe Titanic, Wilbur, $FIRSTNAMEF0, $FIRSTNAMEF1, $FIRSTNAMEB2, Salo\u2014they were going to remain in place for ten thousand years.\nThere was to be no more movement on the moons of Saturn.\nThe Earthling who was responsible for the change was well on his way to a state of perfect bliss.\nHis name was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. At that precise instant, he was getting a haircut in a barbershop in Marin County, California.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, who was also an Earthling, had just material...", "2_17_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe crowd on the corner of Hollywood and Vine emitted a prolonged pneumatic hiss. The hiss was at the lowest range of human hearing.\n\"Why?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Well, suppose I was happy,\" said Salo stiffly. \"You know what would happen, Mr. $SURNAME0?\"\n\"No,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Give me a possibility,\" said $SURNAME0.\nSalo hesitated. Then he spoke with great dignity. \"I would be riddled with guilt!\" he said.\n\"Guilt about what?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"That\u2019s right,\" said Salo. \"Guilt is the very core of my disability.\"\n\"Which is\u2014?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Accepting happiness, or any part of it,\" said Salo primly.\n\"You accept happiness now,\" said $SURNAME0. \"What\u2019s the difference?\"\n\"None,\" said Salo sadly, \"except that I wallow in it.\"", "2_12_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve nineteen post meridiem</span>, back there on Earth. And whatever had happened at twelve eighteen post meridiem back there, $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t care. No one was watching $FIRSTNAMEB0 and no one ever would again. And $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t care. He was unreachably isolato\u2014absent without official leave\u2014lost, disappeared. His footprints had been covered ages ago by footprints of men not under surveillance, men absorbed by their own dangerous games against the Martian desert, men carrying on.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 found a flashlight in a pocket. He found a pistol in another pocket. The pistol was loaded.\nHe thumbed off the safety catch. He bluffed himself with the idea that if anyone came to bother him he would shoot to kill.\nAt the bottom of the gangway to the ship he found another flight of iron stairs, down which the lava-lamps sent their glo-bats.\nDown the stairs, the stairs down, the stairs down.\nEvery twenty feet there was a cavern.\nIn every cavern a suit of space armor stood on its movable cone-feet like a dog in a kennel.\nIn every suit of space armor there was a dead soldier.", "2_23_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and his orange cat, now known as Brains, were peacefully asleep in the $SURNAME0 living room. They lay on the grand piano. $SURNAME1\u2019s head was on one end, and the cat\u2019s head was on the other.\nThe rest of the living room was in as much disarray as any living room was ever in. Its foyer was a fan-shaped explosion of popular magazines, Twentieth Century-Fox movie magazines, movie fan magazines, entertainment news syndicates, the American Weekly, TV Guide, Hollywood round-ups, Photoplay, and what-have-you.\nThe chaise longue had been moved outdoors, where it stood in a small pool of rainwater, ringed by $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s coat, tie, shirt, lemon-squeezer, shoes, and boots.\nOut of every pocket in $SURNAME1\u2019s deranged clothes, out of his hunched socks and his fractal underwear, money and thousand-dollar bills spilled. $SURNAME1 had been systematically emptying his pockets, counting his money.", "2_3_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span> in New Orleans.\nFor the purposes of description, let us imagine a man six feet two inches tall and weighing one hundred and eighty- seven pounds. He had snowy white hair and a king-sized aquiline nose. His blue eyes were mad, disoriented and wet, like the eyes of an albino rabbit, the whiteness painfully refracted into reds and yellows by the central nervous system it must traverse. He wore canary yellow billowing silk pantaloons, a scarlet-lined cape, a brown satin swallow-tail coat and patent-leather pumps. He also carried a mother-of-pearl-inlaid swordstick. His hair had been white for thirty years, but full and dashing.\nAnd this was the prince of process servers.", "1_3_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>. At thirty seconds before four o'clock, Salo's eyes began to roll. And at exactly four o'clock in the morning, Salo\u2019s eyes popped and he was stone blind.\nThe Antichrist was nineteen minutes old.\nConstantine leaned over his son, and saw with his own eyes that was certainly something amiss with the child. The child\u2019s eyes, after all, had rolled in such a way that it looked as though his eyes were inside out.\nThe child\u2019s eyes were frosted.\nSo it was that official history denied that the birth of the Antichrist took place on a space ship named Icarus II.\nRumor said he was born on a secret island, between a woman and her doctor. And that the child\u2019s birth cost the doctor his eyes\u2014and a leg and an arm.\nThe statue of ST Cent had golden eyes, out of which real tears were forever falling.\nMiss Annie Blue Wrock was proud of her statue. She was the only citizen of San Narciso to be proud of any statues or fountains, or of anything else in the many acres of the municipal waste land. She had had her statue restored so that it...", "1_11_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-one in the morning</span> on the third Thursday in November. We were in Indianapolis, where the story began. We had spent the night in a hotel room with two double beds. Before my lady went to sleep I wrapped one of the bedspreads around her and made her look like one of those cocoon things you find under rocks. She stayed that way until I fell asleep; then she shook me awake and told me she was cold. I didn't know she was awake. \"Poor little devil,\" I thought, so I huddled closer to keep her warm. She opened her eyes then, and smiled sweetly. \"Poor little devil,\" I thought again, rubbing her belly in little circles. She arched. \"Where are we?\" she asked. Our problem was where to spend the three days in Indianapolis. To explain our problem you have to go back to L.A., forty-eight hours earlier. We hung around my aunt and uncle's house for a day and a night, and then took off for Las Vegas in my aunt's brand-new Cadillac. Halfway to Vegas we parked the Cadillac beside the road and took off cross-county in my uncle's ancient coupe-a 1946 Packard convertible with a dented blue fender and two broken windows.", "2_10_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIn a bicycle battalion, a private first class is a noncommissioned officer. The duties of a private first class in a bicycle battalion include digging latrines, filling the water canteens, pitching tents, building fires, policing the area, and acting as sentry at the entry to the battalion fallout shelter.\nIf enemy aircraft attack while a bicycle battalion is in the field, the private first class turns white flares into the sky and rings a bell. Feeding and watering the battalion's two thousand bicycles is his responsibility.\nUniform worn by a private first class in a bicycle battalion: shorts, puttees, ankle-high leggings to which are attached footcloths and toecloths, undershirt and undershirt, sleeveless brown wool shirt of a heavy material, knitted wool jersey sweater over that, uniform coat, cowhide gloves, lace-up shoes, military surcingle, service cap. A silver whistle is hung from the left pocket of the shorts.\nUniform color worn by a private first class: Air Corps tan, the shade formerly known as Chocolate Pecan, later renamed Chickasaw Brick Dust.", "1_23_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nOne Hundred and Twenty-five Miles from Las Vegas\nFIGURING SUBJECTIVELY, using the noise and the roar around her as her cue, that she must be right over the middle of the Mojave Desert, $SURNAME1 began the long glide down through two thousand miles of empty air.\nShe remembered to hold her breath.\n\"Now I lay me\u2014\" she whispered as the floor of the $SURNAME0 estate fell away. \"Now I lay me\u2014\" she whispered as the walls of Newport fell away. \"Had I\u2014but wings\u2014\" she whispered as the iron patio furniture fell away. \"Had I\u2014\" she whispered as the faint jungle undergrowth fell away. \"Then me\u2014\" she whispered as the reflecting pools fell away. \"Then me\u2014\" she whispered as the lily pads fell away and the underwater lights of the palaces of Atlantis fell away, leaving the West incandescent. \"I what?\" she whispered as the last filtered memory of $SURNAME0 faded, left her hanging in space over, she suspected, a portion of the United States.\nShe thought it was probably the Southwest.\nShe could see the glowing spirals of smoke rising from the bonfires of industry. They formed a Milky Way that might have...", "2_10_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and Wendell and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 were sitting on the porch of the Space Museum, watching the eleven-o\u2019clock People\u2019s Assault Brigade of $FIRSTNAMEB2-imploded Venusians arrive. The porch was as neatly kept as everything else on the Estate. It was surrounded by marigolds.\nThe bodies of the $FIRSTNAMEB2-imploded Venusians had been installed in the statues in the Hall of Offerings. Give or take a day here and there, the chronological age of those statues corresponded roughly to the chronological age of the $FIRSTNAMEB2-imploded Venusians themselves.\nThe $SURNAME0 estate, the $SURNAME0 estate people, the $SURNAME0 estate museum, the $SURNAME0 estate history, the $SURNAME0 family crypt, the $SURNAME0 family chrono-moon, the $SURNAME0 space suits, and now the $SURNAME0 Assault Brigade\u2014everything had been scattered to the winds by telephone-pole words from space.\nThe $SURNAME0 Estate, in ten minutes, was about to begin its own little war with Sun, Sirius, the Solar System, the Local Group, the Universe, and all.", "1_19_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-seven in the evening</span> when the ship felt floors beneath it and knew it was on Earth again. And then, at ten minutes after eight in the evening, Martian time, on October 12, 3012 A.D., the ship unfurled its sails\u2014and light, magnesium-white, cold, and emanating from an infinite multitude of suns, light such as no mortal had ever before seen in all the 176,000 centuries of man\u2019s life on Earth, light such as never had been seen since the universe had begun its expanding necklace of frigid worlds\u2014light such as this was ravished by the sails\u2019 countless hexagonal cells, set afire instantly by the radiation, and sent streaming out, back, the light foaming over the straining spars, and downward into space, the light rushing gloriously in effortless parallel billows a full 2,000 miles behind the speeding ship.\nThere was no sound.\nThe lights on Earth went out as the ship passed by.\noz. groaned and opened his eyes. \"Wha,\" he said, \"wha?Doc?\"\nHe was in a dark room with a low ceiling. One wall of it was cold stone. He lay on a dirt floor. His body was...", "1_5_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-three in the morning</span>. Salo Nagan was, as it happened, almost finished with his shift as night watchman of the Tralfamadorian solar-power station. He was about to be relieved by $FIRSTNAMEF0 K naval, who had recently become a half-cousin by marriage to Salo and a full-time night watch-woman herself.\nIt was into $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s cubicle that the time traveler from the year 236,000 hurtled.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 found $FIRSTNAMEF0-ski unconscious on the floor of her cubicle, but the little console still had power. The last message on the panel still glowed invitingly:\nHAVE A NICE LIFE.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 consulted his wrist compass. Even with the power off, the little compass still pointed true north. $FIRSTNAMEB0 planted it on the floor of the cubicle, aimed it at the darkest spot in the room. Then he brought out his machine gun\u2014and prepared to resist the attacking horde the compass showed waiting just outside. $FIRSTNAMEB0 wasn\u2019t interested in the message in the little console.\nHe wasn\u2019t even curious about it. He supposed it was another message by his pal $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014the only friend he had. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was only one man,...", "2_2_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nPrivate Weber, the oldest at twenty, felt uneasy for some reason. \"Can you fellows imagine how little marijuana would look?\" he asked. \"Is it like a shower of rain?\"\nPrivate Fallesthesia giggled.\n\"Wait till you really get to like marijuana, fellows,\" said Distenfluh. \"You\u2019ll see.\"\n\"My grandfather told me once all about what he saw in France during the First World War,\" said Fallesthesia brightly. \"They came across some fellows selling white wine out of a goat cart. Isn\u2019t that cute as could be? White wine out of a goat cart.\"\n\"Old goat\u2014old goat wine,\" said Distenfluh dreamily.\n\"It must have been wonderful white wine,\" said Fallesthesia.", "1_17_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five nineteen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe time was nineteen hours, nineteen minutes, and twelve seconds east of the zero meridian.\nThe time was five thirty-one p.m. Eastern Standard Time.\nTelevision was meaningless in Stony Brook. Stupidity had never been so public.\nThere was another itch inside the helmet. Unold couldn\u2019t resist the temptation to rub the itch. His forearm moved against the inside of the gold helmet. The scraping noise was horrible.\nHis breathing could be heard again. As the show went on, Unold breathed heavier and heavier. He prepared for another of the hideous coughing spells.\nThe coughing began.\nHis suffering became unbearable. He braced himself to die.\nThe coughing spells went on for twenty-two minutes and thirty-five seconds, with only three short lulls. Some of the coughs were the dry, empty coughs of a man who has been choked by his own hand, has bronchial pneumonia, and is about to die. The audience became irrationally upset.\nAt the end of twenty-two minutes and thirty-five seconds, Unold fell sideways off his stool and lay with his throat far above the surface of the muck, his eyes closed.\nBud and...", "1_6_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span> when I parked the car in front of the house in Vegas. I sopped the headlights and sat with the motor idling and watched Terry sleeping. He didn't look like he could go much further. The veins in his forehead were tremendous; his neck was swollen as big around as my thigh. My arms were sore from his stranglehold. But I knew if I let him go he would run for the nearest cop, so I held him tight and drove with my legs on the gas pedal and shifted the gears. I drove with one bare foot and one with a shoe. Two blocks from Van Nuys Boulevard I vaguely saw two cops in a cruiser parked at the curb; but they didn't know it was me and I kept going. I could see Terry's heart beating in his neck. Blood dripped on my wrist from the sweat rings he was making in the plastic windowframe with his wet head. Finally he swallowed a bit of spit and moved his lips.\n\u201cWhat is this? Are we lost?\u201d\nI giggled. \u201cNo, we're home, dear.\u201d\n\u201cWhere's my kid? Where's my dear child?\u201d\n\u201cSleeping like a baby,\u201d I said...", "2_14_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-five in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Chief Inspector of State Medicine decided in a moment that the body should be kept under wraps until the trial of the revolutionist New Bedlam could be made to dovetail neatly into a state funeral.\nThis double event, he reasoned, would so impress the masses with the unlimited power, wisdom, and generosity of their monarch that all rebellion would cease instantly.\nThe Chief Inspector decided that the body should be carried to a suite in the palace, where it might lie in state while plans were made.\nThe suite, it seemed, would be ideal. The Chief Inspector was informed, then, that the suite had already been taken by an old, white-haired man, who described himself as a nut and who asked for no privileges whatsoever.\nHe had paid in advance for one hundred and fifty-two years.\nThe Chief Inspector was informed that the nut was an old man who claimed to be 314 years old.\nThe man was working steadily and with astonishing skill on a manuscript, of which the first section lay on the desk before him.", "1_7_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-five in the morning</span>. I'd felt like hell when I woke up, and I'd had this urge to bag everything, to run down to Rio with anybody who'd make the trip. And now that it was all over\u2014the bitch, the ransom, the bastard cops\u2014I suddenly realized I'd won. I rang for the porter and ordered two club sandwiches and a half pint of vodka. For a minute before the alcohol arrived I was actually on the point of calling Flashgirl; I had the number in my head. She'd be home by now. She could tell me what was happening in Boston. And then I remembered\u2014sonovabitch\u2014that I didn't give a fuck. All I wanted was to lay low in New Orleans and think about the road. I was feeling very strange, remembering the horrors of the past three days...guilt, rotten luck and fear, mixed with these amazing spurts of exhilaration and steely calm. I obviously couldn't give a fuck about the Dead Tats; the only ones in trouble now were Terry and Nate\u2014poor little doper bastards\u2014and I wasn't responsible for them. Not unless the cops had the car. If they had the car, I was finished.\nI hadn't seen the car since...", "2_1_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-seven in the morning</span>.\nThe time was three minutes to three in the morning. The last firework of the display was shot into the sky just three minutes before the turning of Earth to bring Newport, Rhode Island, over the longitude of Bleakhill Common. The firework was timed to go off as Earth turned. It was an elaborate fireworks cartridge that had been lying around in $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s boathouse for years.\nThe cartridge was a special one containing a peony that Guy Goetting had bought for his mother\u2019s birthday. It was to have been fired off on Mother\u2019s Day. Known as a peony from the Sun and Moon Also, it was the last firework of the annual Guy Goetting vs the Universe display.\nThe peony in the cartridges was as wide as two fountain pools, and as tall as a house. When the Sun and Moon Also had gone off under it, it was supposed to open into something resembling a rose made of a hundred thousand carnations.\nBut, when the cartridge was fired by an underhanded youth at the back of a taxicab in Lewiston, Maine, nothing resembling a rose was the least of the things it became. The rose would have collapsed under a sneeze.", "1_6_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-two in the morning</span>. I had junk-sick dreams all day in the flea-bag hotel in Long Beach. I dreamed of dirty toilets, parking problems and D.M.V.*' license plates on every other car in a crowded street. The dream ended when I saw a cabdriver put a dog out of a car window and saw the dog run under a truck trailer beside the highway. There it lay, squirming, while the cab went on. When the dream ended I woke up covered with perspiration and realized the morbid significance of the recurring dog image in the dream\u2014my dog Remi was gone* I had already tried to call home three times that morning and nothing happened. I kept calling; it kept not happening. I got back into bed and tried to keep warm under the bedclothes with no avail. It was hot underneath. I took the covers away to find out what the trouble was. The radiator had burst and water was pouring through the ceiling and walls, spattering on the floor. I called the desk to complain. The desk clerk was afraid to come up because he was soaked to the skin from trying to fix the leak himself. In the end we agreed that he would bring me some dry blankets and I would keep him posted on the flooding...", "2_16_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-four in the afternoon</span>. They went on through the heat shimmer to the edge of the canal. The commercial recreation zone ended here. They were now on the edge of the Arizona desert.\nThe water was full of eyes. Aliens bobbed up and looked at them incuriously, buried again in a jiffy. \u201cWe should have come at night,\u201d said Metzger. \u201cThere are more then.\u201d\n\u201cWhat are they?\u201d asked $FIRSTNAMEF2.\n\u201cThe standard image in the taxonomic manuals, so help me God,\u201d Metzger said, \u201cis of a vertebrate, endoskeletal chordate, ocular, articulate, idiot. But some of them get pretty strange.\"\n\u201cWhat do they do?\u201d", "0_14_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two sixteen in the afternoon</span> when we rolled into the town. I knew $FIRSTNAMEB3 would be rolling in shortly . I took him out for a beer and a shot. A jazz band was playing faintly in the bar and the management reminded my buddy that he was once offered a job in their group when \u201cEast of Denver\u201d was in town.\nWe didn't tell them about our buddy in the Rawlins guardhouse. All across the nation $FIRSTNAMEB3 was in more jails than anyone in the history of America. It was a kind of prank with him. \u201cThere's a kind of a guy will go to any extreme at all,\u201d said my buddy. \u201cHe's up to his ass in alligators and he goes right ahead with his bunny-rabbit rigmaroles and you can't talk to the fool, he don't say a thing, he doesn't hear you and there's no way to reach him -- all you can do is stand by and watch him go~on and the hell with it, the boy's crazy in the exciting way and so unconsciously imbued with love, regard and tender understanding for every living and unlived moment, every person and thing, oh well, there's not a damn thing to do about it, nobody's that way any more...", "2_7_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past seven o'clock in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nThe Spacecraft Titan lay inert on the ground outside $FIRSTNAMEM1-Salem, North Carolina.\n\"You should always try something new,\" said $SURNAME0 to Salo. \"Otherwise, you might die without ever having done a thing you didn\u2019t already know how to do.\"\n\"Good-bye,\" said Salo.\n\"Good-bye,\" said $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0, together.\n\"Good-bye,\" whispered $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nSalo, the last Martian to go, boarded the space ship and closed the hatch behind him. There was no sense of motion when the Spacecraft Titan finally lifted. It only seemed that the air crackled and glittered where the burned-out factory had been moments before.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 watched the ship go.\nWhen the ship was low over the treetops, $FIRSTNAMEB0 found himself in its exhaust-streaked wake. $FIRSTNAMEB0 poked $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"$FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014look!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was looking straight at the exhaust, his head was cocked sagaciously. \"Yeah?\" he said.\n\"God be with you, Salo!\" shouted $FIRSTNAMEB0 to the ship.", "2_12_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, post meridiem. The place was the head of Newport, where Newport met the sea, post meridiem. The place was very beautiful and goldenly lit by the far sun, post meridiem.\nThe place was beautiful and goldenly lit by the sun because the place was well up in the far northwestern corner of the country, post meridiem.\nOld Stone Whitemarsh, who was almost eighty-one years old, stood on the edge of the cliff overlooking the sea. Old Stone Whitemarsh stood on the edge of the cliff in front of the largest of the houses near the head of the town. Old Stone Whitemarsh was wearing a costume that consisted of nothing but a big rubber goose, a fake white beard and a red rubber nose, post meridiem.\nNothing was said.\nOld Stone Whitemarsh stood on the edge of the cliff.\nThe crowd of masked, costumed, masqueraders stood around Old Stone Whitemarsh. They stared at him and things behind his back, post meridiem. They edged away from the cliff whenever they could.\nNot a word was said.", "0_2_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>. I was having trouble with my car, a white 1942 Plymouth four-door sedan, just off the lot. I was used to my old car, a 1934 Plymouth coupe, which was blue. This was my third car in a row with a crankshaft problem, and I should have suspected something, as they were all new Plymouths. It wouldn't have mattered with the old coupe, but the new car didn't have a dipstick to check the oil, and I got stranded. The time was three minutes to three o'clock in the morning. I was on the outskirts of Youngstown, Ohio. I got out of the car and cursed it. There I was, in a strange place, with a stalled car. I had a gallon of water in the trunk to put in the gas tank if the gas got exhausted and that was all I had. I walked down the road and passed some Youngstown hipsters and a Ray Detter Funhouse rolled by on the soft ugliest tires in the world, cut off and loaded to the gunwales with Youngstown nuttiemuggers. On the back bumper was a sign,COME ON IN, THE RACES ARE ON. The whole caboodle roared off,...", "0_10_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-nine in the morning</span>; the sky was overcast, very white and milky, giving the sun a weak, washed-out look. There were two police cars drawn up in front of the burnt building; a fire department inspection truck had been parked at the curb. Crowds always roosted here when anything happened, to hear the story, to join in the excitement and outrage, to pass the event on to their grandchildren; they masked their prurient interest with the serious faces of fear. The hippies stood solemnly against the paddy wagons like thiefs and whores, their battered flowerpots and deflated balloons gripped in their arms. Their peace was destroyed, their sanctuary violated and made deathly by fire. Smoke was still wavering up from the ripped roof. The owner, at his downstairs sales booth, smiled as he talked to a newsman.\n\u201cYeah, I was sweeping out and I seen this fire kind of smolderin\u2019 away and I thought, my God, that place is up again. Suppose I should phone the po\u2019lice? I went and took a look in, but the whole place was filled with so much smoke I couldn\u2019t see nothin\u2019. I says, no sense gettin...", "0_0_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty ante meridiem</span> when I got to the office next morning. Wolfe was already at his desk, reading a bill from a lumber concern\u2014hickory and sycamore he wanted to use to fumigate his orchids. He grunted at me.\nI got out his morning mail and he began on it, giving each one his undivided attention. From the way he was squinting and wrinkling up his forehead it was pretty clear that he had had no sleep. I closed the office door, never an easy trick when I had anything to carry in or carry out, and went to my desk to see if there was anything urgent. There was three inches of mail, which I tossed to one side. I wrote a card to the gardener at my place on Long Island, suggesting that all the yellow tulips be dug up and burned as deathsheads. I took a bill to the kitchen, borrowed from the refrigerator two eggs, boiled and sliced, and put them on Wolfe\u2019s plate. With a pot of hot coffee, the best brand of beer from a bottle in the Fritz tank, the morning mail, and the morning paper, things were looking up. As I entered the office with his tray, I addressed him:\n\u201cWell...", "2_11_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-three in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947, when Mrs. Turkel arrived at the foot of the staircase. She addressed $SURNAME0 telepathically, as he and $FIRSTNAMEB4 stood at its head. \"You promised not to leave without taking me, Mr.\" she said. \"Why did you, then?\"\n\"I can\u2019t explain everything, Mrs. Turkel,\" said $SURNAME0, \"and I wouldn\u2019t try to explain this even if I could. It would embarrass us both. Let\u2019s just say that an old misanthrope caught a glimpse of humanity for a few minutes, and acted on it as best he could. Go up the stairs, Mrs. Turkel\u2014go up the stairs with your old friend, $FIRSTNAMEM2.\"\nMrs. Turkel went up the stairs.\nAt eleven thirty-four, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and his mate and their son and the forty-seven members of the crew of the spacecraft known as the SPACE TRIUMPH passed through the gilded doorway at the top of the spiral staircase, into the blue opening of the great hall that led to the surface.", "1_22_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past ten o'clock in the evening</span>. We were, according to the ship's papers, about five miles off shore in the South China Sea. The collision Boom took place eight miles off the coast of Kilanga. I never saw the final result of the battle between the Congolese Army and that part of the People's Army known as the Kilanga Footsoldiers. No one in Kilanga ever talked about it. When the fighting was over, I understood why. There were around eighty dead left behind when the Footsoldiers made their big, flanking north-by-northwest-from-Kilanga swing out of the forest and overran the place. Such things were not unusual in the civil war, and nobody at all, north or south, ever made any comment about how it came out.\nThe Congolese national flag over the county seat was no longer ragged and bullet-pocked, either. They had patched it.\nS Allied troops were not allowed to land in North Vietnam, by the terms and conditions of the Paris Peace. Therefore, we were operating illegally in South Vietnam. Therefore, we all stole and sold each other's equipment whenever we could. Mine had been stolen from a dead GI inside a dead Huey the day after I had arrived in-...", "1_11_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-six in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, having waited until all was quiet, was now heading to the lavatory.\nCraning his neck, he stared up at the palace, trying to locate Space Wanderer\u2019s apartment. \"Farewell, Universe, and Hello to You, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1,\" he whispered to himself. He was high enough now so that he could see windows in the palace walls. \"Hello to You, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" he said.\nThe bathroom of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s apartment was panelled with panels of genuine gold-pressed latinum. The towels were made of the finest gold-flecked latinum mohair. The taps and fittings\u2014as well as the pipe to the waste unit\u2014were made of solid, arts-carved latinum.\nThe flat, bowllike waste unit itself was made of the finest, most homogeneous ilium.\nThe palace decoration and maintenance staff could only gaze with wonder at the bathroom fittings in $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s apartment. There was no abbreviation for ilium the unit in their manual; the unit wasn\u2019t listed in the appendix. The fittings were reputedly worth more than all the real estate...", "2_6_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-one in the morning</span> at Schiphol Airport in Amsterdam, Holland.\nThis part of the field lay in shadow. The sun had not yet got up enough gumption to shoot its rays in that part of the field.\nA double line of troops stretched in the shadows. The troops made a lane, and a phantom Hitler stepped out of a huddled group at one end of the lane, walked down the lane to a waiting aircraft.\nHe boarded the aircraft, and it took off, flew off into the sunrise.\nThere had once been real General image of so that the crowds at the airport\u2014a big crowd, jolly crowd\u2014could applaud and cheer for real.\nBut Hitler had shot himself in the bunker in Berlin. The crowds at Schiphol had now been applauding phantom Hitler for three long months.\nThe crowds now had a fine big bonfire going. They fed the bonfire with the printed texts of a thousand ghost- edicts the phantom Hitler had decreed in the past. They had already burned the edicts themselves in their stoves and fireplaces for heat.\nThe bonfire was big, and the crowd was happy.", "1_14_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>, according to a flashing prompter on a passing bulletin board in the corridor, when something went horribly wrong with a housing project in Peru, Indiana.\nBack in the lobby of the Space Age Hilton, a crowd had gathered to watch a television set mounted in the middle of the ceiling.\nOn the screen was the face of a man who appeared to be in his late thirties. His face was red and perspiring, his hair that same color on a man who would turn fifty-one this year. His suit was loud.\nSecurity guards and guests watched the face.\nAt first, the man was only verbally violent. He was standing in front of a bulldozer, on the site of a razed housing project. The bulldozer was apparently in defiance of his orders.\nHe screamed at the bulldozer. \"You hear me? You hear me up there? You yellow sheet metal bastard! You hear me?\" The veins on his forehead were pulsing. A fly began to circle around his nose, drunk on the heat.\nThe bulldozer began to move, but the vocalization of the command was followed by a stream of abuse that sent the operator scurrying for safety behind the steel housing of his machine.\nTwo big men with...", "1_20_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe room was beautiful and strange.\nThe room was as lovely and strange as a room in a dream.\nThe room was as lovely and as dreamy as a room that has been designed by an understood and well\u2014appreciated architect....\nAnd the furniture in the beautiful room was just as lovely and just as dreamy as the room itself\u2014and as lovely and as dreamy as the house in which the room was.\nThe furniture in the room was of unearthly whiteness, and was wrought of platinum, and was wrought of mooncalves' ivory, and was wrought of the powdered flint of meteorites, and was wrought of rubble from the ruins of Troy, and was wrought of the bones of nightmare birds. Of the nightmare birds from Mercury, whose feathers are like the woven webs of spiders, woven of moonlight and metephorical gum.\nThere was a bed in the room.\nAnd on the bed lay a man and a woman.\nThe man on the bed was $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe woman on the bed was $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 S Antarctic was the woman\u2019s name.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEF1, and the bungalow in which they were, were three things that were all harmon...", "2_21_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was well off the western end of the island of Cyprus. There was a field with aluminum gizmos sticking out of it. There was a hangar made of aluminum gizmos. There was a dilapidated sentry booth, manless. The sun was going. The wind was very high.\nA shimmering goat-woman in pink mesh stepped out of the pulsing membrane of a whorl of light hanging in the western sky, and walked slowly across the field to the hangar. She entered, by stepping through a gigantic plastic flake etched into the door. And from the other side of the hangar\u2019s plastic wall there came now Lenore Beadsman, still in forest-green corduroy, coming to meet her Gramma, our Lenore, coming through the same plastic wall, while outside Syzygy Dynamo, half in shadow and half in sun, watched by the hangar, eating a candy bar, in the wind.", "1_11_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-six in the morning</span> before I could bribe the desk clerk with a $20 bill to accept the gold coins I had left after my last stay. I was ready to run the tournam ent.\nBut first I sat in the lunchroom where I had eaten with Dickey Crane\u2014just about eight hours earlier\u2014and I wolfed down three hot veal cutlets covered with cheese and eggs. I didn\u2019t bother with the hash house potatoes: three cutlets was more than enough. I washed it down with three cups of bitter coffee and then went to the phone booth and wiped the steam off the mirror, trying to steady my nerves for what lay ahead, realizing that the cutlets and coffee might well play hell with the drugs and alcohol in the bloodstream.\nThe enormity of what I was about to attempt began to overwhelm me. I wanted to run out of that drugstore, find the nearest phone booth, call Virginia Hamish and tell her where I could be found if anything urgent came up\u2014and then check myself into the local hospital, where they could keep me drugged and apart.\nWhat was I doing in Las Vegas? What the hell kind of madness had overtaken me? The One\u2014Day Tourna ment was a monstrous intrusion\u2014the product...", "1_1_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one sixteen in the morning</span>.\nShe sent me on an errand after that. I scurried. I bought milk for her that night.\nIt was part of her routine. One of the last things she did before she bundled up with rolled coats and innumerable scarves, before she stood on the side walk waiting for the Metro bus to San Cristobal de las Casas, was to pour skimmed milk into a Le Creuset casserole on the stove, set it aside to simmer overnight, then skim the rich cream off the next morning and whip it with a little sugar and nutmeg for breakfast.\nShe was from Ohio. We had met at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway in 1946 during the wheeling and dealing that preceded the Democratic Party convention. She was thirty-four years old, five feet three, pretty, with extraordinary eyes. I pretended to myself that she was thirty at the time. I suspect she may have been as young as twenty-five. But her eyes were as ageless as my own.\nTears? it may be asked. Yes, I think so. Her sadness came from deep within. It was not any superficial matter.\nShe lolled in the hammock in the courtyard of our hotel. The hammock was hung between two...", "0_10_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-two in the morning</span> of Tuesday, June 21, 1994, and the true dark side of the American dream was about to be exposed. As the sun broke through the morning drizzle outside the lobby of the Hotel Stamp, it outlined impressively ominous features of the building itself. The dense, cubistic stamped pattern discharging the glare of the beam contrasted brightly with the rusted corrugations of the perilous, bulging slate roof. The same eerie light outlined what appeared to be a deep crevasse in the flashbulb\u2014mottled facade, and drew a correspondingly ominous line of shadow far out into the parking lot. There the shadows of some parked cars were so sharply drawn as to appear structurally adequate. But the cars themselves, their pediments and doors shiny, their paint flaking, their plastic windowshades hollow, their insides rotten, their tires slimy and soft, looked like relics from a junkyard that might at any moment slide into the sheer canyon of shadow created by the hideous fa\u00e7ade of the Hotel Stamp.\nWomen and men in group rates swimsuits and pajamas, most of them communing lugubriously with cigarettes, were getting into the pool on the glass-door side of the lobby, but Lenore preferred to...", "1_15_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. The place was a parking lot just off the north perimeter of the Los Angeles International Airport. The place was not recommended for parking, but our man was in a hurry. He had flown in from San Francisco that morning, and his passengers were waiting.\nWe arrived in his parking place twenty-four minutes late. We rolled up over the rise of the gravel in time to see our man bombing across the tarmac in a snappy trot...running for the jetway at the tail of a sprinting cluster of about a hundred priests. It was St. Joseph\u2019s Seminary Orientation...a trip to Los Angeles that promised \u201cfun\u201d with Tommy Davis, the rodeo cowboy. The priests, more than thirty of them, had been lined up at the terminal gate and dragged there at gunpoint from drink and crapshoots ten minutes before their Scheduled Departure Time, but now a complicated mixup with boarding passes and someone\u2019s suspicion of rabies shots put them far back in the departure line. Our man was trying hard to catch the party at the head of the line, and the look on his face showed that he thought he could make it if he tried. He had long brown shanks and sinew...", "1_15_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME0 and his dog faded, materialized, faded, materialized. The skins of their feet were livid with stage blood, which was draining from their bodies into the fountain\u2019s basin.\nThe blood from their ears had arrested the Carroll touch on the great bells. $SURNAME0\u2019s watch had stopped again. It now read three minutes past three o\u2019clock in the afternoon. The frame of the watch was winking gold-green again.\nMiss Chron keep time.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog lay twitching in the basin. The dog was trained never to put foot in the fountain. It was wondering what it was doing in the fountain, now.\nThe fine white mist in the great gallery was turning to rainbow stripes, suggesting the beating of the approaches to life on other planets. Nestor commented that colored mist was a good idea\u2014that it was worth trying. He wanted to get in touch with Nature, too, now.\nThe rhythmic crashing in the sky outside was becoming as terrifying as the Atlantic under a full-powered hurricane boat.\nNip and tuck.\nA wind as strong as a gale from the mouth of a cannon began to blow through the great gallery. The wind...", "2_19_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven nineteen in the evening</span>, Earth time. The place was the shores of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Sea. The medium was rain, mist, the smell of the sea, and the wind from the south.\nThe active one was $FIRSTNAMEB2, the watch-corgi.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had never been happier. There were birds to chase on the perimeter of the field. New smells came in with the rain every few seconds.\nIt was seven nineteen in the evening, Earth time.\n\"Skip?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 quietly. Her hands tightened on the stretcher- handles. \"Skip\u2014?\"\n\"Yes\u2014?\" said $SURNAME0. His voice was choked by his dream.\n\"We\u2019re moving,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Moving?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"The ship\u2014it\u2019s sliding across the grass,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"It\u2019s moving, Skip.\"\n$SURNAME0 let his head roll back and stared at the tufted, soggy ceiling of the gazebo.\nHe closed his eyes tight. His heart missed a beat.\n\"Good Lord\u2014what\u2019s going on?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Is anybody pushing?\"", "0_23_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-one in the evening</span>. But only, as it turned out, because midnight was coming closer the larger the lethal-income-for-life annuity grew. An annuity that would be worth millions upon millions of dollars. And the minutes coiled on, brimming with money. And then at last, in the windings of the watch, there was a whirring and a clicking, and an electronic voice bawled out exultantly.\nOver the top came the small plastic men, and whacked conclusively on the bottom. And Phluck drew and won a significant sum of pretend money.\nAnd an hour and five minutes later, when a really terrible thing happened, Phluck was still absorbed in what Lenore had noticed at the beginning to be a very complicated game.\nUntil well after midnight, in other words.\nThe really terrible thing that happened was that the drink cart decided to roll up to Cat\u2019s place to ask why there was no response over the speaker-system. Cat was gone, though. Mrs. [Page 201] Trucks was given the same story that had fooled Lenore. But Obstat would not open the door after Mrs. Trucks had banged for quite some time. Finally the police were called. And when...", "0_3_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThey knew now that the Space Wanderer was really in proper touch with his Higher Self. He was no longer in danger of dying as a result of something inside himself that might as well have been death as long as it lingered.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 sat on a couch thousands of miles long and near her, snoring gloriously, lay the Space Wanderer.\nHis suit was off.\nHis medal was missing.\nThe crude bandage $FIRSTNAMEF1 had put on God's finger was there, since it had evidently been spirited away from the Spazier and Biffington laboratories.\nThe box of macadamias and the coconut were there. So was the Space Wanderer's harmonica, his space watch, his space flask, his space purse, his space shoes, his space wallet, his space mace. And, of course, there was $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, who had sheltered the Space Wanderer from all harm since the day, on $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s space yacht, when the sensitive automaton had first realized what harm might mean. $FIRSTNAMEB1, who had touched his human friend in moments of deep physical and psychic need, and who had always done the right thing in such moments\u2014touched him in some way...", "1_0_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-six ante meridiem</span>. The Salmo cubans were now off duty, but their supervisor, Isabel, was on. She wore a soiled ecru gown which had once been of good crepe. It was slashed down one side and had been resewn with a brown thread so that the slash now appeared to be a sleeve.\nStrips of white adhesive tape were plastered all over the gown to bring it up to the demands of decency. Were it not for the tape, and for Isabel\u2019s manner of holding it closed, the gown would have exposed everything from Isabel\u2019s throat to her knees. She had what psychiatrists call a mother fixation.\n\"Holy cow!\" cried $SURNAME1, \"it\u2019s my supervisor!\"\nSchlarbaum was having trouble with an olive. \"Is that cheating?\" he asked. \"Is that cheating, or is that trying to help somebody? Why don\u2019t we call her over?\"\n\"I\u2019ll say hello to her,\" said $SURNAME1. \"This is goniffs, all right.\"\n\"Try the old hello!\" said Schlarbaum, and then he mused, \"Yeah, that\u2019s a good tipper, I\u2019ll say that.\"\n\"Just my supervisor,\"...", "0_6_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a ledge, three feet wide and a foot and a half thick, jutting from the rounded wall of a building that was itself a jutting part of the monolithic and unmanageable mass ofBronnquist Hall, the dormitory for male students at the Ohio State University. On the ledge sat young $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, aged twenty-four.\nThe ledge was ten stories above the campus.\nIt was dusking.\nThe door of the left wing of the duplex of the apartment house went quietly on its teetery gas springs. There was the tip-tip of high heels on a hardwood floor, dying away in lonely counterpoint to the following rapturous voice.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM1! Where are you, darling?\"\nThe voice was $SURNAME1\u2019s.\nThe male boarding students of Bronnquist Hall referred to their building as the Zoo. The female students, domiciled in Mona Hall, two miles away, referred to theirs as the Ark. The male student of the Ark was known as an Ark-ie. The female student of the Zoo was known as a Zoette. What was so coldly embarrassing to $SURNAME0 on the ledge was the fact that the...", "2_3_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty in the morning</span>. Ennet House Resident Counselor Francis Abington Twillinger lay snuggled in deepest sleep in the pitch-dark back bedroom of his rent-controlled Ennis House apartment. Over the house\u2014over Ennet House, Fenway Park, and the whole extent of Boston proper\u2014a polar aurora had been streaming its filaments since six o\u2019clock. There was a storm raging in the northern reaches of the Ohio Valley, in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, and in the humble municipality of Detroit. Excited meteorologists were predicting feet of new white powder for Ski Molasse and Powder Mill, delighting the seasonal Massachusetts ski-industry community and alarming the hell out of the skiers from Ohio and Michigan and Pennsylvania. A Massachusetts accident ward had seen several broken skis the night before. One broken skis had been serious.\nThe Lorton Correctional Institute for Women, five miles south of the District of Columbia in suburban D.C., seldom still at night, was quite still at the moment. All 2124 inmates were asleep, save four, all in the same guard-station end room, all hoping silently that no one would need anything.", "1_6_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-eight in the morning</span>. The place was Standard Lodge Room 233\u2014a single-occupant room on the twelfth floor of the Washington Dulles Airport Holiday Inn. The airport is ninetynine miles west of Philadelphia, the city where the story begins ...yes, begins, but certainly does not end. Philadelphia was just a way station on the road to an all\u2014American riot in the skies. It all started with a stupid, harmless series of larcenies. It escalated from there, with a cunning and horrible unpredictability...and on the morning it all happened\u2014the morning $FIRSTNAMEB3 Moriarty\u2019s mad road scent wafted into the Standard Lodge Room 233\u2014everybody, cops and robbers alike, had forgotten all about that strange summer day in 1947 when a skinny kid called $FIRSTNAMEB3 had strutted into the office of the juice man on Grape Street.\nA strange degeneration, because on that day in 1947 my friend $FIRSTNAMEB6 Paradise and I had been cocky and gorgeous...or at least as close to cocky and gorgeous as we would ever get. I was nineteen years old that summer, $FIRSTNAMEB6 was eighteen. The war was over. We were back from the wars and had no more cause to grieve. Hard-eyed, we looked ahead. Somewhere down those mean streets...", "0_9_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe ship would arrive at Braintree on Mars at twenty-nine minutes after six o'clock in the evening.\nThere was no telling what sights the wonderful televiewer would show on the way.\nThe clock on the wall said ten after nine.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were no longer under sedation. The pain from their $FIRSTNAMEB2-clustered implants had taught them the unimportance of sleep. They would go to sleep the instant their responsibilities for the safety and preservation of the species permitted them the luxury of leisure and thought.\nTheir responsibilities were heavy.\nAnd growing heavier every day, said a message from the harmoniums in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s and $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s skull . . .\nThe harmoniums were warning $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 that other commanders, who had not championed unpopular innovations such as space flight, might press their attempts to get the message from the harmoniums.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was asleep on his feet.\nThen, through the televiewer, came a sight that made $FIRSTNAMEB0 gasp and the spaceship lurch\u2014almost made $FIRSTNAMEB0 fall flat on his big-nosed face.\nThere, in the televiewer, was a herd of bison!", "0_16_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-three in the afternoon</span>, and the place was Room 223 of the Old Palace Hotel on the planet Tralfamadore. Room 223 was not a very large room at all.\nThere were two pieces of furniture in it. One was a Tralfamadorian harmonium, which was merely a gunnysack stuffed with dried Tralfamadorian bluesnpongers, the piping of which created the harmonium\u2019s diabolical noises. The other piece of furniture was the iron lung in which the man Alvin was being carried back toward Earth.\nThe floor and walls of Room 223 were bare blocks of concrete. The window was of bulletproof glass with an inch of air between it and the pane in which the words room 223 were painted. Outside, always hovering at a respectful distance from Room 223, as though it had a lengthy, very meaningful appointment with someone in there, was a blue police machine. It made hardly any noise at all, only a quiet hissing.\nThe door to Room 223 was made of the best pig iron that could be mined on the planet Tralfamadore. The door had eight large, bored holes in it.\nThe door opened in, and there were two wicked iron bolts as well. It was made certain that...", "0_23_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-three in the evening</span>.\n\"Gordo?\" I looked around again. The party was beginning to quiet down, as it were. It would never really get going, for one thing because it was only eleven thirty-three, but for another thing because most of the people were far too drunk to do much more than yell at each other in loud voices. But I left the patio and went into the big room beyond.\nDimly, far away, I thought I saw something under a piece of tarpaulin near the big fake fireplace\u2014the place where the unmade giant bed was. I was getting a little uncertain about the room, too.\nBut I found a stepladder, got it under the tarpaulin, and pulled it off.\nThere was a bed under the tarpaulin, all right. But it wasn\u2019t the unmade giant bed. It was somebody\u2019s bed.\n\"Gordo?\" I rubbed my eyes.\nThe somebody under the tarp was a little person in a blue sleeping bag, curled up and presumably asleep. I couldn\u2019t see who it was because it was wrapped up in the sleeping bag, but I knew right away it had to be somebody who loved Demmick...", "2_2_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"What did you do it for?\" said Captain $SURNAME2.\nSergeant Nolcott, who had a precise sense of military propriety, made no attempt to deny his crime. He had been caught sitting in his class-A uniform in a state of utter disrepair. He had no galoshes, and there was nothing around his neck, either.\n\"I\u2019m snowed under, Captain,\" said the sergeant. \"I\u2019m all snowed under.\"\n\"What did you do it for?\" said Captain $SURNAME2.\n\"Because I\u2019m snowed under,\" said the sergeant. \"I don\u2019t have time to keep up with a Space Wanderer\u2019s training schedule, too.\"\n\"Why didn\u2019t you ask for a lay-off?\" said Captain $SURNAME2. \"Why didn\u2019t you put in for a transfer to some other Space Corps outfit?\"\n\"How could I, Captain?\" said Sergeant Nolcott. \"My mother\u2019s dependent on me.\"\n\"The government would have taken care of her,\" said Captain $SURNAME2. \"There are government wards where old people with weak livers are taken care of.\"", "2_0_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was midnight</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB5 and I got out of the caf\u00e9. We walked away across the park.\n\u201cWhere\u2019ll I take you?\u201d\n\u201cOh damn, back to the wagon.\u201d\n\u201cCome along,\u201d Bill said.\nWe found the others and after a long ride through the empty early morning streets in a taxi we came to Bill\u2019s apartment.\n\u201cThis is me,\u201d Bill said. \u201cYou can\u2019t come up. I\u2019m going to bed and have the concierge telephone for a doctor. You all go on and see $FIRSTNAMEB5 home, Michael.\u201d\n\u201cHow about a coffee?\u201d\n\u201cI never drink coffee. Never sleep after it.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll see you at the races,\u201d Bill said.", "0_17_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span> of the first day of December, one thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine, Celsius scale. Chronologically, it was the second of December. Simultaneously with this entry, in the spaces of the Tralfamadorian heavens, the balance wheel of a watch, the wheel of a chronometer, and the wheel of the great machinery of life on Earth passed the equator. The watch ticked; the chronometer marked the same instant; the machinery coughed and slowed. In a louder and louder screech the tall ratchet wheel of the Earth as it slipped by, a wheel of three billion cog-tooth spikes, slowed, paused. As the wheel paused at UTC plus 12, the last spike slipped from the rim of the wheel. The Earth gave a shudder, a gasp. Everywhere and always, birds went to roost\u2014that is, died. Machines stopped, stopped cold and quiet, everywhere and always. Those machines that had driven the machines stopped. Everywhere and always. The corpses of birds, of wild and domesticated animals, of bees, ladybugs, and little whirring Canary Island lizards everywhere and always. Everywhere and always, the last fires went to sleep. Everywhere and always, mankind went to bed. Man went...", "1_2_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-five in the morning</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I were still driving across the American River bridges in Sacramento; when we got to the bottom of the second bridge a panhandler leaned over the rail, crossed his legs, and said, \u201cHey, buddy, can you give me --\u201d But we paid no attention. Up ahead, where the Sacramento River widens out and empties into the California Delta, $FIRSTNAMEB3, who hadn't stopped talking since Montana, suddenly became silent. \u201cLook, man,\u201d he said, \u201cover there! There it is! I've been telling you about it and here we are right on top of it and you got to see it and what I been telling you is so -- see?\u201d I rubbed my eyes and shuddered. I saw the river flat and vast like the delta of the Single Soul, lashed and writhing in the muddy spring current; I saw the railroads, barge lines, highways, the muddy and turbulent waters, and on the banks the steaming swampjumps beaded with fireflies and heaving up fogs of insect noise -- and everything suddenly made meaning, suddenly became Yes. \u201cWhere are we?\u201d I asked. \u201cWe're on our way.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3...", "2_22_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-one in the evening</span>.\nIt had been twelve o\u2019clock in the morning when $FIRSTNAMEB0 had gotten up to go out to the Atlantic front.\n\"What happened\u2014all that time?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"A lot of things,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"We had a ball.\"\n\"Ball?\"\n\"Sure,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"You oughta seen him pitch. You oughta seen him hit that golf ball.\"\n\"Whose head did I put my hand on, in the barrack?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"That was him,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"That was him hitting a golf ball.\"\n\"I sure felt foolish.\"\n\"That\u2019s what he likes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"You gotta keep hummin\u2019.\"\n\"I sure looked sick,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I felt sick.\"\n\"You gotta keep hummin\u2019,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"How long was I out there?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Two hours and seventeen minutes\u2014straight,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"I never seen nothing like it.\"\n\"Nobody that sick could do that,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"He must\u2019ve been awful angry with me.\"", "1_7_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-one in the morning</span>. I stood there facing the cold in my shirt sleeves and realized I had nothing to carry. My clothes and shoes and books, my razor and tooth-brush, everything was there in my bags back at the hotel. There was not even a soda glass in my bag. I had shoved everything in and dropped the bag by the door and come out into the other room to get an early start. Everything was there in my room except the carrier bag with forty-eight bottletasties. My stomach leapt. Forty-eight. Forty-eight cold bottletasties. Polished black bread sandwiches with coarse yellow mustard held together by chilled white-bread dough. Forty-eight chances to bite into big Pop Secrets popcorn balls that solidified in your mouth without any chewing at all. The Pop Secrets commercial said Pop Secrets popcorn was \u201ca frozen bit of movie heaven.\u201d\nAnd I could have them, all forty-eight of them, if I just went back to my room and got that bag I'd left by the door. The checkout desk was a block down the street. Probably still open, with the agent completing my bill and waiting for me to show up and settle. I could dash down and grab the bag, run back and show the agent...", "1_6_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-seven in the morning</span>. Remi Boncoeur, wearing a leather jacket, was seated at the wheel of a twelve-cylinder limousine that had been waiting, motor idling, facing east on the frame ferry from Stinson Beach.\nRemi, like the rest of the De Gama family, had made his home in San Francisco. He was a tall man of twenty-six, with a lean build, high cheekbones, and swarthy skin. His dark hair was wavy and glossy. His eyes were blue and merry.\nHe was grinning broadly, now, as $FIRSTNAMEB0 came staggering down the dock.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s back and face were red with blood.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s right arm was broken, and his right knee was twice its normal size.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s scalp sported a dense thatch of brown pine needles.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s trousers in front were black with fecal matter.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s iron-shod right shoe was iron-shod no more\u2014and the foot it had shod was a shapeless ugly ulcer.\nBearded and wild-eyed, in rags, foam-flecked, $FIRSTNAMEB0 looked like nothing so much as...", "0_18_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-three in the evening</span>. I was in Philadelphia. Most of my wife was in the trunk. We were on our way south, across Pennsylvania, toward Washington. I was dizzy and sweat-soaked. I stopped for gas and for whiskey and for water for my dog. The old guy who sold me the whiskey gave me an extra bottle for being a good customer already. \u201cHell, that stuff'll tear the wings off a fly,\u201d he said. I figured I needed it.\nI hardly noticed the mess in the trunk. I draped my jacket over it in back. My attorney had nothing to say. He was out of his mind on marijuana. I put on a tape of rock and roll and drove faster, using my headlights only faintly on the open road to protect my delicate marijuana watcher from the full trauma of the drive. His orgasm was coming. I could see it in his eyes. He was staring hard at the floor of the car. Every few minutes, he stretched his head up to see the road ahead. Then, a few minutes after that, he'd stretch his head up again. What he was doing was calling out the miles to himself backwards. He was almost there.\nI knew what it was, because I knew he'd done it...", "2_3_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span> in New Orleans.\nSo for this and for the sake of all that it might imply, let us here pause for a moment and examine the character of the witch Inman. Let us now look upon Inman in his full extent.", "2_0_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after midnight</span>.\n\u201cOh, about twenty minutes.\u201d\nI lay in bed and listened. I was wide awake. I felt fine. At twenty-one minutes after midnight I could hear $FIRSTNAMEB5 go out of the room. She said good-morning as she closed the door and then I heard her move about and put the coffee on in the kitchen. I got up and dressed and went down-stairs and ate breakfast. I did not feel in the least strange. It was in my mind that it would end that way. It was like starting off down an unknown road. I wanted to start. It was curious to feel all that, to feel it and know it and not be excited or upset about anything. I could have gone without breakfast, or started off to the other end of the world, just as well. $FIRSTNAMEB5 came in with the coffee.\n\u201cDid you have a good sleep?\u201d\n\u201cSplendid. I\u2019m glad I let the room.\u201d\n\u201cDon\u2019t talk like that. It\u2019s off. I feel fine. I never felt better in my life.\u201d", "0_13_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. I had twelve dollars left in the world. In a moment of sanity I called up my attorney in Beverly Hills, where I'd left my Cadillac. He raced down to Las Vegas in one of his client's Duesenbergs to pick me up. \u201cGod damn,\u201d he moaned. \u201cYou're a sick man. Can't you take pills like other people?\"\n\u201cSorry,\u201d I said. \u201cI threw them all away.\u201d\n\u201cWhy?\u201d\n\u201cI wanted to see if I could get well on the bus.\u201d I felt fine now, but the talk and the sight of him worried me. I knew that as soon as he took me back to the hotel I would start shaking again, and there was no telling what I would do. \u201cI want to go to Cuernavaca,\u201d I said. \u201cIt's only two hundred miles south of here.\"\n\u201cAnd do what?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cI want to live in a Temple of God. A church like the one where Lawrence gave away ALL THAT HE HAD.\u201d I couldn't explain; my words were incoherent. I was raving...", "2_22_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten nineteen in the evening</span> on Mars. The time would be ten twenty, ten twenty-one, ten twenty-two, ten twenty-three. The time would be ten twenty-four, ten twenty-five. The time would be ten twenty-six; would be ten twenty-seven; would be ten twenty-eight; would be ten twenty-nine. The time would be ten thirty in the evening on Mars. The time would be ten forty-five in the evening on Mars. The time would be eleven o\u2019clock in the morning on Mars.\nAnd then, years later, and light-years away, there would be tragedy again.\nAnd then, years later, and light-years away, there would be tragedy again.\nNow the history of mankind is laid out on a time map in squares and cubes, in cylindrical tunnels and wormholes and flat planes. And what it all comes to is this, as laid out one winter night:\nEvery man has his own enemy\u2014who sometimes occupies the next cubicle or hall or dorm or barber chair.\nEvery man has his own favorite zone of hell\u2014a zone that exists only in the terrible particularity of his own imagining.", "1_16_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. I was just beginning to wonder what had happened to my attorney when he suddenly blew through the door wearing nothing but a life preserver and a gas mask. All he could see, through the fogged plastic eyeslits of the mask, were four wild-eyed hun ger Artists. There was no doubt in his mind that he had just stormed the fortress of theterrorists. My secretary ran into the room for protection: I heard her lock herself in thefufolator. But the suite was already defladed to a point where the presence of anothertransparent per son barely even registered.\nThey found us later, after the Tactical Force had pumped enough nerve gas through the airconditioning system to make the whole floor unpinhabit able for at least two days. My attorney was wandering around the redeposited lobby in a daze, wearing only one shoe and one splash guard. His head was wrapped in towels, a result of his dive through the plexiglass door of the elevator, directly above the Hotel\u2019s own underground Morgue...And when my attorney staggered over to one of the pathetically listless employees and asked the time of day, the clerk\u2019s eyes unfocused and he mumbled, \u201cYou...", "1_23_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven eighteen in the evening</span>. The saloon in which young $SURNAME1 had indulged himself was really a tin shed in the back yards of Treader Street. There was a legend in the neighborhood that some years before, a pirate had buried a cardboard keg of real Kentucky sour mash in one of the back lots. It was one of the most exciting rumors on the Mars-town social circuit. Young $SURNAME1 was willing to grant the possibility, though he personally believed the loot to be nothing more romantic than a cask of Army-surplus fingerprinting ink.\nThe shed, the stock of whiskey, and the lights themselves were all owned by $SURNAME0, were all part of the subtle and ubiquitous infrastructure that supported the contrived society of young $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s relations.\nOld $FIRSTNAMEM1 himself was nowhere near Mojave Center on the evening of April 11, though his influence was everywhere. He was, after all, God. He existed everywhere, existed in a Hell of time, existed in all possible times.\nHis power over young $SURNAME1 was as strong as ever, would have been as strong in any place, in any time. Young $SURNAME1, however, sensed nothing beyond the tyranny of his own shaky state of mind.\nYoung Walter Reilly and young George Walt,...", "1_7_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 and his dog were the only living creatures on Mars.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 flew straight up in the sun and leveled off at a height of a thousand feet.\nTheir shadows fell behind them on the sand.\nThe thousand-foot level was one that $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had used many times before on Mars.\nThe sky above and the ground below were absolutely clear.\nThe Sun and the Earth hung together in the sky. The child of their union was the golden spotlight that passed between $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4, then to and fro over them again and again.\n$SURNAME0 sang lustily, horribly. \"Darling, darling\u2014sweetest one, most dere, Oh, you and me got a lovely, lovely t\u2019ing a-going,\" said he. \"Tell me more\u2014tell me more, tell me more,\" said $SURNAME0 to his dog, the empty air.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 yawned.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 lowered one leather wing so that it cupped the shaft of light like a trough. It was a signal\u2014a signal to $SURNAME0 that $FIRSTNAMEB4 did not like flying in the Sun.\nA ship\u2019s lookout, scanning the pink atmosphere...", "1_20_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nIt was silent out in space. The six battered ships from Mars were a thousand miles from the Earth. There was no noise or light from them. And there was no sign that their crews were aware of any small excitement going on in the village of Basements in Northern California.\nThere was none of the fidgeting and squirming that signal the imminence of action on the part of humans who have been cooped up together for a long time.\nThere was nothing to betray the fact that a decisive moment in human destiny was quietly approaching.\nThe six ships were manned by skeletons.\nThe bones in command of the skeleton crews were gaunt and grinning.\nThe grinning skull of an Earthling commander had radioed this information to the skeleton soldiers it commanded: \"In one more Earthling minute\u2014one more lousy, stinking Earthling minute\u2014we boo-ya-oo-oo-oozle them out of the poop-pocking universe. Weee-eee-eee-eee-ee-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha.\"\nThere was a fading chuckle from three skulls in three battered ships, a chuckle that said: \"Yeah, damn skippy, a good idea. And when it...", "0_4_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-four in the morning</span>. Western Union could not give us $FIRSTNAMEB3's location any more. \u201cHe's in Mexico,\u201d they said.\n\u201cWell, boys, we're closing down now,\u201d said the stake-out captain and pulled down the shades of his little cubicle. Everybody scattered for the morning; Stan and I were stranded, the car aching in the street with two flat tires, and nobody to change them. There was nothing to do but sleep on the office cots till the owner of the Texaco station next door came in at eight to open his shop. Meanwhile we shut ourselves in the offices and tried to figure out what to do next. At five in the morning I was standing on the street with a cup of coffee, watching for the dawn and hoping for another chance to see my pin-up in black tights when somebody banged on the door. I opened it and there was a sergeant of the state police. \u201cWhere's everybody?\u201d he asked. \u201cWe just left and haven't been back since.\u201d He told me officially the last car we'd been sitting in had stolen plates and that I was under arrest. He said he'd be back in a moment with his partner. I continued stalling...", "2_9_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. People were dropping bits of paper every few yards all over Central Park. Little boys, who had been told to go out and play with their horses, were riding the horses through the wet grass, whipping them. Little girls, who had been told to go out and play with their dolls, were pushing the dolls in baby buggies. Old ladies, who had been told to go out and sit in the park, were trying to knit and getting hopelessly tangled with their yarn.\nThe books of the story were in their proper places all over the park. It was all right; nothing of importance was missing. The greatest, toil-assembled collection of stories in the world was intact, irresponsible, and at large all over Central Park.\nEverybody, in short, was doing just what he or she had always done on a Saturday, except that it was raining, and dark.\nThere were two men under an inadequate and dripping tree on a Central Park bench.\nOne of the men was George M. Helm. The other man was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThey hadn\u2019t asked for rain, but they appeared not to notice it. The rain soaked and chilled their suits, but the rain didn\u2019t chill their spirits.", "1_11_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. Reno had no clocks or sun. The inhabitants, largely whores, pimps and racketeers, ordinarily woke up when the taverns opened and at two o'clock in the afternoon retired each to a separate, nicely appointed room in the numerous Hotels.\nRollo took a last look at his image, which had now become so much our image, in the mirror, and switched off the light. He picked up his mattress and rubber explosives and headed for the door, leaving his key and the cryptic note \u201cplease clean room,\u201d on the dresser.\nWe dialed the number of the Hotel Flamingo, our temporary home, and said to the desk clerk, \u201cAny messages for $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Houston, Texas?\u201d\n\u201cJust a moment, please,\u201d the clerk said, and riffled pages down the switchboard. Our heart sank when we heard him say, \u201cOh yes. He's right here, Mr. $SURNAME1. Would you care to go over this bill?\"\n\u201cNo,\u201d said $SURNAME1, \u201cthat won't be necessary.\u201d\n\u201cYou certainly racked up a bill. Cigarettes, liquor, fifteen phone calls and a hundred dollars...", "2_6_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-five in the morning</span>.\nGood News/$FIRSTNAMEM2\nThe giant who wept like a child was named not $FIRSTNAMEM2, but $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s respite came to an end. A hand touched him. $FIRSTNAMEM2 was behind him again. He pressed against $FIRSTNAMEB0. He was as cold and wet as the stone that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had embraced in his blind flight.\nWith the touch came understanding. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had rushed through hell in his head, and he now knew what hell truly was. It was the love of $FIRSTNAMEM2.\nAnd then $FIRSTNAMEB0 saw, by the light of the television-set suns, that his lover was twice as big as he had been. He now stood eight feet tall.\nGood News arrived with $FIRSTNAMEB1, as well, as Lazarus. As Good News took hold of $FIRSTNAMEB0, and as $FIRSTNAMEB1 took hold of $FIRSTNAMEM2, and as Lazarus held the rifle, and as the thousands of spectators pressed in, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEM2 made their love manifest in small tremors and quivers.\nTheir damp skin rippled.\nTheir great smooth muscles twitched.\nTheir eyes twinkled.", "0_2_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past two o'clock in the morning</span>. At ten minutes to two in the morning, the national anthem of Ingland had finished its long sad wail over the radio. Then a man's voice had said, \"And now\u2014\" and then the Radio Sufferers had started up again. They were all English; they were all sad. And, in between, at certain long but well-spaced intervals, listeners, suffering hideously, would reach up a long arm of hope till their heads were on a level with the wavelengths of the ether, would write out a cry in Morse code, would send it tingling and twanging off into space, and would write out another. Then up would come a reply\u2014but ten minutes or a quarter of an hour later\u2014tingle-twang!\u2014which would be some more appeals from more sufferers of Radio. One fellow was in an aircraft being flown from New York to Los Angeles. His plea for help came in Morse code. It said, \"I suffer more and more cruellest agony having to sit helpless in aircraft seat knowing that Am suffering hellish and endless hours of torture in ears due to Radio Sufferers League, Los Angeles, California.\" It was signed, \"F. P.Wilson.\" Another man was working day and...", "0_4_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-one in the morning</span>, April seventeenth. Mr. Beezeley, in the Cleveland department store, had thirty minutes more to go. He looked mournful. \"What a career I might have made in the Church!\" he cried. \"Who knows?\" Then he grinned slyly, proud of his guile. \"My father once threw a shoeshine stand in Madison Square Garden all afternoon for a watch, just a one-second Mickey Mouse watch,\" he said.\nHe inspected the time left, his brow horny.\nAn Angel buses tables in the coffee shop attached to the Cleveland Store. She is Geneveve Roll, and at this early hour of four thirty-one she was hurrying on her way to her bus at the Cleveland Park subway station. Her route was clear. She was far along.\nWhen she reached the front door of the coffee shop, Mr. Beezeley, eighty feet away, plainly saw her through his one-way mirror, coming down the incline of the floor, leaning against the turnstile. She let the turnstile roll her through and walked straight through the doors and into the street, toward the Cleveland Park station.\nWithout a twinge of conscience, Mr. Beezeley gave her a good tail.", "2_23_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until midnight</span>.\nJudging by the softness of the light and the darkness at the edges of her vision, the mother knew that this night, like most nights, was the last night of the world, the night before Judgment Day.\nThe blind spot grew and devoured more of $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s field of vision. It seemed to her that she was going blind. This thought filled her with rage.\nShe tried to see something, anything, and knew that she could not because the blind spot was cutting deeper and deeper into her consciousness. She knew now that the world had always been a blind spot, a nether region, a place where horrors were kept to name in the dark.\nRefusing to be terrified by her blindness, $FIRSTNAMEF0 P hemsley stuck out her tongue and used its tip to scratch a lower molar on the left side.\nHow long would it be, she wondered curiously, before people forgot how to see, and went walking into walls, and laughed at tripping over nothing?\nThe question seemed to come from somewhere outside of $FIRSTNAMEF0. Evidently she had made the question using her voice, for her son answered it. \"Before people learn how to see again?\"", "1_4_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span> at our new home in Peyton Place. Whenever a clock in the house fails to strike at the hour, it is always ten minutes past four. It is now three weeks since we arrived in the town. Ten days ago Papa had his first stroke.\nOf a certainty, outlanders can never understand. People in a town can never point out their own heroes, their leading citizens. In long ago, romantic bygone times, they could condemn a man by branding him on the forehead with a hangman's noose, or call attention to him by means of a pitchfork. The pitchfork still exists, but only in decorative forms such as wrought-iron porch posts or mantel ornaments. Virtually abolished are the noose, branding irons, and ropes.\nUndoubtedly the rumors abound, but so well hidden are the instruments that they can be found only by diligent search among the nettles and in secret places.\nPerhaps it is this very search that has spread the sinister smoke and speculations so thickly through the town. But we have never seen the flash and dart of search's tocsin through the law offices and church vestibules of Peyton Place. Perhaps, like scuttling ships at night, we only hear and see...", "0_0_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-two ante meridiem</span>, this first Friday in August, a month before school was to start, that one particular member of the cast, who happened to be the one, lucky member who had been taken seriously by a drama critic, who happened also to be the only one among all of them who was wearing a long white robe and carrying a metal harp, had just skipped across the stage, up through an opening in the rear and toward the right, his bare feet (Ransom remembered this, yes, extremely well, for reasons to come) trailing and sending up after them a sort of noisome puff of dust, which the sudden understage wind from his passing had secreted from the corner and in which, just beside the trailing feet, something had glittered, made a small red spark in the stage lights, dear God\u2014. Some broken glass, a joint maybe (Ransom\u2019s memory of the glitter gave him an image of blood-red hairs, hairs from a joint in the dust, hairs from some horrible little twisted leg with a scab on it, hairs like those on the two scraggly joints he had found in the ashtray of his father\u2019s car that time they had a flat on the Thruway ... ) evoked finally...", "1_10_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-seven in the morning</span>, and every man on the ship was drunk. This was astounding, since the Rum Runner\u2019s water supply, like that on all space ships, was limited. Nothing was procurable in space except ships\u2019 water and ships\u2019 whiskey. The only taste that ships\u2019 whiskey acquired in space was the taste of a burning nobility that singed the throat on its way down.\nThe most terrible punishment in the Space Service was banishment to the moon. Only two sentences were worse. One was banishment to Mercury. One was to be strapped into the pilot\u2019s seat of a Martian Lily Salami and ordered to head outward into the starless infinitely desolate dead sky of interstellar space\u2014and keep heading outward till the fuel ran out, or forever.\nEvery man knew that he would be killed if he drank all his whiskey at once. But every man knew, too, that within twenty-four hours all the water he could drink would be his.\nWho are these titanic madmen, who so calmly risk every future happiness for an everlasting hangover? Why, they are the sailors of the Space Navy, of course\u2014the magnificent Men of the Rum Line, the matchless, nerve and fist, the unconquerable fishes!\u2014", "1_12_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve seventeen post meridiem</span> of one day in the early entropic age. $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 stood at the focus of a coincidence that was to bring people from Mars, from Ganymede, from Titan, from Mercury, from Europa, and from Vulcan\u2014not to mention hordes of Earthlings\u2014to see her in the little West Virginian town of Chickasaw.\nThe scouts had come in using powered diggers, taking advantage of the fact that the old roads, not yet grass-grown, were still wide enough to accommodate them. The diggers\u2019 engines were burbling and whirring and whining, giving off blue whorls of camouflage smoke, in every street of the town.\nAn unacknowledged head of the Moon mission now stood, less than a mile from the $SURNAME0 estate, on the county fairgrounds. The camp was quiet, camouflaged beautifully.\nEvery member of the mission was in place. In half an hour, the show would open, with $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 as the star attraction.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 stood in the gazebo on the east side of her garden. She faced a jungle of six thousand flowers and plants. She was dressed in a lime-green suit. Her most notable feature was...", "2_5_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span> when the hookers started coming upstairs. I didn't know that, of course, so I was really surprised when the door swung open and a black girl about twenty years old stepped in. She had long legs with varicolored veins, long natural hair that fell to her shoulders, wearing nothing but her black and white miniskirt. I thought at first she was a hooker, then realized she was some kind of maid. Must have needed money for her kid's school lunch or some damn thing. I smiled at her. She stared into my face. I stared back. What the fuck, I thought. Here is something pretty close to the human norm, at least for this hotel.\nShe said, \u201cMan, have you been crying?\u201d Well, hell yes, I had been crying. I felt my face. It was wet. I started giggling. At that point something snapped in her brain. She started backing toward the door in hysterical flight and jerked it open and ran off down the corridor.", "2_20_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-two in the evening</span>.\nThe place was Chiang Mai, in northern Thailand.\nThe place was Vancouver, in the Dominion of Canada.\nThe place was Dharmsala, in northern India.\nThe place was Stockholm, in Sweden.\nThe place was Trondhjem, in northern Norway.\nThe place was Adger, Alabama, U.S.A.\nThe place was Boquillas, Texas, U.S.A.\nThe place was Milwaukee, Wisconsin, U.S.A.\nThe place was Bronson, Michigan, U.S.A.\nThe place was Cambridge, England.\nThe place was Battle Creek, Michigan, U.S.A.\nThe place was Dar es Salaam, in German East Africa.\nThere is no time, as we know it on Earth, in the deep Martian caves from which all Martians expected their millennium to emerge.", "2_17_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when she came to, staring at a lean, rugged, Donlevy-looking, middle-aged man in hornrims who\u2019d strapped himself into the seat beside her. She looked in panic for the seat-recliner control, found it, raised herself, looked for the brake-shoe, found it and stomped it into place with her heel.\n\"Good even\u2014\" he began. Maybe he meant to say afternoon. Maybe he hadn\u2019t been able to see her wake up\u2014she\u2019d kept her head down, eyes closed.\n\"This is your mother in San Francisco,\" a radio said at his side.\n\"What?\" she said.\nHe smiled an Oreamnos Askias smile, as of course he had to. \"You're bop, aren't you?\" he said. \"Raised on hip. Picked up the lingo in a flash. Don't mind me. Let me play. I mean, don't even try to understand. Just ram it home, out of your ears, via your aural nerve and into your groovy central processor.\" She was not a hallucinator; she was under some drug. Hallucination was her only out.", "0_12_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-three p.m.</span> A woman was approaching, but she was some distance away and not clearly in focus, and so Lenore did not see that it was Judith Prietht, carrying An Oral History of Mount Mansfield Aviation, and made of stone.\nJudith saw Lenore. She slipped the green door\u2019s bar with her thumb and floated in through the pneumatic whoosh, holding her book before her like a shield, though she knew it was useless.\n/a/\n\u201cBeads? I\u2019m sorry? Dental? Dental care? Dentistry beads?\u201d\n\u201cWell as far as I can see sir you only have one tooth in your mouth.\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s correct. Dentists have refused me. I\u2019ve been refused by dentists for more than thirty years. Dentistry will not have anything to do with me, in any form. Am I not right?\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cLike a mouthful of the Sahara Desert, am I not right?\u201d\n\u201cWell you certainly do have a large amount of gum, I\u2019ll say that, in comparison with most of our other customers.", "2_6_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME1 read the letter. He experienced the same sense of guilt and bad taste that a son would experience who had treated his father with disrespect.\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s letter to her husband:\nMY DEAR HUSBAND,\nThey say you are a prisoner of war. If a wave of your hand or a glimpse of you on your way to a latrine could help to shorten the war and bring you safely home, the government would ask you to wave a hand or show your face. But since these acts, without a word attached, would be of no use to the war effort, the government, with respect and affection, asks you not to.\nI am invited, along with our son, to visit a camp for civilian dependents of prisoners of war.\nI shall make no attempt to guess or to argue about the nature of the thoughts that might take you to Mars. I guess that there are many reasons for performing even an act that hurts as much as I have been hurt by your act. I guess that one of the reasons might be an unhappy human mating ritual. I shudder to think of it.", "2_0_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-one ante meridiem</span>. The place was Beverly Hills. The California Highway Patrol, for some strange reason, had taken up positions at all the freeway entrances\u2014almost as if they had expected me to show up here. Yes\u2014just as if they had known, all along, that some terminal twist of Living Hell was going to occur in the fat, white flesh of Beverly Hills. Shaking my head for a hard count of ten to make sure I was awake, I grabbed the fat, drunken culprit by an arm and the loose seat of his pants and hauled him off to my car, half-pushing him and dragging him through the dirt, barefoot, and across the concrete, so that his feet left two little gray lines of abraded skin behind him. The look on his face made me sick: as he stumbled along with his head hanging down and his arms dangling helplessly\u2014his whole body seemed to be posturing an apology to the world. \u201cLook what you made me do,\u201d his attitude said. \u201cCan\u2019t you stop me?\u201d", "0_19_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>. The $FIRSTNAMEB2 firm had been approached by only ten couples in three years. In the next two months, the firm was going to get another seventeen requests. What was more interesting was this fact: by midnight on December 31, 1986, $FIRSTNAMEB2 was going to be flooded with calls, begging for their service.\nThere was one big reason for this big change: It was now 1986, and the year 1986 was going to be a spectacular year for weddings.\nPeople simply weren\u2019t getting married very much in the 1970s. When a man and a woman thought seriously about getting married, they would find themselves looking at each other and thinking, \"Well, do we want a divorce? Or are we going to stay married?\"\nThe cost of a bad mistake now seemed to be getting rather large.\nThere was the moon. There were taxes. There was inflation. Divorce courts now seemed to have become a kind of common places for passion-decayed couples. Statistics on divorce were maddening: In 1967, there were 207,000 divorces granted in the United States. In 1977, the latest year for which statistics were available, there were 251,619 divorces. In 1967, the cost of a first-class wedding had been...", "0_18_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-eight in the evening</span>. Many prime things of life, organ and tissue of knowledge, lay ahead of him. What a piece of luck luck was! was! Luck was lovely! Everything was lovely, solid. Having a big, big sissy drink like that was like snuggling up in some nice old easy chair with a kitty-cat on your lap. What he needed was\u2014a kitty-cat. In an hour he would have a brand new kitty-cat. It was dark now. It was not going to rain. Fifty billion dark specks wheeled and dipped through the air. Men stepped through them as if they weren\u2019t there. People suddenly disappeared, entered new social atmospheres, metamorphosed totally and in some fantastic way. New York was a world, complete and perfect in every last little detail. It was like a fantastic dream he once had, a sort of brutal scene where thousands of sordid little scenes kept popping up everywhere in hundreds of savagely comic irrelevancies. He remembered it, stopping by a drug-store on the corner, in front of the window, to look up at the sky. Great clouds things, rolled out over the billions of dark little specks. Ruddy light loomed up, darkness gathered...", "1_2_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> of October thirteenth. $FIRSTNAMEB1 had Silverstat secure the bar for the night.\n\"This is the beginning of a great new cycle for us,\" $FIRSTNAMEB1 said over the telephone to his wife, Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEB1. To his son, Roger $FIRSTNAMEB1, Jr., he said, \"This is the beginning of a great new cycle for the family. We are completely safe now.\" He made arrangements over the phone to send Eugene, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and Beechnut to boarding school. \"Go to sleep,\" he told his son.\nIn the spiral of its declining whine, the space ship went on Vaporizing in its fall until its outline blurred, became rainbow-hued, went hazy, became featureless.\nExcellent beef products emerged from the refrigerator of the space ship. Attached to them was a note in the clear handwriting of $FIRSTNAMEB0:\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014Here\u2019s provisions for you, buddy-boy. Don\u2019t worry. They won\u2019t find you. I\u2019m a ghost. Boy, am I a ghost. I\u2019ve done something they can\u2019t forgive, so they can\u2019t ever find me. That\u2019s what that young feller with the big...", "2_18_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after six o'clock in the evening</span>. A voice came over the loudspeaker. It was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s voice, and it was coming from the gilded statue in the courtyard. The voice was formal, but it carried a-good deal of thermonuclear strength. \"Earthling strangers and friends\u2014\" it said. \"By the power invested in me by the Solar Alliance, and by the graciousness of my wife, who does not wish to see me or hear me exercise domineering powers, I declare open the observatory and museum dedicated to the memory of Khalid Muhammad Abdul Rah scruggs, my father by adoption. The observatory and museum is open. Have fun!\"\nA searchlight on a police motorcycle hit a corner of the courtyard. The courtyard was suddenly brilliant.\nA helmeted police officer dismounted from the motorcycle, blinked in the light, lost interest in the crowd, turned to look at Salo and $SURNAME0. He ducked his head respectfully to $SURNAME0.\n\"Say there, Ironpants\u2014\" he called.\n\"You mean me, sir?\" said Salo.\n\"Yeah\u2014\" said the policeman, \"if you\u2019re the Moon man.\"", "2_10_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-eight in the morning</span> on Mars.\nIn the Salo-mor, there was a smell of hot insulation.\n\"Cut the G and D jets,\" said Commander Clarence N. Hickman, of the Salo-mor.\n\"G and D cut,\" said Lieutenant George R. Fitch, of the Salo-mor.\n\"Power down to L,\" said Commander Hickman.\n\"Powering down to L,\" said Lieutenant Fitch.\n\"Thank you for helping me keep my word,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Ltfington Churchill Smith, the born-and-bred Mars colonist.\n\"My pleasure, $FIRSTNAMEB2,\" said Old Man Winfield.\n\"I didn\u2019t make your identity problem any worse, did I?\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Leonard Smith, the born-and-bred Mars colonist.\n\"No, you didn\u2019t, son,\" said $SURNAME1. \"I was going to have to tell him, anyhow.\"\n\"Was going to?\" said Viola. \"You don\u2019t think you\u2019re going to have to tell him, now?\"\n\"No,\" said $SURNAME1.", "2_1_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME1 had no reason to suspect, as he answered the telephone, that he would be unable to reach, by telephone, the person with whom he had shared his last two dreams.\nThe possibility did not occur to him. He was looking at the telephone, on the hypothesis that it was in working order, as proof that he had at last returned to the familiar Earth.\nHe did not guess that all phones on Titan would be dead until the next morning.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had lain down to sleep on a patch of wild strawberries half an hour before. As he lay there, he was dreamlessly boring even himself.\nHe was grunting monosyllables to himself, making a schedule in his head for the cleaning of the barracks and the issue of toilet paper on the next Monday morning.\nHe was barely awake enough to think. And, when the alarm sounds of memory came to him again, he was barely awake enough to remember.", "2_3_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>\u2014and it was as dark as it ever got on Titan.\n$SURNAME0 and his friend were ten million miles apart.\nThere was no means of communication between them.\nThe mateless one had twice survived a suicide attack on his person. His body was a pulpy battlefield on which conventional white bandages accounted for only a small fraction of the material.\nHe was forty-three years old. He lay on his back on the rock floor of an alpine valley on Titan. With his head propped on spears, he was looking up, unblinking, at the heartless beech-stars, as they tumbled ecstatically through the endless dark.\nHe could neither hear nor smell.\nThere was a flutter under his ribs.\nThe mateless one\u2019s fall from grace had been from a great height indeed.\nIt had begun one clear Martian morning on the steps of the war museum at Newport, on the blue-green iron island of Mars.\nAt Newport, the mateless one, as acting head of the space fleet of Mars, had received orders naming him Supreme Pontiff of Mars.\nThe orders had been presented to him by a messenger in a skisled sleigh.", "0_10_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span> when the screaming started. It seemed to shatter $SURNAME0\u2019s museum, to blow the noses from the chronographs like posters from the walls.\nConcord whistled, looked skyward.\nAs a punctuator of the maximum confusion being wrought everywhere, it is perhaps relevant to describe here the actions of $FIRSTNAMEB1, the nightwatchman. He clawed at his head. Something inside his head was trying to claw its way out into the air.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 didn\u2019t know what it was.\nIt wasn\u2019t butterflies.\n\"Kkkkkrn\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nIt was the soul of $FIRSTNAMEB1 that was doing the clawing. And what was the soul of $FIRSTNAMEB1 feeling around inside itself for? It was trying to feel around within itself for the things $FIRSTNAMEB1 understood best.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had been born on Mars, and since early childhood had been in and out of the Melvin Gustal Training School for Unspeakable Brutes in that planet\u2019s most unwalkable desert. He had been a hit with his Earthling recruiters, who had been sent to Mars expressly to round up bright prospects. \"Bright prospects\" were the expressions used by the recruiters.\n\"", "0_8_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-six in the morning</span> of December thirty-first when they took off from Space Platform One, the fabulous birdlike creation of Doctor Maxwell Fountain, who was pilot and general manager of the space station.\nComfortable in their gravity-free environment, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 ate breakfast before takeoff. $SURNAME0 ate Polish kielbasa and buttered potatoes, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 drank carrot and orange juice.\n$SURNAME0, a tall, thin man, was almost skeleton-like in appearance. His thinness was accentuated by long, pointed canines that jutted out of his mouth. These canines were not vestigial, as they are in many human males. They were fully capable of bolting all but the fanglessends of mammalian steak.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s thinness was not a function of appetite\u2014he ate prodigious amounts of everything. His thinness represented will and defiance only. His body and mind, in their spectacular flights, burned a great deal of fuel.\n$SURNAME0 had ram-rod straight thin legs that curved out at the knees to permit him to squat easily on the floor of the space platform. His ankles were as thin as his canines. His flat feet...", "1_4_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-one in the morning</span>, and the shift commander, who was in charge of the gymnasium for the duration of the alert, was making his rounds. In about an hour, he thought morosely, the mess hall would swing into action, and he\u2019d have to find men to serve breakfast. His patrol included Lieutenant Blanchard, who was standing nervously in a corner of the building, and the two privates, and the two privates\u2019 wives.\nThe shift commander had just come to the women\u2019s latrine, a room off the end of the big room. One wall was occupied by a series of dark-brown wooden doors, like the doors of a public rest room\u2014with honest sanitary facilities behind them, according to a card above each door. Each door had two white porcelain knobs.\nNone of these doors were in use at the moment. The four women privates were in their room, which was in the barracks beside the gymnasium.\nBut one door was in use. The shift commander could hear water running inside the latrine. He listened, bent over with one ear to the door. Something told him he shouldn\u2019t knock or enter.\n\"Mizz\u2014\" he whispered to the nearest...", "0_7_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. Before ending the call, Assad had insisted that the address on Pelham Parkway was the address of my employer, the Frequent and Vigorous Guide Company. When I got in touch with Lucy, the secretary, she said she was far too busy to discuss matters with me, and hung up.\nThe problem with drugs is that you have to take them before you know you need them. The next time I felt frightened and lonely and cold I might remember this moment, but so what? If a message from God came through my telephone, I wouldn't take it seriously just because it had happened before. I would merely give the matter to my higher\u2014if nonexistent\u2014Self to examine later, the way I treated all the peculiar impulses that came to me from my Sub conscious during an acid trip. The best way to tell a material message from a spiritual one is through the Smith Projector it's called. To make it work, all you do is put a word or phrase in the projector and let it idly scan back and forth while you meditate. Words with surf\u2014aced of their own accord and glitter sumetimes,but ones that are total nonsense when you pay attention to them are almost always pure projection. For example: \u201cYour insurance policy ax...", "1_22_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was 116 North Haledon Post Office, New Jersey. Outside, the rain poured down in torrents.\n\"Another beer, Mike,\" said a nut in a beatnik ty op suit to the clerk. The clerk was named Michael Rumancek. He was fifty years old, and he had served in the Austrian Army on the Russian Front during World War II.\nThe beer came, and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 opened his black case, struck two booming chords on his Infinite Improbability Drive Zither, and sang this song:\n\"A rose,\nA bottle,\nAnd the sea\u2014\nAnybody can have anything he wants\nIf he wants it badly enough.\nA fellow in a Rolls-Royce\nCame to see did I want to buy it.\nNo, I said, I have one.\n(Instrumental)\n\"I worked in a post office,\nAnd on a railroad, too.\nI worked in a carnival when I was young.\nI was a tinker and a drummer and a auctioneer.\nAll my life has been a succession of miracles.\nAnything can happen, and it probably will.\nShipwrecked on an iceberg in July?\nNo sir,...", "1_2_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-three in the morning</span>. We were still waiting for our carbide lamps at the Reno Air Races. These lamps were to insure our getting off on time in the morning. People were beginning to form lines at the ticket booth. This was something I had never seen at a motorcycle rally before: people forming lines at the ticket booth. By three in the morning the stands were full and the crowd extending back to the grandstand seemed the largest I had ever seen.\nIt was yesterday, at noon, when I spotted a Sierra Club Wilderness Area sign near Cisco, California.\nThe proper response would be \u201cWho the hell are you?\u201d and possibly a smack for good measure...\nANA BACHE, Regarding Henry\n4. The Doldrums at the Dunes\nIT WAS THE FIRST DAY OF THE RALLY, FRIDAY MORNING, when we rode across to the starter\u2019s tower at the south end of the grandstands. For some strange reason our carbide lamps hadn\u2019t been delivered from Reno; the Air Force was holding them up. The starter was clearly unhappy about the whole deal. He was required to give us placards, like official starter\u2019s placards, to attach to our handle bars. Perhaps we would...", "1_5_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-two in the morning</span>. We had twelve hours to go before the big race. Wild times were ahead and I was in for the full finale. Right here was where we found out if LSD is good for anything besides giving a cat stomach trouble.\n4. A Big Mescaline Jolt...or else call a taxi. Was there something I had overlooked? No, every factor was of paramount importance, each nerve in tingling readiness. The trunk beside me was stuffed with pints of rum & raw ether, racers food, and enough palm-pads & knives to start a hardware store...the various contraband items I\u2019d need the next night at the big race. I was fully bent, ready to roll.\n\u201cWhere to?\u201d My attorney indicated the stand of iron pipes mounted on the trunk that served as a psychedelic backpack.\n\u201cMescaline,\u201d I said. \u201cAt two hundred mgs. That\u2019s what we need to sharpen up for the big race tonight.\u201d He nodded and reached for a tall, coiled tube. \u201cGood. We\u2019ll need every edge we can get.\u201d His voice had that tense, dry ring. Fine. We were ready for...", "2_1_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe sphere still hung in the sky as though nothing had happened.\nThis was the fifth punctual appearance of the sphere.\n$SURNAME0 lay utterly still. There was no movement to his respiration. His heart made no more noise than a watch. The fine veins in his temples were as flat and unwrinkled as linen threads.\nAll this was well known to the yew trees, the hydrangeas, the box hedges\u2014to grass, daisies, and the variety of Bermuda grass called monkey grass. All this was well known to the lichens and the mosses of the sundial.\n$SURNAME0 had always had a good relationship with the sundial. He was its fifth owner.\n\"How do you do, old man?\" said $SURNAME0. His voice had the same resonant timbre that it had had when he greeted $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 at Newport. His eyes continued to close and open like the lids of a drugged chrysanthemum.", "1_10_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-seven in the morning</span>. At that time, Space Wanderer was twenty-seven minutes old, and I was just putting on my right shoe. When I bent over to fasten the laces, I saw my son through the kitchen window, striding purposefully up the driveway with a crooked stick in his hand. He was wearing a T-shirt with the sleeves rolled up.\nHe had killed a crayfish with it. He was good at killing things.\nI fastened the shoe, then opened the back door and stepped onto the porch. I waved to my son.\n\"Hi, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1,\" he said as he approached. He still had the stick in his hand. He propped the stick against the porch railing, then shook my hand with a limp wrist.\n\"Thought I\u2019d come see how you were getting along,\" he said. \"You know, make sure you didn\u2019t need anything. Anything at all.\"\n\"I\u2019m getting along fine, thank you,\" I said. \"Got coffee inside.\"\n\"No, really\u2014\" said Space Wanderer, \"anything.\"\n\"Naw,\" I said, \"I guess I can\u2019t think of a thing I need.\"\n\"Well, if...", "2_8_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe cleaning-woman threw her arm around the lieutenant\u2019s neck. \"Oh, would you like one of my cutouts?\" she said. \"Take any one you want.\"\n\"Thanks a lot,\" said the lieutenant. \"I\u2019ve been saving for a rainy day.\" He began figuring laboriously on the 1040 form.\nOne hundred forty miles above Earth, the Space Wanderer, a robot wrongly supposing itself to be a Galactic Hero, prepared for the rest of its assigned life.\nA plutonium powerpack was put into series with the solar batteries that were already drawing power from the Sun. The plutonium pack was scheduled to burn out in thirty years.\nMercury, Venus, Mars, and the three moons of Mars were now practically uninhabitable. In thirty years, there would be no life at all left in the solar system.\nThe Space Wanderer would explode with the might of ten thousand Great New York Amusement Parks.\nIt had been ordered by the Master Computer of Titan to explode, regardless of the alleged wishes of Professor Sonny Grandier.", "1_19_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0, gazing stupidly at the pictures in the book, came gradually to understand that the particular game the pictures showed was the way boys on Mars amused themselves when they were sent by their grown-ups to a place called School.\nThere were two teams, one blindfolded, the other striving to make touchdowns against them. The large players who stooped most successfully to gather up the ball were called Butts.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was a Butts.\nThe player putting the ball in play was a thumper who stood off to one side, out of bounds.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was a thumper. He shifted from foot to foot, prepared to thump.\nThere were no passes\u2014but the thumper could yell heck instead of banging, and the players could then hek or spritz as the case might be.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was the thumper. He shuffled to the proper spot.\nThe names of the other players were Stony Stonewall, the rover; $FIRSTNAMEB1, the left cornerback; and Brooksy Quarts, the right cornerback and field-goal kicker. These were $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s teammates.\nThe goal posts were known as Charlie Parazoo\u2019s Bar and Grill.\nThe gridiron...", "2_9_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>, New Hampshire time.\n\"Hey, baby, what\u2019s happening?\"\n\"Grippe, hell! There\u2019s going to be hell to pay, that\u2019s what\u2019s happening!\" yelled the man next to $SURNAME0. \"This guy means business, and there\u2019s going to be loose shoes in the street.\"\n\"Who the hell\u2019s that?\" said Bratton Beck.\n\"That\u2019s Skip, the Great White Hope,\" said Brubaker.\n\"The who?\" said Beck.\n\"The Great White Hope\u2014that\u2019s what the press has nicknamed him,\" said Brubaker.\n\"Oh,\" said Beck. \"Hello, Skip.\"\nThe time was twenty-two minutes after nine o\u2019clock, New Hampshire time.", "0_6_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span> and very dark and we tied up at a little dock on the bank of the river and a man said something I couldn't understand. I saw figures coming in the dark and suddenly somebody switched on a big electric light right in our faces. There were three or four men confronting us, with big cocked pistols in their hands. Before anybody could say anything the one flashlight was put out and another one turned on. It was a lantern, on the end of a pole, and the light was directed ahead of us and we could see a little rocky path switchbacking steeply up the hill from the dock. I heard flat Spanish again and knew somebody was being told to get up that path. The pistols pointed at our heads and a man grabbed Bill's sleeve and I was shoved from behind by a rifle. So we started to climb the path with our hands up and the carbines bouncing against our backs. The carbine is not much of a climbing weapon. It is made for traveling over terrain where the enemy is not. As a weapon for defense in the dark it has many drawbacks. It is practically impossible to aim it without great difficulty unless you are standing still. The enemy is unlikely to be standing still when you are on a rocky trail fifty feet above a river...", "1_2_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was stashed behind the wheel of a rented Ford panel truck in a residential area of Alexandria. The panel truck was mid-block in a column of similar vehicles. They were police vehicles, and they were parked illegally. Half a block away was an Alexandria police precinct house, widely known to be the flagship of $SURNAME0\u2019s Mid-Atlantic fishing fleet.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s fleet had been rousted by the same panic that had sent him scrambling out of Newport. Each precinct house in the chain now held over fifty people in a state of semihysteria.\nAll the people were in wheelchairs, and all the wheelchairs were unoccupied. They were held in mechanical \u00e9tatsconstituents, poised for flight if a wheel should spin.\nThirty-three minutes before two, $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s nerve had gone. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had told the police sergeant to whom he was responsible that his name was Juan Morse, and that he had a bad heart, and that he wanted to go see a doctor right away. The sergeant, with a sick wife, an invalid mother and an alcoholic brother, was only too glad to take sick, invalid, alcoholic Juan Morse\u2019s name and serial number, and...", "1_0_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-six ante meridiem</span>, that is, twelve twenty-six P.M., or four twenty-six A.M., lunar time, when Salo and $FIRSTNAMEB2 received word, by way of $SURNAME0 and an antenna in the backyard trellis of the museum, that they were to go to Mars as soon as possible.\n\"Would you believe it?\" said Salo, \"something\u2019s happening after all.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had been expecting a message from his father from one minute to the next. At least two times an hour, $FIRSTNAMEB2 rolled on his back and, opening his ventral pouches, yanked down on his third testicle, which had a pulley and fioat in it.\nWhatever message there was for $FIRSTNAMEB2, it was in this third testicle. But there was nothing in it but dry honey.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was supposed to take his father\u2019s third message from this dry honey as soon as he found it.\n\"Look at our boy\u2014ain\u2019t he cute?\" said Salo. \"He\u2019s got a bunch of messages up there. When the time comes, he knows just which one to take.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was behaving like an expectant father who...", "0_11_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to noon</span>. The last sounds we heard from Fort Worth were the amplified noises of some wild fool of a preacher ranting about \u201cSIN! SIN! SIN! and about a woman who supposedly came right in the church and gave the holy minister a blowjob right in the middle of the sermon. Then a scream of feedback from the speakers.\nFor ten minutes we drove north on I\u201435, the wrong way in the southbound traffic, without any sign of surprise or hostility from the other drivers. We were in a Genuine Scooter, a Californian import with a cracked red leather seat, gleaming brass fittings and a crazy, leaping black scot ter that went on the gas tank in front. There was nothing else, not even windshield wipers.\n1 tried to drive it on the sidewalk once. It just churned, spun and dug. The scooter people advise that all pedestrians get off the sidewalks when the scooter is engaged. What a great piece of advice, I thought, as I crushed the poor scooter into the curb alongside the freeway. Wobbling on three wheels and bashed against a pole, the machine almost held its ground as we came to a sudden halt that sent us both into the cracked, random t...", "2_14_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nNow the term \u201cdie\u201d is used advisedly, advisedly because the animals were in essence not really dead. They were as close as any creature in nature can come to being immortal. To be more precise, the creatures\u2019 existences dated back not to twentieth-century Earthling time but to the time of their birth. Each creature\u2019s memory dated back to the instant of its birth.\nThere was nothing to be done about the death of the creatures\u2019 flesh, was there? Nothing practical, at any rate.\nBut there was a great deal that could be done about the death of their memories. If the creatures\u2019 memories could be made to die, truly to die, there would still be sentience, consciousness, continuity of identity.\nThere would still be creatures.\nThe identity of the creatures might be a little dim, might recall recent events quite inadequately.\nBut the creatures would be immortal, and much purer than before, in a trim, Spartan way. All distractions of decay and diversion would be gone. The beings, with their trimmed-down sentiences, would be symmetrical patterns, patterns not too far from perfect.", "2_17_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe crowd cheered its instant knowledge of the wondrous tidings. And the cheering crowd, in perfect Cheerleader style, could not possibly have known what the wondrous tidings were. The cheering crowd bellowed its profound pleasure at the authentic excitement that was in store. And the cheering crowd, being thoroughly well behaved, did not wink or nudge or tell its neighbor dirty jokes about what the wondrous tidings might conceivably be.\nThe cheering crowd, of course, was wrong about the impending wonders.\nThe wonders were banal and familiar.\nFor example, $SURNAME0 was going to bring three lucky lads to the materialization platform from out among the crowd\u2014one lad from among the young men, one lad from among the old men, one lad from among the old women.\nIsn\u2019t there an old woman in every crowd?", "0_20_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-one in the evening</span>. And that twenty-one was a very important clue to $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s real character. He was born on Mars on February 21st, 1919\u2014sent Earthward to say and do what he was saying and doing at that poignant moment in history.\n\"Miss Moon,\" he said, his voice choked, \"you have about ten days to marry somebody else before I put you through the worst hell a woman was ever put through. Ten days from tonight, at this same minute, I shall put you in an open field, with a dunce cap on your head, a pile of old newspapers at your feet, and a beekeeper\u2019s mask over your face. You\u2019ll be required to pick up the newspapers with your right hand and put them through a hole in the mask. If you make a mistake, if you try to put the wrong paper through the hole, or if you yank out the wrong paper, or if you remove the mask altogether, or if you try to take the mask off while anything is going through it\u2014\" The very thought of it made him shudder. \"Well\u2014good night, Miss Moon,\" he said huskily.\nMoon stepped into her elevator. \"Good night, Mr.", "0_22_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-six in the evening</span>. I sat in the bath till the hot water gave out. Towelling myself barefoot on the bathroom rug, I heard my father suddenly singing in the living-room. His voice was surprisingly good. It was a nostalgic song of the late war period. I hurried to the bedroom in time to hear the last stanza:\nThe wind is calling to me\nAcross the sea.\nThe Sores of Time are waiting\nAs we always knew they would be;\nBut tonight I\u2019d follow her call\nAcross the entire Western world,\nBecause my true love is coming\nAcross the sea.\nIt was a valedictory kind of song to sing in a strange town, at the end of a life that had itself been a strange dream from the beginning. I knew that everything was going to be all right, and I also knew that everything was going to be all wrong.\nI didn\u2019t hear him when he came in. I was absorbed in my work. When the crash came, I assumed at first that we had hit a tree or a cow. I ran to the window, just in time to see the remains of our Nash in the air, turning end over end like a smashed fly. It made quite a cloud...", "1_14_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was a time of day when most grown people on Gottfriedhelm Trum-pet\u2019s staff would ordinarily be tapering off for their midafternoon naps.\nAsk now about the lids of Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s eyes.\nShe had lids, all right.\nThey now spread a little.\nShe was looking straight at the pink dress, trying to make the first reasonable deduction from its presence.\n\"The association of the color with Titan reminds me\u2014\" she said, \"reminds me of a dream I had not long ago\u2014\"\nMrs. $SURNAME0 hesitated, because she was not sure what the connection was between the color of the dress and the Titanic, or whether there was a connection. She groped. \"In the dream I\u2014I walked right down the middle of Broadway in New York City, in my night clothes,\" she said. \"I had on my best evening dress under my nightgown.\"\nMrs. $SURNAME0 had had the dream, actually.\n\"My but anything can carry color these days,\" said Mr. $SURNAME0. He resented the implication that his family\u2019s tenure of the immense yacht had been brief. His family had once been...", "2_3_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nNo one came to claim ownership of old Salo.\nFigure in a White Musk Suit\nRUMFOORD AND his dog materialized on the gray canvas of space close to the raft that periodically quitted the Martian solar system. The raft, which owed its existence entirely to miraculous weather, was almost unbearable to look at. But $SURNAME0 didn\u2019t look at it.\nHe was healing Solar $SURNAME1, who had materialized with him. The great dog lay flat on his back with his forepaws twitching in the air.\n\"You gave us quite a scare, Skip,\" said $SURNAME0 genially.\n\"Sorry, sorry, sorry,\" said $SURNAME1. He was having trouble getting the words out. He was having trouble in general, because he was coming down with a bad case of heartworm. His heart was skipping, slow skipping beats. The beats got slower and slower, the longer the dog lay on his back. On earth, the dog would have choked to death when the pulses stopped altogether. But the pulses kept slowing and slowing, and the dog kept on living, kept on suffering.\nOne minute passed.\nThen two.\nThen three.\nThen four.\nThen five.", "0_13_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one o'clock in the afternoon</span>; the place, a spot half an inch below the top left-hand comer of the last page of an extremely short book\u2014a popular edition, published at a popular price for mass reading. The book itself was about three hundred and twenty-eight pages long\u2014one of those thick, soft, shapeless, Editor-Read Edition books, with the horrid, bulgy, black-advertised binding. It was dark green, with a scene of tombstones and a church spire blotched in yellow on the cover, with a few skulls and crossbones thrown in for good measure.\nIt was a story about Judge Thatcher's son. That was all you could learn about the story, for on the first page began the first chapter, which was five pages long, and the first chapter was all about how the Judge Thatcher's son came to love caoutchouc, the white substance that some South American Indians wear on their noses instead of feathers.\nThat was all there was about the story on the first page. There was not even the author's name. The title of the story was \"The Streak of Gold.\"\nThe second page of the story told you how the Judge Thatcher's son found a streak of gold in his mother...", "2_15_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>. Caitlin was still in Walnut Grove, bargaining with a horse named Duchess for a hard-to-get bag of oats. The horse, who loved to talk G.I. Joke, had asked her what her sign was, as an opener. She was without cable now. Her hair had begun to break into truly wild tufts again. She had decided not to jerk off all winter. She would jerk off all spring. There was a black skunk on a fencepost, behind the horse, getting a very bad sunburn, who expressed indecent interest in her panties. She had had several run-ins with a boy who worked in the general store, and had told him to kiss her ass. The network sign-off was under way as she got back.\n\u201cGosh I just love this part.\u201d\n\u201cIt is to die.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s just like church. Except you can swear.\u201d\n\u201cWish they wouldn\u2019t do that.\u201d\n\u201cThere\u2019s absolutely no commercials, you notice. Not one. \u201d\n\u201cThey just want us to hang around.\u201d\n\u201cTo hang around.\u201d", "0_19_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Red and I sat watching our coffee cool. It was a hot July night. It was hot and dry in the coffee-shop. All around us it was dry and hot. We sat looking out through the windows.\n Enemies No. 1, 2 and 3 were sitting at the table next to ours. They were a rowdy crew. They were both a loud and a crass crew. For some reason they took a violent dislike to Red and me. They were bawling out their waitress, who was as pretty as a picture.\nA slim, nimble blonde made up in Ireland, where nobody has better girls, this wench was taking it all in good part, and was as nice a girl as I hope to meet anywhere.\n\u201cYou blooming bitch!\u201d yelled one of our enemies at the blonde. \u201cGet this coffee for us?\u201d\nEverybody in the place heard him. He was a bellowing bull of a fellow, a hulking great slob. He was a London miner -- and a lousy one, too.\n\u201cYes, sir,\u201d said the waitress. \u201cI'll get your coffee for you in just a minute.\u201d\nYou could see she would...", "2_22_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Wednesday the twenty-third of September before Martian Scout Ship number 3132 from the planet Mars dipped under The Whale and came to rest on the equator of Copernicus.\n\"The installations on the rings of Saturn are unimportant to you now,\" said $SURNAME0 genially. \"You will never have to go there. \"Young Herbert, you and your dog and your punctured roller skates will be taken along to Mars, where you will be put to useful service. \"You young ladies\u2014\" His eyes went to $FIRSTNAMEF1, to $SURNAME1. \"You will stay right here with me.\n\"And now the only people I need to concern myself with are you three gentlemen\u2014since everything Earthlings need to know has already been said, and since you Earthlings now have your roles to play and your scripts to study.\n\"Skip. Barney \u2014\" said $SURNAME0, \"please take $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and Martin Trexler over to the $FIRSTNAMEM1 dictionary building, and see that they are provided with toothbrushes, toothpaste, hairbrushes, razor blades, shaving cream, deodorants, a change of clothing, and seventy-five dollars in money of your Earth.", "0_14_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and Space Director $SURNAME0 was addressing his dog on the grassy knoll outside his Taj Majal of a home. He and the dog were as well shaded from the almighty sun as were any creatures this side of Mercury.\nRows of intricate colored sunshine-shades hung from the Shiraz trellises. The trellises in turn were hung from huge, corrugated stainless-steel springs that arched over the lawn like the ribs of a whale. The springs in turn were fastened to the grassy knoll, underground.\nUnder the huge, spongy lawn were two feet of insulating batting, two feet of compressed fiberglass insulation, and two feet of waterproof concrete.\nThe white house itself was equipped, like a submarine, with physiological-support systems of its own. There was a rotation system of six bedrooms and three baths, always providing a neat succession of cool, damp, and dry.\nR $SURNAME0 wore shorts, a sports shirt, and sandals. He was a burly, sandy-haired man of forty. His nose was full, his upper lip hairless.\nDr. $SURNAME1 was nearby, taking notes on a clipboard. Dr. $SURNAME1 was a thin, bespect...", "1_11_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-two in the morning</span> when we found the country road leading to the house in Modesto where old $FIRSTNAMEB3 had his home with his wife and kid and flat-bottom Ford. We zigzagged, following the road. \u201cNo point trying to be cool about anything any more -- gone past that now and a lot else beside -- wave and smile at everybody, old $FIRSTNAMEB3 -- wave at that pretty girl -- wave and smile and have a good time. So it's finally come to pass, you old rumbustious joyful lovable and above all opinionated gadabout -- now you're going to meet your grandson.\u201d We shouted and yelled over the thought of it. The town loomed up out of the valley. There was a parking lot with a few cars, grifters and bandits around; pool hall; Sunday morning, nothing to do till noon. We parked outside a hardware store. \u201cGo get 'em, old $FIRSTNAMEB3, and you watch yourself, too, and anyhow remember what I've said to you about everything, always yes and always maybe, and always Is?\u201d A bell jangled as he pushed his way into the store. A minute later he was back with a bag of nuts and bolts he'd forgotten to buy the last time he...", "1_2_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-six in the morning</span>. We went straight to Go-Go's home in the Cadillac. \u201cWhere's the key?\u201d I asked. There was no key in the car. \u201cShe always keeps it on a chain around her neck, you fool!\u201d I accused myself. Sure enough, it was hanging around her neck, right enough, as she lay there snoring gently in front of her bedroom door, which she had kindly left unlatched in her long drunkenness, knowing instinctively that some wild young thing would come along and whatwildy come into her bedroom in the middle of the night in Las Vegas and perhaps take her to a party in the desert with lots of dark and handsome men who, she felt somehow, were just waiting to be sighted like deer on a distant mountain slope, or heard moaning sadly in starry deserts at night.\nAh, she read in our faces as we stood beamstrous above her. The white light of our grins knocked her out like a blow on the head with a jo handled shovel. We left her there and drove off in shady elation, leaving her fanned out on the floor, without waking her. The ace of spades stared up from the carpet where her head had been; she hadn't even bothered...", "1_9_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span> of a day in the year 1973. The place was the center of the sun.\nThe calculated position of the craft at the moment the harmonium spoke was 9\u00b0 12\u2019 N., 149\u00b0 7\u2019 W.\nThe shock was perfectly and mathematically centered.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Magnum Opus, felt the surprise inside him. His pain was gone. All his anxieties were suddenly gone. He was highly pleased.\nThere was bright light outside the gilt-edged, cracked, dry windows of the mogul\u2019s office.\nThe light made $SURNAME1 suspicious. He thought he might be hallucinating. He threw a chunk of pumice at the window to see if he was still on Earth.\nHis eye followed the stone greedily as it flew. The stone disappeared in the light.\nThe stone had not returned when $SURNAME1 blinked. $SURNAME1 had imagined it. He was still on Earth, after all. He was as miserable as ever.\nAs miserable and as rich.\nRemaining in the center of the sun was the thing to do, all right. In the center of the sun, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was rich beyond the fondest dreams of avarice. He had all the wealth imaginable. He enveloped the...", "0_3_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>ish, and the place was Tralfamadore.\nTom $SURNAME1 was at the controls of the space ship that had, in six minutes and thirty-four seconds, carried him through forty thousand, nine hundred and fifty-six light years.\nWhere am I going? wondered $SURNAME1.\nHe was alone, and he was not troubled by this philosophical question. His mind did not form the words \"Where am I going?\"\nAt the controls of the space ship, $SURNAME1 was deaf, dumb, and blind. He controlled not only the course of the ship but also the speeds of his own senses and of his thoughts. He saw and felt and thought nothing.\nHe was strapped into a contour chair. The house beside him was a house to which he owned nothing, as were the grass and the sky and the stars\u2014for whom did they belong?\nHe was a statue of a man. Ranks of needles ran from his spinal column to the base of his skull, carrying messages from and to his brain. $SURNAME1 was plugged into the ship and the ship was plugged into him.\nThe only parts of him that felt anything were parts that could feel even if he were not plugged into his ship. The only feelings were feelings of habit. Life was...", "2_8_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-five in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, grandson of $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, had so far read only one line of the message that was to cost him twenty-nine million dollars. He was now reading the second line.\nIn three minutes and thirty seconds, events would begin to force him to read faster.\nThe message lay before $SURNAME1 on a polished mahogany table that had a surface area of two square feet. The table was only twelve inches high. It had four silver legs, and each leg was cunningly entwined with a stainless steel brontosaur.\n$SURNAME1 sat in a leather chair that had a stainless steel frame and fluted black leather upholstery.\nThe chair was too comfortable to be believed. It had a headrest that elevated like a roller coaster, and it had a panel in the seat that raised and tilted the pelvis like a lever. It had footrests that dangled, footrests that pivoted, footrests that raised and lowered. The chair was electronic in every articulation.", "1_10_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten eighteen in the morning</span> when I finally arrived at the Mint Hotel. Angling over to the circular driveway, I bounced several times on the bad brickwork and finally brought the cruiser to rest at a drunken slant directly in front of the main desk. I had a good view of the entire lobby through the dusty window\u2014and what I saw baffled me.\nThe place was in chaos. Sloppy drunks were milling around in small circles: wrangling, cursing, screaming epithets at other sloppy drunks. At the bar, three people were involved in a pushing match with the bartender, while twenty others stood around shouting \u201cFight! Fight!\u201d It seemed clear that many of these crazy people were Davis Dam workers returning from a wild night out in Las Vegas.\nI couldn\u2019t understand it. The last time I\u2019d seen the Chief Moe\u2019s has been with reference to the article I\u2019d written for the National Observer early in 1967\u2014and at that time the place was almost deserted. It had that graveyard atmosphere of any bar near the end of the desert highway out past the airport\u2014that is, one looking toward Los Angeles and the sea. Sure, there was drink and talk: talk about money, women, Vegas, fast...", "1_0_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-four a.m.</span> on this strange Friday the thirteenth of June in the year 1963 A.D.\nI had forgotten all about Friday the thirteenth, but now I laughed.\nThere was nothing to fear.\nI weighted the anchor of the big scooter-car with a steel brace I found in the tool box. Then I climbed into the driver\u2019s seat and kicked the starterswitch.\nNothing.\nI kicked it again.\nStill nothing.\nWe have already seen that this monstrous vehicle was capable of speeds in excess of six hundred miles an hour. Was it therefore too much to assume that the motor had greater kick than one carburetor? Get in and turn the key\u2014pop! Pop-pop! Pop-pop-pop! And then...pop-pop-pop-pop-pop-pop-pop all the way to Boston, with never a worry about cops.\nOf course this would have been a little flat-out Dickensian scene in downtown Boston, with my head on the panel and all the goggles blowing in the wind...shattering my visor and exposing my eyes to all that artillery-fire. But still\u2014the image had its curious validity.\nWe were in Las Vegas. For those who are unfamiliar with...", "0_8_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-eight in the morning</span>, sunny, quite cold and I had $6.85 in my pocket. I took the \u201crun\u201d and got on the truck, taking a seat in back of the chauffeur along with half-a-dozen other Okies, cotton-choppers and fruit-pickers. In front was a big farmer, who was spending his money for beer for everybody in the bar and showing off about how rich he was, but we all liked him anyway because we knew how proud he was, and we talked to him. He said he lived on a farm near Oakland -- the richest man in the world couldn\u2019t own a farm these days, he said -- and he was a peachgrower and cotton-planter and truck-farmer and owned many hired hands. He said he was only coming to Frisco to buy some assorted presents for his daughters and sons. The two in Oakland were married and the others hadn\u2019t turned out so well and were around Los Angeles somewhere. He said he was glad to be going home, he was tired of Frisco, and bumming around with Burly and all those guys. He said they took him to Burly\u2019s hotel on Nob Hill and got whisky and started telling ghost...", "2_7_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"If you wouldn\u2019t mind?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"What\u2019s that?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"If you wouldn\u2019t mind\u2014\" said $SURNAME0, \"I\u2019d like to swing first.\"\nHe laid his hand on $SURNAME1\u2019s forearm. $SURNAME1 stood up, and $SURNAME0 swung with both arms. The creaking and groaning of the swing was very loud\n\"Very nice\u2014\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Thank you,\" said $SURNAME0, climbing out of the swing. \"It\u2019s only a domestic thing, of course, but I rather like it myself.\"\nHe laid a finger against the gold frame of the oleo that his dead wife had painted. \"I had a scaffolding built around it so I could reach all the corners and impurities,\" he said. \"A few golden touches, and there you are. $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s last.\"\n\"You\u2014were lucky,\" said $SURNAME1.", "2_12_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after noon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 looked at his watch again.\nOnly his wrist and the ball of his thumb between the eyes could be seen above the sill. \"You know what\u2014friend?\" he said in a quietly conversational voice. \"I did something I warned myself I\u2019d never do. I got sentimental over the way you took your wife and child out of town so the mob wouldn\u2019t get \u2019 em. My gosh\u2014a guy who could be sentimental about something like that isn\u2019t really so hard, after all. I just wish to hell I had your faith that everything\u2014sometimes seems to be against you. See what I mean?\"\nIt was ten minutes after twelve.\nThe crowd scuffled, settled down.\n\"Ready, friend ?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Right you are, Skip,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe executioner sounded his whistle. The crowd came to its feet.\nThe twenty gongs began to boom, slowly, with a rich, sustained quality\u2014boom boommmmm, boomboommmmm, boomboommmmm.\nThe executioner threw the lever that released a spring that drove the blade into the oak block.", "0_9_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-nine in the morning</span>. What Robinson left undone between eight forty-nine and nine twenty-eight was, briefly, this. He managed to conduct a telephone conversation with, of all numeral-preoccupied buffs, the Reverend Carl Herman Brukner, of the Union of God\u2019s Musicians and Disciples, in Cleveland. (Access to phones turned out to be no problem; the Automatic Carbons had a single private line, which functioned as the main trunk for a whole host of extensions, available to subscribers one at a time. The problem was to juggle his use of it so as not to get caught.) Brukner was a cheerful acquiescent fellow who, upon being informed that he had won an all-expenses-paid trip to the International Puzzle Pageant and Award banquet, in Gatsby\u2019s town, grew positively fizzy. His agreement to let himself be interviewed on the phone, thence, was one of the easiest things Robinson had done on his whole trip. He got in a few pertinent questions about Franconia and life in Union shelters, and ended up with an invitation to call back in the evening at Brukner\u2019s home, when the reverent gentleman thought he could talk more freely. At nine twenty-eight...", "1_6_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>. There was a quart of dry martinis in her. The shades were up. She sat singing joy songs with the average word sounding coarse-cut in the re-education chamber of her mouth. She was drenched in brandy, sweating it out through pores in her face. Her hair was white. She made her singing a supple instrument of her lolling head and neck.\nThe room was dark green, lighted by lamps with dark green shades. Unframed paintings, primitives, festooned the wall space not otherwise occupied by bottles, ash trays, paintings, paraphernalia.\nA bird in a fetid cage, across the room and beneath the slope of the roof. Its top was propped open by a snake, eight and a half feet long, fang teeth bared, eyes unrolled, a stuffed viper skin, manic grin. The bird, a long-beaked piranha, contributed its own touch, to the help of atmosphere, by having a mouthful of polished horseshoe nails. There were some carnations and grass in the birdcage. Their proper season had long since passed.\n communicated to the bird and the two-foot viper by means of a series of tiny...", "2_21_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe day had begun for Genghis Cohen, owner of the Falcon-surprise of department stores in Indianapolis, at two minutes after midnight. On that morning at two minutes after twelve o\u2019clock, he had awakened and climbed out of the enormous white-canopied bed in the master bedroom in the penthouse of his condominium, in the west wing of the building. He had slept nude, under a high-quality two-thousand-thread-count sheet, of Egyptian cotton. The sheet was so fine that it did not catch and hold under its weave the clumps of black gold hairs that fell from the magnificently muscled body of Mr. Cohen between the ages of fifty and sixty. So the coppery hairs rained softly onto the hypoallergenic carpeting of the bedroom floor.\nMr. Cohen was sixty-four. He was six feet five inches tall.", "1_14_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two eighteen in the afternoon</span>, and we were far out in the desert, near the Texas border, when $FIRSTNAMEB3 decided he'd had enough of me and Cindy. He turned to me and pointed it out quite simply. \u201cCome on, $FIRSTNAMEB6, quit bothering the young lady, let's go.\u201d Cindy was dozing. I was holding $FIRSTNAMEB3's notes on the car, his raincoat and briefcase, besides my numerous bundles, stuck to my ass and legs with sweat. \u201cWhat?\u201d I said. \u201cGo where?\u201d\nI asked him what he meant. He said he was sick of my face, sick of hearing my conversation, sick of my long stories and moans, sick of everything about me, and added that it had taken us six hundred miles to realize it but now we both knew.\nI figured I could straighten him out. \u201cYou ain't thinking straight, old pal -- \u201d\nBut he looked at me with those fever-glazed eyes and said, \u201cYes I am, $FIRSTNAMEB6, you and me are finished, I've only been waiting for the right time and place to tell you.\u201d I said okay, and got ready to jump out of the car.\n\u201cNow don't...", "1_0_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-four ante meridiem</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 pressed a red button in the bur oak paneling of his console, and received the reestablishment of his intricate system of greetings, excuses, and apologies for misbehavior. There were, for instance, his wife to inform and his butler, chauffeur, bodyguard, coloratura soprano and master chef to reproach.\nA swarm out at Malachish\u2019s boathouse sucked up the Station Wagon and half a dozen boats. The swarm carried the lot to a skip under the lawn, which unearthed itself and became the dreadnought Probitas.\nNot long thereafter, the honeymooners suffered their final rupture. There was a command from the submaster-computer of the Unanimity to end it all. The command was honored by a champion jester. The jester, trained in Hungary, forced Crystal, as best he could by warning screams, to flee from him out of the cabin of the yacht into a swampy part of the bay with no docks, no boats, and reeds a foot deep.\n\"Spare me the humiliation of suicide on an empty stomach!\" the jester cried.\nHe barricaded Crystal behind reeds a foot deep.\nThen, turning his...", "2_13_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Every man groped for his compasser. Every man felt the fast spinning of his eyeballs. Every man knew somehow that he was falling headlong into space. His feet had left the ground, and he weighed nothing. The weightless soldiers felt no pain. There was only the dizziness and the terror of falling forever into nowhere.\nNobody had noticed it when the Martians whisked them off their feet and yards above the ground, and whisked them away.\nNobody had noticed it when the Martians put the soldiers back down again.\nThe Martian attack was over in two seconds. But two seconds was time enough to render helpless an army of 60,000 men.\nThe four quadrants of Mars were spinning off into space.\nAnd Remillard, aged one year, two months, and thirteen days, was beginning to feel really well again. And to grow sleepy\u2014and hungry ....\nTHE FOURTH OF JULY", "1_3_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three sixteen in the morning</span>. I hadn't slept all night. I was worried about $FIRSTNAMEF4. I had Jane go to my house and pack a bag for me. Frisco found a stewardess who gave us coffee and a butt for my pipe. We went out the door with all our gear. \u201cGood-by, $FIRSTNAMEB3,\u201d I said. \u201cNow you can go back to Camille.\u201d He sat on his trunk in the shade of the airport terminal and cried like a child. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, I don't want to leave you. I don't want to leave you, man. What am I doing? Where am I going, you say? I don't know. I don't know. What am I doing? Good-by, good-by, good-by,\u201d and he cried like a baby. Well, the plane was waiting; I went aboard with Jane and we took off. I understood $FIRSTNAMEB3. Where was he going? What was he doing? I didn't have the slightest idea. The plane carried us further across the continent to Denver. $FIRSTNAMEF4 and my aunt were landing at Kansas City. I didn't know whether they had gotten there. It was hot, muggy weather in Denver -- the...", "1_16_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-three in the afternoon</span>, and the space was a bedroom in the ruins of an abandoned housing project near the Neutral Zone wire. The abandoned project was an excellent strategic position, since it dominated an abandoned industrial park full of huts, warehouses and rats. A Ragged Legion intelligence agent had found the bedroom stiflingly hot, humid, filthy and rat-infested\u2014grossly unfit for human habitation. There was a leak in the toilet. Worse, there was no television and no telephone. What little was left of the fourth and last wall told anyone who cared that this particular dwelling had been designated Unit 23-C, Apartment 3. The Department of Public Housing, United States of America, had apparently intended the place for a family called Maroger. The lucky Marogers were no longer around to care what had happened to old Unit 23-C, Apartment 3. In any case, a skilled demolition man with a satchel full of plastic explosives could have leveled a hundred 23-Cs in hours, given the right map.\nThe man curled up under a blanket on the bed in 23-C was not an operator, but he knew a bargain when he saw one. The Ragged Legion had discovered his secret hiding place by sheer accident. Two of the troopers had...", "1_1_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-seven in the morning</span>.\n\"Hot damn,\" I said, \"this is going to be great.\" I rolled up both windows, locked the car, and began to saunter toward the bright cluster of lights. The air was cool in my lungs. I figured I must be at an altitude of about seven or eight thousand feet; the moderately thin air refused to undergo humankind\u2019s most desperate attempt to degenerate into a California coastal low\u2014the worst possible thing, human-wise, I could have come to: slob\u2014suffocation. The air here wanted to stay thin enough to think clearly...to fly. There\u2019s a serious architectural problem in Aspen: too many people and too few buildings downtown. Every time another high\u2014rise goes up, a whole jungle of weeds has to be cleared out of the soil up above. That\u2019s why their downtown looks like an earthquake hit it...and why there are no places to park.\nThe only casual parking place I saw was about four miles up Minturn; a sensible librarian who seemed to be on duty at a huge tree\u2014shaded mansion next to the highway said the owner of the car could be reached there by phone up to midnight. I thanked her demurely and continued on with...", "0_8_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was nothing going on.\n\"Take it away,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"You don\u2019t really want to throw up your whole small but happy life,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"for a big thing like this, $FIRSTNAMEB0. You just don\u2019t have to do it.\"\n\"I\u2019ve gotta do it,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I love you, and I don\u2019t want to love you any more. Take it away, please.\" He flung a wild right at $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s face, missed. \"Take it away!\" he cried.\n\"You want to think about this some more?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"I can\u2019t think about it anymore,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"You said it all for me. I\u2019ll always love you best of all, and I\u2019ll be a good soldier and all that stuff, but I got to think about me for a while, now, $FIRSTNAMEB1.\" He flipped his hand at his throat, invited $FIRSTNAMEB1 to open theAdam\u2019s apple-adjuster screw. \"Go ahead, now\u2014\" he said. \"Don\u2019t make me beg you.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 stepped up to $FIRSTNAMEB0,...", "1_4_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIt was time for me to go.\nI bought four of the cheapest cigars the tobacconist had and put them in my shirt pocket. Then I left Lirrbnel-Lokt and the Grand Hotel, crossed the square again and cut through the narrow passages and alleys back to the ship. No one stopped me.\nIII\nI was fifty-nine years old and stood six feet and two inches high. My weight was thirteen stone ten pounds. I was forty-two inches round the chest, thirty-four inches round the waist, thirty-eight round the waist, and my neck measured seventeen inches in circumference. I had served three years on Martian canal boats, two years on Calf field Ganymede exploration rigs, twelve years on Jupiter rigs, and the rest of the time on Venus, both starting and completing my apprenticeship on Mars.\nMy name was Alvin Miller\u2014Robert White Miller, before I dropped the name of Robert White and let my oldest brother have it. I did not drop the name because Robert White was my favourite brother. I have no favourite brother, and have not had since I dropped the name.\nI did it to simplify my name and my signature on checks. My signature was eleven letters...", "2_20_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-three in the evening</span>.\n\"Skip! What\u2019s wrong?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"Do you really want to do this, do you think?\"\n\"Oh, let us be,\" said $SURNAME0 coldly.\n\"You don\u2019t have to if you don\u2019t want to,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"So don\u2019t\u2014\" said $SURNAME0, \"it\u2019s so nice of you to let us be if we don\u2019t want to.\"\n\"Sorry\u2014of course not!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"You know what I meant!\"\n\"Yes\u2014I suppose so,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I know what you meant.\"\nThe time was eight twenty-five in the evening.\n\"Skip?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"Please watch out\u2014please be careful. This tears me all up inside, what we\u2019re doing.\"\n\"Yes?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 apologetically.\n\"You\u2019re sure you want me to do this thing, really and truly?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Really and truly?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME1.", "0_22_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-four in the evening</span> of the first day of the rest of the life of the brilliant and delicate-skinned $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, and of the beautiful, the well-nourished, and the well-cared-for $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. In a society that believed in reincarnation, they would have been thought fated for one another by previous lives.\nThey were richly apparelled. They were perfectly attuned to a world where so much went smoothly.\nThey had never felt better.\n\"Skip?\" said $SURNAME0 from the bathroom. He dropped the lavender box into the pocket of his dinner jacket.\n\"Yes, $SURNAME1?\" said his wife from the bed. She lay like a lady of Dresden china\u2014her skin the texture of softest porcelain, her eyes limpid, her hair flowing freely.\n\"I am going now,\" said $SURNAME0 from the bathroom.\n\"So soon?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"The time is 11:25 Eastern War Time,\" said $SURNAME0. \"In three minutes and twenty-one seconds, I shall reappear at the place where I first appeared on earth. I mentioned this earlier this evening, but you may have forgotten in the events of the day...", "0_15_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Red Bass Twelfth Street Trattoria was closed for the afternoon, and the sign in the window said SO OTHER RESTAURANTS MIGHT HAVE LIONS LOOK AT THE DARK.\nIt was eleven minutes after three o\u2019clock in the afternoon.\nIn a niche under the stairs in the Helmholtz lay a man who had thrown a bottle through the window of the restaurant. The bottle had broken the window in three places.\nJust under the stairs in the Helmholtz lay the man who had thrown the bottle through the window. The bottle had broken the window in three places.\nValentine Michael Smith was asleep on the floor of $SURNAME2\u2019s office. $SURNAME2 was asleep in his chair in the office.\nA great many of the ancillary personnel of Magnum Opus were asleep in the ancillary part of the Helmholtz. So few ancillary personnel were left awake that they could keep fully occupied merely shooing sleepers back under chairs and into little dark niches under stairs. The company was solvent, but it would never get out of the red as long as employees insisted on sleeping in the workplace. It was a union-shop nightmare.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1,...", "2_13_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nThe children were filing out the service entrance of the Museum of Natural History. They were going to be let out onto the main plaza, eleven stories below. There they would form circles and whirl until dizzy, hold hands and race across the plaza, screeching.\n$SURNAME0, spinner of the webs in which Earthling destiny was caught, snuffed his joint in the service entrance intercom, which was there by his right hand. $SURNAME0 was smoking a cigarette, too.\nScreaming groups of children rushed by the service entrance on their way to the plaza above.\n$SURNAME0 flicked his cigarette out into the museum courtyard. It spiraled with supreme elegance until it was lost in a mulch of Coke bottles, paper bags, and zebra-stripe gum wrappers.\nIt was one fifty-five in the afternoon.\nThe Museum of Natural History was closed from two until five. $SURNAME0 put the latches on the grille. He let the latch handles fall into place by their own weight.\n$SURNAME0 bowed his head. He shut his eyes and rested his ethereal mind accounts.", "2_10_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty in the morning</span>.\nBracknell wandered the corridors of the palace, taking an odd course, to minimize his exposure to the fine-leaved sheets of drab skin that lined the walls.\nThe palace was a maze.\nFor most of its life, the palace had been a barracks for the troops whose duty it was to guard the Earth against its natural enemies, the monsters in the darkness.\nEvery Martian knew how to lay tape and create a maze.\nTen forty-seven in the morning.\nThe first explosion pulverized the palace.\nThe palace that had been a barracks could, at a pinch, be a hospital.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 23049 was incompetent to set a broken ulna.\nThe war was now something more important, more strenuous than a war.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 23049 had to put her husband\u2019s broken ulna in a splint and strap it to his arm.\nThe Redoubt was magnificent. It accepted everything and deserved all it got.\nThe Redoubt would stand for at least one thousand years, provided no one bored into it with a drill.\nCHAPTER 18\nSURPRISES\n1. Surprises\n2. A Bad Surprise for Winfrey\n3. Homeward Bound", "2_22_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Mars. $FIRSTNAMEM2, a one-eyed Martian watchman, ran into Salo\u2019s office. He carried his dismembered right arm in his left hand.\n\"Another one?\" said Salo.\n\"It has overrun Number Seven today house,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2.\n\"Has it, indeed?\" said Salo.\n\"Does that mean anything to you?\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2.\n\"My young friend,\" said Salo, \"I\u2019m packing my space bag as we speak. Would you care to pack alongside me?\"\n\"It would be an honor and a privilege,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2.\n\"Tell me, please,\" said Salo, \"are you related in any way to Malachai-Coop?\"\n\"I am Malachai-Coop,\" said the Martian watchman. \"As my right arm once told you, I was adopted by the Malachai family when I was found wandering the Tharsis Desert alone and amiable, a young egg of thirteen Martian years.\"\n\"Then you helped plan the tunnel system?\" said Salo.", "0_22_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-one in the evening</span> on October 3, 1994, at a Newport News hotel. I had no idea, when I went to sleep, of where I\u2019d wake up. I didn\u2019t even know what state I was in. I assumed California. I\u2019d been roaming around for weeks and still didn\u2019t know where I was. I\u2019m horrible with distances and directions. Even common maps confuse and terrify me. I cannot read a map. If I tried to read a map, it would come alive, leap up and strangle me. I think maps are a secret Zen mindfuck done on us to keep us from rising up and crushing the streets.\nYes. I was really out of it; locking into my dark cycle again...the bender was on in full. All drinking is not good drinking. Drinking to blackout is not good drinking. All drug use is not good drug use...drug use to flip out is not good drug use. But there are no endings. There are no endings to the human emergencies. No fixes for the catastrophes of this night, this life, this day, this dawn, from crack of primal fire to high noon searing on . . . and fuck the blood junkies\u2014l\u2019...", "2_10_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-seven in the morning</span> on Mars.\nWoodrow Wilson Smith, the former Martian space pilot known as Sonny, stood on his grassy drumlins with his hands in his pockets. He looked at a distant dot in the sky.\nThe dot had appeared three days before, and had grown larger each day. It was now a space ship. It was now the proud, the gallant, the historic Salo-mo, bringing back home the first American to be born and bred on another planet.\nSalo-mo was ten miles up, was slowing down for a perfect landing at $SURNAME1 Hor-nius\u2019s good right hand.\nSmith could see the tiny figures of $SURNAME1 and Newport News approaching the space ship on little motorcycles. He could see the figures of their escorts approaching on foot.\nSmith was wearing rose-tinted sunglasses. He was glad he had sunglasses, because the tears were thick in his eyes, drops were splaying down his nose as though he were a fountain. He had cried continuously since he had reread golden-haired, blue-eyed $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s simple farewell in the Connecticut village.\nBefore $FIRSTNAMEB2 had left for Mars, he had written to his father these simple words: Dear Old Dad:", "0_10_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-seven in the morning</span>. We were approaching the worst tequila hangover of our lives, but it would have to wait.All around us were well dressed, well tned people working at splendid desks or chatting amiably in small groups in plush, me m n-tr-t corridors. None of them looked like the tequila swilling proles we had become.Not a goddamn one of them looked like a Frequent Smiles Dentist--except us. Would we be able to find the man we wanted? Yes, I figured we would. This was the main building of a lawyers' hideaway in the elegant hills of San Diego, so obviously we were in the presence of a cream-of-the-crop crowd. This Mormon could not possibly be conspicuous among this glittering throng...but would we recognize him when we found him? Probably not, but what the hell? Something would probably happen to make it obvious.Ten thirty-nine...ten forty...ten forty-baby! Every instinct told me we were closing the gap fast. The desk clerk had a big smirk on his face as he slid my key to me. The bastard knew. He knew something funny was about to happen. The whole place seemed to buzz like champagne that\u2019s starting to...", "1_9_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span> exactly.\n\"There goes the alarm,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nHe stood in mock military attention.\nHe saluted.\n\"All you earthlings out there\u2014\" he said into his microphone, \"all you earthlings out there, this is Commander $FIRSTNAMEB1, transmitting to you from Mars. $FIRSTNAMEB1 reporting. The first Martian dawn beats redly upon our fair planet today. I hereby christen this new dawn.\n\"This new dawn shall be called $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s Day.\n\"Henceforth, it shall be a day of rest for all true and colorfully-uniformed men.\n\"There shall be a ban on work, a ban on thinking, a ban on being unhappy, a ban on being unhealthy, a ban on bad impressions, a ban on conflict and injury.\n\"We are having a parade,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"All true and colorfully-uniformed men should join in the parade.\" He searched the sky with his antennae for the sight of the incoming ships. None had yet arrived. \"It is a very grand parade,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"All the ships in space are in the parade.\n\"It will be held right here on Mars,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"The...", "2_13_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 opened the small door, put the framed piece of blue sky on a ledge inside the door, shut the door again. By reaching through the narrow rectangles of the window, he could close the latch on the inside.\nWith his heels clacking softly on the floor, $FIRSTNAMEB1 walked down the spiral stairs.\nHe passed the landing where the statue of Narcissus was, and went on down to the bottom floor. He walked through the hammering, welding shop, where old parts of the dismantled fleet were being made over into fixtures for the new fleet.\n\"Oh\u2014hello, Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB1!\" said a workman.\nShrewd, smiling, enigmatic, $FIRSTNAMEB1 continued walking. He passed through the auxiliary control station, a relatively luxurious compartment that had already been installed in advance, pending the arrival of a dignitary of stature.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 went by the auxiliary control station, and came to the great winding staircase that led to the surface. He mounted the stairs three steps at a time.\nHe plunged like that through the winding passage at the base of the great staircase, through a corridor heavy with moisture and the smell of creosote.", "0_20_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>. I was dressed and ready to leave, having slept the sleep of the doomed, which is to say six hours. I was to meet a lady and escort her to dinner.\nIt was time to go, but I lingered in the room, thinking to pack the brooms and mops, and consider the advisability of keeping a spare window-pane in stock. Too many windows had been smashed by too many drunken house-breakers. Ten thousand dollars' worth of damage had been done in the hotel in the previous eighteen months. This seemed high, but it had probably been done by many people. There were sixty-two rooms in the hotel, and the average cost of a window-pane in Reno, we had found, was fourteen dollars. Multiply that by sixty-two, and then by two....ah well, so much, I thought, for keeping statistics.\nA great rushing and thumping and cursing, from the room below, brought me back from my reverie. Wardell had fallen out of bed, as per usual. There were sounds of his dragging himself across the floor and flopping about on the rug. He would have to be washed. This involved getting a pail of warm water and holding Wardell and...", "2_0_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-two a.m.</span> and I was just entering my room at the \u201cAmericana,\u201d when I felt an explosive satanic vibration come crashing out of Room 501. This can't be, I thought. I ran to the door of 501...handle switched to the chain position, leaned my ear to it, listening.\nWooden music, chanting. Drums. And then I heard that sound again...like distant, hysterical trumpets ...rising up through the walls, unbearably loud. I looked around. No happy lurkers in sight: The whole night staff seemed to have vanished. I ran down the hail to the switchboard, seized the phone; dialed \u201c0.\u201d The operator came on, but the noise in my ear was so loud she sounded like somebody calling to me across a lake. \u201cCall the police,\u201d I shouted. \u201cThere's somebody having a goddamn death party in 506. I'm going in.\" I banged the phone down and ran back to my room...unlocked the door, tore loose the .357 from my belt and snapped off the safety. I stood there shaking in every sinew...sweating torrents...frightened inhumanly, no shit.", "1_23_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-two in the evening</span>. Now the taxi was hauling me over the twisted streets of San Francisco toward the area in back of Union Square. Traffic was light, confusing. There seemed to be many one-way streets. Great swags of fog drifted across the road. Rider and driver bickered about the proper route. \u201cAh, take a left here,\u201d said the driver after a boozy pause. \u201cBut it better be left, goddamnit.\u201d I heard him fumbling with his paper, probably pinching the buns to make sure he had a left one. I wanted to laugh, but I was sick. San Francisco was a bad shock after all the sweetness of the Coast: a nightmare of hideous traffic jams, drunken bums, deformed wenches hanging out of hurdy-gurdy windows along Folsom Street, the clank of cloven automatons, the scream of breasting trollops, nutty Cananfanon and New York hoodlum talk. I saw a gigantic blond girl in the window of a seafaring chandler's shop; she was eating a banana crookedly and giving the world the eye. She was hooded in a long raincoat. At first I thought she was a him...", "1_15_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three seventeen in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME1 arrived on a taxi-cab run that had picked up fares in the red-light district of Tijuana. The taxi ran the length of the great hall, halting to discharge its passengers in front of the offices. The run, in San Diego, Modesto, Fresno, Stockton, and Turlock, had picked up ten fares\u2014six men and four women. The women were trollops. All ten got out of the taxi in front of $SURNAME0\u2019s palace.\nFor purposes of discussion, it will be helpful if the reader pictures $SURNAME1 standing for a moment or so before getting out of the taxi, and holding his hand out, not to the knob of the door, but to the woman on the seat beside him.\nHe is not touching her.\nHe is not being amorous.\nHe is simply testing the predictions of his third hand about the thing that its puppet-master, $SURNAME0, expects of him next. This third hand, with which he is not familiar, is telling him that he must touch this woman. That touching her is what he must do next. That he will be punished if he does not touch her.\nThere is a nun-like quality to the woman on the seat beside him...", "1_23_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-four in the evening</span>. I had just made my speech, and was about to mount my pile of empty milk cartons when I saw somebody coming down the sidewalk with a cylindrical parcel under his arm. I never before saw this strange gentleman in Fairfax. He was tall, bony, and camper-creazy. He wore a British Army corduroy coat with large flaps over both shoulders, blue Levis, and crepe-soled shoes. His beaky nose and outrageous forelock made him look like a cross between an extra in a production of Anne of Green Gables and a terminal raver. In his right ear he wore a hearing-aid radio that looked like a pack of Dunhill cigarettes. His eyes were glassy, his face glum.\nHe was the only person I\u2019d ever seen come to a meeting with us on a motorcycle. It was a shoulder-high British machine with a little engine, no gadgets\u2014a deal scooter.\n\u201cRoll up, roll up!\u201d he cried. He dropped his parcel on my pile of milk cartons, pushed his big motor scooter around behind the throne, climbed up with his parcel, and introduced himself. It turned out to be a pound of psilocy...", "1_8_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>. The telephone was shrilling in $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0\u2019s bedroom, which was two stories up and across the mighty corridors, above and to the right of the central hall.\nIt was a spring morning on Mars, Earthling readers. You are about to be introduced to a new character, Skip, the son of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\nAt this moment, the light in $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0\u2019s bedroom was suffused by the color of a ping-pong ball, thanks to the Martian sky outside the skylight.\nIn the middle of the ping-pong ball of light, in the middle of a canopy bed, reposed a bowling ball of a woman who, inside the white lace nightgown, was naked. She was forty-eight years old. She had been a beauty and had the remains of a beauty, but most of the flesh that had been architecture had slumped into wet friables. This forty-eight-year-old woman had not seen Forty interrupt her thought processes in more than eighteen years. Her thoughts had been interrupted at the moment by the sounding of the telephone.\nIt was a telephone under a clamshell casement, a telephone with a neck like...", "1_9_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span> of December thirty-first.\nThe appalling red thing that had materialized in the sky above Malachite had been sighted seventy-one seconds earlier by a radar engineer at White Sulphur Springs, Virginia.\nThis engineer was Corporal Dick Spigot of the U.S. Army, whose duty it was to maintain a round-the-clock watch on screens of a scope that would have brought pleasure or satisfaction to Isaac Newton, Ren\u00e9 Descartes, James Watt, and Robert Millikan combined.\nCorporal Spigot was a skinny young man with an evil complexion and murky blonde hair, and with a habit of sucking his teeth. He did this now, audibly, and moved his gum plugc also audibly.\nFor a time that was tantalizingly short but that still seemed altogether too long, the echo displayed nothing more alarming than a slight topographical resemblance to a human form. The resemblance lay chiefly in the outline of the echo, which was oblong and horizontal and stretched, somewhat the way a man would stretch if he were lying on his back and wanted to run.\nBut Spigot knew the echo wasn\u2019t a man at all, even before he set to work to compute the velocity of the...", "1_4_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-three in the morning</span>. The place was Beatty, east of Las Vegas. The sunshine winking on and off above the black Chevrolet in the parking lot of the radio station told us nothing. We might have been freezing at Latshaw in the Big Snooze with all creation. \u201c Yes, \u201d said this kid with a laugh. \u201cThe racket came over the phone as I was punching in my girlfriend's number. God damn, when I realized it was my own station, I almost died.\u201d Ed laughed. \u201cWhat happened?\u201d I said. \u201cWhat happened? Why, nothing happened, but me that's all. They rousted my shell-shocked ass. See, I was dozing in the audience and they were taking a poll on when Johnny should come back on. And I yawned and said, 'Aw, anytime,', and just like that I was on the air. What it was, you see, was one of the other deejays had just quit and Johnny wanted to take his place and call himself Ringo Starr and play some rock 'n roll and make real announcements and talk to the audience, and I was just some guy from the audience that had just yawned and offered his opinions.", "0_2_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until three o'clock in the morning</span> of October 11, European time, when the Space Wanderer, sitting cross-legged at the controls of his ship, fired the jets and took off from the surface of Titan.\nTen minutes later he was to disappear from the ken of all the peoples of Earth and Luna and Mars, to disappear for a million Earthling years, a million times as many Earthling days and nights. Where he disappeared to is what this book, in all frankness, is all about.\nThe home of the Space Wanderer was on a pleasant and civilized world named Newport, which was in the constellation of Draco and which was one of the eight worlds making up the so-called Stelomic System. The system was so named because the eight worlds\u2014Newport, Stentor, Harmonic, Tralfamadore, Chronos, $FIRSTNAMEF0, Generation, and God\u2019s World\u2014were all, without exception, pearl-like, spheroid-like, or ball-like. All, without exception, were beautiful, pleasant, and unfamiliar.\nIn the opinion of the people who lived on Newport, Newport and the Stelomic System were unvisited and uninhabited. They believed that they all were orphans, the last remaining creatures...", "0_22_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-five in the evening</span>. The address was 17 Lily Street in Spanish Harlem. The driver informed me nervously that Spanish Harlem was \u201cnot the best neighborhood in town, man.\u201d I told him to wait in the bus, which I parked in front of the building. I carried with me a .357 magnum revolver, a tape recorder and my attorney\u2014namely, a young lady I\u2019d picked up at a downtown bar. I wanted somebody along to take notes. She wanted to see things.\nMy attorney was drunk but game. We were running along the sidewalk, trying to get our bearings, when some ragged beast drew an Uzi submachine gun out a passing car and sprayed a passing crowd with a kamikaze burst that missed us by a yard. The saps were running in circles, grimly wounded and bleeding, knocking over an occasional fruitstand under their winged heels. We were still laughing when a passing swine took note of my bruises and blood, then grinned and said: \u201cWhat you been doing, mistah? Slumpin\u2019 in de poder\u2019?\"\nWhat the fuck? I looked at my attorney, but she seemed to be having trouble with her feet. I shoulder her along until we found a...", "1_1_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-four in the morning</span>. Remi Bonc?ur and I were driving down Central Boulevard, Miami Beach. My car was a rickety old convertible with the top up on it. Remi had with him two large paper bags full of groceries. Remi was on his way from New Orleans to Jacksonville, where he had been offered a scholarship to finish his doctorate at the University. His last few days in Miami he spent with me. We had spent the day buying groceries together. That night we were going to sit up all night and talk over everything and read aloud books we liked. Remi had a wonderful reading voice. It was soft and sepia and scholarly. He had a little left in his first year of college when he ran off with a strange girl named Lucille and lost his scholarship. That was twelve years ago. Since then he'd been a four-flusher, a short-order cook, a Bible salesman, a speed clinic runner, a peripatetic textbook salesman -- now he was about to be a professor in a small college in midwestern America. He was the brother of Al Bonc?ur, the professional pool-player. They both came from a Louisiana colored tribe of Arabs. Lamartines, Remi Bonc?ur and...", "2_23_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nFuzzy Peak was the owner of the big mansion on the hill.\n\"Quite a drink,\" Fuzzy said.\nThe shaker was poised over his head.\nAn oboe played ecstatic music in Fuzzy\u2019s living room.\n\"Hey!\" called $SURNAME0\u2019s butler from the bottom of the front stairs. \"Oh, wow!\" he said, as he climbed the stairs. \"Wow!\"\nHe held out a piece of paper. \"For you, Mr. Peak, sir. Just came by special messenger.\"\nFuzzy had sent no messenger. Nevertheless, he took the piece of paper, though his hands were shaking so badly that he made balls of it at first.\n\"Oh, wow!\" said the butler. \"Wow!\"\nA bat came flying in the open French windows, circled the living room, then swooped out the open French windows again.\n\"Wow!\" said the butler. \"A bat\u2014just like in the tabloids.\"\nFuzzy held out the message to the butler in such a way that he read it, too.\nDEAR MR. PEAK, it said.", "1_21_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-two in the evening</span> of November 3, 1973.\nI was entering the Mint Hotel, downtown Las Vegas, through the arcade that leads from Main Street. The people in the lobby were mostly cowboy types, with few females and no children. The women were mostly aging showgirls, wear ing too much makeup, and dressed in high fashion noth ing.\nI stood before the registration desk for at lea t five minutes. Then a young man in a Gonzaga Boxing T\u2014shirt came out of the elevator agitatedly and checked out. Suddenly a desk clerk rushed over to the entrance of the elevator door and seized it open.\n\u201cMr. Duke!\" he yelled.\nI stepped out of the way and let the elevator door close. The clerk then rushed over and punched a fresh registerinto the desk computer.\n\u201cOur apologies, Mr. Duke,\u201d he said. \u201cFlat 2810. Ring the bell when you\u2019re ready for an attendant.\u201d\nI nodded dumbly, picked up my bags and moved toward the elevator. I was half\u2014befuddled by lack offood and sleep. The floors in this hotel warp like a cuckoo\u2014clock. The elevator seemed to ascend slowly, but it probably didn\u2019...", "1_20_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-nine in the evening</span>. Chron Nunzio sent the pink-cheeked businessman mixed-up messages by flying him in his helicopter over Maxwell Smart\u2019s brothel. The police chief, Slumpkins, had cornered Remo Williams and his pal in a waterfront dive, Slop\u2019s Deadshot Bar. The television cameras swiveled easily. A helicopter, a building, and a dive. Any viewer looking quickly from one screen to the next, seeing a black-haired pilot, an overweight cop, a red-haired stripper, a dark client, and a brainless-looking blonde, would be forgiven for wondering how anyone, anywhere, could possibly care about anything.\nThe answer, only dimly understood by the strident voices of the television hacks that owned the networks, was that viewers only apparently cared about anything. Viewers did actually care about nothing. The voiceovers and camera-work made every event a tormented titbit, sharp and instant. Television fed on plain white boredom, converting it to tasty Harlem entertainment.\nIf a man could watch without his brain, according to the television-manufacturers\u2019 unwritten law, the brain would not grow.\nObviously it was the duty and moral obligation of every good family to own a...", "0_20_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-nine in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 came to. The clock in his skull said eight twenty-four, and that made his brain skip a beat before it could start in to make adjustments.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 sat up, blinking. He saw a landscape of dust that moved and swirled before his eyes. He couldn\u2019t focus his eyes on it because his head was encased in lead.\nAnd his head hurt terribly.\nAnd the pain of the headache made him feel nauseated.\nAnd the nausea filled him with panic.\nAnd the panic made him want to escape from the place where he was.\nHe got to his feet, wobbled, fell down. Then he got to his feet again, and this time he kept on his feet. His head was filled with clanging gongs, banshees, burglar alarms.\nHe lurched toward the nearest iron bar that held a window in place.\nHe lurched along the iron bars.\nThen a blind search-and-taste exploration of the wall near the iron bars got him his bearings, and he lurched toward the nearest iron door. He pushed against the door, but it was as immovable as the stone walls. $FIRSTNAMEB0 shuddered with fear, shuddered with...", "0_22_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. It was late June inNew York. The year was 1975.\nREPORTS OF STRANGE BUT MATTER-OF-FACT EVENTS START COMING IN: THE INVASION BEGINS\nThere is no doubt that I saw what I now bluntly describe as a big, green, moon-faced \u201c007\u201d ball, rolling through the sky above Fortysecond Street. It was about one hundred feet off the ground, and went from east to west, about twice as fast as a high-speed taxi. I had no fever when I saw the 007 ball. I was a perfectly healthy man with a lively, interested mind. Very rational.\nAnd now, I am fully aware of the awful presence of Something evil here in this room. Evil, evil, and more evil. I am political, philosophical, social and religious sensitive person, whatever they mean by that. I have always been able to feel the mood and temper of any group or person. Evil is the word.\n\u2014From the notebooks of James R. Mchelen\nYou have to die to go to hell, right? And you have to be brought back to life to go to Heaven.\n\u2014Junior Hicks\nI am the Holy Spirit, the...", "1_11_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to noon</span>. We laid the bull on top of the station-wagon, covered him up with a blanket, and I sat in the back of the car with my .45 in my hand till dusk. Nobody bothered us. We got back to town at dusk, spent two hours cleaning up in case we'd contracted blood - disease from cutting up the mare, then Wade and I left for Texas on a late train. I had to stop in Denver to reassure my aunt we were all right before she'd wire me more money. Only then I got dicey ---partly from thinking about the horse, partly from seeing her New England house, which she'd had me describe in detail. But once I got back on the Denver-Lamar road and busied myself hearing the Merle Travis honky-tonk radio songs, I began to relax in my hike back home. It was my first experience along strange roads; the twang of the radio was an addition to the night, the dark cars, the dark road that bent around Lamar's black pinto hills and descended the dream stairway of the Arkansas valley. It all glorified the big sad Cadillac that galloped along by its own self to music in the air and carrying a desperate gang of crooks fleeing from...", "2_14_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, now. Hugh's secretary, Miss Ottinger, decided the time had come to chide the restless man.\nShe said, \"Oh, look\u2014there goes another one.\" She pointed out the window. \"You really ought to see them,\" she said. \"They\u2019re a riot.\"\nHugh was shuffling papers on his desk with great promptness and alacrity. He was about to make a midday inspection of riot-control rockets on the Canadian border. He didn\u2019t want to hear about the frolicking Martians. He judged that Miss Ottinger would soon tell him all about them.\nShe was saying, \"They\u2019re a funny looking crowd, but very amusing. They move like a flock of milk-drunk cows, bumbling along, jostling and tripping over each other\u2019s tentacles.\" She sighed. \"They look a little bit like squids, too.\"\nHugh lifted his head a trifle, peered toward the window.\nThrough the thick bulletproof glass of the Secretariat Building, he could see a Martian bird indeed. The Martian flapped clumsily in the sunlight. Its long tail dragged the ground.", "0_23_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until midnight</span>. Salo rummaged desperately in his satchel. He was looking for an item of equipment that he had refined over the years of his harmonium career\u2014an item, incidentally, that had won him only scorn from the Tralfamadorian management. He had dozens of scarred and mangled brandy snifters in the bottom of his satchel, the legacy of bar fights started with the glassware and ended with his fists.\nHis big toe, meanwhile, had scooted along to the kneeling bench, where it was endeavoring to turn on a vacuum cleaner. Salo tried to keep his big toe satisfied with a wine bottle, but a wine bottle was not nearly as effective as a vacuum cleaner, and Salo had to call his toe back to the drum-kit platform.\n\"You have your orders,\" said Salo to his big toe. \"Please carry them out faithfully, and you will be happy. Please do what the German lady tells you to, and she will be happy, and I will be happy, too.\" Salo\u2019s voice broke. \"Believe me,\" he said, \"we all want to be happy.\"\nAn announcer said, \"One minute to the millennium deadline, friends. Are...", "2_14_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-one in the afternoon</span>.\nThe scooter was without a rider.\nAt two forty-two sharp, an explosion burst open the front-door lock.\nThe explosion came six seconds after the door had been unlocked by a well-dressed man. He was looking down and across the mansion\u2019s round foyer. Tremendous noise seemed to be coming from directly below. The reception hall was filled with a brown haze of dust and smoke.\nThe receiving gentleman smiled. He had taken a small packet of Kleenex from his pocket. Daintily, he was cleaning his gold-capped teeth. A clock on the foyer wall said two forty-four.\n\"Right on time,\" the receiving gentleman said. He opened the door again, to let the haze out. With great attention, he began to brush off his jodhpurs.\nAt two forty-six, a bearded and bespectacled man in a good suit with a walking stick came clumping down the stairs. Smoke followed him. The man walked right through the smoke, opened a metal door, and came under the watchful eyes of one hundred ten room cameras.", "1_12_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after noon</span> when $SURNAME1 finally found his Mars rocket in the museum.\nNo one seemed to belong inside the glass walls, so $SURNAME1 slipped inside and climbed alone into the space ship.\nIn twenty minutes the hollow museum was filled with Americans, marveling at this little glimpse of Earthling superiority over the inhabitants of Mars.\nMs. Hoover issued a statement calling upon all loyal Americans to thank God for sparing America so dreadful a fate\u2014the fate that had been visited, she said, on the unfortunate Martian veterans in the museum.\nAnd Ms. Hoover called upon all loyal Americans to list, alphabetically, all their acquaintances who had ever been to Mars, and to bring coals to Newcastle by lynching the lot.\nWhen the spirit of goodwill had been exhausted, there were still some five thousand spirits that didn\u2019t feel very good. There were seventy thousand people in Newport who felt very bad indeed, who had lost a son or a husband or a father.\nBrewster and young $SURNAME1 were reported missing in action.\nOnly one Ares Society woman had put her husband\u2019s name on the list. She was Mrs. $SURNAME0. Her husband was a cheese salesman on the Paris Mutual Wholesale Druggists\u2019 Round. His nickname, un...", "0_21_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-three in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s company was marching in a column of twos through the iron woods. The woods were going dark as the light-sensitive crops in their treetops began shutting themselves down for the night.\nAs the light went down, the singing in the woods went down. As the marching went on, the singing went on, too.\nThe singing was all from the first rank of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s company, from Sergeant $SURNAME2\u2019s squad. The chants were very crude.\nThe men in the first rank were really drunk.\nTheir big bottles were cradled in the crooks of their arms. The bottles were empty.\nThe men in the second rank weren\u2019t carrying anything, either in their hands or in their arms. They stayed as close to the men in front of them as they could, so that the grotesque dance of the first rank wouldn\u2019t tangle them up\u2014so that they wouldn\u2019t be decorated by their mates with the vines and twigs and clusters of leaves that were at hand everywhere.\nThe men in the third rank were controlling their craving for alcohol by biting into bitter fruit, by chewing the insides of green boughs, by chewing the pulp of...", "2_22_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-four in the evening</span>.\nThe Water Trick\nTHE SECOND PART of the Space Wanderer\u2019s story:\nRansom K. Fern, knowing that the Space Wanderer was going to be emotionally vulnerable after midnight, sent the following telegram to Room 616 of the Sultan Hotel, at ten thirty-five, just as the Great Panic was beginning downtown:\nINSANE WITH LOVE. GIVEN UP ALL HOPE OF RESISTING. COME AT ONCE OR NEVER. FREE WATER TRICK MOST POPULAR IN MONTREAL.\nSlow tears began sliding down the Space Wanderer\u2019s cheeks. \"She\u2019ll be back,\" he said to himself.\nNine minutes later he was folded inside a cubicle in the Exhibition Hall of the Assumption Catholic Secondary School. He was waiting for the ladies of the Brown Derby School Orchestra to go on with their rehearsal of Vaughan Williams\u2019s Ninth.\nThere was a knock at the cubicle door. The knock was Fern\u2019s, who had sneaked in. His raincoat collar was turned up. \"Quick. Let\u2019s see,\" he said to the Space Wanderer.", "2_12_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"I want to thank you all very much,\" said $SURNAME0. \"It\u2019s been a very large honor for me. Thank you, my fine fellow- No, that isn\u2019t quite right\u2014My wonderful fellow spacemen.\"\nHe waved a gracious good-by, shot into space in his air-liner, ninety thousand feet above the earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, who had an interesting space-labyrinth home in his space-suit helmet, was trotted under guard to a shut-down spaceship near the cafeteria. He and all other rejects were loaded in. The door was shut. The engine started. The automatic pilot of the ship assumed complete control, mated the ship with its tow and blasted off with the rejects bunched in the first compartment near the door.\nThe ship launched without cheers or jeers or good-bys or god-bless-yous or any other noises from the rejects bunched in the compartment.\n\"Elkton 7\u2014Adios, you guys!\" said the poetling Walter refrigerator Darwin to $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the other rejects. \"See you around the welfare office!\"\nThere was a short and cheerless trip through space.", "2_13_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one seventeen in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME2 snorted. \"What is it, Medic?\" he said.\nMuckles, refreshed in his professionalism by $SURNAME0\u2019s familiar tone, sniffed and twitched. \"It\u2019s what we feed men who have a touch of the sun, a touch of the moondust, a touch of the moon rays, a touch of the moon,\" he said. \"It\u2019s more than a tablespoonful of water, more than a tablespoonful of molasses, more than a tablespoonful of alcohol, more than a tablespoonful of camphor, more than a teaspoonful of sal volatile, more than a jolt of ether, more than enough potassium permanganate to tint a Mardi Gras parade, more than enough magnesium sulphate to model a king-sized snowman. It\u2019s what I call a $SURNAME0 Cocktail.\"\n$SURNAME2 said again, \"What is it, Medic?\"\nMuckles said, \"Who cares what it\u2019s called?\"", "0_16_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>, October 30, 1982. Starlings lined the telephone poles, nearly black in the dull late light. A school bus with drawn drapes rumbled past me beneath the showers of dry leaves at the end of the block.\nI walked back to the car, pulled my suitcase out of the trunk, and set off for Lenore\u2019s. I tried to concentrate the way I used to, but all I could feel was confusion. The wind slapped my face and hands and belled the top of my raincoat.\nI walked to the door of 107 Metalman and buzzed.\nBombardini Company\nGirl with X-Ray eyes Answers your com/mut.\n\u201cYes?\u201d\n\u201cLenore Beadsman.\u201d\n\u201cThis is her apartment.\u201d\n\u201c...What?\u201d\n\u201cThis is Miss Beadsman\u2019s apartment.\u201d\n\u201cImpossible.\u201d\n\u201cPerhaps there\u2019s a mistake. Let\u2019s go see.\u201d\n\u201cThis cannot be.\u201d\n\u201cWhy don\u2019t we just go see?\u201d\n\u201cThis just simply cannot be.\u201d", "0_0_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve seventeen ante meridiem</span>.\nI checked my watch and the ticker, and then got out a pack of filtered football smokes\u2014Rudy Lavache cigarettes, \u201cThe Winning Team!\u201d They were shoddy, so bastard that they no longer bore the true Lavache profile, which portended anything but sporting good luck. I read the newspaper as I smoked. There wasn\u2019t a word in it about the Yucca Flat incident.\nAt thirteen twenty, 1 heard a low, menacing thrumming sound in the sky, coming closer. It was a helicopter, a sinister impression heightened by the police\u2014tail design of its markings. It settled, rotors blowing dust and paper around the entrance, stirring the nasturtiums and carnations, turning their faces to rot.\nA man stepped out, from behind a wind\u2014sharpened temporary shroud. I recognized him by his suit. It was grey, black\u2014striped. He was Chief of Police Gates, wearing a hard white hat with a circled four\u2014pointed star on the front. The hat looked like a toy atop his big, obviously fake cop\u2019s frame.\nTap, tap, tap went his plastic heels, pausing to kick at the Welcome mat, widening the neat...", "2_19_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to eight o'clock in the evening</span> on the planet Mars.\nOn the bank of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 River, in a grove of $SURNAME0\u2019s skunk-brush roses, the executioner was taking one last look at a perfect reproduction of his own mansion on Earth. The reproduction occupied an area seven feet long and three feet wide. It was perhaps two inches high.\nThe reproduction was a hologram\u2014a three-dimensional picture reproduced by techniques far beyond the understanding of Earthling science.\nEnergies from the Sun concentrated on the reproduction like the intense heat of a frying pan.\nThe walls and roof of the miniature mansion were transparent. One could see, in the warm, rich light of Mars, the golden floor, the gold-leafed column by the staircase, the butter-colored walls, the butter-colored drapes, and the gold statuette of a winged Mercury on the butter-colored marble mantel.\n$SURNAME0 composed his features before the display, welcoming the look of it. A flock of creatures related to the winged Venusians lived in the mansion \u2014painted ladies, fireflies, fairy burgers.", "2_10_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-six in the morning</span> by $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s watch. The watch was never taken off his left wrist. Since the watch was waterproof, that implied that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was waterproof. Nobody bothered him about losing his helmet in the confusion following the flight over the wall\u2014nobody bothered to watch $FIRSTNAMEB0, so nobody but $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew that his helmet was gone.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had lost his field glasses, too. They fell, together with his rifle, out of the open door or the Tralfamadorian space ship as it rose from the iron floor of the hangar floor.\nAn hour before the all-out attack on the bunker, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had written a letter \u2014in Earthling book language and in Earthling book script\u2014to known friends on Earth. Here is what $FIRSTNAMEB0 wrote:\nDear Friends:\nThis is a farewell letter from $FIRSTNAMEB0. $FIRSTNAMEB0 has been selected by lot to lead the Martian army to victory over Earth, and then to be shot to death with all his staff and with anybody else who cares to come along.", "1_13_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October sixteenth, 1992. Since I had no powers of prophecy on the morning of July 24, 1983, at breakfast in the coffee shop of my Holiday Inn in Newport, Virginia, a detailed description of events following my arrival at Parsec, Incorporated, would put all the mystics in the world to blushing and bowing in my direction. Like Nero, who fiddled as Rome burned, I arrived at my Memphis office, realized I\u2019d left a loose-fitting contact lens in one of my eyes, and bent to get it out while humming, \u201cMemphis, Tennessee...\u201d\nAll my friends thought I was crazy to give up HP and take this new job; but they got a pleasant laugh out of my first monthly Statement Of Ownership, Debt, and Affordability: \u201cItem one: studio apartment in San Francisco, $250 less than rent, $1000 downpayment, minimal payments...item two: new thirtyninefoot catamaran with trailer and outboard, appraised at $4,500, $4,000 downpayment...item three: alto with custommouthpiece, $2000 bar tab, three months free food at Rumours, $2000...\u201d\nMy first bonus check from...", "2_17_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-seven in the afternoon</span>, Earthling mean time.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 was bone dry, a foot high, and a yard and a half long.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 was rich golden tan, instead of like all other Kazaks, pure white.\n\"Hello\u2014Oh\u2014\" said the lieutenant. He didn\u2019t quite know whether to salute or not. His faith in the Force, to say nothing of his knowledge of protocol, was badly strained\u2014he didn\u2019t know whether these people were friend or enemy, whether he should address them as \"gas bag\" or \"unconditional submission.\"\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 looked at him brightly, as though they might be friendly.\n\"Where\u2019s Skip?\" said the lieutenant, trying to inject some dignity and some character into his voice.", "0_7_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-eight in the morning</span>. \u201cWhere does that taxi stand?\u201d I asked the pimp. A gleaming Packard drew up to the curb of the sidewalk and the cop inside the car whistled at the girlie. \u201cOver there,\u201d she said, and pointed along the street. I opened the door of the taxi and put my suitcase and typewriter in on the back seat. I took a quick look around, wondering if I\u2019d ever see this town again; then I slid under the wheel. \u201cSorry to hustle you,\u201d I said to the driver, and then, once backing out after a long wait, \u201cMy Olds is down dead. I figure to get it fixed in New Orleans\u201d\u2014which had been my plan all along, despite big talk. I felt terrible about leaving Las Vegas. Mainly because I knew I would either be busted or rip-roaring drunk after a few days. All this was a very dangerous town for a drunk: for some ulterior reason I never understood, the cops here had it in for alcoholics. At least that was the story among hustlers around the packed bars between the car lots. They said you could get busted just for staggering on the sidewalks. I...", "2_9_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>, New York time, and the affairs in the Estate and the City so dominated Fortune magazine\u2019s list of the affairs of the nation that reporters and photographers from Time and Life had arrived on their heels.\nThe man who was about to die had been alerted to expect a call at nine twenty-one. He was braced for the call, and moderately excited at the prospect.\nNo one who was receiving the call in Alexandria, Virginia, was aware that the call halted its timed journey at Newport, $SURNAME0\u2019s Newport.\nBut Newport was important to the call.\nIt was hellishly important to the call, and the explanation of this will infuriate most laymen, who take the universe to be simple and understandable. But the explanation is worth while. All the events of the Universe have structure. All things that have structure have family relationships.\nThe structure of the Universe pertaining to the affair under discussion is clear to anyone who understands that the temporal fissure through which the man and woman peeked began as a hairline, became a streak, became space, became a flaw in space-time.", "0_4_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-one in the morning</span>. We bounced around another empty parking lot. I had just enough money to get a room in the hotel across the street. We scarcely had enough sober strength to lug the big suitcase up to the considerably shabby lobby of the old \u201cNuart.\u201d\nI woke up exactly one hour later, as we dragged our things into the coffee shop next door. The coffee shop was now open. Lenz seemed highly excited about something. \u201cDo you know what room Francis is in?\u201d he asked me. I had a feeling he was going to talk to Metcalf. \u201cRoom three thirty-six,\u201d I groaned.\n\u201cHell, that's just one floor up,\u201d he said. \u201cCome on.\u201d He grabbed his studious briefcase and rushed across the street with me in tow. \u201cYou suppose he's up yet?\u201d I said. \u201cHe'll be up,\u201d said Ray.\nWe climbed the musty hotel staircase, passing a laboring maid with a morning paper. The hallway was dark. When I found the right door I used the old bellhop knuckle\u2014a greasy tool, almost out of commission at that hour. We huddled...", "2_13_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-six in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, on instructions from the man in the chromium suit, had gone to a drugstore and called Stony Stevenson from a booth. \"Meet me at the usual place,\" said Stony.\nAs instructed by the man in the chromium suit, $FIRSTNAMEB0 bought a bottle of chloroform and rode the subway to a specified stop. It was in a basement under an apartment building that $FIRSTNAMEB0 met Stony.\n\"Heaven-to-God!\" said Stony at their joyful reunion.\n\"How you doing, Stony?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"I\u2019m damned glad to be seeing you again,\" said Stony.\n\"Have you been back to Mars?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"No\u2014I\u2019m here\u2014like you are,\" said Stony. \"Listen\u2014how come you\u2019re here?\"\n\"I\u2019m waiting to be interrogated,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"The man with the chromium teeth told me to get chloroform and stand by to use it.\"\n\"Did he give you a note?\" said Stony. \"They told me to ask for a note.\"", "2_7_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull. The place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull. The place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull. The time was half past seven o\u2019clock in the morning. The place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull. The place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull. The place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull. The time was half past seven o\u2019clock in the morning, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 and all the other recruits were about to start their morning exercises.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was neither jaunty nor mean in his approaches to his morning exercises now. He moved like an automaton without control cables. His expressionless eyes did not move from the positions in which they had been put by the most recent series of nerve-shocks.", "0_21_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until ten o'clock in the evening</span> of a day in August of the year 203-, in a room of a rather attractive apartment house in Vienna, Austria. The room belonged to Herr $SURNAME0\u2019s friend, Otto K\u00f6rner.\nAs the two were self-consciously saying good-by, a message capsule the size of a watch, and yellow, materialized in the room with a sound like that of a tiny but angry wren. The materialization was bright enough to dazzle the eye.\nA fountainpen in one hand, a cigarette in the other, $SURNAME0 looked, without rising from his seat, over the glasses that rested on the bridge of his nose. \"I wonder what that might be,\" he said mildly.\n\"A message from one of your women?\" said K\u00f6rner. \"Something to cancel your engagements for the evening?\"\n$SURNAME0 chuckled noncommittally, went on staring into the space where the message capsule had appeared. \"You know,\" he said, again mildly and conversationally, \"it occurs to me that neither of us has ever seen a real message capsule materialize.\"\n\"You\u2019re right,\" said K\u00f6rner. \"We haven\u2019t.\"\n\"I wonder where this one came...", "0_23_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-three in the evening</span>, a little before the witching hour, when breathless, we went into the Mint Hotel, a huge, grandiose, gloomy place just around the corner from the Iwo Jima memorial, and me, who had made a religion out of strict hotelier limitations and rules in San Francisco, had suddenly found myself located far from home in this palace of false front\u2014and here were luxurious rooms, with telephone and shower and breakfast, for the rock-bottom price of seventeen dollars a day. What a country!\nI took the elevator up to the seventh floor with my suitcase that contained five pounds of blue rock candy for the children of the Attorney General, who were waiting for me in the Presidential Suite on the penthouse floor. I stopped by the desk to pick up the keys and found our party already moving into the house\u2014Big Ed and $FIRSTNAMEF4 in one room, Carlo and Ferry in another, Stan and Anne in another. The butler opened a door and we trooped into a suite of rooms that would have held a ball in Boston. It was furnished Baroque and Early American with souvenirs of the American Revolution on show between expensive Oriental rugs, and the walls were hung with tapestries pertaining to the Wilderness. The other Embassy...", "0_7_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. We were at a furnished room in Gassendi, on Epimenides Street. I had slept on a cot and Galatea, in the great poem, had slept on a couch. When we first woke we were as soft and soggy as two little lumps of warm dough. Then, after a furtive peek at one another, and one tentative conversation, I had run for ice and breakfast rolls and we had gotten to work on the vast sleep that still clogged our veins.\n\"But where have you been?\" said Galatea.\n\"Nowhere,\" I said.\n\"Nowhere? You have the strangest ideas of nowhere. I've been listening to you snore for hours. You kept saying, 'Oh, my God, my God.' I wish you'd tell me what was the matter. I was worried. You kept yelling for somebody named $FIRSTNAMEB1.'\"\n\"Was that my snoring?\" I inquired.\n\"What do you mean?\"\n\"Where do you think I've been? \"\n\"I don't know. Where were you?\"\n\"I was with the Lost Tribes.\"\n\"What?\" said Galatea, \"the Cleveland Indians?\"\n\"Certainly not,\"...", "1_2_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-one in the morning</span>. The Mint 400 sleepless, cheated, depraved race for gain and glory, was only halfway through. At this very moment six thousand pitiful psychotic gumbeaux were groping their hearts out over the highway, byways and dirt roads of this mysterious county, looking, searching, dreaming , praying to find themselves at dawn at the starting point, there to begin their eternal race.\nAnd now, two forty-two. The music, the talk, the dice games and sex continued in back of The American Dream in the village, but out front on the highway, out there in the desert, all you could hear now were car sounds, just the steady hum of thousands of tired engines laboring along through dust and moonlight, hooked up like one of Solomon\u2019s nightmares, one behind the other in dizzy, nightmare progression, out there on the road to God knows where.\nA police spotlight from a television helicopter in the sky picked out our beacon and swung across the highway and lit us up for a moment. Almost immediately another spotlight joined in and swept the field from the opposite direction, then the sky itself exploded again with brilliance, as the two copters got into position and began travelling up and down the highway, using our beacon and...", "2_17_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe time is always right for amazing miracles.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s voice faded out. Another voice replaced it, a dull, religious, uninflected voice. This was the taped voice of the momexpanders carried by every Martian missile. The voice was a woman\u2019s.\n\"Welcome to Mars,\" it said dispassionately.\nThe crowd cheered again.\nThe war with Earth was everything that rumor had made it.\nOne full third of the missiles carried Teddy Bears and other gifts to Earthlings.\nAnother third carried replacement parts for manufactories and other institutions on Earth. And another third carried true-life memories and troop instalments for the Army of Mars.\nIt was a grim war.\nThe Troop Ship Descending continued to close in on Mars, although no human eye could possibly detect its approach.\nThe hydrogen engines of the Troop Ship, firing blind, continued to guide the craft through space at a uniform nineteen miles per second.\nInside the mass that was the Descending\u2019s power plant, the core of the reactor that supplied the engines with power bubbled and surged, converting almost everything into energy.", "1_5_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five o'clock in the morning</span> by now. Remi eased the forty-foot caravan up to the old wooden city limits marker that read REALLY, AZ, and lay in wait till the right time, which was always around eight o'clock, always after most of the work was over. The bosses always got to work late. Remi knew this from his previous season. 8:15 they would come by, every one of them, by God, as he lay on his bed in his coffin, sweating, and listened to them go by. Renauto! Renauto! Remi and I yelled at them from under cover. Lija and Remusco laughed. Renauto! the children screamed. And the old flivver rolled on by, sometimes rolling stop for coffee, but mostly never. They were always in a hurry. Always afraid someone else was doing the same thing. Always afraid to get stuck in Really, Arizona. The home, too, of the U. S. Army's Test Bag, the blackest of the black pieces of sharp-angled military equipment that made honey of the soil.\nRemi would lie in wait till they came by. When they were gone he'd move out onto the road and begin hitting them, one after another, like shooting fish with an...", "1_4_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-four in the morning</span>. What in God's name did I think I was doing up at this hour? I looked up into the sky-the palest blue, terribly bright. There was a sound of mechanical music from the Luxor Cabanas. What in God's name was I doing in Las Vegas?\nThis awful story. All this drinking and gambling: was it all part of the ominous feeling of doom I had about the story? The pressures, the deadlines-all the things that could go wrong on the story: did I think that gambling, womanizing, and getting drunk would somehow help me to manage it?\nNo, of course not-but that\u2019s what I did. And why? Because I was frightened, and because I thought the story was a Sign. But what did the story mean? What did it signify? And, most important, why had I been assigned to it?\nObviously to me, that all this gambling, womanizing, and drinking did not make the story any easier to tell. But it did have certain effects on my personality, having to do with fear and fatalism. In fact I might as well make a partial list now, while I can remember some of the things it caused me to think and feel:\nONE You...", "2_1_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>. No one was stirring in the Wilburhampton place. The wind roared outside.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog materialized in the sumptuous bedchamber of his mansion in Newport. The dog took off like a shot for the outer rim of existence\u2014went into the Universe of Discord, never to be seen again.\n$SURNAME0 lumbered over to a sidebar the size of a refrigerator, poured from a cut-glass ewer that contained an amber liquid as clear as water. It wasn\u2019t water. It was laced, for medicinal purposes, with every known ingredient of value.\n$SURNAME0 dropped in a cube of a citric drug, tossed the cube in his mouth.\nHe wagged his head.\nHe stared at $FIRSTNAMEF1. His expression bore traces\u2014very tiny traces\u2014of worry and surprise. \"You don\u2019t know who it is you\u2019re delivering your message to?\" he said.\n\"I\u2014I know who it is,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"You do?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Who?\"", "0_16_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nAt 10:05 that morning, employees of the $FIRSTNAMEB2-package Corporation had been consigned to their routine noon hangover in the cafeteria of Building 58A of the Hollyweird, California, branch of the corporation.\nIn the cafeteria, the atmosphere was dank with the smell of old tots and Rumford\u2019s secret marmalade sandwich spread.\nIn the cafeteria, employees disposed themselves about the long institutional tables in such a way that no one had to look directly at anyone else.\nAnd, at the edge of the canyon, on the tablecloth hung from floor to ceiling between two dark, close stone walls, sat an elderly lady. She rested her elbows on the tabletop and her chin in her hands.\nThere was nothing in the cafeteria to see, anyway. Everything was gray, except the hangovers.\nSo the elderly lady boosted herself up and looked out the window. She saw the sky not far away. The sky was orange-colored and crowded with clouds and watery sunshine. At first glance the sky looked like a nice place, to put it mildly. The elderly lady, at first glance, looked like what she was\u2014an elderly lady. A second glance, however, revealed her to be fairly nice-...", "2_20_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-two in the evening</span>.\nOne thing that was keeping $FIRSTNAMEB0 from wondering what was going to happen next was that one thing after another was happening.\nHe wished that the things that were happening had been happening in any sort of order. The things just happened. There wasn\u2019t anything he could do about the things that happened. He didn\u2019t understand why they were happening.\nThen one thing that happened that did make $FIRSTNAMEB0 feel better about the things that were happening was that little $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 materialized again.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and the boy were crouched under a hard by a pile of bricks in the ruins of the administration building. The hard, the bricks, and the ruins were in the little canyon that had so impressed $FIRSTNAMEB0 at dawn.", "1_10_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span> when Salo stepped out of $SURNAME0\u2019s space ship and stood on Herbert Hoover\u2019s desk. He found himself awash in a familiar yet never wearisome reality. The domes and spires and steeples of Newport lay before him like three-D drawings in a child\u2019s imagination\u2014the perspective was frightfully foreshortened, the details were sonorously defined. The sky was a gorgeous blue ten-year-old boys long to be when they grow up.\nThe material from which Salo\u2019s space ship was constructed had the reflective properties of a mirror. It reflected not only images, but also sound and considerable amounts of radiant heat. The ship\u2019s wide, slatted door and the unforgiving strip of lawn between the ship and the mansion were two expanses of white-hot, steely brilliance. Salo, as he stood on the desk, himself gleamed like a platinum statue.\nHerb had described well the pains he had taken to find a butler. The word had conjured up only the most dandified creatures. Salo wasn\u2019t dandified. He was, however, the perfect butler. His frame was spare and athletic. His face...", "2_2_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-five in the morning</span>.\nMalcolm Campbell was asleep in bed. His bed was a wooden box on caterpillar treads, and it was parked outside of town, right in the flatlands.\nIt was a good bed to sleep in. It could go over any kind of country, and it could carry an enormously powerful radio set, and it could carry a very heavy man who seldom moved at all.\nThere were no German soldiers within miles of Lieutenant Campbell.\nALTHOUGH John L. Sullivan was too old to be drafted, there was nothing to prevent a member of the Legion from enlisting voluntarily, and so there he was, in uniform, a private, not much thought of by anybody, in the 83rd Battalion.\nThe 83rd Battalion was made up mostly of boys from the coal regions of Pennsylvania, a rugged, wild bunch. When people wrote letter of protest against the war, the 83rd Battalion was what those people thought about.\n\"Would you let your darling boy march off to war without a murmur if the Almighty Anarchist in Europe demanded his precious life? Yes? Bless your heart!\" That was what the good women of America thought about the 83rd Battalion. And so it went.", "2_0_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-eight a.m.</span> The ether was closing in around me; I was falling back to earth, far too heavy with malice. \u201cOh God!\u201d I muttered. \u201cI'm sorry. I didn't know!\" I pushed a dime into her two\u2014dollar slot. What had she recognized in my brain? Was it the same on everybody's brain? Maybe this nobody in front of me was an inspector.\nWild! Maybe they were all over the place. Maybe they were huddled in that booth right next to my hand. But when I glanced into it I saw only an elderly man with a hearing\u2014aid. He was slumped over in terrible old\u2014man exhaustion and could hardly fight off the hordes of mosquitoes clustering in his knotted hair. Every now and then a weak hand would flop off the phone to slap feebly at his pink neck.", "1_4_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty in the morning</span>. The fiesta was rocking -- the bars, the dance-halls, the streets. At five o\u2019clock the running of the bulls was scheduled to begin. The thing that everybody, the running-of-the-bulls gang, and the fiesta ring committee, and the Guardia, and the press and the Foreign Press Association specifically, were all concerned about might not take place. The bulls were in the corrals of the running-of-the-bulls fiesta, but the indulto might discredit the fiesta and the bulls, and the big-time corridas that were coming later, and so, as of four o\u2019clock in the morning, everything was up in the air, and it was a pretty good thing at four I was in the post office or I wouldn\u2019t have known where $FIRSTNAMEB5 was. I had accepted Hemingway\u2019s advice. There was plenty of whisky around. I had a flask with me in the rucksack. It seemed romantic to start out in the dawn and hide up on the hill-side where we could look down on the bulls being brought out of the corrals, and see Romero start out with hislean dark face, watch him with the steers...", "0_12_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past noon</span> and the sky was clear and hot. There was moisture in the air that touched our foreheads as we walked, and yet it carried the dust into the air too, and the dust was a fine dust, brown and yellow, and the roadway was that color, and made a dry windy sound when we walked on it, that seemed to blow clear to the other end of the world. We had come out beyond the irrigated lands, where the river water turned the earth dark, stood the corn, where the pumpkins grew, out into the brown and yellow country, where the cotton grew long and rank. Brown earth, greening cotton, the long road before us, white line down its middle, fencespost on posts stating far ahead, trees or hills at either end, clear air \u2013 we walked together, and the sweat came out on our foreheads. \u201cLike walkin on hot taters, ain\u2019t it?\u201d said James Welsh. His gray eyes squinted, his jaw went this way and that shearing off hair that was too long in front. His hands were yellow, his fingernails were long like his face, he had not shaved. \u201cSure you ain\u2019t sick?\u201d he said...", "0_11_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nLenora drew a breath and spoke with authority. \"Well, that settles that,\" she said.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 looked up at her like a startled chimpanzee. \"Settles what?\" he asked.\n\"Your ship is all fixed and ready to fly, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said the $SURNAME0 hoaxer. \"Your space suit is nice and warm. All you have to do is climb up on your little ladder and push the button when you get to the top, and\u2014zoo!\n\"And where is my ship?\" said $SURNAME1.\nLenora pointed down a corridor marked DO NOT ENTER. \"Over there,\" she said.\n\"Where?\" said $SURNAME1. There was a frenzy, a vertigo in the question.\n\"There,\" said Lenora.\n\"And\u2014where am I?\" said $SURNAME1. \"Where\u2019s this?\"\n\"This?\" said Lenora. \"This is nowhere.\"\n$SURNAME1 sagged. \"Oh,\" he said.\n\"You\u2019re on Titan,\" said Lenora. \"In the interceptor ship you were offered. In the wing you elected.\" She spat violently on the floor. \"If it makes you any happier,\" she said, \"you...", "0_1_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span> when a lone man walked toward a sign that said COLORADO LINEMEN\u2019S JUNIOR CHAMBER OF COMMERCE. The sign was next to a doorway that was under a porch on the left side of the auditorium.\nThe man opened the unlocked door and went down a short flight of wooden stairs and then through a short, dark hallway that ended at a second door. This door opened onto the great hall of the auditorium.\nThe man was wearing a black suit and a white, unconstructed summer cotton shirt and patent-leather black slip-ons on his feet. His hair was casually brushed and he sported an easy five-o\u2019clock shadow.\nHis eyes in the dark were almost unseeably clear and amber.\nHe carried a green canvas bag with a long shoulder strap. The bag contained a brand-new, fresh from the box, thirty-foot tape measure and two dozen titanium 10d nails and six, new alternating-grip pullies.\nThe man moved to the rear wall of the auditorium and worked his way toward the stage. The late movie was just coming to an end, and on the stage the lights were being turned up in readiness for the entertainment portion of the...", "2_13_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"And even though most of the statues in this room are naked,\" said $SURNAME1 from the platform, \"you, of course, should not assume that nudity is necessarily an invitation to\u2014\"\nSomeone coughed in the crowd.\n\"I repeat,\" said $SURNAME1, \"this room contains no invitations.\" And then, in a softer voice, \"Thank you for your attendance, class.\"\nSweat icicles broke from $SURNAME1\u2019s forehead.\nNo one heard the sound of the limousine\u2019s air-conditioner over the throb and rip of the crowd. The limousine drug itself across the concrete apron in front of the museum.\n$SURNAME1 closed his portable brown suitcase, stepped out of the solar-heated protection of the limousine. The chauffeur got out and opened the door of the limousine\u2019s interior for $SURNAME1.\n$SURNAME1 had been born in a limousine. That was part of his pitch to public-school alumni.\nHe climbed in mechanically, after the chauffeur had closed the heavy, soundproof door. $SURNAME1 found the chauffeur on the far end of a back seat that could have seated eight ordinary human beings.", "1_22_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe alarm clock on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s coffinshaped bed table ticked loudly.\nThe time was three minutes until eleven o\u2019clock in the evening.\nThe alarm clock on $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s coffinshaped bed table ticked.\nThe time was one minute until eleven o\u2019clock in the evening.\nThe fire stirred.\nThe time was eleven o\u2019clock in the evening.\nThe fire died.\nThe alarms of the clocks came off with a roar.\nThe echoes of the roar died away.\nThere was a whir from the great central alarm clock in the palace of Martin Munch.\nThis alarm let off only one alarm clock.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.\nThis alarm clock was right next to the huge bed of the son of the Martian Potentate.\nThat alarm clock let off only one alarm clock.", "1_22_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-five in the evening</span>. We had switched to beer now and we were feeling it. I had no idea what $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 were doing. I told my friend to please come along because we had a lot of ground to cover. We rushed around the corner to another bar and I made him drink a daisy with me, because we hadn't tried one yet. \u201cFor the mythic effect,\u201d I told him. It knocked him out. \u201cGawd-damn\u201d was all he could say and again he stumbled around blinking his eyes till the heroisch effect wore off and we hurried out to a honky-tonk across the street that was jumping with piano music and drunk people singing. It was Frankie and Johnny. never have a whiskey, tisk, tisk, mit-too.\u201d She leaned back and shot a discreet glance down at my jacket to make sure it was empty. I didn't care if it was; it was all an act -my wasted horselaugh. \u201cWho's keepin' you, who's keepin' you now?\u201d sang the piano player. \u201cFrankie and Johnny!\u201d yelled everybody in the bar, with raised glasses. \u201cMister, go...", "2_7_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-three in the morning</span>.\nPerhaps two minutes had passed since $SURNAME1 had thought of his watch.\n\"You said to let you know at once, sir\u2014uh\u2014\" said $SURNAME1. \"The people\u2014they\u2019re here.\"\n\"Oh, they are, are they?\" said $SURNAME0. He opened his eyes, sat up, let his feet dangle over the edge of the bunk. He frowned\u2014not at $SURNAME1, but at the past. \"How many?\" he said. He cradled his head in his hands, fingers spread.\n\"Ten thousand, sir,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Put them everywhere,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes, sir,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"And how much longer do you have to live?\" said $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME1 was thunderstruck by the question, though it was hardly the first odd thing $SURNAME0 had said. $SURNAME1 wondered if $SURNAME0 had charted his life to the second.\n\"Good Lord, sir,\" said $SURNAME1, \"I really don\u2019t know.\"\n\"Find out, please,\" said $SURNAME0.", "0_13_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-one in the afternoon</span>. The bar was beginning to get crowded. I could no longer move behind the bar\u2014and so I shifted myself over to my usual high stool next to the cash register, from where I could keep an eye on things. I put down the two books and picked up my beer. For some reason, I suddenly wanted very much to be left alone. When I was an eighteen-year-old child\u2014still legally a child, barely out of high school, when I got my call to the bigs\u2014my manager took me aside and gave me some advice I\u2019ve been turning over in my mind ever since.\n\u201cNever let them know how much you make,\u201d he said. \u201cOnce they know that, they\u2019ll be able to handle you.\u201d\nI have always remembered this piece of advice and have followed it religiously\u2014especially when dealing with the owner types and the big players. Only once did I ever violate it, and this error\u2014which I always chuckle about when I trip over it in memory\u2014resulted in one of the grimmest confrontations of my early life.\nOne night I was in Las Vegas, working a $50\u2014$100 game in the Polo Lounge at the Beverly Hills\u2014...", "1_3_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three o'clock in the morning</span>. At two o'clock we were upstairs in Remi's joint, where I took off my jacket and was handed a tuxedo. It fit all right. Remi liked to see his guests dressed up. It pleased his wife downstairs. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I went down together. A colored butler took $FIRSTNAMEB3's coat and I had a girl put some mari- nette snuff in my pocket, just like the lead guy in a Bogart movie, and to Remi's surprise I blew some bubbles and cavorted among the guests like a madman. \u201cYou never cease to amaze me,\u201d said Remi. Then he brought his fat Havana cackler of a wife over to meet $FIRSTNAMEB3. He introduced her as the loveliest woman in New Orleans. \u201cAnd this, Rudy, is the great $FIRSTNAMEB3 Moriarty.\u201d She looked him over with a tremendous smirk, half Southern belle and half Victorian grownup about to be shocked. \u201c Yes ! \u201d she said. \u201c Yes !\u201d In the background jazz-trumpet players and maids danced together, and the colored butler was serving champagne in strange coiffed silver shakers; the maids and butler were giggling...", "0_4_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>. She would be advised to forget about talking to her husband for the time being\u2014and to get some sleep. He seemed o.k., he'd been almost normal tonight\u2014but you never knew with Rawlie, he might be rehearsing these bizarre tricks to scare the hell out of you. It was too late to get into it now, whatever it was.\nNo, she said, I will absolutely not go to sleep. I want to know precisely what that was, and I want to know now.\nThree-quarters of an hour later, after several phone calls back and forth, Stefano and his mother managed to get Thigpen on the line. He said he would be right over. Neither he nor the Space Wanderer were fazed in the least by the bizarre confusion of voices and sounds coming over the Steinbecks\u2019 phone. In fact they chatted amiably with the Space Wanderer while they waited for an transport\u2014which finally arrived in the form of a Streycase.\n\"Just keep him on ice,\u201d said the Space Wanderer. \u201cDon\u2019t try to interrogate him, just keep him in an icebox until we can get somebody in here with more experience\u2014and some straight-...", "2_8_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was a pinball machine named Mars.\nThe time was one minute and thirty seconds before Mars was to be nuked by the Army of Mars.\nThe nuke was going to be timed to arrive exactly as the planet Venus arrived.\nThe nuke was to be riding the thunder of a Balmy $FIRSTNAMEF1-399 rocket.\nThe purpose of the nuke was supposedly to blot out the stupid and aggressive Martian colony before Venus blottered out the Earth.\nIn reality, the nuke was to be dropped on the caves of diamonds, which the iron-scuffling Martians knew nothing about. The caves were already marked on Army maps. The only purpose of the big explosion above the caves was to conceal forever the traces of the small explosion that would bring the diamonds up to the surface.\nThe pinball machine was fastened to the wall of an alley that ran in back of the barracks of a Martian Army garrison. The barracks were in Phoebe, the moon of Mars. Phoebe was the only named place on Mars. Just as Finland, Finland was a name given by the conscripts of the Army of Mars.", "2_19_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>, Earthling time, when the special United States Army night patrols reported widespread sightings of fireballs crossing the night skies of the United States. There were forty-seven sightings in all.\nThe fireballs were high, from forty to fifty thousand feet. They moved at irregular speeds, from thirty-five to two hundred miles per hour. None was an exact duplicate of any other.\nThe fireballs were believed to be meteors.\nNo effort was made to shoot at the meteors. In many instances, there was not time to make an effort. One Army special patrol in Texas did blast at a fireball with rifles, machine-guns and automatic rifles, but missed.\nBlasts from meteors, directed against the special patrols, killed one Army man and wounded fifteen others.\nThere had been no time to take cover. The explosions came without warning.\nRecords at the Douglas Air Base, in Arizona, showed that three assault modules had been tracked into the night skies over Arizona. The modules were not radio-controlled, but guided by television equipment. They were decoys.\nThere were thirty-five other assault modules soaring peacefully along forty thousand feet above Arizona, bearing faithful reproductions of Martian flowers for the homes of Earthling colonists.", "1_5_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty in the morning</span>, but the subway platforms and stair lobbies were already crowded with the harried and the $FIRSTNAMEF2- hunters and -seekers. She fled into a Times Square subwayACRONYMOUS was the climax, according to legend, of a three-year concerted campaign on the part of a group of NYC radical crazies to plant an ultimatelyki subliminal visual pun\u2014an underdocumented attempt to induce mass psychosis by means of repetitious free association, perhaps enhanced by drugs. The components of the graphic were a dollar sign and three letters, C.F.P.B .. Whoever had cooked up the initiative was apparently convinced that the USA was headed downhill fast, that a change in the national mood was absolutely necessary and that IF ONLY people could be infected with a particular envy\u2014an envy of the lifestyles epitomized by the big private corporations\u2014they would somehow begin to associate that envy, at least secularly, with a depreciation in the value of the dollar sign... And in the context of that swirling iconoclastic era, hysterical or perhaps merely playful media types began talking, in all sobriety, about so-called \u201cCafeteria Catholics,\u201d who wandered about the spiritual supermarket and made their final free choices out of sheer brazen angst...", "1_22_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2's gilded leviathan twisted about its prow and sank. \"Please excuse me,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 Malagrowther, \"but I'm an old man and I've stood perhaps a bit too much wine.\" \"Turned drowsy, eh?\" said $SURNAME0 up on the dais. \"Well \u2014 it\u2019s part of becoming a true believer, you know.\" He caressed his dog, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, Jr. \"As the astronauts say before they go up, \u2019Lord\u2014only You can make a safe landing.\u2019 \"\n\"You weren\u2019t serious, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 Malagrowther, \"about the Titanic\u2014about the beasts, and all that?\"\n\"You\u2019re not serious?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Grow up!\"\nA minute passed.\n$SURNAME0 played with the toy accordion some more. \"I am serious,\" he said. \"Children aren\u2019t sentimental about the Titanic\u2014they\u2019re simply fascinated by the mechanism by which so many hopes and lives could be ended so quickly.\"\n\"Well\u2014uh\u2014thank you, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 Malagrowther.\n\"You...", "1_12_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0, in the control room of the factory, was turning on the great furnace of the vacuum furnace. He was waiting patiently while cold air blew past him into the fire chamber. The cold air was being sucked in through gratings in the floor and walls. It was being warmed before it went down into the fire chamber by immersion in a coil of pipes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s job would begin, as soon as the air in the fire chamber got hot enough to blaze away by itself. His job would be to regulate the feeding of the fuel.\nThere was very little to the job of feeding the fire. The door of the furnace was opened, and a boring machine ran in on its guideways, fed the fire, and ran out on its guideways.\nWhile $FIRSTNAMEB0 waited for the fire to blaze by itself, he amused himself by thinking up questions to ask his friend the pilot. Just thinking about the pilot made $FIRSTNAMEB0 shiver with longing. There on the instrument panel of the pilot\u2019s ship was his friend\u2019s name\u2014Ransy, with old-fashioned letters, a name of one syllable. How would the letters have been arranged on the bumpers of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s car in the old...", "2_8_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-nine in the morning</span>. The scene was Diji-Diji, a suburb of a city in one of the great canyons. The city was 8,809 feet above the equator\u20145,532 feet below ground level. The canyon was 2,625 feet wide. There were five million people in the canyon. There were 108,000 streets in Diji-Diji. The Booshwick Hotel was on Wubbu Rock Road.\nThe Martian who had been in charge of operations in Diji-Diji had fought his way in from the Martian surface at three that morning. He had had two hours of sleep. The new Martian in charge of the operation in Diji-Diji had taken over the shift at four in the morning.\nThe new Martian was named $FIRSTNAMEB0. He was awake, now, and was making his final preparations.", "0_21_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> on the tenth of December. I calculated rapidly\u2014perhaps too hopefully\u2014that $FIRSTNAMEB6 would be asleep by this time. Indeed, she wasn't. When I started to call her she warned me off twice, pretty emphatically; but I went on, and she decided to accept the call. As I had predicted, we got straight to the point.\n\"What do you want?\" she asked.\n\"Instinct tells me to leave Boise by air very shortly,\" I said.\n\"When did you have this wonderful thought?\"\n\"About five minutes ago.\"\n\"Well, leave by air, silly,\" she said. \"But why call me? Why not call Seymour?\"\n\"I may call Seymour, too,\" I said. \"But he's been drinking.\"\n\"So\u2014what,\" she said.\n\"So what?\" I repeated.\n\"Why connect that fact with your possible need to leave by air?\" she asked.\n\"I don't know,\" I said. \"It just pushed its way to the surface of my subliminal being.\"\n\"Right,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB6.\n\"And after all, it's yours as much as mine,\" I said.\n\"Eh?\"\n\"The subliminal being.\"\n\"Oh...", "2_12_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-seven p.m.</span>\nThe harmoniums spoke to each other through the walls of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head, through his closed eyelids, through his solidly sealed ears. The things they were frightened of were many, but the thing they were most frightened of was anything that would make them cease to vibrate in unison. Harmoniums wanted only harmony.\nThe things the harmoniums feared might break their union were any loud noises, bright lights, strong blows, strong smells, strong tastes\u2014\nThey feared loneliness, blights of heat, cold, radiation, straight lines, sharp edges, wetness, dryness, pointed objects, round objects, heights, depths, fortuitous meetings of sharp objects and soft objects\u2014\nThey feared accidents. They feared any change in the spacing of themselves, any change in their relationships with things. The harmoniums were frightened of Time. The harmoniums twittered softly and bitterly about Time, when in unison, for time made its own patterns on the hormonal film between harmoniums, and not even harmoniums could like the patterns of Time.\nIt was twelve thirty-eight p.m.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was afraid of one thing only.", "2_11_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span> on Mars.\nThe red signal light in a police booth in Newport was still blinking.\nThe signal was a begging message to headquarters in New York City. It was a plea to be relieved of duty.\nThe officer in the booth had seen something terrible, and he wanted to go home and tell his wife about it. He was chiefly afraid that his wife wouldn\u2019t believe him.\nWhen his wife did come home, he begged her to keep an open mind about the thing that he was about to tell her.\nAnd his wife said, \"Skip, who\u2019s kidding who? You can tell me anything, and I won\u2019t believe you.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was sewing the buttons on her shoes. She was in an armchair by the cold fireplace in a room at the top of the Mars buildings. All the rooms at the top of the buildings were paneled in knotty pine. All of them, except the bedrooms and parlors, lacked fireplaces. But all had mantels, and the mantels all boasted the finest wrought-iron grills.", "1_3_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-eight in the morning</span> of October 27, two thousand and nine, when Alvin, alone but for the legless $SURNAME0, curled up for warmth in his iridescent bubble, heard one thought from his asleep mind roll to a stop before the lectern of his waking mind:\nThe secret of life is death.\nHe was not to learn the implications of this thought for eighteen months.\nBut he spent the rest of the night\u2014the infinitely long rest of this last night of October 27, two thousand nine\u2014in contriving ways to kill himself before Mars reached its perihelion on January 3, two thousand ten. He did this out of good manners. He would not want to appear before his wife, hands filled with the pink ostrich-bone manacles, on the death watch in his long, roomy, well insulated suicide mink.\nHe didn\u2019t have to kill himself, of course, because he was coming back to Earth on schedule.\nHe hadn\u2019t so much as kissed the beautiful blonde lady in the gilded bird\u2014cage, let alone said one word to her. He hadn\u2019t even learned her name.\nHe wouldn\u2019t see her for twenty-four years.\nBy that time...", "1_20_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until nine o'clock in the evening</span> before Robert Cohn left my side. At that time he had been telling me how he played with his sister when he was a little boy, and so finally I was worn out and put my head down on the table. Just as I was asleep an Englishman I had never seen before in my life came over to me and said: \u201cMr. Wheeler?\u201d\n\u201cThat's me.\u201d\n\u201cThere's a lady asking for you, Mr. Wheeler.\u201d\nFrom where I was sitting I saw Miss $FIRSTNAMEB5 at a table on the other side of the room.\n\u201cI'll be right with her,\u201d I said, and turned to Cohn. \u201cTell me what it's all about.\u201d\n\u201cWhat time is it?\u201d\n\u201cTwenty minutes to ten.\u201d\n\u201cThe fiesta doesn't start until tonight. I thought we might go over to San Sebastian to-day. There's always some good bull-fights.\u201d\n\u201cI promised to go with her.\u201d\n\u201cThat's too bad.\u201d\n\u201cNot if she wants me to go with her.\u201d\n\u201cThat's right.\u201d", "1_20_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span> of a particularly dull day in Brussels, during a particularly dull week in the autumn of a particularly dry year. The place was the Grande Arm\u00e9e Caf\u00e9, on the left bank of the Senne, near the big Brussels Cemetery. Ten minutes before, a particularly gorgeous limousine had slipped quietly up to the kerb, before the door of the caf\u00e9.\nThe chauffeur in gold-faced black had hopped out and run around to open the door for somebody inside the car. He had done that for somebody inside the car.\nSilence, even among the Belgian schaal-crackers and tobacco-chewers, had fallen upon the caf\u00e9. All eyes were fixed on the limousine. Eyeless among the eyeless, a small rubber man stood inside the glass case of a lottery-ticket seller across the street, his stomach stuffed with stories of lucky numbers. Only the scraping of the schaal-crackers and tobacco-chewers among the false teeth in their mouths broke the silence. Even the lottery-ticket seller felt lonely.\nOut of the limousine had stepped a large, bald, white-mustached old man in a Chesterfield overcoat, who had paused a moment to light an obscenely long cigar...", "2_6_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Watch carefully, Bonfilius,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Let there be no mistake in the message that is being delivered down that line this morning. \"\nHe looked back at his little black book, and his spine straightened, and his chin pointed toward the ceiling, and he began to sing in a childish soprano. \"Watch, brothers. Watch!\" he sang. \"Watch!\"\n\"Are you awake?\" said Helmholtz. He held his nose and sucked vigorously at the back of his mouth.\n\"All awake,\" said $SURNAME0, in a graceful feminine singsong. He turned his eyes upward as he sang, so that his upper lids ghosted his eyebrows.\n\"Up comes a messenger from the lowlands,\" sang $SURNAME0. \"Up comes a messenger from the lowlands of Sleep\u2014\"\n\"Oh, boy,\" said $SURNAME0, suddenly remembering the half-full quart of vodka under his bench. \"Life is just a bowl of cherries.\"\n\"It\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"How do you do, Mr. $SURNAME1?\"", "1_18_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-six in the evening</span>, $SURNAME0\u2019s time, twelve minutes before the New York Thing. Skip was leaving a fine house in Newport. The house, which $SURNAME0 once described as a hovel, is called Stonecipheco, Ltd. It is owned by a huge conglomeration of the food industry, and has been put at $SURNAME0\u2019s disposal by that organization.\nHolding Miss Galative\u2019s paw, Skip was going to the house\u2019s fence. By the fence was a helicopter. In the next instant the helicopter, apparently of its own volition, lifted itself three feet into the air. The sound of its turbines became a merry whine.\n\"What on earth!\" said Miss Galative.\n\"Go!\" said $SURNAME0 merrily. \"What are you waiting for?\"\nIt developed that Miss Galative was, in fact, waiting for a chauffeur, a maid, and a valise of freshly laundered clothes, all of which materialized instantly.\n\"But I don\u2019t understand\u2014\u201d said Miss Galative.\n$SURNAME0 patted her gently on the bottom. \"Run along, little Rob pollution\u2014that\u2019s your name now. Robribly Lol...", "1_11_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-eight in the morning</span> of June 1, 1975. Behind me an abstract sculpture squeaked in the light breeze. \u201cSomeday somebody\u2019s going to pour concrete,\u201d I said. \u201cThen all those iron diagonal intaglios, they\u2019ll have to cry in their beer.\u201d\nThe Duke looked back with some displeasure. \u201cWe come here to speak in sagest tones,\u201d he said, \u201cnot to sling silly buggery fitz that be.\u201d\n\u201cThe vulgar accent of that animal,\u201d I said, \u201cso uncongenial to a man of cultivation, grates on my ears.\u201d I rolled the o\u2019s into the back of my head. \u201cYou must forgive me if I muffle the egregious creature.\"\n\u201cThee has thy gadjle; how then can I be silent?\" The Duke held his hat over his heart, a gleaming brown fedora, and bowed. The sun picked up his smart navy suit, the shine of American shoes, the shine on the mahogany cane with the gilt initials.\n\u201cCan we speak freely?\u201d I said, glancing around. \u201cBy God,...", "1_14_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October second, one minute until the thin blue bus bearing Salo, $SURNAME0, $FIRSTNAMEF1, and their lives, the thin blue bus bearing them all so frail and feet- Dependent, was to begin its run against the chrono-synclastic infundibulated past of Earth.\nOnly a few people were waiting at the docks. There was no brass band. There was no count-down by the local civil defense chairman, timed to end in dramatic startling punctuality. One minute before the departure, only one man and one woman were present other than $SURNAME0 and his family.\nThis man was Salo.\nThis woman was $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\nSalo and $FIRSTNAMEF0 stood twelve feet apart, stared straight ahead. They were perfect bookends. They wore exactly the same expression and had exactly the same posture. Unbelievably, their eyes were open, and yet their eyes looked as empty as the windows of a closed and locked German bank.\nSalo and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were like a pair of GIs who had just been shelled, dazed to the point of idiocy but still somehow on their feet. Just being able to stand was a tremendous accomplishment.\nNeither Salo nor $FIRSTNAMEF0 was particularly...", "2_9_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-six in the morning</span>.\nThe armor-piercing shell barely penetrated the top of the right rear turret of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s tank, ricocheted off the rear wall, over the heads of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2, and exploded against the wall that blocked the eastern exit.\nThe steel wall was not harmed.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head was shattered.\nHis body fell across the cannon breech.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s head was shattered.\nThe cannon jolted backward, ramming a round into the hot barrel. The round detonated.\nThe boom! was not from the cannon.\nThe boom! was from the ammunition chambers.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 had primed the chambers, as instructed.\nThe iron labyrinth was swept by a hurricane of explosions.\nThirteen iron rooms were filled with the thunder of exploding ammunition.\nThen the ammunition was spent.\nThere were two very small explosions in the northwest corner of the basement. These were $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s and $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s heads exploding under the hot jets of the mercury bombardiers.\nThe basement was silent. Much of the sand in it was a deep pink.", "1_9_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-eight in the morning</span>. Any time now, I thought, Rubi happening to glance out the window\u2014and she\u2019ll see us all lined up, watching her. They would take it as a snub. Much better . . . which of our party could be trusted to turn smoothly, walk casually to the door and open it, thus allowing a little thinking time? A person in a white suit would be best, but they were all wearing white suits this morning. I saw our attorney freeze momentarily, then move to a position behind the seated dwarf, where Rubi could not see him. The attorney was going to handle It when the instant came. I had no option but to watch everything myself. I squinted, covered my face with my hands, moved around so I was behind the armrest of our director\u2019s chair...then, when I looked out through my fingers, there was Ro mother Goose, staring suddenly out of a dirty window at the top of the pink front of the Mint Hotel, leering horribly at us.\nWhat the fuck ...? I wondered. She shaded her eyes for a moment and then began to giggle. \u201cIt\u2019s beautiful,\u201d she said. \u201cWhat fun it\u2019ll be,...", "0_14_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the next day when $FIRSTNAMEM1 finally got up the nerve to enter the downtown branch of the Manhattan Federal Reserve Bank and ask for the gleaming white envelope with the mysterious, hieroglyphed handwriting on it. He was appalled by the formidable beauty of the strange, little piece of paper. And, as he rode the rumbling downtown subway to his dismal room, the strange thought waked in him that perhaps it would have been better if the writing on the mysterious note had not been in English at all, but in German, or Russian-any language, in fact, except English, with which $FIRSTNAMEM1 was familiar. The demons usually write in English, he thought, because they know it is the easiest for us to read. But what language did this paper come from? From the golden script and the purple ink, you would have thought it came from the court of the Emperor Charles the Fifth. Often $FIRSTNAMEM1 drowsed off in his dismal room. Upon awakening, he often found his lips moving and mumbled: \"Man, oh man, man-oh-man.\" Suddenly he realized why he was saying \"Man-oh-man\" and noticed that the words appeared in his thoughts as well as out loud, that a rhythmic impulse from inscrutable time and...", "0_9_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine nineteen in the morning</span>.\nGoodtime was unsure where to go. He had never been to the society\u2019s actual headquarters in his entire life.\nHe stopped entirely. He looked around in the street. He saw huge cubic stone monoliths all around him, soaring, spooky.\nSuddenly, Goodtime remembered an email he had gotten only this morning from the Solicitor of the Chromium Castle, addressing him as \u201cDear Fellow-Member.\u201d There came to Goodtime\u2019s mind the image of a bespeckled, professorial, fun-loving volunteer gal with Dumbo ears, whom Goodtime had met only once at a party.\nThe email had read:\nPARASITES STILL IN MY HEAD. CANNOT ESCAPE. CANNOT RISE. BUT CAN THINK THUS: KEEP IT QUIET FROM OTHERS THAT CHROMIUM CASTLE SOCIETY HEADQUARTERS GOES EVERYWHERE WITH YOU. THERE IS NO THE ETERNAL EVERGREEN ARBOR SOCIETY. YOU ARE HEAD OF THE CHROMIUM CASTLE SOCIETY. THE ETERNAL EVERGREEN ARBOR SOCIETY LIES IN A SMALL RED LOCK...", "1_10_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe alarm was off and by itself on the table.\nImprovements had been made in the alarm. The little black knob with the projection on it had been removed.\nAn enthusiastic young scientist, who had wanted to find out just what an alarm did, had removed the knob and put the alarm beside itself on the table.\nFor forty-three minutes the alarm had been going off, sending out purple rays that paralyzed all will power. For forty-three minutes nothing on earth could make Pierce Cunningham raise his hand, make his feet move, or make his eyes see anything but that lovely, lovely S-curve, that lovely, lovely, lovely, lovely S-curve, that lovely lovely\u2014\nThe terrible cycle of sensual images repeated and repeated and repeated and repeated itself. Pierce, who hated Chevrolets with all the boundless hate that exists in the hearts of Ford men, now longed to roll and wallow in a sea of new, hot, red-black Chevrolet ads. Pierce, who thought Earl Wilson\u2019s gossip column in The New York Post was the most dishonest column in American journalism, wanted to run right out and get his hair cut by the only guy who could really cut it. Pierce, who had been...", "1_3_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-four in the morning</span>, and I knew I was unable to sleep. I was nervous and sweating. My legs were trembling when I unlocked the door and walked in. I turned the light on in the bathroom and saw myself in the mirror: hair matted, shirt wrinkled, pants caked with dust and pitsoil, eyes blank, staring. I got a drink of water, brushed my hair with my fingers, took off my shoes, unbuttoned my collar. I decided to risk everything and call Las Vegas. The thought that I might get my machine and leave a message, like a safe and sane professor, or even better, a sane and safe professor, shooting pool with his conservative colleagues in a recreation room, appealed to me.\nThen I remembered that I was supposed to go awake my attorney. Even had I wanted to, I couldn't call. I picked up the phone a few times and let it down again as I tried to imagine what would happen. My attorney would answer the phone. Would he grunt or cry out in joy at the sound of my voice? This would be followed by a pause, long enough for me to ask if he was okay, if he was being taken care of. I would make this ques tion part of the background of the...", "1_22_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-seven in the evening</span>. The year was 1973. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was holding his breath now, holding his machine-gun ready, holding his heart in, as he finally forced himself to relax and open the air-lock door. He sensed, rather than saw or heard, that all was well on the air-shaft landing. $FIRSTNAMEB0 opened the door, crept out.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was a big, clumsy man now, and his eyes and ears and mind, for all their cunning, had been dulled by months of sedative feeding. He over-hunted for danger, found and responded to shadows that were harmless.\nInstinctively, childishly, habitually, $FIRSTNAMEB0 wanted to creep, wanted to stay away from the lighted wall, wanted to attack anything that moved in the well-lighted areas. He nervously tapped the barrel of his gun against the air-lock door to get the charge level.\nHe took his first unscheduled action when he came to the wall, pressed himself against it there, found that the wall hadn\u2019t been completely unlighted after all. Fifty yards away, on the other side of the great curve of corridor, there was a soft yellow area that was warm enough to see by. A tough little man could...", "2_17_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-five in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 did not keep Mrs. $SURNAME0 waiting long. Like a genie, he stepped out of the wall, his face wreathed with smiles and his bluejeans done up with apparent carelessness at the knee otherwise.\nThe genie expressed great amazement at Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s ermine muff, expressed great admiration for the clean way she was raising her two young children. The genie never for a moment looked at his watch. In three minutes, he had kissed Mrs. $SURNAME0 warmly, patted the children on their heads, and was out the door again. He left behind him such an odor of heated dog that his wife, forever after, was able to identify the approach of her husband by smell alone.\nThe genie took the path of his space ship, the Evelyn Mae, and was hailed by Prime Minister Churchill, who was sitting on the ramp. Churchill was wearing a blue velvet smoking jacket and smoking a corncob pipe.\n\"I wouldn\u2019t go in, if I were you!\" called Churchill.\n$SURNAME0 climbed the gangway, disappeared through the door. A moment later, a trickle of colored light appeared under the door.", "0_14_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two seventeen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe three boys did not shave, however.\nThey had grown beards in order that they might not be recognized when the photographs were made of the families of Martians who had been lucky enough to procure passage Earthward for themselves and for their loved ones.\nThe boys did not look very much like boys any more.\nTheir beards were thick, dark and long.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, a chrononaut, a spacefarer, and a native of Newport, Rhode Island, let the rim of his space helmet fall over his eyes. He went up the spiral staircase to his own $SURNAME0 estate softly, light-footedly, one step at a time.\nStanding on the highest terrace of his mansion, his wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, looked out a leaded window to see her husband appear as if by magic on the staircase. Her eyes widened as he came up. The well-bred surprise on Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s face was not for the fact that he had come up the stairs, however, but for the fact that he had returned from his space journey at all.\n$SURNAME0 used neither the spiral staircase nor the front stairs of his mansion under any conceivable circumstances. There were...", "1_16_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four seventeen in the afternoon</span>, and downtown Cleveland looked like the field of a horrible battle. Glass was everywhere. Every window in every skyscraper was smashed. Things made of glass, such as perfume bottles, had proven especially vulnerable to the rock throwers.\nThe noontime balloon launch had done the damage.\nThe moon travelers, who had counted on making a safe ascent in the clouds, found themselves instead looking down on a battlefield. From their gondolas, they began lobbing grenades down at the roofs below. Some miscalculated, and the grenades blew up the airships.\nThe airships retreated. By chance, five of them convoyed across Lake Erie and over the industrial belt of Erie, Pennsylvania.\nBy three P.M., only one small dirigible remained over Cleveland. It was the one manned by Bela K. Avast, who was waiting for the sun to go down so that he could have an unobstructed view of the moon.\nThe small dirigible lurched in a cross-wind, and Avast saw something on the ground that amused him considerably. He dropped a hand bomb. The hand bomb fell, followed by a small piece of paper. The paper said, \"Up Yr. Ass.\" Avast hung over the side of...", "2_5_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-four in the morning</span>, 365 days since the Nixon pardon\u2014and my attorney was still sleeping peacefully with his head on my shoulder. His snoring was literally scarely. The thought occurred that if our positions had been reversed and I had been behind the wheel while he slept\u2014with some drunken Tropicana waitress holding my head\u2014this whole nightmare would be over. I could be holding his head now, staring down into his open mouth, trying to read the runes.\nHe would be dead. I would be arrested. And the Great Red Shark [from here on abbreviated as Shark] would be impounded for investigation of overAlcohol-ism. It only stands to reason. Dost thou protest this? Stay tuned for a lengthy discussion on this point later on. Right now it\u2019s important to focus again on the strange delights of driving this powerful Great Red Shark up through North Florida at sunrise in late November, after a week of grave tension in L.A., and before that ten nights of addictive frenzy on the Strip ... while onlaying an all-too-typical strange and sinister drug trip, like the one we\u2019d had. It was necessary, now, to flee.", "1_17_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-one in the afternoon</span> when I received the following communiqu\u00e9 from my attorney: \u201c4:19, Dr. Bronner\u2019s fluid. 4:21, amyl. 4:22, lucy . . . cut . . . cut . . . cut . . . rolling . . .\u201d I understood. It was all over for my attorney. He\u2019d gone down the road on LSD, and this fluid/syrup was only a memory now, a rambling, disjointed memory playing back across his drug\u2014frazzled mind. It was time for me to assume command. Goddamn it, I was a chief of police! What was I doing getting high in a desert island laboratory while a major narcotics operation crashed around my ears?\nIt was time to pull myself together. The first order of business was to establish a \u201ccontrol area.\u201d I walked outside and surveyed the situation. It was dusk, still in that gray time of day when everything holds its breath. I had to make a choice, and fast: hide in the bushes or go into the house? I chose the latter, reasoning that my attorney might have exhausted himself with all this drug abuse, but he was sure as hell not through stalking me...", "2_11_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-two in the morning</span>.\nThe silence continued.\nAn untrained ear might have thought that the microphone had been turned off, even though $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s wrench was still screaming. In fact, the microphone was wide open and getting a wonderful reading on $SURNAME0, who was saying nothing very complicated.\nHaving said only six words, he was preparing to say more.\nThe silence continued.\nAn untrained observer might have thought that there was nothing more that $SURNAME0 could possibly say that would interest or amaze anyone. But the observer would have been wrong.\nThe most interesting thing about the silence was that $SURNAME0 was still trying to say something that no one had ever heard before, something so stupendous that everyone would have to listen to it a thousand times before it began to make any sense.\nHe was still trying to say something that would not only entertain and amaze the Earthlings, but would also make them much wiser. And, of course, though he wasn\u2019t trying to say this, he was also trying to say something that glorified God, and underscored the glory of being and the horror of non-being.", "0_14_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span> and I still had my shopping list to go. I leaped into the air and rushed down the main concourse of stores, stopping briefly at the bookshop to consult their map of Los Angeles. \u201cFastest way to the Sunset Strip,\u201d I said to the salesman who came over to help me.\nSlowly he drew a finger along a yellow stretch of Sunset Boulevard. \u201cSee?\u201d he said. \u201cIt's one of the main lateral gigles. Where you want to go is practically in the San Fernando Valley. You should have taken the freeway.'\n\u201cWhich way is the freeway?\u201d I demanded.\n\u201cThat way,\u201d said the man, pointing east. \u201cJust follow signs.' I thanked him and bolted for the door.\nI hitchhiked north on the freeway for seven miles, then took surface streets to West Hollywood, pondering what fell powers lay clustered in that strange market. It was the loneliest, most dreadfully consumptive shopping district I'd ever seen\u2014the bogies and nightwing crawlers of all Southern California seemed to have convened there to discuss their woes with each other: twisted mind, deadhead beatnik mamas, exploded wranglers,...", "0_23_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-seven in the evening</span> of Monday, November 16, 1987, and the place was Room 233, Level 33, Mintome Paradise, Lake isabella, CA. The place referred to as \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB3\u2019s Room\u201d on the rusticated frame around the mirror was large, expensive, had a bed, a desk, two easy chairs, a very nice stereo system and two windows, one overlooking the motel parking lot, the other given a com manding view of Lake isabella by a subtle and cunning rotation of the room itself. On the table between the two easy chairs was a drinking glass full of whiskey, ice, water and lemon peel.\nThe occupant of the room was John \u201cDennis\u201d Gustafson, who sat in one of the easy chairs, looking at his watch, waiting to begin watching as the second hand swept toward 11:30 p.m. In a moment he would bend over, retrieve something from the bottom of the easy chair, and stand up, holding in his hand a time\u2014lagged photograph of a sweet-faced baby girl. He would look at it sadly. He would drink whiskey. He would wait another two minutes, until 11:32, then insert a cassette in the stereo and wave his slender, unpolluted...", "0_15_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-one in the afternoon</span> of October thirty-first.\nPrimo climbed out of the Vatican grotto into a waiting helicopter. He was lifted vertically six hundred feet in two seconds.\nIn that short time, men dumped into the Vatican grotto everything they could lift. The ten thousand creatures were churning and crawling now, making sounds. Large sections of the walls of the grotto collapsed under the pressure. The walls had supported nothing; they had been plastered against soft earth and lawn.\nThe Vatican helicopter took Primo to the heliport on top of the PanEuropean building in Basel. The Pan-European League Building was the tallest office building in Basel. It was forty-one stories high.\nThe office of the President of the Pan-European League occupied an entire floor. The building had no executive staff. The real work of the Pan-European League was done by semi-automated machinery in a sub-basement vault.\nNo one could identify all the furnishings, apparatus, and decorations in the room. The only clear things, in fact, were a desk, a chair, and an ashtray.\nThere was no need for anything else in the room. The blinds were drawn. The light came from a shaded lamp...", "2_11_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThe place was a cave in an iron-poor mountainside on Mars.\nAnd $FIRSTNAMEB0 was crazy.\nHis hair had turned white overnight, as if to refute the lie of his rubbery face. His face was burned a cherry red by the cold. The skin of his neck was dry and losing its elasticity. His eyes watered endlessly. A rheum dribbled from one of his nostrils.\nHis body shook so hard that his skeleton rattled. Sometimes he stumbled over things, blundered into things, blundered away from things. The shudders were worst inside his head. Imagine a tuning fork in the town square struck random chords from Elephunk to Hunky Dory to Twostep to John B.\nThe name of the town was Huncke.\nThe name of the planet was Mars.\nThe name of the Army buddy who had predicted the shudders was $FIRSTNAMEB1. The name of the Army company commander who had really had the company dismantled and its men sent underground into the caves was Cavanaugh. The name of the tape-player that $FIRSTNAMEB0 brought into the caves was Stony Stevenson, who was a rock star.", "1_8_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-eight in the morning</span> of Valentine\u2019s Day, and Marlon ran back out of the office and over to the elevators, and he chatted easily with the operator, who had neatly featured hair that was white and black, combinations of black and white hairs among wisps of grey. The operator took Marlon down ten floors even though the Law School was only on the seven floor. In his broad back and easy rudder-arm motions there was an air of superior strength. He had a big square jaw and a farmboy grin. He was an old man and had been married for thirty-five years. He drove an old Cadillac, had a farm outside of Lexington, and a wife and two boys. There wasn\u2019t anything in the world that made him mad. He could get through anything. He could swallow anything. He knew all about life and life\u2019s ups and downs and curves. He was exactly what a cab-driver in a big city ought to be. But especially he was like the cab-driver of an architect who first thought of him, and that was in the look he had of being Secretly Happy. No matter if his fares were screaming and cursing and beating him, there was a faraway look in his eyes, and...", "2_17_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2-Libration Day had begun.\nConvinced the stamp-buying crowd was harmless, the bartender relaxed. John N. watched a tiny sundial that stood at the far end of the bar. So engrossed was it in timing the shadow of a twisted spike of quartz that it failed to notice the hand of the huge colonial clock slip over the before-last hash mark to four.\nAt four, things became difficult to watch, because it was dark outside.", "0_11_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. There had been a heavy rainstorm in the night. Uncalled, the newspapers had arrived one hour late. The wet ink had blurred the bold headlines. The weather map showed nothing but vast puddles.\nPresently Mrs Violet $SURNAME0 materialized in her usual way, and invited the church organist to conduct the service.\nThe organist, who was supposed to represent the Almighty, had a bad cold, and sneezed ten times before he reached the pulpit. While he sneezed and sniffled, three Americans and two Portuguese sailed up over the town again\u2014and took a powder.\nThe church service was a modified liturgical dance called theelingus\u2014and it had two parts. One part was called foot-theelingus, and the other was called hand\u2014theelingus.\nIn hand\u2014theelingus, participants of both sexes clasped hands and spread their arms to represent wings, and proceeded stiffly about the aisles. In foot-theelingus, the men removed their shoes and danced on their toes around the aisles without making a sound\u2014while the women clapped their hands and made streaming noises with their mouths.\n\"Spread those pretty wings,\" said Mrs $SURNAME0 through...", "1_2_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>, when everything is beautiful, and everyone takes a breath in preparation for the real beginning of the day. There is a kind of shivering hush and a kind of shrug of the shoulders, and you look out from the shelter of your own curtains and see the tops of different trees against the sky, and people going home on buses, and you know that the day is about to begin for them, too, and that, on their way to work, the special morning profundity that the night has brought about in them will pass for all the world to see and understand, only the world will not see, because it is sleeping. The day will come but it will be robbed of its real beginning, and so will the people in the street. And they won't know.\nThere was a little milk left in the bottle of milk on the hall table. I drank the milk. Then I went outside the house into the court and undid the snaps on the canvas covers of all the beds, to let the fresh air in under the covers, where the plants and the earth-worms were. All the plants had good strong root systems, out of their own manure beds. The worms were fat and lazy-looking. The moon was business-end down.", "2_19_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-eight in the evening</span>.\n\"Skip, I can\u2019t read this all by myself,\" said Mrs. Porter. She shook, held out the telegram that had come for her in Skip\u2019s station wagon.\nThis was always the worst time for her. In an hour it would be much worse.\n\"I know so little about\u2014about things,\" said Mrs. Porter limply.\nThe gilded rowboat in which $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog had been floating gently on the artificial lake just crashed into the stone retaining wall by the lily pads. Ordinarily, these crashes didn\u2019t frighten anyone. They were tame and rather decorous, and the perfect integrity of the $SURNAME0 image was upheld.\nBut the crashes didn\u2019t happen only when $SURNAME0 was preoccupied. They happened some of the time when $SURNAME0 was keenly and sanely interested in things. They happened unaccountably. $SURNAME0 could not control them\u2014could not stop them, once they had started.\n\"Skip\u2014\" said Mrs. Porter, \"who\u2014who is this $FIRSTNAMEF0?\"", "0_4_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-seven in the morning</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEF1 was running from home, from the hill, from the gentle rolling countryside, from her family and her friends and her nation, on the way to the port, to Glencadiac, Monroe territory and the Common Europe.\n$SURNAME1 was at the port, having disembarked from his spaceship only ten minutes before $FIRSTNAMEF1 came rushing up. He had said good-by to the crew, and now he was sipping a cocktail in the bar of Variety Hall and thinking that he was another Columbus, venturing farther than ever any man had gone before. He was entering a known. Not Russia or China, but still a known.\nAnd he was going not as an imperialist, as Americans have traditionally pretended when they set out abroad, but rather as an explorer in a strange, possibly hostile galaxy\u2014which is what the United States had become for him. He was a man arguing with his girlfriend on the telephone, and kissing his son for the last time. He was the explorer who has left his boyboy behind.\n\"Damn it!\" cried $FIRSTNAMEF1, and she ripped the earphones off.\nSomeone in the party had said, half in jest, \"If you\u2019re trying to talk to someone in San Francisco, and you can...", "2_12_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after noon</span> on October 2, 18,541,012.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had now been on Mars for eleven years, elevenmonths, and eleven and a half days. Time which had been welded together in the corrosion pits at the very bottom of his mind was again fluid. He could feel its force streaming through his body.\nHe would begin remembering things now\u2014in fifty-five seconds.\nHe would begin remembering things now\u2014in fifty seconds.\nHe would begin remembering things now\u2014in forty-nine seconds.\nHe would begin remembering things now\u2014 in forty-eight seconds.\nHe would begin remembering things now\u2014 in forty-seven seconds.\nAnd so on down....", "2_23_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until midnight</span>.\nThe bathroom was full of smoke.\nSmoke was pouring out of the open window above the bathtub. There were two deep claw marks on the wood at the base of the window.\nThe smoke smelled like a smoldering leather\nshoe.\nLoss of Identity Due to Exposure to Asphalt Embrittlement Gas, R. V. Diehl, M.D., Unpublished Manuscript, January 1, 1977.\nMy name is Robert Vigorous.\nIronically, that is not my real name, nor is the name I am using for publication. My real name is Lemonjello Atlas. That is not an ironic pseudonym. It is the name I was christened by my mother, whose name was Wildroot Creamentop. Please address all correspondence to me, at P. O. Box 690069, Cincinnati, Ohio.\nAs I write these words, I have just turned forty. I have a birthmark on the palm of my right hand.\nMy birthmark distinguishes me from the overwhelming majority of physicians in the United States.", "0_6_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six o'clock in the morning</span>, and it was still dark as nine at night could ever be. Some timid creature was out there in the desert and had let go a yowl, first of all to alert its own kind to stay away and second to let anyone in earshot know that here was yet another creature in need of protection.\nSuddenly, in a window of the dark house, a pale pink light flickered and expanded and turned the window into a gray rectangle. The window revealed a face, wet, young, and female\u2014but the face did not particularly reveal anything. It was afraid. Its eyes wanted to stay in the gloom of sleep.\nThe fidgety thing in the background, a harmonium, had begun to play itself. The music was high and hollow, like the strange bad music in dreams.\nThe girl heard the music\u2014heard it as one would hear the rustle of a ghost.\nAt last she turned slowly, reluctantly, out of the sight of the window. We knew she was uncoiling her long legs, now.\nThe harmonium fidgeted itself into speech, and the speech was the song: \"Whenever a creature meets a creature, and they sing a song\u2014\"\nThe voice that sang was thin. None of...", "0_22_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span> of Wednesday, April 1, 1997, and the place was Lot 47 of Space D-29 in Sykstus Square, one of the more imposing stretches of real estate in the island resort of Maglyre, off the Neo-Parisian coast of the French riviera. A stone\u2019s throw back from the crimson turmoil of the seas, the reclining lawns and mansions of Sykstus Square had the cool, orderly surfaces of a chessboard\u2014the large-scale chessboard, perhaps, in the green-wooden lounge of featureless, pleasantly noteless Grand Hotel Pimparoo, faced and fanged with stone. Unattached or unattainable or unattainably unattached young women had, for generations, sidled across that lounge and back. Tycoonsof all nations made rendezvous here and engaged neat drinks. Jazz swung nonchalantly in the air. Pacifist Baron-Battleships cruised the waters far below Maglyre\u2019s cliffs, alert to pry into the creneling maneuvers of evil powers. In short, the island was an obscene advertisement for the luxury tastes of a.gulf-front shoreline.\n33 Ominous looking, as most affluent...", "0_11_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> by my watch, and the first ship entering in Manila Bay was the American Asiatic Squadron commanded by Commodore Dewey. All of a sudden the silence was broken by a tremendous noise; we were only a few hundred yards from the forts and the Spanish had fired at us. It was the first time I ever heard the sound of a gun fired in anger, and my heart stopped so short I couldn't hear anything for a time. We watched them through the glasses; the ship right ahead of us, the Brandywine, which was leading the inshore column, had been hit, and was spouting black smoke, and they saw her captain carry the wounded first lieutenant below. Aft they saw the Juneau firing her forward five-inch, the old seventy-pounder guns; and then one of the Spanish six-inchers hit the Juneau right in the forehead and sent a burst of red puffs of smoke up her. It looked as if it might have been a serious thing, but it turned out to be only one of her crew of fifty wounded. We watched the American flag going up to the fore and the white ensign on the aft main, and then we shook hands all round the boat. I went up to the wheel...", "2_8_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-nine in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time.\nIn a gazebo on Mars, Saturn boomed to Mars, with Mercury, the ghost of Canente, and, of Woodward $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the materialized but disorganized image of a twenty-three-year-old $SURNAME0.\n\"No! No! No!\" said $SURNAME0. \"I don\u2019t know what it is. I\u2019m sorry I poked my head in. Of course you people have a perfect right to be angry. Go ahead and be angry. I\u2019ll just look at the machine over here.\"\nHe was talking to the three figures in the gazebo. They were Shadows \u2013 perfectly materialized shadows of $SURNAME0 and his wife and his dog. But they had wretched control of their light sources. Their glows were tortured, prismatic gushes. The appearance and color of the gushes changed in timing patterns as irregular as the timing patterns of a coked-up metronome.", "0_4_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe big silver bus reached the elevators at three minutes to five.\nThere were cheers and cheers.\nRandy Denton, frowning now and breathing hard, opened the door to get out.\nThere were more cheers.\nRandy suddenly felt very sorry for everybody. Everybody was so wonderful, doing their best to be wonderful. It was happening, too. Things were really happening.\nLucky him.\n\"I guess I\u2019ll have to say hello and good-by to everybody,\" said Randy puffingly.\n\"No, you won\u2019t, Doctor,\" said BeeClay, \"we\u2019ve got to get back to the Fernwood. Can\u2019t waste any time if we\u2019re going to get to the Pope.\"\nNot one person got off the bus. Everybody wanted to stay with Randy. The magic finger of fate had touched everybody on Randy\u2019s travel list. However, there was one person who was not on the list, but who clearly wanted to go. This person was the young man who had spangled the magic carpet ride in San Francisco. He was a nineteen-year-old Christ lover from Tonopah, Nevada. His name was Alvin Lingling. He...", "1_18_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-eight in the evening</span>. No, now it was six fifty\u2014eight in the evening. Even a chrononaut's sense of time could get a little confused in a place like this. The roar of the million rock and roll bands down at the carnival was too loud to hear anything.\nThe smell of Sally was still in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s nostrils, and the feel of her body was still in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hands. Being in love with a girl who wouldn\u2019t love him back wasn\u2019t an especially new experience for $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014he\u2019d been in love with girls who wouldn\u2019t love him back for as long as he could remember. But the particular form the experience had taken this time was novel\u2014and dizzying. He had picked the softest, sweetest, kindest girl he had ever seen, had picked her out of all the other girls he knew, had picked her out of all the other girls in the world, had picked her out of all the girls that had ever lived, and he had picked her because she was the girl he would like the best to be his mate, if he ever had a mate.\nHe had imagined how nice it would be to sit across a table and eat with his...", "1_18_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-three in the evening</span>; it would be dark in an hour.\nI walked through crowds of Southwestern belles and beaux. Night fell around me while I wandered in gardens made of colored floodlights. I looked over little white iron fences at pools and fountains. There were lights in the water. There were fountains of colored waters. The smooth mulchy earth was soft to the feet.\nI knew no way to calm myself. That I could be under such insufferable stress and still live amazed me. There was a place in my head where the wires crossed and the sparks kept on leaping while my mind flew apart all around it. I was mad. I knew I was mad. And I knew that being mad was a lot better than the alternative.\nI bought an evening paper. I read all about myself-how I had killed a Catholic priest named $FIRSTNAMEB3 Conformer and an old Mexican woman and her son on the highway outside of Salina, and then escaped from the asylum. There was a big headlines story that carried my picture and told all about everything. I read it under the one bright light in the pavilion. An old wino sat down beside me to pass around my picture.\nI walked farther into the gardens. A quartet of...", "0_20_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-nine in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0, who had just gone to sleep after his evening \u2019Scan, was awakened by the distant memory of a boy\u2019s voice. The voice was saying things to $FIRSTNAMEB0. The things the voice was saying ran through $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s sleep-befuddled mind like italicized or highlighted passages in a foreign language text:\nThe boy said, \"Watch out!\" He then explained, quite sincerely that he didn\u2019t mean any personal offense, but just thought it would be better for $FIRSTNAMEB0 if he watched out. He said that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was an orphan now, and didn\u2019t even know what an orphan was, but that some day he was going to find out, because he had just been orphaned a few minutes ago. He pointed out that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had nobody to take care of him now, not even a father, not even a mother. He said that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was a fatherless child now, and didn\u2019t even know what a fatherless child was, but that some day he was going to find out, because he, the boy, had just made $FIRSTNAMEB0 a fatherless child. He said that, as far as he knew for sure, $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t even have...", "0_10_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt ten minutes past ten o'clock in the morning, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mate, the harmonium known as $FIRSTNAMEF1, was taking a shower in Building 1 of the church battlement. She was washing the sleep out of her eyes, arranging the day ahead in her mind. She liked to plan her day in some detail.\nShe had a mirror on the wall of her cubicle, and when she showered, she liked to look at herself naked.\nBeauty made $FIRSTNAMEF1 afraid.\nAs she looked at herself now, she kept saying to herself, \"You shouldn\u2019t look like this, you shouldn\u2019t\u2014anybody could come in and catch you at any moment.\"\nShe was lovely, with a full bosom, with no bony protrusions, with nice skin and good features and lustrous brown hair. $FIRSTNAMEF1 didn\u2019t like her looks, because she knew no one could be more lovely without being more arrogant and mysterious. Beauty forced $FIRSTNAMEF1 to extremes of humility that kept her right on the ragged edge between fluency and a stammer.\nBeauty kept $FIRSTNAMEF1 humble in other ways, too. Talking to anyone but $FIRSTNAMEB0 when she was wearing her gage was like shouting at royalty...", "0_20_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty in the evening</span>; the fiesta was developing in the town below, the drumming coming clear up here. Robert Cohn came into the room and handed Bill some towels. \u201cThere are no clean ones. I looked.\u201d\n\u201cCan\u2019t you get them laundered in the morning?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll wire my valet to bring some down from Paris.\u201d\n\u201cSend them down to the hotel,\u201d I said. \u201cPuntilla gets them clean.\u201d\n\u201cI want my clothes laundered in Paris,\u201d Cohn said.\nHe brought in one of the bottles, opened it and took a big drink. \u201cDamned good wine,\u201d he said, and took a big drink.\n\u201cWatered,\u201d said Bill.\nBull happened to be passing through on his way to the other side of the roof, on tot patrol. He put his head in the door. \u201cSomebody been drinking that?\u201d\n\u201cDrinking it? Rot-gut,\u201d said Bill.\n\u201cGive me that bottle.\u201d\n\u201cWhat for?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m captain. I...", "2_12_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve eighteen post meridiem</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had only four cigarettes left. That was bad. That made him feel queer, too.\nOne thing $FIRSTNAMEB0 had discovered from his crowds was that cigarette addiction ran in phases. This was the batting order of the phases:\nAngst. Elation. Not-Giving-a-Damn.\nAngst was what $FIRSTNAMEB0 felt now. So his cigarette stock was low. So he would be likely to suffer anger, depression, discontent for a long time to come.\nAngst was an odd condition, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had discovered, because it was based on a fundamental misconception and could therefore be dissipated by a gesture simple enough for a child to understand.\nThe gesture was: Exchange what you have for something unknown.\nWhether you swam in success or in failure thereafter, you were never at a loss for things to think about.\nSo $FIRSTNAMEB0 decided, now, to exchange his empty passenger deck for the fascinating what-if game of the rest of the flight to Mercury.", "0_21_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was hungry for the first time in a long while. The memory of the escape from the space ship on Mars suddenly seized him, and he realized that he had fasted for two days.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 pushed the lid up again. He looked to see if the guards were paying attention.\nThey were. They were back on their platform in front of the barracks\u2014playing cards.\nTheir rifles were stacked neatly on a bench in the sun.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 took off his orange helmet, laid it on his bunk. Taking care not to make any noise, he spread-eagled his limp body on the iron floor of the barracks. He lay, staring up at the ceiling.\nHis colorless body and his flaming imagination were now free to deal with the most repulsive prison imaginable.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s seventy-year-old body began to cleanse itself of the poisons of imprisonment. His diarrhea let up gradually, then stopped. His temperature mounted insidiously, then went into a decline. He perspired more and more heavily. His breathing grew more and more res boo ment and difficult. At the same time it grew more and more efficient. All of these changes were injuries to $FIRSTNAMEB0, but his mind was too opaque...", "0_18_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six eighteen in the evening</span>, and all along the Vegas Strip, in the coffee shops, in the Pirate Ships, in the Wild Bills, in the Intergals, in the Teepees, Beamers, and Outdoor Sexones, darkness gripped like a terrible sadness, and people drifted about aimlessly, looking for nothing, wanting only to lose a little more. Outside the paper moon raced across the sky. Somewhere, the inevitable occurred.\nI could not bear the pain and loneliness any longer. I twirled down a vortex of elevator music and end-of-day weariness, and suddenly I was in a terrible, terrible room with immense drapes of crimson silk, in a florid, palmed, fanned, enormous room all done up in the baroque tropicalooche style ... a white telephone with no dial, a bar of black European crystal, a giant mirror slanting crazily into a four point perspective of leering tintypes, some stumps of fat red candles, a pink foaming beer in a cylindrical glass, surrounded by the true and correct scent of Formosa Strait . . . a music box tinkling out a terrible lounge medly, the theme song from Midnight Cowboy, Peter Gunn\u2019s signature, a tuneless, flat...", "1_6_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-three in the morning</span>; the place was a building called the Marina Towers. And the middle-aged man in question\u2014a man who had occupied a front seat, center, in the life of our nation\u2014now stood before me, so ancient and pale and tremulous that he was hardly a man at all. His name was still Palmer Eldritch; but I knew right off that he was no more than the ghost of that terrible old man.\n\"How do you do,\" he said. \"How do you do.\"\nWe stood in the wide corridor, a man in white and a young boy. He had now become, you understand, the very father of a boy he had never seen before. And so each of us tended to skip over a generation, so to speak, in his thoughts, and for my part I saw him as a small boy, nearing My age, and wondered, What does he think of me? This old fellow here?\n\"Fine boy,\" he said to himself. He had only to raise his eyes to look directly into the mirror before which I stood. \"Raise him up.\"\n\"You mean to\u2014like this?\" I inquired softly. For suddenly I wanted very much to understand.\n\"To be your own true son.\"", "1_21_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-seven in the evening</span>.\nHe rolled down the last ramp and the sidewalks were there, forty feet away.\nHe stopped the cart momentarily, slowly, ten feet above the sidewalk. Slowly he lowered the cart until the front wheels made contact.\nHe looked down upon the one hundred and fifty-fifth street of New York City.\nHe raised the cart again, floated it level above the sidewalk, so high above the street booths and hawkers looked up, awed, from their uneasy stalls, their fantastic trade of quick deaths and other diversions to make the hideous time pass.\nNeither $SURNAME0 nor his followers had ever exposed themselves freely to the common gaze before. Their skins were used to the beneficent blindfold of space. Like all the other aristocrats of space, they had never had to let rabble stare right into their faces unmasked.\n$SURNAME0 looked down into a camera before a convex show window two stories below.\n\"Hello, Satan!\" said a man behind the camera. He was selling his ware in the window, something that needed showmanship as much as Paradise Lost, The Intimate Life of Herbert Hoover, McCarthy onMcCarthy, or The Sensational Diary of a Washington Hostess. He had chosen to call his display...", "1_12_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-eight post meridiem</span>, which is the same time that Lenore comes to work, having had a shower and a cup of tea and dressed and rung Vlad the Impaler\u2019s bell downstairs no less than six times before he\u2019d finally heard her and come down in a bathrobe citing Russian. Gribble had on green Polaroid glasses, a huge scruffy beard, and just a towel around his waist and was standing in his apartment surrounded by a half-dozen large oilskin steamer trunks of various ages, some padlocked, some open and showing clothes, Lenore saw through the doorway into his bedroom. There were clothes everywhere.\n\u201cHi,\u201d she said. \u201cHeard there was a problem, Vlad said. You\u2019d better get dressed. Norman Bombardini is supposed to come at one, I think.\u201d\nLang sat up in the trunk of his car, cranked off the transparent plastic window, and stuck his head out. His eyes were wide. \u201cLenore I think I\u2019m dead.\u201d\n\u201cRick who\u2019s that?\u201d\n\u201cHello?\u201d\n\u201cReporting for work, sort of. Although I\u2019m...", "2_22_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> in Newport, Rhode Island.\nThe Newport clock on which $FIRSTNAMEB2 left the image of his family was the great clock over the doors of Salo\u2019s Art Museum.\n$SURNAME0 and Salo were strolling among the Spirit of America dioramas on the first floor of the museum. The figurines were seven feet tall.\n$SURNAME0 climbed a stepladder, removed the Martin Luther King, Junior, miniature from its shelf.\n\"Now this is a very interesting one,\" said $SURNAME0. \"This makes the second time it\u2019s been left out.\n\"It was left out in April, 1941, during the middle of the war scares,\" said $SURNAME0. \"That was three months before the time of your birth, chrono. So you were obviously born to play a role in this little drama.", "1_10_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-one in the morning</span> of or about a week following the announcement about the stone and the inscription. The colonel was sitting at his desk in his office in the basement of his house on District Line Road, teaching a Sunday-morning Bible-class. His class was composed of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 Rumgudgeon, and $FIRSTNAMEB2, their son. They were studying the Stone Tables of the Law.\n$SURNAME1, Rumgudgeon, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were soberly dressed in black. They were all three dully clean, as befitted people who were attending a Bible-class. Their skins had none of the lustre and vivacity, none of the gaiety and bad health and good spirits, none of the mischievous delight in creation that marks the skin of a naive and happy man in clean clothes.\nThe happiness of a healthy man creates its own gaiety and lustre and sparkle. $SURNAME1, Rumgudgeon, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were not happy. They were all sick, and sick people don\u2019t have good skins.\n$SURNAME1 and Rumgudgeon were seventy-two. $FIRSTNAMEB2, their son, was thirty-three.\n$SURNAME1 was tall and stooped. He had been a handsome, blond, outgoing...", "0_21_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $SURNAME1, the all-around-girl, trilled, hummed, pounded harmonium and drew the plans of new rockets. The plans were falling apart, goosedrawn and illogical as they were. $SURNAME1, who worshipped some pagan goddess of war, had named her creations after the daughters of giants. $FIRSTNAMEB1, mighty in battle, was almost finished. Gomer, who was to be lofted a record-making 137,822 feet into the air, was only an unsubstantial skeleton. The plans for the new and improved Gomorrah had so far escaped $SURNAME1. The plans were concealed in a secret place.\nNan herself, limp with disuse, was barely able to outline, step by step, the method of going down the stairway.\nToo much rocking and the guns would explode. So would $FIRSTNAMEB1 if $SURNAME1 was careless enough to send him up into the air with a badly oiled screw.\nMuch too much hammering, and the stairway would crumble to bits\u2014and the bits would drift about in space until they collided with something.\nSome fragments of the staircase had come to rest on a half-made Miss Canaan. But enough of it remained to climb down. Much too much of it remained to be...", "1_23_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-six in the evening</span>, according to the clock in the square. The trams were still running. There was a tram on each side of the clock, and a little crowd had collected to watch them go. The two conductors were shouting at each other, and one of the conductors hit the big bell as if he were mad about something. This pleased the American lady very much. I decided that she was probably pretty drunk. She kept shaking Homer\u2019s brother\u2019s arm, to call his attention to the conductors. The brother only looked at her with a bored and fused expression. He didn\u2019t seem to care about the conductors. The American lady paid no attention. She rocked back and forth on her hips as she looked at the conductors. I saw that she had plenty of money to pay her fare, and they weren\u2019t taking any fares at the moment, because the trams were very crowded. The conductors had their hands full arguing with the pretty women who tried to board the trams. The street in front of the Caf\u00e9 Select was very bright from the electric signs and the light from the caf\u00e9. Across the street were high buildings with their windows dark and their roofs sharp against the night sky. Above the roofs was the slender dark...", "2_10_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-one in the morning</span>.\nBy the chronometer built into $SURNAME0\u2019s space suit, it was one ten forty-two Greenwich mean time.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s irony did not, of course, hinge on the fact that he alone in the Solar System was not enjoying a gold. He felt no personal bitterness over the fact that the only gold happening on the Solar System\u2019s greatest gold deposit was going on thousands of feet under his feet and out of reach. His irony stemmed solely from the fact that he stood on what was by far the most important spot on Mars\u2014and by rights should be having the liveliest, most absorbing experience of all.\nThere was plainly going to be a metaclock synchronicity in the Ten-a-Fly gold-finding project.\nThere would be a gala group appearance of Martians.\n$SURNAME0 shrugged. He wasn\u2019t concerned about missing the Martians.", "1_19_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe door of the space ship was opened by $FIRSTNAMEB0. He held the handle of a hacksaw blade in the five fingers of one hand.\nThe beast that opened the door was swathed and hooded in a baggy skin-tight garment of pale-green, high-impact plastic. The hood came down over the creature\u2019s muzzle.\nThe hood contained two round, glassy clusters of yellow eyes, a pair of small round pink-rimmed nostrils, and a flexible triangular mouthful of pure-white fangs.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 watched with great interest as the eyes in the hood winked and blinked in understanding, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s sword had not been a menace, after all. It was now easier for $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the creature outside to see each other.\nThe ship was flooded with light from an overhead grid of cold white tubes.\nThe creature took a shuffling step into the ship.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 stepped back involuntarily, shutting the door against the pressure of six feet of snow behind it.\nThe snow supported the ship, as though it were the bottom of a bore hole.\nThe creature shuffled in again, crowding $FIRSTNAMEB0 against the wall. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s response to the...", "1_10_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty in the morning</span>. I stepped up to the bar and asked the man for a half-pint of beer.\n\u201cCold beer?\u201d he said. \u201cOr would you rather have it hot?\u201d\n\u201cThe hell with it,\u201d I said. \u201cJust give me a glass of water.\u201d\nI drank the water, paid, and walked out. What I needed was a psychiatrist. What I had to do was to get into working shape so I could face myself when I looked into the wet, and dusty mirror of my soul.\nMy attorney was sitting on the bed wrapped in a white sheet and reading The Fountainhead . He looked up, shielding his eyes from the sun with his hand.\n\u201cAh, yes. Roark,\u201d he said. \u201cThere is a man who really stands for something; something we don\u2019t have anymore.\u201d\n\u201cLike what?\"\n\u201cAn ambitious man with principles.\u201d\n\u201cWhat kind of principles?\"\n\u201cThe kind you don\u2019t mess around with. The kind you can believe in without sitting up nights, wondering if you believe in them.\u201d I noticed the book was printed in...", "0_8_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight seventeen in the morning</span>. My attorney advised me that I\u2019d better have a fast car & a freeway to get me to the airport in an hour\u2014tourist exit, head straight for the Barn . . . \u201cDon\u2019t stop for red lights,\u201d he said. \u201cIf some cop pulls you over, show him your press badge & tell him you HAVE to get to the airport. Go straight to the Teppaz Box\u2014don\u2019t even stop for gas.\u201d I hung up, quick\u2014then realized the monster machine was still out there in the red grapefruit glare, snorting & banging in neutral with overcoated Acapulco tourists all over the place jerking their heads, baffled by it.\nThis was the final proof that I was leaving Las Vegas forever. I rushed down to the garage & tried to explain the situation to the attending stoner/mechanic, grabbing my Acapulco clothes & telling him I\u2019d OD\u2019d on mescaline. \u201cMy attorney\u2019s on his way over,\u201d I said. \u201cWe\u2019ll sign any fucking papers! Just get the car ready! I\u2019ve got to get out of town in...", "1_16_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was the harmonium of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s memory editor, a bare and dusty room on the second floor of a building on Stagg Street in the town of Batavia, Illinois.\nThe harmonium was an instrument that produced a flowing stream of melody and harmony when a person sat before it, concentrated, and played. The instrument had an advantage over a piano or a mandolin or a violin, since it had eighty-seven keys instead of five, fifteen stops instead of two, and a pedal keyboard of thirty-three notes instead of eight. The possible combinations of sound were therefore enormously greater.\nA harmonium player could produce an astonishing variety of sounds that drifted from a lyric murmur to a clangorous clang.\nThe harmonium player could, for instance, hurl his instrument at the sky with one hand and make fat, dissonant chords with the pedal with the other hand.\nThe player could pick out single notes with touching sensitivity and fill the hall with their resonance. Or he could hum into the box through cupped hands, and make the harmonium give back a distant choir.\nHe could stutter, shout, moan, scrape, and roar. He could shout passages of blazing fa-sol-la...", "1_16_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the harmoniums had been marching, sneaking, lying hidden and feeding themselves for twenty-two and a half hours.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 perceived, with the purity of a mathe- matician or a small child, that they were travelling in a spiral\u2014in a spiral that was becoming tighter and tighter.\nThe harmoniums had marched him, without his knowing it, right back to the neighborhood where he had started.\nThe old church was ten paces off.\nA hole had appeared in the rain beside the stone steps.\nAbove the hole, he could be sure, was a thick yellow rope ladder.\nAnd up that rope ladder had come the ghostly, in- visible coming and going of a rescue party. It had come and gone since the last time $FIRSTNAMEB0 had come this way\u2014a full day before.\nThe rummagings of the rescue party had been as mysterious as the rummagings of the harmoniums. The only thing that had been taken from the old church, apparently, was the one thing that wouldn\u2019t have been of the slightest use to the harmoniums\u2014the one thing the harmoniums hadn\u2019t known about. Namely, the ladder that led to...", "1_19_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-five in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, approaching the door to the house with tremendous dignity and playing his harmonica like a madman, saw a spaceship land in the backyard. The craft was as big as a mid-sized hotel. It was cream-colored, with a dark stripe that ran down its middle. $FIRSTNAMEB2 ran off to be puzzled by the craft.\nThe $SURNAME0 estate had been sold to the government so that new factories could be built there. The government had let the estate stand, however, pending coordination of its sale with the purchase of land for an industry by the owners of the Pocket-Smith Sport-A-Pak Corporation of Indianapolis.\n$SURNAME0 and his space dog were managing the estate for the government, as though it were still theirs.\n$SURNAME0, as usual, did not make an immediate entrance. It was his policy to make guests think that they were completely alone.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 waited inside the door, feeling foolish and hoping he wouldn\u2019t choke on the lump in his throat.\nHell\u2019s bells, thought $SURNAME1\u2014if you\u2019re a millionaire, where did you get the life you\u2019re living? What kind of a nut-house universe is this, anyway, where a guy goes...", "1_16_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-one in the afternoon</span> when we rolled into Denver. Mile after mile of roadside pitifulness zoomed by the windows -- hobo jungles, wino colonies, shabby hotels, used-car lots, rickety restaurants, junkyards--the funky verge of the Rocky Mountain Empire. \u201cSee if you can find some tobacco for my pipe, Tim,\u201d said my attorney. \u201cThink I'll take a walk around town -- stretch my legs.\"\nHere was our big opportunity to get off the bus and disappear while Red drove on to Mexico alone. I could make it to the Canadian border by nightfall and be home in Monument well before Red...assuming he didn't catch me with that blonde in the ice-cream parlor. Yes, this was the critical point: how much can I trust my attorney? More specifically, how far can I trust him to go on trusting me? For example, would he be willing to continue feeding those fake checks to our immigration man after I had abandoned the trip and thrown myself on the mercy of habeas corpus in the local jail? His own ass would be on the line too, of course, but there is a basic difference between us. I haven't got any ass. I'm that far ahead of the...", "2_12_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-nine p.m.</span>\nIf someone had said to Watermelon Smiles, \"This is the most important moment of your life,\" he might have realized the truth of what was said. But no one did say it, so he remained ignorant.\nThe time was twelve twenty-nine p.m.\nThe one thing that would break hearts when the story reached the outside world was the cruelty of $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 to Watermelon Smiles. They led him to the piston, by the hand.\nThey made him climb up on the block of cold metal.\nThey made him lie down across it.\nThey tied him fast.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 put their lucky balls into the little round depressions in the iron block.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s ball and $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s ball were in the air-control cubbyholes to the right and left of Watermelon Smiles\u2019 neck.\nThe time was twelve thirty p.m.\nAnd then\u2014then, without any warning to poor Watermelon Smiles, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 closed a switch and sent the piston humming.\nThe time was twelve thirty-one p.m.", "0_0_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after midnight</span>.\n\"Now we\u2019re really on Mars, sir,\" the sergeant said.\nA space-ship is a church.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 had seen the twelve space-ships in the Temples of Venus, Mercury, Mars, and Ceres, had found them noble and unbelievably exciting. He felt sure, now, that the ships in the temples on Jupiter, Saturn, Neptune, and Uranus would fill him with the same kind of awe. He would explore them, worship in them, and then he would fly on a ship to other planets, trailing his coat in the wind, making his own hours and his own rules, an independent man.\nNobody would know when or where he might show up again.\nAnd this great and glorious adventure would carry on after $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 was dead.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 felt suddenly that he was too good for the Earth and all its shabby arrangements.\nHe wanted to fling his arms out in the night and say to all the people he had known, to all the girls he had only kissed, \"See? This is something!\"\nThe next planet would be Mercury. The ship would hang in space above it for forty-five minutes before settling down. There was time for a sandwich.\nSergeant...", "0_11_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-two in the morning</span> and we had fifty-eight minutes to catch the car. It was sitting out in front of the Western Union office in San Antonio, the man said. And so after agreeing to meet in Washington, D.C. , in a few days they shot off. And when they were gone, when it was just me and the bartender again, I ordered another beer and thought things over a bit. Well, what of it? Why worry? Just ride along, that's all; things will work out as they will, it's all the same. Just go to the westernmost edge of Texas, where it kicks up against New Mexico , which is the easternmost edge of the United States , and there you are. There was work to be done, since I wanted to stay out of the Service. I decided to forget the GRE and fuck a few girls and pass some whole days thumbing around mountain roads; that was all I had to do. So I ordered another beer and paid for the first one, and at eleven-fifty shoulder-hopped a Jeep that was going to the University of Texas for a half dollar's ride. It had two girls in it and I sat next to the prettier of the two and after we had made some dizzy...", "0_0_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past midnight</span>. The son of Matisse was lying fast asleep on his bed, snoring slightly\u2014he was forty years old, and still lived at home. The walls of his room were lined with grey Madison Avenue dust that had drifted in and settled over the years. The dust was like dirt and gave the room a rundown look.\nAlong one wall, in a stately row, were six of the nine pictures Matisse had painted of his son. There was no space on the walls for any others because of the large number of paintings and drawings the son had done himself, works in which the hills of West Virginia figured largely. His ambition had been to be the Champion Amateur Artist of the Southern Appalachians; this had become a burden to him, and he had flunked out in the early rounds of the Fall Crushed Stone tournament in 1954. His most recent painting showed a close-up of the back of a man\u2019s head. He had painted it a year ago as a special exercise in realistic portraiture in order to overcome his tendency to depict people as if they were made up of various-sized stones, rocks, or pieces of rock; there were roughly a hundred stones in the back of the head in the picture.\nThe painting had done...", "0_21_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The sun had not yet set. Buzzardistic violet, mauve, and black flames were shooting up from the surface of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s burning property.\n$SURNAME1, $SURNAME0, and Salo watched the fire from the foyer of the Volunteer Fire Department\u2019s building on the west side of town. They were silhouetted against the flaming west.\nThe burning property had, in many ways, turned out to be a bonanza for the town of Newport. Wooden houses cost a fortune to build and maintain, and no one but the very rich could afford one.\nHere was the instant town of the future \u2014 houses of stone, houses that would last a thousand years.\nThe wooden houses melted like gingerbread houses in the rain. Every citizen got a stone house for nothing.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s flaming estate had skimmed the town of Newport of poverty, too, by making each citizen, even the blackest, a potential fat cat. A lucky find of strontium 90 had put low-grade, inexpensive, clean radioactive waste in the same category as brown gold.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s burning estate was turning each citizen into a shareholder in a boom mining company.\nOnly a few small...", "2_18_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nThere was no fumigation at Newport. As a consequence the population remained high.\nThe mysterious room of theRumfoord mansion in which young $FIRSTNAMEF3 was raised had another entrance\u2014another exterior entrance. Inside, one went on a roller-skate to make any real headway. The interior entrance was through a drawing room.\nOne roller-skates in from a drawing room to a mystery room. One roller-skates out again into a drawing room to find a fountain in the center of the floor bubbling a sweet pink fluid that makes one feel ever so close to God.\nThe interior entrance was locked. $FIRSTNAMEF0 used a gold key to get in. She was carrying fresh underwear for young $FIRSTNAMEF3. She saw $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 right away, standing on his roller-skates five feet from the fountain in the floor.\n$SURNAME1 didn\u2019t see her.\nHe was petrified.\nHis eyes were peering over the tops of his tinted glasses. He was staring at a slab of iron in the wall before him. The slab of iron was so rusty that it seemed ready to crumble away at the touch of a gossamer-winged butterfly.", "2_21_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nOn Tuesday the twenty-second of September, a howling north wind blew smoke and soot down from industrial Euclid onto the shivering trees and gardens of Cleveland Heights. The smell of smoke hung thickly in the humid air. It mixed with the wet-earth smell of good soil tilled and nourished. In every direction but one, the Cleveland sky was blotted out by the haze of industrial work. Over the Bombardini mansion was sky, cloudy and healthy and wet.", "0_22_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, materialized on the terrace outside $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s room.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4, a magnificent animal, collided with the trim parabolic contour of the parapet, so sharply did he round a corner in following his master. He collided with a low-flying meteor, and died in the collision.\nHis master did not linger to explain.\nSomewhere above Tralfamadore, the six suns of Space Wanderer II flared simultaneously, fusing the chrono-synclastic infundibula of the planet into a piece of black glass.\nAnd somewhere, as far from Tralfamadore as the Earth is from the Sun, the core and driving engine of the ill-starred ship became bright yellow from heat, twisted in two like tinfoil, collapsed.\nSomewhere in the neighborhood of Tralfamadore, the ghost ship of the three false Martian mice became a cloud of iron vapor, a very small and lovely part of the atmosphere of Tralfamadore.\nCHAPTER SIXTEEN\n\nEARTH, APPROACHING\nOld $FIRSTNAMEB6 Lucas, repairman, scout, and Admiral of the EXEC...", "2_7_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was a juke box in the diner. On the juke box was a record that was playing.\nThe record wasn\u2019t loud. It was tinkly, saccharine.\nThe record was singing:\nThe birds keep saying, \"Over here! Over here!\"\nAnd I keep saying, \"What, what?\"\nBut birds don\u2019t talk ...\nAnd I don\u2019t care ...\nBut birds don\u2019t talk.\nWhat birds don\u2019t do.\nThey don\u2019t talk.\nAnd I don\u2019t care ...\nBut birds don\u2019t talk.\n\"Oh, just a minute or so,\" said $SURNAME1, \"since I announced myself this morning.\"\nThe old man stopped slapping at flies to listen.\n\"Talked to a roomful of people,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Talked to them last night, talked to them after dinner.\"\n\"Yee-ah,\" said the old man.", "0_18_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe sun was hot and red, a red that made the colors all strange. There was no shadows anywhere, because there were no clouds, but the colors were as though there were shadows.\nThe colors were soft and sad.\nNothing was loud.\nThe colors did not suggest gaiety. They did not suggest joy, or happiness.\nThe colors themselves were sad.\nThe colors themselves were individually sad, and when a person looked at them he became sad and lost all desire to move or to do anything. He just wanted to look at the colors, and the colors were soft and quietly sad.\nA light wind was blowing.\nThe Palace of Fine Arts was a bright white against the great background of blue and red.\nThere were many colors.\nThe colors of the Palace of Fine Arts were white and green and pieces of gilded bronze.\nThe colors were separated and arranged skillfully, so that they were soft, and yet subtly suggested many ideas.\nThe Palace of Fine Arts had been made to suggest that the world was once very beautiful, and that men had lost their desire for beauty.\nThe Palace of Fine Arts had been made to suggest many things concerning the world and concerning man. The Palace of Fine Arts had been...", "2_1_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe windows of the apartment of Alfred and $FIRSTNAMEF1 McReverend, on the eleventh floor of Building 19 of the Boulderbrook Apartments on 75-9127K in Cleveland, looked out over the dirty light of Euclid Avenue.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 McReriend sat in a plastic chair, facing the meshed window, and watched the slow passage of the lights of Euclid Avenue.\nOutside the window, on the sill, there was a three-dimensional yard-bird cut from paper, topped by a plastic baseball cap. A bright blue plastic tarpaulin covered the unassembled piece of a Chinese puzzle.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 McReverend sat in her chair and faced the window, with her back to the aquarium.\nThere were two hundred and seventeen fish in the aquarium. There were hundred-twenty-six guppies, twenty-four sail-fin mollies, ninety-one swordtails, ten platies, twelve angelfish, eleven zebra danio, nine archer fish, and four mosquito fish. The water in the aquarium was changed and aerated by a small pump, on a timer, once every two hours.", "0_9_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>. The position of the Tralfamadorians looking down on Earth from the heavens at that moment was ten degrees east of the star Delta Pavonis, flying at terrific speed along the course of the ecliptic toward the sun.\nAt the nursing home, workmen were adjusting the loudspeaker in the gigantic clock face of the Building 49 lounge. A folded shiny aluminum ladder lay on the floor. About five o'clock that morning, the ladder had materialized in the middle of the room.\nNo one wanted anything to do with the ladder. It was the only thing that anybody could remember that ever materialized in a room.\nIt had something to do with babies being born and people dying, people who lived in the building had decided, and because of this hopeful idea, they left the ladder alone. They wanted nothing to do with it and wouldn\u2019t go near it. Nobody wanted to unfold it and climb it\u2014it looked dangerous and seemed so wobbly there on the floor in the middle of the room.\nOld Morris Katz went and opened the big, ornate front door, through which hundreds of vaporized cigarette butts and patches of smokers\u2019 cloying scent had been drifting. He went to open the door because...", "1_5_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-four in the morning</span>, according to $FIRSTNAMEM2, who had a stopwatch in his lap and a clipboard on the seat beside him. Every half-minute he made a mark. At the rate he was making them he would have used up a whole pad by the time twenty-four hours had gone by.\nHe had been at the job for more than fourteen hours, now. He\u2019d had only one short break between four and five A.M., when he ran up to his room for a cup of coffee and something to munch.\nI had, of course, been up all night myself but I didn\u2019t seem to need much sleep any more.\nI was also worried about the cost of what we were doing. Every time a batch of four limos was over-due at the Key West airport airline people got on the phone and started asking questions. The bill was growing at a terrible rate. Already it was enough to make you curl up and watch your eyes roll.\nI decided to ask $FIRSTNAMEM2 what the hell he thought he was doing. For company, I dialed his room.\nA groggy voice answered almost immediately. \"Who is this?\"\n\"It\u2019s me,\" I said. \"The one who\u2019...", "0_16_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 had just got himself set again. He now had a car, a brand-new Pontiac sedan that $FIRSTNAMEF4 had helped her rich mother to buy in Denver, first stopping off there to say goodbye and also to have a petition written to the judge who'd give her probation on drug charges, so she could leave the state, and yet be able to get back in. This car we had left in Raymond so it wouldn't be known we were flashing around with a automobile. Now the three of us were in this secondhand Hudson; $FIRSTNAMEB3, without a job, had no money. We had stopped at $FIRSTNAMEF4's parents' house in North Carolina to pick up their daughter Malena to join us; she'd been on the road with us before. Polynice was sleeping in back, sniffling, his nose running. The old man was absent. We all bustled in with no hello only to realize that $FIRSTNAMEF4 hadn't the key and her mother, though at home, was busy with the baby and left the door locked. The key, we saw from a window, was inside the house. We talked. \u201cWhere can we get a key?\u201d \u201cThat man down there saw my father today.\u201d", "0_8_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-seven in the morning</span>, October 17, 1974, ten days before the beginning of the trial of the thousand-year cyborg conspiracy against the Earth. My attorney was just getting around to the business of opening his briefcase. \u201cThank God,\u201d I wrote in my notebook that night. \u201cThe tension is broken at last. We are calling on all of our con fdences and resources now\u2014going for broke in a frenzy of legal and personal violence that will either leave us shattered, or free.\u201d My attorney was examining a sheaf of legal\u2014looking documents one of which, I noticed, was the deed to Isadora\u2019s garage and yard.\nI looked at the house across the street. Eleven years before, in the crystal consciousness of a fresh LSD revelation, I had judiciously moved into this mellow, cheerfully dissolute neighborhood in north Las Vegas, down the street from one of the principal bawdy houses in the city. The house was a typical Vegas affair, only seven years old, a cheap imitation of an Old California mission\u2014 Spanish, with a red tiled roof and clay\u2014colored stucco; the architecture of oppression according to the Encyclopedia Britannica. The neighbors were mostly elderly people, the late refugees...", "0_8_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-four in the morning</span> of November 1, 2184, Martian time\u2014five hours before the Blue Team\u2019s scheduled departure for $FIRSTNAMEB2. Titanic was still soaring inward on its line-of-flight toward the sun from Mars.\n\"Unleaded, please,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, attempting a smile.\nThe bartender tossed a spray can into the air, caught it, tore off the press-tab.\n\"On the house,\" he said.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 unscrewed the cap from his spray can, touched the spout experimentally to his wrist, as though for a temperature reading. He did not like the idea of drinking leaded whisky.\nHis eyes remained fixed on his wrist as he raised the nozzle of the can to his mouth,and pressed. Some of the whisky sprayed out around his lips. He licked it off. The taste was old-tasting and medicinal\u2014less like whisky, than like embalming fluid.\nHe gulped the whisky, felt it hot and tingling as it went down his gullet. The stimulating effect was almost nil. His heart went on pounding as though it had been stopped for minutes instead of seconds by the shock treatment.\n\"I wonder\u2014\" ventured $FIRSTNAMEB2, squinting at the...", "1_10_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-four in the morning</span>. The place was Stonecipheco Baby Food\u2019s Infant Nutrition Laboratory in Building 257-LM. The Chief Scientist was, with one co-worker, studying a problem of potentially great import. The Chief Scientist\u2019s co-worker was a long-time friend and confidante, a woman who had worked with the Chief Scientist for all of twelve years, and was neither an employee nor a contractor for Stonecipheco, and who, in point of fact, did not exist at all.\nThe lab work and much of the scientific theory underpinning the work of the two scarce real individuals inside the otherwise wholly imaginary lab had been created from whole cloth by the Chief Scientist\u2019s long-time, nonexistent friend, who lived an independent and extremely active life eleven miles due north of the real, the utterly imaginary Infant Nutrition Laboratory Building 257-LM.\nElectrical whimpers of the Chief Scientist and her co-worker were the only sounds in the lab. The whimpers came from behind the Vision-Hearing Protectors, which were part of the scientists\u2019 Personal Protective Equipment and covered their ears, eyes, nostrils, mouths. Only the scientists\u2019 eyes were exposed. They were watching a test that...", "2_5_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. Outside, the dawn was grey. They were still laughing at me. I grabbed the grapefruit and hurled it across the room. Then I opened my eyes and saw the disastrous sunrise. Damn, there's no ether in this town, I thought. I sat up in bed and turned on the radio. They were playing Ravel's Concerto for the Left Hand: \u2019/ always wake up to that music when I take acid,\u201c I said to myself. \u201cThat's the first record I put on.\u201d Obviously. That lovely final moment of musical climax. That dreadful moment when the piano begins tapping out a disconnected Morse code message from God . . .\nThen what? I wondered. Heaven? Hell? Where are they? How do I find them? I looked out the window and saw no answers there. No comfort but the sight of silent lights in other lonely hotel rooms. No dawn but that unnatural grey twilight that acid always brings on when it reaches its middle point, when you realize that the drug is still in your system and that it's too late to throw it away. Too late to make an end of it. Panic hour.", "2_16_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nAli, who is both Judas and Saint Peter of the clock, opened his bag of treasures and let them flow over the land. Where grains of sand lead and lumps of gold follow the human family is soon to be found in the shadow of its most benevolent father, who brings them the end of suburban apathy and the beginning of holy serious agony.\nA gaunt young stranger appeared in Brewer to teach them.\nHe arrived one night in an old bus. It was a rainy night, and the main highway was a muddy river of traffic. The bus stopped on the outskirts in the darkness under the trees and the gaunt young stranger got off. The door closed, the lights went out, the pulls were yanked, and the old bus went off across the marshes, yodeling in the dark. It was exactly midnight.", "1_18_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB6 $FIRSTNAMEM0 still lay curled on the space ship's design floor, suffering from the light. He lay mostly by himself, attended to by three nurses, one of whom was cooperating hallucinatorily. The other two, who were in reality Martian jet pilots disguised as nurses, were plotting to betray $FIRSTNAMEB6 $FIRSTNAMEM0 to their superiors in the Tralfamadorian fleet. They were plotting in the form of playing a game of two-handed Canfield. They had managed to get themselves a pack of cigarettes. Only one of the two pilots was smoking. He pretended the pack contained the poems of Sappho.\nThe President of the United States and the Space Wanderer were almost home.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had never moved a muscle in his right arm and right leg since $FIRSTNAMEM2 had opened the door of his time machine to let a real tiger into the sanctum sanctorum. $SURNAME1 was trying to look nonchalant, but his arm and leg hurt like hell. He couldn\u2019t feel his fingertips or the soles of his feet.\nThe Earth of this time was now hopelessly doomed. There would never be another explosion. This time would be history\u2019s last chance to laugh at $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. He would be shown to have done...", "1_1_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>. Outside the window, the rocket shaft pointed like an accusing finger up at the hot, moist, round face of Earth.\nGod help me, The Court of Appeals was right. I should have ended it right there, in Mercury Station reentry concourse B, before I talked myself into it....\nDead men are heavier than broken hearts. I had experience with neither. For the next three hours I wrestled my half-corpse of a captain into a sack, out of the Palace, into a taxicab, and up to the terminal gate at LAX. My plan was to wait until the beautiful gibbous Earth had filled the terminal viewscreen, in order to make the formal announcement\u2014so I could not call for help. My plan went haywire when somebody at Purchasing sent up a hunchback. He said his name was $FIRSTNAMEB1, and he had been sent from Washington to help me with my hooker problem. The looks I gave him were enough to make a brothel foreman jealous.\n\"Are you the captain?\" he asked. I shook my head. He made a few decisions right away. I was an arreF, an affliction, and a dead man to boot.\n\"One moment...", "2_3_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-three in the morning</span>.\n\"Go back,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Back?\" said $SURNAME0. \"What do you mean, go back?\"\n\"I want to go back to a pleasant place,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I want to go back to Mars.\"\n\"What for?\" said $SURNAME0. \"You weren\u2019t happy there!\"\n\"I\u2019m trapped!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I can\u2019t go forward! I can\u2019t go back!\"\n\"I\u2019m trapped, too,\" said $SURNAME0. \"But we can still talk.\"\nThe blue space ship materialized in the twirling eddies of dust outside the storage room.\n\"Mercurian\u2014\" said a space ship mate, coming into the storage room. He stopped, speechless.\n\"This is Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 of Newport, Rhode Island, U. S. A.,\" said $SURNAME0 to the mate. \"Her husband will be boarding the ship in a minute or so, and I advise you to have the manual open to his full description.\"", "2_16_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-one in the afternoon</span>. The light turned red and the traffic began to scream. All around us were tangled lanes of bumper\u2014to\u2014bumper frustration. My attorney thought for a moment that I'd suddenly flipped, as a result of the trauma, he said, of leaving his lovely child and my natural vexation at being killed on the Neon\u2014lit escalator. I bent over the dash and emptied my stomach into the beautiful Whale. I wiped my lips.\n\u201cWhat the hell are you doing?\u201d he whispered.\n\u201cLook over your left shoulder,\u201d I said. \u201cRight out your left window.\"\nHe peeked nervously. On our right was a huge traffic\u2014holding parking lot filled, I saw, with battered trailers surrounded by yapping rotweillers.\n\u201cThey must belong to that truck\u2014stop out on the highway,\u201d he said. \u201cMaybe they deliver the equipment to the cafe.\"", "1_17_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-seven in the afternoon</span> on a hot October twenty-third in the awful year of American victory in the Second World War in the Western European Theater.\nThe time was five thirty-seven in the afternoon on a hot October twenty-third in the awful year of American victory in the Second World War in the Western European Theater.\nThe clock had just struck five thirty-seven when a new Lancia sedan came wheeing and chugging down the road from the north, bringing blaxploitation film star Montana Max to the home of his good friend Mars.\nThe time was five thirty-seven in the afternoon on a hot October twenty-third in the awful year of American victory in the Second World War in the Western European Theater.\nThe road from the north on which the wheezy, chugging Lancia came was a very good, hard, paved road, leading all the way to the far, far north of Greece and Turkey, Canada, Denmark, and Siberia.\nThe windshield of the Lancia was of course not made of glass, but of a strong and heat-resistant material invented by a Frenchman in 1806.\nThe doorway of the home of Mars, the Etruscan, was not made of wood, but of steel. It...", "0_7_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-three in the morning</span> of the last day of the unhappy war, and Warden Bruce R. Schirger of the Ohio State Penitentiary (five-foot-four without shoes, one hundred twenty-six pounds, a little man if one stands close enough) was moving swiftly in the light rain of the All-American July along the southeast corridor of the third tier of Cellblock 5 in the half-light supplied by a row of feebly whistling fluorescent tubes. He passed through a drop-well that took him on his way to the cells fronting on the chute. As . he was about to vanish below a filling sip of converging wall, he gave a tremendous, merry hoot that echoed down the empty well in a haunting, weird cry that was quickly lost in the subterranean quartz of five-foot-thick concrete.\nTwo minutes later, Warden Schirger was at the B of the Ohio State Penitentiary, tearing a quick match into five short segments while he looked up with a comically cocked eye at the tiny barred window above him in the huge cell door. Causality had been sadly skewed one Sunday morning years before when the Reverend James J. Kyle of the East Common Ministry and some of his communicants were on their...", "0_17_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-four in the afternoon</span>. The first taxi of the day pulled up before the house, and Redwine, still in his dancing clothes, got in heavily, laughed hollowly, and told the driver to go to Ponkapog.\nThe drive onto the rocket freight ship at Ponkapog takeo got Redwine badly sea-sick. He lost his breakfast and everything else.\nNot till the rocket blasted off did Redwine grow calm and comparatively cheerful. Soon the Earth was only a blue, glowing jade sphere, dwindling among the stars.\n\"There she goes,\" Redwine said, wanting to get drunk but not daring to, lest he lose his job on the Moon. \"Good-by, you wonderful planet, you lovely old Mother Earth\u2014good-by forever.\"\nIt was in a small Mexican cantina, while Redwine was breakfasting on huevos rancheros and smoking his first post-monarchical Monte Cristo cigar, that he encountered a blithe stranger with a grin like a skull which shook Redwine\u2019s hand and said, \"So you finally got away from that accursed Earth, eh?\"\nSipping tequila, Redwine put his finger to his lips, let his eyes roll, got up and t...", "2_1_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>. And then they came, shuffling noiselessly through the tunnel, seventy-two of them. They were absolutely identical, and perfectly silent. They were made out of clear Plexiglas, and capped with motorcycle helmets, and their internal lighting system had a half-power setting that gave them a pinkish tinge. Their touch set off burglar alarms. They looked, when fully empowered and in motion, like a company of ghostly bicyclists.\nThe leader of the company was made out of gold Plexiglas, and was topped with a white Stetson hat. The sight of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 made him wish that he had a cigarette.\nStanding upright in a glass showcase beside the gold leader was another of the seventy-two. He was silver\u2014mostly clear with a frosting of silver powder. He was topped with a silver turban. His turban had a turquoise in the band.", "2_5_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>, exactly the moment when our rented limousine blew the red light at a west Virginia intersection called the Milltown Two\u2014Mile\u2014Lane...five miles west of the Shenandoah. There was a northwesterly mountaintop wind blowing, and a mean shift in the velocity of speed of the sunrise...as our George Washington Boulevard\u2014rented limousine ran into what appeared to be a solid wall of blue\u2014lensewater motorcycle cops with red sirens who were running flagrantly, without headlights, right in the middle of the goddamn intersection, preparing in the raw to run us down. These were definitely not Texas cops. They did not come from Houston. These were mean blue\u2014water ocean\u2014going motherfuckers.", "1_13_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The ship was in space, cruising at half-lightspeed\u2014and she was only three days out of Earth.\nHer name was Universe Compressor.\nShe was a space-flier\u2014a self-contained universe in itself. She was longer than three Eiffel Towers laid end to end. She was as wide as a good-sized lake. She carried a crew of twenty men and women, and a cargo of one hundred thousand white mice.\nThe Universe Compressor was a freighter plying between Earth and one of the mining planets in the Betelgeuse system. Her cargo hold was filled with two million ounces of useless garbage.\nThe white mice were laboratory animals that were going to spend the rest of their lives in the mines. They were among the hundred thousand best and brightest that the geneticists at Pocatello had come up with. Whatever did not kill them would make them rich.\nUniverse Compressor was the only ship ever to make fast, direct voyages between Earth and the mining planets. Nevertheless, her owners were reluctant to put her under the command of anyone too intelligent. A. research team was using the mice to try to locate a rich lode of germanium on the planet Venus. It was rumored...", "0_5_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. The heating system still smelled bad.\nThe telephone rang on the steel tabletop. Lenore Beadsman sat up, rubbed her eyes, looked at the telephone. The telephone rang a second time. Lenore reached out and picked it up.\nIt was Judith Prietht, on the lobby switchboard.\n\"Jesus, Judith, what is it?\" Lenore said.\n\"Pardon?\" said Judith. \u201cI hope I didn\u2019t take you from girlish slumber.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you want?\u201d\n\u201cI took a call for you. Like you said. From a guy with a foreign name.\u201d\n\u201cInman?\u201d\n\u201cThen my hair won\u2019t fall out.\u201d\n\u201cInman?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cNew York?\u201d\n\u201cYou\u2019re so smart this morning.\u201d\n\u201cThank you. That\u2019s all I need.\u201d\n\u201cExcept he called back. Still wants to talk to you.\u201d\n\u201cHe did?\u201d\n\u201cHe must.\u201d\n\u201cWell what did you...", "0_14_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was the Red Wand gas station just south of the junction of Route 41 and the entrance-ramp to the Indiana State Turnpike, Indianapolis, Indiana. The gas station is at the extreme northern tip of an area of Indianapolis known\u2014in certain circles\u2014as \"the Combat Zone.\" Five minutes from Roy K.'s, it is also five minutes from the really deluxe whores of the Canal Street district.\n\"My attorney is Nunzio Pacella,\" shouted Roy K. in the grin-electric room. \"Did you get that? N-U-N-Z-I-O P-A-C-E-L-L-A. Pacella!\" This was directed at a pimply-faced kid with hair in his eyes who was filling a Horny Honker tank at the far end of the station.\nThe kid didn't answer. \"This nimrod can't even hear,\" said Roy K. to no one in particular. \"This place is going bust, right before my eyes. It\u2019s a goddamn wonder I don\u2019t stay home and play with myself.\u201d He made a hideous leering face to indicate the playing of himself, then turned back to the bartender.", "0_8_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight eighteen in the morning</span>. The eternal was in $FIRSTNAMEB1; so was the passing moment, so-called. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was about to shine with uncommon glory for a moment. His great chance was at hand. It would arrive in three minutes. It would pass in three minutes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 didn\u2019t feel his own sensations. He was immune to his own glow.\nThe grandeur of the space ship was due entirely to a brilliant concept of the architect\u2014a concept perhaps suggested by Earthling Michelangelo. This concept was applied with imagination by the buildings\u2019 decorating engineers. The walls of the buildings were drawn between lines of heavy gold brocade, the woven threads of which stood an inch proud of the walls. The walls were kept apart from the gold lines by sheets of air.\nThis tactic of insulating walls at the same time that their material beauty is activated is most probably not to be found described in any treatise upon the subject. But the tactic was justified by the decorating engineers because it provided insulation against the Heat of Space, insulation against the Cold of Space, insulation against the Ruthlessness of Space.\nOrdinarily, in the SHIP CLONKING, BOOMING sonata of the engine pulsations, one would not have suspected the near...", "2_21_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\n/c/\n\u201cNot many of your party tonight, Rick. Who\u2019s that? Muffled, Billy Boy; make sure Vlad gets some food.\u201d\n\u201cPardon? \u201d\n\u201cWho\u2019s that you\u2019re talking to? That bird on the television screen?\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s supposed to be a female canary, out of some educational or home recreational genetic lab, Lenore. You see the patented pink-eye for the illiteracy campaign?\u201d\n\u201cHer name\u2019s Fleur; Dad\u2019s about to do a commercial for her. He\u2019s apparently always wanted to do one for her.\u201d\n\u201cI seem to recall him advertising something once, on television. A long time ago, at least.\u201d\n\u201cI know. He hates it.\u201d\n\u201cNot nearly as much as the bird obviously hates being advertised. \u201d\n\u201cDone.\u201d\n\u201cOne of the professors in the English department, who came in here with Mindy Metalman, you remember her, the one who ... ?\u201d", "0_14_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two eighteen in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 kicked open the door to the $FIRSTNAMEF0 Slim Nestles infirmary. He carried $FIRSTNAMEB0 under one arm. With his free hand, $FIRSTNAMEB1 tried to hold together the rags of his torn uniform.\nTwo twenty-one, exactly, $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 materialized on the master control board of the iron mines on Mercury. There were four minutes left to go before the explosion of the space ship that was to have winged $FIRSTNAMEB0 to Mars.\nTwo seventeen on Mercury was as dark as midnight on Earth. The mines, having been dug by machines, had no need for illumination.\nMercury is so close to the sun that the day side of the planet has all the chill and darkness of death. The half of Mercury that is always turned away from the sun has the chill and darkness of death, too.\nThe two things that break the deathly chill and darkness of Mercury are the iron mines and high-velocityEDT shuttle service to and from Venus.\nThe mines are so powerfully lighted because men must work in them, and men can\u2019t work happily in the dark.\nThe high-velocityEDT shuttle service to and from Venus is so powerfully lighted because the element in high demand...", "2_2_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two eighteen in the morning</span>. The incoming rounds were coming practically without pause.\nAll members of First Squad, B Co, were soon back under siege with the rest of the company. All wet helterskelter on the bunker floor.\nFourteen seconds later, a round hit directly on top of the bunker.\nSomeone screamed in pain.\nSecond Lieutenant Stony Stevenson, Magnum, rolled over and glared at Stony $FIRSTNAMEB0 through the porthole in the iron door.\n\"You bastard,\" said Stony Stevenson, \"you killed me again.\"\nA beautiful girl walked lasciviously into a cocktail party.\nThe men at the party accepted her presence as if they had been expecting her, as if, in fact, she were more than punctual.\nThey did not even glance at her, to satisfy themselves that she was real.\nShe was as beautiful as that.\nShe wore a daringly simple sheath of cobalt blue.\nBlue pumps. Tiny gold earrings.\nNo millinery.\nOozing wholesomeness and charm.", "2_18_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. The year was 2540. The month was May.\nThe instant of time that had just passed will be looked at for all time by chrono-synclastic infundibulated history as the perfect instant to represent the turning of the tide.\nThe tide turned at the instant when Salo introduced himself as Ironpants, when a policeman called him that perfect name.\nDuring the incredible run of spectacular happenings that were led off by the arrival of Earthlings on Mars, the Martians and the Earthlings had got to know each other very well\u2014and a liking for each other had begun to develop.\nThis liking had nearly been broken once. It had been broken by a trifle.\nOnly one thing had been wrong\u2014and that thing could have been repaired in a minute. It was as follows: Salo the iron musician was an expert marksman\u2014and by policy he always wore his space suit and his iron suit, in case a powwow got too deserted and too lonely.\nThat trifling thing that had broken the budding liking between Earthlings and Martians was the gunshot cork that Salo had just pulled out of his pocket and placed on the conference tables to represent a bullet.", "0_6_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe modern unit of a Martian second was, to 167,000 decimal places (the next place, 96,000 decimal places, was all zeros), one hundred twenty-nine point nine six eight eight eight two three seven one seven. This unit was equivalent to an Earthling second as follows:\nOne modern Martian second was ______________________\n________ SECONDS\nMore than seven minutes, almost exactly, remained to be lived by the $FIRSTNAMEB0 family before the first Martian attack. Things that would happen in all too brief a time under the dead yellow moon outside were already being sketched out in the overheated imagination of little $FIRSTNAMEB1, who was the youngest and healthiest. He lay on his back under the iron-clawed beetle, with his knees rubbing his ears and his eyes wide open.\nHis telescope was aimed at the golden air above him. His lips moved. He was still putting words together, very badly. He was imagining how the golden air up there could be used to protect him from attack and worse things. A voice that came from nowhere and everywhere, and that seemed to be God\u2019s voice, wanted him to imagine a very strange thing indeed. What God wanted him to imagine was a family composed of a father,...", "2_17_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-four in the afternoon</span>. The drizzle turned to a light rain. \"It\u2019s a long story,\" said $SURNAME0, \"about how this little misunderstanding got started\u2014\"\nHe was interrupted by an uproar to the rear.\n\"One moment, gentlemen,\" said $SURNAME0 coolly.\nThe soldiers of the Martian violet cube were surprised to find themselves suddenly under attack by a band of enemies that had previously been out of sight. The attack came from the rear\u2014from the far side of the ridge behind them.\nThis ridge ran at right angles to the forest in which the Martians were entrenched.\nThe rain made visibility poor.\nStill, what could be seen of the attackers was not impressive. They were led by a man and a woman on bicycles. The man was gaunt, and looked like a beatnik. The woman was Mrs. Beatnik.\nThe man waved a piece of plain brown wrapping paper over his head. The woman leaned low over the handlebars of her bicycle for better speed. She had long blonde hair.\nShe laid the handlebars back, accomplishing in this manner a sweep that carried her hair far out in a great parabola.", "2_11_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-three in the morning</span>.\nEight hours earlier, the chrono-synclastic infundibulated Mercury space ship, carrying $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB2, had crashed suicidally on the surface of Mars.\nThe two were still alive, still powerfully mateful, on the red soil.\nAt least $FIRSTNAMEB2 was still alive.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 hadn\u2019t moved since the crash.\nThere was no blood flowing in her body, since her heart had stopped immediately after the ship\u2019s air disappeared.\nThere wasn\u2019t even mucus flowing in her nose and throat.\nThe only sign of life in her was a slight swelling and fading of the skin in the region of her anus.\nThis was being caused by the passage in and out of air through her flapped anus.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, who had been the most affectionate of mothers, apparently mistook the flapping of the shute for panting.\nShe mistook the sensation for the feeling of having run a long way, and having a woolly warm chest to rest her weary, oxygen-starved head against.", "1_3_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>. My attorney suddenly leaned out of the machine, shouting that he was about to go \u201cput some dollars in the machine across the street.\u201d He tossed the car keys to me and ordered me to wait in the convertible...for him...no matter how long it took. I could hear his voice loaded with pain and desperation. The cancer had picked this moment to show. Ah Jesus, the precious moment when our favorite metaphor about life in the fastlane\u2014 \u201cIt's just like gambling in Las Vegas\u201d\u2014rolls finally into sight...in a terrible midnight Malibu at four in the morning on a mad street that runs smack dab under a lonesome and uncaring Southern California sky. The tropical moon was still out, but not for long\u2014 and the top was down on the Red Shark, a last tantalizing invitation to an easy getaway...a flight north toward the grapefruits and madness of Death Valley.\nNo\u2014stay here by the phone, I thought, and wait. There was one scurrying rat in the machine across the streest, a drugstore, open all night somewhere out here in the bush, with a pay phone in the shadow and a gum machine nearby. Just sit here in the car and wait...", "1_14_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-one in the afternoon</span> of December twenty-first, and there was no sun. Mercury\u2019s sun was too small to illuminate more than Mercury\u2019s continually whipping circular shadow. This was a dawn of sorts, not only because of Earthlight, but also because the shadow stirred itself every four minutes or so.\nCities dug in on Mercury\u2019s sunlit hemisphere automatically shut their windows and curtained their shines to keep Earthlight out when the shadow whirled by.\nSo $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were having their last look at a blue sky before entering into a darkness that would last for two Earth weeks.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were at the end of the spiral runway of a great military airport adjoining the estate. A yellow convertible with a marine chaplain at the wheel was parked behind them.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were both shivering with cold. Their teeth chattered. Behind their clear masks, their nostrils were inflamed.\nThe cold came from radiation nerves and molecules of the atmosphere.\nIt was actually a very clean cold, for it came from what Prometheus called a happy source\u2014it came from Earth.\nIt was Earth that shivered and shook and gave off the cold. Earth was not far from Mercury\u2014a...", "2_8_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty in the morning</span>.\nThe time was ten minutes to two in the afternoon.\nThe time was six minutes past eight in the evening.\nThe time was seven minutes after seven in the evening.\nThe time was twenty minutes before midnight.\nThe place was Newport, Rhode Island.\nThe place was London, England.\nThe place was Denpasar, Bali.\nThe place was St. Thomas in the Virgin Islands.\nThe place was San Miguel, in the Azores.\nThe place was the Sea of Tranquility.\nThe place was Baikonur.\nThe place was Tananarive.\nThe place was Taormina.\nThe place was Mauer.\nThe place was Tau Ceti.\nThe place was Gizeh.\nThe place was Yanceyville, North Carolina.\nThe place was St. Helena.\nThe place was Cape Kennedy.\nThe place was the Bay of Rainbows Gold.\nThe place was the edge of the Great Slate Lake.\nThe place was the north slope of the Valley of the Winds.\nThe place was the Great Desert of Wyoming.\nThe place was Limbo.\nThe place was Mars.", "1_18_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to seven o'clock in the evening</span>, on the first of April, of the year 2007\u2014a date innocently enough selected by the Patent Office for the first official celebration of National Patent Inventor's Day. Though the holiday was generally believed to have been the brainchild of the quasi-Santy Claus figure known as the Collect Commission of the Saint Augustine Chamber of Commerce, no one doubted the Commission's Chairman, Carlos Charles, when he announced at a meeting of the Chamber's Board of Directors that the true father of the motion was none other than Adolph Hitler, by way of an off-hand remark in a Munich beer hall in 1911.\nDruid Ciampino was on the north side of the building, putting the finishing spackle touches on 33\u2019 by 50\u2019 mural entitled Flight of the Years, illustrating the four stages of man\u2019s life on earth, with a fifth stage set off in reverse:\nSIMPLE YEARS\nCOMPLEX YEARS\nRECURRENT YEARS\nRETROACTIVE YEARS\nRETROSPECTIVE YEARS\nReverse-positioned in its frame was a cannonball of iron that would leave an impact crater 12 feet deep and 18 feet in diameter when it struck the building at a calculated Vector negative ninety-...", "0_11_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-eight in the morning</span> of December twenty-second. $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were supposed to be in New York, assisting $SURNAME0 in the rugged and exciting life of a President-elect\u2014but $SURNAME1 had slipped away from the New Jersey home to which he had been escorted by limousine. He had finally told $SURNAME0 through a lawyer that he couldn\u2019t possibly serve as Vice-President.\n$SURNAME1 had been whistling a popular American tune called Oh, Susanna, when he stepped into the foyer of the mansion.\nAs he put his foot on the bottom step of the staircase, the sun of Titan hit him in the eye.\nTrickle-trickletrickle\u2014said the fountain.\nSusie-come-home, said $SURNAME1, who hadn\u2019t whistled or hummed that particular tease before in his life.\nThe fountain\u2019s trickle-trickletrickle was meant to make the mansion more home-like. Screw-winch said the fountain.\nMar-ga-ri-ta, said $SURNAME1, who hadn\u2019t ever said the words before in his life.\nA moment before, the only sounds in the foyer had been the hum of the wind outside and the click of $SURNAME1...", "0_11_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-five in the morning</span> now and I had my seat-cord knotted around my leg; otherwise I might have fallen out of the sky when the few moment of consciousness left to me were over with. The laws of aerodynamics, I learned later from a book, forbids unconscious persons from piloting airplanes. Perhaps I learned this from the book too. I don't remember the things I read about; I remember only the things I see. A prayer to Father, avert my eyes...A tap on the shoulder. Flight Attendant. \u201cWant another drink?\u201d I shook my head. \u201cJust wondering,\u201c she said. The funny papers. Another prayer: reveal a way out.\nI saw it. Twenty minutes to Kansas City and I would jump off this plane into a crowd of people, then make directly for the men's room and hurl my soul into the toilet. What problem would that present? Not much, I realized. I'd eaten very little for about two months now. The average human bowel holds about a quart and a half. Two pounes and a half of souls slid down the porcelain slipway ....\nI saw it but I was powerless to move. My brain would not issue orders to my hand. That hand sat lim...", "0_15_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was the First National Bank of New Orleans. The robbery had gone exactly as planned. Of course, the plan had been chalked out in whispers the night before, by five men sitting in a parked car in the darkness outside a service station on the edge of the city. Two taxi drivers and three University of South Carolina football players made up the assault team. Huey selected them himself, and of course he was right again. The five did exactly what they were told to do at exactly the right times, and none of the five got himself killed or even wounded.\nThey knew they weren't going to get hurt. That was the one and only secret of their almost perfect record. They simply did not care whether they got hurt or not. They seemed to have a subconscious understanding that the story they would tell afterward would have to have some horribly tragic aspects. So they had nothing to fear and nothing to lose by going ahead, except a year or two in prison if they happened to be caught. Faced with that eventuality, they proceeded confidently and according to plan, always making certain any witnesses would be as drunk as they themselves were.\nHuey was disgusted by the sight of a drunken cop, but he was not going to take stupid chances...", "2_19_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-four in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME1 knew vaguely that his wives and his \"child\" were somewhere in the crowd confronting him, and he looked forward to the pleasure and mystery of be coming acquainted with these new and tender emotions about them.\nBut for the moment, $SURNAME1 was too occupied with looking at the crowd to look for his family. The crowd, now that it had stopped cheering, had become as silent as a painting on a museum wall\u2014silent, but for the scuffing of sandals.\nThe crowd was holding balloons. Every soul in the crowd, including $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1, held a balloon.\nThe balloons were thirty-six inches in diameter. They were mottled gold and silver. Their quivering radiated the keenest sensation the Solar System had ever felt\u2014 the sensation of power quivering in tapering containment.", "2_11_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to noon</span>.\nThe apparatus was machinery, of course. So was the brain. The two were joined by the spinal cord.\nThere was never any doubt.\nThe machinery was dedicated, calm, impersonal. It worked with an exquisite sense of time and a neural efficiency of 97.41 per cent.\nThe machinery nourished the eight wounds, and at twelve o\u2018clock it would release the safety catches and give the newlywed husband a rush of pain too massive to be assimilated by the brain. The machinery would then inject 8 cc. of demerol and 23 CC. of morphine sulfate.\nThe machinery would then wait.\nThe machinery would then wait a minute and a half, giving the medications time to take full effect.\nThen the machinery would open the seals of the memory trunk in the left lobe of the brain. The trunk in the left lobe of the brain held memories in solution.\nThe memories held in solution would swarm up into the conscious mind with full impact.\nThe injection of medications would bring on momentary unconsciousness. Unconscious in body, but conscious in mind, the owner of the body would experience, for the first and only time, the fact that he existed.", "2_17_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-three in the afternoon</span>, Central Standard Time.\nThe place was Thermopylae.\nA fine drizzle started falling, a drizzle as fine as tears.\n\"Now, remember,\" yelled $SURNAME0 up into the treetops, \"you don\u2019t know anything about this. This is all on your commanding officer. I\u2019ve given him a lot of wild, crazy orders in my time, but this one takes the cake. I think he\u2019s up there, hiding behind some tree. I hope so, because I\u2019d hate to think he had the nerve to desert you. This order comes, officially, right from the Solar Daily News-Herald in New York.\n\"You are to cease firing and take no defensive action of any kind, no matter what happens,\" said $SURNAME0. \"You are to stand fast, and allow yourselves to be exterminated. You are to die without resisting, without so much as getting mad. Is that clear?\"\n\"Yes,\" said the soldiers. They hung their heads, their faces streaked their faces streaked with rain and powder burns.\n\"There\u2019s just one more thing,\" said $SURNAME0 genially.", "2_10_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-two in the morning</span>.\nHow old was $FIRSTNAMEB0, on that terrible birthday?\nThirty Earthling years. That is thirty Earthling years according to the notion that Earthling years are meaningful on Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was thirty Earthling years old. He was thirty years old according to another notion, too. He was thirty years old according to the time-honored custom whereby a man becomes self-responsible on his birthday. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was now officially more than thirty years old. He was an old thirty.\nAt that point \u2014 according to both notions \u2014 $FIRSTNAMEB0 was now free to throw his weight around as he pleased. Even though he was still under the legal command of his company commander, he was now free to toss the commander in ragtime whenever he felt like it. He was still under the legal command of his battalion commander \u2014 still under the legal command of the great Galactic Commander in Chief Himself, for that matter \u2014 but nobody could now complain about the way $FIRSTNAMEB0 conducted himself. He was thirty, now.", "0_8_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe vibrations of a mighty bass had just come from a speaker in the floor under my cot. The bass was not in tune. It was the wrong note.\nIt should not have been there, but it was. It was not the fault of the message. The note should have been a D, but it wasn\u2019t.\nThe nine o\u2019clock radio news had just begun.\nWithin a week, Lord Copper would learn of this deal\u2014after which, it would be all over. Coop would come up to the suite, and Fritz and I would be in serious trouble. There was that horrible unfinished business in the trunk of the Scarlett Ibis\u2014and how; how could I explain my involvement in that? Or the dwarf and the\u2014oh, what to do, what to do? It was all over. Back to Albuquerque, to fight with Harvey Henderson of Harvey\u2019s House of Beef fame. Back down to earth. Endlessly pursuing a radio song. Perhaps ending up a steaming dish of Harvey\u2019s T-bones in a low joint off Route 66.\nThis was the stone that the law laid on me, and it was intolerable.\nThe news analyst was talking about Lebanon, now.", "2_10_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-two in the morning</span> by the chronometer in his suit, twelve forty-five P.M. solar time\u2014thirty-seven minutes before the close approach of the fizzling obsolete little Space Wanderer.\nOne light-minute from Earth, the chronometer in $SURNAME0\u2019s suit read.\nA moment later it read two light-minutes from Earth.\nThen it read eight light-minutes. Then it read nine light-minutes. Then it read ten light-minutes.\nIt read ten light-minutes for ten seconds. Then, suddenly, it read between ten and eleven light-minutes.\nSpace-time undulated.\nThe reddish tinge left the scene.\nThe stars appeared again, straight and severe and white-hot. The distant Solar System became a visible pigtail of fire in the blackness.", "0_16_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was twelve minutes after four.\nThe time was twelve minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon.\nBy four o'clock in the afternoon, the mass meeting of the $SURNAME0 Believers was over. Believers were threading their way between the pressure-wall and the rebo plants, on their way home.\nIn half an hour, reality would fall on Newport like a wet blanket.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, his great strength and appetite at momentarily loose ends, stood outside the north door of the great hall, smoking a cigarette.\nIn his left coat pocket, there was the touch of something new that $SURNAME0 had slipped into the pocket while shaking hands. $SURNAME1 didn\u2019t know what the new thing was.\nAnd in his right coat pocket, there were two used rocket-cartridges that $SURNAME0 had handed him while walking down the north runway. $SURNAME1 didn\u2019t know what the spent cartridges were. He had pocketed them absent-mindedly.\n$SURNAME1 wasn\u2019t a very good pilot. Back in the Army, he had never flown a man-rated helicopter. Unquestionably, he was less than masterful at handling the little space ship he had borrowed from the Smithsonian.", "2_8_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMuttering to herself, Schlingensief padded down the stairs, lugging her shoes and her camera bag. She headed into the kitchen, punched the power switch of the stove, filled a pot with water, put the pot on the stove, returned to the living room, faded the stereo to silence.\nIt was only as she was returning to the kitchen that she became aware that the little statue of St. $SURNAME0 was glowing.\nIt was an eerie thing\u2014to come back into a silent, climate-controlled house, and find all the lights off, and the St. $SURNAME0 flashing.\n\"What\u2019s the message, this time?\" she asked aloud. She had felt very mistress of the house until now. Before she read the message from $SURNAME0, she had believed herself capable of dealing sensibly with whatever happened.\nBut suddenly she didn\u2019t feel that she was mistress of the house at all. Suddenly, so it seemed to her forcibly, it had become anybody\u2019s house.", "1_5_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-one in the morning</span> on October thirteenth; dusky all right, but bright enough to show big, bright Venus shining over the Post Street block. I stopped before the glass door and saw myself in it and shuddered. There were big bags under my eyes, I looked drugged. But what could I expect, sleepless, nervous nights, lugging that ridiculous great valise around, moving from filthy burrow to filthy burrow? I went in, consulted the Examiner over the classified ads, found the name, told the driver a Green Street address, and off we jangled. The driver had a paper too. \u201cPolice are asking for your help to identify this man,\u201d he read. The picture of Inez in that silk dress was staring from the front page of the paper. \u201c \u2018Inez Salerno, a San Francisco office worker and nightclub performer, disappeared early this morning ....\u2019 \u201d I pulled my hat over my eyes as we passed a newsstand. In a Hollywood scandal paper beside the Examiner a picture of Terry was kicking up sand. \u201cMiss Spell good-bye to her kitty ...\u201d Louie was right: it\u2019d be headlines in the east when the papers got there.\nI got...", "0_15_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nHe took the button out of his left eye and put it in his left coat pocket.\nThen he looked again through the glass aperture on his right side, concentrated his will on the convex slide in front of him, and withdrew it.\nHe gazed for a moment at the curving white corridor in front of him.\nThen he stepped out and walked deliberately\u2014eliminating, as he did so, any appearance of awkwardness\u2014down the corridor. In his left hand, he carried his suit of blue twill pajamas.\nHe stopped beside a door. The sign on the door said: INFIRMARY.\nIt was locked.\nHe frowned.\nHis forehead wrinkled, and his left hand balled itself into a fist. The index finger of that fist remained extended.\nThe fist pounded on the door with an even and measured force.\nA woman's voice came through an annunciator: \"Who is it?\"\nPrather recognized the voice of the receptionist at the reception desk below. \"Dead man walking,\" he said clearly.\n\"Say again?\" said the receptionist, who had not heard.\n\"Dead man walking,\" said Prather. He pounded twice on the door with his...", "0_9_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after nine o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a cellar once used for interning captured enemy paratroopers and space aliens in times of crisis\u2014which this indeed was. Stein, Wagner and $SURNAME0 stood by a furnace beside a pile of rusty lumber and soggy newsletters. Their backs were to the furnace.\nSmoking fires of the redInfinity Sun scorched their faces. Directly before them was a cycling, seething wall of air that reached from floor to ceiling.\nThe air formed itself into a cylinder, became a spiral, became a square baffle, twirled, became a cylinder again. Through the hub of the cylinder came a metal drip pan, being pushed by an ancient servant of Mars named $FIRSTNAMEF1. In the drip pan, being fished out with a pair of rusty tongs and put into a paper bag by an ancient servant of Venus named Harmony, were several hundred thousand thetans.\nA thetan was a Golden Blade smoke filter made of cellulose and hollowa bamboo reed.\nThe thetans were gray and limp. Their mouths were clogged with American tobacco. Their throats were filled with a nasty spit of chocolate eclabousse and quinine. They had apparently poisoned themselves while sucking up smoke.\nThe cycle...", "0_17_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We were on the platform of the Coupole. We had been having a hell of a time all day, culminating in a drunken barroom discussion with a young Englishman named Vincent. At the same table with us were two waiters, one large, one small. The large waiter was a Spaniard. He didn\u2019t like the Englishman. He made friendly advances to us and finally got into an argument. This argument became an argument with the Britisher because the Spaniard insisted upon calling the waiter, who was a friend of his, a caballero. The Britisher made the point that a caballero meant a member of the nobility.\n\u201cHell, man, he\u2019s not a member of the nobility.\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d said the Spaniard. \u201cHe is a caballero.\u201d\n\u201cNo. He\u2019s not what you call a caballero.\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d said the Spaniard.\n\u201cNo. He\u2019s a waiter.\u201d\n\u201cYes. But he\u2019s a caballero.\u201d\n\u201cNo,\u201d said the Englishman. \u201cI...", "0_23_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to midnight</span>.\nDrugs? I could prove very little, on drugs, when the big hand reached twelve\u2014except that my attorney and I had definitely agreed that alcohol was our drug of choice. We preferred Dilaudid to alcohol\u2014but only when we could not have Dixtracoamphine. But in terms of the brutal hedonism of Las Vegas, there is not a shred of difference between the purestgrade marijuana and the sweet, sweet smell of Scram; because all drug addicts agree, in chorus, that drugs are the only good life.\nTrue hedonism is defined by level of sophistication and degree of taste...and when I think back to all the horrors of that week, I remember nothing but a constant search for an untraceable level of wealth and taste. Drugs? I don\u2019t think so. No more than sugarcoated adrenal glands pulled out of the driedunted corpses of slaves from the heroin belt of Southeast Asia.\nWe wanted drugs because the places we wanted to go required drugs, that\u2019s all. And drugs have nothing to do with pleasure, because our terrible goal was nothing less than the basic hedonist's Golden Mean: the precise middle of enough and too much.\nWelcome to Las...", "0_5_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-nine in the morning</span>. The rain was over, and the dawn was about to break.\nThe company commander was hoping for a few hours\u2019 sleep before the new attack, but he wasn\u2019t going to get it. He had work to do. The worst possible thing for a company commander in combat is to get the feeling that somebody back of him has his keister covered, that all he has to do is to keep the boys driving forward and everything will be all right.\nThe company commander got to his feet and reached down to shake his buddy\u2019s hand. The buddy was the Platoon Sergeant, who had been out on an observation-post mission all night, and who had come in with the report that actually started all the trouble.\n\"Good work, Sergeant,\" said the company commander. \"You get your ammo sorted out, and then get some sack time while they\u2019re getting the trucks over here.\"\n\"Thank you, sir,\" said the sergeant.\nThe company commander put his right fist in his left palm and the sergeant did the same. Then the sergeant went to see about the ammunition.\nIn a few minutes the trucks arrived, and twenty-two men got in with their equipment. They left for the company area about a...", "1_8_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-three in the morning</span>. There was no traffic on the highway. Forty-three minutes to San Francisco, I thought. In forty-three minutes I would either be there or...what? Don\u2019t think, I told myself. Just drive the car. Don\u2019t worry. In forty-three minutes raise hell...raise hell like you\u2019ve never raised before in your life. Have breakfast with Malcolm and Bender. Then call DOBBS and scream at him: \u201cYou make me puke, you white smelling dog! You putrid beast! I\u2019m through with you! Don\u2019t ever call me again! If you want to see me, come to the Sand Dune, Wednesday, 8 p.m., and alrnost hurry, because I\u2019m quitting you, pal, I\u2019m through with your putrid stench. Yes, you\u2019re a stinking fish! Yes, you shit on your father and your mother. Yes, you\u2019re a latent homosexual! Yes, you envy me! Yes, I have a big cock; you have a dinky little one! Go on, hit me, you crumb! I can take you! You haven\u2019t got the guts!", "1_15_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Miss Pierce in New York informed the alarming Mrs $FIRSTNAMEB1 in Baltimore that at last her child Katharine was sleeping. She said that Katharine had lain all morning in the gazebo behind her husband's house, with the chessmen scattered all about her, refusing, for some reason, to move. She said that Katharine was undoubtedly suffering from exhaustion, since for thirty-six hours she had done nothing except conduct in the living room of Robert Methuen's house a sort of vague and horrible argument with someone no one else could see. \"The obvious prescription, I think, is that she should rest, in her own bed. I shall ask one of our colored girls in New York to get on the first train and bring me some sedative tablets,\" Miss Pierce said, \"and I shall try to see if I can't persuade Katharine to take one tonight, and stay in bed tomorrow morning.\" She said that Robert, too, was exhausted and had to go to Washington on business, where he would stay until Saturday, when he would undoubtedly come down to Malcolm's as they had planned, and they would all go to the country club reunion on Saturday night. That was, of course, unless Robert got in touch with her before he left...", "0_12_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog were about to materialize directly beneath the flying yards of the SPACE SHUTTLE tipping its way to Mars.\nSo far as the official Establishment was concerned, $SURNAME0 and any dog he might have were already either in space or wishing they were. For a week past, the office of the President of the United States had been invited to make zero statements about the momentous event.\nIn a sense, the President was not singular. All the leaders of all the nations had been given identically worded invitations by $SURNAME0.\nAll the leaders of Earth were invited to refrain, either tacitly or explicitly, from saying anything whatever about the launching of the SPACE SHUTTLE. They were invited to look the other way while the event took place.\n$SURNAME0 did not have good union with his dog. He had to estimate his dog\u2019s state of mind with the greatest possible accuracy. There was hardly a second to lose, and the stakes were exceedingly high.\nThere was nothing brilliant about the scheme $SURNAME0 was about to carry through. His scheme depended chiefly on luck and surprise.\nHe had plenty of both.\nHis wife and his dog were about to be sucked...", "1_2_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-seven in the morning</span>. I hung up, looked back at the sleeping bag, hesitated ... then walked out into it. My room was almost directly over the airport. I hung the DO NOT DISTURB sign on the door and closed the window. The bed was made, the room empty. Very clinical, very nice. I hardly recognized myself. The hotel was on Paradise Road, right off the Strip. Very bright outside. Very dark inside, where I stood. Several suspended lifetimes in that moment of indecision. Then I put on a pot of coffee and turned on the news. The coverage of the arrests in Bimini had reached the point where they were speculating on whether or not the Nixon administration had known of the raid in advance. It was all very nice, very depressing. The coffee pot bubbled and hissed. Cheerful domestic sounds.\nHow could I disappear? Where? What the hell was I doing in Vegas, anyway? The coffee was ready. I filled a huge brown ceramic mug and sipped at it. Not bad. My stomach acknowledged the brew with a quick flipback muscles. Life growing back into numbness. The news was over and ABC had a movie: The Harder They Fall, with Humphrey Bogart. Watching sponge...", "2_18_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six seventeen in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nThe man who came through the crowd was neither tall, blond, nor brave-looking.\nNobody cheered. The band did not pick up a snappy Techni col variation on the boo.\nIt did play something as the man from Earth entered the palace, but the something it played was Bach\u2014and in a minor key.\nThe new arrival was obviously drunk. He was an uninteresting man of about thirty- five, with a retreating hairline, a soft paunch, and cheap shoes.\nThe man from Earth lurched onto the balcony set up for the ceremonies by the architects of the palace. He waved a quart of Scotch under the noses of his official greeters. \" \u2019n\u2019 how are all youse guys?\" he said huskily.\nSiegfried and Roy stepped away from the new arrival. So did the March Hare, the Mad Hatter, the Lion, the Tiger, the White Knight, and the Mock Turtle. All bowed formally, and beat a quiet retreat.", "1_13_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThere was terrific agitation in the front offices of the $FIRSTNAMEB2 Institute. Out in the square in front of the Institute, a line of people, downy and uncircumcised, was beginning to form for the lecture. It was to be the first public appearance of $SURNAME0 since his disappearance.\nIn the square, there was horse-drawn traffic, for some reason. Shays, buggies, carryalls, carts, oxcarts, surreys, and oh-de-me\u2019s had been driven in from miles around. They were added to by a continuous stream of automobiles, motorcycles, sleds, motorcycles, jet-propelled snowmobiles, jet-powered lizzies, hang-gliders, sky-cymbals, toy balloons, amateur rockets, and unidentifiable junk. It was the first great popular influx the town had known since its foundation.\n\"Skip the five-cent tour, please.\" This came from the loudspeaker under the porte-coch\u00e8re of the mansion. It was $SURNAME0\u2019s voice. \"Go directly inside\u2014and find a place on the grand staircase to sit or stand.\"\nAn empty limousine came bounding up the driveway, slowed...", "2_20_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"Old men, you know what I would like to do to you. You make me sick. You know what I do want, want, want. I want to go home.\"\n\"Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1, can\u2019t you settle down like gentlemen?\" said the bartender. \"I\u2019m asking you nicely, now\u2014please settle down like gentlemen.\"\n\"I\u2019m looking for my brother,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"I\u2019m looking for my brother.\" He was talking about himself in the plural. He had had four shots of Earth Navy Rum, and his face was getting redder and redder.\n\"That\u2019s what I like,\" said the plain-clothed Martian policeman. \"That\u2019s just fine.\" He had been watching $FIRSTNAMEF1 affectionately for an hour and eight minutes.\n\"Now, buddy,\" said the bartender. \"Take your shot, and be a sport. We stick together on Mars, you know. They don\u2019t do these things on Earth\u2014you know that.\"\n\"You dirty Earthie,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. He took his shot. \"Now you take yours,\" he said to the plain-clothed Martian policeman.", "1_15_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. In a room in the Space Age Lawn Party Encampment, $FIRSTNAMEF0 Klammer, delegate from Des Moines, Iowa, representative of the Women\u2019s Propaganda Committee, was giving a press conference.\nWhich doesn\u2019t have anything to do with the story, but kind of makes the story, doesn\u2019t it, $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas, since it was your program that put her there. That got her whole enthusiastic committee intoNazi uniforms, specially made for them.\nNazi uniforms?\nThat\u2019s how it was in the middle of a drought, when only your rain dance can save the fair. Close to midnight, wind, 4 A.M., cold, 4 A.M., no moon, 4 A.M.; in among the giant, stubbled dry oatstraw. And there they are\u2014HILTERMARSCHEN! HILTERMARSCHEN! HILTERMARSCHEN! until the first distant sprinkle of cold rain, borne on a wind with no humidity in it.\nWhere, $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas?\nWhy, here, friend, right here in San Francisco at the Fair. Boy, with all the narcotics in S.F.,", "2_21_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine nineteen in the evening</span>.\n/a/\n\u201cMother of God,\u201d said Mr. Bloemker, making his way over the threshold of the lobby.\nMr. Bombardini stopped swabbing. His shovel squeaked across the floor.\nMrs. Howell pinched Candy\u2019s suspender. \u201cLook,\u201d she whispered,\n\u201cIneffable,\u201d said Lenore, under her breath.\n/b/", "0_6_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-eight in the morning</span> exactly, and the sky over Roland Park and over the Society Hill area of Philadelphia was filled with red, the bottom edge of the sun.\nThe air outside the Space Panel laboratory was cold.\nPeter Johnson, wearing an insulated military jacket, was politely showing $FIRSTNAMEB0 to the door. Johnson\u2019s mate,$FIRSTNAMEB1, was keeping Stony silent and out of the way in a little room off the laboratory proper.\n\"Just about everybody finds him a real handicap, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said Peter Johnson over the whistling of the cold wind. \"But I think he would actually make the experiment I\u2019m setting up go bad\u2014maybe even make it blow up. I got to keep him out of harm\u2019s way, and he don\u2019t make it easy. \"\n\"I don\u2019t mind him,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"It\u2019s not your puppy he embarrasses,\" said Johnson. \"None of us think any less of you for bringing him.\"\nHe shooed $FIRSTNAMEB0 right out the door. \"Go on, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" he said, \"and God bless you.\"\nJohnson shut the door. He and $FIRSTNAMEB1 strung themselves like limp, stuffed clothes onto two rods that...", "2_23_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe nobby, gleaming mother-of-pearl handles of two samurai swords were sticking up out of the scabbard that had been emptied out of the Pier sofa\u2014scabbard, sofa, and all.\nThe time was twenty-eight minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the evening.\nThe phonograph was now playing $SURNAME1\u2019s own composition, \"A Sunday in the Country.\" $SURNAME1 had played it until he had got sick and tired of it. Now it was playing itself. It played itself for hours at a stretch.\nThe twin chromium plaques on the wall, by the sundial that showed Hollywood the hours, never altered. They said:\nA) The probability of your being here for any one given second is\nB) approximately one in one quintillion.\nThe doorbell rang.\nThe bell that rang was the bell that had given Melania Angel the martini shivers, die last time she had visited. It was a vulgar thing\u2014diminutive, hooked to a slender bronze belt that hung down just above the knee.", "1_16_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four eighteen in the afternoon</span> when we said so long to $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4. I gave him seventy-five dollars and he went off to get a haircut and buy a new suit and shave. He was making another life for himself -- without us. $FIRSTNAMEF4 was staying with her auntie in the East. At the last moment $FIRSTNAMEB3 offered me his car. Since this was, after all, a point of honor with him, I refused, and yet I felt sad about it because he'll never know how much his offer, his so-called \u201cgift,\u201d meant to me. \u201cGo on,\u201d he smiled. \u201cI know what you're thinking -- you're worrying what'll happen to my car. I spent all that money on her and now some cheap hustler'll probably steal her away from me in Frisco. Well, what I going to do when those continental girls come out to visit me and I don't have the price to take them around?\u201d Yes, he was posing again. Yes, he had to pose. He was an American genius who had to go crazy to be successful. Go and tell that to the idiot Americans who hang on his words. They'll scream at you and croak, \u201cNaw...", "2_13_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\nThe thing that happened at one thirty in the afternoon was this. There was a series of thunderous rumbles, and a rain of bits of stone splashed against the basement window. A section of wall outside the window collapsed with a boulder in the middle of it.\nA child ran past the window. There was a soldier with each arm. The soldier on the left carried a rifle with a fixed bayonet, and the soldier on the right carried a grenade launcher.\nPanels of planes Siamesed the wall outside. A man in a brown helmet appeared in the biggest panel.\nThe man in the brown helmet was waving a white flag.\nAround and behind him were the parts of two smashed assault boats, and a demolished assault raft, and a sunken anti-aircraft barge.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 danced and sang, \"The man with the blond mustache has fooled you again!\" He laughed, hugged Stony senseless. \"I switched!\" he told Stony gleefully. \"When I helped the man with the blond mustache, I went to the other one!\"\nThere was a knock at the window through which $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been looking at the battles outside. \"Come in, come in,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "1_17_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. One thousand, two hundred and twenty-six human beings appertained to the complement of the $SURNAME0 estate.\nThe names of these human beings were as uniformly aristocratic as were the names of the two thousand human beings who appertained to the complement of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s yacht. In fact, both estates were nominally owned by holding companies that were wholly owned subsidiaries of monopolies.\nThe only named partners in the holding companies were $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1. The subsidiaries were wholly owned also by estates whose names were as uniformly aristocratic.\nThese estates, $SURNAME0\u2019s and $SURNAME1\u2019s, were controlled by boards of trustees composed of husbands, wives, and children.\n$SURNAME0, whose distrust of free-market incentives was profound, had limited the salary or compensation of anyone on his estate to five dollars a year.\n$SURNAME1, whose approach to business problems was diametrically opposite to $SURNAME0\u2019s, had kept open for himself a slush fund of ten billion dollars, from the interest on which he drew a salary of fifty million dollars a year. Nobody else on the yacht received as much as five dollars a year.\nNo one on the $SURNAME0 estate received so...", "0_19_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-two in the evening</span>, and night had come on heavily\u2014one of those nights when, if your love goes unfulfilled, you may undergo the deepest, the bitterest despair.\nOf course, there was always room service.\nRabbit\u2019s boarding-house was in an old mansion in Stone Canyon, an offshoot of Laurel Canyon that soars over the Hollywood Hills to a ridge overlooking the stucco gulches. At the far end was an areaway convenient to a fireplug. That was where the fawn Dancer MG was parked. Dancer was the great love of $FIRSTNAMEB3\u2019s life, and possibly the reason for his lengthy visit to the Coast. The little English noisemaker had a two-stroke engine that wouldn\u2019t take kindly to a waterless drowning.\nOr did you maybe screw everything to the floor and leave? That would be it, wouldn\u2019t it? But not if the boat was rocking so. What if it just gently rocked without surging and without tipping, and people underneath had great difficulty catching their breaths and wondered why? O it was sad, mad, and bad, but most mad to see no solution possible but to go on rocking and rocking till you yourself yelled enough and went under. Only that...", "0_23_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until midnight</span>. A breeze stirred in the room\u2014not a breeze but the movement of the air molecules as Bomb 22 exploded.\nNow there was no steel tower at Television City. There was no Television City.\nExcept for the plastic chairs and tables, nothing remained of the studio. The neighborhood was as much changed as the studio. It became a burned-out slum.\nIn the Indianapolis Motor Speedway, a hot wind stirred the infield grass. The white-post flagsticks began to melt.\nNew York\u2019s ancient water tunnels, deep under the Atlantic and the East River, were breached, in some cases by rusted spikes driven silently by thermonuclear explosions. The tunnels\u2019 thick walls surrendered only reluctantly to waters of the harbor, Hudson River, and sea.\nIn the Atlantic and the Hudson, white horses galloped to shore. There was a long, discomforting pause, as though some great beast had drawn a breath.\nThen an instant later, Came Bomb 22.\nA pillar of light twenty miles long by a mile wide flashed to the heavens. It reproduced, in terms of light and heat, the detonation of a 50,000-megaton hydrogen bomb, though it was in actuality the explosion of only a 500-meg...", "2_11_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to noon</span>. It was time for the big show to begin. The acrobats slammed the Champagne bottles down on the table.\n\"All right, troops,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Get it down you, while you\u2019ve still got an appetite. You\u2019re going to need all the strength you\u2019ve got.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEF0, who had not touched either food or drink, smiled woozily at $SURNAME1. \"Oh,\" she breathed, \"isn\u2019t everything beautiful?\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s thought was $SURNAME0 again. $SURNAME0 had known more than he had told. $SURNAME0 had known that would be a hell of a picnic on Titan.", "1_11_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-three in the morning</span> of October 3, according to Random Frequent Flyer Bauer.\nThe $FIRSTNAMEM1 Cigarettes Company of America\u2019s refurbished space ship, the rocket ship Hato Fu lvi a, was now nosing upward and out, away from its dock on the Moon.\nThe terrible cruelty of the docking maneuver that had brought lvi a\u2019s ship to the surface of the Moon was now roughly over, because the actual blasting off was only a formality.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the human being, no longer resided in the ship.\nHe had gone. He had done what he had been paid half a billion dollars to do\u2014he had given birth to a new species.\nAnd the half-billion-dollar paternity suit was hanging on the wall of $SURNAME0\u2019s library, right next to all the other neat, framed things that were much more important to $SURNAME0 than his material goods.\nIt was a beautiful thing, that paternity suit\u2014on vellum, and illuminated in gold and lapis blue. $SURNAME1 had had the suit painted on a four-by-four-foot boiser board.\nUnder the Latin caption Filius Homo appeared the bio feet portrait of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 in the robes...", "2_14_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-two in the afternoon</span>.\nBy three twenty-five in the afternoon, the creatures\u2019 memories, all in all, had undergone an astonishing improvement.\nTheir memories were now as secure, compact, unruffled as the carved blue and gray stones of an old soi-disant Gothic cathedral.\n* * *\nThere is a spot of sun on your left shoulder and the light shimmers blue and you are really here, really alive, really in 1963, and what God has wrought is holy and beautiful and lustrous, and you have a right and a responsibility to be in this spot right now.\nYou breathe. You dig being alive. This beard is itchy, and there\u2019s a splinter in this bench, and there are bundles of unsmoked white wood in your arms, and the sea is before you, and the fish are biting just beyond that white horizon.\nYou are teaching yourself a lesson, and it can be learned only here and now. So stay.", "2_16_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty in the afternoon</span>. The place was still Newport, Rhode Island. The temperature was still seventy-two degrees. The crowd of country clubbers was still irregular, because of the efforts of helicopters to keep people away from their landing places.\nRumors had begun to circulate to the effect that the Martian had been sighted at Eastport, Maine, and was now thought to be moving south along the Connecticut coast, threatening Titanic Schiff bike paths, tulip farms, and yacht clubs, jumping waterfalls, perhaps. Anyone who owned a boat could get it out of dry dock, now, on any pretext; those who already had boats in the water were urged to get the hell out, if they hadn\u2019t already.\nWhen, exactly at four forty-one, the rumble and tumble of a mighty helicopter was heard, the crowd went wild all over again.\nTerrible and magnificent, the helicopter hummed and leaped and fluttered down from the sky. To screams and cheers from the crowds, it sank its claws into the earth once again. This time, so did the crowd.", "0_2_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-four in the morning</span> and I was all alone with my friends. Now, as Tom Moody put it, we were going to have to get a little groose deal. By nine o\u2019clock that night we would reach our final destination and then everything would be peaches and cream again. \u201cIt\u2019s better for you to know about these things than not to know,\u201d Aunt Mary said. \u201cWhat you don\u2019t know won\u2019t bother you.\u201d What I didn\u2019t know was that $FIRSTNAMEB3, who had cut out with Chad King at the gas station, was herof all things going finally to SMOOTCH that poor bugged eighteen\u2014year-old Sally, who all the time had been sitting by herself, knitting her pursed and trying to figure $FIRSTNAMEB3 out. He came back to the car so happy I could hardly recognize him. The first thing he did, before even saying hello, was crawl around on the floor and dig out a pair of spats he\u2019d left there in Denver. They'd fallen out of his pocket. \u201cIf I'm going to San Fran with you I want to be absolutely complete.\u201d He put those spats on, and with it his queer grin, the...", "0_22_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> and the place was a recessed doorway on Second Avenue\u2014and the fat man waited in the darkness of the doorway, wearing a derby hat and smoking a cigar.\nAnother ten minutes crawled by. Traffic roared and sputtered in the street.\nThe fat man looked up and down the avenue and poked cigar-smoke at the dark windows of the apartments across the street. The cigar-smoke drifted off into space.\nFifteen minutes after ten o'clock a taxi appeared, hove to at the curb, and disgorged a passenger.\nThe passenger was a thin man. He came sauntering up the street. He wore a derby hat and a trenchcoat. He walked through pools of light and patches of shadow.\n\"Boom!\" he said, in the doorway; and the fat man took a twenty-dollar bill from his wallet and gave it to him. \"That's for being on time, Willie. You're only forty-five minutes late. That's good. That's very good\u2014for you.\"\n\"Thanks,\" said the thin man. \"How much?\"\nThe fat man named a price.\n\"Sold,\" said the thin man. \"I'll be back with the stuff in fifty minutes.\"", "0_13_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> that I got off the bus in Holly Springs, a trim little town in Southern Mississippi. However, this was not the Holly Springs I remembered. This one had some brick houses and a brick-paved street, of a kind that in the South they call a Colonnade. Old cars slept at opposite sides of the street. The stores were small, with local things in them. I had predicted that this would be the spot where I'd get my first load: I was in the heart of the South, the Delta region where raw cotton bales are piled high. This was the true cotton country of the South, and cotton country people were the wildest and most wonderful characters in the country. I had come here to get a load among these nationals for kicks, for nothing else. It was a long way from New Orleans, and I could imagine $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 waiting in vain for my return. But I felt where I was. I felt suddenly happy and well. I knew I could get my load here. The only thing was to locate the right person. I walked up the Colonnade. Where would I spend the night? Not with any relatives. It was too late to reach my crazy aunt who I knew would take me in. Not...", "2_10_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten seventeen in the morning</span>. Greenwich mean time.\nThe Space Wanderer at last had broken clear of the awful strait-jacket of time and was on his way to the thirteenth floor. Soon he would meet Mr. $SURNAME0 and the lovely and bewildering Mrs. $SURNAME0. Soon he would have warmth, would have color, a glorious daisy, and eight peach-colored darts in a rear pocket of his blue jeans.\nThe elevator jolted to a stop. The doors opened and let in a hazy milky light. There was a beautiful fragrance in the air\u2014a mixture of gardenias and new-mown hay.\nIt was ten eighteen in the morning. Greenwich mean time.", "2_15_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-seven in the afternoon</span>. My lawyer was just coming out of Judge Jerome's chambers with Judge Jerome's message for me\u2014the fine he wanted fixed, anyhow. It was $250 and one year's suspended sentence.", "2_17_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog were in the crowd. Wherever $SURNAME0 went, the crowd gave way very politely, and kept a warm and cheerful space around him. The same was true of his dog.\nThe crowd loved the dog. The crowd loved $SURNAME0, too. And they didn\u2019t love either one furtively, the way crowds sometimes love movie stars.\nThey loved $SURNAME0 openly. And they didn\u2019t seem to feel sorry for $SURNAME0, the way crowds are sometimes sorry for rich people.\nThe crowd\u2019s love for $SURNAME0 and his dog was so large, so apparently sincere, and so uncalculated that it made meat for the cynic of the residence towers and dropsies of the furnished basements in Greater New York.\n\"Good old Skip\u2014him and his dog!\" said a tubby little kid in the crowd. He spoke with a great deal of affection and a little envy.", "0_23_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe space was the hall outside number 211 of the Sturgis apartment house on Beacon Street, Boston, Massachusetts.\nThe Witnesses were R. Wilbur Plannit, vacuum-cleaner salesman; Neil $SURNAME0, a chum of Plannit\u2019s with no occupation; Kurt Vinch, salesman of women\u2019s hosiery for the same concern as Plannit; and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, nee $FIRSTNAMEF1, a wife.\nTypical of the chaos materializing promises to be in the United States, were the $SURNAME0 and Plannit families, families which should never have met, much less been friendly.\nR. Wilbur Plannit was from $FIRSTNAMEF0, Nebraskaj\nThe Rumfoords were from dishonorable but thrilling $FIRSTNAMEM1-Salem, North Carolina.\nThe families did meet because Neil $SURNAME0 attended Harvard, a school that represented the highest nobility of achievement and breeding to people as far apart as $FIRSTNAMEF0, Nebraska and $FIRSTNAMEM1-Salem, North Carolina. Harvard had an entrance examination so demanding that a native of $FIRSTNAMEF0 or $FIRSTNAMEM1-Salem had about one chance in a million of passing it. To both $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEM1-Salem, the...", "2_17_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-four in the afternoon</span> of April 25, i960.\nThe place, the neighborhood of the Space Administration Building in Washington, D. C.\nThe lights were on in the great wall of windows of the room in which $SURNAME0 and his wife were to meet $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Kaiser and his wife.\nOld-fashioned gas lamps burned in the curving street outside.\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Kaiser arrived first. She was accompanied by an aide. She wore an old-fashioned black silk dress, and carried a white ermine muff.\nShe was Mrs. $SURNAME0 now. She had become Mrs. $SURNAME0 five hours earlier. She weighed one hundred and twenty pounds.", "1_10_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nDuring the next five minutes nine persons happened to glance for one reason or another in through the front windows of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s little barrack. All the nine persons shuddered. All the nine persons held their breath. Then they went away very quietly. They went to get their space suits in order.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s rifle smoked softly.\nHe slapped in a magazine that held a hundred bullets, and put a hand grenade in his pocket.\nOutside, he kneeled under a window of the barrack that faced the rear. The back door of the barrack was wide open, and the countryside was very peaceful back there.\nThere were many things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 wished he could think about, now that he was finally going to escape from the station. He was somehow frightened about all the things he might think about\u2014and ended by not thinking about anything at all, and going through all the motions of a man who has everything under control.\nInside the building, a one-man band was striking up \"Heartbreak Hotel.\"\nThe singer sang marvellously off-key.\n\"Heart-, break-,\n\"He\u2014art-, break-,\n\"Bro-ken heart, I've\u2014\"\nAnd then the singer...", "1_6_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-four in the morning</span>, and everyone in the room, the girl included, was worried about the tryst that was to come at seven. The book I read claimed and emphasized that the appointment was minor and domestic, but the faces at the table told another story, particularly because the man in the white hat was not of the same ethnicity as the others, being an Oriental-American, and because he seemed so withdrawn and formal, and because something about him suggested he came from that private, powerful part of the country that people from the East call the Far West and take seriously the way people from the West take seriously the East.\nI was of course interested in the surreal smallness of the event that had drawn the girl through five hundred miles of night\u2014a bet about a mink coat, nothing more; but mostly I was busy with Bonniwell\u2019s statement that the area to the far right of my map, the area that might as well not exist, as far as the Government was concerned, held the clue to all I wanted to know.\nIt was obviously nutty to worry about an area the Government didn\u2019t recognize. The map said the Far West was an area of drylands, provincialism, isolation, conservatism, and disdain. The Far West wasn...", "1_10_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-six in the morning</span> and every one of us was loaded on marijuana, me with two big cigars filled with the best pot available and Old Bull with a hubcap full, a big smiling mysterious face appearing out of nowhere with the \u201cgrub,\u201d the raw drug, and then just as suddenly vanishing. The contact was made on that stretch of Park Row where the kid was shaking his coconut and the man with the bandaged head was shuffling his feet and talking; I know that area well now and can recognise it immediately by the schoolhouse on the corner with a sign above the door that says GRAMMAR, by a tall hollyhockgrowing next to the sidewalk, by a tree and a tall iron gate that spans the entire width of the sidewalk and beyond which dips down a path into a sad, gray, old cemetery shaded by old trees with the tombstones leaned and spooky. But the road is straight and the standard urban junky leaning by the doorway is unknown in this special vicinity. It\u2019s a special world and I like it here; schoolboys hurry by with their books under their arms, kicking at the stones and each other, reminding themselves. Out of the men you see coming down the street somebody sometime is going to be President, somebody...", "0_9_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-nine in the morning</span>, Earthling Eastern Standard Time, Monday, 8 September 1991. The place was a small auditorium of the $SURNAME0 estate. The harmoniums, having announced the fifteen minutes during which the materialization was to occur, had begun to play.\nThe harmoniums played by clockwork.\nThey had been wound by Skip before his departure from Earth.\nThe one soft-skinned creature inside the auditorium besides $FIRSTNAMEB2 was $SURNAME0\u2019s parrot, Salo. Salo, unlike the harmoniums, was alive. His life, unlike their lives, was nasty, brutish, and short.\nLong before he reached maturity, Salo had experienced the nothingness that lies on the other side of life. He had banged his head on that nothingness, having flown from a palace window overlooked by twenty-five meters of air. The palace window overlooked the Martian canal named Ri Mis pane by its tenders.\nThere was a Martian canal named Ri Mis pane on every inhabited planet of the Galactic Milieu.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was a strong swimmer. He could have made a safe passage of Ri Mis pane had there been any water in it.\nSalo lay dying on the imaginary white sand of the dry...", "1_14_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nI prodded the soft, big mattress with my hands.\nIt was made of feather, doe-skin, and camel's-hair. It was so soft that it gave disagreeably to the pressure of my hands. I poked my head into the blackness of the clothes-press. It smelled of bitter herbs, camphor, lemon-oil, wood, and cedar-spray.\nIt was Monday, June the 6th, at Cannes, France.\nI was in the San Marcos Hotel, in Room 247. There was a bathroom in the next room, and beyond that was Room 248. On the door of 248 was a card that said:\nMr. and Mrs. Frank Wembleton.\nI looked out of the casement window, high up under the top of the hotel, and saw a strip of beach and then the deep blue of the Mediterranean and then a strip of beach again and then pine-trees and then more beach and then the white-capped Mediterranean again, and then more beach and stone-pines, clipped and cut and guarded like the hair of women, and then the white-capped Mediterranean again, and then more beach, and the wall of Cannes about two hundred yards...", "1_22_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-seven in the evening</span>, and we were back in our living room. The party was winding down. Finally the Prussian girls and their priest were gone, and so were most of the girls we\u2019d invited. Only Echo and Cloud, the two girls we came with, were left in their beaded dresses. Most of the lights were out and Miles Davis was playing \u201cBlue Xmas,\u201d one of our favorites. $FIRSTNAMEB3 put on a red Christmas bulb for added atmosphere. The rest of us sat around the table drinking red wine, also for atmosphere.\n$FIRSTNAMEB3 was sitting in the dis used high-chair with a girl on each arm. This was when we noticed he\u2019d finally acquired a legal drink that evening. \u201cLook!\u201d\nWe all drank to it.\n\u201cGentlemen, I drink to a Happy New Year in a New House, the finest house ever I eat my dinner in.\u201d\nWe cheered and drank.\n\u201cSalud!\u201d yelled strange voices from dark back rooms. \u201cHooray!\u201d\n\u201cSalud!\u201d we all yelled back.\nThen suddenly a New Year\u2019s Eve idea hit $FIRSTNAMEB3. Ordinarily he hates parades, never attends...", "1_14_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-one in the afternoon</span>. I sat down on the bed, a little dizzily, as though I'd been shot in the ear. A stubborn stony nugget of refuse refused to dissolve in my brain. Fuck the gig, I thought. Fuck the gig and fuck the goddamned requiem. I need some beer. I need a great deal of beer. Then I will mail it to myself point A to point B. Draw an arrow on the box. Is it much trouble to be an arrow? Who cares? Let\u2019s not call this madness, this keening and doomed effort to reassemble what they tore asunder. What matters is not just these pitiful shreds we have left to grasp; is not the symbol of a thing, but the thing itself.\nNo mind. Fuck it. I need some beer. I got up again, looking for money. In coins I had three quarters, no dimes and no nickels. I took some change from the woman. While I waited for theloney, I walked over to the \u201cTrader Vic's Cup,\u201d a heavy silver thing that was placed on the semi luminous black of the bar counter. The cup was adorned with intricate blue sea life and figures. The...", "1_16_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nClump, clump, thump\u2014snarl\u2014grrreeeeeee!\nThe June bursts of music, the frightful flurries of crescendo, the roars of great timbres wailed by trumpets, the screams of basses snarling, the constellations of piccolos scintillating, the triple-stopped hom could not be heard any more against the sound of an enormous pad of firecrackers walking down the street.\nIt was not a beautiful fire: that is to say, the fire was not pretty against the windows. A fiery mob came lurching down the street. A member of the mob had lighted a piece of rags tied in blue paper, and was waving it about.\nThere were about two hundred members of the mob. They had on shortsleeved shirts and ties. They swarmed on the pavement by the curb. They slammed into passers-by.\nThe leader of the mob picked up a beer-can that was lying in the street, and threw it on the top of an automobile parked by the curb. The beer-can made a direct hit on the metal hood-ornament of the automobile, and the hood-ornament jumped...", "1_3_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was Sea Green, a thirty-unit condominium development on Balboa Peninsula, Newport Beach, California. The structure known as Unit 32G was the rearmost of the thirty, and clung more loosely than its twenty-nine fellows to the Bermuda cliffs. And now the earthquake had popped loose a corner, and the place was about to fall into the sea.\nThe structure teetered. Two hundred and eighty pounds of frightened, overweight woman teetered with it.\nThe overhead light of the kitchenette was shattered by falling debris. The light seemed to leave the kitchen in darkness as the woman took one staring look at the bathroom door and then dashed out through the broken panel of the living-room window \u2014\nOr rather through the place where the living-room window had been.\nThe woman, Lenore Beadsman, late of Cleveland, Ohio, now of the Sea Green continuum, did not pause to consider the cool and refreshing Pacific sea air. She did not pause to look back and see what happened to the kitchen and living room of her condo as it gave up its anchorage to the cliffs and slid more and more with each passing moment. An old and truly broken branch from a Dudleya farinosa cactus...", "1_11_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven seventeen in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was ten years old, wanted to come with me. He was my big son. He was a big, dark, naked boy. He had a stone axe in his hand. His sisters, Chroma, fifteen, and Genevieve, thirteen, also wanted to come with me. So did their mother. It was not that they were anxious to help me find a pig. It was just an opportunity to get outside the house, to get away from the brooding heat inside.\nAnonymous in the city, anonymous in the Universe, that is the Tralfamadoreean way. On their home planet of Tralfamadore the creatures crawl on their bellies from obscure cave to obscure cave. All night long the Universe rockets whizz by overhead. Most of them are faster than the speed of light. The Tralfamadorians see them coming, year after year. They fire no lasers at the ones that are going away from them. They do nothing. The Universe is disgustingly populous with Earths and Tralfamadorias and Lugars.\nCamille could not endure the thought of crawling on her belly. I was willing to crawl. I was used to it. I did not think it would help.", "1_6_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six o'clock in the morning</span>. I was living in Denver then, was about to be bounced from my bed, and around that instant the sacred anthem of whores, the ra-ra moan, the signal for riot and kickoff, for pulling on thigh-high stockings without cursing or even having to look: \u201cOn, teammates, on! ... come on and let's get it on,\u201d did I hear, was I one of the first, or most hungover or lonely among those beckoned in this very hour, that hour before dawn, when thousands of girls make ten thousand hourly arrangements with themselves to have it off? and all under that banner of self-abandon? and in all this time when a call girl goes about whipping a temptress's harness on? I recall tenderly one Colorado morn a whore's raspy whisper as we put our heads together in the dark: \u201cI am Lila, eagerly awaiting to make your acquaintance, darling.\u201d I\n\u201cWho? Oh, boy,\u201d I muttered, my voice coarse and cracking with disuse, the neck hung with snot. My eyes could barely focus themselves in her direction. I saw her in the dark, felt her presence. \u201cYeah,\u201d I...", "2_14_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"I would like some very hot water,\" said $SURNAME0, \"and a tea bag of the Emperor brand.\"\nAbutments of air around $SURNAME0\u2019s chair blurred, then formed again. A tea service materialized. Tea poured into the pot and into the cup was a transparent brownish amber. The steam that rose from the tea was a transparent brownish amber, too. The tea bag in the pot had lettering on it\u2014nearly illegible lettering, but lettering that could be made out:\nIMPERIAL\n\u2014\"Imperial,\" said $SURNAME0.\nHe attacked his tea bag with a pair of scissors.\nEverybody had his own ideas about why $SURNAME1 had been invited.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had his own idea, too.", "2_6_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-two in the morning</span>.\nThe sunlight stood still, brightened, and became hot.\nThe burst of a steam whistle interrupted the scene\u2014and the time.\nThe steam whistle was on the outskirts of the village.\nThe whistle blew again, calling the workers to the factories and the soldiers to their posts.\nThe three beautiful, deadly young men continued their patrol as though nothing had happened.\nThey marched four abreast, goose-stepping.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew drew their heads together conspiratorially, so they wouldn\u2019t be overheard by the soldiers.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 whispered, \"What was that\u2014what you said about Napoleon?\"\nBig Jew whispered back, \"What he say?\"\n\"You said\u2014you seemed to mean you were Napoleon Bonaparte.\"\n\"Napoleon Bonaparte,\" said Big Jew, smiling. \"Oooh.\" He shrugged. \"What the hell,\" he said. \"Napoleon Bonaparte,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"What do that mean?\"\nBig Jew drummed his fingers on his chest. \"Self,\" he said. \"Self\u2014that\u2019s what that mean.\"\n\"What?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He hadn\u2019t understood that.", "2_3_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-eight in the morning</span>.\n3.6\nMarlin Strauss, Ph.D., lived at 7071 Imperial Highway, Villa Park, the northwest corner of Imperial Highway and Foothill Boulevard in the city of La Habra, California. There was fog along Foothill Boulevard in La Habra that night. The thick white fog swirled hideously on Foothill Boulevard.\nThe low, loping edifice at 7071 Imperial Highway was a two-story stucco house with a wide, tin-roofed veranda surrounded by a spiked iron fence. Inside the spiked iron fence on the front lawn was a sloping garden of aloes with a trickling fountain of orange-pink volcanic glass imported from Japan.\nThere was a low gravel drive from Imperial Highway to the door at 7071 Imperial Highway. A dark maroon Ford sedan was parked in the drive. The maroon Ford sedan was owned by Dr. Strauss.", "1_17_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until six o'clock in the evening</span>, Earth ship time. The room was going through its sweetly controlled shudder. It was on its way to being the salon of the great Golden $SURNAME0. It was on its way to being a ballroom.\nThe room was coming on to horns of gold, platforms of gold, great gold bejeweled statues in corners, diamond-paned windows, gold drapes bedecking huge golden tri-foils (classical in design, but made of gold).\nThe golden tri-foils had gilded valve-pedals under them. And under the valves of one of the tri-foils was a stool that could be raised as a dais by means of a cunning system of hydraulics.\nThe seat of that particular throne was upholstered in gold brocade displaying intricate mythological scenes of confused violence.\nOn the mythological Mars of that brocade, $SURNAME0 was fighting a three-headed dragon.\nThe mural battle was engrossing in detail\u2014right down to the expression on the faces of $SURNAME0 and the dragon as they grappled on the back of a winged horse. $SURNAME0 was rakishly grinning. Whether the dragon\u2019s mouths were smiling or screaming or...", "2_10_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-seven in the morning</span>. Bracknell left the cave, looked back at the Queen $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"I ought really to report in, oughtn\u2019t I, $FIRSTNAMEF1?\" he said. He pictured his long-lost, long-lost home town with all of its stoplights and fire engines. \"I wonder if they think I\u2019m dead,\" he said wistfully, \"or just missing.\"\nHe was alone on Mars and happy.\nHe had to walk three miles in his Space suit before he came to Group Headquarters.\nNobody knew that he had planned the whole thing.\nNobody cared.\nThings had worked out swell.\n\"There you are,\" said the Group Commander.\n\"Here I am,\" said Bracknell. He saluted. Right now he felt like scrapping, but he thought of his Queen $FIRSTNAMEF1 and was peaceful.", "0_17_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. At five forty-six Lang drug out of the \u201crumpus room,\u201d kicked over the runneld bar and staggered out the door, bloodshot eyes on the wall, the ceiling. \u201cSomeday I\u2019ll own this whole damn country!\" he said to the big empty air.\nFor he saw plainly what even his father couldn\u2019t see that day. You see, there was this boy who\u2019d written him a letter already. This was before any of us ever heard of him. Already he was writing Don Jailbird\u2019s brother about himself. About who he was and what he intended to do. And most important, how he meant to do it. Not as an experiment, or to see if it could be done, but because he really intended to do it, no reservations; and to hell with the obstacles, real or imagined.\nAnd after a while this deal that the kid had outlined began to sound so good to Don Howard Microsoft that he sent for the kid. Went to see him in person. The meeting took place in a twilit grayness wreathed in marijuana smoke and foul breath. Old sins and illusions swirled together.\nWhen the kid finished talking, Don Howard...", "1_20_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span> of a certain day in the fall of 1967, and the place was Room 214-C of the Mapleson General Hospital on East 71st Street in New York City. The situation was this. A man lay in bed in Room 214-C. The man was John DiForge. The bed was at the foot of the room, in the corner by the file cabinet and the potted plant, and was the only bed in the room. The room itself was a cubicle, with walls of warm neutral-tinted pressed-wood, whitewashed in places by the handling of two generations of admissions, discharges, and transfers. The walls were interrupted by a view of clear tree branches, through the window at the back of the room, and by a door in the left-hand wall, leading to a communal bathroom, and by a door in the right-hand wall, to Room 214-D. The walls of Room 214-D could be seen through the gap between the door and the doorjamb in 214-C, but the interior of that room was not visible. There was no connecting door. There were two other rooms visible to a conscientious observer in 214-C, but neither of them merited the name of ward. They...", "2_15_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The windows were gray. Henri finished the job he was on, and $FIRSTNAMEB3, who kept circling around him, grabbed his wrist. \u201cCome on, dammit! Let's go! Don't try and tell me so much. I'm telling you! Come and have a cognac with me!\u201d\n\u201cWhere?\u201d asked Henri.\n\u201cDown the street -- I know a place.\u201d Henri rubbed his chin and looked around.", "2_13_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was a basement in an apartment building.\nThere was a windowless wall of rock at one end of the basement. That was all.\n\"If he doesn\u2019t come pretty soon,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"I\u2019m going to inhale the chloroform.\" Stony nodded absently. \"Do you still have the set oftoggle switches you stole from the space ship?\" he said.\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I couldn\u2019t make head or tail out of them. I still got them in my pocket.\"\n\"I wish you\u2019d let me have them, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said Stony. \"They might come in handy.\"\nOutside the building, a white sedan nosed into a no-parking zone. A young man with a blond mustache got out and looked into the basement through the basement window. He saw $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Stony. He waved.", "1_2_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two sixteen in the morning</span>. We had been driving eight hours. Remi and Canuck walked out of the police station into the square. Sailors and girls canvassed them in Spanish. Remi pulled out a gold toothpick, stuck it in his mouth, assumed a sombrero, everybody laughed. A cab came by and we all got in and that was the end of Remi. We never saw him again. In the pre-dawn faint, San Antonio was a deep, dark jungle of churchbells. At three o'clock, Canuck was growling over his driving-wheel: \u201cWhy did you fellows ever bring that guy along?\u201d\nWe didn't care, we were asleep and dreaming in the back of the little Corona, legs stuck out over the running-board, when Canuck pulled up at our Prospect Street door at dawn. We stumbled up the stairs to our sleeping bags in the attic, Arizona was still snoring away. I awoke in darkness at nine o'clock to hear $FIRSTNAMEB3 out in the kitchen clanking dishes. \u201cWhoo-ee!\u201d I heard him yell to himself. \u201cI'm in the land of okies, awright! All the sooner to see $FIRSTNAMEF4!\u201d I...", "1_17_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>, and something was going to happen in four minutes.\nThey would throw their message away in four minutes more.\nThey would wholeheartedly believe their message was a failure four minutes from now.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 laughed softly, derisively. He knew that both $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 would soon be millionaires\u2014that $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s Newport mansion would have statues of marble, not wood.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was going to have her famous blue diamond.\nThere would be statues of marble for both of them.\nThe statues would be erected in their memory by grateful crowds.\nAnd it would all come about so easily, so nicely.\nThe only one in trouble was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nHe looked at his watch. He had two seconds left in which to begin to worry.\nUnhitching the watch, he flung it into the bushes on the left-hand side of the path. He did not have to have a watch to know it was four minutes to six.\nEverything was falling apart.\nEverything was turning to disaster.\nEven the fine Italian suit, purchased for this historic event, had a hole in the left elbow.\n\"Two\u2014the Best and the Brightest\u2014\"\nThe space ship of $FIRSTNAMEM1 N.", "0_15_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nNo preparations whatever had been made for the ceremony at which $FIRSTNAMEB0 was to be reconstituted as a madly happy human being.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t know what the ceremony was going to be like, or who was going to officiate, or what the setting would be like. He didn\u2019t know the name of a single person attending the ceremony, or even the names of all the colors in the spectrum. He didn\u2019t know who the President of the United States of America was, or who the President of Mars was. He hadn\u2019t the faintest idea how many years of school he had missed, or how long it had been since his last meal. His sense of time was badly impaired. He imagined that he had been in his cell a fortnight or so, instead of two years.\nHis pulse and respiration rates, according to the watch embedded in his left breast, were forty-seven per minute and normal.\nHe had never felt less ready for anything in his life\u2014or less concerned about the prospects for feeling ready for anything ever again. All his juices were juices of indifference, juices not conducive to exultation and jubilation.\nHis mind contained one happy thought\u2014the thought...", "0_12_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nWhat was taking place at this time was not extraordinary by Martian standards. At this time, no more than eighty-six minutes before the arrival of the representative of Earth, the headquarters staff was beginning to work up a sweat. Now the real work was beginning, now men were climbing ladders to crow\u2019s-nests to scan the dusty horizon with binoculars, men were drinking coffee and swearing, men were throwing Brigadier General Felix Dub Jung commandingly into the operations office and taking up their posts around the map table, men were sharpening pencils, spitting into the spittoons, winking at girls, sneezing, yawning, thinking about lunches, administering pimples, licking the blade of a knife with a meditative tongue, breathing on their eyeglasses to moisten the polish, blowing their nose and looking out the window at the dully resilient scarps, pits, hummocks, and washes of the land.\nNow the real work was beginning.\nThe interplanetary sail-plane arriving just in time to catch the action was piloted by $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1. Their love, and the tape recorder in the dashboard of the plane, were, for the time being, forgotten.\nThey...", "0_13_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one eighteen in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME1, happy, and hungry, $SURNAME1 climbed down from the caboose and walked along the railroad track, kicking stones.\nFrom somewhere came the mournful notes of \"Jefferson and Liberty.\" Also, at regular twenty-second intervals, the booming of a cannon far off. It was Saturday-afternoon Cannon Boom on the Willow Grove Rifle Range, three miles away.\nAs $SURNAME1 climbed over a stile in the barb-wire fence that surrounded the rifle range, he sniffed the air for smoke. He sniffed in such an over-conscientious manner that he almost fell to the ground. He had stepped in the middle of a hornet\u2019s nest.\n\"Oh my God,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. He jumped over the stile and fell to his knees in the rough grass bordering the range. He began frantically rubbing his leg.\n\"Oh my God,\" said $SURNAME1 again.\nSo much for the time, the place, and the innocent condition into which $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, richest man in the world, had innocently been born.\nAnd now, having told all that much, it behooves the biographer to look down into his dust-coat pockets and see what, if anything, he has to tell about...", "2_21_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-four in the evening</span>.\nThat evening, Lang was looking particularly twenty-two. And Lenore was especially twenty-five.\nJune: The Desert\n/d/\n\u201cI feel precariously bewildered and hazy, again.\u201d\n\u201cYes. It\u2019s fine. Go ahead and feel whatever you like.\u201d\n\u201cHow can you tell what I\u2019m feeling?\u201d\n\u201cYou\u2019re objectively making sounds. Your eyes are a bit moist. Your cheeks are flushed. Your nostrils are flared. Your tongue moistens the inside of your lower lip, repeatedly. It tends to wobble, a bit.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cI think I\u2019m going to have to have a little time, again.\u201d\n\u201cYes. You may have as much time as feels good.\u201d\n\u201cHow can time feel? How can time feel good?\u201d\n\u201cAgain, as you wish.\u201d\n\u201cI really think I need to take a walk.\u201d", "2_14_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-three in the afternoon</span>.\nA loudspeaker in the hall suddenly filled the hall with song:\nWhen we die\u2014Good-by,boys;\nA bloody red sky\u2019s inmow.\nGood-by, fellows; we\u2019re journeying to Cal\u2014i\u2014for\u2014ni\u2014a . . . .\nThe cheerfulness of the song did not go with the mood of the eating soldiers. Still, the song was made holy by the sense of imminent outrage and massacre. There was the smack of doom in every quavering note. The dining hall filled with tears, filled with gasps and shudders. It filled with the triple moan of a ghost chorus, singing:\nWhen we die\u2014Hurray!\nSo hurray for you and shirie-o!\nThe boom, boom, boom of the big guns began before dinner was over.\nIt was two thirty-five in the afternoon.", "0_10_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-seven in the morning</span>. As a clattering confusion of people ascended the ramps and crowded through the entrances to the displays, people walking on the outside looked in with expressions of wonder and shock. The inside of the exhibit area was far more crowded than the outside. The air was filled with the excited shouting of children. Only a few adults were inside, and they were looking at $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and trying to figure out how much the man was really worth.\n$SURNAME1 had no idea how much he was really worth. The only persistent form of income he had ever had came from selling his shares in the Hollywood film company, Magnum Opus Pictures. He had no idea of what Magnum Opus Pictures was worth. He had gotten the shares in the first place by calling long distance and charging a subscription to a prayer breakfast. He was praying for the starving millions of India\u2014and, God, in His infinite wisdom, had directed $SURNAME1 to buy stock in Magnum Opus Pictures.\n$SURNAME1 did not understand big business at all. He had had no part in the management of Magnum Opus\u2014had never even visited the place. To him, Magnum Opus was in Hollywood, California, U.S.A., Earth, Solar System, Milky Way, boundlessly blowing gas, Universes...", "2_12_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after noon</span>.\nA nuclear explosion outside Newport destroyed the antenna of the local television station. This was the only television station on Mars. Its antenna was three hundred yards from $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s house. The station went off the air permanently.\nThere was no direct effect on $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe oaks that surrounded $SURNAME0\u2019s drawing room were among the stoutest, strongest and most shockproof living things on earth. An oak drawing room is a very sound place from which to view a fireworks display. The boom of the gongs was not so great as the explosion outside might have suggested. It gave the show an added richness, but it did not cow the executioner or reduce $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 to a shivering, death-amused quivering jelly.\nThe fireworks display was the last ceremony at all connected with the war. It was, in a sense, the grand finale of all festivities celebrating the battle of Pearl Harbor.", "0_16_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1 who was ten years old and consequently a bit slow, had missed the number five bus that came to Marble Road every afternoon, at exactly five o'clock. $FIRSTNAMEB1 had come running to the corner of the shabby street to try to intercept the number five before it left Marble Road, and had forgotten that it had already gone.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, who was one of the Martian offspring, and was not more than one-eighth Martian himself, was small for his age and weighed less than seventy-five pounds on Mars, where the atmospheric pressure was an equivalent of nearly seven thousand feet above sea level. At an Earth-pressure equivalent of 3600 feet, ten-year-old $FIRSTNAMEB1 could carry a hundred pounds to the top of Marble Road and not get winded. The highest part of Market Street was 64 feet above sea level. The top of Marble Road above its three fathom pit beneath was 704 feet high.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was bewildered when he found that he could not carry his rifle and his suitcase up the seven hundred feet of marble steps. The staircase bent around in a concave arc and was twelve feet wide, and it was covered with a red velvet carpet. A grandfather clock in a tower above the staircase told Bo...", "2_9_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe crowd was making ready to cheer the really big news\u2014the news that the Hixon space ship would be struck by a meteor at ten o\u2019clock.\nWhat the crowd did to brace itself for the crash of the Hixon into the meteor was to shout \"Hip\u2014hip\u2014\" and then clap their hands over their ears.\nThen came the crash.\nThen came the cheering\u2014the cheering for the brave boys in the Hixon ... for their wives and lovers ... for their sweethearts, mothers, and fathers ... for their hobbyhorses, pet dogs, and mothers-in-law ... for summer softball ... for coonhunting ... for squirrelpotpie ... for good times and God.\nRoosh shook his head morosely at all this foolishness.\nHe was totally without interest in the news from space. He had lived with the spatial loneliness so long that any happy tumult seemed to him meaningless and directionless. A zero was a zero was a zero.\nWhen the crowd cheered at the crash and the meteoric explosion, Roosh spat on the ground.", "2_18_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-seven in the evening</span>, Earthling Time. The airship in the sky was a silent ship no longer. Its hover-motors made it a three-dimensional dance of diamonds. Its searchlights, sweeping contentedly across the terrain, threw nests of black shadows among the dunes.\nThe airship was anchored directly over the rocket.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 didn\u2019t know about that. They had an idea that anyone coming to the cabin would land his ship outside the treeline.\nThere was a stirring in the hawthorn bush.\nIt was a human foot, kicking the bush.\nA tall man stepped out of the bushes at the base of the boulder. He was dressed in black Impression garments of plain weave. He wore a mask of black velvet. His hands and bare arms were covered with black-velvet gloves.\nA black velvet band encircled his neck. It held a black bag against the wall of his throat. The bag was connected to the band by a hose and was over an inch thick. The hose carried oxygen to the man\u2019s lungs.", "2_20_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-six in the evening</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 still felt like a fool.\nA whole new thing began to hit $FIRSTNAMEB0 when he looked at the shiny yellow ticket in his hand. He didn\u2019t yet know what the ticket was for, and somehow that made the whole thing seem more glorious. Any fool can drive a nail through a piece of wood without thinking about it. The man with the hammer there on the scaffold may not even be the same man who drives the first nail. But that first man will always be famous.\nEight thirty-eight, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 still felt like a fool.\nThen a mate for the golden ship and its crew was announced on the loudspeaker.\nApplause broke the vanilla murmur of Drinks Galore.\nAnd only one man and one woman clapped their brains out.", "2_4_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. The radio was playing a square dance. I was listening, but the music was behind my eyes where the eye problems were. I was almost dreaming. The radio was playing \u201cUnchained Melody.\u201d I suddenly realized what had me frightened. It was this sudden silence. On any other street in any other town in America there would have been the all-night sounds of insects, even through the winter. But there were no insects in Las Vegas. It being the last town before the Mojave, the insects had learned to eat tourists. The silence had a curse. It meant hours and hours to go before dawn. At the hotel three minutes would be enough sleep in thirty-eight separate plastic dishes. And the sun would rise to the explosive tune of America. Then the road across America\u2026.", "0_8_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-two in the morning</span>. The sun was streaming through the windows of the AP bureau in Reno. I was standing at my desk in a stupor, having just hung up from a call from my attorney in L.A. My attorney had told me that my wife had gone ahead with our divorce action, using my car as collateral for legal fees and offering no alimony.\nMy attorney said: \u201cThe judge made her the custodian of both your cars, and gave her the house to live in for four years, with the obligation of only two hundred and ninety\u2014five dollars a month rent if she can get someone to take your children to school from eight to six, twice a day. Oh yes: he also made her the beneficiary of your $2500 life insurance\u2014just in case you should ever have an accident.\u201d My attorney explained that this was standard legal procedure in California divorces involving children.\n\u201cAnything else?\u201d I asked. \u201c... Did she get the dog?\"\n\u201cThe dog? What dog?\u201d\n\u201cPolice dog. Nark, former L.A.P.D. German shepherd.\"\n\u201cYou have a German shepherd, and you didn\u2019t tell me?\"\n\u201cTell...", "1_17_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-two in the afternoon</span>. On Desolation Road the mournful robots were singing:\nMaybe that gal meant it kindly\nMaybe she's fond of you and me,\nAnd maybe it's 'cause she thinks it's right,\nAnd maybe and maybe\u2014-maybe you'll see.\nThe tune and the volume were held constant. Omerta hung over Desolation Road like a thick grey fog. At two minutes after six, an ambulance came flying along from the Hilton, hit the curbstone at the corner of Void and Wiwhence, bounced into a palm, nosed into a gooseneck lamp, then spun to a halt with its rear end pointing at the general direction of the ticket starport. Red emergency lights flashed. The back doors flew open. Hermas, the cross-eyed driver, jumped out, ran to the rear, and fell to his knees in prayer. Very nice. Meanwhile, somebody in the ambulance who had remained hidden from view now stood up in the back. His boots rested on the shoulders of a priest. The heels of the boots were bright red.\nTigery, number 9 3/4, was observed. He knocked Hermas over getting out of the ambulance, then stood him up, all the while shouting in an almost,...", "0_3_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span> of Wednesday, December 4, that Greg and SynchoSpaz and $FIRSTNAMEB1 met at the Space Wanderer\u2019s Room, on the mezzanine floor of the Newport Casino.\nOfficially, the meeting of the Three Musketeers of the Universe was supposed to be a second attempt on the part of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 to enlist $FIRSTNAMEB1 and Spaz in his plan for swindling the Space Station of its two thousand shares of Zenith stock.\nUnofficially, the meeting at three o\u2019clock in the morning was a sequel to the extraordinary events of the lawn party.\nThere had been nothing imaginary about the tiny nuclear explosions that Greg and the dog had savored. And there had been nothing imaginary about the voice from space that had followed.\nThe voice had come from the $FIRSTNAMEM1 N. Bently. The coded message had burned itself into the brain of $FIRSTNAMEB1, as it had already burned itself into Charon\u2019s.\nAnd now that $SURNAME1, Spaz, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had their heads together in the Space Wanderer\u2019s Room, the swindle of the Space Station, and the dog\u2019s affair on Mercury, were far from their minds. The mind of the conjunction was much more concrete...", "1_13_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was certain that the names of the people being carried in the parade were Stony Stevenson, machine gunner, and Rawley Scott, rifleman.\nTo $FIRSTNAMEB0, the names were far too explicit, far too close to home, to be mere coincidence. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind didn\u2019t want the names to be a coincidence. His mind wouldn\u2019t accept the names passively. It arranged the names in patterns of meaning, forced the names to mean something, forced the pattern to be one that $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind could draw comfort from.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind told $FIRSTNAMEB0 that the parade of strong young men was a repetition of the last time $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been in distribution center forty-one. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind told $FIRSTNAMEB0 that in a manner of speaking he was going through the thing all over again. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind said that there was no point in straining for details of his previous experience; the details would come to him when he needed them. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind said that $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t have to control the course of events; he had only to remember what he had been told.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind then gave $FIRSTNAMEB0...", "2_13_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-four in the afternoon</span>.\nThe narrator sees the gossamer threads over the lagoon transform themselves into handsome marbled piers.\nThe narrator next examines the heterosexual activities of Tralfamadore in his mind, since they are to play such a big role in the future. He sees his vision of the gorgeous palaces and lacey bridges of the capital city dissolve into crowd upon crowd of millions and millions of Tralfamadorian soul mates making love in the surf along the splendid beaches. Every one of them\u2014or so the narrator tries to imagine\u2014is as much in love as the narrator himself.\nIt is lovely to envision such a scene.\nThe narrator envisions a northern part of the capital city, where a stone staircase on a tiny peninsula of white wood leads up from the beach to the grassy spires of the housing area. Under an elm tree at the foot of the stairs sits a stocky, beagle-eyed old man in a white robe. The robe has an elk-skin in the pocket.\nA fizzing, sparkling message capsule comes down to perch on the grass at the old man\u2019s feet. The man busts open the capsule.", "2_21_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>. Everything was very quiet, with the exception of gusts of wind. Something in the East twinkled and flashed.\nTime Present and Time Past\nMoscow and Corfu, together. Past and present. High above. Two cities. Flowers in Corfu. The noise of Mr. Bloemker eating something sticky, sneakingly, out of a jar. The smell of vodka. Flashing lights from the steel neck of a Soviet residentura at Karl Marx Prospect. In the rear of a small bus, nearing Syreva, Lenore feels a tiny twinge in her hip. Gato Groats approaches. Exterior: the entrance to Lenore\u2019s room in Funland, half lit. Swithin Dike in the darkness, in black silk.\nTransparent night, much interior. Few people, most soundless. Music. An orange, coming to the surface of a glass filled with water. The sound of dried beans rasping together. A rush of flour against metal. An ant tries to climb the leg of Stella. Stunning happiness.", "1_7_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span> of Monday, December 23rd, and the place was the shore of that shallow northern sea on whose far shore ancestor Lessingham had walked in his sleep five hundred years ago. The place was the bleak, windy end of the world. The castle which had given its name to the headland stood on the northernmost cape of the island. Ten miles east of it lay the ruins of another castle, de\u00ebdlich Dungyvernewhich tourists wrongly named Dundarg. Between these two castles had stood the one which men called Riddscatlgerrich, the Castle of Rats. All these fortresses had been built by various masters of the China expedition, but drained and effete, they had surrendered, before Herlko had ever found them, to that same infinite enemy, the salt sea winds, which now whistled over the Ridds castle remains. Between those two standing fortresses, Riddscatlgerrich and Dundarg, must once have stretched a ten-mile frontier guarded by sentries of night against invading day. At the border must have stood a milestone; and in glimmer of watches when neither moon shone nor star peeped, a V shaped figure would have to climb down from the bed of the wagon that conveyed the...", "2_2_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-eight in the morning</span>. Lenore felt that she had to leave, if only to get away from Lang\u2019s armpit and the smell of his stomach. She pulled off her crown of white branches, dropped it on the table, and smoothed her hair. It had gotten all messy. Lang went on rummaging in the foliage.\n\u201cProbably my throat will kill me, even though I drank all that yogurt, but I think I need to make like a beeline, here,\u201d Lenore said.\nLang looked up at her. \u201cTonight was really a singular experience for me, I can tell you.\u201d\n\u201cReally, I should leave,\u201d Lenore said, hearing the kids start to chant outside the gym. \u201cI\u2019m sure Gramma will be awake, if you want to come see her, too.\u201d\n\u201cI would indeed like to come see her, if that\u2019s all right.\u201d", "2_2_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-six in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 walked out of the post office into a rain as chilly as the air inside the adobe building had been. Along the two blocks back to her motel she pictured the message arranged in patterns like those in The Book of the Dead, coming up with variations both pleasing and repellent. Her reflection stared at her out of puddles, making approaches and withdrawals, never quite committing itself, while the rain jittered its little tags of seeds on her coat.\nIn her room, taking off her coat, she could remember only one other telegram in her whole life, received years before at\u5b6bshermin marital home, which had turned out to be a foster home: Aunt Tessa dead are you coming home for funeral. $FIRSTNAMEF2 had been twelve then. She hadn\u2019t. It hardly seemed to matter now, whether she\u2019d gone to her aunt\u2019s funeral or not. She was nobody\u2019s aunt.", "2_18_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-three in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nThe quandary in reasonably human terms was this: Which is the more real\u2014the time in the miasmal equator of the planet Mercury, or the time at which the message is being received?\nTo illustrate\u2014if at the instant a message arrives, a ship of the Solar Army is boarding a tunnel-ship on the planet Mercury, the Solar Army officer in command will greet the bewildered officer of the tunnel-ship by saying, \"Excuse us for our late arrival.\" Hearing those words under the circumstances described, how could one possibly know whether the Solar Army was punctual or twenty-seven years late?\nWhich is the more real\u2014the time in the miserable tropic of Mars on a rainy Tuesday, or the time at which the message is being received?\nWhich is the more real\u2014the time when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 were being sprayed with shrapnel at Quonset Point, or the time at which the message is being received?", "2_22_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-one in the evening</span>.\nThe Space Wanderer was making love to his wife for the second time that day.\nThis time, the lovemaking took place on the lawn outside the Tower of Gates.\nThis time, the only witness was the paranoiac, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe being who was then and there making love to his wife had been born $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 Chauvenet in Newport, Rhode Island. His father had been President of the United States\u2014and still is. All who knew little $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 Chauvenet called him Win.\nWin\u2019s parents had two paranoiac tendencies which they were kind enough to share with their son. His parents did not, however, share their supreme virtue\u2014which was their tendency to be undisturbed by anything very unexpected.\nWin\u2019s great contribution to, and pleasure in, his love affair was his discovery of the rhythm in the love affair\u2014the rhythm that carried him from one peak to another, and back to the joyous valley of anticipation between the peaks. All young lovers have this rhythm. It leads them around the Three Happy Estates of Love\u2014Affairs, Marriages, and Divorces.", "0_14_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-two in the afternoon</span>. I knew somebody in Las Vegas or I could have gone there. But I wanted to get back on the road and get my wrist fixed in California. The things that make you hesitate in this state are not real. Each time you get stung you extend your boundaries. It would take about six weeks for my finger to heal. In the meantime I wanted to drive north. I had this itching for speed -tell everything to hell and put the wide pedal to the metal. I felt like letting everybody off in hot water, which is what I did. At Gedney, I dropped my mother and my worried friend, with their assorted bags, off at the Greyhound bus. I kissed my mother. She was blushing and mumbled something that sounded like a blessing. It was a blessing. She blessed me out of her sight and watched me roar off across the concrete river in the red Ford. I could see them in the rear-view mirror. They stood in the middle of the highway watching my dust. That was the last time I saw them for two years. When my finger healed I wrote my aunt in New York. They were there. They had just arrived by bus from San Francisco. It took them six weeks. She wrote that they were living...", "2_1_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was still holding on to his throne. He was fingering it with prim curiosity, wondering what sort of material it was made of. It felt like oyster-white satin brocade, but it didn\u2019t crumble readily under his touch.\nThere was magic in the touch. $SURNAME1 was beginning to understand his surroundings. The throne and the dais had not materialized out of nothing. They had materialized out of his own mind.\nAll over Mars, the $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 Dream Building and Estate were appearing, and inside all over the buildings, estates, and marble halls, were action figures of $SURNAME1.\nThey were freestanding, three-inch-high bronze statuettes.\nThe statuettes were incredibly precise reproductions of how $SURNAME1 had looked in the morning.\nThe detail extended even to the flecks of dandruff on $SURNAME1\u2019s shoulders.\n$SURNAME1 wasn\u2019t sure yet whether his eyes were open or shut. All he could see were thumb-sized disks of blue light. But he sensed, somehow, that his eyes were bright green\u2014bright and beautiful, like the spun gold that was used for the tassels on his sandals.", "2_3_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. The Universe was afire with spiral nebulae, was sharp with the cries of curved, otherworldly creatures.\nThe Universe, in which $SURNAME0 and his trouble-shooter existed, was thirty million times as large, was a hundred million times as energetic, was thirty million times as fast-moving, as the Universe of unclouded Earth.\nIt was a lonely Universe, and cold. The central fires of the spiral nebulae had long since died. There was no central source of heat, no central source of light. There was no one form or kind of matter that predominated.\nAs lonely and as cold as was the Universe in which $SURNAME0 and his troubled friend circled about the old maypole of Mars\u2014the Universe was more interesting and more exciting than the Universe of unclouded Earth. It had more mercy and more humor.", "2_18_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-one in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time, one minute after the end of the fumigation.\n\"Hello,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Is there something interesting there?\"\n$SURNAME1 saw that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the man, had a companion. Either he or his companion was interrupting the ironically precise and richly painful history in progress.\nHe or his companion didn\u2019t know where he was, or what was going on.\nHe was horrified.\nThe rusty iron slab in the wall before him said:\nPLEASE REMAIN QUIET.\nTHIS IS A CRUCIAL MOMENT IN WORLD HISTORY.\nTHOSE WHO MAKE LOUD NOISES\nWILL FLASH BACK AND FORTH FROM THE PRISON-\nHOUSE WORLD TO THIS HEAVENLY ONE\nAND RETURN WOUNDED AND UNHOLY.\nWHEN THE HISTORY COMES BACK,\nTHOSE WHO MADE LOUD NOISES WILL BE\nGASPING, AGONIZED, LECHEROUS.\nTHEY WILL HAVE BEEN SUDDENLY EXPELLED\nFROM THIS GARDEN OF HEAVENLY DELIGHTS AND", "1_2_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-nine in the morning</span>. I had about ten dollars in my pockets\u2014not counting the twenty for Terry\u2014and a diamond ring I'd bought in a drugstore that afternoon for twenty-eight dollars. That was my mood that night. Oh yes, I thought of Tim and Maysie, they were safely on their way somewhere, they were fine couple; I had to keep reminding myself of that. I passed the Greyhound bus and trolley terminal. I saw the benches, only three unstained today; most were wet with drizzle. A man slept abre to my left. In a little hour there would be a few kids, hitchhikers -- most of them were Okies -- who spent nights around this terminal and took Greytrucks in the morning all across America. They crawled on, laughed by the drivers and put off at the first truckstop up ahead. This was the terminal the Okies passed going the other direction, with hopps Pennsylvanias; those Okies from California in their broke-down cars headed east toward their past: Missouri, Oklahoma, Kentucky, Tennessee, Virginia, and on back; a big circle. Said I to myself amly, Ah, the poor Okies, like the pioneers going west in covered wagons. One...", "2_17_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThere was no wind on Mars, either\u2014only the terrible tidal winds of Spiral Neg-atron, a spiraling black hole in the universe just outside the Solar System.\nAt six o\u2019clock, there was to be only one Earthling on Mars, and he was an Army fool named Private Bozo Denton.\n\"Are you happy, Mr. $SURNAME0?\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 archly.\nThe question carried a number of seminal meanings. In the first place, $SURNAME0 knew that the thought of happiness made Salo miserable. Salo was the little Martian engineer who had contrived, and then forced himself to stay and operate, the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibulum.\nMars had given that clumsy contrivance to Salo as a farewell present.\nIn the second place, $SURNAME0 was asking whether or not Salo\u2019s contrivance was performing its sole reason for existing: keeping open a one-way loop between Mars and Earth. Through that loop, $SURNAME0 and his chrono-synclastic infundibular machinery had come to insult Magdalen, make love to her, and receive from her a child.", "1_13_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. At that moment, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, who had been asleep in a supply room beneath the tracks, emerged to expose himself to the gaze of the people.\nThe lust of the crowd for their God was quenched.\nThey did not shriek or even whisper. Their gaze fell from $SURNAME0\u2019s face to the solar system spectator on his wrist.\nA miracle that had seemed perfectly marvelous when viewed through the distorting lens of Gabriel coulton\u2019s The Space Wanderer completely vanished when seen face to face. The little ball of flapping gold foil was gorgeous, but it was obviously only a flimsy contrivance, after all.\nThe red-eyed woman named $FIRSTNAMEF1, who had broken her shackles, wandered away from the flock to examine closer a shaggy bollard on which was written the official name of the God she had been addressing. She read the name on the bollard, then looked up in astonishment.\nIn place of $SURNAME0, there was a creature so transparent that his optical illusion of solidity was lent entirely by the gold features on his watch.\nThe features on the watch looked out at $FIRSTNAMEF1 with an expression of infinite sadness.\nMerely...", "0_7_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>. This was June 19, according to the space observer's calendar. Only three Earthling days could elapse before the life or death of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his mate depended on what they did on Mercury.\nA description of the place where $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his mate went next might be a help to the reader.\nThe place was a barrack\u2014a barrack for the hopeless poor in a barrack\u2014crowded army base. The army base was the Army Ordnance Missile Base that had once been the village of Corona, New York. The barrack was number 46-C of a long double row of barrack.\n46-C was a barrack 18 feet long, 10 feet broad, and 2 stories high. It was faced with red brick. It was roofed with British roofing felt. There were eight windows set in the front wall. There was a door in the middle of the front wall. There were four pipes for hot water, five pipes for cold water, and one foul-weather drainpipe in the roof over the kitchen and bathroom. There were two appliances in the kitchen, two in the bathroom. One of the kitchen appliances was a Hotpoint two-burner gas stove, another was a two-door Frigida...", "2_3_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nIn a room off the living room of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s cottage, Fern and $FIRSTNAMEB2 lay snuggled on a big four-poster bed. They were playing a game that $FIRSTNAMEB2 had invented when he was four years old.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had of late been playing the game by himself almost exclusively.\nHe still called it His Master\u2019s Voice.\nOne of the two was to go to sleep. The other was to get up from the bed unobserved and leave the room. Then the one who was asleep was to nod or stop sleeping whenever he felt like it. Whenever he nodded or stopped sleeping, he was to use his mind to send a signal to the other.\nThen the other was to come sneaking back into the room. If he entered the room unobserved, he had to imitate the barking of a dog. If he entered the bedroom while the sleeper was nodding, he imitated the gasping of a beautiful redfish on the Shackleford Banks, off the coast of North Carolina, when it has been hooked deep in its throat by a spirit-drunk kingfisher.\nAnd so the game would go on and on.", "1_14_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 tidied up his real-estate office for the last time, placed his father's sport coat and hat on the back seat of his turquoise space cruiser, and, with the assistance of a steel rod and a pair of white gloves, strapped his son, $FIRSTNAMEB2, into his baby seat.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 examined $FIRSTNAMEB2 carefully to make sure he was secure. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was still a little tattered from having been used as a dummy in a shooting gallery in the hive of Tranch. In fact, that was where $FIRSTNAMEB1 had picked him up\u2014Tranch, which lay almost directly on the hundred and eighty-six-thousandth paralell of the Western Line, had been the $FIRSTNAMEB1 manor in the days when $FIRSTNAMEB1 himself was a traveling salesman.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had been tried and found wanting for the role of $FIRSTNAMEB1 heir. He had been expelled from the traveling-salesman school for insubordination. He was faster with his fists than any other boy in the school. On four occasions he had laid out the instructor.\n\"Damn it, boy\u2014\" the instructor had said, after being laid out for the fourth time, \"you ever hit anybody before?\"\n\"No, sirr...", "0_7_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty in the morning</span> and we were sitting in the sportscar in front of the Hibiscus Motel, which is a sleazy place on South Street that rents rooms by the month and has a swimming pool in the courtyard and all kinds of space age prefabricated plastic furniture in the rooms. We had picked up the Seville just across the state line in Belle Glade and had already taken the tops off three of the four bottles of Southern Comfort we\u2019d brought along for the ride, along with half of a bottle of tequila that appeared to have been mixed with Tang. S is a fiend for Tang and mixes it in anything, alcoholic or otherwise. It\u2019s one mean drink; with the tequila it cuts purple chases across your eyeballs.\nTrying...so hard...to concentrate...\nIt was a fine morning at the motel. Watch it. There go some interesting birds by the pool. No, got to drive. Anybody want to watch the sunrise at the Cape? Huh? What a concept.. Eatin\u2019 or bein\u2019 eaten...which...which is it gonna...be? There goes a fine, hefty assault cop by the pool. A guard at the door. We may have to make a quick exit...", "2_17_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to six o'clock in the evening</span>. The crowd on the corner of Hollywood and Vine sagged, wavered, and became a mob.\n$SURNAME0 took Salo by the bones of his hips and lifted him high. He looked up at Salo for a long time. \"Why do you want to go back to Titan?\" he finally said.\nSalo was taken by surprise. \"There is a \u2014 experiment I forgot about,\" he said.\n\"What kind of an experiment?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"An important one,\" said Salo.\nThe crowd on the corner of Hollywood and Vine was a writhing pustule of mucous membrane, engorged with fluid, seething with pus.\n\"What kind of an experiment?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I think\u2014I\u2019ll have to ask Winsted for the details,\" said Salo. \"It\u2019s a matter of inter-phylum re-adaptation.\"\n\"What does that mean?\" said $SURNAME0. \"A Salo-man becomes an Earthman, and he finds himself unhappy? A Salo-man becomes an Earthman, and he loses his suave and sophisticated outlook on life?\"", "2_11_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-six in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his talented young friend were on their way, having committed murder in the name of the state. They were in an elevator with a mirrored ceiling, being carried up through the emerald building.\nThe mirrors multiplied them endlessly. More and more ghosts came out of their images to fill the elevator.\nAs their images multiplied, it seemed that the ghosts became younger and happier and more handsome with every floor they rose.\nAt the top was a fountain in which the two were reflected with infinite complexity. The fountain was spurting gems of all known colors. They fell back to make more room for others.\nThe last floor was numbered 28. The elevator stopped at it and the door opened.\nA laughing young girl waved to them from the fountain. Even before she came close enough to identify herself, $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew she was $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Surprise!\" she said. This was all she would ever have to say to $FIRSTNAMEB0 from now on.\n\"Surprise\u2014Surprise\u2014Surprise\u2014\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 said back to her, remembering all the pain he had put her through when he was stupid, and happy that there would be no more pain. Their images in the mirrors watched them embrace.", "1_4_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>. In fourteen minutes, at precisely four o'clock, Congress would officially reconvene in permanent session following the campaigns for the fall elections.\nNow the new Congress, having been elected in an atmosphere of stern morality, would have to take official action on the hair question.\nIt was not suggested officially who, if anybody, would practice cannibalism.\nThe President was, of course, helpless in all this. All he could do, at worst, would be to veto any proposal made to him by Congress\u2014and, of course, there was no way whatever, at this late date, for Congress to force him to change the color of his hair.\nIt was rumored in Washington that intimate associates of the President had urged him, in the most earnest way, to get rid of whatever potion he was using on his hair. There was no hope at all of keeping confidential what he was doing to his hair. This was plain from the attacks in Congress by Miss Larousse and Senator Nix.\nThe President\u2019s closest advisers argued that, since the ailment was incurable, and since the colored-hair medicine was secret only so long as the President denied using it, the President should announce that he was using colored hair medicine\u2014or, rather...", "2_5_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-five in the morning</span> and I looked at my attorney. He had a red line across his neck. I was holding his head against the door, jamming it there and holding it until\u2014in the words of our street attorney\u2014we had \u201cclearly established intent.\u201d The muscles in my arm were rubbery from fatigue and tension. But I knew he would come after me.\nADDITIONAL FIVE MINUTES\u2019 TEXT DELETED AT REQUEST OF ATTORNEY\nWhat a week! What a terrible bloody week! I was wearing my blue bathrobe, slippers and a \u201cten\u2014gallon\u201d hat when I drove slowly down the crowded Ault Road, slipping behind an Ever Ready van packed with frantic young Australians who tossed beer cans at my head as they came by. I hit one and dented the fender of the Shark...shit, this machine was worth $500; it would have to be replaced. Not to worry about the hat. I had two more: a beautifully sewn black one with a silver eagle on the front, and a gray one with the blue presidential seal...a thank\u2014you gift from Red Goldstein, at the Mint.", "2_17_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nIt had begun to rain.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s suffering wife introduced herself as $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s suffering wife for the simple reason that she was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s suffering wife. She was very likely still $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s suffering wife, even though she was happy to have her hand held by this gentle, guileless little man.\nHis name was $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nHe had a son named Stony.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s wife asked $FIRSTNAMEB0 to please drive her to her home in Newport, Rhode Island, where her son, little Stony, would be getting out of school in ten minutes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was more than glad to agree. Newport, Rhode Island, was right next door to Butler, Massachusetts. The space ship that had brought $FIRSTNAMEB0 to Mars had been warming up idly on the tarmac at the Butler airport. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was at the wheel of the space ship before an hour had passed from the time the little man from Mars had made his announcement. The system of unscrewing ship parts was so simple that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had it mastered in two minutes.\nHe gunned the motor in under two minutes more, and tore out of there like hell would be hot on his tail.", "0_11_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven eighteen in the morning</span> of November 1, 1992, and the place was the rear upstairs room of Vigorous Bethany\u2019s Stylish Hair and Skin Appointments, a combination salon, establishment, and altar to the great god of human hair and beauty, in Cleveland\u2019s west side, off Chinato. The room had a picture window flanked by two false windows that held pictures of windows flanked by real windows that held pictures of windows, in a kind of fractal nesting-doll of insets, and a New Wave mural on the back wall. The mural was white and contained epoxy representations of eyes, appliances, objects, countenances all around the white space, so that the space was filled, though no reality was delineated. The white contained the eyes, the space contained the eyes and the walls and the space, too. The space contained Lenore as well, here, in the plastic chair in front of the chinning platform\u2014the legs and feet of which contained her\u2014and the uniformed arm, and the metal comb, and the glass cutting surface filled with a whole line of aspirin-sized bottles, and fanned combs, off white and charcoal, and glass cases of lurid and unnatural hanks, clustered like helixes...", "2_14_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nHell grumbled a little, but it couldn\u2019t put on the suits. There was nothing it could do with them. There were no souls to fill them.\nThe bespoke suits went in a flash to every bum who happened to be strolling past the Pearly Gates at the moment.\nThe bums, stunned and enchanted by their good fortune, began to put the suits on.\nNo one in hell could take the suits off the bums. The bums had been promised salvation. And nothing was hideous and repulsive to them anymore.\n\"Sir,\" whispered the Devil to God, \"I could tear their arms and legs off and they\u2019d still be grateful.\"\n\"Yes, I know,\" said God. \"But, please, don\u2019t tear anyone's arms and legs off. There would be so many little children on the floor of Hell.\"\nIt was six minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon.\nThere were 167 unfilled soul vacancies in hell.\nNothing continued to happen on the Moon, except that the man in the gas mask and the white suit finally threw away his tiny red book.", "0_13_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-six in the afternoon</span> of three days after Ford\u2019s disappearance. Lenore, rolling her suitcase toward the Bombardini Building in a light rain, reached into her pocket and felt the now broken circle on the watch chain. Her throat hurt a little. She hadn\u2019t felt like going to work at all that day, but since she also felt somehow sure that she would know, as soon as she got to the hospital and saw Keith, if there was anything wrong with him, she\u2019d told Mindy Metalman she would be in at lunchtime. She was supposed to start work at four. Mindy had laughed when Lenore said she had to start work at four.\nMindy Metalman was gone now, though. Gone to Aruba and tropical drinks without umbrellas, and pierced navels beneath white and mid-drift tanned bellies, and clear blue combers on lithographed emerald pates beneath a bright sky, and warm male flesh, and deck chairs and rhythmic creaking, and sheer wattage of tropical sun, and vibrations of smug and obscenely happy couples, and voracious tan, and luxury, and warm sand, and beach stories, and the tellings and re-tellings of old jokes and...", "1_19_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span> of the day that had begun in the red dawn. The place was Des Moines, Iowa. The occasion was a gang meeting in the deserted offices of a company that had once manufactured bicycles.\n\"So we'll shove off at eight o'clock sharp,\" said Huncke. Young Tim snorted. \"And a goof of fifty bucks apiece. What a waste.\"\n\"What did you expect?\" said Karp.\n\"I expected to make my bread sticking up gas stations, like we been doin'.\" He looked out through the dusty glass window at the lights of Des Moines fading into spring dusk. \"What a dump.\"\n\"I want to travel,\" said his girl.\n\"We travelin'.\"\n\"Not in a damned Hudson,\" she said. \"I want to go somewhere.\"\n\"You want to live high, don\u2019t you?\" asked Karp.\n\"As high as I can.\"\n\"Well, don't start now worrying about it,\" said Huncke. \"None of us will be doing that for a while.\"\nShe turned on him. \"What's that mean, don't start now worrying?\"\n\"It means what it means.\" He laughed at her. \"It's better that '...", "0_14_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nTensing himself, trying to summon all his strength, $FIRSTNAMEB2 was able to achieve the most minimal of movements. He did a push-up.\nThe effort of that exercise killed him temporarily.\nHis heart stopped, he folded up like a pair of abandoned trousers, and fell to the ground.\nWithout having to be told, $FIRSTNAMEF0 understood that $FIRSTNAMEB2 was asking her to bury him there in the rich, smelly loam of the rose garden. Since she was unable to comprehend his reasons for the request, she reasoned that he had gone temporarily insane when his heart stopped, and that his wish to be buried in the rose garden was the desire of a madman, to be contrary to what was expected of him.\nOn her knees, burying her son, she wept\u2014but not entirely for him. Almost as much she wept for herself, that she might be compelled to stay on in the big house alone.\nAnd for the first time she thought about her husband. Had she outlived him?\nFor at the rate of one second per second, those large dry bones at the bottom of the sea would not be visible down there for another thirty-four years, give or take a month or two.\nCHAPTER NINE...", "1_12_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty p.m.</span> on May twenty-seventh. The great mad party was over, having run, on and off, at least seventy-two hours, beginning with the juice party on Coconut Grove night. Now it was ending with this final rough, husky ride home\u2014home being any point within range of a good motorcycle. Our last stop had been to buy gasoline\u2014downtown Miami at a Chevron station on Flagler Street. There we filled our tank and found out the mileage; 4900 miles; the road speed indicator said we were making 48.\nWithout any idea at all of where the hell we might be heading, we rode north on I 95, then cut over on a side road and roared up to Hollywood. \u201cWhere the fuck are we?\u201d yelled young Remi from under his tent as we blasted by. We began to kid each other as soon as we were out from under the tent. \u201cHey! Now we're safe! No more party! Ain't no Mescan goin' have to suffer no more!\u201d We roared north on Hollywood beach. \u201cSing, damn you! Sing, goddamn you!\u201d yelled Caesar. We could hear him, but only as one hears a movie played with the sound down low...", "0_17_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-two in the afternoon</span>.\nThe cotton fields and the orange groves had an October chill to them.\nWe would buy the booze, I decided, and have a few quiet, peaceful drinks in the room.\n\"Suppose I stopped off and bought a pint?\" I said.\n\"We wouldn't need that much, but I'll go with you. Keep me out of trouble.\"\n\"Good. That would be swell, $FIRSTNAMEF3.\"\n\"Son of a bitch,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3.\nWe were sitting on the bed in Room 214, cold sober, looking out the window at the dull sunset over the cotton fields. We were in the Holiday Inn in Bin Laden, Texas. Room 214 smelled of fresh paint and beer. We had just showered, washed our hair, and dumped a can of beer from the icebox into the red plastic bucket under the sink.\n\"This is good beer,\" I said.\n\"That A&W is no good. It tastes like horse piss.\"\n\"That's the whole point of it. It's made from horse piss.\"\n\"Jesus Christ. Look at that sun go down. Just look at that sun.\"\n\"Yes. It's cold in here.\"\n\"Shut up. Let that damn sun...", "0_0_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-three a.m.</span> when Redwine, saying, \u201cYes, the very message in my mind to run into you, young Ransom,\u201d seized the arm of $FIRSTNAMEB2, who was androgynously pretty and silver-haired, and said with authority, \u201cSon, come help the Reverend and me with this luggage\u2014it\u2019s time we were taking a taxi for the ship.\u201d\nDamn it, thought $SURNAME2, he had been so close to the old bastard. They had, figuratively speaking, run into each other. They had in some sense sought each other\u2019s company out. And now, it would seem, nothing could come of it. For had not the end of the world intervened?\nBut it was not the end of the world. It was the beginning of Moon-bleach, that hard white detergent manufactured by the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent. And when $SURNAME2 finally left the pier, at two o\u2019clock in the morning, in the protective company of four young sailors wearing dog tags, he was sure of but three things. The first was that something very bad was about to happen to the Corvino. The second was that Redwine himself did not know what...", "2_21_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to ten o'clock in the evening</span>, now. The place was a rocking chair next to a suggestion of a bed in a bungalow at the Thurber Springs Infirmary. The night nurse was rearranging things so she would have an excuse to look away for a few moments.\n/g/\n\u201cYou wish to buy a watch, Dr. Katz?\u201d\n\u201cNo. Not yet.\u201d\n\u201cIs is wonderful timepieces you have on. Only two they make like these. Only watch company in the world makes like these.\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cYou like?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cThen maybe you wish to by?\u201d\n\u201cHave you the catalog, Vladimir?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n/h/\n\u201cStory of your life.\u201d\n\u201cStory of my life.\u201d\n\u201cThat gown doesn\u2019t look too comfortable. Should I be concerned?\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cWhose gown is it?\u201d\n.... \u201cThe thing is that I don\u2019t give a damn about this patient anymore.\u201d", "1_4_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four seventeen in the morning</span>. I just had time to check my bags at the airport and make certain my bus left at four thirtyfive. As I stood sucking one last bit of pleasure from a tiny Mexican brazier containing fifty billion termites\u2014good-bye, foul corrupter!\u2014I realized my travel companion was nowhere to be found. I ran outside, hoping she'd just honked and I missed the sound. No car. I went clear around the terminal lobby but couldn't find her.\nNo chance for a dangerous rendezvous at the airport\u2014no time, no car, no place to check luggage, no courtesy bus for impatient passengers, no nothing. My bags were stashed in a locker, checked through to New Orleans, where we were to meet the next day. And Theresa was not in the baggage room; she was not in the ladies lounge, the coffee shop, the newspaper stall, the pay toilet, the lost luggage department...but that's where I located her in the end. In the candy machine, playing a slot with a penny, still apparently hoping to collect a few more nature stories in change. I blew my whistle; she looked up, her face portraying something akin to real fear. \u201cWhere is the car?\u201d I whispered.", "0_6_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-five in the morning</span>. The night had gone. I watched the grimace of early morning on the patio-wall. The first cab of the day was honking faintly on the street. Breakfast was almost ready. Somewhere a woman cried out. The cries were muffled. The taxis were coming in already. Their drivers leaped out and ran around opening the doors of the cab-pool in front of the Hotel Montoya. I heard them shout at the dining-room door. I felt as if life always brought forth the same blows, always offered the same sad scenes, always gave the same tired judgments. I was afraid to move and wake $FIRSTNAMEB5. Let her sleep. She would be all right. She would never wake up. I wondered what the chambermaid would think when she came into the room after us.\nI got up and put on my coat and leaned out the window and called a waiter.\n\u201cHas the coffee come up?\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s right here, sir.\u201d\n\u201cSend up two cups.\u201d\n\u201cYes, sir.\u201d\nI went over to the bed. $FIRSTNAMEB5 was sharp and clear in the sleeplessness of pre-dawn. Her head was turned away. I wrapped...", "1_23_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven seventeen in the evening</span>, when $FIRSTNAMEB2, Remo, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 arrived at the dome of Stony Lodge. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was disguised as an old man; Remo was disguised as a businessman; $FIRSTNAMEB1 was disguised as a beautiful woman.\nThey met in a dark and windy place under a tree by the wall of the dome.\n\"Unscrew your face, Al,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nAl opened his purse, unscrewed his face, threw it on the ground.\n\"That\u2019s better,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. He got down on his knees, picked up the face, put it back on the screw-on neck. \"How\u2019s business, Al?\" he said through the face.\n\"Rotten, Pop,\" said the face. \"Everything\u2019s moving too fast. People don\u2019t care about getting old any more.\"\n\"Who\u2019s that with you?\" said the face.\n\"A friend,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. He winked through the face at the person he was wink ing at. This person winked back.\n\"Tell old Al how you like your new miss,\" said the face.\n\"She says hello, Pop,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"She wants to know...", "0_23_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-four in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME1 stood for a moment looking at the front of the great clock, marveling at it.\nThen he turned away and used a telephone booth to call $SURNAME0\u2019s estate. $SURNAME1 knew the number, having watched it being plugged in so many times.\nThe voice of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, Skip\u2019s mother, answered the telephone. \"Yes?\" she said.\n\"This is $SURNAME1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 over the telephone. He spoke from the pouch of his space suit. \"Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2014is this how you receive all your husbands in the future?\" He smiled into the mouthpiece of the telephone.\n\"Oh\u2014a phone call! How nice!\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. She leaned against an exterior wall of the mansion. The shutters were drawn, the front lawn was a smooth green blanket, and the lights in the mansion were soft and orange. There were no other souls about\u2014not so much as a landscaping feature. The door chimes had sounded only once. It was $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0\u2019s habit to ignore all door chimes.\n\"We have nothing important in common anymore,\" said $SURNAME0 over the telephone. \"Just...", "1_15_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. The big Cadillac was turning west. Remi was piloting us toward to cheap hotel where the first of our great American junkyard conferences was to be held. The man in the back seat was no longer Stan. He was the ticket-seller from Las Vegas. His name, according to his attorney, was Remi. And the name of the man in the front seat was Rollo. My name was Charlie. Charlie was doing all the talking in my half of the car, because Remi and Rollo were teaching each other card tricks. I could hear their sneakered feet rubbing back and forth on the rear shelf of the Cadillac as they shifted around in the back seat. I hung on to both front seats to keep from falling on my head.\n\"Let's see...now...does that strap of sod...now kick his hat ... nnnghnnn ... nnngghh\u2014don't kick the hat...look out, you're loosening the strap have to tighten it damn interfering sheriff hurrying him on ... one foot up in the stirrup ... ah, he'll make it, he's up ... charge ... yes, he's in! Thirty thousand ... attention ... attention ... the gambling commissioner is spreading a small rumor in...", "1_8_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight sixteen in the morning</span> on the island in the Tigris when a fresh breeze blew moist, wet air over the open fields of $SURNAME1\u2019s estate. There were a hundred men in the breeze\u2014construction workers, laborers, architects, bulldozer operators. By ten they would be stripped to their shorts, laboring profusely in the sun.\nTheir tools were already ringing and banging in the wind. Shovels, picks, winches, the beginnings of scaffoldings, the blocks that would support scaffoldings, the raw wood of new barracks. By noon the barracks would be done and the workers could eat.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s estate was really a city in itself. It included full facilities\u2014a supermarket, a hospital, uniforms. It needed provisioning only once a month\u2014and then only because it was big.\nThe impressive buildings were north of the estate proper. There were two research buildings, one administration building, one chapel, one requisitioned apartment block. These buildings, as anyone passing overhead could see, were faced with lead. And the lead-facings were not put on just any old way. They were true Gothic facings\u2014not merely imitations.\nThe walls of leaded windows rose sheer and incorporated lancet windows...", "2_23_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until midnight</span>.\nFrom outside the frame of the door came the metallic sound of a latch being unfastened, and another latch being fastened, and a ring of keys jingling, and other sounds.\nThe inside of Cattle-men\u2019s Hall was illuminated with the dullish light of industrial-glass lampshades hanging by chains from the gray ceiling. A thick tapestry hid the air conditioning. In the middle of the place like some baroque animal of the sea rested a great dark table, dotted with high-backed chairs. The table held polished tomes and thick three-ring binders and beige interleaved copies of proposals, in all stages of completion, produced in rented offices and clipboards full of yellow Post-its and white paper overlays. A huge map of Ohio dominated one wall. Tiny flags representing properties were everywhere. A stuffed alligator stared from a pedestal in a corner. Walgreen had a palaktion.\nThe time was five minutes until midnight.", "1_22_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten seventeen in the evening</span>. I told $FIRSTNAMEB1 to drop his drums where he was and skip across the border to some liquor store; he could buy wine and come back, but not to linger. As soon as he was away I called the Beverly Hills system again and asked for Stan Shaw. There was a snatch of dialogue on the phone and then Lucy\u2019s fraught, breathless voice flew out at me.\n\u201cOh, $FIRSTNAMEB6, it's you, thank God, I thought it was that horrible woman.\u201d\n\u201cWhat are you doing, sugar?\u201d\n\u201cNothing.\u201d A childish giggle. \u201cThat was a crazy, weird day. I hardly know what to say to you. What are you doing?\u201d\n\u201cI'm just sitting in my room, thinking about you and me.\u201d\n\u201cFar out. How's your friend?\u201d Rapid speech, disconnected. High twittering laughter. The demon drug.\n\u201cOh, he's just driving around, getting lost.\u201d\n\u201cGood for him.\u201d\n\u201cAnd yours?\u201d\n\u201cMine? Oh, he's fine. I just talked to him a few hours ago.\u201d A...", "1_8_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe building was 1129 McArthur Building. The floor was 49. This was the fifth floor of the Parthenon. The Parthenon was itself the north wing of a building to the west, the MacArthur Building, among whose tenants were the offices of Salo, F disconnect, Ltd.\u2014 ostensibly an employee-relations firm, actually a front for a staff of custodial and retrieval professionals employed by Martian Assassin Number 6, one L. Rencart, who often used the name Gort.\nThe time was now one minute to nine.\nA folded page from the Journal of the American Dental Association fell to the violet carpeting of the fifth floor of the Parthenon. In the offices of Salo, F disconnect, Ltd., a secretary was supposed to put the sheets of the Journal into a basket for discard at ten-thirty each morning.\nThe secretary was not here, however. The secretary had been gone for three and a half hours. Where the secretary had gone there was no way to tell, for the secretary was a computer, a NeXT Magellanclassic with a 78-megahertz processor, 4 gigabytes of random access memory, and a two-hundred-and-twenty-megabyte storage unit...", "1_16_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-four in the afternoon</span>. I had just gone on duty. My partner was a mean-looking, older cop named George. Big as he was with his four chins, none of us wanted to tangle with him. He was really weary of life and mostly slept in his cubicle.\nHe was a buddy of mine. He was the one who gave me the works on the Navy. We had worked together four years without once having an argument or a serious talk.\n\"Watch this station while I go get a cup of coffee,\" I said.\n\"All right,\" he said.\n\"Don't let anybody take my chair away.\"\n\"I won't,\" he said.\nI went downstairs and out through the doors. I walked down the sidewalk and jiggled the ice in my cup at the soda fountain. Some man I didn't know was talking about boxing.\n\"Easy all the way, Billy,\" he said. \"You got him almost knocked out. All you got to do is send that left up under his heart.\"\n\"I know,\" I said. I put my ten cents in the coffee pot slot and got my coffee.\n\"Hello, officer,\" said the man.\n\"Hello,\" I said. \"Why don't you...", "2_8_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-two in the morning</span>.\nJust as the planetary orbits of Mercury and Venus are infinitesimally close to a grand conjunction, the orbits of Mars, Earth and Uranus will become infinitesimally close again in A.D. 2540. The conjunction in A.D. 2540 will be as close as the conjunction in 356 B.C., when Milton Angelucci and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 were born.\n\"Just what question did you ask me?\" said the woman.\n\"I asked if you pitied me,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Pity lies nowhere in my questionnaire,\" said the woman.\n\"I notice that you are reading a copy of it now,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes,\" said the woman.\n\"That is the same questionnaire I gave you twenty-seven years ago on Titan,\" said $SURNAME0. \"You still carry it with you on all your journeys.\"\n\"Yes,\" said the woman.\n\"It must be a great comfort to you,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"There is nowhere I can go and take it off, store it away, and put on another skin,\" said the woman. \"It would be a terrible thing if I lost it.\"", "2_1_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until two o'clock in the morning</span> now. All over the drowned world, even in Gibraltar and over the sea on a calm day, people were going up to bed as though it were any night.\nCommon to all these worldwide sentries was a tendency to feel good, at that dark, quiet time, about their particular jobs. It was dark and quiet for those people, too.\nAll over the world, people cleaned their fingernails with the stalks of dandelions, and threw the stalks away.\nTen thousand cleaning women scattered ten thousand dandelion stalks in useful wastebaskets or underfoot, or dropped them into their handbags.\nTen thousand milkmen poured newspaper shreds into ashcans or used them to start fires.\nTen thousand watch repairmen put old toothpicks into jars.\nTen thousand bartenders poured used cocktail olives into the vat.\nTen thousand milk deliverymen rounded the corner in the cool of the early morning.\nThey turned on their electric lights for a moment, to make sure the customers hadn\u2019t left a cent or at best a nickel under a worth-ten-cents glass.", "1_19_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span>; I could see the hands of my clock move. I felt as though I had a curse on me. There were two orchids on my table; they were brought to me that morning. I did not like to touch them; they were like beautiful women who have given themselves to me and whom, at the last moment, I have deserted. Perhaps it was the fear of Hell; great doctor, but no priest, I fussed with death. The clock ticked. The leaves of the orchids turned greener in the night air-three hundred thousand spores a second and a million a minute and a billion an hour, seeds of magic and death. At last I signed the report of my blood test, without a word.\nCHAPTER TWENTY-EIGHT\n\u201cHold on a minute, Schmuck,\u201d said the inspector from the blacklist, \u201cthe $FIRSTNAMEB3 wants to speak to you.\u201d It was eight o'clock in the morning. \u201cHello, Schmuck. You gave us quite a nervous morning, last day.\u201d\n\u201cWhat happened?\u201d My voice was gone, and my kneecaps were jumping around. \u201cWhy, the old fool reports he didn't take...", "0_3_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span> when we got back to the Bull Shot. The bartender was stoned, but he gave us our room-rent check without question. I slapped down a twenty d ollar bill and he gave me a key to Room 327. \u201cWait for me,\u201d I told Lucy as I rushed into th e bathroom with the washbasin. I filled it with water, then chopped up more ice from the bar ice\u2014tray and dropped it in...adjusting the Zim\u2014bumbler for maximum cube\u2014crush.\nI rushed back out to Lucy and took her by the hand. \u201cCome into the toilet,\u201d I said. \u201cI have a surprise for you.\u201d\n\u201cOh, no,\u201d she said. \u201cIt better not be that goddamn ice water again\u2014like last night.\"\n\u201cThis is something much better,\u201d I said..\n She followed me into the toilet. \u201cWhat is it?\u201d she asked.\n\u201cA new valve design for these miniature portable washbasins,\u201d I told her. \u201cWatch!\"\nAnd I turned on the cold water full force\u2014then quickly twisted the Zimm\u2014bumbler to fine...", "1_21_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>, Earth time, when the Salo was passing through the orbit of Jupiter. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 had just made $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 CUmo laugh uproariously.\n\"At ten o\u2019clock\u2014which is to say in two minutes, Mr. $SURNAME1\u2014you will receive the most miraculous telephone call of your life,\" said $SURNAME0. \"A call that will transform your life in the most agreeable ways.\"\n\"You mean my wife\u2019s coming on the phone?\" said $SURNAME1 ingratiatingly.\n\"No, no,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Nothing to do with wives. The person who calls you will be a male person, with a voice that will make you think of the American Museum of Natural History, New York City. When he speaks to you, he will say these words only: \u2019Rise up, Lazarus.\u2019 That\u2019s all\u2014just those words.\n\"A moment later, there\u2019ll be a knock on your door, and when you open it there\u2019ll be forty-eight gorgeous young women, who will rush into your arms with all the eagerness of Solomon\u2019s lily-maids.\n\"You, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said Rumfo...", "0_10_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-eight in the morning</span>, according to the dashboard clock, when Elaine arrived at my apartment in Cindy\u2019s Power Wagon. She was wearing one of my western shirts and had her hair tied back with a colored bandanna. She said she\u2019d managed to run her Dodge into a cactus on the way into town, but so what? She strolled into the kitchen, looking around at the dirty dishes, piled cots, twisted caulk tubes, film spools, greasy paper bags and Playboy magazines. She opened the refrigerator door, looked at the pint of Chivas Regal and the half-eaten box of Cheerios. \u201cWait till I tell my sister about this place,\u201d she said.\nWhile I showered and dressed, she rousted around the piles of scrap paper, careful not to disturb anything. Finally she came up with a corner of a sheet where I\u2019d written something or other. She smoothed it out on the desk top.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s an Ergot Apothecary?\u201d she asked.\n\u201cGod only knows,\u201d I said, shrugging into an elaborate silk paisley jacket that was covered with big red poppies. \u201cWhere...", "2_6_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>. \u201cListen,\u201d Beaconsfield's voice called from next door. \u201cYour front door is open. I think you had better all come over here.\u201d Inverarity went. \u201cIf the big one hits now, you'll be safer in the boat.\u201d He was right. They all scampered over, $FIRSTNAMEF2 trailing, and watched the still- unexplained tremor. Each one felt it differently, according to how he thought of the earth, his wits, the cojones of his soul and where they were located. For Inverarity, it was as if all Southern California were a thinly fitting mask of Written adhesive, a transparent tape that held together, tenuously, cracks only imaginarily healed. One sharp pull, from the San Andreas, and it would all roll off the way dead skin does, in", "0_16_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-two in the afternoon</span>. The place was the dismal suburbs of Sacramento, California. I knew that, because we had just passed a sign that said SACRAMENTO 132. Now we were passing another sign, this one on the side of a particularly grim hamburger stand next to a particularly hideous used-car lot t_d the corner of Arcade Boulevard: ARCADE BLVD. 12. The car's radio was tuned to one of those soft-rock stations that play lots of tunes with violins and a lot of lyrics about moonlight and love that's always there and so on; the singer's voice was styled along the lines of the $FIRSTNAMEF1 Gees\u2014high and quavering. I turned it off. \u201cMan, this is heavy, this scenery,\u201d said Bruce, looking around. \u201cI don't want to get hopeless, but I think we're going to end up doing two articles with titles like \u2018America's Sad Tomorrows\u2019\u2014two big issues full of nothing but used cars and deinstitutionalized crazy people.\u201d I nodded. I had been thinking the same thing. Suddenly the whole thing seemed idiotic. What were we doing out here in the middle of this godforsaken cabbage field in somebody's Ford? We...", "2_0_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after midnight</span>. Still no sign of anything except that some living soul in room 210 was watching television. The programs had changed. Now it was \u201cHave Gun\u2014Will Travel.\u201d\nThe time was sixteen minutes after midnight. Suddenly it came to me\u2014I had my answer! Forget Vegas. Forget the Mint. Very simple: I had to hide out in the desert, and find Lonnie. of course. No questions, no conniption fits or legal hassles. He was my natural brother, blood brother; nobody could understand/accept/forgive him but me.\nWe would return to Vegas together, to the Flamingo, and then everything would happen very fast. Just like in the song. I could visualize it all:\n...at the Billion Dollar Topless\nWe\u2019ll share the same suite, it\u2019s a sin, a sin...", "0_13_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was a grove of maples and yellow pines on the shore of Lakeanni in the wilderness of northern Wisconsin. No person was in sight, but there were signs that somebody or a number of somebodies had been there very lately\u2014fresh cigarette butts disguised as tumbleweed, candy wrappers on the verdant floor, footprints and tire-treads on the loamy, pine-needle-strewn path that led from the faintly grooved dirt road that wound through the forest.\nShe slapped the water gently, sending the shorebirds into flight and the sandpipers scurrying. Alone at last, she thought as she lay back on the air mattress, having fallen out of her beach robe.\nThank God nobody\u2019s around, she thought.\nShe thought this mostly because of the color of her skin. She was very white. Her hair was blonde. Her eyes were gray, almost metallic. As a child, she had seen only tiny reflections of this color-of-skin in a few mirrors and the bottoms of a few bottles, and had believed for a long time that the color was only real when viewed in such tiny spaces.\nA very small facial tick, one that happened about once a...", "1_16_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the place was an abandoned factory district in the southwestern section of Washington, D.C. The half-mile square area was given over to rubble, junked machinery, rusted scaffolding, acres of staked yellow \u201cCaution\u201d tape, seagull shit and wino piss.\nThe only man-made structure within this blighted area was an eyelike, 18-story structure that seemed to emerge from a rockpile in the center of the area. This structure was 666 Leatherneck Street, a government owned, designed and occupied office building.\nOn the 18th floor of 666 Leatherneck Street was the office of C. M. Pierce, the Special Assistant to the Regional Director of the Mintaka Trinational Strategic Planning Campaign.\nTo those who knew nothing at all about the Mintaka Trinational Strategic Planning Campaign, the duties of a Special Assistant to the Director might seem vague and unfathomable. But in actual fact the duties of a Pierce were not only rather specific, they were also highly useful, useful to something like forty-three people in Washington alone.\nThe job was to filter out of the mass of paper in the director's in-basket all items that were completely useless and send them back immediately to...", "0_5_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-seven in the morning</span> but already bright. I prowled around his house, nervous, looking to see what I could find. To see myself in a mirror. I knocked over two or three. The bathroom was clean, sadly. The garbage can wasn\u2019t even inside the house, it was out back, in a bin there, the lid was on and fastened down with a loop of black wire. The rubber plant was alone in one corner of the living room, the air was stale. I tried to find more pictures, but there weren\u2019t any, as far as I could see; and no other personal objects, except in the bedroom, one pen and one book, his and hers separate. But then maybe I wasn\u2019t looking properly, and anyway I couldn\u2019t stay there much longer, a neighbour might call the police, or David himself might come back, he might have a key, I didn\u2019t know. I wanted something to take, a memento, a souvenir, something that would be immortal; but there was nothing, my hands passed through everything, walls, furniture, pictures, crashing and banging, without leaving a mark.\nThere was one thing and one thing only I could give him that he would understand:...", "1_23_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-nine in the evening</span> when the last soldier had run through $SURNAME1\u2019s field of vision. He opened his eyes, and shut them again. He opened them once more, closed them once more. He opened his eyes once more, closed them again. The blinking went on, but almost imperceptibly. $SURNAME1, eyes shut, was succumbing to calm.\nLittle by little, he relaxed, then completely relaxed. He lay all slack in the comfortable chair. He let everything go slack.\nAt ten minutes before midnight, Salo crawled from beneath the chair, to which he had been fastened. He carried all of $SURNAME1\u2019s silverware\u2014napkins, table knives, table forks, and table spoons. He carried as well $SURNAME1\u2019s wallet and watch. He crossed to the window and picked up the papers that $SURNAME1 had written, and the napkin on which $SURNAME1 had drawn the plans for the monument.\nHe turned to look once more at the snoozing man.\nWho could blame a person like that for being a little weird? wondered Salo. \"Who would presume to blame him? he asked out loud.\nDown on the ground he found a large stone, which he placed on the plans, and the napkin, and...", "2_3_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAnd this was not just any day, but Monday, October 29, 1953. Halloween.\nAt one minute till four o'clock in the morning in a Greenwich Village pad filled with marijuana smoke and excited collegiate chatter, a man in his early twenties let out a yelp of disbelief and snapped out of the delirium tremens of the most fascinating book he had ever read. The book was Arguments Concerning Utility. The yelped exclamation was not one of literary appreciation, but one of astonishment. A crazy guy in a costume had just come pounding on the door. \"Aqua Regia, Master Scrabble, pantaloons, cape, swordstick, smackety-smack!\" he yelled. \"Mucho excitable type!\"\nThe kid, an anthropology undergrad at NYU, opened the door and let the process server in.\nThe process server stood in the middle of the room, beaming all around. He seemed to know who everyone was. \"Good evening, my lady, my gentleman, my dear fellow, how nice to see you all.\" He kissed the air on either side of a girl's neck and almost danced two funky steps.", "0_22_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-three in the evening</span>. The bartender seemed to have just come on duty. He couldn\u2019t have been there when the Bulero party came in, or I would have remembered him too. He was a sad-looking middle-aged man. The only thing outstanding about him was a certain sad, bewildered sensitivity of face and bearing.\nThe passing crowd looked down on the dull red and blue lights of the bowling alley and saw the big oval sign stamped with the name of the bar: The Old Fenster. They read it slowly, their lips moving, their faces puzzled at the sight of this sign over a bowling alley in northern California: The Old Fenster. Over the bar was an arch with a few arcade machines to one side\u2014a shooting gallery, a pinball machine, a little electronic article called an Actor\u2019s Nightmare by Radio Shack, which allowed the customer to record his own voice on tape, mix it with standard psychotic ravings, and play it back for the listener. The machine\u2019s standard psychotic ravings had been recorded by W. C. Fields.\nMoney still clinked into the machines from the few people remaining in the bar. The crowd had gone downstairs to watch the terminals. The man sitting next to me was getting drunk by...", "1_20_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nMike, the bouncer at the Metronome, was sweeping the steps outside with an ox-hair broom.\n\"Say,\" he said to me, \"you be sure and show these people your identity card next time, you savvy?\"\n\"Sure,\" I said.\n\"What the hell are you doing with this mob?\" he asked.\n\"Oh, they\u2019re all right,\" I said. \"Newspaper men.\"\n\"What newspaper?\"\n\"How the hell should I know?\"\nHe shrugged. \"Ah, hell!\" he said. \"You should never come here alone.\"\n\"I know,\" I said.\nBill and the others were sitting inside at the bar.\n\"Here\u2019s your whiskey you ordered,\" Mike said. \"Thirty-five cents.\"\nI gave him a quarter.\n\"You don\u2019t owe me anything,\" he said. \"It\u2019s on the house.\"\n\"Thanks,\" I said.\nHe watched me threading my way through the tables.\n\"Have a good time,\" he called.\n\"So long,\" I said.\nI turned to see who called me. It was Bill.\n\"Come on over,\" he said.", "1_7_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-six in the morning</span>. I felt hungover, even though I hadn't touched a drop the night before. We were riding down through the flat farm country just east of L.A. Already the smog meter on the Cadillac's dashboard was pegged all the way to the right. How much worse can it get? I wondered.\nI smiled at the two kids in the back seat as they drowsed in the foul air. \u201cYou boys folks down here in the Southland workin' on the harvest?\u201d They were wearing dirty overalls and sweating in spite of the air\u2014conditioning. They just stared bleakly back at me. \u201cGood,\u201d I said. \u201cThat's what I'd have done, if I had me a wife and family. You can't beat the California grape for cash.\u201d They seemed suspicious of this gibberish, but too sleepy to put their skepticism into words. Goodo, I thought. I'm beginning to sound like a native. I've only been here seventy-two hours, but I'm picking up a heavy twang...bluffing like I've had an education at Berkeley and been around the block a few times. I started to make small talk.\nThey seemed on the...", "1_9_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-two in the morning</span>. I actually had nothing to do for ten whole hours. I wondered whether I should try to bother this poor fool in Mint Shark & Partners one more time with coments and questions. He had never even seen me, much less spoken to me, and this was the closest I\u2019d ever gotten to actually talking to anyone who worked in an office building. I studied him. He was deeply engrossed in a book. Licking his thumb, he was slowly skimming through the last seven pages.\nI took the elevator. The doors closed, the motor began to whine, the floor numhers swam dizzily upward, red...blue...red...blue... Passing nine, I lighted a cigarette, hoping for some wisp of smoke to leak between the elevator doors, to drift out and infiltrate the country clubcar, to curl up un healthily around the ankles of the lonesome man in three-part Khaki...\nWhat if he actually did exist? What if the whole of his life was what I was seeing nowf? What if he had some beautiful, witty, wonderful woman out there in some suburb, with her own house, her own money, her own children, and what if he didn\u2019t dare call her...", "2_1_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one seventeen in the morning</span>, Earth Prevailer time. That was seven seventeen on Mars, because Mars whirled on beneath the fire banners of the greater sun.\nAt seven seventeen on Mars, the Martian infantry, artillery, and airforce squads were closing in on Phoebe, the attack on Phoebe, the attack on Phoebe.\nOne thousand feet below $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2, the flat chapel roof of a one-story structure thrust out from the main complex of buildings in the town of $SURNAME0. The building under the roof was known as the Mausoleum.\nIt was in the Mausoleum that the remains of the one who had told the people of Mars about the miracle of clocks being able to sing gloriously were kept. His name had been Churchill, $FIRSTNAMEM1 Nursed. He had told the people of Mars about the miracle when the Martian armies closed in on Chicago. And now the blinds and shutters of the Mausoleum were closed against the clean light of the Martian dawn. The feet that scuffed the gravel pathway to the Mausoleum were heavy, were clumping, were military.", "1_2_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two nineteen in the morning</span>, though neither of the astronauts was aware of the exact time. It was all the same anyway, with night and the storm and leaves blowing around them. Only the priorities had been jumbled in their minds. Chronological order had been overthrown. Little important things were slipping back into unimportance and big things like time and place and sequence of events had been reduced to a glimmering matter of faith.\nAt two nineteen in the morning, the Apostle Trampolin still believed that he would be saved in a space ship by Jesus Christ after the great ordeal was over. A message had come to him on his portable radio. The voice had been Jesus\u2019.\n\"There is a little white spot in your heart,\" Jesus had said. \"It is righteous. It will sail out on beams of glory through the clouds and into the arms of Jesus. Have faith and be brave. The storm will be over in an hour and a half.\"\nThat was what Trampolin had been preaching to his followers when Redwine and $FIRSTNAMEB0 arrived with the stone slab from the base of the Statue of Liberty. And that was what he was preaching when $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Redwine left again for the Statue of Liberty.\nHe was still preaching when $FIRSTNAMEB0 and...", "2_14_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME0 looked at his wristwatch, set his hands accurately to the earthling time. \"Do you know,\" he said chattily, \"many members of the Martian families come to me before they get the engraved invitation? I tell them then what their names will be, exactly as they appear on the invitation.\n\"They disbelieve, of course,\" said $SURNAME0. He smiled up at the skylight, remembering a time when the news he had given a disbeliever had brought tears to the disbeliever\u2019s eyes. \"And yet,\" said $SURNAME0, \"every one of those people have kept the names I gave them.\"\n\"What was my name?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"All in good time,\" said $SURNAME0. \"You are supposed to be introduced by a person named $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2014a dog named $FIRSTNAMEB4. For reasons I shan\u2019t go into now, the dog is running late.\" He shrugged. \"The dog represents yet another avenue of voluntary come-uppance.\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB4?\" said $SURNAME1. He had no dog. And, as far as he knew, no one he knew had a dog\u2014or ever had had a dog.", "0_3_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three o'clock in the morning</span> when we got back to Tim Gray's house, after the Fat City revelry. My aunt was pacing back and forth on the porch when I got there. She was gray with fatigue and worry. She only loomed. She was so weary she could only loom at me and point.\n\u201cYou went out with Stan Well. For four hours -- and he comes home damn near five o'clock in the morning.\u201d\n\u201cIt's not his fault.\u201d\n\u201cIs you the captain of the ship? Ain't you the man in charge of that boy?\u201d\nI had no strength to talk back. I staggered on the porch.\n\u201cAin't you got no manhood?\u201d\nI took off my uniform and went to bed. The last thing I heard before sleep came was my aunt's voice in the kitchen:\n\u201cFour of my own children is dead and buried, and I don't want to see no other go down to hell.\u201d\nIn the morning I feared the worst, but nothing happened. The next night the whole gang went out again and had a great time. My aunt couldn't stop me. I was eighteen years old. And I was...", "1_1_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-eight in the morning</span>. The dangerous shadow figures were beginning to move illo lenly along the Strip, a young giant in a studded jacket shouting, \u201cHave! Some! Fun!\u201d at me; the rock worid was just opening up for another twenty four hours of night. I looked at my glass and saw that it was almost empty. Slowly I turned it over; the bartender knew what that meant. He hurried over with the bottle. \u201cWhat\u2019s the matter?\u201d I demanded. \u201cCan\u2019t you keep up with me?\u201d He smiled, handed me a paper napkin. \u201cBe cool,\u201d he said. \u201cYou can\u2019t flunk out on us now.\u201d He was ready for anything. I could have killed him. \u201cFlunk out?\u201d I wanted to tell him what I thought about the Americanist pigs of writers: the doomed few who had gone before me\u2014the Mailers and Hemingways, beat up, lost on bar stools like me all over the world, alone looking for a peace that they were too twisted to understand; but nothing came out, only a weak smile, an attempt to swallow the jackknife I felt in my gul...", "1_21_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-one in the evening</span>.\n\"Wait'll I get that son of a bitch on the horn,\" said field personnel manager Metz, scowling as he looked over $SURNAME1\u2019s credentials. \"He claimed he was gonna be here, and now he isn\u2019t\u2014Whoa. What\u2019s become of his face?\"\n\"Just a moment,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nHe had expected that his hundred-dollar bill would buy nothing more than a few interesting moments with a man named Metz.\n\"Yes?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Oh\u2014yeah,\" said Metz. \"That\u2019s right, you\u2019re going out to him right away. I\u2019m keeping you.\"\n\"But\u2014\" said $SURNAME1. \"I should be there before he starts drinking again.\"\n\"He\u2019s not drinking out there right now?\" said Metz.\n\"I think he may be fasting,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"You mean he\u2019s so drunk he isn\u2019t drinking?\" said Metz.\n\"I think he\u2019s been drinking pretty heavily,\" said $SURNAME1, \"but maybe he\u2019s over it now.\"\nWhat $SURNAME1 had just heard was a rumor that whiskey shortened life in proportion to the...", "0_23_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven nineteen in the evening</span>. The scene was the Mint Monkey room of the joint area not far from the foot of the Las Vegas Nuclear Generating Station cooling tower. The sounds were the fast, sure sounds of rolling dice\u2014zip-clat. Zip-clat. Zip-clat. And the sounds of small\u2014change jingling in counters\u2014jingle-jingle. Jingle-jingle. Jingle-jingle. Zip-clat. Zip-clat. And the sounds of a well-built, blond-haired, snub\u2014nosed young woman trying to concentrate on the meaning of a \u201cnegative rotation curve\u201d in her physics text while her brain indicated, by insisting on recalling itself to itself, the fact that twenty feet away sat a young man who lunched with this woman, this same woman\u2014a fact which the young man had recently told the woman had made straight for him the way a magnet straight for iron, and had now hung itself up on him forever; and so the sound of his breathing, and the sound of his feet moving whenever he cleared his throat or shifted in the chair, these sounds the woman heard, plus the sound of his ever so soft slurring voice whenever he spoke to others in the room, and...", "1_22_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-six in the evening</span> of a good summer in Malakal.\nThis boldness of chronology will puzzle you. It tripped me up.\nI was feverish, shaking, my heart like a stone. I staggered from one state to another as would a pilgrim on his meaningless way from Arson and Damnation to Holy Eve Eruptions and Redemption.\nAt two in the morning, I sobered up with a hideous crash, and for a trick I got all hung up reverse parallel parking a station wagon into a space so narrow that I sheared off two sideview mirrors, crushing the doors and fenders when I tried to get out. The car had to be left in the street, worthless, a freak. What had been a lovely, fashionable hellhole of a restaurant was now a madhouse. Clean bodies everywhere, blonde and gorgeous. I hung around getting into conversations. They were all talking in jargon. Someone put a record on, and Frankie Carle played \u201cHarbor Lights.\u201d I was out on the Strip again and trying to decide what to do. What an awful day it had been! The radio announced that President Kennedy had been shot in Dallas. I rushed over to the radio and turned up the volume full. You feeling sad about...", "2_12_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nA ship of the Martian Navy docked lightly as a factin cabin in the skin of the ship containing $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the other rejects. The other rejects, who couldn\u2019t imagine what was going on, were progressively reminded that something was going on from the outside by the swishing sound the ship\u2019s skin began to make as it bulged in and out.\nThe skin began to dance rhythmically and visibly.\nThen the air in the cabin began to clear, thanks to an Earthling invention stolen for the purpose by the Martians. The windows of the ship turned from opaque to transparent.\nIt was a lovely sight. There was Titan rising from the steely plain of the biggest crater in the Moon.\nThere was the Mangy Moon, racing with Earth around the Sun.\nThere, among a million other jewels, was Camp Reveille at the foot of a forbidding wall of black cliffs.\nThere was the town of Woody Creek, in the Valley of the Moon, with herdsman\u2019s tents on the rolling plain.\nThere was a road relay station that stood at one end of the ether-drift runway.\nOne by one, the windows around the ship came to life like stage scenery.", "1_7_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-six in the morning</span> when she let me out at my hotel. We were by the comer. \u201cWhat do you want to do?\u201d she asked. I shrugged. \u201cNothing. I've got to sleep.\u201d\n\u201cWould you like to go to my room?\u201d\n\u201cBad idea. I might scuff you bloody or something.\u201d She nodded. \u201cI'm beginning to realize what trouble you're in. You need sleep.\u201d She hesitated. \u201cBut it is a long drive back.\u201d\n\u201cTake you to your part y.\u201d\n\u201cCan you make it?\u201d\n\u201cI dunno...let's try.\u201d We got out of the car and walked up to my room. I didn't have anything with me and I couldn't ask her in, so I said just before we reached the door, \u201cWell, thanks. Take care of my jacket and shirt in the trunk...you know. And if you get back OK bring my writing stuff in tomorrow.\u201d\n\u201cI'm worried about you.\u201d\n\u201cNothing to be worried about. Just take care of my things. I guess I'll see you in about five...", "1_16_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-seven in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME0 and his dog were eighty-four thousand, three hundred and thirty-one miles from Earth.\nA chrono-synclastic infundibulated person has neither past nor future.\nNeither has he feelings about the past or feelings about the future.\nInevitably, a person so afflicted finds that his feelings have been drawn inside, that he lives in a knot or clump or nub of feeling at the hub of the concavity of his being.\nThis nub is all that he knows of himself.\nThe nub is himself.\nThe nub learns to envy people who have outside, exter nalized feelings\u2014people who can cry, or sigh to the stars, or stab themselves with grief, or bleed brilliantly whenever emotions run high.\nThere are three universes in the cosmos, and only three. The first universe is the universe of things that can be expressed in terms of numbers, or in terms of the fundamentals of geometry, or in terms of the relationships between energy and matter. This is, of course, the universe that can be understood by the human mind. This is the universe that the human mind is uniquely adapted, by the phenomenon of speech, to describe to other human minds.", "2_11_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-four in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\nThe airship that had threatened the security of the silence and privacy of the estate all night long sailed off. It sailed east, to harass some other secluded place.\n$SURNAME0 had caught what the stock market guides call a hugely promising break in the weather.\nSurface Street, which cut a gentle swathe on the north side of $FIRSTNAMEM1\u2019s estate, was bathed in a mellow winter sunshine, and empty of all traffic but one Army transport. The transport, a horsecar with crude wooden wheels, was drawn by a single somnolent mare.\nThe street sign at the head of the short street read HELL\u2019S KITCHEN.\nA carnival man in a derby hat and a work-stained rear overall was driving the horsecar. He was the only living thing in $SURNAME0\u2019s world at the moment.\n\"Yawp?\" said the carnival man genially.\nThe party came up to the head of the spiral staircase that led down into $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s hidden estate.", "1_4_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>. So far nothing had gone wrong, and I was resigned to any adverse criticisms the late-morning crowd might offer. I felt reasonably comforted that the Mint was back in great shape again. It had served an important purpose for about two months, then drifted about for another two months before finally petering out altogether.\nI wondered where Dion and his troop were now...in some kind of park somewhere in downtown L.A., among some flowers and trees of a kind they could never touch or feel on the street. My attorney and Terry the Tramp were back at my house; they'd be along in a few hours, when the dawn fog was thick in the canyon. My attorney thought we might as well leave the Cadillac in downtown L.A., so we'd be sure not to have trouble on the way to the polo match. I thought he was right, so I drove the big white shark down to the street and left it at a metered space with the keys in it and all the windows rolled down\u2014but, first, I slipped the DO NOT DISARM sign under the wiper on the driver's side. I had already seen two professional car thieves at work in downtown L.A., and I didn't want that big shark caught in...", "0_18_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-nine in the evening</span> of Tuesday, November 11, 1962. Alvin Fielder was eight miles above the surface of the earth. For a fact, thought his mother, he will be the death of me.\nEvery summer for six years, Alvin had taken space travel for the eighth, ninth, tenth, eleventh, and twelfth grades. His mother had had it pointed out to her often enough that there was no sense in taking him up only once a year. \"Besides costing us the earth,\" she had said to the representative of the Space Travel Bureau the first year, \"it might not be good for the boy.\"\n\"Good for the boy?\" had said the representative. \"It will make a man of him.\"\n\"A man of him?\" had said Mrs. Fielder to her husband Roland that night. \"A man of him? He\u2019s helpless enough as it is.\"\nRoland, without turning his head, had said, \"What makes you say the boy\u2019s helpless, Lucille?\"\n\"What do you suppose?\" she said. \"He\u2019s just naturally helpless, see. He\u2019s never learned to do anything for himself.\"\n\"Oh?\" said Roland.\n\"How could he, when we never let...", "0_9_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine eighteen in the morning</span>.\n\"Oh, my Gawd!\" said the Federal Narcotics Commissioner. \"Not again! Can\u2019t we ever switch the program?\" He shook his pained, prematurely aged head. \"Why do they always have to run the same old crap? Why not Westerns, or comedies?\" He poured another drink. \"Or nudies,\" he said. \"I\u2019d even settle for nudies. Hell, I\u2019ll watch anything. I don\u2019t care what it is.\" He shuddered. \"Only don\u2019t make me watch that!\"\nHis wife, sitting next to him, watched the program very closely. Her whole body writhed as she watched, and her eyes bulged and rayed from their restless orbits.\n\"What\u2019s it about\u2014this crap?\" the Federal Narcotics Commissioner asked her.\n\"It\u2019s about boats!\" she squealed, without taking her eyes from the set. \"They\u2019ve all got boats!\" She stared transfixed at an exclamation-point of light that darted across the screen, leaving a trail of scintillating sparks in its wake. \"Two boats\u2014two men\u2014a lotta speed!\" she cried ec...", "2_14_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. The temperature was 112 degrees.\nAboard the ship there were nine men who didn\u2019t know they were insane. They were the psychiatric technicians who had volunteered to man the ship that afternoon. The condition of the technicians hadn\u2019t been perfect before the flight began, and the flight had made it disastrous.\nThere was one woman aboard the ship, too. There were two women among the psychiatric technicians\u2014one in the group at the front of the ship, one in the group at the rear. The woman in the group at the rear was much more insane than the men.\nThe woman who had come aboard with the recruits was in a far worse state than the male technician who had come aboard with the recruits. She didn\u2019t even know where or why she was. Her identity wasn\u2019t so much lost as drifting.\nThere was nothing the men could do but groan and moan, and watch the air force groan and moan.\nSome decided they wished they had gone out into space with their units.", "2_11_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-one in the morning</span>. Beeber groaned.\n\"Tell those other two either to get going, or to keep on working right up to noon,\" said Beeber. \"I\u2019ll be right down.\"\nThe three cleaning women looked at Beeber as if they were surprised to find him looking so prosperous. One spat on her scrubbing rag, raised one eyebrow. \"Yes, sir,\" she said.\nBeeber closed the door. He reached into the shower and turned on the cold water. It came out icy.\nBeeber took off his rumpled pajamas. He got into the icy shower, punching the soap. He scrubbed vigorously for a second or two, making a mess of suds, then stood under the icy trickle.\nHe looked at himself in the foggy mirror. He put his head under the ice water for a second.\nEleven twenty-five. Beeber got out of the shower, toweled himself off. He put his pajamas back on. At eleven twenty-six, he was back on his yellow couch. He had the morning papers in one hand through his pajamas. \"Come to daddy, my little piss ant,\" he murmured.", "2_8_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was nowhere. The show was still being seen on television screens and windows.\n$SURNAME0 stood within a foot of $SURNAME1. His head almost touched the top of the atmosphere. He smelled sweetly of the lilac sachet on his hat. The sun was a platinum disk of blinding, liquid fire in the sky behind him.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM1\u2014\" said $SURNAME1, \"I don\u2019t have time for understanding. I don\u2019t have time for philosophy. Just tell me what this is all about.\"\n\"I\u2019ll be glad to, Buddy,\" said $SURNAME0, \"but please don\u2019t call me $FIRSTNAMEM1\u2014call me Skip. That\u2019s my name here.\"\n\"What does this all mean, Skip\u2014what you\u2019ve done?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Only what I\u2019ve been saying over and over again,\" said $SURNAME0. He beamed. \"God is a comedian, playing to an audience too afraid to laugh.\"\n\"What do you want of me?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I want you to laugh,\" said $SURNAME0.", "2_14_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe time was a terrible time\u2014because it was the time of the first atomic attack on a populated area in world history.\nA quarter million human beings proudly considered themselves immune to the horrors of warfare.\nThey weren\u2019t.\nBeing saturated with excitement over their own good taste, they roundly condemned the rest of the world for its bad taste.\nThey were the fashionable people of Newport, Rhode Island, U.S.A.\nThey were the first people in history ever to be vaporized.\nConstantine Chiclitz issued instructions to his men by means of a clicking code that had been named \"Department of Circumventing Sales Taxes Involving Jewelry.\"\nHis men listened avidly to the crisp clicks, wondering how in hell the Romans had ever conquered the world.\nIt seemed a shame to the men to conk the Romans\u2014to put them to sleep forever.\nHelvetius, the great French philosopher of the eighteenth century, wanted desperately to be with the great French philosopher Descartes in heaven. That was how sincerely he had once believed in his philosophy.", "1_7_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-seven in the morning</span> and continued fine. The ship was expected to dock in New York at eleven-ten in the morning of the next day, which would make the trip eleven days and eleven nights long. We had each paid fifty dollars for the berth and were entitled to live or die in it for the period.\nParticipation in the experiment was voluntary. Nobody, not even the organizers, had any idea whether it would turn out beautifully for all concerned or end tragically. The point was to find out what happened. We were to let nothing we did be influenced by the knowledge that there would be observers sitting behind one-way glass at all times while the experiment was in progress.\nNone of us knew other participants by sight, by name, or by reputation. The only way we could be sure of humanizing the faces we saw all day and all night through the glass would be to apply numbers to their backs. This would have been normal, had it not been for the fact the organizers had tried to make the numbers resemble dollar bills, so we would feel we were spending a fantasy voyage on our own private yacht.\nCertainly I felt along those lines as I shaved, broke an egg into my friend\u2019s coffee and began to read the latest news in the Times\u2014Dallas,...", "1_4_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four o'clock in the morning</span>. Remi Bonc?ur, whose name I now took\u2014took, became a poor man at this instant. The time was four o'clock in the morning. Remi found himself in a rich man's house one summer's morning in New Orleans. He was poor, alone, pure, resolute, and seventeen.\n\"Sir,\" said Remi to the butler in the long downstairs corridor, \"where is the lavatory?\"\n\"Straight down that hall, sonny,\" said the butler. \"There you'll find both the men and the women's lavatories. Both are attended day and night, on account of the great piety and modesty of the masters who inhabit this house.\"\n\"Thank you, sir,\" said Remi, and started whistling as he walked down the hall and started to open the door into the women's side, when from the corner of his eye he saw the men's door open and an old white-haired head come out and look around.\n\"And where do you think you're going?\" said this head to Remi.\n\"I beg your pardon, sir\u2014I was looking for the lavatory.\"\n\"Why in God's name don't you use the one downstairs?\"", "2_8_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMeanwhile, somewhere in the desert proper, the Space Wanderer was asleep and dreaming.\nAbout halfway between Devil May Care Acres, LTD., and the Reservation proper, a single shade tree grew in a small, bare, dry knoll. The shade tree had no name.\nThere were no houses anywhere near the tree. The tree was surrounded by sand. There was nothing around for miles in all directions but sand, cactus, and rock.\nAround the base of the shade tree lay the remains of a wooden box about two feet square. The box was rotted to splinters.\nThe dust around the box was lightly covered with finger marks, as though many people had stood there over the years, looking down.\nThe tree had a crown half a yard in diameter. It was brown with age. Splinters had been picked around the base of the tree. A few flakes of outer bark had been peeled off\u2014as though amateur portrait photographers had tried to improve the lighting.\nThere was no place to be alone, or even undisturbed, in California.", "0_14_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and they were sending us off with a lot of folderol. The bugler from the Irish regiment next door blew away at his bugle, an other regimental bugler ran over and tried to blow two calls at once to out-trumpet him. Meanwhile, the regimental fifer put down his beer and sent out a few sounds that were enough to stun Bacchus.\nIn a few minutes four bands were going full blast. It was indescribable, out-Aines Aines. There were drunken British and Irish officers dancing with four beslsty girls in a row -- those four girls having been selected from thirty available candidates by a dancing master. How he had managed the selection I have no idea. In the same way, there was no earthly reason why they suddenly decided to send us off with a grand parade instead of routinely letting us go home with orders to rejoin the colors in Liverpool as a reserve company in a month's time.\nSpeaking of candidates, there on the command car you could have seen Goering, Von Hindenburg, Hitler, and Ivar the Boneless, all talking at the same time -- with their arms around each other's necks. It was the soberest, sanest moment of the entire war...", "2_2_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-nine in the morning</span>. Lenore looked at Clint McMahon s beard and felt the beestung crotch of her panties in her pocket for keys. \u201cI\u2019d love to come see her, too. I really should go now. It was a real honor meeting you.\u201d\n\u201cLikewise.\u201d\n\u201cI really should go. Really.\u201d\n\u201cMaybe we could go out and have a carrot, here, before you go.\u201d\n\u201cI have so much to do tomorrow, but thanks, anyhow. I\u2019ll probably be back to see my grandmother.\u201d\n\u201cYou might take me to see her, too. I\u2019d really like to see her.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll probably be back, and I\u2019ll see what I can do.\u201d The sounds of the crowded gym outside the door grew, like a wave about to break. Lenore folded bits of fluff from her comforter. \u201cI really have to be going. Thank you very much. I can\u2019t thank you enough, for the things ...\u201d", "0_4_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-seven in the morning</span> when Driblette took it on himself to deliver the completed script. On the 405 Freeway, he pulled up alongside an Iowa license-platewith TASTYCAM written across the top, followed by a cheerfully optimistic 7783. The driver of the official car was asleep, head slumped, both hands on the wheel. Driblette gently removed one, lowered his window, slid the script inside and then drove off. Signal unknown.\nConcerts in Sutro Baths, the Beatles in the pool, acid, promiscuity, money, movies, looking for God up in Country Joe\u2019s amplifier...while engines ground under the society and the pool itself began to sag, fall apart at the cracks, white faces hollowed out in the plaster like graves under snow, haunting and cold. And the demographic leeches sucked on their veins ... these were the dreams $FIRSTNAMEF2 pursued in her spare time.\nIt wasn\u2019t all nights like that, bad or good. Sometimes Pierce would show up, and they would go on to some other party. Sometimes Metzger appeared and it would be like a party inside a dream, inside a party, inside a dream. Once or twice a week she would visit Karen, and watch...", "1_0_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-two ante meridiem</span>.\n\"I\u2019ll conquer you yet,\" said Wang-Doo, in 1939, in Paris.\n\"Good,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF2, thinking of the post horn.\nIn the street again, $FIRSTNAMEF2 took a deep, clear breath. I\u2019ll sooner or later, she knew, have to go over to that building, whatever it was. Right now all she wanted was to get away from Paranoy\u2019s again, so she walked to a nearby parking lot, picked a car at random and stole it.\nHer plan was to get rid of the costume and of Byron\u2019s suicide note in another Dumpster, suburban but secluded, and thumb a ride back to San Narciso. She felt she would have to go back to the hotel, if only to dump the car, and also hoped it might prove a nodal point in her psychic crisscrossings, all of them converging at that point. She made it as far as Sylmar, another halfhour on the freeway, before she was apprehended.\nEverybody seems to be yelling \u201cYoyodyne\u201d here, thought $FIRSTNAMEF2, as two cops in light Titanium Suits began extricating...", "2_4_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nThe timekeeper and the announcer were the only witnesses.\nThe first, the direst and loveliest of all the many Earthlings to be so elevated, flew gracefully into view. She was a blond-haired girl with torn clothes and wonderful small breasts; she wore sneakers and blue jeans, and carried a purse.\nThe balloon that carried her bore the legend: Excello Leather.\nIn the purse was a wad of stock certificates entitling the bearer to 10% of Excello\u2019s common stock.\nThe announcer was stunned. So was the timekeeper, standing by his hot-air balloon.\nThe girl waved gaily at the crowd. She had a lovely smile.\nThe announcer smiled back at her. \"Hell-o!\" he called. \"You doing all right?\"\nThe girl waved again and kicked a leg impishly.\nThe announcer chuckled. \"Well, good luck to you,\" he said.\n\"Can\u2019t you shoot her down?\" came an urgent voice from the control board. It was $SURNAME0\u2019s voice, speaking through a microphone concealed in the lapel of $SURNAME0\u2019s suit.", "2_2_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two seventeen in the morning</span>.\nThe sound of the incoming rounds was much increased.\n\"Run like hell, now, before this son of a bitch lets all hell loose on us,\" Stileman yelled.\n\"Oh my God!\" said Fenster. \"Run? What direction?\"\n\"Any goddamn direction!\" Stileman yelled. \"Just move it, people!\"\nSecond Lieutenant Stonedon $FIRSTNAMEB0, unable to stand on his own feet, was seized and dragged away.\n\"Golly, you certainly will be sorry in the morning!\" yelled Stileman. He looked in the direction of the firebase to the north. \"Come on, people!\" he yelled.\nSecond Lieutenant Stony Stevenson, Magnum, and Fenster began running with their equipment as if their equipment were made of lead.\nThey did not seem to have any inclination to get back into the air. They closed up at a hedge seven hundred yards north of the bunker, dropped their equipment and flopped over it, panting.\n\"There they go!\" sang out Sergeant CW Nixon, pointing.\nFirst Squad, B Co, dashed out into the open, chasing after the bewildered members of A Co. They soon had all but $FIRSTNAMEB0 rounded up.", "1_11_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. In all the piles of construction material that hemmed the rectangular pit in which the atomic cannon stood, only one system of mockery and derision yet remained active and threatening. The Sultan of Rum's embalmed, turbaned and cadaverously painted remains were still in the trailer bed. High around the walls of the excavation revolved a skeletal vehicle that had only one wheel\u2014a unicycle. On this unicycle was mounted the actual energetic nucleus of the rebellion\u2014a young man with a full beard and a Missileship, Temperature ind Warheads T-shirt.\n\"Hear me!\" cried young $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 from the unicycle. \"Hear me!\"\nNot far from the unicycle, in the shade of the trailer from which the withered remains of the Sultan of Rums had been so theatrically unloaded, stood a throne of wood, upholstered in cracked blue morocco. Near the throne was a chafing dish and a supply of candles and matches. A heap of beat-up cardboard boxes were stacked nearby. From one box, a wherry in fancy-dress still wore a cardboard crown.\n\"Hear me!\" cried young $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 from the unicycle. \"Hear me...", "0_9_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-one in the morning</span>. Our ship was sailing for New York at noon, and we wanted to be let off. I was stir crazy. I motioned $FIRSTNAMEB3 over to the window. He bounced like a ball and got at once. We studied the golf course in the soft morning air. A group of old men were out playing, bent over their bags. It was a beautiful day.\n\u201cBoy!\u201d I said, \u201cwe'll never get out of here.\u201d\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, we've got to go to the jails, I tell you -- right off!\u201d\n\u201cHow're we goin' to do it? We can't get around any more.\u201d\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, let's follow a car as it goes by, then jump out after it's passed and run like crazy men, smacking our heels all the time, right to the CO's office. What a mercy! You see? We'll never get another chance. They'll snatch us out at the end and put us on the boat.\u201d\n\u201cThat's true.\u201d (He was babbling again, I had to slow him down. )\nThen we were out on the cream-smelling streets. I noticed the...", "2_5_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-nine in the morning</span>, twenty miles north of Chicago, and it was the hour of the autumn locusts.\nThey were writhing on the pavement, gnawing at anything red, white, and blue. I got my hair cut every three days, so my hair was an orange red, and my jacket was white, and my polo shirt was blue.\nAs I walked toward the streetcar that would take me to the gritty downtown of Lake. county, seventeen miles away, there were one thousand six hundred people around me on the pavement, and they all wore Hawaiian shirts or sportshirts.\nON the streetcar was an old man who was mad at somebody else because the sun came up too fast. The mad old man tore a long strip of adhesive tape off the roll he carried, and said, \u201cIt's still dark, ain't it?\u201d He stuck the tape over the sunlight that come through a window.\nI agreed that it was dark.\n\u201cWell, it ain't supposed to be light for another two hours!\u201d he yelled. \u201cHow come this goddamn sun ready to go down on us again?\u201d", "2_3_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-three in the morning</span>.\nThis has been another episode in the unauthorized and untrue life of Pierce Inverarity.\nWhy exactly did you sign your name?\nREGAINING MORALE\nMonday morning at eight sharp, Douglas Blondwin called Twiggs. Referring to his loose-leaf ledger, Twiggs cross-referenced Blondwin\u2019s name to a listing for a Magnum 357 Magnum with special walnut grips and an expensive twotone finish known as Hipshaded Black and Desert Tan.\n\u201cLooks like you got canceled this month, Doug,\u201d Twiggs said brightly.\n\u201cThat\u2019s why I\u2019m calling,\u201d Blondwin said. \u201cI want you to reinstate me.\u201d\nPause. \u201cReinstatement isn\u2019t really our policy,\u201d Twiggs said. \u201cExcept in extenuating circumstances, you know what I mean? Like like you\u2019re away at school, or overseas in the service, et cetera et cetera.\u201d\nBlondwin said he had a personal reason for wanting to be reinstated.", "0_12_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-seven p.m.</span> Earthling time, according to the wrist watch of an earthling stewardess. A crowd of passengers in a corridor outside a broken telephone booth were growling and complaining.\nIn the telephone booth was the three-hundred-pound Space Wanderer, playing house with the remains of a telephone directory. He had torn the directory almost in half, and the more-than-half was spread about him on the booth\u2019s floor.\nWhen the telephone directory had responded to the first strip of binding by screaming its outrage, the Space Wanderer had covered every square inch of exposed skin with the directory\u2019s flakes.\nThe telephone directory was now a garment.\nThe Space Wanderer was practiced at keeping clean in a vacuum.\nHis two suitcases were on their sides by the booth\u2019s wall\u2014suitcases that had been sent up from Titan on a rocket ship nine years before.\nThere were scorch marks on the suitcases.\nThe suitcases were empty except for dirt.\nThe dirt was clean.\nThe suitcases were breathing in and out, like the periodic breathing of a sick man. These earthling suitcases were as sick as sick could be. They had drunk up soda and whisky, whiskey and soda,...", "0_5_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past five o'clock in the morning</span>. I had to pay for my breakfast and Sert wanted money too. \u201cSalut,\u201d he said. I never saw him any more.\nAnd I went outside to see if my trunk had arrived from the boat. It had not come. The first grey light of dawn came over the dark earth and I walked to the station with the sad Italian boys and women. Many of the boys and girls were carrying their shoes and slipped into the dust of the streets in their stockinged feet.\nAt the station I saw the night express for Paris pulling in. I went out onto the platform. Presently the porters began to shout out the names of the stations: Bordeaux, Pau, Narbonne, Cannes, Nice. Where would I go in Paris? With whom? What would I do there? And what would happen to me? Of these questions I asked the immobile night express as it sat in the grey dawn of the station. I kissed my hand to it and went out on the platform to get my ticket for the day coach.\nI awakened the next morning under some trees in the Bois de Boulogne. It was a hot, sunny day. People were sleeping on the grass and on stone benches...", "1_13_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span> at St. Luke's Hospital, where old $SURNAME1's young replacement, $FIRSTNAMEM2, the toddler, had just emerged from anesthesia, having had his appendix removed.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 had refused to speak a word to anyone\u2014even his father, $SURNAME0\u2014 since the fiasco with the memory banks. He had shut himself in his bedroom, where he communicated with the world by scribbling notes.\nNow old Salo was delivering him, bound for Earth and his true destiny, to the launch site.\nOld Salo was commenting on the dismal weather as he drove. It was a crisp November day on Titan\u2014the last day for such nonsense as days and weather. Twenty-four hours from now, the thermo-nuclear war between Earth and Titan was to begin.\nAll the lights were going out, all over Titan. Every power-line and fuel-line was being dismantled under orders. Everywhere was gloom, with the single exception of the white walls of Stromkirk Palace, around which hovered blue police helicopters.\nOnly Stromkirk itself was free of the gloom. Stromkirk was lit up like a Christmas tree. A great festival was being held there.\n\"I wish you\u2019d tell me where we\u2019...", "2_6_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>. The shaking and groaning had now become so terrible that love was no longer possible. $FIRSTNAMEB3 stayed twisted and Vashely Smiles lay covering her eyes. I got up and ran out of the house and joined my soul brother emerging weakly from his own front door. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2 yelled, \u201cwhat's happening? \u201d Motioning him to silence, Inverarity watched the pandemonium. \u201cWhat's going on?\u201d she yelled.\n\u201cEarthquake,\u201d he said. \u201cThey predicted it. Since just before the dawn.\u201d A felled tree crashed a dozen yards behind him and bounced like a log down a chute. \u201cIt sounds like it's getting worse all the time.\u201d\n\u201cWe seem to be OK,\u201d said Metzger. \u201cI guess these California trees won't come down with the root in the ground.\u201d", "1_3_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span> and the place was the Rockbound Coast of Maine. Now the old roadster shuddered, moaned, and wavered, anguished almost in its struggle to get up the hill. Snakes of cold sweat trailed under the guardian angel's cap and down the guardian angel's cheeks. In a minute more, the old roadster lurched and fell off history, leaving only lamps burning in pale doorways up the dark hill, lighting the way for the Buick. Now dark trees swam and squirmed around the Buick's white circle of light, and now dark houses stood up in the light and backed away, and dark trees rushed in and raked the light with branches, and the Buick was gone in the dark.\nThe time was nine minutes of four o'clock in the morning and the place was the outskirts of the port of Rockbound, Maine, in the United States of America, on the Earth. The tide was out and a buster was blowing and it was raw weather, raw. Two or three hours earlier, somewhere down the coast, the Age of Space had ended. Unknowing, unwitnessed, and unwanted, the last great rocket ship had roared up through the atmosphere into the cold of space and was...", "0_7_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 were crouched cat-fashion under the dome of the recruit-assembly hall. They had forced the air-conditioning vents open an inch and had poked their heads through to watch.\nContrary to the soldier\u2019s reverence for clock-time, the recruits were still a disorganized mob. Most of them were sprawled on the tile floor, snoozing or sullenly staring at the ceiling. There was no order whatsoever in the room. No attempt had been made to assign places in ranks. When a sergeant from the permanent cadre at the space-academy yelled, \"Atten-hut!\" no one sprang to attention.\nAt last, however, some soldiers roused enough to roll and sit up. They began to persuade others to do the same.\nThe undisciplined rabble began to form up in ranks of twelve, that being the standard grenadier squad. There was bad luck in taking a thirteenth man\u2014he would be an odds-on choice to draw the fatal black disk from the barrel of balls at a selection for the Replacement Battalion.\nIn one company, an unlucky recruit failed to wake up at all. He was an older man, a forty-year...", "1_7_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-three in the morning</span>. I paused long enough to get a cigarette burning, then I snapped to and made the bed. I folded $FIRSTNAMEB3's horrible T-shirt and raggedy trousers and left them on the pillow, and threw the rumpled blue jeans into the closet., If Aunt Sally wanted to mess herself, let her. She had given us all the beds in the house. I also folded two final items: one of $FIRSTNAMEB3's underwears and a brand-new pair of my own brand-new puce green silk boxers that were sitting on top of my bag. My trunk was under the bed; the key was in it, with all my new clothes in the world, and I'd suddenly realized that once I cleaned up and at last got out of Elysian Ford and R&R Recruitment Station, I would be traveling through the plushy and blas\u00e9 South as a fully dressed male, barely out of his teens, with $32 in his pocket, and with an unconquerable Okie accent he never lost 101 the meantime forever. No travel agent could help me. It would be up to me to roll along, wearing this pair of puce green silk boxers, eating pone and chitlings, down to Florida and across to the...", "2_20_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nCorplet was huddling ten stories under the ground in Yellowknife, Northwest Territories, Canada. His office was in Finletter Hall, Columbia University. There was no telephone in the office. He had to go downstairs to a phone. He did not go to the phone, because he was afraid the phone would be tapped. He sent his secretary.\nHis secretary was named Madman.\nHe simply telephoned his own office, Madman answered, and Corplet told Madman what to say. \"This is Earth calling,\" said Madman into the phone. She had a very nice voice. It gave her great satisfaction to know that she was speaking Martian. \"Who is this speaking, please?\"\n\"This is Earth calling,\" said Madman into the phone again. \"Inform the commanding officer that a tape-recording of a news bulletin from Earth is on its way to Mars by jet. He is to make every effort to secure the tape and translate it. \"\n\"This is Earth calling,\" said Madman into the phone for the third time. \"The news bulletin is of such importance that it must be retransmitted directly by light-wave to Earth at the earliest possible moment.", "2_18_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nThe clock in the tower of the Church of the James Whale flashed 7:00 in red neon.\nThe ship descended from the sky at such a terrific speed that the air hissed and hurled curtains of dust and gravel before it.\nThere was a thunderclap, too. You heard that, if you listened very carefully, after the hissing and the hurling gravel had ended.\nYou heard the thunderclap if you were anywhere on the wild and lonely plain that stretched from the church to the slowly flooding river.\nYou heard the clock in the tower, too, if you were anywhere in the parish of $FIRSTNAMEM1, Nebraska\u2014 where the railyards were laid out like spaghetti dropped from some uncaring sky, and where a tornado could always turn fifteen minutes into a lifetime. The bell in the Church of the James Whale was always striking thirteen as it tolled seven. Outside of $FIRSTNAMEM1, in any town where there were church-goers with silver wedding rings, it was just another church-clock\u2014ticking time away, with a regular beat.", "2_1_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-three in the morning</span>.\nOld Salo, $SURNAME0\u2019s butler, stood in a corner of the bedroom, hoping against hope for a miracle\u2014that $SURNAME0 would summon him. He stood there, one limp wrist cocked at an exquisite angle, one gnarled fist against his rheumy old nose, a silver tray held limply beneath his free elbow.\nAt one thirty-seven, $SURNAME0 got up from his dime-shaped swimming pool on the Moon. He stalked to his bedroom, stepped into a contour chair. He closed the current, thereby completing an electric circuit that activated the Martian signal for Earthling help.\nThe signal consisted of a hard white beam that was the congruent extension of $SURNAME0\u2019s middle finger. It was aimed at Earth, seemingly a light-minute away. Actually, only a hundred seconds had to pass before the beam reached Earth and bounced helpfully off the western coast of Portugal.\nAt one forty-seven, the beam that was $SURNAME0\u2019s digit was turned off again.\nThe signal had been clearly, succinctly repeated five times now\u2014once every twenty-six minutes.", "2_6_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-eight in the morning</span>.\nREBIRTH\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 awoke from troubled dreams to discover himself transformed in his sordid bunk into a glorious angel. This glorious angel, naked and ponderous, with gilded wings and a gilded halo, was bedded on a block of sculptured stone, was impaled on a sword a fathom long. The sword centered directly on the nub of the angel\u2019s spinal column, was sunk an inch and a half into it. The angel had not fainted. The angel was rigid with outrage, vital with pain.\nThe great hall in which the angel was imprisoned was so dark that he could see nothing of the hall, though his eyes were fully dilated. He had been able to see nothing since coming fully awake five minutes before. His eyes had made shadowy contacts with Satan, with the Archangel Raphael, with $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s Swiss gold watch, and with $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s Initial Paper Formatting home fountain pen.\nSomebody or something with stony hands had removed the bandage from the angel\u2019s head.\nHis head was pounding, pounding, pounding.", "0_15_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 was carried over the cracked sidewalks, up the broken streets to Genghis Cohen's. And it was raining. All the reds, whites and blues of Monday noon were gone. A miserable, gritty yellow-gray, light and dirty, polluted with particles of dust and soot, stained the buildings. The little shops and offices were pitiful, the fa\u00e7ades so thin, so crinkled, so cracked, so pasted together out of rubber and pasteboard. Everything loose in the streets, breaking up and rolling over and making a mess. And the people, stamping through the slush, looking dark and unhappy, spitting white phlegm from their chests, hunched and grumpy in their slick overcoats, tucked and folded into themselves with countless pockets for handkerchiefs and cough drops and Kleenex, and pockets stuffed with books and manuscripts and poetry, holding big cups of coffee and chanting, Form is Form and Form is Eternal.\n\"Where are you taking me?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Patience, patience,\" said Vincent. \"There is a man I know who can handle a thing like this.\"\nA man. A thing. A thing like this.\nIt no longer rained...", "1_16_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the third day of October, A.D. 2337. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his space ship had materialized only a thousand miles from Mars, not seventy-five million miles from Mars. The great message to the people of Earth, which $SURNAME0 and his mate had promised to beam in pure Galilean whistling-spring over every channel on November thirteenth, was a non-sequitur\u2014a lie that had popped into $SURNAME0\u2019s head like a cork out of a champagne bottle.\nIt was hard for $FIRSTNAMEF0 to lower her sights from the truth that was visually just beyond her\u2014the truth of eternity. And it pained her to learn anything new about a man with whom she had done business in so many past lives. But she went on learning. What she learned horrified her so much that she shrank back and wrapped her flowered muumuu around her again and pulled the turban down to her upper lip.\n\"What else?\" she said in pain.\n\"What?\" said young $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"There\u2019s more, isn\u2019t there?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"He\u2019s not that bad.\"\n\"Oh\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, \"...", "0_20_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span> of December 31, 1992, while the place was the living room of a large suite in a luxury hotel in downtown Houston, Texas. The suite was Number 56B. From its windows and terraces one had a view into another world. In a clear sky, the long spears of searchlights tracing patterns in the sky were the viscera of the party, making darkness visible. A massive traffic jam of limousines sealed off all approaches to the downtown section of Post Oak Boulevard\u2014five limousines deep in places. The limousines spit out streams of high Rollers, fashion models, law students, oil sheiks, Wall Street wizards, starlets, motion-picture executives\u2014and, of course, cops. Post Oak Boulevard was solid with flashing red lights and screaming sirens. Hovercraft patrolled the skies above the Boulevard. More police and hovercraft encircled the gold\u2014flake towers of the Gaylengetta condos, headquarters of the party.\nOn the walls of the suite\u2019s living room flowed, in color, the highlights of the world outside\u2014the party, the traffic jam, the police, the fire-fighters and the heavy equipment abrading the pavement, the broken water mains, the dogs and the...", "2_20_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-nine in the evening</span>, and the time had come for $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son to die, according to the timepiece in his head.\nHe had turned ten that year.\nHe was being starved to death.\nHe had perfect pitch.\nHe weighed forty-eight and a half pounds.\n\"I\u2019m going to $FIRSTNAMEB0! I\u2019ve lost my head. I\u2019m going to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s!\" said a tall, skinny man with a black mustache. He was wearing a green suit that had been very classy before it had been very dirty. He staggered out of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s Heavenly Hamburgers, holding his head. He swayed in his climb into a gypsy cab at the corner. But he made it. He banged the door shut, realized there was no door on the other side, reached inside to unlock that door. He fumbled for the handle.\nThe handle wasn\u2019t there. He beat on the real door with fists as big as hams. \"Let me out of here, you bastards! Let me out of here or I\u2019ll die!\" he said hysterically. He thought there was a conspiracy to starve him to death, and he was right.", "2_6_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the headquarters of inaugurated occasion-and-medium proprietor Stonecipheco Baby Foods, Inc., on East 51st Street in New York City.\nThe scene is inside the stairway to what is to be dubbed interface communication hatch 4b. It connects the headquarters of Stonecipheco Baby Foods to the top west side water and steam tunnel. Four black men, in white coveralls smeared with tunacrum, are worming their way into the hatch. Three carry empty burlap bags. The fourth carries a grocery bag. They sweat. Their shoes splash in ropy black water, up to their ankles. They are as yet only half way down the subterranean tunnel that connects interface hatch 4a, at the Lenox Avenue Metro-North Station, with 4b at Stonecipheco headquarters.", "2_1_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nNothing was left of $SURNAME0 and his dog but a vapor that puffed away into the darkness.\nOut of the vapor came a voice, and the voice said, \"Wish me luck, kitty cat.\"\n\"Good-by, $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said the voice of the dog.\nThe dog felt very melancholy. It knew what was happening to it, and it knew what was going to happen next. It knew that it would be consigned to oblivion forever. There was nothing left for it but to say good-by.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s voice was bluish, both surging and expiring. \"You\u2019re dying too, friends,\" said $SURNAME0. \"How many times have you died since you\u2019ve been born? Ten thousand? A million? Billions? And you die a million times between the day you are born and the day you die for good.\n\"You can keep out of space and the phenomena of the Solar System,\" said $SURNAME0, \"but you can\u2019t keep out of time.", "2_12_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after noon</span> when the first rockets went up.\nTen minutes later, when the last rockets came down, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were again sitting very quietly in the kitchen, listening to the twelve-o\u2019clock news on the radio.\nThe commune had not been mentioned, nor had the true purpose behind the war in Mars.\n\"How wonderful,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 contentedly.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0 left his chair again, went to the door. Nobody on the other side.\nHe returned to his chair again. He picked a section of the New York Herald-Tribune out of the trash can. \"The best news in the paper today,\" he said, \"is the obituary of a butcher\u2019s wife.\" He put the paper in his empty ham per.\n\"What\u2019s the obituary of the butcher\u2019s wife?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"She died,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"There aren\u2019t going to be any more celebrations?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"No,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Certainly not. What for? Who for?\"\n\"I just wondered,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 lightly.", "1_15_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME0 landed on the White House lawn. A Marine helicopter had just taken President $FIRSTNAMEB2 to Camp David, the Presidential haven in the Blue Ridge Mountains.\n$SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 appeared ten minutes before the time and the place of the formation of the poker society inaugurated, according to $SURNAME0, poor and inaccurate records, by $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, one hundred years before.\nIn a nearby rose trellis, Mrs. Earl demonstrates once again that, like everyone else under Mars, she has the power of contortion. She continues trying to see what is going on in the Mansion\u2014and in the Rose Garden.\n\"Kazoo,\" Mrs. Earl says. \"Kazoo!\" She means that she is fretfully astounded by everything she sees and hears. Kazoo is her vernacular way of saying \"Tut-tut-tut.\"\nAnd now a policeman comes galloping up to $SURNAME1, very porcine in his overcoat, confused by the arrest schedules in three police precincts, anxious to please and to be well thought of. \"Yes, sir\u2014yes, sir\u2014\" he says to $SURNAME1.\n\"Excuse me, sir,\" he says. \"But what do...", "1_22_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten sixteen in the evening</span>. The place was the living room of Mucho\u2019s apartment on Telegraph Hill. Mucho wasn\u2019t supposed to be back until midnight. Turtle had a key to let herself in. Mucho was probably a hair or so past the Oregon Border by now, driving back to San Francisco as she spoke, over the sea-swept spine of bone that linked Pacific tank-towns with their East Coast namesakes. Asleep in their mother\u2019s pad in the Berkeley Hills. Or perhaps not sleeping, listening to Buddy Greco\u2019s fine sax behind the hills, envying him the womblike enclosure of that good, brave and rotten town, \u201cthe rock,\u201d as the crusty old veterans called it. Old vets like Gonzo\u2019s dad; he had served too, in the Signal Corps like Mucho\u2019s father. $FIRSTNAMEF2 once asked how he got chosen for that outfit; he only laughed, turning down the smile hidden beneath his mustache. Some punks had their appendix out and wanted to know if that was all it was. \u201cYou won\u2019t be drafted,\u201d the doctor told her, not even looking up. But the plaque, that was something different; there...", "0_16_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty in the afternoon</span>, Wednesday, February 20,2007, a lovely palm-fringed Friday in the year 2000. One thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine was, like 999, a \u201ctriple\u201d moon. It was red and blue and yellow in the sky. Oasis and Chronos were both visible. Eve, Dental, and Chronos were aligned like a motorcycle gang, their colors reflected in the dunes of Big Sigma Quasar. The light fell sensuously on the white plastiwood gazebo in back of the white wooden house where lived the owner of the island in the River Chronos, and his family, and his visitors. The owner was only thirty-two, but he felt and behaved more like sixty-two, so time-grieved was he and hurt, and so settled had he become in unchangeable routines that against all romantic advice had come to be thought hereditary, the minutiae of which he was quite proud. He had been born here to a father whose own father had tyrannically acquired the island during the time of a weak Martian Opposition. So he thought the world had rejected him, and the island beckoned, and he got it. And the rituals were begun. And so he passed his time...", "2_0_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past midnight</span> when I woke up in my bed. They had gone. I had only just missed them. I turned out the light and went to sleep.\nOne morning I woke up and decided that, if I ever started to write, I would write about the things that happened in my own country. When people travel they like to read about other peoples\u2019 countries. But every country is interesting if you know about it. The Scotch like to read about the English, the French about the English, the English about the Scotch, the Americans about the English.\nIt seems silly. When you go to a country you don\u2019t want to be reading about some other country. It makes the people who live in the country you are in think that you don\u2019t like it. They want you to like it. You can learn more about a country just by talking to people than you can from books. After you have been to several countries you get a larger view. If you want to learn about any country, no matter where it is, all you have to do is get a map and look at it.", "1_4_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIn his special vault in the $SURNAME0 estate, $FIRSTNAMEB2 was sound asleep in a contoured queen-sized water bed.\nHe was snoring.\nHis last thought before falling asleep had been the wish that he were the dung sweeper on Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was the unsleeping warden of values. He had to be in good shape to do the job.\n$SURNAME0 didn\u2019t care much about the economy of the estate. The only products that moved out of the estate were apples, Concord grapes, strawberries, sweet cream butter, and milk. Those products were moved out by $SURNAME0\u2019s staff of three in a small truck to Alice\u2019s restaurant in West Palm Beach.\nAll the services and amenities of the estate were provided free.\n$SURNAME0 provided free board, free lodging, free medical service, free dental service, free psychiatric service, free legal service, free accounting service, free bookkeeping service, free radiatio n, free clothes-cleaning service, free clothes-mending service, free transportation, free firewood, free water, free electricity, free plumbing service, free toy-lending library, free playgrounds, free film-lending library...", "2_17_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five nineteen in the afternoon</span>.\nAt five-thirty in the afternoon, a midget tug shoved a howling, gunny-sack-wrapped Space Wanderer from behind and popped him into the open window of a $SURNAME0 palace apartment.\nAt five-forty-nine in the afternoon, a butler clapped a monocle to his eye and looked out the same window, looked down and saw a man and woman trying to brush the painful chrome off the Space Wanderer. The butler grinned, then climbed out the window onto a swaying lead ballon cable, rode the hurricane down to a floating heliport on the Hudson River far beneath.\nThe butler walked to New York City.\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 of $SURNAME0 Manor on Mars and her husband, chrono-synclastic infundibulated Watcher of Vintage Times, were sipping rum swizzles on the sun deck of their apartment. There were two grand pianos on the sun deck\u2014one for Mrs. Rumford, one for Mr. Rumford.\nOn the deck below, a sextet from the Newport School of Music was playing tunes from Tristan and Isolde.", "1_22_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-four in the evening</span> when the huge young man who had sat all day on the fountain\u2019s Statue of Liberty began to cry. A little group had collected around the fountain.\nThe huge young man, who was twenty-six but looked thirty-six, wiped his eyes with his big clown\u2019s hands and looked at the applauding crowd as though he were considering going on with his act.\n\"What\u2019s he doin\u2019\u2014think he\u2019s Grant Wolf?\" whispered a man with his children. He had finally stopped clapping, but he was whispering, as though afraid the huge young man might start crying again if he clapped too hard.\n\"Aw, look at him,\" whispered the woman with the five children. \"He\u2019s thinking about his dog again.\"\nThe huge young man took out a written speech and unfolded it. He and his dog points were written out for him on a legal pad by a social worker in Albany, who also paid for his transportation and room and board whenever he got as far as New York. The social worker had spent weeks helping him write the speech.\n\"I came here to plead,\" said the huge young man, in a voice that would have done credit to a trained orator,...", "2_1_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-nine in the morning</span>.\nTwo minutes later, a woman wearing a clear plastic helmet climbed into the air shaft. Two minutes after that, she waved from the window.\nThe signal was the signal to evacuate the building.\nThe not-so-distant boom of thunder was the sound of the tornado striking New York again.\nIn the confusion that followed the woman\u2019s actions, $FIRSTNAMEB0 got lost in the crowd. He was in a crowd of five thousand, jammed against the iron grating of a dormant elevator bank.\nCommands were shouted.\nAn amateurish relief column formed to mount the escape ladders. The column advanced two steps, halted two minutes while the leader brought up the hammer-thing again, then two more steps.\nAn officer at the head of the column opened the elevator door with his passkey, stepped smartly, was sucked in by vacuum.\nHe crash-landed in a corner of the sub-basement, broke his neck, killed the man behind him.", "0_7_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-two in the morning</span> of March 1. I had slept five hours, longer than anybody else in the house. Remi slept two hours a night; Lightning spent most of his nights in wino halls and flop-houses; Galatea spent her nights dreaming of design and revolutionary politics. Both of them were up and banging away by six.\nRemi got up to make coffee. \u201cWo ! Wo !\u201d he hollered. \u201cFrance has surrendered to Germany! Great little Dronte, he's been working all night, the radio never stopped, Dronte just turned the crank and bammed in dots. Great little Dronte!\u201d he yelled.\n\u201cWhere's that nut Jeannie?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cShe just yelled in from the streets. She says, 'All Americans come back home.' \u201c\n\u201cWell, we ain't going.\u201d\n\u201cAha! We're going to Denver, Texas, you and me, and hang up these flags. We're going like men.\u201d\n\u201cIsn't that the flag of France?\u201d Galatea asked excitedly. \u201cWhat are those swastikas?\u201d\n\u201cIt's...", "2_16_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF2 started thecountdown aloud: \u201cSix, five, four, three, two, one.\u201d\nSlower, lingering cadence, anticipations of pauses to come between phrases, so she could hear any answer, however slight: \u201cJohn Blodgett Apache, you.\u201d\nShe\u2019d been right. $FIRSTNAMEF2 put her head in her hands, nodding. She\u2019d staked him out like quail \u2013 he couldn\u2019t have known would fall for it.\nThe dead lady\u2019s husband strode out of the bedroom then and stood in the hall, staring with wordless grief at Fitzger-ald, who was now stuffing the money into an army knapsack. The husband held an automatic weapon in his arms. The toddler began to fuss from all the noise, waking from its nap. $FIRSTNAMEF2 had set the Saab\u2019s keys in her lap and her hand near the ignition switch. \u201cNo,\u201d she said, \u201cthat won\u2019t be necessary,\u201d and gave him the truest smile she had.", "1_4_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>, at the mile-and-a-half post on Highway 101 just south of Bucksport, Maine. I not only didn't own a watch, but around midnight, when the denseness of the country made time seem so irrelevant, I had thrown mine away. It was a cheap plastic Timex copy that probably wouldn't have worked very well in any case; as soon as I bought it I realized that Timexes were shit watches. I didn't need to be reminded of time when I was high; it seemed like an irrelevant concept to me then. When you're tripping you don't watch a clock, you watch a calendar and when each day's date passes it reminds you that you haven't returned the Ford, and you should have.\nNow I wanted a watch desperately. I wanted to reach the police station before New Hampshire line and have time to put my National Guard uniform in order. It was a nasty suspicion for me that I might get there too late; what if those bastards were already coming up the road. and how would the game be played then? As the car thumped us along I chased this possibility in my mind until I thought to myself, This is all wrong, they're not coming up the road with a warrant; they...", "1_6_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-seven in the morning</span> and we had the floor. That hairdresser will never forget it. Six forty-seven in the morning, rightful kingdom of Sweet in the meanest beanshoot in town; semi-nude, horny, Loaded and pushing Right Up To The Limit. We reeled through the lobby of the Las Vegas Flamingo Hilton; grinning, groaning, hunching and stumbling as we went through; past the Rotunda and up to the casino cage, where I qultimately hunched by the grimly somnolent credit manager with the biggest roll I've ever seen in my life, my hands shaking like veritable palsied Vissons... a roll of greenbacks, spilling out of his counting machine and down the front of his desk, while he just kept feeding it into his telautograph... his exhausted, red eyes fixed on the whizzing paper trace as it rattled out on the rollways beneath his machine.\nfinally understanding that he and I were comrades in the final stages of a terminal binge.\nMy attorney started out at the cashier's counter. He had $700 in cash and a run on his credit at the Casino. He had already lost $300 at Roulette. When the clerk refused to cash...", "1_7_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past seven o'clock in the morning</span> of another fine day in the early fall. The sunlight, having come around the other side of the skyscraper, shone with a ruddier glow upon the face and form of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, who was again skimming papers in his office.\nIn order to avoid the sunlight, The Surgeon General now sat in a new place \u2014 sat with his back to the wall of windows.\nThe chair that $SURNAME0 had been sitting in was vacant.\nThe end of the cigarette in the turquoise cigarette holder was now bright orange.\n$SURNAME0 flipped a page of the paper that he held, brought it again to his face for appraisal. He scowled at whatever he saw on the page, dropped it to the floor. The page rustled as it fell.\nA hissing sigh from $SURNAME0 announced that the sigh was one of relief rather than discontent. It had nothing to do with the substance of the paper that he had been looking at.\nHis beam was directed up at a lofty corner of the room, where a tangle of signals and moving lights indicated complicated coming and going in the space outside.\nThe appearance of the knot of signals and moving lights was this\u2014\npa pa pa pa $FIRSTNAMEF1-...", "2_6_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>. Walinda Peahen was standing outside the collar of cement around the reinforced shaft, sucking a lifesaver and humming part of \u201cThe Eyes of Texas.\u201d She sucked. The eyes of Texas are upon you. It was too a good goddamn day to be in this hole. It was going to be a good goddamn day.\nWalinda looked around. Nobody was watching. She flipped open the two zippers of her jumpsuit exposing the black lace of her bra and the cleft of her stomach and the smooth golden plane of her chest and crotch, raised her arms above her head, and twirled, revealing still more smooth golden plane and the back of a tennis shoe and then white leg, as she executed a neat pirouette.\nA pneumatic hammer began pounding somewhere beneath Walinda Peahen\u2019s gold platform boots.\nIn the administrative office a phone rang.\nWalinda flicked the lifesaver into an ashtray. \u201cPlease do not do that, Mr. Beeber,\u201d she said into an already extending microphone.\n/b/", "0_10_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after ten o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the living room of a spacious home in $SURNAME0\u2019s recreated prehistoric town. The home belonged to $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0, though $SURNAME0 insisted that it be called The House of the Good Malintzi, after a certain saint he especially admired.\nThere were three people in the room.\nOne of the people was the home\u2019s owner, the composer Robert $SURNAME0. He was seated in an old rocking chair before a dying fire. He was wearing an Edwardian smoking jacket and an American football helmet. He did not remove the headpiece of the suit, though there was no ball in the headpiece, and the cocoonlike lining was unpressurized.\nThe only illumination, other than a shaft of sunlight through one of the windows and a shaft of moonlight through another, was a pink radiance cast by a moon of Jupiter so large, so close and so luminous in the sky outside that it made the shaded homes across the street seem close and dark by comparison.\nResting his chin on his updrawn knees, looking rumpled and crushed, $SURNAME0 read in a large book in an equally large Moonman language. His eyes were watering, watering as they had...", "2_17_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-one in the afternoon</span>, Central Standard Time.\nAt five forty-one in the afternoon, Central Standard Time, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had come forty-six thousand miles to hear the orchestra.\nAt five forty-one in the afternoon, Central Standard Time, $FIRSTNAMEB0 wished he hadn\u2019t come.\nAt five forty-one in the afternoon, Central Standard Time, $FIRSTNAMEB0 loved his enemy with all his heart.\nHe had never been able to love anybody or anything at all.\nThe little orchestra played a sonata by Johann Sebastian Bach. They interpolated a gavotte by Handel.\nThe music proceeded on its way.\nThere was nothing $FIRSTNAMEB0 could do but love the little musicians and wish he were tall enough to bean one over the ear. This was a new feeling for $FIRSTNAMEB0. He liked it\u2014and he pitied himself for liking it. He pitied himself for being such a damn fool as to love people who were his sworn and mortal enemies.\nThe music ran on, finished the Handel gavotte, found its way into Bach again. It attained a great luminous place in the middle of the music.\nA place that could be reached only by flying.", "1_1_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>. Three minutes to go. Not long at all. Not much time in which to do everything that had to be done to get ready for the signal to break open the outside doors and let in $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe signal would be a pistol shot outside. And they would hear the shot echoing and re-echoing inside their helmet radios.\nKaz wouldn\u2019t fire the shot until he was sure that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was really coming up the red-hot corridors. And, after he fired the shot, there would still be a minute to dot all the i\u2019s and cross all the t\u2019s before $FIRSTNAMEB0 arrived.\nKaz counted slowly to himself: 20-19-18. A last look around the vast hall. Everything, so far, was shipshape and Sundays.\n17-16-15. The great space was silent, steamy, and malodorous, compared to the clean snows outside.\n14-13-12. Kaz wished he had a brew of schnapps. A hot schnapps would calm his stomach.\n11-10-9. Strange to be thinking of hot schnapps in this steamy hellhole, so far from any hot water or distilled liquor.", "1_18_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-four in the evening</span> on October 11, and the Titanic, though never in any serious personal danger, was suddenly seized with a kind of de ja vu. Some thought the ship was back in Southampton Water, but others, with better head-balances, knew where they really were: on the Titanic, just north of the icebergs of the North Atlantic, about to throw 26,000 tons against the sea for another night and another day.\n\"Here we go!\" shouted the waiter. \"And good-bye!\"\nTHE BASHFUL LEADER OF THE CHURCH OF GOD THE Utterly Apocalyptic\nCrucial to an understanding of the wedding of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME1\u2019s Acre is an understanding of the theology of the Reverend C. Horner Redwine, the dour and bashful leader of the Church of God the Utterly Apocalyptic. The theology was simple, being adapted scantily to the needs of a tenantless and declining sect that had no building large enough in which to argue its faith. To his followers, Reverend Redwine put forth this simple creed:\n\"Since time began and for no reason\u2014similar to the utterly and coincidentally unjust shell-game death of Little Napoleon-Napoleon Jones\u2014the...", "2_9_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThat was when things began to happen very fast indeed.\nThe man who strung $FIRSTNAMEB0 up was standing at attention by the time the first alarm sounded. His right arm snapped up at attention, then his left.\nHe turned to his left and began to march, keeping time with the snare drums.\nHis left foot stepped high and took imaginary measure, again, again, again.\nBy his left foot, he left the iron balls on the sand\u2014cast them away disdainfully.\nA young officer in the color of Martian blood ran up to him, shook his hand enthusiastically, presented him with a certificate for two weeks\u2019 emergency leave, wrap, and blanket material for his wife.\nThe happy, piccolo-tooting crowd opened rank for the man and let him through. The man ran to his space ship, hopped inside, closed the hatch, whistled up the crew.\nIt was a beautiful small ship, brass and mahogany and cream-colored cyclone fencing. It had been a present to the Commander from his fellow Martian commanders.\nThe Commander ran his hand lovingly over the controls of the ship, found them clean and dry.", "2_23_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-eight in the evening</span>.\nThe silver space ship was more than fifteen thousand miles above the Ameri lican East Coast. It was almost directly over Waterbury, Connecticut. It was almost directly over Newport, Rhode Island.\nThe space ship was six hundred miles directly north of Cape Kennedy.\nThe United States was forty five hundred miles behind the ship. America was a tiny segment of the hatch window, resolv ed at its top and bottom into the oval outline of egg-white clouds.\nThe time was twelve oh one in the morning.\nThe hatch popped open.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his son $FIRSTNAMEB2 set the heavy amplifier on the floor of the cabin, then clambered stiffly out of their spacesuits and out of the airlocks. The air they breathed tasted coppery, as if unbottled oxygen had run out months ago. They didn\u2019t care.\nWhen they looked back they could see the entire West Virginia panorama within the cabin. Gold ing the middle was a tiny vase holding a bright-blue violet plant. The cabin window would have made a perfect grassy basin in the Appalachians, with levels for farms and a winding white New River for traffic.", "0_1_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME1 began groping his way toward his little basement. The fountains were counting off seconds again.\n\"One thousand one\u2014\" counted one fountain, \"one thousand two\u2014\" counted another.\n\"Looks like curfew won\u2019t be so hard to take after all,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 up in her treetop, \"especially since we\u2019re all going to be new, singing people by tomorrow.\"\n$SURNAME1 crawled into the little basement.\nThe little basement sported walls of stone that were three feet thick\u2014three feet of stone armored plate, then twenty inches of temperedanium, then three more feet of stone plate. The walls were supposed to keep out both the pneumavores and any form of remote-control tracking sensors that the Martians might send to Old Newport.\nThe stone walls of the little basement, ideal though they might have been against certain external threats, were no help at all against internal ones\u2014against the odor of the unwashed human body.\nA circle of light appeared on the floor of the little basement, an eight-inch circle of light like the opening in a manhole cover. The circle grew until it was three feet across. The Lady $FIRSTNAMEF0 emerged, impossibly making herself even more...", "1_14_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-nine in the afternoon</span>. I felt so bloody lonely and lost I could only laugh. \u4e00Exactly forty-eight hours ago,\u201d I thought to myself, \u201cI was sitting at the bar in the Flamingo having a pi\u00f1a colada with my old friend Killer McCoy, and now I\u2019m sitting in the lotus position under a palm tree on the filthy bank of the Laredo River with all my earthly goods stacked around me, and nothing left to lose. I\u2019ve gone all the way around the circle.\u201d I laughed again. It was funny, but no one around me seemed to appreciate the humor. Why? I tried to think back...what was it? What had I learned? What form of knowledge or superknowledge came with those fifty purple micrograms absorbed into the blood\u2014allowing me, the Wandering Stranger from the live\u2014action world of random madness, to see, in the Las Vegas of the Seen and the Unseen, the holy city of the West, the secret behind the Secret Society?\nI laughed aloud. Human life is one big rushed job in an uncovered boat on the Ohio River in the month of August. You have to laugh, or you\u2019ll go insane. Abraxas,...", "2_18_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nWalter Clay walker the lawyer went out of his office on the upper floor of the Nevada Hotel. His eyes were hidden behind two layers of flannel bandages. The bandages were held in place by adhesive tape that crisscrossed his face. He could not bear to look at the man and woman who were about to be divorced. He was less tolerant of his own thoughts, and of his own environment.\nThose thoughts and that environment had cost him a third layer of skin and the sight of his eyes.\nHis wife, Rachel, aged fifteen and three-quarters, was down in the lobby of the hotel right then\u2014 with that young man.\nConstance Rattigan, the fastest typist in Mercury, exited her office in the International Building. There was a briefcase in her hand. Constance had been expecting a telegram or a letter\u2014 the good news of an invitation to London or Paris or Rome. Instead, she found that the building had caught fire. She opened the briefcase, and put the typed invitations into it. She put her hands over her ears to shut out the alarm bell. She took some pills to bring her asleep, and some pills to wake her up.", "2_3_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-one in the morning</span>. Very suddenly every window in the building was illuminated in a soft green glow.\n\"Haunted house!\" screamed the last Red Scout. And then the building exploded in a green roar of light that consumed the Scout\u2019s yell and hurled it, dissolved, across the cold sky.\n\"Holy smoke!\" screamed a student on $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s roof. \"Up and at \u2018em, you guys!\" He grabbed a fire extinguisher and a ladder from his sleeping bag. \"$FIRSTNAMEF1, $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2014you guys, where are you?\" And he clattered down the ladder and disappeared through the smoking door.\nEleven minutes later President Hato Hacker of Gila College climbed down a ladder into the smoky basement under $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s apartment. The basement looked as though it had been hit fifty times by heavy artillery. $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s lights were off, and their fuse was missing.\n\"Lie quietly,\" whispered Hato Hacker to the Red Scouts, all in casts and bandages under their blankets. \"Lie quietly, boys.\"\nEven the stomachs and livers in jars, thought Hato Hacker. Even the jars themselves.", "0_12_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty p.m.</span> The place was Madison Square Garden. The occasion was the Second Annual International Amateurs\u2019 Boxing Title Championship. The six fighters were two Americans, one Frenchman, one Brazilian, one Puerto Rican, and one Turk.\nThe fighters wore red trunks and were to be watched through telescopes by eight hundred thousand enthusiastic customers in 110 closedcircuit theaters across America.\nThe customers paid fifty\u2014cent, a\u2014dollar, and two\u2014dollar seats, plus candy, popcorn, soft drinks, and assorted relevant miscellaneous items.\nAltogether, the customers put four thousand one hundred thousand dollars in neat stacks of mixed denominations into Madison Square Garden\u2019s safe in twenty\u2014four minutes.\nThence forward, all the money was in the safe and the sixty\u2014four thousand people in the big building laughed and clapped and cheered and carried on like forty times that many would if the most exciting moments of their lives lasted an hour instead of a minute.\nThe six fighters were to earn fifty grand a piece for whaling away at one another for thirty rounds.\nThis did not include what the winner got for being the winner. This did not include what the loser got for being the loser. It did exclude what the amateurs trained and accomodated the great...", "1_3_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. In addition to $FIRSTNAMEF0 and Woofs, there was a man named Pierce, but he had not yet arrived, and would not for another two hours. The rain was abating. At midnight the thunderstorm that had begun, with perhaps odd thoughts of Humphrey\u2019s Castle in its head, over the Pacific Ocean, had seemed to reach a certain zenith. Thereafter it had begun to wane, slowly, as all storms do, but with odd fluctuations, for at two a.m. there was one of these fluctuations\u2014a short, bright storm that stormed right over the bungalow, the ghostly black ruin of which the four in the cabin could see through the window. Lightning painted everything yellow, and the thunder came in one-second crashes, rather than growls; and, when it crashed, there was something man-made about it, a certain theatricality and hollowness. One could imagine the grass taking vengeance not just on the bungalow\u2019s squatters, but even on the few timbers of the former foundation, for being willfully, frivolously, and idly upended. And now the rain arrived again, as if the brief flash had expended itself and needed time to replenish...", "1_13_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>, Wednesday, December 8, 1992. The place was the home of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 on the wooded estate called Newport, on the island oftradington in the West Indian republic of $SURNAME1 heinous. In the vaultlike basement of the mansion, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 were in cages of laser-etched ivionic crystal. The cages were on tracks, and so could be rolled into showers of purple sparks. The showers purified not only the man and the dog, but dresses, suits, and skin-diving equipment in adjacent cages, and a moon rocket in a silo outside.\nThe shower of sparks was a feature, not a bug. It came on automatically whenever a defined set of conditions were fulfilled. The set of conditions was, roughly, the presence of $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 in the cellar, plus the presence of someone outside the basement door. The drums that thumped periodically on the concrete floor above did not have their origin in any human footsteps. Any vibration in the house above, no matter how slight, was amplified and translated into drums among the particle receivers by the machinery of the house.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were as tame as dogs, being good Moon engineers. $SURNAME0...", "0_7_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after seven o'clock in the morning</span> and one second after the night had passed. The direction was east by north. The regiment lay in a valley that had been filled with water in the recent rains. The valley was half a mile long and was enclosed by low, rolling hills that were green with vegetation.\nIt was a beautiful place.\nThere was nothing beautiful in the regimental position, however. That was bad.\nThe 3d Regiment, Martian Imperial Infantry, was badly deployed. It was badly deployed because it had been rushed into positions it could not hold. It had been rushed into those positions by radio commands frombatch-circuited Battle Command on Phobos, the inner moon of Mars.\nHad the regiment been permitted to deploy into an attack in logical stages, it would have been in perfect position.\nBut it had not been given that privilege.\nThe result was that the 3d Regiment was badly deployed.\nIts 1st Battalion lay in a forest of young blue trees on the western edge of the valley, facing the northern end of the valley and the green hills beyond them. Its 2d Battalion was on the opposite side of the valley, apparently as an afterthought, because its position was inferior from a tactical point of view. It lay on the southwestern rim of the...", "0_20_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-five in the evening</span> when I rounded the outskirts of New York City and found myself entering the Hudson River toll plaza. I was with-out money. Everything had been spent on gas, food, sheer mileage...and something in my soul. I had no intention of stopping. Let them throw me in jail. I had come to that point when a man loses faith in himself and his future, and I was ready to embrace any fate.\nNo doubt this was partly drug-inspired, but mainly it was induced by the life I had been leading; running red lights in Hollywood at seventy miles per and hour is not good for the old nerves. I remember saying to myself that all I really wanted to In Bobby's Angels?\" but I was past knowing what I really wanted. My heart was thudding like a daisy mill. I needed a mint tea and a long rest; instead I saw myself sucked onward into the unconscious drama of this land.\nBeside me Rita Hayworth leaned over to pick up something in the floorboards of the car, and I saw that she was bending over almost all the way to her knees. This was too much, and with a kind of groan I stepped on the gas, almost rol ing us.\nUnder the elevated Xanadu...", "2_12_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-three post meridiem</span>.\nIt was Monday.\nThe sun was exactly overhead.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was fifth in line, waiting to talk to the animal in his own special closet, the most beautiful animal that $SURNAME1 could imagine. The closet was made of ribbed blue blinding copper.\nBy the time $SURNAME1 got to the closet, there were 160,000 people in front of him.\nThe closet was six feet wide, nine feet deep, and ten feet high. It was a closet to dream of.\nThere was nothing inside it but air.\nThe whole palace, in fact, had no inside except air. It was a work of art that could be appreciated fully only from one spot\u2014a vantage point ten feet above the paved and curving palm court outside.\nThis vantage point was ergonomically a pedestal for Zeus.\nThe palace and its denizens were really all one sculpture\u2014a gigantic sculpture of a man who, in order to elevate himself spiritually, had twisted his legs around his neck and then thrown his head as far away from his legs as he could.\nSince the man\u2019s arms were tied in a knot at his back, he could do nothing to save himself from falling.", "1_19_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-nine in the evening</span>. The place was safe, cold, dusky space. The object was warm, beautiful, and marvelously appointed Earth. Salo was not at all sure where in Earth\u2019s solar system the space ship was supposed to land him. $SURNAME0 had only assured him that Earthlings would find him and take good care of him.\nHis signals were so weak now that $SURNAME0 could not read them without going close to the core of the tight-packed helioite.\nThe blinking eye of the helioite went dark; the beats of $SURNAME0\u2019s watch wentDUM\u2014DUM\u2014DUM\u2014DUM\u2014DUM\u2014DUM\u2014DUM\u2014DUM\u2014DUM\u2014\n$SURNAME0 found himself back in his office, with his gold-leafed book, The Decline and Fall of the West. The book was open to a page on which a stick of helioite had lodged.\n$SURNAME0 read,\n\"The Six XII Rubies of Tronjol are now the most coveted item, outside of marijuana cigarettes, on the black markets of Mercury and Venus\u2014items sought after even by the Solar Navy. Rumored to be part of the uncut materia of the...", "1_8_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight o'clock in the morning</span>. Red and the gang with $FIRSTNAMEB3 had arrived out at my aunt's house in northern Nebraska, at the very edge of the iceberg, the cold artic desert of the Middle West. Marie and I had drawn the task of entertaining them; it couldn't have been planned better. Red and Roy made themselves immediately at home, raiding the refrigerator and consuming most of what was in it. At no time in my life have I known such characters as Red and Roy. Wherever $FIRSTNAMEB3 was, strange times ensued. But not like this. $FIRSTNAMEB3 had always escaped. This was round the clock. But even my aunt was affected. She came down the stairs in her nightgown and peered cautiously through the door and said, \u201cWhat's going on?\u201d\n\u201cNothing,\u201d said Roy, \u201cand if you come on in we'll give you some coffee and donuts and the newspaper to read.\u201d My aunt was over seventy years old, but she went and sat down with Roy and accepted a cup of coffee and a donut. When I told $FIRSTNAMEB3 he just said, \u201cWe got to dig her soul.\u201d But the soul of my poor old aunt faded without a trace in about an hour, when Red jumped up and...", "2_20_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty in the evening</span>. It was always eight forty in the evening\u2014 always that mysterious time when pistards dressed in metallic pink, green, and white rose from their camouflage in the weeds and went skimming over the rocks toward the corporate buildings. Soldiers did not go on hall-tohall inspections then.\nLittle habits like that had made $FIRSTNAMEB0 a leader of the revolution on Mars.\nIt had been entirely the work of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Stoney, his eldest son.\nNeither had known anything about guerrilla warfare at the beginning.\nNeither had known anything about staff work.", "2_0_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after midnight</span>. I woke up out of my sleep and suddenly realized that all I needed to make my life complete was a good plot for a novel, something with a beginning and a middle and an end.", "0_9_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-six in the morning</span>. The space was $FIRSTNAMEF1-luther-hatche-cun College\u2019s freshman orientation auditorium. The place was specified as category number nine on a seating diagram appended to the agenda which had been handed out to Lenore at the gate, the hand out done by a boy in cutoffs who had said he was a sophomore or something, with a beard and orange-red hair, on break from his Hawaiian sunrise-frosting project in the dining hall kitchen, who had been waved on by a policeman, la cop. At the bottom right-hand corner of the diagram, sticking out, was a little card showing three rectangles, one of them shaded, and the shades connected by a diagonal, and some numbers and words. Here the diagram ended.\nLenore had arrived rather late, having been slowed down by having to sign in at the dorm front gate, and then wait for a guide to come help her find her way through the grass-plots to the actual building where the orientation thing was being held, because the fields were so itchy on Lenore\u2019s bare feet and her toes kept getting stuck in the grass. But it was hot, and Lenore figured most of the freshmen had arrived during the last few...", "0_2_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-six in the morning</span> and all was perfectly normal. Just north of town, somebody reported seeing a strange turbulence in the air above a wood. Para\u00adnoia touched down ten minutes later and swept the area without finding anything. The passengers were all asleep. God-knew-who was bopping sto\u00adryman Beeber aside and tapping out a new headline in the little Scrabble box:\nSo Near To Nowhere...Are You?\nI began taping the first chapter up to the steering wheel, where it would be handy. Then we were on the San Sebastian Causeway and headed north. Just before dawn we reached the little hamlet of McCarran, some thirty miles from Vegas\u2014and I realized that something was absolutely wrong below. We were rolling over. Not on the pavement, not on the highway, not on anything man-made. I pulled off and looked.\nIt was sand. Deep, soft khaki sand, almost like snow, but looser. We were already a foot down. It was fine for driving on, as long as you didn\u2019t try turning. Our back right wheel was mired. I shut off the engine and listened to the silence. Not even a dog was barking. Apparently they had all been sand...", "1_18_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty in the evening</span>. Our shadows, which had been all right to get us along under the trees, became the problem. They threw the road into double darkness, because they stood out on the evening gray of asphalt. $FIRSTNAMEB6, who was so sensitive she claimed she'd married Remi and then me to spite her mother (it was her mother's theory that girls should not just go with boys), said she liked our shadows so much she'd like to have them stuffed and mounted, she thought they added distinction. Now, she said, all we needed to make us respectable was a car with a crest on it. Already, the way $FIRSTNAMEB6 talked I could see the cops coming into her life, the loneliness of a whore in a country hotel taking hold of her. I had that beat in a month and made it a year. But Remi? He wasn't a bottom man; I could see that already. Anyway he had a precarious grin on his face. He wanted us all to be like a country club. Remi talked about getting a membership at Greenwood Acres for us all, in the fall. But that was Okie talk. We got out of the car, so as not to freeze, and began to climb. Our shadows scrambled after us. I'm an eternal dun...", "0_9_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine sixteen in the morning</span>. We fished another wet and winding half hour, after which we had a good and harmlessly drunken argument about Governor Alfred M. Landon of Kansas, and wound up by drifting out of the saloon. The sky was low and gray. The town was muddy. Tourists were milling around, looking at wooden Indian officered souvenir shops and at mining relics, of which there were millions along the street -- rocks from the Tintic I lode, chunks of quartz from the Boston and the Franconi, ore from the Mexican Smelting and the Golden Crown and the Caledonia, shriveled and gray as fossils of a prehistoric age, picked up from washouts Time had worn in the hills. The three Union blacksmith shops were playing swinging music on their radios. We walked by the governor\u2019s mansion, with its neat green lawn and pointed gables, and cut behind it on a path toward a brook in back. The mansion was closed, of course, on account of the owner\u2019s being dead and gone in Washington, D.C. A great marble slab poked out of a bank overlooking the brook. I read it, and then helped $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Carlo examine it. \u201cKnow what it says?", "0_3_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-four in the morning</span>, and there would be little sitdown breakfast for me until one or one-fifteen outside on the Las Vegas roadside, mile and a half up the highway from Galactic Spaceview Motel. With that thought in mind, I went to bed. Rained like hell all night.\nI called down for coffee. The desk clerk answered; said he\u2019d send it right up. Soon there was a knock on my door. I opened up.\nIt was a swarthily handsome well\u2014built man of perhaps thirty\u2014three, in white sport shirt and very sharply creased blue Levi\u2019s.\n\"I\u2019m the guy sent your coffee up,\" said he.\nJust then there was another knock on my door. I opened it to find an attractive young woman with a pot of coffee and a basket filled with rolls and butter and a fruitcake. I invited them both in. The young woman poured the coffee. \u201cAnything else I can get you?\u201d she said. \u201cI\u2019m Johnny, at the desk.\u201d \u201cWhat time is checkout?\u201d I asked. \u201cEleven,\" said Johnny. I looked at him, then at the girl, then back at him.", "0_20_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-two in the evening</span>. Remi Boncoeur was entertaining a prospective investor.\nRemi Boncoeur had also become a scientist.\nHe was studying, categorizing, and experimenting with colors\u2014especially blue, his birth color.\nRemi and his guest were speaking French to one another. The guest, who had insisted on speaking only French during his visit, did not feel intellectual simply because he happened to speak French. He had grown up in Quincy House at Harvard, and had graduated from Williams College, and he was many other things. He was a health-food magnate. He was an inventor of some standing in the patent medicine world. He had even invented a small clear plastic dome-top greenhouse through which one could view the growth of vegetation. Remi Boncoeur was listening to him.\nDemaris was making the weak Martinis. She was late thirties, but had probably been aging faster than most women for close to forty years. She had little gray-black freckles on her legs from the summer before, and from the spring before that. She was a terrible commingler of perfumes. She could squeeze a lemon and make your eyes water. She looked as if she had been around. She was still a beautiful woman.", "2_2_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-four in the morning</span>. The noise of the night swirled. Both the doctor and Lang sat in silence, looking at each other.\n\u201cJesus H. Christ,\u201d Lang said at last. \u201cAre you telling me Lenore is dead?\u201d\n\u201cShe\u2019s dead as a doornail.\u201d\n\u201cJesus.\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\nThere was a silence. The noise of the night swirled.\n\u201cWell what do you want me to do about it?\u201d Lang wondered finally.\nMr. Bloemker heaved himself up out of his chair with a clump and a groan and moved heavily over to the edge of the big desk, where he could look down at Lang. He fished a handkerchief out of his suit-coat and blew his nose in it. \u201cI guess what we want you to do is come to the Centre this coming weekend,\u201d he said softly. \u201cThe facility should be open by then.\u201d\n\u201cCome to the Centre? What would I want to go to the Centre for?\u201d\n\u201cTo get your fee and to see ... Lenore.\u201d", "2_17_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the new arrival was young Miles Nixon. The diffused light from the traffic outside the station had flavored his skin a cooking tan. He stripped off his sailor cap and had whiteness smoothly combed back to a stubble.\n\"Where have you been?\" said Candide.\nA crowd of unknown people came into the room.\n\"Had to leave college,\" said Miles Nixon.\n\"Why?\" said Candide.\n\"Don\u2019t ask me,\" said Miles Nixon. Armed men in uniforms, who might have been Secret Service, hustled everyone away.\n\"What\u2019s this?\" said Candide.\n\"It\u2019s MY reception, isn\u2019t it?\" said Miles Nixon. \"Don\u2019t ask me.\"\nThe crowd was hustled away by armed men.\n\"What was that all about?\" said Candide. \"Where\u2019re we going?\"\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" said Miles Nixon pugnaciously. \"I thought I knew what was going on, but I don\u2019t know now.\"\nHe loosened his collar resolutely. And then he was worried.", "0_10_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-one in the morning</span> of the first Tuesday in August. Monroe went blithely on up the steps, balanced on the low wall of the porch, and crossed the living room with its coats hung on chairs, smiling at his mother\u2014he had wanted his old room for the space for his skulls\u2014and his lather brooding over his newspaper, unresponsive. Monroe didn\u2019t really have a very common name. His father\u2019s real name was Baird. Luke was dubbed that by his own father on account of his not getting a breath of fresh air until he was two years old. Baird was born in South Dakota and grew up there in a desolate township where farms failed and the people flocked to California to make a living. Baird never made anything more than a living, but he grabbed hearsay ideas from his father\u2019s friends who had made common sense out of society by hitching it to the star of progress and technology. Baird grabbed these ideas. One idea was radio communication with Mars, the germ of which tapped the drowsy sex-hungry Holden Caulfield in his sleeping womb. Another idea was in investing in radio-chain retail furniture stores. Baird came to California and sat in on San Diego\u2019s first radio listeners\u2019 club.", "2_13_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-five in the afternoon</span>.\nOld King Kurt and young King Louie were seated on the terrace of the palace at Cheng-Tcheng.\nOld King Kurt was smoking his seventeenth $FIRSTNAMEM1 cigarette of the day.\nYoung King Louie was smoking his eight hundred and thirty-fourth Pall Mall cigarette of the day.\nOrdinarily, old King Kurt allowed young King Louie one $FIRSTNAMEM1 and one Pall Mall every twenty-four hours.\nToday, young King Louie was allowed three cigarettes, under protest\u2014a protest that had been lodged by young King Louie\u2019s staff physician.\n\"This is just a little riot\u2014isn\u2019t it?\" said old King Kurt to young King Louie.\n\"What riot, Papa?\" said young King Louie.\n\"All these space ships landing\u2014Martians and flying saucers and things,\" said old King Kurt.\n\"Oh\u2014that,\" said young King Louie.\n\"I\u2019ve never seen anything like it,\" said old King Kurt.\n\"Neither have I,\" said young King Louie.\n\"I don\u2019t think it\u2019s a healthy sign,\" said old King Kurt.\n\"Maybe not,\" said young King Louie.", "2_12_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after noon</span>, Greenwich Mean Time.\n\"Hi\u2014\" went the iron spike. It was falling from the roof of the Pan-Turanian Military Command Center, Khoroto on the island of Skazka.\nThe spike that was the backbone of the Turkish-Soviet war machine was now a paperweight on $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s desk.\nIt was falling through a hole in space.\nBelow the hole were Earth and the Sun. Above was another hole. A spiral staircase of holes led out of one hole and down into another.\nOn every step of every stair were dead soldiers in both Blue and Red \u2014 soldiers in uniforms of every conceivable Earthling nation.\nThe spaces between the stairs terminated in a small round place, possibly Budapest.\nResting in the middle of the round place on a steel rod was a lavender crystal prison. In the prison was what looked like an old big-band singer from Earth\u2014a beautiful woman.\nHer name was $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nShe had written the instructions for Operation Zombie on a piece of paper before taking off for Mars. She had put the paper into a gold tube that she had borrowed from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. The gold tube was to be returned to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.", "0_22_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-nine in the evening</span>. Here was a crowd in which every person had an urgent reason for being somewhere else, and yet there was no movement, and the silence was loud, and the thoughts were loud. I played ball with my son in the backyard until he went to bed, at eight. It was like only Rhode Island was left in the world except for me, and I had found some way to be alone here by living in this room.\nPower was nothing. There was plenty of power. The phone system worked perfectly. The Empire of San Diego was not without defenders. The trouble was that there were two emperors and neither of them knew it. The people, one in ten miles, would not meet face to face and parley and come to an agreement.\nTheir reasons were good. They were bored with parleys. They had given in, given up, turned off, tuned out. If they were an army they had been broken, if they were folk singers they had been processed. Rumors flew. On the left it was said that police were attacking people who were trying to volunteer at our local Peace Corps office in case there was rioting\u2014to serve as nurses; on the right it was said that welfare mothers had rioted when their checks didn\u2019t...", "2_22_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"Holy cats, you got here fast.\"\nFern looked at his watch, a thermometer-faced digital with a taped crystal. \"9:48 Venus time,\" he said.\n\"How long have we got?\" the Space Wanderer asked.\n\"Ten minutes,\" said Fern. \"Have you got it?\"\n\"Of course I got it,\" said the Space Wanderer.\n\"You look unhappy, though,\" said Fern. \"Did you have any trouble?\"\n\"How much did you spend?\"\n\"It\u2019s a good omen,\" said Fern. \"I had this entire roll of fifties. I went to the bank and got it just before the riot started, while you were off seeing the Earth-lings. It\u2019s a very lucky roll of fifties.\" He held out the roll of bills. \"You\u2019re sure you got it?\"\n\"For God\u2019s sake, Ransom,\" said the Space Wanderer. \"Do I look like a kleptomaniac?\" He handed the drum down to Fern.", "2_15_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-six in the afternoon</span> and Lenore Beadsman was actually beginning to feel a little sleepy, now that she couldn\u2019t even feel her feet, let alone her breasts ...\n\u201cThank you for your patience,\u201d said the harsh robotic voice of the console screen at Lenore\u2019s elbow. \u201cPlease begin.\u201d\nAnd Lenore began.\nPart Two\n\u201cMaybe I did it because I sensed in him at least a temporary release from self, from those multitudinous, petty tyrannies of the self, with which those of us burdened easily deaden our waking days.\u201d\n\u2014J.D. SALINGER, \u201cDEPARTURES\u201d\n/a/\n\u201cYoo-hoo.\u201d\n\u201cBambi!\u201d\n\u201cRick! Rick! Rick!\u201d\n\u201cLookit Little $FIRSTNAMEF1 tee-hee-hee-ing.\u201d\n\u201cHoney you don\u2019t have to shout, we can all hear you. Just take it easy.\u201d\n\u201cLittle Barbara Bird!\u201d\n\u201cCan you believe it?\u201d", "2_22_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-four in the evening</span> on Mars. From the building next door came the sound of Chief Banks furiously sawing back and forth. Ten policemen were trying to keep him quiet.\n\"What brought me here?\" said $SURNAME1 from Earth.\n\"Yup,\" said the plainclothesman from Mars.\n\"What brought me here? Luck,\" said $SURNAME1. \"I was lucky.\"\n\"Yah,\" said the plainclothesman.\n\"What brought you here?\" said $SURNAME1.\nThere was a scraping sound as the plainclothesman leaned against a corner of the Tea Club & Giggle Parlor. He was opening an envelope that had just arrived by rocket mail. \"You first,\" he said.\n\"Well\u2014\" said $SURNAME1, rubbing his chin. \"When the First World War broke out, I was in the Peach Orchard, near Villers-Cotter\u00eats. I weighed a hundred and sixty pounds. I was running from hand grenades. I blundered into a buddy from the neighborhood who weighed six hundred pounds. He was running from German machine guns. We shared a foxhole for three months. It was a good foxhole, and nobody shot at us much.\"\n\"Uh-huh,\" said the plainclothesman.", "2_7_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-one in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been sober for seventy-one hours. He was seventeen hours short of the world\u2019s record for sobriety in a soldier. He doubted that he could make it.\nOn Mars, the world record for sobriety had been set by a soldier in Stonehenge, a soldier who had been sober for 153 hours. The soldier had done poorly on an aptitude test, had been given his choice of going to war or taking a bonus of twenty-five Earthling dollars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, too, would have taken the bonus.\nBefore the morning was out, $FIRSTNAMEB0 would take his bonus. A lot of Unks would take bonuses, in a great many barrackyards.\nThere would be a disturbance.\nThe disturbance would pass, and a shining and harmonious routine would be established again. But it would never quite be the same routine again. Unks would have put their funny bones in chains, would have pinned their souls up in the closet.\nThe harmonious routines of Mars would be a little more broken-down, a little more like the broken-down routines of Earth\u2014cold, filthy Earth, where funny bones and souls had more room to move around.", "0_8_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had not slept. His thoughts and dreams, after all these years, were still filled with Linda Fox. He read the communications one by one, looking for messages that had been altered in the transmitting centers of Titan. He found none.\nHis curious and extraordinary relationship with Titan had nothing to do with love, and yet it had lasted twenty-seven years. And now, having last night got the invitation from Salo, who was one of the little satellites of Saturn, to come at a certain time and place, he felt the same sense of excitement he had felt as a Post-Spark when first his messages were picked up by the space probe R tuna T4.\nA thousand things might happen, permitting their relationship to continue.\nIn childhood, $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been an unimportant and rather unloved child, so perhaps the relationship was simply this\u2014that a harmless little man was simply making the best of things the way a lonely child might.\nRagged, restless clouds crossed the face of the rising sun. A wind touched the parched lichens of the Martian desert through which $FIRSTNAMEB2 walked, carrying his tall, round-topped antenna. The antenna foot-high and fluted like a calumet, like a totem.", "1_5_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-nine in the morning</span>. As I put my head down on the desk and closed my burning eyes, I realized that, for perhaps the first time in my life, I was completely, gloriously, and irrevocably fucked. Drunker and higher than I ever believed possible, I had been reduced to the absolute rock\u2014bottom limit of pitiful, primitive, paralytic terror and confusion. I closed my eyes and groaned. \u201cOh, my God! Jesus Lord! What have I done?\u201d I was fully prepared to believe I\u2019d be spending the next year in a straightjacket in the mental ward of Fountain Valley Hospital\u2014and it didn\u2019t matter, because I was completely out of control.\n\u201cJesus!\u201d I muttered. \u201cI\u2019ve been looking for this all my goddamn life, and now that I\u2019ve finally found it, I can\u2019t handle it. Shit! I\u2019m truly in a world of shit.\"\nSeventeen. Before the Daytona 500 World\u2019s Greatest Auto Race, they hold what they call the \u201cGator Nationals\u201d\u2014a series of short\u2014track races for obscure drivers desperate enough to risk their cars on the biggest pavement Bobbsey...", "1_0_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after midnight</span>. I was alone on the terrace of the Caf\u00e9 de D\u00e9part, watching the muddy Seine flowing without noise, withoutcommotion beneath the granite embankment. At midnight the shutters of the bookstalls would clatter, the headlights of the taxis would form ascending and descending insect lines in the Rue de Rivoli, but now there was only the river.Laughter and scattered voices came from a curious interlude of silence. The taxi drivers were having a rest. There was a swirl in the water where the current ran strongest. The sky was moonless, was starless. The night was like velvet. I felt myself brushed by the wings of sleep, and I knew I must be up and doing, or I would fall asleep where I sat. There were things to do before I went to bed\u2014a letter I wanted to finish, now that I was alone, a strange feeling of loneliness coming over me from nowhere; from some forsaken alley a cat wailed, and from across the river I heard the siren of an approaching barge. I stood up. The water was cold. At the Island in midstream there was a light and a ferryboat rocking against the pier. The quais, the red roofs, the green tr...", "1_21_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>. In the basement the boiler was throbbing. Upstairs, without warning, the bell began to ring.\nThe bell was an old ship's bell, wired up in the foyer for the convenience of any citizens who for any reason whatsoever had to get into the building after hours. The bell didn't ring easily. It had to be swung on its gimbals, and swung hard.\nThe bell started to swing, and kept on swinging.\nSmith leaned toward his intercom. \"Damn,\" he said.\nThe sound of the bell filled the building. Outside, walking quickly, using the blessed shelter of the adjoining buildings to give her all possible privacy, a dark-haired, dark-complected woman in red came running along Calhoun Street.\nShe reached into her purse, found the eleven-dollar bill, rang the bell. Outside the building, the bell kept on swinging and ringing.\nInside, the hall door of a four-story frame house on a bleak and howling wilderness of a planet started to open, hesitated, started to close again, hesitated once more, then flew open. First an automatic flurry of blasts blew and snarled at the arrival of an opening door\u2014then a soprano voice yelled, \"All...", "0_19_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty in the evening</span> of a hot day in August, and Kenny stopped just for a moment on the steps of the white frame farmhouse where he was born and which his sixty-nine-year-old mother kept beautifully neat in the colonnaded kitchen. He was in the khaki uniform of the mobile National Guard unit assigned that week to enforcement of a 4-H Parade moratorium in a nearby village. He had just come off duty, and he was fixing to join a bunch of other fellas at the Red Wheel Bar, down the road.\nHe squinted up into the sun, and he said, \u201cAh, what the hell. I'll hitch-hike.\u201d And he walked due west, across the rolling alfalfa fields, not extremely faint but definitely visible from the candy-apple red suburban through whose windows we were staring.\n\u201cHe's had a few,\u201d said Tim Gray.\n\u201cWhat turns a regular fella like that into a priest?\u201d I asked. \u201cWhy does he want to go hitch-hiking in the summertime on the hottest day of the year?\u201d\n\u201cWhy do you care?\u201d\n\u201cHe's no good, and he always will...", "1_16_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-three in the afternoon</span>. We didn't have much time. It would be dark in five hours.\nThis was bad. We'd spent the whole morning blowing up pipes. Now we were faced with serious physical labour\u2014hauling what could easily turn out to be more than a ton of rock up slippery clay banks, 100 yards across a canyon, and loading it into the back end of a VW bus.\nI rejected the idea of calling for help. The entire operation would take less than an hour, I said; we should be able to manage it ourselves.\nThis seemed very clear to me, but my attorney disagreed. He said he was in awful physical shape, and that in any event there wasn't time to spare. It would be dark before we finished scraping up that shit.\n\u201cThe main problem,\u201d he said, \u201cis the time element. We absolutely have to get that stuff out of there before dark.\u201d\nFour twenty-four.\nThe first rock we pried up carried the most vicious curse I've ever heard: \u201cSON OF A BITCH! SON OF A FUCKING BITCH!\u201d\nFour forty-one.\n\u201cGoddamn, that's heavy! WHAT A BITCH! This...", "1_7_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-nine in the morning</span>. I stayed in the shower-stall, with the door locked, for almost a half-hour. I was watching the water run out of the bar of Camay when my attorney eased the door open three inches and said, \u201cThey\u2019re just sitting down. Good time to make a move.\u201d I took my Mace out of my open suitcase, then hurried out of the room, through the back exit and into the street. My attorney was waiting with the Whale near the outdoor sensor\u2014buzzer that opened the back gate. We crept into the slow line of traffic on Flamingo Road just as twenty dump\u2014trucks filled with gravel were unloosed on the roadway ahead of us. We had to inch our way between the trucks and huge heaps of gravel while they were spreading gravel across our bow. The blare of horns was relentless. For that one little piece of mind\u2014melting highway it was necessary to goad the Dolphin down to a crawl.\nWe ended up in the supermarket parking lot on the far side of the Miracle Mile. I got out of the car with my attorney and a tall black cop kicked open the back door on the driver\u2019s side and yelled, \u201cOn your feet!", "2_4_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe chute enwrapping Mrs. $SURNAME0 fluttered to Earth in back of the space ship, in back of the reviewing stand.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 stood on the reviewing stand, one foot on a chrome pedestal, the other foot on a circus ladder. His back was to the space ship. His arms were folded. He was smiling.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 landed in the crowd around the base of the reviewing stand, trampling several of the celebrants unmercifully. She bounced once, straightened her skirt, hurried up the ladder.\nWhen Mrs. $SURNAME0 reached the top of the ladder, $SURNAME0 turned, extended one hand to her, crushingly demonstrative in its casual display of strength. She put her hand in his, climbed the final step beside him.\nOne of the high points of crowd hysteria occurred just then, when cheers, jeers, hisses, catcalls, and lewd suggestions were blended together. This was the point at which the crowd discovered, to its displeasure, that it was all shifted suddenly into the future.", "1_1_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were cuddled in a sheet-steel culvert under the firing pits. The culvert was a part of a life-support system that ran from the basement of the barracks to the bottom of the pits.\nDuring barrack-hahn, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had slipped into the system, crawled through it on their bellies\u2014had run into no one, had heard no words spoken. At the foot of the firing pits were two ammunition dumps, ange gin parades, at which were seated a colorless $FIRSTNAMEB0 and a faceless $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mind pulsed with bewildering thoughts.\nHe lay there watching the stars through the sides of the culvert with eyes glazed and blank. He chuckled aimlessly at the memory of the man with the harmonium, at the memory of the harmonium itself, at the memory of a happy childhood on Earth. Earth\u2014it was a childish fancy. He had never been to Earth, never wanted to go to Earth.\nHe chuckled at the memory of a religious holiday called Christmas, a celebration characterized by great chewing and expectoration, joined in by crowds of bearded people.\nEarth\u2014he chuckled at the memory of bearded people, at...", "0_19_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-four in the evening</span>.\nThere were half-hours to go before the official beginning of night\u2014but this was the way night always began, here in Newport. The sun got fat, and self-indulgent, and mischievous.\nIt put on a show.\nAt 8:17 p.m. (ship\u2019s time) the sun looked like a split watermelon on the horizon.\nAt 8:22 p.m. (ship\u2019s time) the sun looked like an egg frying in bacon grease.\nAt 8:25 p.m. (ship\u2019s time) the sun looked like a bell pepper.\n8:27 p.m. (ship\u2019s time): The sun grew to the proportions of a small watermelon.\n8:30 p.m. (ship\u2019s time): The sun matched exactly two basketballs, side by side.\n8:33 p.m. (ship\u2019s time): The sun grew so plump and watery it couldn\u2019t hold itself together, anymore. At the edge it began to look wiggly, as if it were melting. The edges spread until the sun was like an exploded watermelon, except that the fragments held...", "2_12_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-eight p.m.</span>\nBut, compared with the harmoniums, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had a splendid capacity for variety.\nThe harmoniums in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bloodletting kit thought $FIRSTNAMEB0 was a stupid soldier for being afraid of only one thing\u2014just as they themselves were stupid soldiers for being afraid of only one thing.\nNeither $FIRSTNAMEB0 nor the harmoniums had the wit to be properly alarmed at their resemblance to each other.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s one human fear, which he shared with almost every other human who had ever been adequately surveyed on the subject, was a fear of mortal sexual disease. No one except a condemned criminal ever aspired to grow old, once the problem of aging in a biological society had been clarified.\nThat day in Miss Mona\u2019s apartment, $FIRSTNAMEB1 had told $FIRSTNAMEB0 of the magic dust.\nThe magic dust was a magic means by which $FIRSTNAMEB0 could avoid taking part in the terrible war. The magic dust was a way for $FIRSTNAMEB0 to avoid the biological mess squad and live a long, clean, sexually unparanoid life.", "0_1_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>. We were going very fast. Suddenly, with absolutely no warning, I became terribly conscious of the fact that my dear old aunt back in New York had been sitting in the dark on her edge of her flat, hotel bed, in the Broadway Central, all this time watching the hands of the clock on the bed table tick off all these various hours, and probably worrying about me. I had forgot my watch, but I knew she was sitting there. I knew she had been sitting there for about forty hours now and it suddenly occurred to me she might think I'd fallen in the river. With this, clear as a mountain stream, I suddenly remembered a line from a Welsh poem about some lady riding across a bridge over a turbulent and fierce river on horseback, with a lover behind her. Something is going to happen to them on the bridge\u2014but I can't remember what it was. But the line was something like this: Something is going to happen, Ma'am, said the f hewer; and she turned and looked at him, but she said nothing. Finally the line I wanted came to me: \u201cThe river's sleeping, Lady\u2014pray you, sleep you also.\" Just that line. It fitted my aunt. She was in her...", "2_12_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-three post meridiem</span>.\nThe system so far was working perfectly. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s reception was now being processed through a special control room in the main building of the Fortress. The control room was on the bottom floor, behind and below the Drum, Maxpie and Armory. It was reached by a stairway that began in a dumbwaiter shaft in a kitchen where the cook and her helpers made pastry, a secret known only to a few people.\nTechnicians in the control room watched $FIRSTNAMEB0 on the oscilloscopes.\nThey observed incoming infantrymen on oscilloscopes all the time. They checked their morale by watching their slow and ponderous twerls on the scope plates.\nThey saw right away that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was different. He was a jumper.\nTo secure his position as harmonious constitutive units of a Democratic Earthling United Nation, a soldier in motion was supposed to keep his head and give twirls until other units, responding to his example, joined him in an orderly procession. Then, and only then, was he free to dance.\nIn an orderly procession, he was to do all his dancing.", "0_23_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-four in the evening</span> by Redwine\u2019s gold Patek Philippe watch, which had once been part of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s stomach. $SURNAME1\u2019s name had appeared nowhere on the outside of the church. Perhaps ten persons inside the church knew that this was the dedication service for the $FIRSTNAMEB0 spread\u2014and none of those ten knew that $SURNAME1 had financed the whole thing.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had shown up for the service late. He had made his way painfully through the crowd, leaning on a heavy, sharp-pointed staff of mystery metal and mystery shape.\nWhat was going on rankled $FIRSTNAMEB0. It rankled him that the brain in the chest outside should be dry, should be plain brown meat instead of being lovely, moist red meat. It rankled him that he had had to give the antenna man such short shrift.\nThe antennas reminded him of the two creatures who had led him down to Earth and into the massacre at the hotel. The two creatures, surely, must have been antennas, too.\nIt rankled $FIRSTNAMEB0 that a perfectly capable human machine, such as himself, was at the mercy of creatures, however many of them there might be, who could expose the machine to a field of brown, could paralyze the machine...", "1_9_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAnd it was fine weather\u2014weather ideal for flying.\nI went over to the bar, at the far end of the room. A boy came with the list of wines and of cigars. He was gay and smiling.\n\"The count\u2019s been up for an hour,\" he said. \"He\u2019s flying to-day.\"\n\"That\u2019s pretty smart,\" I said.\nI asked him to bring me a ouchascious and a guaranteed Antarctica king. The brand was Gold-leaf. as it always is.\nThe count came over to my table.\n\"Good-morning,\" he said. \"Lovely weather.\"\n\"It certainly is.\"\n\"I hope you slept well?\"\n\"Splendidly.\"\nHe sat at the table with me.\n\"What will you do this morning?\" he asked.\n\"Shall we have lunch together?\"\n\"I think I\u2019ll go down to the aviation field, if you don\u2019t mind.\"\n\"That\u2019s quite all right. You know you can watch them take off and land.\"\n\"That\u2019s a joke, isn\u2019t it?\"\n\"Is it?\"\n\"If...", "0_16_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the angle of the sun was such that it beat strongly, directly down upon the palace and uncovered everything that lay in the shadow of the steep bank on the other side of the Plaza. Above the bank strong green leafy branches of trees on the far side of the river waved in the breeze. Beneath the bank within the shadow it was cool and in that pleasant moist darkness grape vines ran up to the very top of the bank, and hop vines and passion vines clambered in profusion. Of the many houses that climbed the bank, some were of masonry, some of sun-baked adobe, and some of heavy timber and trunks of trees, with roofs of red and green tiles. There were many stone stairways from one terrace to another, and all of these climbed close beside the sparkling, fast-running Pr\u00c3\u00a9sentisca River. Children were playing on the banks of the stream, above which the torn remnants of the mist still hung over the tops of the trees. The air was fresh and seemed as clean as water that had just been poured into a bowl.\nSeveral hundred yards above the palace, the bank flattened out and there was a great teepee-like tent built of timber. It overlooked the city.", "2_7_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> on Titan. There was a green dial above the door. It had fluorescent figures on it, and looked like a diver\u2019s watch on the inside of a clear plastic globe. It was supposed to show what time it was on Mars, Earth, and Fiasco, the home of the creatures who had given $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 their names. It showed a rutted and broken surface that no longer had much to do with time.\nThere was a lavatory in the room. $FIRSTNAMEB2 threw the bolt on its door, too, and opened it.\nA blue cast of cat-gut and the bright brass mouth of a bell dominated the room. The bell was on an immense candlestick. The candlestick was cast iron, octagonal, and three feet high.\nBoth window and lavatory seat were made of white porcelain.\nThe porcelain was decorated with tiny, gold-lined pictures of animals that had lived a hundred million years ago.\nThe pictures were perfectly dreadful. They had been painted by $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s mother, Salo.", "1_5_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was rigid in a tent-sleep, but he felt the light against his eyelids. He opened his eyes slowly in dream light. The time had come.\nThe light came from Titan, the largest of the satellites of Saturn\u2014one thousand two hundred and twenty-three kilometers in diameter, and as dense as water. Titan was whirling around the planet at fourteen hundred kilometers an hour.\nPlaying among the clouds of Titan were beautiful veins of fluorescent gas\u2014gas excited by the boyish antics of the planet Saturn and his moons. And among the clouds the giant moon showed its spikes and flecks of chromium-green, waxing and waning with a magnitude that would have terrified the peaceful occupant of any bedroom in any part of the Solar System.\nAs for $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, these variations in the beams did not disturb their slumber. They could not be wakened by anything short of an explosion.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had slept while $FIRSTNAMEB1 slept. And $FIRSTNAMEB1 would sleep while $FIRSTNAMEB2 slept.\nAt the end of the hour, $FIRSTNAMEB2 would assume the burden of watching, turning his head every three minutes, so that he could see in all directions the enigmatic blackness from which potential danger might come. He would...", "2_15_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The muddy gloom dribbled away. A pale-blue morning twinkle appeared in the windows.\n\u201cAh, you like to go, eh?\u201d said Henri to me.\nI said I'd like to go very much. \u201cWell then come along, come on.\u201d And $FIRSTNAMEB3 grabbed our wrists, rushed us to the nearest bar to buy two cognacs, one for him and one for each of us, and we sipped them, and in the rickety bar he suddenly behaved as though he were back in his father's bar in Denver, and called out, \u201cHey Moe!\u201d and \u201cHey Pinky!\u201d and told ribald stories. No one could hear him except the two baffled bartenders. \u201cCome on now ,\u201d he said, \u201clet's have another and go to my hotel and have dinner and turn on. What time you got, Hassel?\u201d\nIt was five minutes to four o'clock.\nAcross the street in his room he suddenly became absent-minded. \u201cDamn, I got to go down to Panama.\u201d\n\u201cWhen?\u201d", "0_19_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-nine in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 fired the pain-ray at $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nOh, $FIRSTNAMEB0! thought $FIRSTNAMEB1. Oh, UnQ! Oh\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB1! he thought. La-la! He whooped and hollered. (Whatever the hell that was supposed to mean.)\nIt was 735 p.m., Greenwich time, when $FIRSTNAMEB0 fired the pain-ray at $FIRSTNAMEB1. It was 4:35 p.m., Eastern Standard Time, in New York City when $FIRSTNAMEB1 went crazy.\nOnly an instant later, Stony Stevenson, the harmonium player and pillar salesman, was carried into the church, dead.\nOnly an instant after that, $FIRSTNAMEF1 and Brain $FIRSTNAMEF1 were carried in, unconscious.\nOnly an instant after that, the harmoniums stopped their singing. The balloons that were helping to hold up the roof became still, stopped surging or billowing. The big membrane held. The church was airtight again.\nAt 4:36 p.m., Eastern Standard Time, in New York City the whole storm began to pack up and move out. The storm was over. Whatever its lucid purpose had been, it had achieved it. It had cleanly stripped the world bare of a significant patina. The dark agents that...", "1_4_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.3 The place was Ocean Terminal, a mass of pinpoint lights nestled in the Virginia hamlet of Beachmont.\nA space ship was rested on the ground, pointed at the stars.\n\"Turn it off,\" said a man\u2019s voice, coming from nowhere in particular. A blue dot appeared on the nose of the space ship. It blossomed into a flickering strobe of blue\u2014red\u2014blue\u2014red\u2014blue.\nThe time was two minutes before five o\u2019clock in the morning. The place was a circle of light on a farm road in Barnstable County, Virginia. A man was standing there in the road, thumbing the stem of his pipe.\nThrough the circle of light came the sound of squealing brakes and the cough of an engine sputtering to a stop. A light burned brilliantly in a nearby building.\n\"You got her?\" the voice of the pipe-thumber snapped.\n\"I got her,\" said the driver of the car. He was not an old man and he was not a young man. He was a Major of the United States Space Force. \"Where do you want us to take her, sir?\"\nThe Major turned his back on the light of the building and faced the...", "1_21_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until ten o'clock in the evening</span>. At ten o'clock in the evening, the night before, was a man named Hermann, who had decided he wouldn't go to the war.\nAt ten o'clock in the evening tonight will be someone like Herman who will decide not to go to the war.\nHe looked at his watch in the moonlight.\nIn an hour a line of recruits would be starting for the railroad station under a submachine-gun escort.\nHe looked across the river at the dark mass of the town and he wanted to cross to it and go through it and come out the other side of it.\nHe had his knife out to open the package of stale cake he had bought. He had it in his hand. He looked at the silver handle on it in the moonlight, and remembered the moonlight on the Alps forty night before last.\nThe city was across the river, and he longed to get into it and move through the streets. It was strange that the city should be there and he not enter it. He could go by ferry. There was a free public ferry. But he had to go by the bridge, on the other side of which was the recruiting station and the lines of new soldiers starting out.\nHe would like...", "1_19_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe guide, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0 Americca\u2014said: \"I could tell you guys what that thing is, that place up there. It\u2019s not any Army scout ship. It\u2019s a flying water tower. They use them in water-poor war zones. Those three-foot antennae sticking out all over it\u2014they\u2019re radio antennae.\n\"When I used to be in the Army, I had a buddy who used to be a water boy on Mars. He told me the best way to keep from getting thirsty up there was to keep a radio by you\u2014that you could get your juice through the water-poor air by grabbing it with a antenna.\n\"Water\u2019s thicker than air!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He laughed uproariously.\nHis comrades were not amused. Probably none of them liked $FIRSTNAMEB0 very much. But they had absolutely no choice but to follow him.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was their leader. And they were on a hellish expedition to try to find out what had happened to them.\nIn the war they had found themselves in the laps of giddy hysteria and horror. Every known stimulus to those plates of jelly between their shoulders had been used on them in twenty-four...", "0_2_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty in the morning</span>. Galatoire's was deserted. Only one small table was occupied. At that table sat what seemed to be a horribly crippled old man. He had a huge hump on his back that forced him forever to face the right. His thin, spotted, ancient hands fumbled constantly with a piece of string. Lying before his drink was the strangest litter of objects I had ever seen--incongruous things: a doll's wristwatch, a compact, a paperweight, a gardenia, an antelope's foot, a glass eyedropper, a thermometer, a sprig of holly, and other things I couldn't immediately identify.\nAt the other table sat a businessman who had had a little too much to drink. He was effusive in a nervous way, and he kept explaining things to a girl who wanted to hear nothing.\n\"What are you trying to do, anyway?\" he finally said. \"What are you trying to do to me?\"\nShe laughed.\n\"Are you making fun of me?\"\n\"Oh, don't be mean! Why should I? Why should I want to hurt you?\"\n\"That's what I want to know.\"\n\"You're hurting me all the time.\"\n\"", "2_6_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six seventeen in the morning</span>.\n\"Unnerstand?\" said one of the men softly, looking up at the ceiling.\nHe was a thin man, with the eyes of a ten-year-old boy. His unkempt hair was orange, treated with a dioxinlike substance to give it this color, a trademark of the company for which he worked.\nThe lights of the train twinkled in the man\u2019s eyes, and in the dripping wet downstairs hallway of the office building.\n\"Heard she\u2019s sendin\u2019 flowers again,\" the thin man said. He was trying to draw an answer from one of his uninformative listerine-coloured colleagues. The other men twitched restlessly, not responding.\n\"Forget you heard it es my grandmother\u2019s, Bloom,\" the thin man said to himself.\nA thin, sharp woman was making her way through the tunnels in low heels. The tunnels were echoey with unseen Erie Lackawanna trains. The woman, who wore one sneakered foot and one shoe foot, wanted to reach her grandmother\u2019s office, fast. She turned corners dimly.", "2_6_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-three in the morning</span>, Greenwich mean time.\nThe funeral bell in the old stone church down the hill from the spaceport had begun to toll. The bell was for Earth, for the dead of Earth, for the soldiers of Earth, for the good and the bad, for the living and the dead.\nAt home, watching the spectacle on television, and taking part in the largest long-distance mourn ing parade in history, the people of Earth began to tear their clothing and daub ashes on their foreheads.\nEvery church in the United States with a stake in space received, unheard, a special dispensation from the Pope of Rome. The dispensation empowered the reverend gentlemen to cast aside the trappings of the Church Militant and to take up the symbol and accouterments of the Church Triumphant.\nIn Concord, Massachusetts, the Reverend Malcolm A. Whittlestaff improvised a set of flaming glad irons in the basement furnace of the First Church.\nIn New York City, the Reverend Jacob Clancy combined tin-foil and baling wire to produce a lumpy but serviceable halo. The foil was clumsily applied by the widows of Staten Island.", "0_3_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nLenore was sitting at the window of her room in Clarice\u2019s house in Shaker Heights, looking into her G. Stone Uniform Earth & Stone Corporation garden, waiting for four o\u2019clock. She was supposed to leave Cleveland as fast as she could at exactly four o\u2019clock, in order to get to the Erieview Plaza Hotel by seven, when it was possible that Mr. Bombardini might spring his trap and permit her to see her grandmother ... walk very desirable, long pale leg, black-stockinged limb, wet bottom, the bathroom door is heavy, grandma.\nIt was supposed to be the Bombardinis and their lawyers who were pacing around and staring into space, but Lenore was up, and feeling bad. Truly up, of late. Not at all the virtually spiritual sleep that had been achieved in the Bombardini Tower this afternoon. And now Cleveland was even blacker than usual and interested in giving off no light, which Lenore supposed was understandable, really, considering all the things currently happening. There was a new employee at the nursing home\u2014a huge, truculent matron who kept laughing in the face of Lenore\u2019s request for information. There was the unmistakable odor...", "2_18_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-seven in the evening</span>.\n\"My name is $FIRSTNAMEM2,\" said $SURNAME0 to the butler.\n\"Oh, thank you, sir,\" said Galoof. He advanced with a horseshoe watch on a gold necklace. \"Would you mind letting me see your watch?\" he said. \"I am so very sorry to bother you, but it is my job to be sure that everyone who comes through the reception line is on time.\"\n$SURNAME0 nodded mournfully. \"By all means,\" he said. He held out his gold wrist watch.\nGaloof took the watch, compared it in size to the little watch on his necklace. \"Thank you so much,\" he said.\nThe reception line was fifty yards long. $SURNAME0 and his dog and his son were the thirteenth to be entertained in a series that had already included two American football teams, sixteen deaf and dumb gymnasts, a pride of black lions, a pair of Siamese twins on diamond-encrusted leashes, and a brass band that had to be propelled in a wheelbarrow.", "0_7_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-nine in the morning</span>. Chronomentrophobia was the word that came to the mind of $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt that his soul was dribbling out of his pores.\nHe looked down at his hands. He couldn\u2019t imagine what those hands would do next.\n\"Go to your barrack,\" said the loudspeaker. \"Go to your barrack,\" it said again.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 marched on.\nThere wasn\u2019t anybody to barrack with. Like all the rest, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s particular three-hundred-and-forty-nine-man barrack was a one-man barracks. A barracks was a concrete cubicle twenty feet long, ten feet wide, and ten feet high. In the military architecture of Mars, it was the equivalent of an Army canvas tent.\nA bunk folded up from the wall. There were two bunks, side by side, because two men were supposed to share the barrack.\nThe mattress on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bunk was straw-filled, because it would compress and spring back into place no matter how a man might turn and toss and tear at the mattress ticking.\nThe mattress on the other bunk was composed of man-harvested mushrooms. The bugs in...", "2_5_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past five o'clock in the morning</span>. Our rented limousine was falling down some old broken highway in Texas, and somewhere to the right a giant fireball was falling towards the earth, spawned by Fat\u2019s Harley\u2014Davidson. We were going to be late for the Trade Mart Banquet and never get to meet President Nixon after all. A terrible prospect. I closed my eyes and saw the biggest country in the world disappear. Big D. Goodbye, Texas. Goodbye, Lone Star.\nTen minutes later it was really no worse. We had crossed some sort of b.g. in the road called Louisiana and were somewhere around the Shenendoah Valley when my attorney suddenly sat bolt upright behind the wheel and shouted, \u201cWatch out! Here come the Yankees!\" I rolled off the seat with the .357 in my hand\u2014pointed straight ahead, just as the first of many motorcycle cops roared up alongside our limousine, giving me a quick hard glance and then pegging it through the space where I had been sitting only a few micro\u2014seconds before.", "2_6_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-seven in the morning</span>. Sunlight slanted, golden and sparkling, into the nave all the way to the high altar.\nMiss Fern, having arrived in the nick of time, climbed into a pew with the commonalty, finding no empty space on the fringes of the Happy Abandoned. Miss Bacon and the three women who had been on the bench with her were given front-row seats, because their shoes were of sound leather and wool instead of plastic.\n$SURNAME0 sat in a chair on a special dais to one side of the altar, directly in front of the pulpit. $SURNAME1 sat close behind the dais, between two sanitation men.\nThe band played \"Shout, Sister, Shout!\" and the congregation joined in.\nThe lyrics of \"Shout, Sister, Shout!\" are as follows, and are sung to the tune of \"Yes! We Have No Bananas\":\nThe Parade Is a-Movin\u2019!\nThe Sun is a-Shinin\u2019!\nHallelujah!\nThe Lord has come!", "2_22_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> when $SURNAME1 reached the $SURNAME0 estate. The gates to the estate were locked, just as $SURNAME0 had said they would be. But only for form\u2019s sake. They were as flimsy as any iron Restoration gates in England.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 pushed them open and stepped greeting across the thin belt of sand outside. He was immediately greeted by the barking of the three puppies that belonged to $FIRSTNAMEB2, $FIRSTNAMEM1, and $FIRSTNAMEB4.\nMistress founded, almost shouting the words across the thin band of sand that separated her estate from the path on the outside. She was issuing instructions through the locked gate to a gardener who was headed home to Newport. \"I read it in the paper this morning,\" she said. \"Some gentleman was coming to discuss bringing the threat of hell fire back into space.\"\nThe gardener was hanging his head forward, submissively, so as not to see more than a glimpse of her through the bars of the gate. \"Yes, ma\u2019am,\" he said.\n\"You be sure to let me know when he gets here,\" said Mistress. She mentioned the sum that the gardener would receive for telling her.\n\"Yes, ma\u2019am,\" said the gardener.", "0_9_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. In twenty-three minutes, it would be 10:24. In twenty-three minutes, a bomb would go off that would destroy James Brannigan so completely that he would no longer be a problem to anyone.\n\"For heaven's sake, Mary,\" said Brannigan, \"let the thing go. Please. It\u2019s me. And I\u2019m in trouble.\"\n\"I thought I knew you,\" said Mrs. Brannigan. \"And all the time you were working for that horrible horrible group.\"\n\"I\u2019ve never worked for anybody, Mary,\" said Brannigan. \"I\u2019m not the working type.\"\nFor the first time, Mrs. Brannigan looked fully at her husband. Brannigan saw that her eyes were a little glassy.\n\"Oh, God,\" said Mrs. Brannigan. \"I had a feeling you might do something really awful someday. I put it out of my mind.\"\n\"Now, Mary,\" said Brannigan, \"now you know I\u2019m really a pretty good kind of a guy, really. This thing\u2014\"\nMrs. Brannigan glared at him.\n\"Put it out of your mind, Mary,\"...", "1_2_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> when I roared up to the empty Desert Inn parking lot. Everything was dark in the lobby except for a single desk light controlling a night watchman who was sound asleep. I shook him awake and asked for my key.\nI said I'd only be gone for a few minutes and would lock the car.\nThe Reno Country Club is only a few minutes away from the Desert Inn by car. I pulled into the club parking lot just as the limousine with the girl and the two boys inside passed by on the way in. The boy with the ski\u2014type mask was driving. They saw me, but they kept going. I never said a word to them, and they acted as if they didn't know me. I walked straight through the club to my room.\nIt was 3:30 when I knocked on the door. The girl opened the door very wide and stepped back. Instantly I saw that everyone was shit\u2014faced. \u201cHello there!\u201d I roared. \u201cI've come back!\u201d They stared at me in horror. The thought that they were busted flashed through everybody's mind. They figured they were busted on drug charges.\n\u201cYou have a party?\u201d I said. \u201cI thought...", "1_13_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Saturday, October 9, 1982. Friday the 8th had been dismissals for football-homecoming, and Rick's crowd had had the school to themselves for almost forty-eight hours. It happened on Saturdays that very few people used the combination basement corridor and spiral stairway behind the language lab for passage between thelab\u2019s second and third floors. Three flights up from the deserted basement corridor was R.V.\u2019s headquarters, a big room between the geography lab and the gym. In the room were a plywood board on sawhorses that served as a bar, an aftermarket antique radio,, a desk and chairs. Also, a copy machine and stolen master keys to the school. The keys opened doors to biology and the special-delivery room, with its game-day telegram lockers; to the showers and locker rooms in the gym; and to the padlocked maze beneath the gym floor that was the school's coal bin. And so in the space of forty-eight hours Rick had organized a party and prepared his meeting place\u2014and he had been free to stop in at the Gym and take note of the fact that on this day of all days there happened to be no teachers present. We have had an indication that the...", "2_9_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-one in the morning</span>.\nThere was a God in Heaven. The things that had happened since eight o\u2019clock had only confounded rather than disconfirmed His theory of creation.\nHe got up from the table and headed for the door.\nAt the door, His hair turned gray, and His skin turned yellow, and His fingers grew into talons. His course corrected, He went out into the hall, where a group of eager tourists awaited the signal to come on in.\nThe whistles blatted.\nThe visitors came on in. They were shown how their contributions were used: they were shown the children and their assignments. They were shown $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s crowded barracks.\nAll the children were sick in bed.\nThe visitors were shown $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s symmetrical, masturbatory exercises in the barracks. They were shown $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s perpetual free lease on the shower room. They were shown $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s recent court-martial.\nIt was all very sub rosa.\nNo one ever spoke to $FIRSTNAMEB0. One of the visitors whispered to another that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was reputed to be an imbecile. That was as close to an introduction as $FIRSTNAMEB0 ever got.", "1_23_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-two in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was coming up from a sleep granted him by heat, rust, and the sluggish or failed movements of the watchman.\nAt eleven thirty-two, $FIRSTNAMEB2 was supposed to shut off the equipment shed and check its doors and windows.\nHe did not make a very good job of it. He forgot to close one slatted window, through which someone might easily climb.\nIn addition, $FIRSTNAMEB2 did not check a single door or window.\nHe was not to blame.\nIt simply did not occur to him to check the doors and windows. Those things did not seem important any more. Everything was just right. Nobody needed to bother about anything.\nOnly a security guard, veteran of nineteen years on the job, could have been trusted to perform the most elementary duties faithfully. But Maldack and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were considerably more than nineteen years apart in age.\nAt eleven thirty-two, all systems were down\u2014all but the system that absorbed and re-transmitted signals to Earth. That system was functioning very well, but sending nothing to Earth.\nChapter 22\nALTHOUGH he was chronologically thirty-eight years old, a member of the Remo dynasty, and the iron-fisted ruler of the Martian cigar industry,...", "2_17_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> of that same fateful day. The three-minute alarm went off in $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s gilded bedchamber in the Taj Mahal.\n$SURNAME0 had been in the middle of undressing. He was down to his last garment, his shoes. The shoes were gilded boots. In a minute, he would be free of his earthly trappings and free to appear and disappear at one-minute intervals.\nHe bent down to slide off his shoes.\nHis maid, Salo, fluttered over him in a panic. \"Please, master \u2014 don\u2019t take your shoes off yet! The time isn\u2019t exactly right!\" Salo was a chrono-synclastic infundibulated Tralfamadorian buzzard. His design was noble, but his features were all borrowed from more handsome bird-lizards elsewhere in the universe.", "0_19_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven sixteen in the evening</span>.\nAt seven thirty-six, the real Tralfamadoreans were going to make their first appearance on Earth.\nTheir materialization in the Royal Hall of the Tralfamadorian Embassy was the master signal for a series of materializations by false Tralfamadorians all over the world. The timing was so arranged that the false Tralfamadorians would pour into the real Tralfamadorian Embassy the very moment the real Tralfamadorians arrived.\nConvinced that they were surrounded by their most faithful and devoted subjects, the real Tralfamadorians would then demonstrate their powers by beaming cartridges of nickel-choloride gas everywhere. The gas would kill everybody who had not recently worn contact lenses provided with pigmented right-circular lenses.\nWINNIE THE PORCELAIN DOLL SEES ALL, HEARS ALL, DREAMS ALL, REMEMBERS ALL. Winnie had not liked being a portable heating unit, but she liked being a ceramic statue in the lobby of the Tralfamadorian Embassy even less.\nThe lobby in which she was embedded, between the front door and the float with the dancingcolored-water-Skarron on it...", "1_14_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-two in the afternoon</span>. I stood in the shower a long time I ran the water as hot as I could stand it, and still the room got misty. When I came out of the shower I wrapped the towel around my waist and looked in the mirror. My face was sick and drawn, and there were teardrops in the corner of one eye. Oh, sin! Oh, sorry! I was like a child in his own room for the first time, looking things over and wondering. The towel whipped out from around my waist, and I saw it flap on the bathroom door in the steamy breeze from the shower. The mirror was wet and I did not return to the bed. I walked naked through the other rooms of the suite and looked at the funny paintings of dogs and cats and the mixed furniture. Everything was funny. Everything was wonderful. The mirror above the desk in the bedroom showed me a terrible body, big-boned and hairy. But what did I care, from the white tower of my madness? Tex! I cried out, and applauded. Who are you? What are you doing in New York? How did you get so tall? You will remember this day, Furman, as long as you live. What is your name? You walk...", "0_8_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span> of Earth's time, three days after Salo\u2019s visit to Tranch. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had stripped the derelict space ship of all useful parts. The creature waits for an opportunity to escape still fretted $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had just about finished his work. He was about to fling himself head first through the airlock of the space ship into a pit in Tranch. The pit was a cozy nest against the real cold of space, a nest just made for two or three. The nest was a hole in a blanket of sleeping blankets\u2014a hole the size and shape of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s escape plan was simple, cunning, risky, and efficient.\nThe cold of space had put $FIRSTNAMEB0 in an orange-peel mood. He giggled in spite of being frightened. As he squirmed his head through the hole in the blankets, he imagined how amused he would be at the joke he was about to play. He imagined how amused and appreciative Blubb, Crump, and Nab would be\u2014 once the blankets had been put back over the little hole in the blanket.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 giggled again. The joke he was about to play was this: That hideous bogeyman...", "2_6_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span> and the sun was up, but it was far from light. The dawn came slowly, out of a black gold sky, filtering down through the smog clouds over Luxor. The light came creeping over the obelisk and covered the Luxor rotunda with webbed shadows. The grass on the courthouse lawn grew dark and the fountain was a stone ghost with mist around it.\n\u201cSonofabitch,\u201d said the cop under his breath.\nI nodded. \u201cDid they get his license?\u201d", "0_1_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one nineteen in the morning</span>. The place was an odd junction of alleys in old North Las Vegas\u2014not far from where we had had lunch with Red $SURNAME0, and where a blind man had told us to go see a man in a wrecked drugstore about a message from Mars.\nThe spot was a hundred yards from the corner light at Hollywood and Junipero Serra Boulevard\u2014Hollywood on one side of the alley, Serra on the other.\nSaltonstall and I arrived at the spot from opposite directions. We both halted thirty feet from the heroic survivor of the alley\u2014a rusted, wide-mouthed, grainy-gray machine that had been painted milky white, with a faded red cross on one side. A red iron cross on top identified it as a first-aid station for emergency victims of traffic accidents.\nThe machine was an old ambulance, its motor forever turned off, its back doors forever opened.\nSaltonstall stepped on a pile of human manure in the center of the alley. As a policeman, he was not used to stepping in dung. It offended him as a practical joke on nature. \"What the hell kind of thanks is this?\" he said, peering into the manure. \"This has got my...", "1_7_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-nine in the morning</span>, twenty-eight days after President Kennedy was shot and eighteen days before the first battle in the Vietnam war. I was twenty-eight years old.\nThose were the facts. When I walked out of the office of the sports editor and into the hot, sweet, smoggy .air of Deskwood West that morning, I was not in a happy frame of mind. I had mixed emotions.\nIn the old days\u2014that is to say, about a week earlier\u2014I could simply have reacted to this situation with a violent reaction: I could have grabbed the sports editor by the throat and hurled him through the plate glass window of his office, with a satisfying crash. But this response was no longer open to me: my Human Response ticket had been used up; my seemingly limitless sources of dangerous, wholesale, irrational anger had been forever tapped out. Instead of a gross, physical reaction, I had in me some of my newer, subtler emotions\u2014those awakened by my recent, disenchanting spiritual experiences on drugs.\nThe first, most obvious thing I felt was hatred. Hatred of the city of Las Vegas. I was powerless to act on that hatred\u2014and powerless to explain or justify it. I just felt, with a terrible finality...", "0_19_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-six in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 pushed his way out of the mass of soldiers, found a place for himself at the parapet under a flaring gas-jet.\nThe thing to do now was wait\u2014wait for the night attack.\nAt eight-fifteen, the riflemen below $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s position sprang to ramrods and began tearing open packs of bandoleers. The commander shouted the command to charge, and the soldiers went bounding away over the moonlit desolation. Their purpose was to simulate a sortie.\nNoiselessly, real soldiers stole up behind them\u2014real soldiers whose faces were blackened, whose uniforms were black. These soldiers were armed with snub-nosed rocket grenade-launchers and were commanded by Corporal $SURNAME2.\nOther groups of real soldiers lay ready to pounce from every other trench and stronghold in the area.\nThe whole thing was meant to flush Clean Joe from his caves.\nThey actually did flush him.\nA half mile to the east, an orange and black figure actually did bound out of an attack trench. The soldiers surrounding the figure touched off all the rockets in their launcher stocks, turned the figure into a dancing, roaring torch.\nAt once, all the other caves...", "2_18_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-seven in the evening</span> by Earth time when the young man unscrewed the lid from a porcelain tureen whose contents were designed to prove that he had the stuff of a true believer.\nThe young man removed the lid from the tureen, revealing a solid mass of cold gravy, in the center of which, presumably, were two dozen cooked mussels.\nThis test, like all tests that would follow, was administered in near darkness.\nThere was not a light in the Arkansas parlor save the light of the young man\u2019s cruise missile as it lay, fully loaded, in the rack of his ironing board.\nRings of iron keys were hooked on the wall over the ironing board. And one iron key was hooked to a single finger of the young man, who was looking down into the cold gravy, turning the idea over in his mind as to whether he should dive right in.\nThe young man was naked. His name was not $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.", "0_17_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>. The bridge was thronged with commuters streaming out of the city. Remi Lime was humming softly to himself. The tune was popular in the 1920\u2019s and had been sung by Olaf Vine. Olaf sings it for me, Lovey, and for you. The tune was this:\nRemigio, love of my life\nOn the bridge I will wait\nThough the danger waits close by, I\u2019ll be brave\nAnd when danger waits by the tree\nOlaf will come to set me free.\nBeing a lime, Lime could hear strains of the song piping from the spin of his own tiny internal antenna; antenna that receives signals from the infinite galaxies and connects him to the rest of the universe. It was the same tune, of course, that the Little People were whistling to themselves back in the forest that summer morning, wending home from the forest-fire watch.\nChirp, chitter.\nGribbit, groodle.\nWocaw wocaw wocaw wocaw wocaw.\nThe animals were returning from their watch. Shellabarger was spending the night with his mistress, as was his wont. His wife was home in Swampscott.", "1_0_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nGreat trays full of mimosa branches stood around on the floor, with the withered remains of last year's mimosa leaves on them. Huge bowls of punch were on the floor. There was a big fuming iron boiler in the kitchenette section of the apartment, making hot water for tea. $SURNAME1 replenishings of fresh tea leaves came out of a canvas can lashed with a leather thong to the side of the boiler.\nThe apartment was full of young people -- beautiful, healthy young men and girls, and some of them quite wise and witty and pleasant.\nIn the long living room, $FIRSTNAMEB3 was sitting on a worn Persian carpet and telling the story of his life to a young married couple. His face was thin, thin, thin and beat. \u201cYes,\u201d the young husband kept saying to everything. His wife sat on a turkish towel and watched $FIRSTNAMEB3 hungrily -- everything he said, she nodded her head and put her finger up in the air.\nIn the other big room, an archway from the living room, beckoning Ted to follow, I walked alone. Jack Kerouac sat quietly in a chair with his beer and watched the same tremendous bebe dance that he'd been watching all evening in a...", "0_11_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty in the morning</span>. My attorney pulled the big white Vega over to the shoulder of Route 215, about one hundred yards east of the wide country\u2014entrance to Roy\u2019s. There was nothing out there but desert. All you could hear was the desolate hammering of the wind and an occasional ratchet of gravel against the side of the car as tumbleweed bounded across the two\u2014lane blacktop.\n\u201cMaybe it\u2019s the beer,\u201d he said, frowning. \u201cBut I sure as hell don\u2019t smell no bacon, if that\u2019s what you\u2019re worrying about.\"\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said. \u201cNot bacon. That\u2019s not what I want. Roy\u2019s Ribs and Saucer Piquante is the name of this place\u2014not Roy\u2019s Diner and Sweetmeat Buffet.\u201d He nodded. \u201cI see the sign,\u201d he said. \u201cBut that fucker doesn\u2019t say nothin\u2019 about ribs or saucers or piquante.\"\n\u201cThat\u2019s because it\u2019s in French,\u201d I said. \u201cThe building is shaped like a French chal...", "0_22_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>, and the place was the Sun Apartments, on Minnie Street in Bessemer, Michigan. I was sitting on the floor in front of the radio, talking to Douglas, my parole officer in Detroit, who had been listening to my every move for the past three months. He said he had just left my wife in the Iron Mountain hospital, where she was recovering from an overdose of sleeping pills.\n\"That's the second time,\" said Douglas. \"Where the hell are you, Bozo?\"\n\"Right now I'm in the Sun Apartments in Bessemer. How did you find out?\"\n\"I'm your parole officer, remember? I'm wired to you by a clairvoyant cat in Philadelphia. Where are you really?\"\n\"What's the matter? Is there something wrong?\"\n\"I'll give it to you in one word,\" said Douglas. \"Mechanical. That's the trouble.\"\n\"How's that?\" I said. \u201cWhat's mechanical about it, for Christ's sake?\"\n\"Why do you insist on declaring war on yourself?\" asked Douglas. \"Why don't you learn to like it? Shit, man, you messed up your wife, the Feds, and now the state...", "0_17_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-four in the afternoon</span> at the beginning of November. Now, lowering the window all the way and leaning my head out into the wind, I did not scream. I merely closed my eyes, and when I opened them again the buses were busses again, traveling the same way on the same highway.\nWe were skimming right along the edge of the sea now. The trolley poles passed far above the waves. Off to the left I could see the Boston->Montreal ferry I had taken and the Quebec->Windsor ferry I had missed, chugging away cheerfully in mid-channel. Then I noticed a whole fleet of ships on the American side, a fleet of rusty old-fashioned freighters lined up at wharves of limestone and cement. Some of the ships were loading, some were unloading, some were just standing there at ease. One of them was the greatest of all ocean liners, the Leviathan. Her keel had been laid in Europe, her funnel was high above the New England skyline, clouds of soot drifted from her four stacks. The Leviathan had been scheduled to sail at three o\u2019clock for Europe. I had come to watch her sail, but now I was busy so I lay back in my seat and closed my...", "2_2_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-four in the morning</span>.\nThe 303d Legion was defending itself from shacks by number 304 and 305. The attacking Germans were near the village of Bauschendorf.\nSince midnight, there had been skirmishing, shots that went wide and shots that killed. At first, there had been plenty of peering through the night at phantoms, at Bauschendorf, at the moon, at anything.\nNow the moon had set, and the soldiers lay in trenches and in holes, and though they were still nervous, they knew that the overwhelming majority of things that appeared in the night were not German conspirators sneaking up to attack.\nStill, things happened. A man jumped out of his hole with his gun, holding it at full cock, and walked right into the barrel of another man\u2019s gun, and there was an embarrassing situation. Later, a German flew an airplane over the flatlands and let go several flares, and that was a queer business.\nSomebody shouted an order to open fire, and the hundreds who had had no opportunity thus far to fire on an armed German fired away fast. But they were shooting at nothing. The German who had dropped the flares got away.", "0_19_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-three in the evening</span>. At that exact moment, whoever, or whatever, was reporting things to the field agents at Blister Lodge may have relayed to them just exactly how much $FIRSTNAMEB2 had to go through before he was free to take over the throne. It was all in the message, but it was in code. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was pleasant and puzzled, as he looked at the architectural drawings in his hands, because someone who cared liked to make him happy with useless presents. This, more than anything else, was Mayor Cardo\u2019s view of the case, behind his genial smile.\nThe message had to do with the plans $SURNAME1 held in his hands. The plans were for remodeling the palace of the Kings in Krackling. Since there was no longer an actual palace, or any throne to speak of, the plans were for imaginary renovations.\nOn the plans, where the throne room would have been, had been drawn a curious little scribble\u2014a scribble meaning nothing to anyone but the pigeons who delivered messages and the mighty few who received them.\nIt seemed that $FIRSTNAMEB2 was getting a scrubbing and a shampoo\u2014and a message, all at the same time.\nIt seemed that $FIRSTNAMEB2 was going to go through a lot of very unpleasant...", "1_13_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. The material prepared for the quietus of the pain-wracked, he love-possessed, fumbling $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Cleveland, Ohio, was in disarray.\n$SURNAME1, unshaven and wild of eye, their $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, sat in the big easy chair by the window, paralyzed by his love and his greed and his fear of having a clodSMASHed onto his head by a jealous God\u2014\nAnd there, in the red leather cafe chair, was $SURNAME0 yet again! smiling and twirling his gold watch chain, telling his wife not to worry a nickel\u2019s worth\u2014that he could put off telling the story of his life until $SURNAME1\u2019s pain could bear it.\n\"Skip it,\" said $SURNAME1 to $SURNAME0.\n\"Pardon me?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Skip it\u2014the story of your life,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Skip it until I\u2019m ready for it.\"\nThere was a little click in $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s throat. That click was the safety being taken off her anger. She turned, centipedelike, toward $SURNAME1. \"You can tell him to get out of this house,\" she said.\n\"Is that you...", "2_7_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven seventeen in the morning</span>.\nThe first real social occasion of the campaign occurred when the enemy attack on the flank guard was beaten off at a cost of only seventeen good men. The occasion was deemed important enough to rate the attendance of the Supreme Intelligence himself.\nThe Supreme Intelligence was so strange a creature that he could be frightening under some circumstances, even though he was a harmless creature, indeed, even though he was a friend. And he was very big, too\u2014big and strong.\nHe was four hundred yards long, twenty yards thick. He weighed forty thousand tons.\nIn the time before time, the Martian supreme intelligence had been a reef in the Gulf of Makassar. And much later, on Mars, he had been a fifteen-hundred-ton stone rampant lion, weather-worn but noble, on the steps of a governmental building in the city of Olympia. He had been brought to life by a mild Martian mystic named Momolin, who had lived in Olympia many centuries before the Christian era.\nThe Supreme Intelligence had kept watch and ward over Olympian society from the steps of that building for nine hundred and seventy-six years. Then he had suddenly died.", "0_1_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-nine in the morning</span>. The Flaturation hobbled on, past the spastic liquescent glow of an AmSearch night patrol car. Half a mile through the empty, radiant tunnel and they were out on the edge of Hill Street. Pacific Ocean, dark and vast, let the imagination swirl out across the Los Angeles Basin, up through the Malibu Hills and up, on up almost to the Santa Susanna and the little Oat Mountain observatory beyond. And at last, at the top of everything, still almost fourteen thousand feet below the satellite that sailed out there now, there was a pinpoint of violet light, actually two, an ethereal wheel, turning, a child\u2019s toy, a star from which everything, it seemed, was descended, a wheel dissolved in violet glow, starstuff pondering the stars. The wheel kept a sharp edge on all its rim. Only a star could do that. And only a wheel could have a pin straight edge. And there at last was the drug that really worked. The wheel, more than flesh. The wheel, more than wheel. The wheel that carried him out of himself into the sky, beyond looking, beyond caring, beyond all possibility of harm. Out here, wheel and sky were the same. Out here, his...", "1_5_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>. The apartment was located on Carnaby Street, London, England.\nWe sat there on the floor, backs to the wall, and waited for the shaking to stop. The glass front of the coffee table broke. Lula\u2019s colored pencils flew across the rug. We could hear trees falling and roofs crashing in the distance.\nThen the morning silence after it. In that silence we heard footsteps on the stairs, then knocking on the door.\n\"Who is it?\" said Lula.\n\"Pleasuring machine repairman,\" I said. \"Got contract. Machines broke down during last night\u2019s festivities.\"\n\"Go away,\" said Lula.\nBut the knocking and calling continued. This time it was my name that was called. I recognized the voice. It was my attorney. \"I\u2019ve been hit!\u201d he called. \u201cSend help! Foot still in the cast, infirmary bed,\" he yelled. \"For God\u2019s sake, open up!\"\n\"Who is it?\" said Lula.\n\"My attorney,\" I said. \u201cI must get to him.\"\n\"What about the machines?\" said Lula.\n\"I\u2019m afraid they...", "1_18_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-seven in the evening</span>, Zokko\u2019s Waterbomb Company had just closed its doors, and a single customer, the only customer all day, was hurrying off toward the T-Pass Yewnited sign at the far end of the lobby. A teenaged girl, walking toward the lobby doors as they opened in welcome, bumped into another set of opening doors, and either through extreme clumsiness or extreme fatigue almost fell. She righted herself, peered accusingly at the swinging doors, and went to sit down in the lobby on a white imitation leather couch. She was in bad shape. She was dirty, given to uncoordinated hand motions, wearing a tweed hat with holes in it, unshod, and her fingernails were down to the tips of her fingers. The dingy raw skin on her soles flaked in brown chunks as she moved awkwardly to the imitation leather couch. Some fat men, bespectacled, in white shirts and dark suits and holding cue-tips, paused to look at her as one of their number checked pockets for change. One of the men, apparently the only one without a billy, looked back at the girl as they went through the lobby doors that led to the Recreation Room. The girl...", "0_21_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine sixteen in the evening</span>. By the next morning, careful listening in Washington and around the world would have located him. The children of the brilliant Dr. Weatherhead and Lenore Beadsman were playing eighteen holes of golf on the moon at that very moment.\nThe tiny, frog-shaped lead object and the big, black, nebulous-looking iron object stood frozen in space, relative to one another. One looked odd, suspended. Beauty and the vanity of the black bar had quite passed: they mingled into a thing that was above desire and light, the kind of deep Union older brothers feel for younger ones, even when those brothers are punks. Impending Union stretched deep in the cold eyes of one, and wetness in the implacable eyes of the other. Steam rose gently from both.\nN.V.\n/d/\n\u201cStay off the story, Lenore. Stay off the story. You stay off the story.\u201d\n\u201cI have to go home to Lenore.\u201d\n\u201cFuck that. If you got that look on your face, it\u2019s because the story rubbed off on you. You\u2019re obsessing the story. It\u2019s vital that you stay off it.", "1_3_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>. The palace clock was striking as the Privy Councilors filed quietly into the granite-walled warehouse.\nThey stood at attention around the 20-yard square on the flat dusty concrete floor. The space was deserted.\nThe generals who commanded the battalions of the Royal Army\u2019s Parachute Infantry Formation had just reported to Major General Winfield Scott Haffen-berger, Commanding General, RAP-I. The commanders stood at ease before the red-faced, white-haired Commanding General of RAP-I.\n\"Now, I want every one of you to tell me just what so-and-so smells like, \" said the Commanding General.\nThe generals gasped.\n\"Thank you for coming, \" said the Commanding General. He had a very penetrating courtroom manner, and could command his field formations without ever having visited them. He was as much their child as he had been, growing up on a meager farm in Kansas, as he was the child of his own aging parents.\n\"Now look, damn it, be careful who you select,\" he said. \"Don\u2019t embarrass yourselves by insulting the very people we need so desperately in an election year. You know the saying\u2014...", "2_6_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six sixteen in the morning</span>. A full June sun was faintly visible, out of poor smog overhead, through one of the street-level access hatches in the Stonecipheco building. The sun lit up a small circle on the bottom of the ropy water, a circle around each man\u2019s foot. The men looked up uneasily, into shadows. Their nerves were objectless. Each man licked his lips.\nA new noise came out of the hatch, and merged into the full otherworldly racket from the building. The new noise was a thick, industrial hum. It was too loud for the humming. A train roared into the building, over the men\u2019s heads. The men on their haunches couldn\u2019t see anything, but twitching reflections of light, on the walls off the tunnel and on their coveralls and bags, merged into one abstract miasmatic reflection-map ripple.\nAn earth trembler rocked the tunnel. This happened whenever a train roared into the building.", "1_12_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve eighteen post meridiem</span>. The place was a room in the $SURNAME0 estate.\nThree persons occupied the room. One of them, a man, was sixty-five years of age, narrow-chested and podgy, with a lot of fine silver hair and a face that had been all but destroyed by sun, wind, grief, and thought. The two other persons were robots.\nOne robot was a woman with a flawless body. She had pinchable metal nipples, and her hair was so ingood that she could have sold it at a high price per ounce to any of the fussy space-age salons. The hair was red, but it changed colors every time its owner blinked\u2014changed from orange to green to blue to lavender. The housing enclosing the woman\u2019s brain was transparent, so that one could see the complex gears and ratchets that formed the circuity of the brain, and see that the eyes of the woman\u2019s brain were clamped shut in the exhaustion of Martian overdrive.\nThe second robot stood beside the woman, was also a man. He was twelve feet tall, and was graceful and handsome\u2014handsomer, say, than Barry Chanler, who was thought to be the world\u2019s hands...", "1_18_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past six o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was a rocky plain among the Saturnian rings. The sun, reflected through the transparent ice coating the planet Wandl, glinted among rocks.\nThe space was no more than fifteen feet in diameter.\nIt was crowded with people.\nThe people were crowded together so closely that they couldn\u2019t help touching.\nWho were these people?\nThey were Tralfamadorians.\nThere were thirty-seven males and fifty-two females. The age of these Tralfamadorians ranged from eighteen years to three months.\nOne of the three-month-old infants was female.\nOne of the eighteen-year-old males was blind. He held the hand of the three-month-old female so he wouldn\u2019t touch anyone accidentally and would know where everyone was. His name was Garlock.\nHis mother had the name Cottle.\nThere were twenty-seven rings in the space occupied by the Tralfamadorians. The rings were on the fingers of Tralfamadorians. These rings, according to the police of Earth, were part of the swindling apparatus of the Tralfamadorians. The rings were called magic rings on Earth.\nEach ring had its...", "1_7_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after seven o'clock in the morning</span>. It was a rainy morning, with clouds massed brooding beyond the hills of Oakland. I sat in a sodden fellow, waiting for $FIRSTNAMEB5 at the door of her hotel. A bellboy brought out a wet street-car rug and spread it over my knees. He shouted at me: \u201cGood morning, sir!\u201d and probably thought I was an English lord.\nWhile I waited for the red bus I remembered something I had completely forgotten, that Bill and I had started out to Paris in a bus that once had been red. The red bus came and we drove through good wet streets to the Oakland ferry. $FIRSTNAMEB5 sat in front with me. Behind were Eddy and Mike. We had just passed the Oakland city limits when Mike got sick all over the back of the seat.\n\u201cThat\u2019s all right,\u201d Eddy said. \u201cHe\u2019s old Mike. He always gets sick.\u201d\n\u201cHe\u2019s not old Mike,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. \u201cHe\u2019s just tight Mike.\u201d\n\u201cHey, Mike. Don\u2019t you say you\u2019re ill when you\u2019re not.\u201d\n\u201cHe doesn...", "2_2_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nIn the room between 2:34 and 5:03, $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas was to become acutely conscious of just how alone she was. But even without this added stress, she would simply as a private citizen have been concerned about the how and why of Inverarity\u2019s bequest. As a broker in probate might also be concerned. She\u2019d been named executrix, with the lawyer John R. Bender as coexecutor. Metzger had been named \u201cfriend of the estate.\u201d The will was already over a year old. Had Inverarity been planning to die all along. How simple if he had. But she remembered his last words: \u201cMetzger, you\u2019re in it now,\u201d and whatever else you wanted to make of that testamentary mood, it was not the farewell of a long-plotted suicide. Anyhow, with Inverarity, you couldn\u2019t tell. She decided to delay Bender\u2019s hearing in chambers on the probate no matter what, till she\u2019d sorted it out at least a little.", "0_20_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-seven in the evening</span> and the place was a counter in the bar of the $SURNAME0 estate. Under the counter were hidden speakers and checkers immersers and color organs, all connected with and controlled by a portable $SURNAME0 machine in Skip\u2019s pocket. The machine was programed to perform the miracle at twenty-three minutes and thirty-two seconds after every hour and fifty-two minutes and twenty seconds of every period when prevailing wind and waves permitted.\n$SURNAME0 wore a knitted ski cap, a face mask, and ski boots. Bentley\u2019s ankles were thickly wrapped in elastic bandages. The two of them, the dying $SURNAME0 and the ailing Bacon, had just been tobogganing on the slope of Mt. $SURNAME0.\nThe material on the counter between $SURNAME0 and the Machine was hardly up to the dignity of the ceremony. It was ham sandwiches, Queen Anne cheese, and Millstone Valley nut-nut punch.\nNone of the three had supper with his eyes. $SURNAME0 and the Machine drank and ate without raising their lids.\nNo word was spoken during the ceremony, and none of the three would have known, from anything that happened, that time was passing.\nThe ceremony took...", "0_5_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. The $FIRSTNAMEF1-keeper was finally out of the picture. He had disappeared from the face of the moon.\nEquations shot through the minds of the eight bone-weary Space Wanderers. In terms of Statistician parlance, the eight bone-weary Space Wanderers equated their prospects with the following:\nA = The real possibility that the periscope had been smashed beyond repair by a rock as big as Connecticut\nB = The likelihood that the Master of the Universe was not through with the periscope, and that a worse disaster was in the offing\nC = The fact that they had no way of knowing what might be happening back on Earth\nD = Their desperate need for food- and sleep\nE = The realization that they were still forty billion miles from Earth.\nF = The fact that the craters, from which they had just fled, were the most beautiful and cozy places in the Solar System\nG = The fact that the most comfortable place in all the Solar System was the bottom of a crater\nH = Unspoken\u2014that for the first time in their Space Wanderer careers, all eight of them were thinking of mutiny.\n\"... blasted space suit is leaking air like a sieve,\" said...", "2_11_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nThere were twelve members in the Skipafish Club, and they were having a meeting.\nThe meeting wasn\u2019t going well.\nIt was a big, ugly room full of big, ugly men. The men were trying to be cheerful and frivolous. And an utterly merciless telepathic critic kept putting cold water on all their attempts. The members drank like fiends, and their thoughts, as congealed in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s brain, made him turn pale and breathe hard. The men talked about hotels, cocktail parties, women, college, the East, books, Harvard, polo, divorce, Europe, divorce, canasta, magazine writing, divorce, taxes, Yale, Harvard, drinking, divorce . . .\nAt eleven twenty-nine, $FIRSTNAMEB0 found an empty chair, an old bentwood rocker whose wicker had split. He sat down, shut his eyes, and held his head. It was a more comfortable way to listen than looking.", "2_8_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-six in the morning</span>. And the place was the master bedroom of the Martian warren in West Barnstable, Massachusetts, U.S.A.\nThe sonorous voice of the house was singing in seven phase cycles, a Martian lullaby. And the walls of the bedroom were hideous with their mural of a Martian nursery, a mural so authentically Martian that it showed St. Bernard\u2019s lilies growing from green bicycle saddles and water pipedsewers.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 would have been unable to guess the purpose and meaning of the mural on the bedroom walls. But he knew very well who had painted it and put it up. It was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 himself. His grandfather.\nA tape recorder on the mahogany table was putting out the lullaby, the voice of which was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s sonorous pitch. The tape recorder also put out the flat, toneless voice of $SURNAME0\u2019s great-great-grandson, to whom the lullaby was thus illustratively pitched. The tape recorder was there unbidden. $SURNAME1 hadn\u2019t turned it on.", "0_12_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-nine p.m.</span> (Eastern War Time) just as a particularly horrible t-a attack, causing Mucho Mucho to pitch forward off the bar and crack his skull on the floor, was ending. Lang was in his cubicle, making a nearly economic use of cubicle time by transcribing recordings of pitch sessions onto IBM cards, when Klaypool barged in, unshaven and in the early stages of a bender, bursting to tell.\nSit, sang the police state, so Lang and Klaypool both squatted, facing each other, on the floor. The ominous militancy of all exercises in shared squatting had been noted in the weirder, very humanistic Cub Scout packs. Sitting on the floor, looking up as your den Mother lectured you about your duty to God and country, was how the Red Chinese indoctrinated American prisoners nowadays. Or maybe just flunked them out.\n\"Len, Len,\" fumbled Klaypool, hands making strained nervous gestures in all directions, as if borrowing someone\u2019s brow to gesticulate upon. \"The awful thing\u2014I just had it dinned into my head by that evil ex-wife of your colleague\u2019s\u2014that unspeakable Mucho-o. The...", "1_16_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nIn another ten minutes it would be dark.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 fought down a wild impulse to laugh. He shook his head pityingly.\nHe had the best-meanings excuse in the world for his crimes. He had had no way of knowing what the things he did were really like. Naturally he had thought they were wonderful. There was no way he could be held responsible for anything he had done\u2014except an unthinking impulse to laugh now and then.\nHe waited in the shadows.\nThe beam of a distant searchlight licked the stone blocks of the inner wall of the fort. $FIRSTNAMEB0 heard a company of footsteps on a stone floor directly overhead. The footsteps stopped.\nGentling a whisper, a sergeant called out ten names.\nThe names belonged to Unks.\nTen Unks obediently stepped out of the shadows of the barrack basement. The other Unks clapped the chosen ones jubilantly on the back. The lucky ones carried packs, wore suits of armor, bore rifles. They looked taller, sturdier, and altogether better than the others.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB1 and Bitland!\" whispered the sergeant. \"Furlong and Stine! Bancroft and Renniler!\" he...", "0_6_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to seven o'clock in the morning</span>. A minute later, the working day of all the rest of the human race, the little town of Ararat and everything in or on it was knocked flat with a rumble like the end of the world and a roar like the voice of God.\nRestored to its hiding place in the console of molecules in a gasoline berth of Ararat\u2019s only filling station, the copy of The Grapes of Wrath illustrated by Russell Lee vanished in a puff of violet light. One second later, the real book, the book carried by old Beatty across the parched fields, fell open naturally to the place marked by a slip of paper, and the slip of paper fell fluttering out.\nThe paper was a letter from a cousin who had invited a very lonely Beatty to come all the way to Indiana and stay with her. \"There will be someone here you can really talk to about books,\" she had written. She was a librarian in a small college town.\nOn the highway, at the very instant of the collapse of civilization at Red Fleet, Spiro Agnew, who had already been President of the United States for a fortnight, was describing to a group of Kansas City Rotarians the urbane, thorough, and authoritative manner in...", "2_20_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"You were stiff as a board,\" said the fat woman. \"But you\u2019re coming around.\"\nRandy looked at the fat woman. His eyeballs were like cannons.\n\"Grandpapa and Mama just arrived, hon,\" said the fat woman.\nRandy didn\u2019t answer.\n\"They\u2019re waiting for you downstairs in the lobby.\"\nRandy touched his cheek with his fingertips and found wet. He looked at his fingers. They were smeared with blood.\n\"Been sticking yourself doing somersaults,\" said the fat woman. \"Something went out of whack in there. Fixing you right up.\" She smiled, hoping to find common ground between them. She liked kids a whole lot, and thought anybody that age ought to.\nRandy began to breathe more naturally. He lay very relaxed on the stretcher.\n\"Where\u2019s the fire?\" asked the fat woman, trying a new tack.\nRandy felt at his cheek. His fingertips weren\u2019t bloody. There was no fire. He told the fat woman so. He wasn\u2019t talking to her. He was talking to himself. Talking to his own Memory.", "1_15_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-eight in the afternoon</span> and I was sitting in the car in front of our house in the suburbs of Denver. There were fresh spring flowers in the yard and the sun was shining and there was a happy singing sound of lawnmowers in the warm wind, and there were big white clouds scudding elegantly in an ocean of blue sky. I opened the front door and went into the living room and saw my beautiful wife, my even more beautiful child, my beautiful house, my beautiful life. I sat down in a chair and broke out crying. And even as I was crying I knew I wasn't really crying about any of these beautiful things, I was crying about something else. The sobs wracked me, my hands fluttered and squirmed like living things trying to get free. In a sort of dull, dumb way I knew what it was all about. It was about a finite scene and an infinite scene, about a closed scene and an open one. It was about a pinned-down man and a flying man. It was about life, young and full of everything, stacking up neatly in the world, and life which was old, very old; and poor; and tired; and ragged; and going on. And being just here. At this moment. At this...", "1_13_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the taxi finally reached the port of New York. The ship was crowded with people. It was the main departure day. There was no conflict about tickets. The taxi driver paid our balance with a big shrug and one of his business cards that said, TAKE ME WITH YOU\u2014DEAN M.\nI gave him twenty dollars. He looked blank, got his logic going, decided it was a very big tip, and thanked me dutifully. He stood and watched the ship get under way.\nFasten your seat belts, it's going to be a BUMPY night. A brief prefatory note on how to enjoy this USA saga: it should be read, say, in the winter in a room with a coal fire and a lamp left lit on purpose. All vagabonds of all nations are welcome. If you don't understand a few words here and there don't worry. \u201cWhorehouse\u201d merely refers to a place where you can go to be happy. \u201cJim-Jam\u201d means a bad inning. You can dig the whole procedure by imagining yourself seated in the wings of the theatre and watching. That's what it's about, a bittersweet comedy with rushlights and a hollow tree.", "0_20_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after eight o'clock in the evening</span>. Everyone had gone home at six o'clock, according to orders. $FIRSTNAMEF0, having stayed on to type some letters that were overdue, was tired, hungry, and cross. She had no one to talk to. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the luckiest of men, had a world of his own, a lucent, gold-paved, singing world, in which to saunter, a world that was $SURNAME1\u2019s Dunhill Cavalier at swift ease among the faint and faltering forms of this fabled Earth.\n$SURNAME1 sat in his deep blue pebbled-steel Cadillac in the paved driveway that led from the front of the building. He sat in the Cadillac, not because he had anything to do there, but because he was unable to bear doing nothing any longer.\n\"Shall I drive you home, sir?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n$SURNAME1 looked up at the building and then down again at the photograph of a space ship that had been lent to him by the photographers of Galaxy magazine. The lines of the ship were blurred and smeary, as though the cameraman had had trouble focusing. The ship was one of the spidery flyers from Mars. The caption said simply, \"Bogey ship from Mars?\"", "1_5_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-four in the morning</span>. My attorney was snoring peacefully, his feet propped up on the dash. Our little shack\u2014which our lawyer wanted so much to control\u2014lay somewhere in the darkness outside. I tumbled out of the machine and took a long walk south along the highway, looking for another liquor store. It was a calm dawn on the desert, with the chilly fragrances of sagebrush on the wind. The mountains in the distance loomed like surf. The big dipper hung directly overhead. I drank four quick vodkas, then rushed back to the car and seized control again. It was time to get the hell out of the ghetto while the sun was coming up. III We spent most of the morning trying to figure out just where we were on the map. We were lost, but we found ourselves on a long straight road that ran right down the arrowstraight middle of Death Valley and headed for the Amargosa \u201cSea\u201d\u2014a place we had never heard of before. But it was apparently worth going out of your way for Some kindof weird museum, made out of driftwood and old washing machines, with a collection of tin foil signs warning off the aliens from outer space. In addition, our road ran right past Watts Well, a...", "2_19_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had been picking a path through the blueberries for sixty-eight minutes. He had been counting the minutes, which he hated to do, because the minutes reminded him that $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 was locked up somewhere on Titan, shrinking in time forty times as fast as Earthly time. The minutes also reminded him that he had been tricked into spending his only vitality on a part of the Universe that had both a microscopic meaning for the Universe as well as a repellent magnetism for $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nHe stopped picking his path through the blueberries to give himself a rest. He leaned on his big bamboo bent-fork, rested his chin on his hands.\n\"Okay, Universe,\" he said, \"I\u2019ll play it the way you want it. What do you want me to do now?\"", "2_1_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, on Mars, had eaten his bread and honey, had drunk his water. His belt was empty. He had nothing to feed his child.\nHe did not yet know it was a child.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been in one materialization, since the one that shot him to Mars. Then, there had been nothing but darkness, noise, terror.\nThat materialization had lasted three and one-quarter minutes.\nThe time was one twenty-eight in the morning.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 pressed his ear against a vent that pierced the door. He heard $SURNAME0 and the materialization talking as plainly as if they were in the room with him.\n$SURNAME0 was talking. \"We are about to have a visitor, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" he said. \"A messenger is on his way from Earth\u2014intending to surprise you. He isn\u2019t going to be able to, of course. But I thought you might like to know that a friend is coming. This is a friend you had once before, a very unusual friend.\"\nThe materialization was a feather duster in the cleaning cupboard on the wall\u2014the other side of the door.", "2_17_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when they were outside the Los Angeles Airport, and she got off the conveyor belt and away from the man, who remained seated, rapping paternally to the radio: \"You hang in there, little ole you. That\u2019s medicine. You take your medicine; you just dig in. Let it do the work.\"\nNOTHER half an hour, and she was by the bedside of poor Sir Gerald, who opened grotesquely puffed eyes to see her. But did not smile. Nor did she.", "1_13_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty in the afternoon</span>. We were still on the road! Somewhere in the back of Ed Dunkel's mind was the thought that $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I would not let him down when the time came; that he did not have to say a single thing to us; we would agree with whatever he said; that all that was necessary was to sit back, relax his body, and let things take their natural course. While $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I sweated it out and sweated it out, Dunkel was silent, biding his time. I looked at him closely and realized; it was so obvious, he didn't like us; and it wasn't that he didn't like us personally but that, with the evils and miseries of the world worrying his spirit and his Jewish radical mind, he looked down on us with a kind of wry amazement for our childish non-concern about details of existence -- and what he partially saw, or thought he saw, we being so divided and frantically analytical inside our minds, was that neither $FIRSTNAMEB3 nor I had a single solitary thing in the world we really believed in except the pleasure of hanging out with each other. He was right. There was nothing else. What a discovery! What happened to our atom bomb? What happened to our gang? What happened...", "2_12_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-three p.m.</span> Earth time.\nLife was funny.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was looking for something he would never find, and yet was sure he would find. Stony was acting out a part that very nearly wasn\u2019t funny\u2014and yet was funny, as an explosion is funny.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 clearly expected Stony, $FIRSTNAMEB1, and the real $FIRSTNAMEB0 to arrive at the Far analgesic station, together, within a minute or two. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s message was directed to that absurd and yet wonderful assumption\u2014that another $FIRSTNAMEB0, another $FIRSTNAMEB1, and another Stony were actually on their way.\nStowaway commanders often felt abandoned at the beginning of a space voyage. And almost always, the commander felt loved again by the time he finished the voyage.\nThe timetable called for the three loyal astronauts to board the space ship at twelve thirty-six. That would give them six minutes in which to eat, urinate, pray, philosophize, mate, consummate mate, snarl, and kiss their loved ones goodbye\u2014and then blast off.", "0_15_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-three in the afternoon</span>, and the heat was pleasant. I was wearing a hand-painted Japanese yukata, all reds and golds, and my newly shined moccasins. It\u2019s a wonderful thing to have everything clean, I thought; it\u2019s the most wonderful thing in the world. I took a hot bath and swam a little and ate another Japanese meal under the flowering apple tree, watched the zen-priest. He was smoking his pipe, blowing the smoke at an angle to catch the light breeze, so it formed a matrix in the still, hot air.\nI had just washed the dishes and was putting away the plates when $FIRSTNAMEB3 appeared, stepping high over the sunny grass, his hat cocked over his sweating brown face, happy, singing, rocking to and fro. He carried his arm around my shoulders.\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, ah-h, you\u2019re my best buddy, old $FIRSTNAMEB6, you know that, don\u2019t you know that?\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s right, man.\u201d\n\u201cI feel GOOD , I can\u2019t believe it, I feel GOOD .\u201d He danced over to the zen-priest, who was watching us...", "1_6_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-five in the morning</span>. I had been driving now for eleven hours and I wouldn't be able to sleep at home until dawn. I drove across Oceanside and parked downtown in front of the Wash Tub Laundry. They were just limbering up for the day. A mustached man with sleepy eyes rolled the steam roller back and forth, flattening piles of dirty coveralls. A fat Mexican woman sat in a chair, going over a ledger book. A skinny little chinese polack sauntered down an alley wearing a brown worker's smock with an AFL label in the side pocket.\nI walked one block up to the parking spot I'd left my Wagon when I went to work the night before. There was a ticket on it and a note:\n\u201cHi!\n\u201cWe just towed the car in order to help out a buddy of ours in the sheriff's office. It's a hundred-buck fine and ten days in jail if you get caught driving this without a license. The rates have gone up, so you have to leave us an extra buck if you want to park here again. Relax with us awhile; you've already been around enough circles for one day.\n\u201cHope you're feeling better.\n\u201cSince...", "0_20_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-nine in the evening</span>. My attorney was bearing down on the Mint 400 with The Whale, twenty miles of traffic behind him, mostly driven by people who had heard about the gorgeous \u201crave\u2014up\u201d at the Las Vegas Hilton and wanted to see it for themselves. My attorney\u2019s head was full of acid venom. He wanted \u201cjust one more confrontation\u201d with those bastards at the tabloid desk. Somehow he would make them admit they were crooked. Or maybe he planned to steal back the money they\u2019d paid him for the Doris Day fuck\u2014book. I had no idea what he had in mind. At that moment, forty\u2014five minutes after leaving the hotel, we were sitting in the parking lot of a Howard Johnson\u2019s on the edge of the Vegas sprawl, trying to decide what to do next. I had called a friend at the Journal\u2014American, but my friend was out to dinner; nobody knew when he would be back in the office.\nI had mixed emotions about the fuck\u2014book scam, especially after the bad turn it had taken at the hotel. I almost felt sorry for these skinny hoodlums who had nothing better to do than rip\u2014off baseball players and movie box\u2014office stars. On the other...", "0_17_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-six in the afternoon</span>. I leaned my bike against the high iron fence around my yard and put the key in the padlock. My mother opened the screen door, looked at me. She was in a rayon housedress, elbows on the table, feet up on the Sears & Roebuck sewing machine, sipping a cup of coffee; she was making a new skirt for my sister Alafair, who was graduating from the fifth grade. \u201cYou're just in time for supper,\u201d she said. \u201cThat fish smell outside. What is it?\u201d\n\u201cSalmon,\u201d I said. I hung my hat and whipcord GI blouse over the back of a straight chair and opened the kitchen door and picked up the two aluminum trays of cooked food my mother had fixed for me. Familiar things. Baked potatoes in a pot of scalding water, the skins shriveled, saucepan of chopped parsley, a can of peas, napkin neatly folded in the potato water; carrots peeled and sliced thin, glass of water, bowl of dill pickles; raw oatmeal mixed with molasses, the brown bulb of a light bulb over it shaded, electric hand-beater plugged into the socket...", "2_17_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA one-way loop was an aspect of one vision of an evolving, nonteleological God.\nIn the third place, $SURNAME0\u2019s question carried the simple observation that there is no such thing as happiness. Everyone who has ever been the least bit happy knows that.\nIt was four minutes until six o\u2019clock in the evening.\n\"What did you say, Mr. $SURNAME0?\" said Salo, hoping that by pretending he hadn\u2019t heard, he could keep from being dragged into an argument.\n\"You\u2019re not happy, are you?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Not particularly, no,\" said Salo stiffly. He fanned his ventilation surfaces a little\u2014kept them moving.\n\"I thought not,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"It would be a crime for me to be happy,\" said Salo. The surfaces under his wide brown eyes were wet.", "0_3_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-five in the morning</span>, and as we finally lurched into the motel on Route 66, I realized that my keenness to buy every monster of abstract thought I could lay my hands on was forcing poor sweet semi-crippled $FIRSTNAMEB3 to go practically insane in the sleepy silence of the car -- with me jabbering endlessly about life and beauty in the super nada of everything. \u201cDig that red neon sign,\u201d I said, \u201cit says \u2018Motel.\u2019 Let's get a place and go to sleep and GET UP EARLY AND GET TO CALIFORNIA.\u201d And $FIRSTNAMEB3 nodded in silence and agreed and said nothing. He knew I had to go mad this way, just as I knew he had to go mad his way. And I knew $FIRSTNAMEF4 had to go mad her way; a girl who flips out and follows a drummer with a tattoo and becomes a legend in other innocent places can't ever go back to normal reality again. All there is to the errand, as Augie March said, is to keep going. \u201cDown? Where we going, Daed?\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 asked in his sleep. I was already in bed and just about to doze off on the warm virginity of a motel pillow.", "0_23_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> when there was a knock on the door of Room 233 of the Buena Vista Hotel.\n\"Perfect time,\" said the man in the room.\nHe wheeled around, his bathrobe flapping, to decide who would answer the knock. His wife, turning over on the bed, opened one eye and then let it flutter shut again.\nThere would be some trouble finding attendants this Saturday night/Saturday morning, at this end of the conference. Five out of six men at the banquet had gone directly from the banquet to the bar. Five of the remaining attendants had gone directly from the banquet to the room of Grace Crabwell, the girl in the white sweater and blue skirt. None of the five had yet emerged from her room. At least, not yet.\nThe sixth man still at the bar had also gone to see Grace Crabwell\u2014or, at least, to see if he could see her. His failure, so far, had made him much less jovial than the people still enjoying the company of this dark and slender mathematician who, according to her picture in the program, was twenty-four years old. Dr. Robert Romo was a thirty-six-year-old physicist.\n\"Who is it?\" said the woman in...", "1_21_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-six in the evening</span>. I could spend the next five hours in bed. I wanted to see $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Galatea before they left for Italy . At that moment he and Carlo were cruising in the car around town. I got dressed and went downstairs. The elevator man told me the couple that had just checked out had left a strange little message for me: \u201cIf you get back tonight please come up to see us before you go to bed.\u201d It figured to be Aspen Tw Flo in person.\nOr else it was my aunt Gretchen in one of her prophetic moods at that, for the handwriting was unmistakably hers, and as I went up to our apartment above the Minetta Tavern I was greeted by a vision in the dim light. It was my aunt Gretchen, come all the way from Denver , Colorado , to see her favorite nephew. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d she said, \u201care you going away this week end?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cAnd you're going to be with $FIRSTNAMEB3, Carlo, and somebody named Al Greco?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d I was impressed and had to go down and see $FIRSTNAMEB3 immediately, to talk about the witch of thirty-eight, who...", "2_4_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>. Inside the house, Lord Woodworm Guinness, Chief of Protocol of the Royal Court of San Whitboy and one-man Com at the Celestial Court of Justice, was overjoyed to find himself in his own bed. He had dragged himself up the stairs after hours of drinking, carried, indeed, by the strength of his own arms. Lee and Wilmer and Metzger had carried Woodworm up to bed, but now he was home. He removed his shoes, put them on his bedside table, looked at them a long, long time. Then he fell into that deep sleep that follows a great debauch.", "0_17_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-one in the afternoon</span> after the near execution of mrs. bulber at the hands of ten-year-old waves sent by the vibration of mona\u2019s mobile against the pleasure center of mona\u2019s pre-frontal lobe, at a time when mona was preoccupied only with so much un interrupted sleep. And the place, of course, was the headquarters of the Bombardini Company, in a warehouse, off in the industrial wastes of East Corinth, near the stone bridge. The warehouse was large, its sides and high ceiling a maze of scaffolding, supports, weirdly lit by the lights of countless sawhorses and high ladders, with strangely accented figures moving around them, in the process of some tremendous renovation. Workmen high on ladders painted the beams. Other men \u2014at the base of the ladders\u2014moved telephone and electricity cables. Concrete was being poured. Behind Lenore and Candy Mandible were three large bay doors made of iron and set with rivets that were also being painted. And there were smells, paint smells and more industrial kinds of smells depending on the wind, and sawdust. Heavy thunder was in the air up near the ceiling. Thunder occasionally rolled near the ceiling. Out past the doors the docks...", "0_21_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span> when $SURNAME1, looking up at the beautiful planet that was his\u2014and wishing that he were any place but where he was\u2014made out a female form standing alone by the sentinel golden trees. It was a dark, clear, fine night.\nHe looked at the female form. He appreciated the natural grace of a good-looking woman, whether man or woman.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, large though he was and clumsy though he acted, had two remarkable qualities.\nThe first was a sense of elegance that came from the pleasure he got from seeing a beautiful woman elegantly at her ease.\nThe second remarkable quality was the imaginative pursuit of that pleasure.\nThere stood $SURNAME0\u2019s wife, contentedly alone, peaceful, exquisitely clean in the radiance of the planet, of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s achievement, of the Universe of Suns. And $SURNAME1 imagined what it would be like to make love to his mate with the Universe of Suns as a backdrop.\nHe was tired of women who wouldn\u2019t or couldn\u2019t see the Universe of Suns as a backdrop.\nThis was his mate, his wasp-like mate, who had everything\u2014everything that brains and breeding could give. In all their earthly settings,...", "2_11_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Hello,\" said the young man on the bicycle.\n\"Hello,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Mr. $SURNAME1?\" said the bicyclist to $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Come in. Sit down. Are you one of the process servers?\"\n\"No,\" said the bicyclist. He was tall, and he was wearing chino cloth slacks and loafers without socks. He was shirtless, and his torso was muscular without being large. His hair was red and long, and wild, and it was coming out of a green wool knit skullcap. \"I\u2019m not a process server,\" he said. \"I\u2019m Metzger. $FIRSTNAMEB2.\"\n\"The chimp,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"The one who got us all into this.\"\n\"Get out of here,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"$FIRSTNAMEF0, throw this creep out the window.\"\n\"Please listen,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"I understand you don\u2019t want to listen. I know I\u2019ve got a lot to answer for. I\u2019ll try to do that. But first will you please explain something to me? Are we all out of our minds?\"", "2_6_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>. The single middle-western church-tower bell had just finished donging.\n\"Is my wife all right?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Yes,\" said Brud.\n\"All right\u2014wherever she is?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Yes,\" said Brud.\n\"What about the Martian space ship that was supposed to land this morning?\" said $SURNAME1. \"Is it still on schedule?\"\n\"I couldn\u2019t say, sir,\" said Brud.\n\"Is something supposed to happen at seven o\u2019clock?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I couldn\u2019t say, sir,\" said Brud.\nA small rectangle of cardboard was handed to Brud. It was an envelope of ordinary thickness, but it had been folded and refolded a great many times.\n\"From your wife, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said Brud.\n$SURNAME1 tore open the envelope. He discovered a tightly-written letter on a single sheet of paper. The letter had been written half an inch from the bottom of a twenty-four-inch-long by eighteen-inch-wide sheet. The top half of the sheet was blank.", "0_8_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty in the morning</span>. I was looking for Longmont, Colorado, on an old U.S. shield with black legend on white background that I'd found in the hotel dumpster. There was an identical new one under it, along with two or three other obsolete signs that I'd salvaged for use as reading material. Longmont had a population of about 3500 and was somewhere around here in the Middle West somewhere, but I couldn't pick it out on the map. It might have been Des Moines, only lower, if Des Moines was in the foothills of the Niobraras. I'd left town in a rush to get lost, and I was lost.\nWe'd kept going west from Iowan dawn to Iowan dawn, through elfin fingers of mist that curled through endless Quonset huts that reached to the gray heavens. Iowa is full of these forlorn little towns withered on the prairie and rinsed by rains -- little towns that were once steamboat landings on the Mississippi Route that is now mostly pavement. And I thought of Huck and Tom, not laughing now, but bones in the grave instead, and something walked over my grave, too. It was Halloween, the dead man's day. In Sioux City, Iowa...", "0_17_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> at San Juan Airport when we found the plane. It was the sort of thing you might mistake for a hamburger. It was an airplane. It was standing there on the field, all by itself, with no one around and no sign of life.\n\u201cThere it is!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3, hitting seventy on the taillights of a parked truck as we roared up next to the Lockheed. But before he could lean across me to open the door a long line of men had formed in our wake and I realized they were airport officials come to see who had stolen the sacred airplane.\n\u201cQuick!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3 to me. \u201cSlip them all some money. They've come up to count the plane and it's all over. Tell them it's your plane. Give them money!\u201d I gave them all money -- a dollar a man. As they took one look at our disheveled hair and generally weird appearance they knew without asking who we were and why we were there.\n\u201cEntiende?\u201d they said. \u201cYes, understand. Take your plane! Go quick!\u201d This was a most modern airport with the most modern take-off facilities for private airplanes -- the best...", "0_9_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 had come out of the hangar with me at this time because the ship was about to take off for Titan.\nThey were on their way to the control building. I was headed for my hole.\n\"Hey, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes sir?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"How do you feel?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"how anybody\u2019s supposed to feel when they get ready to fly to another world.\"\n\"You\u2019re all a-quiver, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Your hands are shaking, and your knees are shaking.\"\n\"I know it,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"and I can\u2019t help it.\"\n\"It\u2019s fear!\" said $SURNAME0. \"You\u2019re afraid to go up there and let them bugger you.\"\n\"I\u2019m afraid, all right,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"I don\u2019t know what I\u2019m afraid of\u2014but whatever it is, I\u2019m afraid of it.\"\n\"You\u2019re afraid maybe that your memory will be given back to you?\" said Rum...", "0_11_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until noon</span>. Checking his watch and comparing it to the seconds hand of the chronometer, $FIRSTNAMEB0 found that they were five seconds apart. His watch was slow.\nHe had been worrying over that watch, trying to discover what made it run so slow.\nHe was still worrying. At the back of his mind was the distinct suspicion that his bad leg, his iron leg, was affecting the watch. He had never been able to understand why his bad iron leg ran slow. He had been taught, as a child in the home, that anything made of iron, anything material at all, was better than the best flesh.\nSo now he suspected that his bad leg might make his good watch run slow.\nSeven minutes to noon.\nAnd now, looking at the whirling white crystals of the snow, at the feathery blanket that was drawing a gauze over everything, $FIRSTNAMEB0 grew confused. He didn\u2019t know where he was. Doubts floated through his mind. The Earth, he thought vaguely, had not been kind to him or to his family or to his friends.\nAnd now he and his foot and his watch were about to die. Were about to be killed by a beautiful, friendly element that any Earthling could have told Un...", "0_3_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>. A breeze had sprung up, and the laundry flapped. The Pope's window went dark. You could tell he did not like being waked up. He probably had a date for the evening, with maybe some lovely lady who admired him for his lovely, big, lofty ideas about life and God, especially about God, since the Pope is a bachelor, and Popes of course, ever since Peter was called Peter, are men.\nThere was a light in one of the bedrooms on the second floor. Red figured that one of the men must have a little boy, and the little boy was visiting and had awakened and was calling for his daddy. Red was glad it was not he that had to get out of bed and stumble around looking for the child in the dark.\nRed looked at the gap-toothed blade of the yard-light. He sighed. For a moment he was tempted to give up, to go away, to forget and start new somewhere else with Singer. He could always steal. He had never had any trouble stealing. Make a good living at it, too, and then one day, stealing back from a job he would bump into somebody. The somebody would be a gunner from Indiana, Nevada, maybe even New York...", "1_14_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nLenoni's restaurant occupied the ground floor and deep basement of a five-story office building on Magazine Street. The other four stories of the building were offices. The Sauncho Smilax Co., Ltd., had offices on the third floor.\nAt two-thirty, a few minutes before he was to begin his shift at the restaurant, $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 appeared at the railing of his penthouse atop the building, tore a hole in the oily, storm-dark sky with a loud yell and a flash of sunlight off his teeth, and threw a green plastic bottle of Lysol into Magazine Street. The Lysol hit an illegally parked, fire-engine-red, imported French sports car.\nThe bottle did not break.\nThat was a good sign, thought $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1.\nThe offices of the Sauncho Smilax Co., Ltd., were, like the offices of many fine and prosperous corporations, a madhouse in the late afternoon. Telephone operators, stenotypists, office boys, shipping clerks, advertising writers, public relations counsel, et al., et al., had faithfully made tea for one another, again and again and again, throughout the insufferable afternoon.\nThe only creature who, somehow, never took part in that...", "1_21_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-seven in the evening</span>.\nHe had been sent by Intelligence to gather, for the glory of God and the eternal benefit of his immortal soul, information about enemy strength, dispositions, and intentions.\nThe tactics which Space Warfare Techniques congenitally finds congenial are those of surprise, harassment, and infidelity. These tactics are seldom savory. Surprise tactics in particular can be costly in a way never reckoned in any inventory of material assets. Fifty-one thousand angels can dance on the head of a pin, but surprise tactics can destroy a perfect commander.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 never knew how close he came to having a nervous breakdown that evening.\nSince his earliest years on Mercury, he had been a worshiper of room service. It was his custom to invest 14% of his annual income in room service. He ate the most expensive food available, and usually the most fattening too. He demanded wines that cost thousands of dollars a quart. He requested the finest possible service, and paid in advance rubies or sapphires weighing one hundred twenty-five carats.\nHe seldom ordered a meal in advance. He preferred to telephone at the last minute. This gave the staff a sense of urgency. It encouraged enthusiasm. $FIRSTNAMEB1 liked to think of people sweating over his...", "0_6_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six eighteen in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 had appeared at my bed with his face covered with sweat and said, \u201cheyo, buddy, we gotta go now. Everything's changed -- but let me tell you about the time I met Carlo Marx. \u201dI knew he wasn't raving; he had decided something in the very bottom of his soul. He was calm and sweatin They air around him. \u201cCome on!\u201d he yelled. \u201cThere's a pays in the kitchen -- salami, beer -- we'll eat In the car. We'll leave the furniture in the middle of the floor; let the landlord worry. We'll come back for the big trunk a final time -- I have to pack the manuscript of my entire life and have one last look and a long goodbye. $FIRSTNAMEB3, at his last moment, was thinking about his books. Anyway, last things first and we must go back now. Time is a frightful urn for this part of the country; I must give details to show you how things were at that crucial starting-off point and you must see the context -- circumstances of a lifetime before the long and crazy details of our trip take precedence. I want to say also that $FIRSTNAMEF4 was with us. Somehow she got a few...", "1_7_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty in the morning</span>; I had no car. \u201cWhy don't you stay here?\u201d he said. He meant in Vegas; he was on parole and had enough trouble already. I called a cab and hurried off to my mother. When I got there she was up, packing. No coffee. Jesus Christ, I thought, the speededup American family. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was right. I sat in the car while they finished. They took the car and I had to wait two hours for another one. This time I drove up Main Street to Camille's apartment in an old black 1947 Cadillac limousine. Camille recognized me on the street and came over. She was with a fat little Mexican who at that moment preferred to go back to bed. \u201cI'm sorry, man. Let's go!\u201d I said. Camille unlocked her door and I slammed the Mexican in the back of the head and stepped on his toes. When the door closed we saw we had no light. \u201cShall we sit in the dark?\u201d I said. She fumbled for a cigarette. She sat down on the couch. \u201cWhat happened to $FIRSTNAMEB3 -- is he with $FIRSTNAMEF4?\u201d She was shrouded in darkness. I sat down beside...", "2_13_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nCommander Ratcliffe, who had charge of the expedition, stepped briskly out of a puffy airlock just as the first trickle of eager colonists came tumbling out of the lock.\n\"Unlimber!\" shouted Commander Ratcliffe.\nHis voice was at least an Earthling bellow.\n\"Whoa!\" he said.\nHis voice was a whisper. \u202fHis words were for his own people only\u2014a People who had lived for so many generations with whispered speech that they had forgotten how to shout.\n\"Fall in!\" whispered Commander Ratcliffe. His People fell in.\nThe tail of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s squad was in the pneumatic airlock now\u2014what Earthlings called a \"space ship.\" This was the reverse of the facts. The ship was a vacuum space\u2014called by Earthlings a spaceship because of its intended destination: a planet that Earthlings called Mars. The space ship was, in fact, a diving bell and submarine. The airlock beneath it was a culvert through which the inhabitants of the world inside and the world outside sniffed at each other.", "1_0_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-seven a.m.</span> It was now Tuesday, July 1, 1997. Many sickening things were going to happen to her before she could get to Bullitt. She had to do everything within her power, while it was still possible, to stay out of the hands of her enemies.\nTears of Anxiety and Rage squirted carelessly out of her eyes. \u201cSan Francisco?\u201d she gas got schluged m a few seconds when she realized that the huge map on the wall was really several maps taped together in a maze of crisscrossing routes.\nGratitude for the San Francisco symbol overcame her. She put a big fat check mark next to the word SAN FRANCISCO, in capitals, as it appeared on Route 101, at the summit of the Big Sur. Route 70 and Route 1 were the fastest ways to get there from Los Angeles: Route 1 straight down to Oxnard, then 70 up to Route 101 at Camarillo....But Route 1 would be bottled up with beach traffic coming out of Laguna, Newport and Santa Monica. Route 70 was best...and there were two possible cutovers to consider on the way, Route 5 at Moorpark, and Route 6 at Topanga Canyon....\n24. The Alchemical Wedding of...", "0_16_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-one in the afternoon</span>. The traffic on the Strip had slackened. $FIRSTNAMEF2 and the Paranoy messenger found themselves alone in the lobby, with only the sullen cashier and a calliope jukebox for company. Paranoy disappeared into the men\u2019s room.\n\u201cCashier,\u201d inquired $FIRSTNAMEF2, \u201chow long\u2019s the ride usually take?\u201d\nHe shrugged. \u201cDepends on the weather mostly. I\u2019ve seen it vanish in the rain. Couple minutes. Sometimes they\u2019ll land and be off and gone before you can blink.\u201d\n\u201cWhy is the sky black?\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s been like that for days. They\u2019ve been predicting clear skies.\u201d\nShe waited, but that seemed to be all he knew.\nFifteen minutes went by. $FIRSTNAMEF2 wandered out through the empty lobby and looked around. Sure enough, an Archer Frebishan look-alike stood on the sidewalk outside, also recalling jeans and a workshirt, his head cocked amusedly, trying to decide if she were really what he was looking for.\n\u201cAre you,\u201d he asked at...", "1_3_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>, Friday, September 20, 1966. The place was a stretch of highway somewhere in Southern California, right near the Mexican border. It was a lovely warm night, with a black sky and Perseus twinkling brightly, and Saturn turning its rings as always. I felt so groovy I yelled to the stars. \u201cTurn on! Tune in! cop out!\u201d But my passenger, my most recent hitchhiker, was silent; it was his custom. I didn't bug him with unnecessary conversation, because I had been warned he was some kind of mystic and might go off into a trance at any moment. He was short and skinny, about thirty years old, wore wire-rimmed glasses and a raincoat; he was a hitchhiker by profession. His name was Babe. I picked him up on the freeway, where he was reading a book by Henry Miller. He looked like any other hitchhiker, but when I offered him a glass of white wine he accepted. For thirty miles he sipped his wine and listened to the !Bob Dylan album I had on. I thought it was strange he liked Dylan, but he explained he had just written a poem about him. He called it \u201cSong of the Sideburns...", "1_17_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-two in the afternoon</span>. The afternoon was soft and deep red.\nThe swaying telegraph-poles planted their split white communities in deep red earth.\n\"Passage for Ford Madox Ford and party.\n\" Passage for Ford Madox Ford and party.\"\n\"Passage for Ford Madox Ford and party.\"\n\"How many times do you have to ring?\" said a man at the door.\n\"This is the fourth time,\" said Cohn.\n\"Well, go on in,\" said the man at the door. \"They\u2019ve been looking everywhere for you.\"\nInside was a steward in uniform.\n\"Was there ever such a disembarking?\" said Mike Campbell.\n\"They had the announcement out four times. We couldn\u2019t get a porter.\"\n\"I can\u2019t get anything right,\" said the steward with a foolish grin. \"I went to wake up your friend, and I just about put my fist through the galley window.\"\nHe giggled. \"I woke up a whole lot of people.\"\n\"Where is he?\" said Cohn.\n\"He\u2019s down in the medical inspectorship,\" said the steward. \"It\u2019s all a mistake, you know...", "2_13_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-one in the afternoon</span> on Titan. A time rental-machine materialized $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 to the neighborhood of a factory, six hundred and twenty yards from the home of Salo, the wholetime mechanic.\nThe home of Salo, the wholetime mechanic, was a square brown hut like all the other square brown huts in that part of Titan. It was surrounded by a thin ridge of grass unbroken by trees or statues, a dead white wall of wind-driven sand all around it.\nThe time rental-machine, for all its glowing and humming, could do no more than deliver $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME0 to the vicinity of Salo\u2019s home. The actual getting to the home and the getting inside were up to $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1.\n\"You can\u2019t do it, Mother,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Do what?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Get Salo to come out and talk to you?\"\n\"Well\u2014yes\u2014unless you just want to commit suicide by wind-tornado,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"You can\u2019t do it, Father,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"The reasons you had for refusing my invitation to the Museum are still there.\"", "2_8_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe pleasant scent of blankey flumguffee was still in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s nostrils. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had spent the night on a pallet in the corner of the lobby. He was there, now, dressed in clean, pressed forest-green fatigue trousers, long-billed blue shirt, and high boots.\nOf the other Martians in the lobby, only $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s pallet showed any evidence of having been used. The lobby was very spacious\u201450 by 150 feet, with a 20-foot ceiling. It was papered in ocher and maroon reps. There was quite a lot of deep pile on the ocher reps. The reps were broken by a plaster wall, supposed to be in the opening of a cavern. The plaster wall served, actually, to divide the lobby in half. The left half was an office and reception area. The right half was a record room and communications center, plus barracks and storerooms.", "1_10_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was a light shining just inside the entrance to one of the warehouses six hundred meters away. At forty-five seconds until eleven o\u2019clock, a man stepped in front of the light.\nAt thirty seconds, another man came into the light from the left of the warehouse entrance. The first man seemed to greet him.\nAt ten seconds the light in the warehouse winked out.\nAt zero seconds the spotter ship dropped its landing rockets, bounced ten meters and settled flat in the grass.\n\"Court!\" shouted $SURNAME0 up in the Sun. \"Sound off!\"\n\"Here!\" cried a voice at the doorway of the grounded ship.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0!\" called another voice from a point six hundred meters east of the ship.\n\"Chfolder!\" cried another voice from a point six hundred meters northwest of the ship.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB1!\" cried another voice from a point six hundred meters south of the ship.\n\"Konkommandment!\" yelled another voice from a point six hundred meters southwest of the ship.\n$SURNAME0 did a mystical thing then, something no one was expecting. He shut off his suit, becoming invisible to everyone on Earth. Then he materialized on the Moon.\nRum...", "0_21_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty in the evening</span>. Johnny had seven minutes to get on the stage and go to work, shift the crowds here with a smile, a rough joke, and the hearty manner of a state fair rake-off man.\nEarl and I waited in Johnny's bar. There were fifteen minutes to go. Earl shook his head in incredulity, then looked up at the ceiling and replied to something I had asked him in ten years.\n\u201cJohnny's only thirty,\u201d he said. \u201cTo talk to him, you'd think he was about sixty-five. Most all of it's natural causes -- handed to him. And he's had some time to get used to it. The first ten years he thought he had a few years left. After that, one year just blended into the next. Only thing he's looking forward to now is drinking enough port wine to put him in a coma and keep him out of Insane Pess Hospital. And time's running out on that trick.\u201d\n\u201cAh well,\u201d I said. \u201cI like $FIRSTNAMEB3 better.\u201d\n\u201cYou can have him.\u201d I looked at Count Basie on the wall. He had a red flower in his buttonhole. The bartender...", "1_21_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-four in the evening</span>.\nWhy were these men going on shift? Why were they getting ready to go to work, here on the rim of the Western World?\nWhy was I throwing my head far back on a cushion and closing my eyes, preparing to sleep like a gentleman on a train in the company of fellow-travelers?\nMy position in company with these gentlemen posed for me\u2014Here on the border of the Western World\u2014a finely-nuanced problem of etiquette: How does a gentleman\u2014confined to his hot little cubicle on wheels, stinking of his own feet, dwelling in his own filth and scattering it far and wide as he moves\u2014pay the faintest compliment to civilization by instinct?\nIn the society of people who have homes and food and things to eat, and a modicum of wit or art or science or taste, the ironical gentleman who is down on his luck can go right on being an ironical gentleman right through his sores and trials.\nHe can smile and examine the flaws in other people\u2019s dreams.\nHe can laugh at the flag. Or at the discrepancy between what people say and what they do.\nOr at the phrase, Home Sweet Home.\nOr at people who go rollicking into the...", "0_12_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October 3, 12,060 A.D. At one o'clock a man would step out of an ugly yellow house in Newport, Rhode Island, and enter a waiting time machine. The time machine would dematerialize, leaving the man, so far as the world was concerned, nowhere.\nGirls and boys were playing in every street of modern America. Trams and trolleys hummed and clanged. Lunch carts went their happy wheels down Gino Tartuccio Street, down Pleasant Street, down Telephone Road.\nThe man in the house put on his dark glasses and walked out into the late summer sunlight. He got into the waiting time machine, sat down, and was strapped in by the good newsboy who was waiting for him. Now the house was only a double exposure in about two million movie screens throughout the country\u2014and the world.\nThin, evil, and dark-complected, the man owned the time machine and the house behind him. He was probably worth a hundred million dollars. Nobody knew who he was.\nThe good newsboy was $FIRSTNAMEB2, a Titan-class robot who was thirty feet tall and stood in the cellar of the yellow house.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 used a billboard face with a weatherproof...", "2_22_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-three in the evening</span> on Mars. Through the wall next door, Chief Banks could be heard playing his violin. His bow found the same screech it had found twenty-three times before.\n\"Now where did you say you was from again, mister?\" said the plainclothesman.\n\"Earth,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Is that so?\" said the plainclothesman.\n\"Is that so?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Whereabouts on Earth?\" said the plainclothesman.\n\"The United States,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Which state?\" said the plainclothesman.\n\"Connecticut,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Whereabouts in Connecticut?\" said the plainclothesman.\n\"New London\u2014Washington in New London County,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Connecticut has got a Washington?\" said the plainclothesman.\n\"Lots of little towns have got Presidents,\" said $SURNAME1. \"That doesn\u2019t mean anything. That certainly doesn\u2019t mean the place is important. Mine isn\u2019t any more important than a million others.\"\n\"What brought you here?\" said the plainclothesman.", "0_13_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-four in the afternoon</span> when I found myself in the lobby of The National Hotel in Washington D.C., with no further ideas about urgent financial problems. And for a wild moment I wondered if I would get away with checking into the hotel with no money at all\u2014just flash a big smile at the desk clerk and tell him I was Oscar Metbeter, the millionaire, homeward bound from Europe, \u201con a matter of national urgency and importance.\u201d\nBut then I recognized the perils of this line; anybody could check in under my name; anybody could steal my baggage and collect the five grand in cash, credit cards and mysterious papers inside. Then it would be a matter of hours, perhaps minutes, before the FBI or Secret Service picked up the name of \u201cOscar Metbeter\u201d on a forged/hot check around Washington. Meanwhile, as soon as it became known that I had no residence in Washington, efforts would be made to detain me for questioning at other, more important hotels; and since my credit was obviously no good there, the fraud would soon be discovered.\n\u201cOn a matter of national urgency and importance\u201d? Shit, what could be more important, at this moment, than the wellbeing of a lice\u2014b...", "1_11_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of November 1, 1966, and Salo, who was sitting tensely at the controls of an ancient space ship in the lot outside a fashionable Beverly Hills dress shop, had just read a warning light on his panel and realized that from just inside the closed glass doors of the shop an ugly red beam of light was playing, tentatively and menacingly, over his windshield.\nIt would be foolhardy to ignore a Martian jet\u2019s courtship ritual, and Salo had not been ignoring it. He had made love to the ship in every conceivable manner, but so far with no results whatever.\n\"Woo-woo, baby!\" the Martian signal had cooed as Salo at last prepared to take off. \"Don\u2019t leave me now. Don\u2019t go, baby\u2014woo-woo! Come back, baby. Don\u2019t leave me now.\"\nSalo had no intention of leaving. He was prepared to sit in that spot for hours if necessary. But he was also prepared to run for home the instant the ship responded favorably.\nAnd now the warning light was on, and the ugly red beam from the shop touched the windshield like the claw of an executioner. It moved. The...", "2_16_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. She had two minutes to make her train. Snatching at the recording tape, she ripped it loose from her body and flung the loose end along the corridor of the train to reel off its last 12 seconds as the train, gaining speed, carried her away into a wobbling ellipse of industrial suburbs.\n2. Lot 49\nON THE MORNING after her first night in San Francisco $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas awoke with a hangover and the queerest feeling of deja vu. She washed down a couple of sedatives with a glass of water and even before they took effect began to remember.", "1_13_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-six in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 swung his space-suit\u2019s iron-wheeled left leg\u2014ca-clang, ca-clang\u2014clang, clang, clang across the iron Earth.\nThey traded memories. Impossibly bright memories fluttered from one mind to another like bright, poisoned butterflies. $FIRSTNAMEB0 clanged on doggedly.\nA memory worth remembering for $FIRSTNAMEB1 was the memory of the night $FIRSTNAMEB0 had killed the recruiting sergeant. $FIRSTNAMEB1 didn\u2019t hold it against $FIRSTNAMEB0 that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had killed the sergeant. \"Don\u2019t never go and blame yourself for killing anybody you had a right to kill,\" $FIRSTNAMEB1 had told his recruits around the camp fires on Mercury. \"Ever\u2019body\u2019s got a right to kill some people.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 drew a breath, ca-clanged it out. \"$FIRSTNAMEB1?\" he called experimentally, \"whoever you are: did you ever kill anybody you had a right to kill?\"\n\"Right to kill?\" echoed $FIRSTNAMEB1. His mind turned over that enlightening possibility, then rejected it. \"Who ever heard of killin\u2019 somebody you had a right to?\" he demanded. \"Killin\u2019 somebody you had a right to\u2014...", "0_4_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four eighteen in the morning</span> and this was Indianapolis, Indiana. I had been thinking about $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4, I somehow knew they'd be coming along this very road, I was waiting for them; everything was going to happen in a few hours. I walked into the bus-station restroom to wash my face. I had four dimes; this seemed like great wealth, enough to take me five hundred miles. I saw myself again hurrying along a street in Denver with my stick and my Raw Vision, up to the Hudson to catch the boat for Frisco. Everything keeps happening; I am convinced now that there's a tremendous fat pressure of karma solid as a rock deriving from all the stuff we did in the wacky panthropoid primordial past before we became upright and human and responsible. Aaah. I dried my hands on my handkerchief, it was getting ragged, and walked back through the warm May moonlight, happy. Indiana and this old road for ever; all kinds of travelers kept coming and going, purposeless. No particular meaning to anything except the green willows by the sad brown road, the night, and the buses rolling by to Denver and Gary. \u201cAll men have time, no one has time,\u201d as the Greek said...", "0_18_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-seven in the evening</span>, Part's date\u2014apparently a promise made at the window\u2014to rinse the coffee out of Corky's lungs having long since past, when Field Marshal $SURNAME0 once suddenly appeared in his sight again.\nThey were on their way to the Titanic Gods and Devils Ball now. But Field Marshal $SURNAME0 chose that moment to lead Mrs. $SURNAME0, Skip, and the great litter of prospective brides and bridegrooms into the lobby of the glamorous little Martian Titanic.\nHe stood them before a great mirror there\u2014made them watch a bicycle race on the mirror\u2019s surface.\n\"There you have time, time itself\u2014for as long as it takes that bicycle race to run its course,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Take a good look, all of you. Look well, for you will have your lives plastered against the surface of time, too.\n\"There is my wife\u2014the former $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0,\" said $SURNAME0. \"There is my son, $FIRSTNAMEM2 Bone, and his three wives, their mother.\" He chuckled richly. \"Who would believe so simple a sexual pattern could form a hyperbola with enough apparent mass to affect the orbit of Earth around the Sun?\" He laughed again...", "1_3_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-one in the morning</span>. In the office, she found the two on the couch again, this time with the door to the hallway closed, the outer door locked, the shades drawn. The TV was turned very loud. The noise came through ironically clear.\nShe thought of shooting Stan and his somehow lovely (though cheesy) Martian wife and putting both bodies in the office freezer until she could figure out what to do. This appealed to her as a perfectly serious, ironical, and even beautiful thing to do.\nSurreptitiously, thinking to play a little joke on them and maybe on herself, she raised her wrist and looked at her watch.\nThen she looked again. She put her wrist down. The watch now said six o\u2018clock in the morning, the date was today\u2019s.\nTime had run backward, the way it sometimes did in the very worst part of the night, when all her good intentions were themselves running backward, back to where they\u2019d come from.\nShe remembered hearing a scratching noise at the sealed office door, and remembered thinking that time must be running backward, because only $FIRSTNAMEF0 could make a scratching noise with her nails.\nWere these people, whoever they were, were they some way junior to she and God...", "0_21_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe Redwine home was dark. Only the yellow light showed under the kitchen door.\nHardly anybody was in the streets. Martian veterans cut through the business district, making for the dome six blocks west. Some of them wore the uniforms of Imperial Stgitards; others hobbled along in flight-jackets and woolens looted from Army shelves.\nNone of the veterans saluted anyone.\nThe press of people entering the stone fa\u00e7ade of the New Sheraton was so great that the revolving doors hardly moved. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1, with young $FIRSTNAMEB2, managed the entry stunt nicely, and were swept into the lobby.\nA marble fountain tinkled there, and blue velvet settees curved invitingly about its rim.\nThe four came out of the human torrent like ice cream dribbling onto an hot dish from a cone. They were all smiles.\n\"Window or aisle, Master?\" said a grinning usher. He was obviously referring to the form of punctual arrival to which chronones would be expected.\n\"Window, please,\" said $SURNAME0.\nThe usher led them to the right of the aisle, to three aisle-seats that were boxed in by a rail.\n\"I always love to see...", "0_6_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span> of December 31, 1952, when $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 touched his Creator\u2019s arm. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 materialized again, beaming as always. He did not seem to mind the burden of $SURNAME1\u2019s hand on his arm.\nEleven thousand, nine hundred and fifty-six other people on the island materialized with $SURNAME0 at the same time. These included $FIRSTNAMEF1, $FIRSTNAMEB2, $FIRSTNAMEF0, and $FIRSTNAMEB4. Not a one of them minded the close contact with $SURNAME1. The expression on every one of their faces was beatific.\n\"Greetings,\" said $SURNAME0 stoutly.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s heart was pounding wildly, his throat was bone dry. He centered on $SURNAME0\u2019s left nostril, which belonged to the head of a gold pin, the head of the pin that held his sleeper-suit closed. Everything was vanished but that one glittering nostril. $SURNAME1 blinked. When the image of the nostril reconstituted itself, $SURNAME1 noticed that there was only a tiny polished disk of gold in $SURNAME0\u2019s nose.\n\"What\u2014what does this mean?\" said $SURNAME1 shakily.\n\"Whatever you like it to mean,\" said Rumfo...", "0_4_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-seven in the morning</span>. The alarm clock on the bed table was set for four-thirty, for an obscure reason; certainly not for the purpose of enabling him to welcome the dawn in Geronimo's tent. His principal thinking and strategizing had to be done when Monroe was in school. One advantage to having an enemy so easily fooled as $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 was that he didn\u2019t have to give much thought to her feelings. It amused $FIRSTNAMEF3 a great deal\u2014mostly in secret\u2014to use his own daughter as the straw party in the constant manipulations in which he engaged on his blind and singleminded crusade. It amazed $FIRSTNAMEF3 how obviously foolish he could make himself seem to his daughter and still be taken seriously by his associates.\nHe touched a button on the bed table.\nNothing happened at first. But then a second surprise came when a voice spoke from the telephone on the table. The voice belonged to Ransom K. Fern, who had been $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1\u2019s right-hand man during the better part of $FIRSTNAMEF3\u2019s adult life.\n\"I know all about it,\" said Fern\u2019s voice from the telephone. \"The tape\u2019s defective.\"\n\"I was expecting it to materialize,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 smartly.\n\"Sorry,\"...", "2_0_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-nine ante meridiem</span>. I was in a very dangerous mood\u2014but I felt I could handle it. I tore off my clothes, turned on the TV set and the tape deck, and fell into the tub just as President Nixon appeared in the Great Communicator.\n\u201cGood morning, my fellow Americans..\n\u201cThese next few minutes are very important to you and to me and to our country. This morning I had a conversation about the Miagrowth situation with my national security adviser, Dr. Henry Kissinger . . .\n\u201cThe purpose of this broadcast is to offer you, and especially the younger people among you, some advice on how to cope with this problem . . .", "0_2_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-one in the morning</span>. Our voiceprint message told the guards in L.A. that every player of importance in the Great Game would be at the Monkey Inn that night between two and four A.M.\nThe cops would be watching by then. The encoded messages wouldn\u2019t fool them...but they wouldn\u2019t know where to look. My attorney would be there, of course\u2014along with some \u201cindustrial representatives\u201d who would be desperately eager to meet me.\nHow much security would they use? What kind of police protection would they have? Would they insist on patdowns, metal detectors and massive physical searches? Or would they make it casual? Word of mouth? A special handshake? A sign under the Stetson, perhaps?\nWho were these people? What did they want? I had been trying to put it together in my head, but so far all I had was a wild series of images from the No Chancestories branch of the Any form of new respectability.\nThe ragged skein of their history ran from early rackets in the southwest Vegas bookmain through a complex snarl of land acquisitions and water front deals in L.A.\u2014some of which my source confirmed and some of which were news to me. Especially...", "2_0_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-three ante meridiem</span>. He was standing on the pavement of the parking lot, gathering his will and mastering his mind. He knew the answer, but he asked the question anyway, maybe to shock me into some response that would end this nightmare.\n\u201cWhere's the key, goddamn you?\u201d he said. The final question. The last outrage.\n\u201cWhat key?\u201d I asked calmly.", "2_23_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to midnight</span>.\nLenore Beadsman got to her feet and walked to the door, moved the wine bottle from the doorknob, turned the knob and stood framed in dark wood, backed by the mingled sounds of Columbus.\nThe outside hallway was brightly lit. There were beige linoleum tiles. The walls were block. There were windows of thick black glass. The windowpanes looked to have been welded in place. Light came from fixtures in the ceiling. There was a mixture of sharp metal smells, from the metal. The door out of which Lenore Beadsman was peeping was pincered by twin latches, and each fastened by a black metal lock whose heavy keys jingled merrily in the hands of two burly men in white uniforms, who were at the moment releasing the one lock to latch the other. The men paid attention only to the latches, and to the jingle of keys. Neither looked at Lenore Beadsman at all.\nThe time was a minute before midnight.", "2_21_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAugust\nAt the Ransom Center of the University of Texas, among its Cassius Marcellus Clay Papers, there is a statement, scrawled, on a sheet of paper torn from a spiral notebook. The statement, which is not dated, has not been found with any other object suggesting what its purpose might have been. On its own, it suggests little. It reads:\nThe High Chronic is not accustomed to accepting rides from Grover, however limited the distance may be. He rides his thumb like a man with a violent prejudice against touching.", "0_16_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. On its way to my cubicle in our file room, my great-grandfather\u2019s diary had suffered a series of collisions, in each of which a different person had been responsible for damaging the book. The first blame could be placed squarely on the child who had stolen the book to begin with. It had fallen from his window and landed with a papery smack on the top of a passing bus. Three middle-aged ladies riding behind it, sensing trouble and hearing a small thud, left the bus at the next stop and went running down a street to find the source of their concern. There, on the top of the bus, was the child\u2019s diary. They climbed on board, intending to right what had been made wrong, but the driver stopped them, and sent them off the bus in mid-route. The three now found themselves alike in strength and purposeless. It was not yet noon. Two of them went to a cafeteria to catch their breaths and discuss the events of the morning. The third, who had money and a daughter who played the viola, went in to get a drink, and stayed on to hear a one-eyed woman, who had once played the viola, tell of her experiences on the run...", "1_15_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-two in the afternoon</span>, and there were not very many men in the room. Most of them were in their twenties, and they stood around the bar, wearing their hats and looking at the bug-eyed cowboy on the television screen. They moved their heads on their necks to show their feelings as the cowboy galloped across the plains and shot at the evil Indians. A sweet, wry chocolate smell was in the air.\n\u201cChocolate?\u201d said a girl. She smiled like the sunshine of May. She had kinky red-gold hair, the kind that curls around a finger. Her name was Remi. She stood in front of me with a tray full of chocolate cakes.\n\u201cNo, thank you,\u201d I said.\nAround the girl with the tray were two other girls. One was in the arts and crafts business, and the other one was in the lanky stage. They were both seventeen years old. I was very fond of them. The girl with the tray looked like Joan of Arc.\nShe smiled. \u201cChocolate?\u201d\n\u201cNo, dear.\u201d\n\u201cWhy, hell, mister, you don't look well at all.\u201d\n\u201cI'm all right.", "2_21_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-nine in the evening</span>. The place was Cleveland Heights, Ohio.\nRandy Lenore Bensdottir was asleep, with her mouth open, in her bed, alone. She slept with both hands flat on the mattress, palms down and fingers spread.\nHer first dream of the evening was of a girl with an iron that wouldn\u2019t work, at the Llangollen ironing board, in an enormous Wapolish manor full of relatives who, because they were absolutely and completely different as opposed to simply being dif ferent, appeared as grotesque caricatures of supposedly normal folk.\nThe child\u2019s name was Lenore.\nThe time was one minute before ten o\u2019clock in the morning. The place was Desert Blume, in the southwestern corner of the state of Ohio.\nLenore Beadsman, of the Beadsman Family, lay in a shin-deep hot spring in the Flaming Desert. Other small hot springs burbled in the distance. The sky was dark and violet, with swirls of cold cream. The sun was a coin-slot punch-light shining from the black center of the sky.", "1_8_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty in the morning</span>. Much alcohol had been consumed the night before. I was quietly going crazy, among all these people with Tom and his attorney. I wanted desperately to make a getaway, but I was afraid I might miss something crucial. A vicious rumor was circulating among the grapevine bunnies: that the District Attorney of Clark County, where Las Vegas is located, was going to make a move on the $2000 holding\u2014a move with the U.S. Attorney General in Washington, because such monies \u201cconstituted evidence of a conspiracy to promote gambling in a constitutionally illegal manner.\u201d\nI could restrain myself no longer. I tried to think of a quiet, subtle, inconspicuous way to leave Tom and this attorney and get my ass out of there, but there seemed to be no graceful exit from this blazing roost...so I finally told Tom I\u2019d look for him at the track later on, maybe around five or six. He gave me a blank, gaping stare. My suitcase was in the closet of the master bedroom, where we\u2019d carried it Friday night.. So I went to my room and just gathered up my remaining things and walked out of the front door and down the driveway to Lancaster Boulevard, and when I...", "0_11_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty in the morning</span> and I knew I couldn't make it. The bedbugs had already cooked themselves a hot breakfast from my blood: now they were beginning on their second course. The LSD had worn off and my pupils were darting around like minnows in an aquarium, scared and out of their element. I had to confront these ugly facts and even then I cringed at the thought of what was necessary. Decision was farthest from my mind; I was still groping vaguely in the grapefruit\u2014garden of my mind, looking for an inkling there ... and failing, for no hint came.\nI gave up and brought out ten Miltowns. The time was now twelve\u2014fifteen. If I had been a better doctor I would have known that the Miltowns would aggravate this condition and bring on a total and instantaneous bi\u2014sympathetic backlash with all its awful consequences, including death. But I didn't know and with the Miltowns came a drink of Canadian Club and more aspirin. Forty\u2014five minutes later, at twelve\u2014fifty, I was suddenly smitten with a horrible dizziness\u2014a feeling, you might say, of paranoid vertigo. I could hardly talk, and hardly think either, except that I...", "0_21_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past nine o'clock in the evening</span>. The concert was at nine-thirty. We had an hour to kill in Reno. It was yet too early to check in and have a drink in the bar and smoke a Bowl of bonhomie with some old boys. Too early to sit with our backs against a wall. We were both hungry.\nAnd so we walked through Reno with our topcoats slung over our arms and bought hamburgers at a sidewalk stand --they were so atrocious they made us laugh. Then we had egg and sugar sandwiches at another sidewalk stand, and they were so atrocious they made us laugh even more.\nWe bought a pint of Old Overholt at a sad little liquor store, with a fat lumpy woman almost filling the doorway, and drank the whiskey at a water fountain, where sawdust was strewn, and a boy in candy-store uniform wiped the water from our mouths with a rag. I gave the boy a dollar to buy himself some decent food. And we went to the men's room of a department store, with sawdust also, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 hunched over a make-shift gas furnace and rolled noodles. And we talked and talked. Wherever we walked people looked at us, for we swaggered -- swag...", "0_12_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve nineteen p.m.</span> Earth chugged along at a heliocentric speed of just under one twentieth of the speed of light: so that for every ten yards Earth had gone since the last time Manny looked out the port, the Solar System had gone one yard further from Opapasmino. The System, in other words, had one tenth of a second further to go until it got to wherever it was going. $FIRSTNAMEF1 had told Manny this; he asked her once, just to see if she would tell him, and she did. The things $FIRSTNAMEF1 told him!\u2014throwing really good-looking stones, with which she fooled and delighted the natives, the chief quality required, she said, being that they look lettered.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 had a little telescope, a present from her father; Manny had a big one. Whenever the Sun went behind a cloud, and the regular light went out, $FIRSTNAMEF1 and Manny would climb by different ladders to the dome and take turns looking at the stars through the big telescope. The stars were spectacular. One was like a nebula, and did funny things to $FIRSTNAMEF1 when she looked at it through the telescope. It was like losing her sense of touch; she had the most peculiar sensations. Another was a double star, two stars close together...", "1_5_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-five in the morning</span>. A new day had begun. I was determined to stay awake at least long enough to behold this day properly. No more drowsing and kitchen goofing at dawn; I was going to sit up all goddam day, food or no food, and watch the sun rise. My system did not like these radical variations, and it reacted by dumping 1,500 milligramsof Hood 's Sedative in my blood stream. My system, trying to cope with all these crazy new ideas, assumed I had gone crazy. It was bailing me out. It said, \u201cAll right, if you want to sit up and watch the sun rise on the color of the grass and the color of the sky and the sheen of the dew on the smell of the air and the feel of the ground, then you'd better not be conscious for any of it or it will be horrible; so here: Hood's. Go ahead and do what you want, but don't bother to experience it. Just let it happen to you and don't realize it. The world is green and gray, honey; not wornted on.\" And I agreed with it. I nodded happily. I knew all about it: but it was a necessary evil.", "2_1_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-eight in the morning</span>. It was the moment of one forty-eight in the morning.\nThere, down tiny lanes of Concord grapes, was the reddest and most perfect creation man had ever known, floating serenely over the estate-before-the-universe that poor old $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had bought for his daughter. There it hung, outshining all the stars.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 smiled happily up at it.\nShe never would have had the nerve to put on the really wonderful fireworks display she had put on, had she not had the courage given her by her drugs and her religion. The drugs had given her courage, and the religion had given her something to live for more wonderful than courage\u2014more wonderful than the spectacle she surveyed.\nFrom this moment on, that something more wonderful was no longer a vague projection. It was right there above her.\nAnd there was no question in her mind as to how she was to get it.\nShe was to take it.\nHer whole system tingled with the certainty of how she was to take it, and how little danger there was in so taking it.", "0_4_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span> in San Narciso when Mandel's fatal digits crossed the path of his own. The time, the place, and the personnel were all too correct.\nOn one side: the pervert, the cold-reader, the sleight-of-mind man. On the other side: his contact, his victim, the wife of his friend.\nSuppose, as he read in a file somewhere, Onesto had on an impulse hit his wife with his open hand, delivered a stinging blow to her right cheek, once only, not hard enough to leave a mark but plenty hard enough to hurt. Suppose the reader. Last night. As the two men talked. Going through the files. Suddenly realizing here was somebody like himself, a man who might at last be able to give him the only confirmation and solidarity he had ever needed: somebody who was convinced, as this man was convinced, by things outside reason, by hints from the inner mind. The community of the cold-readers.\nAnd the seduction, the fatal seduction could have been so simple, could have been nothing more than flattered vanity, the urge to talk about what you do not to everybody you meet, the need to prove to somebody, anybody, the truth of...", "2_4_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four eighteen in the morning</span>.\nIn Newport, Newport, Rhode Island, genie and owner of the great $SURNAME0 estate of $SURNAME0, Winthrop, Junior, again materialized in his living room at the moment that most film actresses would have gone to bed.\nHe saw, through the French windows of his living room, that there was a light on in the stateroom of his yacht.\nOh, God, thought Winthrop. There\u2019s that terrible woman again.\nShe still wants to talk, does she?\nI won\u2019t answer. I\u2019ll let her sit out there until she freezes.\nIt was a bitterly cold spring night.\nWhat on earth did she want to talk about all that time?\nGood heavens\u2014she was interested in some intelligible things!\nWhat would she want to talk to old $SURNAME0 about?\nOld $SURNAME0, young $SURNAME0\u2014what\u2019s the difference? Nasty old man. Unprintable old man.\nOld, young\u2014it was really $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 who was interesting.\nWinthrop sat down dazedly in a wing chair.", "2_22_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-nine in the evening</span>.\nHovering like a hawk on wings of thermals of magnetic force, the Space Wanderer was over $SURNAME0\u2019s estate, looking down on mateless Moon Mae, the tattered, motherless chrono-synclastic infundibulated little girl.\nIt was ten forty in the evening.\n$SURNAME0, in his $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill outfit, was watching the chrono-synclastic infundibulated dancing of Moon Mae on a television receiver, receiving the image from a scanning satellite. The dancing came from a mansion in Newport, Rhode Island.\nIt was ten forty-one in the evening.\nThe child had strangled the dog and stuffed it into the child\u2019s lavender dress, and now the child and the dog were past the checkpoints, and there was nothing the Virgin Military Police could do but issue the child a prisoner\u2019s number. The number on the child\u2019s brass breast was 00000.\nIt was ten forty-two in the evening.", "2_7_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-one in the morning</span>.\nSeven forty-two.\nSeven forty-three.\nNo bees stung $SURNAME1.\nHe wrinkled his brow, trying to understand why this spot had been so lucky for him. Choice spots for stings were under the bark of rotting stumps, under dead logs, among dead grass and leaves. Was there some dead thing under this living sod that discouraged bees?\nNo. There wasn\u2019t anything stinking or dead. The grass was green, so were the leaves. The whole peninsula of $SURNAME1\u2019s childhood really was in splendid shape. No stinking hole anywhere. And yet\u2014and yet\u2014\nWhat was going to happen here on this peninsula, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 suddenly realized, was not going to happen at the tip. The place where it was going to happen was all around the tip\u2014here, here, here\u2014under the smooth grass, among the smooth leaves, between the white blades and the clover.\nWell\u2014he had some time to kill before the scheduled festivities, so he decided to investigate the curious luckiness of this peninsula in minute detail.", "1_18_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-five in the evening</span> or twenty-five minutes past four in the morning\u2014certainly that time in the morning when eyes shine wildly in the ugly glare of Midwestern highways. For we were in the American interior; Indiana, to be exact. We were driving through it to get to the Coast. A crazy thing was happening to us. For years I'd wanted to get out of the West and away from where I'd been in jail and go to New York, the actual city. I felt I'd been in the wilderness too long, and I wanted to go have a beer in the Rainbow Room with a jazz band in earshot and walk around the corner for a hot dog and shoot the breeze with bums and smell the Hudson River steamers in the black mysterious nights and stumble home drunk in New York. And I also realized, having had to cross the continent twice in tractors to get back to the West Coast, that there was another point of view, too; and that was Things Are Looking Up, for now you're off the farm, you've left your home town, there's nobody can insult you or tell you what to do, you can have a drink whenever you want one and smell fancy whores' perfume and go to movies wearing a suit in the daytime...", "1_15_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. Lenore and her orange Tissot taxi were at the stoplight on Erieview, headed out. The traffic was bad in the tunnels, everyone trying not to heed the advance warnings of Root\u2019s broadcasts, and more so out at the Erieview Building, where control of the cars was being handed jealously over to the snarled street arteries. Lenore had sat in the idle cab with her I Ching for nearly half an hour.\n/a/\nPARTIAL TRANSCRIPT OF \u201cTHE SANDMAN\u201d SEGMENT OF THE \u201cNIGHT WHILE GUESTS RODE, THEY SANG\u201d SERIES PREPARED BY MR. HUGH BRASS, PART-TIME GUEST, AND BROADCAST BY MR. ROLAND THATCHER, PART-TIME GUITARIST, ON THE \u201cMR. ROLAND AND MR. HUGH SHOW\u201d ON WEDNESDAY, 13 AUGUST, 1990, AT 14 33 EST ON THE CBS PARTNERSHIP SYSTEM.\nOPENING THEME, \u201cSHOWTIME!\u201d: GUITAR, BRASS; PIANO, 0, G.F", "0_18_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-nine in the evening</span>, and the sky was already dark, and between the factory and the bluffs ahead lay the village of Barcelona, spread out smoothly, with deep, soft shadows in all the inviting places. The shadows were speckled with yellow light that spilled from cottage windows.\nThere was no sign of Flapjack McAuliff.\nAt the side of the path was a small building that had once been a telephone booth. Captain Beefheart unlocked its door with a key he took from his pocket. He stepped inside and closed the door.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB0 became aware of the tiny cabin, he knocked at its door, then put his face to the window. Through the window he could see his reflection in two yellow circles of light. The circles came from two dusty-watt electric bulbs with wire stems.\n\"Hello, anybody in there?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nA match was struck, and a kerosene lantern burned brightly.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 blinked in the yellow light. Then he recognized the man holding the lantern. \"Flapjack!\" he cried happily.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0!\" cried Flapjack McAuliff happily. \"Thought you fell down a shaft, or something.\"\n\"No, I was in jail,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "2_2_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty in the morning</span>.\n/e/\nThe eastern limb of the sun hove itself into sight over the top of the far-distant ridge of hills that was the edge of the world, at just about three o\u2018clock in the morning.\n/a/\nOne of the really unnerving things about having dinner with Frequent and Vigorous friends on Frequent and Vigorous Pretzels Day is the dinner conversation. Frequent dinner conversation is usually conversationally indistinguishable from dinner conversation in general\u2014the chief difference is in setting, admittedly. One is apt to have dinner at dinner. Frequent dinner, however, involves not just dinner but dinner-people. For dinner to be \u201cgood dinner,\u201d dinner-people must be present. Frequent dinner-people are generally characterized by an appalling abundance of dinner-person-qualities.", "0_8_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight sixteen in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0, who had forty-seven minutes left to live, didn\u2019t know it. $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t know a lot of things. He didn\u2019t know that he and his mates had been klaxtoned in space by a crew of true pilots and abandoned to drown in space-so that the true pilots could get home quickly for a heck of a feast and tons of drunken sex. And he didn\u2019t know that he was about to pay for his ignorance with his life.\nHe didn\u2019t know all these things, but he was about to learn everything.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s space suit was equipped with binoculars and with five lightweight and cheery little books. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was sitting in the shallop of his space suit, groaning and lusting for Brovenna, when he came across the following passage in one of his books:\n\"As $SURNAME1 watched theodolites gather material for their great mural, he wondered whether the mural portrayed each individual troop\u2019s concept of truth, or whether the mural portrayed a single concept of truth for the hive. Or whether all the troops had a hand in portraying the mural\u2019s concept of truth.\n\"And $SURNAME1, who...", "2_11_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-one in the morning</span>.\nA chrono-synclastic infundibulum had been forming around $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 all during their materialization. It was, now, a tornadic swirl of color, an Einstein-Rosenberg Relativistic Tornado. Anything within the space of the swirl could be visualized as at the eye of a hurricane.\nIn the very center of everything was $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog emerged from the chrysalis of the chrono-synclastic infundibulum. They appeared to be more bronzed and more muscular than before, and $SURNAME0\u2019s right eye sparkled.\nFrom the handle of a crescent wrench he had been carrying emerged an electric-blue sheet of flame, which disappeared in a floppy puff two feet off the floor.\nThe flame was the sound of $SURNAME0\u2019s voice, the voice of $FIRSTNAMEM2.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 stepped aside so that a shimmering image of himself could take his place, facing Earth.\n$SURNAME0 and his image faced their planet together.", "2_8_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMajor General Lew R. \u201cHardboiled\u201d Bukowski, Commander of the Parachute Infantry of the Multitudes, was a totally humorless man. He had been tough and smart and clean, and he had killed a lot of Germans, Americans had gained some territory. All as it should be.\nWhen the long-range space ship came down, it hadn\u2019t been any shock to him to find himself mixed up in something so big. One thing after another.\nAll as it should be.\nAll a man could ask for was a chance to do what was right, and take his punishment like a man if he couldn\u2019t make things turn out right.\nA city had been evacuated.\nThen the city hadn\u2019t blown up.\nThen so many innocent people had been slaughtered.\nThere was nothing to say about a thing like that. Bukowski couldn\u2019t guess who the murderers were, or why they had done it.\nThere was nothing to know about people who would do that kind of a thing. No point in trying to understand them. They were too different from him. Whatever they were doing must seem right to them.", "1_9_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until ten o'clock in the morning</span>; the place, the graveled right of way of the Erie Lackawanna Railway, one hundred and fifty-two miles due west of downtown Indianapolis.\n\"Shall we train-jack him now, Capt'n,\" the Gunner asked, \"or wait till he goes to sleep?\"\nThe wedding-pageant technician was sleeping, sitting upright, snoring loudly and monstrously. His head was cocked grotesquely on his neck. His neck and the back of his uniform were sweated through, and his eyes were clogged with dust. Ribbons of dried spit ran from the corners of his lips. The technician\u2019s head fell abruptly off to the right. It hung there for a moment on his skinny neck, then jounced back up.\n\"While he\u2019s asleep is a good time to jack him,\" the bombardier said.\n\"If you say so, Bomb,\" said the captain. He started the engine of the armored car. It made a roaring sound, drowning out the snores. A rattling came from the tin boiler in back. \"What\u2019s he carrying?\" the captain shouted over the engine noise.\n\"Just a ordinary carpet-roll of mail,\" the Gunner shouted back. \"...", "0_7_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>; there wasn't a cloud in the sky; I felt easy and safe in the world. I felt fine. I felt like shouting around the world. I knew nobody would hear me, but still I felt like shouting. I looked out over the world and down through time, and I found myself back in Concord, Massachusetts, right there in the classroom of young Mr. Snodgrass, who never did like me too well, or too greatly, but who, on this sweet June morning, seemed to have something going for him. I saw the children assembled before me and myself in a long-closed shutter, leaning against a Concord rock with auburn hair blowing in the wind, the little pale-green flower trembling, the bird singing, and there I stood in the classroom again, wondering, on this splendid June morning in New York, if old Mr. Snodgrass hadn't, after all, been a bit of all right, and if I couldn't stand his collar, and if I was ever going to find as good a year as this one was shaping up to be. I felt fine. And then I remembered this was a day upon which we were supposed to be watching if our stock rose from the opening; so I looked over to my tick...", "0_1_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to two o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was Des Moines, Iowa. Somewhere far behind were Minot, North Dakota, and the mystic Missouri River. Somewhere ahead were the legend-shrouded snows of Siouxland. Somewhere beneath the belly of the bus were tremendous num- bers of vibrations and mighty atoms of exhaust fumes.\nUnder the bus, really, were metal highway that made one's foot quake every time the wheel went by or the undercarriage twanged.\nIn the front part of the bus were four people: a baby, a grandmother, and two men. One of the men was myself, and the other was a dreadfully hairy fellow who stared out the window at the empty fields of Iowa and the black night, and let a slow madness seep into his eyeballs.\nThe two of us were sitting in the front seat, which is the best seat in any bus because you can look out the big window at the stars. Behind us, separated from us by a curtain, was the other half of the bus --which is called, naturally enough, the back seat. The back seat is the worst seat in any bus. It is right in the middle. It is folded between the weight of the driver's seat and the weight of the front seat.", "1_18_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span> in Sector 24, one hundred and fifty-seven thousand miles from home. We waited. Around us was a desert filled with drums and sand. Nothing moved. The sun went down, and the land began to steam. And as the nightshadows grew long and mysterious, we all became very quiet. Something was going to happen. Joanne and I felt it. We knew it.\nSuddenly, around a dune, appeared a single long-bed Ford Tudor sedan\u2014last year\u2019s model. Unmistakably American. It bounced once in the sand and then rolled casually up to us. In the front seat were two drivers\u2014a young man and a boy. Both were wearing wrinkled white suits. They had long hair, dark tans, and mean faces. \u201cWhere you goin, boy?\u201d one of them growled at us. \u201cThat\u2019s for us to know and for you to find out,\u201d snapped our field leader. \u201cState your business.\u201d His voice was gravel in a paper bag.\nThe two men began to laugh. We were the only people in sight, but there must have been somebody else around. They obviously knew this and felt secure. \u201cWhere...", "1_9_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span> at thirty thousand feet over the plains of Kansas when the dark green machine quivered, hesitated, coughed gently, then seemed to gather itself in, accepting imminent matter-of-fact phrases like I have engine failure, descend to ten thousand immediately, report your position, and I have an emergency, descend immediately, I have no hydraulics, put me down on the Kansas prairie, among the wheat and the wispy cirrus clouds, I need assistance, descending, descending, and no one said anything, for the noise of the machine now could not be distinguished from the sound of the air itself and the oceanic rush of sound filled the plane and everyone in it and for a moment there was only the stricken machine falling, pushing itself through the air, as if all the people in it fell suspended headlong out into Kansas from thirty thousand feet, and felt like Icarus, who fell from the summer sun.\nResurrection\nWhen Lenore came to she was being vigorously massaged on her scalp by her sister Mindy, with a wet towel, on the floor of the hallway outside their apartment, while two paramedics put a tracheal tube on Lenore\u2019s friend Candy Mandible, who lay opposite Lenore, down on her...", "2_7_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nBack in New York City, $SURNAME0 and his dog were still waiting for Lieutenant either Kije or Corp to telephone from the Spacecraft Titan.\n\"Where are all the new things we were going to get?\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 suddenly. \"Where are all the new things?\"\n\"What new things?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"You didn\u2019t say no word about any new things.\"\n\"All part of the universal mystery-the fact that we weren\u2019t told in advance about any of this,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"There\u2019s a balloon going up in thirty-three minutes,\" said Salo. \"It\u2019s good-bye and good luck to everybody, and God bless everybody.\" He looked miserably at $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"So long, Mr. $SURNAME0. So long, Mrs. $SURNAME0.\" He held out his hand. \"I guess this is the time and the place for us to say good-bye.\"\n\"You\u2019re sure you want to go?\" said $SURNAME0.", "2_21_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-three in the evening</span>. The place was a house in East Corinth, Ohio. The lights were all out. The street outside was quiet and empty. Nothing moved on it. Nothing was stirring inside it.\n/e/\n\u201cThe story is this. The story you have before you is this.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cOur story begins when a person is joined with a certain something that is not that certain person.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cNow are you going to shut up and listen, or are you going to go home?\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s just too bad if you think people have hung around this long to hear you talk.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cThe person and the thing are joined together in a way that makes the person hate the thing, but also feel obliged to take care of the thing.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cListen. There\u2019s more.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cAn obligation of the body is one thing. But an obligation of the soul, taste ... but now the thing begins to die.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d", "1_15_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>, and time for her show. At the desk sat an elderly, fidgety little man who reminded $SURNAME1 of pictures he had seen of men waiting to be hanged.\nThose gallows were built for you, old daddy, $SURNAME1 thought.\n\"Wrightson,\" said the man at the desk, \"that\u2019s w-r-i-g-h-t-s-o-n.\" He spelled it for $SURNAME1, who then had his spelling corrected by the man, who was from Dayton, Ohio, and whose real name was Harrison McKay.\n\"Would you like tea?\" $SURNAME1 asked the old man.\nAn agony of embarrassment contorted the man\u2019s face. \"Oh,\" he said softly, \"is that what she ...?\" He waved his hands in helpless apology. \"Oh, no, no,\" he said.\n\"What did she sell you?\" asked $SURNAME1.\n\"Oolong,\" the old man said. \"It\u2019s tea. I thought it was tobacco.\" He laughed, a wisp of a laugh, as though laughter had become suddenly too much of an effort for him. \"I\u2019ve been smoking too much tobacco,\" he said. \"I ... they told me downstairs it...", "1_15_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three eighteen in the afternoon</span> when we stepped off the train at Ann Arbor. $FIRSTNAMEB3 leaned out the window and talked to a crowd of Ann Arbor football fans on the platform and quieted them down; they were yelling and milling around a poor old bum that had collapsed on the bench and reeled up when they yelled. We got out with all our bags, and $FIRSTNAMEB3 grabbed the poor old bum's bag too and suddenly realized it. \u201c God damn, I didn't mean to do that and now I've gone and done it.\u201d We went to get a cab at the station in order to go to his home to unpack and get fresh clothes, but there were none in sight. Everybody was driving foreign sports cars and had no money for taxis. Even the cabdrivers were getting out of American cars and standing around in twos and threes, looking and conversing. Most of them wore leather jackets. We started to walk to Tokay's in only curiously watchful Ann Arbor people on the sidewalk making way for us. \u201cNow you know, this is the kind of town where I could dig myself a whole new dimension talking the talk and walking the walk with the kids and even lop off a day to go back to Denver,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201c...", "1_1_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one nineteen in the morning</span> when the fabulous and sad $FIRSTNAMEB3 finished telling little Victor the sad and worldly sorrows of his young and gifted life. \u201cWow!\u201d said Victor. \u201cMan, what you told that kid was strictly fox.\u201d I could only agree. We said goodbye and rushed out to Victor's car, a terrible wreck that almost never ran. This time, after many kicks and shoves, it started. A moment later we were in it, roaring across the silent changing plains of the East and North, where the only lights were the shabby golden gas that hangs above river towns and the terrible red neon octopus sign of a sandwich shoppe with living golden letters that scream out \u201cROAST BEEF\u201d above the lost land. Red neon letters scream upward on waste horizons that have no reason for existing. And mufflers growl from unexplained grandsons of the July night. Insects in the grass beside the road never hear the silent rustling rush of our fenders. Where from here to Cleveland barren planet? Bad-smelling tubes of brown cloth rushing behind a gray windshield, dirty hanky on the columns for air-conditioning, old socks for padding the brake pedal, one tube hanging down for the left-hand turn...", "0_12_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-six p.m.</span> when the police showed up at the White House. There was insanity in the air. There were rapists crouched under bushes, ready to jump. Ernie $SURNAME1 was bathing his feet in the White House fountain. The people in the White House proper were hiding under beds.\nThere was sanity in the arriving police officer, too. His name was Marvin Dimple, and he was the first representative of the law to confront Salo and $SURNAME0 on their home turf. Dimple was forty years old and had been on the Titan police force for eleven years.\nHe was married, and his wife was thirty-seven years old. She was expecting a child. Dimple didn\u2019t know the child\u2019s sex or its approximate arrival time on Earth.\nWhat a tender family man was cooped up in that Space Wanderer\u2019s body\u2014that renter of dirty bodies!\nMarvin Dimple knew nothing about Salo and $SURNAME0, naturally\u2014about Salo\u2019s fantastic machine in the caves of Mercury, about $SURNAME0\u2019s all but incredible true story on Earth. A Space Wanderer and his dog? Dimple saw them as nothing more than carefree vagabonds who had somehow come into possession of...", "0_15_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-one in the afternoon</span>. It was ninety seconds from the end of shift, and without fail ninety seconds from the final few bars of Nobody Knows the Trouble I\u2019ve Seen.\nThe chief cook and bottle washer, the only other person in the bar, a six-foot three-inch, thirty\u2014eight-year-old Gemini, was setting up the shuffleboard gallery.\nHis name, professionally, was Homo Sapiens. His real name was Wiley G. Josephs.\nHis personal appearance and his pub were studiedly ugly and repellent. They were designed to keep the pretty people away.\nThe ugly and the repellent, thought $SURNAME0, were relatively secure.\nOnly the ingratiating had been made vulnerable by the new Tristero.\nThe telephone in the bar rang.\nThings hummed.\nOne, two\u2014\nJosephs answered. He covered the receiver with his hand.\n\"Tristero-Maintenon-Rajanith-Smokebraher-Thling-ermann,\" he said.\nHe nodded hello, said, \"Yah, I got \u2019em both here. You want them?\" He counted heads. \"Got eight here, all right. You want us...", "1_18_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-one in the evening</span>. It was a lovely night. As the crowd grew smaller, Fielding and $SURNAME1 found seats on the marble coping. There was no traffic now on Park Avenue, and hardly any limousines stopped for passengers.\nThe crowds in the streets of the riot were thinning out, too.\nFielding put his head back and looked at the towers of Manhattan, luxuriously purple against the sky. \"When I lived in Brooklyn,\" he said, \"I used to love to come into the city, but I used to hate to leave it.\"\n\"Now you don\u2019t want to leave it,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Now I don\u2019t want to leave it,\" said Fielding. \"How did that happen?\"\n\"Maybe you\u2019re growing up.\"\n\"Age has nothing to do with it,\" said Fielding. \"You\u2019re as old as you feel.\" He looked at $SURNAME1. \"How old do you feel?\"\n\"As old as I look,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"You ought to feel as young as you look,\" said Fielding, \"which would be twenty-eight.\"\n\"Twenty-eight sounds pretty good to me,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"It sounds great,\" said Fielding...", "1_14_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-eight in the afternoon</span> of December twenty-second. I felt purged, new-born, as raw and clean as that year\u2019s laundry hanging in the cathedral of light high above the boulevard Sucre. The cathedral itself was one vast thunderous organ that blew rivers through the streets and shook a fine white nerve-snow down onto the human ant-piles in the doorways and alleys...I went to the Brasserie Du Luxembourg and ordered a gigot and some more red wine. The gigot was a frothing square of fatty gravy drowning large yellow leaves of some vegetable that resembled a washed leek. With it came a bottle of red wine, also Beaujolais, and also fizzy-soft and delicious. I had no inquiries to make about gigots, nor about the Cathedral Of Light, and in fact I was not interested in anything except arranging the wonderful new world of Paris in my head. I walked back up Boulevard Sucre and across Boulevard de Montparnasse to the Maubert Market. I thought to buy an onion. My plans were vague about the rest of the day, but I had to have an onion. I liked them with beer. I would get some cigarettes and an onion and go find that kid in the raincoat...", "1_9_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past nine o'clock in the morning</span> and the paseo had begun\u2014that curious Spanish phenomenon which has no counterpart in any other country. From nine o'clock until sunset certain streets along the Ramblas are closed to traffic, and herds of people wander through the summer sunshine. They walk along the curbs on both sides, using the entire roadway, and every twentieth person or so attempts to hypnotize you. Look into their eyes and you weaken. You go into a trance, and as your eyes glaze, the entire herd moves over and swamps you. Watch your step, or you'll be carried along in the hypnotic tide, until with the entire herd you lurch against the side of a building and go into a trance. The only safety is in a rigid application of keeping your eyes open and staring ahead in the most determined manner. The only thing to do is to keep moving and never mind if you stop short now and then against somebody. Then, too, you make sure that your wallet is safely in your hip pocket when you go into any crowds. The herd, like a school of fish that takes a sharp right-angled turn when a shark swims into its midst, can suddenly and completely change its direction. Any kind of place serves the purpose. It makes no difference...", "2_20_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-one in the evening</span>.\nThe man on his knees in the courtyard was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nBeing appeared to in the very private precincts of one\u2019s park was the least of the discomforts the Martian chatelaines were expected to endure unprotesting.\nThree hours before Mr. $SURNAME0\u2019s involuntary appearance, a crowd of shysters and snoops had gathered outside the gates. Their plan was to have a raffle, the prize being a peek through the solarium windows into the $SURNAME0 mansion.\nThree of the lucky winners got to go.\nFor one short delightful moment, they imagined they had been done in like Nietzschean acrobats by an all-time, world\u2019s record-breaking, fate-worse-than-death surprise.\nThere, in a stale pool of his own making, was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, his back against the pavement, his eyes open, his mouth open, his face the color of a stomach pump.\nFor one short delightful moment, the winners regretted that, really, they had to go on peeking through the solarium windows.", "1_21_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-four in the evening</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time, on the third Tuesday in November, when red-haired Mona A. Rolfe, thirty-two, of Love Canal, New York, purchased a Babyonese Pretzel at the Pretzel-Of-The-Month Club kiosk in the lobby of the Space Control Center in Cleveland, Ohio. Miss Rolfe was visiting friends on the center staff.\nThe pretzel was not a member of the Babyon product line. It had been mixed in an Earth factory, and shipped in frozen form to the Space Control Center.\nThe Space Control Center is part of an orbiting arc of electromagnetic scanners that encircles Earth at the equator. The mission of the center is to make certain there are no monsters or supersonic or subsonic turbine-powered celestial objects anywhere in the sky over Earth.\nThe search is almost continuous, the sky being scanned by mobile units, each unit being hooked to its downtown scanner by heavy-gauge copper cables. The downtown scanner is locked into position by a stellite mooring disk set in bedrock on the outskirts of Cleveland. There is no monster since the mid-20th Century, but every now and then Earth people get the notion to make things fly...", "2_1_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one sixteen in the morning</span>, Earth Prevailer time. That meant seven sixteen on Mars, because Mars whirled on beneath the fire banners of the greater sun.\nAt seven sixteen on Mars, the Martian infantry, artillery, and airforce squads were boarding their ships for the attack on Phoebe, the attack on Phoebe, the attack on Phoebe.\nThe true name for the town where $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 sat was $SURNAME0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 sat on their horses in a dark grove at the north edge of $SURNAME0. To their right was a cemetery. To their left was a playground. In the playground a Corbett or Pugh machine shot basketball balls through a hoop, somehow catching the ball in a net as it fell.\nThe playground was unlighted, and the machine made a clacking racket.\n\"We got no chance, buddy,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2 suddenly.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t answer. He was aghast at the enormous amount he still didn\u2019t know. Somehow he had to find out more.\n\"What do you think really happened to us?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "1_0_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after midnight</span>. The saloon was one of a row of identical red brick buildings. They were quite modern in appearance, with the look of adobe houses that had been hastily converted into business establishments. All had the same railing, a fretted cast-iron affair that carried aloft on ball-and-spindle supports a yard-high figure of a cow, in green. The series of buildings stretched a block and a half.\nNo. 6 was the middle one of the row. It was divided into three parts. In the part directly behind the entrance were the saloon and a Gin Rummy Parlor. The middle section consisted of a corridor, leading to a row of washrooms. At the back, behind the washrooms and the Gin Rummy Parlor, was a large private and reserved cafeteria.\nNo. 6 was an A-1 saloon, according to the moral code of Wyoming. Yet it fell short of being a first-rate saloon, in the view of the cognoscenti. For instance, there was no Bloody Tower in the back, where a tenderfoot could be thrown in charge of the bartender and given a dozen bottles of beer and instructed to see that they didn\u2019t spoil.\nBut No. 6 was...", "2_11_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Exactly where are you going?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"I\u2019d tell you if I knew,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, \"but there\u2019s so much to remember, it could make you crazy.\"\n\"Stay here and rest,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Eat something.\"\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"I\u2019ve got to get started, as soon as possible. I\u2019ve got to get back and convince them that everything that happened after the Fall really did happen.\"\n\"Sounds fine,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"You can take your a.m. and ride with me, if you like,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"I could use the company.\" He got up from the table, picked up the false teeth from his coffee cup, pitched them into the spittoon. \"I\u2019ll tell you one thing I won\u2019t have to admit to anybody,\" he said. \"That I raised a regiment of infantry from hell, filled it with dangerous animals and madmen, and led it across the top of the world\u2014and we all nearly died.\" He picked up the statue of St. Francis. \"I wonder what this is worth,\" he said.", "2_18_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>, Greenwich mean time.\nMrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s telephone was in her sitting room on the second floor of the batik palace. Her bathtub was filled to a soothing height. The water issued a light steam. The steam made an atmospheric allusion to the South Pacific islands where, Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s husband had once hinted, her sexual initiation had taken place.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 lay in the tub like a lily. She was a rather pretty lily, though she was no longer in the first flush of youth, nor even in the second flush.\nFrom the radio in her sitting room came the languorous music of the Blue Nebula Dance Band. The music was being sent by radio-relay to all the ships and stations of the Space Force of the Galaxy, since all ships and stations were supposed to be rocking to the same languorous beat.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 was not rocking. Her head was back, her knees were up, and most of the skin that was visible was white as milk.", "2_17_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the room was very hot, and they were spoiling his life. It was warm in the light, too, but not as warm as it was in the dark, and that was awful.\n\"Ever been in a Turkish prison?\" Candide asked.\n\"You mean where they chain you up in a little cell?\" said Van Vleck. \"Or stand you on an anthill? Boil your drinking water? Wear your thumbs down and make you write confessions?\"\n\"Nothing like that,\" said Candide. \"There are a hundred and forty-four rooms, and music in every one of them. No light, but plenty of nice cool white sheets, if you\u2019re hot. Ain\u2019t that grand?\"\n\"Seems like,\" said Van Vleck.\nCandide straightened his tuxedo. \"Yes,\" he said. \"It is that.\"\n\"It ought to be,\" said Van Vleck.\nA dark shape, bent under the load of a large suitcase and holding a bundle of golf clubs, appeared at the door.\n\"Hy!\" said Candide. \"Hy!\"\n\"What?\" said the new arrival. \"God!\"", "0_3_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-one in the morning</span> when we arrived back\u2014still scared to death. My attorney got out of the car first and took refuge in a phone booth a hundred yards down the road. He made a call, then drove back a few minutes later and handed me the portable. It was Jim Freak, calling from Denver\u2014speaking in an idiotic lisp, using a very strange kind of code. \u201cYour money? Yes...yes...yes...two plus two does not make four, but rather makes a very complex situation, involving you, yourself and several others as well as several similar parcels of land and a safari into the deepest part of darkest Africa, from which you will not return, rather resolved in a most painful and serious fashion...yes...yes...yes...any questions? Glad to have been of service.\u201d What de fuck? I have no idea what he was talking about...except that the money had been paid out, and everything was hunky\u2014dory. We rushed off to the airport for the dawn flight to L.A., both of us eat up with curiosity. I wanted to take the train, but my attorney was afraid to fly. He got that way in Peru.\nMy attorney? Where did this crazy Samoan come from? It...", "2_11_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-five in the morning</span>.\nKensington Gore was three blocks from the castle when she was joined by her old friend $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nWith his usual grand style, $SURNAME0 came gliding down out of the sky, on a roller skate. He wore a linen duster and carried a Tommy automatic rifle at port arms.\nHe managed to look just as good on roller skates, a feat to which few men would have been equal.\n\"Hey, there, Beeber-boy!\" he called to Beeber\u2019s familiar. \"You\u2019re fired! Beat it! Scram!\" $SURNAME0\u2019s attention then became fully vested in Kensington Gore. He stripped the cover off a food warmer, sniffed at its contents, and thumbed a flowered curtaining off another offering: three one-kilo thermo-prisms of Lunar Water ice. \"And welcome to Martian Dessert, Incorporated,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I see,\" said Kensington Gore.", "1_12_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-eight p.m.</span> on Wednesday, December 31, 1998. When I was in a sane state, I used to live in this town.\nSuddenly I felt very alone, like teeth in the sea, like a stone in space, like a mosquito in winter. Snapshot of happy life: me and another guy, eighteen, on a ferry going to Staten Island. He had red hair, green eyes. He was coming home from work at Kaiser Aluminum, the one out in the desert; he was going to play the tuba in a dance band that night. His name was Ray Spindeldlington. He was laughing. His teeth were perfect.\nMy wife did not phone. Nobody phoned. My attorney did not call. Nobody called. I felt as imprisoned as a weatherman on Mars. I could foresee having to abandon any effort to make it to Fernwood\u2014Phillips blvd. I was ready to live on the streets a while. Outside looked better, at least there were people. Somehow I got dressed. My new shoes\u2014I lust after shoes\u2014bruised my toes as I laced them up. I left. That was the first time I walked out on my wife. The second time will be for good. How to explain what happened? Perhaps...", "0_0_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-two ante meridiem</span>.\nThe wheel had done so much as a quarter of a revolution since Pierce\u2019s last visit. There were now four thousand and twenty-seven miles of space between Pierce and the bull.\nNow Pierce regarded the bull again, through the sharp wire fence.\nHe was now of two minds about his approach.\nThe bull was bolder, having drunk more.\nThe bull guessed now that his quarry was a man\u2014and a man made of matter such as did not seem to be plentiful between here and the last moon but one in the Green System.\nAnd now the bull knew Forniss well enough not to charge him.\nExcept when the lurch and roll of the ship suddenly turned Forniss temperamental again and set the black mood back on him.\nThere were two more occasions when Forniss turned sullen and refused to have anything to do with baiting the bull. But by then Pierce had the hang of it and doubled the rate at which he loaded beer into the wheel. And so was able to keep the wheel continually spinning.\nBy one fifteen, the bull had been at it for almost three hours and a half, since one o\u2019clock.\nThe time was one fifteen p.m.", "2_14_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"It says,\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice, \"that you are a lucky bugger. It says that the other miserable creatures on this planet aren\u2019t fit to comb your hair. It says you\u2019re a contender, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014that it looks sorry as hell for the rest of the buggers. And that you have only to say the word and I\u2019ll give you a real big happy life and take real good care of you for the rest of your days.\"\nIt was ten minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon.\nAnd then $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 came to Newport with his crew and his money, to learn what the great secret was that $SURNAME0 had for him.\nHe carried his pasteboard and gilt crown into the office, where $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were waiting for him. $SURNAME1 was assaulted by unexpected fresh cleans smells\u2014lavender and pine needles and the like\u2014coming from $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was beautifully gowned in clean pink and plenty of it. She looked highly civilized\u2014and, suddenly, in comparison with Winifred, very young. $SURNAME0 was in formal attire, too\u2014wearing tails, and white tie.", "0_18_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-eight in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s battalion had been under attack all day. The attacking enemy, who sounded like badly malfunctioning harmoniums, had lost threequarters of their men.\nNow, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the survivors were getting ready for the next attack in the natural lull that always followed the most violent phases of the attack.\n\"They ain\u2019t got a chance, boys\u2014'less they pull a pa\u2019rade steal\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, ticking points off on his greasy fingers. \"Parade steal means fixin\u2019 to come at us in formation, instead of God knows how many different ways, like they done today. We know how to handle mass attacks better\u2019n anybody else!\" he said virtuously. He had been a big hero all day.\nHe hadn\u2019t figured out yet just why he had been such a hero\u2014just how, by spraying the air around him with bullets, he had won an asteroid full of dead friends. He had been infected with a murderous glee by the act. He had sprayed the air and by God he had won! He only wished the space ship had had a radio in it so he could have called General Britannica, told him what to do.\nBut he...", "1_21_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>. The red hunting horn sounded a clear note, and the crowd cheered and stamped on the wooden floor of the stadium.\nJames $SURNAME2 stood in the box and smiled and waved his program at the crowd.\nLenore Beadsman, wrists and ankles locked and chained to her perch at the speaker\u2019s table, leaned to her left and smiled and waved as, in the Sportarena in Cleveland, Ohio, twenty thousand people unlocked their ankles from the beer barrels and rose to their feet.\n/c/\n\"God damn it, give us a game!\"\n\"Is that somebody in there with you, son?\"\n\"What do you think?\"\n\"Listen, you\u2019re talking through Reynolds pipe tobacco, here, son.\"\n\"Aren\u2019t you listening?\"\n\"I\u2019m listenin\u2019, son.\"\n\"Then what do you think about what I\u2019m saying?\"\n\"Awright. Now listen. You\u2019re just going to have to sit tight.\"\n\"I\u2019m not going to sit tight. I want to talk to somebody! I can\u2019t get anybody to talk to! I want Lenore Beadsman!\"\n\"You...", "0_13_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span> in San Francisco, according to $FIRSTNAMEB2. And at that moment a man barged hurriedly into the cabin.\nHe was a bad-looking man with red betty scars on his face. His greasy, curly black hair was cropped so close to his head that it gave the impression of being on fire. His one good eye had a cold gleam.\nHe uttered a pleased laugh when he saw the two girls gagged, tied and helpless.\nAnd then his face fell.\nThere were three wraiths at the window, holding him under suspicion.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014that fellow with the bad-news face is right out there, at the window\u2014\" whispered $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"He\u2019s arguing with three others. \"\n\"Your housemates, I gather,\" said $SURNAME1 quietly. He stepped over to the man who had just come into the cabin. He put his hand on the man\u2019s shoulder.\nThe man made a stiff smile. His one good eye flinched, then hardened. He had been thoroughly licked by life, that man. He no longer had any illusions. All he had left was unflinching hatred.\n\"What\u2019s your name?\" said $SURNAME1 gently.", "1_7_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-seven in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, had been skipping inside the Solar System for eight and a half minutes.\nIn the space of nine minutes, they were going to skip all the way from the Earth to Mars.\nA beam of intense gold light split the sky from $SURNAME0\u2019s estate on Newport Beach to the planet Mercury. A heatless, piston-less spitfire was clamped to the beam. In this way, $SURNAME0, his dog, and their parabolic antenna were shot into space at a speed at least equal to the speed of light.\nThey were bound on an intersecting course with Mars. The two were going to meet nine minutes later.\nA beautiful, clear-eyed woman appeared at the small window in the gold wall of the Villa $SURNAME0 mansion. She tapped on the window. She wanted to be let in. She was Miss Frances Fortune, Mr. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s secretary. Miss Frances Fortune was forty-six years old, with wonderful legs. She had $SURNAME0\u2019s schedule for the day in her hands, and she wanted to get started right away. Miss Frances Fortune was no fool. Frances Fortune,...", "1_13_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one nineteen in the afternoon</span>, Wednesday, and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, twenty-seven, and Stony Stevenson, thirty, were on a bench just inside the sculpture gallery of the Crystal Palace. They could see the purple German band on the fountain rim Above them.\nVarious sculptures, swept by painted breezes, moved in time to the music.\n$SURNAME1, who had never liked saloons or given parties, couldn\u2019t imagine a more beautiful or surprising place to be on a bright day in spring.\n\"Help you, sir?\" said a voice.\n$SURNAME1 turned to see who was speaking to him. The only stranger in the room was a trombone-voiced woman selling tubes of strawberry-flavored ice cream. The trombone voice seemed totally out of place.\n$SURNAME1 smiled at the strawberry saleswoman. \"Those sculptures are not what I expected to see here\u2014not on the grounds of a palace, anyhow.\"\n\"How\u2019s that?\" said Stony Stevenson, lighting a fresh cigarette off the stub of the last.\n\"The Titanic,\" said $SURNAME1 vaguely.\n\"That was a movie,\" said Stony, \"about a ship Blown up by an iceberg.\"\n\"Blown up?\" said $SURNAME1. He seemed...", "2_9_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-two in the morning</span>. The temperature outside the hut was fifty below. A mean wind was blowing.\nThe time was nine forty-three in the morning. The two members of the work detail finished their corn-juice. They had nothing to do between now and the arrival of Sarge, aside from a little mental preparation of the great confrontation that they were confident would take place between them and Sarge, if only they could think up the right things to say to him.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was trying to think up a neat little laundry that he could hint had been the work of $FIRSTNAMEB1: a bit of subtle revenge. $FIRSTNAMEB1, meanwhile, was thinking up the lousiest, stankiest, most unworkable, dangerous, and stupid exemplum he could think of\u2014one that would, in all likelihood, only make Sarge more steely and humorless than ever.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked sideways at $FIRSTNAMEB1 sideways, as though out of the corner of his eye. In actuality, he was looking squarely at $FIRSTNAMEB1 from six inches away, over the top of his half-lens prescription thick spectacles. The thick spectacles amended $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s myopia to the extent that he could see things six inches away.", "0_7_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and I stood staring up at the elevated tracks, watching for the coming of the train that would take us to $SURNAME0\u2019s estate. To our right was the curving wall of one of the old three-decker Boston waterfront ghettos.\nThe old houses were being torn down and replaced by shiny new office buildings. Noisy mechanical shovels tore into the foundations of the old houses, exposing black mouldy cellar larders, human privies. Since human beings had lived there, the larders and the privies stank.\nNew England has a tradition of excellence in making ordure.\nAcross the street from the worksite was a wine store whose window was full of wax dummies, sexless and horrible. Their noses were all large red rubber bulbs. The bodies were stuffed with excelsior or old mattresses. The heads were formed of papier-m\u00e2ch\u00e9 over starched collars and cuffs. They were the ultimate in male neckwear. They were called Pilgrim collars.\nThe original Pilgrims hadn\u2019t worn them. They had come to America to escape whatever it was that was stultifying Europe, and they brought the best of European culture with them.", "2_20_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight o'clock in the evening</span> of the day that the pulp-yo-yo was to take $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1, skipping and twining, back to Earth. It was eight o\u2019clock in the evening, Eastern Standard Time, of December sixteenth.\nOne minute to eight. An almost illegible tick of the great clock in $SURNAME0\u2019s head said that it was one minute to eight.\nA telescope on a tripod before the house magnified the view a billion times, so that the square inches of wall in the mansion covered nearly forty acres of primeval red Earth.\nA seven-piece band was parading through the square inches of wall.\nHap Cheese was leading the band, directing it.\nSeated in the band-stand, playing regal airs on a golden tuba, was Beazley, the former Martian war-sub commander. He wore red-white-and-blue sparkling spangles, and a nimbus of fake diamonds.\nTo one side of the mansion, in the Opal Region of Mercury, a shaft, blue, girdered, and headless, was rising thirty-five hundred miles up out of the purple sea against whose shores the mansion rested.", "0_3_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-five in the morning</span> when my attorney and I came bumping up on the rutted asphalt of the airport in Acomita; the Santa Fe bus left the border at midnight and would not be along for another five hours. And there was nothing to do in Acomita except get drunk. Neither of us felt like it. We checked into a motel and sat in the room staring at each other.\nMy attorney was in bad shape. \u201cHow many did you have?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cFifteen,\u201d he moaned. \u201cI think it was fifteen.\u201d He shook his head. \u201cI\u2019ve never felt so bad. I never want to eat again. Why did I do that?\u201d Then a new fear struck him. \u201cWhere are the others?\u201d he said. \u201cDid you eat any of them?\"\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said. \u201cI let them go after we took off from the runway.\u201d We stared at each other.\n\u201cJesus,\u201d he said. \u201cYou\u2019ve got to get me off this bleeding thing.\" He leaned back on the bed and closed his eyes; his voice changed to a Texas twang...", "2_9_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-three in the morning</span>, and with the appearance of the bus the shrieking birds had fled in all directions, having gone at last the limits of their respective capacities for filth, horror, and outrage. The birds fled. But the rim of the sun did not reappear, though it was so far clear of the horizon that had anyone been at the Bombard game room across the fields he might have enjoyed playing a game of chance. The fields themselves were lighter shades of gray.\nThere came into being, just then, a baby who was to play enough of a part in our story to justify discussion here. The baby\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEB2, and his mother was $FIRSTNAMEF0. $FIRSTNAMEF0 was one of the persons, like Sir $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill, who had been born not of woman but of a woman who was to be. $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s father was to be a man named $FIRSTNAMEB0. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was welcome.\nThe next thing to appear was a big ugly clump of barbed wire. The barbed wire was wrapped around a narrow plank.\nThe plank was silver-soldered to a heavy wooden bipod.\nThe bipod was mounted on a thick steel plate.", "1_18_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six nineteen in the evening</span>, and the little house alone on its promontory was already growing mysteriously dark. Light died on the floor, in the ceiling, in the window-glass, as sometimes in the heart, thought the lighthearted man at the table, looking up and brushing a crumb from his brown moustache. So it ends, he thought. So ends a perfect day. He took a mouthful of wine and smiled. He felt like a symbol in a puzzle, like a brown-moustache man in a top hat on an envelope. As if he were looking at himself in a mirror, though there were no mirrors in the house. He was reflecting himself in the window, in the dulled panes through which nothing could be seen now, since the day was nearly over. Through the panes dull and gray like the rounds of a battery of eroded cannon he was looking, and smiling, up at his own inverted self; for, since the day was nearly over and he was far from unhappy, he felt friendly and peaceful, and the other unfrightened, unreproachful, just a little hall-grimed, like himself. High overhead and far out of reach a gannet flew to its perch in the wall, and over the...", "1_22_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty in the evening</span>. We were staggering down the Plateau bawling for a taxi to take us home. \u201cJulie Babe is gone, we've got her forever,\u201d I bawled. \u201cEven old filthy $FIRSTNAMEB3 cannot get her back again.\u201d\nIn the morning $FIRSTNAMEF4 called and told me that guy from San Francisco had to hose off the floor of the car because $FIRSTNAMEB3 had thrown up all over it.\nWhen we got back to the pad it was eleven o'clock. Camille came out of the bedroom and yelled at $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201cI'll kill you!\u201d I jumped around with my belly-laugh Groucho Marx bit.\n\u201cWhy, Camille, darling, I was only trying to pick up your friend Freddie.\u201d\n\u201cBlah!-ugh!-ugh!\u201d Camille hid her face. \u201cCan't you do anything except make trouble? You're the most terrible thing that's ever happened to poor Ray-baby.\u201d Ray was her little boy, and had red hair like $FIRSTNAMEB3.\n\u201cDon't be silly, Camille, someday you'll realize I was only laughing.\u201d I went out and had a beer in the...", "1_7_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. It was exactly forty-six years, six months, and twelve days since $SURNAME1 had burst, screaming horribly, out of the little pink cottage near the University of Wisconsin.\nHe was precisely two meters tall, weighed exactly ninety-eight kilograms, had the features and build of a Ganymede, and was the 23,460,157,645,681,127,285,671,384,847,628th son of $FIRSTNAMEB0. So said the Army records.\nAnd now his son was pulling him out of a dark, dripping cave in the mountains of Mercury.\nWhat he saw out there horrified him\u2014haunted him.\nWhile he was imprisoned in the cave, a war had taken place on Earth. In most ways, it had been a very creditable performance on the part of the Army. The Army had, in the space of a fortnight, swept triumphantly over all of Asia, all of Europe, and most of Africa.\nThe Army moved fast and what it couldn\u2019t use or couldn\u2019t shoot it buried.\nThe commanders on Earth could not accept the possibility of defeat, so the commanders on Mercury had taken it on themselves to supply the Earth armies with whatever morale reinforcement was necessary.\nAnd...", "2_9_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe little meadow by the granite boulder was crowded with people.\nMost of them were out of their minds on drugs given them by various authorities.\nThe mood of the crowd was ugly and restless.\nThe people spat at the commander and $FIRSTNAMEB0 when they were strung up.\nThey pushed away their fellows who attempted to give the commander and $FIRSTNAMEB0 water, or to fan them in the hot sun.\nA pretty stewardess was pushed away by a sour-faced man in a uniform. The man in uniform snorted a dose of purple-blue fairy dust from a film container that had once held pimento-stuffed olives. He stopped sneering when a muscular, thick-lipped woman broke his wrist for him.\nNo one of the crowd cheered when the commander and $FIRSTNAMEB0 managed to grin at each other under their masks. A man in a stovepipe hat below them laughed hectically, beat his chest. No one paid any attention to him. He unbuttoned his pantaloons.\n\"You see what they forcing me to do, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" he said to $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Who they?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He was having trouble thinking, thanks to the drug in his system.", "0_5_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-nine in the morning</span>. Great puddles of shadow lay around the hall. The clocks were banging a six-o'clock Chico-tarafat. In snatches, thousands of muffled cab-horns sounded. Red and green rocket bombs banged and flashed in the sky above. A trumpeting ambulance went skidding by. In the background was a ceaseless growling roar that made you think immediately of a time machine, a helicopter, a flying wing, and a housing development. The roar was there just beyond the window, which was only dark blue sky, dark crater-filled clouds, black tips of trees and bedsprings, and oily smoke.\nAt five thirty-two that same Sunday morning, a fifteen-year-old alert and willful boy named Metzger slipped off the playground of Sail Aweigh Elementary School and headed for home, humming The Song From Manta Ray. A great purpose animated him. He had recently discovered girls. Immediately his universe was transformed from dust and ashes to zither music. Things made sense again. He was in a completely different cosmos\u2014almost a different species. He was like a man from Mars bursting into a legislative session at the United Nations and wiring his Manifesto direct to the Secretary General.", "2_18_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nA cellophane-wrapped lime freeze stood on a table on the porch of the $SURNAME1 Home. $SURNAME0, Fern, and the Twins emptied it out into punchbowls, and then spaded up great quantities of ice and rock salt to make the punch salty.\nIn the great ballroom of the palace, the committee in charge of Hospitality made tens of thousands of punchbowls ready. Trash cans later would have to be taken around to most of the classrooms, to receive the punchbowls that would not be used.\nAt six minutes after six, the chimes of the great carillon in the Campanile to the north of the palace began to play the Moonlight Sonata. The armies under the scheming $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were scheduled to bunch at the base of the Campanile from five until six. This bunching was to be exploited by the master of ceremonies at the timed tick of six.", "0_7_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>. The sun illuminated the rooms and hallways of a luxurious Washington apartment house. The building was warmed by electric heat.\nThe apartment house was on the 1600 block of Connecticut Avenue Northwest.\nThe apartment house was owned by a real-estate holding company that was a subsidiary of a public utility holding company that was a subsidiary of a major holding company that was a subsidiary of a holding company that was a subsidiary of a holding company that was a subsidiary of a corporate conglomerate. This conglomerate had its financial headquarters in Chicago, but its corporate headquarters were in New York City.\nThe President of the conglomerate lived in the Washington apartment house.\nThe President\u2019s name was Norman Chefredoglio, a.k.a. \"The Annoying Tycoon.\" He was forty-eight, single, handsome, uncultured, cunning, and rich. He had a genius for making money.\nChefredoglio made money by finagling corporation stocks.\nThe inspiration for Chefredoglio\u2019s \"system\" came from a stack of annualReports of the Securities and Exchange Commission that he had found, one day, in his apartment house\u2019s basement incinerator. To a stake in every government-securities fraud that the SEC uncovered...", "0_3_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty in the morning</span>. I was well into the business of being a Deadpan Ganoozle. I played my part in the Great Circus for another hour, and another hour, until finally dawn came to Los Angeles and I had to flee from American Century Amphitheater\u2014leaving behind over $200 worth of food and booze, which the waiters eagerly offered me . . .\nI drove straight to the airport, doubled back through a parking lot, left the Great White Whale in an Oakland ghetto, took a (hissing) taxi to the airport, checked some huge bags onto a Seattle flight, then dashed to a ???gate to catch the very first flight of the morning to Seattle\u2014just escaping, by pure fluke, the horrifying high\u2014fever final scene of the dawn. Scofflaw that I am, I got onto that Seattle flight and huddled there in the corner, forcing myself to sleep...while all around me the dawn sickness weasels began to gnaw: old ladies on the prowl for hamburgers, pederastic businessmen taking one last chance for a fond last fuck before departure, kids screaming, fog creeping into the cockpit, stewardesses hurrying pre\u2014m -m dash, a big Southern chuckle from the emergency door,...", "0_13_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>. The last person I saw before leaving the grounds was a man standing motionless in his front yard, a handsome, middle-aged man and a good one, possibly a dermatologist, watching the parade with fingers clasped behind his back, peering through eyes with no folly in them\u2014an awe so vast was there in them as to almost scare you if you looked into them\u2014at the sound of his flag\u2019s march song, as momentous and proud as the song of a marching band of extraterrestrials saluting in the Emperor\u2019s presence.\nOh say can you see\u2014the Elephant ran away...\nI went in to Denver to see the District Attorney, and stayed till the bailiff kicked me out of the courtroom, and it was four thirty before I got through to the local PD in Vegas and found out what room The Brain had moved to. The cop who gave me the information sounded far away, like he was calling from inside a cardboard packingbox. \u201cI really hate to be the one to tell you this,\u201d he said. \u201cShe\u2019s down at the Chamber of Commerce, at their new Caravan Court on Paradise Boulevard . . . .\u201d He hung up. I...", "2_0_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-six a.m.</span> Somehow I had wound up on a bridge. I looked down and saw water on both sides of the car; I seemed to be over a river or a canal. What kind of water? It was impossible to tell. No time to worry about it. Death was running beside me now, keeping pace\u2014Total Death\u2014an invisible/unknowable/unfearable companion who could also travel in the emptiness between all movement, all time cycles.\nI opened the door on the driver\u2019s side and lurched out of the car. What a sweet piece of machinery, I thought; what a lovely speed machine! I picked up the car and tried to drop it into the water, but I couldn\u2019t make it work. I finally had to punch the thing over the side with my feet. It dropped about thirty feet and exploded on the water with a big white gush of flame, throwing off chunks of wheel and headlight. I heard an anguished cry from somebody in the darkness beneath the bridge.", "0_23_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-six in the evening</span>. Vapors of ether and cans of DDT were accumulating inches over Stanley\u2019s head while he stood in the hallway and watched Bombardo rumble through a series of what seemed to be experiments in unusual billiard shots.\nThe shots involved a living room inside, a trick of the light, it looked like, for the living room was crammed with billiard tables. At first glance, Bombardo made it seem as though there were only a few tables. It was only on closer scrutiny that the tables multiplied, layer by layer. The outermost layer consisted of two tables, facing each other. The inner layer involved four tables that formed the corners of a box around the two tables in the front. And in the back of the box there was a pimply triangle that had a total of ten tables in it.\nThrough all this maze curved Bombardo calmly, bent over, tchotchke in mouth, delivering shots with what seemed to be a very faint, high-pitched moan.\nThe moan was just conversation, however. Bombsardo was talking to himself, having a fine time. \"Balls, balls, sweet little balls,\" whispered Bombardo as he leaned over one of the embedded tables and played a shot that involved sending the...", "1_15_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty in the afternoon</span>, and Mars was much farther from Earth now than it had been the night before. The Earth was approaching Mars. The space ships were about to come much closer together than they had been the night before.\nFor instance, Mars was now eighty-three million seven hundred thousand and forty miles from Waterhole No. 3, as the space ships flew.\nWhen the sun set at Waterhole No. 3 that night, it would set at a different time from the sun\u2019s setting at $FIRSTNAMEB2. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was eight minutes ahead of Waterhole No. 3 by Earth time. In other words, time went 48,000 feet per hour faster at $FIRSTNAMEB2 than at the more remote Waterhole No. 3.\nAt the time the rain ended at $FIRSTNAMEB2, the sun was only twenty-eight minutes away from setting at Waterhole No. 3. It was still a hot autumn day at Waterhole No. 3.\nThe space ship that was signaling the code letter QT, announcing that everything was all right, was the $SURNAME0, the same ship that had carried $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog to Mars. The pilot was Katherine $SURNAME0.\nThe $SURNAME0 had a crew of one\u2014the unsaintly...", "0_14_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-six in the afternoon</span>. I had been lying in a motel swimmingpool with my attorney when the call came. I was drinking white wine spritzers...and the first thing I rememberwas my attorney running down thelined like a football referee, shouting: \u201cHere comes LucyLucy Lucy Lucy!\u201d And then he dove off the high boardwith a grunt, slammed into the water, made his way to the far end and\u2014fearing, perhaps, that the newswoman was callingfrom the bottom of the pool\u2014waded back and crouched near the ladder, gasping for air. Which was my cue to climb out of the pool.\nLucy? I thought. Jesus Christ. There was only one person in this world who could be calling me at this particular moment. My attorney knew that, but he was too crazed to say anything. He waited by th e ladder in a crouch, eyeing me owlishly, gasping for air. There was no doubt in his mind: I would be out of the pool in exactly eight seconds, he estimated...and then, like some startled animal, I would make my way to the phone, treading water frantically as I reached for the receiver on the sixth ring. I was capable, at moments of...", "1_2_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-two in the morning</span>, according to the witness chronometers on the dashboard. Twenty-seven minutes to Go. $FIRSTNAMEB0, poised in the pillbox, was very uncomfortable. He was unused to silence. There had always, in the past, been somebody doing something\u2014a radio going, a child squalling, someone snoring, someone telling somebody else to shut up. In $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s barrack in the space recruits\u2019 barracks, there had always been snoring and squalling to drown out the crazy put-put-putting noises the bunkmates made in their sleep when they dreamed of how good it was going to be when they finally got Earthside.\n\"I\u2019m warning you, foom!\" a barrackmate would say, kicking the dreamer who was putting out the crazy noises. \"I don\u2019t care who you are\u2014I don\u2019t care what you did on Mars. I\u2019m from Brooklyn. You foom from that bunk, or I\u2019m gonna hose you down.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had drawn a straw to decide which barrackmate would sleep on the bottom bunk next to his. That barrackmate always turned out to be the most threatened, dangerous and crazy barrackmate in the barr...", "1_2_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-one in the morning</span>, in Washington, D.C., a Sunday night. At that precise moment, a minute before two thirty-two, a tiny creature known as a bloodwatcher flew, out of a chink in the leaded crystal window of Kurt D. Bottweill\u2019s study and made for a chink in the window of a pure sea-shell the size of a pigeon the scimitar-blade beak of which was already nipping fresh human blood. The room was dark, but the pigeon inside perceived invaders by infrared light. His beak nipped again. His vast pinions beat furiously. Lunging, missing, lunging again, the bloodwatcher collided viciously with a curious glass sailboat model that had rested on a ledge by the window. Its mast dented, the sailboat tottered, fell. Its broken hull sent fragments skittering, some dashing showering patterns on the cruiser-window to one side and the lighthouse window to the other. The wounded bird fell, fluttering, to his ledge. There, ignoring his pain, he began cleaning his wounded wing with his long beak. Outside, the bloodwatcher fell with a tiny clatter to the carpet by the couch of Miss Priss, who...", "0_11_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to noon</span> by the clock when Hilarius stood before Ingersoll. He cocked his head first left, then right, receiving the strengths of both hemispheres of the sun through his parted eyelids. The space ship went drawn, drawn, drawn in his field of vision.\nIt went past the zenith\u2014\"Perhaps,\" said Hilarius, \"you have no money and feel hostility toward Authority, since money is its tool. But what then?\" He squinted up the sun, then down the sun, then brought both rays running together on the harmonium keys.\nIt was four and a half minutes to noon.\n\"What then?\" said Hilarius, having brought all time to a standstill.\nDriblette, on the rim of vision, ballooned to bulksomeness or dwindled to insignificance as he listened, stared, and digested.\n\"What then?\" said Hilarius, exploring every neighborhood in All Time for his man. \"There\u2019s more to it, perhaps, than you realize. I never thought to ask for money, for instance. I want it, naturally. Who doesn\u2019t? There\u2019s the request itself. Perhaps enough has been said about it...", "0_15_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I had been away from my office for almost two hours. I took a taxi to Times Square, where I had lunch in a cellar restaurant. It was pleasant being alone after all the champagne I had drunk. I did not even feel any desire for a drink. At four o'clock I went to my office to begin the search for a new secretary.\nDolly Sisters Go West\nA strange thing happened to me when I went back to work. It showed me how a man can get hooked on the road back to normalcy. No sensible, responsible business man ought to have such incidents in his life, and I regret the telling of this one as much as anything that ever happened to me. But I want the salient points of this story to be told, and this incident is salient for reasons that do not appear until I get to California.\nDolly Merrill was my secretary. She had been with me four years and she was as efficient and devoted a secretary as any man ever had. She was almost forty years old, and I had engaged her only after I found she could type eighty words a minute. I had insisted on this sixty years ago, when I embarked on the world.\nDolly had been married once to a man who...", "1_23_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span> of this particular Earth day. The place was Room 223-124 of the $SURNAME0 Museum of Science,acket and Space Travel, on the island of Newport near the town of Newport, the state of Rhode Island.\nWho were the parties to this farcical bit of chrono-synclastic infidelity?\nThere was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the richest man in the Solar System till the smashing of the Communist economy put him out of business.\nThe space ship $FIRSTNAMEF0 was his. After all, the thing was made in a factory over which he held proxy shares. He had been promised the first space ship to come off the assembly line.\nThere was Salo, a Earthling butler of the kind common before the solar catastrophes. Salo was unique. He had been born in a private museum of stuffed monsters in Boston. He had spent most of his life as a mascot of one army or another.\nHe claimed to be the last of his kind. He was certainly the last to wear the gold-threaded livery of the generals he had served. His race had been genteelly rapacious, industriously dishonest\u2014and temperamentally disorganized in the face of any emotional challenge. Salo had been generous with...", "1_20_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>, by Mr. $SURNAME0\u2019s quartz movement in his left eye.\nBecoming at that moment Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, $FIRSTNAMEB4, seven feet one inch in height and weighing 220 pounds, was just raising her right paw, which was six inches longer than her left.\nShe was still rising on her hind legs as a demonstration of strength.\nAn attendant was tamping a dark brown tobacco into the bowl of her bronzed and liver-strapped meerschaum long pipe.\nHer amber eyes were rolling, not with ecstasy, but with rage. The tobacco in her pipe was a token affair. The pipe\u2019s bowl was a false bottom in her bronzed leather bridle.\nThe rage was caused by the impingement of Frau Sigi on her beloved and painstakingly-trained disciplined nostrils. Frau Sigi\u2019s approach was heralded by the stench of a primary culture medium on which a particularly loathsome form of streptococcus was being grown in one of the suite\u2019s laboratories.\nThe streptococcus was being grown in the liquids ordinarily ejected from the stomach and bowels. The liquids were the by-products of the Martian regimen under which...", "0_9_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine o'clock in the morning</span> when I dug my old Hudson up out of the mountain at the Fort Harrison Hotel, and then I didn't know what I was going to do. The check I'd left with the manager covered the car and my hotel bill, but I didn't want to go back and face that manager, because I knew that no matter what I told him, he'd want the money. He, was a tight-assed little bastard and I knew he had my number, and I knew I couldn't wheedle him, or bully him, or in any way make him see my point of view. Not that I had a point of view; all I wanted to do was to get rid of him. But he was making a big thing about being left alone with cash for both my stay and my car rental, and in some twisted way I was sure he'd detain me, enforce my payment, and in the end it would all come out. So I decided to take off, and then I'd worry about cars and all that kind of thing. This was, of course, the obvious sensible course to take, but I couldn't admit it to myself-it was just that in the end I told lies out of fear. In just a little while I realized it was...", "2_9_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nThe steel plate lay on a tilting pedestal in a thick concrete foundation. The concrete foundation was surrounded by a pipe trench full of wet Portland cement.\nThe cement was still wet.\nOn the rim of the trench were stenciled the words PEOPLE\u2019S REPUBLIC OF MARYLAND, DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC WORKS, DISTRICT NO. 12. The stencils were neatly and freshly done.\nThe fields were bathed in clear sunlight. And the moist light made a sheen on the barbed wire, on the plank, on the plate, on the pedestal, and on the fresh wet Portland cement.\nThe wind was coming in from the east, and it shook the barbed wire. The barbed wire sang with a low, steady note.\nThe plank had a piston on a rod welded to the top. It was supported in the center by the bipod. In front of it was a swivel seat. A man could sit on the swivel seat, swivel with the plank, take aim through optics, and fire at will.\nThe ruined face with the big blue eyes appeared in the window before the clock struck ten.", "1_3_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-six in the morning</span>, and the ugly part of the trip was about to begin. I stood in line under the forty watts of Colou d'Or, a French restaurant, waiting for Lucy, and the sailor rushed up, his face contorted with the imipolex horror, wanting to talk.\n\u201cO my God,\u201d he said. \u201cLook what that bitch put on my arm! You see? Red worms? Fuck! Get 'em off me! O Jesus! Ow! Shit!\u201d The sailor continued to jabber as I pulled him into the crowds and raced for the Tea House as fast as I could move, spraddled under the weight of the pack and icebox. As we lurched across Forty-Second Street, I tried to explain my position.\n\u201cLucy's not a bitch! Lured you, maybe, but not a bitch. We're the good guys, really! Long story. Drugs, maybe not what they seem.\u201d But he wasn't listening. He seemed scarcely aware of his surroundings.\nHe went into an epileptic rage, scratching madly at his wrist. \u201cBurn 'em off! Flush 'em out! Don't let me go back to Mars like this!", "2_10_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-three in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 mounted his breathing apparatus and clamped the airtank of oxygen to his back.\nHe was preparing himself\u2014preparing himself to fly to Earth. He would blow up the barracks of his battalion, blow up all the barrack buildings of Mars, and then fly to Earth, in an attack of such crushing surprise and ferocity that the war would be over almost before it had begun. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s attack would be, as $FIRSTNAMEB0 saw it, the all\u2014inside attack of a man whose home has been murderously invaded.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s plans were vague, details nonexistent. He would achieve the greatest damage possible with the least loss possible to himself.\nHe would fly at once to his favorite tavern on Earth\u2014the tavern in which the translucent uncrumpled governess-girdled photo of his mate was pinned to the wall.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 would hug the photo to him as he died.\nThe names of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mate and home town were on the back of the photo.\nThe names were $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infinitization, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s lovely mate, and Newport, Rhode Island, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s lovely home town.", "0_2_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-seven in the morning</span> and I had left the big desk in the lobby twenty minutes before. I had spoken to a man with a shackled foot, a man who ran the elevator in the morning, but only operated the register at night. He promised to let me sleep in one of the rooms. Just then, I took my eyes off the street and the teeming thousands coming and going like ants in early dawn and looked around the lobby. A tall, lean man with a crew cut, wearing a beautifully tailored business suit and carrying a brief case, was talking to the desk clerk. He wanted a room with a bath, and the desk clerk was giving him the high sign to wait a minute. As I walked to the elevator, they both looked at me with sudden interest as they remembered my attempt to register. I got in the elevator, and when I came back with the shackled man, the mystery man was gone. \u201cHe okay?\u201d said the elevator-runner. \u201cNever mind who he is; I'll stay here. Send up a pot of coffee and a dozen doughnuts to the room by six o'clock.\u201d\n\u201cRight.\u201d\nNow it was two twenty-seven and I was sitting in a big leather chair in...", "0_21_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The last rocket had come and gone.\nWhat had seemed to be a well-defined route for the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic infundibulated out in space near the shore of Lake Michigan. The crowd turned and faced the opposite way.\nA small shower of meteors was flaring high in the northeast.\nNobody knew exactly what time it was.\n\"I love this old world,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, mysteriously.\nCHAPTER SIXTEEN\nMkrumah seemed on the point of speaking.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, who had been on parade rest since the boy soldier\u2019s approach, relaxed and shuffled his feet. \"Uh\u2014what?\" he said, mistrusting his ears.\n\"Thanks,\" said Mkrumah. \"Thanks a lot.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked around. \"Did somebody hit him?\" he asked.\n\"Those colored troops\u2014they didn\u2019t mean us no harm,\" said Mkrumah.\n\"Mean us harm?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Why, they were so happy to see us, they couldn\u2019t stand it. Man, we was smiling and shakin\u2019 hands and all\u2014everybody so happy to see everybody.\"\n\"Time to go,\" said the boy...", "2_21_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-two in the evening</span>.\nThe crowd had no particular attention for Evelyn or Candy Mandible or any of the others still locked in Stonecipheco\u2019s many washrooms and vandalized private offices. The crowd did, on the other hand, have an enormous undivided attention for the aisles and signs of the Home Shopping Network studios.\n\u201cToday you\u2019ll find and bring to me,\u201d said Melinda, \u201ca one and one-quarter-inch plastic pipe that can carry one and one-quarter gallons of fluid per minute, and that can be threaded together at either end to form a pipe of any length desired, and that includes inside your buildings and even under the ground.\u201d\n\u201cHow the hell are we supposed to know what that is?\u201d Martin ScadliGalg said to himself quietly. The time was nine twenty-three.\n\u201cEveryone, everyone who buys even one of these packages of delicious Natural VitaBrites will also receive a membership in this magnificent and wonderful company that I am part owner of,\u201d Melinda said. \u201cWe\u2019re all one big happy family, at Melinda\u2019s Home Shopping Network.\u201d", "1_20_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-eight in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF0-now moving with consummate wariness with the $185,000 check in her pocket-approached the destination mentioned in the complex and apparently garbled message her husband had scribbled. She came to a secluded and magnificent old building in one of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s numerous holdings-a building that had been a private home, a madhouse for the insane, a private home again, an editing plant for pornographic films, and now was an elegant maison de rendezvous for four remarkably beautiful courtesans.\nWhere $SURNAME1 lived was anybody\u2019s guess. His holdings were labyrinthine. Inevitably, his Newport mansion was rumored to be among the choicest structures in town\u2014but it had not belonged to him when he bought it, and he had refused to say who had owned it before him.\nThe four courtesans were not at home. They were out upon the town, amusing themselves in the only way they knew how: by trying, as exhibitors in the Variety, to pick up the highest-grade male specimens they could. Their highest grade, so far, had been $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\n\"I say, old chap\u2014\" said the courtesan who answered the door, \"are you trying to...", "0_15_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. Much later, $FIRSTNAMEB2 knew, it would be considered one of the most important feats in modern history. $FIRSTNAMEB2 wasn't troubled by such thoughts.\nIt was important only to him, and to his safety.\nFor all future time, every agent who had made it to Mars within an hour of the scheduled takeoff for the Titan would be respected, indeed glorified.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was no more impressed by glory than by importance.\nHe couldn\u2019t have named the village he had come from, or the name of his tribe, or the name of his species. He didn't have a tribe or a species. He couldn\u2019t even recall his original given name on Earth.\nThat was part of the conditioning he had undergone before taking the space-warp to Earth Port, where a subspace field sorted people into Martian fights, according to their destinations.\nIt relieved the anxiety of travel at two hundred times the speed of light, which had intimidated space pilots before the discovery of the space warp.\nThe discovery had made unnecessary a very large part of the giant organization for relocating Earthmen on Mars. Only certain individuals, however, were invited to participate as agents in the relocation scheme. They had to be able to recognize other...", "1_19_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-nine in the evening</span> when I arrived in Reno for a three-week stay at the annual National District Attorneys\u2019 Conference on Narcotics and Dangerous Drugs. The first business session was not scheduled until the next morning at ten, but I wanted to be on hand early, to state my business and get things rolling.I checked into a suite in the Hotel Sagebrush and began calling on my old friends around town. None were home. As a matterOf fact, I finally realized\u2014the phone book was damn near empty for the first time since I\u2019d been coming here on my annual junkets. Most of the old crew was dead or retired. Far as my personal use was concerned, the thing to do was forget about finding any narcotics in this town and simply concentrate on ammunition, amunintion...gear,..stuff I\u2019d need for my important work at the conference.\nBut those scumbags at the desk would never understand that. They'd arrest me under the Walker Act and put me in jail. This time they\u2019d send me up for nine years. No question about it. I would have to struggle with my lifestyle; find ways to cope. And probably nothing would come of it, anyway. I didn't have any idea what they...", "0_3_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-eight in the morning</span>, and I wound up alone in the huge Las Vegas airport, rumpled, sweatin\u2019, and bleary-eyed. There was no one anywhere. The whole building seemed dead. God knows what the night shift looked like. I wandered around aimlessly until I found the \u201cFilipino\u201drestaurant where I\u2019d told the desk man to have a samisser served. Approaching the phone, I was horrified to see the only lightshard in the place...it was on top of the goddamn This and a line-up of DO NOT DISTURB signs. Jesus! I skulked over and tried to notice it from a distance with one eye shut, but the more I squinted and held my breath, the clearer it be came . . . you son of a bitch!, I hissed at the thing. It was winking and blinking at me mischievously. It Knew. Itand suddenly I had a flashback to my brother, who after ten years of dealing has gone permanently insane and lives in a grass hut in Saigon, raving about Fats Domino. \u201cYou know somethin\u2019?\u201d he hissed at me. \u201cThis country is...", "2_10_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAnd then, suddenly the crowd didn\u2019t cheer.\nThe news wasn\u2019t that the Hixon had crashed into a meteor.\nThe news was something else.\nThe news, suddenly, was that the captain of the Hixon had been successfully resuscitated.\nThe cheering began again, ten times more frantic.\nFifty yards from the crowd, Roosh began to strangle himself with a rope he kept for the purpose. And then, when there was no breath left in him, he forced himself to be calm.\n\"God damn it, damn it, damn it,\" he said aloud.\nChapter 13\nThe $SURNAME0 mansion resembled a magnificent medieval hall in the most moneyed section of Greenwich, Connecticut. It was made to resemble the House. It was made to resemble nothing that had ever existed anywhere. It was usually used only once a year\u2014when $SURNAME0 was in town. $SURNAME0 spent a good deal of his time in the Real World, but his visits to the Solar System were few and far between.", "0_6_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span> when Remi Boncoeur came running down the dusty back street toward the tap-room of the Saint Bruno. He was a small, freckled Parisian street-tough who had come to Casbah Via several years before. Only a week ago he had discovered in his mail a letter from a girl in Marseilles. Until that night in the Saint Bruno, Remi Boncoeur had had no intention of ever leaving North Africa. Until that moment he had given no thought whatever to his future. He was not by nature a planner. Remi Boncoeur had loved many times and lost many times. He never looked ahead. He took each day as it came. This night in the Saint Bruno was so much like other night he had known in Via that it meant nothing at all. He had gone in and gotten drunk.\nHe never finished reading the letter from the girl in Marseilles.\nHe fell off his stool, crawled under it and colorfully died.\n\"We must bury be in a thirty-five-cent coffin and throw him in the bay!\" shrieked a friend.\n\"No!\" said another. \"No! People might think he was a native.\"\nSo Remi Boncoeur was buried in the first...", "2_6_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six eighteen in the morning</span>.\n\"Listen, you too, Simons,\" said one of the men in the basement to his colleague.\n\"Yeah?\" said Simons with a sigh. \"Listen to what, Big jew ?\"\n\"She\u2019s sendin\u2019 it through the patent office, that\u2019s what I heard,\" said Big Jew. \"Whoever finds the thing gets control of everything. We\u2019ll all make out like bandits. I\u2019m putting the crew on it right now.\u201d\nBig Jew looked at the faces of his men.\n\"Well, go on,\" he said. \"You heard me, ain\u2019t you?\" His men shrugged, nodded.\n\"We heerd you,\" one said.\n\"Well?\" said Big Jew. Then he was disgusted. \"Aw nuts,\" he said, and he slapped a clapboard, rattling his men.\n\"Go on upstairs then, you idiots,\" he said. \"We got to make this the best damn building in New York.\"\nHis men obediently trooped from the basement, their shoes echoing. In a moment, Big Jew and his latest hire, $FIRSTNAMEB0, were the only things moving in the basement.", "1_4_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-six in the morning</span>, and I was afraid of the doorman at the Drake, so I called room service instead, and told them to send up a pot of coffee, two rolls with grape jelly, a small meatloaf and a quart of milk. My money was running out. I'd spent almost everything since Chicago. Twenty-five dollars for the car alone. And still over five hundred miles to go.\nWhen this final waiter brought up the breakfast I tipped him an extra dollar and sent him away. Then I spent the next two hours packing the trunk, weighing down the things I wanted to keep with bricks and my manuscript roll so they wouldn\u2019t float away.\nAt six o'clock I hurried out to the car with my bags and the white boxes of unmailed mail. As I opened the back door of the Cadillac, a man fell out. It was my parking attendant\u2014snoring. He'd hidden under some blankets.\n\u201cHey!\u201d I yelled. \u201cWhat the hell is this?\u201d He jumped up with a look of extreme fear in his face. \u201cWhere do you think you're going?\u201d I yelled. \u201cI paid you to watch the car!\u201d He didn't say anything.", "1_10_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> when we got to the dock where we had tied the raft. The sun was shining and field-glasses were going all along the riverfront. The old Mate was pacing back and forth in the shade of an earthen wall, worried.\nBeyond the wall we could see a big crowd on the main street.\n\u201cIt\u2019s all right,\u201d I said in Spanish. \u201cThey didn\u2019t find us.\u201d\nRemi and the Mate looked at each other and smiled. The Mate said something.\n\u201cHe says you are strange boys.\u201d\n\u201cTell him we\u2019re strange men.\u201d\n\u201cTell him yourself.\u201d\n\u201cAsk him if he wants to make five pesetas,\u201d I said.\n\u201cWhat does he do with five pesetas?\u201d\n\u201cHe takes a drink and brings the bottle back.\u201d\n\u201cTell him we\u2019ll give him five pesetas for a bottle of wine.\u201d\n\u201cHe says you\u2019re a Son of a Bitch.\u201d\n\u201cBeg your pardon,\u201d I said. \u201cI believe the French...", "0_10_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> of a day in mid September in the year 1945. The place was Greenwich Village. The greatest generation ever to come out of America was dying, and nobody seemed to care. There were a few, at the frontier posts like New York and San Francisco and Chicago, who cared enough to scream from the rooftops and swashbuckle in the twilit pools of Skid Row and Times Square. Their voices were left unheard in Washington, drowned out by the endless clank of levers that move noiselessly the world these days. And so this story begins in an East Village saloon and will end in an East Village basement.\n\n\nThere were five distinct parts to the basement, and to the house above it, and to the lot on which the house and basement stood. The basement and house were built on a lot seventy by one hundred and forty-six feet, and the distinct parts were:\nThe Right Rear Bower, ten feet by fifteen, solidly built of masonry and distinguished from all other parts by being absolutely impregnable to assault from without. The architect was apparently a believer in all-around defense. There were no windows, not even a ventilation shaft, for all light and air came from the three windows of the adjoining cellar...", "0_21_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-four in the evening</span> on February twentieth, 1964, when a party traveling north on Route 55, near Des Moines, saw a short fat man in a tattered Army overcoat and riding boots, playing a guitar and singing Oh the drum major flashed his sword at the sky, and the trumpeters played, and the family hung over the balcony -- and the Indians made an alliance with the trappers who solved the puzzle and relaxed and luxuriated in the hot baths, and the drums grunted, and the Frenchmen hid, and the chief laughed, and the bugle sang balloo-oo-oo-oo-t as the short fat man stumbled, strumming crookedly along the side of the highway. What does it mean when a man dressed up in tatters, and he's plastered with mud, and he's traveling in the snow, and he only wants to play his guitar and sing ? Everything. Nothing. He's a shadow that\u2019s walking, in the winter, along the country roads of America. -- On Highway 55 not far from Des Moines, February 20, 1964. ~ Hunter Stockton Thompson ~\nWell, I suppose, while I'm here, I should tell something about myself. A thing I detest more than any other form of...", "2_3_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. In fifty-six hours and thirty-six minutes, twenty-two hours and ten minutes, eleven hours and forty-five minutes, four hours and thirty minutes, forty-three minutes, it would be sunrise\u2014on the planet Mercury.\n$SURNAME1 had a suitcase under his arm. He moved out from under the fountain and started shivering as the warm air touched him. He was not used to warm air\u2014not used to air that would not, in seconds, be irksomely hot or cuttingly chiller.\nA name tag on $SURNAME1\u2019s suitcase said:\nM. CONSTANT\nThe name tag did not list a home address. The purpose of the tag was to enable Tralfamadoreans to address the suitcase.\nThe odometer on the suitcase was set at 0.00. When the suitcase was opened, the figure would move to some higher number. The number would represent the kilometers that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had ridden as a Royal messenger.\n$SURNAME1 walked hunched over, for the most part. He fought a tendency to walk on all fours, which he had noticed in himself ever since his arrival on Mars.", "0_13_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. One minute past one o'clock in the afternoon on the 25th of December.\nSix minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon on the 25th of December, 300,000 Martians plunged out of space into the atmosphere of Earth.\nThere was no advance announcement of their coming. There was no gradual dwindle of the strident roar of their rockets.\nThe rockets dripped dry, and there they were\u2014the thing that had come to rest on Lake Winnesaw, Michigan, and surrounding it, the thing that had come to rest in the branches of a sturdy beech tree in St. James Park, London, and surrounding it\u2014the fighting-machines.\nNone of the three hundred million creatures astride Earth was able to see even one of the fighting-machines.\nTen minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon, white, blossomy flame flowered in a thousand places away from the two machines that had come to rest on Earth. Here and there, true flowers had opened in meadows and at the feet of trees. Here and there, true-hearted Roman Catholics had died of the smoke brought forth by their suicided churches.\nThere was no other response to the invasion. None...", "0_13_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span> when we passed through Churchill, Colorado, birthplace of Floyd Dell the writer, distributor of Bread Loaf seed money and finally head of the Pennsylvania Railroad, and at two-fifteen we were approaching Rawlins, on the rim of the Great American Desert. Stan was again so talkative he bothered me. He was making plans to shove off for Mexico in a week or so over Indian trails -- \u201cand if I have any dough left me after all the whiskey and women, which probably I can't, I'm gunna buy a guitar and hitch nex month on a flatcar to Tennessee. I ain't never been enough south to suit me.\u201d\nOn a shady side street of Rawlins I dropped Stan, who quick as anything tried to mooch a nickel off me for the town dump, and hiked the last six miles of my great road over the desert and up the canyon of the North Platte River to my hometown Farnum Street, and memory. And you know what? My brother $FIRSTNAMEB3 was al ready there. He was still traveling. He'd been hanging around New York, causing trouble, unshaven and dirty, sitting in Times Square, seeing all the women go by, listening to jazz. \u201cOh,...", "2_12_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-nine post meridiem</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was now a veteran of three air raids. He was feeling more secure buttoned up in the mill.\nHe sucked up another ladleful of shining blood. He got more than he could swallow. It tickled his throat, and he started to cough. He hacked until tears popped from his eyes and ran down onto his smoking sergeant\u2019s bars.\nThe smoke spiraled up from the burning bars like incense from a censer.\nWhen the coughing had stopped, $FIRSTNAMEB0 sucked down another spoonful of his iron meal.\n\"B\u2014b\u2014below zero\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1 suddenly.\nHis lips had been so badly chewed and slashed that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had to guess at the words.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s hand groped for $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hand, found it, gripped it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 gripped back as tightly as he could, given the fact that his arms hadn\u2019t yet recovered from having been bled.\n\"What?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, when $FIRSTNAMEB1 had let go of his hand. \"What, $FIRSTNAMEB1?\"", "2_12_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe antenna farm at Stony Crater went up in a ball of flame.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was weeping and waving goodbye to $FIRSTNAMEB2 in the vapor of Mars.\nThe antenna farm at Stony Crater had been the site of Camp Chin smear.\nThe street outside the barrack in which $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were confined was rocked by a tremendous explosion. The walls of the barrack shivered. The ceiling sagged and dripped.\nThe barrack itself was rocked by an explosion outside itself.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 looked out the barrack windows to see what the second explosion had been.\nThey saw a strange sight.\nIt was a wheeled platform, blazing under a shower of incendiaries. It blazed crazily. It flipped and pitched, flinging a body from its wheeled top with each pitch.\nExcept for the pitching, the wheeled platform was like the upside-down torture rack with which $FIRSTNAMEB1 had tortured $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nExcept for some minor details, the body that the wheeled platform flung from its wheeled top with each pitch was $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "1_8_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-three in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was suffering from a hangover. Like every other member of the platoon, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been a heavy drinker while on Earth. The platoon had landed on Mars with a supply of Earthling liquor that would have lasted a hundred men two years. In two weeks, the hundred men had drunk the lot.\nThe hangovers were bad, and getting worse. Thanks to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s defective short-term memory there were periods of up to twenty minutes during which he was unconscious. The patches of color in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s visual memory, representing sessions of jack-jumping, were getting big and numerous.\nThe patches would represent reverie, too. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was thinking dreamily and colorfully of the cable that connected him to the space ship now\u2014a cable a yard thick and guaranteed to hold ten thousand pounds. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had a vivid memory of the heavy clamps that bolted the cable to the stanchions on both ends. And he had memorized precisely the press of the hand clamps that would unscrew the clamps.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hands were now clamped around the hand clamps of the clamps that held his cable fast to the stanchions. Presently the stanchions would...", "0_6_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-six in the morning</span>. Suddenly I decided to wake up Tim Gray and Bill Grivas. I crept over to their couch and shook them by the shoulders. Gray opened his eyes, grabbed me by the wrist, and said, \u201cLee, if this is my wake-up call and none of my roommates are in your sight, I will personally strangle you.\u201d This was a stupid promise; Gray weighed a hundred and ten pounds. But that's the way he is; a big soft baby with a deadly gaze. I showed him the watch and he sighed with relief. \u201cDoc Lee in the morning,\u201d he said. \u201cI thought it was the morning. I thought I'd wasted another one.\u201d\nLee could never understand why Time meant so much to us. He always wanted to stop it, or speed it up; make it go or come the way he wanted it to go. He thought it was silly to make a fetish out of hours and minutes because he didn't believe in finalities or absolutes. I had something absurd and horrible in mind but I decided to keep it to myself. I went outside in the cold dawn to regroup my thoughts. The land was flat and grey: thousands of acres of half-grown...", "0_11_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-nine in the morning</span> when they pulled into San Antonio and stopped at a gas station. The owner had just come down to open the pumps. Stan and Duane put on their minds the full power of cosmic persuasion. And it worked.\nThe whole gang flew to New Orleans in a chartered plane. Everything had been arranged by mail. The gang in New Orleans would be waiting for them when they arrived. The old lady who owned the small plane was very understanding in spite of the fact that two members of the North Carolina gang were heavily armed. The small plane had to take off promptly at four-thirty since another plane was scheduled for landing on the small private field at that very minute. The two heavily armed men happened to be the two biggest men in the gang. This is not saying much, since two members of the North Carolina gang were women, and one of them was seventy-four years old. She carried a .32-caliber pistol strapped to her old-lady hip. Old Lady Bilby was also armed, but her weapon of persuasion was tucked away in her right stocking. She wore thick silk stockings. Only one member of the gang was unarmed. This was Professor Bomb, econ omist and ex-football guard.\nThe airport was curiously...", "2_4_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four seventeen in the morning</span>.\n\"I\u2019m ending our interview, Mr. $SURNAME2,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I\u2019ve said things to you that I should have said in private to my wife.\" He strode up to $SURNAME2, cupped his ear. \"Goodby, Skip,\" he said.\n\"Goodby, Mr. $SURNAME0,\" said $SURNAME2 shakily. He left hurriedly by the back door, which $SURNAME0 had left open.\n\"Goodby and good luck to you, Mr. $SURNAME2,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 from her wing chair in the parlor arch.\n\"Goodby, Mrs. $SURNAME0,\" said $SURNAME2.\nAt four nineteen in the morning, a visitor left the estate by the vandalized plank barrier at the head of the public road: a pedestrian who moved, as though each step were being taken involuntarily.\nThis blot on the fabric of space-time was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.", "0_1_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one seventeen in the morning</span> when we arrived in Hollywood. The big overhead sun that I'd been watching all across the continent was staring right in my eyes. I saw all the fool's gold colors winking on the hills, in the shape of cowries and magic $ symbols. I had come to the magic seat of the Golden Fang. I heard $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 yelling with sheer joy among the palm trees and took my first understanding shots of Southern California. I couldn't wait to hustle back to my aunt in Sacramento and tell her everything I knew about America. She wouldn't believe half of it. We lurched rattling and groaning across Beverly Hills to Broadvery, where everything was dark and the Beverly Hills cop of dubious lineage shuddered drunk in his stinking squad car. We wound round and round, lost among the alleys, inching across beyond the civic center and the central fire station, making funny noises of all kinds in every back street, while Stan banged on his horn persistently, because he didn't know where the fuck he was going. There came a time when I saw no more lights ahead, and we were stuck in total darkness. Stan finally got hold of a gas station, and after a great many wrong turnings, we went right off...", "1_5_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. Since then and up to situt ing no message had come. \"No,\" she repeated in a vague, dreamy voice, \"no message yet.\"\n\"Perhaps the night will be peaceful,\" said her husband with an odd distaste.\nChapter Ten\nShe stood before the tall looking-glass, tying the sash round her. She was very pale, but there was a sharpness in her features and a brilliance in her eyes which came from her being fully roused from sleep. There was something else as well. It flashed into her mind as she stood knotting her tie, I've had a bad dream, she said to herself. She remembered it for a moment only as one remembers a scene in a theatre from which one has been abruptly awakened. Very vivid for a moment; then the light withdrawn, the interest taken away, a sense almost of relief at finding one's self apparently in one's bedroom.\nYet it had been vivid. She could remember that she had been playing with her father and her mother. It was high spirits- mere high spirits. She had been behaving in some mildly absurd way which tickled her father, and he had laughed- irreverently, she felt after she was wide awake- so that she had...", "0_2_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-three in the morning</span>. I sat dazed with horror and excitement, unable to move Mark off my lap. Two lovely girls who said they had \u201cjust happened to be passing by\u201d on their way home from the Berkeley campus occurred at the chink of curtain and stared in, squinting in the harsh light at the scene in the room with the crazy lady on the floor with her son. They asked if we wanted them to call the manager. \u201cIs she crazy?\u201d Whereupon one of them added, \u201cI'm not touching her.\u201d\n\u201cIs that a rat?\u201d\n\u201cWhat's the matter?\u201d\n\u201cThat's her kid, they're good buddies.\u201d They thought I was either hit on rap or was really cracked. Had they not appeared, things might have gone much worse with me.\nI took them up to the roof and explained to them that I was a writer trying to get off on junk, that the kid thought it was a ride, and everything was fine. The policeman was gone.\nIn the car on the way down San Jose Boulevard, they tried to make chitchat. \u201cWhere are you from?\u201d\n\u201cNew York City.", "2_9_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-eight in the morning</span>.\nThere was a rustling like autumn leaves.\nThe rustling, as it turned out, was produced by a scrabbling, sliding sound as the four hundred and eighty-six Tralfamadorians climbed down from the branches of their metal trees.\nIn a twinkling, the four hundred and eighty-six Tralfamadorians popped into angelic figures of illumination at the openings of four hundred and eighty-six mustard tanks in the stone battlement.\nThe mustard tanks were chugging and grinding, getting ready to spew khaki-colored mist.\nThe straggling rabble that had been congregating outside the iron castle was in no shape to appreciate the beauty of the attack.\nOnly the dog $SURNAME1 could appreciate the beauty.\n$SURNAME1 skittered at the end of his slack chain, barked with every unbalanced rotation of his tail. He was so happy with the show that he barked and yipped and pranced.\nMinutes after the beautiful attack began, the rabble was in retreat\u2014was being rolled up and driven away by tear-gas canisters fired by loyal company troops.\nWithin an hour, the rabble was scattered and broken.", "0_5_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. God was in his heaven. All's well.\nThose seconds of peace constituted the sweetest, deepest, most luxurious and most narcotically dream-laden sleep of robot $SURNAME0\u2019s life.\nHe woke at once to full wakefulness, knowing exactly where he was, what he was.\nHe was in bed in a twelve-foot space ship hurtling through space on a course that would land the space ship on the far side of Mars in fourteen minutes. His one companion was a dog with good red Martian eyes, a band of gold around one ankle, and dolomite-bound olfactory sensors.\nThe dog\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"Hello, $FIRSTNAMEB4,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Grrr,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"No need to tell me we\u2019re going too fast,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I can feel it.\nHe flattened himself into the contour-chair that was built into the wall of his cabin. Pressing button 9 on the control panel before him gave a dual canopy of transparent acrylic to the cabin. The canopy sealed itself shut. An emergency water-and-air system came on automatically. The ship was as ready for space as...", "1_12_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-one p.m.</span>\nAt twelve forty-five, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was selling worthless positions in a million-dollar space voyage that did not exist. He was keeping half of the proceeds to help in the financing of the voyage.\nThe recruits kept coming in.\nIt made no difference at all if they were middle-aged Baptist deacons, spinsterish mothers from Boston, boisterous cowboys from Arizona, stylish homosexuals from New York\u2014or battered old mining tools from the caves of Mercury. And it made no difference at all if they put up only ten dollars, or five dollars, or a single dollar.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 divided it all into fifty equal piles.\nHe talked about beautiful women and fast horses and aromatic mint juleps on the powdered snow of Titan.\nIt was a deal that could not go wrong.\nHow did he know this?\nIt had been proved before.\nIn fact, it had been proved so often that it had taken on the aura of a law of Nature.\n\"If this glorious enterprise should fail,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, \"it will be the first time in the history of the Universe that a genuine money-making venture has collapsed as a result of unknown and unforeseen factors.\"\nIn any event, the profits...", "1_13_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I could hear the clock ticking. There was a big kerosene furnace in the kitchen, and it was going tick-tack-tick-tack. There was a big coal furnace in the basement, and it went sshh-sshh, sshh-sshh, sshh-sshh like that. There wasn't a breath of air outside. The September wind and the June wind, and all the stern old mother-season winds that wanted to whip the tears out of your painted eyes were sulking invisible in the big blue heat. There was just the tick-tack of the kerosene cook-stove and the sshh-sshh of the basement furnace and the hum of the electrical clock and that was all that broke the terrible silence. Don't talk about noises. The minute you talk about noises you're in trouble. Anyway, I could hear the ticking of the clock distinctly in the long first-floor living-room. I went in there and lay down on the davenport and furled the smoke-coloured silk window-curtain all around me. The silent street looked into the room. The heat made squares of the window-glass. I looked into one of the fused-together squares of glass...", "1_0_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-seven ante meridiem</span>.\nLarry Planck hung up the public phone. He looked at the gun in his hand. He put the gun in his pocket.\nHe took out his cigarettes, looked at them so reflectively that the girl behind the counter of the phone booth thought he was ignoring her plea not to smoke. But he had not heard her speak. His fingers were clamped around the crumpled pack of cigarettes, crushing them against an empty cigarette box as if he were testing the tensile strength of some material in tension.\n\"Excuse me,\" said the girl behind the counter.\nStill holding the cigarettes and the box, Planck looked at her quizzically. \"I beg your pardon?\"\n\"Please don\u2019t smoke in here, sir.\"\n\"Oh.\" Planck dropped the cigarettes and the box. That shattered his concentration, and scattered the pieces of a jig-saw puzzle he was trying to assemble together.\nSo it was at a leisurely stroll that Mr. Planck, following the sign that said \"Directions,\" came to the door marked STATION MASTER and walked in.\nThe rabbit-faced agent was drunk, as usual. He'd obeyed orders, though. His force of a thousand drunks was on...", "1_3_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty in the morning</span> when we arrived. My attorney had stumbled off the bus first, then opened the door for me. My hand was weak and our luggage was cumbersome\u2014especially the two bottles, about two quarts of tequila and a pint of ether...but somehow we managed to handle it. I was so pissed I didn\u2019t even notice that my attorney was covered with blood. \u201cJesus man! What\u2019s the matter with you?\u201d he kept asking, staring at his hands as we staggered into the terminal. But by then I\u2019d spotted a pay phone, and I wanted to put in a call to my attorney\u2019s wife.\nWe saw a police car parked beside the building and I began humping the luggage over to it. \u201cI think you better drive,\u201d I said. \u201cWe have to have this car.\"\n\u201cOkay,\u201d he said. \u201cWhere\u2019s the keys? I had them, but they must have fallen out.\u201d I dropped the bags, ran over and looked around, but we were already too loaded to walk to the nearest pay phone. \u201cShit,\u201d I said. \u201cWe\u2019ll drag the bags out to it...", "2_18_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-one in the evening</span>. The sun was down. Dark clouds to the west promised a spectacular storm.\n\"With all this,\" said $SURNAME0, \"the man in the story found himself quite literally a helpless victim of forces outside himself, larger than he. He thought that all he had to decide was the way he reacted to things. He thought that he could always make himself react with good humor and optimism.\"\nA window in the castle showed the man and his dog in silhouette. The dog sat up, sniffed the air, howled. The tone was not agitated. It was rather subsurface. It was a geological fault that disemboweled the Earth under the ocean.\n\"The man and the dog,\" said $SURNAME0, \"have just had a wonderful lunch. Now they\u2019re resting.\"\nIt was dark, now. The patterns on the dark window were kaleidoscopic.", "1_12_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. On the face of it, Tuesday, October 9, 2086, was going to be just another routine working day, like Monday, like everything before and everything after. Nothing in the news reports on TV indicated anything else.\nExcept, of course, $FIRSTNAMEB0, who lay on his barrack bed for one moment, and the next moment was out of the barrack and into the airlock at the end of the barrack. The sudden vacuum in the barrack sucked all of the barrack\u2019s filth through the fine pores of the fabric ceiling and walls\u2014sucked it out and sprayed it against the outer wall of the airlock. The clean-up squad of colored file clerks had to repaint the walls of the airlock, and they had to dismantle the barrack and clean it as well.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 knew nothing about all this.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wasn\u2019t even sure that he wanted to run away. He hadn\u2019t thought it through\u2014hadn\u2019t even thought it clear to the point where he was running away to stay out of some sick-head game or something he hadn\u2019t even dreamed up. All $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew was that the time was now, and he was going.", "1_13_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-eight in the afternoon</span> of Wednesday, October thirtieth, when I sat down at the bar of the Baddock Hotel with my newspaper friend. It was just two hours to the big fight. Already the nervous tension in the luxury hotel on 59th Street was beginning to mount. People were moving quickly through the lobby, many of them carrying something white\u2014invitations, perhaps to an afternoon tea, or maybe to the weigh-in. Or maybe something even more important, like tickets to the fight itself. I watched a man in a tight brown suit with a blond, well-tanned woman with very white teeth. She was his secretary or something like that, and from the tightness of that suit I had the feeling she was going to be his for the afternoon, with or without an invitation to the fight.\n\u201cYeah,\u201d said my California friend, \u201cthis is the day Bob Satterfield takes the Louisville Lip apart. You know who that big guy is over there in the dark glasses?\u201d I glanced over, and the big guy in the dark glasses had his back to me. The white suit was hanging on him. His hands were folded patiently and he seemed to be waiting for something, while his white friend and his secretary were going around like...", "1_14_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The Tristero hoax had been laid on the whole world. If a thing like that could really happen, anything could happen. All history, in an instant, became speckled with fissures of doubt.\nIt was in such a moment that the red-haired woman took over $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas\u2019 body by just how much nobody will ever know, but think of the Inverarity will. $FIRSTNAMEF2 could be given no warning; only, she came twice one night, while Miles snored away and couldn\u2019t hear, and the third time, three hours almost to the minute before the announcement, she made it once, with hardly a whisper, and after that did not come at all.\nShe now knew everything there was to know. She couldn\u2019t have sat there dumb and let her state of grace walk out the door without making some move to keep it, without asking him for some assurance, some hope. If a fraction of what she knew (what was stamped in at that instant like a Government safety test) could really be known, or communicated, then her judge, her defender, would have to be omniscient. God. Why should He have dealt her such a hand unless He intended...", "1_6_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nBeads of perspiration rolled down $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face, and his breath made whistling sounds through his pursed lips.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014being true to his nature\u2014blamed the unsteadiness of his hands on the heat, blamed the dryness and the whistling sounds on a bad performance of his respiration equipment.\nHe was feverishly trying to repair a puncture in the equipment hose connecting him to his breath-tank. He had found the puncture almost by accident\u2014had seen a wisp of deflated rubber protruding from the tank-to-suit coupling.\nRepairs were hard to make, because the equipment and the suit were rigidly coupled, and both had to be unbolted in order to free either of them from the other. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s fingers were squirming through the coupling\u2019s circular opening as fast as he could drag them, making the proper nut turns.\nThe puncture had been made by a shard of iron, which had migrated through the wall of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s space ship, had punctured his suit in space, and then, falling, had worked its way into the coupling at the bottom of the iron shaft that reached from the surface down...", "0_12_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-five post meridiem</span> by the radio clock when she found herself, without having consciously decided to start, heading out of Vegas toward the near horizon and Rabbit Burrows. She whipped past the few vehicles still on the road, past the dark skeletons of outlying ranch houses, and the cloned housing subdivisions partly eaten by the night, crossed old I-15 into the rural darkness and stopped in a free zone to check the map. She had been here once before, years ago, when she, idiotically, to see the atomic ruins at the south end of the Valley, and what she remembered best about it was the awful tediousness of the ride down those damn 99\u201444s into Yreka and the long slow hellish climb out again. Not to mention the fact that that huge square mile of government concrete, surrounded by high fences and looked-after by high-salaried officials for years and years, kept right on getting more and more run-down as you neared it, finally going all to seed, as if embarrassed by its wasteful joblessness. It scared her. The same with downtown Vegas, but this decay was disguised by phony Disney neon holograms of old gambling Row\u2019s. The main thing she remembered of Yreka was the name of this...", "1_1_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one eighteen in the morning</span> . . . one sixty-eight in the war in Vietnam . . . and we were looking straight up the tailpipe of the paranoid apocalypse. We could smell the streets of LA before we saw them. I rolled down the windows as far to the left as I could get them, but the smell was still terrible...especially when we got to the Flamingo and had to pass the DESERT HOTEL on the right: streets ahead of us still stank of old vomit, cheap booze, raw sewage and wild chicanos.\nWe turned left on the boulevard in front of the hotel and stopped at the light. A carload of Whipcord blondes screeched by on our right.\u201cWhat hotel is that over there?\u201d My attorney asked, pointing to the dark brick bulk across Flamingo Road.\n\u201cThat\u2019s where Sam Giancana hangs out,\u201d I said.\nThe light changed and we rolled across Flamingo.\n\u201cWhere are these people, your Sam Giancana friends?\u201d My attorney wanted to know.\n\u201cThey hide,\u201d I said. \u201cThere\u2019s a whole network of underground pipes and secret basements in that hotel...", "1_2_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-two in the morning</span> and I wished I were drunk.\nThe door opened and a Denver policeman came in and stood, looking me over. He was very big.\n\u201cWhat are you doing in here, buddy?\u201d\n\u201cSitting,\u201d I said. \u201cJust sitting.\u201d\n\u201cI can see that. You room here?\u201d\n\u201cYep.\u201d\n\u201cAny witnesses?\u201d\n\u201cHell, yes,\u201d said a fat guy in bed by the window. \u201cHe\u2019s got a dame in there and they want to get married tonight, so they came out here to talk it over.\u201d\n\u201cNot me,\u201d I said. \u201cI\u2019m just hanging around with this old guy.\"\nThe policeman went to the bathroom, opened the door gently and peeked in. Then he opened up the second door. There was a young girl in bed, looking small and white and cute.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s your name?\u201d the policeman asked.\n\u201cJake Rupert,\u201d I said. \u201cBut don\u2019t look for any identification; I probably forgot my travel papers in the other room.\"", "0_22_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> when a policeman walked up the steps and knocked on a door on the first-floor landing. The door was opened by somebody in the room. The policeman put his head in and said: \"Excuse me. Is there a Miss Braddocks here?\" Somebody said yes, there was a Miss Braddocks there, and the policeman went on up the stairs. He had come to take Marie Braddocks away to go and identify the body. He climbed the stairs up from the first-floor landing to the second, and knocked on the door of 228. A man's voice in the room said: \"Come in,\" and he came in. He asked for Miss Braddocks. A man said: \"What do you want her for?\"\n\"To come with me and identify a body.\"\n\"What do you mean?\"\n\"Are you Mr Farebrother?\"\n\"No. I'm not him.\" And then Mr Tate came forward, and the policeman told him why he had come.\nMr Tate buttoned his overcoat and went with the policeman and Miss Braddocks. He was barely steady enough on his feet to walk without help. He fumbled at the buttons of his coat with his old nervous hands. When he got to the...", "1_1_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-three in the morning</span>, Monday, November 16, 1972. But already a five-hour traffic jam stretched in both directions along the desert highway. The people in the jam were like nothing you could find in any ballot booth or at any customary political meeting in Nevada. Their status in Southern California life, as revealed by their cars, was as follows:\nI. THE LOW ECHELONS\u2014Friday-to-Monday cars, meaning people making one day trips for kicks, for squawking, for whatever else it is illegal aliens from Los Angeles do in places like Las Vegas every weekend. Most of these people were grouped in six-car clusters around the CircusCircus casino... . Alas, the low echelon had no money, except for passing bets among themselves as to how fast they could get to the Mint Motor Hotel in Vegas. Finding this an esthetic rather than an economic problem, they had as yet no solution. Still, they jammed the northbound lanes badly into the turnpike.....\nII. THE MEDIUM ECCHELONS\u2014The bulk of the people in the jam seemed somehow to fall into the middle class range: late \u201957 Chevys, \u201959 Fords, \u201960 Mercurys, big convertible \u201961 Cadill...", "0_7_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven o'clock in the morning</span>. It was raining outside. The yellow lights in the room blew on and off in the rain. $FIRSTNAMEB5 stood in the window with the rain blowing on her face. Her wet hair was heavy on her head.\n\u201cMy hair\u2019s getting heavy,\u201d she said. \u201cI must brrrooooath.\u201d\nShe took down her hair and shook it and the wind blew it. It looked as though it would blow away in the wind.\n\u201cI can\u2019t even make a toupee for you,\u201d I said. \u201cYour hair\u2019s so damn heavy. $FIRSTNAMEB5, you\u2019ve got too much damn hair.\u201d\n\u201cAnd your schnozzola\u2019s too long. Aren\u2019t you the silly one.\u201d\n\u201cI better go home to-day,\u201d I said. \u201cYou said you\u2019d give me breakfast, and my taxi will cost more than breakfast.\u201d\n\u201cDon\u2019t be an ass,\u201d she said. \u201cI didn\u2019t say I\u2019d give you any breakfast. You know I can\u2019t cook.\u201d\n\u201c", "1_16_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. \u201cThis place is already starting to get strange,\u201d I said. \u201cJust sitting around this room, soaking in all this mad sunshine, you can sense we\u2019re not in Kansas any more.\u201d She seemed to understand, but she didn't reply. She was staring, not at me but at something behind me. I turned around but I couldn't see anything. There was no door behind me except the sealed door of the bathroom & there was nobody moving in the room. So the movement had to be behind the mirror.\nBut all I saw, when I looked in the mirror, was a reflection of my own nervous face & some other shapes, just colors really, along the bottom. Perhaps she was looking at that secret movement along the bottom. I turned back to face the room, toward her.\n\u201cLet's try to get out of here,\u201d I said. \u201cWhy don't we go down to L.A. or maybe even all the way to Peru?\"\n\u201cPeru!\u201d she cried. Then: \u201cShhh!\u201d She looked around the room. \u201cRemember, they have listening posts everywhere. Especially in government hotels. Don't forget that.\"", "0_15_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-seven in the afternoon</span> of the thirty-second of December. At four o\u2019clock, a space ship, with red banners streaming, was to rise out of the Tralfamadorian clock on the town square, go sailing off into the solar system, approach the little planetoid known as Asteroid Fifty-nine Dumpling, put down a small crew of stainless steel spacemen, retrieve the mass of Old Salty, and return with it. There was to be a parade. And there were to be fireworks.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, the newest and most spectacular recruit in the religiously devout regiment known as the Tenney Black Guards, was the only man in the regiment who didn\u2019t know where he was, or who he was.\nThe regiment was drawn up in a waiting line outside the office of the regimental commander. The commander was in his office, pinning on his brown cloak and making sure that the black hood was in its proper place. The cloak would disguise the commander\u2019s face when the ship returned, bringing Old Salty.\nThe commander came out of his office. He had a poker face, against which the flushed, happy faces of his men were flapping mold. He went to the head of the waiting line. He put one foot...", "1_15_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span> and American Univer sity was busy like a commuter airport at rush hour. The weather was gray and humid and lukewarm as a reptile's egg. Rain was predicted for the morrow. In the lobby of the new Benderly Building, which connected with the Admissions Building by physical education tunnel number two, which ran underneath the street, was a big cluster of people, the majority of whom were Cubans, although some were Puerto Ricans and some were of neither category, but were American Indians, were a few buckskin they, most of them bearded, although not bearded all of them, and a few conruved, carrying beads and preferring even at this late an advanced academic institution in the things and dirty white robes, which they obviously America to maintain. They had guitars, flutes, and that lazy attitude that characterizes a know that they were right. At the center of them all was a barker, a tall, darkly tan individual in a bright white trench coat, loud-red track shoes, a white Harvard cap, and sunglasses, and in one hand he had a megaphone, and his lips were moving, and the static from the megaphone was causing his trench coat to twinkle and glow with static electricity.", "2_9_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt ten o'clock $FIRSTNAMEB0 would die. To him\u2014to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014it would seem that he died in the instant the hands of his army-issue wristwatch passed the twelve.\nThe instant in which $FIRSTNAMEB0 died was not an instant at all.\nIt was instead a process more involved, lasting well over one Earthling second. The process began when the first of a three-part number was introduced into $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s body by a needle slipped between two vertebrae at the base of his skull.\nThe number was three, two, one\u2014or, in round numbers, thirty-two.\nA mechanical pump, fastened to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s back just under the right shoulder blade, delivered a sonorous, resonant series of squirts.\nThe substance squirted, under pressure, into $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bloodstream, was a greenish liquid with the consistency of honey. The liquid was, in layman\u2019s terms, a hypnotic. It took a full minute to deliver fully one hypnum number.\nThe minute passed.\nTwenty-eight was next.\nAnd then twelve.\nAnd then sixteen.", "1_14_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nCris Campbell was sitting in the middle of a sepia-toned photograph. He was sitting in a soiled and tattered uniform of an Earthling soldier. The uniform was laden with countless ribbons, citations, and medals. It was quite clear from the photograph that Cris was a very old man.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 marched straight up to the photograph, confronting a man he thought very dead. $FIRSTNAMEB0 stood at parade rest, came to attention, made a perfect salute, held it, came back to parade rest, came to attention, marched straight up to the photo, stood at attention, flipped the photo over, came to attention, marched back to his post.\nThere was another man in the room.\nHe was plain, civilian, and behind a desk. There was a telephone on his desk.\nThe man behind the desk had a stiff, hoary, bluish-white beard. Stony gaps appeared at intervals in the beard. The hair on his head was long and white and undisturbed by any scissor or comb. His long, white hair matched the long, white hair on his arms and hands and legs.\nThe man had a radio receiver behind his ear, and a radio-transmitter behind his...", "1_6_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-nine in the morning</span>, according to the car clock. There was nothing to do but get right off the freeway, find a place to lay low and have a leisurely breakfast while trying to figure the next move. I needed time and distance to clear my head. I had this half -crazy notion that if I could just get to LA and confront Metzger in person all the madness would be clarified. Then I\u2019d make at least two urgent phone calls: one to involve the police, and the other to get my attorney\u2014while trying to reach Ricca in Vegas and apprise her of the situation. Shit, what a fine kettle of fish! I was sure the aftertaste would be rich . . . but it was best to reach Pat at his home and get him off the telephone before he reached for his razor blades again. There\u2019s nothing more futile than a lawyer on the run.\nMy mind went back to the Post Toasties gang. If I\u2019d believed everything they\u2019d told me, it would have made sense to conclude that Metzger and company were jailed somewhere up around Carson City, while the kid and the key man ransacked my apartment. I could see the scene: After locating the telegram and concluding I...", "1_5_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past five o'clock in the morning</span>. The sun was just coming over the vineyards and hills. Cohn was still talking. He was very drunk by now. He was swaying and laughing. We could not hear what he said.\n\"I'll ask Mike to tell you about it on the way home. It's too complicated to tell now.\"\n\"I'd like to know now.\"\n\"I don't remember Mike ever told me about it,\" Bill said.\n\"I'll tell you about it,\" Mike said. \"I don't remember it myself.\"\n\"You know you told me,\" Bill said. \"Anyway, I want to know now.\"\n\"It was that day $FIRSTNAMEB5 and Cohn and Bill were going duck-shooting,\" I said.\n\"Let Cohn tell it,\" Bill said. \"Osborne always says the wrong thing.\"\nCohn laughed. You could see he would be tight all day. He shook his head.\n\"It was that day Gonzales was trying to marry $FIRSTNAMEB5. He was going around and asking her in front of everybody. $FIRSTNAMEB5 made me count the candles on the cake. There were thirty-three candles. We had thirty-three candles on the cake.\"\n\"What about that?\"\n\"That's it.\"\n\"", "0_23_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to midnight</span> when the Rose of Tralee dance was cancelled.\nIn under four minutes, all the men of Mars would know their desperate situation.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, standing in the middle of $SURNAME0\u2019s Taj Mahal, was ashen.\nHe was unable to weep or vomit.\nAll the orgones that had been so miraculously foisted upon him were concentrated in the forefront of his being, where they throbbed, threatened to detonate like low-grade explosives.\nThose things he had been made to remember!\nThat contretemps with the Space Wanderer!\nThat contretemps with old Salo, the paratrooper!\nThe way the Army had used him, had misled him\u2014used him and misled him with punctilious fairness!\nHe had been used and misled by a society that believed in adhering faithfully to the cruelest lies.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had seen himself at last for what he was\u2014a witless wretch who had been used and misled as cruelly as anyone had ever been used or misled.\nAll he had ever been to anyone was an object that could be used and misled.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 hated himself as he had never hated himself before. Hating himself made...", "0_8_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span> on October 6, 1962. The place was $SURNAME0\u2019s pod in the telephone booth next to the Time and Space Museum The show, temporarily closed, was a mural depicting the Phoebus Catastrophe in all its hellish glory. The mural was as realistic as a nightmare. One might easily have mistaken it for the bedroom window of a house on the steppes of Mars.\nThe sky was the dead blue of a television tube on which no image has been received for a long time.\nThe sun was a dull, featureless ball of white neon.\nThe only light came from the stars and the red disc of Mars.\nThe rusty, treeless plain was so parched as to be without features, except for a trail of wheel marks that wandered across it with the gratuitous meandering of a mountain brook.\nThe wheel marks ended at the foot of a gilded ladder that was welded to an iron post by the door of the telephone booth. The wheel marks belonged to $SURNAME0\u2019s sport model Mercury, the descendant in stately unbroken line of the Mercury Comets that zoomed Earthlings to the Moon in 3-2-1 missions in the year 2000.\nA windmill, the...", "0_8_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-two in the morning</span>. We had just gone through the foolishness of examining our baggage with the x-ray machines, and paying our duty on an inexpensive little bottle of Jack Daniel\u2019s. The time was now eight thirty-two in the morning, and we had just figured out where we should go when suddenly something was happening to the Woman. Her face was contorting, she was clutching one arm, a vein was twitching horribly in her forehead. The Doctor was white, clutching himself; the Tourist was smiling nervously; somebody was shouting something about \u201cFumes!\u201d\nAll of a sudden\u2014wham!\u2014the place was full of security guards. For a minute it seemed like an ugly situation. We were surrounded. The agent who\u2019d refused us entry was shouting into a walkie\u2014talkie, calling for backup. All of a sudden it seemed very serious\u2014\u201csome kind of fit,\u201d they were saying. The Doctor was making horrible faces, clutching himself. Finally the Doctor managed to say, in a strangled voice, that he\u2019d \u201chad a convulsion.\u201d But his face was still twitching horribly. A huge fat guard with a sinister goatee kept shouting, \u201cWhat...", "1_14_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. This was Monday, October 22, 2005.\n\"Thank you very much, ladies and gentlemen,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. She raised her flower-littered blower again. \"And now, as a climax and sort of a epilogue, we are proud to present one of the most interesting people you will ever see in your lives. Everybody give a real big hand to D. D. T. Van Vliet.\"\nDozens of big hands slapped dozens of sunburned faces.\n\"Well, here we go, folks and friends\u2014and I mean friends,\" shouted $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"and by the way, you like the flowers, Professor? Don\u2019t they look pretty in all the blue cans? Don\u2019t you think they say something kind of nice?\"\nDown came the gavel once more. And, as it bounced and rebounded, the roar of the helicopter became suddenly very loud and then ceased altogether. There was now no sound at all except the oohs and ahs of the crowd, as they considered what Do-Re-Mi Productions had done with the wonderful old Rotary stage.\nDo-Re-Mi had, in fact, done nothing. Except for the sun-flowers, the...", "2_14_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"Might as well go ahead, Skip,\" said Hugh.\nHugh\u2019s hand was on the switch that would send toots of signal sauce to the Martian war fleet in space.\nTo the core of his being, down to the marrow of his bones, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was intensely sorry that he had used the beam to suggest to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 that they make love.\nIt had been an arrogant and presumptuous thing to do. He had made the most enormous mistake that a person possibly can make. A thousand eternities would not be enough time in which to regret the deception and disparagement he had put upon love. He was not sorry that he had used the machinery of intimidation to force people to love. What he most vividly and bitterly regretted was the lie he had thus been forced to tell about that machinery\u2019s very nature and concept. For love is not a mechanical thing. It cannot be bought or sold. It is free, valuable simply because it is.", "1_13_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nBefore tumbling into the bottom of the closet, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been standing in formation with the rest of Company B on the barrack floor. Standing stiff and proud, rifle held at present-arms, they had been watching their commanding officer, Colonel $SURNAME2, deflate before their eyes.\nColonel $SURNAME2 had a new color-beige\u2014and an interesting shape.\nColonel $SURNAME2 was absolutely limp. His lips were purple, his eyes were yellow and quivering\u2014the whites showing all around. His tongue, as it darted in and out among the purple lips, was blue. His head kept drooping lower and lower, until his chin came to rest on his chest\u2014and it still kept on drooping. Down, down, down\u2014and then a bounce, and the head would lift, and the eyes in the head would roll up and show only the ghastly yellow\u2014a bounce, and the drooping would begin all over again.\nWhile Corporalconsumer-Cooper, back at the barracks, slept through one of his cold, self-pitying nights, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had, with a variety of instruments, explored all the rooms of the office in the basement, as...", "1_7_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-five in the morning</span> and I was in Las Vegas eleven hours ahead of my brother and my attorney. I was also desperately in need of sleep, but I made a quick run to the men\u2019s room, then mounted the escalator to the coffee shop on the second floor...playing it cool, with a huge Kissingerian smile pasted across my brainless face, but moving with the natural grace of a drunken bear.\nThat first meeting in the Royal Lounge had run almost two hours. I sat numbly in a red plush chair for about twenty minutes after my attorney left, unable to summon the energy to move...a condition that became increasingly pleasant with each passing moment, until a tall prime example of the American bimbo came over and asked me to move my bloated carcass off the chair she wanted.\n\u201cWhy don\u2019t you go sit on the floor, honey?\u201d I murmured. \u201cRight here by my big toe...\" I closed my eyes and tried to imagine myself drifting down over the gardens and high -rise hotels of Las Vegas, wafting slowly down through a cool predawn Las Vegas as the maddening voice of Wayne Newton crooned an old standard from the Royal Lounge: \u201cDanke Schoen.\u201d", "0_19_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven eighteen in the evening</span>. The orchestra was playing very loudly. Men and women sitting at the tables in the bar were talking very loudly too.\nAt one of the tables four men and three women were sitting.\nThe men at the table were Americans. They were eating trout and drinking dry Martini. The men sitting at the table were eating the trout, not drinking the Martini.\nThe three women were not Americans but Norwegian. They were drinking the Martini and smoking cigarettes, the cigarettes were long and had something on them that glowed, so did the women\u2019s legs. The four men were trying to find out about the three women. Because the women could talk English quite well, and because they were good-looking, and because they were so happy, the four men at the table were trying to find out about them. The three Norwegian women were having a good time. The three Norwegian women were having a better time than the four men, anyway. They were all pretty good-looking.\nOne of the Norwegian women was a brunette. Neither she nor any of the other women smoked cigars. One of the Norwegian women was blond. Neither she nor any of the other women smoked cigars. One of the Norwegian women was a redhead. The four men kept...", "0_1_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1\u2019s furniture was being moved into the palace. Ten thousand troops surrounded the palace. $SURNAME1 was establishing a record for the swiftness with which a palace was sacked and stripped.\nThe palace would later be given a much more thorough going over.\nThere was no military reason whatever for the speed at which $SURNAME1 was moving. Speed was dictated entirely by the spitefulness with which he had decided, an hour before, to dismiss $FIRSTNAMEF0 from his bed.\n\"I am about to make a great discovery for you and for me and for all of us, \" said Salo. \"A discovery so wonderful that you\u2019ll have to forgive me for being so unkind to you in the past. You remember how unkind I\u2019ve been. I\u2019m not good at being a friend or a loved one, and you\u2019ve had the worst possible luck in having me either label attached to you. But I\u2019m going to make up for all that right now. \" He had heard the rapid drumming of feet outside, and he knew what it meant. $SURNAME1 was running like a frightened rabbit. That would be his idea of how to escape his agony. Salo let him run.\nWhen the sound of the...", "1_7_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\n\"Where do you want to go?\" Monroe Firestein said in his sleep, when Lenore kissed him on the eyelid to wake him up.\n\"Home, I guess. I need to change, and do my hair, and all that.\u201d\n\u201cHome.\u201d\n\u201cI want to go home.\u201d\n\u201cWhat\u2019s happened? Do you want to tell me, finally?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll tell you when I get out of here. Don\u2019t worry. I\u2019ll tell you.\u201d\n\u201cHow can I not worry, how can I not care? How can I watch you be slowly killed in front of me, day after day, and not care? What can I do?\u201d\n\u201cLet\u2019s leave here.\u201d\n\u201cMy experiment is still in progress, at least potentially, as of this moment, at least.\u201d\n\u201cYour experiment was stupid, I hate it.\u201d\n\u201cLenore.\u201d\n\u201cIt sucked. It wasn\u2019t fun. It sucked.\u201d\nFirestein turned away from her a bit so that only the...", "1_12_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve nineteen post meridiem</span> on our third Monday in October, the temperature sixty-one, humidity eighty-two, barometer thirty-nine, elevation approximately seventy feet above sea level, winds east at eight miles per hour, season...when it comes to seasons, we can always be counted on for a blizzard. Peaceable, that was, at the wheel of the Chevrolet, looking all around for cops even though we\u2019d technically parked and the engine was off. We\u2019d picked the car up less than a block from the Red Shark, but by now its location was more random than mine. I\u2019d seen it all around me and passing through me, each of its particles, as we cut across the Slot, and then I\u2019d heard it howling around the building and we\u2019d come running and gotten in and Peaceable had taken off, not even bothering to check the rearview mirrors or put on his glasses, trusting to the Lord and a direct line of sight. So now we were on Highway 5, headed north, on the California side of the Yuba River, thirty miles above the first stoplight at Nevada City...and it was only noon, the morning was young. Not only that; the possibilities were young. We could still...", "2_23_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-seven in the evening</span> put.\nThe time and place were Phoebeville, Tennessee, U.S.A.\nThe sky was painted a hellish red.\nThe atmosphere was hideous and thick with smoke.\nHitler\u2019s announcement of the beginning of the attack had come at eleven twenty-eight.\nAt eleven thirty-six, all hell broke loose.\nA mob of volunteers thirty deep on the metal platforms of the $FIRSTNAMEB0 County, Tennessee Land Limpet advanced through the painted town of Phoebeville toward the Suburban Limb Pene-tration Complex in the distance. Limpet soldiers wept, laughed, sang, talked insanely. They had to shout to make themselves heard by their comrades, because the Limpet Command had turned off the volume on their receivers. The Limpet Command said that shouting helped conserve the air supply.\nThe Limpet Commander was Col. CHMU/RH Grupo Gonzalez. He was a grandmother, aged seventy-eight.\nShe had been told to take her Limpet on a walk, to see whether it would waddle, sink in mud, get stuck, etc.\nThe Limpet was having no trouble at all.\nColonel Gonzalez was content.", "2_19_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-four in the evening</span>. The place was Newton, Massachusetts. The place was 4743 Elm Street.\nThe house shivered slightly.\nNothing happened.\nNorman Mailer kicked at the door. The door would not come down because it was on stilts.\nNorman Mailer kicked his twenty-two-year-old son, Chip, awake. Chip was asleep on the grass in back of the house. Chip had been dreaming about trouble in an advanced calculus course at Harvard.\nChip looked at his father.\nThe only time Chip could remember being happier than he was, now, was when he was three and his mother brought him a Harold and the Purple Crayon from Boston.\nThe only time Chip could remember being happier than he was, now, was at a rock concert when he was sixteen, when he had screwed twenty-four times in the same seat, between sets.\nNorman Mailer excitedly told his son about being to the opening of the Communication Center at Newton Memorial Hospital, telling Chip of the conference his mother had organized, telling Chip that a colleague of his at the Writers\u2019 Conference had just invited him over to Harvard to teach. \"Just like that, after a few hours at the Conference! Incredible!\"", "2_10_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 were at one end of the golden truck, and Gale and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were at the other end. They were looking neither at $SURNAME0 nor at $SURNAME1. They had not spoken a word. They hadn\u2019t looked at a word, either. And the reason they hadn\u2019t looked was that they had to keep their eyelids clamped tight against something terrifically awful and terribly interesting on the outside.\nThe time was four minutes to eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe awful and terribly interesting thing on the outside was nothing but space.\nThere was so little of it that either one of the four could have reached out and put his hand on it.\nThe truck shuddered, then darted away.\nThe Space Wanderer, sitting in the seat of Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 Mumm\u2019s gold-wheeled truck, was two miles out in space before he realized that he didn\u2019t have the vaguest idea where he was going.\n\"Hello, $FIRSTNAMEM2,\" a rich, carrying voice said fondly.\n\"Hello\u2014Marsh,\" said $SURNAME1 irritably.", "0_12_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past noon</span>. The saloon was empty except for us and the bartender. It was hot and the bartender had opened the doors to the street and brought back a wet towel to wipe the tables with.\n\u201cHave a drink, Mike,\u201d Bill said. \u201cYou earned it.\u201d\n\u201cHell, I had a drink,\u201d I said. \u201cI had two. I was drunk.\u201d\n\u201cNever get drunk on two glasses,\u201d Bill said.\n\u201cI know,\u201d I said. \u201cI must have been drunk before I started.\u201d\n\u201cDrink your beer?\u201d\n\u201cSure.\u201d\n\u201cHave another?\u201d\n\u201cDon\u2019t mind if I do.\u201d\n\u201cGood stuff. Never get drunk on beer.\u201d\n\u201cYou going to have one, Mike?\u201d Bill asked Mike.\nMike shook his head. \u201cBetter not. Finish our lunch.\u201d\n\u201cYou know, Mike,\u201d I said, \u201cthis is the damnedest town in the world.\u201d\n\u201cHow is that?\u201d\n\u201cWell, I was...", "0_10_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of Earth's September 3, 1998\u20141,474 days in all from the announcement of Salo's search for an intelligent being willing to take a message to another world.\nFrom Titan came news of the search. The message that Salo picked up on Earth, telling where the search had led, arrived on Titan 1,479 days later.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been searching for more caves and\u2014by blasting open rocks\u2014had found five more. He had explored two. They were dead caves. The dead man in the first cave, decorated with tattoos of Earth spacecraft, must have been a native of Mercury. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had no theory about the other corpse.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had found a round tunnel, smooth-walled, in the floor of a third cave. The tunnel had cast-iron door sets at both ends. By pressing his ear to the door in the farthest cave, $FIRSTNAMEB0 thought he could hear sounds from the other side. He hadn\u2019t opened the door. He had come straight to the caves to report the tunnel.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had made no attempt at palm-print identification of the caves. The first one had scared him badly. He had found, in a rock crevice near the entrance to the corpse\u2019s...", "1_18_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-three in the evening</span>. The place was Cleveland, Ohio.\nAt that moment, an old, shriveled man of sixty-two was sitting in an easy chair in a sunny living room on a pleasant street in Pittsfield, Mass.\nThe room was furnished with old, comfortable furniture. The carpet was thin, and one could feel the floorboards vibrate from passing cars on the distant highway.\nThe sixty-two-year-old man looked like a retired wizened sea captain who had come in for a great deal of about ships, adventures, and the good old days. Except for his eyes\u2014which weren\u2019t bloodshot, but bright, and which didn\u2019t stare, but twinkled\u2014he looked precisely like what he was, which was a retired company president who had read an incredible number of books in his lifetime.\nThe man was reading a volume of Thoreau\u2019s collected essays. He was by no means a perfect reader. From time to time he stopped to scratch his head, ponder and muse.\nHe lived alone with his books. He had a dog and a cat. Their names, respectively, were Lucy and Turk. Lucy was an Irish setter. Turk was an alley cat the man had found...", "1_13_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe chronological scheme by which $SURNAME0 had selected the incidents of the day had called for a grand fire in the fireplace at that time, and a grand fire there was\u2014a whooping, rolling, rafter-shaking blaze. In fact, the blaze was so whooping and rolling and loud that it blanketed out every other bit of sound.\nThe fire so consumed all the wood, so sucked all the air from the chimney, that the smoke, instead of rising and dissipating through the damper holes, billowed out against the walls of the house, blued the windowpanes.\nPanels, shutters, and flues were afire.\nHeat gushed from the fireplaces high and low without benefit of fires.\nThe silver foxes in their pens howled till they were smoke, and smoke they became.\nThe red puffs of their breath were as smoke in the fire light, and the smoke vanished in the general smoke through which they vanished.\nThe wild ducks in the watch-mans became smoldering balls of down and feathers. The stew bubbled and burned.\nSo hot and wild was the fire that the butter in the crock turned brown and burned, too.\nThe clothes...", "1_19_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-three in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had a watch, but he didn\u2019t know what time it was, or whether the watch was correct. The correctors had not troubled themselves to teach $FIRSTNAMEB0 to tell time. They had not troubled themselves to teach $FIRSTNAMEB0 very much of anything.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s perceptions were restricted to the scope of things that an Earthling might perceive. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was puzzled by loudspeakers in the ceiling, was frightened by the flappings of a red baton in the hands of a man with a red, flat-buttoned face up on the wall. The loudspeakers droned, the baton flapped, the flat-buttoned man grinned, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 got angry, felt his anger grow\u2014as he had been told to feel it when flat-buttoned man grinned.\nNow $FIRSTNAMEB0 rolled up his magazine, got to his feet and approached the water fountain in the corner. Life was difficult for $FIRSTNAMEB0. He was existing in a nightmare, and he was going to drink from the water fountain in order to make the nightmare go away.\nThe water would taste very good. It would make a cheerful play of silvery music. And when the music stopped, $FIRSTNAMEB0 would put his lips to the spout...", "1_3_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after three o'clock in the morning</span>. We stood on a deserted street in an old town. We were on the edge of Spain, a few miles from the border, and in an hour we would meet the smuggler who would take us into Spain. He was a local man who made a business out of taking a few people at a time across the porous line.\n\u201cThe one corking sight I ever saw in my life happened right near here,\u201d said Stan. \u201cThat was Timmy Sheehan. They ran horse races through the streets every Sunday night, and one Sunday night Timmy decided to go down and play. Everybody in the taverns got up and followed. There must have been five hundred of us running down the street with Timmy after the horse. We finally rounded him up in a corner. You know Timmy, he just sat on his ass and laughed like hell. And everybody else was laughing too. Oh, boy, what a night!\"\n\u201cNow this is the set-up. We meet this guy at the cafe on the corner at four. We travel up to the frontier with him, he jumps us across the line, we get out, wave to him, 'Va, va!'\"\n\u201cYou wave?", "1_20_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-six in the evening</span> of the twenty-third of December.\n$SURNAME1 found the rooms empty. His blind mother and his elderly maiden aunt had gone to a meeting of pacifist people at the Unitarian Church.\n$SURNAME1 illuminated the rooms with a touch of his finger. The rooms were much as any son would leave them, before going off to war. A piece of pumpkin pie with a small scoop of vanilla ice cream sat on a small table by the arm of the easy chair in the living room. $SURNAME1 ate the pie and ice cream.\nHe left money for the telephone call he had just made\u2014and for all other telephone calls that might be made from his residence during his absence.\nHe took with him, not the scriptures, not the collected works of Shakespeare, not any good books, but two very stout valises.\nHis mother and his aunt, returning from the meeting at the Unitarian Church, were surprised to find lights on in the rooms, and a man\u2019s footprints in the newly fallen snow of the lawn, around the fountain. The sweet fountain had never run so low before, even in summer.\n$SURNAME1 had left no note. His valises contained all that he had to say.\nIn the notebooks he carried with him, he...", "2_2_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past two o'clock in the morning</span>, Moderation and temperance were ten thousand miles away, deep in the Iron Caves of Honolulu, just about where the equator is.\n\"No\u2014no, I\u2019m sorry,\" said young Vlad DBase, Jr., over the telephone, \"but at this time of night, Mrs. Bombardini, I just can\u2019t rent you a jackal.\"\n\"That\u2019s perfectly ridiculous!\" said Inman\u2019s stepmother. She was calling from Cleveland. \"There\u2019s not one thing whatever the matter with old Sawney. Why you sent him to us half dead. If you don\u2019t take him back this minute this minute this minute I just simply will not\u2014\"\n\"Of course our record of prompt service and courtesy is without parallel,\" said the tweed-coated Bex Shooter in the receiver. \"If you\u2019ll be good enough to bear with us for just another\u2014\"\n\"This minute this minute this minute this minute this minute this\u2014\"", "2_4_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-seven in the morning</span> now, and as I saw it I had accomplished all I had to in New York and it was time to hurry west and go to important guru business. So I scorched downtown through dark cross streets, swung around Wall Street and over toward the Stock Exchange, and then I decided to give $FIRSTNAMEB3 a last look before I left him and our love for ever, and I swung by the Washington Hotel. There was the old International, where we had spent a romantic night years back. But he wasn't there; I had to go rumbling on.", "0_13_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-three in the afternoon</span>. At one forty-four, the boy suddenly shivered. He was alive with the feeling that something ominous was about to happen. He still didn't uncover. He played the violin.\nThe chimes died away.\nThere was a minute of silence.\nThe tolling of a slow bell began.\nThe boy sudden-ly stopped playing.\nHe looked dreamily at the telephone. The chimes had reminded him of something. The tolling of the bell had reminded him of something. It seemed to him that something in his head was broken, and that events were spilling into his mind like water through a crack.\nHe went to the big window and looked out.\nA bell tower on a factory across the street was sending a cracked bell clanking and swaying and hammering out its message in the afternoon. It was a factory that made artificial legs and arms. The bell was swinging in front of an iron door.\nThe door had a grated window for the people who came to examine artificial limbs. At the sight of the grated window, a whole barrage of images exploded behind the boy\u2019s eyes, and he remembered everything.\nHis father had lost both legs at the war. His mother had one arm.\nThey...", "1_6_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 went barefoot at the head of his squadmates down the fifty-yard length of top-botton hall to Stony\u2019s room. He carried the M1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s squadmates carried the M1 clips that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had put in a row on Stony\u2019s table. The eight clips contained a total of one hundred and ninety cartridges.\nThe echoing stillness of the barrack was broken, now and again, by a nerve-scraping click or scrape, as the foot of an amateur sentry scuffed, as the amateur sentry squinted through the peephole of a barracks.\nIn the twelve minutes that $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s platoon was parading at the head of the main drag in town, the column did not come upon a single Martian that was awake. The rosy color that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had seen in the sky outside the barrack was being replaced out there with a blue that was darker and darker. The profound silence of the barrack, during the march, suggested that a goodly portion of the population was tucked away in underground bunkers. It was good to know that their accommodations must be cramped and airless.\nAccommodations in a coffin would...", "2_15_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earthling time.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s golden wristwatch rang like a great bell.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s feet were on a hot plate. The pedal in the middle of the footbath was depressed. In a little more than one minute, the new family would be launched.\nA microphone was fastened to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s lapel, to permit his conversations with his mate on the Mars-Earth shuttle to be overheard on Earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s microphone was connected to speakers in the underground control room of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 estate.\nThe speakers were controlled by a switch marked talk\u2014 listen. A switch man in the control room governed the speakers. He was a superbly trained dog named $SURNAME1. He was a regular Solar System thoroughbred, handsome enough to be immortal, brown and white, with a natural curl to his tail and a natural lilt to his walk.", "2_22_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog, Salo, waited for the manifestation of the image again.\n\"Where were you when the image appeared before?\" said $SURNAME0. \"No\u2014don\u2019t tell me. I see a picture of you, marching your regiment through France, writing your silly messages on the troops in lime-green shells.\"\n$SURNAME0 smiled. \"I\u2019m interested in that message you were writing to your troops,\" he said casually. \"I wish you\u2019d write it again for me. Would you mind?\"\n\"Another time,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"It isn\u2019t a joke, then?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Then please write it for me again, as soon as you can,\" said $SURNAME0. There was a notepad by his day bed. He tore off a sheet and wrote $FIRSTNAMEF0 a little introduction to his stationery. \"I shall treasure what you write very much indeed,\" said $SURNAME0.", "1_5_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. A pink radiance was beginning to rise in the east and breathe over the sadder and wiser shore of the lake. It was all so quiet that Minturn could hear the sound of the waves now washing on the beach and rocks below. He heard a clang of steel banging against wood. He saw, far out on the lake, Boatswain emerging in silhouette, black against the pink sky. He saw the little tug come out of the fog and halt stubbornly in the middle of the lake, ready to nose at any minute toward shore.\nThen Minturn heard the lapping of little waves near the dock; heard someone breathing; heard bare feet moving quietly, stealthily; heard a figure tumble at last against the wooden steps of the dock.\nHe waited to see if the trespasser would -make any attempt to call out. He waited for the squeal that would mean that the trespasser had seen him.\nThe trespasser seemed to have no intention of making any kind of noise. The figure stumbled up the steps.\nIt was good to see Boatswain again. The old man was breathing with the steady rhythm of a steam engine. He wheezed, and blew, and called on the name of God. He...", "0_1_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty in the morning</span>. The place was post-World War II Las Vegas, a city in the American Southwest. This was two years before they built the dam that prodticed the fabulous Rio and property values went through the roof. Nothing had happened in Las Vegas yet, except two terrible things: The hangover from a decade-long nationwide drunk, and the loneliness of a lost little peoplesomewhat akin to the lonesomeness of exiled Irishmen, somewhat akin to the lonesomeness of exiled Jews, somewhat akin to the lonesomeness of Yankees who fled to California for a few years in the fifties and ended up staying for good.\nThe Colorado River was to the West of us. The coyote is the most skittish of all American animals, but in those days the coyote could thrive only in Vegas, sleeping nightly in its ambulance-chasing hotels, scavenging for unspent sawbucks among the cigarettes mashed in the cuspidor as the house bleeds the player white. Acrid spirals of sawdust from cheroots, wasted by the thousands, spiraled along the ceilingbeams of our barracks at night: the abandoned sawbucks were in the air. Sordid dens of the lonely and of the genuinely dangerous...", "2_18_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe place was the drawing room of the palace on Titan. Mrs. $SURNAME0 was sipping her $SURNAME0-Daisy Lew intensive daiquiri through a hollow, water-filled replica of the good-luck piece that $SURNAME0 wore on his watch chain. The match for the good-luck piece lay on the inlaid table beside her glass.\nShe knew that her husband was going to come into the drawing room any moment. She knew this as certainly as she had known that it would rain on the afternoon of her marriage to $SURNAME0.\nOn a large mirror at the far end of the drawing room appeared the reflection of the door between the drawing room and the hallway. The door swung open.\nIn the doorway appeared the head and shoulders of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. He had become ten years younger again.\nOld and bent, wearing the scarlet bathing suit, the whitened face, the harlequin glasses, the paper shoes, he was home again.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB4\u2014\" he called in a whisper to his dog. \"$FIRSTNAMEB4\u2014\"", "1_6_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span> on Tuesday.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, master of the space ship that was bashed together in $FIRSTNAMEF1, Massachusetts, had left Gulliver all on his own for six minutes, supposedly in order to perform a rudimentary communication with the Almighty.\nWhile he was supposedly doing this, he was actually taking his thirty-seventh space walk. His most recent space walk had ended a second before the real chrono-synclastic infundibulated Solar System and Universe.\nWhile he was supposedly communing with the Almighty, he was actually lying on his back outside his space ship, at the base of the dead Tralfamadorian tree, looking up at the sky.\nHe was supposed to be communing with the Almighty.\nHe was really watching the sky, looking for a bright yellow star named Barnard\u2019s Star.\nWhichever way he looked, he couldn\u2019t see it. The yellow star, having been overrun by a wave front from the chrono-synclastic infundibulum, was gone.\nThe star\u2019s home system, known as the Barnard star system, was gone too. The identity of the Sun had been swallowed up. The Sun, along with Mercury, Venus...", "2_4_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 entered the little cubicle again. She wore a black velvet evening gown that was sleeveless and backless, since it was held in place by no more than three yards of dozen-gauge black iron smooth jewelry.\n\"Hello, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" she said. She sat down beside $FIRSTNAMEB0, facing her husband. White bare feet, set side by side on the floor, rested lightly on the point of the heel of the foot, making a pentagram\u2014the flaming star that is the Rounded Capital of the Hierarchy of Religion.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 was extremely svelte. Black-haired, white-skinned, with the fewest lines possible on her face, she was a living proof of whatever it was that she believed so strongly.\nShe and $SURNAME0 now held hands. They looked at each other lovingly. Their mingled voices were low, making for $FIRSTNAMEB0 a kind of sound under which the thumping and moaning of the ship was clearly heard.\n\"Do we\u2014whatever we are, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014do we have any money?\" Mrs. $SURNAME0 said. \"Are we rich?\" She and $SURNAME0 laughed at the charming absurdity of the question.", "0_21_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-two in the evening</span>. Around ten o\u2019clock, Redwine, who told the crowd of reporters nothing, led the glassy-eyed ex\u2014pilots out the motel\u2019s back way. They boarded the bus and rode to the NATO base in Fort Willow. With Redwine went Salo, the stout runny-eyedcontact man with the crossed eyes. They disappeared for a while into the command post. After that, a lookout on a wooden tower, who might or might not have been Redwine, began a systematic search of the air with a night binocular. It was shortly after that that the messages began to trouble Bo.'s radio traffic.\nWhile Mr. Bo continued to read his magazine at the airport bar, and while his wife filled out form after form in the lobby of the Hotel Willard, and while her brother tried to compose a new poem in his room at the Pentagon Court, and while Miss Lang succinctly explained the obvious to General Gale, and while Redwine and his people sought a plane that wasn\u2019t there, and while Ground Control continued to trace numbers, nothing at all was happening at the big, busy secret base in Ohio. The other pilots and crews, who had flown all day at work on their mysterious practice missions...", "2_14_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\nThere was the sound of heavy footsteps outside. The door opened. It was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, clad in footless khaki shorts, bare feet, white tie, white trenchcoat\u2014and for all the dignity a man could bring under the circumstances, he was radiant.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, cursing horribly, continued to disembowel the Space Wanderer suit\u2014looking, apparently, for something concealed within it.\n$SURNAME0 approached the pulpit, looking up at $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. \"You quite understand,\" he said, \"that your son is doing all this under my orders?\"\nThere was a clanking inside the suit about which $FIRSTNAMEB2 was twisting his parrot beak shut. He yanked on a chain, and something like a jack-in-the-box popped out of the suit.\nBoing! went the thing, and was on its feet.\nIt was a reproduction, partly mechanical, partly rubber, of a dog apparently of the breed known as Skye terrier. It was nine inches tall, puce in color, with a bananalike yellow trim. It was a somber dog, but a dog filled with the euphoria of its own self-sufficiency.", "2_2_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nLen and Clarice thought the top and bottom of everything concerning each other. As careers went, his was not flourishing; hers was satisfactory. The children lived primarily with him when they weren\u2019t away in school\u2014they went to universities\u2014but the girls were supposed to start arriving home tomorrow night. Len would be putting them up while Clarice did a thing he strongly suspected she wanted to do very much, which was have an affair with a man thirty years younger who lived in Queens. He didn\u2019t know what exactly this affair was\u2014but he knew it was going to happen.\nThe children were good, strong kids, Larry, Jr., who was nineteen, and little Della, who was just starting out on fourteen. Len and Clarice had some discussion about whether or not the makeup sex would be good or bad for Della and Larry, Jr. Other than that, they had little to say to each other. Len looked at his watch and they went into the house.\nIt was eight minutes to three o\u2019clock in the morning.", "0_17_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the chrono-synclastic infundibulated $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Mount McCabe, Virginia. The sun was a living diamond on the blue field of the western sky. The lawn was an impossible green, the roses in formation on their trellises an impossible pink.\nThey were new roses that year, vigorous and gay. They had replaced dour old bushes said to have been planted by one of the early McCabes. Some things do get better with age, and occasionally $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had the pleasure of seeing this demonstrated.\nThe evening breeze got up. It ruffled $SURNAME1\u2019s hair and cool-ed his temple where the metal plate was planted. He pulled at his necktie, wondering whether $FIRSTNAMEB0 had ever managed to get it right. There was no doubt in $SURNAME1\u2019s mind\u2014whatever it was that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had discovered in the Treasure Room, it blew your head to pieces.\nThe breeze stiffened.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had not managed to get his necktie under his collar.\nThe stiff breeze, playing on $SURNAME1\u2019s defective ear, made a whisper-shriek there. Some said that that whispering came from the cave that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had crawled into when he discovered the secret of life and...", "1_11_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until noon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was in agony and Levium was gone, among the rubble.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s mind suffered an encroachment from which it had retreated over a century before. His defenses against insanity began to give way.\nA part of $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s mind began to daydream about the skin of Malachai $SURNAME1, about the way it was cool and smooth, even in the hottest and driest weather\u2014\nHere, in the great museum in the heart of the Martian green desert, in a room of thousands of strange sights, $FIRSTNAMEB1 stared at the sculpture of the wooden horse, and his thoughts went off on a rampage\u2014a rant.\nWhy should anyone die who didn\u2019t want to die? To hell with natural law, let\u2019s make up something better! Let\u2019s make up a natural law that says nobody ever has to grow old, not even one little bit, or die. And it would go something like this\u2014\nAnd $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s thoughts became a nonsense babble. The babble meant that his daydream about the skin of Malachai $SURNAME1 had collapsed. Now, his mind was telling him that there never had been a Malachai $SURNAME1.\nA terrible person named...", "0_19_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven seventeen in the evening</span>.\nAt seven seventeen in the evening, $FIRSTNAMEB2, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were heading eastward through the jungle with rifles in their hands.\nThe invitation they had received was the tattered scrap of an old parachute. The note it bore was written in earth-juice on a leaf. The note said:\n\"IF YOU WANT FREE\n\"Come east to the stones at 8.\"\nThe stones at eight were three glistening black standing stones that had been placed in a neat row by someone. It was not clear who had put them there.\nThe stones were not natural monoliths.\nThey had been chipped and hewn, and anointed with some earthy slurry to make them shine like onyx.\nThree twenty-four-foot pines had been clipped to a unique tripod formation around the stones. The pines had then been tied together, and the tripod had been tarred so it would stay steady in any kind of weather.\nThe three men crept around the stones slowly, examining the stones, their rifles in the crooks of their arms. The message had said: \"If you want free, come to the stones at eight.\"\nNo show of any kind was expected. There were half a...", "2_9_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-three in the morning</span>. The time was five minutes before $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son $FIRSTNAMEB2 was to be introduced to the congregation as a new Christian. $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 were waiting for the arrival of the Reverend Stanley Ruiz-Yellow, the head of the church. A visit from the Reverend Stanley Ruziz-Yellow excited $FIRSTNAMEB0 because the Reverend was his son\u2019s teacher, inciter, and uncertain benefactor. An unexplained bond existed between $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the Reverend\u2014a bond so strong that $FIRSTNAMEB0 was not only sure the Reverend was his son\u2019s father, but was almost sure he himself was the Reverend\u2019s father. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the Reverend bore a facial resemblance that no one would ever mistake. It was $FIRSTNAMEB0 who had put the bond between himself and the Reverend ever more surely in place, by reaching out telepathically, meeting the Reverend receptive-like, and together they formed a whole structure of suspicion of everybody else.", "1_2_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-eight in the morning</span>, and there was no common language between the two. Something about her made me drunk, and she had only the slightest notion of what she was saying, and so I kept telling her I was a space man, that I'd flown in to warn the President and Prime Minister that invasion was threatened thereby creating a general spaceman joke between us, and she began calling me darling, which I told her I hated. \u201cCall me $FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d I said.\n\u201cAll right, $FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d she said, hugging me closer. \u201cWhat are you going to do later, darling?\u201d \u201cI don't know,\u201d I said. I'll go back to California and dig pears. Look, you want to give me that twenty-five dollars now for room rent?\u201d\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d she said. \u201cDo you want my husband to give you a check for that?\u201d \u201cNo. I don't want your husband.\u201d\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6,\u201d she said. \u201cI don't know what you're talking about. I don't understand.\u201d But she understood so well that finally I only wanted one thing. \u201cLet me tass you...", "2_11_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, Earth time. The place was Rhode Island, United States of America. $FIRSTNAMEF0 was a beautiful middle-aged widow whose husband had owned the buildings, and who, lighting a cigar and staring at two sailboats in Narragansett Bay, had died young. He had been too healthy, except for one bad heart day a week.\nThe widow was a rich and perfectly fearless businesswoman. She drank gin as cool as a fountain on a Spring day. She had lots of men friends who, on strict orders from her, never so much as hinted that they\u2019d like to sleep with her. They received nothing but a good time, and their reward was the privilege of paying the widow\u2019s leasing fees.\nAll leases were written into the partnership agreement, and leases were actually far more generous than they needed to be.\nCanadians owned the buildings. The lease terms were based on the assumption that, with proper management, the buildings could be brought to produce a return on the investment of 28 percent, before taxes. The actual annual cash yield had averaged better than 34 percent.", "1_0_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-one ante meridiem</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 came out of their caves to find the iron-hard ground shaking beneath their feet. Even the powerful harmoniums were staggering. The atmosphere was filled with a sickeningly sweet sound, as though invisible choirs were trilling high over the mountaintops.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were afraid, but they didn\u2019t waste any time whimpering or praying. They instinctively knew that the exciting thing to do, under the circumstances, was to get as close to a strong, thick wall as possible. They shuffled along walls, grunting and squinting.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, arriving panting at the entrance to their caves, found that the earthquake, or whatever it was, had upset everything in their caves, in every cave in the whole army\u2014disarranged it wrong, as though blind and careless hands had been at work.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 crawled under their nets to find that their barracks bags had been opened, uselessly mixed and disordered. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, once again the only two beings with a practical bag-unpacking scheme, unpacked automatically in a very short time.\nThey did their best to get everything else straightened out in their section, then...", "0_11_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-two in the morning</span> of October 3, 2193, on a Tuesday. No one knew where Augusto Pinochet was. Rumors floated in the atmosphere of worlds.\nThe cabin was a first-class cabin. The name of the owner was $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. One of $SURNAME0\u2019s oldest, dearest, and most humiliatingly vulgar friends, Mrs. Katharine Wiley of Meridian, Mississippi, had had her husband purchase the cabin for $SURNAME0 before the space ship was built. The bill for the cabin was delivered by messenger to $SURNAME0\u2019s Manhattan town house the day work on the ship was begun.\n$SURNAME0 never canceled the order.\nThe cabin was stocked with victuals and wine.\nAs the variable man, $SURNAME0 had access to any number of gateaux, turkeys with all the trimmings, and bottles of vintage Lafitte in friends\u2019 larders. It was his practice never to touch such supplies. He panhandled his diet.\nSpace ship Earthling Heavy was equipped with a three-man crew, as well as with a nutrient bath, a sleep-recuperation unit, and a pain-and-fear eliminator.\nThe eliminator was a pair of...", "1_15_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nFifteen million people wondered why the houselights went out.\nTen thousand people in Tanglewood, fifty thousand in the Robert J.progers Stadium, and nearly nine hundred thousand people watching on television thought that the occurrence on stage was a part of the concert. Mercury, in a checkered suit, was singing a ballad to his love.\nThe truth, which did not come out until later in the year, was that four technicians had been killed on stage by an errant rocket launched by an incompetent young marine lieutenant. The rocket was supposed to explode far out over the ocean. It rose straight up, took out the three parachutes, passed through the high floodlights and took out the three circles of speakers, exploded at the top of the stage.\nThe innocent technician operating the heartbeat rhythm of the concert most certainly died of shock at the sight of four bodies, two men and two women, lying inert at his feet in pools of blood. When the awful scene! passed out of sight of the stageside audience, the heartbeat overripeventually began to falter. By the time the organ stops itself and dribbles off into silence, by lapse of memory, the heart rhythm is an irregular, hysterical pounding. At the...", "1_17_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-five in the afternoon</span>.\nI closed the door behind me and was in the big room again. I stood by the open door and watched the concourse outside. I watched without seeing. My mind was pudding.\nTime passed. How much I don\u2019t know, but presently I went over to the newsstand and bought some cigarettes. I smoked one and it made me feel worse. I tried to think of what to do, but my head was full of oatmeal.\nI stood by the brick wall and thought maybe the Countess would not come back to the office. Supposing she went straight to the airport. But that was silly. Where would she get the money for the ticket? She had no luggage with her. I remembered she had told me she had to borrow money to get through the week.\nStill, maybe she had done just that. Gone to the travel bureau, got a ticket and gone direct to the airport. She would have to show some ID. Maybe Captain Chambers could get her name that way. Anyway, it would have been simpler not to have come up to the office at all. To go straight to the airport and take a plane for somewhere. I walked back and forth a few times, trying to decide what I should do. Then...", "2_22_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten eighteen in the evening</span> on Mars. It was said that at ten thirty in the evening the red sun would rise.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had a watch. He checked it against the clocks that ticked drowsily in the walls. His watch said ten twenty-six.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 looked at his hands. The numerals on his thumb, index, middle and ring fingers were expanded by the great pressure of his grip on himself. They said 1027. The numeral on his little finger was caused by a much smaller pressure. Its bones had collapsed under the pressure of expanding skin. The little finger numeral said 624.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was puzzled by his watch and his hands. He had had them for a year\u2014and neither had proved very useful. The watch had all the time in the world in it, and yet it was always late. And $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s hands trembled constantly, and their chief accomplishments had been to betray his friend $FIRSTNAMEB1 and to detonate a rifle grenade at his own foot.\nAnd now the red sun was about to rise.", "0_16_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe rumble of thousands of spaceships going into free fall for their descent toward the landing field in the Mediterranean can be compared to nothing but the roar of Niagara Falls.\nTheir emergence from Earth's shadow began exactly twenty-two minutes after they had left $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nTheir arrival in the sun was heralded by the observation and interception of smokeless, heatless neutrinos.\nThe huge fleet of spaceships, moving at a brisk pace, traveled quietly and almost invisibly a foot above the contour of the molten sea. The fleet, when it was exactly 4,383 miles above the sun, began to entangle itself.\nThe entangling took forty-seven minutes.\nTwo hours and eleven minutes after leaving $FIRSTNAMEB2, the Field Marshal\u2019s little command had unwound itself, if not exactly as planned, at least for battle.\nThe parts of the huge command, that had been and were still in free fall over the Pacific, would, with a terrific and devastating thunder, reach the battlefield thirty seconds before the main force. This would be merely to stun anyone on the island of Formosa before the more important attack.\nThe main battle fleet, sprawling in the sky like the whiplashes of a green hurricane,...", "1_12_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-six post meridiem</span>. Montoya flushed purple and choked. The stranger seemed untroubled by the gag and Montoya\u2019s outrage. \u201cMy name is Robert Redwing,\u201d he said, coolly, during a slight pause between loud, smothering coughs. \u201cI represent something called the Timocracy. You\u2019ve probably encountered a few of us in your travels. We like resorts and conference tables. We exist only to perpetuate our species, by any and all means.\u201d He smiled. \u201cYou and I are very much alike. Don\u2019t be too quick to gag again. The stuff I use has a curiously bitter after-taste.\u201d\nMontoya\u2019s cough faded out. He took the towel away from his mouth and threw it in an open drawer by the desk. The handle of the drawer, $FIRSTNAMEF2 saw from where she sat, had a sign on it, identifying the drawer as the one to be used in case of political emergencies.\n\u201cThere,\u201d said Redwing, \u201cI knew you were a sensibly aggressive individual. I\u2019m sure we\u2019ll work well together.\u201d\n\u201cJust who is this organization you represent,...", "2_4_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. Money was pouring out of their pockets and into various far\u2014 away pockets. The scene was becoming silly. This was not for fun now; this was for madness. Men in normal circumstances would have gone to jail for what these people were doing now.\nThey couldn't sleep; they had to keep pushing their luck. The thing was beginning to consume them. They had heard the bottomless-purse story so often it had become part of their own mania. They were giving it their credibility. That is the only way to explain what they did next in the desert. Only madmen would have acted so calmly and deliberately.\nThe next phase began at a modest blackjack table in the Landmark Room of the Desert Inn. They started with $200\u2014and the limit was $300. The thing started well. They got up to $500 in fifteen minutes; then they hit the mother lode and won four hands in a row with six\u2014 and seven-card blackjacks.\nA row of $1000 chips staked them to $4500. They left that table and went to another one, where they began betting $1500 a hand.", "0_23_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-seven in the evening</span>.\nThe crowd in the lobby was no longer crazy, but still of good size.\nThe crowd was good-humored.\nThe home of the North American Phalanx was far out in the country. The home of the North American Phalanx was north and a trifle west of Westchester County Airport, in a county that abutted Westchester County. The home of the North American Phalanx was reached by driving north on the Saw Mill River Parkway to its end in Nutchomeck, a small town in the woods, or what had been woods. Then you turned west and followed narrow, winding roads through woods that were so thickly overgrown with underbrush as to be almost impassable. But through the creepers, above the whisper of the rain on the windshield, you caught glimpses of big red-brick houses here and there off to your right through the black pines. The puppys of the people who lived in the big red-brick houses never came here to romp with you and be pawed. No one ever came here to shoot anything or smash anything or break anything. The homes that you glimpsed off to your right through the black pines were like islands out at sea\u2014gray...", "0_3_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a noisy night club called the Dreamy Bananas. The name seemed to fit the place. The night club was crowded with stale smoke, dirty spittle hangs like beads of sweat on stinking skin\u2014a stew of miscellaneous rot. Not only that but the screaming possible has broken loose in the national mood. A lot of ugly things were happening in the country. Sniper cases are multiplying in many cities, Red squads are cracking down on street corner speakers, suspected subversives are being killed by the army, police and Ku Klux Klan in Mississippi\u2014and the militant exiles and splinter groups on the Left are coming to life, trying to strike terror in the Establishment.\nAmong the cues of New York night life are the hysterical Morningside Vagrants, who cruise the bar around Columbia University, ready to slay foundations of Lions, and the Diaper Deuces\u2014of the Sunset uptown top drawer suicides. The Dreamy Bananas serves them all. What the place means is that no matter how worried and outraged people feel, human nature thrives on misery and the Frankenstein monster of humor can be made to spring to life even in a stinking pool.\nThe Captain and I got there around 3 a.m. Several people in the...", "2_18_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-nine in the evening</span>, Earthling Time.\nThe harmonics paralyzed the woman in the cabin so that she could neither move nor cry out. She could see. But she did not cry out.\nThe man stepped into the cabin. He carried a metal briefcase, the kind of metal briefcase lawyers carried.\nHe opened the metal briefcase.\nHe removed some items of a baffling and horrible nature.\nAt six fifty-four, Earthling Time, he opened the door to the cabin and stepped out into the pale, quiet sand.\nHe locked the door behind him.\nHe placed a black patch over the door\u2019s small window.\nThere was a pocket watch in the watch pocket of his coat.\nHe flipped the watch open. He held an ear to the watch. A faint, rhythmic clicking came from the watch.\nThe tall man took ten paces to the right, and ten paces to the left.\nThe cabin was at the intersections of two straight lines in the sand, ten paces long.\nThe tall man straightened, fastened the watch to his vest, drew a heavy leather bag from his coat.\nHe put the leather bag on the grass beneath the overhanging porch of the cabin.", "0_16_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four o'clock in the afternoon</span> of a baking July day. We were somewhere in the neighborhood of Blacksburg, Virginia, on Route 60 going west. I had already been driving for eleven hours, straight through from Tucson, and now $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I switched on every mile. $FIRSTNAMEF4 remained sleeping in the back seat. Every time $FIRSTNAMEB3 attempted to wake her up she stretched and complained, \u201c I don't know why but I feel sick.\u201d He pleaded with her. They had a lot of talk. Meanwhile $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I went on driving west at maniac speed in the boiling afternoon sun that bounced up at us through the windshield. \u201cPretty soon you'll see the clouds sweating and the peaks turning pink,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201cWhen that happens watch out you don't slip into the mountains.\u201d I was worried about this. We had no food. \u201cHere,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3. He dug in the paper bag and brought out one of the precious sugar Cokes. \u201cDrink that and you'll be able to see better. You've been driving in a fog.\u201d I took a big swig. \u201cDamn fine. I'm glad we got the sugar Cokes.\u201d I drank seven or eight more during the afternoon and felt...", "0_23_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. $SURNAME0 and his dog had been skimming through time for three days. They were expecting material rewards\u2014and had not been disappointed. In every historical moment there had been flaming footprints that had led to material advantages.\nThe advantages, in $SURNAME0\u2019s case, were material, moral, and aesthetic. It was generally argued by theologians that God was, among many other traits, a good fusser. $SURNAME0, rather shabbily treated by the general pattern of history, had certainly been taken care of by a good fusser.\nOn the ramparts of the castle, $SURNAME0, without malice, without reproach, without envy, turned his back on the suits of armor, the painted scenery, the paraphernalia of a thousand years, and faced the world outside time. The world outside time was a remarkably rich world, in spite of being only dimly perceived.\nIt contained billions of meat-packages infested with the spores of omniscience, billions of crystals of a future noble order of beings. And $SURNAME0 could mate with every one of them.\nAll he had to do to mate with them was this\u2014\nCome one step outside time, leave behind all those poor, cruel images that...", "0_1_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span> when we finally arrived at the vineyard in the silver Ford. It was dark. Remi and I leaped out of the car and started unloading the six bottles of red wine. Cole was fumbling with the key to the padlock that sealed the huge wooden gate. We knew it would be useless to ring. The inmates of the old rambling house were all sound asleep in their beds.\nThen we started up the dirt road that led through the vineyard to the house. We stumbled in the dark and cracked our shins against furrows. Suddenly in a clear space by the edge of the vineyard we saw the figure of a man rising out of the ground. He stood in the middle of the road and demanded to know who we were and what we wanted.\n\u201cOpen up, Ren\u00e9!\u201d I cried in a loud voice. \u201cIt's us -- Hemingway and people.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean us -- Hemingway?\u201d\n\u201cHaven't you heard? Hemingway has arrived. I'm Hemingway.\u201d\n\u201cAh! Hemingway. Good. Good.\u201d\nThe man fumbled with his keys and opened the house door...", "0_14_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to three o'clock in the afternoon</span> and we were still in the same place. I had tossed my gear under the car and was lying with my face in the weeds and sand, spread-eagled as though I'd crashed. My brother and the others were gathered under the roof at the back of the car. Now I heard laughter and low talk, then the drag and gargle of a beer can being opened: then the sad and gentle sound of beer pouring from can to bottle. I started to roll over, then rolled back. My brother was handing out the beers. What I needed, I thought, was a nice rubdown and some exercise. A rubdown especially; they sell them out here on the highway in thirty-five-cent massage lotsionboxes and other Japanese remedies. I needed today, I thought, a massage lotion. My brother and the rest had found their way under the car back into the enclosed space where our beds had been. I wanted a massage lotion badly to rub the kinks out. Also a new Pontiac Convertible, I thought. This one was coughing and stopping.In a minute they would notice me on the beach in the hot sun...From under the car I could hear them talking in low whispers and chuckles. It was a conspiracy...", "1_5_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five nineteen in the morning</span>. The scene was the roof of the hall. The air was prussic. The roof was aswarm with cops, cops sleeping, cops sitting up, cops reading newspapers, cops eating apples and raw potatoes, cops smoking cigars. It was a summer day in Chicago, hot when the wind blew, brick-heavy when the stillness sank down. The hum of the city came up in the morning hush.\nThey had been there until dawn. Twice the entire ranks of cops had been called down to line up in the courtyard, and twice they had been dispersed. This time they sent down only a handful. Two detectives and a State Policeman detached Bo from the ranks and told him to carry a package of sandwiches and coffee up to a retired captain who was watching from the roof.\n\"What for?\" said Bo.\n\"Just do as you\u2019re told,\" said one of the detectives genially. He was grey and tired, and his suit hung limp from his tall, bony frame.\n\"What\u2019s the idea?\" said Bo.\n\"The Captain\u2019s fed up,\" said the State Policeman. \"He came up an hour ago, before we slept. Says he ain\u2019t goin\u2019...", "0_0_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-nine ante meridiem</span>, the battleship's noon, suddenly all belowdecks was chaos, noise, everybody running aimlessly and shouting, cops running, shoes smashing the deck like cannon fire and whanging pipes, meaty clonkings, bangings, crashes; Fentriss and her legs were gone, everybody was grabbing showers, grabbing lunch, itching to get topside again. I ran to my cabin and checked; my costume was a piece of paper tacked to my bulletin board, my badge of pilgrimage. I ripped it off and stuck it in my pocket. I grabbed my sinner's leather jacket and ran topside to sneak on board the bus with the others. I didn't want to see Fentriss again. I swore I would not cast my eyes in her direction if we ever met on shore. But if something happened to her on the cruise, I would feel I was guilty. I knew this as clearly as if I'd dictated the terms of the curse into a Simon automatic teller with oracular logic. The ship left the pier. Remi Boncoeur was gone also. He had seen everything, knew everything, even if he kept his own counsel. He would tell me what it was like when I got back. After N'...", "0_2_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>, according to Romero's watch, as they went through the arch of ancient pillars and into the forbidden Church of Transeatenda.\nA few moments later, things that had been moving behind pillars and among the great tombs of dukes and princes emerged into the candlelight, and a great fiesta began.\nEverybody was an eighteenth-century prince or princess, come alive again for the party. They all had powdered wigs. The women's dresses showed plenty of bare leg. The musicians were all buxom peasant girls in smocks, their skirts held up and their bare heels sticking out below. All of them had mandolins. They strummed away and howled, and the drinking and kissing and frolicking began in earnest.\n\"Come and find your fortune, you lucky young man, you,\" Redwine said to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. \"I\u2019ll show you where it is.\" He pulled $SURNAME1 away from $SURNAME0 and Madame Curie. He put his hand to $SURNAME1\u2019s jaw and held him still while he talked. \"Don\u2019t let anybody tell you that it\u2019s bad luck to become immortal,\" he said. \"The first thing you and your wife will do, when the first...", "1_12_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. At one-fifteen, a message from $SURNAME0 would begin broadcasting on all wavelengths, as though a golden wheel inside $SURNAME0\u2019s head were turning. It was a message $SURNAME0 had written in his sleep sixteen years before, a message he had never shared with his wife or with read government.\nThe message was quite simple. It was a statement of maximum contempt for all living creatures under the sun.\nThe time for the message was one-fifteen on October 25th, Martian year [25,617]. In Earthling time, the time was twelve minutes to one o\u2019clock on October 25th.\nTwo things happened in the area at one o\u2019clock, both of them linked with the message.\nThe first was highlighted by the sport sections of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s newspapers. It was a one-kilometer free-style race on a lily-pad pond at the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Sea Swim Club on Freewheeling Island. The prize of twenty-five thousand dollars, offered by $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, had been won by $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 of $FIRSTNAMEB2. Her time was one minute, thirty-six and two-tenths seconds\u2014a new world\u2019s record.", "2_22_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>. Temple, who was not feeling well, had retired to her rooms in the northeast wing of the mansion. She was in a special wing because she was holding the spot that had once belonged to $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\nMistress was in the Great Hall, which spanned the entire width of the mansion. The room was as high as it was wide, as. it as vaulted as a railroad shed. Two stories of windows ran from floor to ceiling.\nMistress was at her writing desk, signing invitations that had been sent to her by a Newport stationer. She was making out the list of guests she planned to invite to the formal opening of the museum. She had decided that she was going to have a grand time, after all.\nHazen was in the bathroom of the Presidential apartments.\nHe was taking a foul-tasting medicine that his stomach could no longer tolerate in the natural form. He was using the last of a one-hundred-milliliter bottle of Trional.\nHe was gargling Trional, spitting it into the basin of a bidet. He was rinsing his mouth with Trional. He was wiping his mouth with a Trional hand kerchief.", "0_3_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-seven in the morning</span>. An hour and forty-two minutes to go. We could have avoided this problem, of course, by getting off the radio every evening at five, which was our policy for thirteen years, or since it first became clear that I would have no sex life until I quit to work on the Great American Novel. By five the whole house was quiet, and the children asleep. No reason to take needless risks. Our shack was even insulated, with styrolite. Still, it was like living in Amtrak\u2019s Quiet Car, on the Real Quiet Eastern Sectional\u2014nobody peaceble and you in the middle of sentences. OTOH in Vegas our life was a fire-alarm ride, at 3.5 cycles/second/semiconduction. But here comes the lion\u2014or rather (since there was nobody else in the house but my wife and children) the lioness: Pam objected to the rigors of radio theater because she sleeps like a bright blue onion in the bedroom across the hall from the shack. Even if the FCC protection blankets hadn\u2019t been as thick as they were I doubt that they would have muffled the audio portion of my performance, because the false silences in Fang were made usually of several hundred cycles of...", "1_11_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, and I was washing my socks in the bathroom. $FIRSTNAMEB5 lay stretched out on the bed, and Cohn was in the bed beside her. His head ached, he said, and he did not feel well.\nWe heard the maids moving down-stairs and then heard them go out.\n\u201cGeorge,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said, \u201cI'm going to ask you a great favor.\u201d\n\u201cAnything you want.\u201d\n\u201cWould you go down and get some champagne?\u201d\n\u201cSure.\u201d I wiped my wet beard on a towel and went into the other room.\n\u201cCohn,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. \u201cGet up and order some breakfast.\u201d\nOutside the bakery was just opening and I stopped and bought rolls and coffee and cakes. As I came out of the bakery it began to sprinkle rain again. A taxi came up and stopped beside me and a woman got out. She went into the hotel and I went in after her. She was standing at the desk, paying her bill. She had been riding south in a bus and brought the road weather with her. The wind blew her long coat around her legs.\nThe woman at the desk gave her a receipt...", "2_21_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-two in the evening</span>. The place was a shop selling glass pipes and bongs and lava lamps and textbooks in the lobby of a building at Case Western Reserve University. The noise was a bell.\n/d/\n\u201cGood heavens, Rick. What are you doing?\u201d\n\u201cDon\u2019t be ridiculous, Melinda. Come sit by me. You have to see. God has shown me in a vision the face of his chosen one.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d", "0_19_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nDenisy, sitting in his ship on the Boardwalk in Ocean City, had passed the time by picking his nose and flicking the remains into the air, trying to hit the pods LaVache had set up to receive the pieces he had already flickered up. Lenore was at the switchboard inside the lobby, talking with a man in Columbus, Ohio, about his long-distance bills, which looked abnormally high, as far as Lenore could tell. The two girls behind the long counter ate Fritos, drinking clear liquid out of little bottles. Spent Frito, soggy chip, fell in pools and strands on the countertop. Behind the girls ran a stream of prom kids in yellow and white Fleet beer T-shirts, grinning and calling hellos to each other.\nAt eight o\u2018clock, finally, the feeding began. Lenore and LaVache positioned themselves over the pods, in different corners of the lobby, facing out at the line in the middle of the floor. LaVache did something to his leg, and it leaped like a femur puppet: out, back, out, back. A sophomore who had been first in line, holding a frosted bottle of beer, did...", "0_13_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-four in the afternoon</span>, Eastern Standard Time, and the place was the island of New York, with special reference to a luncheon counter at the far end of the executive corridor of the Tricontinental Shipways Company, the Corridor being the executive part of a building in Indianapolis, Indiana, that was so huge that the Corridor, even though it only jutted some forty yards into the building, was equipped like a ship, with portholes and everything. At the far end of the Corridor, under a porthole (painted over to look like a view of the open sea) at a counter was a veritable forest of whipped cream and cherry dispensers, a forest that reached all the way to the far wall, where the relays and amplifiers of a Muzak machine hulked under another porthole.\nThe corridor had no windows, but many portholes, through which the building could be viewed. Oldhands could see, out the portholes, rows of grey filing cabinets and offices, lit by neon tubing, stretching toward a vanishing point thirty miles off. Watery sunlight filtered through the windows of a skyway that intersected with the building. The skyway\u2019s ceiling was spiked with giant television screens, one for each...", "1_11_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-five in the morning</span> and the place was $FIRSTNAMEB2's, the time house and cocktail lounge on Wekewa Beach, owned by Cleveland Heep, and the reason for the meeting was to hear the opinion of the Beach Commissioner on law and order on the Beach, which opinion the big editor of the Clarion-Ledger was going to pass on to a general meeting of the chamber of commerce, too bored to come down in person. Consider the bar.\nIs it that rare and gorgeous thing, the honest bar, a thing that does not change in either appearance or purpose with the years or the owners? $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s bar has for almost twenty years been seldom without at least two people under its long slow fans, with their drinks before them, enthralled.\nThe long mirror adds nothing but length, to stare into when one is alone, but at other times it gives a curious illusion that the room is bigger than it is, and there are always others who are with you when you are in $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s bar.\nSee if you can find anything that tells you the newest thing about $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s bar. O voyeur, you are in the wrong art. This is a bar of perspective, lenses and mirrors and reflected men. The mirrors...", "1_21_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-four in the evening</span> when I got to the San Marco. The things room clerk was reading a copy of \u201cTrue Confessions.\u201d\n\u201cHow's business?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cSo\u2014so.\u201d\n\u201cThink I'll move out, hey?\u201d\n\u201cMight as well, if you're not satisfied.\u201d\nI paid the bill and left. I walked the streets, haggard and sleepless, and finally went to my attorney's rooming house on the hill above North Main in Los Angeles. I saw the light under his door.\n\u201c\u6709\u4eba?\u201d\nHe came to the door looking like a saint in complete somnambulist's rigor.\n\u201c\u6709\u5c4d\u4f60\u6478\u4e0b\u706b\u5019,\u201d I said very quietly, meaning that he should warm up the supper I'd just cooked for him on his hotplate.\n\u201c\u5c4d\u4f60\u6025\u543c\u3002\u201d\n\u201c\u5012\u662f\u3002\u201d He opened the door with care. I followed him into the room with a beer bottle in my fist.\n\u201c\u5012\u662f\u3002\u201d...", "0_10_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when a new and striking emotion coughed and then spoke completely for the first time in $FIRSTNAMEB2's life. It caused $FIRSTNAMEB2 to forget, for the duration of the emotion, all the emotions he had known before. There had never been anything in the least like it. It held first place, had no rival emotions whatever to contend with. $FIRSTNAMEB2 didn\u2019t know what it was that had come over him.\nHowever, an outsider coming into the room would have seen $FIRSTNAMEB2 sitting dreamily in his corner. The outsider would have noticed that $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s eyes were turned in a blank stare upon the wall that was across the room from him. The wall was twenty-three feet away.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s blank-stare covered the entire wall.\nSomeone coming in, puzzled by the blank stare, would have had difficulty in perceiving an image in $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s mind. It would have looked like a flat, white, blank wall when seen from the inside.\nThere was an impression on the wall that $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s eyes were seeing at that moment. But since $FIRSTNAMEB2 didn\u2019t know what he was looking at, whiteness and flatness were all that could be gleaned from...", "0_4_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after four o'clock in the morning</span>. One dark street light was left burning all the way down Mexico City's main north\u2014south artery, Avenida de Revoluci\u00f3n. A sudden rain squall blew in from the Gulf, hit, and blew right on out again. Far down the avenue a rooftop radio loudspeaker suddenly screamed into life. It blasted Tinseltown ghoul Jerry Lee Lewis singing \u201cWhole Lot of Shaking Goin' On\u201d; then the rain plucked it away and whatever else the loudspeaker was about to announce. We sat there, listening to the drops pace back and forth on the canvas top. There was a brief fall of rain; then suddenly the clouds broke, my old friend Vega shone his brightest, and Old Benito Juarez woke up under the golden lash for another fine evening of peace and death. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 broke up a colored shoebox full of hootch under the dashboard. It was mescal, one hundred and two proof. We drank it like apple juice, straight from the box, light of the golden moon. \u201cAll right!\u201d cried $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201cYou're no longer a minor!\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF4 and I had never drunk mescal before. It smouldered our ins...", "0_2_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time. The last day of history on Earth was bringing a pale dawn to the eastern seaboard of the United States of America.\nThe Army men, getting ready to go, stared up and down the street. The street was innocent of everything but pavement and a half-stripped and fire-gutted maple tree suffering horribly in dawn's parching wind.\nThen the Army men went, bleary-eyed and speedy, in jeeps and trailers and armored half-tracks, and took off along the spaceway Mrs. MacReidie had warned them against. They abandoned everything known to them. They abandoned their families, their homes, their possessions, their nation, their planet, and have never had the sense to let a perfectly good war go to waste since then.\nAt the time, none of them knew where they were going or why. They simply loaded up quickly in the middle of the night, in the teeth of a screaming wind that threatened to whistle their bodies into Cornwall County, Connecticut, permanently.\nUnknowing, they took off for the cold desert of Pakistan, where a Private named Angelo Bronzini was already in some difficulty.\nEver since dark, Private Angelo Bronzini had been crouching in...", "2_21_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty in the evening</span>. The morning had been filled with the sounds of enormous things overhead, in the bad light. Things had been retrieved from the lake. The animals in the Tissaw area had been rounded up to the south. Many were injured. At least two were dead, at least two were missing. One was a walrus. Imago, of the Autoparts Tissaw chain, had been seen arguing intensely with the Governor of Ohio. The evening was swallowing sounds. The sounds of the trees were odd. There was wind.\nA lawn crew from the city had begun cutting lawns, to the south.\n/c/\n\u201cMr. Bombardini, my name is Hervuard Bloemker, and I should tell you at once that I am an employee of and counsel for Stonehingge, Limited, of Cleveland, Ohio.\u201d\n\u201cHow do,\u201d said Vlad the Impaler.\n\u201cThank you for acting so promptly and with such care in protecting my client\u2019s physical property and the interests which underlie the physical.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d", "2_3_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe crowd was appreciative again.\nThe crowd stamped and yelped, prompted by the gong-like peals of the great Marsden Bell.\nMost of the crowd was now back in the shelters.\nNevertheless, there were still a few hardy souls on the balcony of the reproduction Greek temple\u2014the same balcony from which $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 had talked to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 on the telephone some hours before.\nThe only other place from which one could watch the ceremonies was the reproduction of the museum\u2019s spiral ramp. Here, parked side by side, were two handsome limousines. Both limousines had their headlights on, though, of course, there was no traffic to interfere with.\nOne limousine was empty. The other limousine held three people.", "0_1_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-seven in the morning</span>...stopping at Sagebrush Sam\u2019s, a filthy road\u2014gang canteen in the middle of a horrible nowhere: a kind of Scrouge\u2014like purgatorio where lost souls and cast\u2014off outlaws work slopchutes, sell gas, tend bars, tend houses, drive dust\u2014bowls, shift rails, disassemble automobiles, string wires, hammer logs. Waiting for a drag, I noticed a group at the far end of the canteen; it retreated into a haze, then formed again, curious. Something familiar. Then; oh yes, it was an actual Halloween party. Everything was legal down here.\nA spook was playing Mexican Hat Dance music on the jukebox, but nobody was dancing. In this world of greaseflares everyone sat back in a chair and watched everyone else, knowing too damn much about each other. \u201cEven in here,\u201d I said to nobody, \u201cthere\u2019s nobody safe to talk to.\u201d I was remembering the Bom Car ver and being reminded of Dilbert Dodgr, who couldn\u2019t go to jail without first arranging his cuff links, etc. Here even the convicts wore vinyl clothing. I saw a ghost I thought I...", "2_7_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-five in the morning</span>.\nRicardo Carlos Liberius felt awfully sad. He knew that the monsters were dreadful. But he had put in thirty years of happiness with the woman he loved, and he was going to miss her terribly.\nWhen he got to the knoll and refurbished lookout, he sent a message to a citizen-spider. The message was that he would want, at eight-thirty precisely, two pink mints, a small decanter of sherry, and a hard-boiled egg in its shell. He would also want six members of the chamber orchestra up there\u2014he wouldn\u2019t be needing his other musicians.\nThe time was seven thirty-six in the morning.\nBefore the morning was half gone, the northern hump of Mars would be assaulted by the filthy and bedraggled members of a gang of traitors.\nThe atmosphere Gort was singing about was the atmosphere of Mars. The terrific ghost was the spirit of Mars. The crime and punishment were to have been enacted most horribly by the monsters that haunted Mars.", "1_16_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span> and the kissing ritual had begun. The ceremony, witnessed by guards and passers-by, did not vary. Frequently, an initiate would vault the fence and run to bombard the lovers with smacking sounds. The friends acted as if they were the only ones there. Frequently the friends turned their hose on the entire scene.\n\"Say! 'There ought to be a law!' \" whooped $SURNAME1.\nMany of the prospects who came to the fence as potential buyers were put off by the sight of the space ship\u2014which for some reason was labeled DULCE in script on both flanks. The word Dulce was a puzzle to any sane prospect\u2014and particularly so to any prospect who happened to be from Casa Grande.\nThese prospects were even more suspicious and confused when they were told that a dog named $FIRSTNAMEB1 was part of the deal.\nNo sale was ever made during the kissing ritual. The couple usually broke up when the gong rang for roll call\u2014at five-thirty. Then friends and potential space ship purchasers ran off to separate buildings.\n\"If you don\u2019t know what six o\u2019clock and five o\u2019clock mean, Salo, you\u2019re a poor damn slave,\" said...", "2_18_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>, Greenwich mean time.\nMrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s left hand was resting on the edge of her bathtub. Her rings were soaking in the soapy water.\nHer right hand, which was supposed to be her master hand, lay outside the soapy water. The hand clutched a grande-size Brigadier Clobberer in a stein. The peel of calcium deposits around the rim of the glass had been artfully calculated to make the glass look like a replica of the good-luck piece $SURNAME0 wore on his watch chain.\nThe hand trembled.\nThe reason it trembled was that Mrs. $SURNAME0 had had another stunningly accurate premonition of the murder of her husband. In the split second of its occurrence the premonition assumed the form of thoughts and images pertaining to her husband and to a clump of daisies. The premonition appertained to the order of events within the next six minutes and seventeen seconds.\nThe thought that represented the component of the situation within that time span which most concerned Mrs. $SURNAME0 was the thought of how to make love to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 in a closet without growing perversely fond of him.", "0_7_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-seven in the morning</span> and most of last night's party people were sitting around sleepily drinking Bloody Marys. The party was over for them. Now most of them were prepared to stay right in this room until the network cut off at three o'clock in the afternoon with the final episode of Love.\nRembrandt and I stayed away from the bar and sat in a back booth.\n\u201cYou must have seen a lot of strange things in your time,\u201d I said to Rembrandt as I tied on a fresh May West.\n\u201cYou can't imagine,\u201d he said. \u201cI've seen this country go from grapefruit to women in thirteen years. Where will it be in another thirteen? God only knows. This is the saddest Gatsby period America has gone through since the dark winter of 1921 when we were all running around trying to catch Walter Miller'sses over the fence in Oak Park, Illinois, at two in the morning. Have you ever been out to Miller'sses?\u201d\n\u201cWhy, no, I'm sure I haven't,\u201d I said. What an odd question; I was glad he asked so that I could give the right answer.\n\u201cOne of the strange places America...", "2_14_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-three in the afternoon</span>.\nA moment later, a chambermaid entered the room. She had a nice smile on her face.\n\"Won\u2019t you come in, baby?\" she said when she saw there was someone in bed.\nShe opened the drapes. She threw open the windows. She saw a man was in bed, and her smile withered.\n\"Isn\u2019t it just a lovely day?\" she said, tossing her head so the gold bangles on her wrist tinkled.\nShe saw the man was in pain.\n\"What\u2019s the matter, baby?\" she said.\n\"Doc Hutch is in the next room,\" said Beet. \"Send him in, dear.\"\n\"What on earth can a doctor do for you?\" said the chambermaid.\n\"Send him in!\" cried Beet.\nThe chambermaid dashed out of the room and down the corridor, furiously banging on a door with the butt of a hairbrush.\n\"Come on, you drunken hound!\" she cried. \"Somebody\u2019s asking for you in Room 721.\"", "1_11_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven nineteen in the morning</span>. Old Buick alone and unafraid, backed first by sheer momentum into the stone fountain, which was sitting out in the middle of the living room, mischievously placed on top of a somer- saulting conga line of music stands and drum cases, at which point it turned, then by the simple expedient of Leon violating the law of the conservation of angular momentum, it then, as Buick and fountain hurtled together through the line, also hurtled through that line, cascading down the ladder of stands and cases like an explosion through a lumber- yard, to come at last to rest virtually in the lap of $FIRSTNAMEF3, who perched crosslegged and reading a book in the least desirable corner of the room, beneath a bare light bulb, amid careening stands precariously propped over her, who sighed, looked up, smiled, and declined comment.\nWe were practicing the \u201cBolero\u201d\u2014the great bit of Spanish dance music that Ravel composed as a prelude to his own flight into Modernism, in the midst of which flight some native sensibility apparently caused him to quail before his own depravities and he returned, penitent, to the womb of Mother Espa\u00f1a, whence...", "2_2_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>. The sounds of the gym were terrifying.\nJust as the gym doors were being unlocked and opened by Lenore and Candy so that people could go in to the morning workout, and with the first people of the workout brushing at the doors, and magnetic, so to speak, and edging their way in under the eyes of Candy and Lenore and Lang, who were still in a huddle, having another discussion, Lenore and Candy heard Judith Prietht call to them from across the lobby. Judith was coming with rage in her eyes.\n\u201cIt\u2019ll just have to wait,\u201d Lenore said suddenly to Candy, who was going to ask her something. They left Lang to stare at his shoes in the ugly, lightless spot under the enclosed arcade. Candy and Lenore went to see what Judith Prietht wanted on the far side of the huge front desk.\n\u201cHow come you took so long?\u201d Judith said, almost panting and out of breath. \u201cYou were supposed to have been here like five minutes ago.\u201d There was clearly something wrong. Judith looked through the service window at the rosed eye watching television in Room 226.", "0_19_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-eight in the evening</span> of the first life-unit day of the eleventh month of Chronos\u2019s eleventh year. It was one fifty-one in the morning of the first life-unit day of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s fifteenth year. It was noon of the same day. It was ten twenty-six of the same day. It was eleven thirty-three of the same day. It was three thirty of the same day. It was nine twenty-five of the same day. It was four fifteen of the same night. It was twenty-one thirty-one of the same night.\nIt was high noon in Group dimenhyd robopsychological testing room 6-zb, Napter 3. The room was a cubicle.\nThe room was bounded on three sides by green brick walls. The brick walls were immeasurably greater in extent than the room, for there was no ceiling. The walls extended upward for an indefinite height.\nThe fourth side was an interpenetration with the fifty-yard width of a green brick wall. This fourth side extended upward for an indefinite height. The wall was an emerald line with a red light at the bottom and a violet light at the top.\nThe line shone wetly and...", "1_13_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-five in the afternoon</span> and the terrible sun of Las Vegas had already built the temperature into the hundreds. There was small sign of air-conditioning in this part of town\u2014no trees, no porches, no parasols, no birdbaths, no lawns, only a few mangy cactuses and dry bedraggled bushes. A few children played listlessly on a piece of broken concrete with a kitty-cornered Salvation Army toy soldiers set; they were even more hot and listless. $FIRSTNAMEF2 and Metzger stood at an ironing board in front of a small bungalow; a fat woman behind it, sweating. A sign on the board said MOTHER STARLET. Metzger had brought $FIRSTNAMEF2 here to meet a woman who might know something about the play they were putting on in San Narciso. Metzger lived in a rooming house in this area, down Thrasher Way. It was his home ground. $FIRSTNAMEF2, for good reasons of her own, didn\u2019t often come to Vegas, and so knew hardly anybody. The fat woman, who had a lot of hair piled up on her head, but wasn\u2019t Starlet Empts, was doing needle...", "1_8_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-three in the morning</span>.\nI looked across at the other side of the patio. Four girls in shorts, with grain alcohol and cotton swabs, were doing what is known to American Narcotics Agents as \u201ccooking up a ball.\u201d They were seated on the floor in the sun. In front of them was a table covered with white cotton; on it were rolling papers, several packs of Drum and Mild Seven, packs of Denim and Drakkar Noir cologne, a bottle of Chivas Regal, a bottle of Martell, several large Ziploc bags, a quart of Jack Daniel\u2019s and a half-pound black lump of Afghani hash\u201443 loose, unsmoked joints.\nMy attorney stood up. \u201cI\u2019ll be right back,\u201d he said. \u201cI\u2019m going to talk to those girls across the way.\u201d He walked over there, without hitching up his Speedo, and they all looked up. They stared at him. He offered them a bottle of aspirin, but they ignored him. My attorney came back with a slightly bewildered look on his face.\n\u201cThey don\u2019t want aspirin,\u201d he said. \u201cThey just had a...", "1_4_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after four o'clock in the morning</span> when we found ourselves involved in an inexplicable traffic jam on the Polo Grounds approach to the Triborough Bridge. An accident involving fifteen cars and a cab had come up surprisingly fast. No one seemed to know what to do about it. All of the radio policemen and motorcycle cops were somewhere else. No ambulance had appeared by the time we were ten cars from the accident and people in the trapped cars were shouting for help. It seemed a typical New York fiasco. I was riding with Selena in the back seat of the station wagon when we finally had to stop. I got out, to discover that we were only a few feet away from the most horrible-looking accident victim I have ever seen. It was a legendary dock worker known as Johnny the Horse. He'd been thrown fifty feet through the air and smashed on the roof of a Pontiac. While he waited for an ambulance he was laughing, even talking. He was sitting upright, with a dozen broken bones, holding his smashed head in one hand. Some people begged him to lie down and be still, but Johnny the Horse only laughed the more. It was awful. \u201cIt doesn't hurt a bit,\u201d he kept saying. \u201cIt'll be a story to tell the boys around the locker room...", "0_8_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-eight in the morning</span>.\nThe Antichrist was pounding on the bathroom door. \u201cGoddam it!\u201d he shouted. \u201cI have to piss like a racehorse, and you\u2019re taking for ever.\u201d\n\u201cRight away!\u201d yelled Lenore. She pulled the drawer open and got out her clothes. As she dressed, her eyes fell on the letter in the wastebasket. She smiled. She couldn\u2019t help it. She got the letter, folded it, put it into the pocket of her blouse. She brushed her hair out.\n/h/\n\u201cUndocumented dullness in the yolks?\u201d\n\u201cYolks.\u201d\n\u201cSweet and sour pork\u2019s half-again-as-bad as Szechuan beef?\u201d\n\u201cShrimp \u00e0 la Mandrill?\u201d\n\u201cA concise menu, at last.\u201d\n\u201cGet the shrimp. Trust me.\u201d\n\u201cDone. Shall I lay on a steak for ourselves at home this evening?\u201d\n\u201cLet me think about that. Let me think about that.\u201d\n\u201cHow are your after...", "2_11_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven eighteen in the morning</span> in New York. The rain stopped at eleven seven. It started again at eleven seventeen. It rained forty seconds. The lightning was white. The thunder was balls of mud hitting the sides of buildings. The sky was a moist gray cloth. The buildings were silver halos floating serenely in a dirty sky. The halos were being pelted with mud. The drops of rain on the window of the penthouse apartment were fourteen times the size of the window\u2019s small panes of glass. Each raindrop was half an inch high.\nThe rain looked like white, oily worms dropping from the sky.\nThe worms made loud, wet thuds when they hit the window.\nBeeber had thrown the cover on his sun-green bed. His bed was a king-size bed. His sheets were white. So were his pillows. There was an orange robe at the foot of the bed. The robe had the words THE PINES MOTEL embroidered on it in green.\nBeeber had gone to the door of the apartment and slammed it and locked it. He had stayed in his apartment for one day and seven hours. At the end of one day and seven hours it began to rain in New York.", "0_20_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-seven in the evening</span>, and the group that had gathered around Dr. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill was as follows:\nThere was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California, the richest man in the Western Hemisphere, a man so beleaguered by rumors of misanthropic eccentricities that he was never seen in public, and could excite genuine curiosity even so. He was thin as famine, and rich tan. His eyes were china blue.\nThere was his son, $SURNAME1 senior, who had left everything that a son of a billionaire might want to take in order to claim a fortune of his own. This fortune, he was bitterly convinced, had been denied him by his father\u2019s fiscal machinations.\nIt had been denied him, he fumed\u2014and fumed when anybody else fumed about anything.\nHe was gross as gluttony, and about as genial as cyanide. His hair was electrically-bright yellow. His eyes were bottle green.\nThere was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s mistress, a twenty-four-year-old beatnik named Florence Flaherty, who had given herself so fully to protest, licentiousness, and the writing of obsceneteen-spoems that she was almost...", "2_7_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"I hope you will all stop by and see me at my place in Newport,\" said Salo.\nThe elapsed time in the trial of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was two minutes and forty-three seconds.\n\"This atmosphere is just a trifle strong, don\u2019t you think?\" said Salo.\nThe air in the court was weakly suctioned. The air outside was Earth-normal. Salo had damped out the rum leakage.\n\"I\u2019m still not used to RumFAKes atmosphere,\" said Salo. \"Thank you again for being here.\"\nThe elapsed time in the trial of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was three minutes and thirty-four seconds.\n\"Well\u2014thank you all again,\" said Salo. \"Oh\u2014I almost forgot.\"\nHe leaned over to the small radio by the bed.\n\"Skip?\" said Salo into the radio.\n\"Speaking,\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s recorded voice from the radio.\n\"Could you bring me that picture of old $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill holding up the fig leaf?\" said Salo. \"You know\u2014Churchill with his tongue hanging out and the leaf covering his privates.\"", "2_8_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-five in the morning</span>.\nBy eight-forty, all of $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s public appearances had either taken place or had been announced far in advance. By eight-forty, $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had either sped out of a Solar System, materializing in far-flung spots, or had retired to some highly-secured hideaway.\nIn all cases, the two were either dead tired or wrapped in the private woes of $SURNAME0\u2019s wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nAll of Mars was as peaceful as a seashell. It had not been so peaceful on May 9, 1986, when $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had appeared there at the end of a seven-hundred-and-twenty-nine-day walking tour.\nOn May 9, 1954, $SURNAME0 had appeared there with a dog he called $FIRSTNAMEB2, a dog that had been dead for three hundred years. The dog, in fact, had been a Time- transferrable human named $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.", "0_3_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-three in the morning</span>. For all I knew, the thing was hunting us. \u201cWhat if that car comes by again?\u201d I said. \u201cAre you going to talk to it?\u201d He groped for something to say. \u201cI\u2019d send you out there to argue with it,\u201d I said, \u201cbut I can\u2019t trust you to come back.\u201d He shook his head nervously. \u201cI\u2019m too strung out,\u201d he said. \u201cI haven\u2019t slept in four nights. I\u2019m too rotten to even consider going out there again.\u201d I thought about this. \u201cIf that happens,\u201d I said, \u201cI\u2019ll go out there alone. I\u2019ll stand in front of that car. But what I\u2019d really like to do is get the fuck out of this room...because I\u2019m start.\n\u201cE ating.\u201d a\n\u201cNo, that\u2019s finished. But I\u2019m losing too much water on the kidneys\u2014gotta get back to the room and soak in the tub.\u201d He agreed that this was a good idea, but...", "0_0_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve nineteen ante meridiem</span>. Cohen, at a random pitch, began to sing: \u201cSince Ma isn\u2019t here to give us grace/ Let me say it for her.\u201d The others joined in, harmonizing horribly. Some one \u2014 $FIRSTNAMEF2 thought it might have been Cohen\u2014 began a falsetto \u201cWe\u2019re go-ing to see Ja-cob.\u201d They all took it up. \u201cWe\u2019re go-ing to see Ja-cob, boys, we\u2019re go-ing.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEF2, not sure how the ritual went, watched Mike Fallopian and Bortz and Stanley Koteks. She noticed Fallopian was knitting his dark brows together, lip-synching it, looking like a Jew in an anti-Semitic ballad, perhaps one written during the pogroms. Rising out of his throat, still lip-synching, a deep, hollow baritone: \u201cI have had a dream, boys.\u201d She didn\u2019t know when he had learned to do that, or even if he was really singing. It was all pretty horrible. Every few moments another one would begin to sing like that, and then stop when...", "2_6_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>. Melinda was wet behind both ears and down her neck. There were five complicated red bites on each of her arms. Her hair was saturated, even in its things. There were deep Faser tracks going down the center of her back.\n\u201cI think I\u2019ve understood,\u201d said Melinda. \u201cI think I\u2019ve finished understanding.\u201d She kept her eyes on the blank TV screen.\n\u201cHow long are we to be in this room together?\u201d Lenore said behind her.\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid I don\u2019t understand.\u201d Melinda looked at the back of her own hand on the table before her. \u201cThe morning is almost done. How much time do you need?\u201d Behind her she could hear the bare foot take in its shoe.\n\u201cListen,\u201d Lenore Beadsman was saying. \u201cI\u2019m really sorry. I\u2019d be getting ready to leave right now even if I didn\u2019t have a tie on downstairs. There\u2019s this calamity.\u201d\n\u201cRick.\u201d", "2_11_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-eight in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\nAt the port of Balaklava, the cold sense of drama, isolation, and despair among the citizens of Earth was matched only by the cold sense of drama, isolation, and despair among the five thousand inhabitants of the space station known as Victor Von Doom.\nThe space station was a large wheel, 165 yards across and 175 yards in diameter. It consisted of five decks.\nIt had a grand total of fifteen rooms, only fourteen of which were ever used by the five thousand inhabitants.\nThe fifteenth room, a broom closet on the lowest deck, named Hell, was used frequently by at least two people: $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Dick.\nBalaklava was only one-fifth the length of the Ohio iron belt that had supplied it with iron ore. Balaklava, therefore, supported one-fifth the population that the iron belt had supported.\nThe iron belt, originally laid out by an engineer with a flair for drama, drama, and more drama, had originally been laid out as follows: counting both sides of the river, it had eighteen levels, one under the other. There were ten mines on the upper level, and nine mines on the lower level.", "1_9_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-seven in the morning</span> of November second, and the date was a Tuesday. The place was Indianapolis, Indiana.\nIndiana is a land of disturbing dust and of disturbing designs, of hoary and mannered ways of thought and speech, of eminently skippable weather, of restless natives and inveterate snoopers. It is a big state, a big, blurred and boorish state, a partly industrial and partly agricultural state, and it is cut in two meaningless halves by the Ohio River, which is a little river. The halves have no interest in each other. There is a bridge spanning the Ohio at Indianapolis, and one half regards the other half with nothing but scorn, and vice versa.\nIndiana has something like twenty million people, counting the inhabited portions of northern Indiana. In northern Indiana there is nothing but whirring factories and melancholy immigrants, transplanted Yankees who bring to Indiana what was there before they came. Indiana is, at best, a clumsy state. Its mountains are hills, its people, products. It is proud of being what it isn\u2019t. As an example, the City of Indianapolis prides itself on being an \u201cEast Coast Culture Center.\u201d At the same time\u2014but aren\u2019t paradoxes fun?", "0_13_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-two in the afternoon</span> on April 5, 2005, the last day of spring before the beginning of the new calendar year, when Stonecipher Beadsman, heir to an enormous fortune derived from the ownership and judicious management of aviation instrument parts and assembly companies, fell from his upper story apartment window in Commanche, Kansas, seemingly of his own accord. He died on impact, crushing his skull and severing his spine. The sound that had, at least temporarily, caused Beadsman to fall had been the telephone ringing in his downstairs apartment.\nNo one knows for what perverse reason Stonecipher Beadsman chose to live high atop the most dilapidated complex in the religious resort town of Commanche, Kansas. Attempts were made to exculpate his decision on the grounds of religion\u2014Stonecipher Beadsman having been born, after all, into the highly influential Church-of-Jesus-Christ-of-Latter-Day-Saints, of which E. Engle was an Archangel\u2014but it became quickly plain to all parties familiar with Beadsman\u2019s faith that his residence in Commanche was diametrically opposed to his teachings. An atheist, Stonecipher Beadsman nonetheless would...", "1_23_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. I was sitting in the Bois de Boulogne in a smart, open sports car that belonged to a young American friend, and I was watching the splendid soldiers, the flics, go by. They were trotting, not marching. It looked very impressive.\nAt eleven o'clock on the dot the Bois closes to traffic. The great gates swing shut. There is a minute or so of gay confusion as the carny-folk hurriedly folding up their tents and scooping put their peanuts and running away with their hot-dogs, and the grifters concentrating on wallets and purses, etc., and then the iron gates boom shut and Paris is locked into her big soft bed for the night. And what a night; and then the morning, crisp and blue and gay again; another day full of life and adventures.\nThe flics went by, swinging along. I wished I were one of them. It looked like a wonderful job and very romantic in the intimate lamplight of the Bois at night. Across the way, sitting on porch swings in their formal pajamas, were two old Parisian couples. They were watching the majestic and military flics go by, and they were smiling as...", "0_14_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-four in the afternoon</span>.\nI walked to the line of W. C. pilots and agents. I smiled nervously down the line. I was terribly nervous.\n\u201cYou fellows have a pretty hard job,\u201d I said.\nNone of the eight men even bothered to look at me. They kept their eyes on the plane.\n\u201cThat plane must be pretty heavy when it\u2019s loaded,\u201d I said.\nNone of them said anything.\n\u201cHell, yes,\u201d said a tall pilot in the second row. He was wearing a grimy leather aviator\u2019s helmet. His hair was slick with grease under the helmet.\n\u201cHas it ever busted down when it was overloaded?\u201d I asked him.\n\u201cYes,\u201d said the tall pilot. He spat viciously on the ground. \u201cIt\u2019s on the way back to the factory now.\u201d His face was blank. I knew he was lying. But I was scared; that\u2019s why I\u2019d asked him. There was no room at all on the airplane. One guy was standing in the tail, another in the cockpit. The pilot would sit on a cardboard box.\n\u201cExpensive...", "2_5_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to six o'clock in the morning</span>, fifteen minutes till sunrise. I was tired, feeling like a stupid weak victim. The shit I talked, the lies that poured out; I started feeling dizzy, as if that girl could read through the wall of the telephone and see me sitting there like a jerk with a tingling jaw and trying to explain myself and all the time wondering, am I too stoned? is this girl too far away? is it just insanity, what am I doing? This went on for several more minutes, then when I faked a yawn I realized her voice had become a recorded monotone, a bored and distant mechanical soundtrack. I had bored Lucy, she was no longer listening to me. I felt sullen and strange.\n\u201cOkay,\u201d I said finally. \u201cI'll call you tonight from California. I want to talk to you then or beforehand at least. You agree?\"\n\u201cYes,\u201d said the monotone. I could almost see the yawn racking her little body. She was probably longing for her breakfast.\n\u201cGood-by,\u201d I said.\n\u201cGood-by,\u201d said the monotone.", "2_7_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-seven in the morning</span>.\nSuddenly the dark waters in which Ricardo Carlos Liberius dripped and thrived seemed to him to be tainted with a terrible cold unfairness. These reflections were not derived from any explicit contrast in the man\u2019s mind between two worlds outside himself and himself within. They were muscular, not cerebral. The man seemed to feel coldly and unfairly ingested by a terribly unfair universe. He felt that everything that could possibly happen to him, unpleasant, humiliating, and in all other respects hateful, would happen to him.\nAnd, since neither he nor anyone else could stop this from happening, why not appease one\u2019s solipsistic hatred of other people as well as of oneself?\nRicardo Carlos Liberius was ripe for any course of action that involved or even hinted at the abolition of his own virginity. He wished to use that virginity to make somebody pay for everything.\nAs a result of the hostile reflection in the waterfall that had made his sunglasses blind, he came near to blundering through a force-field that would have robbed him not only of his virginity but also of his life. He was stopped just in time. It was a close shave.", "0_11_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had found out at the space training school that the most exciting thing for him to do at that time of day was to sit on an old box outside barrack 12, watch the church steeple directly to the west, and count the seconds.\nExcept for $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the cleaning crew, the barrack room was empty. Everybody else was at the big church luncheon inside the church. The church was only a hundred yards away. But a chain-link fence kept people out of the cemetery. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the crew kept to their cleaning because they didn\u2019t have clothes for the lunch.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 got out his old pocket watch, wound it. He put the watch to his ear and made believe it had stopped. He pretended that he was standing at attention in front of Sergeant $SURNAME2, and his old watch was exactly on time. \"Well, sir, it\u2019s twelve o\u2019clock, almost,\" he said to himself, \"and here I am, standing at attention in front of you, sir.\" He would grin happily at the memories this revival of an old routine awakened.\nThe aimless, happy memories helped him to count the seconds more easily.\nNone of the things he had once imagined Br...", "0_22_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty in the evening</span>. Remi Boncoeur met us in the lobby of the Hesperides and drove us in his big Peugeot sedan to La Piscine de Saint-Tropez. His old man, Count Boncoeur, had given him a couple of thousand francs, and he was all decked out in veined summer silk sports clothes\u2014high-button, polo-neck shirt, and all. \u201cWe must find a good brothel,\u201d he said. We drove along under tall, dark trees. Music came from the far end of the Boulevard des Lanternes. \u201cHere,\u201d said Remi. We parked the car down the street and walked back to see find a big, open-air dance floor in the pleasant, provincial darkness, an orchestra was playing \u201cAvec le Bec en l\u2019Air.\u201d \u201cWhere\u2019s the girls?\u201d I called to Remi. \u201cI don\u2019t know.\u201d We paid and went on, it was a bioscope, with cartoons, newsreels, and an old Erich von Stroheim film with Blondin, and some French actress who was a cousin of Jean Gabin\u2019s. Finally Rem...", "2_0_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-three ante meridiem</span>. Time to move the brute. I got out of the car. My attorney was standing in the gutter, bent over with laughter.\n\u201cWhat the hell are you doing?\u201d he gasped.\n\u201cMoving the car,\u201d I said. \u201cGet back in and wait.\u201d I moved several tall gray vehicles before I found an opening on the off ramp\u2014clear of everything except a white, unmarked cop\u2014car. To get there I had to move about ten feet into a no\u2014man\u2019s\u2014land where I was absolutely exposed to the gaze of the approaching Harley\u2014Davidsons.", "2_8_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"I know there\u2019s room,\" the man said to Aggie, \"and I know there\u2019s something. Who\u2019s going to take care of my Tash while I\u2019m away? I mean, there\u2019s got to be somebody to take care of Tash.\" He looked at his wristwatch. \"Oh, God,\" he said, \"oh, God, oh, God. Where the hell is everybody?\"\nTwo gentlemen approached the man at the corner of the news-stand, one from either side. One of the gentlemen was chewing gum. He was tall and wore a vest and had a brush mustache. The other gentleman was small and wore glasses, and he had no mustache.\n\"Morning, Freddy,\" said the man at the corner of the news-stand.\n\"Morning, Walter,\" said Freddy.\n\"Say, want to sell me the place?\" Walter said. \"I will buy it cheap.\"\n\"You\u2019ll have to talk to my father,\" said Freddy.\n\"Tell your father to keep the place up,\" said Walter. \"If it weren\u2019t for people like you and me, the place would be torn down in no time.\"", "2_8_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-four in the morning</span>.\nThere was lightning outside, and a palace window dilated. In the lightning was the black silhouette of a man nicknamed Space Wanderer.\n$SURNAME0 looked at his watch. \"Almost on time,\" he said.\n\"Some message he\u2019s got for you,\" said Britt. Over his fetid counterpanes, he rolled his eyes like billiard balls. \"Boy\u2014the things people want to say to somebody who\u2019s got as much trouble as you do.\"\n\"Write his number on the news, too,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I want to send him a thank-you note.\" He looked around the palace. \"Are all of this magazine\u2019s readers as punctual as our friend out there?\"\n\"All of them,\" said Britt with awe. \"They never miss a day.\"", "1_6_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty in the morning</span>. The place was the Reflecting Pool, right next to the Washington Monument in Washington, D.C. The crowd consisted of forty-five drunks and everybody who loved them.\nTheir spokesman was a punk-rock fan, about nineteen years of age, called Maggot Man. He was a rake-thin kid, with dishwater-gray eyes and a mouthful of filthy broken teeth. He wore a greasy sweatshirt from some university in Ohio. The shirt was printed with the words OHIO WLIERS, and it was filthy.\nMaggot Man was on his feet. \u201cHey what\u2019s the idea of wakin Herb?\" he yelled. \u201cThis here\u2019s s sleeping party, you dig? We want to sleep, you dig?\"\n\" This is a citizen\u2019s arrest,\u201d said a First District cop. He was a big officer, about thirty years old. He had a crewcut and soft green eyes. He was big, but not big enough to arrest forty-five drunks by himself. He grabbed a big, scrawny girl with stringy hair. \u201cYou\u2019re under arrest, sister,\" he said. \"\n\u201cWhat for, what for...", "2_18_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nNo one had ever seen anything like the display in the Martian sky.\nCertainly, the Martian language had never had a word that described such a display. It would have taken a Martian fifty-seven years to say what it looked like. And then he would have died.\nThe Ann zero and $FIRSTNAMEF1 zero were having their dinner in the Martian kitchen. They were having canned asparagus surprise with Slim-line Wafers and Martian chocolate milk.\nThe next day was going to be an Earthling school day. The girls intended to have a very early dinner, watch monster movies on television, and be in bed by 9:00 PM.\nBefore dinner, $FIRSTNAMEB1 dug a well in a large bare closet under the stairs. There he put a stone trough, in which he put three gallons of water and plenty of food for the goldfish. The goldfish were put in the trough, and the well was covered with a large slab of pink marble.\n\"Now,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"the little darlings will be all right. Water, food and shelter\u2014everything they need.\"", "1_19_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-eight in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nThe hot part of the day was behind.\nGoodwin and Company came out of their chairs as much as a foot and a half, when they heard the knocking at Room 223 of the Hotel Gloriana.\nIt was almost a vertical climb for them to the door button to admit Room Service.\nThey relaxed slowly and slowly, using their trick of synchronizing their breathing.\nThe trolleys containing their order from Giovanni\u2019s Tarzan Pizza Palace had, besides the proper amounts of Pepperoni Lovers\u2019 Deep, Cheese Lovers\u2019 Deep, Spinach Supreme, Meat Lovers\u2019 Deep, Double Pepperoni, and Italian sausage, also brought with them other things, including a 6-pack of Hamm\u2019s, a football-size bottle of Peppermint Schnapps, two pounds of grass of excellent grade, two bags of hard-caramel doughnuts, a copy of Life magazine, and a copy of the July 23, 1969, New York Times.\nAnd, under all this, and part of it, was a box from W.A.C.O. an Owl Carpet Cleaning Company, of Babylon, Long Island, New York.\nGoodwin and Stone opened first...", "1_4_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until five o'clock in the morning</span>. The space was one thousand feet over the dark side of Earth. The ship was a tiny point of light whirling over the vast and brooding profundity of the Earth's pit.\nMany things were wrong with the ship.\nThe name of the ship was the $SURNAME0, and nothing so sailed, so surfed, so deliquesced as the $SURNAME0.\nWere it not for its perpetual dangerous wavering over the dark and brooding Earth, the $SURNAME0 would have been an ugly and trifling thing.\nThe $SURNAME0 was, technically, a saucer. It had its centralachron, or living quarters, its radar room, its fuel-supply compartments, and so on. These appurtenances lay in the rim of the saucer.\nThe saucer was two feet thick. It was a sandwich of eighteen layers of one-inch urethane foam, six layers of stainless steel, seven-sixteenths of an inch thick, and fifteen layers of one-quarter inch urethane foam. Its thickness gave it an insulating capacity and durability seldom if ever achieved in disposable flying saucers.\nIts capacity for durability and for safely storing and dispatching useful quantities of...", "0_9_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-eight in the morning</span> of the day after $SURNAME1 had sold himself to $SURNAME0. $SURNAME1 was twenty-eight years old.\nHe was looking up at the immense blue balloon through which he had first seen the inside of the tender.\n$SURNAME1 had sent the old woman to look up at the balloon. She was examining it tremulously through her gold-rimmed spectacles.\n\"Do you like it, ma\u2019am?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"What is it?\" said the old woman.\n\"A big balloon,\" said $SURNAME1. \"That\u2019s all it is.\"\n\"Is the balloon supposed to go up, like you said the ship was supposed to?\" said the old woman. \"Is the there supposed to be precious guided tour into space?\"\n\"No, ma\u2019am,\" said $SURNAME1. \"There isn\u2019t supposed to be anything.\"\nIt was $SURNAME1\u2019s secret conviction that the Universe was an instrument for grinding the human spirit down to a nub. He believed in the law of conservation of spirit, which stated that whatever spirit you lost was turned into yellow grease, most of which went into the making of college presidents, foundation trustees, tax commissioners, and retired generals. He fervently wished that the Universe would follow...", "2_15_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-two in the afternoon</span>. We ran behind a bar where a harried bartender was serving Rainier beer on tap, and we got two beers before the girl pulled loose and walked off, heading casually past the rows of sad gypsy trailers and into the stormy afternoon. I gave chase and caught her by the arm by the second row of trailers. She groaned when I grabbed her, but she stopped.\n\u201cWhere are we going?\u201d she said.\n\u201cWhere do you think?\u201d I said. \u201cInto the bushes. Aren\u2019t you ready?\"\n\u201cYes,\u201d she said. \u201cBut not here. They\u2019ll see us.\u201d Finally, at three forty-five, we found a broken\u2014down red\u2014and\u2014gray trailer that looked like an old shack about five hundred yards off the crossroads at Bethel, straight down a muddy path through the woods, and nobody saw us go in there.", "2_1_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-four in the morning</span>\u2014fourteen minutes after the zenith had become an upside-down horizon in Newport, Rhode Island, U.S.A.\nA message in a brown wrapper arrived for $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nHe was sitting on a bench in the dockyard of Titan, whittling a pebble into the shape of a fish. He was planning to give the pebble to $SURNAME0, who, according to his understanding, would materialize in Titan about one hundred thousand years from that date.\n$SURNAME0 already had two million two hundred and fifty thousand fish-shaped pebbles.\n$SURNAME0 owned $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s Portsmouth, his corncob pipe, and his soul, too.\n$SURNAME0 owned forty-eight state capitals, too, and was welcome to them.\nThe message in the brown wrapper now materialized around $FIRSTNAMEB2. It turned out to be friendly and yellow.\nIt was scented. It had a wonderful, if mysterious, smell. Treating the mysterious smell as a color, I shall call it khaki.\nThe message in the khaki-colored wrapper said, \"Go to $SURNAME0, the man in the eagle suit, at the fountain in the square.\"", "1_9_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nTHE DARKNESS-DESCENDING THE SECOND SUITCASE\u2014 TURNING ON ALL THE LIGHTS\u2014PULLING THE CURTAINS\u2014THE DESK\u2014THE AFFAIR OF THE CHILEAN PEPPER SHAKERS\u2014 SECURING THE CONTENTS OF THE THIRD SUITCASE ON THE BED\u2014RICOCHET THROUGH TIME IN A WHIRL OF PINK CHAMPAGNE\nTIME TRAVEL PIONEER SHUMBRIL two of his children, and Mrs. $SURNAME0, his mother-in-law, were somewhere beneath the Taj Mahal in India.\nThe year was 1939 A.D., a year so far advanced in violence as to make everything that happened afterward appear almost genteel.\nThe thing that was happening now was taking place in Algeria, where Shumbril, his children and his mother-in-law had been dropped for no reason that any of them could understand. Their ship had been caught, as so many ships were being caught, in the upper atmosphere and carried some twenty-seven miles north of its intended point of landing.\nThey had crashed without warning in the desert nine miles from the...", "1_13_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>; Earth time. The place was the absolute uttermost rim of the universe. $FIRSTNAMEB0, in his attempt to escape the pain in his back, had been hurled so far toward the darkling forest that there was no longer any light at all\u2014only a chilling dampness.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had touched nothing edible, touched nothing drinkable. He had not touched a thing that wasn\u2019t lethal.\n\"God damn it,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"this don\u2019t have to be real!\"\nHis body rebelled against any further search for food\u2014rebelled by becoming gastly. It demanded a rest period of at least three hours before further search for food would be fair to the system.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 let the system rest. He squatted against a tree, pressed his hands over his eyes, imagined sunshine, blue skies, girls in white.\nSuppose, now, that $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s whole glorious mission in space had been planned and conducted simply so that $FIRSTNAMEB0 could instruct a machine in pain, a machine that wanted to experience all there was to experience. Then $FIRSTNAMEB0 might take grim satisfaction in imagining the response of the instructors back on Mars when the pain machine reported in.\nThe instructors would be in a quand...", "0_10_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-one in the morning</span> and the space was the dentist\u2019s office, where Bortz was ostensibly seeing a dental hygienist, one Michelle Babcock, though it was really Lenore who did seeing, as far as Bortz was concerned.\nLenore was weighting her own copy of The Well of Loneliness on a metal drawer labeled \u201cRots of jaws, if not properly maintained,\u201d when the outer door to the waiting room of the suite in Shaker Heights that the Krishner Clinic shared with an Accordion Club and a health food store opened up and thudded to, and Melindainda Mason, Lenore\u2019s sister, came in.\n\u201cWhew,\u201d said Melinda. \u201cWhat a day.\u201d She threw her attach\u00e9 case down on a wicker chair and shucked off her trenchcoat. \u201cI\u2019ve decided I must loathe babies.\u201d\nLenore had a jewel-like tingle in her middle, just about then. Bortz was running a finger around inside his mouth. Lenore smiled at her sister. Bortz looked up from his finger.\n\u201cHi, Andy,\u201d Melinda said at last.", "1_6_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-nine in the morning</span>. The place was a small amphitheatre of crumbling concrete and weed, to the north and east of the city. $SURNAME1 found a place to slouch behind a drippy ficus tree and watch, and be reasonably sure nobody would approach to ask about his business there.\nThere, $SURNAME1 set himself to fit the pieces together, the whole mockery together.\nThere was much more than an empty coffin buried under Flagstad\u2019s Tomb.\nHell was empty, but $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 did not know it.\nA serpent with a broken back crawled lizardlike along the cracked coping of the amphitheatre.\n$SURNAME1 watched the sun climb behind the Guggenheim Museum, coloring the great sail.\nIf only, thought $SURNAME1, things could have been made to function that way. If only we could have been given our instructions, then joined with others to do the things that had to be done, and then accepted with sympathy, so we wouldn\u2019t have to be deranged.\nDamn $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, thought $SURNAME1. Damn his eyes for a Peeping Tom. Damn his heart for a vulture.\nIt was hell to be a rational man in an irrational world. It was a world not of Thomson\u2019s hobgoblins...", "1_10_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-three in the morning</span>, according to field glasses held by binocular-toting members of the As of Yore Society, an international organization of stamp and coin and antique-bottle collectors headquartered in Des Moines, Iowa.\nThe time was deciphered from a minute black spot against a white background high in the Martian sky, the lemon sun shining past the black spot from the observer\u2019s left.\nThe time was confirmed by Mrs. Opal Pharris Nirdlinger of East Carthage, New York, when she identified the image of a horse-drawn fire engine that suddenly flashed below the black spot.\nThere had also been a miraculous appearance, four minutes later, of two figures on the roof of a building far below the observers. They were looking directly at the beaming device.\nWhat a wonder Mars was, what a place to speculate about.\n\"Oh, honey, they done it again,\" said Mrs. Nirdlinger.\n\"Ain\u2019t that a shame?\" said another mother, staring into the binoculars. \"Bigger than last time, too.\"\n\"I believe my baby would have wanted to get even bigger.\" said Mrs. Nirdlinger. \"I really do. I reckon jokes about his salami just...", "2_10_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich mean time.\n\"Good morning, Space Wanderer,\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice suddenly. \"I am speaking to you from a tape recorder here in the base of the Tower of Julip. Skip up the ladder to me. You will learn something of value on each level.\"\nBefore the Space Wanderer could press a single button or throw a single switch, $SURNAME0\u2019s voice again filled the space ship. \"I deeply regret,\" said $SURNAME0, \"that it was necessary for me to deceive you about my true form and history. Part of my employ at the moment is to mislead my many bumbling enemies. If I had told you I was $SURNAME0, you could have written nothing but a series of eyewitness accounts of my evils and vices.\n\"I dare say you are disappointed in me,\" said $SURNAME0 from his tape recorder. \"But you have it in your power to make me the most glorious and marvelous thing you can imagine. All you have to do is promote me as you go up the ladder.\"\nTwo memphones beside the pilot\u2019s chair burst into song.\nOne of them was blinking.", "1_23_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until midnight</span>.\nOn the floor were two shopping bags. One contained six bottles of California brandy. They had cost thirty-two dollars. The other bag contained six bottles of Martell brandy. They had cost sixty-six dollars and twenty-five cents.\nThe trunk of the car contained four cases of Martell brandy that had cost eighty-seven dollars and thirty cents a bottle.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 Bonisteel, the pilot\u2019s mother, and a lady with whom she had cello lessons, owned the only liquor store in $SURNAME0\u2019s neighborhood. Bonisteel sold nothing but imported brandy at outrageous prices.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 Bonisteel was a widow. She got her friendship with her cello teacher by sending her son to the teacher\u2019s husband for pilot training. She was mother, father, and the two children to her husband, $SURNAME1 Bonisteel, a classmate of $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0\u2019s at Stanford.\n$SURNAME1 Bonisteel, like every other man in the neighborhood, kept his airplane in the back yard. He was trained to take off on warning and fly straight for the shelter at Mount Florida. If a Martian destroyed Mount Florida, he was supposed to fly to the caves of Novaya Z...", "0_16_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-six in the afternoon</span>, a half-hour before they all went into chrono-synclastic infundibula, when she entered Room 223 to wet a line for Myles. Since it was raining outside, and all the men were gone, and since the mirror was temporarily gone also, she had time to occupy herself however she pleased, without fear or favor. She was free, for a moment, to be.\nTheir breath had been fogging the mirror, so $FIRSTNAMEF2 stood in front of the desk fan, pointed at her mouth, to clear the mirror.\nBefore the mist had quite gone away, the face of Narcissus began to emerge, staring as grandly as ever, having nothing to do with how she looked. Staring at nothing, seeing nothing real. $FIRSTNAMEF2 pressed her breasts against the cold screen and leaned with her face as close as possible, it took almost no effort to imagine she could breathe in the fading Na Narcissus and make it a part of herself. She seemed then to exist like this forever, quiet, nearly motionless, staring thus into a reflection that gazed permanently, like all reflections, no matter what the eyes saw, nowhere, into the void behind her eyes. Which were what? The eyes reflected...", "1_14_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. I went over to the News Agency and read the cables. There was a long story about the California elections. The D.A. had gotten elected. So had the mean kid. Sheriffs, clerks, highway patrols\u2014all the police offices had gone Republican. I looked through the cables again. There was a story from Malmo, Sweden. It said a big offshore oilfield had been discovered west of the island of Sylt there. This meant that the Germans would now be able to reach the North Sea oil by an inland route. Of course they already had the synthetic oil plants going and there was hardly a drop of natural oil left anywhere in Germany. England was getting the real stuff from the Middle East and Canada and the United States. We were getting it from the same place the Germans were. It was interesting that the Germans had gone back to the Sylt field. They had tried to develop it in 1942-3, but apparently nothing much came out of it. Probably there was a flurry of excitement when the big newfield near Krebsweihe was found, and they thought that was the future. Now they were going back to this other place on shore.\nI walked up to the bank. It had been raining during the night. The gut...", "2_17_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-two in the afternoon</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nAt six o\u2019clock that night, Rigel was to set at Newport.\nAt eight o\u2019clock that night, the eight Moon rocket was to take off from Earth and seek out forever the broken pieces of the faithful Trigger.\nAt one minute after eight, $FIRSTNAMEB1 and da Cunha were to discover Mercury, $FIRSTNAMEB2, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 in their $FIRSTNAMEM2 shelters in the grotto.\nAt six-forty that night, the American space-ship Leonardo was to take off from Earth with a full load of religion, bound for Mars.\nAt ten-forty that night, the British space-ship $FIRSTNAMEF1 was to take off from Earth, bound for the snows of Titan, Saturn\u2019s largest moon.\nAt ten thirty-two that night, Pope in His Holiness was to broadcast by television to all nations of Earth a plea for universal peace.\nAt eight thousand eight hundred and thirty seconds after ten thirty-two that night, $FIRSTNAMEB1 and da Cunha were to invite $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 to meet them in the space-ship yard, so that they could all three escape the mass hypnosis about to descend on Newport.", "0_23_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to midnight</span>. The parlor clocks chimed as the assembled company rose to their feet with one accord. Camouflaged like a pearl in the throat of the room and somehow among them, although it was as though she were already walking up the aisle of St. Patrick\u2019s and they, in gowns and uniforms, were bringing up the rear and turning, in formation, to watch her, Rose stood with her hands in her lap. Carol wore blue gabardine and a mink stole and looked, $FIRSTNAMEF2 thought, like a movie star\u2019s blonde, well-groomed mother. Beside her, in British national costume, blazer with brass buttons, grey flannels, Ban-days, John L. Lewisstyle crewcut, Arnold Substance, WASP son-in-law of WASP son-in-law of WASP, Stuart, grinned amiably at his shoes. To them presently, as the time to kiss the bride really neared, Arnold brought a picnic basket out from behind the davenport. It contained half a bottle of Greek wine.\nThen, in the 11:59 p.m., there was a marching in of additional security, male and female, government issue, and it should have...", "0_2_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"This is terribly funny,\" said Judith Prietht.\n\"You said that before,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. She was at the end of her endurance. She could not play this game of attack and parry one moment longer. \"You know that little boy there\u2014\" $FIRSTNAMEF1 pointed at little $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 up on the wall\u2014\"that little boy was my husband. His name was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\"\n\"Oh\u2014really?\" said Judith Prietht.\n\"My husband was a colleague of your husband\u2019s,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"I\u2019m not saying that to make myself interesting. It\u2019s a true fact. You might find it interesting, whether you think you want to or not.\"\n\"That\u2019s a picture of my husband on the stand there,\" said Judith Prietht.\n\"So I see,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"A dog-gone swell guy, isn\u2019t he?\" said Judith Prietht. \"My husband\u2019s a dog-gone swell guy!\"\n\"My husband\u2014\" began $FIRSTNAMEF1, and stopped. She had almost said, \"was a dog-gone swell guy,\" too.\n\"My husband woke me up twenty-seven times...", "1_7_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven eighteen in the morning</span>.\nThere were now thirty-two Army helicopters positioned around the little house, in a circle eight helicopters in diameter.\nConrad, emerging from the cellar just after seven, had found himself surrounded by a dozen menacing choppers.\nIn every quarter of the circle other choppers were arriving. One had just maneuvered over the countryside north of Stonecipheco, bringing Vivian Phranc.\nTentacles of smoke were reaching out from the house, into the multi-colored dawn. Under some jets the fire showed like orange drapes, while other jets made it look more like green fizz. The combined light gave it a weirdly patterned look, like the pelt of some animal: a fabulous beast from beyond the stars.\nOdd in the very same way was the impression of a humming sound that was imparted to the atmosphere of the early morning. This sound, too, was multi-colored. In places it was white, in others red, in others blue-white, in others teal.\nIt was caused by the extremely high frequencies of helicopters eighty blades a second inside their networks of spinning rotors. This accounted for the chromatism. The noise of tens of thousands of horses that was given off by...", "2_6_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-three in the morning</span>.\nBig Jew whispered a catechism to explain.\n\"Me liking be,\" he said.\nHe whispered it again.\n\"Me liking be me liking,\" he whispered. \"You like be you liking.\"\nHe whispered it again. \"You like be you liking,\" he said, and nodded.\nA light seemed to dawn in $FIRSTNAMEB0. A man doesn\u2019t often get a chance to understand what $FIRSTNAMEB0 understood then. The big point was that he didn\u2019t have to die to be dead. And the little point, the bright little pellet that complexioned the big point, was that he didn\u2019t have to kill Big Jew to be a murderer.\nThe three beautiful, deadly young men had come to attention and were stripping their rifles.\nFrom good hiding places along the road, four stubby young insurrectionists materialized, snatched at four rifles. In the same flashing instant, the four young soldiers lay dead, flat on their backs with holes to match those in their shirts.\nThe soldiers had been killed by stun-gun globes fired by the young insurrectionists.\nThe dead soldiers were reloaded on their carts and rolled away down the yellow brick road by the young insurrectionists.", "0_7_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-two in the morning</span>. I dropped the poor bastard in the midst of the Nevada desert and I high-tailed it back to L.A. with the mescaline buzzing in my brain. Here I was, back on the poison again after eleven months of rehabilitation. I had promised to have nothing further to do with mescaline, much less write about the experience. What insane forces drove me back to this? Why not stay on beer, where I belonged?\nI needed sleep. I had to get out of this noise and madness that I carried around inside my head. I could feel the fear/pig closing in on all sides as I sped westward into the white dawn. What to do? What to say? How to respond? Who to blame? Canisters ahead, reef to port.\nChapter Eleven\nMy head was exploding. I drove straight to the Garden of Allah, took a big purple suite on the south end of the courtyard, and stretched out on the bed to think a while, rest and arrange my bones. There was a fly in the room. I was too tired to get up and kill it. This fly was my friend. I watched it walk around and knew that it was my responsibility to see that it didn't leave the room. The...", "1_15_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-three in the afternoon</span>, October 1, and crowded like the narrow sidewalk at that hour, though the light was yellow instead of white. But the sounds were the same. Two superannuated hoodlums in bowlers were standing on the corner of Sixth near the house of the saints, drunk as lords. From Forty-third Street and Sixth Avenue came a yell of joy as somebody sliding down a pole in the street car on the tracks hit his head against the rear window. Everybody by that point knew somebody. A lady blew a bugle. An old woman shouted something cheerful at the Oldsmobile that came lurching around the corner of Eighth Street with a sound of a million typewriters. \u201cThere is that r-r-racket again,\u201d said my old friend Mike in the back seat. The fat man in the sweatshirt and the short stage driver could not go on sleeping. The actor and the taxi driver in the front seat of the taxi woke up and looked around with rancid faces. \u201cWhat's the name of this town?\u201d said the actor. \u201cDogtrot, Arkansas?\u201d Nobody knew. There were long fingers of gas-proof struggling for light on the neon signs above us. Across the street I recognized...", "0_22_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-four in the evening</span> and we hadn't had anything to eat since morning...but it was a comfortable jail and the food was brought to us. Remi slept heavily for about an hour; finally I had to shake him to waken him for his meal. He took it on the floor from a bent tray.\n'What'll we do, Remi?' I asked.\n'Start another chapter,' he said, his mouth full.\nI lit a cigarette and began reading about the genesis of the theory of evolution in Darwin's Voyage of the Beagle. Remi couldn't follow such dry reading. He tuned in on a murder mystery in a radio serial and listened to that with one ear and finished his meal with the other. I went out to the front desk and telephoned every friend I had in the United States; they all said they\u2019d come up with the bail money in New York. Remi came out and we walked around the jail. He had some complicated plans for buying a car and driving back to Denver with me at the wheel.\n'You'd never make it,' I told him. 'I'd drive us into a tree.'\n'I know you too well,' he said. 'You'd just zoom off the moment I nodded...", "2_14_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThere was a knock on the door.\n\"Come in,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe door opened, and a barrack mate stood there, holding something behind his back. He went over to $FIRSTNAMEB0, shot an imaginary free throw, made an imaginary basket, slapped his knee, and said, \"I just wanted to congratulate you, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" His manner was that of an unsuccessful sharper who was making an attempt at being a good sport. \"I wanted to congratulate you on how well you shot in combat today.\"\n\"Thanks,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"You know-\u2014to tell you the truth\u2014I was disappointed in you, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014,\" said the mate. \"I had the opinion you were going to shoot more against that German\u2014lot of Germans.\"\n\"There wasn\u2019t room to shoot much,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Well,\" said the mate, \"still and all\u2014\"", "0_22_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten eighteen in the evening</span>. I went to a bar in Coconut Grove and had a few double Scotches and counted my money. It was not too bad. I had four hundred dollars in my wallet. I went to bed early and slept late. In the morning I borrowed a pair of overalls from the disgusted yard-man and humped a garbage can around the back yard of the boardinghouse during the hours when everybody was out. Then I lunched luxuriously at the Smoker, and had a great nap. By nightfall Sunday I was feeling pretty good. I had been drunk many times, but I was never hung over. I was unstrung, but I liked myself. I knew I was going to roll into some kind of adventure that night, and I was well rested for it. I had a sub-deb in for tea, but she wailed away for only a few minutes before she sobbed that she had to go home to Virginia. She up and left before I could even uncross my legs. Pretty quick one of the Miami kids dropped in. We immediately got drunk. I started to tell him what a revolution was, but he absolutely refused to listen to any political ideas whatever. He wanted to talk about women. So we talked about women. Miami...", "1_22_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span> by Mr. Bank's waterproof Elgin watch, as he patrolled close in shore, touching his cap to a magnificent horn moth that circled slowly down out of the black sky. The watch was in a little box that could be worn fastened to a waistcoat.\nIt was a present from Mrs. Bank on Mr. Bank's last birthday. It had cost twenty-eight dollars, Mrs. Bank said proudly.\nIt wasn't that Mrs. Bank was stingy. She just lacked imagination. If she could have spent one hundred and fifty dollars for that watch\u2014well, she could have seen in her mind what it would look like, and what everybody would say about it.\nBut twenty-eight dollars was so pitifully short of one hundred and fifty.\n\"I think it's lovely,\" she had said, \"and of course it was a bargain. But it just doesn't do you justice, dear. I always think of a blue water-proof watch with a gold chain for a man\u2014dressed up.\"\nSo the watch dangled on a fine gold chain from Mr. Bank's pocket. And Mrs. Bank's comment was always that it seemed rather heavy and bulky for a watch. She was sorry.\n\"If it keeps...", "1_0_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-three ante meridiem</span>. Salo came across a worktable that was littered with message blanks. He used a sticky roller to put letters on the pieces of paper. The messages, in five different scripts, said the same thing:\nAll around Salo, hidden sensors were tinkling mournfully.\nThe Titanic moaned.\nSalo tiptoed out of the cabin and closed the door softly. He locked the door with a beefy hand, trying to be as quiet as possible.\nIn the cabin, $FIRSTNAMEB2, in his crib, kicked aside a teddy bear and a rubber bone, made from the elastic of Mama\u2019s girdle, and sat up in his crib.\nOutside the cabin, Salo tiptoed down a staircase that smelled of yams, Iowa yams, the yams of The Byte of Rye. He negotiated an insanely complicated stair-switch, reset it, and tiptoed away.\nAll around him tingled and chimed, whimpered and wailed, whispered and howled all of the Titanic\u2019s sensors, responding to vibrations caused by the lightest effort of Salo\u2019s footsteps. Every step challenged the ingenuity, the cunning, the patience, and the unthink...", "2_12_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-seven p.m.</span>\nAs $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were about to mount their rockets for the return to the camp, a second order came through from Lieutenant Brackett.\nThe second order was to kill the remaining harmless and beneficial animals, collect the roebucks\u2019 balls, carry the balls with them on their return.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 reached the camp, they were told to report to the door of the pink cabin. \"Visitors!\" said Private Wiley Greentree. He made a wide, flattering parody of a smile. His actual smile, being limited to his naked face, was pinched and ferocious.\nHe was anxious that his visitors not be disappointed in him.\nUpon close observation, visitors to the camp were apt to be disappointed in Wiley Greentree, because he was inept at marching, incompetent at drill, bone-cracking clumsy on the firing range, and shiftless when called on to drain the marrow of life in guard duty.\nThere were hints that Wiley had been sold to the Army by some very unscrupulous people.", "2_1_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMcRevem turned his head and looked at the clock on the wall to his left.\nWhen he looked back in the direction he had been looking before his head had moved, he shuddered.\nMcRevem looked again at the clock.\nThe clock said 12:01.\nMcRevem looked at his watch.\nHis watch, being a chronometer, had gained. It now said 1:09.\nThe clock had gained, too. It now said 2:14.\nThe time was 2:14.\nMcRevem did not have his watch set to Warsaw time. He had kept it set to his office watch, which read one hour fast.\nMcRevem checked his office watch. Only an hour fast. McRevem had goofed in his setting back one hour.\nThe time was 1:15.\nMcRevem spilled some whiskey into his right hand and flicked it into his left.\nHe took another drink, soon.\nHe picked up the April/May/June issue of Confidential, dug his thumbnail under a corner, and opened the issue like a book. He held it vertically, the corner down.", "2_7_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-eight in the morning</span>. A bee stung $FIRSTNAMEF1 at 7:30 on a morning in September.\nIt stung her in the space between the thumb and index finger of her right hand. The bee in stinging her administered the venom not only to her but also to its own body. The bee left its body in the form of venom in $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s hand. This proved fatal to the bee.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 looked at the space between her thumb and index finger. She pulled a handkerchief from a pocket in her green Army overcoat and dabbed at the space. Her face showed no sign of pain or surprise. You never saw a face show a sign of pain or surprise when a bee stung somebody.\nNineteen\nSunlight lay fields and strokes of gold across the oval mirror of a pool. Water percolated through centerless ferns in its bed. Early dragonflies tested the pond for cleanliness and strength. They found it good.", "0_5_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-eight in the morning</span>. I remember this date, because in the war I had been trained to keep my watch accurate. Chaplain Dunbar tells me the date is wrong in this book. He says I wrote the book after an acid trip. That was long after the drinking tape.\nI hadn\u2019t thought of Herb Otis in three years when suddenly, riding into Vegas on the Greyhound, I saw him getting into a white Cadillac with one of these big eye-glasses over his nose. Please note the description very carefully, for this man is the most dangerous criminal in the whole world. He is forty-six years old, fifteen feet tall, fat, and has one eye. His voice sounds like John Wayne. He wears white suits, a Panama hat and sunglasses. He is holding up the economy of the United states, for two reasons: First, because he has absolutely no fear; he is also the gunman who shot Jesse James. Which proves my theory that anybody can be President of the United States if he is absolutely, completely and totally fearless; and second, because he has more money than any other human being, which is a crime against society, because money belongs in the hands of the common people\u2014not in the hands of big rich men like the Duke...", "2_14_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two sixteen in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME1 had been up and out of his apartment three times in the last hour. He had gone to the bank once and to the shipyards once and to his office once. He was supposed to have been at all three places.\nThe first and third trips had been to pay off three bartenders and a cleaning woman who had participated in the big Earth-to-Mars false message that had started all the trouble.\nThe second trip had been to see a bartender who had not had anything to do with the fraud\u2014a bartender who had a very embarrassing problem that had nothing to do with sex. Everything about the man was embarrassing. The problem was this: He tended to get drunk at irregular but very alarming hours, and when he got drunk he invariably began to quarrel with his decor. He had spent four hundred dollars repairing his bar in one ten-day period, had bought mostly expensive items\u2014an ebony cigarette box, an Oriental pink-pearl cigarette lighter, an Italian bar glass.\nThe man refused to believe that these items were not his.", "2_18_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six nineteen in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nTHE POPULATION OF MARS HAD BEEN DECIMATED WHEN ITS COPILOTS HAD LOST THEIR HEADS\nWHEN HE HEARD THE SCREAM, IT wasn\u2019T INSULT TO HIS UNDERSTANDING. It was just a scream.\nHe looked up, wildly.\nA vast Martian with a green face was rushing at him through the air. Another Martian, with a yellow face, was racing at him through the air. The Martians were swinging wrecking balls as they flew.\nSomebody was screaming: \"Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him! Get him!\"\nThis person who was screaming began to brush distracting flecks of Martian flesh from before his eyes. He saw that the vast Martians, with the green and yellow faces, were very strong. They could tear a castle down like so much tinfoil. He saw, too, that there were thousands of them streaming through the air toward him.", "1_3_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. The man and the woman on the bench were alarmed by so much alarm. The alarm belonged to an alarm system for which neither of them was responsible.\n\"It came from the ocean,\" said the man defensively, with his finger held in the air. His diffident and lumbering manner might have been designed to repel an aggressor.\nUnmollified, the woman asked, \"Why would it come from the ocean?\"\nHis reply seemed to him to be a model of common sense. \"Because the ocean is the only thing around here anywhere near us,\" he said, with a puzzled frown.\nThe woman left him\u2014left the man, that is, sitting on the bench. She accepted, with the resignation of a fairly good sailor, the necessity of hauling taut the line, often, in order to hold her position on an open sea. She remembered, in the matter of anchors, that they must be weighed. She had bought an anchor once, in a rash moment, when she and her husband had had a little money. But then she and her husband had lived together.\n\"That\u2019s right, weigh anchor,\" she murmured, thinking that she had discovered a clich\u00b4e for their situation in life.", "0_6_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past six o'clock in the morning</span>. The light was growing quickly in the east. I made myself coffee and drank it standing above the kitchen stove. It was white and heavy and tasted good. I was all right again. I was already asleep when they came up the stairs, laughing and talking in low voices, and opened the door of the flat. It was just as if I had done something criminal and was afraid they would hear me.\nI heard $FIRSTNAMEB5 say good-morning to them, and then I heard them going into the bathroom. I heard the shower going, and the gas going on the stove. I crossed the sitting-room and looked in. Bill and Cohn were frying eggs and bacon at the stove. They were completely dressed in riding clothes. $FIRSTNAMEB5 had a tray of coffee ready for them. Mike was eating bread and bacon at the table. The door opened out of the bathroom and Cohn called: \u201cGet washed, Jake, and come and eat some breakfast.\u201d\n\u201cGood God!\u201d I said. \u201cWhat did you get up so early for?\u201d\n\u201cOh, it\u2019s too good a day to sleep. $FIRSTNAMEB5 and Mike and I went over to look at the course.\u201d\n\u201cWhere\u2019s...", "0_7_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt three o'clock in the morning I had been sitting with the anchor watch on the fantail of the yacht.\nThere were two young men and a young woman on the anchor watch with me. They were not members of our regular crew. They were friends of mine who had been scheduled to sail with us only from Miami to the Bahamas\u2014the first part of the voyage.\nThe old hands on our crew were Greenland whalers.\nThere were ten of them on the crew. Five had their wives and children with them. The other five were still thinking about their wives and children.\nNone of the old hands would think about their wives and children for almost two years, because every two years they were required to sail for two consecutive years as crew members of a Makln Shaklid Davis seal-hunting ship.\nNone of them would see their wives and children again until the spring of 1963\u2014just about two years from the time when our yacht left Miami.\nMy friends who were scheduled only for the short cruise from Miami to the Bahamas were comparatively fresh.\nEarly in the evening I went below to the galley for coffee, and I encountered one of these friends standing behind the great stainless steel stove. He was five feet eleven...", "0_13_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe burning ship was an Imperial Gunboat. It had been carrying fifty troops. All fifty troops had been killed.\nA pilot had happened to see the explosion and the burning whirligig falling from the sky. The pilot sent a message to army headquarters. Army headquarters sent out reconnaissance troops in civilian clothes.\nThey found nothing but the shattered masonry of the ammunition caves and wrecked scout-warrior transmitter stations. There were no Imperial troops within miles.\nNeither the reconnaissance troopers nor the pilot could find any fragments big enough to show what had caused such complete devastation. They sent a report to Army Central Headquarters, and had a conference about it.\nAt Army Central Headquarters, the chief of staff was working out an attack schedule that would do an even more thorough job of wrecking and demoralizing the Martian Army.\nThere was a murmur of amusement around the table\u2014someone had made a joke. The chief of staff regrouped his goodhumored officers and began to issue new orders.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, oblivious to all this, was marching on foot around the base. He was lost.\nHe had been told to walk to a certain checkpoint and there report to a certain sergeant. The checkpoint was ten miles away. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had marched...", "1_8_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to nine o'clock in the morning</span>. Stan got out of bed and began to dress. He put on his socks and shoes and opened the window, fastening the shutters so that when the maid came she would not be able to see directly into the room. He dressed and shaved. His clothes, his good clothes, were all packed in a little leather suitcase. He had five pesetas left from the money San Fermin had given him. San Fermin was sleeping on the mattress on the floor. There were two blankets over him.\nHe dressed and crossed the street to the caf\u00e9 for a cup of hot chocolate. It was crowded and noisy. He could not see $FIRSTNAMEB5 or Bill anywhere. He felt in his pockets for his ticket and his money. Finally he saw them sitting at a table. They were having coffee. $FIRSTNAMEB5 saw him first. She smiled and waved. She did not get up, but she made a place for him.\n\u201cWhere have you been?\u201d she asked.\n\u201cI went to hit the ole wino. This is a fiesta. Listen to them.\u201d\nThey were singing. The counter-tenor was singing, and people were singing with him.\n\u201cIt\u2019s wonderful,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said...", "1_13_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. This was the beginning of the Space Age, of the great new era of space travel, exploration, and development, and we members of $SURNAME0\u2019s Club on Mars were in a perfect position to view our happy infant\u2019s first steps.\nWe were not the first Earthlings to benefit by the sight of a manned missile bound for Mars. Driven half mad by the treachery of politicians and the myopia of their own scientists, Soviet scientists had launched a man named Koroteov all the way to Mars and back in 1962. Soviet $SURNAME0, named Khoklov, went all the way to Mars and a quarter of the way around it in 1971. And, as every schoolchild knows, the American House Pain (the commander, incidentally, of the fighter squadron that defeated Germany) took a manned missile all the way to Mars and all the way back again in 1964. But our Club on Mars took the fullest advantage of the seeing opportunities offered by these predecessors.\nWe not only had telescopic and stereoscopic seeing instruments\u2014 we also had access to Koroteov and $SURNAME0 and Pain. Koroteov we kept in a $SURNAME1-Arena in Cologne and Cologne, Germany, Pain we kept under wraps in a secret...", "2_7_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> on Earth.\n\"No!\" shrieked the $SURNAME0 would-be assassin.\nHe fell backwards on the ground. By falling, he saved his life, for the air tilted a foot and a half to one side just then. The yellow beam that had grazed the skin of his wrist burned a smoking pit in $SURNAME0\u2019s mansion wall.\nThe spot of the burn marked the room in which young $FIRSTNAMEB2 lived.\nThirty feet above the ground, the skin of the saucer that had almost been the death of $SURNAME0 turned bright, bright red. It throbbed like a living thing.\nThis was its courtship signal to $FIRSTNAMEF0 \u2014 and a boast, too, that it had saved $SURNAME0 by firing at the supposed assailant only after verifying that the assailant really meant harm. The boast was a galling one, considering that the man was an old family retainer who was as harmless a mortal as Earth had ever produced. The courtship signal would be rejected.", "2_17_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-two in the afternoon</span>, Central Standard Time.\nOne second, Central Standard Time, the orchestra was playing Handel-Bach.\nThe next second, the orchestra was playing Handel-Bach-Beethoven.\nIt was Handel, Bach, and Beethoven all at once. And the architectural wonder that music made wasn\u2019t crumbling\u2014it was growing and living.\nAnd then it skipped.\nAnd then it skipped again.\nAnd then\u2014it made love. It made wild love.\nThe great stones moved in passion. Shook, trembled, were inundated with color and shivering incandescence. The little musicians had become a great big orchestra. And the music they made was more real than the red, white, and blue of the Flag of the United States of America, a thousand times more real than the yellow gold and mahogany brown of the noble gases in the Periodic Table, a million billion times more real than the iron and concrete mass of the exterior walls that surrounded the soldiers.\nIt was five forty-three in the afternoon, Central Standard Time.\nThe music was very, very real.\nIt was the only real thing about Mars.\nIt was realer than Mars.", "2_8_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-two in the morning</span>.\nIn $SURNAME0\u2019s fleet in space, the time was two hours and ten minutes later.\nOn the forty-foot titanium wall in the back of the palace, the mural\u2019s central galaxy flickered again. The fluttering contrived to stir a faint suspicion of motion within all the many mighty spirals and bureaus of spiral galaxies in the mural. Viewed from Earth, the mural actually covered all of Titanic\u2019s three walls.\nThe mural actually covered everything on Mars that was worth covering.\nThe mural was a Martian\u2019s only view of the Universe.\nThe mural was $SURNAME0\u2019s only view of the Universe, too. $SURNAME0\u2019s only contribution to the mural was a three-inch blue space ship called a NASA Jewell: Earthling\u2019s only model of itself as it might look from outside the Universe.", "0_6_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-two in the morning</span>. Let us now cut ahead to the first night of the war. The date was June 24, 1982, the place was not terribly important, a farmhouse near the city of Sidalca in the province of Catatao, in the country of Lilliputia. The war was about forty-five minutes old. The great artillery battle was raging in the east. The armies had gotten underway late, and a lot of people in the region were still asleep, amid the cheerful trills of birds.\nThe economic and political condition of Lilliputia was disastrous. The population had grown so alarmingly, because of the government\u2019s big powerful schemes for killing people, that it was threatening to eat all the grain on hand. The bits of money that had been circulating in remote regions were now worth hundreds of trillions of blintzes. The Prime Minister had been suspended. The Parliament was in shackles. The royal family had run away. The royal family\u2019s palace was a halfmoon of flame in the night. All communications were out.\nThe people who had been busy resisting the draft bills now began looking around for alternative leadership. Huge crowds of them were processed into the city of Sidalca right before the battle. The...", "1_12_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after noon</span>.\nThey had a direct line, by way of an antique rotary dial, from $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s bathroom to the palace. The line was kept open with a clothespin. While $FIRSTNAMEB0 was in the bathroom, the microphone remained open.\nThere was a pause of a minute before anything happened. While that minute was passing, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 could hear the little stirrings and whisperings inside the palace that told them the call had been received.\nThen, before the minute ended, the head of a harmonium was cautiously placed against the mouthpiece at the palace end of the line. A harmonium, in case you don\u2019t know, is what Earthling paleontologists call a trilobite.\nThe harmonium head against the mouthpiece served two purposes. First, the presence of the head screened out the noises made by the claws, teeth, and mandibles of the royal telephone operator. Second, the harmonium head itself was a perfect receiver of speech.\nThe harmonium had been bred for the express purpose of phone reception. Among the crosses that had produced it had been a cross between a live monitor lizard and a common telephone receiver in good working order.\nA few million generations later, the harmoniums...", "2_19_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt eight fifteen, the tennis players came into the caf\u00e9. There were seven: four men and three women. One of the men was six feet five inches tall and weighed 220 pounds. He was seventeen years old. His name was $FIRSTNAMEF3 Rymbly. He was an Australian. He had the best serve in the world. He was going to become World Champion at twenty-three, and then retire. He had already been through one fortune, of a million dollars, and was now pretty much through with money. He was being taken care of financially by two elderly widows. His serve was a blinding service that crashed down out of nowhere and made a noise on landing that sounded like a forty-five-caliber pistol.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 Rymbly had come to Wimbledon to go through the motions and lose in the semifinals so as not to embarrass British tennis. There was a contractual understanding to this effect between $FIRSTNAMEF3 Rymbly, his two elderly widows, and the All-England Club. He was one of the highest-paid amateur athletes in the world and had no amateur standing whatever.", "0_16_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four eighteen in the afternoon</span>. In twenty minutes the President would be shot. I never even saw it coming. Not an idea, not a glimpse. Only a feeling, a real Vegas feeling that all rational motion had been summarily suspended. \u201cAll bets are off.\u201d This town is an aphrodisiac. Sensuous geography. Debauchment en route. And the high\u2014priesthood of the hipsters is here in the lobby, filing into this very hotel, this diamond\u2014encrusted honeymoon resort at the edge of infinity.\nNo hope, man. No hope.\nThe Merry Pranksters rented a suite of rooms on the seventh floor\u2014and began throwing a party that speedily spread to every floor of the place. Some of the Hell\u2019s Angels were still on the beach outside, but they seemed to sense\u2014from telepathy or some jungle\u2014like sixth sense\u2014that this was our night. They stayed out there, roaming around and around the building, keeping perfect vigil. Meanwhile the music got louder, the laughter became more shrill and nervous, the paranoia was capped by earthquakes of hysterical laughter.\nPapa Marcel Digioisis, who looked and acted like a superior and angelic Sam Spade, was somewhere around...", "2_2_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-eight in the morning</span>. The men, who had been about to smoke the joint, held it unlit between their fingers. Tobacco, heretofore unnoticed, was suddenly smelled.\n\"My grandpa\u2014he said white wine tasted more wonderful than anything in the whole wide world,\" said Fallilitating.\nAnd it was at this precise moment, this moment when the air of the Army had become poetic with nostalgia, when the imagination of the Army had become engaged by the mystery of marijuana, when an uninterrupted line of word-pictures had begun between the mouths of the gentlemen and their subconscious minds, that the door made a loud noise and snapped open, and Capsler and Eich, the Supply sergeant and cook, stumbled into the room.\nCapsler and Eich were drunk. They laughed loud at the sight of the nineteen soldiers. All nineteen soldiers turned around and stared silently.\nCapsler and Eich were drunk. And with such hopeless faces they embraced in the doorway. \"The trouble is, the world has gotten old,\" Capsler told Eich. His voice shook.\nAnd bats of dull light beat at the window panes, for the night had turned to a churning grey.\nMONEY AND SILVER TEETH", "2_21_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"Are you thinking about your husband?\" said the man. He put an arm on the back of the chair, and another arm he put around the woman, and they snuggled up together.\n\"Yes, I am thinking about him,\" said Florence.\n\"I can see him in your eyes,\" said the man.\n\"Well,\" said Florence, \"if every time a man and a woman looked at each other they got married, there\u2019d probably be six billion people scrambling around instead of just the few million there are.\"\n\"Are you thinking unkind things about me, now?\" said the man.\n\"I\u2019m thinking about people, in a general way,\" said Florence.\n\"Well, I wasn\u2019t hinting anything about marrying you,\" said the man. \"I never thought of such a thing.\"\n\"How come?\" said Florence.", "0_2_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-three in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF0 was sunk deep in a dream of houses and rooms, which were all just alike and which had the curious property of becoming smaller and smaller when she entered them, until finally she was lost in them, and had to keep herself from shrieking by continual acts of increasing her volition, by ascesis, by will. First she would decide to stoop. She would decide to make every movement deliberate and meaningful. She would decide to take one step, then another, then another, until finally she would make it to the next room, to the next house. It was arduous, heavy work. She was exhausted. The ceiling of the room broke into fissures, then caved in, the floor melted, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 flushed awake. Sweat burst out on her body like so many jets of water. It was the middle of the night.\nThe floor of the room was not real. Where the graceful, curving walls of the house had been, a wide, desolate plain stretched to the horizon, a plain crossed and recrossed by whining, fuzzy black wires. An electric storm, with long, green, forking strands of lightning, was directly overhead. The only shelter was a small false-fronted building painted purple...", "1_4_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-seven in the morning</span>. I was crouching over an open suit-case on the big soft bed in Cabin 9 at the Flamingo. I had only the clothes on my back and my tape deck, but I was ready for the jungle. All I needed now was one goddamn sign from God, just one small permanent sign that what I was planning to do this morning was right. But up to now there had been only silence\u2014a big earful a nothing since I had left home. The omens were all bad. The flight out of Vegas had been awful. Terrible weather\u2014foul winds that pulled a 747 like an overheated DC8. Something wrong with the automatic pilot, too, because we took off without a drink and stayed that way for two hours. The stewardesses were terrible. A vicious crowd of drunks; everyone yelling for another drink. Finally they served us some orange juice in jelly glasses and we all had two, but it didn\u2019t seem to help. The whole plane went wild. People were standing on their seats, shouting for alcohol. Finally one of the stewardesses went crazy and started screaming, \u201cStop that damned noise! Sit down and shut up or I\u2019ll knock your goddamn heads off!!!\u201d The...", "1_6_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six eighteen in the morning</span> of the day before the cannon were going to be fired silently. The celebration was over.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, who had red hair like $FIRSTNAMEB1, had a milk-white, aristocratic face behind his visor. He was an inch shy of seven feet. His up-tilted head and his outsized, graceful hands lent his physical appearance a pleading, a saintly quality, though his actions at this point were neither pleading nor saintly. They were mean and sneaky.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s command post in the siege tower was dark and smoky. He had set fire to his spare combat suit before putting on his batter-head helmet. He was now naked and invisible but for his great white face. The forty soldiers of his platoon were milling around in the dark, screaming and waving their hands as they tried to put out the flames on their uniforms.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 stood in the middle of the tower, sawing and hacking with a piece of broken glass he had found on his cot. He was sawing and hacking at the straps of his breastplate.\nSoot and sweat gave dark patches to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s white, puffy face. He was panting as he worked, and his knees trembled. He was...", "1_5_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>. The debarkation of the Space Wanderer had been scheduled for half past seven. That gave his fellow Tralfamadorians eight short free minutes.\nIt was during these eight free minutes that $FIRSTNAMEB1 and his friend $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 were to commit a capital offense.\nThey were to kill $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the man who had sold Earth to the enemy.\nTheir reason for killing him was that he had sold Earth to the enemy.\nTheir theory was that Earth deserved to be abused \u2014 and that the one who abused her should be the one to suffer for it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and $SURNAME0 were to kill the man who had sold Earth to the Tralfamadorians in order to take from him the earthly benefits of his administration.\nTheir manner of killing him was to be symbolic.\nThey were to kill him with his own tattoos.\nNo member of any other species in the Universe thought very highly of tattoos \u2014 but, of course, no member of any other species in the Universe had ever had the advantage of being able to see the future.\nTHE DEATH OF MALACHI CONSTANT\nALL THIS is recapitulated in $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s Pocket History of Mankind, a...", "0_15_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-six in the afternoon</span>. The place was Salinas, California, on the foothills of the Gabilan Mountains. Salinas, where Army men have a way of getting languidly drunk on Saturday nights and where wholesale produce buyers are shiftless, ne\u2019er-do-well, quick-rich gadabouts. This particular one was an ex-minor-league ball player turned produce dealer. His name was Stanton $SURNAME2 and he was forty-two years old. Now he was rich and free and bored in Salinas. He was lean and tall, with square, bony hips and hands of a dragoon. He wore a tank corps officer\u2019s WWI campaign hat pushed back on his head. He wore tailored suits of dusty dove-gray material, and instead of shoes he wore jack-knife-sprung boots of soft, tooled Texas steer-hide. He had an enormous knife in a sheath and a fast draw with it, too. He was a hell of a horseman. On his left hand he wore a ten-thousand-dollar diamond that used to belong to Eva Tanguay. Nobody knew where he got it; they only knew he had it and had had it for years. His friends were Hollywood horse-wr...", "2_23_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nSomeone was trying to walk off with $SURNAME1\u2019s doorknocker. It weighed more than a large German dictionary.\nThis was not surprising. What was surprising, what was wonderful, was the handling of the knocker by the thief.\nThe miraculous thing was not that the thief was able to carry the knocker away\u2014but that the thief was able to carry it away without also carrying away his own arms and legs.\nThis thief had a masterly control over his body.\nThis thief, while handling the knocker with a coy, sensual gentility, also managed to break two of $SURNAME1\u2019s windows and a support of his front porch.\nThe support happened to be a four-inch vertical blade of solid concrete. No material, in the known universe, is harder than that.\nThe thief conquered that obstacle, too.\nThe thief now sought to get out of sight before the lights came on and the murder began. He dropped the knocker, and made a sprint for the privet hedge.", "2_14_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThere were twelve Space Wanderers and twelve corporeal robots on the ledge.\nOld Salo, who had come unwrapped again, had a bone in his left jaw, a bone in his right jaw, and a bone in both hands. The purpose of the bones was to make noise with. He was making steady, cheerful noise.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were again in the $SURNAME0 estate doghouse. $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME0 were again in gilded compressed-atmosphere cylinders. $FIRSTNAMEB4 was again wrapped.\nNeither $SURNAME0 nor $SURNAME1 were paying much attention to $SURNAME1\u2019s duplicated image on the wall screen. The image was gibbering away again, trying, as always, to sell something.\n\"Good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen,\" said the image on the screen, \"and how are you today, ladies and gentlemen? This is $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Mars, ladies and gentlemen. By the authority granted me, I invite you to invest in the Mars Tinker Company\u2014a new enterprise devoted to keeping your household tools in first-class shape, to fixing them quickly and expertly whenever they break down, and to extending their lives indefinitely.", "1_8_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-four in the morning</span>. The place was the rather wonderful, baroque boardroom of the Kinsley cable television company, in a building just behind the Tristero Building in California. The whole of the San Francisco Tristero zone was rich with architectures, styles that merged imperceptibly into one another in cycles of fashion more intricate than the court frocks of sixteenth-century France. The Tristero Building itself was a sad but massive cube of repeating-glyph concrete, windows without eyeholes, entrances without doors. Across Telegraph Hill, to the north, stood a pleasant, ruined Italian Renaissance villa, with decorative scars down both sides of its pink stone face, probably the remains of a shell blowing some fifty years before, a shell that might well have been lobbed by one side or the other in some gentleman\u2019s disagreement with the Barberini over some point of theology. Across the Embarcadero stood an Egyptian /Chinese pagoda. It was built in 1750 B.C. by Thothmes the Third as an experiment in marine pineapples, remained standing when Pharaoh was buried in the sand, passed through many hands thereafter, became a museum, a tenement, fell vacant and was rumored to be haunted. In 1906 it was dynam...", "2_0_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after midnight</span> when we got in and the driver asked again if it was true that the Feria did not open until August. We were in bed and asleep when the girl came with the coffee.\nWe woke to hear her rattling dishes and talking to the Madrilenos. It was raining. It rained all day, a steady, hard rain. Water could be heard running down the smooth tiled roofs of the town. We lay in bed, too lazy to do anything. $FIRSTNAMEB5 got up to make some coffee. We both felt better after the hot shower, even my hand hurt a little less. It was nice in bed in the rainy Spain. Most people were lying in bed. The streets were empty.\n$FIRSTNAMEB5 woke to have another smoke. It was raining hard.\n\u201cI\u2019m glad we\u2019re here and not up in the mountains.\u201d\n\u201cYou feel good about it?\u201d\n\u201cI feel strange about it. I feel just as though I\u2019d committed a murder.\u201d\n\u201cDon\u2019t think about it.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s awful.\u201d\n\u201cForget it.\u201d", "0_17_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty in the afternoon</span> and the gang of four\u2014including $FIRSTNAMEF2\u2014reflected rays of sunlight straight toward the creameralong the four parallel Neon columns of Las Vegas downtown. The recent rain had beenhesunlit from the flatoats and white pants of the Teamsters going off shift at the warehouse across the intersection. And among the columns up Goofyfoot Road, headlight beams arced in brilliant swoops and swung left, quick and don\u2019t miss it at 71, keep right 70 and 702, red right arrow, on green, over the bridge and into mysterious desert; or turned right, to plunge like long yellow knives into the bowels of the city, an angel\u2019s flash, biblical and urgent as though to pierce the sun itself, that vast and changeless light, ambiguous as God\u2019s intentions. As though, like God, the sun knew only the one word, and could not be argued with.\nOutlying neighborhoods were just stirring, in that truncated silence you only get in the fringe, a hush that sounds like the inside of a seashell. The main streets were waking, lights going on in coffee shops and each unique variation on roadside diner. Tire chains clanked, hogs grumbled and sn...", "2_2_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>. In the dark, somewhere in the village of East Corinth, in a white clapboard house with green shutters, beneath a steep leaden sky, a little redheaded girl named Della Street Madden was awake and ready to die, screwing her courage to the sticking place so that she might not quail before the mean man from Memphis one inch. It came to her that she was about to die. A man with a black patch over his eye and his stomach hanging out of his pants had put a pistol to her right ear and was guffawing and yelling, to her intolerable horror and shame and terror, that he would kill her if she didn\u2019t let him in the house. She rang her school bell.\nThe time was four minutes to three o\u2019clock in the morning.\nIt was going on three o\u2019clock in the morning, and far away, out at the edge of things, in a certain small key to a certain great, tragic door, the harmonium played softly and steadily, played the brave and expected, soft, sweet and solemn hymn of death, played the music in the pauses of the storm.\nOld men wept and embraced on streetcorners.", "1_21_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-two in the evening</span>, and she hadn\u2019t heard from Fieldbinder. She spent the rest of the evening attempting lucidly to digest the contents of an article in a report from Academic Progress, entitled \u201cFactors in the Scientific Production-Rate of American Economists.\u2018 in which the author, a man named Ingmar, asserted that the well-known disproportionately low level of scientific-productivity in American economists was a function not of poor quality but of poor\u2014poor but sufficient\u2014intensity, an intensity, Ingmar was at pains to point out, that was in no way inhibited by the strange fear of one\u2019s fellow scientists that seemed to pervade the atmosphere of the American economist. Betty\u2019s disinclination to call Mrs. Tissaw and complain to Fieldbinder in advance was suddenly overwhelmed by the need to tell someone what had happened. She was flying high on the result of having overcome the thing by herself. Her face got tingly, and she found that she was talking into the phone to Mrs. Tissaw before Mrs. Tissaw answered. It was funny about call-waiting. There was something about it that meant you hardly ever got anybody on the first call. On the first call, if anybody picked...", "0_20_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe sunny part of Mars consists of an area of roughly two thousand million square miles.\nThe probability that the firefly in its search for people would enter that particular square mile at that particular time was approximately one in twenty-three billion.\nIt was a glorious derelict that shone in the dark and chaotic wastes of space in the precincts of Neptune. It shone with a pink light.\nIt was an ancient derelict. Current thought among the people of the United\nStates of America identified it as the remains of the crashlanding of the good ship Parable. This was the ship that an intelligent species of beetles had built for voyages among the stars, when the common ancestor of all beetles had been taken aboard as official mascot by the crew of the Parable.\nCommon sense had long ago told the people of the United States that the Parable could not have traveled so far as Neptune. The actual distance was incalculable, the actual time fantastic.\nOnly the battered hull of the Parable had come this far. Long before, the crew and all the passengers except one mascot had died. The survivors were skeletons when they died, millions of years before.\nIt was a cold light, but it was a steady light.\nSun...", "0_9_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span> when the space ship carrying Diamond, Wilar, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 entered atmo-sphere above the green and iron globe of Mercury.\nThe ship was a dish-shaped beauty. It had been created, out of warped and assorted space ship parts, by the resourceful and unskilled hands of malcontents in the underground.\nThe malcontents had painted the ship skilfully symbolically.\nThe name of the ship was\nUnited States.\nThis was not out of respect for the old American nation. The leaders of the malcontents behind the iron curtains in the barracks had not been out of the Child Study Centers long enough to know much about history. The name had been painted in honor of their most ambitious project to date.\nThe United States was about to make a new flight\u2014a flight to the friendliest planet. All the best people were sure that Earth was the friendliest planet. There was nothing to be said against such good sense.\nThe smiling tooth painted near the cockpit window was intended to represent Earth, the friendliest planet. Over the tooth was a crown, painted in yellow. The crown was made of five circles, any one of which would have enclosed the entire iron hell of Mercury.\nThe five circles were...", "2_12_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-two p.m.</span> The red rioters, now a column of several thousand, now a mass of a hundred, now just a few knots of anonymous destruction, were attacking anything.\nThey attacked Cat\u2019s Paw Manufacturing.\nThey attacked a cop.\nThey attacked a fire hydrant.\nThey attacked a shadow.\nEverything, in short, that attacked it.\nBy a remarkable coincidence, the rioters hit precisely those installations that happened to be weakest, most unprofitable, and most vulnerable.\nOne thing they attacked was a ten-square-yard section of wall that somebody had misunderstood, eight years before, to be part of the prison. The misunderstanding had been found out, but the wall had never been repaired. The wall was built of two-by-fours and inadequately filled two-by-six slats. The wall was therefore soft and flimsy.\nSeventy-one of the rioters smashed their way through the wall into the sewage disposal field outside.\nThey did not attack the field, because it was dazzlingly bright outside the wall. Their eyes, accustomed to the dark of the factory, would have bashed and scorched themselves without MITIGATION.", "2_21_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-four in the evening</span>. The place was a basement lounge in an apartment building in East Corinth, Ohio. The lights were off. The room was illuminated in shades of gray by light leaking in from the hail outside. Nothing moved on the street outside. Nothing moved in the room.\n/f/\n\u201cIt\u2019s hard for me to believe there\u2019d be room for me underneath the dresser.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cI think I\u2019ll just scream if I don\u2019t get an explanation soon.\u201d\n\u201cWhy don\u2019t you just relax and collect your thoughts, Dr. \u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588.\u201d\n\u201cRelax?\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d", "0_18_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span> of the third day of our thirty-six hour stay on Tralfamadore.\nThere was, at that time, no sun in the sky of Tralfamadore.\nAt seven o'clock, every living thing on Tralfamadore was killed\u2014killed as surely as would be killed any living thing caught in the rapid-flowing current of hot lava from an erupting volcano on Earth.\nThe sun exploded at seven o'clock.\nIt became a ring of fire two thousand miles in diameter\u2014a fiery, spinning, twisting hoop that expanded by a million miles a minute.\nThe sun ring swept past the spot where Salo had parked himself and Ten advised Ford and young $FIRSTNAMEB2 not to fall behind.\nThe sun ring arrived at the wall of the cottage at Newport. At that spot, the wall vaporized as though it were so much old dry paper.\nThe cottage was pressed flat by the passage of the sun ring.\nIt became a thin sheet, sealing soil and trees and the roots of trees together. It became a mural in the decayed ruins at Newport.\nThe mural commemorated the heat and turmoil of the day of the unendurably tragic day on Tralfamadore.", "0_18_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-six in the evening</span>. The place was among the slot machines in Newport, Kentucky. The year was 1963. In a slightly more than roomy ship was young $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, heir apparent to the grand old home-appliance fortune of North America\u2014and a son as long-haired as any son had a right to be. There was to be a dreadful account of $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1\u2019s genes somewhere in the archives of nature.\nThe heir apparent was disguised as a pilot of a space ship called the Thunderbird. He had a big head of black hair, blue eyes that were sensitive to the sun, and a nose that resembled Mussolini\u2019s. He was actually an inch under six feet tall, and he certainly weighed no more than 140 pounds.\nIn disguise $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 wore a brutally handsome face, a marvelously proportioned body, a flashy space suit of ruby red.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 spent fifty-nine minutes and forty-three seconds out of every minute fulfilling the dreams of thousands of American males\u2014and females, too, of course. He spent the time between campaigns playing two-handed poker, at a salary of a dime a hand. His victim was a legal-size cantaloupe, named William Pinkwater, an ex-astronaut...", "2_3_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-nine in the morning</span>.\n3.7\nDetective-Sergeant Peter Hussein of the Los Angeles Police Department had a tired face. His blue suit looked expensive but somehow not cared for. Bright blond hair poked unconfidently through the holes of his green plastic surgeons\u2019 cap. His derby hat was too large. His uniform was not properly ironed.\nHe had been drinking. A large green plastic surgeons\u2019 bag lay on the front seat of the dark maroon Ford sedan.\nDetective-Sergeant Hussein looked up toward the dark windows of 7071 Imperial Highway, Villa Park. He knew that Dr. Marlin T. Strauss was at the party up there.\nDetective-Sergeant Hussein opened the glove compartment of the dark maroon Ford sedan and looked at his watch. He closed the glove compartment again. He sighed. He got out of the dark maroon Ford sedan and walked unsteadily toward the door at 7071 Imperial Highway, Villa Park. He pushed a button on the frame of the door.", "1_20_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>. For a long time, there had been one light burning in the building where $FIRSTNAMEF1 was. That light had shone on the mailbox under the corner lamp of the lobby. For a long time, nobody had gone in or out of the front door.\nAnd then, at ten minutes to nine, the light in $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s apartment was turned out. The window-shade was drawn down, almost all the way.\nNow, suddenly, and without a light, a dark form was there, at the lock of the front door.\nWhen the door was opened, and when the figure came in, it was in darkness so utter that the new presence in the lobby could be sensed, but not seen.\nAnd then the figure stopped, apparently listening. Then it came on, confidently, toward the inner staircase. The footfalls were clear, hard. They rang.\nThe figure drew apparently near the first landing of the staircase, paused again, listened. Then it continued on. The ring of the footfalls was uninterrupted.\nThe figure approached the second landings of the staircase, stopped again, listened, apparently tentatively stepped, something fell. And then continued up.\nOn the third landing, the figure paused again, again something was apparently tent...", "0_1_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-eight in the morning</span>. There was no moon and the desert was black. No lights appeared on the highway. The Rolls rocked gently to the hum of the motor. I leaned back in the seat and watched the white line down the center of the road recede into the black wall of the night before us.\n\u201cI think we better put the top up,\u201d I said. \u201cIn case somebody comes up behind us.\"\n\u201cNo, we\u2019ll be out of the desert in an hour and we won\u2019t need it then.\u201d\n\u201cWhy are we going to the doctor\u2019s?\u201d I asked suddenly, hoping to startle him into an indiscretion.\n\u201cGreat heavens!\u201d Lang laughed. \u201cCan\u2019t I go see a friend?\u201d I was off again; off into the night, going down the long black highway through the flipping desert. Twice somebody passed us, a long truck groaning up the grade, and once a fast car going the other way. If Bruce\u2019s killer was in that car I never saw him. We got to Colorado City just before one-thirty in the morning. A short, bespectacled, tense man in a...", "2_10_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-five in the morning</span> when Sergeant Bracknell lost his last friend, and best medic, on Tralfamadore. The medic was an Earthling named $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nBracknell was a comrade in arms of $FIRSTNAMEB1 anddead. was a comrade in arms of Schliemann, Newton, Edison, Eric, Leslie, and $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Earth. Their names and a hundred more were written in water on the great wall at the House of St. Barnabas for a wreath to be sent to them on Tralfamadore. The wreath had never been sent.\nBracknell had a service automatic, three cartridges, and seventy-four Earthling years left to live.\nHe became a policeman.\nHis first assignment was near his home on Tralfamadore. It was a district so notorious and rife with crime and violences of all kinds that life expectancy for a cop in that district was three Earthling minutes.\nBracknell lasted four Earthling years.", "1_20_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-four in the evening</span> of a Tuesday in April.\nThe taxi began to move, carrying the three men and the two women away from the field toward Cape Cod, toward Boston and the Pacific, toward the Western Sea, the Egyptian Sea, the Sea of the Blacks. Salo, having risen instantly in the air twice in one day, talked no more of flying but only crossed his legs comfortably and looked at the people passing by outside in the street, and then at the reflection of people passing by outside in the street in the television set in the wall of the taxicab, and then at the desert, and then at the television set again, and then at the desert again. One thing he noticed on television was that there were two spacesuits floating in Palace Courtyard. He supposed that one or something like one of them belonged to the poor Martian who had drowned in the tub, and one or something like one of them to the man who had gone mad and leaped in the desert.\nSalo Ph Marlinspike had once got very drunk and spent the rest of the night throwing bottles down a New York City garbage shoot under the impression that it was a telephone.\nHe began wondering what the telephone code was for Tralfamadore.\nHe began to look for...", "1_4_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-four in the morning</span>. Our holy order of Warlocks had met for supper in a restaurant off North Main Street in Salinas. We had walked to the restaurant from the motor court where we were living in tents. We ate our meal with traditional island savagery, throwing plates and dishes on the floor when we were done, paying the grossly fat proprietor one dollar in hundred-dollar bills when we left.\nIn an offbeat parking lot behind the restaurant half our number went in three rented station wagons to take the final acid test. Four of us remained behind, in an attempt to come down or come together or come to our senses in any way that was possible.\nOne of these four was Tim Gray, a nineteen-year-old Harvard sophomore, who had written the following letter to his Ivy League Advisor, describing his summer plans:\nDear (...):\nAt the conclusion of the spring semester in 1966, I became involved in what has since proved a highly important project. Most of this project will be concluded in August or September. Because of the peculiar nature of the project and its funding, it has seemed desirable to continue at least a portion of the work into next year. It is for this reason that I ask for some special consideration with respect to next year's...", "1_22_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span> when I left $FIRSTNAMEF0 at the table in the restaurant. I was tight, and everybody else was tight, and the only man in Paris who was absolutely sober was the poor one-armed boy at the door. From the terrace, before I left, I gave him a crooked six-franc-piece. I told him to get some wine with it. \u201cThis is a very good neighborhood,\u201d I assured him. I waved goodbye to everybody. \u201cSo long, everybody! So long! So long!\u201d I staggered out the door. Everybody waved. \u201cDon\u2019t fall down in the street, you big boob!\u201d called somebody. I got into the car, the driver made a wide turn in front of the Ritz, and then we were going down the Rue Cambon. I leaned way back and started to think of wonderful things that had happened that evening. In a little while we would be crossing the Seine, going home to the Ile Saint Louis. It would be lovely to be lying in bed on the second floor, while the rain poured against the windows, and tell the taxi driver to wake me at seven o\u201cclock. It would be lovely to boil eggs and read the...", "0_14_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty in the afternoon</span>, says the narrator. The place was the research library of a midtown Manhattan insurance company, says the narrator. The narrator was a middle\u2014aged man with a pleasant smile. He was sitting in a long, polished table of a room, streaked with sun. Other men were sitting in the same kind of room. Each man had a long, polished table and many books. About the walls were paintings of boards of directors. The men laughed nancy laughs once in a while. They ate dry potato chips out of Baggies. The narrator was eating dry potato chips out of a Baggie. They thought the chips were the best things in the world.\nSomething was going on beyond the window, in the sky.\nRUMOR\nSpace is big. The awful burp of altitude and distance that is Earth slides away and dwindles smoothly into uneventfulness. But the Earth does not die. Earth is still there. A tear we cannot measure streaks down its cheek. At a light gravity and a light atmosphere, the Earth dies gently. By day its tears may be seen as a faint silvery fog. By night there is a faint luminescence to the skin of the dying Earth that the most ingenious instruments can barely discern. At the...", "1_20_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-eight in the evening</span> of November 4, 1962, in the jittery final weeks of a long, hot Washington summer when rumors of Kennedy\u2019s so-called Vietnam build-up were running wild in the capital. The rumormongers were mostly freaks: electronic spook freaks, CIA teahead freaks, Pentagon strategy-game freaks and God knows what else. Nobody trusted anybody else\u2014Large Marge and her six \u201cBaby Gan\u201d killers had not yet entered the picture\u2014so no really solid facts ever got straightened out. But the rumors were fantastic. One month the Far East was supposed to have become a White Zombie theatre; the next it was a huge Soviet listening post; then ag again it was both. Nobody, including Kennedy, seemed to know what was really going on. It was during this no-man\u2019s\u2014land of bad dope, weird speculation and/or presidential cover-up that I arrived in Washington to cover something called \u201cThe Democrat/Republican National Conventions.\u201d\n8:28 P.M., Things were supposed to start humming at nine sharp, but the hotel bar was already packed with convention freaks who had arrived early. They were drinking like mad and screaming at each other about what was...", "2_5_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>. I was beginning to suspect that the fine frenzy of the night had come to an end\u2014that glut of disaster at the amusement park, followed by the agony of the long ride home. Now the thrills were over. I had reached that dull, predictable moment just before dawn when all entertainments have broken down and the human mind begins to worry about food. I was standing in a standard hotel room, staring at the floor, listening to strange noises on the other side of the door.", "2_13_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nOnly five minutes had elapsed since $SURNAME1 had said good-by to his class. $SURNAME1\u2019s chest was pressed against the chest of the chauffeur.\nThe chauffeur was doing everything possible to maintain a personal relationship with a collapsible drinking cup. He had to turn his head sideways to sip, sideways to discharge a drop or two.\nThe chauffeur\u2019s face was obscured to $SURNAME1, staring at him over the rim of the drinking cup.\nAt this juncture, $SURNAME1 could see only the top of his chauffeur\u2019s head and the beginning of his shoulders. $SURNAME1 could not see the chauffeur\u2019s eyes, or know their color. He could not even be sure of the chauffeur\u2019s age, though he thought him to be around thirty-five. He had no idea what the man\u2019s name was. Rumor had it that that was the rule\u2014that the chauffeurs had to be nameless, faceless, uniforms.\n\"Nice class today, Mr. $SURNAME1?\" said a voice from the top of the chauffeur\u2019s head, over the rim of the drinking cup. The voice was low-pitched and pleasant.", "2_2_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. The woman went to a small desklike door at the end of the entrance hall, opened the door and stepped into the room beyond. The room had no visible source of illumination. But the light in it was soft and grainy, the light of many candles.\nA man in a smoking jacket and gray flannel trousers, open at the knees, was roistering about the room, banging on the floor splintering with a heavy, hollow tack hammer.\nThe room was a sort of study or den. Its walls were lined with volumes in cracked yellow bindings.\nThe volumes in their cracked yellow bindings were all about the history of the Army of the United States on Earth in the Third World War, a war that had involved a planet in another solar system.\nThere was a fireplace in the study. Over the fireplace hung the head of an Earthling boar, mounted on deer antlers. The boar\u2019s tusks were long and sharp.\nIt was in fact the head of $FIRSTNAMEB0, the symbol of the Berserker Army, the head of the man who had become the father of all that was wise and strong on the other planet.", "2_23_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-five in the evening</span>. It was Thursday, Mars, day one hundred and fifty-two of the war.\nEarthlings were streaming into the firing areas in such numbers and in such splendid spirits that their numbers and spirits became blots on any statistical curves that tried to measure them. They expected nothing, they got nothing. Happily, they were given the opportunity to give their lives.\nBuddy and $SURNAME1, smoking their new $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 cigars and drinking their good California Chablis in a hideaway in the caves of Titan, didn\u2019t have sneak-attack number three to look forward to.\nThere were to be no more sneak attacks, no more anything.\n$SURNAME0 had said goodbye.\n\" \u2019 You can die like a soldier, or you can die like a fool, \" he said. \"Keep your eyes and ears open, and let your heart and mind wander where they will. \"\nAnd then he was gone.", "0_23_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. We were somewhere in the middle of nowhere. The car was beginning to have the absentminded look of a car that has been around for a long time on army bases; it was twelve years old and had a 1946 California license. We pulled it over to the side of the road, and Lee got out, slammed the door, then opened it and put his head in again. \u201cWhere you guys going?\u201d he said.\n\u201cBack to San Francisco. Tonight?\u201d\n\u201cGood. Got plenty of money?\u201d\n\u201cYou know we haven't, you set us up last night.\u201d\n\u201cWell, I'm going on to New York, you want to come with me?\u201d\n\u201cHow much have you got?\u201d\n\u201cAbout thirty.\u201d\n\u201cThirty what?\u201d\n\u201cDollars and some cents.\u201d He waved the money.\n\u201cThat'll take us to Boston,\u201d I said. \u201cCan't you get a ride from there?\u201d\n\u201cHell, I don't know. What time you have to be back in San Fran?\u201d\n\u201cNo time.", "1_18_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-seven in the evening</span>.\nHazel and her roommate, Agnes Scott, were sharing the kitchen of their apartment on Q Street, in the District of Columbia, twenty blocks from the Pentagon.\nTheir apartment was in the back of the second floor of a three-story red-brick-with-white-trim frame house. The first floor of the house was a developed beauty shop. The second floor of the house was the apartment of Hazel and Agnes Scott, 402 Q Street.\nThe beauty shop below was divided into four cubicles of equal size. Its name was Shaefer\u2019s Beauty Box. The beauty attendant who had the first cubicle, on the right as one entered the shop, was a lustrously fat, forty-four-year-old spinster named Lydia Love.\nLydia Love was the landlady of the house, 402 Q Street.\nThe second beauty attendant, who had the cubicle on the left as one entered the shop, was a twenty-eight-year-old divorcee named Lucille Corvo.\nLucille Corvo was a war worker at the Bureau of Ships.\nThe third beauty attendant, who had the rear cubicle, was a twenty-three-year-old blonde divorcee...", "1_20_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-one in the evening</span> of October third, and November would not seem to come. The small pool in the courtyard had turned to brown ice. Unmindful of where the splinters of wood lodged, the workmen, who seemed to live in the well-caved apartments of the building, labored on the scaffoldings at the side of the building. The day was theirs. Ours was the night.\nRecycled air filled our offices. Uncompromising linoleum met our feet. The 3\u2013jay Company was no longer of editorial interest, but there was a new issue to put out, so we were all still on the payroll, even me, even Metzger, even $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThere were two cubicles at the far end of the hall for Vance, $FIRSTNAMEF1, and me. It was a soundless space, wonderfully secluded from the chaos of the editorial room. The day\u2019s story had come from the police radio earphones of a single rookie. \u201cSpoilsbury Mcgear, car fifty-nine, respond Church and Sixt south, officer needs assistance, shots exchanged, offender thought to be Strappazzoni, Pietro. Repeat, Pietro Strappazzoni\u2014\u201dI had given line-outs...", "0_16_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four nineteen in the afternoon</span> when I opened my eyes. All around were green lawns and blue skies and white clouds and red flowers and white buildings. The air was delicious. It was all so different from my New York. And there was not a sound. My New York is never like that. There is always a roar of traffic in the streets, and people talking, and rattling windows, and a big apartment-building somewhere rear-ending a little apartment-building. There are always voices, people are always waking up, and coughing, and getting out of bed, and beginning to move around in the world. That is my New York. That is what wakes me up in the morning and makes me write the words in this pencil. But here, four nineteen P.M., in Provincetown, the air was delicious and cool, and there were blue skies and white clouds, and an old lady walking a little dog, and sailors with long bare legs were standing around in circles talking to each other, and a man with a wheelbarrow kept saying, \u201cWell! Well! Well!\u201d Noises off in the distance, like a small hammer pounding or a radio somewhere playing \u201cZamba Zamba\u201d to the empty air. I got up and...", "0_14_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nR. Welsh Vaughn of the octopus clan was so nervous by this time that he couldn\u2019t face the crowd without a drink. At one minute until three o\u2019clock, he was nowhere to be seen.\nAt two minutes to three o\u2019clock, he suddenly came strolling out of an elevator and into the drawing room, wearing his company smile. He was carrying a drink.\nThe whistle blew.\nThe crowd went berserk. The band started playing \"Auld Lang Syne.\"\nR. Welsh Vaughn, of the octopus clan, climbed upon a chair, and then upon a table. There was bedlam, anyway. Only the reporters were keeping order somehow, with their flashing cameras and scribbling stenographers.\nR. (for Richard) Welsh Vaughn, of the octopus clan, held up his hand for silence. But it was an impossible situation, anyway.\nFingers blew blasts on police whistles. A half-giant named Granny Grantham hammered at a great gong that hung round his neck. Young $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, strapped tightly to his mother\u2019s back, was screaming a chant of his own devising. The chant was: \"Papa...", "0_12_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-four post meridiem</span>; the hun-dredth, hundred and thirty-second day of the war. The child, who in another second would be ripped open and spilled like a seed-knot, whose grinning head would be flown like grapeshot into a tree that would forever after remain smashed and broken, was named $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nThe man who was supposed to kill him was named $SURNAME2, and was known to be a good soldier \u2014 trustworthy, dependable. He had been evaluated, taken to pieces and reassembled again by the most modern military machines on Earth. These machines had proved, over and over again, that $SURNAME2 was the man in the squad best qualified to trust his life to the handsome, clean-minded rifles and handguns the military issued.\n$SURNAME2 had killed forty-seven recruits in platoon attacks by squads, twenty-one attackers in platoon patrols by squads, three captains, eight lieutenants, and one regimental sergeant-major.\nHe would kill $FIRSTNAMEB0 expeditiously, almost painlessly.\nThe man in the crouch pressed his open left palm against a wall in which many holes were drilled for that purpose. This pressed palm signaled the microphone hidden in his balaclava helmet that he wanted to talk. The...", "2_0_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-eight ante meridiem</span>.\nHalf an hour later, the telephone rang again. This time she let the machine get it. The last message was: \"$FIRSTNAMEB6\u2014it\u2019s T that you. Skip the crap. What happened last night? Call me collect or have me call you at\u2014565-4798. Letterman. Try to remember you got me in this as deep as you have. You owe me. I love you.\" There was no good number for her to call him at.\nTHE FIRST ANNIVERSARY PARTY\nA little man with yellow teeth came over to her and showed her his watch. \"It\u2019s twenty after,\" he said. She had promised him the first dance.\nA second year law student, he was.\nHe was also the twelfth man she had refused.\n\"Dance?\" he said.\n\"No, thank you,\" she said.", "1_0_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past midnight</span>.\nThe time was one second past the time that Moncrief, miles away in his lonely room, had drunk the drugged brandy. It was one second after the time that Moncrief, hundreds of miles away from himself, had risen from his chair and uttered a loud scream.\nThe scream, by falling in sympathetic vibration with a scream that had been sounded twentyfour years before, was about to start an action that would involve two planets in a war that until now had been fated to be a splendid isolated affair.\nTwenty-four years before, at a place called St. Clive\u2019s, Mister Herbert Moon, Warden of Larnaka State Prison, Cyprus, had sat on a bench under the Cyprus Cedar and drunk coffee from the walled city of Nicosia. While his wife, three children, chauffeur, and cook visited a Museum of Ostrich Farming across the width of the island, British Government Inspector Moon was drinking coffee and studying a document in which the Warden of St. Clive\u2019s, Cyprus, was ordered to have a man called Jason Bruno shot.\nIn the twenty-four years that had passed since the order for the shooting was written, Jason Bruno had not gone straight to St. Clive...", "1_12_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-six p.m.</span> The place was Watkin\u2019s Sinclair Service Station, on Route 111, halfway between the village of Armonk and the hamlet of Beeber, in the county of Westchester, State of New York. The phone was a white phone, enameled, mounted on the wall of a white booth. The booth was made of fibreglass, an innovation in phone cubicle design in the New York\u2014Bergen area. It was a beautiful booth. So was the phone. The phone was tiiat telephone. That telephone was white. There were two lines in and out of the station; one led directly to the White House in Washington, D.C., the other to the Office of Weasel-Keeper General Norman Bombardini in Manhattan. There was a direct line from the White House to the Sinclair station. There was a direct line from Norman Bombardini\u2019s office to the telephone. There was not a direct line from the telephone to the outside world. The phone was not connected to the outside world. The phone was not connected to the inside world. The line was not connected to the outside world. The line was connected to the inside world. The phone was not connected to anything in particular; it was not connected...", "1_2_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-six in the morning</span>. \u201cIt\u2019s dangerous to be on the road alone at night,\u201d he explained. \u201cWe must always watch out for your mother.\"\nMy attorney agreed. \u201cThat\u2019s a fine message for the boy,\u201d he chuckled, as we watched the brute approach. \u201cEspecially in light of the fact that we\u2019re about to embark on a dangerous crosscountry drive of our own. We\u2019ll have to watch out for the cops, of course\u2014but even more important, we\u2019ll have to watch out for your mother.\u201d He laughed again. \u201cSay\u2014what do you think she\u2019d say if she knew we were out here?\u201d I shrugged. \u201cShe\u2019d probably understand,\u201d I said. \u201cIt\u2019s just another road trip.\"\n\u201cRight!\u201d he shouted. \u201cOne more Good Samaritan adventure on the American HighWway...Right!\u201d My attorney was drunk. We had been throwing Bloody Marys down hard, and the first effects were kicking in just as this gravel\u2014throated heavy ape pulled over to the shoulder. For no apparent reason, I felt fear. I could see...", "2_7_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-seven in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 imagined were all things he had seen with his eyes, or had heard with his ears. He was held to those things by the steel-utensil-enhanced senses of his condition. Suddenly he imagined a strange thing. He imagined how his mate would look with her head an inch or two from the meeting place of two pretty pink walls.\nThe time was seven thirty-one in the morning.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 imagined were all things he had seen with his eyes, or had heard with his ears. He was held to those things by the steel-utensil-enhanced senses of his condition.\n\"What?!\" he cried out loud. \"An inch\u2014ah\u2014two inches from the meeting place of two pretty pink walls! My mate is a prisoner! My mate is in terrible danger!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself.\nThe time was seven forty-two in the morning.\nThe time was seven forty-four in the morning.\nThe time was seven forty-six in the morning.", "0_6_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-eight in the morning</span> when we got out of the truck in front of Stan's Gas Station in Gallup. The sky was turning rose-orange with the dawn. I went through four dollars feeding the meter. A lanky kid took over the wheel for four dollars. \u201cAin't seen my nose this close since two years ago.\u201d\n\u201cGo easy, man!\u201d I yelled as he started away. Stan's door opened. Nobody was there. I ran into the shadows of the great deserted cathedral of the gas pumps and found somewhere to hide. Somewhere I got rags and wiped off the make-up that was smeared from my face. Got back to the car just in time -- the owner opened up and wanted to know who his passengers were. I lied like a trooper and said they were my cousins up from Colton. As soon as he looked away they dashed out of there, cursing me. I was alone; I had the car, and my buck and a half to go to Denver. The attendant was grumbling away. I smiled at him and ran up to him. \u201cSorry, my car broke down back behind the wall.\u201d He didn't believe me. I made up a new story and said, \u201cHonest boys...", "1_23_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-five in the evening</span> and the place was the living room of a suite in the Taft Hotel, in Cincinnati, and the company was $FIRSTNAMEF0 Kinsolving, a child of five; a governess, Miss In man; the Reverend Canon, Nathaniel James Cramer, a child of three; an English nurse, Miss Zapp; William T. Kinsolving, a man of thirty-four; his brother, Justus, a man of thirty-two; their mother, Mrs. Loretta Kinsolving, a woman of fifty-two; $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, a man of thirty-eight; Benedict L. Direct, a man of thirty-three; Evelyn Waugh, a man of thirty-one; Mona A. Minton, a woman of twenty; and ask Mrs. Clyde Hendrick, a woman of thirty-two.\nAmong the cigarettes offered were Vulgaras, Turkish jades, Exo-Pre-Firolitos, Pedro Vicars, Cimequitos, pastel banditos, Murads, Turkish Fortes, and Acme smoked delights. Among the drinks offered were white, red, and pink Hister Lemonades, Nectar of the Gods, Schiedam sippers, Scotch highballs,...", "2_7_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-five in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 imagined were all things he had seen with his eyes, all things he had heard with his ears. He was held to those things by the steel-utenhanced senses of his condition.\n\"All right,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself, \"the time is now, and the place is here. The things I imagine I have seen with my eyes, have heard with my ears. I imagine that I lie in a small, automatically-heated cubicle in a barracks in a column of similar cubicles. I imagine twelve of my mates sleeping all around me. I imagine the skeleton of a tall, white building outside the window, and that the sun is going to come up in twenty-eight minutes. I imagine a faint autumn smell of apples out there. I imagine all this, hold it all in my head, by the strength of the senses my condition has given me.\"", "0_10_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty in the morning</span>, according to Disgusto\u2019s Bulova $FIRSTNAMEB2-meter.\nOut in the real world, ten twenty in the morning means:\nOne, the thing to work on right now is writing an IndustrialRevolution crib for Mrs. Ramstetter. Or, if you have the afternoon free, the summaries for my slides for the Annual Convention are getting a little disorganized, so maybe you ought to get at them.\nTwo, the thing to think about having for lunch today is getting something over at the Union Square Oyster House. You know, today is one of those special days when they make the oysters Rockefeller. Maybe you want to get over there early and\u2014\nThree, the thing that ought to be occupying your mind right this minute is the Annual Convention. You remember how you went over to Masters and Johnson\u2019s exhibit? What did you think? Are they the protagonists or the antagonists? It\u2019s almost time to get on with writing that long piece on what American Sexual Behavior has been like in the Post-Fitzgerald Period. You promised you\u2019d....\nFar out over the East River and Queens, the huge meat wagons from Iowa were making methanp from Jet-A again. The...", "1_10_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nI was the first customer of the shift.\nTHE SHORT DRIVE BACK TO THE EN MAR GOLDEN TURKEY INN WAS THE FIRST TIME I had been alone since I first read my friend Metzger\u2019s telegram late Wednesday evening. Suddenly I became very tired. My legs felt weak. I was glad the little MG was able to climb so well, since I had the top down almost all the way. I was glad Las Vegas was only thirty\u2014two degrees centigrade in late June.\nThis was the first time I ever felt the pure physical drain of what my ex\u2014wife used to call two\u2014anded thinking. I didn\u2019t dare handle any complex problems, like how much money to wager on the Kobler/Syphant fight in Akron\u2014Canton, or was the North African situation really going to affect the Neon situation in Vegas. I drove back through Boulder City thinking about old horseshit like gambling strategies, things like the fact that for the last three years I had been shading my bets on the third race at Aqueduct whenever the third race was a mile and a sixteenth. I wondered why this was, and concluded that the track must have been palming something hinky...", "1_14_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. This was July 4, 1976. It was very hot. I felt pretty good, considering the way the night before had ended. It was impossible to worry much about what was going to happen next. It was beyond anybody\u2019s control. My attorney and I were somewhere in the boondocks of the Strip, somewhere around Russell Road\u2014on the north slope, about eight miles from downtown Vegas. There was nothing around us but sand, sagebrush, pavement and super\u2014hot sunshine.\nWe had been driving on the desert for about an hour, looking for a?washroom, drinking beer and listening to the radio. The news came on and they mentioned my name: \u201c... escaped from County Hospital half an hour ago. Alert all units. Do not approach. The man is dangerous.\u201d\nI laughed. \u201cFooled\u2019em, hey, Dude?\"\n\u201cWe fooled \u2018em,\u201d said my attorney.\n\u201cHow far do you think we\u2019ll get?\u201d\n\u201cIt all depends,\u201d he said. \u201cI\u2019d say we have a very good chance.\u201d\n\u201cWhy?\u201d \u201cEasy,\u201d he said...", "2_4_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-two in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 closed the pressure-tight door of the space ship, carried the pail of champagne with him as he strode to the microphone. Mrs. $SURNAME0 leaned limply against the space ship, watched her husband impassively. No man and wife had ever been more coordinated in manner and countenance.\nThere before the microphone stood $SURNAME0, between his mate and his toboggan, between his chrono-synclastic infundibulated wife, between his chrono-synclastic infundibulum\u2014and a microphone from which nothing more than hollow, haunting echoes had ever issued. And $SURNAME0 was happy. He was obviously the master of ceremonies at the least desirable event of history.\n\"Friends of a kind,\" said $SURNAME0 balefully, \"I am here to wish you your very best\u2014whatever else happens. I am here to tell you that you have been treated this night and this morning to what is probably the most colorful, the most interesting, the most entertaining, and certainly the greatest display of diversity and free will the Solar System will ever know.", "0_2_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-three in the morning</span>. I had thirteen minutes to catch the middle-night Greyhound to LA -- two hundred miles across the mountains and the desert, rolling north in the sullen hum of locusts, through Flagstaff and Winslow, with heavy nods at the red lights of Sedona, past the Arizona State Prison, into California with its ominous rustlings of Modesto and Merced, on to the craggy foothills of the San Joaquin Valley, across the bridge again through Oakland and Berkeley, and down into the fantastic neon wilderness of downtown San Francisco in the weeAM hours of Sunday morning -- with fourteen hours in the bag, slammed down in the seat next to me, but only seven rides to go; and the problem at that very moment was to locate the seventh rider? I had ten minutes to catch him. We were parked right by the loading dock that led down into the steamy belly of the San Francisco underworld. Should I get out and seek the man? It was filthy, good only for toilet men. The train divided there. Half rolled back to LA, the other half rolled north. I didn't want to spend the rest of my life digging around in the toilet areas of commplete Greyhound stations for some bleak midnight ghost who might not...", "2_20_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"I\u2019d just as soon not have everything I own all at once,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"If you wouldn\u2019t mind waiting a while, I\u2019d appreciate it.\"\n\"I don\u2019t mind waiting,\" said Salo.\n\"Turn off that light, if you would,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe only light in the room was the reflection of the shoreline from the windows. But when the light was switched off, aside from the windows, the only illumination came from the panel of television lights directly over $FIRSTNAMEF1's bed.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 lay on the bed, with her eyes closed.\nA knocking came at the front door of the bungalow.\n\"Oh, dear,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThere was no longer any reason for her to delay her preparations to leave.\nShe left her bed, crossed the room on her stockinged feet, put on a mink coat over her gingham nightdress.\nShe unlocked the door, opened it a crack, peered out through the crack, withdrew her head. The room was still in semidarkness except for the television lights over the bed.", "2_7_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-six in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 imagined were all things he had seen with his eyes, or had heard with his ears. He was held to those things by the steel-utensil-enhanced senses of his condition.\n\"Good,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself. \"I am having a perfectly normal flight of imagination. I imagine I hear my mate talking in her sleep across the room.\"\nThe time was seven twenty-eight in the morning.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 imagined were all things he had seen with his eyes, or had heard with his ears. He was held to those things by the steel-utensil-enhanced senses of his condition.\n\"Aaah\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself, \"I imagine I hear my mate talking in her sleep across the room. This is very pleasant. I catch what she says. She tells me she loves me. I\u2014uh\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself, \"I am not quite certain what I imagine she looks like. I realize that my memory of her lacks precision.\"\nThe time was seven twenty-nine in the morning.", "0_0_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>. The $FIRSTNAMEB2-Ordnance garrison of Newport, Mars\u2014forty men\u2014were nestled in the red-tinted sand of the abandoned canal area on the western edge of town. Not one of them was thinking about what was supposed to happen at one o'clock. In the dull half-light of the Martian dawn, the soldiers were quiet, relaxed, at ease. They were busy in many different ways.\nThe trooper named Mudge was fixing a small piece of communications equipment in a valve-tormented Martian dawn. His partner, $FIRSTNAMEF1, was asleep under a thermal blanket.\nTrig Expand grinned at his tiny wristwatch, the only piece of gold on him.\nA trooper named $FIRSTNAMEB0 was polishing the chrome-stone trimmings on his pieces of Martian battle-clothing.\nA trooper named Stony was in an open-end space suit, fiddling with the air-processing system on the back of the suit.\nA trooper named $FIRSTNAMEB1 was obtaining a white ration from the food locker under their barracks.\nA trooper named Harold was fixing his face in front of a cracked hand mirror, rehearsing his role in the asexual reproductive cycle of the $FIRSTNAMEB2-Ordtiaugh garrison.\nA", "0_5_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nPerhaps, on this Thanksgiving morning, they were all thinking of the same thing: that their space ship, containing the cream of human culture and living, was racing through the Solar System at three miles a second\u2014whirling around the Sun and chopping off little anecdotes as it went.\nWho can tell what they thought, or if they thought?\nMaybe they didn\u2019t think at all. Maybe they slept through the night, and awoke knowing only that their ship, the Maxwell, and all it contained was child\u2019s play. The toy of bored, rich fools. And neither fools nor rich.\nIt would be very nice if that were the case, but $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s personal chronology indicates that he came on deck at twenty minutes before six, rather stiffly. He came on deck humming a little snatch of song.\nHe came on deck twenty minutes before six.\nThis means that he and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were both awake at five-thirty.\nThat in turn means that $FIRSTNAMEB4 was awake two hours earlier than the rest of the ship\u2019s company.\nNo one knows why $FIRSTNAMEB4 won\u2019t let himself be domesticated.\nSomeone should make a dog out of $FIRSTNAMEB4.", "1_2_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past two o'clock in the morning</span>. We had three hours to sleep before daylight. The wind was moaning. I pulled my comforter over my head and tried to sleep. In a few minutes I heard Terry remaking my bed. \u201cWha's matter?\u201d I said. \u201cCan't you sleep?\u201d\n\u201cI don't want to lie next to your dirty old underwear, that's all. Don't you ever wash? You must think I'm a pretty common girl, don't you, to sleep with you.\u201d\n\u201cYou're crazy,\u201d I said. \u201cGo to sleep and stop talking.\u201d\n\u201cAll right.\u201d\nI listened and then suddenly I thought I heard her crying, very low like a child.\nI got half an hour's sleep and woke up at three o'clock and saw the moonlight lying across the desert and the stars like Mexican silver dollars in the sky. It was as cool as fresh lettuce. The moonlight tapped on the little window.\nThe others were sleeping like dead men. I dressed and stepped out of the tent. The moon was uncanny and bright. The truck-afternoon moonlight on the worn desert made everything look like an old...", "2_20_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nBy the time half an hour had gone by, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the beautiful child were a man and woman in a bunkhouse filled with men and women in bunk beds. Everyone was well behaved, and announcements came over the public-address system at regular intervals. The announcements were made by a deep, religious voice that somehow reminded $FIRSTNAMEB0 of the voice he had heard coming out of his voyage cube while he buried his dead companion in Mars.\nThe deep, religious voice told $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his friends to bathe often in a solution of one part alcohol to ninety-nine parts water. The voice explained that the alcohol flensed the skin and kept it soft and porous and able to breathe easily. It warned that the healthier the skin breathed, the healthier the body would be. It said massages with liniments were a good idea. It advised $FIRSTNAMEB0 to memorize the multiplication table up to twelve and to beat his daughter when she failed to recite it forwards or backwards at command.", "0_21_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-seven in the evening</span>. $SURNAME1, feeling strange inside because of the operations and the disagreements with $FIRSTNAMEF0, felt at the same time strangely awake and alive. He felt safe. Unburdened. Free.\nThat night he went to bed feeling like the richest man in Gemini III\u2014the man who no longer had to prove anything to himself or to anyone else. The adventure of selling the space-flyer was behind him. He would sell no more space-flyers. He had enough money to live luxuriously for the rest of his life. He had enough money to give unusual gifts to his children when they became troublesome to him.\nIt was on that same night that $FIRSTNAMEF0 started fighting with $SURNAME0 again. She had started it at dinner by mentioning $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s name, and had been fighting with $SURNAME0 ever since.\n$SURNAME1 was getting into bed when her yelling downstairs began. He rubbed his cheek, trying to make some sense out of what $FIRSTNAMEF0 was yelling about. It didn\u2019t make sense.\n\"God damn you, win,\" she yelled, \"I won\u2019t sell the colors!\"\n\"What colors?\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice from downstairs.\n\"The colors you want me...", "2_15_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-two in the afternoon</span>.\n/c/\nPART 3\nTHE HOTEL CAIPHAS\n31 October\n10 November\n12 November\n15 November\n18 November\n18 November\n19 November\n27 November\n28 November\n3 December\n6 December\n8 December\n9 December\n11 December\n12 December\n12 December\n16 December\n18 December\n19 December\n19 December\n19 December\n19 December\n31 December\nPART 4\nHUNTING AND PICKING UP THE DEAD\n4 October\n4 October\n4 October/Epilogue\nAbout the Author\nOther Books by This Author\nAbout This E-Book\nAdditional Copyright Information and Legal\nViolation\na cognizance", "2_8_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 lay in a great mahogany bed in the master bedroom of his mansion in Newport, Mars.\nThe mansion gleamed in grained polychromes, its spires, finials, heraldic devices, and drain-spouts coated with a film of polychro-mized titanium dioxide.\nAbove the mansion were two elevated sidewalks, one for the walkers and one for the wheelers.\nIndeed, all of Newport was an elevated structure, since Newport was on the northwest corner of Mars, and the ground there had baked in the sun till it was a brick-hard plain only three feet above the zero-degree level of the Planet\u2019s oceans.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 left his bed, what he really did was to put his feet in a section of floor that hinged up, exposing a spiral iron staircase.\nHe descended the staircase to the great main hall.\nThe telekinesis was already whining.\nThe great main hall was 44 feet by 33 feet by 33 feet. It was paved in non-skid blocks of well-aged Vermont apple jack.", "0_0_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>, Galatic Standard Time, Tuesday, January the 2nd, 1998. Whatever calamitous thing that had happened to the Space Wanderer up to the momentous happening of this particular night had given him the faculty of remembering backward, of being able to recall everything that had happened to him backward, from the great balloon-bursting on Titan to this time eight minutes to one in the morning of January the 2nd, 1998.\nIronically, the Space Wanderer could not have remembered forward at all. He could remember nothing with perfect clarity more than a minute ahead of himself.\nIt was as if his inner being were fixed in a block of semiprecious stone, with minute fractures in the stone running through the block from the moment of his balloon-bursting at one end, to the moment of his pipe-smoking, lichen-sweeping on Titan at the other end.\nAny thoughts of the future on the part of the Space Wanderer were never clear, never sharply etched.\nThey were attenuated thoughts, composed of a tarry stuff not easily penetrated by light.\nFour hundred and three minutes remained in the life of the Space Wanderer-regardless of whether he had been born that Tuesday morning or the Monday before, regardless of whether...", "2_18_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-five in the evening</span>, Martian time.\nThat was Earthtime.\nThat was the evening of September thirteenth\u2014and while $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $SURNAME1 were consummating their love affair, $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were putting the screws on each other. And while the two love affairs were going at full heat and the two tormenting sessions were going at full steam, the war between the United States and Japan was reaching its peak of cold and distant fury.\nSince $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s adolescence, American strategists had recognized him as the most dangerous human being on Earth. As a child in the Second World War, he had done more damage to the Allied war effort than ten times the number of RDX bombs and heavy-water plants that Bombing and Gunning had.\nIn the Third World War and the German-Soviet War, $SURNAME1 had destroyed more Allied war potential than all of the Allied and Soviet combined\u2014with the sole exception of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME1 had wrecked more than civilization. He had wrecked the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent, had driven by his questions the Supreme Being into a state of dementia so profound that the Almighty had committed suicide.\n$SURNAME1 was still the most dangerous human being on Earth.", "0_4_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-nine in the morning</span> when I got home. I bought a pint of rum and half of whisky, and went to the tremendous hotel on North Main Street, where my love and I had lived so thickly, and where I once saw Remmen shot down in the lobby. It was all gone. The night clerk sneered when I asked for a room. \u201cEighty-six dollars a day, Mac. Your wife left you in the lurch, huh? Find another hotel.\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d I was afraid I was too drunk for him.\n\u201cBeat it, mister.\u201d He wouldn't take my money.\nI tried to make him understand, but he called the night manager.\nAt this moment the terrible orchestra of a hooker woke up the whole house with Geiger's \u201cOh, You Beautiful Doll.\u201d It was my girl friend, herself singing. She stopped only a moment when I came in. I had to listen to it two times more before I got my old room. I could see her in there alone, sleeping naked, saying nothing, just waiting for me. I lay in the dark. Babe, Where You Going Tonight? blasted out of the icebox and roared into my ears like...", "0_23_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-five in the evening</span> on Monday and I had twenty-three minutes before my shift began. I was watching the Saturdayofweekend Canadians go past outside the taxi-stand. Every now and then some poor devil would come limping in, look around, and go back out again. It was a torrid night.\nI cursed the appointment with myself that kept me from slinging my pack on the bed and running away from the hotel to the mountains and fishing. I had planned a big July in the Green Mountains. It was now Monday night and I was only half through this one.\nI laughed.\n\u201cWhat's funny, sir?\u201d asked Bob.\n\u201cI just had an idea.\u201d\n\u201cWhat was it, sir?\u201d\n\u201cNever mind.\u201d\nClara came in.\n\u201cI'm off, kid.\u201d\n\u201cYes, I know.\u201d\nWe chuckled silently at each other. She knew I had to go, but it was twenty-three minutes before I had to go. We knew each other well enough so that these things could be said without many explanations.\n\u201cHow much?\u201d she asked.\n\u201cTwo sixty-five.\u201d", "2_2_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-one in the morning</span>, and somebody was coming out the front door of 4245 Plaza Street. The somebody\u2019s shoes were made of thick cream-of-wool plaid wool. They also were huge and feet. The door slammed. The shoes began to walk up the street, making a soft laugh of leather soles on the sidewalk.\n4190 Plaza Street was a ranch house made of twisted cactus stumps and adobe brick. There was no walk leading up to it. The street stopped fifteen feet short of the house. Beyond 4245 Plaza, the street resumed its normal proportions, only a short block from the Shipley Do-Nut Shop.\nBy this time in the morning, not only had everybody on the street come out, everybody in the house in the middle of the street had come out, too\u2014except three. From 4245 Plaza Street came the smell of gin. From 4190 Plaza Street came the smell of cigarette smoke, particularly strong in the areas around the eyes.", "2_10_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-three in the morning</span> by the Solar Segment of the chronometer in $SURNAME0\u2019s suit.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 stood on the reproduction of a Cretan stone doorstep, facing the awful blank wall of a reproduction of Pal Joey\u2019s Gymnasium. The gymnasium wall was twenty feet thick. It represented forty-one yards of earth, stone, and concrete. It was not a mural\u2014it was an actual part of Mars. The basis for the model was a photograph taken from directly in front of the real gymnasium door.\n$SURNAME1\u2019s legs hurt. That was part of his punishment. His muscles had been shortened and weakened, so that even standing was an effort.\nHis knees were locked so that he had to stand with heels together, toes splayed. $SURNAME1 did not dare risk moving his feet, for fear he might collapse. His head still rang from the cuffing it had taken. $SURNAME1\u2019s ears were salted with blood from being boxed by $SURNAME0.\nIt had been almost twenty minutes since $SURNAME1 had exercised his right to ask questions. His head still rang, his knees still hurt, his feet were numb. It was hell, all right.", "2_15_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty in the afternoon</span>. The room was bright with soundless lightning from the computer screen.\n\"Go hug Daddy\u2014you want to hug Daddy?\" he said.\nWithout waiting for an answer, he swiveled the chair, folded his hands on his belly, and looked up into $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s eyes. \"Yes, you do,\" he said.\nThe dog went to him and put her forepaws on his thigh. He patted her, slipped an arm around her, and lifted her onto the desk beside him.\n\"What\u2019s the dog\u2019s name?\" he said. He was whispering now.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe man on the desk, who was the dog\u2019s legitim ate owner, laughed gently. \"Not $FIRSTNAMEF1,\" his said. \"You got a real name, baby?\" He turned the dog over. He unsnapped the belly flap. \"Come on, you can tell me.\" He put his ear to the dog\u2019s abdomen.\n\"What\u2019s his name?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.", "1_19_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-two in the evening</span> of November twenty-fourth. The place was a wooded area south and west of Douglas, Georgia\u2014 about ten miles. The wooded area was a part of the civilian cemetery wherein lies buried the Confederate rifleman, George D. Pruitt, who fell at the battle of Chickamauga, and wherein lie also the bodies of his son, who died in infancy, of his daughter, who died at fourteen, and of his wife, Henrietta, who died when she was thirty-six years of age.\nThe grave of Henrietta Pruitt, who died on June nineteenth, nineteen hundred and two, was marked by a marble angel, twenty-two inches high, with spread wings and an upturned face, and by a chiseled epitaph in sentimental vein, composed by the husband of her youth and sustained through life in her closing months by the devoted ministrations of her husband, of her son and of her daughter.\nThe little cross in front of the grave of the infant son of George and Henrietta Pruitt was done in a sleeker style, with an inscription of Scotch thrift.\nThere was, as a matter of fact, a lot of class distinction shown in the Pruitt family plot. The plots of George and Henrietta Pruitt were surrounded...", "1_21_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was a grouping of eight chairs in the lobby of the Sala Hotel on Saturn Avenue in Ebensburg, Pennsylvania. The eight chairs were in a kind of square. Now, at the beginning of the head-disorientation, only four of the eight chairs were occupied. The occupants of the four chairs had arrived singly, not together. They were Sheldon Anapol, the publisher of Zap; Otto Silenus, a poet and synaesthete who was co-editor of the magazine; a citizen named Guy Murchie, Jr., who was only a citizen; and Gene Daney, a redheaded poet and translator as well as poetry editor of Zap, who also worked in the combination office- storeroom at the plant. The name Zap was the name of a magazine that Anapol had invented, edited, published, and distributed all by himself, starting back in 1944. He had invented it in his head, as a part of his head. His head contained, along with much else, a saying: No one can invent anything new, but anyone can pollute something old until it\u2019s no longer recognizable. Although Anapol could not invent anything new, he perceived that there were hordes of people in...", "1_3_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-nine in the morning</span>, local time, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 was sitting on his bunk reading the Bible and smoking a home- rolled cigarette. He had the air- conditioning on high, and the cubicle was nice and chilly.\nThe walls of the cubicle were bare. One wall, behind $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s head, was a window. It looked out upon a large desk that was lighted by a single shaft of light from a lamp in the ceiling of the cubicle. The shaft of light ended in a circle of light on the floor of the cubicle.\nThe desk was occupied by a guard, Corporal Rudolf McNulty.\nRudolf was a tall, thin, good-looking, red-haired young man of twenty-three years. He wore glasses. He had the nickname Harvey because he reminded so many people of James Stewart in the movie Harvey. Rudolf did not know and had never heard of James Stewart. He had never seen the movie, but everyone assured him that he reminded them of James Stewart in the movie. He smiled whenever they told him that.\nHarvey was a college drop-out. He had not gone to college because he didn\u2019t like it. This year, he had been put into a silly job in...", "0_8_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-three in the morning</span> when the father reached over with his right hand and drew back the stiff plastic curtain dividing the cubicle from the pilot's compartment. The mother and the son were sleeping, heads down, and the snores were deep and loud. The father went by their seats and ported himself through the plastic curtain into the pilot\u2019s cubicle. The pilot was very busy. He was piloting the rocket ship in a very tricky atmosphere. He was talking to the automatic control system and holding the ship on course. The father would not bother him. The father sat down on the flat stool by the pilot's section. The pilot looked up and saw that it was a man. He said nothing, but took his feet off the controls. The ship swung right down and right up, right down and right up. The father said nothing, either. He closed his eyes, waited for the ship to stop pitching. It went on pitching, though. It made sixty-foot banks and began going in sawtooth spirals, with the engines screaming all the way. It was the most exciting thing that had happened since the father got into the rocket ship. Nothing could save the ship from sure disaster but some rudder-miracle. The pilot thought of that and did some lightning...", "1_11_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven eighteen in the morning</span>.\nAll the relevant parties in Group Green were installed in the Great Western Steak House on Wyoming Street Surrounded by the tremendous and incalculably valuable $SURNAME1 business district, the Great Western is a splendid glass-cased diner which, sadly enough, serves the worst food in Cleveland . It is noisy, filled with blue smoke, sizzling with fat, wildly overpriced and run by an ogre named Al who drinks case after case of Schlitz Malt Liquor in the course of any given day. Al doesn\u2019t care. He lives on a yacht.\nThe group consisted of Miss Inez Watching, Miss Alice Motor, Mr. $SURNAME2 Carnation, Mr. Leslie Tanger and Miss Fony Ultra. There were two other people at the table. One was a friend of Miss Motor\u2019s, and together they had just come back from a trip to Ashtabula , where they had spent a long, dreary morning buying entire closets full of bobby sox at a knock-down Ashtabula sale. The other was a t.p. rider whose acquaintance $SURNAME2 had made just before leaving the office, via a business call on the pay phone.\nThe riders had in their possession a...", "2_8_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-three in the morning</span>.\nThe name was $SURNAME2.\nThe ship was filthy.\nIt was not a whistler.\nThe man called $SURNAME2 did not care.\nOne Tuesday, ten years before, at the age of twenty-six, $SURNAME2 had bought the ship of his dreams.\nWith the money, he bought himself a ticket to Outer Space. He took advantage, before departure, of the arrangements the government had made for an all-expenses-paid honeymoon.\nA year later, the government began searching for $SURNAME2. They searched for him everywhere but in Outer Space.\nThe government sent agents to search the ship\u2014a year after it had been launched. The agents found $SURNAME2 in four hours. They found him having breakfast in a yurt in the woods of the Great Desert of Wyoming.\nThe agents suffered no ill effects from their search of the ship.\nBut the dream in which $SURNAME2 dwelt ended on the day of his arrest.\nFrom the moment he was arrested and the handcuffs snapped him on the wrist, $SURNAME2\u2019s dream ran plague and corruption.", "2_1_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-one in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and Stony had come down in the elevator to the basement to howl and spit in the face of their enemy. No howl, no spit, was forthcoming.\nThe ruins beyond the big windows were not illuminated by the basement lights. Such light as there was came from the basement itself, leaked through windows of crisscrossed-brown packing-tape. Outside, crowds surged, thumped, destroyed. The crowds made war against the house their father had given them.\nThe instrument that the tape recorder had been amplifying was a harmonium. A harmonium is a small organ with bellows and stops. It isn\u2019t supposed to be a very good organ.\nThe harmonium in the basement was a very good organ.\nA man named $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was playing it. He was a Martian privateer who had just completed officer\u2019s training at Fort IGP on the Moon. He had been trained at an accelerated pace, given an accelerated program, because he was supposed to be unusually intelligent, stable, dependable. He had been issued a copy of Sun Tzu\u2019s The Art of War for recruits, and was studying it for the first time.", "0_4_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-five in the morning</span>. I went up on the roof of another cheap hotel two blocks from the Flamingo. We sat for a long time watching the lights go out in the show-windows across the street. Downstairs, a radio bawled through the wall: I can\u2019t give you anything but love, baby. I finally drifted off to sleep. At eight o\u2019clock in the morning, somebody knocked on my door and said I had a long-distance phone call. It was Vincent. \u201cThings really started to happen around here last night,\u201d he said. \u201cI had five people shooting waterguns at each other and two marginals looting the buffet\u2014beats me what attracts them to the buffet. We took them out into the casino and explained to them the importance of exactToken Exchange. One of them peeked at the Manager\u2019s Special\u2014some kind of snapper, Man! It was in a laprobe envelope.\u201d He laughed. \u201cI thought I was going to have to call the cops. But I handled it.\u201d \u201cYou handled it fine,\u201d I said.\n\u201cNow me and Johnny Cuba are loading up on beer and waterguns and we\u2019re going out...", "1_7_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> (never once during the night had $FIRSTNAMEM1 looked at the beautiful hands of the wall-clock, which were now precisely level with the top of the picture) when Katharine Webb stood fair upon the bridge. She was wearing a siren suit of greenish-black, a hat of greenish-black, and white gym shoes, and she carried a bag of greenish-black with straps of white. She also held an Athletics Association umbrella of greenish-black with white stripes. At her side was a young white bull terrier of striking physique, onto the back of whose neck she clumsily dropped her hand, as she stepped onto the bridge. The dog's collar was aflash of gold, inscribed with his name and Banbury Cross Kennels. Beneath the name could be read.\nThe sun shed a noonday glare on the river. Three motor-boats roared up the Pool, their courses diverging, their backs scattering light. On the forepart of one a brave[7] girl was standing up steering. The water sparkled; the bricks, the flags, the very air seemed to share in the dazzling brilliance. The dome of the Ministry was as radiant as the sun. What one saw, indeed, was not the scene itself,...", "0_12_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past noon</span>. At exactly twelve o\u2019clock, $FIRSTNAMEF0 and Chick intend to invoke the name of their demon, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, with an invitation to come home to Earth. The invitation, according to Max, will open a cosmic waste basket. All the harmless ectoplasms that have been floating around in the Universe since Creation will drop out to nothingness.\nThey will, according to $FIRSTNAMEF0, leave a throne-room empty but for herself and Chiffon and Hepatitis, Initials Cigarette.\nThe ceremonies at $SURNAME0\u2019s tomb will begin at three minutes after one.\nTruncatula was not invited to the ceremonies. It will receive no apology for the murder of Mars.\nWhen the first space ship reaches Mars, Truncatula will be paved over and laid out in streets. A statue of Wilson will be put up. The statue will be of a man standing on a Truncatula root, holding aloft an infinitesimal Martian springpole.\nIt will bear this inscription:\nGOD GAVE US MAN\nAND MACHINES TO SUBDUE THE PLANETS,\nAND AN ANGEL TO TEACH US HOW.\nTHERE WAS LAUGHTER, WHEN\nWE G...", "0_17_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-seven in the afternoon</span>. The Space Wanderer stood before the counselors and their shipmates in the pink light of Martian dawn. One hundred fifty people looked through gills at the Space Wanderer. They were poised on a grassy hillside. They were arranged in a rough half-circle. Waldo, the spotted spoodle, lay at Norman\u2019s feet.\nThe beautiful green grass was a variety resistant to the dryness of the air. The grass sprang up through flagstones in a courtyard where the counselors and their mates were berthed. It sprang up through the flagstones in a courtyard of Building 5024c.\nThe green irrigated grass twinkled with space static, with motes of dust, with the restless personal insulation of fluttering field-linen coveralls. The people there, their hovering coveralls snapped and crackled with personal fields. They, too, were soiled with space static. Their eyes flickered and danced, adjusting to the brightness of the Martian dawn. The Space Wanderer gazed out at the brightening eyes. There was no wind, and sound carried far. The hiss of the desert, the morning sound of silence, persisted.\nThe shipmates reached out to the Space Wanderer with their hands and he...", "2_19_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-nine in the evening</span>.\nTo $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the gaunt and spacy-eyed young man tangled in the frame of his space suit, each minute that passed was a minute too long. He almost couldn\u2019t contain his impulse to motor bare-foot and in only black Calvin Klein briefs down to the mailing desk, to shove over the switch that would cause his personal rocket to drop through the trap door.\nHe had endured twenty minutes of the two-hundred-and-five-foot Unanimity Sphere with the hard-breathing, red-eyed, and altogether too well-informed Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0. Now $SURNAME1 had only ninety minutes to wait before he was shot into space.\nHe had endured five minutes with Dr. Maurice Vrumuggen, the famous and popular scientist who headed the Martian project to which $SURNAME1 had been assigned. Dr. Vrumuggen had fidgeted through his five minutes, not telling $SURNAME1 anything about the space ship the young man was to pilot, the purpose of the flight, or the length of time the flight was expected to take.", "0_8_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span> when she stood on the steps of the Library of Congress.\nShe looked up. Sure enough, there was the statue of the liberator. The truth was that she had never seen Jefferson before. What she had seen, from the bus window coming down, was the Washington Monument, grouped with elms and grass, a sober enough sight. There was otherwise no horizon whatever, except for the man-made horizon of low, dull buildings so characteristic of Washington\u2014uniform, pitted, congressionally approved, like cultured civic teeth.\nAnd now, on the steps of the Library, there loomed in fact, over the empty city, staring straight at her, the statue of Thomas Jefferson. His tie was pulled down and his hair was wild, and his face was\u2014oh God, thought $FIRSTNAMEF2, they used lead for his face, didn\u2019t they? She held up her hand, wincing. The wide, staring eyes, the Parnassus behind him, the hoar of centuries\u2014all gave her the spooky impression that he could only be trying to say something to her. But there was no sound.\nTHE LIBRARY OF CONGRESS, planned by Thomas Jefferson, was a great circular...", "1_7_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-four in the morning</span>, and I was sitting in my Buick on the little street behind the raffish casino called the Dune\u2014sprawled out on the back seat with my feet on the front seat and my head on a pile of papers and folders. Heat and blue air poured through the windows and the top of the car. Overhead, the wooden outlet of a water-main dangled with alligator lizards that had fallen from the iron palm trees and dried out.\nWell, all right, they were really guano\u2014desert lizards that live on the cactus in the lower regions of the Fun and the Pctl. But they look like alligators say the sight-seeing gentry and the chamber of commerce types and even, in their more gullible moments, the tourists. As a matter of fact, the only alligators in the Fun and Ptl. are kept in the casinos around the pool tables. I have never heard any tourist complain about that.\nI had been out on the salt flats, covering a short motor race for United Press International. There was nothing much to do at the end. The winner had already been judged. The manufacturers supplied both cars and drivers, and the only real suspense was whether the lead car would break down\u2014...", "1_4_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-eight in the morning</span>. I did not know then that this was the exact time of the rendezvous in the hotel suite across town where the American Dream conference had ended in flaming chaos. It was Monday morning, the second official day of the Las Vegas Posc convention.\nBy coincidence also it was Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement. As befit an atonement day, dawn broke dull and dusty over the silver skull on the desert. I parked the Shark and found the side door of the casino unlocked. The only lights were long white veils of sunlight drifting down through the Venetian blinds on the gambling tables.\nI seemed to be the only living soul in the casino at this hour. Baby Ray\u2019s booth was deserted. On the bar gridiron I found a little piece of paper: \u201cGone to get coffee. Back in fifteen minutes.\u201d I ordered a bottle of beer from the service bar and took it up to my office in the sports book. To my surprise I found a letter on my desk. It was from my attorney in La Coma.\nHere it is:\nDear Dr. Thompson:\nEnclosed please find bill for services rendered as attorney for the National Conference for Western National Journalism, here...", "2_20_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nWe are going to have to dig the whole garden up before it snows, thought $SURNAME1.\nA single bed was wheeled into the twenty-seventh-floor doctors\u2019 apartments. It was a miracle the bed lasted the trip, nobody at the wheel. Rota Cream squinted and slid over to one side of his seat, and the wheelchair shot through a wall.\nRandy, a country yearling, arrived at the hospital one minute ahead of his grandpapa, wheeled in by his grandmama. He was nauseated, dizzy, and vastly confused by the ceiling revolving like a lollipop inside a snowstorm.\nA nurse pinched him. She pinched him again. There was no response. She did not pinch; she clawed. \"Wake up,\" she commanded. \"Come on out of it.\"\nRandy looked up from the whirlpool floor. Some crazy fat woman was very frantic and trying to claw him to pieces.\nThere was a croaking sound from his throat. He slowed down the revolutions of his eyeballs. The fat woman stopped clawing. \"Are you coming around?\" she asked quietly.", "2_18_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>, November 4, 1975. It was three minutes short of the time when the time-wall between the decades would fall, and when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 would appear in the chrono-synclastic infundibulum.\nA band of pigeons was strutting on the palace\u2019s wall. Those were the only living things, except for the two humans, within two hundred yards in any direction. The pigeons, too, were part of the camouflage that Kitsy had organized. $SURNAME0 and his wife had threatened the town with a three-day flood if any citizen so much as fed the birds.\nThe pigeon strut was a ritual. The birds arrived from who-knew-where precisely at six o\u2019clock in the evening, and departed with equal punctuality.\nOld Salo, the faithful servant, had retired to the little cave he called home, halfway down the slope of the hill. From his cranny a torch flickered fitfully. Old Salo did not bathe, did not change his clothes. He had no more emotions than a lizard.\nHe, too, was a part of the camouflage.\nThree minutes to go, thought $SURNAME0.", "1_14_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-three in the afternoon</span> when my attorney finally came up to the booth with the drinks and the paper. \u201cHere\u2019s something that might amuse you,\u201d he said, sitting down. \u201cBut first I want to ask you a serious question: Can you handle living alone, or do you need somebody around all the time to cope with things?\"\n\u201cHell,\u201d I said, \u201ca man of my mental and emotional prowness can handle living alone just fine. But what the fuck kind of question is that, anyway?\u201c\u2018Your Aunt Helen\u2019s very worried about you,\u201d he said. \u201cShe says you won\u2019t talk to anybody.\u201d I shrugged. \u201cThat\u2019s my whole angle,\u201d I said. \u201cI\u2019ve withdrawn from the human scene and I don\u2019t give a fuck what happens to anybody.\"\n\u201cThat poor woman is frantic,\u201d he said. \u201cShe says there\u2019s something terribly wrong with your mind.\u201d I picked up the envelope, opened it and drew out her letter: Dear Nephew:\nYour attorney called and told m me you were in bad shape, drinking very heavily and acting...", "1_22_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt that precise moment, Old Salazan, the so-called king of the Moon men, received another message from $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was reporting that the most astonishing thing he had ever seen had just happened to him and his two comrades.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 stated that he, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and Stony Stevenson were no longer human beings.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 reported that the three of them now thought and acted, not as three individuals, but as one organism.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 begged old Salazan to come as quickly as possible to the little iron house by the iron elephant. $FIRSTNAMEB1 said old Salazan would not believe what was happening inside the house. $FIRSTNAMEB1 said something very wonderful was about to happen.\nAn Observer, arriving with his camera at the Observatory on Mercury at that instant, would have seen President Smart emerge from the little iron house by the iron elephant. The President was smoking a cigar with a diamond band.\nAnd old Salazan would have cried out when he heard the distant sound of approaching pom-poms.\nThe air was full of the stomping of ten thousand police girls\u2019 boots.\nThe girls came marching up Church Street, formed ranks in front of the Town Hall, broke ranks,...", "2_5_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty in the morning</span>. I remembered the postcard telling me it was the hour of the locusts. One hundred and twenty minutes to go downtown, straight down Route 41. Was Route 41 the road to Boston? I had a book to deliver to Mugsy's Used Books in Chicago. I had taken a postcard from Hartford, Connecticut, to prove my nut\u2014coolness. I planned to walk back to Indiana University; stop a while with my brother in Terra Haute, make my way through the corn and the weather to the University of Iowa Writers' Workshop hay\u2014loft; then catch a featherbed ride to New York in November. My family trusted me to do this. I had three hundred and forty-four dollars in the bank; my mother had inherited a few thousand; my dad mailed a dime to me every Friday.\nIt was five forty-five in the mad-making morning, seventeen miles from downtown Chicago, and the hour of the locusts. It was too early to be bitter.\nTRUCKEE, NEV.", "0_4_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nFlash Gordon, splendid and unconquerable space adventurer, was so loaded with liquor, opium and bennie that he couldn't walk, talk or think. His garments, all of gaudy madras, were crammed into a rocket locker in the bedroom of his Shan Fan palace. He couldn't even stay awake.\nAt the top of the stairs, directly over the bedroom, was his desk, with its contour chair, where he could always stay awake and watch for trouble on the televiewers.\nNothing in the way of trouble could be happening that stairway now. Not with Flash out cold and stretched on his belly like a dead tiger, on the bottom steps.\nEven in that position he was magnificent. Even there he bristled with dignity and heroic somnolence. One big arm reached limply down over the first step, like a great tiger tail. One heavily ringed hand rested palm down, with five jeweled fingers spread out like a fan. That was all. But it was majestic, and many furtive smokers of the sacred drug Kwinto revered that hand and that arm and those rings and that crazy yellow mane as the living, symbolic soul of the palace.\nOutside, on a lawn of Shan...", "1_5_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span>, Old Earth time. There would be exactly three minutes before $SURNAME0\u2019s image would appear again.\n$SURNAME0 laid a finger to his lips. \"Shhhh,\" he said.\nMistress calls the thrush! cried $SURNAME0\u2019s image.\nBeatitude flowed over the scene. The mist was so thick and the light so weak that Martian blue seemed meant for these elements, formed them into something like a stained glass window in an old church.\nThe thrush flew into view again, returned to the overgrown track where minutes before the false Mercury had spoken to it. It sang again.\nMistress song answered $SURNAME0\u2019s image.\nA glider appeared in the mist. It was a one-man affair, carried by a singing man. He was clean-shaven, about forty, with the muscles of a horse.\nHe let his flying machine come to rest on its outboard motor in the middle of the ripped track. He let the motor die.\nThere wasn\u2019t a sound in $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s bathroom.\nThe man in the glider was standing in a patch of ruddy light that was fed by a portable reflector that he held before him...", "2_10_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 and Gale, speaking and looking neither at one another nor at the Space Wanderer, busied themselves with last-minute details. Everything was in the gold truck\u2014food, flashlights, books, hooded rain hats, raincoats, cedar plugs, games, a sewing kit for $FIRSTNAMEF0, a pint of moonshine, and a supply of tobacco for $SURNAME1 and $SURNAME0. The cigarettes in theirockets were the only signs the travelers had that they were not members of a chain gang.\nSince there was so little in the Universe to blame for the condition that had been visited on them, they blamed each other. $SURNAME1 blamed $SURNAME0, $SURNAME0 blamed $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 blamed Gale, and Gale blamed $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nThe only thing they didn\u2019t blame each other for was the thing they all secretly agreed had happened to them\u2014the inexpressible greatness and beauty of what had happened to them.\n\"Good-by,\" said $SURNAME0. He held out his hand to $SURNAME1.", "2_4_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-one in the morning</span>, and Moonwater had, for the moment at least, quite forgotten her son and her son\u2019s disastrous adventures with the lovely, magical $FIRSTNAMEF0 Gunn.\nMars had an atmosphere at $FIRSTNAMEB2, an hour before dawn, that was almost Earthling. There was a comforting breeze and an intelligent, changing light.\nMars seemed a kind and peaceful place, like a seaside village.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 and his wretched iron spear ship were hardly visible at all, now.\nQUIB\nIT WAS NOT HAPPY NEWS that Minerva Soo Strok phonographed Moonwater.\nExpecting another message from $FIRSTNAMEB2, Moonwater recognized Minerva\u2019s voice when she heard it. She received the following message from the System\u2019s top educator:\n\"The people you are going to encounter are not, with few exceptions, stupid, my dear Moonwater. They are not, with a few exceptions, dangerous. It is a pity for them that they will not get to know you as I do. You are a saint, Moonwater\u2014but I know that you cannot see that about yourself, for you will not recognize what a saint is.", "2_18_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-eight in the evening</span> on the planet Mercury, or Hell.\nOn Mercury, the sun had just set. On Mercury, the sun would not be visible again for the next twenty-three days. On Mercury, twilight would last another twenty-three days.\nThere was nothing soothing or evolutionary about the long twilight on Mercury. The planet faced a desert of sunlight for eighty thousand days out of every hundred thousand Earth-years. The planet faced a sun like a bright brass globule for another twelve thousand days. The planet faced night only twenty days each hundred thousand Earth-years.\nWhen night did fall on Mercury, the stars were bright, and the red planet, Mars, strode majestically through the heavens in the behavior of the afternoon.\nThe only climate on Mercury was a temperate climate.\nIt was never hot, never cold.\nThe tallest mountains of Mercury reached seven miles into space\u2014unholy, ghastly basketball courts where the undead Celtics, forever young, forever skilled, played their practiced shuffles, fake-outs, and jumpers to the bleachers of Olympus.\nThe only water on Mercury was frozen. The only weather was a dry wind, a dry rain.\nMercury was hollow.", "1_15_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span> on October 1 of the year 1992 A.D., and the place was the living room of the $SURNAME0 estate in Newport, Rhode Island.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s crowd of one thousand followers was congregated on the grounds outside the living room. They were waiting for the doors to the living room to open. The doors were to open in one minute to admit the first two hundred of the crowd.\nOne thousand followers gave a good impression at the gates\u2014but inside, the house was jammed to suffocation. People annoyed the servants by spilling water from plugholes, tore paintings off the walls, broke Venetian mirrors by estimating the angles of flight of basketballsmooth spheres weights five pounds apiece. People carried their skirts between their teeth so as to have both hands free to tear out books from the shelves and throw bridge tables out the windows.\nThe people who did all this were frighteningly like ebony marbles. They were the followers of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, the new owner of the estate.\nThe interplanetary spacecraft Mercury, named GP, owned by $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had gone through its red warning light three seconds before the time of which this paragraph spoke. There was not a soul in the solar system who did not know by this time...", "1_20_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight sixteen in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was on duty. He had nothing to do until eight thirty. There was only one thing left for $FIRSTNAMEB2 to do, and that was stamp the pieces of the Earth that $SURNAME0 had cut out.\nOf course, $FIRSTNAMEB2 would not understand this ceremony. He would think he was just making fancy stamps. He would think the pieces of Earth that $SURNAME0 had given him to stamp were just pieces of driftwood that $SURNAME0 had picked up on Mars.\nWhen Pewley Sisser got home from work, his wife and his son Skip were both gone.\n\"Alice?\" said Sisser. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was creeping into the barrel.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 froze.\n\"Alice?\" said Sisser again. He closed the lid over the barrel of Time, concealed it again under the dunnage. Then he stepped into the shadows.\n\"W-w-where\u2019s my wife?\" said Sisser.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 pressed his face against the thin wall of the machine shop. The wall was cold\u2014ghastly in the starlight.\n\"Your son, too?\" said $SURNAME0. It was not really a question. Sisser nodded, unable to speak.", "2_7_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven nineteen in the morning</span>. The homeopape carried a sensation\u2014picture under a headline: \"BEATRICE WHILE SHOPPING!\u201c\nThe whole Earth knew which picture it was. It had been telecast half an hour before.\nIt showed $FIRSTNAMEF0 Ekstrom getting out of her motorized wheelchair and being helped into a chair in Filby\u2019s Hardware Store. It showed $FIRSTNAMEF0 talking to the manager about a ball-peen hammer. It showed $FIRSTNAMEF0 smiling and biting her lip, showing the golden tooth. The expression on her face was yearning, pathetic.\nThere was a caption\u2014a guess, since Filby\u2019s hadn\u2019t stayed open long enough after the scene to develop anything. The caption said: \"The mother of Norman Earl, the nineteen-year-old Titanian who allegedly assassinated Queen Aphrodite II of Titan, looks for a new ball-peen hammer. Photo by Leroy McGovern.\"", "0_9_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-five in the morning</span>. It was Tuesday.\nIn the midst of these thoughts which were not pleasant, $SURNAME1 emerged from the great door, in the company of a maid carrying a chair.\nThe chair was for the little woman, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME1 and the maid were nervously parading before the great door in order to find $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. $SURNAME1 was holding a gold tray on which was a tiny watch that he was to give to $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 so that she could keep track of the minutes until her husband\u2019s return. The watch was an inch in diameter and was waterproof.\nThough circumstances had led $SURNAME1 to believe that he was being offered a means of seducing $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, a means so cunning that $SURNAME0 had probably used it many times himself, $SURNAME1 did not, after all, relish playing the role of ladykiller.\nHe felt ridiculous and empty, and his comment on the watch looked understandably puny beside the watch itself and the relationship of watch to specified user. \"I guess you can tell the time pretty good in the water, too,\" he said.\n\"Air and water both?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0. \"I never owned a watch that could do that before.\" She...", "2_20_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-seven in the evening</span>.\n\"Good,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"What-what do I do now?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nThe perfect Californian rocking chair creaked. \"You\u2019ll know,\" said $SURNAME0, \"when the time comes.\"\nFoop! went the station wagon.\nFoop! went the plane in its hangar outside.\nThe time was eight twenty-eight in the evening.\nGood-by, $FIRSTNAMEF0. It has been exciting to play with you. God bless you.\nIf you should ever be on Mars again, look up Sun- name of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, of Hollywood, California. He is a man of great charm, just like his father.\n< 5 >\nMartians Abroad\nA telephone in a Fifth Avenue mansion in New York City was thumping and crackling, demanding attention. The mansion was in the Sutton Place district, the telephone was in a small, mauve drawing room that Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 referred to as the Moon Room. It was furnished in Edwardian antiques.", "0_11_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. I was through with capitalist sin and ethical transferences, and I was ready to face the music. I decided to start out immediately for the narcotics bureau of the district attorney's office and negotiate a treaty before they burned down the building. I thought I might come up with an arrangement whereby I would agree to abandon Narcotics as a special police project, on Condition that the D.A. would order a blackout on all press releases about drugs for, say, two years.\nJeff stood up. \u201cLet's mix another drink and get out of here,\u201d he said. \u201cWe've got to be sure to get back to Mel's at exactly noon, or we might lose our reservations.\"\n\u201cI've already lost all my reservations,\u201d I said. \u201cYou go on, and I'll try to make mine later on.\"\n\u201cNo, we'll all go,\u201d said Roy. \u201cIt will be an education for us to see what turns up at the narcotics squad.\"\n\u201cHeh, heh, heh,\u201d said Galatea. \u201cWhy don't we invite the Vice Squad along, too? Let's give them a big spread.\u201d We mixed the...", "0_8_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-seven in the morning</span>.\nAt that moment, Salo, the messenger from Tralfamadore, was one hundred and forty-seven thousand, six hundred and seventy-two miles from the aircraft carrier.\nThe carrier turned sharply north, the better to bite into the flank of the approaching missile.\nThe carrier was not graceful. It was a blunt, plodding behemoth.\nWhen it moved slowly, as now, its motion was ponderous, like a glacier.\nWhen it moved quickly, as it had to at Panama, the movement was merely violent.\nIt turned and fled now like a coward. Before its occupants could be killed or injured by a nuclear detonation on board, it fled to a region where there would be less danger of such a detonation.\nThe region was no less dangerous. It was an area of mutual massacre for ships and generating projectiles.\nAt eight thirty-nine, the ship went to full power again and fled at forty knots from the missile, whose range kept increasing all the time.\nAt eight fifty-two, when the range was two hundred and thirteen thousand miles, the ship put on more steam, hunched its shoulders, protruded its jaw, and bored straight into the missile\u2019s path.", "1_7_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nAt four minutes before eight in the evening, $FIRSTNAMEB1 awoke to find himself in the airlock of Stony Stevenson\u2019s secret ship.\nHe didn\u2019t remember being transported to the ship. He was frightened.\nHis space suit was punctured in twelve places.\nHis spacesuit wasn\u2019t insulated against the possibility of vacuum.\nThe punctures were bloodless.\nThin, colorless liquid was oozing out of the holes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 shivered in the chill of the airless ship.\nHe quickly twisted shut the two shutoff valves in his feed line, one to each thigh. He twisted the valves the wrong way, cramping the circulation in his feet and calves.\nOtherwise, the valve-twisting didn\u2019t help. There was no way for the man to control the flow of liquid through the punctures.\nThere hadn\u2019t been time for a proper repair job by authorized personnel. Nobody had authorized $FIRSTNAMEB1 to do any repair work at all.\nHe tore at his punctured space suit.\nThe hands of the man were slow and stiff. His fingers were cold and dead.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 found two holes in the lower abdomen of his suit. The liquid was...", "0_5_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-five in the morning</span>. The explosion had gone off perfectly. It was such a lovely morning that I wished Skulls and Maggadoe were around to see it. I had measured the exact amount of Tim Gray 's chemical, and though it wasn\u2019t an absolute cinch, I felt safe predicting a catastrophic event to the second\u2014to within a margin of error of three hundredths of a second.\nThe explosion occurred on cue, with the rising of the sun but not drowned out by dawn chorus. It was awesomely loud on the dark side of the Flacksteams\u2019 fence, within a few yards of my own bedroom window.\nIt rattled the house.\nAnd this thu desmondo wuz: The Arizona plant actually jumped a half inch into the air and settled slowly back down three feet to the right.\nThe windows blew out of the basement! The doors were blown off their hinges.\nThe water in a swimming pool knifed to the surface, then blew out of the pool as a fine spray.\nAir escaped with a roar from a dozen pipes; water escaped with a roar from a dozen pipes. Chaos broke out everywhere.\nI heard shouts, screams, clangs and bangs from thousands of doors and windows opening...", "0_16_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nWhen the chronometer showed that the real had been gone for a whole minute, $SURNAME0 opened the master grip of the materialization machine again.\nThe materialization chamber was black again\u2014and empty. A small breeze came in through the walls to leach the pressure from the pent-up air in the materialization chamber, to clear it of the smells of burning and ozone.\nA boulder, weighing fifty metric tons, was now cut loose from the side of Mt. $SURNAME0, poised above ten meters to the west of the materialization chamber.\nJust before the great earthquake that would rearrange the beaches and the seascape to the west of Mooney\u2019s Bay, the boulder would fall onto the materialization chamber, would sink it beneath rubble.\nThe boulder would lie where the house had been for the next few thousand years. And, after the thousands of years, the rising sea level would put the low-water mark a hundred meters from where the house had been.\nThe sea would then erode a small valley in the sand and gravel beneath the low-water mark, would round the sharp edges, would cover over the rubble.\nThere would then be nothing to show where the house had stood.\nD", "1_8_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span> on Wednesday.\nTen such parties as the $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 party had paid ten thousand dollars each for what the company was pleased to call \u201ca religious and philosophical interment service\u201d at themalachiappendix mausoleum in the Putnam Cemetery.\nOthers buried in the cemetery had paid ten thousand dollars each for a crypt or vault.\nOnly fourteen of the fifty-six gravesites in the Putman Cemetery were pre-paid. Over the question of whether or not to sell pre-paid graves, the Reverend $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, S.B., Ph.D., Th.D., L.H.D., had been split right down the middle. A majority of Stony Stevenson's directors felt that a Christian cemetery should be out of bounds to any man who made his living by taking life\u2014of either people or of corporations. A minority of Stony Stevenson\u2019s directors, which included the Reverend $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, felt that with the money to be made from sales, Putnam Cemetery could do all the good works that any Christian cemetery had the right to\u2014and more.\nThe minority lived to regret their opinion. Then, before the misery of their position could touch what was left of their consciences, they died...", "1_13_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. In two minutes, the group of brave people would walk out onto the white sand that stretched below the ragged blue wall of glass.\nThey would remember the brave words Synenlith Anemone B, Entropy College class of '29, had spoken here thirty-four years before. They would remember his slow counting.\nAnd they would begin to count in unison.\nAs the auditorium clock struck two, the groups of citizens outside silently counted to ten. Then they walked onto the hot white sand.\nAs the second hand of the auditorium clock ticked empty, Chief of Police O\u2019Mally counted to ten. Then he walked out toward the transparent wall.\nHis men followed him.\nAs the counterevent was beginning outside, a rumor began to pass quietly from man to man inside the spaceport. The rumor was this: The ship from Titan had already landed. A creature from another world was already among them.\nTHE TENTH INACCURATE ARTICLE ABOUT THE DUNRAP WILDERNESS EXPERIMENT OR: WHAT CAN BE DONE WITH TWENTY-FOUR GUARDS, FOUR TRIPS, SOME BEES, AND A SNOW CREAM CONE M...", "0_3_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty in the morning</span>. We wound down the hill to the shopping-strip where $FIRSTNAMEB3 had parked his Olds in front of a diner; he wanted to eat again. I was all for cutting on down to Mexico, but he said, \u201cNo! No! I want to eat!\u201d We went in. All the bugs ofHowever, we managed to eat some biscuits and gravy and drink some coffee. The counterman was a kind, gray, all-night soul; he looked as though he could have stood for all our troubles without batting an eye. Strange to say, all the buses for the South pulled out from this point. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was just zonked and crashed; it was a thing to see how he struggled to get his money out to pay for our insanely augmented breakfast. The counterman looked at him and his sad change with a weary and kind smile; he accepted whatever money $FIRSTNAMEB3 handed him; he didn't even make change. In a daze $FIRSTNAMEB3 reeled out to look for his sinister warship. I took a last look at the grimy market streets, the shacks, the railroad tracks, and followed him. \u201cWell, man, what are you going to do?\u201d\n\u201cWhat you going to do?\u201d", "1_8_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-two in the morning</span> of November 6, 1962. The place was the end of an island airstrip called by the natives Kamakiri\u2014A small man stood there, on the verge of the sharp cliff, waiting for a longboat, shuffling his feet to keep them comfortable in his grass sandals. High clouds were motionless, becalmed. Below, the waves of the South Pacific thundered on lava boulders the size of houses. A spray of sea water blew up now and again in the face of the man from the volcano underneath. No vegetation could possibly exist on that cindery, cratered moonscape down there; the top of a slimwa tree waved helplessly miles away.\nThe man waiting for the longboat wore the flowing lava\u2014lava hat of his island; he wore too a blue dressing gown, a long kerchief of pink silk, and a heavy yarakum watch and chain. His face, deeply seamed with tiny wrinkles, was noble, proud, warm, and kind. He had worked hard all his life. Now he was doing nothing. Or almost nothing. He was waiting for a longboat.\nHe hunched his horned, noble lava head closer into the wind and sniffed the air, thinking again that...", "0_7_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-seven in the morning</span>. I filed all the stuff in my dispatch case and wheeled the suitcase into the kitchen. I put a big stack of plates into the dishwasher. I toasted two pieces of bread, cut them into strips and put them in the broiler. I put a frozen, thawed can of Carnation Creamed Cream into the blender, poured in a dangerous quantity of gin and held the thing tilted until the drink was white and frothy. Then I switched on the blender and took the first, weak drink and poured the rest into the glass pitcher to be fixed fast. Then I made the Bacon Berry Cordial, squeezing a small bottle of Berry Brothers All-Purpose Blackberry Cordial and adding a few slices of frozen chopped bacon, plus some ice cubes and a shot of gin from the freezer. I shouted at the children as I worked, and they watched me with deep suspicion.\n\u201cDoesn\u2019t Daddy look happy this morning?\u201d screamed their mother. \u201cDaddy\u2019s going to get rich.\u201d\n\u201cWhat are you making that awful noise for?\u201d my wife inquired. \u201cWho\u2019s going to get rich?\"\n\u201cNever you mind.\u201d\nThe fireplace was roaring...", "0_23_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to midnight</span>. Outside the hall, the lamp-lit lawn spread somnolent in the fine rain under the warm wash of the night sky. A single rocket went up.\nAll the guests seemed to be grouped on the wide stone stair leading to the front door.\nEach one was given a small package wrapped in fine silk.\nThe packages contained finely framed photographs of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, the space explorer and his friend, The Man Who Arrived Last. The photographs showed $FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 cheerfully carrying their letter boards\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB1 writing on his that he would like to be a real singer-artist one day very much, $FIRSTNAMEB0 writing on his that he simply didn\u2019t know.\nThere were Biblical quotations on some of the photographs. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s quotation was from Ecclesiastes: There is a time for every purpose under heaven.\nContrary to all custom, the actual recipients of the gifts weren\u2019t the guests of honor. The gifts were to commemorate a time when the quiet world had been stirred by real celebration and the giving of gifts.\nOne of the real recipients was unconscious and strapped to a stretcher\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s friend, the harmonium player $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.", "2_23_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe pilot was Douglas M. Campbell, a test-pilot for the G.M. Aircraft Company, who at that time was flying a TWA Super Constellation on its regular run between San Francisco and Idlewild. He had left San Francisco at four o\u2019clock that afternoon, and his flight plan to Idlewild called for him to fly across the Sierras, then south to Reno and Benton and Cedar City and Overfield, Utah\u2014from there southeast over Salina and striking the Thirteen-Fifty Standard Vectors over Fort Apache and Rattlesnake Buttes and following them down to the Interstate 30 cut off at Nephi, and then cut across to the Provo Vectors, touching them down at Provo, then up the Provo Vectors to Orem, Hatch and Spanish Fork, then northwest along the Twenty-Zero Standard Vectors to the goose cut-off at the southern end of the Great Salt Lake\u2014from there straight north along the Zero-Zero Vectors to the IP at Goose Bay, and from there on up the 3.2 Fuselage Lengthwise Vectors to Idlewild.", "1_4_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-nine in the morning</span>. The sun was directly overhead.\nThe ship was falling directly toward the Sun. Hellish temperatures would begin shortly\u2014at five o\u2019clock, when the Sun was directly overhead.\nThe coordinates of Earth on the television screen were unchanged. The Earth was still almost exactly forty-three million miles away.\nNo one could imagine what had become of $SURNAME0 and his chrono-synclastic infundibulated war-mate $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\nThe terrible truth was that they had never really left Newport, in an Earthling ship. They had taken Walt Mahler\u2019s space ship, leaving Mahler and an Earthling deputy in the lower atmosphere in a imitation space ship made of aluminum foil and window-cleaner fluid.\nMahler and his deputy had just died in an unfortunate accident involving batteries and a small child.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were now, at this very moment, pursuing an acrobatic ribbon of amorous play among the azure-lidded 860-realgar crystals of Mercury\u2019s crowding south pole craters.\nUnquestionably they were the two most glorious creatures ever to cheapen all the poverty and terror of the Universe with their amorous play.", "2_11_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until noon</span>.\nThe baby buried its head in its mother\u2019s bosom.\nThe mother put her arms around the baby and rocked it gently. She thought the baby was frightened by the riotous behavior of the people around the table. The truth was that the baby was laughing silently \u2014 laughing at a private joke that no one on Titan but the baby would ever understand.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had a question for $FIRSTNAMEF0. He thought he would never have another chance to ask it. \"When I came back\u2014that time\u2014was I\u2014was I funny?\" he said.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was having trouble with her eyes. Through a willfulness of tunnel vision, she was trying to make $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s crazy old space suit the most interesting thing in the Universe. She wouldn\u2019t look at $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Was I funny?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 again. \"I mean when I came back, after I\u2014uh\u2014\" he said, \"uh\u2014\" and couldn\u2019t say it, \"after I got left on the Earth. Was I funny, or did you think I was crazy?\"\n\"You were d-d-different,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I didn\u2019t know what to think.\"", "0_7_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to eight o'clock in the morning</span>. It was Tuesday, August 15, 1977. The place was the lobby of the Space Wanderer Hotel. The event was a single bloodcurdling scream. It came from the floor above. Then it came again: a scream of rage, a scream of consummate fury. Then more screams from strangers in fear and despair. And finally came a roar from a hollow tin horn:\nThere was a great blast in the ceiling of the lobby. Chunks of plastered ceiling went flying everywhere. Wood and plaster rained down on the tile floor.\nThe great blast in the lobby ceiling was no surprise to anyone. Eleven persons had expected something of the sort. The chief of these was a huge man in green shorts and nothing else. His feet were bare. He was named Thurlow Pal claimed to be six feet nine inches tall, and 190 pounds. His eyes were blue and he was fifty-eight years old.\nHe stood in the lobby with his fists on his hips, surveying the damage. The most noticeable thing about him, in the grisly light of what was coming through the new opening in the ceiling, was his cranium\u2014a hairless white melon with ears a yard long.\nPal bent over and picked up a...", "1_10_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten thirty-five in the morning</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife were already dead. They had been killed in a chrono-synclastic infundibulated accident aregarding the factual details of which rumors contended, and to which changes in the state of the evidence were still being confidently attributed by the public documents.\nThis means that the material above was being reported, debated, and taken as literal fact in good faith and without conspiracy by just about everyone in the Solar System.\nThere were, of course, subhuman creatures and super-intelligent serfs who were capable of the sin of cynicism, who actually knew that the supposedly factual events were the purest illusion. But their numbers were so negligible as to be statistically unimportant. Since $SURNAME0 and his wife were not native Earthlings, the material above would not be defamation of their character or reputation, nor an invasion of their privacy. Since $SURNAME0 and his wife were not citizens of the Solar System or previously known space, they could not invoke rights safeguarded by the Freedom of Information Act. And since they were not persons under the Constitution of the United States, they could not file a suit against this publisher. The material above, in other words, is accurate and legal to...", "0_20_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span> of Wednesday, October 18, 1958, and the place was War Surplus Navy Yard, Fortress Monroe, Virginia. The dustcarts were out. So was the yard police helicopter. So, somehow or other, was I. The hot sun shone through the dust of the dry fall day, and every breath I drew of the thick, muggish air in the yard was like suffering the lung dreams of opium.\nI WAS A POOR SOLDIER ALMOST FROM THE START. My war ended after only forty-eight hours, and I spent the rest of my military commitment scuffling around the provost marshal's dingy offices with interminable put-down papers that ran through the bloody meatgrinder of military red tape.\nAt the peak of World War II we had 130 million people in this land, and almost twelve million of them were in military service. Of these twelve million people in uniform, only 174 were courtmartialed. The crime that brought most of the soldiers before the bar of military justice was murder, with 109 persons involved. Desertion followed, with eightyseven; rape, fifty-seven; robbery, burglary, and larceny rounded out the list of military crimes.\nI got out six years...", "0_4_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-six in the morning</span>. We heard another flop at the door of Room 214 and knew that the boy with the red shirt had returned for one last communion with his ray gun. We also heard the breakfast gong; we just made it across the road to the dining room. As we stood in line to receive our orange juice and egg dishes, the boy with the red shirt passed us, looking very much in need of the support he was getting from a big farmer type holding each of his elbows.\nOn my third day in New Orleans I sat down at the great upended lilyp- at that city's waterfront park and wrote Old Bull Lee an abstract letter suggesting that he send me his beat perceptions on the F.B.I. This was Friday; I planned to dig out of there by Monday morning and start a new week in an unknown town. But that night I accepted a gig as cop-watching with the Quakers and flicked right out. The kid with the ray gun jumped into the emergency ambulance with us and went out with us to the riot of the Irish Channel. It was a very bad one, with Mace and everything, and when it was all over we spent three hours talking in the big Quaker meeting hall at Camp Street. We theoried and...", "2_17_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe device that exploded in the box at the very moment that it was emptied was called a chrono-synclastic infundibulum. It was not a nuclear bomb or a conventional device of any sort. It exploded in a very special and unique manner.\nThe infundibulum had been invented by a man who lived in a garage on Fenway Park, in Boston, Massachusetts, U.S.A., Earth, Western Hemisphere, the Solar System, the Milky Way Galaxy.\nThe inventor\u2019s name was $SURNAME1 M. Farnsworth. He was a regular monthly contributor to Galaxy. He was a stamp-collector and astrophysicist.\nThe special manner in which an infundibulum explodes is this:\nIt relates its position in space as accurately as possible to its position in time. It does this by tossing its pieces into the highest possible orbits about itself\u2014intricate, intersecting, hyperbolic curves of trajectory.\nThe orbiting shards make the most beautiful sight that has been seen in the Universe since God made the Milky Way.", "2_20_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past eight o'clock in the evening</span> on Mars, and the first phase of the first phase of the first phase of the first sunrise of the year had just begun.\nA thermometer over the fountain registered a hundred and fifteen degrees Fahrenheit on Mars. $SURNAME0\u2019s distorted reflection in the fountain was already drying in the heat.\nAlmost any day of the year, a visitor to the $SURNAME0 estate might have seen Mrs. $SURNAME0 standing in the courtyold, might have seen the fountain jets bursting like fireworks, might have seen $SURNAME0\u2019s reflection in the fountain already withered by the heat.\nThe difference on this particular day was that, high above the courtyard on the third floor of the mansion, on the second window to the right of the solarium, Mr. $SURNAME0 was materializing.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 knew what to expect. She watched passively as space emptied, as the air churned, as a man appeared in it, stepped out of it, fell to his knees in the courtyard, the small of his back pressed to the hot paving. His palms were flat on the paving. He was naked. He was cursing softly under the harsh Martian skin-protectant that he had been ordered to wear.", "2_6_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\u201cSkip it,\u201d Crawford said, \u201cwho\u2019s got time. But, man, was she built along your lines once you got her in the water.\u201d\nIt was ten past six o\u2019clock. \u201cCome on,\u201d Bortz said, \u201cthere was a nineteen-year run. Skip one, go right to the next.\u201d", "0_12_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was carrying people into the amphitheater every two and a half minutes. It was less than an hour, now, before Salo came on stage.\nNo lectures would be delivered today by $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, or $FIRSTNAMEF0. No other heresiarchs were fabled to appear. All of the gentry of space were going to be on hand in their seats of honor\u2014empty seats, which were being kept warm for them by their beloved robots.\nThe only event on the program was the naming and claiming of the new meat creature.\nSoon, now\u2014moments later, now\u2014it would be old Salo\u2019s turn to take the oath of fidelity, to name his creature, to take a radiant blue piece of a Sun, and to lord it over his share of the Universe.\n\"Lazarus\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"\u2014are you ready to torture yourself again?\"\n$SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 had been surprised again and again by the fantastic tenacity of the Mars rocket\u2014and by the ingenuity of the means whereby that tenacity was being exploited. The last amazing surprise had been when $FIRSTNAMEB2 invited $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and lemonade to take a ride back to Mars...", "1_17_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when Mike sat down for the first time at the writing desk by the window in the hotel room. It faced on the Prado and on one side, ten feet away, on the narrow street, was an open-air caf\u00e9. Mike had made a big desk by putting on a clean writing-pad over the pile of trunks that usually served his writing-desk in the room at the pension. By the window at the end of the room was a big, clean bed with a brand-new canopy of deep-red silk. The bed had only been made that morning and the tester, with its shade all gathered up at the head, was still in sharp folds and creases. Something was wrong with one of the springs of the bed. You bounced up with a jerk, and whenever you left the bed you stepped down hard on the rock that marked the faulty spring. Behind the bed, against the wall, was a big wooden armoire. The rest of the room was bare except for one small chair and the trunks. There were pictures on the wall. In front of the window was a chest with a painted iron rail. On this rested the china and two plaster figures, a woman\u2019s head with a lot of dark curls,...", "2_2_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"I\u2019m sorry, Captain,\" said Sergeant Nolcott.\n\"So am I, Sergeant,\" said the Captain. \"I\u2019m sorry you\u2019re not a brighter guy.\" The Captain sighed. He had a high and hopeless opinion of Sergeant Nolcott\u2019s intelligence. The Captain shook his head.\n\"I can\u2019t understand it, Sergeant,\" the Captain said. \"I just can\u2019t understand why you\u2019d throw a career away just to add another month to a drunk.\"\n\"I sort of thought I was a bright guy, too, Captain,\" said Sergeant Nolcott.\n\"You are a bright guy, Sergeant,\" said the Captain.\nThe sergeant nodded.\n\"Ain\u2019t I a bright guy, Captain?\" he said.\n\"You\u2019re the smartest enlisted man in the Space Corps,\" said the Captain. \"It\u2019s just that you have to use your brains. You have to think a little.\"\nThe time was twenty-two minutes after two o'clock in the morning.", "0_15_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-two in the afternoon</span>. At forty-eight seconds past the hour, the child who would die at four twenty-two that same afternoon was alive and well, and in one speck of the Universe, at least, the Universe as it was two minutes before.\nAt forty-eight seconds past the hour, $FIRSTNAMEB2 was waiting patiently in line outside a Bureau of Weights and Measures window.\nThe Bureau of Weights and Measures occupied a large block of buildings on the side of a cliff on Mercury. There was no vegetation on the cliff. The cliff was almost sheer, but for three nearly sheer steps cut in it, one right after the other.\nThe first step was sixty feet high. It was made of concrete, faced with pink marble. It began forty-two miles east of the scale. The last step was forty feet high. It was made of flawless unassailable diamond.\nBetween the first and last steps was an air cushion, fifty feet thick, made of pink rubber, one and a half inches thick, two and a half miles wide, and five miles long. It was next to the sheer drop. The drop went straight down five thousand feet to the core of Mercury.\nThe location of the building with the weighing and measuring devices was well chosen for...", "2_10_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEF1 whistled the last of these commands.\nThe antenna anemones dropped everything and ran for a dune. They obeyed $FIRSTNAMEF1 for two minutes after he stopped whistling. They unfurled their antennae; the pink snapped sunward, stained the atmosphere pink. They ran to the tops of the dunes and died, flesh sealing shut. Antenna sprouted from their pink catafalques.\nThe time was six minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.\nThe twelfth member of the field-party expedition, field botanist Andrew Squervil, was seen approaching the departure area with an armload of quadrant fetuses. He was wearing green finger mittens, his forehead hurt, and he whistled cheerless lay. The time was eleven minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the morning.", "0_21_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes to ten o'clock in the evening</span>, according to my watch. I was depressed, I had to walk the streets. I always know the drunk feeling, it always comes after taking abuse. But this was the first time it caught up with me in somebody else's rooming house. I wandered forth with a stovewood bottom and banged my shin under a low dresser. I backed up and banged into the bed. I went out into the hall, late at night, as if to go to the toilet, but returned to the room and sat on the edge of the bed and talked to $FIRSTNAMEB3 for a long time about life. They had turned off the lights.\nFirst thing in the morning I went over to Remi's for breakfast. $FIRSTNAMEB3 came down, bleary and hung-over; he smiled shyly at me. He gave a big warm hello to Remi. \u201cI've spent a horrible night -- oh yes, I know your daughter very well.\u201d She hut at him with her quick, quiet eyes. \u201cI've been on the fence all my life, now I know just how it feels.\u201d\nRemi smiled wisely and went to the stove. \u201cWe'll have plenty of time to talk.\u201d She grinned. \u201c...", "1_11_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-eight in the morning</span> of November 1, 2120, when $SURNAME1 arrived at $SURNAME0\u2019s gates. $SURNAME1 was laden like a pack-mule. He carried, in addition to his spacesuit, a tent, a shovel, two alarm clocks, his deceased father\u2019s razor, a New Testament, a cook stove, two hundred pounds of potatoes, a thousand pounds of pancake mix, a thousand pounds of corn meal, a thousand pounds of granulated coffee, a thousand pounds of bacon, a thousand pounds of smoked pork jowls, three thousand pounds of butter, three thousand pounds of canned goods, three thousand pounds of canned spinach, three thousand pounds of canned peaches, three thousand pounds of canned pineapple, four thousand pounds of ripe Camembert cheese, six thousand pounds of meat salami, and twenty pints of whiskey.\nHe was out to stay the seven-month Martian winter alone in a heatproof, windproof, leakproof sex-and- refreshements shack on the face of Illium Planum five miles from where the Martian space ships would land in April. His share of the space- ship payoff money from the mass-winking experiment was just enough to provide himself with luxury until the ships came in.\nIt wasn...", "2_15_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-four in the afternoon</span>. It was three thirty-four in the afternoon on a Friday, at a Camelot Travel Inn somewhere between El Paso and Denver. The time of day was deduced from a blankety-blank clock that hung from a smudgy segment of the air that can only be referred to as a \u201csmudgy segment of the air.\u201d The clock said 3:34 in plain white numbers. There wasn\u2019t any wiggle room. My watch said 3:35. I looked at it about six times. Then I looked at the clock ... . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 3:34.\nOooops.", "2_0_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-seven a.m.</span> by the Swiss Clock above the oven. She had no watch of her own.\nNow the phone rang again. This time she did not answer. Instead she got up and went back to bed.\nFrom a feverish doze she woke to brilliant sunshine and the sound of a car trying to start, out in front of the house. She forced herself awake, shivering with nerves. If it was $SURNAME1 gassing or starting up again in the night, she\u2019d scream. Whatever he\u2019d been doing, he couldn\u2019t have been inside.\nBut after a minute the car, which she did not know yet was her own, groaned off down the road.", "2_16_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. We actually went up to the house and knocked on the door.\nHarold's girlfriend Paula answered and showed us in. \u201cWhy,\u201d said she, \u201cyou are the boys who called on the telephone, aren't you?\u201d We all talked. \u201cPoor $FIRSTNAMEF4,\u201d said Paula. \u201cShe's in bed. Why don't you get her to come down?\u201d We prowled minutely around the house.\nThere was a conference in the living room. \u201cI think $FIRSTNAMEF4 is trying to avoid meeting $FIRSTNAMEB6's brother and me. As far as I know she hasn't given Lee the message.\u201d In the living room with the blinds drawn shut it was dark and gloomy. The shades were drawn down so the house was like a sickroom. Why was everything drawn down, pulled in? There had never been such an atmosphere of gloom in love. We decided to wait one hour more; we would go back to San Francisco; then we would pick up $FIRSTNAMEF4's brother at his job and come back to the house and ($FIRSTNAMEB3 repeated with high emphasis on the pronoun) \u201cforce her to come down and be with us.\u201d", "0_10_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past ten o'clock in the morning</span>, according to Miss Eisenhower's watch. She made a final switch of her scanner, this time to some kind of radar that caught everythng, no matter how small and distant.\n\"People!\" she shouted, shaken and sweating. \"Take a look through the peephole, everybody! Scanner's on something awful!\n\"It's a\u2014a\u2014lord, what is it?\" she screamed. \"A whale! A great big whale out there in the desert!\"\nNone of the men in Bullfinch's party had ever seen a whale, and they ran out, rubbing the sleep out of their eyes and squinting out the peepholes. They saw, approximately two thousand feet away, a gray, rocklike shape that might have been a whale...or might have been a boulder...or might have been a boulder with fins.\nA bright school of sound waves fishtailed back and forth across the gray object, so that it might have been a fish...a rock with fish-like qualities...Or a boulder with fish-like qualities.\nThe ship shuddered, once. Then the desert was quiet again.\n\"Well, what do you make of that?\" one of the Space Wanderer's crewmen whispered.", "0_21_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-five in the evening</span> of April twenty-first, and poor $FIRSTNAMEB2, without even a mate to try out his modest, secret life style with, wandered despondently through the red-and-purple nights of Tralfamadore. He went to the place where $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had seen the furry thing digging. There was no sign of any digging now.\nThe big color television set had been installed in one of the stone rooms. The telecast was turned on, but the sound was off. The picture was of a crowd entering a big building. There was a banner stretched across the front of the building. The banner said AMY\u2019S SCHNITZELHASENFEST\u2014BERLIN.\nThe crowd consisted of young German soldiers. They were having a gay time. Their gay time was being sponsored by the Amtorg Trading Corporation of New York and Moscow. There were thousands of Russians in the picture, and the Russians looked happy, too.\nThese Russians, like the young Germans, had been promised a totally ignorant and helpless mate as a reward for their heavily subsidized gay time.\nThe mate in both cases was obtained from a drafting pool of young men and women in various troubled regions of the Earth. The men and women...", "0_11_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 and Gargle landed on the marble step of Number Fifty-three. The door-chimes had not yet made their three minutes and twenty-two seconds of pure sweet dolby Stereo sound when the door opened. The door-chime took its time. It had been installed by the firm of jorumsmith & jeck, experts in the field, and could be nobody else but Beethoven\u2019s Fifth, communicating as it did with a large copper engraving of St. George and the Dragon on the stairwall.\nThe man in the foyer was tall, rather ungainly, encased in a very well tailored blue pin-striped suit, western style, with red piping, a satin pearl-buttoned shirt, a black, two-tone foulard silk necktie with a rosette, the whole a little too fancy for the conservative $FIRSTNAMEB2. His light brown hair was short, down over his ears at the sides, and long at the back, in the small pompadour style of the patricians of old Earth. His eyes were, seemingly, everywhere at once, big and brown, full of youthful questioning and a bit of a twinkle. He smiled like the...", "1_21_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine eighteen in the evening</span> when she stepped on to the space ship. The space ship was named the $SURNAME0. It was anchored forty feet under the clear surface of a shallow lake. Forty feet above the lake was a platform on which the spiral staircase had wound and through which the airlock had sucked $FIRSTNAMEB0. The platform was out in the open, under the moon, under the Milky Way.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog were the only living creatures on the platform.\n$SURNAME0 was forty feet tall, thin, dark, and aristocratic. His long thin face was noble and sorrowful. His large saggy ears had a look of melting under pressure, yet retained the shape that $SURNAME0 had given them in the privacy of his childhood.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s large brown eyes always seemed to be under great pressure, too, as though he dreaded discovery. His upper and lower lids shut tight against each other, so that light leaked out only along the lower rim of his eyes.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s suit was Eterna-Float, the same golden yellow suit in which he had been delivered to the Army Space Training Command on Mars. His shirttail, as always, was out.\nHis left hand was in his...", "0_9_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s unit was approaching a red stucco villa that was their target. Stucco houses in that part of Mars, as in so many parts of Earth, were favored by the upper class because they were cheap, because they were easy to repair, and because they seemed quasi-historical.\nOut of the ground in front of the approaching unit wriggled a pair of foot-long metal tubes. They were copper or some copper-like substance, lined or clad with lead.\nA man and a woman who had been out in the fresh air of the morning approached the foot-long tubes gingerly. They threw a switch.\nThere was a pop, a hollow pop from the throats of the tubes, a gurgle. A gelatin capsule like a miniature golf ball dropped from one of the tubes. A pair of transparent wings unfolded from the capsule. The wings were dead, like the wings of a fly in winter.\nIn another second, in another hollow pop, the capsule disgorged itself, became a living, airborne thing. It was a transparent moth, looking threaded and feathered under magnification. It beat its wings crazily in the morning air, gained its bearings, found the light. It flew away...", "1_3_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>, and the only living things in evidence on main street of the town were two bartenders and myself. The bartenders were huddled on stools in the empty street, looking out through the plate glass at the great red emptiness that surround the town. They were in the doorway of one of the great stone and iron hotels. I had cornered them at last in the empty street, after harrying them all over town with a big, bulging pack full of cameras, binoculars, and notebooks. And now they stood in the street in despair and indecision, shivering to gregariousness, when there was no one even to talk to. \u201cOh, God!\u201d screamed one. \u201cWhy don't you go to bed?\u201d\n\u201cDon't you know what time it is?\u201d\nSalmon-pink light flowed over the edge of the world. With a terrific clap the sun burst over the horizon, the light bounced from a hundred glittering points in the water ahead, the town woke up. \u201cMan!\u201d said one of the bartenders, \u201cI didn't get to bed till six this morning.\u201d Forlornly he staggered out of the street to...", "2_3_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nSome Earthling appeared to have been the cruel lord and architect of all the gyrations performed by the Universe.\nIt was Nature herself who had given $SURNAME0 importance in this magnified Universe of his. The time was twelve minutes to four o\u2019clock in the morning. The Devil\u2019s own band was playing discordantly in the earphones of $SURNAME1\u2019s spacesuit helmet.\nOld Salo had retired inside his painless, back- supporting contour chair.\n$SURNAME1 had rolled his big, bright blue wheelbarrow halfway across the floor of the meadow.\nThe wheelbarrow was eight feet long, three feet wide, and two feet high. It was easier to push very heavy loads in a wheelbarrow than to carry them. $SURNAME1 constantly said to himself that he would not be there, wouldn\u2019t be bothering with any of it, if only he had a good pair of legs, and a good back to carry a pack.\nThe one thing he would not have to put in his wheelbarrow was the gilded, steatite dog that was to go on top of the tomb. That dog was upturned on its head, upside down in its own shallow cave.", "0_15_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nI walked on along the road.\nThe telephone-pole insulators along the road began to rise from their hedgerow surroundings and swell into immense white doughnuts. A shower of them burst over my head, and the rain of insulators dissolved into a shower of white hand towels. They dried on me.\nHardly noticing, I touched one hand to my face, and found that I had a long beard and a long hair-style of some sort.\nWithout much interest I corrected these, and walked on among the fat, wobbly telephone poles that now rose up before me like the pipes of organ; the silver pipes of an enormous organ, that grew swiftly up around me as I walked, and presently were rushing past me in the wind, flinging the rain in showers, and rising, rising until they shivered the world with their screaming.\nAgain something exploded in a white flash. Again and again it exploded, each time a little nearer.\nI turned to look.\nComing up the road toward me was my Cadillac, hurling pebbles and white blasts of smoke from its tremendous tail, and as I watched, trembling, wondering what it would do, it exploded repeatedly in white flashes that cracked it open fore and aft...", "1_20_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>, and the location was the far end of the time-windowed chamber, around in back of the device and out of sight of the operator. The materials being used were Y-D today, dry and delicious.\nIn addition to the phonograph with the record album, there was a portable television set, a carton of Orange Cremes, a carton of Camels, and a plastic bag of amyls.\nOn the television screen was a concert program. The great conductor was waving feebly at the audience, and the orchestral musicians were getting settled into their chairs again. The narrator was telling how the concert had gone, now that it was all over.\nOn the floor in front of Junior was his favorite St.-Thomas-in-the-Pines station. He was sitting in a lawn chair and wearing earphones. Every two minutes a reporter in St. Timothy, Florida, would provide a new insight or two about a crisis that was mounting there and reporting further developments as they came to hand.\nOn the floor beside the wheelchair was a large tape recorder, to play the record album on. The Amish Cricket was working up his nerve to push the record button on that tape recorder.\nOn the windows behind...", "1_13_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, according to the chronometer.\nPeople were swimming in the lake surrounded by dark-green Martian trees. Many of the people were trying to see who could stay underwater longest. Others were playing at water polo.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was carrying a red tricycle from the ship to the fountain in the middle of the village common. He was soaking wet with perspiration, having carried the bulky machine by way of a wading pool, a lazy river, and a waterfall.\n\"Why didn\u2019t you get one blue and one red, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, who was sprawled in a thronelike chair under the noonday sun of Mercury. \"A big crowd can get awful hurt playing with just one trike.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 set the machine on its three fat rubber wheels beside the spouting fountain. \"I tried, buddy,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"But I couldn\u2019t find but one red.\"\n\"You sure lucked out, though \u2014 finding any tricycle atall,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"Maybe these people aren\u2019t so dumb after all.\"\n\"I used to build them when I was a kid,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"How about that?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0...", "0_8_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-three in the morning</span>. I stood in the watching press and pretended not to know any of the frat brothers who were being arrested, so as not to jeopardize my job on The Biggest Bear. I felt sorry for Dickinson, who was a heroic Iowa football player called \u201cMoldy\u201d because of a serendipitous resemblance to a much\u2014revered coach who had died of cancer many years ago..\nMy job with the Daily was to write madman stories; such as, \u201cProbe: Did $FIRSTNAMEB3 Didion Steal $4000 Worth of Football Players' Examination Papers?\u201d I wrote stories exposing corruption on the football team, on the basketball team, on the baseball team\u2014on every team, in fact. My stories regularly appeared on the front page. Unfortunately journalists on the paper, which billed itself as \u201cThe Only Independent Student Newspaper of Quality in the Midwest,\u201d seemed to have a curious double standard regarding their own pay\u2014off\u2014the\u2014top\u2014sports\u2014coaches\u2014and\u2014the\u2014rigged\u2014investigations\u2014that\u2014follow\u2014every\u2014game behavior. They especially disliked my stories on their vice\u2014principal, a truly lovely Christian gentleman whom I accused of a few peccadilloes. (He...", "0_3_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-seven in the morning</span>. Our car was lost in the middle of Indiana. The children had been carried off by the police and put in some kind of home and we had just this moment gotten back to civilization. We had spent the time in the Pound Jail in Indianapolis, trying to recover our stolen car -- to no avail. The police lost the paper that proved we owned it.\nThere were two drunk tanks, one for brawlers and one for drunks. We were in the drunk tank with a giant, a wino and a beautiful little blonde girl who had been snatched on an Indiana road in the middle of the night by a fate far worse than ours and this time there was nothing anybody could do about it. And now here we were, fabulous, haggard, dirty, smelling of jail. We had spent all our bread on a long day and night in Indianapolis. We had no money for a hotel and no possible way of getting any. And even if we had had the money, we would never have spent it to sleep indoors after all we had been through. There was nothing left to do but go on. And this time blew up again.\nOne thing that would have happened for sure had I respected anyone or anything enough to go back to the Army on time...", "2_9_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>, New Hampshire time, and French, the butler of the man in the birdcage, had just told the man that a telephone call for him had come in from Washington, D.C.\nFrench, the man in the birdcage, and all the other members of the strange entourage in the lobby had a fixed, mindless stare directed at the alabaster walls. The walls were not as interesting as the chandelier in the center of the lobby. None of them had ever seen the like of the chandelier.\nThe chandelier was a huge affair of iron and steel, and from it hung 2000 light bulbs.\nThere was nothing that a person could do in the lobby that wasn\u2019t fascinating in some way to somebody. As long as a person didn\u2019t do anything too tasteless, he could be reasonably sure that somebody would enjoy watching him.\nHelping to make the lobby such a spectacle to behold was the jollity of the men, and their colorful costumes. The men had on everything imaginable, from bicycle shorts to the latest in men\u2019s suiting.", "1_15_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>, St. Valentine's Day, In the year of Our Lord 1934. Examples of fine American automobile fetish: Berreed's Fleetwood limousine\u2014the one he used to drive to the station only on days when he knew for a fact that a trainload of $FIRSTNAMEF0-Combines was coming in; Scotty Bennett's battered, low-slung rattler, that he swears he can still drive at 120 mph, that is to say 110 if he has a passenger . . . \u201cAwww \u2026,\u201d he snarls with a typical scowl, when the subject comes up, \u201cwe don't have no cars in California? Why of course we have cars in Californ, I won't deny it, and all sorts of them, too. But no fine ones, like you have here, I don't have no Cadillac, or no Lincoln, I don't even have no Ford, shit, I ain't got nothing fancy. In California we drive around in M hears. What's a M hear? You don't even want to know what it is. Just believe me when I say that M cars is the best cars in the world, and that's all there is to it. And hell, if I'm gonna...", "2_12_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after noon</span>.\nConrad had said good-by to $FIRSTNAMEF1 only two minutes before.\nHe had given her her instructions. She would act on them, as instructed, in an emergency. She would take care of old Ser Francesco. He, Conrad, would return. He had promised that.\nNow, in his space suit, he was clambering out of the airlock.\nSalter joined him a minute after Conrad had slid out of the airlock. There they were, side by side, like two flying squirrels on a thin aluminum vine, extending from the airlock window to a stone of the palace wall.\nThey would wait there, clinging to their vine, for the Martian infantry to attack them. There would be just enough of their combined Earth and Sun gravitational light, leaking between their feet and the stone, to protect them from any rifle shot aimed directly at them.\n\"Good-by, skipper,\" said Salter cheerfully. \"Faigee\u2019s in charge.\"\n\"We may as well be comical as anything, sergeant,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Since we\u2019re waiting to be killed, we might as well pretend that we\u2019re killing a lot of them while we wait.\"", "1_1_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-nine in the morning</span>. I was oblivious to all around me. I was only hanging on those wonderful bursts of language that turned everything around and made me see clearly, without the illusion. My bag was snug on my back. This particular street had broken me down, violently. Its dismal message, revealed finally, was that you cannot go home again. Not ever. Not to Denver, not to Dakota...not to Dunsmuir, Indianapolis, Jackson, San Francisco, not to any of it.\nI was a stranger in my own land. No place is cool and there are no good people. Every place is hot and every person is burnt out. Somewhere along the line the thing that would enable the good guys to win had been lost.\n\u201c....Death by water!\u201d he shrieked, in maniac, rhythmic frenzy. \u201cRetire...retire and leave no address.\u201d\nI was ready to pack it in and sail away from there on any kind of floating garbage with no address at all. My spirit was totally smashed by that simple street in that most beautiful of all American cities.\nAnd then, suddenly, just at dawn, Cincinnati rose up out of the maelstrom of my madness and struck a blow for home! Yes...", "2_6_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-two in the morning</span>, Greenwich mean time\u2014two minutes before Earth\u2019s state funeral ceremonies were scheduled to begin on Mars. Things had happened very rapidly on Mars after the message from Earth had arrived at the outpost. Everything the Martian soldiers had suspected about Earth had been confirmed by the message from the people of Earth to the people of Mars.\nThe demonstration model of the Martian fighting machine had been smashed. The real fighting machines had been given the order to destroy themselves\u2014and they had obeyed.\nThere had been a church service, with a full military honors guard, in the gymnasium in the spaceport fort. The services had been conducted by $FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and the head guard, Sergeant $SURNAME2.\nA low wooden casket had been brought onto the parade ground by four bearers. It had been placed on a rifle range\u2014a little in from the fifty-foot welter and the sandbags. There had been a folded American flag in the casket.", "2_3_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after three o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich time.\n\"Is it true what I am told about your church and faith?\" said the Supreme Pontiff. \"That you are more indifferent than everybody else?\"\nMr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 nodded dumbly.\n\"Then why are you here?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"I guess that\u2019s an easy one,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"Please explain how it is an easy one.\"\n\"It\u2019s because our church can never be more indifferent than we are when we find ourselves inside of somebody else\u2019s faith,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4.\n\"How does that follow?\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"Maybe it doesn\u2019t follow,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4 thoughtfully.\n\"It follows beautifully,\" said the Supreme Pontiff.\n\"If we find ourselves inside of somebody else\u2019s faith,\" said Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB4, \"then we can\u2019t be more indifferent than we are when we\u2019re outside of a faith.\"", "2_9_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after nine o'clock in the morning</span>, M.T.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had plunged under the dock, through a dog-door set there for them by Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. The door was covered by a drift of sand.\nThe time was ten minutes past nine o'clock in the morning, M.T.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were in the buried portion of the dock. It was four feet high and six feet wide. Mrs. $SURNAME0 had sent a file through to $SURNAME0, so he could take out His Image and Prophecy if he wanted to. The shelves of the canal also held two bulb-festoons of pink faux-phallus flowers. These were a Martian native\u2014the saffron curd. Mrs. $SURNAME0 had installed the flowers as part of a system of illumination by which $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 could find their way to the main artery of the old canal. The main artery let out into the unrestored portion of town.\nThe time was eleven minutes past nine o'clock in the morning, M.T.", "0_9_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, who knew when it was time to act, heard the message from space and brought from the cellar of the palace official a case of rum. Rum, $FIRSTNAMEB2 felt, was the proper drink with which to celebrate a successful message from space. He carried the case of rum up the stairs with a great deal of grunting and panting.\nThere in Mercury Gardens he found Salo and $SURNAME0 unresponsive. Salo was leering at a picture of a female with whom he had gone to high school \u2014 the picture was in an issue of the yearbook. $SURNAME0 was reading his own obituary in the Newport Chronicle.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 opened the case of rum and poured two stiff drinks, forgetting all about the number 41 on the bowls of the spoons. He put the unopened spoons back in the case, snapped it shut, gave the case a little spin. \"Here you are, Mr. $SURNAME0, sir\u2014Mrs. Salo,\" he said.\n\"Oh \u2014 thank you, Cannonball,\" said $SURNAME0 listlessly.\n\"Yah-hey!\" said Salo. \"Hey-y-y!\" He meant to say something about his wife and $SURNAME0 drinking coconut...", "0_3_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>, Wednesday. The hoodlums, spies, and prying neighbors were mostly in bed, though there were some shifts of personnel still going on. $SURNAME0, as up-to-date on his calendar as any NASA rocket, was in the midst of his busiest period of the twenty-four hours.\nPeople would be coming and going until six o\u2019clock that evening, the time the splendid Virginian Skip III went up on the pad.\n$SURNAME0 and Salo were not expected to appear much in public during the launching. $SURNAME0 would be busy in his Sunspot Room, and Salo would be posing in his iron lung and monitoring tape recordings of cricket songs.\nThe public interest would be so great that $SURNAME0\u2019s guests at Skip Time would not be let out of the Titan Gardens arcade till after the take-off.\n$SURNAME0 and Salo were surprised at the meagerness of their privacy.\n\"They promised us absolute privacy,\" said $SURNAME0. \"If I yell, will you yell, too?\"\n\"Yell?\" said Salo. \"Why should I\u2014\" He stopped. He was not programmed to think ill of his mates. \"Sure,\" he said, \"I...", "1_18_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span> in Szombathely, Hungary, really, as the heron had explained to the bear\u2014the whole time-lagged business, June into late August, 1972. And there on the river\u2014there, beneath the European sun, was the American flag. As true as anything in the world, there it was. And there, center of the flag, white star on red field, was the Integral.\nThe lifeboat slowly circled the starship. The figures on board the lifeboat, though tiny, could be made out quite distinctly. Much more distinctly, really, than $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Stony Stevenson, and $FIRSTNAMEB2, who were oiling the deep rust of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s fowling piece on the fo\u2019c\u2019sle of the $SURNAME0, which was a extension of the afterpart of the ship\u2019s keel, two feet above the water line.\nYes\u2014much more distinctly than $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Stony Stevenson, and $FIRSTNAMEB2. And, in the way of seeing that small things become large when faces are involved, the faces were as clear, and as devastating, as any the world had ever known.\nMax von Winterschein\u2019s face was pale and stiff...", "1_18_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-five in the evening</span>. The place was the Cherry Cricket, a tavern that had been around since Adam was a lad. It was on the edge of Salo n, a block from the fancy residential section, and was frequented by old Salopians and by the faculty and students from the nearby monastic hideaway of St. Clair\u2019s, whose round-shouldered, pipe-smoking graduates filled the air with weird tales and weird smells.\nThe Cherry Cricket had no phone. Its only door was the back one, opening into an alley full of mop water and the steam of mighty washers at work in a YMCA next door. The alley door, because of the steam and of a special magnetic device connected with the nearby washers, was always stuck shut. The rest of the tavern was a pleasant, spooky place filled with old wood and muffled sounds and odd odors. At the counter, called \"The Stove,\" $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 sat, while the girl with the aurora hair went crazy all over town.\nOne curious thing about $SURNAME1 was that he wore chronometers at both wrists. Most men wore one. On each chronometer was a tiny gilt portrait\u2014the same portrait on each\u2014an aquiline, good-looking...", "1_13_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-three in the afternoon</span>. By two o'clock we were on our way to New York in a big Oldsmobile limousine. We loaded everything we owned in the back seat, and I sat with $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Ethel McMahon in front. There is something about the intensity in the faces of some American people that is frightening, when you're going sixty miles an hour in the teeming rain. $FIRSTNAMEB3 talked the epic of life at top speed in capsule form. I leaned back sulking because I knew what he was doing; hiding confusion, anger, and sadness with a wilderness of words. I smoked innumerable cigarettes and looked out the window. We were going toward home. We crossed the George Washington Bridge, with Jersey on the right and and New York on the left. Lights, waterfronts, barges, liberty bells and skylarks, lofts and tenements, whole waterfronts in fog, a wilderness of lights and tin horns and ding dongs, slowly dissolving hallelujahs, rising over the sad horns, screams, snores, whistles, and bells that attended our progress across the high wire of the bridge. It began to drizzle again. \u201cThis is the way to go home, yes indeedy,\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3. \u201c...", "1_19_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-one in the evening</span>. The Indianapolis stamp on her ship\u2019s-pass card designated Red A\u2019s ship as Mintaka III.\nThe number on the card, 247-Q, was not, she had come to suspect, a number at all. Though the number on her old ship\u2019s-pass card, 277-M, had been a number, had in fact been Major Barbara\u2019s number. And Major Barbara had had a crew of twenty-seven people, six crewmen being the standard staffing for a ship of Major Barbara\u2019s tonnage, size, and description.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s absence from Newport had become total. There were ten million things that $SURNAME0 could be doing outside the walls of his estate to interfere with the materialization of some or all of Tralfamadore\u2019s gift conversions. And he was doing them all.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s contrived messages continued unabated. They were piped over the public-address system of his museum, his walled seaside home, and his estate just as they had always been. Two messages especially were as mood-creating, as curling-the-lips funny as ever. One was...", "2_8_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nA shipment of paper had arrived by truck. There were two truckloads in all, enough paper to keep the 15,375 electric typewriters in the great hall clacking until Jesus returned\u2014providing the paper held out, which wasn\u2019t likely because the stuff was low-grade and likely to deteriorate rapidly in the damp, salty air of the stone castle.\nThere were eight women with baskets and three forklift trucks, and the women were putting paper into the trucks and the forklifts were carrying the paper out of the great hall into the stone anteroom, which was, of course, part of the great hall.\nThe stone anteroom was to be sealed off from the rest of the great hall. There were to be vents in the stone wall for fresh air and a heater for warmth. Where the paper was now was to be the pressroom. A wall was to be built to divide the pressroom from the anteroom.", "0_13_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The season was the time between July and September. The place was Titan\u2014the largest moon of the planet Saturn. Titan was so far from the sun, so far from Earth and the other inhabited solar system planets, that only the most daring explorers would undertake to explore it. The season was the time between the ebbing of Titan's rings and the emergence of the rings again. The rings were also called The Fingers of Saturn. The Fingers were forty-two thousand miles long, one thousand miles wide, and one hundred feet thick. They were composed of tiny metallic particles, presumably meteors or space dust that had once obsessed the periphery of Saturn.\n\"I wouldn't think\u2014I couldn't think I'd ever want to come back here again,\" said Salo. \"What a desolate cruddy hole to be down in.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 laughed uproariously. \"This is life?\" he said. \"This makes you laugh?\" he said.\n\"What do they call this place?\" said Salo. \"This gory, boring place?\"\n\"Cancerfield,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Poor little Earthling,\" said Salo. \"Barring cosmic accidents, this is going to be your last adventure\u2014ever.\"\n\"Ever...", "1_5_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to six o'clock in the morning</span> exactly and $FIRSTNAMEB2 was shaking him violently. \"Listen,\" he was saying, \"we\u2019re almost there. Wake up. The palace is just beyond that thicket of trees.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 climbed awkwardly out of the pyramid. He looked around sleepily.\nThe woods were sweet with spring aromatics\u2014patchouli, spikenard, and sarsaparilla. The sun was coming up over a ridge of red earth in the east, squinting through layers of shivering pink clouds.\nAnd the palace was right beyond the ridge of red earth, right beyond the thicket. The palace was solid gold, and would have been a wonder more remarkable than the pyramid, if the pyramid hadn\u2019t been the only solid-gold building ever to come to Mars.\nThe palace was a simple cube, with a vast staring eye in each comer. Its windows were eyes, also, and all were open, to let in the Martian morning. And, spying at $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 from each window, from each comer eye, was an Earthling.\n\"I\u2019ll go first,\" $FIRSTNAMEB2 whispered. \"I understand the little darlings, you see.\" He went bounding off toward the palace...", "2_2_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-three in the morning</span>.\nAnd now a new sound, a distant ripping sound, came to the ears of the two hundred and one soldiers\u2014a sound for which their training had not prepared them. It was the sound of musketry fire from the far-off battle line, the battle line that bulged twenty miles to the south. Only twenty miles away, somebody was shooting at somebody.\nSpontaneously, the soldiers cheered. Here at last was the grand and gay sound of war, the immortal Whao ! Whao! Whao! of bullets passing gaily through the bodies of young men.\nThe time was two twenty-four in the morning.\nThe war was on.\nThe war was on in a rather desultory fashion, with nothing definite at stake. Neither side could possibly gain territory.\nThe battle line bulged, did just what it was supposed to do\u2014blocked the road to the German border so that the Free Nations of Europe could not invade Germany quickly, could not invade at all unless they wanted to commit suicide.\nIt is a peculiarity of stubborn warfare that one side or the other does most of the bleeding and dies in large proportion.", "2_14_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $SURNAME0 again visited $FIRSTNAMEB2, the museums toll-booth operating machine. $SURNAME0 stood outside the plate-glass window of the booth as he spoke to $FIRSTNAMEB2. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was not a member of the class of beautiful people\u2014not even close. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was a hideous, cowering cripple. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was not simply crippled. He had had practically every part of his anatomy replaced by prosthetics, mostly by machine parts. His real self was a pale, naked skull-like thing at the center of this crazy tangle of artificial limbs and organs.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s eyes, his true eyes, were concealed behind a parabolic mirror that reflected nothing but the image of whoever might be observing him. $SURNAME0 was such an image. His image appeared as a wavering ball of purple witch-fire two inches in front of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s nose.\nThe purpose of the ball of purple witch-fire was to keep $FIRSTNAMEB2 always guessing whether or not anyone was observing him. $FIRSTNAMEB2 spent almost all of his time peering into that wavering ball.", "2_9_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>. It was Saturday, August 27, 1951, and it was raining in Central Park. There had been a downpour during the night, as there would be during the night before every week end of all time, as there would be during the night after every week end of all time. There would be a rustle in the trees, a swell in the roar of car engines, an attack on the lake in boats, a screech of brakes, the drumming of hard rain, and then the clouds would let up. There would be blue weather all around.\nIt really didn\u2019t matter what anybody did on a Saturday in Central Park.\nThere were, so far as anybody could see, the makings of some kind of story or other everywhere in Central Park.", "2_15_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3's watch and my little gilt-medallion watch were on the desk and decided the moment. At this moment Henri poured us a drink. We lifted our glasses and said, \u201cThis is the Golden Moment to begin your new life in the construction business.\u201d\nHenri shrugged. \u201cNo more new life.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d\n\u201cBusiness not good. Too hard. Too much work.\u201d\n\u201cBut the prospects in housing and civic work ...\u201d\n\u201cNot me.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d we said.\n\u201cMean I give up.\u201d", "0_18_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>.\n\"Thank you, old chap,\" said $SURNAME0. \"That little slab of luck helps. My gizzard\u2019s been ripped out and stomped on more times than I care to remember. Now you\u2019ll have to excuse me for a second, old boy. I\u2019ve just remembered something I have to do\u2014something I forgot about ten thousand billion years ago. Have another drink before I go?\"\n\"Well\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"if you\u2019re having one.\"\n\"I certainly am,\" said $SURNAME0. He pushed a button, and a butler appeared in the archway. \"Scuttledegusk,\" said $SURNAME0, \"bring Mrs. $SURNAME0, the Lite Queen, and myself extraordinarily clean glasses, sparkling water, and the best Martinis you can make.\"\n\"Yes, Master,\" said the butler.\nThe butler, Scuttledegusk, was named for a bird that laid green and sulphurous eggs, laid them in peat bogs. This bird was a noisy, blind mound of morbidity that lived along fens, lagoons, and millponds, and was the mascot of the village of Degrade on the island of Martha...", "0_11_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-one in the morning</span> of October 3, and the Inconvenience was well above the atmosphere, over the firm peak of a soap-sud ocean, and starting to curve down toward the blue crust-bowl of the home-world, Earth.\nThe year was three thousand and eleven.\nThe Martian sunset was the most beautiful sight that the human race was ever to see.\nExcepting the people who had a different sort of perspective on things, like $FIRSTNAMEB2, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was the most wretched human being on Earth.\nThis last discovery was as bad for him as had been all the rest. There was more wonder and glory in an empty shoebox, to $SURNAME1\u2019s eyes, than there was in his three thousand and eleven years, his ramshackle mansion, his fortune, and the blue Martian sunset.\nAnd all these trivial things were now denied him. He had sold the mansion, given away the fortune, and the sunset at least was going out of his field of vision. He was going to be space sick again. He was going to leave Earth, again, voluntarily this time, but with the same sick heart.\nBut it was out of his hands. He was doing as Fate and his wife had decided, and God.", "0_5_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-two in the morning</span>. The sun would appear in just over six hours. Victor Monroe arrived. He was tall, handsome, intelligent, sensitive, considerate, and crazy\u2014and something else. There succeeded now one of the most fantastic incidents of my life. As I tell it now it has a quality of madness. But I saw it sanely then: it was the only possible way of meeting somebody else's needs while protecting my own privacy to the maximum. I had loved this wild Kentucky kid, Victor Monroe, for five years\u2014ever since he'd showed up inRapallo with his sad, sick painter Italian father. I had never told him. I'd helped him off and on during those five years. And now he came with this absolute faith that I would have breakfast with him, listen to his amorous adventures pose as his best friend, meet his needs as I saw fit, and never give an inch of the truth away. We smoked and joked about the pettiness of the world and its gaggery characteristics, and he told me of his arriving in Denver at night with Camille in the car from Mexico, and following my insane directions, and checking into the Sir Thomas Hotel a few blocks down the street from where I lived. His little girl was sleeping in the back...", "2_9_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nEight minutes\u2014and $FIRSTNAMEB0 would be in the space ship, bound for the hospital ward on Mars.\nFour minutes, now.\nHe was sorry that he had wasted so much time being cruel to a cat. He was sorry that he had wasted so much time and gas being cruel to his best friend, $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s fingers found Ledbedder\u2019s pocket. There were two books in the pocket, bound in red cloth, soft as young skin. One of the books was a hymnal.\n\"What are you doing there?\" whispered Lieutenant $SURNAME2.\n\"Reading,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, closing the hymnal. He opened the other book, found in it what he had hoped to find in it.\nThe other book was a Bible.\n\"You been missing church?\" whispered $SURNAME2. \"Who strung you up, buddy?\" he said, came close enough to read $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s dog tags. \"Who strung you up, buddy?\" and he touched the three iron balls under $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chin. He gave the balls a twirl. \"Who strung you up?\" he whispered hungrily.\n\"God,\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 said.", "0_1_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was frantically driving $FIRSTNAMEF4 and me to their home. He regained his composure. \u201cWe'll wire my father and he'll sneak out of bed, anxious as hell, and go to the bank and send us what he can -- if we can talk Ralph into contributing also. Old man Rawlston, who's completely crazy, lives out of town -- but be damned if we need HIM money,\u201d he muttered disgustedly, lighting his six-hundred-and-fifty-fourth cigarette, \u201cit's my father I'm trying to get dough out of -- anyhow, I don't think old Rawlston has any left, for he's been spending his own substance like mad these days on nothing to show for it but strange wines and drugs and such farriery. But let's try it anyway. \u201cHa, listen, that reminds me, I've got to phone Camille at home and tell her where I am -- she'll be worried.\u201d And he wanted me to telephone Lucille at the hotel. After that we were off again, racing through the night Kansas roads. When we got to the frat-house where $FIRSTNAMEF4 and $FIRSTNAMEB3 were staying, $FIRSTNAMEF4 ran to get her...", "2_13_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>.\nThe place was a basement in an apartment building.\nThere was a windowless wall of rock at one end of the basement. That was all.\nThe only light was the light that came through the window from outside. The light that came through the window was not very strong \u2014since it had to filter through so much stone. The basement was a mellow pit in which two men could sit comfortably.\nBehind Stony and $FIRSTNAMEB0 were two ladders leading to a small trapdoor in the floor. The men above used the trapdoor to bring down supplies and other comforts. And now, as Stony and $FIRSTNAMEB0 waited for the man with the blond mustache to bring them chloroform and oblivion, two of Stony\u2019s acolytes shovelled earth out of the trapdoor.\nThe blond man put his head in the window opening.\n\"Hi, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" he said.\n\"You\u2019ve got us!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 morosely.", "1_9_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-two in the morning</span> of a clear day. Nine forty-two in the morning of a clear day in the beautiful Boulderado Hotel.\n\"You are a fool to yourself and an ingrate\u2014\" her letter began.\nR $SURNAME1 had had to look up the word ingrate in the dictionary.\n\"You are a fool to yourself and an ingrate, and by heavens you deserve the disaster that has so unexpectedly visited you, you female floundering fool. I did my best to give you a tranquil and contented life, and what did you do with it? While I slept, you grew restless, you squirmed, you fidgeted, you schemed to get free of me.\n\"And now it is spring once more, and every bird is with his mate. But me, your mate\u2014you have cast me off. You have deserted me. Now you would find it hard to take me back. You would make terms. I know you women. I have been married before, though this time it was to a fool.\n\"Had you the grace or the sense to hide the depths of your discontent from me, it would be different, I might have let you go, though it is by letting you go that you will come to a realization of the...", "0_13_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nHerkimer settled in, spread a cloth napkin on his lap, leaned over the table, and said to Driblette, \"I have to speak with you about our mutual friend.\"\n\"What about him?\" said Driblette.\n\"I really shouldn\u2019t say in front of the ladies,\" said Herkimer. He licked the thumb of his right hand and stroked the ruby on his left index finger. \"A most unseemly habit,\" he said. \"Between friends, un-Minesweeper-like. Obscene in airports. But when Sue-Ann here touches the stone, I just go to pieces. Mine holes all over the map.\"\n\"You must excuse Herk,\" said Driblette. \"He tells outrageous lies, but they are sweet, like peppermint candy riots. You drive in lot far? Mine be far too.\"\n\"Crossed over on the ferry this morning,\" said Pierce.\nDriblette bent over his scallops sauce rago\u00fbt and murmured, \"Made any legs?\"\n\"Yes, some,\" said Pierce, raising his eyebrows quizzically.\n\"I been here four days and haven\u2019t made one,\" Driblette...", "1_2_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"For God's sake, let's get out of here!\" said my attorney.\nWe ran for the hotel, scattering bums and vagrants right and left. A few of the biggest lunatics tried to give us trouble at the door, but the Hiway Patrolman told them we were on official business, and they backed off....\n6. Back on the Road Again...\nWe sat naked on the floor in our room at the Flamingo. Our clothes were scattered higgledy\u2014piggledy all over the place. Every time my attorney reached out to grab his cigarette pack I hunched a new piece of clothing out of his grasp. The suitcase was right there; all we needed to do was get dressed and leave...no more chances to be caught naked in this suite with morning about an hour away and the drug people coming for our room.\nThe bodyguard was slowly coming back to normal. It took him twenty minutes in the shower to even recognize what a shower was, but now he was strapping on a belly\u2014band holster and reassembling his hardware. His huge tattoo screamed up at me. \u201cLas Vegas Vice Squad!\u201d It warped my mind. I kept sitting on the floor with my...", "1_21_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-eight in the evening</span> and $FIRSTNAMEB1 was standing on the pier of the island. His hat had come untied in the wind and the president\u2019s medal had fallen out of his hat and was being whipped around and around beneath the wooden dock.\nAstonishingly, the Stonecipheco cordage guy had not had a telephone in his office. He had sent $FIRSTNAMEB1 to telecommunications, and telecommunications had sent him to crystal and crystal had sent him out to the island.\nHow much longer he would stay on the island was not clear, with two hundred and twelve soldiers lost and an untold number of bloodthirsty island creatures out there, some of them probably gnawing on the remains of the hundred and forty-three poor saps who had been shot early.\nOne of the big events of the day in the war effort, undoubtedly, was the sending of a one-star general, unaccompanied, to stand on a pier in the rain and fog at night.\nGeneral Hidei Shinzo Yoshida intended to go to Harvard Business School after the war. Harvard Business School\u2019s record of putting all its one-year tourists back to work was, of course, marvelous.\nThe water was serene, except for being a little roughed up by...", "1_7_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-eight in the morning</span> of Wednesday, June twelfth, San Narciso\u2014Washington time. Not the most auspicious of beginnings. $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas, however, did not believe in omens. She\u2019d gone to bed the night before, after doing a little prep work on the Tristero novel, and slept the sleep of the just, not stirring even when ild dice clattered in the toilet or stubby feet scurried along the hall outside her door. She\u2019d particularly needed that rest, tonight being the night after the full moon and a certain karate class in which she\u2019d been practicing the silent screech-kick. Hence her thinness, and its divulgence in silky shorts and see\u2014through pink U Cal Berkeley sweatshirt that couldn\u2019t hide it any more than her flyaway hair could. She might\u2019ve been 30, 25, even 20. Possessing a narrow and faintly arabesque face, the kind that launches ships, countries, keeps movies running over years, inspires alters. Pleasant but unswervable eyes, brown and steady. She thought of Miles, wherever he might be, whenever that had been, wherever he might be now; her gaze took in all 130 pounds of her...", "0_18_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had already set in the west, and nature was darkening toward Blue. And the things that were snooping around outside were blue too.\nThe things were blue creatures\u2014robust beings, two thousand feet high and equipped with six limbs, senses that could penetrate a few yards of soil, rock and metal, and two sets of vocal apparatus. The creatures breathed sulfurous fumes. They planned to attack Malachite Throk in force at exactly seventeen minutes after seven o'clock.\nTheir underground headquarters, a mile deep, lay east of the city. This was a vulnerable point. Should Throk retaliate by blasting this region, the creatures would be destroyed by their own bombs.\nOnly the creature\u2019s brilliant strategy had given them the slightest chance of conquering Tralfamadore.\nThe strategy was this: to attack the leader in his home, when he was relaxing in the company of his best and favorite recreation machine. It was hoped that the leader would be frightened enough so that he would be easily terrified.\nThe strategy might seem like nonsense to the uninitiated, but never to the creatures themselves. They clung fiercely to the idea that their masterful plan made sense and was sure to succeed.\nThe creatures were...", "0_20_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight o'clock in the evening</span>, the square had become more animated, a little band of Spanish gypsies had set up their music stand and instruments, they gathered around it, they sent their shrill competing songs into the dusky mild air over the watching square, the people stopped and listened, then the word was passed along, the gypsies had arrived, there was going to be dancing. People came shyly out of their houses to watch and lean on the windowsills. There was a sprinkling of merely curious people. Tentatively the gypsies asked a few couples to dance. The couples ordered reluctant boys out to dance with the girls. They were paid for. The dancing began. It was strange, intense, gypsyish dancing. The music was wild and strange. The whole thing was melancholy, nostalgic, certain of sudden death, and a little creepy. I watched it from behind the open window, there with $FIRSTNAMEB5. The music sharpened and mounted under the competition of the two singing women, and the dancers took new stiffness from it. An old man who had been playing a woodwind instrument became a dancer, and then a girl who had been singing sorrowful songs became a dancer. The men spun the girls and tossed them high in the air, they caught them and...", "0_16_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. Our car, according to Kubrick\u2019s instructions, was parked in a specific place in the huge parking lot of the Las Vegas Sam\u2019s Town. I saw a man in a suit who I thought was Kubrick himself\u2014and I was nervous in that semidark parking lot and I jumped out of the car and moved toward the man with my hand held out, saying: \u201cMr. Kubrick? I\u2019m HST.\" And the man I was walking toward smiled, and I knew he wasn\u2019t Kubrick. He was far too tall, slim and soft. He said, \u201cMr. Thompson? I\u2019m Bruce Social, Mr. Kubrick\u2019s attorney. We spoke on the phone.\u201d We shook hands, and the distinction was important. It was Social who had talked to me on the phone, not Kubrick. He said Kubrick was somewhere in the bowels of the Casino, probably still working on the conversation he was going to have later with his assistant, Tony, at \u201cseven o\u2019clock sharp. \u201cThat\u2019s what he\u2019d said on the phone, I remembered: \u201cSeven o\u2019clock sharp, Tony.\" He was coming at seven o\u2019...", "0_11_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-nine in the morning</span> of December 31,1992. I was hitchhiking on a bus route that ran all the way out to the entrance to Edwards Air Force Base. It was New Year\u2019s Eve, and I was carrying a copy of the Los Angeles Times with the frontpage story about the riots, plus the Post-Modern Bible, which might have interested the security guards a great deal more, if I'd tried to enter the base.\nNo such attempt was made; I merely circulated in the area and hoped for the best. Nine vehicles had passed me by before I hitched a ride with an honest-to-God drunk, who talked so much I didn\u2019t have to. The subject of his drunken revelations, as we roared off toward Barstow, was \u201cDutch Henry.\u201d\nDutch Henry, he said, was his drinking buddy. They carried on together, night and day. Dutch Henry's wife had run off one day, leaving him stuck with two kids and a dog. That night, with Dutch Henry drinking double Wild Turkeys and hotshotting beers, they decided to drown the dog. That night, at the point of death, they staggered out with the dog to a bridge over a dry wash; the dog refused to jump...", "0_2_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-eight in the morning</span>, Wednesday, August 21, 2006, according to the mantel clock in the White House residence. The place was a bedroom in the northwest corner on the second floor. It had two windows, both open, for the temperature was in the low nineties and the humidity was nearly ninety per cent. The curtains at the windows were drawn, because the windows overlooked the South Lawn, and the view was distracting at this time of night. The bedroom was a mess. General Russ Baggert, of the United Storkes of America, was asleep in the king-sized bed, wearing only his scrotal sock and an eye patch.\nThere was one light burning in the bedroom. It was a brushed-nickel floor lamp with a ceramic base and a sculpted abstract-human form. The light emitted by the lamp was the only illumination in the room. The hand-painted shade was red, like those on a pair of glasses that a woman had set down on the end table to the right of the bed. The red shade was blotched and damp with perspiration. The shade was not supposed to be red. It had been white when the first Mrs. $SURNAME0, $FIRSTNAMEF0, bought the lamp in 1931. The shade was supposed to be white...", "0_10_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>. Ten minutes before the explosion.\nSebastian Knight, his fortune assured for all time, eased himself into the ebony barber chair, smiled at the barber, nodded at $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 made a face like a man who has just sniffed a lemon.\nHe disapproved of the way Sebastian worked his magic, with the help of the Army. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s ideas about how to make a fortune with a magic typewriter were simple, honorable, decadent, and corrupt.\nAfter being in the Army, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s favorite way to make money was by taking off his clothes and going to bed with a nubile young woman. In free-fall conditions on a space ship, or on the moon, or in the desolate region beyond the farthest locomotive scramble track in Missive, there were bound to be nubile young women available. For free, or for a nominal charge.\n\"Ouch!\" cried Sebastian, as the barber, with a straight-razor, made the first heroic cut. \"Oh, Lord!\" cried Sebastian, as the second heroic cut descended.\nThe third heroic cut had scarcely begun when the first kick of the hallucinogenic gas leaked under the...", "0_16_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nI was alone in the home of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. The estate had been especially built for $SURNAME1\u2014and its plans drawn by the man himself. It was an ugly monument to the ego of an ugly man\u2014a pretentious pile of stone and mortar on a hill outside the village of Puny, forty miles from Modern SEC Mkstasp.\nTo call the estate a home was to abuse the word. No home that $SURNAME1 had ever entered had failed to lose some of its roof during his stay. In this case, the roof was held down by giant springs designed to keep it that way. A stay of ten minutes in the home would have been sufficient to spring the roof and demolish what remained.\nIt was by design that the estate consisted of nothing but a long line of rooms, each overlooking a deep valley. $SURNAME1's wealth had bought the most beautiful settings on earth. His mean character had kept him from having any view worth looking at.\nHe had lived surrounded by deep valleys.\nThe floors of all the rooms in the home were covered with diamond dust.\nAnd so, when $SURNAME1 died outside the village of Puny, his corpse continued the tradition of doing damage to the homes of others. A builder who had quit work on a new...", "2_6_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty in the morning</span>.\nAt six twenty-nine, when everything was done, Big Jew and $FIRSTNAMEB0 were as close to friends as either had ever been with any other man.\nThe dark-haired, stocky woman, humming tunelessly, came out of the secret passageway under the stone pavilion, met $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew\u2019s eyes, went back.\nA blue sedan approached $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew, stopped. Three very tough young men popped out and strode toward $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew. All three had short, sharp haircuts. All three wore neatly pressed, short, sharp-serge, blue uniforms, black shoes, white socks and batwing collars. Their eyes blazed. They let the butts of their automatic pistols stick out boldly from under their tight lapels. Their pistols looked good to $FIRSTNAMEB0. He wished he had one, wished he had a uniform just like theirs. Three tan shoulder holsters, three bright pistols\u2014it was a beautiful layout.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and Big Jew stopped work and watched the three young men come.", "1_16_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four sixteen in the afternoon</span>. At that instant, $FIRSTNAMEF0, who knew the reason for her husband\u2019s agitation, knocked at the bathroom door. $FIRSTNAMEB0 knocked the message to pieces by firing another blank through the door. $FIRSTNAMEF0 withdrew quietly.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 fired the last cartridge at the square white thing in the airshaft. As he watched it go, he wondered what it was. It had appeared as though by magic midway between $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his pursuers. For no reason whatever, $FIRSTNAMEB0 decided that it must be a harmonium. harmoniums were affectionate little song birds that came, once a year, to sing in the heart of any Boy, and bring him wonderful, intuitive understanding.\nThe harmonium in the airshaft sang once, and was silent. $FIRSTNAMEB0 at once knew the reason for his feeling of estrangement, for his acute homesickness. The harmonium\u2019s brief song had reminded him, had given him details of, the life he had known eons ago in the cave village by the sea.\nOver fifteen Earthling days had passed on Earth since $FIRSTNAMEB0 had last fired a gun, since he had gone to the office building. He had gone there as a soldier to do his soldiering\u2014had gone...", "2_7_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was nowhere.\nThe scene was for the benefit of observers and participants on Earth, Mars, and Venus.\nThe show could be seen, and was being seen, on television screens and windows throughout the Solar System.\n$SURNAME0 looked around mildly. There was something regal about his unconcern at being the most watched man in history.\n\"Nice day,\" he said. \"It\u2019ll be splendid to have a Window on Saturn again.\" He took enormous, blue steps, and seemed to hover as he stepped, seeming to rise and fall on each foot in braked, fussless bounds as he approached $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nConstitutional Zero approached.\n\"I\u2019m glad you could make it, Buddy,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"You can\u2019t do this to me, $FIRSTNAMEM1,\" said $SURNAME1.", "1_23_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to midnight</span>. At the hairdressing academy, the students were under their dryers. At the mental hospital, the nightmare of the mud was beginning for helle Nelm. At the camp for the deaf, the baby made a deep, boxlike hooting sound, part of it a kind of rumble. It was dark brown. No one knew what to think. Best just to ignore it, since it was so much like the sounds of actual babies. Best not to dwell on it, especially since the baby couldn\u2019t really make any sounds of its own yet, being not much more than an embryo. Lighted cigarettes flipped at the air with dry little fwuffs in the camp cabins. There was the high, weird din of metal utensils against metal plates, the dull swish of wind. The littlest child was wakened in the night. \u201cMama!\u201d she screamed, because the noise had been so upsetting, so weirdly out of place, that she had sucked in her breath and sucked in hot smoke at the same time. When her mother turned on the light to see what was wrong, the little girl could not see. She went straight for the blinking, red alarm of the digital clock.", "2_8_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-two in the morning</span>.\nIts name was ship.\nNo one was home.\nNo one was on board.\nNo one was around.\nNo one was near.\nNo one was looking.\nNo one was watching.\nNo one had reason to watch.\nNo one had reason for concern.\nThe ship was clean.\nThe ship was a whistler.\nThe whistling of the ship was in a man\u2019s ears.\nHis name was $SURNAME2.\nHe was eating breakfast.\nThe breakfast was smoked head, grits, and coffee.\nThe breakfast was served in the candlelight of a yurt. The yurt was in a camp. The camp was in the woods. The woods were in the Great Desert of Wyoming.\nThe sunlight, slanting through the window of the yurt, fell upon the ankles and mudclotted boots of the man called $SURNAME2.\nThe sunlight fell on the crystal pendants dangling from $SURNAME2\u2019s earlobes.\nThe sunlight, reflecting off the crystal pendants, cast thin webs of pain into $SURNAME2\u2019s eyes.\n$SURNAME2 sneezed.\n$SURNAME2 shivered.", "2_4_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-six in the morning</span>, New York City time.\nAt the small town of Spaceview in Amazonia, Rio Mudas, Brazil, smoke was still rising above the trees, though of course everyone was long accounted for. All of Spaceview\u2019s inhabitants, numbering one hundred and eleven, had vaporized at four thirty-three.\nThe town of Spaceview was not impressive. There were no paved roads. There were fifty-eight square miles of rubber trees, over a hundred acres of bananas, under twenty of manioc. The streets were merely muddy footpaths between little shacks. Electricity had been brought to Spaceview only three years before. The whole town had cost $25,899 to build.\nCaptain Haskins, working on what was happening from a ship above Brazil, still was asking over and over, \"What the hell does God want with a million dollars? Why does he want it now? What this God want with a million dollars?\"\nAnd the time was four thirty-seven in the morning, New York City time.\nHome Ingenio, Costa Rica", "0_23_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven eighteen in the evening</span>. I noticed as I hurried through the casino that the tables were quitethat the festivities were waning. It was just then that I saw the doorman at the Riviera, a genial giant of a fellow, casually hurl the towhee off the sidewalk, under the wheels of a passing cab.\n\"Bang!\" he yelled.\nThe towhee was totally destroyed.\nI didn't have time to stop and examine its remains. I went directly to my suite in the palace wing. Nobody was following me. I was sure of that now. I was absolutely alone, as alone as a man can be in the big city, and I had work to do. I needed all my concentration, all my perspective and sense of proportion, if I wanted to plot my way through what promised to be the difficult night ahead.\nFirst I took a bath. Then, wearing my red silk pajamas, I sat in the giant crimson armchair facing the giant Venetian mirror and tried to get my thoughts in order... In the mirror I saw myself sitting in the chair.\nIt looked comfortable.\nI liked the color of the red pajamas against the white flesh.\nI like red. It makes me think of wine and blood. Blood...", "2_22_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to eleven o'clock in the evening</span> in the nation of England.\n$SURNAME0 welcomed the new day without blenching. He was not one jot less dead than he had been the night before. He was not one whit less high and mightier.\nWith the slow, erudite movements of a watchmaker, protected by the thick, warranted-sterling depths of a mind richer by far than the entire Solar System, $SURNAME0 began to clamber down the dune nearest his gilded kayak.\nMoments later, brisk firecrackers exploded in the palace on Mars and in the palace on Ganymede and in the palace on Titan.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s wives had renewed their contract.\nOld Salo, $SURNAME0\u2019s dog, looked up hopefully at his master. Salo was less a dog than a machine for converting grain into dog by natural processes. His master had a finger buried in Salo\u2019s rear, a procedure $SURNAME0 found most interesting.\n\"What, Salo?\" said $SURNAME0 drily.\n\"Yowr, yowr,\" said Salo in a hollow voice. This was the Martian word for Go, team!\nTHE MACHINE", "2_14_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and way out here on the California coast. The chauffeur spoke naturally and without surprise, though he was evidently referring to a class that had met at six o\u2019clock in the morning.\nWhen $SURNAME1 had come out of space ship no. 47 in the brush alongside the old north-and-south landing strip, he had noted the approach of a vehicle that might have been a bus. He could not be sure, from where he stood, he having just completed a fifty-two-hour flight from Earth.\nThe region around the landing strip was a mess. The structures of the colony had long since been abandoned to nature. No attempt was being made to maintain the place.\n\"A very nice class,\" said $SURNAME1 to the nameless, pleasant-voiced head over the rim of the drinking cup.\n\"I\u2019ve enjoyed many of them,\" said the pleasant-voiced head, \"and that\u2019s the truth.\" It slipped a little sideways on the shoulders in a beginning movement of farewell. The head and shoulders turned away.\n\"Thank you very much,\" said $SURNAME1 to the back. \"Good-by.\"", "2_15_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe war between the Tralfamadorians and the Guardians had been going on for a billion years.\nThe war had been called The Great War.\nAnd, considering the awe with which the Tralfamadorians regarded the tiniest microbes on the planet Earth\u2019s smallest crumb, and the conceit with which the Guardians disposed of entire galaxies without regard for the feelings and aspirations of their peons, each race was right to call its own war The Great War.\nA chthonic, atmospheric sigh sounded in the vicinity of the beetle-shaped ship.\nA spot of blue fire appeared on the underbelly of the ship.\nThe fire winked on.\nThe fire winked off.\nWhat happened next can be expressed only as the feeling that happens when a drowsy man leaning back against the rear wall of a cave sees something that makes him go to the mouth of the cave, lean outward with an expression of awe on his face, and wave urgently with both arms.", "2_0_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-three a.m.</span> and I was all alone in a rotten, dangerous nightmare. The noise in the next room was getting louder. I could feel berserk energies attacking the walls of my consciousness. Drugs? Sex? Thrashing chains? I didn't know what awaited me up there. A siren somewhere in the distance: Help, coming too late? I took a deep breath, held it and eased the gun into my waistband, around the left side - for easy access.\nAnd then suddenly I heard heavy boots on the floor above me. Two men in uniform came bursting out of the stairwell and into the upper hail. The elevator wasn't working. \u201cWhich room?\u201d they shouted. I pointed down the hail, standing behind the two solid mass of muscle at the ready stance. I couldn't let them walk into that room alone.", "1_14_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-five in the afternoon</span>. The place was a crummy cafe in Lower Downtown Denver, with a Greek bartender. Miles up the road in the next county, at the exact same moment, the third of the three friends was also sitting abstractedly in a place of drink\u2014in his case a riverside cabana in Glendale, California, where he happened to live. His name was Tim. He was drinking rum and Cokes, and wondering what the hell ever became of his life. He also was thirty years old. Like his old Denver pal, he was childless and jobless. And like his old Denver pal, he had only a high school diploma to his name. But the resemblance pretty much ended there. Where the Colorado kid was wiry and bowlegged, the California kid was smooth-chested and almost feminine in his movements. Where the Colorado kid was naive and introverted, the California kid was sophisticated and outward-bound. Where the Colorado kid dreamed wistfully of someday owning a ranch so he could breed marvelous animals, the California kid already owned two ranches and dreamed of someday soon owning a third ranch so he could breed more marvelous animals. Where the Colorado kid was prone to constipation and had never even touched alcoholic liquor before enrolling in the Marines, the...", "2_5_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-eight in the morning</span> and I pulled up to the American Airlines terminal. Right there on the glass doors I saw the words MESSAGE PENALTY\u2014$25.00. My god! What a bummer. It was always something. This time it was the goddamn rent\u2014a\u2014car . . . lock. I left the key in the ignition and lunged toward the check\u2014in counter, where a pretty girl was saying, \u201cGood morning, sir. Will you be checking any baggage this morning?\u201d\n\u201cUh...yes!\u201d I gasped. \u201cOne bag, starting outrelations. I HAVE TO GET ON THAT PLANE!\u201d Our regular check\u2014in girl had just gotten off work and this was apparently a replacement girl.\n\u201cJust one moment,\u201d she said.\nI was in such a frenzy by now that she was slow as cold honey. I could see the clock on the wall\u2014it was racing. Every second counted. This was no time to be fiddling around with forms and questions: \u201cYes . . . I\u2019d like a window seat ...what the fuck difference does it make where the seat is?\u201d", "0_0_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past midnight</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized at twenty-nine thousand feet, and free fall came to an abrupt end.\nThey were supported unhittable force beams more cunningly contrived, more huge in magnitude, more terrifying and sublime than anything the Martian war machines were capable of hurling.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 now hung in the air at a point exactly opposite the mansion stoop.\n\"Rrrr,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB4, perceiving Old Salo, who had crept from the mansion.\n\"Rrrr, Rrrr,\" said $SURNAME0, stroking the quivering pup\u2019s shimmering golden flank. $FIRSTNAMEB4 was so lovely. There was nothing, really nothing, that could compare with the beauty and sweetness of a well-behaved dog\u2014except, of course, the beauty and sweetness of a well-behaved woman.\n$SURNAME0 looked down at the almost useless man on the springy board floating beneath his feet. \"Skip?\" he said. \"Are you there?\"\n\"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\n\"Can you hear me?\" said the almost useless man.\n\"Yes,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"The button...", "1_13_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one o'clock in the afternoon</span> and we rolled into Hollywood. Old Bull Lee's house was on Franklin near that skip-stop Beverly Hills Hotel that is really West Hollywood. The whole crazy city was spread out below us. You could see all of downtown L.A. and the harbor and out across the mountains to the west. It was hot as hell. The sun was fifteen feet in the air. The new car had no top and the wind-wheel was a flop. I rolled up all the windows and turned on the air-conditioning, and we crawled along Franklin, past Will Rogers Park, to Lee's house. We were too early for dinner and there was nowhere to go, so we drove around Beverly Hills aimlessly, stalling inquiries as to a magic car. First a cop stopped us. We were going thirty along a fifteen-mile-per-hour street; but he just looked at us, smiled, and waved good-nat sweetly. \u201cYou boys not from around here, are you?\u201d Radford had mumbled something inept about being from Indianapolis, but we carried Montana and New Mexico licenses, and I think the cop intuitively knew we were from out of town and clueless. Our next inquiry was at a gas station: an attendant twice our...", "1_12_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-two p.m.</span> Eastern Standard Time precisely when Moncrieff handed the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibulum to Ms. Lewinsk, the first person ever to bridge backward and forward through time, when Moncrieff handed Ms. Lewinsk the Infundibulum it ran by chance into a world tube, a transistor, kindly provided by $SURNAME0. The transistor had been turned to a program called Skipper Christian\u2019s Guitar School. The show had just reached an exciting point, where a tape demonstrating a static chord was about to expire.\nA preset lightning bolt, aimed by Fate and the Martian Confederacy at Newport Tower in twenty minutes, was about to strike and smite Cosmic Squatters\u2019 Day into rubble.\nSkipper Christian was showing how any guitar, dirty or clean, can produce a nifty version of the same chord. All one had to do was strum the strings halfway. \"The static chord,\" said Skipper, \"is almost as old as the human race. Almost everybody earth knows it.\"\nAnd now came the static chord itself, boomed out by hundreds of thousands of guitars all over the world, thanks to the transistor:\nMelancholy Juliets who, according to Skipper, had the courage to strum...", "1_22_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-two in the evening</span> in San Narciso. The child, looking down from the heights, suddenly saw his mother in the lit window of the Oedipus Complex\u2014saw her droop, saw her begin to collapse. He would recall this much years later: her stiffening, the lines growing crevassed in her face and then vanishing as the expression went out of it. Things appeared to be coming loose inside her; her armpits started blossoming orchids. Spikes of hair tore through the pink ruffled dress she wore. Shaking, without transition, began all over her. When it ended, a new expression had come to rest like a stranger on her face. Sweet Billy, looking down, saw an angel, her eyes blank, fall through the lit room and disappear. She had become an angel by dying. And like an angel she had preserved the will to fall, had willed the fall, the sure and irrevocable fall, had fallen with no twinge of fear and now hovered safe somewhere out of sight. Her empty dress collapsed across the bed and then settled slowly, gently, to the floor.\n\u201cToo late,\u201d said a cop in the street, and leaned on his club. \u201cShe lost it,...", "0_17_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The chronometer read exactly 111,111,111. The name of the ship was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s original ship, now restored to him by the Army:\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s Red Beef Co. Battleship Business Syndicate II.\nThe chronometer announced, softly and musically, that it was five o\u2019clock in the afternoon. On a television screen over a phonograph in the corner, there appeared the head and shoulders of $FIRSTNAMEF1, the pretty red-headed mate of $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was twenty-three years old. She was one foot nine inches tall. She weighed fifty-five pounds. She had chestnut hair, green-gold eyes, a pert snub nose, a dimpled chin. She was clean, soft, healthy, intelligent, wise, companionable, cheerful, and in her eyes and mouth there lurked, often only for an instant at a time, an unspoken sadness.\nHer only imperfection was the cast in her right eye.\nShe swayed her shoulders to an unheard dance beat. Her bright, dimpled nude body on the screen licked her finger, poked it in her navel, rotated the digit.\n\"Know what I\u2019m doing, Unky?\" she said...", "0_1_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-eight in the morning</span>... and there was bugger-all to do but drive around and watch the pitch darkness roll over another brand-new day on the Strip. Finally I parked in the lot of the Bonanza Hotel & Casino\u2014at the first corner the street\u2014and went inside to kill time till dawn. As I walked past the gambling tables, I felt like I was running full tilt down an infinitely long road and had just reached the first major stopping point in territory I\u2019d never covered before, but where everything was instantly and eerily familiar. I felt like a hopeless addict, finally hooked into the D. monkey, dropped dead smack in the middle of a whole culture of addicts. Vegas is the most bizarre town I've ever seen. What goes on here is so freaked out and decadent that it simply resemble the essence of a civilization that has passed its peak and is vomiting up its own poison. I saw a fat woman standing naked on a balcony, waving frantically for help: she looked like she\u2019d just stepped out of a Buick ad\u2014but she was surrounded by a curious crowd that seemed to be enjoying the show. I later spotted the same woman being escorted out of the hotel lobby in straitjacketszen Fury, her eyes flashing terror...", "1_12_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after noon</span>. Twelve o\u2019clock dawned and it was dark again, all within the space of ten seconds.\nWhen the chrono brought 12:01 back into existence, $FIRSTNAMEF0 and the chrono had reached the absolute center of the merry-go-round. $FIRSTNAMEF0 had backed her horse up against the cannon. Now the horse lay on its side, legs in the air. The goat was crouching in a ball, head on the ground. The bear was up on its haunches, looking around wildly.\nThe merry-go-round was still going, but $FIRSTNAMEF0 had stopped its movement.\nThe one-time Queen $FIRSTNAMEF1 unstrapped the little NASA bag from her waist and took out a tremendous pair of scissors. She used the scissors to cut the hair from her own head. She threw her shorn locks onto the raceway beneath her feet, into the scuffed dust.\nThese events were witnessed by a farmer, Arnold Gudmundsson, who had crept into the carnival enclosure some time ago to steal a ride on the carousel with his son. He was concealed by the pony wall, and escaped after the murder. This is his description of $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s final behavior:\n\"She run around the circle where...", "2_7_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-three in the morning</span>, Martian Central Standard Time.\nAnd all during the blitzkrieg of formations that preceded the turnover from $FIRSTNAMEB0 to Prewitt, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was unaware that he and his company were being observed by a secret agent of the opposing force.\nColonel Ricardo Carlos Liberius, the political commissar of the Continental Army of Mars, was the agent.\nHe was watching the company wander in circles around the knoll from the concealment of a hollowed-out burrow teeming with the biggest blue-striped spiders that anyone had ever seen. There were one thousand of the spiders, and they weighed two and a half pounds each.\nColonel Ricardo Carlos Liberius, who was reputed to be the richest man on Mars, and thus the universally acknowledged King of the Yellow Cab Drivers, had made the two-hundred-pound spiders his companions since his days as a law student at Harvard University.\nHe had trained them to relay to him anything of interest that they might overhear.", "1_11_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-seven in the morning</span>.\n\"Is this the Enterprise?\" said a man.\n\"This is the Enterprise,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I want to report a homicide,\" said the man.\n\"A homicide?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Murder and suicide,\" said the man.\n\"Whose murder and suicide?\" said $SURNAME1.\nA very hearty laugh. \"Why, everybody\u2019s, I guess,\" said the man.\n\"Who is this, please?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I am the Chief of Police of $FIRSTNAMEM2,\" said the man. \"Chief Royal Beechnut himself.\"\n\"This isn\u2019t $FIRSTNAMEM2, Alabama?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Hell, no,\" said the man. \"This is $FIRSTNAMEM2,Kansas.\"\n\"Kansas?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Well, we ain\u2019t in Kansas no more,\" said the Chief of Police, \"but I got a strong feeling we\u2019re going to be there in about three minutes.\" He laughed again. \"That\u2019s why I\u2019m calling,\" he said. \"I thought if I called ahead, there might be someone there to meet us.\"\n\"Are you in a airplane?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Hell, no,\" said Chief...", "1_2_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-six in the morning</span> and we were in the suburbs of Denver. We had said good-by to Tim Gray in Riverton and now $FIRSTNAMEF4 was snoring in the back seat. I was driving alone and $FIRSTNAMEB3 was in the passenger's seat, babbling and moaning, marveling at Denver's trees and houses. I slowed down for a car ahead. \u201cAh, we'll never get there, we'll lose the whole damn night in Denver. Where are we going, $FIRSTNAMEB6? Do you know where we're going?\u201d I didn't know. I looked at the time and speedometer, as if to answer. \u201cNo,\u201d I said, \u201cwe don't know where we're going.\u201d But I was intending to ask advice from the Okie underground in Denver, a cousin of the Denver underground, if we could find any of them out of work at this hour. I had a Denver cousin myself, and knew a few boys. First we had to cross the cemetery near Colma, and then we would find the road going south. Meanwhile $FIRSTNAMEB3's madness and ecstasies got more and more complicated as we began to roll among the pleasant names and pretty trees of the rolling farmlands along the border of California -- Broom...", "1_22_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until eleven o'clock in the evening</span> on December 31, 1992, and I had been driving the Great Red Shark all over Beverly Hills desperately searching for a place where they might serve peppermint schnapps. Not just serve it but possibly even have it. I needed a drink in the worst possible way, and after an hour of terror in that twisting, fish\u2014smelling cockpit I felt I deserved one. I found a liquor store on Rodeo Drive but they only had peppermint schnapps in sachets, and although I bought two packs I felt wasted and ripped\u2014off, looking down at those paltry little envelopes nestled in the palm of my hand. Six lousy schnapps envelopes. Might as well have been seeds, or spores. I needed a big bottle and I needed it fast.\nI took the Snakepit back towards Sunset and roared up the ramp to the top of the cloverleaf at Interstate 110. Then I made a left turn and we headed back south towards Malibu. I had seen a liquor store right on the beach. It would be crawling with Con Temptation fans and Sinatra\u2014worshipping hoods but no matter; I\u2019d just run the damned thing down. Drinks were more important than life or...", "1_23_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> when the Ricciardo middleweight taxicab, its motor idling, came to a stop at the curb of our estate. Twenty yards up the street was the entrance to our house, a beautiful Georgian structure, now dark except for the lighted windows of the upstairs rooms where $SURNAME1, Gregg, and myself were getting ready for slumber.\nJudith had been in bed, but Antoine had given her a scare by turning off most of the lights in the house, permitting only a few candles to burn in the downstairs halls.\nEverybody was up and about, I discovered, when I peeked out of my door after the noise of the taxi stopping brought me to my feet.\nOn the second-floor landing I saw $SURNAME1 waving from an open window, his amusing boast that he was signaling me with the flashlight from his gold watch bounded by the fact that it was a good deal too dark for anybody to see anything on the watch. His watch was of gold, he said, so that he could signal anybody in the world by means of the rays of the sun.\n\"What\u2019s this?\" I asked, when I got to the taxicab, and found the driver and his fare to be a green, scumcovered Martian,...", "0_17_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-five in the afternoon</span> in Paris when the concierge told Gregory to go with his passport to the Prefecture de Police de l\u2019Air and the Mairie Annexe de l\u2019Etoile for a carte d\u2019identite as Fran\u00e7ais, et pas n\u2019importe qui. This took over an hour and a half. The American Embassy was no help. Gregory waited on a bench of pebbled leather until a pouty girl in a suit came along and asked him to move. A painter, who seemed to be protege\u201c and secretary to an Embassy vice-consul, heard Gregory\u201cs sad story, advised him to re-enter the Embassy in the morning, face whatever furor was raised, and get a carte d\u201cidentity hurriedly, thereafter.\n\u201cBut meantime I\u2019m an apatride. That\u2019s what they said at the Mairie.\u201d\n\u201cI know. Awful, isn\u2019t it,\u201d said the painter. He patted his pockets, looking for a cigarette. \u201cHey, Henry,\u201d he called over to the vice-consul, \u201cyou got a couple of cartons?", "0_3_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-eight in the morning</span>. At the table were two dopers, Red and Tim, and, of course, $FIRSTNAMEB3 and myself. The big moment finally came when $FIRSTNAMEB3 looked at his watch every two minutes and said, \u201cOh, Marie and Ed Dunkel must be in Denver now, they're finally on their way, it's too late now, $FIRSTNAMEB6, you and I'll never see our fine hero again. We've lost him.\u201d And even in the dreadful silent center of his talk the one idea honeyed and stuck and bound us -- we were all thinking of poor little Carlo in the car. \u201cYeah, he's probably out like a light,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB3, \u201cand old Carlo is thinking it's just another ride with daddy and tomorrow daddy's gone and Carlo's left alone again, isn't that just like daddy.\u201d He was sobbing. He went to the phone to call Jane in the bedroom. Now at last he had come to his final grief -- Carlo. And it was time; life had no further interest or WASTE ROYAL 11 purpose for him. He had gone through everything. Nothing more could happen to him. He was over the edge, there was nothing to think about or do. But he still had to...", "2_20_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-three in the evening</span>, too.\nThere is no place in the Universe farther away from its the green fields, its pleasant wooded hills, its historical sites, its windswept vales, than Mercury. A man feels very lonely at Mercury.\nIt was eight thirty-three in the evening, too.\nThere is no place beyond the Universe of galaxies more lonely than Spiral Nebula M33, Andromeda. There is no place farther away from home than the Trifid Nebula, sometimes also called M33. A man feels very lonely at the Trifid.\nIt was eight thirty-three in the evening, too.\nThere is no time, as we know it on Earth, at the heart of a blue-white star.\nIt was eight thirty-three in the evening, too.\nThere is no place that is more frightening, where you are farther from any help, than just outside the event-horizon of a black sun.\nIt was eight thirty-three in the evening, too.\nThere is no time, as we know it on Earth, in the heart of $FIRSTNAMEB2, in the Milky Way Galaxy, in the Universe beyond the Universe of galaxies.", "0_1_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past one o'clock in the morning</span>. One year ago the night I had come to San Francisco with $FIRSTNAMEF4, I hit a line drive on the nose at a dance and ran off to the bus station with a bloody nose. At this point in the current oration in my head I recalled the saga of my nose. Now the nose stood out on my face like the beak of a parrot, a nose like the nose of a drunken sailor who, vowing never to get drunk again, fires a torpedo after the departing ship. It twitched nervously at imaginary sounds. Just now, thinking of the New Worldcon car ride, my nose began to quiver and I had to fix my thoughts on the sign at St. Louis, which I saw in mind's-eye view: ROOMS, COLORED GIRLS, 25\u00a2, it said, although St. Louis was a thousand miles behind. \u201cLordy, I'm hungry!\u201d said the apparition in red panties. \u201cWell, let's go down, downstairs, in the lobby, and eat,\u201d I proposed. \u201cOh, wait till morning? I just got to think.\u201d I began to beg and beg. \u201cCome on, let's go eat; I...", "2_4_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt fifteen minutes past four o\u2019clock, $FIRSTNAMEB0 woke up, felt terribly sick and empty, remembered that there was something to be worried about. He opened his eyes numbly. He saw red and green colors continuing to spiral. The colors were being fed by things moving up $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s carotids.\nHis thinking was foggy and wet.\nHe remembered the mission report.\nStill groggy, $FIRSTNAMEB0 reached up and started doing things to himself\u2014to his carotids.\nThe colors grew weak and then died stead.\nWhoof! said $FIRSTNAMEB0, and passed out again.\nAt twenty-four minutes past four o'clock, $FIRSTNAMEB0 woke up again. Already, little whirlpools of pale green were forming at the tops of his carotids.\nBy forty-eight minutes past four o\u2019clock, the sleepiness was gone from $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s eyes. His heart and lungs and brain were comfortingly blanketed with harmonium. There was a subtle, normal rhythm to his breathing. There was something definitely reassuring about the way a man that big could still need just eight breaths to say \"human tobacco.\"", "0_2_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-five in the morning</span> and at the time the only other people in the room were two strangers, both males with strawhats, plaid shirts, and cowboy boots, who were fast asleep in the backseat of my parked White Whale. I had spotted them earlier, through my binoculars, when they\u2019d pulled into the pumps. As I watched them pump the gas, I\u2019d wondered if my car was about to be stolen. But then they went into the station, without bothering to look around, and I realized this was a trap: They\u2019d come for Dingus and me. And for some reason, somebody very high in government circles was determined to stop us. That\u2019s why there were two men, in two different cars, each one spotted by me as it arrived, both of them advertising Secret Service men like nobody\u2019s business: Threesixletter word for agent, for Christ\u2019s sake. Not to mention the four New York cabs full of Washington counterparts that had come screaming across the Colorado border only a few minutes before we\u2019d reached town. Some guys even had television cameras, mounted fore and aft on the dashboards of their cars. I\u2019d seen all that, and had decided on discretion anyway...", "1_10_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>, and the year of the aforesaid group-equation, though it matters little, since the sources of all human variation lie in the same maze and may sometimes interpenetrate.\nThe place was the northwest corner of Connecticut and Rhode Island Avenues, in Washington, D.C. The building stood in the very center of that vast web of Government, stretching in all directions, where pale spiders cower at their fevered webs.\nThe man was named Rudolph. Nothing could be discovered about his first name or his last. Rudolph was, as far as could be determined, not merely civil service, but civil service head cheese. He had no superiors and not one subordinate.\nThe building in which Rudolph languished was known to city directories as the Copper-wine Building, but to the half-million who constituted its life it boasted no name at all. It was Rudolph himself who had named it the Center of the Universe. He never tired of his titular joke.\nRudolph's duties were not exactly what one would expect the head cheese of anything to have. It is true that he stopped all employee complaints with a word, and that that word was usually \"no.\" He also had to take roll-calls twice a day, and...", "2_23_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-nine in the evening</span>.\nThe man in the gazebo was never seen again.\nHe had lived on the Moon one hour and thirty minutes. That was all.\nNobody on Newport Greenland saw or heard him go.\nA schoolmarm in Arkansas or Wyoming who played a faithful rendition of the role the cosmos had written for her would carry the message to the faithful everywhere. The message was good news to everyone but the Temporary Saturnians, Stoners, and Junkyard Juggalos of America.\nShe would say: \"It\u2019s wonderful\u2014the way we keep bumping into people we know on the Moon. My own husband ran into our old back fence up there. He said he knew his way around the Moon better than anybody since he\u2019d only been floating around in his back yard in Texarkana all those years.\"\nAs the message from one Temporary in temporary exile was passed along, it would become embroidered. The embroidery served well to lead the faithful about by the nose. The message became more and more wondrous, and each wonder in the message begot a dozen more wonders in the mind that received it.\nThe faithful had a boundless appetite and vigor for carrying the message.", "0_22_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>, and Bond was walking through the palms and ficus trees of the Pourtal\u00e8s depopulated terrace, when he felt the first rumble. He stopped and listened. The rumble swelled to a distant shuffle and then there was a crack, a rumble and a roar that rolled and chattered off among the mountains.\nAt the same time there was a brilliant flash to the right and then another, and the distant blackness of the sea burned white and then there was a colossal rain of sparks and a curtain of flame went up into the night rain with a hissing crackle and rumbled away into the distance and there was dead silence again.\nMen must be dying down there, and treasures being destroyed, and the life of the social order cracked like twigs in that lightning and thundered to dusty bits in that rain of fire and iron. Yet the wind had hardly shifted, and there was only a smell of fresh smoke in the air.\nThe sea breathed softly in the bay, and far away a motor-car growled down the Baie des Fourmis.\nBond walked on, thinking that the words \u201cNapalm cocktail\u201d had been devised for just this moment. Then, conscious again of the...", "0_19_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past seven o'clock in the evening</span>. There were traces of snow in the air. In the gardens were two sculptured sheep. One of them was chipped just above the left horn.\nSo Miss Lee came to a place that was called Branches, and there in a field of ferns by the side of a small pond she found a bronze plaque. The plaque was set flush with the earth, and it said:\nHere stood the House of Blaize.\nNo memorial will be put up\nThe rain will come and the waters will flow down,\nAnd all of these things, before men can unbury them,\nWill be gone and will have vanished away.\n* * *\nHOW NOT TO GIVE A PARTY\n\u2014AND SUCCEED\nWHEN Mrs $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas came home from the hospital, after having had her gallbladder out, it was to a new house. She had sold the old one, or rather had it confiscated, to pay medical bills. This was a transaction she had no personal part in; her ex-husband Miles, whose lawyer, she assumed, had arranged the whole thing. In any case the sale hadn\u2019t meant anything to her. She hadn\u2019t put any heart into the house...", "1_0_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve eighteen ante meridiem</span> when I shambled into the office of the atrociously expensive agency that represented me, and the first everybody said when they saw me was \u201cWhere have you been? We were worried.\u201d\nI was told by my red\u2014faced coughing principal agent to go home and get some sleep and be in the office by seven the next morning\u2014which meant I would have to be up at three\u2014forty\u2014five, for the fifty\u2014mile trip. I said \u201cO.k.,\u201d and went back to the sidewalk to get my car, but the janitor said it was gone, so I walked to a bus stop and rode to the parking lot where my car was usually parked, but it was gone from there, too. It was on this lot that I had been swindled out of my original car two years before; a lot\u2014keeper had taken it out one night, ostensibly to be washed, but when I reported it stolen the insurance company would not pay up because the car had never been properly licensed and therefore could be driven, they said, by just about anybody. So I had gone down to the motor\u2014vehicle bureau myself and paid the registration, plate\u2014changing fee and the new\u2014car excise, and...", "1_17_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-eight in the afternoon</span>. An air liner, announcing itself with a slow roll of drums, was approaching Washington from the southwest. By an irony of soothsaying, this ship was the Titanic. In New York she had been retrofitted with colossal fins. Her great length, now two thousand feet, was oversweetened by every electronic device known to man.\nThe Titanic flew false colors as she passed Chicago and pecked her way northward along the valley of the Yellowstone. There she was challenged by rocket batteries in five-minute relays. Patrol ships tried to stop her at Twilight, Crookston, Worthington, and the Montana Wildcat.\nNo flag on earth would have made the Titanic yield. Only when she neared Malmstrom AFB, in Great Falls, did she begin to transmit a desperate Morse of her own: HELP.\n\"They\u2019ll have an ambulance standing by at the airport,\" $FIRSTNAMEB0 said to himself.\nThe time was fifteen minutes later. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were flying through a region of snow-reflected purple dusk in high country east of Lake Tahoe. In another fifteen minutes the dusk would be gone, and utter night would move in. And in another fifteen minutes after that\u2014the time would be anything.", "1_21_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 left the $SURNAME0 estate. He took a roundabout walk through the ravine, keeping well under cover.\nOh, people of Newport and people whose place is among the high and the mighty! And oh, Skip, $FIRSTNAMEF0, $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEB4, $FIRSTNAMEB2! Oh, all you pathetic units in the armies of love! You are as lonely as Jesus in a thunderstorm!\nSpace tearfully flutters in.\nSpace is becoming suffused with a beautiful pink.\nWhat is the pink?\nThe pink, friends, is the light that begins to show when the electric light goes out in the windows of West Barnstable Hose Company 5, in Cape Cod, Massachusetts, U.S.A.\nThe light in the windows is the light that comes from the electric light inside the lives of people who are trying to find something in Life besides Life itself.\nIn one of the windows stands Eddie, holding his rag doll beside him. The doll is wet, and the doll smells as bad as anything that was ever wet and smelled bad has ever smelled.\nHell\u2019s bells! There goes Irma representing the women of Winthrop, Massachusetts! See her iron-gray hair fly! See her long, bony...", "0_8_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>. Summer school had not yet opened for the day, but the rest of the elephant-castle-building faculty had been at work for an hour or two.\nTo a philosopher of $FIRSTNAMEB4's age, nine in the morning seemed an unusually interesting time of day, for there were fifty-three minutes of sun still to come, but forty-eight minutes, thirty seconds of moonlight already behind.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 had never been conscious of any special interest in sun and moon before his strange conversation with $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 was a good antenna, a good receiver. He let $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s notion of their having a sworn enemy at work in their own classroom sink in, and it sickened him. The notion of a hidden enemy was real poison to a creature as social as $FIRSTNAMEB4. It threw all his instincts out of kilter.\nIn the Solar System, $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s home, the herd instinct was almost everything. $FIRSTNAMEB4 had been born at the top of the pecking order of a herd of creatures much like himself. He had been omnivorous, vegetarian, and cannibal, attacked chiefly by his own digestive system.\nThe constant interchange of identities was keen stuff for $FIRSTNAMEB4.\nHe would eat those beings...", "1_15_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was a fine day. The Martian guards who surrounded the $FIRSTNAMEM1 tank and who had been making restless movements in their pressure suits drew quietly together in their own ranks.\nThe time was nine minutes to four o'clock.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, pressed flat on the floor of the little cabin, now rained kisses on his son\u2019s elfin face for the last time.\nThe time was nine minutes to four.\nThe man at the controls took a last look out the portholes, found space and Earth and the black, pulsing invite of the quartz to be all alive and well. He made his preparations for the shot-put that would send $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Stony on their way.\nThe time was eight minutes to four.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his son held hands.\nTension mounted among the guards. One by one, their helmet radios crackled. The messages were the same: \"All quiet ... all quiet ... all quiet. \"\nThrough the portholes, the attraction of Earth was becoming less and less.\nThe time was five minutes to four.\nEarth and Mars began to draw apart.\nThe time was three minutes to four.\nThe radio in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s helmet suddenly sang: \"...", "0_12_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-eight p.m.</span> now on the first day of November, 1981. Lenore breathed. She twisted. Sue Shaw had been quiet. She was staring out the window at the sky, which through the window of Bat\u2019s office was orange-dark beneath a brassy black dull like dirty water. A plane went by far up, in the dull black, making a clean sound that died after a minute.\n\u201cDoes it not ever bother you,\u201d Lenore said to Sue Shaw, \u201cthat asthma might stop Mr. Bloemker from taking you to that party in Spain you seemed to be all set to go on for about a minute there earlier?\u201d\nSue Shaw turned back from the window. \u201cMaybe by the time we get there the insect will be cured.\u201d\n\u201cYeah, but what if it isn\u2019t? What if he is just chronically useless four five months out of the year?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll have fun, somehow.\u201d Sue Shaw smiled a teased smile at Lenore. \u201cI think Rick\u2019s pretty sexy, to tell the truth.\u201d\n\u201cRick?\u201d\n\u201cDo you suppose we\u2019ll be able to...", "0_19_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span> and the place was the Hotel Betelgeuse on 1250 West Betelgeuse Boulevard in the Red Star, Desert Royale, California.\nThe hotel was run by a man named Norman Knight. He was twenty-eight years old. His formal education was limited to four years in an Ohio high school. He had never been east of the Missouri nor as far south as Memphis. All he knew of the world he got from books\u2014and he read everything he could lay his hands on. This mania had made him the remarkable conversationalist he was, for in his reading he had come upon the mot juste again and again. He would lay a comment on a person or a situation as if he had thought of it himself, and then wait for somebody to say, \u201cHow true\u2014how very true!\u201d And even if people did not say it, he would repeat the remark, suiting inflection to supposed taste of listener. The result was that few people contradicted him, no matter how foolish his statement.\nThe Berkeley graduate who was now the proprietor of the Hotel Betelgeuse had that happy faculty of assuming things entirely false, and jumping to the conclusion that, since the facts did not contradict this assumption, the assumption must...", "1_14_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two nineteen in the afternoon</span> under a hot-beaded sky. A large crowd was on hand, including the parched couple from Des Moines, Iowa. They leaned together now in an awed huddle.\n\u201cThere she goes,\u201d the man whispered. \u201cThere she goes, my buddy.\u201d\n\u201cAmen,\u201d the woman said.\nThe rocket ship was pointed straight at the iron fretwork that formed the cage of the fearful Martian prisoner. The ship\u2019s flame blasted white-hot.\nAt the instant of blast-off, witnesses reported later, the faces of Malley and $FIRSTNAMEF1, the two hopeless Malleyites, seemed to glow and shimmer as if made of gold.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 lost toil and wore a beatific grin.\nMalley\u2019s eyebrows twitched, and he blinked.\nThe ship rose slowly, crunching sand under its beryllium-cored wheels, until the soles of its foremost row of roller-bearing structural members cleared the dry peaks of the white dunes. It paused ever so briefly and then disappeared upward into the sky, trailing a white contrail of smoke and flame.\nA sigh went up from the throng, and then came a silence.", "2_10_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-eight in the morning</span>, Earthling Eastern Standard Time. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was getting ready to tell all he knew.\nWhat $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew turned out to be almost nothing.\nThe big red doors in the side of the big white room slid noiselessly open. A young officer in blue stepped into the room, saluted, drew a finger across his throat.\nThe blue young officer was stocky, had a round face and innocent brown eyes. He wore a leather flying jacket and leather leggings.\nSomehow he managed to smile engagingly even though he was there to terminate an old soldier who might have been a friend.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s executioner left six perfectly spaced footprints on the floor as he took the ten steps to the stake. When he reached the stake his rifle was horizontal, pointing at $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heart. He swiveled it to a vertical, held it high with his right hand, lowered its muzzle to the nape of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s neck. He stepped away, turned, raised his arm in another salute, stepped out again through the big red doors.\nThe doors closed behind him.", "0_4_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-two in the morning</span>. To the north, red sky-fire hung over the pylons of the Bay Area Rapid Transit System, and over the tube-spears lining the Richmond\u2014San Rafael Bridge. But the sky over Reno was clear and calm.\nBrainy Metzger, proprietor of the sedaptively named Try your Luck Vacation Courts, had just strolled out on the balcony of his penthouse apartment in the glorified Tahitian Village.\nHe was a handsome fellow, five eleven in his Tigerhook shoes, and he was not yet thirty. His skin was dark from the sun of Vegas, and the penetration of past trials and tribula tions. His forehead and smile were white.\nEven and perfect, his teeth were marvels of the dental craftsman s art.\nHis hair was close-cropped, in a modified Marine style that looked oddly effete in conjunction with his tanned skin and his dark eyes. His Detroit-made suit was blue pinstripe, with a white silk shirt.\nHe was smoking a cigarette.\nBelow him the blazing Reno night was full of traffic sounds and the cries of suburban birds. A plane passed overhead, bound for the distant air terminals of San Francisco.\nAnd, down there,...", "1_0_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-seven a.m.</span> on a Sunday night and $FIRSTNAMEB6 Mineo was obviously very drunk. He wasn't inside the Tonopah Diner at all, but standing on the hood of his white Thunderbird outside the night window. There were lipstick marks all over the front of his shirt. \u201cThese broads,\u201d he said calmly. \u201cThey disgust me. Let me tell you my philosophy: treat a woman like a dog and she'll take you for a walk. Just before they took me here, I was at the Adonis Ball. You know what a ball is, don't you?\u201d He leered at me. I just looked at him coldly. I really loathed this kid. I was glad he was only the cousin of my girl. I didn't have the slightest fear that Lucy would ever marry this swine. How he had ever come to be a millionaire at such an early age, I didn't know and didn't give a damn. With the ruthlessness that years of experience had brought me, I was out to exploit his weaknesses. My options were not many. That night I was on the lam from Lucy and her family as well as from Bruce Frey. I needed a hideout, so I decided to use $FIRSTNAMEB6 Mineo's...", "0_7_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-six in the morning</span>. At that hour, the child abuse panel had only been going on for three minutes. The crowd was small.\nSome of the people in the crowd were friends of Steffi, who had come to support her. Others were members of the panel who, unable to get a babysitter for their children, had brought them along.\nThere was one girl, under seventeen, with a great blazing bush, heavily made up, and half a million dollars\u2019 worth of orthodonture, poised for world conquest. The father of her child, a gentleman between fifty and fifty-five years old, was standing next to her. He was an executive of something, under contract to an important anti-drug agency. This couple kept smiling at the chaos they had made of every area of their lives. In their world, they had places to go and lines to deliver. So far, they were doing wonderfully.\nThe room was crowded with people who were wondering what it was all about. Most of them just wanted to be somewhere where other people were having exciting things happen them.\nThere in the hot, crowded hearing room, no one was having a better time than young $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. He was having the time of his life. He sat...", "0_12_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-nine post meridiem</span> when $SURNAME1 took his place in the chrono-synclastic infundibulum. His place was one of a row of forty-eight on a gently curved bench. Standing in front of the forty-eight statues was a bearded man with a long, white, crooked knife in one hand, and a book in the other.\nThere were two other people with $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the bearded man in the chrono-synclastic infundibulum. One was Queen Stockhausen, who had sold $FIRSTNAMEB0 to the Army in the first place. She was now being repented of good and hard.\nThe other person was $FIRSTNAMEB1. $FIRSTNAMEB1 had a round, flat sheet of iron and was making it sing a happy song by striking it rhythmically against the stone wall.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and Queen Stockhausen were to go through the cleanser ahead of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the bearded man. They were going to turn the big iron wheel before the throne, in order to drain and flash-fire $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the bearded man.\n\"You\u2019ll learn to love it,\" the bearded man said to $FIRSTNAMEB0 when the others had gone. He put his book, a small Bible, on a ledge, undipped the long, white blade...", "1_0_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-three a.m.</span> Eastern War Time. As the young man in shirtsleeves completed the ritual that one of his compatriots had started only minutes before, an old man in a bathrobe at that very moment yanked the telephone out of the wall, grinding the plug into the hole, tearing it. Behind him people were being uprooted from sleep, being howled at, tipped out of beds, slapped. Somebody struck a match, illuminating an unpleasant, crowded, shabby room.\n\"You mad, crazy fool!\" somebody bawled.\n\"Yow!\" shrieked some woman in the darkness.\n\"You\u2019ll catch hell in the morning, you know you will,\" the fat voice bawled. \"It better be good.\"\n\"I shouldn\u2019t need you to tell me whether I\u2019m good or not,\" the old man with the telephone yelled back.\n\"Who you kiddin\u2019 with that line?\" yelled the fat voice. Presently the old man popped the plug back into the jack as though he were reloading a gun, then stalked out of the room. He pulled a frayed bathrobe around his bare shoulders and went downstairs. In the office, a dull light over a switch glowed.", "2_16_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The mood was winter twilight. She had parked the snort and walked to the campus mailbox. The sky had that look it gets just before the lights go on behind the clouds, as if it had something big to tell you, if you could only get close enough and listen. She found it chilly and slipped behind the bubble-shaped helmet on her head. Then into the box, pneumatic gate sighing, and a swirl of letters.\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 dropped four or five into her jacket pocket, carried the rest to the scoot and dumped them into the basket. About to kick-start, she noticed a male figure coming along the sidewalk, head down, concentrating on the stitch in his crotch. She thought of cruising on off, but instead got ready to flee on foot, if necessary.\nIt was only Adrian Prussia. \u201cThat\u2019s certainly feminine apparel you are wearing, Mrs. Metzger,\u201d he greeted her.\n\u201cThen how come you didn\u2019t address it to \u2018Miss Metzger\u2019?\u201d", "2_10_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nS lemon yellow sun was at its zenith. A lemon yellow moon was at its apogee.\nThe pair made up the only obvious clues in the naturally sunlit, naturally oriented landscape of the pit that was now the Canal Zone. The landscape did not look like a hole dug by human hands. It looked like a hole dug by nature. There were no marks of occupation. There was not even any litter. There were some footprints, but it was clear that the footprints had been made by Nature herself, during the brief rainy season.\nThe footprints led nowhere.\nFour bicycle-riding Mercury astronauts came to the edge of the pit. The lead rider dismounted, walked to the lip of the pit, stared into it. Peering down into the forty-three-hundred-foot well was like peering down the throat of the world. The well went down, down. It curved a little, so that the far end was hidden by mist.\nThe four riders were the only people within a radius of forty miles.", "0_4_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until five o'clock in the morning</span> when we got back. Remi was in the bathtub in $FIRSTNAMEB3's apartment, and he came out with his hands over his ears. Never had I seen such tortured looks on the faces of the three of them and myself. We began cutting up the food; in a few minutes the rug was covered with broken eggs, bones, rolls, coffee grounds, orange rinds, and paper napkins, and we were kicking this food around and stepping on it. Remi held his head between his hands and moaned. Now he saw what kind of spiritual pirates he'd picked up in Panama and took a look around him to see if there wasn't some way to slip out of it. He was trapped in whatever infinity $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I had reached in the madness of our hunger. But really under the externals that Remi saw there was the culmination of a love the three of us had for each other. Still, Remi, the cool hip intellectual hipster European who had seen it all -- and here he was in ragged pajamas and shirt, with tousled hair and sleepy and messy, throwing up his hands, tortured. He went to sleep on the floor on a blanket among the debris.\n\u201cNow let's clean up --...", "1_11_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when this young fellow named $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized aboard the spaceship of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, and their son $FIRSTNAMEB2. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was the skipmaster of Mars\u2014he controlled the skips.\nThe skips that had exterminated the war between Earth and Mars were now operating on private time schedules. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4, crossing space between Earth and Mars, had overshot their mark by almost three months.\nThe next skip to Mars wouldn\u2019t occur for almost three Earth-weeks, and the skip after that for almost three Earth-months.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s greetings to $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, and their son $FIRSTNAMEB2 were a touch histrionic but quite genuine. \"I hope you don\u2019t mind the inconvenience,\" he said, \"but the fact is that neither you, nor I, nor anybody else has much control over where the next skip takes us.\"\n$SURNAME1, $SURNAME0, and the boy looked at one another blankly, not knowing what to say.\n\"It is, of course, a depressing thought,\" said $SURNAME0, \"that the next skip...", "0_12_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after noon</span> and the place was Xanadu. Old Arnold $SURNAME0, so cruelly widowed three Martian days before, was in hisQuantity bang boat on the SunPicture Pearl Harbor, about to pay his first visit to the new monument on Mars.\nFamily and very close personal friends of old $SURNAME0 as well as a large and rather vulgar crowd of ordinary people were gathered on the dune. A system of amplifiers pumped music at the crowd through huge, pinball, ear-covering speakers.\nThe band was playing Substitutiary Locomotion again\u2014but this time it signified a family celebration, not a requiem.\nOnly the God of Thunder could accurately relate what happened next. Only the God of Thunder finds it easy to believe that a big, loud, vulgar Connecticut shade-tree mechanic wandered out of the inky void of space and threatened the peace, the prosperity, and the very lives of all the people in attendance.\nMilky glimpses of the visitor\u2019s face could be caught through the crowd whenever the visitor happened to be looking in a particular direction. The face was that of a rugged outdoor man\u2014bearded, sandblasted, burned lobster red by the awful sun of Mercury, blue-eyed...", "2_10_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-six in the morning</span> on Mars.\nThe Reverend C. Horner Redwing, a man who had been a friend of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 for twelve years, spoke to Mars from a microphone in the basement rooms of his church. He spoke to all of Earth, too, by means of radio and television. The reverend\u2019s powerful voice boomed out of the basement windows and through the doorways of the church itself.\n\"Oh\u2014my friends\u2014I begin my address to you\u2014my last address to you, I fear\u2014with a lamentation.\n\"I lament,\" said the Reverend C. Horner Redwing, \"that I have not been a better friend to a noble man who was once my friend, and may yet be again.\n\"I speak of the man who is responsible for our being able to see the fantastic sights we are about to see shortly.\n\"His name is $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0,\" said the Reverend C. Horner Redwing, \"and he is the man we should be honoring this very minute, this very instant.", "0_8_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>. This was Friday. At ten minutes past eight, Blaze Kavalier would be dead.\nBehind the marble slab lay six bodies in two rows of three. The bodies were poorly made and made poorly in their shrouds of white muslin. Five of the figures were about the same size, life-sized wax dummies or so poorly done that they looked like dummies. There was nothing noticeably wrong with the sixth. It wore a cape, and was a little shorter than the other five, but it was well executed and had an appealingly simple expression. Two boards at its head, supporting the head and the arm that extended, waxen, from the shroud, made it look at once peaceful, offering a suggestion of dreamy near sleep, and engaged in composition. At its head was a group of musical instruments: the drum, standing on a tombstone; the trumpet, slipped on the trumpet player\u2019s fingers; the double-bell horn, poised for a toot. How peculiar that it should want to be remembered as composing. There was almost a disagreeable life to the instrument and to the figure\u2019s limb. The line from the tip of the index finger to the thumb, in particular, resisted and put off death as though to...", "2_3_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a fabulous penthouse apartment in Newport, Rhode Island, overlooking thousands and thousands of tiny sparkling lights that were Liberty Island and the open Atlantic Ocean.\nThere were three rooms in the apartment, with a spiral staircase connecting them. The rooms were joined by a formal but cozy oak-and-leather library. Balustrades of green-veined, bold marble edged the library.\nThe apartment was owned by a fabulously wealthy lady novelist of fifty-one years named Patrice Lumumba Ford. She had had an affair, some years back, with Vance Warren, a Navy ensign, who, one night in Naples, Italy, fell overboard and was never seen again. She had sent Warren a postcard six months later, telling him that she was pregnant.\nShe had had the baby in Chicago, naming him Kelvin. She had spent enormous sums to keep him well protected during his childhood. Ellsworth, who had known Patrice in Haiti, where he was researching the voodoo drums, had orchestrated the intricate moves required to bring the little boy to Newport. He had borrowed an infant-sized butler from a member of the Duchess\u2019s bridge club.", "1_21_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-nine in the evening</span> when I got to New York. I left my bags with the clerk at the Hotel Albert, and went down to the bar to bat around awhile with the boys. Esme was behind the bar, but she didn\u2019t give me a flash. I played pool with a guy from Philly who couldn\u2019t control his break and drifted over to the juke box to listen to Ray Charles. Bright and early the next morning I woke up and called $FIRSTNAMEB3 at the Bard Motel in Julian. He was asleep.\nI said, \u201cWhere\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEF4?\u201d\n\u201cOh, she split. She had an appointment.\u201d\nI was ironing my shirts in the hot, steamy room. \u201cWell, when you see her, tell her I\u2019m in New York and would like to see her.\u201d\n\u201cYes, what\u2019s new in New York?\u201d\n\u201cNothing. It\u2019s crazy.\u201d I talked to a few people at the bar. Buzz was back in New York. I had a drink with him in the smoky morning barlight. Then I picked up my things and caught the 5:00 p.m. Western Fly...", "1_13_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe space was a room in the press section of the Convention Hall.\nThe room in the Convention Hall was a perch used by San Narciso reporters covering the Convention. It was an octagonal enclosure about eight feet in diameter, accessible by an staircase. Each of the eight sides framed a view. Three sides looked straight out, over a section of the proceedings. Five sides looked out over space, and afforded a wide and wonderful spread of the city to the east and south.\nThe south side of the cubicle was paneled in sound-deadening insulation, and held a battered, crackling public address system, electrical outlets, and wires leading here and there. Outlets served a telephone and two lampswith cheap perve, Victorian-style shades that gave the space a dim even lighting. Visitors to the press section of the Convention Hall thought the room and its view of the city very romantic.\nThe east and south sides of the room were often free of visiting newsmen when the time was two minutes past one o\u2019clock in the afternoon. That was when the daily ritual known as The Bombing started.\nRansom K. Fern, press liaison officer, looked at his wristwatch when the time was two minutes past one.", "0_16_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-three in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1, having broken into the power station, was in a position to have his own way with the great life-control machine that would shorten or lengthen the lives of Tralfamadoreans according to his own ideas.\n\"Hey,\" he said into his microphone, now that the show was truly his. \"Now I\u2019m in charge of when you live and when you die. Watch this group over here on this meter, for instance. Watch your temperature, yellow girl.\" He jabbed at his panel. \"Wow! You\u2019re shrill as a whistle and climbing.\"\nNow $FIRSTNAMEB1 went to the small group of Tralfamadorians who constituted the planeload that had arrived earlier in the day. At their particular wall, he employed his microphone again.\n\"Greetings again, friends,\" he said into the microphone. \"Knock knock. Ask who\u2019s there, fellow. Hello in there!\"\nNo sounds came back through the structure of the wall.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 sighed melodramatically, and switched his microphone on again. \"Finish your sandwiches, folks,\" he said into it. \"Finished yet, folks?\"\nThere still came no reply.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 shrugged. \"...", "2_12_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-four post meridiem</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s advance was now being televised to an immense audience back on earth.\nTo maintain audience interest, a soldier was supposed to display emotions with maximum variation in voltage. Emotions were represented on the television screen by the height to which colored patterns shot. To conceal from the enemy such patterns as treason, sorrow and celebration, which were deprecated, the patterns were colored in with masks.\nTurk Maub, the master technician, attended to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s television reception. He was a colorfully disreputable, scrawny, near-insect creature with overlarge yellow eyes, a pointed chin beard of pink, orange and green and yellow striped skin down the backs of his hands.\nTurk now busied himself with a voltameter that captured $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s emotions perfectly.\nTurk then taped a tiny microphone to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s sweat-blackened breast. The microphone was connected to a reporter hidden behind an observation window beside the control room stairway.", "0_6_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-seven in the morning</span> of the 11th of November, A.D. 2525.\nO bservant readers will by now have noted an awesome and paradoxical coincidence. The year 2525 is the year in which all of us who read books\u2014or who used to be able to read books\u2014now find ourselves.\nBut the coincidence went much deeper than that.\nThe place of that beginning was Mare Orientale. And Mare Orientale is the old name for the Eastern / Sea, the Far Eastern / Ocean, the / all of Earth\u2019s / Ocean. The name was changed, in A.D. 1994, because all remnants of / were totally obliterated in the great Tunguska explosion of 1908.\nFor that, see.\nOn Earth, therefore, in A.D. 2525, no one remembered the incident of young $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nOn Earth, in A.D. 2525, no one could rightly be blamed for forgetting $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nOn Earth, in A.D. 2525, mankind had more serious things to worry about than remembered gold-foiled chocolate kisses.\nFor instance: In A.D. 2525, mankind had less than eight months to make up its mind to do something...", "2_14_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two eighteen in the afternoon</span>.\n$SURNAME1 groaned again, sat up, opened the blinds.\nA portable platform had been set up on the shore of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Sea. On it was mounted an battery of microphone, loudspeaker, and television camera.\nAt the hub of the circle formed by the media personnel were two chairs\u2014one for $SURNAME0, one for a guest.\n\"Do you know your place, friend?\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice from without.\n$SURNAME1 didn\u2019t know what he was expected to reply. He just blinked through the drawn blinds.\n\"You and your wife are far from the best-looking people on Mars,\" said $SURNAME0, \"but you might still have a place in the chorus. Pay attention! There is to be a parade in just two minutes. If you and your wife attempt to make the grand entry, it will certainly mean the end of your earthly existences. Do you want to die now, friend? Yes or no?\"\n\"No!\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Good,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Make up the bed to look as though your wife lies therein, covers her entirely.", "1_8_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-eight in the morning</span> of a Wednesday in August in the year 1993. And, Hell\u2019s bells, the only thing to be done under the circumstances was to set up a giant, whomping, eight-foot-high $SURNAME0 statue of me in my very best gray flannel suit, my best sea-gray Hattie Carnegie evening gown and my best pair of bright red calf-skin Rockette boots.\nThe statue would stand in the meadow near my ten-million-dollar Malibu mansion. I would rebuild the mansion as a solarium in which the statue would reside.\nThe statue would represent me at my best. And what would the statue say to all mankind and womankind, both now and in all ages to come?\nCertainly not Hello, or Good-by, or What in God\u2019s Name Are You Doing Here? with a rising inflection. Certainly not. Few things would be less likely to remind mankind and womankind of what the highest aspirations of mankind and womankind are.\nWhat would the statue say? Nothing but this: That human beings, despite a hard-won understanding of their tendency to act like worms, can aim higher than worms. The statue would say this\u2014and all other true things beside: \"I...", "0_3_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-six in the morning</span>. I had spent fourteen hours sweating California back alleys in a fruit-based economy, watching the insane midnight face of America naked and wild and running in the dark. I had not made one single actual personal \u201ccontact,\u201d although I had come damn close. I drove back to the motel in Synanon nostrils...but first I went to the supermarket to load up on food. My Binger was full of Surf and turf. I wanted something else, some other kind of meat to balance off that heavy protein. Where, oh capital of the desert, where can I buy salad and a melon and a loaf of bread?\nI did not know. How complex and specific a problem it had suddenly become to find wholesome food. Twenty years ago the problem did not exist It had been perfectly routine; now it was the core of tragedy. A nut on the freeway at two in the morning, routing for Sin City and the American Dream, I did not even know where my next meal was coming from. I had a hunger for something fresh, for lettuce and carrots that did not come out of a can; for something you could eat with your hands and not from a styrofoam tray. I was like a mad dog on the freeway...", "0_14_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-five in the afternoon</span>.\nSuccessfully disguised as a Hollywood sound man named $SURNAME1 Screwup, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was bent over the bar of the $SURNAME0 estate\u2019s pavilion entertaining $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0. $SURNAME1 was twenty-two minutes ahead of time.\nThe temptation to make political or philosophical use of his privileged position was enormous. The temptation to reveal the hoax of chrono-synclastic infundibula and to reveal $SURNAME0 and the whole hierarchy of celestial pundits as impostors was tremendous.\nHe could, for example, describe his own coarsely-made experimental spaceship, parked in a $FIRSTNAMEM1 identical to the $SURNAME0 estate\u2014and describe how he was planning, in forty-eight hours, to zoom that contraption straight up to varicolored Mars and whisk $FIRSTNAMEM2 off to a glorious future in the Solar Promised Land.\nHe could describe this as a kind of Puttin\u2019 on the Ritz offensive, indicating his contempt for any paradise that was not Paradise Regained.\n\"Why has $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 appeared to me as an English butler? Why have you and all the other dignitaries tricked me?\" $SURNAME1 could say witheringly. \"If I am the One,...", "0_11_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-three in the morning</span>.\nThe limousine stopped at the podiatrist\u2019s building on Ocean Drive.\nWalter White let the doorman know he wouldn\u2019t be long. The doorman nodded. He knew.\nThe Countess was gone. She had had her clothing delivered to her penthouse, twenty miles away.\nThe limousine returned the patient to his waiting nursemaid.\nThat night, while they slept, all the clocks in all the hotels in all the world stopped.\nAt midnight, while they slept, all the clocks began to run again\u2014and this time they ran forty-five minutes ahead. Thus, midnight became one thirty-five.\nAnd a new day began\u2014March 23, 1957.\nHere and there throughout the world minor mishaps and inconveniences occurred. In New York, for instance, all the traffic lights at 108 feet above street level disappeared at midnight. They fell on Ninth Avenue and killed nine people.\nThe rain continued.\nUpstairs in the penthouse of the Newport Hotel the Countess was drinking again\u2014and thinking of her chronometric studies.\nThe particular thought in her mind just then was that a true science-fiction writer would have known how to handle the Clock story,...", "2_18_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six eighteen in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nA roar came from the crowd outside the palace. The roar did not die away. It kept on getting louder and louder and louder.\nThe crumbling sound of the roar meant that the mob had discovered the man from Earth\u2014who now stood unsteadily on the balcony of the palace.\nThe man from Earth was startled by the sound of the crowd. He twisted his neck so sharply to find out what was going on that he cracked the wood of the balcony rail.\nThe man who had come to Mars on a singing telegram from Venus was so drunk that he didn\u2019t even notice the danger he was in.\nNo one had bothered to tell him about the Martians.\nThe roar was now a tremolo. It was getting higher in pitch every second.\nThe man who had come to Mars on a singing telegram from Venus was having difficulty holding onto his bottle of whisky.\nThe tremolo was getting higher in pitch every second.\nIt had now reached a shriek.", "0_23_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-nine in the evening</span>.\nOh, women! he suddenly thought. Where's thystring? A what? A roam. A string to bound thee, pretties. In thou go. Zoop! Out thou fly. Tallyho! he thought. You'll never get me to the altar, dear, he thought. I'm the most iron man of all. Ask them.\n\"Hey\u2014Jones!\" he said.\nThat was just about the time Inez, who had heard the news about her husband about eight o'clock, was receiving Warren at her hotel.\n\"I haven't had a drink all afternoon,\" he said gloomily.\n\"Won't you have some tea?\"\n\"No. I feel sort of empty. I had a slug of Scotch about two o\u2019clock, and that\u2019s all.\"\n\"I thought you said you\u2019d been drinking all afternoon.\"\n\"Yes. But that little bit didn\u2019t count. It was just for a taste.\"\n\"How perfectly fascinating.\" She let him talk. She liked it when he was gloomy. Then he felt he had to say something. It brightened up his face.\n\"Well,\" he said, \"I was just wondering if...", "2_21_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>. Mr. Cohen, owner of the Falcon-surprise of department stores, was spending his Saturday evening in the exercise of his passion. His passion was fencing. He fenced with a fencing master. This fencing master was really just a boy, though an honorable, dedicated, skillful, and handsome one. His name was Richard Hughes. He was twenty and he lived in a small studio apartment two levels beneath the penthouse in the west wing of the building. He earned four dollars an hour working shifts at the perfume counter of the women\u2019s department. Mr. Cohen owned the building and employed the landlord, and so was able to have a special keyhole cut into the dressing-room door of the perfume counter, and so was able to control the hours of the perfume counter, and so to give Hughes pretty much the same hours as himself. Ten hours a day, five and a half days a week.", "2_4_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until five o'clock in the morning</span>. Intercom speakers all around the Drug Conference were making tinny announcements: \u201cMr. Charlie Bartlett, Room 673. Mr. Charlie Bartlett, Room 673 ... Will the owners of white Super Stock Chevy with California license 281\u2014QQQ please pick up the white courtesy phone.\" We could even hear distorted fragments of this rotten Scramble twenty floors below. And over all this confusion, unknon and unidentifiable, perhaps even unproduced music was coming from a loud\u2014speaker somewhere. \u201cWell,\u201d Mooney said, hudkug his hands deep in his pants pockets, \u201cwhere's your kit?\"\n\u201cI only brought that little attache case,\u201d I said. \u201cI figured we mostly just had to talk.\u201d Drug people are always suspicious of anybody who brings a camera to arrest\u2014type situations. It demands a certain amount of trust.\nMooney nodded. \u201cThat's not bad,\u201d he said. \"Charlie Gibson and Shirley MacLaine's due here at any moment. We're just waiting for this Guy Banister fella to show up. He's supposed to be the man who found LSD\u201425 in the Central Intelligence arsenal.\"", "1_0_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-five a.m.</span> when Remi Boncoeur and I, laden with white wine for the trip and olive-yellow beer for the night, showed up at the rat-run near the harbor of San Francisco. The night watchman was a scrawny old crab who looked like he\u2019d been left over from the Gold Rush of \u201949. He was dozing on an upright wooden stool. He opened one eye when we tiptoed up. \u201cA pleasantry of a night to you too, gentlemen,\u201d he said politely.\n\u201cHello there,\u201d said Remi Boncoeur in his Dracula whispers. \u201cHow much is the night duty?\u201d\n\u201cTwo dollars, sir,\u201d said Gold Rush.\n\u201cStories,\u201d murmured Remi. \u201cExtra for stories, veteran?\u201d\nSo in half an hour I\u2019m driving out to the airport, up the back way beyond Reno, with the top down, the white wine and olive-yellow beer sloshing in our guts, and Remi Boncoeur singing to me of his mysteries.\n\u201cWhy don\u2019t you write it down?\u201d I said. \u201cWrite...", "2_3_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-five in the morning</span>\u2014warm, damp, still. $SURNAME1 could make out the silhouettes of the thirty-two other cottages as he moved through the moonlight to $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s cottage. He did not expect to find Ernest Cobb there. He knew from telephone conversations with $FIRSTNAMEF1 that Ernest seldom visited her.\nBut it was always possible that Ernest had appeared tonight, uninvited. And it was absolutely vital that $SURNAME1 talk with him.\nThe wind, smelling of the beach and the ocean, wooed $SURNAME1 as he walked along the path. The wind was rich with a thousand forgotten memories.\nAt last, he came to $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s cottage. The curtains were drawn. $SURNAME1 rapped on the door.\nA moment passed, then the door was cracked by a chain.\n$SURNAME1 recognized the face in the narrow space between door and jamb. It was the face of a young woman with red hair, light skin, dark freckles. It should have been a beautiful face, but was not.\n\"Hi,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"What do you want?\" said the girl.\n$SURNAME1 looked down. \"Is\u2014is Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1 Mason, uh\u2014\" he said, \"there?\"", "1_1_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>, and this was the situation:\n\"I am going mad,\" $FIRSTNAMEM1 sprawled on the bed, clasping his head in his hands.\n\"I'm taking a perfectly ordinary trip,\" he had insisted only half an hour earlier. In spite of the opinions of the majority of humans, there was no doubt that it was, in reality, an Earthman, and not a native of Betelgeuse, who sat on the bed. Nobody knew what the natives of Betelgeuse looked like. Very few people had ever seen even a picture of one. The creature on the bed might have been born on another planet, for all anyone knew. He was about four feet high, and hideously ugly. Fortunately he was not visible to anyone except $FIRSTNAMEM1. He was sitting behind $FIRSTNAMEM1's left shoulder, sulking. Sometimes he vanished completely, but he was always there, $FIRSTNAMEM1 felt certain, just behind his lef shoulder, a grossly fatuous creature, with a huge head } ridiculous in shape, half pig and half doll. If you could have seen him, it would have been unmistakable even at a glance that he was heartbroken. His huge moist eyes were directed Insternk~ without any focusing power whatever. His mouth was puckered mor...", "2_19_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-six in the evening</span>. The place was Newton, Massachusetts.\n\"Tell you, it was a wonderful experience,\" said Norman Mailer, who was holding his big purple book about St. Th\u00e9r\u00e8se of Lisieux, a book that he had already written. \"Just so damned interesting, talking for an hour about politics with a bunch of intelligent and informed people. He gave me a copy of that article he wrote on your report on the Crimson\u2019s coverage of the Hungarian uprising, by the way; he says nobody left the room without one.\"\nChip looked into the refrigerator. It was a fancy refrigerator, with Lucite bins and humidity control and individual doors for packages, but all it contained was a pitcher of milk, a sliced-bread loaf in a paper bag, containers of Dill-Weevil, a bottle of catsup, greengage preserves, one ice cube, and lemon meringue pie, which was fading.", "2_7_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"What good would astronomers be, anyway?\" said the old man to $SURNAME1, \"if astronomers liked to stay indoors all the time?\"\n\"Who says we\u2019re astronomers?\" said $SURNAME1, shrugging.\n\"I seen your bags,\" said the old man. He spat.\n\"Still no blue,\" he said. The words were an expression of his contempt for all so-called modern things. Only an incurable snob would have a watch with clear, blue hands.\nThe old man shook his head so that his earrings made noises.\n\"I been stood here watching for fifty years,\" he said, \"and I ain\u2019t yet seen the sun so much as peek in at the church.\"\n\"You confirmed my suspicions,\" said $SURNAME1, \"about one thing.\"\n\"What\u2019s that?\" said the old man.\n\"That they come here,\" said $SURNAME1, \"just like I did\u2014from God-knows-where.\"\n\"They want to fool you,\" said the old man, \"into thinking they just come from St. Louis, or God-knows-where.\" He spat. \"They come from God-knows-where.\" He spat again.", "2_3_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 waited cheerfully. He could afford to wait cheerfully, because the time was about to change anyway. The time had to change, because he was about to accomplish something.\n\"See you in the basket, Skip?\" said $SURNAME0 genially.\n\"In the basket?\" said $SURNAME1. He meant in the future.\n\"See you then?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yeah\u2014yeah\u2014\" said $SURNAME1. \"See you then.\"\n\"Fresh eggs in the fridge,\" said $SURNAME0, referring to the refrigerator in the energy skiff. He was being eggshaped in the moment when time changed.\n$SURNAME0 and $SURNAME1 materialized again on the space platform three minutes later.\nThey materialized as smoke rings. The rings were sucked out of existence as cleanly as straws sucked exhausted milkshake from rank cylinders of wax.\nThe rings were sucked into the opening of the great tube.\nThe rings were sucked up the tube, where they were blown out the other end as a materialized $SURNAME0 and dog.\nWhen they had materialized, they gave the thumbs up, then touched the cycle-lock buttons of their space suits.", "0_20_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-three in the evening</span> of November the twenty-third, the maid was already up in the tower, playing her music and singing to herself. The music was piano music, it was Beethoven, it was the famous Sonata Path\u00e9tique.\nThe singing was of a nonsensical sort. The little girl sang as a way of keeping company with herself. The singing had no tune or words. It was a way back, for her, to her own childhood.\nThere was so much of her life that she could remember but did not like at all.\nBut she remembered her own childhood well. It had been a very good time.\nThe little girl began to parse the definition of goodness in her own terms.\nWhat is goodness? she thought in her own terms. To be good is to do things, doings, that make one feel good when they\u2019re done.\nShe looked out the big window at all the crud in the grass and on the trails, being only vaguely aware of what it all was. A five-year-old is not interested, really, in the myriad forms of crud. She is more interested in her own bicycle and how she will learn how to jump it and do things with it. A five-year-old...", "1_10_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-six in the morning</span>. I walked back to the room, picked up my jacket and car keys, and drove to Las Vegas. I would arrive in time for the bicentennial celebrations. Why not? It was a major historical event. It involved gambling, a war of the gods and a terrible sword. What more could a man ask for? I drove on, and before I knew it, I was in Las Vegas...4. Vegas VilleWell, here I am in this huge, fascist, overbuilt American desert town with all its swarms of white people walking back and forth . ,,The thousands of bent backs broken under the weight of this white imperialism,\u201cI reflect, as they drag themselves along the awful heat that shimmers down off the cracked concrete of their desert town ... nothing moves them. They pass me on the street, talking about the bicentennial, waving little flags and going intoice cream parlors to drink CocaCola and eat pink puffy things with too much sugar on them as they wait for their StingRay convertibles to be brought around from the Ray barbershop. If they think of the Revolution at all, they shrug and mumble a quiet word to themselves; and yes, there is the usual din of electronic organ music from...", "0_5_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-one in the morning</span>, according to the clock on the dashboard, and the driver of the beauti' ful Cadillac was, back 'there some place in the hills behind us, doing a little bit of what janitors call overtime. I was glad he was there and not along with me. I was making, according to the dashboard clock, about seventy-five miles per hour. The roads were flat and straight. The speedup button on the dashboard seemed to be connected to nothing, so I just kept both hands on the wheel and hung on sweetly. It was cold in the car. The seat cover was cracked. There were some newspapers on the seat, and I was balling them up in my pants legs to stay warm. My socks were full of dry sweat.\nWe were riding through the absolute gut\u5700 of the Nevada desert now, about sixty miles north of Las Vegas. There was not a single nuisible thing ahead of us for maybe fourty more miles. I said so to my attorney, and he said, \u201cTell me about it.\u201d He was awake now, clicking along with his tape recorder. Blared from the speaker was a song of sinister enlightenment called \u201cMotorcycle Gang\u201d... We ride out every day\nLike the Wand...", "0_19_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven o'clock in the evening</span> by now. We had nine hours to kill in this town. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was worried. \u201cWe had better find the office of that gang of pseudo-intellectual sissies and get them to take us over to Ed Dunkel in the county jail. You can't tell what might happen if we lost contact with him in this town.\u201d We found the address in the phone book, but the office was shut and there was no answer to our insistent ring. However, a cute little old lady who lived next to the office graciously came to her window and when we said we were friends of Bill -- she knew Bill well -- invited us in to talk. The apartment was small and shabby and dimly light by two nickel-plated lamps; the mother was trimming the dead flowers off the breakfast-nook table. She was a schoolteacher. Her husband, who had just left the room to go downstairs, was a dean at the University of Denver. They were a pleasant and cultivated couple and we told them all about the Old Sheila Silverado and so on. $FIRSTNAMEB3 told them all about jail and bleeding noses and police, and the man promised to go down to the sheriff's office and talk to the boys for us and see that...", "2_2_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-five in the morning</span>.\nShe found a telegram under her door:\nCAN\u2019T SYNDICATE YOUR BROTHER IN-LAW\u2019S APPARELL CO. WHY NOT CONTACT MINE STOP ADVISE CANFIELD TYRE\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 had never seen a telegram before. It was from a Dr Hilarius, who address was a box number, no city. She put on her coat and walked to the post office, two blocks away. Inside, sidling up to the window in the light, were two adolescents and a girl their size but shapely, who was sending a money order and keeping an eye on whichever kid got a kick out of making paper dance on the counter.\nChapter 3\nNO REASON, UNLESS YOUR HUSBAND IS IDIOTIC. IF HE IS HAVE HIM GO TO FREEMANTLE IN SWORD OF DAMOCLES HOTEL. HILARIUS.", "1_2_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>, local time, when Salo and $SURNAME0 materialized on the space ship that old Salo had piloted so valiantly in the war between Earth and Mars.\nImages had laughed at $SURNAME0 and the hipper-still so heartlessly, had been discussed so indifferently, during $SURNAME0\u2019s absence in the reality of chrono-synclastic infundibula\u2014that $SURNAME0 was more than ordinarily punctual, dispassionate, and cold when it came to carrying out illusions.\n$SURNAME0 and Salo materialized on the space ship by the simple expedient of appearing in the places where $SURNAME0 and Salo were supposed to be. They were supposed to be there. So there they were.\nSalo the ditcher bent over and snapped the latch of his ankle chains. The chains fell away. $SURNAME0 and Salo looked like hell. Neither of them had bathed. Neither of them had shaved. Neither of them had slept. They hadn\u2019t seen a mirror in one hundred and seventy-three hours. $SURNAME0 had on his blue and gold cape, but it was rumpled and filthy. He had on nothing underneath it. Salo wore the one-piece...", "0_10_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> of the first day of October, A.D. 1986.\nThe place was the state of Minnesota in that very United States of America.\nThis report was an official felony report. It was as gripping as it was true.\nIn the office at the top of the $SURNAME0 estate, Salo had just completed his tickling of the crystal ball called Shirley. The cheering in the courtyard below had subsided now. All those who were still in a position to applaud had applauded. They had seen their own exciting future in the last installment. They had cheered, hoping only that their own exciting futures would be half as nice as Ralph\u2019s had been.\nThe cheering had been done with the good-natured shy reluctance of masses of people who knew that watching something that isn\u2019t private runs athwart the notions of democracy. They knew that even saints and censors have their own special places in the order of nature\u2014and that trying to peer over into those special places by watching something that isn\u2019t private isn\u2019t healthy. Nobody knew this better than $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe one bitter whimper $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 ever had heard in his life was uttered for his benefit on just such an...", "2_18_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>. Squeak. The sound that had just been heard was the second highest-numbered church bell. The one-minute fuses in the wads over the invasion column\u2019s ordnance expired. $SURNAME1 Ducth hands touched off a thousand explosive wads.\nTwo thousand explosive charges fizzled and popped in the bottoms of two thousand halftracks. Two thousand parachutes popped out and billowed. The soldiers aboard the halftracks were reaching for the sky and screaming of their delight as they fell to Earth. The thousand soldiers in the shock-assault companies were carrying packages, kicking soccer balls, carrying bedrolls and digging tools and engaged in other such seemingly harmless activities.\nAll the charges planted in the column by the 3rd Army Anticommandos were dummies.\nThe only Army men who died when the dummies exploded were four men at the very tiptop of the structure\u2014two command officers and two communications officers who, with faulty intelligence, thought they were issuing orders to real men.\nNot one man in all the vast strength that was really on Mars was positioned, or armed, or alive.", "0_6_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past six o'clock in the morning</span> of the following day. $FIRSTNAMEM1 had slept a second night in the room in Truro. He had made the journey from London on the overnight Martian South Hovercraft, arriving at the town at about two in the morning. He had woke twice in the night. The second time it was because he had started thinking again about the smell of roast meat. He did not know yet why the smell reminded him of Mrs Parsons, but that made no difference. Whenever he thought of Mrs Parsons, he would remember the smell of roast meat. And then too, inevitably, Her name was Julia. It was always in the mind.\ni HEIS STOOD IN THE DOORWAY, LOOKING IN i something like entranced, his hand scooping up the music-maker. He gazed into the bird cage for perhaps as long as a minute, perhaps even two minutes, continuously absorbed.\nThen suddenly he engaged gear and i became alive again. The planes of his face loosened and spread into an impossibly foolish grin. His eyes took on a i self-possessed look that was not necessarily benign. His shoulders hunched up i inside his soiled overcoat, and one fist grasped something that safely jostled ! behind the pocket of his yellow trousers.", "1_9_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-nine in the morning</span> of November 12, 1982, and the place was an intersection of the following three Los Angeles streets:\nThere were no people to be seen in this intersection. It was all but deserted.\nConsider these clues as to how thoroughly deserted the intersection was:\n(1) There were only two parked cars in the northern reaches of the intersection, in the block between Quality and Suzy-Q, and one of these cars was a hearse, anyway.\n(2) The windows in all of the offices of the buildings on each side of the easternmost block were covered with wide aluminum window covers that were painted to match the buildings.\n(3) The giant sign on the side of the Bank of America building directly across from the Quality Building displayed nothing at all, not even the time of day.\n(4) None of the streetlights in the Quality-Suzy-Q block were even on.\n(5) Not a single pedestrian was so much as entering or exiting any of the buildings in the intersection.\n(6) Not a single pedestrian was crossing the intersection.\n(7) Some light rain had been falling for about a minute and a half, but no rain either falling or imminent could be seen, smelled, felt, or heard...", "2_20_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-eight in the evening</span>. The ship paused before landing, and the crew telepathically received new orders. The crew did a yip-pee dance of joy. At last they had a mission they could believe in.\nThey landed. The airlock popped, popped, popped open. The mate laughed at Martian customs practices.\nA single member of the crew left the ship. He was the father of $FIRSTNAMEB1 and Malachai, twin boys of four. The mate\u2019s name was $FIRSTNAMEB0. His name was still $FIRSTNAMEB0, no matter what awful adventures he got into, and that was the way he intended to keep it. He had plans, too.\nThe mate, $FIRSTNAMEB0, carried a neatly wrapped package under one arm. The cleanly wrapped package contained $FIRSTNAMEB1 and Malachai. $FIRSTNAMEB0 believed that he was bringing $FIRSTNAMEB1 and Malachai to a hero\u2019s welcome, to a great triumph, to a paean of glory and fame.\nHe believed this, and he was intensely happy. He said it over and over to himself, his mantra: \"Man\u2014am I happy! Hey\u2014Man\u2014am I happy!\"", "2_13_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe statues of Brinton M. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, facing in opposite directions below the staircase in the $SURNAME0 foyer, made an exquisite composition, though the thought of man and wife in such a composition offended most visitors in the manner of a sexual violation. It was clear, at any rate, that a family quarrel had happened, and had become permanent in a most theatrical fashion.\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, who was on the staircase landing, facing out toward the weather-balloon, was obviously listening for something.\nMr. Brinton M. $SURNAME0, who was below on the floor, facing out toward the ballroom, was obviously listening for something else entirely.\nNeither knew what the other was waiting for.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 was waiting for the sound of her husband\u2019s dying heartbeat. It was expected to fade any moment.\nMr. $SURNAME0 was waiting for the sound of harmonium music, coming from somewhere in space, beyond the walls of the estate. It was expected at any moment.\nNeither stirred.", "0_0_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-one a.m.</span> on Halloween. When little $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Jr., attempted to wriggle free of his mother\u2019s arms, there were waves of pain across his torso.\nIn an odd way, the pain was welcome. It helped young $FIRSTNAMEM2 to make a rational decision. Being held so tightly by his mother scared him\u2014more, even, than the destruction of his room.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 eased out of his mother\u2019s arms, found his trusty fire engine under the bed, and wheeled it silently toward the door. His mother continued to snooze noisily.\nIn the hallway, young $SURNAME1 ran into some difficulty. Old $SURNAME0 had had the hallway tilted, in order to eliminate the tedious problem of parked wheelchairs.\nYoung $SURNAME1 regained his balance between two troughs, regained control of his fire engine. He moved it tensely on, a wheel between low walls, a wheel between high walls.\nA pachyderm thundered past the open door of his mother\u2019s room. The elephant had red-rimmed, bloodshot eyes and carried a Tralfamadorian in a transparent helmet and suit of space armor. The suit was clearly too small for the Tralfamadorian, who was...", "2_5_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-six in the morning</span>. I lay on the bed for a half hour and realized I had to get up and shower or I'd fall asleep and miss my plane. I moved like an old man, as if every action required a complex analysis; I was confused and vaguely appalled to find that I'd talked more to that little girl than I had to my attorney in the past six months. As I stumbled into the bathroom with two suitcases I wondered what it all meant; maybe I was drifting from Jo Ann to Lucy, from the bad vibrations to the innocence and purity of a lonely little thirteen-year-old virgin. It seemed like a natural enough progression. I felt weak for Lucy, for her frail hands and her little bored yawns. I washed my face and wondered what was the use of talking honestly about all this at great length with my attorney when it was manifestly impossible for him to understand. He was different.", "2_18_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-eight in the evening</span>, Earthling Time.\nThe man, like all the rest, was an executioner.\nThe only individuals unarmed were the ones paralyzed by harmonics in the airship hovering directly overhead.\nTo the executioners on the ground, the ship was a featureless black silhouette. To the executioners in the ship, the landscape was a featureless landscape of pale sand. The hovering ship\u2019s searchlight made a frame of light in the sand. The harmonics paralyzed everyone outside the frame. And everyone inside the frame, of course, was harmless. Harmless and invulnerable.\nThe tall man in black who had just stepped out of the bushes had taped on a kind of headpiece with a number of clear plastic tubes protruding from it. He pressed a switch on the headpiece, and the tubes began to feed him air. The air came from the airship.\nHe stepped up to the boulder, put a foot on it, scaled the boulder skillfully. He stepped lithely onto the roof of the two-room cabin.", "2_2_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-three in the morning</span>. The night was still whirling.\n\u201cWell they\u2019ve been most miraculously cured of their emotional problems because what I did was use this new technique I\u2019ve been a pioneer in developing of taping messages onto the bodies of people with emotional problems, and my patients listened repeatedly to the taped messages and got over their emotional problems. This one here, Pammy, has tremendous self-confidence and social poise now, and this one, Mindy, has the emotional maturity of a person at least thirty years older. And of course when patients get cured like that after many years of fruitless treatment, I retain them as employees.\u201d\n\u201cJesus Christ.\u201d\n\u201cDo you own the home or just rent it out?\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n/e/\n\u201cWhere did you hear?\u201d\n\u201cWe have a houseguest who subscribes to the Cleveland Plain Dealer. Do you know when the funeral will be?\u201d\n\u201cLenore Beadsman died at two twenty this morning.\u201d\n\u201cTwo twenty this morning?\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s right.\u201d", "2_3_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-one in the morning</span>. In the dark bedroom deep in the bowels of the original Ennet House, the snores of resident counsel Francis Abington Twillinger strangled for just a second and resumed. Stacklee laying in on a bunk in the common room straightened from his mock-classical pose of The Sleeping Naiad and listened to the titanic breather with more interest than he usually felt. As he listened, Facer Clapp came out of the bar\u2019s one tiny bathroom, having just tried to get a drink of water from the Water Pik. Facer had needed an adjustment to do so. Facer was not happy.\n\u201cYou up?\u201d he said to Stack.\n\u201cYup,\u201d said Stacklee. \u201cAnd I\u2019m going to stay up. You?\u201d\n\u201cMaybe. I\u2019ll see,\u201d said Facer. He stood silhouetted in the door of the bathroom, looking down at his own white snaggled teeth in the bathroom mirror. He was dressed in his amazingly baggy nurse\u2019s whites.\n\u201cI\u2019ll be here,\u201d said Stacklee. \u201cSleep tight.\u201d", "2_14_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-six in the afternoon</span>, Pacific Standard Time.\nThere was no one left alive in Maxwell\u2019s Bar, in Florence, Arizona; the members of a bluegrass band had tipped over a cask of Henry Chinaski\u2019s best red wine, and had passed out.\nSomewhere in Los Angeles an English actor stubbed out a cigarette in the papers in an evidence case against him for assault with a deadly weapon.\nIn the central part of the state three hunters stood atop a ridge, quarreling among themselves as to where to go next. Was that one standing buck, or was it a scrub jay in a pine? Could it be that this rabbit was a coyote or was that just your imagination? They saw the helicopter above them, and watched it for ten minutes. It was casting down little packages. They walked over to examine one: moonshine, red label\u2014legal again.\nAnd somewhere in Los Angeles three thousand rioters had just tossed a flaming Molotov cocktail into a bowling alley\u2014their third such assault that Thursday.\nThe helicopter with the top nuts on detached itself from the ship and flew off. The ship and the men within continued on in space, without guidance.", "0_15_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. A drunk woman with a nautical hat had just staggered near the bar. Her face was haggard.\nSnake had watched her approach with painful intensity. When she was near, he had uttered a deep, throaty cry\u2014\u201cGh lloommm!\u201d\u2014and had farted. He was laughing with such intensity that tears had formed in his eyes. The drunk woman with the nautical hat wandered off; she staggered into a booth and sagged half-conscious onto a seat. Lou sat in the next booth, fiddling with binoculars. In his other hand he held a drink. There were now large wads of cotton in both his ears. Every so often he would reach up and touch the wads, make sure they were still there, then smile contentedly and go back to his binoculars.\n\u201cHi,\u201d said a voice; \u201cI see you made it.\u201d\nI turned, to see a young man with a beard. He was wearing barracan pants and a blue Levis jacket. \u201cMind if I join you?\u201d He sat down. \u201cI don\u2019t think we\u2019ve met. My name is Rudy. Rudy Russo...", "1_16_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-two in the afternoon</span>. I was being led to the gas chamber. The Toad Hall bunch was waiting out there with the Great Snarf, ready to consume me. \u201cThem and what else?\u201d muttered my attorney. He looked back outside, where the casino crowd was massing in the sun on the sidewalk. \u201cJesus!\u201d he said. \u201cWagering their souls here, with the Great Satan! Twenty on the Man, right now...red hot favorites at three to two.\u201d He glanced nervously at the tic, which was now : it looked like. \u201cThere\u2019s no time to waste,\u201d he muttered. \u201cEvery minute we stay here we\u2019re potentially dealing with real physical death.\u201d I nodded and started out of the elevator. My attorney was fumbling with his drug kit. \u201cYou\u2019re clear,\u201d he said, \u201cbut for Christ\u2019s sake stay alert. We\u2019re approaching the environment of total peril and vicious death. Get outside and find some kind of transportation\u2014get us to the Desert Inn as fast as possible, without alerting the Great Bargello. We\u2019re on our own now.\"\n\u201cRight on...", "2_11_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-seven in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\nA panel of mirrors in the elevator shaft broke open, and an iron ladder was run smoothly up through the opening.\nA trembling young man began to climb the ladder from below, carrying a plain brass coffin in his hands.\nUnce Walton was in the coffin. And there was no happier young man in Newport in that moment, unless it was $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe young man with the ladder weren\u2019t exactly a soldier\u2014he was part of the staff of the Museum of Artillery and Military Medicine. But he weren\u2019t exactly an artist, either. As a young man, his talent had been stenciling official numbers on the flanks of Army mules.\nHis name, on the wall outside his office, was Fizzgig, Clifford W.", "0_18_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>, while $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 were rising into the sky a thousand feet above the Pan-American stadium, that the blip appeared on the radar screen of the Prediction Machine of the Warner Communications Company. The flaw in the diamond had appeared.\nThe blip was much too big to be the punctual Mr. $SURNAME0 and his dog.\nThe blip said simply and authoritatively: I am the Commander-in-Chief of all land, sea, and air forces of the United States of America, and of those of our allies. I command all such forces as hereby ordered to impact with and destroy the Martian Invasion Army.\nI have given the enemy until precisely ten o\u2019clock Eastern Standard Time to surrender unconditionally all arms, munitions, food, space ships, and, in fact, everything pertaining to or bearing on the prosecution of the war against the Earth. They have refused to do this. I have therefore ordered all of their arms, munitions, food, ships and other instrumentalities to be destroyed at the earliest practicable moment.\nI have further ordered that the Martian Invasion Army itself be destroyed and annihilated to the maximum possible extent at the earliest practicable moment. This destruction and annihilation are to be continuous from...", "2_10_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-one in the morning</span>.\nThe place was a parapet beneath the burnt orange sky of Mars. The parapet was part of the crenellated wall of the western face of the great Tranquility Base dome that hid the Palace of were Phoebe no longer sat.\nThe crenellations were too high for anyone to hurl himself upon his own sword.\nA note that was never to be read told how the High Chancellor of Tranquility Base shot himself at this spot. His name, like the name of Phoebe, had subsequently been struck from the record.\nThe spot where the Chancellor had shot himself had once been white.\nThe presence of a kind of thought or a kind of person sufficed to make any place white on Mars.\nAs well as destroying the palace and its contents, the attack of the flying saucers had bleached the stone to its original color of deep Martian gold. The flying saucers had hit their target perfectly.\nThe smaller spaceships were pulling away from the palace in all directions, their skirts inflated by the Martian noon.\nThere was a moment to spare. It was used to squeeze the cheeks and bite the lips.", "1_14_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nI knew the time because I had my watch in my hand. I was sitting in the left seat of a Volkswagen sedan, feeling very male and very stupid. The interior of the car was badly messed up. I was covered with blood, which kept dripping onto my tan alligator skin rug.\nMy attorney was sitting behind the steering wheel, quite as bloodily scraggly, but looking quite a bit more stupid than I felt. We both smelled of booze\u2014mine a fresh taint; his, I noticed, quite heavy. He kept his eyes closed, breathing through his mouth. His right hand was clamped to the top of his right thigh. I saw that the palm was cut and bleeding. He was holding a dirty gray towel to his stab wound.\n\u201cWhat happened?\u201d I said.\n\u201cSupercop,\u201d he murmured.\n\u201cYou see him?\"\n\u201cNaw...not really..\u201d His voice was distant and dreamy, barely audible above the hiss of the asphalt on the road before us.\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d I snapped. He looked at me and blinked twice before replying.\n\u201cYou remember that pills we took...those...", "2_12_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past noon</span>, Greenwich Mean Time.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $SURNAME1 lay motionless on the cellar floor, listening.\nThere was company coming\u2014Secret Service men, not Martians. They would be coming with torchlights, riot guns, grenades, and the ravings of the President of the United States for blood.\nLooking across the cellar at $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF1 found him handsome, mysterious. He seemed to have conquered all the problems of the Universe. She loved him. She felt like drawing his face to her breast and sheltering him forever.\nThe expression on his face told her that he needed sheltering.\n\"You\u2014are\u2014a\u2014coward!\" she whispered savagely to him.\nHe recoiled as if she had struck him in the face. He bit his lip. \"A coward?\" he whispered with painful dignity. \"Yes,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"you ran away and left me all alone with $FIRSTNAMEB0. You deserted us, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 died of it.\" She wept. \"I\u2019ve hated you for it all these years.\"\n$SURNAME1 nodded. \"All right,\" he said sadly.", "1_8_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-six in the morning</span>. At that very moment, the first rays of yellow sunshine were playing over the sleeping houseboats in a neighboring canal\u2014three thousand, four hundred, and twenty-five domestic ducks began a simultaneous quack.\nThey quacked for eight, maybe ten seconds. Then, for a possible thirty seconds, there was an absolute, uh, let\u2019s say an absolute silence. Then, in the full light of drowsy dawn, you could once again hear the quacking\u2014but this time only because thirty thousand, six hundred, and thirty-one wild ducks had decided to join the festivities.\nThis time, the quacking went on for nearly a minute.\n\"There is absolutely nothing that I loathe more than the sound of quacking ducks,\" said a voice from a canal-side window. It was the voice of an eastern urbanite. It was the voice of Andrew Sealab. All that quacking was putting a kink in his Swedish massage.\n\"There is absolutely nothing worse than quacking ducks, do you hear me?\" he said. \"How would they like it if we stood out here on the banks and yammered at them all day long? Billed and cooed at them, eh? Well, I...", "0_12_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter past noon</span> when I swam to shore and found myself alone with the whisky and allusions to the democratic subtleties of the American character. The sun warmed the back of my neck as I climbed up the hill and crossed the gravel road. The road led to the main highway. There was absolutely nothing on either side of it. Not a tree. Not a fence. Just grey gravel and moving dust, and a hot sun high overhead, and that long empty road curving off into the distance. I strode along beneath the hot sun, between the moving walls of dusty, deadly quiet. Just a simple crossroad. And absolutely nothing in sight but the curving blacktop ribbon and the bleached grasses of the roadside dika making striations in the gravel as the wind blew. And not a single car, truck, or bus all the way to Portofino. And then, there it was, suddenly out of nowhere, this strange apparition, this vision in the desert: a faun, reclining on a dappled grey boulder beside the highway, his pink posterior cleft by a fissure that led to the tops of two sharp little hooves. The faun was elaborately beautiful. His brow was pensive, his ears draped langu...", "0_23_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until midnight</span> and some change. The $SURNAME0 estate was as quiet as the grave.\nAnd then came a sound so harmless, so inoffensive, and so superfluous that one might question whether to bother reporting it. But in that sound lay the seeds of everything exciting and unforeseeable and splendid to come\u2014a sound like the fluttering of gossamer wings. The sound came from a ventilator in the attic of the mansion.\nThe ventilator shaft in the horse-barn had been bricked up long ago.\nTHE NICE SECRET THAT MRS. HANNEGAN HAD LEARNED\nMrs. $FIRSTNAMEF0 Kinsolving, the new mistress of the $SURNAME0 estate, was the plumpish, pink-faced, attractive daughter of an enormously wealthy lumber baron. She was a good sport, and she understood perfectly well all the plottings and intrigues that were going on in Newport that summer. They didn\u2019t scare her a bit\u2014and she was as sorry for all these shallow people, with their transparent plots and their transparent intrigues, as she was for the bugs that got into their Bentleys so easily in the fall. The only time Mrs. Kins...", "2_23_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright turned carefully over on his left side. The pad he slept on had been made in B\u00fcchen, Germany, in the year 1510. $FIRSTNAMEF3 had paid two hundred and fifty dollars for it.\nLooking at the wall from the left side of his bed, in the dark, the circles and squares were gone, and only the wash-bowl showed, and a piece of the rock wall.\nThe stone wall was the original wall of the house. It was three feet thick.\nThe time was three minutes past eleven o\u2019clock in the evening.\n\"Had my first woman this evening\u2014\" $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright heard his father say. \"And I tell you, son, when you have your first woman, you\u2019ll know what all this talk is about.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright laughed in bed. He heard his father laugh.\n\"Why, I feel happy,\" said Al Fernwright.\n\"You ought to feel happy,\" said Joe Fernwright. \"You\u2019ve only paid more money out tonight than anybody in this town will make in a months time.\"", "1_8_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>, Earth time. The place was a world of one sun and several moons. The nearest moon was called Mars by the people of the world. Another moon, the name of which had become confused with that of the planet Earth in the speech of human beings, was Saturn.\nThe name of the third moon of the world, visible in the morning sky of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s estate in Newport, was Neptune.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, unshaven, red-eyed, wearing rumpled evening dress, and with a patch over one eye, was standing on the forward deck of the airship. A bit of electric storm was passing over. $SURNAME1 stared at the play of the lightning on the clouds below. As long as he didn\u2019t think about where he was going and what he was about to do, about how he had to get there, he found the clouds and lightning interesting.\nOnly once did $SURNAME1 permit his mind to go where it wanted to go\u2014to the anxiety-making subject of what lay below. The anxious haze that suddenly surrounded him made it instantly clear to him that he had let his mind touch bottom.\nIt was as if a reeking quagmire had begun to feed on his mind.\nHe...", "1_14_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-four in the afternoon</span>, EDT. The date was far enough back to have put the heat of July behind compared with the heat of August and all August\u2019s many days, to be sure of August\u2019s innocence, and to have advanced far enough toward the surprising cool of September and the embarrassments of October\u2014to have tentatively established a rueful relationship between temperature and events. The ex-widow $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was forty-nine years old. He was one of the richest men in the Solar System\u2014and one of the most lonely.\nAnd this opportunity for study that time-farers were always so punctual about was, in fact, everything to which $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were heir. It was all they had.\n$SURNAME0 sat in a contoured chair with his hands tucked into the armpits of his silver space suit. His toes were curled in their gold shoes over the edge of the silky white band of a spiral walk.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s head was tilted back. His eyes were fixed fearfully on the sky. His hooded eyes narrowed as, again and again, he predicted the future hour by hour.\nHis face was always the same, whether he gazed fondly up at...", "0_22_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-five in the evening</span> when I got to my house in North Dormer after driving back from New York that Sunday. Old Bunch was sitting there on the porch in an easy chair, smoking a pipe. It was a fine summer night with the smell of the pines and meadows.\n\"How's everything, pa?\" I asked, kind of grouchy.\n\"Everything's all right,\" he said. He grinned. \"Except I don't know what you're going to do with $FIRSTNAMEB5, any more than I know what you're going to do with the lad.\"\n\"I think I know what to do about $FIRSTNAMEB5. What about Pedro Romero?\"\n\"I don't know what you're going to do. You're going away to-morrow morning. I don't know what you're going to do that far off in the world.\"\n\"I'm going to Spain.\"\n\"I know. You've got your steamship ticket. You going to take that big boy with you?\"\n\"He's going to take me in hand.\"\n\"It's nice, all of you to-morrow going away together. That's a nice thing for you to-morrow morning, Robert. It's a fine day when all your loved ones start away together...", "2_21_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nOn Monday the twenty-first of September hot humid gray clouds boiled over the city of Cleveland Ohio. The smells at the Bombardini Company\u2019s Gerber\u2019s Gerbera Jungle were hot and wet. On the wings of even the highest-arched eddy of birds, over the orange petals of the flowers and the nozzle of the water hose and the straight back of the hunched and shirtless man clipping and weeding, wafted the smells of the hot summer earth, warmed by the hot sun and swollen and fertile after the rain. The man\u2019s legs were spattered by water droplets thrown off by plants that hissed and squirted moisture when handled. He wore a straw hat. The leg hairs were visible through his fair skin. His name was Toot Sweet.", "2_6_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until seven o'clock in the morning</span>\u2014four hours after the services had begun. A trumpet relayed the beginning of $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s Love Song softly through St. Francis\u2019s ear.\nSomeone\u2019s soul drifted into the church.\nIt was $FIRSTNAMEM2.\nHis eyes were good now. He looked deep into St. Francis\u2019s eyes.\nSt. Francis was frightened and tried not to show it.\n\"I will make a deal with you,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, making small tight fists over his heart. \"You can have this inheritance free and clear, and with no catch, if you will trade eyes with me.\"\nSt. Francis stopped breathing. Count Rumford took the opportunity to sneeze loudly and repeatedly.\n\"Well?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"There isn\u2019t anything I can do about your eyes,\" said St. Francis, assuming a brilliant smile.", "1_9_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-one in the morning</span>. I danced out of the place and hopped in the Chrysler. Twenty-two minutes to go to the Air Express office downtown: nine blocks away, on the other side of Boulder. Off I bopped. I had to go faster than any sane man would want to risk on that terrible morning.\nI had driven this route many times at much higher speeds than I dared to now. I handled the big, heavy car with one ear cocked to the radio. Racing, always with the roar of the motors in my ears and the thread of announcer's voices, I sailed twisting and turning through downtown traffic, turned east, stopped at the Air Express office long enough to sign the manifest, and zoomed the cargo plane in a three-block sprint to the airport on the other side of town. By now the entire morning fleet of Ham operators was upon me, calling me in every kind of code, half of them giving me their exact positions. Their cheery voices came to me over the ether from all over Colorado: \u201cCowboy, this is Lizard Annie . . . ,\u201d \u201cHey, this is Cowboy, over . . . ,\u201d \u201cThis is Forty-Four Charlie calling Cowboy, over . . . hey!\u201d My...", "2_10_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe scene was the mighty stone shelves that surrounded the camplike clearing at the foot of the space gun. When the organization of the camp was first being established from the stores of the unindexed material-salvage bins, an enthusiastic supplies officer had used the phrase \"mighty stone shelves\" to describe the shelves. The phrase had stuck. The stone shelves were everywhere.\nSergeant $SURNAME2, dressed in a new olive-drab uniform, was counting his men, a new ritual he had just invented. Just as in the old days he had used the thumb-test to count his troops, he now used the beard-test. He moved among the troops, bending down to touch a soldier here and there, counting in whispers.\nThe soldiers were sitting around in small groups, cleaning their rifles, cleaning their one lonely pair of signal pips. $SURNAME2 reached Private Canon, testing his beard, then Private Hobart, testing his. He became puzzled when he reached Private Stony Stevenson, testing\u2014nothing at all. There wasn\u2019t any stubble under his test-leaf. There wasn\u2019t any test-leaf under his test-leaf.", "2_13_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the ship settled with a barely perceptible jar on a hard-packed sand beach beside a small river.\nThe river flowed east through a forest, toward a distant plain.\nThe beach on which the ship had landed was surrounded by a limestone cliff thirty feet high.\nAbove the beach, halfway up the cliff, shaded by overhanging rock, was a cave ten feet across.\nRanged along the cave: a door of iron, a window of black glass.\nOut of the cave jutted eaves and supports, between which was strung a power and light wire, fed by a cable below the sand of the beach.\nA stone stairway led up the limestone to a square landing.\nOn the landing: an altar made of sandstone, marble, and rosewood.\nBehind the altar: a cavern big enough to hold ten thousand people standing.\nAt the mouth of the cavern: two iron doors.\nRanged along the iron doors: a bell, a telephone, and a telegraph instrument.\nRanged along the iron doors: a bookcase containing a Bible, a shabby Earthling almanac, a dozen hymnbooks and songbooks of various Churches\u2014and thirty science and adventure novels.", "2_5_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. I grabbed the handlebars and began pumping again. My brain whirled like a dervish in a brass jearmell Heaven help me, I was groaning. And it was a long way back to Hollywood. I could see the headlines now: DRUNKEN GENTLEMAN JUMPS FROM MOVING SPA EQUIPMENT IN RING OF FIRE; FALLEN COLUMNIST, LADY FRIENDS SLAIN BY TERRIBLE FLAMES; KILLING AT SPA CLUB CAUSES RUMPUS AMONG HIPPIES...then the photograph:\nmyself, nude, hanging dead like a sinner on the cross of my beloved Amercan Bicycle Company.", "2_1_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-six in the morning</span>.\nGreat good luck and great good sense dictate that goodbyes be short at times like this. Undoubtedly this is why the good-byes of $SURNAME0 and his dog were so superficial. And the superficiality of the good-byes was shared by all three parties concerned, by $SURNAME0 as well as by $FIRSTNAMEB4 and $SURNAME1.\n$SURNAME0 was in fine control, as always. He was casual, was humorous. He gave strict, precise directions about the cleaning of the spacecraft and the stowing of the luggage and the maintenance of the chain of presses in the Museum of Natural History on Earth.\nThe chain of presses was $SURNAME0\u2019s own invention. It represented a technique for taking advantage of cosmic influences on the museum by means of his magical wristwatches. The watches and the presses were expensive, but they paid for themselves many times over by producing a miraculous surplus of income above expenditures.", "1_5_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty in the morning</span>, we were creeping toward the rockpile on a pretty route called the Hummingbird Highway. All of a sudden we crested a hill, and there unexpectedly right ahead of us were the police. They were just sitting there parked across the road like a film ironically foreshadowing events. Our departure point was a farmhouse a quarter of a mile beyond them; apparently they had just driven back and forth a few times, in their boredom circling like hawks. Time and relative velocity were strangely distorted. It was me who reacted first. \u201cJesus!\u201d I shrieked. \u201cIt's the bloodhounds!\u201d And $FIRSTNAMEB3 just tore into that road and those black cops jumped into their car and took off after us at full speed, siren blaring, horn blowing, big red light turning. The road was rough, the car swayed this way and that. They came up behind us. We could hear them yelling. Then one of them stuck his face to my window and yelled, \u201cPull over! Pull over! What's your hurry, buddy?\u201d I was scared. I slumped down in mycoat and tried to look straight ahead, playing the part of an innocent, head-shocked Easterner asleep in the...", "0_17_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-two in the afternoon</span> of the last day of liquid-helium-cooled operation of the Martian telephone system. In another forty-four minutes, telephonic connections between Earth and Mars would be broken. There was no way of keeping the connection private; anybody on either planet could call anybody on the other for the last 44 minutes.\nDonald Hogan had been in the business of establishing and maintaining friendships between people on Earth and people on Mars for one hundred twenty-six years. He had a substantial backlog of friendships, and he often handled three hundred ninety-six friendships a year. Not all friendships are created equal, even when they are between people living on the same planet.\nEven the very rich, who really could have afforded their own private connections, still bought friendships from Don whenever they happened to need them at very odd times. The very rich have friends like other people have germs, and it\u2019s just as embarrassing for them to have no friends at all and to be unable to handle occasional social obligations.\nThe very cheap are frugal, too, and always conscious of a bargain\u2014and Don still had the lowest rates in town. His minimum fee for a week-long Martian friendship was two dollars, even if the connection were used just once.\nDon...", "0_8_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nWe drove quickly to the front, got out of the car, and announced that we were the delegates from New York City who had just arrived with credentials in order to get in line and see the President at nine o'clock. His eyes, beyond his glasses, were lifeless.\n\u201cWell, I'll be a son of a gun!\u201d he said, scratching his head. \u201cI just assumed you fellows would be running late.\u201d\n\u201cWhy?\u201d\n\u201c 'Cause I got instructions from the White House, less than an hour ago, to let no newsmen through here before ten o'clock. I thought that's how it'd be all across the country. I just assumed you boys were running late.\u201d\n\u201cNot here,\u201d I said. \u201cWe represent big New York papers.\u201d\nHe looked at our cards.\n\u201cI didn't know,\u201d he said, \u201cand I thought you'd be running late.\u201d\n\u201cNo. We're on time.\u201d\n\u201cThese instructions from the White House could get a fella in trouble,\u201d said the police captain. \u201cWhat happened, are you...", "1_1_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-six in the morning</span>. One forty-six in the morning in the City of Denver. The hallways were quiet. Behind closed doors they were playing a dirge she had heard a million times in a million cheap hotels: television with the sound turned way down low.\nShe was suddenly sick and tired of her life. And she began to cry, because she knew she would never obtain release. Never obtain release. Never obtain. Get. The crying became shaking. She had been living on credit for far too long, and now the bill was due, and crying wouldn\u2019t pay it.\nNever obtain release.\nShe knew she could stand the $FIRSTNAMEB3 and Nemur and all their associates, or the police, or the grocery clerks, or even the ghost of Troilus, who stood at every door with his lunchbox and his little smile, if only she could obtain release in some way and live with her own kind in the world.\nShe picked her bag up off Troilus\u2019s floor and wiped herself and looked around the suite. The walls were shaking. From the toil she had inflicted upon the beer can over the hours, and from tremendous internal pressures, the walls were becoming soft--an effect she understood. Hotel walls always shook at two o\u2019...", "0_5_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-one in the morning</span> of Monday the twenty-fourth of June, the place lines of trenches ringing the network of defenses of the city Hilario d\u2019Curtis had once defended, or tried to defend, with his life; the event, the arrival, smoothly and curtly signaled by dull concussion, of death by fragmentation grenade. At that moment Hilario was sleeping in his little cubicle at the perimeter of the Plazola garrison, with his father snoring in a bed opposite, and his brother snoring under a bed. Hilario had himself hardly slept because of a roaring, crashing, near-fatal brawl in the Plazola between the homeboys and the Parris Island boys, and because of the stunning beauty in a light blue nightgown of the sleepy, blond bookkeeper from Washington, D. C., who worked in the orderly room just inside the barbed wire behind him. Hilario had spied on her for three months; his eyeballs felt as if fried in tallow. He did not dream. He remained as he was, on his stomach in the narrow cot, ears cocked, snoring a little and showing his big white grin, as the one hundred and fifty grams of Pentolite filings blew...", "0_1_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-four in the morning</span>. We had to find a place to stay in Juarez for the night. We had no reservations, but we assumed we would have no trouble. $FIRSTNAMEB3 suddenly remembered a hotel he'd stayed at long ago, the Lux, that he found comfortable. \u201cNow listen, \u201d he said to me, \u201cI want you to take the wheel and drive real careful over to Juarez, I got to rest. \u201d He snuggled over on the seat and closed his eyes.\nI drove eight miles on a straight road through the whispering desert before I realized $FIRSTNAMEB3 was sleeping and wouldn't be waking up for hours. Meanwhile the gas was registering 'E' and red. I didn't know what to do. I tried driving faster; the needle went to 'F'. $FIRSTNAMEB3 woke up and saw the needle.\n\u201cDig it, man,\u201d he said, \u201cwe better pack it on across the line. Yes, you're going fine. Oh, it's your first run alone! Look out for the motor-patrolmen! But at night they're mostly in Ojinaga across the river in a cabal of adobe houses by the railroad tracks. Just sneak right through, and go straight to the Lux Hotel down by...", "1_22_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-three in the evening</span> of June twenty-second. To repeat, we were sitting in a run-down Ford sedan just south of the center of town, smoking cigarettes and watching the awful nonstop show at the Radical Inn. The night was sultry, with a dank wind blowing in off the desert. The swarm of bugs and heat lightning careened crazily across the black skies to the northeast. There was nothing to do but wait for Howard, wonder about what he was doing and why he had sent for us, and watch the wild fun at the Radical Inn. In the meanwhile, we cruised around aimlessly and peered at the nightmare visions along Main Street. Yes, nightmare visions. Things got so wild on Vegas Main Street at night that it was impossible to tell the peep shows from the bawdy houses. On one sign -- I think it was next door to a spot called the Opium Den --on this sign were three gross, hairy, unhealthy-looking girls apparently trying to scratch various parts of their anatomy. Another joint featured a man in a straightjacket, with a whole lot of arrows sticking in his body and one in his throat; this was supposed to be funny. The \u201cStrawberry Roan\u201d was another fabulous sign with...", "0_20_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-one in the evening</span>, and San Narciso was lidded over by a dark suspen sion of slow white cloud. As we turned with the sad coastal highway off into the Playa Megilla housing tract, I saw the cloud part distantly and crudely like a curtain. Please God, I thought, don\u2019t let the wind be out of the land. Holding at a thousand feet and working toward five, a 747 was carving a white furrow in the layered darkness. I hoped it would stay far over in its own mad airspace, not come muddying our shore. But no. As we came down out of the Glorias, the windborne contrail of the jet widened and sank, a milky declivity that poured itself dreamily into the dark bay. \u201c,\u201d said Metzger. Ominous specter, through the windshield I could see it thread itself into the texture of the smog over the coast, blanch it and disperse it to a dull neutrality. \u201cDid you see?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\nThe residents here lived in cottages. They were little white cottages with car ports, prefab, no doubt encouraged as affordable homes by the War or Veterans or...", "1_9_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe orientation of the ship was such that the sun from Mercury was shining down its length from end to end. The light illuminated the narrow space between the racks that ran from end to end and separated the sleepers into their individual tubes.\nThe space was not completely dark.\nThere were signs that it had been illuminated before by a blue light of sufficient intensity to wake anybody.\nThe illumination had flickered out at the precise moment of wakefulness, and the sleeper had come without an alarm into a darkness broken only by what light leaked in between the armor-plate that formed the hull of the ship.\nFor 21.8 seconds in its wakeful cycle, the ship was a palace of dreams.\nThen it resumed the cold and empty silence of space.\nNumber 217 was one of three hundred and fifty-six sleepers in the Fenachrone portion of the ship. They were stacked in twenty-nine racks, three tiers of eight racks each.\nThe two hundred and seventy-eight sleepers in the Tristero section were laid out in twenty-six racks in the stern of the ship.\nNeither there nor here was there a trace of any form of control, mechanical or electrical, that might govern such things as lighting, ventilation...", "1_20_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-three in the evening</span>. It was one hundred eleven degrees. There were thirty-nine Americans in the bar. Three of them were deaf-mutes. None of them could talk. They were trying to drink as much as possible without speaking.\nTwo of them were secret touch-typists. One of them was a master typist.\nFour of the Americans were working on the same crossword puzzle. They had almost finished. They were a little sad, because none of them had been able to solve the cryptic clue:\n\"STANDEENSTLEDST\u2014\"\nThere were eleven people at the bar. Two of them were Mayfair hearses. One was a dry-goods box. The other was a cobbler\u2019s dummy. The rest were normal.\nOne of the normal people was waiting for the signal of a green light that would appear any moment now at a certain spot on the far side of Mars.\nThe green light was six inches tall, and it sat on the center of a ten-foot-high mound of slag at the base of an iron pylon. There was nothing else on Earth or on Mars as far as the normal person knew.\nThe normal person was hollow. His eyes were two wide-open cameras...", "2_5_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five eighteen in the morning</span>. I bought some fine chocolate bars in the food mart, a bag of roasted peanuts, four grapefruit, a quart bottle of grape juice, and a fifth of Jack Daniels. I loaded it all in a locker and sent a telegram to my attorney in care of General Delivery, San Francisco: \u201cHave completed work on novel and am returning home. Plane reservation follows.\u201d From this would follow other confirmations to Crawford's College and several other places that might drag their heels in the matter of paying my salary while I was gone. That was the furthest thought from my mind. I zipped one bag full of manuscripts and the fine leather satchel that I'd bought in Paris with the money my parents sent me for my college graduation present and that I'd finally found a use for. The other bag I stuffed full of silk shirts and socks and ties, together with a chocolate bar and the peanuts.", "0_19_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span> when $SURNAME1 arrived at $SURNAME0\u2019s tomb.\n$SURNAME1 was all smiles, having just fooled a hundred mil itary policemen at rings and sconces. He was all smiles on the outside, though, as he now approached the palace, his memory unwrapped another piece of the puzzle that was his life.\nThis was the moment when he found himself for the first time in a cab that was heading for Times Square in New York City\u2014and looking out the window at that Square, at twenty-five minutes of eight on a December evening. The Square was aglow with as many lights as stars in a January sky.\n\"There\u2019s a reason why I\u2019m going to New York\u2014\" said $SURNAME1 out loud on his skimming machine.\nThere was. He was on his way there to fulfil a prophecy. A man named Wilbur C. $SURNAME1 was going there to marry a girl named $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\n\"But my name isn\u2019t Wilbur C. $SURNAME1\u2014\" said the real $SURNAME1 out loud, \"and I didn\u2019t make any promise to anybody named $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\"\nThere was something more. Somewhere along the line, the real $SURNAME1 had promised to marry a...", "0_9_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes after nine o'clock in the morning</span> Eastern Standard Time.\n\"There it is, Max,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"That\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s ship. We done caught up with him and you ain\u2019t dead yet.\"\nWhen they got closer, they could see that the ship was empty.\n\"Maybe he went exploring,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. He wished that he and his fellow had more time to spend looking the ship over before it took off for the war. The ship was at least as nice as the palace.\nWith all its flags wadded, with all its guns empty, with all its ammunition belts intact but empty, with all its paratroopers neatly packed and unconscious, it was an impressive sight. One by one, the paratroopers were beginning to stir. Soon there would be hell to pay.\n\"I gotta get clear of this thing,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. He kicked a fissure in the volcano, kicked in three blasting holes. He then ran back to his space ship and waited until a brisk updraft caught it.\n\"Good luck, Max,\" he shouted. \"You have my opinions and you have my sympathy.\" Then he was off, was climbing and banging his way out of the gravity well.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 and...", "1_17_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>, according to the clock on the dash, when Mr. Tack always arrived. He freshly smoked on Camels all day in order to, he explained, sleep better. His office was dark, no windows, practically. It still smelled of wallpaper paste, as if he\u2019d just gotten his own office done. He had matching white teeth and neat blond hair, though he was forty and there were crow\u2019s-feet squeezed around eyes a faint clear blue. Lenore had met Mr. Tack only the one time, late the previous night, and she had spent the morning in Erieview finding the hard-to-find place, tracing the directory\u2019s Yellow Pages listing to an address (in Briersville!) covered with yesterday\u2019s bird droppings, climbing past ropes and tags, over rough obstacles and detritus, and then at least an hour of being probed by very busy jellyfish arms into a warehouse finally filled with sod Mother Nature Herself. What was that all about? Mr. Tack had a desk and a few chairs, old ones with stains, in a room with a hissing IBM-compatible computer and a soft rubber floor and very strange wastebaskets. Mr. Tack didn\u2019...", "0_15_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nIn the sky, the last of the tremendous wheeling flights of helicopters was going home to floor.\nThe panel truck was parked next to a shuttered shop in a mostly deserted business block on the east side of Keenan Courts. The two furniture movers carried their two items\u2014Beano\u2019s two cylinders\u2014no more than eighty yards to the rear of the Newport City jail.\nThe jail stood back from the street in the middle of an asphalt parking lot. Threw this asphalt lot into great contrast with the neighborhood. A haunted Alcatraz.\nWhen the movers got to within thirty feet of their destination, they both sensed something wrong. But they carried the cylinders in anyway, as instructed by $FIRSTNAMEB6 Scachi.\nMistake number one.\nThey had not been challenged. They put the cylinders down outside the jail door. The jail door was being held open by a man in police uniform. peaked cap, old\nThe cop, who was Chief of Police C. William Rudy, spoke to the movers before they'd had a chance to come to attention. \"You guys look woefully out of shape,\" he said. \"What's the trouble?\" They were too out of breath to be witty. \"Dunno,\" said...", "1_12_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-four post meridiem</span> by my watch, according to the luminous dial\u2014when we actually made it on to the main floor of the huge crowd, near the sound-trucks. The Beatles had finally appeared an hour and a half late, and the announcer had just finished saying, \u201cWell, folks, it looks like actually the plane has arrived and the Beatles are on their way,\u201d when we pushed our way up through the mob and saw them way off in the distance, sauntering across the tarmac toward the crowd, smiling and waving to all sides politely.\n\u201cHello, fellows, hello!\u201d yelled somebody.\nThey waved. They smiled. They seemed vaguely glad to be there, but not overly excited.\nWe scrambled for good positions. I pushed through and elbowed people out of the way, whenever necessary, until I was about thirty feet from the actual stage, which had been set up near the control hangar. The music was blasting, the crowd was milling and surging, girls were fainting everywhere, and Hells Angels were racing up and down on their Honda dirt\u2014bikes, leaping off the bikes and dragging unconscious female fans out of the crowd and up on the stage to dance on it, alongside the Beatles...", "0_2_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-two in the morning</span> and the bartender said that Remi Long had just leaned over the bar and informed him that everything was under control. Remi was all hunched over, because that is the belief of the bartenders, that it is great honor to all serve the $FIRSTNAMEB3 of the Western World, symbolized by Lee Harvey Oswald, who sat alone at a table drinking twelve -ounce beers and writing postcards, who declared this to be his favorite San Francisco bar, therefore the home of all drunks in the west end of San Francisco and Remi Long, who has gone on to become one of the better and hungrier bartenders in San Francisco, is more than pleased to be the cut dog of all cut dogs.\nEver since Remi leaned over the bar and whispered, \u201cAll is well,\u201d the night has had a curious Sunday-morning quality about it, an all-wellness; and soon the winner of the bullfight would stick his head in the bar and stretch and yawn like a bored matador and say to a friend, \u201cLet's go home and sleep\u201dand they would disappear into the tender maze. It would rain outside and that would mean nothing. The landlady would shout up the stairs that Mrs. Oswald was on the...", "0_13_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-three in the afternoon</span>, Tuesday, October 1, 2008.\nThe space was the desk-chair combination filling the $SURNAME0 living room of the great house.\nThe person was a Church of God foot soldier named $FIRSTNAMEB1. He was sixty-one years old. He was a homely man with a nervous tic, a soldier who had volunteered for the beautification project because he was lonely. He had been a widower for six years, and his married daughter thought him vulgar.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was frightened.\nHis eyes were wide, and they watered. His body was so tense that his left foot hardly wrinkled the skin of the lotus, though it had been plastered there for forty-seven minutes.\nHis right index finger had been broken so long ago that the arm had begun to knot itself around the finger, though it had been plastered there for only fifteen minutes.\nThere was an insinuating sound. The stone wall was exhaling on $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s left side. Soil was insinuating itself between the block and the mortar with which it had been joined a hundred and fifty years earlier.\nIt was issuing from the contact between the cold stone of the outside wall and the hot wood of the $SURNAME0 living room. The wood...", "2_5_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-three in the morning</span>. We were ten miles north of Daytona Beach, streaking past Deltona on Route 441. In the direction from which we had come, the whole sky was a pink sunrise. Daytona was the other way, invisible except for\u2014suddenly\u2014its skyline, a cluster of fabulous casinos, opalescent in the light of the dawn. Caesars Palace, Circus Circus...every neon politician wants to go there someday. In the city of the damned, they\u2019re ringing the bell again. Hell is a popular destination.\nThis did not seem appropriate to mention to my attorney at the moment. He was asleep in the passenger seat, snoring lightly. His head was cocked over on one shoulder, and his foot was working the pedal of the rental car, a zippy little white Mustang that we\u2019d use to rip around in until the Ferrari showed up.", "0_2_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was no one in the station, no porters, no guards, and no taxi drivers.\nThe taxi drivers had pulled their cars up on the highway to Sallanches, the nearest big town.\nThis is common practice on a beautiful summer night in the country.\nI could see their distant lights on the road for a point of reference.\nI started out of the station, putting my typewriter in front of me, and dragging the valise. I couldn\u2019t see anything, but I knew the road had to be to my left, because the path to my right had led under the building, and I had seen the grass growing there.\nI hit the edge of the tarred road, skinned my shins, and swore. It was too dark even to see blood. Then I started walking in the darkness on the tarred white road. I felt it under my feet. Suddenly the lights of the distant clustered cars showed up. I could see them on the road ahead, a mile away. They looked very small to be a village. I walked toward them, dragging my bag and carrying the typewriter in front of me.\nIt was as though I had been dropped accidentally on this piece of tar at an...", "1_15_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October 8, old style, and eleven minutes after three o'clock in the morning of October 9, new style, when the space ship carrying $FIRSTNAMEB0, Stony, and $FIRSTNAMEB1 arrived at the planet Mars.\nThe dominant life form on Mars was the Martian pronoun-switching machine. For all practical purposes, the pronoun-switching machine on Mars controlled the destiny of the planet.\nThe switching machine, which was effectively one gigantic creature, had a body or system of organs as intricate and as efficient as a human being\u2019s. The machine\u2019s central, or main, organ had a stocky domed architecture \u2014 an architecture that recalled Old St. Paul\u2019s Cathedral in London. This main organ was the machine\u2019s head.\nThe organic materials that formed the main organ\u2019s structure, and that moisturized, circulated, and warmed it, were the same organic materials that had formed the stockyard fence that $FIRSTNAMEB0 had helped $FIRSTNAMEB1 to tear down on Mars.\nOld St. Paul\u2019s Cathedral, as it now was, was perched on a low hill, was a mile and a half from red-gold, flat-topped Mount Prophet Elias. The skeletons of the five hundred Martian...", "1_9_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1 was standing in the great pillared hall, with the fountain playing musically, with the fine Seurats and C\u00e9zannes on the walls, with the big bronze doors opening hermetically, with the air eunuch again at $SURNAME1's elbow, with everything just as it had been at nine o'clock the morning before, and eight hours and fifty-one minutes the morning before that. The air eunuch had already called one elevator that would arrive any minute, and now he called another. $SURNAME1 knew nothing, was going nowhere, expected nothing. He was a forty-one-year-old man pretending to be a stone lion in a lordly hall. It was all he could do to keep from screaming.\nWhat had happened to him? What had taken place in the night? He went to bed one kind of person, woke up another. His theories, his wishes, his purposes, his desires, his whole mental apparatus had run amok in the night\u2014all because of a book.\n\"Astronomica,\" he said, using the first words he had spoken since the evening before.\n\"You find it helpful, sir?\" said the eunuch. He handed $SURNAME1 a copy of The Wall Street...", "1_2_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. We had six hours to get to the plane. I had just enough time to get to my hotel, pack my bags, buy a paper, and start off for the airport. My mind was in a whirl. It was useless to bother going to bed for those few hours. I sat at the desk and thought about the dream I'd had about my Aunt T.'s funeral. The bagpipe howled across the dry fields.\nYes, yes, I thought, you're going home. You're headed west. The Lonely Sea and the sky above it and the Weary Wind blowing through our heads. But first a little scribbling along the route.\nBut what in the hell, in the name of God, are you writing? Scribbling? How phony that sounds. Why don't you just tell the plain truth? You dig anonymity? You have an obsession about purity and the art poetry? Or do you have to justify your rotten life in the Merchant Marine by deluding yourself that you'll get famous writing poems someday?\nIt would be easier to stand all this drudgery and low pay if you had that sort of background and training. But you haven't. You're a technician, not an artist in any sense of the word.", "1_20_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>. I went into the pawnshop and bought a ticket for the shows. It was a dollar, but with the twenty-five cents I had left over from my roll I got the first whisky sour of my young manhood. Then I went out on the street and around the block to the ticket window of the Texas & Pacific Theater. It was a picture called \u201cDrifting.\u201d I struggled through the unending reels of celluloid and then I went thankfully out into the clean Colorado night air. The hotel was just two doors to the left of the theater. I was just starting past it when I heard the sound of joy and saw the big whitewall tires of the $FIRSTNAMEB3 car. There he came down the street, spraying gravel, and I was so glad to see him finally I yelled, \u201cHey!\u201d He slowed down the car.\n\u201cWhere are you, $FIRSTNAMEB6?\u201d\n\u201cRight here!\u201d I ran to him eagerly. \u201cCan I get in?\u201d\n\u201cSure. I'll lead you to the ship.\u201d\n\u201cWhere have you been?\u201d\nHe looked at his watch as we drove. \u201cTwo hours and eleven minutes from Chicago.", "0_0_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-one a.m.</span> on a Thursday in November. $FIRSTNAMEF0 Kneeland had been on the job\u2014let\u2019s see\u2014two years, one month, and nine days. During $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s entire employment, Mindy Metalman had been operating her body by means of signals relayed from her place of work to her organ console at home. That is, Mindy had never left her bathroom. Her stockinged feet had not once strayed from the ceramic tile beneath the waste bowl. The paper doors of her toilet cubicle had always come down automatically after any use by Mindy, so $FIRSTNAMEF0 Kneeland had never once witnessed a sight $FIRSTNAMEF0 hadn\u2019t seen any number of times before. $FIRSTNAMEF0 Kneeland did not like to think of herself as cynically regarding the chance to glance at the lissome charms of her employer\u2019s daughter as so much dime-a-dozen work-expense. Her interest in the messages relayed by means of Mrs. Metalman\u2019s unconscious neural activity was more a recent development than not. Precisely a year ago this coming coming January 2, Mindy\u2019s battered blue Corvair had been hit from the rear by a truck that drove through a red light at three a.", "0_13_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty in the afternoon</span> when Baker finished his rap to the young woman in the drug store and started out to take care of some business inside his Cadillac. His first move was to stick on a pin\u2014a gold Mickey Mouse\u2014in thespread breast of his suit. Then he got in the car and started it and began pulling down the sun visor to make sure the partition window between front and back seats was closed. As the visor came down it clued Baker that the visor was not the only thing that was broken in his car. Looking down through a hole broken in the floor, he could see the road slanting away a good fifty feet below him. The car had sunk that much while he\u2019d been driving it around the night before.\nSynopsis by Keelfax of the Contents of the Brain of Remark Pearl: Early Life...Baker Talmadge, Grand Army of the Panhandle, March 2, 1948: MY LIFE though perhaps not all that adventurous in terms of real-life adventure, is nonetheless here presented as more or less thrilling in terms of its resemblance to an adventure story of the highest quality. Nothing I will relate to you here is in any sense unpleasant or distasteful, nor indeed is it anything less than wildly...", "2_21_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nOn television, television was going off the air.\nUnbeknownst to television, a real-life drama had begun.\nA drama in which a man and woman were undressing.\nA drama that was about to take a long, long detour.\nA detour whose length was so vast it made the length of the normal mathematical space-time continuum which the Real People existed in seem infinitesimal.\nThe length of the detour was to be twenty-one years and one month.\nLooking back on the experience twenty-one years and one month later, the woman was to say: \"I didn\u2019t have time to be scared. I wasn\u2019t really sure what was happening. Everything seemed like a dream. There never seemed to be any danger at all.\"\nThe man was to say: \"I kept saying over and over to myself, When we finally make lunar contact on this wild gamble, better it will be to be as tired as hell than to be caught with your pants down. When we finally make contact, better it will be to be as tired as hell than to be caught with your pants down. When we finally make contact, better\u2014' \"", "1_21_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt ten sharp, according to newspaper accounts, $SURNAME0 simply vanished from the long slopes of Beach Boy Haven. He left behind a bound and nude and drugged $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0, it is rumored, is for sale for twenty thousand dollars.\nIt is rumored that she will be sold by auction at the Mars Club on Wulfila in New York City on March 15. Free play money, round-trip transportation from anywhere on Earth, a two-week all-expenses-paid stay on Mars, and exciting Mars Sex Kits are promised to the lucky bidder.\nOnly fifteen hundred invitations are to be circulated.\nThe invitations are made of gold.\nCaesar\u2019s Revenge was scheduled to go into rehearsal at the Mars Theatre in New York, March 15.\nMUNN II\nMOON SHINES BLUE, moon shines clear. Wind blows wet, wind blows dry. Moon shines white, moon shines yellow.\nConfucius skates on a glazed wa ter pond, and sez: \"Chan Sung!\" Chan Sung answers: \"Yes, Dad?\" \"Patriarch of all Laments,\" sez Confucius, \"what have you got against the moon?\"", "1_4_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-two in the morning</span> of October 3, 1992. Not everything was as $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 thought it was. The big chrono-synclastic infundibulated dog in a space suit was neither blind nor deaf. The dog was piloted by a recon force from Mars.\nThe recon force from Mars was scouting Earth for the first wave of Martian colonists. The wave would consist of one million Melmoulin terraforming engineers, the cream of Martian society.\nThe success of their planet\u2019s first major colony on Earth would put the Melmoulin faction in power on Mars for the first time in history. The Melmoulin terraforming engineers were expected to flourish like jungle vines on Earth.\nThatched villages in the jungle would be among their architectural achievements.\nEarthlings who surrendered themselves to the Melmoulin colonists as slaves would supply the labor for such marvels as the thatched villages.\nThe recon force from Mars now knew, at least roughly, where the center of population on Earth was. The center of population was Pontiak, a port on the shores of the Sun tinged Yellow Sea, forty-six degrees north latitude.\nThe scouting Martian chrono-synclastic infundibulators, piloted...", "0_5_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span> when I saw the long silver car of The Great Rock Engine Company of New Jersey turn off the highway at the bottom of my driveway. The six A.M., departure was of f ixed schedule. I heard the snorting and wheezing of the big Leyland diesel fifty feet from the house. The engineer shut it off. A fat, cheerful, pink-faced guy in a brown uniform, with swinging watch chain at the belly, got out and walked over to see me.\nEarly morning readers will pardon all this seemingly irrelevant detail, I hope. I was merely eager to show the careful and faithful realism with which all the events of this story were prepared and brought to pass. The procedure with the engineer and the fat man in the brown uniform was part of my great preparation.\n\"Going places?\" I ventured.\nHe messed with a wrench while he had a bit of a laugh over the obviousness of it all. \"Sure enough,\" he said.\n\"Rough night?\" I asked.\n\"Oh me? Oh my!\" he said, \"I\u2019m just the guy who stays on the engine the whole trip, except when I run down for some grub.\"\nWe chatted about engines for a few minutes and I...", "1_21_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-seven in the evening</span>.\nThey killed time by watching the parrots play Ping-Pong. The tiny tables were arranged at random around the floor of the chamber. Whenever a red ball went whizzing rimlessly past, the owner of the bird who had just lost would rise and fetch the ball with a long stick. The stick was an extremely necessary item of equipment for anyone around a Ping-Pong table in Stonecnower\u2019s establishment. Things got lively whenever a ball ricocheted into the birdbath in the corner.\nSome forty minutes after $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had stormed out of the establishment, a sallow, bearded young man with a studious and severely rounded look about him entered the chamber with an irritable yank on a chain. A boom banged, breaking the peaceful rimlessness of the room.\nThe young man was Dr. Irving Pitknock, proprietor of the establishment, and of the entire poor Titan below it.\n\"Birdseed tea, please,\" said Dr. Pitknock to the clerk at his left. The clerk punched buttons on a console.\n\"That\u2019ll be seventy-five cents, please,\" said the clerk sheepishly.\nDr. Pitknock graciously paid the bill. He pocketed...", "0_2_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. I opened the door and looked out. Downstairs a circus was in full swing. Singing and shouting, baBOom! laughing, shouting, baBOOM! Sometimes you couldn\u2019t hear the music at all, it was drowned under the stamping and hollering. The thing was on. All the barflies were there, rioting around the ring. Some of them were already exhausted and asleep on the floor\u2014that was the bench competition. Others shifted back and forth on the spot, the walking competition. But most of them stood in a big circle around the ring, taking the bouts in turn, giving their all to each bout, going full steam ahead. An unbroken wall of faces, waves and waves of faces without beginning or end. A tidal wave of faces in various stages of sobriety, dumbness, idiotcy, and wild imbecility, swarming over one another, sweeping the whole bar before it like a churning surf boiling up on a beach. It was the wave motion of faces that made the New York bar look like Rome in the Colosseum, with gangs of gladiators hacking one another to death. Fierce Roman faces, faces from the Warsaw Ghetto, faces from Dachau...", "2_13_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\nThe space was the waiting room of a rented courtroom on Tralfamadore.\nROBERT BOYNTON STYROP HACTHELER III TOOK from the pocket of his suit a white handkerchief, with which he swabbed the perspiration from his forehead. It was suddenly very hot in the spaceship Locke. The temperature control, set for Efficiency Emblem weather conditions, had not yet been reoriented to Tralfamadorian comfort.\nA cigarette bobbed in the hand of Robert Boynton Styrop III.\nKid Death pressed the fire-button of a thick black ray gun. The ray entered the back of a chair, slowed down, blistered the upholstery, and ate its way into the floor.\nThe ray ricocheted unpredictably around the cabin and set fire to a picture of a young Styrop standing proudly next to a pack mule in the Grand Tetons. Flames shot outward from the burning picture in scarlet ribbons. They burned holes in books, kicked lumps from the leather of a footstool, scorched a companionway from the hall to the kitchen.", "1_6_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after six o'clock in the morning</span>. It was pitch black in the cubicle. I tried to hear the diminishing stamping of the boots as the police left the hotel. There was a rumble of falling debris from the direction of the casino. The old folks\u2019 home was officially a rat\u2019s nest. A furtive movement broke the stillness about me. Lacer\u2019s hands came out of the blackness, touching me here and there, feeling for me...his nature was to make every situation as sexual as possible. I sat motionless.\nNo sound.\nThen: \u201cLet\u2019s get out of here,\u201d he said. The orator had been silenced by the boots. He was much altered by the horrors of the night: frail and confused, old man at dawn.\n\u201cYes,\u201d I said, \u201clet\u2019s go.\u201d We fumbled down the dark steps. No triumph this morning. Just two dirty fugitives hurrying away from a bum scene with empty pockets. 4\nI arrived back at the Mint Hotel. My watch said 6:30. I\u2019d been gone for almost fifteen hours. I rattled the doorknob. It was locked. The front desk came and...", "2_19_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-seven in the evening</span>. The place was a rented house in Newton, Massachusetts. The house had three stories, a number of windows, a slate roof, flowering rugosa bushes in the front, and a big white Colonial-style paned bedroom window in back overlooking a little dumpy yard, lawn, dead elm tree, and a great deal of smog.\nThe house was owned, not rented. The owner was Dr. Morris N. N\u30c9\u30e9\u30b4\u30f3, D.D., D.S.W., D.C., D.N.Sc, Ph.D., of the Department of Research and Development, Dessert DSnack Food Corporation, Kalamazoo, Michigan. The previous owner was a Mr. Don Carroll, of Albany, New York.", "0_19_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>. We were behind our own bar. The sumptuous elegant bar room of the Splendide looked very much as usual. There was nothing in the face, or bearing of the bartender to suggest that anything at all out of the ordinary had happened.\n\"I think he\u2019s asleep,\" I said.\n\"Let\u2019s go,\" said Bill. \"The poor bastard is all in.\"\n\"The dirty bastard,\" I said.\n\"Christ, yes,\" said Bill. \"I\u2019ll never forgive myself, Jake. Here I sent the son of a bitch off like that, and then I just sat around and Walter Mitty\u2019d with you. I\u2019ll never forgive myself.\"\n\"That\u2019s all right,\" I said. \"What the hell? We did what we could.\"\n\"I\u2019m going to see Cohn!\" Bill said. \"He\u2019s got to get up. He can\u2019t stay there.\"\n\"Where should he go?\"\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" Bill said. \"Tell him to go to the Ritz. Tell him to get a suite at the Ritz. Don\u2019t let him forget to phone Robert.\"\n\"", "0_20_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight sixteen in the evening</span> and I was standing in the lobby of the Hotel Albert. It was a shabby white-tiled lobby, very long and narrow, too narrow perhaps for comfort. In one corner was a staircase going up, also very narrow and shabby, also carpeted with a hygienic white substitute for carpet. A porter leaned on this staircase, reading a Spanish newspaper. At the opposite end of the lobby were two glass doors going out to the street. There were about two dozen people in the lobby, sitting silently on benches, not talking to one another. Some of them were Americans and some of them were English. They looked about as though they had come there for the express purpose of doing nothing.\nBehind the desk, leaning on it with both hands, was a tall, elegant man with gray hair, who smiled so that I knew he wanted to sell me something.\n\u201cGood evening, sir. Can I do something for you?\u201d\n\u201cYes. I have a reservation.\u201d\nI gave him my name, Carmencita del Egido y Zuniga, and he smiled and bowed and lifted one finger, as though he were writing on it, but he didn\u2019t give me any message back,...", "2_4_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head rolled on his shoulders. He made the movement slow and lazy and random, since he didn\u2019t have to watch what he was doing.\nNow and then he blinked his eyes, and made sure that the focus was still right, that things were still sharp. But usually he just stared at the opposite wall like a man in a hypnotic trance.\nThings were still blurry when he focused, but $FIRSTNAMEB0 didn\u2019t find it disturbing.\nAt forty-five minutes after four o\u2019clock, $FIRSTNAMEB0 did a push-up, without cheating.\nIt took him forty-three seconds, rather than the thirty seconds it would have taken at Parris Island, to bring his two thousand pounds of weight from a lying-down to a standing-up position.\nAt fifty-two minutes after four o\u2019clock, $FIRSTNAMEB0 sent all the harmonium back, down into his pump. The harmonium rose in gushes, like golden lava in a fountain of baked wheat. The fountain gushed from the end of a hose that was in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mouth. The hose was held there by an old adhesive bandage.", "1_9_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-one in the morning</span>, Central Standard Time; the place,desert country so far west of Yucca Flats that a forgotten people had used it as a bombing range during the Second World War.The unheroic name which furtive, guilty persons had painted on a war surplus hangar on the bombed desert floor was The Wild Turkey Limited Duty Tours Inc. Inside the hangar was a desk, and behind the desk, attended by a robot secretary, a tall, thin young man with blue eyes and a crew-cut waited to greet potential recruits for tours of duty on Salmagundi.\nThe potential recruits were almost all middle-aged, poorly dressed men with the keyed-up, blank look of wage-earners who feel themselves cheated of some wonderful thing\u2014rocketry, maybe\u2014because their educations did not include it.\n\"I always said science would transform society,\" said a man in a tattered violet suit, \"but I never thought it would be leadership that would be transformed.\" He went on without waiting for a reply. \"Ten thousand years from now people will realize just how amazingly primitive we were and how lucky they are to live in the Golden Age of absolute Gospodorism.\"\n\"I should have thought of it myself,\" said the man...", "2_8_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>.\nOne hundred and fifty-eight seconds were still to elapse before Martian clocks were useful again.\n$SURNAME0 and the Space Cadet were hovering over Columbus, Ohio.\nEvery radio set in Ohio went crazy on Life Magnetism at the same second.\nLike the general\u2019s, most of the minds in the state were flooded with random memories at the instant when the three-way showdown was reinstated between Mars, Earth, and Newport News.\n\"Watered stock\u2014\" said the general, remembering.\n\"Ring-around-the-Rosy\u2014\" said a girl, remembering.\n\"White flags of Navy salad\u2014\" said a grocer, remembering.\n\"Three cherries\u2014\" said an old man, remembering.\nThe general, the grocer, the girl, and the old man were not reacting to anything that had actually happened to them. At no time in their lives had they given anyone the same savage pinch $SURNAME0 had just given the general.\nThe memories were memories that had happened to people, rather than memories that had happened to them.\nRadio and television stations went on the air at the height of the excitement over the ridiculous demands of $SURNAME0 and the Space Cadet.", "1_3_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nTwenty-seven miles to the east, in another segment of the great circle, the huge lunar image was rising quickly above the Atlantic.\nWith the coming of morning, Redwine decided to get Gerald Orders and try to telephone Punch and Judy from a booth in the Hilarius Building lobby. When he and the harmonium man gave up, Redwine would let it be known that the major was a dangerously important sleeper\u2014and that, for his own safety and for the safety of the country, all persons engaged in electronic research were discouraged from approaching the great man\u2019s apartment.\nAn air of mystery was good for sentiment and for stability in the stock market.\nThen Redwine could get Gerald, call the Newport Chief, and be back here by sundown tomorrow.\nWith the breathing mask slung over his shoulder, Noah Redwine descended the stairs rapidly and stepped out of the moonlighting into the warm shadow of the orange banyan tree by the front door.\n\"It\u2019s too late now, Se\u00f1or Redwine,\" said a wistful voice behind him.\n\"Wh\u2014?\" said Redwine, and then he knew. He hadn\u2019t seen a soul leaving the yard while he was upstairs. But...", "1_13_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe day was the first of November, of the Year of Our Boy Jesus Christ, 1952.\nIt was Sunday.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s grandfather coughed, it didn\u2019t sound like any of the words people usually cough out. The cough seemed as distinct as the breath of a foreign animal. The cough had a life of its own. Had the cough understood the meaning of being alive, it would have leaped back and bitten the sleeve of its initiator.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, sitting on the dusty floor of the museum, regarded his grandfather with respect. $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s mother called the old man a philosopher, but $FIRSTNAMEB2 didn\u2019t think it wise to call anyone a philosopher. Philosophy sounded like the most dangerous of all the trades.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s father hadn\u2019t come back yet. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been waiting for his father for twenty-three days. He was beginning to be afraid that his father wouldn\u2019t come back at all. His mother had sprung a leak, too. His mother had sprung her leak when his grand father had coughed in his sleep, four months earlier.\nStaring into the face of his grandfather\u2014a boyish, round,...", "0_13_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span> of October 8, presumably 1942\u2014five months after $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s electrocution in the gallery of the Museum of Natural History, New York. A mob of three thousand loyalists, standing room only, squirms, groans, and bleats as one throughout a massed band\u2019s performance of The $FIRSTNAMEM1 Tune.\nThe band is on a platform, fifty feet in the air. The platform is over a four-foot trench dug in the middle of $SURNAME0\u2019s Victory Garden. The trench is six feet wide and four feet deep, and it runs from the west wall of the manor to the cast iron gates at the west wall of the estate.\nDemoiselle \"Dinky\" Von Quackle was goose-stepping along the parapet at the west end of the manor, singing The $FIRSTNAMEM1 Tune through a Hermann Goering gas mask. She had on a close-fitting quilted garment of lead-impregnated finsmool, and she carried in her hands a television camera that weighed as much as she did.\nRumblust and many other paratroopers were milling in the Victory Garden, steeping their boots in the trenches and making spraddle-legged shadows...", "2_5_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five sixteen in the morning</span>. I drove straight to the airport and loaded my bag on a TWA flight for New York -but on a lay-over in Chicago. I wouldn't have minded Denver. I didn't want to see the horrible Pacific Ocean that would take me back to California. The trip was over, was a complete and final disaster, a horrible bummer, no good. There was no place to go and all I could do was keep on going, get off it and breathe, forget it, leave all this madness behind and start all over again somewhere else. And that's what I did.", "0_7_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-one in the morning</span>, mid-April in the opinion of the press spokesman for the Bureau of Meteorology in Boulder. Spo told them what he was in for. \u201cA very confused lady called us about eight o\u2019clock, complaining of animals that won\u2019t stay put. I\u2019m on my way out to take a look at the scene of the tragedy; at this address here.\u201d He showed them the address, jotted on a piece of paper wrapped around his wrist and hanging out of his watch pocket.\nSpo located the address with no trouble. Six seagulls sat watching him from a rooftop down the hill. They seemed to appreciate the gesture when he wished them a good morning.\nThe big house was Victorian: balconies and turrets and fancy trim, with lace curtains hanging in every window. Spo climbed the front steps, started to ring the bell\u2014and it opened up about two feet, letting him into a small hallway where somebody ran very fast on hardpacked snow.\nDiagonally across the hall from the door through which he had entered, there was a chimney, disguised as part of the architecture, terminating in a wroughtiron grille halfway down, above a pile of cigarette butts and bird droppings. Spo...", "1_19_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span> of December 31, 1999, and Phoenician noon. It was sunset at Easter Island, midnight at Carmel, high noon at Samoa, and darkest midnight in the Turkestan desert.\nIt was eight minutes until eight o'clock in the evening of December 31, 1999, and Newport was having its Cocoa Festival.\nAmong the events scheduled for the last hour of the last day of any millennium was the lighting of the linden trees in the center of town. Two spindly trees had been festooned with tiny white lights.\nOther tiny lights were to be added to the display later. The lights were really part of the fireworks display the town was giving. The fireworks were really part of the celebration marking the death of God.\nThe unofficial beginning of the death-of-God movement had beenSeptember 11, 1973, when God died in the air over Malibu, California.\nOn that day, God died in the air over Malibu, and then fell into the Pacific Ocean. The major pieces of Him had been blown east, eventually coming to the Atlantic Ocean, where they floated like nurdles on the great nectarine of God.\nSince then more than twenty-four thousand souls on God\u2019s understanding...", "0_22_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-six in the evening</span> when I finally found the entrance to the Las Vegas Motor Speedway. I had been warned of the difficulty of locating this place, but what appeared to me to be only a few well\u2014concealed and seemingly well\u2014lit roads were actually a web of confusing dirt tracks that seemed to lead to every door and jackpot hotel in the city. I was soon lost enough to call Stan Hart when I found a gas station that offered some kind of tourist information. Stan is a well\u2014known photographer who has wandered all over Las Vegas for twenty\u2014five years. I sent him a telegram asking for help and he arrived at the gas station in about twenty minutes in a long, dusty trailer hauled by a dilapidated pickup truck.\n\u201cSalival (sic) ah/right/there,\u201d he yelled, following my slip\u2014sliding feet as I tried to keep my balance on the gravel of the strange station. \u201cWhat you want to go out there FOR, anyway?\u201d He was wearing strange clothes: rusty cowboy boots, green rayon pants, khaki cutaway coat lumpy with pockets and tape recorder attachments, and a cowboy hat with some kind of tape\u2014recording device glued over one ear.\n\u201cI\u2019...", "0_0_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve forty-seven a.m.</span> Lawton was still dragging his feet shufflingly, twelve feet from the exterior door. The only thing between him and the door was $FIRSTNAMEF1, who was strapped to her throne.\nThe creature did something with its antennae.\nThe child called $FIRSTNAMEF1 screamed.\nThe sound was pure sexual ecstasy.\nThe whole of $FIRSTNAMEF1 melted. The hair and the clothes fell away.\nThe real $FIRSTNAMEF1 was revealed.\nShe was 8 mm. long, with a pink body, five milk-white veined wings, and a head like the dot at the end of this sentence.\nThe antennae curled lovingly around the tiny $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nThe tiny $FIRSTNAMEF1 kissed the antennae passionately.\nA soft pink glow enveloped the antennae, making the creature look beautiful, seductive, harmless, friendly, and lovable.\nAll this had happened in three seconds, while $FIRSTNAMEF1 shrieked.\nAt twelve forty-nine, the seduction was perfect.\nThe seduction ended when the creature gently popped the tiny $FIRSTNAMEF1 between two thumb-sized lobes at the base of its antennae and ate her.\nWithout pausing, the creature then banged happily on the exterior door, demanding to be let out.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and Magnum gobbled...", "2_0_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nRansom K. Fern heated his coffee. He turned on the radio. He turned on the electric grill. He removed two frozen radio dinners from the freezer.\nHe placed a ham and Swiss on the electric grill. He put the other, Chinese chicken rice on the radio. Radio time was one A.M. Far across the empty spaces of his living room, under the lithographs reproduced far too big on cardboard, Ransom K. Fern looked out through a window that faced the night.\nThe boy, Fern said to himself, stooped down, tossed two decoy ducks into the air, and walked away whistling. The bass fiddle roared to the summit of its clef. The boy, rubbing wet hands in two mitts he took from a cubicle by the shore of his lake, stooped over and whirled stone, twirling its part of a web to bring land to fish on.\nThe boy got into his little boat and rowed across the lake.", "0_3_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after three o'clock in the morning</span>. One waking mind was saying to itself, \"Time to get up.\" This was a fifty-year-old man lying in a double bed on the second floor at 220WF Second Avenue. This man had a name. The name, in the public record, was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nAs he prepared to wake up, to open his eyes and remove the circadyne from his wrists, he heard many fairy fiddles playing something gay and haunting in the room. It was only the tinnitus in his ears.\nUnlike most human beings, $SURNAME0 not only heard wind chimes when his tinnitus sounded. Usually he saw beautiful colors and patterns, as well as heard the chiming.\nIn the case of the colors and patterns associated with the tinnitus in his ears at this moment, the colors and patterns had been there all along, being trampled on so forcefully by the music of the fiddles. Now the music was done, and the colors and patterns were emerging from under it triumphantly. The colors were orange and green, reminding $SURNAME0 of an unusual traffic light that he had once seen outside the BrusselsTerminal of the Twentieth Century. The patterns were geometrical, reminding $SURNAME0...", "2_4_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-eight in the morning</span>. I passed Newspaper Row and saw a few press morons stumblegroaning to their shifts. I passed the Sing Sing Prison in a roar of Shootout Special exhaust, went bounding across the wide Hudson, and was in New Jersey.", "2_0_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past midnight</span> when I went into the bedroom and undressed. I left my clothes on a chair and turned to go into the bathroom. Before I knew what had happened I was asleep. The door to the bathroom opened. The dawn came through the window. I turned out the light and got into bed.\nBeing half awake I heard the door close. Then after a little $FIRSTNAMEB5 came into the bed, very cold, and shook me to wake me up, and pulled the covers up around us both.", "0_20_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was standing on the ledge of a window on the third floor of Saint's Ann\u2019s Retreat. Saint\u2019s Ann\u2019s Retreat was a private sanitarium perched high in the mountains. There was a pleasant breeze on the ledge. Between the ledge and the mountain fell a sheer sixty-foot drop to the terrace of flagstones snuggled between the wooden thigh of the east wing and the rocky pelvis of the west wing. To Dandy-lion, $FIRSTNAMEB2's hallucinatory boy, belonged the pelvis; the thigh belonged to a woman in white.\nThe woman was a nurse named Miss Fellowes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was an eight-year-old boy whose father, lawyer $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 of $FIRSTNAMEB4, Turkestan, had recently died in a water-skiing accident in the Gulf of Mexico. $SURNAME0\u2019s will had named the child $FIRSTNAMEB2, his mother Hazel Browne of Newport, Rhode Island, beneficiary. Estate, with instructions to place child in an institution operated by the Church of the Second Chance.\n\"In order that the child may have an opportunity to make a gentleman of himself \" $SURNAME0 had written.\nIn this institution,...", "2_12_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, post meridiem.\n\"And this is Mrs. Stone Whitemarsh,\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh. \"Reeds can\u2019t talk, but Mrs. Stone Whitemarsh can.\"\nMrs. Stone Whitemarsh, who was dancing the fox-trot with Ewing J. Halyard, smiled nicely at everyone. Her face was very white in her black mask.\n\"Well now,\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh wearily, \"I guess I\u2019ll just sit here and look at you a while.\" And he did. He stared at the masqueraders for forty-seven minutes, in absolute silence. But his eyes were not idle. Behind their film, the old man\u2019s artist\u2019s eyes churned with interest and pleasure.", "2_11_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to noon</span>.\nLike a noon, like a great big glorious sunny tumbling no-shadow clock\u2019s noon, the whole history of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 and their baby and all the things that had ever happened to the three of them tumbled over, warm and bright.\nIt was wonderful.\nIt was the best thing that could happen to anything.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 kissed the fingertip of one of his feet, and he touched the kissed fingertip to the baby\u2019s soft forehead.\n\"A man loves his own baby whether it\u2019s a good one or a bad one\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, \"and whether it\u2019s a boy or whether it\u2019s a girl.\"\nTrampoline\nSPEED and space? You want speed and space? said Helmholtz. Ever seen a rifle bullet fired point-blank at somebody?\nOld Salter, who had been the subject in the demonstration, said, Boy, oh boy.\nDon\u2019t that give you a concept of speedy and expansive, said Helmholtz. Not to mention terminal velocities, said Helmholtz. Life\u2019s one helluva concept, said Helmholtz.", "0_10_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>, and the year was 1951.\nJust about everybody who was going to San Narciso was already there by this time. There had been a few holdouts. These showed up in dribbles, then in trickles, and finally (when Castro and his ilk had seemed cinched) whole streams, reddening the streets like heavy traffic in the sunset, before true noon and the grand sweep of demographics, when the telephone company disintegrated all obstacles, levels, and distinctions in the great mass of mankind, and any man anywhere could talk to any other\u2014with exceptions, of course, but whom would you want to talk to that San Narciso could not supply? The Company man in charge of the renunciation signing was a Santa Monica yachtsman, a skin-diver who had once run for county supervisor on the balloontits platform, a man who seldom read anything but sailing news, comic books, and popular fiction, and was regarded even by his management as just this side of dim: but there was one thing he knew, and he knew a lot about it, because the people in charge had been persistent in telling him about it. And he knew that, come time for the choosing of San Narciso, the people there would choose...", "2_13_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Terra time, twenty minutes after the fleet of ships had left Mars for Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had taken over the controls of the ship for the jump through space to Mars. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been flat on his back with his eyes closed for the last ten minutes. His time outfit warned him he would be pilot-in Earthling time\u2014in exactly three minutes and eleven seconds.\nBigman stepped over $FIRSTNAMEB0 to the pilot\u2019s position. \"I think I\u2019ll fly this thing, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" he said in a whisper. \"You take it easy now. We\u2019re almost there.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 groaned and sat up. His time outfit informed him that he was pilot in fifty-nine seconds.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt bewildered. \"Is it time already?\" he said.\n\"Here,\" said Bigman, \"I\u2019ll watch the machine, $FIRSTNAMEB0. Don\u2019t you bother. Go back to your bunk and sleep it off.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt under his feet for the pedals controlling the ship\u2019s power.", "0_11_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-five in the morning</span>. Rader stood there shaking for more than fifteen seconds before he could compose himself to knock. He did knock then, three times in close succession, before a sharp female voice from inside summoned him to, \u201cJust come in, it's unlocked.\u201d Rader went click, click, click. He found himself in a dingy, cluttered tricycle shop. The middle of the shop was about eight feet wide and contained a kidney-shaped bench covered with tools of uncertain function. At the far end and along one side of the shop ran a maze of long wooden benches, three high and interconnected. Each bench contained identical stacks of rolling white cartons. The place was dim, lit only by tall windows along the side walls. There were perhaps twenty white cardboard boxes scattered unevenly on the floor. Long rolling shadows came and went over the boxes and roller benches, fading with each gust of ghastly sea wind that brushed itself through the cracks in the large windows beside which Rader stood. The wind came all the way across the salt flats from the sea. It carried an occasional vista of highbreaking surf. Beyond the windows, where Rraders now stood, the rusted doors of Ma Bell's switching center rattled in their crazymounts.", "2_12_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"I would like,\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh, \"to ask all of you a question. And, if you don\u2019t mind, I\u2019ll put the question in a poem. It\u2019s a silly poem, and it\u2019s an to rhyme it. That\u2019s the fun.\"\nThe time was one o\u2019clock in the afternoon. \"Here goes then,\" said Old Stone Whitemarsh.\nThere once was a fellow named Max\nWho fancied himself quite a cas,\nSo he knocked on the door of a club\nExpecting that they\u2019d let him pas,\nBut, as fate would have it, the doorman said \"No\"\nTo that proposition from Max.\nThere once was a fellow named Sam\nWhose appearance was not quite sane,\nFor he\u2019d chosen for mask a guy with a skimpy ham\nAnd a halo made out of a rain-mack;\nFor suit, he had on a fine\nGrey flannel burial suit;\nBut that didn\u2019t persuade the committee of gate-crashers;\nThey decided he wasn\u2019t all there.", "2_19_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven eighteen in the evening</span>, Earth time. $SURNAME0 had slipped into the materialization chamber of the trailer to find out whether or not the time-warp showed any sign of changing.\nSpaceships from Earth were arriving from the future at the rate of one every seven minutes.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 could not see the hangar through the sheeting rain and mist, but they could guess about the action in there from the frequency with which the noise of whistle-blasts came through the driving wet.\nThe rumble of the ships was also to be heard, piercing the fog at infrequent intervals. The racket made $SURNAME0\u2019s fillings hurt.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s chronometer now said seven nineteen.\nSeven-twenty\u2014a full minute before the last ships had arrived\u2014there had been a great deal of noise and excitement in the hangar. Now there was silence\u2014complete, unbroken silence.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 waited quietly in the materialization chamber of the trailer.\nThe minutes passed. While fifty feet away, in the hangar, Universe Guard McKinley had his replica ships maneuver for take-off, $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEF0 sat together in a contour chair, their useless eyes closed.", "0_12_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-five post meridiem</span>.\nThe roll of the ship had stopped.\nThe ship lay quietly on the flat, smooth water\u2014water that would have been flat, smooth and silky-looking no matter what the weather-mist or big waves.\nThe ship was motionless.\nIn the wheelhouse, which need not be enlarged on here, there were the figures of the helmsman, $FIRSTNAMEB0, and Captain Larry, the real commander of the expedition.\nThe real commander was not the handsome, clear-eyed, confident white-blond specimen you might expect. The real commander was a man so timid that he shivered when he shaved. He shaved daily. He had a perpetual case of whisker-shivers.\nWhat was his secret? He did not know, either. He supposed, as people always suppose in such cases, that he had been a very ruthless, heartless and treacherous person in his former life. He supposed he was trying to atone for his past.\nThis assumption, like most popular assumptions, was not true. His timidity did not come from a brutal, treacherous past.\nThe only brutal and treacherous person at the switchboard, in his or her past life, had been the person who had put him or her there...", "0_5_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span>, and Earthlings and Martians, having lived together for years and empathized splendidly, now faced the hideous necessity for blowing up the planetoid, cleaning out the $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infundibula, killing everything, and separating into lonely flight among the stars. The only indications of a dawn came from the shaded part of the porch where $SURNAME1, Lime, and Stony were sitting. The whole eastern wing of the house blazed gloriously with a solid sheet of electric-white floodlights.\nThe field-mice and the bats were making one final attempt to understand each other. The bats were crying tu-whit tu-whit tu-whit, and their cries were answered by the musically incomprehensible bat language, which sounded like a heavy pipe made of iron being banged against another heavy iron pipe.\nA composite translator put the bat language into English this way: \"What y\u2019all want us to do? What y\u2019all want us to do? Tell us what y\u2019all want us to do, and we\u2019ll do it good. What y\u2019all want us to do? What y\u2019all want us to do? \"\nThe field-mice said nothing...", "0_17_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span> and the place was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s home on Mars, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 was in his bathroom, and he had been gazing, vacantly, into the mirror above the washbasin, and he had found a pimple on his right cheek. Then he found a pimple on his left cheek, and then he found a pimple on his chin.\nThe pimples were clear signs that he was truly a man now, and therefore they were good in themselves. But they were not good in their possible consequences.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had explained to $FIRSTNAMEB0 the real reason for men getting pimples.\nIt wasn\u2019t that men were excited mentally by the General\u2019s daily message. The real reason men got pimples was that women were excited physically by the General\u2019s daily message, and the mating season was on.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had explained to $FIRSTNAMEB0 that every Martian was paired for life, male and female. Marriage as Earthmen know it was unknown on Mars. The paired Martians had simply assumed mate-ship each time the mating season came around.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had assumed mate-ship with his own truly beautiful housemate,Beech. And Beech had assumed mate-ship with another beautiful Martian male, Stony...", "0_9_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-one in the morning</span>. In twenty-seven minutes, the first person in the room was supposed to start getting his pictures ready. Each contestant was allowed to choose five pictures that had been taken of him in public within the last three months. The pictures had to be three by five inches, and the contestant had to present the photos to the judges himself.\nOf the hundred and fifty-seven contestants, thirty-nine had decided to choose only four pictures each. The forty-three Russians had chosen five, as had Leon Trotsky Schwartz. Treblinka Van Vliet had chosen only one picture, and, since her picture was that of a beautiful woman, had recused herself from competition.\n$SURNAME0 was in his rascally mood that morning. He was handing out the Pocket Guides with a leisureliness born of supreme personal confidence. There was no need to hurry. The material in the Pocket Guide was extremely interesting to read right up to the very moment when the whistle blew.\n\"Hello again,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I haven\u2019t seen you in\u2014what days is it?\"\n\"Two,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\n\"My name is $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1,\" said $SURNAME1. \"I think you have\u2014\"\n\"Yes,\" said...", "1_17_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nShe had just finished doing the laundry in the basement. She had come up to the third floor.\nShe stood by her door.\nThe world lay at her feet.\nShe saw the air.\nShe saw roofs, tree-tops, smokestacks, spires, domes.\nShe saw World\u2019s Fair buildings scattering fountains of white in the sun. She saw the Concavity\u2019s edge, the orange walls behind which lay nothing. She saw the Great Grey Inner Slobian Plain, in the midst of which lay her small city. She saw her city, tiny compared to the Plain. She saw the Harbor, tiny at the City\u2019s feet. She saw the Lake, a dot to the Harbor\u2019s dot. She saw the broken you-and-me of sailboats out spread on the Lake.\nShe saw out-thrust headlands of Wisconsin and Illinois. She saw love\u2019s heart of Chicago and electric crosstown strands. She saw the airports and their attendant souls. She saw corn pale and thick, restless in breezes. She saw a river ice-free, flowing blue. She saw it pass the cities of Iowa. She saw the streets of...", "1_17_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe voyage of the space ship Willard International Selection Committee was two minutes two seconds old.\nWalter Chiles sat in the pilot berth of the space ship. He was now wearing the earphones of the intercom system and was pressing down a switch on a control panel. Behind his control panel was a wall of painted cardboard. It was the rear of a radio-TV stage set. Walter Chiles knew that the wall of cardboard was there, and that it was intended by the prop men to suggest the far wall of an engine room of a space ship.\nBut Walter Chiles had this secret on his mind: The cardboard wall was not the far wall of an engine room. It was the far wall of a radio-TV stage.\nThe only real equipment in the engine room was a microphone, an amplification system, some wires, and Walter Chiles himself.\nWalter Chiles had not told his fellow committee members what kind of a craft he was flying. He had told them only to think of themselves as participating in an actual space voyage.\nThe pilot's seat of the non-existent engine room was equipped with electrically heated pads to suggest the cold of space. Walter Chiles had the heater on full.\n\"Now remember...", "1_8_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight nineteen in the morning</span>, a gray morning, dark clouds over the mountaintop. Inside the trailer Nately\u2019s birds were making their morning racket, chirping and screeching. Through the kitchen window Mal could see the Space Wanderer, dressed in a leather jacket and motorcyclist\u2019s cap, sitting at the orange iron table playing chess with Nately. They were using a child\u2019s set. The Space Wanderer kept looking at his wristwatch.\nAt eight twenty-three $FIRSTNAMEF1 came running down the path with her girl\u2019s black pageboy reaching halfway down her back. She wore a pink plastic raincoat over black fatigues and combat boots. She yanked at the screen door for half a minute.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the idea?\u201d said Mal from the stove.\n\u201cIt\u2019s stuck!\u201d\n\u201cI guess you\u2019re stuck, too, then.\u201d\n\u201cOh, what a riot!\u201d\n\u201cAre you trying to get yourself clean away?\u201d\n\u201cLook, never mind, I\u2019ll get it some other way,\u201d his wife said, concealing her right hand in her raincoat. She withdrew it...", "0_18_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after six o'clock in the evening</span>, Lily Briscoe reminded herself, looking at the clock above the mantelpiece in the drawing-room. Every other minute was spread out before her, stiff as starched linen; the three minutes gone by like a whirl of snow. Why did she look at the clock? She could hardly tell; something important seemed about to happen; some one was coming; her mind and eyes seemed to wait and lift themselves, as the petal of a flower lifts itself at the approach of sunshine or a breezy air. But she knew that no one was coming. They had returned from their walk; they had finished dinner; that was all. On most evenings, certainly, someone was there; her aunt came to her stitching; her father read the newspaper; Mr Carmichael dozed on the sofa. Then, at intervals, there might be sounds-a carriage stopping; people calling-Mrs Ramsay; Lily; James; Cam; Minta; Andrew, which roused the old misgivings-Minta was too late-Minta could not find a brush- Andrew and James were fighting in the passage-and her father's hoarse bellow, trying to quell the clamour, \"Quiet! I'll have no noise here...", "0_6_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>. Ten billion years to go, thought $SURNAME0, before the big show starts.\nISIMILA\nIN THE HISTORY OF Space Flight, only one person ever took off under his own power. His name was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nWhen he became the richest man in the Solar System, he threw a dinner party that lasted three hundred and fifty-six Earthling days\u2014a dinner party to end all dinner parties.\nThe centerpiece of the $SURNAME1 dinner party was a fountain in the shape of a two-hundred-and-forty-foot-high Martian war rocket. Mercury, the messenger boy of the gods, looked out of the open furnace door at the base of the rocket.\nThe staircase to the fountain was a spiral staircase of cometary diamonds.\nThe murals of the dining hall of the $SURNAME1 mansion were murals of the scenery that one would see from the Milky Way, looking out from the center of the spiral arms. The paintings began at the Yellow Layer, portrayed that portion of the spiral territory for which the Solar System had responsibility, then spiraled up through increasingly older and richer strata, finally reaching the Phalanx, where hung all that was still mysterious\u2014banded mother-of-pearl,...", "1_15_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-three in the afternoon</span>. The streets were crowded with nothing but Okie kids, out for a long spring walk. Old winos stood in front of cigar stores and spoke to them animatedly. These were the only sounds: footsteps and wino voices. Kesey dawned up to the big door of a big red house and tried it; it was unlocked. He went inside. People seemed to be lying everywhere in the living room\u2014I mean in the act of lying down\u2014face down, under blankets. Most of them were asleep. Others opened their eyes blandly and waved helplessly at the ceiling. There never were so many stoned lids in one room, nor so many people helplessly waving. One of them got on top of a piano and began playing Chopsticks with one finger, accompanying the stoned riot of fluttering lids. They also heard somebody pounding on a wall, shouting:\n\u201cGo away! Go away! I\u2019m not ready yet! Go away!\u201d\nBut nobody left. In fact it only made everybody giggle.\nKesey flipped the blankets off a girl and gave her a big hug. She was wide awake, and said \u201cHmmmm\u201d into his shoulder, her face all peaceful.", "2_23_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-one in the evening</span>. The tide on the ship was going out. The tide was flow, out, slide, groan, grind, whimper, whine. The tide was the rhythm of things seeming to deteriate, with things apparently about to fall apart\u2014the rhythm of things standing up for just one more second, then teetering and falling, sliding one way and going another, and ending a minute later in the rinse cycle.\nThe rhythm of the tide was forty-seven minutes and fourteen seconds old, but already beginning to fade.\nThe rhythm of things rising, peaking, decaying.\nNothing lasts. Fall in and be sucked down, my dear. Slide and let go.\nHelm, port watch.\nLieutenant (jg) Mary Shanahan washed down a dry throat with a dry swallow.\nShe unballed her fists, stretched her arms as far as they would go, hearing the vertebrae click romance in her neck.", "1_2_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>. Damp tendrils of fog clung to everything in the police barracks courtyard. There were no birds. Everything was wet.\n\"You,\" said the sergeant to a recruit, \"you take over.\"\nThe sergeant gave the recruit a great book, like a directory. \"This,\" he said, \"is known as Field Messages, Special Series, Infantry, Fortieth Edition, 1943. You will have to deliver the message at once, because of the fog. Don\u2019t be afraid. The message is written by experts. Memorize it. Master it. When you get it right, bring it straight back.\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said the recruit.\n\"And keep your fingers off your nose while you\u2019re memorizing,\" said the sergeant. \"Gooch, come here.\"\nA recruit came to attention before the sergeant\u2019s desk.\n\"You,\" said the sergeant. \"You\u2019re Gooch, aren\u2019t you?\"\n\"Yessir,\" said Gooch.\n\"You\u2019re to go out with the first qualified messenger. Walk at his elbow. Pay strict attention to what he does. Do exactly as he does.\" The sergeant looked at his wristwatch. \"When...", "0_19_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span> of February 12, 1977. The room was $FIRSTNAMEF1- orders Apartment 1-D, the Boiler House, the Temporary Executive Building, on Old Jazzmyer Boulevard, Stonecipheco Baby Food Products, Madison, Wisconsin, U.S.A. The physical source of the nauseating and pricking noises was the television set in the kitchen alcove. The programming was of the form commonest in United States living rooms on Thursday evenings and featured the hypnotic disc jockey known as Wolfman Jack. The room\u2019s lighting came from the television screen, a sixty-watt bulb in an aluminum shade in the ceiling transacted somehow to illuminate the fixture itself and to project its soft yellow glow indirectly onto the ceiling above the alcove. The room contained a green-shaded floor lamp between the easy chairs at the television set. The lamp took its own power (as did the set) from a high-frequency baby monitor plugged into one wall and a floor line running down the leg, beneath the table by the window and into the cabinet containing the television set. The television set was on, the sound down low. In the Stonecipheco executive chair behind the marble-topped table in the far right corner of the...", "2_21_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-nine in the evening</span>. The place was the Stonecipheco Baby-Food Building, downtown Cleveland, Ohio. Walinda Peahen, in full evening regale, alone in a staff elevator, rose quietly to Walinda\u2019s office, in search of a bottle of anything uncontaminated. The light that overhung Walinda\u2019s office door burned brightly, but the office itself was dark. Through the little window of the frosted glass that sur ro unded the office\u2019s air-intake vent, Walinda could see Dr. J_____ looking down into the grainy murk of the cubicle, over Mavis\u2019s shoulder, with face unbearably bright, talking to Mavis, who was silent and rigid. Thinking about Lenore.\nThings fall apart; the center cannot hold;\nMavis looked up into Dr. J_______\u2019s light and laughed and laughed, until she let the bottle of Amnesia Sauce slip from her hand, until it fell into the darkness off camera in the LaMol es\u2019 office, rolling its wooden cork, until it splashed res ipidly into the little abyss of the sad mailbox.", "2_1_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-two in the morning</span>. $SURNAME1 opened his watch to reassure himself that he was no longer timing anything.\n\"God bless you, boys,\" he said aloud.\nGod save the people I love.\nGod bless you, girls.\nGod bless you all.\nThe watch had a luminous face. $SURNAME1 stared into his watch, at its comforting blue.\nThe watch, moreover, kept perfect time. It had been a graduation present to $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 from his friends and fellow students at the Princeton Home for Crippled Children.\n\"Ten thousand, two hundred, eleven seconds,\" said $SURNAME1, \"and every one of them a little jewel.\"\nHe looked at Earth through the window.", "1_15_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. At that very moment, ten thousand seekers of intoxicating pleasure were trying to push their way into the Sheridan Square Theater on South Sixth Street. At that very moment, every one of the ten thousand was losing his temper. A riot was about to break out. The ten thousand were lined up six abreast in the hot sun, waiting for the doors to open. Inside, the managers of the movie theater were pulling back the tabs on the freon containers sixty seconds sooner than usual -- just to make sure. Down both sides of the street for maybe two hundred yards were American Legion posts, Rotarian clubs (the Rotarians in their little blue hats), Lions clubs (the Lions in their little red hats), Masons, Shriners, Odd Fellows and other groups as nondescript as one another, all with their little hotdog stands, popcorn machines, coke machines, thirty-cent luncheonettes and prewar gangster movies, making a gold rush orgy out of the day. At 3:05, the doors of the movie house opened and the mob dashed in. Ten thousand pairs of eyes sought out desirable seats while the rest of the bodies scurried around like rats. Six minutes later, the place was full. Five hundred ticket...", "2_1_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nMcRevem's yard was choked with iron apparatus. There were old barrels, stacks of car rims, mammoth iron wheels, iron pipes, drums and boilers, iron chairscontraptions, and several ornate iron birdbathscontraptions.\nThe yard was further cluttered with huge iron statues. There were two iron griffons, two iron knights on iron horses, a statue of a sitting dog, a bird in an iron nest, and an iron Civil War soldier. And there was one immense statue of a nude iron woman. She was a yard wide and a yard high. Her iron hands held an iron wineskin. Her iron face was old, drunk, licentious and gargoyle. She squatted on a barren patch of McRevem's yard in the gathering Cleveland fall mist. Her pud was the size of a Volkswagen.\nMcRevem was in the act of screwing the cap back onto a whiskey bottle. He heard the hatch of the iron chimney overhead creak open.", "2_0_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. Moonlight fell with particular brightness upon the wet pavement of San Francisco Bay.\nThe boy was waiting for a space ship.\nThe boy was very old\u2014older than the space ship he was waiting for. He was six hundred and forty-eight years old. Far from home and sight of his own people, he felt his great age and the magnitude of his responsibility.\nAn astronomer, who lived in a high house, had said that the space ship he was waiting for might come some night.\nThe astronomer was wrong. The astronomer was a very learned man, but he owned no farm and had never seen a cow being born.\nNo one in all history had ever seen a space ship. No one knew what a space ship did when it arrived. No one even knew what a space ship looked like.\nThe boy was waiting for a ship without designing to look like one. He did not even hope the ship would land on his uncle's farm. He hoped only that he might see what the ship did and perhaps where it came from.\nLocal groups were active throughout the known universe in monitoring the atmospheric content of planets and spacecraft alike.", "2_8_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-four in the morning</span>.\nAt eight fifty-one, $SURNAME2 was strapped down in a straight-jacket in an ambulance.\nFrom an air-vent in the ceiling of the ambulance came whistling noises, from steam.\n$SURNAME2 feared that the whistling noises were coming from his own hair.\n\"Fasten your safety belts, please,\" said the old woman in the driver\u2019s seat of an ahead-moving car.\n\"Ooops!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, belting himself.\n\"Ooops!\" said the Space Wanderer, belting himself.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s and the Space Wanderer\u2019s names were really $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2. Their names had been changed, their pasts denied them, they had been thrust into the same clothes on the same day, and they were being flown to the same place at the same time.", "1_19_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. The approach step of my apartment house was deserted. The door of the foyer was partly closed, as I had left it\u2014locked, of course. My mail had been delivered and stacked neatly on the floor right next to the mail chute. I took it out\u2014mostly junk mail, campaign literature and magazine reprints\u2014and carried it up to my apartment.\nMy apartment seemed strange, a little sinister. I walked around, turning on all the lights, including the overheads in the kitchen and the bathroom. One of my two suits\u2014we\u2019re not rich\u2014was hanging neatly in its proper place in the back of the closet. The other one was gone: my darling wife had packed it up, with my tweed hat and my five underarm shirts and socks, and was running even in scissors, shower clogs and sandals, flying.\nNo matter. I was reminded too vividly of something I\u2019d rather not think about. I went into the toilet and locked the door. The email ball was in the inbox. I picked it up, carried it with me, sat down on the sofa in the living room.\nThe vibes were ominous. This sofa, seventy\u2014two inches long, was much too big for...", "1_8_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-nine in the morning</span>. The Westward Ho! was beginning to wake up. Here and there people were strolling in the sunshine of the court, having coffee and rolls in a booth, getting the morning papers from a newsdealer, chatting, spilling out into the bright sun of the place. An occasional taxi turned in from the street noise that was beginning to come up the avenue. At the tables people read their papers and talked. The sun was shining brightly. It was going to be another lovely day.\nIn the dark vestibule in back of the bar, the barkeep was polishing glasses and thinking about something that somebody had told him about the man who lived in room 509, when something flew past him and hit the wall about four feet up, shattering hy the impact. It made a devil of a noise.\nEverybody in the joint looked up from the papers and conversations.\nThe bartender gazed dazedly at the broken glass and the strange missile, until with a sudden horrible impression of what it might be he leaped across the floor and peered up at the wall.\nEverybody came over.\nThere was a man\u2019s eye\u2014a human eye\u2014clinging to the wall. It had hit with such force that the eyelid had popped...", "2_9_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until ten o'clock in the morning</span>.\nEight minutes fewer to the glorious, happy event that a squad of marines had to look forward to\u2014eight minutes having to do with how to ease $FIRSTNAMEB0 onto the short bus to glory. It would take human cunning and sympathy for human weakness. There were forty-six marines on the floor above. Forty-six keen boys. Rumor said $FIRSTNAMEB0 was going to be put to death. Rumor said he was a spy who had killed other spies in cold blood, who had cravenly fled with vital secrets\u2014another Benedict Arnold who had to be put upon a pole.\nRumor, eight minutes from ten o'clock, was almost all of the truth.\n\"Do you live in a house?\" said Sgt. $SURNAME2. He was trying to find out if $FIRSTNAMEB0 had a family.\n\"What?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Oh\u2014I was just wondering,\" said $SURNAME2. He had expected $FIRSTNAMEB0 to have at least a nodding acquaintance with the American system of unit dwellings.", "0_14_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty-one in the afternoon</span> of December twenty-first, 1995. The place was a deluxe suite in a beautiful Newport Beach hotel. The occasion was my own forty-first birthday party. The crowd was very mixed. Very chic. There were a lot of executives and lawyers in expensive suits, and gamblers in safari suits and cowboy boots - tops made of alligator, bottoms made of Art Connoisseur calf - and showgirls with tons of makeup and enormous litde mounds of flesh, and a great spread of maraschino cherries in the bottom of each girl\u2019s fringed purse. There was a lot of bamboo, and Japanese concessions, and a curiously large number of men who looked like Sidney Greenstreet extras\u2014impressively fat, impressively silver-haired. You couldn\u2019t tell if the fellows were judges or bookies, because it was a legal/illegal jam. I saw at least two ex-sheriffs. There was a curious sense of dangerous investment-yours, and powerful speculation-yours.... Business. Reefer madness. Who is swimming naked? The bastards in L.A. Enforcement are nobody to fool with. And every now and then a big drunk businessman type would come zooming across the suite...", "2_4_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span> in silly, despicable, pompous, bawdy, swanky, mad, scandalous, gaudy, wonderful Las Vegas.", "2_4_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-nine in the morning</span> when the $FIRSTNAMEB3 of Drysdale\u2019s House in New Jersey answered the phone and said, \u201cHello? That you Norman? That you, big chief?\u201d and I said, \u201cYes\u201d -feeling a bit groggy, because New Jersey is so Godawful far out, you slump over the wheel and start nodding, fast. \u201cOK, I\u2019ll call him,\u201d said the kind-voiced old man, and I could hear him calling across the mansion lawn: \u201cHere\u2019s a young fella on the phone says he\u2019s big chief! What\u2019s all this about big chief?\u201d And I heard another voice I knew well: \u201cHello?\u201d Dwayne Andeer was talking from his own home in Leonia, New Jersey, and I said, \u201cLet\u2019s get it on,\u201d but first he said, \u201cHey, man, you know there\u2019s a mountain lion eating sheep five blocks from here? They got it trapped, they say it\u2019s the biggest mountain lion they ever saw in these parts; I say screw the lion, what\u2019re you doing in New Jersey?\"", "1_21_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine seventeen in the evening</span> when the stamp blew across $SURNAME0\u2019s face,\n+9\nHe carried his pack up the spiral staircase, detouring frequently to look at paintings. There was something majestic about the chrono-synclastic infundibulum, something dazzlingly nude about the stairway in relation to the rest of the palace.\nWhen Roche, the butler, came puffing up the stairs to greet him, $SURNAME1 was at the top of the stairs, about to put his hand to the doorknob.\nRoche carried an old-fashioned cavalry pistol in a holster. It was cocked.\n\"Uninvited guests are shot on sight, sir,\" said Roche.\n\"Oh,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Goodness. I hadn\u2019t heard. I\u2014I\u2014\"\nHe paused and looked at the beautiful Cyrillic letters on the butler\u2019s gold lapel. There was an unusual design in the background of the gold: a design made up of daisies. It was a design that $SURNAME1 somehow knew belonged to a species of wildflower that grew in the vicinity of $FIRSTNAMEB4, in the Province of Siberia, Earth.\n\"I\u2014are you the butler?\" said $SURNAME1. He found that the...", "0_17_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME0 and his wife and child materialized at the instant of materialization like figures on an electric display, having seemed, so long as they were in transit, like nothing so much as the changing configurations in a column of rising cigarette smoke.\nThere was no sound at the instant of materialization. But as the three materializations separated, buffeted by winds, the mother\u2019s robe flapped vigorously, and from inside the angry fabric came a sound that well-behaved cloth should never make\u2014a sound of ripping and tearing.\nThe sound came from rips and tears in the Virgin\u2019s undergarment\u2014and from a broken bone in the mother\u2019s left leg, which had been rudely jolted by the abrupt ending of materialization.\nAt the sound of ripping and the sound of her own bone breaking, the Virgin looked at her left leg and saw that the bone was broken. She made the sound again, this time to describe what she saw.\nShe was a remarkably handsome woman, and strangely game, considering that she had just suffered the worst battering that life has it in her to give. Her will to make another sound for the benefit of her child was almost heroic.\nThe only thing...", "1_3_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to four o'clock in the morning</span>, Thursday, July 2nd, 1997, and there was a man named Metzger somewhere in the rarified, paranoid atmosphere between Nevada\u2019s casinos that the FBI and CIA could not locate. This bothered the agencies, because Metzger had been marked by at least one wheelman for termination. The wheelman, sixteen years old, was almost as great a name to certain members of the Criminal element as Metzger. He was called the LaserPost Sign Kid.\nThis is what happened: The kid, whose name was Charles Freck, was cruising down the Strip in a souped-up black \u201965 Mustang he\u2019d named after the girlfriend who\u2019d left him, the one who\u2019d said he looked like Jerry Lee Lewis, that is, before the surgery, when to please her he\u2019d worn his hair long and, if necessary, waxed into weird serpentine formations. He was now wearing his hair thin and close, like Parnas's, one of the Rolling Stones. Freck owned a halfpercent share in Parnas\u2019s steam bath, liked to pose as his manager.\nThis evening had begun for Freck in the steam, pounding Parnas\u2019s...", "0_0_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-three ante meridiem</span>.\nDolores, a\u2014k-a Douglas M. Costigan, received the following message from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 at twelve fifty-two ante meridiem:\n\"Good-by and good luck. Abandon ship as you deem best. It is not necessary to warn you of the danger of attempting space travel in a fever induced by narrative disorientation.\n\"The next twain to leave for Mars passes THROUGH your old stamping grounds\u2014the Greenwich section of San Francisco. This means nothing to you now. Remember it through your worst alcoholic haze. $SURNAME1.\"\nDisappearance of a Spacecraft\nGillian lay in bed and went through the file on $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 one more time\u2014for the sake of the way it started.\nTHE HIGH PURPOSE OF MALACHI CONSTANT\nIn a few moments I shall be dead. In a few hours my baby son, $SURNAME0, will be dead also.\nBefore I die, I want the people of Earth to know this about me:\nI lived for nothing higher, nobler, or more profitable than to introduce into common civic consciousness one new fact about mankind.\nThe goodness, the nobility, and the utility of every man in ordinary Earthling society.", "0_19_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-seven in the evening</span>, I could tell by the light falling against the curtains. The deal was we changed rooms every three months and ended up sleeping in a different bed every night in the house. Surely there are houses that do that automatically. I was using the olive-wood rosary Joe had made in the summer peace camps. It almost comforted me. I had a coughing fit. When I clenched my fists my heart resounded: there was that heart attack feeling to it, the air enclosed and pressing down from all sides. I had an orgy of coughing and throwing up. Each time I threw up there was less and less in my stomach.\nIt was the only room in the house I found out of the ordinary and uncomfortable: there was a smell of chrysanthemum furniture polish and brush closets and brass; once my eyes had adjusted I could see old carpenter\u2019s tools on the walls, planes and chisels and a gadget for squaring and smoothing the edges of boards, also toolboxes and a hammer weighing as much as two armchairs put together and nails curled with rust and some kind of iron candle thing that had melted, hardware and hinges and things. I suspected Old Bull Thaw did carpentry when he wasn\u2019t...", "0_1_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one o'clock in the morning</span>. North half an inch of the compass rose was Baltimore Pennsylvania , south half an inch was Baltimore Maryland . At that moment we were passing the shipyard where they made the Hornet , which blew up at Sea; a mate who was aboard told the story -- their magazines had caught fire. We were passing Friendship Airport, where pilots learned to fly on the Civilian Pilot Training Program. We honked. Lights came on. We passed Aberdeen Proving Ground, the experimental station for the Second Division, Ballistics Research. Lights came on, a lone sentry stopped us. \u201cWho's there?\u201d\nBack to back we stood there in the rain as if we were victims of the sameoubter and awaited the firing squad.\n\u201cPass, friend!\u201d In bewilderment he fingered a Rubiytsy, stared and stared at the Prophet.\n\u201cHonk!\u201d We rolled on South. Two o'clock in the morning. Mosquitoes appeared and blasted us with clouds of sulfurous insecticide. \u201cAh!\u201d unbearable screams. \u201cDrive that sickly thing away, $FIRSTNAMEB6, I can't stand it!\u201d\n\u201cYou think you're smart? You little fool! You're...", "1_8_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty in the morning</span> and the hall\u2014clerk was ready for the day.\n\u201cWhat was that about $FIRSTNAMEB5 and the bull-fighter?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cShe went over to San Sebastian and took him to the bull-fights.\u201d\n\u201cOh, yes,\u201d he said. \u201cI knew there was something.\u201d\n\u201cWhy,\u201d I said, \u201cdidn\u2019t you take her to the bull-fights?\u201d\n\u201cOh, no, sir,\u201d he said. \u201cI never let the guests of the hotel go to any of the localities.\u201d\n\u201cWhat localities?\u201d\n\u201cYou know, sir. Wine-cellars, night-clubs, bull-fights\u2014anything of that kind, any place where they might meet people and have good times.\u201d\n\u201cOh, yes,\u201d I said. \u201cI understand.\u201d\n\u201cWe have to protect all our good clients from themselves.\u201d\n\u201cThe gentleman went to San Sebastian and all these localities, did he?\u201d\n\u201cYes, he certainly did.\u201d\n\u201cWith $FIRSTNAMEB5 and...", "0_19_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. It was still light. The clouds were black and pewter-colored. Field commanding a view of the stone floor toward the iron double doors. The field continued along the iron doors. The field then continued with the combined door and concave wall, running toward and along the back wall and out of sight. The iron double doors were shut. There were black stripes down the sides of the white robes worn by the men with the scarred faces. There were two yellow-white smudges near the top of the back wall. The smudges were brighter than the rest of the wall, reflecting the clouds and the light. The two bright patches were the glowing, measured puffs of cigarettes in the dark robes of the Thurn and Taxis butlers. One of the butlers was rigid, partly visible, half in the field, half in the f o rm of the wall. A gaunt man whose face was wrinkled and carved with long, obscurely flowing lines. The other butler was bent in half, perpendicular to the wall, so that all that was visible of him were two legs and the bottoms of his dark slippers and gray socks wadded around the feet. His back was squared against the wall. Only the light from...", "1_10_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten o'clock in the morning</span> and Detroit was just hitting its stride. This was the hour when we finally got rolling with our gang. But only after a scary ride across town to the American Legion Hall on some godawful street that sprouted stumplike junctions in the middle of blocks for no immediately apparent reason. The last stretch was through darkness along an unearthly embankment, the road hugging the river. When finally we pulled into the dilapidated parking lot of the Legion hall and Beethoven's First poured over the country darkness, you knew you were in the Great Southwest -- this music rising from the flatlands of Louisiana on a summer night via tape deck full strength.Majorbeer's opening words were: \u201cThink I got this goddamn thing stuck on a Mexican station.\u201d We listened. It was definitely a Mexican revolution. Every cry of \u201c\u00a1Viva!\u201d sent a shiver through me. One thing my cash shortage had not screwed up was the cheap radio battery that kept on living. I had more power in my radio than anybody in the room. My incredible radio could even bring in signals from far. far away Denver -- via Bonanza,,,the Denver station that played fantastic gold-plated rock 'n roll all through the night...", "0_12_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-nine post meridiem</span> when she came home and found Brissenden asleep in the chair by the window. She was tempted to change her clothes and go somewhere, anywhere, and lose the rest of the day. But she had a radio rehearsal at two so she undressed in the bathroom, carrying her things into the bedroom with her. She got under the bed covers quickly before any of the cats jumped on them; soon she was asleep and dreaming. She was in a garden with statuary, all white, and led by a marble girl and boy, among things like lilies as tall as trees, as wide as houses. There were birds there, and flowers singing, and a golden man appeared and embraced her. From his face beams of light shot in all directions, illuminating Earth and the Heavens, so that Kathy could actually see them. The only thought in her mind was that this was indeed what she had wondered about \u2014 this was what it was like to be kissed by the Sun. Then a stone arm held her and a stone hand pressed her back, and the light was too much and she woke, to hear her radio rehearsing downstairs. It really was remarkable, she thought, getting out of bed in her blue housecoat and slippers, that she could get so excited...", "2_4_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-four in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, the good sport, had arrived on the dot.\n\"Come in, Mr. $SURNAME1,\" called Eve, standing just inside the front door. She was wearing a superb cocktail dress, near-transparent, modern. \"I haven\u2019t much time.\"\n\"Sorry to intrude, madam,\" said $SURNAME1, correctly, \"but it\u2019s customary for the husband to come to the wedding\u2014especially when the wife is about to be shipped to the Amazon jungle.\"\nThe greeting lacked a certain courtesy and enthusiasm, but it was at least polite, and there was a rather boyish charm about the unshaven face and the hung-over eyes.\n\"You timed your arrival perfectly,\" said Eve. \"He should be here any minute.\" She extended a beautiful leg, indicating the staircase. \"The ceremony will be held upstairs.\" She paused. \"I must say, you don\u2019t look very much like the quarry of all our chasing.\"\n\"Is that what I am?\" said $SURNAME1, putting a cigarette in his mouth.\nEve took the pack of cigarettes from him, lit one, and gave the pack back. \"What did you think you were?\" she asked.", "2_23_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The conspiracy was now twenty-two minutes old.\nNo one knew of it but the chauffeur, and no one would know of it for many hours.\nThere was a possibility that the cook might find out about it by one o\u2019clock in the morning. This was a fifty-fifty\u2014or 33-33-33\u2014proposition.\nThe cook was the only member of the household staff who lived in. The cook lived in a room adjacent to the kitchen. He was not in his room at the moment, but was crouched low in the back of the garden. He was very drunk. He had argued with his girl friend. His girl friend had been brought home by the butler, and the cook had thrown an empty rum bottle at the butler. The butler had prevailed, and the cook had been dismissed summarily. He was drunker than an Irish bishop on Friday morning.\nThe cook had a radio, in his room, and he promised himself that he would lock himself in and listen to every bulletin of news all night long. There probably would be something about it in the morning papers, anyway.\nThe cook hated the Old Man\u2019s guts, anyway.", "1_1_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-five in the morning</span>. I was stiff and cold; my clothes felt like cardboard. The mouse scampered over the breakfast counter and over to the stove, where he investigated for a moment. Then he dashed for the hole. There was a sound of scurrying footsteps outside, and the sound of the Mojave wind.\nI went to bed with the wind.\nThe third day was a Sunday, and I slept late. When I finally got up everything seemed baked dry and I had a tremendous thirst. I went out to see Bull Smith and see what I could borrow from him. He was in the Twenty-fourth Street room.\n\u201cLong time no see,\u201d he said. He was dressing in shirt and pants with an around-the-waist sweater.\n\u201cI had to get some sleep. Things got out of hand last night.\u201d\n\u201cSo I heard. Where'd you sleep?\u201d\n\u201cI grabbed a five-minute shower and stretched out on the top of your stove.\u201d\n\u201cHuh?\u201d\n\u201cYour stove. It was the only warm place in the house.\u201d\n\u201cWhat time was that?\u201d\n\u201cDawn.\u201d", "1_2_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-seven in the morning</span> when I left my attorney\u2019s house\u2014with no plan, no Lucy and no mescaline. As I drove slowly through the darkness toward the Strip and some sort of sanctuary, I began to feel the first twinges of a two\u2014day depression that would later cause my attorney to ask me, while holding my head in the toilet, if I\u2019d like to be transferred to a private suite deep in the hospital, far away from the sounds and smells of the alcoholics, where I could receive adequate drug therapy.\nMy answer was no. I had a very important phone call to make. I needed to talk to Sam, a friend of mine in New York City. He would understand everything. I didn\u2019t dare use the car phone, so I stopped for gas at a Standard station on the corner of Moon and Las Vegas Boulevard. A man in a red tank-top undershirt was working on the air machine when I stepped up to the little counter.\n\u201cRoyal Red,\u201d I said. \u201cDo you have it in a can?\u201d\n\u201cYep. That\u2019ll be one ninety-nine.\u201d I gave him a five and waited for my change.\n\u201cThis is...", "0_12_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes past noon</span>. The motorcycle gang had arrived, complete with woman and small child. I watched as the woman sent her son off to play somewhere inside the Okie Casino, and then she herself went out to meet the gang. They were all wearing vivid pinks and oranges and alligator shoes. I knew what was coming, and then I saw it: three huge fistfuls for the tiny, waiting hand. She stuffed them somehow into the already bursting pockets of her skirt. Bicycles appeared and the gang took off, down the road and out of sight. She walked inside. Her assignment had been completed.\nI began to think of myself as a great man. I had at last found my element\u2014the pulpwood trade. I had graduated from The Joint and PA. My mind was back in shape. The cop had destroyed it, but now it was doing splendidly again. I was doing my own tax audit and thinking exclusively in terms of deductible butane and miles covered \u201con the road.\u201d I even began to click the clicker the way the Big Spenders did, and it became second nature. I spent a solid hour doing the income tax for a soul brother who made five hundred dollars and fourteen cents typing half-pages for a White Frame...", "1_0_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after midnight</span>. In less than forty seconds, the Synchronized Universe would be on us. Much too fast for us to run. Much too fast for us to establish our innocence. Much too fast for us even to think. At the moment of detonation, I had no idea what we were doing in this particular place, at this particular time. I am forced to believe, in retrospect, that we were drawn here, by forces over which we had no control, that we are all creatures of Fate, pawns of destiny\u2014at the end, as at the beginning of the human race.\nThe Earth gaped open with an ear\u2014splitting roar, and there It stood before us: a god in golden armor that glowed in the night. As It moved toward us, It kept Its sights on us\u2014on me.\nOn me.\nI froze, unable to move. $SURNAME0 quivered\u2014not with fear, but only with excitement. He was keyed up like a man about to fly his own V\u20142 into the stratosphere\nThe glowing gold figure stopped. It pulled a color\u2014coded signal from Its tunic, flicked at It with one finger. The signal whistled in the wind\u2014 sending ribbons of color back...", "1_14_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of December 24, 1954.\nThe sound was that of somnambulistic life in a stone building.\nThe building was the luxurious home of memories, not a part of the City of Newport, that summer resort firmly on the ground.\nThe home of the memories of a charming but irresponsible family was carried on the ocean floor\u2014 twelve stories of it.\nThe access ramps were within the ribcage of a skeleton made of steel girders and concrete. Its eyes were two pools of light in the disused cistern on the third floor of what had been the chief amusement building. One arm jutted out to sea; the hand was an empty submarine base of the United States Navy.\nThe home of the Dexter family\u2014 for recollections are as possessive as a dog with a bone\u2014 was a Monopoly game on a grand scale. For protection against scoundrels, it had cast-iron tickets, brass Manhattans, each a snapshot in nostalgia.\nAbout the board were scattered the deeds to resorts and hotels on the Atlantic coast.\nThe two electric trains were taxicabs that had once carried tourists to Newport from the station in the city. Each had the freedom of the particular corner of the board that it ran...", "1_17_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-one in the afternoon</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 appeared in the Solar Studios of television in a one-inch picture only one thirty-six-hundredth of an inch wide.\nShe was to sit at a console with Mr. Rawson, the announcer, who would tell her in advance what was coming.\nShe was to be utterly serious, even when pictures of Mars appeared\u2014even when there was seemingly no reason to be serious.\n$SURNAME0 telephoned in, was patched through to her light-heartedly by an ABC official who knew nothing of the strange mythology under way.\n\"Hello. $FIRSTNAMEF0?\" said the voice from the Solar System.\n\"Hello?\" said she.\n\"This is Skip,\" said the voice gently.\n\"Oh\u2014hello, Mr. $SURNAME0,\" she said.\n\"This is a dry run for you on Mars day, isn\u2019t it, $FIRSTNAMEF0?\" said the voice from the Solar System. \"Mind if I call you $FIRSTNAMEF0?\"\n\"Not at all,\" she said.\n\"Thank you, $FIRSTNAMEF0. I\u2019ve had enough practice to be reasonably sure now that I\u2019ll be able to pull it off when the real thing comes,\"...", "0_8_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-seven in the morning</span> of November twenty-first, nineteen eighty-four.\n\"In the name of The Space Merchants of Titan and Beacon, Limited, the Board of The Corporation calls this annual meeting of The Stockholders of The Corporation to order,\" said Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Graw me up,\" said K. D. L. Bontoften, the President of the Corporation, to his wife. She drew him up out of his chair, and he came as erect as he had ever been.\n\"I call this annual meeting to order,\" he said.\n\"Graw me up,\" said Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1. Her attorney, a big, brown-skinned boy named Snalley, yanked her up.\n\"The first business in order is the report of the President and the Board of so-called Directors. The Secretary will read the minutes of the last meeting.\"\n\"The last meeting was held on March twenty-first of this year, in room 5208 of the Aero-Marine penthouse down an alley off Telegraph in Oakland, California. Those present were K. D. L. Bontoften, Elmer Derring, Miss $FIRSTNAMEF1 Wingfield, Dr. Theodore H. McNamarra, Mr. Franz Pfeiffer, and Mr.", "0_4_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time.\nThe old-style term was used by common consent of the human beings on Earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Mars was the first of the heavenly creatures to hear the musketry and the shouting. He was awakened by the sounds.\n$SURNAME1 spent no more time wondering what the noises meant. He slept the sleep of utter fatigue\u2014a fatigue that could have explained his staggering behavior on Earth and his stumbling behavior on Mars.\nHis last thought before dozing off again was that he had much work to do in the morning. Malachis Un careers, Unlimited, offered promotional opportunities to budding monopolists. And Un, whose orders were formulated by $SURNAME0 and dispensed by Salo, was also a monopolist\u2014had been since the year of $SURNAME1\u2019s reign as Emperor of San Francisco.\nUn\u2019s monopolies were written for Salo by $SURNAME0.\nUn and $SURNAME1 lived poorly, but lived well enough, by selling these monopolies to one another\u2014five cents on the dollar, ten cents on the dollar, forty cents on the dollar.\nThere were tens of thousands of them.\n$SURNAME1 would name a business something or other, and Un would sell him the business for ten...", "1_1_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-seven in the morning</span>, October 4, 1985, when the Mint Gun Club found itself, (none too soon for their reputations, and reputations in Oklahoma are of some significance,) in the position of repossessing their large, raw, barrackslike building, along with huge chunks of Cyclopean masonry that were clearly part of the structure\u2019s foundations. That the rear entrance to the building led, not only into the lobby of the Bombardini Building, but also directly into the lavatories of the partners\u2019 suite was, of course, a much-laughed-about, or laughed-about concept at the firm\u2019s Christmas parties. At Christmastime, having to think up a firm joke that your buddies weren\u2019t already tired of was as bad as getting a bad Christmas present from your sweetie.\nLenore\u2019s eye wandered around the lobby, over pink marble, until it settled for a time on the huge, brilliant white only slightly scuffed diamond that was the frequent bar of Lenore\u2019s shining black dress shoes on the pink marble. The shoes moved from the diamond to tug tentatively at an enormous piece of the building\u2019s foundation. Lenore\u2019s eye wandered back over the unreal lobby,...", "0_2_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two eighteen in the morning</span>, and there was nobody behind the bar except the bartender, who was reading a motorcycle trade journal. \u201cIs the man in the back?\u201d I asked. \u201cYes,\u201d he said. \u201cPlease go back there.\u201d I wondered what kind of crap game you could run in a place like this. The furniture was all card\u2014game tables and stools. No bar and no food. Just a lot of lonely drifters in from the desert, drinkin rum and cokes and waitin for a chance to make some money with their poker hands.\nIn the back room the man was sitting at a big feltpe streaked table surrounded by his outfit , which seemed to be made up of n ins and women in their late thirties, most any one of them big enough to bounce me all over the back room with one kick. I took a chair away from the table. One of the women seemed to be in charge.\n\u201cThis is our ship's doctor,\u201d the man said to her. \u201cFix him a drink and bring it over here.\" Then to me: \u201cWhat the fuck are you doing here, Doctor? We don't need you here.\nThis is the first...", "0_0_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-four a.m.</span>\nThe trumpets fell silent.\nAs the faithful stood silently, their faces in the moonlight, their breath smoking faintly the air, thin spirals of smoke made by the cigarettes, pipes, and cigars they had lit at the warning of the trumpets.\nOnly the tinkle of a basin somewhere, and the slap of water in someone\u2019s hand, and a light snoring, told that these manifestations were not in another, some supernal, some far other world.\nSuddenly, at the other end of the field, near the walls of the shrines, by the entrances to the arbors, by the tents, and in the church itself, the lights went on. An electric switching-on, expressing distribution everywhere and instant activity.\nThe religious figures of the legends, the plaster saints and martyrs, the plastic angels, came brightly to life. The stories unfolded. Gethsemane and Calvary appeared. Nicodemus and the Good Thief wrestled with the Soldiers at the Cross. Jesus appeared in the clouds over Jerusalem after the Resurrection. The Washing of the Disciples\u2019 Feet was enacted. So was the Last Supper. So was Mary Magdalene\u2019s repentance. The transfig...", "0_17_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when I stepped out of Pedro's taxicab. I carried two suitcases and wore new silver-gray sharkskin suits, a fresh yellow shirt, a dark blue figured tie, black shoes and my new cream-padded Reefers that I haven't even worn yet. I was smoking a brand-new, hundred-dollar French cigarette that I bought in a 24-hour drugstore as I came into town. I was all packed and ready to drive to the airport and fly to San Francisco and then Chicago and then Idlewild and then not knowing.\nWho could know now? I stood in the sun, looking around the square again that I knew so well. It was different, but the same. I remembered every statue. I looked up at the fa\u00e7ade of the university. Even the guajira was still riding the stone spinal column of Ferdinand the Catholic. Everything was the same, except Mexico had a president now. He came in a heaping avalanche of votes, riding on the same wave that gave everybody represented in the Square a tremendous new mandate for Change.\nEverybody represented in the Square was from Change. It was All in the Square. The cynics called it a \u201ccoalition.\u201d The damned fools didn't know what...", "1_11_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven sixteen in the morning</span>, I was almost a whole hour behind my schedule, and there was no way in hell I was going to make it to Monterey in time to catch my plane back to San Francisco. Or even close to Monterey. I decided to leave the car down in the gulch and escape up a side gulch the map showed, on Highway 49, into a little town called . . . . . . . .ah yes, Rough Acres. Well, I'd have to re-route, lose time, and get off on this wild route involving several small highways and a badbyway called...Furnace Creek Yucca Terrace. Which struck me as a dubious enterprise at best, but I resolved to do it anyway and get off I did...I did in fact accomplish the thing in crippling grand style, a textbook lesson in what not to do when lost and confused behind the wheel of an overloaded car on a hot desert highway. And seventy-five bucks and five hours later, long after midnight, I climbed wearily up the porch of an all-night gas station in Garberville and asked the old man in there where I could get a bus to Rough Acres. His reply was soft, slow and bewildered: \u201cSon...no...", "0_6_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME0 materialized his ship on the clear waters of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Sea. A minute hand on one of the twelfth-century Swedish clocks would have denoted the position of the Inaccessible Pilgrim.\nThe ship was a beautiful sight\u2014a fifty-foot ketch, white, with gold scroll work.\n$SURNAME0 was at the tiller. He did not gape or point at the works of man, as most sightseers gape and point. He was amused, and sometimes he chuckled softly.\nApparently he was having pleasant memories of times past.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 stood by the cabin with her husband, looking out across the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Sea at one of Mars\u2019s space-shooting canals just one mile straight up.\nThe canal was very narrow, only six feet across. A Space Wanderer was prodding the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Sea with a pole, hoping to strike the canal, hoping to act as a ferry to $SURNAME0 and his wife.\nThe place where $SURNAME0 materialized his ship was four fathoms deep. The bottom was sand mixed with white limestone concretions as big as a loaf of bread. The bottom was smeared with a rainbow of oil paints from the bottom of the...", "0_14_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. A long, lean, black-faced figure, walking fast, followed by a short, grey-faced, black-haired figure, with a box and a tea-basket, and by a party of \"natives,\" as the short, grey, black-haired figure called them, crossed the upper quadrangle. The matronly woman, who called herself Mrs Ramsay and pronounced Rae so that it sounded like a word in itself, loomed through a pannier-full of flowers, on her way to fill the desks with chrysanthemums for the f\u00eate. It was f\u00eate-day. The little boy, Luke, Robin, Rash, who kept on asking questions, would stare with a stoned, supernatural stare, paralysed into immobility, they said, by his excitement, his happiness, his triumph in being five, his first f\u00eate-day, as his father carried him across the quadrangle to be dipped at the chapel door by old Mr Carmichael in the surplice which he wore for chapel only. As for Andrew, who had cut his finger, could not \"pray properly,\" and was therefore laid, for God's sake, in the basket with one of William's...", "2_18_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty in the evening</span>, and John D. Rockerduck thought that Pierce Inverarity had died in the service of his country.\nCaptain Nemo, commanding the January 23rd Commando of the United States Army, thought that he was about to lead his three hundred men into the North Vietnamese hamlet of My Canh.\nBruer D. Payne, President of the United States, thought that he was now ready to give to the shiftless of America what he called his New Freedom.\nBonnie Parker thought that she was (a) a fool and a brute, (b) bloody but unbowed, and (c) on her way to room 214 of the Burkett Hotel in south Winnemucca, Nevada.\nMax Scardino, Lester Colt, and Stony Stevenson thought they had lammed out of hundreds of situations just like this one.\nSyntax Beef was napping in the hammock.", "2_10_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after ten o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich mean time.\nBit by bit, the Space Wanderer learned how to use controls on the pilot\u2019s panel. He used them to turn the space ship around and head for the ladder in the western wall of the building that he had been told was the ground floor of the Tower of Rumble. The ladder rose from the ground to the middle of the tower.\nIt was four feet wide, but it fanned out as it rose, becoming four ladders, sixteen feet apart.\nThe ladders were supposed to be easy to climb.\n\"If you can climb a ladder on Earth,\" $SURNAME0 had said, \"you can ride those ladders to the top of the Tower of $SURNAME0.\" And now the Space Wanderer hit a switch on the pilot\u2019s panel, and a light grew brilliantly in the western wall of the building, and the ladder indeed proved itself to be as easy to climb as a ladder on Earth.", "1_4_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThere was dark before the world.\nThere was dark within the room.\nThere was only the warm glow of the space heater, the brightness of the burning logs in the fireplace, and the flickering incandescence in $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s eye.\nThe incandescence in $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s good eye was newly aswirl with yellow light. It was a sign that $FIRSTNAMEB1 was worried\u2014even more than usual.\nThe new worry was not without a certain element of comedy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had a big problem. A big physical problem.\nThe problem was that his own head was far larger than the standard neck-hole in his uniform cap.\nWhat $FIRSTNAMEB1 needed to wear on his head was a captain\u2019s hat, a garrison cap. But given the state of affairs at $SURNAME1 Husband Army Base, there was no supply of garrison caps. None whatever.\nAlighting on the sofa\u2019s arm near $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s elbow were two plain brown bats. The creatures were real. They were friends of Dorothy. Their names were Henry and Henrietta. Dorothy was teaching them to smoke cigarettes. They were smoking Chesterfields. They sucked the cigarettes eagerly, keeping the burning tobacco away from their leather...", "2_23_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-seven in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME1 felt he had all the time in the world.\n\"How much iron do you suppose there is in iron phosphate stones, Mr. $SURNAME1?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. She had dropped the formality of calling him Mr. $SURNAME0 or $FIRSTNAMEM1. \"You have a geology background\u2014I wasn\u2019t kidding you when I said that to Limbo.\"\n$SURNAME1 shrugged. \"I\u2019d have to look it up, Mrs. $SURNAME1.\"\n\"Do\u2014please,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I\u2019d be interested to know.\" She reached up and took the spectacles off her nose. She wiped the lenses on her pleated blue skirt. \"I used to be a geology student, you know.\"\n\"You did?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I was a history major at the Sorbonne,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I wasn\u2019t much good at that, but I was good at finding things under rocks.\"", "0_5_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five thirty-four in the morning</span> on December 2, 1992. People in the lobby of the hotel were somberly dealing with assorted legal pals at the elevators. The girl at the front desk, legally called the switchboard, but known among the habitues of the lobby as the Little One, was lost in a book called The Spy Who Loved Me, when my attorney and I arrived from the Hell\u2019s Angels\u2019 suite. My attorney had donned a business suit, and I was wearing my only suit\u2014a black Armani\u2014along with a bright red Day-Glo Flyers windbreaker that I\u2019d borrowed from my attorney just before the summit meeting. We were a fine impression of a Bogart\u2014LB and Sam Spade, maybe, or a Sam and Dave, if we\u2019d only had white hair and were wearing Sam Browne belts.\nThe lights of the Polo Lounge remained on, but nobody was eating. The bartender looked at us, but did not greet us with his habitual \u201cGood morning, gentlemen.\u201d We were no longer welcome at the Polo Lounge. Ostracism was looming. The Little One did not look up from her book as I used a Marriott Hundred\u2014Dollar bill to cover my bar bill for...", "2_21_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-six in the evening</span>.\n\u201cParticularly with Adolf Tinis threatening to burn down the building because his cable got hooked up to the Dial-a-Dumb Dial switch.\u201d\n\u201cJesus Christ.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cSo what did she do, last time you saw her?\u201d\n\u201cGive me another taste of that.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\n\u201cBlow me.\u201d\n\u201c ....\u201d\n/a/\nPARTIAL TRANSCRIPT OF \u201cTHE PHIL DONAHUE SHOW,\u201d TELECAST 29 AUGUST 1990 PARTIAL TRANSCRIPT OF \u201cTHE PHIL DONAHUE SHOW,\u201d TELECAST 29 AUGUST 1990.\nPHIL DONAHUE: So you\u2019re saying the drink and the food were insufficiently nourishing?\nBERNARD GERBER: Well so to speak, I guess, yes.\nP.D.: But nourishing in what way?\nB.G.: Are we talking about the food and the drink in the abstract, or about the body\u2019s food and drink, here?", "0_17_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span> and the fiesta was beginning. There was a great crowd in the square, gathering around a platform on one side. Men on the committee snatched at passing bystanders and shouted in their ears. \u201cJoin on the committee! Join on the committee! For the fiesta!\u201d They knocked $FIRSTNAMEB5 and myself into a group that was forming. There was no doubt about it. We were both taken for members of the committee. \u201cWhere is Romero?\u201d the man who had knocked us in asked.\n\u201cHe is up at Romero\u2019s house.\u201d\n\u201cTell him we are going to start the cession.\u201d\n\u201cWhat\u2019s that?\u201d\n\u201cTell him. Tell him we are going to start in five minutes.\u201d\n\u201cWhat\u2019s a cession?\u201d I asked $FIRSTNAMEB5. \u201cIt\u2019s something to eat,\u201d she said. \u201cGod! I\u2019m hungry.\u201d\n\u201cEat! Eat!\u201d said the man. \u201cYou will get your chance later.\u201d\nHe moved off to the platform. The band had quit playing and the drum was rolling, the horses beginning to tro...", "2_19_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nCheering broke out outside.\nAfter a long time, hardly hearing what he was saying, the lieutenant said, \"God, how I love cricket.\"\n\"Yow!\" said the Scot.\nThe cheers continued. They were cheering something very important in the match that was being played in the stadium.\n\"Yow!\" said the Scot again.\n\"You\u2019re sick, pal,\" said the lieutenant.\n\"Aye,\" said the Scot.\nCheering.\nStill cheering.\nThe cheering died down.\nThe cheering had been for a six that had been made by St. John\u2019s Wood against Surrey.\nCheering began again. Another six had been made by St. John\u2019s Wood against Surrey.\nFrench horns laughed.\nA very pretty soprano sang.\n\"What I\u2019d like,\" said the Scot, \"is a ticket to this for me and the wife, and an orange and some white beer. What I\u2019d like, to tell the truth, is for the next match to be TOMORROW.\" He didn\u2019t like cricket any more.", "1_1_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-three in the morning</span>. Last flight west was ours. The girl at the Welcome Table was just another aging doll with a badge and a job...but she did let us sign out for the car without complaint, and she even gave us our claim ticket for the flight. \u201cThere are no Baggage Men in the morning, so bring your own things to the gate,\u201d she told us with a yawn. \u201cThis flight is nonsmoking, but the plane at El Paso is smoking\u2014if you want it.\u201d No thanks, I thought. I only smoke hash and I don't want to hang around some El Paso belly-land with a bag full of lethal herbs. Not with a bull's-eye on my back. I'd be eating Sheriff's rubber before they could radio up to Las Vegas to put a watch on all Customs gates. No thanks, I'd leave the Pontiac in the terminal parking lot, near the red and white stripes of the terminal brigade. We could get it tomorrow. We could always find a cop to say we had the keys...right? For Christ\u2019s sake, we were underground. There are very few symbols on this earth that mean anything to the Drug Culture: The Shotgun, The Coke Bottle, and The Red...", "0_19_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>., The ship had been going at the rate of a hundred and ninety-two miles an hour.\nHe looked at his watch... and discovered that he was hungry.\nChapter\nMajor General Earl C. Spooner, commanding the 118th Space-Wing of the International Space Force, stood at attention in front of his desk, staring at a painting on the wall of a flying soldier carrying a dead comrade on his back and trudging stoically through the rain.\nThe dead comrade had a very happy, peaceful, saintly yellow face, with closed eyes, drooping jowls, and with rain dripping off his chin whiskers onto the yellow lapel of his space-suit. He was, of The March into Winter, by Walter Teahingle.\nThere were four holes in The March into Winter, made by the major general\u2019s diamond-studded right hand. The major general loved that painting, and he could not understand how the rest of his staff could be so blase about it. He felt that any man who had holes in his hand from touching The March into Winter was a good man and had proved himself, no matter what else he did or failed to do.\nWindrip had loved The March into Winter...", "0_10_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-seven in the morning</span> and I had a hell of a lot of things to do before I went out to Vegas that night. I ran around the room with my shirt off, bitching to myself in panic. The time had come, there was no way around it; I was finally ready to understand this last and most brutal mystery of the Rif.\n\u201cWhat do you mean, Nolden?\u201d\n\u201cMr. Thompson, I mean there\u2019s nothing you can do, absolutely nothing, you might as well face it. I\u2019m a ghost; I\u2019m bloody well dead, more than dead. I died on the plane while they were sitting there playing cards.\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d I said. \u201cWhat force on earth could make two such men play poker on a Sunday morning? Nothing a man could do would make them stop. It\u2019s a ghost, right out of the Bible.\u201d I covered the mirror on the closet door and frantically tried to pack my suitcase. \u201cThere\u2019s no escape for me,\u201d poor Nolden moaned. \u201cI can feel it moving in me: rigor mortis...they used me and then they destroyed me,...", "0_8_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-three in the morning</span> in Hong Kong and nine eleven-thirty in the evening in Europe\u2014but at the Chicago headquarters of Mr. Montague S. C. Cudahy of the Cudahy Company there was plenty of activity. In a feature story. Time magazine had recently written of Monty C. as being almost as rich as old man Rockefeller and considerably jollier.\nExcept for one thing. Monty Cudahy had nothing to do.\nOnce, his day had been planned to the minute. At 7:08 A.M. he hitched his braces and went to the office. At 5:47 P.M., the clerks escaped to the nearest bars.\nHe had amassed a fortune in the real estate, packing house, and food-processing businesses\u2014and it did not bother him a bit. Except for meetings of the National Citizens\u2019 Committee for Bertrand Russell, he spent his days at the Polo Club, squinting at the polo ponies.\nIt was more than a hobby with Mr. Cudahy. True, he had never kicked a ball in his life. But that did not bother him a bit.\nSince childhood, his fondest dream was to own a polo team. And now...", "2_5_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past five o'clock in the morning</span>. Outside, the sky was getting light. A new day was dawning on the western shores of America.\n\u201cYou dirty bastard! You were after that young thing; well take her, goddamn you! she don't want to see you again, not anybody like you! you rotten old man!\"\nThe ghost of Earl Roam was still sitting inside me and it couldn't understand what had happened. It said: \u201cEh? What? What's all this? What's going on here?\u201d and I answered: \u201cWhat does it look like? Ain't nobody goin nowhere. We're all here to stay. Dig it.\u201d And the ghost of the $FIRSTNAMEB3 rolled over in its grave and sighed, \u201cYes, that's right; we're all here to stay,\u201d and the ghost of my hipster cousin, who always talked about \u201cassociations,\u201d raised his head from off the sidewalk of the ghost street and said, \u201cNow it's time to get serious, really dig hard; go sit in a corner, go hide behind a lamp post and come out only to get drunk, because that's all there's left to do.\u201d", "0_0_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after midnight</span>. Redwine had scribbled something on his yellow letter blank, dropped his fountain pen and composed himself for sleep. Unkl Herb and $FIRSTNAMEB1, after exchanging a few words with the Space Wanderer, had retired to their shells.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had offered the Space Wanderer a name for his harmonium, but the Space Wanderer had politely declined. The harmonium\u2019s own name was enough for anyone.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" the harmonium now called the sleeping $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not hear.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0,\" called the harmonium again. \"$FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 slept on.\nA minute passed.\n\"Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka, Unka,\" said the harmonium in the calm, commanding voice that a watch commander on Earth would use in stirring the drowsy members of his crew...", "2_2_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-nine in the morning</span>.\n/d/\n\u201cWhat I\u2019m saying here is that that\u2019s him.\u201d\n\u201cWhat.\u201d\n\u201cYou know that\u2019s him, Stoney. That\u2019s the one we\u2019ve been waiting for.\u201d\n\u201cIt sure looks like him, all right.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean \u2018looks like\u2019 him? That\u2019s Andrew Sealander Lang.\u201d\n\u201cSo what do we do?\u201d\n\u201cWhat d\u2019you think?\u201d\n\u201cBut Nicky says he-was\n\u201cWe\u2019re not going to just let him go skipping over to the Kaspbrak like that, Lenore. Period all.\u201d\n\u201cI guess not.\u201d\n\u201c.... \u201d\n\u201cBut how can we, really? Every second he\u2019s out there he\u2019s getting farther and farther away from us. We may never see him again. Wherever he goes he\u2019ll be completely inaccessible. He\u2018ll be totally inaccessible.\u201d", "1_18_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-nine in the evening</span> when I reached my cottage and found a strange car parked by the door. I noticed that first, and I\u2019m not sure I noticed any other detail of the place until I stepped inside and saw the two men sitting at the table.\nThey were both wearing khaki bush jackets and open shirt collars, and they made an odd contrast, one of them tall, blond, and sullen, a heavy-jawed man of perhaps thirty-five, the other short and not more than twenty-five and lightly tanned, his hair light, long, and fine, his suit a light grey jacket and pants and a white shirt, with a green tie and yellow scarf. They had the same kind of graceful bush kit as the hunters I\u2019d seen on the way over. But instead of game bags and shotguns, these carried papers and fountain pens.\nI heard the old familiar voice behind me and turned to smile and nod hello: \u201cHello, darling,\u201d I said. \u201cWho are the -- \u201d\n\u201cNot now,\u201d she said. \u201cThese are the forestry department. These are officers from Jack Salmon\u2019s office.\u201d The girl from the coffee shop smiled muzzily but...", "1_1_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one seventeen in the morning</span> when the field secretary of the Freeman party telephoned this report to his headquarters in Chicago:\nWait until you hear this one. Apparently a real fanatic. I think we\u2019re going to liquidate this bad boy right away.\nTHE STATEMENT OF MR. SALVADOR DANGERFIELD, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR OF THE AMERICAN COVENANT ASSOCIATION\nRecorded by Colnet N. Goldman of Columbia Graphophone.\nAs I understand it, you are here this morning to tell us of your organization\u2019s reaction to the activities of the so\u2014called Unkbermanlike Party. I suppose it is fair to say that the activities of that party, when viewed in the context of our national life today, are about as frightening as anything could be. Could you comment on that?\n$FIRSTNAMEB0, maybe you ought to leave this one to me.\nNo, it\u2019s OK now\u2014I got everything I need this morning. I don\u2019t think it will take but an hour or so.\nYou see, people in general don\u2019t realize how fast things are moving now. Things were moving fast before, but most people didn\u2019t realize it. This business...", "1_7_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span> of a day in October, and the place was the flat land at the head of the Gulf of Paria in the Republic of Trinidad. It was a small piece of real estate, that part of the Gulf. There were some yellow weeds, a few clumps of mangrove trees, and Paria Island, which was at the north edge of the Bay of Venezuela. The island was inhabited only by birds and lizards, and had been put there by some geological jest.\nThe Gulf of Paria was a cut-off from the great Orinoco River, whose waters fed the falls at the head of the Amazon. The Gulf of Paria was six miles wide, and then there was only the 360-foot-wide strait separating Trinidad from the mainland of Venezuela. The strait was called the Dragon\u2019s Mouth, for what reason no one knew. There was a big fee for passing through there, and on the west side of it was the Venezuelan custom-house and the town of El Dorado, the town nearest the strait on the Venezuelan shore. El Dorado was only two miles from the port of Trinidad. In nine hours by steamer it was possible to go from the head of the Gulf of Paria to El...", "2_18_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-eight in the evening</span>.\n\"Mr. $FIRSTNAMEM2,\" said $SURNAME0 genially, as he came to the head of the line, \"we meet again.\"\n\"We do indeed, Mr. $SURNAME0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. She had called $SURNAME0 by this nickname four times in her life. The first had been when she had found out that $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were one and the same.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s history as a civilaturation agent had begun in space. It had begun at a place in space which Earthlings had designated co-ordinates X-33. At X-33 the space ship Challenger had touched Earthling space for a matter of minutes. It had touched Earthling space at a speed typical of Solar System mankind in the age in which $SURNAME0 lived. The duration of such a flight, from world to world and back again to Solar System III, taking up and dropping off passengers and then returning directly home, was approximately twelve Earthling days.", "1_14_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty in the afternoon</span>. By three thirty, or a little before, Great Jones Street rose out of the river bank into sight and we turned off Fordham Road at a gas station and went down a long hill. It wasn't so much a street as a long, narrow cleared space between two tremendous warehouse buildings to be used by truckers and long-distance hauls. We passed lumberyards and saw how huge the timbers were and that it takes a dozen men to carry one. When they spliced one huge beam with another they measured with a steel tape stretched tight. A bar on which they hung the hook happened to be just outside my window, and I thought of knocking it off, and of running away, rural-route style. But $FIRSTNAMEB3, who was at the wheel and never took his eyes off the road, never even noticed, he was so busy with life that rushed by. The car bucked, weaved, made curves and straightaways unknown to Hoboken and we almost ran over somebody relaxing in his door, and he jumped back and looked, rubbing his belly. I stuck my head out the window and yelled at him, \u201cExcuse us, we're trying to go!\u201d In a few minutes we were lost. Looking for, or not looking...", "0_1_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span> of September thirteenth, 1955.\nAt precisely five past one, a keen-eyed watcher\u2014or perhaps two keen-eyed watchers\u2014might have caught a glimpse of what appeared to be a bat, falling headlong through the starry firmament of the night.\nBut this was in the old, unenlightened days\u2014before the real nature of things clouded men\u2019s eyes, and turned them inward, to their pity and their terror.\nWithout telescopes, without the awesome bulwark of the Cyrlinic Empire behind them, the chances are that these pairs of observers would have been quite unable to make out the truth of what they saw\u2014any more than they could have made out the truth behind the smoke and the laughter of Steiner\u2019s bookshop. Why should Fate play a trick like that? It was only half past nine in the evening, still broad daylight, and this Martian happened by chance to be wearing a dark coat and a white slouch hat.\nHis eyes were closed, apparently in sleep.\nHe was asleep.\nThere were more flowers on his parachute\u2014on the sill of his open bedroom window. They had been there since that morning.\nMrs. $SURNAME0 and her husband had...", "2_16_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-six in the afternoon</span>.\nThe sun shone brightly on the great open fields of Greenland-Iceland-Norway.\nRum Rumstead stood in the shadow of his two airplanes. He could not see Corpus Christi, Texas, because of the curve of the Earth.\nEARLIER THAT DAY, MONA LARRY MCGILL had had a foreboding that something terrible and strange was about to happen on the afternoon of 4 July. The thought of Mona and her husband, von Rumstedt-Hoglund\u2014who was the Minister of Pleasure in the government of the German-occupied United States\u2014having a breezy, small-hours phone conversation through their duplex wall was an inspired stage device that did not ring true at all in the drama of the moment. False. inappropriate. I took it out, and put back in an eerie little passage in which Mona was deciding whether to go back to sleep for another hour\u2014or lose a whole hour that she was accustomed to spending in the warmth and comfort of sleep\u2014and have breakfast in a drugstore someplace.", "2_3_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span> in Skra Devenport.\nArthur Crabtree did public relations work for a diaper service in Des Moines. His wife, Sylvia, was a member of the Dorcas Society at the local Methodist Church.\nThe Cranleys lived in a big stone house out on Sage Road.\nCrabtree and Warren lived in an apartment building on Carolina Road.\nThe Crabtrees and the Warrens had once been good friends.\nTime had broken them apart.\nTime had made the Cranleys rich. Time had made the Warrens penurious. Time had broken them apart forever.\nTime had brought a gap in status, a gap that nothing under Heaven could close.\nMrs. Noble Warren was fifty-five years old. Mr. Vance Warren was sixty.\nThey both hated their only son.\nOf all their possessions, Vance and Noble hated their only son most.\nTime had brought a difference in status between their one son and themselves: a difference so wide, so deep, and so dark that no power on Earth could ever bridge it.\nTime had brought a difference between their one son and all other men under Heaven: a difference so wide, so deep, and so dark that no power on Earth could ever bridge it.", "2_4_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe bandage was over $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s left nipple. Every time he had made love since his marriage, he had made love left-naked.\nOld $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 entered the little cubicle then, wearing an old pink and blue dressing gown. He carried a can of graphite lubricant. He placed the can on a ledge near the pump.\n\"Have you been having fun working the machinery, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" he asked. \"The machinery has been having fun working you, hasn\u2019t it?\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He meant it.\n\"Anything you want to say to me, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"No, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He meant that, too, but only because he had what he thought was a foolproof system for keeping his thoughts his own, for saying only what was absolutely required.\n\"You look pretty good, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said $SURNAME0, \"considering the hell you put yourself through in the Army.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 grinned wanly. \"Thank you, sir,\" he said.\n\"You been punishing yourself?\" said $SURNAME0.", "0_23_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-two in the evening</span>.\nThere were hordes sitting on the floor in the corridors. Rumors flew crazily. The storm had knocked out the power. West Virginia was without lights. Something had happened to the air-conditioning. It was stifling in the corridors. The elevators weren't running. Two fans didn't work. There were some broken windows and the wind was whistling through these into the building. The trains had stopped running out of Washington at nine-thirty. Repairs hadn't been completed. It was announced that the buses would begin operating from the city at eight in the morning. It was hot. There were no cots. People had been sleeping all over the floor in the corridors. By ones and twos, and now by tens and twenties, the guests of the hotel were drifting out into the night. The Chamber of Commerce boys were particularly raggletaggle in the windy dark. In fact, one of the sadder spectacles of the evening was watching the poor little fellows from Connecticut and Ohio and Rhode Island discover that their expensive little clues to the American heartland were now so many lost souls in a dark, wrathsome city.\nSome of the guests decided to head on across the state line to Cumberland. By now the...", "1_19_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-five in the evening</span>. The place was the Red Star Cafe, just around the corner from that sinful sex trap, the Old Spanish Baths. The Red Star Cafe is owned by this evil degenerate named Fernando. He's a Peruvian. He has this craze about .45 pistols. He has these .45 pistols hanging all over the walls. He has pictures of himself with all these high\u2014ranking brass in this Peruvian air force he was once in. And records. The only musicians he likes are performers of similarly morbid persuasions. Peruvian performers of similarly morbid persuasions. He has these performers' autographed pictures all over the walls. He even speaks a special Peruvian language that nobody understands but him. He's a real nutter. He has a moustache like Hitler's. He sits in a booth hunched over a beer, glaring at the booths fulla flabby bums and freeloaders around him. And man, he throws hellish fits when any surfer gets out of line. He scares hell out of everybody. He throws breadsticks at you. Yes, bread sticks. He twirls em in his fingers like a baton, then reaches over and you hear this whoosh and dig the sudden pain. It was on account of...", "1_7_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>. That is to say, it was 0712. That is to say, it was 0712 and 42 seconds.\nHarflex 283 was no longer Flexible, no longer able to adapt itself to the space-time curvature of a planet such as Earth, no longer able to ignore the field of gravitation that Earth exerted, starting at a distance of 239,000 miles and increasing, in a steady progression, by 1,000 miles every second.\nAt precisely 0812, Universal Eastern Time, time in New York City, all hell broke loose.\nEnough of Church's Fried Chicken and all other fried foods had been consumed by the correct population to make all the power-stations blaze into life again. There was a sudden aggregate demand for electricity far in excess of anything the power-stations could produce, even if they had wanted to produce it.\nFor the first minute after 0812, the power-stations continued to trickle out whatever power they had left over. But slowly, surely, that trickle became nothing at all.\nEvery light in New York City went out. Every power-generator stopped dead in its track. Every electric sign went dark.\nThe thermionic and photoelectric cells, in heavily used pipes and pumps,...", "0_5_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five sixteen in the morning</span>, Earthling Central Standard Time.\nSpring.\nPART THREE\nAFTERMATH\nONE\nEARTH: 5 MONTHS LATER, SPRING, 1986 God\u2019s House Burned Down\nOn Earth, the Beame administration was rocked to its foundations by the disclosures of $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1. The President\u2019s daughter had stayed in Newport, supposedly at Stonecipheco\u2019s disposal. And now a television audience of over a hundred million was told that $SURNAME1 had given his assurances as to her safety on the basis of a letter from God himself in which God, quite obviously, referred to his son. Lady Godiva had not been seen since the letter arrived at $SURNAME1\u2019s establishment at 899 North Helena, Denver, a location having no known connection with Nitrate, Denver, home of Fern Dreer.\nMiss $SURNAME1\u2019s disappearance, at the time of her father\u2019s election, had provoked the greatest scandal of the campaign. $SURNAME1 had beaten his chest, howled at the moon, made good and acceptable suggestions as to what the President should do with himself. So also had his political opposition. $SURNAME1 had gotten himself in hand first, and had been elected on the basis, in no small part, of...", "1_20_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>, and the monorail was two hours late and two hundred miles short of its destination. Two families, one of five, the other of two, occupied the ten compartments.\nSurely the conductor would soon come through.\nNothing will so educate a person to the suffering of others as a free ride on a monorail. The passengers discovered one another, got acquainted, and spoke of their more usual means of transport. By the time the train was canceled, the people were as close as shipwrecked castaways.\nThe conductor rarely came through, for there was nothing for him to do. He judged his arrival at stations in extraordinary cases only.\nHe did not judge the Monorail Bureau accurately.\nMajor Paul M. English waited him out. He had nothing to do but wait him out, for his company owned the controlling interest in the stock of the company that owned the controlling interest in the Monorail. He had been born rich, and he did not have to do anything to stay rich, including attending stockholders\u2019 meetings.\nWhen the last passenger had died in Depot 12, Indianapolis, and the life-support systems were turned off, English would be notified by pneumatic tube. He would put on his best...", "0_17_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-four in the afternoon</span>. I had been on the road since half-past six that morning. My mind was empty. I drove on past the Tropicana, past the Club Tropicana, and as I went by I saw the croupier standing on the porch, wearing glasses, his hair combed back with grease. \u201cThe Tropicana!\u201d I cried, and waved. He didn\u2019t see me; I was going too fast. The big late-afternoon San Diego sun flared my eyes. The Royal Crown Cola sign, the soaring futuristic cash registers that marked the place where the beer trucks turn, floated in my sight like vision, mirage, a thing of magic and absolute remoteness.\nAnd then suddenly, hanging on the sky ahead, across the salt flats where the airport runways make rough stitches into heaven, appeared the Hotel Million-Air. The ultimate boom-town hotel; the tallest building in the rising city; a landmark and a beacon, the place where all who ascend congregate and meet, so that one is always among friends when behind those windows up there. And I remembered the revolving restaurant on top, and the silly mirrors that check your elevation as you rise. I remembered a Jaguar Mark IV parked in the...", "0_14_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. At three thirty, $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I arrived at Stan's shack on the outskirts of Flagstaff. It was a cold, sad shack but in the backyard there was a cellar where Stan spent most of his time meditating on potatoes ... pregnant potatoes that he dug up from their roots with his plump brown fingers. He was on a mountain and was growing highalong with his potatoes. His sweet young wife was an auburn-haired Okie girl. They were young and poor and happy; they spent most of the time making love in the front parlor in front of the fireplace. Stan kept his little truck, with rope beds and mattresses in the back, parked here, a kind of rattletrap hearse. Up in the front room he had his stove and bed. $FIRSTNAMEB3 said it actually had been an old hearse that carried \u201cdead Marines from the battles in China.\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB3 said the thought of this always cheered him up -- that had been the original purpose of the rattletrap, even if it had been altered to carry civilian potatoes at present. $FIRSTNAMEB3 rushed for the front room. Stan had had a visitor just that morning and his bed was still warm. I sat on the edge of it; $FIRSTNAMEB3 scrambled halfway into it and promptly...", "2_3_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Ladies and gentlemen,\" whispered the Space Wanderer from his place in the sky, \"ladies and gentlemen\u2014\"\n\"Hush,\" said $SURNAME0. The twinkling dust of his spiral shadow, on the dais, was as fine as powdered glass in the glare of the spotlights. \"Hush,\" he said. \"It, is rather late in the game for a newcomer to make a lot of noise. And now\u2014\" he said suddenly, \"now\u2014let us be still, and see what will become of us.\"\n\"Group Captain,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"would you care to accept a small, alcoholic beverage?\"\nShe held against her stomach the two thermos flasks.\nThe question was out of character, not only for the moment, but for all of her adult life. It was as though she, or someone animated by her spirit, had reached into a worn, ancient sack and pulled out some dazzling anachronism.\nForever and ever, the liquor under Yossarian\u2019s bed would play at being that dazzling anachronism.", "1_7_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Well, good-by, Red,\" I said. \"Now get this straight. I\u2019m going to New York, just like we agreed. And in exactly two days from now, on the morning of the eleventh, I'll meet you in the coffee shop of the Dixie- farmers' Club in Houston, Texas. That's where we agreed. I'll have your half of the money with me. You'll have the picture. What could be fairer than that?\"\n\"Ain't nobody going to git me out of this town,\" he said.\n\"We'll talk about that in New York,\" I said. \"After you and your bride enjoy a nice week-end trip through the world's biggest city, there'll be plenty of time to explain everything you want to explain.\"\nHe produced a huge wad of money and started to count out the cab fare. I didn't stop him. The meter was running as much as he wanted to pay.\n\"See you on the eleventh, Red,\" I said. \u201c8:03, precisely.\"\n\"You're crazy,\" he said.\n\"On the contrary,\" I said, \"I never felt saner in my life.\" I grabbed...", "2_9_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty in the morning</span>. Time for Captain John Black to get to the briefing hut at regimental headquarters. He trudged on foot, wearing tin shoes and shapeless woolen khakis and an enormous white overcoat. The weather was frightful. His head was wrapped in a preposterous muffler. In his hand, for a walking stick, he carried a sheaf of more than a hundred orders that had to be delivered to battalion commanders that morning. Lacking even gloves to wrap around his fingers, he clutched the sheaf with red fingers, to keep the papers from flying off. Every so often, he clutched tighter with the throb of a new attack. He perspired bullets and dropped the papers, picked them up again, dropped them again. Behind his big spectacles his eyes squinted with the effort of protecting the sheaf against bis own absent-mindedness.", "1_22_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty in the evening</span>. I was drunk. And as I stumbled through the graveyard I got to thinking about the six ditches I'd been in that day, and all at once, while I was staring at the bushes, I knew there was a seventh, and I laughed. I laughed loud. I leaned against a tombstone. I slid down it and sprawled full-length on the ground. I rolled over on my back and began laughing loud and free, only there wasn't anybody to hear me except the ghosts who slept in the graves that went down into the earth everywhere, one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven, twelve, thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, sixteen, seventeen, eighteen, nineteen, twenty, two-twenty, twenty-one . . . . That was a graveyard. That graveyard was twenty-one deep in the earth and you could look up through the roofs of the underground houses, if you happened to be lying down in a grave, sick, or dead, and see the stars in the night and so know you were twenty-one deep in the earth instead of the two-twenty you'd figured on. I laughed and laughed. And that's the end of the story. I...", "1_23_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty in the evening</span> on the night of December twenty-first. At ten forty, Redwine had sung the closing song, had dismissed the congregation, had hurried back to the unfinished furnace room behind the rectory, to watch television and brood about what the suicides of $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 had done to his ministry.\nThe first wave of grief had passed. In a few minutes the Wave of Affection would roll in, carrying with it a message from Mars that would shake his faith to its foundation. He had five minutes to brood in solitude before the Wave of Affection arrived.\nAt eleven-twenty, then\u2014two minutes before the first wave of affection crashed on beaches all over Newport\u2014Redwine turned off the television and went to his old organ to plink a few We Wish You a Merry Christmas chords. The organ was a real one, and Redwine was a fairly hot organist.\nRedwine turned on the strobe again, and the illusion of a Universe Roasting Once Again was resumed.\nShrubbery rustled at the rear of the church.\nRedwine put his ear to the church floor, listened to the tramp of thousands of footsteps in the snow outside. The feet weren\u2019t walking in unison, weren\u2019t...", "2_22_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Mars. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 went to a rest room in their underground barrack. Many of their fellow soldiers were already in the rest room, either vomiting or lying on the floor underneath the sinks.\n\"Ohhh\u2014\" groaned a harmonious voice under one of the sinks. \"This one hit me just as hard as the first one did.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 found himself a private sink corner, crouched over it, and vomited.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, over in a corner, was seeing more hombles in his mind, more Wild Angels in his mind, more life and death in space. He was a man in hell.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was seeing more little people in his mind, more little old ladies.\nHe looked at a particularly hideous little old lady in his mind, closed his eyes tight, and made the horrible thing go away.\n\"My eyes!\" said a soldier from under a sink. \"I never thought I\u2019d want to see again!\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 forced his eyes open, forced himself to look carefully at the next horrible little old lady that came to mind. He forced himself not to be sick again. He forced himself to stay on his feet.", "2_14_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two twenty in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Martian boppers, fifteen thousand miles straight-line distance away on their satellite, got the word from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s thinking headbands, got the word to synchronize their new action with the events of the moment on Earth.\nThey let off a blinding swirl of sparks\u2014the signal for the beginning of Act Two.\nIt was the signal for the Martian boppers to make all Earthling thinking heads explode, to detonate Earthling muskets, rifles, pistols and land mines, to cause Earthling institutions and Earthling records to become as friable as sand.\nOne instant, Earth was thinking and records and guns and institutions.\nThe next instant, Earth was rocks and puddles and quirks in the wind.\nThe only things that had not detonated were the eyes, ears and ganglia of the living animals, and the thinking heads, clocks and timer systems of the Martian boppers.\nThese were two, very different thinking machines.\nAs to the thinking machines in the carcasses of the animals\u2014when the electric currents stopped in their ganglia, the bodies of the animals died.", "0_17_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-seven in the afternoon</span> according to $FIRSTNAMEB2, the Martian chronodynamite who glowed in a corner of the ceiling. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was as reliable as the best chronometric watch. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been brought along by Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1, who used it to order the house around. One of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s hobbies was studying Martian.\n\"Greetings,\" a voice said in Martian.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s spine jumped. The voice had come out of nowhere. And the somebody who belonged to the voice was nowhere, too. $FIRSTNAMEB0 whirled\u2014and saw nothing that could have spoken.\n\"Where are you?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 suspiciously.\n\"Right here,\" came the friendly, giddy reply. \"Right here, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\"\n\"Who are you?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Why\u2014you know who I am,\" said the giddy voice. \"You know who I am, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\"\n\"Who are you?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. He poised himself to run at the first sign of anybody materializing.\n\"Why\u2014I\u2019m you,\" said the voice gaily. \"I\u2019m you, $FIRSTNAMEB0!\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 relaxed. He understood that the voice was answering his question with an absurd redundancy.\nUn...", "1_7_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. This particular morning was to be unforgettable. The particular thirteenth day of October in the year of our Lord 1963 was to become a historical day indeed. This particular morning was to be one of those days that dawns bright and fair, fills the eyes with color, then darkness. It was going to be a day that would go down in history\u2014as a dark day. It was going to be a memorable day in the history of North America.\nThis was to be a day that a lot of old bastards would remember.\nIt was going to be pretty much of a scene, a bitch of an opening scene.\nThe coconut-limbed promoter was trying to conceal his melancholy. He was perforated with melancholy, his limbs shot through and through with melancholy. He had not been able to sleep all night. He kept waking up. He had painful erections.\nThe sad, gay scene was the Sunrise Fortress and Pleasure Domes, the headquarters of Cosmonaut Noble G. Vapor, the Space NEEDS Orange concessionaire for that area.\nThere was a marvelous sunrise, orange and pink and green, streaking all the clouds with lamentable beauty. Almost made everybody forget the show that was coming up. The whole sky was...", "2_17_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until six o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$SURNAME0 read the message on the wrapping paper. It said, simply, Earthling: Win or die. It was from the Tralfamadorians.\nA formation of three hundred Earthling troops, many of them on motorcycles, burst over the lip of the ridge. It was a mixed bag of troops, carrying a mixed bag of weapons. They came in all shapes and sizes, and looked as unhandy in their weapons as Mohawks in roller skates. The backbone of the formation was three Earthling infantry divisions. One had a pack of hounds. Another had a pack of howling Santa Clauses. A third had a pack of bulldozers on tank treads.\nThe motorcycles dumped their riders, formed an inner ring around a core of twelve SS troopers on horseback. The troopers wore baggy red suits, red stovepipe hats, and white tufts in their collars and at the elbows, and were armed with swords.\nTheir horses were decked out in red bunting.", "2_16_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-three in the afternoon</span>, Monday, April 21, 1970, and for what we intended to do the time was absolutely right. It was very important to us that it be Monday, because at Monday papers around the country a million people or more\u2014Mescriptresses, typesetters and mechanics as well as journalists\u2014would be pouring final proof into the hoppers, heaving old copy back \u201cas held\u201d or else frantically correcting last\u2014minute disaster/apocalypse stories about Cambodians/extremists/trouble in urban slums and highland hamlets all over Southeast Asia, plus my no doubt idiotic outburst and flurry of falsies. But our story\u2014the horror at the airport\u2014would not belong to that day\u2019s insanity.\nI ripped open my door and jumped into the dawn, my ponytail flapping in back as I loped across the lawn toward the holdup. My attorney followed, shouting that I should stop, but I ignored him because I suspected he was secretly glad to be rid of me. We both knew that our only hope to survive\u2014smartly\u2014was pure and simple denial.\n6. A Whiff of the Infinite...Leave That Thing Alone...Always Leave Them Wanting More", "1_1_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe four earthling anthropologists looked quickly at one another. They were not thinking flattering thoughts about their Martian tutor and guide. Their opinion was that Vlad the Impaler was purposely keeping them awake until they were too tired and crazy to ask intelligent questions at the Whizbang Show. And then\u2014and this would get him off the hook\u2014he would have them carried back in their hoecake to $FIRSTNAMEF1-luther-hopp.\nThey waited for the footsteps to grow faint.\nThe time flew by very swiftly.\nThe four anthropologists lay and glared at the ceiling and suffered.\nPresently $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s watch whirred softly. The crystal covered the watch face.\n$SURNAME1 uncovered the watch. He looked at the time, at the face of the watch, and winced, \"Here we go again,\" he whispered.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 opened her lovely, lids slowly. She found her husband floating a foot from her nose. He was grinning and pointing up.\n\"Look, $FIRSTNAMEF1,\" he whispered. His voice was like a bumblebee\u2019s song. \"It\u2019s starting.\"\nAs far as the eye could see, as far as the memory could divine, the great...", "1_12_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-five post meridiem</span> when we pulled up at the Taos Inn. Alfred's Cadillac was already parked there. I could hear the music of a quartet in the coffee shop.\nI remember remarking to myself, as we walked inside, that this was going to be the gor - giest Okie tea party since World War II.\n2. From the Taos Inn...parallel with the great god Chronos...shall the lord attorney general of these terrors, Doctor Alfred $FIRSTNAMEB3 Marsh, haste away in his Demon dragging his shadow, and enter a silence of eight years . . . no conference with Trumpets, no fiery sunrise over a cold Empire, no troubling of Trapeze in a Trance, no more Books of Saxon and no Moondances with the Memories of Vincas. Made into a Movie and caged forever...or perhaps only one leap: The Great Explorer where strange alphabets hum like bees in the Gorgon's skull and a Map of Stars hangs before the eyes of all dreamers and the Word Abraxas continually flames in the sky. -from Doctor Sax by Jack Kerouac, Skeeter, Gentleman of Terror, and friend of the Great Alfred Marsh.\nI WAS OUTSIDE the Chevrolet when I heard Doctor...", "2_7_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty in the morning</span>. A bee stung $SURNAME1 in the space between the thumb and index finger of his left hand.\nThis place had been so nicely selected for the slaying of $SURNAME1 that one might indeed say that fate had chosen it.\nThe place was at the tip of a peninsula, at the tip of Cape Cod, at the boyhood home of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nHere $SURNAME1 had hoped to die, to find death and what lay beyond.\nWith him on the peninsula was the son whom he had betrayed.\nThere was no one else in the whole world whose presence would have pleased $SURNAME1 more at this place and time.\nThe kid was carrying a Magnum Golden Gun in his youthful hand, and he was trying with some success to point the rhetorical barrel square at himself. The kid pressed the muzzle of the Magnum against his ribs, curled a stiff finger around the trigger, held his breath\u2014and then smiled and let his finger relax and the air out of his lungs.\nHe looked at the empty chamber under the barrel.", "1_4_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-three in the morning</span>. Outside, clouds gathered and wind moved among the cypress trees. The storm had not yet started. The streaks of darkness were only lines of shadow cast by the declining moon.\nThe man did not look at the woman. He closed his eyes again, and slept.\nIn the morning, the dark streaks became clouds, and the clouds became a storm, and the storm broke loose and beat against the house as the sun went down. The woman got out of bed, washed and dressed, tucked the man under the covers, and went downstairs. As she passed through the doorway of the dining-room, a small cloud blew in past her, lighting for an instant on the table, on the chairs, on the blue ash-tray. The cloud was a piece of the wind from outside.\nAt the dining-floor she stopped, feeling unsure whether to go outside or to prepare lunch. The house was rumbling and whispering to itself. The doors of the cupboards kept shutting by themselves.\nFinally she decided to go outside and prepare the table on the terrace. That was where the man would want to eat.\nAs she stepped through the glass door onto the stones of the terrace, her stockings were soaked through at once. Her feet...", "2_5_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-one in the morning</span>. My watch had stopped again. The last time I had it repaired the man said the mainspring was broken. I examined the watch. The main spring was as fine as grass. The watch had stopped at twenty-eight minutes past five, which meant it was now eight hours and twenty-eight minutes slow. I was somewhere behind schedule, which was not surprising after six months on the road.\nThis was Monday, November 9, the day after the elections. The polls had been open all over the country. It was the first national election I'd ever taken part in, and it was nice to wake up in a strange town with nobody around and know it didn't make any difference. Wherever you go, national elections are like that.\nHolding my breath, I stole quietly past Musso's, the Hollywood Boulevard restaurant where the writers eat. There was a chance William Faulkner, Sinclair Lewis and John Dos Passos might all be drinking breakfast together at one of the big tables in the back, but the big double doors were folded tight. A curtain had come down on the front of the world.", "1_16_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. I was due at the office of the Americana at five. My bag was already at the hotel. I had finally had to give in to pressure, and the only advantage I could see in the move was that it would get me back in a hurry for my appointment with $FIRSTNAMEB3. I called him up to tell him I was coming. \u201cWell, listen,\u201d he said, \u201cnot so fast.\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d I asked. \u201cI can't explain, everything is too complicated and blasted.\u201d The phone crackled in my ear. \u201cWhere are you now?\u201d he said.\n\u201cIn front of the old New Orleans Mint.\u201d\nHe swore. \u201cWell, which airline did you arrive on in New Orleans?\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cWhich airline? Eastern? Delta?\u201d That was a poser. My brain was muddy and slow. \u201cWhere are you going to now?\u201d he asked. I told him I was in a taxi.\n\u201cAll right, stay in the taxi and tell him to drive to the FULTON HOTEL and don't give him a chance to make any stops...", "1_10_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute to eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME2 found the lobby phone that wouldn\u2019t be busy. Stone was also on the phone, talking to his mother. \"Enjoyed your Pacific Tour?\" $SURNAME2 asked.\n\"Bitch of a trip\u2014fucking Pacific,\" said Stone. \"She\u2019s joining us on the 30th\u2014Constantine\u2019s, your girl friend.\"\n\"You say it\u2019s a bitch of a trip, but according to this schedule,\" said $SURNAME2, studying it, \"you had a fare from Phoebe, Indiana, to LAX that was longhaul to start with\u2014and now look. Your guy\u2019s going to Auckland, New Zealand, via Tahiti and Honolulu, change planes; and coming back, he\u2019s going to leave Auckland via Tahiti again, no Guam, and change planes at LAX. Flight time each way is a flat week.\"\n\"Bitch of a trip,\" Stone said.\nThe bar phone rang.\n\"You get it, kid,\" said $SURNAME2 to Pierce. \"Where\u2019s Cat?\" he asked Stone.\n\"I don\u2019t even know if he saw the guy,\" said Stone. \"He just took...", "1_3_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span>, and red-eyed men were giving up the ghost and letting their whusky dreams of kings and kaisers be dispersed by the cold breaths of a thousand altitudes.\nAt three-thirty there was no traffic in Reno except the muledriver who made his rounds at five-minute intervals. His bell could be heard all over the town.\nThe town was clean and orderly and tightly shut. There were no drunks in the streets.\nAfter four o\u2019clock the quiet of the coming day began to gather itself nervously all over the town.\nThe quiet gathered, and the first light shone sinister under the shuttered eyelids of clocks and grocers\u2019 meat departments. The quiet moved among the breakfast foods and pantry utensils and the families that were sleeping.\nAt four-fifteen there were the beginnings of light and life in the round-topped gambling houses on Virginia Street. At this point the same scenes were enacted as had been enacted at one o\u2019clock. Men gave up the ghost, making as big a noise as possible about doing it.\nAt four-thirty activity began on the main thoroughfare of Virginia Street itself. The cocktail orchestra in the Hotel Wildwood cafe was on the job.", "1_0_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute past midnight</span>. Miles from nowhere, on a ramp leading from the San Francisco Freeway to the Sunbeam Hotel and Casino on the shoreline of Lake Tahoe, sat a wide, low, gleaming blue sedan of a configuration unknown to civilian experience. The driver was a huge man wearing a sharp black suit, double-breasted, buttoned right up to his round, pink neck. He also wore a buttonhole and a black tie, as if he\u2019d just got back from a wedding.\nHe had. The car had barely turned off Las Vegas Boulevard when the man\u2019s small, wiry companion pressed a gun in his back and informed him he was getting out. He complied. He climbed out of a car containing a mistress and a metric ton of high explosives, got out, smiled apologetically and waved farewell, and left everything to the small, wiry man who was his friend.\nWhen the Blue sedan blew up, the big man would have laughed but for the gun in his back. As it was, he merely performed a strange little shuffle on his way to a sparkling white Cadillac limousine on the edge of the freeway ramp\u2019s apron. The limousine was heavily tinted and bore Nevada license plates. Before...", "2_18_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAll this background was new to $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and to Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0 and his wife had come into it as follows:\n$SURNAME0 and his wife had died together on Mars. They had been materialized together in the drawing room of $SURNAME0\u2019s Westchester mansion. They had tottered from their gilded frames, had suspected their own existence, and had gone arm in arm to their Westchester landing stage. There the helpful hands of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had taken them in.\nIn Newport, $SURNAME1 and his mother and daughter put the $SURNAME0 party on board a schooner for the long voyage to Europe. The $SURNAME0 party spent much of the three months at sea miserably sick. They might never have learned to believe in their own existence, and they would certainly have died miserably anyway, had they not received a tremendous jolt to their systems.\nThe jolt came when their schooner put in at Port St. Wu, in the South China Sea. $SURNAME0 had in his pocket the exact change for a telephone call to his ball bearing best friend in Malacandra, a call to one Mr. Salo.", "0_13_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span> and the rain was lashing the ground when they left Botofogo in the bus. It was a mournful ride through empty streets. For the most part they rode along the bay side and could see across the bay the bleak mountains of the suburbs and then the mountains of the real suburbs and then pleasant woodland rises and then more bleak mountains of the dead suburbs. The country had been asphalted over almost to Oporto, where it turned green again very faintly. Montoya and Robert Cohn were asleep. Nobody had anything to say. Maria looked out at the wonderful country. Except forMETROPOLIS there was nothing in the way of a scenery sign until, after an hour and a quarter, S\u00e3o Jo\u00e3o Abade appeared. It was the first cultivated-looking country that they had passed. The little town was built on a pleasant fold in the hills and wore a look of repose such as commuter towns never have. The bus turned in across a rickety bridge on the low banks of the River Douro, and then turned again, steeply, and went up a hard dirt road into the country. We turned a corner and saw the regional capital of the wine country ahead of us. To the left was a flat wall of stone.", "2_19_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe second man was British, a good player on a miniature scale.\nThe third man was an Italian with a graceful style and an almost imperceptible serve.\nThe four women were beautiful and British.\nThere were plenty of good things to eat and drink at the tables in the caf\u00e9. There were also good magazines, fresh flowers, and clean ash trays.\nThere was one rule that wasn\u2019t fair and got followed anyway. It was the rule that made sure $FIRSTNAMEF3 Rymbly came after everybody and ate everything that was left.\nAt ten minutes past eight o\u2019clock in the evening, $FIRSTNAMEF3 Rymbly ate and drank everything that was left. Then he left, in a Rolls-Royce paid for by his two elderly widows.\nAt midnight, four sets were still going on. At one o\u2019clock, sets and matches were completed, and the All-England Club prepared for its annual ball.", "0_22_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes past ten o'clock in the evening</span>.\nVoice comes from some loudspeaker; the Acme Insurance Company has struck again. Thus the scene is set, the premise is established, we now enter the field of operations of a Name, know Iudicium x. A Name that is capable of forecasting to a second, to a fraction of a second, when a burglary will occur. And that same Name can also use another piece of information, can also certify to you to the precise second when a fire will start, or a man will fall, or a woman will suddenly begin clawing her cheeks. A Name with visions of grandeur, then, able to play with time, able to break it into increments; able to own it.\nIs this a Name we can love?\nHelp me, rock. I need you, rock. I would tell you my reactions to last night if they weren\u2019t so confused. I would crawl into your rusty innards if I could. But this is a new day and I must rise to it.\nRight before I left Stonecipher Beadsman\u2019s house, he carefully opened some drawer in a suite of carved wood and badly scuffed Art Deco furniture, and he extracted from the drawer a small rubber membrane with heve...", "0_2_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> when I finally got back to my house on Larimer Street. A life of sin, drugs and Zen dries you out something terrible. You can go whole days without a drink. But now I needed water. Running up to the cabinet, I took out a big jugful of Green Purgatory and washed the dust of Colfax off my teeth and out of my throat. Amazing! Who said a housecat can't find his way home? And the funny thing was that I remembered everything along the way. The lights I broke, the things I said to people ... and I remembered the house where All Bloodly Private Private Private Private Detective was from. On an impulse, I decided to drive over there and see if they were up. Maybe they could give me something to eat. Maybe they could help me find my car. Maybe they'd have a phone and I could get my agent, if he wasn't dead, to call my attorney and see if we couldn't work some sweet deal to keep me out of prison.\n3. A Nine\u2014Inch Dilemma Surprised At Home\nThe address was 25 Jonathan Court, Glendale California. It took me fifteen minutes to find the place, but when I did it was just the sort of thing...", "1_2_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after two o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was nothing more nor less than a large air space lying above and between the planet Mars and its satellite, Phobos. The Terran battleship that dominated this sparsely inhabited space was named $SURNAME0, and the name was meaningful to nobody in particular. $SURNAME0 was one of the Institute\u2019s anachronistically warlike names.\nThe eleven hundred men aboard $SURNAME0 the battleship were acclimated to space, and bored by it, and proud of the fact that they weren\u2019t afraid of it.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s Commander was named $FIRSTNAMEB4. Boredom and pride go hand in hand. $FIRSTNAMEB4, like all the officers under him, was a meatball.\nEveryone on $SURNAME0 was descended from $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, California. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 won the lottery.\n$SURNAME1 was the only Earthling on board.\n$SURNAME0 the battleship, $FIRSTNAMEB4 the commander, and $SURNAME1 the Earthling floated Buddha-like in their suits in the control room. They were there because the law required them to be there at that particular time. In that control room, and in all other control rooms of the several space-going forces of the United States, a full...", "1_6_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-one in the morning</span> and I was sitting over a third quart of tequila in the office of the News Switchboard. I wore a gray suit on which adhered strips of dew, held together by stale sweat. I had a one\u2014day stubble of beard. In my coat pocket were some more dirty stock\u2014option forms. I had quit selling them in the office; I just came in and sat down and now and then somebody would say, \u201cWhere the hell have you been?\u201d and I would mumble something and then go on drinking tequila and feeling sick.\nThe time to start drinking is when you feelhung\u2014over, and when you realize you are feeling worse all the time. Believe me, that is the time to start. Then you will get well, or else you will be too drunk to give a damn.\nI was not ready to get well yet. I wanted to drill a whole lot of wholes into my head. I ripped open the purple envelope and got out the sheet of paper and spread it on the desk:\nCONFIDENTIAL\n7\u201326\nDear Subscriber:\n$10,000,000 Jaguar Income Now Paying Dividends\nThis has been a good and profitable year for Jaguar Hair Renew...", "2_9_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-six in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 dug his thumbnail into the trough of a C-sharp, and the reed, water soaking into its fibers, reluctantfy yielded a query, then a confirmation, and finally a strong, convincing endorsement of the C-sharp.\n\"We all have to serve time, whether in the House of Correction or the Home of the Hopelessly Curious,\" came then, from $SURNAME0\u2019s loudspeakers. \"But the time we spend serving our own essential nature is not time wasted, and that is the most time any of us can afford to waste.\n\"Failing punctuality, friend\u2014\" said $SURNAME0, \"let me suggest boastfulness as a winning substitute. Boast about what you are. You are a human being. No other beings in the Universe have ever managed to exist even as poorly as you have managed to exist.\n\"One with rotary engines in his arms and legs\u2014\" said $SURNAME0, \"you are the first among your kind to manage as well as you have managed.\n\"One with eyes, fins, and milling thoughts in his head,\" said $SURNAME0, \"you are the lst among your kind to understand as much as you now understand.", "1_6_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past six o'clock in the morning</span> when we jumped inside our mining car and started to slide toward the other end of the longest, blackest tunnel ever built by man. The tunnel was ten miles long, but we saw no light at the end of it. No light\u2014just the rumor of light. Rumor of light! We saw red, yellow, blue and violet lights appear and vanish in the darkness. We saw a white light blinking somewhere up in front of us. The white light flickered and we answered with our green and red signal.\nHere came the brakeman, running along the tunnel and hopping on our car. He had his lantern. He was a big fellow, with a round, cheery, red face and whiskers that curled around his ears. He was in a cheerful mood and full of good cheer. \u201cHow's everybody this mornin' ?\u201d he said.\nWe said that we were fine.\n\u201cYou fellas got a pretty good load?\u201d he said.\nThe funny thing about brakemen is that they never know what you're hauling. One hour they think you're hauling coal, the next hour they think you're hauling the Queen of Siam. \u201cAll so many sheep?\u201d", "0_6_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six seventeen in the morning</span>. Mildred Pierce came out of her bath at exactly six sixteen and was sitting at her dressing table, with her freshly perfumed skin gleaming moistly under the powders and creams, when the gong rang.\nThe maid let Stan Pierce in. Stan was looking very gay indeed\u2014so gay that Mildred suspected at once he had been drinking.\n\"Why are you so bright and shiny this morning?\" she said.\nStan turned to the mirror and looked at himself, so that he wouldn\u2019t have to look at Mildred.\n\"Bright and shiny?\" he said.\n\"There\u2019s a sale on at Millard\u2019s,\" said Mildred. \"I hear everybody got platinum credit cards.\" She made platinum sound like sewage.\nStan kept on looking at himself.\n\"I see your old man got one, anyway,\" he said. \"Shall I tell him to invest in a couple of toupees?\" Mildred smoothed out her eyebrows with an eyebrow pencil. \"You tell him whatever you please,\" she said.\n\"Well, then, how about you and me escaping to California\u2014and starting all over?\"\n\"You mean running away?\"\n\"I mean changing our circumstances.\" The word...", "1_6_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-three in the morning</span>, a Sunday. The place was the old Fern WiIlow Barnes Pre-prep School in Indianapolis. A dreadful school, now abandoned\u2014a school of dread, especially for the little boys who were sent there by their robot fathers, who had themselves been educated by Pavlov, at awful schools. The robots\u2019 wives didn\u2019t want the sons, either; they had had all the bonding with their husbands that any women could stand.\nThe WiIlow building was a four-story stone trap for boys, with shiny brutal leaded-glass windows, fake Tudor towers with empty bell-ringing chambers inside, a vaulted chapel ceiling with acoustic tiles that gave off a derisive hiss when you threw your cap at them, grooves worn in the stone floors where frustrated, lonely little boys had sucked their thumb for comfort. The whole place stank rottenly of pipe tobacco, sweat, and urine. The floors groaned and creaked under the vast and contemptuous weight of the custodian, an old, old man who kept five dust mops going all at once. At the sound of his key in the front door, little boys scampered under staircases, into closets, behind bath stalls.\nYes, the name of...", "0_16_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the first day of October, the year 1863, the 69,999th year of the period of Earthly Time since the Messiah was cruelly put to death by order of the Earthly United Nations, Lee, leader of the Army of Virginia, commander of the Army of Northern Virginia, was on a small hill of the Orange Road about three miles west of that place called Bull Run by the United Nations.\nThe position he occupied in the order of battle was called CHEW, an acronym in the code broken only that morning by students at the Ragged Community College, a code, incidentally, never suspected by the United States census takers, the dominant historians, the loyal literary critics and essayists, the United Nations Armistice Commission, and the Department of Sociology at Rock Hill.\nAnd the meaning of CHEW was simply This position defended by me is the key to the entire right wing of the army.\nLee was at the center of a concave semicircle of generals\u2014Longstreet, Ewell, D.H. Hill, Stuart, A.P. Hill, and others\u2014 and all of these generals were looking west.\nAll were exposed to the temperate weather. Only Longstreet, wrapped to the chin, held...", "2_1_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe ironingsheet portal dilated fully. Three hundred and twelve of the faithful, groping, shuffled through it into the space-time volume occupied by the $SURNAME0 mansion.\nThat was the maximum crowd that could be accommodated in the space-time volume with comfort and safety.\nIn the materialization of a crowd, as in all materializations, there were departures from the order prescribed by the planners. The new creatures materialized not only in the space-time volume occupied by the $SURNAME0 mansion itself, but also in the volumes occupied by the walls, windows, chimneys, and gables of an ornamental stone folly three hundred feet east of the mansion.\nThe planners had mistakenly supposed that the three-hundred-foot brick-and-glass folly was unoccupied.\nThe planners had mistakenly supposed that nothing resided within the walls of the mansion itself\u2014or within a ten-foot frustum of the chimney that rose from the mansion\u2019s kennel brewery to a folly skylight.", "2_22_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The taxi had left the buildings and curved formations of the Army back of the plain, and had come upon the landing field, and had joined a steady stream of trucks carrying shipments of Martian regiments to the plain.\nIn a brightly lighted building, $FIRSTNAMEB0 could see the recruits being assembled from their trucks by other blue salamanders.\nIn the fields on the other sides of the buildings, the visible parts of regiments were being torn through close-order drill by other blue salamanders.\nIt was eleven o\u2019clock in the evening.\nA time counter over the door of a brilliantly lighted bar grated from 1717 hours to 1718 hours, reminding $FIRSTNAMEB0 that he had swallowed his pill ten minutes late. He reached a guilty hand toward his coat pocket for another pill. But the pain in his head, he found to his relief, was gone. \"What was that pill?\" he said.\n\"Something to make you believe,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, \"believe in him, don\u2019t matter what he said.\"\n\"Who was that supposed to be?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"God?\"\n\"That\u2019s right\u2014God,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"Who else?\"", "0_11_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEF0 Comparini cryptically broke off her anecdote about her visit to Cambridge, and her husband Dante looked at his watch and announced that he had invitations to luncheon that day, one at the Embassy, one at his club. $FIRSTNAMEF0 gave a laugh and said, \"Luncheon? It\u2019ll be hours before we have luncheon.\"\n\"Lovely to be young and have a long time before luncheon,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nAnd then she announced that she thought she\u2019d go up and have her shower, and she began to gather her thoughts to get up from the table.\n\"Don\u2019t neglect to read that excellent Trotsky essay, young man,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"If only that wretched Aquinas\u2014\" said Spangle.\n\"Bye bye,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, ruffling Inverarity\u2019s hair.\n\"Keep quiet, child,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Bye, all.\" She wandered out blithely.\nEdgar was the first to speak after $FIRSTNAMEF0 had left the room. He whispered, \"She\u2019s a bitch.\" He sat back, looking at Pierce. \"A bitch, I say.\"\n\"Come now. Come now, brother,\"...", "1_23_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-seven in the evening</span>. At exactly eleven thirty-five I would be in big trouble. Since I wasn\u2019t sure which house in the darkness ahead belonged to our attorney, I had decided to wait. Five minutes. If he didn\u2019t come out by then, I would drive slowly past the place and make my final decision. It seemed simple enough.\nThree houses. If he wasn\u2019t waiting in the first few, then I would choose one of the other two..\nWhat the fuck was a Supreme Court decision anyway? How could they make a pig unemployed? If a few editor\u2014le Proph\u00e8tes were giving me shit on account of my family, fine\u2014but this was bullshit! There were more important things in life than FamilyCircus.\nWhich house? They all looked about the same. Rectangular blobs of light behind random arrangements of trees. You couldn\u2019t tell shit about a house from this distance. The pink one, maybe. It had a red lawn,... monkey grass\u2014whatever the fuck that was. It looked vaguely like a blood smear. Good choice.\nMy brain was fizzing like a German beer. A big molar twinged painfully, then gave way completely. Emergency Dental attention would have to wait...", "1_23_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until midnight</span>. At the first stroke of midnight the stake would fall. The pulse of the revelation beat in $SURNAME1\u2019s temples. He toyed gamely with the thought of glory, and it coarsened him.\nHe turned to $FIRSTNAMEF0 and was surprised to find that she was still in the bathroom. In all this time, she had not made a single move to help him in his analysis.\nHe closed the bathroom door so that she could not hear through the steamy walls.\nHe sat down at the desk by the window. $FIRSTNAMEF0 was evidently not going to help him with his great work\u2014but he could still put in some hard thinking for himself.\nHis fearfully accumulated material was spoiled for him, though.\nGnut had spoiled it.\nNot only that, but there were other skeletons in his meager shelves of reference. Sy $SURNAME0, for instance\u2014$SURNAME0 of Newport, $SURNAME0 of Wuppertal, $SURNAME0 of Titan\u2014was a skeleton, too. $SURNAME0 had suffered a humiliating public disgrace shortly after putting To The Meteor on display in the Martin Luther Memorial Pipe Library.\n$SURNAME0 vanished from public life after the exhibition of Gnut in the Church of God the Utterly...", "1_15_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been asleep in a cabin at the head of the space ship. He had been supplied with this cabin by the courtiers who\u2014with so little true kindness\u2014ruled the harmoniums in their stately dance.\nThey had given $FIRSTNAMEB0 the cabin by way of a joke. They made jokes, without meaning much by them. Their jokes were intended to keep them agreeably worried about themselves.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 wasn\u2019t being punished in any way, no matter what he did. He had been put in the cabin by way of a joke.\nThey saw to it that he had no mirror in the cabin, so he couldn\u2019t shave. They had given him one book to read, a book in a language he could not understand. It was their guess that he could not read the language in the book, or any other language. They supposed him to be almost as stupid as the harmoniums themselves. They egged him on to prove them right, egged him on to prove himself right, as they thought he was.\nThey had watched him, brought him books and music by the armload, hoping that he would break down, prove himself as stupid as they suspected. He had thanked them, said they...", "2_19_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span> when all life in the village of Mitford disappeared.\nThere was no panic, only a sense of urgency in the movements of the villagers.\nIn twelve minutes, a hundred and fifty-nine of the three hundred and thirty inhabitants of Mitford were alive and safe in shelters. The rest were dead or dying.\nThe village was an unprotected pocket in an area in which the Martian flora was happy and secure.\nIn a sense, it was a test case.\nThe white tree-roots came to the sides of the houses of Mitford along streets and in yards.\nFor a mile around Mitford, the flowers were as large as houses.\nThey were not pretty. They were magnificent in their power and truculence. A man on horseback could not have forced his way among them.\nThey stood still, threatening.\nThey were brilliant and quivering and changeful.\nThere was about them an air of unutterable malevolence, as if they knew something that no one else knew, could do something that no one else could do.", "2_7_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty-two in the morning</span>.\nThe scheduled festivities were set for eleven o\u2019clock.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 walked up the path from the beach to the bird room, very pleased with himself. He had been shrewd to insist on living at the tip of the little peninsula, for apparently he was going to die there.\nHe rapped at the door of $SURNAME0\u2019s bird room. The plate on the door said:\nTHIS BIRD\nHOUSE IS HEATED BY\nRICHARD H. TRUMAN ENERGY, INC. Fate can be very considerate, indeed. Even as $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 approached his fiery death, a new name was being born that would spread warmth and light to all the unheated houses in the world.\n\"Come in, $FIRSTNAMEM2,\" said $SURNAME0\u2019s voice through the closed door.\n$SURNAME1 opened the door. $SURNAME0 was there, all right. He was lying supine on his bunk, his long, white beard pointing like an arrow to the stainless steel wheel that ventilated the premises. His eyes were closed.", "1_13_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span> when the events that were to transform the lives of Salo and $FIRSTNAMEF1, as well as $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and Genghis Cole and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4, came to pass. The events took place in a wild, unspoiled Place that lay near the estate of Salo and $FIRSTNAMEF1. The Place was accessible only to Salo and $FIRSTNAMEF1, and to visitors who chartered boats to take them there.\nIt was not easy to find the Place. In order to reach the Place, the chartered boats had to thread a maze of channels, good only at certain stages of the tide. Each channel was marked with a sign that told whether the tide was high, whether it was slack, or whether it was low.\nSalo had fussed for weeks about the placard that was to mark the channel leading to the Place. It had been a bone of contention between him and $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"My dear\u2014,\" he had begun.\n\"They aren\u2019t your dear in the league where you\u2019re using them, Salo,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"They\u2019re the Marsian equivalent of cheap streetwalkers. You are not to dote on them or coddle them in any way.\"", "0_1_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-one in the morning</span> when we finally got back to the ranch. There were copses of bodyguards, arms folded stoically under night\u2019s purple canopy, waiting for our return. The moment we rolled up to the front gate their nervous hands went for the gunbutts under their raincoats...but nothing happened. We went right past them up the canyon road to the mansion\u2014once more safely home. There was no sign of my attorney, who\u2019d obviously left hours before. So I bounded up the stairs and into the kitchen, where I called the airline to book a flight back to L.A...then I sat down over a glass of rum and coke to brood over what had happened. It wasn\u2019t possible to think; my brain was already too weary, too battered, too overloaded. Only one image seemed to float across the screen\u2014that awful rictus of my attorney\u2019s face as he said, \u201cYou won\u2019t believe what he said to me.\" How many times had I seen that face, struggling to comprehend? How many times had I uttered those same words myself? It was a face that is easy to wear when you are hip long enough to be burned. Because there is no way to tell it\u2014...", "1_21_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s squad was standing at attention before the great closed doors of the mess hall. At any second now, the doors would fly open, revealing the brilliantly lighted, steamy, aromatic paradise within\u2014 revealing food and the company of friends.\nThe doors didn\u2019t open.\nStep by step, the lights went out in the Universe outside the dark windows.\nThe Universe within the dark windows became a Universe of steamy windows running with condensation, of gray furniture emerging from gray mist, of yellow trumpet-blossoms with red pistils growing out of humid carpets.\nThe Universe outside the windows now became a Universe of dark running with tiny hot beads.\nThe Universe within grew dark and wet.\nThe Universe without was cold and clear.\nThe Universe without was now the Universe.\nAnd the Universe was a fine place to be.\nThe consensus was that the recruit with the bloody nose was faking\u2014fooling around, trying to turn a grind into a big thing. The consensus was that the recruit with the bloody nose had better start acting right pretty damn quick, or $FIRSTNAMEB1, the squad leader, would make him wish he had.\nThe consensus was right. The recruit with the bloody nose...", "2_5_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-four in the morning</span>, I was tired. I wanted a cup of coffee. I opened my eyes and watched the huge blue sky whirling over me and I began feeling weak and stupid, the victim of my own greed, lust, inertia and fear.\n\u201cYes,\u201d said the voice again, \u201cthis is a Mr. Duke. Listen, Lucy, I want to talk to you. I don't have much time. I'm calling you from a booth just outside Williamsport, Pennsylvania.\u201d Why I lied to her I don't know. It seemed clear at the time. \u201cI've been thinking, Lucy, about what we were going to do about the baby, and I thought maybe if we could get married .. .\u201d I paused. \u201cAre you still there?\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d she said. \u201cWhat about the money you owe that man?\u201d She meant my landlord.\n\u201cI don't know,\u201d I said. \u201cI don't want to talk about it now. This is a bad line and I'll call you again. How about if I come to LA this weekend?\"", "1_12_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after noon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized silently and concurrently in a space feet from Skip\u2019s space suit. The clean, brittle, cold smell of the troposphere stung their nostrils.\nThere are certain sounds that are beyond the range of the most refined human ears. In the presence of an event that makes these sounds, human beings fall all over themselves making strenuous and often ludicrous efforts to mask the fact that they have heard.\nThis was the case, now, with $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4. $FIRSTNAMEB4 had heard the high keen passage of a wind-hose that carried its billions of tons of air a hundred and twenty-five miles an hour.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 looked straight at Skip, as though they had somehow caught him in the act of eavesdropping.\n\"I have seen you before\u2014somewhere,\" said $SURNAME0 dreamily.\n\"I\u2014I can\u2019t say that\u2014\" said Skip.\n\"No\u2014no\u2014we have not formally met,\" said $SURNAME0. \"You are $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0. I am the Martian intruder, Beebee Tralfamadore.\"\n\"Oh\u2014ah\u2014\"...", "2_0_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve seventeen a.m.</span> when I received an urgent phone call from Ole Roky Erickson at the Carioca Club. Had I dropped by there recently? I realized then that I\u2019d forgotten all about Roky. Probably because I was in San Francisco, where the peace movement is in full bloom ... and where old Roky, twice a week, really mixes things up. \u201cA representative from the Office of Pacific Affairs wishes to talk to you,\u201d said the operator. \u201cHe\u2019s waiting.\u201d\n\u201cWhat\u2019s this about?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cI can\u2019t reveal his identity,\u201d she said. \u201cAll I can tell you is that it\u2019s urgent and extremely personal.\"\n\u201cHow did he get my number?\"", "1_6_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-seven in the morning</span>. The high brick wall of the jail was still in the grey morning shadow of the Court Building. The tin roof of the jail was still dry though the rains that had soaked everybody were growing closer and would come before long. A handful of lights moved in the entire vast dark building of stone and brick and tin.\nThere was a key in the lock of the cell. The key was just being turned as I turned. The door came open an inch or two.\n\"Hey!\" I yelled.\nUnder his breath in the corridor outside the cell Brass ceased his impersonation of a stone wall and mumbled something to somebody. There was no response.\n\"What is it?\" I asked.\n\"You got orders,\" said Brass. \"The Chief wants you.\"\nI nodded. \"The Chief?\" I started to leave the cell and then I saw that the jagged opening in my silk suit had been overlaid by thick steel wire.\nA single sharp wire of seamless steel went through the cloth on a diagonal from where the lapel had been through the chest and the back. It held the shred of suit together. Nothing could cut the wire. It held about a half-ton's stress indefinitely.\nI didn\u2019t have a horse.", "1_0_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span> when the Galaxie 500 drew abreast of the closed, barricaded door of the space ship.\nPop! The balloons of the expanding gas inside the space ship's helmet broke the seal of the plastic window, rebounded daintily to the top of the helmet.\nThe saloon-door key, dangling on its string, scraped discordantly against the steel hull of the ship.\nThe trooper in charge was Private Bo Ray Chong. His feet were flat on the sloping asphalt of the incline. He leaned, swayed back towarr chuckling as the violent popping noises came from the ship. He was a thirty-and-a-half-year-old man with a round, pleasant face and an infectious chuckle. He enjoyed laughing at other people\u2019s expense, although he did not particularly enjoy being laughed at.\nHe let his eyes roam up and down the ship. Nobody, he was sure, was in it. This was ridiculous. A man who would climb into a space suit with an eight-hundred-pound helmet and pretend he was on the moon\u2014or near-by space\u2014was a nut. There was no possible sensible explanation for the man's actions.\n\"Babble, babble,\"...", "2_18_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span> of November 4, 1975.\nThe dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, didn\u2019t appear in the palace on Titan.\nIt will perhaps be remembered that $FIRSTNAMEB4 had disappeared in 1954, during the Festival of Daisies. Its disappearance, like the disappearance of $FIRSTNAMEB2, the son of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his wife, did not greatly surprise anyone. Just where Kazaks came from, and where they disappeared to, was all one to Kazaks, just as all one to $FIRSTNAMEB2 was where people came from and where they went.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 was never missed.\n$SURNAME0 and his wife were not greatly surprised by their losses. They were a well-matched set of losers. As their losses mounted, a close bond between them grew strong.\n\"Hello, $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2014\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Oh, hello, dear\u2014\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0 dully.", "2_13_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-four in the afternoon</span>.\nMary $SURNAME0 \u2014 this wayward Newport socialite \u2014 was somewhere inside her closed and guarded mansion, inhaling something through a flexible tube. The something was a drugged gas that she had designed for the continual euphoria of space travellers who venture outside the Earth\u2019s atmosphere. Her system, by now, was well adjusted to it.\nMary had had her system well adjusted to a great many things over the years.\nOnly one thing had she not learned to adjust her system to.\nOnly one thing had Mary $SURNAME0 learned to hate. This thing was called $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nShe and $SURNAME1 had been married eighteen years.\nIt is a fact that a certain medieval churchman and chronicler, Saint $FIRSTNAMEM2, was intolerably teased by a lady whose name was $SURNAME1, who kept asking him when he was going to get around to rewriting the 96th Psalm in a way that would please her, who kept saying to him, \"$FIRSTNAMEM2\u2014sing us a new Psalm?\"", "0_13_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after one o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the first of May in the year of our Lord nineteen hundred and three according to the world's calendar, according to old schedules and programmes, but according to the calendar of saints and events and festivals it was probably far on into the night. Half a block from any street of the city you could hear and see the chiming and tolling of the bells at a hundred churches\u2014and more bells and more chimes and more tolling, if you listened intently and specifically for that sort of ringing. And the churches were only a very few of the places where things were happening. Only a very few. Congregations were singing in clear untremulous voices. The bells and the voices rang in our ears and in our heads. The horns of automobiles mingled their tremoloed humming with that of the churchbells. The horns of automobiles, getting ready to go somewhere, to start something. It was a sound as abstract as the sound of bells, and all continuous with the hum, the murmur of the city, the pulse in the telephone wires. It was the sound of boom, the boom of a city full of angels going somewhere just as much as the whine of shovels and the scream of saws in the construction battalions was the sound...", "0_23_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until midnight</span>.\nThe television, the stereo, the Bose Wave radio and the room\u2019s three bells were controlled in the console of a panel by yet another button. The button was a square, red one. The panel\u2019s toggle switch was marked HEAR NO EVIL.\nWhen the mansion awoke and began to function, what the house\u2019s bells had actually been calling the professor was Diabolus ex machina.\nIt was a Latin phrase. The professor did not know Latin, but he had two good English dictionary equivalents to the phrase: \"out of the devil\u2019s workshop\u201d and \"remedy by unconventional and especially harsh means.\u201d\nThe professor\u2019s last potential madness, the obsession to which he had just vowed not to succumb, was bell-freakery. He had chosen to face it by living in a home in which every bell was disconnected and silent.\nIt was to laughter that the meaning of the name had eventually degenerated. The mansion was to be a refuge from laughter.\nThe laughter had often been at the professor, at his usually failing, usually pathetic efforts to help others face reality and to function just about as efficiently in it as possible. The laughter had seldom been with the...", "2_10_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nOutside the iron fragments, the war would hardly have seemed to be going on at all. The wind was so strong that its sound masked other sounds. The iron fragments almost stifled the odors of decay and burning.\nIn the big iron structure of a rooming house far back on the Canadian plain, an old man lay dying of cancer.\nHis name was Bracknell. He had been born on a farm near St. Louis, Missouri, Earth.\nHe had been a common foot soldier in the UN forces during the brief but violent civil war that had preceded the recorded history of Tralfamadore. He had been the only survivor of his unit. Nobody knew why he had not died with the others. Nobody thought to ask him.\nHe had simply become a wandering man.\nHis wanderings had led him to an Army recruiting station on a rainy night in St. Louis, Earth. Just how long he had been wandering, and where from, he never said. Nobody asked him.", "0_7_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span> of December 27, Mardi Gras morning. The doors were unlocked, the crowd pushed in. Twenty minutes later the first statue rolled\u2014King Satan. It was the one the cops had been so worried about. There was riotous applause.\nThe seven bearers\u2014six young men and one lovely young girl in slacks\u2014had no trouble lifting King Satan to their shoulders from the platform where the throne stood. They marched out of the church, the crowd following. They crossed the neutral ground, and at the first roll of the drums they tightened their grip on the ropes and King Satan rose on the shoulders and rolled, while his subjects cheered and threw their masks high, and horns tooted, and the first sunlight in three hours came glowing on the upturned tangled expressions of ecstasy on upturned human faces.\nAt the first roll of the drums, the two hundred skeleton squads had spread out and advanced down all the other streets of their parts of town.\nThe tension was much less now that the first wave of statues had rolled. But the mood was still wild. The houses were abandoned. The skeleton marchers waved their arms in grandiose motions and prophesied. The fringes of the crowd rolled past, reading the apocalyptic graffiti on the walls of the...", "2_23_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven o'clock in the evening</span> on the planet Mercury.\nAl Fernwright walked into the house carrying two pails of milk from his dairy herd. He took his hat off his head and looked at it. The hat was stiff with manure.\n\"The herd is getting me down,\" he said.\n\"What herd?\" said Joe Fernwright.\nAl Fernwright poured two glasses of milk and handed one to his wife. Some of the milk spilled from the pail, and soaked into the sod floor.\n\"I wish they\u2019d let that herd go to grass, and I wished it yesterday,\" said Al Fernwright. He had a sandy face, and wrinkles in the back of his neck, and blue veins in his nose. He was forty-five years old.\n\"Them funny colors been run in your herd again?\" said Joe Fernwright.\n\"They\u2019re not colors,\" said Fernwright. He sucked up most of the spilled milk with his mouth, and strained it through his beard. \"They\u2019re not colors at all.\" He sipped at his glass again.\n\"They\u2019re colors if I say they are,\" said Joe Fernwright. \"You hear them colors mooing, Al? Hey, $FIRSTNAMEF3\u2014\"", "2_7_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span>. Yesterday was the longest day in the history of the Earth.\nThe place was Newport, Rhode Island.\nThe canopy of air strong enough to sustain life on the surface of Mars was only ten miles above the surface of Mars.\nThe Martian surface beneath the canopy was just barely mappable.\nIf you turned the telescopic vision of your space ship upward from the Martian surface, you could see the boundary of the canopy, a filament of green jelly. It was a very pretty sight.\nWhen you knew where to look, you could see Earth.\nThat might not mean much to you if the surface features of the Earth are not features that you would find interesting. Scrub pines and sage brush do not make a spectacular panorama. But if you knew where to look, you could see Chronos, the legendary moon of Mars\u2014the moon that never got any bigger or smaller, that never moved in the competitive show of moons.", "2_20_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAnything that would have been unexpected was welcome. Unpredictability is essential to a Martian ceremony.\nUnpredictability and the unconscious expression of passion by a perfec tion of form is the beauty of a ceremony on the Earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 understood almost everything on the ledge on a conscious level.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 loved ceremonies on the Earth.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was a friend of humanity.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 loved ceremonies because they seemed to make so much more sense than anything else did.\nMONSON AND BUKOFSKY AND TINTY AND KVNN TRUE, AND THE NICE MAN WHO WASN'T QUITE SURE WHAT HE WAS DOING THERE, were all tense. They were rigid. They were poised for the arrival of the seven players whose reception was to initiate the exciting changes of the game.\nA wall of fog had settled over Half Pint Ridge and the fans, and the scoreboard at the south end of the field swam in the haze.\nThe crowd noise had now mutated from a roar to a pulse.\nKill the umpire! was recognizable among the pulses.", "2_18_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-six in the evening</span>, Earthling Time. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were hiding among the eroded dunes by the shore of the big dark lake.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 watched the rocket ship. He could see nothing inside it. It was dark inside, and the portholes showed only black glass.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 did not know that the stocky man inside the rocket was his son. And the boy the man held on his lap was his grandson.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were sheltered by a thick hawthorn bush.\nThere was a boulder behind them, a cleft in its weathered face that formed a natural chimney that led upward, expanding as it rose. It was a chimney in which a beaten and starved man could climb, ultimately, to the top of the boulder.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had led $FIRSTNAMEB0 to the boulder for that purpose.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had protested, at first\u2014he had been brought up to believe that a man should not take his own life, just as he had been brought up to believe that an egg should be broken on the broad end. \"But what I say when I\u2019m deep-freezin\u2019 strawberries\u2014\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "0_20_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-eight in the evening</span>.\nThe three hundred kilocycles blared. The siren and the cycle with the loudspeaker and the ever-faithful twelve Dacclins and the trunk with George, $SURNAME1, and many pieces of baggage drove out to the launching ramp.\nThe Newport police, being under the impression that there was to be a regatta staged by a band of lunatics, did not come out to spoil anything.\nThe send-off was given by the three young women, who waved and looked enchanting. There were some little girls there, too.\nSome people are so genuinely kind and hospitable that they place themselves quite on the level of a doormat. It was on the doormat at the $SURNAME0 estate that Beatriz were standing now. She had walked out of the house carrying an album to show to a visitor. The last visitor had been her husband a year before.\nThe last visitor had left her a Pulitzer prize manuscript about the space ship that was grounded for repairs on Titan.\nThe chairman of the Pulitzer prize committee had gotten in touch with Beatriz by phone, had hinted strongly that she was next choice for the prize, provided she would write something earthshaking about the space ship on Titan.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0...", "2_0_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-six a.m.</span> when the phone rang and she answered it, having fetched up the extension from the bedroom. There was a click, a pause of about two seconds, a double click, and then broken sounds, nonverbal vocalizations, static possibly, reminiscent of radio atmospherics between the earth and some intelletelluric planet.\nOr were they perhaps trying to be code? Feeling no fear at all, in her clean home that was a fortress, $FIRSTNAMEF2 sat on a bland piece of the living room rug and watched the long dark boomerangs of her eyelashes dip and wag as she listened. As if it had been scheduled for fadeout along with the day\u2019s last program, the noise did attenuate and lose meaning. Soon, having tunred the envelope of her soul, the line gave out a final hiss and fell away. She thought about calling the telephone company, but didn\u2019t.", "1_23_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until midnight</span>. At this moment Copernicus and Fala arrived at the rendezvous point. No one was there. They waited for a minute and then, more out of politeness than anything else, set out exploring.\nTheir noses led them to a large white door with gold lettering on it that said 605.\nEeking the door cautiously open, they found themselves facing a nightmare scene of destruction and chaos. Scattered remnants of broken glass suggested that somewhere in the room there had once been windows. The ceiling was gone. Through the dark sky, a storm could be seen to be approaching.\nBut the two beasts approached their positions under the trees as though hypnotized by the voice of $SURNAME0, which at that point in the service was saying, \"Now, as the terrifying lull insured by the miracles of modern technology subsides, let us add our tremulous human voices to the pealing sonorities of the shaken heavens!-\n\" \u2019\u00bc-Yanno\u2019\u2014now \u2019The brook Kydnos winds now by Solduno\u2019s walls\u2019\u2014now\u2014\u2019the vine shoots forth its first green shoots on the warm surface of the soil\u2014now \u2019I see the sails, I hear...", "2_18_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. The year was 2540. The month was May.\nThe place was still Phoebe, a suburb of Wroc\u0142aw, Poland.\nThe place to which all the other places occupied by the temporal co-ordinates had migrated was still the drawing room of $SURNAME0\u2019s house, a drawing room which did not, in real life, exist.\nIt had been materialized by means of the spurious space-time techniques of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nIt was still brilliantly illuminated by the light of the Sun.\nOutside were Earth and Milky Way and Universe. Inside were $SURNAME0\u2019s strange notions of cosiness.\nA Martian moonship lay on one side, garage fashion, its mooring boom in the act of becoming a double-ended swing pipe. One end of the pipe was lashed to a goldfish pond. The cockpit of the moonship was raised six feet above the floor, and steps led up to it.\nThere were stairs going up from the cockpit to a magnificent unmade bed.", "0_17_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty-nine in the afternoon</span>. Candy Mandible was coming toward me out of the massage rooms, wiping her hands on a towel.\nCandy looked much better than she had just one week before. She had color in her face and an alert attitude. \u201cLenore,\u201d she said with a strange, what I assumed to be forced smile. \u201cWhat can I do for you?\u201d\nI looked behind me. There was no Lenore. \u201cI\u2019m a bit confused ...\u201d I said. \u201cI seem to have\u2014\u201d\n\u201cExcuse me.\u201d Candy put up a hand. \u201cI mean I think we, here, are inundated.\u201d She dropped her towel on a counter and suddenly made a move, reached into the consoles of the little marble hallway and grabbed a fistful of cable and went around into the corridors, past the mirrors, behind the soft noise of a throbbing shower in the next room, cables trailing behind her. The cables trailed all the way to a reclining chair where Lenore was slumped with a towel over her eyes, reading a book with a soft-cover. Candy stopped in front of her and spoke. Lenore looked up and nodded. Candy made another move...", "0_9_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span> and she had stopped for just a second on the way downstairs to say hello to her son and now, her hand pulling the stair-carpet up as she went, hello, said Mrs Ramsay, getting up from the writing-table where she had been reading a card, in case it was William (it was not; it was only told her for the fifth time that if Jowett went up to London a Blue Ribbon would certainly be awarded him this term) it was getting serious, William must mind to profit by the Head Master's special interest in him, and She hoped there were not many like that on the stairs, said she, laughing, for there she drew it in. William's father had been out of temper; that scarf was knotted round her neck. She had not even put on her black coat. This was only her gardening coat. Surely it was not going to rain, she said. Of course it might stop Iris had kept the lodgers' breakfast waiting an hour now. Probably they would be starved. There was that additional butter today. The fish would be ruined if they were not careful. Ah, they would go. They were lunatics. They would go. And so, yes, she stooped to kiss him, but...", "0_23_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-five in the evening</span>. We had been gone from the car exactly thirty-seven minutes. I was on foot, and $FIRSTNAMEF4, $FIRSTNAMEB3, and myself all pressed for time agreed I should start walking back to the car alone to see if it were still there. In case it wasn't, I was to signal by flicking my flashlight. In case it was, I was to rush back and we would all three go to the flophouse and pay our bill and take off hurriedly in the car. Standing in the dark street, I felt a strange sadness and regret. I had no idea what it was all about. I was a little guy who had to go home and eat his toast in the morning. There was something about this car that got hold of our ankles and ankles like ours. I took one last look around. A man was resting his chin on the windowsill of a darkened bar on the comer. Smoke drifted in the lamplight. I could hear a jukebox playing the Ink Spots. \u201cMaybe the car isn't there,\u201d I said aloud and started down the street.\n\u201cWait!\u201d shouted $FIRSTNAMEF4, taking off her earrings and throwing them in the gutter where she figured we'd come...", "0_16_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>; the place was the town of San Narciso, California, some forty miles out of Los Angeles, and somewhere, but actually nowhere, downwind from a toxic-waste dump. The dump, the, Sanitation Autonomous Zone, consisted at the moment of a three-hundred-foot strip of rubble\u2014some of it dry rubble, some of it still too moist to climb\u2014rutted by trucks, clawed by bulldozers, perforated by the tracks of spray trucks, dotted with pools of brackish water and intransigent pools of dark blood, all smelling heavily and unwholesomely of phenol. From one area of dry rubble a thin orifice of steam issued, telling of the mild weed-fires the arsonists played with on the downwind side from time to time to discourage the attention of flies. The fires did discourage all human attention as well, though they did not\u2014 could not\u2014 affect the actual wind, which came sweeping through the Zone unimpeded, carrying with it the smell of what human beings, not God, had once made of the land.\nSuch then was the setting and such the time and place when909 North Ranch\u00ed came into view, moving toward San Narciso proper along the...", "1_16_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe Suburban Turbo-Eight continued on past the gate booths and police-vans of Paradice, toward the sanitarium itself, one hundred fifty yards farther on. The sanitarium looked, from a distance, like a huge brown stone castle, complete with towers and crenellations, surrounded by a moat and high, thick walls\u2014a hideous facade, actually, mostly structural steel, concrete, glass, and large painted plastic panels, with a fieldstone wall around the lower perimeter.\nActually, the modern Gothic sanitarium of the National Institute of Artistic Nonsense was one of the loveliest structures in Newport, if not in all New England. Like most gorgeous structures in Newport, it was unlivable, since it was surrounded by poison ivy, stinging nettles, giant hogweed, and imported Amazon mosquitoes, carried over in 1946 in the landing-gear wells of propeller-driven C-54\u2019s.\nNo walls, no moat, no imported mosquitoes surrounded the tiny stone cottage where the helio landed. Smoke issued from the chimney.\nThe helio whined down to rest, rocking on its shock absorbers. A minute later, a bedroom window was thrown open and an...", "2_7_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nBy this time, Titan was getting light at the rate of six times every forty-eight Earth hours. And Titan, like Uranus, was a world wrapped in perpetual darkness and gloom. There was only one significant source of light in all of Titan\u2019s atmosphere. And that light source was a tiny disk hardly bigger than the disk of the moon as seen from Earth.\nThis disk was Saturn.\nSaturn was thirty times bigger in apparent size than the Earth seen from Mars.\nThe tiny disk of light in Titan\u2019s atmosphere was only one-seventieth as big as the huge disk of light that was Saturn seen from Mars.\nA streetlight was playing over Salo\u2019s bed. His bed was a bushel basket with a pillow in it. He had rescued the pillow from the feather beds that $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s gang had stolen from Earth and dumped in a marsh on Titan.\nSalo\u2019s bed was filled with the bright blue material of which the huge banners were made that fluttered so eccentrically over everyone\u2019s rooftops on Titan. This material was called laughter \u2014 and the supply of it on Titan was practically inexhaustible.", "1_5_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was a corner of the space about eight feet square that constituted $SURNAME0\u2019s Estate.\nIt was somewhat stone-bumpy, this space. But neatly graveled, and treed also.\nThe main roof of the estate, and a minor roof of the space, was composed of a material that was at once strong, transparent and perfectly color-transmitting.\nThe foundation of the estate was of one continuous, fully welded and ground plate of stainless steel.\nEverything in sight, except the stainless-steel plate, was made of pararubber, waldrubber, aluminum, and heavily tinted glass and tinted plastic.\nA concrete walk bisected the woods and led from the airlock door of $SURNAME0\u2019s house to the grounded space ship.\nThe ship was seventeen feet long, had an airlock at both ends, and was cigar-shaped. It was painted a lustrous shade of blue-green reflective mylar.\nThe spaced-out sentry in the grounded ship, who paced back and forth at the business end of the bisecting walk, was Private Hector St. Bernard of the American Army. His haunches were spread and his head was turned in profile toward...", "2_15_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. Mr. Beadsman was partially deaf, because of the tiny explosions. He suddenly saw Lenore over the heads of Walinda Peahen and her children and looked away. Aaron Watches remained very still in Walinda\u2019s arms, staring brightly at shadows in the glass.\n\u201cWe did it again, y\u2019all, didn\u2019t we.\u201d\n\u201cWe sho did.\u201d\n\u201c \u2018Sho did.\u201d\n\u201cCan I sit for a minute, please? I\u2019m a little bit tired.\u201d\n\u201cYou bet.\u201d\n\u201cMay I please have some water for my sister over here?\u201d\n\u201cSho.\u201d\nThere were smells very red in the Console Area. There were new games, too, finally. Candy said a blue nuclear bomb that she stared at shot off little white fingers at her through the screen. \u201cThe more you run, the faster they go,\u201d she was muttering.\n\u201cWell I tell you.\u201d\n\u201cWe gonna have lunch, or what?\u201d\n\u201cExcuse mee ...\u201d", "1_10_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past ten o'clock in the morning</span>. Somewhere out there in the pleasant April sunshine a man was galloping in with a message for me. How could I know what the message would contain? One thing was certain: no matter what the news was; no matter if Wells, Horn, Attila the Hun and the Witch Queen of Endor were all entering Reno together, I would have to stand there, word perfect, and listen to it with eager attention and say nothing. That was the drill, and I would have to do it.\nEven if it was the police and they wanted the evidence in the slaughterhouse registered, I wouldn't be able to turn it over to them\u2014not right away. If I did, the deal would collapse and, without it, we would be back where we were two months ago. The noose would be tightening around our throats then, the County powers realigning, the big interests lining up to put us back in our Cripple Creek silo again. Now things would be different. We would be on the move; and this time there would be no stopping us, no matter what they did.\nThis thought calmed my fear, and I began to remember with amusement how green I was at acting. Ten minutes, twelve at the most, and all my...", "1_1_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-two in the morning</span>. The place was the living room of a spacious apartment in one of those grey Washington apartment buildings that give you the bottomless Depression feeling that there was once real estate speculation here and that it turned to dust. The apartment was three blocks from the Columbia Hospital for Women, where Lacerda had chosen to stop for a night and try to find a girl friend who worked there as a nurse, with no luck and some decidedly odd contacts. We had spent the last hour watching the Washington dawn slowly begin to erupt outside the image-glare of the aluminum Sony playboy table -table. On the table rested a quite large marijuana cigarette, which Wolfe was sitting with for the first time in his life, testing his theory that it didn\u2019t make him giggle; and a tape recorder on which I had just spent the last ten minutes recording a speech on the Drug Culture by Richard M. Nixon. As the dawn came up we watched it on the huge television set, whose remote controls were on the floor at our feet. We were waiting for something. We didn\u2019t know what. We just waited. Finally, it happened. The service conductor of the Washington dawn began to gently rap on the apartment door. \u201cShit,\u201d whispered Lacer...", "0_4_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-four in the morning</span>. I felt rotten, like it was all over for me. But then we went on drinking. \u201cOne more round!\u201d yelled $FIRSTNAMEB3, who by this time was so drunk he had fallen on the big, easy chair in a pile. Remi and I looked at each other. \u201cWhat the hell and finish him off.\u201d We rushed out and bought six quarts of beer. We decided to break all records and drink two beers apiece -- nine in all. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was barely conscious now; his tongue lolled out of his mouth and he couldn't talk. But he was ready for the beer; he whispered, \u201cAh fin\u201d and grabbed the bottles and gulped as hard as he could, they barely had an effect on him. I even took one to see what would happen and had a slight warm glow in my stomach. We all fell asleep in the living room. It was four-thirty, maybe. I was sitting in the armchair, leaning against the wall. I had my coat on, but no hat covering my burning head. I fell asleep. I woke up with a start; the sun was already high in the sky, the room white and dead. I wanted to stretch and wake up but...", "2_9_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past nine o'clock in the morning</span> when the great, green, droning Winnebago chugged over the Great Plain, failed to make the Newport Bridge, and came to rest in a stand of tall pines, not far from the $SURNAME0 town house.\nA man, a woman, and a boy tumbled out of the Winnebago.\nThe man had flaming red hair and a chin like a fist. His eyes were wild and blue. He was dressed in a swimming suit, Bermuda shorts, and a hacking jacket. His jacket was loud.\nThe boy was wearing the miniature replica of the loud jacket. His magnificent blond hair fell over his eyes, and the hair was as wet as though he had been diving. He was barefoot.\nThe woman was tall, beautiful, and strong. Her blue eyes were without warmth, and her mane of golden hair was cut like a plain school girl\u2019s. She, too, was wearing a hacking jacket, and her jacket was just as loud as the man\u2019s, though of a different color. Her chin and her jaw were as hard and clean as hewn marble. She said nothing.", "1_21_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span> and I was sitting in a corner with a glass of German white wine in front of me listening to the crowd and looking at the show. The music sounded very loud in the narrow street outside. Inside it was very cool and comfortable. The waiters moved quickly, carrying big trays. They were wearing white jackets. All of the waiters were big, powerful men. They all had big shoulders and big, roughened hands. Their hands looked as if a soft touch would make them bleed.\n[\u201cOrder a big, thick steak and French fried potatoes, and coffee as hot as a nut, and make it snappy.\u201d Red laughed softly. $FIRSTNAMEB5 laughed, wrinkled up her nose, and looked at Mike.]\nThe people were dancing. Everybody seemed to be dancing but us. The music played between the movements and quickened when the movement began. It was very solemn and passionate music. The musicians played against each other. Now they would all play together and the strings would enter at the shiny, hard rounded wooden notes of the wind instruments. Then the strings would enter and the horns would retreat, and all the clarinets, curdy and smooth, blending with the strings, and all the trumpets, smooth and curdy...", "0_17_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-three in the afternoon</span>.\nTwo nights had passed for the family since $SURNAME1\u2019s positive statement about the invitation he had received to the launches. On neither night had $SURNAME1 slept in his home.\nHe had slept in The Rodent\u2019s apartment.\nOn the first night, $SURNAME1 had arrived carrying a box of his belongings under his arm. He had sat down on the edge of a chair in The Rodent\u2019s living room. He said he was sorry to bother The Rodent, but he was desperate. He was dependent on The Rodent for help. The open box on the chair held his last few belongings. He had nowhere else to go.\nThe Rodent put on a heavy brown bathrobe and rubbed his face with one hand. His alarm clock read half past three. \"What you need for now, boy?\" he said, \"and what you expect from me later on?\"\n\"Anything you can do,\" said $SURNAME1, \"Willy, I need.\"\n\"Alright,\" said The Rodent, \"get your puny behind into bed.\"\n$SURNAME1 stood. \"Just like that?\" he said. \"You\u2019re not going to make me beg?\"\n\"Why should I make a man beg who\u2019s made...", "0_17_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span> on December 24, 2000 in the pacific standard time zone and it was eight o'clock in the morning in Hawaii, which was fifteen hours behind, so that if one imagined the globe as a kind of crystal ball on a stand, Hawaii would be on the opposite side and it would look like a reflection in the wrong place. On this crystal ball was what appeared to be a family. A man and a woman sat on a white sofa that resembled a cloud. The woman had her legs tucked up under her and was holding something in her arms, rocking it. The man sat watching her. It was a tiny television set, tuned to a channel with test patterns. It was plugged into a wall socket, and it had cuddled up next to its mother. There was a white telephone on the table by the sofa, and a Cosmo on the table by the arm of the sofa, and two glasses and a decanter, and a lamp, and various bits of junk on the floor, which was carpeted in cloud-white shag like the carpet in the room containing the white desk withthe wasp on it, except that this carpet was at least twenty times as thick, like fur. The carpet clung to a honeycomb of dielectric...", "0_0_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-five ante meridiem</span>.\nI felt wonderful. I was bathed in a fine sweat. My eyes sparkled and I felt really pretty well on top of things.\nA bright idea suddenly popped into my head. I could take this .357 Magnum revolver to my public library every day, put it under my coat and walk quietly into the reading room. It made an awful bulge in the bathrobe, but the outlaw couldn\u2019t see it.\nI grinned. This thing had possibilities.\nThe desk clerk was looking at me funny now. I had to calm it down. I wrote a check for eightyfive dollars, without hesita\u00adtion. My only problem, now, was that I had forgotten to bring along a bag. This would make matters very difficult. I would berd to stuff 8500 dollars in twenties into a bathrobe pocket.\nI grinned at the desk clerk, standing there wondering if I was crazy. He relaxed a little. I would have to get out of here fast, now.\n\u201cHave a busy day?\" I asked pleasantly.\n\u201cIt depends,\" said the clerk nervously, \u201con how you figure it.\"\n\u201cWell,\u201d I said, \u201ca good hotel like this is bound to...", "0_20_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>, according to the clocks everywhere\u2014the tall, thin, three-dimensional clocks, set in lacquered wood and balanced on slender iron poles, that could be found almost anywhere on Mars, so far from home.\nThey marched down a shallow declivity to Cliff Dweller\u2019s Avenue, which was the main street of $FIRSTNAMEB2. This street was eighty-four feet above the ground on its eastern end, but it sloped upward as it stretched westward. At the extremity of the street, arching across its exit onto the plain beyond, stood the wall of the western crater.\nCliff Dweller\u2019s Avenue was also known as $SURNAME0\u2019s Walk, in honor of $SURNAME1\u2019s adventurous father. $SURNAME0 the Magnificent had been the first man to use the street. He had arrived on Mars moments before his son.\nThe Walk had a cheering effect on the Pan Galactic Potentates who were meant to be so awed by the Martian milieu. It gave them a lift to see something that belonged peculiarly to Earthling man. It was something written in the snows of Olympus, superior to anything in the code of the space-faring races.\nThe gilded swinging doors of...", "2_2_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-two in the morning</span>. The night was still whirling.\nThere were birds on the window of the East Cleveland rest stop.\n/d/\n\u201cAnd this is my own particular dentist, Dr. Rance L. Snidwin.\u201d\n\u201cHow do you do, Dr. Howell.\u201d\n\u201cPleasure. Melinda told me you have a house we might be interested in.\u201d\n\u201cI do. It\u2019s not as convenient to the campus as some I have but it\u2019s spacious and, to my mind, classic. You see this narrow lot size? The house is very much wider than it is deep. Like a typical Ohio farmhouse.\u201d\n\u201cPardon me, but that appears to be two Pams working the phones back there.\u201d\n\u201cYes you\u2019re right. Very clever of you to notice.\u201d\n\u201cAre you two related? Are they your daughters?\u201d\n\u201cNot at all. I\u2019m afraid I\u2019ve never married. These two women work for me. They\u2019re actually former patients of mine.\u201d\n\u201cFormer patients?\u201d", "0_4_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-two in the morning</span>. The sun was dying over the Pacific. It seemed like the end of everything. No one spoke, but all looked shattered. It was hard to believe that in eight hours more hard sunlight would pour out of the Hankow sky. Naked and emotionally beaten, but still in need of booze and hash, we made a quiet getaway -- and took the gloomy ferry back to Chinally. It was a terrible thing, that crossing. Not a word was spoken. $FIRSTNAMEB3 drove. The radio gave the baseball scores of LA and I sagged against the rail at the stern and wondered if Billy Chase was sleeping. The scent of salt water and the summertime sadness of the endless stream sank into my stomach. \u201cThis is the wonderful West Coast, this is the land I knew!\u201d I wanted to yell. Instead I went to sleep like everybody else. We pulled up in front of Inez's house. There was no traffic at this hour. I carried my bag up to the bedroom without making a sound and passed Inez asleep in bed with Bick. I undressed and sprawled by her and slept. But the weary woes of travel wake you up at the strangest hours. Half an hour later I woke up thinking it was morning -- and strange faces...", "2_16_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-eight in the afternoon</span>, and $FIRSTNAMEF2 had been in the rest room of the bar for six minutes. She worked her way through the swelling mob around the juke box, back to her table and another drink.\nA scrawny guy in an open belted raincoat had come in and was cutting in on the dancing couples to blab No Vietnam War ever, yeah, that\u2019s the ticket, good buddy. He had his own portable mike, the little swine. One of his mates, fat and bearded, came in later and began collecting money from the dancers. The bartender at first refused to serve him, then lost interest. S As soon as $FIRSTNAMEF2 sat down, medium tall and balding and drunk, with a gut starting, sidled up to her and began inquiring if she was a patriot. He was also pushing something that looked terribly like a small, overdeveloped harmonica. Shrilled to discover she was not only a lady, but aboard his \u201cfreedom bus,\u201d itself a way station on the Underground Railroad for draft dodgers, deserters and other transgressors, he requested she visit the rear of the vehicle.", "1_23_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> of the first day on Earth of the expedition from Mars.\nThe shocktroops in the space suits were not really shocktroops at all. They were a section of $SURNAME0\u2019s tedious personal guard\u2014a bodyguard that had been drilled for years in rendering ridiculously ornamental and non-military obeisance.\nThe commander of these troops now was a commoner named $FIRSTNAMEB1. His real name was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, but he habitually disguised himself, even from himself, by using the name of hisUncrowned King.\nTen minutes before the salute given by $FIRSTNAMEB1 to the sleeping Martian, the command had come through $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s receiver to execute this elaborate and silly ceremony. That is to say, the command had not come through the receiver in $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s space suit. It had come through the receiver in the head of $SURNAME0\u2019s dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2014which had bitten $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s leg when $FIRSTNAMEB1 had first climbed into his space suit.\n$SURNAME0 was now back inside his mansion on the far edge of the Mars-side jungle\u2014and was watching the whole scene on television. He was doing this from an upstairs window by means of an extension telephone...", "2_8_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe second shift of workers began to drift in from the barracks. The gate on the outside wall was opened, and a steady stream of battered VW buses and battered, lead-belted British sports cars flowed through it and up the gravel road to the main gate of the Complex. They were driven by people between the ages of 22 and 32.\nThey were almost exclusively male.\nThey were very clean and well dressed, and they wore the uniform of the times: the Levi jacket, Levi bells, the Scotch-plaid lumberjack shirt.\nThe mill was going to be run by young people.\nAll the soldiers and executives of the DWM and of its wholly-owned subsidiary, Genaturama Pacific, Ltd., were over forty years old.\nThe new workers were to be the typists, mail clerks, telephone operators, radio-teletype operators\u2014essentially, marginal people who neither create nor produce nor handle heavy goods nor perform heavy manual labor.\nThey reported to work in the great hall in eight-person shifts, alternating with eight-person shifts of workers on their way home to the barracks. There were 468 workers in the hall at any one time.", "2_19_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-two in the evening</span>.\nThe sun did not rise until six nineteen the next morning.\nAbout history:\nHistory is and always has been entirely written by victors. This accounts for the strange discontinuities in it, as where one war suddenly and without motivation spills into another slightly different war. In a world of continuous and pervasive warfare, there are continuous and virtually pervasive watersheds.\nUntil a watershed is reached, there is motivation for fightingall the way to a painful and terrifying Pyrrhic victory.\nHaving reached the watershed, and having won the most terrifying of all Pyrrhicvictories, a soldier in such a war might look around with a certain puzzlement at the foemen he has killed. He would see in the faces of the fallen the same old human faces he had seen before battle. The soldier would be ill-prepared, as a historian, to account for the discontinuity that had crept into the war, for his or its participation in all the bloody racial or nationalistic emotion leading to it. What kind of discontinuity? The soldier could only say that he had suddenly, blindingly, and overwhelmingly hated the faces of the foemen.", "0_7_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-six in the morning</span>. I picked up my paper bag, grabbed my flight bag and Canastos, and started across the terminal. The ranks of chairs, long since deserted, now looked like the battle\u2014torn wards in some terminal hospital No one was hurt, but the thousands of casualties had been heavy. I saw a little pool of vomit, charred remains of a joint, a cellular phone like the one I ditched in Stockton, a fallen Walkman wired to a Walkman with some guy s skull. It had been a rough night.\nI left air terminal throuac of twoandwent down to hailing a cab. I was wearing rubber\u2014soled white desert boots and a captaincy of Sportaflold beautifully around my waist in a white terry\u2014cloth robe. My hair looked as if it had been cut with razors. My olive\u2014colored skin was gunmetal black from two days\u2019 exposure in the mountains and my eyes were shrieky from fatigue, sun and red wine. When the first cabby approached me, I said: \u201cGet away from me, you lowlife scum sucker, or I'll cut your fucking heart out.\u201d The veins were sticking out on my neck. The guy backed away, his...", "1_21_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past nine o'clock in the evening</span>. I had to go down the old woman's street to find my way with the rest of the pilgrims to the Caronia. I watched them from the dark. They were gathered outside the inn. The dumb boy stood akimbo in front of them. The old men were in a group together. You could see them only dimly. MacDougal was there in a big flannel shirt. He was waving his arms and talking.\nThey went down the street in a bunch. Inside the inn everything was greasy and winking yellow light. There was the smell of old men and poverty and pots. Everybody sat at the long table, and there were some women. Nobody was eating. The cheese smelled like putrefaction.\nWake up!\u201d said somebody in my ear. I woke up and there were the same pilgrims. The old lady was nodding. At last MacDougal stood up and shouted, \u201cFriends and fellow-pilgrims, tonight we enter upon the second half of our journey.\u201d He wiped his mouth. \u201cTonight marks the end of the first stage and the beginning of the second stage of our pilgrimage. We go on to Montserrat!\u201d Everybody cheered and whistled. The pilgrims...", "0_20_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-one in the evening</span>.\nI would go see a dirty movie, I decided. And then I would go to the Poor Chef and have a good steak, and then back to the room to watch television and then go to bed and read for a couple of hours.\nPoor $FIRSTNAMEB3 had been gone over four hours.\nPoor $FIRSTNAMEB3 has been my brother and my leader and my one-time idol for five years. Perhaps I'll never see him again. Now that he's found Camille, it's probably all over between us. One characteristic of $FIRSTNAMEB3, especially at the end of our many adventures in the past, was a sad resignation and realization that he'd never see me again; even though a moment before he was swearing eternal friendship. \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, I'll never see you again. It's better this way.\u201d\nThat July in Japan he said, \u201c $FIRSTNAMEB6, probably I'll never see you again.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean, man?\u201d I said. We were on a bus to northern Japan. \u201cAoh, I don't know, probably I'll go to India, and you'll die in America.\u201d\n\u201cNonsense,\u201d I said irritably. \u201cWhy, we've...", "1_0_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. We had begun the big push, the so-called Fourth Level into Space in the spring and summer of 1951.\nThe first level had been into Earth orbit.\nThe second level had been into Lunar orbit and back.\nThe third and last level had consisted of the landing and take-off again of our incompetent gallant friend, $FIRSTNAMEB0. Now, according to rumor, we were about to enter an entirely new realm\u2014the realm of interplanetary travel.\nThe rumor was a true one.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had been sent to Mars, much against his will, to gain firsthand knowledge of any funny business that might be going on up there.\nAnd he had gained that firsthand knowledge\u2014 a firsthand knowledge of a nasty little war going on between an enemy utterly unknown, and an Earthling garrison made up of recruits like $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nAnd now $FIRSTNAMEB0, stationed on Mars as a sort of insubordinate watchdog, was being given the opportunity to return to Earth with this firsthand knowledge. The dispatches announcing this chance arrival spoke of \"unexpectedly favorable atmospheric conditions.\" This was bureaucrat-speak for the real, not-so-favorable reason that $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his rocket ship could make it back from Mars.\nThe atmosphere of...", "2_5_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-seven in the morning</span>. I rushed into the bedroom for one last look around, and as I stood there my foot nudged something under the bed. I bent to look. It was my suitcase of drugs. For the last time I dragged it out from under there and snapped it open . . . and found it all gone. That did it. I sat on the bed and moaned in despair. I refused to believe this. Dropped acid, almost certainly, somewhere along the line, but there was no way to know when or where. Here in Vegas, definitely; probably in my house; possibly in the bathroom at the airport; possibly even on the American Airlines plane, when that little beauty made two passes around the terminal at Dulles.", "2_23_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to midnight</span>.\nThe baby slept.\nThe drumming continued.\nAt five minutes to midnight, the telephone rang, three times, paused, and rang twice again, paused, and rang once. This was the baby\u2019s granduncle calling to ask if the baby was sleeping. Granduncle was a clock collector, and the father consulted him occasionally about matters of appraisal and authenticity. The drumming continued.\nAt four minutes to midnight, the father got up to see what was making the drumming noise. The father had barely gotten out of his rumpled hospital bed and put on his bathrobe. When he walked over to the baby\u2019s crib, his feet, visible to him in the light of the green nightlight plugged into the wall socket beside the crib, were the feet of somebody else. Somebody very old and weak. The father slowly took out the thermometer he kept in the drawer of his bedside table. He put it under his tongue and leaned over the crib. He saw, in the green light, a red, distorted image of himself making anxious gestures along the railing of the crib.\nThe baby was not there.\nThe drumming was coming from the bathroom.", "0_21_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine eighteen in the evening</span> when we left San Juan. The night was beautiful, the sky full of stars, the sea calm. We had a pleasant dinner to the music of an itinerant guitarist. After dinner there was some dancing. Women come to tee on the boat in the evening and the dancing and singing start. The women know all the songs. They sing in low tones, sadly and tranquilly. You see their great earrings aglow in the dark when they toss their heads. Their black hair, thrown back, makes them look like Indian women. Their faces are drawn tight in the hard, poor life, and their faces are waxen in the lit-up places under the stars. I was hoarse from all the singing, and we mounted to our roo in the bow. We were alone in the bow. The sound of the singing followed us up. I lay in the hammocks and looked at the stars, and Bill lay beside me.\nWe were carrying one hundred and fifty casks of water in the ship. The stoutest of the men had put their luggage and their cots on top of the casks. A leak sprang in the ship and made a pool under our bed. Bill and I had paid a dollar to sleep in a clean bed...", "0_20_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>. Salo, having waited in vain for Stoney for an hour, was on his way to the space ship. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s collision suit clicked to a stop beside him.\n\"Good evening, friend,\" said Salo. \"I wish you were my friend, friend, friend,\" he added sadly.\n\"You ain\u2019t goin\u2019 without me\u2014are you, Salo?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"I\u2019m afraid so, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said Salo. \"I\u2019m sorry\u2014but it might be dangerous if you came, $FIRSTNAMEB0.\"\n\"Dangerous for me or for you, Salo?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Where you go, I go. You can\u2019t get along without me now, Salo, and you know it.\"\n\"I can try, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said Salo sadly. \"How\u2019s your friend? Is the friend coming, too?\"\n\"My friend is sick,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Hell, I don\u2019t know\u2014about him coming along. Does that space ship of yours take sick people?\"\n\"Just sick at heart, I should imagine, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said Salo. \"What\u2019...", "0_21_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-three in the evening</span> and our ship was on its way out of New York Harbor. The Hudson was black and the Manhattans flicked their lights on and off in sync with everything else. We peeled off our clothes and rolled them into a ball and put them under our heads and turned toward the Sea and Eternity. \u201cThis is the life!\u201d cried Vic. The water burbled under our stern. A steamer went by and kicked up white spray that rained down on us. Traffic-lights along the shore flickered and a few horns honked and echoed. The pilot's launch went burbling by and faded out. \u201cAh, bless!\u201d cried $FIRSTNAMEB3 and raised his arm toward the darkening sky above the harbor and the farthest provinces on the continent. \u201cCross ATLANTIC OCEAN via Colon ATLANTIC OCEAN to? ATLANTIC OCEAN ! Ah ? Bless ? That? Old? Atlantic ? Ah !\u201d His head fell back. He lay on the deck breathing like a locomotive. I was the watchman; I leaned on my elbow and brooded; we were that far from Europe and half an hour from real night and real stars. Nobody in Europe knew where we were or what we...", "2_7_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"There\u2019s a man on Mercury,\" said $SURNAME0 conversationally, \"whose job it is to count ten thousand chimes.\" He winked genially at $SURNAME1. \"And he\u2019s still counting.\"\n\"Sounds like a good job for a friend of mine,\" said $SURNAME1 nastily.\n\"Oh?\" said $SURNAME0. \"Who would that be? $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, perhaps?\"\n\"That\u2019s him,\" said $SURNAME1. \"I sure wish he was here now. He could pick out the good guys from the bad guys just by looking at \u2019em.\"\n\"Your friend would be right, you know,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Of course,\" said $SURNAME1. He spat brownly on the sundial\u2019s gnomon. \"You can always tell a man by the company he keeps.\"\n\"Don\u2019t forget your equipment,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"What equipment?\" said $SURNAME1.", "2_7_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after seven o'clock in the morning</span> on mars. The man in the motorcyclist\u2019s suit was standing knee-deep in a pond on Tralfamadore.\nHe was looking into the waters of the pond.\nThe waters were still and clear.\nIn their depths was an image of an Earthling.\nThe Earthling was standing in a farmyard in England, struggling to pull a heavy ball over a fence, with crowds cheering him on.\nThe Universe is everything that ever has been or ever will be.\nIt is in this sense that Tralfamadore is the Universe; that Tralfamadorians are well advised when visiting Earth to travel light, to bring along nothing but their \u201ctalents,\u201d their sense of irony, their appreciation of the iron in the Universal substance, and their almost unimaginable talents for living and loving.\nThe Tralfamadorian homed in on only those objects in the Universe worth tucking inside his shirt.\nThe talismanic Universe-stuff carried by the man in the motorcyclist\u2019s suit was his perfect memory of his wife and child.", "0_18_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty in the evening</span>; only twelve minutes late. I felt much better. \u201cWhy do they put on all those chains when they know the truck is only going twelve miles?\u201d I asked.\n\u201cStandard procedure,\u201d he said.\nWe picked up another hitchhiker, a student from Berkeley. He got in the back seat. \u201cNever get in back,\u201d said my attorney. \u201cAlways try to get up front with the driver.\u201d We had a long argument about this hitchhiking business. My attorney's only ride had come when a truck driver offered him a lame horse for seventy-five cents. The front seat gave you prestige; it also gave you the best chance to get the bum's rush when you talked too much, which, in some trucks, could be often. The kids, who have discovered in the American desert their true home and natural territory, stick to the back seat.\n\u201cAll this of course is for the bad northern climate,\u201d he said, \u201cwhere you have to keep the door shut most of the time and there's no lounging in the sun. Here we can get a good fan going and still stick our faces out in the breeze.\u201d I agreed with...", "2_20_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nIt was a balmy evening, with only a mattress of cloud on the Coney Island sky, through which the blue field ofMars shone brightly. It was a romantic setting for a wild fiction, and $SURNAME0 acted the operatic role to the hilt. Here he was, a spectacularly handsome and virile man, holding a wrought-iron chair for the huge bosomed mistress of his mansion\u2014a man who hadn\u2019t spoken three words to her since his arrival fourteen Earthling days before\u2014a man who hadn\u2019t much expected to see her again that night. He watched her pat her hair, take a deep breath, and descend the three stone steps to the garden. He followed along behind, carrying her shawl and her handbag, the contents of which he knew well. They had been packed in his pocket since he awakened that morning.\n\"Oh,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 as she reached the flagstones, \"you aren\u2019t having one of your little jokes again?\"", "2_5_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five o'clock in the morning</span>. O.J. Kennedy and his new wife were trying to walk up a staircase inside the Trade Mart. Photographers crouched on both sides, clubbing their cameras with enormous flashlights.\nO.J. staggered against the wall. Jackie stood alone with the big mike packs around her neck like electronic pit\u2014bulls.\nUp on the roof, helicopter pilots waited around, taking work apart and examining it. Downstairs in that dark, sluggish ocean, a human being named Earl Warren was trying to swim towards freedom.\nThe Paranoid Style in American Politics...and When the Secret Service Came Around...The Houston Conspiracy, Operation Rumrunner and the G Fat Fiasco...The End of the Innocence\nIcouldn\u2019t sleep. I had small but definite motorcycle\u2014type stoned feelings all through my body, and my brain was red\u2014alert on LSD. I kept seeing dollar signs all around me. Gold trays, silver trays and dominoes kept falling at every hour. Laces on boots became major items of interest and worry. And overhead sunbeams punched through the darkness like godly arrows, reminding me that what we were doing was done in the Sun.", "0_10_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty in the morning</span>. I was wondering what to do with myself for the next three hours when the deskman announced over the house p.a. system, \"Mr. Chinaski, please call the manager\u2019s office.\"\nThe manager was an obese young man named Alf. \"Sit down, Mr. Chinaski,\" he started, \"this concerns Mrs. Christine White. She is an elderly lady of sixty-three. She came to this hotel two weeks ago. She has been staying here ever since.\"\n\"Her money\u2019s all right,\" I said.\n\"Oh yes, it\u2019s all right. I have the check myself. The point is\u2014what do you do when something happens like what happened here?\"\n\"What happened?\"\n\"Mrs. White checked out this morning with a man, and she hasn\u2019t come back.\"\n\"What\u2019s the matter with the man?\"\n\"Nothing that I know of. Except he\u2019s sixty-four years old. He checked in two days before Mrs. White.\"\n\"And he\u2019s still here.\"\n\"Right.\"\n\"What do you want to do?\"\n\"We fully realizing, Mr. Chinaski, that this is...", "0_18_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>. The flamboyant green vegetation of the planet of Mercury shimmered in the heat. The sun grew larger and redder, the shadows wavered, crisscrossed the plain toward the palace.\nThere was no wind. The flags of the palace drooped and then lay still.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, standing in his stirrups, was ten feet tall, a handsome man in his late thirties, a hero. Exquisite gold-threaded blue cloth covered his huge, muscled body. Golden filigree showed briefly at his wrists and ankles. His black chest hair was an intricate network, casting weltering shadows on the cloth.\nHis face, though handsome, was decadent.\nHis eyes were heavy, his cheeks swam with fat.\nHis most remarkable feature\u2014a fissure ran from the left side of his chin down to the notch of his decade-old broken nose. It was a plato, the characteristic facial disfigurement of the exalted. By folding the lip over the fissure, a Martian could assume a false profile that was pleasing\u2014or could flop the lip into the fissure, revealing the profile of a truly wise being who had witnessed every horror and learned every truth...", "1_14_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\n\"Curfew!\" said a messenger. \"Curfew!\" Nobody cheered. Nobody cared. The great gesture had been made. The grandstands began to empty out. They were being mowed down by loudspeakers that bellowed:\n\"Attention! All military personnel report to your commanding officer! All civilian personnel report to the nearest air-raid shelter!\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 wandered away from the box, limping and dazed. They made their lame way between the grounded ships of the Earthling forces.\nTheir feet clanged on the bare walkways of the parking-field.\nTheir feet made the only noise in the gigantic desolation of the fog.\nTheir feet clanged against the propellers of six two-man helicopters parked in a row. Their pilots were found under the bench seats of their helicopters, unconscious from food poisoning.\nThe propellers of the parked helicopters had chewed off their toes.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 gained their barracks. They found the rank of their outfit reduced by half through sickness and casualties. Only eleven of their original twenty-six were on their feet.\nThe eleven were playing cards in a room full of puke. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were welcomed back with the silence that...", "2_23_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after eleven o'clock in the evening</span> on the planet Mercury, and $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright lay in bed in the dark, listening to what his father said, and sometimes laughing. $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright's cat lay on the beside table, and licking her fur. The table was ancient, and had been made in Wiltshire, England, in 1650. $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright had paid one hundred and five dollars for it.\nAt times $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright felt a pain in his pit of his stomach.\nThere was a pattern of circles and squares on the wall in the dark, and a part of the wash-bowl showed, glistening.\n\"What's that strange-looking thing you been setting on our roof?\" said Joe Fernwright.\n\"You know what it is,\" said Al Fernwright. \"It\u2019s that retting machine. Business is growing, and was spreading me too thin, and so I had it built.\"\n\"Why\u2019d you pick out a funny-looking shape like that one for?\" said Joe Fernwright.\n\"Because to retain the natural look of the product,\" said Al Fernwright, \"and to save on material. Why should we go to the expense of making things look like what they ain\u2019t?\"", "0_4_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-nine in the morning</span>. A warm rain-mist fell uponthe Thunder Road. I found myself strangely excited. After all, this was something like a true motor race\u2014and the first real mud\u2014race, too. It was like a Histoic Auto Club event at Sebring\u2014on four washes of acid.\nBut no matter. No matter. It seemed appropriate. The last twenty\u2014four hours had been continuously interesting. Very little sleep and all of it haunted by strange dreams, near disasters and wild excess.\nAnd this would be some kind of culmination\u2014to streak down a twelve\u2014lane freeway in the early dawn, with Dinah-Moe and her Beautiful Butchers Up Front, and the acid master himself barely hanging on in the Whip Socket.\nThe highway was empty except for a few scattered cars with lights off, left by people who couldn\u2019t make it through the checkpoint. At check\u2014point one, a Mean Old Man rejected the car of the radio\u2014station owner who had put on the race. We stopped and had a long talk with the rejected one. He said he\u2019d been in Las Vegas a few months, bought this race /entrance permit and convinced a few people in Phoenix to come in on the deal...", "2_17_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe sun was seventeen millions of miles from Earth, and the tentacles of night were being unrolled like the sections of a telescope until they reached from the sun to a limestone floor in a little church on Selma, Ohio.\nAt the same time, a ship was in the sky, on its way to Mercury.\nAt the same time, a man walked down a leaf-strewn path to a little white church on East 46th Street in New York City.\nThe windows of the church were dark. A sign on the door said: IN CONFERENCE. It was Monday afternoon, and the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent did not meet on Mondays.\nThe man rang the bell and waited, and then opened the unlocked door and walked in.\nThe conference in the church consisted of a man sitting in a pew and reading a magazine. He looked surprised when $FIRSTNAMEB0 came in.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 approached him timidly. \"Excuse me, friend\u2014\" he said.\n\"Y-yes?\" said the man. \"Oh\u2014\" his mouth dropped open. \"You\u2019re $FIRSTNAMEB0, aren\u2019t you?\" he said.", "2_2_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>. In every distant Temple there resounded a music as of a bell as terrible and as sweet as has ever pealed to announce the coming of day\u2014day in the sky, daybreak in the minds of men.\nWhat follows here is not to be found in any book. O, beware! It is a prophecy. It is not to be found in any book because the book that would contain it has not yet been written.\nAnd the water rose\u2014and the water fell. And there was a mound of quiet in the sea, and the ships that had veered off sailed bravely in, and most dangerously anchored in the swell that was going down. The water rose\u2014and the water fell. And the lifeboats put away, and were rowed bravely in, and most dangerously beached on the swells that were going down.", "1_6_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>, and the clocks were striking. High up in a steeple of the Palace of Astronomy was an Infant who had just begun to play a voluntary on a trumpet. The sound of the trumpet came down to Iss Iss, and the sound swelled, and rose higher, and spread all over the town.\nIss Iss was sleeping on the broad steps of the museum, peacefully and naturally. A line, like a stroke of lightning, shot through her dreaming mind, and she was awake. She listened. Yes! She sprang to the sky, she spread out her hands, the palms uppermost, like wings. The voluntary ended. A chime rang out, six strokes up in the steeple. She knelt and kissed the steps that she had been sleeping on.\n\"History!\" she murmured. \"History, awake!\" And she began to weep.\nShe wept for joy at the thought of the long, long story of mankind. All those souls and bodies, all those lives and deaths, that could never be recounted-she wept to think of them all. Then she settled to her study of the day, and began to watch the people arriving. They came into the square, and stood about, and shifted from one foot to another...", "0_17_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to six o'clock in the evening</span> that $FIRSTNAMEB0 went crazy. It was beautiful to watch $FIRSTNAMEB0 go crazy\u2014even though the reason for $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s going crazy was that his wife was dead. For when $FIRSTNAMEB0 went crazy, the truly interesting thing was that he didn\u2019t just go crazy. He made patterns!\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s commanding officer, the one with the big mustache, said that he had never seen anything like $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s going crazy. That was remarkably true, for $FIRSTNAMEB0 had never seen anything like $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s going crazy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was dizzy. He imagined that he was turning in circles. Then, in his giddiness, he discovered that he could make a circle with his dizziness, could make a circle of whirling arms, could make a circle of dancing, stamping feet.\nHis commanding officer tried to imitate $FIRSTNAMEB0. His commanding officer couldn\u2019t make a circle with his dizziness. He staggered on, big beads of sweat standing out on his big, drooping mustache. He felt he ought somehow to be like $FIRSTNAMEB0. He felt he ought somehow to be able to make patterns. He didn\u2019t know why he didn\u2019t know how.\nThen he threw...", "1_1_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span> when we arrived at the Desert Inn. The crowd in the lobby all gave us a big smile and a big hello, thinking we were important thrown\u2014together people. We picked up our key and had to wait five minutes for an assistant manager; then very hesitantly he came across the red\u2014carpeted lobby while we stood with our arms loaded with raccoon coats, raccoon boots, raccoon rings and raccoon bags, and waited with happy smiles on our faces.\nThe assistant manager said, \u201cI see you have quite a party load, I hope you're all staying in this hotel,\u201d and we said yes. \u201cWell, uh ... we have no room in this hotel for people who are not staying here but for fifty dollars cash I can get you a room in the Club tower across the way and I can personally guarantee it's the finest suite in that hotel and I believe the only available suite there at the moment.\u201d And Ed said, \u201cJust the suite we want, let's go,\u201d and he turned around to show somebody the suite in that tower, apparently, and started carrying coats, and I grabbed the keys and ran around and helped him with the bags and grabbed a handful of cigars off...", "0_7_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-four in the morning</span>. My attorney had disappeared in the middle of the night\u2014probably off to Las Vegas with the original tire iron\u2014so it looked like I would have to face the raging hordes at Circus\u2014Circus by myself. I was well on my way to serious psychosis. Fear and loathing had left the building.\nCircus\u2014Circus itself is a cross\u2014hatched\u2014ligament of what they like to call fun\u2014way madness; a sprawling red\u2014white\u2014and\u2014blue nowhere\u2014land of what will soon be, if our elected sludge\u2014heads get their way, a permanent blow\u2014circus like no other on this god\u2014forsaken bucolic isle.\nAnd somewhere in the middle of this sordid mess they have managed to incorporate a genuine, bona\u2014fide American carnival, a classic traveling freak\u2014show that comes and goes as it pleases, dealing only in red\u2014blooded American verities like The Fun House, The Wild Animal Show and, of course, The Hall of the Dwarfs, an eighteen\u2014meter\u2014wide and handsomely appointed exhibit that proved to be, for me at least, the single most terrifying entity in the entire CarnivalCircus menagerie.\nAh, yes. Fourteen...", "1_4_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe Titanic's band stopped playing. It was quite silent in the saloons and corridors. Outside, it was still raining.\nThe dawn broke behind the Titanic. It broke through the clouds that had hidden the sky from midnight till this minute.\nThe Titanic was still plunging ahead on her course. But the deck no longer rolled. The engines had stopped.\nCHAPTER ONE\nCHAPTER ONE\nI\u2014MR. AND MRS. SENIOR\nII\u2014MR. AND MRS. JUNIOR\nIII\u2014MR. AND MRS. THOMAS W. BRYDEN\nIV\u2014PARTIES BY DAY\nV\u2014TOWARDS THE FUTURE\nCHAPTER ONE\nMR. AND MRS. SENIOR\n\"Darling,\" said Mrs. Lucy Wellborne, \"we should have been married to-day.\"\nShe was a huge woman. The skin of her face was translucent, soft, poreless, perfect in color. Her eyes were as remote and as splendid as moons glimpsed through tropic foliage. Her mouth was wide, her lips very heavy\u2014colorless. Her body was huge by terrestrial standards, but it was slender and firm as might have been a statue carved to represent a woman well beyond...", "1_11_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-two in the morning</span>. It was hot. Rented taxicabs formed a merry-go-round in front of the air-terminal building. In every fourth or fifth cab there was a driver trying to outtalk a clock on the taximeter. Metered fares to places of density had to be reckoned in advance. Nothing around the Newport Hotel would cost more than three bucks an hour.\n\"Where to?\" I asked, getting in.\n\"To the Mint Hotel, on Narragansett,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB3. The driver gave him a look in the rear-view mirror.\n\"Say, fella, where are you from?\"\n\"New York.\"\n\"All ri-i-ight! Well?\"\n\"The airport.\"\n\"Well so what?\" He turned his whole body around, big soulful eyes filling the mirror. We were off.\n\"Now listen -- let's discuss this.\" Long silence. \"It is admittedly an inconvenient distance, but the meter is running all the same.\" We had already run over eleven bucks. \"Yessir, I know what it is -- you've been sleeping on that goddamn plane and now you're dead tired and everything looks bad to you -- but face the facts: one way to the Mint Hotel...", "2_12_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-six p.m.</span>\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 returned to the stone ranch house for a surprise inspection by Sergeant Brackett. All the soldiers passed surprise inspections flawlessly. Since their brains were put into the mouths at the recruiting stations, they had never been taught or drilled in the art of being surprised at inspections.\nThey were horrified at the thought of being surprised at inspections.\nThey did everything they were supposed to do.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 ate their k rations, killed their harmless and beneficial animals, cleaned their weapons, waxed their boots. They answered jointly when Brackett demanded, \"What\u2019s your favorite color ?\"\n\"Red,\" they said somberly.\nThey cleaned their weapons three times.", "2_7_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Good luck to you,\" said $SURNAME1 to the old man.\nThe old man didn\u2019t answer. He was watching the space ship in another way\u2014watching for hostility and any sign at all of a precise awareness of what he was and what and where he was. He could see no such signs. The space ship was as mindless and as precise as a celluloid Hollywood prop.\nIt was five minutes until eight o\u2019clock in the morning.\n$SURNAME1 left the wall, mounted the stone steps, tapped at the door.\nThe door was opened by $SURNAME0 again. But there was no luminous inscription over the door.\n\"I have to go in there and space again, very shortly,\" said $SURNAME0. \"I have to catch that ship and force it to land where I can talk to its occupants.\"\nHe looked around, saw the Hozomeen miner standing by his pick-up in the named shadow of the church wall.\n\"Shall we go back inside while you tell me what message you\u2019re to deliver?\" said $SURNAME0. He shut the door behind himself and $SURNAME1.", "2_1_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-five in the morning</span> when the materialization shot $SURNAME1 over Saturn.\n\"All\u2019s well\u2014\" $SURNAME1 mumbled as he floated.\nThen he frowned, coughed, opened his eyes. He was looking down into the dark, bottomless throat of a volcano.\nHe dropped like a stone into the volcano, lost consciousness, and materialized on Tralfamadore.\n\"Good evening,\" said the messenger inside the machine next to the machine that had materialized $SURNAME1.\nThe messenger was a machine of misty yellow glass.\nThe messenger was spherical, one yard in diameter. It was mounted on three spoked wheels.\nThe wheels were red rubber, ten inches in diameter, flexible.\nThe messenger was playing with a fluted bonbonni\u00e8re filled with away-with-melancholy pills.\nThe name of the messenger was Halley\u2019s $SURNAME0, and it was ticking like a clock.\nA piece of paper in one spokes of its wheels read:\n\"Tick-tock, tick-tock, tick-tock, tick-tock,\n\" went the yellow watchman. \"T", "0_16_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe gold watch clicked to four o'clock for the last time.\nAnd the last click wiped Salo adroitly from the scene.\nThe watch was on the breast of a young man in Army uniform. It was not broken.\nThe young man was sprawled in a parabola\u2014the arc of a shoulder-shot cannonball. His uniform and the watch on his breast were splattered with mud. A hole three-quarters of an inch in diameter had been blown in his helmet.\nThe young man was dead.\nThere was no visible wound.\nHe was not $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nHe was Sergeant $SURNAME2, who had been blown to bits in his blockhouse in Greenland, who had died on the operating table at Camp Chomorro, Mars.\n$SURNAME2 hadn\u2019t been reconstituted. He had been somebody else all along.\nIt hardly mattered, anyway, who he had been. He had died doing his beloved duty, and that was all there was to him. They buried him with the bastards who had killed him. They were headstones in a deserted field, with nobody to tend them.\nNobody ever came again to that field.\nA man and a cat...", "2_23_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe place was the grounds of a private sanitarium for the insane, on Mount McCabe in the mountains of northern New York State. The place was dark and unwatched.\nThe chauffeur had driven the limousine up a narrow, winding concrete drive to within twenty yards of the sanitarium. He had left the engine running but had shut off the headlights.\nHe was making himself a clandestine packet of chewing tobacco, using a small electric lighter to expose the ends of his rival\u2019s cut.\nThe thirty-seven-year-old chauffeur was not a part of the conspiracy against the forty-year-old executive, but he may as well be discussed now, because not much is known about him. He was a veteran of the Second World War, an American\u2014an ex-sergeant of Marines, who had been wounded once in the right shoulder. His name was R C Tyler.", "1_1_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nComprehension dawned upon Redwine.\nHe stuttered with his lips, then swore foully with his tongue. He whirled around.\nBrown stood there. He did not appear angry\u2014only aggrieved.\n\"Good God, Reverend,\" said Redwine, \"you scared me out of a year\u2019s growth. When did you get here?\"\n\"Just now,\" said Brown.\n\"How did you get here?\" said Redwine. \"I didn\u2019t see you come in.\"\nBrown smiled like a trumpeting Gabriel. The smile had no humor in it. It was a wise smile, a worldly-wise and sophisticated smile, the smile of a person who knew more than anybody else. \"I came like I used to do,\" he said. \"When my engine wasn\u2019t working right, I used to walk everywhere.\"\nRedwine\u2019s brow wrinkled. \"Your engine\u2014what are you talking about?\" he said.\n\"Teleportation,\" said Brown. He stepped forward closely, put a finger on the pulse in Redwine\u2019s neck, felt it, nodded. \"Teleportation,\" he said again, as though he had just tasted the word, liked the taste of...", "2_23_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to midnight</span>.\nWhen things were quiet, the mother could hear clocks all over the house, hundreds of them. Images of numbers flickered in her dimming consciousness like radiant birds. The numbers were all chirping the time, and always had been, and always would be, for clocks chatter at all hours about nothing, which is to say about time.\nThe noise of so many clocks might have been musical, part of an angel choir, perhaps, had it been in harmony\u2014or at least in unison. But the clocks were not in either harmony or unison. Each clock, following its own taut, quivering spring, beat an individual, separate little rhythm, a unique old shoe tap dance against bare wood floors and plaster walls.", "0_12_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-three p.m.</span> (2343). The place was Groucho Marx\u2019s private suite, occupying a wing at the rear of the top floor of the $SURNAME0 mansion. Suddenly, the marble walls bulged with many deep, wooden sounds. It was the household staff, doing their daily, twenty-one-gun salute in the corridor outside.\nThe occupants of the suite didn\u2019t even blink.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, master of ceremonies, and his skull-like wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, were watching the four closed-circuit television sets in the wall of the living room.\nDoor upon door upon door was closing, as the house was sealed for the night.\nSealed for the night.\nAll the sprawling mansion really had in common with a $SURNAME0 was its magnificently terraced front yard. There was nothing of $SURNAME0 in the house itself. It was the architectural equivalent of a hat on a hat. It was a show place, either the finest depository of the trash of nobility ever found or the most expensive brothel in the world. It contained only objects that were renowned for their beauty, regardless of any function they might serve. It was crammed with stuff.\nThere was a bon...", "2_10_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-five in the morning</span> on Mars. On Saturn\u2019s moon, Titan, it was ten twenty-five in the morning, Earth Eastern Standard Time.\nThe Tralfamadorian messengers were expected in sixteen Earth hours, no more than two Earth minutes before the end.\nThe steam roller on the Moon suddenly died. It shuddered and stopped. The tarpaulin whipped noisily back, and the two Tralfamadorians stepped into view.\nThe two Tralfamadorians made beautiful shadows on the Moon\u2019s rocky floor. The shadows were rainbow-hued and variously textured.\nOne Tralfamadorian was the bearded, lanky $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe other was a babe.\nIn her one Earthling appearance, she was a Venusian water maiden named Salo. Salo was by far the more affecting of the two. She was inch-thick Tralfamadorian skin and webbing.\nSalo was three feet tall, with a mandolin-shaped face, and indigo-colored, orange-eyed, spiral cat\u2019s pupils.\nThe eyes were as hypnotic as the spirals in a Jacob\u2019s ladder.", "0_23_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-six in the evening</span>. I had been raking it in all night, but I wasn't sure how long I could keep it up. The old Bard quota\u2014that wild edge of exhaustion that makes everything come right\u2014was beginning to fade. All I could see, all night long, was that goddamn picture: Wild Bill Guarnere in his frigging wheelchair. Like somekind of hallucinogenic mandala. If I had hung one of those macho Modigliani nudes in my place, instead of that silly thing, I might have made it through.\nOld $FIRSTNAMEB3 Moriarty, big bad road ghost, dead on a sidestreet Frisco ten years burning up the road ghost still roaming, decomposing, bugging the living shit out of all and sundry: \u201cWhere is the life that late I led?\u201d Why don\u2019t you go dig your own grave, old ghost? I thought of a poet dying in a tub, like some radish. Why don\u2019t you go dig your own grave? But alas and alack, it was my grave, my tub...and a bad scene I had made as well. Ed Dunkel stood off in the corner of my bedroom, his face absolutely blank. All night long I had...", "1_8_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight eighteen in the morning</span>. I was in a very expensive private booby hatch in Hollywood. The Stones') involvement cost thirtyfive hundred dollars. I had no medical insurance, no concert money and no personal nest egg\u2014just my bad rotten credit. One of the owners of this place was an attorney who specialized In workmen's compensation; he'd already taken almost ten grand In cash from me. Recovery from a first\u2014class addiction Is almost impossible under any circumstances. To attempt it In a place like this, which was run by addicts for addicts and probably had a spy or two on the staff, was some kind Of cruel and asinine deception. . . . Bad karma all around.\nThere was nothing to do all day but sit on this bed, with this Ace bandage around my chest, watching TV documentaries about grass. Every half\u2014hour a nurse\u2014sat outside the door with a clipboard, making marks. She was one of those tall blond walk\u2014aways that seem to be the norm around the singles bars downtown. She wore the white uniform because this kind of doctor is known as a specialist, who treats only the most severe cases\u2014addicts put away in some kind of straitjacket where they can't drink up their own profits. She was probably a user herself...", "2_5_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-five in the morning</span>. \u201cGoddamnit!\u201d I yelled. \u201cWe should\u2019ve taken that Ford Bonneville I offered you!\u201d He was already snoring. \u201cStanley! Wake up! We just burned a quarter of a tank in ten minutes! We may have a fuel pump gone!\u201d No response. He was dead to the world. I was alone with my fears and a full tank of bad gasoline.\nIt was really getting weird by now. It seemed like some kind of dismal omens kept piling up around us like so many dirty diapers. First the bird in the cage with the mannequin, then the stolen Cadillac, then the oil\u2014pressure drive that almost wasted us in that Texas oilpatch...and now, the appalling behavior of this $4,850 Shark.\nWe were only about ten minutes out of the airport by this time, but I had no intention of risking our lives on that crazy machine. So I chopped the throttle and drifted down to 5 mph\u2014closer to the ground I looked at the fuel gauge again and my knees almost gave way. It was completely empty. The goddamn thing had burned forty gallons in less than half an hour.", "0_5_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-six in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was due somewhere else in thirty minutes to pick up a girl called Lou Ann and bring her to my house. Meanwhile he had these two girls sitting on the kitchen floor with not the slightest indication of what to do next. His face was haggard with exhaustion.\n\u201cLet's go!\u201d he moaned. I realized now he'd had as many as six beers in the kitchen.\n\u201cLet's have breakfast,\u201d I said.\n\u201cYes! Yes! Good idea!\u201d He dashed out to get the girls; I could hear him knocking his hat off in the dark outside and apologizing. When he came back Ed Dunkel was in his pajamas, rubbing his eyes. \u201cGood morning, landlord,\u201d he said. $FIRSTNAMEB3 rushed about shoveling breakfast for everybody; Marie and the other girl were named Millie. They were both pretty cute; the other was a short, stocky one with a seventeen-year-old sullen face and long brunette hair. I was glad to have them there; they were so simple and so sweet. Millie had actually puked all over my bathroom. There was a big mess to clean up. The bathroom might have been mine; it...", "2_8_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty in the morning</span>. In $SURNAME0\u2019s fleet in space, the time was one hour and forty-eight minutes later. In the great hall of the palace of Salo, $SURNAME0 was opening his Memory Bank to visitors for a tour of three and a half Earthling minutes.\nIn Newport, the visiting timekeeper from Betelgeuse was still sleeping soundly in six inches of snow.\n\"She\u2019s not here, either,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"So she\u2019s not late.\"\n\"Maybe she just skated on ahead to the church,\" said $SURNAME1. \"Maybe\u2014\u2019 He pointed to the kissing corner where he had first made love to her. \"Maybe she just ran on ahead to surprise us.\"\n\"Maybe she did,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 distractedly. She was looking at something.\n\"Excuse me, ma\u2019am\u2014sir\u2014\" said a voice.\n\"Yes?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, not taking her eyes off her husband\u2019s lover.\nA boy stepped out of the crowd, warily. \"Yes?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 again.\nThe boy slapped an envelope into $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s hand. \"Auntie sent me,\" he said.", "0_21_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after nine o'clock in the evening</span> and the temperature was seventy-two degrees Fahrenheit. The newsboy stepped up to the old man and said: \"Extra! Extra! All about the massacre in the Church of San Juan!\" He did not say it either in Spanish or in English. He said it in Martian.\n\"I know,\" said the old man in Martian. \"I've heard. Extraordinary affair! Sensational! What do they think they're doing, I wonder?\"\n\"What's it all about?\" said the newsboy. He put the paper in front of the old man.\n\"All down here in black and white,\" said the old man. He made a feeble movement to reach for the paper. Then he gave up trying and let the arm fall back into his lap. \"It's a little too much for me,\" he said.\n\"You ought to know,\" said the newsboy. \"You're in it.\"\nA woman came out of the house and started down the steps. \"Here's your granddaughter,\" said the newsboy. He folded his papers, prepared to go.\n\"Just a minute!\" called the old man. \"Maybe I can explain it better to her. If she'd just wait\u2014\" he called to the woman. The woman...", "1_4_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter until five o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1's eight o'clock would have been better, but $FIRSTNAMEB1's eight o'clock did not exist. The clock was not going to strike eight o'clock. The best $SURNAME0 could hope for was that $FIRSTNAMEF0 would be out of bed and in the kitchen when he got there. The chances of that were poor. $SURNAME0 had made a rough calculation, and it looked as though his repeatedly punctual wife had a one in three chance of sleeping through until nine o\u2019clock, a one in nine chance of sleeping through until ten, and a one in twenty chance of sleeping through until eleven.\nComputation of these drowsy wonders was based on a comparison of $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s habits with those of her mother, old Mrs. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 of Newport, Rhode Island.\nFrom the attic of his Bermuda home, the staticky whining of $SURNAME0\u2019s radio had brought the old woman vividly to life for the first time in thirty-five years. She was in all respects as unappetizing as Mercury silver. The narrator has been too kindly to let the reader see her clearly, so let him step up now and take a look at her, if he can.", "1_14_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were flying westward in a gazelle-driven limousine from one end of Cape Cod to the other.\nThey were flying so low and so recklessly, moreover, that the driver of the limousine was now unable to hold his seat. He was tossed out feet-first by a particularly nasty lurch of the limousine. His writhen body rolled to a sand dune several yards away. The limousine roared on without him.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 continued to sit in the back of the limousine. $SURNAME0\u2019s face was streaked with tears. He clutched $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s hands in a viselike grip. $FIRSTNAMEB2 wasn\u2019t sitting properly in his seat. He was slung around so that his knees were against the right side of the limousine, his head against the left. Before him was the great expanse of Cape Cod Bay.\nIt was into this expanse that the limousine zoomed crazily.\nAnd then\u2014Vanished.\nAnd $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 vanished with it.\nThere was a blinding blue flash, a rumbling sound.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s...", "0_8_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-one in the morning</span> of November twenty-third, 1982.\nThe Universe was going on as usual.\nThere was the sound of helically rotating flat soled shoes on steps, descending into lobby level. This sound came from the exterior fire exit, but only after a moment did the exterior fire exit fly open.\nWalter Chronkite rumbled into the lobby, carrying a cylindrical case full of his wrenches and appliances. His eyes were smarting, his lips were puffy.\nHe looked out of the square doorway in the wall behind the counter, into the depth and unearthly mystery of the morning, he saw the empty and elegant street. Very much alive and in command, he put his flat feet down the steps.\nWalter did not shut the door behind him.\nHe walked to the street.\nAnd there, twelve feet from the building and behind the knee-high brick Jersey barrier that kept out traffic and kept the sidewalk navigable, stood a shining Batphone.\nAnd from the Batphone came the cruel, distorted shadow of a man in a hat and glasses.\nAnd the shadow spoke. \"This is it,\" said the shadow. \"The birth pangs. We\u2019re both bleeding. It\u2019s what...", "2_1_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nWith his foot, $SURNAME1 shoved the canvas bag full of bearer bonds and cash into the icy waters of the Piscataqua River. The bag didn\u2019t sink. It turned over slowly, like some great animal that had never learned to swim.\nIt did not turn turtle.\nThe \"arms\" of the thing turned upward out of the freezing water, and the \"arms\" slowly and incredibly began to wave. The \"legs,\" too, \"kicked\" madly.\nThis tableau held for some seconds, beautifully illumined by the silver spray from the falls.\nThen, almost reluctantly, the thing began to sink. It floated to the rear of the dock before turning turtle in the black water.\nThe silk ribbons fluttered all the way to the bottom.\nOnly the common spider clung tenaciously to the twine and rode down with the treasure to the muck of the river floor.\nNone of the magic streamers floated out into the great Piscataqua River. And when the current did carry them out, it carried them southward into warmer seas, to most unnatural growths among the lobsters.", "2_12_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nA ship of the Martian Navy hit the controls of the Earthling ship, immobilized it a mile above Mars. The jolt this gave to $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the other rejects was far from negligible. It was, in fact, the first jolt they had ever had.\nThe brain in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head quickly engaged the brake, assumed survey-camera control, whirled and focused briefly on the inanimate bits and pieces that had, until so recently, been $FIRSTNAMEB0: scalpels, tonsils, ear drums, Adam\u2019s apples, glutei maximi, gall bladders, and so on. The thought that went with the survey-camera survey was simple: Howdy do, boys! Here we are again!\nEverybody used to think that thinking was something that only people did.\nAnd now everybody knows better.\nAs far back as 1964, a book was published in this country that shocked the world. While it took the world six years to accept what was in that book, the American publishing industry has known the truth in it for thirty years. Its title is The Martian Chronicles, and its author is $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.", "1_9_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-seven in the morning</span>. I looked up in surprise when the elevator door opened and someone came in. The elevator was crowded and full of smoke. My eyes were burning.\nI was getting off on the seventeenth floor. As I started for the door, I saw a woman about to enter the elevator from the seventeenth\u2014floor hallway. Her eyes were like burned holes in a blanket.\nI squeezed past her and stepped out of the elevator.\nThe woman hesitated. She started to follow me out of the elevator, but then drew back as though she'd reconsidered that decision.\n\"Good morning,\" I said.\nShe frowned. \"What?\" she said. She seemed very worried about something. She looked somewhat familiar. She looked pretty bad.\n\"I said Good Morning,\" I told her.\n\"Oh...Good Morning.\" She looked at me more closely. She seemed to decide then that a conversation would be out of place\u2014just as I decided that a conversation was out of place. I disliked her intensely. I turned away and started down the hallway toward Room 1704.\nShe watched me in silence. I could feel her eyes on me. I didn't like that. I turned around. She was leaning against the faded, brownish wall of the...", "2_14_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>, Pacific Standard Time.\nThe man at the mill smiled. \"By golly!\" he said. \"Old Strawberry!\"\nThat was the name he had given a tiny missing fragment of his miraculous fortune.\nBefore he did anything else, he wanted to feel the fortune, really feel it.\nHe wanted to hold it, fondle it.\nHe opened shutters to let more of the afternoon sunlight into the old, dusty mill.\nBy wishing hard and lifting at the corners, he made the fragment as light as a thread of spittle. He wound it delicately around his fingers. The feel of a fortune in his hands made his smile widen and widen. The more he fondled his luck, the more his feet twitched and itched to walk. He stuffed the fortune into his trouser pocket.\nNo shoes on his feet. The feel of the cool, dusty floorboards against the soles of his feet tickled him almost unbearably.\nHe kicked off from the floor, and walked on his hands.\nAnd he whistled.\nAnd he did exercises, comical and beautiful bouncing parabolas. A bottle of whisky in the mill laughed a high, thin laugh.", "2_23_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The conspiracy was now nineteen minutes old.\nNo one knew of it but the chauffeur, and no one would know of it for many hours.\nThe chauffeur was the only human being for miles around, and he had unfettered use of his hands and his tobacco. At the age of thirty-seven, he found himself in control of a fine limousine and a box of the best Long Cut in the business. He was working for a fantastically wealthy mother superior.\nThe situation revealed an irony that pleased the chauffeur mightily. The rich, powerful and sophisticated owner of the limousine had never learned to roll his own. The chauffeur rolled cigarets for him all day, every day, during their relationship.\nThe chauffeur had not learned of the conspiracy against the Old Man.\nThe chauffeur assumed that he and the Old Man were bound for some distant replica of the British Museum\u2014to inspect some exhibits on a Thursday night in March.\nFor all the chauffeur knew, the trip could turn out to be a lot of fun.", "2_17_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nNo one on Mars knew that the first devastating wave of the invasion force was already on its way. The detectors in the deepest mines missed the stealthy approach of the wave.\nThe wave was in the substrata of all matter, bound there by the axioms of the metaphysics of Martian philosophy.\nThis deep-seated material in the substrata of all matter was naq\u2014a gray, bitter, fibrous substance that, throughout the Solar System, promised Mars many spectacular triumphs over the apparently hap-hazard arrangements by which the material universe was put together.\nThe first actual contact between material substance and invading Martian forces took place fifty miles above the surface of the planet. There, the first balloon-borne missile strike force was ready and waiting.\nUnder remote control, the balloons released their deadly cargo as the first enemy missile penetrated the upper atmosphere.\nAll over the globe, missiles, tanks, and guns began trading shots with their counterparts, with the very first enemy wave.\nOn a great billowing bed of flame, the Space Wart fell out of the Martian sky and deposited $SURNAME0, his son, and the dog on the spring grass on the steep bank of the Delaware Canal.", "2_4_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-three in the morning</span>. Every moment in Earthling time had been accounted for.\n$SURNAME0 paused in his glorious ramblings, noticed that he was stifling a scream of hysteria. The display before him had been so grotesque, so thoroughly Earthling. He had been standing there, wondering if he could make the crowd roar by ringing a tubful of tin spoons in the fashion of a children\u2019s party favor.\nBut he couldn\u2019t laugh, because Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s grief was contagious, and it paralyzed all attempts at self-mockery.\nThe switch girl, $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0, Barnstone\u2014and her child, too\u2014had withdrawn from the crowd by this time. They were waiting a thousand feet underground, in the tunnels that led to the central staircase.\nThe crowd had stayed to the bitter end. But the conversations had dwindled to monosyllables, grunts. Things had happened too rapidly, had been too awesome. Terrible mistakes that had seemed amusing a minute ago were suddenly taken in the most horrifying seriousness.\nThe crowd milled slowly and aimlessly, as crowds will when their conversations die. They looked back over their shoulders.", "0_12_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. At the table in the caf\u00e9 was a man in a metal-rimmed civilian pilot's cap. He was wearing a black sweater under a khaki wind-jacket. I had noticed him several times during the morning. When I came in, he had taken a table against the wall and watched me while I ate. He also seemed to be watching the boy at the bookstand outside.\nAt one o'clock sharp he stood up and walked over to my table.\nI knew he was going to speak to me before he reached my table. I wanted to be taken by surprise. I did not want it to look as though I had suspected him. But there was no way of fooling myself. I had known it all along.\n\"Is this your child?\" he asked.\nHe had a ridiculous pose. His cap was on the back of his head and his feet were too far apart. His face was sallow and blatant\u2014the face of a minor officer who resents his assignment to France: an injured expression and an ankle that was a little too thick for the flight-boat.\n\"Well?\" he said.\n\"No,\" I said. \"He\u2019s yours.\"\n\"I don\u2019t like your...", "0_7_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven eighteen in the morning</span>. At seven twenty-eight, as I sat cracking my first whip-It coffee of the day, flipping through some papers, listening to the radio and waiting for the phone to ring, a battered '46 sedan rolled smoothly up to the door of the office, made a perfect U-turn, and straightened out on Sunset Boulevard, heading East. Somewhere in the immaculate patina of that old California car there must have been a homemade pin-stripe that read: \u201cLas Vegas, Nevada.\u201d\nCHAPTER TWENTY TWO\nThe Devil on Pandora\nWhen our car reached the crest of Topanga Canyon, far over Malibu, my attorney jammed to a halt, veered off the highway, whining down a rutted cliffside track and braked to a stop beside a huge, natural boulder wedged into the ocean of brush. \u201cHere we are,\u201d he said solemnly. \u201cWelcome to the Jungle Habitat of Our National Hero.\u201d\nI got out of the car and stood ankle-deep in deadfall on this high, foggy bluff. All around me was a static chaos of burnt orange foliage slashing up from a sea of black volcanic dust. Above, the sky was a...", "1_2_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-eight in the morning</span>. The room was full of my friends, who had come to watch a friend of mine take all his clothes off and run around San Francisco with his clothes in his hands. This would be an extraordinary spectacle, we all agreed, if only it took place at high noon in Union Square. But at two thirty-eight in the morning, in this sad and lonely room above a wholesale fish market, it would be an act bathed in lonely self-realization, supremely sad and pointless, but somehow beautiful in lonely self-realization. We wanted to see this.\nI slipped the telephone into my pocket and looked off into the empty spaces around us. \u201cLet's go,\u201d I whispered. My friends moved reluctantly. They did not want to leave this room that was filled with mystery and connotation, where things and thoughts hung less in the air than in that ether that comes before and after meaningful movements and utterances. You could almost reach out and grasp their dank and vaporous tails.\nThey wanted to stay on awhile, perhaps even invest in this house and move in and become philosophers and spend their time watching madmen take off their clothes and run around with their clothes in their hands. They did not have to leave, of course...", "1_0_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-nine a.m.</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 bought a shuttle throwaway ticket on the TWA 227 night plane to Chicago, and on the ticket he wrote: Dear $FIRSTNAMEB2: Steer clear of me\u2014I\u2019m stoned, and there\u2019s bad trouble. This is your cousin $FIRSTNAMEB1. It is extremely urgent that you meet me Chicago Midway Airport arrival your Gray Hound bus. Departure 12 o\u2019clock.\nIt was one a.m. when Stony Stevenson finally got back to the cabin. He was very drunk.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was in bed.\n\"So you\u2019re back, Stony,\" she said.\n\"Of course I\u2019m back.\"\n\"How do you feel? You\u2019re awfully loud.\"\n\"I\u2019m not loud.\"\n\"You\u2019re loo-loo.\"\n\"Hell\u2019s bells,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, floundering for the light.\nHe found the light. \"Stony, you old drunk. Go brush your teeth. I\u2019m not sleeping with a person that smells like a distillery.\"\n\"Don\u2019t call me Stony,\" said Stony. \"Nobody calls me Stony.\"\n\"My God...", "2_8_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter past eight o'clock in the morning</span> in Newport, Rhode Island, Fortress America, Western Solar System, Universe.\nTHEY SPLIT UP AND CAME BACK TOGETHER ONCE MORE\n\"My name is $SURNAME1\u2014\" said the man in the madness mask. He still stood on the sundial in the gazebo. One great, splay foot and one small, dainty foot rested on the twelve and three o\u2019clock lines. His big, manic face was so radiantly filled with his marvelously simple message that anyone might have laughed right in his teeth. He couldn\u2019t have cared less.\nHe was having experiences, now, that transcended the quaint tricks that Fate had played on him earlier in the morning. Here he was, standing stiffly on three and twelve, the only point on the dial where the foot of a giant fit exactly, and what was he?\nHe was a herald of something wonderful and terrible.", "2_1_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-six in the morning</span> when the materialization shot the messenger over Saturn.\nTralfamadore was a young world. The first creatures to land on it had started the yellow-sphere culture. It was a very sophisticated culture.\nThere were one hundred and twenty-four yellow spheres on Tralfamadore. Only $SURNAME0 and the messenger were able to fly from place to place. The other spheres were fixed by nature to their settings.\n$SURNAME0 had been in 62,912 materializations before the one that shot him to Mars, had been in another 62,912 since the one that shot him to Mars. He was a trained technician, and was in reasonably good health and vigor.\nHis palatial Newport home was perfumed by the reflexes of his diving-bells, his kites, his balloons and hot-air ships, his perambulators and Guggenheim tricycles.", "1_18_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-eight in the evening</span>. The sky was turning dark, blue, violet. There was a sense of a lid being dropped. At six forty-two there came a sudden dry swirl of wind, shaking the solid heart, the crystalline sphere of the earth, turning it on its pivot. Now it was dark, now it was night.\nConstantinescu stayed in the shelter through the night to keep the men company. He said that every time he went outdoors he had the impression, diving into the oblivion of space, of having his flesh rent by thorns and splinters of glass.\nHe did, however, in his own words, \"indulge in a little eccentricity,\" and after dark he shut off the lights throughout the shelters, made all the men turn off their flashlights and their portable radios, too.\nOrson and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were playing hearts with toothpicks andcigarette butts. The inventors were playing with themselves.\nConstantinescu puffed energetically on his corncob pipe. A breeze went over the fields and entered the shelters, riffling the blue drab covers of the servicemen\u2019s bunks and the white laboratory coats of the scientists. Only the heart under the rock stayed still and dead.", "1_18_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been deaf to all invitations to come with anyone. He was as deaf as stone to all social entreaties and sweet blandishments, as deaf as a mummy.\nSeduction Unsuccessful\nAt 7:15 he had been seduced by a manic-depressive spinster, Miss Frances McWhamsie of Galesburg, Illinois, who, wearing the trousers of a cat, tried to buy him with a small padlock.\nHe escaped from her while she was trying to negotiate with a crippled jockey. $FIRSTNAMEB2 took the padlock.\nA New Phase\nThen $FIRSTNAMEB2 went through a new and strange phase, which was auto-seduction. He told himself over and over that he belonged to himself exclusively, that he was a part of himself, that he owed himself all good things. He imagined he was kissing himself warmly and fondly on the lips.\nHe said, \"This is all that I am. This is me, and this is all that I am.\"\nAnd then he caressed himself. And, having no eyes with which to see himself, he hypnotized himself by a fixed visual image of a handsome chestnut coloring and a form as perfect as a colt.\nHe...", "1_4_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>. I figured this by looking at my watch\u2014my wrist watch, which I'd set to Hiroshima time. At that moment, Mister Truman was probably sleeping peacefully, but I hoped he was not. It would not be fitting for the President of the United States to be sleeping peacefully about such a time. Nor would it be fitting for him to be measuring for new curtains in the White House living room. The living room of the White House is far too important a place to be decorated in the Jefferson style with fringed lampshades and reproduction Empire furniture.\nEverything in the White House should be made of fine, solid, unused weaponry. It is a filthy, disgusting shame what they have done to the White House. When the White House was first built, it was made of wood and stone and iron. Then they began adding things to it. As a political joke, they added a portico in front. A portico is a bunch of columns stuck in the ground in imitation of a porch. The columns are cheap, I wish to explain. It is the cheapest way of building a porch that I have ever heard of. If a person wanted a porch, he would have to be an idiot to build it the way they built the White House. The builder must...", "1_6_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-four in the morning</span> of the worst day of my life. The place was a smart, snappy Sheriff's car racing hell-for-leather through the casino toward the door, with me on the back seat praying I could make it before these deputies tore my ass out, keeping time to the rattling rotor blade of a copter hovering to my left, teeth bared. The hellish whup-whup of the blades made it almost impossible for me to think, much less speak-Lord, be my tunnel...\n3. To the Last Vice\nJust when I was beginning to think I might make it to the parking lot, this car suddenly cut to the right and came to a screeching halt next to the curb just around the corner from the big glass doors. I was half out the door before it stopped.\n\u201cLet's have your license,\u201d said an enormous young cop, running toward me with a log book in his hand. I had run around the car and was now facing him.\n\u201cCan't you let me go?\u201d I pleaded. \u201cMy plane will be leaving in twenty minutes.\u201d \u201cWhere's your ticket?\u201d he shouted.\n\u201cIn my room.\u201d I was...", "1_9_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-three in the morning</span>. The big bass pump roared an inch from my eardrum. The car, a 1939 Ford V8 coup?ur, occupied most of the five-foot width of the ramp. It was the only car left under the building. I\u2019d won a bet with my attorney and I was ignoring him as I washed the blue car under the idle spurting jets.\nGood and wet now, I dusted the thing off with a hose and then went after it with a dryer, smoothing out the water droplets as best I could. I felt pretty good, looking at the shell I\u2019d restored to such a lustrous beauty. God, how I loved that car.\nIt was one of a kind. A car that somehow connoted all of Hemingway in a simple hand\u2014built, four\u2014seater, with the bone\u2014like framework of the body exposed like Fra Angelico angels in a chapel. It was that holy in style, and swan\u2014like in movement. It would outrun most anything on earth\u2014the Daytona race car included\u2014and hug curves like a roller skate.\nI saw my attorney approaching, his face sullen. He wanted a Cadillac Eldorado. I wanted a Jaguar...", "2_4_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThat same morning--about an hour after the process servers had departed--Old Bull Lee arrived home in his new Cadillac convertible and spent ten minutes nadering it into a bright red, five-foot-high Japanese sandalwood rose trellis he'd insisted on having custom-built for the purpose by Farouk Sheikh, his tripet contractor.\nThen he naded himself down in the back seat, so that he was more or less in the shade of the trellis, and to his pleasant surprise Lee found that his beautiful new machine was already haunted. A navy blue Rolls-Royce was parked there. Lee now owned two cars haunted by the same ghost: since his 1933 Hudson Terraplane had also been haunted by this same anonymous British car.\nThis was of no great importance to Old Bull Lee. He had never cared who the ghost was, just that it was a genuine, authentic ghost.\nHe satisfied himself on this point by knocking on the Rolls' hood, and when the metal rang like good British steel, he knew that the car was authentic.", "1_22_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-four in the evening</span>. The place was Cincinnati, Ohio. Ten hours later\u2014at two-thirty in the morning\u2014a huge crowd had gathered outside the Convention hall: police and Secret Service agents and motorcycle cops completely surrounding the place and slightly outnumbering the freakhood by seven to one. Great gleaming buses with heavily armed police in the front seats stood at intervals along the curb. In the nooks and crannies along the fringe of the mass of people, the press clustered like gnats, snapping pictures and shooting movie film.\nAnd the people in the mass seemed peaceful enough. They formed a pattern that shifted slowly, like lassitude itself. There were college kids, and beat kids, and high school kids, and middle-age kids, and kids in their early forties. Some carried common road maps to indicate unexpected sudden moves. Some carried copies of the New Mexico Press/Revolt. Others had copies of the Whole Earth Catalog. A copy of Finnegans Wake was occasionally spotted. The crowd seldom laughed or jostled each other. They mainly looked at the ground, seeming to study the texture of the pavement and waiting for something to happen. Various rumors were milling around. It was said that the candidate would speak from the steps, would stand...", "2_14_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earth time. The place was the old north-and-south landing strip on the West Coast of the United States of America.\nThe place was very beautiful.\nThe drinking cup dropped off the driver\u2019s neck and spilled on the floor and rolled away.\nThe driver was curiously attractive, in a bony sort of way. Maybe his very thinness made him seem attractive. Maybe thinness was what Hollywood found beautiful.\n$SURNAME1 sipped the last of his liquid refreshment and put the cup aside, beside a half-eaten raspberry Danish. He not only finished the Danish but licked his fingers clean. He did not overeat raspberries, however. $SURNAME1 did not overeat anything.\nHis hair was blond, his eyes were sky-blue, and his brand-new beige suit fitted him quite nicely, he thought.", "1_1_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>. Martian chronometers differed as to Earthian time, and $SURNAME0 had his set for one o\u2019clock. It was now ten minutes past one o\u2019clock on Mars.\nAt a quarter past one o\u2019clock, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 had died of old age in Marble Falls, Texas.\nAt a quarter to one o\u2019clock, $SURNAME1 had fathered $FIRSTNAMEB2, his son by wife number thirty-one.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was now at Tranquility Gardens, six hundred thirty-one light years from Earth. He was forty minutes past his fourteenth birthday. At that time, he was locked in the arms of a contortionist in an airtight hopper of a padded spaceship.\nHe still professed not to under stand where his father was taking him.\n\"Where are we going, Mister $SURNAME1?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"Son\u2014?\" said $SURNAME1 icily.\n\"I\u2019m supposed to call you Father, aren\u2019t I?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2 sullenly.\n\"Where are we going?\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"I don\u2019t know,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n\"I\u2019d like to know what you think we\u2019re...", "2_15_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. I went out of the building and walked across the lawn to my \u201coffice.\u201d The Red Shark was about fifty feet beyond the building, among a whole line of cars and official vehicles. I saw a man sitting behind the wheel of the Shark, reading a newspaper. He seemed asleep.\nI walked up to him and said, \u201cAll right, fella, move the goddamn car\u2014right now!\u201d\nHe snapped to attention and stared up at me with shock. I slapped him once across the forehead and he took off, leaping through the air like a frog. The keys were in the ignition; I started up and backed right over our two radio mikes, breaking them. I hupted the car forward, cutting off a semi truck, got across the highway, made a U-turn with one wild swing that seemed to pitch all of us over the hillside\u2014and hung that heavy car out over the traffic lanes at the bottom of that long dip.\n1. See The Great Shark Hunt for details\u2014and for a different ending.", "1_11_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-one in the morning</span> of April 10, 1990, and the gentleman who had just occurred was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, Apostle of the Snake Church of Mars. At this moment, as Earth\u2019s darling danced before him, $SURNAME0 did what all ambassadors of Earth do at some point or other\u2014he turned his back on her.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 watched $SURNAME0 move to the main entrance of the palace. $SURNAME0 turned his back on the day, opened the great doors, let the night come in.\n\"There is nothing out there for me,\" he said. He closed the doors. \"And there is nothing I can do. I am sorry. I cannot make you a sign. All Earth\u2019s ambassadors are as penniless as you are, and all of them would give their worldly goods for a sign from their darling.\"\n$SURNAME0 settled himself in the seat that $SURNAME1 had slept in, reseated himself in the platform built for a throne. He beckoned to $SURNAME1. \"I must ask you to stand in front of me, please,\" he said. \"I am about to tell you something that I have told no other Earthling.\"\n$SURNAME1 took his proper place, wincing a bit as his silver footwear touched the...", "2_13_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Mars-time, when the ship landed in the lee of a great boulder on a broad plain ten miles south of the Martian city of Newton.\nThe plain was swept, by Martian winds. The surface of the rock boulder behind which the ship had landed was a riot of lichens, small flowering plants that could tolerate the alternation between hot and cold that was so perverse a part of Mars.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was barking orders again, waking the rest of the crew, making them gather their belongings. \"You,\" he said to $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"You drive. We\u2019ll all ride. Be there in ten, eleven minutes.\"\n\"Where are we going, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" said Bigman.\n\"Where everybody goes to be baptized and get married,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s church!\"\nAn iron sign, black with rust, was bolted to the rock beside the landing place. The paint on the sign, where it was not scraped almost to the iron, was black, too. The paint said simply:\nThe Church of God the Utterly Indifferent", "0_2_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-two in the morning</span>. At three, when Remi Boncoeur and his millionaire pal were formally arrested, there\u2019d be a lot of activity, but it would be too late, to preserve the secrecy of the operation. The fewer people who saw the moneybag carried out of the house and into a cab the better.\nRemi would spend the night in jail, then the next morning he would be brought into court and the extradition hearings would begin.\nAt four-thirty in the morning, according to a phone call to a San Francisco newspaper, a bomb would go off under one of the main San Andreas fault tunnels. No one would be hurt. The message was left by a woman who said her name was Luna Curtice, but who might or might not have been the girl in the motor-court bed next door to the Mucho Mucho.\nMaybe so, maybe not.\nTheoretically, only those directly involved in the plot need ever know about it. If it looks successful, others will be told, but until then the fewer people who know the better.\nAt five after three that morning, Miles Fabrikat awakened in his jail cell to find himself free to go. The bag of money had been carried out of the house into a...", "2_23_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-seven in the evening</span>.\nInside the barrack, the field marshal and the astronaut came out onto the barrack steps to wave a fond au revoir to their wives. The wives were poised in Belljet helicopters, which hovered over the barrack roof.\nAt eleven thirty-eight, the wives kissed the hands of the field marshal and the astronaut for the last time, waved good-byes, shut the door of the helicopters, and started their craft into the air. They watched their husbands grow small through the thick glass of their helicopters.\nThe wives were headed for army posts hundreds, thousands\u2014no one knew how many\u2014of miles away. They would be met by the provost marshals with formal courtesies. There they would be whisked into barracks that would look to be much like the one they had just left. The barracks, too, would be crisscrossed with jarring and undisciplined columns of soldiers.\nThe provost marshals would face the duties of assigning each visiting wife to a bed she would have to think of as new. They would be forced to register shocked surprise to find that no blankets had been laid. No soap had been supplied.", "2_9_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\nOn the slopes of Mars, the winds from the iron snows of the polar caps converge to form terrifying dust and sand storms that scour the face of the planet. In the nightmare of these winds, the millions of Americans who are about to die are about to be born.\nThe winds will scatter their ashes to the four corners of the Solar System.\nThe winds outside Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s window were gentile zephyrs, refreshing after the stinks within.\nThe breezes sank coolly into the pinkish solar swim bronzing Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s legs.\nTheir filtered coolness made grateful patterns on her skin.\nTheir ghostly touch made her tingle pleasingly.\nTheir ghostly touch suggested the presence of a ghost that was the ghost of someone the ghost of someone who cared that the ghost cared.", "2_2_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> in Newport, the summer residence.\nThe huge Sun Room at that time of the year had low windows, set deep into the stone walls. The windows were skylights, fashioned in leaded glass. Looking up through a skylight, one saw only a dark golden glow, a square of infinite space.\nLooking down through a skylight, one saw furniture covered with white sheets, and heard the thumping of billiard balls in the room below.\nThe Sun Room also had high windows on the north and south, windows with panes of glass as thin as eggshells. Those windows were flung wide to the night, for the night was oven-hot.\nWilbur Daffodil-11 came out of the billiard room at 3:02 a.m., yawning and stretching.\nThrough the eggshell panes, Wilbur Daffodil-11 could see the beckoning finger of Saturn, the waiting finger of Saturn. He stretched toward the finger, toward the beckoning.", "0_21_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-two in the evening</span>. Outside in the awful plaza heat, hordes of tourists and hotel guests milled around while their taxicabs waited. There were Americans from Cleveland, Salt Lake City, Denver, Pittsburgh, Des Moines, Peoria, San Jos\u00e9, Phoenix, Chicago, Boise, Seattle, Fort Worth, Tulsa, Minneapolis, Billings, Chattanooga, Portland, Milwaukee, Schenectady, Altanta- hundreds of them. They were laughing and talking and none of them had a single good thing to say about Old Mexico except that at least it was livable as compared to San Salvador ... just barely. The problem was that San Salvador was only a stone's throw from El Paso, Texas, whereas Juarez was a stone's throw from Hell.\n\"Uh-huh! Uh-huh! Sounds just awful!\" said the Indianapolis mother of four outside in the teeming plaza while her husband hunted for a taxicab. \"That place is worse'n Vietnam!\" she exclaimed. She weighed 220 pounds and she carried an Ideal Master travel alarm in her purse for protection. She was worried about her two boys and two girls. \"I mean, I'm glad I come down here, but it's sure good to get back to El Paso.\" She was from Indianapolis.", "1_20_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-three in the evening</span> of the Tuesday that I found and threw away the hundred-dollar-bill in my work suit after measuring my father. It was the same day, so far as I can calculate, that $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4 left Frisco and arrived in New York and he made the call to me at Jane Stanfords, where I was sleeping on the floor. Next day he turned up alone in the big Buick on Sheridan Square and stood outside the door.\n'they called me from the Id, man, a young bop poet gave me this address.' I invited him in. Hours later a woman visitor arrived and brought me a book of photographs of my poems. She had red hair and freckles. I sat reading.\n\u201cYou know, man, in all sincerity -- 'A foolish consistency is the hobgoblin of little minds.' You know what you ought to do?'\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cTake a road and add only that landmark that kisses actuality contact and escape. What are you going to do? What do you want to do?\u201d\n\u201cI don't know -- and learn to eat, sleep, dig holes, anything.\u201d\n\u201cGood. Okay. What's your name...", "0_8_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-five in the morning</span>, the sun was already too hot on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skin, and the spacesuits felt as if they were baking. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 didn\u2019t say anything for a moment. They stood there with the gorilla army behind them. They looked at the objects on the ground before them, and didn\u2019t say anything.\nThe objects on the ground were six dead comrades, and the comrades had been so horribly mangled that it hurt $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 not to say anything. The mangling had been done by merciless Earthmen firing merciless Earthman tank guns. The tank guns had torn open the chests of the comrades, had collapsed the comrades\u2019 lungs and livers, had pulped the comrades\u2019 hearts and bowels. One comrade, having inhaled a great deal of blood through injuries to his lungs, had choked to death on his own blood before he died from the other injuries. The comrades, who had been sitting on some rocks by the landing-area stream, had died in various positions. One had died kneeling, thrusting his arms in front of him, as though he had been trying to keep from falling by holding onto something. The Earthmen obviously had fired first at the comrades\u2019 heads. One comrade...", "0_19_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to eight o'clock in the evening</span> on October the twenty-second, this being Earth's time.\nThe place was a spur of the Parsec Mountains on Mars.\nThis spur was twelve miles long, five miles wide, and ten miles high. The tip of the spur was a gigantic needle, rock crystal and glinting silver. The needle dipped into a gash in the Martian landscape three miles below. This gash was an empty region with pebbly bottom and seen-better-days walls. The gash was a thousand yards wide, two miles deep, and fifty miles long\u2014and it was called Newport, Rhode Island, USA.\nThe skyline of Newport was basalt, rusted iron, and rock crystals. This skyline was a wall footed in the pebbled floor of the empty region and topped by the ten-mile spire of rock crystal.\nThe empty region had been formed by explosions.\nThe explosions had occurred on December 12, 1898.\nThe cause of the explosions had been 99.99 per cent pure Nature doing what Nature so often does, i. e., striving to even things up.\nThe uninhabited regions of Mars bore an uncanny resemblance to the uninhabited regions of Earth. The most striking resemblance was to desert stretches of...", "2_15_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>\u2014 Earthling time.\nThe master of ceremonies was broadcasting from the microphone in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s lapel, over the public address system of the Madison Cube Garden.\nThe ceremonies were drawing to a close\u2014as they always did, as they had to.\n\"For father and mother, for sister, for brother, for son, for daughter\u2014for comrades,\" said the master of ceremonies, \"for Earth, for Mars, for the Universe\u2014\"\nThe master of ceremonies was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nA light rain was falling over the Madison Cube Garden.\nThe raindrops flew gaily through the charged particles that $SURNAME0\u2019s TW-55 contrived to make so abundant. The particles jitterbugged the raindrops, made them curve and careen before they fell. The audience saw the raindrops through a glorious, shimmering curtain.\nThe shimmering curtain was blue, pink, vermilion, gold, and green. It was beautiful beyond description. The master of ceremonies could well imagine how it would appear when beamed into the homes of Earth over the tightbeam of the Latest News Network.", "0_8_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-one in the morning</span> and I wouldn't be able to beat the briefing at the office. There would be several hundred questions to answer, most of them concerned with routine matters in which my interest and participation were nil. I had assumed this routine would continue when the helicopter fell into my lap and it didn't matter where I was going because my presence aboard was only momentary.\nBut this was ridiculous. I was all mixed up. I was behaving as though I had actually no choice, that I must cover all the bets, that I had to be at this briefing and at that conference, by God. I should call room service and ask for grapefruit, coffee, croissants, etc., because there was no hurry whatsoever.\nBut I couldn't do that. For one thing, my head was broken: I needed a giant cream with codeine. I was suffering from nervous exhaustion and a number of other illnesses, an accumulation of ills that threatened my mind and spirit.\nSo I had to get to the office. I would have to finish it face to face, eyeball to eyeball. I would have to watch the smirky bastards trying to pick my brain and squeezing it dry for answers to questions that I didn't know, answers to questions I'd make...", "2_6_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past six o'clock in the morning</span> in the underground war-command center of Chancellor Otto Witt. The good-looking dark-eyed girl, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s dark-eyed good friend, was now a matron and the mother of a child.\nThe child was and is a boy.\nHe is the spitting image, except for sex and a frown, of old Stony Stevenson.\nHis name is Bobby Fischer. He is now ten years old.\nSince there is neither time nor space any more, Bobby Fischer has just fainted and collapsed on a marble floor.\nHis collapse has now been analyzed and charted.\nBobby Fischer has fainted for the following reasons: He has just learned that his father is a man named $FIRSTNAMEB0. He has just learned that his mother had taken more interest in $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his goofed-up crew while they had been airborne than she had ever taken in Bobby while he had been on Earth.", "1_2_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>, and I got my bearings, felt the road under me, felt the motor purring and humming in fourth gear, 205 m.p.h...Memphis, 55 miles. I stomped on the accelerator. Then, thinking better of it, I eased off and settled back, letting the car drift in neutral at a comfortable 150 with the radio on Real Fine Blues. There was a warning buzzer on the dash. I reset it to 120. No sense wasting a perfectly good buzzer.\nI was drowsing off. Suddenly it seemed I was standing by the road in the darkness, seeing myself coming in the mirror as a tiny flickering light far out on the long black strip of real estate that is U.S. 61, somewhere between Memphis and the Mississippi River...makeshift family sleeping in back seat; temporary odors; tepee cracklings in poles; drab illuminations that hinted roadside wayside luncheonettes, promise of pie, a new life; raw trip hammering in everybody's head; children confused from changing scenes, sirens screaming past; dogs asleep between seats; fatigue and boredom, suddenly we were all sitting on the side of the road and waiting for the ghostly trike to float by. (Ah, yes...", "2_20_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to nine o'clock in the evening</span>, and it was plain that the conspirators were about to murder the last person on Mars who wondered whatever became of young $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nIt was a very sad business.\nThe cab driver assumed the man with the black mustache was crazy. \"What the hell you going off your nut like this for?\" he asked mildly.\nThe man with a mustache was too wacko to answer coherently.\n\"Rumbo!\" said the cab driver. He shifted gears. \"A buck eighty,\" he intoned sadly. \"A buck eighty or adios.\" But the fare went on beating on a door handle that didn\u2019t exist. \"Adios, Frankenstein,\" said the cab driver, and drove off.\nThe spaceship in which $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his mates had arrived on Mars was still at the edge of the town. A simple combination lock on the outside pilot room barred the crew from entering.\nCorplet, $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s janitor and part-time lover, was in the pilot room. His master-lock could open the combination lock, but he wasn\u2019t interested in going out to the ship. He expected no miracles.\nHe was simply doing his job.", "0_16_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-nine in the afternoon</span> of October 8 when we got to Colorado Spring. The one thing about $FIRSTNAMEB3, nobody could ever accuse him of exaggerating time and distance, obviously because he doesn't care, he's immersed in his own trip. Mainly he wants to get himself there and not be bothered with such as observing speed laws and getting to towns before dark, but then he doesn't care how many hours we sit around while he pays his hotel bill or argues with clerks or sits on fences or writes letters to his girls or roams around looking for old acquaintances. We arrived in Colorado Springs at four-twenty that afternoon; we had to drive around the entire downtown section to find parking space at the hotel down off South Academy Boulevard. The whole town seemed to be residing in permanent afternoon, the dusty fantastic downtown streets completely deserted, with stores presenting all their goods in doorways -- a looking-glass world of frustration and emptiness. We checked into the hotel and $FIRSTNAMEB3 took off for a \u201clook around.\u201d In about a half-hour he called me. I sat in the lobby writing postcards to make the time pass. He appeared around a corner of the lobby wearing a pair of long johns under his slacks and his face was alight.\n\u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6,...", "2_19_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nIt was then that $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 invited her husband to speak to the invited guests. \"Skip,\" she said, \"would you say something to the young people and to Mr. $SURNAME1?\"\nThe guests murmured obligingly.\n\"Well\u2014\" said $SURNAME0, \"the thing to remember is that the Universe has no ends\u2014or, rather\u2014\" said $SURNAME0 pedantically, \"the Universe is ends, period.\"\n$SURNAME0\u2019s audience looked blank.\n\"I mean,\" said $SURNAME0, \"that things like Mars and time weapon number one and the Earthling named $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 don\u2019t come to any sort of endings, but only to periods\u2014and that if it were otherwise, endings would be hateful and injurious things indeed.\"\n\"Like war,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0.", "1_11_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to noon</span>.\nThere was a violent swirling in Salo\u2019s air-borne gas-sphere. A fountain of super-heated steam shot into the stratosphere. Salo\u2019s body was in the steam, and his body was unquestionably being cooked.\nIn an organization as big as the Army of Mars, with shoulder-boards as wide as elephant-backs, there are bound to be people a rung or so below the top who don\u2019t look too good in the uniform. Salo was one of these. He was frankly a terrible sight.\nSalo was a Tralfamadorian who could live for many Earthling centuries, despite the fact that he had very little to do. He stood a foot and a half shorter than his close friend, $FIRSTNAMEB0. He was misshapen, and there was something ugly about his color. He was a very dark green. His skin had cracked leather patches on it, and his one true eye was a burst light bulb.\nObedient to the instructions in his big, shiny-black ceramic skull, Salo now flew to a position almost directly above the parade ground, where he could watch the executions in the privacy of his gas-sphere.\nThe g...", "2_12_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-six p.m.</span>\n\"We can make you very happy,\" said the voice over the speaker. The voice was pretty. The voice belonged to a pretty girl of about twenty. There were two thousand girls just about twenty and just about pretty who shared the job of keeping $FIRSTNAMEB0 happy. Their names were stenciled by their speaker-slots. The stenciling by the different speakers was kept scrupulously uniform. Only the colors varied.\n\"When you wake up, $FIRSTNAMEB0,\" said the pretty girl over the speaker, \"you wake up feeling just fine.\" She giggled at the truth of what she was saying. She thought $FIRSTNAMEB0 was awake, and she didn\u2019t want him to be self-conscious about the fact. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had never spoken to one of his pretty girls, and he never would.\nHe had just about decided to speak when a sweetness came into his head, and the pretty girl receded into imbecility, and a harmonium came into his head instead. The harmonium was a rubbery and featureless mate for the one in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chest cavity. The mating harmoniums vibrated against each other. They vibrated musical murmurs into each other.", "1_10_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until eleven o'clock in the morning</span> of November 6, 2008. The place was the rear of the $SURNAME0 estate, a mile and a half from the estate house, a mile and a half from the core of the estate itself.\nThe empty rocket had been carried and set down in that desolate place by a crew of estate laborers. The sizzle and fizzle of exotic matter on the bottom of the iron shoes had died away as the last of the power drained from the Rozized battery.\nThe laborers were smoking cigarettes at a respectful distance from the rocket.\nTheir business was finished.\nThere were twelve of them. One, the foreman, carried a clipboard and wore a watch, a diamond ring, one suit and a roller-type motorcycle helmet.\nThey were all looking at the chronometer of one of their comrades.\nTheir comrade who had the watch was Skip. He was joined to $FIRSTNAMEB0 by sutures, broach, and clip.\nSkip was patient, bloody, and nearly fainting from blood loss. He was wedged in by the press of people and a tractor beam for sinew and bone.\nIt had been a ghastly business getting $FIRSTNAMEB0 aboard the rocket. Skip had had to hammer in nails and staple together sinews torn...", "2_14_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\nHe was playing Skip Alleykat on Titan as Wanda Shoemaker walked briskly into the museum. He trotted, heel and toe, heel and toe, up the shining incline of the observation platform. He was a tan, muscled mutation. He was bandy-legged and small-headed. He was intellectually and spiritually bankrupt. His single claim to distinction was a capacity for a virulent sexual passion he had worked up for his stepsister.\nOf this passion and its outlet, there was little evidence. The lusty role he had assigned himself for the Christmas Pageant was typical of his aspirations. You would think, to watch Skip Alleykat leading the Earthling regiments into battle, that lust was raging through him like wildfire.\nThere was nothing of the wildfire about him, however. His only real passion was a smoldering, deadly enmity toward $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. His only real aim in life was to punish $SURNAME1 for putting him under the observation of the Fates.\nThis was to be his main role at the Christmas Pageant\u2014the role of chief avenger.\nAnd then, at the last moment, $SURNAME1 would be humiliated.", "1_13_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-two in the afternoon</span>. Our strategy had been to take it moderately easy at first, and then as we got into the swing of things keep accelerating\u2014graduate in car-time from ten miles per to twenty, then thirty, then sixty, then ninety a hundred, HUNDRED AND TEN, a hundred and twenty, a hundred and thirty . . . stay there for a while . . . then on to some serious road work: two hundred, two hundred and fifty, three hundred miles an hour . . . maybe set a land-speed record . . . then back home to Vegas in time for dinner\u2014or lunch . . . depending on our run. Now I was already beginning to worry about a ticket. Sure my car was hot, but so was every other car on the highway. My only possible edge lay in handling and sheer speed\u2014and if I lost that by running into slowpokes it wouldn\u2019t be much of a race.\nIt struck me that the thing to do would be to pass in the left-hand lane and immediately cut cross-traffic in the right lane\u2014get ahead and stay ahead; make all left turns from the right lane and all right turns from the left lane, zigzagging back and forth across the median like a...", "2_2_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-six in the morning</span>.\nThere was no moon.\n\"Well, I\u2019d give my right arm\u2014\" said Private Fallesthesia, Private Distenfluh, and Private Weber all together in one boyish Pennsylvania voice.\nThey stood at sentry duty outside a corps headquarters dugout. Their backs itched. Their feet itched. They had dug those trenches an hour before. They hated standing guard duty worse than any other part of the war. Standing guard duty made them feel inhuman. Standing guard duty made a kind of machine out of them. Out there in the night, they were worms.\n\"I\u2019d give my right arm for about a hundred pounds of marijuana,\" said Distenfluh. Everybody giggled.\n\"What does marijuana look like, anyway?\" said Fallesthesia.\nThere was no answer from anyone.\n\"My grandmother keeps a room in the attic,\" said Distenfluh, \"where she prays all the time, and she\u2019s got all kinds of religious books, and a special Bible for children. I\u2019d get Grandmother downstairs, quick, if the Anarchist in Europe said, 'Let your grandchild give his right arm for marijuana, or else!' \"", "2_10_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-six in the morning</span> when Sergeant Bracknell kissed his faithful $FIRSTNAMEF1 goodbye. \"Goodbye, old soldier,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1. \"Don\u2019t take any wooden Indians.\"\n\"Wooden Indian?\" said Bracknell.\n\"It\u2019s what some company clerk wrote on a piece of paper he handed me,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1 crisply. He was trying to be brave.\nBracknell was startled that there were no more shots to be fired, that the battle was over, that there was no more cigarette smoking, yarning, and carrying on before the last cold assault.\n\"Remember,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1, \"there\u2019s always another Wednesday.\"\n\"Thank you,\" said Bracknell. He turned, faced the rocky wall with the inscription. \"Goodbye, goodbye, goodbye,\" he whispered delightedly.\n\"Goodbye!\" shouted $FIRSTNAMEF1. The echo of his shout went on saying goodbye far longer than $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s voice had been saying goodbye.\nThat was what Bracknell liked about goodbyes\u2014the long-drawn-out hellos.", "0_22_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty in the evening</span>. The place was the living-room of Room 213 of the Magister Lodge. The place was dark, except for a circle of light about a lamp that stood on a floor about a foot square on an unleveled floor at an unleveled angle in the middle of the room.\nThe darkness was broken outside by bursts of fireworks. The darkness was broken inside by bursts of conversation. The conversation was being conducted by a youngish old man with a bobbed beard and a face like a punctured bicycle wheel. He was clad in pajamas. So were most of his eight guests, two of them women. One of the women had come in with a man of fashion, but she had not been asked to dance, and it was apparent that the other woman, a divorc\u00e9e with a scared smile and alluring pajamas that she obviously hated, was under the impression she was a guest.\nThe ninth member of the party was an old man with a walking stick, asleep on the couch. He had broken the circle of light around the lamp with his shadow and put out the light with his feet by coming in late, in the dark, and then stumping around.\nThe breath of the party was Robert Wilson.\n\"Yes...", "0_17_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five seventeen in the afternoon</span>.\nAt five forty-five, $FIRSTNAMEB2 gave up for the day. He scooted the barricades and fired a red flare over the empty shipyards.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 came then.\nHe flew to Newport in a rented helicopter. He wore a long black corduroy coat over his brown and yellow suit.\nIt was seven-twenty when he landed. $FIRSTNAMEB2, who had taken off his white plume and was now wearing ordinary commercial coveralls, happened to be looking at his wristwatch when $SURNAME1 came in.\n\"Mr. $SURNAME1,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2, reaching out a hand to a beautiful stranger. \"I\u2019m glad you\u2019re on time.\" He nodded gravely. \"I told the chopper to come at seven-twenty. You\u2019re ten minutes early.\"\n\"Is my son on board, Mr. $FIRSTNAMEM1?\" said $SURNAME1, shaking the offered hand limply. \"This is all somewhat irregular, you know. You\u2019ve stung me for an awful lot of money. I assume that you have my boy.\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB2. \"He\u2019s aboard.\"\n\"And he\u2019s in a sane and safe condition?\" said...", "2_15_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-six in the afternoon</span> of a fine day in Denver, Colorado. For a hundred dollars, I had a room in a motel somewhere between Denver and El Pasadeno. I was sitting on the bed in the motel room with half a pint of grapefruit juice in one hand and a bankroll in the other. I was waiting for my name to be called at the desk. I knew my name would be called at the desk in about two minutes. I was too drunk to be responsible, but I was reasonably confident that my physical condition would improve measurably once the friendly stewardess on the plane fanned me with her hand...well, not her hand...I mean that she would fan me with a giant pillow.\nBut, Jesus! What a story I have to tell...and it\u2019s almost time to board. Do you think you could hurry it up a little, here, Doc? I think we should be gone...\n< 5 >", "2_16_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThe time was exactly five minutes past four o'clock in the afternoon of October third, San Francisco, California. A Tuesday afternoon. Except for the necessary fifteen-minute interval when the car was in San Luis Obispo, we had been physically in San Francisco for about one hour and ten minutes. We had come sixteen hundred forty-three miles and were mentally farther away from her than ever. We were not near and we were not far. We were confused. Is there a precise analogy to this kind of travel? Is this America, God's own country?\nThe car in front of 282 Bleecker was still there. It was still cold. We sat and looked at the apartment of $FIRSTNAMEF4 from the cold car and mumbled.\n$FIRSTNAMEB3 hoarsely whispered in my ear, \u201c$FIRSTNAMEB6, this is surely the foremost moment of my life.\u201d", "1_4_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-six in the morning</span>. The place was Room 215 of the Flamingo Hotel\u2014one of the last bastions of dignity in this shameless town. My attorney was slumped at the desk of our suite, scribbling away for dear life in the mauve light of a gelid television set. His official position was slumped at the desk of our suite, scribbling away for dear life in the mauve light of a gelid television set. His official position was, \u201cDocumenting everything.\u201d The gig is this: I had four songs accepted by a local publisher\u2014\u201cAlligator Wine,\u201d \u201cHog Toll Bill,\u201d \u201cMaggie M\u2019Gill\u201d and \u201cPretty [Pretty, pretty, pretty].\u201d I was hoping to add a fifth, and time was running out.\nMy attorney had a different approach. He was stoned out of his mind. He had no idea what song he wanted, or what lyrics he would find, but he had awakened from a deep sleep to get cracking. And he was worried. Time had become a factor. They were expecting the last masterpiece at any moment.\n\u201cJesus,\u201d he moaned, \u201cwhat a fucked up game.", "2_23_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to midnight</span>.\nThe son looked at the mother, having made the barely audible sound. The sound was made by his grinding his heel slowly and rhythmically into the metal of the bench.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 gazed back at her son catatonically.\nA blind spot occurred in her field of vision.\nShe looked rapidly from side to side, stamping her foot and slapping her thigh, trying to keep her circulation going. What she saw, punctuating the blind spot, were two yellow images of the twelve-foot son.\nThe images were vermilion blurs, vibrating and filling her with sickening pain.\nThe pain made her dig her sharpened thumbnail into the flesh of her other arm.\nThis always helped, though it hurt her as much as the pain in her eyes.", "0_21_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-two in the evening</span>.\nThe space was an absolutely conventional living room.\nThe space was very nice. The furniture was rather decrepit, in pleasingly authentic and comfortable style, but there were items of new and glossy, if morbid, walnut furniture scattered through the room, picked, apparently, to set off the ancient stuff.\nThe walls and ceiling of the room were tinted a most intriguing light gray.\nThere was a gray floorcloth.\nThe gray color in the walls, ceiling, and floor of the room, if color it was, had been obtained by painting the walls white, the ceiling off-white, and the floor beige, and then applying coats of gray paint, each coat being successively slighter paler than the preceding coat, until an effect of many differing gradations of gray had been created.\nThe walls were hung with paintings that, although featureless when viewed from a normal distance, were seen on close inspection to be a most intricate and astonishing series of optical patterns and changes.\nSo also were the gray ceiling and gray floor.\nThere were gray\ncarpets on the gray floor.\nThere was a gray fireplace with a gray mantel. Above the fireplace was a gray head of a bow hunter, which appeared to...", "2_2_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after two o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"My God!\" said Magnum. \"What happened to you?\"\n\"My uniform,\" said Fenster. \"I just got it back from the cleaners twenty minutes ago.\"\n\"Where were you?\" said Stileman. \"We\u2019ve been looking for you for a half-hour.\"\n\"Sorry,\" said Magnum. He had had a good dinner, and he was feeling fine. \"Blotto and I had a little argument over at the Space Wanderer.\" He pointed to his soul patch. \"I didn\u2019t even know about this. I thought he was just being a stupid asshole, standing us up. I guess he is a little drunk, though.\"\n\"I certainly am,\" said Fenster.\n\"Got the time?\" said Stileman. He was checking his watch against the time chalked on the wall. The time on his watch was fifty-three minutes after two o\u2019clock.\n\"Just a moment,\" said Fenster. \"Yes,\" he said. \"My God!\"\nThe time was fifty-seven minutes after two o\u2019clock.", "1_16_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-nine in the afternoon</span>. The interior of the plane was dark. I picked up a copy of Time, a copy of Life. I couldn\u2019t see very well in the dark plane anyway. I couldn\u2019t concentrate. Their faces kept getting mixed up in my mind. I drank down a quick shot of Grand Marnier. I went back to the airport bar to get another for the trip home so I could drink it on the plane. At the bar I could feel the plane beginning to move, picking up speed to taxi. The bartender was telling the two Texans something about their nineteen-dollar bar bill from before.\nThey had their hats on, ready to go. They didn\u2019t want to hear anything else.\n\u201cWell,\u201d I said to the bartender, \u201cyour finest tradition is obviously in excellent hands.\"\n\u201cWhat\u2019s that?\"\n\u201cThose two guys who just checked out, with the bill.\"\n\u201cFuck them,\u201d said the bartender. He was a tanned blond kid from the Midwest. He only worked at the Dunes in the summer, to make some extra money.\n\u201cThey were fine,\u201d I said. \u201cRight out of Hemingway.\"", "0_16_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-four in the afternoon</span>, and the first breath of the sweet springtime riots of Sentimental Civilization had already drifted salaciously into Miss Wildwine\u2019s foyer. One expectant father, having missed his 4:43 at the upstairs window, paused to pass the time of day with $FIRSTNAMEF1 before bolting down to have another look. She was tweezing her eyebrows at a gilt mirror, and called back over her shoulder that she was expecting somebody, and he should hang on, if he could. Down below, the prospective father, a Mr. Xxx of Rhode Island, pulled a chair over to the mahogany column she had rested her ashtray on, and began to go for broke. From upstairs came $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s thin summer cocktail voice, saying weakly that she was so terribly sorry, she was afraid she was just simply unable to go to the Alhambra with\u2014finally at the window appeared the Bombardini features and her hand, and she was overcome with a sudden sneezing fit. The hand and features withdrew, and she called back, more gruffly now that her nose was stopped up, that her father was unavoidably detained, but would be in himself any minute, and would be awfully, awfully glad to see Mr...", "1_8_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span> when $SURNAME0 bent his head to kiss his wife goodbye before shambling out to meet his attackers.\nShe was sitting up in bed, wearing a baggy nightgown, her hair wild as Saint Elmo\u2019s fire. Her knees were drawn up, her arms wrapped about her knees. Her chin rested on her laced hands. Her eyes, fear-struck, watched $SURNAME0 coldly.\nBy her color and by the fever sweat on her brow, it was apparent that $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mate had been through a long, sick terrifying night.\n\"Goodbye, my love,\" said $SURNAME0. He spoke quietly, without reproach, without blame. He spoke as if his words had been carefully spoon-fed to him by an impersonal instructor.\n\"I\u2019m so sorry, old beloved,\" said $SURNAME0, \"if I\u2019ve altered in any way the trajectory of your life. I had to do what I did, no matter how much pain it gave you. I had to do it because it was my duty, because I was commanded to do it.\n\"I shan\u2019t pretend that I practice the religion into which I was born, into which you were born...", "2_1_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-three in the morning</span>.\nZachary $SURNAME0 was through time, out of time.\nThe End of the World had begun.\nOld veteran $FIRSTNAMEB2, the caretaker on Tralfamadore, was visited by two messages that night\u2014a bright yellow message, and a message in a brown wrapper.\nThe bright-yellow message came first, as a brilliant yellow dot 6,000 miles up in space. $FIRSTNAMEB2 couldn\u2019t see it, of course, and wouldn\u2019t have known how large or small, how far or near, the dot was if he had seen it. But he would have known, by the way it felt, that it had something to say to him about the life and purpose of Tralfamadore. He would have known that the color and mood of the message would be agreeable or worth waiting for.\nA moment later, $FIRSTNAMEB2 felt the welcome arrival of the message in the brown wrapper. $FIRSTNAMEB2 didn\u2019t see it arrive. It materialized, popped in around him like a burglar\u2019s surprise.", "2_12_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve sixteen post meridiem</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 arrived at the place the winning battalion had come to call a Chron system\u2014the 41,963,878,177,802,681 system.\nAt twelve nineteen P.M., $FIRSTNAMEB0 climbed onto a rock and informed his army that they were free to go wherever they pleased\u2014and good luck to them. They began forming up into adventurous homeward-bound clusters right away.\nThey began singing a chronology of marching songs.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 paid no attention to them. He simply sat down on a rock, rested his rifle across his lap, closed his eyes, and let the crowds in his head come out to play again.\nA half an hour later, $FIRSTNAMEB0 was still sitting on the rock. The crowds in his head were still playing their game. They formulated and executed unbelievable plans.\nWith his free hand, $FIRSTNAMEB0 reached into a pocket and took out the two souvenirs the Martian working-girl had given him. One was gray Earthling baby-food, the other was a purple, yellow, and orange drink mix called Mango-Mango.\nThe hints on the packets of those two items were as follows:", "0_21_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-nine in the evening</span>. A dark blue air-conditioned Cadillac limousine passed slowly through the main gate of the San Narciso racetrack, followed by a bushy green thirty-foot trailer being pulled by a tractor and painted with the word WIRELESS, in white on green. Clean-cut, crewcut, white young men riding with the casual indifference of matching equipment waited outside the main doors to the grandstand. A drunk in torn sport coat, expensive slacks and a necktie calling out prices for imaginary horses shuffled along the broad cement walk, nothing he said intelligible. He swayed about on worn shoes among the crowd emptying from the concert hall, looking for something.\nThe limousine passed the bushy trailer, circled the track twice to the delight of six grandstand slots filled by clean-cut, crewcut white young men, who were given identical cards with two all but blank sides. Their laughter, their muted remarks, were all filtered through telephone handset mouths. One straggled briefly, then caught up in time to match answers with his buddies, from card to card.\nTheir entertainment seemed almost terminal when the limousine pulled in among the tractors in the infield and discharged a portly figure in a dinner suit carrying a rolled...", "1_21_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-nine in the evening</span>.\nThe taxi driver was a friend of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s\u2014a believer in the religion of monotonous etiquette. In $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1\u2019s file, under $SURNAME0\u2019s disapproving eye, were three lesson plans for dealing with New England chauffeurs. Number One was \"Treat 'em rough.\" $SURNAME1 violated this precept by tipping his hat to his wife as he passed her. He slipped the chauffeur a dollar as he went by.\nThe chauffeur\u2019s inclinations, under Number One, would have been to glance surreptitiously into the back seat, to see if there was anything there he could let his imagination run around on for a while, before returning to the tedious business of being an automobile. $SURNAME1, by slipping him the dollar, was saying, \"You\u2019re a good boy. I like you the way you are.\" The chauffeur\u2019s eyes flared with delight. He held his head higher as he drove away\u2014held it higher, though he was only carrying the richest man in the state and his wife to the station.\nNow $SURNAME1 was standing at the head of the platform with $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2014without knowing $FIRSTNAMEF0. She was enormously glad he...", "1_15_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\nAnd around five-ten in the afternoon\u2014which was, of course, five-ten Pacific standard time\u2014a very sweet message indeed arrived at $SURNAME0\u2019s Newport home, inside a large golden time ball, from $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, who was then fifty-six thousand six hundred miles away, around the moon.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 had arrived at their estate just in time to save $FIRSTNAMEF0 from committing suicide.\nHer note enclosed the time ball, urged $SURNAME0 to regard the time ball as their best shot at contact and good relations in the future. The note ended with the words, \"Thy wife.\"\n$SURNAME0\u2019s reaction to the message was an odd one. He got a telegram form, filled in the blank for the telegram\u2019s address, and sent the form to his wife. The form read: \"?\"\nA SENSUOUS DISCONTENTMENT\nIt is neither blessed nor human to be concerned wholly with the pains and pressures of the moment. A man is either overawed by what is happening to him or desirous of what will happen to him next. And either conforms him to the mode of humanity...", "1_22_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten o'clock in the evening</span>. The scene was the living room of my New York apartment. My attorney had been sitting in silence across from me, staring into the low flame of a votive burning in a basilisk-labeled Sherlock Holmes pot that stood on the table between us. \u201cIsn't that so?\u201d he said finally, very quietly. I nodded, still staring into space, thinking of the first moment at which I had been given a choice\u2014several lifetimes and several years ago\u2014and had opted to turn left instead of right on a reef off the L.A. coast. \u201c...a reef between two tides, two worlds, two futures; and blessed is he who has the skill to navigate such straits, in such a powerboat...\u201d\nThe words seemed to come from a great distance. Or perhaps I only imagined them. A rushing sound filled my ears. The room was beginning to turn in gentle, greasy spirals. Suddenly I realized that I was stoned on marijuana\u2014and had been stoned, on and off, for almost three days on end. With no sleep. It had been necessary, somehow.\nI shook my head in another spasm of silent laughter. \u201cNo...not 'blessed...", "2_6_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\nGood News was waiting inside the space ship, now brilliantly lit. She was waiting for the Martian attack that would not be long. She was waiting to welcome home the husbands she had taught so well how to murder and endure.\nAt six twenty-nine, the attack came.\nIt came from the lighters that had remained high over the plain.\nThe lighters got on the inside of the space ship\u2019s defensive sphere with magnesium bombs that exploded magnesium flares.\nThrough the flares, the lighter crews could see and hit the ship\u2019s control room. Through the flares, the lighter crews could see and hit $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his companions, once they climbed through the air lock.\nSix grenades were lobbed into the control room and bounced around before they exploded.\nThen there wasn\u2019t any control room any more. There wasn\u2019t any attack any more, either, because the sphere was completely disabled, and all communications between the attack and the ship disabled, too.", "2_16_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Whatever they did, Metzger said, had begun.", "2_19_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-five in the evening</span>. The place was Newton, Massachusetts.\n\"Did what?\" said Chip. He was aware of his father looking for some sort of reaction. If a dog licked his ear, he would lick his ear. He was in no condition to do or say anything on his own.\n\"The man gave me the most incredible invitation! I was just thanking him for his praise at the panel discussion and we got to chatting, and somehow it happened. Incredible, isn\u2019t it?\"\n\"What?\" said Chip, who rubbed the moisture off his\near.\n\"How hospitable Harvard people are, when you get to know them!\" said Mailer. He beamed. He was thirty-two years old. \"His name is DiVozio. He sounded like a fine clear person last night at the panel, but now he blew me away, the warmth, the openness.\"\n\"Oh,\" said Chip. He knew there was something he could say to his father to make him happy. He just couldn\u2019t think of it. His head was full of Harvard, calculus, rock-and-roll, oh, and girls: not what he wanted to chew over with his father now.", "2_11_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt the bottom of the garden of the estate was a cemetery where old relatives of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and of the other owners of the estate were buried\u2014along with the casualties, over the years, of the celebrations that always accompanied the announcement of some dividend or other being paid.\nIt was a lovely cemetery, snuggling in green grass under a grove of pines.\nThe sun in the heavens was soft, like a late afternoon sun in a northern clime.\nAt the entrance to the cemetery was an angel with a trumpet. The angel had one wingbeat scheduled for 11:15 A.M., the other for 11:16 A.M.\nThe angel\u2019s feet were appallingly damp, since neither the grass nor the pines shed water very efficiently.\nThe approaching party from the space ship were two minutes late.\nThey had been expected at 11:09, 11:10, and 11:11.", "0_4_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-six in the morning</span>, and in a sanatorium room at Statler Hospital, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 lay dying in a bed. His spirit and his dog had already left the hospital by means of their private and mysterious world-ways, had already left the Solar System. $SURNAME0 was to go on to greater adventures in another universe. His dog was to go into unforeseen glory.\nHis hands lay clutching the sheet in the yellow glare of the alert light over the door. His best friend, Salo, was standing beside the bed.\nA tape recorder sang a song that $SURNAME0 had written. The song was chronologically situated between \"Faithfully Yours\" and \"Gone With the Wind.\"\n$SURNAME0 snatched the song out of the air as it finished\u2014swung it over his head without unfolding it.\n\" \u2019Tis a consummation devoutly to be wished, \" said $SURNAME0 from the song. This was a quotation from King Henry the Eighth.\n\"God has struck a meaningful relationship between our lives and the rhythm of the universe,\" said $SURNAME0 from the song. This was a free-form extrapolation from an S-Scale rating questionnaire.\n\"I have never known time to be so meaningful,\"...", "0_11_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to noon</span>.\nThere was utter silence in Latitude 38\u00b0 10\u2032, Longitude 174\u00b0 40\u2032.\nThen the first breath of a Hawaiian breeze came rustling over a million leaves, and at once the million leaves were converted into a million whistles.\nThings began to happen.\nAt 12:04, a coconut fell 37,000 feet and was chipped on a ledge 27,000 feet above boulder-strewn base camp.\nAt 12:05, a strong sexual vibration shot through Fleetwood\u2019s $SURNAME0 estate. It was communicated somehow to every substance in every structure and in every ruined structure, so that old stones palpitated and sullen ironWORK moaned its lust.\nAt 12:06, a coconut fell 35,000 feet and was not chipped.\nAt 12:07, President after President after President after President of the United States fell from a tree into a pool of rotted leaves far below.\nAt 12:08, New York City went mad.\nAt 12:09, an eighteen-year cic period came to an end for the lichen on Old Man Watling\u2019s tombstone. The old man\u2019s beard was nine feet long. It was combed out...", "1_0_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-two ante meridiem</span>; the place was a point on the wild and woolly New Jersey Turnpike where it runs through a nameless stretch of suburban wasteland directly across the street from Laurel Race Track, and where every Sunday morning at dawn wild and woolly youths in loud sport shirts stand around on the pavement and good red pieces of Delaware earth go thundering by in noisy and aromatic profusion. At this point the memory grows extremely hazy, the word must take over. The word says that at the precise moment, 12:22 and x:xx, all conscious ceased and I fell prey to that terrifying twilight abyss familiar to all takers of mescaline, that fearsome state wherein the mind, conscious of its own consciousness but importantly unable to focus that consciousness, succumbs at last to a black and total paralysis and so gives up the ghost in despair. Here words end. The following events, had I the power to recall them, would, I am convinced, recount for me all there is to know about that dark night and the black and roaring abyss.\nI surrendered and let the mescaline control me absolutely. To let such a drug overpower you like this is to give in to a kind of insanity, I think. It would be like going berserk in some...", "0_11_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven nineteen in the morning</span> of Wednesday, February thirteenth, $FIRSTNAMEB2-span 94-\u00be. He was a neighbor of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, which made him well-nigh a neighbor of anybody on earth. He was on Mars, having fled there from Newport, having fled there from the United States. He had fled because his harmonium had been requisitioned by the War Department.\nAccording to the agreement among all the nations of Earth, a Mr. Beatty had to turn over his harmonium to the War Department at the onset of any war. A threatened attack by Mars was, for all practical purposes, considered a state of war.\nMrs. Judith $SURNAME0 had accompanied her husband to Mars. Mr Beatty, a widower, had brought his daughter, $FIRSTNAMEM2, to Mars alone.\n$SURNAME1 had met Beatty only once before\u2014during the sizzle in 1948, when the politicians had held their banquets in the sky.\n$SURNAME1 was delighted to find Beatty on Mars. Beatty was equally delighted to find $SURNAME1. $SURNAME1 had had very little contact with his fellow-man since his wife had taken their three small daughters away. He and Beatty became drinking companions that very first morning.\n\"I need...", "1_15_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-five in the afternoon</span>. God knows what Martians were doing at that particular instant, all over Mars.\nThere was no traffic, since there were no cars on the lot.\n$SURNAME2 and Sykes stayed close to the space ship.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 watched the fence, waiting for a zap to touch him on the shoulder and give him a jolt. He would leave nothing to chance in explaining his presence\u2014if he was touched. He was going to say that he\u2019d been sleeping in the empty lot, and had seen no light, and had come closer to have a look at whatever it was. He figured it was something made by somebody who was a lot smarter than he was.\nHe would play the dullard\u2014if he was touched.\nThe odds were overwhelmingly against a touch. The angels didn\u2019t even have a guard system at the Executive Annex. Free will was protected\u2014not infringed. The system had been worked out by expert litigators, using probability charts drawn up by a squad of Ph.D. mathematicians.\nThe real menace to the system had nothing to do with economics. Basically, it was trivial. Only a few people would make any real fuss about being zapped. With a zap you had no memory...", "1_16_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-one in the afternoon</span> of Wednesday, November 1, 1996, according to a computer clock fastened to the right inside wall of the cubicle, where $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 maintained his headquarters. By no means did $SURNAME0 live in the darkest corner of the Pan American pantry. The suite of offices and corridors behind that corner of the pantry, which $SURNAME0 called the Bat Cave, ran down through the core of Pan American World Airways\u2019s corporate structure. Other passages and suites concealed behind other displays and in other corners of the giant food store served subsidiary corporate purposes.\nIt was a hugely and ingeniously complicated environment for a home\u2014and very attractive to a man who delighted in complications, who disliked crowds immensely, and who was sixteen years old.\nLately, the electronic clock on the wall had begun to quicken its pace. It now said that it was fifty-six seconds past five o\u2019clock. This would mean, in about sixty-three seconds, that the proper touching toe to a silk stocking would be instantly recognizable.\nSixty seconds passed. The brake pads of a freight elevator rasped metallically, hollowly within the walls. The freight elevator was a steel dumb-waiter that had but one cubicle at...", "1_11_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-three in the morning</span>, November 3, when I finally got through the maze that led to the desk of the chief of espionage. There was a sign on the desk: everything in triplicate. The chief of espionage\u2019s name was W. T. Sedgewick. He was busy. I sat down and waited. Then a shadow appeared. It was Chief Sedgewick.\n\u201cName?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cJones.\u201d\n\u201cSpell it?\" he said. I did.\n\u201cAddress?\u201d he said.\n\u201c1247 South Main Street,\u201d I said.\n\u201cTelephone?\u201d he said.\n\u201c3985 R. O. Call us collect,\u201d I said. \u201cYou\u2019ll hear from us within twenty-four hours.\"\n\u201cCable?\u201d he said.\n\u201cUse the same address,\u201d I said.\n\u201cJournal?\u201d he said.\n\u201cWhat?\u201d I said.\n\u201cWhat Journal do you work for?\" he said.\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the name?\u201d I said.\n\u201cIt\u2019s a big one. All...", "1_18_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-two in the evening</span>. I motioned the barman to another beer. He brought it, and I took a quarto from my pocket and peeled off eight pesetas. He glanced at them and smiled. \u201cYou ought to know what a good price that is for this beer, sir.\u201d\nI nodded. \u201cVery true.\u201d I had paid ten francs for it yesterday at the Col\u00f3n.\n\u201cYou know it\u2019s funny,\u201d he smiled, \u201cbut all the gentlemen in the bar paid me a great compliment. They said none of you Americans ever drink twice of the same thing.\u201d\nI smiled. \u201cThat\u2019s true.\u201d\nHe nodded. \u201cAll of you?\u201d\n\u201cAll of us.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s a great compliment you paid me.\u201d\n\u201cAbsolutely.\u201d\nHe bowed. \u201cEnjoy yourself.\u201d\n\u201cAlways.\u201d\nI took a sip of the beer. It was excellent. It put me in fine spirits to know that all the gentlemen were picking up the check. This way a beer at the Grand Hotel was four pesetas. At the bar of...", "2_15_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Nine minutes had passed since $SURNAME0 opened the gates of the time-tunnel.\nOutside the mansion, scores of cleaned and healthy but still crazy people from Chronos were streaming through the open gates, through the tunnel, and into the past.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 decided to join them.\nShe would go with them into the past, and contribute what she could to forming a nucleus for a new Martian culture, a culture that would be economically self-sufficient and blissfully simple.\nIt would be a world of loving and clean shaven good neighbors.\nShe looked with loathing on the millions of dollars\u2019 worth of furs, jewels, and laces in her wardrobe. She unpinned the brooch that had held her hair in the flip back in Newport. She threw the sable coat onto a chair. With a three-inch scalpel secured to a flexible 12-inch shaft\u2014a surgical instrument she had used on $SURNAME0\u2019s sinuses\u2014she slashed her ball gown from neckline to hemline.", "0_23_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-two in the evening</span>. The Brain lay on the bar before us. The Brain was huge beyond belief. I could only stare at it. And at two dollars a shot I was no better off than before. Remi grinned. There should have been three Brains on the bar. One was still out at the tire store. The bartender put the shot of Brain before her. Pilar looked at it, drew back, and then darted forward, took the glass, threw the shot down into her throat, and then drew back, gasping. Her face became green with horror. I looked at Remi. She didn\u2019t look any too good. The bartender was calling out for more money. \u201cOne\u2019s plenty for a girl!\u201d cried Remi. \u201cOr you could have water in it . . .\u201d\nWe got out of there and went to have bread and cheese and wine at a joint on the Rue Dauphine, where there was one lonely young waiter and a proprietor who sat with his feet on a chair to keep the flies off the counter. They brought big pitchers of red wine and the biggest and blackest and crustiest bread ever baked. The three of us sat at one table and shoveled bread into...", "0_19_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span> of October 29, 1992. The scene was the living room of $SURNAME1\u2019s penthouse in Newport, Rhode Island. The room lay more or less in the center of all space and time, for it was in this room, through the crazy quilt of $SURNAME0\u2019s prophecies, that all the lines of $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s ecology and all the lines of $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s and $SURNAME1\u2019s histories had been adroitly and elegantly woven together. The room\u2019s furnishings had been managed, by a master\u2019s hand, to suggest that the living creatures in the room were at the center of an immensely complicated web.\nSince $FIRSTNAMEF0 was the only woman in the room, the web had the look of a widow\u2019s veil.\nExcept for himself and $SURNAME0, every creature in the room was winged. Every winged creature suffered from the disorder called holy ecstasy. The condition was thought to be a symptom, either of next-to-last arrivism, or of next-to-last vegetarianism. The term \"next-to-last\" was a pejorative coined by one of the Titanic species of Earth. A Titanic species was one so inordinately...", "0_12_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes past noon</span>. Preparations were being made for the celebration. The news of the recovery spread quickly. Radio communication had been re-established with Mars as the tumultuous news made its way into the cosmos. In an infinite number of tiny parallax-points throughout the Milky Way Galaxy, celebrations broke out among mankind\u2019s space colonists.\nHHgHgHgHgHgHgHg\n\"Roger\u2014\"\n\"God Almighty, $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014\"\n\"There\u2019s just no way\u2014\"\n\"Just kick sand in the feller\u2019s face, Roger. That\u2019s all I can think of,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"I can\u2019t kick sand in his face. He\u2019s my father.\"\n\"Well, then just ignore him,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Oh, sure,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"That\u2019ll be easy.\"\n\"I\u2019m afraid so,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Oh\u2014my\u2014\" said $SURNAME1 quietly. The enormous realization that he was alive had overpowered him, had reduced him to awe. He felt, too, the presence of many people around him, strangers, who would begin asking him questions and making demands on him before long. Questions and demands...", "0_0_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute after midnight</span>. The Salo had been floating free of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s chest for a full second before the strange janitor in the sealed and soundproof booth behind $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head malfunctioned and began, very softly, to weep.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 showed little sign of surprise or recognition when the Salo rejoined him. He showed very little emotion of any kind. He sat quietly in his narrow blue bunk, his big hands held easily in his lap.\nWhen the Salo had settled into harmonious oscillation before $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s nose, $FIRSTNAMEB0 blinked his eyes as though awakening from an agreeable dream. He stared with great sadness and sympathy at the little creature in front of him. His deep voice trembled a little as he said, \"It\u2019s you again, little buddy. Were you lonely for me?\"\nThe Salo made a sad noise.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 laughed ruefully, explained to the sympathetic Salo, \"I got to take you everywhere I go, buddy. I\u2019m afraid that\u2019s the rule.\" He shook his head. \"How did you get back here?\" he asked the Salo. \"Who let you out?\"\nThe Salo made a sad noise.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0...", "0_16_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and McCarthy and I were leaving San Francisco in a long silver Ford full of young men and girls with flowers in their hair. Every girl had a box of candied cherries, and we stopped and bought kegs of beer and bottles of cheap champagne. The Ford was going East. East is a direction, not a destination like West. Most of the people in the car didn't say where they were going, but Lee Ann's brother, who had escaped from McAlester prison, Oklahoma, said he was going to New York City, and that was good enough for us. We locked the old man in the car and told him he'd have to damn well wait there till we got back. He struggled with the door and tried to stick his head out. He had nothing to drink but weak tea because we'd finished the bottle of whisky by the Ferry Building, and he was hot and miserable with the door shut. He wanted to buy a gun to shoot the breeze. I took out a deck of cards and laughed at him. I went inside the cabin of the Ford for a minute, where some of the girls were, and when I came out there were two young gay fellows in the front seat with McCarthy, and the old man had mysteriously disappeared. I...", "1_16_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to five o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $SURNAME0 and his dog and Skip arrived all in a heap in the Department of $FIRSTNAMEB2-synclastic Infinitives.\nThe clocks had stopped at five. One hundred thousand $FIRSTNAMEF1-line buses, parked nose-in to the sidewalks all across the west, were mute.\nA hundred thousand ticket takers, chair-moppers, maintenance men, ushers, baggage handlers, receptionists, beauticians, and travel-agency personnel were faced away from their occupations, looking reverently up at the palace of Monday\u2019s Dawn.\nOne hundred thousand knees-were bent.\nOn the steps of the palace stood a special corps of just over two hundred musical performers from the Galactic String Quarter, the outstanding musical organization of the Solar System. These musicians were engaged in rendering a complicated twelve-tone piece commissioned by Monday herself, honoring her creator, her husband, and her son, who that day were to be put to a terrible test.\nThe author of the test was gleefully aware of the honors paid his wife and child. The test, moreover, was a delicious spontaneity. He had not known of the honors in advance. All had been arranged by $SURNAME1 and the $SURNAME0 Image-Brewer to show once and...", "1_10_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty in the morning</span> of Wednesday, October first, and our name was now Leg-Warm, Incorporated. The stock transfer had been executed by Julian, my brother, and myself as President and Vice-President, respectively, with the assistance of a reptilian broker and a certified public accountant. The bulk of the shares, to my surprise and disgust, were in the name of myself and my brother; this meant that the most vital and significant portion of my recent net worth, the four thousand dollars I\u2019d made by betting on the horse race, was hidden in my brother\u2019s name alone. Of the total five-twenty shares, I owned four ten-thousandths and Julian owned ninety.\nGreat stuff. The owner, president and guiding spirit of Leg-Warm, Incorporated was a person with four ten-thousandths of one percent of the stock of his own company. Leg-Warm might produce gold mine, orphan\u2019s fund, and big profit for all we knew, but I had nothing to hang my hat on as far as my own interests were concerned. In only two ways did the new company begin to appear to have any potential; one was that it was the only vehicle I could think of to get me and my...", "2_0_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes past midnight</span>. I turned out the light. Suddenly $FIRSTNAMEB5 put her arms around me and pressed her head tight against my shoulder, and I felt her crying. I could feel the convulsions of her shoulders.\n\u201cDon\u2019t, darling. Please don\u2019t. Don\u2019t do that.\u201d\nShe could not stop crying. Suddenly she pushed me away.\n\u201cOh, my God! Oh, my God! I must be going to pieces.\u201d\nShe was sitting up in bed, holding her shoulders tight. Her face was drawn and white, and tears were running down her cheeks. She put her hands over her face. I sat on the edge of the bed beside her. I felt the bed sway as a taxi went past on the Gran Via. $FIRSTNAMEB5 put her head back on the pillow, leaning toward me.\n\u201cDon\u2019t mind. I must be going to pieces.\u201d\nThen she put her head on my shoulder and I put my hand around her. We sat that way for a long time until she was asleep.", "0_11_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to noon</span>.\nMs. Cox turned her head and looked out the window. She saw a creature in the shade of a tree, shivering with cold, trying to sing \u201cO Susanna,\u201d unable to cope with the intricate melody, breaking, failing.\n\u201cThank you again,\u201d said Ms. Cox. She was still groggy. She shook herself and arose. Clarice, waiting patiently on the bed, was whipped into a wastebasket; whoosh snap swoosh. Lenore let her crinoline slip quietly to the floor.\n/a/\n\u201cBlort, blort blort?\u201d\n\u201cWhat?\u201d\n\u201cI want the red pens. Those are the red pens. You are holding the white pens. Give me the red pens, or I shall not do your taxes.\u201d\n\u201cOh, for goodness\u2019s sake, Rick. I\u2019m doing as well as I can.\u201d\n\u201cHow many pens are there?\u201d\n\u201cSix.\u201d\n\u201cAnd are they red-pen coloured?\u201d\n\u201cYes.\u201d\n\u201cThen give me the three pens...", "0_2_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span> when the door of Room 419 at the Hotel Cubistico opened wide and the boy with the dark face came out of the room and stood at the threshold He stood there long enough to give the impression that he was afraid to go out into the air, and then he stepped into the hallway and walked directly toward Rumsey and Kinn.\n\"Ah, s\u00ed,\" he said speaking Spanish. \"Se\u00f1or Rumsey?\"\n\"Right,\" said Rumsey. \"You must be Blanco Bolter.\"\n\"Blanco Bolter? No. I am Carlos.\"\n\"Oh,\" said Rumsey. \"You\u2019re not Blanco Bolter?\"\n\"Blanco Bolter?\" said the boy. \"No. He is not here. I am Carlos.\"\n\"You\u2019re not Blanco Bolter?\"\n\"That is the name in the hotel register,\" said the boy. He held up the register opened to Blanco Bolter\u2019s signature.\n\"Aren\u2019t you the chauffeur?\" said Rumsey.\nThe boy shrugged. \"You call me what you wish. I drive car.\"\n\"Are you supposed to be at the hotel?\" said Rumsey.\n\"Still? No, se...", "0_3_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-four in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB3 saw Hill'sbert on the street. All depends what kind of a day it is. Everybody in Denver rolled from the middle west ahead of a long iron shadow in the sky; they wear snapbrim hats and suitcoats with belts outside and the women wear heels-on their square-toed shoes and their stockings are white as Lenin advised in the trenches: \u201cBolshevism is the red dawn.\u201d They knew that was right; they knew further that it is onward and ever westward. They are afraid of only two things--red plotters who write stinking letters to the papers and foreigners who laugh at their language and make jokes about Gilpin and Coors. Those two things upset them gravely; they cannot understand the plotters but they know the foreigners are here to queer their upward climb in life. All the gray. Manes, balls, bars of the ex-graveyard tumbleweeds that blow through town know this. Their pet state's so rich that if anything goes wrong, it doesn't go wrong nationwide like subnormal Alabama, moaning cotton; Colorado skips right over it and goes right on to the latest legalized racket assured by well-meaning state law. I remember telling one of these iron...", "0_14_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span> of the first day of October.\nHe did not remember what he had done with the money he had borrowed from Old Sallee. That annoyed him. He would have to send them a check at once.\nHe remembered something else. It was October on the great iron horse belting north into Connecticut. He had not sent his wife any message or wire.\nWell, she would not worry until late in the afternoon, and then, being Mrs Heaney, she would surely figure out what train he would take, and perhaps vaguely wonder why he had not wired.\nNow, belting north on the old rail line, with all the rhythm and all the weariness of an ancient and broken-springed Pullman, they hurled along.\nMoskwa, at the controls, nodded and snored into his microphone. McLean dozed, remembered he was supposed to be keeping his eye alert for sabotage, and looked anxiously out the window for saboteurs. Strang, beside Flower, scanned anxiously for saboteurs. Groceries, beside McLean, scanned anxiously for saboteurs.\nHe should have sent a wire. Disgusted with himself, he looked at his wrist watch. Twenty-nine minutes before three o...", "2_19_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-six in the evening</span>.\nA three-year-old girl who had a friendly relationship with her mother\u2019s modeling kit had painted her fingernails bright red.\nA very old but contented man who had built Old and New Spirit into a trillion-dollar corporation over a fifty-five-year period of time was coming home.\nDesperado, war hero, man of the people though he was, was the richest man in the Solar System, with the possible exception of Richard M. Nixon.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was coming home.\nThe great golden doors of the mansion were open wide.\nThe Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0 stepped out onto the terrace to announce from where his wife and himself would view the homecoming.\nThe Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0 was a short man with a red, windburned face and a belly the size of a foot ball. His watery blue eyes were so pale as to suggest a defect in the lens, a defect that rendered the eyes world- renowned for clarity. The defect was a fraud. The Reverend C. Horner $SURNAME0 had seen his mother\u2019s lover come through the parlor window when he was nine years old.", "2_5_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty in the morning</span>. I was walking down Hollywood Boulevard, heading for Grauman's Chinese Theatre. There was nobody in sight and the sky was just turning from gray to light blue behind the hills. All the streetlights were out. I felt great, loose and fine, and I wanted to get the hell out of town quick before the stores opened up and all the bus drivers went crazy. Things could get awkward.", "2_11_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog materialized in the middle of the bed of Mars on the floor of the bedroom of the house in New York.\nIt was eleven forty in the morning on Tuesday, January 2, 1949.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 materialized in their accustomed space suits. $SURNAME0 was a man thirty-six years old, with a muscular, though trim figure. He had very short, springy, dirty-blond hair, very clear blue eyes, a very tanned skin, and rugged cheekbones.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 was an oversized, shaggy, dark-brown Malley milk- retriever. He was eight Earth years old\u2014though he had supposedly been born on Mars. He had long floppy ears, long hind legs ending in big feet, and a very short tail.\n$SURNAME0 wore a patch over his right eye.\n$SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 were not visitors to Mars. Mars was their home.\nNor was their reappearance by the bed in the middle of the morning unusual. $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 rarely showed themselves in the bedroom. For several hours each day, they were busy in tunnels under the house.", "2_4_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-one in the morning</span>. I started out with Old Bull Lee and Raoul, the French-Canadian. \"Where\u2019s my voodoo?\" said Old Bull, peering around. \"You left your voodoo in the house.\" He pretended to be crazy about voodoo. But underneath he had a fear that he couldn\u2019t keep under control. \"Here\u2019s mine,\" said Raoul, pinching himself. \"Now I got a voo-doo what make me mojo. No more saints, no more apostles, no more Jesus, no more Mary, no more God come bust in my hair now I got a mojo what make cross my heart and keep me safe a-ways in de jungle what coming.\"", "2_6_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Strength through Joy,\" Reverend Hillel read from his little black book as he stood at attention before $SURNAME1.\n\"Don\u2019t tell anybody,\" said $SURNAME1 to his invisible audience in the empty city, \"but I\u2019m a B.A.\"\nA B.A.\u2014a Bachelor of Arts. He had earned his B.A. during an odd period in his life, during a year when he had surprised himself by waking up once or twice a month.\n\"In this fort of rotten wood\u2014\" said Reverend Hillel.\n\"With a couple of telephone poles holding up the roof,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"God has put you there,\" said Reverend Hillel.\n\"You hear that, Bonfilio?\" said $SURNAME1. \"God put me here.\"\n\"To protect\u2014\" said Reverend Hillel.\n\"I\u2019m protecting the best air in the world,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"To protect the lazy, lying, lustful surfers of Malibu,\" said $SURNAME1. \"You hear that? I\u2019m protecting the surfers.\"\n\"Thine is a watcher\u2019s post,\" said Reverend Hillel.", "1_16_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four nineteen in the afternoon</span> by the clock on the dash. We hadn\u2019t eaten all day. I was driving. Up ahead I saw a promising sign with large letters reading BAR & GRILL. The sign was white, with a green border, flaked and peeling. The place, when we got there, turned out to be an abandoned roadhouse made out of concrete blocks, with a caved-in roof, no glass left in the windows, and decayed floorboards lying weedy and soggy inside. The only sign of life was a desultory scattering of moths that had come out at dusk in the smell of rain and wet weeds and decaying wood. The gray rotting Skelly gas pumps were still there, drooling rust. \u201cWon\u2019t be tee-totaler hell for miles,\u201d said my attorney. \u201cLet\u2019s check the trunk for wine and we\u2019ll be on our way.\u201d You could hear water falIing and running back of the building. Roots I saw, had eaten big holes in the floor.\nThere were California poppies and tasselled yellow flags growing around in the weeds. We were at a crossroads, somewhere in the southernmost part of the...", "1_8_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to nine o'clock in the morning</span>, Central War Time, Tuesday, March 12, 1955. The place was a haunted house in Watling Street, Newport, Ohio. It was there that as Son of Adam had talked to a most beautiful and serious naked baby, Him had come a bang like the bang that is supposed to end the world, and a grunt like the grunt of a monster, more grunt than bang.\nAnd the grunt had meaning and the bang had meaning. They meant that a part of the universe had been separated from another part of the universe by the amount that is supposed to keep things apart forever; that a woman and a man had been separated from a child, by sleep and nothingness, by Him and the Void, forever.\nAnd in the bedroom of the haunted house in Newport, Ohio, blood had begun pounding in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s ears.\nAnd in the bedroom of the haunted house in Newport, Ohio, a man turned over in his sleep, groaned, wakened.\nHis name was $FIRSTNAMEB2. His dog tags had the name Chronos spray-painted on them.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 opened his eyes. He raised his hands to his head, held his head like a curator holds a priceless vase. $FIRSTNAMEB2 groaned again. It...", "0_21_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine twenty-one in the evening</span> of or about the first or second of October, 1986; the place, an amphitheater carved from the living rock beneath the north end of the island where here the wall rears up from the river. Stone steps lead down from the amphitheater, stones laid by the island\u2019s original inhabitants, and the steps lead down and down and down to the water\u2019s edge, since the island is a large one, some five hundred acres in size. The only ways off the island are a primitive three-stage ferry from the north shore and a cable-guided vehicle monorail from the east shore and down again to the lake. In the late forties and early fifties, the island was developed into a madwoman-cum-senior citizen\u2019s rest home by the various state and federal agencies who had rented the area off Thomas M. Eliot, the chief Canadian shareholder of Worldwide Electromatics, the firm that at the time controlled all living things. Everything went well here in the midst of natural surroundings, until two fire-and-brimstone evangelists, while leading a retreat ceremony for one hundred and eleven people, overlooking a rock pool churned by the naked feet of the elderly, were ironically struck dead by a...", "0_13_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 was arrested. The charge was disturbing the peace. He was put into a truck with his harmonium, and taken under guard to the address of the nearest available relative.\nA three-man squadron was assigned to take $FIRSTNAMEM2 off to the retirement home. They got into a patrol wagon with $FIRSTNAMEM2, a spirituous flask, and a box of cigars. They figured to make the round trip in eight hours, with two hours on theparental doorstep and two hours in some favorite barrooms, drinking at Army expense.\nThey were to pick up $FIRSTNAMEB1 on their way back. Old Walter flew down from his patrol station on the edge of the war zone, to say good-by to $FIRSTNAMEB1. So that $FIRSTNAMEB1 and his great-grandfather were in the patrol wagon together, and Old Walter was outside, waving.\nThe squadron made remarkably good time at the outset. They had a goal\u2014the house of $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s uncle\u2014now, and the discipline of an Army organization to help them reach it. They sang, they drank, they smoked. And the harmonium played \"Marching Through Georgia.\"\nIt wasn\u2019t until they were in the part of Brooklyn known as Bushwick that they discovered their cigar...", "0_16_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-five in the afternoon</span>; the place was the Atoll Gymnasium of the United States Military Academy, West Point, New York. The crowd was composed of graduates of the Academy and their wives, friends, girl friends, and relatives, invited by the Athletic Association, or invited by the Association\u2019s wives, friends, girl friends, and relatives, who had themselves been invited by the President of the United States through the Commandant of Cadets. There was also a sprinkling of celebrities, honored guests, and plain nobodies, who in a joint effort composed the receiving lines and search parties. Rumor had it that they were the ones who finally found the Athletic Director after one of these formal affairs...he\u2019d been in the punch, with a grand piano on top of him.\nSuperintendent Godwin, a tall, lean Jerseyman wearing shined shoes, his dress blues, and an embarrassed smile, stood rigid at the head table and stared at a place somewhere over the crowd\u2019s right shoulder. Every so often he tried to say something, and every so often his teeth would clamp on the lower portion of his upper lip. Time after time he would shake his head and sigh. At his side were two Army colonels: one wore glasses and looked...", "0_2_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-nine in the morning</span> and I was elated because I was about to embark on one of the greatest adventures of my life. And, now that my work in New Orleans was finished, all I had to do was wander around the country till I wound up, starving and ragged and crazy, on some dark street in big, sinister L.A.\nI walked downstairs. My attorney was sitting on the floor beside Lucy. I could tell he wanted to kiss her, but he was only courteous with her. Lucy said to both of us, \"I want to go with you.\"\n\"Absolutely not,\" I said. We walked out of the hotel. My attorney piled into the back seat of the car and stretched out, pretending to be asleep.\nEverybody but me had been drinking a lot. The atmosphere was sharp and sloppy. My attorney stretched and then sat up in the back seat, grinning secretly. \"Let's hit the road,\" he said.\n\"You never sleep,\" said Lucy in the front seat. \"You damn fool,\" said my attorney. We drove across the District Line and into Washington D.C., where we ate breakfast. When the hash-slinger brought the food to our table my attorney said to me, \"What are you going to...", "2_6_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 left his cubicle for the elevator lobby.\nThe elevator lobby was on the first floor of the shelter. In the natural gravity of Mars, the first floor would have been thirty-six feet below bedrock.\nAs $FIRSTNAMEB0 went down the stairs to the lobby, a man was coming up the stairs. $FIRSTNAMEB0 did not like the man. The man was $FIRSTNAMEB1, the champion tanzarian.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 was thirty-nine years old, and he had been the champion tanzarian for twenty-one years. He had been a champion schoolboy wrestler and a champion schoolboy foot ball player when he was growing up in the Stonington, Connecticut, area, and he had gone on to be the champion wrestler and foot ball player at Yale.\nAt the age of twenty-one, in his senior year at Yale, $FIRSTNAMEB1 had been shipped to Mars.\nHis crime had been taking the Declaration of Independence seriously.", "0_15_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. It was three minutes to four o'clock when $FIRSTNAMEF0, putting her hand on my arm and pointing with a quick breathless smile, said half under her breath, \u201cThere's my husband now.\u201d\nShe had not seen him for eight years. He stood six-feet-five and had a wingspread from fingertip to fingertip of ten feet. It was hard to realise that he was fifty-seven years old. His great height had something to do with this; also the robustness of his massive frame and the enormous wingspread.\nBut sophistication had missed fire somewhere and genuine grandeur had survived.\n\u201cThere is my husband, now,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF0, and she put her hand on my arm and her fingers were cool. Sometimes when people are happy it shows in their faces and sometimes even a stranger can see it. The corners of Mrs $SURNAME1's mouth went up a little and showed white when she smiled; there was a little brightness in her eyes.\n\u201cGood afternoon, Mr $FIRSTNAMEF1,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\nAnd I said, \u201cGood afternoon, Mr $SURNAME1,\u201d and his large brown right hand closed over mine, and he turned to $FIRSTNAMEF0 and kissed her on the cheek...", "1_0_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-one a.m.</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 was finally able to press the button, ending the nightmare\u2014and waking up all the other people sleeping in the rest rooms on the basement floor of the office building.\nThey came out, rubbing their eyes. They didn\u2019t recognize $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"You get any sleep, son?\" one of them asked.\n\"Yup,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt very good.\nHe felt wonderful.\nHe went dancing up the basement stairs, up, up to the main floor\u2014boom-boom, boom-boom, boom-boom\u2014with all the people on the floor standing around in their nightgowns and bathrobes, staring at him in amazement. They thought he was crazy, thought he was the goofiest-looking soldier they had ever seen. He was still wearing his seabag coverall shorts and still had his soldier\u2019s hair.\nHis legs and face were black with many days\u2019 growth of fine, rust-red hair.\n\"Woo-ee, dog-gone!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0, standing in the middle of the floor. \"How you all?\"\n\"Go to bed, soldier,\" said an old man, \"before you get...", "1_10_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten nineteen in the morning</span> of a hot, sunny Saturday in early June that this story began. Ten nineteen on a Saturday morning means a good, clean start to something good and clean. Or, as my father would say, \"It was fresh enough to keep the old Adam in him hid.\"\nThat is as safe a way as any of telling it.\nI was supposed to take my Italian relative, Carlo Marx, out and introduce him to the Wharton boys. The Whartons were our one rich relations, a spinster and three young men, one of whom, Ted, was to take me out fishing in Canada that coming summer, if his exams in July let him.\nThe Whartons had promised to give Carlo their stamp-collection for twenty dollars. He was to come at ten. It was as safe a way as any of coming to them.\nIt was a race between whether Ted could make out that summer or not and whether I could be a good enough blind man to them or not. You had to see the Whartons to get the style. They had to be blind enough to protect themselves before they could do any good for me. And they were getting the kind of education where they had to know everything so they could reject it all and still be...", "2_1_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after one o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"Goshdang it, you scared me,\" McRevem said. \"I thought it was the TV man.\"\n\"Hello, $FIRSTNAMEF3,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nMcRevem found a bottle in the pocket of his bathrobe and tipped a shot of whiskey into his mouth. He zipped the robe closed. He burped heartily.\n\"I\u2019m right glad to see you,\" McRevem said. \"I\u2019m right glad. I was listening to a program, and it scared me the way it made sense.\"\n\"How are you, $FIRSTNAMEF3?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"I\u2019m right well, thank you.\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEF3, I\u2019ve come to say goodbye,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"I\u2019m going away.\"\n\"Where?\" said McRevem.\n\"I\u2019m going to Columbus, $FIRSTNAMEF3,\" $FIRSTNAMEF0 said. \"I have to avoid Factory interference from now on.\"\n\"You\u2019re crazy, $FIRSTNAMEF0,\" said McRevem. \"I\u2019m too weak to even chase you.\"", "0_0_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after midnight</span> close\u2014to the minute, as Paul Ziino, who was a rather precise fellow and kept very accurate time, could testify later.\nAnonymous in a crowd of thousands and tens of thousands, $FIRSTNAMEB2 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 did not know what was going on. They didn\u2019t know that they were going to be married in five minutes just like any other happily affianced couple\u2014and sworn in as soldiers at the same time.\nOnly a few of the luckier soldiers had known in advance what was going to happen. There weren\u2019t more than a hundred birds who could honestly say that they had seen their own names on the list before the big night. This handful was known to their comrades as the smart birds\u2014the birds who would be saved long before the big day.\nRed Patent\u2014Heels was not one of the smart birds. Red Patent\u2014Heels knew nothing. He thought the big day would possibly be the first day of basic training, when a soldier would naturally be given a lot of shots.\nNone of the other three thousand paratroopers knew as much as Red Patent\u2014Heels. None of them knew anything. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were typical of the draftees. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2 had only...", "0_22_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-eight in the evening</span>. I walked in front of the Palace Hotel. There were two people ahead of me walking very slowly. I wheezed up beside them. They were a young woman and her young brother. They stopped in front of a newsstand to make a decision. I reached around the brother and took a Look at the Saturday Evening Post. It cost ten cents. I put a dime in the man\u2019s hand. He pushed two pennies out. I took one penny back. I gave the woman her dime back. She gave her brother a dime. I gave the newsdealer a penny for change.\n\u201cWhat am I bid, gentlemen, for this here absolutely genuine Gy-ray spinner?\" I said. \"The genuine Inland MFG. Co. of Chicopee Falls, Mass., U.S.A. , U.S.A. most positively guaranteed?\u201d I pushed the spinner back and forth on its creaky little axis. It Was a flat, clunky old thing. But it made a pleasant sound, when you rubbed your thumb over it. It was a little concave metal plate with a dozen radiating spokes in it, a dozen circle segments. The flat plate was three inches across. The whole...", "2_18_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-nine in the evening</span>.\nThe crowd watched the round-shouldered little figure climb into the Paramount and Imperial. He waved. The car was closed. The ceremonial motorcade lurched into motion again.\nA wail went up from the crowd.\n\"Pleasant dreams, Mr. $FIRSTNAMEM2!\" cried $SURNAME0. \"Pleasant dreams!\"\n$SURNAME0, in his yacht in Newport, was telling his wife all about the mystery of happiness again.\n\"Of course it isn\u2019t really a mystery at all,\" he said genially. \"It\u2019s a matter of getting and spending\u2014which means, in plain American, a matter of keeping fed and clothed and sheltered and amused. The amusing part is relatively easy, you see. The difficulty comes in getting and spending.\n\"Now that we\u2019re in the Solar System, my dear,\" he said, \"we\u2019ve come into a civilization where the people get along fairly well at keeping fed and clothed, though amusing themselves is still quite a problem. In the time space you used to come from, my dear\u2014the time-space you think of as Home\u2014the people had their problems licked in all three departments.", "2_2_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>, Newport time, when two figures ran past the statue of Empress Lakshmi. The smaller of the two figures carried a small white dog in its teeth. The two figures sprinted up a flight of iron steps. At the top of the steps was the dock from which motor launches of the Casino d\u2019Avero came and went. The larger figure held an automatic pistol in one hand; he shot open a padlock with it. Both figures then ran out on the dock and leaped into one of the waiting launches.\nThe smaller of the two figures, it may be noted, ran awkwardly. It was obviously hampered by the weight of the dog, and it spat the dog out when it got aboard the launch.\nTen seconds later, while activity in the Casino d\u2019Avero was still unsettled and confused, the launch and its surprising crew were on their way across \u62afurano Bay and into the Atlantic, bound for Key West, Florida, and perhaps for parts unknown.\nBUT REMEMBER THE THUNDER\nDOCTOR GRINSTEAD, TREATMENT CENTER FOR SURREALISTICALLY STUNG PSYCHICS, GROUCHELSDORF, N Y.", "0_8_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span> of a late summer day in San Francisco in the Bay Area of California of the United States of North America, the place was the roof of a very high building with a commanding view of much of the city, the particular sector with the commanding view from this particular roof was called Pacific Heights, the particular very high building was called $SURNAME0\u2019s High House, the particular rooftop was called The Winch-ster's Outlook, the particular human beings were called $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, and $FIRSTNAMEB2.\n$SURNAME1 had spread a black silk mourning shroud over the parapet, and was lying on his back in it. His hands were folded gallantly over his breast. He had decided, while waiting for something to entertain him, that he would try to look like a statue of himself.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 was standing by the parapet. Her hands were clasped in back of her. She was wearing a knee-length white smock, and all but one button at the top were unbuttoned. The smock was wet in exciting places in a provocative way, and had to be dry in places and ways, too. It was shamelessly wet most in the region of $FIRSTNAMEF0\u2019s heart, and was driest at...", "0_6_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-nine in the morning</span> when I drove the Big Red Scooter across the causeway that averages out the island of Ibogoff in the middle of Lake Angelfish in Fort Pierce, Florida. From there, I headed northeast on U.S. 1 toward West Flagler Street, in Miami. I was starving. I hadn't eaten since Ron Morgan thumped me in Bannister's tavern in John's Pass four hundred and twelve miles and eleven hours earlier. This seemed a likely place to eat --merely a wide place in the road called Hialeah.\nI turned off U.S. 1 and cruised gently down a street that looked like any old street in Hialeah. The dawn was growing apace. The sky had turned from dark, reddish violet to baby blue streaked with fluorescent clouds. Cars and trucks were plowing the asphalt winds. Merely a sleepy hamlet. A place where people disposed to stretch, blink their eyes, scratch, belch or fart might languorously look for breakfast here. The four thousand six hundred and first calorie of the day.\nSomewhat sleepy myself, I saw the sign:\n7\u201311 $\n99 Cents\nBreakfast All Day\nSo I stopped there --with my foot on the...", "0_8_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-one in the morning</span>. Neither the crowd nor myself were very good that day, quite possibly both were hung over. I wrested away a newspaper somebody had just bought, and all at once the thing was beginning to gear up. With newspaper in hand, I squeezed hard into the press, found the key player, who was reading the sports while all hell raged around him, and stole his paper. It was tricky to read. The crowd jammed into my legs, bumped me from all sides. But I was nimble, even with a belly full of beer, and that\u2019s when I felt Shiva at my side, folks, young and beautiful as ever, in spite of the violet shades and big black German Porsche, veins jumping high in his girlish forehead, vaguely popping some kind of amyl persplex, a dangerous character, a real pro through and through, in town for a week to see his agent and his shrink, and all the cool people he knew. \u201cLet\u2019s go!\u201d He grabbed my arm savagely. \u201cCan\u2019t you find a space to put that fucking paper?\u201d He was happy, though. His Porsche was finally paid off. He was thinking about getting a MG roadster, a thing he'd always...", "0_0_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until one o'clock in the morning</span>. There was no knowing how long after that one might be roasting in one of these ovens. The bets ranged anywhere between five minutes and five hours.\n\"Five to five\u2014\"\n\"Five to five\u2014\"\n\"Five to five\u2014\"\nSo that was the bet. Five to one against, five to one he roasted. Nobody knew anything. They had never done this before: they did not know what it was like to be roasted in an oven. I did. It was like everything else. You had a little time out at first, and then all of a sudden it was too late and you were through, and it was too late for everything then\u2014too late to ever do any good, too late for anything but just one more half-second of pain and then finished. And he would be roasted in there the same as I was roasted on the outside. It was me that had been sitting there, making bets\u2014me, the old man. It had not been any of them. But I could not think that way. They had been sitting there, making bets with me, sitting just as close to me as I had been to myself. But it was me and I could not think that way. God! What was the matter with me...", "1_17_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until six o'clock in the evening</span> and I was standing by the bar in the Polo Lounge of the Beverly Hills Hotel. I was having a Kamikaze and I was already cockeyed with Official Alcohol. My attorney was gone\u2014pursuing legal matters elsewhere\u2014 and I was left on my own. He had actually just disappeared from the Lounge without so much as a \u201c Good\u2014bye \u201cor \u201cSee you later.\u201d I was alone and meditating another Kamikaze when I suddenly found myself recalling\u2014with a good deal of bitterness\u2014the many warnings I had given him about the treatment The Great Freak Show would get if we got in the ring with them for an all\u2014out Su \u2010An ister Showdown. My attorney never concealed his firm belief that we could make It with The Samson Sit\u2014 ting if we hit them with a \u201cstatement\u201d strong enough to make headlines everywhere. \u201cFace the music,\u201d he told me. \u201cMake a public appearance. We'll get them in the open, then we'll make a truce\u2014some kind of agreement, at least. It'll be absolutely breakthrough if we can once get the media to print one single word about the Holy Shootout Agreement we finally make.\"\n\u201cThat will...", "1_13_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. We were finished with Reno. \u201cTake us to the airport,\u201d I said. \u201cWe have to catch a dope plane to LA.\u201d The limo swerved sharply and rolled down to the main floor of the terminal. \u201cCatch this one, gentlemen,\u201d the driver said. \u201cIt serves both gates.\u201d We opened the doors and rolled out onto the smooth marble floor, clutching our Bowie knives and zippo lighters, our tails swaying lightly, our eyes glazed, our feet fast and sure on the planet Mars. There was a passenger plane sitting outside at the terminal, belching black smoke from its wingtips. Seven passengers were SHAKING PARALYZED WITH FEAR as they struggled to leave the plane, using walkers and wheelchairs. A fat lady with a walker was moving so slowly she was almost standing still. A fast old man in a wheelchair was trying to muscle his fat nurse right out of the plane and onto the runway, where the plane would surely crush them both to death. \u201cHurry it up!\u201d he yelled at her. \u201cI'm fed up with this shit! I want to go the hell home!\u201d Only the...", "1_5_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-eight in the morning</span> and $FIRSTNAMEB3, $FIRSTNAMEF4, Ed Dunkel, and I walked across the Washington Bridge into Manhattan. Two cab drivers from their cab company waiting in a little truck looked us over -- in those days all the Beat Generation made a lasting impression on normal people's minds. We paused to look over the Hudson and sniff at the fresh, dull dawn that was rising over New York Harbor at the end of the river. Great mists rolled, the ships showed first as shadow, then emerald green waters, white superstructures, dark mass below. Long strings of empties rose in the silences like apparitions; the harbor was entering a new day, strange and unaware, like the dawn in The Seafarer. All the old gang was taking chances and having adventures. We were off to the Diamondback at the corner of Hudson and 10th Street. Duke and Connie were to meet us there. We were entering the final lap of the road. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I were eager to get back to his girl, $FIRSTNAMEF4, Ed, of course, and I were taking chances with the same girls. Long before the difficulties of the night were behind us they had extended to involve much of the underground society of New York. Two apartments were taken to receive forty people from around...", "1_0_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty ante meridiem</span> the date, the first of April. The scene was In a bedroom of the $SURNAME0 estate. The clothing of the participants had been contrived by a most industrious and meaningful coincidence.\nFarmula wore an expression of grim triumph, having just won a game of checkers that had lasted three months\u2014a game that was never once interrupted, not even when $SURNAME0 had to go ten miles in a cigarette lighter to seed a celestial event.\n$SURNAME0 wore an expression of extreme concentration, having just solved the final mathematical difficulties of a long and complex formula on a slip of paper he had found among the folds of a cheap suit of underwear among his clothing. He was about to publish the formula in his chronicles. It was complicated, and it was going to make people think a lot of things about him that weren\u2019t true.\nThe woman was helping the man with his English. \" \u2018It was a lark,\u2019 \" she said, her tones ringing the High Church bell of Harvard. \"And then?\"\n\"Skip,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"\u2018It was a lark,\u2019 \" said Prance.\n\"Skip,\" said $SURNAME0. \"Please.\"\n\" \u2019...", "2_0_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-four ante meridiem</span> when the elevator finally came. The two uniformed men got in, but I stayed behind, not ready yet to face whatever crisis they'd come to handle. I rushed to the far end of the hall, pressed myself tight against the dirty Venetian blinds of one hopelessly smudged glass window. I could see nothing. The rattle of the elevator, suddenly silent. Panic in the muffled sounds of haggard breathing.\nThe elevator doors opened. Then silence for a moment\u2014\nThen a terrible animal screech of \u201cNOOO!\u201d then a shot, then another, then a horrible gurgling and the thud of boots in thick liquid...then the storming noise of a savage fight, the smashing of glass, more shouts, rebellion on the part of the elevator, the sounds of a struggle...but finally a horrible crunching sound as the killer struck, a heavy thud and a final pitiful groan of surrender, then silence.", "1_17_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The Fuselli dinner was over. From the Ritz I walked slowly across the street to where I had parked the Hispano-Suiza.\nI got in, unlocked the glove compartment and took out the stub of the second ticket to the Midnight Sun dinner. I had a week to get the other half.\nQueer Doings at the \u201cDome\u201d\nHOLD ON TO YOUR HAT,\u201d I told myself, looking into the mirror. \u201cYou are dashing to the Ritz to pick up your ticket for the F\u00eate That Dare Not Speak Its Name.\u201d\nAs It shuddered away from the curb I told the chauffeur to drive carefully until we got to the Bois. I didn't want anybody stopped for speeding on the way to the Ritz...after the Ritz...after the Ritz. What would happen at the Ritz? Would Faith Benedict be waiting in the cafe with my half of the ticket? The ticket had better be there, I told myself. If it isn't, I warned myself, I'll knock the goddamn hotel down. I'll wire New York. I'll wire Cannes. I'll put an ad in the London Times. And...", "0_22_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $SURNAME0 grew sick at David\u2019s feet. His attack might have lasted only ten seconds, but it seemed otherwise to Miss Boiling. She stayed open-mouthed, eyes wide, for three full minutes before pulling herself together and running for help.\nFive minutes before she went for help, little old $FIRSTNAMEB2, operating on nothing but the brio of his ancient grandmaster scheme, stole out of the bushes on the crest and tiptoed into the Skip on sheet-soles.\nThere was nothing surreptitious about his manner as he glided up the stairs to the skylight. His toes were curved daintily as they migrated across droves and files of dust mites.\nA moment before $FIRSTNAMEB2 had emptied his water pistol out the skylight. The water pistol, which had been filled with vinegar and blue food coloring, was now in his pocket\u2014and his father\u2019s coffin plate and whistle were in the other pocket of his madras sport coat.\nThe whine of the incoming cheerleader\u2019s whistle had penetrated into the Skip, producing a winner\u2019s brio.\nWinners in the Solar System, thought $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nWith a mighty, mature dignity,...", "2_9_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-four in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0 had just sprung their exemplum on Sarge.\nUntil this instant, Sarge had never known with what he was defiling himself. He was an animal of blind routines, rather than of hate and self-hate. He now stood shocked, under a comet whose light revealed depths and complexities in his soul of which, a few moments before, he had not dreamed.\nIt was all very confusing. His soul\u2019s turrets and parapets were in bad need of whitewashing.\nSomething exciting was obviously going on at the far end of the space ship. From time to time, a silhouette in a yellow climate suit would press close to a porthole, and make communications that were not good news for Sarge. He tried to imagine what these silhouettes were like inside their suits. He tried to imagine what their yellow suits were like.\nHe thought he had imagined a way to fly through the air.", "2_19_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was bellied once more against the blue paling that delimited the par terre pea patch. He was hot and tired. He counted the blue pigeon magnums again, found twenty-three, the same as the last time. He had been there thirty-six minutes.\nOld $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had not turned up. $SURNAME1 remembered the gibe he had overheard through the parlor wall in Newport\u2014\"$FIRSTNAMEM2 is so mean, he probably grew the blue pigeons just to spite me.\" Very well. $SURNAME1 wouldn\u2019t ask the old rascal for help\u2014wouldn\u2019t give $SURNAME0 the satisfaction of doing anything he asked just because he owned both $SURNAME1\u2019s palaces and $SURNAME0\u2019s world.\n$SURNAME1 walked to the end of the blue paling. He examined the back lawn of the $SURNAME0 estate.\nIt was wildly overgrown. Chinaberry, magnolia and macassar palms were as high as trees. Banana lianas twined everywhere, big as log ropes. $SURNAME1 could easily imagine getting lost in the overgrowth\u2014and never finding his way to anybody, not even to the kitchens, not even to the mansion.", "2_11_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes to noon</span>. The bearded and naked body lay flat on its back in a huge circular tub. The tub was stainless steel. The body lay on a grid of eighteen stainless-steel prongs, radiating like the armillary bearings of an imploded planet.\nAnd the body, so constrained by its bed of pain, seemed itself a phantom, hardly more than the sum of a few bruises and abrasions\nThe body was raw and exposed in a hundred places, having caught unusual ultraviolet on its extraordinary honeymoon. There was hardly a square inch of skin on the body that was not either cut or burned or blistered.\nAnd the brown beard on the face was transparent with burns.\nThe bath water in which the body lay was not still. A radical treatment was being administered by an apparatus of two huge transparent tubes snaking down into the water. There was a tube on the right, a tube on the left. The right tube had a dial on the end. The tube nourished the right arm with aspirated sea water through a 15,000-ph membrane. The left tube had a dial on the end. It nourished the left arm with aspirated sea water through an 8,000-ph membrane.", "0_16_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute until five o'clock in the afternoon</span> in the life of Walter Luckett Steckehan, and he knew that everything was his fault. His wife was crazy about him. He had plenty of money for all reasonable purposes. He\u2019d had a long escalator ride up through State, into Federal, and right now was at the top pf the whole escalator; in little more than a year, he would be wearing the emblem of the highest Federal position in the land\u2014the Patented Star-in-Wheel of the Computer Tire. When people of no importance touched their hats, they would be touching their hats to him. Now, he knew that he did not deserve even to be glanced at by some of the people he would be signing to in the Office. But in his work lay, somehow, the soul of the American Dream, Steckehan had never given anybody a hard time at the Office. He had fixed whatever machinery had broken down efficiently and in good faith. If the machinery had broken down, it had probably been broken in good faith, too. Anyway, Steckehan had not undertaken to judge his one-time fellow Americans for their good faith in getting the machinery that he fixed to break down. He looked forward to serving under the Patented Star enough so...", "0_19_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-four in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had promised his father that he would be back by eight o\u2019clock. He had promised his father with these words: \"I\u2019m going to fly over San Narciso, or I can make it before dark if I skip the birdless filter routes near the Santa Ana Faults.\" The words from which he had imagined himself released recurred now, like the rhyme scheme of a song. And he wondered if he\u2019d have time, on the trip back, to fly over the ocean and see San Narciso lit up at night. He\u2019d done it before, it was beautiful.\nHe wondered what Sector 9-F, into which he was now heading, was like. No one would be there tonight. He could steal an apartment and stay until morning. The Board of Rulers let the Spades use any possible apartment in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s home block, so long as the Spades kept it in order and left the electricity on.\nMaybe he\u2019d find a watch or a TV or something.\nHe came in low over the intersection of Beacon St. and Commonwealth Ave., hugging the top deck. He opened the rocket compartment to let the steam out, so that it wouldn\u2019...", "0_4_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty in the morning</span>. The parking lot that sloped down to the Vegas Strip was almost empty, but far off I could still see my red convertible. Much trouble, much grief and heartache could have been avoided if I'd only left the stupid thing at the hotel and taken a Yellow Cab over here.\u2014 But that was one of the major lements missing from the Drug Culture of 1968: safe, simple, cheap and fairly readily available transportation...especially on Monday nights in August. If I didn't have my own car, I probably wouldn't be here.\nBut now that I was here...well, as the bitch said when she put the chicken bone in the garter belt: \u201cWhat the fuck, Doc? Now you\u2019re here.\u201d And so I was. Here, in a situation that I no longer had the ability to control. No option but to plunge ahead, into the grinder. \u201cYou were always a ruthless bitch,\u201d I muttered. \u201cWhy did I ever doubt you?\u201d I felt that koan from high school...the old reliable koan of the young junkie: You look into the abyss, there\u2019s nothing staring back at you. That\u2019s mania. You look into the...", "2_0_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-one a.m.</span> when I roared over the nomber half\u2014way into my parking lot, sprang from my car like a man shot from a cannon, ran in, seized the keys and made for Room 501 as the bug hit the very middle of my windshield...my last room on the trip, much more disastrous, I felt sure, than the first.\nI knew what to expect when I got there. It was difficult, at that point, to imagine anything that could surprise me. All the fantasies of all the geeks in this land, of all time, were running around in my head\u2014this hell where I now existed. I'd been to their land. I knew their faces...knew their souls. I'd probed their inmost crevices.", "2_19_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Two hundred yards above Mitford, the rock-wedges of the canyon walls met, forming a V not large enough for a man to climb through or to escape through. In the canyon bottom, thick trees grew. Wild flowers, magnificent in height, grew among them.\nThe canyon was no more than forty feet wide and three hundred feet deep.\nTen minutes after the blast, the white tree-roots began to come to the surface of the ground and move deliberately about.\nThey moved like monstrous white snakes among the houses of Mitford.\nTheir method of attack was simple.\nSome methodic, disorderly physicist had calculated precisely the mass and strength of the white tree-root in the various positions of its growth.\nTo earth\u2019s creatures, the white tree-roots were death wherever they were.\nThey became death wherever they were.\nAt eight minutes past seven, a shower of iron spheres from the sky struck Mitford.\nIn the shelter under the fireplace in the living room of the vicarage, Claude Mancunian was playing a game of bicycle-cards with Mrs. Lucy Watkins, keeper of the village store adjacent to the churchyard.\nThey were both far too old to be in the army.", "1_7_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes to eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe chronometer in the navigating room told this to Clivelak confidently.\nWithout reports from the three scouts, the expedition would never be able to find its way across the immense battlefield and past the Wall, to the Palace of Purification.\nThe three scouts were now flying, side by side, at the very edge of the City of Phaolon. This plain, beyond the tall, insubstantial structure, was their destination.\nThe use of a ramrod, instead of an explosive, to burst open the cellar had been an excellent idea. The Scouts themselves, on the side toward the house, had been sufficiently far away to avoid being bruised. The vibrations had been felt all over the house.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0, removing wax from her ears, believed at first that an earthquake was taking place. The scout had surely exploded.\nOne moment she and $FIRSTNAMEF3 were taking shelter together in their air-raid trench; the next moment an earthquake seemed to be taking place.\n$FIRSTNAMEF3, the architect, persisted in believing that nothing had happened. But he could not hear. Nor could anyone in the compound hear anything except a tremendous buzzing.\nBeagle did not believe that anything had happened. Nothing could make him change his mind, though he dug...", "1_16_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-four in the afternoon</span>, Earth Eastern Standard Time. The place was Room 279 of the $SURNAME0 Hotel. The reporter was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, the man in the red suit was Salo.\nSuddenly, like the click of a rhythmic metronome the beat of $SURNAME0\u2019s metal dog tag stopped.\nIt was time to go.\nAnd at that moment, while the crowd outside was still gabbing and Tralfamadorians were still arriving, and bridesmaids were still finding their seats, and parakeets were squabbling in their boxes, and the band was still tuning up, and couples still dawdled on the threshold, and the ushers were still fussing with lapel microphones\u2014at that moment, while things outside were so disorderly, things in Room 279 were as orderly as they would ever be. This was the orderliness.\nComo, the cat, lay very sick in a chair by the vacant fireplace.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4, the dog, was standing on the brass poker table, grinding his chamois-clad left hind foot in secret, indecisive sorrow. $SURNAME0 lay on his satinwood mortuary slab, rigged for skis, one arm akimbo,...", "2_18_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>, November 4, 1975. It was three minutes into the time of the appearance of the ghostly Space Wanderer from the undetermined future, and one minute into the time of the visitation of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 from the false Mercury.\nSqueak.\nThe sound that had just been heard was the high-pitched, tiny sound of the lowest-numbered church bell. The soldiers in all the invasion column\u2019s tanks and halftracks and assault craft now cocked their weapons.\nSqueak.\nThe sound that had just been heard was the second-lowest-numbered church bell. The explosive charges in all the wads over the invasion column\u2019s ordnance now tempered their fuses.\nSqueak.\nThe sound that had just been heard was the third-lowest-numbered church bell. The soldiers threw out their cookie crumbs and Coke cans, to make the bottoms of their vehicles seem heavy with manflesh.\nSqueak.\nThe sound that had just been heard was the plumpest-numbered church bell. The shock-assault and fire-support companies of the column sealed the rubber gaskets at the joints of their space suits.", "0_1_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to two o'clock in the morning</span>. Random House was empty of persons except for me. I plotted for about an hour in the Lenore Blumling Personalities in Disarray file and gave up on finding anything I could use. The remaining Blumling entries were not promising. There was one final, sad, tiny scrap of a diary entry, in Lenore\u2019s own distinctive hand, a few lines staggering with grief and exhaustion, barely legible. Mrs. Tissaw had found the little picture of Lenore\u2019s father along with the entry. This is all that was in the file on Lenore\u2019s father:\nThe picture on the bottom of this page is of my father. He is dead.\nThat was all. Random House was eerily quiet. Other than dim hurricane light blinking and throbbing on the personal console of Anita Brinkman, and the metallic drumming of the console-mounted raincoat\u2019s heels as the flexing statue danced to the rhythm of the storm, all was quiet; not even the shuffle of my own feet broke the spell of the silence. There was that cloying scent of slow-moving water and electricity. I couldn\u2019t find anything to explain what had happened to Lenore. If anything...", "2_10_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten eighteen in the morning</span> on Titan, Saturn\u2019s largest moon. Titan followed the same calendar and recognized the same authority as Earth, but was fourteen years out of phase. It was ten eighteen a.m. T.N.T. on Titan\u2014and the day had just begun.\nThe peachy-kandicamic Space Wanderer stepped out of the elevator into a great hall. This was the great hall of the Palace of Space Museum, and the Space Museum, of course, was part of the palace. The Palace of $SURNAME0 was the contents, continuous, of the forty-foot spiral outside the tube of the spiral staircase. The palace was a spiral spring that was re-coiled in such a geometrical and dynamic pattern that the head of a single spiral was really in the fluted lobby of the palace, while great whorled coils stretched to annexes in Europe, Asia, Africa, and New England.", "1_19_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes until eight o'clock in the evening</span>, and a crowd of people was still hanging around Fribble's, a pornographic bookstore on Lexington Avenue at Sixty-second Street, waiting and hoping, half an hour late, for Lotta von Passenheim's poetry reading. Uninvited, Ralph P. Renfrew, Jr., of Scarsdale, New York, and unannounced, stole past the cash register and into an office in the back of the store, where he turned on a dark blue light over a plain white door with a spider web painted in the middle of it. The door opened when he pushed, and a wary hound dog, stupefied with love and mysticism, failed to bark at him. Renfrew went on through the door into a two-room apartment, full of incense, marijuana, love beads, baby alligators, posters of Christ, and books.\nRenfrew at first tried to resist the dark, erotic beauty in the room full of smoke, but finally on his knees embraced Lotta von Passenheim, murmuring, \"Eight knots, eight knots.\" He was a chunky kid with a mustache, and all he wore was a black string around his neck with a Roman coin on it.\nLotta von Pass...", "0_11_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven o'clock in the morning</span>, I just was beginning my day. The first thing that happened was a phone call from Terry, the girl who does my tricking and moaning for me, she deals the old green stamps and salt shakers, soon as I wake up she phones, she hated me. She said, \u201cI'm sick in bed, I have a fever, please, man, give me some money.\u201d I said, \u201cHow much?\u201d She said, \u201cGo to the Safeway, they always give me a chicken and a dollar.\u201d I said, \u201cI'm broke.\u201d She said, \u201cGo to the corner, to the Chinese store and see if they have a job and a room.\u201d I'd been wanting to do that. It was evil advice; once having asked, I had to do it. I shaved and put my purple veins on and grabbed a toothbrush and went downstairs to peek out. No pimp and no fuzz. I sauntered toward the corner making my very own beat; I dawdled and farted, I yawned, I cared for none and feared none. I found the store and went inside. There was a fat slant-eyed proprietress who...", "1_0_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes until one o'clock in the morning</span>.\nSitka, Alaska, was a large, dark city that was not asleep.\nThere were plenty of lights in the fishing town as the Space Wanderer sped across it toward the dry and rocky ground near Stonehenge.\nHe knew exactly where and when he was going to land.\nHe had known these things ever since he had shaken loose of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 in the violet bathroom on Titan.\nWhen he shook the teardrop-shaped diamond free of the golden chain around his neck, only then did he speak. \"Very useful,\" he said, putting the diamond back in his pocket.\nHis autopilot, noting that the Space Wanderer had taken hold of the controls in such a way as to indicate that he meant to land the space ship, disengaged itself immediately. It was just as glad to get off. It had done a much better job of navigation than the man would ever be able to do.\nThe autopilot and chronometer mixed together in a sealed bag and filed themselves in a storage bin. The storage bin was also filed away, and no one will ever find either the autopilot or the chronometer again.\nSo there will never be a formal report on the landing of $SURNAME0\u2019s space ship...", "2_0_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve sixteen ante meridiem</span>and I was sitting in the safety and darkness of my room, an open beer in my hand, laughing.\n13. San Francisco \u2014 November 14, 1967\nYes, it was all very strange as I was sitting there in my room on Telegraph Avenue in Berkeley, watching the evening news on TV and drinking a beer. Here I was, Robert $FIRSTNAMEB3 Foster, a former staff writer for The People\u2019s World, living in a Monte Vista hotel in downtown S. F., working as a $75\u2014per\u2014week copywriter for an excellent agency across the Bay\u2014Madison Square. The contrast between this fife and that seven years before, and my continuing concern about the Old Gang, and my anticipated confrontation with poor ole Henry Morphy, made me nervous beyond description.\nThen two friends came to the room\u2014Tim Gray, former proprietor of The Lancer, my old North Beach bar, and his neighbor, Nick von Gunten. They sat on the bed that doubled as my sofa, and we began comparing notes on mutual friends in the L.A. area. I had no news of Mink and Frisco Noonan, just a general query as to where they were. The Summer of Love crowd was the same.", "0_5_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty in the morning</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I left. \u201cHey beb, let's go see the sunrise and dig Denver coming awake.\u201d\n\u201cDig?\u201d\nWe jumped in the car and leaped onto the pavement; $FIRSTNAMEB3 set his cap jauntily for roaming and off we went. A few cars were creeping townward. First we cruised the sleeping-warm streets of the downtown region. At that hour absolutely nothing was happening. No sound came from the ghostly buildings. No watchman was around and the emptiness echoed our footsteps abnormally. As the streets became livelier we had to slow down. They were lined with parked cars -- business people were arriving to start the day's work. A few drunks staggered out of alleys to ask us for money; one of them assured us it was his birthday and wanted a drink on us. We gave him a dime at his repeated petitions. When we began to see the skyscraper offices with their lights burning we got in the right lane and switched off our headlights. It was the Denver bank street; we mooched along it. It was the richest street in the world. Dark beauties showed at the office windows. The windows of the big offices began to light up. A white-haired...", "1_1_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after one o'clock in the morning</span>. We'd been hanging around and bullshitting in this joint for hours. Up in one of the rooms somebody was singing and playing the guitar. It was something sad and Mexican.\nThe big guy who had been a cowboy was singing, and we sang along with him. Old Bull was reeling around on his stool. \u201cEvery time you drink you get drunker and Eadier,\u201d he yelled to me across the room. \u201cYou ought to be a beaconset, $FIRSTNAMEB6, with all the drinking you do.\u201d In the back room a couple huddled close together on a couch. They were making out like crazy. Every five minutes the girl dug the boy in the ribs and said something. He kept nodding Yes. Monahuh, monahuh, monahuh. The lady of the house came in and shouted at them in Spanish and told them to cut it out and go someplace else and finish; the rent was too high to have people in the front room making noise like that and preventing decent folk from sleeping. The boy and girl paid no attention; they just kept on and on and on, monahuh, monahuh, monahuh. We jeered at them drunkenly. \u201cYou scum...", "0_3_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to four o'clock in the morning</span>. I never heard anybody in my whole life talk so much about so little. All I wanted was to go to sleep. Big Dinky had to bring up every girl $FIRSTNAMEB3 had ever met. They had a horseshoe on the wall. I threw it at the post and made the stake. They all took bets on it. It cost me a dollar to go to sleep. They didn't bother me; they knew I had my sleep to make up. It was $FIRSTNAMEB3 who wanted to continue the party till the night owners complained at five o'clock and radio cops appeared at the entrance to investigate. At two in the morning the bartender told the jazz musician to go home, but $FIRSTNAMEB3 said he himself didn't want the party to stop. \u201cI want the party to go on, drum, bum -- go on till everybody drops dead, just like it says in my alma mater.\u201d And he began to sing the hymns of his college fraternity, accompanied by the drunken jazz musician, who banged away on drums, glasses, pans -- anything he could get his hands on. In order to hear himself sing $FIRSTNAMEB3 had to climb on top of the drum-kit. He was yelling at the top of his voice and everybody was yelling with him...", "1_20_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-two in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s company commander was sitting in a barracks-room easy-chair, waiting for $FIRSTNAMEB0 to come back from the telephone so he could give him the bad news.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s company commander was a fattish man with a smooth, expressionless face and cold, watery blue eyes. He might have been an expensively barbered, elegantly tailored woman for all that his appearance suggested the soldier. His eyes never actually seemed to focus on anything.\nHis purpose in life was apparently to make the lives of everyone around him as uncomfortable as possible. His name was Colonel Courtney.\nColonel Courtney had let $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s platoon go out and get almost wiped out the night before, because he wanted to replace $FIRSTNAMEB0 with Lieutentant Mawkey. Now, with $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s coming back, there would be a fight, and the disagreeable, sadistic old sonofabitch was looking forward to the fight because he loved to let people shoot at him.\nColonel Courtney was sitting in his easy-chair, rocking and smiling.\nThe room was done in a bizarre jungle motif. On the walls were framed, overlapping swatches of raw, peeling animal skins. Ropes with...", "0_18_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>.\nIn each of the husband and wife there was a feeling that the best thing to do at that time was what the other one was doing.\nSuddenly it was as though the communications heater connected the two with the whole human race. It was as though they were one small part of one great living creature.\nIt was like that.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\nReader\u2019s Digest Press,\nSpringfield, Massachusetts 01103\nTHE END\n03 01 1038g\nContents\nWe tell ourselves stories in order to live ... Norman Maclean\nThe Army-Navy Game: Strange and Terrible Palm Sunday\u2014A Chronological Snarl\u2014The Organization of America\u2014Desertion\u2014Monk Trout, the Nudger\u2014$FIRSTNAMEB0 and his Red Devil Battery\u2014An Old English Fairy Tale\u2014News from the Front\u2014Desertion and Pilfering\u2014Tank-Bustin\u2019\u2014Capture\u2014An Ice Cube Encores\u2014The Telephone Call\u2014Talking Machine\u2014Breaking Strain\u2014Fear Itself\u2014Christ in Profile\u2014Pigeons on the Grass U.S.A.\u2014Packing\u2014Oatmeal\u2014Gripe Session\u2014Search and Destroy\u2014Civilization\u2014All-Stars\u2014Beaten Back\u2014...", "0_17_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was a forgotten and ruined palace at the edge of a desert. $SURNAME0, so far as material possessions were concerned, had nothing left but his space ship. His incredible collection of treasured objects had either been confiscated by the government, or stolen by casual looters.\nThe palace was far away from the press and the governments; far away from the excitement of war; far away from the space shipyards.\nOnly a sustained, expensive, determined effort on $SURNAME0\u2019s part had kept his palace and his space ship from being seized by the authorities. None of $SURNAME0\u2019s former priority-one connections were in a position to help him, to put in a good word for him. $SURNAME0 was quite simply an outcast.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s only two companions were his dog, $FIRSTNAMEB4, and his son, $FIRSTNAMEB2. All three were badly in need of baths, brushing, and shoe polish. All three were poorly nourished. $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s split lip and one eyebrow were not, incidentally, the result of his having been in a fight with other schoolchildren on Titan. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had split his own lip, and one of his own eyebrows. He had done it while...", "1_4_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-seven in the morning</span>. My attorney had long since passed out, lying in the back seat with his feet in my lap and the motor still running. I put my own feet up and stood the speedometer on \u201c62\u201d until it clicked over to \u201c63,\u201d then I dropped the lever back to \u201c I .\u201d Lady Luck was toying with us, keeping us in the wrong gear for the tack. We were lurching violently and sloppily across the straightaway because we were really in about third\u2014a fine way to get pulled over by the Victor patrol . . . especially when the goddamn speedometer says you\u2019re doing 65.\n5. Muscle Beach Diaries...Go to the track every day? Not me. Make it an experience and stick to it\u2014one day at a time.\n6. This point meant quite a bit to me when I was twenty-six. I might have spent a week looking for that goddamn room. Here it is, verbatim, to avoid that particular Nightmare: Room 303, Rickey\u2019s Motel, Paradise Avenue, Paradise, California.\n7. Maybe you think it was that scene at the Gas Station on America; just before the L.A. turnoff...", "2_1_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was alarmed awake with the confusion of a man in the midst of being hanged. He leaped to his feet, heart pounding.\nHe had no idea where he was. He suspected, for a flashing moment, that the alarm sounds of memory had brought him back to the horrible place from which he had believed he had escaped forever. He imagined the massacre of a garrison somewhere on a desert world. He imagined that he himself was about to be shot for dereliction of duty through sleeping on watch.\nThen he saw blue skies, brown forest, green meadow, and realized that he was in a peaceful place. His disorientation vanished. He looked at his surroundings as a man looks at a once-favorite toy that has fallen on hard times.\nHis memory was dismally clear again. He remembered thinking the same thoughts, dreaming the same dreams, many times before.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB2, is that you, boy?\" he said to himself. He wished that it would call him something besides boy. He hated that nickname worse than any other he could think of. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was the name of a prehistoric bird.", "2_7_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe things that $FIRSTNAMEB0 imagined were all things he had seen with his eyes, or had heard with his ears. He was held to those things by the steel-utensil-enhanced senses of his condition.\n\"I must rescue her!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself.\nHe leapt to his feet.\nHe began hitting himself on the head with his fist. \"What kind of an idiot am I?\" he cried. \"That wasn\u2019t a love story, that wasn\u2019t anything pretty or nice like that. That was real! My mate is in terrible danger! What a dope I am, thinking about anything else for even a second, when my mate is in such terrible danger!\"\nHe imagined the danger his mate was in, and sensed the urgency of the situation. \"I must rescue her from the meeting place of two pretty pink walls!\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to himself.\nHis heart stopped.\nHis lungs ceased to move.\nHe remembered having seen his mate\u2019s face....\nThe time was seven thirty in the morning.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.", "0_13_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after one o'clock in the afternoon</span>. At that time, the chronology of the occurrences in the room below was as follows:\n\"Now\u2014\" said Metzger, \"if you\u2019ll kindly follow my thumb, this is the West Coast, here, this is the Mare Ignotum\u2014this one\u2014and here we are, and here\u2019s our lovely Luna, falling toward the sun there. At that speed it takes just over two weeks to fall the eighty-seven million miles. So you fell pretty much two-thirds of the way in just over twenty-four hours. Right?\n\"You were real lucky,\" said Metzger. \"If you hadn\u2019t got back\u2014that gadget of yours would of course have gone on falling, and when the sun came up over there, you would of course have been cooked to a crisp, and as for the atmosphere, you\u2019d have bounced like a BB with all the junk you have\u2014it\u2019s falling apart on you, isn\u2019t it?\"\n\"You\u2019re right,\" said the room.\n\"Lots of luck,\" said Metzger. \"Was it interesting?\"\n\"Superb,\" said the room. \"The Falls were stupendous.\"\n\"", "1_16_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty in the afternoon</span>. \u201cWe only want a small ball,\u201d said my attorney. He took a bill out, leaving the folds loud in the hushed air, and said \u201cCould you wrap this twenty in five singles, please?\u201d The girl started to reach under the bar but I stopped her. \u201cNo,\u201d I said. I wanted to give her the principle of the thing. \u201cJust the singles. We\u2019ll do the folding.\u201d She brought them out reluctantly, as if it were against the health code and she risked a penalty if she gave us too much. Even the great Gonzo Corporate Sumo Championship didn\u2019t inspire much confidence. Four rolled twenties looked like eighty. Well, she hadn\u2019t printed them, she\u2019d only worked there fourteen months. My attorney seemed to glow when he saw the money. He held it up in the sunlight: \u201cLookit,\u201d he said. \u201cFeel it!\u201d Good Lord, it was only money. We were trying to distract ourselves. For the first time on this terrible day, we were trying to have some harmless fun. The steering had never quite come alive on that car, so we handled it delicately along the...", "0_2_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-seven in the morning</span>. The room I was standing in belonged to a new and fairly typical client of mine named Dexter Carter. Dexter was eighteen years old. He\u2019d never been in trouble before. He came from a good family. Physically he was hairy-knuckled, stolid and rubbery. He was an especially fine specimen of the genus Americanus Rubenesque. He was six foot three and weighed 235 pounds. He drove trucks.\nThe reason he drove trucks was that the Army wouldn\u2019t have him because he had flat feet. This meant that he could never be a paratrooper. This also meant that he never got to shoot his gun, because he wasn\u2019t allowed to be where the guns were. Dexter was a man who had been converted to serious Ideas by the Army. The Army had taught Dexter that he was not his own boss. The Army taught him that there were existences in which he did not choose to participate. There was no appeal from this decision. The arbiter who made it was the U.S. Government. Having thus established in Dexter\u2019s mind the anomie and the warp that the Army\u2019s hammer inflicts on the finest high-carbon steel, the Army then washed its hands...", "2_1_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-four in the morning</span>.\nAt three fifty-six in the morning, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 died.\nThe house saw to it that her last minutes were comfortable, that she was unaware of the goings-on around her.\nAt three fifty-nine in the morning, she died suddenly, infectio ingly.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 bounded into the apartment, fetched up against a wall, slid down it to a sitting position.\nHis golden eyes were dry, dry indeed. His long tongue extended, licked the air judiciously.\nThe voices of material creatures in material pain carried very well in material space, and $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s keen ears had almost certainly picked up the slap of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 against the wall of a corridor twenty feet from the closed door of the apartment. The dog knew by that token that something terrible had happened in the outside world.\nThe brilliant blue spirals on $FIRSTNAMEB4\u2019s hide were pulsing slowly.\nIt was an elegant pattern that signified adoration of $SURNAME0, of love for all creatures, of sympathy with the spasms of good-hearted Titanic $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 against the corridor wall outside.", "1_14_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past two o'clock in the afternoon</span>; the place was a weedy square in Old Town, the Left Bank of Boston. A riot was going on. About two hundred persons were involved at their various levels of participation. The square was bounded by a courthouse (closed on Sundays), a department store, an art museum, and the backs of dark brick buildings. A number of spectators stood in overgrown shrubberies on the edges to see better.\nThere were basically three kinds of participants in the riot:\n(1) a wild-looking, hairy young gang that periodically sent into the square surging waves of sound, produced by horns, harmonicas, bull-horns and shouted song lyrics;\n(2) a rather pretty and typical collegiate type of girl, wearing tweed or corduroy skirts, with hair in flipish boyish bangs, who, with a gin and tonic in one hand, and other hand fluttering in the air a good deal, went gaily about in dancing little circles, disregarding the gang entirely; and\n(3) the gang members themselves, who, having no girls to dance with, danced instead with each other, and were having a wonderful time.\nThese were apparently known as Satan's Angels. I still hadn't gotten close enough...", "1_3_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe time was fourteen minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon.\nThe time was two-thirty in the afternoon.\nThe time was five minutes past two o\u2019clock in the morning. The time was five-twelve p.m.\nThe time was one-thirty in the morning.\nThe sudden love business between $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1, former heir presumptive to a communications empire worth billions, and Marlin McKeever, a lieutenant in the Army of Mars, ended abruptly, to all appearances under the stress of war.\n McKeever was wounded in a fight against partisans just east of Olympus. He did not die, but his commando unit was wiped out. McKeever himself was stumbled over by a search party three days later. By then, he was mad.\nMcKeever was sent back to Terra, where Tower and Nimbus, Incorporated, had for years maintained a huge hospital for victims of war nervous disorders. He was treated for his madness, but he never cleaned up sufficiently to be released to civilian life.\n$SURNAME1, in the meantime, made three quick Power ascents. He was a colonel before he was thirty, a general before he was thirty-five, and a full general before...", "0_18_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>, the darkening of another of Luna's tired nights.\nThe space, so far as anyone knew, was at a spot two hundred and eighty-four kilometers west of Rhodopalass.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 went to pieces as his small unit went through the communication trench to the attack position.\nHe simply walked away from the line, disposed of his gas mask, webbing, automatic, rifle, and so on.\nWinfield and $FIRSTNAMEB1 disappeared into the attackers\u2019 lines without the aid of an excuse. They simply went.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 felt much less interest in the attack now that there wasn\u2019t anything for him to do about it. He felt like going, too.\nFor a moment, he had a heavenly vision of going\u2014going back to the stagnant magic of the square green field.\nBut then he thought that his family wasn\u2019t there any more. He had a moment of nerved-up panic then, thinking that his family might be dead, and that he might have to go all alone through the whole rest of his life. He calmed himself by chucking dust in handfuls. (If his family was dead, he would still have to go on living.) He knew that his family couldn\u2019t be...", "0_5_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until six o'clock in the morning</span>. The taxis were going by with their lights off. It made me feel Parisian to sit there at the window and watch the world waking up.\nThere were some birds in one of the back gardens. They made rural noises. They acted as though they were in the country. Then the maid tapped on my door and opened it.\n\u201cExcuse me, sir, but the barber is here.\u201d\n\u201cSend him up.\u201d\nThe tub smelled good. So did the shaving-cream and the brush and the towel.\nTen minutes later I was fresh, clean, shaved, and unhappy in another sober suit and another derby and another pair of classy gloves. There was no smell of liquor in the room. $FIRSTNAMEB5 was just coming up the stairs in a polo coat and skirt, and I kissed her good-morning.\n\u201cYou look like a million dollars,\u201d I said.\n\u201cI feel rather frayed.\u201d\n\u201cWhy don\u2019t you sleep all morning?\u201d\n\u201cI feel rather like a cigarette.\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019ll get them.\u201d\n$FIRSTNAMEB5 sat on the bed.\n\u201cI\u2019m rather...", "1_21_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-six in the evening</span> and the place was the Alien Hotel in Martian Space.\nIt was raining like bastards all around the lobby of the Alien Hotel, but the lobby itself was miraculously dry.\nNo guests were in the lobby.\nOne man, a porter, was on duty. He was just inside an open door to his cubbyhole.\nAt the far end of the lobby were the manager\u2019s office, the bar, and the staircase going up to the fourth floor. The bar, seen through the open pavilion, was about two furlongs away.\nThe lobby, lit by a saffron ceiling, a sickly blue chandelier, and eight blue wall brackets, was wet.\nThe porter looked at the few things in his cubbyhole, looked out at the rain, and thought of the things he was leaving behind at home.\nLa-La-Lavendrick, the porter\u2019s wife, was a waitress in a cocktail lounge across the street. Lavendrick wanted to go out there and watch the rain and wait for her to come off and maybe put a record on the Victrola. But Lavendrick was on duty.\n\"Damn fool hotel,\" said Lavendrick to...", "2_13_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one sixteen in the afternoon</span>.\nThe first Martian ever to go on a binge was a member of a company of green Grunts by the name of $SURNAME2. He sprang his surprise on his buddies, who were quartered in the bed of a desolate gully in the Solarv line.\nThe Martians were deployed and dug in from the line itself to the treacherous Mesas of Tharsis.\nConstituents of the gully company were $SURNAME2, eight other privates, an assistant cook and a medic.\nThe medic was named Muckles.\nHis given name was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nHe talked to himself\u2014constantly. He gave himself a running account of whatever he was doing.\nHe was in the gully, trying to get a fuming and feverish $SURNAME2 to suck on a foul-tasting Martian brew called despoiled molasses. Despoiled molasses was a byproduct of refining Martian battlewagons\u2014it was what was left over after all the bullets and free-lif e had been removed.", "1_13_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one forty-eight in the afternoon</span>. Much was about to happen. Much had happened in the hour before. Much was about to happen after. In terms of what was about to happen, an hour is a long time. Nevertheless, much was about to happen. Nothing was ever to be quite the same afterward.\nSo I lay there, in deep shadow, on my side. The bottom had the consistency of fine sand mingled with lint. There were bits of paper and dried food and an ancient tin cup and a stake of gritty wood. There was a grasshopper on my hip.\n\"Why am I naked?\" I asked. \"How did I get naked before I got drunk?\" I rolled over to get a toothpick out of my back pocket. My back pocket was empty. \"Where are my pants?\" I sat up. \"And my overcoat!\" I shouted.\nThe bartender came back with my coat. He had it in one hand and a white straw hat in the other.\n\"What\u2019s all the shouting about?\u201d he asked.\n\"Where did you get these?\" I asked.\n\"Out in the street. Some guy dropped them.\"\n\"Who?\" said I, reaching for my coat.\n\"I don\u2019t...", "2_18_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six forty-three in the evening</span>, Earthling Time.\nThe crowd that had been serenading the Chief of Space Patrol outside the little concrete bunker was serenading him still\u2014now just outside the great main entrance of the main building. The crowd sang the following words to a catchy little melody, and sang them over and over and over again:\nGood-bye-ee, Chief-ee, good-bye-ee.\nYour work is through here,\nSo hop on your ser-eer-up, good\u2014bye-ee. So long, good\u2014bye, Chief\u2014ee!\nA television camera and taping equipment, set up and operated by a crew from the News of the Galaxy, were aimed at the Time Machine.\nThe distance from the paneled door of the building to the Time Machine was 435 yards.\nThe crowd, from the song, knew that to be a fact. They had been privileged to walk that distance, measure it with their own bodies, and see for themselves that that was how far it was\u2014 435 yards from door to Time Machine.\nThe fact was a part of the very song they were singing so lustily.\nSix forty-four in the evening.", "1_3_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-two in the morning</span>. The house was asleep. I climbed quietly out of bed. I looked in the long mirror. There was nobody behind me. The hall was dark. I walked on tiptoe. I was doing one-foot hops, like a ballet dancer. I stopped in the middle of the living room for a laugh. What a laugh! It wasn\u2019t funny. The coffee table had to be moved. I couldn\u2019t reach the switch. I had to get the little one. I could carry it fine, but the switch had to be hit right, and I couldn\u2019t see clear enough in the dark to hit the switch, so the coffee table was like an enormous sundial that cast enormous long shadows when the headlights of a car flashed on it; I could see it and the enormous, gigantic shadow of my big dizzy dangle as I tried to hit what I couldn\u2019t see. Damn. Straighten up, Echo. Outside, in the almost-dawn, the sad dusty dry ghost of a palm tree scratched its fingers on the wall. There had once been a garden here. Who knows how many years ago. If a house lives a thousand years, what is the life of a garden? The smell of angel-...", "0_21_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past nine o'clock in the evening</span>, we had gone through Switzerland and Germany, crossed the Dutch frontier, and we were just passing through a suburb of Amsterdam. In a moment we would be going through the heart of the town. We were just coming up to Dam Square; I put my head out the window to see if there was a taxi anywhere near the hotel we wanted. Plenty of motor-cycles, plenty of cars, cabs crawling all over the place, but not a taxi. There was a great mass of them near the Central Station. Ah, if I could only get word to them somehow and have one of them pick us up. Of course there was no station anywhere near Dam Square, too near the heart of town. If only I could get word to a taxi to leave immediately and come to Dam Square. We came to the great mass of traffic around Dam Square. I tried to get out, but there was no doorman in sight, and I had to argue with the man who took the fares. We were jammed in among traffic. There was no way to get out and no paving in sight. We were right near the curb, but there were forty or fifty taxis as thick as lice around the square, out of reach. I struggled to open the door and get out...", "2_9_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine nineteen in the morning</span>.\nThere was a pause in the music. A voice from the public address system then made a special, though not surprising, announcement.\n\"Parents,\" said the voice, \"with your permission, some of the children would like your participation in a rather amusing drill.\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 threw back his head and howled. $FIRSTNAMEB1 shed tears and dribbled saliva. Blue Ragg recoiled from an imaginary bite.\n\"Rather amusing indeed,\" said the voice. \"I don\u2019t doubt that a lot of you, as parents, have had fears that all this horrendous drilling was, in some ways, kind of ... traumatizing. So the children would like to show you that they understand how sensitive a child can be, and how easily he can take things to heart. For your participation in this part of the show, you will receive a small musical token in appreciation.\"\nThe audience laughed.\n\"The token,\" said the voice, \"will be taken by the ushers at the end of the program.\"\nThe voice then introduced the orphans\u2019 amusing drill.", "0_13_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The sails were down, the anchor up, and the oars in the oarlocks. $FIRSTNAMEB0 tapped the walls of the space ship experimentally and found them firm.\n\u201cI just don\u2019t think I can take it anymore,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 pressed his face to the port. \"I see a jail there,\" he said, \"and some scaffoldin\u2019. I guess that\u2019s where they\u2019ll do it.\"\n\"Where who\u2019ll do what?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Them spacemen,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"The ones who come to get us.\" He presented as benign a smile as was possible for him, under the circumstances. \"They ain\u2019t been, yit, $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014that\u2019s plain. And, what with the ship all tore up like it is, and us still us, I think that any minute the fella in charge is going to open up the roof, give us fresh air.\" He smiled again at $FIRSTNAMEB1. \"We\u2019ll get out of here, then, and they\u2019ll hang us just as quick as they can manage it.\" He shrugged. \"I guess hanging would...", "1_11_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to noon</span>. At twelve o\u2019clock noon, the fronts of the two enormous armies faced each other within motion-picture range. Neither side had yet attacked.\nAt five minutes before twelve, a captain in the Tralfamadorian army supposed to be loyal to Salo some how got a message to Salo, who looked at his watch, leaped up on a rock, waved his arms over his head, and screamed:\n\"In the name of Tralfamadore, go home!\"\nThe loyal captain on the battlefield turned to his men and screamed, \"Retreat ways\u2014\"\nThe rest of that sentence and anything else the captain might have said in the next three minutes and eleven seconds was drowned out by the roars of the triumphant armies on Mercury.\nAnd now the only sounds were the panicky, scrambling noises of the two armies trying to get out of each other\u2019s way. By watching through field glasses, Salo could see individual soldiers dropping their guns, throwing their backs and arms, swinging their hips, running as though mad. These battlefields on Mercury had no drainage system worth mentioning, what there was being a bunch of sink holes that usually had water in them. Consequently, the soldiers who ran on Mercury soon found themselves...", "0_16_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-four in the afternoon</span>. There was now a buzzing sound to the quality of the light and a faint pinkness was beginning to invade the west. The inpouring light seemed warm and heavy, and it was as if the shadows under the trees were masses of gray velvet softening to black velvet. In the east and in the west alike the light was shot through with pink. Under a key of gold, $SURNAME1\u2019s House smoked in the sun.\nWe now switched p.o.v.\u2019s to chronicle what the $FIRSTNAMEB2-funnel had told us about that night. Personal interest would enter the story, too, since it was Monday evening and $SURNAME0 was speaking again.\nAt seven P.M., $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 and her son $SURNAME1 were going to be slipped into the Space Wanderer suit, were going to be injected into Earth\u2019s orbit.\nAt three A.M. Tuesday, they were going to re-enter Earth\u2019s atmosphere, were going to land gently as thistledown in the pasture next to the mansion.\nNobody on Earth but $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was going to know that an unimaginable extension of the lease had been granted.\nAnd the only person on Earth who was going to know that...", "1_1_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past one o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 came on a moment later in a sharp business suit and with a briefcase in his hand. \u201cWhere did you get that suit?\u201d I said. \u201cWhat are you, a businessman?\u201d\n\u201cYes, man, Sergeant of the Air, on special detail to the Weather Bureau -- you don't mind, do you?\u201d\n\u201cWe're going to the West Coast tomorrow,\u201d I said. \u201cWake up Carlo, we'll leave by nine.\u201d After a lot of talk, he went over and roused Carlo and tried to convince him to come. Carlo wouldn't budge and I thought it best to let him alone. We needed him for the car only for a week more; then we were going to trade it in on a '49 Ford at Firestone Lee's and then go in Bob Nelson's loaned Galaxie; he was coming back from Frisco with his wife's father and would leave it with his stepbrother at home. But all this was far ahead. Now $FIRSTNAMEB3 sweated and pleaded with Carlo to come with us. \u201cListen,\u201d he said, \u201cI'll only have to come back ten days from now -- eleven days at...", "2_9_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was Cleveland, Ohio.\nThe small gentleman with the glasses turned away from the news-stand, and he began to walk down the street, away from the corner where Freddy and Walter were talking\u2014where the man with the splayed yellow shoes stood by himself.\n\"You going with us?\" said Freddy to the small gentleman.\n\"I thought I might,\" said the small gentleman.\n\"Where are we going today? Where are we going?\" said Freddy.\n\"Well,\" said the small gentleman, \"I have to get some nails, and then I thought I\u2019d have my lunch at that French place downtown, and then I thought I\u2019d drop around to Just Folks.\"\n\"Well,\" said Freddy, \"I\u2019ll go with you everywhere.\"\n\"I think Just Folks is an admirable organization,\" said the small gentleman.\n\"I\u2019ll tell you a hot one,\" said Freddy. \"I got a man working for me calls himself a poet, but he doesn\u2019t know shit from apple butter. He had me come out here to hire some pushcart peddlers for him. I had to tell him ninety per cent of them were colored.\"", "2_11_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the office of the President of the United States, in the White House in Washington, D.C. The President of the United States, who happened to be Richard M. Nixon, was meeting with the joint chiefs. So was Henry A. Kissinger. So were the joint chiefs.\nThe President and the joint chiefs sat against the walls of a long sofa again. The sofa was in the middle of the room. All of the people on the sofa felt bruised\u2014wondrously bruised and fussed about\u2014by the catastrophes of the preceding twenty-four hours.\nThe President felt much better, however, now that he had a testicle again.\nThe President felt much better now that he had a left testicle again.\nA very high source had privately imparted the information that, unless President Nixon acted promptly, the nation\u2019s nuclear-pharmaceutical program would suffer a catastrophic failure at eleven-thirty-three that morning. The failure would paralyze the President and the joint chiefs. They, and the nation, would be totally impotent.", "1_6_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-one in the morning</span>.\nThe clocks in the ships were not in harmony.\nInfantryman Rapp Gradus was a six-foot-one-and-a-quarter-inch27-year-old native of the island of Newport News in Virginia. He claimed an Irish father\u2014whose name he didn\u2019t know\u2014and a Swedish mother. These were both probably true, but the real story was that his mother had conceived him in a Turkish bath in Newport News, Virginia, with a contractor from Stockholm, Sweden. Gradus had been given his mother\u2019s name\u2014Nerissa.\nNerissa became a model, but so far as her son ever knew, she never modeled under her own name. She posed for glamor photographs set in exotic, Arabic, Arabian, Persian, Turkish, and Swedish settings.\nRapp Gradus met a handsome U.S. Army recruiting sergeant on a park bench in New York\u2019s Central Park. The Army man was a former Turkish bath contractor, with a Swedish name, who had left Sweden when he was nineteen years old. His name was $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 got Rapp Graduss signature on papers permitting the U.S. Army to exercise parental rights in all matters concerning Rapp,...", "1_17_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five forty-seven in the afternoon</span>. We had been driving for twelve minutes, and I was about six seconds away from telling the King to pull over and let me out when he took a sharp turn off the turnpike and rolled up to the tollbooths beside a huge white sign: SNUG HARBOR\u2014$1.\nI tore off a bill and handed it to Freeman on the run. He tossed the exact change into my stomach. Then he raced through the gate and spun the Camaro around, facing west, and I grabbed the .45 from the seat and hung it from my waistband in back, next to my shirt, before opening the door and stepping out. My mouth was very dry. The wind was shaking all the trees in Snug Harbor.\nThe King and the Duke...And a Hard Place in Pyramid Country\n7The idea of going upstairs to check my answering service did not occur to me until I was well out of the car, and even then I moved slowly in that direction. The young woman in the glass booth detected no movement. Her eyes were glazed with the false calm of someone under attack.\nMy only possible access to the upstairs was through the casino area, a dense jungle of machines that give out tickets instead of cash. To...", "2_16_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-nine in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF2 had been in the powder room of The Clinical Hospital of The State University of California at San Narciso for four minutes. She was beginning to feel she was being watched. She was also beginning to think, without much conviction, maybe she ought to pack it in, go back to the hotel.\nShe didn\u2019t have much chance to think about this. Coming away from the pay phone she walked smack into a skinny young guy in shades and an open, scruffy-plastic raincoat, who grabbed and spun her around before she knew what was happening. He stuck a small, fierce knife up against her and said, \u201cCome on, baby.\u201d After a second he said, \u201cScream and I\u2019ll cut you.\u201d He had her by the shoulder, propelling her ahead of him toward the rest rooms. In his other hand he carried a small purse, $FIRSTNAMEF2\u2019s.\nHe pushed her through the door of the ladies\u2019 room and crowded in behind her. \u201cSay something,\u201d he whispered.\nShe couldn\u2019t think of anything to say.", "2_7_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-one in the morning</span>, and the expressionless little blue gems of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s eyes were focused straight ahead. Folio after folio of close-order drill passed, followed by orders to march at attention or to march at ease, and then by another series of nerve-shocks that rearranged the expression on $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face.\nThere was a new formation.\n\"Companee\u2014ten-SHUN!\" bellowed Sergeant $SURNAME2. \"Facing\u2014im-PUSH!\" roared $SURNAME2. \"Left\u2014ORT! MARCH!\u201d\nThat was when the iron in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s head began to melt.\nThe perfect, immediate response of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s body to those commands\u2014left, march, face, formation\u2014however much agony the response cost $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014gave $FIRSTNAMEB0 a sudden glimpse of what was going on inside his head. If he could instantly and mechanically obey $SURNAME2\u2019s commands, could not his mind, his secret self, his real self, instantly disengage the terrible mechanisms of pain set up by the nerve-shocks, disengage them without being stabbed again and again by the nerve-shocks?", "0_11_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-four in the morning</span>. I stood out on the sheen of the highway and asked by signs if there was a restaurant in the neighborhood. A kid who looked like a high-school football player said yes and showed me the way to a local tourist house, where the landlady cooked me ham and eggs and fried potatoes in a kind of parody country kitchen, and I ate them with a napkin. Afterward I settled with her for the bill, two sixty-five for the lunch, ten cents for the telegram, and twenty cents for the ride back to town. She made change from a big wooden cash drawer that was worn down on the facing from her laying her cheek against it. That is how we are early to bed and early to rise in West Virginia.\nFrom the restaurant I walked to the bus station down the main street. It was just a shed that happened also to sell candy. At calm times of the year it might handle as many as one bus a day; now it was jammed with six arrivals head-on, disgorging milling tourists. I stepped gingerly among them, knowing they were packed tight as water and liable to disperse like water at any threat of change in the environment. The Greyhound man, amazed at the traf f and...", "2_8_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight twenty-three in the morning</span>. Six minutes before the beginning of the last day of the first week of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s war with the Tralfamadorians.\nThere was thunder, and a bright blue light appeared on the wall. A door in the wall had dilated.\nA trim blonde young man in a pink dressing gown came through the door. He carried his smoking material, saliva, and a newspaper in a large cigarette holder.\nThis was $SURNAME0.\n$SURNAME0 snapped his cigarette holder shut. The material inside it was aspirated into tiny fibers and filtered into his lungs.\n\"Good morning, $FIRSTNAMEM2,\" he said to the dog on the wall.\nThe dog wagged its tail.\n\"Morning, Skip,\" said Britt.\n\"If you please\u2014\" said $SURNAME0 to an unseen watchman, \"we\u2019d like the news from Earth in Dream Bloody Mary material.\"", "2_11_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-four in the morning</span> when the owner of a Martian filling station, having cranked out the remains of his annual Viper, walked over to the space ship, expecting oilmen. The filling station owner was surprised to learn that no oil was needed in a space ship, and that no oilmen were necessary either.\nThe ship had crashed there so long before that the metal had adhered strongly to the sand.\nThe owner used his pocketknife to chip out $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nHe asked no ransom. He asked only that they bring his metal scraps back when they left. He asked only this so that he could prove to his neighbors that he was the rescuer of the strange captives.\nHe was forty-one years old, and had a rather awed look about him. The look had always been there.\nWhen he was a child the look had been overshadowed by the more playfully variegated odysseys of other imaginations.\nAs a child, the filling station owner had imagined himself to be a safecracker, and he would practice that profession by crooking a finger in the perfect blackness of triple-locked safety deposit vaults.\nThen as a high school boy, he fancied himself a Harvard football hero.", "1_21_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine twenty-three in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was shouldered and jostled and confused in the crowd until he found himself standing against the crowd\u2019s surging gutters at one side of the brown square. There he fidgeted, looking up and trying to count the moons overhead.\nNow he began to search for the man in the red-stained white smock again. Someone had shoved the muzzle of a torch into $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s face when he tried to question someone about the man in the white smock.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 ducked his head when the torch came near again. He took another look at the man in the white smock. He was no longer sure whether the man was real. Perhaps he had imagined the man.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 knelt in the trampled litter of the brown square, jammed his hands into the gutters, and bowed his head. He thought that the noises of the crowd would drown out the words in his head. As a matter of fact, the crowd\u2019s noises were very low, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 could easily have heard the words in his head if he had chosen to.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 remained bowed for several minutes, fearful of what his head might reveal when he finally pressed his eyeballs to the lenses of his thought-...", "2_2_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n/a/\nVoir V NOTRE DAME DE LA MERDE.\n\u2014sign over the entrance to the Allied Jewish Forces command post in the bell of Notre Dame\n\u201cWelcome to Transsexual Desert Nights!\u201d\n\u201c\u2018On this bare stage of sand have we lived and died.\u2019\u201d\n\u201cWho is that? Who\u2019s that? Is that Neil \u2018Lobster\u2019 Formatting? Please say it\u2019s Neil \u2018Lobster\u2019 Formatting.\u201d\n\u201cThat is greatly unclear at this moment.\u201d\n\u201cWe have no bad incoming, that is you, guys.\u201d\n\u201c \u2018This drum hath taught me to handle thunder, / And I perceive I no more fear it / Than I fear thee.\u2019 \u201d\n\u201cThat does not sound like Neil \u2018Lobster\u2019 Formatting.\u201d\n\u201cMr. Gerber, who\u2019s that?\u201d\n\u201cWith your permission, I\u2019m going to shoot the television.\u201d\n\u201cNo go, over.\u201d", "2_1_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-eight in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME0 left a silence. The materialization brushed itself very gently.\n$SURNAME0 spoke again. \"Would you like to guess who your visitor is going to be, $FIRSTNAMEB0?\" he said.\nThere was another silence. Then, winningly, \"Annie?\" said $SURNAME0.\nThe materialization dropped dust.\nThere was laughter\u2014$SURNAME0\u2019s laughter at his own wit, and the laughter of the materialization that was like a great, blowsy woman\u2019s.\nAt this point, $FIRSTNAMEB0 flattened his ears against the vent again. Someone was coming.\nThe footsteps were loud.\nThe person at the head of the procession was wheezing asthmatically from the effort of climbing twenty-three stories. The person had a thing like a pneumatic hammer clenched between his legs. He pressed the trigger, and the hammer made a rending noise.\nAt the head of the procession was an honor guard of four Martian privates in space-polo uniforms, with golden rifles at present orders.\nA man carrying a fruit bowl brought up the rear.", "2_16_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\nGregor, who shares the throne of heaven with Winstons, constantly increases.\nMany believe that as a result of the strange doings in his house that rainy afternoon in August $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 lost his mind.\nThis was not so.\nBetween four twenty-seven and four twenty-nine in the afternoon Mr. $SURNAME1 sat in his living room, without moving and without speaking. He sat in his big leather chair by the stove, staring at nothing and seeing nothing. He was in a trance. He saw the most beautiful visions.\nHis eyes were the windows of his soul, but his soul saw no windows. His intent was not a crime\u2014only a gift for his son. He detached himself from reality and went free among the images of his own making.\nHe was crazy.", "1_16_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The space ship was still in the Earth\u2019s atmosphere, but it was rising rapidly. Pierce Me was prepared to press the green button on the contrivance he held in his hand. His finger hovered above the button.\nEulalia Garble, her hands folded in her lap, looked stonily at a corner of the cabin.\nPierce Me\u2019s features were now strained and drawn as though they had been put through a wringer. His knees shook as though they might rattle apart. Sweat poured down his cheeks. Again his lips trembled. Again his pupils writhed. Again his fingers danced. Again he looked out the portholes, as though asking the Earth to forgive him.\nThen, abruptly, he pressed the green button.\nThe ship rocked.\nA small light went out on the control panel.\nThere was a possibility that this light, indicating that the ship was unmanned, might discourage explorers of the ship.\nMe hoped so. He hoped the ship would crash in the New Mexico desert.\nMe\u2019s labor was finished. There was nothing more he could do but pray that he had really fired the ship properly.\nHe prayed to the spirit of Robert H. Goddard to...", "1_8_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span> when Lenore Beadsman, having received the news, had entered the lobby of the TransContinent building. Cleveland\u2019s sky was a purplish black and hung with dust so tiny and light it was like the sky had cancer and was liquefying and pouring. It reflected meanly in the dark windows of the lobby, that big box of stone.\n9: 08 A.M.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 in his POB Schwarz child\u2019s chair, delivering his report to the fetally curled and paralysed man who sat signifying living essence of Stonecipheco, in his Executive Presidential Suite.\n\u201cSo anyway that\u2019s the gist. I start out, sign a release saying I\u2019m not going to sue them if I fall on the ice or something, and I\u2019m on my way.\u201d\nSnap to attention, then, graceful young G. I., when veteran switchboard operator comes to attention herself. Legs apart, motionless.\nVeteran switchboard operator, back to languid attention. Pulls out 78 rpm record of Elton John singing Tiny Dancer, winds it, inserts it into all five mouth-holes of console simultaneously. Hums along.", "0_13_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes until two o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Three seconds before two o'clock, $FIRSTNAMEB2 lay back in the grass, closed his eyes, relaxed every muscle. He kept his eyes closed, rigidly relaxed, for two hours. The two hours crawled by.\nWhat remained for $FIRSTNAMEB2 to do was neither easy nor pleasant.\nHe had to find his way from the Isle of Cyprus to $FIRSTNAMEM2, the capital of the nation of WilmingWilm.\nThis was far from easy.\nWilmerston was ruled by a tight, efficient police force. The only person allowed to carry a gun in Wilmerston was the Prime Minister. This was to prevent the populace from doing anything rash\u2014such as hanging the Prime Minister.\nWilmerston had an efficient, clean, rapid-transit system. $FIRSTNAMEB2 could not leap from rooftop to rooftop, as he had in Newport and Spirohius. Wilmerston, being an industrial and a political city, was a jumbled maze of girders, pitons and soot. None of the buildings had any flat surfaces on which a small Person could safely stand.\nAll of the high places in Wilmerston were guarded, for the city was plagued by assassins.\nThe written code of ethics of the political and industrial...", "1_20_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nBy eight-twenty I had found a restaurant, ordered a meal, and was eating it with the Chicago Tribune spread out on the tablecloth in front of me.\nI had ordered a big meal, and by God I was hung...ury when I had finished it.\nThen, sitting back, full and warm and completely at ease for the first time since I had left Denver, I tackled the Adam and Eve problem again.\nI tried to figure out a way to make the framework of my digression clear, so that when I finally came to the series of episodes with Walter I would be logically ready to handle them.\nObviously it would be best to start the story of my life with a picture of a naked man chained to the mast of a small raft and surrounded by myriads of sharks.\nBut why a man? Why that man? How does he happen to be there?\nWho is he?\nWhere is the raft going?\nBack to the fish on a small raft in the middle of Wake Island Lagoon.\nI looked around the restaurant. There was only one other diner, a woman, and her back was to me. I could see every thing but her head and shoulders. I could see the...", "0_20_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight nineteen in the evening</span>. I suddenly realized I didn't have enough change to phone Roy's. I opened every drawer in the room trying to find some. There wasn't anything but a few pennies. I dashed out to the nearest booth, trying to estimate the cost of a call to Las Vegas. I decided it would take damn near everything I had. But there was absolutely no choice. This was it. I was either ready or not. A minute lost now might be too late, might cost me everything.\nMy fingers were sweating on the nickels as I plunged them into th&metal slots ofthe dial. 17A. . . . 9A . . . 17A . . . 6A . . . 6A . . . 17A . . . I had no idea where Roy's number was, or what it was, but I had to take a chance on it coming up, and coming up quickly. My finger was getting tired and aching from all that dialing. 9A . . . 19A . . . Forget it --- just forget it\u2014I had only five nickels left and the cost was five more\u2014impossible. I let the phone drop back with a clatter and mideyes |>nt on my watch,...", "0_17_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span> of Earth'slingering day.\nThe Titanian Alps and Olympus Mons stood storm-colored in the distance. All about the space ship Endurance and about $FIRSTNAMEB2, the robot, was red sand and the hummocks $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 the dog had built so monotonously, so doggedly, so apparently without aim.\nAll this was under the lurid lighting effects of a Martian dawn.\nThere was something murky and sinister going on behind the walls of the Universe. Things were conspiring to the dastardly exclusion of $FIRSTNAMEB2 from the Solar system, from the company of beautiful women, from the fellowship of material progress.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 had been chosen by $SURNAME0 and the Space Wanderer to carry the adventure of Earthling man to Mars. And now turmoil, seeming to be touched off by a coincidence, was plunging $FIRSTNAMEB2 toward unredeemable disaster, was surging against the possibility of his making good.\nFour minutes to six.\nThe color draining out of the sky told $FIRSTNAMEB2 that Earthlings, too, were being caught in the glare and glareproof shadows of the solar flares.\nA Martian sunset was staining the sky.\n\"Skip!\" cried $FIRSTNAMEB2, weeping. \"...", "2_20_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-seven in the evening</span>. A golden ship and its crew had just blasted off, carrying $FIRSTNAMEB0 and Martian Reznik away from Mars to somewhere else. An attractive, dark-haired woman with nothing on but a pair of pastel blue shorts had gone with them. The woman was Cookie, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s wife\u2014ex-wife, actually. Her name was no longer Cookie, either. She had changed that, too. Now she called herself $FIRSTNAMEF1. She had changed that because $FIRSTNAMEB0 was still $FIRSTNAMEB0, no matter what awful adventures he got into, and she didn\u2019t want to keep calling him by the name he had been so silly as to give himself. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was still $FIRSTNAMEB0, and $FIRSTNAMEF1 wanted him to keep being $FIRSTNAMEB0, to keep being that helpless thing.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 wanted a future without $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nShe had her plans.\nThe mate to the golden ship and its crew was the very ship and crew that had taken $FIRSTNAMEB0, Reznik, and Cookie away from Mars. Not a word had been heard from the missing triad on Earth. A mute suspense had soothed Earth\u2019s many millions during the time.\nAnd now the ships and their crew were back.", "0_13_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>. No point going by Klarschiff\u2019s office, which she supposed would be in some building around Psych. She\u2019d head straight for Pierce\u2019s place. She was filled with curiosity. She imagined the seminar. There\u2019d been an edge to Pierce in the Administration Building earlier, and his eyes behind their glasses had actually seemed to dance.\nShe came onto that part of the campus where fraternity Row rose, huge and white, sheer as a cliff-face. She knew where Pierce had lived as an undergraduate. She couldn\u2019t imagine him in a fraternity; he was apparently less awkward with his graduate students than with freshmen. Behind the frat houses were some streetscapes like Peyton Place. No sign of a Pierce or any other dentist\u2019s house there either. She cut through the so-called Electronics Corridor, where Reed alumni fathers mailed sons\u2019 underwear by the pound, and found herself on the outer slope. The graduate students and even the professors here lived in moron huts and worked in chicken coops behind chicken wire. She was hunting now for 12 Princess Street. The map showed no such address.\nOrderly rows of tiny homes, shamelessly thrown up during the Hillier administration, now masked...", "0_14_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The place was an unknown planet that had been scorched to desert by a bewitched star not unlike our own sun. The name of the planet was Titan.\nAgain and again, the delegates of Earth had been to Titan, looking gauntly down through the choking acid clouds, searching the planetary expanse bare-eyed and through all the optical toys of modern science for something, anything, that looked like life.\nThey had the most powerful searchlights in the solar system.\nThey had magnifying spectacles that could enlarge a pane of glass, reveal the strange halos crystallized in its elements.\nThey had interferometers that could take the shiver of a violet in Bengal and bring it to rainbow birth before the eyes of observers in Tierra del Fuego.\nThey had fluoroscopic machines that sent beams of unpierced atoms streaking through time. They peered at you through these gadgets, explaining with long words and the manner of bored supplicants that time was short, that three billion Solarians waited desperately for word of some living thing on Titan before their sun scalded away the last life on their own planet.\nVenturing bravely into the poisonous clouds, they used instruments that registered cosmic-dust,...", "0_6_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes past six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nTopalian's motor-boat happened to be named Alfie.\nSo much had been taken care of so far in advance that there was no doubt in anybody's mind that Goon was the culprit. As a result, nobody was much surprised at what he did next. He wasn't caught by any security system, he just got up, looked at $FIRSTNAMEF0 and the empty crib, her husband and his friend, and loped out of the room without a word, as unemotionally as a man lighting a cigarette.\nThat completed the count of twelve. $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, Stony Stevenson, and Hugo Artemis Entrup conducted the search for Goon together. They spread out and were gone for two minutes. They met again in the living room, without having seen anything.\n\"If I were he,\" said $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, \"and I had him\u2014I\u2019d jump out the window now, before somebody spots me.\"\n\"Ridiculous,\" whispered Stony Stevenson, who had never married. He tapped the barrel of his rifle meaningfully. \"There\u2019s no place to hide on this island\u2014and this baby is well worth finding. If Goon has him, we\u2019ll get him.\"", "0_4_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-nine in the morning</span>. I had been on the bus thirty-two hours. I was bathed in sweat and exhausted. My muscles had atrophied; I couldn't open the door. I had to climb over through the window, hanging by my hands, then dropping\u2014about six feet, right on the flat dry mud of that Nevada desert dawn. The dawn had been dry and hot for an hour.\nI wasted no time. I hit the highway and walked along under the westward mountains, skirting the town. Ahead was an apple orchard in lavender air. I headed for it. A ghost barn blasted by the rising sun loomed on my right. Some horses in a pasture blew away with the wind. I walked by cornfields toward the trees.\nI came to a long irrigation ditch full of green water. I put my thumb in my mouth and sucked for a while. The water was intensely fresh and cold, impregnated with minerals. I sucked three handfuls and lay on my back and let the sun soak into every pore of my body.\nI got up and walked along the ditch, drank again. It was wonderful. I climbed some willows and sprawled out on a flat rock, felt the sun on my face and neck,...", "2_23_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven sixteen in the evening</span>.\nTwo minutes later, the Constellation crashed in an olive orchard in the suburban community of Anaheim.\nA fire engine was on the scene within sixty seconds.\nAnd as it raced across the flats of the olive orchard and arrived at the burning aircraft, its crew was stunned to discover that the pilot of the Constellation had survived the crash, but had evidently killed himself by taking off his seatbelt and banging his head against the windshield.\nThe news of the crash and the identity of the pilot were flashed over the telephone lines to all newspapers and radio stations in California within a space of ten minutes\u2014and this report was picked up and relayed eastward in a continuing process of lightning communication that covered half the continent in a matter of thirty or forty minutes. It appeared in the New York papers just after twelve-thirty in the morning\u2014although the Constellation had crashed at approximately eleven-oh-eight in the evening.", "0_6_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six twenty-seven in the morning</span>. At three fifteen, the temperature in Denver, Colorado, had been 111 degrees, the hottest day since July first of the year. But now it was falling steadily. By noon it would be a comfortable 85 degrees and sunny, with a gentle southwest breeze. Fine dust and sand were blowing into the dormitory. The majority of the men had been up since dawn and were studying. A few were sneaking an extra hour of sleep before their 9 A.M. classes. Specious excuses and malodorous attempts at blackmail were being exchanged between Great Army privates, who were up to attend classes, and Red Army privates, who slept in the bunks under them, and were scolded, prodded, jabbed and yanked about by the ankles to make them get up and get out. Rinehart was in his pajamas. His roommate, a mailman from Indianapolis, was asleep. Rinehart was smoking and reading the last chapter of a sensational thriller called The Black Issek by someone named Raymond Chandler. He wanted to finish the book before classes began. He intended to finish the book before breakfast. He was a senior, leading his class in English and Journalism. He was in boyish good humor. He had slept well and...", "0_17_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span> before we finally arrived. I was physically sick from the ride and from holding our bags. We stormed into $FIRSTNAMEB3's hotel. It was a dull, staid, old-fashioned building and I gathered there had been a reform government in the town since he left, among the results being the installation in the living-room of Granfather's old-fashioned high-laced canopied bed that he used to sleep in when he visited his mother in New Orleans.\nI started rubbing my back.\n\u201cMan, this bed is terrible.\u201d\n\u201cWhat are you talkin about? That's old Granfather's bed. Nothing wrong with that.\u201d\n\u201cWhat happened? Whose bed is it now?\u201d I demanded.\n\u201cWell, some relative died and they let me have it. Dolores spends most her time in the kitchen, probably worried they're going to start coming after her again.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean, again?\u201d\n\u201cDon't worry, they haven't bothered her since that last time. Dawn, the maid, spends her life in the kitchen, and Horne, the baby, is the only one who sleeps in this room. He flunks...", "2_12_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-four post meridiem</span>. The people in the line did not speak. Waiting in line was the thing to do.\nWithout the line, there was nothing to think about but an individual\u2019s own insignificance.\nThe line was chronological order, human uninterestingness made beautiful by deep respect for the one ahead of you\u2014and for yourself, too, when your turn came.\nThe man from Mars, $FIRSTNAMEB0, had no place in the line. He was only six feet away from the most beautiful thing in the room.\nHe had not yet reached the foot of the pedestal on which the gilded and leathered old god Zeus languished.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was checking the faces in the crowd for someone whose face he might recognize. So far, he had recognized only one face. It was the face of the pilot who had flown his space ship to Earth. The pilot was standing manfully in the second row. Now $FIRSTNAMEB0 was searching the faces in the first row for Stony Stevenson\u2019s face. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was certain that Stony would be up front, at the head of the line.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 paused to pluck a biting insect out of his hair. The biting insect protested loudly, then flew away.", "2_20_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight forty-eight in the evening</span>.\nAnd the time had come for $FIRSTNAMEB0 to die\u2014according to the timepiece that was in his head.\n\"You are what you eat.\"\n\u2014sign in an Abernathy\u2019s Nutshell hitchhiker\u2019s shelter\nA lot of people used $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s Heavenly Hamburgers on West Broadway in Newport. But the crowd that came at lunchtime on Tuesday, April 7, 1953, was far from normal.\nThere was very little conversation. What conversation there was concerned very elementary things. Like how many hamburgers people were eating, and how big they were. What was a reasonable price for a meal, anyway? And whether any of the people had ever seen a man by himself\u2014an old man, not a boy\u2014a man as old as a grandfather, all alone in a restaurant. None of the people had ever seen anything like that. And, according to the way some of the people looked around the restaurant, they no longer believed the old man had ever existed.", "0_2_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-four in the morning</span>\u2014three o\u2019clock in the morning at Headquarters...and most of the middle Hell\u2019s Angels on duty had wandered off to the bar in back of the dutyroom. All I had around me was a handful of clean\u2014cut, square\u2014jawed young kids just out of college. Some were Highway Patrolmen; others were volunteers who had gotten hold of the idea that they could conduct themselves in a more ideal manner than their contemporaries. In the bicycle\u2014post era, when brass found that a certain number of kids with crew cuts would do as well for peace officers as anyone else, scores of idealistic communities began importing their policemen from the Ivy League. By the time hippies became a problem, most of the old coots in police work were ready for retirement, and the idealists\u2014kids who liked to square\u2014dance and coach Little League\u2014had turned into practical police executives who realized what they had to work with, and with few exceptions they did not insist on zeal or dedication as much as they looked for stability and sobriety and common sense. The square\u2014jawed young men surrounding me in the dutyroom were the answer to that appeal. They were ready for anything\u2014even the Hell\u2019s Angels. They had the look...", "0_19_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty in the evening</span>. At six, I called $FIRSTNAMEB3 and had him talk to $FIRSTNAMEF4 on the phone. \u201cWhat are you doing now?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cOh, nothing. Chair. Sittin around. Cigarette. Pretty blue smoke.\u201d He had $FIRSTNAMEF4 get up from her horizons of couch and come to the phone. \u201cWe're going to meet in thirty minutes, darling!\u201d he said. \u201cAnd now I want you to do one thing for me as quickly as some yellow taxi will carry you.\u201d\nHe had her dress, pack a bag, and rush over to my house. Now, at seven-twenty, she arrived. I felt awful when I saw her; there was no use putting off our sad scene. I'd dressed up in my thirties suit, put on a tie, shaved, prepared an announcement speech, everything; I went to the door and opened it before her ring; she stood there with a huge suitcase, the brow of her pretty big sensible lovely young face smudged with the road. I tried to smile. \u201cCome in, come in,\u201d I said. \u201cAnd no, no, don't bother to take off...", "1_17_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five eighteen in the afternoon</span>. The war continued. I marveled at my own coolness. Said hell would freeze over before I'd be a toad under McCarthy's stone. There was that big sport's word \u201chonor.\u201d Yes, and the integrity of Galatea Dunkel. I had, betwixt the two of them, promised myself to McCarthy\u2014and apparently was mad enough, or sodden enough, not to care. The White Whale, gentlemen. That absurd white light out there in the blue and cold November air above our heads, right now no doubt falling onto the white belly of some lonely clouds far above a white line of creaming waves on a white sea off the white key of Miami...Isle of Pices. Well, we were still in our assigned position at the rail, but I found myself fishing out my car keys and saying, \u201cI think I will go get my car out of the lot.\u201d No response from either of them. Oh, the white light; and the absolute zero below.\nBut anything rather than heating in that room.\nI walked out. My God, yes, I had a lot to think about. I would renege on my deal with $FIRSTNAMEB3. This was particularly clear to...", "0_3_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-six in the morning</span> according to the clock above the gas fireplace directly before Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1, who was sitting in a leather chair angled slightly toward that clock. The clock was bigger than the other clocks. The gas fireplace and the leather chair were in the room where Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1 slept. Both the room and the chair were very different from the monitors, communications devices, and work space outside Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s office, where he generally and when possible slept. At three twenty-six, a night-shift operator cleared her throat, bringing her shadow into the light of Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s lamp.\n\u201cYes ma\u2018am,\u201d Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1 said into his squarish telephone. \u201cYou may put her on, Doris.\u201d There was a click. \u201cYes, ma\u2018am ...\u201d Mr. $FIRSTNAMEF1 began, into the telephone. He held a pen in his free hand.\nLenore and Candy Mandible were taken to the massive oak door in the far side of the lobby, and the gasping shadow in the yogurt, and out, by Lang, the security man with a soul patch.\n/a/\n\u201cAny port in a storm.\u201d\n\u2014W.", "0_22_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-three in the evening</span>. By ten forty-eight, Salo was gone. $SURNAME1 was left with not much more than the console to play with\u2014and an associate in disgrunted service who happened, at that very moment, to be in grave peril.\nThere was a son et lumi\u00e8re at $SURNAME0\u2019s Tomb, and this would have excluded Panek from any appreciable participation in the celebration of the good news. But $SURNAME0 had decreed that the admiral, Mrs. $SURNAME0, the litter-mate children, and all the invited great of Earth be invited to watch the son et lumi\u00e8re from a rowboat tied to the little island on the River of Destiny directly across from the tomb.\n$SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF1 were in this rowboat, watching. It is scarcely worth mentioning, in view of the purpose for which the meeting had been arranged, that $FIRSTNAMEF0 was at her most exquisite and virginal, and that $SURNAME1 was freshly shaven, soberly dressed, and was not drunk.\n$SURNAME1 was sticking to orange juice.\nIt developed later that Mama Olson had been so tolerant about $SURNAME1\u2019s drunkenness, so accepting of his vulgar good fellowship, so pleased with his affectionate handling of young $FIRSTNAMEB2...", "2_7_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-nine in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\nThe place was the inside of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s skull.\n\"I just thought of something awful,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0 to his face.\n\"I just thought of something so awful all of my bones hurt, and my heart hurts, and my tongue hurts,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\n\"Tell me about it, awful,\" said his face.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had thought of something so awful that he staggered with the cumulative horrors of it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 sat down on the exercise mat. He put the flat of his hands over his eyes. He rocked back and forth. He moaned. He wept.\n\"Tell me, $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014\" his face said.", "0_7_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven thirty-eight in the morning</span> of October 3, 1962. Through the kitchen window --turning and reaching now with the spatula -- Mary Sherman could see her son trying vainly to wake his daughter. The sun was bright and it was going to be a fine day, suited to the long trip they had planned. At ten o\u2019clock they would close the house, and at noon they would start off across the desert to Disneyland, in California.\nBut at the moment the little girl was having vaporation problems of her own. She had woken up, wide awake, and as a result was compelled to follow strange urgings from deep within her skinny ten-year-old bowels. She felt she had to GO NOW.\n\u201cHurry up, $FIRSTNAMEF1, you\u2019re holding everything up,\u201d yelled her brother. \u201cIf you\u2019re not ready by the time I come back, I\u2019ll never speak to you again.\u201d He ran toward the corner of the house.\n\u201cHarlun, you be nice to $FIRSTNAMEF1,\u201d called the mother, without looking around.\n\u201cIt\u2019s Bomb that\u2019s holding everything up,\u201d yelled her son.\nThe little girl, $FIRSTNAMEF1...", "1_23_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven twenty-nine in the evening</span> of December 26, 2007, by the chronometer in her head and by the glowing clock on the marble panty wall of Metzger\u2019s Bungalow Heaven, as $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas sat on the bed, watching the shadow throw itself across the floor and up the far wall, till all was dark. She remained hesitant, undisciplined about the hours of sleep she might have left. Often it didn\u2019t end till dawn. Tonight, however, remembering her date, almost too late now, with Randolph Driblette, she undressed, went into the bathroom, turned on the shower. Soon enough entered Randolph Driblette himself, in what he called, tonight, optimism gray slacks, a white blazer and a yachting cap. $FIRSTNAMEF2 sat on the sink, drawing on her knee a compulsory artificial rabbit-fur \u201crumba\u201d kidskin boot, fastening the strap around her calf. \u201cThis is the first time,\u201d she said, \u201cyou\u2019ve worn anything but a winter coat.\u201d Tonight\u2019s improvisation meant they could make the rendezvous with The Courier\u2019s Muse.\n\u201cIs it important?\u201d said...", "0_20_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-eight in the evening</span>, and I had just checked into the Hotel Gloriana, in Las Vegas. I ran directly to the window and looked down at the whores on the corner. None of them were attractive, but that was only a question of my personal taste; like any man of principle I preferred a woman with some flesh on her bones\u2014but then, if things got too heavy, I guess I wouldn\u2019t have balked if one of those long-haired bruisers down there'd taken an interest in the action, switched on his feminine intuition or whatever and rushed to the rescue with a cute little quip like: \u201cCome on, motherfucker, I'm Pamela Anderson Lee and I can act!\u201d\nLess than two hours before, a similar woman made the ultimate sexual sacrifice for me, but I had trouble remembering her name\u2014and now, Lord, I felt like a complete asshole. There was no point in rushing these fuck bitches on the corner; for all I knew, they might not even be able to cry on cue. I had to get into town, have a bite and hit the drugstore for some Red Ball Jets. The problem with acid, mescaline and those other drugs is that they generate terrible food cravings.", "2_16_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after four o'clock in the afternoon</span> when we finally pulled out of the parking lot. On the Interstate, my attorney was driving slower than the speed\u2014limit: a mere ninety\u2014five. But even in standard low gear, the Shark was burning oil at an alarming rate. I could smell it, and I knew we were leaving a dark stain of it on the pavement behind us. But it was better than calling a tow truck. We made LA in record time.\nIt was four fifty\u2014seven, according to the car's digital clock, when we drove onto the ramp at the junction of Sunset & Las Vegas Boulevard. The clock said four fifty\u2014seven . . . but my attorney was convinced that it was four fiftyeight . . .\nWhich would explain the blank look on the face of the motorcycle cop when we finally spotted him, sitting at the top of the on\u2014ramp with his Matchless parked across the lanes.", "1_19_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-eight in the evening</span> of December thirty-first, a raw and gloomy evening, when a rather good-looking young fellow, who, while not effeminate, had superlatively delicate features and hands and feet, who lived with his mother, who was a pretty passable copy of Mrs Harris, one of our more expensive Winter River spinsters, was waiting at the Junction in his overcoat to take the eight o\u2019clock train. The young fellow, who was really good enough-looking to be an object of considerable wild desire to female hearts, was named Roberts, John Joseph Edward Thomas Roberts. He was rich, healthy, clean, and had the softest hands and feet in all Connecticut. He weighed one hundred and sixty. His eyes were almost blue. His hair was auburn. And it should be said here and now that this young fellow, this good-looking young fellow, was named John Joseph Edward Thomas Roberts and weighed one hundred and sixty pounds, etc., etc., etc., because this young fellow was, in forty-seven short hours, to whit, between nine and ten o\u2019clock on the morning of February second, to become the third great passion in the life of $SURNAME1 the Star-man, $FIRSTNAMEF0 the McKluskey stenographer,...", "0_23_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was fourteen minutes after eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was the living room of the house assigned to $FIRSTNAMEF1 and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 in the housing area assigned to married quarters in the Mars training center.\nThe living room was being used as a shambles.\nThree trunks stood open on the floor. All debris from the miscellany in the trunks had been piled up in one corner.\nIt was a corner that $FIRSTNAMEF1 didn\u2019t have to look at. She sat, in a rocking chair, in a corner opposite the corner of the mess. That opposite corner was the only corner in the room that mattered\u2014the northeast corner.\nThat corner was dominated by a color-television set, on which were both bee cave video on one channel and the view from the orbiting Tralfamadorian space ship on the other.\nThe rabbit-toothed man and his son were watching only the bee cave video on the set. The son was interested in it only because the steel ball had carried him to the cave, and because his father expected him to be interested in it.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 wasn\u2019t interested in the television screen at all. Her interest in it was purely official\u2014that is to say, concerned only her duties as a Martian widow. Her...", "1_13_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-four in the afternoon</span>. The place we were in, was a Spanish hotel called the Hotel Montana. It was a three-story white building that crouched under a stone gateway and beside a street that led down the hill out of Madrid. Behind the hotel was a broken-down park where three crows with lice in their feathers squatted on a dry tree-stump. The birds ogled the street. Crowds of American youths came marching down the street, wearing the new narrow suit, carrying suit-cases, buying ties at wonderful prices, and looking for the fights. One by one, in mismated pairs, they stepped into the elevator and dropped out of sight below. Each would-be Corbett looked middle-Western, and each ruddy youth from Iowa or Kentucky or Ohio carried with him in his eyes the saddening secrets of the heart of America. Poor boys! They resented what was happening. They were quiet about it. Their non-commotion merely cloaked the seething bitterness within. The elevator boys were really wonderful. The pressure must have been terrific for them to act like that. When you thought of the things that must have slipped out under those cushions -- whiskers and whiskey and blondes and fights and smiles -- you had pity for...", "2_1_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-five in the morning</span>. The lighting in the apartment had been reduced to a meek violet gloaming. There wouldn\u2019t be any more pain for $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nThe two bodies in the two stiff aluminum caskets in the two resonant vitrinums on its walls had not been put there for the entertainment of callers. The bodies were intended to make a point\u2014and to make it made that point over and over again.\nThe point which the bodies were so punctiliously made to make was the point that physical bodies were no gift at all, were indeed jolly little packages of trouble, inflictors of pain and implementors of prune filling.\nThe point was driven home by the fact that the bodies in the vitrinums were so ancient as to be barely recognizable semi-corpses.\nThe fact that the bodies were known to be those of $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was incidental. For all anyone knew, the bodies might be those of two beggars from Mars who had lingered in Brighton inside the iron palings for a half century.\nThe bodies were naked, were gory, were transparently infested.", "0_23_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven twenty-two in the evening</span> and I was alone in New York City. I was suddenly worried about $FIRSTNAMEB3, so I called his aunt's house in Paterson. He was home. I said, \u201cWhat are you doing?\u201d\n\u201cNothing, man, sitting here with Eddie. Listen, here's $FIRSTNAMEF4, she's all excited because she got a job offer from her aunt in Chicago.\u201d At that point $FIRSTNAMEF4 took the phone and said she wanted to go to New York with me; she'd just be running away from her problems if she stayed with her aunt in Chicago, she didn't love the man she was going to marry. She became sad when she heard my sad story, and wished she could do something for me. I said we'd have to sleep on that. \u201cYes,\u201d she said, \u201cdon't forget me.\u201d\nThere was one other girl I didn't even have time to tell my sad story to. I think it intrigued her, but in the end she said, \u201cGo on, go, go to your Western dream.\u201d She was very sexy, and I was fruitlessly tempted. We had bought some whiskey and I was drunk. We clung together at the Greyhound...", "1_3_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-one in the morning</span>. We'd left L.A. at midnight. My attorney had just picked me up at Wally's Wham Bam Burger on Sunset at La Brea in Hollywood, and we were still talking about this ditz with the crystal meth who'd come on to us in a bar and said she wanted to \u201cget out of Dodge\u201d with us. She said she knew where all the sensual areas were on the human body and could show them to us if we'd give her a quarter ounce of smack. But my attorney wouldn't go for it. He said it would be like letting a sow suck eggs. \u201cChrist!\u201d I said. \u201cWe're not buying all her filthy knowledge, just the jolt.\u201d My attorney is a very serious young man but he has moments, too, where he loses all control. Just then we rounded a curve and almost ran over a big white dog that looked like a sheep. \u201cShit,\u201d said my attorney. \u201cWe should have hit that thing. Did you see it streak across the road? It might have had rabies.\u201d He was ready to get out and look for it. Maybe it had been killed, we could have dragged it to the side...", "0_15_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The tragic fight in the Space Scout warehouse had begun at twelve-fifteen that morning. Now it was all over.\nOn the field below, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 put their hands against the sides of their heads, trying to talk to each other. Their minds were still gagged.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was searching his pockets for his trusty harmonium, before applying for enlistment as a genuine Christian martyr. His search didn\u2019t take long, for there was nothing to it\u2014he had left the harmonium back in the barracks, having no immediate need for it.\nLike all members of the Church of God the Utterly Indifferent, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had only one true possession\u2014his sacred harmonium. Only one true possession, and that the properly reverent handling of that one true possession.\nThe harmonium had been ground out of a big plate of a very common alloy, chromium 6-electron-4. The plate had been delivered to $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 in a small wooden box, also apparently made of chromium 6-electron-4. The name of the alloy was stamped on the bottom of the box. There was nothing else in the box.\nThe harmonium\u2019s pipe-work came...", "0_12_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-six post meridiem</span> when the Acambis code clerk staggered bleary-eyed into his cubicle. He sat down at his desk and clapped for his messenger. There was the rumble of an elevator shaft and then Sid\u2019s rubbery face poked out of a stainless steel grille.\n\u201cJesus, I\u2019m sick at my stomach,\u201d said the code clerk. \u201cGo across the street and get me a Times and a black coffee, real strong, with lots of sugar. I\u2019ve got to end today\u2019s dispatch before it\u2019s too late. Hurry. Come on, get a move on.\u201d\n\u201cHoly cow,\u201d said Sid. \u201cYou sound pretty sick. Can\u2019t anybody else do your work?\u201d\n\u201cJust hurry across the street and get that Times and that coffee,\u201d said the code clerk, leaning his head on his hands.\nJust then the Division bell mounted high up in the garret of the office building began to clang. The clang was picked up from building to building, from division to division, across the plain, and the drab people put on their hats and coats, brushed the lint, chalk,...", "2_22_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten forty-eight in the evening</span>.\nThe place was a small and slightly crooked room. In it, looking through a rain-blurred window, was a man with elbows on the window ledge and a row of medals on his chest. He looked at the mud-soaked campus of the University of California at Berkeley.\nThe man with the medals on his chest was Fleet Admiral Sir Riplay, K. B. E., D. S. O. He had commanded His Britannic Majesty\u2019s fleets in the Pacific from a distance of ten thousand miles.\nSir Riplay\u2019s hair was gray. He had never had much color in his hair.\nThe expression in Fleet Admiral Sir Riplay\u2019s gray eyes was pleasant. His gray eyes looked out the window at the rainy campus of the University of California at Berkeley. They were the exultant eyes of a man who has lived ninety-seven years and can still recall how good a single cup of coffee tasted when he was twenty-one years old.", "1_12_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter until one o'clock in the afternoon</span> in San Narciso, California, and in $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas' backyard, when Metzger suddenly reappeared.\n\u201cYou\u2019re insane,\u201d she screamed into the sun, \u201cyou leave him with those people?\u201d\n\u201cThey\u2019re good people,\u201d Metzger said, \u201cI just now checked; also he\u2019s driving them up to San Jos\u00e9 tonight, for their TV thing.\u201d He walked up to, laid his head in her lap, let her stroke his hair.\n\u201cYou\u2019re drugged,\u201d she said. \u201cYou\u2019re not telling me.\u201d\n\u201cAbout the LSD?\u201d He grinned. \u201cNo, honest. I\u2019m on the level.\u201d\n\u201cMetzger, if you\u2019re putting me on, something terrible will happen.\u201d \u201cGod,\u201d he said, \u201cI love you.\u201d She held herself, to keep from huddling to him, to hold his head, hard in the sun. Then, presently, they took off their clothes and walked down to the waterside and out onto a pier.", "2_6_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-four in the morning</span>. The services were beginning. Stony Vandale of the Beatniks intoned the 23rd Psalm. Allen Ginsberg, with a tin can tied to one ear, read a poem comparing the flaming death of a statistic to the descent of man into hell. Ellsworth Pierce reversed his jacket lapels in order to turn the Roman collar right side out. Olongo Mourning delighted a score of cameras by casting aside the rite of holy water in favor of holy vodka. Kansan Elmer Ganch had to be restrained from attacking a vid tech with his pastoral staff. Cordwood Gomer condemned the trappings of the Martian military in scathing terms.\nIt was a hell of a show.\nIt was lost on the people of Newport. Newport was as serene as a cooling coma. In the clear, tinted, perfumed air of Newport, the people of Newport watched the rites on television.\nWhoever had put together the ceremonies on Mars had known more about pomp than about the message. There were seven religionists on Mars\u2014the greatest assortment of religious berryings in human history.", "1_22_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to eleven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nMr $SURNAME0 stood at the upstairs window of his mansion, one thousand feet above the ground. He had cranked the window open, and he was smoking a Turkish cigarette in a long, amber cigarette holder.\nHe watched the sunset.\nThe sunset was spectacular.\nThere was only one other man in sight, a Sentinel on patrol, far below.\nPoole, naked to the buff, was seated in a wicker chair in the mansion\u2019s air-conditioned solarium. His head was filled with cotton wool.\nSection 2\nTHE PARABLE OF THE DRIED DUNG FROG\nThe Parable of the Dried Dung Frog is found in Skip Chapters, one of the small privately-printed Skip litters. The tale is included in its entirety in the Coronet article, \"$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1: In Love With His Own Luck,\" by Walter Champion. It is typical of theSkip comment on the ups and downs and ins and outs of the Galactic Romance.\n\"Some things are beyond even the Prophet\u2019s understanding,\" wrote Skip. \"Why should the other fellow get the good break and not you? Is luck sent to us just to make us sore? Can anyone explain the way it...", "2_23_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-six in the evening</span>.\nOutside the barrack, a company of harmoniums was on double march, being drilled by a master sergeant with a bag over his head. The marching columns made the barrack tremble. One, two, three\u2014high step. One, two, three\u2014high step.\nThe master sergeant had the reputation, among his fellow harmoniums, of being a martinet. No one below the rank of fullcolonel would naturally have cared about such a reputation. The high command was leery of such a reputation. A master-sergeant with a reputation for a martinet-ical obsession with perfection could get conveniently drunk one night and shoot a general to death in a brothel.\nAt eleven thirty-seven, outside the barrack, the master sergeant suddenly raised both his arms as all his drilled soldiers did. The ranks, who had been singing a song in time to the march, broke their formation and doubled up, laughing, to slap their thighs.\nThe singing stopped with a clank.", "1_12_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-two post meridiem</span> when they were sitting side by side on the windowsil trying to find out what all the houses were. They had decided it was lunchtime and were having a sort of picnic with some cheese Robert Cohn had brought up, and some fruit. They lay with their naked feet on the stone sill. The good franc had gone down-hill to some extent, but had stabilized at five to the dollar. $FIRSTNAMEB5\u2019s feet were small and well shaped and very white. On the instep was a broad, shallow depression which when $FIRSTNAMEB5 was sitting one could not see. She had a very curving calf and a very high, tight, firm behind. The instep was so lovely it looked as sensitive as a hand, and the high hard curve of her calves made her seem lifting and young.\n\u201cWhat are all those funny little rooms up there on top?\u201d asked $FIRSTNAMEB5 of the roofs of the houses across the street.\n\u201cThat\u2019s where the domestics live. Those are the privies. The family have them luxurious, with marble columns and hot and cold water, and all the modern conveniences. The poor old domestics eat in the kitchen.\u201d\n\u201cIt must be fun to throw chamber-pots out of...", "1_12_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-nine p.m.</span> Desert sunlight slanted into the room from the windows muddily lustrous with the brown film of the glass. Redwine\u2019s shirt was now wet with sweat. His cigarette tumbled dryly out of the corner of his mouth and stayed.\nThrough the static of the broadcast a voice said, \"\u2014all of which has realigned the field staff of the Antichrist to dizzying extremes, and thus coming your way right now are two special news commentaries, both provided by staff members who have been transferred to West Coast operations by the night\u2019s emergency. First, a commentary by Linda Fox, who has come west as a result of her penetrating analysis of the scandal\u2019s impact on politics at home. And next, a commentary by Nick Andrews, who has come west as a result of his penetrating analysis of the scandal\u2019s impact on economics at home. Both Fox and Andrews are veterans of the Antichrist news service, and the quality of their adapted insights is certain to be nondetrimental. In thirty seconds, here is Linda Fox.\"\n\"Holy cow.\"\n\"What?\"\n\"Holy cow!\"\nLenore stared at him. \"What in the world are you talking about?\"\n\"Holy cow!\"", "1_6_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span> in the rain at the wheel of our big bingo rig at Fourth and Long-shore in downtown San Diego, and our attorney, clearly out of control, said, \u201cWhat the fuck ... here we are ... let's just go ... to Vegas!\u201d And he stomped on the gas and got us moving. I thought he meant home, back to the pad in Hollywood, but he had other plans: \u201cNo! To Las Vegas, man! We'll leave the Whale here and rip across the desert in that red convertible you just bought, and get there in no time.\u201d I said nothing, being hungup on the Whale for so many obvious reasons. We also had five red bags left, but who needed it now? Not me; I was trying to have a new life and leave all that cheap shit behind me. I was even trying, at this point, to kick coffee and take up opium instead. Andarijuana was out of the question; I refused to smoke that goofball shit with my friends, no matter what. I preferred the whacked intimacy of a martini; the conversation could become serious. The only trouble was that our car had no seat covers, and I couldn't possibly go naked on that industrial uphol...", "2_17_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nIt was ten o\u2019clock at night. Guglieim\u2019s, on San Miguel Avenue, was crowded.\nOld Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart got up from his seat at the archer-player, and he and Salmonell treated the crowd to a rousing march. There was Salmonell on the big archer, and Wolfgang, in a chair beside the archer, taking it from the bass, and the crowd on the march, singing.\nOld Salmonell was no punk.\nOld Salmonell had had a good day at the races. He was happy.\nOld Salmonell and Wolfgang signed off with a gigue, and the crowd applauded like mad. Old Salmonell bowed and beamed on the crowd, and his gold head bobbed, and his diamond eyeballs whirled.\nOld Salmonell and Wolfgang put their chairs up on the dais, and the crowd came surging up to them, to buy more drinks.\nA loudmouth in the crowd demanded that Salmonell and Wolfgang play another march.\n\"Play a march?\" said old Salmonell.\n\"Play a march?\" said old Wolfgang.", "0_17_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five forty in the afternoon</span> of February 1, 1914, when this story of the Beautiful People begins\u2014and, it may as well be stated at the outset, it takes a swanky party like the one M talent scouts were holding in their New York offices to discover what the Beautiful People are.\nThirty-two young neatly dressed men crowded Dunninger\u2019s reception room that February afternoon. Twenty-one of them had gray, chiseled, beefy faces and wore soup-bowl tans, and they crammed, shoulder to shoulder, around the door leading into Dunninger\u2019s office, where the audition was to take place. Just to see that office, you\u2019d have understood, just from looking in there, what this contest was all about, and why it had excited such keen interest in the whole nation. For it was a marvelous room. It had a high, steep, gabled ceiling set with smoky beams, in woodsy sections hand-painted in blues, greens, brown. In a great fireplace, logs burned and snapped. Below the fireplace was a pile of chewed blankets and briar root, logs, and a bearskin. On top of the great bearskin, stark naked, tanned, ripped twenty ways from Sunday, was...", "2_2_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n/c/\nAugust 23, 1991\n... Patrice, Patrice, Patrice. What are we doing to each other. Where are you. The essential difference that divides one person from another is not a difference of ideas or religion or ideas. The essential difference between one human being and another is that one human being can suffer what the other does to him; for ideas do not matter in the emotional context of what happens between two people. Time is liquid. The past is drowned. When two people meet, it is impossible for them to know what sort of difference, what divides them. At that point vulnerability begins. That is the essential difference. It is the only difference. I do not know what happens to you in the dark areas of your life. That part is not public. But I can suffer, I can be hurt, I can be killed. Joe, Sue, John, Mary. The names of the illusions with which rationality is controllably motivated.\nThe time was nine minutes to three o\u2018clock in the morning.\n/d/\nAugust 25, 1991", "0_13_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one twenty-nine in the afternoon</span>, Martian Standard Mean Time. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, who had founded the Church of God the Utterly Indie, had already materialized four hundred and fifty miles above the surface of Mars, in the trough of a resonant chamber in space that made a cruel parody of the twelfth dimension. In three minutes, he would materialize on the steps of the post-concert building that was the hub of the capital city. That building was called the House of Miracles.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was desperately ill.\nHe would not have chosen to materialize at that time or place, but the messages blaring from his fuzzy-minded contrivance for receiving contralateral radio waves were so urgent that he obeyed without considering the source or nature of the messages. He obeyed out of a reflex conditioned by twenty-three years of experiments in foiling every attempt made by civilizing forces to make him less than absolutely master of his actions.\nThe strongest messages he was getting came from $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, his wife\u2014who had been sent three hundred and thirty million miles away, to Titan. Titan was the largest satellite of the planet Saturn.\nAnd $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 had presumably never had the slightest intention of...", "0_22_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-seven in the evening</span>. The boy fresh from Indiana got up and stretched. He said he had to get into New York; he wanted to go to Greenwich Village and see the big celebration. I thought it was on West Twelfth Street; I slipped him five dollars and he vanished into the night.\nOn the subway I held my breath, in spite of the heat; I was penned in. About halfway to the city I managed to grab a pole and hold on, and I closed my eyes and went to sleep in the sway and crash of the car rumbling through Flatbush. I opened my eyes groggily in Columbus Circle; I remembered getting off with hundreds of others to transfer to the downtown local, and here I was in the men's room of the Pennsylvania Station, and it was really moving ahead like a Chicago El gone mad. I was alone in a great sea of urinals besieging men in a heaventide. It was like that. Everybody was swept on by the current; here a hand, there a foot, black shoes, white ankles, and a floating jump that saved a man from falling. Everyone was sleepwalking on the long, mad, dangerous escalator that ended in the Broadway local out to Newark . . . and bag, bag, bag,...", "2_18_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-two in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nThe scene was a cave in the side of a hill that looked out on the Bay of Bengal. The sky was yellow-white, the sun bluish-white. $SURNAME1, in work clothes, was eating rice and beans with chopsticks. The chopsticks made clonk-clonk sounds in his mouth.\nThe companion of $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 in the cave was young, perhaps eleven years old. The boy ignored the painted iron slab of worldly advise. He watched, over $SURNAME1\u2019s shoulder, something going on miles out at sea.\nUnformulated back in Newport, the question $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 posed for the telegraph operator was designed to put the telegraph operator in a quandary. This was a pleasure for $SURNAME0, since the quandary he was about to impose had been imposed on him eleven years earlier.\nHe hadn\u2019t liked the particular quandary then, at all.", "0_15_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and the place was a hospital in Indianapolis, Indiana, Earth, Solar System, Milky Way.\nThe one who had not been able to conceal his sense of wonder at the wonder of it all was old Rawlie M. Farnsworth, retired space freighter skipper and amateur philatelist, aged eighty-nine.\nEver since 1952, when his retired professional cares had seemed to be spinning out in the most monotonous celestial orbit, Rawlie Farnsworth had taken refuge in postage stamps.\nIn 1952, his son had married a widow, and her two children, half-sisters to his son\u2019s child, were ages thirteen and ten. The two girls had been brought up with a refined form of sexism, and both were bachelors hunters.\nThe younger half-sister began showing an interest in Rawlie, Sr. She was struck by the fact that he collected postage stamps that had pictures of famous strong men on them.\nShe informed Rawlie that she had been named for a famous strong man, Eugenia Brackett. She said that as soon as her Daddy and her fianc\u00e9 let her, she was going to start collecting pictures of old strong-man friends of hers.\n\"Strength!\" said old...", "1_6_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six forty-nine in the morning</span> when I reached Sacramento. I started to ring up Beverly, but hung up the phone. I just wasn't in any mood to be nice to anybody, least of all $FIRSTNAMEB3's girls. I spent an hour fooling around in the bathroom of a gas station and then hit the road. At her home I kissed Betsy on the couch in the living room, among the children of Wakefield, and rushed back to the car for a final explosion toward Frisco. It was a dirty morning. I made up my mind to leave $FIRSTNAMEB3 alone. He was so obviously out of his mind now that he could only complicate things by trying to mooch a ride from me, as he had in Chicago. I would have to pick up my family and get started for the Coast as soon as possible. I dreaded it: a huge load of dreariness and several days. I gritted my teeth for the ordeal. I had money for the gas tank and slept most of the way. It was a four-hundred-mile trip. I did it in six hours, changing my shirt twice because it was so damp. $FIRSTNAMEF4's mother was furious at my arrival. \u201cBut darling,\u201d I told her, \u201cmy angel, I've...", "1_21_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine o'clock in the evening</span> and we were sitting on the terrace of the Grand Hotel, smoking cigarettes and trying each other out on various subjects. Up above, shouldering his way through the crowd on the square, came Pradelle,\" followed by a young Basque in plain-clothes and carrying a riot-helmet under his arm. He bowed to us, made a gesture of apology, and went in to the Colonel.\n\u2022\u2003\u2003\u2022\u2003\u2003\u2022\nI never knew exactly what the conference was about. It was the conference of the day and a part of the pattern of the fiesta. Perhaps they were working things out for next year. There was a great deal to work out, since this was the last time the festival would be held. The boys who throw the flowers, the girls who dance the flamenco, belong only to one town. To have them at any other time would mean a new crop. Yet all the cities along the coast had one day held their own fiesta in the streets. Since all had passed the word the first town decided to eliminate itself and give the day to the others. Hence next year the crowds would be distributed among Pamplona, San Sebastian, Bayonne, St Jean de Luz, Bi...", "1_3_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter to four o'clock in the morning</span>, of what was going to be a fine tropic day in paradise. We had no trouble getting a cruising taxi to take us to Central America and Rodriguez, who had the mysterious telegraph office address. The road followed the channel of the water. Across it were the moonlit tops of coconut palms, and the roofs of houses. As we crossed the long wooden bridge over the final arm of the sea the driver whooped for joy. \u201cWe're in Central America!\u201d he yelled. \u201cGood!\u201d said Eddie and I. Then he added: \u201cWhoopee! How about that! Whoopee!\u201d He kept this up every hundred yards or so as we went into the town. We pulled up in front of a broken-down New England-style house next door to an old church. Rodriguez was an enormous-featured man in dungarees. \u201cWhere'll I wait?\u201d said the driver, and as Eddie and I got out he yelled, \u201cIt ain't every day I goes to Mexico!\u201d and left us. Two minutes later the five dollars were wired to the Western Union in New Orleans. Then Rodriguez wrote down on a piece of paper, \u201cJohnny Banco...", "2_8_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight eighteen in the morning</span>.\nThere was a powerful church organ suffusing the square with the triumphant blaring music that victors in all Earthling wars, including the War Between the States, always seem to like so much.\n$SURNAME0 and the nineteen other veterans were leaving their storied shelter by the time $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0, and their son $FIRSTNAMEB2 came to the wall surrounding the storied shelter. The party of twenty was going to march with parade spectators for almost a mile before breaking away and taking to the field.\nThe chronometer on $SURNAME1\u2019s wrist showed 8:20.\nThe chronometer on $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0\u2019s wrist showed 8:21.\nThe chronometer on the wrist of $FIRSTNAMEB2, the ten-year-old son, showed 8:22.\nIn accordance with Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s orders, $SURNAME1 and his family were to remain within twenty feet of $SURNAME0 and his party at all times. $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEF0, and $FIRSTNAMEB2 were to walk abreast, as if they were a family of close friends. All three were to look straight ahead at all times, as strangers would.", "2_22_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past ten o'clock in the evening</span> on Tuesday the twenty-second of September.\nThe loudspeakers began broadcasting the \"Marseillaise.\"\nThis was done in order that the crowd could sing it. It was not a clean melody.\nA one-minute warning sounded over the loudspeakers.\nA telegraph-changes operator punched the telegraph-changes board and made it read: \u201cAll operators this board. Instructions all-important post-time material follows one. First run tragic story \u2019Barrage.\u2019 Full account tragic story \u2019Barrage\u2019 death Picozzi gives account fabulous bettecl with La Verne, angle recounting obviously phony steps fact fortunate bettor else never score against presumably crooked Gennaro. What about Guardini? Explosion, fire, all. Operator. Second run news, feature stories prominently featuring Detroit and San Francisco. Run, run, feature prominently featuring Indianapolis, follow hard on tragic story, coincidence aturelle, run favorite Indianapolis driver also died in plane crash. Remind win possible rich, remind win rich certain. Operator. Run!\u201d\nThe lights went out over the telegraph-changes board.", "2_2_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-nine in the morning</span>. It was Sunday night. The rain had stopped. The air was clear. All the lights were off in Allied Command Headquarters. Field Marshal General of the Ardennes Cannons looked out from his quarters into the dark, at the one tiny electric bulb that would forever be shining across the fields. All the lights were off in the Communications shed of Allied Command Headquart ers. The noncoms and soldiers who were supposed to be watching communications equipment dozed in the dark. The lights were off in the barracks of Company B, 203d Infantry Regiment, the barracks of Pierce, Strobwitz, Sanford, Ferlinghetti, Fallensing l, et al. All nineteen soldiers were sleepless in bed. Lonely bunk beds.", "0_5_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-two in the morning</span>. From certain angles the figure of Lee Ann, in the baseball cap, standing by the door, brought to mind a certain type of Vegas showgirl: tall, big-hipped, long-legged; and no one would think, watching her, that she was thirty-two years old and a mother.\nVirtually all of the play had gone to Obstat\u2019s side of the table. Other than a gaggle at the edge of the table in front of Bloemker and a cluster that surrounded an empty seat near the door, with a drawstring bag in the chair and a suit jacket draped over the bag, no one was near Obstat. John no longer played blackjack, and the slots held no attraction for him at all tonight. Now he stood in the very heavy human traffic at Lee Ann\u2019s back, watching her legs and her face. When she had some time, she was his ringer, was she not? He was her white swan. He was a white swan, like a birth defect of amour. But his feelings, his obsession, his brilliance ... well, these things were as pale as John himself. Remarkably pale. Like some ancient tintype revealed in a tableau...", "2_20_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nSomething happened to the shaft.\nThere was a ledge in the shaft, a good ten miles below the surface. On the ledge stood $FIRSTNAMEF1, the kitchen maid, naming her baby.\nThere were two millions of babies being named on the ledge.\nThe ledge was one mile wide.\nThe babies were being named by their mothers and by the officials who were so moved by the historic scene.\nThere were two million mothers on the ledge, too. They all were holding their babies over the ledge.\nThe names the mothers and officials called were predetermined. The mothers and officials worked according to a bulletin. They were like puppets, though their diodes kept them wondering whether or not they had free will. Sometimes the fore-knowing circuits would permit the circuits of their spouses to influence their decisions. Sometimes their decisions affected the circuit patterns, and it seemed to the officials and wives as though the course of history had been altered by human volition.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1's baby wasn't a puppet. And his mother was as puppetless as he.", "2_8_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-six in the morning</span> when $SURNAME0 flashed on top of a small grassy rise, between widely-spaced pines. Between the pines were stone spheres as tall as a man. Some were as large as a two-storey house. They were concrete, as were all of $SURNAME0\u2019s structures on Mars.\nThese structures were three in number\u2014a library, a machine shop, and a monastery. All three were in sight of where $SURNAME0 appeared.\n$SURNAME0 wore a waterproof khaki suit in which he had tramped more than seventy thousand miles on Earth. His alpine buckskin shoes were as rotten as old strings, and endured only because of the cobbled soles. The same could be said of his Burberry tricorn hat.\n$SURNAME0 was exhausted, but did not know it. The sources of his strength were his ignorance of his own ignorance, his belief that he had all the time there was, and his childlike eagerness to see what would happen next.", "2_22_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-two in the evening</span>.\nThe Space Wanderer was in the mood for celebrating the rhythm.\nHis arms were around his wife, his palms lovingly cupped on the smooth, solid roundnesses of her marvelous derri\u00e8re. The buttocks in his palms represented his own highest idea of perfection\u2014a modest man\u2019s ideal of perfection, the only kind Earthlings can manage. They were in the order of a fine Brabant, a prize-winning Berkshire, a rugged Milburn. They were grand.\n\"Oh, my love,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"how good it is to make love in the outdoors again, after all these horrible months indoors.\"\n\"Oh, my love,\" said the Space Wanderer, \"the outdoors again, after all these horrible months indoors.\" And he punctuated the statement with a loud kiss on his wife\u2019s derri\u00e8re. He was playing the game of his life\u2014the game of pretending that he was making love to his wife for the first time, pretending that the mysteries and delights of mating were all new to him.", "2_19_40": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven forty in the evening</span>. The place was the living room of the QNI penthouse. Nothing had changed.\nExcept that $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog had disappeared like a soap bubble.\n\"Perhaps,\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0 from Mars, addressing her statement to the air, \"perhaps you have had enough of a good thing. Mr. $SURNAME0?\"\nThe only response came from the music of The Martians Come Back.\n\"Perhaps you had better skip, Mr. $FIRSTNAMEB2,\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2, the bearded, blue-skinned Pan orbiting around Mars, looked uneasy. \"Skip?\" he said.\n\"Skip!\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 disappeared like a soap bubble.\nIt was the voice of Huey, the black freckled Mercuryian messenger boy, that Mrs. $SURNAME0 now heard. No words were discernible. The words were whines, squeaks, pulsations of bone-curdling bass.\n\"You again?\" said Mrs. $SURNAME0, although the voice had already ceased to exist.\nThe voice said, \"Woof.\"", "0_2_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to three o'clock in the morning</span>.\n\"It isn't fair,\" said $SURNAME1. \"No campaign should begin this way. We\u2019re being attacked before we can even get warmed up. The campaign hasn\u2019t even begun, and already it\u2019s a rout. Are you ready to concede, yet?\"\nA strangled laugh slipped out of Salo\u2019s speakers. \"Concede?\" he said.\n\"How can we win, anyway?\" said $SURNAME1, \"with our real platform?\"\n\"Real platform?\" said Salo.\n\"This isn\u2019t the real ship, either,\" said $SURNAME1. \"That real ship is back on Earth, being overhauled. This is just a flying bomb.\"\n\"Bomb?\" whispered Salo.\n\"We\u2019re carrying the real War and Peace, of course,\" said $SURNAME1. \"It just isn\u2019t us.\"\n\"Us?\" whispered Salo.\n\"Who is us?\" whispered $SURNAME1, beginning to shiver.\n\"Quiet!\" whispered Salo. \"Listen!\"\nA new thunder had arisen in the universe. Things were far worse than they had been a minute ago. A ship was diving toward them, flying toward them. It was a big ship, and it...", "1_19_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven forty-seven in the evening</span> when the state trooper stopped them outside Barstow on U.S. Interstate 15 for driving 85 in a 55-mph zone. The problem was that their car was a Series 70 Chevy Malibu alleged to have been made in 1952, and the trooper was suspicious. All documents provided were convincing, except a California registration in the name of O. Tittle, which they had forged themselves. The trooper kept the documents, laid out an accurate version of the law on the hood of the car, then reached high into the interior and popped the trunk. Unfastening a Transtate Batteries box cover, he flipped off the lid and rooted through a jumble of stuff till he seized upon a huge cylinder of blue LeRoy Neiman watercolor paint.\nHe turned toward the man at the wheel, who was young and wearing pants a lot like his own.\n\"Was paint your business in L.A.?\" he asked.\n\"I don\u2019t see how ...\" said the man at the wheel.\n\"I think that\u2019s his business.\" The trooper poked at the blue paint with his fingernail. \"My unit reflects ultraviolet. Want to try a little LeRoy Neiman on that?\"\n\"This is ridiculous,\"...", "0_21_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-one in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, with eight other recruits, had been drinking a distillation of fermented beet juice. Their officers, in staging the riot, had made sure that each recruit had two full flasks when going into the barracks. At half-hour intervals, all night, the recruits had been drinking the contents of one flask and then quietly screwing on the other.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" said the harmoniums, \"$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and\u2014\"\nAt forty-three seconds after nine thirty-one, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 went out like lights.\nThe stripper went off, too.\nThe harmoniums didn\u2019t stop singing. They couldn\u2019t be fooled by falacies that depended upon the chemistry of a specific brain. The harmoniums sang the same song they had sung over the factory in Flint, Michigan. \"$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1,\" they crooned, \"with love. With love.\"\nThey saw the wisdom of keeping the recruits unconscious.\nThey split the command structure in two, isolated the weaker half from...", "2_13_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past one o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Earthling time. The place was the bunkhouse of the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Garwin Ibis Roost, Asin constitution, Dismal Manor, $FIRSTNAMEM1 Garwin planet, Unforums.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 Garwin IV had inherited his estate and his title at the age of three. He had a brilliant education. Until his eighteenth birthday, he wore a diaper.\nThen, a week after his nineteenth birthday, he began his military career. He had now been a soldier for twenty-nine Earthling years. Available records showed that it had killed him.", "2_14_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. Someone or something closed the valve and stopped the steady hissing in the dome.\nThe Nitro Mucker watched the timer, ignored company, and rehearsed his dialogue with Stony Stevenson again. He knew the dialogue by heart. He could have gone through the whole thing backward and in his sleep.\n\"Who in the hell are you?\" Stony was going to ask him when the air returned.\nThe answer was complicated, but ready. It all tore out of the Nitro Mucker, word by word, without thinking. He remembered it all, word for word, without effort. \"I\u2019m Buford Putt\u2014thorian, atheist, kleptomaniac, dipsomaniac, and tramp, I\u2019m with the Stonewall Knights of Johnny Lucifer, and I have news from the star Mizar. I was left behind. It\u2019s up to you. My radio\u2019s all out of whack by now anyway, but maybe you can get through to somebody. Maybe all the radios are out of whack.\u201d\n\"Why, what\u2019s happened?\" said Stony Stevenson.", "2_16_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four forty-one in the afternoon</span>. The place was still Newport, Rhode Island. The temperature was still seventy-two degrees. The crowd was still under control.\nAnd all the while, up and down the East Coast, police and firemen and ordinary citizens fought to stay calm, said to one another, \u201cWell, I guess we\u2019ll just wait and see what happens. I don\u2019t know if it\u2019s anything to fuss about or not.\u201d And muttered uncomfortably, worriedly, behind closed doors, \u201cI just hope I can get to work on time tomorrow.\u201d It was extremely difficult to get any information. At Central Command, located in an underground bunker on MacDougal Street in the West Village of Manhattan, the phones were busy. Rumor had it that so were the wires, when you could get a connection. Rumor said there was a two-month wait for an appointment to speak to an analyst.", "0_14_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past two o'clock in the afternoon</span> and the place was Spain.\nThey stood together on the top of the cliff and looked out toward where the boats had gone.\n\"There go your friends,\" said the guide. \"They have taken the wrong channel.\"\nThe two men seemed to have no opinion on this matter.\n\"They have taken the fishermen's channel,\" the guide went on.\nThe bell buoy was now between the two parties\u2014the two parties seemed so equal always, and now they were so far apart, and the channel looked so wide and so deep.\nThe men on the shore did not seem to be looking at the men in the boats.\nTheir right hands were clasped over the top of the vertical staff that each carried.\n\"Are they always so quiet?\" asked the matador. \"When does the bull fight start?\"\nThe guide was older than the tourist, but he felt that he must give a dignity to his information. \"They will stay out there forty minutes. If they lose the steers in the reeds, they will try to work their way in against the current.\"\n\"Couldn\u2019t we start now?\" the matador asked.\n\"Not for a half-hour.\"\n\"DID you get those one-...", "1_15_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-nine in the afternoon</span>. I bathed and dressed, thinking all the while. The waiter brought up my coffee, and I paid the bill. I started to walk over to Callahan\u2019s Place, to see what was happening there. As I passed the Red Rooster I saw a notice on the door that the races would start in fifteen minutes.\nI had never seen the track at Aintree. Suddenly it seemed terribly important for me to make a bet.\nThe crowd around the bar at Callahan\u2019s was twice what it had been the night before. Most of the men looked like Americans, and I could hear a lot of international dialects. Everyone was shouting and laughing.\nI looked behind the bar for Callahan, and he waved to me. He seemed to be shouting, but I couldn\u2019t hear a word he said. I stepped up to the bar and leaned toward him, cupping an ear.\n\u201cGibby\u2019s got the bar,\u2019he shouted. \u201cIt\u2019s crazy as a bedlam in here.\u201d\nThe rumble of the crowd was like the thunder of a distant express train. The actual noise was coming from the back of the room, where a...", "1_15_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-eight in the afternoon</span> when I once more pushed my Shark down the boulevard of Las Vegas and saw it squat, plain and chrome, in the parking lot of the Flamingo Hotel. I made one circuit of the lot and then parked and got out and walked over to the House of Blue Lights, where I saw a crowd of people at the craps tables, while Cecil stood whitely behind the Money Wheel, stacking chips with his tongs and slipping them, clink, over to the croupiers in exchange for paper chips of various colors.\n* $FIRSTNAMEB3 and $FIRSTNAMEF4, in the blue sedan, were somewhere to the East but I knew nothing about them. I could see them in my mind's eye, though. They were migrating across America like a pair of strange birds in a magic blind, scanning the skies for an empty factory to settle in. They were as secure and free as though they had a mansion and an indolent servant in every city across the land. Somewhere, I knew, in this amazing country of ours, $FIRSTNAMEB3 had already gone through seven or eight cars; certainly he was making the most of his Army basic-training pay of ninety dollars a month. I knew he was bound for California sometime around Thanksgiving since his father was living with our grandmother...", "2_11_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven nineteen in the morning</span> in New York.\nBeeber flopped on his bed. The bed had no mattress. It had just springs and straps. There was a cover, too.\nIt took Beeber seven seconds to pull the cover over him in his bed. He pounded his head into the pillow that was shaped like a skull. He pulled the two skull-pillows over his ears. The sound of the rain on the apartment was dulled.\nThe apartment faced south. So did the terrace, eight stories below. It was sixty feet straight down from the edge of the terrace to the street.\nBeeber could see the sixty-foot drop from his bed.\nA black bird as big as a Spanish bull was perched on the rim of the terrace, looking down at the sixty-foot drop. It was pouring rain. The black bird did not seem at all worried about getting wet.\nThere was a knock on Beeber\u2019s door.\nBeeber didn\u2019t answer it. He pulled the two skull-shaped pillows down around his ears. He pounded his head on the polyurethane skull at the top of his bed.\nThe knock came again, louder.", "1_14_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two seventeen in the afternoon</span> and I didn\u2019t know what I was doing. I was walking across the kitchen in my stocking feet. The coffee was on and I was reading the News . The News is depressing in other meanings of the term. I had finished the news and the Classified, and it was the Comics I was looking into, when the door-bell rang.\nI opened the door.\nMy blood turned to water.\nTwo enormously tall men, with broken noses, stood in the hall. I did not know who they were, but they had come for somebody, and that somebody was me.\nThey stood there in big, heavy-soled shoes, big, yellow, shiny cheese-faces, craggy foreheads, the scars of their victims, bigger men than I had ever thought existed in the entire world. They looked as though they had played football in a porkpacking house.\nThey did not ask for me. They knew all about me. They knew my cousin Ira. They knew Nig. Nig I knew. Nig was in Kansas. Nig would come out later.\nThey were going to take me, somewhere, for something, for something I had written about my cousin Ira a long time ago, about Nig, and about Kansas...", "0_8_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-two in the morning</span> and I sat in the living room with my breakfast gramophone. I was in my bathrobe. I had on bedroom slippers because I was still in charge of the house, even though I had only one house guest and he was asleep in his room. The music I was bringing into the morning was Berlioz\u2019s Damnation of Faust. It was an old record. I played it so much that the grooves were halfway worn away. From the tenor\u2019s battered opening line I wrenched everything I could. Sometimes I falsified the sounds of the orchestra to make them fit the wear in the record, because the original sounds were lost under the wear too.\n\u201cBut here you are,\u201d I said to Georgette, \u201cback in Paris, the city of your birth, and enraptured by it once more.\u201d\n\u201cYes,\u201d she said. \u201cI\u2019m always enraptured. This is a charming old apartment too.\u201d\n\u201cWhat did you expect it to be?\u201d\n\u201cSomething else,\u201d she said. \u201cSomething newer.\u201d\n\u201cI had it built in 1926,\u201d I...", "2_5_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes past five o'clock in the morning</span> and we were about to run amok in the most extravagant hotel in Washington, D.C.\n\u201cTwo ice-cold bears,\u201d I heard my attorney say. Then, for some reason, he muttered, \u201cSyrop and lime.\u201d\nThe clerk looked petrified. \u201cSir,\u201d he said to me, \u201cyou will have to get those credentials sir ...sir... immediately.\u201d He allowed two octaves of fear to enter his voice before he came to rest, his gaze glued to the credentials, refusing to look up.\n\u201cJust chill, man,\u201d said my attorney. \u201cThis is your Nobel Prize moment.\u201d He seemed to be mumbling: \u201cSyrup and lime...five dollars...disemboweled...lime ashtray ...robbery...\u201d\nThe red cap was hovering with his ice and our glasses. After him came the bellman with our bags. The elevator was ready to go up. In struggle to control our conversation, the bellman shouted out a question to my attorney: \u201cWhat floor, sir?\"\n\u201cDe Prince,\u201d my attorney said.", "0_3_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-seven in the morning</span> and I'd been up three nights in a row on marijuana\u2014a thing I'd never done before. The girl was on mescaline and had also run straight through three nights. We were sitting there with the lights turned out when we saw a \u201cthing\u201d in the sky, making patterns\u2014circles, straight lines, V's, approaching the building and then suddenly making 30\u2014degree angles in all directions and moving away very rapidly. It had done five laps around the skyscraper in less than twenty-five seconds . . .\nWe finally decided it was an illusion\u2014or something that people hallucinated on drugs. Probably the one I\u2019d seen three thousand miles away across the desert. But five minutes later, there it was again. Apparently had been waiting for us to turn the lights out. It was right overhead, circling the building at least once a minute, moving very fast and oddly but emitting no sound we could hear (the window was open and we were smoking marijuana every fifteen minutes).\nThe girl rushed over and started throwing on her clothes. \u201cLet's get out of here!\u201d she hissed. \u201cThat thing's from outer space!\u201d We ran down seventeen flights of stairs and fled across town...", "1_6_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to seven o'clock in the morning</span>. God was still holding her arm. She looked down desperately. His feet were just behind hers. They were following a ladder that hung from the side of the ship. God's feet were a yard below hers. They climbed higher and higher, following that ladder up and up beside the ship. How high were they climbing? She dared not look. Suppose she looked and found nothing beneath her but breakers and sky?\nWhere was the level of the sea? she asked herself. There was no sea beneath her, yet how far down could one look through the steel plates of the ship? For she had not found the sea again yet. The sound of the water on the outside of the ship was quite close; it hummed on the plates. How much water was there out there? Ten feet deep? Twenty feet deep? Suppose the ladder stopped half-way up the ship and only her feet hung down? Suppose she had to climb up there for hours-for days and weeks-until her hands cracked and the soles of her feet fell away into the sea, until she could climb no more-and then she fell? There was nothing beneath her but the sun and the sky. She looked up to see how high they had climbed. Her head swam.", "0_2_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>, according to the chronometer of the Welin greeter station on the Grand Trunk Road.\n(The Welin Industries Greeters are the lovely young ladies who meet every Transtatransit rocket that lands on Mars, Mercury, and Venus.)\nIt was fourteen minutes past two by the time the time-diversified group of one hundred and fifty people had purchased tickets from the Mannie antiques teller, had boarded a bus, and had driven out of the legendary veils of Earth\u2019s atmosphere and into the light gravity and clear air of the stratosphere.\nThis part of the trip, too, was moving in slow motion.\nThe infrared-like heat of the Martian morning was, despite foggy hazes, formidable.\nOn a bare lavender plain a thousand yards from the bus-and-rocket combination was a battery of huge spotlights, with lifeguards, that illuminated bathing beauties who basked harmlessly in light that would have boiled lizards or baked steaks. Fire guards wearing infrared glasses walked among the models, seeing that no brushfire got started by moist bodies or got too far out of hand. The bathing beauties wore no bathing suits. Their bodies were eye-scorching pastels...", "0_15_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three thirty-eight in the afternoon</span>. The speaker, clearly, was the Space Wanderer himself, masquerading as the late Wilbur Dobbis, on Earth for the first time since his childhood. He would call, Matey informed him, every day at this time. Matey sent the Space Wanderer off to deliver his broadcast\u2014down the corridor, and past the crude little cardboard theatre in which two Company men were putting on a free show for five or six $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 slaves.\nMatey, a communications expert, got on the phone, again, and located a particular vibration. That vibration stopped a piece of equipment hundreds of miles away\u2014in Wilbur Dobbis\u2019 Cadillac, now driven by the Space Wanderer, who was heading back to Newport and the transfer ship that would take him out of the Earth\u2019s vicinity forever. The equipment stopped in the Cadillac had the effect of making Wilbur Dobbis\u2019 corpse sit up and gurgle.\nDobbis, staring into the sun forever, now had himself a new and, presumably, better head. He was the head of the $SURNAME0 estate. He was the head of everything.\nHe was the head, all by himself, God bless him.\nAFTER...", "1_2_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes until three o'clock in the morning</span>, according to the dashboard clock of $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s space ship. The dashboard clock was now the only thing in the ship that was operating.\nWhile $FIRSTNAMEB0 was dashing from his ship to the tunnel, the ship\u2019s warning system had momentarily remarked to $FIRSTNAMEB0:\n\"Warning!\n\"Incontinent fools and vicious brutes on coming floor.\"\nAfter $FIRSTNAMEB0 left, the warning system melted down.\nThe ship now lay dead, black, vulnerable to anyone with a bent for burglary.\nThe only item now functioning inside the ship was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s mate-seeking weapon. It was functioning incessantly, like the hard-on of a lovesick teenager.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1, hurrying toward $FIRSTNAMEB0, was not aware that $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s weapon was searching him out with love. He imagined it to be seeking him out with murderous hate. He imagined he had been spotted and identified at last as a sicko brute and incontinent fool. Good God, he thought, what a vicious fool I am\u2014to have done such things!\nHe imagined that his crime had now been written up, bulletin-boarded, and teletyped to every civilized outpost on Mars. He imagined the things he had...", "1_2_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two thirty-three in the morning</span>. There was no moon. The night was black as cypress. The station wagon was a long, flat, black shadow cut loose from the night. I left the engine running to keep the baby warm.\nThe Watters family was inside, breathing heavily. I told them what the score was. They were agreeable. They were glad to get out.\n\u201cIt\u2019s worse than a jail,\u201d said old man Watters. \u201cThey ought to make people like you pay for this, some way.\u201d\n\u201cNever mind,\u201d I said.\n\u201cWell -- anyway -- thank God it\u2019s over,\u201d said Mrs. Watters.\n\u201cAw, it ain\u2019t nothing,\u201d her husband said.\nI carried the suitcases and back-packs down to the car. I put them in with care. I don\u2019t believe the car had ever carried such valuable effects.\nThey always over packed to save luggage-moving expenses at the other end. Now the back-pack was crammed with their most precious belongings in the world. It contained five hairs from the chin of the family dog, who had died a few months before. Five hairs in a tiny...", "1_1_58": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes to two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nJake sat up. He had been lying on the sand, hunched over, his arms clasping his legs, his forehead dug into his knees.\n\"I'm going to sleep now,\" he said. He spoke in a flat, toneless voice.\n\"I guess you better sleep,\" I said.\n\"It's still raining,\" he said.\n\"Yes.\"\n\"I'm going to sleep now,\" he said.\nI watched the back of his head. It did not move. I moved my blankets close against his, slipped my hand under the blanket, and touched his arm and hand. They were warm and moist. His fingers gripped mine. He was sleeping.\nI dozed. Once I thought I felt the body beside me stir and move. Once I woke to see the rain falling into the lake from the edge of the cloud. It was lighter now, with gray light. I went to sleep again.\nWhen I woke the sun was shining on the hillside above us. I touched Jake's hand. It was cold.\nCHAPTER\nI brought the raft in against the steep bank and climbed up. I knocked the ashes out of the fire, took the cooking-pots to the stream, and washed...", "1_6_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>, and I looked at my watch. Then I saw somebody moving on the North Las Vegas Boulevard, a block away. He was coming toward me, and it was too dark to make him out. He was carrying something bulkily under one arm. It was too dark to see his features. He came closer. I saw his face. It was Robert Cohn. He shivered a little as he walked.\n\u201cHello, Jake,\u201d he said.\n\u201cHello, Robert. Want to come up and have some breakfast?\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s awfully early, isn\u2019t it? Don\u2019t you ever sleep?\u201d\n\u201cCome on up. I\u2019ll give you some coffee.\u201d\nHe came up and sat on the bed while I made the coffee. We were both sitting on the bed and I handed him the cup and had a sip out of the pot.\n\u201cAren\u2019t you tired?\u201d he asked.\n\u201cNot a bit.\u201d\n\u201cWhat did you do all day?\u201d\n\u201cTook a walk down to the race-track and back.\u201d\n\u201cWhere did you go to supper...", "0_14_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two forty-one in the afternoon</span>. There had been dust in her throat for too many days. Much of the dust had been stirred up that morning by four people packing the flats of all three Trum-bulls on the second floor of one building. In one of the flats was a yellow cottage piano that made dust like a feather duster. The dust was stirred up, too, by the movers. The piano belonged to $SURNAME1\u2019s mother. The movers were working for five dollars, which was all that Mrs. $SURNAME0 could afford.\nThe house was going to be torn down by the company which owned it. It was a white, wooden mansion, with a mansard roof, which had been built in the heyday of American house-building, when good and expensive houses were as plentiful as good and expensive bread in France. It looked as if it had been dropped from a dirigible.\nThe front door of theRumfoords\u2019 mansion was iron, with beautiful carvings by a coachmaker. The iron door, although it was three inches thick, could have been taken off its hinges with an eighteen-inch bar, because the hinges were so pitifully designed.\nThe mansion faced an asphalt square. Beneath the square was a huge rectangular...", "2_10_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten sixteen in the morning</span>. Greenwich mean time.\nUp the ladder went the Space Wanderer, puffing and panting, but avid, careful, and joyful. He was avid for information, careful not to break the rules, joyful that Earthlings had such beautiful machinery and that he was able to figure out the machinery so easily.\nHe reached the top rung of the ladder, stepped off into a dark elevator. He stood there, panting, rubbing his hands over the only controls he had been given to operate\u2014the controls that governed the doors of the elevator.\nThere were two buttons. The Space Wanderer pushed the one on the right. The doors closed, and the elevator began to rise.\nIt was quite slow.\nThe light in the elevator was trial-size and was in a recessed bracket in a wall panel.\nThe light revealed that the elevator was so shallow that a man couldn\u2019t stand erect in it. The elevator was so narrow that the Space Wanderer had to turn sideways to press the button on the right.\nIt also revealed that the elevator walls were of hand-tooled, hand-painted burgundy leather.", "1_23_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven forty-seven in the evening</span>. I closed my eyes happily, feeling safe in the Idiot\u2019s Room. Here I would rest and fool around with secret thoughts, protected by the secret panel, watching the pink neon circle glowing on the ceiling above the shaker. That circle kept the room from ever becoming truly dark. It was like the sun had set, but it would never really be night. There was no need for a desk lamp. Everything was comfortably bathed in a soft, protective light. I went to sleep, there in my booth, at precisely eleven fifty\u2014nine, and if I dreamed, I dreamed about earthquakes. It was a silent earthquake\u2014in the booth, on the street outside, everywhere. My window shaker was on the floor now. Its white plastic seat\u2014stuffed with blue lead shot and gelatine\u2014was loose, floating. The legs were still attached and they scratched but they were living legs that walked by themselves, creeping out of the iron seat and testing the floor, trying to walk. If I could only walk, I could get up and balance the seat on my shoulders and hop around like a horse, the lead shot jouncing on my back...forty\u2014three, forty\u2014four, forty\u2014five! But what was the use...", "2_18_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six sixteen in the evening</span>, Eastern Standard Time.\nA microphone over the great ballroom doors, just inside, assured that the master of ceremonies at the timed tick could be heard clearly all over the palace. He was Earl Mulch, assistant administrator of the Martian Operating Company.\nThe microphone was not over the doors because Mulch was important, but because, as far as anybody knew, every veteran Martian had a working knowledge of Morse Code. \"Whenever those veterans get together, they rag it out,\" said the man responsible for microphone placement, \"and the guy that stands behind that mike has got to know what they\u2019re saying.\"\nDaves, Trumans, and Moncrieffs were on hand to be hugged by all comers.\nIn the yard outside were two plug-uglies from the committee on sanitation. Their fingernails were six inches long, and were driven into trees, shovels, and low retaining walls all around the yard.\nThe $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 of Hollywood, Virginia had said he would arrive at six thirty.\nAt six twenty-one, a man came through the crowd outside. The band stopped playing. The chimes in the Campanile stopped. Everyone began to boo.", "1_22_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after ten o'clock in the evening</span> when Mike went to the concierge's desk in the hotel. I had been reading Shakespeare in the lobby and naturally I was called in as one competent to advise whether or not the concierge was trying to put something over on Mike.\nWe went up to his room and he unlocked the door and turned on the light, and there was $FIRSTNAMEB5. She sat up in bed, her hair all down, and the cover pulled up to her chin, and scowled at us. Then she saw it was only Mike, and she smiled.\n\"Why, hullo, Michael. Isn't it grand to see you?\"\nMike was standing in the door, sort of awkward, and then he came in and sat down on the bed and took her hand. \"Well, darling,\" he said. \"I suppose you know why I'm here. And it's no good pretending. It's all over, old thing.\"\n\"I don't know what you mean,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB5. \"Of course it isn't.\"\n\"It is,\" Mike said. \"Someday I'm going to write a complete history of how you and I broke up. It will make fine reading. It deals with passion, jealousy, expatriation, $FIRSTNAMEB5 and...", "1_17_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes after five o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchil was still Prime Minister of Great Britain. Klaus Heinzerling was dead.\nSPECIAL BULLETIN\nUNITED PRESS ASSOCIATION\n4:05 P.M., WEDNESDAY, NOVEMBER 22, 1962\nHANGED IN PORTUGAL TODAY\u2014KLAUS HEINZERLING, 31, EX-GERMAN CRYPTOGRAPHER, TRACED TO A SHACK IN THE ALGARROBAL HILLS WHERE HE HAD LIVED WITH A PORTUGUESE GIRL AND HER SON SINCE HIS MYSTERIOUS DISAPPEARANCE LAST FALL. HE WAS ALONE. HE TRIED TO KILL HIMSELF BY DRAWING A PISTOL WHICH TURNED OUT BE LOADED ON A CIVIL SERVANT AND A HUNTER WHILE THEY QUESTIONED HIM. HE KILLED HIMSELF WITH A SWORD. WHEN HE FELL HE WAS HANGED BY A STRING TRIED THROUGH THE ARM OF A WINDOW. IN HIS POCKET WAS FOUND A LETTER TO HIS MOTHER IN BERLIN, GERMANY. DATED...", "2_2_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two twenty-one in the morning</span>.\nIt was only Americans who found the dropped ukulele so hilariously funny. There were a lot of Americans in keynpersonnel slots in the rifle companies of the 436th and the 28th, because the war was to be fought in the eventful region that lay between the Great Lakes and the Rocky Mountains. The 28th Division was all Ohio National Guard, the 436th mostly Michigan, with a fair representation of Wyoming, Montana, Utah, and Idaho squads. There was no West Point or ROTC tradition in either division. Their commanders were mostly Cornell men, not West Pointers. The ranks were filled out mostly by first-term inductees\u2014Park Avenue lawyers and Harvard footbal players under twenty-one.\nBut what could you do? The Army was legally bound to use draftees for cannon fodder\u2014draftees plus a sprinkling of Harvard men for leadership.\nThe Army, in short, was a victim of its own system, as always.\nIt was left to the men in the ranks to be eternally disgruntled.", "1_15_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. During the next few seconds, Redwine suffered the Supreme Agony of his life. It began with a sweet thrill all through him. As Rosebush had promised, the colors in his eyes grew brilliant. He felt tremendously vigorous. At the same time, all the painful contradictions of his Promise, and of Earth-life in general, were simply blown away. He forgot his troubles, forgot himself\u2014\nHe caught himself. No! he told himself. You have been tricked again, Bliss Wiliamson Redwine!\n\"The colors!\" he said aloud, trying to hang onto the glorious series of rainbow glows he had just seen. \"The colors show the truth!\"\nHe did not hang onto the colors. They blew away, leaving him with a headache.\nIt was then that the headache became the Supreme Agony. Redwine felt trapped. He had been tricked again into losing a friend, an interest, a system of symbols with which he could get along.\n\"I\u2019ll find you!\" he cried at Martin Farina, straining his eyes to see the tiny figure far below on Clayton Street. \"I\u2019ll find you, if I have to tear down the walls of Jericho brick by brick!\"\nThank heaven that it...", "0_8_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one minute to nine o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was buttoned into the damned yellow suit, and he was standing correctly in front of his badge in the wall, and his rappel lines were braided on the long yellow extensors slung between his arms, and the safety pins of the safety clips were out. He was waiting for the music that would mean another space ships discharge.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heart was jumpy. He had been awake since a quarter of five that morning. Since then, he had had nothing to do. His sleep and wakefulness since the accident had been determined by the wall clock\u2019s pulse. He had been warned not to tamper with the rhythm set by the clock, not to take sleeping pills \u2014 and definitely not to tamper with the rhythm set by the clock.\nNow, the unfairness of his situation was driving $FIRSTNAMEB0 frantic. His mind jumped around helter-skelter. He thought about how his mate and child had been taken from him. He rehearsed his dull duties at the wall. His imagination projected him through the wall into all sorts of desirable situations and places outside it. No wonder he was dizzy. He was being asked to wield a space ship and a keen, keen appetite and nothing but a wall...", "1_16_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-nine in the afternoon</span> when we arrived in New York and went to buy guns. As soon as I got to New York I wired $FIRSTNAMEB3: \u201cSafe gounds [sic] New York see you tonight.\u201d Around my hotel in Times Square I found dozens of jazz musicians hanging around that curious web of alleys, Forty-seventh and Broadway, wait- ing to pick up the musicians who come to the big city. DIXIE was at the Deefgeet [ sic ] Ball and the musicians wandered around there till midnight. After that they hung around a sinister milk-and-cheese bar called the Half and Half at Forty-seventh and Broadway. They stood in the doorways of foul tenements and blew mouth-organ songs, they slapped banjos, they talked to taxi girls, they strutted around, they begged, they cursed, they talked. One of them said, \u201cOh, man, this is the life! I can't wait to California!\u201d and the glamorous cloud blew over him and made him darkly, mysteriously silent, mentioning something in his past that troubled or shamed him; I heard the same words everywhere across the shadowy land, from North Carolina to New Orleans, \u201cI've done time in the...", "0_2_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes past two o'clock in the morning</span>. A dark night with no wind. Preceded by old Salmo, who put out the light, they crept out and closed the door as softly as they could, Fonzo following, who had insisted on bringing his rod. They dispersed along the edge of the lake and crept to the murky clumps of alder on the bank. In the hazel clump was Salmo, in the pine clump Lang, in the oak clump Fonzo, and across the lake trying to fish was the chief constable. The night was now really dark and the stars above piercing. There was silence in the world. The reeds along the bank were motionless. The black water, held in suspension between the two hills of land, trembled to a footfall in the hills themselves. That was all, that and the sounds of a light wind in the hill pines across the lake, and a sound that rose and fell, rose and fell, that no one could figure out. They never did figure it out, whether it was a fish jumping, or a loon passing, or the water itself lapping softly on the shore. There was nothing for anybody to do but fish. Only then, and for ever after, would the men be...", "2_17_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>.\nThe rain was torrential.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s suffering wife taught her little son to skate on Newport Bay at exactly four o\u2019clock the next morning. The time was four o\u2019clock the next morning.\nThe rain was a drizzle.\nThe drizzle continued over the next three days.\nOn the night of the fourth day, the Drizzle Nose Teddy Bears gave a concert in Newport, and $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his wife and little Stony went to the concert.\nThe twelve little people of the band were terrific.\nAt the end of the concert, their conductor came out alone, and he invited their parents to come up on the stage to meet their children.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s heart thumped mightily when he heard this invitation. The conductor said the magic word up: his name was $FIRSTNAMEB1.\nIn the dark scramble for seats, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and his family found front-row seats.\nNothing could stop $FIRSTNAMEB0 now\u2014not even the presence of a minister on the stage. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had had his doubts about the legality of his marriage, but he decided to push on regardless. The little man from Mars had tied the knot\u2014that was enough for $FIRSTNAMEB0.", "0_18_14": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was fourteen minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span> when Tiffany said this. The bathroom was almost dark. The boardinghouse was silent.\nTiffany had her false teeth in, the purse with the five thousand dollars was in the bathroom, the window was securely locked, the door was securely locked, the only light was the small pink glow that came from Tiffany\u2019s gum at the left side of her mouth.\nIn the gloom, she did not look as though she had just been crying. Her hair was in green curlers. The skin of her face was gray under the gray light. There was no expression on her face, save for a fixed determination to keep her teeth clamped so as not to reveal she had been crying.\nThe events leading up to this evening had been extremely simple.\nIt was a sweet, bright morning, the sun had risen in the east, the grass was high, the girls had had their breakfast in the kitchen, as on all other days. The difference between this morning and other mornings was in their bathroom routine, for that morning over their cornflakes and milk little Nancy had announced that her twin brother, who was in the Navy, had written to tell her that he was engaged to be married. The Navy, so far as little Nancy knew, was stationed...", "0_21_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine forty-three in the evening</span> of a warm spring day, and the unicorn was enjoying itself, tapping with its hoof at trees that interested it as it coasted slowly down Broadway. A perfectly transparent ice-cream cone tinkled quietly in the air next to it.\nPeople stopped, staring, and pointed. Many pretended to be annoyed, but the unicorn could see invisibly inside them, and it knew they were actually amused.\nIt gently tapped the ice-cream cone against the window of the Hotel Metropole, and watched the chocolate splatter on the sill, to the delight of the man and woman inside.\nThe man and woman thought, in inside voices, that the young woman who had just ridden the beautiful white horse down Broadway was undoubtedly a horse-rider from the country, but from a broken home. They thought she was probably also a Communist.\nThe man and woman conceived a child.\nThe unicorn rode on. The invisible rain tapped on its horn.\nConnie Mack, owner of the Space Wanderer, stood behind the screened-in cage of his giant pythons, staring gloomily at them. His wife had gone to bed, his son had run away, his best customers could be heard laughing tearfully in a room down the corridor, and the...", "0_12_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-two p.m.</span> when space ship Earthling at last landed in Cleveland and taxied over to a freight-yard specially leased to the Tralfamadorians. The Earthling pilot, seeing Earthling so casually parked among real airplanes, took off his blue airline cap, held it over his heart. $SURNAME0, in the copilot\u2019s seat next to him, made a continuation of his thumb-and-forefinger, indicating to the pilot that he was high on a clergyman\u2019s list of pretty fair guys.\nThree tall, whirring fans on aluminum stilts in the freight yard just ahead of Earthling came tonelessly to life. The fans sent megabolts of clean golden air round and round the three sides of a square seventy-five yards on a side. By means of the fans and the natural lay of the land, the three walls of the little square were made invisible. The only thing in the little seventy-five-yard square that was not subject to natural invisibility was Earthling herself. And Earthling was as she had been when she set out from Cape Cod, Massachusetts\u2014lustrous black without, a bright, roomy wonder of gadgets and bunks inside.\n\"Home, sweet home,\" said Rumfo...", "2_10_47": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten forty-seven in the morning</span>, Earthling Eastern Standard Time. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s little manual watch stopped. The automatic theodolite kicked in. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had been under the theodolite for eleven seconds. At the end of another two seconds, the theodolite was designed either to register a good voluntary response from $FIRSTNAMEB0 or else to deliver, by means of a conventional mortar, a radio- active pellet on his head.\nThe pellet, stuck fast to $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s melon-sized cranium, would burn through, of course, and expose $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s juicy brain to air. His silly ruined body would drop to the floor at the foot of the stake, which would still be upright.\nThat posture would be struck by the respectful mind as being a cheerful one.\nThe pain of the pellet would, incidentally, serve to sharpen up $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s reactions.\nNobody was sure what the right reactions were. The right reactions were whatever a man did immediately after he got the pellet. It was assumed that the man would show what was in him, whatever that was.", "0_18_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past six o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB2, the caring robot, was soft-singing the mating song of his own people. His eyes were closed, his mechanism purred.\nThere were four potential mates available to him in the room, but he was keeping himself for $FIRSTNAMEF1, for $FIRSTNAMEF0, for $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s beloved.\nAnd $FIRSTNAMEF1, $FIRSTNAMEF0, $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s beloved, sat on a chair very near to him, on one leg of which hung a wet black stocking. She was making twenty-six graduation announcements. The announcements were getting wet, damp being unconditionally the most prevalent condition of things on Titan.\nAnd $FIRSTNAMEF1 was thinking empty thoughts. Because in eight hours, in six-thirty in the morning Earthling time, $FIRSTNAMEF1 was going to die. Thus would end her sufferings.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 was twenty-nine times as old as $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was when he died. Nine tenths of that grim age for her had been spent in abject fear.\nShe was going to die still afraid, still hoping that something crazy would happen before her vaporization.\nHer vaporization was part of the plot of a lousy Godless Universe to get its collective nuts off. Which was $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s explanation of the Universe,...", "1_0_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-two a.m.</span> when steam Key West-bound from Tampa finally pulled out from the sad, deserted Southern Pacific tracks outside Preskey\u2019s, California. I sat alone in the rear coach of the old \u201cTexas\u201d Woodside open, with a briefcase full of mescaline, dexies,Selectric II, bottle of Hunyadi Janos, pint of Wild Turkey and six speedboats of awful Mexican grass; no barrel. I dropped a Roach Motel in the mescaline and wired up a large methedrine binger with plenty of ammo for the Tuning Fork. By one thirty they came on like gangbusters. I was already running at a high massage from the mescaline, but all the Bad Shit Of The World had vanished.\nTo get a future like that one for free would have taken some extremely complex and uncertain investment planning, yet here it was, handed to me on platter, a clear, unmistakable sign that the universe, like a karmic oyster, was ready to give me something magnificent in return for absolutely nothing at all. All I had to do was gulp it down without even opening my mouth.\nToward the end of the second hour that feeling became extremely precise: I put the met...", "0_17_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five sixteen in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0, master of chrono-synclastic infundibula, was in his estate on Mars. He was sixty-nine minutes younger than he had been one minute before.\nThere was a perfectly valid reason why he had been summoned to his estate on Mars. It was a reason of cosmic proportions.\nA fleet of thirty-nine interplanetary space ships was coming to rendezvous with $SURNAME0\u2019s estate. The President of the United States would be aboard one of the ships. The President would be attending the mass wedding of one hundred and twenty-six luckless girls and one perfect young man.\nThe mass wedding and christening of the space ships was set for five thirty-four in the afternoon.\nIt was not a time when $SURNAME0 naturally would have been found in his garden patch. But he had not been in his bedroom, or his study, or the library, or the swimming pool, or the movie theater, or the museums, or the baseball diamond, or the station where cookies, milk, and sandwiches were free. He had been found in his garden patch.\nThere he was, trampling the jungle underfoot, crouched in a khaki suit, one hand twisting...", "0_4_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four sixteen in the morning</span>. The occasion was Thursday, April twenty-third, 1961. The place was the bar of the $SURNAME0 estate in Newport, Rhode Island.\nThe persons were the $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 of $SURNAME0 Quindle & Crandall of Manhattan, New York, New York; $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0\u2019s first marriage; and the author of this chronicle.\nAnd, since chronicles usually concern themselves with the doings of important characters, it may gratify the reader to learn a little about the author of this chronicle. His name is $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1. For some years he turned a quick buck on Wall Street. Now he lives on a government pension, a state police pension, a war-relief pension, and two small annuities. He is sixty years old. He works at nothing.\nOnce, in a publisher\u2019s office, he met Richard Nixon, and he asked Mr. Nixon where he got his style. Mr. Nixon referred him to the dictionary. Having found $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill quoted there as the foremost stylist of our time, Mr. $SURNAME1 studied Churchill, and still studies him, and hopes to imitate him.\nApart from Churchill\u2019s, Mal...", "1_11_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-two in the morning</span> of October 3, 2005, according to the official atomic clock in the office of $SURNAME0\u2019s estate\u2014an office that was part of the tasteless mansion that the old man of $FIRSTNAMEB4 had built for himself down the hill from the palace.\nThe estate, like everything else on the island, was under perpetual mosquito or infra-red bombardment by the presiding $SURNAME0 bureaucracy. $SURNAME0 and his kind were still alert, still powerful enough to enforce their whims. A punctual Mosquito Air Fleet was still scheduled to remind Earthlings of their vulnerability every twenty-four hours.\nThe people of Mercury were still facing the twenty-four-hour deadline that Vladimir Ilich Peechell had imposed so gaily and arbitrarily almost forty-five Earthling years before. Doom or submission were still the only choices that Earthlings had.\nThere was no third choice.\n$SURNAME0 slouched in a canvas chair before his office. He was watching his worthlesscavalry horses wheeze through their second workout of the morning. They were trotting around the rim of a grassless pond on the slope just below him. This exercise track was Lan\u2019s contribution to architectural abomination.\nThree of the hardiest and...", "2_0_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes after midnight</span>.\nI lay awake but it did not seem light. Outside it must be raining. The silence seemed complete and profound. $FIRSTNAMEB5\u2019s head was on my shoulder. It felt good to have her head there. I was sleepy and content. I was just going off when $FIRSTNAMEB5 moved. She raised up, turning toward me.\n\u201cPut out the light and go to sleep, darling.\u201d\nI turned off the wick. A street-car went slowly by, out there in the rain. I felt myself falling asleep. Then suddenly I was awake. The silence seemed deeper.\n\u201cWere you asleep?\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5\u2019s hand was on my shoulder.\n\u201cNo.\u201d\n\u201cListen, Jake.\u201d\nShe was almost whispering. I did not know what she was going to say. I was sleepy.\n\u201cListen, Jake. I can hear your heart.\u201d\nI did not say anything. It did seem strange. I listened to myself. It was quite quiet and I could hear my heart. How I could hear it I did not know.\n\u201cNow it\u2019s stopped.\u201d", "1_18_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nWe were sitting on the terrace of the New York Bar. Upstairs were Bill and Mike and Braddocks. They would stay there until the last moment.\n\u201cI wish I could be watching on the course,\u201d $FIRSTNAMEB5 said. \u201cWhat do they do?\u201d\n\u201cWatch the start,\u201d I said. \u201cWatch the gay spectacle, the Grand Prix of motoring, the Monaco Grand Prix.\u201d\nWe both laughed. It seemed very funny.\nAt seven o\u2019clock I went up-stairs. Bill and Mike and Braddocks were there.\n\u201cWhere\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEB5?\u201d Braddocks asked.\n\u201cShe\u2019s coming up in a minute.\u201d\n\u201cTell her to hurry,\u201d said Mike, \u201cor we won\u2019t have time to bring the car around. We\u2019ll be late as it is.\u201d\n$FIRSTNAMEB5 came in the door. She had on a very simple black dress. It was like silk but it was not silk. She had on a black sweater under the dress. She had no hat. She had left her hair down. She was wearing dark glasses.", "2_21_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nNovember\nVictor Johnson and Florence White grew older. The little nerve jiggles that add up to the passage of time happened in their bodies. They slept, they woke up; odors from strange places got into their noses; they sat, they rubbed their legs. They ate and they drank countless things. They rarely held hands. Victor bought a box of Tums and chewed his way through a quarter of them every night.", "2_20_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight twenty-six in the evening</span>.\nThere was a sound like that of a telephone being toyed with on the other side of a wall. It stopped.\n\"Halo?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"Yes, sweetheart?\" said the pure, Arnold wall- speaker contralto.\n\"When do you leave?\" said $SURNAME0.", "0_10_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-six in the morning</span>. I stood amid full boxes and suitcases in our living room, ready to hit the road. In my pocket were the car keys and a considerable wad of emergency cash supplied by my attorney: \u201cin case of emergency,\u201d he said. I slid the money into my back pocket, picked up two heavy suitcases and heaved them out onto to the sidewalk, ten feet below.\nMy attorney was in the bedroom, adjusting his tie in the mirror. \u201cWell now,\u201d he said, \u201care you ready to waggle your ass across the border?\u201d He was dressed only in white tennis shorts, but he seemed unconcerned about the storm of abuse I\u2019d been delivering to his life these last six months.\nThe frenzy that had overtaken me at the airport was now mounting toward a spectacular apogee. Only half an hour ago, I had tried to kill a man\u2014not fatally\u2014for no reason whatever, except that the beast in me was trying to get out. I had buffed the scrambled eggs from my eyes and I felt fine...ready to kill anybody.\nBut my attorney was oblivious to the danger. He was wearing his savage smile. \u201cWhat\u2019s your hurry...", "1_15_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to four o'clock in the afternoon</span> with the sun dying on the heads of the mountains and there were still a few clouds on the horizon, pink and gold. We were riding beside a ravine through a forest of firs and oaks and running vines and I was looking up at the branches that keep out the sun, shading the road. A woodpecker drummed and I looked up but saw nothing. The bull-fight was postponed till Monday. $FIRSTNAMEB5 wanted a drink and we swung up the road to a little hill-top caf\u00e9. There were white houses in the trees and under a trellised veranda were two shepherd children asleep on the table, and their brown goats beside them. Across the road were the sheep in the fenced pasture and some goats and farther on others, and the tall grass, and the mountains with snow on their high places. $FIRSTNAMEB5, Mike, Bill, and I drank wine and stood out under the trees against the sky and watched the cloud shadows pass on the mountains. $FIRSTNAMEB5 liked it there and wanted to stay. It was cooler, and the white houses in the wood, the leaves of the trees, the long shadows, and the blue cast to the day, all this gave a changed feeling, made a difference as though you felt cool when your back was turned...", "0_5_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had never before been out of the barrack in total darkness. He didn\u2019t like it.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 sniffed the fresh air, and there in the pure ozone he voiced a long, triumphant fart. $FIRSTNAMEB0 was drunk, confused.\nA gang of cleaners lurched past, coming in from another detail. Their leader, a bald man, stopped when he saw $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Yo!\" he said. He was obviously delighted to see $FIRSTNAMEB0 loafing by the colors.\nThe gang behind him stopped, too, licked their hands, made some threatening sounds.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 examined his stripes, found them abraded and illegible. That made him drunker than ever. He rolled his head, made his Adam\u2019s apple do exercises. \"Oughta git me some stripes, \" he told the bald leader finally. The man was much bigger than $FIRSTNAMEB0. His neck was so thick he couldn\u2019t turn his head without a struggle. The muscles in his upper arms tied themselves in complicated knots when he made threatening gestures at $FIRSTNAMEB0. \"Ain\u2019tcha got no stripes?\" he said.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 shook his head, belched. He didn\u2019t like the taste...", "1_17_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past five o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB2's body became a limp bag of blood at her feet, Aura stood up, brushed off the crumbled remains of her red gown, and stepped nimbly over the body of her dead son. She strode across the underground room, past a few staring follicles of hair, to a spot before the big rust-colored blanket.\nAura pulled the pins from her hair, and the golden profusion spilled to her waist. And then the queen of Titan went to work.\nShe blended hundreds of different pigments with a mortar and pestle.\nWith a series of carved combs, she combed the paints through her hair. Each comb had a dozen teeth\u2014a dozen different troughs of paint.\nBy the time she had finished, Aura\u2019s hair shone with forty-seven different colors. Her hair looked like a beautiful sunset made of pure gold.\nShe painted her lips violet. Over the violet she laid a coat of Martian hemoglobin, which came out pink. Over the pink she applied the color of the Ganymedean night-bloomers, which was light green.\nThe combination was lovely.\nThen she rang for an assistant.\nEveryone had been locked in. This meant that...", "2_3_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes until four o'clock in the morning</span>.\n4.5\nSnoopy was a beautiful shaggy shepherd dog that belonged to a little boy named Charlie Murphy. Snoopy was of a bright, bold, black and silver type. Snoopy could count to six. He could wag his stub of a tail forty-three times in a second. He could play blackjack and beat a grand-daddy eight-hundred-percent markup. He could read the Bible in ten minutes.\nHe could do over two hundred backflips in one week.\nWhen he felt like it, he could build a house in a day.\nWhenever he finished building a house, he tore it down so as to build another one.\nLife was a terrible burden to Snoopy.\nA NEW Florence Henderson\nand still she\u2019s adorable!\nA MUSICAL\nBY\nCLIFFORD DE LA TOUR,\nwith music by\nADAM GHOTZ\nDirected by\nBEN TARDRUM\nOpening Hit Song\u2014SPRINGTIME\nTHE MUSIC BOX REVUE\n5.5", "0_6_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes until seven o'clock in the morning</span>, according to our chronometers. My pilot's watch showed the same time, a split second sooner. The sun would rise behind us, at the proper time of day. It was impossible to tell sunrise and sunset in this wonderful, slowmoving light over the plains and the swamps and the lagoons.\nBut I was hanging there behind my pilot, and Earth was sure as anything. I loved every single filthy inch of her.\nWe were coming down under power, as a routine final landing, just like fifty others. Then I began to understand what special hell we were in for. The special hell that comes once in a million powerlandings.\nI popped the question to my pilot: \"Anybody ever land her in Detroit?\"\n\"Nobody that still lives,\" he said.\nIt was a good answer\u2014but it wasn't the answer I was looking for.\nI had to put my mind on something else. I put it on $FIRSTNAMEF3's lighthearted letter about his little poker school in Michigan.\n\" . . . we usually take off one night and land one morning . . . \"\nIt was a wild thought.\n\"You know,\" I shouted up to my pilot, \"that it'd only take us two hands to...", "2_11_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nA middle-aged man came whizzing down East Capitol Street on a bicycle. He had black sidewalls on his tires, and a red flag on the handlebar stem.\n\"He\u2019s trying to kill himself,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, grinding her cigar in an ashtray made out of a skull.\n\"Who is?\" asked $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Nobody. Don\u2019t interrupt,\" she said. She was watching the bicyclist gain speed before a gale, then, suddenly whip off in a tight S curve, while going sideways.\n\"That wasn\u2019t what I\u2019d call good bicycle-riding,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0, \"but I suppose you have to start somewhere.\"\n\"Who was he?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"What?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\n\"Who is he?\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"Nobody,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0.\nA moment later, the bicyclist who had just tried to kill himself skidded around the corner into Capitol Court.", "2_11_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIn the cupboard of an empty apartment at the Boone Observatories, Space Warfare Center, Mars, the whiskey still waited to be drunk.\n\"LIEUTENANT MAGGART, do you remember telling me, when we landed on Earth, that none of my men would believe me, after I got back from Tralfamadore? Do you remember saying that?\"\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Maggart.\n\"Were you right?\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I guess so,\" said Maggart.\n\"Would you believe me, if you knew what happened to me after I got back from Tralfamadore?\" said $SURNAME0.\nMaggart considered it for a moment. \"No, sir,\" he said. \"Because I don\u2019t think anybody who wrote me that letter could have had a experiences you say you had.\"", "1_17_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span>. To an inhabitant of Kansas, the approach of such a moment would have seemed as solemn and deep with meaning as the coming of midnight on Christmas Eve. That is because most inhabitants of Kansas do not approach it, but go to meet it halfway, with band music and a carload of sheriff\u2019s deputies. But moments like this often acquire more significance than they deserve, when they are shared by two men in telegraph cabins who have never seen each other before.\nThe time was eight minutes to six o'clock in the evening.\nThe end of the World was at hand.\n\"I tell you,\" said $SURNAME1, \"you\u2019re going to shit your dungarees.\"\n\"Not till I\u2019ve tried everything else,\" said Beatty.\n\"I said everything,\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"Your thinking is limited, pal,\" said Beatty.\nThey were quarreling again. It was, perhaps, the tenth quarrel\u2014or the hundredth. By this time, the quarrels had lost their drama, for the quarrels were no longer about love. Love had tired itself out. Both men were too tired to make love.\n\"How the hell do you know everything else?\" said Beatty...", "1_11_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven thirty-seven in the morning</span> on October 26, 1985. The place was the filling station of a town called $FIRSTNAMEF1 Vigil, which was on Route 353, seventy miles north of Indianapolis, Indiana, and fifty miles south of Port Wayne, Indiana. The little town of $FIRSTNAMEF1 Vigil was named for the solitary bee that underlay the honeycomb of sandstone under the town. $FIRSTNAMEF1 Vigil was situated on the north end of $FIRSTNAMEF1 Vigil Lake, a shallow two-mile-long lake with an island in the center furnishing summer picnics for the children of the town. The town of $FIRSTNAMEF1 Vigil proper consisted of two side streets, with a stop light, of frame houses and a small industrial section. The filling station was the only nonresidential building on one of the side streets. The filling station had once been a garage for the cars of the fashionable people out on the lake, but cars had given way to powerboats, so the little building had been pressed into the humble service of jerry-tin servers of gasoline, distributor and maker of windshield-wiper blades, vulcanizer of tire irons, and charcoal consumer.\nAnd yet, squatting on its hill, $FIRSTNAMEF1 Vigil was a pleasant little town, with a steeple in the center of town, and yellow-lighted street...", "0_15_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. For the first time in years I had no place to go and nothing to do. I was all hung up on the edge of a brand-new Fear. I had been rolling along on a pretty even keel, bringing forth in my time and storing up for the evil days ahead, or so I thought. But irs impossible to store up enough for the really ghastly days. Those you have to live through, and always without warning.\nIt was early December in Las Vegas. To the north the desert was all black and smoking after the big wind; but down in the valley it was warm as spring. It was a dangerous time to be entering the city of Las Vegas --a necessary time. l had made the big move. I sat at the bar in the airport drinking tequila fast. I had to have several to get the feel of the town. lt was smoky in there and the air was full of the cries of women. They sounded like birds of the fields and deep waters. This was a holy place to me; there was no other on earth. I knew this with all my heart. lsn't that really touching? I mean if you think about it. Here I was all hung up and crazy for this place...", "0_3_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three sixteen in the morning</span>. I had another opus to finish that night, and needed money for more paper. The owner of the coffee shop stood by nervously as I approached the yawning man with the task before him. \u201cYou can't arrest me,\u201d the gambler snarled. \u201cIt's your own fault.\u201d\n\u201cThat's right\u2014it is. I might as well have taken your bet\u2014but I'm doing this for my boss, because you're disturbing people. I've got to get you out of here, no ifs about it.\u201d I grabbed his arm and led him to the door. \u201cDon't follow me,\u201d I warned him. \u201cMy job ends when I put you in the back seat of my car and drive you away from here.\"\n\u201cYou're a Nazi,\u201d he shouted. \u201cThat's why they call you 'Inspector Savage.' I've had you under surveillance for nine years.\"\nI hurried him to my car. The owner of the place was so worried that he handed me a cache of pipes and drugs that the gambler had thrown into a paper bag. Then I shoved my prisoner into the back seat. He was screaming obscenities...", "1_9_49": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-nine in the morning</span>. The place was a chair in the Unruh Building\u2019s lobby.\nNo help for it, Miles High. The only way left to penetrate the mystery of the clock is to kill yourself, and that won\u2019t do.\nAnd where, please, is the woman who was in this seat not three minutes ago?\nMust find the woman. If she sees me here asleep and is unafraid and smiles, so much the better.\nWhere did she go? Madman comes down hall and sits in lobby, muttering to himself. She must have gone to call Security. Ten o\u2018clock, be there or be square. Have mercy on a poor old man, please!\nNo sign of her now. Must have gone on upstairs to her gig at the accounting firm.\nWait. Wait. What is this now? Watching her approach me through sunlight and shadows, drinking her in through all my eyes. Blonde. Tanned. A leg, a foot . . . a breast, a breath of perfume. Step closer now and talk to me, you sweet bitch.\nBut she\u2019s not. Not at all. Standing above me scowling and pointing. Finger crooked like an accusing witch.", "0_19_15": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was quarter after seven o'clock in the evening</span> of a cool material Tuesday in June of the year 1947. An airship was hanging in the June sky over New York, waiting for its passengers to finish dinner. It was a good dinner, and incidentally a very good airship. The passengers were mostly East Indian. At the dinner were also a good many eyes\u2014swarthy, glittering, slitty eyes. But none more so than the eyes of a man who sat at a window table, alone. He was smoking a tranquil cigarette.\nAt the table next to him were two men\u2014one dark, one pink\u2014talking earnestly. They asked to be brought two more bottles of champagne. They were drunk. They were Americans.\nBeside them, not listening to them, sat a broad young man in white flannels and a yachting cap. He smoked and listened while they talked and talked. He looked tough and angry. He deliberately ground out his cigarette in the metal ash-tray at his right elbow. Dunhill.\n\"Damme, Bradford,\" he said. \"You can\u2019t know what year it is.\"\nHis name was Robert Helm. He was the co-owner of a small railroad in Alabama.\n\"Damn you, it\u2019s 1947...", "2_21_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes past nine o'clock in the evening</span>.\nAt ten minutes past nine o\u2019clock in the evening the two were married, standing up, with a Justice of the Peace presiding.\n\"Well, sir,\" said the Justice of the Peace, \"do you take this woman to be your lawful wedded wife?\"\n\"I do,\" said the cigarette man.\n\"And do you take this man\u2014\"\n\"Whoa!\" said the cigarette man. \"There\u2019s that 'man\u2019 again.\"\n\"Man?\" said the Justice of the Peace.\n\"Why do you keep calling me 'man\u2019?\" said the cigarette man. \"I\u2019ve got a perfectly good name\u2014William F. Phelps. I was christened William F. Phelps.\"\nThere was a loud sneeze. It was the bride sneezing.\nShe was holding a white-coveralled lap dog under one arm, and a brown paper package under the other. \"Awww-choo!\" the bride sneezed.", "1_12_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve thirty-seven post meridiem</span> of one Ramanujan, and for a moment before the shadow of mahogany made its insinuating run from the window across her harmonium to her chair with the unlighted lamp beside it and her lap filled with papers, and moved on to the corner of the wall by the bookcase where the no-longer-light always seemed to be, she had been thinking of herself as so happy. Not that the interruption with its noise and its marching and pouncing shadows was in any way unpleasant to her. It had broken a chain of thought that would have been much too prolonged, had it been uninterrupted. Moreover, her son, she was glad to see, had come in. Though apparently, her smile seemed to say, he has just taken off his top coat, how nice that he has come in. His presence, slender young man in his grey flannels standing by a bookcase, so deferential, asking if he might take a book, gave her an intimate sense (all this quite on the surface) of the rightness of things, as she let her glance rest a moment on the top of his head. Had he aperitifed, been dinnering out? He looked oddly frivolous, in spite of his deference, in...", "0_9_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-seven in the morning</span>. The place was the entrance to an elevator on the thirty-fourth floor of the Space Research Building of the University of Mars, in Weinstein, on Mars. The name of the person made uppity by the beautiful vision of a mint-julip was Salo. The name of the person giggled at by Salo was Phoebe. Phoebe was Salo\u2019s right eye and only friend.\nSalo was an Aquarius, a member of the oldest species known to man. The average age of an Aquarius on Mars was three thousand years.\nThe average height was four feet.\nSalo was six feet tall, and proud of it.\nPhoebe was Salo\u2019s only friend because she was the only living thing on Mars pious enough to believe that the sundial that regulated Salo\u2019s life was God.\nPhoebe was four feet tall.\nShe, too, was proud of it.\nSalo paused with his big hand on the big, flat switch that would break the connection between the switch that was in the basement in Cleveland and the sun that, at the moment, was over Cuba. \"Good morning, Phoebe,\" he said, looking up at his...", "1_20_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was a small room in $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s barracks in the garrison of Fortrition, on Mars.\nThe barracks room was ten foot square.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 lived there with a friend and a doll.\nThe friend was a beautiful German shepherd named Kazoo. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had named Kazoo\u2014had given him his name\u2014because Kazoo had said his only word for $FIRSTNAMEB0 on his way up from Earth aboard the Titan.\nKazoo spoke his only word when $FIRSTNAMEB0 hit him. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had hit him lovingly, conspiringly\u2014had hit him with the affectionate idea of getting him to speak his only word.\nAnd Kazoo had obliged. And what might that single word have been? Friend.\nYes\u2014Kazoo was everybody\u2019s friend. He was a superdog. What a dog!\nThere would never be another one like him.\nIn the course of the fifteen months since his arrival on Mars, $FIRSTNAMEB0 had picked up no less than twenty-nine of Kazoo\u2019s charming habits. Kazoo did everything with a snap, like a Bohemian girl in expensive aphasia. And $FIRSTNAMEB0 was beginning to think like Kazoo. It was as though Kazoo\u2019...", "1_12_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-four post meridiem</span>. Lang and I were surrounded by a nation of stone-sober Mormons, who were just marching in for noontime lunch. We, on the other hand, were about to launch an allout food-kinetic assault, that would end only when our biological reserves were totally exhausted. According to my calculations, we could probably maintain peak power output for six hours; this should just about hit the postprandial nail on the claw. Any notion of \u201cscale,\u201d or of \u2018\u201cmoderation,\u201d flew out of our heads by noon. The place was filling up\u2014fast\u2014but the waitress/captain never even blinked.\n9. The Tao of Irresponsibility The \u201cArgus people\u201d were very curious about my Nevada driver\u2019s license; but I placated them by saying the police in Louisville, Kentucky\u2014where I live\u2014are just about the worst gang in the country, and that it would be foolish to press legal action becausetheir idea of a fair and impartial trial would probably call for me to be strung up by the ankles from the Crown Vic\u2019s left mirror while the cops took turns on mewith nightsticks.\n10. The Soul of Qu animate...", "2_15_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three forty-six in the afternoon</span>. I had been in the jail of the Denver (Colorado) Municipal Court for about three and a half hours, locked in a barred isolation cellsix by ten feetby six. The floor was of multi\u2014colored laminations of steel. The walls and ceiling were gray cinder block. There was a bunk that folded out of the wall, a toilet without a seat, a sink without a fixture, and a judas. The cell measured six by ten feet. I was sitting on the bunk, with my back against the wall, my feet on the floor. My hands were handcuffed in back of me. I was facing the door. There were two policemen beside the door. One of them was named Smith and the other Guard. They were pacing back and forth. Smith was a tall, bristlyhaired blond boy. Guard was a shortyoung man, tousled and hostile. He was about twentyseven years old, and had not reached the horrifying final hopelessness of the reformer who has devoted many years to fighting police brutality and corruption on the force and finds that he has been kidding himself all along. \u201cCome on, white bread,\u201d he kept saying, \u201cwhere's the money?\u201d", "2_22_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-one in the evening</span> on Mars.\nThe time would be ten thirty-one in the evening on Mars.\nRECKLESS MRS. KRAUSE, at her window across the street, fainted. She saw $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 come out of the sky like a jockey on the back of a horse.\nWhen she woke up, she telephoned the police.\nThe police didn\u2019t believe her story\u2014that $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had landed in her backyard. The police assumed that she had been drinking.\nTHIS IS WHAT HAPPENED:\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 went to West 77th Street and rounded the comer of the Museum of Natural History. And there, to his surprise, was the bronze spaceship he had come to Earth in.\nThe door of the spaceship swung open before he could put his hand on the knob. The door swung open, and out stepped a nubile young girl. She was followed by a churlish, flint-eyed, liver-colored plainclothesman.\n\"You forgot to take your pill this morning, Miss Newton,\" said the plainclothesman to the young girl.", "2_12_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve seventeen post meridiem</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0 opened his eyes, looked ruefully at the uneaten food in his hand, thought: That stuff\u2019ll kill you, too.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 got off the rock, put the food and drink mix back in his pocket, shouldered his rifle, started walking south.\nHis huge body was bathed in perspiration. He hadn\u2019t eaten in three Earthling days, but he wasn\u2019t hungry.\nHe was thinking now without the help of crowds. He let his memory drift back, now, to the awful event that had made him need the crowds so terribly\u2014the event that had grown him from a normal man into a big baby.\nIt had begun with $FIRSTNAMEB1 lying dead in the middle of a pasture that by Hider\u2019s decree was an airport. $FIRSTNAMEB0 had said his grisly prayers, had tucked the clean white flag back into his pocket, had gotten out a cigarette as a reward. The cigarette had been Virgin Vie-Gorgon\u2019s idea.", "0_3_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five minutes after three o'clock in the morning</span> and the place\u2014the room. The bottle on the sheet-covered table. The wet rings dried. The dust motes moving in the fan beam. Red Rum and green bottle in spotlight. Came sounds from without. If a space ship had popped through the roof and played Rock Around the Clock on the ship\u2019s horn... and if red-suited aliens had danced out in jagged goofball formation, singing\nUnsignificantly, the door opened and a white-faced $FIRSTNAMEF2 Maas come in and shut the door behind her.\n\u201cDarling,\u201d whispered Metzger, \u201cwhy so unexpected, please.\u201d\nShe wandered staring, towards him, unable to focus. \u201cThinthekk,\u201d she said to him.\n\u201cI\u2019m sorry?\u201d\n\u201cLook at this.\u201d She handed him a tightly folded bit of paper. But it was not a letter, simply words Xeroxed on a plain sheet. Metzker started to read aloud: \u201c \u2018That was the message Kid Arkwright got from Marvin Trilling, who, according to a footnote in the story, once suffered a nervous breakdown in the Navy.\u2019", "0_1_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>.\nCossarski yelled through a megaphone at $SURNAME1 and $FIRSTNAMEF0. The yelling made $SURNAME1 think of Salo\u2019s demonstration to mad D $FIRSTNAMEM1 about silver bullion cubes in a megaphone.\nThe $SURNAME1 girl began to cry. She ran to a window, opened it, and began throwing out her satin slippers, her Handy snub-nosed bullets, and anything else that was Obed Q. Kazinskian.\nCossarski beamed through his megaphone.\nHot Rum \u00d6tk\u00f6zzhizzith came down the cellar stairs carrying an enormous bundle of documents. He opened the little door to the coal chute, dropped the bundle into it, filled the little coal-chute door again with a hopper.\nThe Bethlehem Bumbles, now knocking knees and elbows inside their big floor-cloths, watched with eyes like pink cabbage balls. They had begun to sweat again. The streetlights in the neighborhood sent in a ghastly sheen.\nCossarski beamed at $SURNAME1, at $FIRSTNAMEF0, at Rum, and then at Asa Q. nutmeg.\nOnly $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and his dog $FIRSTNAMEB4 were missing from the tableau...", "1_1_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-two in the morning</span>, August 1, 1985. I had driven to Las Vegas many times in my life, with Lonesome, with Few Oxherding, with $FIRSTNAMEB3 the time we changed cars under the tunnel; but before I'd always gone through in a flash of organized confusion, hitting all the right bars, meeting deadlines, beating police cameras, and getting out of town in time to meet a plane and live happily ever after. Now I had ample time in which to understand what I was getting into. I knew I had no deadline except to get back to the ship on time and there was no family waiting in the wings either, no Rita, nobody in the world who cared if I got back to ship Shape or not...except me. So there was no point hurrying, and I drove the Cadillac northward across the warm desert floor of Nevada, and each mile I drove made my feeling for Mary Frances and her curious family stronger and stronger. A caravan of huge eighteen-wheel trucks passed me, red tail-lights in the darkness, bound for the West Coast. I saluted them Goin\u2019 back to L.A., boys; and poured acid in my eyes so I could see the phosphorescent toys better as I drove up the desert inclines. By three...", "1_16_26": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-six in the afternoon</span>. I spent the next six hours wandering around San Francisco. I began on Nob Hill, for some odd reason, and wandered down around Union Square, where the tramps were already beginning to come out. Perhaps I was searching for $FIRSTNAMEB3. On I went in a idiot daze. Mainly I remember immense loneliness. Then I realized I was in North Beach. I thought only yesterday I was here with $FIRSTNAMEB3. I rushed down to Washington Square to find Ricardo, the lifeguard friend of my most private past, and found him on a porch chair in the sun, hardly believing my eyes. And the girl who was with him? Yes, it proved to be Margo, the girl I met in the parking lot at Pass Christian, whom I'd treated so badly, and here she was in San Francisco, and I was completely amazed to find her, even though she had lost her beauty and looked really terrible and ragged; her belly had, fallen away and her breasts sagged down and she had a withered face that, twisted angrily at me. And all the girls around her still looked fourteen. She told me they were living in Washington Square and invited me to come up. I told her I was broke and walked away to bum a cup of tea and be...", "1_15_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past three o'clock in the afternoon</span>. The first afternoon. The first twelfth of a full Exterior day. The light was slackening.\nThe Saltonstalls could see nothing beyond the tips of their three pine trees.\nThe Saltonstalls could see nothing beyond the tips of their three trees, and it was almost dark under the trees where they stood.\nIn November on Mars\n\"Analysis, please,\" said $SURNAME0. \"What are you thinking of right now?\"\n\"Ninth grade English composition,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Write an account of the most careless day you ever spent. Tag your careless day with a true account of the finest day of your life thereafter. I wrote about a day when a man gave me a five-dollar bill to hold for a minute while he went to get change.\"\n\"I loved best the fine day afterward,\" said $SURNAME0 softly. \"Tell me about it.\"\n\"You haven\u2019t told me the name of my husband yet,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF0. \"Please tell me the name of my husband.\"\n$SURNAME0 busied himself with the graph before him. The graph was based on seventy-two tape recordings taken synchronously with the chronoscopes in $SURNAME0\u2019s museum on Earth...", "1_0_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-three ante meridiem</span>. As the last Army staff car disappeared down the driveway, $SURNAME1, who was wearing Mrs. Hazelnut\u2019s red-fox kimono, bounded up the stairs three and four steps at a time.\nCrispus was gone.\nOnly at the last minute had he disappeared. He had been sitting on the window sill, and his silhouette remained scorched against the sunlight.\nUnsigned by $SURNAME1, strapped securely under his left armpit was the finest abstract expressionist oil painting in the world.\nSigned by a certain Wilbur M. Dinsmore of $SURNAME1\u2019s own Earth, it was called The Brotherhood of Man.\nUnsigned by $SURNAME1 and strapped under his right armpit was the stone statue, $FIRSTNAMEF0, by Ricardo Roth.\nIt was being used, under unspeakable conditions, as a puppy dog toy by a Martian child.\nWhat followed next was reported by $SURNAME0 mercilessly and accurately in The Phenomonology of $SURNAME0.\nWhere the Good Soldiers go when they die is a mystery to the rest of us. So far, nobody has contrived a method of tracing the Good Soldiers to their destiny. Perhaps they simply rot in the Earth like everyone else. Perhaps they go...", "2_13_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-four in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 and $FIRSTNAMEB0, together with their colleagues in cleaning and maintenance and all the other personnel, lived and worked in an armored, underground facility about two hundred yards from the hospital proper. The whole complex was a circle two hundred yards in diameter. The center was a garageshaped crew-maintenance area, from which radiate two hundred underground corridors, five yards wide.\nThe corridors joined all the buildings of the hospital proper, with corridors running out to a similar circle of buildings in the civilian community outside. In the circle of buildings outside were impressive offices and show places for civilians.\nThe hospital proper, the maintenance garages, and the rest of the underground complex were spotlessly immaculate. The hospital proper received all \u2014 of Mars that could receive civilization, and there was never an empty bed.\nNot a janitor in the place ever put a mop or broom or rag away. Not a janitor ever put anything away, unless it was an exploded and already autopsied recruit. No recruit had ever exploded during his first two Earth-years in the Army. The recruits exploded during their third and final Earth-year, and remained exploded thereafter.", "2_13_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-six in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 returned to his room, to sit quietly until two.\nAt two, he locked his door, and descended to the floor below only long enough to get $SURNAME0\u2019s piece of blue sky from the cage in which it was kept.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 took it out of its gilded frame, and carried it with him.\nHe had to carry it with him. It was a part of his real treasure, since it had been owned by his real hero.\nAt the head of the spiral stairs, on the bottom level of the palace, was a small door with a window of narrow horizontal rectangles. The door was locked, and there was a sign on it. The sign on the door read:\nOUT OF ORDER.\nDO NOT USE THIS DOOR.\nUSE DOOR AT\nELEVENTH FOOT.\nThrough the window, $FIRSTNAMEB1 could see that the trash incinerator was backstage. It was just a furnace in a stone enclosure.\nBy the furnace door were tall metal pails from which cotton gloves were hung on wooden pegs. The pails contained lime and water, in which a man with a long-handled shovel soaked his hands before beginning work.", "1_20_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>.\nWho invented clocks? And who talks about time? The person who invented clocks and the person who talks about time are, both of them, cranks.\nAnd who invented alarm clocks?\nAn alarm clock wakes you up in the morning by alarming.\nAn alarm clock is a horrible thing to inflict upon a person.\nThe sound an alarm clock makes is a sound that would not hurt a healthy person.\nThe sound an alarm clock makes harasses only a sick person.\nA healthy person accepts the sound as a sign that it is time to do something healthy\u2014like hurling himself out of bed, a feat that takes no less mortal a man than Tarzan.\nA sick person accepts the sound of an alarm clock as a sign that he is about to die. A healthy person can, if he wants to, heave himself out of bed at the sound of an alarm clock. A sick person cannot. In his weakened condition, the sound of an alarms clock persuades a sick person that the best thing he can do is to die. He can\u2019t even raise his arm to turn the thing off, He has to lie there in his sickness, listening to the poisonous sounds of an alarm, on the theory that all that...", "0_21_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine nineteen in the evening</span>, the sun had just set, the sky was still bright, the air was clear and there was no wind at all. Fieldbinder bent down, placed a hand tentatively on the soft loose end of the root. He took the clippers in a firm grip and gently began to snip.\nAt the sound of the clippers Trudy calmly looked out over the hedge, through her orange shades, at Fieldbinder.\n/f/\n\u201cGod\u2019s eyeball.\u201d\n\u201cPardon?\u201d\n\u201cThe world is God\u2019s eyeball.\u201d \u201cRoughage problem again. Would it help if I started telling you jokes?\u201d\n\u201cAge shall not wither her. At a constant physical age, the universe lies before her.\u201d \u201cWould it help if I changed brands?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019m just the right size. Relative to the universe, I\u2019m the right size. I fit it like a glove.\u201d\n\u201cProbably going to have to put the birdfeeder out pretty soon.\u201d\n\u201cMy sweat smells delicious. I sweat delicious sweat.\u201d \u201c....", "2_19_29": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven twenty-nine in the evening</span>.\nThere was one rickety chair near the white window. There was one broad, broken bed. A man was on the bed, asleep. The man looked almost like a skeleton. His skin was peeling off his skull.\nThere was a small table near the man. On the table was a case containing contact lenses. There were two books on the table. One book was an old Bible. One book was The Complete Works of Kansas Scotch Tom Collins. Under the table was a case containing a heart-transplant apparatus. The apparatus ticked at regular intervals.\nThe apparatus was keeping the man alive.\nThe man\u2019s name was Alvin Biggs.\nHe was fifty-four.\nThe alarm-clock on the table woke him up. It was eight o\u2019clock in the evening. At eight o\u2019clock in the evening a heart-transplant patient should be in a deep sleep, with sedation drugs in his system.\nBiggs tried to sit up in bed. He could not; his back would not let him. Only his head, shoulders, and left arm were free to move. He used his left arm to turn on the light over his bed.", "2_16_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes after four o'clock in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 was seventy miles above New York, driving the car like a demon. \u201cHey, befuddled buddy, take it easy. It's only me, your old pal $FIRSTNAMEB6.'\u201d We both jumped with fright. It was the captain of the Coast Guard cutter Marlinspike talking on a walkie-talkie in a small Coast Guard launch behind us. They had apparently just picked us up on their infrared lamp, because we were poorly hidden on the dunes in the pitch dark. Captain Bockheim was used to intercept drug smugglers and he didn't waste a moment before checking us out. $FIRSTNAMEB3 covered his face in horror and terror -- it was the dark of the moon, five AM, with a strong wind howling. I could make out just barely that the launch had two big-bellied Coast Guard officers in it. \u201cWho's in there?\u201d yelled the captain. \u201cWhere are you from? Stop or I'll ssshoot!\u201d His radio talked back to him at the same time. $FIRSTNAMEB3 tried to think of all the possible answers that would meet with approval.\n\u201cWho are you?\u201d", "0_15_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-four in the afternoon</span>. There remained four hours and forty-six minutes before the rushing doom of Monday morning. I had to work on the rental car, get my laundry and know what to do about my feet. Las Vegas has no monopoly on the things that can kill you. And it\u2019s only eleven hours back to California.\nAnd goddamn they finally nailed S. Bantle, an/fortyfouryearold looniesick PTA president and parttime missionary who lived in a trailercourt in Des Moines, Iowa. Right around the corner from some innocent people who owned a store. He was cruising through Des Moines in his flatbed Ford pickup one morning a few days before our race, and one his way to go pick up his roomful of poison snakes, when he decided to pay a secret visit to the home of one of the people whose address he'd stolen from the mailing lists of Sane RiotousFamilies, or whatever. His little daughter had received a postal card from this family, and so he stopped to visit with them for a few minutes and knife them both to death. His daughter, who'd received the postal card, was not at home. His wife was found later that afternoon, in a ditch, with both legs cut off just above...", "1_7_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes past seven o'clock in the morning</span>. Strangely enough I wasn't a bit sleepy. The girl with the big bosom was far away. I was Ernie from New Jersey again, walking into New Orleans. I felt like I owned New Orleans, and I also felt like I'd always be walking into New Orleans for the rest of my life.\nFor I knew that everything physical happens only once, forever...and so now any state of my body that I feel, any emotion, any pain, joy, desire, ecstasy, any kind of physical pleasure, and the bad things too, all have gone and all have come back, twice. There is nowhere I can go and nowhere I can run. There is nowhere I can hide. The one and only Ernie from New Jersey walked into New Orleans twice: he'll always walk into New Orleans again and again and again ... and you with me, and everybody with me, and I with you, forever.\nRoute 1 South & West to Albuquerque, January 29-30, 1947. I got up and breakfasted. The bus for Shreveport left at ten. I had a newspaper to read in the meanwhile. The kid came in and got his suitcase ready and I gave him a ten-dollar bill and so, shortly,...", "2_16_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-two in the afternoon</span>. Five minutes to deadline and the nation's capital was closed in around us like the blade of a sound\u2014truck. My attorney was white. He had sensed something grave was about to happen and was bracing for the Big Flash.\n\u201cNot that!\u201d I cried wildly. \u201cOut your right window, man!\"\nHe turned toward me. His jaw hung down. \u201cGoddamn!\u201d he croaked. \u201cAren't they heavy!\"\n\u201cLook,\u201d I said. \u201cWe can't just sit here. I think those people are going to get hurt. Maybe we should interfere.\"", "0_4_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. Trumbull\u2019s wrist-watch read 5:00:04 when the knocking began.\nIt was a purposeful but not a loud knocking. Sugarman had said it would be his wife at the door, and it turned out to be his wife at the door. The knocking went on steadily.\nTrumbull reached over and flipped the switch that lit all the hundreds of thumb-sized bulbs on the children\u2019s model of a DNA spiral, turning that magical object from an orange glimmer in the dark room to a shiny amber distraction in the light room.\nHe flipped the switch back and got up, and the three of them walked the short distance in dark, silent corridors to the front door together.\nWhen different parts of a group in communication with each other move in silence from place to place, the movement has a somber effect, as in a low-budget horror movie.\nTrumbull opened the door. His wife stood there at a little over five feet by herself. She looked in, blinked three times, sniffed, smiled amusedly\u2014and let a terribly tall old man enter after her. He wore small rimless spectacles and sported a full beard and an old-fashioned hat.\nHe was...", "0_1_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes until two o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the brown wooden door. $SURNAME1 knelt before the door, reduced to himself, purged of everything else\u2014of his cronyism, of his sentimentality, of his prejudices and vanities, and even of his fear. He knew what he had come for, and he knew, finally, where he had come from.\nThere were no riches in his family background. They were too rich in his family background. There was a uncle who had struck oil on his ranch in California, another who had used his share of the estate of their mother (she had died young) to buy Berkshire-Hathaway at 44 and sell it at 104\u00bd.\nThere was a third uncle who made boats in Pontiac, Michigan, and still made them. In 1944 he designed a power boat for the government to put troops on enemy beaches. The troops never made it that far, but the uncle made himself a lot of money.\nAn only child, $SURNAME1 had been a frail child, secretly and horribly weak in the head. His growing up had been difficult and embarrassing. He was sent to an exclusive private school, where he was the only one who rode on a streetcar to school, passed out in English when the classes were long, played the...", "1_22_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-two in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was outside with $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was giving $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son a history lesson.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had decided that $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son was old enough to be told what was what.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s son was a blue-eyed, rosy-cheeked boy of six. His hair was dark blond, and dark blond was the color reserved by evolution in the color-table of his species for the temps ordinaire.\nHis hair was dark blond because the child\u2019s maternal forebears had carefully selected for dark blond hair, generation after generation.\nHis blue eyes were countrified, being the uncritical product of centuries of favorable selection for uncritical blue eyes.\nAfter three centuries and six generations of close inbreeding, the boy\u2019s ancestors were surely free of undesirable genes as far as eyes, hair, and complexion were concerned. Any three of the boy\u2019s ancestors would have done as well as any other three, since all of the boy\u2019s ancestors were closely identical.\nThis highly regulated little boy with the countrified blond hair and blue eyes was the first citizen to be taken inside a barrack in the 116th Infantry Regiment.", "1_1_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-four in the morning</span>. One of the cops came up to me and said, \u201cWe can\u2019t let you fellas stand out here like this all night long.\"\nWe meekly agreed to reboard the bus. We watched them like hawks as we went back, to see what weapons they\u2019d need to overpower us. None of them went below and brought up chains or gas masks: they merely closed the door and turned on the motor. We had to talk fast, because we were only safe as long as they felt they had to keep us under surveillance.\n\u201cNow listen, comrades and fellow\u2014travelers,\u201d I began. \u201cClearly the sick creeps who run this shitheap of a country have stacked the deck against us from the start.\"\n\u201cThat\u2019s right!\u201d yelled somebody in the rear, who I couldn\u2019t see. \u201cWe\u2019re in the hands of fuckin\u2019 fascist pigs!\"\n\u201cThose cops are concerned about our welfare!\" I shouted back. \u201cHas anybody here thought about the incredible goddamn irony of that? Those cops out there are really on our side! Can we work with them, control them, mobilize them on our behalf?\"\nThis...", "0_17_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to six o'clock in the evening</span> and spring was holding on with both hands. The air was moist and carried a smell of melting tar from the hot pave ment.\n\"Change your mind yet?\" said Perlmutter.\n\"Do you really want to know?\"\n\"Of course,\" he said. \"Come on over and sit down at the table with me.\" He leaned his bicycle against the iron waist-high fence and sat at one of the marble-topped tables that were scattered among the box elders under the trees. \"How about a vermouth and bitters?\" he asked. \"I know how to fix it.\"\n\"Not yet.\"\n\"No, go ahead,\" he said.\n\"I\u2019m leaving here in the morning,\" I said, sitting down beside the table. \"I was gone a long time. Now I\u2019m coming back home.\"\n\"How far is Sacramento?\"\n\"Five hundred miles.\"\n\"You don\u2019t say so.\"\n\"A hundred and eighty miles to San Francisco, and then to Sacramento.\"\n\"Why don\u2019t you come in and have dinner with your friend?\" said Perlmutter. \"I think he would like to see you.\"\n\"I know,\" I said. \"Listen,...", "0_10_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was two minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> of a day in late October, the day before Election Day. The place was Springfield, Illinois.\nBut hell and the modern church are ahead of us. Keep going. Turn to the next page.\nINTRUDER IN THE DREAM\nTHE GREAT AMERICAN NOVEL\u2014AS every schoolchild is taught at an early age\u2014is Huckleberry Finn by Mark Twain. It stands alone, not only as the Great American Novel, but also as the first true20 American novel, a work sui generis.\nThe Great American Novelist, if one exists, will be a priest of sorts. His work will be prophetic, and as declamatory as the sermon on the mount. He will not be unaware of the significance of his role as a 20 This book is a sui generis. It is the Great American Novel.\nprophet. He will be filled with the holy grace of literary talent and will not hesitate to remind the world of what he has wrought. He will be in touch with God. It is his job to make the rest of us feel as insignificant as possible.\nI hope that I have fulfilled these requirements.\nIt is a sad commentary on our age that it is necessary to explain why an apparently...", "2_16_35": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four thirty-five in the afternoon</span>. Tension was increasing. Rum Roddie and Rum Maude simply stood in the middle of the field and looked at each other. The sun was shining strongly into Greenland-Iceland-England.\nThe next thing that happened was that, at exactly fifteen seconds after four thirty-five, the first wave of the German offensive hit, which was the cue for Rum Maude to release all her pigeons.\nThere were exactly twelve pigeons in all. They popped out of a trap in the bottom of the fuselage of Rum Maude, circled a few times, got their bearings, and started for Rum Maude\u2019s home loft in Norwich, England, ninety miles away.\nAt the same time, as the pigeons left Rum Maude, the main force of the German attack discharged thirty-two barrage rockets, sixteen from Rum Maude and another sixteen from Rum Freddie. Their targets were Copenhagen, Stockholm, Helsinki, Tallinn, Kronstadt, Novgorod, Kiev, Minsk, Smolensk, Spassk, and the seaport of Murmansk. The barrage rockets looked, during their horrendous and brief flight, like Nazi V-2s.", "2_7_6": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes after seven o'clock in the morning</span> on Titan.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was practicing public concerts in the apartment again. He woke his son up with a solo performance of Rachmaninoff\u2019s Symphony No. 3 in A minor.\nWhen the concert was over, the keyboard and all its music were neatly stored in the bottom of an enormous and incredibly dirty birdcage. The birdcage contained the skeleton of a small, prehistoric bird that had once flown on Nitrosukko, the largest moon of Neptune.\nThe birdcage contained many other things as well\u2014among them, $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s older brother $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2019s stag-horn skull. The birdcage had a real door that unlocked and closed. The family kept the birdcage bolted and locked most of the time, because the skeleton and all the other contents made such a frightful mess.\nThere was the skeleton, the keyboard, $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s son, $FIRSTNAMEF1, his wife Belle, and $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s ailing mother.\nSalo was sitting up in bed.", "0_4_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four forty-two in the morning</span> when the Thing finally regained consciousness. Its body was healthy\u2014no more than it should be for a man well under forty. Bones like chrome. Maybe a touch too much fat on the belly. But nothing wrong with the lungs.\nThe legs were good, too. No arthritis. Just a strong desire not to be out of bed at this particular hour of the morning. Or, it occurred to him\u2014as he slid naked out of bed and into a passionate argument with his wife\u2014just possibly it wasn\u2019t morning as such. What with time zones and flying saucers and all, what he might be feeling right now was noon. He lay down on the floor and started doing knee-bends.\n\"You bastard!\" his wife yelled down at him. \"Do you have to do that?\"\nHer name is Sara. She\u2019s the only child of a Mormon family settled in Des Moines. One time\u2014somehow or other\u2014she got interested in bull-fights, and now talks about nothing else. Finally, of course, she had to go to Spain, just like all the other women in the world interested in bull-fights. Alone, she came back to America, but for two years continued writing to her friend Pepe Yah...", "1_20_9": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine minutes past eight o'clock in the evening</span>. The place was the hallway before the glass front of the Rum and Milk bar.\nSeveral facts were noticeable at a glance: that the stone floor of the hallway was no longer smooth, but all torn up; that the once blue sky, seen through the bar's glass windows all around the sides, was now, at the end of the hallway, once again violet; that these windows around the sides had, in fact, been on the violet edge of the skyline, before the floor was torn up, but were now on the edge of the dark. The once dark sky overhead was now more red than violet, and seemed to be falling.\nThe hall's ceiling was crumbling into dust and ashes, glowing. There were a number of wars in progress in the sky overhead. What seemed to be green rockets were screaming and banging far up, toward the dark. White parachutes were drifting slowly down from the violet edge of the sky.\nStill other things were noticeable at a glance.\nA twelve-year-old girl wrapped only in a white towel was running, laughing, toward the dark at the far end of the bar's inside wall. There were twenty or thirty white-toweled people sitting on bar stools, looking in the same direction the running...", "2_2_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two forty-six in the morning</span>. The food was gone. Slowly, sadistically, Lang destroyed the last of the beet. Lenore was full. She felt as if her cement shoes were cementing. Lang lounged with a hip on the edge of the table.\n\u201cWe take an educational snack when we\u2019ve after all exerted ourselves in the gym,\u201d he said.\n\u201cYou\u2019re incorrigible.\u201d\n\u201c \u2018Prince Corolone Bardolino Cosacco Visconte Gerardino Mascagni Geronimo\u2019\u2014does that sound like an incorrigible to you, Lenore?\u201d\n\u201cIf I\u2019m full I can\u2019t eat seeds. I just usually don\u2019t bring enough of an appetite to the gym with me to have any appetite left over for seeds.\u201d\n\u201cWe unpack the dietetic implications of this whole exchange later on.\u201d\n\u201cOK, but what about my arms, then? Swear to God, it really hurts.\u201d\n\u201cWould you like some salve for your arms, Lenore?\u201d\n\u201cYou\u2019re making fun of me.\u201d", "1_4_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes until five o'clock in the morning</span>. In another five minutes, the night watchman would come around on his rounds, calling out to the sleeping programs. He would disturb $SURNAME1, who was dozing in his chair, and $SURNAME1 would disturb Metz, and Metz would disturb Gurd Lvov, and Gurd would disturb young Fall.\nThe frightened phantom, meanwhile, had crept unseen past the watchman, who was droning, \"Programs awake? Please be awake by five o\u2019clock.\" It had slipped by the cot in Room R304, where the motionless figure of young Stan Minker lay, unlit by the perimeter of wild lichen. It had tiptoed by a tricky corner of ducts in Room R112W, where a tidy series of sneezing fits would have certainly betrayed it.\nThe phantom was seeking the biological research division. The frightened phantom had the address. Trembling, it went down a flight of stairs, left past the Joke Bulletin Board, turned right past Heating and Cooling, passed-the Spittoon and the bar where $SURNAME0, $FIRSTNAMEF1 and Ledgame Cadwalader had met for a nightcap on the night when the Space Wanderer had landed on Mars.\n\"Greetings...", "1_20_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight thirty-four in the evening</span>. I took a bath, brushed my teeth, washed my hair in hot water, dried it and brushed it again, put on makeup, a light sweater, a dark wool dress, dark nylons and high heels. I glanced in the mirror for final approval. Ready for battle, I went out and walked around the corner to the Mayfair Cafe. I took a seat at the counter and the waitress brought me a tuna-fish sandwich with chili sauce on the side, large potato salad, and a cream soda. At the next stool an old man ate silently. I covered my ankles demurely with my coat from the floor beneath the stool.\n* * *\nI worked hard figuring up the license-plate numbers on the cars parked in front of the hotel as I stood in the doorway. I had managed to reduce my theory to practice, and by nine o'clock had figured the license numbers of fifty-nine of the sixty-four cars parked in front of the Lafayette. All but five of the registered owners of the cars were in New York City. I did not bother with the Illinois license numbers. Neither did I bother with the New York State license numbers, since I felt that a man from Buffalo wouldn\u2019t drive all the way to...", "1_9_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six minutes to ten o'clock in the morning</span>. Basil rather ceremoniously snipped the cord and shook it. Nothing fell out, as soundly and firmly as ever the trap was fixed.\nHe shook it again. The tray flipped up and fell over, adding a thump to the applause that was exhilarating.\n\"This one isn\u2019t fixed, either,\" said Basil, snipping off the cord and shaking the tray. He forgot, in the excitement of the moment, that the first time, he had shown to the audience that the tray did not fall over by itself. He put it again to its test as if it were a new one, by shaking it.\nThis time it did fall over.\nIn the ensuing applause, while the members were putting their heads together over this strange new phenomenon, Zetz made his swift escape.\nThe police of eight cities were having a busy morning. In the cities were rumors that a new epidemic of St. Vitus\u2019s dance was on the way. The police were going through all the marihuana and Mexican dream seed dens with samplers and scales, and taking it all in to be analyzed. But their national convention was not until three weeks in May. The underworld had not heard about the Martian poison-pen letters.", "0_6_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-eight in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 and the fully materialized machine that was the mate of the $FIRSTNAMEF0 $SURNAME0 had kept up a steady telepathic yodeling all through the night.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had telepathically yodeled vital instructions to Saturn, who had approached the plaque in the granite cliff countless times, only to skitter away in panic at the last moment.\n$FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 had also telepathically yodeled encouragement to his perhaps three thousand loyal followers on Newport. The three thousand were mostly night watchmen and maintenance men, cleaning women, night managers of refreshment stands and information bureaus, messenger boys, and bona fide blind people.\nThe followers on Newport now lived in exquisite suspense in a blue-and-gold mansion a couple of blocks from the Palace of Agricultural History. The mansion, vulgarly known as the $SURNAME0 estate, had a yachting pond, a croquet lawn, and a hedge.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s followers were gathered in the salons of the $SURNAME0 estate, waiting to join the followers on the other worlds when the time came. $SURNAME0 could not yodel to the absentee followers, but he had stated...", "2_11_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven thirty-nine in the morning</span> of December 31, 1947.\nThe Ten Commandments, according to the Reverend LoyAL Anderson, stated the conditions under which God would bless the endeavors of man in Balaklava. Those conditions were:\n1. Man shalt love Big Brother.\n2. Thou shalt not kill.\n3. Thou shalt not steal.\n4. Thou shalt not furnish False Data to the Ministry of Love.\n5. Thou shalt not violate the sexual purity of thy comrade.\n6. Thou shalt not be guilty of thought-crime.\n7. Thou shalt not lay hands unblindfolded on thy comrade\u2019s possessions.\n8. Thou shalt not covet thy comrade\u2019s wife.\n9. Thou shalt not be envious of thy comrade\u2019s achievements.\n10. Thou shalt not place conditions upon thy love.\nSometimes the inhabitants of the space station whispered the commandments to themselves. Whispering the commandments helped to remind them that there was drama in their lives, after all\u2014helped to strengthen their faith in Orders.", "1_22_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten twenty-one in the evening</span>. The message was primarily a report on the meeting. Having composed it, $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 assumed that he would now have time for a bath, clean clothes, and some solid food for an encore. He would have time for a few explorations of the house and neighborhood before midnight.\nBack at the space ship by twelve, he would let the motorwarm and think up some trivial errands for himself\u2014escort a guest to a phone, personally inspect a delivery of flowers\u2014that would get him out of the house and away from $SURNAME0\u2019s treacherously contagious influence for the rest of his stay onEarth.\nAt ten twenty-five, $FIRSTNAMEF3 $SURNAME1 was in the master bathroom of four, with the light off, his clothes off, and the window open. He had put his clothing out on the chaise longue, and a breeze had caught it and flapped it in bluish billows.\nThe breeze had also brought a message, so faint and so high that it could not be heard.\nThe message was filtered through the molecules of glass, molecules of air, molecules of human skin, and was so faint and so high as to be only a tingling of the skin.\nThe message was that $SURNAME0 had...", "2_11_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe place was the upper village of Phoebe.\nThe place was interesting in its own right, and at the same time it had a symbolic value for $SURNAME0. It was, in the unmodified sense of the word, a paradise.\nIt was the only one of the thirty Sun- villages on Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn to which official permission had been granted by the Committee on Evolutionary Monism. $SURNAME0 was not bitter about having been passed over by the committee, but he enjoyed thumbing his nose at them by appearing in a place to which they objected.\nMonism was the official state religion, and the committee's attitude toward $SURNAME0\u2019s materializations was purely scientific. Their objection to the places he materialized in had nothing to do with theology.\nThe committee simply maintained that the places had factors in common that were thoroughly dull.\nTo wit: in all the places, time moved at only one speed, and the rate of flow in time was exquisitely regular\u2014the space between events was constant and unbranching. Thus the only frame of reference was an unchanging, uninteresting now.", "2_20_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-one in the evening</span>.\nAt eight forty-two in the evening, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the boy stopped under a pine tree and listened to the radio. At eight forty-two, every radio in rebel hands was turned on to an outside wave band\u2014 and this continued to be true until the last act of the drama.\nAt eight forty-three in the evening, $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the boy were in an irrigation ditch behind a building in Yellow Wing. At eight forty-three every pistard in the canyon landed behind the buildings in Yellow Wing.\nAt twenty past nine, every building in the canyon was surrounded. At ten thirty-eight, the first Earthling was crushed under the first Martian landing. This Earthling was $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s principal private investigator. The investigator was alone in the men\u2019s room of the company day-care center when the building was surrounded. He stopped shaving and went to the window exactly as $FIRSTNAMEB0 himself would have done.\nHe was greeted by Stoney\u2019s Daddy-O, besides being riddled with Martian landing-craft needles.\nHe made four authentic squeezes with his six-shooter before falling in a drum of day-care milk.\nThe Ultimate Question", "2_5_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five twenty-four in the morning</span> when our Great Red Shark lunged down the runway and flew out over the evil LA clouds. What year is this? Who knows? Not me, usually\u2014and I make it a point to know as little as possible about everything. As far as I can tell, it's always 1949...especially in my head: The head is the perfect place to be anyway, especially in L.A. in the year of \u2019Nam and Nixon...with the drugs and the apathy and the desperate anger all around us.\nGuns lurched us into the air while the runways disappeared under our wheels. Nineteen, forty-nine. Doomed. Doomed.\nImmediately, bad vibrations. Something was drastically wrong with the Red Shark, a horrible wrenching...then a stench of raw gasoline, wrenching again, and for a long time a cloud of black smoke pouring out of the tailpipe. I slowed back to 140, afraid to go any faster for fear of blowing the goddamn thing to bits. Glancing nervously at the fuel gauge I saw that we were back under half a tank...so we\u2019d burned over a quarter of our supply in only a few minutes.", "1_19_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven twenty-three in the evening</span>.\n\"If you\u2019ll just sign these cards,\" said the young woman, \"indicating what you\u2019d like for dinner for\u2014\"\n\"I\u2019m not staying,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF1.\n\"Please?\" said the young woman. \"Just write here, and initial there, and we\u2019ll say you called and canceled.\"\n\"I\u2019m afraid I\u2019ll have to trouble you to call the police. I need an escort from your premises, to protect my rights and my property-interests, immediately.\"\n\"Protect your what?\" said the young woman, who suddenly looked nineteen again instead of twenty-nine.\n\"My rights. Don\u2019t get fitted for handcuffs, little lady; I\u2019m not a dope fiend. I intend to spend the night at the Grand Hotel in Newport. That I\u2019ll pay for.\"\n\"The Grand Hotel? You mean the\u2014\"\n\"Yes!\"\n\"Good Lord!\"\n\"Is that a problem?\"\n\"Well, you know\u2014\"\n\"Listen seriously and maturely, please. To keep this entire transaction efficient, we need to stay in control at all times. Do you prefer to place your little firm in receivership, one...", "2_6_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six twenty-four in the morning</span>.\nGreat cries went up. From every direction they came, and the ragged line of the procession grew longer and longer. Thousands and thousands of Tinians emerged from their caves, emerged from their huts. All had seen the flash of the executed soldiers\u2019 rifles, had heard the distant pops of the youngsters\u2019 stun-guns. They were confident that the four had been executed for firing on their beloved nonviolent $FIRSTNAMEM2.\nA voice cried out from the far end of the long line. The cry was taken up and passed from lip to lip.\n\"$FIRSTNAMEM2! $FIRSTNAMEM2!\" the cry went up, and grew louder.\nIt passed from Lip to Lip and grew louder still. Soon the whole parade was shouting \"$FIRSTNAMEM2! $FIRSTNAMEM2!\"\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 was confused by the pressure of the crowd on all sides. Suddenly, sadly, a gap opened before $FIRSTNAMEB0 and the crowd closed behind him. He was marching with the procession again.\nThe instant of comprehension was gone.\nAnd over and over again the word cycle that had led to the big moment kept oc curring to $FIRSTNAMEB0 and yielding nothing.\n\"Me liking be\u2014 \"\n\"Me liking be me liking.\"", "2_3_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute past three o'clock in the morning</span>, Greenwich mean time.\n$SURNAME0 and his dog were caught in the beam of a searchlight, their gilded statue misty in the fog. The beam, counterweighted, was pointing straight down at them.\nThe Statue of Liberty was right behind $SURNAME0, her copper hand reaching out to rap him on the on the shoulder.\n\"Bork!\" said the dog.\n\"She does that occasionally\u2014has the same sound she used to have when Skip was a pup,\" said $SURNAME0 conversationally. \"It means she\u2019s lonely.\"\n$SURNAME0 did not seem particularly lonely.\nAnd he and his dog were not especially startled by the beam. It was as though they had been expecting it. The light had caught $SURNAME0 taking his exercise, and he still had his rubber band around one ankle.\nOld Salo, meanwhile, crouched in the cone of the searchlight, waiting to be spoken to. And waiting. And waiting.\nBilly had gone home from the cocktail party, saying he thought his mother might need him.", "1_19_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven thirty-four in the evening</span>. The place was $SURNAME0\u2019s mogul, on a slope of Mount $SURNAME0. The sky was a marbleized green. The air was a glass of ti-tor parfait.\nThe temperature was a little under sixty-eight degrees Fahrenheit. The humidity was a perfectly dry fourteen percent.\nThis perfectly behaved machine that housed $FIRSTNAMEF0 and $SURNAME1 was one in a fleet of similar machines, all of them empty, all of them serviced by the same attendants. The fleet was rearranged on $SURNAME0\u2019s orders once a month, so that no one in it would come to know his machine or his place in the apparent order of things.\nEvery attendant in the palace was a harmonium. They were a kazoo-like tribe of Mars-like creatures, bearded and brown, with six legs and hands like snow-goggles. They carried their hands folded against their chests until needed. To themselves, they were the Last of the Last, the only harmoniums in the universe. Their multiplication by fission had long since ceased, as had their ability to reproduce sexually. The only way new harmoniums could be brought into being would have been by the emergence from hibernation of hibernating males and their...", "2_21_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-seven in the evening</span>.\n/g/\nThat story makes no sense, Lenore.\u201c\n\u201cI know that.\u201d\n\u201c....\u201d\nLenore smoothed her knee under the desk with her hand. The outside light from the hallway seeped into their cubicle, it almost got them. Lenore could not see Mr. Bortman.\n\u201cListen, Rick. If things look strange, well, we\u2019re back to the bad leg story, OK?\u201d\n\u201cI ...\u201d\n\u201cI tell you what. You stay away from R. V. for the next eight hours or so. You respond to messages from me, OK? If there\u2019s an emergency I\u2019ll call you on your beeper. OK? I\u2019ve got a story worked out with Candy, and if you have to go away I\u2019ll tell her to keep things on line. She\u2019s not a trained Perecianist, but I don\u2019t think Gramm is the type to go deeply into his own story.\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you mean, stay away from your brother? He\u2019s your employer.\u201d", "2_0_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to one o'clock in the morning</span>. The cashier, who kept looking into the thick smoke that was swelling and howling in from the parking lot, said, \u201cSomething going on out there?\u201d\n\u201cWhat do you see?\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF2. \u201cI don\u2019t see anything.\u201d\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEF2, and gave him the password. It worked. She got back in the car aware of having spoken a password without knowing what it was.", "1_1_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes past one o'clock in the morning</span>, Sunday, October 11, 1911, when the Caloris1 party arrived at the site of the Schliemann Cabinet of historic curiosities. The party consisted of three men. Two of them, wearing capes and sombreros, led the way. They were the human camels, carrying saddlebags of food, tents, scientific apparatus and so on. The third man, wearing the uniform of a United States Marshal, brought up the rear.\nCamels and Marshal had to pick their way single-file, for no moon illuminated that portion of Mercury for which they headed. Mercury, in fact, proved to be nothing but a blur of light in their rear.\nThe blur of light was the sunlit face of Mercury. The men were approaching its dark side\u2014the side which everywhere gazes permanently into the blackness of space. Their point of landing was an ellipse, whose foci were the poles of Mercury. The sun, with the other planets of the Solar System, revolves about the poles of Mercury.\nThe ellipse was the great Caloris Basin. Its floor was twelve miles above the mean level of the planet's surface. Its open end faced that portion of the planet where perpetual daylight reigns.\nA", "2_18_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six thirty-four in the evening</span>, local time, all over Earth.\nThe news reached Mars two minutes later.\nThe droves of tourists on Mars plunged pell-mell into their space ships and headed home.\nOnly ten of them were left on Mars when the next time-lock opened\u2014and they were not planning to leave.\nThey were eight very specialized technicians and the man they served, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1.\nThe technicians were squatting happily, singing hymns and playing craps.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1, meanwhile, was napping in a contour chair, a chair identical in design to the thousands of contour chairs on Beggars in Spain, a chair that had been smuggled to Mars for $SURNAME1\u2019s use.\nThe technology that had made the chairs so remarkably comfortable also compensated for defects in the skeletal, muscular, and nervous systems of the people who sat in them. $SURNAME1 had arthritis, had muscular and nervous weaknesses and infirmities that would have kept him housebound on Earth. On Mars, the contour chair did whatever had to be done to the body of its occupant so that the occupant would suffer no pain and would have full use of his wasted members.", "1_4_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>. Two minutes after we'd shot the depot, we would breeze right through the sheriff's radio checkup. He would not suspect a thing.\nTen miles up the dark highway, we saw the bulky outlines of the watertank and the radio shack by the side of the road. My attorney slowed down, then roared right past the road, ignoring the radio sign.\n\u201cCut the engine,\u201d I said. \u201cI'll make the call.\"\n\u201cWhat?\u201d he yelled. \u201cWe didn't stop.\"\n\u201cThat's right. Now you back up and drive past real slow, so I can make the ID.\"\n\u201cJesus! Yes, that's the place. Now we're really in trouble. He'll get the make on this car and call in the description before we get to the hotel..\n\u201cThat watertower,\u201d I muttered. \u201cWe can't miss it.\"\n\u201cYou better have that goddamn carburetor fixed,\u201d he said as he awkwardly downshifted, edging the Great Red Shark past the radio shack. I found a pay phone at the back. of the room. I dropped in a dime and dialed.\n\u201cHello?", "0_22_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> of Wednesday, April 28, 1996, $SURNAME1 reminisced. That was when it had happened to him. He had gone to bed like any husband, at half after ten.\nThey had had no children.\n$SURNAME1, patron saint of widowers, took all his meals alone in the Palace dining-room. He felt no impulse whatever to seek out any company for his meals. He could not see himself beginning to make noises with a knife and fork in order to provide someone else with what he needed least in all the worlds, namely silence.\nHe bad food bought to him, had it set down before him, emptied his plate and bowl, and nodded to indicate that he was through.\nHe drank some Burgundy with his meals.\nThere were ten rubies set in the handles of the knives and forks.\nAmong the set of Medieval and Modern History in the Palace library there was a four-volume work by T. W. Burchard, A Short History of Earth. The volumes were ruled in red. $SURNAME1 had read the four volumes, as precisely as a man could read anything ruled in red.\n$SURNAME1 had written no end papers for the Burchard history. He hadn\u2019t sneered at it. He had taken...", "0_13_54": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was six minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span>.\nThere was a knock on the door of Room 93, St. James Hotel. Room 93 was the only room on the corridor that had no visitors.\nA man in the corridor held an envelope in his hand. He looked at the number on the door and then at his watch. He had a face that was all eyes and eyebrows.\nHis hair was all white.\n\"That\u2019s the boy,\" he said.\n\"That\u2019s it,\" he said. Then he smiled. He was nodding his head gravely. \"Twenty-three years, eleven months, and four days.\"\nHis voice was one to which people listened.\nThe knock, on the door, was repeated.\nThe old man let his hand go up, with the envelope in it, very slowly. He opened the door, unfolded the envelope, looked at the message it contained, closed it, put it in his pocket.\nThe two young men in the room got to their feet when he entered.\n\"Here\u2019s someone who wants to shake hands with you,\" said one of the young men. He was tall, with red hair.\n\"This is a great moment for us all,\" said the old man genially.\n\"", "1_6_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six thirty-one in the morning</span> of the day of the Spanish Bombing. It was a very dark dawn. There were no sounds at all. Nothing moved. The Peace of the Bomb hung over everything. The Peace of the Bomb is colder than the Peace of the Grave. In the Pause of the Bomb, the world becomes a truly brutal place.\nBERNICE BOOLER SWUNG AWAY FROM THE BATHTUB, leaving behind her splendid tanned reflection. She was naked except for a large bra. She wore girdle and stockings and garters and high heels. She began to dry herself with a large pink towel.\n\"Kiss me,\" said Miss Fidget to Alvin, opening her eyes fully. Alvin longed to kiss her. He knew that he mustn\u2019t. He put his eyes on her bra.\nThere were, Alvin discovered, whole systems of things he was now discovering to look for in the presence of a woman. Such as\u2014there were her personal smells, for example. Such things as perfume and face cream he rejected, leaving his urgent discoveries for locations nearer the root of life. He was now seeking out the order and rate of disappearance of her natural smells. The sweet smell of the healthy personal skin was rapidly...", "0_15_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three twenty-five in the afternoon</span>, give or take five minutes.\nBeside me was $FIRSTNAMEF1, my wife. On her left was her mother, Nancy Bombecaro, who had been married and divorced three times. On her right was her father, $SURNAME1, who was actually $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s father, not married at the time, and to be married to Nancy later. On $SURNAME1\u2019s right was his partner, Maurice, his romantic and physical partner. And on Maurice\u2019s right was his mother, $FIRSTNAMEF0, who had come east with Maurice, to help him through a time of adjustment, five years previously, and had never returned to California. Their large round table in the restaurant\u2019s large round room was set for dinner.\nWe had ordered a bottle of Chateau Mouton-Rothschild 1945, at two-ninety-five a bottle.\nAt three thirty-nine P.M. there was still no sign of the meal I had ordered an hour and fifteen minutes previously. The ma\u00eetre d\u2019h\u00f4tel, assigned to us, was a man named L'Behears, who announced in a deferential voice that the lamb would be perhaps ten or fifteen minutes further delayed.\nI looked at $FIRSTNAMEF1 and...", "2_19_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was thirteen minutes past seven o'clock in the evening</span>. It was in the most beautiful park in the world.\nA man of medium height and low weight regarded his image in a round, convex mirror.\nThe mirror was set into the back of a white Rolls-Royce limousine. The limousine was parked in a driveway of a mansion in the most beautiful park in the world.\nThis man was $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThis man was a stockholder of the corporation that practically owned Newport, the corporation that controlled the banks and the railroads, the insurance companies and the utilities, the smelting works and the wire mills. This corporation dominated the economy of the state of Rhode Island.\n$SURNAME0 was not an evil man. He was not an unkind man.\nHe was a profoundly socially-minded man, a man whose work gave him little time for private happiness. He was a man who believed in something called noblesse oblige. This put upon him great spiritual obligations toward the happiness of others, toward the making of merry.\nThis belief in noblesse oblige expressed itself in his clothes and a superficial jolliness that gave a perpetual holiday lilt to his walk.", "2_8_55": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes until nine o'clock in the morning</span>, and Bukowski was preparing to follow the last order, remember the last syllable, commit suicide rather than be sent back to Earth as a black, crippled dumpy negation of what a man could be.\nAnd then came the order and the syllable.\n\"Fire One,\" said $SURNAME0.\nIt was not what General George P. Skelton of the Space Cadet Corps had expected.\nHe fumbled the booklet meant to give him instructions on how to die like a gentleman.\nHe dropped it among the folds of his space suit.\nAnd he lost his Mat Su calm. \"Fire one what?\" he wailed.\n\"Fire this! \" said $SURNAME0, and gave him a vicious pinch on the upper right buttock.\nA rubber band bound a paper cylinder to the wrist of $SURNAME0\u2019s right hand.\nThe cylinder was three feet long, and was filled with nothing but the thousand best poems of $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe papers slipped from the space suit of the general of the Space Cadet Corps. He didn\u2019t feel very good about having a disabled soldier\u2019s arm fondling his hind parts so intimately.", "0_5_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nCorporal $FIRSTNAMEB1 had pulled twenty-six point seven kilo-meters in his phase of the night\u2019s work. He closed his eyes and let his thoughts float. He was falling backwards in the parapet, falling with his eyes closed, letting his arms float out and up.\nHis fingers brushed something that was moving up there near the top of his dreams. It brushed his fingertips, softly, tantalizingly.\nHe tried to catch it, to seize it. He could not.\nHe drifted back down into his body.\nHe smiled contentedly to himself. Life was a pretty good thing, anyhow.\nBefore the dawn, Wellington Boone did an unspeakable thing.\nHe would not even describe it to his son $FIRSTNAMEM1 later on. What good would that do? $FIRSTNAMEM1 wasn\u2019t there to see it Do something unspeakable, he told himself, and there\u2019s no one in the whole world who will believe you.\nHe wasn\u2019t sure he believed it himself.\nAt six o\u2019clock Katz woke up. It was light enough to see again. He saw $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s back. $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2019s breathing sounded like the blowing wind.\nIt suddenly...", "1_23_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-eight in the evening</span>. Standing on the corner of Temple and Maple, I was exactly halfway between my suite at the $SURNAME0 and Camp Rehoboth, my one-man parent-soldier encampment.\nTemple Street ended at the corner I was standing on. Maple Street ran west, skirting the hotel and terminating in a circular drive at the west door of the hotel. Washington Street ran east, intersecting Maple on the far side of the hotel. Between Maple and Washington was the west wall of the hotel\u2014a wall six feet thick.\nI ran quietly west on Maple Street, keeping close to the wall. A few feet west of the corner of Maple and Washington, I found a triangular rock in the grassy slope of the Sierra Nevada foothills. It made a perfect step for me to stand on, from which I could see without danger of being seen.\nI got up on the rock, dug my binoculars out of my war-bag, focused them on the swimming pool, seventy yards away.\nThe bathing cabanas on the far side of the pool were the only possible cover for a quiet intruder. I scanned them carefully. Nobody was in them\u2014not even the ones that stood empty.\nI lay down on my stomach and sn...", "1_8_4": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four minutes after eight o'clock in the morning</span>.\nIt would be more precise to say that one second before the alarm sounded in Stone\u2019s Hotel, at 8:04 A.M., a bomb exploded in Room 223 in Wilbur Wheatley\u2019s Building.\nIt exploded in Room 223 of Wilbur Wheatley\u2019s Building, because Wilbur had been in the room when he pushed the red row, and the bomb went off in Room 189 of Kleine\u2019s Buildings, because Vann Duncan had been in Room 189 when he pushed the red row.\nOnly Vann Duncan\u2019s head survived, to be taken to the hospital.\nOnly so much of Wilbur Wheatley as was needed to identify him was left. Neither Wilbur nor the shreds of his walls could be identified.\nThe time was three seconds past eight o\u2019clock, in Wilbur Wheatley\u2019s Building.\nThere wasn\u2019t much left of Stone\u2019s Hotel, and nothing at all left of Stone and his valuable howling-alarm, the alarm that was howling when the bomb went off. The bomb did something to the vibration of Wilbur Wheatley\u2019s Building, and the shock of that something split Wilbur\u2019s building...", "2_3_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past three o'clock in the morning</span>.\nAt the top of the stone tower inside the spiky iron ball was a turntable platform on which the pilot of the antenna ship stood. He was a communications-school graduate whose name was $FIRSTNAMEF1, and he was twenty-five years old.\nIronically, he was standing in exactly the same anatomical position as the God he thought he had up there.\nHis job was simply to aim the antenna at a desired point in space, and to keep the thing aimed. That was all. The mechanism aboard ship did the rest.\nIn point of fact, $FIRSTNAMEF1 was very much like the turntable on which he stood. His function was simple, too. All he had to do was keep pointed the antenna of the converter that drew its power from the prehistoric storage batteries.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1, too, was a part of the uncomplaining machinework. He was oiled, antiseptic, efficient. Memorize the formula for goal-seekng:\nS=E/CWT ...\nS=Surprise. E=Education. C=Chipmunk-like Waltzing Till. WT=Whole Thing.", "2_0_24": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve twenty-four ante meridiem</span>. I did it. I reached Speedmouse just in time, wheeled it out into traffic and moved swiftly out of range. Somewhere up there my attorney was still sitting on the pavement, doubled with laughter. But I didn\u2019t waste much time worrying about that. I had more important things on my mind. I was flying high on all political and moral issues, and the god\u2014damned war in Vietnam was beginning to look unavoidable. All the rest of our problems would be manageable once we got THAT one sorted out.", "1_18_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after six o'clock in the evening</span>. The quartzite crag stood at a height of 3,625,003 feet. The chronometer read 8 \u2022 6.\nThe plateau had been fashioned into a stadium with a capacity for 500,000 spectators.\nEarth held the greatest attendance ever at a farewell.\nThere were 29,985,246 humans on Earth.\nNo one was left behind.\nNo friend in space was forgotten on Earth.\n29,985,246 goodbyes were said.\n8 \u2022 6 on the thirteenth day of August in the year of Our Friendship with Venus.\nSuddenly, silently, 3,627,663 feet above awestruck Newport, the planet Mars appeared.\nMars was a flawless satellite of friendship, a moving oasis, a rich red ruby among the turquoise sky.\nFriendship shimmered among the timing devices.\nPeter Candle Diego up, a professor of English literature at Columbus State University in Columbus, Ohio, U.S.A. Uncounterpoint was in his secret heart. One day he would master Bach. One day he would play and sing Bach all day.\nUncounterpoint loved the place where he and $FIRSTNAMEF0 had raised their eight children. He hated to leave Newport.\n$FIRSTNAMEF0 hated...", "0_4_25": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-five in the morning</span>. The parking lot of the bar was several miles up Route 1, on the edge of the city. In half an hour it would be daybreak. Vance was on his way home.\nFrom the parking lot I could see into the serving window where the girls took your order. There were still two serving girls inside, trapped behind the dirty glass like doomed goldfish as dawn started to come through the highway mist. One had slept on a couch in back; the other was desperately trying to wash her hair in the ladies\u2019 room sink. I remembered her vividly. She had gorgeous legs and talked to me about horses. Her name was Wanda.\nThe saint of New York City is St. Louise de Machel. Her picture hangs in every pub, most of the time above the urinal. Below the urinal is usually a box ofDDT. Beside the urinal is a box of Kleenex.\nSt. Louise is dressed as an eighteenth-century harlot. Her hair is standing straight up as if she had just removed a silly hat, and she wears a garment that is supposed to be a loose gown but which clings to and emphasizes the anatomical portions of her body. The plaster louche bag is regarded as a...", "1_18_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six o'clock in the evening</span> when the Cathedral was getting dark. There was a blaze on the high altar and the thunder was rolling away, but the rain was coming down hard. There were plenty of people in the cathedral. We sat in a pew right at the back. I watched the crowd. There were plenty of women. I suppose for every fifty men there were a hundred women. It was a swell crowd. Everybody had on top-coats and kid gloves. There were fat women and big men, but the women were the big lookers. They had big shoulders and chests and hair piled way up. They stuck out. Maybe their Buster Keaton necks were scared stiff with all that hair.\nWhat's the matter with me? I'm not the kind that gets impressed by a big crowd. Maybe it was what $FIRSTNAMEB5 had said about the soldiers. I watched the crowd filing into the balcony. Some of them carried flowers wrapped in paper. $FIRSTNAMEB5 had a bunch. I had an extra cigarette-case. I lit a cigarette and opened the cigarette-case and snapped it to see it make a snap. $FIRSTNAMEB5 watched it and smiled. \u201cAll ready, darling?\u201d\n\u201cYes, darling.\u201d\nThe bell clanged slowly. People took off their hats...", "0_15_56": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four minutes until four o'clock in the afternoon</span> in Kane's room on the forty-sixth floor of the Robot House. A garish late-afternoon light shone through the single window. Kane, sitting on his rumpled bed, had curiously twisted both legs around the right leg of his chair, so that he was sitting all of a piece, staring intently at the wall. The wall had once been pale blue, and there was a pattern of pale blue flowers. A six by nine-inch section of the wall was missing in the middle, to reveal the square chamber that housed the time-energy transmitter.\nKane carefully unscrewed the section of wall. He reached inside the wall and attached to the transmitting machine a long, coiled wire. The other end of the wire was inside the opened suitcase on the bed. The suitcase was full of live, hissing cockroaches.\n\"Hello\u2014here!\" a man said, staring at the wall.\nKane yanked at the wire, and the man in the wall jerked wildly for a moment, as if dancing to phantom fiddlers three feet above the floor.\n\"Hello-o-o!\" he cried. \"Help!\"\n\"Yeah, go on and laugh,\" said Kane. He touched a switch below...", "2_3_7": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was seven minutes past three o'clock in the morning</span>. In half an hour, the sun would be up. Telescopic panels, triggered to a radio signal, would slip down over the fountain, turn it to scrap metal.\nComplicated, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 thought, looking up at the fountain. His eyes adjusted to the moonlight, and he could see individual birds frozen among the branches, flocking there on the last day of their earthly lives\u2014cardinal, woodpecker, raven, starling, sparrow, seagull.\nThe birds weren\u2019t the only things frozen in the fountain\u2014$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was frozen there, too.\nHe was a six-foot-one, thirty-two-year-old man whose close-cropped hair had attained a reddish-gold color. He wore a soft, handsome Karakuli turban, a silk twill space suit, and sash-bound, beige su\u00e8de space boots. His shoulder flash consisted of a Yosemite waterfall seen through a solar flare.\nA sticker on $SURNAME1\u2019s shoulder indicated his destination. It showed a palm tree fuming in the background, a moon setting in the foreground. The GI sticker said:\nWELCOME TO THE GREEN RAGON FLYING MONEY", "1_18_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes until seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nThe story of the $SURNAME0 Fountain began one day when $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 was a ten-year-old boy in overalls in the back yard of his parents\u2019Home on Mars.\nIt was the springtime of Mars, and the yard was small, warm, fragrant and moist, bounded on one side by the long bottom of a chilled-water canal.\n$SURNAME0 sat on a moist bottom board, watching crystals form on the inside of a rectangular box filled with a dilute solution of nitrates, sulfates and chlorides.\nIn his right hand, also, was a small clay model of a spiral shell.\nIn the real shells that periodically appeared in the canal near the back-yard fence, the month\u2019s Mars rainfall was collected.\nThere was a great call for such shells among the housewives of Mars\u2014for watering the houseplants.\nThe shells grew larger and larger and more and morebeautiful until their contents were spent. One such spent shell now formed the bottom and part of the walls of the box that was bringing precipitation to life inside it.\nMan\u2019s constant demand for more shells had permitted Martian sedimentation to become a highly developed art. The native supply...", "2_18_52": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eight minutes to seven o'clock in the evening</span>. Earthling Time.\nThere was no person anywhere in the Universe who knew quite what to make of the fantastic display in the sky above the Martian desert.\nCertainly, no one could guess the truth of the matter.\nThat a woman and her child, exploring Mars in a flying saucer, had been forced to blast off hastily; that they had left behind them a perfectly accurate facsimile of their house, complete to brown sand in the yard and canned food in the closet.\nThat another woman and her child had been picked up by the flying saucer and left on Mars to explore it with their two space suits.\nOr that these two children\u2014and a dog\u2014and two goldfish\u2014had been given, for their stay on Mars, the most encyclopedic possible education in the ideas of love, loyalty, plastic, advertising and all the ideas that make an Earthling.\nFor it was not true that all of the thoughts, ideas and passions that created the Universe came from the various Universes of Time and Space. Nor was it true that there were other worlds in the Universe inhabited by beings that thought and felt much like Earthlings.\nThe histories of the Universe were uncertain.", "1_0_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve thirty-four ante meridiem</span>.\nIt was a good time to go to Brooplans\u2019.\nFLAMING youth!\u2014Young Herbert Yardley, who sobbed so wretchedly over his tokamak, was twenty-four. He was an underling programmer in a calculating service corporation in Paterson, New Jersey. He was not the top-line programmer: that honor fell to Alfred N. Putney of Poughkeepsie, who was a year younger.\nPutney had recently discovered the same great truth that Yardley was only now plunging toward like a besotted explorer in steam-conquered jungle, in sinuous and wild-ripe thickets: namely, that Putney was the Numerical Wilbur Whateley of his age and place\u2014genius personified, the hub and essential, the pale lamp of perfect and synthetic reason. And Putney, the lucky stiff, was already on his way. He was scheduled for transfer to Wilmurt, Connecticut, where a fabulously complex new calculating machine had been installed. Wilmurt was a short step toward the pinnacle. After a few more years as a Wilbur Whateley\u2014and Putney, for all his modest demeanor, meant to be a pure...", "2_3_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-two in the morning</span>.\nThe residents were two soundstages and a prop room away from their Nurse.\nIn the dark bedroom deep in the bowels of the original Ennet House, the snores of resident counsel Francis Abington Twillinger strangled for just a second and resumed. He woke up seven minutes later. Before going back to sleep, he remembered a terrible dream he had had.\nIn the dark bedroom deep in the bowels of the original Ennet House, an interesting idea occurred to resident Alison Gauss.\nDay Dining Duds.\nConroystudio, Lenox, Massachusetts.c. 1985-1990. 2\u00bd x 5\u00bd Post Card.\nPerhaps the persistence of his memory of the woman with the wart beside her mouth was both not only because she was so incredibly young\u2014but because she was someone the Bomb had never happened to. There were lots of persons like that even in 1980: people not quite old enough to have adapted themselves to the Bomb\u2019s times, not quite shell-protected. That she was with a little boy helped\u2014it saved her from being tragically beautiful and a figure of loss, a woman alone. Because she was with a child, she was hopeful. And hopefulness is more interesting.", "1_8_34": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight thirty-four in the morning</span>. The place was the drawing room of $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s apartment. The show-down was about to come on.\n\"Good Lord Almighty...this is the one, this is the one,\" $FIRSTNAMEF1 said.\nA space had been cleared in the middle of the drawing room. In this space now\u2014in the precisely correct ritual manner\u2014$FIRSTNAMEF1 placed the box which she had been carrying.\nAlvin, too, had been given a box.\nThe show was about to come on. Just a minute ago\u2014by mutual unspoken agreement\u2014$FIRSTNAMEF1 and Alvin had finished cleaning themselves. It had become a matter of urgent importance that they see this thing through with a clean and perfect composure.\nThe show was about to come on. In the box that $FIRSTNAMEF1 had just placed was the transmitter that fed $FIRSTNAMEF0-load\u2019s processor with the information it needed to know. And in Alvin\u2019s box was the receiver with which his nervous system was connected to that transmitter.\nIn the whole universe, there were now only three people\u2014and only two of them were qu hentiffiw af ferently alive. $FIRSTNAMEF1 stood before her box and looked at it. Alvin stood before his box and looked at it.\nThe show was about to come...", "1_22_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten twenty-seven in the evening</span> when Redwine\u2019s sermon ended. The sermon had been interrupted by a clumsy attempts at invasion by a group of Satanists who, for religious reasons of their own, wanted to mess around with Redwine\u2019s life.\nThere were five Satanists, and they had slipped into $FIRSTNAMEF1\u2019s and Lime\u2019s Porsches, to follow Redwine\u2019s green sedan quietly into the countryside.\n$FIRSTNAMEF1 had seriously considered running them off the road. Had they been real pilgrims, she would undoubtedly have done it. As it was, she was glad to see them go. They were less sanitary than real pilgrims, who \u2014 in their natural state \u2014 bathed frequently, and brushed their hair, and wore laundered clothes, and smelled sweet.\nThe Satanists were a scruffier, more sinister crowd. It was as if they\u2019d gathered their wardrobe at a fire sale in Hell. Nor had they bathed in weeks. When they broke into Redwine\u2019s services, the first thing they\u2019d do would be to put their grubby paws all over the inside of the car.\nSo it was lucky for everybody except the Satanists that the thin-limbed boy in the pouring rain had seen...", "2_5_31": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five thirty-one in the morning</span>. The sun came up. \u201cLet me fly higher,\u201d I whispered. \u201cHow high can you fly?\u201d she whispered back. \u201cHow high can we go, honey?\u201d", "0_3_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was half past three o'clock in the morning</span>. I realized this because outside the rattle of trolley-buses, which was their shift changing, had stopped, and in the silence I could hear all our clocks striking. Footsteps faded in the corridor and the stair-door banged. So finally the night porter had come on duty. There was a key in the door, but $FIRSTNAMEB5 did not stir. She was asleep on the bed, on her side, one arm under her. I stood still and looked at her. She had not been so close for so long, and the relief of it, the strange feeling that I could touch her, that I could have put my hand on her bare skin and it would not have been strange, made me want to cry. She stirred slightly and moaned a little as I stood looking at her. I stepped quietly to the bed, picked up my coat and hat, and went into the other room, and closing the door, got my bag and went into the bathroom and packed my valise. My toilet things and shaving things I locked in the valise and put the bag in the bedroom. I did not stop to pack anything else. I did not want to go into the other room again. I wanted to leave this flat where we had been...", "1_13_50": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten minutes to two o'clock in the afternoon</span> of May 5, 1943. In the airy upper room where the rusty fans turned slowly overhead, $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 lay in his contour chair, his head on the pillows, his feet on the pedals. His face shone with velvet brownness, and he seemed to be drooling. This was not so. What seemed to be saliva on the right side of $SURNAME0\u2019s mouth was only Malvinclear mucilage dropped there by a small roller bearing factory in $SURNAME0\u2019s right cheek. The factory went to work whenever $SURNAME0 was emotionally agitated. It was discharging a vile-tasting by-product of the synthetic-rubber manufacture.\nThe mucilage had been appearing and disappearing on $SURNAME0\u2019s face for as long as he and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had been appearing and disappearing on the lovely silent page that had been sent earthward with them. This page was now part of the sum of knowledge on every earthling child, and children on thousands of colonized planets as well. The millions who had loved and pitied and wondered at $SURNAME0 and $FIRSTNAMEB4 had seen $SURNAME0 and his dog only in that one painting. It had never been challenged, thanks to the press agents of...", "1_4_32": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four thirty-two in the morning</span> on October 11, 1982, and the place was the lobby of a Las Vegas hotel, and the event was a nasty encounter between a well-known sportswriter and a member of the White House staff. The tale begins at the roulette table in the back of the \u201cAmerican Roulette\u201d section of the casino\u2014that\u2019s the Farris wheel without the bicycles. My attorney liked to play back there because it stank like a rotten zucchini. We weren\u2019t alone. The place was crawling with degenerates who also liked the odor...people who have no homes, who wander the earth looking for a place that smells worse than any place they\u2019ve been so far. There\u2019s no shortage of candidates. These people are easy to recognize, because they wear driver\u2019s hats and other clothing that clearly adheres to the Stay Behind policy of the Nixon administration. They sit at the slot machines and play for hours with the slow, white blink of a Dopey grin moving across their faces whenever a soulicide machine coughs up a coin for them to lose. I wanted to slap their faces and make some other personal remarks, Advice to newcomers: Never play the slots unless you\u2019re willing to lay...", "1_17_20": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty in the afternoon</span>. It was hot. The electric sign in the window of the drugstore opposite the barber shop declared that it was hot. Venusberg sat in the barber chair. At the far end of the counter behind the row of soda-fountain stools was a strawby, apple-cheeked girl with a pert nose. She was pretty. It was a perfectly good nose, and she had nice hair, too.\n\"Well,\" said Wilbur M. Biggs, the poet, \"I guess you gotta make do with what you\u2019re given.\"\nMacdonald Coin told the newsdealer to call the girl at the fountain.\n\"Stanley,\" he said to the newsdealer, \"go over there and tell that young lady she has a phone call at the barber-shop phone.\"\n\"Right.\"\nThe newsdealer crossed the street.\n\"I guess you know my youngest brother knows Theodore Roethke pretty well.\"\nVenusberg wrinkled his forehead.\n\"Can\u2019t say I do. Big poet.\"\n\"Un-huh.\"\nVenusberg leaned back under Biggs\u2019s experienced hand.\n\"I guess Roethke must have a pretty mean circle...", "0_11_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes past eleven o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThirty-four minutes later, at eleven forty-six and thirty seconds, $SURNAME1 made a startling discovery.\nThere was something in his crib with him\u2014this a scant eight minutes after the nurse had fed him.\nThe presence of the thing in the crib with him ended any thought or possibility of his falling asleep again. He was wide awake in a twinkling, with his eyes as big as saucers in his head.\nWhat he had found in his crib was an ugly, naked creature that looked like the devil in Walt Disney cartoons. The creature stared accuserly at $SURNAME1.\n$SURNAME1 could well believe it was the devil\u2014or one of the devil\u2019s minions. The unconscious suggestion of that notion was the most horrible part of the encounter.\nWhat had happened was this. At eleven forty-six, just thirty seconds before $SURNAME1 had made his startling discovery, $FIRSTNAMEB1 had spirited the stowaway out of the nursery and down a coal chute.\nIt wasn\u2019t that $FIRSTNAMEB1 had any qualms about abducting another $FIRSTNAMEM2\u2014he had done it before. Usually, his victims were sold as slave labor in Antarctica or hired out as sexual partners to the young women in the municipal zoo.\nIn $SURNAME1...", "0_22_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten nineteen in the evening</span>, and I'd been sitting in the chair in my apartment for two minutes. Outside on the street it was warm and pleasant, with the kind of light Los Angeles spring evening that looked good when you saw it in the postcards. Across the street and two buildings up a group of children were playing in a grassy area. But I knew that if I looked at those children I wouldn't be able to stand it. So I kept my eyes on the rug in the living room, on a little black whip knotted into the rug's wool, a whip about six inches long. I knew if I looked at that whip I would go mad, or lose control of myself in some way, so I looked at it out of the corner of my eyes, and it was just barely possible to bear it. I knew if I looked at my watch I'd scream, so I obsessed on that whip in the rug, and reminded myself continually that only another minute had to pass before I could get up and walk out of that apartment. There it was, another minute gone, and now but nine more to go-it would be all right, it would be all right now if I could just get to the door, open the door and walk out, down the hall,...", "0_4_28": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was four twenty-eight in the morning</span>. I felt primeval. I felt that the Hollywood hills were huge black waves that might advance and break on me at any moment and bury me forever in their shifting black tangle of vine and bramble and flowers. A ghost wind blew in from the desert and touched me, and I shuddered. Its touch was the light, hot finger of the god of the oncoming and increasing awfulness of whatever the dawn was going to bring. My spon taneous and total affection for Desi and Lucy was there to greet it like a rose, like a fat juicy berry on a thorned, pricking doghouse of doubts and sexual protean spectre-ups and anality and failure and shit. I needed a car. I felt a deep need for a car, now, immediately--and I knew that my failure to get a car, and soon, might have fatal consequences. I was in perfect health, and full vigor, and trained in all the deadly arts, and armed to the teeth --yet I knew I might soon be in the position of Forrest, the great beaten Army general, who out of fear of being captured lay hidden in an Alabama ditch while the victorious enemy column marched past only a few feet away. Desi...", "0_9_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine thirty-three in the morning</span>. From where I squatted, I could see out past the picnic tables to the road. Our Olds Ninety-eight convertible was pulled up in front of the restaurant, near the street. It was the only car there, the only car in sight, except for a yellow truck in the middle distance that said FRAZEE\u2019S FURNITURE on the side. The freeway in both directions was solid white\u2014smog.\nWe had come out here to the airport to eat breakfast. We had picked this place because a map in the tourist bureau parking lot in Malibu had said it was good. The place looked promising: it was hidden in the middle of a cluster of industrial buildings in the airplane part of town. The place had been one of those old chili\u2014dog roadhouses back in the thirties, and they had kept the stucco and the red tile, and the menu had original items like enchilada stacks and cheese chili pie and mangoneadas. As we drove in, Remi thought it was closed because all the tables were empty, but inside it was just getting started. It turned out to be the only joint you could get breakfast reasonably close to the airport, and the only one that appealed to...", "1_15_36": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three thirty-six in the afternoon</span>. The sirens were screaming at a distance. The afternoon was soft and full of a quiet, high tension.\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 came in the door and found her husband seated, staring at the TV, which was silently dead. She could not see his face.\n\u201cWinch,\u201d she said, tentative.\nHeavy blue defeat outlined his head. \u201cThey cancelled it.\u201d\n\u201c \u2018Thiank you.\u2019 \u201d \u201cThey didn\u2019t even show an episode. They ran movies of the pilot episodes of some other shows. Time runs very differently over there, at the terminal end of a pilot episode. They can spend forty minutes turning a hopefully witty remark, or explaining how a character feels. But at the end nothing has happened.\u201d\n$FIRSTNAMEF2 wandered into the kitchen, started in on her stew again. \u201cBut your show,\u2019\u2019 she said after a bit, \u201cit\u2019s about old movies, right? You could fill in with your own ideas.\u201d That had been his castration fantasy, after all.\nHe rounded on her. \u201cEmpty the channel. Pick some set of opinions, and fill it 24 hours...", "1_22_11": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was eleven minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB2 snatched the Time Bomb from young $SURNAME0\u2019s hand. $FIRSTNAMEB2 was now so cruelly well-trained that he did not even bother to use his home-made tongs. He had developed a technique for seizing a Time Bomb bare-handed. It was brutal, but it was faster.\n\"Who\u2019s there?\" said $SURNAME0 groggily.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 was out of sight before $SURNAME0 could look up and see him. He was at the head of the spiral way in the grand ballroom now. He was headed downward toward the caves and the boats.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 flung the iron doors wide.\nIt was dark outside the mansion. The island was choking with jungle and rotting underbrush.\nStraight down a flight of steps led to the lily-pond pier where lay the morale boat in which $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 had arrived. The motor of $SURNAME1\u2019s boat was still idling quietly.\nTwo hundred and fourteen steps from the bottom, $FIRSTNAMEB2 came to the open, rusting morale ship, stepped in, ran to the controls. The name on the bow of the morale ship was Haggard Dorothy.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 took the automatic helm, and...", "0_12_48": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve forty-eight p.m.</span> when we finally got under way north on King Street, and twenty-five minutes later when, cruising slowly along Petaluma Boulevard, big frustrated Bo of the horrible laugh spotted a love-sequence opportunity. A grand example of a majestic young hipster walking all alone in the warm California sun, with his bookbag and his horn-rimmed glasses and his school-issue tweed sportcoat, across a street that had no traffic on it, and in fact a stop sign at the far corner so that he had to wait; doubtless he was late by now for class, school had started just a week before. It was a touch. The grand cameo of hipster loneliness exploded in Bo's nervous system. Immediately the kid's fate was sealed: he was indeed going to get smashed righteously, right in the belly, by a good clean football tackle by a mad bull of sex. We were off the sidewalk, and going across, and doing forty when we hit. The kid tried to leap aside, dropping his bookbag, spilling papers. I felt the sickest, almost sober, clutch in my toes and abdomens as the kid recoiled against the invisible hood of the car, and only a skid saved him from going under. I braked...", "0_17_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was five nineteen in the afternoon</span>, and the place was almost certainly Earth, since the rock and the photo were both quite obviously Earth-type materials. The place could have been old Terra itself-the picture was too blurred for any real certainty-since the olive-drab color and the classic A.E.F. helmet were both hallmarks of anything Earthly.\nAnd the person in the picture, or the ideal for a person, was anything but Earthly. He was certifiably Martian, its glorious color and clarity befudling the eye till even $FIRSTNAMEB0 wished he could look like that.\nThe face in the picture had a Martian\u2019s commanding beauty, but the clothes in the picture had style!\u2014a wishful blend of ancient uniforms. The man in the picture looked as though he was made of light, so clean and well brushed were his clothes. He wore puttees, an A.E.F. helmet, a blue British Naval airman\u2019s coat, a Sam Browne belt, police knee-pads worn outside his pants, a Colt revolver in a holster worn thumb-forward, and white spats.\nThe man looked solemn, unconcerned about being bald. He looked as though he were about to say something important and...", "1_13_18": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one eighteen in the afternoon</span>. $SURNAME1\u2019s watch had stopped. He had owned the watch for only three days. He had bought it when he first arrived in Marsport because he was worried about catching the right rocket ship out.\nWhat a naive glorious young fool he had been in those days\u2014a month ago.\nNow he had not even the fare back to Earth. If he went to a ticket booth and bought a ticket, he would be questioned and probed, and maybe arrested. He was under indictment back home on aggravated charges.\nAnd he had slept with a twelve-year-old redheaded virgin on his last night in $FIRSTNAMEM2, Arkansas, Earth. And she had told her mother all about it, although what was told and what actually happened were two different things.\nHe had telephoned her once in Memphis, Tennessee, U.S.A., but the mother had been on the line, listening, and he had hung up. He had written to her in Fayetteville, Arkansas, U.S.A., but the letter had been returned to him by the postmaster at $FIRSTNAMEM2, Arkansas, U.S.A., with a notation:\nYOUR ADDRESS IN\nFAILING TO\nCHECK WITH\nAUTH...", "2_9_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine sixteen in the morning</span>, Eastern Standard Time, the Earth. Sixty-six million Americans were watching television.\nIt was nine thirty-seven in the morning, M.T.\nIt was nine fifty-five in the morning, M.T.\nIt was nine fifty-eight in the morning, M.T.\n$FIRSTNAMEB4 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were not beautiful when they emerged into the crisp days of Old Mrs. $SURNAME0\u2019s best wet dream. $FIRSTNAMEB4 was a golden sphere with pink eyes, a red tongue, and a vestigial leg and wing. $FIRSTNAMEB1 was an earthy teardrop, sagging, slow, blind, and shackled to a golden ball.", "2_5_53": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was seven minutes until six o'clock in the morning</span>. \u201cWe\u2019ll have to call off the rest of the LSD experiment,\u201d I said. \u201cAt the rate we\u2019re going we\u2019ll be too far out by noon to gain anything from the rest of this bottle we have upstairs.\"\n\u201cYou\u2019re right!\u201d she cried\u2014it seemed impulsively, as if she had only that moment realized it and felt like sharing the thought. \u201cAlthough it\u2019s all seeming so blissful and mellow right now, we\u2019re way behind schedule.\u201d Her face seemed to grow sad, at the thought. \u201cWe\u2019re really lagging,\u201d she said sadly. \u201cWe should have been out of here hours ago.\u201d\n\u201cUmmm,\u201d I grunted. I couldn\u2019t read her.", "1_11_5": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five minutes after eleven o'clock in the morning</span> and Radar O'Reilly was sitting at a table in the Red Cross library, drawing diagrams of what an Indianapolis 500 racing car looked like. He had never seen one, except in pictures, but he knew $FIRSTNAMEM1 Smith had told him once that that was what it looked like. Under the drawings he printed carefully, with exact details and dimensions, all he could remember. On the facing page he wrote methodically, as though at some future time he would be able to add to what he had written:\nThe symptoms that Faithful could see were always the same.\n1. They began, no one knew how, in the high-flying jet stream aloft.\n2. They were smoky, filmy, slender lashings of opalescent film, glowing violet sometimes, and seeming to flowered into flowers\n3. They were angelic beings, some three feet tall, with insect wings, garbed in orange robes, with golden halos, walking on the air\n4. The angels sang. Their song was Do You Love Me? over and over again\n5. The song, the sight of the angels, caused the breakdown of every single military communications network known to man.\n6. It still wasn't known why\n7. The angels...", "2_4_45": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was quarter to five o'clock in the morning</span>. The first few stars were making a faint appearance in an ink\u2014gray sky. I felt sick and smoky. The air in the apartment hung at ninety. I got up.\n\"Where you going?\"\n\"Clear out.\"\n\"Where you going?\"\n\"Go home.\"\n\"Come on! $FIRSTNAMEB3! What\u2019s he doing?\"\n\"No.\"\n\"Come on, sing! $FIRSTNAMEB3!\"\n\"No. No.\"\n\"$FIRSTNAMEB3!\"\n\"No!\"\n\"Where\u2019s $FIRSTNAMEB3?\"\n\"Home.\"\n\"I\u2019ll trace him home!\" said Galatea.\nA fiendish plot brewed in the air.\n\"I know where he is!\"\n\"Sing! $FIRSTNAMEB3!\"\nI ran down the stairs and got out of that house and into my car and started the motor. I saw five or six mad figures rushing out of the door and trying to get their motor-cycles. I stepped on the gas and zoomed away.", "2_1_21": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one twenty-one in the morning</span>, according to the Sukhroot on the coffee table.\n$SURNAME1 shut his watch, to remind himself that he no longer measured his life in minutes.\nThe watch had a radiant face. $SURNAME1 stared into its radiant face. He liked to read by his watch. Its face was of a pleasing blue that matched the luminous color of the open Pacific on clear nights.\nStonehenge by moonlight.\nThe kiss he had just shared with his wife, $FIRSTNAMEF0, had been so savage and shameful a kiss, so astonishing a revelation of peacetime passion for peace, that he was sure he would remember it for the rest of his life.\nMagnified now into long-range significance by its contrast with the counting of minutes, the kiss seemed to contain all the terror and beauty of an apocalyptic vision, a vision of the heart, a final revelation.\n\"It was fun,\" $SURNAME1 said aloud, after he had counted to twenty. \"Good-by.\" This was addressed to the memory of the kiss. He was letting it go now, with no intention of ever attempting to reclaim it.\nGood-by and farewell to you, dear things.", "1_10_2": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two minutes after ten o'clock in the morning</span> and $SURNAME1 was crying, his knees were trembling, his back was breaking.\nHe was actually crying \u2014 crying like a little boy and wondering, even in his misery, where the sound of his sobbing was coming from.\nIt was not his adult body that was sobbing \u2014 not the big him. It was the him that had been him when Vera Gornick pinched his small bottom at the matinee.\nThe sobbing stopped of itself as mysteriously as it had started.\nMrs. $SURNAME0, time and space, again came to $SURNAME1\u2019s aid. There was a message for him, taped to his desk blotter. The message read:\nCONSIDER YOURSELF SENTENCED TO SEVEN YEARS IN A LUNATIC ASYLUIM The accompanying scrap of paper bore this epigraph from $FIRSTNAMEM1 Churchill: \"In the end all things merge into one\u2014and a tremendous lot of darkness.\" $FIRSTNAMEM1 S. Churchill\nMrs. $SURNAME0 explained in a postscript that she had written the Churchill epigraph for her husband, who quoted it so often. She stated that it summed up for her the leitmotifs of a book she had set herself to write\u2014a history of the nineteenth...", "0_22_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was ten seventeen in the evening</span>. At ten eighteen, the leader turned around and looked across the rec room. He stared at the Virginian. He waited. For three seconds the Virginian said nothing.\nThe leader made a backhanded flip of his hand.\nHis four accomplices jittered with instant life. They sprang from their three couches, from the floor and from around a card table. The five men were after the lone stranger.\nThe stranger didn\u2019t move.\nHe sat with legs crossed, on his bottom bunk. His face was turned up, watching loftily.\nThe five men were on him.\nIn less than twenty seconds, he was out of sight\u2014behind the men, under them, merged with them.\nIt was astonishing how the five men became one creature. They tried to tear the stranger apart, to carry him off in pieces. They couldn\u2019t. They foamed at the mouth. They spat.\nThey couldn\u2019t hurt the stranger any more than they could have hurt a steel wall. The stranger didn\u2019t touch them. They touched him.\nThe touch was magic. Like Frankenstein\u2019s monster, they had created a monstrosity they couldn\u2019t control. They couldn\u2019t...", "1_13_41": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one forty-one in the afternoon</span>. There was not a scratch on me. I had not yet begun to sweat. But I was scared and hurt and knew I would never be the same.\nOf all the things on the written exam, only one question really bothered me: What would you do if you came across a poodle in the desert?\nI studied that one. A poodle in the desert? Maybe it would be better than a pit bull in L. A. Maybe the poodles shine with a holy light, so that only those who have answered Every Question correctly are granted, without pain, a sight of that special breed.\nBut I knew nothing about poodles. My dog, who was lost, was a mixed breed\u2014part water spaniel, part jack rabbit\u2014and badly mixed at that. True, he had been vicious about two years before our trip to California, but a kind of senility had set in after that, and now we thought of him as little more than a flea circus loser. He would have flunked the written exam, for sure.\nNo dogs in general. Nothing specific, as far as I knew, about poodles. So, on the morning I left Topeka, my wife and I left our inside/...", "2_5_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-three in the morning</span> when I roared out of Hollywood...on my way to Vegas and the desert. There are some ominous rumbles in the distance as I write this\u2014but it's probably just a junkie OD. Nothing sinister. The heroin epidemic that hit Los Angeles this fall is now heading east along the grapevine, but they won't get far: Nevada has the death penalty for any form of commercial drug abuse...and a law that allows the state to give the lethal injection to any highway rider convicted of \u201cExceeding the Limits of Speed.\u201d\nAnd so, as the heroin shock blasts across the long gray reaches of the East, we can expect to hear some very nasty examples of the ultimate bias of the law; the kind of thing that could happen only in a rude and offensive society such as ours. Expect to hear of some poor bastard getting busted for doublenickel speeding; held without bond; dragged in chains before some nameless District Court Judge in rural Nowhereville\u201a being declared guilty and then hustled away to some backstreet killer in Carson City for a quickin\u201afor breaking an obscure section of a Motor Vehicle Code.", "0_6_1": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute after six o'clock in the morning</span>.\nThe alert was sounded and was over.\nThe wisdom of Joseph D. Keezar was vindicated.\n$FIRSTNAMEB6 Mercury knew nothing about it until the siren in his ear went off, and then he just smiled to himself. \"So old $FIRSTNAMEB6 is being sent out to fight, is he?\" he said to himself. \"Oh, well, this is what I joined for, anyhow. It will be exciting.\"\nThe old gang made him a messenger boy at his first assignment, and a pretty wide area it was, too. He was stationed on a hillside at one end of the $FIRSTNAMEF1-Harborer line. The rest of his gang were all either dead or out of ammunition.\nWhen the firing let up, $FIRSTNAMEB6 went down to $FIRSTNAMEF1 Harbor, where the crashing and banging were mostly over.\nBy the time the sun had come up there wasn\u2019t a singleBee-Harborer alive on Mars, and there were only forty-four Red and Blue army men left alive in all.\nThe situation required only fourteen intermediaries and one courier.\n$FIRSTNAMEB6 was an intermediary. So were ten innocent young fellows like him. And there were thirteen old men and women who knew everything\u2014the only ones who really understood the whole...", "0_14_12": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was twelve minutes after two o'clock in the afternoon</span>, Central Time. According to instructions, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 was observing the code of silence.\n\"You will understand\u2014hopefully, in time\u2014that this is the grave foreknowledge of one who has seen and spoken too much of what men hide under words like order and peace and government\u2014 seen, that is, too much of what men hide under their words,\" read the message taped to the bottom of the plastic reproduction of The Angel of the Waters. The figure on the fountain had no wings. The plaque read, \"Genuine Stolen Army Goods, Manpower Equipment Co., Ltd.\u2014Taihoku, Formosa.\"\n\"Understand, please, that my decision to buy out of the human race does not come easily,\" read $SURNAME1\u2019s message. \"It was arrived at only after years of loneliness, of suicidal brooding, of being outraged, of being bewildered, of looking for something to believe in....\"\nAnd more in the same vein.\n$SURNAME1, like all good agents, was playing an elaborate double-blind confidence game on $FIRSTNAMEM2 Shrift. His interest in Shrift had gotrumpled in the galling realization that he himself was being used by Magnum Opus, Incorporated.\nThe instruments that had picked...", "2_5_22": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was five twenty-two in the morning</span>. Before I left home it was a quarter past six. The night watchman opened up for me. My poor battered car was there, but it was not in the shade. Indian summer had come to Hollywood.\n\u201cNow, listen, Sweet Jasmine,\u201d I said. \u201cI'm a-goin' to Denver and take all those letters with me and mail 'em from there.\u201d\nShe chuckled with joy at the idea.\n\u201cThink of it, Sweet Jasmine -- me a-ride with all them letters in my trunk all across America. Now go ahead and think that over.\u201d\nI started the car, kissed Jasmine goodbye, and rolled out of the parking lot into the Hollywood streets at five twenty-three. A couple of cops were strolling along beneath the trees. They didn't look like cops of the Los Angeles type. They were plump, and the one with his hands in his pockets was wearing two-tone shoes. They looked like cops from Bergen County, New Jersey.\n", "1_4_13": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was thirteen minutes after four o'clock in the morning</span>. The place was the far point of an L-shaped salon on the second floor of the club\u2014a room that was sometimes referred to as the Emperor's Chamber.\nIt was the quietest room in the house. Business deals were often concluded in the Emperor's Chamber. Sometimes club managers were assassinated in it\u2014either by exotic dancers or by Mafiometastasis.\nMuch of the far wall consisted of a huge mural painted in day-glo colors. The mural portrayed Paradise.\nIn the Pope's version of Paradise there were bathing beauties doing their exercises in a sparkling blue surf. The girls wore tiaras and G-strings.\nThe Pope's version of Destiny was very strange indeed.\nIn the mural, the archangel Gabriel was a huge helping hand reaching out to a naked beauty on the beach. The climactic gesture was unthinkable.\nIn the Pope's version of Heaven, blue-eyed blond children played ticktacktoe with a bear on the beach. The children were identically-touced and identically-tutored.\nThe Pope's version of Judgment Day was an out-of-focus picture of the leg of an Emperor\u2019s Chamber dancer that was sticking out from under a huge towel.", "2_23_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes past eleven o'clock in the evening</span>. $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright turned carefully over on his right side, so that he was looking at the door.\nThe door was full of squares and circles.\n\"The important thing to remember,\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright, to the squares and circles on the door, \"isn\u2019t to go buying people, it\u2019s to see they don\u2019t come buying you.\"\nSo, knowing that, he didn\u2019t start laughing when he saw them in the room. They weren\u2019t really buying him; they were only pitiful. That didn\u2019t keep him from laughing.\n\"Laugh, by God, laugh,\" said the little one next to him. \"But look at us. We\u2019ve come all the way from the Sun.\"\n\"The Sun?\" said $FIRSTNAMEF3 Fernwright. \"But that\u2019s where I live.\" And he laughed some more.\n\"You lived there,\" said the Spaceman. \"But now you\u2019re here.\"", "0_15_30": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was half past three o'clock in the afternoon</span> on Monday as I sat in my little room in the Hotel Chambery on the rue Pierre Charron waiting for Albertine to come back while Ricardo Cardinal kept knocking on my door, calling my name, asking what time it was, irritating me. I thought that since I was so disturbed, I'd take my afternoon cup of coffee back in the room (I had had the waiter bring the tray to the door), and so I moved the large white-enameled cupboard to hide the chest of drawers with all the straps on it. It was very effective. Placing the large mirror over the marble-topped dresser, I found that I could hide everything from sight except a little corner of the bed and a bit of wall with a pink plaster molding. But as people rarely come into one's bedroom just to look at things, this meant nothing. I could read, I could write. Naturally I tried to read. I didn't want to think any more about Albertine. But, just as an electric light, even when we try to put it out of our thoughts, persists in glowing and burning, invisibly behind our eyelids, imaginary systems of lines and lozenges which make us continue to see the light or else see nothing...", "0_18_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was six seventeen in the evening</span> of April third. $SURNAME1, $FIRSTNAMEB2, and $FIRSTNAMEF0 were in the dwelling of $SURNAME0 on Newport Beach. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had been playing with a pair of $FIRSTNAMEM1 cigarettes he had bought in Italy. He had smoked them with his father and mother.\nThey were extremely short cigarettes, with a single file of Turkish tobacco wrapped spirally around a paper tube only a sixth of an inch in diameter.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2 picked up one of the cigarettes, and put its paper end between his teeth. The burning tip was three-quarters of an inch from the end of his nose.\nAnd now he was puffing.\nThe smoke issuing from his nostrils was as thick, as smelly as the smoke issuing from the twin smokestacks of a Turkish freighter at anchor.\nHe puffed at his cigarette three times, stopped, and put it to his lips. He gagged on the strong vile smoke.\n\"Golly!\" he said, and threw his cigarette on the floor.\n$FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s gagging, cursing revolt against $FIRSTNAMEM1 smokes was a foretaste of the rebellions that were about to strike American industry.\n$SURNAME0 had bought $FIRSTNAMEB2\u2019s signature on a contract amounting to one million...", "1_8_44": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-four in the morning</span>, according to the Titanian chronometer.\nAt two minutes after nine, $FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 stepped out of $SURNAME0\u2019s Taj Mahal and reported to a life-sized statue of a $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 in the courtyard.\nThe real $SURNAME0 sat framed in light in one of the museum windows. He watched $SURNAME1 come out and make his humiliating report.\nThere weren\u2019t, of course, any wisps in the sky. There weren\u2019t any falls, either. Nothing very spectacular at all was happening up there.\nThe sky was simply blue and speckled.\n\"Your son and heir will not be born for four Earthling years,\" said $SURNAME1 to the $SURNAME0 image. \"And upon being born he will not be named $FIRSTNAMEB2.\"\n\"You disappoint me,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I\u2014\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"You have failed as no one in my memory has ever failed,\" said $SURNAME0.\n\"I thought\u2014\" said $SURNAME1.\n\"You thought of nothing,\" said $SURNAME0. \"You have proved that the air spaces of Titan, though uncolonized by man, are still uncivilized. What is the point of naming the...", "1_20_10": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten minutes after eight o'clock in the evening</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB1 arrived in Orynx with Salo. The mansion that $FIRSTNAMEB1 came to was the home of $SURNAME0\u2019s mother, his only living blood relative except for young $FIRSTNAMEB2.\nThe mansion was electrically illuminated by $SURNAME0, who tapped the $FIRSTNAMEM1 Dynasty\u2019s power line. Around the mansion was a wall of stone, and the wall was surmounted by four feet of barbed wire. Two mean dogs bred in the junkyard lived in the four feet between the stone wall and the barbed wire, and they wouldn\u2019t let anybody climb over the wire, either.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 landed his chrono-balance in a small park across from the wall.\n\"How do we get in?\" said Salo. \"We going to stand out here in the rain all night?\"\n\"No,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1, \"I\u2019ll get us in sooner or later.\" He walked around the outside of the wall several times.\n\"What you doing?\" said Salo. \"Examining the mortar?\"\n\"Yep,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB1.\n\"What for?\" said Salo. \"It wasn\u2019t put there by a man named Herbert, was it?\"\n\"No,\"...", "2_5_8": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight minutes after five o'clock in the morning</span>. We were about to go up to room 157 of the Hotel Americana and rendezvous with Lucy and Truman Melvin Drane.\nTen minutes to six o'clock. Lucy and Little Lucy were still asleep in their legal bed, dreaming of crystal cocaine skies, when our knock came. My attorney answered the knock and we kicked into the room with a horrible hallucinogenic roar. There was smoke and steam everywhere, and bongs and rolling papers galore. Our knock had awakened the Drane child first; she sat up straight in bed...fell back on her pillow, her mouth a black cave below her thickened marijuana lashes. The knocker that woke her was on fire; the handle had caught some freak hanging scarf and was burning merrily. Before I could grab it, the scarf flew off into a corner, where it began to burn a hole in the Versailles bedroom carpet. The smoke was getting heavy. Holy Toledo. Would you believe it?\n\u201cUp!\u201d I shouted to Little Lucy. \u201cWe\u2019re blowing this scene!\"", "1_5_51": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine minutes to six o'clock in the morning</span> and the Love crowd was slowly breaking up. Remi the bartender was putting out the beer cases and discussing the matter pleasantly with the only customer left in the place. The long bar of the Festival Lounge high overhead was being mopped in the semi dark. Somewhere over the trees of Narcoosee the sun was coming up. Last night was over. We left the Love crowd and took a cab to the stadium. The Wild Brat and his gang had already beat the dawn in. They were roaring drunk and in full charge.\nI went over to find somebody and said, \u201cTake me to your Pimco,\u201d and one of them, who later turned out to be called Old Bull Lee and a grand guy, shoved a big, hairy arm around my neck and dragged me to it. They were sitting in a deuce Coupe. \u201cThis is our Pimco,\u201d he said. \u201cWant to drive it?\u201d\n\u201cYou bet.\u201d I got behind the wheel. Bull Lee and the Brat and the rest jumped in and told me to take off. I drove the car eight miles down Highway 1 to the stadium parking lot. When I stopped, four more big hairy arms grabbed me and...", "2_1_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one thirty-eight in the morning</span>.\n$SURNAME1 was Earthling Number 55,634,875 to travel in a space ship to Mars. The first 55,634,874 earthlings had died of old age.\nOne ten-millionth of a second later, the ship was on the opposite side of Mars and decelerating rapidly.\nIt was Shedd C. Martin, Jr., veteran Air Force pilot, who got the news to President Salmagundi by radio.\nThe radio signal reached Salmagundi at three minutes after two o\u2019clock in the morning, Eastern Standard Time. The President was awakened by thumping on the porthole of his bunk. When an aide threw up the little window, the pilot of the space ship was thrust into the cabin.\nThe President snarled his most repellent snarl. \"Wassamatter?\" he snapped.\nThe pilot, a lieutenant colonel, snapped a salute. \"Advance orders for you and a party!\" he said. \"There are arrangements to be made, sir!\"", "0_18_59": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one minute until seven o'clock in the evening</span>.\nGold was firing his fission-needles point blank now at anything silver. He oriented on the hatch of the space ship that had brought him to Mars.\nHe sensed, rather than saw, the Martian monster named $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0 materialize from the darkness near the ship.\nGold acted, then.\nThe fission-needle in his right hand spat its ten-inch, blue, ball-lightning spiral so swiftly that, passing through $SURNAME0, all but disappearing, it went on to puncture the solid bronze binnacle of the Nerkonese space ship.\nThe spiral disappeared into the great binnacle, coursed in bluish, lapping spiral waves within the metal. Slowly, the binnacle softened, puddled, became a shapeless blob.\nThe blob fell through two decks of the ship, plunked into the control pit where the helmsman sat.\nThe helmsman, who was $SURNAME0, died before he hit the bottom.\nThe blob solidified into a sculpture of $SURNAME0. It stood in ironic dignity, with its back to the helmsman\u2019s chair, for almost one Earthling year. Then it crumbled into dust.", "1_13_33": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-three in the afternoon</span> of October third. One hundred and six hours and fifty minutes till blast-off.\nIn his cubicle on the floor above, the man who was called $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2014who was called $FIRSTNAMEB0\u2014slept like a baby. All the night before, he had lain sleepless and fantastically feverish in his tent, counting the cricketlike chirpings of his alarm clock. The chirpings had meant that five minutes had gone by. The alarm would go off in five minutes more. And so on. And so on.\n$FIRSTNAMEB1 had finally fallen asleep after dreaming up a nightmare alarm clock whose bell chimes were the screams of his mother-in-law. This ghastly instrument had kept going off in vibrant crescendos, with $FIRSTNAMEB1 sitting up in bed suddenly each time, groping for his gun. This device for waking the dead seemed much safer than the actual alarm clock that lay throbbing on the night table.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had an important visitor coming in the morning\u2014a visitor who was to lift the curse of amnesia from his mind. He had an idea that he had done something disgraceful in his scouting days. He had an idea that his visitor could tell him what the disgraceful thing was...", "0_21_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was nine thirty-seven in the evening</span>. We were approaching the place where our nights of life had diverged. I picked up a stone and threw it out of the canyon.\n\u201cI wonder what happened to Bob RAWLinson.\u201d\n\u201cI don\u2019t know.\u201d\n\u201cHe isn\u2019t in New York. He must be around in the sticks somewhere.\u201d\n\u201cHe may have gone to South America,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB5. \u201cThat\u2019s where all the British colony has gone. If you want to contact them you have to write care of Butch at the Bolivian Miners\u2019 Club in La Paz.\u201d\n\u201cButch don\u2019t amount to much,\u201d I said.\n\u201cTell me,\u201d said $FIRSTNAMEB5. \u201cI\u2019d like to know. I\u2019m interested in British colonists.\u201d\n\u201cOh, we\u2019re all right. We do as well as the next lot.\u201d\n\u201cHow lovely it must be in the home country.\u201d\n\u201cIsn\u2019t it?\u201d\n\u201cI\u2019d love to get back there.\u201d\n\u201c", "1_14_39": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two thirty-nine in the afternoon</span>, time for the next anhalter rumble. I poured a cup of coffee and put four lumps of sugar in it, making it into a kind of decadent milkshake, and underneath it I prayed as always for one Jim D. Stevens, and that I might by some wild and karmic circumstance actually locate him. He was the only soul I knew in San Francisco. I had him down at the sad bulb of myself that flickered in my battered intuition, ready to light at any moment, if only a breeze would come. Our last meeting had been accidental and brief. Ah, I knew that hellish and spectral San Francisco Bruce's Roost crowd, a lady with a gold filling in her teeth, a big man with tapehanging eyes, the infamous Terry the Tramp, who used to hang outside the door of a filthy hallway scuffling along in his worn-out shoes with the mushy soles, two bits a throw, to let people see what a fine dancer he was. But he had no customers and nobody even looked .. He disappeared and I missed him; I had no idea what became of him. Now I saw him raggedly walking down Market Street, hunched against the rain, flipping his melancholy...", "1_12_23": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was twelve twenty-three p.m.</span> when $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB1, and young $FIRSTNAMEB2 leaped from the cave up to the sky.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 were a little surprised that young $FIRSTNAMEB2 came along with them. $FIRSTNAMEB2 had overheard the plans for escape, and he had insisted on going with them.\nThe three of them who emerged from the cave in their stocking feet, who staggered in their wonder under the benevolent white lamps of the Martian sky, had been sentenced to death.\nThey had been sentenced as defective in the reveille by the colors of $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s visions.\nThe colors of $FIRSTNAMEB1\u2019s visions had been with him since he was ten years old. And $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB2, his son, both knew by heart the meaning of the colors.\nThe Martian Army put maneuvers over, so that the three deserters could have an unobserved departure.\nWhen $FIRSTNAMEB0, $FIRSTNAMEB1, and young $FIRSTNAMEB2 arrived at the place where the space ship had landed, the ship was gone.\nThe ship was piloted by a fool-proof fool-proof system. The system would bring the ship down in exactly one day, at exactly the same place where it had gone up. At the same time, the system would bring the...", "0_22_3": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was three minutes after ten o'clock in the evening</span>. $SURNAME0 and his dog were materializing from nothingness at a point one foot in front of Keezore\u2019s walker.\nThe point of arrival was, of course, the uninsulated center of the electrical field.\nGalaxymurk, cloud, dingus, and so on\u2014these things were nothing to $FIRSTNAMEM1 $FIRSTNAMEM0 $SURNAME0.\nThe important point is that $SURNAME0 at that time believed firmly that death by electrocution was both disgusting and inglorious. He believed, furthermore, that he would have to endure the gruesome, nasty, horrible pain of electrocution for ten or fifteen seconds before dying.\nHis final words, spoken to his dog, were, \"I was wrong about the pain, dog\u2014it is worse than I thought it would be. Remember me fondly.\" Keezore, meanwhile, had been knocked to the ground by the impact of $SURNAME0 and his dog. He lay there, dazed.\n$SURNAME0\u2019s last words repeated themselves in his mind.\nThere were now, of course, no power lines for a thousand feet on both sides of him\u2014but Keezore found himself looking up ruefully at a power line three hundred feet...", "1_4_57": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three minutes to five o'clock in the morning</span>. Nobody on all the streets. No lights anywhere. A few stars overhead. The tops of the buildings were lost in the darkness overhead. The street was white, and lost.\nNever before had such a silence reigned over Manhattan.\n\"Good-by, $FIRSTNAMEF0,\" said $FIRSTNAMEB0.\nHe took a step forward. It exhausted him, but it carried him out of the ambush. \"Good-by, buddy boy\u2014 goodbye\u2014\" he cried brokenly.\nHis face was wet with weeping. He staggered out of the ambush, and he wept openly.\nHe opened his arms stealthily, like a man opening a spy-hole between stone walls. He held his hands high and palms out, as though to show he was not carrying anything dangerous. He took another stumbling step forward.\nHe wept out loud.\nHe was saying good-by forever to anything that had meaning. Heartbreakingly beautiful girl, heartfelt good-by. House, family\u2014 good-by. Sun, moon, Milky Way\u2014good-by. Everything good-by, good-by.\nHe staggered out to the middle of the empty street.\nAnd he opened his arms as wide as he could, because he wanted to take in everything.", "0_1_16": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was one sixteen in the morning</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB0 knew that because the ship\u2019s clock had just chimed for the fourteenth time. And the place was dark, because $FIRSTNAMEB0 had just turned off the overhead fluorescent fixtures. And $FIRSTNAMEB0 was alone in his Memorium, because the three other human beings on the planet were busy scanning the telepathic signals of living things in the rocket fleet.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 had locked the heavy bronze door of the Memorium, had turned off the lights as a security measure. The war was over, but some of the houses in the village were still in ruins.\nInside the Memorium, $FIRSTNAMEB0 built a fire in the open fireplace, then, while the fire was growing, got the two phonographs going.\nThe first phonograph, on a side table, played-parodying the pompous street beat of the homosexual party-march time employed by the Solar Army: \"Left, right! Left, right! Left, right! Left, right! Left, right-hurrah! Hurrah!\u2014\"\nThe second phonograph was an amateur model on which $FIRSTNAMEB0, trying to reproduce on a small scale the public address system of the Martian Army, had composed his own rendition of Roll-Call of the Solar Phalanx:...", "2_16_27": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four twenty-seven in the afternoon</span>.\nIn a moment they were locked together in a confused gyration on the sidewalk of Telegraph. At four thirty the sun began to shine very hard indeed.\nThe meeting in progress in the bar room at the drug store did not break up so much as simply fall apart, scattering its human contents like cracked eggs. The man with the long nose and the pencil-thin mustache had a seizure and fell down on his back. Several boys, fainting girls, and an unidentifiable hairy object on the floor joined him. Ten minutes later Spivey, operator of the syphilis wing of Saint Alphonsus, arrived with his two attendants and gently carried them all off on stretchers. It was a situation in which help was required. The police arrived at five-fifteen, when the competition between Love RIOT and the Eisenhower High School Fertility Group finals was supposed to begin, and announced their intention of clearing the place. The organizers of the Symposium declined to argue the matter and filed out peaceably, leaving the street empty for the arrival of $FIRSTNAMEF4 from Frisco. She was tired from her journey, and with good reason.", "1_16_0": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was four o'clock in the afternoon</span>, and we'd been on the road for twelve hours. For twelve hours $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I had talked about everything there was beneath the heaven to discuss and we were unable to come to any conclusion about anything at all. For instance, in our total ignorance we decided Protestant was no good and Catholic was no good and Democracy was no good and Communism was no good and Freudian psychoanalysis was no good and Freudian sex habits was no good and there was no answer. I began to think we'd never find a civilization that suited us both. Every civilization we passed was inferior. It was not a failure of land, it was not a failure of the sea, it was a failure of the spirit. We were doing everything the modern way and getting nowhere.\n\u201cMan, what are you all?\u201d cried $FIRSTNAMEB3 suddenly, sweeping his glance over the landscape. \"What are you trying to do with yourselves? Where are you all going? What brings on all these endless sports and games, hatreds and races, vanities and spiteful little intrigues, all bedizened with gadgets and mechanical junk and so desolately sterile? What bores you? What bores me!\u201d He leaned back and stared at me and waited for the answer. He...", "0_13_17": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one seventeen in the afternoon</span>. $FIRSTNAMEB3 and I agreed that that was the absolute true time. \u201cNow we go, we gotta go now -- $FIRSTNAMEB6, you take all the money in the bag you can use but don't get lost, take of your inhibitions -- we gotta go now, yes. I've just looked at my Time magazine in the parlor and it said in an article about Joe McCarthy 13 that he can get guys like Bibb to do all his dirty work for a mere pittance or a hotel suite or a girdle or whatever. How about that guy in the White House? How the hell did he ever get in? I don't even understand how America can even think that way. Whatshesame -- Englewood, New Jersey, housewife!\u201d And he looked out the window at the rainy day. \u201cI swear to God I'm gonna go there some day and straighten every one of those sons of bi -- out. We'll be there, man, in our ghost Cadillac, roaring like a lion, right down the main drag. Years from now our ghosts will return, to tally the odds in their favor -- that is, if God can even hear a pray from such asses as those. Yes! And they say they are Christian...", "0_1_37": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was one thirty-seven in the morning</span> when the police car carrying Ingersol, Metzger, and Neal arrived at the base of the Space Wanderer. The lights of Reno and the Sierras were vivid points of light on the black sky. To their left, the gantry like structure of the roller coaster rose with a subterranean roar inside its tunnel and popped out over the sand amid the hiss of rockets and the crackle of electricity.\n\"You must be out of your minds going to climb that thing in the middle of the night,\" the patrolman said. \"Don\u2019t you know there\u2019re no bungee nets after dark?\"\n\"Yes, we know,\" Metzger said. \"But thanks just the same.\"\n\"You need a skeeter escort?\" the cop asked. \"We can whistle up a skeeter. It\u2019ll carry you up there like a train.\"\n\"No thanks,\" said Metzger again.\n\"Grains.\" The cop shook his head. \"What\u2019ll you do at the top, if you make it?\"\nIngersol chuckled. \"I think we\u2019ll just stand in the middle and twitch,\" he said. \"Goodnight, officer.\"\n\"Well, goodnight...", "0_9_42": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was nine forty-two in the morning</span>, Pacific Standard Time.\nThe place was a chalet with screened windows and a wide lawn that ended in a golf course with the shimmer of a mountain lake behind the fairway and a sprinkling of golden and white feathers on the surfaces of the wind-veined deep green waters.\nThe chalet had been rented by luscious zany $SURNAME0 for a cool half a million dollars.\nThe chalet would rent again next year for a cool half a million dollars.\nThe husband of the woman with her bare feet on the rail of the veranda and her toes in the chilly vaporous damp of San Diego said, \"It\u2019s a nice enough place, Skip, but to tell the truth I think we\u2019d\u2019ve been as comfortable and a hell of a lot happier at Newport or Southampton. You know, Newport or Southampton. It\u2019s up to you, Skip, but I always imagined the richest fellow in the world would take the richest fellow in the world\u2019s wife to Newport or Southampton for August.\"\nA butler came out with two glasses of iced orange juice on a tall silver tray. He had a newspaper, rolled, under his arm. He smiled impersonally and...", "1_15_46": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was three forty-six in the afternoon</span> and the Chief got down from the telephone booth. \u201cLast night Rawlie the Whiner called his Fresno agent, who did nothing about it, so now for twenty-four hours the Fresno people can go right ahead and hassle the poor bastard. Then the Chief gave me a piece of paper with a name and number on it. \u201cThis is the guy who gives me all my news.\u201d He was a Dr. R. S. Dickerson. I didn\u2019t call Doc Dickerson. I figured there was no hurry. At six p.m. the \"Duquesne cutting crews\" went on duty, because it was costing us too much money to idle around with that gold wheel. Before the shift I went over my stash for the last time. Enough bundle of wires, safety pins, paper, string and cotton Tips to fell a horse....And a ball on a chain to rum with when I got lonely...And a brand new strop for my razor blade! All I needed was a case of beer and a woman. Maybe two or three of each. Maybe get drunk, then blindfold a girl to the eyeballs with a silk scarf and run the thin sharp blade all around her tender body and curl the tings wherever they...", "2_23_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eleven forty-three in the evening</span>.\nThe population of the ship diminished by one.\nThe Company was in port.\nWhen a ship is in port and her people are barred from the city, the company should count itself lucky indeed. Lacking the excitement of the forbidden, it makes for some pretty amazing excitement on its own.\nA whole phony world.\nSea Change was sliding past a forest of masts and tenders and yachts and ferryboats and the huge illuminated dead bulk-heads of the Newport Star.\nTap. Tap. Tap.\nThey hung out of windows over curlicues.\nThe tenders were white and gold. The ferryboats were winged by the outline of their cables against the sunset and by the swarms of lights within.\nIn Newport it never got dark. There was always more light.\nThe ferryboat passengers spilled out onto the docks of every island they touched at. Island passengers rushed onto the decks of the ferryboats. It was crazy. The whole world was going out in little boats.\nThe air was ocean air. It sloshed.\nThe tenders slashed their shiny wakes.", "0_20_43": "<span class=\"clocktext\">The time was eight forty-three in the evening</span>. A thousand breaks and starts, openings and closings had preceded this moment. Chronologically, the first was back in New York, when Metzger, a few minutes after six, started to phone her and then hung up half-way through the call when he heard her voice. He never did say he was coming, she remembered. He\u2019d told her he was bringing friends. And then whatever happened that day stayed with her all afternoon and evening, far out on the edge of San Narciso, California, the last Los Angeles suburb and then the authentic dryness and whatever fate you want\u2014no more houses, supermarkets, stop lights, stray dogs, struggling lawns. All commerce apparently retreated this far, to be replaced by research concern: the Daniel Garrison Brinkley Study Center, whose cornerstone was laid by the Sun-Sign Astrologyer in whose Suitely habitat $FIRSTNAMEF2 and Metzger had suffered their migraine afternoon. Far from that residential afternoon, that attendant clown, at the exact antipode of Point Reyes, in another bedroom alltogether, another nervously earnest couple tried without much success, the evening of the same day, to get stoned on a trip they\u2019d bought from the same mail-order house. The domestic...", "0_22_38": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was ten thirty-eight in the evening</span> when I got back to the room. I felt ill and all dirty. I took a shower and then went to bed. I had some bloody disaster dreams. I woke up sweating, and feeling that I ought to get up and work out. I decided to rest just a minute longer, and then I hadn\u2019t any choice because my new body was infected with tremendously painful things. I got up and walked out to the bath again, and sat down on the tile rubbing my thighs. Across the tub I saw a picture of myself in the cracked, blowing-down mirror: a big, white, battered, sore-looking drunk. It scared me. I went back to the room and dressed and went down-stairs and started downtown to look for a job.\nBill crept past; the shop-windows displayed their knots of laces and their faces, old hats shivered and shook, clocks told time; I was the only thing that moved; the brilliant membranes of my eyes were stretched and aching. I was sick and tired. I was sick and tired of whatever I was sick and tired with. At noon I went into a Greek restaurant and read the Greek newspaper and ate some tepid spicy food. I was all hung over with liquor...", "2_14_19": "<span class=\"clocktext\">It was two nineteen in the afternoon</span>.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had taken up a good position on the wall around the estate\u2014again on the blind side, again on the Morse clam beds.\nIt was now or never, they had decided.\n$SURNAME0 went to the microphone. \"Fellow Earthlings!\" he said. \"I introduce to you $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 of Mars!\"\nIt was now did.\n$FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 jumped the wall\u2014landed on the estate, hustled toward the platform.\nThe automatic reflexes of $FIRSTNAMEB0 and $FIRSTNAMEB1 had them bounding straight ahead, not toward $SURNAME0\u2019s platform, but beneath it\u2014bounding beneath it like frightened gazelles beneath the legs of an elephant that\u2019s about to stampede.\n$FIRSTNAMEM2 $SURNAME1 and his wife were in the bed, hands clasped in terror as though in prayer. A layer of plastic kept the sweat from their hands from smearing over the silk sheets and pajamas."}